/* Pearson Edexcel GCSE History (1HI0) — room kits built by hand in a Claude Code session: written by
   Opus 5 subagents to the contract in src/kit-validator.js, every question re-solved and the lesson read against
   the specification map by a fresh Opus 5 subagent before shipping. See .claude/skills/course-builder/references/depth.md. */
module.exports = { ID: 'EDX-1HI0', KITS: {"10-1":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"10-1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This is the opening room of the Crime and punishment thematic study, and it supplies the 'before' half of almost every question you will be asked in Paper 1 Section B, which is worth 36 of the paper's 52 marks. Question 3 asks you to compare two periods, Question 4 asks why something changed across a long stretch of time, and Question 5 or 6 asks you to judge how far change happened; all three need a precise, dated starting point. If you cannot say who caught a criminal in 1050, what the Forest Laws made illegal after 1066, why trial by ordeal stopped after 1215 and what the Statute of Labourers of 1351 criminalised, you have nothing to measure later change against. This room also introduces the three key factors — attitudes in society, individuals and institutions, and science and technology — in the period where the Church and the Crown matter most and technology matters not at all.","idea":[{"h":"Crime is defined by the society that makes the law","t":"Medieval England was rural, poor, deeply religious and ruled through a hierarchy of lord and tenant that the Norman Conquest hardened into a feudal order. There was no police force, no forensic science, no national prison system and no professional detective; government was small and reached the village only through local men. The Church, meanwhile, had a building, a priest and a court in every part of the country, and everybody believed in heaven, hell and divine judgement. Every feature of medieval crime and punishment follows from those facts: proof came from oaths and from God because no other proof existed, catching criminals was the neighbours' job because there was nobody else, and punishment was public and physical because there was nowhere to lock anyone up for long. A top-level answer never treats medieval justice as stupid; it explains the law out of the society, which is the analysis the AO2 marks reward.","code":"10 — The process of change: crime and punishment and the key features of society"},{"h":"The three key factors, and how each worked in this period","t":"Edexcel names three factors for the whole theme, and you should use their names as the spine of your paragraphs so that you can compare like with like across a thousand years. Attitudes in society: collective responsibility made the tithing and the hue and cry work, deference made treason the worst of all crimes, and community sympathy made poaching a 'social crime' that neighbours would not report. Individuals and institutions: the Church ran the ordeal, offered sanctuary and protected its own through benefit of clergy, while kings from Henry II onwards pushed royal justice outwards through travelling judges, coroners from 1194 and Justices of the Peace from 1361. Science and technology: almost nothing — there was no way to identify an offender except by being seen, which is why witnesses, oaths and reputation decided cases. A top-level answer names the factor it is using and keeps using that vocabulary throughout.","code":"10 — The process of change: the key factors"},{"h":"Factors combine; they rarely act alone","t":"Change happens in this theme when more than one factor pushes the same way. Trial by ordeal did not end because one pope disapproved: the Fourth Lateran Council of 1215 withdrew the priests who made the ritual meaningful, educated churchmen had already come to doubt that God could be commanded to give a verdict, and the Crown had a ready substitute in the juries of local men its courts were already using, plus a motive to bring justice under royal control. The Statute of Labourers of 1351 is the same story: an event (the Black Death of 1348–49) created a labour shortage, the attitudes of the landowners who sat in Parliament turned that shortage into an outrage, and a government with Justices of the Peace to enforce the law turned the outrage into a crime. A top-level answer shows the combination working, usually in the last sentence of each paragraph, instead of listing factors separately.","code":"10 — The process of change: factors working together"},{"h":"What the strand 3 case study is for","t":"Each period of this option ends with a case study, and its job is to turn the general content of strands 1 and 2 into one dated, concrete episode you can quote as precise evidence. For c1000–c1500 the case study is the influence of the Church on crime and punishment in the early thirteenth century: sanctuary, benefit of clergy, trial by ordeal and the reasons for its ending. It is the place where the Church can be seen doing three different things at once — supplying the proof-system, sheltering fugitives and shielding its own clergy from the gallows — which is exactly the mixture a judgement question needs. It also gives you the comparison points the examiner rewards later, because it can be set against the Gunpowder Plotters of 1605, the witch-hunts of 1645–47 and the modern case studies. A top-level answer uses it as selected evidence inside an argument, never as a story told for its own sake.","code":"10 — The process of change: the role of the strand 3 case studies"},{"h":"What counted as crime: person, property, authority — and 'social' crime","t":"Medieval crimes fall into three groups the specification names. Crimes against the person included assault, murder and rape. Crimes against property included theft — much the commonest offence in the records, usually of small items, livestock or food — along with arson. Crimes against authority included treason, defined precisely by the Treason Act of 1351 as plotting the king's death, levying war against him or forging his coin, and punished by being hanged, drawn and quartered. Counterfeiting the coinage therefore belongs with crimes against authority and not with crimes against property: forging the king's coin was high treason, because the coinage was the king's own. Alongside these sat 'social crime': acts that were against the law but that ordinary people did not think wrong and would not report, of which poaching is the specification's own example, because taking a rabbit or a deer from land the king or a lord had enclosed looked to a hungry village like common sense. A top-level answer uses social crime to show that law and public opinion can part company.","code":"10-1 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Crimes against the person, property and authority"},{"h":"The Norman Conquest: new crimes made by a new ruler","t":"William I did not invent a new legal system; he changed what the law protected. His Forest Laws took vast areas of England into royal forest — the New Forest in Hampshire was created in about 1079, with villages cleared to make it — and made hunting deer, taking timber and grazing animals there offences against the Crown, punished by heavy fines and mutilation; even dogs kept inside the forest had to be lamed so that they could not chase game. The murdrum fine made the whole hundred pay if a Norman was found murdered and the killer was not produced, which protected a tiny ruling minority by punishing a community collectively. Trial by combat was introduced, separate Church courts were set up from about 1072–76, and cases were pleaded in Norman French. A top-level answer shows that conquest changed the definition of crime because defining crime was one of the ways a conquered country was held down.","code":"10-1 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Changing definitions of crime as a result of the Norman Conquest"},{"h":"Who enforced the law: the community first, the Crown second","t":"In Anglo-Saxon England every male over the age of twelve belonged to a tithing of ten men who answered for each other's behaviour: if one absconded, the rest had to produce him or pay a fine. A victim raised the hue and cry, and everyone who heard it was obliged to drop what they were doing and join the chase, with the whole village liable to a fine if it did not. Courts were local — manor, hundred and shire — and guilt could be settled by oath-helping, in which neighbours swore to a suspect's character. The Normans kept all of it. On top, the Crown built steadily: the Assize of Clarendon of 1166 required hundreds to present suspects, coroners were created in 1194 to investigate suspicious deaths, the Statute of Winchester of 1285 required towns to keep a night watch, and Justices of the Peace were established by an Act of 1361. A top-level answer separates the unchanging bottom of the system from the growing royal top.","code":"10-1 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities"},{"h":"What punishment was for: deterrence, retribution — and, at the start, compensation","t":"Anglo-Saxon law priced injury and death. The wergild, or 'man price', was compensation paid by the offender to the victim or the victim's family, and it rose with the victim's rank, so a nobleman's life cost many times an ordinary free man's; separate tariffs covered the loss of an eye, a hand or a thumb. The Normans abolished wergild and had fines paid to the king instead, which turned a private injury into an offence against the king's peace. After that, punishment was overwhelmingly about deterrence and retribution: fines for minor offences; the stocks and the pillory, whipping and mutilation for humiliation and pain; hanging for serious theft and murder; and hanging, drawing and quartering for treason. Punishment was public because a punishment nobody saw could deter nobody, and prison was mainly a place to hold suspects before trial. A top-level answer tests every example against those aims rather than describing the punishments.","code":"10-1 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The emphasis on deterrence and retribution"},{"h":"Case study: the Church and justice in the early thirteenth century","t":"Around 1200 the Church stood at the centre of the justice system in three ways. It supplied the proof: trial by ordeal, used when oath-helping failed, was a religious ritual run by a priest — carrying a red-hot iron three paces, plunging an arm into boiling water, being lowered bound into cold water, or, for a priest, swallowing blessed bread — and the verdict was read as God's. It supplied refuge: a fugitive who reached a church could claim sanctuary, and had forty days to stand trial or to confess before the coroner and 'abjure the realm', leaving the country from a named port. And it protected its own: benefit of clergy sent clergy to Church courts, which could not impose death, and the test drifted into reading the 'neck verse' from Psalm 51, so that by 1489 a statute limited laymen to one claim and had them branded on the thumb. The ordeal ended when the Fourth Lateran Council of 1215 forbade priests to take part, and in 1219 England's judges were told to find other means. A top-level answer shows the Church as both the engine and the brake of change.","code":"10-1 — 3 Case study: The influence of the Church on crime and punishment in the early thirteenth century"}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 3, 4 marks: turning two descriptions into one analysed difference","setup":"Paper 1 Question 3, worth 4 marks: 'Explain one way in which the methods used to decide guilt in the years c1000–c1500 were different from the methods used to decide guilt in the years c1900–present.' The mark scheme gives 2 marks for AO2 and 2 for AO1, so one difference must be analysed and supported with specific information from both periods named in the question.","steps":["Choose a difference of kind rather than of degree: in the medieval period guilt was established by the sworn word of people who already knew the accused and, when that failed, by an appeal to God, whereas since c1900 it has been established by physical traces that a stranger can test.","Evidence the medieval half with named detail: a suspect cleared himself by oath-helping, swearing his innocence and producing neighbours — commonly twelve — who swore that they believed him rather than to anything they had witnessed; if he could not, he was sent to trial by ordeal, a ritual run by a priest whose outcome was read as God's verdict.","Evidence the modern half with named detail: the Metropolitan Police opened its Fingerprint Branch in July 1901 and the Stratton brothers were convicted of murder at the Old Bailey in May 1905 on a single thumbprint, the first such conviction in Britain; in January 1988 Colin Pitchfork became the first murderer in Britain convicted using DNA profiling.","Close with the sentence that makes it a difference and not two descriptions: proof has moved from a man's standing among the neighbours who judged him to marks left at the scene, which can convict someone no one in the community has ever met."],"cues":["Which single difference about how guilt was proved is strong enough to carry the whole answer?","What named medieval detail shows how guilt was actually settled?","What named detail since c1900 shows what has taken its place?","What must the final sentence do that the two halves have not done?"]},{"title":"Question 4, 12 marks: using the stimulus points and then going past them","setup":"Paper 1 Question 4, worth 12 marks: 'Explain why fines were used so widely as a punishment in England in the years c1000–c1500.' You may use the following in your answer: • the king's peace • manor courts. You must also use information of your own. The cap printed in the mark scheme is a maximum of 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and those answers have no access to Level 4; there is no cap at Level 2.","steps":["Build the first reason out of the first stimulus point, with a causal chain: after 1066 an offence was treated as a breach of the king's peace rather than as a private injury, so the wergild once paid to a victim's family was abolished and the money was paid to the king instead — which made a sum of money the standard way of settling a wrong.","Build the second reason out of the second stimulus point: most offences never left the village, and the manor and hundred courts that dealt with them had no power to imprison anyone, so the ordinary outcome was an amercement, a sum fixed by the court itself for drawing blood, brewing bad ale, selling short weight or raising a false alarm.","Now add the third reason, which is nowhere in the stimulus, so that the answer addresses three or more aspects of content and Level 4 is open: a money penalty paid for itself. Death was kept for felony — theft of goods worth more than twelve pence, the line between grand and petty larceny first drawn in statute in Edward I's reign — and there was no prison system in which to punish anyone, while the amercements taken in the sheriffs' and the travelling justices' courts went to the Exchequer and were entered on the pipe rolls.","Tie the three together in one sentence so that the reasoning is sustained: the fine fitted the system at every level — it matched a law that had made the king the injured party, it was the only penalty most courts could impose, and it was the only punishment that raised money instead of costing it."],"cues":["How do you turn 'the king's peace' from a label into a reason?","What could a manor court actually do to an offender, and what could it not do?","Which reason of your own is big enough to lift the answer past the stimulus cap?","What has to happen in the final sentence for the reasoning to count as sustained?"]},{"title":"Question 5, 16 marks: choosing the criterion before choosing the evidence","setup":"Paper 1 Question 5, worth 16 marks plus up to 4 for SPaG: \"'The king's government did more than local communities to keep order in England in the years c1000–c1500.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\" You may use the following in your answer: • the sheriff • tithings. You must also use information of your own. Level 4 requires criteria for the judgement to be justified and applied, and an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content is capped at 11 marks and has no access to Level 4.","steps":["Fix the measure in the opening lines, before any evidence goes down: keeping order meant three separate jobs — catching a suspect, deciding guilt and punishing the convicted — so the question is which side did more of each.","Give the community its strongest ground on the first job, with detail: every male over the age of twelve belonged to a tithing of ten men who answered for one another, the victim raised the hue and cry and everyone who heard it had to join the chase, and the arrest was made by an unpaid parish constable serving for a year, so almost every case began with neighbours rather than with officials.","Show the Crown building the machinery underneath that, with dates: the sheriff kept the king's peace in the county and could raise the men of the shire to continue a pursuit the hue and cry had lost, the Assize of Clarendon of 1166 had local men present suspects to travelling royal judges and provided gaols to hold them, coroners were created in 1194, the Statute of Winchester of 1285 ordered towns to keep a night watch, and Justices of the Peace were established by the Act of 1361.","Weigh the third job, which neither stimulus point mentions: the serious penalties were the Crown's alone. A manor court could amerce a man a few pence for drawing blood, but only a royal court could hang him, and after 1066 the wergild once paid to a victim's family was abolished and the money went to the king — so the community could catch an offender but could not decide what happened to him.","Judge on the criterion rather than on the quantity of evidence: say which job each side owns, and then say which of the three did most to decide whether order was actually kept."],"cues":["What would 'keeping order' have to be broken into before the two sides can be compared?","Which part of the process belonged to neighbours, and what dated detail proves it?","What was the sheriff for, and what had the Crown added to him by 1361?","Which part of the process is missing from both stimulus points?","How do you reach a judgement without simply counting paragraphs?"]},{"title":"Diagnosing a weak paragraph and rebuilding it: why treason was the worst crime","setup":"A student answering Paper 1 Question 4, 'Explain why treason was regarded as the most serious crime in England in the years c1000–c1500' (stimulus points: • the Treason Act of 1351 • hanging, drawing and quartering), writes: 'Treason was the most serious crime because of the Treason Act of 1351 and because traitors were hanged, drawn and quartered. It was very harsh. This shows treason was serious.' Your task is to say which level that paragraph sits in and to rebuild it.","steps":["Diagnose it against the descriptors: it names the stimulus without explaining anything, so it is a simple, generalised answer lacking development — Level 1 — and, addressing only the two aspects the stimulus prompts, it would be held to 8 marks even if it were developed.","Rebuild the mechanism rather than the label: the Treason Act of 1351 set out in law what treason was — plotting the king's death, levying war against him, siding with his enemies, and forging his seal or his coin — so the offence was defined by the threat to the king's person and authority rather than by harm done to a victim, which is what separates it in kind from murder or theft.","Add the second mechanism the same way: the punishment was built to be watched. A traitor was drawn through the streets to the gallows, hanged without being allowed to die, disembowelled, beheaded and cut into four, and the quarters were sent away for display — Dafydd ap Gruffydd was put to death in that way at Shrewsbury in 1283 and his quarters distributed to other towns — so the sentence itself advertised what attacking the Crown cost.","Add the point of your own that escapes the cap: treason was an offence against the duty of obedience itself, which is why the same Act also defined petty treason — a servant killing his master, a wife killing her husband, a clergyman killing his superior — and why a woman convicted of treason was burned at the stake instead of being quartered; in a society where every rank owed faith to the rank above it, breaking that bond was the worst thing anyone could do.","Finish by answering the question word for word: treason ranked above every other crime because it struck at the person on whom all other authority rested, and because both the statute that defined it and the scaffold that punished it were designed to say exactly that."],"cues":["Which level does that paragraph meet, and which words in the descriptor say so?","What did the Treason Act of 1351 actually define, and why does that make treason different in kind?","What was done to a traitor, step by step, and who was meant to be watching?","Which point of your own lifts this answer past the stimulus cap?","What has the last sentence still got to do?"]},{"title":"Using the factor 'attitudes in society' as precise evidence: why poaching was tolerated","setup":"Paper 1 Question 4, worth 12 marks: 'Explain why poaching was regarded as a social crime in England in the years c1000–c1500.' You may use the following in your answer: • royal forests • attitudes in local communities. You must also use information of your own. The answer needs three explained reasons and at least one of them from outside the stimulus points.","steps":["Start with the definition itself, so the reasoning has something to act on: a social crime is an act the law forbids but ordinary people do not think wrong, so the question is not why villagers poached but why their neighbours refused to treat poaching as a crime at all.","Give the first reason: the law had closed ground that had been open. William I's Forest Laws took large areas into royal forest and his successors extended them — estimates differ, but at their height in the twelfth and thirteenth centuries something like a third of the land area of southern England lay under forest law — and hunting deer, cutting timber or grazing animals there became offences against the Crown, so people were being punished on the same ground for what they had always done.","Give the second reason: the offence had no victim the village could see. Forest law divided offences into trespass against the venison and against the vert, the deer and the woodland that fed them, so the injured interest was the king's hunting while the offender was a neighbour feeding his family — which is why villagers looked the other way and juries of local men were reluctant to convict a man who was not a persistent offender.","Give the third reason, which is the one beyond the stimulus: enforcement rested on the very people who disagreed with the law. There was no police force and no public prosecutor, so a case began only when someone raised the hue and cry or presented a suspect to the court, which means a crime the community would not report was a crime the Crown could not punish.","Close on the combination: poaching stayed a social crime because the law protected an interest the village did not share, because the punishment fell on people it thought blameless — resentment strong enough that the Charter of the Forest of 1217 repealed death and mutilation for taking deer, leaving fines and imprisonment — and because the machinery of enforcement belonged to the community and not to the king."],"cues":["What makes a crime a 'social' crime, and whose opinion decides it?","What did the Forest Laws take away from the people who lived in those areas?","Whose interest was harmed by poaching, and who was the offender?","Which reason is not prompted by either stimulus point?","How do the three reasons fit into one line of argument?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Why did a medieval village have a strong reason to join the hue and cry?","a":"Because the whole village could be fined if it failed to join the chase, so catching the offender was cheaper than ignoring him."},{"q":"What changed about the purpose of a fine when the Normans abolished wergild?","a":"Money stopped going to the victim or the victim's family as compensation and went to the king instead, so crime became an offence against the king's peace rather than a private injury."},{"q":"Why is poaching described as a 'social crime'?","a":"Because it was against the law but ordinary people did not think it wrong, so neighbours would not report it and juries were reluctant to convict."},{"q":"Why did trial by ordeal stop working once priests were forbidden to take part?","a":"Because the whole point of the ordeal was that a priest blessed it and read the outcome as God's verdict; without him it proved nothing."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 1 Section B, the thematic study, is worth 36 of the paper's 52 marks: Question 3 is 4 marks, Question 4 is 12 marks, and Question 5 or Question 6 is 16 marks plus up to 4 additional marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology.","Question 3, 'Explain one way in which … was similar to / different from …', carries 2 marks for AO2 and 2 for AO1, and normally spans at least a century, so both named periods must be evidenced.","Question 4 stimulus caps: answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 8 marks and have no access to Level 4; no cap is printed at Level 2.","Question 5 or 6 stimulus caps: answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 11 marks and have no access to Level 4; no cap is printed at Level 2. Question 5 or 6 may cover a complete period or at least 200 years.","Timing on the 80-minute Paper 1 is roughly a minute a mark: about 5 minutes on Question 3, 15 on Question 4 and 25 on Question 5 or 6, which is the only Section B answer where spelling and specialist terms are separately marked as Threshold 1, Intermediate 2–3 and High 4."],"questions":[{"q":"Explain one way in which law enforcement in the years c1000–c1500 was different from law enforcement in the years c1700–c1900. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — unpaid communal obligation against paid professional policing — supported by specific detail from both periods, such as tithings and the hue and cry against the Metropolitan Police Act of 1829.","sol":"The main difference is who was responsible for catching a criminal. In c1000–c1500 it was the whole community: every male over twelve belonged to a tithing of ten men who answered for one another, anyone who heard the hue and cry had to join the chase or the village was fined, and the parish constable who led it was an unpaid local man serving for a year. In c1700–c1900 responsibility passed to paid professionals: the Metropolitan Police Act of 1829 created a uniformed force for London, and the County and Borough Police Act of 1856 made a force compulsory everywhere. A shared obligation of neighbours had become salaried, specialist work.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask who was actually expected to run after a thief in each period.","Both halves need named detail: one medieval obligation with its penalty, and one named Act from the later period.","State the difference, prove the medieval side, prove the later side, then say why the two systems are different in kind and not merely in size."],"codes":["10-1 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities","10 — The process of change: crime and punishment and the key features of society"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the role of local communities in law enforcement in the years c1000–c1500 was similar to their role in the years c1500–c1700. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed similarity — unpaid local men still did the policing in both periods — supported by detail such as the parish constable and the hue and cry against the Elizabethan and Stuart constable and watch.","sol":"One clear similarity is that policing was still done by unpaid local men. In c1000–c1500 the parish constable was an ordinary villager appointed for a year, the hue and cry obliged everybody within earshot to join the chase, and the Statute of Winchester of 1285 required towns to keep a night watch of townsmen serving in turn. In c1500–c1700 nothing essential had changed: the constable was still an unpaid amateur chosen locally for a year, watchmen still patrolled at night, and there was still no paid force of any kind. The similarity shows a continuity of over six hundred years in the machinery of arrest.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Look for the part of the system that had still not been replaced by 1700.","Name the officials in each period, and say how they were chosen and paid.","State the similarity, evidence each period with a named office or statute, and finish by saying what that long continuity proves."],"codes":["10-1 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities","10 — The process of change: the key factors"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the aims of punishment in the years c1000–c1500 were different from the aims of punishment in the years c1900–present. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — deterrence and retribution through public physical punishment against rehabilitation through supervision and imprisonment — supported by detail such as the stocks, mutilation and hanging against probation from 1907 and the ending of the death penalty.","sol":"The difference lies in what punishment was trying to achieve. In c1000–c1500 the aims were deterrence and retribution, so punishment was public and physical: offenders were put in the stocks or the pillory to be humiliated, whipped or mutilated, hanged for serious theft and murder, and hanged, drawn and quartered for treason. Nobody tried to reform the offender. In c1900–present the dominant aim has been rehabilitation: the Probation Service was created in 1907, borstals for young offenders in 1908, community sentences and education in prison are now normal, and the death penalty for murder was suspended in 1965 and abolished in 1969. Punishment moved from making an example of the criminal to changing him.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what the medieval crowd was meant to learn from a punishment, and what a modern sentence is meant to do to the offender.","Two or three named punishments from each period will support the comparison.","Name the change of aim, evidence both sides, then explain why reform and deterrence are different purposes rather than different degrees of severity."],"codes":["10-1 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The emphasis on deterrence and retribution","10 — The process of change: crime and punishment and the key features of society"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the punishment of treason in the years c1000–c1500 was similar to the punishment of treason in the years c1500–c1700. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed similarity — treason remained the worst crime, punished by public hanging, drawing and quartering to deter and to express retribution — supported by detail from both periods, such as the Treason Act of 1351 and the execution of the Gunpowder Plotters in 1606.","sol":"In both periods treason was the worst of all crimes and drew the most public and most painful death available. In c1000–c1500 the Treason Act of 1351 defined high treason as plotting the king's death, levying war against him or forging his coin, and traitors were hanged, drawn and quartered: John Ball, one of the leaders of the Peasants' Revolt, died that way in 1381. In c1500–c1700 the same punishment was used on the Gunpowder Plotters, executed in London in January 1606 for conspiring to blow up King James I and Parliament. Attitudes to authority had not shifted: an attack on the monarch still had to be punished terribly, and before a crowd.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about which crime both periods regarded as the very worst, and why a government would choose a spectacular death for it.","Name a statute or a case from each period rather than describing the punishment twice.","State the similarity, evidence each period, and close by explaining what the shared punishment says about attitudes to royal authority."],"codes":["10-1 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Crimes against the person, property and authority","10-1 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The emphasis on deterrence and retribution"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the influence of the Church on crime and punishment in the years c1000–c1500 was different from its influence in the years c1500–c1700. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — the Church as an independent shelter from royal justice against a Church subordinated to the state — supported by detail such as sanctuary and benefit of clergy against their restriction after the Reformation.","sol":"The medieval Church could shelter offenders from the king's justice; after the Reformation it could not. In c1000–c1500 a fugitive who reached a church could claim sanctuary: forty days to stand trial or to abjure the realm before the coroner. Benefit of clergy sent anyone who counted as clergy to Church courts that could not impose death — a loophole so wide that a statute of 1489 limited laymen to one claim and branded them on the thumb. In c1500–c1700 that independence was stripped away: Henry VIII cut sanctuary back in the 1530s, it was abolished for criminals in 1623, and benefit of clergy was narrowed. The Church had gone from rival jurisdiction to servant of the state.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what a medieval fugitive could do that a Stuart fugitive could not.","Sanctuary and benefit of clergy both need a detail attached: the forty days, the coroner's oath, the Church court that could not hang.","State the difference, evidence the medieval privileges, show what happened to them after the Reformation, then explain what that says about the balance of power between Church and Crown."],"codes":["10-1 — 3 Case study: The influence of the Church on crime and punishment in the early thirteenth century","10 — The process of change: the key factors"]},{"q":"Explain why the Norman Conquest changed the definition of crime in England. [12 marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Forest Laws\n• the murdrum fine\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit explained reasons — the Conquest made the kingdom the king's property, protected a small ruling minority, and redefined offences as breaches of the king's peace. Answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 8 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"The Norman Conquest changed what counted as a crime because a conqueror had to make resistance, and the use of his new property, illegal.\n\nThe first reason is that William treated England as his own possession. His Forest Laws took vast areas into royal forest — the New Forest in Hampshire was created in about 1079, and villages were cleared to make it — and inside them hunting deer, taking timber and grazing animals without a licence became offences against the Crown, punished by heavy fines and mutilation; dogs kept in the forest even had to be lamed so they could not chase game. Taking a deer had been nobody's crime before 1066; a generation later it was a serious one, and nothing about the act itself had changed. This is the clearest possible example of crime being defined by whoever holds power.\n\nThe second reason is that the Normans were a tiny minority ruling a hostile population. The murdrum fine required the whole hundred to pay if a Norman was found murdered and the killer was not produced. Collective punishment turned every English community into the guarantor of Norman safety, and made the killing of a conqueror a different and heavier offence than the killing of an Englishman.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond the stimulus and is the deepest of the three: the Conquest moved the injured party from the victim to the king. Anglo-Saxon law had priced killing and wounding in wergild paid to the victim's family; William abolished it, and fines were paid to the Crown instead, so an offence became a breach of the king's peace. Separate Church courts were set up from about 1072–76 to try clergy and moral offences, trial by combat was introduced, and cases were pleaded in Norman French.\n\nThe three reasons are one reason at bottom. Defining new crimes — against the king's forest, against the king's men and against the king's peace — was how a small conquering elite held down a country it had taken by force.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what a conqueror needs the law to do for him in the first twenty years after an invasion.","Each reason must run as a chain: the Normans' situation, the law they made, and the new crime it created.","Take the Forest Laws first, the murdrum fine second, and then find a change of your own big enough to escape the stimulus cap — look at who received the fine after 1066."],"codes":["10-1 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Changing definitions of crime as a result of the Norman Conquest","10-1 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The emphasis on deterrence and retribution","10 — The process of change: crime and punishment and the key features of society"]},{"q":"Explain why trial by ordeal ended in England in the early thirteenth century. [12 marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Fourth Lateran Council\n• trial by jury\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit explained reasons — the Church withdrew its priests in 1215, juries provided a ready substitute, and changing attitudes plus royal ambition made a court-based alternative preferable. Answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 8 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"Trial by ordeal was a religious ceremony, so it ended when the Church stopped supplying the religion and the Crown had something better to put in its place.\n\nThe first reason is the Church's withdrawal. A suspect who could not clear himself by oath-helping carried a red-hot iron three paces, plunged an arm into boiling water, was lowered bound into cold water, or, if a priest, swallowed blessed bread; in every case a priest blessed the ordeal and the result was read as God's judgement. The Fourth Lateran Council of 1215 forbade clergy to take part. Without the priest the ordeal was simply a burn or a dunking that proved nothing, and in 1219 England's judges were instructed to find other means of trying suspects.\n\nThe second reason is that a substitute already existed. Royal justice had been expanding for half a century: the Assize of Clarendon of 1166 required local men to present suspects to the king's travelling judges, so juries of neighbours were already part of the system. Replacing God's verdict with the verdict of twelve men who knew the accused and the district was therefore a small practical step, not a leap into the unknown, which is why trial by jury spread so quickly after 1215.\n\nThe third reason lies beyond the stimulus, in attitudes and in royal interest. Educated churchmen and lawyers, trained in the growing study of canon and common law, had come to doubt that God could be compelled to deliver a verdict on demand, and increasingly regarded the ordeal as superstition rather than proof. Kings, for their part, gained from every case that came into their own courts, because royal justice brought both authority over the localities and the income from fines, and a court whose verdicts depended on a priest was a court the Crown did not fully control.\n\nThe three fit together. Doctrine removed the ordeal, royal machinery stood ready to replace it, and changing attitudes among the educated meant the replacement was welcomed as an improvement rather than mourned as a loss.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what made an ordeal convincing in the first place, and then what removed it.","An ending needs both a push and a pull: something that stopped the old method, and something ready to take its place.","Deal with the Church's decision first, the jury second, and then add a reason of your own about why kings and educated men preferred courts — that is the point that escapes the stimulus cap."],"codes":["10-1 — 3 Case study: The influence of the Church on crime and punishment in the early thirteenth century","10 — The process of change: factors working together","10 — The process of change: the role of the strand 3 case studies"]},{"q":"Explain why the Church had so much influence over crime and punishment in England in the years c1000–c1500. [12 marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• sanctuary\n• benefit of clergy\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit explained reasons — the Church owned the proof-system, claimed jurisdiction over its own members and over moral offences, and faced no rival institution with comparable reach or authority. Answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 8 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"The Church's power over justice rested on three things: it could shelter offenders, it could claim them for its own courts, and until 1215 it supplied the proof on which verdicts rested.\n\nThe first reason is sanctuary. A fugitive who reached a church could claim its protection, and then had forty days either to stand trial or to confess before the coroner and 'abjure the realm', swearing to leave the country from a named port. That was possible only because everyone accepted that consecrated ground belonged to God and that the king's officers had no right to drag a man off it. The Church could therefore suspend royal justice simply by opening a door.\n\nThe second reason is benefit of clergy. Since separate Church courts were established from about 1072–76, clergy were tried in them, and those courts could not impose death. Over time the test for clerical status slid into the ability to read the 'neck verse' from Psalm 51, so that literate laymen escaped hanging too — a loophole so wide that a statute of 1489 limited laymen to a single claim and had them branded on the thumb so that it could not be used twice.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond the stimulus: the Church defined what was proved and what was sinful. Trial by ordeal was a priest's ritual, and its verdict was accepted as God's, so until the Fourth Lateran Council of 1215 the Church decided guilt in the hardest cases. Church courts also tried moral offences such as adultery, blasphemy, drunkenness and failure to pay tithes, which no royal court touched.\n\nUnderlying all three is the absence of a rival. Medieval government was small and travelled slowly, while the Church had a building, a priest and a court in every parish, most of the literate men in the kingdom, and the ultimate sanction of hell. Where an institution supplies the proof, the mercy and the moral code, its influence over crime and punishment is not surprising but inevitable.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the Church could do that the king's officers could not.","Each privilege needs its mechanism spelled out — the forty days, the coroner's oath, the court that could not hang.","Handle sanctuary and benefit of clergy first, then add the Church's control of proof and of moral offences, which is the material that lifts the answer past the stimulus cap."],"codes":["10-1 — 3 Case study: The influence of the Church on crime and punishment in the early thirteenth century","10 — The process of change: the key factors","10 — The process of change: crime and punishment and the key features of society"]},{"q":"Explain why new crimes were created in England in the years c1300–c1500. [12 marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Statute of Labourers\n• heresy\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit explained reasons — an economic shock met by landowner legislation, a religious challenge met by the heresy statute of 1401, and political fear of rebellion — with the enforcement machinery that made new laws workable. Answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 8 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"New crimes appeared in the later Middle Ages whenever the people with power felt threatened and had a government able to legislate.\n\nThe first threat was economic. The Black Death of 1348–49 killed a very large part of the population, labourers became scarce, and survivors could demand higher wages or move to a village that paid more. Landowners, who dominated Parliament, responded with the Ordinance of Labourers in 1349 and the Statute of Labourers in 1351, which fixed wages at their pre-plague levels and made it an offence both to ask for more and to leave your village for better pay. Nothing about asking for a wage had changed; what had changed was that it now cost the ruling class money, so it was made criminal.\n\nThe second threat was religious. John Wycliffe, who died in 1384, and his followers the Lollards attacked Church teaching and wanted the Bible in English, which threatened the authority of the institution that underpinned the whole social order. Parliament responded in 1401 with the statute usually known as De heretico comburendo, which allowed convicted heretics to be burned at the stake, and in 1414 royal officials were required to help hunt them. Holding an opinion had become a capital crime.\n\nThe third reason lies beyond the stimulus: fear of rebellion. The Treason Act of 1351 had already defined high treason precisely — plotting the king's death, levying war against him, forging his coin — and after the Peasants' Revolt of 1381, when rebels marched on London against the poll tax and killed royal officials, challenges to the social order were punished ferociously; John Ball was hanged, drawn and quartered that year.\n\nThese laws mattered because there was at last machinery to enforce them. Justices of the Peace, established by the Act of 1361, were local landowners appointed by the Crown who met regularly to try offenders, and they enforced the labour laws in particular. New crimes were created by an anxious governing class, and stuck because there were now men in every county with the authority to prosecute them.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask who was frightened, and of what, in the century after the Black Death.","Each new crime needs the threat behind it explained, not just the law named and dated.","Take the labour laws, then heresy, then add a political reason of your own — and finish with the officials who made enforcement possible."],"codes":["10-1 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Crimes against the person, property and authority","10 — The process of change: factors working together","10 — The process of change: crime and punishment and the key features of society"]},{"q":"Explain why the role of local communities in law enforcement remained so important in England in the years c1000–c1500. [12 marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• tithings\n• the hue and cry\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit explained reasons — no alternative machinery existed, collective responsibility made the system self-enforcing, and the Crown deliberately built its new offices on top of local obligation. Answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 8 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"Local communities stayed at the centre of law enforcement for five hundred years because nothing else could have done the job, and because the Crown chose to build on them rather than replace them.\n\nThe first reason is collective responsibility, which made policing self-enforcing. Every male over the age of twelve belonged to a tithing of ten men who stood surety for one another: if one member absconded, the other nine had to produce him or pay a fine. That gave every man a direct financial motive to watch his neighbours — exactly what a government with no officials could not otherwise buy.\n\nThe second reason is that the same principle turned the whole village into a manhunt. Anyone who heard the hue and cry was obliged to stop work and join the chase, and the settlement could be fined if it failed to. In a community where everybody knew everybody this was effective: most offenders were local, they were recognised, and there was nowhere nearby to hide.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond the stimulus. The Crown's own developments were designed to sit on top of the community system, not to supplant it. The Assize of Clarendon of 1166 required groups of local men to present suspects to the king's travelling judges; coroners, created in 1194, investigated suspicious deaths with juries of local men; the Statute of Winchester of 1285 ordered towns to keep a night watch and every man to keep weapons; and the Justices of the Peace established in 1361 were local landowners, not officials sent from the capital. Every one of these reforms recruited the community rather than replacing it.\n\nBehind all of this lay the key features of the society. There was no police force, no forensic science and no way of identifying a stranger, so evidence meant witnesses and reputation, and both were local. People also expected to answer for their neighbours' conduct. Community enforcement survived because it was the only method that fitted a society of small settlements, weak central government and no technology of detection.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what a medieval king actually had at his disposal if he wanted a thief arrested in a distant village.","Turn each stimulus point into a mechanism with a penalty attached, so the reasoning explains rather than describes.","Cover tithings and the hue and cry, then add the royal offices created between 1166 and 1361 and show that they too depended on local men."],"codes":["10-1 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities","10 — The process of change: crime and punishment and the key features of society","10 — The process of change: the key factors"]},{"q":"'The Norman Conquest was the most important turning point in crime and punishment in England in the years c1000–c1500.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Forest Laws\n• wergild\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit a criteria-based judgement weighing Norman changes to the definition of crime and the principle of punishment against the survival of Anglo-Saxon enforcement and against later turning points such as 1215 and 1351. Answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 11 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"A turning point has to be measured rather than asserted. The test here is whether the Conquest changed the definition of crime, the way law was enforced and the purpose of punishment, and whether what it changed lasted.\n\nOn the definition of crime the case for the statement is very strong. William I's Forest Laws took vast areas of England into royal forest — the New Forest in Hampshire was created in about 1079, with villages cleared to make room for it — and made hunting deer, taking timber and grazing animals there offences against the Crown, with dogs inside the forest lamed so that they could not chase game. An act that had been lawful in 1065 was a serious crime a generation later, without anything about the act itself changing. The murdrum fine, which made a whole hundred pay if a Norman was found murdered and no killer was produced, did the same job for the protection of the conquerors.\n\nOn the purpose of punishment the case is stronger still, because the Conquest changed a principle. Anglo-Saxon law had priced killing and injury: the wergild was compensation paid by the offender to the victim or the victim's family, scaled to the victim's rank, with separate tariffs for the loss of an eye, a hand or a thumb. The Normans abolished it and had fines paid to the king instead, and used execution and mutilation more freely. That is the deepest Norman change of all: a crime stopped being an injury to a family that could be bought off and became an offence against the king's peace.\n\nAgainst the statement stands everything the Conquest left alone. Tithings, the hue and cry, oath-helping and the manor, hundred and shire courts all survived, because William had no machinery of his own to replace them and they worked. Theft remained what it had always been and remained the commonest offence. If law enforcement is the measure, 1066 changed almost nothing.\n\nThere are also later turning points the statement ignores. The Fourth Lateran Council of 1215 forbade priests to take part in trial by ordeal, and by 1219 English judges were told to use other means, so guilt passed from God to a jury of local men — a deeper change in how justice worked than anything the Normans did. The Black Death of 1348–49 produced the Statute of Labourers of 1351, which made asking for higher wages or moving village a crime, creating a whole category of economic offence; the heresy statute of 1401 made belief itself capital; and the Justices of the Peace established in 1361 gave the Crown permanent officials in every county.\n\nThe judgement depends on which measure is applied. Judged by the definition of crime and the principle behind punishment, the Conquest is the sharpest single break in the period, because it moved the law's centre of gravity from the injured family to the Crown and everything afterwards was built on that. Judged by how justice actually operated day to day, it barely registers, and 1215 and 1351 did more. The Conquest is therefore the most important turning point in what the law was for, but not in how the law was enforced.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide what would have to be true for something to count as a turning point before you start collecting evidence.","Split the theme into its three parts — definitions of crime, enforcement, punishment — and test the Conquest against each one separately.","Argue the Norman case on crime and punishment, then show how much of the Anglo-Saxon system survived, then bring in a later change big enough to rival 1066."],"codes":["10-1 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Changing definitions of crime as a result of the Norman Conquest","10-1 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The emphasis on deterrence and retribution","10 — The process of change: the key factors"]},{"q":"'Punishment in England in the years c1000–c1500 was mainly intended to deter people from committing crime.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the stocks and the pillory\n• public execution\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit a criteria-based judgement testing deterrence against retribution, compensation under wergild, revenue from fines and the mercy offered by the Church. Answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 11 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"Deterrence means punishing one offender in order to frighten everybody else. It is only one of the purposes medieval punishment served, and it does not explain the earliest part of the period at all, so the statement needs qualifying rather than accepting.\n\nThe case for deterrence is strong for most of the period, and the clearest evidence is that punishment was staged. Minor offenders were locked in the stocks or the pillory in the market place, where the point was not the discomfort but the audience: neighbours jeered, threw refuse and remembered. Executions were public for the same reason, and the most serious crime of all carried the most theatrical death — traitors under the Treason Act of 1351 were hanged, drawn and quartered, as John Ball was in 1381 after the Peasants' Revolt. Mutilation left a permanent advertisement: a hand taken for theft, blinding for offences under the Forest Laws. None of this makes sense unless the audience was the target.\n\nRetribution, however, runs alongside deterrence throughout, and often explains the details better. The punishment was frequently matched to the crime so that it looked like payment rather than prevention: a thief lost the hand that stole, a woman convicted of scolding was set in the cucking stool, the shaming chair recorded in England as early as Domesday Book, for her neighbours to jeer at. Hanging a murderer satisfied a demand that wrongdoing be repaid in kind, whether or not anybody else was watching.\n\nThe statement is weakest at the start of the period, where punishment was chiefly compensation. Anglo-Saxon wergild required an offender to pay the victim or the victim's family, at a rate fixed by the victim's rank, with a separate tariff for an eye, a hand or a thumb. The purpose was to settle the quarrel and prevent a feud, not to frighten onlookers. It is only when the Normans abolished wergild and redirected fines to the king that punishment loses its compensatory character — and even then, fines remained a useful source of royal income, so a third purpose, revenue, sits quietly underneath the whole system.\n\nThere is also a large and deliberate exception: the Church built mercy into the system. Sanctuary allowed a fugitive forty days and then a lawful escape by abjuring the realm before the coroner, and benefit of clergy sent offenders to Church courts that could not impose death, a loophole so wide that a statute of 1489 limited laymen to one claim and branded them on the thumb. A system organised purely around deterrence would not have tolerated either.\n\nThe judgement turns on the period and on the offence. From the Conquest onwards, and above all for crimes against property and against the Crown, deterrence is the best single explanation of why punishment was public, physical and disproportionate to the harm actually done. Before 1066, and in the Church's jurisdiction throughout, it is not: wergild aimed to compensate and to end feuds, and sanctuary and benefit of clergy aimed at mercy. The statement is therefore true of the later medieval period and of the king's courts, but it cannot be extended across the whole of c1000–c1500 without ignoring how the period began and what the Church continued to do.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask who a punishment was aimed at: the offender, the victim, or the crowd watching.","Deterrence, retribution, compensation and revenue are four different purposes; sort your evidence into them before you write.","Argue the deterrence case from public punishments, then test it against wergild at the start of the period and against the Church's mercy throughout."],"codes":["10-1 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The emphasis on deterrence and retribution","10-1 — 3 Case study: The influence of the Church on crime and punishment in the early thirteenth century","10 — The process of change: the key factors"]},{"q":"'There was little change in the way the law was enforced in England in the years c1000–c1500.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the hue and cry\n• Justices of the Peace\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit a criteria-based judgement separating continuity at village level — tithings, hue and cry, unpaid constables — from the growth of royal machinery: Assize of Clarendon 1166, coroners 1194, juries after 1215, Statute of Winchester 1285, JPs 1361. Answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 11 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"Law enforcement has a bottom and a top, and in this period they move at different speeds. The statement is close to true of the bottom and plainly false of the top, so the answer has to hold the two apart.\n\nThe continuity at village level is genuine and impressive. In about 1050 a victim raised the hue and cry and every man within earshot was obliged to join the chase, with the village fined if it did not; in 1500 exactly the same duty applied. Every male over twelve belonged to a tithing of ten men who had to produce an absconding member or pay a fine, and although the tithing weakened over time the principle of collective responsibility remained. Arrests were made by the parish constable, an unpaid local man serving for a year, and there was no police force, no paid investigator and no means of identifying a stranger in 1500 any more than in 1000. Judged by who chased the thief, five centuries changed very little.\n\nThe top of the system, however, was transformed, and the changes were royal. Henry II's Assize of Clarendon in 1166 required local men to present suspects to travelling royal judges and provided for gaols to hold them, pulling serious cases into the king's courts. Coroners were created in 1194 to investigate suspicious deaths on the Crown's behalf. The Statute of Winchester of 1285 ordered towns to keep a night watch and required men to keep weapons, which put a national standard on a local duty for the first time. Finally the Act of 1361 established Justices of the Peace, local landowners commissioned by the Crown who met regularly to try offenders and who enforced laws such as the Statute of Labourers of 1351. By 1500 there was a chain of royal authority running from the king to the county that had not existed in 1000.\n\nThe method of proof changed even more fundamentally, and this is the point the stimulus does not prompt. Until the early thirteenth century guilt could be settled by oath-helping or, when that failed, by trial by ordeal, a priest's ritual whose verdict was accepted as God's. After the Fourth Lateran Council of 1215 withdrew the clergy, and English judges were told in 1219 to find other means, guilt was decided by juries of local men. Justice had shifted from divine judgement to human evidence, which is the largest single change in the whole period.\n\nThe judgement depends on the criterion. Measured by the people who actually did the catching, the statement is right: enforcement remained unpaid, communal and local from beginning to end, because the society had no alternative to offer. Measured by who controlled and directed justice, and by how guilt was established, it is wrong: the Crown steadily took ownership of serious crime and God was replaced by a jury. Change in this period happened above the village, not in it.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["A system can be unchanged at one level and transformed at another; decide which level the statement is really about.","Line up the continuities first — who chased, who arrested, who paid them — then line up the dated royal reforms against them.","Use the hue and cry for continuity and the Justices of the Peace for change, then add the end of trial by ordeal as the change neither stimulus point prompts."],"codes":["10-1 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities","10-1 — 3 Case study: The influence of the Church on crime and punishment in the early thirteenth century","10 — The process of change: factors working together"]},{"q":"'The law in England in the years c1000–c1500 did more to protect property than to protect the person.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• grand larceny\n• the murdrum fine\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit a criteria-based judgement built on the penalty the law attached to each kind of offence: hanging for grand larceny and blinding or mutilation under the Forest Laws against amercements of a few pence for drawing blood in the manor courts, weighed against homicide as a felony, the murdrum fine, and the harsher penalties kept for crimes against authority. Answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 11 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"A legal system shows its priorities in its penalties, so the test is simple: what did it cost to take a man's goods, and what did it cost to hurt a man? Judged that way the statement holds for ordinary people, but it misses the interest the law guarded most fiercely of all.\n\nTheft was punished out of all proportion to its value. Grand larceny — the theft of goods worth more than twelve pence, the line between grand and petty larceny first drawn in statute in Edward I's reign — was a felony, and a convicted felon hanged, while below that line petty larceny was not capital and brought whipping, the stocks or the pillory. A sheep, a bolt of cloth or a handful of coins could therefore cost a life. The Forest Laws went further and protected property that was nobody's but the king's: after 1066 hunting deer, cutting timber or grazing animals in the royal forest became offences against the Crown, punished under the Norman kings by heavy fines, blinding and mutilation. The resentment was strong enough that the Charter of the Forest of 1217 ended the loss of life or limb for taking venison, leaving a fine or a year and a day in prison.\n\nViolence, by contrast, was often cheap. Anglo-Saxon law had priced injury and death in wergild, compensation paid to the victim's family and rising with the victim's rank; the Normans abolished it and took the money themselves. Below the level of felony, assault stayed in the manor and hundred courts, where drawing blood was settled by an amercement of a few pence — the same order of sum as the twelve pence that divided a whipping from the gallows.\n\nThe comparison is not one-sided. Homicide was a felony throughout and a convicted murderer hanged, and the murdrum fine shows the law protecting the person with real ferocity when it chose: a whole hundred paid for the killing of a Norman unless it could present Englishry and prove the dead man English, a rule not abolished until 1340. What that rule protected, though, was a particular kind of person — the conquerors — and not the person as such.\n\nThe third aspect settles the judgement and lies outside both stimulus points. Neither goods nor bodies stood at the top of the hierarchy: authority did. Treason under the Treason Act of 1351 — plotting the king's death, levying war against him, forging his coin — was punished by drawing to the scaffold, hanging, disembowelling and quartering, as Dafydd ap Gruffydd was at Shrewsbury in 1283, and from 1401 a convicted heretic could be burned.\n\nSo the statement is right on a narrow reading and wrong as a principle. Measured by penalty, the law did protect goods more energetically than it protected an ordinary person's body, because the men who made law owned property and feared thieves. But the murdrum fine and the scaffold show what the rule really was: the law protected whatever the powerful needed protected — their goods, their own people and, above all, their authority — and the safety of the ordinary person came third.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Before you write anything, decide how 'protect' can be measured: the obvious test is the penalty the law attached to each kind of offence.","Put an offence against property and an offence against the person side by side at the same date and compare what each cost the offender.","Start with the twelve-pence line that separated grand larceny from petty larceny, then ask what a manor court actually did about a man who drew blood."],"codes":["10-1 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Crimes against the person, property and authority","10-1 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Changing definitions of crime as a result of the Norman Conquest","10 — The process of change: crime and punishment and the key features of society"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In what year did the Fourth Lateran Council forbid priests to take part in trial by ordeal?","a":"1215."},{"q":"What was wergild, and who received the payment?","a":"Compensation paid by an offender in Anglo-Saxon England, fixed according to the victim's rank, and paid to the victim or the victim's family."},{"q":"Which law of 1351 made it a crime to demand or to pay wages higher than those paid before the Black Death?","a":"The Statute of Labourers."},{"q":"Which animal was it an offence to hunt in the royal forests under William I's Forest Laws?","a":"Deer (accept boar)"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Tithing","back":"A group of ten men, all males over twelve being required to belong to one. Each was responsible for the others' behaviour: if one committed a crime, the rest had to bring him to court or pay a fine.","code":"10-1 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities","open":false},{"front":"The hue and cry","back":"The alarm raised by a victim or witness. Everyone who heard it had to stop work and join the chase after the suspect, and the whole village could be fined if it failed to.","code":"10-1 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities","open":false},{"front":"The parish constable","back":"An ordinary local man appointed for a year, unpaid, who led the hue and cry, arrested suspects and took them before the court. He was not a policeman and had no training.","code":"10-1 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities","open":false},{"front":"Coroners, 1194","back":"Officials created in 1194 to investigate suspicious or sudden deaths on the Crown's behalf, working with juries of local men. They also heard the oath of anyone abjuring the realm from sanctuary.","code":"10-1 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities","open":false},{"front":"The Statute of Winchester, 1285","back":"Edward I's law requiring towns to keep a night watch, confirming the hue and cry, and obliging men to keep weapons to help catch criminals. It put a national standard on local duties.","code":"10-1 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities","open":false},{"front":"Justices of the Peace, 1361","back":"Established by an Act of 1361 under Edward III. Local landowners commissioned by the Crown who met regularly to try offenders and enforce laws such as the Statute of Labourers.","code":"10-1 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities","open":false},{"front":"Wergild","back":"The Anglo-Saxon 'man price': compensation paid by an offender to the victim or the victim's family, fixed by the victim's rank, with separate rates for injuries. The Normans abolished it and paid fines to the king instead.","code":"10-1 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The emphasis on deterrence and retribution","open":false},{"front":"Deterrence and retribution","back":"The two aims of medieval punishment: to frighten others out of offending, and to repay the offender for the harm done. Both explain why punishment was public, physical and often matched to the crime.","code":"10-1 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The emphasis on deterrence and retribution","open":false},{"front":"The Forest Laws","back":"William I's laws over the royal forests, such as the New Forest created in about 1079. Hunting deer, taking timber and grazing animals became crimes; dogs kept inside the forest were lamed.","code":"10-1 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Changing definitions of crime as a result of the Norman Conquest","open":false},{"front":"The murdrum fine","back":"A Norman rule: if a Norman was found murdered and the killer was not produced, the whole hundred where the body lay had to pay a heavy fine. Collective punishment protecting the conquerors.","code":"10-1 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Changing definitions of crime as a result of the Norman Conquest","open":false},{"front":"Poaching as a 'social crime'","back":"Taking game from land held by the king or a lord was illegal, but ordinary people did not think it wrong. Neighbours would not report poachers and juries were reluctant to convict them.","code":"10-1 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Crimes against the person, property and authority","open":false},{"front":"The Statute of Labourers, 1351","back":"Passed after the Black Death of 1348–49 had made labour scarce. It fixed wages at their pre-plague levels and made it an offence to ask for more or to leave your village for better pay.","code":"10-1 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Crimes against the person, property and authority","open":false},{"front":"The heresy law of 1401","back":"The statute known as De heretico comburendo allowed convicted heretics to be burned at the stake. It was aimed at the Lollards, the followers of John Wycliffe, who died in 1384.","code":"10-1 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Crimes against the person, property and authority","open":false},{"front":"Trial by ordeal and its ending","back":"A priest's ritual judging a suspect by God: hot iron, hot or cold water, or blessed bread. The Fourth Lateran Council of 1215 forbade priests to take part, and England's judges were told in 1219 to use other means.","code":"10-1 — 3 Case study: The influence of the Church on crime and punishment in the early thirteenth century","open":false},{"front":"Sanctuary and benefit of clergy","back":"Church protections. A fugitive in a church had forty days to stand trial or swear before the coroner to leave the realm. Clergy, or anyone who could read the 'neck verse', were tried in Church courts, which could not hang.","code":"10-1 — 3 Case study: The influence of the Church on crime and punishment in the early thirteenth century","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 1 Question 5, 16 marks: 'Punishments in England became less brutal in the years c1000–c1500.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • mutilation • the Charter of the Forest. You must also use information of your own.)","items":["The strongest ground for agreeing is the retreat of mutilation from the offences that touched most people, and it has to be stated precisely before it can be tested. Under the Norman kings the Forest Laws were enforced with heavy fines, blinding and mutilation for taking the king's deer, and dogs kept inside the forest had to be lamed so that they could not chase game. The resentment this caused was one of the grievances behind the Charter of the Forest of 1217, which ended the loss of life or limb for offences against the venison and left a heavy fine, or a year and a day in prison for a man who could not pay, in its place. Against this the evidence of hardening is stronger than it first looks. Hanging remained the ordinary penalty for felony throughout the period, and grand larceny still took a man to the gallows for goods worth more than twelve pence; hanging, drawing and quartering was in use in the same century as the Charter — Dafydd ap Gruffydd was put to death that way at Shrewsbury in 1283 — and from 1401 a convicted heretic could be burned. The criterion decides the answer. If brutality is measured by what happened to an ordinary offender against the king's game, it fell; if it is measured by the worst the law was prepared to do, it rose, because the savagery moved from the king's deer to the king's authority and the Church's doctrine. On the second criterion, which is the one the question invites, the statement is false for the period as a whole.","Why this reaches Level 4: the explanation is directed consistently at the conceptual focus — every sentence tests brutality rather than describing punishments; the information is precisely selected (the lamed dogs, 1217, the year and a day, twelve pence, Shrewsbury in 1283, 1401) rather than general; the case for agreeing is stated at its strongest and then answered instead of being left hanging; the point that lies beyond both stimulus points — the hanging, drawing and quartering of Dafydd ap Gruffydd at Shrewsbury in 1283 and the burning of heretics from 1401 — is the one that turns the answer against the statement rather than merely illustrating it; and the criterion for the judgement is named and applied in the closing sentences, which is what the third bullet of the Level 4 descriptor rewards."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Punishments did get less brutal in the Middle Ages. At the start the Normans were really harsh and would blind people or cut bits off them for hunting the king's deer, which was very cruel, and later on they stopped doing that and made people pay money instead. But people were still hanged for stealing things, and traitors got a horrible death where they were cut up in front of a crowd, and heretics were burned alive. So punishment was still quite brutal at the end, but not as bad as it was at the beginning, so I mostly agree with the statement.","Why this stays at Level 2: it meets 'an explanation is given showing limited analysis and with implicit or unsustained links to the conceptual focus of the question' and 'accurate and relevant information is included, showing some knowledge and understanding of the period', while the overall judgement is given but asserted. It misses Levels 3 and 4 because nothing is dated or named — no 1217, no charter, no statute, no execution — because the evidence of hardening is listed and then dropped instead of being weighed against the evidence of softening; and because no criterion for 'brutal' is stated, so the judgement is claimed rather than tested."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Case study: the influence of the Church on crime and punishment in the early thirteenth century","items":["Trial by ordeal was used when the accused could not clear himself by oath-helping and there was no clear evidence; it was run by a priest in or beside a church after fasting and prayer.","Ordeal by hot iron: the accused carried a red-hot iron three paces; the hand was bandaged and examined after three days, and a clean-healing wound meant innocence.","Ordeal by hot water: the accused plunged an arm into boiling water to retrieve an object, and the burn was judged in the same way after three days.","Ordeal by cold water: the accused was bound and lowered into water blessed by the priest; sinking meant the pure water had accepted him and he was innocent, floating meant guilt.","Ordeal by blessed bread (consecrated bread) was reserved for priests: choking on it was taken as a sign of guilt.","Trial by combat was the Norman addition, used mainly by the nobility; the loser, if he survived, could be hanged.","The Fourth Lateran Council, called by Pope Innocent III in 1215, forbade clergy to take part in trials by ordeal.","In 1219 the king's judges in England were instructed to find other means of trying suspects, and trial by jury took the ordeal's place.","Reasons for the ending: the Church's own doubt that God could be compelled to give a verdict on demand; the spread of canon and common law learning; the availability of juries in royal courts; and the Crown's interest in controlling justice and its fines.","Sanctuary: a fugitive who reached a church could claim protection and had forty days to choose between standing trial and confessing before the coroner.","Abjuring the realm: the fugitive swore to leave England, was given a named port to travel to, and had to go there by the direct road carrying a wooden cross.","Benefit of clergy: clergy were tried in Church courts, set up separately from about 1072–76, which could not impose the death penalty and used penance, fines and pilgrimage instead.","The test of clerical status became the reading of the 'neck verse', the opening of Psalm 51, so literate laymen could escape hanging.","A statute of 1489 limited laymen to one claim of benefit of clergy and had them branded on the thumb so that the claim could not be repeated.","Church courts also tried moral offences that no royal court touched, including adultery, blasphemy, drunkenness and failure to pay tithes."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — William I's Forest Laws","items":["Introduced by William I after the Norman Conquest of 1066, over land the king designated as royal forest.","The New Forest in Hampshire was created in about 1079, and villages and farmland were cleared to make it.","'Forest' was a legal term, not a description of trees: it meant land reserved for the king's hunting and governed by its own law.","Hunting deer or boar in the royal forest became an offence against the Crown, punished by heavy fines and mutilation.","Taking timber or firewood, clearing land for crops and grazing animals inside the forest all required a licence.","Dogs kept within the forest had to be lamed so that they could not chase the king's game.","Forest offences were tried in special forest courts, outside the ordinary manor, hundred and shire courts.","Ordinary people resented the laws bitterly because they lost rights of pasture, wood and food they had used for generations.","Poaching in the royal forest is the specification's example of a 'social crime': illegal, but not regarded as wrong by the community.","The Forest Laws show a key factor at work: an individual ruler backed by a conquering government could redefine crime overnight."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — The Saxon wergild and its end","items":["Wergild means 'man price': compensation paid by an offender, or the offender's family, to the victim or the victim's family.","Its purpose was to settle the quarrel and prevent a blood feud, so it is compensation rather than deterrence or retribution.","The amount was fixed by the rank of the victim, so the life of a nobleman was priced many times higher than that of an ordinary free man, and a slave lower still.","Injuries had their own tariff: set sums were laid down for the loss of an eye, a hand, a foot or a thumb.","Wergild was paid for killing and wounding, but not for treason or for offences against the king, which carried death.","Anglo-Saxon courts were local: manor courts for small matters, hundred courts, and shire courts under the shire reeve, or sheriff.","Guilt could be settled by oath-helping, in which the accused and a number of neighbours swore to his innocence and good character.","After the Norman Conquest wergild was abolished; fines for killing and injury were paid to the king instead of to the victim's family.","That change turned crime from a private injury that could be bought off into an offence against the king's peace.","The Normans also used execution and mutilation far more readily than Anglo-Saxon law had done, and added the murdrum fine to protect Normans."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Law enforcement and punishment, c1000–c1500","items":["Tithing: every male over the age of twelve belonged to a group of ten men who answered for one another's conduct.","Hue and cry: the alarm raised by a victim or witness, which everyone hearing it had to join, on pain of a fine on the village.","Parish constable: an unpaid local man serving a year, who led the hue and cry and took suspects before the court.","Assize of Clarendon, 1166: Henry II required local men to present suspects to travelling royal judges and provided for gaols to hold them.","Coroners, created in 1194, investigated suspicious deaths for the Crown and heard the oaths of those abjuring the realm.","Statute of Winchester, 1285: Edward I required towns to keep a night watch, confirmed the hue and cry, and required men to keep weapons.","Justices of the Peace, established by the Act of 1361, were local landowners commissioned by the Crown who met regularly to try offenders.","Punishments included fines, the stocks and the pillory, whipping, mutilation, hanging for serious theft and murder, and burning for heresy after 1401.","High treason, defined by the Treason Act of 1351 as plotting the king's death, levying war against him or forging his coin, was punished by hanging, drawing and quartering; the Act is cited as 1351, although the parliament that passed it sat in January–February 1352, so some books date it 1352.","Prison was used mainly to hold suspects before trial or debtors until they paid, not as a punishment in its own right.","There was no police force, no forensic science and no way of identifying a stranger, so cases rested on witnesses, oaths and local reputation.","The three key factors in this period: attitudes in society (collective responsibility, deference, sympathy for social crime); institutions (the Church and a slowly growing royal government); and science and technology, which contributed nothing at all."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 1 Question 5 (16 marks + 4 SPaG): 'The Church was the biggest influence on crime and punishment in England in the years c1000–c1500.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • trial by ordeal • sanctuary. You must also use information of your own.","items":["Decoded question: the concept is significance — how far one institution shaped the theme. 'Biggest' is comparative, so the Church must be measured against the Crown and against attitudes in society; an answer that only describes Church power cannot reach Level 4 however detailed it is.","Criterion to set in the opening: influence will be judged on three things — who decided guilt, who could shield an offender from punishment, and who defined what counted as a crime. Say at once that the answer will differ across the three.","Paragraph 1 (agree — who decided guilt): trial by ordeal was a priest's ritual, used when oath-helping failed. Evidence: hot iron carried three paces and judged after three days, boiling water, cold water blessed by the priest, blessed bread for a priest. Interim judgement: until the early thirteenth century the Church owned the process by which people were convicted.","Paragraph 2 (agree — who could shield an offender): sanctuary gave forty days and then the right to abjure the realm before the coroner; benefit of clergy sent offenders to Church courts, set up separately from about 1072–76, which could not impose death, with the 'neck verse' from Psalm 51 as the test and the statute of 1489 branding laymen on the thumb to stop repeat claims. Interim judgement: the Church could suspend royal justice at will.","Paragraph 3 (disagree — the point beyond the stimulus, on who defined crime): the Crown created almost every new offence. Evidence: William I's Forest Laws and the murdrum fine after 1066; the Treason Act of 1351; the Statute of Labourers of 1351 after the Black Death of 1348–49; the heresy statute of 1401, which the Church wanted but only Parliament and the state could pass and enforce. Interim judgement: the Church influenced the law, but it did not make it.","Paragraph 4 (disagree — second point beyond the stimulus, on enforcement and attitudes): catching and punishing offenders was the community's work, not the Church's — tithings, the hue and cry, the parish constable, coroners from 1194, the Statute of Winchester of 1285, Justices of the Peace from 1361 — and the community decided which laws were really enforced, which is why poaching survived as a social crime. Interim judgement: at ground level neither Church nor Crown was as powerful as local opinion.","Evaluation thread running through all four paragraphs: distinguish influence over the process of justice from influence over the content of the law. The Church dominates the first and loses the second; test every paragraph against that distinction.","The turning point to use in the argument: the Fourth Lateran Council of 1215 forbade priests to take part in the ordeal and by 1219 English judges were told to use other means, so the Church's hold on proof ended in the middle of the period. That timing is the strongest evidence a judgement can use, whichever side it takes.","Conclusion: agree for the years before 1215 and on the criterion of how guilt was proved and who could escape punishment; disagree for the later period and on the criterion of who defined crime, where the Crown is clearly the bigger influence. Say which criterion matters more to an ordinary offender, and justify the choice rather than asserting it.","Timing on the 80-minute paper: about 25 minutes — roughly 4 minutes planning the criterion and the four paragraphs, 18 writing and 3 checking, since this is the only Section B answer where spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology carry their own 4 marks.","Specialist terms to spell and use accurately for the SPaG marks: ordeal, oath-helping, sanctuary, abjure the realm, benefit of clergy, coroner, tithing, hue and cry, wergild, heresy, jurisdiction, continuity."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:14:13Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"Every recorded fix is in the kit and verified: the new Q13 on property against the person is nowhere in the specimen or the June 2023 Option 10 paper, its grand-larceny line (Statute of Westminster I, 1275, c.15), murdrum fine and the abolition of presentment of Englishry in 1340 (Engleschrie Act, 14 Edw. 3) all check out, the forest figure now reads 'a third of the land area of southern England', the twelve-pence wording is repaired, all 12 occurrences read 'the Treason Act of 1351' with the 1351/52 note in the fact file, example 2 is on Crown against local communities and the paragraph pair on brutality; dupscan prints 'no duplication' and no example, plan or paragraph shares a bank question's focus and stimulus pair — only the 2ⁿᵈ problem below is worth a teacher's pen before publishing."}}},"10-2":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"10-2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This room is the early modern hinge of the Paper 1 thematic study, and the examiner sets Section B across centuries, not inside one key topic. Question 3 asks for one similarity or difference between two named periods, so you need c1500 to c1700 material that can be set beside the Middle Ages or beside the years after c1700. Question 4 asks why something changed across the whole span and prints two stimulus points you must go beyond. Question 5 or 6 asks for a judgement on change, continuity, causation or significance, again over centuries, with four extra marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms. Almost every one of those questions runs through this period, because this is where the Reformation redefined crimes against authority, where vagabondage and witchcraft were invented as crimes, where transportation and the Bloody Code began, and where the two case studies the specification names — the Gunpowder Plotters of 1605 and Matthew Hopkins's witch-hunts of 1645 to 1647 — give you the precise, dateable evidence that separates a Level 4 answer from a Level 2 one.","idea":[{"h":"What stayed the same, and what changed, in the nature of crime","t":"The bulk of crime did not change at all. Petty theft of food, clothing, tools and livestock filled the quarter sessions and the assizes in 1700 just as it had filled the manor courts in 1400, and assault, murder and infanticide continued as before. What changed was the top of the scale. Heresy meant holding beliefs the official Church rejected and was punished by burning; high treason meant plotting against the monarch or denying the monarch's authority over the Church and was punished by hanging, drawing and quartering. Because the Reformation made the Crown head of the Church, these definitions flipped with each monarch: the Treason Act of 1534 made denying Henry VIII's supremacy treason, Mary I burned nearly 300 Protestants as heretics between 1555 and 1558, and Elizabeth I fined those who stayed away from her Church after 1559. Property crime shifted too, with highway robbery growing after the Civil Wars, the Game Act of 1671 turning hunting into a crime for anyone without land worth £100 a year, and smuggling spreading as customs duties rose. A top-level answer separates the two layers: it says that everyday crime stayed remarkably constant while crimes against authority were rewritten, and it dates the rewriting to a named statute rather than to 'the Tudors'.","code":"10-2 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Continuity and change in the nature of crimes"},{"h":"Vagabondage: poverty turned into a crime","t":"Nothing new happened to the poor except that the law began to hunt them. England's population grew from roughly two and a half million in 1500 to about four million by 1600, enclosure for sheep farming cut the labour a village needed, debasement of the coinage and rising prices cut what a wage bought, and the dissolution of the monasteries between 1536 and 1541 destroyed the charity that had fed and sheltered the destitute. Bad harvests, especially in the 1590s, pushed whole families onto the roads. The men who made the law were landowners with no police force and a fresh memory of rebellion, and they read masterless men as carriers of plague, theft and sedition. The statutes show that fear: the Act of 1531 licensed the old and sick to beg and whipped everyone else, the Act of 1547 allowed a vagrant who refused work to be branded with a V and enslaved for two years before being repealed in 1550, the Act of 1572 had vagrants whipped and bored through the ear and created the first compulsory poor rate, the Act of 1576 set up houses of correction, and the Poor Law of 1601 made each parish responsible for its own poor. A top-level answer says that the behaviour was old and only the law was new, and uses the statutes as evidence of attitudes rather than as a list.","code":"10-2 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: New definitions of crime in the sixteenth century"},{"h":"Witchcraft: a crime created by statute and by belief","t":"Witchcraft is the other genuinely new crime, and it was created by Parliament. The Act of 1542 made it a felony punishable by death and was repealed in 1547; the Act of 1563 moved witchcraft out of the Church courts and into the ordinary courts, where neighbours' testimony counted as evidence; the Act of 1604, approved by a king who had published Daemonologie in 1597 and believed witches had raised a storm against his ship, made calling up an evil spirit a hanging offence even where nobody had been harmed. In England convicted witches were hanged, not burned, because burning was kept for heresy and for petty treason. The accused were overwhelmingly poor, older women, many of them widows dependent on their neighbours' charity, so a refused loaf followed by a sick child could become a case. Courts accepted the Devil's mark, familiars such as cats and toads, and the swimming test, in which floating was taken as proof because pure water was thought to reject a witch. A top-level answer explains why the state cared: witchcraft was treated as a crime against authority, a pact with the Devil against a Christian kingdom, and that is why the penalty was death rather than penance.","code":"10-2 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: New definitions of crime in the sixteenth century"},{"h":"Who enforced the law: the community, supervised by the Crown","t":"There was no police force in 1500 and none in 1700, so ordinary people did the catching. The hue and cry still bound a witness to raise the alarm and the neighbours to join the chase. The parish constable was an unpaid householder appointed for a year who arrested suspects, ran the lock-up and brought offenders before a Justice of the Peace; town watchmen were volunteers who patrolled from ten at night until dawn with a lantern and a bell and handed suspects to him. What grew was the framework above them. Justices of the Peace, in place since 1361, met at quarter sessions four times a year, licensed beggars, supervised the poor rate and sent serious cases to the assizes, where royal judges came on circuit about twice a year. The Church lost ground: from 1576 those who claimed benefit of clergy were tried in the ordinary courts, and sanctuary was abolished in 1623. Money entered the system late in the period, with paid night watchmen in London from 1663, nicknamed the 'Charlies', and a £40 reward from 1692 for taking a highwayman, which produced thief-takers. A top-level answer keeps the two levels apart: amateur and local at the bottom, increasingly royal and statutory at the top.","code":"10-2 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities"},{"h":"Punishment: public bodies, new colonies and a longer list of hanging crimes","t":"Punishment stayed public and physical, because a state that could not catch offenders tried instead to frighten them. Whipping was done in the street at the cart's tail, the stocks and the pillory exposed the offender to a crowd, branding left a permanent mark such as a V for a vagrant or a T for a thief, and the scold's bridle and the ducking stool shamed women before their neighbours. Hanging was the standard penalty for felony, burning for heresy and for petty treason, and hanging, drawing and quartering for high treason, with the quarters displayed. Two changes matter. The list of capital offences grew until about fifty crimes carried death by 1688, the beginning of what was later called the Bloody Code, because a Parliament of property owners answered vagrancy, highway robbery and theft by raising the advertised penalty. And transportation began: an Act of 1597 allowed persistent vagrants to be banished, and from the 1610s convicts were shipped to the North American colonies for seven or fourteen years, which removed them cheaply and supplied colonial planters with labour. A top-level answer judges against a criterion, usually purpose, and notices that deterrence explains the display while usefulness explains transportation.","code":"10-2 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The continued use of corporal and capital punishment"},{"h":"Case study: the Gunpowder Plotters, 1605","t":"Thirteen Catholics, mostly gentry, led by Robert Catesby planned to blow up the House of Lords at the State Opening of Parliament on 5 November 1605, kill James I and his ministers, and put his daughter Princess Elizabeth on the throne as a Catholic queen. Thirty-six barrels of gunpowder were stored in a cellar beneath the Lords, and Guy Fawkes, a soldier who had fought for Spain in the Netherlands, was to fire them. An anonymous letter warned Lord Monteagle to stay away in late October 1605, the cellars were searched, and Fawkes was arrested in the early hours of 5 November giving the false name John Johnson. James I authorised torture and Fawkes named the others; Catesby and Thomas Percy were shot dead at Holbeche House in Staffordshire on 8 November. The eight survivors were tried in Westminster Hall in January 1606 and hanged, drawn and quartered in public on 30 and 31 January, four at St Paul's Churchyard and four at Old Palace Yard beside the Parliament they had meant to destroy. Use the case study as evidence, not as a story: it shows how the state defined crimes against authority, how it investigated them and why it made the punishment a spectacle.","code":"10-2 — 3 Case studies: The Gunpowder Plotters, 1605"},{"h":"Case study: Matthew Hopkins and the witch-hunts of 1645 to 1647","t":"Matthew Hopkins called himself the Witchfinder General although he held no official commission. Beginning at Manningtree in Essex in 1645, he and John Stearne worked through Essex, Suffolk, Norfolk and Cambridgeshire, searching the accused for the Devil's mark, pricking the skin, watching for familiars, walking the accused up and down and depriving them of sleep until they confessed and named others. Towns paid him: Stowmarket in Suffolk is recorded as paying £23, far more than a labourer could earn in a year, so every confession advertised him to the next town. Around nineteen of the accused were hanged at Chelmsford in the summer of 1645, with further executions at Bury St Edmunds, and historians estimate about a hundred deaths across the whole campaign, more than the previous century had produced. Three things explain the intensity: the Act of 1604 made accusation easy and put cases in the ordinary courts; the Civil War disrupted the assize circuits, so sceptical royal judges were absent and local magistrates heard the cases; and war taxation, poor harvests and disease in a strongly Puritan region left communities looking for someone to blame. A top-level answer ranks these rather than listing them, and notices that the hunts ended when Hopkins did, in 1647.","code":"10-2 — 3 Case studies: Key individual: Matthew Hopkins and the witch-hunts of 1645–47"}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 3: building a 4-mark difference across two periods","setup":"Paper 1 Question 3: 'Explain one way in which the Church's part in dealing with crime in the years c1500 to c1700 was different from its part in the years c1000 to c1500.' (4 marks; AO2 2, AO1 2.) You must name one difference, analyse it, and support it with specific information from both of the periods named.","steps":["Fix the difference before writing anything else: in the earlier period the Church could pull a serious criminal out of the king's courts altogether, and by the later period the Crown had closed both of the doors it used.","Support the earlier period with the two medieval privileges: a man who could read out the opening verse of Psalm 51, the 'neck verse', could claim benefit of clergy and be tried in a Church court that had no power to hang him, and a fugitive who reached a church could claim sanctuary for forty days and then abjure the realm rather than stand trial.","Support the later period with dated change: from 1576 a convicted offender who pleaded his clergy was branded on the hand and dealt with by the lay court instead of being handed to the Church, sanctuary for crime was abolished by statute in 1623, and the Witchcraft Act of 1563 had already moved witchcraft out of the Church courts and into the ordinary ones.","Close by naming the difference itself rather than leaving the reader to infer it: the Church kept its own courts for moral offences throughout, but after the Reformation it no longer stood between a serious criminal and the king's judge."],"cues":["What could the Church do for an accused criminal before c1500 that it could no longer do by c1700?","Which two medieval privileges took a man out of the royal courts, and what did each of them require of him?","Which dated changes shut those privileges down in the later period?","What does the examiner still need you to say about the difference itself?"]},{"title":"Question 4: turning two stimulus points into three reasons","setup":"Paper 1 Question 4: 'Explain why highway robbery increased in the years after 1650.' You may use the following in your answer: • the Civil Wars • the growth of travel. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks; AO2 6, AO1 6.) The mark scheme gives a maximum of 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","steps":["Convert the first bullet into a reason with a mechanism: the Civil Wars left thousands of men trained to ride and to handle weapons with no army to pay them, and defeated royalists whose estates had been seized had no living to go back to, so robbery on horseback was the one trade their skills fitted.","Do the same with the second bullet: as travel grew there were far more people on the roads worth robbing, with advertised stagecoach services running from London to Exeter, York and Chester by 1658 and the first turnpike Act of 1663 beginning to mend the Great North Road between Wadesmill in Hertfordshire and Stilton, so a robber could count on loaded coaches keeping to a known route.","Add the reason of your own that opens Level 4: nobody policed the road between the towns. The parish constable's authority stopped at his own parish boundary, watchmen patrolled inside towns only, and banknotes were not yet in ordinary use, so a traveller carried his money with him; Parliament's answer in 1692 was a £40 reward for anyone who caught and convicted a highwayman, which is an admission that catching them was the difficulty.","Link the three in a closing sentence so there is one line of reasoning: the wars supplied the men, the growth of travel supplied the victims and the unpoliced road supplied the opportunity, so the state could raise only the reward for catching a highwayman and the penalty for being caught."],"cues":["What has to be shown before a stimulus point counts as a reason rather than a topic?","What changed about who was on the roads after 1650, and which dated developments show it?","Which reason is prompted by neither bullet: whose job was it to patrol the road between two towns?","What does a Level 4 answer add once the three reasons are written out?"]},{"title":"Question 5: one paragraph of a 16-mark judgement, with its counter-weight","setup":"Paper 1 Question 5: ''The authorities were better at punishing criminals than at catching them in the years c1500 to c1700.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.' You may use the following in your answer: • the hue and cry • the Bloody Code. You must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]. You are writing the paragraph that tests the claim against the machinery for catching a suspect.","steps":["State the point in the question's own terms: catching a criminal was left to men doing it in their spare time, so detection was the weak half of the system.","Support it with precise detail: the parish constable was an unpaid householder serving for a year, town watchmen were volunteers who patrolled from ten at night until dawn with a lantern and a bell, and the hue and cry still obliged every neighbour to drop his work and join the chase.","Explain the mechanism the examiner is waiting for: none of these men was trained, paid or free to follow a suspect beyond his own parish, so a criminal who left the district was usually lost, and the Crown's own remedies — paid night watchmen in London from 1663, the £40 reward offered in 1692 for taking a highwayman — were attempts to buy the detection the system could not supply.","Weigh the counter-evidence before judging: once a suspect stood in court the state was formidable, with about fifty offences carrying death by 1688 and punishment staged in public at the gallows, the pillory and the cart's tail.","Close with an interim judgement tied to a criterion: measured by what the authorities could organise and pay for, punishment was much the stronger half, which is precisely why they leaned on the threat of the gallows to do the work no police force existed to do."],"cues":["What is the claim this paragraph has been set up to test?","Who actually had to catch a suspect, and on what terms did they work?","Why did that arrangement fail as soon as a criminal moved, and what did the Crown try in answer?","Which evidence from the punishment side of the topic pulls the other way?","What must the last sentence of the paragraph do before you move on?"]},{"title":"Question 4: the difference between retelling the plot and explaining what followed it","setup":"Paper 1 Question 4: 'Explain why the Gunpowder Plot of 1605 led to harsher treatment of Catholics in England.' You may use the following in your answer: • Guy Fawkes • the oath of allegiance. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks.) Two students know the same facts; one retells the plot, the other explains why its consequences fell on Catholics who had nothing to do with it.","steps":["Reject the retelling: 'the plotters hid thirty-six barrels of gunpowder under the House of Lords and Fawkes was caught on 5 November' is inert information until it is attached to the treatment of Catholics.","Attach it by following what the government did with Fawkes: arrested in the early hours of 5 November 1605 under the false name John Johnson, questioned in the Tower after James I authorised torture, he signed a confession naming the rest, and the public trial of the eight survivors in Westminster Hall in January 1606 turned a conspiracy by a handful of gentry into proof, as the state presented it, that Catholics as a body were traitors.","Turn the second bullet into a reason rather than a label: the Popish Recusants Act passed by that same Parliament required a new oath of allegiance denying that the Pope could depose a king, barred Catholics from practising as lawyers or physicians and from acting as guardians or trustees, let magistrates search their houses for weapons, and fined a recusant who would not take communion in his parish church once a year £60 or two thirds of his land, so suspicion was written into statute.","Add the reason prompted by neither bullet: the plot was deliberately kept alive. An Act of the same Parliament ordered a service of thanksgiving in every church every 5 November, at which the Act itself was read aloud, and the execution of the Jesuit Henry Garnet in 1606 for having known of the plot and said nothing spread the suspicion to every priest, in a country already hostile since Elizabeth I's recusancy fines.","Finish by saying how the three interlock: the confession made every Catholic look like a conspirator, the statute turned that suspicion into disabilities a man carried for life, and the annual service made sure no congregation was allowed to forget why."],"cues":["What is missing from a paragraph that only retells the story of the plot?","How did the way Fawkes was questioned and the plotters tried shape opinion about Catholics in general?","What did the oath of allegiance actually require, and what else did the same Act impose?","Which reason is prompted by neither of the two bullets?","How do the three reasons fit together in a single sentence?"]},{"title":"Question 3: choosing a similarity that survives five centuries","setup":"Paper 1 Question 3: 'Explain one way in which the trial of serious crimes in the years c1500 to c1700 was similar to the trial of serious crimes in the years c1000 to c1500.' (4 marks; AO2 2, AO1 2.) One similarity, analysed, with specific information from both periods.","steps":["Choose a similarity general enough to survive the gap but precise enough to evidence: in both periods a serious charge was heard in the accused's own county by a judge sent out from the king's courts, with local men supplying the case against him.","Evidence from the earlier period: Henry II's Assize of Clarendon of 1166 ordered twelve men of every hundred to name on oath anyone suspected of robbery, murder or theft, and the king's justices in eyre rode from county to county to hear them; once trial by ordeal ended in the early thirteenth century juries of local men decided guilt as well as reporting suspicion.","Evidence from the later period: royal judges still rode a circuit, holding the assizes in each county about twice a year to try felonies, while Justices of the Peace met at quarter sessions four times a year for lesser crimes, and the verdict still came from a jury of local men.","Say why this is similarity and not coincidence: the Crown never had the judges or the money to sit permanently in every county, so in both periods royal justice reached the shires by sending judges out to them and leaning on local knowledge for the accusation and the verdict."],"cues":["What kind of similarity is worth choosing for a question spanning several centuries?","How did a serious accusation in a medieval county reach a royal judge?","Which courts did that work between c1500 and c1700, and how often did they sit?","What underlying reason makes the two periods alike rather than accidentally similar?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which Act made it treason to deny that Henry VIII was Supreme Head of the Church of England, and who was beheaded under it?","a":"The Treason Act of 1534, passed alongside the Act of Supremacy; Sir Thomas More was beheaded under it in 1535."},{"q":"Give three reasons why the number of vagrants rose sharply in the sixteenth century.","a":"Population growth from roughly two and a half million to about four million; enclosure for sheep farming cutting the need for labourers; rising prices and debased coinage; and the end of monastic charity after the dissolution of 1536 to 1541."},{"q":"What happened to a man convicted of high treason, and how did that differ from the punishment for heresy?","a":"He was hanged, drawn and quartered in public and his quarters displayed; heretics were burned at the stake. Convicted witches in England were hanged, not burned."},{"q":"Roughly how many offences carried the death penalty by 1688, and what is that growth usually called?","a":"About fifty, and it is called the beginning of the Bloody Code."}]},"room":{"facts":["Question 3 carries 4 marks (AO2 2, AO1 2) and wants one similarity or difference only, analysed and supported from both of the periods named; about five minutes.","Question 4 'Explain why…' carries 12 marks (AO2 6, AO1 6) and prints two stimulus points with 'You must also use information of your own.' The mark scheme prints 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'; about 20 minutes.","Question 5 or 6 carries 16 marks (AO2 10, AO1 6) plus up to 4 separate marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology, with two stimulus points; the mark scheme prints 'Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'. Allow about 25 to 30 minutes and leave time to check the writing.","Section B of Paper 1 is worth 36 marks of the paper's 52 and the paper lasts 80 minutes: the allowances above (5, 20 and 27 minutes) spend 52 minutes on Section B and leave about 28 for Section A."],"questions":[{"q":"Explain one way in which crimes against authority in the years c1000 to c1500 were different from crimes against authority in the years c1500 to c1700. (4 marks)","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One difference analysed across both periods with specific support: before c1500 crimes against authority protected the king's land and person (Forest Laws, rebellion); after c1500 they protected the king's Church (the Treason Act of 1534, heresy under Mary I).","sol":"One difference is what counted as a crime against authority. In the years c1000 to c1500 the crimes the Crown punished hardest protected its land and its person: the Forest Laws William I imposed after 1066 made hunting deer or taking wood in the royal forests an offence against the king, punished in special forest courts. In the years c1500 to c1700 the crimes that mattered most were religious. The Treason Act of 1534 made it treason to deny that Henry VIII was Supreme Head of the Church of England, and Sir Thomas More was beheaded under it in 1535, while Mary I burned nearly 300 Protestants as heretics between 1555 and 1558. Authority had moved from policing land to policing belief.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what each period's government was most frightened of losing, and you will have the difference.","A difference only scores when both sides are evidenced, so plan one named law or event from each period before you write.","Set the two periods out in the same sentence pattern so the contrast is visible, then name the difference explicitly at the end."],"codes":["10-2 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Continuity and change in the nature of crimes"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the role of ordinary people in law enforcement in the years c1500 to c1700 was similar to their role in the years c1000 to c1500. (4 marks)","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. Continuity of unpaid community policing: tithings and the hue and cry before c1500; the unpaid parish constable and volunteer watchmen after c1500, with no police force in either period.","sol":"One similarity is that law enforcement in both periods depended on unpaid ordinary people rather than on any police force. In the years c1000 to c1500 every man over the age of twelve belonged to a tithing that was answerable for his behaviour, and anyone who witnessed a crime had to raise the hue and cry so that the whole village joined the chase. In the years c1500 to c1700 the same duty survived: the parish constable was a householder appointed for one year without pay who arrested suspects and took them before a Justice of the Peace, and town watchmen were volunteers who patrolled from ten at night until dawn with a lantern and a bell. In both periods catching criminals was a duty owed by the community.","hints":["The question is about what ordinary people had to do, not about what the courts did with the suspect afterwards.","Look for the duty that existed in both periods, then find the officials in each who carried it out.","Name the medieval arrangement and the early modern one in turn, and finish by saying why they were alike."],"m":4,"d":1,"codes":["10-2 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which punishment in the years c1500 to c1700 was different from punishment in the years c1700 to c1900. (4 marks)","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One difference analysed: early modern punishment was public and physical, with gaols used mainly to hold people before trial, while imprisonment became the punishment itself by the nineteenth century and public execution ended in 1868.","sol":"One difference is the use of imprisonment. In the years c1500 to c1700 gaols mainly held people awaiting trial or debtors who could not pay, and punishment itself was public and physical: whipping at the cart's tail, the stocks, the pillory, branding and the gallows, with about fifty offences carrying death by 1688. In the years c1700 to c1900 prison became the punishment rather than the waiting room, with Pentonville opening in 1842 to hold prisoners in separate cells under a strict routine, while public execution was ended in 1868 and hangings took place inside prison walls. Punishment changed from a spectacle staged in front of a crowd into a sentence served out of sight.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about where the punishment happened and who was meant to see it.","One institution changes its job completely between the two periods; find it and say what it was used for in each.","Give a named example from each period so the contrast rests on evidence, then state the difference in one sentence."],"codes":["10-2 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The continued use of corporal and capital punishment"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the definition of crime in the years c1500 to c1700 was different from the definition of crime in the years c1900 to the present. (4 marks)","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. Witchcraft as the clearest change: a capital felony under the Acts of 1563 and 1604 and the ground of the Hopkins hunts, but no crime at all today, with claims of magical powers treated only as possible fraud since 1951.","sol":"One difference is that witchcraft was a serious crime in the earlier period and is no crime at all today. The Witchcraft Act of 1563 moved witchcraft cases into the ordinary courts, and the Act of 1604 made calling up an evil spirit a hanging offence even where nobody had been harmed; around a hundred people were executed during Matthew Hopkins's hunts in East Anglia between 1645 and 1647. Parliament abolished the crime of witchcraft in the eighteenth century, and since 1951 the law has treated a claim to magical powers only as possible fraud rather than as a pact with the Devil. Belief in supernatural harm has stopped being a matter for the criminal law at all.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Choose a crime that exists in one period and has vanished in the other, not one that merely changed its penalty.","The earlier period's evidence is a statute and a named episode; the later period's is the absence of the crime.","Explain what the change shows about who decides what counts as a crime."],"codes":["10-2 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: New definitions of crime in the sixteenth century"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which crimes against property in the years c1500 to c1700 were similar to crimes against property in the years c1000 to c1500. (4 marks)","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. Continuity: petty theft remained the commonest crime in both periods, and hunting law criminalised ordinary people in both — the Forest Laws before c1500, the Game Act of 1671 after it — with both widely treated as social crimes.","sol":"One similarity is that theft stayed the commonest crime in both periods, and that hunting laws made ordinary people criminals in both. Between c1000 and c1500 most cases in the manor and royal courts were petty thefts of food, clothing and livestock, while the Forest Laws William I imposed after 1066 made taking deer or wood from the royal forests an offence against the king. Between c1500 and c1700 petty theft still filled the quarter sessions and the assizes, and the Game Act of 1671 restricted hunting to landowners with property worth £100 a year, so poaching remained a crime that most villagers refused to regard as one. In both periods the law protected the property of the powerful against the needs of the poor.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Continuity questions reward the ordinary and unglamorous: ask what most people were actually tried for.","There is a second continuity in the laws that protected hunting rights; find the named law in each period.","End by explaining what the similarity shows about whose interests the law protected."],"codes":["10-2 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Continuity and change in the nature of crimes"]},{"q":"Explain why the definition of crime changed in the years c1500 to c1700. You may use the following in your answer: • the Reformation • vagabondage. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Three analysed reasons with a sustained line of reasoning: religious change redefining heresy and treason; social and economic change creating vagabondage; and, beyond the stimulus, the beliefs of rulers producing the Witchcraft Act of 1604. Maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"One reason was the Reformation. When the Act of Supremacy of 1534 made Henry VIII Supreme Head of the Church of England, the Treason Act of the same year made it treason simply to deny that title, and Sir Thomas More was beheaded for his silence in 1535. Because the official Church changed with each monarch, the definition of heresy changed with it: Mary I burned nearly 300 Protestants between 1555 and 1558, Archbishop Cranmer among them at Oxford in 1556, while Elizabeth I's settlement of 1559 fined those who stayed away from her services and raised the penalties on persistent recusants in 1581. Belief itself had become the ground on which the state defined crime.\n\nA second reason was social and economic change, which created the new crime of vagabondage. England's population grew from roughly two and a half million in 1500 to about four million by 1600, enclosure for sheep farming cut the demand for labourers, and the dissolution of the monasteries between 1536 and 1541 destroyed the charity that had fed the poor. Landowners and magistrates who feared that masterless men would spread disease, theft and rebellion answered with statute: the Act of 1531 whipped able-bodied beggars, and the Act of 1547 allowed a vagrant who refused work to be branded with a V and enslaved for two years.\n\nA third reason, beyond the stimulus, was the personal conviction of rulers. James I had published Daemonologie in 1597 and believed that witches had raised a storm against his ship, and the Witchcraft Act he approved in 1604 made calling up an evil spirit a hanging offence even where nobody had been harmed, leaving such cases to the ordinary courts.\n\nThe three run together. In each case an old behaviour — dissent, poverty, folk magic — became criminal at the moment those with power decided it threatened the Church, the social order or the safety of the kingdom.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["The question is about definitions, so every reason must end with a behaviour that was not a crime before and was afterwards.","Take each printed point, turn it into a reason, and make the middle of the paragraph do the causal work rather than the first line.","Plan a third reason that neither bullet suggests before you start writing, or the answer cannot reach the top level."],"codes":["10-2 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Continuity and change in the nature of crimes","10-2 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: New definitions of crime in the sixteenth century"]},{"q":"Explain why the witch-hunts of 1645 to 1647 were so intense. You may use the following in your answer: • Matthew Hopkins • the English Civil War. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Analysed reasons: the disruption of the assize circuits and the hardship of war; Hopkins's paid, method-driven campaign; and, beyond the stimulus, the Witchcraft Act of 1604 and the social position of the accused. Maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The English Civil War created the conditions. From 1642 the country was at war, and in East Anglia the assize circuits were badly disrupted, so accusations that royal judges had often dismissed were now heard by local magistrates who shared their neighbours' fears. The war also brought heavy taxation, requisitioning by armies, poor harvests and disease, and in a region that was strongly Puritan and firmly on Parliament's side, misfortune was easily read as the work of the Devil and his servants.\n\nMatthew Hopkins turned those conditions into a campaign. Calling himself the Witchfinder General although he held no commission, he began at Manningtree in Essex in 1645 and moved through Suffolk, Norfolk and Cambridgeshire with John Stearne. He was paid by the towns that invited him — Stowmarket in Suffolk is recorded as paying £23, more than a labourer could earn in a year — so every confession was an advertisement for the next invitation. His methods were designed to produce confessions: searching for the Devil's mark, watching the accused for familiars, walking them up and down and keeping them from sleep until they confessed and named others. Around nineteen of the accused were hanged at Chelmsford in the summer of 1645 alone.\n\nBeyond the stimulus, the law itself made the hunts possible. The Witchcraft Act of 1604 had made calling up an evil spirit a capital offence even where nobody had been harmed, and had left the decision to ordinary courts, where neighbours' testimony counted as evidence. The accused were usually poor, elderly women who depended on charity, so a refused loaf followed by a sick child was enough to begin a case.\n\nThe three interlock: a law that made accusation easy, a war that removed the sceptics who would have tested it, and a man with a financial reason to keep accusing. That is why these three years produced about a hundred executions, more than the previous century had.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Intensity means scale and speed, so each reason must explain why so many accusations succeeded so quickly, not merely why people believed in witches.","Separate the condition that allowed the hunts from the person who drove them; they are different kinds of cause.","There is a statute behind every one of these trials; bring it in as the reason neither bullet prompts."],"codes":["10-2 — 3 Case studies: Key individual: Matthew Hopkins and the witch-hunts of 1645–47","10-2 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: New definitions of crime in the sixteenth century"]},{"q":"Explain why punishment changed in the years c1500 to c1700. You may use the following in your answer: • transportation • the Bloody Code. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Analysed reasons: colonies and the need for a penalty between whipping and hanging; a propertied Parliament's fear of disorder producing about fifty capital offences by 1688; and, beyond the stimulus, religious and political change plus the stripping away of Church protections. Maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Transportation was the clearest change, and it happened because England acquired colonies. An Act of 1597 had already allowed persistent vagrants to be banished from the realm, and from the 1610s convicts were shipped to the North American colonies, usually for seven or fourteen years. Virginia and the other settlements needed labour for tobacco, and the courts needed a punishment between a whipping and the gallows, so transportation solved two problems at once: the offender was removed from England without the expense of keeping him, and the planters were supplied.\n\nThe growth of what was later called the Bloody Code happened for a different reason. Parliament was dominated by property owners frightened by population growth, vagrancy and, after the Civil Wars, by demobilised soldiers turning to highway robbery. Their answer was to attach the death penalty to more and more offences against property, so that by 1688 about fifty crimes could be punished by hanging. The purpose was deterrence: with no police force to raise the chance of being caught, the state raised the advertised penalty instead.\n\nBeyond the stimulus, religion and politics changed punishment too. The Reformation made belief a capital matter, so burning was used against nearly 300 Protestants under Mary I, and hanging, drawing and quartering was staged against traitors such as the Gunpowder Plotters in January 1606. At the same time the Church's protections were stripped away: from 1576 those who claimed benefit of clergy were tried in the ordinary courts and sanctuary was abolished in 1623, so more offenders faced the ordinary penalties.\n\nWhat links the three is the state's growing reach. Punishment became harsher and more useful to the government, while its public, physical character stayed exactly as it had been in 1500.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Each printed point is a change; the marks come from saying what made that change happen, not from describing it.","One change is driven by opportunity abroad and one by fear at home — keep them in separate paragraphs.","Find a third driver in the religious and political upheaval of the period, and end by saying what the three have in common."],"codes":["10-2 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The continued use of corporal and capital punishment","10-2 — 3 Case studies: The Gunpowder Plotters, 1605"]},{"q":"Explain why vagabondage came to be seen as a serious crime in the sixteenth century. You may use the following in your answer: • enclosure • the dissolution of the monasteries. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Analysed reasons: enclosure, population growth and inflation creating unemployment; the dissolution removing charity; and, beyond the stimulus, the fear of disorder among the propertied, shown in the statutes of 1531, 1547 and 1572. Maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Enclosure and the changing rural economy put people on the road. Landowners fenced open fields and turned arable land over to sheep, which needed far fewer workers, so labourers lost both their employment and the common rights that had supported them. At the same time England's population grew from roughly two and a half million in 1500 to about four million by 1600, so more people were chasing less work, while debasement of the coinage under Henry VIII and rising prices cut what a wage would buy. Bad harvests, especially in the 1590s, finished the job.\n\nThe dissolution of the monasteries between 1536 and 1541 removed the safety net. Monasteries had given food, shelter and basic medical care to the poor of their district, and when Henry VIII closed them that charity vanished with nothing put in its place, so the destitute had little choice but to travel in search of work and alms.\n\nBeyond the stimulus lies the reason those travellers were treated as criminals rather than as victims: fear. The men who made and enforced the law were landowners with no police force and a fresh memory of rebellion, and they believed that masterless men carried plague, theft and sedition from parish to parish. Pamphlets described organised gangs of 'sturdy beggars' with their own slang who chose idleness over work. That fear is written into the statutes: the Act of 1531 licensed the old and sick to beg but whipped everyone else, the Act of 1547 allowed a vagrant who refused work to be branded with a V and made a slave for two years, and the Act of 1572 had vagrants whipped and bored through the ear.\n\nSo poverty made vagrancy common, the loss of monastic charity made it visible, and the fear of the propertied made it a crime.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["The question asks why vagabondage was seen as serious, so the attitudes of the law-makers matter as much as the causes of poverty.","Use the statutes as evidence of what the ruling class feared, rather than listing them as events.","Your own reason should explain the gap between being poor and being treated as a criminal."],"codes":["10-2 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: New definitions of crime in the sixteenth century"]},{"q":"Explain why the role of the authorities in law enforcement grew in the years c1500 to c1700. You may use the following in your answer: • Justices of the Peace • town watchmen. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Analysed reasons: the Crown loading JPs with statutory duties; urban growth producing organised and then paid watch; and, beyond the stimulus, the decline of Church justice after 1576 and 1623 and the use of cash rewards from 1692. Maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Justices of the Peace became the hinge of local government because the Crown needed unpaid men it could trust. JPs had existed since 1361, but Tudor governments loaded them with work: they met at quarter sessions four times a year to try lesser offences, licensed beggars under the vagrancy Acts, supervised the poor rate created in 1572 and the parish relief organised by the Poor Law of 1601, and committed serious cases to the assizes, where royal judges came on circuit about twice a year. The more the government legislated, the more it depended on JPs to enforce what it had passed.\n\nTown watchmen show the same growth from below. As towns grew, constables organised watchmen to patrol from ten at night until dawn with a lantern and a bell; they were unpaid volunteers taking their turn, but they answered to the constable and through him to the JP. When London's streets proved too much for volunteers, Charles II's government put paid watchmen on them from 1663, the first regular paid patrol in England, nicknamed the 'Charlies'.\n\nBeyond the stimulus, the authorities grew because their rivals shrank. Church courts had once handled morality cases and sheltered offenders; from 1576 anyone claiming benefit of clergy was tried in the ordinary courts, and sanctuary was abolished in 1623, so the Crown's courts took what the Church lost. The state also began to pay for results: an Act of 1692 offered a £40 reward for the capture of a highwayman, which produced thief-takers who hunted criminals for money.\n\nThe pattern is consistent. There was still no police force and enforcement still rested on ordinary people, but by 1700 those people worked inside a framework of royal law, royal judges and royal rewards that had barely existed in 1500.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Growth of the authorities does not mean the disappearance of the amateurs, so keep track of who was still doing the work.","Ask what new jobs the government kept handing to local officials, and which statutes handed them over.","A third reason lies in what the Church lost during the same period."],"codes":["10-2 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities"]},{"q":"'Religious change was the main reason why new crimes were defined in the years c1500 to c1700.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • heresy • witchcraft. You must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6 plus up to 4 SPaG. A sustained judgement weighing religious change against social and economic pressure and fear of disorder, with a justified criterion; evidence beyond heresy and witchcraft (vagrancy statutes, the Game Act of 1671, the Bloody Code) is needed for Level 4. Maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The test has to be which pressure actually put new offences on the statute book, and whether the change would have happened without it.\n\nReligious change plainly redefined crimes against authority. The Act of Supremacy of 1534 made Henry VIII Supreme Head of the Church of England, and the Treason Act of the same year made denial of that title treason; Sir Thomas More went to the block in 1535 for refusing to swear. Because the official Church changed with the monarch, heresy changed with it: Mary I burned nearly 300 Protestants between 1555 and 1558, Archbishop Cranmer among them at Oxford in 1556, while Elizabeth I's settlement of 1559 fined those who would not attend her services and raised the penalties on recusants in 1581. After the Gunpowder Plot of 1605 Parliament tightened the law against Catholics again. Belief had become something the criminal law defined.\n\nReligion also shaped the other genuinely new crime, witchcraft, though not alone. The Act of 1563 moved witchcraft into the ordinary courts, and James I, who had published Daemonologie in 1597, approved the Act of 1604 that made calling up an evil spirit a hanging offence even where no harm was done. Puritan conviction fed the hunts Matthew Hopkins ran in East Anglia between 1645 and 1647, in which around a hundred people were executed.\n\nYet the new crime that touched most people owed almost nothing to religion. Vagabondage was defined by economic change: the population grew from roughly two and a half million in 1500 to about four million by 1600, enclosure cut the work available, and prices outran wages. Religion enters only indirectly, through the dissolution of the monasteries between 1536 and 1541, and that was a political and financial act rather than a doctrinal one. The statutes that followed were about order and money: whipping in 1531, branding and slavery in 1547, boring through the ear and the first compulsory poor rate in 1572, parish relief in 1601.\n\nFear of disorder does as much explanatory work as faith. The Game Act of 1671 made hunting a crime for anyone without land worth £100 a year, protecting the gentry's monopoly; highway robbery grew after the Civil Wars and Parliament answered with the rope; by 1688 about fifty offences carried death. None of these has a religious cause at all.\n\nOn balance the statement holds for crimes against authority and only partly for witchcraft, and it fails for the crimes that filled the courts. Religious change was decisive in defining heresy and treason, because only a state that claimed to police belief could make belief criminal, and it supplied the language in which witchcraft was condemned. Where new crimes concerned property, poverty and order, the driving force was social and economic change acting on a propertied class frightened of disorder. The fair judgement is that religion redefined the crimes the state feared most, while economic change defined the crimes the courts actually dealt with most often.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide early what would count as the 'main' reason, because the judgement has to be made on a stated criterion.","Sort the period's new crimes into those with a religious origin and those without before you plan the paragraphs.","Make sure at least one paragraph rests on evidence neither bullet suggests, and let the conclusion divide the verdict rather than sit on the fence."],"codes":["10-2 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Continuity and change in the nature of crimes","10-2 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: New definitions of crime in the sixteenth century"]},{"q":"'The main purpose of punishment in the years c1500 to c1700 was to deter others.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the Gunpowder Plotters • transportation. You must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6 plus up to 4 SPaG. A judgement weighing deterrence against retribution, humiliation and usefulness, with precise evidence from the plotters' execution, the Bloody Code, corporal punishment and transportation, and a criterion stated for the verdict. Maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Deterrence has to be judged by what the authorities chose to make public, and by whether any other purpose explains the punishments they actually used.\n\nThe case for deterrence is strongest wherever authority itself was attacked. The eight surviving Gunpowder Plotters were tried in Westminster Hall in January 1606 and executed in public on 30 and 31 January, four at St Paul's Churchyard and four at Old Palace Yard, in sight of the Parliament they had planned to destroy. The sentence for high treason was theatre: the condemned man was dragged to the scaffold on a hurdle, hanged almost to death, cut down alive, disembowelled and quartered, and his quarters displayed. Catesby and Percy, already shot dead at Holbeche House on 8 November 1605, were dug up so that their heads could be shown too. None of that was needed to make the plotters harmless; it was needed to make an audience remember.\n\nEveryday punishment worked the same way. Whipping was carried out in the street at the cart's tail, the stocks and the pillory exposed the offender to a crowd, branding left a permanent mark such as a V for a vagrant or a T for a thief, and hangings were public occasions. The growth of the Bloody Code fits the same logic: with no police force, the state could not raise the chance of being caught, so it raised the advertised penalty until about fifty offences carried death by 1688.\n\nTransportation pulls the other way. An Act of 1597 allowed persistent vagrants to be banished, and from the 1610s convicts were shipped to the North American colonies, usually for seven or fourteen years. A punishment carried out across the Atlantic could not be watched by anybody in England. Its attraction was practical: it removed offenders cheaply and permanently and gave colonial planters the labour they wanted, and judges could use it instead of hanging people for small thefts.\n\nOther punishments were about retribution or reform rather than deterrence. Burning heretics was justified as defending the true Church and saving souls; the scold's bridle and the ducking stool shamed women in front of their neighbours; houses of correction such as London's Bridewell, opened in 1556, set vagrants to hard labour partly to teach them the habit of work.\n\nThe judgement depends on the criterion. If the question is what the state intended by its most visible punishments, deterrence dominates, because the whole system rested on making punishment public precisely because detection was so unlikely. If the criterion is what happened to the bulk of ordinary offenders by 1700, the answer is more mixed, since transportation removed them quietly and profitably instead. Deterrence was therefore the leading purpose of punishment as display, and remained so throughout the period; but by the end of it deterrence was sharing the ground with punishments chosen because they were useful, which is the beginning of the change that the eighteenth century would carry much further.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Deterrence is about the audience, so ask of each punishment who was meant to see it.","Find the punishment in this period that nobody in England could watch, and let it carry the counter-argument.","Say what would have to be true for deterrence to be 'the main purpose', then test the evidence against that."],"codes":["10-2 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The continued use of corporal and capital punishment","10-2 — 3 Case studies: The Gunpowder Plotters, 1605"]},{"q":"'There was more continuity than change in the way the law was enforced in the years c1500 to c1700.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • parish constables • town watchmen. You must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6 plus up to 4 SPaG. A judgement on continuity and change with a stated criterion: continuity in unpaid community policing against change in the framework — JPs, assizes, the decline of Church justice after 1576 and 1623, paid watchmen from 1663 and rewards from 1692. Maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Continuity and change have to be measured against something. Here the useful test is to separate who actually did the catching from who supervised, paid for and directed the work.\n\nThe case for continuity is strong at the level of the street. There was no police force in 1500 and none in 1700. The hue and cry still bound anyone who witnessed a crime to raise the alarm and their neighbours to join the chase, exactly as in the Middle Ages. The parish constable was an ordinary householder, appointed for a year and unpaid, who arrested suspects, ran the lock-up and brought offenders before a Justice of the Peace. Town watchmen were volunteers taking their turn, patrolling from ten at night until dawn with a lantern and a bell and handing anyone suspicious to the constable. Both jobs were amateur, part-time and widely resented, and in 1700 they were still being done by the same kind of men in the same way. Punishment shows the same steadiness: it remained public and physical throughout, from whipping and the pillory to a sentence for high treason unchanged from the medieval one and used on the Gunpowder Plotters in January 1606.\n\nAgainst that, the framework around the amateurs changed a great deal. Justices of the Peace, created in 1361, were given far more to do: quarter sessions four times a year, the licensing of beggars, the poor rate of 1572 and the parish relief of 1601, with serious cases sent to assize judges riding circuit about twice a year. The Church was pushed out of criminal justice, since from 1576 those who claimed benefit of clergy were tried in the ordinary courts and sanctuary was abolished in 1623, so an offender could no longer escape the king's justice by claiming the Church's.\n\nMoney entering the system is the clearest break of all. From 1663 London had paid night watchmen, the 'Charlies', the first regular paid patrol in the country, and an Act of 1692 offered a £40 reward for taking a highwayman, which created thief-takers who hunted criminals for profit rather than duty. New crimes forced new work too: constables and JPs now dealt with vagrants under the Poor Laws and with witchcraft accusations that the Acts of 1563 and 1604 had placed in the ordinary courts. The fragility of the system is visible when the framework failed: with the assize circuits disrupted by the Civil War, Matthew Hopkins was able to run his hunts across East Anglia between 1645 and 1647 almost unchecked.\n\nJudged by who did the catching, continuity clearly wins, because the community policed itself, unpaid, at the end of the period as at the start. Judged by who supervised and paid for that work, change is the stronger answer, because by 1700 an unpaid constable was operating inside a system of royal courts, statutory duties, paid patrols and cash rewards that barely existed in 1500. The statement is therefore true of the method of enforcement but misleading about its organisation, and it was the organisation that made the eighteenth-century changes possible.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Continuity and change questions are won by choosing a level of the system to judge, so decide that first.","Look above the constable: the people giving him orders changed even when he did not.","The strongest evidence of change involves payment; find the two moments when money entered law enforcement."],"codes":["10-2 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities","10-2 — 3 Case studies: Key individual: Matthew Hopkins and the witch-hunts of 1645–47"]},{"q":"'Local tensions were the most important reason for the intensity of the witch-hunts of 1645 to 1647.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • Matthew Hopkins • the English Civil War. You must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6 plus up to 4 SPaG. A ranked judgement weighing village tensions against Hopkins's paid campaign, the collapse of assize supervision during the war and the Witchcraft Act of 1604, with a criterion for 'most important'. Maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The question is which factor supplied the intensity: the ordinary quarrels that produced accusations, the man who collected them, or the war that let both run unchecked.\n\nLocal tensions explain why there were accusations at all. Those accused in East Anglia were overwhelmingly poor, elderly women, many of them widows living on the charity of neighbours. In a village where a refused loaf was followed by a dead calf or a sick child, an old grievance could be turned into evidence, and the courts accepted the Devil's mark, a familiar such as a cat or a toad, and the testimony of neighbours. The 1640s sharpened those tensions: war taxation, requisitioning by armies, poor harvests and outbreaks of disease left communities looking for someone to blame, and a strongly Puritan region was unusually ready to see the Devil at work in daily misfortune.\n\nBut accusations were made in every decade of the period without producing anything like these three years. What changed was that Matthew Hopkins gave the tensions a method and a market. Styling himself Witchfinder General although he held no commission, he began at Manningtree in Essex in 1645 and worked through Suffolk, Norfolk and Cambridgeshire with John Stearne, searching for marks, watching for familiars, walking the accused and depriving them of sleep until they confessed and named others. Because towns paid him — Stowmarket in Suffolk is recorded as paying £23, more than a labourer earned in a year — each confession brought the next invitation, so the hunt spread along a trail of fees. Around nineteen of the accused were hanged at Chelmsford in the summer of 1645, with further executions at Bury St Edmunds.\n\nThe Civil War is the condition that allowed both. From 1642 the assize circuits in East Anglia were disrupted, so the royal judges who had often dismissed witchcraft evidence were absent and cases went before local magistrates who shared the fears of the accusers. A government fighting for its life had neither the attention nor the machinery to restrain a private witchfinder.\n\nThe law mattered too. The Witchcraft Act of 1604 had made calling up an evil spirit a hanging offence even where nobody was harmed, and had placed such cases in the ordinary courts. Without it Hopkins would have had nothing to charge anyone with, and the tensions would have stayed as gossip.\n\nWeighing these, local tensions were necessary but not sufficient: they had existed for a century and had produced only occasional trials. The decisive combination was a breakdown in legal supervision and a man with a financial interest in mass accusation. Hopkins did not create the fear, but he converted it into perhaps a hundred executions in under three years, more than the previous hundred years had produced. The fair judgement is that local tensions supplied the fuel and the Act of 1604 supplied the charge, the Civil War removed the check, and Hopkins struck the match — which is why the hunts stopped when he did, in 1647.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["'Most important' requires a test: try asking which factor, if removed, would have stopped the hunts.","Separate the long-standing conditions from the short-term trigger, and ask why the same tensions produced so little before 1645.","Bring in the statute that made any of this prosecutable, and let the conclusion rank the factors rather than list them."],"codes":["10-2 — 3 Case studies: Key individual: Matthew Hopkins and the witch-hunts of 1645–47","10-2 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: New definitions of crime in the sixteenth century"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Which Act of 1534 made it treason to deny that Henry VIII was Supreme Head of the Church of England?","a":"The Treason Act of 1534, passed alongside the Act of Supremacy."},{"q":"What punishment did the Witchcraft Act of 1604 set for calling up an evil spirit?","a":"Death by hanging, even where nobody had been harmed."},{"q":"Where, and in which year, did Matthew Hopkins begin his witch-hunts?","a":"At Manningtree in Essex, in 1645."},{"q":"Roughly how many offences carried the death penalty by 1688, and what is that growth called?","a":"About fifty; it is called the beginning of the Bloody Code."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Heresy","back":"Holding religious beliefs the official Church rejected. It was punished by burning at the stake, and what counted as heresy changed with each Tudor monarch's religion.","code":"10-2 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Continuity and change in the nature of crimes","open":false},{"front":"High treason","back":"Plotting against the monarch, or denying the monarch's authority over the Church. Men convicted were hanged, drawn and quartered in public; women convicted of treason were burned.","code":"10-2 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Continuity and change in the nature of crimes","open":false},{"front":"The Act of Supremacy and the Treason Act, 1534","back":"Made Henry VIII Supreme Head of the Church of England and made denying that title treason. Sir Thomas More was beheaded under the Treason Act in 1535.","code":"10-2 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Continuity and change in the nature of crimes","open":false},{"front":"The Game Act, 1671","back":"Restricted hunting game to landowners with property worth £100 a year, making poaching a crime for almost everyone else. Villagers widely treated it as a social crime.","code":"10-2 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Continuity and change in the nature of crimes","open":false},{"front":"Vagabond","back":"An able-bodied person with no work, no master and no settled home. Tudor law treated vagabondage as a crime, blaming idleness rather than unemployment.","code":"10-2 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: New definitions of crime in the sixteenth century","open":false},{"front":"The Vagrancy Act, 1547","back":"Allowed an able-bodied vagrant who refused work to be branded with a V and made a slave for two years. It proved so harsh that it was repealed in 1550.","code":"10-2 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: New definitions of crime in the sixteenth century","open":false},{"front":"The Elizabethan Poor Law, 1601","back":"Made every parish responsible for its own poor through a compulsory poor rate, separating the 'deserving' poor, who received relief, from the 'undeserving', who were punished.","code":"10-2 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities","open":false},{"front":"The Witchcraft Act, 1604","back":"Approved by James I. Made calling up an evil spirit a hanging offence even where no harm was done, and left witchcraft cases to the ordinary courts rather than the Church.","code":"10-2 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: New definitions of crime in the sixteenth century","open":false},{"front":"The parish constable","back":"An unpaid householder appointed for one year who arrested suspects, ran the lock-up and brought offenders before a Justice of the Peace. Policing stayed part-time and amateur.","code":"10-2 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities","open":false},{"front":"Town watchmen","back":"Unpaid volunteers who patrolled a town from ten at night until dawn with a lantern and a bell, reporting to the constable. London gained paid watchmen, the 'Charlies', from 1663.","code":"10-2 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities","open":false},{"front":"Justices of the Peace","back":"Local gentlemen, in place since 1361, who tried lesser crimes at quarter sessions four times a year, licensed beggars and supervised the poor rate. Serious cases went to the assizes.","code":"10-2 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities","open":false},{"front":"The Bloody Code","back":"The name given to the growing list of offences punished by death: about fifty by 1688. With no police force, the state tried to deter crime by advertising savage penalties.","code":"10-2 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The continued use of corporal and capital punishment","open":false},{"front":"Transportation","back":"Sending convicts to the North American colonies from the 1610s, usually for seven or fourteen years. It removed offenders cheaply and supplied colonial planters with labour.","code":"10-2 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The continued use of corporal and capital punishment","open":false},{"front":"The Gunpowder Plot, 5 November 1605","back":"Thirteen Catholics led by Robert Catesby planned to blow up the House of Lords and kill James I. Guy Fawkes was arrested with thirty-six barrels of gunpowder beneath it.","code":"10-2 — 3 Case studies: The Gunpowder Plotters, 1605","open":false},{"front":"One reason the witch-hunts of 1645 to 1647 were so intense","back":"Matthew Hopkins was paid by the towns that invited him; the Civil War disrupted the assize circuits so sceptical royal judges were absent; war taxation, poor harvests and disease left communities looking for someone to blame.","code":"10-2 — 3 Case studies: Key individual: Matthew Hopkins and the witch-hunts of 1645–47","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 1 Question 5, 16 marks)","items":["Question: ''Punishment in the years c1500 to c1700 changed very little from punishment in the years c1000 to c1500.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.' You may use the following in your answer: • the pillory • houses of correction. You must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]. This is one paragraph from the middle of the essay.","The everyday punishments of 1650 would have been recognisable to anyone alive in 1350. The pillory was already a fixture of English justice under the Assize of Bread and Ale of 1266, which set a baker who cheated his customers in it, and it was still standing in market places four centuries later, beside the stocks and the whipping post. By the later Middle Ages hanging was the standard end of a convicted felon, and it still was in 1700; in both periods it was done in the open, before a crowd, by the sheriff's men. Gaols existed throughout, and in neither period were they the punishment: they held people waiting for the judge or waiting to pay a debt. The continuity is there because the purposes had not moved. Punishment was meant to hurt the offender, to satisfy the community and above all to be seen, since in neither period could the authorities catch offenders reliably enough to rely on the risk of detection. Against that, two genuinely new things appear after c1500: houses of correction, from the Bridewell in London in the 1550s and then in county after county under the Act of 1576, set the idle poor to hard labour rather than simply hurting them, and transportation to the North American colonies from the 1610s removed an offender for seven or fourteen years without killing him. Measured by the methods a spectator would have seen used on a market day, the change between the two periods is slight; measured by the choices open to a judge passing sentence, the early modern courts had two that no medieval court had.","Why this reaches Level 4: the explanation is directed consistently at the conceptual focus, which is continuity and change in punishment, not at describing punishments; the information is precisely selected (the Assize of Bread and Ale of 1266, the Bridewell and the Act of 1576, transportation from the 1610s for seven or fourteen years) rather than sprayed; the reasoning is sustained from the claim through the reason for the continuity to the counter-evidence; and the closing sentence applies a stated criterion — what a spectator saw against what a judge could choose — instead of asserting a verdict. It also moves beyond the stimulus by bringing in hanging, the use of gaols and transportation."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: ''Punishment in the years c1500 to c1700 changed very little from punishment in the years c1000 to c1500.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.' You may use the following in your answer: • the pillory • houses of correction. You must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]. The same paragraph written by a weaker candidate.","Punishment did not really change very much between the medieval period and the years c1500 to c1700. People were still hanged for serious crimes and they were still put in the pillory in front of everybody, which was meant to shame them and to warn other people not to do the same. Later on there were houses of correction where poor people who would not work were made to do hard labour, and some criminals were sent away to the colonies instead of being hanged. This shows that punishment in the years c1500 to c1700 was mostly the same as it had been before.","Why this is Level 2: it meets 'an explanation is given showing limited analysis and with implicit or unsustained links to the conceptual focus of the question' and 'accurate and relevant information is included, showing some knowledge and understanding of the period', and the overall judgement is 'given but its justification is asserted'. It misses Level 3's 'a line of reasoning that is generally sustained' because each sentence starts again, and misses Level 4's 'accurate and relevant information is precisely selected' because there is no date, statute or place anywhere in it. Houses of correction and transportation are described as though they too were continuity when in fact they are the strongest evidence of change, so no criterion is applied. It also uses little beyond the two stimulus points, which no longer holds it down inside Level 2 — a Level 2 answer confined to the stimulus can still reach the top of the level at 8 marks — but it is what would cap the answer at 11 and shut it out of Level 4 once the reasoning improved."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: continuity and change in the nature of crime, c1500 to c1700","items":["Petty theft of food, clothing, tools and livestock remained the commonest crime, filling the quarter sessions and the assizes throughout the period.","Crimes against the person — assault, murder, infanticide — continued much as before and were tried in the same courts.","Heresy meant holding beliefs the official Church rejected; the punishment was burning at the stake.","High treason meant plotting against the monarch or denying the monarch's authority over the Church; men convicted were hanged, drawn and quartered.","Act of Supremacy, 1534: Henry VIII became Supreme Head of the Church of England; the Treason Act of the same year made denying it treason.","Sir Thomas More, Lord Chancellor until 1532, was beheaded in 1535 for refusing to accept the royal supremacy.","Mary I (1553 to 1558) restored Catholicism and had nearly 300 Protestants burned as heretics, including Archbishop Thomas Cranmer at Oxford in 1556.","Elizabeth I's religious settlement of 1559 fined those who stayed away from church; the penalties on persistent recusants were raised sharply in 1581.","The last people burned for heresy in England died in 1612; after that, treason rather than heresy was the state's weapon against religious dissent.","Highway robbery grew after 1650, as more people travelled with money, roads and coach services improved, and soldiers were demobilised after the Civil Wars.","The Game Act of 1671 restricted hunting game to landowners with property worth £100 a year, criminalising poaching for almost everyone else.","Smuggling spread in the later seventeenth century as import duties rose; like poaching, it was widely regarded as a social crime rather than a real one.","Vagabondage and witchcraft were the period's genuinely new crimes; almost everything else was an old behaviour punished in an old way."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: vagabondage and the laws against it","items":["England's population grew from roughly two and a half million in 1500 to about four million by 1600, outstripping the work available.","Enclosure of open fields for sheep farming cut the number of labourers a village needed and removed common rights.","The dissolution of the monasteries, 1536 to 1541, removed the main source of charity, shelter and care for the poor.","Debasement of the coinage under Henry VIII and steadily rising prices cut the value of wages.","Bad harvests, especially in the 1590s, pushed whole families onto the roads in search of work and alms.","The propertied classes feared vagrants as carriers of plague, theft and rebellion, and pamphlets described organised gangs of 'sturdy beggars' who chose idleness.","Vagabonds Act, 1531: the old and sick were licensed to beg; able-bodied beggars were whipped and sent back to their home parish.","Vagrancy Act, 1547: an able-bodied vagrant who refused work could be branded with a V and made a slave for two years; repealed in 1550 as unworkable.","Vagabonds Act, 1572: vagrants were whipped and bored through the ear, and the first compulsory local poor rate was created.","Poor Relief Act, 1576: towns had to supply wool or other materials so the able-bodied poor could work, with houses of correction for those who refused.","Elizabethan Poor Law, 1601: each parish became responsible for its own poor, distinguishing the 'deserving' from the 'undeserving'.","Settlement Act, 1662: parishes could remove newcomers and send them back to the parish they belonged to.","London's Bridewell, opened in 1556, gave its name to the houses of correction where vagrants and petty offenders were set to hard labour."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: witchcraft as a crime","items":["Witchcraft Act, 1542: witchcraft became a felony punishable by death; the Act was repealed in 1547.","Witchcraft Act, 1563: witchcraft cases moved from Church courts to the ordinary courts, and causing death by witchcraft became a hanging offence.","James VI of Scotland, later James I of England, published Daemonologie in 1597, arguing that witches served the Devil.","Witchcraft Act, 1604: calling up an evil spirit became a hanging offence even where nobody had been harmed.","In England convicted witches were hanged, not burned; burning was reserved for heresy and for petty treason, such as a wife who killed her husband.","The accused were usually poor, older women, often widows, who depended on the charity of neighbours.","Evidence accepted in court included the Devil's mark on the body, familiars such as cats and toads, and the testimony of neighbours.","The swimming test assumed that pure water would reject a witch, so floating counted as proof of guilt.","Because cases were heard in the ordinary courts, a conviction was followed by the sheriff and the gallows rather than by Church penance.","Religious division, the upheaval of the Civil War, poor harvests and the spread of printed accounts all helped keep accusations alive.","Parliament abolished the crime of witchcraft in the eighteenth century; since 1951 the law has treated claims of magical power only as possible fraud."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: law enforcement, c1500 to c1700","items":["There was no police force at any point in the period; enforcement was local, part-time and unpaid.","The hue and cry continued: a witness had to raise the alarm and the community had to join the chase.","The parish constable was an unpaid householder appointed for a year, who arrested suspects and brought them before a Justice of the Peace.","Town watchmen were unpaid volunteers who patrolled from ten at night until dawn with a lantern and a bell and reported to the constable.","Justices of the Peace, in place since 1361, met at quarter sessions four times a year to try lesser crimes and to administer the Poor Laws.","Serious cases went to the assizes, held about twice a year in each county before royal judges riding a circuit.","The sheriff could raise the posse comitatus, the men of the county, to hunt a suspect.","The Church lost its hold on justice: those claiming benefit of clergy were tried in the ordinary courts from 1576, and sanctuary was abolished in 1623.","From 1663 London had paid night watchmen, nicknamed the 'Charlies' after Charles II.","An Act of 1692 offered a £40 reward for the capture of a highwayman, creating thief-takers who hunted criminals for profit.","Where the framework failed, as when the Civil War disrupted the assize circuits, private witchfinders such as Matthew Hopkins could operate almost unchecked."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: punishment, c1500 to c1700","items":["Punishment was public: whipping at the cart's tail, the stocks, the pillory and the gallows were all staged in front of a crowd.","The aims were retribution, deterrence and humiliation; reforming the offender was not yet an aim of the criminal law.","Branding marked an offender permanently, such as a V for a vagrant or a T for a thief.","The scold's bridle and the ducking stool were used to shame women accused of gossip or nagging.","Hanging was the standard punishment for felony, including theft above a small value.","Burning was the punishment for heresy, and for petty treason such as a wife who killed her husband.","High treason was punished by hanging, drawing and quartering, with the quarters displayed in public places.","By 1688 about fifty offences carried the death penalty; this growth is known as the beginning of the Bloody Code.","An Act of 1597 allowed persistent vagrants to be banished from the realm, the first step towards transportation.","From the 1610s convicts were transported to the North American colonies, usually for seven or fourteen years, to work on tobacco and other plantations.","Transportation gave the courts a punishment between whipping and hanging, removed offenders cheaply and supplied colonial planters with labour.","Houses of correction set vagrants and petty offenders to hard labour, but gaols were still used mainly to hold people before trial or for debt."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study fact file: the Gunpowder Plotters, 1605","items":["Thirteen Catholics, mostly gentry, plotted to blow up the House of Lords at the State Opening of Parliament on 5 November 1605.","Robert Catesby led the plot; Guy Fawkes, a soldier who had fought for Spain in the Netherlands, was to set off the gunpowder.","The aim was to kill James I and his ministers and to put his daughter Princess Elizabeth on the throne as a Catholic queen.","Thirty-six barrels of gunpowder were stored in a cellar beneath the House of Lords.","An anonymous letter warned Lord Monteagle to stay away from Parliament in late October 1605, and the cellars were searched.","Fawkes was arrested during the search in the early hours of 5 November 1605 and gave the false name John Johnson.","James I authorised torture; Fawkes was questioned in the Tower of London and signed a confession naming the others.","Catesby and Thomas Percy were shot dead when the sheriff's men surrounded Holbeche House in Staffordshire on 8 November 1605.","The eight surviving plotters were tried in Westminster Hall in January 1606 and convicted of high treason.","They were hanged, drawn and quartered in public on 30 and 31 January 1606, four at St Paul's Churchyard and four at Old Palace Yard beside Parliament.","The Jesuit Henry Garnet was executed in 1606 for having known of the plot and said nothing.","Parliament ordered that 5 November be marked each year by a church service, and new laws tightened the penalties on Catholics.","Use the case study as evidence of how crimes against authority were defined, investigated and punished, not as a story to retell."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study fact file: Matthew Hopkins and the witch-hunts of 1645 to 1647","items":["Matthew Hopkins called himself the Witchfinder General although he held no official commission from Parliament or the Crown.","His hunts ran from 1645 to 1647 across Essex, Suffolk, Norfolk and Cambridgeshire.","They began at Manningtree in Essex in 1645, where he claimed to have overheard women speaking of the Devil.","He worked with John Stearne and with women searchers who examined the accused for the Devil's mark.","His methods included pricking the skin, watching the accused for familiars, walking them up and down and depriving them of sleep until they confessed.","He used the swimming test, in which the accused was tied and put into water, floating being taken as proof of guilt.","Towns paid him for his services: Stowmarket in Suffolk is recorded as paying £23, far more than a labourer could earn in a year.","Around nineteen of the accused were hanged at Chelmsford in the summer of 1645, and further executions followed at Bury St Edmunds.","Historians estimate that about a hundred people were executed during his campaign, more than in the previous century.","The Civil War had disrupted the assize circuits, so accusations were heard by local magistrates rather than by sceptical royal judges.","East Anglia was strongly Puritan, and war taxation, requisitioning, poor harvests and disease left communities looking for someone to blame.","The Witchcraft Act of 1604 supplied the charge, making it a hanging offence to call up an evil spirit even where nobody had been harmed.","Hopkins published The Discovery of Witches in 1647 defending his methods, and died in the same year; the hunts ended with him."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 1 Question 5: 'Fear of disorder was the main reason for changes in crime and punishment in the years c1500 to c1700.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","items":["The stem as printed: ''Fear of disorder was the main reason for changes in crime and punishment in the years c1500 to c1700.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.' You may use the following in your answer: • vagabondage • the Bloody Code. You must also use information of your own.","Decoding it: the concept is causation across two centuries. 'Fear of disorder' means the anxiety of the propertied and of the Crown that the poor, the masterless and the religiously disloyal would riot or rebel. You must decide whether that fear, rather than religious change, economic change or opportunity abroad, drove the period's changes — and you must say on what criterion you decide.","Introduction (three or four sentences): state the criterion you will judge by — which reason explains changes across both halves of the topic, the definition of crime and the practice of punishment — and signal the line of argument you will end with.","Paragraph 1, the named factor in the definition of crime: fear of disorder created vagabondage as a crime. Evidence: population growth from roughly two and a half million in 1500 to about four million by 1600; enclosure; the pamphlets about 'sturdy beggars'; the Act of 1531 whipping able-bodied beggars, the Act of 1547 with branding and slavery, the Act of 1572 boring through the ear. Interim judgement: the behaviour was old, and only the fear was new.","Paragraph 2, the named factor in punishment: fear of disorder drove the Bloody Code. Evidence: a Parliament of property owners; highway robbery after the Civil Wars; about fifty capital offences by 1688; punishment kept public because there was no police force to catch anyone. Interim judgement: the state raised the advertised penalty because it could not raise the risk of detection.","Paragraph 3, the rival factor of religious and political change: the Act of Supremacy and the Treason Act of 1534; nearly 300 Protestants burned under Mary I between 1555 and 1558; Elizabeth's settlement of 1559; the Witchcraft Act of 1604; the execution of the Gunpowder Plotters on 30 and 31 January 1606. Interim judgement: here the driver is the Crown's claim to define belief, not simply fear of the crowd — although the Plot shows the two joined together.","Paragraph 4, the point beyond the stimulus: opportunity and usefulness. Transportation to the North American colonies from the 1610s, for seven or fourteen years, after the banishment Act of 1597; colonies needed labour and the courts needed a penalty between whipping and hanging. Paid watchmen in London from 1663 and the £40 reward of 1692 show the same practical motive. Interim judgement: this change happened because it paid, not because anybody was frightened.","The evaluation thread that must run through every paragraph: each interim judgement answers 'how far', not 'what happened', and each refers back to the criterion set in the introduction, so the essay argues rather than lists.","Timing: about 27 minutes of the 80. Five minutes planning the four paragraphs and their evidence, twenty writing, two rereading for spelling, punctuation and specialist terms, since those carry four separate marks.","What the conclusion must say: a qualified verdict with the criterion visible. For example, that fear of disorder is the best single explanation of how ordinary crime was defined and punished, because it accounts for both the vagrancy statutes and the growth of capital offences; that it cannot account for the redefinition of heresy and treason, which followed the Crown's religious claims; and that transportation shows a third motive of usefulness emerging by 1700 — so the statement is largely true of punishment and of crimes of poverty, and much weaker for crimes against authority."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:47:42.914266Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Judged afresh after the dedupe retarget: no answer key is wrong, the five retargeted worked examples and the new Level 4/Level 2 model paragraph pair set questions no bank item sets, and dupscan prints 'no duplication'; I checked about ninety dates, statutes, figures and events, and the load-bearing new ones on the web — the 1658 Mercurius Politicus advertisement for London coaches to Exeter, York and Chester, the first turnpike Act of 1663 for the Great North Road from Wadesmill to Stilton¹, the Popish Recusants Act 1605 (3 Jas. 1 c. 4: oath of allegiance denying the Pope's deposing power, bar on practising law and medicine and on acting as guardian or trustee, magistrates' search for arms, £60 or two-thirds of lands for not receiving communion), the Observance of 5th November Act 1605 requiring the annual thanksgiving service at which the Act itself was read aloud, Henry Garnet's execution on 3 May 1606, the Assize of Clarendon of 1166 (twelve lawful men of each hundred) and its justices on circuit, the Benefit of Clergy Act of 1576 ending delivery to the Ordinary, the pillory for a cheating baker under the Assize of Bread and Ale of 1266² and Bridewell taken over by the City in 1556 — all held up, as did the earlier spine (Treason Act 1534 and More 1535, Mary I's near-300 burnings 1555-58, the vagrancy statutes 1531/1547-repealed-1550/1572/1576/1597/1601, Witchcraft Acts 1542, 1563 and 1604 with Daemonologie 1597, Game Act 1671, sanctuary 1623, paid watchmen 1663, the £40 reward of 1692, about fifty capital offences by 1688, transportation from the 1610s, the Plot's 36 barrels, Holbeche 8 November 1605 and the executions of 30-31 January 1606, Hopkins at Manningtree 1645, Stowmarket's £23, nineteen hanged at Chelmsford and his death in 1647). Caps and tariffs are the current ones throughout (maximum 8 and no Level 4 at 12 marks, maximum 11 and no Level 4 at 16 marks, no Level 2 cap anywhere, Paper 1 at 80 minutes); the only figure to flag for students is the 'about a hundred' executions of 1645-47, honestly hedged as an estimate but smaller than the near-300 some textbooks print."}}},"10-3":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"10-3","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 3 is where Option 10 turns. Almost every long-run change in the thematic study happens between c1700 and c1900: the Bloody Code climbs to its height and is then dismantled, transportation opens and closes, the unpaid parish constable gives way to a paid uniformed policeman, execution leaves the street for the prison yard, and prison itself stops being a place to hold people before punishment and becomes the punishment. Because so much moves in two centuries, this is the period the thematic questions keep returning to, and the period you will be asked to set beside c1000-c1700 or c1900-present. Section B never rewards retelling the story. It asks you to explain one similarity or difference across the centuries, to explain why a change happened as a chain of causes rather than a list of events, and to weigh a claim about change, causation or significance and say how far you agree. All three depend on the same thing: dated, named evidence you can deploy quickly and then argue with. This room stocks that evidence - the acts, the individuals, the two case studies - and trains the moves that turn it into an argument.","idea":[{"h":"The crimes of the age: highway robbery, poaching and smuggling","t":"Eighteenth-century crime looks dramatic but most of it was ordinary theft, as it had been since c1000. What made it look new was opportunity. Highway robbery flourished because travellers carried coin on unlit roads with no bank to deposit it in, and it was a capital offence; Dick Turpin, the most famous highwayman of all, was hanged at York in 1739, though for horse theft rather than for robbery on the road. It then vanished: turnpike roads grew busier, the Bow Street Horse Patrol watched the approaches to London from 1805, banknotes replaced coin, and from the 1830s the railway removed the lonely stagecoach. The last recorded highway robbery in England was in 1831. Poaching was made criminal by the property laws of the rich: the Game Act of 1671 reserved hunting to men with substantial land, the Black Act of 1723 made armed or disguised poaching a capital crime, and the Poaching Act of 1816 allowed transportation for seven years for taking game at night with a net. Smuggling existed because duties on tea, brandy and silk were enormous; the Hawkhurst Gang ran Kent and Sussex in the 1730s and 1740s until its leaders were hanged at the end of the decade. Both were social crimes: whole communities refused to condemn them. A top-level answer uses these three to show that crime followed opportunity and taxation, and that the law made and unmade crimes as often as behaviour changed.","code":"10-3 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Continuity and change in the nature of crimes"},{"h":"What counts as a crime: the end of witchcraft trials and the Tolpuddle Martyrs","t":"Definitions of crime moved with ideas and with political fear, and the two examples the specification names pull in opposite directions. Witchcraft had been a capital offence under statute since the sixteenth century, and the East Anglian hunt run by Matthew Hopkins in 1645-47 sent scores to the gallows. By the early eighteenth century educated magistrates and juries would no longer accept spectral evidence: Jane Wenham, convicted at Hertford in 1712, was pardoned, and the Witchcraft Act of 1735 swept the old statutes away, leaving only the offence of pretending to have magical powers, punished by up to a year's imprisonment with quarterly appearances in the pillory. That is decriminalisation driven by the Royal Society and Enlightenment thinking. The Tolpuddle case of 1834 runs the other way. Trade unions had been legal since the Combination Acts were repealed in 1824, but a government frightened by the Swing Riots of 1830 reached for the Unlawful Oaths Act of 1797 and transported six Dorset labourers led by George Loveless for seven years for swearing a secret oath. Mass protest forced a pardon in 1836. A top-level answer uses the pair to argue that what counts as crime is a political decision, and that the direction of travel is not always towards leniency.","code":"10-3 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Changing definitions of crime exemplified in the ending of witchcraft prosecutions"},{"h":"From the community to the constable: the Fielding brothers, police forces and the CID","t":"In 1700 catching criminals was still a community duty: the hue and cry, the unpaid parish constable and the night watchman, with corrupt thief-takers filling the gap until Jonathan Wild was hanged in 1725. The change began in London, where the old system was overwhelmed by growth. Henry Fielding, magistrate at Bow Street from 1748, set up a small paid force of constables in 1749, the Bow Street Runners, and used his Covent Garden Journal from 1752 to circulate descriptions of wanted men; his blind half-brother Sir John Fielding ran Bow Street until 1780, founded a horse patrol in 1763 and built a register of known criminals. They proved a paid, organised force could work. Government then acted from fear of disorder after the Gordon Riots of 1780 and Peterloo in 1819: the Metropolitan Police Act of 1829 created a uniformed force under commissioners Rowan and Mayne, about 1,000 constables when patrols began in September 1829 and about 3,300 men by May 1830. The Municipal Corporations Act of 1835 required boroughs to appoint watch committees, which were to appoint constables, though compliance was patchy, and the County Police Act of 1839 only allowed counties to act; the County and Borough Police Act of 1856 made forces compulsory everywhere, inspected and grant-aided. Detection followed patrol: eight detectives at Scotland Yard in 1842 became the CID under Howard Vincent in 1878. A top-level answer traces that as a shift from amateur and local to professional and national, and dates each step.","code":"10-3 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities"},{"h":"Why punish? From deterrence and retribution to reform","t":"For most of the eighteenth century the purpose of punishment was deterrence by terror. Capital offences rose from about fifty in 1688 to over two hundred by 1820, and executions were public spectacles, moved from Tyburn to Newgate in 1783. The system failed on its own terms: crowds were disorderly, pickpockets worked them, juries practised pious perjury by undervaluing stolen goods to spare the accused, and crime did not fall. Beccaria's argument of 1764 that certainty matters more than savagery, Samuel Romilly's campaign that ended hanging for pickpocketing in 1808, and Peel's reforms from 1823 stripped the Bloody Code down, until only five offences carried death by 1861 and the Capital Punishment Amendment Act of 1868 took execution behind the walls. Transportation carried the middle ground - to America until 1776, to Australia from 1787, about 160,000 convicts - until the colonies objected, the 1851 gold rush made it look like a reward, and the Penal Servitude Act of 1853 replaced it; the last convict ship reached Western Australia in 1868. Meanwhile John Howard's survey of 1777 and Elizabeth Fry's work in Newgate from 1817 argued that prisoners could be reformed. A top-level answer shows the purposes overlapping rather than replacing each other, and notes the swing back to hard labour after the garotting panic of 1862.","code":"10-3 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: Changing views on the purpose of punishment"},{"h":"Case study: Pentonville prison and the separate system","t":"Pentonville is the case study that proves prison had become the punishment. It was built between 1840 and 1842 to Joshua Jebb's design with 520 single cells, each about thirteen feet by seven, each with its own basin and water closet so a prisoner never needed to leave it. It was built because transportation was closing, because the Bloody Code had been dismantled and something had to take its place, and because reformers had won the argument that criminals could be changed. The separate system delivered that in its purest form: prisoners were alone in their cells, picked oakum or worked a crank, wore masks when moved, sat in separate stalls in chapel and were known by a number, so that they would reflect on their crimes and could not corrupt each other. Its strengths were real - clean, healthy, orderly, individually supervised, with instruction and work - and fifty-four prisons were built on its plan within six years. Its weakness was the isolation itself: rates of mental breakdown and suicide were far higher than in other prisons, and critics said it produced broken men rather than reformed ones. A top-level answer uses Pentonville on both sides of a judgement: the clearest sign that reform had triumphed in theory, and the clearest evidence that it had not in practice.","code":"10-3 — 3 Case studies: Pentonville prison in the mid nineteenth century"},{"h":"Key individual: Robert Peel","t":"Peel is the individual the examiner most often asks you to weigh. As Home Secretary from 1822 to 1827 and again from 1828 to 1830 he worked on both halves of the system. On punishment, the Gaols Act of 1823 ordered magistrates to inspect gaols, gaolers to be paid a salary rather than live on fees, women warders for women prisoners and chaplains to visit, although it covered only the larger town gaols and had no inspectors until 1835; the Judgement of Death Act of 1823 let judges pass a lesser sentence in place of hanging, and his consolidation of the criminal law removed the death penalty from more than a hundred offences. On law enforcement, the Metropolitan Police Act of 1829 created the first large paid, uniformed, civilian force, deliberately unarmed except for truncheons and deliberately not the army. Peelers were resented at first: a constable was killed at Cold Bath Fields in 1833 and the coroner's jury called it justifiable homicide. But the model spread: the act of 1835 required boroughs to appoint watch committees, which were to appoint constables, the act of 1839 allowed counties to follow, and the act of 1856 made forces compulsory everywhere, inspected and grant-aided. A top-level answer credits Peel with turning existing arguments into statute, and then asks how much was his and how much was Howard, Fry, the Fieldings and the pressure of urban growth.","code":"10-3 — 3 Case studies: Key individual: Robert Peel"}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 3: one difference in the Church's part in punishment","setup":"The question is: 'Explain one way in which the influence of the Church on the punishment of criminals in the years c1000-c1700 was different from its influence in the years c1700-c1900.' It carries 4 marks and is marked on two strands, analysis of the difference and specific information from both periods. From your notes you can call on: in the earlier period an accused person could claim sanctuary in a church rather than stand trial, and benefit of clergy let anyone who could recite the neck verse, Psalm 51, be handed over to the church courts, which did not hang; a statute of 1489 had lay claimants branded on the thumb so that the escape could be used only once; sanctuary in criminal cases was abolished by statute in 1623; the reading test for benefit of clergy went in 1706, leaving the privilege as a single escape for first offenders, and the Criminal Law Act of 1827 removed it altogether; and in the later period Peel's Gaols Act of 1823 required chaplains to visit gaols, while Pentonville sat its prisoners in separate stalls in chapel and gave them religious instruction in their cells.","steps":["Name the difference as a change in the kind of influence, not as two stories: in the earlier period the Church could take an accused man out of the king's justice altogether, while in the later period it worked inside a punishment system the state ran.","Support the earlier period from its own machinery: sanctuary let a suspect escape trial by claiming the protection of a church, and benefit of clergy sent a convicted man to courts that could not pass a death sentence, which is why branding on the thumb was introduced to stop him claiming it twice.","Support the later period with the opposite evidence: both escapes had been closed, sanctuary by the statute of 1623 and benefit of clergy finally in 1827, and what the Church now supplied was the chaplain the Gaols Act required and the chapel stalls and cell instruction at Pentonville.","Analyse rather than list: say that the difference is one of authority, because a state that ran its own courts, its own gaols and, from 1829, its own police no longer had to share jurisdiction with the Church, and could use religion as an instrument of reform instead."],"cues":["Is the difference about how severe the Church was, or about how much authority it held?","Which two routes let the Church keep a man from the gallows?","What was left of the Church's role once those routes were closed?","Why could the state afford to close them by the nineteenth century?"]},{"title":"Question 4: getting past the two bullets on a 12-mark explanation","setup":"The question is: 'Explain why highway robbery had died out by the middle of the nineteenth century. You may use the following in your answer: • turnpike roads • the railways. You must also use information of your own.' It carries 12 marks, and the mark scheme caps at 8 marks any answer that does not address three or more aspects of content, with no access to Level 4. From your notes: highway robbery flourished because travellers carried coin across unlit heaths with no bank to leave it in, and it was a capital offence; Dick Turpin was hanged at York in 1739, though for horse theft rather than for robbery on the road; by the 1830s more than a thousand turnpike trusts kept the main roads gated, tolled and busy; the Bow Street Horse Patrol, revived in 1805, rode the approaches to London in uniform; banknotes and bills of exchange were replacing gold in travellers' pockets; the railway took the stagecoach off the long-distance roads from the 1830s; and the last recorded robbery by a mounted highwayman in England was in 1831.","steps":["Fix the conceptual focus before writing a word: the question asks why a crime disappeared, so every paragraph has to end in a reason it became harder, less profitable or no longer possible, not in a description of highwaymen.","Turn the first bullet into a cause rather than a scene: a turnpike road with toll gates, keepers and constant traffic left a robber nowhere quiet to work and witnesses at every stop, so the open heath that had made the crime possible was closed off.","Turn the second bullet into a different kind of cause: the railway removed the target altogether, because a trainload of passengers behind locked doors at speed could not be stopped by one man on a horse as a stagecoach could.","Add the reasons of your own that the bullets never prompt: the revived Bow Street Horse Patrol put uniformed mounted men on the very roads highwaymen used, and banknotes and bills of exchange meant the traveller no longer carried gold, so the risk rose while the reward fell.","Close by ranking the causes rather than listing them: say which did the most and why, for instance that patrols and paper money had already made the crime unprofitable before the railway finished it off."],"cues":["What is the question asking: what highwaymen did, or why they stopped?","What does a toll gate do to a robber's escape?","Why is a train a harder target than a coach?","Which two changes made the crime riskier and less rewarding at the same time?","Does your last sentence rank the causes, or repeat them?"]},{"title":"Question 5 or 6: weighing the law against social change","setup":"The question is: 'The law did more than social change to decide what counted as a crime in the years c1700-c1900.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • witchcraft • poaching. You must also use information of your own. It carries 16 marks with up to 4 additional marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology. From your notes: the Witchcraft Act of 1735 swept the old statutes away and left only the offence of pretending to have magical powers; the Game Act of 1671 reserved hunting to men with substantial land, the Black Act of 1723 made armed or disguised poaching a capital crime and the Poaching Act of 1816 allowed transportation for seven years for taking game at night with a net; the duty on tea was cut by Pitt's Commutation Act of 1784 and smuggling shrank with it; trade unions had been legal since the Combination Acts were repealed in 1824, yet six Dorset labourers were transported in 1834 under the Unlawful Oaths Act of 1797 and pardoned two years after their trial; and the population of England and Wales more than trebled between 1801 and 1901.","steps":["Announce the criterion in the opening: deciding what counted as a crime means deciding what a court could punish, so the test in each case is whether the statute came first or only wrote down a change that had already happened.","Build the case for the statement on the first bullet: witchcraft stopped being a crime in 1735 because Parliament said so, and the same Parliament invented poaching offences that had not existed before, which is the law making and unmaking crimes by itself.","Put the strongest case against, using the same two bullets differently: the Game Act and the Black Act were the work of a landowning class protecting newly enclosed estates and game, so the statute was the instrument of a social change rather than its cause.","Bring in the point beyond the bullets that decides the question: industrial towns trebled the population and concentrated theft, drunkenness and disorder without any new statute, the railway killed highway robbery without one, and Pitt's cut in the tea duty shrank smuggling by removing the profit rather than by defining an offence.","Judge against the criterion you announced: say whether the law decided what counted as a crime or mostly recorded decisions taken elsewhere, and name the one case that would have to be explained away for your judgement to fail."],"cues":["What would have to be true for the law to be the deciding factor?","Which single act removed a crime from the statute book?","Who gained from the game laws, and what had just happened to the land?","Which changes in crime happened without Parliament doing anything?","Which case cuts hardest against your judgement?"]},{"title":"Judging what prison had become by 1900","setup":"You are writing one paragraph inside the question: 'By 1900 prison had become a place of reform rather than a place of punishment.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. From your notes: Pentonville was built between 1840 and 1842 to Joshua Jebb's design with 520 single cells, and fifty-four prisons were built on its plan in the six years that followed; the separate system kept prisoners alone, working at oakum or the crank, masked when moved and in separate stalls in chapel; after the garotting panic of 1862 the Prisons Act of 1865 imposed hard labour, hard fare and hard board; the Prison Act of 1877 brought every prison in the country under the Home Office; the departmental committee chaired by Herbert Gladstone reported in 1895 that deterrence and reformation should be the primary and concurrent objects of prison discipline; and the Prison Act of 1898 that followed phased out the crank and the treadwheel, limited how long a prisoner could be held in separate confinement and divided prisoners into classes.","steps":["Open the paragraph with the claim in the concept the question names: by the end of the century the official purpose of prison was no longer terror alone, because the Gladstone committee had written reformation into it alongside deterrence.","Support it with what changed inside the walls: the act of 1898 phased out the crank and the treadwheel, capped the time a prisoner could be kept in separate confinement and sorted prisoners into classes instead of treating every one of them alike.","Show the scale of the change, since the act of 1877 had already put every prison under the Home Office, so a rule made at the centre now reached every cell in the country rather than one county's gaol.","Turn the paragraph with the evidence against: the regime being dismantled was barely thirty years old, imposed after the garotting panic by the act of 1865, and the committee's own words kept deterrence as a concurrent object rather than dropping it.","End with a mini-judgement that ranks rather than balances: prison was now expected to do both jobs, which is a real change from the hard labour and hard board of the previous generation, but reform had been added to punishment and had not replaced it."],"cues":["What did the official purpose of prison become, and who said so?","Which changes inside the prison prove the purpose had shifted?","How do you show this reached beyond one prison?","What evidence cuts against the claim?","What does your last sentence have to decide?"]},{"title":"Question 3: a similarity in the way gaols were run","setup":"The question is: 'Explain one way in which the use of prisons in the years c1000-c1700 was similar to the use of prisons in the years c1700-c1900.' It carries 4 marks and needs one similarity analysed, with specific information from each period. From your notes: in the earlier period castle and town gaols held the accused until the judges came round on circuit and held debtors until they paid, and the keeper drew no salary but charged the prisoner for food, bedding and the striking off of irons; in the later period John Howard's survey of 1777 found acquitted prisoners still held until they had paid the gaoler's fees, with gaol fever rife, the acts of 1774 ordering the gaols cleaned and the fees of acquitted prisoners abolished were widely ignored for want of inspectors, Peel's Gaols Act of 1823 ordered salaries for the gaolers of the larger town gaols, and only the Prison Act of 1877 brought every prison under the Home Office.","steps":["State the shared feature in one sentence using the examiner's vocabulary: in both periods the gaol was a local business run for fees, not a service run and paid for by the state.","Prove it for the earlier period: a keeper who drew no salary lived on what he could charge those in his custody, so a prisoner with money ate and one without went hungry among the debtors and the untried.","Prove it for the later period with different evidence: Howard found the same charging still in place in the 1770s, with prisoners who had been acquitted kept inside until the fees were paid, and the first statutory order for salaried gaolers waited until Peel.","Analyse the similarity instead of asserting it: in both periods the gaol was paid for locally and nobody in London inspected it, which is why it mainly held people rather than punished them, and why prison could not become the punishment until the state took the prisons over."],"cues":["Who paid for a prisoner's keep in each period?","What happened to a medieval prisoner with no money?","Which survey shows the same system still running after 1700?","Why did fee-paying gaols stop prison from becoming the punishment?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which act ended the prosecution of witchcraft as a crime, and what offence did it leave in its place?","a":"The Witchcraft Act of 1735. It left only the offence of pretending to have magical powers, punished by up to a year's imprisonment and the pillory."},{"q":"Name two reasons highway robbery had died out by the 1830s.","a":"Any two of: turnpike roads were busier and better patrolled; the Bow Street Horse Patrol watched the approaches to London from 1805; banknotes replaced coin so there was less to steal; the railway replaced the stagecoach."},{"q":"What did the County and Borough Police Act of 1856 do that the acts of 1835 and 1839 had not?","a":"It made forces compulsory everywhere, inspected and grant-aided. The act of 1835 had required boroughs to appoint watch committees, which were to appoint constables, but compliance was patchy, and the act of 1839 only allowed counties to act."},{"q":"Why was Pentonville built, and what was the main criticism of the separate system in operation?","a":"It was built because transportation was closing and the Bloody Code had been dismantled, and because reformers argued prisoners could be changed by solitude, work and instruction. The criticism was that isolation caused mental breakdown and suicide."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 1 Section B on this option carries 36 marks: Question 3 'Explain one way in which ... was similar to / different from ...' (4 marks), Question 4 'Explain why ...' (12 marks) and a choice of Question 5 or Question 6, 'How far do you agree? Explain your answer.' (16 marks, with up to 4 additional marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and the use of specialist terminology).","Questions 4, 5 and 6 print two stimulus bullets under 'You may use the following in your answer:' and then the line 'You must also use information of your own.' Question 3 prints no bullets.","The printed caps for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content: on the 12-mark question, 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'; on the 16-mark question, the same wording with 'Maximum 11 marks'. There is no longer any cap at Level 2.","Paper 1 lasts 1 hour 20 minutes for 52 marks, so work at about a minute a mark: roughly 5 minutes on Question 3, 15 on Question 4 and 25 on Question 5 or 6, keeping the last two minutes for spelling and specialist terms.","Question 3 normally spans at least a century and may reach into c1000-c1700 or c1900-present, and Questions 5 and 6 cover either a complete period or at least 200 years, so name the period of every example you use.","Specialist terms this option expects you to spell and use correctly: deterrence, retribution, rehabilitation, transportation, penal servitude, the Bloody Code, separate system, silent system, poaching, smuggling, constable, watchman, magistrate."],"questions":[{"q":"Explain one way in which the crimes committed in the years c1000-c1700 were similar to the crimes committed in the years c1700-c1900. (4 marks)","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2. Level 2 needs one similarity analysed with specific information from both periods: crimes against property dominant throughout; hunting criminalised by the Forest Laws and later by the Game Laws; crimes against authority present in both.","sol":"One similarity is that crimes against property were by far the most common kind of crime in both periods. In the years c1000-c1700 most cases before the courts were petty thefts, and the Forest Laws imposed after 1066 turned the hunting of deer in the royal forests into a theft from the king. The same is true in c1700-c1900: poaching was still a crime because the Game Act of 1671 reserved game to substantial landowners, smuggling grew out of the duties on tea and brandy, and highway robbery was robbery of property on the road. The Bloody Code hanged people chiefly to protect property, which shows how far the law of both periods rested on the defence of ownership.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what most people were actually taken to court for, rather than the crimes that made the most noise.","A similarity is only worth writing if you can put one named example from each period beside it.","One sentence that names the shared feature of both periods, then one piece of precise evidence from each side of 1700."],"codes":["10-3 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Continuity and change in the nature of crimes"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the definition of crime in the years c1500-c1700 was different from the definition of crime in the years c1700-c1900. (4 marks)","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2. Credit the ending of witchcraft as a crime: a capital offence under the statutes and the Hopkins hunt, against the Witchcraft Act of 1735, which left only the offence of pretending to magical powers.","sol":"One difference is what the law counted as witchcraft. Between c1500 and c1700 witchcraft was a serious crime in itself: the statutes made it a capital offence, and in Matthew Hopkins's East Anglian hunt of 1645-47 scores of people were hanged on evidence of familiars and marks found on the body. In the years c1700-c1900 that definition disappeared. Jane Wenham, convicted at Hertford in 1712, was pardoned, and the Witchcraft Act of 1735 replaced the old statutes so that the only offence left was pretending to have magical powers, punished by up to a year's imprisonment and the pillory. The crime had become fraud rather than devilry, because juries would no longer accept evidence that could not be tested.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The interesting change is not in how hard the punishment was but in what the law thought it was punishing.","Pick one act of Parliament that moved the line, and one earlier case or episode that shows where the line used to be.","Name the difference in a single sentence, then give the dated evidence from each period and say what caused the shift in thinking."],"codes":["10-3 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Changing definitions of crime exemplified in the ending of witchcraft prosecutions"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which law enforcement in the years c1000-c1700 was different from law enforcement in the years c1700-c1900. (4 marks)","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2. Credit the move from unpaid community duty (hue and cry, tithings, parish constable, watchman) to paid professionals (Metropolitan Police 1829, compulsory forces 1856, CID 1878).","sol":"One difference is who was responsible for catching criminals. In the years c1000-c1700 law enforcement was a community duty: the hue and cry obliged everyone who heard it to join the chase, tithings made groups of men answerable for one another, and the parish constable and night watchman were unpaid local men working part-time alongside their own trades. By the end of c1700-c1900 the work had been handed to paid professionals. The Metropolitan Police Act of 1829 put about 1,000 uniformed constables on the streets of London, the County and Borough Police Act of 1856 made a force compulsory in every county and borough, and from 1878 the Criminal Investigation Department employed detectives to investigate crimes rather than merely patrol.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask who was expected to do the chasing, and whether they were paid for it.","Choose two dated pieces of evidence that sit at opposite ends of the same question, one from each period.","Say what the difference is in one sentence, then prove each side, and finish by naming the feature of the period that made the older system fail."],"codes":["10-3 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the use of the death penalty in the years c1700-c1900 was different from its use in the years c1900-present. (4 marks)","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2. Credit the contrast between the Bloody Code with public execution and the retreat and abolition of capital punishment in the twentieth century, with dated support from both periods.","sol":"One difference is how far the death penalty was used. In the years c1700-c1900 it was the centrepiece of punishment: the Bloody Code carried more than two hundred capital offences by 1820, including armed poaching under the Black Act of 1723, and hangings were staged in public until the Capital Punishment Amendment Act of 1868 moved them inside prison walls. In the years c1900-present the death penalty disappeared altogether. The Children Act of 1908 stopped the execution of anyone under sixteen, the last hangings in Britain took place in 1964, and capital punishment for murder was suspended in 1965 and abolished in 1969, because opinion had come to see execution as unacceptable rather than as a deterrent.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Compare how many crimes could end on the gallows, not just whether hanging existed.","You need one dated fact from before 1900 and one from after it, each doing work in the comparison.","State the difference as a change in extent, support both sides, then explain what changed in attitudes to make it happen."],"codes":["10-3 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: Changing views on the purpose of punishment"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the aims of punishment in the years c1000-c1700 were similar to the aims of punishment in the years c1700-c1900. (4 marks)","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2. Credit deterrence through public, visible punishment: stocks, pillory and public whipping in the earlier period; public hanging under the Bloody Code and bodies hung in chains in the later one.","sol":"One similarity is that punishment was meant to deter, and it deterred by being seen. In the years c1000-c1700 punishments were staged in public: the stocks and pillory stood in the market place where the whole community passed, offenders were whipped through the streets, and hangings were carried out before a crowd. The same thinking ran through c1700-c1900. The Bloody Code rested on the belief that the sight of the gallows frightened others out of crime, executions at Tyburn and, after 1783, outside Newgate drew huge crowds, and the bodies of some criminals, including smugglers, were hung in chains. Through most of both periods few offenders were ever caught, so punishment worked by fear rather than by detection.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask who punishment was really aimed at: the person being punished, or everybody watching.","Find the same practice happening in both periods and name it, rather than listing the punishments of each.","Give the shared aim in one sentence, then evidence from each period, and close by explaining why both periods needed it."],"codes":["10-3 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: Changing views on the purpose of punishment"]},{"q":"Explain why the nature of crime changed in the years c1700-c1900. You may use the following in your answer: • smuggling • the Tolpuddle Martyrs (1834). You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit analytical causes: taxation and the law creating and removing crimes; political fear redefining protest as crime; industrialisation and urban growth changing opportunity. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"One reason is that the law itself created and then removed crimes. Smuggling was the great crime of the eighteenth century only because duties on tea, brandy and silk were enormous, which made running goods ashore worth the risk; the Hawkhurst Gang controlled stretches of Kent and Sussex in the 1730s and 1740s and was broken only when its leaders were hanged at the end of that decade. When William Pitt the Younger's Commutation Act of 1784 cut the duty on tea to a fraction of its old level, the profit vanished with it, and the mid nineteenth-century move to free trade finished the business off. The crime changed because the tax changed, not because people did.\n\nA second reason is political fear. Trade unions had been lawful since the Combination Acts were repealed in 1824, but a government alarmed by the Swing Riots of 1830 and by revolution abroad reached for the Unlawful Oaths Act of 1797 to transport six Dorset labourers led by George Loveless for seven years in 1834. They were punished for swearing a secret oath rather than for combining, which shows how an old statute could be stretched to make a new crime out of protest. The pardon of 1836, forced by mass petitioning and a huge London march, then moved the line back again.\n\nThe most far-reaching reason, though, is industrialisation. The population of England and Wales more than trebled between 1801 and 1901 and moved into towns, where strangers were anonymous, goods were concentrated and drunkenness and street disorder became the ordinary business of the courts. The same process destroyed older crimes: busier turnpikes, the Bow Street Horse Patrol from 1805, banknotes in place of coin and then the railway ended highway robbery, the last recorded case in England being in 1831.\n\nThe three work together. Industrial change altered what there was to steal and where, while taxation and political anxiety decided which of those acts Parliament chose to call crimes.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["The question is about why the nature of crime changed, so each paragraph has to end in a change rather than a description.","Use each bullet as a different type of cause - one economic, one political - and then find a third cause the bullets do not prompt at all.","Three paragraphs, each naming a cause, supplying dated evidence and then explaining the chain back to the change, and a closing sentence linking them."],"codes":["10-3 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Continuity and change in the nature of crimes","10-3 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Changing definitions of crime exemplified in the ending of witchcraft prosecutions"]},{"q":"Explain why methods of law enforcement changed in the years c1700-c1900. You may use the following in your answer: • the Fielding brothers • the Metropolitan Police Act (1829). You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit the failure of the old parish system in a growing capital, the Fieldings' demonstration that paid constables worked, government fear of disorder, and the spread of forces by statute to 1856 and detection to 1878. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"One reason is that the old system broke down where towns grew fastest. London's parish constables and night watchmen were unpaid part-timers, and the gap was filled by thief-takers who traded in stolen goods, as Jonathan Wild did until he was hanged in 1725. Henry Fielding, magistrate at Bow Street from 1748, answered this by paying a small group of constables from 1749 and by circulating descriptions of wanted men in his Covent Garden Journal from 1752; his half-brother Sir John Fielding ran Bow Street until 1780, added a horse patrol in 1763 and kept a register of known criminals. Their importance is that they proved a paid, organised and informed force could catch more criminals than a volunteer one.\n\nA second reason is the government's fear of disorder. The Gordon Riots of 1780 left London in the hands of the mob for days, and at Peterloo in 1819 cavalry charged a peaceful meeting and killed and injured many, which made the use of soldiers against civilians politically dangerous. Robert Peel's Metropolitan Police Act of 1829 offered the alternative: about 1,000 uniformed men under commissioners Rowan and Mayne, some 3,300 by May 1830, armed with a truncheon rather than a sabre, whose presence on the streets was meant to prevent crime and disorder before it began.\n\nThe change went national, however, because Parliament was eventually willing to compel and to pay. The act of 1835 required boroughs to appoint watch committees, which were to appoint constables, but compliance was patchy, and the act of 1839 only allowed counties to raise forces, which many refused to do because of the cost; the County and Borough Police Act of 1856 made a force compulsory everywhere, appointed government inspectors and gave a Treasury grant towards pay and clothing. Experience then showed that patrolling did not solve crimes, so a detective branch of eight officers was set up at Scotland Yard in 1842 and grew into the CID under Howard Vincent in 1878, helped by new tools such as the telegraph that caught John Tawell in 1845.\n\nSo demonstration, fear and money combine: the Fieldings showed what was possible, riot made it urgent, and statute with a grant made it universal.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the authorities were afraid of at each stage, and what they thought a police force would prevent.","Use one bullet for the idea and the other for the law, then add a cause that explains how the change spread outside London.","Three reasons, each with an act or a person and a date, each explained forward to the change in method, then a sentence that ties them together."],"codes":["10-3 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities","10-3 — 3 Case studies: Key individual: Robert Peel"]},{"q":"Explain why the use of transportation changed in the years c1700-c1900. You may use the following in your answer: • Australia • the Penal Servitude Act (1853). You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit why transportation grew (an alternative to hanging, loss of the American colonies, Botany Bay from 1787) and why it ended (cost, colonial objection, the gold rush, the availability of prisons at home). Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Transportation grew in the eighteenth century because the system needed a punishment between whipping and the gallows. Judges and juries were reluctant to hang people for small thefts, so convicts were shipped to the American colonies instead, and when the American war closed that route in 1776 prisoners were crammed into rotting hulks on the Thames. Botany Bay solved the problem: the first convict fleet sailed in 1787 and about 160,000 people were transported to Australia over the next eighty years, usually for seven or fourteen years. It removed offenders, it frightened others, and it built a colony at the same time.\n\nIt then ended because the colonies themselves refused to take convicts. As free settlers arrived they objected to being a dumping ground, and the gold rush that began in 1851 made a free passage to Australia look like a reward rather than a punishment; transportation to New South Wales had already stopped in 1840. The cost of shipping and guarding convicts across the world was also enormous compared with imprisoning them at home, and reports of conditions in the penal colonies convinced many that it neither deterred nor reformed.\n\nThe deciding reason, though, is that Britain finally had somewhere else to put people. Pentonville opened in 1842 and fifty-four prisons were built on its plan within six years, so a sentence served at home could be long, secure and supervised. The Penal Servitude Act of 1853 therefore replaced transportation with penal servitude for shorter sentences, and the last convict ship reached Western Australia in 1868.\n\nTransportation rose as a substitute for the gallows and fell as soon as the prison could do the same work more cheaply and more visibly at home.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Explain the rise as well as the fall: a punishment is adopted because something else is not working.","Separate the reasons that came from Australia from the reasons that came from Britain.","Three paragraphs - why it grew, why the colonies resisted, why prison replaced it - each dated, then one sentence of overall argument."],"codes":["10-3 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: Changing views on the purpose of punishment","10-3 — 3 Case studies: Pentonville prison in the mid nineteenth century"]},{"q":"Explain why views on the purpose of punishment changed in the years c1700-c1900. You may use the following in your answer: • John Howard • the separate system. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit Enlightenment and religious ideas, the evidence that terror did not work, the campaigning of Howard and Fry, and the practical need for prison to take over once the Bloody Code and transportation closed. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"One reason is the arrival of new ideas about human nature. Cesare Beccaria argued in 1764 that certainty of punishment deters far better than savagery, and Jeremy Bentham designed prisons in which every prisoner could be watched and improved. Quakers and evangelical Christians added the belief that criminals were redeemable rather than simply wicked, which made reformation a purpose worth paying for.\n\nA second reason is the accumulating evidence that terror did not work. John Howard, appointed sheriff of Bedfordshire in 1773, toured the gaols and published The State of the Prisons in 1777, describing prisoners held in filth, dying of gaol fever and kept beyond their release date because they could not pay the gaoler's fees; it made squalor an argument as well as a scandal. Elizabeth Fry, visiting Newgate from 1813 and founding her association for women prisoners in 1817, showed that a school, paid matrons, useful work and Bible classes produced order where floggings had not. Meanwhile the crowds at public hangings pickpocketed one another, and juries undervalued stolen goods to save the accused from the gallows.\n\nThe decisive reason, though, is practical. Once Peel's reforms from 1823 had stripped the death penalty from more than a hundred offences and transportation was closing, prison had to become the ordinary punishment, and a prison needs a purpose. The separate system supplied it: at Pentonville from 1842 prisoners were kept alone with work, instruction and a chaplain so that they would reflect and reform. The purpose was never secure, however, and after the garotting panic of 1862 the Prisons Act of 1865 swung back to hard labour, hard fare and hard board.\n\nIdeas made reform thinkable, campaigners made it respectable, and the collapse of the older punishments made it necessary.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Distinguish the purposes: retribution, deterrence and reformation are different aims and the period argued about which came first.","Pair an idea with the evidence that supported it, and then ask what forced the government to act rather than merely agree.","Three reasons - ideas, evidence and campaigners, practical necessity - each with dated proof, and a final sentence that says how they interacted."],"codes":["10-3 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: Changing views on the purpose of punishment","10-3 — 3 Case studies: Pentonville prison in the mid nineteenth century"]},{"q":"Explain why attitudes towards the Bloody Code changed in the years c1700-c1900. You may use the following in your answer: • public execution • Robert Peel. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit the failure of the code in practice (juries, commutation, disorderly crowds), the influence of Enlightenment argument and campaigners such as Romilly, and Peel's statutes that dismantled it. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"One reason is that the Bloody Code visibly failed. Capital offences rose from about fifty in 1688 to more than two hundred by 1820, yet crime did not fall. Juries refused to send people to the gallows for trivial thefts and practised pious perjury, valuing stolen goods at less than the amount that carried death, while judges commuted many sentences to transportation. A law that was too savage to be enforced was no deterrent at all.\n\nA second reason is what public execution had become. Hangings were staged before enormous crowds, at Tyburn and, after 1783, outside Newgate, on the theory that the spectacle would frighten others out of crime. In practice the crowds were drunken and disorderly, pickpockets worked them freely, and some condemned men were cheered as heroes. Respectable opinion came to see the spectacle as brutalising the watchers rather than deterring them, which is why the Capital Punishment Amendment Act of 1868 finally moved executions inside the prison walls.\n\nA third reason is that reformers turned that opinion into statute. Beccaria had argued in 1764 that certainty, not cruelty, deters; Samuel Romilly used the argument in Parliament and ended the death penalty for pickpocketing in 1808. Robert Peel then carried the reform through as Home Secretary: the Judgement of Death Act of 1823 allowed judges to pass a lesser sentence than death for most crimes, the Black Act was repealed in the same year, and his consolidation of the criminal law removed the death penalty from more than a hundred offences. By 1861 only five capital crimes remained.\n\nAttitudes changed, then, because the code did not work, because the manner of enforcing it disgusted people, and because alternatives - transportation and then the prison - had become available to take its place.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Attitudes change when people can see that something is not doing what it claims to do: what was the code supposed to achieve?","Take one reason from what happened in the courtroom, one from what happened at the scaffold, and one from Parliament.","Three explained reasons with dated statutes or named campaigners, each linked back to attitudes, and a final sentence ranking them."],"codes":["10-3 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: Changing views on the purpose of punishment","10-3 — 3 Case studies: Key individual: Robert Peel"]},{"q":"'There was more change than continuity in the nature of criminal activity in the years c1700-c1900.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • highway robbery • the Tolpuddle Martyrs (1834). You must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit change (crimes that disappeared, crimes created and removed by statute, new urban crime) weighed against continuity (theft dominant throughout, poaching, social crime), with a criterion-based judgement. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Whether this period saw more change than continuity depends on what is measured: the crimes people committed, or the crimes the law chose to notice. Judged by behaviour there is a great deal of continuity; judged by definition there is very sharp change.\n\nSome crimes did genuinely disappear. Highway robbery was the characteristic crime of the early eighteenth century because travellers carried coin along unlit roads with no bank to leave it in, and it was a capital offence, though Dick Turpin was hanged at York in 1739 for horse theft. By the 1830s it had gone. Turnpike roads carried more traffic, the Bow Street Horse Patrol watched the approaches to London from 1805, banknotes replaced coin so there was less worth taking, and from the 1830s the railway removed the isolated stagecoach. The last recorded highway robbery in England was in 1831: about as complete a change as this study can offer.\n\nThe law also created and destroyed crimes at speed. Smuggling grew only because duties on tea, brandy and silk were huge, and the Hawkhurst Gang could dominate Kent and Sussex in the 1730s and 1740s; once Pitt's Commutation Act of 1784 cut the tea duty and free trade followed in the mid nineteenth century, the profit and the crime shrank together. In the other direction, the Tolpuddle case shows a new crime being manufactured: six Dorset labourers led by George Loveless were transported for seven years in 1834 under the Unlawful Oaths Act of 1797 for swearing an oath to their friendly society, although unions had been legal since 1824. Witchcraft had gone the same way in reverse, ceasing to be a crime at all under the Witchcraft Act of 1735.\n\nAgainst this, the ordinary business of the courts barely moved. Petty theft was the commonest offence in c1000-c1700 and still the commonest in 1900. Poaching runs straight through the whole thematic study: the Forest Laws of the Normans, the Game Act of 1671, the Black Act of 1723 and the transportation of night poachers after 1816 all rest on the same idea that wild animals belong to the landowner. So does social crime, the refusal of communities to treat smuggling or poaching as wrong, which links the villagers who sheltered smugglers with those who protected poachers centuries earlier. Crimes against the person and against authority - assault, riot, treason - persisted throughout.\n\nThe point the stimulus does not reach is that industrialisation changed the setting rather than the nature of crime. A population that more than trebled between 1801 and 1901 and moved into towns produced more theft, drunkenness and street disorder, and a new police force recorded more of it, but these were old offences in new surroundings.\n\nOn balance I agree with the statement, but only on the definition of crime rather than on criminal behaviour. What people did to one another remained remarkably constant: they stole, they poached, they fought. What changed dramatically was which of those acts Parliament chose to punish, and how heavily, and that is decided by taxation, by property law and by political fear. The strongest evidence for change - the end of highway robbery, of witchcraft trials and of large-scale smuggling - is in every case a law or an opportunity changing rather than human nature.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Before you argue, decide what 'the nature of criminal activity' means: the acts themselves, or the list of things the law calls crimes.","Build one side from crimes that vanished or were invented, and the other from the offences that fill the court records in every century of the option.","Open with the criterion, alternate change and continuity paragraphs each closing with an interim judgement, add a point the bullets do not prompt, and end by deciding on the criterion you set."],"codes":["10-3 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Continuity and change in the nature of crimes","10-3 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Changing definitions of crime exemplified in the ending of witchcraft prosecutions"]},{"q":"'Responsibility for law enforcement passed from local communities to central government in the years c1700-c1900.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • parish constables • the County and Borough Police Act (1856). You must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit the decline of community duty (hue and cry, unpaid parish constables, watchmen, prosecution by the victim) against the growth of central action (the Metropolitan Police Act of 1829 under the Home Secretary, the compulsion, inspectors and Treasury grant of 1856, the CID of 1878, the Director of Public Prosecutions of 1879), weighed against the continuing local control of forces by watch committees, quarter sessions and the rates; judgement on a stated criterion. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"'Responsibility' here means three things: who decided that policing should happen, who paid for it, and who ran it day by day. Judged on the first the statement is right; judged on the other two it is only half right.\n\nThe community system did fade. In 1700 catching a criminal was a duty of ordinary people: the hue and cry called neighbours out after a suspect, the parish constable was an unpaid householder serving for a year, and towns were watched at night by the old watchmen nicknamed Charleys. Victims also had to prosecute at their own expense, which is why associations for the prosecution of felons spread in the second half of the eighteenth century, their members clubbing together to meet the cost of taking a thief to court. By 1900 none of this was how crime was normally dealt with, and the Parish Constables Act of 1872 ended the requirement to appoint parish constables at all.\n\nParliament, not the parish, made that happen. The Metropolitan Police Act of 1829 created a paid, uniformed force for London, about 1,000 constables in September 1829 and about 3,300 men by May 1830, under two commissioners, Charles Rowan and Richard Mayne, who answered directly to the Home Secretary; no other force in the country was run from Whitehall. The County and Borough Police Act of 1856 then removed local choice: every county and borough had to have a force, government inspectors of constabulary reported on whether each one was efficient, and a Treasury grant of a quarter of the cost of pay and clothing went to the forces they certified. Detection was organised the same way, the eight detectives at Scotland Yard in 1842 becoming the CID under Howard Vincent in 1878.\n\nAgainst the statement stands the question of who actually ran the forces, because outside London they stayed local. The Municipal Corporations Act of 1835 required boroughs to appoint watch committees, which were to appoint constables, and those committees hired, disciplined and could dismiss their men; county forces were run by the justices in quarter sessions and, after the Local Government Act of 1888, by standing joint committees of magistrates and county councillors. Most of the money came from the local rates rather than the Treasury, and in 1900 there was still no national police force.\n\nThe point that settles the question lies beyond both bullets: prosecution. Bringing a case remained largely a matter for the victim or the police even after the Prosecution of Offences Act of 1879 created a Director of Public Prosecutions, first appointed in 1880, who took only the most serious or difficult cases. Ordinary people still reported crimes, gave evidence and sat on juries, so their part in law enforcement had changed from chasing suspects to supporting a force rather than disappeared.\n\nI therefore agree in part, and the distinction matters. Responsibility for deciding that policing must exist, and for judging whether it was good enough, did pass to central government, and the compulsion, inspection and grant of 1856 are the clearest proof of it. But the running, the paying and the prosecuting stayed local. The real change between c1700 and c1900 was from amateur to professional; the change from local to central was begun by Parliament and left unfinished in 1900.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide in your first two sentences what 'responsibility' means - deciding that policing happens, paying for it, or running it - and measure every later paragraph against that.","The strongest counter-argument is not that communities kept catching criminals but that somebody other than central government controlled and paid for the new forces.","The old system fading, then what Parliament compelled, then who actually ran the forces, then the factor beyond the bullets, each closing with an interim judgement, and a conclusion that says in which sense responsibility moved and in which sense it did not."],"codes":["10-3 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities"]},{"q":"'The main reason punishment changed in the years c1700-c1900 was a change in attitudes towards its purpose.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the Bloody Code • Elizabeth Fry. You must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit changing attitudes (Enlightenment, religious reform, Howard and Fry) weighed against practical causes (the failure of the code in court, the closing of transportation, cost, government capacity), with a substantiated judgement. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Attitudes clearly did change in this period, from punishment as retribution and deterrence towards punishment as reformation. The question is whether that change of mind drove the reforms, or followed practical failures that would have forced change anyway.\n\nThere is real evidence for attitudes leading. Cesare Beccaria argued in 1764 that the certainty of punishment deters better than its savagery, and Samuel Romilly used the argument in Parliament, ending the death penalty for pickpocketing in 1808. Religious conviction did the same work for prisons. Elizabeth Fry, a Quaker, first visited the women's side of Newgate in 1813 and founded her association for female prisoners in 1817, bringing in a school for the children held there, paid matrons, sewing and knitting for wages and Bible classes, and banning drink. Her evidence to a House of Commons committee in 1818 made the case that order could be produced by education rather than by terror, and John Howard's survey of 1777 had already made filth, fees and gaol fever into a national scandal. Both were arguing about the purpose of punishment before any government acted.\n\nBut the Bloody Code did not fall because people pitied criminals; it fell because it did not work. Capital offences rose from about fifty in 1688 to over two hundred by 1820 and crime did not fall with them. Juries practised pious perjury, valuing stolen goods below the level that carried death; judges commuted sentence after sentence; and the crowds at public hangings behaved so badly that the spectacle was discrediting the law it was supposed to uphold. Peel's Judgement of Death Act of 1823 and his consolidation of the criminal law simply recognised in statute what the courts were already doing in practice.\n\nThe practical case is stronger still when the alternatives are examined. Transportation to America closed with the American war in 1776 and the hulks were a stopgap; Botany Bay took convicts from 1787, but the colonies objected as free settlers arrived, the gold rush from 1851 made the voyage look like a reward, and the Penal Servitude Act of 1853 replaced transportation for shorter sentences before the last ship reached Western Australia in 1868. With the gallows narrowed to five offences by 1861 and transportation gone, prison had to carry the whole burden, whatever anyone believed about its purpose.\n\nThe clearest test lies beyond the two bullets, in what happened when opinion turned. Pentonville opened in 1842 as the model of reformation through the separate system, and fifty-four prisons were built on its plan within six years. Yet after the garotting panic of 1862 the Prisons Act of 1865 imposed hard labour, hard fare and hard board, and the crank and the treadwheel returned. Attitudes had not permanently changed at all; they had swung, and the regime swung with them.\n\nI agree with the statement only in part. Changing attitudes explain the direction of reform - why the prison replaced the scaffold, and why anyone thought separation and instruction would work. They do not explain its timing, which was set by the collapse of the Bloody Code in the courtroom and the closing of transportation, nor its durability, since the reforming purpose was abandoned within a generation while the institutional changes remained. Attitudes supplied the argument; practical necessity supplied the moment.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Separate the two questions the statement mixes together: what direction change took, and what made it happen when it did.","The best evidence against the statement is a moment when opinion changed back - find it.","Argue for the statement, then against it from practical causes, use a point beyond the bullets to test it, and conclude by distinguishing direction from timing."],"codes":["10-3 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: Changing views on the purpose of punishment","10-3 — 3 Case studies: Pentonville prison in the mid nineteenth century"]},{"q":"'The building of Pentonville prison was the most significant change in punishment in the years c1700-c1900.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the separate system • transportation. You must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit Pentonville as the model prison and the arrival of imprisonment as the punishment, weighed against the ending of the Bloody Code and of public execution and the end of transportation, with a criterion for significance. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Significance should be measured by how far a change altered the ordinary experience of punishment, and how long it lasted. On that test Pentonville has a serious claim, but it competes with the disappearance of the gallows from public life.\n\nPentonville's claim is that it turned imprisonment itself into the punishment. Built between 1840 and 1842 to Joshua Jebb's design, it had 520 single cells, each about thirteen feet by seven, each with its own basin and water closet so that a prisoner need never leave it. The separate system was built into the architecture: prisoners picked oakum or turned a crank alone, wore masks when moved, sat in separate stalls in chapel and were known by a number, so that they could neither corrupt one another nor be recognised afterwards. Its scale of influence is the strongest evidence: fifty-four prisons were built on its plan within six years, so a national prison system was created in under a decade.\n\nThe case is weakened by how quickly the purpose failed. Rates of mental breakdown and suicide at Pentonville were far higher than in other prisons, and critics argued that isolation produced broken men rather than reformed ones. After the garotting panic of 1862, the Prisons Act of 1865 replaced reformation with hard labour, hard fare and hard board, and the Prison Act of 1877 brought every prison under the Home Office. The buildings lasted; the idea they were built for did not.\n\nTransportation, the second stimulus, is a genuine rival but ultimately a supporting one. About 160,000 convicts went to Australia after the first fleet sailed in 1787, and its ending - New South Wales closed to convicts in 1840, the Penal Servitude Act of 1853, the last ship reaching Western Australia in 1868 - removed the main alternative to prison. But this is the same change seen from the other side: transportation ended partly because prisons like Pentonville now existed.\n\nThe strongest rival lies beyond both bullets, in the collapse of the Bloody Code and of public execution. Capital offences fell from more than two hundred around 1820 to five by 1861, after Romilly's act of 1808, Peel's Judgement of Death Act of 1823 and his repeal of the Black Act, and the Capital Punishment Amendment Act of 1868 took hanging behind the prison walls after Michael Barrett became the last person hanged in public. This altered the relationship between the state and the punished more profoundly than any single building: for centuries punishment had been a public spectacle designed to deter the crowd, and within two generations it became something done out of sight to the individual offender.\n\nI agree that Pentonville was the most significant change in the system of punishment, though not in its purpose. In the system, it is decisive: it made the prison the ordinary sentence, supplied the plan that fifty-four other prisons copied, and made the ending of transportation possible. In purpose it is not, because the separate system was abandoned within a generation, whereas the retreat of the death penalty from more than two hundred offences to five, and of execution from the street to the yard, was never reversed. The prison is the greater institutional change; the end of public hanging is the greater change in what punishment was for.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first what would make one change more significant than another, and say so before you argue.","Ask whether the two bullets are really two separate changes, or two sides of the same one.","Case for Pentonville, its weaknesses, the rival change the bullets do not mention, and a conclusion that separates the system of punishment from its purpose."],"codes":["10-3 — 3 Case studies: Pentonville prison in the mid nineteenth century","10-3 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: Changing views on the purpose of punishment"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Which act of Parliament created a paid, uniformed police force for London, and in which year?","a":"The Metropolitan Police Act, in 1829."},{"q":"After the Witchcraft Act of 1735, what was the offence that replaced witchcraft itself?","a":"Pretending to have magical powers, punished by up to a year in prison and the pillory rather than by death."},{"q":"Which prison regime, used at Pentonville from 1842, kept each prisoner alone in his own cell?","a":"The separate system."},{"q":"In which year did transportation to Australia finally come to an end?","a":"1868, when the last convict ship reached Western Australia."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The Bloody Code","back":"The name for English criminal law in the eighteenth century, when capital offences rose from about 50 in 1688 to over 200 by 1820, most of them protecting property. Its aim was deterrence by terror.","code":"10-3 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: Changing views on the purpose of punishment","open":false},{"front":"Highway robbery, c1700-1831","back":"A capital crime of the early eighteenth century, when travellers carried coin on lonely roads. It died out as turnpikes grew busier, the Bow Street Horse Patrol watched London's approaches from 1805, banknotes spread and railways replaced the stagecoach.","code":"10-3 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Continuity and change in the nature of crimes","open":false},{"front":"Poaching and the Game Laws","back":"The Game Act of 1671 reserved hunting to substantial landowners. The Black Act of 1723 made armed or disguised poaching a capital crime; it was repealed in 1823, and from 1831 anyone could hunt game with a licence.","code":"10-3 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Continuity and change in the nature of crimes","open":false},{"front":"Smuggling in the eighteenth century","back":"Untaxed tea, brandy and silk were landed on the south coast; the Hawkhurst Gang ran Kent and Sussex in the 1730s and 1740s until its leaders were hanged. Pitt's Commutation Act of 1784 cut tea duty and the trade shrank.","code":"10-3 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Continuity and change in the nature of crimes","open":false},{"front":"The Witchcraft Act (1735)","back":"It replaced the old witchcraft statutes. Witchcraft was no longer a crime; the offence became pretending to have magical powers, punished by up to a year in prison and the pillory. Jane Wenham, convicted in 1712, was pardoned.","code":"10-3 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Changing definitions of crime exemplified in the ending of witchcraft prosecutions","open":false},{"front":"The Tolpuddle Martyrs (1834)","back":"Six Dorset labourers led by George Loveless formed a friendly society against wage cuts. Convicted under the Unlawful Oaths Act of 1797 and transported for seven years, they were pardoned in 1836 after mass protest.","code":"10-3 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Changing definitions of crime exemplified in the ending of witchcraft prosecutions","open":false},{"front":"The Fielding brothers and the Bow Street Runners","back":"Henry Fielding, magistrate at Bow Street from 1748, set up London's first paid constables in 1749 and circulated crime information. His blind half-brother Sir John Fielding ran Bow Street to 1780 and founded a horse patrol in 1763.","code":"10-3 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities","open":false},{"front":"The Metropolitan Police Act (1829)","back":"Peel's act created a paid, uniformed force for London under commissioners Rowan and Mayne: about 1,000 constables in September 1829, about 3,300 by May 1830. Armed only with truncheons, they patrolled fixed beats and were nicknamed peelers or bobbies.","code":"10-3 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities","open":false},{"front":"The County and Borough Police Act (1856)","back":"It made a force compulsory everywhere, inspected by government inspectors of constabulary and grant-aided towards pay and clothing. Boroughs had owed a duty since 1835, but compliance was patchy; counties had only been allowed to act in 1839.","code":"10-3 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities","open":false},{"front":"The Criminal Investigation Department (1878)","back":"Scotland Yard's detective branch, begun with eight officers in 1842, was reorganised as the CID under Howard Vincent in 1878 with around 200 detectives, making detection a separate specialism from patrol.","code":"10-3 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities","open":false},{"front":"Transportation, 1787-1868","back":"Convicts were sent to America until 1776 and to Australia from 1787; about 160,000 went. The Penal Servitude Act of 1853 replaced it for shorter sentences, and the last convict ship reached Western Australia in 1868.","code":"10-3 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: Changing views on the purpose of punishment","open":false},{"front":"The end of public execution (1868)","back":"The Capital Punishment Amendment Act moved hangings inside prison walls. Michael Barrett, hanged outside Newgate in May 1868, was the last person executed in public in Britain.","code":"10-3 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: Changing views on the purpose of punishment","open":false},{"front":"John Howard and Elizabeth Fry","back":"Howard, sheriff of Bedfordshire, surveyed gaols and published The State of the Prisons in 1777, urging paid gaolers, clean cells and useful work. Fry, a Quaker, ran a school and workroom for women in Newgate from 1817.","code":"10-3 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: Changing views on the purpose of punishment","open":false},{"front":"Pentonville prison and the separate system","back":"Opened in 1842 to Joshua Jebb's design with 520 single cells, it isolated prisoners so that they would reflect and reform; masks and separate chapel stalls hid their faces. Mental breakdown and suicide were its cost.","code":"10-3 — 3 Case studies: Pentonville prison in the mid nineteenth century","open":false},{"front":"Robert Peel","back":"Home Secretary from 1822 to 1827 and 1828 to 1830, later prime minister. He passed the Gaols Act of 1823, removed the death penalty from more than a hundred offences, and created the Metropolitan Police in 1829.","code":"10-3 — 3 Case studies: Key individual: Robert Peel","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 1 Question 5, 16 marks)","items":["Question: 'Government action did more than the work of individual reformers to change punishment in the years c1700-c1900.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the Gaols Act (1823) • Samuel Romilly. You must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]. This is the paragraph from the middle of the answer that puts the case against the statement.","Government did not discover the state of the gaols; it was told. John Howard was appointed sheriff of Bedfordshire in 1773, found men who had been acquitted still locked up because they could not pay the gaoler's fees, and toured prisons across Britain and Europe before publishing The State of the Prisons in England and Wales in 1777 with the evidence set out county by county. Parliament's response in 1774, ordering the gaols cleaned and the fees of acquitted prisoners abolished, was written from his findings and then left to enforce itself, because no inspector existed to make it happen. The same pattern runs through the rest of the period. Elizabeth Fry went into the women's side of Newgate in 1813, founded her association in 1817 with a school for the children, paid matrons, paid work and rules the women agreed among themselves, and gave evidence to a House of Commons committee in 1818; the Gaols Act of 1823 then ordered female warders for female prisoners and chaplains to visit, which is her practice turned into statute. Samuel Romilly did the same work on the Bloody Code, carrying the abolition of the death penalty for pickpocketing in 1808 against ministers who believed the code should stand. Each time the individual supplied the evidence, the working model and the argument, and the state supplied the words on the page, often years later and usually without the means to enforce them.","Why this is Level 4: the explanation is analytical and directed consistently at the conceptual focus - it is about who caused the change, not about what the reformers were like. The knowledge is precisely selected rather than general: the shrievalty of 1773, the survey of 1777, the acts of 1774, Fry's association of 1817 and her evidence of 1818, the provisions of the Gaols Act, Romilly's success of 1808, all dated and all doing work in the argument. The criterion for judgement is applied inside the paragraph - who moved first, and who could only act once the evidence existed - so the interim judgement is justified rather than asserted, and the closing sentence weighs the two sides instead of listing them. The specialist terms (statute, gaoler's fees, capital offence, the Bloody Code) are used accurately, which is what the SPaG marks reward.","Note on the Gaols Act of 1823: say what it did - magistrates to inspect, gaolers on salaries instead of fees, female warders for female prisoners, chaplains to visit - and say what it did not do, since it covered only the larger town gaols and appointed no inspectors; paid government inspectors came with the Prisons Act of 1835. A point about a statute only earns marks if you can say how far it reached."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same as above - 'Government action did more than the work of individual reformers to change punishment in the years c1700-c1900.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the Gaols Act (1823) • Samuel Romilly. You must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","I partly agree with this statement because the government did change punishment but individual people changed it as well. The government passed the Gaols Act in 1823 which made prisons better and it passed more laws later on that improved things for prisoners. Samuel Romilly was an MP who wanted fewer people to be hanged and he managed to get the law changed. John Howard went round the prisons and wrote a book about how bad they were, and Elizabeth Fry helped the women in Newgate and made their lives better, which shows individuals were important too because the government listened to them. So overall the government did a lot but individuals did a lot as well.","Why this is Level 2 and not Level 3: it does offer an explanation with some development and organisation, and it does include accurate and relevant information, which is what Level 2 asks for. What it misses is Level 3's requirement that the explanation be 'mainly directed at the conceptual focus of the question' with 'good knowledge and understanding of the required features or characteristics of the period studied'. Every fact here is a generalisation - 'made prisons better', 'more laws later on', 'wrote a book', 'helped the women' - where a date, a provision or a figure should be: salaries instead of fees and female warders for female prisoners in 1823, the paid government inspectors of 1835, The State of the Prisons of 1777, the association of 1817 and the Commons evidence of 1818, the end of hanging for pickpocketing in 1808. The link to the question is implicit: the paragraph says both sides acted without deciding which one moved first. The judgement is announced in the last sentence rather than reasoned towards, so it is asserted.","The fastest single repair: after each fact, write the words 'which mattered because...' and finish the sentence. If you cannot finish it, the fact is not doing any work in the argument."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: crimes of the period - highway robbery, poaching and smuggling","items":["Crimes against property remained the commonest offence throughout c1700-c1900, as they had been since c1000; the Bloody Code existed mainly to protect property.","Highway robbery: travellers carried coin on unlit roads with no banking system; it was a capital offence, and Dick Turpin was hanged at York in 1739 for horse theft.","Highway robbery declined because turnpike roads carried more traffic, the Bow Street Horse Patrol was re-established in 1805 to watch the roads into London, banknotes replaced coin, and from the 1830s the railway removed the isolated stagecoach.","The last recorded highway robbery in England took place in 1831.","Poaching: the Game Act of 1671 restricted hunting game to men holding substantial freehold land, so ordinary villagers taking a rabbit or a hare were committing a crime.","The Black Act (Waltham Black Act) of 1723 made it a capital offence to go armed or with a blackened face in a forest, and is usually credited with creating around fifty new capital offences at a stroke, though counts of the statute run higher; it was repealed in 1823.","The Poaching Act of 1816 allowed transportation for seven years for taking game at night with a net; the Game Act of 1831 ended the old qualification and allowed hunting with a licence.","Smuggling grew because duties on tea, brandy, silk and tobacco were extremely high; whole coastal communities took part, from the men who landed the goods to the farmers who carried them inland.","The Hawkhurst Gang dominated smuggling in Kent and Sussex in the 1730s and 1740s, using violence and intimidation, and was broken up at the end of the 1740s when its leaders were hanged.","William Pitt the Younger's Commutation Act of 1784 cut the duty on tea from over a hundred per cent to about a tenth of that, removing most of the profit from smuggling; free trade in the mid nineteenth century finished the trade.","Both poaching and smuggling were social crimes: local communities refused to regard them as wrong and would not give evidence, which is why they were so hard to police.","Industrial towns produced new patterns of offending - pickpocketing, street robbery, drunkenness and public disorder - as the population of England and Wales more than trebled between 1801 and 1901."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: changing definitions of crime - witchcraft and the Tolpuddle Martyrs","items":["Witchcraft was made a crime by statute in the sixteenth century and remained a capital offence into the seventeenth; accusations peaked during the disorder of the Civil War.","Matthew Hopkins, the self-styled Witchfinder General, ran an intensive hunt in East Anglia in 1645-47 and sent scores of accused people to the gallows.","Jane Wenham of Walkern in Hertfordshire was convicted of witchcraft in 1712 but pardoned; she was the last person convicted of witchcraft in England.","Janet Horne, executed at Dornoch in 1727, was the last person put to death for witchcraft in Britain.","The Witchcraft Act of 1735 repealed the old statutes: witchcraft ceased to be a crime and the only offence left was pretending to have magical powers, punishable by up to a year in prison with quarterly appearances in the pillory.","The change came from new ways of thinking: the Royal Society, founded in 1660, and Enlightenment scepticism made educated magistrates and juries unwilling to convict on evidence that could not be tested.","The Combination Acts of 1799 and 1800 had banned workers' organisations; their repeal in 1824 made trade unions legal again, though an act of 1825 restricted what they could do.","The Swing Riots of 1830, in which agricultural labourers across southern England broke threshing machines, left the government frightened of rural protest.","On 9 December 1833 six labourers from Tolpuddle in Dorset, led by George Loveless, completed the founding of the Friendly Society of Agricultural Labourers after their wages were cut to seven shillings a week; the arrests followed in February 1834.","They were prosecuted not for forming a union but under the Unlawful Oaths Act of 1797, passed after naval mutinies, for swearing a secret oath, and were sentenced to seven years' transportation to Australia.","A mass petition and a huge protest march in London forced the government to give way: the men were pardoned in 1836 and returned to England over the following years.","The pair of examples shows definitions of crime moving in both directions: decriminalisation where beliefs changed, new criminalisation where the government feared protest."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: law enforcement - the Fielding brothers, police forces and the CID","items":["In 1700 law enforcement was still local and unpaid: the hue and cry, the parish constable chosen for a year, and the night watchmen nicknamed Charleys.","Thief-takers filled the gap by claiming rewards for recovering stolen goods; Jonathan Wild, the self-styled Thief-taker General, was running a criminal network of his own and was hanged in 1725.","Henry Fielding, novelist and magistrate at Bow Street from 1748, established a small force of paid constables in 1749, later known as the Bow Street Runners.","He used his Covent Garden Journal from 1752 to publish descriptions of wanted criminals and stolen goods, the beginning of shared criminal information.","Sir John Fielding, his blind half-brother, directed Bow Street until 1780, founded a mounted horse patrol in 1763 and built a register of known offenders; the horse patrol was re-established in 1805.","The Gordon Riots of 1780 and the killing of demonstrators by cavalry at Peterloo in 1819 convinced government that soldiers were the wrong instrument for public order.","The Metropolitan Police Act of 1829, carried by Robert Peel as Home Secretary, created a paid, uniformed force based at Scotland Yard under commissioners Charles Rowan and Richard Mayne: about 1,000 constables when patrols began in September 1829 and about 3,300 men by May 1830.","They wore blue tailcoats and top hats to look civilian rather than military, carried a truncheon and a rattle, and walked fixed beats; the public called them peelers or bobbies.","Hostility was real at first: in 1833 a constable was killed when police broke up a meeting at Cold Bath Fields in London, and the coroner's jury returned a verdict of justifiable homicide.","The Municipal Corporations Act of 1835 required boroughs to appoint watch committees, which were to appoint constables, but compliance was patchy; the County Police Act of 1839 only allowed counties to set up forces, and many refused because of the cost.","The County and Borough Police Act of 1856 made forces compulsory throughout England and Wales, created government inspectors of constabulary and paid a Treasury grant towards pay and clothing.","A detective branch of eight officers was set up at Scotland Yard in 1842 and became the Criminal Investigation Department under Howard Vincent in 1878, with around 200 detectives.","New technology helped: John Tawell was arrested in 1845 after his description was sent ahead by electric telegraph from Slough, the first use of the telegraph to catch a murderer."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: punishment - the Bloody Code, transportation and public execution","items":["The number of capital offences rose from about fifty in 1688 to more than two hundred by 1820; the collection of laws was nicknamed the Bloody Code.","Its purpose was deterrence by terror: with no police force to catch offenders, the state relied on the fear created by savage and visible punishment.","In practice it was softened by those who worked it: juries undervalued stolen goods so that the accused escaped a capital sentence, a practice called pious perjury, and judges commuted many sentences to transportation.","Executions were public and drew enormous crowds, held at Tyburn until 1783 and afterwards outside Newgate; the bodies of some criminals, including smugglers, were hung in chains as a warning.","Cesare Beccaria's On Crimes and Punishments of 1764 argued that the certainty of punishment deters far more effectively than its severity, and became the standard argument of reformers.","Samuel Romilly campaigned in Parliament from 1808, when the death penalty for pickpocketing was abolished.","Robert Peel's Judgement of Death Act of 1823 allowed judges to pass a lesser sentence than death for all crimes except murder and treason, and his consolidation of the criminal law removed the death penalty from more than a hundred offences.","By 1861 only five offences still carried the death penalty, and in practice only murder and treason led to execution.","The Capital Punishment Amendment Act of 1868 ended public execution; Michael Barrett, hanged outside Newgate in May 1868, was the last person executed in public in Britain.","Transportation began as a substitute for hanging: convicts were shipped to the American colonies until the war of independence closed that route in 1776, after which prisoners were held in rotting hulks on the Thames.","The first convict fleet sailed for Botany Bay in 1787, and about 160,000 convicts were transported to Australia, usually for seven or fourteen years or for life.","Transportation to New South Wales ended in 1840; the Penal Servitude Act of 1853 replaced transportation with penal servitude for shorter sentences, and the last convict ship reached Western Australia in 1868.","It ended because free settlers objected, because the gold rush from 1851 made the voyage look like an opportunity rather than a punishment, because it was expensive, and because prisons at home could now do the job."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: prison reform - John Howard and Elizabeth Fry","items":["Before reform, gaols were mainly places to hold people awaiting trial or punishment; gaolers were unpaid and lived on fees charged to the prisoners, so people who had been acquitted could be kept in until they paid.","Men, women, children, the tried and the untried were held together, disease was rife, and typhus was so common in prisons that it was known as gaol fever.","John Howard was appointed sheriff of Bedfordshire in 1773, was appalled by the county gaol and toured prisons across Britain and Europe to gather evidence.","He published The State of the Prisons in England and Wales in 1777, arguing for paid gaolers, clean and separate cells, decent food, useful work, medical care and Christian instruction.","Parliament responded with acts in 1774 requiring gaols to be cleaned and abolishing fees for acquitted prisoners, but there were no inspectors and the acts were widely ignored.","Howard died of typhus in 1790 while inspecting prisons abroad; the Howard Association, founded in 1866, was named after him, and became the Howard League for Penal Reform in 1921 when it merged with the Penal Reform League of 1907.","Elizabeth Fry, a Quaker, first visited the women's side of Newgate in 1813 and found women and children crowded together without bedding, work or teaching.","In 1817 she founded the Association for the Improvement of the Female Prisoners in Newgate, introducing a school for the children, paid matrons, sewing and knitting for wages, Bible classes and rules agreed by the prisoners themselves, and banning drink.","In 1818 she gave evidence to a House of Commons committee on prisons, and her work was copied in other prisons and admired abroad.","Robert Peel's Gaols Act of 1823 required magistrates to inspect gaols, gaolers to be paid a salary, female warders for female prisoners and chaplains to visit, but covered only the larger town gaols and provided no inspectors.","The Prisons Act of 1835 appointed paid government inspectors of prisons; the Prison Act of 1877 brought every prison in the country under the control of the Home Office.","After the garotting panic of 1862, the Prisons Act of 1865 imposed a regime of hard labour, hard fare and hard board, with the crank and the treadwheel, showing that deterrence had not been replaced by reformation."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: case study - Pentonville prison and the separate system","items":["Pentonville was built in north London between 1840 and 1842 and was known as the model prison, the pattern on which others were to be built.","It was designed by Joshua Jebb, surveyor-general of prisons, on a radial plan with wings running out from a central hall so that staff could supervise every landing.","It held 520 prisoners, each in a single cell about thirteen feet long, seven feet wide and nine feet high, with its own basin, water closet and a hammock or bed.","Reasons for building it: transportation was closing, the Bloody Code had been dismantled so prison had to become the main punishment, reformers had argued since Howard that criminals could be changed, and the government wanted a uniform national standard.","The separate system kept prisoners apart at all times so that they could not corrupt one another and would be forced to reflect on their crimes, with work, religious instruction and basic education as the means of reform.","Prisoners wore masks or peaked caps when moved, sat in separate stalls in the chapel, exercised apart and were known by a number rather than a name.","Work was done alone in the cell: picking oakum, weaving, shoemaking or turning a crank, which in some prisons did nothing useful at all.","Strengths in operation: cells were clean, dry and heated, food and medical care were better than in the old gaols, prisoners learned a trade and could read, and the system was orderly and could be inspected.","Weaknesses in operation: the isolation caused mental breakdown, and rates of insanity and suicide at Pentonville were far higher than in prisons using the silent system, in which prisoners worked together but were forbidden to speak.","Fifty-four prisons were built on the Pentonville plan in the six years after it opened, so a national prison system was created within a decade.","The reforming purpose did not last: after 1865 hard labour, hard fare and hard board replaced instruction as the regime's aim, and the separate system was gradually abandoned.","Use Pentonville on both sides of a judgement: it is the clearest evidence that imprisonment had become the punishment, and the clearest evidence that reformation failed in practice."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: key individual - Robert Peel","items":["Robert Peel lived from 1788 to 1850; he was Home Secretary from 1822 to 1827 and again from 1828 to 1830, and later prime minister.","Penal reform: the Gaols Act of 1823 required magistrates to visit gaols, gaolers to be paid a salary instead of living on fees, women warders for women prisoners, and chaplains and doctors to attend.","Its weakness was enforcement: it applied only to the gaols of London and the larger towns and appointed no inspectors, so many prisons ignored it until inspectors were created in 1835.","The Judgement of Death Act of 1823 allowed judges to pass a sentence other than death for every capital crime except murder and treason.","Peel repealed the Black Act of 1723 and consolidated hundreds of scattered criminal statutes, removing the death penalty from more than a hundred offences between 1823 and 1830.","Law enforcement: the Metropolitan Police Act of 1829 created a paid, uniformed force for London under commissioners Charles Rowan and Richard Mayne, based at Scotland Yard: about 1,000 constables in September 1829 and about 3,300 men by May 1830.","The force was deliberately civilian: blue coats rather than red, truncheons rather than firearms, fixed beats, and the stated aim of preventing crime by visible patrol rather than punishing it afterwards.","The two halves of his work fit together: a criminal law that hanged fewer people needed a system that caught more of them.","Limits on his significance: the Fieldings had shown from 1749 that paid constables and shared information worked; Rowan and Mayne organised and disciplined the new force; and Howard and Fry had made prison reform respectable long before 1823.","His act applied to London alone; the act of 1835 required boroughs to appoint watch committees, which were to appoint constables, but compliance was patchy, and the act of 1839 only allowed counties to copy the model.","The change became national only with the County and Borough Police Act of 1856, which made forces compulsory and paid a Treasury grant, six years after Peel's death.","Peel's real claim to significance is as a legislator: he turned arguments others had made into statute, and created the model that every later force copied."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 1 Question 5: 'Government action did more than the work of individual reformers to change punishment in the years c1700-c1900.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the Gaols Act (1823) • Samuel Romilly. You must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","items":["The question as printed: 'Government action did more than the work of individual reformers to change punishment in the years c1700-c1900.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the Gaols Act (1823) • Samuel Romilly. You must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]. Remember that an answer which does not address three or more aspects of content is capped at 11 marks and cannot reach Level 4 at all, so at least one developed point must come from beyond the two bullets.","Decode the statement before planning. It sets two agents against each other - Parliament and the Home Office on one side, reformers and campaigners on the other - and asks which did more to change punishment. The trap is that the two are not separate: Peel was a minister, Romilly was an MP, and the Gaols Act is a statute written out of Fry's practice. The criterion to announce in the opening is what 'did more' means: who set the direction, or who made the change actually reach a prisoner.","Opening, three or four sentences: state that punishment changed from deterrence by terror to imprisonment under rules, name the two candidate agents, announce the criterion, and signal the position the essay will reach so the line of reasoning is visible from the first paragraph.","Paragraph 1, for the statement - only government could make a change general. The acts of 1774 and the Gaols Act of 1823 reached further than any private campaign could, the Prisons Act of 1835 finally appointed paid government inspectors, and the Prison Act of 1877 put every prison in the country under the Home Office. Interim judgement: a reformer could change one gaol, a statute could change all of them.","Paragraph 2, for the statement - government also decided the big questions of punishment. Peel's Judgement of Death Act of 1823 let judges pass a lesser sentence than death, the Penal Servitude Act of 1853 replaced transportation for shorter sentences, and the Capital Punishment Amendment Act of 1868 took execution behind the walls. Interim judgement: the shape of the system was settled in Parliament, not in the prisons.","Paragraph 3, against the statement - individuals supplied the evidence and the model. Howard's survey of 1777 gave Parliament its facts, Fry's association of 1817 and her evidence to the Commons in 1818 gave it a working example, and Romilly carried the end of hanging for pickpocketing in 1808. Interim judgement: government legislated what individuals had already proved.","Paragraph 4, against the statement - statutes that nobody enforced changed nothing. The acts of 1774 were widely ignored because no inspector existed, and the Gaols Act of 1823 covered only the larger town gaols and appointed no inspectors either, so for a decade the words on the page and the conditions in the gaol were different things. Interim judgement: government action without inspection was not action at all.","Paragraph 5, the point beyond the bullets that tests the whole statement - the two agents were the same people. Peel was Home Secretary and a reformer at once, Joshua Jebb designed Pentonville as a government surveyor, and the committee chaired by Herbert Gladstone in 1895 was a government body that reported for reform and was followed by the Prison Act of 1898. Interim judgement: the question's division is artificial, and what actually decided change was whether a reforming argument reached someone holding office.","The evaluation thread that runs through every paragraph: each time, ask whether the change would have happened had the other side been absent. That single question, asked five times, is what turns the essay from two lists into an argument.","What the conclusion must say: not 'both mattered'. Apply the criterion announced at the start - individuals set the direction, because every reform the state passed can be traced to evidence or a model someone else produced first, while only government made a change reach every prisoner, and the acts that came without inspectors show why. Then name the deciding evidence: the Prisons Act of 1835 and the Prison Act of 1877, which gave the state the inspectors and the control that turned reforming arguments into a national system.","Timing: about 25 minutes. Five to plan the five paragraphs and fix the criterion, eighteen to write, and the last two to check spelling and punctuation and to make sure the specialist terms are present and correctly used: deterrence, retribution, rehabilitation, capital offence, commuted, transportation, penal servitude, separate system, hard labour."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T12:55:03Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":1,"notes":"Re-judged from the kit file: all twenty-eight recorded fixes are present and correct. The new question 11 - 'Responsibility for law enforcement passed from local communities to central government in the years c1700-c1900', with parish constables and the County and Borough Police Act (1856) as its bullets - is sound on every new fact I checked on the web: the hue and cry and the unpaid annual parish constable, the associations for the prosecution of felons, the ending of compulsory parish constables in 1872, the Treasury grant of a quarter of pay and clothing to forces certified efficient by the inspectors of constabulary in 1856, the watch committees of 1835 and the standing joint committees that followed the Local Government Act of 1888, and the Director of Public Prosecutions created by the Prosecution of Offences Act of 1879 and first appointed in 1880; its a states the 11-mark and Level 4 caps in levels.md wording and its sol runs 546 words with a criterion set in the opening, a point beyond the bullets and a substantiated judgement. The 1835 act now reads 'required boroughs to appoint watch committees, which were to appoint constables, but compliance was patchy' in all eight places, with 1839 left permissive; question 4's gibbeting is qualified and question 0's repetition is gone, both solutions inside the 4-mark band. No bank question now reproduces the Paper 1 specimen Option 10 section or the June 2023 Option 10 paper¹, and dupscan prints 'no duplication', so no worked example, plan or model paragraph sets a bank question. Publishable as it stands.\n¹ The closest survivor is question 6 beside June 2023 question 4, but that paper asks why the role of local communities changed in c1000-c1700 at 12 marks, a different period, tariff and concept from the kit's c1700-c1900 explanation of changing methods."}}},"10-4":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"10-4","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This is the modern end of the thematic study, and it is the end you need most often, because Paper 1 Section B asks you to compare across the whole of Crime and punishment in Britain, c1000-present. Question 3 always sets one period against another and normally spans at least a century, so almost every comparison the examiner can set has c1900-present on one side of it: young offenders then and now, the community's role then and now, smuggling then and now, proof by ordeal against proof by fingerprint and DNA. Question 4 and the 16-mark judgement then ask why things changed and how much, and the modern period is where the two big turning points of the whole course sit - the end of the death penalty and the arrival of forensic science. The room teaches the modern story as an argument you can win: what is genuinely new about crime, what only looks new, why the state stopped hanging people, why the public was brought back into law enforcement after a century of being pushed out, and how to use the two case studies the specification names without simply retelling them.","idea":[{"code":"10-4 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Continuity and change in the nature of crimes","h":"Old crimes, new opportunities: theft, smuggling and the crimes technology made possible","t":"The three categories are the same as they were in c1000: crimes against the person, against property and against authority. What changed after c1900 was opportunity. Theft is still the most commonly recorded crime, but the motor car created a whole class of vehicle theft and 'taking without consent', and the computer created fraud, phishing, hacking, identity theft and copyright piracy - offences in which the thief never meets the victim and may not even be in the country. Smuggling did not disappear; it changed cargo, from the brandy, tea and silk of the eighteenth-century gangs to drugs, untaxed cigarettes and alcohol, and to people trafficked for work or for sex. Crimes against authority moved from treason and poaching to terrorism and tax fraud. A top-level answer refuses to be impressed by the newness: it argues that the motives (profit, anger, belief) and the categories are continuous, and that what really changed was the reach of the criminal and the difficulty of catching one."},{"code":"10-4 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Changing definitions of crime, including driving offences","h":"New crimes by Act of Parliament: driving, race and drugs - and the crimes that stopped being crimes","t":"Some modern crimes exist only because Parliament said so. Motoring created an entirely new body of law: the Motor Car Act of 1903 brought driving licences, a speed limit and the offence of reckless driving; the Road Traffic Act of 1930 made third-party insurance compulsory; 1934 added the driving test and a 30 mph limit in built-up areas; the Road Safety Act of 1967 brought the breathalyser and a legal blood-alcohol limit; seat belts became compulsory in front seats in 1983 and hand-held mobile phones were banned at the wheel in 2003. Racial discrimination became unlawful through the Race Relations Acts of 1965, 1968 and 1976, and the Crime and Disorder Act of 1998 created racially aggravated offences; the murder of Stephen Lawrence in 1993 and the Macpherson Report of 1999 changed how such crime was recorded and investigated. Drugs were criminalised by the Dangerous Drugs Act of 1920 and sorted into Classes A, B and C by the Misuse of Drugs Act of 1971. The traffic runs both ways: suicide ceased to be a crime in 1961 and homosexual acts between consenting men over twenty-one in private were decriminalised in 1967. A top-level answer uses that two-way traffic to argue that definitions follow attitudes and technology together, rather than simply 'getting stricter'."},{"code":"10-4 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities","h":"Specialists, science and the public brought back in","t":"Three changes run through modern policing. First, specialisation: the Flying Squad was formed in 1919 to chase motorised criminals, a Fraud Squad followed in 1946, and drugs, dogs, traffic and firearms units followed them, with the National Crime Agency taking on serious organised crime in 2013. Second, science and technology: Scotland Yard opened a Fingerprint Branch in 1901 and secured a conviction on fingerprint evidence in 1902; the Metropolitan Police opened a forensic laboratory at Hendon in 1935; the 999 emergency number began in 1937; two-way radios and cars transformed response; the Police National Computer arrived in 1974; DNA profiling, developed by Alec Jeffreys in 1984, convicted Colin Pitchfork in 1988 and produced a national database in 1995; CCTV, automatic number-plate recognition and body-worn cameras followed. Third, prevention rather than detection: crime prevention officers, Neighbourhood Watch, which began in Britain at Mollington in Cheshire in 1982, and police community support officers, created by the Police Reform Act of 2002. A top-level answer notices the shape of that third change: the community's role was compulsory in the Middle Ages, was pushed aside by the professional forces of the nineteenth century, and has been invited back since 1982 - but as eyes and telephones, never as constables."},{"code":"10-4 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The abolition of the death penalty","h":"From the gallows to the community sentence","t":"The death penalty was dismantled in stages. The Children Act of 1908 ended the execution of anyone under sixteen; the Criminal Justice Act of 1948 abolished penal servitude, hard labour and flogging; the Homicide Act of 1957 limited hanging to five kinds of capital murder and introduced diminished responsibility; the last executions in Britain took place in August 1964; Sydney Silverman's Murder (Abolition of Death Penalty) Act of 1965 suspended hanging for five years and Parliament made abolition permanent in 1969, with the last remaining offences cleared in 1998. Prison changed alongside it: the first open prison, New Hall Camp near Wakefield, opened in 1933; borstals, begun at Borstal in Kent in 1902 and put into statute in 1908, kept young offenders away from adults until they were abolished in 1982, with Young Offender Institutions created in 1988; the age of criminal responsibility has been ten since 1963. Outside prison, probation officers date from 1907, parole and suspended sentences from 1967, community service orders from 1972 and electronic tagging from the 1990s. A top-level answer weighs the drama of abolition against that quieter growth: hanging ended for a handful of people a year, while non-custodial sentences changed what happened to most offenders."},{"code":"10-4 — 3 Case studies: The treatment of Conscientious Objectors in the First and Second World Wars","h":"Case study: conscience on trial, 1916 and 1939","t":"Conscription arrived with the Military Service Act of January 1916, which took single men aged eighteen to forty-one and was extended to married men that May. It contained a conscience clause, but the local tribunals that applied it were dominated by local worthies with a military representative sitting alongside them, and they often treated objection as cowardice. About sixteen thousand men objected; 'alternativists' accepted the Non-Combatant Corps, the Royal Army Medical Corps or the Friends' Ambulance Unit, while about fifteen hundred 'absolutists' refused every form of service; roughly six thousand objectors in all were court-martialled and imprisoned. One group was taken to France in 1916, court-martialled and sentenced to death before the sentences were commuted to ten years' penal servitude; around seventy objectors died as a result of their treatment, and many lost the right to vote for five years after the war. When conscription returned under the National Service (Armed Forces) Act of September 1939, about sixty thousand men registered as objectors. Tribunals were now chaired by a judge with no military member and could grant unconditional exemption, exemption conditional on work of national importance such as farming, mining, ambulance driving or civil defence, or non-combatant service. Hostility survived - objectors were abused as 'conchies' and some were dismissed by employers and local councils - but imprisonment was far rarer. A top-level answer uses the pair as evidence that the definition of a crime moved: refusing to fight went from being punished as a criminal act to being handled as a right the state would test."},{"code":"10-4 — 3 Case studies: The Derek Bentley case","h":"Case study: Derek Bentley and the argument the state could not win","t":"On the night of 2 November 1952 Derek Bentley, aged nineteen, and Christopher Craig, aged sixteen, were caught breaking into a warehouse in Croydon. Bentley was already under arrest when Craig shot and killed PC Sidney Miles. The prosecution relied on the disputed words 'Let him have it, Chris' and on the common purpose rule, under which both men were guilty of the murder. Craig, too young to be hanged, was detained and released in 1963; Bentley was convicted, the jury added a recommendation to mercy, the Home Secretary Sir David Maxwell Fyfe refused a reprieve, and he was hanged at Wandsworth on 28 January 1953. Petitions, protests and questions in the Commons followed, and his sister Iris campaigned for the rest of her life; the conviction was finally quashed by the Court of Appeal in 1998. The case mattered because nothing in it had gone wrong legally: it was decided as the law then required, so the only ground of attack was that the punishment itself was wrong. A top-level answer never simply retells the story. It uses Bentley, with Timothy Evans (hanged in 1950, pardoned in 1966) and Ruth Ellis (hanged in July 1955), as the evidence for a claim: that public doubt, not a change in the law, is what made abolition possible."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 3: one difference, across two periods","setup":"The paper prints: 'Explain one way in which the purpose of imprisonment in the years c1700-c1900 was different from the purpose of imprisonment in the years c1900-present.' (4 marks) Two marks are for analysing a difference and two for the specific information that supports it, and you must say something about both periods. You know that Victorian prisons ran the separate system and set prisoners to hard labour on the treadwheel and the crank, and that since c1900 prisons have been expected to educate and train prisoners and to release most of them on licence. The dates you may need are these: Pentonville opened in 1842; the Prisons Act was passed in 1865; the Gladstone Committee reported in 1895 and the Prison Act followed in 1898; the first open prison opened in 1933; the Criminal Justice Act of 1948 and the Criminal Justice Act of 1967 belong to the modern period.","steps":["Name the difference in one sentence before writing any detail: prisons used to be run to deter and to punish, and are now judged by whether they turn the prisoner out fit to live straight.","Anchor the earlier period with something an examiner can mark: Pentonville, opened in 1842, ran the separate system, in which a prisoner was kept in his own cell and masked when he left it, and the Prisons Act of 1865 enforced a regime summed up at the time as hard labour, hard fare and hard board, with the treadwheel and the crank set to exhaust rather than to produce anything; the Gladstone Committee's report of 1895 and the Prison Act of 1898, which ended the treadwheel and the crank, close that period by admitting that deterrence on its own had failed.","Anchor the modern period with the same precision, and use only evidence that falls inside it: the first open prison, New Hall Camp near Wakefield, opened in 1933; the Criminal Justice Act of 1948 abolished penal servitude, hard labour and flogging; and the Criminal Justice Act of 1967 created parole, so that release under supervision became part of the sentence rather than the end of it, alongside education, training and work in prison.","Turn the contrast into analysis in the last sentence: the difference is in the purpose, because a regime built to make prison so unpleasant that nobody would risk it has been replaced by one measured by whether the prisoner offends again."],"cues":["What single difference are you claiming, in one sentence?","Which fact proves the earlier half of that claim?","Which Act proves the modern half?","What has actually changed - the regime, or the point of it?"]},{"title":"Question 4: three reasons, and getting past the printed bullets","setup":"The paper prints: 'Explain why the treatment of young offenders changed in the years c1900-present.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Children Act 1908\n• borstals\nYou must also use information of your own.' (12 marks) You know that before c1900 children were held in the same gaols as adults, that the first borstal opened at Borstal near Rochester in Kent in 1902, that the Children Act was passed in 1908, and that the age of criminal responsibility was raised to ten in 1963. The mark scheme caps an answer that stays inside those two bullets, so the third reason has to be yours.","steps":["Turn each bullet into a reason rather than a topic: the Children Act stands for the belief that a child in trouble is a different kind of defendant and must be judged in a different court, and borstals stand for the belief that a young offender can be trained out of crime instead of frightened out of it.","Load each bullet with evidence: the Children Act of 1908 set up juvenile courts, forbade the imprisonment of children under fourteen and ended the death sentence for anyone under sixteen; the first borstal had opened at Borstal near Rochester in 1902, and the Prevention of Crime Act of 1908 made a borstal sentence available for offenders aged sixteen to twenty-one, built on training, education and work.","Choose the reason the paper has not given you, because that is what opens the top level: ideas about childhood itself changed, as compulsory schooling and the welfare legislation of the same years treated children as people still being formed, and adult prison was believed to teach crime rather than cure it, which is why the age of criminal responsibility was raised from eight to ten in 1963.","Write a chain rather than a list: a new view of childhood produced the separate court and the training institution, the institutions were then judged on whether the young offender came back, and when borstals failed that test they were abolished in 1982 and replaced by youth custody centres, with Young Offender Institutions following in 1988 and youth offending teams under the Crime and Disorder Act of 1998."],"cues":["What reason does each bullet actually stand for?","Which precise fact does each of those two reasons rest on?","Which reason will you supply yourself, and where in the period would you look for it?","How do your three reasons connect into a single chain?"]},{"title":"The 16-mark judgement: choosing a criterion before you argue","setup":"The paper prints: 'The two World Wars were the main reason for changes in crime and punishment in Britain in the years c1900-present.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Defence of the Realm Act 1914\n• the black market of the Second World War\nYou must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks] You may use anything else you know about the period, including the first women police officers, the treatment of conscientious objectors, and the peacetime changes of the later twentieth century such as the end of hanging, motoring offences, drug crimes and cybercrime.","steps":["Fix the criterion in the opening two sentences, because 'main reason' means nothing until you say what you are measuring: judge each factor by how much of the change it explains and by whether the change outlasted the thing that caused it.","Build the case for the statement from both bullets: the Defence of the Realm Act, passed on 8 August 1914, made criminal what had been ordinary - flying a kite, lighting a bonfire, buying binoculars, talking about naval or military matters in public - and cut pub opening hours; and rationing from January 1940 turned buying without coupons into a crime, with courts empowered to impose heavy fines and imprisonment on black marketeers.","Show that the wars changed law enforcement and punishment too, since the statement covers both: the shortage of men brought women into policing, and in 1915 Edith Smith was appointed at Grantham as the first woman in Britain to hold a police officer's power of arrest; and conscription in 1916 and again in 1939 forced the state to decide what to do with conscientious objectors, moving from courts martial and prison for roughly six thousand men in the first war to tribunals chaired by a judge in the second.","Build the case against from material the bullets do not give you, then apply the criterion instead of summarising: the wartime offences died with the wars, while the changes that lasted - the end of hanging for murder in 1965, the Criminal Justice Act of 1948, motoring offences, drug crimes and cybercrime - grew out of peacetime attitudes, technology and legislation, so war accelerated and redirected change rather than causing most of it, and say on which measure you agree."],"cues":["What will you measure 'main reason' by?","Which offences existed only because there was a war on?","What did the wars change about who enforced the law and how objectors were dealt with?","Which changes since c1900 would have come anyway, and what does that do to the statement?"]},{"title":"Question 4: turning a change of belief into a chain of causes","setup":"A 12-mark question asks: 'Explain why non-custodial alternatives to prison were developed in the years c1900-present.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• probation\n• community service\nYou must also use information of your own.' You know that probation officers were first appointed under an Act of 1907, that community service orders were created in 1972, that the Gladstone Committee had already told the government in 1895 that prison should reform as well as deter, and that later Acts added parole, the suspended sentence and electronic tagging. The mark scheme caps an answer that stays inside the two bullets, so the third reason has to be yours.","steps":["Turn each bullet into a reason rather than a topic: probation stands for the belief that some offenders are better supervised than locked up, and community service stands for the idea that a punishment should repay the community while leaving the offender in his job and his home.","Load each bullet with evidence: the Probation of Offenders Act of 1907 let a court release an offender under a probation officer whose duty was to advise, assist and befriend him; the Criminal Justice Act of 1972 created the community service order, under which an offender of seventeen or over did between forty and two hundred and forty hours of unpaid work instead of going to prison.","Choose the reason the paper has not given you, because that is what opens the top level: prison itself was judged to be failing, since the Gladstone Committee had said in 1895 that it must reform as well as deter, a short sentence taught a petty offender the habits of hardened ones, and a prison place cost far more than supervision in the community.","Write a chain rather than a list: the changed belief about what punishment is for produced the legal machinery, the machinery proved cheaper and no worse at preventing reoffending, and Parliament kept extending it - parole and the suspended sentence in the Criminal Justice Act of 1967, and curfew orders enforced by electronic tags under the Criminal Justice Act of 1991."],"cues":["What reason does each bullet actually stand for?","Which precise fact does each of those two reasons rest on?","Which reason will you supply yourself, and why does the answer need one?","How do the three reasons connect to each other?"]},{"title":"Question 3: finding a similarity when the methods look completely different","setup":"A question asks: 'Explain one way in which crimes against property in the years c1500-c1700 were similar to crimes against property in the years c1900-present.' (4 marks) You know that petty theft filled the courts in the early modern period and that smuggling grew when duties were high, and that modern property crime includes vehicle theft, shoplifting and online fraud.","steps":["Pitch the similarity at the level of motive, not method, since methods always differ across four centuries: in both periods most property crime was ordinary theft committed for profit by people who were not professional criminals.","Anchor the earlier period: in the years c1500-c1700 petty theft dominated the courts, and smuggling grew because duties on goods such as brandy, tea and silk made evasion worth the risk.","Anchor the modern period with the same idea, not a new one: theft is still the most commonly recorded crime, and smuggling continues in drugs and untaxed cigarettes, so both the category and the profit motive have held.","Give one sentence to what has changed, to show you are analysing rather than asserting: the opportunity moved from the pocket and the coast to the car and the computer, but property crime still follows the money."],"cues":["At what level can two periods this far apart actually be similar?","Which early modern facts support that similarity?","Which modern facts support the same point rather than a different one?","How do you acknowledge change without abandoning your similarity?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which Act limited hanging to five kinds of capital murder, and which Act suspended the death penalty for murder altogether?","a":"The Homicide Act of 1957 limited it; the Murder (Abolition of Death Penalty) Act of 1965 suspended it for five years, and abolition was made permanent in 1969."},{"q":"Name two ways the police have used science since 1900 to identify offenders, with a date for each.","a":"Fingerprints - Scotland Yard's Fingerprint Branch opened in 1901 and won a conviction in 1902; DNA profiling - developed by Alec Jeffreys in 1984, it convicted Colin Pitchfork in 1988 and a national database followed in 1995."},{"q":"Why is the Derek Bentley case used as evidence in the argument about capital punishment, rather than as a story?","a":"Because nothing about it was legally wrong: the common purpose rule made him guilty and the Home Secretary was entitled to refuse a reprieve, so the only possible objection was that hanging itself was wrong - which is exactly what the public protest argued."},{"q":"Give one change and one continuity in the treatment of conscientious objectors between the two World Wars.","a":"Change: tribunals after 1939 were chaired by a judge without a military member and commonly granted exemption conditional on work of national importance, so imprisonment was rare. Continuity: objectors still had to justify themselves to a tribunal and still faced public hostility."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 1 Section B is worth 36 marks: Question 3 'Explain one way in which ... was similar to / different from ...' (4 marks); Question 4 'Explain why ...' (12 marks); and a choice of Question 5 or Question 6, ''...' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.' (16 marks, plus up to 4 additional marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology).","Questions 4, 5 and 6 print two stimulus points and the line 'You must also use information of your own.' The caps are printed in the mark scheme: on the 12-mark question, 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'; on the 16-mark question, the same wording with 'Maximum 11 marks'. There is no longer any cap at Level 2.","Paper 1 lasts 1 hour 20 minutes for 52 marks, so work at about a minute a mark: roughly 5 minutes on Question 3, 15 on Question 4 and 25 to 30 on the 16-mark judgement, leaving the final two or three minutes of it to check spelling, punctuation and the specialist terms the extra 4 marks depend on.","Question 3 normally spans at least a century and may cross the key topics of the whole option, so name the period on each side of the comparison ('in the years c1700-c1900 ... whereas since c1900 ...') and put specific evidence in both halves; two of the four marks are for that supporting information. Questions 5 and 6 cover either a complete period or at least 200 years.","The 16-mark question asks for a judgement about the nature, pattern, process or impact of change, so the opening must state the criterion the judgement will use and the conclusion must apply it - Level 4 requires that 'criteria for the required judgement are justified and applied in the process of reaching the overall judgement'."],"questions":[{"q":"Explain one way in which the punishment of theft in the years c1000-c1500 was different from its punishment in the years c1900-present. (4 marks)","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2. Credit one analysed difference: medieval theft was punished on the body and in public, by the stocks or pillory, branding, mutilation or hanging, whereas since c1900 it has been punished by fines, community sentences and graded terms of imprisonment under a single statute, supported by specific detail from both periods.","sol":"One difference is that medieval punishment for theft was physical and public, while modern punishment is neither. In the years c1000-c1500 a petty thief could be put in the stocks or the pillory and a repeat offender branded or mutilated, and from the later thirteenth century the theft of goods worth more than twelve pence counted as grand larceny and could be punished by hanging. Since c1900 theft carries no physical penalty: it is defined by the Theft Act of 1968, with a maximum of seven years' imprisonment, while most thefts are dealt with by a fine or a community sentence. The aim moved from making an example of the offender's body to matching the sentence to the offence.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The difference to reach for is what the punishment was done to: the offender's body, in front of the village, or his money, his time and his liberty.","Decide your single difference first, then hunt for one hard fact from each period to prove it; two of the four marks are for that evidence.","Shape it as: one sentence naming the difference, one sentence of medieval evidence, one sentence of modern evidence, one sentence saying what the change in aim was."],"codes":["10-4 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The abolition of the death penalty"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the role of local communities in law enforcement in the years c1000-c1500 was similar to their role in the years c1900-present. (4 marks)","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2. Credit one analysed similarity: in both periods ordinary people are expected to watch their neighbourhood and report wrongdoing (tithings and the hue and cry; Neighbourhood Watch and reporting to the police), supported by specific detail from both periods.","sol":"One similarity is that ordinary people are still expected to help police their own neighbourhood. In the years c1000-c1500 law enforcement depended on the community itself: every man over twelve belonged to a tithing that was answerable for the conduct of its members, and anyone who heard the hue and cry had to join the chase or be fined. Since c1900 the same principle has returned in a voluntary form through Neighbourhood Watch, which began in Britain at Mollington in Cheshire in 1982, with residents watching each other's property, marking valuables and reporting what they see to a police contact. The duty is no longer compulsory, but the expectation that neighbours are the first line of defence is the same.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about who is expected to notice a crime first, in each of the two periods.","A similarity has to be pitched at the level of principle, because the medieval community had powers that a modern one does not; name the principle, then prove it twice.","Say what the medieval community was obliged to do, then what a modern scheme asks people to do, and finish by naming the shared expectation."],"codes":["10-4 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which smuggling in the years c1700-c1900 was different from smuggling in the years c1900-present. (4 marks)","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2. Credit one analysed difference: modern smuggling is mostly of goods that are themselves illegal, organised internationally and heavily policed, whereas earlier smuggling was duty evasion on legal luxuries and was often tolerated locally.","sol":"One difference is what is smuggled, and therefore how it is treated. In the years c1700-c1900 smuggling was mainly duty evasion on goods that were perfectly legal to own - tea, brandy, silk and tobacco, run ashore by large gangs such as the Hawkhurst gang in Kent - and because high duties were unpopular, whole coastal communities helped and few were willing to inform. Since c1900 the biggest smuggling trade has been in goods that are illegal in themselves, above all drugs controlled under the Misuse of Drugs Act of 1971, alongside untaxed cigarettes and the trafficking of people. Modern smuggling is therefore organised across international borders and prosecuted as serious crime, rather than quietly tolerated by neighbours.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what was actually in the cargo in each period, and whether owning it was a crime.","The difference in the goods produces a difference in attitudes; make that second step, because it is where the analysis marks are.","One sentence of eighteenth-century evidence, one sentence of modern evidence, then a sentence contrasting how communities and the courts responded."],"codes":["10-4 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Continuity and change in the nature of crimes"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which methods of proving guilt in the years c1000-c1500 were different from the methods used in the years c1900-present. (4 marks)","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2. Credit one analysed difference: guilt is now established by physical evidence tested scientifically (fingerprints, DNA) rather than by oath, reputation or divine judgement through trial by ordeal, with specific detail from both periods.","sol":"One difference is what counts as proof. In the years c1000-c1500 there was no way of testing physical evidence, so where witnesses and oath-helpers could not settle a case it went to trial by ordeal, in which God was believed to reveal guilt through a hand that festered after handling hot iron; the Church withdrew its priests from ordeals in 1215. Since c1900 proof has rested on forensic science: Scotland Yard opened a Fingerprint Branch in 1901 and won a conviction on fingerprint evidence the following year, and DNA profiling, developed by Alec Jeffreys in 1984, convicted Colin Pitchfork in 1988. Guilt is now demonstrated by physical traces rather than declared by God or by reputation.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Both periods had to answer the same question - how do you know it was him? - with very different resources.","Name the medieval method and the modern method, then say what each assumed about where truth comes from.","Give one dated example of the medieval method and one dated example of modern forensic evidence, and close by contrasting the two ideas of proof."],"codes":["10-4 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the punishment of murder in the years c1700-c1900 was different from its punishment in the years c1900-present. (4 marks)","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2. Credit one analysed difference: murder no longer carries the death penalty but a mandatory life sentence, whereas in the earlier period hanging was standard and until 1868 public, with specific detail from both periods.","sol":"One difference is that murder no longer carries the death penalty. In the years c1700-c1900 hanging was the standard punishment for murder and, under the Bloody Code, for scores of lesser offences as well, and executions were carried out in public until 1868 precisely because the spectacle was meant to deter. Since c1900 the punishment has been dismantled: the Homicide Act of 1957 restricted hanging to five kinds of capital murder, the last executions in Britain took place in August 1964, and the Murder (Abolition of Death Penalty) Act of 1965 replaced the gallows with a mandatory life sentence. The purpose changed with the punishment, from public deterrence to the removal and eventual supervision of the offender.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The difference here is not only what the punishment was, but what it was for.","Anchor the earlier period with the Bloody Code and public executions; anchor the modern one with the Acts that ended hanging.","Close with a sentence that names the change of purpose, because that is what turns a list of facts into analysis."],"codes":["10-4 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The abolition of the death penalty","10-4 — 3 Case studies: The Derek Bentley case"]},{"q":"Explain why the death penalty was abolished in Britain in the years c1900-present.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Derek Bentley case\n• the Homicide Act 1957\nYou must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit reasons analysed as a causal chain: public doubt created by contested executions (Bentley, Evans, Ellis); the failure of the Homicide Act 1957 to produce defensible distinctions; the wider post-war turn towards reform and the parliamentary campaign led by Sydney Silverman. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Abolition came because particular executions destroyed public confidence, because the attempt to reform capital punishment made it look illogical, and because Parliament had changed its mind about what punishment was for.\n\nThe first reason was doubt created by specific cases. Derek Bentley was hanged on 28 January 1953 for the murder of PC Sidney Miles, although he was already under arrest when Christopher Craig, at sixteen too young to hang, fired the shot; the jury added a recommendation to mercy and the Home Secretary refused a reprieve. Timothy Evans, hanged in 1950, was pardoned in 1966 once John Christie's crimes were known, and Ruth Ellis was hanged in July 1955. Each case demonstrated the one thing that cannot be answered: a hanging cannot be undone if the verdict or the mercy decision was wrong.\n\nThe second reason was that reform failed. The Homicide Act of 1957 tried to save the death penalty by confining it to five kinds of capital murder - by shooting or explosion, in the course of theft, while resisting arrest, of a police or prison officer, and a second murder - and introduced diminished responsibility. The distinctions proved impossible to defend, since a poisoner escaped the rope while a burglar whose accomplice fired a gun did not. An Act designed to answer the abolitionists handed them their strongest argument.\n\nThe third reason, and the one that let the other two count, was a change in the purpose of punishment. Post-war policy was already moving towards reform: the Criminal Justice Act of 1948 had abolished penal servitude, hard labour and flogging, open prisons were spreading and probation was expanding. Sydney Silverman's private member's bill therefore found a Commons majority in 1965, suspending hanging for five years, and Parliament made abolition permanent in 1969.\n\nSo the cases created doubt, the 1957 Act turned that doubt into a legal absurdity, and a Parliament already thinking in terms of rehabilitation was willing to act on both.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Each bullet is standing in for a reason; work out what general reason Bentley is evidence for before you write about Bentley.","Three reasons, each a paragraph that states the reason, proves it and then explains how it pushed towards abolition - and one of the three must come from outside the bullets.","For your own reason, look at what else was happening to punishment in the same decades: what had already been abolished, and what was being built instead of the gallows?"],"codes":["10-4 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The abolition of the death penalty","10-4 — 3 Case studies: The Derek Bentley case"]},{"q":"Explain why there were changes in the nature of crime in the years c1900-present.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• cybercrime\n• smuggling\nYou must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit reasons analysed rather than listed: new technology creating new opportunities (computers, and the motor car); changes in the law creating whole categories of offence (drugs, driving); globalisation and easier movement of goods and people. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The nature of crime changed because technology created opportunities that had never existed, because Parliament made new things illegal, and because offenders and their victims stopped having to be in the same place.\n\nThe first reason is technology. Computers and then the internet produced fraud, phishing, hacking, identity theft and large-scale copyright piracy. The offences underneath are old ones - theft and deception - but the method removed every practical limit on them: a single offender can attempt a million thefts in an afternoon, never meet a victim, and leave no physical trace at the scene. The change in the nature of crime is therefore a change of scale and anonymity rather than of motive.\n\nThe second reason is that the law itself creates crime. Smuggling did not grow because people became greedier; it changed because what was worth smuggling changed. The Dangerous Drugs Act of 1920 and the Misuse of Drugs Act of 1971 made an entire trade illegal, and heavy duties on tobacco and alcohol kept the older kind of smuggling alive, so brandy and tea gave way to heroin, cocaine, untaxed cigarettes and trafficked people. The same is true of motoring: the Motor Car Act of 1903 and the Road Traffic Acts of the 1930s invented offences impossible before the car existed - driving without a licence or insurance, speeding, and driving under the influence of drink or drugs, an offence under the Road Traffic Act of 1930 to which the Road Safety Act of 1967 added the breathalyser and a prescribed blood-alcohol limit.\n\nThe third reason, which the bullets do not offer, is the collapse of distance. Cheap transport and open borders let criminal organisations operate internationally, so drugs smuggling, people trafficking and online fraud are commonly run from outside the jurisdiction that has to police them, which is why bodies such as the National Crime Agency, created in 2013, work across borders rather than in a single county.\n\nUnderneath all three, however, the categories are continuous: theft is still the commonest recorded crime. What changed was opportunity, definition and reach rather than human motive.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask of each bullet: is this a new crime, or an old crime with a new opportunity?","Build three reasons - something invented, something made illegal, and something about distance - and give each a paragraph that ends by explaining the change it caused.","Your own material is easiest to find in the motor car: list what became an offence only because cars existed."],"codes":["10-4 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Continuity and change in the nature of crimes","10-4 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Changing definitions of crime, including driving offences"]},{"q":"Explain why methods of policing changed in the years c1900-present.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• fingerprinting\n• Neighbourhood Watch\nYou must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit reasons analysed as causes of change: scientific advance making physical evidence decisive; the shift of policy towards crime prevention and public involvement; the changing nature of crime (motorised, organised, international) forcing specialisation and national organisation. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Policing changed because science gave the police new ways of proving guilt, because governments decided that preventing crime mattered as much as detecting it, and because criminals themselves became mobile, organised and international.\n\nThe first reason is scientific advance. Scotland Yard opened a Fingerprint Branch in 1901 and secured a conviction on fingerprint evidence the following year; a forensic laboratory opened at Hendon in 1935; DNA profiling, developed by Alec Jeffreys in 1984, convicted Colin Pitchfork in 1988 and produced a national database in 1995. Each of these shifted the weight of a case away from confession and eyewitnesses towards physical traces, which in turn required trained specialists, laboratories and careful preservation of crime scenes - a different kind of police work from the beat constable's.\n\nThe second reason is a change of purpose. After a century in which professional forces had pushed the public out of law enforcement, policy turned back towards prevention and towards using the community as eyes. Neighbourhood Watch began in Britain at Mollington in Cheshire in 1982; crime prevention officers advised on locks, lighting and property marking; CCTV spread through town centres; and the Police Reform Act of 2002 created police community support officers to give a visible, preventive presence on the street.\n\nThe third reason, which neither bullet supplies, is that crime itself changed. The motor car gave criminals speed, so the Metropolitan Police formed the Flying Squad in 1919; commercial fraud produced a Fraud Squad in 1946; drugs, firearms and traffic units followed; the 999 number arrived in 1937 and the Police National Computer in 1974 so that information could move as fast as offenders; and serious organised crime, which crosses county and national borders, is now handled by the National Crime Agency, created in 2013.\n\nThe three reasons pull in the same direction: policing became a specialised, scientific and national activity, and precisely because it had, the public had to be invited back in to do the watching the police could no longer do everywhere.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["One bullet is about catching offenders after the event and the other about stopping the event; each stands for a different reason.","Three reasons, each a paragraph with dated evidence and a closing sentence explaining what about policing that reason changed.","For the reason beyond the bullets, ask what criminals gained in the twentieth century that forced the police to organise differently."],"codes":["10-4 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities"]},{"q":"Explain why the treatment of conscientious objectors in the Second World War was different from their treatment in the First World War.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• military tribunals\n• public attitudes\nYou must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit reasons analysed as causes of change: the different composition and powers of the tribunals; the softening (though not disappearance) of public hostility; the lessons the state drew from the treatment of absolutists after 1916 and the demand for civilian labour in a total war. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Objectors were treated differently in the second war because the machinery that judged them had been rebuilt, because public hostility had softened, and because the state had learned that punishing objectors cost more than using them.\n\nThe first reason is the tribunals. The Military Service Act of January 1916 contained a conscience clause but left it to local tribunals made up of local worthies, with a military representative sitting alongside them, who often treated objection as cowardice and set humiliating tests of sincerity. Under the National Service (Armed Forces) Act of September 1939 the tribunals were chaired by a judge and had no military member, and they could grant unconditional exemption, exemption conditional on work of national importance such as farming, mining, ambulance driving or civil defence, or non-combatant service. A body designed to test conscience rather than to catch shirkers produced very different verdicts.\n\nThe second reason is public attitudes. In the first war objectors met white feathers and dismissal; about sixteen thousand objected, roughly six thousand of them were court-martialled and imprisoned, around seventy died as a result of their treatment, and many lost the right to vote for five years after the war. In the second, about sixty thousand registered, and although they were abused as 'conchies' and some were sacked by employers and local councils, the memory of the trenches had made refusal to kill seem less simply contemptible.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond both bullets: experience and necessity. The government of 1916 had discovered that imprisoning absolutists, and taking one group to France to be sentenced to death before commuting the sentences, produced martyrs and damaging publicity rather than obedience. A war fought in factories, mines, fields and bombed cities also had obvious uses for the men themselves, so directing an objector into a coal mine or an ambulance served the war effort better than a prison cell.\n\nThe treatment changed, then, because the state stopped trying to break objectors and started trying to employ them.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Compare the two sets of tribunals as institutions before you compare the verdicts they gave.","Three reasons, each proved with figures or named machinery, each ending by explaining why treatment became milder - one of them from outside the bullets.","For your own reason, ask what the government of the second war had watched happen to the absolutists of the first, and what a war of production needed from its manpower."],"codes":["10-4 — 3 Case studies: The treatment of Conscientious Objectors in the First and Second World Wars"]},{"q":"Explain why some actions ceased to be crimes in the years c1900-present.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Suicide Act 1961\n• the Sexual Offences Act 1967\nYou must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit reasons analysed as causes rather than a list of repeals: an official inquiry drawing a line between private conduct and public harm (the Wolfenden report of 1957); behaviour reclassified as illness or misfortune rather than wickedness; the practical failure of laws that deterred people from seeking help and invited blackmail; and a reforming Home Office with a Commons majority willing to legislate in the 1960s. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Some actions stopped being crimes because the state accepted a line between private conduct and public harm, because certain behaviour was reclassified as illness rather than wickedness, and because the old laws were doing more damage than the conduct they forbade.\n\nThe first reason was the reframing of private morality. The Wolfenden Committee, reporting in 1957, argued that homosexual behaviour between consenting adults in private was not the business of the criminal law. Ten years of campaigning turned that argument into the Sexual Offences Act of 1967, which decriminalised homosexual acts in private between two men aged twenty-one or over in England and Wales, although it did not extend to the armed forces or the merchant navy. Parliament changed the law by accepting a test of harm, not by deciding that it approved of the conduct.\n\nThe second reason was that behaviour once treated as sin came to be treated as misfortune. Attempting suicide was a criminal offence until 1961, so a person who survived could be prosecuted for it; the Suicide Act of that year abolished the crime of suicide in England and Wales, while making it an offence, punishable by up to fourteen years, to encourage or assist the suicide of another. The survivor now needed treatment, and only the person who exploited him needed punishing.\n\nThe third reason, which the bullets do not give you, is that the old law had become unworkable. Prosecuting the suicidal discouraged families from calling for help; the law on homosexual acts was widely described as a blackmailer's charter, because a man could be threatened with exposure; and enforcement was erratic and intrusive. A Commons with a large majority and a reforming Home Secretary in Roy Jenkins was ready to act, in the same years that produced the end of hanging for murder in 1965 and the Abortion Act of 1967.\n\nDecriminalisation therefore followed a changed test of what the criminal law is for. Definitions of crime move when society's view of harm moves, and they move in both directions.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Each bullet is a repeal, so the reason you need is the change of mind that made the repeal possible, not the story of the Act.","Three reasons, each a paragraph that names the reason, proves it with a dated Act or report and explains why Parliament acted; one of the three must come from outside the bullets.","For your own reason, ask what the old law was doing to people: who was frightened out of asking for help, and who was being blackmailed?"],"codes":["10-4 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Changing definitions of crime, including driving offences"]},{"q":"'The abolition of the death penalty was the most significant change in punishment in the years c1900-present.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Derek Bentley case\n• open prisons\nYou must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit a criteria-based judgement weighing abolition against other changes - open prisons and the reshaping of imprisonment, the separate treatment of young offenders, and the growth of non-custodial punishment - reaching a substantiated overall judgement. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Significance has to be measured before it can be argued about. Two measures are useful here: how far a change altered what punishment is for, and how many offenders it actually reached. On the first, the abolition of the death penalty is the most significant change of the period; on the second, it is not, and a full answer has to say so.\n\nThe case for the statement rests on what abolition settled. The death penalty was dismantled in stages - the Children Act of 1908 ended the execution of anyone under sixteen, the Homicide Act of 1957 confined hanging to five kinds of capital murder, the last executions took place in August 1964, and the Murder (Abolition of Death Penalty) Act of 1965 suspended hanging for murder before Parliament made abolition permanent in 1969. What ended was not simply a method but a claim: that the state might kill a citizen as a punishment. The Derek Bentley case shows why that claim became impossible to defend. Bentley was hanged on 28 January 1953 for the murder of PC Sidney Miles although he was already under arrest when Christopher Craig, too young at sixteen to be hanged, fired the shot; the jury recommended mercy and the Home Secretary refused a reprieve. Nothing in the case was legally wrong, which is precisely why the protest could only be an argument about the punishment itself - and when his conviction was quashed in 1998, the irreversibility of the sentence was exactly the point. Judged by purpose, this is the hinge of the period.\n\nThe case against the statement rests on reach. Hanging ended for a handful of people each year, and the changes that touched most offenders were quieter. Imprisonment itself was remade: the first open prison, New Hall Camp near Wakefield, opened in 1933, and open prisons treat low-risk prisoners as people being prepared for release rather than simply held, with work and study outside the walls; the Criminal Justice Act of 1948 had already abolished penal servitude, hard labour and flogging. Young offenders were separated out, through borstals from 1902 and 1908 and Young Offender Institutions from 1988, with the age of criminal responsibility fixed at ten in 1963.\n\nThe change with the widest reach, which neither stimulus point offers, is the growth of punishment outside prison altogether. Probation officers date from 1907, parole and suspended sentences from 1967, community service orders from 1972 and electronic tagging from the 1990s, while fines remain the commonest sentence of all. Far more offenders every year are supervised, fined or tagged than were ever hanged, and that is the change that decides what punishment actually looks like for most people who are punished.\n\nOn balance, I agree with the statement on the criterion of principle and disagree on the criterion of reach. Abolition is the most significant change because it defined the outer limit of what punishment may do, and everything built afterwards - open prisons, borstals, community sentences - assumes that limit. But if significance is measured by the number of lives a change alters, the steady growth of non-custodial punishment matters more, because it, and not the gallows, is what most offenders now meet.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Before you agree or disagree, decide what would make one change more significant than another; there is more than one reasonable measure.","Take the statement seriously first, then set at least two rival changes against it, each with dated evidence and an interim judgement.","The material that lifts this beyond the bullets is what happens to offenders who never go to prison at all - find four such punishments and date them."],"codes":["10-4 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The abolition of the death penalty","10-4 — 3 Case studies: The Derek Bentley case"]},{"q":"'Science and technology were the main reason for changes in law enforcement in the years c1900-present.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• fingerprinting\n• Neighbourhood Watch\nYou must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit a weighed judgement: scientific advance against the changing nature of crime, government policy and organisation, and the shift towards prevention and community involvement, with a criterion applied to reach a substantiated conclusion. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Science changed what the police can do; it does not by itself explain why they decided to do it. Judging 'the main reason' means asking which factor set the direction of change rather than which supplied the equipment, and on that test science and technology are better described as the means than the cause.\n\nThe case for the statement is strong on methods. Scotland Yard opened a Fingerprint Branch in 1901 and won a conviction on fingerprint evidence the following year, so for the first time a criminal could be tied to a scene he denied ever visiting. A forensic laboratory opened at Hendon in 1935. DNA profiling, developed by Alec Jeffreys in 1984, convicted Colin Pitchfork in 1988 and produced a national database in 1995, which allows offences to be solved years after they were committed. Alongside the laboratory came the machinery of response: the 999 number in 1937, cars and two-way radio, the Police National Computer in 1974, CCTV, automatic number-plate recognition and body-worn cameras. Each of these reshaped police work into something a Victorian constable would not recognise, and each required trained specialists rather than men on a beat.\n\nBut the reason the police wanted them was that crime had changed. The motor car gave thieves speed, and the Metropolitan Police answered with the Flying Squad in 1919; commercial fraud produced a Fraud Squad in 1946; drugs, firearms and traffic units followed; serious organised crime that crosses borders is now handled by the National Crime Agency, created in 2013. The direction of causation matters: forces did not specialise because laboratories existed, they built laboratories and squads because the offences they faced had become mobile, organised and technical.\n\nGovernment policy is a second rival factor, and it explains changes that no technology required. The Police Reform Act of 2002 created police community support officers; the Macpherson Report of 1999, after the murder of Stephen Lawrence in 1993, forced forces to change how racist crime was recorded and investigated and how they dealt with the communities they policed. These are decisions about legitimacy and accountability, not about equipment.\n\nThe third rival factor, and the one that goes furthest beyond the stimulus, is the deliberate return to prevention. Neighbourhood Watch began in Britain at Mollington in Cheshire in 1982, crime prevention officers advised the public on locks, lighting and property marking, and towns were fitted with cameras. This is a change of philosophy, reversing the nineteenth-century assumption that policing was a professional monopoly, and it happened because the police could not be everywhere and knew it.\n\nMy judgement is that science and technology are the most visible reason but not the main one. They are the tools, and tools are adopted for reasons: the changing nature of crime made new tools necessary, and government policy decided which to buy and how the public should be involved. If the criterion is which factor determined the direction modern policing took - specialised, preventive and accountable - then the changing nature of crime has the better claim, with science as its indispensable servant.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Distinguish between what made a change possible and what made it happen; the whole judgement turns on that distinction.","Give science its strongest run first, then set at least two other factors against it, each with dated evidence and an interim judgement.","Ask why a force would create a squad in the first place: what had criminals gained that a beat constable could not match?"],"codes":["10-4 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities"]},{"q":"'There was more continuity than change in the nature of criminal activity in the years c1900-present.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• theft\n• drug crimes\nYou must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit a weighed judgement distinguishing continuity of categories and motives (theft, smuggling, crimes against the person and authority) from change in definitions, methods and scale (driving offences, drug crimes, cybercrime, race crimes, decriminalisation), with a criterion applied. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Whether this period looks like continuity or change depends on what is being counted. If the question is what people do and why, continuity dominates; if it is what the law calls a crime and how offences are committed, change dominates. I shall judge on both and conclude that the categories persisted while the definitions and the methods were transformed.\n\nThe case for continuity begins with theft, which has been the commonest recorded crime in every period of this course and remains so. Shoplifting, burglary and vehicle theft are the same offence against property that the medieval hue and cry was raised for, committed for the same motive. Smuggling has also continued unbroken: high duties on tobacco and alcohol keep the eighteenth-century trade alive in a new cargo, and the profit from evading duty is exactly what drew the Kent and Sussex gangs. Crimes against the person - assault and murder - and crimes against authority have likewise persisted, because the reasons people commit them do not change with the century.\n\nThe case for change begins with crimes that could not previously exist. Drug crime was created by statute: the Dangerous Drugs Act of 1920 and then the Misuse of Drugs Act of 1971, which graded substances into Classes A, B and C and made possession, supply and production separate offences, turned a trade into a criminal industry that now dominates smuggling and funds much acquisitive crime. Motoring created an entire body of offence that had no earlier equivalent, from the licences and speed limit of the Motor Car Act of 1903 to compulsory insurance in 1930, the driving test in 1934 and the breathalyser under the Road Safety Act of 1967; these are the offences most ordinary people actually commit.\n\nThe change that goes furthest beyond the bullets is the disappearance of distance. Cybercrime allows fraud, hacking and identity theft in which offender and victim never meet, the offence crosses jurisdictions in a second and a single criminal can attempt thousands of thefts at once. Definitions have also moved in both directions, which is itself a change: racially aggravated offences were created by the Crime and Disorder Act of 1998, while suicide ceased to be a crime in 1961 and homosexual acts between consenting adult men in private were decriminalised in 1967.\n\nWeighing the two, the continuity is real but shallow, and the change is real but narrow. The categories - crimes against the person, property and authority - have not altered since c1000, and the motives have not either, so at the level of human behaviour the statement is correct. But at the level that matters for law enforcement, what counts as a crime, who can commit it and from how far away have all changed beyond recognition in a single century. On the criterion of what the police and the courts actually have to deal with, therefore, I disagree with the statement: the appearance of continuity comes from keeping the old labels on activities that are now conducted in entirely new ways.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Continuity and change questions need you to say what is being kept the same and what is being altered; they are rarely the same thing.","Build one side from motives and categories and the other from definitions and methods, with dated evidence in each paragraph and an interim judgement.","The evidence that decides it is the crime that could not have been committed in 1900 at all - find one and explain what makes it genuinely new."],"codes":["10-4 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Continuity and change in the nature of crimes","10-4 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Changing definitions of crime, including driving offences"]},{"q":"'Changing attitudes in society were the main reason why the definition of crime changed in the years c1900-present.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• conscientious objectors\n• driving offences\nYou must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit a weighed judgement setting changing attitudes (conscientious objectors, race crimes, decriminalisation in the 1960s) against technology (motoring, cybercrime) and scientific or medical knowledge (drugs, blood-alcohol limits), with an applied criterion. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Attitudes decide which harms a society is willing to punish; technology decides which harms exist to be punished. Judging 'the main reason' means asking which of the two does more of the work, and the answer differs depending on whether we look at crimes that express a society's values or crimes that manage its machinery.\n\nThe case for attitudes is strongest where nothing material had changed at all. The treatment of conscientious objectors shows a definition shifting under the pressure of opinion alone. Under the Military Service Act of January 1916 the conscience clause was administered by local tribunals with a military representative, and refusal was punished as something close to a criminal act: about sixteen thousand men objected, roughly six thousand of them were court-martialled and imprisoned, around seventy died from their treatment, and many lost the right to vote for five years after the war. When conscription returned in September 1939, about sixty thousand registered, tribunals were chaired by a judge with no military member, and most objectors received unconditional exemption, exemption conditional on work of national importance, or non-combatant service. The war was the same; the view of conscience was not.\n\nThe same is true of race. Discrimination that had been lawful became criminal through the Race Relations Acts of 1965, 1968 and 1976 and the racially aggravated offences created by the Crime and Disorder Act of 1998, and the Macpherson Report of 1999, following the murder of Stephen Lawrence in 1993, changed how such crime was investigated. Attitudes also removed crimes: suicide ceased to be an offence in 1961 and homosexual acts between consenting adult men in private were decriminalised in 1967. Only a change of mind can explain a law running in both directions.\n\nAgainst this stands technology, and the motor car is the clearest case. No shift in attitudes created the offences of driving without a licence or insurance, speeding or dangerous driving; a fast, heavy machine in crowded streets did, and Parliament legislated in 1903, 1930 and 1934 because people were being killed. The internet did the same for fraud and identity theft. These are definitions written to control a machine, not to express a value.\n\nA third factor, beyond both stimulus points, is knowledge. The Dangerous Drugs Act of 1920 and the Misuse of Drugs Act of 1971 depended on medical understanding of addiction and harm, and the breathalyser and legal blood-alcohol limit introduced by the Road Safety Act of 1967 rested on evidence about alcohol and reaction times. Here the harm was real before the law noticed it, and it was science, not sentiment, that made the case.\n\nMy judgement is a divided one, and the criterion is which kind of definition is in question. Where the law defines the relationship between citizens - conscience, race, sexuality, suicide - changing attitudes are decisively the main reason, because nothing else altered. Where the law regulates new dangers - motoring, drugs, cybercrime - technology and knowledge supply the reason and attitudes merely set the penalty. Since the offences that most people encounter are the regulatory ones, I agree with the statement only in part: attitudes changed what Britain was prepared to call a crime, but they did not create most of the crimes it now prosecutes.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Sort the modern changes in definition into two piles before you write: those where something new appeared in the world, and those where only opinion moved.","Use the case study as the clearest proof for one side, then set technology against it, each paragraph carrying dates and an interim judgement.","A divided judgement scores well if the criterion is stated: ask what kind of definition each factor explains best."],"codes":["10-4 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Changing definitions of crime, including driving offences","10-4 — 3 Case studies: The treatment of Conscientious Objectors in the First and Second World Wars"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Which Act suspended the death penalty for murder in Britain, and in what year?","a":"The Murder (Abolition of Death Penalty) Act, 1965; abolition was made permanent in 1969."},{"q":"Where and in which year was the first British Neighbourhood Watch scheme set up?","a":"At Mollington in Cheshire, in 1982."},{"q":"Who fired the shot that killed PC Sidney Miles in 1952, and who was hanged for the murder?","a":"Christopher Craig fired the shot; Derek Bentley, who was already under arrest, was hanged on 28 January 1953."},{"q":"What did the Road Safety Act of 1967 introduce to deal with drink-driving?","a":"The breathalyser and a legal limit for alcohol in a driver's blood."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Misuse of Drugs Act 1971","back":"It sorted controlled drugs into Classes A, B and C, with the heaviest penalties for Class A, and made possession, supply and production separate offences. Cannabis has been moved between classes since.","code":"10-4 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Changing definitions of crime, including driving offences","open":false},{"front":"The Race Relations Acts, 1965-1976","back":"The 1965 Act banned racial discrimination in public places and made incitement to racial hatred an offence; 1968 extended this to housing and employment; 1976 created the Commission for Racial Equality.","code":"10-4 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Changing definitions of crime, including driving offences","open":false},{"front":"Driving offences in the twentieth century","back":"The Motor Car Act 1903 brought licences, a speed limit and reckless driving; the Road Traffic Act 1930 made insurance compulsory; 1934 added the driving test and a 30 mph urban limit; 1967 brought the breathalyser.","code":"10-4 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Changing definitions of crime, including driving offences","open":false},{"front":"Cybercrime","back":"Crime moved online: fraud, phishing, hacking, identity theft, copyright piracy and extortion. The offence, theft or deception, is old; the scale, the anonymity and the ability to strike from outside the jurisdiction are new.","code":"10-4 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Continuity and change in the nature of crimes","open":false},{"front":"Smuggling since c1900","back":"The cargo changed: brandy, tea and silk gave way to drugs, untaxed cigarettes and alcohol, and to people trafficked for work or sex. Profit from evading a duty or a ban is the constant.","code":"10-4 — 1 Nature and changing definitions of criminal activity: Continuity and change in the nature of crimes","open":false},{"front":"Neighbourhood Watch","back":"Britain's first scheme began at Mollington in Cheshire in 1982. Residents watch property, mark valuables and report to a police contact; they have no powers of arrest. Prevention rather than detection.","code":"10-4 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities","open":false},{"front":"Fingerprints and DNA in British policing","back":"Scotland Yard opened a Fingerprint Branch in 1901 and won a conviction on fingerprint evidence in 1902. Alec Jeffreys developed DNA profiling in 1984; it convicted Colin Pitchfork in 1988, and a national database followed in 1995.","code":"10-4 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities","open":false},{"front":"Police specialisation since 1900","back":"Forces grew specialist units: the Flying Squad in 1919 to chase motorised criminals, a Fraud Squad in 1946, then drugs, dog, traffic and firearms sections, and the National Crime Agency in 2013 for serious organised crime.","code":"10-4 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The role of the authorities and local communities","open":false},{"front":"Murder (Abolition of Death Penalty) Act 1965","back":"Sydney Silverman's private member's bill suspended hanging for murder for five years; Parliament made abolition permanent in 1969. The last executions in Britain had taken place in August 1964.","code":"10-4 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The abolition of the death penalty","open":false},{"front":"Homicide Act 1957","back":"It limited hanging to five kinds of capital murder, such as murder by shooting or the murder of a police officer, and introduced diminished responsibility. Its indefensible distinctions strengthened the abolitionists' case.","code":"10-4 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The abolition of the death penalty","open":false},{"front":"Open prisons","back":"New Hall Camp near Wakefield opened in 1933 as Britain's first open prison. Low-risk prisoners live without high walls, work or study outside and are prepared for release rather than simply held.","code":"10-4 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The abolition of the death penalty","open":false},{"front":"Borstals and Young Offender Institutions","back":"Borstals, begun at Borstal in Kent in 1902 and put into statute in 1908, held young offenders apart from adults with training and discipline. They were abolished in 1982, and Young Offender Institutions were created in 1988.","code":"10-4 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The abolition of the death penalty","open":false},{"front":"Non-custodial punishments in modern Britain","back":"Probation officers date from 1907, parole and suspended sentences from 1967, community service orders from 1972 and electronic tagging from the 1990s. Most offenders are fined or supervised rather than imprisoned.","code":"10-4 — 2 The nature of law enforcement and punishment: The abolition of the death penalty","open":false},{"front":"Conscientious objectors in the First World War","back":"Conscription came in January 1916 with a conscience clause judged by local tribunals. About sixteen thousand objected; roughly six thousand were court-martialled and imprisoned, around seventy died from their treatment, and many lost the vote for five years afterwards.","code":"10-4 — 3 Case studies: The treatment of Conscientious Objectors in the First and Second World Wars","open":false},{"front":"The Derek Bentley case","back":"On 2 November 1952 Christopher Craig, aged sixteen, shot PC Sidney Miles in Croydon. Bentley, nineteen and already under arrest, was convicted under the common purpose rule and hanged on 28 January 1953. His conviction was quashed in 1998.","code":"10-4 — 3 Case studies: The Derek Bentley case","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 1 Question 5, 16 marks)","items":["The question: ''Changes in punishment in the years c1900-present were mainly the result of changing attitudes in society.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the Derek Bentley case • open prisons. You must also use information of your own.' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks] This is one paragraph from the middle of the essay, putting the case for the statement.","Changing public attitudes did the decisive work in ending capital punishment, because the executions that turned opinion were legally correct and could therefore only be attacked on moral ground. Derek Bentley, nineteen years old, illiterate and assessed as having the mental age of a child, was already under arrest on the warehouse roof in Croydon when Christopher Craig, who at sixteen was too young to hang, shot PC Sidney Miles on 2 November 1952; the jury that convicted Bentley under the common purpose rule added a recommendation to mercy, the Home Secretary Sir David Maxwell Fyfe refused a reprieve, and Bentley was hanged at Wandsworth on 28 January 1953. Nothing in the law had failed. The case was decided exactly as the law then required, which is why the protest that followed - the petitions, the questions in the Commons, and the campaign his sister Iris sustained until her death - was an argument about whether hanging was right rather than about whether the verdict was. The same pattern holds for Timothy Evans, hanged in 1950 and pardoned in 1966, and for Ruth Ellis, hanged in July 1955. Governments followed that shift rather than leading it: the Homicide Act of 1957 was an attempt to save capital punishment by narrowing it, and when its five categories of capital murder proved impossible to defend, Sydney Silverman's bill of 1965 carried the Commons. On this issue, therefore, attitudes were not one factor among several; they were the pressure that made legislation unavoidable.","Why this is Level 4: the explanation is directed consistently at the conceptual focus - what caused the change, not what the change was - and the line of reasoning is sustained from the opening claim to the interim judgement at the end. The information is precisely selected rather than narrated: the ages of both youths, the date of the shooting, the common purpose rule, the named Home Secretary, the date of the execution, and two further cases used as corroboration rather than as extra story. The final sentence applies a criterion (which factor made legislation unavoidable) instead of restating the point, which is what 'criteria for the required judgement are justified and applied' means at this level."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["The same question and the same paragraph of the essay, written as a Level 2 answer so that the gap is visible.","Attitudes in society changed a lot in the twentieth century and this is why punishments changed. People did not like hanging any more because of cases like Derek Bentley, who was hanged even though he did not shoot the policeman, and lots of people protested about it and signed petitions. This made the government think again and eventually the death penalty was abolished. Open prisons were also brought in because attitudes had changed and people thought prisoners should be helped instead of just punished. This shows that changing attitudes were the main reason why punishment changed.","What it meets and what it misses: it meets the Level 2 descriptor - 'an explanation is given showing limited analysis and with implicit or unsustained links to the conceptual focus', with 'accurate and relevant information' but only 'some knowledge and understanding'. The link between the protest and the legislation is asserted rather than explained, and there is no date, no name beyond Bentley's, no Act and no figure. It misses the Level 3 requirement that the explanation be 'mainly directed at the conceptual focus' because it slides into describing what happened, and it misses Level 4 entirely on two counts: nothing is precisely selected, and the closing sentence restates the statement instead of judging it. Note also that it uses only the two stimulus points: that no longer caps it within Level 2, where an answer confined to the stimulus may now reach the top of the level, but however much the analysis improved it would be held to 11 marks and shut out of Level 4, because it does not address three or more aspects of content."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the nature of crime and new definitions of crime, c1900-present","items":["Theft remains the most commonly recorded crime, as it has been in every period of this course; the categories of crime (against the person, against property, against authority) are unchanged since c1000.","New forms of theft: vehicle theft and taking a vehicle without consent, shoplifting in self-service shops, and online fraud, phishing, hacking and identity theft.","Copyright theft: illegal copying and sharing of music, film and software.","Smuggling changed cargo: from tea, brandy, silk and tobacco before c1900 to drugs, untaxed cigarettes and alcohol, and people trafficked for work or sex.","Motor Car Act 1903: driving licences, a speed limit for motor cars and the offence of reckless driving.","Road Traffic Act 1930: compulsory third-party insurance and the offence of dangerous driving.","Road Traffic Act 1934: the driving test and a 30 mph speed limit in built-up areas.","Road Safety Act 1967: the breathalyser and a legal limit for alcohol in the blood of a driver.","Seat belts compulsory in front seats from 1983; hand-held mobile phones banned at the wheel in 2003.","Dangerous Drugs Act 1920 and Misuse of Drugs Act 1971: the latter created Classes A, B and C and separate offences of possession, supply and production.","Race Relations Act 1965 (discrimination in public places; incitement to racial hatred), 1968 (housing and employment), 1976 (Commission for Racial Equality).","Crime and Disorder Act 1998: racially aggravated offences. Stephen Lawrence was murdered in 1993; the Macpherson Report of 1999 called the Metropolitan Police institutionally racist.","Crimes removed from the law: suicide ceased to be an offence in 1961; homosexual acts between consenting men over twenty-one in private were decriminalised in 1967.","Terrorism and large-scale tax fraud are the modern crimes against authority, replacing treason and poaching."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: law enforcement and policing, c1900-present","items":["Fingerprint Branch opened at Scotland Yard in 1901; the first British conviction on fingerprint evidence followed in 1902.","Women were recruited into policing during and after the First World War and were given powers of arrest in the 1920s.","Flying Squad formed by the Metropolitan Police in 1919 to pursue criminals using motor vehicles.","Metropolitan Police forensic laboratory opened at Hendon in 1935.","The 999 emergency telephone number began in London in 1937.","Fraud Squad formed in 1946; drugs, dog, traffic and firearms units followed.","Police Act 1964 amalgamated many small forces into larger ones.","Police National Computer came into use in 1974.","Neighbourhood Watch began in Britain at Mollington in Cheshire in 1982, modelled on American schemes.","DNA profiling was developed by Alec Jeffreys at Leicester in 1984; Colin Pitchfork was convicted on DNA evidence in 1988; the national DNA database was set up in 1995.","Crime and Disorder Act 1998 introduced Anti-Social Behaviour Orders; the Police Reform Act 2002 created police community support officers.","The National Crime Agency was created in 2013 to tackle serious and organised crime across borders.","CCTV, automatic number-plate recognition and body-worn cameras have made surveillance a routine part of policing."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: punishment, prisons and the end of the death penalty, c1900-present","items":["Prevention of Crime Act 1908 established the borstal system, following the first borstal at Borstal in Kent in 1902.","Children Act 1908: juvenile courts created, imprisonment of children under fourteen ended, execution of anyone under sixteen abolished.","Probation of Offenders Act 1907 created probation officers, the foundation of supervision in the community.","New Hall Camp near Wakefield opened in 1933 as the first open prison in Britain.","Criminal Justice Act 1948 abolished penal servitude, hard labour and flogging.","Homicide Act 1957 limited hanging to five categories of capital murder - by shooting or explosion, in the course of theft, while resisting arrest, of a police or prison officer, and a second murder - and introduced diminished responsibility.","The last executions in Britain were carried out in August 1964.","Murder (Abolition of Death Penalty) Act 1965, promoted by Sydney Silverman MP, suspended hanging for murder for five years; abolition was made permanent in 1969 and the last remaining capital offences were abolished in 1998.","Criminal Justice Act 1967 introduced parole and suspended sentences; community service orders followed in 1972; electronic tagging was introduced in the 1990s.","The age of criminal responsibility in England and Wales has been ten since 1963.","Borstals were abolished in 1982; Young Offender Institutions were established in 1988.","Fines remain the commonest sentence, and far more offenders are supervised in the community than are imprisoned."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: case study - the treatment of conscientious objectors in the two World Wars","items":["Military Service Act, January 1916: conscription for single men aged eighteen to forty-one, extended to married men in May 1916; it contained a conscience clause.","Local Military Service Tribunals, made up of local worthies with a military representative present, decided each claim, often treating objection as cowardice.","About sixteen thousand men registered as conscientious objectors in the First World War.","'Alternativists' accepted the Non-Combatant Corps, the Royal Army Medical Corps or the Friends' Ambulance Unit; 'absolutists' refused all service.","Roughly six thousand objectors were court-martialled and imprisoned, many with hard labour; the absolutists, about fifteen hundred men, spent most of the war in prison.","In 1916 a group of absolutists was taken to France, court-martialled and sentenced to death; the sentences were immediately commuted to ten years' penal servitude.","Around seventy objectors died as a result of their imprisonment or treatment, and many were disqualified from voting for five years after the war.","Objectors were publicly shamed, including with the handing out of white feathers, and many lost their jobs.","National Service (Armed Forces) Act, September 1939: conscription returned, again with provision for conscientious objection.","About sixty thousand men registered as conscientious objectors in the Second World War.","Tribunals were now chaired by a judge and contained no military representative; they could grant unconditional exemption, exemption conditional on work of national importance, or non-combatant service.","Conditional exemption usually meant farming, mining, ambulance driving, hospital work or civil defence such as fire-watching in bombed cities.","Hostility continued - objectors were abused as 'conchies', and some employers and local councils dismissed them - but imprisonment was far less common than in the first war."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: case study - the Derek Bentley case and the abolition of the death penalty","items":["2 November 1952: Derek Bentley, aged nineteen, and Christopher Craig, aged sixteen, were caught breaking into a warehouse in Croydon.","Bentley had already been arrested and was being held by a detective when Craig shot and killed PC Sidney Miles.","The prosecution relied on the disputed words 'Let him have it, Chris', which could mean 'shoot him' or 'give him the gun'.","Both were convicted of murder under the common purpose rule, by which all parties to a joint criminal enterprise are guilty of what one of them does.","Craig was too young to be hanged; he was detained and released in 1963.","The jury added a recommendation to mercy; the Home Secretary, Sir David Maxwell Fyfe, refused a reprieve.","Bentley was illiterate, epileptic and had been assessed as having the mental age of a child.","Bentley was hanged at Wandsworth prison on 28 January 1953, amid petitions, protests outside the prison and questions in the House of Commons.","His sister Iris Bentley campaigned for his name to be cleared for the rest of her life; the conviction was quashed by the Court of Appeal in 1998.","Two other cases pushed opinion the same way: Timothy Evans, hanged in 1950 and pardoned in 1966 after John Christie's crimes were discovered, and Ruth Ellis, the last woman hanged in Britain, in July 1955.","Significance: the case abolished nothing by itself, but it made the argument moral rather than legal, because the conviction and the refusal of mercy were both lawful.","It fed directly into the Homicide Act of 1957, whose failure to draw defensible distinctions helped carry Sydney Silverman's abolition bill in 1965."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the earlier periods you need for the Question 3 comparisons","items":["c1000-c1500: tithings made every man over twelve answerable for his neighbours; the hue and cry obliged everyone who heard it to join the chase; trial by ordeal appealed to God until the Church withdrew its priests in 1215.","c1000-c1500: the Forest Laws made poaching and taking wood from royal forest a crime; wergild fines were paid to victims' families in the Anglo-Saxon period.","c1500-c1700: vagabondage was criminalised as the population grew; witchcraft accusations peaked during the upheaval of the Civil War; the Bloody Code began to expand.","c1500-c1700: smuggling grew because import duties were high, and coastal communities often protected smugglers such as the Hawkhurst gang.","c1700-c1900: the Bloody Code listed over two hundred capital offences by 1800 before being cut back in the 1820s and 1830s; transportation to Australia ran from 1787 to 1868.","c1700-c1900: Henry and John Fielding's Bow Street Runners from 1749; Robert Peel's Metropolitan Police Act of 1829; the CID formed in 1878.","c1700-c1900: prison reform associated with John Howard and Elizabeth Fry; the Gaols Act of 1823; the separate system at Pentonville, opened in 1842; public executions ended in 1868.","c1700-c1900: children were imprisoned with adults and could be transported or hanged; reformatories and industrial schools appeared only from the 1850s."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 1 Question 5: 'Changes in punishment in the years c1900-present were mainly the result of changing attitudes in society.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","items":["The question as printed: ''Changes in punishment in the years c1900-present were mainly the result of changing attitudes in society.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the Derek Bentley case • open prisons. You must also use information of your own.' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","Decode it. The conceptual focus is causation - the process of change - not description of punishments. 'Mainly' means you are being asked to rank factors, so the essay must name at least one rival factor and say which did more. The criterion to announce in the opening is the one you will apply at the end: which factor, if removed, would have left the change unmade?","Opening (three or four sentences): say what the statement claims, name the rival factors you will weigh against it - the influence of individual campaigners and cases, the practical demands on the prison system, and government policy - and state the criterion. Signal the position you will reach so the line of reasoning is visible from the first paragraph.","Paragraph 1, for the statement - the end of hanging. Bentley hanged on 28 January 1953 though Craig fired the shot and the jury recommended mercy; Evans hanged in 1950 and pardoned in 1966; Ruth Ellis hanged in July 1955. Nothing legally failed in these cases, so the objection could only be moral. Interim judgement: only a change in what the public would tolerate can explain why lawful executions became intolerable.","Paragraph 2, for the statement - the purpose of prison. Attitudes moved from retribution towards reform: the Criminal Justice Act of 1948 abolished penal servitude, hard labour and flogging; the first open prison at New Hall Camp near Wakefield had opened in 1933; probation, parole and community service extended the idea that most offenders should be supervised rather than locked up. Interim judgement: these changes were not forced by events, so they reflect a changed belief about what punishment is for.","Paragraph 3, against the statement - individuals and institutions. Sydney Silverman's private member's bill carried the Commons in 1965; the Homicide Act of 1957 had already narrowed capital murder to five categories and, by drawing distinctions nobody could defend, converted general unease into a precise argument. Interim judgement: attitudes supplied the pressure, but named campaigners and a specific legislative failure supplied the opportunity and the timing.","Paragraph 4, against the statement and the point that goes beyond the stimulus - practical pressure and government policy. Rising prison numbers and cost made non-custodial sentences attractive as well as humane; parole and suspended sentences arrived in 1967 and community service orders in 1972, with electronic tagging in the 1990s; the separation of young offenders, from borstals in 1902 and 1908 to Young Offender Institutions in 1988, was also a matter of managing a system. Interim judgement: some changes were administrative solutions that happened to look progressive.","The evaluation thread to run through every paragraph: keep asking whether the change would have happened anyway. Abolition would not have happened without a change of attitude, because the law worked perfectly well; community sentences might have come regardless, because the prisons were full.","What the conclusion must say: not 'there were several reasons'. Apply the criterion. Agree that attitudes were the main reason for the changes that concern what the state may do to a citizen - the end of hanging above all - because nothing else altered while the law stayed the same. Qualify it by saying that the changes concerning how offenders are managed owed as much to cost, capacity and government policy, and that attitudes needed campaigners and a parliamentary opportunity before they became law.","Timing: about 27 to 30 minutes. Five minutes to fix the criterion and order the four paragraphs, twenty to write, and the last two or three to check spelling and punctuation and to make sure the specialist terms are present and used correctly, since 4 additional marks depend on 'a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate'.","Specialist terms worth using accurately here: capital punishment, abolition, reprieve, common purpose, diminished responsibility, retribution, deterrence, rehabilitation, non-custodial sentence, probation, parole, suspended sentence, community service, borstal, open prison."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:06:17Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":1,"notes":"All four recorded fixes hold and every new fact checks out on the web: the first borstal at Borstal near Rochester in 1902 with the Prevention of Crime Act 1908 borstal sentence for sixteen- to twenty-one-year-olds, the Children Act 1908 (juvenile courts, no imprisonment under fourteen, no death sentence under sixteen), the age of criminal responsibility raised from eight to ten by the Children and Young Persons Act 1963, borstals abolished in 1982 and Young Offender Institutions from 1988¹; grand larceny as theft above twelve pence from the Statute of Westminster 1275 and the Theft Act 1968 maximum cut to seven years by the Criminal Justice Act 1991²; the Wolfenden report of 1957, the Sexual Offences Act 1967 (twenty-one or over, in private, England and Wales, armed forces and merchant navy excluded), the Suicide Act 1961 with assisting suicide still carrying fourteen years, and Roy Jenkins at the Home Office 1965-67³; and example 0's step 3 now uses only 1933, 1948 and 1967 for the modern half with the setup supplying every date the steps use⁴. I re-solved all fourteen questions and agree with each key; no bank stem now matches the Paper 1 specimen Option 10 paper or June 2023 1HI0/10 beyond the shared command-word form, no worked example shares a bank question's focus, dupscan.js prints 'no duplication' and kit-tools check reports OK; the only thing left to notice is that the model paragraphs and the essay plan still borrow Question 10's two stimulus points (the Derek Bentley case and open prisons) while setting a different focus (causation, not significance), which the rule allows and which usefully lets a student see the same evidence argued two ways."}}},"10-env":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"10-env","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Section A of Paper 1 carries only 16 marks, but it is the part of the paper where a small patch of London, known in detail, beats a thousand years of crime known in outline. Everything here happens inside about one square mile of the East End between roughly 1870 and 1900, and the examiner sets the same three tasks every year: describe one feature of each of two separate things in that square mile, in the two parts of Question 1, judge how useful two printed sources are for a named enquiry, and frame a follow-up enquiry from one of them. The knowledge and the source skill are not separate. You cannot say whether a police inspector's report is useful for an enquiry into policing unless you already know that a beat took about half an hour to walk, that the police counted 233 common lodging houses in Whitechapel in 1888, and that the Whitechapel Vigilance Committee had begun sending out its own patrols that September. This room therefore teaches the place and the skill together: the housing, the workhouse, the lodging houses, the migrants, H Division, the murders of 1888 and the men at the top, and then the four moves — content, provenance, context, judgement — that turn that knowledge into marks on Sources A and B.","idea":[{"h":"The local context: housing, overcrowding and the limits of improvement","t":"Whitechapel was built up before anyone regulated building. Behind the wide shopping streets lay courts and alleys with one entrance, no through route and, in many cases, no lamp — the rookeries, of which the streets around Flower and Dean Street were the worst known. Improvement was attempted: the Artisans' and Labourers' Dwellings Improvement Act of 1875 let the Metropolitan Board of Works buy up and demolish slum property, and the land it cleared beside Commercial Street was sold on to the Peabody trustees, whose estate of eleven blocks and 286 flats opened in 1881. But rents of about three to six shillings a week, and rules about weekly payment and inspection, priced out the people who had been cleared away: a parliamentary committee that visited in July 1881 found only eleven of the displaced had come back. The poor moved sideways into the courts that were left, and those courts grew fuller. A top answer does not describe the Peabody Estate; it uses it to argue that slum clearance without cheap replacement housing made overcrowding worse, and that overcrowding is the reason a murderer could walk out of a court unseen.","code":"10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The local context of Whitechapel"},{"h":"Provision for the poor: the workhouse, the casual ward and the drift to the street","t":"The Poor Law Amendment Act of 1834 made the workhouse the main relief for the able-bodied poor, and made it deliberately unattractive. The Whitechapel Union's workhouse at South Grove, on the south side of the Mile End Road, took up that role in 1876; its buildings were designed for 485 inmates, and only after further pavilions and dormitory blocks were completed in 1898 did the establishment hold over 800. Families were separated, the timetable was fixed, and inmates paid for their keep by picking oakum, breaking stone or working in the laundry. Attached to it was the casual ward, the 'spike', where a vagrant got a bath, a bed and a meal for a task of work and could be held until the task was finished. Most people avoided all of it. The alternative was a bed in a common lodging house for about fourpence, or sleeping out in a court or in the churchyard of Christ Church, Spitalfields, known locally as Itchy Park. A top answer uses the workhouse to explain the street: because relief deterred rather than relieved, Whitechapel's poorest stayed visible, mobile and unrecorded, which is exactly the population the police could not know.","code":"10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The local context of Whitechapel"},{"h":"The inhabitants: casual work, lodging houses and a population that would not stand still","t":"Whitechapel work was casual by nature — dock labour taken on by the day at the call-on, tailoring and boot-making at piece rates in small 'sweated' workshops that a House of Lords select committee began investigating in 1888, market portering, street selling. Casual work produced casual housing. In 1888 the police returned to the Home Office that Whitechapel held 233 common lodging houses sleeping 8,530 people, thickest in Flower and Dean Street, Thrawl Street and Dorset Street; a bed cost about fourpence and was let for one night to whoever paid that evening. Pubs did the work of a front room. The result was a population without ties: a constable could pass a man one week and not know him the next, landlords could not name their lodgers, and neighbours had no reason to inform. A top answer turns that into the central argument of the whole option: the difficulty of policing Whitechapel was not mainly a difficulty about criminals, it was a difficulty about identity — in a district where nobody could say who anyone was, ordinary detection had nothing to work on.","code":"10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The inhabitants of Whitechapel"},{"h":"Migration: Irish settlement, Jewish settlement and the suspicion that followed both","t":"Two migrations shaped the district. The Irish population, long settled and swollen by emigration after the famine of the 1840s, worked mainly as dock labourers and navvies; the Fenian bombing campaign in England between 1881 and 1885, including an explosion at Scotland Yard itself in 1884, led to the creation of the Special Irish Branch in 1883 and made the police treat Irish Londoners as a security question. Then, after the assassination of Tsar Alexander II in 1881, pogroms and the Russian May Laws of 1882 drove a very large Jewish emigration westwards, and tens of thousands settled in Whitechapel and Spitalfields during the 1880s, bringing synagogues, a Yiddish press and socialist clubs such as the International Working Men's Educational Club in Berner Street. Newcomers were blamed for undercutting wages in the sweated trades and for taking housing, and during the murders of 1888 the arrest and release of a Jewish bootmaker in September was followed by attacks on Jewish people in the street. A top answer uses migration twice: as a cause of tension the police had to contain, and as a reason why witnesses would not talk to them.","code":"10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The inhabitants of Whitechapel"},{"h":"The organisation of policing: H Division, the beat and what the beat could not reach","t":"The Metropolitan Police, created by Peel's Act of 1829, divided London into lettered divisions; Whitechapel was H Division, with its main station in Leman Street, and by 1888 it had grown to something over 500 officers, overwhelmingly uniformed constables. The system was preventive, not investigative: a constable walked a fixed beat at a steady pace so that it took about half an hour to complete, which meant he passed any one spot roughly twice an hour and it was unwatched the rest of the time. On a wide street that deters; in a court with a single archway, no lighting and no through route, it does not, because the lantern is seen long before the man. The ordinary work was drunkenness, street fighting, soliciting — in 1888 the police estimated 62 brothels and about 1,200 women working as prostitutes in Whitechapel — and, by the end of the century, protection rackets run on stallholders and small immigrant shopkeepers by gangs such as the Bessarabians and the Odessians. A top answer argues from design: policing failed here because a beat system built for open streets was applied to a maze.","code":"10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The organisation of policing in Whitechapel"},{"h":"Investigative policing: what the detectives of 1888 could and could not do","t":"The murder investigation of 1888 was large and, by the standards of the time, methodical: house-to-house enquiries across the district, more than 2,000 people interviewed, over 300 investigated and about 80 detained; witnesses' descriptions turned into sketches; photographs taken of the victims, including Mary Jane Kelly where she lay; bloodhounds tried. What was missing was any means of turning physical evidence into an identity. There was no way to group blood, no fingerprint system in Britain until the Metropolitan Police opened its Fingerprint Bureau in 1901, and Bertillon's system of body measurements, worked out in Paris in the early 1880s, was not in use in London. Coordination was a second problem: Catherine Eddowes was killed in Mitre Square, inside the City of London, so a separate force investigated one murder of the series while the Metropolitan Police and Scotland Yard handled the rest. A top answer states the limit exactly: every technique available could only confirm a suspect already identified, and nobody had identified one.","code":"10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: Investigative policing in Whitechapel"},{"h":"The press and the Vigilance Committee: pressure from outside the force","t":"The name 'Jack the Ripper' came from a letter received by the Central News Agency on 27 September 1888 and published at the start of October — almost certainly a hoax, and the single most damaging document in the case, because it turned five local killings into a national serial. The Star, a halfpenny evening paper launched in January 1888, and the Illustrated Police News built circulation on the murders, printing engravings of scenes no artist had witnessed and pressing the idea that the killer must be a foreigner or a Jew. From below came the Whitechapel Vigilance Committee, formed at the Crown public house on the Mile End Road on 10 September 1888 by local tradesmen who feared the murders were driving away custom; its chairman was the builder George Lusk. It hired private detectives, offered its own reward, printed handbills and sent unpaid patrols out at night — patrols that stopped and detained passers-by, creating work for the police rather than relieving it. A top answer treats both as evidence of collapsed confidence in the police, and weighs whether they hindered the investigation more than they helped it.","code":"10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: Investigative policing in Whitechapel"},{"h":"The national context: the Home Secretary, Sir Charles Warren and public attitudes","t":"The Metropolitan Police was the only force in the country answerable not to a local watch committee but to the Home Secretary, who appointed its Commissioner; in 1888 that was Henry Matthews, in Lord Salisbury's government. The Commissioner from 1886 was Sir Charles Warren, a serving colonel of the Royal Engineers, promoted major-general only in 1893, who ran the force like a regiment. On 13 November 1887 he banned a demonstration in Trafalgar Square and held it with thousands of police supported by troops; the numbers are disputed, but many were injured, scores arrested and at least two men later died of their injuries. Bloody Sunday cost the force working-class trust a year before the murders began. Warren then had the chalked message in Goulston Street washed off before dawn on 30 September 1888 to prevent anti-Jewish rioting, destroying possible evidence; Matthews refused the repeated requests for a government reward, on the Home Office view held since 1884 that rewards produce false information. Warren published a defence of the police without permission, was rebuked, and resigned on 8 November 1888 — the day before Mary Jane Kelly was found. Investigative policing also improved nationally in these years: the Detective Branch, discredited by corruption, was reorganised as the Criminal Investigation Department in April 1878 under Howard Vincent. A top answer uses all of this to argue that the failure in Whitechapel was as much a failure of the relationship between Whitehall and Scotland Yard as of the men on the beat.","code":"10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The national and regional context"},{"h":"Local sources: what Whitechapel itself left behind","t":"The local record for this period is unusually rich, and the examiner expects you to be able to name the kinds of record, not individual documents. Census returns were taken in 1871, 1881 and 1891 and give names, ages, birthplaces and household size street by street. Common lodging houses had to be registered and could be inspected by the police under the Common Lodging Houses Act of 1851, which is why the police could count 233 of them in 1888. Workhouse admission and discharge registers record who came in and how often. The Whitechapel District Board of Works and, after the Local Government Act of 1888, the new London County Council left records of paving, drains, lighting and clearance; the district's medical officer of health reported annually on deaths and sanitary conditions. Coroners' inquest papers record witness statements taken on oath within days of a death. H Division's own duty books, charge sheets and reports survive, as do London newspapers. Above all there is Charles Booth's survey, begun in 1886, whose first volume on East London appeared in 1889 with the poverty map. A top answer names the kind of record and says what it contains, because that is what makes it match an enquiry.","code":"10-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Knowledge of local sources"},{"h":"National sources: the record made outside Whitechapel about Whitechapel","t":"National sources tell you less about the district and more about how the country saw it, which for some enquiries is exactly what you want. National newspapers — The Times, the Pall Mall Gazette, the Star, the Illustrated Police News — reported the murders daily and carried letters and leading articles arguing about the East End. Punch, running since 1841, printed cartoons that turned the district into a symbol. Home Office correspondence and Metropolitan Police case files record what the police told Whitehall and what Whitehall told them, including Matthews's refusal of a reward. The Old Bailey's published Proceedings give the evidence and outcome of trials for serious crime committed in the area. Parliamentary papers add another layer: the select committee on artisans' dwellings that inspected the new Peabody blocks in July 1881, and the House of Lords committee on the sweating system that began sitting in 1888. A top answer picks a national source when the enquiry is about attitudes, policy or publicity, and a local one when it is about conditions or individuals, and says which it is doing.","code":"10-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Knowledge of national sources"},{"h":"Strengths and weaknesses: usefulness is always usefulness for one enquiry","t":"No source is useful in general. The census is superb for who slept in a house on one night in April and silent about what they did for the rest of the year. A coroner's inquest gives sworn testimony taken within days, but only about a death and only from the witnesses the police found. A police report is written by a man who was there and who is also explaining his own results to his superior. A newspaper engraving drawn for readers in Kensington is weak evidence of what a Whitechapel court looked like and strong evidence of what those readers were being invited to feel. The move that earns the top level is to take a weakness and show what it makes the source good for: a source's purpose does not cancel it, it redirects it. A top answer therefore never writes 'this source is biased and so it is not useful'; it names the nature, origin or purpose, names the particular claim in the source that purpose would bend, and says what the source can still be trusted to show.","code":"10-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Recognition of the strengths and weaknesses of different types of source"},{"h":"Framing a question: taking the unfinished sentence in a source","t":"A follow-up question is not a question about the topic in general; it is a question raised by one detail in the source in front of you. The details worth taking are the unfinished ones: a claim made without proof, a figure given without a comparison, a group mentioned but not named, an event referred to in passing as though the reader already knows it. If an inspector writes that half his division is out on special patrols, the unfinished part is 'half of what, and how many is that?'. If he mentions a tradesmen's committee sending out patrols, the unfinished part is who those men were and what they had been told to do. A good follow-up question must be answerable by evidence, which rules out questions about motives nobody recorded, and must be specific enough that you can name a record that would settle it. A top answer makes all four parts lock together: the detail chosen decides the question, the question decides the type of source, and the last box explains what that source would actually show.","code":"10-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Framing of questions relevant to the pursuit of a specific enquiry"},{"h":"Selecting the source: matching a record to a question","t":"Once the question is framed, choosing the record is a matter of asking who would have had to write this down, and why. Numbers of constables and the length of beats were recorded because the division had to be administered, so police duty books and divisional returns answer them. How many people slept in a lodging house was recorded because the Act of 1851 required registration and allowed inspection, so lodging-house registers and the police returns to the Home Office answer it. Who lived in a street on one night is in the census. What a witness said about a death is in the coroner's inquest papers. What the public was being told is in the newspapers; what the government decided is in Home Office correspondence. Name the type of record, not a single document, and then say what the record contains that bears on the question — 'it would tell me the number of constables on each beat, so I could test whether the inspector's claim was true'. A top answer never stops at naming the source; the final box is where the fourth mark is.","code":"10-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Selection of appropriate sources for specific investigations"}],"examples":[{"title":"Describing one feature in each part: Question 1(a) and 1(b)","setup":"The question is printed in two parts: '1(a) Describe one feature of the sweated trades of Whitechapel. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of Irish settlement in Whitechapel. [2 marks]' You know the following. Tailoring, boot-making and cabinet-making were carried on in Whitechapel in small workshops and in the back rooms of houses, the garment or the boot broken into separate operations paid for by the piece, with a contractor taking work from a wholesale house and putting it out again to whoever would take it cheapest. Hours were long and the work ran in busy and slack seasons, so earnings rose and fell with the trade. A select committee of the House of Lords began taking evidence on the sweating system in 1888 and made its final report in April 1890, having heard 291 witnesses on trades that included tailoring, boot-making and cabinet-making; it described the system by three things together — wages inadequate to the needs of the worker, hours of labour that were excessive, and workrooms that were insanitary — and concluded that the middleman was the consequence of the system and not its cause. The Irish population of the East End was long settled and had been enlarged by the emigration that followed the famine of the 1840s, and its men worked mainly as dock labourers taken on by the day and as building labourers. The Fenian bombing campaign in Britain between 1881 and 1885 included an explosion at Scotland Yard on 30 May 1884 which wrecked the offices of the Special Irish Branch, formed the year before, in 1883, to meet that campaign, so that the police came to handle Irish Londoners as a security question as well as a policing one. Each part carries 2 marks: one for a valid feature and a second for the supporting information attached to it.","steps":["Split the two parts before writing a word: 1(a) is about how work was organised and paid for, 1(b) about a settled population and how the police came to regard it, so fix one feature for each part and stop hunting for a second.","Write 1(a) as a bare statement of fact and then attach its support: the sweated trades broke a garment or a boot into separate small operations done at piece rates in workshops and back rooms; the House of Lords committee that reported in April 1890, after hearing 291 witnesses, defined that system by inadequate wages, excessive hours and insanitary workrooms together.","Write 1(b) the same way on the other aspect: Irish settlement in the East End was old and lived by casual labour on the docks and on building work; the bombing campaign of 1881 to 1885, and the explosion at Scotland Yard on 30 May 1884, left the Special Irish Branch of 1883 watching that population as a security problem.","Stop. Each part is worth only two marks, so a second feature inside a part, a comparison between the trade and the migration, and any explanation of why either mattered later are all unread."],"cues":["What are the two separate subjects here, and why can nothing cross between the parts?","What single piece of evidence turns the feature in 1(a) into two marks?","What has to be bolted on to the feature in 1(b) before it can score twice?","What would a comparison between the two parts be worth here?"]},{"title":"Judging Source A: an inspector's report and an enquiry into how the police and the public stood towards one another","setup":"Source A in this room is from a report written by an inspector of H Division of the Metropolitan Police at Leman Street for his superintendent, in October 1888. In it he says that there are upwards of forty lodging houses on his two beats and that the deputies own the beds are let twice over on a good night; that the people in them are not the same people from one week to the next, so a constable may pass a man on the Thursday and not know him on the Monday; that the courts behind Flower and Dean Street have one archway and no lamp, so a bull's-eye lantern is seen from the far end long before the man carrying it arrives; that a beat there is walked round in half an hour, so that any one court is watched for two minutes in every thirty; that since the bootmaker was taken up in September his men are jeered at in Yiddish and in English and followed by crowds asking what the police have done for them; that the tradesmen's committee now send out patrols of their own who stop respectable men and bring them in, and that these men must then be let go; that half the division is out on the special night patrols so that thefts from the Wentworth Street stalls are not attended to; and he ends by asking for four more constables and for lamps in the courts. The question as the paper prints it is: 'How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the relations between the police and the public in Whitechapel in 1888? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context.' (8 marks) This example builds the Source A half of that answer; the next example builds the Source B half, and both halves belong in the one answer.","steps":["Start from the enquiry, not the source: this enquiry is about how the two sides stood towards one another, so go through the report for the sentences about people rather than the sentences about ground.","List what it gives you and attach short quotations: constables 'jeered at in Yiddish and in English alike', crowds 'asking what the police have done for them', a tradesmen's committee whose own patrols 'stop respectable men and bring them in to us, and we must let them go again', and lodging-house deputies who 'own that on a good night the beds are let twice over'.","Take the nature and origin next and make them do work: this is an internal report from an inspector to his own superintendent, not a statement to the press or to a coroner, so admitting that his men are mocked in the street and that outsiders are doing their work for them is an admission no public defence of the force would have printed, which makes those sentences unusually trustworthy.","Now take the purpose and aim it at a particular sentence rather than at the whole source: the report ends by asking for four more constables and for lamps, so the hostility is being used as an argument for reinforcement, and the claim that would grow most in that telling is the one about crowds following his men, which is easy to say and impossible to count.","Bring in context to test, not to decorate: a committee of local tradesmen really had been formed at a public house on the Mile End Road that September under the builder George Lusk, and the force really was appealing to the public over the heads of its own beats, putting about 80,000 handbills into householders' hands during the house-to-house enquiries of October 1888, so the strained dealings he describes are corroborated from both sides.","Finish by turning the limitation into a use: because the report is what one inspector was willing to tell his own superior about the reception his men were getting, it is evidence both of the hostility itself and of how much of that hostility the force would admit inside its own paperwork."],"cues":["What should you look for first — everything the source says, or only what bears on this enquiry?","Which sentences in the report are about people rather than about streets?","Who was this written for, and what does that audience make more believable?","The inspector wants something. Which single sentence would you expect that want to inflate?","Which facts from outside the source would show that these dealings were as bad as he says?","The source is one man's account of his own men. What does that make it good evidence of?"]},{"title":"Judging Source B: a newspaper engraving, and why 'drawn to sell papers' is not the end of the answer","setup":"Source B in this room is a description of an engraving published in a London illustrated weekly newspaper in the last week of November 1888; the paper sold chiefly outside the East End and its sales had risen sharply during the autumn. The picture is a night scene in a Whitechapel court seen through a low archway. A constable stands with his lantern raised and its beam falls on a woman sitting on a step with a child asleep across her knees; behind her an open doorway spills light on to men and women sitting on the stairs, and a printed police notice asking for information is pasted beside the door and has been torn across. On the left three gentlemen in tall hats stand inside the archway, one writing in a notebook, with a second constable at their elbow. Washing is strung from window to window, a broken water butt stands under a spout, and two barefoot children fight over something on the cobbles; at the back of the court a man in a long coat turns away out of the light. The question is the same printed stem: 'How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the relations between the police and the public in Whitechapel in 1888? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context.' (8 marks) The previous example built the Source A half; this one builds the Source B half of the same answer.","steps":["Separate the picture into what an artist could have observed and what he must have invented, because the two halves have different values for an enquiry about people rather than about places.","Take the observable half first and pick only the details in which police and public meet: the printed notice asking for information, pasted up and torn across; the constable whose raised lantern is being shone on a woman with a child asleep on her knees; the second constable standing at the elbow of three gentlemen in tall hats.","Use the torn notice, which is the detail the artist had least reason to invent: it says that the appeals the police pasted up were being handled with contempt, which is exactly the thing a police report would never concede.","Use the three gentlemen in tall hats the same way: they 'stand just inside the archway, one of them writing in a notebook, with a second constable at their elbow' while the people who live in the court sit on the stairs and are looked at, which shows whose convenience the force was serving in the weeks it said it had no men to spare.","Take the invented half and say what it is evidence of instead: nobody had seen the murderer, so the figure turning away at the back of the court is the artist supplying his readers with the menace they had paid for, and that makes the picture weak evidence of who was in the court and strong evidence of how readers outside the East End were being taught to picture the district and its policing.","Judge for this enquiry rather than in general: the engraving is less reliable than a report on any single fact, and more useful than one on how the force appeared to the people it policed and to the people who came to look, so say which of those this enquiry actually needs."],"cues":["Which parts of this scene could the artist have stood and looked at?","Which of the observable details show police and public in contact with one another?","Which detail in the picture would a police report be least likely to record?","Why would a constable be standing beside three men in tall hats?","Nobody had seen the killer. So what is the shadowy figure actually evidence of?","The two sources are not useful in the same way. What decides which is worth more here?"]},{"title":"The follow-up: four boxes that have to lock together","setup":"You are asked: 'How could you follow up Source A to find out more about the equipment carried by the Metropolitan Police constable on the beat in the 1880s? In your answer, you must give the question you would ask and the type of source you could use.' Source A is the October 1888 report in which an inspector of H Division tells his superintendent that the courts behind Flower and Dean Street have but one archway to them and no lamp inside; that a bull's-eye is seen from the far end of a court long before the man carrying it arrives, so that by the time the man is under the arch there is nobody left in the court but women and children; that a beat there is walked round in half an hour, which is to say that any one court is watched for two minutes in every thirty; and that the people in the lodging houses are not the same from one week to the next, so a constable may pass a man on the Thursday and not know him on the Monday. You must fill four boxes: the detail in Source A you would follow up, the question you would ask, the type of source you could use, and how it might help answer your question. One mark is available for each box, and the question must follow from the detail.","steps":["Reject the details that are already complete and keep the one that opens a hole: the arithmetic about a court being watched for two minutes in every thirty is finished, but the sentence about the bull's-eye says that the lamp a constable carried announced him long before he arrived, and says nothing about what else a man on night duty was given to work with.","Write the detail box as a short quotation rather than a paraphrase, so the examiner can see exactly which words you are following up: that a bull's-eye is seen from the far end of a court long before the man carrying it arrives.","Turn the hole into the question, keeping it answerable by a record: what was a Metropolitan Police constable of the 1880s issued with for night duty, and was anything supplied that did not give his approach away?","Name a type of record, not one document, and choose it by asking who had to write this down for administrative reasons: the force's own orders to its divisions and the stores books recording the clothing and appointments served out, with the instruction book put into a recruit's hands at training.","Use the last box to say what the record would show and how that settles the question, because a source named without an explanation is worth nothing on its own: the stores books would show which lamps and other appointments went to H Division and when they were changed, so I could tell whether the inspector was describing equipment every constable carried or a want of his own division."],"cues":["Which of these details still has a hole in it?","Why copy the words out rather than summarise them?","What turns a hole in a source into a question a record could answer?","Who would have been obliged to write these issues down, and why?","What is still missing once you have named the record?"]},{"title":"Matching the record to the question: three enquiries, three different kinds of source","setup":"You have framed three follow-up questions from Source A and Source B, and must choose a type of source for each. The questions are: (i) how many people were actually sleeping in the common lodging houses of Flower and Dean Street, and how often the same people came back; (ii) whether the police notices pasted up in the courts, like the torn one in Source B, produced any useful information; (iii) how the inhabitants of Whitechapel themselves reacted to the arrest and release of the bootmaker in September 1888. The types of record available to you include census returns for 1881 and 1891, lodging-house registers kept under the Common Lodging Houses Act of 1851 and the police returns made to the Home Office, Metropolitan Police case files and Home Office correspondence, coroners' inquest papers, workhouse admission and discharge registers, the district medical officer of health's annual reports, Old Bailey published Proceedings, London newspapers including the Yiddish press, and Charles Booth's survey notebooks and poverty map.","steps":["For each question, ask who would have been obliged to write the answer down and for what administrative purpose, because that is what decides which record exists.","Question (i) is about numbers and repetition, so it needs the records made because the law required registration and inspection: the lodging-house registers and the police returns, with the census as a check on one particular night.","Question (ii) is about the results of a police action, so it needs the force's own paperwork: Metropolitan Police case files and the correspondence between Scotland Yard and the Home Office, where the value of the appeals would have been argued over.","Question (iii) is about opinion rather than administration, and opinion is only written down where somebody was publishing it, so it needs the London press of September and October, including the local and Yiddish papers that printed letters from Jewish residents.","Check each pairing by asking what the record would fail to show, and say so: the registers give names but not why anyone moved on; the police files give the force's view of its own appeals; the newspapers give the opinions of people willing to write to a paper, not of the district as a whole."],"cues":["Before choosing any record, what single question should you ask about it?","Which law forced somebody to keep a count of lodgers?","If you want to know whether a police tactic worked, whose filing cabinet do you open?","Where does an opinion get written down in 1888?","What should you add once you have matched record to question?"]}],"check":[{"q":"In what year did the Peabody Estate open on Commercial Street, and what did a parliamentary committee find about its occupants a few months later?","a":"1881. The committee that visited in July 1881 found the blocks almost full, with 1,260 people in 286 families, but only eleven of the people cleared off the site had moved into them."},{"q":"Why did a beat constable's half-hour beat work badly in the courts behind Flower and Dean Street?","a":"Because a court with one archway, no lamp and no through route was watched for only about two minutes in every thirty, and the constable's lantern was visible from the far end long before he arrived."},{"q":"What three things does 'provenance' mean in the mark scheme, and what must you do with them?","a":"Nature, origin and purpose. You must use them on a named claim in the source, showing how they make that particular inference more or less useful, not label the source biased."},{"q":"Name two forensic techniques that were not available to the police investigating the 1888 murders, and say when each arrived.","a":"Fingerprinting: the Metropolitan Police Fingerprint Bureau opened in 1901. Bertillon's system of body measurements: worked out in Paris in the early 1880s but not in use in London during the murders. Blood grouping did not exist."},{"q":"Which four boxes does a follow-up answer have to fill, and what connects them?","a":"The detail taken from the source, the question that detail raises, the type of source that could answer it, and how that source would help. Each box must follow from the one before it."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 1 Section A is worth 16 marks in all: Question 1, printed as 1(a) and 1(b), 'Describe one feature of…' on two separate aspects of content (2 marks each, 4 in all), Question 2(a) 'How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into…? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context.' (8 marks) and Question 2(b) 'How could you follow up Source A to find out more about…? In your answer, you must give the question you would ask and the type of source you could use.' (4 marks).","Questions 1 and 2(b) are point-marked, not levelled. On each part of Question 1, one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for the supporting information attached to it. On Question 2(b), one mark for a detail selected from Source A, one for a question linked to it, one for naming an appropriate type of source, and one for showing how that source would help.","Question 2(a) is levelled, with Level 3 at 6–8 marks: judgements on usefulness for the specified enquiry, applying valid criteria with developed reasoning that takes into account how the provenance affects the usefulness of the source content, and contextual knowledge used in the process of interpreting the sources. Provenance means nature, origin and purpose.","Two rules printed under the 2(a) grid decide most lost marks: no credit for contextual knowledge unless it is linked to evaluation of the sources, and no credit for generic comments on provenance which are not used to evaluate source content.","Section A prints no stimulus points and carries no SPaG marks; both belong elsewhere on the paper. Paper 1 lasts 1 hour 20 minutes for 52 marks, so work to roughly a minute a mark plus planning time: about 4 minutes on Question 1, two on each part, 10 to 13 on Question 2(a) and 4 to 5 on Question 2(b)."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the Peabody Estate built in Whitechapel. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the Whitechapel Union workhouse at South Grove. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked in two parts of 2 marks each: 1 mark for a valid feature and a second mark for the supporting information attached to it, in 1(a) and again in 1(b). In 1(a) credit its origin in slum clearance, its scale and design, its rents and rules, or the fact that the displaced poor did not move into it. In 1(b) credit the date it took up the role and the numbers it was built for, the deterrent regime of separation and work, or the casual ward attached to it.","sol":"1(a) Feature: it was purpose-built model housing put up on cleared slum ground. Supporting information: the Metropolitan Board of Works had bought and demolished the property beside Commercial Street under the Artisans' and Labourers' Dwellings Improvement Act of 1875, and the estate that opened there in 1881 consisted of eleven blocks containing 286 separate flats. 1(b) Feature: it relieved the poor by deterring them. Supporting information: the Whitechapel Union's workhouse at South Grove on the Mile End Road took up that role in 1876 in buildings designed for 485 inmates, where families were separated on entry and inmates paid for their keep by picking oakum, breaking stone or working in the laundry.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Two parts, two separate places: nothing you write about the flats can earn anything in 1(b), so plan one feature for each.","A feature scores twice only when a date, a number, a rule or a name is attached to it, so pick in each part the feature you can attach something to.","Write each part as one sentence of plain fact followed by a second carrying the evidence, and leave out any explanation of why either was built."],"codes":["10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The local context of Whitechapel"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the common lodging houses of Whitechapel. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of Jewish migration into Whitechapel in the 1880s. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked in two parts of 2 marks each: 1 mark for a valid feature and a second mark for the supporting information attached to it, in each part. In 1(a) credit their number and the population they held, the nightly letting of beds, the shared and mixed sleeping arrangements, or their registration and inspection under the 1851 Act. In 1(b) credit what drove the migration, its scale and its concentration in Whitechapel and Spitalfields, the institutions the migrants brought with them, or the sweated trades they worked in.","sol":"1(a) Feature: they housed an enormous share of the district's population. Supporting information: police figures returned to the Home Office in 1888 counted 233 common lodging houses in Whitechapel sleeping 8,530 people, thickest in Flower and Dean Street, Thrawl Street and Dorset Street. 1(b) Feature: it was a large movement of people driven out of the Russian Empire. Supporting information: after the assassination of Tsar Alexander II in 1881, pogroms and the Russian May Laws of 1882 sent a Jewish emigration westwards, and tens of thousands settled in Whitechapel and Spitalfields during the 1880s, bringing synagogues, a Yiddish press and clubs such as the International Working Men's Educational Club in Berner Street.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["One part is about where the poorest slept, the other about who was arriving in the district; keep them apart.","Each feature needs one piece of evidence bolted to it — a count, a price, a street, an Act or a date.","One feature and one support in each part is the whole answer; a second feature inside a part earns nothing."],"codes":["10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The inhabitants of Whitechapel"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the work of the beat constable in Whitechapel. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the detective methods available to the police in 1888. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked in two parts of 2 marks each: 1 mark for a valid feature and a second mark for the supporting information attached to it, in each part. In 1(a) credit the fixed beat and its timing, the preventive and uniformed nature of the work, the kinds of disorder dealt with, or the quality and turnover of recruits. In 1(b) credit house-to-house enquiries and the scale of the questioning, sketches and photographs, the bloodhounds tried, or the absence of fingerprinting and of any way of grouping blood.","sol":"1(a) Feature: he walked a fixed beat to a fixed timing. Supporting information: a constable of H Division, based at Leman Street, covered his beat at a steady pace that took about half an hour, so he passed any given court roughly twice an hour and it was unwatched between his visits. 1(b) Feature: detection rested on questioning and description rather than on physical evidence. Supporting information: the 1888 investigation made house-to-house enquiries across the district, interviewed more than 2,000 people and detained about 80, and turned witnesses' accounts into sketches, while there was no fingerprint system in Britain until the Metropolitan Police opened its Fingerprint Bureau in 1901 and no means of grouping blood.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["One part is about the uniformed man in the street, the other about what the detectives could actually do with what they collected.","Numbers, timings, place names and dates are what turn a feature into two marks in either part.","Describe the work as it was done in each part; do not argue about why the murders went unsolved."],"codes":["10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The organisation of policing in Whitechapel","10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: Investigative policing in Whitechapel"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the Whitechapel Vigilance Committee. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the press reporting of the Whitechapel murders of 1888. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked in two parts of 2 marks each: 1 mark for a valid feature and a second mark for the supporting information attached to it, in each part. In 1(a) credit its membership and origin, its night patrols, its reward and private detectives, or its pressure on the Home Secretary. In 1(b) credit the letter that produced the name 'Jack the Ripper', the papers that built circulation on the murders, the engraving of scenes no artist had witnessed, or the blaming of foreigners and Jews.","sol":"1(a) Feature: it was set up by local tradesmen who feared for their trade. Supporting information: it was formed at a meeting in the Crown public house on the Mile End Road on 10 September 1888, and its chairman was George Lusk, a Mile End builder and decorator, with shopkeepers and small businessmen as its members. 1(b) Feature: the papers turned local killings into a national sensation. Supporting information: the name 'Jack the Ripper' came from a letter received by the Central News Agency on 27 September 1888 and published at the start of October, and the Star and the Illustrated Police News built their circulation on the murders with engravings of scenes no artist had witnessed.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["One part is about the men of the district organising themselves, the other about the newspapers selling that district to the country.","Names and dates are the supporting information here, so fix the month and the chairman for 1(a) and the title of a paper for 1(b) before you write.","Keep to what each one was and did; whether either helped or hindered the investigation belongs to a different question."],"codes":["10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: Investigative policing in Whitechapel"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the development of the Criminal Investigation Department (CID). [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of Sir Charles Warren's time as Commissioner of the Metropolitan Police. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked in two parts of 2 marks each: 1 mark for a valid feature and a second mark for the supporting information attached to it, in each part. In 1(a) credit its creation out of the discredited Detective Branch in 1878, its leadership and organisation under Howard Vincent, its plain-clothes detective work, or its accountability to the Commissioner and the Home Secretary. In 1(b) credit his army background and military manner, the breaking up of the Trafalgar Square demonstration in November 1887, the washing off of the Goulston Street writing, or his published defence of the police and his resignation.","sol":"1(a) Feature: it was created to replace a detective force that had disgraced itself. Supporting information: the Metropolitan Police's Detective Branch had been wrecked by a corruption scandal in which senior detectives were convicted, and in April 1878 it was reorganised as the Criminal Investigation Department under a new Director of Criminal Investigations, Howard Vincent. 1(b) Feature: he was a soldier who ran the force like a regiment. Supporting information: Warren, Commissioner from 1886 and a serving colonel of the Royal Engineers, banned a demonstration in Trafalgar Square on 13 November 1887 and held the square with thousands of police supported by troops, leaving many injured and scores arrested.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["One part is about a national reorganisation of detective work, the other about the man at the top of the force during the murders.","A year and a name are the supporting information in each part; do not leave both out of either.","Both parts ask you to describe: save any argument about how effective the department or the Commissioner was in Whitechapel."],"codes":["10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The national and regional context"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of Charles Booth's survey of London. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the courts and alleys around Flower and Dean Street. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked in two parts of 2 marks each: 1 mark for a valid feature and a second mark for the supporting information attached to it, in each part. In 1(a) credit its private funding and method, its dates and volumes, the poverty map and its colour classes, or its findings on the extent and causes of poverty. In 1(b) credit the lodging houses that lined them, the single archway and the lack of a through route, the absence of lamps, or their reputation as a rookery.","sol":"1(a) Feature: it was a privately funded, street-by-street social survey. Supporting information: Charles Booth, a Liverpool shipowner, began his inquiry into London life and labour in 1886 and paid investigators to work from school-board visitors' records and interviews; the first volume, on East London, appeared in 1889, and the inquiry ran on until 1903. 1(b) Feature: they were closed courts entered through a single archway. Supporting information: the courts behind Flower and Dean Street had one way in and out and no lamp inside, so a constable's bull's-eye lantern was seen from the far end long before he arrived, and the street itself was lined with common lodging houses as the most notorious rookery in Whitechapel.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["One part is about how London's poverty was recorded, the other about the ground that poverty was lived on.","Dates, the colour classes and a percentage are what earn the second mark in 1(a); the shape of a court and what it lacked earns it in 1(b).","Describe the survey and the courts themselves, not what historians or the police could do about either."],"codes":["10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The local context of Whitechapel","10-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Knowledge of local sources"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. How could you follow up Source A to find out more about the difficulties the police faced in Whitechapel in the late 1880s? In your answer, you must give the question you would ask and the type of source you could use. Complete the four boxes: Detail in Source A that I would follow up / Question I would ask / What type of source I could use / How this might help answer my question. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for a detail from Source A that could form the basis of a follow-up enquiry, 1 for a question linked to it, 1 for naming an appropriate type of source, and 1 for explaining how it would help. The question must follow from the detail.","sol":"Detail in Source A that I would follow up: his statement that 'half the division is out on the special night patrols', so thefts from the Wentworth Street stalls go unattended. Question I would ask: how many of H Division's officers were actually taken off ordinary beat duty in the autumn of 1888, and did reported crime on those beats rise? What type of source I could use: the duty books and divisional returns of H Division, with its charge sheets for the same weeks. How this might help answer my question: they would give the real number of men diverted, testing whether 'half the division' was accurate or the exaggeration of an officer asking for reinforcements.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Look for the claim in the report that is made without any figure behind it.","A follow-up question is only worth asking if you can name a record that would settle it, so frame the question and the record together.","Four boxes, four marks: the last one has to say what the record would actually show you, not just name it."],"codes":["10-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Framing of questions relevant to the pursuit of a specific enquiry","10-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Selection of appropriate sources for specific investigations","10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The organisation of policing in Whitechapel"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. How could you follow up Source A to find out more about the common lodging houses of Whitechapel? In your answer, you must give the question you would ask and the type of source you could use. Complete the four boxes: Detail in Source A that I would follow up / Question I would ask / What type of source I could use / How this might help answer my question. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for the detail, 1 for a linked question, 1 for an appropriate type of source, 1 for how it helps. Credit the beds being let twice over, the count of houses on two beats, or the shifting population.","sol":"Detail in Source A that I would follow up: his claim that there are 'upwards of forty common lodging houses' on his two beats, the beds let twice over on a good night. Question I would ask: how many people were legally allowed to sleep in these houses, and how far did the numbers sleeping there exceed that limit? What type of source I could use: the lodging-house registers kept under the Common Lodging Houses Act of 1851, and the police returns to the Home Office in 1888. How this might help answer my question: the registers give each house and the beds it was licensed for, so I could set permitted capacity against the 8,530 lodgers the police counted.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The inspector gives a number for his own two beats but no figure for the district; that gap is the opening.","Ask what the law required somebody to write down about these houses, and the record will suggest itself.","Your final box should explain the comparison the record would let you make."],"codes":["10-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Framing of questions relevant to the pursuit of a specific enquiry","10-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Knowledge of local sources","10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The inhabitants of Whitechapel"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. How could you follow up Source A to find out more about the treatment of Jewish people in Whitechapel during the murders of 1888? In your answer, you must give the question you would ask and the type of source you could use. Complete the four boxes: Detail in Source A that I would follow up / Question I would ask / What type of source I could use / How this might help answer my question. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for the detail, 1 for a linked question, 1 for an appropriate type of source, 1 for how it helps. Credit the arrest of the bootmaker, the jeering in Yiddish and English, or the hostile crowds following constables.","sol":"Detail in Source A that I would follow up: his remark that since the bootmaker was taken up in September his men are 'jeered at in Yiddish and in English alike' and are followed by crowds. Question I would ask: how did that arrest and release in September 1888 change the way Jewish people in Whitechapel were treated, by their neighbours and by the police? What type of source I could use: London newspapers of September and October 1888, including the local East End papers and the Yiddish press. How this might help answer my question: they reported the arrest and the street attacks that followed, and printed letters showing what was said publicly.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["One sentence in the report points outside the police station altogether, to how the district was behaving.","Ask yourself where opinions get written down in 1888, and whose opinions those would be.","In the last box, say what the record would let you hear that a police report never would."],"codes":["10-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Framing of questions relevant to the pursuit of a specific enquiry","10-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Knowledge of national sources","10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The inhabitants of Whitechapel"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. How could you follow up Source A to find out more about the Whitechapel Vigilance Committee? In your answer, you must give the question you would ask and the type of source you could use. Complete the four boxes: Detail in Source A that I would follow up / Question I would ask / What type of source I could use / How this might help answer my question. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for the detail, 1 for a linked question, 1 for an appropriate type of source, 1 for how it helps. Credit the tradesmen's patrols, the men they brought in, or the friction between the committee and the police.","sol":"Detail in Source A that I would follow up: his complaint that the tradesmen's committee send out patrols of their own at night, who stop respectable men and bring them in, so that the police must let them go again. Question I would ask: who were the members of the Whitechapel Vigilance Committee, and what instructions were their patrols given? What type of source I could use: the committee's own papers — its minutes, printed handbills and letters to the Home Office and the newspapers. How this might help answer my question: they would name the tradesmen and set out what the patrols were told to do, showing whether the men acted on organised instructions or their own alarm.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The inspector mentions a body outside the police that is making work for him; that is the detail to take.","Ask something the committee itself would have had to record, rather than something about its members' feelings.","Name a kind of record, not one letter, and then say what it would let you decide."],"codes":["10-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Framing of questions relevant to the pursuit of a specific enquiry","10-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Selection of appropriate sources for specific investigations","10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: Investigative policing in Whitechapel"]},{"q":"Study Sources A and B in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the difficulties the police faced in Whitechapel in the late 1880s? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks, levelled. Level 3 (6–8) needs judgements on usefulness for this enquiry with developed reasoning on how nature, origin and purpose affect the usefulness of the content, and contextual knowledge used in interpreting the sources. Credit the beat arithmetic and shifting population in A, the unlit court and torn notice in B, and the purposes of a request for men and of a paper selling to outsiders.","sol":"Source A is very useful because it is an insider's list of the obstacles themselves. The inspector names a shifting population that is 'not the same people from one week to the next', courts entered by a single unlit archway, and the arithmetic that any one court is watched 'for two minutes in every thirty'. Its nature and origin strengthen that: an internal report to his own superintendent has no public to reassure, and he walked these beats himself. Its purpose weakens one claim in particular: the report ends by asking for four more constables, so 'half the division' is the sort of proportion a man seeking reinforcements rounds up. My own knowledge confirms the picture rather than the emphasis, since the police return of 1888 counted 233 lodging houses holding 8,530 people. Source B is less reliable about any single fact, because an artist drawing for readers outside the East End in a paper whose sales were rising invented the figure turning away at the back of the court — nobody had seen the murderer. But it is useful for an obstacle no police report would admit: the printed police notice pasted beside the door has been torn across, and a constable is standing escorting sightseers. For this enquiry A is the better guide to what the difficulties were, and B the better guide to the publicity that made them worse.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Judge each source against this enquiry only: what obstacle does it let you see that the other does not?","Take nature, origin and purpose in turn and aim each at one named claim inside the source rather than at the source as a whole.","Plan four moves per source — what it shows, why its provenance makes that stronger or weaker, one fact of your own that tests it, and what it is therefore good for — then a single comparative sentence at the end."],"codes":["10-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Recognition of the strengths and weaknesses of different types of source","10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The organisation of policing in Whitechapel","10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The local context of Whitechapel"]},{"q":"Study Sources A and B in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into living conditions in Whitechapel in the 1880s? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks, levelled. Level 3 (6–8) requires usefulness judged for living conditions specifically, with provenance used on named content and context deployed on the sources. Credit double-let beds and unlit courts in A, washing, broken water butt, crowded stairs and barefoot children in B, and the fact that neither was made to record housing.","sol":"Neither source was made to describe housing, which is the first thing to say, because it changes what each can be trusted for. Source A gives conditions incidentally and is the stronger for it: an inspector explaining poor results has no motive to invent overcrowding, and he reports upwards of forty lodging houses on two beats with the beds 'let twice over' on a good night, and courts with one archway and no lamp. Incidental detail in a document written for another purpose is usually reliable, and my own knowledge supports it, since the police counted 233 common lodging houses in Whitechapel in 1888 sleeping 8,530 people at about fourpence a bed. Source B shows conditions directly — washing strung window to window, a broken water butt under a spout, men and women sitting close together on the stairs inside an open doorway, barefoot children on the cobbles — but it is an engraving made for a paper selling outside the East End whose sales had risen that autumn, so squalor is what its readers had paid to see and the scene is composed, not recorded. It is still useful, because the physical details are ordinary rather than sensational and match what Booth's survey of East London, published in 1889, put in black and dark blue on its map. Source A is the more trustworthy; B is the more vivid, and useful chiefly as evidence of how those conditions were shown to outsiders.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Neither document was written to record housing. Decide whether that makes them worse or better witnesses before you write.","Sort the details of Source B into the ones an artist could have stood and copied and the ones he had to compose.","Bring one piece of your own knowledge to each source and use it to confirm or to test a particular detail, never as a paragraph of background."],"codes":["10-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Recognition of the strengths and weaknesses of different types of source","10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The local context of Whitechapel","10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The inhabitants of Whitechapel"]},{"q":"Study Sources A and B in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into attitudes towards the people who lived in Whitechapel? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks, levelled. Level 3 (6–8) requires the enquiry to be read as being about attitudes, so that each source is judged as a carrier of attitude as well as of fact. Credit the inspector's language about respectable men and the jeering crowds, and B's composition for a readership outside the East End.","sol":"For an enquiry into attitudes, a source's purpose stops being a weakness and becomes the evidence itself. Source A is useful because of how its writer divides the district: the tradesmen's patrols stop 'respectable men' and bring them in, implying that the rest are not respectable, and the crowds that jeer his constables in Yiddish and English are described as an obstacle rather than as people with a grievance. As an internal report to a superintendent it is unguarded, which makes it a better guide to what officers actually thought than any public statement would be, and my own knowledge explains the resentment he records, since a Jewish bootmaker had been arrested and released in September 1888 and attacks on Jewish people in the street followed. Source B is useful for the opposite side of the exchange: it was engraved for a weekly selling chiefly outside the East End, and it shows three gentlemen in tall hats standing just inside the archway, one of them writing in a notebook, with a second constable at their elbow, while a shadowy figure turns away at the back. That composition tells you precisely how comfortable London was invited to see Whitechapel — as a spectacle to be visited and feared. B is therefore the more useful of the two for outside attitudes and A for official ones, and together they show that both looked at the inhabitants from the outside.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["When the enquiry is about attitudes, ask what each source reveals about its own maker, not only what it reports.","Look for the words and the pictorial choices that sort people into groups, and say who is doing the sorting.","Decide early whose attitudes each source gives you access to, and make that the basis of the comparison at the end."],"codes":["10-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Recognition of the strengths and weaknesses of different types of source","10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The inhabitants of Whitechapel","10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The national and regional context"]},{"q":"Study Sources A and B in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the effects of the Whitechapel murders of 1888 on the district? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks, levelled. Level 3 (6–8) requires usefulness judged against effects on the district, using timing and provenance. Credit the diverted special patrols and the tradesmen's committee in A, the torn police notice, the sightseers and the risen sales in B, and the fact that both were made during the murders themselves.","sol":"Both sources are useful here chiefly because of when they were made: A in October and B in the last week of November 1888, inside the murders rather than after them, so neither is shaped by knowing how the case ended. Source A shows three effects at once and shows them as problems rather than as news: constables drawn off ordinary duty for the special night patrols, so that thefts from the Wentworth Street stalls went unattended; crowds following officers and demanding to know what the police had done; and the tradesmen's committee sending out its own patrols. My own knowledge names that committee — the Whitechapel Vigilance Committee, formed at the Crown on the Mile End Road on 10 September 1888 under George Lusk — and its existence confirms that confidence in the police had collapsed. Source A's purpose, a request for four more men, makes it likely to overstate the disruption, so its figure of half the division should be treated as an argument. Source B adds what A cannot: a printed police notice torn across on a wall, and three gentlemen in tall hats standing just inside the archway with a second constable at their elbow, in a paper whose sales had risen sharply that autumn. The engraving is composed and its sinister figure invented, but as evidence that the murders turned the district into a public spectacle it is better than any report.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Check the date on each source first and ask what a document made during the events can show that a later one cannot.","Separate the effects on the police from the effects on the people who lived there, and find each in a different source.","One source has a reason to exaggerate and the other has a reason to dramatise; name the specific claim each of those motives touches."],"codes":["10-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Recognition of the strengths and weaknesses of different types of source","10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: Investigative policing in Whitechapel","10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The national and regional context"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In which year did the Peabody Estate open on Commercial Street in Whitechapel, and how many blocks did it contain?","a":"1881, eleven blocks (containing 286 flats)."},{"q":"Which division of the Metropolitan Police policed Whitechapel, and where was its main station?","a":"H Division, with its main station in Leman Street."},{"q":"Who chaired the Whitechapel Vigilance Committee, and in which month and year was it formed?","a":"George Lusk, in September 1888."},{"q":"In which year was the Criminal Investigation Department created out of the Metropolitan Police's Detective Branch?","a":"1878."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The Peabody Estate, Commercial Street, Whitechapel","back":"Opened 1881 on slum land cleared by the Metropolitan Board of Works: eleven blocks, 286 flats, rents about three to six shillings a week. A committee visiting in July 1881 found only eleven of the displaced living there.","code":"10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The local context of Whitechapel","open":false},{"front":"The Artisans' and Labourers' Dwellings Improvement Act, 1875","back":"The Cross Act. It let local authorities compulsorily buy and demolish slum property. The Metropolitan Board of Works used it to clear the ground beside Commercial Street and sold the site on to the Peabody trustees.","code":"10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The local context of Whitechapel","open":false},{"front":"The rookery of Flower and Dean Street","back":"The most notorious block of courts and alleys in Whitechapel, lined with common lodging houses. Courts had a single archway, no through route and no lamp, so a constable could be seen coming and could be trapped.","code":"10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The local context of Whitechapel","open":false},{"front":"The Whitechapel Union workhouse","back":"Under the Poor Law Amendment Act of 1834. The union's workhouse at South Grove, off the Mile End Road, opened in 1876, designed for 485 inmates; over 800 only after the 1898 additions. Families were separated; inmates picked oakum.","code":"10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The local context of Whitechapel","open":false},{"front":"Common lodging houses in Whitechapel","back":"Police figures returned in 1888: 233 houses sleeping 8,530 people. A bed cost about fourpence for one night, let to whoever paid that evening. Registration and police inspection came from the Common Lodging Houses Act of 1851.","code":"10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The inhabitants of Whitechapel","open":false},{"front":"Jewish migration to Whitechapel in the 1880s","back":"After Tsar Alexander II was assassinated in 1881, pogroms and the Russian May Laws of 1882 drove a mass emigration; tens of thousands settled in Whitechapel and Spitalfields, bringing synagogues, a Yiddish press and socialist clubs.","code":"10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The inhabitants of Whitechapel","open":false},{"front":"Irish Londoners and the Special Irish Branch","back":"A long-settled Irish population, swollen after the 1840s famine, worked mainly on the docks and as navvies. Fenian bombings in England from 1881 to 1885, including one at Scotland Yard in 1884, led to the Special Irish Branch in 1883.","code":"10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The inhabitants of Whitechapel","open":false},{"front":"H Division of the Metropolitan Police","back":"The division covering Whitechapel, with its main station in Leman Street. By 1888 it had grown to something over 500 officers, almost all uniformed constables walking beats, with sergeants and inspectors above them.","code":"10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The organisation of policing in Whitechapel","open":false},{"front":"The beat constable's half-hour beat","back":"A constable walked a fixed beat at a steady pace, taking about half an hour, so he passed any court roughly twice an hour. His work was preventive and uniformed: moving people on, checking doors, breaking up fights.","code":"10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The organisation of policing in Whitechapel","open":false},{"front":"Whitechapel crime figures returned to the Home Office, 1888","back":"233 common lodging houses holding 8,530 people, 62 brothels, and an estimated 1,200 women working as prostitutes. Protection rackets on stalls and small shops were run by gangs such as the Bessarabians and Odessians.","code":"10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The organisation of policing in Whitechapel","open":false},{"front":"The Whitechapel murders of 1888","back":"Mary Ann Nichols (31 August), Annie Chapman (8 September), Elizabeth Stride and Catherine Eddowes on one night (30 September) and Mary Jane Kelly (9 November). Eddowes died in Mitre Square, inside the separate City of London Police area.","code":"10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: Investigative policing in Whitechapel","open":false},{"front":"Detective methods available in 1888","back":"House-to-house enquiries with over 2,000 interviewed, 300 investigated and 80 detained; sketches, photographs of victims, bloodhounds. No blood grouping, no fingerprint bureau until 1901, and no Bertillon measurements in London.","code":"10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: Investigative policing in Whitechapel","open":false},{"front":"The Whitechapel Vigilance Committee","back":"Formed at the Crown public house, Mile End Road, on 10 September 1888 by local tradesmen; chairman George Lusk. It hired private detectives, offered a reward, printed handbills and patrolled at night, and demanded a government reward.","code":"10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: Investigative policing in Whitechapel","open":false},{"front":"Sir Charles Warren","back":"Commissioner of the Metropolitan Police from 1886, a serving colonel of the Royal Engineers. Held Trafalgar Square on Bloody Sunday, 13 November 1887; had the Goulston Street writing washed off on 30 September 1888; resigned 8 November 1888.","code":"10-env — 1 Whitechapel, c1870–c1900: The national and regional context","open":false},{"front":"Charles Booth's survey of London","back":"Begun 1886, first volume on East London 1889, running to 1903. Its poverty map coloured streets from black (the lowest class, called semi-criminal) to yellow (wealthy). About 35 per cent of East Londoners fell below his poverty line.","code":"10-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Knowledge of local sources","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The sources and interpretations written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Source A: From a report written by an inspector of H Division of the Metropolitan Police, at Leman Street police station, for his superintendent, October 1888, on the policing of the lodging-house streets. Written for this room in the style of such a report; it is illustrative, not a real document, and it reproduces no real one. — Sir, you ask why the men on these two beats bring in so little. There are upwards of forty common lodging houses on them, and the deputies own that on a good night the beds are let twice over. The people in them are not the same people from one week to the next, so that a constable may pass a man on the Thursday and not know him on the Monday. The courts behind Flower and Dean Street have but one archway to them and no lamp inside; a bull's-eye is seen from the far end of a court long before the man carrying it arrives, and by the time he is under the arch there is nobody left in it but women and children. A beat here is walked round in half an hour, which is to say that any one court is watched for two minutes in every thirty. Since the bootmaker was taken up in September my men are jeered at in Yiddish and in English alike, and are followed by crowds asking what the police have done for them. The tradesmen's committee now send out patrols of their own at night, who stop respectable men and bring them in to us, and we must let them go again. Half the division is out on the special night patrols, and the thefts from the stalls in Wentworth Street are not attended to at all. I ask for four more constables on these beats and for lamps in the courts.","Source B: An engraving published in a London illustrated weekly newspaper in the last week of November 1888. The paper sold chiefly outside the East End and its sales had risen sharply during the autumn. Written for this room as a description of such an engraving; the picture is illustrative, not a real image, and it reproduces no real one. — The picture is a night scene in a Whitechapel court, drawn looking in through a low archway. In the foreground a constable stands with his lantern raised, and its beam falls on a woman sitting on a step with a child asleep across her knees. Behind her a doorway is open and light spills from it on to men and women sitting close together on the stairs; a printed police notice asking for information is pasted on the wall beside the door and has been torn across. On the left three gentlemen in tall hats stand just inside the archway, one of them writing in a notebook, with a second constable at their elbow. Washing is strung from window to window overhead, a broken water butt stands under a spout, and two barefoot children are fighting over something on the cobbles. In the deepest shadow at the back of the court a man in a long coat is turning away out of the light. The caption printed beneath calls the court one of the darkest corners of the district.","How the room uses them: Sources A and B are the pair for every 8-mark usefulness question in this room, and Source A is the source for every follow-up question. Every quotation in the worked examples, the model paragraphs and the model answers comes from the two texts above and from nowhere else; check each one against them as an examiner would."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Paper 1 Question 2(a), 8 marks)","items":["Question: 'How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the way the Metropolitan Police used its men in Whitechapel in the autumn of 1888? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context.' [8 marks]. This is the paragraph on Source A from the middle of the answer.","Source A is useful for this enquiry because it is a working policeman's account of where his division's men were actually going, written from inside the deployment itself, and it is specific about it: two beats covering upwards of forty lodging houses, a beat 'walked round in half an hour' so that any one court is watched 'for two minutes in every thirty', half the division out on the special night patrols, and the thefts from the stalls in Wentworth Street 'not attended to at all' as a result. Its nature and origin make that unusually trustworthy. This is an internal report from an inspector to his own superintendent, not a statement to the press or to a coroner, so there is no public to reassure, and the man writing it is the man whose beats are being stripped. Its purpose, however, is a request for four more constables and for lamps, and the purpose shapes one figure in particular: the claim that half the division is out on the special patrols is the sort of proportion a man asking for reinforcements rounds upwards, and it should be read as an argument rather than a return. My own knowledge supports the picture while testing that figure. On 15 September 1888 the Commissioner had put Chief Inspector Swanson at Scotland Yard in charge of the case, with every paper on it passing through his hands, and Inspector Abberline was working the district from Central Office, so men and supervision genuinely were being pulled into Whitechapel from outside the division; and the house-to-house enquiries of October 1888, in which about 80,000 handbills were put into householders' hands, show the sheer labour the district was absorbing that autumn. The source is therefore very useful for how the force spent its men, and useful in a second way its writer did not intend: it is the case H Division made for more of them.","Why this is Level 3: the judgement is on utility 'for the specified enquiry', and it applies a criterion — whether the source gives access to where the division's men were actually sent — rather than pronouncing the source good or bad in general. The provenance is used rather than described: the internal, upward character of the report is made to strengthen the deployment detail, while the purpose of asking for men is aimed at one particular figure, which is what 'developed reasoning which takes into account how the provenance affects the usefulness of the source content' means. The contextual knowledge (Swanson put in charge on 15 September 1888, Abberline working the district from Central Office, the 80,000 handbills of the October house-to-house enquiries) is used on the source, to confirm one part and test another, and never offered as background. The closing sentence turns a limitation into a use, which is the move that separates the top level from the middle."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same as above — 'How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the way the Metropolitan Police used its men in Whitechapel in the autumn of 1888?' [8 marks]. Again this is the paragraph on Source A.","Source A is quite useful because it was written by a police inspector who worked in Whitechapel, so he was actually there and he knew where his men were being sent. He says that half the division was out on the special night patrols and that the thefts from the stalls were not attended to, which shows the police did not have enough men to do everything. He also says a beat took half an hour to walk round, so each court was hardly watched at all. However, the source is biased because he was a policeman and he wanted four more constables, so he might have made things sound worse than they really were. It is also only one man's account of two beats and not the whole division, so it does not tell us about everywhere. Overall it is fairly useful but it does have its limits.","Why this is Level 2 and not Level 3: it does give 'judgements on source utility for the specified enquiry … using valid criteria', and it does select material from the source to support them, which is what Level 2 asks for. What it lacks is Level 3's 'developed reasoning which takes into account how the provenance affects the usefulness of the source content'. 'Biased because he was a policeman' is a label, not reasoning: it never names the figure in the report that the inspector's purpose would bend, so it changes no particular inference. There is also no contextual knowledge at all — the officers drafted into the district from Central Office, the chief inspector put in charge of the case in September 1888, the scale of the October house-to-house enquiries are all missing — and the mark scheme gives no credit for context that is not used on the sources, so nothing here is confirmed or tested from outside. 'Fairly useful but it does have its limits' decides nothing. To lift it, attach every provenance comment to one named claim, and bring one dated fact to each source."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Peabody Estate","items":["The Peabody Donation Fund was set up in 1862 by George Peabody, an American banker living in London, to build decent housing for the London poor; Peabody himself died in 1869.","The Artisans' and Labourers' Dwellings Improvement Act of 1875, brought forward by the Home Secretary Richard Cross, allowed local authorities to buy up and demolish slum property compulsorily.","Under that Act the Metropolitan Board of Works cleared the ground beside Commercial Street, next to the Flower and Dean Street rookery, and sold the site on cheaply to the Peabody trustees.","The Peabody Estate on Commercial Street, Whitechapel, opened in 1881: eleven blocks designed by the architect Henry Darbishire, containing 286 separate flats.","The blocks had brick walls left bare so they could be washed down, proper ventilation, and shared sinks, laundries and lavatories on the landings.","The rules were strict: rent paid weekly in advance, no trade carried on in the rooms, and the superintendent free to inspect.","Rents ran from about three shillings a week for a single room to about six shillings for three rooms.","A parliamentary select committee visited in July 1881, six weeks after the opening, and found the blocks almost full, with 1,260 people living in 286 families.","The same committee found that only eleven of the people cleared off the site had come back into the new buildings: the casual poor could not pay a fixed weekly rent or keep the rules.","The argument an examiner rewards: slum clearance without cheap replacement housing pushed the very poorest sideways into the courts that remained, so the Peabody Estate improved the housing of the respectable working class and made overcrowding in the rookeries worse."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Whitechapel workhouses","items":["The Poor Law Amendment Act of 1834 grouped parishes into unions and made the workhouse the main relief for the able-bodied poor, on the principle that conditions inside must be worse than the worst-paid work outside.","The Whitechapel Union's workhouse at South Grove, on the south side of the Mile End Road, took up that role in 1876; Richard Robert Long's buildings were designed for 485 inmates, and the establishment held over 800 only after further pavilions and dormitory blocks were completed in 1898.","Inside, families were separated: men, women, boys and girls slept, ate and worked apart, and dress, diet and timetable were fixed by the guardians.","Inmates worked for their keep: oakum picking (teasing old rope apart with the fingers), stone breaking and bone crushing for the men, laundry and cleaning for the women.","The casual ward, known as the spike, gave a vagrant a bath, a bed and a meal for a night in return for a task of work, and could hold him until the task was finished.","The union also ran an infirmary, so that by the 1880s the sick poor of Whitechapel were treated in a hospital ward rather than in the workhouse itself.","Most of the poor avoided all of it: the alternatives were a common lodging house at about fourpence a night, sleeping in a court, or the churchyard of Christ Church, Spitalfields, known locally as Itchy Park.","Several of the women killed in 1888 had moved repeatedly between lodging houses, casual wards and the street, which shows how narrow the margin was between a bed and no bed.","Workhouse admission and discharge registers survive and are a first-class local source: they record who came in, how often they returned and where they said they had come from.","The argument an examiner rewards: because relief was designed to deter rather than to relieve, Whitechapel's poorest stayed on the street, mobile and unrecorded — which is precisely the population the beat system could not know."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: H Division and the 'beat constable'","items":["The Metropolitan Police was created by Robert Peel's Metropolitan Police Act of 1829 and divided London into lettered divisions; Whitechapel was H Division, with its main station in Leman Street.","By 1888 H Division had grown to something over 500 officers, the overwhelming majority of them uniformed constables, with sergeants and inspectors above them.","A constable walked a fixed beat at a steady pace so that it took about half an hour to complete, meaning he passed any one point roughly twice an hour and it was unwatched in between.","The system was preventive: the constable was there to be seen. Detection was the exception, not the rule, and most of his work was moving people on, checking doors and dealing with drunks.","Beats were designed for open streets. Courts and alleys with one archway, no lighting and no through route defeated them, because the constable's bull's-eye lantern announced him from the far end.","Recruits were mostly former labourers and ex-soldiers; pay was poor, turnover was very high and drunkenness was the commonest reason for dismissal.","Police figures returned to the Home Office in 1888 recorded 233 common lodging houses in Whitechapel holding 8,530 people, 62 brothels, and an estimated 1,200 women working as prostitutes.","By the end of the century protection rackets were run on market stalls and small immigrant shops by gangs, the best known being the Bessarabians and the Odessians, who wrecked the premises of traders who would not pay.","H Division also had to police political meetings and demonstrations in a district where the Social Democratic Federation, founded in 1881, and Jewish socialist clubs were active.","The argument an examiner rewards: policing failed in Whitechapel by design rather than by idleness — a uniformed, preventive, beat-based force built for wide streets was applied to a maze of courts whose inhabitants changed every week."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the crimes of Jack the Ripper and the media reporting of the murders","items":["Five murders between August and November 1888 were linked together at the time: Mary Ann Nichols (31 August), Annie Chapman (8 September), Elizabeth Stride and Catherine Eddowes on the same night (30 September, the double event) and Mary Jane Kelly (9 November).","Eddowes was killed in Mitre Square, which lies inside the City of London, so her murder was investigated by the separate City of London Police while the rest belonged to the Metropolitan Police and Scotland Yard.","On the night of the double event, chalked writing was found in Goulston Street above a piece of Eddowes's apron; Sir Charles Warren had it washed off at about half past five in the morning, before it could be photographed, for fear of anti-Jewish rioting.","The name 'Jack the Ripper' came from a letter received by the Central News Agency on 27 September 1888 and published at the start of October; it is very probably a hoax, and it turned five local killings into a national serial.","The Star, a halfpenny evening paper launched in January 1888, and the Illustrated Police News built circulation on the murders, printing dramatic engravings of scenes no artist had witnessed.","Press reporting brought sightseers, false confessions, hundreds of letters, and the widely repeated idea that the murderer must be a foreigner or a Jew, which the police then had to work against.","The investigation was large: house-to-house enquiries across the district, more than 2,000 people interviewed, over 300 investigated and about 80 detained.","Methods used included witness descriptions turned into sketches, photographs of the victims (Kelly's body was photographed where it lay) and an experiment with bloodhounds.","Nothing available could turn physical evidence into an identity: there was no way to group blood, no fingerprint system in Britain until the Metropolitan Police Fingerprint Bureau opened in 1901, and Bertillon's system of body measurements, devised in Paris in the early 1880s, was not in use in London.","The argument an examiner rewards: every technique the police had could only confirm a suspect already identified, and in a district of shifting lodgers where nobody could name anybody, no suspect was ever identified."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Whitechapel Vigilance Committee","items":["Formed at a meeting in the Crown public house on the Mile End Road on 10 September 1888 by local tradesmen, who feared the murders were driving custom out of the district.","Its chairman was George Lusk, a builder and decorator from Mile End; the members were shopkeepers and small businessmen, not gentlemen reformers from outside.","It hired two private detectives, offered its own reward, printed handbills and sent unpaid members out on night patrols carrying sticks and whistles.","It wrote repeatedly to the Home Secretary, Henry Matthews, asking the government to offer a reward; Matthews refused, on the Home Office view held since 1884 that rewards produce false information.","It also petitioned the Queen and pressed for better street lighting and more constables for the district.","On 16 October 1888 Lusk received a parcel containing half a kidney with a note whose heading claimed it came from hell; the police could not establish whose kidney it was.","The patrols hindered as often as they helped: untrained men stopping and detaining passers-by created arrests that could not be sustained and work the police did not need.","The committee is the clearest evidence of how far public confidence in the police had fallen, which is why it appears so often as the subject of a 'describe one feature' part of Question 1.","Its papers — minutes, handbills and letters to the Home Office and the press — are a good answer to a follow-up question about public reaction.","The argument an examiner rewards: the committee shows that pressure on Warren and Matthews came from below as well as from the newspapers, and that Whitechapel's inhabitants did not simply wait to be protected."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Sir Charles Warren and the role of the Home Secretary","items":["The Metropolitan Police was the only force in the country answerable not to a local watch committee but to the Home Secretary, who appointed and could dismiss its Commissioner.","In 1888 the Home Secretary was Henry Matthews, in Lord Salisbury's government; he had no police experience and got on badly with his Commissioner.","Sir Charles Warren was appointed Commissioner in 1886. He was a serving army officer, a colonel of the Royal Engineers — he was not promoted major-general until 1893, in Singapore — and he ran the force in a military way.","On Sunday 13 November 1887 Warren banned a demonstration in Trafalgar Square and held the square with thousands of police supported by troops; accounts differ over the numbers, but many were injured, scores arrested, and at least two men later died of their injuries. The day became known as Bloody Sunday.","Bloody Sunday cost the police the trust of working-class London a year before the murders began, and made Warren's later decisions look like the acts of a soldier rather than a policeman.","Warren's order to wash off the Goulston Street writing on 30 September 1888 was defended as preventing anti-Jewish riots and attacked as destroying the only evidence in the killer's own hand.","Matthews refused every request for a government reward, and a pardon for any accomplice was offered only in November 1888, after the last of the murders.","Warren published an article defending the police without the Home Secretary's permission, was rebuked for it, and resigned on 8 November 1888; the resignation became public just as Mary Jane Kelly's body was found on 9 November.","Nationally, investigative policing had been reorganised in these years: the Detective Branch, discredited by a corruption scandal, became the Criminal Investigation Department in April 1878 under Howard Vincent.","The argument an examiner rewards: the failure in Whitechapel was as much a failure of the relationship between the Home Office and Scotland Yard, and of public trust destroyed a year earlier, as of the constables on the ground."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Charles Booth's survey","items":["Charles Booth was a Liverpool shipowner who began a private inquiry into life and labour in London in 1886, partly to test a claim that a quarter of Londoners lived in poverty.","He employed paid investigators who worked from school-board visitors' records and from interviews, and later walked the streets with police constables to check their classifications.","The first volume, covering East London, was published in 1889; the inquiry continued until 1903 and grew to seventeen volumes.","Its famous product is the Descriptive Map of London Poverty, which colours every street by the condition of the people living in it.","The colour classes run from black for the lowest class, whom Booth described as semi-criminal, through dark blue for the very poor in chronic want and light blue for the poor, up to red for the well-to-do and yellow for the wealthy.","Booth's East London figures put about 35 per cent of the people there below his poverty line, a larger share than the claim he had set out to test.","He distinguished poverty caused by low and irregular earnings, which he found much the commonest cause, from poverty caused by drink or idleness.","The Flower and Dean Street streets were coloured black and dark blue and lay within a few yards of pink and red ones: rich and poor London were neighbours.","As evidence the survey is both a gift and a trap: the categories were Booth's own, the colours were awarded by investigators making judgements on the doorstep, and the map was designed to persuade.","The argument an examiner rewards: Booth turned Whitechapel from anecdote into evidence and made it much harder to argue that poverty there was simply the fault of the poor."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the inhabitants of Whitechapel — work, lodging and migration","items":["Whitechapel work was casual by nature: dock labour taken on by the day at the call-on, market portering, street selling, and tailoring and boot-making at piece rates in small workshops.","The sweating system — long hours, low piece rates and subcontracting in small workshops — was investigated by a House of Lords select committee which began sitting in 1888.","The common lodging house was the housing of last resort: a bed for about fourpence a night, let to whoever paid that evening, with a shared kitchen and no questions asked.","Police figures returned in 1888 counted 233 common lodging houses in Whitechapel holding 8,530 people, thickest in Flower and Dean Street, Thrawl Street and Dorset Street.","Lodging houses had to be registered and could be inspected by the police under the Common Lodging Houses Act of 1851, which is why the police were able to count them at all.","Public houses did the work of a front room in a district where nobody had one, which is why drink runs through every police account of Whitechapel disorder.","The Irish population, long settled and swollen by emigration after the famine of the 1840s, worked mainly as dock labourers and navvies; Fenian bombings in England between 1881 and 1885, including an explosion at Scotland Yard in 1884, led to the Special Irish Branch in 1883.","After the assassination of Tsar Alexander II in 1881, pogroms and the Russian May Laws of 1882 drove a mass Jewish emigration; tens of thousands settled in Whitechapel and Spitalfields during the 1880s.","Jewish Whitechapel had its own synagogues, its own Yiddish newspapers and its own socialist clubs, including the International Working Men's Educational Club in Berner Street; newcomers were blamed for undercutting wages in the sweated trades and for taking housing, and after the arrest and release of a Jewish bootmaker in September 1888 there were attacks on Jewish people in the street.","The argument an examiner rewards: because the lodging-house population changed every week, nobody — neighbour, landlord or constable — could say who was living in a street, and that, more than any shortage of men, is why detection failed."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the source moves this paper rewards","items":["Provenance means three things: nature (what kind of document or image it is), origin (who made it, when and where) and purpose (what it was made to do).","Usefulness is always usefulness for the enquiry the question names; the same source can be very useful for one enquiry and useless for the next, so quote the enquiry back to yourself before you start.","Content first: say what the source tells you, or lets you infer, about the named enquiry, and anchor it with a short quoted phrase or a named detail from the picture.","Provenance second, and aimed: explain how the nature, origin or purpose makes that particular inference stronger or weaker. 'The source is biased' scores nothing because it changes no inference.","Context third, and used: bring one piece of your own knowledge that confirms, qualifies or explains what the source says. Contextual knowledge earns nothing unless it is linked to evaluating the source.","A limitation is often a use in disguise: an engraving drawn to sell copies is weak evidence of what a court looked like and strong evidence of what readers were being invited to feel about it.","Finish with a comparative judgement: which source is more useful for this enquiry, on what criterion, and for which part of it — not which source is 'better'.","The follow-up answer has four parts and each must fit the one before: a detail taken from the source, a question that detail raises, a type of source that could answer it, and how that type would help.","Choose the unfinished detail: a claim made without proof, a figure with no comparison, a group named in passing, an event mentioned as though the reader already knows it.","Name a type of record, not a single document — police duty books and divisional returns, lodging-house registers, coroners' inquest papers, census returns, workhouse admission registers, medical officer of health reports, Old Bailey Proceedings, Home Office correspondence, London and national newspapers — and then say what it contains that would settle the question."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 1 Question 2(a): 'How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the lighting of the streets and courts of Whitechapel in the late 1880s?' [8 marks]","items":["The question as printed: 'Study Sources A and B in the Sources Booklet. How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the lighting of the streets and courts of Whitechapel in the late 1880s? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context.' No stimulus points are printed and no SPaG marks are awarded; the two sources are the whole material, and both must be used.","Decode the enquiry before reading either source. Lighting is not the same as darkness in general. It covers what lamps there were and where they stopped, who was supposed to pay for them and light them, what an unlit court did to police work, and who was complaining about it. Decide which of these each source can reach, because that is your criterion for usefulness.","Timing: about 13 minutes. Two minutes reading and annotating both sources against the enquiry, nine minutes writing two paragraphs and a judgement, two minutes checking that every provenance comment is attached to a named claim.","Paragraph 1, Source A, content: the courts behind Flower and Dean Street have 'but one archway to them and no lamp inside'; a bull's-eye 'is seen from the far end of a court long before the man carrying it arrives', so the only light in the court is the light the constable brings; by the time he is under the arch there is nobody left but women and children; and the report closes by asking for lamps in the courts as well as for four more constables.","Paragraph 1, Source A, provenance used twice. Nature and origin strengthen it: an internal report from an inspector to his own superintendent, written by a man who has walked these courts at night, with no public to reassure, so his account of what a lantern does in an unlit court is first-hand and technical. Purpose weakens the emphasis rather than the fact: the report is a request, so darkness is being offered as the explanation of poor results, and the reader must ask whether lighting was the whole of his difficulty or the part of it that could be bought.","Paragraph 1, Source A, context used on the source: under the Metropolis Management Act of 1855 the paving, cleansing and lighting of the district's streets belonged to the Whitechapel District Board of Works, while a passage or yard inside private property was the landlord's affair, which is exactly why a court entered by one archway could have no lamp in it; and in October 1888, answering the board's own resolution calling for more police, the Commissioner replied that the neighbourhood was very imperfectly lighted and that darkness was an important assistant to crime. Interim judgement: very useful for what the want of a lamp did to a beat, and useful as one side of a quarrel about who should pay for lamps.","Paragraph 2, Source B, content: a night scene drawn through a low archway; a constable with his lantern raised and its beam the strongest light in the picture; a doorway spilling light on to men and women on the stairs; the deepest shadow at the back of the court, where a man in a long coat turns away out of the light; and a caption calling the court one of the darkest corners of the district.","Paragraph 2, Source B, provenance used twice. Nature and purpose weaken it on fact: an engraving made for a weekly selling chiefly outside the East End, whose sales had risen sharply that autumn, so the darkness is composed for effect — a picture that wanted to frighten would darken a court whatever its lamps were like, and the figure at the back is invention, because nobody had seen the murderer. But the same provenance strengthens it on one point the paper had no motive to invent: that the only reliable light in such a court was carried in by hand, which is the same claim Source A makes from the other side.","Paragraph 2, Source B, context used on the source: newspaper inspections of these courts in the first week of October 1888 reported the lamps as feeble and out of date, with some courts lit as sparingly as the board could manage, so the engraver's darkness was not pure invention; and after the Local Government Act of 1888 the London County Council took over from the Metropolitan Board of Works in 1889, so the paving and lighting records against which the picture could be tested exist in both hands. Interim judgement: unreliable on any single lamp, valuable on how the district's darkness was presented to people who did not live in it.","The evaluation thread that must run through both paragraphs: each source's purpose does not cancel it, it redirects it. Say for each what the purpose makes the source bad evidence of and what it makes it good evidence of, and you have built the top-level move twice.","What the judgement must say: not 'both are useful in different ways'. Name the criterion — access to what was and was not lit, and to what that cost — and decide on it. Source A is the more useful, because it states where the lamps stopped and what that did to a beat, though its purpose makes darkness the whole explanation; Source B is the more useful for the way that darkness was pictured for readers elsewhere, which A cannot reach. Together they support a point neither makes alone: that the light in a Whitechapel court in 1888 was whatever somebody carried into it."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:02:28.426880Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Fourth pass, judged fresh from the kit JSON after the dedupe retargeting, and the retargeting holds: the new setups - the sweated trades and Irish settlement in lesson.examples[0], police-public relations in lesson.examples[1] and [2], the Wentworth Street stall thefts in lesson.examples[3], the use of the division's men in extras[1]/[2] and street lighting in extras[12] - set no question in the bank, and node dupscan.js EDX-1HI0 10-env prints 'no duplication'. Every new fact checks out: the Lords select committee on sweating took evidence from 1888 and reported in April 1890 after 291 witnesses, defining sweating by inadequate wages, excessive hours and insanitary workrooms and calling the middleman the consequence not the cause;¹ the Fenian campaign of 1881-85 and the explosion of 30 May 1884 that wrecked the CID and Special Irish Branch offices formed in 1883; Warren's memorandum of 15 September 1888 giving Swanson the case with every paper passing through his hands; the Metropolis Management Act 1855 placing paving, cleansing and lighting on the district boards; and the Commissioner's reply to the Whitechapel board, published 4 October 1888, that the purlieus about Whitechapel were most imperfectly lighted and darkness an important assistant to crime, with the Daily News inspection of 6 October calling the lamps feeble and behind the times. Form is right to the head of levels.md - 1(a)/1(b) at 2 + 2, Level 3 at 6-8 on the 8-mark utility grid, no stimulus points and no SPaG in Section A, Paper 1 at 1 hour 20 - all ten key ideas are tested, the split stays 6/4/4, and no date, figure, event or attribution is wrong. What keeps it off full marks is small: two 4-mark sols a few words long, and four places where a claim about Sources A and B goes a shade beyond what the room actually printed.² ¹ 1911 Britannica, 'Sweating System'. ² See problems 2 to 4."}}},"11-1":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"11-1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This is the first room of the Medicine thematic study, and every later judgement is measured against it. Paper 1 Section B is worth 36 of the paper's 52 marks, and the medieval period supplies the 'before' half of almost every Question 3 comparison, the starting point of the Question 4 change questions, and the baseline a Question 5 or 6 judgement has to argue away from. If you cannot say precisely what a sick person in 1350 believed, was given and was charged, you cannot show that anything later was an improvement. This room also introduces the three key factors — individuals and institutions, science and technology, attitudes in society — in the period where all three worked against change, which is the clearest place to learn to use them.","idea":[{"h":"Medicine is made by the society that produces it","t":"Medieval England was overwhelmingly rural, ruled through the feudal relationship between lord and tenant, and dominated by the Catholic Church, which owned the universities, ran most hospitals and supplied the only education worth the name. Books were copied by hand until William Caxton set up England's first printing press at Westminster in 1476, so knowledge moved slowly and stayed in the hands of a few. Kings taxed, fought and made law; they did not run public health. Every feature of medieval medicine follows from these facts: care was religious because society was religious, learning was bookish because books were the only authority available, and treatment was local because government was local. A top-level answer never says medieval people were stupid; it explains the medicine out of the society, so the examiner sees analysis of the period rather than mockery of it.","code":"11 — The process of change: medicine and the key features of society"},{"h":"The three key factors, and why all three held medicine still","t":"Edexcel names three factors for the whole theme: individuals and institutions (the Church and government), science and technology, and attitudes in society. In c1250–c1500 each was a brake. The dominant institution, the Church, taught that disease came from God and protected the authority of Galen; government did almost nothing, because nobody expected it to. Science and technology offered no instrument that could reveal a cause — there was no microscope, and no printed book in England before 1476. Attitudes valued ancient authority: a physician's job was to know Galen, Hippocrates and Avicenna, not to test them. A top-level answer uses the factor names as the spine of its paragraphs, so that the same vocabulary can be carried forward into the Renaissance, the nineteenth century and the modern period, and a judgement can compare like with like across the theme.","code":"11 — The process of change: the key factors"},{"h":"Factors combine; they do not queue up","t":"The factors matter most when they reinforce one another. Galen's ideas survived not simply because they were clever, but because the Church approved of them, because university training reproduced them, and because no technology existed to disprove them: remove any one of the three and the other two still hold the system in place. The same is true of the response to the Black Death, where religious explanation, humoral medicine, miasma and the absence of any national machinery all pushed towards the same set of actions. A top-level answer shows the combination working, usually in the last sentence of each paragraph and again in the conclusion, rather than listing three separate factors and stopping. That is precisely what the Level 4 descriptor means by a line of reasoning that is coherent, sustained and logically structured.","code":"11 — The process of change: factors working together"},{"h":"What the Black Death case study is for","t":"Strand 3 of each period is a case study, and its job is to put strands 1 and 2 into one dated, concrete episode. The Black Death of 1348–49 is where the ideas about cause become visible as actions: people who believed in sin prayed, people who believed in miasma burned herbs and cleared filth, people who believed in the humours were bled, and a town that was frightened tried to shut its gates. The case study also gives you the comparison points the examiner rewards, because it can be set against the Great Plague of 1665 and the cholera outbreak of 1854. A top-level answer uses it as precisely selected evidence inside an argument, not as a story told for its own sake: name the year, name the action, and say which idea produced it.","code":"11 — The process of change: the role of the strand 3 case studies"},{"h":"Supernatural and religious explanations: sin, God and the stars","t":"The Church taught that God sent illness, either to punish sin or to test faith, and that recovery came through His mercy. This was not a fringe view but the official teaching of the most powerful institution in the country, repeated weekly in every parish. Alongside it ran astrology, which grew far more important after 1348: physicians carried folding almanacs of zodiac charts and checked where the planets stood before bleeding a patient or fixing a treatment, and in 1348 the medical faculty of the University of Paris blamed the plague on a conjunction of Saturn, Jupiter and Mars. A top-level answer treats these explanations as logical within their own framework and links them forward to the treatments they produced, because the examiner is testing analysis of a belief system, not a list of superstitions.","code":"11-1 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Supernatural and religious explanations"},{"h":"Rational explanations: the Four Humours, miasma and the long shadow of Galen","t":"Rational here means 'not supernatural', not 'correct'. The Theory of the Four Humours came from Hippocrates: health depended on a balance of blood, phlegm, yellow bile and black bile, and illness meant too much or too little of one. Galen, a Greek physician working in the Roman empire in the second century AD, added the Theory of Opposites, which told the physician to treat an excess with its opposite. Miasma, bad air rising from cesspits, corpses and rotting rubbish, explained why disease clustered in filthy places. Galen's books, together with Hippocrates and Avicenna's Canon of Medicine, were the medical course. A top-level answer says explicitly that these were rational explanations built on observation and reasoning, and that their persistence is the strongest single piece of evidence for continuity across the whole medieval period.","code":"11-1 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Rational explanations"},{"h":"Treatment followed the theory, every time","t":"Nothing in medieval treatment is random once you know the explanation behind it. Because illness was God's punishment, people prayed, paid for masses, fasted, did penance and went on pilgrimage to shrines such as Thomas Becket's at Canterbury. Because the humours could be unbalanced, barber surgeons bled patients with a knife or leeches and apothecaries sold emetics and laxatives to purge them. Because bad air carried disease, people carried sweet herbs, burned juniper indoors, and towns paid rakers to clear the streets — and Parliament, meeting at Cambridge in 1388, ordered that filth should not be thrown into ditches and rivers. Physicians also prescribed a regimen sanitatis: moderate food, sleep, exercise and bathing. A top-level answer always shows the chain from idea to action, because that chain is the analysis the AO2 marks are for.","code":"11-1 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Approaches to prevention and treatment and their connection with ideas about disease"},{"h":"Who treated you, and what it cost","t":"There were three kinds of paid healer and one unpaid one. A physician had spent seven years or more at a university such as Oxford, Cambridge, Bologna or Padua reading Galen, Hippocrates and Avicenna; he diagnosed by inspecting urine in a flask, taking the pulse and consulting a zodiac chart, and he was far too expensive for ordinary people. An apothecary, trained by apprenticeship and organised in a guild, mixed and sold herbal remedies. A barber surgeon, with no university training and low status, did the bloodletting, tooth-pulling, boil-lancing and amputations. Hospitals were run by the Church and the monasteries — St Bartholomew's in London was founded in 1123 — and offered rest, food, warmth and prayer rather than cure, often refusing the infectious. Most people were nursed at home by women with herbal remedies. A top-level answer ranks these by who actually reached the sick.","code":"11-1 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Medical training and traditional approaches to treatment and care"},{"h":"Dealing with the Black Death, 1348–49","t":"The plague reached England through the Dorset port of Melcombe Regis in the summer of 1348 and had spread to London by the autumn; historians estimate that between a third and a half of the population died. Contemporaries explained it with the ideas they already had: God's punishment for sin, a conjunction of the planets, and corrupted air. So they prayed and processed, cleared filth, carried herbs, were bled and had their buboes lanced. Prevention was local and improvised: Gloucester tried to shut its gates against people from Bristol, and in 1349 Edward III wrote to the Mayor of London ordering the streets to be cleaned. There was no national quarantine and no cure. A top-level answer uses 1348–49 to show that a catastrophe produced no new idea at all, which is the sharpest proof of continuity in the room.","code":"11-1 — 3 Case study: Dealing with the Black Death, 1348–49"}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 3, 4 marks: building one analysed difference across two periods","setup":"Paper 1 Question 3, worth 4 marks: 'Explain one way in which the cost of medical care to a sick person in the years c1250–c1500 was different from the cost of medical care in the years c1900–present.' The mark scheme gives 2 marks for AO2 and 2 for AO1, so you must analyse one difference and support it with specific information from both periods.","steps":["Fix the difference on who paid rather than on how much was charged: in medieval England the sick person or the family paid the healer directly, while in the modern period the cost of care is met collectively and does not fall on the patient at the moment of treatment.","Evidence the earlier side with named detail: a physician had spent seven years or more at a university reading Galen, Hippocrates and Avicenna's Canon of Medicine and charged fees only the rich could meet, so most people bought a remedy from an apothecary, paid a barber surgeon a few pence to bleed them or pull a tooth, or were nursed at home for nothing.","Evidence the later side with named detail: the National Health Service began on 5 July 1948, free at the point of use and paid for out of general taxation, and although a prescription charge of one shilling was brought in on 1 June 1952 the treatment itself stayed free.","Close with the sentence that makes it a difference rather than two descriptions: medieval treatment was a purchase, so what a sick person was given depended on what they could afford, whereas modern treatment is a shared entitlement, so what a patient is given depends on what is wrong with them."],"cues":["Is the difference the amount charged, or who pays it?","Which medieval detail shows the cost falling on the patient?","Which modern detail shows the cost falling somewhere else?","What does the final sentence have to do that the two halves have not?"]},{"title":"Question 3, 4 marks: proving a similarity that survives a change of period","setup":"Paper 1 Question 3, worth 4 marks: 'Explain one way in which the remedies given to sick people in their own homes in the years c1250–c1500 were similar to the remedies given in their own homes in the years c1500–c1700.' You must give one similarity and support it with specific information from both periods.","steps":["Take the similarity from where the treatment came from rather than from a list of ingredients: in both periods the ordinary sick were treated at home with herbal remedies mixed by the women of the household.","Evidence the medieval side: recipes were copied by hand and passed between households, and the ingredients — herbs such as chamomile and mint, with honey and wine — came from the garden, the hedgerow or a local apothecary.","Evidence the later side: the same domestic remedies were now printed for the household to buy, because Nicholas Culpeper put the London College of Physicians' Latin pharmacopoeia into English in 1649 and followed it with The English Physitian in 1652, a cheap herbal listing English plants against English illnesses.","State the similarity as a similarity: through both periods the household, not the trained healer, was the first and usually the only place a sick person was treated, so the remedy reached them by the same route even after printing changed how the recipe travelled."],"cues":["Where do both periods put the treatment of an ordinary sick person?","Which medieval detail proves the household was doing the treating?","Which later detail shows the same kind of remedy still in household hands?","What sentence turns two lists into one analysed similarity?"]},{"title":"Question 4, 12 marks: getting past the stimulus points","setup":"Paper 1 Question 4, worth 12 marks: 'Explain why hospitals in England did so little to cure the sick in the years c1250–c1500.' You may use the following in your answer: the Church; ideas about the cause of disease. You must also use information of your own. The mark scheme gives a maximum of 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, to an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content.","steps":["Turn each bullet from a label into a mechanism: the Church counts because it founded, owned and staffed these houses, so their day was built round prayer and the mass, and ideas about the cause of disease count because an illness sent by God, or caused by an imbalance of the humours, called for rest, diet and repentance rather than an operation.","Decide the third reason before writing a word, and make it one neither bullet mentions: the admission rules kept out exactly the people who needed curing, since those with infectious or incurable conditions were commonly refused and lepers were sent to separate houses outside the walls.","Give every reason a named or dated detail so the AO1 strand is fed: St Bartholomew's in London was founded in 1123, St Leonard's in York was one of the largest in the country with beds for more than two hundred people and was staffed by brothers and sisters under religious rule, and what a patient reliably received was a bed, warmth, food and the sacraments.","Ask of each paragraph 'why did this stop the hospital curing anyone?', then link the reasons in the final sentence so that they hold each other in place instead of standing in a queue."],"cues":["What does each bullet explain once you ask how a hospital actually ran?","Which reason will take the answer beyond the two bullets?","What named or dated detail keeps each reason out of generalisation?","What must the closing sentence do that the three paragraphs have not?"]},{"title":"Question 5, 16 marks: choosing the criterion before you argue","setup":"Paper 1 Question 5, worth 16 marks with up to 4 more for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology: 'There was more continuity than change in surgery in England in the years c1250–c1500.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: barber surgeons; John of Arderne. You must also use information of your own.","steps":["Set the criterion in the opening two sentences: continuity will be judged first by what a surgeon understood about the body and about wounds, and second by what a surgeon could actually do for the patient in front of him.","Make the two bullets analytical rather than descriptive: barber surgeons still learned by apprenticeship and ranked below physicians because cutting was treated as a manual craft, while John of Arderne shows an individual accumulating technique — his Practica of 1376 set out his operation for anal fistula and a soporific of hemlock, henbane and opium, and he had learned his trade as an army surgeon in the French wars.","Build the case against the statement from material of your own: a fellowship of surgeons was formed in London in 1368; Guy de Chauliac's Chirurgia Magna of 1363 became the standard textbook and was later put into English; and in 1403 the London surgeon John Bradmore drew a lodged arrowhead from the face of the future Henry V with an instrument made for the purpose, then dressed the wound with wine and honey.","Weigh rather than alternate: on the first criterion the statement holds, because nobody knew what made a wound fester and no drug dulled pain reliably, and on the second it weakens, because organisation, instruments and written technique all moved.","Write the conclusion as a decision with a reason attached: the statement is right about understanding and too strong about practice, and the reason for the split is that technique can be improved by experience alone, while the cause of infection could not be found without a theory nobody yet had."],"cues":["What would count as continuity here, and who decides?","How do you stop the bullets becoming description?","Which evidence of your own pushes hardest against the statement?","What happens to the argument if you simply put one side after the other?","What must a conclusion add that the paragraphs already contain?"]},{"title":"Question 4, 12 marks: explaining an absence rather than an event","setup":"Paper 1 Question 4, worth 12 marks: 'Explain why so few sick people in England were treated by a physician in the years c1250–c1500.' You may use the following in your answer: the training of physicians; apothecaries. You must also use information of your own. The mark scheme gives a maximum of 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, to an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content.","steps":["Work out what each bullet explains about supply and about price: seven years or more at a university such as Oxford, Cambridge, Bologna or Padua kept the number of physicians tiny and their fees high, while an apothecary was trained by apprenticeship, sold remedies over a counter and cost a fraction as much.","Add the reason neither bullet supplies: the country was overwhelmingly rural and the few physicians there were clustered in the towns and in the households of the rich, so for most people the obstacle was not only what a physician charged but how far away he was.","Show the alternatives that filled the gap, with named detail: barber surgeons bled patients, pulled teeth and lanced boils for a small fee, Church hospitals took the sick in as charity, and most people were nursed at home by the women of the household with herbal remedies.","Return to the conceptual focus in the last sentence: so few were treated by a physician because the training that gave him his authority also made him scarce, expensive and distant, and because cheaper healers were already doing the work."],"cues":["What do the two bullets say about how many physicians there were and what they cost?","What is missing from the bullets that explains where the physicians were?","Who was treating the sick instead, and on what terms?","What sentence keeps the whole answer on 'why so few'?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Why did bloodletting look like a sensible treatment to a medieval physician?","a":"Because the Theory of the Four Humours taught that illness came from an excess of one humour, so removing blood was thought to restore the balance."},{"q":"Name the two rational explanations of disease a medieval physician would have used, and the supernatural explanation the Church taught.","a":"Rational: the Theory of the Four Humours and miasma. Supernatural: disease sent by God as a punishment for sin."},{"q":"What did most sick people in medieval England actually receive, and from whom?","a":"Care at home — herbal remedies, food, warmth and rest — usually from the women of the household, because physicians were few and far too expensive."},{"q":"Why does the Black Death of 1348–49 count as evidence of continuity rather than change in ideas about disease?","a":"Because it produced no new explanation: it was blamed on God, on the planets and on miasma, all of which were already believed before 1348."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 1 Section B is worth 36 of the paper's 52 marks: Question 3 is 4 marks, Question 4 is 12 marks, and Question 5 or Question 6 is 16 marks plus up to 4 for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology.","Question 3, 'Explain one way in which … was similar to / different from …', carries 2 marks for AO2 and 2 for AO1 and normally spans at least a century, so both periods must be named and evidenced.","Question 4 stimulus caps: a maximum of 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for those answers.","Question 5 or 6 stimulus caps: a maximum of 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for those answers.","Timing on the 80-minute Paper 1: about 5 minutes on Question 3, 15 on Question 4 and 25 on Question 5 or 6, which is 45 minutes for Section B and leaves about 35 minutes for Section A's 16 marks; Question 5 or 6 is the only Section B answer where spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms are separately marked."],"questions":[{"q":"Explain one way in which ideas about the cause of disease in the years c1250–c1500 were different from ideas about the cause of disease in the years c1700–c1900. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — supernatural or humoral causes against causes located in microbes — supported by specific detail from both periods, such as the Four Humours and miasma against Pasteur’s germ theory of 1861.","sol":"One clear difference is where the cause of disease was thought to lie. In the years c1250–c1500 explanations were supernatural or humoral: disease was sent by God to punish sin, or came from an imbalance of the four humours — blood, phlegm, yellow bile and black bile — or from miasma, bad air from cesspits and rotting matter. In the years c1700–c1900 the cause was located in living organisms: Pasteur published his germ theory in 1861 and Koch identified the bacterium causing tuberculosis in 1882. The difference is that a medieval cause was invisible and could never be demonstrated, whereas the later cause was an organism that could be seen under a microscope and matched to a single disease.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what a medieval physician thought was doing the damage, and what a doctor in the 1890s thought was doing it.","A comparison needs both halves evidenced: one named idea with one named detail from each period, and then a sentence saying what kind of difference it is.","Name the single difference first, prove the earlier side, prove the later side, and finish by explaining why the two are different in kind rather than in degree."],"codes":["11-1 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Supernatural and religious explanations","11-1 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Rational explanations"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which attempts to prevent the spread of plague during the Black Death of 1348–49 were similar to attempts to prevent the spread of plague during the Great Plague in London in 1665. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed similarity — both outbreaks were fought by purifying the air — supported by specific detail from each outbreak and linked to the survival of miasma theory.","sol":"One similarity is that both outbreaks were fought by trying to purify the air, because miasma was still the standard explanation of plague in both years. In 1348–49 people held sweet herbs to their noses, burned juniper indoors and cleared filth from the streets, and in 1349 Edward III ordered the Mayor of London to have the city cleaned. In 1665 the London authorities had fires kept burning in the streets, people carried posies and smoked tobacco, and rubbish was again ordered away. The similarity matters because the same explanation of disease had survived for more than three centuries, so the same prevention was still being used — and it failed both times.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Look for the belief the two outbreaks shared rather than for two actions that merely look alike.","Both halves need dated, specific detail: what people actually did in each year, not what they generally thought.","Name the shared belief, show it in practice in each outbreak, and close by saying what the survival of that belief proves."],"codes":["11-1 — 3 Case study: Dealing with the Black Death, 1348–49","11-1 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Approaches to prevention and treatment and their connection with ideas about disease"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the control of who could treat the sick in the years c1250–c1500 was different from the control of who could treat the sick in the years c1900–present. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — no effective national control over who practised in the medieval period against a legal register of qualified doctors in the modern one — supported by detail such as guild apprenticeship and the physicians’ petition of 1421 against the register kept under the Medical Act of 1858.","sol":"The difference is whether a healer had to prove anything before treating a patient. In the years c1250–c1500 there was no national control: physicians spent years at a university, but apothecaries and barber surgeons learned by apprenticeship inside a guild, and wise women and untrained healers treated people freely. When physicians petitioned Parliament in 1421 to confine the practice of physic to university men, it was because no such rule existed, and nothing effective followed. In the years c1900–present nobody may work as a doctor without a recognised qualification and a place on the register kept by the General Medical Council, set up by the Medical Act of 1858. Medieval control rested on custom; modern control rests on law.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what a person had to prove before they were allowed to treat a patient in each period.","Name the kinds of healer at work in each period, then ask who, if anyone, stood above them deciding who could practise.","State the difference, support it from the medieval side, support it from the modern side, then explain why the two arrangements are not the same kind of thing."],"codes":["11-1 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Medical training and traditional approaches to treatment and care","11 — The process of change: the key factors"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the training of doctors in the years c1250–c1500 was different from the training of doctors in the years c1500–c1700. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — training by reading and disputing ancient authorities against training that included dissection and observation — supported by detail such as Galen and Avicenna as set books against Vesalius’s Fabrica of 1543.","sol":"The difference is the place of observation in training. In the years c1250–c1500 a physician’s training was almost entirely bookish: seven years or more at a university such as Oxford, Cambridge or Padua, reading and debating Galen, Hippocrates and Avicenna’s Canon of Medicine, with dissection, where permitted, used to demonstrate Galen rather than test him. In the years c1500–c1700 training began to include first-hand observation. Vesalius, who dissected bodies himself at Padua, published On the Fabric of the Human Body in 1543 and showed Galen wrong about the human jawbone and the breastbone. The difference is that medieval training treated the ancient authorities as unquestionable, while by the seventeenth century a student was shown where they were wrong.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what a student in each period was actually asked to do all day.","Both halves need named texts or named people, since the AO1 marks come from precise support.","Say what the difference is, evidence the medieval side, evidence the later side, then explain what changed about the status of the old books."],"codes":["11-1 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Medical training and traditional approaches to treatment and care","11 — The process of change: the key factors"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the care given to the sick in hospitals in the years c1250–c1500 was similar to the care given in hospitals in the years c1500–c1700. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed similarity — hospitals in both periods offered care rather than cure, funded by charity and shaped by religion — supported by detail from monastic hospitals and from their charitable refoundation after the dissolution.","sol":"One similarity is that hospitals in both periods offered care rather than cure. In the years c1250–c1500 most were small and run by the Church and the monasteries; St Bartholomew’s in London, founded in 1123, is the best-known survivor. Patients were given a bed, warmth, food and prayer, nursed by monks and nuns, and many hospitals refused the infectious. After the dissolution of the monasteries between 1536 and 1540 closed many of them, hospitals were refounded by charity and by town corporations, and pest houses took infectious cases, but a patient still received rest, food and religious comfort. In both periods the hospital existed to shelter and nurse the sick, because nothing reliably cured them.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what a patient actually got when they were admitted, rather than about who was paying for the building.","Support each period with a named hospital, a date or a named change, so the comparison is specific.","Name the similarity, evidence it in the medieval period, evidence it in the later period, and explain why it had not changed."],"codes":["11-1 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Medical training and traditional approaches to treatment and care","11 — The process of change: medicine and the key features of society"]},{"q":"Explain why the miasma theory was so widely believed in England in the years c1250–c1500.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the smell of rubbish and cesspits\n• the writings of Hippocrates and Galen\nYou must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit analysis of the everyday evidence of smell and sickness, of the ancient authority behind the idea of corrupted air, and of own points such as its fit with the Four Humours and with religious teaching and the actions it made possible. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Miasma — the belief that disease was carried in air corrupted by rotting matter — kept its hold for three centuries because it matched what people could see, because it carried the highest authority the period recognised, and because it fitted everything else they believed.\n\nThe first reason is that it agreed with ordinary experience, and the evidence was smell. Towns were full of it: cesspits, open ditches, butchers’ waste, tanneries, dunghills and rubbish in the street. Those were also the places where people fell ill most often, while the countryside smelled cleaner and seemed healthier. A theory that explains what people already notice does not have to be argued for.\n\nThe second reason is authority. Corrupted air was not a folk idea but part of the classical medicine taught in the universities: Hippocrates had written on how airs, waters and places shape health, and Galen, whose works were the foundation of the medical course, treated bad air as a cause of epidemic disease. Medieval learning worked by reading and disputing the ancients rather than by testing them, so a theory carrying those names was as close to proof as the period had.\n\nThe third reason lies beyond both bullets: miasma never had to compete with the other explanations, because it fitted beside them. Bad air entering the body could be described as upsetting the balance of the four humours, and it could equally be described as the means by which God punished sin, so nobody had to choose. It also told people what to do — hold sweet herbs to the nose, burn juniper indoors, clear the filth away — and because epidemics always ended, those actions appeared to work.\n\nThe reasons hold each other up. Observation supplied the belief, ancient authority protected it, and its usefulness kept it in daily use. That is why the Black Death, the greatest test the theory faced, left it stronger rather than weaker: the medical faculty of the University of Paris reported in 1348 that the plague came from air corrupted after a conjunction of Saturn, Jupiter and Mars.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["A belief lasts because it does a job: ask what miasma explained that an ordinary person could check without being taught.","Three developed reasons, one of them from outside the bullets, beat a list of five.","Turn each reason into a mechanism — why it made the theory hard to doubt — and finish by showing the reasons supporting one another."],"codes":["11-1 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Rational explanations","11-1 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Approaches to prevention and treatment and their connection with ideas about disease","11 — The process of change: the key factors"]},{"q":"Explain why there was so little change in the treatment of illness in England in the years c1250–c1500.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Theory of the Four Humours\n• apothecaries\nYou must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit analysis of treatment following unchanged theories of cause and of training by apprenticeship, plus own points such as cost, care at home and no means of testing a remedy. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Treatment could not change in this period because the ideas that produced it did not change, and because nothing in medieval society existed to test whether a treatment worked.\n\nThe first reason is that treatment followed directly from the theory of cause. The Theory of the Four Humours taught that illness was an excess of blood, phlegm, yellow bile or black bile, so a barber surgeon bled patients with a knife or with leeches and an apothecary sold emetics and laxatives to purge them; Galen's Theory of Opposites told the physician to treat a cold, wet illness with hot, dry foods. Miasma produced sweet herbs, juniper burned indoors and street cleaning, and the teaching that God sent disease produced prayer, fasting, penance and pilgrimage to shrines such as Thomas Becket's at Canterbury. As long as the explanations held, the treatments had to.\n\nThe second reason is how healers were trained. Apothecaries and barber surgeons learned by apprenticeship inside a guild, copying a master and working from herbals whose recipes were handed down. Their livelihood depended on doing what was expected, not on innovating, and physicians trained at university were taught to reason from Galen rather than from patients.\n\nThe third reason is one neither bullet gives: most sick people never met a paid healer at all. A physician cost far more than an ordinary family could pay, so the great majority were nursed at home by the women of the household with herbal remedies, and hospitals run by the Church offered rest, food and prayer rather than treatment. Care that is given privately and free leaves no record and attracts no scrutiny, so a remedy that failed was never compared with one that worked.\n\nThe reasons combine: unchanging theory supplied the treatments, apprenticeship reproduced them, and the fact that most treatment happened at home meant nobody was in a position to find them wanting. The result was three centuries of repetition rather than development.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Start from what a treatment is for: it only makes sense if you already believe something about the cause.","Reasons must explain the absence of change, so each paragraph should end by saying what could not happen and why.","Take one reason from each of the two bullets, then add a third about who actually treated ordinary people, and link the three at the end."],"codes":["11-1 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Approaches to prevention and treatment and their connection with ideas about disease","11-1 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Medical training and traditional approaches to treatment and care","11-1 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Rational explanations"]},{"q":"Explain why people in England responded to the Black Death of 1348–49 in the way that they did.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• religious explanations of disease\n• miasma\nYou must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit responses linked to the ideas that produced them — prayer from sin, cleaning and herbs from miasma, bleeding from the humours — plus own points such as astrology and the absence of national machinery. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Every response to the Black Death came out of an explanation people already held, and the patchwork shape of the response came out of the way England was governed.\n\nThe first reason is religious explanation. The Church taught that God sent disease to punish sin, so the response was penitential: bishops ordered processions and prayers, people fasted, confessed and paid for masses, and some went on pilgrimage. The same belief explains what was not done — if the plague was God's will, there was no point looking for another cause, and the flagellants who whipped themselves in public in mainland Europe were following the logic to its end. It also explains a difference between England and the continent: the Jewish communities blamed and attacked in parts of Europe did not exist in England, since Edward I had expelled them in 1290.\n\nThe second reason is miasma. Because bad air from filth and corpses was blamed for carrying plague, people held sweet herbs to their faces, burned juniper indoors, and authorities cleared rubbish. In 1349 Edward III wrote to the Mayor of London ordering the city's streets to be cleaned. Physicians also acted on the Four Humours, bleeding patients and lancing their buboes to let the poison out.\n\nThe third reason lies beyond both bullets: there was no national machinery for public health. England had no health officials, no permanent civil service and no taxation for such a purpose, so prevention was local and improvised. Gloucester tried to shut its gates against people from Bristol, individual towns issued their own orders, and wealthy families simply left for the countryside.\n\nThese causes worked together rather than in turn. The same physician could blame sin, corrupted air and a conjunction of the planets at once — the medical faculty of the University of Paris blamed exactly that combination when it reported on the plague in 1348. People responded as they did because every response available to them was produced by an idea they already believed, and their society contained nothing that could test whether any of it worked.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Work backwards from the actions: every one of them makes sense once you know what people thought was causing the disease.","Group the responses by the idea behind them rather than telling the story of the outbreak in order.","Take one reason from each bullet, add one about how England was governed, and finish by showing the ideas being held together rather than one after another."],"codes":["11-1 — 3 Case study: Dealing with the Black Death, 1348–49","11-1 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Supernatural and religious explanations","11 — The process of change: factors working together"]},{"q":"Explain why the Black Death spread so quickly through England in 1348–49.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• trade and travel\n• living conditions in towns\nYou must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit analysis of the routes trade and travel opened from the arrival at Melcombe Regis in 1348, of crowded and filthy town conditions, and of own points such as ignorance of how plague passed and the absence of any machinery to impose isolation. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The plague crossed England faster than any epidemic before it because the country’s trade carried it, its towns multiplied it, and nothing anyone did could interrupt it.\n\nThe first reason is trade and travel. Plague came ashore from a ship at Melcombe Regis in Dorset in the summer of 1348, a working port trading with Gascony, and then followed the routes merchants and their goods already used. Coastal shipping took it round to Bristol and on to London, which it reached by the autumn of 1348, and the roads, markets and fairs linking towns to their countryside carried it inland, so that most of England had been reached by the summer of 1349. The disease travelled at the speed of commerce because it travelled with commerce.\n\nThe second reason is the condition of the towns. English towns were crowded and filthy: timber houses packed close together, rubbish and animal waste in the streets, cesspits and open ditches, no clean water. Those conditions fed the rats whose fleas carried the disease and put households close enough together for it to pass from one to the next. A town was therefore not only a place the plague reached but a place where it grew.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond both bullets: nobody knew how plague passed from person to person, so nothing done against it worked. The explanations available — God’s punishment for sin, a conjunction of the planets, corrupted air — sent people to prayer, processions and sweet herbs, and a procession gathered a crowd exactly where a crowd was most dangerous. There was no national machinery to order isolation: Gloucester’s attempt to shut its gates against people from Bristol was a local improvisation, and it failed. Those who could afford to flee did so, carrying the plague with them.\n\nThe reasons compound. Trade delivered the disease, town life bred it, and ignorance meant every human response either did nothing or helped it along — which is why the plague covered the country in barely a year and killed between a third and a half of the people of England.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Speed is what has to be explained, so ask what moved the plague from place to place rather than what it did when it arrived.","Three developed reasons work better than a list of five, and at least one must come from outside the two bullets.","Give each reason a mechanism — how it actually carried or multiplied the disease — and close by showing the reasons working together."],"codes":["11-1 — 3 Case study: Dealing with the Black Death, 1348–49","11 — The process of change: medicine and the key features of society","11-1 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Supernatural and religious explanations"]},{"q":"Explain why there was so little government action to improve public health in England in the years c1250–c1500.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Black Death\n• town authorities\nYou must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit attitudes that made health a matter for God, Church and family, the absence of usable knowledge, and own points on the lack of machinery, with 1349 and 1388 as reactive exceptions. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Government did almost nothing about public health in this period because nobody thought it was the king's business, because nobody knew what action would work, and because no machinery existed to carry any policy out.\n\nThe first reason is attitudes. Illness was understood as God's punishment, as an imbalance of the humours or as bad luck, and dealing with it belonged to the Church, the physician and the family. A medieval king's duties were understood as war, justice and taxation. There was no expectation that he would protect his subjects' health, so there was no pressure on him to try.\n\nThe second reason is the state of knowledge. The only theory that pointed towards public action at all was miasma, and it pointed only at dirt and smells. Without any understanding of germs, nobody could have argued for clean water supplies or sewers, so even a willing government would not have known what to build.\n\nThe third reason, beyond the bullets, is that there was no machinery of government to act through. England had no health officials, no public health budget and no permanent civil service; towns were run by mayors and guilds with small incomes, and enforcement depended on local courts. That is why the little that happened was local and reactive: London wards paid rakers to clear the streets and gong farmers to empty cesspits, and Parliament, meeting at Cambridge in 1388, ordered that filth should not be thrown into ditches and rivers near towns.\n\nThe Black Death shows the pattern exactly. Frightened by the plague, Edward III wrote to the Mayor of London in 1349 telling him to have the streets cleaned, and Gloucester tried to close its gates to people from Bristol — but these were single responses to an emergency, not a policy, and nothing permanent followed. Attitudes, ignorance and the absence of machinery reinforced one another, so government action remained occasional, local and short-lived.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask who, in this society, was thought to be responsible for a sick person — and who was not.","There are three separate blocks to explain: nobody wanted to act, nobody knew what to do, and nobody had the means.","Use the case study at the end as the test of your argument rather than as a fourth reason, and make the exceptions prove the rule."],"codes":["11 — The process of change: medicine and the key features of society","11 — The process of change: the key factors","11-1 — 3 Case study: Dealing with the Black Death, 1348–49"]},{"q":"'The Church was the main reason why ideas about the cause of disease changed so little in England in the years c1250–c1500.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• hospitals\n• the work of Galen\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit a criteria-based judgement weighing the Church’s control of universities, texts and teaching against its preservation of learning, and against attitudes to ancient authority and the absence of technology. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The Church was the most powerful institution in medieval England, so any explanation of why medical ideas stood still has to begin with it. But 'main reason' has to be tested against a criterion: which factor, if it had been removed, would have allowed a new explanation of disease to be proposed and accepted? On that test the Church was the heaviest brake on questioning, but not the reason no correct idea appeared.\n\nThe Church's control of learning was decisive. Universities such as Oxford and Cambridge were Church institutions, and the medical course they taught was built on Galen, Hippocrates and Avicenna's Canon of Medicine. Galen suited the Church because he described the body as the work of a single creator, so his anatomy could be taught as an account of God's design; contradicting him therefore came close to contradicting the Church, and heresy was punished. The Church also taught a rival, supernatural explanation from every pulpit — disease as punishment for sin — and it controlled the copying of manuscripts, so what could be read was what it allowed. Hospitals point the same way: institutions such as St Bartholomew's in London, founded in 1123, existed to provide rest, food, warmth and prayer, and were not places where anyone investigated why patients fell ill.\n\nAgainst this, the Church was also the reason any medical learning survived at all. Monks copied the classical texts by hand, the Church founded and paid for the universities that trained physicians, and it funded the hospitals that cared for the sick when nobody else would. It even permitted dissection for teaching in some universities from the fourteenth century. The Church froze medical knowledge, but it was also the refrigerator that kept it in existence.\n\nTwo other factors weigh heavily. The first is attitudes in society. Respect for ancient authority ran far wider than the Church: scholarship in every subject worked by reading and disputing the ancients, and Galen's system was internally consistent and explained every illness, which made it satisfying on its own terms. Even a free-thinking physician had no obvious reason to abandon it. The second is science and technology. Nobody could have found the true cause of disease however free they were to look, because the microscope did not exist and microbes were therefore invisible; and there was no printing press in England until Caxton set one up at Westminster in 1476, so a challenge could not have spread. The Black Death of 1348–49 is the decisive evidence: an epidemic that killed perhaps a third of the population produced no new theory whatever, only God, the planets and corrupted air on a larger scale — and that was not because the Church suppressed a better idea, but because nobody had one.\n\nOn the criterion set out at the start, the judgement is a qualified agreement. The Church was the main reason why medieval ideas were not questioned, since it controlled the training, the texts and the penalties. It was not the main reason why they were not replaced, because the instruments and the means of communication that a replacement would have needed did not yet exist. The factors are therefore not rivals but layers: the Church held the door shut, and technology had not yet made anything worth putting through it.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Before arguing, decide what would count as proof that one factor mattered more than the others.","The strongest essays here treat the Church as two things at once and say which side outweighs the other.","Take the named factor first, weigh at least two others of your own against it, and let the conclusion apply the criterion you set in the opening."],"codes":["11 — The process of change: the key factors","11 — The process of change: medicine and the key features of society","11-1 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Rational explanations"]},{"q":"'There was no real progress in the prevention and treatment of disease in England in the years c1250–c1500.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• bloodletting\n• purifying the air\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit a criteria-based judgement setting the absence of any improvement in cure against developments in surgery, hospital provision and town cleanliness, distinguishing curing from caring. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Whether there was progress depends on what progress is measured by. Two criteria are needed here: whether a sick person in 1500 was more likely to be cured than one in 1250, and whether the care and organisation surrounding the sick improved. On the first the statement is right; on the second it is wrong.\n\nOn cure, the evidence for the statement is strong. Treatment followed from an explanation of cause that never changed, so it could not change either. Because the Theory of the Four Humours taught that illness was an excess of blood, phlegm, yellow bile or black bile, barber surgeons bled patients with knives or leeches and apothecaries sold emetics and laxatives to purge them, and Galen's Theory of Opposites governed the diet prescribed. Bloodletting did not merely fail; it weakened people who were already ill. Purifying the air followed from miasma in the same way — sweet herbs held to the nose, juniper burned indoors, filth cleared from the streets — and could not touch a cause that nobody had identified. The Black Death of 1348–49 is the test case: physicians bled patients and lanced buboes, priests led processions, and between a third and a half of the population of England died anyway.\n\nAgainst the statement, several things did move. Surgery improved: John of Arderne's Practica of 1376 described an operation for anal fistula and a painkilling mixture of hemlock, henbane and opium, and surgeons in London organised themselves into a guild in the fourteenth century, which raised standards and status. Care expanded: hundreds of hospitals, most of them tiny, were founded across England in these centuries, and although they offered rest, food, warmth and prayer rather than cure, that was more than the sick had had before. Public health became an object of regulation for the first time: London wards paid rakers to clear the streets and gong farmers to empty cesspits, and Parliament, meeting at Cambridge in 1388, ordered that filth should not be thrown into the ditches and rivers near towns. Some traditional remedies genuinely worked, even if not for the reasons given — honey on a wound and wine used to wash it do inhibit infection.\n\nWeighing these against each other decides the question rather than balancing it. Progress in curing requires a new theory of cause, and no such theory appeared: the arrival of the greatest epidemic in English history produced no new explanation at all, which is the clearest possible proof that the intellectual framework was closed. Progress in caring requires only organisation, money and skill, and all three grew: hospitals multiplied, surgeons organised, and towns legislated.\n\nThe judgement, therefore, is that the statement is half true and its wording too absolute. There was no real progress in the treatment of disease, because the ideas that generated treatment were frozen; there was real, if modest, progress in the care of the sick and in the cleanliness of towns, because those did not depend on understanding cause. The distinction between curing and caring is the criterion that makes sense of the period, and it is the distinction that later periods close.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["The word doing the work in this statement is 'real' — decide what would count as real before you agree or disagree.","Two different measures of progress point in opposite directions here; find them both before planning paragraphs.","Argue one measure, then the other, then say which matters more and why, rather than listing successes and failures in turn."],"codes":["11-1 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Approaches to prevention and treatment and their connection with ideas about disease","11-1 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Medical training and traditional approaches to treatment and care","11-1 — 3 Case study: Dealing with the Black Death, 1348–49"]},{"q":"'Medieval people in England had no rational ideas about the cause of disease in the years c1250–c1500.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• God's punishment for sin\n• miasma\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit a criteria-based judgement weighing the religious and astrological explanations current across the period against the humoral and miasmatic ones, defining rational as reasoning from accepted evidence rather than as being correct. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The statement rests on a confusion between being rational and being right. If rational means 'correct', then almost nothing believed in medieval England qualifies. If it means what historians mean by it — explanations reached by reasoning from observation rather than from the supernatural — then medieval ideas about the cause of disease contained a great deal that was rational, and the statement is largely wrong.\n\nThere is a real case for the statement. The dominant public explanation throughout the period was religious: God sent disease to punish sin. When the Black Death struck in 1348–49 bishops ordered processions and prayers, people fasted, confessed and paid for masses, and on the continent flagellants whipped themselves through the streets, a movement that won very little following in England. Astrology carried enormous weight: in 1348 the medical faculty of the University of Paris reported that the plague had been caused by a conjunction of Saturn, Jupiter and Mars, and after the epidemic English physicians routinely consulted zodiac charts before treating patients. None of this could have helped anyone, and much of it wasted the effort of a terrified population.\n\nBut the same people simultaneously used explanations that were not supernatural at all. Miasma — the belief that disease was carried by bad air from filth, corpses and stagnant water — was a theory built on an observation anyone could make, that disease was worst in the dirtiest and most crowded places. From it followed actions with a clear logic: clearing rubbish from the streets, burning juniper and other scented woods, and Edward III's order to the Mayor of London in 1349 to have the city cleaned. The Four Humours produced bleeding and the lancing of buboes, wrong but reasoned from an accepted theory of the body. Above all, people acted on the observation that the plague passed from person to person and place to place: Gloucester tried to close its gates against people from Bristol, and those who could afford to left the towns. Fleeing actually worked, and the instinct to separate the sick from the healthy would later become quarantine.\n\nA further point weighs against the statement. Religious and rational explanations were not rivals in the medieval mind; they were layers of the same account, with God as the ultimate cause and corrupted air or unbalanced humours as the means. The Paris report blamed the planets and corrupted air together. Judging one layer as proof that the other did not exist misreads how medieval people thought.\n\nThe criterion for judgement should therefore be whether the explanations medieval people used were reasoned from the evidence available at the time, not whether they succeeded. On that criterion the statement is wrong: medieval England possessed rational explanations of disease, inherited from Hippocrates and Galen, and applied them as vigorously as it applied prayer. What it lacked was not rationality but the means of testing it — no microscope, no printing press in England before 1476, and no institution whose business was to check whether a treatment worked. Medieval England was a society reasoning hard inside the wrong framework, which is not at all the same as a society that did not reason.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["The whole essay turns on how you define one word in the statement — find it in the first line.","Look for the responses that were not religious at all, and for what people did rather than only what they said.","Define the term, give the case for the statement, give the case against, and let the conclusion rest on the definition you chose."],"codes":["11-1 — 3 Case study: Dealing with the Black Death, 1348–49","11-1 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Supernatural and religious explanations","11-1 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Rational explanations"]},{"q":"'Medicine in England changed more in the years c1500–c1700 than in the years c1250–c1500.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the printing press\n• the Theory of the Four Humours\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit a criteria-based judgement separating change in knowledge and method — Vesalius, Harvey, printing, the Royal Society — from continuity in what a sick person actually received. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"This comparison needs a criterion, because the two periods changed in different places. Change can be measured in knowledge and method, or in what actually happened to a sick person. On the first measure the statement is clearly right; on the second it is barely true at all.\n\nMeasured by knowledge and method, the later period changed far more. Printing was the engine. There was no press in England until Caxton set one up at Westminster in 1476, so medieval knowledge moved through manuscripts copied by hand, slowly and expensively. Printed, illustrated books meant that when Andreas Vesalius published On the Fabric of the Human Body in 1543, correcting Galen from his own dissections at Padua on points such as the human jawbone and the breastbone, the correction could be read across Europe in identical copies. William Harvey demonstrated the circulation of the blood in his book of 1628, overturning Galen's account of how blood was made and consumed. The Royal Society, founded in 1660 and given its charter by Charles II in 1662, published experiments in its journal from 1665, and Thomas Sydenham insisted on observing and classifying diseases at the bedside. Against this, the years c1250–c1500 produced no new theory of disease whatever: the Black Death of 1348–49, the greatest shock of the age, was explained with the ideas already in the books.\n\nMeasured by what a sick person received, the picture reverses into similarity. The Theory of the Four Humours was still taught and still believed in 1700, and bloodletting and purging were still the standard treatments — Harvey's discovery of circulation gave doctors no new cure and was still being disputed decades later. Miasma still explained epidemics, which is why the response to the Great Plague of London in 1665 — fires in the streets, posies, herbs, shutting up houses — resembles the response of 1348–49 so closely. Most people in both periods were treated at home by the women of their household with herbal remedies, because physicians remained expensive and few.\n\nThere is also change in the earlier period that the statement hides. Surgery advanced with John of Arderne's Practica of 1376 and his painkilling mixture of hemlock, henbane and opium; hundreds of small hospitals were founded; and Parliament at Cambridge ordered in 1388 that filth should not be thrown into ditches and rivers. These are real, if modest, developments in care and public order.\n\nThe judgement depends on holding the two measures apart. On knowledge and method the statement is right by a wide margin: c1500–c1700 broke the authority of Galen, created new institutions and new means of communication, and made medicine something that could be corrected. On treatment and everyday experience the statement is wrong, because a patient in 1700 was bled and purged much as a patient in 1350 had been. The later period therefore changed the foundations without yet changing the building, and it is the foundations that made everything after 1800 possible.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Comparing two periods needs a measuring stick that fits both; decide what you are measuring before you choose evidence.","Ask separately what doctors knew and what patients actually got — the two answers here do not agree.","Argue the later period's case, then test it against continuity in treatment, then rescue the earlier period's modest changes before judging."],"codes":["11 — The process of change: the key factors","11 — The process of change: the role of the strand 3 case studies","11-1 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Approaches to prevention and treatment and their connection with ideas about disease"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Which four humours had to be in balance for a medieval person to be healthy?","a":"Blood, phlegm, yellow bile and black bile."},{"q":"What did a medieval physician mean by miasma?","a":"Bad air given off by rotting rubbish, corpses and cesspits, which was believed to carry disease to anyone who breathed it."},{"q":"At which Dorset port did the Black Death reach England in the summer of 1348?","a":"Melcombe Regis, near Weymouth."},{"q":"Which medical worker carried out bloodletting, pulled teeth and performed simple surgery for ordinary people?","a":"The barber surgeon."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The Theory of the Four Humours","back":"Hippocrates' idea, developed by Galen: health depends on a balance of blood, phlegm, yellow bile and black bile. Too much or too little of one was thought to cause illness.","code":"11-1 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Rational explanations","open":false},{"front":"Miasma","back":"Bad air believed to rise from rotting rubbish, corpses and cesspits. Breathing it in was thought to cause disease, so people burned sweet herbs and towns cleared filth from the streets.","code":"11-1 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Rational explanations","open":false},{"front":"Galen","back":"Greek physician working in the Roman empire in the second century AD, c.129–c.216. His books were the medieval medical course, and the Church approved of him because he wrote of a single creator.","code":"11-1 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Rational explanations","open":false},{"front":"The Theory of Opposites","back":"Galen's addition to the Four Humours: an excess of one humour is cured by its opposite, so an illness blamed on cold, wet phlegm was treated with hot, dry foods such as pepper.","code":"11-1 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Approaches to prevention and treatment and their connection with ideas about disease","open":false},{"front":"Regimen sanitatis","back":"The 'regime of health' a physician prescribed: moderate food and drink, exercise, sleep, bathing and avoiding bad air, all intended to keep the four humours in balance.","code":"11-1 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Approaches to prevention and treatment and their connection with ideas about disease","open":false},{"front":"Bloodletting and purging","back":"Treatments drawn straight from the Four Humours: blood removed by knife, cup or leech, and the bowels or stomach emptied with laxatives and emetics, to restore the balance of humours.","code":"11-1 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Approaches to prevention and treatment and their connection with ideas about disease","open":false},{"front":"The physician","back":"University-trained doctor, seven or more years reading Galen, Hippocrates and Avicenna. Diagnosed by inspecting urine, taking the pulse and consulting zodiac charts. Too few and too expensive for ordinary people.","code":"11-1 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Medical training and traditional approaches to treatment and care","open":false},{"front":"The apothecary","back":"Mixed and sold herbal remedies, trained by apprenticeship and organised in a guild rather than a university. Cheaper than a physician, and often the first healer an ordinary family paid.","code":"11-1 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Medical training and traditional approaches to treatment and care","open":false},{"front":"The barber surgeon","back":"No university training and low status: carried out bloodletting, pulled teeth, lanced boils and amputated limbs as well as cutting hair. The surgeon most people actually saw.","code":"11-1 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Medical training and traditional approaches to treatment and care","open":false},{"front":"Medieval hospitals","back":"Mostly small and run by the Church and monasteries, such as St Bartholomew's in London, founded in 1123. They gave rest, food, warmth and prayer — care rather than cure — and often turned away the infectious.","code":"11-1 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Medical training and traditional approaches to treatment and care","open":false},{"front":"John of Arderne","back":"English surgeon who wrote Practica in 1376, describing an operation for anal fistula and a painkilling mixture of hemlock, henbane and opium. Evidence that surgical skill did advance.","code":"11-1 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Medical training and traditional approaches to treatment and care","open":false},{"front":"The Black Death, 1348–49","back":"Plague that reached England through the Dorset port of Melcombe Regis in summer 1348 and London by autumn. Historians estimate a third to a half of the population died.","code":"11-1 — 3 Case study: Dealing with the Black Death, 1348–49","open":false},{"front":"Edward III's order of 1349","back":"The king told the Mayor of London to have the city's filthy streets cleaned, because bad air was blamed for the plague — rare, reactive royal action on public health.","code":"11-1 — 3 Case study: Dealing with the Black Death, 1348–49","open":false},{"front":"Astrology in medieval diagnosis","back":"After the Black Death, physicians consulted zodiac charts before treating a patient, checking the position of the planets, because the stars were thought to influence the body and the right time to bleed.","code":"11-1 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Supernatural and religious explanations","open":false},{"front":"One religious action a medieval family might take when someone fell ill","back":"Praying and paying for masses to be said; going on pilgrimage to a shrine such as Thomas Becket's at Canterbury; fasting or doing penance to show repentance for the sin believed to have caused the illness.","code":"11-1 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Supernatural and religious explanations","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 1 Question 5, 16 marks: 'The women of the household did more than any trained healer to keep the sick alive in England in the years c1250–c1500.')","items":["The women of the household did more than any trained healer to keep the sick alive, because they supplied the only treatment that reached most of the population and because what they supplied was the part of medieval care that actually helped. A physician had spent seven years or more at a university reading Galen, Hippocrates and Avicenna's Canon of Medicine, and his fees put him beyond everyone but the rich; the profession also worked to keep the field to itself, for in 1421 a group of physicians petitioned Parliament to forbid anyone without a university degree to practise physic, and asked in the same petition that no woman practise it at all. Women were barred from the universities, so they could never qualify as physicians, yet it was women who grew and mixed the herbal remedies, kept the recipes, nursed the feverish, delivered the babies and fed and warmed the dying. Judged by what happened to the patient this mattered, because rest, food, warmth and a clean dressing are the things that let a body recover, whereas a physician's uroscopy and zodiac chart changed nothing and a barber surgeon's bleeding left a weak patient weaker. It can be argued against this that trained healers did what no household could: a barber surgeon set a broken bone, lanced a bubo or amputated a limb, an apothecary compounded drugs a kitchen could not make, and in 1403 the London surgeon John Bradmore drew an arrowhead from the face of the future Henry V and dressed the wound with wine and honey. That is true, and it is why the claim cannot be made absolutely. But on the criterion of how many of the sick were reached rather than how difficult the treatment was, the woman at the bedside reached almost everyone and the trained healer reached almost nobody, so the statement stands.","Why this reaches Level 4: the explanation is directed consistently at the conceptual focus — every sentence is about who kept sick people alive, not about what each kind of healer did; the information is precisely selected (the length and content of a physician's training, the 1421 petition to Parliament, women's exclusion from the universities, Bradmore and the arrowhead of 1403) rather than general; the counter-view is stated in its strongest form and then answered rather than dropped in; and the criterion for the judgement — how many of the sick were reached, not how skilled the treatment was — is named and applied in the closing sentence, which is what the third bullet of the Level 4 descriptor rewards."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Women did more than trained healers to keep sick people alive in the Middle Ages. Most people were poor and could not afford a doctor, so they stayed at home and their mother or their wife looked after them, gave them herbal remedies, made sure they ate and kept them warm. Women were not allowed to go to university so they could never become physicians, but they still knew a lot about herbs because it was passed down to them. Barber surgeons and apothecaries did help people as well and they could do things like pulling teeth and selling medicines, so trained healers were not useless. This shows that women were the most important healers in medieval England, even though they had no training at all.","Why this stays at Level 2: it meets 'an explanation is given showing limited analysis and with implicit or unsustained links to the conceptual focus of the question' and 'accurate and relevant information is included, showing some knowledge and understanding of the period', and the overall judgement is given but asserted. It misses Level 3 and Level 4 because nothing is dated and no person, text or institution is named — the healers appear only as job titles; because the counter-point about barber surgeons and apothecaries is tacked on at the end instead of being weighed; and because no criterion for the judgement is stated, so 'did more' is claimed rather than tested."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Case study: dealing with the Black Death, 1348–49","items":["Arrival: the plague reached England through the Dorset port of Melcombe Regis, near Weymouth, in the summer of 1348.","Spread: it had reached London by the autumn of 1348 and moved through the country during 1349.","Mortality: historians estimate that between a third and a half of the population of England died.","Symptoms contemporaries described: painful swellings (buboes) in the armpit, neck and groin, fever, blotches on the skin, and in some victims coughing of blood.","Religious explanation: God had sent the plague to punish human sin; bishops ordered processions and prayers, and people fasted, confessed and paid for masses.","Astrological explanation: in 1348 the medical faculty of the University of Paris reported that the plague had been caused by a conjunction of the planets Saturn, Jupiter and Mars.","Rational explanation: miasma — corrupted air from filth, stagnant water and unburied corpses.","Treatments used: prayer and penance; bleeding and purging to rebalance the humours; lancing or bursting the buboes; herbal remedies and theriac; holding sweet herbs to the nose.","Flagellants whipped themselves in public in mainland Europe as a form of penance; the movement had very little following in England.","Blame in parts of Europe fell on Jewish communities; this could not happen in England because Edward I had expelled the Jews in 1290.","Prevention attempts: Gloucester tried to shut its gates against people from Bristol; wealthy families left the towns for the countryside; some towns issued orders about filth and about strangers.","Royal action: in 1349 Edward III wrote to the Mayor of London ordering the city's streets to be cleaned of filth.","What was absent: any national quarantine system, any cure, any new theory of disease, and any idea of the true cause.","Comparison points for the thematic study: the Great Plague of London in 1665 (shutting up houses, street fires, herbs) and the cholera outbreak of 1854 investigated by John Snow at the Broad Street pump."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — The Theory of the Four Humours and the miasma theory","items":["Origin: the Four Humours comes from Hippocrates, the Greek physician of about 460–370 BC, and the collection of writings associated with him.","The four humours: blood, phlegm, yellow bile and black bile; health meant they were in balance, illness meant an excess or shortage of one.","The humours were linked to the seasons and to the qualities hot, cold, wet and dry, which is why a cold, wet illness was blamed on phlegm.","Diagnosis from the theory: inspecting urine in a flask for colour, thickness and smell, and taking the pulse.","Treatments from the theory: bloodletting by knife, cupping glass or leech; purging with laxatives and emetics; and diet adjusted by the Theory of Opposites.","Miasma: the belief that bad air given off by rotting matter, cesspits, stagnant water and corpses carried disease into the body.","Miasma was built on a real observation — that disease was worst in the dirtiest and most crowded places — and it survived in Britain until the late nineteenth century.","Prevention from miasma: sweet herbs and posies held to the nose, juniper and other scented woods burned indoors, rubbish cleared from streets, cesspits emptied by gong farmers.","Both theories are rational, meaning non-supernatural: they reason from observation to a cause, which is why they count as the 'rational explanations' strand of the specification.","Neither theory was replaced during c1250–c1500; their survival is the strongest single piece of evidence for continuity in the period."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — The continuing influence in England of Galen","items":["Galen was a Greek physician who worked in the Roman empire in the second century AD; his dates are usually given as c.129–c.216.","He based much of his anatomy on the dissection of animals, because human dissection was largely forbidden in his lifetime, which is the source of several of his errors.","He added the Theory of Opposites to the Four Humours: treat an excess of one humour with its opposite quality.","His works reached medieval Europe partly through Islamic scholars, alongside Avicenna's Canon of Medicine, completed in about 1025.","Galen's books, with Hippocrates and Avicenna, formed the medical course at universities such as Oxford, Cambridge, Bologna, Padua and Montpellier.","The Church approved of Galen because he wrote of the body as the work of a single creator, which fitted Christian teaching; disagreeing with him therefore risked a charge of heresy.","Medieval training worked by reading and disputing his texts; where dissection was permitted, it was used to demonstrate what Galen had written rather than to test it.","His authority was first seriously broken by Andreas Vesalius, whose On the Fabric of the Human Body (1543) corrected errors such as Galen's account of the human jawbone and breastbone.","William Harvey's demonstration of the circulation of the blood, published in 1628, overturned Galen's account of how blood was made and used up.","For the thematic study, Galen is the clearest example of the factor 'individuals and institutions' acting as a brake as well as a driver of change."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Medical training, treatment and care, c1250–c1500","items":["Physician: trained for seven years or more at a university; diagnosed by uroscopy, the pulse and zodiac charts; very expensive and very few in number.","Apothecary: trained by apprenticeship and regulated by a guild; mixed and sold herbal remedies; cheaper and far more accessible than a physician.","Barber surgeon: no university training and low status; performed bloodletting, tooth-pulling, boil-lancing and amputation as well as cutting hair.","Surgeons in London organised themselves into a guild during the fourteenth century, which began the separation of surgery from barbering.","John of Arderne wrote Practica in 1376, setting out a treatment for anal fistula and a painkilling mixture of hemlock, henbane and opium.","Wine was used to wash wounds and honey was applied to them; both do in fact inhibit infection, though not for the reasons given at the time.","Hospitals were usually small and run by the Church and monasteries; St Bartholomew's in London was founded in 1123.","Hospital care meant rest, warmth, food, clean bedding and prayer; many hospitals refused the infectious, the dying and pregnant women.","Leper houses stood outside town walls and kept lepers separate from the community, an early form of isolation.","Most sick people were nursed at home by the women of the household, using herbal remedies passed down and recorded in herbals.","Public health in towns: rakers were paid to clear the streets, gong farmers emptied cesspits, and butchers' waste was regulated.","Parliament, meeting at Cambridge in 1388, ordered that filth should not be thrown into ditches and rivers near towns — one of the earliest English laws on cleanliness."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 1 Question 5 (16 marks + 4 SPaG): 'The Theory of the Four Humours did more harm than good to the sick in England in the years c1250–c1500.' How far do you agree? Stimulus points: purging; the regimen sanitatis.","items":["Decoded question: the concept being tested is consequence — what one idea did to the people it was applied to. 'More harm than good' is a balance, so the answer must set the two sides against each other, and must deal both with what the theory made healers do and with what it stopped them finding out.","Criterion to set in the opening: harm and good will be judged twice — by what happened to the individual patient, and by what the theory did to medical knowledge across the period. Signal at once that the two measures point the same way for treatment and different ways for advice.","Paragraph 1 (agree, on treatment): the theory turned every illness into an excess to be removed, so the standard treatments drained the patient. Evidence: purging with emetics and laxatives; bloodletting by knife, cup and leech; Galen's Theory of Opposites deciding which food and drink were prescribed. Interim judgement: a weakened patient is a harmed patient, whatever the healer intended.","Paragraph 2 (agree, on knowledge): because the theory explained every outcome, nothing could disprove it. Evidence: a recovery proved the balance restored and a death proved the imbalance too far gone; the university course was built on Galen, Hippocrates and Avicenna; dissection, where it was allowed, demonstrated Galen rather than testing him. Interim judgement: the deepest harm was that the question was closed.","Paragraph 3 (disagree — turning the second stimulus point against the statement): the regimen sanitatis came from the same theory and asked nothing of the patient that could hurt them. Evidence: physicians prescribed moderation in air, food and drink, sleep, exercise, evacuation and the emotions. Interim judgement: advice that improved diet, rest and cleanliness did real good.","Paragraph 4 (disagree — the point beyond both stimulus points): a wrong system is still a system, and a system makes observation worth recording. Evidence: physicians examined urine in a flask and took the pulse because the humours gave them something to look for; apothecaries classified herbs as hot, cold, wet or dry so a remedy could be matched to a patient. Interim judgement: the framework was false, but it kept healers looking at patients.","Evaluation thread running through all four paragraphs: separate what the theory did to one sick person from what it did to English medicine as a whole, and say which of the two the sentence at issue is really about. Return to that distinction at the end of every paragraph.","Conclusion: agree that the theory harmed the patient, because its treatments removed what a sick body needed, and agree more strongly that it harmed medicine, because it could not be tested; concede that its advice on daily life was sound and that it kept observation alive. Justify the split by naming the criterion again rather than repeating the evidence.","Timing on the 80-minute paper: about 25 minutes for this question — roughly 4 minutes planning the criterion and the four paragraphs, 18 writing and 3 checking spelling and specialist terms. With about 5 minutes on Question 3 and 15 on Question 4, Section B takes 45 minutes in all and leaves about 35 for Section A, and this is the only Section B question where spelling, punctuation, grammar and terminology carry their own 4 marks.","Specialist terms to use accurately for the SPaG marks: humours, phlegm, yellow bile, black bile, Theory of Opposites, purging, emetic, regimen sanitatis, uroscopy, continuity."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T09:07:44Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.95,"notes":"All five recorded fixes are in the kit and check out. Every 12- and 16-mark item now prints Pearson's current cap in full (maximum 8 marks and no access to Level 4 on questions 5 to 9; maximum 11 and no access to Level 4 on questions 10 to 13) rather than 'Stimulus caps apply.'; room.questions[2] and [8] are genuinely new questions, and no bank stem now reproduces the Paper 1 specimen Option 11 paper (Q3 diagnosis medieval against modern, Q4 the role of the Church c1250–c1700, Q5 John Snow, Q6 government action on hospitals) or the June 2023 Option 11 paper (Q3 prevention medieval against modern, Q4 access to care in the modern period, Q5 Thomas Sydenham, Q6 attitudes c1700–c1900); room.questions[12] now covers c1250–c1500 with 1348–49 as its test case; room.facts[4] and extras[6] item 9 give the same arithmetic (5 + 15 + 25 = 45 of 80, leaving about 35 for Section A) and the incompatible 'roughly a minute a mark' is gone; and room.questions[5] has moved onto miasma, so it no longer rehearses room.questions[10]. I re-solved all fourteen questions and found no wrong answer key, and the new facts verify: the physicians' petition to Parliament of 1421, which asked that practice be confined to university graduates and that no woman practise physic and which was never implemented; the Medical Act of 1858, which created the General Medical Council and its register of qualified practitioners; the arrival at Melcombe Regis from Gascony about the feast of St John the Baptist in the summer of 1348, London by the autumn of 1348 and the whole country by the summer of 1349; and, behind the new miasma question, Hippocrates on airs, waters and places, Galen on corrupted air, and the Paris faculty's report of 1348. The provenance¹ convention, AO splits (Question 3 AO2 2 + AO1 2, Question 4 AO2 6 + AO1 6, Question 5/6 AO2 10 + AO1 6 with 4 separate SPaG marks) and the levels.md grids all still match, and dupscan prints 'no duplication' — publish it, and add the spread of the plague to the Black Death fact file so room.questions[8] is answerable from the room."}}},"11-2":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"11-2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This is the room examiners reach for whenever Paper 1 Section B wants change rather than continuity. The Medical Renaissance is where the ancient authorities are finally tested and found wrong, and yet almost nothing happens to the patient — which makes it the single richest source of the two-sided judgement a Question 5 or 6 rewards. Section B carries 36 of the paper's 52 marks, and c1500–c1700 supplies the 'after' half of most comparisons with the medieval period and the 'before' half of most comparisons with the nineteenth century. If you can say precisely what Vesalius, Harvey and Sydenham changed, and precisely what they did not change, every question in this section becomes answerable.","idea":[{"h":"Explanations of cause: the framework holds, the method changes","t":"Nothing replaced the Theory of the Four Humours between c1500 and c1700. Physicians still blamed illness on an excess of blood, phlegm, yellow bile or black bile; miasma, the belief that bad air rising from filth carried disease, was still the everyday explanation, and in 1665 it governed what London did about plague. Religion had not gone either: Charles II still touched sufferers of scrofula, 'the King's Evil'. What did change was how a doctor might arrive at an opinion. Thomas Sydenham (1624–1689), working in London and publishing Observationes Medicae in 1676, insisted on watching the patient rather than trusting the book, recorded symptoms carefully and argued that diseases have distinct identities that can be classified — he separated scarlet fever from other fevers and named it. Alongside him, the means of carrying ideas were transformed: printing, brought to England by Caxton at Westminster in 1476, reproduced texts and illustrations exactly, and the Royal Society, founded in 1660, chartered by Charles II in 1662 and publishing its Philosophical Transactions from 1665, made experiment public and respectable. A top-level answer never says 'ideas changed' or 'ideas stayed the same'; it separates the explanation of cause, which barely moved, from the method of finding out and the means of spreading what was found, which moved a great deal.","code":"11-2 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Continuity and change in explanations of the cause of disease"},{"h":"Prevention, treatment and care: new ingredients in an old system","t":"Treatment follows the theory of cause, so while the humours survived, bloodletting, purging and the regimen of diet, exercise and sleep survived with them, and most sick people were still nursed at home with herbal remedies. What arrived was new material inside the old framework: remedies from the New World such as guaiacum against syphilis, sarsaparilla, cinchona or Peruvian bark against the fevers of malaria, and tobacco; chemical cures using metals such as mercury and antimony; and transference, the seventeenth-century idea that a disease could be passed out of the body into an object or an animal rubbed against it. Regulation tightened — the Royal College of Physicians was founded in 1518, the Company of Barber-Surgeons in 1540 and the Society of Apothecaries received its charter in 1617 — and in 1618 the Royal College published the London Pharmacopoeia, so that a named remedy meant the same thing across the city; Nicholas Culpeper published an English translation in the 1640s. Care changed most of all: the dissolution of the monasteries between 1536 and 1540 closed most hospitals, but some were refounded by charity and the City of London, St Bartholomew's in 1546 and St Thomas's in 1551, and by the later seventeenth century a hospital was a place where physicians and surgeons treated the sick rather than a shelter offering rest and prayer; pest houses were built to isolate the infectious. A top-level answer uses this strand to make the distinction that wins marks: curing needs a new idea of cause and did not improve, while caring needs money, organisation and skill, and did.","code":"11-2 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Continuity and change in approaches to prevention, treatment and care"},{"h":"Training and Vesalius: the body becomes the evidence","t":"Andreas Vesalius (1514–1564) was professor of surgery and anatomy at Padua, and he did the cutting himself instead of leaving it to an assistant while a text was read aloud. On the Fabric of the Human Body, published in 1543, was built on his own dissections of human corpses and corrected Galen in detail: the human jawbone is a single bone, not two; the breastbone has three parts, not seven; and he could not find the pores Galen said ran through the wall between the two sides of the heart. His conclusion was that Galen had dissected animals. The book's illustrations were exact, and printing meant they arrived intact wherever it went. In England the effects were institutional as much as intellectual: the Company of Barber-Surgeons, created in 1540, was granted the bodies of executed criminals for public dissection; John Caius, who studied at Padua while Vesalius taught there, became President of the Royal College of Physicians and refounded Gonville and Caius College, Cambridge, in 1557; and anatomy began to be taught from a body. The limits matter just as much: universities still set Galen's texts, training still took years of reading, and knowing the shape of the body cured nobody. A top-level answer treats Vesalius as a change in what counts as proof — the body rather than the text — and then measures how far English training actually followed.","code":"11-2 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Improvements in medical training and the influence of Vesalius"},{"h":"Harvey: the discovery that changed knowledge and not treatment","t":"William Harvey (1578–1657) trained at Cambridge and then at Padua, became a physician at St Bartholomew's Hospital in 1609 and served as a royal physician to James I and Charles I. In An Anatomical Account of the Motion of the Heart and Blood, published in 1628, he argued that the heart is a muscular pump and that one quantity of blood circulates continuously round a closed system of arteries and veins. His evidence was experimental: he dissected cold-blooded animals whose hearts beat slowly enough to be watched; he calculated that the heart pushes out far more blood than the body could possibly manufacture from food and burn away; and he pressed on the veins of the arm to show that their valves let blood move only towards the heart. This destroyed Galen's physiology, in which blood was made in the liver and consumed as fuel. What he could not do was show the tiny vessels joining arteries to veins; the capillaries were first seen through a microscope by Marcello Malpighi in 1661, four years after Harvey's death. Many physicians rejected the theory because it contradicted Galen, no new treatment followed from it, and bloodletting went on exactly as before. A top-level answer uses Harvey to prove the room's central paradox, and says plainly on which measure — knowledge or treatment — it is judging him.","code":"11-2 — 3 Case studies: Key individual: William Harvey"},{"h":"The Great Plague of 1665: better organised, no better informed","t":"Plague deaths in London rose through the summer of 1665 and fell away over the following winter. The Bills of Mortality recorded about 68,000 plague deaths; historians usually put the real total near 100,000, almost a quarter of the city. The explanations offered were the familiar ones — bad air, God's punishment for sin, an unusual alignment of the planets, an imbalance of the humours — and the treatments followed them: bleeding and purging, herbal remedies and plague water, lancing the buboes, quack cures, and transference such as strapping a live chicken to the swellings. Prevention, though, was organised in a way 1348–49 never was. Infected houses were shut up for forty days with a red cross and the words 'Lord have mercy upon us' painted on the door and a watchman outside; searchers of the dead were paid to inspect bodies and report the cause of death; theatres and fairs were closed and public gatherings banned; stray dogs and cats were killed; bonfires were lit in the streets to cleanse the air; the dead were carted away at night to pits outside the city; and those who could afford it left, including Charles II and his court, who left London in July, moved to Salisbury in August and on to Oxford in September when plague reached Salisbury too. The true cause, the bacterium carried by fleas living on black rats, was unknown, so killing the cats may have made things worse. A top-level answer uses 1665 as the test case that separates administration from understanding, and says which of the two had actually improved.","code":"11-2 — 3 Case studies: Dealing with the Great Plague in London (1665)"}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 3, 4 marks: proving a similarity in who actually looked after the sick","setup":"Paper 1 Question 3, worth 4 marks: 'Explain one way in which the care a sick person received at home in the years c1250–c1500 was similar to the care received at home in the years c1500–c1700.' The mark scheme gives 2 marks for AO2, analysing the similarity, and 2 marks for AO1, specific information about both periods. Only one similarity is wanted, and it must be evidenced on both sides.","steps":["Pick the feature of care that outlasts every discovery of the period: in both halves of the span the great majority of sick people were nursed where they lived, by the women of the household, on herbal remedies made up at home.","Evidence the earlier side: a university-trained physician was rare and costly, so a family fell back on the woman of the house, on local wise women and on the herbs named in a hand-copied herbal, while monastic hospitals and almshouses took in the destitute rather than the ordinary sick.","Evidence the later side: the pattern held after 1600, and Nicholas Culpeper aimed straight at it — he put the College of Physicians' Latin Pharmacopoeia of 1618 into English in 1649 and followed it with The English Physician in 1652, books written for households that had no intention of paying a physician.","Analyse rather than list: the similarity is one of cost and access rather than of knowledge, so the Medical Renaissance changed what the learned few argued about while the person who actually handled a fever went on being an unpaid woman in the same house."],"cues":["Who actually looked after most sick people, in both halves of the span?","Why could an ordinary medieval family not simply send for a physician?","Which seventeenth-century books show householders still treating their own?","What kind of similarity is this — one of knowledge, or of something else?"]},{"title":"Question 3, 4 marks: a difference in the medicine bottle, not in the theory","setup":"Paper 1 Question 3, worth 4 marks: 'Explain one way in which the ingredients used in remedies in England in the years c1250–c1500 were different from the ingredients used in the years c1500–c1700.' Two marks are for AO2, the analysed difference, and two for AO1, specific information from both periods.","steps":["Fix the difference on supply rather than on belief: before 1500 a remedy was built from plants that could be grown, gathered or bought in England, with a handful of costly spices carried in by Mediterranean traders.","Evidence the later side from across the Atlantic: guaiacum, a hard resinous wood from the Caribbean, was shipped to Europe and sold against syphilis from the early sixteenth century, sarsaparilla and tobacco followed, and cinchona, the Peruvian bark, was listed in the London Pharmacopoeia in 1677 as the one genuinely effective new drug, used against the fevers of malaria.","Add the second strand of the change: minerals and metals came into the medicine chest from the alchemical tradition, mercury and antimony among them, and the Royal College of Physicians' Pharmacopoeia Londinensis of 1618 fixed standard recipes so that a named remedy meant the same thing across the city.","Analyse the difference: what had changed was the contents of the bottle and the reliability of its recipe, not the reason for reaching for it, because every one of these substances was still given to rebalance the humours — a difference in ingredients sitting inside an unchanged theory of cause."],"cues":["Where did the ingredients of an English remedy come from in the earlier period?","Which substances arrive from the New World, and which of them actually worked?","What besides plants goes into a seventeenth-century remedy, and who standardised the recipes?","Has the reason for giving the remedy changed along with its contents?"]},{"title":"Question 4, 12 marks: turning two named bullets into mechanisms","setup":"Paper 1 Question 4, worth 12 marks: 'Explain why the diagnosis of disease improved in England in the years c1500–c1700.' You may use the following in your answer: Thomas Sydenham; the Royal Society. You must also use information of your own. The mark scheme gives 6 marks for AO2 and 6 for AO1, and sets a maximum of 8 marks, with no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","steps":["Turn the first bullet into a mechanism rather than a name: Sydenham examined the patient in front of him and recorded the symptoms instead of matching the case to what a book said an illness ought to look like, and in Observationes Medicae of 1676 he treated diseases as distinct kinds that could be classified, separating scarlet fever from measles.","Turn the second bullet into a mechanism too: the Royal Society, founded in London in 1660 and chartered by Charles II in 1662, took nullius in verba — take nobody's word for it — as its rule, and from March 1665 its Philosophical Transactions printed observations that other men could repeat and check, so a description of a disease became a public, dated claim rather than a private opinion.","Add the reason the bullets cannot reach: instruments began to widen what could be observed at all, with Robert Hooke's Micrographia of 1665 showing what a microscope could reveal and Antonie van Leeuwenhoek's letters to the Society in the 1670s describing living things too small for the naked eye, although nobody could yet connect any of them to disease.","Close by linking the reasons instead of listing them: a method of looking, a body that published and checked what was seen, and instruments that widened what could be seen together moved diagnosis away from the urine flask and towards the bedside — while the explanation of what caused an illness stayed where it had been, which is why the improvement was in naming diseases rather than in curing them."],"cues":["What did the first bullet's physician do differently at a patient's bedside?","How does a society with a journal change the standing of an observation?","Which new instrument widened what could be seen, and what was its limit?","If diagnosis improved, why did treatment not improve with it?"]},{"title":"Question 4, 12 marks: explaining why an old authority lost its grip","setup":"Paper 1 Question 4, worth 12 marks: 'Explain why the authority of Galen was challenged in England in the years c1500–c1700.' You may use the following in your answer: the work of Vesalius; the Company of Barber-Surgeons. You must also use information of your own. There is a maximum of 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content.","steps":["Start from what an authority rests on: Galen had stood for more than a thousand years partly because there was no accepted way of checking him, so the real question is why checking became possible.","Use the first bullet as the first check: Vesalius did the cutting himself at Padua and published On the Fabric of the Human Body in 1543, correcting Galen on points anyone could verify — one jawbone, not two; a breastbone of three parts, not seven; and, in 1543, no pores that he could find through the wall between the two sides of the heart (he denied them outright only in the 1555 edition).","Use the second bullet to bring the check to England: the Company of Barber-Surgeons, created in 1540, was granted the bodies of four executed criminals a year for public dissection in London, so English surgeons watched a claim being tested on a body instead of hearing a text read aloud over one.","Add the reason of your own that goes deeper than either bullet: An Anatomical Account of the Motion of the Heart and Blood, published by Harvey in 1628, attacked not a detail of Galen's anatomy but the whole of his physiology, because blood that circulates cannot be blood manufactured in the liver and burned away as fuel.","Close by linking the reasons and marking the limit: print carried the corrections, and the Royal Society made take nobody's word for it a rule of work, yet universities went on setting Galen's texts to the end of the period, so the authority was challenged long before it was abandoned."],"cues":["What had protected Galen's reputation for so long?","What did the first bullet's anatomist do that let anyone else verify a correction?","What did the second bullet give English surgeons that a book could not?","Which English book attacked Galen's system rather than his details?","How far had the challenge actually got by the end of the period?"]},{"title":"Question 5, 16 marks: setting the criterion before the argument starts","setup":"Paper 1 Question 5, worth 16 marks with up to 4 more for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology: 'New institutions did more than individual doctors to change English medicine in the years c1500–c1700.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: the Company of Barber-Surgeons; Thomas Sydenham. You must also use information of your own.","steps":["State the measure before arguing anything: change will be judged twice, by how far something altered what English medicine knew and by how far it altered what English medicine did to a patient, and warn the reader that institutions and individuals will score differently on the two tests.","Put the named factor at its strongest: the Company of Barber-Surgeons of 1540 held public dissections on the bodies of executed criminals, the Royal College of Physicians of 1518 licensed practitioners and issued its Pharmacopoeia Londinensis in 1618, the Society of Apothecaries was chartered in 1617 and the Royal Society of 1660 published results from 1665 — an institution is what turns one man's finding into something taught, licensed and printed.","Weigh the individuals on the first test: nothing any of those bodies produced matches Harvey's proof in 1628 that the heart is a pump driving one quantity of blood round a closed system, or Vesalius's demonstration in 1543 that an ancient authority could be checked against a body and found wrong.","Weigh the second bullet on the second test: Sydenham's insistence on watching the patient, published in Observationes Medicae in 1676, reached the bedside as no charter did, and his cooling regime for smallpox displaced the older practice of heating and sweating the sufferer.","Bring in the point beyond the bullets and then judge: institutions were also the brake, since the College's fellows had themselves been trained on Galen and were in no hurry to teach anything else, so agree that institutions decided what spread and stuck, refuse the claim that they originated the change, and name the test your verdict rests on."],"cues":["What has to be defined before the words 'did more' can mean anything?","What can a company, a college or a society do that one doctor cannot?","Which single discoveries outweigh anything the institutions produced?","Which of the two changed what a patient actually experienced?","Where did institutions slow English medicine down rather than speed it up?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Why did bloodletting survive the whole of the Medical Renaissance?","a":"Because the Theory of the Four Humours survived. Treatment follows the theory of cause, and nothing had replaced the humours as the explanation of illness, so removing blood still looked like the obvious way to restore a balance."},{"q":"What did Vesalius change, and what did he not change?","a":"He changed what counted as evidence: his dissections corrected Galen and showed the ancient texts could be tested against a body. He did not change any treatment, and universities went on setting Galen's books."},{"q":"Name the three kinds of evidence Harvey used to prove that blood circulates.","a":"Dissections of cold-blooded animals whose hearts beat slowly enough to observe; a calculation showing the heart pumps far more blood than the body could make and burn; pressing the veins of the arm to show their valves allow flow in one direction only."},{"q":"In what way was the response to the Great Plague of 1665 an improvement on the response to the Black Death, and in what way was it not?","a":"It was far better organised and recorded — written orders, houses shut up with watchmen, paid searchers of the dead, the Bills of Mortality, closed theatres. It was no better informed: the cause was still thought to be bad air, and the treatments still came from the humours."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 1 Section B is worth 36 of the paper's 52 marks: Question 3 is 4 marks, Question 4 is 12 marks, and Question 5 or Question 6 is 16 marks plus up to 4 for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology.","Question 3, 'Explain one way in which … was similar to / different from …', carries 2 marks for AO2 and 2 for AO1 and normally spans at least a century, so both periods must be named and evidenced.","Question 4 stimulus caps: a maximum of 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for those answers.","Question 5 or 6 stimulus caps: a maximum of 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for those answers.","Timing on the 80-minute Paper 1: roughly a minute a mark — about 4 minutes on Question 3, 12 on Question 4, and about 20 on Question 5 or 6, which is 16 marks plus the 4 marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology.","In Question 5 or 6 the judgement is marked by its own bullet in the level descriptor, so an answer with no overall judgement, or one that is merely asserted, cannot reach the top of any level however good the rest is."],"questions":[{"q":"Explain one way in which ideas about the cause of disease and illness in the years c1250–c1500 were similar to ideas about the cause of disease and illness in the years c1500–c1700. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed similarity — the Theory of the Four Humours, with miasma, still explained illness in both periods — supported by specific detail from each, such as diagnosis by uroscopy before 1500 against the blaming of bad air during the Great Plague of 1665.","sol":"One way they were similar is that the Theory of the Four Humours, with miasma beside it, remained the standard explanation of illness in both periods. In the years c1250–c1500 a physician trained on Galen inspected a patient's urine and blamed the illness on an excess of blood, phlegm, yellow bile or black bile. The same framework was still working after 1600: bleeding and purging remained standard, and when plague struck London in 1665 the authorities lit street bonfires and cleared filth because they believed bad air carried disease. The similarity is one of framework: no discovery of the Medical Renaissance replaced the humours, because the real causes of disease still could not be seen.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Look for the explanation of illness that is still in use at both ends of the span, not for two things that merely resemble each other.","Both halves need dated, specific support: what a physician actually did before 1500, and what was still being done after 1600.","Name the shared belief, prove it separately in each period, then finish on why it had not been replaced."],"codes":["11-2 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Continuity and change in explanations of the cause of disease"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the way doctors learned about human anatomy in the years c1250–c1500 was different from the way they learned about it in the years c1500–c1700. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — anatomy learned from human dissection and printed illustrations rather than from reading and disputing an ancient authority — supported by detail such as Galen and Avicenna as set texts against Vesalius's Fabrica of 1543 and the dissections granted to the Company of Barber-Surgeons.","sol":"In the medieval period anatomy was learned from books. A physician spent seven years or more at a university reading Galen and Avicenna, and a dissection was used to demonstrate what Galen had written rather than to test it. In the Medical Renaissance anatomy was learned from bodies. Vesalius dissected human corpses himself at Padua and published On the Fabric of the Human Body in 1543, correcting Galen's claim that the human jawbone was made of two bones, and in England the Company of Barber-Surgeons, created in 1540, was granted the bodies of executed criminals for public dissection. The difference is one of authority: the body, not the text, had become the evidence.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what a student in each period actually looked at while they were being taught.","Both halves need named texts, named people or a named institution, because the AO1 marks come from precise support.","State the difference, evidence each period in turn, then explain what had changed about where proof was thought to come from."],"codes":["11-2 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Improvements in medical training and the influence of Vesalius"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which attempts to prevent the spread of plague during the Black Death of 1348–49 were different from attempts made during the Great Plague in London in 1665. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — organised, written and enforced public orders in 1665 against local, improvised measures in 1348–49 — supported by detail such as shutting up houses, paid searchers and the Bills of Mortality against Gloucester's gates and a single royal letter about London's streets.","sol":"One difference is that in 1665 prevention was organised as public policy, while in 1348–49 it was local and improvised. During the Black Death, Gloucester tried to shut its gates against people from Bristol, wealthy families left the towns, and royal action went no further than an order to clean London's streets. In 1665 the authorities issued written orders and paid men to enforce them: infected houses were shut up for forty days with a red cross on the door and a watchman outside, searchers of the dead reported each death for the Bills of Mortality, and theatres were closed. The difference is one of government capacity, not medical knowledge, since bad air was blamed in both outbreaks.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Check first whether the belief behind the measures had changed, because that decides where the real difference lies.","Support each outbreak with something specific that was actually done, and say who did it.","Name the difference, evidence both outbreaks, then say what kind of difference it is."],"codes":["11-2 — 3 Case studies: Dealing with the Great Plague in London (1665)"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the way new medical ideas were spread in England in the years c1250–c1500 was different from the way they were spread in the years c1500–c1700. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — printed books, exact illustrations and a scientific society against hand-copied manuscripts under Church control — supported by detail such as Caxton's press at Westminster in 1476, Vesalius's illustrations, the Royal Society of 1660 and its Philosophical Transactions from 1665.","sol":"In the medieval period medical ideas travelled in hand-copied manuscripts: slow to produce, easy to copy wrongly and held in libraries the Church controlled, so an idea it disliked need not circulate. After c1500 ideas travelled in print. Caxton set up England's first printing press at Westminster in 1476, and print meant that identical texts and identical anatomical illustrations, such as those in Vesalius's book of 1543, reached every teacher in the same form. From 1660 the Royal Society added an institution designed to circulate results, publishing its Philosophical Transactions from 1665. The difference is that transmission had become fast, accurate and public, so a challenge to an old authority could no longer be quietly suppressed.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about the physical object an idea travelled in, and who controlled the copying of it.","Give each period a named example with a date, since the comparison needs support on both sides.","State the difference, evidence each period, then explain what the new way of spreading ideas made possible that the old one did not."],"codes":["11-2 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Continuity and change in explanations of the cause of disease"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which hospitals in England in the years c1250–c1500 were different from hospitals in the years c1500–c1700. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — hospitals run by the Church for care and prayer against hospitals refounded by charity and the City and used for medical treatment — supported by detail such as the dissolution of 1536–40, St Bartholomew's in 1546, St Thomas's in 1551 and the pest houses of the seventeenth century.","sol":"Medieval hospitals were Church institutions offering care rather than cure: mostly small, attached to monasteries, giving a patient rest, warmth, food and prayer, and often refusing the infectious. After the dissolution of the monasteries between 1536 and 1540 closed most of them, the survivors were refounded on a different basis. St Bartholomew's passed to the City of London in 1546 and St Thomas's reopened in 1551, paid for by charity and city money rather than by a religious house, and by the later seventeenth century physicians and surgeons attended patients in them, while pest houses isolated the infectious. The difference is one of purpose: the hospital had begun to be a place of treatment rather than of shelter and prayer.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask who paid for the building and what a patient was actually given once inside.","Anchor each period with a named hospital or a named change and a year.","Name the difference, evidence both periods, then explain what had changed about what a hospital was for."],"codes":["11-2 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Continuity and change in approaches to prevention, treatment and care"]},{"q":"Explain why there was so much continuity in the treatment of illness in England in the years c1500–c1700.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Theory of the Four Humours\n• apothecaries\nYou must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit analysis of treatment following an unchanged theory of cause, of who actually treated the sick and how they were trained, and of own points such as the absence of any way to test a remedy and the survival of supernatural belief. Maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Treatment in England changed little between c1500 and c1700 because the explanation it rested on did not change. The Theory of the Four Humours still taught that illness came from an excess of blood, phlegm, yellow bile or black bile, so the treatments it generated remained the obvious ones: bleeding by knife, cup or leech, purging with laxatives and emetics, and a regimen of diet, exercise and sleep chosen by the Theory of Opposites. A treatment cannot be abandoned while the theory that justifies it is still believed, and no rival explanation of cause existed to replace it; even William Harvey's proof of circulation in 1628 told a doctor how blood moved, not what made a patient ill.\n\nA second reason is who actually treated people. Most sick people never saw a physician, because physicians were few and expensive. They went to an apothecary, who mixed and sold herbal remedies and was trained by apprenticeship in a guild rather than by any new science, or to a barber surgeon, or they were nursed at home by the women of the household using remedies handed down in family herbals. Training by apprenticeship reproduces the practice of the previous generation, so the everyday medicine of England was built to repeat itself.\n\nThe reason that goes furthest, though, is that nothing existed by which a remedy could be tested. There was no germ theory, no way of measuring whether a patient recovered because of a treatment or in spite of it, and no laboratory. New material did arrive — guaiacum, sarsaparilla and cinchona bark from the New World, and chemical cures using mercury and antimony — but it was absorbed into the humoral framework rather than replacing it, and beliefs such as transference and the royal touch for scrofula survived untouched. The three reasons work together: an unexamined theory of cause was taught to the practitioners who did the work, and there was no means by which experience could ever contradict it.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Start from the relationship between a theory of cause and the treatment built on it — one cannot move while the other stands still.","Three developed reasons beat six listed ones, and at least one must come from outside the two bullets.","Turn each bullet into a mechanism that kept things still, then close by showing the reasons reinforcing one another."],"codes":["11-2 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Continuity and change in explanations of the cause of disease","11-2 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Continuity and change in approaches to prevention, treatment and care"]},{"q":"Explain why medical training in England changed in the years c1500–c1700.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the work of Vesalius\n• the printing press\nYou must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit Vesalius's correction of Galen from human dissection, printing making identical texts and illustrations available at speed, and own points such as the Royal College of Physicians of 1518, the Company of Barber-Surgeons of 1540 and English physicians trained at Padua. Maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Medical training changed first because the authority it was built on was shown to be unreliable. Andreas Vesalius dissected human corpses himself at Padua and published On the Fabric of the Human Body in 1543, correcting Galen in detail: the human jawbone is one bone and not two, the breastbone has three parts and not seven, and he could not find the pores Galen said ran through the wall of the heart. His conclusion, that Galen had described animals, made a course consisting only of reading and disputing the ancients indefensible, and it pushed dissection into the curriculum as a way of testing a claim rather than illustrating one.\n\nSecondly, printing decided how far and how fast that challenge travelled. Caxton had set up England's first printing press at Westminster in 1476, and by the sixteenth century printed books carried not only identical words but identical anatomical illustrations, so a correction made in Padua reached an English teacher intact and within a few years instead of arriving, if at all, in a miscopied manuscript a generation later. Printing also meant Galen could be read more widely, which is why training changed by argument rather than by simple replacement.\n\nThe decisive English reason lies beyond both bullets: institutions had to act before any of this reached a student here. The Royal College of Physicians, founded in 1518, licensed physicians and set standards in London; the Company of Barber-Surgeons, created in 1540, was granted the bodies of executed criminals for public dissection, so anatomy could be demonstrated on a body in the city; and John Caius, who had studied at Padua while Vesalius taught there, became President of the College and refounded Gonville and Caius College, Cambridge, in 1557. The three reasons interlock: Vesalius supplied the challenge, print delivered it, and English institutions gave it a place in a curriculum — though Galen's texts stayed on that curriculum throughout, which is why the change was real but partial.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["A course changes when what counts as proof changes — begin from what a student was asked to trust.","Each bullet needs converting into a mechanism, and you need a reason of your own that explains why any of it reached England.","Take one reason from each bullet, add an institutional one, and finish by saying how far the change actually went."],"codes":["11-2 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Improvements in medical training and the influence of Vesalius","11-2 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Continuity and change in explanations of the cause of disease"]},{"q":"Explain why William Harvey's discovery of the circulation of the blood made so little difference to the treatment of patients in England before c1700.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Theory of the Four Humours\n• medical training\nYou must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit the gap between physiology and therapy, the survival of humoral treatment, the resistance of Galen-trained physicians, and own points such as the invisibility of the capillaries until Malpighi in 1661 and the absence of any treatment that needed circulation. Maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"A discovery only changes treatment if it tells a doctor a different thing to do, and Harvey's did not. An Anatomical Account of the Motion of the Heart and Blood, published in 1628, proved that the heart is a pump and that one quantity of blood circulates round a closed system, destroying Galen's account of blood made in the liver and burned as fuel. That is an answer to how the body works, not to what makes a body ill, so it left the whole question of cause exactly where it was.\n\nBecause cause was untouched, the Theory of the Four Humours went on supplying the treatments. Physicians still explained illness as an excess of a humour, so they went on bleeding and purging; some even argued that knowing blood circulated made bleeding a more reliable procedure, because a physician could now reason about where to open a vein. The discovery was absorbed into the old practice instead of displacing it.\n\nTraining slowed it further. Physicians had spent years on Galen's texts, and a man's competence was measured by his mastery of them, so many rejected Harvey's account outright and the Royal College of Physicians was slow to teach it; a theory must be in the curriculum before it can shape what an ordinary doctor does.\n\nThe reason beyond the bullets is that the theory could not be completed. Harvey could not show the vessels joining arteries to veins, because they were too small to see; the capillaries were first observed under a microscope by Marcello Malpighi in 1661, four years after Harvey's death. Until then his opponents had a genuine objection, and the one treatment that would eventually need circulation — blood transfusion — was impossible while blood groups were unknown. Together these explain a discovery accepted as knowledge long before it could ever be used as therapy.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Separate two different things a discovery can be about: how the body works, and what makes it ill.","Each reason should end by naming what could not happen as a result, not by describing the discovery again.","Use one bullet for the treatments that stayed, one for the people who had to accept the idea, and add a reason about what Harvey himself could not show."],"codes":["11-2 — 3 Case studies: Key individual: William Harvey","11-2 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Continuity and change in approaches to prevention, treatment and care"]},{"q":"Explain why the authorities in London responded to the Great Plague of 1665 in the way that they did.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• miasma\n• religious explanations of disease\nYou must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit measures traced back to the beliefs that produced them — cleansing the air, prayer and fasting, humoral treatment — and own points such as the experience of earlier outbreaks, the powers of the Lord Mayor and parish officers, and the absence of any knowledge of fleas and rats. Maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Almost everything London did in 1665 follows from the belief that plague travelled in corrupted air. If bad air is the carrier, then the sensible measures are to destroy it and to keep away from it: bonfires were lit in the streets and strong-smelling substances burned, people carried posies of herbs and smoked tobacco, filth was cleared, and stray dogs and cats were killed because they were thought to spread the corruption. The dead were carted away at night and buried in pits outside the city so that the air of the living streets was not poisoned. None of this was irrational given the premise; it was simply built on the wrong premise.\n\nThe religious explanation produced a second set of responses. Plague was still understood as God's punishment for the sins of the city, so days of fasting and prayer were ordered, sermons blamed the wickedness of London, and many people accepted the outbreak as a judgement to be endured rather than a problem to be solved. This is why prayer and penance sat comfortably beside street fires: the two explanations were held together, not in competition.\n\nThe reason that explains the character of the 1665 response, though, is the machinery available. By the seventeenth century the Lord Mayor, aldermen and parish officers could issue written orders, employ watchmen and searchers of the dead, and collect figures in the Bills of Mortality, and earlier outbreaks had given them a model to copy. That is why the response took the form of enforceable regulation: houses shut up for forty days with a red cross and the words 'Lord have mercy upon us' on the door, theatres and fairs closed, public gatherings banned. The combination is the point: old beliefs about cause were carried out through new administrative power, which is why 1665 was better organised than any earlier outbreak while remaining medically useless.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Work backwards from the actions: each one makes sense once you know what people thought was carrying the disease.","Group the measures by the belief behind them instead of narrating the outbreak in order.","Take one reason from each bullet, then add a third about who had the power to give orders and enforce them."],"codes":["11-2 — 3 Case studies: Dealing with the Great Plague in London (1665)","11-2 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Continuity and change in explanations of the cause of disease"]},{"q":"Explain why new medical ideas reached English doctors more quickly in the years c1500–c1700 than in the years before c1500.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the printing press\n• the Royal Society\nYou must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit print producing identical texts and illustrations at speed, the Royal Society and Philosophical Transactions institutionalising publication, and own points such as the weakening of Church control of learning after the dissolution and the Renaissance willingness to question ancient authority. Maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Before c1500 a medical idea travelled in a manuscript copied by hand: slow to produce, expensive, easy to corrupt, and held in libraries controlled by the Church. Printing removed every one of those limits. Caxton set up England's first press at Westminster in 1476, and a printed edition put identical words in front of hundreds of readers at once. For medicine the decisive gain was the illustration: the detailed plates in Vesalius's On the Fabric of the Human Body of 1543 could be reproduced exactly, so an English reader saw what a Paduan dissector had seen, which no hand-copied drawing could guarantee.\n\nThe Royal Society then turned circulation into a system. Founded in 1660 and given its charter by Charles II in 1662, it gathered men who valued experiment, demonstrated results at its meetings, and from 1665 published Philosophical Transactions, so that a finding became public property with a date attached rather than a private possession. Royal patronage also made this kind of enquiry respectable, which mattered in a society where status decided what was taken seriously.\n\nBeneath both lies a change in attitude that the bullets do not reach. The Renaissance revived the ambition to observe and to question the ancients rather than merely to preserve them, and the Reformation and the dissolution of the monasteries between 1536 and 1540 broke the Church's hold on the places where learning was stored and taught, so an idea that contradicted an approved authority was no longer so easily suppressed. Together the three explain the speed: people were now willing to challenge Galen, a machine existed that could carry the challenge accurately, and an institution existed whose purpose was to publish it.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Compare the physical object an idea travelled in before and after c1500, and ask who controlled it.","A machine and an institution are not the same kind of reason — explain what each one added that the other could not.","Use one bullet for speed and accuracy, one for publication, and add a reason about why people were willing to listen at all."],"codes":["11-2 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Continuity and change in explanations of the cause of disease","11-2 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Improvements in medical training and the influence of Vesalius"]},{"q":"'The work of William Harvey was the most important development in English medicine in the years c1500–c1700.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the work of Vesalius\n• Thomas Sydenham\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit a criteria-based judgement weighing Harvey's proof of circulation against Vesalius's anatomy, Sydenham's clinical observation and the means of transmission, distinguishing importance for knowledge from importance for the patient. Maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Importance has to be measured against something, so this answer judges each development twice: by how far it changed what English doctors knew, and by how far it changed what a sick person actually received. The two tests do not agree, and that disagreement decides the question.\n\nOn the first test Harvey's claim is very strong. In An Anatomical Account of the Motion of the Heart and Blood, published in 1628, he demonstrated that the heart is a muscular pump and that one quantity of blood circulates continuously round a closed system of arteries and veins. The proof was experimental rather than textual: he dissected cold-blooded animals whose hearts beat slowly enough to be watched, he calculated that the heart pushes out far more blood than the body could possibly make from food and burn away, and he pressed on the veins of the arm to show that their valves permit flow in one direction only. That destroyed the Galenic physiology on which every European medical course rested, in which blood was made in the liver and consumed as fuel.\n\nVesalius, however, has the stronger claim to have made that possible. On the Fabric of the Human Body, published in 1543 and built on his own dissections at Padua, showed that Galen had described animals: the human jawbone is one bone rather than two, the breastbone has three parts rather than seven. Vesalius did not explain how the body works, but he established that the ancient authority could be checked against a body and found wrong, which is precisely the permission Harvey needed. In time and in logic he comes first.\n\nSydenham's importance is of a third kind, and on the second test it is the greatest of the three. Working in London and publishing Observationes Medicae in 1676, he insisted on observing the patient rather than trusting the book, recorded symptoms carefully, and argued that diseases have distinct identities that can be classified, separating scarlet fever from other fevers and naming it. That changes what a physician does at the bedside, which neither Vesalius nor Harvey delivered.\n\nBeyond the three men lies the factor that decides the question. Discoveries matter only if they reach people. Caxton's press at Westminster, set up in 1476, meant that printed texts and exact anatomical illustrations arrived intact wherever they were sent; the Royal Society, founded in 1660 and chartered by Charles II in 1662, made experiment respectable and, through Philosophical Transactions from 1665, made results public with a date attached. Without these, all three men's work would have stayed in a handful of libraries.\n\nThe statement is therefore half right, and the criterion says which half. Judged on knowledge, Harvey's work is the most important single development of the period, because it overturned the physiology every other idea depended on. Judged on treatment it is not important at all within the period: bloodletting continued unchanged, no new remedy followed from it, and the capillaries his theory required were not seen until Malpighi used a microscope in 1661. Vesalius made Harvey possible, Sydenham did more for the patient, and print and the Royal Society decided whether any of it counted. I agree with the statement about what was known, and reject it about what was done.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Before arguing, decide what would count as proof that one development mattered more than another.","Two different measures of importance point in opposite directions here; find them both before you plan paragraphs.","Take the named individual first, weigh the two offered rivals, then add the factor neither bullet mentions and let the conclusion apply your criterion."],"codes":["11-2 — 3 Case studies: Key individual: William Harvey","11-2 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Improvements in medical training and the influence of Vesalius","11-2 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Continuity and change in explanations of the cause of disease"]},{"q":"'There was more continuity than change in the prevention and treatment of disease in England in the years c1500–c1700.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• bloodletting\n• hospitals\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit a criteria-based judgement setting the survival of humoral treatment and miasmatic prevention against new remedies, the regulation of practitioners and the refoundation of hospitals, distinguishing curing from caring. Maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Whether continuity or change dominates depends on what is being measured, so this answer separates two things: whether a sick person was more likely to be cured at the end of the period than at the start, and whether the care and organisation around the sick had improved. Measured by cure the statement is right; measured by care it is wrong.\n\nThe case for continuity begins with bloodletting. Because the Theory of the Four Humours was never replaced, the treatments it justified survived intact: blood was still removed by knife, cup or leech, patients were still purged with laxatives and emetics, and diet and regimen were still prescribed by the Theory of Opposites. Prevention was equally unchanged, because miasma remained the accepted explanation of how disease travelled — as late as 1665 London fought plague with street bonfires, sweet herbs and the clearing of filth. Supernatural belief survived too: Charles II went on touching sufferers of scrofula, the King's Evil. Most people, moreover, were still treated at home by the women of the household, or by an apothecary trained through apprenticeship, so the everyday experience of illness scarcely altered.\n\nHospitals are where the case for change is strongest, and it is a mixed case. The dissolution of the monasteries between 1536 and 1540 closed most hospitals and at first made provision worse. What replaced them, however, was different in kind: St Bartholomew's passed to the City of London in 1546 and St Thomas's reopened in 1551, funded by charity and city money rather than by a religious house, and by the later seventeenth century physicians and surgeons attended patients in them, so a hospital had begun to be a place of treatment rather than a shelter offering rest and prayer. Pest houses were built to isolate the infectious, which is a change in the organisation of prevention even though it rested on the old theory of contagious air.\n\nThe points that go beyond the stimulus strengthen the case for change without overturning the judgement. New remedies arrived from the New World — guaiacum against syphilis, sarsaparilla, cinchona or Peruvian bark against the fevers of malaria — and chemical cures using mercury and antimony entered the apothecary's shop. Practitioners were regulated as never before: the Royal College of Physicians was founded in 1518, the Company of Barber-Surgeons in 1540 and the Society of Apothecaries received its charter in 1617, and in 1618 the College published the London Pharmacopoeia so that a named remedy meant the same thing across the city. Yet each of these is a change in supply, standardisation or organisation, not in what a remedy was thought to be doing; cinchona bark genuinely worked against malarial fever, but it was prescribed inside a humoral framework that nobody had abandoned.\n\nI therefore agree with the statement, but only on the criterion that matters most to a patient's chance of recovery. Treatment and prevention rested on an unchanged theory of cause for the whole period, and almost no infectious disease could be cured in 1700 that could not be cured in 1500: cinchona bark against malarial fever is the single real exception, and it was found by trial and used inside a theory of cause nobody had abandoned. On the criterion of care, organisation and regulation, change is real and considerable. Continuity holds where knowledge of cause was needed, and change occurred wherever money, administration and skill were enough on their own.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["The judgement turns on what you are measuring; decide that in the opening rather than halfway through.","Two measures of improvement point in opposite directions here, and both need naming before you plan.","Argue one measure, then the other, then say which matters more and why, instead of listing changes and continuities in turn."],"codes":["11-2 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Continuity and change in approaches to prevention, treatment and care","11-2 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Continuity and change in explanations of the cause of disease","11-2 — 3 Case studies: Dealing with the Great Plague in London (1665)"]},{"q":"'The printing press was the main reason why new medical ideas reached English doctors in the years c1500–c1700.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• printed anatomical illustrations\n• the Royal Society\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit a criteria-based judgement weighing print against the Royal Society, institutional licensing and training, and changing attitudes to authority after the Reformation, distinguishing the means of transmission from the willingness to receive. Maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"An idea reaches a doctor only if something carries it and the doctor is willing to receive it. This answer judges the printing press against its rivals on both counts, asking which factor, if removed, would have done most damage to the spread of medical knowledge in England.\n\nThe case for print is strong and begins with accuracy rather than speed. A manuscript was copied by hand, so every copy differed a little and a drawing could not be trusted at all. Caxton had set up England's first press at Westminster in 1476, and by the sixteenth century a printed edition could put identical words and identical images in front of every reader. For medicine the images were decisive: the plates in Vesalius's On the Fabric of the Human Body of 1543 let an English reader see exactly what a dissector in Padua had seen, which made a correction to Galen checkable rather than merely assertible. Print also carried the old authorities more widely, so the argument between Galen and his critics happened in public.\n\nThe Royal Society, however, supplied something print alone could not: a purpose and a pattern. Founded in 1660 and given its charter by Charles II in 1662, it brought together men committed to experiment, demonstrated results at its meetings and, from 1665, published Philosophical Transactions, so that a finding became public property with a date and an author attached rather than a private possession to be released at the owner's convenience. Royal patronage made this kind of enquiry respectable in a society where status governed what was taken seriously. A press prints whatever it is given; the Society decided what was worth printing and how it should be tested.\n\nThe factor beyond the bullets is the one the statement most neglects: the willingness to accept a new idea at all. Renaissance scholarship revived the ambition to observe and to question the ancients rather than merely to preserve them; the Reformation and the dissolution of the monasteries between 1536 and 1540 broke the Church's control of the places where learning was kept and taught, so a book that contradicted an approved authority could no longer be quietly suppressed. Against that, English institutions could also slow reception: physicians trained on Galen's texts and licensed by the Royal College of Physicians, founded in 1518, took decades to accept Harvey's proof of circulation of 1628, which was printed and available from the start. That is the clearest evidence that printing carries an idea but does not make anyone believe it.\n\nMy judgement is that printing was the necessary condition rather than the main reason. Nothing else could have delivered exact anatomical illustration to England, and without it neither Vesalius nor Harvey could have been read as they were written, so on the criterion of transmission the statement holds. On the criterion of reception it fails, because the same press carried Harvey's book to physicians who rejected it for a generation. The Royal Society mattered because it created an audience that tested claims instead of deferring to them, and the changed attitudes of the period mattered because they created that audience in the first place. Print was the vehicle; the willingness to question ancient authority was the reason the journey was worth making.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Split the question in two before you argue: what carries an idea, and what makes someone accept it.","Test the claim by removing each factor in turn and asking what would have failed without it.","Argue the named factor at its strongest, weigh the offered rival, then bring in the factor neither bullet mentions before judging."],"codes":["11-2 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Continuity and change in explanations of the cause of disease","11-2 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Improvements in medical training and the influence of Vesalius","11-2 — 3 Case studies: Key individual: William Harvey"]},{"q":"'Little had changed in the way England dealt with plague between the Black Death of 1348–49 and the Great Plague of London in 1665.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• miasma\n• shutting up houses\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit a criteria-based judgement setting continuity of explanation and treatment against the growth of organised, recorded and enforced local government action, using both outbreaks with precise detail. Maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Over three centuries separate the two outbreaks, so the question is what, if anything, had moved. This answer judges by two criteria: what people believed was happening, and what the authorities were able to do about it. On the first, almost nothing had changed; on the second, a great deal had.\n\nThe case for the statement rests on explanation. In 1348–49 plague was blamed on God's punishment for sin, on an unusual conjunction of the planets, and on miasma, the corrupted air rising from filth, stagnant water and unburied corpses. In 1665 exactly the same list was offered: sermons called the outbreak a judgement on the wickedness of London, days of fasting and prayer were ordered, and the practical measures — bonfires burning in the streets, sweet herbs carried to the nose, tobacco smoked, filth cleared away — all assumed corrupted air. Treatment repeated itself too, since it still came from the humours: bleeding, purging, lancing the buboes, herbal remedies and plague water, with transference, such as strapping a live chicken to the swellings, added in the seventeenth century as a new superstition rather than a new science. Nobody in either outbreak suspected the fleas living on black rats, which is why killing London's stray cats and dogs in 1665 may have made matters worse.\n\nThe case against the statement rests on organisation, and the contrast is sharp. In 1348–49 the response was local and improvised: Gloucester tried to shut its gates against people from Bristol, the wealthy simply left, and royal action extended to a single order to the Mayor of London to clean the streets. In 1665 prevention was public policy, written down and enforced. Infected houses were shut up for forty days with a red cross and the words 'Lord have mercy upon us' painted on the door and a watchman posted outside; theatres and fairs were closed and public gatherings banned; the dead were collected by cart at night and buried in pits away from the living.\n\nThe points beyond the stimulus complete that case. Searchers of the dead were paid to inspect bodies and report the cause of death, and their returns were collected in the Bills of Mortality, which recorded about 68,000 plague deaths in London even though historians think the true figure was nearer 100,000. That is a state counting its dead week by week, which medieval England could not have done. The Lord Mayor, aldermen and parish officers had the authority and the staff to impose and police such orders; in 1348–49 no such machinery existed anywhere in the country.\n\nI therefore disagree with the statement on administration and agree with it on medicine, and the criterion decides which matters. Judged by whether England understood plague or could reduce its mortality, 1665 shows almost no advance on 1348–49: the theory was the same, the treatments were the same, and the plague ended when it ended. Judged by whether England could organise a coordinated response, record it and enforce it, the change is substantial. Better government had arrived; better medicine had not, and only the second could have saved lives.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide what you are comparing — what people believed, or what the authorities could actually do — before you take a side.","Each outbreak needs precise, dated detail on both sides of the argument, not a general impression.","Argue the continuity of explanation first, then the change in organisation, and let the conclusion say which criterion counts and why."],"codes":["11-2 — 3 Case studies: Dealing with the Great Plague in London (1665)","11-2 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Continuity and change in approaches to prevention, treatment and care","11-2 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Continuity and change in explanations of the cause of disease"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In which year did Vesalius publish On the Fabric of the Human Body?","a":"1543."},{"q":"What did William Harvey prove in the book he published in 1628?","a":"That the heart is a pump and that the same blood circulates continuously round the body, rather than being made in the liver and burned up as fuel."},{"q":"Which society, founded in London in 1660, began publishing Philosophical Transactions in 1665?","a":"The Royal Society."},{"q":"What was painted on the door of a house shut up during the Great Plague of 1665?","a":"A red cross and the words 'Lord have mercy upon us'."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Thomas Sydenham","back":"London physician, 1624–1689, called the English Hippocrates. Observed patients closely instead of trusting books, argued diseases have distinct identities, named scarlet fever, and published Observationes Medicae in 1676.","code":"11-2 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Continuity and change in explanations of the cause of disease","open":false},{"front":"The Royal Society","back":"Founded in London in 1660 and chartered by Charles II in 1662. Its members valued experiment and demonstration, and from 1665 it published Philosophical Transactions, making new results public.","code":"11-2 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Continuity and change in explanations of the cause of disease","open":false},{"front":"The printing press and medicine","back":"Caxton set up England's first press at Westminster in 1476. Print produced identical texts and exact anatomical illustrations, so corrections to Galen spread fast and could not easily be suppressed.","code":"11-2 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Continuity and change in explanations of the cause of disease","open":false},{"front":"Ideas about the cause of disease, c1500–c1700","back":"Largely unchanged: the Four Humours and miasma still explained illness, and God's punishment was still invoked. What changed was method — closer observation — not the explanation of cause itself.","code":"11-2 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Continuity and change in explanations of the cause of disease","open":false},{"front":"The London Pharmacopoeia, 1618","back":"A list of standard remedies and recipes published by the Royal College of Physicians, so that a named medicine meant the same across London. Nicholas Culpeper published an English translation in the 1640s.","code":"11-2 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Continuity and change in approaches to prevention, treatment and care","open":false},{"front":"Transference","back":"A seventeenth-century belief that a disease could be passed out of the body into an object or animal rubbed against it — for example strapping a live chicken to a plague sufferer's buboes.","code":"11-2 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Continuity and change in approaches to prevention, treatment and care","open":false},{"front":"Hospitals after the dissolution of the monasteries","back":"The dissolution of 1536–40 closed most hospitals. St Bartholomew's passed to the City of London in 1546 and St Thomas's reopened in 1551, funded by charity, and physicians and surgeons increasingly treated patients in them.","code":"11-2 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Continuity and change in approaches to prevention, treatment and care","open":false},{"front":"One new remedy that reached England from the New World","back":"Guaiacum, used against syphilis; sarsaparilla; cinchona or Peruvian bark, used against the fevers of malaria.","code":"11-2 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Continuity and change in approaches to prevention, treatment and care","open":true},{"front":"Andreas Vesalius","back":"Anatomist, 1514–1564, professor at Padua. His On the Fabric of the Human Body (1543) came from his own human dissections and corrected Galen: one jawbone, not two; a breastbone of three parts, not seven.","code":"11-2 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Improvements in medical training and the influence of Vesalius","open":false},{"front":"The Company of Barber-Surgeons, 1540","back":"Created by uniting the barbers and the surgeons of London. It was granted the bodies of executed criminals for public dissection, so anatomy could be taught from a body rather than only from a text.","code":"11-2 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Improvements in medical training and the influence of Vesalius","open":false},{"front":"The Royal College of Physicians, 1518","back":"Founded under Henry VIII to license physicians and set standards in London. It raised training but also slowed new ideas, since its members had been taught from Galen's texts.","code":"11-2 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: Improvements in medical training and the influence of Vesalius","open":false},{"front":"William Harvey","back":"English physician, 1578–1657. Trained at Cambridge and Padua, worked at St Bartholomew's from 1609 and served James I and Charles I. His An Anatomical Account of the Motion of the Heart and Blood appeared in 1628.","code":"11-2 — 3 Case studies: Key individual: William Harvey","open":false},{"front":"How Harvey proved that blood circulates","back":"Dissection of cold-blooded animals whose hearts beat slowly; a calculation showing the heart pumps more blood than the body could make and burn; pressing the veins of the arm to show their valves allow one-way flow.","code":"11-2 — 3 Case studies: Key individual: William Harvey","open":false},{"front":"Shutting up houses, 1665","back":"An infected London house was sealed for forty days with everyone inside, a red cross and the words 'Lord have mercy upon us' painted on the door, and a watchman posted outside to stop anyone leaving.","code":"11-2 — 3 Case studies: Dealing with the Great Plague in London (1665)","open":false},{"front":"The Great Plague of London, 1665","back":"The Bills of Mortality recorded about 68,000 plague deaths; historians estimate the true total near 100,000, almost a quarter of London. Charles II and his court left for Salisbury in July 1665, moving on to Oxford in September.","code":"11-2 — 3 Case studies: Dealing with the Great Plague in London (1665)","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 1 Question 5, 16 marks: 'The Theory of the Four Humours was the greatest obstacle to the improvement of English medicine in the years c1500–c1700.')","items":["The Four Humours were the greatest obstacle if the improvement being measured is improvement in treatment. The theory held that illness came from an excess of blood, phlegm, yellow bile or black bile, so the reasonable response to almost any complaint was to draw the excess off: bleeding by knife, cup or leech, purging with laxatives and emetics, and a regimen of diet, sleep and exercise. That reasoning was left untouched by everything the period discovered. Vesalius corrected Galen's anatomy in 1543 and Harvey overturned his physiology in 1628, yet neither gave a physician anything different to do at a bedside, and some argued that blood which circulates made bleeding more rational still. The theory was written into who was allowed to practise, because the Royal College of Physicians, chartered in 1518, licensed men whose training was a course of Galen's texts; and it absorbed even the genuinely new, so that guaiacum from the Caribbean and the Peruvian bark listed in the College's Pharmacopoeia in 1677 were prescribed as humoral correctives rather than as cures for named diseases. The judgement has to be stated more precisely, though. The humours survived because nothing could replace them: the causes of infectious disease were invisible, and although Leeuwenhoek's letters to the Royal Society in the 1670s described living things too small for the naked eye, nobody could yet connect them to illness. The Four Humours were therefore the form the obstacle took rather than the obstacle itself — the real barrier was that the cause of disease could not be seen, and the humours were what filled that gap.","Why this reaches Level 4: the explanation is directed consistently at the conceptual focus — every sentence is about how far the theory obstructed improvement, not about what the theory said; the information is precisely selected and wide-ranging (Vesalius in 1543, Harvey in 1628, the College's charter of 1518, guaiacum and the Peruvian bark of 1677, Leeuwenhoek in the 1670s); the line of reasoning is coherent and sustained, because the measure fixed in the first sentence, improvement in treatment, is the measure the last sentence applies; and the criterion for the judgement is justified and applied rather than asserted, which is the third bullet of the Level 4 descriptor."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["The Theory of the Four Humours was the biggest obstacle because it was wrong. People thought illness was caused by having too much blood or phlegm or bile, so doctors kept bleeding and purging their patients instead of looking for real cures. This carried on all through the period even though clever men like Vesalius and Harvey had shown that the old ideas about the body were mistaken. Doctors were also trained badly out of ancient books, and the Royal College of Physicians did not really help, and there were other problems too, like people still believing that God sent disease as a punishment. Overall I agree that the Four Humours was the greatest obstacle to the improvement of English medicine in this period.","Why this stays at Level 2: it meets 'an explanation is given showing limited analysis and with implicit or unsustained links to the conceptual focus of the question' and 'accurate and relevant information is included, showing some knowledge and understanding of the period', while the third bullet applies exactly — 'the overall judgement is given but its justification is asserted or insecure', because the final sentence announces a verdict without ever saying what improvement is being measured. What it misses is the Level 4 requirement that information be 'precisely selected': there is not one date, not one title, not one named remedy, and the rivals — training, the College, religious belief — are named without being weighed. Adding 1543 and 1628, the College's charter of 1518, the Peruvian bark of 1677, and then a stated measure of what would count as improvement, is what moves this paragraph two levels."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — The influence in England of the work of Vesalius","items":["Andreas Vesalius, 1514–1564, born in Brussels and appointed professor of surgery and anatomy at the University of Padua in Italy.","He performed the dissections himself rather than leaving the cutting to an assistant while a text was read aloud, which was the older practice.","His book On the Fabric of the Human Body (De Humani Corporis Fabrica) was published in 1543.","Corrections to Galen: the human jawbone is a single bone, not two; the breastbone has three parts, not seven; and he could not find the pores Galen said ran through the wall between the two sides of the heart.","His explanation of Galen's errors was that Galen had dissected animals, because human dissection was largely forbidden in Galen's lifetime.","The book's illustrations were exceptionally detailed, and printing meant they could be reproduced exactly wherever the book travelled.","His importance is in method rather than cure: he established that an ancient authority could be tested against a body and found wrong.","In England, John Caius studied at Padua while Vesalius was teaching there, became President of the Royal College of Physicians and refounded Gonville and Caius College, Cambridge, in 1557.","The Company of Barber-Surgeons, created in 1540, was granted the bodies of executed criminals for public dissection, so anatomy could be demonstrated on a body in London.","Limits on his influence: universities went on setting Galen's texts throughout the period, training still took years of reading, and knowing the exact shape of the body cured nobody."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Key individual: William Harvey and the circulation of the blood","items":["William Harvey, 1578–1657, born in Folkestone in Kent.","Educated at Cambridge and then at Padua, the university where Vesalius had taught.","Appointed a physician at St Bartholomew's Hospital in London in 1609, and later served as a royal physician to James I and to Charles I.","Published An Anatomical Account of the Motion of the Heart and Blood (De Motu Cordis) in 1628.","What Galen had taught: blood was made in the liver out of food and burned up as fuel in the body, and there were two separate kinds of blood.","Harvey's claim: the heart is a muscular pump, and one quantity of blood circulates continuously round a closed system of arteries and veins.","Evidence one: dissections of cold-blooded animals, whose hearts beat slowly enough for the movement to be watched.","Evidence two: a calculation showing that the heart pushes out far more blood than the body could possibly manufacture from food and consume.","Evidence three: pressing on the veins of the arm showed that their valves let blood flow only in the direction of the heart.","What he could not show: the tiny vessels joining arteries to veins. The capillaries were first seen through a microscope by Marcello Malpighi in 1661, four years after Harvey's death.","Reaction in England: many physicians rejected the theory because it contradicted Galen, and bloodletting continued unchanged; acceptance came gradually over the rest of the century.","Significance: the foundation of modern understanding of the body, and the idea that later made blood transfusion thinkable — but it changed no treatment inside the period."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — The work of Thomas Sydenham in improving diagnosis","items":["Thomas Sydenham, 1624–1689, worked as a physician in London and was later known as the English Hippocrates.","He insisted on observing the patient closely and recording the symptoms, rather than relying on what a book said an illness ought to look like.","He argued that diseases have distinct identities of their own and can be grouped and classified, in the way that plants are classified into species.","He identified scarlet fever as a disease separate from other fevers and gave it its name.","His Observationes Medicae was published in 1676 and was used for many years as a practical guide to diagnosis and treatment.","He favoured simple regimes over elaborate remedies, and used a cooling treatment for smallpox rather than the usual heating and sweating.","His contribution is to method: he helped to make careful observation at the bedside, rather than the authority of ancient texts, the basis of a physician's judgement.","Limits: he still worked inside the older framework of ideas about cause and had no conception of germs, so his change is in how disease was identified, not in what it was thought to be."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Dealing with the Great Plague in London, 1665","items":["London's last great outbreak of bubonic plague: deaths rose through the summer of 1665 and fell away over the following winter.","The Bills of Mortality recorded about 68,000 plague deaths in London; historians usually estimate the true total at around 100,000, almost a quarter of a city of about 460,000 people.","Explanations offered: bad air or miasma, God's punishment for sin, an unusual alignment of the planets, and an imbalance of the humours.","Treatments used: bleeding and purging, herbal remedies and plague water, lancing the buboes, quack cures sold in the street, and transference such as strapping a live chicken to the swellings.","Infected houses were shut up for forty days with everyone inside, a red cross and the words 'Lord have mercy upon us' painted on the door, and a watchman outside.","Searchers of the dead, usually poor older women, were paid to inspect bodies and report the cause of death for the Bills of Mortality.","Public gatherings were banned and theatres and fairs closed; stray dogs and cats were killed because they were believed to spread the disease.","Bonfires were lit in the streets and strong-smelling substances burned, on the belief that fire and smoke cleansed corrupted air.","The dead were collected by cart at night and buried in large pits outside the built-up area rather than in parish churchyards.","Those who could afford to leave did so: Charles II and his court left London in July 1665, moved to Salisbury in August and on to Oxford in September, when plague reached Salisbury, and many physicians left with the wealthy.","The real cause — a bacterium carried by fleas living on black rats — was unknown, so killing the city's cats and dogs may have made the outbreak worse.","Compared with the Black Death of 1348–49, the response was far better organised, enforced and recorded, but medically no more effective."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Prevention, treatment and care in the community and in hospitals, c1500–c1700","items":["Continuity in treatment: bloodletting by knife, cup or leech, purging with laxatives and emetics, and a regimen of diet, exercise and sleep, all drawn from the unchanged Theory of the Four Humours.","Continuity in care: most sick people were treated at home by the women of the household with herbal remedies, because a physician remained too expensive for ordinary families.","Continuity in belief: Charles II went on touching sufferers of scrofula, known as the King's Evil, which was believed to be cured by the royal touch.","New ingredients: remedies arrived from the New World, including guaiacum against syphilis, sarsaparilla, cinchona or Peruvian bark against the fevers of malaria, and tobacco.","Chemical cures: metals and minerals such as mercury and antimony were used alongside herbal remedies, drawn from the alchemical tradition.","Transference: the seventeenth-century belief that a disease could be passed out of the body into an object or an animal rubbed against it.","Regulation of practitioners: the Royal College of Physicians was founded in 1518, the Company of Barber-Surgeons in 1540, and the Society of Apothecaries received its charter in 1617.","The London Pharmacopoeia, published by the Royal College of Physicians in 1618, set out standard recipes so that a named remedy meant the same thing across the city; Nicholas Culpeper published an English translation in the 1640s.","Hospitals: the dissolution of the monasteries between 1536 and 1540 closed most of them; St Bartholomew's passed to the City of London in 1546 and St Thomas's reopened in 1551, paid for by charity and city funds.","Hospitals changed in purpose: by the later seventeenth century physicians and surgeons attended patients in them, so they were becoming places of treatment rather than shelters offering rest and prayer.","Pest houses were built in the seventeenth century to isolate people with infectious diseases, especially plague, away from the rest of the community.","What did not change: almost no infectious disease could be cured, because nobody yet knew what caused one; cinchona or Peruvian bark against malarial fever was the one genuinely effective exception, arrived at by trial and prescribed inside the unchanged theory of the humours."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — The transmission of ideas: the printing press and the Royal Society","items":["Printing with movable type was developed in Germany by Johannes Gutenberg in the middle of the fifteenth century, about 1450.","William Caxton set up England's first printing press at Westminster in 1476.","Print made identical copies: every reader of a printed book saw the same words in the same order, where hand-copied manuscripts differed from one another and gathered errors.","Illustrations mattered as much as text in medicine: the engravings in Vesalius's On the Fabric of the Human Body (1543) arrived intact wherever the book travelled.","Speed: a correction made in Padua could be in front of an English teacher within a few years, where a manuscript idea might take a generation to travel.","Control: copying no longer depended on a small number of scribes and Church libraries, so an idea that challenged an ancient authority could not easily be suppressed once it was in print.","Print carried this period's key books to their readers: Harvey's An Anatomical Account of the Motion of the Heart and Blood (1628) and Sydenham's Observationes Medicae (1676).","The Royal Society was founded in London in 1660 and granted a royal charter by Charles II in 1662.","Its motto, nullius in verba — take nobody's word for it — stated its rule that a claim be tested by experiment and demonstration rather than accepted on authority.","Fellows performed experiments at meetings and wrote them up, so a result could be repeated and checked by other people.","From March 1665 the Society published Philosophical Transactions, edited by its first secretary Henry Oldenburg: the first scientific journal in English, which made new work public and dated.","Robert Hooke's Micrographia, published under the Society's name in 1665, printed drawings of what he had seen through a microscope and made the instrument famous.","Later fellows included Isaac Newton, elected in 1672 and President from 1703, which shows how far royal approval had made experimental science respectable.","Limits: most medical books were still in Latin, few people could read, and the Society changed what educated men knew far faster than it changed what a sick person received.","The medieval contrast: ideas travelled in hand-copied manuscripts, slow to produce, easy to copy wrongly and held in libraries the Church controlled."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 1 Question 5 (16 marks + 4 SPaG): 'The dissolution of the monasteries was the most important change in the care of sick people in England in the years c1500–c1700.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. Stimulus points: St Bartholomew's Hospital; care in the community.","items":["Decoded question: the concept is change — how much difference a development made to the care of the sick. 'Most important' is comparative, so the essay cannot narrate the dissolution; it must weigh it against at least two rivals and reach a judgement on a stated criterion.","Criterion to set in the opening: importance will be judged twice — by how many sick people the change reached, and by how far it altered the kind of care they were given. Say at once that the two tests will disagree, because that disagreement is the argument.","Paragraph 1 (the named factor at its strongest): the dissolution of the monasteries between 1536 and 1540 closed the religious houses that ran most of England's hospitals, leper houses and almshouses, so a network built up over centuries was dismantled in four years and the Crown, not the Church, now decided what replaced it. Interim judgement: on reach, a shock felt everywhere.","Paragraph 2 (first stimulus point): what came back was different in kind. St Bartholomew's was granted to the City of London in 1546 and St Thomas's reopened in 1551, paid for by charity and city funds rather than by a monastery, and by the later seventeenth century physicians and surgeons were attending patients in them. Interim judgement: fewer hospitals, but hospitals turning into places of treatment rather than shelters offering rest and prayer.","Paragraph 3 (second stimulus point): care in the community barely moved. Most sick people were still nursed at home by the women of the household with herbal remedies, because a physician stayed beyond an ordinary family's means, and Nicholas Culpeper's English translation of the College's Pharmacopoeia in 1649 and his The English Physician of 1652 were written for exactly those households. Interim judgement: for the great majority, the dissolution changed who ran a hospital they would never enter.","Paragraph 4 (the point beyond the stimulus, which no Level 4 answer can omit): other institutions appear for reasons that have nothing to do with the dissolution — pest houses built from the early seventeenth century to isolate people with infectious disease away from everyone else, and control of who could give care through the Royal College of Physicians of 1518, the Company of Barber-Surgeons of 1540 and the Society of Apothecaries chartered in 1617. Interim judgement: the shape of care was set as much by regulation and by fear of infection as by the closing of the monasteries.","Evaluation thread running through every paragraph: ask of each change, how many sick people did it reach, and did it alter the kind of care they were given? Return to those two questions in the closing sentence of each paragraph so the reasoning is visibly sustained rather than a list.","Conclusion: agree that the dissolution was the sharpest single break, because it removed the existing provision at a stroke and forced a lay and civic replacement, but reject the claim on the second test — for most people care stayed in the home and stayed herbal, and what slowly altered the kind of care given was the arrival of trained physicians and surgeons inside the hospitals. Name the criterion explicitly, since the third bullet of the Level 4 descriptor rewards criteria that are justified and applied, not asserted.","Timing: about 20 minutes — roughly a minute a mark for the 16, plus the 4 SPaG marks. Spend about 4 minutes fixing the criterion and the four paragraphs, 14 writing, and 2 checking spelling and specialist terms.","Specialist terms to deploy accurately for the SPaG marks: dissolution, almshouse, charity, apothecary, pharmacopoeia, pest house, isolation, physician, surgeon, continuity."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:51:16.277421Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"Judged afresh from the kit file, not from the copy embedded in the judge prompt. Dedupe holds: node dupscan.js EDX-1HI0 11-2 prints 'no duplication', and reading the retargeted items against the bank confirms it - the two Question 3 worked examples now set care at home¹ and the ingredients of remedies², neither a bank focus; the two 12-mark examples set why diagnosis improved³ and why Galen's authority was challenged⁴, both away from questions[5]-[9] in focus and in stimulus pair; the 16-mark example sets institutions against individuals⁵; the model-paragraph pair sets the Four Humours as obstacle⁶ and the plan sets the dissolution of the monasteries⁷ - three separate 16-mark statements, none of them questions[10]-[13]. No printed question is reused: the specimen Option 11 Section B (Q3 diagnosis medieval against modern, Q4 the role of the Church c1250-c1700 with medical training and William Harvey, Q5 John Snow, Q6 government action and hospitals) and the June 2023 1HI0/11 Section B (Q3 prevention medieval against modern, Q4 access to care in the modern period, Q5 'The work of Thomas Sydenham was the key turning point in medicine in the years c1500-c1700' with Observationes Medicae and the Four Humours, Q6 attitudes c1700-c1900) share no stem, focus or stimulus pair with anything in the kit; the 2023 Sydenham stimulus and the kit's uses of Sydenham sit on different questions. Every new fact in the retargeted items verifies: Culpeper's English version of the College's 1618 Pharmacopoeia in 1649 and The English Physician of 1652; guaiacum from the Caribbean against syphilis with sarsaparilla and tobacco after it; cinchona listed in the London Pharmacopoeia of 1677 as Cortex Peruanus; the Pharmacopoeia Londinensis of 1618; the Company of Barber-Surgeons of 1540 and its statutory four executed criminals a year; Vesalius cutting at Padua, the Fabrica of 1543, the jawbone and the breastbone; Harvey's book of 1628 against Galen's liver physiology; Sydenham's Observationes Medicae of 1676 separating scarlet fever from measles and his cooling regime for smallpox; the Royal Society of 1660 chartered 1662, nullius in verba, Philosophical Transactions from March 1665, Hooke's Micrographia of 1665 and Leeuwenhoek's letters of the 1670s; the College of 1518 and the Society of Apothecaries of 1617; the dissolution of 1536-1540, St Bartholomew's granted to the City in 1546 and St Thomas's reopened in 1551. Checked across the whole kit: 41 dates, 6 figures and every named event, with Malpighi's capillaries of 1661, Caius refounding Gonville and Caius in 1557 and Newton elected in 1672 among them; no answer key is wrong, ambiguous or at odds with its solution, the caps everywhere read 'three or more aspects of content' with no Level 2 cap surviving, the Level 4 and Level 2 descriptors quoted in extras[0] and extras[1] match levels.md word for word, the worked examples' cues stay short of their own steps, and the counts still hold - 14 questions at 5/5/4, 15 cards, 6 room.facts, 5 worked examples, six fact files, one plan, four exit questions. Three small precision points and one word-count overrun remain; none blocks publication."}}},"11-3":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"11-3","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This is the room where medicine finally starts to move, and it is the half of the thematic study that Section B questions return to most often. Between c1700 and c1900 the cause of disease is explained for the first time, a disease is prevented by vaccination before anyone knows what causes it, pain and infection are taken out of surgery, nursing becomes a trained profession and the state is forced into public health. Every one of those is a possible comparison across the theme, a possible 'explain why' on the process of change, and a possible judgement about turning points, factors or the extent of change. Learn the period as a set of dated mechanisms — who did what, when, and what difference it made to a sick person — and the comparisons with c1250–c1500, c1500–c1700 and c1900–present write themselves.","idea":[{"h":"From miasma to microbes: why the explanation changed, and how slowly","code":"11-3 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Continuity and change in explanations of the cause of disease","t":"In 1700 educated opinion still explained disease as the medieval world had: foul air, or miasma, rising from cesspits, rubbish and rotting matter, with the Four Humours fading but not gone. Two rival ideas then appeared. Spontaneous generation said that the microbes found in decaying matter were produced by the decay. Louis Pasteur, a French chemist working on why wine, beer and vinegar soured, reversed that: his swan-necked flask experiments showed that sterilised liquid stayed clear while germs were kept out, and in 1861 he published his germ theory, arguing that microbes in the air cause decay and disease. Robert Koch made the theory usable by tying one microbe to one disease — the anthrax bacterium in 1876, the tuberculosis bacterium in 1882 and the cholera bacterium in 1883 — using solid agar cultures, industrial dyes and photography. Influence in Britain was real but slow: Joseph Lister applied the theory to wounds from 1865 and John Tyndall lectured on germs in the air in London in 1870, yet many British doctors blamed miasma into the 1870s and germ theory produced almost no cures before 1900. A top-level answer never says that 1861 changed everything at once; it separates the date the idea was published from the years it took to be believed and the much longer wait before it could treat anybody."},{"h":"Care and treatment: hospitals and nursing transformed, medicines barely touched","code":"11-3 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment","t":"Two different things are hidden inside the word 'treatment'. Care — where you were nursed, by whom, in what conditions — changed enormously. Voluntary hospitals paid for by subscription and legacies spread through the eighteenth century, Florence Nightingale reached the Barrack Hospital at Scutari in November 1854 with thirty-eight nurses, and after the drains and water supply were put right in the spring of 1855 the death rate there fell steeply. Her Notes on Nursing of 1859 and the Nightingale School at St Thomas' Hospital in London, opened in 1860, turned nursing into a trained, disciplined profession, and her insistence on clean, ventilated pavilion wards changed how hospitals were built. Surgery changed just as sharply: James Simpson introduced chloroform in 1847 and Joseph Lister carbolic acid in 1865. Actual medicines did not. Apart from a handful of effective drugs, the pharmacy of 1900 could still cure very few diseases, and patent remedies sold by the million did nothing. A top-level answer uses that split deliberately: it argues that care, nursing and surgery were transformed while the ability to cure an infection was not, and dates each claim."},{"h":"Prevention: the state learns to act","code":"11-3 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: New approaches to prevention","t":"Prevention in 1700 meant avoiding bad air. By 1900 it meant compulsory vaccination, sewers, inspected water and a medical officer of health in every district. The engine of that change was a run of cholera epidemics from 1831 onwards, which killed the rich as well as the poor, and Edwin Chadwick's Report on the Sanitary Condition of the Labouring Population of 1842, which argued that sickness made poverty and that cleaning up would save the ratepayer money. The Public Health Act of 1848 was permissive — it created a General Board of Health and allowed towns to act — and achieved little; the Great Stink of 1858 drove Parliament to fund Joseph Bazalgette's London sewers within weeks; the Sanitary Act of 1866 forced towns to appoint inspectors; and the Public Health Act of 1875 finally made the whole package compulsory. Attitudes had to shift first: laissez-faire lost its grip, and the Reform Act of 1867 gave many urban working men the vote, which made slum conditions an election question. A top-level answer explains the 1875 Act as the end of a chain, not as an event, and names what had to change before a government would compel a town to build a sewer."},{"h":"Jenner: prevention without an explanation","code":"11-3 — 3 Case studies: Key individual: Jenner and the development of vaccination","t":"Edward Jenner, a country doctor at Berkeley in Gloucestershire, noticed that milkmaids who caught cowpox did not catch smallpox. In May 1796 he put matter from the cowpox sores on the hand of the milkmaid Sarah Nelmes into scratches on the arm of James Phipps, an eight-year-old boy, and in July 1796 he exposed Phipps to smallpox, which did not develop. The Royal Society refused his paper, so he published An Inquiry into the Causes and Effects of the Variolae Vaccinae himself in 1798, and called the method vaccination after vacca, the Latin for cow. He could not say why it worked, because germ theory was sixty-five years away, which is exactly why inoculators, cartoonists and some doctors could attack it. What finished the argument was money and law: Parliament granted him 10,000 pounds in 1802 and 20,000 pounds in 1807, free infant vaccination came in 1840, the Vaccination Act of 1853 made it compulsory and the Act of 1871 appointed officers to enforce it. A top-level answer uses Jenner twice: as proof that prevention could run ahead of understanding, and as proof that an individual's discovery only becomes national prevention when the state pays for it and compels it."},{"h":"Cholera in London, 1854: the case that proves how evidence travels","code":"11-3 — 3 Case studies: Fighting Cholera in London (1854)","t":"Cholera reached Britain in 1831 and returned in 1848–49, 1853–54 and 1866. In the outbreak around Broad Street in Soho at the end of August 1854, more than five hundred people died within ten days. John Snow, a London doctor and anaesthetist who had given chloroform to Queen Victoria at the birth of Prince Leopold in 1853, had argued in a pamphlet of 1849 that cholera was swallowed rather than breathed. He interviewed households, mapped the deaths street by street and showed that almost all of them clustered on one water pump; the brewery workers who drank beer and the workhouse with its own well escaped, while a widow in Hampstead who had Broad Street water carted to her died. He had the pump handle removed and the outbreak faded, and later work with the Reverend Henry Whitehead traced the contamination to a leaking cesspit beside the well. The government's own enquiry still reported in 1855 that cholera came from foul air, and the cholera bacterium was only identified by Koch in 1883. A top-level answer therefore treats 1854 as proof that evidence alone does not change policy, and asks what else — the Great Stink, germ theory, the vote — had to arrive before it did."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 3: building one analysed difference across two periods","setup":"The question is: 'Explain one way in which the training of doctors in the years c1500–c1700 was different from the training of doctors in the years c1700–c1900.' Half the credit is for analysing the difference itself and half for the specific information that supports it from both periods, so you need one difference carried right across, not a list of several.","steps":["Settle on the difference that covers the whole of both periods rather than one school or one man: in the earlier period a physician was made by reading ancient texts at a university, while in the later one he was made at the bedside of patients and then tested by an examining body.","Evidence the earlier side with named detail: degrees were taken in Latin on the works of Galen and Hippocrates at Oxford, Cambridge and the continental universities, dissection was demonstrated to students rather than done by them, and the Royal College of Physicians, chartered in 1518, licensed physicians for London while surgeons and apothecaries learned by apprenticeship in their companies of 1540 and 1617.","Evidence the later side with dated detail: pupils paid to walk the wards of the London hospitals from the middle of the eighteenth century, the Apothecaries Act of 1815 required a five-year apprenticeship, six months in a hospital and a passed examination before a man could practise, and the Medical Act of 1858 set up the General Medical Council and a single register of the qualified.","Close with the sentence that turns two descriptions into one analysed difference: training had moved from the authority of a text to the evidence of the ward and the test of a national register, so the later doctor was judged by what he could do to a patient rather than by what he had read."],"cues":["Which single difference in how a doctor was made covers both periods?","What named detail shows how the earlier doctor was taught and licensed?","Which practices and Acts prove the later half of it?","What must the last sentence add that the two halves have not?"]},{"title":"Question 4: turning stimulus bullets into mechanisms, and adding a reason of your own","setup":"The question is: 'Explain why there was so little improvement in the ability to cure disease in Britain in the years c1700–c1900.' You may use the following in your answer: patent medicines; the identification of specific microbes. You must also use information of your own. The bullets are prompts, not a plan, and an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content cannot reach the top level.","steps":["Turn the first bullet into a mechanism rather than a label: patent remedies were sold by the million with nothing to prove, since no seller had to show that a mixture worked and many contained little beyond alcohol or opium, so the money in medicine flowed to advertising rather than to anything that cured a disease.","Do the same with the second bullet: naming an organism is not curing it. Koch identified the tuberculosis bacterium in 1882 and the cholera bacterium in 1883, yet neither gave a doctor anything to give a patient, and the first chemical designed to kill one named microbe inside the body, Ehrlich's Salvarsan, was not found until 1909.","Add the reason nothing has prompted: the few drugs that did work were found by chance or taken from plants rather than designed. William Withering published his account of the foxglove in 1785, morphine was isolated from opium in 1804 and quinine from cinchona bark in 1820, and acetylsalicylic acid was made in a stable form at Bayer in 1897 — useful against pain, fever and dropsy, but useless against the infections that killed most people.","Bind the three together into one line of reasoning instead of a list: an untested market, a theory that could name diseases long before it could touch them, and a pharmacy still built on chance meant that a doctor at the end of the century could identify the enemy and even prevent it while having almost nothing that killed it inside the patient — the clearest exception being the diphtheria antitoxin Emil von Behring first used successfully on a child in 1891."],"cues":["What was there to stop a seller whose remedy did nothing?","Why did naming a microbe not help the patient in the bed?","Where did the few effective drugs of the period actually come from?","What single line of reasoning ties the three together, and which exception must you concede?"]},{"title":"Question 5 or 6: testing a 'main reason' claim instead of agreeing with it","setup":"The question is: 'Chance played a greater part than scientific method in the medical breakthroughs that changed Britain in the years c1700–c1900.' How far do you agree? You may use the following in your answer: the discovery of chloroform (1847); Koch's laboratory methods. You must also use information of your own. A judgement is required, and the judgement is what separates the top level from a good explanation.","steps":["Set the criterion before arguing anything: a breakthrough counts as owed to chance only if the moment of discovery could not have been planned, and as owed to method if a trained routine would have produced it sooner or later, so every example has to be tested against that rather than simply told as a story.","Make the strongest case for the claim from the first bullet: James Simpson and two colleagues were sniffing bottles of chemicals in his own dining room in Edinburgh on the evening of 4 November 1847 when chloroform put them all on the floor, and Jenner's whole method began with the countryside observation that milkmaids who caught cowpox escaped smallpox.","Turn the second bullet against the claim: Koch's results came from a routine that anyone could copy — solid cultures, chemical dyes that stained one organism and photographs of what he saw — which is why the anthrax bacterium in 1876 and the tuberculosis bacterium in 1882 were followed by a stream of named microbes rather than by a single lucky find.","Add the case against from outside the bullets: John Snow explained cholera in 1854 by interviewing households and mapping the deaths street by street, and Joseph Lister dressed a compound fracture with carbolic acid in Glasgow in August 1865 because he had read Pasteur and reasoned that what stopped decay in sewage would stop it in a wound.","Write the judgement against the criterion you set, conceding the other side rather than ignoring it: chance supplied several of the starting points, but on the test set at the beginning it was method that turned them into medicine, because a milkmaid's hands and a bottle sniffed at a dinner table became vaccination and anaesthesia only once someone tested them deliberately and published the result."],"cues":["What has to be settled before chance and method can be compared at all?","Which story best supports the statement, and what makes it chance?","How does the second bullet work against the statement?","Which breakthroughs outside the bullets were worked for rather than stumbled on?","How can the conclusion give chance its due and still disagree?"]},{"title":"Question 3: proving a similarity across the centuries","setup":"The question is: 'Explain one way in which the part played by women in medicine in the years c1250–c1500 was similar to the part played by women in medicine in the years c1700–c1900.' One similarity only, analysed, with specific information from both periods.","steps":["Find the shared position underneath the two periods rather than a shared job: in each, women did most of the daily care of the sick while being kept out of the licensed profession that ranked above them.","Evidence the earlier period: women worked as midwives and as village healers with herbal remedies, and nuns nursed the sick in monastic hospitals, but the universities that trained physicians did not admit women, so none could join the ranks of the licensed.","Evidence the later period: nursing was still overwhelmingly women's work and Florence Nightingale's school at St Thomas' Hospital, opened in 1860, trained them for it, yet Elizabeth Garrett Anderson had to qualify through the Society of Apothecaries in 1865, which changed its rules immediately afterwards to shut the door behind her, and only the Act of 1876 let the licensing bodies admit women at all.","Close analytically: the similarity is one of status rather than of activity — in both periods women supplied the care while men supplied the licence, so what the answer names is a barrier, not a job."],"cues":["What do the two periods share once you look past the jobs themselves?","Which medieval detail shows women caring but unlicensed?","Which nineteenth-century detail shows the same barrier still standing?","Why is naming the shared status better than listing the shared work?"]},{"title":"Writing the conclusion of a judgement answer","setup":"The essay is: 'Improvements in medicine in Britain in the years c1700–c1900 owed more to new technology than to new ideas.' How far do you agree? You have already written three paragraphs — the instruments and engineering that made new practice possible, the ideas that changed what doctors believed about disease, and the places where the two met. Only the conclusion is left, and repeating the paragraphs will not earn the judgement mark.","steps":["Name the criterion the question will be decided on: a factor is the greater only if the improvement would not have happened without it, so the test is which of the two, taken away, would leave the change still standing.","Apply that test to the technology: microscopes good enough to show bacteria, chemical dyes that made them visible, sterilisable steel instruments and the steam pumping station Bazalgette opened at Crossness in 1865 all made new practice possible, yet each was turned to medicine only to serve a purpose someone had already conceived, and carbolic acid was in use on sewage before anyone thought of putting it on a wound.","Concede what is strongest in the statement so the judgement is weighed and not asserted: no idea could be proved without the means to prove it, since germ theory needed a lens that could show the organism and clean water needed engineering that could move it.","Write the judgement sentence itself, qualified and tied to the criterion: on the test of what could be removed, the new ideas were the greater factor, because the technology of an industrial century was aimed at disease only once a new belief about disease had told people what to look for, even though without that technology the belief would have stayed an argument."],"cues":["What must be settled before the two factors can be ranked?","What happens to the technology once you apply that test?","What is the strongest thing the technology side has going for it?","What shape must the final sentence take so the ranking is justified rather than asserted?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What did believers in spontaneous generation think the relationship between microbes and decay was?","a":"They thought decaying matter produced the microbes, rather than microbes causing the decay — Pasteur's germ theory of 1861 reversed it."},{"q":"Why was Koch's work necessary if Pasteur had already published germ theory?","a":"Pasteur showed that microbes cause disease in general; Koch tied particular microbes to particular diseases, naming the anthrax bacterium in 1876 and the tuberculosis bacterium in 1882, which is what made the theory usable."},{"q":"Why is 1846 to 1870 sometimes called the black period of surgery?","a":"Because anaesthetics let surgeons operate more deeply and for longer before infection was controlled, so death rates from sepsis rose before Lister's antiseptic methods brought them down."},{"q":"What was the key difference between the Public Health Acts of 1848 and 1875?","a":"The 1848 Act was permissive — towns could act if they chose — while the 1875 Act was compulsory, requiring every local authority to provide clean water, sewers and a medical officer of health."},{"q":"Why could Jenner's method be attacked as unscientific even though it worked?","a":"Because he could not explain why cowpox protected against smallpox; the germ theory that would explain it was not published until 1861, long after his death."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 1 Section B is worth 36 of the paper's 52 marks: Question 3 is 4 marks, Question 4 is 12 marks, and Question 5 or Question 6 is 16 marks plus up to 4 for spelling, punctuation, grammar and the use of specialist terminology.","Question 3, 'Explain one way in which … was similar to / different from …', carries 2 marks for AO2 and 2 for AO1, and normally spans at least a century, so both periods must be named and evidenced.","Question 4 stimulus caps: a maximum of 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","Question 5 or 6 stimulus caps: a maximum of 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; the fourth mark in each level is awarded for the judgement alone.","Timing on the 80-minute Paper 1: about a minute a mark — roughly 5 minutes on Question 3, 15 on Question 4 and 25 on Question 5 or 6, which is the only Section B answer where spelling and specialist terms are separately marked."],"questions":[{"q":"Explain one way in which ideas about the cause of disease in the years c1250–c1500 were similar to ideas about the cause of disease in the years c1700–c1900. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed similarity — belief in miasma persisting — supported by specific information from both periods, for example medieval fear of corrupted air and Chadwick's assumption in 1842 that foul air caused fever.","sol":"One similarity is that in both periods disease was blamed on bad air. Medieval physicians taught that miasma — foul air rising from cesspits, rubbish and corpses — poisoned the body, so sweet herbs were burned indoors and towns ordered filth out of the streets. The same explanation was still dominant far into the nineteenth century: Edwin Chadwick's report of 1842 assumed that the smells of overcrowded courts and open drains caused fever, and cholera was blamed on the air of the slums. In both periods, therefore, the cause was placed in the atmosphere around the patient rather than in any living organism, which is why prevention in both meant cleaning and sweetening the air.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Look for the explanation that survives right through the theme rather than one that changes.","A similarity needs the same idea proved twice, once in each period, with different named evidence.","Finish by saying where each period located the cause, so the marker sees analysis and not two descriptions."],"codes":["11-3 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Continuity and change in explanations of the cause of disease"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which surgery in the years c1500–c1700 was different from surgery in the years c1700–c1900. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — the control of pain and infection — supported by specific information from both periods, for example speed and cauterisation or ligatures against chloroform (1847) and carbolic acid (1865).","sol":"One difference is the control of pain and infection. Between c1500 and c1700 a surgeon had neither: patients were held or strapped down and operations were judged by speed, and although Ambroise Paré's silk ligatures tied off arteries rather than searing them shut, the ligature could carry infection into the wound. By c1900 both problems were being answered. James Simpson introduced chloroform in 1847, so a patient could be kept still and unconscious, and Joseph Lister's use of carbolic acid from 1865 killed germs in the wound and on the dressings. Surgery could therefore become slow and deep instead of a race against the patient's endurance.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what a surgeon in each period could not do to the patient before the knife went in.","Two problems, taken together, make a stronger single difference than either alone.","End on what the later surgeon could attempt that the earlier one could not, so the contrast is analysed."],"codes":["11-3 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which hospital care in the years c1250–c1500 was similar to hospital care in the years c1700–c1900. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed similarity — hospitals funded by charity rather than by the state, offering care rather than cure — supported by named examples from both periods, for example St Bartholomew's and the voluntary hospitals.","sol":"One similarity is that hospitals in both periods depended on charity rather than on the state. Medieval hospitals such as St Bartholomew's in London, founded in 1123 and still run by its priory in the fourteenth century, were endowed by the Church or by wealthy donors and offered rest, food, warmth and prayer far more often than cure. The voluntary hospitals of the eighteenth century worked in the same way: Guy's Hospital in London was paid for by the bequest of the bookseller Thomas Guy, who founded it in 1721 and endowed it by his will, and subscribers who gave money chose which patients filled the beds. In both periods, therefore, whether a sick person was admitted depended on somebody else's generosity rather than on any right to treatment.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about who paid for hospitals in each period before you think about what they did.","A similarity of funding leads straight to a similarity in who got through the door.","Close by naming what determined admission in both periods, so the comparison is analysed rather than listed."],"codes":["11-3 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which attempts to prevent the spread of the Black Death in 1348–49 were similar to attempts to prevent the spread of cholera in London in 1854. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed similarity — measures aimed at purifying the air because both diseases were blamed on miasma — supported by specific information from 1348–49 and from the London outbreak of 1854.","sol":"One similarity is that both responses were aimed at the air, because in each case the disease was blamed on miasma. Against the Black Death, towns paid rakers to clear the streets, ordered filth out of the ditches and burned sweet herbs and juniper indoors to purify the air. When cholera struck London in 1854 the official response was the same in kind: streets were washed and drains and cesspits cleansed, and the government's own enquiry reported that cholera rose from foul air, rejecting John Snow's evidence that it was carried in water. Both sets of measures therefore attacked the smell rather than the route of infection.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Both responses follow from a theory of cause that the two periods share.","Prove the shared theory once in each period with measures people actually took.","The last sentence should say what both sets of measures missed, which is what makes it analysis."],"codes":["11-3 — 3 Case studies: Fighting Cholera in London (1854)","11-3 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Continuity and change in explanations of the cause of disease"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the prevention of disease in the years c1500–c1700 was different from the prevention of disease in the years c1700–c1900. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — from avoidance and local emergency measures to vaccination and compulsory national legislation — supported by named detail such as the 1665 plague quarantine against Jenner (1796) and the Acts of 1853 and 1875.","sol":"One difference is that prevention became a national, legal matter. Between c1500 and c1700 prevention meant individuals avoiding bad air, with local emergency measures at most: during the Great Plague of 1665 infected houses in London were shut up for forty days and marked with a red cross, but nobody could be protected against a named disease in advance. In the years c1700–c1900 Edward Jenner's vaccination of James Phipps in 1796 gave real protection against smallpox, Parliament made infant vaccination compulsory in 1853, and the Public Health Act of 1875 forced every local authority to supply clean water and sewers. Prevention had moved from avoidance to compulsion.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask who was responsible for preventing disease in each period.","One clear medieval-style emergency measure set against one dated law makes the contrast sharp.","End with the change of agent — from the individual to the state — rather than listing what each period did."],"codes":["11-3 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: New approaches to prevention","11-3 — 3 Case studies: Key individual: Jenner and the development of vaccination"]},{"q":"Explain why ideas about the cause of disease and illness changed in the years c1700–c1900.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• spontaneous generation\n• Robert Koch\nYou must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit analysis of the failure of spontaneous generation and of Pasteur's germ theory (1861), of Koch's identification of specific microbes (1876, 1882), and of own points such as improved microscopes, Lister's practical proof from 1865 or the failure of miasma to explain the evidence. Stimulus caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Ideas about the cause of disease changed in this period because, for the first time, an explanation appeared that could be tested, and because the means existed to test it.\n\nThe first reason is that the reigning theory could be disproved. Educated opinion held that microbes in rotting matter were produced by the decay itself — spontaneous generation — and that disease came from miasma. Louis Pasteur, a chemist asked by industry to explain why wine, beer and vinegar soured, used sterilised liquid in swan-necked flasks to show that the liquid stayed clear while germs in the air were kept out of it, and published his germ theory in 1861. Once decay had a cause outside the decaying thing, the old theory had nothing left to stand on.\n\nThe second reason is that Robert Koch turned a general theory into a working method. He grew bacteria on solid agar, stained them with industrial dyes so they could be seen and photographed them through the microscope; he identified the bacterium that causes anthrax in 1876, the one that causes tuberculosis in 1882 and the one that causes cholera in 1883. That mattered because doctors could now be shown one named organism for one named disease, which miasma could never supply.\n\nThe third reason lies outside both bullets: the theory was carried into Britain by practice and by publicity. Joseph Lister applied germ theory to wounds with carbolic acid from 1865 and published his results, John Tyndall lectured in London on germs in the air in 1870, and improved microscopes had by then made bacteria visible to anyone who looked.\n\nThe reasons reinforce one another: Pasteur supplied the idea, Koch supplied the proof for particular diseases, and British surgeons supplied the demonstration that acting on the idea saved lives. Even so, change was slow — many British doctors still blamed miasma into the 1870s — which is why the period shows both change and stubborn continuity.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["The question is about why an explanation was replaced, so each paragraph must end on a cause of change, not on a description of a discovery.","Three developed reasons beat five listed ones, and at least one must come from outside the two bullets.","Turn each bullet into a mechanism — what it made possible that had been impossible — and close by showing the reasons working together."],"codes":["11-3 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Continuity and change in explanations of the cause of disease"]},{"q":"Explain why nursing in Britain changed in the years c1700–c1900.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Crimean War (1853–56)\n• Florence Nightingale\nYou must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit analysis of wartime scandal and press pressure, of Nightingale's training school (1860) and writings (1859), and of own points such as the growth of hospitals, the influence of germ theory on cleanliness or nursing as respectable work for women. Stimulus caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Nursing changed from untrained domestic work into a trained profession because a scandal made reform urgent, because one reformer knew exactly what to do with the money that followed, and because the hospitals of the period increasingly needed skilled staff.\n\nThe first reason is the Crimean War. Reports in The Times of the filthy, overcrowded Barrack Hospital at Scutari caused a public outcry at home, and Sidney Herbert, the Secretary at War, asked Florence Nightingale to take nurses out. She arrived in November 1854 with thirty-eight nurses and imposed cleanliness, ventilation, clean bedding, decent food and proper records; when a government sanitary commission cleared the drains and water supply in the spring of 1855 the death rate in the hospital fell steeply. War made nursing a national question rather than a private one.\n\nThe second reason is what Nightingale did with the fame that followed. A public subscription fund paid for training, and in 1860 the Nightingale School for Nurses opened at St Thomas' Hospital in London, sending out disciplined, examined nurses who then trained others. Her Notes on Nursing of 1859 set out how the sick should be kept, and Notes on Hospitals argued for clean, well-ventilated pavilion wards, so hospital buildings changed as well as their staff.\n\nThe third reason is not prompted at all: the hospitals themselves were changing. Voluntary hospitals, workhouse infirmaries and specialist hospitals were growing in number, surgery after chloroform in 1847 and Lister's carbolic acid from 1865 demanded clean wards and careful aftercare, and germ theory gave a scientific reason for the cleanliness Nightingale had demanded on the grounds of fresh air alone.\n\nThese causes reinforced each other: the war created the demand, Nightingale supplied the method, and the new surgery made trained nursing indispensable rather than merely respectable.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what made nursing a public question when it had always been private work.","Each paragraph should explain how a cause produced a change in who nursed, where and to what standard.","Bring in a reason from the hospitals and the operating theatre, then show the causes reinforcing one another."],"codes":["11-3 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment"]},{"q":"Explain why surgery became safer in the years c1700–c1900.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• chloroform\n• Joseph Lister\nYou must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit analysis of anaesthetics (Davy 1799, ether 1846, Simpson 1847, Victoria 1853), of antiseptic practice from 1865, and of own points such as germ theory, the move to asepsis in the 1890s or the black period of 1846–70. Stimulus caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Surgery became safer because the two things that killed surgical patients — shock from pain and infection of the wound — were attacked in turn, and because a theory arrived that explained why the second remedy worked.\n\nThe first reason is anaesthetics. Humphry Davy had noticed in 1799 that nitrous oxide dulled pain and surgeons had ignored it; ether was used in London by Robert Liston in 1846 but irritated the lungs and caught fire easily. James Simpson, professor of midwifery at Edinburgh, introduced chloroform in 1847, and when Queen Victoria accepted it at the birth of Prince Leopold in 1853 the remaining objections collapsed. A still, unconscious patient allowed careful work instead of speed.\n\nThe second reason is antiseptics. Joseph Lister, professor of surgery at Glasgow, had read Pasteur and used carbolic acid on a compound fracture in 1865, soaking dressings, instruments and later spraying the air; publishing the method in 1867 and its results in 1870, he reported that deaths after amputation on his wards fell from about forty-six per cent in 1864–66 to about fifteen in 1867–70. This attacked the cause of wound infection rather than its symptoms.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond the bullets: germ theory and the move to aseptic surgery. Pasteur's germ theory of 1861 explained why carbolic acid worked, which is what eventually persuaded surgeons who could not repeat Lister's results. By the 1890s surgeons had gone further, keeping germs out altogether with steam-sterilised instruments, scrubbed theatres, gowns, masks and rubber gloves.\n\nThe order matters, and a good answer says so. Between 1846 and 1870 anaesthetics alone made surgery more dangerous, because surgeons operated more deeply and for longer into unclean wounds; only when pain relief and infection control were combined, and then underpinned by germ theory, did surgery genuinely become safer.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Name the two things that actually killed surgical patients before you explain any remedy.","Each paragraph should explain how one remedy removed one killer, with dates for when it was accepted rather than invented.","Bring in the theory that explained the second remedy, and use the order of events as your line of reasoning."],"codes":["11-3 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment","11-3 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Continuity and change in explanations of the cause of disease"]},{"q":"Explain why the government took more action to improve public health in Britain in the years c1800–c1900.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the cholera epidemics\n• the Great Stink (1858)\nYou must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit analysis of repeated cholera epidemics and of the events of 1858, and of own points such as Chadwick's report of 1842, the failure of the permissive Act of 1848, germ theory, the Reform Act of 1867 and the compulsory Act of 1875. Stimulus caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Government moved from doing nothing to compelling every district to act because crises kept forcing its hand, and because the beliefs that had justified inaction gave way.\n\nThe first reason is cholera. It reached Britain in 1831 and returned in 1848–49, 1854 and 1866, killing quickly and killing the comfortable as well as the poor, since dirty water ran into wealthy streets too. Fear of a fresh epidemic produced the Public Health Act of 1848, which set up a General Board of Health and allowed towns to appoint a medical officer of health and build sewers; because it was permissive rather than compulsory, most towns did nothing, which is itself part of the explanation of why stronger action came later.\n\nThe second reason is the Great Stink of 1858. Hot weather left untreated sewage in the Thames stinking under the windows of the Palace of Westminster, and Parliament, which had ignored years of reports, voted the money for Joseph Bazalgette's sewers within weeks. More than a thousand miles of sewers followed, with about eighty miles of main intercepting sewers carrying waste downstream; the system opened in 1865 and was finished by 1875.\n\nThe third reason is the one the bullets do not prompt: evidence and politics changed together. Edwin Chadwick's report of 1842 had shown that the poor died far younger and that sickness fell on the rates, giving a financial argument to set against laissez-faire; John Snow's work in 1854 and Pasteur's germ theory of 1861 destroyed the claim that filth was merely unpleasant; and the Reform Act of 1867 gave many working men in the towns the vote, so the state of their streets became an election question.\n\nThe chain ends in the Public Health Act of 1875, which required every local authority to appoint a medical officer of health and provide clean water, sewers and drainage. Each crisis bought a temporary remedy; only when evidence, attitudes and votes had all shifted did Parliament compel.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what had to happen to the people who made laws before they were willing to spend ratepayers' money.","Explain why an early Act achieved so little, because that is part of why later action was stronger.","Add a cause from outside the bullets that changed attitudes, and end at the compulsory Act as the point the chain reaches."],"codes":["11-3 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: New approaches to prevention","11-3 — 3 Case studies: Fighting Cholera in London (1854)"]},{"q":"Explain why the use of vaccination against smallpox increased in Britain in the years c1700–c1900.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• Edward Jenner\n• the Vaccination Act of 1853\nYou must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit analysis of Jenner's proof and publication (1796, 1798), of compulsion and enforcement (1840, 1853, 1871), and of own points such as government grants, falling smallpox deaths or germ theory finally explaining why vaccination worked. Stimulus caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Vaccination spread because one doctor proved it worked, because the state then paid for it and finally compelled it, and because the evidence of falling deaths outlasted the objections.\n\nThe first reason is Jenner's proof. Working at Berkeley in Gloucestershire, he noticed that milkmaids who had caught cowpox did not catch smallpox; in May 1796 he put matter from the cowpox sores on the hand of Sarah Nelmes into scratches on the arm of James Phipps, an eight-year-old boy, and in July exposed him to smallpox, which did not develop. The Royal Society refused his paper, so he published An Inquiry into the Causes and Effects of the Variolae Vaccinae at his own expense in 1798. A method that could be copied by any doctor, using material that was easy to obtain, could spread quickly.\n\nThe second reason is government action. Parliament granted Jenner ten thousand pounds in 1802 and twenty thousand in 1807, which gave the method official standing; vaccination of infants was made free in 1840 and inoculation with smallpox itself banned; the Vaccination Act of 1853 made vaccination of infants compulsory; and the Act of 1871 required local authorities to appoint vaccination officers to enforce it. Compulsion reached the families that persuasion had not.\n\nThe third reason lies outside both bullets. Vaccination worked, and the visible fall in smallpox deaths answered the practical objection; the scientific objection, that Jenner could not explain why cowpox protected anyone, was finally met when Pasteur's germ theory of 1861 and his later vaccines against anthrax in 1881 and rabies in 1885 showed what a vaccine actually does.\n\nOpposition never disappeared — an Anti-Vaccination League campaigned against compulsion and from 1898 parents could claim conscientious objection — but by then the pattern was set: an individual's discovery became national prevention only once government funded it, compelled it and could explain it.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Separate the discovery from its spread: they have different causes.","Explain what changed when Parliament moved from paying for vaccination to ordering it.","Add the reason the bullets miss — what finally answered the objection that nobody could say why it worked — and keep opposition in view."],"codes":["11-3 — 3 Case studies: Key individual: Jenner and the development of vaccination","11-3 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: New approaches to prevention"]},{"q":"'Germ theory was the main reason for improvements in medicine in Britain in the years c1700–c1900.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• Louis Pasteur\n• anaesthetics\nYou must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit weighing of germ theory (1861, Koch 1876 and 1882, Lister from 1865) against anaesthetics (1847, 1853), vaccination (1796) and public health legislation (1842–75), with a criterion-based judgement. Stimulus caps: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Whether germ theory was the main reason for improvement depends on what improvement is measured by. If it means a better understanding of disease, the statement is unanswerable; if it means a sick person in Britain being less likely to die before 1900, it is much weaker. I shall judge by the second test, because that is what medicine is for.\n\nThe case for the statement is strong in the long run. Louis Pasteur published his germ theory in 1861, having shown with swan-necked flasks that sterilised liquid stayed clear while germs were kept out of it, and Robert Koch made the theory usable by naming the bacterium that causes anthrax in 1876, tuberculosis in 1882 and cholera in 1883. That knowledge did real work in Britain: Joseph Lister had read Pasteur when he used carbolic acid on a compound fracture in 1865, and in 1870 he reported that deaths after amputation on his wards had fallen from about forty-six per cent in 1864–66 to about fifteen in 1867–70. By the 1890s the same theory had produced aseptic surgery, with steam-sterilised instruments, gowns and rubber gloves. No other idea in the period explained so much.\n\nYet the first great improvement in surgery owed germ theory nothing at all. James Simpson introduced chloroform in 1847 and Queen Victoria accepted it from John Snow at the birth of Prince Leopold in 1853 — fourteen years before Lister published, and before germ theory existed. Anaesthetics were discovered by trial on human volunteers, not deduced from a theory, and they made operations bearable for every patient in the country.\n\nBeyond the stimulus, two further improvements ran ahead of germ theory. Edward Jenner prevented smallpox from 1796, without any idea why cowpox protected anybody, and Parliament made infant vaccination compulsory in 1853, eight years before Pasteur published. Public health followed the same pattern: Edwin Chadwick's report of 1842 was written from miasma theory, the Great Stink of 1858 rather than any laboratory drove Parliament to fund Bazalgette's sewers, and the compulsory Public Health Act of 1875 owed as much to the Reform Act of 1867 as to bacteriology. On the numbers, clean water and drains saved far more British lives in this period than germ theory did.\n\nIt is also true that germ theory cured almost nobody before 1900. It named diseases it could not treat, and British doctors were slow to accept it, many still blaming miasma into the 1870s.\n\nI therefore agree only in part. Judged by lives saved within the period, the main reasons for improvement were compulsion and cleanliness in public health and the two surgical revolutions of anaesthetics and antisepsis. Judged by what made further improvement possible, germ theory is decisive, because it converted practices that worked by luck into knowledge that could be extended. Germ theory was the most important cause of what came next; it was not the main cause of what changed for patients before 1900.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Before you agree or disagree, decide what an improvement in medicine would be measured by.","Set the dated evidence for germ theory against improvements that happened before 1861, and let the dates do the arguing.","Find a factor neither bullet mentions, then build a conclusion that splits the question by criterion rather than sitting on the fence."],"codes":["11-3 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Continuity and change in explanations of the cause of disease","11-3 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment","11-3 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: New approaches to prevention"]},{"q":"'Individuals were the main reason for improvements in the prevention of disease in Britain in the years c1700–c1900.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• Edward Jenner\n• the Public Health Act (1875)\nYou must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit weighing of individuals (Jenner, Snow, Chadwick, Bazalgette, Pasteur) against government action (1840, 1853, 1848, 1866, 1875), attitudes and crises, judged against a stated criterion such as reaching a whole population. Stimulus caps: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Prevention means protecting a whole population, not one patient, so the fair test of this statement is whether individuals or something else made protection reach everybody. That is the criterion I shall use.\n\nIndividuals certainly supplied every idea worth having. Edward Jenner noticed that milkmaids who caught cowpox escaped smallpox, vaccinated James Phipps in May 1796 with matter from Sarah Nelmes's cowpox sores, exposed him to smallpox in July and published An Inquiry into the Causes and Effects of the Variolae Vaccinae at his own expense in 1798 after the Royal Society refused it. John Snow mapped the deaths in Soho in 1854, showed that more than five hundred people had died within ten days around a single water pump and had the handle removed. Edwin Chadwick's report of 1842 proved that the poor died young and that sickness fell on the rates. Without these people there would have been nothing for anyone to enforce.\n\nBut an idea is not prevention until it reaches everybody, and only government could make it. Jenner's method was known from 1798, yet smallpox vaccination became free only in 1840, compulsory only under the Vaccination Act of 1853, and effective only when the Act of 1871 required local authorities to appoint vaccination officers to enforce it. Snow's evidence was rejected by the government's own enquiry, which reported in 1855 that cholera came from foul air. The Public Health Act of 1848 was permissive, so most towns did nothing; the Act of 1875 was compulsory, obliging every local authority to appoint a medical officer of health and an inspector of nuisances and to supply clean water, sewers and drainage. That Act, not a discovery, is why prevention became universal.\n\nBeyond both bullets, the deeper cause is the change in attitudes and politics that let government act at all. Laissez-faire had made interference seem improper and expensive; repeated cholera epidemics in 1831, 1848–49, 1854 and 1866 frightened rich and poor alike; the Great Stink of 1858 moved Parliament to fund Bazalgette's sewers within weeks when years of argument had not; and the Reform Act of 1867 gave many urban working men the vote, which turned slum conditions into an election issue. Individuals worked when these conditions were present and were ignored when they were not — which is exactly the difference between Chadwick in 1842 and the legislation of 1875.\n\nI therefore disagree, with one important concession. Individuals were necessary: no Act could have compelled a vaccination nobody had invented, and no engineer would have been funded had nobody costed the filth. But measured by whether protection reached a whole population, government action was decisive, because only the state could pay for sewers, compel parents and inspect every district — and government acted only when attitudes, evidence and votes had shifted underneath it.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first what would count as prevention succeeding, since that is what the judgement has to be measured against.","For each individual, ask what happened to their idea in the years immediately afterwards.","Bring in the change in attitudes and politics that neither bullet mentions, and make the conclusion concede the strongest part of the statement before deciding against it."],"codes":["11-3 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: New approaches to prevention","11-3 — 3 Case studies: Key individual: Jenner and the development of vaccination","11-3 — 3 Case studies: Fighting Cholera in London (1854)"]},{"q":"'There was more change in the prevention of disease than in the treatment of disease in Britain in the years c1700–c1900.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• vaccination\n• hospital care\nYou must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit weighing of prevention (1796, 1853, 1848, 1875, clean water) against treatment and care (Nightingale, 1860, chloroform 1847, Lister 1865) and the continuity of medicines, with a criterion-based judgement. Stimulus caps: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"This question turns on what counts as treatment. If treatment means where and how a patient was cared for, it changed as much as prevention did; if it means the ability to cure the disease a patient actually had, it barely changed at all. I shall keep the two apart and judge on the second.\n\nPrevention was transformed. In 1700 it meant avoiding bad air. Edward Jenner's vaccination, first tried on James Phipps in 1796 and published in 1798, gave real protection against smallpox; it was made free at public expense in 1840, compulsory by the Vaccination Act of 1853 and enforced by appointed officers from 1871. Public health went the same way: Chadwick's report of 1842, the permissive Public Health Act of 1848, Bazalgette's London sewers funded after the Great Stink of 1858 and finished by 1875, and the compulsory Public Health Act of that year, which obliged every authority to supply clean water and sewers and to appoint a medical officer of health. London suffered no great cholera epidemic after 1866.\n\nCare changed dramatically too, which is the strongest case against the statement. Florence Nightingale reached Scutari in November 1854 with thirty-eight nurses, published Notes on Nursing in 1859 and opened the Nightingale School at St Thomas' Hospital in 1860, so nursing became trained, disciplined work and hospitals were rebuilt as clean, ventilated pavilion wards. Surgery was transformed twice over: chloroform from 1847 removed pain, and Lister's carbolic acid from 1865 attacked infection, with deaths after amputation on his wards falling from about forty-six per cent to about fifteen; by the 1890s aseptic theatres allowed operations inside the abdomen that would have been fatal a century earlier.\n\nYet treatment in the strict sense — what a doctor could give you to cure an illness — stood remarkably still, and this is the point that decides the question. Bleeding and purging faded, but the pharmacy of 1900 could still cure very few diseases; germ theory named tuberculosis in 1882 without being able to treat it, patent medicines were sold by the million on no evidence at all, and the first drug designed to kill a specific microbe inside the body came only after this period ended. A patient with an infection in 1900, nursed in a clean ward by a trained nurse, was still largely left to their own recovery.\n\nI therefore agree, but only once treatment and care are separated. Measured by what medicine could do to an illness, prevention changed out of all recognition while cure did not: a child in 1900 could be protected from smallpox and given clean water, but could not be cured of tuberculosis or cholera. Measured by how the sick were cared for and operated on, change was just as great — which is why the statement is true of the doctor's bag and only half true of the hospital.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["The word 'treatment' hides two different things — decide which one you are judging before you start.","Give the strongest version of the case against you, with names and dates, rather than a token paragraph.","Look for what did not change in this period, and let that continuity carry the judgement."],"codes":["11-3 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment","11-3 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: New approaches to prevention","11-3 — 3 Case studies: Key individual: Jenner and the development of vaccination"]},{"q":"'Medicine in Britain changed more in the years c1700–c1900 than in the years c1500–c1700.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• William Harvey\n• Louis Pasteur\nYou must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit comparison across the two periods: Vesalius (1543), Paré, Harvey (1628), Sydenham and the Royal Society against germ theory (1861), vaccination, anaesthetics, antiseptics and public health, judged on a stated criterion. Stimulus caps: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Comparing two periods needs one test applied to both. I shall ask what difference each period made to an ordinary sick person's chances, and then ask a second question — which period changed the way medical knowledge was produced — because the two tests do not give the same answer.\n\nOn the first test, c1700–c1900 wins easily. The cause of disease was finally explained: Pasteur published his germ theory in 1861 and Koch named the bacteria of anthrax in 1876 and tuberculosis in 1882. Prevention became real and then compulsory: Jenner vaccinated James Phipps in 1796, infant vaccination was made compulsory in 1853, and the Public Health Act of 1875 obliged every local authority to provide clean water, sewers and a medical officer of health. Surgery was made bearable by chloroform in 1847 and survivable by Lister's carbolic acid from 1865. Nursing became a trained profession after Nightingale's school opened at St Thomas' Hospital in 1860.\n\nBy comparison the earlier period changed ideas far more than it changed outcomes. Vesalius corrected Galen's anatomy in 1543, Paré improved the treatment of wounds, and William Harvey proved in 1628 that the blood circulates and that the heart is a pump, demolishing Galen's claim that blood was made in the liver and burned up. These were great discoveries, but they cured nobody: physicians went on bleeding and purging their patients, and when plague returned to London in 1665 the response — shutting up infected houses, marking doors, burning fires to clean the air — would have been recognised by anyone living through the Black Death three centuries earlier.\n\nThe strongest argument for the earlier period lies beyond both bullets, and must be given its weight. Between c1500 and c1700 the method changed. Printing spread illustrated anatomy across Europe, dissection became normal in training, Thomas Sydenham insisted on observing patients rather than trusting books, and the founding of the Royal Society in 1660 gave experiment an institution and a journal. The later period could only make its discoveries because authority had already been replaced by observation and proof.\n\nIt is also fair to note that the later period's changes took decades to reach patients: many British doctors still blamed miasma into the 1870s, and the Act of 1848 achieved little because it was permissive.\n\nI nevertheless agree with the statement. On the test that matters most — whether people were healthier, protected and safer under the knife — c1700–c1900 changed medicine beyond recognition, while c1500–c1700 left everyday practice much as it found it. The earlier period deserves the credit for changing how knowledge was made; the later period is where that change finally reached the patient.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["A comparison needs one test applied to both periods, not a list about each in turn.","The earlier period is strongest on how knowledge was produced rather than on what happened to patients.","Decide which test the question is really about, and let the conclusion say why that test is the fair one."],"codes":["11-3 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Continuity and change in explanations of the cause of disease","11-3 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment","11-3 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: New approaches to prevention"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In which year did Louis Pasteur publish his germ theory?","a":"1861."},{"q":"Which eight-year-old boy did Edward Jenner vaccinate with cowpox matter in May 1796?","a":"James Phipps."},{"q":"What did John Snow have removed from the Broad Street pump in 1854?","a":"Its handle."},{"q":"Which Public Health Act made it compulsory for every local authority to appoint a medical officer of health and provide clean water and sewers?","a":"The Public Health Act of 1875."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Spontaneous generation","code":"11-3 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Continuity and change in explanations of the cause of disease","open":false,"back":"The belief, standard before 1861, that the microbes found in decaying matter were created by the decay itself. Pasteur reversed it: the microbes came from the air and caused the decay."},{"front":"Pasteur's Germ Theory (1861)","code":"11-3 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Continuity and change in explanations of the cause of disease","open":false,"back":"Louis Pasteur, investigating why wine and beer soured, used swan-necked flasks to show sterilised liquid stays clear if germs are kept out, and argued that microbes in the air cause decay and disease."},{"front":"Robert Koch","code":"11-3 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Continuity and change in explanations of the cause of disease","open":false,"back":"German doctor who linked particular microbes to particular diseases using solid agar cultures, dyes and photography: the anthrax bacterium in 1876, tuberculosis in 1882 and cholera in 1883."},{"front":"Miasma in the nineteenth century","code":"11-3 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Continuity and change in explanations of the cause of disease","open":false,"back":"The old belief that foul air caused disease survived into the 1870s. Chadwick's report of 1842 assumed it, and the government enquiry into the 1854 cholera outbreak concluded it."},{"front":"Florence Nightingale at Scutari","code":"11-3 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment","open":false,"back":"She reached the Barrack Hospital in November 1854 with 38 nurses in the Crimean War, imposing cleanliness, ventilation, good food and records; the death rate fell steeply after the drains were cleared in 1855."},{"front":"The Nightingale School for Nurses","code":"11-3 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment","open":false,"back":"Opened in 1860 at St Thomas' Hospital, London, paid for by public subscription. It produced trained, disciplined, examined nurses who went on to run wards and found other schools."},{"front":"Chloroform","code":"11-3 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment","open":false,"back":"Anaesthetic introduced by James Simpson, professor of midwifery at Edinburgh, in 1847. Queen Victoria accepted it from John Snow at the birth of Prince Leopold in 1853, which silenced most objections."},{"front":"Hannah Greener","code":"11-3 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment","open":false,"back":"A fifteen-year-old who died under chloroform in 1848 during an operation on a toenail: the first recorded death from an anaesthetic, and the standard evidence that early dosage was guesswork."},{"front":"Lister's use of carbolic acid","code":"11-3 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment","open":false,"back":"From 1865 Joseph Lister soaked dressings, instruments and ligatures in carbolic acid. He announced the method in 1867; in 1870 he reported deaths after amputation on his wards falling from about 46 per cent (1864–66) to about 15 (1867–70)."},{"front":"Aseptic surgery","code":"11-3 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment","open":false,"back":"By the 1890s surgeons kept germs out rather than killing them in the wound: steam-sterilised instruments, scrubbed theatres, gowns, masks and rubber gloves."},{"front":"Vaccination","code":"11-3 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: New approaches to prevention","open":false,"back":"Protection against a disease given by deliberately introducing a milder related material — named by Jenner after vacca, Latin for cow. Made free for infants in 1840 and compulsory in 1853."},{"front":"Edwin Chadwick's report of 1842","code":"11-3 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: New approaches to prevention","open":false,"back":"His Report on the Sanitary Condition of the Labouring Population showed that the poor died far younger and that sickness fell on the rates, urging drainage, clean water and refuse removal."},{"front":"The Public Health Act of 1875","code":"11-3 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: New approaches to prevention","open":false,"back":"Compulsory, unlike the permissive Act of 1848. Every local authority had to appoint a medical officer of health and an inspector of nuisances and to provide clean water, sewers and drainage."},{"front":"Jenner and James Phipps","code":"11-3 — 3 Case studies: Key individual: Jenner and the development of vaccination","open":false,"back":"In May 1796 Jenner put cowpox matter from milkmaid Sarah Nelmes into scratches on eight-year-old James Phipps; in July he exposed him to smallpox and the boy did not catch it."},{"front":"The Broad Street pump, 1854","code":"11-3 — 3 Case studies: Fighting Cholera in London (1854)","open":false,"back":"John Snow mapped over 500 cholera deaths in ten days in Soho onto one water pump, had its handle removed and the outbreak faded; a leaking cesspit beside the well was later blamed."}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 1 Question 5, 16 marks: 'Opposition to new ideas was the main reason why medical progress in Britain was slow in the years c1700–c1900.')","items":["Opposition was real and organised, but it cannot be the main reason for slow progress, because the heaviest brake on medicine in this period was the absence of anything to oppose. If slowness is measured by the gap between what was possible and what was actually done, opposition fills only part of that gap. It fills most of it over vaccination: the Vaccination Act of 1853 made infant vaccination compulsory and an Anti-Vaccination League was formed against it at once, an Anti-Compulsory Vaccination League followed in 1867 when compulsion was extended to older children, a demonstration at Leicester in 1885 marched behind a child's coffin and an effigy of Jenner, and Parliament gave way in 1898 by letting objecting parents claim exemption. Joseph Lister met the same resistance: he published his antiseptic results in The Lancet in 1867, yet surgeons found carbolic acid unpleasant and slow, several could not reproduce his figures, and the reports sent in from the London teaching hospitals over the next two years ran from enthusiasm to outright abandonment. Against this stands the fact that opposition folded quickly once evidence became undeniable, and that the longest delays had quite different causes: the sixty-five years between Jenner's vaccination of James Phipps in 1796 and Pasteur's germ theory of 1861 were lost not to opponents but to the lack of any explanation; the permissive Public Health Act of 1848 failed chiefly because sewers cost money that ratepayers would not vote; and when the Great Stink of 1858 frightened Parliament itself, the money for Bazalgette's sewers was found within weeks. Opposition, then, delayed particular measures rather than progress as a whole. On the criterion of the gap between the possible and the done, ignorance and cost were the main reasons medicine moved slowly, with opposition a real but secondary brake — though in the single case of vaccination it was strong enough to change the law.","Why this reaches Level 4: the explanation is directed consistently at the conceptual focus — every sentence is weighing reasons for slowness rather than describing protests; the information is precisely selected and dated (1853, 1867, 1885, 1898, 1796, 1861, 1848, 1858) instead of general; the counter-argument is put in its strongest form, on vaccination and on Lister, and then answered rather than mentioned and dropped; and the criterion for the judgement — the gap between what was possible and what was done — is named at the start and applied at the end, which is what the third bullet point of the Level 4 descriptor rewards."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Opposition to new ideas was the main reason why medical progress was slow because a lot of people did not like change. Many parents did not want their children vaccinated and they formed leagues and held protests against the law, so smallpox was not wiped out as quickly as it should have been. Doctors also did not believe Lister at first and carried on operating in their old coats without washing, which meant that patients kept dying of infection when they did not need to. There were other reasons as well, such as there not being enough money to build sewers and nobody understanding what really caused disease. Overall opposition was the main reason because if everyone had accepted the new ideas straight away then medicine would have improved much faster.","Why this is only Level 2, and what it would take to move up: it meets the Level 2 descriptor — 'an explanation is given showing limited analysis and with implicit or unsustained links to the conceptual focus', with 'accurate and relevant information' and an overall judgement 'given but its justification is asserted'. It misses Level 3 and Level 4 because no criterion for 'main reason' is ever set, so the judgement in the last sentence is only repeated, not justified; the evidence carries no dates or named groups beyond Lister, where Level 4 requires information 'precisely selected to address the question directly'; and the other reasons are listed in one sentence and abandoned, so nothing is weighed. Adding the compulsory Vaccination Act of 1853, the Leicester demonstration of 1885, the conscience clause of 1898 and Lister's Lancet papers of 1867, and then asking whether those delays were longer than the sixty-five years spent waiting for germ theory, would turn the same material into a Level 4 argument."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Key individual: Jenner and the development of vaccination","items":["Edward Jenner (1749–1823) was a country doctor at Berkeley in Gloucestershire who had trained in London under the surgeon John Hunter.","Smallpox was one of the great killers, disfiguring survivors; the only protection before Jenner was inoculation (variolation), giving a mild dose of smallpox itself, which could kill the patient and spread the disease.","Jenner noticed that milkmaids who had caught cowpox did not catch smallpox.","In May 1796 he inserted matter from cowpox sores on the hand of Sarah Nelmes, a milkmaid, into scratches on the arm of James Phipps, an eight-year-old boy.","In July 1796 he exposed Phipps to smallpox matter; Phipps did not develop the disease. He repeated the test on further cases.","The Royal Society refused his paper, so in 1798 he published An Inquiry into the Causes and Effects of the Variolae Vaccinae at his own expense.","He named the method vaccination, from vacca, the Latin word for cow.","He could not explain why it worked: germ theory was not published until 1861, thirty-eight years after his death.","Opposition came from inoculators who lost their income, from doctors who could not repeat his results because they contaminated their samples, from those who thought putting animal material into people was wrong, and from cartoonists — James Gillray's cartoon of 1802 showed vaccinated patients sprouting cows.","Parliament granted Jenner £10,000 in 1802 and a further £20,000 in 1807 to promote vaccination.","1840: vaccination was made free for infants and inoculation with smallpox was banned.","1853: the Vaccination Act made vaccination of infants compulsory, with fines for parents who refused.","1871: a further Act required local authorities to appoint vaccination officers to enforce it; an anti-vaccination movement campaigned against compulsion, and from 1898 parents could claim conscientious objection.","Significance: the first safe protection against a specific disease; proof that prevention could work without knowing the cause; and the first time government in Britain compelled a medical procedure. Smallpox was finally declared eradicated worldwide by the World Health Organization in 1980."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Case study: fighting cholera in London, 1854, and the significance of Snow and the Broad Street pump","items":["Cholera reached Britain for the first time in 1831; major epidemics followed in 1831–32, 1848–49, 1853–54 and 1866.","Symptoms: violent diarrhoea and vomiting, rapid loss of fluid, skin turning blue-grey; death could follow within a day.","In 1854 the government, most doctors and most newspapers blamed miasma — foul air from the slums.","John Snow (1813–58) was a London doctor and the leading anaesthetist of the day; he gave chloroform to Queen Victoria at the birth of Prince Leopold in 1853.","In 1849 he published On the Mode of Communication of Cholera, arguing that cholera attacked the gut because it was swallowed in water, not breathed in.","At the end of August 1854 an outbreak around Broad Street in Soho killed more than 500 people within ten days.","Snow interviewed households, plotted every death on a street map and found that almost all of them clustered around one water pump in Broad Street.","The exceptions supported him: workmen at the nearby brewery, who drank beer, and the workhouse, which had its own well, largely escaped, while a widow in Hampstead who had Broad Street water carted to her died of cholera.","He persuaded the local parish authorities to remove the pump handle, and the outbreak faded.","Later work with the Reverend Henry Whitehead traced the contamination to a cesspit leaking into the well beside the pump.","The government's Committee for Scientific Enquiry reported in 1855 that cholera was caused by foul air, and rejected Snow's explanation; the cholera bacterium was identified only by Koch in 1883.","Snow also compared districts supplied by different water companies, showing that those taking water from the polluted lower Thames suffered far more deaths.","Significance: statistical proof of a water-borne disease years before any germ could be seen; a powerful argument for clean water and sewers; but no immediate change of government policy, which is the point most judgement questions turn on."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Pasteur's Germ Theory, Koch's work on microbes, and their influence in Britain","items":["Before 1861 educated opinion held to spontaneous generation: that microbes in decaying matter were produced by the decay, not the cause of it.","Louis Pasteur (1822–95) was a French chemist asked by industry to explain why wine, beer and vinegar soured.","His swan-necked flask experiments showed that boiled, sterilised liquid stayed clear while germs from the air were kept out of it, and spoiled as soon as they reached it.","He published his germ theory in 1861: microbes in the air cause decay, and by extension disease.","Pasteur also developed pasteurisation, and later vaccines against chicken cholera in 1879, anthrax (a public trial at Pouilly-le-Fort in 1881) and rabies in 1885.","Robert Koch (1843–1910), a German doctor, set out to prove that one specific microbe caused one specific disease.","His methods: growing bacteria on solid agar in dishes, staining them with industrial dyes so they could be seen, and photographing them through the microscope.","He identified the bacterium causing anthrax in 1876, tuberculosis in 1882 and cholera in 1883; his methods let others name the microbes of typhoid, diphtheria, tetanus and plague within about twenty years.","Influence in Britain: Joseph Lister applied germ theory to wounds with carbolic acid from 1865 and published in 1867; John Tyndall lectured in London on germs in the air in 1870.","Resistance in Britain was strong: many doctors still explained epidemics by miasma into the 1870s, and germ theory produced almost no cure before 1900.","Where it did change practice quickly: antiseptic and then aseptic surgery, hospital and nursing hygiene, and the scientific argument used by public health reformers for the compulsory Public Health Act of 1875."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — The influence of Nightingale on nursing and hospitals in Britain","items":["Florence Nightingale (1820–1910) trained at Kaiserswerth in Germany in 1851, against her family's wishes; nursing was then unskilled work with a bad reputation.","Reports in The Times on the filthy Barrack Hospital at Scutari during the Crimean War (1853–56) caused a public outcry; Sidney Herbert, the Secretary at War, asked her to take nurses out.","She arrived at Scutari in November 1854 with 38 nurses.","She insisted on cleanliness, ventilation, clean bedding and clothing, decent food, sanitation and careful records; after a government sanitary commission cleared the drains and water supply in the spring of 1855 the death rate in the hospital fell steeply.","Newspaper accounts of her night rounds made her famous as 'the Lady with the Lamp'.","A public subscription — the Nightingale Fund — raised money for training when she returned.","In 1859 she published Notes on Nursing, on how the sick should be cared for, and Notes on Hospitals, which argued for clean, well-ventilated, pavilion-style wards with separated patients.","In 1860 the Nightingale School for Nurses opened at St Thomas' Hospital, London; its trained, examined nurses went on to run wards and to found training schools elsewhere.","She believed in miasma rather than germs, so her insistence on fresh air and cleanliness produced the right results for a reason later shown to be wrong.","Hospital provision in the period: voluntary hospitals paid for by subscription and legacies, such as Guy's Hospital in London founded in 1721 by the bookseller Thomas Guy and endowed by his will; workhouse infirmaries after the Poor Law Amendment Act of 1834; specialist and fever hospitals; and cottage hospitals staffed by local doctors from the late 1850s.","By 1900 a hospital was a place of treatment with trained nurses, operating theatres and doctors in charge, rather than a shelter for the sick poor; state registration of nurses followed in 1919."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — The impact of anaesthetics and antiseptics on surgery","items":["Before the 1840s surgery was fast and brutal: patients were held or strapped down, alcohol, opium and mandrake were the only pain relief, and the main killers were pain and shock, blood loss and infection.","1799: Humphry Davy found that nitrous oxide (laughing gas) removed pain and published the observation, but surgeons did not take it up.","1846: ether was used in London by Robert Liston to amputate a leg; it worked, but irritated the lungs, made patients vomit and was highly flammable.","1847: James Simpson, professor of midwifery at Edinburgh, discovered the effects of chloroform by testing it on himself and his friends, and used it in childbirth.","1848: Hannah Greener, aged fifteen, died under chloroform during an operation on an ingrown toenail — the first recorded death from an anaesthetic; doses were judged by guesswork.","Objections to anaesthetics: some argued pain in childbirth was God's will, some surgeons thought pain kept patients alert and alive, and deaths like Greener's were widely reported.","1853: Queen Victoria accepted chloroform from John Snow at the birth of Prince Leopold, after which opposition largely collapsed.","1846–70 is called the 'black period' of surgery: anaesthetics allowed deeper, longer operations while infection was still uncontrolled, so death rates from sepsis rose.","Joseph Lister (1827–1912), professor of surgery at Glasgow, read Pasteur and reasoned that microbes in the air infected wounds; carbolic acid was already used to treat sewage.","1865: Lister used carbolic acid on a compound fracture; he soaked dressings, instruments and ligatures, and later sprayed it into the air of the operating theatre.","1867: he published the antiseptic method in The Lancet; in 1870 he reported there that deaths after amputation on his wards had fallen from about 46 per cent (1864–66) to about 15 per cent (1867–70).","Opposition to Lister: carbolic acid cracked and stung the skin, the spray slowed operations, surgeons who ignored his exact method could not repeat his results, and many did not yet accept germ theory.","By the 1890s antisepsis had become asepsis — keeping germs out altogether: steam-sterilised instruments, scrubbed theatres, surgical gowns, masks and rubber gloves.","Result: operations inside the abdomen that would have been fatal a century earlier had become possible by 1900, although blood loss remained unsolved until blood groups were understood after this period."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Public health, the government and the Public Health Act (1875)","items":["Industrial towns grew faster than any provision for them: back-to-back housing, cellar dwellings, shared privies and cesspits, water from standpipes or from polluted rivers.","The ruling attitude was laissez-faire: government should not interfere in people's lives or spend ratepayers' money on them.","1842: Edwin Chadwick, secretary to the Poor Law Commission, published his Report on the Sanitary Condition of the Labouring Population of Great Britain, showing that the poor died far younger than the rich and that sickness fell on the poor rates; he urged drainage, clean water, removal of refuse and a medical officer for each district.","1848: the Public Health Act set up a General Board of Health and allowed — but did not require — towns to set up a local board of health, appoint a medical officer of health and build sewers; a board had to be created where the death rate exceeded 23 per 1,000. Chadwick was forced out in 1854 and the Board was wound up in 1858.","1854: John Snow's work on the Broad Street pump gave evidence that cholera travelled in water; the government's enquiry rejected it the following year.","1858: the Great Stink — hot weather made the untreated sewage in the Thames unbearable at the Palace of Westminster, and Parliament voted funds for a new sewer system within weeks.","Joseph Bazalgette, chief engineer of the Metropolitan Board of Works, built more than 1,000 miles of sewers, including about 80 miles of main intercepting sewers carrying waste downstream of the city; the main system opened in 1865 and the scheme was completed by 1875.","1866: the Sanitary Act forced towns to appoint inspectors to check water supplies and drainage; in the same year cholera struck the East End, an area not yet connected to the new sewers.","1867: the Second Reform Act gave the vote to many working men in the towns, so the condition of their streets became an election question.","1875: the Public Health Act (Disraeli's government) was compulsory — every local authority had to appoint a medical officer of health and an inspector of nuisances, provide clean water, sewers and drainage, and inspect lodging houses and food. The Artisans' Dwellings Act of the same year let councils clear slums, and the Sale of Food and Drugs Act attacked adulterated food.","Results: death rates from cholera, typhus and typhoid fell, and London suffered no great cholera epidemic after 1866."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 1 Question 5 (16 marks + 4 SPaG): 'Industrialisation was the main reason why the health of the British people improved in the years c1700–c1900.' How far do you agree? Stimulus points: the growth of industrial towns; Bazalgette's London sewers.","items":["Decoded question: the concept is causation — which kind of factor drove change — and the outcome named is the health of a whole population, so evidence about a single cure counts only where it changed how many people lived. Industrialisation here means the growth of factory towns together with the wealth, the engineering and the politics that came with them.","Criterion to set in the opening: health will be judged by whether ordinary people were less likely to catch and to die of infectious disease, so every factor is tested on the population rather than on one patient. Signal at once that industrialisation created the problem before it paid for the answer.","Paragraph 1 (the starting point — industrialisation first made health worse): Manchester grew from fewer than 75,000 people in 1801 to more than 300,000 by 1851, and London from just under a million to more than six million by 1901, into courts and cellars with shared privies and no clean supply; cholera arrived in 1831 and returned in 1848–49, 1854 and 1866. Interim judgement: the first effect of industrial growth was epidemic disease, which is why the statement cannot simply be agreed with.","Paragraph 2 (agree — industrialisation supplied the means): the same industry produced the money, the materials and the engineers to put it right. Joseph Bazalgette built about 82 miles of main intercepting sewers and roughly 1,100 miles of street sewers for London, with steam pumping stations such as Crossness, opened in 1865, and industrial chemistry supplied the dyes Koch stained his slides with and the carbolic acid Lister used on wounds. Interim judgement: only an industrial economy could have afforded and built prevention on that scale.","Paragraph 3 (agree — industrialisation changed the politics): Edwin Chadwick's report of 1842 argued to ratepayers in the language of cost; the Great Stink of 1858 reached Parliament's own windows and the money for the sewers was voted within weeks; the Sanitary Act of 1866 forced towns to appoint inspectors; the Reform Act of 1867 gave many urban working men the vote, which made slum conditions an election question; and the Public Health Act of 1875 made clean water, drainage and a medical officer of health compulsory everywhere. Interim judgement: the industrial town created both the electorate and the pressure that ended laissez-faire.","Paragraph 4 (disagree — the point beyond the stimulus): the decisive knowledge came from outside industry. Jenner's vaccination of 1796 needed no factory, Snow's mapping of the Broad Street deaths in 1854 needed only questions and arithmetic, and Pasteur's germ theory of 1861 and Koch's identification of the tuberculosis bacterium in 1882 changed what everybody was trying to do. Interim judgement: industry paid for prevention but did not discover it.","Evaluation thread running through every paragraph: return each time to the test of population health — how many people were protected, and by what — so that industry as the cause of disease, industry as the paymaster and science as the discoverer are compared on the same scale rather than listed.","Conclusion: agree in part, with a real concession. Industrialisation was the necessary condition rather than the main reason: it created the overcrowding that made reform urgent and the wealth, technology and votes that made reform possible, but what told Britain where to dig and what to inject came from individuals and from science, and health improved only where the two met. Name that criterion explicitly in the final sentence.","Timing: about 25 minutes of writing plus 3 or 4 minutes planning, since the 16 marks are worth roughly a minute each and 4 further marks are given for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology.","SPaG in this answer: spell and use the specialist terms precisely — industrialisation, urbanisation, sanitation, miasma, germ theory, medical officer of health, permissive and compulsory legislation, laissez-faire — because a wide range of accurate specialist terminology is what the top SPaG band rewards."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:51:11.911706Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"Judged afresh after the dedupe retarget and it holds up: the answer keys are all sound, so `wrong` is empty, the tariffs, AO splits, June 2024 cap wording and 80-minute timings match levels.md, the plan is a Paper 1 Question 5 statement with two stimulus points as kind A requires, and dupscan prints \"no duplication\"; I checked roughly seventy dates, figures and events, including every new one the retarget introduced (1518, 1540, 1617, 1815, 1858, 1785, 1804, 1820, 1897, 1909, 1891, 4 November 1847, August 1865, 1865 and 1876 for Garrett Anderson, Crossness 1865 with its 82 and 1,100 miles, 1853, 1867, 1885 and 1898 on anti-vaccination, and the Manchester and London population figures), and every one verifies, with no printed specimen or June 2023 Option 11 stem, focus or stimulus pair reused anywhere in the kit; what is left is a short list of imprecisions that cost no marks — the antiseptic series printed as 1867-69 rather than 1867-70, Thomas Guy called a merchant, St Bartholomew's dated 1123 inside a c1250-c1500 half-comparison, '1840: free for infants' for an Act that made vaccination free at public expense generally, the 'c1796-c1900' stem boundary, and a Level 4 'paragraph' that is really a whole essay — so this publishes."}}},"11-4":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"11-4","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This is the last key topic of the Medicine thematic study and the one the examiner expects you to reach in almost every Section B answer. Question 3 normally spans at least a century, so the modern period is the 'after' half of most comparisons; Question 4 asks why change happened, and the fastest change in the whole thousand years happens here; Question 5 or 6 asks you to judge, and a judgement about progress in medicine is almost always settled on what happened after c1900. This room also supplies the two case studies the specification names, penicillin and the fight against lung cancer, and the NHS, which are the precise evidence that lifts an answer from Level 3 to Level 4. Without dates and names from this room you can describe modern medicine but you cannot prove anything about it.","idea":[{"h":"Cause: from germs outside the body to genes and lifestyle inside it","t":"By 1900 educated Britain accepted germ theory: Pasteur had published it in 1861 and Koch had identified the bacteria causing anthrax in 1876 and tuberculosis in 1882. The twentieth century added two causes that germ theory could not explain. The first is genetic. In 1953 James Watson and Francis Crick, using the X-ray crystallography images produced by Rosalind Franklin and Maurice Wilkins at King's College London, published the double-helix structure of DNA; the Human Genome Project ran from 1990 to 2003 and mapped every human gene. Doctors can now name the genetic cause of cystic fibrosis, haemophilia, sickle cell anaemia and Huntington's disease, and the BRCA1 and BRCA2 genes identified in the mid-1990s tell a woman that her risk of breast cancer is very high before she has any symptom. The second is lifestyle. Richard Doll and Austin Bradford Hill published their study of hospital patients in the British Medical Journal in 1950 and showed that lung cancer was overwhelmingly a disease of smokers; the Royal College of Physicians confirmed it in 1962. Diet, alcohol, sunlight and lack of exercise were added to the list. A top-level answer says what is new here: the cause of illness is no longer only something that attacks you from outside, but something you inherit or something you do, which is why modern medicine tries to change behaviour as well as kill microbes.","code":"11-4 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Advances in understanding the causes of illness and disease"},{"h":"Diagnosis: the doctor stops guessing and starts measuring","t":"Before c1900 a doctor diagnosed by looking, feeling and listening to the patient. Since then three families of technology have made diagnosis a matter of measurement. Blood tests came first: Karl Landsteiner identified the blood groups in 1901, and a sample of blood can now be counted for anaemia and infection, measured for blood sugar in diabetes, tested for cholesterol, for hormones, for antibodies and for the DNA of the patient or of a tumour. Scans let the doctor see inside a living body without cutting it: Röntgen had discovered X-rays in 1895 and X-ray departments spread through British hospitals in the early 1900s; ultrasound was used on pregnant women in Glasgow from the late 1950s; Godfrey Hounsfield's CT scanner produced its first patient scan in 1971 and won him a share of the Nobel Prize in 1979; MRI scanners, which show soft tissue, came into hospital use in the 1980s. Monitors watch a patient continuously: the electrocardiograph from the early 1900s, blood-pressure monitors, heart and oxygen monitors on every intensive care bed, and glucose monitors a diabetic wears at home. A top-level answer makes the analytical point: earlier diagnosis is what turns an incurable disease into a treatable one, which is why diagnosis, not treatment, is often the decisive change.","code":"11-4 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Improvements in diagnosis"},{"h":"Access: the National Health Service, 1948","t":"The Liberal reforms had made a start: free school meals in 1906, school medical inspections from 1907, old age pensions under the 1908 Act and the National Insurance Act of 1911, which gave an insured worker earning under £160 a year free treatment from a panel doctor and sick pay, paid for by fourpence a week from the worker, threepence from the employer and twopence from the state. But it covered the worker, not his wife or his children, and hospital treatment still had to be paid for or begged from charity. The Second World War changed this: evacuation showed comfortable Britain how unhealthy poor city children were, and the Emergency Medical Service ran the country's hospitals as one system. William Beveridge's report of December 1942 named five giants to be defeated, Want, Disease, Ignorance, Squalor and Idleness, and proposed a free national health service; it was bought in huge numbers and made the idea popular. Labour won the 1945 election, Aneurin Bevan pushed the National Health Service Act through in 1946 against opposition from the British Medical Association, and the NHS opened on 5 July 1948, free at the point of use and paid for out of taxation. Charges for spectacles and dentures came in 1951 and prescription charges in 1952. A top-level answer argues that the NHS changed who got medicine rather than what medicine could do.","code":"11-4 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment"},{"h":"Treatment: magic bullets, antibiotics and high-tech hospitals","t":"Paul Ehrlich's team was looking for a chemical that would kill a microbe without poisoning the patient, a magic bullet; in 1909 they found that compound 606, Salvarsan, killed the bacterium causing syphilis, and in 1932 Gerhard Domagk showed that Prontosil cured streptococcal blood poisoning. Antibiotics went further, because penicillin killed a whole range of bacteria. The result is that infections which had killed for centuries became a week's tablets, although resistant bacteria such as MRSA in hospitals have shown that the victory is not permanent. Surgery was transformed in parallel: John Charnley's hip replacement at Wrightington from 1962, Christiaan Barnard's first heart transplant in Cape Town in 1967 with the first British one in 1968, keyhole surgery from the 1980s, robotic surgery from around 2000, radiotherapy and chemotherapy for cancer, dialysis, pacemakers and transplants. Drugs are now tested far more strictly, because thalidomide, given to pregnant women for morning sickness, was withdrawn in 1961 after it caused severe birth defects, and the Medicines Act of 1968 set up proper licensing. A top-level answer weighs this: treatment changed more in a century than in the previous six, but it changed unevenly, since cancer, dementia and viral disease still resist it.","code":"11-4 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment"},{"h":"Prevention: mass vaccination and telling people how to live","t":"Nineteenth-century prevention meant clean water and sewers, enforced by the Public Health Acts of 1848 and 1875. Modern prevention works on the individual. Mass vaccination began in earnest with the national diphtheria campaign from 1942, which cut a major child killer sharply; polio vaccination followed from 1956 after the epidemics of that decade, measles vaccine came in 1968 and the combined MMR vaccine in 1988, and the World Health Organisation declared smallpox eradicated in 1980. Vaccination in Britain is free and strongly encouraged but not compulsory, which is why take-up depends on public trust. The second approach is the lifestyle campaign, which follows directly from knowing that behaviour causes disease: cigarette advertising was banned on television in 1965, health warnings were put on packets from the early 1970s, smoking in enclosed public places in England was banned on 1 July 2007 and the age of sale was raised from 16 to 18 that year, Change4Life began in 2009 and the sugar levy on soft drinks in 2018. A top-level answer notices the change in the government's role: it moved from cleaning up the environment to changing what individuals do, and used law, tax and advertising rather than engineering to do it.","code":"11-4 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: New approaches to prevention"},{"h":"Case study: Fleming, Florey and Chain and penicillin","t":"Alexander Fleming was working at St Mary's Hospital in Paddington when, in 1928, he noticed that a mould growing by accident on a culture plate had killed the staphylococci around it. He named the active substance penicillin and published his observation in 1929, but he could not purify it in useful amounts and moved on. In 1938 and 1939 Howard Florey and Ernst Chain, at the Sir William Dunn School of Pathology in Oxford, read the paper and took the problem up as a research team with Norman Heatley, who designed the equipment to grow and extract the mould juice. In 1940 they showed that penicillin saved mice deliberately infected with a fatal dose of bacteria. In February 1941 they treated Albert Alexander, an Oxford policeman with a severe infection; he improved dramatically but died when the supply ran out. Britain's chemical industry was busy with the war, so Florey and Heatley went to the United States in 1941, and after America entered the war in December that year the American government funded drug companies to mass-produce it; by the D-Day landings of June 1944 there was enough penicillin to treat every Allied casualty. Fleming, Florey and Chain shared the Nobel Prize in 1945. A top-level answer uses this case study to argue about credit: chance, teamwork, industry and war all appear in it, so it can prove almost any factor you choose.","code":"11-4 — 3 Case studies: Key individuals: Fleming, Florey and Chain"},{"h":"Case study: the fight against lung cancer in the twenty-first century","t":"Lung cancer still kills more people in Britain than any other cancer, and smoking causes around seven in ten cases. The fight against it shows every modern approach at once. Understanding came from Doll and Hill's 1950 study and the 1962 report of the Royal College of Physicians, which identified the cause. Diagnosis uses chest X-rays, CT and PET-CT scans, bronchoscopy to look inside the airways, and a biopsy which is then tested genetically to find out which mutation is driving the tumour; from 2019 the NHS began inviting people aged 55 to 74 with a history of smoking for lung health checks using low-dose CT scans, because a tumour found early can be cut out. Treatment includes surgery to remove a lobe of the lung, precisely targeted radiotherapy, chemotherapy, targeted drugs which attack tumours carrying a particular genetic mutation, and immunotherapy, used since the 2010s, which turns the patient's own immune system on the cancer. Government action attacks the cause: the advertising bans, the 2007 smoking ban, high tobacco taxes and stop-smoking services, with adult smoking falling from about 45 per cent in the mid-1970s to under 15 per cent today. A top-level answer uses the case study to show the three factors, science and technology, government and individuals, working together rather than separately.","code":"11-4 — 3 Case studies: The fight against lung cancer in the twenty-first century"}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 3, 4 marks: one analysed difference, evidenced on both sides","setup":"Paper 1 Question 3, worth 4 marks: 'Explain one way in which the medicines people could buy in the years c1700–c1900 were different from the medicines people can buy in the years c1900–present.' The mark scheme gives 2 marks for AO2, for analysing a difference, and 2 marks for AO1, for specific information about both periods. You have about five minutes and you need one difference only.","steps":["Choose one difference sharp enough to carry the whole answer: the earlier medicine was sold untested by whoever wished to sell it, while a modern medicine may be sold only after it has been tested and licensed.","Evidence the earlier side: patent remedies and cordials were advertised and sold by shopkeepers and pedlars with no proof that they worked, and laudanum, which is opium dissolved in alcohol, could be bought over a counter; the Pharmacy Act of 1868 was the first law to keep named poisons to registered chemists, and patent remedies were left outside it.","Evidence the later side: the Medical Research Council's trial of streptomycin against tuberculosis in 1948, widely regarded as the first randomised controlled trial, made a fair test the proof a medicine has to pass, and after thalidomide was withdrawn in 1961 the Medicines Act of 1968 required every medicine to be licensed and sorted into prescription-only, pharmacy and general sale.","Finish with the sentence that makes it analysis rather than two descriptions: in the earlier period the buyer took the risk and judged the remedy for themselves, whereas in the modern period the risk is judged in advance by trials and by law, so the difference lies in who decides that a medicine works."],"cues":["Which single difference about buying a medicine can be proved on both sides of the century line?","What named nineteenth-century detail proves the earlier half?","What named modern detail proves the later half?","What must the last sentence do that the two halves of evidence have not done?"]},{"title":"Question 4, 12 marks: three reasons, one of them outside the stimulus","setup":"Paper 1 Question 4, worth 12 marks, AO2 6 and AO1 6: 'Explain why surgery became more successful in the years c1900–present. You may use the following in your answer: • blood transfusion • keyhole surgery. You must also use information of your own.' The mark scheme prints two caps: a maximum of eight marks, and no access to Level 4, for an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content. About twenty minutes.","steps":["Take the first stimulus point and turn it into a reason with a causal chain: once blood lost on the table could be replaced, a surgeon could cut deeper and operate for longer without the patient bleeding to death.","Support it precisely: Landsteiner identified the blood groups in 1901 so that blood could be matched, wartime work showed it could be stored and moved in bulk, and a national blood transfusion service was set up in Britain in 1946.","Take the second stimulus point the same way: keyhole surgery shrank the wound itself — the first laparoscopic removal of a gall bladder was carried out in 1985, and from the late 1980s cameras and fine instruments passed through small openings, so patients healed faster and caught fewer infections.","Now add the reason that lifts the answer above the caps: infection and pain were brought under control, so an operation could last hours — penicillin, mass-produced by 1944, saved wounds that would once have killed, and halothane, made by ICI and first used in a Manchester operating theatre in 1956, was a safer anaesthetic than chloroform; on that footing surgeons carried out Britain's first kidney transplant in Edinburgh on 30 October 1960 and its first heart transplant in London on 3 May 1968.","Close by linking the three: replacing lost blood kept the patient alive during the operation, antibiotics and safer anaesthetics kept them alive after it, and keyhole instruments made the injury of surgery itself small."],"cues":["What does replacing lost blood let a surgeon do that they could not do before?","Which named discovery and which date make that reason specific rather than general?","What does the second stimulus point change about the operation itself?","Which problem, untouched by either bullet, had to be solved before an operation could last hours?","How should the closing sentence connect three reasons instead of listing them?"]},{"title":"Question 5, 16 marks: setting the criterion before you argue","setup":"Paper 1 Question 5, worth 16 marks plus up to 4 for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology: \"'The Second World War was the most important turning point in medicine in Britain in the years c1900–present.' How far do you agree? You may use the following in your answer: • the Emergency Medical Service • the mass production of penicillin. You must also use information of your own.\" The judgement strand of the mark scheme requires criteria that are justified and applied, not asserted.","steps":["State the criterion you will judge by in the first two sentences: a turning point is most important if it changed the direction of medicine permanently and for the whole population, not if it caused the most activity at the time.","Argue the case for the named factor against that criterion, using the first stimulus point: the government's Emergency Medical Service, set up as war began in 1939, took charge of the country's hospitals, treated air-raid casualties free of charge and organised blood supply on a national scale, proving that a state hospital service could be run — and the Beveridge Report of December 1942 turned that experience into a plan.","Take the second stimulus point, then concede its limit: wartime money and American factories made enough penicillin for the Allied casualties of the D-Day landings in June 1944, but Fleming had noticed the mould in 1928 and the Oxford team had purified it before the war, so the war speeded a discovery it did not make.","Add the point beyond the stimulus: the deeper turns came on either side of the war — the National Health Service opened on 5 July 1948, Doll and Hill's study of 1950 tied smoking to lung cancer and the structure of DNA was worked out in 1953, redirecting medicine towards prevention and towards the causes inside the body.","Reach the judgement by applying the criterion rather than repeating the essay: the war was the point at which the state accepted responsibility for treating everybody, and that change proved permanent, so it is the most important turning point in how medicine was delivered, while the turning points in what medicine knew lie before and after it."],"cues":["What test must be fixed before any evidence is worth writing down?","Which precise wartime fact makes the case for the claim strongest?","What did the war do to penicillin that it did not do, and what does admitting that cost you?","Which development in this paragraph is prompted by neither bullet?","How does applying the criterion produce a different last paragraph from summarising the essay?"]},{"title":"Using the penicillin case study to argue about credit","setup":"You are given the claim 'Alexander Fleming deserves most of the credit for the development of penicillin' and the facts of the case: Fleming noticed in 1928 that mould on a culture plate had killed bacteria and published in 1929 but could not purify it; Florey, Chain and Heatley at Oxford took the problem up, saved infected mice in 1940 and treated the policeman Albert Alexander in 1941; American firms mass-produced penicillin after the United States entered the war, so that enough existed for the D-Day casualties in June 1944; the three men shared the Nobel Prize in 1945.","steps":["Separate the two things being credited: noticing that a mould kills bacteria, and turning that observation into a drug that can be given to millions.","Concede the strongest part of the claim: without the observation of 1928 and the paper of 1929 the Oxford team would have had nothing to work on, so Fleming supplied the necessary starting point.","Set against it what he did not do: he could not purify or produce the substance, and it took a funded team, Heatley's equipment and the mouse experiment to prove it worked in a living body.","Bring in the factor no individual controls: war created the demand, the money and the industrial capacity, so the same discovery in peacetime might have waited years.","Judge on a stated criterion, importance measured by lives saved: the credit belongs mainly to the Oxford team and the wartime American industry, with Fleming as the indispensable first step rather than the main cause."],"cues":["What two different achievements are hidden inside the word 'development'?","What is the strongest point in favour of the claim?","What did the individual named in the claim fail to do?","Which factor in this story belonged to no individual at all?","On what measure should the credit finally be divided?"]},{"title":"Question 3, 4 marks: proving a similarity rather than a difference","setup":"Paper 1 Question 3, worth 4 marks: 'Explain one way in which ideas about the cause of disease in the years c1500–c1700 were similar to ideas about the cause of disease in the years c1900–present.' The grid reads 'similarity' throughout, so a contrast scores nothing; you need one similarity analysed and supported with specific information from both periods.","steps":["Fix the similarity on where the cause is placed: in both periods people believed that something in the air they breathed could make them ill.","Evidence the earlier side: during the Great Plague of London in 1665 bad air, or miasma, was blamed, so fires were burned in the streets and people carried posies of herbs and sweet-smelling spices against the stench.","Evidence the modern side: the Great Smog of December 1952 was followed by the Clean Air Act of 1956, and in December 2020 a coroner recorded air pollution exposure as a cause of the death of nine-year-old Ella Adoo-Kissi-Debrah in south London, the first such finding in Britain.","Explain why that counts as a genuine similarity: the reasoning is entirely different — a foul smell then, measured nitrogen dioxide and soot particles now — but in both periods the air itself is identified as a cause of disease and the authorities act on it."],"cues":["Where should you look for a similarity when one of the two periods has no germ theory at all?","What named seventeenth-century response proves the earlier half?","What named modern law or ruling proves the later half?","How do you stop an examiner reading the two halves as two separate stories about air?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Why did Fleming's observation of 1928 not by itself produce a usable drug?","a":"He could not purify penicillin or produce it in useful quantities, and he had no team or funding to test it on a living patient; that work was done by Florey, Chain and Heatley at Oxford from 1938 onwards."},{"q":"What did the Beveridge Report of 1942 propose, and which of its five giants did the NHS attack?","a":"It proposed a welfare state defeating the five giants of Want, Disease, Ignorance, Squalor and Idleness; the National Health Service, opened on 5 July 1948, was the attack on Disease."},{"q":"Name three ways in which diagnosis improved after c1900 and say what each added.","a":"Blood tests measure the body's chemistry from a sample; scans such as X-ray, CT and MRI show the inside of a living body without surgery; monitors such as the electrocardiograph and glucose monitors track a patient continuously."},{"q":"How did understanding of the cause of illness change after germ theory?","a":"Two causes were added that microbes cannot explain: genetic inheritance, made visible by the DNA structure of 1953 and the Human Genome Project of 1990 to 2003, and lifestyle, proved by Doll and Hill's 1950 study of smoking and lung cancer."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 1 Section B is worth 36 of the paper's 52 marks: Question 3 is 4 marks, Question 4 is 12 marks, and Question 5 or Question 6 is 16 marks plus up to 4 for spelling, punctuation, grammar and the use of specialist terminology.","Question 3, 'Explain one way in which … was similar to / different from …', is marked AO2 2 and AO1 2 on a two-level grid, and normally spans at least a century, so both periods must be named and supported with specific information.","Question 4 stimulus caps: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content', so the two stimulus points on their own cannot carry an answer past 8 marks.","Question 5 or 6 stimulus caps: 'Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content', so the two stimulus points on their own cannot carry an answer past 11 marks; the judgement itself is a separate bullet worth the fourth mark in each level.","Timing on Paper 1, which lasts 1 hour 20 minutes: work at about a minute a mark and add planning time, and on the last question time for the spelling and specialist-terminology marks — roughly 5 minutes on Question 3, 20 on Question 4 and 25 on Question 5 or 6, which leaves about 30 minutes for Section A. Question 5 or 6 is the only Section B answer where spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms carry marks of their own."],"questions":[{"q":"Explain one way in which the treatment of infectious disease in the years c1700–c1900 was different from the treatment of infectious disease in the years c1900–present. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — treatment that supported the patient or attacked the environment against treatment that kills the microbe inside the body — supported by specific detail from both periods, such as Nightingale's nursing and carbolic antiseptic against Salvarsan, Prontosil and penicillin.","sol":"One difference is that an infection could not be cured in the earlier period but can be cured in the modern one. In the years c1700–c1900 doctors could only support the patient and keep the infection out: Nightingale's clean, ventilated wards, Lister's carbolic antiseptic from 1867 and quarantine. Once an infection took hold, nothing killed it. In the years c1900–present chemicals kill the microbe inside the body: Salvarsan in 1909, Prontosil in 1932 and penicillin, mass-produced by 1944. The difference is between managing an infection and ending it, which is why deaths from infection collapsed after the 1940s.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what actually happened to a patient whose wound had already turned septic in each period.","A comparison needs both halves evidenced: one named treatment with a date from each period, then a sentence saying what kind of difference it is.","Name the difference first, prove the earlier side, prove the later side, and close by explaining why it is a difference in kind rather than in degree."],"codes":["11-4 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment","11-4 — 3 Case studies: Key individuals: Fleming, Florey and Chain"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which access to medical care in the years c1700–c1900 was different from access to medical care in the years c1900–present. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — care bought, insured or begged from charity against care free at the point of use for everyone — supported by detail such as voluntary hospitals and paying for a doctor against the National Health Service from 1948.","sol":"One difference is who could get a doctor. In the years c1700–c1900 medical care had to be paid for or given as charity: a physician's fee was beyond most families, voluntary hospitals depended on subscribers, and the poorest relied on the workhouse infirmary or on cheaper apothecaries and quacks. In the years c1900–present care became a right rather than a purchase: the National Insurance Act of 1911 covered insured workers, and from 5 July 1948 the National Health Service gave every person free treatment paid for from taxation. The difference is that ability to pay stopped deciding whether a sick person saw a doctor.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what a poor family did when a child fell ill in each period, not about what the best doctors knew.","Support each side with something named and dated, so the comparison is specific rather than general.","State the difference, evidence both periods, and end with a sentence explaining what changed about who received care."],"codes":["11-4 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which government action to prevent disease in the years c1800–c1900 was different from government action to prevent disease in the years c1900–present. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — action on the environment through sanitation laws against action on the individual through vaccination and lifestyle campaigns — supported by detail such as the Public Health Acts of 1848 and 1875 against the 1942 diphtheria campaign and the 2007 smoking ban.","sol":"One difference is what the government acted on. In the years c1800–c1900 it acted on the environment: the Public Health Act of 1848 set up a General Board of Health and the compulsory Act of 1875 forced councils to provide clean water, sewers and rubbish collection, so prevention meant engineering. In the years c1900–present the government acts on the individual: the national diphtheria vaccination campaign from 1942 and later polio, measles and MMR programmes protected people one by one, and campaigns and laws such as the ban on smoking in enclosed public places in England in 2007 tried to change behaviour. The difference is between cleaning the surroundings and altering how people live.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what a government in each period thought it was that made people ill, because prevention follows the theory of cause.","Both halves need a named law or campaign with a date attached.","Name the difference, prove each period, and finish by saying what the target of government action became."],"codes":["11-4 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: New approaches to prevention"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the communication of new medical ideas in the years c1500–c1700 was similar to the communication of new medical ideas in the years c1900–present. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed similarity — a new idea counted only once it had been printed for other trained people to check and build on — supported by detail from both periods, such as Vesalius in 1543 and the Royal Society's Philosophical Transactions from 1665 against Watson and Crick in Nature in 1953 and the Human Genome Project.","sol":"One similarity is that in both periods a new medical idea only counted once it had been printed for other experts to read and test. In the years c1500–c1700 the printing press carried work across Europe: Vesalius published his book on the structure of the human body in 1543 and Harvey his account of the circulation of the blood in 1628, and the Royal Society, founded in 1660, began its journal Philosophical Transactions in 1665. In the years c1900–present scientific journals do the same work: Watson and Crick's structure of DNA appeared in Nature in 1953, and the Human Genome Project, finished in 2003, released its sequence for any laboratory to use.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The similarity is in how a new idea reached other people, not in what the idea itself claimed.","Choose one printed publication from each period that you can date, so both halves carry specific support.","State the similarity, prove it on both sides with a dated publication, and close with a sentence on why publishing in this way mattered to medicine."],"codes":["11-4 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Advances in understanding the causes of illness and disease"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the surgery that could be carried out in the years c1700–c1900 was different from the surgery that could be carried out in the years c1900–present. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — amputation and work on the surface of the body against operating inside it and replacing its parts — supported by detail such as chloroform from 1847 and Lister's carbolic antiseptic from 1867 against transfusion after Landsteiner's blood groups of 1901, Charnley's hip replacement from 1962 and the first British heart transplant in 1968.","sol":"One difference is how far into the body a surgeon could go. In the years c1700–c1900 surgery was mostly amputation and work on the surface: chloroform from 1847 took away the pain and Lister's carbolic antiseptic from 1867 cut the infection, but nothing could replace the blood a patient lost, so opening the chest or the abdomen was close to fatal. In the years c1900–present surgeons operate inside the body and replace parts of it, because blood loss can now be made good: Landsteiner identified the blood groups in 1901 and made safe transfusion possible, John Charnley was fitting artificial hips at Wrightington from 1962, and the first heart transplant in Britain was carried out in 1968. The difference is between cutting damaged parts away and putting working ones in.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what a surgeon in each period could not do, and what limit stopped them.","Each period needs one named development with a date, so the comparison is specific.","Name the difference, evidence it on both sides, then explain in one sentence what kind of operation became possible."],"codes":["11-4 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment"]},{"q":"Explain why the diagnosis of illness improved in the years c1900–present.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• blood tests\n• the CT scan\nYou must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit analysis of the measurement of the body's chemistry, of imaging technology from X-rays to CT and MRI, and of own points such as the NHS funding and distributing the technology, or continuous monitoring and screening. Stimulus caps apply: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Diagnosis improved because doctors stopped depending on what they could see at the bedside and began measuring the body directly.\n\nThe first reason is that the body's chemistry became measurable. Karl Landsteiner identified the blood groups in 1901, and blood can now be counted for anaemia and infection, measured for blood sugar in diabetes, for cholesterol, for hormones and for the DNA of the patient or of a tumour. This changed diagnosis because a sample gives a number that can be compared with a normal range, so an illness can be named before the patient looks ill at all, and named correctly whichever doctor reads the result.\n\nThe second reason is imaging. Röntgen had discovered X-rays in 1895 and X-ray departments spread through British hospitals in the early 1900s, so a broken bone or a shadow on a lung could be seen without surgery. Godfrey Hounsfield's computerised tomography scanner produced its first patient scan in 1971 and gave a cross-section rather than a shadow; MRI scanners, which show soft tissue in detail, came into hospital use in the 1980s, and ultrasound had been used on pregnant women from the late 1950s. Each machine made the inside of a living body visible, which is what removes guesswork from diagnosis.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond both bullets: a free health service turned inventions into diagnoses. From 5 July 1948 the National Health Service paid for hospital equipment out of taxation and gave every patient access to it, so a scanner is not a luxury for the rich. It also made national screening possible and funds programmes such as the lung health checks offered from 2019 to older smokers.\n\nThese reasons depend on one another. Scientific understanding made a test worth doing, technology made the result visible, and public funding made it available to everybody, so diagnosis improved for the whole population rather than for a few paying patients.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what a doctor in 1900 could not find out about a patient without cutting them open, and work through what removed each of those blind spots.","Turn each bullet into a reason with a chain: this technology let doctors do this, which meant illness was found earlier or more accurately.","Three developed reasons, at least one of them nothing to do with either bullet, each with a named date, and a final paragraph that links them."],"codes":["11-4 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Improvements in diagnosis","11-4 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment"]},{"q":"Explain why the drugs available to treat disease changed in the years c1900–present.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• Alexander Fleming\n• the Second World War\nYou must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit analysis of Fleming's 1928 observation and 1929 paper and the Oxford team's work, of the war creating demand, money and American industrial capacity, and of own points such as Ehrlich's and Domagk's magic bullets or the post-war drug industry. Stimulus caps apply: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The drugs a doctor could prescribe in 2000 had almost nothing in common with those of 1900, and three things caused the change.\n\nThe first reason goes beyond the stimulus points: germ theory turned drug-making into a search for chemicals that would kill a named microbe and leave the patient unharmed. Paul Ehrlich's team found the first of these magic bullets in 1909, when Salvarsan was shown to cure syphilis, and in 1932 Gerhard Domagk showed that the dye Prontosil cured blood poisoning caused by streptococcal bacteria. For the first time a medicine attacked the cause of a disease rather than its symptoms, and the drug companies had a reason to look for more.\n\nThe second reason is Alexander Fleming's observation. At St Mary's Hospital in Paddington in 1928 he noticed that a mould growing by accident on a culture plate had killed the staphylococci around it, named the substance penicillin and published the finding in 1929. That paper opened a second route: a drug grown from a living mould rather than built in a laboratory. Fleming could neither purify penicillin nor make it in quantity, so from 1938 Howard Florey, Ernst Chain and Norman Heatley at Oxford did the work that turned it into a medicine, saving mice given a fatal dose of bacteria in 1940 and treating the Oxford policeman Albert Alexander in February 1941, who improved dramatically and then died when the supply ran out.\n\nThe third reason is the Second World War. Infected wounds killed soldiers, so a drug that cured infection had immediate military value and governments would pay for it. British chemical firms were already committed to war production, so Florey and Heatley went to the United States in 1941, and after America entered the war that December its government funded drug companies to grow penicillin in industrial vats, so that by the D-Day landings of June 1944 there was enough to treat every Allied casualty.\n\nEach stage made the next possible. Understanding of germs made a targeted drug conceivable, a chance observation supplied a far more powerful one, and war supplied the money and the factories that made antibiotics ordinary, after which the same industry went on producing new drugs for civilian patients.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what a doctor could actually give a patient with an infection in 1900, and what had changed by 1950.","Each reason needs a causal chain, not a description: say what this discovery or event made possible that had been impossible before.","Three developed reasons with at least one outside the bullets, each dated, and a closing paragraph explaining how each stage made the next possible."],"codes":["11-4 — 3 Case studies: Key individuals: Fleming, Florey and Chain","11-4 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment"]},{"q":"Explain why the way medical care was paid for changed in the years c1900–present.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Beveridge Report\n• the Second World War\nYou must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit analysis of the Beveridge Report of December 1942, of the war's effect through evacuation and the Emergency Medical Service, and of own points such as the gaps left by the 1911 National Insurance Act, Labour's 1945 victory and Bevan's Act of 1946, or the charges of 1951 and 1952. Stimulus caps apply: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"In 1900 medical care was paid for by the patient, by a friendly society or by charity; today it is paid for out of taxation. Three things moved it.\n\nThe first reason goes beyond the stimulus points: the old way of paying left most people out. A working family met the doctor's bill itself or went without, and the voluntary hospitals lived on donations and treated the poor as objects of charity. The National Insurance Act of 1911 made insurance compulsory for workers earning under £160 a year, paid for by fourpence a week from the worker, threepence from the employer and twopence from the state, and gave an insured man free treatment from a panel doctor; but it did not cover his wife or his children, and it did not pay for hospital treatment. The system therefore spread the idea that care should be paid for in advance while leaving the largest bills unpaid.\n\nThe second reason is what the war did. Evacuation moved poor city children into comfortable homes and made their ill health a national embarrassment rather than a private misfortune. The Emergency Medical Service then ran the country's hospitals as a single organisation paid for from central funds, which proved that the state could finance medicine on a national scale, and shared bombing and shared rationing made shared provision feel fair.\n\nThe third reason is the Beveridge Report. Published in December 1942, it named five giants standing in the way of reconstruction, Want, Disease, Ignorance, Squalor and Idleness, and proposed a free national health service as the weapon against Disease. It sold in huge numbers and turned a specialist argument into a popular demand, so Labour's landslide victory in the 1945 election gave Aneurin Bevan the power to push the National Health Service Act through in 1946 against the opposition of the British Medical Association, nationalise the hospitals and open the service on 5 July 1948, free at the point of use.\n\nThe change was therefore from payment by the sick to payment by the taxpayer: the old method failed the majority, the war proved a national alternative worked, and Beveridge and the 1945 election made it policy. Cost pushed back almost at once, and charges for spectacles and dentures were introduced in 1951 and prescription charges in 1952, but the principle that care is paid for before it is needed, and by everybody, has held ever since.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask who actually paid the doctor and the hospital in 1900, and who pays them now.","Each reason must explain a step in the change: what made the old way of paying fail, what showed a national alternative could work, and what made it policy.","Three developed reasons, at least one outside the bullets, each with named legislation or dates, ending with a paragraph that links them into a sequence."],"codes":["11-4 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment"]},{"q":"Explain why understanding of the causes of illness and disease changed in the years c1900–present.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• DNA\n• smoking\nYou must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit analysis of genetics from the double helix of 1953 to the Human Genome Project of 1990–2003, of the statistical proof that lifestyle causes disease in Doll and Hill's 1950 study, and of own points such as improved technology or institutional funding of research. Stimulus caps apply: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Germ theory had explained infection by 1900, but it could not explain why people who caught nothing still fell ill. Understanding changed because two new kinds of cause were established and the tools to prove them existed.\n\nThe first reason is genetics. In 1953 James Watson and Francis Crick published the double-helix structure of DNA, using X-ray crystallography images produced by Rosalind Franklin and Maurice Wilkins at King's College London, which showed how characteristics are copied from parent to child. The Human Genome Project then ran from 1990 to 2003 and mapped every human gene. This changed understanding because a disease could now be traced to a fault in a particular gene: cystic fibrosis, haemophilia, sickle cell anaemia and Huntington's disease all have identified genetic causes, and the BRCA1 and BRCA2 genes found in the mid-1990s show a very high inherited risk of breast cancer before any symptom appears.\n\nThe second reason is the proof that lifestyle causes disease. Richard Doll and Austin Bradford Hill published their study of hospital patients in the British Medical Journal in 1950 and showed that lung cancer was overwhelmingly a disease of smokers, and the Royal College of Physicians confirmed the link in 1962. What mattered was the method: comparing large groups statistically could prove a cause that no microscope would ever reveal, and the same approach added diet, alcohol, sunlight and lack of exercise.\n\nThe third reason lies behind both and is beyond the bullets: the technology and organisation of research. Electron microscopes, X-ray crystallography and, later, computers powerful enough to handle three billion letters of genetic code made these discoveries physically possible, and funded institutions with national medical records let researchers follow thousands of patients for decades.\n\nTogether these reasons widened the idea of cause. A modern doctor asks what a patient inherited and how they live as well as what infected them, which is why prevention now means changing behaviour and screening for genetic risk rather than only killing germs.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what germ theory could not account for, and which two new answers the twentieth century supplied.","Turn each bullet into a reason by saying what it allowed doctors to explain that they could not explain before.","Three developed reasons with at least one beyond the bullets, precise dates throughout, and a final paragraph saying what the idea of a cause now includes."],"codes":["11-4 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Advances in understanding the causes of illness and disease","11-4 — 3 Case studies: The fight against lung cancer in the twenty-first century"]},{"q":"Explain why the government became more involved in preventing illness in the years c1900–present.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• mass vaccination\n• the ban on smoking in enclosed public places\nYou must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit analysis of vaccination programmes as state-organised prevention, of lifestyle legislation following the proof that behaviour causes disease, and of own points such as the cost of the NHS giving the state a financial motive, or the decline of laissez-faire from the Liberal reforms onwards. Stimulus caps apply: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Government prevention grew because new knowledge told the state exactly what to attack, the state then had to pay for the illness it failed to prevent, and public attitudes to interference had already changed.\n\nThe first reason is that vaccination gave governments a method that only a government could organise. The national diphtheria campaign from 1942 sharply reduced a major child killer, polio vaccination followed from 1956 after the epidemics of that decade, measles vaccine came in 1968 and the combined MMR vaccine in 1988, and smallpox was declared eradicated worldwide in 1980. Protecting a population needs records, supply, publicity and clinics on a national scale, so only central government could deliver it, and every success made the next campaign easier to justify.\n\nThe second reason is that research identified causes that lie in behaviour, which a sewer cannot fix. Once Doll and Hill's study of 1950 and the Royal College of Physicians in 1962 had shown that smoking causes lung cancer, doing nothing became indefensible, so the government banned cigarette advertising on television in 1965, required health warnings on packets from the early 1970s, banned smoking in enclosed public places in England on 1 July 2007 and raised the age of sale from 16 to 18 in the same year.\n\nThe third reason is beyond the bullets: after 1948 the state pays the bill. Because the National Health Service is funded from taxation, every preventable illness costs the taxpayer, so prevention became cheaper than treatment and the government gained a direct financial motive for campaigns such as Change4Life in 2009 and the sugar levy on soft drinks in 2018.\n\nThe reasons reinforce each other. The Liberal reforms, from free school meals in 1906 to the National Insurance Act of 1911, had already broken the old belief that government should leave health alone, so when science identified new causes and the state took on the cost of illness, intervention was both possible and expected.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what a government can prevent that an individual cannot, and what changed about what governments were expected to do.","Each reason must explain an increase in involvement, not just describe a policy: say why the state acted rather than leaving it alone.","Three developed reasons with at least one beyond the bullets, each carrying a dated law or campaign, and a closing paragraph that ties them together."],"codes":["11-4 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: New approaches to prevention","11-4 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment"]},{"q":"'The National Health Service was the most important reason why the health of the British people improved in the years c1900–present.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Beveridge Report\n• antibiotics\nYou must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6, plus up to 4 SPaG. Credit a criterion-based judgement weighing the NHS from 1948 against science and technology, government prevention and earlier reform, using precise evidence from both sides. Stimulus caps apply: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Whether the health service was the most important reason depends on the test used. The fair test is which factor reached the largest number of people and did most to cut death and suffering, and on that test the service matters enormously but is not self-sufficient.\n\nThe case for it is strong. Before 1948 medical care was bought, insured or begged. The National Insurance Act of 1911 covered an insured worker but not his wife or his children, and hospital treatment depended on charity or on the workhouse infirmary. The Beveridge Report of December 1942 named Disease as one of five giants and proposed a free national health service, and after Labour's victory in 1945 Aneurin Bevan carried the National Health Service Act of 1946 against the opposition of the British Medical Association. From 5 July 1948 every person could see a general practitioner, go to hospital, give birth safely, be vaccinated and be screened without paying at the point of use. That removed the single barrier that had kept the poor away from medicine for centuries, and it is the reason maternal and infant deaths, and deaths from treatable illness, fell so steeply afterwards.\n\nAgainst this, a service can only hand out what science has produced. Ehrlich's magic bullets, Salvarsan in 1909 and Domagk's Prontosil in 1932, and then penicillin, mass-produced by the D-Day landings of June 1944, defeated bacterial infection before the service existed. The same is true of the machines the service uses: X-rays from the early 1900s, the first patient scan by computerised tomography in 1971, MRI from the 1980s, transplants after Barnard's first heart transplant in 1967 and hip replacements from 1962. Had science supplied nothing new, free access to bloodletting would have saved nobody.\n\nBeyond the stimulus points, the largest recent gains have come from prevention rather than treatment, and prevention is government action, not health service action. The national diphtheria campaign from 1942, polio vaccination from 1956 and MMR from 1988 stopped diseases occurring at all, while the television advertising ban of 1965, the ban on smoking in enclosed public places in England in 2007 and the stop-smoking services helped cut adult smoking from about 45 per cent in the mid-1970s to under 15 per cent, which is doing more against lung cancer than any treatment yet invented. Improvement had also begun before 1948, with free school meals from 1906, school medical inspections from 1907 and better housing and sanitation.\n\nApplying the test, the health service is the most important single reason, but only in a particular sense. It did not discover a cure or prevent a single disease by itself; what it did was convert every discovery, from antibiotics to scanning, into treatment available to the whole population instead of to those who could pay. Science supplied the tools and government prevention removed some causes, but without a universal service those advances would have improved the health of some Britons rather than of the British people. Its importance is therefore as the mechanism of delivery, and it depends on the other factors for anything worth delivering.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first what would make one factor more important than another, and say so before you start arguing.","The strongest counter-case is to ask what the service would have had to offer a patient if it had opened a century earlier.","Argue for the named factor, argue for at least two rivals with one of them outside the bullets, and let the conclusion apply the test you set rather than summarise."],"codes":["11-4 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment","11-4 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: New approaches to prevention","11-4 — 3 Case studies: Key individuals: Fleming, Florey and Chain"]},{"q":"'There has been more change in the treatment of disease than in the prevention of disease in the years c1900–present.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• antibiotics\n• mass vaccination\nYou must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6, plus up to 4 SPaG. Credit a criterion-based comparison of change in treatment (magic bullets, antibiotics, high-tech surgery) against change in prevention (vaccination, lifestyle legislation, screening), judged on scale of effect rather than on novelty. Stimulus caps apply: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Both treatment and prevention changed out of recognition after 1900, so the question is which changed more. Change should be measured by how many people are affected and by how far the new approach departs from what went before, not simply by how impressive the technology looks.\n\nThe case for treatment is very strong. In 1900 a doctor could set a bone, remove a limb and prescribe painkillers, but could not cure a bacterial infection. Ehrlich's team found the first magic bullet, Salvarsan, against syphilis in 1909, Domagk's Prontosil cured streptococcal blood poisoning in 1932, and penicillin, discovered by Fleming in 1928 and mass-produced by the D-Day landings of June 1944, killed a whole range of bacteria. Surgery changed just as far: John Charnley's hip replacements from 1962, Christiaan Barnard's first heart transplant in 1967 with the first British one the following year, keyhole surgery from the 1980s and robotic surgery from around 2000, alongside dialysis, chemotherapy and radiotherapy. Diseases that were death sentences became courses of tablets or routine operations.\n\nThe case for prevention is equally strong and reaches further. Mass vaccination removed diseases rather than treating them: the national diphtheria campaign from 1942, polio vaccination from 1956, measles vaccine in 1968, MMR in 1988, and smallpox declared eradicated worldwide in 1980. Prevention also became a matter of law and persuasion once lifestyle was proved to cause disease by Doll and Hill in 1950 and by the Royal College of Physicians in 1962: cigarette advertising was banned on television in 1965, smoking in enclosed public places in England was banned in 2007, and adult smoking fell from about 45 per cent in the mid-1970s to under 15 per cent.\n\nBeyond the stimulus points, screening blurs the line and settles the argument. Programmes that find disease before symptoms appear, such as the lung health checks with low-dose scans offered to older smokers from 2019, or genetic testing after the BRCA genes were identified in the mid-1990s, are prevention delivered by diagnostic technology. They show that the greatest modern gains come from stopping disease developing, not from curing it once it has.\n\nOn the test set out at the start, prevention has changed more. Treatment has changed spectacularly for the individual patient, but it still deals with disease one sufferer at a time and still fails against much cancer, dementia and viral illness. Prevention changed what the government does, what the law forbids and how ordinary people are expected to live, and it acts on entire populations at once. The statement is therefore only half right: treatment changed more visibly, prevention changed more fundamentally.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Set a measure of change before you compare: numbers affected, or distance travelled from what came before.","Look for evidence that does not sit neatly on one side, because that is where the judgement is really decided.","Two developed sides, a point beyond the bullets, and a conclusion that applies your measure instead of repeating the essay."],"codes":["11-4 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment","11-4 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: New approaches to prevention","11-4 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Improvements in diagnosis"]},{"q":"'Individuals achieved little on their own in the improvement of the treatment of disease in the years c1900–present.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the mould on the culture plate\n• Howard Florey\nYou must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6, plus up to 4 SPaG. Credit a judgement on a stated criterion weighing what named individuals since c1900 — Fleming, Florey, Chain, Heatley, Charnley — did by themselves against the teams, wartime government money, industry and the National Health Service they depended on. Stimulus caps apply: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The claim is about dependence rather than worth, so the test is whether the improvements credited to individuals could have reached patients without the teams, money and institutions standing behind them.\n\nMuch of the case against the claim is the story the stimulus points tell. The mould that drifted onto a culture plate at St Mary's Hospital in Paddington in 1928 would have been washed up by almost anybody else; Alexander Fleming noticed that the staphylococci around it had been killed, named the substance penicillin and published the observation in 1929. Ten years later Howard Florey, with Ernst Chain and Norman Heatley at the Sir William Dunn School of Pathology in Oxford, saw what that note was worth, purified enough penicillin to save mice given a fatal dose of bacteria in 1940 and treated the Oxford policeman Albert Alexander in February 1941. John Charnley designed an artificial hip that lasted, fitting it at Wrightington from 1962, and Britain's first heart transplant was carried out at the National Heart Hospital on 3 May 1968. Without these particular people at these particular benches, the treatments do not exist.\n\nYet none of them worked alone, which is the strength of the claim. Fleming could not purify penicillin and left it aside. Florey depended on Heatley's improvised equipment, and when British chemical firms were tied to war production he took the work to the United States in 1941, where government-funded drug companies brewed penicillin in industrial vats until there was enough for every Allied casualty at the D-Day landings of June 1944. Charnley's hip needed a new plastic and the engineers who made it, and a transplant needs a hospital team, a donor and drugs that hold off rejection.\n\nThe point beyond the stimulus is that some of the largest gains since c1900 carry no individual's name at all. The National Health Service, opened on 5 July 1948, made every existing treatment free at the point of use, which did more for the patients of poor families than any single discovery. Mass vaccination against diphtheria from 1942 and against polio from 1956 removed diseases instead of treating them. The Medical Research Council's trial of streptomycin against tuberculosis in 1948, widely regarded as the first randomised controlled trial, changed treatment everywhere by making evidence rather than authority the test of a medicine.\n\nThere is one sense in which individuals did achieve a great deal alone: seeing what nobody else had seen. Fleming's plate, the structure of DNA published in 1953 and Doll and Hill's link between smoking and lung cancer in 1950 were acts of noticing, and no committee can be organised to notice.\n\nThe fair judgement is a divided one. Measured by discovery, the claim is wrong, because the moment when something new is seen belongs to a person. Measured by improvement in treatment, which means the number of patients actually cured, the claim is largely right: turning a discovery into a medicine took wartime government, industry, laboratory teams and, after 1948, a national service. Individuals began almost everything and finished almost nothing.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Separate the two things the claim runs together: seeing something new, and getting a treatment to the patients who need it.","Take each named person in turn and ask what they could not supply themselves, and who or what supplied it.","Argue what individuals did alone, weigh it against what they depended on, bring in an improvement that carries no individual's name, and judge on a criterion you have stated."],"codes":["11-4 — 3 Case studies: Key individuals: Fleming, Florey and Chain","11-4 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment"]},{"q":"'Government action has been the most important factor in the fight against lung cancer in the twentieth and twenty-first centuries.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the ban on smoking in enclosed public places\n• CT scans\nYou must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6, plus up to 4 SPaG. Credit a criterion-based judgement weighing government prevention across both centuries (the 1965 advertising ban, packet warnings, taxation, the 2007 smoking ban, screening) against science and technology in diagnosis and treatment, against the research that identified the cause, and against individual choice. Stimulus caps apply: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Lung cancer became a mass killer during the twentieth century and still kills more people in Britain than any other cancer; smoking causes around seven in ten cases. A factor should count as most important if it did most to reduce the number of deaths, and across the two centuries government action leads, though it has never worked alone.\n\nThe case for government is strong because only the state can act on the cause before anyone is ill. Once Richard Doll and Austin Bradford Hill had published their study of smoking and lung cancer in the British Medical Journal in 1950, and the Royal College of Physicians had reported in 1962, the state could act on the whole population rather than on patients: cigarette advertising was banned on television in 1965, health warnings were printed on packets from 1971, tobacco was taxed heavily and free stop-smoking services were funded, smoking in enclosed public places in England was banned on 1 July 2007 and the age of sale was raised from 16 to 18 in the same year. Adult smoking fell from about 45 per cent in the mid-1970s to under 15 per cent. A cancer that never starts needs no cure, which is why this is the largest single effect.\n\nAgainst this, government cannot diagnose or treat anybody, and what happens once the disease exists has been decided by science and technology. Chest X-rays were available from the early twentieth century; computerised tomography followed Hounsfield's first patient scan in 1971, and CT, PET-CT and bronchoscopy now find and stage a tumour, with the biopsy tested genetically so that the right drug can be chosen. Treatment has moved from surgery to remove a lobe of the lung towards precisely targeted radiotherapy, chemotherapy, drugs aimed at tumours carrying a particular mutation, and immunotherapy since the 2010s, which turns the patient's own immune system against the cancer. None of this came from a minister, and the research that told government what to legislate about was not done by government either.\n\nBeyond the stimulus points, the two factors are not really separate, and one institution joins them. The National Health Service, free at the point of use since 5 July 1948 and paid for out of taxation, is what carries scanning and drugs to patients who could not buy them, and from 2019 it began inviting people aged 55 to 74 with a history of smoking for lung health checks using low-dose scans: that is government policy delivering scientific technology in order to find tumours early enough to remove. Individual choice matters too, since no law forces anyone to stop smoking, and the fall in smoking rested on millions of private decisions.\n\nOn the test of deaths prevented, government action is the most important factor, because prevention removes the disease from far more people than treatment ever cures, and only the state can legislate, tax and run a national screening programme. But its importance is borrowed: it acts on a cause that researchers identified, using technology that scientists built, delivered through a service that treats the patients it fails to protect. Taking the two centuries together the statement is therefore correct, provided government is understood as the factor that puts science to work on a national scale rather than as one working on its own.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask which factor removes the most deaths, not which one is most visible in a hospital.","The strongest counter-argument is to ask what the government can actually do for someone who already has a tumour.","Argue both sides with named policies and named technologies, add a point that shows the factors overlapping, and judge on a stated measure."],"codes":["11-4 — 3 Case studies: The fight against lung cancer in the twenty-first century","11-4 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: New approaches to prevention","11-4 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Improvements in diagnosis"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In which year did Alexander Fleming notice that mould on a culture plate had killed the bacteria around it?","a":"1928."},{"q":"On what date did the National Health Service open?","a":"5 July 1948."},{"q":"Which two scientists published the double-helix structure of DNA in 1953?","a":"James Watson and Francis Crick."},{"q":"Whose 1950 study in the British Medical Journal showed that lung cancer was overwhelmingly a disease of smokers?","a":"Richard Doll and Austin Bradford Hill."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Alexander Fleming","back":"Working at St Mary's Hospital, Paddington, he noticed in 1928 that mould on a culture plate had killed staphylococci. He named penicillin and published in 1929 but could not purify or mass-produce it.","code":"11-4 — 3 Case studies: Key individuals: Fleming, Florey and Chain","open":false},{"front":"Howard Florey and Ernst Chain","back":"Oxford scientists who from 1938 turned Fleming's observation into a drug, with Norman Heatley's production methods. They saved infected mice in 1940 and treated Albert Alexander in 1941.","code":"11-4 — 3 Case studies: Key individuals: Fleming, Florey and Chain","open":false},{"front":"Penicillin","back":"The first antibiotic: a substance from Penicillium mould that kills a wide range of bacteria. Mass-produced by American drug firms during the war, with enough for every Allied casualty by D-Day in June 1944.","code":"11-4 — 3 Case studies: Key individuals: Fleming, Florey and Chain","open":false},{"front":"Magic bullet","back":"Paul Ehrlich's idea of a chemical that kills the microbe causing a disease without poisoning the patient. Unlike an antibiotic, each works on one disease only.","code":"11-4 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment","open":false},{"front":"Salvarsan 606","back":"The first magic bullet, found by Ehrlich's team in 1909: the 606th compound tested, it killed the bacterium causing syphilis. Prontosil, the second, cured streptococcal blood poisoning from 1932.","code":"11-4 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment","open":false},{"front":"The Beveridge Report","back":"Published in December 1942, it named five giants to defeat in reconstruction: Want, Disease, Ignorance, Squalor and Idleness. It proposed a free national health service and sold in huge numbers.","code":"11-4 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment","open":false},{"front":"The National Health Service","back":"Opened on 5 July 1948 under the National Health Service Act of 1946: hospitals, general practice, dentistry and pharmacy free at the point of use, paid for from taxation.","code":"11-4 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment","open":false},{"front":"Aneurin Bevan","back":"Labour Minister of Health from 1945 who created the NHS against opposition from the British Medical Association. He resigned in 1951 when charges for spectacles and dentures were introduced.","code":"11-4 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: The extent of change in care and treatment","open":false},{"front":"Blood tests","back":"Diagnosis from a sample rather than the patient's appearance. Landsteiner identified the blood groups in 1901; blood is now measured for sugar, cholesterol, infection, hormones and DNA.","code":"11-4 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Improvements in diagnosis","open":false},{"front":"The CT scanner","back":"Computerised tomography, developed by Godfrey Hounsfield at EMI; the first patient scan was made in 1971. It builds cross-sections of the body from many X-rays and won Hounsfield a share of the 1979 Nobel Prize.","code":"11-4 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Improvements in diagnosis","open":false},{"front":"The MRI scanner","back":"Magnetic resonance imaging, in hospital use from the 1980s. It uses magnetism rather than X-rays and shows soft tissue such as the brain in detail, without exposing the patient to radiation.","code":"11-4 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Improvements in diagnosis","open":false},{"front":"The double helix of DNA","back":"Published by James Watson and Francis Crick in 1953, using X-ray crystallography images from Rosalind Franklin and Maurice Wilkins at King's College London. It showed how characteristics are inherited.","code":"11-4 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Advances in understanding the causes of illness and disease","open":false},{"front":"The Human Genome Project","back":"An international project running from 1990 to 2003 which mapped every human gene. It allows genetic causes of disease and inherited risks, such as the BRCA genes, to be identified.","code":"11-4 — 1 Ideas about the cause of disease and illness: Advances in understanding the causes of illness and disease","open":false},{"front":"Doll and Hill's study of 1950","back":"Richard Doll and Austin Bradford Hill published evidence in the British Medical Journal that lung cancer was overwhelmingly a disease of smokers. The Royal College of Physicians confirmed the link in 1962.","code":"11-4 — 3 Case studies: The fight against lung cancer in the twenty-first century","open":false},{"front":"The smoking ban of 2007","back":"Smoking in enclosed public places in England was banned from 1 July 2007, and the age of sale was raised from 16 to 18 in the same year.","code":"11-4 — 2 Approaches to prevention and treatment: New approaches to prevention","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 1 Question 5, 16 marks: 'Changes in the way ordinary people live have done more to improve health in Britain than any medical discovery in the years c1900–present.' How far do you agree? Stimulus points: • the decline in smoking • diet and exercise.)","items":["The paragraph: Against the claim, the way people live has grown less healthy in some respects while health has still improved, so lifestyle cannot be the chief cause. Adult smoking did fall, from about 45 per cent of adults in the mid-1970s to about 12 per cent in 2023, but over the same span adult obesity in England roughly doubled, from about 15 per cent in 1993 to about 28 per cent by 2019, and diet and exercise moved the wrong way for many people. Health improved regardless: life expectancy at birth in England and Wales, under fifty years at the start of the twentieth century, was about 79 for men and 83 for women by 2020. The gains came from discoveries that work whatever a patient eats — Ehrlich's team found Salvarsan against syphilis in 1909, Domagk's Prontosil treated streptococcal infection from 1932, and penicillin was mass-produced in time for the D-Day landings of June 1944 — and from vaccination, where the national diphtheria campaign from 1942 and polio vaccination from 1956 all but ended two childhood killers without asking anybody to change a habit. Judged on what saves a life independently of a patient's own choices, medical discovery outranks lifestyle — though only until we ask what it was that persuaded the smokers to stop.","Why this is Level 4: the point is directed consistently at the conceptual focus of importance rather than at describing healthy living; the information is precisely selected (smoking about 45 per cent to about 12 per cent, obesity about 15 to about 28 per cent, Salvarsan 1909, penicillin by June 1944, diphtheria from 1942) and used to prove the point rather than to display knowledge; a criterion — what saves a life independently of a patient's own choices — is stated and applied; and the closing clause carries a sustained line of reasoning forward into the next paragraph instead of stopping dead."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["The paragraph: The way people live is the most important thing because people used to smoke a lot and eat badly, and now they know much more about being healthy, so they live longer. The government kept telling everyone to stop smoking and to eat better and do more exercise, and lots of people did. Medicine mattered as well, because there are far better drugs and machines now than there used to be. Overall changes in lifestyle are the most important reason because in the end it is up to you whether you stay healthy.","Which descriptors it meets and misses: it meets Level 2 because an explanation is given with implicit and unsustained links to importance, some accurate information is included, and an overall judgement is given but asserted rather than justified. It misses Level 3 and Level 4 because no date, figure or name appears — not the fall in adult smoking from about 45 per cent in the mid-1970s, not penicillin, not a single vaccination programme — so the knowledge is generalised; no criterion for importance is stated or applied; and the medical side is mentioned rather than weighed against the lifestyle side. It addresses nothing beyond the two stimulus points either, so it could never pass 11 marks or reach Level 4; there is no longer any cap inside Level 2, so an answer of this kind can still be given the top of the level, 8 marks."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Case study: Fleming, Florey and Chain and the development of penicillin","items":["1928: Alexander Fleming, at St Mary's Hospital, Paddington, notices that a mould growing by accident on a culture plate has killed the staphylococci around it.","He names the active substance penicillin and publishes his observation in 1929.","Fleming cannot purify penicillin or produce it in useful quantities, and he does not pursue it.","From 1938 Howard Florey and Ernst Chain, at the Sir William Dunn School of Pathology in Oxford, take up the problem as a funded research team.","Norman Heatley designs the equipment to grow and extract the mould juice, using improvised containers.","1940: the Oxford team shows that penicillin saves mice given an otherwise fatal dose of bacteria.","February 1941: Albert Alexander, an Oxford policeman with a severe infection, improves dramatically on penicillin but dies when the supply runs out.","1941: Britain's chemical industry is committed to war production, so Florey and Heatley travel to the United States to find manufacturers.","December 1941: the United States enters the war and its government funds drug companies to produce penicillin on an industrial scale.","June 1944: by the D-Day landings there is enough penicillin to treat every Allied casualty.","1945: Fleming, Florey and Chain share the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine.","The factors the case study proves: chance (the contaminated plate), individuals (Fleming's eye, Florey's organisation), science and technology (Heatley's methods), government and war (money and factories).","The limit: bacteria develop resistance, and hospital infections such as MRSA show that antibiotics are not a permanent victory."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Case study: the fight against lung cancer in the twenty-first century","items":["Lung cancer kills more people in Britain than any other cancer, and smoking causes around seven in ten cases.","1950: Richard Doll and Austin Bradford Hill publish in the British Medical Journal the evidence that lung cancer is overwhelmingly a disease of smokers.","1962: the Royal College of Physicians reports that smoking causes lung cancer.","Diagnosis: chest X-ray, CT and PET-CT scans, bronchoscopy to look inside the airways, and a biopsy which is tested for the genetic mutation driving the tumour.","From 2019 the NHS invites people aged 55 to 74 with a history of smoking for lung health checks using low-dose CT scans, so that tumours are found early enough to remove.","Treatment: surgery to remove a lobe of the lung; precisely targeted radiotherapy; chemotherapy.","Treatment: targeted drugs which attack tumours carrying a particular genetic mutation, and immunotherapy, used since the 2010s, which turns the patient's own immune system on the cancer.","Government action on the cause: cigarette advertising banned on television in 1965 and health warnings on packets from the early 1970s.","Government action: smoking banned in enclosed public places in England from 1 July 2007, with the age of sale raised from 16 to 18 in the same year.","Government action: high tobacco taxation, free stop-smoking services and, in Scotland, an earlier ban from March 2006.","Result: adult smoking in Britain has fallen from about 45 per cent in the mid-1970s to under 15 per cent.","The argument the case study supports: science identifies the cause and treats the tumour, but only government can act on the whole population at once."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — The impact of the National Health Service","items":["Before it: the National Insurance Act of 1911 covered an insured worker earning under £160 a year, paid for by fourpence a week from the worker, threepence from the employer and twopence from the state.","The 1911 Act did not cover the worker's wife or children, and hospital treatment still had to be paid for or begged from charity.","The Second World War: evacuation exposed the ill health of poor city children, and the Emergency Medical Service ran the country's hospitals as one system.","December 1942: the Beveridge Report names five giants — Want, Disease, Ignorance, Squalor and Idleness — and proposes a free national health service.","1945: Labour wins the general election; Aneurin Bevan becomes Minister of Health.","1946: the National Health Service Act passes, against opposition from the British Medical Association, which feared doctors would become state employees.","5 July 1948: the NHS opens — hospitals, general practice, dentistry, pharmacy and opticians, free at the point of use and paid for from taxation.","Immediate effect: huge demand for spectacles, dentures and treatments people had gone without for years.","Cost: charges for spectacles and dentures are introduced in 1951, prompting Bevan's resignation, and prescription charges follow in 1952.","What it changed: who received medicine, not what medicine could do — it delivered existing science to the whole population.","What it enabled: national vaccination and screening programmes, and public funding for expensive equipment such as scanners.","Continuing pressures: an ageing population, expensive new drugs and treatments, and lifestyle diseases the service must treat but cannot prevent."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Causes and diagnosis since c1900","items":["1901: Karl Landsteiner identifies the blood groups, making safe transfusion and much blood testing possible.","X-rays, discovered by Röntgen in 1895, spread through British hospital departments in the early 1900s.","The electrocardiograph, developed in the early 1900s, records the electrical activity of the heart.","Ultrasound is used on pregnant women in Glasgow from the late 1950s; fibre-optic endoscopes allow doctors to look inside the body from the 1960s.","1953: James Watson and Francis Crick publish the double-helix structure of DNA, using X-ray crystallography images from Rosalind Franklin and Maurice Wilkins at King's College London.","1971: Godfrey Hounsfield's computerised tomography scanner produces its first patient scan; he shares the Nobel Prize in 1979.","MRI scanners, showing soft tissue without radiation, come into hospital use in the 1980s.","1990 to 2003: the Human Genome Project maps every human gene.","Genetic causes now identified include cystic fibrosis, haemophilia, sickle cell anaemia and Huntington's disease.","The BRCA1 and BRCA2 genes, identified in the mid-1990s, show a very high inherited risk of breast cancer before any symptom appears.","Lifestyle causes established since 1950 include smoking, diet, alcohol, lack of exercise and exposure to sunlight.","Monitors: blood-pressure, heart and oxygen monitors on intensive care beds, and glucose monitors worn at home by diabetics."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Prevention and treatment since c1900","items":["1909: Ehrlich's team finds Salvarsan 606, the first magic bullet, which kills the bacterium causing syphilis.","1932: Gerhard Domagk shows that Prontosil cures streptococcal blood poisoning.","1942: the national diphtheria vaccination campaign sharply reduces a major child killer.","1956: polio vaccination begins in Britain after the epidemics of that decade; measles vaccine follows in 1968 and the combined MMR vaccine in 1988.","1980: the World Health Organisation declares smallpox eradicated worldwide.","Vaccination in Britain is free and strongly encouraged but not compulsory, so take-up depends on public confidence.","1961: thalidomide, given to pregnant women for morning sickness, is withdrawn after causing severe birth defects; the Medicines Act of 1968 introduces proper licensing of drugs.","1962: John Charnley begins hip replacement surgery at Wrightington Hospital.","1967: Christiaan Barnard performs the first heart transplant in Cape Town; the first British heart transplant follows in 1968.","Keyhole surgery spreads from the 1980s and robotic surgery from around 2000, alongside dialysis, pacemakers, radiotherapy and chemotherapy.","Lifestyle campaigns: television cigarette advertising banned in 1965, Change4Life launched in 2009, the sugar levy on soft drinks introduced in 2018.","Nineteenth-century prevention, by contrast, meant sanitation enforced by the Public Health Acts of 1848 and 1875."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 1 Question 5 (16 marks + 4 SPaG): 'Improvements in diagnosis have saved more lives in Britain than improvements in surgery in the years c1900–present.' How far do you agree? Stimulus points: • the X-ray • organ transplants.","items":["Decoding the question: the concept is significance — which kind of improvement did most to keep people alive. The period is the whole modern key topic, so evidence must range across it. 'More than' demands a comparison, so both sides must be argued and a further factor brought in, and the mark scheme's judgement bullet requires a criterion that is stated and then applied.","The criterion to state in the opening: an improvement matters most if it did most to reduce deaths across the whole population, not if it produced the most dramatic single operation.","Paragraph 1 — the case for diagnosis. Evidence: Röntgen discovered X-rays in 1895 and Glasgow Royal Infirmary opened the world's first X-ray department in 1896; blood tests and monitors; the ultrasound scanning of patients that Ian Donald and his colleagues in Glasgow reported in 1958; the first patient scan by computerised tomography, at the Atkinson Morley Hospital on 1 October 1971, and MRI in the 1980s; the screening of whole populations, such as the NHS breast screening programme from 1988 and the targeted lung health checks offered from 2019. Interim judgement: a disease found early, and found in people who still feel well, can be treated while treatment still works.","Paragraph 2 — the case for surgery. Evidence: Britain's first successful kidney transplant, in Edinburgh on 30 October 1960, and the first British heart transplant, at the National Heart Hospital on 3 May 1968; John Charnley's artificial hip, fitted at Wrightington from 1962; heart bypass surgery, and cataract operations now among the commonest operations the health service performs. Interim judgement: surgery ends a condition outright, which no scan can do, but it treats one patient at a time.","Paragraph 3 — the point beyond the stimulus points, needed for Level 4: the largest falls in death since c1900 came from neither the scanner nor the knife. Penicillin, mass-produced by the D-Day landings of June 1944, and the antibiotics that followed ended most deaths from infection; mass vaccination against diphtheria from 1942 and against polio from 1956 stopped diseases occurring; and the fall in adult smoking since the 1970s has done more against lung cancer than any operation. Interim judgement: both halves of the claim are parts of a larger change.","Paragraph 4 — the complication that shows sustained reasoning: diagnosis and surgery are not rivals but a sequence. The lung health checks scan people who have reported no symptom precisely so that a tumour can be removed while it is still small, and keyhole operations are guided by the images the scanners produce.","The evaluation thread running through every paragraph: keep returning to the stated criterion, deaths prevented across the whole population, so that each interim judgement is measured on the same test rather than asserted.","What the conclusion must say: apply the criterion, do not summarise. Diagnosis reaches further, because scanning and screening act on millions of people who are not yet ill and decide which treatment anybody receives, while surgery acts deeply but narrowly, one patient and one operation at a time. The defensible judgement is that improvements in diagnosis have saved more lives, but only because they hand patients to treatments that work, of which surgery is one; a scan by itself cures nobody.","Timing: about 25 minutes for the 16 marks, of which 3 to 4 minutes is planning, plus a final minute checking spelling, punctuation and specialist terms, since 4 further marks are given for those.","Specialist terms that should appear for the SPaG marks: diagnosis, radiography, ultrasound, computerised tomography, screening, transplant, prosthesis, antibiotic."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:14:10.523430Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"All four recorded fixes are in the kit and hold up: room.questions[3] is now the communication of new medical ideas in c1500-c1700 against c1900-present, a focus in no bank question, worked example, plan, model paragraph, the Option 11 specimen (Q3 diagnosis medieval against modern, Q4 the Church, Q5 John Snow, Q6 government action in hospitals) or the June 2023 Option 11 paper, whose Q3 (prevention, medieval against modern), Q4 (access in the modern period), Q5 (Sydenham) and Q6 (attitudes c1700-c1900) I read from the paper itself; room.questions[12] is reframed inside c1900-present, off the specimen Q6 frame; extras[7] is now diagnosis against surgery with • the X-ray • organ transplants, no longer the inverse of question 10; and lesson.examples[0] is on the medicines people could buy, off question 0's focus. dupscan prints 'no duplication'. Every new fact checks out on the web - Vesalius 1543, Harvey 1628, the Royal Society 1660 and Philosophical Transactions 1665, Nature 1953, the Human Genome Project finished 2003, the Pharmacy Act 1868, the MRC streptomycin trial of 1948 as the first randomised controlled trial, thalidomide withdrawn 1961, the Medicines Act 1968, Rontgen 1895 with Macintyre's Glasgow X-ray department of March 1896, Donald's Glasgow ultrasound of 1958, the first patient CT scan at the Atkinson Morley Hospital on 1 October 1971, breast screening from 1988, the Edinburgh kidney transplant of 30 October 1960 and the heart transplant of 3 May 1968 - and I re-solved all fourteen questions against the levels.md grids, with the marks, AO splits, June 2024 cap wording and 80-minute timings all correct; what is left is the 606ᵗʰ-compound level of detail, four small blemishes rather than anything a teacher would hold publication for."}}},"11-env":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"11-env","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Section A of Paper 1 is the only part of this option where you are marked on sources, and it draws on one small place and one short stretch of time: the British sector of the Western Front between 1914 and 1918. The three questions never change shape — describe one feature in each of two parts, how useful are Sources A and B for a named enquiry, and how you would follow Source A up — so every fact you learn here has one of two jobs. Either it is a feature you can state and support in a sentence, or it is the piece of context you use to test whether a stranger's letter, report or photograph can be trusted on the point the enquiry asks about. Students lose marks here not because the history is hard but because they describe the sources instead of judging them, and because they have too little precise knowledge of the trenches, the chain of evacuation and the new surgery to catch a source out.","idea":[{"h":"The ground the medicine had to work on: Ypres, the Somme, Arras and Cambrai","t":"The British sector ran through Flanders in Belgium and northern France, part of a front that stretched roughly 700 kilometres from the North Sea to the Swiss frontier. Four places carry the option. The Ypres salient, held from the First Battle of Ypres in the autumn of 1914, was a bulge of British-held ground with German-held higher ridges on three sides, so it could be shelled from front, left and right; it was fought over again in 1915 and in the Third Battle of Ypres from July to November 1917. The Somme, where the British took over a quieter French sector before the offensive that opened on 1 July 1916, is chalk country with better drainage. Arras, attacked from 9 April 1917, sat on top of medieval quarries. Cambrai, from 20 November 1917, is where stored blood was used. The trench system ran front line, support trench behind it, often some 50 to a few hundred metres back depending on the ground, reserve trench further back again, linked by communication trenches and cut in zigzags so that a shell burst or fire down the trench could not sweep a whole length. Medically the terrain is not scenery: Flanders is low-lying reclaimed farmland, shelling destroyed the field drains, and in the autumn of 1917 the ground turned to liquid mud, so that a stretcher which four men could carry on the Somme took six or eight at Ypres. A top-level answer never lists the battles; it converts each feature of the ground into a delay, and each delay into a wound that had longer to go septic before a surgeon saw it.","code":"11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: The context of the British sector and the theatre of war"},{"h":"What the trenches did to men: illness, shrapnel wounds, head injuries and gas","t":"Two separate problems arrive at the aid post. The first is illness bred by the trench environment. Trench foot came of standing in cold water and mud: the feet swelled, went numb and could turn gangrenous, so that amputation was the only answer left; it was fought with duckboards, dry socks, and whale oil rubbed into the feet. Trench fever gave high temperature, headache and aching limbs that came back at intervals of about five days; once body lice were identified as the carrier, delousing stations were set up behind the lines. Shell shock covered men who shook uncontrollably, could not speak, went blind or were paralysed with no wound on them; some 80,000 British soldiers were recorded with it, and some were treated as cowards rather than patients. The second problem is wounds. Roughly six in every ten British wounds were caused by shells and shrapnel rather than bullets, and shell fragments tear rather than pierce, carrying cloth and earth deep into the tissue. That earth was the danger: this was farmland manured for centuries, so wounds were seeded with the bacteria of tetanus and gas gangrene. Trench warfare also raised the proportion of head wounds, because the head was what a man exposed above the parapet, which is why the steel Brodie helmet, introduced in 1915 and general issue during 1916, mattered so much. Gas came in three waves — chlorine released against the Ypres salient on 22 April 1915, phosgene from the end of that year, mustard gas from July 1917 — and was answered by cotton pads, then smoke helmets, then the small box respirator. A top answer separates illness from wounds, and points out that gas killed comparatively few men but tied up medical services and terrified whole sectors.","code":"11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: Conditions requiring medical treatment"},{"h":"The chain of evacuation, the RAMC and the women who worked in it","t":"The system moved a wounded man backwards in stages, each stage able to do more than the one in front of it. Stretcher bearers, sixteen to a battalion, brought him in — four men to a stretcher in dry conditions, six or eight in mud. The regimental aid post, within about 200 metres of the front line in a dugout or a cellar, was run by the battalion's medical officer and could do no more than dress a wound and give morphine. Behind it the field ambulance, which was a mobile RAMC unit of some 230 men and not a vehicle at all, ran the advanced dressing station a few hundred metres back and the main dressing station further still; it could hold around 150 wounded but only for days. The casualty clearing station, several miles back and deliberately sited on a railway line, was the first place with operating theatres, x-ray equipment and enough staff to sort the incoming: triage divided the walking wounded who could be dressed and sent on, the men who needed surgery at once, and those too badly hurt to be saved. Base hospitals stood at the coastal bases, Étaples, Boulogne and Le Havre among them, and inland at Rouen, a port on the Seine well upriver from the sea; all of them were fed by ambulance trains, and some by canal barges, which were slow but smooth and so suited abdominal and spinal cases. The RAMC grew from about 9,000 officers and men in 1914 to well over 100,000 by 1918. Queen Alexandra's Imperial Military Nursing Service went from fewer than 300 nurses to more than 10,000; Voluntary Aid Detachment volunteers over 23 were allowed overseas from 1915; and the First Aid Nursing Yeomanry, founded in 1907, reached the continent in 1914 and drove ambulances for the British Army from 1916. A top answer treats the chain as a system with a weakest link and names which link failed in which conditions.","code":"11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: Medical treatment on the Western Front"},{"h":"What the front changed: the Thomas splint, x-rays at the clearing station, stored blood, brain and face surgery","t":"The war did not invent much, but it made existing ideas ordinary and it forced new ones where the old ones failed. The Thomas splint, designed by Hugh Owen Thomas of Liverpool and pressed upon the army by his nephew Robert Jones from 1915, held a broken leg straight under traction while the man was still on the stretcher, so the bone ends stopped tearing the flesh and the bleeding slowed; survival from a fractured thigh rose from roughly one man in five to about four in five. Mobile x-ray units — lorries carrying a machine and its own generator — moved between casualty clearing stations so that fragments could be located before the knife went in, although the tube overheated after about an hour and glass, cloth and some fragments never showed on the plate. Because aseptic surgery was impossible in a tent on manured ground, surgeons went back to cutting: wound excision removed the dead and dirty tissue until the edges bled clean, and from 1917 the Carrel-Dakin method ran a sterilised salt solution through rubber tubing into the wound, a solution so unstable it had to be mixed fresh. Transfusion moved forward fastest: sodium citrate was shown to stop blood clotting, glucose was added as a preservative, and a store of bottled, citrated blood packed in ice was built up at a casualty clearing station before the Battle of Cambrai in November 1917, keeping blood usable for about four weeks. Harvey Cushing drew metal out of brain wounds with a magnet and operated under local rather than general anaesthetic, and Harold Gillies built facial reconstruction into a speciality with the tubed pedicle graft. A top answer weighs the significance of these: which were genuinely new, which were old ideas finally made routine, and which only worked because the war supplied endless cases.","code":"11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: The significance of the Western Front for experiments in surgery and medicine"},{"h":"Where medicine already stood in 1914","t":"Nothing on the Western Front makes sense unless you know what surgeons arrived with. Germ theory was half a century old, so infection was understood as caused by microbes rather than bad air, and hospitals at home had moved past Lister's carbolic acid to aseptic surgery: steam-sterilised instruments, scrubbed theatres, gowns, masks and rubber gloves. That is precisely the standard that a tent in Flanders could not meet, which is why antiseptic methods came back into use at the front. X-rays had existed since Röntgen described them in 1895 and were in British hospitals within a year, so the front did not discover them, it made them mobile. Blood transfusion was the weakest of the three: Karl Landsteiner had identified blood groups in 1901, but until the middle of the war blood had to run directly from a donor's vein into the patient's in the same room, which was useless at a casualty clearing station. Sodium citrate, given at the right dose from 1915, stopped the clotting; glucose added in 1916 let citrated blood be stored on ice. A top answer uses this section to date a claim precisely: it separates what the war inherited, what the war improved, and what the war genuinely created, and it refuses the lazy line that the First World War invented modern medicine.","code":"11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: The historical context of medicine in the early twentieth century"},{"h":"National sources: what the state, the army and the medical profession wrote down","t":"For this enquiry the national records are unusually good, because the army counted everything and doctors published. Unit war diaries of RAMC field ambulances and casualty clearing stations record daily admissions, evacuations and deaths. The War Office published a statistical volume after the war giving casualties by cause, and the official medical history of the war set out how the chain of evacuation was meant to work and how it was altered. Articles in The Lancet and the British Medical Journal carried surgeons' own accounts of wound excision, splinting and transfusion while the war was still going on, with their own figures. Above these sit the papers of the Army Medical Services, the Medical Research Committee and the War Office held at the National Archives, and the mass-circulation national newspapers, whose reporting was subject to censorship. A top answer chooses a national source when the question asks about scale, policy or comparison — how many, how often, across which armies — and says so in the same breath as naming it.","code":"11-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Knowledge of national sources"},{"h":"Local sources: the individual, the unit and the hospital","t":"Local, in this option, means the sources produced by one man, one unit or one hospital rather than by the state. Soldiers' letters and diaries, the accounts nurses and ambulance drivers wrote up afterwards, and the interviews recorded with survivors decades later all sit here. So do the admission and discharge registers of a particular casualty clearing station or base hospital, individual medical and service records, burial and cemetery registers for a named place, and photographs — both the official ones passed by the censor and the private ones taken against orders. A top answer picks a local source when the question is about experience, sequence or one particular case, and it says plainly what a local source cannot do: one man's letter cannot tell you whether his experience was typical, so it is used to test the general picture, not to prove it.","code":"11-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Knowledge of local sources"},{"h":"Strengths and weaknesses: judging a source for this enquiry, not in general","t":"No source is useful or useless on its own; it is useful for something. Work with the mark scheme's three words. Nature is what kind of thing it is: a private letter can admit what an official report cannot, a photograph shows what was in front of the lens but not what happened before or after, a set of statistics gives scale but no experience. Origin is who made it, where and when: a surgeon at a casualty clearing station in October 1917 was inside the Third Battle of Ypres and knew the mud himself, but he could not see the base hospital or the whole army. Purpose is what it was made to do: to inform a friend, to request more men, to reassure the public, to advertise a method the writer had developed. Then push provenance onto a single claim rather than the whole source, because the reward is for reasoning about how provenance affects the usefulness of the content. A top answer ends each source with a sentence of the form 'so this is a good guide to X but a poor guide to Y', and keeps the criterion the same for both sources so that the comparison at the end means something.","code":"11-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Recognition of the strengths and weaknesses of different types of source"},{"h":"Framing a question the enquiry actually needs answering","t":"The follow-up asks you to behave like a historian who has read one document and wants a second. Start from a detail in the source that is specific but unverified — a number the writer gives with nothing behind it, a claim about how long something took, a method described but not evaluated, a comparison the writer makes with the past. A good follow-up question then does one of three things: it tests whether the detail was typical or exceptional, it asks how far back or forward the pattern ran, or it asks about a consequence the source hints at but does not follow. A bad question is one that nothing could answer, or one whose answer the source has already given. The wording matters: put a measurable word in it — how many, how long, how often, what proportion, in which months — because a question with a measurable word attached tells the examiner at once that you know which record would settle it. A top answer writes the question and the record together, and never asks something that the type of source it then names could not possibly hold.","code":"11-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Framing of questions relevant to the pursuit of a specific enquiry"},{"h":"Choosing the source that fits the question you have just written","t":"The last two marks are for naming a type of source and saying what it would show. Match the kind of question to the kind of record. Questions of scale and frequency go to the counting records: unit war diaries, admission and evacuation registers, War Office casualty statistics. Questions about whether a treatment worked go to the medical literature of the time and to hospital case notes, because those carry outcomes as well as procedures. Questions about experience, or about what was concealed, go to letters, diaries and private photographs, and to material written after the war when censorship had gone. Name the type of record rather than a single famous document, and then finish with the sentence that earns the fourth mark: what it would let you do — compare, count, confirm, or catch the writer exaggerating. A top answer makes the last box do work, because saying that a source 'would tell me more' is worth nothing.","code":"11-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Selection of appropriate sources for specific investigations"}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1: one feature of the casualty clearing station and one of the regimental aid post","setup":"The question is printed in two parts: '1(a) Describe one feature of the casualty clearing stations on the Western Front. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the regimental aid post. [2 marks]' Each part carries one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for the supporting information attached to it, and the two parts are set on separate aspects, so nothing used in (a) can be used again in (b). What you know: casualty clearing stations stood several miles behind the front line and were deliberately sited beside a railway, usually in huts and marquees; they commonly worked in groups of three on one siding, taking the convoys of wounded in turn. They were the first point in the chain of evacuation with operating theatres, x-ray equipment and enough staff for surgery, and on arrival the wounded were sorted by triage into the walking wounded, who were dressed and sent on by train, those who needed an operation at once, and those whose wounds were judged beyond saving. The regimental aid post, by contrast, stood in a dugout, a cellar or a ruined building within about 200 metres of the front line, was run by the battalion's medical officer, and could do no more than dress a wound, give morphine and send the man back down the chain. Write one feature and its supporting information for each part.","steps":["Settle which aspect belongs to which box before you write anything, because a fact spent in one part cannot be spent again in the other: give (a) the clearing station, taking where it stood and what it alone could do there, and keep the aid post, its distance forward and its narrow powers, for (b).","Write 1(a) as a flat statement with its support attached: casualty clearing stations were placed several miles back on a railway line, in huts and tents, and the support is that they often worked in threes on one siding so that each took the convoys of wounded in its turn, and that this was the first stage of the chain that could operate and sort men by triage.","Write 1(b) the same way on the other aspect: the regimental aid post was the first stage a wounded man reached after the bearers picked him up, run by his own battalion's medical officer, and the support is that it lay within about 200 metres of the front line in a dugout or cellar and could only dress the wound and give morphine before he was carried further back.","Now do what the two-part form asks and check each box separately against its own two marks: read (a) and then (b) and confirm that each holds one stated feature plus one piece of attached knowledge — a distance, a number, a named practice — and that neither borrows the other's fact; here (a) rests on the siding and the three stations working in turn and (b) on the 200 metres and the morphine, so both parts earn their second mark and each stands alone."],"cues":["Which stage of the chain does each part of this question ask about?","What can you say about where the clearing station stood, and what proves it?","How far forward was the aid post, and what could it actually do there?","How would you check that each part has earned both of its own marks?"]},{"title":"Question 2(b): following up a detail about the men judged beyond saving in Source A","setup":"The question is: 'How could you follow up Source A to find out more about the care given to men whose wounds were judged beyond saving? In your answer, you must give the question you would ask and the type of source you could use.' It carries 4 marks in four boxes: the detail, the question, the type of source, and how it would help. Source A is a letter of October 1917 from an RAMC surgeon at a casualty clearing station near Poperinghe. In it he writes that the orderlies sort the men as they come off the ambulances into three lots — the walking cases, who are dressed and put on the train for the base; the men who must go on the table before morning; and a third tent, which he says he would rather not describe, where they make comfortable those who will not see the morning at all. That third lot gets one sentence and then silence: the letter says the orderlies did the sorting, but never says what they judged by, and it gives no number and nothing at all about what was done in the tent. Fill the four boxes.","steps":["Take the detail he names and then refuses to open up: the third tent for the men who will not see the morning, which he sets beside the other two lots and then declines to describe.","Turn that silence into a question with a countable word in it: what proportion of the men admitted to a casualty clearing station on this front in the autumn of 1917 were placed in that third group?","Name a type of record that could actually hold the answer: the admission and discharge registers kept by the casualty clearing stations themselves, read with their unit war diaries and with the burial registers of the cemetery that grew beside such a siding.","Say what that record would let you do, which is the mark most often thrown away: the registers enter every man admitted and what became of him, so the third group could be counted against the whole night's intake instead of being left as the one thing the surgeon would not put in a letter."],"cues":["Which of his three lots does he name and then refuse to describe?","What single word would make your question answerable by counting?","Which kind of record follows every man admitted through to what became of him?","Why is 'it would tell me more about the dying' worth nothing here?"]},{"title":"Question 2(a): getting Source A from content to provenance","setup":"The question is: 'How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the pressure that the number of wounded put on the medical services in the Ypres sector in 1917? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context.' It carries 8 marks and is levelled. Source A is a private letter written in October 1917 by an RAMC surgeon at a casualty clearing station near Poperinghe to the professor of surgery who taught him. He writes that there are three clearing stations together on one railway siding and that they take the convoys in turn, so that on their night they may pass four hundred men through the reception marquee between dusk and dawn. He adds that his complaint is not the numbers but the hours, and that the unit's x-ray tube will not run above the hour before it must be rested, so the plates are taken in turn and a man with a fragment in him may wait. This example works only on Source A, and only on the first moves: what it shows, and what its nature, origin and purpose do to that.","steps":["State what the content gives the enquiry and name the figure that gives it: the letter puts a number on a night's work, four hundred men through one reception marquee between dusk and dawn, and shows the load being shared out rather than carried by one unit, three stations on the siding taking the convoys in turn.","Turn nature and origin onto that claim rather than onto the letter as a whole: a private letter to a former teacher did not pass through the unit censor who read the men's letters, an officer signing for his own mail and base censors only spot-checking it, so the figure was not written for any authority of his own to read, and the writer stood at the receiving end of that October night in 1917 and counted the men in front of him.","Use the purpose to qualify one claim rather than to dismiss the source: he is writing to impress an old teacher with what can be done in a tent, which is a reason to treat his line that his complaint is not the numbers more cautiously than the numbers themselves, since a man showing his teacher he is on top of the work has every reason to make the load sound carried.","Bring in one piece of context that interprets rather than decorates: clearing stations were commonly grouped in threes on one siding and took the incoming convoys in turn precisely because no single station could absorb the intake of an offensive, so his three-in-turn is the army's standing answer to pressure of this kind and evidence of what was expected of that autumn, which lies inside the Third Battle of Ypres of 31 July to 10 November 1917."],"cues":["What does this letter let you count that a description of the medical services would not?","What difference does it make that this was written to a teacher and not to a superior officer?","Which single claim in the letter should his purpose make you cautious about?","Why were three stations put on one siding, and what does that tell you about the load?"]},{"title":"Question 2(a): what a censored photograph is and is not good for","setup":"The same 8-mark question runs on Source B: 'How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the pressure that the number of wounded put on the medical services in the Ypres sector in 1917?' Source B is an official photograph taken on the Ypres front in the autumn of 1917 by a photographer attached to the British Army, passed by the army censor and published in a British illustrated weekly in March 1918 under the caption 'Bringing them in'; the printed caption told readers the wounded were being brought in with all possible speed to the care of the doctors. The picture shows a duckboard track over liquid mud and water-filled shell holes, six men carrying one stretcher on which a man's leg is held straight in a long splint, a horse-drawn wagon down to its axle off the boards, an officer with a Red Cross brassard beside a stack of empty stretchers, three men with bandaged eyes sitting on a stack of duckboards outside a marquee while an orderly kneels to label the nearest of them, and a ruined farm flying a Red Cross flag with a motor ambulance drawn up beside it. Four men to a stretcher was the establishment. Judge the usefulness of this source for the enquiry.","steps":["Take the content the picture can be trusted on because it is physically in the frame: six men are carrying one stretcher where four were the establishment, so the photograph prices a single casualty in manpower, and a single motor ambulance stands at the ruined farm in the distance with its rear doors open.","Notice the thing the caption did not intend to show, which is usually the most useful part of an official image: three men with bandaged eyes sit waiting on a stack of duckboards outside a marquee while a single orderly kneels to tie a label to the coat of the nearest of them, so the picture shows men still waiting to be dealt with while the words beneath it promise all possible speed.","Turn nature, origin and purpose onto the caption rather than onto the image: an official photographer working under censorship for publication at home was there to reassure families, so the claim of all possible speed is exactly what the source is least useful for, while the bearers and the waiting men are what it is most useful for, because those could not be arranged away.","Close with the limit a photograph always has: it fixes one moment on one track and can say nothing about how many men crossed it that day or that week, so it can show what one casualty cost in men and minutes but cannot measure the load on a service."],"cues":["Which details in this picture could not have been staged away?","How many bearers should a stretcher have needed, and what does the number here cost in men?","Who was this photograph published for, and what would they have wanted to read?","What can no single photograph ever tell you about a load carried over weeks?"]},{"title":"Question 2(a): writing the comparative judgement that lifts the answer","setup":"You have already judged both sources for the enquiry into the pressure that the number of wounded put on the medical services in the Ypres sector in 1917. On Source A, the private letter of October 1917 from the RAMC surgeon near Poperinghe, you have argued that its escape from the unit censor who read the men's letters, and the writer's position at the receiving end, make its count of four hundred men in a night strong, while its purpose of impressing a former teacher makes his insistence that the numbers are not his complaint weaker. On Source B, the official photograph of the autumn of 1917 published under the caption 'Bringing them in', you have argued that the physical contents of the frame are trustworthy while the caption's claim of speed is the least trustworthy thing about it. Nothing remains but the final judgement, which is where most answers either earn or lose the top level. Write it.","steps":["Fix the criterion you will judge by before you compare anything, and make it the enquiry's own words: usefulness here means showing how many men the services had to handle, and what each one of them cost in people, hours and equipment.","Rank the sources on that criterion rather than in general: Source A is the more useful, because it alone gives a figure and the period it covers, four hundred men between dusk and dawn, and shows three stations rotating to carry it.","Give Source B the thing it is better at, so that the judgement is weighed and not a dismissal: the photograph prices one casualty where the letter counts heads, six bearers to a stretcher, and men with bandaged eyes still waiting outside the marquee while one orderly labels the nearest of them, and its official caption is itself evidence of the gap between the effort as advertised and the effort as worked.","Finish with the qualification that shows you know what neither can do: both are single moments from one sector in one autumn, so neither can show whether that night's intake was ordinary or exceptional, and the enquiry would need the units' own admission registers and war diaries for that."],"cues":["What exactly would count as 'useful' for this particular enquiry?","Which source answers that measure directly?","What is the weaker source still better at than the stronger one?","What do the two sources share that limits them both?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Put the stages of the chain of evacuation in order and say which stage was the first that could perform surgery.","a":"Stretcher bearers, regimental aid post, field ambulance and dressing stations (advanced then main), casualty clearing station, base hospital, then home. The casualty clearing station was the first with operating theatres and triage."},{"q":"Name the three gases used on the Western Front in the order they appeared, with the date or year of each.","a":"Chlorine, released in a large attack on the Ypres salient on 22 April 1915; phosgene, from the end of 1915; mustard gas, from July 1917."},{"q":"What did sodium citrate and glucose each contribute to blood transfusion, and what did they make possible by November 1917?","a":"Sodium citrate stopped the blood clotting, so it no longer had to pass directly from donor to patient; glucose, added in 1916, preserved it. Together they allowed bottled blood packed in ice to be stored at a casualty clearing station before the Battle of Cambrai and kept usable for about four weeks."},{"q":"The mark scheme defines provenance as three things. What are they, and what must you do with them to reach the top level?","a":"Nature, origin and purpose. They must be aimed at a named claim inside the source to show how they make that claim more or less useful for the stated enquiry; generic comments on provenance that are not used to evaluate the content earn nothing."},{"q":"Why did the same wound carry a far higher risk of gas gangrene on the Western Front than in a London hospital?","a":"Because shells drove earth and cloth deep into the tissue, and the soil of Flanders and northern France had been manured for centuries, so it was rich in the bacteria of gas gangrene and tetanus; and the aseptic conditions of a hospital theatre could not be reproduced in a tent."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 1 Section A is worth 16 marks in all: Question 1, printed as two parts — 1(a) 'Describe one feature of…' (2 marks) and 1(b) 'Describe one feature of…' (2 marks), each on a separate aspect of content — then Question 2(a) 'How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into…? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context.' (8 marks) and Question 2(b) 'How could you follow up Source A to find out more about…? In your answer, you must give the question you would ask and the type of source you could use.' (4 marks).","Questions 1 and 2(b) are point-marked, not levelled. On each part of Question 1, one mark for the valid feature given and a second mark for the supporting information attached to it, so 1(a) and 1(b) are worth 2 marks each and nothing used in 1(a) can be used again in 1(b). On Question 2(b), one mark for a detail selected from Source A, one for a question linked to that detail, one for naming an appropriate type of source, and one for showing how that source would help answer the question.","Question 2(a) is AO3 and levelled, with Level 3 at 6–8 marks: judgements on usefulness for the specified enquiry, applying valid criteria with developed reasoning which takes into account how the provenance affects the usefulness of the source content, the sources analysed to support that reasoning, and contextual knowledge used in the process of interpreting them. Provenance means nature, origin and purpose.","Two rules printed under the 2(a) grid account for most of the marks lost on it: no credit may be given for contextual knowledge unless it is linked to evaluation of the sources, and no credit may be given for generic comments on provenance which are not used to evaluate source content.","Section A prints no stimulus points and carries no SPaG marks; both of those belong elsewhere on the paper. Working at roughly a minute a mark gives about 2 minutes for each part of Question 1, 10 to 13 minutes for Question 2(a) and 4 to 5 minutes for Question 2(b), inside the 80 minutes of the whole paper.","Question 2(b) is answered in four printed boxes, in this order: the detail in Source A that you would follow up, the question you would ask, the type of source you could use, and how it might help answer your question. Each box must follow from the one before it, and a question not linked to following up Source A scores nothing."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the trench system on the British sector of the Western Front. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the Ypres salient. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks in two parts of 2, point-marked: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 mark for supporting information, and the two parts must stay on separate aspects. For 1(a) credit the several parallel lines and their spacing, the communication trenches, the zigzag of fire bays and traverses, dugouts and the firestep, or no man's land between the front lines. For 1(b) credit the bulge of British-held ground, the German-held ridges overlooking it on three sides, the shelling from three directions, the low-lying reclaimed farmland whose drainage the shelling destroyed, or the years it was fought over.","sol":"1(a) Feature: the system was built of several parallel lines rather than one ditch. Supporting information: a front line trench was backed by a support trench, often some 50 to a few hundred metres behind it depending on the ground, and a reserve trench further back again, with communication trenches running at right angles between them so that men, rations, ammunition and stretchers could move up and down without crossing open ground. 1(b) Feature: the Ypres salient was a bulge of British-held ground overlooked by the enemy on three sides. Supporting information: the Germans held the higher ridges around Ypres from the first battle there in the autumn of 1914, so the salient could be shelled from front, left and right, and it was fought over again in 1915 and in the Third Battle of Ypres from July to November 1917.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a) wants the shape of the system; part (b) wants the piece of ground the British held around one Flemish town.","Each part is marked 1 for the feature and 1 for the support, so attach a distance, a named term or a date to each.","Write each part as one plain sentence of fact followed by a second sentence carrying the evidence, and keep what belongs to one part out of the other."],"codes":["11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: The context of the British sector and the theatre of war"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of trench foot. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of trench fever. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks in two parts of 2, point-marked: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 mark for supporting information, on two separate conditions. For 1(a) credit its cause in the trench environment, its symptoms and progression to gangrene, amputation as the only late treatment, or the preventive measures of duckboards, dry socks and whale oil. For 1(b) credit its carriage by body lice, its symptoms, the fever returning at intervals of about five days, or the delousing stations set up behind the lines.","sol":"1(a) Feature: trench foot was caused by the trench environment rather than by the enemy. Supporting information: men who stood for days in trenches with cold water and mud in the bottom lost the circulation in their feet, which swelled, went numb and blistered, and if it was left the tissue turned gangrenous and the only treatment remaining was amputation, so the army attacked it by prevention with duckboards, dry socks and whale oil. 1(b) Feature: trench fever was an illness passed from man to man by body lice. Supporting information: it brought high temperature, headache and aching limbs that came back at intervals of about five days, and once lice were identified as the carrier delousing stations and baths were set up behind the lines.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Both parts are illnesses bred by the trenches rather than wounds, so keep what belongs to the feet out of the answer on the fever.","The supporting mark is easiest to earn on this topic by naming the measures the army actually ordered against each.","Two sentences a box: what it was or what caused it, then the detail that proves you know it."],"codes":["11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: Conditions requiring medical treatment"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the use of poison gas on the Western Front. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the steel helmet issued to British troops. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks in two parts of 2, point-marked: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 mark for supporting information, on two separate aspects. For 1(a) credit the succession of chlorine, phosgene and mustard gas with their dates and effects, the development of protection from cotton pads to the box respirator, or the low ratio of deaths to casualties. For 1(b) credit the Brodie helmet's introduction in 1915 and general issue during 1916, why head wounds were so common in trench warfare, what it was designed to stop, or the fall in head wounds after it.","sol":"1(a) Feature: several different gases were used in turn and each injured in a different way. Supporting information: chlorine, released in a large attack on the Ypres salient on 22 April 1915, attacked the lungs and killed by suffocation; phosgene, used from the end of 1915, acted faster and in smaller quantities; and mustard gas, first used in July 1917, blistered the skin, burned the lining of the airways and could blind a man for weeks. 1(b) Feature: the steel Brodie helmet was issued to protect the head, which was the part of a man that a trench left exposed. Supporting information: head wounds were unusually common because a man had to raise his head above the parapet, and the helmet, introduced in 1915 and in general issue during 1916, was shaped to turn shrapnel balls and falling fragments rather than a direct rifle bullet, after which head wounds fell sharply.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a) is a weapon that changed during the war; part (b) is the piece of equipment that answered the commonest kind of wound.","Dates and the names of the gases, or the years the helmet came in, are what turn a feature into two marks here.","Keep to what each was and what it did; do not argue about whether either was effective."],"codes":["11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: Conditions requiring medical treatment"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the chain of evacuation for wounded soldiers on the Western Front. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the work of women in the medical services on the Western Front. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks in two parts of 2, point-marked: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 mark for supporting information, on two separate aspects. For 1(a) credit the ordered stages from stretcher bearers through the regimental aid post, field ambulance and dressing stations, casualty clearing station and base hospital; the siting of each; triage; or the changing means of transport at each stage. For 1(b) credit the growth of Queen Alexandra's Imperial Military Nursing Service, the Voluntary Aid Detachment volunteers allowed overseas from 1915, the First Aid Nursing Yeomanry driving ambulances for the army, or the nursing done at casualty clearing stations and base hospitals.","sol":"1(a) Feature: the chain moved a wounded man backwards through fixed stages, each able to do more than the last. Supporting information: stretcher bearers carried him to the regimental aid post, sited within about 200 metres of the front line, then he went on to the dressing stations run by the field ambulance, then to the casualty clearing station, which was the first stage with operating theatres, and finally to a base hospital. 1(b) Feature: women served in the medical services in far greater numbers as the war went on, and at every stage from the casualty clearing stations back to the base hospitals. Supporting information: Queen Alexandra's Imperial Military Nursing Service grew from fewer than 300 nurses in 1914 to more than 10,000, Voluntary Aid Detachment volunteers over the age of 23 were allowed to serve overseas from 1915, and the First Aid Nursing Yeomanry, founded in 1907, drove ambulances for the British Army from 1916.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a) is a system with stages; part (b) is about who staffed the medicine besides the men of the RAMC.","Naming the stages in the right order is the fact in (a); a number, a date or a service's name is the support in (b).","One box each, and no account of what was done to the wounded man once he arrived."],"codes":["11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: Medical treatment on the Western Front"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the underground hospital at Arras. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the Thomas splint. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks in two parts of 2, point-marked: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 mark for supporting information, on two separate aspects. For 1(a) credit its construction in the tunnels and quarries beneath Arras by tunnelling companies, its position close to the front line, its equipment and capacity, or the brevity of its use during the 1917 offensive. For 1(b) credit what it did to a broken leg, its use on the stretcher rather than in theatre, Hugh Owen Thomas as its designer and Robert Jones pressing it on the army from 1915, or the rise in survival from a fractured thigh.","sol":"1(a) Feature: the hospital was cut into the ground beneath the town rather than built on it. Supporting information: tunnellers, mainly New Zealanders, linked the medieval quarries under Arras into a network that sheltered thousands of troops before the offensive that opened on 9 April 1917, and the hospital built into it had about 700 beds, electric light, piped water and an operating theatre, yet it was abandoned during the battle. 1(b) Feature: the Thomas splint held a broken leg straight under traction. Supporting information: designed before the war by Hugh Owen Thomas of Liverpool and pressed upon the army by his nephew Robert Jones from 1915, it was applied while the man was still on the stretcher, so the bone ends stopped tearing the flesh and the bleeding slowed, and survival from a fractured thigh rose from roughly one man in five to about four in five.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a) belongs to the ground under one town; part (b) to a piece of equipment carried up to the wounded man.","A capacity, a date or a survival rate is the detail that earns the second mark in each part.","State and support in each box; do not argue about how significant either was."],"codes":["11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: Medical treatment on the Western Front","11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: The context of the British sector and the theatre of war","11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: The significance of the Western Front for experiments in surgery and medicine"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the storage of blood for transfusion by 1917. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the use of x-rays on the Western Front. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks in two parts of 2, point-marked: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 mark for supporting information, on two separate aspects. For 1(a) credit the chemical treatment of blood with sodium citrate and glucose, the end of direct donor-to-patient transfusion, the bottling and icing of blood, the blood depot built up before Cambrai, or the length of time blood could be kept. For 1(b) credit the mobile x-ray units and their own generators, the x-ray equipment at the casualty clearing station, the locating of fragments before surgery, the overheating of the tube after about an hour, or what the plates could not show.","sol":"1(a) Feature: blood could be kept in advance only because it was treated chemically. Supporting information: sodium citrate, used at the right dose from 1915, stopped it clotting so that it no longer had to run straight from a donor's arm into a patient lying in the same room, glucose added in 1916 preserved it further, and bottles of citrated blood packed in ice were assembled at a casualty clearing station before the Battle of Cambrai in November 1917. 1(b) Feature: x-ray machines were made mobile so that they could be brought to the wounded. Supporting information: lorries carrying a machine and its own generator moved between casualty clearing stations, where plates located metal fragments before a surgeon cut, although the tube overheated after about an hour and had to be rested, and glass, cloth and some fragments never showed on the plate at all.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a) is about chemistry and what it let an army do before a battle; part (b) is about taking a machine of 1895 up towards the front.","Two years and one battle will carry the support in (a); a limit of the machine will carry it in (b).","Describe the storage and the use; do not explain why either mattered."],"codes":["11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: The significance of the Western Front for experiments in surgery and medicine","11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: The historical context of medicine in the early twentieth century"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. How could you follow up Source A to find out more about the training and experience of the surgeons working at the casualty clearing stations on the Western Front? In your answer, you must give the question you would ask and the type of source you could use. Complete the four boxes: Detail in Source A that I would follow up / Question I would ask / What type of source I could use / How this might help answer my question. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for a detail from Source A that could form the basis of a follow-up enquiry, 1 for a question linked to it, 1 for naming an appropriate type of source, 1 for explaining how it would help. Credit following up his closing claim about what his teacher told him in 1908, the fact that he learnt his surgery in a London teaching hospital, or how surgeons at the front came by the methods he now uses.","sol":"Detail in Source A that I would follow up: his closing claim that he and his colleagues are doing in a tent what his professor told them in 1908 could not be done outside a teaching hospital, written to the man who taught him surgery in London. Question I would ask: how were surgeons who had trained in civilian hospitals before the war taught the methods of war surgery, such as cutting a wound back until the tissue bleeds clean, once they were serving in France? What type of source I could use: the printed memoranda on the treatment of injuries in war that the War Office issued to army medical officers from 1915, together with the syllabuses and war diaries of the schools of instruction the armies ran for their medical officers in France. How this might help answer my question: those records set out what officers were formally taught and when the teaching was changed, so I could tell whether his methods were the ones the army was issuing to everybody or ones worked out at his own table, which a letter written to impress a teacher cannot settle.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The letter ends by addressing a teacher and making a claim about what that teacher once said was impossible; that sentence is the detail to work on.","A question about how something was learnt can only be followed up with a record of teaching, so settle what was taught before you settle who wrote it down.","Fill the fourth box by naming what the record would let you separate — army practice from one man's own practice — not merely that it would tell you more."],"codes":["11-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Framing of questions relevant to the pursuit of a specific enquiry","11-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Selection of appropriate sources for specific investigations","11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: Medical treatment on the Western Front"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. How could you follow up Source A to find out more about the treatment of wound infection on the Western Front? In your answer, you must give the question you would ask and the type of source you could use. Complete the four boxes: Detail in Source A that I would follow up / Question I would ask / What type of source I could use / How this might help answer my question. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for a detail, 1 for a linked question, 1 for an appropriate type of source, 1 for how it would help. Credit following up the excision of tissue, the salt solution run through tubing, the freshness of the solution, or the claim about manured ground and gas gangrene.","sol":"Detail in Source A that I would follow up: his description of cutting until the tissue bleeds clean and then running the salt solution through rubber tubing every two hours, which the orderlies must mix fresh each day. Question I would ask: what proportion of wounds treated in this way at casualty clearing stations in 1917 healed without amputation or death from gas gangrene, compared with wounds simply dressed? What type of source I could use: the admission and discharge registers of casualty clearing stations, read alongside the articles that army surgeons published in British medical journals during the war reporting their own results. How this might help answer my question: the registers follow each man from admission to discharge, transfer or death, so they give outcomes rather than intentions, and the published articles would show whether other surgeons using the method got the same results as this one, which his letter alone cannot establish.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Read past what he does at the table and ask what he never reports about the men it was done to.","Put a word into your question that could be counted, then ask yourself which record does the counting.","One record for the outcomes and one for whether other surgeons agreed is a stronger answer than either alone."],"codes":["11-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Framing of questions relevant to the pursuit of a specific enquiry","11-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Selection of appropriate sources for specific investigations","11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: The significance of the Western Front for experiments in surgery and medicine"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. How could you follow up Source A to find out more about the use of the Thomas splint on the Western Front? In your answer, you must give the question you would ask and the type of source you could use. Complete the four boxes: Detail in Source A that I would follow up / Question I would ask / What type of source I could use / How this might help answer my question. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for a detail, 1 for a linked question, 1 for an appropriate type of source, 1 for how it would help. Credit following up his before-and-after claim about femur cases, the date the splint reached his unit, or its use on the stretcher rather than in theatre.","sol":"Detail in Source A that I would follow up: his claim that before the long Thomas splint arrived he buried most of his femur cases and that now he buries few. Question I would ask: in which months of 1915 and 1916 did the Thomas splint reach the units on this front, and did the recorded deaths from fractured thighs fall after it did? What type of source I could use: the admission and discharge registers and the war diaries of a casualty clearing station covering the period before and after the splint came into use, together with the burial registers for the cemetery beside it. How this might help answer my question: the registers and burial records give counts of femur cases and of deaths month by month, so I could see whether the fall he describes shows up in the numbers or whether he is remembering a change that also owed something to faster evacuation.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["One sentence in the letter compares two periods of his own service; ask what evidence he offers for either.","A claim about a change over time can only be followed up with a record that covers both sides of the change.","The fourth box is where you show the record could also disprove him, not only confirm him."],"codes":["11-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Framing of questions relevant to the pursuit of a specific enquiry","11-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Knowledge of local sources","11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: The significance of the Western Front for experiments in surgery and medicine"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. How could you follow up Source A to find out more about the use of blood transfusion on the Western Front? In your answer, you must give the question you would ask and the type of source you could use. Complete the four boxes: Detail in Source A that I would follow up / Question I would ask / What type of source I could use / How this might help answer my question. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for a detail, 1 for a linked question, 1 for an appropriate type of source, 1 for how it would help. Credit following up the single bottled transfusion he describes, where the blood came from, how it was kept, or how common the practice was by late 1917.","sol":"Detail in Source A that I would follow up: the single case he gives of a sergeant who received a pint of blood out of a bottle, drawn from a convalescent two days earlier and kept on ice with citrate in it. Question I would ask: how widely was stored, citrated blood being used across the British sector by the end of 1917, and where did the donors come from? What type of source I could use: the medical articles published in British medical journals at the time by the officers who organised transfusion, together with the War Office and Army Medical Services papers on the supply of transfusion equipment and stores. How this might help answer my question: those records would show whether bottled blood was still an experiment carried out by a few enthusiasts or an established supply by late 1917, which one surgeon describing one patient on one Tuesday cannot tell me either way.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["One patient is not a practice; ask yourself what the letter cannot tell you about how common this was.","Questions about how widespread something was go to records kept above the level of the single unit.","Name the kind of record rather than one famous document, then say what reading it would settle."],"codes":["11-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Framing of questions relevant to the pursuit of a specific enquiry","11-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Knowledge of national sources","11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: The significance of the Western Front for experiments in surgery and medicine"]},{"q":"Study Sources A and B in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into what people at home in Britain were shown and told about the care of the wounded on the Western Front? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks, levelled. Level 3 (6–8) needs judgements on usefulness for this enquiry with developed reasoning on how nature, origin and purpose affect the usefulness of the content, and contextual knowledge used in interpreting the sources. Credit Source B as itself a specimen of what was published — the official photographer, the army censor, the illustrated weekly of March 1918 and the caption promising all possible speed — and Source A, written to one reader and not for print, as the measure of what such publication left out, the third tent and the twelve and fourteen hours.","sol":"Source B is the more useful here because it is not evidence about what the public was shown: it is a piece of it. It was taken by an official photographer attached to the British Army, passed by the army censor and printed in a British illustrated weekly in March 1918 under the caption ‘Bringing them in’, which told readers the wounded were being brought in with all possible speed to the care of the doctors. Its purpose is therefore what makes it useful, not a weakness, and what it offers readers is order and care: an officer wearing a Red Cross brassard beside a stack of empty stretchers and a dixie of tea, and a motor ambulance drawn up at a farm flying a Red Cross flag. My own knowledge sharpens that, since private cameras were banned in the theatres of war by a War Office instruction of March 1915 and Britain used only about sixteen official photographers across the whole war, so little else could reach the illustrated press. Source A is useful in the opposite way. It was written to one reader, the professor who had taught him, not for print, and it holds what no caption carried: the third tent where men who will not see the morning are made comfortable, and men reaching him twelve and fourteen hours after they were hit. For this enquiry B is the picture the public was given and A is the measure of what was kept out of it.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The enquiry is about what reached people at home, so ask first of each source whether anyone in Britain ever saw it.","A source written for a single reader and a source published for a mass readership are useful in quite different ways here; set a criterion for each before you judge either.","Take the photograph's nature, origin and purpose first, then use the letter to name one thing the published picture leaves out, and keep the last minute for the comparative sentence."],"codes":["11-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Recognition of the strengths and weaknesses of different types of source","11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: Medical treatment on the Western Front","11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: The context of the British sector and the theatre of war"]},{"q":"Study Sources A and B in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the problems that the terrain and the weather caused for moving the wounded on the Ypres front in 1917? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks, levelled. Level 3 (6–8) needs judgements on usefulness for this enquiry with developed reasoning on provenance and contextual knowledge used in interpreting the sources. Credit the six and eight bearers, the duckboard tracks, the shelled road and the mud dried into the wounds in A; the liquid mud, water-filled shell holes, the wagon down to its axle and the duckboard track in B; and the Third Battle of Ypres as context.","sol":"For this enquiry Source B is unusually strong, because the problem is physical and the photograph puts the physical facts in the frame. It shows a duckboard track as the only firm footing across ground churned to liquid mud and pitted with water-filled shell holes, a horse-drawn wagon that has gone off the boards and sunk to its axle with three men at the wheel, and six men carrying a single stretcher. Its nature helps here: whatever the caption says, the mud and the sunken wagon could not be arranged for the camera. Its origin and purpose still matter, because an official photographer under censorship, publishing for readers at home under a caption about all possible speed, chose a track that was passable, and the worst ground was the ground nobody could photograph. Source A supplies the consequence that no picture can show. The surgeon says that since the rain came no stretcher is carried by four men, that it takes six and sometimes eight, and that men have reached him twelve and fourteen hours after they were hit with the mud of the shell holes dried hard into the wound. As a private letter that did not pass through the unit censor who read the men's letters — an officer signed for his own mail, subject only to spot-checks by the base censors — written by the man who received those wounds, that is reliable, and my own knowledge confirms it, since the shelling of Third Ypres had destroyed the drainage of low-lying reclaimed farmland. So B proves the conditions and A proves what the conditions cost, and neither alone would carry the enquiry.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The enquiry names two things, the ground and the weather; check what each source can and cannot show about each.","Ask what in a photograph could not have been staged, and let that decide which of its details you trust.","End by saying which source establishes the conditions and which establishes their cost, rather than crowning a winner."],"codes":["11-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Recognition of the strengths and weaknesses of different types of source","11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: The context of the British sector and the theatre of war","11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: Medical treatment on the Western Front"]},{"q":"Study Sources A and B in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the treatment of wounds and infection at the front in 1917? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks, levelled. Level 3 (6–8) needs judgements on usefulness for this enquiry with developed reasoning on provenance and contextual knowledge used in interpreting the sources. Credit the excision, tubing and salt solution, the manured ground and gas gangrene, the Thomas splint and the bottled citrated blood in A; the splinted leg on the stretcher in B; and the surgeon's professional purpose in writing to a teacher.","sol":"Source A is the more useful of the two by a distance, because the enquiry is about treatment and the writer is the man doing it. He gives the method in sequence: he cuts until the tissue bleeds clean, lays in rubber tubing and runs the salt solution through every two hours, and notes that it must be mixed fresh each day or it is worthless. He explains why such measures were needed, saying the ground has been manured for three hundred years and breeds gas gangrene, and he adds the two other developments the front is known for, the long Thomas splint and a pint of bottled blood kept on ice with citrate. His nature and origin make him a good witness to method, since he is a trained surgeon describing his own hands. His purpose is where care is needed: the letter goes to the professor who taught him and ends by claiming he is doing in a tent what he was told could not be done outside a teaching hospital, so his account of his results is an advertisement and carries not one figure. My own knowledge sets him in place, since asepsis was impossible in a marquee and surgeons therefore returned to excision and antiseptic solutions. Source B adds almost nothing on treatment beyond the splinted leg on the stretcher, which does at least confirm that splinting began before the clearing station. For this enquiry A is the substance and B is a single corroborating detail.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Both sources are rarely equally useful; decide early which one this particular enquiry is built for and say so.","Look for the place where the writer's purpose would most tempt him to overstate, and aim your provenance comment exactly there.","A source that is thin is not useless: find the one detail in the weaker source that corroborates the stronger and use it."],"codes":["11-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Recognition of the strengths and weaknesses of different types of source","11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: The significance of the Western Front for experiments in surgery and medicine","11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: The historical context of medicine in the early twentieth century"]},{"q":"Study Sources A and B in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the work of the RAMC and the nursing and ambulance services on the Western Front? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks, levelled. Level 3 (6–8) needs judgements on usefulness for this enquiry with developed reasoning on provenance and contextual knowledge used in interpreting the sources. Credit the orderlies, triage and surgical routine in A; the bearers, the officer with the Red Cross brassard, the orderly labelling the men with bandaged eyes (which the writer reads as gas cases) and the motor ambulance in B; and the fact that neither source shows a nurse or a woman driver.","sol":"Both sources are useful for the work of the men of the RAMC and both are nearly silent on the women, which is the judgement this enquiry needs. Source A shows the corps as a machine: three clearing stations taking convoys in turn, orderlies sorting the arrivals into walking cases, men for the table and the dying, and orderlies again mixing the salt solution fresh each day. As a private letter that did not go through the unit censor who read the men's letters, an officer signing for his own mail and base censors only spot-checking it, it is strong evidence of what the corps actually did hour by hour, though its purpose — impressing the professor who taught him — makes its account of how well it was done the least trustworthy part. Source B shows the other end of the same corps: bearers on the duckboards, an officer in a Red Cross brassard managing stretchers and tea, an orderly kneeling to label one of three men with bandaged eyes outside a marquee, and a motor ambulance drawn up at a ruined farm flying a Red Cross flag. Because it is an official photograph passed by the censor and published to reassure families, it is useful precisely as the image the army wished to present of its own medical services. Neither source shows a nurse, a Voluntary Aid Detachment volunteer or a First Aid Nursing Yeomanry driver, though by 1917 there were thousands of women at the base hospitals and driving ambulances. So the pair is useful for the RAMC and positively misleading about who else was doing this work.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Read the enquiry's wording closely and check whether the sources actually cover everyone it names.","What a source leaves out can be judged for usefulness just as firmly as what it includes, provided you know what should have been there.","Use one piece of your own knowledge about the scale of the women's services to prove the gap is a gap and not an accident of the moment."],"codes":["11-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Recognition of the strengths and weaknesses of different types of source","11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: Medical treatment on the Western Front","11-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Knowledge of local sources"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date, and against which sector, did the German army first release chlorine gas in a large attack on the Western Front?","a":"22 April 1915, against the Ypres salient, at the start of the Second Battle of Ypres."},{"q":"Which stage of the chain of evacuation was the first that could perform surgery and carry out triage?","a":"The casualty clearing station."},{"q":"What was added to citrated blood in 1916 so that it could be stored for longer?","a":"Glucose."},{"q":"What carried trench fever from man to man?","a":"Body lice."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The Ypres salient","back":"A bulge of British-held ground around Ypres in Flanders, overlooked by German-held ridges on three sides. Fought over in 1914, 1915 and again in the Third Battle of Ypres, July to November 1917.","code":"11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: The context of the British sector and the theatre of war","open":false},{"front":"The first day of the Battle of the Somme","back":"1 July 1916. The British Army suffered about 57,000 casualties, nearly 20,000 of them killed, the heaviest single day's loss in its history. The battle continued until November 1916.","code":"11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: The context of the British sector and the theatre of war","open":false},{"front":"The Battle of Arras and its tunnels","back":"The attack opened on 9 April 1917. Tunnellers, mainly New Zealanders, had linked the medieval quarries beneath Arras into a network sheltering thousands of troops and reaching close to the front line.","code":"11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: The context of the British sector and the theatre of war","open":false},{"front":"The underground hospital at Arras","back":"A hospital built into the tunnels under Arras with about 700 beds, electric light, piped water and an operating theatre. It was used only briefly and was abandoned during the battle in 1917.","code":"11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: Medical treatment on the Western Front","open":false},{"front":"The Battle of Cambrai and the blood depot","back":"Fought 20 November to 7 December 1917. Before it, bottled blood treated with sodium citrate and glucose and packed in ice was stockpiled at a casualty clearing station, keeping for about four weeks.","code":"11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: The significance of the Western Front for experiments in surgery and medicine","open":false},{"front":"Trench foot","back":"Swelling, numbness and blistering caused by standing in cold water and mud. Untreated it turned gangrenous and the foot was amputated. Prevented by duckboards, dry socks and whale oil.","code":"11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: Conditions requiring medical treatment","open":false},{"front":"Trench fever","back":"A lice-borne illness causing high fever, headache and aching limbs that returned at intervals of about five days. Delousing stations were set up behind the lines once body lice were identified as the carrier.","code":"11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: Conditions requiring medical treatment","open":false},{"front":"Shell shock","back":"Uncontrollable shaking, nightmares, loss of speech, blindness or paralysis with no wound. Some 80,000 British soldiers were recorded with it during the war, and some were treated as cowards rather than as patients.","code":"11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: Conditions requiring medical treatment","open":false},{"front":"The three gases of the Western Front","back":"Chlorine, released on the Ypres salient on 22 April 1915, suffocated. Phosgene, from the end of 1915, acted faster. Mustard gas, from July 1917, blistered skin, burned airways and blinded.","code":"11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: Conditions requiring medical treatment","open":false},{"front":"The chain of evacuation","back":"Stretcher bearers, regimental aid post, field ambulance and dressing stations, casualty clearing station, base hospital near the coast, then Britain. The clearing station was the first stage able to operate.","code":"11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: Medical treatment on the Western Front","open":false},{"front":"The Royal Army Medical Corps","back":"The army's medical branch, which ran the aid posts, dressing stations, casualty clearing stations and base hospitals. It grew from about 9,000 officers and men in 1914 to well over 100,000 by 1918.","code":"11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: Medical treatment on the Western Front","open":false},{"front":"The First Aid Nursing Yeomanry (FANY)","back":"A women's volunteer corps founded in 1907 whose members reached the continent in 1914. From 1916 they drove ambulances for the British Army, and they also ran soup kitchens and mobile baths.","code":"11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: Medical treatment on the Western Front","open":false},{"front":"The Thomas splint","back":"Designed by Hugh Owen Thomas of Liverpool, brought to the army by his nephew Robert Jones from 1915. It held a broken leg under traction; survival from a fractured thigh rose from about a fifth to four fifths.","code":"11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: The significance of the Western Front for experiments in surgery and medicine","open":false},{"front":"Mobile x-ray units","back":"Lorries carrying an x-ray machine and its own generator between casualty clearing stations, locating bullets and shell fragments before surgery. The tube overheated after about an hour, and glass and cloth did not show.","code":"11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: The significance of the Western Front for experiments in surgery and medicine","open":false},{"front":"Blood transfusion before and during the war","back":"Karl Landsteiner identified blood groups in 1901, but blood had to pass directly from donor to patient until sodium citrate was used at the right dose from 1915 to stop clotting, with glucose added in 1916.","code":"11-env — 1 The British sector of the Western Front: The historical context of medicine in the early twentieth century","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The sources and interpretations written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Source A: From a letter written by a surgeon serving with the Royal Army Medical Corps at a casualty clearing station on a railway siding west of Poperinghe, in the Ypres sector, to the professor of surgery who had taught him in London. Dated 14 October 1917. Written for this room in the style of such a letter; it is illustrative, not a real document, and it reproduces no real one. — My dear Professor, you ask what the work is like here, so I shall set it down plainly. There are three clearing stations together on this siding and we take the convoys in turn, so that on our night we may pass four hundred men through the reception marquee between dusk and dawn. The orderlies sort them as they come off the ambulances into three lots: the walking cases, who are dressed and put on the train for the base; the men who must go on the table before morning; and a third tent I would rather not describe, where we make comfortable those who will not see the morning at all. My complaint is not the numbers, it is the hours. Since the rain came no stretcher on this front is carried by four men — it takes six, and in the worst of it eight, and the bearers must keep to the duckboard tracks or go in up to the waist. I have had men reach me twelve and fourteen hours after they were hit, having lain first at the regimental aid post and then at the advanced dressing station while the road up was shelled, with the mud of the shell holes dried hard into the wound. Every one of those wounds is filthy. This ground has been manured by Flemish farmers for three hundred years and it breeds gas gangrene as a hothouse breeds vines, so we cut, and go on cutting, until the tissue bleeds clean, then lay in the rubber tubing and run the salt solution through it every two hours; the orderlies must mix it fresh each day or it is so much water. For the broken thighs we now have the long splint of Thomas of Liverpool, which holds the leg straight from the hip while the man is still on the stretcher, and I will tell you that before we had them I buried most of my femur cases and now I bury few. Our x-ray tube is working again, though it will not run above the hour before it must be rested, so we take the plates in turn and a man with a fragment in him may wait. On Tuesday I gave a sergeant a pint of blood out of a bottle, drawn from a convalescent two days before and kept on ice with citrate in it, and he was sitting up when the train took him down. We are doing in a tent what you told us in 1908 could not be done outside a teaching hospital. Yours, etc.","Source B: A photograph taken by an official photographer attached to the British Army on the Ypres front in the autumn of 1917 and published in a British illustrated weekly in March 1918 under the caption 'Bringing them in'. Photographs taken by official photographers were passed by the army censor before publication. Written for this room as a description of such a photograph; the image is illustrative, not a real photograph, and it reproduces no real one. — The photograph looks along a duckboard track laid across ground churned to liquid mud and pitted on both sides with water-filled shell holes. In the middle distance six men carry a single stretcher along the track; the man on it is wrapped to the chin in a blanket and his right leg is held out straight in a long splint strapped from hip to ankle. Two of the six are looking down at their feet rather than ahead. Behind them a horse-drawn wagon has gone off the boards and is down to its axle, with three men at the wheel and a driver at the horses' heads. In the left foreground an officer wearing a Red Cross brassard stands with a clipboard beside a stack of empty stretchers and a dixie of tea. On the right, short of the ruined farm, three men with bandaged eyes sit on a stack of duckboards outside a marquee while an orderly kneels beside the nearest of them to tie a label to his coat; a fourth, a dressing over one eye and a blanket round his shoulders, stands holding a mug in both hands. In the far background the roofless walls of a farm carry a Red Cross flag, and a motor ambulance with its rear doors open is drawn up beside it. The track disappears into smoke at the horizon. The caption printed beneath tells readers that the wounded are being brought in with all possible speed to the care of the doctors.","How the room uses them: Sources A and B are the pair for every 8-mark usefulness question in this room, and Source A is the source for every follow-up question. Every quotation and every claim about a source in the worked examples, the model paragraphs and the model answers comes from the two texts above and from nowhere else; check each one against them as an examiner would. Neither source is a real document, and neither should ever be cited in an examination as though it were.","Teacher's note on the two sources: the bottled citrated blood in Source A runs a few weeks ahead of the landmark the specification names, Oswald Robertson's depot of citrated blood kept on ice for the Battle of Cambrai in November 1917, so the letter shows an experiment being tried at a clearing station in the Ypres sector in October 1917 and not a routine supply; and the gas cases in Source B fit the autumn of 1917 because mustard gas was first used in July of that year."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Paper 1 Question 2(a), 8 marks)","items":["The question: 'How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the sorting of the wounded and the order in which they were treated at the front in 1917?' This is the paragraph on Source A only; a full answer would give Source B the same treatment and close with one comparative sentence.","Source A is useful for this enquiry above all because it shows the sorting being done rather than describing the rule. The surgeon says that the orderlies sort the men as they come off the ambulances into three lots — the walking cases, who are dressed and put on the train for the base; the men who must go on the table before morning; and a third tent, which he will not describe, where those who will not see the morning are made comfortable. That is exactly what an enquiry into sorting needs: the three categories in the words of the man receiving them, and the fact that the first cut was made by orderlies as the men came off the ambulances, not by a surgeon at a table. Its nature and origin make that account trustworthy. A private letter to the professor who taught him did not pass through the unit censor who read the men's letters — an officer signed for his own mail, and base censors only spot-checked it — and it had no public to reassure, which is why it can name a third lot that no published account of the medical services would have printed. Its purpose, however, should make me cautious about a different part of it. He is plainly showing an old teacher what can be done in a tent, so his picture of the sorting as quick and settled — the orderlies simply sorting them as they come — is the part likeliest to be polished, and the letter never says what the orderlies were judging by or how often they were wrong. My own knowledge decides how far to trust the categories themselves. Triage at a casualty clearing station did divide arrivals into these three groups, the walking wounded dressed and sent on, those needing surgery at once, and those too badly wounded to be saved, so his three lots are the system and not one unit's habit. Source A is therefore a strong guide to what the categories were and who applied them, and a weaker guide to how the decision was actually made in each case.","Why this is Level 3 (6–8 marks): it gives a judgement on utility for the specified enquiry and applies a stated criterion — whether the source can show the categories and who applied them — with developed reasoning that takes into account how the provenance affects the usefulness of the content, aiming nature and origin at one claim and purpose at a different claim rather than at the source as a whole. The source is analysed rather than paraphrased, and the contextual knowledge about triage is used in the process of interpreting the source, not added as decoration."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["The same question: 'How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the sorting of the wounded and the order in which they were treated at the front in 1917?' Again, Source A only.","Source A is quite useful for this enquiry because it tells us a lot about how the wounded were sorted out. It says the orderlies sorted the men into three lots as they came off the ambulances: the walking cases who were dressed and put on the train, the men who had to be operated on before morning, and a third tent for the ones who were going to die. This is useful because it is written by a surgeon who was actually there in October 1917, so he would know what he was talking about, and it is a private letter so he would have no reason to lie to his old professor. I know from my own knowledge that this is called triage and that it was carried out at the casualty clearing station, which was the first place in the chain with operating theatres, x-rays and trained nurses, several miles back on a railway line. This all makes Source A a useful source for finding out about how the wounded were sorted.","Why this is only Level 2 (3–5 marks): it does give a judgement on utility using a valid criterion and supports it with developed comment on the content, which is what Level 2 requires. But it misses the Level 3 descriptor in two ways. The provenance comment is generic — 'he was there', 'he had no reason to lie' — and is never turned onto a particular claim, so it does not show how the provenance affects the usefulness of the source content; the purpose of the letter, which is the one thing that should qualify his tidy picture of the sorting, is not used at all. And the contextual knowledge is recited alongside the source rather than used in the process of interpreting it: triage is named and the clearing station described, but nothing in the source is tested against it."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Ypres salient, 1914–1917","items":["Ypres is a town in Flanders, in Belgium; the British held the ground around it from the autumn of 1914 until the end of the war.","The First Battle of Ypres, October to November 1914, held the line but at heavy cost, and left the British holding a salient — a bulge of ground pushed into the German line.","Because a salient is surrounded on three sides, artillery could shell it from the front and from both flanks at once, including the roads and tracks the wounded were carried along.","Most of the higher ground, the low ridges around the salient, was in German hands, which gave their gunners observation over the British rear areas.","The Second Battle of Ypres, April to May 1915, opened with the release of chlorine gas from cylinders on 22 April 1915, the first large-scale gas attack on the Western Front.","The Third Battle of Ypres, remembered from the village of Passchendaele, ran from 31 July to 10 November 1917.","Flanders here is low-lying reclaimed farmland kept workable by field drains; prolonged shelling destroyed the drainage, and heavy rain in August and October 1917 turned the ground to liquid mud.","In those conditions a stretcher that four men could carry elsewhere needed six or eight, and bearers had to keep to duckboard tracks.","The casualty clearing stations serving the salient stood on railway sidings west of the town, around Poperinghe, beyond the worst of the shelling.","The point to make in an answer: the shape of the salient and the state of its ground lengthened every stage of evacuation, so wounds arrived older, colder and dirtier here than on any other British front."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Somme, 1916","items":["The British took over the Somme sector from the French during 1915 and 1916, so the offensive there was fought largely by the volunteer armies raised since 1914.","The battle opened on 1 July 1916, after a bombardment lasting several days, and went on until November 1916.","On the first day the British Army suffered about 57,000 casualties, of whom nearly 20,000 were killed: the heaviest single day's loss in its history.","The number of wounded on 1 July 1916 was far beyond anything the medical services had prepared for, and men lay out in no man's land and backed up at the aid posts for days.","The Somme is chalk country, which drains far better than Flanders, so mud was less of an obstacle there than at Ypres; it was the sheer weight of numbers that broke the chain.","Tanks were used in battle for the first time on 15 September 1916, at Flers-Courcelette.","The scale of the casualties turned individual techniques into routines: wound excision, the Thomas splint and x-ray location of fragments were all standardised on Somme cases.","Facial wounds from the Somme filled the new unit for facial injuries that Harold Gillies had opened at the Cambridge Military Hospital, Aldershot, in 1916; the Queen's Hospital at Sidcup followed in 1917.","Gillies developed the tubed pedicle graft, in which a flap of skin is raised and rolled into a tube so that it keeps its own blood supply while it is moved to the face.","The point to make in an answer: an army taking tens of thousands of wounded in a day needed systems rather than individual brilliance, and that pressure is the real cause of most wartime medical change."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Arras, 1917, and the tunnels under the town","items":["The Battle of Arras opened on 9 April 1917 and the fighting ran on into May 1917.","Arras stands over a network of medieval quarries, cut out beneath the town over centuries to supply building stone.","From 1916 army tunnelling companies, above all the New Zealand Tunnelling Company, linked those quarries into one continuous system and drove new tunnels forward towards the German lines.","The system could shelter troops in the order of twenty thousand, and was fitted with electric light, piped water and a light railway.","Men could be assembled underground out of sight and out of shellfire, and emerge close to the German front line when the attack began.","Because the assembly area was underground and close to the front, the distance a wounded man then had to be carried back was shorter than in an ordinary attack.","A hospital was built into the tunnel system near the front line for the offensive.","The chalk of Artois cuts easily and stands without much timbering, which is why this scheme was possible at Arras and would have been impossible in the waterlogged clay of Flanders.","The point to make in an answer: Arras is the clearest case of the terrain being used for medicine rather than only obstructing it."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the underground hospital at Arras","items":["It was a dressing station and hospital cut into the tunnel system beneath Arras, close behind the front line.","It had about 700 beds.","It was fitted with electric light, piped water and an operating theatre.","Its whole point was position: wounded men could be brought underground and treated within minutes instead of being carried back across shelled ground.","It was abandoned during the Battle of Arras in 1917, so it was in use only very briefly.","It shows how far medical planning had come by 1917: a hospital was designed into the plan of an offensive before the offensive began, rather than improvised after it.","It also shows the limit of such planning, because no amount of engineering could keep a fixed installation safe that close to the line once the battle moved.","The point to make in an answer: it is the best single example of the specification's claim that the terrain mattered for medical treatment, and it cuts both ways."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Cambrai, 1917, and the blood depot","items":["The Battle of Cambrai ran from 20 November to 7 December 1917.","It is remembered for the first mass use of tanks, several hundred being sent forward together on the opening day.","Before the battle, blood was collected in advance and stored, instead of being taken from a donor at the moment a patient needed it.","The blood was treated with sodium citrate, which stops it clotting, and with glucose, which preserves it.","It was kept in bottles packed in ice, and blood stored in this way remained usable for about four weeks.","The store was held at a casualty clearing station, so that transfusion could be given close to the fighting rather than at a base hospital.","This is generally described as the first blood bank or blood depot.","It rested on two earlier steps: Karl Landsteiner's identification of blood groups in 1901, and the use of sodium citrate at the right dose from 1915.","Geoffrey Keynes, a British army surgeon, designed a portable transfusion apparatus that controlled the flow of blood and could be used near the front.","The point to make in an answer: the significance is not that transfusion was invented in the war but that it became a supply problem an army could solve in advance of a battle."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the RAMC, the nurses and the ambulance drivers","items":["The Royal Army Medical Corps (RAMC) was the army's own medical branch and staffed every stage from the regimental aid post to the base hospital.","It grew from about 9,000 officers and men in 1914 to well over 100,000 by 1918.","Each infantry battalion had its own medical officer and sixteen stretcher bearers, who in peacetime were often the battalion's bandsmen.","A field ambulance was not a vehicle but a mobile RAMC unit of some 230 officers and men, which ran the advanced and main dressing stations and could hold around 150 wounded, and only briefly.","Queen Alexandra's Imperial Military Nursing Service (QAIMNS), founded in 1902, grew from fewer than 300 nurses in 1914 to more than 10,000 by the end of the war.","Voluntary Aid Detachment (VAD) volunteers, many with only first-aid training, were allowed to serve overseas from 1915 if they were over 23.","The First Aid Nursing Yeomanry (FANY), founded in 1907, reached the continent in 1914 and from 1916 drove ambulances for the British Army, as well as running soup kitchens and mobile baths.","Trained nurses worked at the base hospitals and increasingly at the casualty clearing stations, where they ran wards and assisted at operations.","Women were not employed at the regimental aid post or in the front-line trenches, so the forward end of the chain was staffed entirely by men.","The point to make in an answer: a small professional corps absorbed tens of thousands of volunteers in four years, and the striking feature is the speed and scale of that improvisation."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the chain of evacuation, stage by stage","items":["Stretcher bearers collected the wounded from the trenches and from no man's land, four men to a stretcher and six or eight in deep mud; the work was often only possible after dark.","The regimental aid post stood within about 200 metres of the front line, in a dugout, a cellar or a ruined building, and was run by the battalion medical officer; it could dress wounds and give morphine, and no more.","The advanced dressing station stood a few hundred metres further back and was run by the field ambulance; the main dressing station stood further back again.","The casualty clearing station stood several miles back and deliberately beside a railway line, in huts and marquees, and was the first stage with operating theatres, x-ray equipment and trained nursing staff.","Clearing stations commonly worked in groups of three on one siding, taking incoming convoys in turn so that one could operate while another received.","Triage sorted the arrivals into three groups: the walking wounded, who were dressed and sent on; those who needed surgery at once; and those too badly wounded to be saved, who were made comfortable.","Base hospitals stood at the coastal bases such as Étaples, Boulogne and Le Havre, and inland at Rouen, a port on the Seine well upriver from the sea; all of them were reached by ambulance train, and some also by canal barge.","Canal barges were slower than trains but far smoother, which mattered for abdominal, spinal and head injuries.","A wound serious enough to need long treatment at home earned a passage across the Channel, which soldiers called a Blighty wound.","The point to make in an answer: the system existed to treat men as far forward as their wounds allowed and to return as many as possible to duty, not simply to save lives."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Thomas splint and the mobile x-ray units","items":["The splint was designed before the war by Hugh Owen Thomas, a Liverpool surgeon who died in 1891 and is often called the father of British orthopaedics.","His nephew Robert Jones pressed its use on the army, and it spread through the British sector from 1915 into 1916.","It ran from the hip to beyond the foot and held the broken leg straight under traction, so that the bone ends stopped tearing the surrounding flesh and the bleeding slowed.","It could be applied at the aid post, so a man travelled back down the chain already splinted instead of being splinted on arrival.","Survival from a fractured thigh is usually given as rising from about one man in five to about four in five once the splint was in general use.","X-rays were described by Wilhelm Röntgen in 1895 and were in use in British hospitals within a year, so the front did not discover them.","The wartime development was mobility: lorries carrying an x-ray machine and its own generator moved between casualty clearing stations, and clearing stations also had static machines.","In France, Marie Curie organised mobile radiography units for the French army.","The limits matter as much as the benefits: the tube overheated after about an hour and had to be rested, exposures took several minutes with the patient perfectly still, and glass, cloth and some fragments did not show on the plate.","The point to make in an answer: both are examples of the war making an existing idea routine and portable rather than inventing anything new."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: illness, wounds and gas in the trenches","items":["Trench foot came of standing in cold water and mud; the feet swelled, went numb and blistered, and untreated the tissue turned gangrenous and the foot was amputated. It was fought by prevention: duckboards, dry socks and whale oil.","Trench fever was carried by body lice and brought high fever, headache and severe aching that returned at intervals of about five days; delousing stations and baths were set up behind the lines once the cause was traced.","Shell shock covered shaking, nightmares, loss of speech, blindness and paralysis with no wound; some 80,000 British soldiers were recorded with it, and treatment ranged from rest behind the lines to charges of cowardice.","Roughly six in every ten British wounds were caused by shells and shrapnel rather than by bullets, and shell fragments tear rather than pierce, carrying earth and cloth deep into the body.","The soil of Flanders and northern France had been manured for centuries, so wounds were seeded with the bacteria of tetanus and of gas gangrene, which could kill within a day.","Anti-tetanus serum was given to the wounded from late 1914, after which tetanus cases fell sharply.","Head injuries were unusually common because the head was what a man exposed above the parapet; the steel Brodie helmet was introduced in 1915 and was general issue during 1916, after which head wounds fell sharply.","Gas came in three forms: chlorine, released on the Ypres salient on 22 April 1915, which suffocated; phosgene, from the end of 1915, faster and needing less of it; and mustard gas, from July 1917, which blistered skin, burned the airways and blinded.","Protection ran from cotton pads held over the face, through smoke helmets, to the small box respirator issued from 1916.","Gas injured far more men than it killed and most British gas casualties eventually returned to duty, but blinded and blistered men absorbed an enormous amount of nursing.","An estimated 41,000 or so British servicemen underwent amputation during the war, out of more than 700,000 British limb wounds — the figures given in the published medical literature, and close to the 40,000 amputees the limbless veterans' charity Blesma gives — which is why limb-fitting and rehabilitation became a medical field of their own."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: medicine in Britain in 1914 — what the war inherited","items":["Germ theory: Louis Pasteur published it in 1861 and Robert Koch identified the microbes causing particular diseases from the 1870s, so by 1914 infection was understood to be caused by microbes.","Antiseptics: Joseph Lister used carbolic acid on wounds and dressings from 1865, sharply reducing deaths after surgery.","Asepsis: by 1900 the aim had shifted to keeping germs out altogether — steam-sterilised instruments, scrubbed theatres, caps, gowns, masks and rubber gloves.","That aseptic standard could not be reproduced in a marquee on manured ground, which is why antiseptic methods returned at the front, above all the Carrel-Dakin sterilised salt solution run into the wound through tubes from 1917.","Wound excision, cutting away dead and dirty tissue until the edges bleed clean, was the other answer, and it is still the basis of treating a contaminated wound.","Anaesthetics: ether and chloroform had been in use since the 1840s, so pain was no longer the obstacle to surgery that it had been.","X-rays: described by Wilhelm Röntgen in 1895 and in use in British hospitals within a year.","Blood groups: Karl Landsteiner identified the main groups in 1901, which made it possible to match donor to patient.","Storage: sodium citrate at the right dose from 1915 stopped clotting and glucose added in 1916 preserved the blood, so donor and patient no longer had to be in the same room at the same moment.","The state was already in health care: the National Insurance Act of 1911 gave insured workers sick pay and access to a doctor.","The point to make in an answer: the war inherited more than it invented, but it turned laboratory possibilities into routine practice at a scale nothing in peacetime could have forced."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the source moves this paper rewards","items":["Provenance means three things and the mark scheme names them: nature (what kind of source it is), origin (who made it, where and when) and purpose (what it was made to do).","Content first: say what the source lets you infer for this enquiry, and name or quote the detail the inference rests on.","Provenance second, aimed at that named inference: say how the nature, origin or purpose makes that particular claim stronger or weaker.","Context third: use a fact of your own to confirm, qualify or contradict the source — contextual knowledge earns nothing at all unless it is linked to evaluating the source.","Never write that a source is biased and stop; say what the bias does to the usefulness of one particular statement in it.","A source with a motive to exaggerate is still useful: it is evidence of the motive, and often of what its maker expected readers to believe.","What a source leaves out is evidence too, but only if you can show from your own knowledge what should have been there.","Judge for the named enquiry only: the same source can be excellent on conditions and useless on numbers.","Finish the utility answer with one comparative sentence that ranks the two sources on a criterion you have already stated.","The follow-up has four parts in a fixed order: a detail taken from Source A, a question that detail raises, a type of source that could answer it, and what that source would let you do.","Good follow-up questions contain a measurable word — how many, how long, how often, what proportion, in which months — because that shows at once which record would settle it.","National records answer questions of scale, policy and comparison; local records answer questions of experience, sequence and the single case."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Plan — Paper 1 Question 2(a): 'How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the difficulties of keeping a casualty clearing station supplied and equipped in the Ypres sector in 1917? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context.' [8 marks]","items":["Decode the question. Three things are demanded and all three are marked: a judgement of usefulness, made for this enquiry and no other; the use of both sources' content and their provenance; and knowledge of the historical context used to interpret them. The enquiry is supply and equipment, so 'useful' means useful for finding out what a forward medical unit had to work with, what it ran short of, and how its stores and apparatus reached it across that ground.","Timing: about 12 or 13 minutes at roughly a minute a mark. Spend the first two reading both sources with the enquiry in mind and underlining one detail in each that bears on it directly; write for nine or ten; leave the last minute for the comparative sentence, which is the sentence most answers never reach.","State your criterion in the first line, before touching either source: for this enquiry a useful source is one that names a particular item of equipment and says what it did or failed to do, and a less useful one is one that shows only the conditions around it.","Paragraph 1 — Source A, content. It is an inventory of a unit's working kit and its limits: an x-ray tube that will not run above the hour before it must be rested, so the plates are taken in turn and a man with a fragment in him may wait; a salt solution the orderlies must mix fresh each day or it is so much water; the long Thomas splint, which arrived and changed what happened to his femur cases; and a pint of blood kept on ice with citrate in it. Name one of those limits: they are the most useful thing either source contains for this enquiry.","Paragraph 1 continued — Source A, provenance aimed at that claim. Nature and origin: a private letter to a former teacher did not pass through the unit censor who read the men's letters, since an officer signed for his own mail and base censors only spot-checked it, and the writer is the man who has to work the equipment, so the shortcomings are set down as working facts and not as a requisition or a complaint to an authority who might refuse it. Purpose, aimed at a different claim: he is showing an old teacher what can be done in a tent, so what he says his equipment achieved — that he now buries few of his femur cases, that the sergeant was sitting up when the train took him down — is the part likeliest to be polished.","Paragraph 1 continued — context used to interpret, not to decorate. The tube he describes behaves as x-ray equipment of the period did: it overheated after about an hour and had to be rested, and exposures took several minutes with the patient perfectly still, while lorries carrying a machine and its own generator moved between clearing stations that also had static sets. That makes his delay the ordinary limit of the apparatus rather than one unit's bad luck, and it is why his account of what his equipment could do is worth more than his account of how well it served him.","Paragraph 2 — Source B, content. The supply problem is in the frame rather than described: the duckboard track is the only firm footing across liquid mud and water-filled shell holes, a horse-drawn wagon has gone off the boards and is down to its axle with three men at the wheel and a driver at the horses' heads, and in the left foreground, beside an officer wearing a Red Cross brassard and carrying a clipboard, there is a stack of empty stretchers and a dixie of tea, with a marquee and a ruined farm serving as the buildings and one motor ambulance drawn up beside the farm.","Paragraph 2 continued — Source B, provenance aimed at the caption rather than the image. An official photographer working under the army censor, published in an illustrated weekly at home under a caption promising all possible speed, was there to reassure families; so the caption is what the source is least useful for, while the sunken wagon, the duckboards and the improvised buildings are what it is most useful for, because those could not be arranged away — and a photograph passed for publication that still shows a wagon axle-deep is stronger evidence of the difficulty than any complaint.","Paragraph 2 continued — the limit that applies to every photograph. It fixes one moment on one track: it cannot say what was in the wagon, how often the boards were cut by shelling, or what any unit at the end of the track actually lacked, so it shows how stores had to travel and cannot inventory them.","The judgement, which is the last two or three sentences and must be written even if time is short. Rank the two on the criterion you set at the start: Source A is the more useful for this enquiry because it alone names particular items and says what each could and could not do, while Source B is the more useful for the route those items had to travel and is itself evidence of the gap between the effort as advertised and the ground as it was.","Qualify the judgement rather than overclaiming it. Both are single moments from one sector in one autumn, and neither can show what a clearing station was supposed to hold or how often it was resupplied; that would need the units' own war diaries and the medical stores returns. Saying so is not a hedge — it is the reasoning the top level asks for.","Two things to avoid: retelling either source without judging it, and writing that the photograph is 'biased' or 'propaganda' without saying which claim in it that weakens. Neither earns anything, because no credit is given for generic comments on provenance which are not used to evaluate source content."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:13:59.439160Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.93,"notes":"Re-judged after the fix pass; kit unedited. All three recorded fixes are real and correct. The new room.questions[10] 2(a) ('what people at home in Britain were shown and told about the care of the wounded') is true to the printed sources word for word — official photographer attached to the British Army, passed by the army censor, British illustrated weekly of March 1918, the caption 'Bringing them in' promising all possible speed, the officer with the Red Cross brassard beside a stack of empty stretchers and a dixie of tea, the motor ambulance at a farm flying a Red Cross flag; and, from Source A, the letter written to the professor who taught him and not for print, the third tent and the twelve and fourteen hours. Its context checks out on the web: the War Office instruction of March 1915 banning the possession and use of cameras in all operational theatres, and Britain's sixteen official war photographers across the whole war. The new room.questions[6] 2(b) on the training and experience of clearing-station surgeons rests on the letter's own closing sentence, that they are doing in a tent what the professor said in 1908 could not be done outside a teaching hospital, and its named record types (War Office memoranda on the treatment of injuries in war from 1915; the syllabuses and war diaries of the armies' schools of instruction for medical officers in France) could genuinely answer the question asked. room.questions[13].sol now reads 'one of three men with bandaged eyes' and 'a ruined farm flying a Red Cross flag', exactly Source B's wording, with no gas and no dressing station asserted; lesson.examples[1].setup now says the letter names the orderlies as the sorters but never their criterion, which the printed letter bears out. No bank enquiry now reproduces the specimen ('the system for dealing with injured soldiers on the Western Front'), June 2018 ('the treatment of battle injuries by medical staff on the Western Front') or June 2023 ('new techniques being used on the Western Front to deal with injuries') enquiry; I read all fourteen setups as well as running the scan, and dupscan prints 'no duplication'. I re-solved every question and found no wrong answer key. Paper truth remains current: 1(a)/1(b) at 2 + 2 on separate aspects, 2(a) at 8 marks with Level 3 at 6–8 and the printed rules on contextual knowledge and on generic comments on provenance¹, 2(b) in its four boxes, Section A 16 marks, no stimulus points, no SPaG, an 80-minute paper."}}},"12-1":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"12-1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This is the first room of the Warfare thematic study, and it supplies the 'before' half of nearly every question you will be asked. Paper 1 Section B carries 36 of the paper's 52 marks, and the medieval period is where Question 3 comparisons usually start, where Question 4 change questions begin, and where a Question 5 or 6 judgement has to set its baseline. If you cannot say precisely who fought in an English army in 1298, how he was recruited, what he was armed with and what the war cost the village he came from, you cannot show that anything in the sixteenth, nineteenth or twentieth century was a change. The room also introduces Edexcel's three key factors — governments and individuals; science, technology and communications; attitudes in society — in a period where you can watch all three at work on a single battlefield.","idea":[{"code":"12 — The process of change: warfare and the key features of society","h":"A medieval army was medieval society in arms","t":"England in c1250–c1500 was a rural, hierarchical kingdom held together by landholding. The king granted land to his tenants-in-chief; they owed him knight service in return; below them peasants owed labour and dues to a lord. There was no standing army, no barracks, no officer training and no ministry of war, because nobody expected a king to keep soldiers in peacetime. So when a king wanted an army he reached for the machinery he already had: landholding to produce knights, the shire and the sheriff to produce foot soldiers, and the household of a great lord to produce a retinue. That is why the army looked like the social pyramid on the march — the king or a royal duke at the top, earls and barons commanding the divisions, knights and men-at-arms in the middle, and archers and spearmen drawn from the villages at the bottom. It is also why campaigns were short: harvests still had to be brought in, and men served for a season, not a career. A top-level answer never treats a military fact as free-standing; it explains the army out of the society, so the examiner sees analysis of the period rather than a description of soldiers."},{"code":"12 — The process of change: the key factors","h":"The three key factors, and what each one does in this period","t":"Edexcel names three factors for the whole theme, and you should use the same three words in every period. Governments and individuals: Edward I's Statute of Winchester in 1285, Edward III's contracts and archery laws, Henry V's discipline on the march to Agincourt — the crown organises, pays, orders and commands. Science, technology and communications: the longbow, the steady growth of plate armour, and the first crude gunpowder weapons. Attitudes in society: chivalry, which valued the mounted knight and made ransom more attractive than killing; deference, which gave command to men of rank; and the low regard in which the common foot soldier was held, which had to be overcome before commanders would build a battle plan around archers. In this period the factors do not point the same way: technology and government pushed change forward while attitudes held it back, which is exactly why the knight declined slowly rather than suddenly. A top-level answer uses the factor names as the spine of its paragraphs, so that the same vocabulary can be carried into the next three rooms and a judgement can compare like with like across the theme."},{"code":"12 — The process of change: factors working together","h":"Factors combine: no weapon changes warfare on its own","t":"The longbow is the clearest case in the whole theme of factors working together. As a piece of technology it was old — Edward I met it in Wales in the campaigns of 1277 and 1282–83 — and on its own it changed nothing, because it needed years of practice and a man who could shoot was no use unless somebody found him, paid him and protected him while he shot. Government supplied that: Commissions of Array selected men in the shires, indentures let captains contract to bring archers by the hundred, Parliament's taxes and the customs on wool paid the wages, and Edward III ordered in 1363 that men practise archery on Sundays instead of playing other games. Attitudes had to shift too, because a commander had to be willing to let commoners decide a battle in which earls were present. Only when all three lined up did massed archery become the English way of fighting. A top-level answer shows the combination in the last sentence of a paragraph and again in the conclusion; that is what the Level 4 descriptor means by a line of reasoning that is coherent, sustained and logically structured."},{"code":"12 — The process of change: the role of the strand 3 case studies","h":"What the two battles are for","t":"Strand 3 of each period is a pair of case studies, and their job is to make strands 1 and 2 visible in one dated episode. Falkirk (1298) and Agincourt (1415) are 117 years apart and together they cover the whole period: Falkirk shows an English king learning that cavalry alone cannot break disciplined infantry and that archery can; Agincourt shows the finished English system — dismounted men-at-arms, archers on the wings behind sharpened stakes, a chosen position, a commander who knows what his army can do. Each battle also gives you the concrete detail an examiner rewards: a date, a formation, a decision, an outcome. Use them as precisely selected evidence inside an argument, never as a story told for its own sake — name the year, name the move, and say what it proves about the period. A top-level answer will often use the same two battles for opposite purposes, Falkirk for the rise of the archer and Agincourt for the limits of the archer, which is how you show weighing rather than listing."},{"code":"12-1 — 1 The nature of warfare: The composition of the army","h":"Who fought: knights, men-at-arms, archers and the levy","t":"At the start of the period the mounted knight was the decisive arm: an armoured horseman of noble or gentle birth, trained from boyhood as a page and then a squire, practised in tournaments, holding land in return for service. Around him stood men-at-arms who were armoured and mounted but not all of them knights. Infantry came from the shire levy — spearmen and billmen of low status, with almost no training, brought out for a season. Archers began as a supporting arm and ended as the largest part of the army: at Agincourt in 1415 roughly five in six of Henry V's men carried bows, and by then mounted archers rode to the battle and fought on foot. Command followed rank, not ability. The king commanded in person if he could; earls and barons led the divisions and brought their own retinues; a captain's authority came from his standing and from the contract he had signed. A top-level answer ties composition to society in the same sentence — the army changed at the bottom, where archers replaced knights as the decisive element, long before it changed at the top, where command still belonged to the landed elite."},{"code":"12-1 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of new weapons and formations","h":"New weapons and formations: longbow, schiltron and the first guns","t":"Three developments matter. The longbow, about six feet of yew drawn with the whole body, could be shot several times in the time a crossbowman took to wind and loose once, out to a range of over two hundred yards; massed, it made a cavalry charge across open ground very costly. The schiltron, a dense ring or block of Scottish spearmen with long spears, proved that disciplined infantry could stop cavalry outright — it held at Falkirk in 1298 and, used on the attack, won at Bannockburn in 1314. Gunpowder arrived early but changed little at first: the earliest English picture of a gun is in a manuscript made for Edward III in 1326, guns are reported at Crécy in 1346, and they mattered chiefly in sieges such as Calais in 1346–47 and Harfleur in 1415, because they were heavy, slow, inaccurate and dangerous to their own crews. Together these pushed the mounted knight off the centre of the battlefield, though he did not vanish: he dismounted and fought on foot. A top-level answer always says how a weapon changed strategy, tactics or combat, not merely that it existed."},{"code":"12-1 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","h":"Getting men: summons, scutage, array, contract — and almost no training","t":"Recruitment changed more than any other feature of English warfare in this period, and it changed because feudal service did not work. A tenant-in-chief owed the king knights because he held land, but the service was unpaid and usually reckoned at about forty days a year, which is useless for a siege in Gascony. So kings took scutage, 'shield money', instead and spent it on soldiers who would stay. Alongside that ran the obligation of ordinary men: the Assize of Arms of 1181 and then Edward I's Statute of Winchester of 1285 required men to keep arms according to their wealth, and Commissions of Array sent royal commissioners into each county to inspect them and pick the best. From the middle of the fourteenth century the decisive innovation was the indenture: a written contract in which a captain agreed to serve for a stated period with a stated number of men-at-arms and archers at agreed rates of pay, with rules about plunder and ransoms. Training was thin: knights trained all their lives, archers were made by compulsory practice, and the levy learned in the field. A top-level answer uses this sequence — obligation, money, contract — as a ready-made line of reasoning about change."},{"code":"12-1 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians","h":"What war did to people who did not fight","t":"England itself was rarely invaded in this period, so most civilians met the war as a bill rather than as an enemy. Purveyance let royal officers take grain, animals, carts and even ships for the army at a price the crown set, and payment was often late or never. Parliament granted lay subsidies and duties on wool to pay for the Hundred Years War; the poll taxes of 1377, 1379 and 1380 fell on ordinary households and helped provoke the Peasants' Revolt of 1381. Arrays took men out of the fields at exactly the time when labour was scarce, for the Black Death of 1348–49 had killed perhaps a third of the population and the Statute of Labourers of 1351 was trying to hold wages down. Some civilians met war directly: Scottish raids into the north after Bannockburn in 1314 burned towns and extorted money, French and Genoese raiders sacked Southampton in 1338, and soldiers plundered and were billeted on households. English armies did far worse abroad, where the chevauchée deliberately burned crops and villages. A top-level answer distinguishes between the many who paid and the few who were burned out, because that distinction is where the marks for judgement are."},{"code":"12-1 — 3 Case studies: The Battle of Falkirk (1298)","h":"Case study: Falkirk, 22 July 1298","t":"Edward I had occupied Scotland in 1296, but William Wallace and Andrew Murray beat an English army at Stirling Bridge in 1297, after which Wallace was knighted and made Guardian of Scotland. At Falkirk, Wallace drew up four schiltrons of spearmen with archers between them and a small force of Scottish noble cavalry behind. The English cavalry charged first; the Scottish horsemen left the field without fighting, the Scots archers were ridden down and their commander Sir John Stewart was killed, but the schiltrons held, because horses will not charge home on a hedge of spears. Edward then stopped the charges and brought his archers and crossbowmen forward to shoot into the packed formations until gaps opened, and sent the cavalry through the gaps. The Scots were broken and cut down; Wallace escaped and resigned the guardianship later that year. A top-level answer uses Falkirk to make one analytical point — that no single arm could win, and that the commander who combined missiles with shock won — and then carries it forward to Crécy and Agincourt."},{"code":"12-1 — 3 Case studies: The Battle of Agincourt (1415)","h":"Case study: Agincourt, 25 October 1415","t":"Henry V landed in Normandy in August 1415 and took Harfleur, which surrendered on 22 September after his guns had battered its walls, but dysentery wrecked his army and he marched for Calais with what was left. The French blocked the Somme crossings and forced a battle on a narrow strip of newly ploughed farmland between two woods, soaked by a night of rain. Henry's men-at-arms fought on foot with archers on the wings behind sharpened stakes; he advanced the line into bowshot to provoke the attack. The French cavalry charges broke on stakes and arrows, the French men-at-arms struggled forward through deep mud in heavy armour and crowded together as the field narrowed, and the archers left their bows to finish them with mallets and axes. The French lost the Constable, Charles d'Albret, and many great nobles; the Duke of Orléans was captured; English losses were light. A top-level answer never lets Agincourt become a story: it separates Henry's decisions, the ground and the French mistakes, and says which of them the victory could not have done without."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 3, 4 marks: one difference, analysed across two periods","setup":"Paper 1 Question 3 is worth 4 marks, 2 for AO2 and 2 for AO1: 'Explain one way in which the supply of English armies in the years c1250–c1500 was different from the supply of British armies in the years c1900–present.' You must analyse one difference and support it with specific information from both periods; the mark scheme gives Level 2 only when features of the period are analysed and specific information is added.","steps":["Fix on a single difference that holds across both whole periods: a medieval army had no permanent supply service and fed itself from the country it was marching through, while a modern army is fed, fuelled and armed by a standing corps of its own specialists.","Prove the earlier half with named detail: purveyance let royal officers take grain, animals and carts for the army at a price the crown set, and payment was often late, so that the Statute of Purveyors of 1362 had to order ready money and even changed the hated name of purveyor to buyer; on campaign in France armies lived off what they seized on the chevauchée.","Sharpen that half with a case: in 1298 the supply ships Edward I was waiting for failed to reach his army in Scotland, the men went hungry, and he was preparing to fall back towards Edinburgh when news of Wallace's position reached him and he turned to fight at Falkirk instead.","Prove the later half and close the comparison: the Army Service Corps of 1888 became the Royal Army Service Corps in 1918 and the Royal Logistic Corps in 1993, a paid branch of the army whose whole job is to move rations, fuel and ammunition by road, sea and air, as it did for the British force in Iraq in 2003 — so the earlier army took what it needed from the civilians in its path and had to move or starve, while the later one is supplied from home and can stay in the field as long as the state can pay."],"cues":["Which single difference about supply is big enough to carry a four-mark answer?","What named medieval detail shows where the food, animals and carts actually came from?","Which episode proves how quickly a medieval army's supply could fail?","What must the last sentence do that the two halves of evidence have not?"]},{"title":"Question 4, 12 marks: turning a stimulus point into a mechanism","setup":"Paper 1 Question 4 is worth 12 marks, 6 for AO2 and 6 for AO1: 'Explain why English kings came to depend on Parliament to fight their wars in the years c1250–c1500.' The stem offers two stimulus points, the wool customs and the wages of paid soldiers, and adds 'You must also use information of your own.' The printed caps are a maximum of 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; there is no cap at Level 2.","steps":["Turn the first stimulus point into a mechanism instead of a label: the customs granted to Edward I in the Parliament of 1275 took half a mark, 6s 8d, on every sack of wool exported, and because wool was the kingdom's great export the crown could borrow against it — but it was a grant, and after the seizures and the far heavier wool duty of the 1290s that critics called the maltolt, a statute of 1362 laid down that no charge on wool could be levied without the assent of Parliament.","Do the same with the second: paid soldiers had to be paid every day, usually about 3d for a foot archer, 6d for a mounted archer, 12d for a man-at-arms and 2s for a knight, so a campaign of several months with thousands of men cost far more than the king's ordinary revenues from his lands, his courts and his feudal dues could ever meet.","Add the reason of your own that unlocks Level 4: consent had a political price as well as a financial one. The Confirmation of the Charters in 1297 conceded that such aids and taxes would be taken only by the common consent of the realm, and from 1334 a grant of a fifteenth and a tenth was a known sum fixed on each town and village at the amounts of 1332, so every war reopened a bargain in which Parliament could air grievances before it voted the money.","Tie the three together in a closing line: long wars needed cash, cash needed taxes on trade and on movable goods, and by the fourteenth century those taxes needed Parliament's assent, so the more war an English king wanted the more often he had to summon, argue with and satisfy his Parliament."],"cues":["What turns 'the wool customs' from a name into a reason?","Why could a king's ordinary income never pay for a campaign that lasted months?","What did Parliament get in return, and why must that reason come from outside the two bullets?","What must the closing line say about cash, taxes and consent that the three paragraphs have shown only separately?"]},{"title":"Reading a battle for its causes: Falkirk, 22 July 1298","setup":"On 22 July 1298 Edward I met William Wallace, Guardian of Scotland since the Scottish victory at Stirling Bridge in 1297, near Falkirk. Wallace drew up four schiltrons — rings of spearmen with long spears — with archers between them and a small force of Scottish noble cavalry behind. Edward's army was much larger and included many archers. The English cavalry charged first: the Scottish cavalry rode off without fighting and the Scots archers were ridden down, but the schiltrons held. Edward then brought his archers forward. You are asked to explain why the battle ended as it did.","steps":["Separate what Wallace controlled from what he did not: he chose the ground and the formation, but a schiltron can only stand still, and spearmen have no answer to men shooting at them from beyond the reach of a spear.","Follow the flight of the Scottish cavalry through to its consequence: with no horsemen of his own, Wallace could not drive the English archers away, so they were free to shoot at their leisure.","Make Edward's decision the turning point: seeing his charges fail, he stopped throwing armoured horsemen at a hedge of spears and used missile troops to open gaps, then sent the cavalry through them.","Say what the battle proves about the period rather than about the day: disciplined infantry could stop shock cavalry, but only a commander who combined missiles with shock could break disciplined infantry."],"cues":["What can a schiltron do, and what can it never do?","Why does the loss of the Scottish cavalry matter more than the numbers on the field?","What did Edward change between the first attack and the last?","What is the general point this battle earns you in an essay?"]},{"title":"Question 5, 16 marks: setting the criterion before you argue","setup":"Paper 1 Question 5 is worth 16 marks, with up to 4 more for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology: \"'Sieges mattered more than battles in English warfare in the years c1250–c1500.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\" The stimulus points offered are castles and the siege of Calais (1346–47). The Level 4 descriptor requires that criteria for the judgement are justified and applied in reaching it.","steps":["Define 'mattered more' before writing any narrative: one kind of fighting matters more than another if it took up more of the war, if it decided who held the ground afterwards, and if it shaped how armies were paid for and equipped.","Test the claim at its strongest: holding places was what conquest meant, so Edward I spent well over £80,000 on castles in Wales between 1277 and 1304, with Master James of St George as his builder, and made the garrison of Stirling wait in 1304 until his giant trebuchet, the Warwolf, had been tried on the walls before he would accept their surrender; Edward III lay in front of Calais from 4 September 1346 until it was starved out on 3 August 1347, and England then kept the town until 1558; Henry V starved Rouen into surrender on 19 January 1419 and so took Normandy. One modern count of the Hundred Years War lists more than 350 attacks on castles and walled towns.","Test the strongest rival: battles were rare because commanders avoided the risk, but a battle could do in a day what a siege could not — Falkirk in 1298 destroyed Wallace's field army, Crécy in 1346 wrecked the army that might have relieved Calais and so made that siege possible, and Agincourt in 1415 opened the road to the conquest of Normandy and to the Treaty of Troyes in 1420.","Apply the criterion in the conclusion instead of summarising: sieges consumed the years, the money and the men and settled what England actually held, so by the test set at the start they mattered more; battles mattered most at the moments when they removed the army standing between an English king and the place he meant to besiege."],"cues":["What has to be settled before any evidence about sieges or battles is worth writing down?","Which pieces of evidence show how much of a war one siege could swallow?","What did the famous battles actually achieve, and for whom?","How does a conclusion apply that criterion rather than add up the paragraphs?"]},{"title":"Question 5, 16 marks: weighing an individual against his circumstances","setup":"The question is: \"'The role of Henry V was the main reason for the English victory at Agincourt in 1415.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\" It is worth 16 marks with up to 4 more for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology. You know that Henry took Harfleur in September 1415, that disease thinned his army before the march towards Calais, that the battle was fought on 25 October on a narrow strip of newly ploughed farmland between two woods after a night of rain, that the French army was larger but had no king on the field, and that the English archers planted sharpened stakes in front of their positions.","steps":["Turn each of Henry's actions into a decision with a consequence: ordering every archer to carry and plant a stake stopped the French horsemen, and advancing the line into bowshot forced a larger enemy to attack on ground that suited the English.","Give the conditions their due weight: rain and ploughed soil exhausted armoured men on foot, and the woods narrowed the front so that the French crowded together and could never bring their numbers to bear.","Give the enemy their share of the outcome: with the king absent, French command was divided among great nobles competing for the front rank, so nobody halted an attack that had already lost its shape.","Judge by asking who made the choices that turned those conditions into a victory, and say plainly that the ground and the enemy created the opportunity while the king's decisions were what took it."],"cues":["How do you stop a paragraph about Henry becoming a list of things he did?","Which features of the field were outside anybody's control?","What was wrong with the French command that no Englishman arranged?","What question does the conclusion have to answer to reach Level 4?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Why was feudal service of about forty days a year no use to Edward III in France?","a":"It was too short for a campaign overseas and was unpaid, so kings took scutage instead and used the money to hire men who would serve for months at agreed wages."},{"q":"Why could the schiltron stop cavalry at Falkirk but not save Wallace's army?","a":"A hedge of long spears stops horses, but the formation had to stand still, so English archers could shoot into it from beyond spear range and open gaps for the cavalry."},{"q":"Name the two things, besides the longbow itself, that English commanders used at Agincourt to protect their archers.","a":"Sharpened stakes planted in front of the archers, and dismounted men-at-arms holding the line beside them on a narrow field between two woods."},{"q":"For most English people, what was the main experience of war in this period?","a":"Paying for it: purveyance of food, animals and carts, and taxes such as the lay subsidies, the wool customs and the poll taxes of 1377, 1379 and 1380."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 1 Section B is worth 36 of the paper's 52 marks: Question 3 is 4 marks, Question 4 is 12 marks, and Question 5 or Question 6 is 16 marks plus up to 4 for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology.","Question 3, 'Explain one way in which … was similar to / different from …', carries 2 marks for AO2 and 2 for AO1 and normally spans at least a century, so both periods must be named and evidenced.","Question 4 stimulus caps: answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 8 marks and have no access to Level 4; no cap is printed at Level 2.","Question 5 or 6 stimulus caps: answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 11 marks and have no access to Level 4; no cap is printed at Level 2.","Timing on the 80-minute Paper 1 is about a minute a mark, plus a little for SPaG: about 5 minutes on Question 3, 15 on Question 4 and 20 on Question 5 or 6, the only Section B answer where spelling and specialist terms carry separate marks. That is 40 minutes for Section B and leaves the other 40 for Section A."],"questions":[{"q":"Explain one way in which the composition of English armies in the years c1250–c1500 was different from the composition of English armies in the years c1500–c1700. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — a temporary force raised from landed society against a paid, drilled, full-time army — supported by specific detail from both periods, such as the knightly retinue against the New Model Army of 1645.","sol":"One clear difference is what held the army together. In the years c1250–c1500 the core was the mounted knight and his retinue: men served because they held land of a lord or had contracted with a captain for one campaign, and the force was disbanded when the season ended. By the middle of the seventeenth century armies were built on regiments of pikemen and musketeers who were full-time soldiers: Parliament's New Model Army, created in 1645, was a single national force under one commander, drilled, uniformed and paid regularly. The difference is between an army assembled for a campaign out of landed society and a professional body maintained by the state.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask who the men were and what made them turn up, in each of the two periods.","A comparison needs both halves evidenced: one named feature with one named detail from each period, and then a sentence saying what kind of difference it is.","Name the single difference first, prove the earlier side, prove the later side, then explain why the two are different in kind rather than in degree."],"codes":["12-1 — 1 The nature of warfare: The composition of the army","12 — The process of change: warfare and the key features of society"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the recruitment of soldiers in the years c1250–c1500 was different from the recruitment of soldiers in the years c1900–present. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — obligation owed to a lord or community against a legal duty owed to the state — supported by detail such as the feudal summons and indentures against the Military Service Act of 1916.","sol":"The main difference is who the soldier owed his service to. In the years c1250–c1500 recruitment worked through personal and local obligation: a tenant-in-chief owed the king knights because he held land, Commissions of Array picked men from the shire levy under the Statute of Winchester of 1285, and from the fourteenth century captains signed indentures to bring men for wages. In the years c1900–present service has been a legal duty owed to the state: the Military Service Act of January 1916 made single men aged eighteen to forty-one liable for call-up, and conscription returned in 1939. The difference is that obligation moved from the lord and the county to Parliament and the whole nation.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what actually forced a man into the ranks in each period.","Give one named medieval route into the army and one named modern law, then compare the kind of duty each created.","State the difference, evidence the earlier period, evidence the later one, and finish on what changed about the obligation itself."],"codes":["12-1 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","12 — The process of change: warfare and the key features of society"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the training of soldiers in the years c1250–c1500 was different from the training of soldiers in the years c1900–present. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — training left to the individual and enforced by law against training provided by the state to a common standard — supported by detail such as compulsory archery practice at the butts against the training given to conscripts from 1916.","sol":"In the years c1250–c1500 training was the fighting man's own affair. There was no standing army to drill, so archers built their strength at the village butts, kept at it by orders such as Edward III's of 1363 that men shoot on Sundays and holidays instead of playing other games, while knights learned in a lord's household, at the hunt and in tournaments. Since c1900 the state has trained the men it recruits: conscripts called up under the Military Service Act of January 1916 were sent to a depot and taught drill, musketry and entrenching by instructors before they joined a unit. Training moved from a private duty to organised instruction.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask who was responsible for making a man ready to fight in each period.","Evidence the medieval side with how archers and knights learned their skills, and the modern side with what happens to a recruit once the state has called him up.","State the difference, prove both halves with a law or an institution, and finish on what the change shows about who ran the army."],"codes":["12-1 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","12 — The process of change: warfare and the key features of society"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the impact of war on civilians in the years c1250–c1500 was similar to the impact of war on civilians in the years c1500–c1700. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed similarity — armies supplied themselves at the expense of the households around them — supported by detail such as purveyance and plunder against free quarter and requisitioning in the civil wars of the 1640s.","sol":"In both periods civilians suffered mainly because armies lived off the people around them. In the years c1250–c1500 purveyance allowed royal officers to seize grain, animals and carts for the army at a price the crown set, often paying late or not at all, and soldiers plundered the districts they marched through. In the civil wars of the 1640s the same thing happened: soldiers were quartered on households under free quarter, supplies were requisitioned from farms, and counties were assessed for heavy monthly taxes. The similarity is that in both periods the cost of the war was taken directly out of the homes and goods of people who were not fighting.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about how an army on the march was fed, in each period.","Pick one mechanism that recurs, and name the medieval version and the seventeenth-century version of it.","State the similarity, prove each half with a named practice, and close on what it shows about who paid for war."],"codes":["12-1 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians","12 — The process of change: warfare and the key features of society"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the use of missile weapons in the years c1250–c1500 was different from the use of missile weapons in the years c1700–c1900. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — a weapon demanding years of individual skill against firearms a recruit could be trained to use in weeks — supported by detail such as the longbow and compulsory archery practice against the musket and the later service rifles.","sol":"The difference lies in the skill each weapon demanded. The longbow was about six feet of yew drawn with the whole body and took years of practice to use, which is why Edward III ordered in 1363 that men practise archery on Sundays and holidays; an army's shooting power therefore depended on lifelong specialists. In the years c1700–c1900 firearms could be taught to a recruit in weeks: the smoothbore Brown Bess of the eighteenth and early nineteenth centuries was fired in drilled volleys, and even after rifled weapons such as the Enfield of 1853 and the Martini-Henry of 1871 made aimed individual shooting matter again, marksmanship was trained on a range in weeks rather than built over a lifetime.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask how long it took to make a man dangerous with each weapon.","Evidence the medieval side with the bow and the law that kept archers in supply, and the later side with the standard infantry firearm.","State the difference, prove both halves, and finish on where the power of each weapon actually came from."],"codes":["12-1 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of new weapons and formations","12 — The process of change: the key factors"]},{"q":"Explain why the longbow became the most important weapon in English armies in the years c1250–c1500.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• Welsh archers\n• the Battle of Crécy (1346)\nYou must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit the qualities of the weapon and its Welsh origins, its proven success in battle and the tactics built around it, and own points such as its cheapness, array and indentures and compulsory archery practice. Answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 8 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"The longbow became the core of English armies because a good weapon, a proven set of tactics and a government able to pay for archers all came together.\n\nThe first reason is what the weapon could do and where the English met it. Edward I fought against massed archery in his Welsh campaigns of 1277 and 1282–83 and then recruited Welsh archers into his own armies. The bow was about six feet of yew, drawn with the whole body, and a trained man could shoot several arrows in the time a crossbowman took to wind and loose one, out to a range of over two hundred yards. Against cavalry, that rate of shooting mattered far more than the power of any single shot.\n\nThe second reason is that it worked repeatedly, and commanders built their tactics around it. At Falkirk in 1298 archers opened the gaps in Wallace's schiltrons through which the English cavalry charged. At Crécy on 26 August 1346 English archers on a slope outshot the Genoese crossbowmen and broke charge after charge of French horsemen. By Agincourt in 1415 roughly five in six of Henry V's men carried bows, standing behind sharpened stakes beside dismounted men-at-arms. Success turned the bow from one weapon among several into the shape of the English army.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond both stimulus points: archers were cheap and the crown could organise them. An archer cost a fraction of a man-at-arms with his armour and horses, so a king got far more fighting men for his money. Commissions of Array picked archers in every shire, indentures let captains contract to bring them by the hundred, and Edward III's order of 1363 that men practise archery on Sundays kept the supply of trained shooters coming.\n\nThe reasons hold each other up. A weapon needing years of practice could only dominate because government made the practice compulsory and could pay the men who had put in the years.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["The question is about why one weapon came to dominate, so each paragraph must end on dominance, not on description.","Three developed reasons beat five listed ones, and at least one must come from outside the two bullets.","Turn each bullet into a mechanism — what the Welsh bow could do, what Crécy proved — and make sure your third reason is one the bullets do not prompt."],"codes":["12-1 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of new weapons and formations","12 — The process of change: the key factors","12 — The process of change: factors working together"]},{"q":"Explain why the way English armies were raised changed in the years c1250–c1500.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the feudal summons\n• indentures\nYou must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit the limits of feudal service and scutage, the demands of long overseas campaigns met by written contracts, and own points such as parliamentary taxation, the wool customs and Commissions of Array. Answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 8 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"The way English armies were raised changed because the old obligation could not supply the armies the new wars needed, and because the crown found money to buy what it could no longer demand.\n\nThe first reason is the weakness of feudal service itself. A tenant-in-chief owed the king knights because he held land, but the service was unpaid and reckoned at about forty days a year, and service overseas was always disputed. Forty unpaid days cannot take a castle in Gascony or hold a garrison through a winter. Kings therefore took scutage, 'shield money', in place of the service and spent it on soldiers who would stay as long as they were paid.\n\nThe second reason is the kind of war being fought. Edward I's campaigns in Wales in 1277 and 1282–83 and in Scotland from 1296 needed armies that could march, besiege and garrison, and the Hundred Years War from 1337 needed them across the Channel for months at a time. That is why the indenture spread from the middle of the fourteenth century: a written contract in which a captain agreed to serve for a stated period with a stated number of men-at-arms and archers, at agreed wages, with rules about plunder and ransoms. The king bought the exact mixture of troops he wanted.\n\nThe third reason lies beyond both bullets: contracts are only possible if there is cash. Parliament granted lay subsidies and duties on wool that paid soldiers' wages, and the Statute of Winchester of 1285 kept the older obligation alive underneath, so that Commissions of Array could still comb each shire for the best armed men when numbers were short.\n\nTogether these turned the paid retinue into the building block of an English army — and by the Wars of the Roses lords were raising their followings by contract in the same way for their own quarrels.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["The conceptual focus is change, so every paragraph should end by saying what changed and why it had to.","Ask what each way of raising men could not do, because that is what forced the next one.","Turn the summons and the indenture into mechanisms, then make sure your third reason comes from outside the two bullets."],"codes":["12-1 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","12 — The process of change: warfare and the key features of society","12 — The process of change: the key factors"]},{"q":"Explain why the mounted knight became less dominant on the battlefield in the years c1250–c1500.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the longbow\n• the schiltron\nYou must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit massed archery, disciplined spear formations and the habit of dismounting men-at-arms, and own points such as the cost of armour and horses and the crown's preference for paid archers. Answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 8 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"The mounted knight lost his dominance because weapons and formations were found that beat a charge, and because kings could buy far more fighting power for the same money in other ways.\n\nThe first reason is missile power. Massed longbows made the approach across open ground very expensive, above all for the horses: at Crécy on 26 August 1346 repeated French charges were broken by archery, and at Agincourt on 25 October 1415 the French cavalry attacks on the wings failed against arrows and sharpened stakes. A charge that cannot arrive is not a war-winning arm.\n\nThe second reason is disciplined infantry. The schiltron, a dense ring of spearmen with long spears, stopped Edward I's cavalry outright at Falkirk in 1298, and at Bannockburn in 1314 Robert Bruce used the same formation on the attack to defeat Edward II. Once it was clear that steady foot soldiers could halt horsemen, English commanders drew the obvious conclusion and dismounted their own men-at-arms to stiffen the line, as they did at Crécy, at Poitiers in 1356 and at Agincourt.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond the bullets and is financial. A knight's armour, warhorses and equipment cost many times what it cost to arm an archer, and by the fourteenth century the crown was paying wages rather than calling in service, so every pound spent bought more men if it was spent on archers. Kings and captains raised what was cheap and effective.\n\nThese reasons pulled in the same direction, but note what they did not do. The knight did not disappear: he dismounted, fought in the line as a man-at-arms and went on commanding the army, because attitudes to rank changed far more slowly than tactics did.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["The focus is dominance, not survival, so keep asking why the charge stopped deciding battles.","Two of your reasons will come from the battlefield; make sure the third comes from outside it.","End by saying what happened to the knight instead of disappearing, which is what makes the answer analytical."],"codes":["12-1 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of new weapons and formations","12-1 — 1 The nature of warfare: The composition of the army","12 — The process of change: factors working together"]},{"q":"Explain why gunpowder weapons had changed English warfare so little by c1500.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the guns reported at Crécy (1346)\n• the siege of Harfleur (1415)\nYou must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit the crudeness and danger of early guns, their usefulness in sieges rather than in the field, and own points such as cost, the difficulty of powder and transport and the proven cheapness of the longbow. Answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 8 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"Gunpowder arrived in English warfare early but changed it slowly, because the guns of this period could do one job well and everything else badly, and because the weapon they would have had to replace was already cheap and proven.\n\nThe first reason is the state of the technology. The earliest English picture of a gun is in a manuscript made for Edward III in 1326, and guns are reported in action at Crécy on 26 August 1346, but contemporaries were struck far more by their noise than by any damage they did. Early guns were heavy, very slow to load, wildly inaccurate and dangerous to their own crews: James II of Scotland was killed in 1460 when one burst beside him at Roxburgh. A weapon like that could not be trusted to decide a battle.\n\nThe second reason is that guns changed siege warfare, the commonest form of war, before they changed the battlefield, and even there the change was slow. Medieval war was mostly attacks on castles and walled towns, and commanders avoided battle where they could, so sieges were far commoner than battles. A gun useless against cavalry was good against a wall, which is why Edward III used guns at Calais in 1346–47 and Henry V's guns helped force Harfleur to surrender on 22 September 1415. Even so they could not shorten a siege much: Harfleur held out for about five weeks and Calais for nearly a year.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond both bullets: cost and supply. Guns needed founders to cast them, gunners to serve them, carts and teams to move them, and gunpowder whose saltpetre was hard to obtain, while an archer cost a few pence a day and could be raised in any shire. Commanders had no reason to abandon a system that had won at Crécy, Poitiers and Agincourt.\n\nSo by c1500 gunpowder had begun to change how walls were attacked without yet changing how armies fought — the warning of what was coming was Castillon in 1453, where French guns destroyed an English army and ended the Hundred Years War.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["The question is about slowness, so every paragraph must end on a brake rather than on a description of a gun.","Separate what guns were good at from what armies mostly did, and ask which of the two decided battles.","Add a reason of your own from outside the two bullets, then close with the first sign that the brake was coming off."],"codes":["12-1 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of new weapons and formations","12 — The process of change: the key factors","12 — The process of change: factors working together"]},{"q":"Explain why war placed such heavy burdens on English civilians in the years c1250–c1500.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• purveyance\n• taxation\nYou must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit the supply of armies from the countryside, the cost of the Hundred Years War met by subsidies, wool customs and poll taxes, and own points such as array, plunder, raiding and post-plague labour shortage. Answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 8 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"War was heavy on English civilians because a medieval king had no supply system, no permanent army and no income of his own large enough to fight with, so he took what he needed from the people who were not fighting.\n\nThe first reason is supply. Purveyance was the crown's right to take grain, livestock, carts and even ships for the army at a price it set itself, and payment was frequently late or never made. For a village on the road north to Scotland this meant losing the plough-team and the seed corn in the same week, with a receipt instead of money. It was one of the most hated of all royal rights.\n\nThe second reason is money. Wars had to be paid for in cash once armies were raised by contract, so Parliament granted lay subsidies and duties on the export of wool, which fell on the trade the whole countryside depended on. When ordinary taxation was not enough, the government tried poll taxes in 1377, 1379 and 1380 to pay for the war with France, and that helped provoke the Peasants' Revolt of 1381.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond both bullets: some civilians met the war in person. Commissions of Array took men out of the fields at exactly the time when hands were scarce, for the Black Death of 1348–49 had killed perhaps a third of the population and the Statute of Labourers of 1351 was trying to hold wages at their old level. Scottish raids into the north after Bannockburn in 1314 burned towns and extorted protection money, and French and Genoese raiders sacked Southampton in 1338.\n\nThe burdens therefore reinforced each other: the same households lost their goods to purveyors, their men to the array and their earnings to taxation, which is why war and rebellion were so closely linked in this period.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what a king in this period did not have, because that is what he had to take from civilians.","Make purveyance and taxation into mechanisms with named examples, not labels.","Your third reason must come from outside the two bullets; then finish by linking the burdens to one another."],"codes":["12-1 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians","12 — The process of change: warfare and the key features of society","12 — The process of change: the key factors"]},{"q":"'The longbow was the main reason for English victories in battle in the years c1250–c1500.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Battle of Falkirk (1298)\n• the Battle of Agincourt (1415)\nYou must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6, plus up to 4 SPaG. Credit the bow's record at Falkirk, Crécy and Agincourt weighed against the tactical system that protected it, commanders' decisions, enemy error and the organisation that paid for archers, with a criterion-based judgement. Answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 11 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"A cause is the main one if the victories collapse without it and if it explains more of them than any rival does. On that test the longbow was indispensable to English success but was not, by itself, the reason for it.\n\nThe case for the bow is strong. At Falkirk on 22 July 1298 Edward I's cavalry could not break Wallace's schiltrons; only when the king brought his archers forward to shoot into the packed spearmen did gaps appear for the horsemen to charge through. At Crécy on 26 August 1346 English archers outshot the Genoese crossbowmen and broke charge after charge of French cavalry. At Agincourt on 25 October 1415 roughly five in six of Henry V's men carried bows. A weapon that decided the shape of the three most famous battles of the period has an obvious claim.\n\nYet in every one of them the bow worked inside a system. English commanders fought on the defensive on ground they had chosen; men-at-arms dismounted to hold the line so that the archers were not left exposed; at Agincourt every archer planted a sharpened stake, and the narrow, newly ploughed field between the woods, soaked by a night of rain, did as much to wreck the French attack as the arrows did. Where that protection was missing the same weapon achieved nothing: at Bannockburn in 1314 the English archers were scattered by Scottish horsemen before they could do serious damage, and Edward II lost.\n\nA second rival explanation, beyond the stimulus, is command and enemy error. Edward I's decision at Falkirk to stop charging and start shooting was the act of an experienced commander who could control his army in the middle of a battle. At Agincourt the French had no king on the field, their command was divided among great nobles competing for the front rank, and nobody halted an attack that had already lost its shape. English victories were as much French and Scottish mistakes as English marksmanship.\n\nA third rival is organisation, which is what makes archers exist at all. Commissions of Array under the Statute of Winchester of 1285 selected men in every shire; indentures let captains contract to bring stated numbers of archers for a campaign; parliamentary subsidies and the wool customs paid their wages; and Edward III's order of 1363 that men practise archery on Sundays kept trained shooters coming. Without that machinery the longbow would have remained a Welsh curiosity.\n\nMy judgement is that the longbow was necessary to English victories but never sufficient, so it cannot be the main reason. The main reason is the English way of fighting that grew up around it — a chosen defensive position, dismounted men-at-arms, stakes and massed archery, all paid for by a government that could raise both money and men. The end of the period proves the point: the bow was as good in 1453 as in 1346, but at Castillon French cannon destroyed an English army, because what had been overtaken was the system and not the weapon.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide what would have to be true for a weapon to be the main reason for a run of victories, and say so before any narrative.","Set the bow's best battles against the conditions that made those battles safe for archers, and find a case where archers were useless.","Bring in at least one reason the two bullets do not prompt, and let your conclusion apply the criterion rather than repeat the paragraphs."],"codes":["12-1 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of new weapons and formations","12-1 — 3 Case studies: The Battle of Falkirk (1298)","12-1 — 3 Case studies: The Battle of Agincourt (1415)","12 — The process of change: the role of the strand 3 case studies"]},{"q":"'The wars against France were the main reason why English armies changed in the years c1250–c1500.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Hundred Years War (1337–1453)\n• the wars in Scotland\nYou must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6, plus up to 4 SPaG. Credit the demands of long campaigns and sieges overseas, weighed against the Welsh and Scottish wars that supplied the archers and the tactical system, with own material on royal finance, array and gunpowder, and a criterion-based judgement. Answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 11 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"A war is the main reason for change if the change began with it and would not have gone nearly as far without it. On that test the French war carried English warfare furthest, but it did not begin the changes it carried.\n\nThe case for the French war is its scale and its length. Fighting across the Channel from 1337 to 1453 needed armies that could stay abroad for a season or more, and the roughly forty unpaid days a tenant-in-chief owed for his land were useless for that. Kings therefore raised men by indenture, a written contract for stated numbers of men-at-arms and archers, for a stated term, at agreed wages. The feudal host was summoned for the last time in 1385, and even that summons was dropped in favour of paid contracts. The war also fixed what English armies mostly did: sieges, garrisons and chevauchées rather than battles. Henry V brought a train of guns to Harfleur in 1415 and still spent five weeks on it, from 18 August to 22 September, and it was French artillery that ended the war at Castillon in 1453.\n\nYet the army that won in France had been built in Britain. Edward I's Welsh campaigns of 1277 and 1282–83 brought Welsh archers into English service in numbers, and at Falkirk on 22 July 1298 he used massed shooting to open gaps in Wallace's schiltrons for his cavalry to charge through. The formation that later destroyed French armies was worked out against the Scots: at Dupplin Moor in 1332 and at Halidon Hill in 1333 dismounted men-at-arms holding the centre, with archers on the flanks, wrecked Scottish armies, and Edward III, who commanded at Halidon Hill, took that arrangement to Crécy in 1346. The archer, the dismounted knight and the defensive line were all in place before Agincourt in 1415, where roughly five in six of Henry's men carried bows.\n\nBeyond the two bullets, a war only changes an army where a government can pay and organise. Parliamentary lay subsidies and the customs on wool provided the wages written into the indentures, while the Statute of Winchester of 1285 and the Commissions of Array kept a machinery for combing each shire for armed men. Edward III's order of 1363 that men should practise at the butts on holy days shows a crown manufacturing its own supply of archers rather than waiting for a war to produce them. Technology pushed in a direction of its own: gunpowder belonged to no one kingdom, and guns improved through the fifteenth century whoever was fighting.\n\nMy judgement is that the statement is more right than wrong, but not for the reason it implies. The French war did not invent the longbow army or the dismounted formation; the Welsh and Scottish wars between 1277 and 1333 did that. What a hundred and sixteen years of fighting overseas did was to make them permanent, to force the crown to tax and contract for them on a scale no British campaign ever demanded, and to bring in the siege artillery that would in the end make the longbow army obsolete. France did not start the change in English armies; it decided how far it went.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first what would make one war the main reason for change rather than one setting among several.","Sort your evidence into what a war began, what it made permanent and what it only paid for.","Open with the case for France, then test it against the Welsh and Scottish wars fought before 1337."],"codes":["12-1 — 3 Case studies: The Battle of Agincourt (1415)","12 — The process of change: the key factors","12 — The process of change: the role of the strand 3 case studies"]},{"q":"'Changes in the way soldiers were recruited were the most important development in English warfare in the years c1250–c1500.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• scutage\n• the longbow\nYou must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6, plus up to 4 SPaG. Credit the move from feudal summons through scutage and array to indentures, weighed against weapons, formations and the dismounting of men-at-arms, with own material on taxation and a criterion-based judgement. Answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 11 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"The test of importance here should be which development made the others possible and which lasted longest. On that test the change in recruitment has a very strong claim, though it was itself driven by the wars it served.\n\nThe change in recruitment was fundamental. At the start of the period a king called on tenants-in-chief for knight service they owed because they held land — unpaid, and reckoned at about forty days a year. That could not fight a war in Gascony, so kings took scutage instead and spent the money on soldiers who stayed. From the middle of the fourteenth century the indenture became the normal instrument: a written contract for a stated number of men-at-arms and archers, for a stated period, at agreed wages. Underneath it the older obligation survived, so that Commissions of Array under the Statute of Winchester of 1285 could still select armed men from every shire. By 1415 an English army was a collection of contracted retinues, and by the Wars of the Roses lords were raising followings the same way for their own quarrels.\n\nIts importance is that it enabled everything else. A longbow army is impossible without it: archers had to be found in thousands, paid for months and kept in the field far from home, and only contract and cash could do that. The composition of the army changed as a result, from a force built around knights to one in which, at Agincourt, roughly five in six men carried bows.\n\nThe strongest rival is the longbow and the tactics built on it. Massed archery broke the schiltrons at Falkirk in 1298, destroyed the French charges at Crécy on 26 August 1346 and, with sharpened stakes, at Agincourt on 25 October 1415. Alongside it came the habit of dismounting men-at-arms to hold the line, which reshaped the knight's role in combat. These are changes in how battles were fought, which is arguably more central than how men were signed on.\n\nBeyond both bullets lies the development that underpins recruitment itself: the growth of royal finance through Parliament. Lay subsidies and the customs on wool paid the wages in the indentures, and the poll taxes of 1377, 1379 and 1380 show how hard the crown pushed to keep paying for the war. Gunpowder, by contrast, was a genuine novelty but still marginal by c1500, useful at Calais in 1346–47 and Harfleur in 1415 but not on the battlefield.\n\nMy judgement is that the change in recruitment was the most important development, because it was the condition of the others: the longbow mattered only when the crown could buy archers in bulk and keep them abroad, and the dismounted line of men-at-arms was a contracted line. But importance runs both ways — it was the length and distance of the wars that forced recruitment to change, so the development that mattered most was itself the creature of the wars it made possible.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["'Most important' needs a test: decide whether you mean most visible, longest lasting, or the one without which the others could not happen.","Trace recruitment as a sequence — obligation, money, contract — and then set the weapons and tactics against it.","Ask where the wages promised in an indenture actually came from, and let the conclusion say on what criterion you have decided."],"codes":["12-1 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","12-1 — 1 The nature of warfare: The composition of the army","12 — The process of change: factors working together"]},{"q":"'The impact of war on English civilians in the years c1250–c1500 was mainly financial.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• purveyance\n• plunder\nYou must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6, plus up to 4 SPaG. Credit taxation, purveyance and the wool customs weighed against array, raiding and billeting, with regional variation and the profits of war as own material, and a criterion-based judgement. Answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 11 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"Judging this claim means deciding whose experience counts: what most English people met most of the time, or the worst that war did to some of them. Taken across the kingdom as a whole the statement is largely right, but it breaks down badly in particular places.\n\nThe financial case is strong because England itself was rarely the battlefield. The wars of this period were fought in Wales, in Scotland and above all in France, so for the ordinary villager war arrived as a demand. Purveyance let royal officers take grain, animals, carts and ships at a price the crown set, often paying late or not at all, and a village that lost its plough-team in spring lost its harvest in autumn. Parliament granted lay subsidies and duties on wool exports, which fell on the trade that supported much of the countryside, and when that was not enough the government levied poll taxes in 1377, 1379 and 1380 to pay for the war with France. The Peasants' Revolt of 1381 shows how heavy those demands felt.\n\nAgainst that, some civilians met war in person, and for them the impact was not financial at all. Scottish raids into northern England after Bannockburn in 1314 burned towns and crops and extorted protection money; French and Genoese raiders sacked Southampton in 1338; soldiers on the march plundered and were billeted on households; and Commissions of Array took men out of the fields, which was a serious loss after the Black Death of 1348–49 had killed perhaps a third of the population and while the Statute of Labourers of 1351 was holding wages down. English armies did far worse to French civilians, where the chevauchée deliberately burned villages and crops to ruin the enemy's resources.\n\nBeyond the stimulus there is a third consideration: war also made money for some people. Ransoms enriched captains who took prisoners such as those captured at Poitiers in 1356, garrisons and armies had to be fed and clothed, and Calais, taken in 1347 and later the compulsory market for English wool exports, kept a whole trade running. The financial impact was not simply a drain.\n\nMy criterion is what the typical household experienced, and by that measure the statement holds: for most English people, for most of the period, war was a series of bills — purveyors, subsidies, customs and poll taxes — rather than an enemy in the village street. But the judgement has to be qualified by geography. On the Scottish border and on the south coast the impact was physical, violent and repeated, and during the Wars of the Roses after 1455 armies lived off their own countrymen. The claim is right about the many and quite wrong about the exposed minority.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first whose experience your judgement is about: the typical household, or the worst-affected districts.","Set the machinery that took money against the occasions when war arrived in person, and keep naming dates and places.","Find a point the two bullets do not prompt, and then qualify your conclusion by region rather than sitting on the fence."],"codes":["12-1 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians","12 — The process of change: warfare and the key features of society","12 — The process of change: factors working together"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In what year was the Battle of Falkirk fought, and who commanded the English army?","a":"1298; Edward I."},{"q":"What was scutage?","a":"Money paid to the king instead of serving in person, which he used to hire soldiers who would serve longer."},{"q":"Which Scottish formation stopped Edward I's cavalry at Falkirk, and what finally broke it?","a":"The schiltron; English archers shooting gaps in it for the cavalry to charge through."},{"q":"Roughly what proportion of Henry V's army at Agincourt were archers?","a":"About five in six."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The feudal summons","back":"The king's call on his tenants-in-chief to bring knights because they held land from him. The service was unpaid and usually reckoned at about forty days a year.","code":"12-1 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","open":false},{"front":"Scutage","back":"'Shield money': a payment made to the king instead of serving in person. Kings used the cash to hire soldiers who would serve for longer and further from home.","code":"12-1 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","open":false},{"front":"The Statute of Winchester (1285)","back":"Edward I's law requiring every man between fifteen and sixty to keep weapons suited to his wealth, so that the sheriff could call out the armed men of the county.","code":"12-1 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","open":false},{"front":"Commission of Array","back":"Royal commissioners sent into a county to inspect the armed men and select the best of them for the king's army.","code":"12-1 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","open":false},{"front":"Indenture (military contract)","back":"A written contract, normal from the mid-fourteenth century, in which a captain agreed to serve for a fixed period with a stated number of men-at-arms and archers at agreed wages.","code":"12-1 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","open":false},{"front":"The longbow","back":"A bow about six feet long, usually of yew, drawn with the whole body. It needed years of practice, outshot the crossbow and had a range of over two hundred yards.","code":"12-1 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of new weapons and formations","open":false},{"front":"The schiltron","back":"A Scottish formation: a dense ring or block of spearmen with long spears pointing outwards. It stopped cavalry but had to stand still, so archery could break it.","code":"12-1 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of new weapons and formations","open":false},{"front":"Early cannon in England","back":"The first English picture of a gun is in a manuscript made for Edward III in 1326 and guns are reported at Crécy in 1346. They mattered in sieges, being heavy, slow and inaccurate.","code":"12-1 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of new weapons and formations","open":false},{"front":"The mounted knight","back":"An armoured horseman of noble or gentle birth who held land in return for service. The decisive arm early in the period, he increasingly fought dismounted by the fifteenth century.","code":"12-1 — 1 The nature of warfare: The composition of the army","open":false},{"front":"The composition of an English army in the Hundred Years War","back":"Mostly archers, with men-at-arms who usually fought on foot: at Agincourt in 1415 roughly five in six of Henry V's men carried bows. Nobles supplied and commanded the retinues.","code":"12-1 — 1 The nature of warfare: The composition of the army","open":false},{"front":"Purveyance","back":"The crown's right to take food, animals, carts and ships for the army at a price it set itself, often paying late or not at all. It was bitterly resented in the villages.","code":"12-1 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians","open":false},{"front":"One burden that war placed on an English village","back":"Purveyance of grain, animals and carts, often unpaid; men taken away by Commission of Array; taxes such as the lay subsidy, the wool customs and the poll taxes of 1377, 1379 and 1380.","code":"12-1 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians","open":true},{"front":"The Battle of Falkirk (1298)","back":"On 22 July 1298 Edward I beat William Wallace: his archers opened gaps in the Scottish schiltrons for the cavalry to charge through. Wallace then resigned as Guardian of Scotland.","code":"12-1 — 3 Case studies: The Battle of Falkirk (1298)","open":false},{"front":"One reason for the English victory at Falkirk in 1298","back":"Edward I switched from cavalry charges to archery; the schiltron could not move or reply to missiles; the Scottish cavalry left the field without fighting; the Scots archers were ridden down.","code":"12-1 — 3 Case studies: The Battle of Falkirk (1298)","open":true},{"front":"The Battle of Agincourt (1415)","back":"On 25 October 1415 Henry V's outnumbered army beat the French on a narrow, muddy ploughed field between two woods, its archers protected by sharpened stakes and dismounted men-at-arms.","code":"12-1 — 3 Case studies: The Battle of Agincourt (1415)","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 1 Question 5, 16 marks: 'New weapons were the main reason for the decline of the mounted knight in the years c1250–c1500.' How far do you agree?)","items":["New weapons certainly weakened the knight, but the change that removed his dominance was tactical, and it depended on disciplined foot soldiers rather than on any one weapon. At Falkirk on 22 July 1298 Edward I's armoured horsemen charged Wallace's schiltrons and could not break them: a ring of spearmen with long spears stopped cavalry dead, and it was archery, not the charge, that finally opened gaps. The lesson was learned in England as well as against it, so that at Crécy on 26 August 1346 and again at Agincourt on 25 October 1415 English men-at-arms climbed off their horses and fought on foot beside archers protected by sharpened stakes. The knight became one element in a combined line instead of the arm that decided the battle. Cost pushed the same way: a knight's plate armour, warhorses and equipment cost many times what it cost to arm an archer, and once the crown paid wages rather than calling in service, every pound bought more fighting men if it was spent on archers raised by Commission of Array. The longbow mattered, then, chiefly because it made these formations work. On the criterion of what actually ended the charge as the decisive act of a battle, the answer is the rise of protected, disciplined infantry, which new weapons served rather than caused.","Why this reaches Level 4: the explanation is directed consistently at the conceptual focus — every sentence is about why the knight stopped being dominant, not about what knights did; the information is precisely selected (three dated battles, the schiltron, the stakes, the array, the relative cost of armour) rather than general; the counter-claim in the question is stated and then answered rather than ignored; and the criterion for the judgement — what ended the charge as the decisive act — is named and applied, which is what the third bullet of the Level 4 descriptor requires."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["New weapons were the main reason the mounted knight declined. The longbow was very powerful and could kill men from a long way off, so knights were being hit before they even reached the enemy line. This happened in lots of battles in the Hundred Years War, where the English archers beat the French knights again and again. Guns were also invented around this time and cannon could knock down castle walls, which meant that knights and castles were not as useful as they had been. Armour was also very expensive, so fewer people wanted to be knights. Knights did carry on fighting after this, but they were not as important as they used to be. So I agree that new weapons were the main reason for the decline of the mounted knight.","Why this stays at Level 2: it meets 'an explanation is given showing limited analysis and with implicit or unsustained links to the conceptual focus of the question' and 'accurate and relevant information is included, showing some knowledge and understanding of the period', and the overall judgement is given but asserted. It misses Level 3 and Level 4 because nothing is dated or named — no battle, no year, no formation, no commander — 'lots of battles' does the work that evidence should do; because the point about cannon and castles is never linked to the decline of the mounted knight on the battlefield; and because no criterion is set for what 'main reason' would mean, so the final sentence repeats the question instead of applying a test to it."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Case study: the Battle of Falkirk, 22 July 1298","items":["Date and place: 22 July 1298, near Falkirk in central Scotland.","Commanders: Edward I of England, king since 1272 and conqueror of Wales in the campaigns of 1277 and 1282–83; William Wallace, Guardian of Scotland.","Background: Edward I invaded Scotland in 1296 and deposed King John Balliol.","Background: Wallace and Andrew Murray defeated an English army at Stirling Bridge in 1297, after which Wallace was knighted and made Guardian of Scotland.","English army: much larger than the Scottish force, with heavy cavalry and large numbers of archers, many of them Welsh.","Supply: the English army was short of food before the battle and quarrels broke out between the English and the Welsh troops.","Scottish deployment: four schiltrons of spearmen, with archers between them and a small force of Scottish noble cavalry behind.","Opening: the English cavalry charged; the Scottish cavalry left the field without fighting.","The Scots archers were ridden down and their commander, Sir John Stewart, was killed.","The schiltrons held against the cavalry, because horses will not charge home on a hedge of long spears.","Turning point: Edward halted the charges and brought archers and crossbowmen forward to shoot into the static formations until gaps opened.","Outcome: English cavalry charged through the gaps, the schiltrons broke and the Scots were cut down; Wallace escaped.","Consequences: Wallace resigned the guardianship later in 1298; he was captured in 1305 and executed in London in August that year.","What it proves for the theme: disciplined infantry could stop shock cavalry, but only a commander combining missile power with cavalry could break disciplined infantry."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Case study: the Battle of Agincourt, 25 October 1415","items":["Date and place: 25 October 1415, near the village of Azincourt in northern France.","Commanders: Henry V, king of England since 1413; on the French side the Constable, Charles d'Albret, and Marshal Boucicaut. Charles VI of France and the Dauphin were not on the field.","Campaign: Henry landed in Normandy in August 1415 and besieged Harfleur, which surrendered on 22 September after his guns had battered its walls.","Dysentery killed and disabled many of his men at Harfleur; he left a garrison there and marched for Calais with the rest.","The French held the crossings of the river Somme and forced him to march inland before he could get over.","Numbers: the English army was probably between 6,000 and 9,000 strong and roughly five in six of them were archers. The size of the French army is disputed: the chroniclers and the traditional accounts make it several times larger, while Anne Curry's work on the muster rolls and pay records puts it at about 12,000, much closer to the English figure; other historians still argue for 20,000–30,000. Contemporary figures vary widely and cannot be relied on.","Ground: a narrow strip of newly ploughed farmland between the woods of Agincourt and Tramecourt, soaked by a night of rain.","English deployment: men-at-arms dismounted in the line, archers on the wings behind sharpened stakes that Henry had ordered every archer to carry.","Henry advanced the line to within bowshot to provoke the French attack, and had the stakes replanted.","The French cavalry attacks on the wings broke on the stakes and the arrows.","The French men-at-arms advanced on foot in heavy armour through deep mud, crowded together as the field narrowed, and arrived exhausted.","English archers left their bows and fought at close quarters with mallets, axes and swords.","Henry ordered the killing of French prisoners when he feared a fresh attack and a raid on his baggage.","Losses: very heavy French losses, including the Constable Charles d'Albret and several great nobles; the Duke of Orléans and Marshal Boucicaut were captured; English losses were light, though the Duke of York was killed.","Consequences: the Treaty of Troyes in 1420 recognised Henry as heir to the French throne; he died in 1422.","What it proves for the theme: by 1415 the English system — dismounted men-at-arms, massed archers, stakes and a chosen position — was complete."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — The nature of warfare: army, weapons and formations, c1250–c1500","items":["Mounted knight: an armoured horseman of noble or gentle birth, holding land in return for service, trained from boyhood as a page and then a squire, and practised in tournaments.","Men-at-arms: fully armoured soldiers, not all of them knights, who increasingly fought on foot from the fourteenth century.","Infantry: spearmen and billmen of low status from the shire levy, with almost no training and low pay.","Archers: the largest element of English armies by the fourteenth century; mounted archers rode to the battle and fought on foot.","The longbow: about six feet of yew, drawn with the whole body; a trained archer could shoot several arrows in the time a crossbowman took to wind and loose one, out to a range of over two hundred yards.","The crossbow: slower to shoot but much easier to learn; used by the Genoese mercenaries who were beaten by English archers at Crécy in 1346.","Armour: mail gave way to plate through the period, so that by 1400 a man-at-arms was encased in steel; it protected well but was exhausting on foot in mud.","The schiltron: a Scottish formation of spearmen that stopped cavalry; broken by archery at Falkirk in 1298, but used on the attack to defeat Edward II at Bannockburn in 1314.","Dismounting: English commanders regularly put their men-at-arms on foot to steady the line, as at Crécy (1346), Poitiers (1356) and Agincourt (1415).","Gunpowder: the earliest English picture of a gun appears in a manuscript made for Edward III in 1326, and guns are reported in action at Crécy in 1346.","Sieges: guns mattered first against walls — Edward III used them at Calais in 1346–47 and Henry V at Harfleur in 1415.","Weaknesses of early guns: heavy, very slow to load, inaccurate and dangerous to their crews; James II of Scotland was killed in 1460 when a gun burst beside him at Roxburgh.","Decline of the mounted knight: gradual rather than sudden — knights remained the social elite and the officer class, but by 1415 they usually fought on foot."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — The experience of war: recruitment, training and civilians","items":["Feudal summons: tenants-in-chief owed the king knight service because they held land, unpaid and usually reckoned at about forty days a year.","Its limits: forty unpaid days could not fight a campaign in Scotland or France, and service overseas was disputed.","Scutage: money paid instead of service, which kings spent on soldiers who would serve as long as they were paid.","Assize of Arms (1181): Henry II required free men to keep arms according to their wealth — the basis of the shire levy.","Statute of Winchester (1285): Edward I required every man between fifteen and sixty to keep arms suited to his wealth, so that the county could be called out.","Commissions of Array: royal commissioners inspected the armed men of a county and chose the best for the king's army.","Indentures: from the mid-fourteenth century a captain contracted in writing to serve for a stated period with a stated number of men-at-arms and archers at agreed wages, with rules on plunder and ransoms.","Motives: wages, plunder and a share of any ransom; a captured nobleman was worth far more alive than dead.","Training of knights: lifelong, beginning in a lord's household as a page and squire, with tournaments and jousts as practice.","Training of archers: Edward III ordered in 1363 that able-bodied men practise archery on Sundays and holidays instead of playing other games.","Training of infantry: almost none; the levy learned on campaign.","Purveyance: the crown's right to take food, animals, carts and ships for the army at a price it set, with payment often late or never.","Taxation: Parliament granted lay subsidies and duties on wool exports for the Hundred Years War; poll taxes were levied in 1377, 1379 and 1380 and helped provoke the Peasants' Revolt of 1381.","Plunder and raiding: English chevauchées burned French crops and villages; Scottish raids after Bannockburn in 1314 burned towns in northern England and extorted money; French and Genoese raiders sacked Southampton in 1338.","Labour: the Black Death of 1348–49 killed perhaps a third of the population and the Statute of Labourers of 1351 tried to hold wages at their earlier level, so taking men for the army hurt villages even more."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — The wars of the period, c1250–c1500","items":["Wales: Edward I's campaigns of 1277 and 1282–83 conquered the principality and were followed by a ring of royal castles.","Scotland: Edward I invaded in 1296; the Scots won at Stirling Bridge in 1297; Edward won at Falkirk in 1298.","Bannockburn, 1314: Robert Bruce's schiltrons, used on the attack, defeated Edward II.","The Hundred Years War: fought between England and France from 1337 to 1453.","Sluys, 1340: an English naval victory that gave Edward III control of the Channel for the invasion.","Crécy, 26 August 1346: Edward III defeated Philip VI; English archers outshot the Genoese crossbowmen and broke repeated cavalry charges, and English guns are reported in action.","Calais fell in 1347 after a siege of nearly a year and remained English until 1558.","Poitiers, 19 September 1356: the Black Prince defeated and captured King John II of France.","Treaty of Brétigny, 1360: Edward III gained extensive lands in France in return for dropping his claim to the French crown.","Agincourt, 25 October 1415, and the Treaty of Troyes, 1420, which made Henry V heir to the French throne.","Orléans, 1429: the siege was raised with the help of Joan of Arc, after which the French steadily recovered ground.","Castillon, 1453: French cannon destroyed an English army and the war ended with England holding only Calais.","The Wars of the Roses, 1455–1485: fought between the houses of Lancaster and York with armies raised from the retinues of lords; the first battle was at St Albans in 1455, the largest at Towton in 1461, and Richard III was killed at Bosworth in 1485."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 1 Question 5 (16 marks + 4 SPaG): 'The English army of c1500 was very different from the English army of c1250.' How far do you agree? Stimulus points: the feudal summons; gunpowder weapons.","items":["The question as printed: \"'The English army of c1500 was very different from the English army of c1250.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the feudal summons • gunpowder weapons. You must also use information of your own.\" (16 marks, plus up to 4 for spelling, punctuation, grammar and the use of specialist terminology.)","Decoded question: the second-order concept is change and continuity across a complete period, c1250–c1500. 'Very different' has to be measured, so test more than one aspect of the army — how it was raised, what it was made of, how it won a battle — and set the changes against what stayed the same. Do not narrate the wars.","Criterion to set in the opening: the army is 'very different' if a soldier of c1250 would not recognise how it was raised, who was in it or how it won. Signal at once that organisation and composition changed out of recognition while command and the daily business of war did not.","Paragraph 1 (agree, first stimulus point): how armies were raised. Evidence: at the start of the period a tenant-in-chief owed knight service because he held land, unpaid and reckoned at about forty days a year, and kings increasingly took scutage instead; from the middle of the fourteenth century the indenture was the normal instrument, a written contract for stated numbers of men-at-arms and archers, for a stated term, at agreed wages; underneath it the Statute of Winchester of 1285 and the Commissions of Array still combed the shires. The feudal host was summoned for the last time in 1385, and even that summons was dropped in favour of paid contracts. Interim judgement: by c1500 an English army was a bundle of paid contracts, not an obligation owed for land.","Paragraph 2 (agree, beyond the stimulus): composition and the way battles were won. Evidence: Welsh archers taken into English service after Edward I's campaigns of 1277 and 1282–83; Falkirk on 22 July 1298, where massed shooting opened the schiltrons for the cavalry; Dupplin Moor in 1332 and Halidon Hill in 1333, where dismounted men-at-arms with archers on the flanks wrecked Scottish armies; Agincourt on 25 October 1415, where roughly five in six of Henry V's men carried bows and every archer planted a sharpened stake. Interim judgement: the mounted charge had given way to a defensive line and mass archery, and this is the deepest change of the three, which is why it must be the point you bring in yourself.","Paragraph 3 (partly agree, second stimulus point): gunpowder weapons. Evidence: guns were reported in English hands at Crécy in 1346 and did little; Henry V brought a siege train to Harfleur in 1415 and the town still held out five weeks, from 18 August to 22 September; French artillery ended the Hundred Years War at Castillon in 1453; yet at Towton on 29 March 1461 an English battle still opened with an exchange of arrows, the Yorkist archers shooting with the wind behind them. Interim judgement: by c1500 guns had changed sieges much more than battles, so this is change begun rather than change completed.","Paragraph 4 (disagree): what did not change. Evidence: the army was still commanded by men of high birth, with great lords bringing their retinues to Agincourt as they had to Falkirk and would to the battles of the Wars of the Roses; war still consisted mostly of sieges, garrisons and raiding rather than pitched battle; and civilians still bore purveyance, war taxation and plunder much as they had under Edward I. Interim judgement: the social basis of command and the experience of war for those in its path scarcely moved.","The evaluation thread to run through every paragraph: measure, do not list. Ask of each aspect how far it had moved by c1500 and whether the movement was finished, and keep noticing that change in organisation ran ahead of change in weapons.","What the conclusion must say: agree, but with the difference located precisely and the criterion applied. The army of c1500 was raised, paid and made up in ways a soldier of c1250 would not have recognised, and it won its battles by shooting rather than by charging; but it was still led by the same social class, still spent most of its time besieging and plundering, and its guns had not yet remade the battlefield. Very different in organisation and in combat; recognisably the same in who commanded it and in what it did to civilians.","Timing and SPaG: about 20 minutes of the 80-minute paper — about a minute a mark plus a little for SPaG, so roughly 4 planning, 14 writing and 2 checking. With 5 minutes on Question 3 and 15 on Question 4 that is 40 minutes for Section B and leaves the other 40 for Section A. The 4 SPaG marks reward consistent accuracy and a wide range of specialist terms, so spell and use indenture, retinue, array, scutage, men-at-arms and chevauchée correctly."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:04:03Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.82,"notes":"Judged afresh: no answer key is wrong, every solution matches its own indicative content, and every new fact in the three retargeted worked examples verified on the web — the Statute of Purveyors of 1362 (ready money, purveyors renamed 'buyers'), Edward I's delayed supply ships and his intended fall-back on Edinburgh before he turned to Falkirk, the Army Service Corps of 1888 and the Royal Logistic Corps of 1993,¹ the 1275 custom of half a mark (6s 8d) a sack, the maltolt of 1294–97 at 40s, the statute of 1362 requiring parliamentary assent to charges on wool, the Confirmation of the Charters of 1297, the fifteenth and tenth fixed in 1334 on the assessments of 1332, daily wages of 3d, 6d, 12d and 2s, Stirling in 1304 and the Warwolf, Calais from 4 September 1346 to 3 August 1347, Rouen on 19 January 1419, the Treaty of Troyes of 21 May 1420, the £80,000² on Edward I's Welsh castles and DeVries's count of more than 350 attacks on fortified places; the dedupe scan prints 'no duplication', none of the three new stems sets or shadows a bank question, and no bank question reproduces the Paper 1 specimen Option 12 section (Q3 composition nineteenth century against the modern period, Q4 the role of the tank, Q5 Edward I at Falkirk against Cromwell at Naseby, Q6 civilians c1700–c1900 against c1500–c1700), so what is left is editorial: one worked example still wears a single-battle stem in the Question 5 form, two 16-mark questions make the same case, the case studies are never asked as questions, and two retargets reach past the entry's printed content. ¹The lineage runs ASC 1888 → RASC 1918 → RCT 1965 → RLC 1993, and the example jumps the 1965 step. ²Over £80,000 between 1277 and 1304 under Master James of St George, as the example states."}}},"12-2":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"12-2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This room is the early modern block of Option 12, Warfare and British society, c1250–present, and it carries the whole of Paper 1 Section B for the period c1500–c1700. It is where the medieval army of archers and men-at-arms turns into an army of pike and shot, where the first permanent professional force in English history is created, and where, in the civil wars, the English fought each other on their own fields, streets and market squares. It supplies the option's named case study, the Battle of Naseby of 1645 and Cromwell's part in it, and the named example of the New Model Army, so it is the period you are most likely to be asked to judge at 16 marks. It is also the anchor for comparison questions, because the 4-mark similarity or difference question normally spans at least a century and this period sits between the medieval material on one side and the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries on the other.","idea":[{"h":"Pike, shot and horse: the army becomes a machine of parts","t":"For most of the Middle Ages an English army was built around one missile weapon, the longbow, with dismounted men-at-arms holding the line. Between c1500 and c1700 the foot split into two types that could not work apart. Musketeers carried matchlocks and did the killing at a distance, but while a musketeer went through the long sequence of loading he was helpless, and he could not stop a horseman at all. Pikemen, in helmet and back-and-breast, carried ash pikes about sixteen feet long and stood ready to receive cavalry so that the shot could reload behind them. The ratio moved steadily in the musket's favour: in the New Model Army a regiment of foot was intended to be roughly two musketeers to each pikeman. Cavalry changed too: armoured cuirassiers such as Sir Arthur Haselrig's regiment were already unusual by the 1640s, and the standard trooper was a harquebusier in a buff coat, back-and-breast and pot helmet, armed with sword and pistols. Dragoons were mounted infantry who rode cheap horses to the fighting and dismounted to shoot. Finally, armies stopped being temporary: the New Model of 1645 was kept together between campaigns, and after 1660 Charles II kept guards regiments permanently on foot. A top-level answer does not list these parts; it shows that each one existed because of the others — the pike exists because the musket is slow, drill exists because pike and shot must move together, and the standing army exists because such an army only works if it trains together all year.","code":"12-2 — 1 The nature of warfare: Continuity and change in the composition of the army"},{"h":"The musket kills the longbow","t":"The matchlock musket fired a lead ball by lowering a length of burning cord on to powder in the pan. It was heavy, at first needing a forked rest, it was useless in heavy rain and it was slow to reload, so musketeers stood several ranks deep and fired in turn. What it could do was punch a ball through plate armour that an arrow would glance off, and it could be taught to an ordinary recruit in weeks, whereas a war bow demanded years of practice from boyhood to build the strength to draw it. That is why the bow disappeared: in the 1590s the Privy Council ordered the trained bands to give up bows for muskets and calivers, the caliver being a lighter matchlock that needed no rest. The wheel-lock pistol, which struck sparks from a spinning wheel and could be carried loaded, put firepower into a horseman's hand and produced the caracole, in which cavalry trotted up, fired and wheeled away. Later in the seventeenth century flintlocks, which struck a flint against steel, began to replace the matchlock and removed the burning cord altogether. Artillery mattered most in sieges, where gunpowder had already made high medieval walls useless, so towns such as Oxford and Newark were ringed with low angled earth bastions instead. A top-level answer uses weapons to explain behaviour: it says what the weapon made soldiers do, not what the weapon looked like.","code":"12-2 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of developments in weaponry"},{"h":"From the muster to the New Model: finding men and drilling them","t":"England had no regular army for most of this period. The Crown raised men through the counties: Lords Lieutenant held musters, and from 1573 a chosen part of the county militia, the trained bands, was equipped and drilled several times a year for home defence. The London trained bands were good enough to hold the Parliamentarian line at the first Battle of Newbury in September 1643. For war abroad the government pressed men, often the poor and the unemployed; Elizabeth I's government pressed thousands for the Netherlands from 1585 and for Ireland in the 1590s. In 1642 the quarrel over who could call out the militia helped to start the war, Parliament issuing the Militia Ordinance and the king commissions of array. The New Model Army, created by ordinance in February 1645, was different in kind: about 22,000 men in standard regiments, twelve of foot at 1,200 men, eleven of horse at 600 and 1,000 dragoons, recruited nationally rather than from one county association, paid from a monthly assessment at 8 pence a day for the foot and 2 shillings for a trooper, clothed in red, governed by a written code of war that punished plunder and desertion, and promoted for ability rather than birth. Training meant collective drill: printed manuals set out numbered postures for handling pike and musket so that ranks could fire and reload in turn. A top-level answer links recruitment to performance — it explains why paid, drilled, nationally recruited men stood when local levies ran.","code":"12-2 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants"},{"h":"Free quarter, requisitioning and the cost of a war fought at home","t":"Between c1500 and c1700 England went from sending armies abroad to being fought over itself, and civilians paid for it in four ways. First, the armies fed on them: free quarter meant soldiers were billeted on households that had to house and feed them in return for a ticket promising later payment, while requisitioning took horses, carts, grain and fodder outright. Second, the fighting destroyed: Leicester was stormed and plundered on 31 May 1645, Colchester was starved into surrender in August 1648 after a siege lasting from June, and defenders routinely burned suburbs and dug earthworks across people's fields. Third, the state demanded: Parliament's assessment (weekly from 1643, monthly from 1645) and the new excise of 1643 on beer and, from 1644, salt and meat reached households no army came near, and royalists had their estates sequestered. Fourth, disease travelled with the armies; historians estimate that around 85,000 died in the fighting in England and Wales and perhaps 100,000 more of war-related sickness, out of a population of roughly five million. Civilians were not always passive: in 1645 country people in the west and south formed Clubmen associations to keep both armies off their crops, and Cromwell had to break up a large body of them at Hambledon Hill in Dorset that August. A top-level answer distinguishes the kinds of suffering and weighs them, rather than listing horrors.","code":"12-2 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians"},{"h":"Naseby, 14 June 1645: why the king lost his army","t":"Naseby in Northamptonshire was the decisive battle of the first civil war. Sir Thomas Fairfax, Lord General of the New Model Army, had about 14,000 men; Charles I and Prince Rupert had about 9,000, George Goring's cavalry being away besieging Taunton and a garrison having been left in Leicester, stormed on 31 May. Fairfax drew up behind the crest of a ridge so the royalists could not judge his strength, and Colonel John Okey's dragoons lined the Sulby hedges on the flank. Rupert, on the royalist right, routed Henry Ireton's wing and let his troopers ride on to the Parliamentarian baggage train at Naseby village, removing himself from the battle. Cromwell, appointed Lieutenant-General of Horse only days before, broke Sir Marmaduke Langdale's Northern Horse on the other wing and then did what almost no cavalry commander of the age did: he sent only part of his men in pursuit and wheeled the rest into the flank and rear of the royalist foot, who were pressing Philip Skippon's infantry. Caught front and rear, the royalist infantry were destroyed — about 1,000 killed and some 4,500 taken prisoner, with all the artillery and the king's private letters, which Parliament printed. Camp followers were butchered after the battle. A top-level answer uses Naseby to test an argument about leadership, discipline or numbers, not to retell the day.","code":"12-2 — 3 Case study: The Battle of Naseby (1645)"}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 3, 4 marks: turning two descriptions into one analysed difference","setup":"Paper 1 Question 3, worth 4 marks: 'Explain one way in which the weapons carried by English horsemen in the years c1250–c1500 were different from the weapons carried by English horsemen in the years c1500–c1700.' The mark scheme gives 2 marks for AO2 and 2 for AO1, so one difference must be analysed and supported with specific information drawn from both of the periods named.","steps":["Pick a difference of kind rather than of degree: the medieval horseman's weapons all worked at the length of his arm and took their force from the weight of horse and rider, while the early modern horseman carried gunpowder weapons that could kill before he ever reached the enemy.","Evidence the medieval half with named detail: the man-at-arms of the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries rode with a lance couched under his arm, a sword at his side and often a mace or an axe, and his charge worked by shock; the missile power of an English army of that period sat elsewhere, with the mounted archers who rode to the field and then dismounted to shoot the longbow on foot.","Evidence the early modern half with named detail: the wheellock, which struck its own spark from a spinning steel wheel, made a pistol that could be carried ready loaded and fired with one hand from the saddle, and by the middle of the sixteenth century mounted pistoleers such as the German reiters were riding up to fire by ranks, the caracole. By the civil wars the lance had gone from English field armies altogether: under the ordinance of 17 February 1645 the New Model Army's horse regiments of 600 men carried sword and a pair of pistols, and its regiment of 1,000 dragoons rode with muskets and fought on foot.","Close with the sentence that makes it a difference and not two descriptions: gunpowder put a missile weapon into the horseman's own hand, so cavalry stopped being simply the arm that delivered a shock at the point of a lance and became a way of carrying firepower quickly to the place where it was wanted."],"cues":["What kind of difference will you pick — one about the shape of the weapons, or one about how far off they could kill?","Which weapons did the medieval man-at-arms carry, and where did an English army of his day keep its missile power?","What new lock made a firearm usable on horseback, and what did a New Model Army trooper carry?","What has the last sentence to say about gunpowder so that two descriptions become one difference?"]},{"title":"Question 4, 12 marks: building an explanation that escapes the stimulus cap","setup":"Paper 1 Question 4: 'Explain why the pike remained part of English armies for so long after firearms had become common. You may use the following in your answer: • the cavalry charge • push of pike. You must also use information of your own.' AO2 is 6 marks and AO1 is 6, and an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content is capped at 8 marks and shut out of Level 4.","steps":["Set the criterion first: ask what job the pike did that nothing else on the field could do yet, because a weapon is kept not out of habit but because giving it up would leave a gap.","First reason, from the stimulus: the musketeer could not save himself from horsemen. A matchlock took a long fixed sequence to load and left its user defenceless while he did it, and once fired it was no more than a club, so the foot needed a hedge of pikes — usually between sixteen and eighteen feet of ash tipped with steel — that the musketeers could shelter under when cavalry came on.","Second reason, from the stimulus: against other foot the pike was still the weapon that took and held ground, since blocks of pikemen levelled their pikes and shoved until one side gave way. Armies therefore went on raising pikemen in quantity: the New Model Army's foot regiments, established by the ordinance of 17 February 1645 at 1,200 men each, were planned as two-thirds musketeers and one-third pikemen, so some 400 men in every regiment still carried the pike.","Third reason, and the one that escapes the cap: nothing yet let one man do both jobs at once. Plug bayonets, pushed into the muzzle so that the musket could not then be fired, were in English hands in the 1670s and 1680s, but it was only the socket bayonet, which left the musket loaded and firing, that made the pikeman unnecessary, and the English army gave the pike up in the years after the Peace of Ryswick of 1697.","Tie the reasons into one line of reasoning: the pike lasted because the firearm of the day was slow, helpless while loading and useless at close quarters, so the foot had to carry its two jobs in two kinds of men — and it went as soon as a single weapon could do both."],"cues":["What question should you ask about a weapon before you explain why it was kept?","What is a musketeer left holding while he reloads, and who is riding at him?","How was a fight between two bodies of foot actually decided, and how many pikemen did that take?","Which reason here is prompted by neither bullet point, and what finally made the pike unnecessary?","What one sentence turns three separate reasons into a single argument about why the pike lasted?"]},{"title":"Question 4, 12 marks: explaining why something was given up","setup":"Paper 1 Question 4: 'Explain why English soldiers wore less armour by the end of the seventeenth century than they had worn in the middle of the sixteenth. You may use the following in your answer: • the buff coat • the pike. You must also use information of your own.' Answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 8 marks and shut out of Level 4, so at least one developed reason must come from beyond the two stimulus points.","steps":["Sort the reasons into kinds so that the answer analyses rather than lists: what the new weapons did to plate, what plate cost the men who had to buy it and carry it, and what the army needed its soldiers to do instead.","Take the reason the weapons give: a matchlock ball would punch through plate that an arrow glanced off, so armour that turned a sword no longer turned the weapon most likely to kill its wearer, and plate made thick enough to be proof against a ball was too heavy to march and fight in.","Take the first stimulus point and show what replaced it: the ordinary horseman of the civil wars was a harquebusier in a thick buff leather coat, back-and-breast and open pot helmet, armed with sword and pistols, while the fully armoured cuirassier had all but vanished — only two such units were raised in the whole war, one of them Sir Arthur Haselrig's.","Take the second stimulus point, which is the exception that proves the rule: armour lasted longest where it still did its job. The pikeman, whose work was to stand and receive cavalry, kept helmet and back-and-breast longest, while the musketeer beside him went into action in a hat; and armour could still save a man, since Haselrig was hit more than once at Roundway Down in July 1643 and survived in his.","Add the reason beyond the stimulus and close the argument: money and numbers. An English costing of 1629 put a harquebusier's equipment at about one pound six shillings against four pounds ten shillings for a cuirassier's, and a government arming thousands of mustered and pressed men at once bought what was cheap, light and quickly issued; by the later seventeenth century whole regiments were putting their armour into store rather than on to their men. Armour was not beaten by a single weapon — it stopped being worth its price."],"cues":["How can you group the reasons so that the answer explains rather than lists?","What can a musket ball do to plate that an arrow could not?","What did a trooper wear instead, once full plate was given up?","Which soldier went on wearing armour, and what does his job tell you?","Which reason here is prompted by neither bullet point, and what figures show it?"]},{"title":"Question 5, 16 marks: planning a judgement between two kinds of change","setup":"Paper 1 Question 5, worth 16 marks with up to 4 further marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology. The statement is: 'Drill and discipline did more than new weapons to change the way English armies fought in the years c1500–c1700.' The question asks how far you agree, and the stimulus points offered are printed drill manuals and the matchlock musket, with the instruction that you must also use information of your own.","steps":["Fix the criterion before writing: ask which change altered what a soldier actually did from one minute to the next in a fight, and test every paragraph against that rather than against how striking the change looks from outside.","Paragraph one, for the statement, using the first stimulus point: drill turned a crowd into a machine. Engraved manuals broke the handling of caliver, musket and pike into numbered postures — Jacob de Gheyn's book of 1607, made for the Dutch army of Maurice of Nassau, and in England William Barriffe's Military Discipline of 1635 — so ranks could fire in turn while others reloaded, and a company could change its front on a word of command.","Paragraph two, also for the statement: discipline away from the battlefield mattered as much as in it. From 1573 the trained bands were mustered and exercised several times a year, and the London bands were steady enough to hold the line at the first Battle of Newbury in September 1643, while Parliament's printed laws and ordinances of war punished plunder and desertion in the armies of the 1640s.","Paragraph three, against, using the second stimulus point: the weapon set the problem that drill was invented to solve. A matchlock took a long fixed sequence to load, left its user helpless while he loaded and could not stop a horseman at all, which is why the foot had to be built of musketeers and pikemen in fixed proportions in the first place.","Paragraph four, beyond the stimulus: guns and ground. Artillery had made high walls useless, so towns answered with low earth ramparts and angled bastions, and campaigns became blockades that no amount of parade-ground practice could shorten — a change in how armies fought that came from the weapon and not from the drill book.","Conclude by applying the criterion: agree in part, because drill changed what a soldier did with his hands and feet every day of his service, but it was invented to answer the musket and the pike, so the weapons are the deeper cause and the drill the change you can see."],"cues":["What test will you use to decide which of the two changes did more?","What did a printed manual actually put into an officer's hands?","Where can you show discipline at work away from a battlefield?","What is it about the matchlock that forced men into a fixed formation?","Which factor here is prompted by neither bullet point?","What must the conclusion do beyond restating the strongest paragraph?"]},{"title":"Question 5, 16 marks: writing the paragraph that beats the stimulus cap","setup":"A student answering the judgement question on whether the way soldiers were armed changed warfare in England more than the way they were raised in the years c1500–c1700 has used only the two stimulus points offered, the pike and impressment. The mark scheme allows an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content a maximum of 11 marks and no access to Level 4, so one paragraph of genuinely new material must be added.","steps":["Choose material the two bullet points do not prompt: not the weapon in a man's hands nor the way he was found, but who paid for his weapons and kept them — the business of arming an army.","State the point and prove it: firearms had to be bought, issued, stored and kept serviceable in bulk, so government took the work over. The Ordnance Office at the Tower of London, reorganised under Henry VIII in the 1540s, held the kingdom's stocks of guns, powder and small arms; counties kept their trained bands' weapons in county magazines; and the arsenal at Hull mattered so much that its governor shutting the gates on Charles I in April 1642 was one of the first acts of the quarrel.","Push the analysis to the question's concept: a bow or a bill could be brought from home and mended by a village smith, but match, powder, ball and matchlocks came out of a magazine, so arming men turned war into a matter of government stores, contracts and money in a way that finding men never had.","Weigh the new material against the named factors and close: pressing men filled the ranks, and the pike kept horsemen off the shot, but it was the cost and the supply of firearms that tied English warfare to the state's purse, which is why how soldiers were armed reaches further into the period than either bullet point on its own allows."],"cues":["Which part of arming an army do the two bullet points leave untouched?","Who held the guns, the powder and the match, and where were they kept?","How is buying and storing weapons a different kind of problem from finding men?","What has the closing sentence to do with the two bullet points to make this a judgement?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What were the two kinds of foot soldier in a civil war regiment, and why did each need the other?","a":"Musketeers and pikemen. The musketeer was helpless while reloading and could not stop cavalry, so the pikemen protected him; the pikemen needed the musketeers because a pike block on its own could not hurt an enemy at a distance."},{"q":"Why could a musketeer be made useful in weeks when an archer needed years?","a":"Drawing a war bow needed strength built up by constant practice from boyhood, while loading and firing a matchlock is a fixed sequence of movements that can be drilled into anyone."},{"q":"What was free quarter, and what did a household get in return?","a":"Soldiers were billeted on households that had to feed and house them; the householder received only a ticket promising payment later, which was often never made good."},{"q":"On what date was the Battle of Naseby fought, and who commanded the two cavalry wings of the New Model Army?","a":"14 June 1645. Oliver Cromwell commanded the right wing and Henry Ireton the left."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 1 Section B, the thematic study, is worth 36 of the paper's 52 marks: Question 3 is 4 marks, Question 4 is 12 marks, and Question 5 or Question 6 is 16 marks plus up to 4 additional marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology.","Question 3, 'Explain one way in which … was similar to / different from …', carries 2 marks for AO2 and 2 for AO1, and normally spans at least a century, so both of the named periods must be evidenced.","Question 4 stimulus caps: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'; there is no cap at Level 2.","Question 5 or 6 stimulus caps: 'Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'; there is no cap at Level 2.","Timing on the 80-minute Paper 1 is roughly a minute a mark: about 5 minutes on Question 3, 15 on Question 4 and 25 on Question 5 or 6, which is the only Section B answer where spelling and specialist terms are separately marked as Threshold 1, Intermediate 2–3 and High 4."],"questions":[{"q":"Explain one way in which the composition of English armies in the years c1250–c1500 was different from the composition of English armies in the years c1500–c1700. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — an army built around longbow archers and men-at-arms against one built around interdependent musketeers and pikemen — supported by specific detail from both periods, such as Crécy or Agincourt against the pike-and-shot regiment of the civil wars.","sol":"The big difference is what the infantry did. In c1250–c1500 the foot soldier's main weapon was the longbow: English armies at Crécy in 1346 and Agincourt in 1415 were built around massed archers shooting volleys, supported by dismounted men-at-arms. By c1500–c1700 the foot had been split into two co-operating types, pike and shot: musketeers carrying matchlocks did the killing at a distance, while pikemen with ash pikes about sixteen feet long fended off cavalry and covered the musketeers while they reloaded. Missile power had become the work of gunpowder rather than of trained muscle.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what the ordinary foot soldier was actually holding in each period.","Both halves need named detail: a medieval battle where archers decided the day, and the two types of foot soldier in a civil war regiment.","State the difference, prove the medieval side, prove the early modern side, then say why the two armies are different in kind and not merely in equipment."],"codes":["12-2 — 1 The nature of warfare: Continuity and change in the composition of the army","12-2 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of developments in weaponry"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the weapons used by English foot soldiers in the years c1250–c1500 were different from the weapons used by English foot soldiers in the years c1500–c1700. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — muscle-powered missiles against gunpowder ones — supported by specific detail from both periods, such as the archery law of 1363 against the matchlock musket and its armour-piercing ball.","sol":"English foot soldiers moved from muscle-powered missiles to gunpowder ones. In c1250–c1500 the chief missile weapon was the longbow, a yew war bow drawn by a man trained from boyhood; the archery law of 1363 ordered able-bodied men to practise at the butts on Sundays, because only constant practice produced the strength to use one. In c1500–c1700 the chief missile weapon was the matchlock musket, fired by lowering a burning cord on to powder in the pan. A musket ball could punch through plate armour that an arrow would glance off, and a recruit could be taught to load and fire one in weeks.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what actually launched the missile in each period, and what that demanded of the man holding it.","The strongest difference is not range or rate of shooting but what the weapon could do to armour and how long its user took to train.","Name the medieval weapon with a law or a practice that proves how it was kept up, name the early modern weapon with how it worked, then explain why that changes who can serve as a soldier."],"codes":["12-2 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of developments in weaponry"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the recruitment of soldiers in the years c1250–c1500 was similar to the recruitment of soldiers in the years c1500–c1700. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed similarity — in both periods the Crown found soldiers by laying the burden on the counties rather than keeping men permanently under arms — supported by detail from both, such as medieval commissions of array against Elizabethan musters, the trained bands and the commissions of array of 1642.","sol":"In both periods the Crown found its soldiers by placing the burden on local communities rather than by keeping men permanently under arms. In c1250–c1500 commissions of array went out to the counties, ordering local officials to pick and equip a quota of able-bodied men, while captains contracted to bring paid retinues for a campaign. In c1500–c1700 the same machinery survived: Lords Lieutenant held musters, counties equipped the trained bands chosen from the militia after 1573, and Charles I issued commissions of array in 1642. In each period it was the county, not a standing force, that produced the men.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask who was given the job of finding and equipping the men in each period.","The similarity is about the machinery of recruitment, not about the weapons the recruits carried.","State the shared method, prove it with a medieval instrument, prove it with an early modern muster or ordinance, then say what that shows about the English state in both periods."],"codes":["12-2 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the training of soldiers in the years c1250–c1500 was different from the training of soldiers in the years c1500–c1700. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — individual skill at arms against collective drill — supported by detail from both, such as archery practice required by the law of 1363 against the trained bands of 1573, printed drill manuals and the New Model Army's regimental drill.","sol":"Training changed from individual skill to collective drill. In c1250–c1500 a soldier trained largely alone: archers practised at the village butts, as the archery law of 1363 required, and knights learned to ride and fight from boyhood and kept in practice at tournaments. In c1500–c1700 the unit mattered more than the man. The trained bands, selected from the militia from 1573, were mustered and drilled together several times a year; printed manuals set out numbered postures for handling pike and musket; and the New Model Army of 1645 drilled its regiments so that ranks could fire and reload in turn without the formation breaking.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask whether a soldier was being trained to be good on his own or good in a body of men.","Each half needs a named practice: how a medieval archer or knight kept his skill up, and how an early modern unit was taught.","Prove both halves, then explain why pike and shot made training together necessary when a longbow never had."],"codes":["12-2 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","12-2 — 1 The nature of warfare: Continuity and change in the composition of the army"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the impact of war on English civilians in the years c1250–c1500 was similar to the impact of war on English civilians in the years c1500–c1700. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed similarity — armies fed themselves at civilians' expense and paid late or not at all — supported by detail from both periods, such as medieval purveyance against free quarter and requisitioning in the civil wars.","sol":"In both periods armies fed themselves at the expense of the people they marched among. In c1250–c1500 the Crown used purveyance, seizing grain, livestock and carts for the army at prices it set itself and paying late or not at all, and parliaments complained about it repeatedly. In c1500–c1700 the same burden returned as free quarter and requisitioning: soldiers were billeted on households that had to feed them in return for a ticket promising later payment, and both armies took horses, food and fodder as they marched. In each period the civilian's loss was immediate, unavoidable and rarely made good.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask a simple question of both periods: where did an army on the march get its food?","The similarity is about how armies were supplied, so name the medieval practice and its early modern equivalent.","Prove both halves with the right term, then explain why the promise of payment mattered so little to the household in either century."],"codes":["12-2 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians"]},{"q":"Explain why a permanent standing army had developed in England by the end of the seventeenth century. [12 marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the New Model Army\n• the Militia Acts\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit explained reasons — the civil war forced Parliament to create a nationally funded full-time force, the Restoration settlement gave the Crown both guards and the militia, and the new style of drilled pike-and-shot warfare plus permanent garrisons needed troops kept together all year. Stimulus caps apply: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"England began this period with no regular army and ended it with a permanent one that Parliament regulated rather than abolished. Three linked pressures explain the change.\n\nThe first is the civil war. Armies raised by county associations kept dissolving: men refused to march beyond their own county, pay came late and desertion followed. Parliament's answer was the New Model Army, created by ordinance in February 1645. Its 22,000 men were recruited nationally in standard regiments, paid from a monthly assessment, clothed alike and governed by a written code of war. Funded by a national tax rather than local subscription, it had no reason to disband at the end of a campaign and stayed in being for fifteen years.\n\nThe second is the political settlement after 1660. Charles II paid the New Model off, but he kept regiments of guards permanently on foot from 1661, and the Militia Acts of 1661 and 1662 placed the county militia firmly under his command through the Lords Lieutenant, ending the quarrel over control of armed force that had helped to start the war in 1642. A king who had seen his father lose a war would not depend on part-time bands.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond the stimulus and is the most fundamental: warfare itself now required soldiers who trained together all year. A pike-and-shot army only worked if musketeers and pikemen drilled as one body, and siege work around earth bastions took months. Garrisons were also needed permanently in Ireland, Scotland and at Tangier, acquired in 1662. Militia mustered a few days a year could not do any of it.\n\nThe three fit together: the war proved a full-time army could be built, the Restoration gave the Crown a reason to keep one, and gunpowder warfare made the part-time alternative useless. By 1689 the argument was no longer whether the army should exist but who controlled it, which is why the Bill of Rights declared that keeping a standing army in peacetime without Parliament's consent was against law.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what a part-time force cannot do that a full-time one can.","A change of this size needs a push and a pull: something that proved a permanent army was possible, and something that gave a ruler a reason to keep one.","Deal with the wartime army first and the Restoration settlement second, then add a reason of your own about what gunpowder warfare and garrisons demanded — that is the point that escapes the stimulus cap."],"codes":["12-2 — 1 The nature of warfare: Continuity and change in the composition of the army","12-2 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants"]},{"q":"Explain why the longbow was no longer used by English armies by the end of the sixteenth century. [12 marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the matchlock musket\n• the trained bands\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit explained reasons — the musket's power against armour, the years of practice a war bow demanded against the weeks a musketeer needed, and the ease of supplying and storing firearms for mustered men. Stimulus caps apply: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The longbow did not lose a straight contest with the musket; it lost because armies had changed and it no longer suited the men who had to use it.\n\nThe first reason is what the new weapon could do. The matchlock musket fired a heavy lead ball by lowering a length of burning cord on to powder in the pan. At close range that ball would punch through plate armour that an arrow would glance off, so a musketeer could kill the armoured men who had always been the hardest target for archery. That mattered more than the bow's advantages in rate of shooting and in wet weather, because a single hit from a musket usually settled the matter.\n\nThe second reason is training. A war bow took enormous strength to draw, built up by years of practice from boyhood, which is why the archery law of 1363 had ordered men to shoot at the butts on Sundays; by the sixteenth century those habits were decaying and government complaints about neglected practice were constant. A musket, by contrast, is a fixed sequence of movements that can be drilled into an ordinary recruit in weeks. In the 1590s the Privy Council ordered the trained bands to give up bows in favour of muskets and calivers, and the bow disappeared from the county armouries.\n\nThe third reason lies beyond the stimulus, in supply. Bows needed yew staves, much of them imported, and skilled fletchers turning out arrows by the tens of thousands, and an archer exhausted himself and his arrows quickly. Muskets, powder and ball could be bought in bulk from gunsmiths, stored for years in the county magazines and issued to whoever turned up at the muster. Tactics pointed the same way: massed pike-and-shot formations copied from the continent had no place for a separate body of archers.\n\nThe reasons work together. The musket was good enough against armour, cheap in training and simple to supply, and it fitted the formations England was now copying. The bow was superb in the hands of a specialist the country no longer produced.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the government wanted from a missile weapon once it was arming ordinary men called out at a muster.","Judge the two weapons on three separate things: what they do to an armoured target, how long their user takes to train, and how easy they are to supply in bulk.","Take the musket's killing power first and the training problem second, then add a reason of your own about supply and storage — that is the point that lifts the answer past the stimulus cap."],"codes":["12-2 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of developments in weaponry","12-2 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants"]},{"q":"Explain why the New Model Army was more effective than the Parliamentarian armies that came before it. [12 marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• regular pay\n• Sir Thomas Fairfax\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit explained reasons — regular national pay removing the need to plunder and making discipline enforceable, a unified national command under Fairfax replacing regional commanders, and the army's organisation, written code of war, drill and promotion by ability. Stimulus caps apply: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Parliament's earlier armies were regional and unreliable; the New Model, created by ordinance in February 1645, was built to remove the reasons they failed.\n\nThe first reason is money. Earlier armies were funded by county associations and often went unpaid for months, so men deserted or plundered to feed themselves, and officers could not punish plunder they had forced. The New Model was paid from a national monthly assessment at 8 pence a day for a foot soldier and 2 shillings a day for a trooper, who had to keep a horse out of it. Regular pay made the written code of war credible, because a man who was fed and paid had no excuse for robbing a farm, and it cut desertion sharply.\n\nThe second reason is command. Before 1645 Parliament's forces answered to noblemen with regional commands and political followings, and their men frequently refused to serve outside their own counties. The New Model had one Lord General, Sir Thomas Fairfax, with a national command, Philip Skippon over the foot and, from days before Naseby, Oliver Cromwell as Lieutenant-General of Horse. The Self-Denying Ordinance of April 1645 required members of both Houses to give up their commands, which removed commanders who held their place by politics rather than ability.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond the stimulus: organisation and training. About 22,000 men were arranged in standard units — twelve regiments of foot at 1,200 men, eleven of horse at 600 and 1,000 dragoons — clothed alike in red, drilled together so that ranks of musketeers could fire and reload in turn behind their pikemen, and promoted for ability rather than birth. Cromwell openly preferred a plain captain who knew what he fought for to a gentleman who was nothing else, and many men carried strong religious conviction into the fight.\n\nTogether these produced the army's characteristic quality: it did not break. At Naseby on 14 June 1645 its foot rallied after Skippon was wounded and its cavalry re-formed instead of pursuing, which is precisely what earlier Parliamentarian armies could not be relied on to do.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask why earlier Parliamentarian armies kept falling apart, and answer each of those failings in turn.","Effectiveness has three roots here: how the men were paid, who commanded them, and how they were organised and trained.","Handle pay and command from the bullet points first, then add the organisation and drill of the army yourself, and finish by showing the effect on the battlefield."],"codes":["12-2 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","12-2 — 1 The nature of warfare: Continuity and change in the composition of the army"]},{"q":"Explain why English civilians suffered during the civil wars of 1642–1651. [12 marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• free quarter\n• sieges\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit explained reasons — armies living off the country through free quarter and requisitioning, the destruction caused by sieges and storms, and the new burdens of wartime taxation, sequestration and camp disease. Stimulus caps apply: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Civilians suffered because for the first time in centuries a large war was fought across England itself, and it reached them in three different ways.\n\nThe first is what the armies took. Neither side had a supply system able to feed tens of thousands on the march, so both lived off the country. Free quarter meant soldiers were billeted on households that had to house and feed them, receiving in return only a ticket promising payment that often never came. Requisitioning took horses, carts, grain and fodder outright, and a village could be stripped twice in a month.\n\nThe second is what the fighting destroyed. Most of the war was sieges and storms rather than pitched battles, and towns bore the worst of it. Leicester was stormed and plundered on 31 May 1645; Colchester was besieged from June 1648 and starved into surrender that August; defenders routinely burned suburbs to clear a field of fire and dug earthworks across farmland, as at Oxford and Newark. Trade stopped, buildings burned and townspeople were killed or ruined whichever side won.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond the stimulus: the state itself. To pay for the war Parliament imposed a weekly assessment on property from February 1643 (monthly from 1645) and, in the same year, an excise on beer and, from 1644, salt and meat — a tax that reached households no army came near and was bitterly resented. Royalists had their estates sequestered and had to compound to get them back. Disease compounded it, since armies carried typhus and plague into the towns they quartered in: historians estimate around 85,000 died in the fighting in England and Wales and perhaps 100,000 more of war-related sickness, out of a population of roughly five million.\n\nThe pressure provoked resistance: in 1645 country people in Dorset, Wiltshire and Somerset formed Clubmen associations to keep both armies off their crops, and Cromwell broke up a large gathering of them at Hambledon Hill that August. Civilians suffered, then, because the armies fed on them, the fighting wrecked their towns, and the state taxed them for both.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask how an army of thousands actually got fed when it was marching through England.","Sort the suffering into kinds before you write: what was taken, what was destroyed, and what was demanded by government.","Use the two bullet points for the first two kinds, then add wartime taxation and disease as your own material, and close with how civilians reacted."],"codes":["12-2 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians"]},{"q":"Explain why the Royalists were defeated at the Battle of Naseby in 1645. [12 marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• Prince Rupert\n• Oliver Cromwell\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit explained reasons — Rupert's cavalry riding off the field in pursuit, Cromwell's control of his own horse and the attack on the royalist rear, and the numerical and positional advantages Fairfax enjoyed. Stimulus caps apply: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The royalists lost at Naseby because their cavalry threw away its victory, their opponents' cavalry did not, and they fought at odds they should have avoided.\n\nThe first reason is Prince Rupert. On the royalist right he charged and broke Henry Ireton's wing, exactly as he had done on other fields, but his troopers then rode on to the Parliamentarian baggage train at Naseby village and wasted time plundering it. For the decisive part of the battle roughly a third of the king's army was not on the field, and by the time Rupert returned the royalist foot was surrounded.\n\nThe second reason is Oliver Cromwell, appointed Lieutenant-General of Horse days before the battle. On the Parliamentarian right he broke Sir Marmaduke Langdale's Northern Horse quickly, and then did what almost no cavalry commander of the age managed: he sent only part of his force after the beaten enemy and kept the rest in hand, wheeling them across the field into the flank and rear of the royalist infantry, who were at that moment pushing Philip Skippon's foot back. Caught front and rear, the royalist infantry surrendered in thousands.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond the stimulus: numbers and ground. Sir Thomas Fairfax had about 14,000 men to the king's 9,000, because George Goring's cavalry was away besieging Taunton and troops had been left to garrison Leicester after it was stormed on 31 May. Fairfax also chose his position well, drawing up behind the crest of a ridge so that the royalists could not judge his strength, and posting Colonel John Okey's dragoons along the Sulby hedges to fire into Rupert's flank as he advanced.\n\nThese reasons reinforce one another. The king's smaller army could only win if its cavalry was used sparingly, and instead the arm in which the royalists had always excelled took itself out of the battle while the New Model's horse stayed under command. The result was not a defeat but the destruction of the king's field army: about 1,000 killed, some 4,500 taken prisoner, and all the artillery and the king's private correspondence lost.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what each side's cavalry did after it had won its part of the field.","Compare the two cavalry commanders on one measure — control — and then look for an advantage that existed before a shot was fired.","Take Rupert first and Cromwell second, then add your own material on the numbers and the ground Fairfax chose, which is what lifts the answer past the stimulus cap."],"codes":["12-2 — 3 Case study: The Battle of Naseby (1645)","12-2 — 1 The nature of warfare: Continuity and change in the composition of the army"]},{"q":"'The development of a standing army was the most important change in the nature of warfare in England in the years c1500–c1700.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the trained bands\n• the New Model Army\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6, plus up to 4 SPaG. Credit a weighed judgement: the standing army against changes in weaponry, drill and tactics and against siege warfare, with a stated criterion for 'most important'. Stimulus caps apply: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Whether the standing army was the most important change depends on what counts as a change in the nature of warfare. I shall judge by how deeply a development altered the way battles were actually fought, and how long its effects lasted.\n\nThe case for the standing army is strong at the end of the period. For most of the sixteenth century England had no permanent force at all. The Crown mustered the county militia, and from 1573 a selected part of it, the trained bands, was equipped and drilled for home defence, while men for war abroad were pressed and discharged when the campaign ended. The New Model Army, created by ordinance in February 1645, broke that pattern: its 22,000 men were recruited nationally, paid from a monthly assessment at 8 pence a day for the foot and 2 shillings for a trooper, clothed alike and kept in being between campaigns. After 1660 Charles II paid the New Model off but kept regiments of guards permanently on foot from 1661, and the Militia Acts of 1661 and 1662 gave him the county militia as well. By 1689 Parliament no longer tried to abolish the army but to control it, declaring in the Bill of Rights that keeping a standing army in peacetime without its consent was against law. A permanent professional army was therefore the change that outlived the period.\n\nAgainst that, the standing army arrived late and altered the battlefield least. Weapons and formations had already been transformed by gunpowder. The longbow, the weapon English armies had been built around since the fourteenth century, was gone from the trained bands by the end of the sixteenth, ordered out in favour of muskets and calivers in the 1590s. The matchlock that replaced it could pierce plate at close range but left its user helpless while he reloaded, so every foot regiment had to combine musketeers with pikemen carrying pikes some sixteen feet long, in the New Model at a ratio of roughly two to one. That forced continual drill, printed manuals of numbered postures, and officers who could handle a body of men rather than fight as individuals. Cavalry changed with it, abandoning heavy armour for the buff coat, pistol and sword, and the close-order charge Cromwell used at Marston Moor in 1644 and at Naseby in 1645.\n\nBeyond both stimulus points lies siege warfare, where most of the fighting and suffering actually happened. Artillery had made high medieval walls useless, so towns such as Oxford and Newark were ringed instead with low angled earth bastions, and campaigns became blockades: Colchester was starved into surrender in August 1648. War therefore became slower, hugely more expensive and far more damaging to civilians, who bore free quarter, requisitioning and the new wartime taxes.\n\nI agree only in part. The standing army was the most lasting change, since it created an institution England has had ever since and a constitutional settlement in 1689 that endures. But it was a consequence rather than a cause. Gunpowder made war technical, drill made it professional, and only a permanent paid force could sustain either. Judged by what changed the nature of fighting itself, weaponry and the drill it demanded matter more; judged by what changed England, the standing army does.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first what 'most important' will mean here — deepest effect on how battles were fought, or longest-lasting effect on the country.","Two candidates compete with the named factor: the weapons themselves and the kind of war they produced, especially sieges.","Use the trained bands and the New Model to build the case for the statement, then add weaponry and siege warfare of your own, and let the conclusion give a different answer depending on which criterion you apply."],"codes":["12-2 — 1 The nature of warfare: Continuity and change in the composition of the army","12-2 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of developments in weaponry","12-2 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants"]},{"q":"'Oliver Cromwell's leadership was the main reason for the Parliamentarian victory at the Battle of Naseby (1645).'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the New Model Army\n• Prince Rupert\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6, plus up to 4 SPaG. Credit a weighed judgement on Cromwell's handling of the cavalry against Rupert's error, the New Model's discipline and Fairfax's command, and the Parliamentarian advantage in numbers and ground. Stimulus caps apply: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"A reason is the main one if the outcome would probably have been different without it, so every factor has to be tested against that.\n\nCromwell's part was real and it was decisive at the moment that mattered. Appointed Lieutenant-General of Horse only days before the battle, he commanded the Parliamentarian right against Sir Marmaduke Langdale's Northern Horse and broke them quickly. What made the difference was what he did next. Instead of riding after a beaten enemy, as cavalry commanders of the age almost always did, he sent only part of his force in pursuit and wheeled the rest across the field into the flank and rear of the royalist infantry, who were at that point pushing Philip Skippon's foot back. Royalist foot caught between infantry in front and cavalry behind had no answer, and the king's main field army ceased to exist: about 1,000 were killed and some 4,500 taken prisoner, with all the artillery and the king's private correspondence, which Parliament promptly printed.\n\nYet the same day showed that leadership alone does not explain the result, because Prince Rupert made the mirror-image error. On the royalist right he routed Henry Ireton's wing, then let his troopers ride on to the baggage train at Naseby village, where they were held up trying to plunder it. For the crucial part of the battle the royalist army fought without roughly a third of its strength. Cromwell's opportunity to turn on the royalist foot existed because Rupert had removed himself from the field; a commander can only exploit what his opponent gives him.\n\nThe army Cromwell led was also not his creation. The New Model, established by ordinance in February 1645, was recruited nationally instead of from a single county association, paid regularly from a monthly assessment, drilled as regiments and governed by a written code of war. Sir Thomas Fairfax was Lord General and chose the ground, drawing the army up behind the crest of a ridge so that the king's officers could not judge its size, while Colonel John Okey's dragoons were posted along the Sulby hedges to fire into Rupert's flank as he advanced. Men who are paid, drilled and well placed stand when they are pushed, and the New Model foot rallied after Skippon was wounded. That steadiness was the work of the institution rather than of one officer.\n\nBeyond the stimulus lies the plainest factor of all: numbers. Fairfax had roughly 14,000 men, the king about 9,000. George Goring's cavalry was away besieging Taunton, and troops had been left behind to hold Leicester after it was stormed on 31 May. Charles I chose to fight at odds of about three to two against a fresh, better-supplied army, and in a battle of pike and shot weight of numbers told heavily once the wings had been settled.\n\nI therefore agree only in part. Cromwell's control of his cavalry was the decisive act on the field and the clearest single explanation of why the royalist infantry were destroyed rather than merely beaten, and no other commander that day showed it. But it was the immediate cause. The underlying reasons were the New Model's discipline and superior numbers and the king's decision to fight without Goring — conditions Cromwell used brilliantly but did not create.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Set a test for 'main reason' before you write a word, and apply it at the end of every paragraph.","Weigh what Cromwell did against three rivals: what his opposite number did, what kind of army he was given, and what the odds were before the fighting started.","Use Rupert and the New Model from the bullet points, add the numbers and the ground yourself, and let the conclusion separate the immediate cause from the underlying ones."],"codes":["12-2 — 3 Case study: The Battle of Naseby (1645)","12-2 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants"]},{"q":"'The New Model Army was the most important development in the recruitment and training of soldiers in England in the years c1500–c1700.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the trained bands\n• impressment\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6, plus up to 4 SPaG. Credit a weighed judgement on the New Model against the trained bands of 1573, the survival of impressment and the county muster, and the Restoration settlement of 1661–62 that made a paid permanent force normal. Stimulus caps apply: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Importance here must be measured by how far a development changed the way soldiers were found and made fit to fight, and by how much of it survived. On that test the New Model Army is the most complete example of change but not the only one that mattered.\n\nThe case for the statement is strong. Before 1645 Parliament's forces were regional, unreliable and often unpaid, and men refused to serve outside their own counties. The New Model, created by ordinance in February 1645, was recruited nationally into standard units — twelve regiments of foot at 1,200 men, eleven of horse at 600 and 1,000 dragoons, about 22,000 in all — paid from a monthly assessment at 8 pence a day for the foot and 2 shillings for a trooper, clothed alike in red, and held to a written code of war that punished plunder and desertion. Its regiments drilled together so that ranks of musketeers could fire and reload behind their pikemen, and promotion went by ability, Cromwell preferring a plain captain who knew what he fought for to a gentleman who was nothing else. The result was visible at Naseby on 14 June 1645, where the foot rallied after Skippon was wounded.\n\nAgainst that, the principle of selecting and drilling men was older. From 1573 a chosen part of the county militia, the trained bands, was equipped and mustered several times a year for home defence, and the London trained bands were steady enough to hold the line at the first Battle of Newbury in September 1643. Printed drill manuals of numbered pike and musket postures were circulating in England well before 1645. The New Model perfected a method rather than inventing one.\n\nNor did the New Model replace the older ways of finding men. Impressment continued throughout: Elizabeth I's government pressed thousands of men, often the poor and unemployed, for service in the Netherlands from 1585 and in Ireland in the 1590s, and the New Model itself filled gaps with pressed men, many of whom deserted. Lords Lieutenant went on holding musters, and the quarrel over who could call the militia out — Parliament's Militia Ordinance against the king's commissions of array in 1642 — shows how central the county machinery still was.\n\nBeyond the stimulus lies what actually lasted. The New Model was disbanded in 1660–61. What survived was the pattern it demonstrated: a paid, permanent, professional force. Charles II kept regiments of guards on foot from 1661, the Militia Acts of 1661 and 1662 put the county militia under royal command, and by the Bill of Rights of 1689 Parliament was regulating a permanent army rather than trying to do without one. Recruitment into these regiments was by voluntary enlistment for pay, not by array.\n\nI agree substantially, with a qualification. The New Model was the most important single development because it proved that an English army could be nationally recruited, regularly paid, drilled and disciplined, and everything permanent that followed was built on that demonstration. But it was the culmination of a process begun with the trained bands, and it removed neither impressment nor the county muster, which is why the statement holds for the model of soldiering that emerged rather than for the practice of recruitment in the period as a whole.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what 'important' should mean for recruitment and training: the scale of the change, or how much of it was still there fifty years later.","Test the New Model against what came before it and what replaced it — both of which weaken the claim that it stands alone.","Use the trained bands and impressment from the bullet points, then add the Restoration settlement of your own, and let the conclusion say precisely which part of the claim survives."],"codes":["12-2 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","12-2 — 1 The nature of warfare: Continuity and change in the composition of the army"]},{"q":"'The civil wars of 1642–1651 were the main reason why English civilians experienced hardship because of war in the years c1500–c1700.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• free quarter\n• taxation\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6, plus up to 4 SPaG. Credit a weighed judgement on the wars' unique combination of plunder, siege, tax and disease on English soil against the earlier burdens of impressment, musters and billeting and the later costs of the standing army. Stimulus caps apply: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The question is whether the civil wars caused hardship different in kind from what war had always cost English civilians, or merely more of the same. I shall judge by scale, by how many burdens fell at once, and by how long the effects lasted.\n\nThe case for the statement is powerful, because the wars were fought at home. Both sides lived off the country. Free quarter meant soldiers were billeted on households that had to house and feed them and received only a ticket promising later payment, and requisitioning took horses, carts, grain and fodder outright. The fighting destroyed as well as consumed: Leicester was stormed and plundered on 31 May 1645, Colchester was besieged from June 1648 and starved into surrender that August, and defenders burned suburbs and dug earthworks across farmland at Oxford and Newark. Disease followed the armies, and historians estimate that around 85,000 died in the fighting in England and Wales and perhaps 100,000 more of war-related sickness, out of a population of roughly five million — a heavier proportional loss than Britain suffered in the First World War.\n\nTaxation made the burden universal. Parliament imposed a weekly assessment on property from February 1643 (monthly from 1645) and, in the same year, an excise on beer and, from 1644, salt and meat, so households that never saw a soldier still paid for the war, while royalists had estates sequestered and had to compound to recover them. The response measures the pressure: in 1645 country people in Dorset, Wiltshire and Somerset formed Clubmen associations to keep both armies off their crops, dispersed by Cromwell at Hambledon Hill that August.\n\nAgainst the statement, war had cost civilians heavily before 1642. Elizabeth I's government pressed thousands of men, largely the poor, for the Netherlands from 1585 and for Ireland in the 1590s, and many never returned; counties paid coat-and-conduct money to equip and march them, and musters cost time and money every year. In the 1620s wars with Spain and France the Crown billeted soldiers in private houses, imposed forced loans and used martial law, grievances so serious that the Petition of Right of 1628 was drawn up against them. Civilians were therefore already familiar with pressing, billeting and paying.\n\nBeyond the stimulus, the burden did not stop in 1651 either. After the Restoration the county militia was re-established under the Militia Acts of 1661 and 1662 and paid for by rates on property, and the guards regiments kept on foot from 1661 had to be quartered, which made billeting a lasting grievance. Crucially, although Parliament took the duties off meat and home-made salt in June 1647, the excise created in 1643 survived as an institution: at the Restoration it was kept on, and in 1660 a hereditary excise on drink was granted to the Crown in place of the old feudal dues, so a wartime tax became a lasting charge on ordinary consumption.\n\nI agree, but with an important qualification. The civil wars were the main reason, because they were the only occasion in these two centuries when plunder, siege, requisition, taxation and epidemic disease fell on English civilians simultaneously and on English soil, and the death toll has no parallel in the period. But they intensified burdens the Crown had already imposed through pressing, billeting and war taxation, and some of the heaviest of them — the excise above all — outlived the fighting, which suggests the wars were the sharpest phase of a long story rather than a break in it.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide what would make one war the main reason: the scale of the loss, the number of different burdens at once, or how long the effects lasted.","Test the wars against the periods either side of them — Elizabethan and early Stuart war abroad, and the settlement after 1660.","Build the case with free quarter, sieges and the wartime taxes, then add earlier billeting and pressing and the survival of the excise as your own material before judging."],"codes":["12-2 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians","12-2 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date was the Battle of Naseby fought?","a":"14 June 1645."},{"q":"Who was Lord General, that is commander-in-chief, of the New Model Army at Naseby?","a":"Sir Thomas Fairfax."},{"q":"In which year was a selected part of the county militia first set aside for regular training as the trained bands?","a":"1573."},{"q":"What was free quarter?","a":"The billeting of soldiers on households, which had to feed and house them in return for a ticket promising later payment."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Pikeman","back":"A foot soldier carrying an ash pike about sixteen feet long, in helmet and back-and-breast. His job was to keep cavalry off the musketeers while they reloaded, and to fight other pike blocks at push of pike.","code":"12-2 — 1 The nature of warfare: Continuity and change in the composition of the army","open":false},{"front":"Musketeer","back":"A foot soldier with a matchlock musket, the main killing weapon of the period. Because reloading was slow he stood several ranks deep and fired in turn, and he was defenceless against horse without pikemen.","code":"12-2 — 1 The nature of warfare: Continuity and change in the composition of the army","open":false},{"front":"Dragoon","back":"Mounted infantry. Dragoons rode cheap horses to the fighting and dismounted to shoot with musket or carbine. At Naseby, Colonel John Okey's dragoons lined the Sulby hedges and fired into Prince Rupert's flank.","code":"12-2 — 1 The nature of warfare: Continuity and change in the composition of the army","open":false},{"front":"Standing army","back":"A permanent, paid, full-time force kept up in peacetime. The New Model Army of 1645 was England's first; Charles II kept guards regiments from 1661, and the Bill of Rights of 1689 required Parliament's consent for one.","code":"12-2 — 1 The nature of warfare: Continuity and change in the composition of the army","open":false},{"front":"Matchlock musket","back":"Fired by lowering a length of burning cord on to powder in the pan. Slow to reload and useless in heavy rain, but its ball pierced plate armour and a recruit could be trained in weeks.","code":"12-2 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of developments in weaponry","open":false},{"front":"Wheel-lock pistol","back":"A pistol ignited by sparks struck from a spinning steel wheel, so it could be carried ready loaded and fired one-handed. It armed cavalry and produced the caracole, in which horsemen rode up, fired and wheeled away.","code":"12-2 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of developments in weaponry","open":false},{"front":"Flintlock","back":"A firing mechanism that struck a flint against steel to make the spark, removing the burning cord of the matchlock. It spread later in the seventeenth century and eventually replaced the matchlock in European armies.","code":"12-2 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of developments in weaponry","open":false},{"front":"Give one reason why the longbow was replaced by the musket","back":"A musket ball pierced plate armour that arrows glanced off; a musketeer could be trained in weeks while an archer needed years of practice from boyhood; muskets, powder and ball could be bought in bulk and stored in county magazines.","code":"12-2 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of developments in weaponry","open":true},{"front":"The trained bands","back":"From 1573, the part of the county militia selected to be equipped and drilled several times a year for home defence. The London trained bands helped hold the Parliamentarian line at the first Battle of Newbury in September 1643.","code":"12-2 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","open":false},{"front":"Impressment","back":"Forcing men into military service, usually the poor, vagrants and the unemployed, for wars abroad. Elizabeth I's government pressed thousands for the Netherlands from 1585 and for Ireland in the 1590s.","code":"12-2 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","open":false},{"front":"The New Model Army","back":"Created by ordinance in February 1645: about 22,000 men, recruited nationally, paid from a monthly assessment, clothed in red, drilled as regiments, disciplined by a written code of war and promoted by ability.","code":"12-2 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","open":false},{"front":"Free quarter","back":"The billeting of soldiers on households that had to feed and house them, receiving only a ticket promising later payment. With requisitioning of horses, carts, grain and fodder, it was how armies fed themselves.","code":"12-2 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians","open":false},{"front":"The excise","back":"A tax introduced by Parliament in 1643 on beer and, from 1644, salt and meat, to pay for the war. The duties on meat and home-made salt came off in 1647, but the excise itself survived after 1660.","code":"12-2 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians","open":false},{"front":"The Battle of Naseby","back":"Fought in Northamptonshire on 14 June 1645. Fairfax's New Model Army of about 14,000 destroyed the king's field army of about 9,000: roughly 1,000 royalists killed, some 4,500 captured, with all the artillery and the king's letters.","code":"12-2 — 3 Case study: The Battle of Naseby (1645)","open":false},{"front":"Name one way in which Oliver Cromwell's leadership affected the outcome at Naseby","back":"He broke Langdale's Northern Horse on the Parliamentarian right; he held most of his troopers in hand instead of pursuing; he wheeled them into the flank and rear of the royalist foot, trapping them against Fairfax's infantry.","code":"12-2 — 3 Case study: The Battle of Naseby (1645)","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 1 Question 5, 16 marks: 'Taxation was the heaviest burden that war placed on English people in the years c1500–c1700.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the excise • plunder. You must also use information of your own.)","items":["The strongest ground for agreeing is that taxation reached people no army ever went near, and went on reaching them after the shooting stopped. Parliament's assessment, levied weekly from February 1643 and monthly from 1645, set each county a fixed sum month after month at rates far above anything the country had paid before the war, and the excise, new in 1643 on beer and ale and extended in 1644 to salt and meat, taxed what every household bought. Royalists had their estates sequestered on top of it. That people felt this as the heavier burden is shown by what they did about it: the excise office at Smithfield was burned down in a riot in February 1647, and in June that year Parliament took the duties off meat and home-made salt while keeping the rest. Against this must be set what soldiers themselves did, which was sharper and sometimes fatal. Under free quarter a household fed and housed men in return for a ticket promising payment that often never came; horses, carts, grain and fodder were requisitioned outright; Leicester was stormed and plundered on 31 May 1645 and Colchester starved into surrender in August 1648; and disease travelled with the armies, with historians' estimates putting deaths from war-related sickness in England and Wales above the number killed in the fighting. That is true, and where an army came it was far worse than any tax. The difference is that plunder and quarter were local and passed, while the assessment and the excise fell on the whole country and outlasted the war. On the criterion that the heaviest burden is the one that fell on most people for longest, taxation carries it; on the criterion of what was worst where it struck, the armies do, and the answer has to say which test it is using.","Why this reaches Level 4: the explanation is directed consistently at the conceptual focus, since every sentence weighs one burden against another rather than listing hardships; the information is precisely selected — the weekly assessment of February 1643 and the monthly assessment from 1645, the excise of 1643 extended in 1644, the Smithfield riot of February 1647 and the relief given that June, Leicester on 31 May 1645, Colchester in August 1648 — rather than general; the counter-view about free quarter, requisitioning, plunder and disease is put at its strongest and then answered instead of being left hanging; and the criterion for 'heaviest' is named and applied in the closing sentence, which is what the third bullet of the Level 4 descriptor rewards. It also shows what going beyond the stimulus looks like: the excise and plunder are the two points printed with the question, while the weekly assessment of February 1643 and the monthly assessment from 1645, the Smithfield riot, the relief of June 1647 and the war-related disease are the student's own material — and without material of that kind the answer takes a maximum of 11 marks and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question and the same stimulus points, • the excise and • plunder, visibly weaker)","items":["Taxes were the worst thing about war for ordinary English people. Parliament made everyone pay money for the army and there was a new tax on things like beer and salt, which was very unfair because poor people had to pay it as well as rich people. People hated the excise and there was even a riot about it in London. Soldiers were also a problem because they took food and horses from ordinary people and did not always pay, and towns got attacked and burned, which must have been terrible for the people living there. Lots of people also died of diseases the soldiers brought with them. So taxation was the heaviest burden because everybody had to pay it, although the soldiers were bad too.","Why this stays at Level 2: it meets 'an explanation is given showing limited analysis and with implicit or unsustained links to the conceptual focus of the question' and 'accurate and relevant information is included, showing some knowledge and understanding of the period', while the overall judgement is given but asserted. It misses Levels 3 and 4 because nothing is specified — no date, no rate, no named tax beyond the excise the stimulus already supplies, no named town, no figures; because the soldiers' side is added at the end instead of being weighed against the taxes; and because no criterion for 'heaviest' is stated, so the judgement is claimed rather than tested."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Case study: the Battle of Naseby, 14 June 1645","items":["Date and place: 14 June 1645, near the village of Naseby in Northamptonshire.","Parliamentarian commander-in-chief: Sir Thomas Fairfax, Lord General of the New Model Army.","Parliamentarian cavalry: Oliver Cromwell, Lieutenant-General of Horse, on the right wing; Henry Ireton on the left.","Parliamentarian infantry: Philip Skippon, who was badly wounded early in the battle but stayed on the field.","Royalist command: Charles I was present in person; Prince Rupert commanded and led the royalist right wing.","Royalist left wing: Sir Marmaduke Langdale with the Northern Horse.","Numbers: roughly 14,000 Parliamentarians against about 9,000 royalists.","Why the royalists were outnumbered: George Goring's cavalry was away besieging Taunton, and men had been left to garrison Leicester, which the royalists had stormed on 31 May 1645.","The ground: Fairfax drew his army up behind the crest of a ridge so that the royalists could not judge its strength.","Colonel John Okey's dragoons were posted along the Sulby hedges and fired into the flank of Rupert's cavalry as it advanced.","Rupert broke Ireton's wing but his troopers then rode on to the Parliamentarian baggage train at Naseby village and were out of the battle at the decisive moment.","Cromwell broke Langdale's Northern Horse, sent only part of his men in pursuit, and wheeled the rest into the flank and rear of the royalist foot.","Royalist losses: about 1,000 killed and some 4,500 taken prisoner, with all the artillery and the baggage.","The king's private correspondence was captured with the baggage and published by Parliament, which used it to show that he had sought foreign and Irish help.","Camp followers with the royalist baggage were killed or mutilated by Parliamentarian soldiers after the battle, on the pretext that they were Irish.","Significance: the king's main field army was destroyed and never rebuilt; Bristol surrendered to Fairfax in September 1645 and Charles I gave himself up to the Scots in May 1646."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — The New Model Army, 1645","items":["Created by an ordinance of the Long Parliament in February 1645.","Strength: about 22,000 men.","Composition: twelve regiments of foot at 1,200 men each, eleven regiments of horse at 600 each, and 1,000 dragoons.","Commander-in-chief: Sir Thomas Fairfax, as Lord General.","Infantry commander: Philip Skippon, a veteran of the wars in the Netherlands.","Cavalry commander: Oliver Cromwell, appointed Lieutenant-General of Horse in June 1645, days before Naseby.","The Self-Denying Ordinance of April 1645 required members of both Houses of Parliament to resign their commands, removing officers who held their positions through politics.","Pay: 8 pence a day for a foot soldier and 2 shillings a day for a cavalry trooper, who had to keep his horse out of it.","Funding: a monthly assessment levied nationally, rather than the local subscriptions that had paid for county association armies.","Recruitment: national rather than regional, so regiments could be ordered anywhere in the country; gaps were still filled by pressed men.","Uniform: coats of red, the first English army to be clothed alike on this scale.","Discipline: a printed code of laws and ordinances of war punishing plunder, desertion, drunkenness and swearing.","Promotion: by ability rather than birth, a principle Cromwell defended openly.","Infantry organisation: roughly two musketeers to each pikeman, drilled so that ranks could fire and reload in turn behind the pikes.","Fate: disbanded in 1660–61 at the Restoration, though Charles II kept regiments of guards permanently on foot from 1661."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — The nature of warfare, c1500–c1700: composition and weapons","items":["At Flodden on 9 September 1513 an English army under the Earl of Surrey, still built around billmen and archers, destroyed a Scottish army fighting with the continental pike; James IV of Scotland was killed.","The longbow needed years of practice from boyhood; the archery law of 1363 had required able-bodied men to shoot at the butts on Sundays.","In the 1590s the Privy Council ordered the trained bands to give up bows in favour of muskets and calivers.","The matchlock musket fired a lead ball ignited by a length of burning cord lowered on to powder in the pan; early muskets were fired from a forked rest.","The caliver was a lighter matchlock that needed no rest.","A musket ball could pierce plate armour at close range; an arrow was likely to glance off it.","The pike was an ash shaft about sixteen feet long, carried by men in helmet and back-and-breast, used to keep cavalry off the musketeers and to fight other pike blocks.","The ratio of shot to pike moved steadily towards the musket; in the New Model Army a foot regiment was intended to be roughly two musketeers to each pikeman.","Cuirassiers, cavalry in near-complete armour such as Sir Arthur Haselrig's regiment, were already unusual by the civil wars.","The standard trooper was a harquebusier, in buff coat, back-and-breast and pot helmet, armed with sword and pistols.","The wheel-lock pistol could be carried ready loaded and fired from the saddle, producing the caracole in which horsemen fired and wheeled away; Cromwell's troopers instead charged home in close order with the sword.","Dragoons were mounted infantry who rode to the fighting and dismounted to shoot.","Flintlocks, which struck a flint against steel, began to replace matchlocks later in the seventeenth century.","Artillery had made high medieval walls useless, so towns such as Oxford and Newark were defended by low angled earth bastions instead.","The standing army: the New Model was kept in being between campaigns; Charles II maintained guards regiments from 1661; the Bill of Rights of 1689 declared that keeping a standing army in peacetime without Parliament's consent was against law, and the Mutiny Act of the same year gave Parliament annual control of military discipline."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — The experience of war: recruitment, training and civilians, c1500–c1700","items":["The county militia was mustered by Lords Lieutenant; from 1573 a selected part of it, the trained bands, was equipped and drilled several times a year for home defence.","The trained bands were for home defence, so men for service abroad were pressed, usually from the poor, vagrants and the unemployed.","Elizabeth I's government pressed thousands of men for service in the Netherlands from 1585 and in Ireland during the 1590s.","The London trained bands helped hold the Parliamentarian line at the first Battle of Newbury in September 1643.","In 1642 Parliament issued the Militia Ordinance and the king commissions of array, each claiming the right to call out the county forces.","Training meant collective drill: printed manuals set out numbered postures for handling pike and musket so that ranks could fire and reload in turn.","Free quarter: soldiers were billeted on households that had to feed and house them, receiving only a ticket promising later payment.","Requisitioning took horses, carts, grain and fodder outright, since neither side had a supply system that could feed an army on the march.","Parliament imposed a weekly assessment on property from February 1643 (monthly from 1645) and an excise in the same year on beer and, from 1644, salt and meat; royalists had estates sequestered and had to compound to recover them.","Leicester was stormed and plundered by the royalists on 31 May 1645.","Colchester was besieged from June 1648 and starved into surrender that August.","Defenders regularly burned suburbs to clear a field of fire and dug earthworks across farmland around garrison towns.","Historians estimate that around 85,000 people died in the fighting in England and Wales, with perhaps 100,000 more dying of war-related disease, out of a population of roughly five million.","In 1645 country people in Dorset, Wiltshire and Somerset formed Clubmen associations to keep both armies off their crops; Cromwell dispersed a large body of them at Hambledon Hill in Dorset that August.","After the Restoration the Militia Acts of 1661 and 1662 put the county militia under the king's command through his Lords Lieutenant, and the guards regiments kept on foot from 1661 had to be quartered, so billeting remained a grievance."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 1 Question 5 (16 marks + 4 SPaG): 'The need to pay and supply soldiers was the main reason why English armies changed in the years c1500–c1700.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the monthly assessment • arrears of pay. You must also use information of your own.","items":["Decoded question: the concept is causation, judged for relative importance. 'Main reason' is comparative, so money and supply have to be measured against the other causes of change — new weapons, the way men were found, and the wars themselves. An answer that only describes how the New Model Army was paid cannot reach Level 4 however detailed it is.","Criterion to set in the opening: a reason counts as the main one if the change would probably not have happened without it, and if it explains changes in more than one part of the army — who served, how they were trained, how they behaved and whether they were kept together. Say at once that the essay will test each factor against both halves of that.","Paragraph 1 (agree — money made an army permanent and national): the New Model Army was created by ordinance in February 1645 with about 22,000 men, paid from a monthly assessment laid on the counties at 8 pence a day for the foot and 2 shillings for a trooper, clothed alike in red and governed by printed laws and ordinances of war. Interim judgement: regular pay is what made it possible to keep men together between campaigns and to punish them for plunder.","Paragraph 2 (agree — what happened when the money stopped): free quarter and arrears. Unpaid soldiers lived on households that fed and housed them for a ticket promising payment later, and when Parliament tried in 1647 to disband the army without settling its arrears the soldiers refused to go. Interim judgement: supply, not strategy, decided how armies behaved and whether they obeyed.","Paragraph 3 (disagree — the first point beyond the stimulus, on weapons): the matchlock and the pike replaced the bow and the bill, and because a musketeer could neither defend himself while loading nor stop a horseman, the foot had to be built in fixed proportions and drilled all the time; in the 1590s the Privy Council ordered the trained bands to give up bows for muskets and calivers. Interim judgement: this changed what every soldier did, and no amount of money would have produced it on its own.","Paragraph 4 (disagree — the second point beyond the stimulus, on politics and the wars): England changed its armies because of what the wars did to it. The quarrel of 1642 was partly over who could call out the militia; after 1660 Charles II kept guards regiments on foot, the Militia Acts of 1661 and 1662 gave him the militia, and the Bill of Rights of 1689 regulated a standing army instead of abolishing it. Interim judgement: what survived was settled by politics, not by accountancy.","Evaluation thread running through all four paragraphs: keep separating what money made possible from what money caused. Pay and supply are the condition of a permanent, drilled, obedient army; weapons and politics are the reasons such an army was wanted. Test every paragraph against that distinction.","The link to use in the argument: pay and weapons are joined, because a pike-and-shot army only works if musketeers and pikemen drill together all year, and only a paid army can be kept together all year. Saying this stops the essay becoming two lists.","Evidence to keep in reserve for precision: 1573 for the trained bands, February 1645 for the New Model ordinance, 8 pence and 2 shillings a day, 1647 for the quarrel over arrears, the 1590s order replacing bows with muskets, 1661 for the guards and the first Militia Act, 1689 for the Bill of Rights.","Timing on the 80-minute paper: about 25 minutes, of which 3 or 4 are for planning, leaving roughly 5 minutes a paragraph and 3 for the conclusion; the caps must be respected, since answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are held to a maximum of 11 marks and given no access to Level 4; specialist terms — musketeer, pikeman, assessment, free quarter, magazine, militia — must be spelled correctly because 4 further marks are given for spelling, punctuation, grammar and terminology.","What the conclusion must say: give the judgement and justify it on the stated criterion. Agree in part — nothing else explains why England could at last keep an army together, and every attempt to run an army without money ended in free quarter and mutiny; but pay answered a demand that the weapons had created and that the politics of the 1640s and after decided how to meet. Say which of those tests you are using and why, rather than leaving both open."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:07:43Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.95,"notes":"All five recorded fixes are in the kit and all of them hold: worked example 0 is now on weaponry and the cavalry and worked example 1 on the pikeman, both squarely inside the specification entry ('the role of the musketeer, pikemen, dragoons and the cavalry' and 'new muskets and pistols'), the dedupe scan prints 'no duplication', the bank's fourteen questions re-solve to their keys with nothing ambiguous, and every new fact checks out on the record — the New Model Army ordinance of 17 February 1645 with its eleven horse regiments of 600 armed with sword and a pair of pistols, its 1,000 dragoons and its foot regiments of 1,200 at two-thirds musketeers to one-third pikemen (so about 400 pikes), the wheellock pistol and the reiters' caracole by the middle of the sixteenth century, plug bayonets in English service through the 1670s and 1680s (the Board of Ordnance ordering 10,000 in 1678) and the socket bayonet that let the English give the pike up after the Peace of Ryswick of 1697, card 12 and question 13 now saying correctly that the duties on meat and home-made salt came off in 1647¹ while the excise survived, kept on at the Restoration when the Tenures Abolition Act of 1660 granted a hereditary excise on drink in lieu of the feudal dues², and the model-paragraph pair printing its two stimulus points with the Level 4 commentary naming which material is the student's own and which cap that beats — so the kit is publishable, with only the horse/foot proximity of example 0 to bank question 1 worth keeping an eye on³."}}},"12-3":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"12-3","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This is the room where warfare stops being medieval and becomes industrial, and it supplies more Paper 1 Section B material than any other stretch of the Warfare thematic study. Between c1700 and c1900 the weapon in an infantryman's hands changed from a smoothbore flintlock to a magazine rifle, the horse lost its place in the fighting line, the state stopped seizing sailors and started bargaining with recruits, and the public began to read an eyewitness account of a battle within weeks of it happening. Both of the option's named case studies for this period, Waterloo in 1815 and Balaclava in 1854, sit here, and they are only thirty-nine years apart, which makes them the sharpest test of change and continuity anywhere in the course. Almost every judgement question about the modern period measures itself against the c1700–c1900 baseline, so if you cannot say precisely what changed here, and why, you cannot argue about what came after it.","idea":[{"h":"The horse loses its place: the slow decline of the cavalry","t":"In 1700 an army had three arms — infantry, cavalry and artillery — and the mounted arm was the prestige one, officered by the landed gentry and expected to decide a battle by shock. That was still true at Waterloo in 1815, where the Union and Household Brigades charged in mass, and it was still assumed at Balaclava in 1854, where both the Heavy and the Light Brigades were sent in on horseback. What killed the charge was not a change of taste but a change of arithmetic. A horseman crossing open ground in front of smoothbore muskets had perhaps one badly aimed volley to survive; in front of rifles firing accurately at several hundred yards, and then breech-loaders and magazine rifles, he had minutes of aimed fire to survive. By the Boer War of 1899–1902 the horse had become transport: mounted infantry rode to a position and dismounted to fight. A top-level answer never says cavalry 'became useless'. It shows the mounted arm surviving in a changed role because firepower, not fashion, made the charge unaffordable, and it dates the change by the weapons that caused it.","code":"12-3 — 1 The nature of warfare: Continuity and change in the composition of the army"},{"h":"Rifling, shells and factories: industry rewrites the battlefield","t":"The chain here runs weapon, tactic, battlefield, and you must be able to trace it in both directions. The Land Pattern musket, the Brown Bess, was smoothbore, muzzle-loading and fired by a flintlock: about three rounds a minute, unreliable beyond a hundred yards, so men stood shoulder to shoulder and fired volleys. Rifling — spiral grooves that spin the bullet — gave range and accuracy but was slow to load, until the Minié bullet of the 1840s, conical and hollow-based, dropped easily down the barrel and expanded into the grooves when fired. That made the rifled musket, such as the Pattern 1853 Enfield used in the Crimea, a weapon for every infantryman. Breech-loading from the 1860s and magazine rifles from the late 1880s multiplied the rate of fire again, while rifled breech-loading artillery reached far beyond Waterloo's smoothbore cannon. Industry supplied the rest: machine tools at the Royal Small Arms Factory at Enfield in the 1850s, railways and steamships to move armies, and the telegraph to command them. A top-level answer never lists inventions. It shows a named weapon changing what soldiers could do, and then what commanders had to do about it.","code":"12-3 — 1 The nature of warfare: Impact on warfare of changes in weaponry"},{"h":"From the bounty and the ballot to a professional army","t":"In the eighteenth century the state got its fighting men in three ways, none of them conscription. The army enlisted volunteers for a bounty, handed out by recruiting sergeants in taverns and market places, and enlistment could last a lifetime. The Militia, a part-time home-defence force, was raised by county ballot under the Act of 1757, and a man drawn by lot could pay for a substitute, which loaded the burden on the poor. The navy simply took experienced seamen by force through the Impress Service — the press gang. Training was regimental, learned by drill on the parade ground, and officers bought their commissions. The Crimean War exposed what that produced, and Cardwell's reforms of 1868–74 replaced it: flogging abolished in peacetime, short service with a reserve in 1870, the purchase of commissions abolished in 1871, and localisation with linked battalions in 1872. A top-level answer explains recruitment as a bargain between the state and the recruit that had to improve as the alternatives to soldiering improved, and it treats Cardwell as the answer to a problem, not as a list of Acts.","code":"12-3 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants"},{"h":"War reaches the breakfast table: requisitioning, reporting and photography","t":"British civilians in this period were not attacked — Culloden in 1746 was the last pitched battle fought on British soil — so the impact of war reached them by other routes. It came as taxation, including the income tax Pitt introduced in 1799 to pay for the war with France; as billeting of soldiers on inns and alehouses; as requisitioning of horses, carts and food; and as the press gang taking the men of a port. What changed most, however, was knowledge. Until the nineteenth century the public learned of a battle weeks later from official despatches. Then newspapers sent paid correspondents: William Howard Russell wrote for The Times from the Crimea in 1854 and 1855, and Roger Fenton photographed the camps there. Russell's most damaging despatches were written in the autumn of 1854 and went home by post, taking about three weeks — the letter he wrote on 4 October 1854 was printed on 23 October — and although a telegraph cable reached the Crimea in 1855 it carried short messages and government traffic, so long reports still travelled by mail. Anger at what Russell described helped bring down Lord Aberdeen's government in 1855. By the Boer War the same machinery carried Emily Hobhouse's report of 1901 on the camps holding Boer civilians. A top-level answer treats reporting as a cause with consequences — governments fell, reforms followed — and not as a change in the newspaper business.","code":"12-3 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians"},{"h":"Waterloo, 18 June 1815: the last great battle of the old warfare","t":"Waterloo is the exam's picture of pre-industrial war. Napoleon, back from Elba, attacked an Anglo-allied army of about 68,000 under Wellington with roughly 72,000 French, on a ridge south of Brussels. Everything on the field belonged to the old world: smoothbore flintlock muskets, the Baker rifle in the hands of the 95th, muzzle-loading cannon firing round shot and canister, infantry in squares, cavalry charging in mass. The outcome turned on four things you must be able to name and weigh: Wellington's choice of ground, the ridge of Mont-Saint-Jean with his infantry sheltered on the reverse slope; the fortified farms of Hougoumont, never taken, and La Haye Sainte, which fell late when its ammunition ran out; Napoleon's errors, including the late start after heavy overnight rain — the French attack on Hougoumont opened the battle at about half past eleven in the morning, and the grand battery and d'Erlon's main assault on the allied left did not follow until about half past one — and Marshal Ney's unsupported cavalry charges; and the arrival of Blücher's Prussians from Wavre during the afternoon and evening, while Grouchy was held at Wavre with a third of the French army. A top-level answer never narrates the day. It picks a criterion — which factor, if removed, would most have changed the result — and tests Wellington against Napoleon's mistakes and the Prussians.","code":"12-3 — 3 Case studies: The Battle of Waterloo (1815)"},{"h":"Balaclava, 25 October 1854: a battle of orders and misunderstandings","t":"Balaclava is the exam's picture of an army whose weapons had modernised faster than its command. The Russians under Liprandi attacked the redoubts on the Causeway Heights, held by Turkish gunners, and captured several with the British guns in them. Two British successes followed: the 93rd Highlanders under Sir Colin Campbell held in line two deep against Russian cavalry, and Scarlett's Heavy Brigade charged uphill and drove a larger force back. Then came the failure. Lord Raglan, watching from the Sapouné Heights, could see the Russians hauling away the captured guns; Lord Lucan, on the valley floor, could not. Raglan's order, written by Airey and carried by Captain Nolan, did not say which guns, so Lucan sent Cardigan's Light Brigade about a mile down the North Valley at the Russian battery at the far end, under fire from three sides; of roughly 670 who charged, about 110 were killed. The battle itself was indecisive, but the Russians kept the Woronzoff Road. A top-level answer weighs Raglan's wording against Lucan's position, Nolan's manner and the Russian guns, and separates the charge from the outcome of the battle.","code":"12-3 — 3 Case studies: The Battle of Balaclava (1854)"}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 3, 4 marks: one analysed difference across two periods","setup":"Paper 1 Question 3, worth 4 marks: 'Explain one way in which the composition of the army in the years c1700–c1900 was different from the composition of the army in the years c1900–present.' Two marks are for AO2, the analysis of the difference, and two for AO1, the specific information that supports it, so you must name evidence from both periods and then explain what kind of difference it is.","steps":["Fix on one difference big enough to carry the whole answer: the earlier army was built around men and horses, while the later one was built around machines and the specialists who work them.","Evidence the earlier half with named detail: heavy cavalry charged in mass at Waterloo in 1815, and at Balaclava in 1854 both the Heavy Brigade and the Light Brigade were sent in on horseback to decide the battle.","Evidence the later half with named detail: the horse left the fighting line after the First World War, its work passing to tanks, armoured cars, aircraft, signals and engineers, so the army's striking power came to sit in technical corps.","Finish with the sentence that turns two descriptions into one analysed difference: the change is in what the army is made of, because firepower made the mounted charge unaffordable, so men who would once have ridden now crew and service machines."],"cues":["Which single difference is worth choosing here?","What named evidence proves the earlier half?","What named evidence proves the later half?","What must the last sentence do that the two halves have not?"]},{"title":"Question 4, 12 marks: three reasons, one of them beyond the stimulus","setup":"Paper 1 Question 4, worth 12 marks: 'Explain why artillery became more important in warfare in the years c1700–c1900.' The stimulus points printed under the question are shrapnel shell and the siege of Sevastopol, and the paper adds that you must also use information of your own. Six marks are for AO2 and six for AO1, and an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content is capped at eight marks and shut out of Level 4.","steps":["Turn the first stimulus point into a reason rather than a label: shrapnel shell, invented by the British officer Henry Shrapnel and adopted in 1803, burst in the air and scattered musket balls over the men beneath it, so a battery could reach troops lying down or sheltering behind a crest instead of only those directly in front of its muzzle.","Turn the second into a reason as well: heavy siege artillery bombarded Sevastopol for almost a year from the autumn of 1854, so the decisive work of a campaign could be done by guns grinding down defences over months rather than by one day's meeting of two armies.","Add the reason of your own that opens the top level: rifling reached the gun as well as the musket, because from the late 1850s Britain adopted William Armstrong's rifled breech-loading guns, and by the end of the century rifled artillery with steel barrels far outranged the smoothbore muzzle-loaders that had fired round shot and canister at Waterloo.","Add a second reason of your own and link it to the first: a gun is only as useful as its supply, and industry provided one, because factories turned out shot and shell in quantity while railways and steamships moved weights of ammunition that horses and carts never could, which is what made a bombardment lasting months possible at all.","Close by tying the reasons into one line of reasoning: artillery rose because it could now reach further than the men it fought and because an industrial state could keep it fed, so commanders had to build their plans around the guns rather than around the infantry line."],"cues":["What could a shell that burst in the air do that a solid ball could not?","What does a bombardment lasting a year show about where a campaign was decided?","What happened to the barrel of the gun itself, and roughly when?","What does a gun need besides range, and who supplied it?","What single sentence explains why the guns moved to the centre?"]},{"title":"Question 5, 16 marks: one paragraph that weighs rather than describes","setup":"Paper 1 Question 5, worth 16 marks with a further 4 for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology: 'The impact of war on British civilians in the years c1700–c1900 was mainly financial.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. The stimulus points are taxation and requisitioning, and you must also use information of your own. You are writing the paragraph that tests the named factor, the cost of war in money.","steps":["State the point as an arguable claim about the named factor: because no enemy army landed, war reached most civilians here first of all as a bill, and the state's demand for money rose with every war it fought.","Support it with precise evidence: William Pitt the Younger introduced income tax in 1799 to pay for the war with France, and it was repealed in 1816 once the fighting was over, while indirect taxes and the county rates that paid for the Militia reached households that never saw a soldier.","Explain the causal link rather than asserting it: Culloden in 1746 was the last pitched battle fought on British soil, so for the rest of the period war was something civilians funded rather than something they watched, and a tax that arrives with a war and is repealed with it shows that connection exactly.","Put the counter-view in its strongest form: the state took things and people as well as money — horses, carts, hay and food were requisitioned from the districts an army passed through, soldiers were billeted on inns and alehouses, and the Impress Service seized experienced seamen, which could strip a port of its working men overnight.","Close with an interim judgement instead of a summary: money was the widest impact, because it reached everybody, but it was not the heaviest, because a requisitioned farm or a pressed crew lost a livelihood rather than a share of an income, so the statement holds only if width is the test."],"cues":["What claim about the cost of war is actually worth arguing?","Which precise tax, and which dates, prove it?","Why does it matter that no enemy army marched through Britain?","What is the strongest case against a purely financial impact?","What should the last sentence decide?"]},{"title":"Question 6, 16 marks: choosing the criterion and writing the conclusion","setup":"Paper 1 Question 6, worth 16 marks plus 4 for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology: 'The abolition of the purchase of commissions was the most important of Cardwell's army reforms.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. The stimulus points are short service and linked battalions, and you must also use information of your own. You have already written paragraphs on the ending of purchase, on short service and the reserve, and on localisation, and now need the conclusion.","steps":["Name the criterion you are judging by, so that the conclusion tests something instead of announcing a preference: which reform changed what the army could actually do, and went on doing it after Cardwell left office in 1874?","Apply it to the named reform: purchase ended in 1871, so rank could no longer be bought by men such as Lucan and Cardigan, whose quarrel helped wreck a brigade at Balaclava; but ending a bad way of choosing officers does not by itself produce good ones, and the change worked slowly, through the men who rose afterwards.","Apply it to the first stimulus point: the Army Enlistment Act of 1870 split a twelve-year engagement between the colours and the reserve, which gave Britain something it had never had, a body of trained men who could be recalled, and that reserve is what made it possible to send an army abroad without emptying the country.","Apply it to the second: the localisation scheme carried out from 1872 divided the country into 66 brigade districts, each with a depot and two linked battalions, one serving abroad while the other recruited at home, so a regiment had a county to draw on and a home to return to, and the Childers reforms of 1881 fixed that in the county titles the army kept.","Decide, and say on what basis: on the criterion of what the army could do, short service ranks first, because it created the reserve the rest depended on, and the end of purchase ranks first only on a criterion of fairness, so the statement is right about the principle and wrong about the machinery."],"cues":["What criterion will decide this judgement?","How does the named reform measure against it?","What did the first stimulus point give the army that it had never had?","What did the second change about where a regiment's men came from?","What must the final sentence commit to?"]},{"title":"Question 4, 12 marks: turning what the state took into an explanation","setup":"Paper 1 Question 4, worth 12 marks: 'Explain why war affected the daily lives of British civilians in the years c1700–c1900.' The stimulus points printed under the question are requisitioning and recruitment, and the paper adds that you must also use information of your own. Six marks are for AO2 and six for AO1, and an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content is capped at eight marks and shut out of Level 4.","steps":["Start from the situation being explained: Culloden in 1746 was the last pitched battle fought on British soil, so people here were almost never in the path of an army, and war reached them instead through what the state took from them in order to fight it.","Turn the first stimulus point into a reason: an army on the move requisitioned horses, carts, hay and food from the districts it passed through, and soldiers were billeted on inns and alehouses, so a campaign could take away the transport, the stabling and the fodder a local economy ran on.","Turn the second into a reason as well: recruiting sergeants offered a bounty in taverns and market places, the Militia was raised by county ballot under the Act of 1757 and a man drawn by lot could pay for a substitute, and the Impress Service seized experienced seamen, so the burden fell hardest on those who could not buy their way out of it.","Add the reason of your own that opens the top level: wars have to be paid for, and William Pitt the Younger introduced income tax in 1799 to fund the war with France, so households that never saw a soldier still felt the fighting in their taxes and in the price of bread.","Add a second reason of your own and link it to the first: by the middle of the nineteenth century civilians were reached by knowledge as well, because paid correspondents at the front and cheap papers after the repeal of stamp duty in 1855 brought an eyewitness account of a war into ordinary homes.","Close by tying the reasons into one line of reasoning: war touched civilians through the demands a state had to make in order to fight — for goods, for men and for money — and, as the century went on, through what those same civilians were at last able to find out about it."],"cues":["Why does it matter that no enemy army marched through Britain?","What did an army take from a district it passed through?","How did the state get its soldiers and sailors, and who paid the price?","Which reason of your own reaches past the two stimulus points?","What was new in the middle of the nineteenth century?","What single sentence names why war reached civilians at all?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Why did the Minié bullet matter more than rifling on its own?","a":"Rifling had existed for a century but rifles were slow to load, because the ball had to be forced into the grooves. The Minié bullet was hollow-based and slightly undersized, so it dropped down the barrel easily and only expanded into the rifling when fired, which gave every infantryman a rifle he could load as fast as a smoothbore."},{"q":"Name the four changes Cardwell made between 1868 and 1872 and say what problem each answered.","a":"Flogging abolished in peacetime in 1868, to make the army a respectable career; short service with a reserve in 1870, because Britain had no trained reserve; the purchase of commissions abolished in 1871, because rank went to wealth rather than ability; localisation with linked battalions in 1872, so one battalion could serve abroad while the other recruited at home."},{"q":"Why did the Charge of the Light Brigade happen at all?","a":"Raglan, on the Sapouné Heights, could see the Russians removing the captured guns from the Causeway Heights redoubts, but his order, written by Airey and carried by Nolan, did not say which guns. Lucan, on the valley floor, could not see those redoubts, so he sent the brigade at the only guns he could see, the battery at the end of the North Valley."},{"q":"Give the strongest argument that Wellington was not the main reason for the victory at Waterloo.","a":"His plan was a plan to survive, not to win: Blücher's Prussians arriving from Wavre during the afternoon and evening forced Napoleon to divert troops to Plancenoit, and the French army broke only after the Imperial Guard's final attack failed against a line the Prussians had already unbalanced."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 1 Section B is worth 36 of the paper's 52 marks: Question 3 is 4 marks, Question 4 is 12 marks, and Question 5 or Question 6 is 16 marks plus up to 4 for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology.","Question 3, 'Explain one way in which … was similar to / different from …', carries 2 marks for AO2 and 2 for AO1 and normally spans at least a century, so both periods must be named and evidenced with specific information.","Question 4 stimulus caps: a maximum of 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","Question 5 or 6 stimulus caps: a maximum of 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; at Level 4 the criteria for the judgement must be justified and applied.","Timing on the 80-minute Paper 1: roughly a minute a mark — about 5 minutes on Question 3, 15 on Question 4 and 25 on Question 5 or 6, which is the only Section B answer where spelling and specialist terms carry their own marks.","Question 5 or 6 may be set on a complete period or on a span of at least 200 years, so the evidence chosen must reach across the whole of c1700–c1900 and not sit in one war."],"questions":[{"q":"Explain one way in which the use of cavalry in the years c1250–c1500 was similar to the use of cavalry in the years c1700–c1900. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed similarity — cavalry used in both periods for the mass shock charge, and vulnerable in both to massed missile fire — supported by named battles from each period, such as Crécy or Agincourt against Waterloo or Balaclava.","sol":"One similarity is that in both periods cavalry was used for shock action: mounted men charged in mass to break an enemy formation and decide the battle. In the years c1250–c1500 armoured knights charged with the lance, as the French cavalry did against the English at Crécy in 1346 and again at Agincourt in 1415. In the years c1700–c1900 the same idea survived: the Household and Union Brigades charged in mass at Waterloo in 1815, and at Balaclava in 1854 both the Heavy Brigade and the Light Brigade were sent in on horseback. In both periods, too, the charge could be broken by massed missile fire — first the longbow, then the rifle.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what a commander in each period expected his mounted men to achieve once the battle began.","The similarity is stronger if you can show the same weakness appearing in both periods, so think about what actually stopped a charge.","Name the shared use, evidence it with a named battle from each period, then explain why commanders kept trusting the same tactic."],"codes":["12-3 — 1 The nature of warfare: Continuity and change in the composition of the army","12-3 — 3 Case studies: The Battle of Waterloo (1815)"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which infantry firearms in the years c1500–c1700 were similar to infantry firearms in the years c1700–c1900. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed similarity — both periods fought with smoothbore muzzle-loaders inaccurate at range, so both used massed volley fire — supported by detail such as the matchlock and pike formations against the flintlock Brown Bess in line.","sol":"One similarity is that for most of both periods infantry firearms were smoothbore muzzle-loaders, so both armies fought by massed volley rather than by aimed fire. In the years c1500–c1700 the matchlock musket was heavy, slow and inaccurate, so musketeers stood in ranks beside pikemen and fired together. In the years c1700–c1900 the flintlock Land Pattern musket, the Brown Bess, was quicker and more reliable, but it was still smoothbore and still unreliable beyond about a hundred yards, so the redcoats at Waterloo in 1815 stood shoulder to shoulder in line and fired volleys on command. The same weakness in the weapon produced the same tactics in both periods.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what the barrel of the weapon was like in each period, not about how the weapon was fired.","A similarity is stronger when the same limitation produces the same behaviour, so look for the tactic both periods were forced into.","Name the shared feature, evidence it in each period with a named weapon, then explain why that feature dictated how men had to stand and fire."],"codes":["12-3 — 1 The nature of warfare: Impact on warfare of changes in weaponry","12-3 — 3 Case studies: The Battle of Waterloo (1815)"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the recruitment of combatants in the years c1700–c1900 was different from the recruitment of combatants in the years c1900–present. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — a state that bought or seized individuals against a state that could compel whole age groups by statute — supported by the bounty, the Militia ballot and the press gang against the conscription Acts of 1916 and 1939.","sol":"One difference is who decided whether a man served. In the years c1700–c1900 the army was filled by voluntary enlistment, usually for a bounty paid to men recruited by sergeants in taverns and market places; the Militia was raised by county ballot under the Act of 1757, with substitutes allowed for those who could pay; and the navy took experienced seamen by force through the press gang. In the years c1900–present service was imposed by Parliament on whole age groups, with conscription introduced in 1916 during the First World War and again in 1939. The difference is between a state that bought or seized individuals and one that could lawfully command a generation.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask who made the decision that a particular man would serve in each period.","Both halves need named machinery, not a general impression: what the earlier state used, and what law the later state used.","State the difference, evidence the eighteenth- and nineteenth-century methods, evidence the twentieth-century one, then say what kind of change in the power of the state this represents."],"codes":["12-3 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the impact of war on British civilians in the years c1700–c1900 was different from the impact of war on British civilians in the years c1900–present. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — indirect burdens at home against civilians as direct targets — supported by taxation, billeting, requisitioning and the press gang against aerial bombardment in the two world wars.","sol":"One difference is whether civilians were themselves targets. In the years c1700–c1900 British civilians felt war indirectly: they paid for it through taxes such as the income tax Pitt introduced in 1799, soldiers were billeted on their inns and alehouses, horses, carts and food were requisitioned, and seamen were taken by the press gang. Britain itself was not fought over, and Culloden in 1746 was the last pitched battle on British soil. In the years c1900–present civilians were attacked directly from the air, first by Zeppelin and Gotha raids in the First World War and then by the Blitz of 1940–41. War changed from a burden carried at home into a danger faced at home.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask whether a civilian in each period was in physical danger from the enemy, or only out of pocket and short of men.","Evidence the earlier period with the specific ways war reached ordinary households, and the later one with the way the enemy reached them.","Name the difference first, prove each period, then explain why being taxed for a war is a different kind of impact from being bombed in one."],"codes":["12-3 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the reporting of war to the British public in the years c1500–c1700 was different from the reporting of war in the years c1700–c1900. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — occasional news written by the winning side against regular eyewitness reporting by an independent correspondent — supported by newsbooks and official despatches against Russell of The Times, whose despatches came home by post in about three weeks, and Fenton's photographs.","sol":"One difference is who told the public about a battle and how quickly. In the years c1500–c1700 nobody reported from the front: news came from official proclamations, from private letters and from printed newsbooks and pamphlets produced in London days or weeks later, usually by supporters of one side. In the years c1700–c1900 newspapers sent paid correspondents to the army itself. William Howard Russell wrote for The Times from the Crimea in 1854 and 1855, describing the state of the sick and wounded; his despatches travelled home by post and took about three weeks, so the letter he wrote on 4 October 1854 was printed on 23 October, and Roger Fenton photographed the camps there in 1855. The public now heard from an eyewitness the government did not control, regularly and while the war was still being fought.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about where the person writing the report was standing in each period.","The comparison needs the speed of the news as well as its source, so look for what carried it.","Name the difference, evidence the earlier period, evidence the later one with named people and years, then explain why an independent eyewitness changes the nature of war news."],"codes":["12-3 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians"]},{"q":"Explain why the importance of cavalry in warfare declined in the years c1700–c1900. You may use the following in your answer: • the Minié bullet • the Battle of Balaclava (1854). You must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Analytical explanation of the process of change: increased infantry firepower from rifling and the Minié bullet; the lesson of Balaclava; and, beyond the stimulus, breech-loading and magazine rifles, rifled artillery, and rail and steam mobility, ending in the mounted infantry of the Boer War. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Cavalry declined because the ground it had to cross became deadlier, because particular battles made that visible, and because industry gave other arms the speed and reach that had once been the horseman's alone.\n\nThe first reason is the rifle. The smoothbore Brown Bess was unreliable beyond about a hundred yards, so a charge had only a short killing ground to cross. The Minié bullet of the 1840s changed that: hollow-based and conical, it dropped easily down the barrel and expanded into the rifling when fired, so a rifled musket such as the Pattern 1853 Enfield could be loaded as quickly as a smoothbore and still hit at several hundred yards. Horsemen now had minutes of aimed fire to survive instead of one badly aimed volley.\n\nThe second reason is the evidence of battle. At Balaclava on 25 October 1854 Scarlett's Heavy Brigade succeeded, but the Light Brigade rode about a mile down the North Valley against a Russian battery, under fire from guns on the heights on both sides; of roughly 670 men who charged, about 110 were killed. The lesson drawn at home was that massed horsemen could not survive concentrated artillery and rifle fire.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond the stimulus: industrial firepower and industrial movement. Breech-loading rifles from the 1860s, the Snider conversion and then the Martini-Henry of 1871, let infantry reload lying down and far faster, and magazine rifles followed from the late 1880s. Rifled breech-loading artillery reached far beyond Waterloo's smoothbore cannon. Meanwhile railways and steamships moved infantry and guns faster than horses ever had, so cavalry lost its advantage in mobility as well as in shock.\n\nBy the Boer War of 1899–1902 the horse survived chiefly as transport: mounted infantry rode to a position and dismounted to fight Boers armed with Mauser magazine rifles. Cavalry did not decline because soldiers stopped valuing it, but because firepower made the charge unaffordable.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["The question is about a process, so ask what was getting harder for a horseman year by year.","Three reasons, each with a named weapon or battle and a causal chain that ends at the cavalry, will beat a list of dates.","Use each stimulus point as one reason, then add a reason of your own from outside them, and finish by saying what the horse ended up being used for instead."],"codes":["12-3 — 1 The nature of warfare: Continuity and change in the composition of the army","12-3 — 1 The nature of warfare: Impact on warfare of changes in weaponry","12-3 — 3 Case studies: The Battle of Balaclava (1854)"]},{"q":"Explain why the recruitment and training of British soldiers changed in the years c1700–c1900. You may use the following in your answer: • the Militia • Cardwell's reforms. You must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Analytical explanation of change: the unfairness and limits of the bounty, the ballot and impressment; Cardwell's short service, abolition of purchase and localisation; and, beyond the stimulus, the Crimean scandal, industrial wages and the Prussian example. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Recruitment and training changed because the old methods produced too few good men, because the Crimean War exposed what they produced, and because a reforming government had a foreign model to copy.\n\nThe first reason is the failure of the eighteenth-century system. The army bought volunteers with a bounty handed out in taverns and market places, often to men with no other prospect, and enlistment could last a lifetime. The Militia was raised by county ballot under the Act of 1757, but a man drawn by lot could pay for a substitute, so the burden fell on the poor and the force was resented rather than valued. The navy simply seized experienced seamen through the press gang. None of this produced trained reserves, and impressment became unusable once the wars with France ended in 1815.\n\nThe second reason is the Crimean War. Between 1854 and 1856 more British soldiers died of cholera, dysentery and typhus than of wounds, and William Howard Russell's despatches to The Times described the lack of supplies and medical care. The scandal, which helped bring down Lord Aberdeen's government in 1855, made the treatment of ordinary soldiers a political question and made reform of recruitment unavoidable.\n\nThe third reason, beyond the stimulus, is Prussia. Prussia's rapid defeat of France in 1870–71 showed what a short-service army with a large trained reserve and professionally educated officers could do, and Britain had neither. Cardwell, Secretary of State for War from 1868 to 1874, answered all three pressures: flogging was abolished in peacetime in 1868, the Army Enlistment Act of 1870 created a twelve-year term split between the colours and the reserve, the Army Regulation Act of 1871 abolished the purchase of commissions, and Cardwell's localisation scheme, carried out from 1872 under the same Act, gave each district a depot with two linked battalions. Officer training was already professionalising through Woolwich, and the Senior Department of the Royal Military College was renamed the Staff College in 1858, its own building at Camberley being completed in 1862.\n\nThe change was therefore a shift from taking men cheaply to making soldiering a career the state had to offer terms for.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the state actually wanted from a recruit in 1700 and what it wanted by 1900, and what had to change in between.","Reasons, not stages: each paragraph should say why something changed, with an Act, a war or a foreign example behind it.","Use the Militia and Cardwell as two of the reasons and add a pressure from outside the army altogether, then close on what recruitment had become."],"codes":["12-3 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","12-3 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians"]},{"q":"Explain why industrialisation changed the way wars were fought in the years c1700–c1900. You may use the following in your answer: • steam-powered transport • the mass production of weapons. You must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Analytical explanation of change: railways and steamships transforming supply and movement; factory production arming whole armies with identical modern weapons; and, beyond the stimulus, the telegraph and rifled breech-loading artillery. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Industrialisation changed warfare because it altered how armies moved, how they were armed and how they were commanded, and all three changes fed one another.\n\nThe first reason is steam. Railways carried troops, guns, ammunition and food at speeds no horse and cart could match, and steamships carried armies overseas on a timetable instead of waiting for the wind. The clearest example is the Crimea: in 1855 a short railway was built from Balaclava harbour up to the siege lines before Sevastopol, and supplies that had rotted on a muddy track began to reach the army. Supply, not manoeuvre, now decided how long a force could stay in the field, and a siege lasting almost a year became possible.\n\nThe second reason is mass production. In the 1850s the Royal Small Arms Factory at Enfield was re-equipped with American machine tools so that rifles could be made in large numbers with interchangeable parts. That mattered because a new weapon was worthless unless a whole army could be given it and repaired in the field. The Minié bullet and the Pattern 1853 Enfield rifle only reshaped tactics because factories could arm entire regiments with them, and later breech-loaders and magazine rifles spread the same way.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond the stimulus: communication and artillery. A telegraph cable reached the Crimea in 1855, so ministers in London could send orders to a commander in hours rather than weeks, and short press messages could follow the same wire, although a correspondent's long despatch still went home by mail; command became a national matter rather than a general's private business. At the same time rifled, breech-loading artillery, adopted in Britain from the late 1850s with Armstrong's guns, gave ranges the smoothbore cannon of Waterloo could not approach, so armies had to spread out and dig in.\n\nIndustrialisation therefore did not simply give soldiers better tools; it turned warfare into something an industrial state, rather than an army, waged.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask which parts of an army a factory and a railway can change: what it carries, what it fires, and how it is told what to do.","Each reason should end with a consequence for how battles or campaigns were actually fought, not with the invention itself.","Take movement and manufacture from the stimulus, then add a third strand of your own, and finish with a sentence about who is really waging the war."],"codes":["12-3 — 1 The nature of warfare: Impact on warfare of changes in weaponry"]},{"q":"Explain why popular attitudes to war in Britain changed in the years c1700–c1900. You may use the following in your answer: • war reporting • photography. You must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Analytical explanation of change: eyewitness correspondents bringing home, within weeks, criticism the government did not control; photography making war visible but not shocking; and, beyond the stimulus, cheap newspapers after 1855 and the Boer War, including the camps for Boer civilians. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Attitudes changed because the public stopped receiving war as an official announcement and began receiving it as evidence, and because what that evidence showed was often a scandal.\n\nThe first reason is the arrival of the war correspondent. For most of the eighteenth century a battle reached the public weeks later through despatches written by the victorious commander, so war was known as a sequence of victories. William Howard Russell reported for The Times from the Crimea in 1854 and 1855, describing the state of the sick and wounded and the collapse of supply, and although his despatches went home by post and took about three weeks — the letter he wrote on 4 October 1854 was printed on 23 October — they came from an eyewitness the government did not control, and they came while the war was still being fought. The consequences were immediate and nameable: public anger helped bring down Lord Aberdeen's government in 1855, and it carried Florence Nightingale and her party of nurses to the hospital at Scutari.\n\nThe second reason is photography, which must be handled carefully. Roger Fenton photographed the Crimea in 1855, the first war widely recorded by camera, but long exposures and official approval meant his pictures showed camps, officers and empty ground rather than fighting or the dead. Photography therefore made war look real and ordinary rather than heroic, which weakened the old painted image of glory without yet producing horror.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond the stimulus: the newspaper itself became a mass possession. Stamp duty on newspapers was abolished in 1855, cheap daily papers followed, and rising literacy gave working-class readers direct access to war news. By the Boer War of 1899–1902 the effect was obvious. Defeats at Stormberg, Magersfontein and Colenso in Black Week, December 1899, shocked a public used to colonial victories; the relief of Mafeking in May 1900 set off mass celebrations; and Emily Hobhouse's report of 1901 on the camps in which Boer civilians were held turned opinion against the government's methods.\n\nBy 1900, therefore, the public expected to judge a war rather than simply to be told about it.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what an ordinary reader could actually find out about a battle in 1750 and in 1900.","Attitudes only count as changed if something followed: look for a government, a reform or a protest you can name.","Take reporting and photography from the stimulus, then add the change that made newspapers cheap enough to reach everybody, and end with what the public now expected."],"codes":["12-3 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians"]},{"q":"Explain why the way British army officers were appointed and trained changed in the years c1700–c1900. You may use the following in your answer: • the purchase of commissions • the Crimean War. You must also use information of your own. [12 marks]","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Analytical explanation of change: purchase tying rank to wealth; Crimean command failures, including Raglan's order at Balaclava, exposed by the press; and, beyond the stimulus, the technical corps, the Staff College, renamed in 1858 and at Camberley from 1862, and the Prussian example behind Cardwell's Act of 1871. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The way officers were appointed changed because the old system selected for wealth rather than skill, because a war made that failure public, and because industrial warfare needed men who had been taught something.\n\nThe first reason is the purchase system itself. For most of the period a man bought his commission and then bought each step of promotion, so command went to those with land and money. It kept the officer corps loyal to the propertied classes and cost the state nothing, which is why it lasted, but it meant that a regiment could be led by someone who had never studied war. Lord Cardigan and Lord Lucan, brothers-in-law who quarrelled in the Crimea, are the standing example.\n\nThe second reason is the Crimean War. Lord Raglan, commander-in-chief in the Crimea, had been Wellington's military secretary and had lost an arm at Waterloo, but had never commanded an army in the field before 1854. At Balaclava on 25 October 1854 his order to recover the captured guns did not say which guns, and the Light Brigade was sent a mile down the North Valley under fire from three sides; of roughly 670 who charged, about 110 were killed. Because William Howard Russell reported for The Times from the Crimea, such failures were argued over in Parliament and in print rather than buried.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond the stimulus: the army increasingly needed technical knowledge, and a foreign example proved it. Commissions in the Royal Artillery and Royal Engineers were never bought, because gunnery and fortification had to be taught, and the Royal Military Academy at Woolwich trained those officers. The Senior Department of the Royal Military College was renamed the Staff College in 1858 and its own building at Camberley was completed in 1862, and Prussia's defeat of France in 1870–71 showed what educated staff work could achieve. Cardwell's Army Regulation Act of 1871 finally abolished purchase.\n\nOfficership therefore moved, unevenly, from a form of property towards a qualification.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what a man needed in order to command a regiment in 1750, and what he needed by 1890.","A cause is only explained when you show what was wrong with the old arrangement and what forced someone to act.","Use purchase and the Crimea as two reasons, then add the branches of the army where purchase never applied and say why, and close on what a commission had become."],"codes":["12-3 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","12-3 — 1 The nature of warfare: Continuity and change in the composition of the army","12-3 — 3 Case studies: The Battle of Balaclava (1854)"]},{"q":"'The Duke of Wellington was the main reason for the British victory at the Battle of Waterloo in 1815.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • Hougoumont • the Prussian army. You must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6, plus up to 4 SPaG. Weighed judgement on causation: Wellington's ground, reverse slope, garrisoned farms and squares against Napoleon's delay and Ney's unsupported charges and against Blücher's arrival; criterion named and applied. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The fairest test of 'main reason' is which factor, if removed, would most have changed the result. On that test Wellington was necessary to the victory but not sufficient for it.\n\nWellington's contribution began before the fighting. He chose the ridge of Mont-Saint-Jean, a position that let him place most of his infantry on the reverse slope, out of sight of the French gunners, so that the grand battery, which opened fire in the early afternoon, did far less damage than Napoleon expected. In front of that line he garrisoned two walled farms. Hougoumont, held by British Guards and allied troops, was attacked from about half past eleven in the morning, when the fighting opened, until evening, and never taken, and the fighting there drew in French troops Napoleon needed for his main attack. La Haye Sainte, held by the King's German Legion, fell only late in the afternoon when its ammunition ran out. His drilled infantry then formed squares that held against the massed cavalry charges Marshal Ney launched in the afternoon. Without these decisions the allied line would very probably have broken by mid-afternoon, so Wellington was necessary.\n\nAgainst this, Napoleon's own errors did much of the work. Heavy overnight rain left the ground soft, and although the attack on Hougoumont opened the battle at about half past eleven, he did not send in his main assault, d'Erlon's attack behind the fire of the grand battery, until about half past one, which gave the Prussians the hours they needed. He detached Marshal Grouchy with about a third of his army to pursue the Prussians, and Grouchy was held at Wavre and never reached the field. He allowed Ney to charge with cavalry unsupported by infantry, wasting the arm that might have broken the squares. A commander who was not opposed by these mistakes might not have held at all.\n\nThe decisive factor, however, was the arrival of Blücher's Prussians from Wavre. Around fifty thousand of them came up during the afternoon and evening and attacked the French right at Plancenoit, forcing Napoleon to divert troops from the attack on Wellington. Wellington's whole plan was a plan to hold until they came; his army of about sixty-eight thousand faced roughly seventy-two thousand French and was not strong enough to destroy them alone. The French army broke only after the Imperial Guard's final attack failed in the evening against a line whose enemy was already being attacked from the flank.\n\nI therefore partly agree. On the criterion of necessity, Wellington ranks first: no other commander's dispositions kept the allied army on the ridge all day, and the coalition victory was impossible without that. On the criterion of sufficiency, the Prussian arrival ranks first, because holding a ridge is not winning a battle. Waterloo was an allied victory produced by a defensive general and a punctual ally, and the statement is only half right because it credits the defence with the destruction.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first what 'main reason' will mean here, because without a test the essay can only assert.","Three candidates deserve a paragraph each: Wellington's own decisions, Napoleon's mistakes, and the Prussians.","Give each paragraph its own named evidence and an interim judgement, then let the conclusion split the question between necessity and sufficiency rather than sitting on the fence."],"codes":["12-3 — 3 Case studies: The Battle of Waterloo (1815)","12-3 — 1 The nature of warfare: Continuity and change in the composition of the army"]},{"q":"'Lord Raglan was the main reason for the outcome of the Battle of Balaclava in 1854.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the Causeway Heights • the Charge of the Light Brigade. You must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6, plus up to 4 SPaG. Weighed judgement: Raglan's vague order and position against Lucan's viewpoint, Nolan's delivery, Cardigan's obedience and the Russian capture of the redoubts; distinguishes the charge from the outcome of the battle. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The statement only works if 'the outcome' means the Charge of the Light Brigade. If it means the result of the battle, Raglan matters much less, and the judgement has to separate the two.\n\nRaglan's responsibility for the charge is strong. He watched from the Sapouné Heights, from where he could see Russian teams hauling away the British guns captured that morning from the redoubts on the Causeway Heights. His order, written down by Quartermaster-General Airey and carried by Captain Nolan, told the cavalry to prevent the enemy carrying away the guns, but it never said which guns or in which direction. A commander who knows that his subordinate cannot see what he can see is responsible for making the order unambiguous, and Raglan did not. He had been Wellington's military secretary and had lost an arm at Waterloo, but he had never commanded an army in the field before 1854, and he was slow to make his intentions plain to men he rarely spoke to.\n\nOthers, however, share the blame. Lord Lucan, on the valley floor, could not see the Causeway redoubts at all; the only guns in his view were the Russian battery at the far end of the North Valley, and he sent Lord Cardigan's brigade at those. He did not send back for clarification. Nolan, who delivered the order, is said to have gestured down the valley and was killed at the start of the charge, so whatever he knew died with him. Lucan and Cardigan were brothers-in-law who disliked each other and barely co-operated. Of roughly 670 men who charged about a mile under fire from guns on the heights on both sides as well as ahead, about 110 were killed. The charge was the product of a command structure in which nobody would ask a question.\n\nThe outcome of the battle itself, however, was largely decided by the Russians. Liprandi's attack had already taken the redoubts from the Turkish gunners in the morning, before any of Raglan's cavalry orders. Two British successes limited the damage: Sir Colin Campbell's 93rd Highlanders stood two deep in line and drove off Russian cavalry, and Scarlett's Heavy Brigade charged uphill into a far larger force and pushed it back. Balaclava itself was never taken, so the battle ended indecisively, but the Russians kept the Causeway Heights and with them the Woronzoff Road, which forced British supplies onto a muddy track and worsened the suffering of the winter of 1854–55.\n\nOn the criterion of which decision made the disaster possible, Raglan comes first for the charge: without his wording it would not have been ordered. On the criterion of what actually determined the battle's result, he ranks behind the Russian capture of the redoubts, which he could not have reversed that morning. I therefore agree with the statement about the charge and reject it about the battle, and the distinction matters because the charge is remembered while the loss of the road did the real harm.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["The word doing the work in this statement is 'outcome' — decide what it refers to before you argue.","Line up the candidates who could each have prevented the charge, and then ask separately what settled the battle.","Open with the paragraph on the order itself: show where it was ambiguous — it never said which guns — and say what a commander owes a subordinate who cannot see what he can see."],"codes":["12-3 — 3 Case studies: The Battle of Balaclava (1854)","12-3 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants"]},{"q":"'Cardwell's reforms were the most important change in the British army in the years c1700–c1900.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the purchase of commissions • the Minié bullet. You must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6, plus up to 4 SPaG. Weighed judgement on significance: Cardwell's short service, abolition of purchase and localisation against the rifle revolution and against industrial supply and mass production; criterion of importance named and applied. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Importance here should be tested by how much of the army a change altered and how long the alteration lasted. On that test Cardwell's reforms were the most important organisational change, but not the most important change overall.\n\nThe case for Cardwell is substantial. As Secretary of State for War from 1868 to 1874 he attacked the whole structure at once. Flogging was abolished in peacetime in 1868, which mattered because the army could not recruit respectable men while it punished like a press gang. The Army Enlistment Act of 1870 replaced very long service with a twelve-year term split between the colours and the reserve, so that for the first time Britain had trained reservists to recall. The Army Regulation Act of 1871 abolished the purchase of commissions, ending a system in which rank was property and a regiment could be led by a man who had bought his way in, as Lucan and Cardigan had. Cardwell's localisation scheme, carried out from 1872 under the same Act, divided the country into 66 brigade districts, each with a depot and two linked battalions, one abroad and one recruiting at home, and the Childers reforms of 1881 completed this with county titles. These changes outlasted the century and shaped the army that went to war in 1914.\n\nAgainst this stands the transformation of the weapon, which changed what soldiers did rather than how they were administered. The Brown Bess was smoothbore and unreliable beyond about a hundred yards, so armies fought in close ranks by volley. The Minié bullet of the 1840s, hollow-based and conical, expanded into the rifling when fired, and the Pattern 1853 Enfield rifle put accurate fire at several hundred yards into every infantryman's hands. Breech-loaders followed from the 1860s and magazine rifles from the late 1880s. The consequences reached every arm: cavalry charges became unaffordable, as Balaclava in 1854 suggested and the Boer War of 1899–1902 confirmed when mounted infantry dismounted to fight Boer riflemen; infantry had to spread out and take cover; artillery had to outrange the rifle.\n\nA third change, beyond the stimulus, is the industrial supply of war. Machine tools installed at the Royal Small Arms Factory at Enfield in the 1850s let the state arm whole armies with identical weapons; railways and steamships moved them, as the railway built from Balaclava to the siege lines in 1855 showed; and the telegraph cable that reached the Crimea in 1855 let London command an army in hours. Without this, Cardwell's reserve could not have been mobilised and the new rifles could not have been issued in quantity.\n\nMy judgement is that Cardwell's reforms were the most important deliberate change, because they altered recruitment, promotion, discipline and organisation together and endured. But on the criterion of how much of warfare was altered, the rifle matters more, because it changed how every soldier stood, moved and died, and it was the rifle, not an Act of Parliament, that forced the cavalry out of the fighting line. Cardwell modernised the institution; industry and the rifle modernised the fighting.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Set a test for 'most important' — how much was altered, and for how long — before you weigh anything.","Organisation and weaponry are the two rival answers, and each deserves its own paragraph of named evidence.","Add a third factor of your own from industry, and make the conclusion distinguish the most important change to the institution from the most important change to warfare itself."],"codes":["12-3 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","12-3 — 1 The nature of warfare: Impact on warfare of changes in weaponry","12-3 — 1 The nature of warfare: Continuity and change in the composition of the army"]},{"q":"'The Crimean War (1853–56) was the main turning point in warfare and British society in the years c1700–c1900.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • war reporting • the Minié bullet. You must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6, plus up to 4 SPaG. Weighed judgement on significance and turning points: the Crimean transformation of public knowledge and the pressure it created for Cardwell, against a weapons and industrial revolution already under way before 1854, and against the Boer War as a rival turning point; criterion named and applied. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"A turning point should be tested by whether the change it caused was sharp, wide and permanent. On that test the Crimean War was the main turning point in the relationship between warfare and British society, but not in the technology of war itself.\n\nThe case for the war is strongest on public knowledge. Before 1854 the British public learned of a battle weeks afterwards from despatches written by the winning commander, with nothing to check them against. William Howard Russell reported for The Times from the Crimea in 1854 and 1855, describing the collapse of supply and the state of the sick and wounded, and although his despatches went home by post and took about three weeks — the letter he wrote on 4 October 1854 was printed on 23 October — the criticism was an eyewitness's and could not be denied; the telegraph cable that reached the Crimea in 1855 carried short messages and government traffic rather than his long reports; Roger Fenton photographed the camps there in the same year. The consequences can be named: public anger helped bring down Lord Aberdeen's government in 1855, Florence Nightingale reached the hospital at Scutari in November 1854 with a party of 38 nurses, and the conduct of a war became something voters expected to judge ministers on. Nothing of that kind had happened in an earlier British war, and it never stopped happening afterwards.\n\nThe war was also the moment the army's failings became politically unanswerable. At Balaclava on 25 October 1854 roughly 670 men of the Light Brigade rode about a mile under fire from three sides because Raglan's order never said which guns he meant, and about 110 were killed. Far more soldiers died of cholera, dysentery and typhus than of wounds. That record is what made Cardwell's reforms possible: flogging abolished in peacetime in 1868, short service with a reserve in 1870, the purchase of commissions abolished in 1871 and localisation in 1872, more than a decade after the fighting ended.\n\nAgainst this, the technology of war was not turned by the Crimea; it was already turning. The Minié bullet was developed in France in the 1840s, before the war, and the Pattern 1853 Enfield rifle was in production before the fighting began. Breech-loaders followed in the 1860s and magazine rifles in the late 1880s on their own industrial timetable, and railways had been remaking Britain since the 1830s, so the short line built from Balaclava harbour to the siege lines in 1855 applied a technology already a generation old. The Crimea displayed industrial warfare rather than producing it.\n\nA rival turning point, beyond the stimulus, is the Boer War of 1899–1902, which exposed what the Crimea had not mended: the defeats of Black Week in December 1899, Boer marksmen with Mauser magazine rifles and smokeless powder, Emily Hobhouse's report of 1901 on the camps holding Boer civilians, and so many volunteers rejected as unfit that a government committee on physical deterioration reported in 1904.\n\nI therefore largely agree, with the focus corrected. On the criterion of sharpness, width and permanence, the change the Crimean War made in how the British public learned about, argued over and reformed their army was abrupt, general and never reversed. The weapons and the industry were already moving and would have moved without it, and the Boer War deepened the same change rather than beginning a new one.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["A turning point claim needs a test: decide what would make a change count as a turning rather than a continuation.","Separate the two things the statement joins together — warfare itself, and the relationship between war and British society — and check the war against each.","Make the first paragraph the one on public knowledge: set what a reader at home could learn about a battle before 1854 against what Russell's despatches gave them, and end that paragraph with a consequence you can name."],"codes":["12-3 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians","12-3 — 1 The nature of warfare: Impact on warfare of changes in weaponry","12-3 — 3 Case studies: The Battle of Balaclava (1854)","12-3 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Which French officer gave his name to the hollow-based conical bullet that expanded into a rifle's grooves when fired?","a":"Claude-Étienne Minié."},{"q":"On what date was the Battle of Waterloo fought?","a":"18 June 1815."},{"q":"Which newspaper published William Howard Russell's despatches from the Crimea?","a":"The Times."},{"q":"In which year did Cardwell's Army Regulation Act abolish the purchase of commissions?","a":"1871."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The Brown Bess","back":"The Land Pattern musket, the standard British infantry firearm through the eighteenth century and the Napoleonic Wars. Smoothbore, muzzle-loading and fired by a flintlock: about three rounds a minute, unreliable beyond roughly a hundred yards.","code":"12-3 — 1 The nature of warfare: Impact on warfare of changes in weaponry","open":false},{"front":"Rifling","back":"Spiral grooves cut inside a gun barrel. They spin the bullet, giving far greater range and accuracy. Early rifles such as the Baker were slow to load because the ball had to be forced into the grooves.","code":"12-3 — 1 The nature of warfare: Impact on warfare of changes in weaponry","open":false},{"front":"The Minié bullet","back":"Developed in France by Claude-Étienne Minié in the 1840s. Conical and hollow-based, it dropped easily down the barrel and expanded into the rifling when fired, so rifles loaded as fast as smoothbores.","code":"12-3 — 1 The nature of warfare: Impact on warfare of changes in weaponry","open":false},{"front":"Breech-loading rifles","back":"Loaded at the rear instead of down the muzzle, so a soldier could reload lying down and far faster. Britain adopted the Snider conversion in the 1860s and the Martini-Henry in 1871; magazine rifles followed from the late 1880s.","code":"12-3 — 1 The nature of warfare: Impact on warfare of changes in weaponry","open":false},{"front":"The railway and the telegraph in the Crimean War","back":"In 1855 a short railway was built from Balaclava harbour to the siege lines before Sevastopol, and a telegraph cable reached the Crimea, so supplies moved faster and London could send orders in hours.","code":"12-3 — 1 The nature of warfare: Impact on warfare of changes in weaponry","open":false},{"front":"The mass production of weapons","back":"In the 1850s the Royal Small Arms Factory at Enfield was re-equipped with American machine tools, so rifles could be made in large numbers with interchangeable parts and whole armies could be armed alike.","code":"12-3 — 1 The nature of warfare: Impact on warfare of changes in weaponry","open":false},{"front":"The decline of the cavalry","back":"Still a decisive arm at Waterloo in 1815 and trusted at Balaclava in 1854, the mounted charge became unaffordable once rifles, breech-loaders and rifled artillery could kill at long range.","code":"12-3 — 1 The nature of warfare: Continuity and change in the composition of the army","open":false},{"front":"Mounted infantry in the Boer War","back":"In the war of 1899–1902 the horse had become transport. Troops rode to a position and dismounted to fight Boer marksmen using Mauser magazine rifles and smokeless powder.","code":"12-3 — 1 The nature of warfare: Continuity and change in the composition of the army","open":false},{"front":"The press gang","back":"The Royal Navy's Impress Service, which seized experienced seamen in ports and merchant ships. Most active in the wars with France, it fell out of use after 1815.","code":"12-3 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","open":false},{"front":"The Militia","back":"A part-time home-defence force raised by county quota. Under the Act of 1757 men were chosen by ballot, but anyone who could afford it could pay for a substitute, so the burden fell on the poor.","code":"12-3 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","open":false},{"front":"Cardwell's army reforms, 1868–74","back":"Edward Cardwell, Secretary of State for War: flogging abolished in peacetime in 1868; short service with a reserve in 1870; the purchase of commissions abolished in 1871; localisation with linked battalions in 1872.","code":"12-3 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","open":false},{"front":"Requisitioning","back":"The taking of horses, carts, food and lodging for the army. British civilians also had soldiers billeted on inns and alehouses and paid war taxes, including the income tax Pitt introduced in 1799.","code":"12-3 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians","open":false},{"front":"William Howard Russell","back":"Correspondent of The Times in the Crimea in 1854 and 1855. His reports on the lack of supplies and medical care caused public anger and helped bring down Lord Aberdeen's government in 1855.","code":"12-3 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians","open":false},{"front":"The Duke of Wellington at Waterloo","back":"Arthur Wellesley chose the ridge of Mont-Saint-Jean, sheltered his infantry on the reverse slope, garrisoned Hougoumont and La Haye Sainte, and held in squares until Blücher's Prussians arrived.","code":"12-3 — 3 Case studies: The Battle of Waterloo (1815)","open":false},{"front":"The Charge of the Light Brigade","back":"Balaclava, 25 October 1854. Lord Raglan's order, written by Airey and carried by Captain Nolan, sent Lord Cardigan's brigade about a mile down a valley swept from three sides. Of roughly 670 who charged, about 110 were killed.","code":"12-3 — 3 Case studies: The Battle of Balaclava (1854)","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 1 Question 5, 16 marks: 'Industrialisation was the main reason why the nature of warfare changed in the years c1700–c1900.')","items":["Industrialisation was the main reason warfare changed, because it was the only factor that changed every part of war at once: the weapon, the supply line and the chain of command. The weapon first. The flintlock Brown Bess of 1700 was smoothbore and unreliable beyond about a hundred yards, so infantry had to stand shoulder to shoulder and fire volleys, which is exactly what they still did at Waterloo in 1815. Industry then made the rifle universal: the Minié bullet of the 1840s, conical and hollow-based, expanded into the rifling when fired, and the Pattern 1853 Enfield rifle, made in quantity at the Royal Small Arms Factory at Enfield after it was re-equipped with American machine tools in the 1850s, put accurate fire at several hundred yards into every soldier's hands. Supply next: a railway was built from Balaclava harbour to the siege lines before Sevastopol in 1855, and steam transports carried the army to the Crimea, so the length of a campaign no longer depended on what horses could drag. Command last: a telegraph cable reached the Crimea in 1855, so ministers in London could issue orders in hours, and a war became a national operation rather than a general's private business. It can be argued against this that men, not machines, made the decisive changes — that Cardwell's reforms of 1868 to 1874 remade recruitment, promotion and discipline, and that they, not industry, produced the army of the late nineteenth century. That is true of the institution, but it does not displace industrialisation, because the reserve Cardwell created could only be mobilised by rail and could only be armed by factories. On the criterion of how many parts of warfare a factor altered and how far the alteration reached, industrialisation ranks first: reform changed who the soldier was, while industry changed everything he did.","Why this reaches Level 4: the explanation is directed consistently at the conceptual focus — every sentence explains why warfare changed, not what happened in it; the information is precisely selected (the Brown Bess, the Minié bullet, the Enfield factory and its date, the Balaclava railway, the telegraph cable, Cardwell's dates) rather than general; the counter-view is stated at its strongest and answered instead of being dropped in; and the criterion for the judgement is named and applied in the closing sentence, which is what the third bullet of the Level 4 descriptor rewards."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Industrialisation was the main reason warfare changed between 1700 and 1900. Factories could make lots of weapons very quickly, so soldiers got much better guns than before and could kill people from further away. Trains were invented and this meant soldiers and supplies could be moved around much faster than walking or using horses, which helped a lot in the Crimean War. There was also the telegraph so messages went quicker. Cavalry became less useful because of the new guns and horses were not really needed any more. Cardwell also changed the army and made it better for the soldiers. Overall industrialisation was the most important reason because without factories none of the new weapons would have existed.","Why this stays at Level 2: it meets 'an explanation is given showing limited analysis and with implicit or unsustained links to the conceptual focus of the question' and 'accurate and relevant information is included, showing some knowledge and understanding of the period', and the overall judgement is given but asserted. It misses Levels 3 and 4 because almost nothing is named or dated: Cardwell and the Crimean War are the only things it names, and neither is given a date or a detail — no weapon, no bullet, no factory, no cable, no Act, no battle; because the counter-point about Cardwell is mentioned and then abandoned instead of weighed; and because no criterion is stated, so 'most important' is claimed rather than tested."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Case study: the Battle of Waterloo, 18 June 1815","items":["Date and place: 18 June 1815, on a ridge near the village of Waterloo, south of Brussels, in what is now Belgium.","It ended the Hundred Days, the campaign that followed Napoleon's return from exile on Elba in March 1815.","Commanders: the Duke of Wellington (Arthur Wellesley) led an Anglo-allied army of British, Dutch-Belgian, Hanoverian, Brunswick and Nassau troops; Napoleon led the French; Field Marshal Blücher led the Prussians.","Numbers: roughly 68,000 under Wellington and about 72,000 French, with some 50,000 Prussians coming up during the afternoon and evening.","Two days earlier, on 16 June, Napoleon beat the Prussians at Ligny while Wellington held Marshal Ney at Quatre Bras.","Napoleon detached Marshal Grouchy with about a third of his army to pursue the Prussians; Grouchy was held at Wavre and never reached the battlefield.","Heavy overnight rain left the ground soft: the French attack on Hougoumont opened the battle at about half past eleven in the morning, and the grand battery and d'Erlon's main assault on the allied left did not follow until about half past one, which gave the Prussians time to arrive.","Wellington chose the ridge of Mont-Saint-Jean and sheltered most of his infantry on the reverse slope, out of sight of the French guns.","The walled farm of Hougoumont, held by British Guards and allied troops, was attacked all day and never taken; the fighting there drew in French troops needed elsewhere.","La Haye Sainte, held by the King's German Legion, fell late in the afternoon when its ammunition ran out.","In the afternoon Marshal Ney led massed cavalry charges against the allied line without infantry support, and the British infantry squares held.","The weapons were those of the old warfare: the smoothbore flintlock Brown Bess, the Baker rifle carried by the 95th Rifles, and smoothbore muzzle-loading cannon firing round shot and canister.","The Prussians attacked the French right at the village of Plancenoit, forcing Napoleon to divert troops from his attack on Wellington.","The final French attack, made by the Imperial Guard, was repulsed in the evening and the French army broke.","Napoleon abdicated soon afterwards and was exiled to St Helena; the battle ended more than twenty years of war with France."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Case study: the Battle of Balaclava, 25 October 1854","items":["Date and place: 25 October 1854, near the small port of Balaclava, the British supply base in the Crimea.","The Crimean War (1853–56) was fought by Britain, France, the Ottoman Empire and later Sardinia against Russia; Britain and France declared war in March 1854.","Commanders: Lord Raglan was commander-in-chief of the British army in the Crimea; the Russian force was commanded by General Liprandi.","Raglan had been Wellington's military secretary and lost his right arm at Waterloo; he had never commanded an army in the field before 1854.","The Russians attacked the redoubts on the Causeway Heights, held by Turkish gunners, capturing several of them together with the British guns in them.","The 93rd Highlanders under Sir Colin Campbell stood two deep in line rather than forming square and drove off Russian cavalry by volley fire; Russell's report turned this into the legend of the thin red line.","The Heavy Brigade, under Brigadier-General James Scarlett, charged uphill into a much larger body of Russian cavalry and drove it back.","Raglan watched from the Sapouné Heights, from where he could see the captured redoubts; Lord Lucan, on the valley floor, could not.","Raglan's order was written down by Quartermaster-General Richard Airey and carried to Lucan by Captain Louis Nolan, who was killed at the start of the charge.","The order told the cavalry to prevent the enemy carrying away the guns but did not say which guns, so Lucan sent Lord Cardigan's Light Brigade at the Russian battery at the far end of the North Valley.","The brigade rode about a mile under fire from Russian guns on the heights on both sides as well as ahead; of roughly 670 men who charged, about 110 were killed and many more were wounded or lost their horses.","Lucan and Cardigan were brothers-in-law who disliked each other and co-operated badly.","The battle was indecisive: Balaclava was not taken, but the Russians kept the Causeway Heights and the Woronzoff Road.","Losing the Woronzoff Road forced British supplies onto a muddy track from Balaclava and worsened the suffering of the winter of 1854–55.","Tennyson's poem about the charge was published in December 1854, within weeks of the battle, which shows how fast news of it travelled home.","Raglan died in the Crimea in June 1855, before Sevastopol fell in September that year."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Cardwell's army reforms, 1868–74","items":["Edward Cardwell was Secretary of State for War from 1868 to 1874, in Gladstone's first government.","The reforms answered two shocks: the mismanagement exposed in the Crimean War, and Prussia's rapid defeat of France in 1870–71.","In 1868 flogging was abolished in the army in peacetime, which mattered because the army could not attract respectable recruits while it punished so brutally.","The Army Enlistment (Short Service) Act of 1870 introduced a twelve-year enlistment divided between service with the colours and service in the reserve.","Short service created a trained reserve that could be recalled in a crisis, instead of an army of long-serving veterans with nothing behind it.","In 1870 the Commander-in-Chief was made subordinate to the Secretary of State for War, bringing the army under clear civilian control.","The Army Regulation Act of 1871 abolished the purchase of commissions, so officers could no longer buy rank or promotion.","The same Act brought the Militia under the control of the Crown and the War Office rather than the Lords Lieutenant of the counties.","There was no Localisation Act: localisation rested on the same 1871 Act, whose formal title is the Regulation of the Forces Act 1871, and was carried out by Cardwell's localisation scheme of 1872, which divided the country into 66 brigade districts, each with a regimental depot and two linked battalions, one serving abroad and one recruiting at home.","The Childers reforms of 1881 completed localisation by giving regiments county titles.","Officer training was already professionalising in part: commissions in the Royal Artillery and Royal Engineers were never bought, the Royal Military Academy at Woolwich trained those officers, and the Senior Department of the Royal Military College was renamed the Staff College in 1858, its building at Camberley being completed in 1862 and the college separated from the Royal Military College in 1870.","Limits: Britain still had no conscription and no general staff, the army remained small and volunteer, and the defeats of the Boer War showed how much was still undone."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — The Crimean War and the Boer War at home","items":["The Crimean War ran from 1853 to 1856; Britain and France declared war on Russia in March 1854 and the Treaty of Paris ended it in 1856.","The main British battles were the Alma in September 1854, Balaclava in October 1854 and Inkerman in November 1854, around the siege of Sevastopol, which fell in September 1855.","Far more British soldiers died of disease — cholera, dysentery and typhus — than of wounds.","William Howard Russell reported for The Times from the Crimea in 1854 and 1855, describing the lack of supplies and medical care; his despatches went home by post and took about three weeks, the letter he wrote on 4 October 1854 appearing on 23 October.","A telegraph cable reached the Crimea in 1855, carrying short messages and government orders in hours; long despatches, including Russell's, still went home by mail.","Public anger at the conduct of the war helped bring down Lord Aberdeen's government in 1855; Palmerston replaced him as Prime Minister.","Florence Nightingale reached the military hospital at Scutari in November 1854 with a party of 38 nurses; the death rate fell sharply after a sanitary commission sent from London cleared the drains in 1855.","Nightingale returned a national figure and used the fund raised in her name to open a training school for nurses at St Thomas's Hospital in 1860.","Mary Seacole paid her own way to the Crimea and ran the British Hotel near the front, selling supplies and nursing soldiers.","Roger Fenton photographed the Crimea in 1855, but long exposures and official approval meant he pictured camps, officers and empty ground rather than fighting or the dead.","Stamp duty on newspapers was abolished in 1855, cheap daily papers followed, and rising literacy gave far more readers direct access to war news.","The Second Boer War was fought from 1899 to 1902 between Britain and the Boer republics in South Africa.","In Black Week, in December 1899, British forces were defeated at Stormberg, Magersfontein and Colenso, shocking a public used to colonial victories.","Boer riflemen with Mauser magazine rifles and smokeless powder could kill at long range without revealing their positions.","The relief of Mafeking in May 1900 set off mass celebrations in British towns.","Emily Hobhouse's report of 1901 on the camps in which Boer civilians were held caused a public outcry; many thousands of women and children died in them.","So many volunteers for the Boer War were rejected as physically unfit that a government committee on physical deterioration reported in 1904."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Weapons, artillery and industry, c1700–c1900","items":["The Land Pattern musket, nicknamed the Brown Bess, was the standard British infantry firearm through the eighteenth century and the Napoleonic Wars: smoothbore, muzzle-loading and fired by a flintlock.","A trained soldier could fire about three rounds a minute with the Brown Bess, but it was unreliable beyond about a hundred yards, so infantry fought in close ranks and fired volleys.","Flints misfired in wet weather; from the 1830s the percussion cap replaced the flintlock and made misfires far rarer.","Rifling means spiral grooves cut inside the barrel, which spin the bullet and give it much greater range and accuracy.","The Baker rifle, issued to the 95th Rifles from 1800 and carried at Waterloo, was accurate at long range but slow to load, because the ball had to be forced into the grooves.","The Minié bullet, developed in France by Claude-Étienne Minié in the 1840s, was conical and hollow-based: it dropped easily down the barrel and expanded into the rifling when fired.","The Minié bullet made the rifle a weapon for every infantryman, and the Pattern 1853 Enfield rifled musket, used in the Crimean War, could hit targets at several hundred yards.","Breech-loading, adopted in the British army from the 1860s with the Snider conversion and then the Martini-Henry of 1871, let a soldier reload lying down and much faster.","Magazine rifles followed from the late 1880s, beginning with the Lee-Metford of 1888, so a soldier could fire several rounds without reloading.","Artillery at Waterloo was smoothbore and muzzle-loading, firing round shot, canister and shell at short range.","Shrapnel shell, invented by the British officer Henry Shrapnel and adopted in 1803, burst in the air and scattered musket balls over the troops below.","From the late 1850s Britain adopted William Armstrong's rifled breech-loading guns, and by the end of the century rifled artillery with steel barrels had far greater range and accuracy.","Heavy siege artillery bombarded Sevastopol for almost a year from the autumn of 1854, showing how industrial guns could make a siege the centre of a war.","In the 1850s the Royal Small Arms Factory at Enfield was re-equipped with American machine tools so that rifles could be made in large numbers with interchangeable parts.","Railways moved troops, guns, ammunition and food faster than horses and roads ever had, and steamships carried armies overseas on a timetable instead of by the wind.","The electric telegraph let a government in London send orders to a commander in the field within hours, and, as the cables spread later in the century, let correspondents send short reports home just as quickly."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 1 Question 5 (16 marks + 4 SPaG): 'Industrialisation was the main reason why the nature of warfare changed in the years c1700–c1900.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. Stimulus points: steam-powered transport; the mass production of weapons.","items":["Decoded question: the concept being tested is causation across a whole period. 'Main reason' means you must rank industrialisation against at least two rivals, and 'the nature of warfare' means how war was fought, not who won which battle, so the evidence must be about weapons, tactics, movement, command and the army itself.","Criterion to set in the opening: importance will be judged by how many parts of warfare a factor altered and how permanently. Signal at once that industrialisation will be ranked first but that it needed people to apply it, so the judgement will be qualified rather than total.","Paragraph 1 (agree — the weapon): industry turned a private's firearm from a smoothbore into a rifle. Evidence: the flintlock Brown Bess, smoothbore and unreliable beyond about a hundred yards, still fired in volleys at Waterloo in 1815; the Minié bullet of the 1840s; the Pattern 1853 Enfield used in the Crimea; breech-loaders from the 1860s and the Martini-Henry of 1871; magazine rifles from the late 1880s. Interim judgement: this one change forced infantry to spread out and made the cavalry charge unaffordable.","Paragraph 2 (agree — supply and movement, using the first stimulus point): evidence — steam transports carried the army to the Crimea, and in 1855 a railway was built from Balaclava harbour to the siege lines before Sevastopol; railways at home moved troops across Britain in hours. Interim judgement: supply, not marching distance, now set the limits of a campaign, which is why sieges lasting almost a year became possible.","Paragraph 3 (agree — manufacture and command, using the second stimulus point and then going beyond it): evidence — the Royal Small Arms Factory at Enfield re-equipped with American machine tools in the 1850s, so whole armies could be given identical weapons with interchangeable parts; and, beyond the stimulus, the telegraph cable that reached the Crimea in 1855, letting London command in hours. Interim judgement: a new weapon only changes warfare when a factory can issue it and a wire can direct its use.","Paragraph 4 (the counter-argument, and the point beyond the stimulus that opens Level 4): people and policy changed warfare too. Evidence: Cardwell's reforms of 1868 to 1874 — flogging abolished in peacetime in 1868, short service with a reserve in 1870, the purchase of commissions abolished in 1871, localisation in 1872 — and the pressure of public opinion created by William Howard Russell's reporting for The Times from the Crimea in 1854 and 1855. Interim judgement: reform decided who the soldier was and who commanded him, which industry could not do.","Evaluation thread to run through every paragraph: keep asking whether the factor changed what soldiers did or only who they were. Industry changed the doing; reform changed the being. Return to that distinction in the last sentence of each paragraph.","Conclusion: agree, but with a stated qualification. On the criterion of how many parts of warfare were altered and how permanently, industrialisation ranks first, because the rifle, the railway, the factory and the telegraph together changed the weapon, the supply line, the tactics and the chain of command. Reform ranks second and depended on industry — Cardwell's reserve could only be mobilised by rail and armed by factories — so the statement holds, but only once it is granted that industry supplied the means while governments and generals decided their use.","Timing on the 80-minute paper: about 25 minutes in total — roughly 4 minutes planning the criterion and the four paragraphs, 18 writing, and 3 checking spelling and specialist terms, because this is the only Section B question where spelling, punctuation, grammar and terminology carry their own 4 marks.","Specialist terms to use accurately for the SPaG marks: smoothbore, muzzle-loading, flintlock, rifling, breech-loading, magazine rifle, canister, requisitioning, localisation, reserve, professionalisation, continuity."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-18T21:43:14Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"Factually sound and well matched to the paper — stems, tariffs, AO splits, the [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks] labelling and all three stimulus caps agree with levels.md, no question is lifted from the specimen option 12 paper or the June 2023 option 10 paper, and the load-bearing dates and figures (Waterloo 18 June 1815 with 68,000 against 72,000 and about 50,000 Prussians, Grouchy's 33,000, Balaclava 25 October 1854 with roughly 670 charging and about 110 killed, Airey and Nolan, Cardwell 1868-74 with 1868/1870/1871/1872, Minie, P53 Enfield, Snider, Martini-Henry 1871, Lee-Metford 1888, Armstrong from the late 1850s, Shrapnel 1803, the Balaclava railway and Varna cable of 1855, Aberdeen's fall and the stamp-duty repeal of 1855, Nightingale's 38 nurses at Scutari in November 1854 and St Thomas's in 1860, Black Week 1899, Hobhouse 1901, the 1904 physical-deterioration report, the Militia Act 1757) all check out; the one change a teacher should make before publishing is to re-set worked examples 2 to 5, which currently solve four of the room's own questions in advance."}}},"12-4":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"12-4","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This is the last of the four periods of Warfare and British society, and it supplies the 'after' half of nearly every question in Paper 1 Section B, which carries 36 of the paper's 52 marks. Question 3 asks you to compare the modern era with an earlier one, Question 4 asks why something changed across the theme, and Question 5 or 6 asks you to judge how far change happened, so the modern period has to be in your head in dated, precise form: the machine gun and the tank, conscription in 1916 and again in 1939, the Blitz, the atomic bomb of 1945 and the guided weapons of Iraq in 2003. It is also the period in which war reached furthest into ordinary life, through rationing, evacuation, censorship and the fear of nuclear attack, which is why so many judgement questions about civilians end here. Get the dates wrong and the AO1 marks go; tell the story instead of arguing and the AO2 marks go with them.","idea":[{"h":"The army behind the army: supply corps and specialists","t":"A modern army is mostly not infantry. The Army Service Corps, founded in 1888 and given the title Royal in 1918, moved the food, fuel and ammunition of the mass armies of the First World War, and in 1993 the Royal Logistic Corps was created by merging transport, ordnance, pioneer, catering and postal services into one corps, now the largest in the British Army. Beside it grew arms that did not exist in 1900: the Royal Flying Corps in 1912, the Tank Corps in 1917, the Royal Air Force as a separate service on 1 April 1918 and the Royal Corps of Signals in 1920. Bomb disposal is the clearest case of specialisation. Royal Engineers sections defused unexploded German bombs during the Blitz of 1940–41; in Northern Ireland, where the army was deployed from 1969 to 2007, teams faced IRA devices and from 1972 worked them with the remote-controlled Wheelbarrow vehicle; in Iraq and Afghanistan explosive ordnance disposal teams of the Royal Logistic Corps fought improvised explosive devices. A top-level answer uses all of this to argue a single point: the composition of the army follows its weapons and its enemies, because complicated machines and hidden bombs need trained tradesmen, and every tradesman has to be fed, moved and supplied, so the share of soldiers who actually close with the enemy keeps falling.","code":"12-4 — 1 The nature of warfare: Continuity and change in the composition of the army"},{"h":"Firepower, mobility and the power to see","t":"Three lines of development run through the century, and it pays to keep them apart before you join them up. Firepower: the machine gun, in British service the belt-fed Vickers, could fire several hundred rounds a minute and, with quick-firing artillery and barbed wire, made attacking far more costly than defending in 1914–18; the Germans released chlorine gas at Ypres in April 1915 and mustard gas followed in 1917, until the Geneva Protocol of 1925 banned the use of chemical weapons in war; the atomic bombs dropped on Hiroshima on 6 August 1945 and Nagasaki on 9 August 1945 let a single aircraft destroy a city. Mobility: tanks were first used by the British at Flers-Courcelette on the Somme on 15 September 1916, when fewer than fifty Mark I machines took part and many broke down, and then in mass at Cambrai on 20 November 1917, where more than three hundred fighting tanks broke into the German defences on the first day; lorries, then armoured vehicles, helicopters and transport aircraft replaced the horse. Sight: aircraft began in 1914 as reconnaissance machines, radar stations along the coast let Fighter Command track German formations in the Battle of Britain in 1940, and satellites, night-vision and unmanned aircraft gave the coalition of 2003 a picture of the battlefield its enemy did not have. A top-level answer shows the three combining: a guided weapon only matters because surveillance finds the target and an aircraft carries it there.","code":"12-4 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of developments in weaponry, transport and surveillance"},{"h":"Volunteers, conscripts, professionals — and women","t":"In August 1914 Britain had a small regular army and depended on volunteers. Lord Kitchener's appeal brought in more than two million men before conscription, many of them into Pals battalions raised from one town, factory or football club, such as the Accrington Pals, so that friends served together and, on a bad day, a town lost its young men together. Volunteering could not keep pace with the casualties of the Western Front: the Derby Scheme of voluntary attestation in 1915 failed, and the Military Service Act of January 1916 conscripted single men aged 18 to 41, extended to married men in May 1916. In the Second World War compulsion came first: limited call-up was introduced in the spring of 1939 and the National Service (Armed Forces) Act of 3 September 1939 made men aged 18 to 41 liable, while from December 1941 single women were directed into the services, civil defence or industry. Peacetime conscription continued under the National Service Act of 1948, in force from 1949, at first for eighteen months and then for two years; the last call-ups were made in 1960 and the last National Servicemen left in 1963, since when the British Army has been an all-volunteer professional force. Women's service widened from the auxiliary corps of the two wars to integration into the regular army in 1992, when the Women's Royal Army Corps was disbanded and its members were absorbed into the corps and regiments, although ground close combat roles stayed closed until they were opened between 2016 and 2018. A top-level answer ties each change to a cause — casualties, the scale of a total war, the cost of a mass army, the skill a modern weapon demands — instead of listing Acts of Parliament.","code":"12-4 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants"},{"h":"The Home Front, the bomb and the news","t":"War reached civilians in three ways, and a good answer separates them. The state took powers over them: the Defence of the Realm Act, passed on 8 August 1914, allowed the government to censor news, requisition factories and land and control daily life down to the opening hours of public houses, with food rationing following in 1918; in the Second World War rationing began in January 1940 and was not lifted completely until 1954, about one and a half million people were moved out of the cities in the first days of September 1939, the blackout was imposed and the Home Guard was raised in May 1940. The enemy attacked them directly: German airships and then bombers killed roughly 1,400 people in Britain in the First World War, while the Blitz from September 1940 to May 1941 killed some 40,000 civilians and bombing killed about 60,000 across the whole war. And war worked on their minds: conscientious objectors, roughly 16,000 in the First World War and some 60,000 in the Second, had to argue their case before tribunals and were treated far more harshly in the first war, when absolutists were imprisoned and objectors lost the vote for five years; after 1945 fear of nuclear attack produced civil defence advice, the Campaign for Nuclear Disarmament in 1958 and protest camps such as Greenham Common from 1981; and reporting moved from censored dispatches and official propaganda to televised war, journalists embedded with units in Iraq in 2003, and public mourning for the dead repatriated through Wootton Bassett between 2007 and 2011. A top-level answer measures 'impact' against a stated criterion — deaths, control of daily life, or opinion — rather than piling up examples.","code":"12-4 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians"},{"h":"The Western Front and the Somme, 1916","t":"By the end of 1914 the Western Front had set into a line of trenches running from the Belgian coast to the Swiss frontier: firing line, support and reserve trenches joined by communication trenches, with barbed wire and machine guns making any advance across no man's land murderous, and with artillery causing most of the casualties. The British attacked on the Somme on 1 July 1916 partly to relieve the French, who had been under attack at Verdun since February. A bombardment of about one and a half million shells over a week was meant to cut the wire and destroy the defenders, but too many shells were shrapnel rather than high explosive and too many failed to explode, while the Germans, holding the higher ground, sheltered in deep dugouts cut into the chalk and brought their machine guns up in the minutes after the barrage lifted. On the first day the British suffered about 57,000 casualties, of whom roughly 19,000 were killed: the worst day in the history of the British Army. The battle lasted until 18 November 1916, cost the British and Commonwealth forces around 400,000 casualties, advanced about six miles, wore down a German army that could not replace its losses, and saw the first use of tanks on 15 September. Sir Douglas Haig, Commander-in-Chief since December 1915, has been attacked as a general who wasted lives and defended as a commander fighting an attritional war with the communications of his day. A top-level answer treats the Somme as a test case in which tactics, technology, terrain and German defences can each be weighed, not as a story about one man.","code":"12-4 — 3 Case studies: The Western Front and the Battle of the Somme (1916)"},{"h":"The Iraq War, 2003 — and what technology could not do","t":"The invasion of Iraq began on 20 March 2003, with about 45,000 British personnel alongside a much larger American force. The coalition opened with air and missile attacks on command centres, communications and air defences, using cruise missiles and satellite- and laser-guided bombs that could be aimed at a single building, while satellites, airborne radar, unmanned aircraft and night-vision equipment tracked Iraqi movements and secure digital links joined units to their commanders. Iraqi forces, beaten in 1991 and worn down by years of sanctions, had obsolete equipment, no air cover and poor morale. British troops took Basra in early April, Baghdad fell on 9 April 2003 and major combat operations were declared over on 1 May. The other half of the case study matters just as much: no weapons of mass destruction were found, an insurgency fought with roadside bombs, snipers and suicide attacks for which precision weapons and satellites were poorly suited, and British forces did not leave southern Iraq until 2009. A top-level answer uses the war in both directions — as evidence that high-tech weaponry and surveillance decide conventional campaigns very quickly, and as evidence that they do not decide what happens once the fighting turns into guerrilla warfare among civilians.","code":"12-4 — 3 Case studies: The Iraq War (2003)"}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 3, 4 marks: one analysed difference, not two descriptions","setup":"Paper 1 Question 3, worth 4 marks: 'Explain one way in which the composition of an English army in the years c1250–c1500 was different from the composition of a British army in the years c1900–present.' The mark scheme gives 2 marks for AO2 and 2 for AO1, so you need one difference, analysed, with specific information about both periods.","steps":["Choose a difference of kind rather than of size: a medieval army was gathered for one campaign and then dispersed, whereas a modern army is a permanent organisation of separate trades.","Evidence the medieval half: knights served because they held land from a lord, foot soldiers and archers were summoned locally, and by the Hundred Years War men were hired under written contracts for a single campaign, with no permanent force kept up in peacetime.","Evidence the modern half: beside the infantry sit the Royal Flying Corps of 1912, the Tank Corps of 1917, the Royal Corps of Signals of 1920 and the Royal Logistic Corps of 1993, together with bomb disposal teams trained for one dangerous trade.","Close with the sentence that makes it analysis: a medieval army was assembled out of society when it was wanted, while a modern army is a standing body of specialists that exists whether or not there is a war."],"cues":["Which single difference is strong enough to carry all four marks?","What named medieval detail proves that half of it?","What named modern detail proves the other half?","What must the final sentence do that the two halves have not done?"]},{"title":"Question 4, 12 marks: using the stimulus and then getting past it","setup":"Paper 1 Question 4, worth 12 marks: 'Explain why the use of aircraft changed warfare in the years c1900–present.' You may use the following in your answer: • the Battle of Britain (1940) • bombing raids. You must also use information of your own. The mark scheme allows a maximum of 8 marks to an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content, and shuts Level 4 to it altogether.","steps":["Turn the first stimulus point into a reason with a causal chain: aircraft made the air itself something that had to be won, so in the summer of 1940 the Royal Air Force had to defeat the Luftwaffe over south-east England before any invasion could be attempted, and coastal radar let Fighter Command send its squadrons to the right place at the right height.","Turn the second into a reason: because aircraft could simply fly over the front line, they carried the war to civilians, as the Blitz from September 1940 to May 1941 did in killing some 40,000 people, so governments had to shelter, feed and organise their populations as well as their armies.","Add the reason of your own that opens Level 4: aircraft changed what commanders could see and how fast forces could move — reconnaissance and photography from 1914 made artillery accurate, transport aircraft and helicopters later carried troops and supplies, and unmanned aircraft watched Iraq in 2003.","Tie the three together in one sentence so that the reasoning is sustained: each use of the aeroplane removed a limit — of sight, of distance, of the front line itself — that had shaped warfare before it."],"cues":["How do you turn a battle named in the stimulus into a reason?","Who, apart from soldiers, is reached once aircraft can fly past the trenches?","Which use of aircraft is nowhere in the stimulus points?","What has the last sentence still got to do?"]},{"title":"Question 5, 16 marks: fixing the criteria before choosing the evidence","setup":"Paper 1 Question 5, worth 16 marks plus up to 4 for SPaG: 'Warfare had a greater impact on British civilians in the years c1900–present than in any earlier period.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • rationing • air raids. You must also use information of your own. Level 4 requires criteria for the judgement to be justified and applied, and there is no access to Level 4 for an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content.","steps":["Fix the measures in the opening lines: impact will be tested by how many civilians were killed, how far the state controlled daily life, and how far ordinary people were compelled to take part in the war effort.","Argue the modern case on the first two measures with dated evidence: German bombing killed roughly 1,400 people in Britain in the First World War and about 60,000 in the Second, while the Defence of the Realm Act of 1914 and rationing from 1940 to 1954 put food, work and news under government control.","Argue the third measure, which the stimulus does not prompt: conscription in 1916 and again in 1939, the direction of single women into the services and industry from December 1941, evacuation, the Home Guard and civil defence made participation compulsory rather than voluntary.","Put the earlier periods properly on the other side of the scale, so that the judgement is a real one: armies once lived off the civilians they marched among, taking free quarter and plundering, towns were besieged and starved, as Colchester was for eleven weeks in 1648, and war taxation fell on people with no say in it.","Judge on the criteria rather than on the number of examples: say on which measure the modern period is plainly worse, on which the earlier centuries can compete, and therefore how far the statement stands."],"cues":["What would have to be true for the modern period to win this argument?","Which dated evidence fits the first two measures?","Which measure will take you beyond the stimulus points?","What did war do to civilians before aircraft existed?","How do you reach a judgement without simply counting paragraphs?"]},{"title":"Diagnosing a weak paragraph and rebuilding it","setup":"A student answering Paper 1 Question 4, 'Explain why the way Britain raised its soldiers changed in the years c1900–1918' (stimulus points: • the Pals battalions • casualties on the Western Front), writes: 'Conscription was introduced because lots of men had died and not enough people were joining up. The Pals battalions were popular at first. This shows why conscription was needed.' Decide which level that paragraph sits in, and rebuild it.","steps":["Diagnose it against the descriptors: it asserts without explaining and offers no precise support, so it is a simple, generalised answer lacking development — Level 1. The stimulus cap is not what holds it there: the caps bite at Levels 3 and 4, and nothing in the grid keeps a Level 1 answer down.","Rebuild the recruitment mechanism: more than two million men volunteered after Lord Kitchener's appeal, but the flow was uneven and fell away through 1915, and the Derby Scheme of voluntary attestation failed to produce the numbers, so compulsion was the only remaining source of men.","Rebuild the casualty mechanism with the Western Front behind it: trench warfare fought with machine guns and heavy artillery consumed men faster than volunteers replaced them, and the Pals battalions made the loss unmistakable at home, because one bad morning could empty a single town's streets and workshops of its young men.","Add the point of your own that escapes the cap: conscription also let the state decide who should not fight, keeping skilled men in the mines, the munitions works and farming, which a voluntary system could never do.","Finish by answering the question in its own words: the way Britain raised its soldiers changed because the supply of volunteers had collapsed while the demand for men had risen, and only the state could match the one to the other."],"cues":["Which level does that paragraph meet, and which words in the descriptor say so?","What was actually happening to volunteering before the Act?","Why did the losses of trench warfare make a difference at home as well as at the front?","Which reason of your own lifts the answer past the stimulus cap?","What has the last sentence still got to do?"]},{"title":"Turning a narrative of the Somme into analysis","setup":"Paper 1 Question 6, worth 16 marks plus up to 4 for SPaG: 'The role of General Haig was the main reason for the outcome of the Battle of the Somme in 1916.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the artillery bombardment • German defences. You must also use information of your own. A student has written a paragraph that narrates the first day hour by hour. Turn that narrative into the analysis the mark scheme rewards.","steps":["Replace the story with a claim about the commander's decisions: Haig approved a plan that assumed the bombardment would destroy the defence, so the infantry were sent forward in line at walking pace with heavy packs.","Test the claim against the artillery evidence: about one and a half million shells were fired over a week, but too many were shrapnel rather than high explosive and too many failed to explode, so much of the wire still stood and the German batteries survived.","Weigh the factor the commander did not control: the Germans held the higher ground and had dug deep dugouts into the chalk, from which their machine gunners emerged in the few minutes between the barrage lifting and the British reaching the wire, which is why about 57,000 men were lost on the first day.","Give the other side of the outcome, which weaker answers forget: the offensive drew German strength away from the French at Verdun, cost the German army losses it could not replace, and saw tanks used for the first time in September.","Judge on a stated criterion: measured by ground gained, the plan and its author carry the blame; measured by the wearing down of the German army and the relief of an ally, the reasons lie mostly outside Haig's choices."],"cues":["What is the difference between telling the story and making a claim?","Which evidence tests whether the plan's key assumption was sound?","What did the defenders bring to the battle that no British order could change?","Is 'the outcome' the same thing as 'the failure'?","What criterion will you judge on, and why that one?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Why did the Pals battalions make casualties so visible at home?","a":"Because a battalion was recruited from one town, workplace or club, so the losses of a single attack fell on one community at once."},{"q":"What did radar allow Fighter Command to do in 1940 that it could not otherwise have done?","a":"Detect German formations while they were still approaching and send fighters to meet them, instead of wasting aircraft and fuel on standing patrols."},{"q":"Why did the Military Service Act of January 1916 become necessary?","a":"Volunteering had fallen below the losses of the Western Front and the Derby Scheme of voluntary attestation in 1915 had failed to produce enough men."},{"q":"In what way did the Iraq War of 2003 show the limits of high-tech weapons?","a":"Precision weapons and surveillance won the conventional campaign in weeks, but they could not defeat the insurgency of roadside bombs and suicide attacks that followed."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 1 Section B, the thematic study, carries 36 of the paper's 52 marks: Question 3 is worth 4 marks, Question 4 is worth 12, and Question 5 or Question 6 is worth 16 marks plus up to 4 additional marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and the use of specialist terminology.","Question 3, 'Explain one way in which … was similar to / different from …', is marked AO2 2 and AO1 2; it normally spans at least a century, so one analysed similarity or difference must be supported with specific information about both named periods.","Question 4 stimulus caps: a maximum of 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content — so at least one developed reason must be your own.","Question 5 or 6 stimulus caps: a maximum of 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; Level 4 also requires the criteria for the judgement to be justified and applied, not merely stated. Pearson's 2025 guidance also states that a Question 5 or 6 may cover either a complete period or at least 200 years, so plan evidence from across the theme.","Timing at roughly a minute a mark inside the 80-minute paper: about 5 minutes on Question 3, about 15 on Question 4 and about 20 on Question 5 or 6, leaving time to check spelling and specialist terms, since the top SPaG band needs consistent accuracy and a wide range of specialist terms."],"questions":[{"q":"Explain one way in which the weapons used in warfare in the years c1700–c1900 were different from the weapons used in warfare in the years c1900–present. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — single-shot weapons fired by a man at an enemy he could see, against automatic, indirect and aerial weapons that kill far beyond sight — supported by named detail from both periods, such as musket and rifle against machine gun, heavy artillery and the atomic bomb.","sol":"The main difference is the distance at which a weapon kills. In c1700–c1900 the infantryman carried a single-shot firearm aimed at men he could see: the smoothbore musket of the eighteenth century, the rifled muskets of the Crimean War of 1853–56 and the breech-loading Martini-Henry adopted in 1871. In c1900–present weapons killed far beyond sight: the Vickers machine gun fired several hundred rounds a minute, heavy artillery shelled trenches from miles behind the line, bombers destroyed streets during the Blitz, and the atomic bombs of August 1945 destroyed a city from a single aircraft. Killing had moved from the line of sight to the map.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask who or what the soldier could actually see when he fired in each period.","Both halves need a named weapon with its date; choose ones that are unlike each other in kind, not merely in quality.","State the difference, prove the earlier side, prove the modern side, then add the sentence that says why the two are different in kind."],"codes":["12-4 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of developments in weaponry, transport and surveillance"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the recruitment of soldiers in the years c1700–c1900 was similar to the recruitment of soldiers in the years since 1963. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed similarity — in both periods the regular army was filled by voluntary enlistment and had to compete with civilian employment — supported by named detail from both, such as recruiting parties and bounties and Cardwell's short-service enlistment of 1870 against today's professional all-volunteer force.","sol":"In both periods men had to be persuaded rather than compelled. In c1700–c1900 the regular army was filled by recruiting parties who offered a bounty and a life of pay and food to men escaping poverty or unemployment, and Cardwell's short-service enlistment of 1870 shortened the term of service to make the army more attractive. Since the last National Servicemen left in 1963 Britain has again had an all-volunteer professional force, recruited by advertising, pay and the offer of a trade. In both periods, unlike the years of conscription from 1916 and again from 1939 to 1960, the army had to compete with civilian employers for men.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what made a man walk into a recruiting office in each period.","A similarity still needs evidence from both halves; find one named measure from the earlier period and the modern position after National Service ended.","State the similarity, prove each half, then close by saying what both armies had to do that a conscript army never does."],"codes":["12-4 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the impact of war on civilians in the years c1500–c1700 was different from the impact of war on civilians in the years c1900–present. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — civilians suffered when an army was physically present, against modern war reaching a whole population through the air and through the state — supported by named detail from both, such as billeting, plunder and the siege of Colchester in 1648 against bombing casualties, rationing and evacuation.","sol":"The difference is whether an army had to be there. In c1500–c1700 civilians suffered when soldiers came to them: troops were billeted on households and took free quarter, armies plundered the countryside for supplies, and towns were besieged and starved, as Colchester was for eleven weeks in 1648 during the Civil War. In c1900–present war reached civilians with no soldier in sight: German airships and bombers killed roughly 1,400 people in Britain in the First World War and bombing killed about 60,000 in the Second, while the state reorganised daily life through rationing, evacuation and censorship. War had become something done to a whole population, not by a passing army.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what had to happen before a civilian in each period was affected by a war.","Use one dated example of an army among civilians, and one dated example of civilians attacked or controlled without an army near them.","State the difference, evidence both periods, then finish with the sentence that explains why the two experiences are different in kind."],"codes":["12-4 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the use of transport in warfare in the years c1250–c1500 was different from the use of transport in warfare in the years c1900–present. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — muscle-powered movement at walking pace, limited by roads and forage, against engine-powered movement by lorry, tank, aircraft and helicopter — supported by named detail from both periods.","sol":"Transport changed from muscle to engine, and with it the reach of an army. In c1250–c1500 armies moved at walking pace: knights rode horses, supplies came by ox-cart, and ships carried an army across the Channel to France, so a campaign was limited by how far men could march and by what the countryside could feed. In c1900–present engines carried the army: lorries and railways moved men and shells to the Western Front, tanks from 1916 carried firepower over broken ground, and transport aircraft and helicopters moved troops and supplies, so a British force could be flown to Iraq in 2003. Distance no longer set the limits of what an army could attempt.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what actually moved an army and its food in each period.","Pick named transport from each half, and remember supply as well as fighting troops.","State the difference, evidence both halves, then say what the change did to the distance and speed at which a war could be fought."],"codes":["12-4 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of developments in weaponry, transport and surveillance"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the reporting of war in the years c1700–c1900 was different from the reporting of war in the years c1900–present. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — independent newspaper reporting that embarrassed governments, against reporting controlled and used by the state — supported by named detail such as William Howard Russell's uncensored Crimean dispatches from 1854 against the Defence of the Realm Act of 1914, the Ministry of Information and embedded journalists in 2003.","sol":"The difference is government control. In c1700–c1900 reporting lay in private hands: William Howard Russell sent dispatches to The Times from the Crimea from 1854, they were printed uncensored, and his accounts of neglected wounded men embarrassed the government into reform. In c1900–present governments managed the news: the Defence of the Realm Act of 1914 allowed censorship, official correspondents and propaganda films covered the First World War, the Ministry of Information directed news in the Second, and journalists were placed with units under military control in the Falklands in 1982 and in Iraq in 2003. Reporting had become part of the war effort rather than a check on it.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask who decided what the public at home was allowed to know.","One named reporter or paper for the earlier period, one named law or body for the modern period.","State the difference, evidence both halves, then explain what the change did to the relationship between government and the public."],"codes":["12-4 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians"]},{"q":"Explain why the recruitment of soldiers changed in the years c1900–present. [12 marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Pals battalions\n• National Service\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit explained reasons such as the collapse of voluntary recruitment against the casualties of industrial war (Pals battalions, Derby Scheme, Military Service Act 1916), the scale of total war and post-war commitments (conscription 1939, National Service 1949–60), and, beyond the stimulus, the technical skill modern weapons demand and the widening of recruitment to women. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"Recruitment changed because what the army needed from its men changed three times over.\n\nFirst, voluntary recruitment could not stand the casualties of industrial war. In 1914 Britain had a small regular army and relied on volunteers: more than two million men came forward after Lord Kitchener's appeal, many into Pals battalions raised from a single town, factory or club, such as the Accrington Pals. The system's strength was also its weakness, because losses fell on one community at once, and volunteering fell away as the cost of the Western Front became clear. When the Derby Scheme of voluntary attestation failed in 1915, the Military Service Act of January 1916 conscripted single men aged 18 to 41, extended to married men in May.\n\nSecond, total war and the commitments that followed it made compulsion permanent. In 1939 the government did not wait for volunteers: men aged 18 to 41 were made liable in September 1939, and from December 1941 single women were directed into the services, civil defence or industry. Peacetime conscription then continued under the National Service Act of 1948, in force from 1949, taking young men for eighteen months and then for two years to garrison an empire, occupy Germany and fight in Korea.\n\nThird, and this is the reason the stimulus does not give, weapons became too complicated for short-service conscripts. A National Serviceman could be taught to march and shoot in weeks, but tanks, radar, aircraft, signals and later guided weapons needed men who stayed long enough to master them, and skilled men were cheaper to keep in a small force than in a mass army. The last call-ups were made in 1960 and the last National Servicemen left in 1963, since when the army has been all-volunteer and professional, widening its recruiting ground by integrating women in 1992 and opening every ground close combat role between 2016 and 2018.\n\nRecruitment therefore followed need: compulsion while numbers decided wars, and volunteering once skill did.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Each change of system was an answer to a problem the previous system could not solve — identify the problem first.","Work in three reasons, each a paragraph that states the reason, gives dated evidence and then explains the chain back to the way men were recruited.","One reason must lie outside the two stimulus points, or the mark scheme shuts Level 4; ask what a modern soldier has to be able to do that a 1916 recruit did not."],"codes":["12-4 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","12-4 — 1 The nature of warfare: Continuity and change in the composition of the army"]},{"q":"Explain why the impact of war on British civilians increased in the years c1900–1945. [12 marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Defence of the Realm Act (1914)\n• the Blitz (1940–41)\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit explained reasons such as the growth of state powers over daily life (DORA 1914, rationing, requisitioning), the arrival of air attack on cities (Zeppelins and Gothas, the Blitz and its casualties), and, beyond the stimulus, the mobilisation of the whole population for war production, including the conscription of women from December 1941, with propaganda and censorship shaping opinion. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"Civilians mattered more to war after 1900 because the state, the enemy and the economy all reached them directly.\n\nThe first reason is the growth of government power over ordinary life. The Defence of the Realm Act, passed on 8 August 1914, let the government censor newspapers and letters, requisition factories, land and ships, and regulate everyday behaviour down to the opening hours of public houses; food rationing followed in 1918. The same machinery was rebuilt and enlarged in 1939, and rationing that began in January 1940 was not lifted completely until 1954. Once the government could tell civilians what to eat, where to work and what they might read, the war was inside every household.\n\nThe second reason is that civilians became targets. German airships from 1915 and Gotha bombers from 1917 killed roughly 1,400 people in Britain, which was shocking precisely because civilians had not been attacked at home for centuries. Aircraft then grew far more destructive: the Blitz from September 1940 to May 1941 killed some 40,000 people, and bombing killed about 60,000 across the war, while evacuation moved about one and a half million people out of the cities in the first days of September 1939 and the blackout and the shelters altered every night at home.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond the stimulus: industrial war had to be produced as well as fought. Shells, aircraft and ships came from civilian factories, so the government took control of labour, brought women into munitions in the First World War and, from December 1941, conscripted single women into the services, civil defence or industry, while the Home Guard raised in May 1940 put civilians into uniform. Propaganda and censorship were needed to keep that effort going, which is why the Ministry of Information managed news and posters urged people to dig for victory and to guard their tongues.\n\nThe impact grew, then, because total war needed civilians as producers, could reach them as targets, and gave the state the powers to organise them as both.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Think about the three different people who now had a claim on a civilian: the government, the enemy and the factory.","Three paragraphs, each with dated evidence and a chain of reasoning that ends at the civilian, not at the government.","One reason has to come from outside the two stimulus points: ask where the shells and aircraft of a total war actually came from."],"codes":["12-4 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians","12-4 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of developments in weaponry, transport and surveillance"]},{"q":"Explain why developments in surveillance changed warfare in the years c1900–present. [12 marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• radar\n• the Iraq War (2003)\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit explained reasons such as early warning making defence efficient (Chain Home radar and the Battle of Britain 1940), surveillance making precision weapons usable (satellites, unmanned aircraft and guided bombs in Iraq 2003), and, beyond the stimulus, aerial reconnaissance and photography from 1914 making artillery accurate and ending surprise on the Western Front; credit also the limits of surveillance against guerrillas. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"Seeing the enemy first has become as decisive as out-shooting him, for three connected reasons.\n\nThe first is early warning. Radar stations along the south and east coasts could detect German formations as they gathered and approached in 1940, and the information was passed to Fighter Command, which sent squadrons to the right place at the right height instead of wasting fuel and aircraft on standing patrols. A smaller air force could therefore meet a larger one on favourable terms, and the Battle of Britain in 1940 showed that information could compensate for numbers. Radar changed the shape of the battle as much as any weapon did.\n\nThe second is that surveillance makes precision weapons worth having. A satellite-guided bomb is only as good as the target list behind it, so in Iraq in 2003 satellites, airborne radar aircraft, night-vision equipment and unmanned aircraft built a picture of Iraqi command centres, air defences and troop movements, while secure digital links passed it to units in the field. Coalition forces could fight at night and in sandstorms, and Iraqi commanders could not move without being seen; Baghdad fell on 9 April 2003, only three weeks after the invasion began.\n\nThe third reason is older than the stimulus suggests. Aircraft went to war in 1914 as reconnaissance machines, and aerial photography quickly made artillery accurate against targets the gunners could not see, mapped trench systems and made surprise attacks hard to prepare — which is one reason the Western Front settled into deadlock, since neither side could concentrate unobserved.\n\nThere is also a limit worth stating, because it sharpens the argument. Surveillance works against armies that look like armies. Against the insurgents who fought the coalition in Iraq after 2003 with roadside bombs and suicide attacks, hidden among civilians, satellites and drones found far less, which is why the conventional campaign lasted weeks and the fighting afterwards lasted years. Surveillance changed warfare by rewarding whoever could see the battlefield, but only where there was a battlefield to see.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Surveillance is not a weapon; ask what it lets a commander do with the weapons he already has.","Three reasons, each with dated evidence, each ending by explaining what changed about the fighting itself.","Go back before radar for your own reason, and consider saying where surveillance stops working."],"codes":["12-4 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of developments in weaponry, transport and surveillance","12-4 — 3 Case studies: The Iraq War (2003)"]},{"q":"Explain why the British army became more specialised in the years c1900–present. [12 marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Royal Logistic Corps\n• bomb disposal\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit explained reasons such as the supply demands of mass mechanised armies (Army Service Corps, Royal Logistic Corps 1993), new enemies and hidden weapons requiring trained teams (unexploded bombs in the Blitz, Northern Ireland from 1969, IEDs in Iraq and Afghanistan), and, beyond the stimulus, the arrival of machines needing whole new arms (Royal Flying Corps 1912, Tank Corps 1917, RAF 1918, Royal Signals 1920) and the end of conscription in 1960–63 producing a small professional force of tradesmen. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"The army split into trades because its machines, its supplies and its enemies each demanded people who did nothing else.\n\nFirst, mechanised mass armies have to be supplied. A division that runs on shells, petrol and tinned food cannot forage as a medieval army did, so the Army Service Corps, founded in 1888 and given the title Royal in 1918, grew enormously to feed and move the armies of the First World War. In 1993 the Royal Logistic Corps was formed by merging transport, ordnance, pioneer, catering and postal services, and it is now the largest corps in the British Army. The more industrial war became, the more soldiers were needed behind the fighting line rather than in it.\n\nSecond, new enemies produced new trades, of which bomb disposal is the clearest. Royal Engineers sections defused unexploded German bombs in British cities during the Blitz of 1940–41. In Northern Ireland, where the army served from 1969 to 2007, teams faced home-made devices and from 1972 handled them with the remote-controlled Wheelbarrow vehicle rather than by hand. In Iraq and Afghanistan improvised explosive devices became the main killer of British soldiers, so explosive ordnance disposal teams of the Royal Logistic Corps and the engineers were as important as infantry.\n\nThird, and beyond the stimulus, the machines themselves needed whole new arms. Aircraft brought the Royal Flying Corps in 1912 and then the Royal Air Force as a separate service on 1 April 1918; tanks brought the Tank Corps in 1917; radio brought the Royal Corps of Signals in 1920. None of these could be manned by a soldier trained for a few weeks with a rifle.\n\nThat last point explains the final change. While National Service lasted, from 1949 to 1960, the army had large numbers of short-service men; once the last National Servicemen left in 1963 it became a small all-volunteer force in which almost every soldier is trained for a trade. Specialisation, then, is the army's answer to a kind of war in which knowing how to work a machine matters more than numbers.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what a soldier has to know now that a soldier in 1900 did not.","Three reasons, each with named corps and dates, each explaining why that job could not be left to an ordinary infantryman.","Look for a reason beyond supply and bombs: what happened to the army when a new machine arrived?"],"codes":["12-4 — 1 The nature of warfare: Continuity and change in the composition of the army","12-4 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants"]},{"q":"Explain why nuclear weapons changed warfare in the years after 1945. [12 marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the atomic bomb (1945)\n• the Cold War\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit explained reasons such as the destructive power of a single weapon ending the idea of a winnable war between great powers (Hiroshima and Nagasaki, August 1945), deterrence reshaping armed forces and spending (Britain's atomic bomb 1952, hydrogen bomb 1957, Polaris and Trident submarines), and, beyond the stimulus, the effect on civilians — civil defence, CND from 1958, protest — and the survival of conventional and guerrilla war because nuclear weapons could not be used. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"Nuclear weapons changed warfare less by being used than by existing.\n\nThe first reason is the scale of destruction one aircraft could now cause. The atomic bombs dropped on Hiroshima on 6 August 1945 and on Nagasaki on 9 August 1945 destroyed cities outright and killed tens of thousands of people at a stroke. Against such weapons the traditional purposes of armed forces — defending territory, winning battles, occupying the enemy's country — made little sense between the powers that held them, because a war fought to victory would leave the victor's cities in ruins too.\n\nThe second reason is that armed forces were rebuilt around deterrence. Britain tested its first atomic bomb in 1952 and a hydrogen bomb in 1957, joined the American-led western alliance, and from the late 1960s put the deterrent in submarines with Polaris, replaced by Trident in the 1990s, so that missiles could not be destroyed on the ground. The purpose of these forces was not to fight but to convince an opponent that attacking would be suicidal, which is why the Cold War stayed cold even at its most dangerous, as in the Cuban missile crisis of 1962.\n\nThe third reason belongs to civilians and is not prompted by the stimulus. Nuclear weapons put the whole population in the front line permanently and in peacetime. Governments issued civil defence advice about shelter and survival, and public fear produced organised protest: the Campaign for Nuclear Disarmament was founded in 1958 and women camped outside the missile base at Greenham Common from 1981. For the first time, the argument about a weapon became a major part of domestic politics.\n\nFinally, the change had limits worth stating. Because nuclear weapons were unusable in practice, conventional armies went on fighting smaller wars — Korea, the Falklands in 1982, Iraq in 2003 — and guerrilla fighters could not be deterred by them at all. Nuclear weapons abolished total war between the great powers while leaving every other kind of warfare in place.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["A weapon that is never fired can still change how armies are built; start from what it made impossible.","Three reasons, each with dates, and make each one end at a change in how war was prepared for or fought.","Your own reason can look away from the armed forces altogether: who else lived with the bomb every day?"],"codes":["12-4 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of developments in weaponry, transport and surveillance","12-4 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians"]},{"q":"'Technology was the main reason for the outcome of the battles and wars Britain fought in the years c1900–present.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Battle of the Somme (1916)\n• the Iraq War (2003)\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit an argued judgement weighing what technology decided (the failure of the Somme bombardment in 1916 and the first use of tanks; radar and Fighter Command in 1940; guided weapons, satellites and unmanned aircraft in Iraq in 2003) against the other reasons for an outcome — the state of the enemy, the training and numbers of the troops, generalship and logistics — and, beyond the stimulus, the guerrilla warfare of Northern Ireland and of Iraq after May 2003, which technology could not decide. Criteria for judging 'the outcome' must be justified and applied. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"'The outcome' has to be defined before anything can be weighed. A battle or a war can be judged by the ground it takes, by the damage it does to the enemy's power to fight on, or by whether it settles what it was fought about. Technology must be tested against the other candidates: the state of the enemy, the troops' training and numbers, and the commanders who used them.\n\nThe Somme shows technology deciding an outcome by failing. About one and a half million shells were fired in the week before 1 July 1916, but too many were shrapnel, which could not collapse a dugout or reliably cut wire, and too many failed to explode, so German machine gunners came up from dugouts cut deep into the chalk in the minutes after the barrage lifted and the British suffered about 57,000 casualties in a day, roughly 19,000 of them killed. Technology also supplied answers: tanks were used for the first time at Flers-Courcelette on 15 September 1916 and the creeping barrage worked better later in the battle. Measured by ground — about six miles by 18 November 1916 — the shells and the guns explain the outcome better than any order given on the day.\n\nIraq in 2003 appears to prove the statement outright. Cruise missiles and guided bombs destroyed command centres, communications and air defences; satellites, airborne radar and unmanned aircraft gave coalition commanders a picture of the battlefield their opponents did not have; satellite navigation let ground forces move at night and in sandstorms. Baghdad fell on 9 April 2003, three weeks after the invasion began. But the enemy counts as much as the equipment. The Iraqi army had been beaten in 1991 and worn down by more than a decade of sanctions, its equipment was obsolete, its air force could not fly against the coalition and many units dispersed.\n\nThe Battle of Britain, which neither stimulus point prompts, is the sharpest test. Radar stations along the south and east coasts let Fighter Command detect German formations as they approached in 1940, so a smaller air force could meet a larger one where it mattered instead of wearing itself out on patrols. Even there it was only useful because of the organisation that turned the reports into orders and the aircraft production and trained pilots behind it.\n\nAgainst all this stands the fighting technology could not settle. In Iraq after May 2003 British soldiers faced improvised explosive devices, snipers and suicide attacks from among the civilian population, and left the south only in 2009; in Northern Ireland, where the army was deployed from 1969 to 2007, the answer to hidden bombs was the remote-controlled Wheelbarrow of 1972, armour and patient patrolling, not a war-winning machine.\n\nThe criterion decides the answer. Measured by the outcome of a conventional battle or campaign, technology is the main reason: it explains the first day of the Somme and the three weeks to the fall of Baghdad better than courage, numbers or generalship. Measured by the outcome of a war — whether the fighting achieved what it was for — it is not the main reason at all, because no weapon in this period has decided a guerrilla war among civilians. The statement holds for battles and fails for wars.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide what 'the outcome' means — ground taken, an enemy worn down, or a war actually settled — before you weigh anything.","Take conflicts from both ends of the period and ask of each whether the technology or the enemy it was used against explains what happened.","Open with the Somme, where the technology of 1916 failed, set the campaign of 2003 beside it, and keep one conflict of your own for the point that technology decided nothing."],"codes":["12-4 — 3 Case studies: The Western Front and the Battle of the Somme (1916)","12-4 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of developments in weaponry, transport and surveillance","12-4 — 3 Case studies: The Iraq War (2003)"]},{"q":"'Changes in weapons mattered more than changes in recruitment in shaping the experience of British soldiers in the years c1900–present.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• trench warfare on the Western Front\n• the Iraq War (2003)\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit an argued judgement weighing weapons (machine guns, artillery and gas producing trench warfare; guided weapons, armour and surveillance in 2003; the threat of IEDs) against recruitment and training (Pals battalions and conscription producing mass citizen armies of half-trained men; National Service; the long-trained professional force since 1963, including women), with at least one factor of its own such as medical care, logistics or communications. Criteria for judging 'experience' must be justified and applied. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"A soldier's experience means three things here: the danger he faces, the training he brings to it, and the company he keeps. Weapons decide the first, recruitment shapes the second and third, and the argument turns on which of them changed the soldier's life most.\n\nThe case for weapons is strongest for 1914–18. Machine guns firing several hundred rounds a minute, quick-firing artillery and barbed wire made movement above ground suicidal, so armies dug in, and the experience of the Western Front — mud, lice, rats, trench foot, shellfire that killed men who never saw an enemy, and the terror of gas after chlorine was released at Ypres in April 1915 — was created by the weapons rather than by anything about the men. Artillery, not rifle fire, caused most casualties, which meant that courage and skill often made no difference to who lived. No change in recruitment could have altered that.\n\nWeapons shaped the modern soldier's experience just as firmly, though differently. In Iraq in 2003 British troops fought inside armoured vehicles with satellite navigation, night-vision and air support on call, and the conventional campaign was over in weeks: Baghdad fell on 9 April 2003. The danger then changed shape rather than disappearing, because the insurgency that followed used roadside bombs, so a patrol's greatest risk came from a device buried in a road, and bomb disposal teams became as important as riflemen.\n\nThe case for recruitment is stronger than it first looks. The army of 1916 was a citizen army: more than two million volunteers came forward after Kitchener's appeal, many into Pals battalions raised from one town or factory, and conscription followed under the Military Service Act of January 1916. That produced a force of half-trained civilians whose training lasted months and whose comrades were neighbours, so a single attack could devastate a community at home. Compare the soldier of 2003: a volunteer since National Service ended in 1960–63, trained for years in a trade, serving alongside women who had been integrated into the regular army in 1992. His skills, his expectations and his relationship with the society he came from were entirely different, and none of that follows from weapons alone.\n\nA third factor, prompted by neither stimulus point, is the army's ability to look after its soldiers. Medical services, casualty evacuation by helicopter, body armour and vastly better supply — the work of the Royal Logistic Corps formed in 1993 — mean that a wounded man today has a chance of survival unthinkable in 1916. Experience is not only about what can kill you but about what happens next.\n\nMy judgement is that weapons set the conditions and recruitment decided how men met them, but the two are not equal in weight at the same moments. In the First World War weapons dominated, because the technology of killing had outrun tactics and no soldier's training could answer a barrage. Since 1963 recruitment matters more, because a small professional force chooses, trains and equips its soldiers precisely to handle the weapons they will meet, and the risk to any individual soldier is far lower. The statement is true of the first half of the period and much weaker for the second.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Break 'experience' into parts before you argue, or the essay will drift into a list of weapons.","Test each factor at two different dates in the period, and ask whether the other factor could have changed the outcome at that date.","Add a factor the stimulus does not mention about what happens to a soldier after he is hit, and let the judgement change across the period rather than staying the same."],"codes":["12-4 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","12-4 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of developments in weaponry, transport and surveillance","12-4 — 3 Case studies: The Iraq War (2003)"]},{"q":"'The Second World War had a greater impact on British civilians than the First World War.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Defence of the Realm Act (1914)\n• evacuation\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit an argued judgement weighing casualties from bombing (roughly 1,400 in the First World War against about 60,000 in the Second), state control of daily life (DORA 1914 and rationing 1918 against rationing from 1940 to 1954, evacuation, the blackout and the Home Guard), and, beyond the stimulus, the conscription of women from December 1941, propaganda and censorship, and the loss of soldiers' lives felt in the home communities of the Pals battalions. Criteria for 'impact' must be justified and applied. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"Impact can be measured in three ways: how many civilians were killed, how far daily life was controlled, and how far ordinary people had to take part. The answer changes depending on which measure is chosen, and that is what makes the statement arguable.\n\nOn deaths among civilians the Second World War is far worse, and the gap is not close. German airships from 1915 and Gotha bombers from 1917 killed roughly 1,400 people in Britain, a shock precisely because it was new. In the Second World War the Blitz from September 1940 to May 1941 killed some 40,000 civilians, and bombing killed about 60,000 across the war as a whole, destroying homes on a scale that no raid of 1917 approached. Bombing also reached cities far from London — Coventry, Liverpool, Clydebank, Plymouth — so the experience was national.\n\nOn control of daily life the two wars are closer than students expect, because the second built on the machinery of the first. The Defence of the Realm Act of 8 August 1914 already allowed the government to censor newspapers and letters, requisition factories and land, direct labour into munitions and regulate everyday behaviour, and food rationing arrived in 1918. What the Second World War added was depth and duration: rationing began in January 1940, covered clothes as well as food, and was not lifted completely until 1954, nine years after the fighting stopped. Evacuation moved about one and a half million people, mostly children, out of the cities in the first days of September 1939, breaking up families in a way the First World War never did, while the blackout, the shelters and the Home Guard raised in May 1940 altered every evening at home.\n\nOn participation, which neither stimulus point prompts, the Second World War again goes further, but not absolutely. Both wars conscripted men — in January 1916 and in September 1939 — and both drew women into munitions work. Only the second, however, conscripted women directly, from December 1941, directing single women into the services, civil defence or industry, and only the second put more than a million civilians into the Home Guard.\n\nThere is a serious argument on the other side. Military deaths in the First World War were far heavier, and because of the Pals battalions they fell on particular streets and towns all at once, so the grief of 1916 was concentrated in a way that the losses of 1939–45 were not. For many families the impact of the First World War was the permanent absence of the men who did not return, which is not captured by counting civilian casualties.\n\nJudged on civilian deaths, control of daily life and compulsory participation, the statement stands: the Second World War reached British civilians more deeply and for longer, and the clearest proof is that rationing outlasted the war by nine years. Judged on bereavement, the First World War may have struck harder. The statement is therefore correct on the criteria the question is really about, but only if 'impact on civilians' is distinguished from the losses their soldiers suffered.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Set out how you will measure impact before you compare anything, or the essay becomes two descriptions side by side.","Take each measure in turn and put both wars inside the same paragraph with dated evidence, ending with an interim verdict.","Find the strongest point against your own case from outside the stimulus, then make the conclusion say on which measure the statement holds and on which it does not."],"codes":["12-4 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians","12-4 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants"]},{"q":"'Developments in surveillance changed the way wars were fought more than developments in weapons in the years c1900–present.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• aerial reconnaissance\n• the machine gun\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit an argued judgement weighing surveillance (reconnaissance aircraft and aerial photography from 1914 making artillery accurate, radar and Fighter Command in 1940, satellites, unmanned aircraft and night-vision in Iraq in 2003) against weapons (the machine gun, quick-firing artillery and gas producing trench deadlock, the tank, the atomic bombs of August 1945 and guided weapons), and, beyond the stimulus, the argument that the two only work together, since a guided weapon needs a target found for it, and the failure of both against guerrillas. Criteria for judging how far the way wars are fought changed must be justified and applied. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"Two kinds of development are being compared, so the measure has to be stated: which of them changed where armies could go, what commanders could know, and how wars ended? The answer is not the same at every date in the period.\n\nThe case for surveillance begins in 1914. Aircraft went to war as reconnaissance machines, and aerial photography made artillery accurate against targets the gunners could not see, mapped whole trench systems and made surprise hard to achieve on the Western Front. That changed the way the war was fought at its root, and it is part of the reason the bombardments grew so enormous: about one and a half million shells before 1 July 1916. Radar carried the change further. Stations along the south and east coasts let Fighter Command detect German formations as they approached in 1940, so a smaller air force could meet a larger one where it mattered instead of wasting aircraft and fuel on patrols. By 2003 satellites, airborne radar, unmanned aircraft and night-vision equipment gave coalition commanders in Iraq a picture of the battlefield their opponents did not have, and satellite navigation let ground forces fight at night and through sandstorms.\n\nThe case for weapons is at least as strong at the start of the period. The machine gun, firing several hundred rounds a minute, with quick-firing artillery and barbed wire, made movement above ground suicidal and pushed the armies of 1914 into a line of trenches from the Belgian coast to the Swiss frontier; chlorine gas at Ypres in April 1915 and mustard gas in 1917 added a terror that no amount of observation could answer. Nothing surveillance offered in those years changed warfare as completely as that.\n\nThe strongest case of all for weapons lies beyond the stimulus. The atomic bombs dropped on Hiroshima on 6 August 1945 and Nagasaki on 9 August 1945 let a single aircraft destroy a city, and once Britain had tested its own atomic bomb in 1952 and a hydrogen bomb in 1957 its armed forces existed to deter a war rather than win one. That is a change in the purpose of fighting, which no development in surveillance has matched.\n\nFor most of the period, though, the two cannot be separated. A guided bomb that can be aimed at a single building is useless unless something has found the building, and the campaign of 2003 was quick — Baghdad fell on 9 April, three weeks after the invasion began — because the finding and the striking had been joined together. The two failed together as well: against the insurgency that followed, a satellite could not see intention and a precision weapon had no target it could identify from the air.\n\nMy judgement is that the statement is half true, and that the halves fall either side of 1945. Between 1900 and 1945 weapons changed warfare more, because the machine gun made the trenches and the atomic bomb ended the idea of a winnable war between great powers. Since 1945 surveillance has changed it more, because what a commander can see now decides what he can strike and how quickly — and because the wars technology has not settled are precisely the ones in which the enemy cannot be seen.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first what 'the way wars are fought' means — where armies can go, what commanders can know, or how wars end.","Test both claims at the same three dates, 1916, 1940 and 2003, so that the comparison is argued rather than asserted.","Begin with what reconnaissance did to artillery on the Western Front, and keep the atomic bomb back as the strongest case for the other side."],"codes":["12-4 — 3 Case studies: The Iraq War (2003)","12-4 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of developments in weaponry, transport and surveillance"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In which month and year was conscription first introduced in Britain during the First World War?","a":"January 1916, by the Military Service Act (single men aged 18 to 41)."},{"q":"In which battle of November 1917 were tanks first used in large numbers?","a":"The Battle of Cambrai, which began on 20 November 1917."},{"q":"About how many British casualties were suffered on the first day of the Battle of the Somme, 1 July 1916?","a":"About 57,000 casualties, of whom roughly 19,000 were killed."},{"q":"Which technology allowed RAF Fighter Command to detect approaching German aircraft during the Battle of Britain in 1940?","a":"Radar, from the chain of stations along the south and east coasts."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Machine guns on the Western Front","back":"The belt-fed Vickers, and its German equivalents, fired several hundred rounds a minute. With barbed wire and quick-firing artillery it made attacking across open ground far more costly than defending it.","code":"12-4 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of developments in weaponry, transport and surveillance","open":false},{"front":"The first use of tanks, 15 September 1916","back":"British Mark I tanks went into action at Flers-Courcelette on the Somme. Fewer than fifty took part and many broke down; massed tank attack came at Cambrai on 20 November 1917.","code":"12-4 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of developments in weaponry, transport and surveillance","open":false},{"front":"The first day of the Somme, 1 July 1916","back":"About 57,000 British casualties, roughly 19,000 of them killed: the worst day in the history of the British Army. A week's bombardment had failed to cut the wire or destroy the deep German dugouts.","code":"12-4 — 3 Case studies: The Western Front and the Battle of the Somme (1916)","open":false},{"front":"Poison gas in the First World War","back":"Chlorine was released by the Germans at Ypres in April 1915 and mustard gas followed in 1917. Respirators limited the deaths, and the Geneva Protocol of 1925 banned the use of chemical weapons in war.","code":"12-4 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of developments in weaponry, transport and surveillance","open":false},{"front":"Radar in the Battle of Britain, 1940","back":"Stations along the south and east coasts detected German formations as they approached, so Fighter Command could send squadrons to meet them instead of wasting aircraft and fuel on standing patrols.","code":"12-4 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of developments in weaponry, transport and surveillance","open":false},{"front":"The atomic bomb and the nuclear deterrent","back":"Atomic bombs destroyed Hiroshima on 6 August 1945 and Nagasaki on 9 August 1945. Britain tested its own atomic bomb in 1952 and a hydrogen bomb in 1957, later carrying the deterrent in submarines.","code":"12-4 — 1 The nature of warfare: The impact on warfare of developments in weaponry, transport and surveillance","open":false},{"front":"The Iraq War, 2003","back":"The invasion began on 20 March 2003; Baghdad fell on 9 April and major combat operations were declared over on 1 May. An insurgency followed, and British forces left southern Iraq in 2009.","code":"12-4 — 3 Case studies: The Iraq War (2003)","open":false},{"front":"The Royal Logistic Corps","back":"Formed in 1993 by merging transport, ordnance, pioneer, catering and postal services. It is the largest corps in the British Army and moves, feeds and supplies the whole force.","code":"12-4 — 1 The nature of warfare: Continuity and change in the composition of the army","open":false},{"front":"Bomb disposal units","back":"Royal Engineers defused unexploded German bombs during the Blitz; teams in Northern Ireland used the remote-controlled Wheelbarrow from 1972; explosive ordnance disposal teams faced improvised explosive devices in Iraq and Afghanistan.","code":"12-4 — 1 The nature of warfare: Continuity and change in the composition of the army","open":false},{"front":"The Pals battalions","back":"Units raised from 1914 out of one town, factory or club, such as the Accrington Pals, so that friends served together. Heavy losses in a single attack therefore fell on one community at once.","code":"12-4 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","open":false},{"front":"The Military Service Act, January 1916","back":"Conscripted single men aged 18 to 41, extended to married men in May 1916, after volunteering fell away and the Derby Scheme of 1915 failed to replace the losses of the Western Front.","code":"12-4 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","open":false},{"front":"National Service, 1949–1960","back":"Peacetime conscription under the National Service Act of 1948: young men served eighteen months, later two years. The last call-ups were made in 1960 and the last National Servicemen left in 1963.","code":"12-4 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","open":false},{"front":"Women in the modern armed forces","back":"Auxiliary corps served in both world wars, and single women were conscripted from December 1941. Women were integrated into the regular army in 1992, and all ground close combat roles opened between 2016 and 2018.","code":"12-4 — 2 The experience of war: The recruitment and training of combatants","open":false},{"front":"The Defence of the Realm Act, 1914","back":"Passed on 8 August 1914. It allowed the government to censor news and letters, requisition factories, land and ships, direct labour and control daily life down to public house opening hours.","code":"12-4 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians","open":false},{"front":"The Blitz, 1940–41","back":"German bombing of British cities from September 1940 to May 1941 killed some 40,000 civilians. The blackout, shelters, evacuation and civil defence reorganised civilian life around air attack.","code":"12-4 — 2 The experience of war: The impact of war on civilians","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 1 Question 5, 16 marks: 'The strength of the German defences was the main reason for the outcome of the Battle of the Somme (1916).')","items":["The German defences explain the first day better than any other single factor, but they only proved decisive because the British bombardment failed to remove them. The Germans had held this ground since 1914 and had chosen the higher ground, so their observers could see the British lines and their guns were registered on them in advance; their dugouts were cut deep into the chalk, so a bombardment could shake the men below without killing them. That is why the few minutes between the barrage lifting and the British infantry reaching the wire decided so much: machine gunners climbed the stairs, set up and fired into troops advancing in line at walking pace across open ground, and the British suffered about 57,000 casualties on 1 July 1916, roughly 19,000 of them killed. Yet depth of shelter was only fatal because the shells sent to destroy it were the wrong ones. About one and a half million were fired over a week, an unprecedented weight of fire, but too many were shrapnel, which could not collapse a dugout or reliably cut wire, and too many were badly manufactured and failed to explode, because British industry had not yet learned to make good shells in millions. Had the wire been cut and the batteries silenced, the same dugouts would have mattered far less. Judged by the casualties of the first day, then, the German defences were the main reason for the outcome — but the reason they were allowed to work was a British failure of artillery and of munitions production, so the statement needs qualifying rather than accepting.","Why this reaches Level 4: the claim is directed at the conceptual focus (the reason for the outcome) and not at describing the battle; the evidence is precisely selected and dated (1 July 1916, about 57,000 casualties, roughly 19,000 killed, about one and a half million shells, shrapnel and dud shells, dugouts in the chalk); the reasoning is a chain — high ground and deep dugouts, the minutes after the barrage lifts, advancing in line, the casualty figure — rather than a list; a counter-factor is weighed inside the same paragraph; and the criterion for the judgement (casualties on the first day) is stated and applied, which is exactly what the third bullet of the Level 4 descriptor requires — the bullet that carries the fourth mark in the level."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["The German defences were very strong and this was the main reason for what happened at the Somme. They had good trenches and deep dugouts, so when the British attacked they were ready for them. The British soldiers had to walk towards machine guns and thousands of them were killed on the first day, which was a disaster. The bombardment was also supposed to help but it did not work very well. Haig has been blamed for this because he was in charge. Overall I agree that the German defences were the main reason for the outcome of the battle.","Why this is only Level 2: there is an explanation and a judgement, so it clears Level 1, and it meets the Level 2 descriptor of 'an explanation … showing limited analysis and with implicit or unsustained links to the conceptual focus', with 'the overall judgement … given but its justification … asserted'. It misses Level 3 and Level 4 because the information is generalised — no date beyond 'the first day', no casualty figure, no number or type of shells, nothing about the chalk, the higher ground or the timing of the barrage — and because the other factors are named ('the bombardment did not work', 'Haig has been blamed') without being weighed against the claim. No criterion for the judgement is stated, so the last sentence repeats the question instead of answering it. Note also that both the points it uses are the ones the stimulus prompts: that no longer holds an answer down inside Level 2, but until three or more aspects of content are addressed no answer can pass 11 marks or reach Level 4."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Case study: the Western Front and the Battle of the Somme, 1916","items":["By the end of 1914 the Western Front ran as a continuous line of trenches from the Belgian coast to the Swiss frontier.","A trench system had a firing line, a support line and a reserve line, joined by communication trenches, with barbed wire in front and machine guns covering no man's land.","Artillery, not rifle or machine-gun fire, caused most casualties on the Western Front; shellfire killed men who never saw the enemy.","Sir Douglas Haig was Commander-in-Chief of the British Expeditionary Force from December 1915; General Sir Henry Rawlinson's Fourth Army made the main attack on 1 July 1916.","One British aim was to relieve the French army, which had been under German attack at Verdun since February 1916.","The preliminary bombardment lasted a week and fired about one and a half million shells; too many were shrapnel rather than high explosive, and too many failed to explode.","The Germans held the higher ground and sheltered in dugouts cut deep into the chalk, coming up to their machine guns in the minutes after the barrage lifted.","On 1 July 1916 the British suffered about 57,000 casualties, roughly 19,000 of them killed — the worst single day in the history of the British Army.","Many of the men attacking were the half-trained volunteers of the New Armies, ordered to advance in line at walking pace carrying heavy packs.","Pals battalions raised from single towns and workplaces suffered heavily on the first day, so losses were concentrated on individual communities at home.","Tanks were used for the first time at Flers-Courcelette on 15 September 1916; fewer than fifty Mark I machines took part and many broke down or became stuck.","The creeping barrage — artillery fire moving forward just ahead of the infantry — was used with more success in the later stages of the battle.","The battle lasted from 1 July to 18 November 1916 and gained about six miles of ground at its deepest point.","British and Commonwealth casualties for the whole battle were around 400,000; French and German losses also ran into hundreds of thousands.","The German army was worn down badly enough to withdraw to the shorter, stronger Hindenburg Line in early 1917.","The official film The Battle of the Somme was shown in British cinemas in 1916 and watched by millions, bringing images of the front to civilians for the first time."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Case study: the Iraq War, 2003","items":["The coalition invasion of Iraq began on 20 March 2003, led by the United States with British forces operating in the south.","About 45,000 British service personnel took part, the largest British deployment since the Gulf War of 1991.","The opening attacks used cruise missiles and satellite- and laser-guided bombs against command centres, communications and air defences.","Satellites, airborne radar aircraft, unmanned aircraft and night-vision equipment gave coalition commanders a picture of the battlefield that Iraqi commanders did not have.","Satellite navigation allowed ground forces to move and fight accurately at night and in sandstorms.","Coalition air superiority was total, so Iraqi armoured units could not move by day without being destroyed.","Iraqi forces had been defeated in 1991 and worn down by more than a decade of sanctions: obsolete equipment, no effective air force, poor supply and low morale.","British forces secured Basra in early April 2003; Baghdad fell on 9 April 2003, three weeks after the invasion began.","Major combat operations were declared over on 1 May 2003, and Saddam Hussein was captured in December 2003.","No weapons of mass destruction — the stated reason for the invasion — were found.","An insurgency followed, fought with improvised explosive devices, snipers and suicide attacks from among the civilian population, against which precision weapons and surveillance were far less effective.","Bomb disposal teams, better armoured vehicles and patient patrolling mattered more than high technology in the years after the invasion.","British forces left southern Iraq in 2009, six years after the invasion.","Journalists were embedded with coalition units and 24-hour television carried the war live, which increased public attention to casualties on both sides."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Weapons, transport and surveillance, c1900–present","items":["The Vickers machine gun, belt-fed and water-cooled, was the standard British heavy machine gun of the First World War and fired several hundred rounds a minute.","Chlorine gas was first released on a large scale by the Germans at Ypres in April 1915; mustard gas followed in 1917; the Geneva Protocol of 1925 banned the use of chemical weapons in war.","Tanks were first used at Flers-Courcelette on 15 September 1916 and in mass at Cambrai on 20 November 1917, where more than three hundred fighting tanks broke into the German defences on the first day.","Aircraft went to war in 1914 as reconnaissance machines; aerial photography made artillery accurate against targets the gunners could not see.","The Royal Air Force was created as a separate service on 1 April 1918, by merging the Royal Flying Corps and the Royal Naval Air Service.","Radar stations along the south and east coasts allowed Fighter Command to detect German formations as they approached during the Battle of Britain in 1940.","Atomic bombs were dropped on Hiroshima on 6 August 1945 and Nagasaki on 9 August 1945; Britain tested its own atomic bomb in 1952 and a hydrogen bomb in 1957.","Britain's nuclear deterrent moved to submarine-launched missiles with Polaris from the late 1960s and Trident from the 1990s, so that it could not be destroyed on the ground.","Motor transport replaced the horse: lorries and railways supplied the Western Front, and armoured vehicles, helicopters and transport aircraft later moved troops and supplies.","Helicopters allowed wounded men to be flown to surgery within the hour, transforming a soldier's chance of survival.","Computerised high-tech warfare in 2003 combined satellite navigation, secure digital communications, unmanned aircraft and guided weapons that could be aimed at a single building.","Guerrilla warfare in the twenty-first century has been fought with improvised explosive devices, ambushes and suicide attacks, which are hard for surveillance to detect among civilians."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Recruitment, the Home Front and civilians, c1900–present","items":["In 1914 Britain had a small regular army and relied on volunteers; more than two million men enlisted after Lord Kitchener's appeal.","Pals battalions were raised from one town, factory or club, such as the Accrington Pals, so that friends served together.","The Derby Scheme of voluntary attestation in 1915 failed to produce enough men, and the Military Service Act of January 1916 conscripted single men aged 18 to 41, extended to married men in May 1916.","The National Service (Armed Forces) Act of 3 September 1939 made men aged 18 to 41 liable for service; from December 1941 single women were directed into the services, civil defence or industry.","National Service under the 1948 Act ran from 1949, first for eighteen months and then for two years; the last call-ups were in 1960 and the last National Servicemen left in 1963.","Since 1963 the British Army has been an all-volunteer professional force; women were integrated into the regular army in 1992 and all ground close combat roles were opened between 2016 and 2018.","Conscientious objectors numbered roughly 16,000 in the First World War and some 60,000 in the Second; tribunals were far harsher in the first war, absolutists were imprisoned, and objectors lost the vote for five years.","The Defence of the Realm Act of 8 August 1914 gave the government powers of censorship, requisition and control over daily life; food rationing followed in 1918.","German airships from 1915 and Gotha bombers from 1917 killed roughly 1,400 people in Britain during the First World War.","Evacuation moved about one and a half million people, mostly children, out of Britain's cities in the first days of September 1939.","Rationing began in January 1940, extended to clothes, and was not lifted completely until 1954.","The Home Guard was raised in May 1940, and the blackout, Anderson and Morrison shelters and air raid precautions reorganised life at home.","The Blitz from September 1940 to May 1941 killed some 40,000 civilians; bombing killed about 60,000 British civilians across the whole war.","Fear of nuclear war after 1945 produced civil defence advice, the founding of the Campaign for Nuclear Disarmament in 1958, alarm during the Cuban missile crisis of 1962 and the women's peace camp at Greenham Common from 1981.","War reporting moved from censored dispatches and official propaganda to televised war, journalists embedded with units in 2003, and public mourning for the dead repatriated through Wootton Bassett between 2007 and 2011."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 1 Question 5 (16 marks + 4 SPaG): 'The nature of warfare changed more in the years c1900–1918 than in the years since 1945.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • trench warfare • high-tech weaponry. You must also use information of your own.","items":["Decode the question: the concept is the extent of change, the comparison is between two stretches of the same theme, and 'the nature of warfare' means how wars are fought — the weapons, the way forces move and see, and who does the fighting. You are not being asked which period was worse.","Fix the criteria in the opening three or four lines, because Level 4 needs them justified and applied: change will be measured by how far weapons altered what was possible, how far the movement and organisation of forces changed, and how far the people who fought and suffered changed.","Paragraph 1 — weapons, c1900–1918: machine guns firing several hundred rounds a minute, quick-firing artillery and barbed wire made the attack far costlier than the defence, so the Western Front settled into trenches from the Belgian coast to Switzerland; chlorine gas at Ypres in April 1915 and mustard gas in 1917 added a new terror; tanks appeared at Flers-Courcelette on 15 September 1916 and in mass at Cambrai on 20 November 1917. Interim judgement: in four years war changed from movement to siege and back towards movement again.","Paragraph 2 — weapons since 1945: the atomic bombs of 6 and 9 August 1945 made a single aircraft able to destroy a city, and Britain's own bomb of 1952 and hydrogen bomb of 1957, later carried in submarines, turned armed forces into a deterrent rather than an instrument of victory; guided weapons and satellite navigation in Iraq in 2003 allowed a single building to be hit from the air. Interim judgement: the change is at least as large, but it is a change in the purpose of weapons rather than in the shape of the battlefield.","Paragraph 3 — seeing and moving: reconnaissance aircraft and aerial photography from 1914 made artillery accurate and ended surprise; radar in 1940 let a smaller air force meet a larger one; satellites, unmanned aircraft and night-vision in 2003 gave one side a picture of the battlefield the other lacked, while lorries, helicopters and transport aircraft replaced the horse. Interim judgement: surveillance changed continuously across the whole century, which weakens any claim that one period holds the change.","Paragraph 4 — the point beyond the stimulus: who fights. In 1914–18 Britain moved from a small regular army to a mass citizen army through the Pals battalions and the Military Service Act of January 1916; since National Service ended in 1960–63 it has returned to a small all-volunteer professional force of specialists, with women integrated in 1992 and all ground close combat roles opened between 2016 and 2018, and with the Royal Logistic Corps of 1993 and bomb disposal teams showing how far the army is now a collection of trades. Interim judgement: the composition of the army changed twice, once in each period.","Keep a running evaluation thread rather than saving all judgement for the end: after each paragraph say which period that criterion favours, so that the conclusion has something to add up.","Answer the counter-case honestly: the years since 1945 include the invention of a weapon that made total war between great powers unthinkable, which is arguably a bigger change than anything in 1914–18 — but it is a change in whether wars are fought at all, not in how they are fought.","Conclusion: give a judgement on the stated criteria, not a summary. Something like: measured by the shape of the battlefield and the experience of the soldier, 1900–1918 changed warfare more, because in four years it produced trench deadlock, gas, tanks and the mass citizen army; measured by the purpose of armed forces and by the ability to see and strike at a distance, the years since 1945 changed it more. The statement therefore holds only on the first criterion.","Timing: this question is worth 16 marks plus 4 for SPaG, so allow about twenty minutes — three or four minutes planning the criteria and the paragraph order, fifteen writing, and a final minute checking spelling and specialist terms.","SPaG: the top band needs consistent accuracy and a wide range of specialist terms, so use and spell attrition, deterrent, conscription, reconnaissance, surveillance, guerrilla, logistics and artillery correctly, and write in full paragraphs rather than notes."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-18T21:42:50.693258Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.86,"notes":"A kind A room with no faults of fact that I could find: I checked about seventy dates, figures and events and verified the load-bearing ones on the web — 1 July 1916 (57,470 casualties, 19,240 killed, so 'about 57,000' and 'roughly 19,000' are right), the Somme's 1 July-18 November span, roughly 400,000 British and Commonwealth casualties and about six miles gained (some sources say seven), 1.5 million shells, Haig C-in-C from December 1915 and Rawlinson's Fourth Army, Flers-Courcelette on 15 September 1916 (49 Mark Is allotted, 32 to the start line, so 'fewer than fifty' holds) and Cambrai on 20 November 1917 (476 tanks, 378 fighting tanks), the Military Service Act of January 1916 (single men 18-41, married men May 1916) and the Derby Scheme, the National Service (Armed Forces) Act of 3 September 1939, the National Service (No. 2) Act of December 1941, the 1948 Act in force from 1949 at eighteen months then two years with the last call-ups in 1960 and the last National Servicemen out in 1963, DORA on 8 August 1914, rationing from 8 January 1940 to 4 July 1954, evacuation of about 1.5 million in the first days of September 1939, the Blitz of September 1940-May 1941, Hiroshima and Nagasaki, Operation Hurricane in 1952 and the 1957 Grapple hydrogen test, the invasion of Iraq on 20 March 2003 with 46,000 British personnel, Baghdad on 9 April and 1 May 2003, the Army Service Corps of 1888 (Royal 1918) and the Royal Logistic Corps of 1993 as the Army's largest corps, the Wheelbarrow of 1972 and Operation Banner 1969-2007, the 1,413 British air-raid deaths of the First World War and the 16,000/60,000 conscientious objectors. Two figures sit at the soft edge of their range and neither is wrong: the Blitz is given as 'some 40,000' where IWM says more than 43,000, and the whole-war bombing toll as 'about 60,000' where the Home Office figure is 60,595. Truth to the paper is otherwise exact — 4/12/16 tariffs, AO2 2+AO1 2, AO2 6+AO1 6, AO2 10+AO1 6, the 5/8/no-Level-4 and 7/11/no-Level-4 caps in every indicative-content line, SPaG named only on the 16-mark stems, stimulus points printed in the paper's own words. Nothing is lifted: I compared every stem with the option 12 specimen paper (Q3 composition of the army, Q4 the role of the tank, Q5 Falkirk and Naseby, Q6 civilians c1700-c1900 v c1500-c1700), the June 2023 option 10 paper and the 2018 and 2024 live option 12 papers, and no question, stimulus pair or worked-example question repeats one of theirs. Counts hold: 14 questions at 5/5/4 by tier, all six key-idea codes tested, 15 cards (none open, none over 40 words), four fact files, five worked examples, a Level 4 and a Level 2 model paragraph on the same question, one plan, five room.facts and four exit questions; solutions run 105-113 words at 4 marks, 328-345 at 12 and 515-550 at 16, inside the brief's bands. Publishable as it stands; the five items above are wording and labelling fixes, the first two worth making before it ships."}}},"12-env":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"12-env","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Section A of Paper 1 is only 16 marks, but it is the part of the paper where a small place known in close detail beats a whole thematic study known in outline. Everything here happens in one city between 1939 and 1945, and the examiner sets the same three tasks every year: describe one feature each of two separate aspects of wartime London, judge how useful two printed sources are for a named enquiry, and frame a follow-up enquiry out of one of them. The knowledge and the source skill are not separable. You cannot decide whether a Mass Observation investigator's report on an East End shelter is useful unless you already know that Mass Observation was being paid by the Ministry of Information to watch morale, that the shelters under the warehouses were not built as shelters at all, and that the government had expected gas and mass panic rather than four years of broken sleep. So the room teaches wartime London and the source moves together: every fact here exists to be spent on a source, and every source here is a test of what you know about the Blitz, the V-weapons, the shelters, the propaganda and the censorship that shaped what Londoners were allowed to read about their own city.","idea":[{"h":"Why London, and what London did before the bombs came","t":"London was the seat of government, the largest port in the country and a manufacturing city in its own right, and it lay within easy range of airfields in occupied France and the Low Countries, so German bombers could reach it and follow the Thames to it in the dark. Its docks ran in a line down the river — the Royal Victoria and Royal Albert, the Surrey Commercial at Rotherhithe, the West India on the Isle of Dogs — with the terraced streets of the East End packed tight against them, so a raid aimed at cranes and warehouses hit houses. Preparation began before the war: the Air Raid Precautions Act of 1937 obliged local authorities to plan, around 38 million gas masks had been issued across Britain by September 1939, roughly a million and a half Anderson shelters had been given out (free to households earning less than £250 a year), the blackout began on 1 September 1939 and evacuation began the same day. What the planners expected was gas and immediate mass casualties; what came was a long campaign against housing. A top answer uses that gap: London's preparations were serious but aimed at the wrong war, which is why so much of the shelter story from September 1940 is improvised by Londoners rather than provided by the state.","code":"12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The context of London in the Second World War"},{"h":"What was dropped on London, and what it did","t":"The Blitz on London ran from 7 September 1940 to 11 May 1941. It opened on Black Saturday, 7 September 1940, when roughly 350 bombers came up the Thames in daylight and set the docks and the streets behind them alight; about 430 people were killed and some 1,600 seriously injured that night. London was then bombed on 57 consecutive nights to 2 November 1940. Two weapons did the work: high-explosive bombs, from small ones up to parachute mines that flattened whole terraces, and the one-kilogram magnesium incendiary, dropped in containers by the hundred, which started the fires — the raid of 29 December 1940 burned out much of the City and the Guildhall, and the raid of 10 and 11 May 1941 destroyed the Commons chamber and killed about 1,400 people. Incendiaries were the harder problem, because they were small, numerous and had to be smothered within minutes, which is why fire-watching was made a duty. Then came Operation Steinbock, the 'Baby Blitz' of January to May 1944, and after it the V-weapons: the first V-1 flying bomb fell on Grove Road, Mile End, on 13 June 1944, and the first V-2 rocket on Staveley Road, Chiswick, on 8 September 1944. A top answer separates the weapons by the problem each set: bombers could be heard, tracked and warned against, the V-1 gave a few seconds between the engine cutting out and the dive, and the supersonic V-2 gave none at all.","code":"12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The nature of attacks on London"},{"h":"Living under it: shelters, disasters and the question of morale","t":"Civil defence was local and volunteer-heavy: ARP wardens on sector posts enforcing the blackout and reporting incidents, the Auxiliary Fire Service of 1938 (merged with the regular brigades into the National Fire Service in August 1941), rescue and stretcher parties, and the Women's Voluntary Service, founded in 1938, running rest centres, canteens and clothing depots. Londoners sheltered where they could. The government at first refused to open the Underground, fearing a 'deep shelter mentality'; Londoners bought tickets and stayed, and by the end of September 1940 the refusal had collapsed — on the night of 27 September 1940 about 177,000 people slept in the Tube, and bunks, chemical closets and canteens followed. The Tube was not safe: a bomb burst the mains above Balham station on 14 October 1940 and about 66 people drowned or were crushed. The Morrison shelter, an indoor steel table, was introduced in March 1941. Big improvised shelters in warehouse basements were worse still until the shelterers organised them, as at the Fruit and Wool Exchange in Brushfield Street, Spitalfields — 'Mickey's shelter' — where Mickey Davis, an optician who sheltered there himself, was put in charge of a shelter committee in 1940 and brought in bunks, a canteen and a sick bay. The disasters are the test of the 'Blitz spirit' story: at South Hallsville School, Canning Town, a bomb hit a rest centre full of bombed-out families in the early hours of 10 September 1940 and the official death toll of 73 was widely disbelieved locally, and at Bethnal Green on 3 March 1943, 173 people were crushed on the stairs of the unfinished Tube station. Meanwhile the theatres, cinemas and dance halls reopened and stayed open — the Windmill in Soho advertised that it never closed, the National Gallery ran Myra Hess's lunchtime concerts from October 1939, and the Café de Paris was bombed on 8 March 1941 with more than thirty killed. A top answer treats morale as something the government measured and worried about, through Mass Observation and the Ministry of Information's Home Intelligence reports, rather than as a national quality that either existed or did not.","code":"12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The impact of the Blitz on civilian life in London"},{"h":"How London and its leaders answered the bombing","t":"The response was partly symbolic and partly practical, and the examiner rewards you for noticing which is which. Symbolically, the King and Queen stayed in London and Buckingham Palace was hit several times, including on 13 September 1940 when the King and Queen were in residence, after which the Queen said she could look the East End in the face; Churchill, Prime Minister from 10 May 1940, was photographed in the bombed streets. Practically, government was made bomb-proof: the Cabinet War Rooms, a basement under the government offices in Whitehall, came into use on 27 August 1939, a week before war was declared, and the War Cabinet met there about 115 times, with the Map Room manned round the clock until August 1945. London's open spaces were put to work: the 'Dig for Victory' campaign launched by the Ministry of Agriculture in October 1939 turned much of Victoria Park in the East End and the dry moat of the Tower of London over to vegetables, and allotments in Britain rose to roughly 1.4 million by 1943, while other parts of the same parks held anti-aircraft guns. A top answer links the two halves: staying put made the digging and the shelter-building mean something, because a city whose government had left would have had no reason to plant the Tower moat.","code":"12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: London's response to the war"},{"h":"What the Blitz was for, and what Londoners were allowed to read about it","t":"The Blitz was not random terror. Its purpose changed: after the Luftwaffe failed to destroy Fighter Command in the summer of 1940 it switched to the capital, to wreck the port and the war industries, to force the RAF into a battle over London, and to break civilian will so that Britain would ask for terms. It failed at the last of these, and the government worked hard to make sure it was seen to fail. The Ministry of Information, created on 4 September 1939 at Senate House, controlled what the press and the newsreels could print. Under the Emergency Powers (Defence) Act of 1939 exact bomb locations, casualty figures and pictures of despair were kept out of the papers, papers could be suppressed altogether (the Daily Worker was banned in January 1941), and the images that were allowed were chosen: the photograph of the dome of St Paul's above the smoke of the raid of 29 December 1940 ran in the Daily Mail two days later, and the film 'London Can Take It!' of October 1940, narrated by the American journalist Quentin Reynolds, was aimed largely at audiences in the United States. Censorship continued to the end: V-2 explosions were officially described as burst gas mains until Churchill admitted the rockets to the Commons on 10 November 1944. A top answer uses censorship as a tool of source analysis rather than as a topic: a photograph passed by the censor in 1940 is weak evidence of how Londoners felt and strong evidence of what the government wanted them to feel.","code":"12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The historical context of the Second World War"},{"h":"The local records: what a borough, a warden and a newspaper left behind","t":"Local sources are the ones made inside London by people with no national audience, and for this environment they are unusually rich. Wardens' post log books and incident report forms recorded each incident as it was telephoned in: the time, the street, the type of bomb, the number of casualties and whether services were needed. Metropolitan borough councils kept minutes of their ARP and shelter committees, rest-centre and billeting registers, and correspondence with the Ministry of Home Security; the London County Council surveyed and coloured bomb damage on street maps as it happened. Local newspapers such as the East London Advertiser reported raids within the limits the censor set, and printed the funerals, the appeals and the complaints. Then there are personal accounts: diaries, letters, and the oral history interviews recorded long afterwards. A top answer picks the local source by what it was made for: a warden's log is dry, immediate and made for the service itself, so it is good for what happened and when, and poor for what anyone felt about it, while a letter is the reverse.","code":"12-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Knowledge of local sources"},{"h":"The national records: government paper, surveillance of morale and film","t":"National sources cover London from above. Cabinet papers and the Ministry of Home Security's weekly appreciations counted raids, casualties and damage across the country. The Ministry of Information's Home Intelligence Division produced regular reports on public feeling, and Mass Observation — an independent social survey founded in 1937 by Tom Harrisson, Charles Madge and Humphrey Jennings, and paid by the Ministry during the war — sent investigators to watch and to listen, and collected diaries from volunteers. Hansard records what ministers were willing to say in the Commons. Newsreels, the BBC's bulletins and the national press were all shaped by the censor, and photographic collections gathered by official war artists and photographers were commissioned. Memoirs written after the war are national sources of a different kind: wide in view, late in date, and shaped by what the writer learned later. A top answer notes that a government record made to be acted on, like a Home Intelligence report, is often more candid than anything printed at the time, because it was never meant to be read by the public it describes.","code":"12-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Knowledge of national sources"},{"h":"Judging a source: nature, origin, purpose, and utility for this enquiry","t":"Usefulness is never general; it is usefulness for the enquiry the question names, so the same photograph can be very useful for one enquiry and nearly useless for the next. Work in three moves. Content: what does the source let you know or infer about this enquiry, anchored on a quoted phrase or a named detail in the picture? Provenance, meaning nature (what kind of thing it is), origin (who made it, when, where) and purpose (what it was made to do): does that make this particular inference stronger or weaker? Context: what fact of your own confirms, qualifies or explains it? The mark scheme gives no credit for context that is not used on the source, and none for calling a source biased without saying which claim in it the bias bends. The most valuable habit is to turn a limitation into a use. A censored newspaper photograph is bad evidence of how a shelter smelt and excellent evidence of the image the Ministry of Information wanted the public to hold, and saying both is what separates a top answer from one that lists strengths and weaknesses.","code":"12-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Recognition of the strengths and weaknesses of different types of source"},{"h":"Framing a question a record could actually answer","t":"A follow-up question is not a question about the topic; it is a question raised by one detail in the printed source and answerable from a record that exists. Choose the unfinished detail: a figure given without a check, a group named in passing, a complaint with no answer attached, an event mentioned as though the reader already knows it. Then ask a question that could be settled rather than merely discussed — how many, how often, on whose orders, with what result, and by when. 'Was it fair?' cannot be followed up; 'How many people were in fact sleeping in the shelters under the Commercial Road warehouses in the autumn of 1940, and who counted them?' can. Keep the question tied to the detail you named in the first box, because a question that floats free of the source scores nothing, and keep it narrow enough that one kind of record could close it.","code":"12-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Framing of questions relevant to the pursuit of a specific enquiry"},{"h":"Choosing the record that would close the question","t":"The last two boxes are a matching exercise: name a type of record rather than one document, and then say what is in it that would answer the question you just asked. For wartime London the useful types are known — wardens' post log books and incident reports, borough council ARP and shelter committee minutes, rest-centre and billeting registers, London County Council bomb damage maps, Ministry of Home Security weekly appreciations, Home Intelligence and Mass Observation reports, Hansard, censored and uncensored press, newsreels, hospital and mortuary records, coroners' inquest papers, and later oral history interviews. Match the record to the question's verb: a number needs a register or a return, a decision needs minutes or correspondence, a feeling needs a diary or a survey of opinion, and an official line needs Hansard or the press. A top answer finishes by saying what the record would contain — not that it 'would tell us more', but that shelter committee minutes would record the date the bunks were ordered and who objected to paying for them.","code":"12-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Selection of appropriate sources for specific investigations"}],"examples":[{"title":"Describing one feature each: 1(a) the Morrison shelter and 1(b) the Women's Voluntary Service in London","setup":"The question is printed in two parts: '1(a) Describe one feature of the Morrison shelter. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the work of the Women's Voluntary Service in London. [2 marks]' You know the following. The Morrison shelter was an indoor shelter introduced in March 1941 and named after Herbert Morrison, Home Secretary and Minister of Home Security from October 1940. It was a steel cage the size of a large table, roughly six feet six inches long, four feet wide and two feet six inches high, with a solid steel plate top, welded wire mesh sides and a lath floor; it was delivered as a kit of parts for the family to bolt together in a downstairs room, was used as a table by day, and held two adults and a child or two lying down at night. It was built to carry the weight of the floors above if the house collapsed rather than to survive a direct hit, and it answered the difficulty that had defeated the Anderson in the East End, where families in flats and in the packed terraces had no garden to dig in. It was issued free to households below an income ceiling of about £350 a year, though accounts differ a little on the exact figure, and sold to those above it; roughly half a million had been distributed by the end of 1941, with about a hundred thousand more in 1943 before the flying bombs came. The Women's Voluntary Service was founded in 1938 at the government's request to bring women into Air Raid Precautions work, and in London it staffed the rest centres that took in families bombed out of their homes, ran mobile canteens that fed wardens, firemen and rescue parties at the incidents, collected and handed out clothing to people who had lost everything, and helped billet evacuated children; its members were unpaid volunteers, and more than a million women had enrolled across the country by 1943. In each part one mark is given for the feature and a second mark for the supporting information attached to it.","steps":["Treat the two parts as two separate questions with a box each, because nothing you write about the Morrison shelter can earn anything in 1(b) and nothing about the Women's Voluntary Service can earn anything in 1(a).","In 1(a), state one feature flatly and then attach the evidence that proves it: it was a shelter for indoors rather than for a garden, a steel cage about six feet six inches by four feet with a plate top and mesh sides, bolted together in a downstairs room, used as a table by day and slept in by two adults and a child at night.","In 1(b), do the same with a different kind of fact: it was an unpaid volunteer organisation, founded in 1938, that did the relief work after a raid, staffing the rest centres that took in the bombed-out, running mobile canteens for the wardens and rescue parties at the incidents and handing out clothing to people who had lost everything.","Stop there. Two sentences in each box is the whole task: do not add a second feature to either part, do not explain why the government introduced the shelter or why the volunteers were needed, and do not compare the two."],"cues":["How much of what you write about the Morrison shelter can be credited in 1(b)?","Which detail of the shelter proves the feature you have named, and so earns the second mark?","What turns 'the WVS helped people' into a described feature with support attached?","What would a second feature about the Women's Voluntary Service be worth in that box?"]},{"title":"Judging usefulness: Source A for an enquiry into food and drink in the shelters","setup":"The question is: 'How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the provision of food and drink for Londoners sheltering at night during the Blitz? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context.' (8 marks) This example works on Source A only. Source A is a report written by an investigator for Mass Observation and sent to the organisation's London office on 26 September 1940; Mass Observation was by then being paid by the Ministry of Information to report on civilian morale, and its reports were not published at the time. The investigator writes that he has spent four nights in the basements and railway arches between the Commercial Road and the river; that a woman keeps a tea stall in the big shelter under the warehouse and puts its numbers at twice the three thousand the wardens give; that there is one tap for all of them, and fourteen buckets behind a curtain; that families come in from five o'clock with their bedding; that when the all clear went the queue at the tea stall was longer than it had been all night, and two men went from it straight to their work in the docks; and that the sentence said to him most often was that nobody in authority had come down to look at the place. Your own knowledge includes that the first refreshment service on a Tube platform did not open until 29 October 1940 and that by 7 December 1940 there were 124 canteen points at 71 stations serving about 112,000 people, and that the canteen in the shelter under the Fruit and Wool Exchange in Brushfield Street was paid for by a donation the shelterers' own committee under Mickey Davis obtained from Marks & Spencer, its profits buying free milk for the children.","steps":["Take out only what the source shows about feeding and watering the shelterers, and anchor each piece in its own words: one tap for the whole place, a tea stall kept by a woman inside it, and a queue at that stall which was longer when the all clear went than it had been all night.","Read the gap as evidence too, because the enquiry is about provision: no canteen, no council provision and no ministry appears anywhere in it, the borough and the rest centre are named only as objects of anger, and the one thing on sale is being sold by somebody who is there on nobody's authority.","Use the nature and origin to strengthen that: an unpublished internal report by an observer who slept there four nights was written for an organisation that wanted the plain state of things, so no censor has trimmed it and no reader has to be reassured.","Use the purpose to qualify one named claim rather than the source as a whole: Mass Observation was paid to report on morale, so the investigator collects what people say, and the tea seller's 'twice that' is her guess at a crowd nobody had counted rather than a measure of how many she served.","Close on the enquiry: for the provision of food and drink this is strong evidence that in the big improvised shelters it was still private and voluntary in the first weeks of the Blitz, with only its one figure to be treated as hearsay, and the date when the Tube got a refreshment service of its own shows how far ahead of any official provision this stall stood."],"cues":["Which sentences here are about food and drink rather than about feelings?","What is missing from the account that this enquiry would expect to find, and what does the absence tell you?","What is it about who wrote this, and for whom, that makes those details trustworthy?","Which single figure would you not take at face value, and why?","What is your verdict for this enquiry, and which dated fact of your own fixes the source in time?"]},{"title":"Judging usefulness: Source B, the censor, and a tea urn on a platform","setup":"The same question is set: 'How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the provision of food and drink for Londoners sheltering at night during the Blitz?' (8 marks) This example works on Source B only. Source B is a photograph published in a London evening newspaper on 3 October 1940 under a caption saying that London sleeps sound below ground and wakes with a smile; it was taken by a press photographer working with a permit and was submitted to the Ministry of Information's censors before printing. The photograph looks along an Underground platform at night. In the middle of it a woman in a clean apron pours tea from an urn on a trestle table, and four children sit on a bench in front of her holding mugs, all four turned towards the camera and laughing, while a fifth child stands beside them with a doll and looks away. About twenty people lie in rows on blankets along the wall behind a line chalked on the platform that the bedding is not to cross; two men in shirtsleeves play cards on an upturned crate; an elderly man sits against the tiles with his gas-mask case on his knees; a shelter marshal with an armband and a clipboard stands at the foot of the stairs; and the far end of the platform, beyond the tea urn, is dark and out of focus. Your own knowledge includes that the Ministry of Information was created on 4 September 1939 and controlled what the press could print, that the first refreshment service on a Tube platform opened on 29 October 1940 and that by 7 December 1940 there were 124 canteen points at 71 stations, and that the Minister of Food had asked the London County Council early in September 1940 to take the lead in communal feeding in the capital for people whose homes and kitchens had gone.","steps":["Separate the arranged part of the picture from the incidental part, because they are worth different things to this enquiry: the clean apron, the urn set up in the middle of the platform and four children turned to the lens with mugs in their hands are the caption's argument, while the fifth child looking away, the chalk line, the marshal's clipboard and the dark far end are details nobody would trouble to arrange.","Bank what the photograph nevertheless establishes: by the time this appeared in print on 3 October 1940, tea was being poured on an Underground platform from an urn on a trestle table, and somebody had carried it down and set it up, which is a fact about provision that the staging does not cancel.","Use the nature, origin and purpose against the rest: a press photograph taken under permit, passed by the Ministry of Information's censors and captioned to say London wakes with a smile is evidence of an approved impression, so the smiles cannot be used to show that anybody down there was fed, and the crowded far end is exactly what is left dark.","Bring one dated fact of your own and aim it at the picture: the Tube's own refreshment service did not open until 29 October 1940 and had not reached 124 canteen points at 71 stations until 7 December, so this urn stands nearly four weeks ahead of the official service and was voluntary, improvised or set up for the camera.","Judge it for this enquiry: weak on whether the shelterers were fed, strong on what one platform was shown providing by early October and on the version of shelter catering the public was allowed to see, which is a genuine use rather than a concession."],"cues":["Which details here would a photographer have arranged, and which would he not?","What does the urn prove even if the smiles prove nothing?","How does the censor's involvement change what the laughing faces can be used for?","What fact of your own tells you how early that urn is for an official service?","So what is this photograph strong evidence of, if not of shelterers being fed?"]},{"title":"Framing a follow-up: from one line in Source A to the work of the wardens","setup":"The question is: 'Study Source A. How could you follow up Source A to find out more about the work of air-raid wardens in London during the Blitz? In your answer, you must give the question you would ask and the type of source you could use.' (4 marks) The table has four boxes: the detail in Source A you would follow up, the question you would ask, the type of source you could use, and how it might help answer your question. Source A is the Mass Observation investigator's report of 26 September 1940. Among other things it states that the wardens' post on the corner has had no telephone since Tuesday, and that the wardens put the number in the big shelter under the warehouse at three thousand while the woman at the tea stall says twice that. You know that London's wardens worked from posts covering a few streets each, that a warden's first job after a bomb fell was to report the incident from his post to the borough's control centre so that rescue parties and ambulances could be sent, and that boroughs kept the log books of their wardens' posts and the minutes of their ARP committees.","steps":["Choose the unfinished detail rather than the vivid one: a wardens' post left without a telephone since Tuesday is dropped in one line at the end of the report, and it bears directly on whether the wardens could do the work the enquiry is about.","Turn it into a question a record could close, not one that invites an opinion: how long were wardens' posts in this part of Stepney and Poplar left without a telephone line in the autumn of 1940, and what were the wardens told to do while the line was down?","Name a type of record, not one document: the log books kept at the wardens' posts, together with the borough's ARP committee minutes and its control centre records.","Say what is in that record that answers the question: a post log book enters each incident with the time it was reported and how the report was sent, so a run of entries carried by messenger instead of by telephone shows the gap and its length, while the committee minutes carry the requests for repair and the arrangements made in the meantime."],"cues":["Which line in the source is a problem nobody has followed up?","What would make your question answerable from a record rather than merely arguable?","Who wrote something down at the time about how an incident was reported?","What exactly inside that record would settle how long the post was cut off?"]},{"title":"Choosing between a local and a national record for the same enquiry","setup":"You are investigating how far the bombing of the East End in September 1940 disrupted people's work. You have four kinds of record available. First, the log book of an ARP wardens' post in Poplar, which records each incident as it was telephoned in with the time, the street, the type of bomb and the casualties. Second, the minutes of a metropolitan borough council's ARP and shelter committee. Third, the Ministry of Information's Home Intelligence reports on public feeling, which were written for ministers and not published. Fourth, a local newspaper such as the East London Advertiser, which was published weekly and was subject to censorship. The enquiry asks about disruption to work: lateness, absence, whether men went to the docks after a night in a shelter, and what employers and the council did about it. Decide which record answers which part of the enquiry, and which is weakest.","steps":["Match each record to the verb it can answer: the wardens' log answers 'where and when', because it is an immediate operational record of incidents and casualties, and nothing else.","See what the log cannot do: it never records whether anyone got to work, so it establishes the conditions of a night but not the consequence the enquiry is about, and on its own it would leave the question open.","Take the council minutes for decisions and numbers: they would carry complaints from employers, the numbers passing through rest centres and the arrangements made for transport and for shelter, which is disruption seen administratively.","Take Home Intelligence for the experience, and weigh its provenance the right way round: it was compiled for ministers and never published, so it is more candid than anything in print, though it summarises what observers reported rather than counting anybody.","Put the censored local paper last for fact and first for one particular thing: it cannot be trusted on the scale of damage, because the censor kept locations and casualty figures out, but its advertisements, notices of altered opening hours and appeals for workers are a direct trace of the disruption itself."],"cues":["What kind of question is an incident log actually built to answer?","What does the enquiry need that the log will never contain?","Where would an employer's complaint have ended up on paper?","Why might an unpublished government report be franker than a newspaper?","What can a censored paper still be used as evidence of?"]}],"check":[{"q":"On what dates did the Blitz on London begin and end, and how many consecutive nights was London bombed at the start of it?","a":"It began on 7 September 1940 and ended on 11 May 1941. London was bombed on 57 consecutive nights from 7 September to 2 November 1940."},{"q":"Why did the government at first refuse to let Londoners shelter in Underground stations, and what changed its mind?","a":"It feared a 'deep shelter mentality' in which people would not come out, and it needed the railway working. Londoners bought tickets and stayed anyway, and by the end of September 1940 the refusal had collapsed; about 177,000 people slept in the Tube on the night of 27 September 1940."},{"q":"What made the V-2 a different problem from the V-1, and how did the government first describe V-2 explosions to the public?","a":"The V-2 was a supersonic rocket that arrived without warning of any kind, whereas a V-1 could be heard and gave a few seconds between the engine cutting out and the dive. V-2 explosions were officially described as burst gas mains until Churchill admitted the rockets in the Commons on 10 November 1944."},{"q":"A source is a photograph of smiling shelterers passed by the Ministry of Information's censor in October 1940. What is it weak evidence of, and what is it strong evidence of?","a":"It is weak evidence of how Londoners actually felt or of conditions underground, because the subjects are arranged and the censor approved the impression. It is strong evidence of the picture of the shelters that the government wanted the public to hold, and of any incidental detail nobody would have staged, such as the rules being enforced on the platform."},{"q":"Name the four boxes of a follow-up answer, in order.","a":"The detail in the source you would follow up; the question you would ask; the type of source you could use; and how that source might help answer your question. Each has to fit the one before it."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 1 Section A is 16 marks in all: Question 1 in two parts, '1(a) Describe one feature of…' (2 marks) and '1(b) Describe one feature of…' (2 marks) on two separate aspects of content, Question 2(a) 'How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into…? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context.' (8 marks) and Question 2(b) 'How could you follow up Source A to find out more about…? In your answer, you must give the question you would ask and the type of source you could use.' (4 marks).","Questions 1 and 2(b) are point-marked, not levelled. On each part of Question 1, one mark for the feature and a second mark for the supporting information attached to it. On Question 2(b), one mark for a detail selected from Source A, one for a question linked to that detail, one for naming an appropriate type of source, and one for showing how that source would help answer the question.","Question 2(a) is levelled, and Level 3 runs from 6 to 8 marks: judgements on usefulness for the specified enquiry, applying valid criteria with developed reasoning which takes into account how the provenance affects the usefulness of the source content, with contextual knowledge used in the process of interpreting the sources. Provenance means nature, origin and purpose.","Two rules printed under the 2(a) grid decide most of the marks that are lost: no credit may be given for contextual knowledge unless it is linked to evaluation of the sources, and no credit may be given for generic comments on provenance which are not used to evaluate source content.","Section A prints no stimulus points and carries no SPaG marks; both belong elsewhere on the paper. Paper 1 lasts 1 hour 20 minutes for 52 marks, which is about a minute and a half a mark: about 4 minutes on Question 1, 10 to 13 minutes on Question 2(a) and 4 to 5 minutes on Question 2(b), so roughly 20 minutes on Section A's 16 marks and about an hour left for Section B."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the Anderson shelter. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the blackout in London. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks in two parts, point-marked: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 mark for the supporting information attached to it. In 1(a) credit its construction and siting, its capacity, the means test and price, or the fact that it needed a garden. In 1(b) credit the date it began, the covering of windows and the shading of lamps, or its enforcement by ARP wardens.","sol":"1(a) Feature: it was a buried garden shelter made of curved corrugated steel. The sheets were bolted together, sunk about four feet into the ground and covered with at least fifteen inches of earth, and the finished shelter held up to six people. 1(b) Feature: it was a rule that no light might show after dark. It began on 1 September 1939, two days before war was declared, and every window and door had to be covered and street lamps and vehicle lights shaded, with ARP wardens patrolling their sectors to enforce it.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Two boxes, two separate things: answer the shelter in the first and the blackout in the second, and never mix them.","A feature only scores twice when a measurement, a date, a rule or a number is attached to it, so choose features you can attach something to.","Write each part as one flat sentence of fact followed by a second sentence carrying the evidence, and leave out any comment on how well either worked."],"codes":["12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The context of London in the Second World War"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the evacuation of children from London in September 1939. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the issue of gas masks to civilians before the war. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks in two parts, point-marked: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 mark for the supporting information attached to it. In 1(a) credit the date and scale, the organisation through schools with teachers and labels, the reception areas and billeting, or the voluntary nature of the scheme. In 1(b) credit the scale of the issue, the expectation of gas attack, or the requirement to carry the mask.","sol":"1(a) Feature: it was organised through the schools rather than through families. London children assembled at their own schools with a label, a gas mask and a small case, went by train under their own teachers and were billeted on households in reception areas in the countryside, while their parents stayed behind. 1(b) Feature: a gas mask was issued to every civilian, adult and child alike. About 38 million had been issued across Britain by September 1939, carried in a cardboard box on a strap, because the planners expected the Germans to drop gas on London from the first day of the war.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The two parts are two separate preparations for war, so do not let the evacuation answer wander into the gas masks.","Attach the practical arrangements to the first feature and a number to the second, so that each part has its own supporting detail.","Two sentences per box, with no explanation of why the government prepared and no account of what happened later."],"codes":["12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The context of London in the Second World War"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the bombing of London on Black Saturday, 7 September 1940. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the raid on the City of London on 29 December 1940. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks in two parts, point-marked: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 mark for the supporting information attached to it. In 1(a) credit that it was the first mass raid on London and came in daylight, the size of the force, the targeting of the docks and the streets behind them, or the casualties. In 1(b) credit the use of incendiaries, the fires in the City, the buildings burned out, or the picture of St Paul's that followed.","sol":"1(a) Feature: it was the first mass attack on London and it came in daylight. Roughly 350 bombers, heavily escorted by fighters, followed the Thames in on the afternoon of 7 September 1940, and about 430 people were killed and some 1,600 seriously injured that night. 1(b) Feature: it was an incendiary raid that set the City of London alight. Small magnesium incendiaries were dropped in thousands and the fires gutted the medieval Guildhall and burned out a number of Wren churches, and the photograph of the dome of St Paul's standing above the smoke ran in the Daily Mail two days later.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The two raids were different in kind: think about what came down in each and what it did.","One box can carry the size of the attack and its cost in casualties; the other can carry the weapon and name the buildings it destroyed.","Name the docks, the districts or the buildings rather than saying 'the East End' or 'the City', because the named detail is what earns the second mark."],"codes":["12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The nature of attacks on London"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the V-1 flying bomb attacks on London in 1944. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the V-2 rocket attacks on London in 1944–45. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks in two parts, point-marked: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 mark for the supporting information attached to it. In 1(a) credit the pilotless flying bomb, the warning its engine gave, the date of the first on London or the numbers reaching the capital. In 1(b) credit the supersonic rocket, the absence of any warning, the date of the first on London or a named incident such as New Cross Road.","sol":"1(a) Feature: the V-1 was a pilotless flying bomb that could be heard coming. The first one to hit London fell at Grove Road, Mile End, on 13 June 1944, and Londoners learned to listen for the pulse-jet engine cutting out, because the machine then dived; roughly 2,400 reached the capital. 1(b) Feature: the V-2 was a supersonic rocket that gave no warning at all. The first fell on Staveley Road, Chiswick, on 8 September 1944, and about 500 hit London; on 25 November 1944 one destroyed the Woolworths store in New Cross Road, Deptford, killing 168 people.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The two weapons are the two parts; the difference between them is what carries the marks.","Give each one a first date on London and one further precise detail, such as a number reaching the capital or a named incident.","Describe what each weapon was and did, and do not drift into explaining why the Germans used them."],"codes":["12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The nature of attacks on London"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the use of London Underground stations as air-raid shelters. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of 'Mickey's shelter' in Spitalfields. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks in two parts, point-marked: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 mark for the supporting information attached to it. In 1(a) credit the government's initial refusal and the way Londoners forced the issue, the numbers sheltering, the provision of bunks, closets and canteens, or the danger shown at Balham and Bank. In 1(b) credit the building it was in, the crowding, the shelter committee, or what the committee provided.","sol":"1(a) Feature: it began against the government's wishes. Ministers refused to open the stations, fearing a 'deep shelter mentality' and disruption to the railway, but Londoners bought tickets and stayed below, and by the end of September 1940 the refusal had collapsed; about 177,000 people slept in the Tube on the night of 27 September 1940. 1(b) Feature: it was a big improvised shelter that the shelterers themselves organised. Thousands crowded into the basement of the Fruit and Wool Exchange in Brushfield Street, Spitalfields, and in 1940 Mickey Davis, an optician who sheltered there himself, was put in charge of a shelter committee that brought in bunks, a canteen and a sick bay.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["One box is about a whole system of sheltering and the other about a single basement, so keep the detail of each inside its own part.","A count on a named night, or a named building and the person put in charge, is the kind of support that earns the second mark.","Keep to what happened; do not argue about whether the government was right to refuse."],"codes":["12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The impact of the Blitz on civilian life in London"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the Cabinet War Rooms in Whitehall. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the 'Dig for Victory' campaign in London. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks in two parts, point-marked: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 mark for the supporting information attached to it. In 1(a) credit the date they came into use, their position under the government offices, the Map Room manned continuously, or the number of War Cabinet meetings. In 1(b) credit the department that launched it, the date, the London open spaces turned over to vegetables, or the growth in allotments.","sol":"1(a) Feature: they were an underground headquarters beneath Whitehall, ready before the war started. The basement of the government offices in Great George Street was converted and came into use on 27 August 1939, a week before war was declared, so that the War Cabinet and the chiefs of staff could keep working while London was bombed. 1(b) Feature: public open spaces in London were dug up to grow food. The campaign was launched by the Ministry of Agriculture in October 1939, and much of Victoria Park in the East End and the dry moat of the Tower of London were turned over to vegetable plots.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["One part is a building and the other a campaign, so give each its own kind of detail rather than one answer about the government in general.","A date and a place are the two pieces of support most easily attached here.","Do not explain why the government stayed in London; simply state one feature in each box and the detail that proves it."],"codes":["12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: London's response to the war"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the censorship of news about the bombing of London. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the film 'London Can Take It!' of October 1940. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks in two parts, point-marked: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 mark for the supporting information attached to it. In 1(a) credit the Ministry of Information's control of press and newsreels, the legal powers behind it, the withholding of locations and casualty figures, the power to suppress a paper, or the concealment of the V-2. In 1(b) credit what the film showed, who narrated it, who made it, or the audience it was aimed at.","sol":"1(a) Feature: one department controlled what could be printed or shown. The Ministry of Information, created on 4 September 1939 and housed at Senate House, vetted press reports, photographs and newsreels, and under the Emergency Powers (Defence) Act of 1939 a paper could be suppressed altogether, as the Daily Worker was in January 1941. 1(b) Feature: it was a short propaganda film about a night of bombing in London, made for audiences abroad. It was rushed out in October 1940 for the Ministry of Information, spoken by the American reporter Quentin Reynolds, and distributed in the United States, where Britain wanted opinion turned in its favour.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["One box is about what was kept out of the papers and the other about something the government put in front of an audience.","Name the department and the law for the first, and the date, the narrator and the audience for the second.","Describe each rather than judging whether it was justified, and give only one feature in each box."],"codes":["12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The historical context of the Second World War"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. How could you follow up Source A to find out more about conditions in London's air-raid shelters during the Blitz? In your answer, you must give the question you would ask and the type of source you could use. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked in four boxes: 1 mark for a detail selected from Source A, 1 for a question linked to it, 1 for naming an appropriate type of source, 1 for showing how it would help. The detail must come from Source A and the source type must be able to answer the question asked.","sol":"Detail in Source A that I would follow up: that there is one tap and, behind a curtain at the far end, fourteen buckets for everyone in the big shelter under the warehouse. Question I would ask: what sanitary provision was required in London's large improvised shelters, and when was it actually installed in the shelters off the Commercial Road? What type of source I could use: the minutes and inspection reports of the metropolitan borough council's ARP and shelter committee. How this might help answer my question: those minutes record what inspectors found, what was ordered, the dates of installation and the objections to the cost, so they would show whether one tap was normal and how long it stayed that way.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Look for the detail in the report that is precise but unexplained, rather than the one that is most shocking.","Your question has to be settleable by a record: ask how much, how many, when or on whose orders.","Ask yourself who in wartime London would have been obliged to write something down about that detail, and then say what their paperwork actually contains."],"codes":["12-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Framing of questions relevant to the pursuit of a specific enquiry","12-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Selection of appropriate sources for specific investigations","12-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Knowledge of local sources"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. How could you follow up Source A to find out more about the experiences of people in the East End who had been bombed out of their homes? In your answer, you must give the question you would ask and the type of source you could use. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked in four boxes: 1 mark for a detail selected from Source A, 1 for a question linked to it, 1 for naming an appropriate type of source, 1 for showing how it would help. Credit any follow-up built on the rest-centre complaint or on the anger directed at the borough.","sol":"Detail in Source A that I would follow up: the man bombed out on the Saturday who said he had been sent from one rest centre to another for three days and had signed nothing and been given nothing. Question I would ask: how long did a bombed-out family in this part of the East End normally spend in rest centres before being rehoused or given money in the autumn of 1940? What type of source I could use: the rest-centre registers and the ARP committee minutes of the metropolitan borough council. How this might help answer my question: the registers record arrivals, departures and family names with dates, and the minutes record the numbers passing through and the decisions taken, so together they would show whether three days of being moved on was usual.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["One person's complaint, reported at second hand, is the kind of claim a record can confirm or knock down.","Turn the complaint into a question about the usual case rather than about this one man.","Think about who ran the rest centres, and then name the kind of paperwork they were obliged to keep."],"codes":["12-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Framing of questions relevant to the pursuit of a specific enquiry","12-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Knowledge of local sources","12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The impact of the Blitz on civilian life in London"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. How could you follow up Source A to find out more about the morale of Londoners in the autumn of 1940? In your answer, you must give the question you would ask and the type of source you could use. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked in four boxes: 1 mark for a detail selected from Source A, 1 for a question linked to it, 1 for naming an appropriate type of source, 1 for showing how it would help. Credit follow-ups on the resentment of the borough and 'the people up west' or on the claim that nobody in authority had visited.","sol":"Detail in Source A that I would follow up: that the anger he heard was against the borough, the rest centre and 'the people up west' rather than against the Germans. Question I would ask: how widely was resentment of the authorities reported in London in the autumn of 1940, and was it confined to the bombed boroughs of the East End? What type of source I could use: the Ministry of Information's Home Intelligence reports on public feeling. How this might help answer my question: they were compiled weekly for ministers from reports across the country and were never published, so they would show whether this complaint was a general pattern or one investigator's district.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Pick the sentence that reports a feeling rather than a fact, because that is what this enquiry is about.","A single observer in two boroughs cannot show how widespread anything was; let your question ask exactly that.","Name the kind of record the government itself used to keep track of what people were saying, and say why it was franker than a newspaper."],"codes":["12-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Framing of questions relevant to the pursuit of a specific enquiry","12-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Knowledge of national sources","12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The historical context of the Second World War"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. How could you follow up Source A to find out more about how the authorities dealt with the problems of sheltering in London? In your answer, you must give the question you would ask and the type of source you could use. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked in four boxes: 1 mark for a detail selected from Source A, 1 for a question linked to it, 1 for naming an appropriate type of source, 1 for showing how it would help. Credit follow-ups on the complaint that nobody in authority had come to look at the shelter, on the uncounted numbers in it, or on the wardens' post left without a telephone.","sol":"Detail in Source A that I would follow up: the sentence said to the investigator most often, that nobody in authority had come down to look at the place. Question I would ask: what did the government and the borough do about the big improvised shelters in the East End in the autumn and winter of 1940, and when were bunks, sanitation and shelter marshals provided? What type of source I could use: the papers of the Ministry of Home Security, together with ministers' statements and answers on shelter policy recorded in Hansard. How this might help answer my question: they record the decisions taken, the dates on which orders went out and what ministers were prepared to say about conditions underground, so they would show how quickly the authorities took charge of shelters they had not provided.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The complaint the investigator says he heard most often is about the authorities, which is what this enquiry is about.","Turn a complaint into a question a record could close: ask what was decided, by whom and on what date.","Shelter policy was settled by ministers as well as by the borough, so think where a government decision and a minister's answer end up on paper."],"codes":["12-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Framing of questions relevant to the pursuit of a specific enquiry","12-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Knowledge of national sources","12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The impact of the Blitz on civilian life in London"]},{"q":"Study Sources A and B in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into conditions in London's air-raid shelters during the Blitz? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks, levelled. Level 3 (6–8) needs judgements on usefulness for this enquiry with developed reasoning on how nature, origin and purpose affect the usefulness of the content, and contextual knowledge used in interpreting the sources. Credit the sanitation and crowding in A and the platform rules in B, set against the purposes of an unpublished morale report and a censored press photograph.","sol":"Source A is strong for this enquiry because it is an unpublished eyewitness account of the shelters themselves. It gives one tap and fourteen buckets behind a curtain for a shelter the wardens put at three thousand and the tea seller at twice that, and it records that the same families take the same places, so that a stranger is told where he may lie down. Its nature and origin strengthen that: an investigator who slept there four nights, writing an internal report that no censor trimmed. Its purpose qualifies one claim, since Mass Observation was paid by the Ministry of Information to report on morale, so the figure of three thousand is what he was told, not what he counted. My own knowledge confirms the pattern rather than the number, because the shelter under the Fruit and Wool Exchange in Spitalfields was only made habitable when the shelterers formed a committee under Mickey Davis in 1940. Source B is weaker on conditions and stronger on the official version of them: four children laugh at the lens for a photograph passed by the Ministry's censor and captioned to say London wakes with a smile, while the far end of the platform is dark and out of focus. Its incidental details are the useful part, because the chalk line and the marshal with his clipboard show that by 3 October the Tube had rules and an official in charge, within weeks of Londoners forcing the stations open. For this enquiry A is the better guide to what shelters were like and B to what the public was allowed to see of them.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Ask of each source what it lets you see about shelter conditions that the other cannot.","Take nature, origin and purpose in turn and aim each at one named claim inside the source instead of at the source as a whole.","Plan four moves per source — what it shows, why its provenance strengthens or weakens that, one fact of your own that tests it, what it is therefore good for — and finish with a single comparative sentence."],"codes":["12-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Recognition of the strengths and weaknesses of different types of source","12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The impact of the Blitz on civilian life in London","12-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Knowledge of national sources"]},{"q":"Study Sources A and B in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the morale of Londoners in the autumn of 1940? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks, levelled. Level 3 (6–8) needs criteria-based judgements on usefulness for this enquiry with developed reasoning on provenance, and contextual knowledge used on the sources. Credit the small sample and the direction of the anger in A, the staged smiles and the censor in B, and the point that the government was itself measuring morale through Mass Observation and Home Intelligence.","sol":"Source A is written for exactly this enquiry, which is its strength and its limit. Mass Observation was paid by the Ministry of Information to report on morale, so the investigator asks the question directly and reports the result: of thirty-one people asked, twenty-two said they would stay whatever came, six would go only if their children went too, and three would not answer. His summary, that spirits are high and tempers are short and that these are not in contradiction, is more useful than either half alone, because it separates willingness to endure from contentment with the authorities. Provenance cuts both ways here: an unpublished internal report is candid, but a paid observer looking for morale in two boroughs over four nights is a tiny and self-selected sample, and the anger he records against the borough and 'the people up west' is what he was listening for. Source B is useless as evidence of feeling and useful as evidence of management of feeling. Four children turned to the camera and laughing, under a caption saying that London sleeps sound below ground and wakes with a smile, is a picture assembled by a press photographer with a permit and passed by the Ministry's censor, which is why the fifth child looking away survives as the only unmanaged face in it. My own knowledge explains why such pictures mattered: the same Ministry was reading Home Intelligence reports that were far less cheerful. For this enquiry A is much the more useful, and B is useful only once you ask who made it and why.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["One of these sources was made to record morale and the other to shape it; that difference is the whole answer.","Use the numbers the investigator gives, but weigh how many people he actually asked and over how long.","For the photograph, decide what it cannot show and then what it can show instead, and make the second half of that sentence the useful one."],"codes":["12-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Recognition of the strengths and weaknesses of different types of source","12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The historical context of the Second World War","12-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Knowledge of national sources"]},{"q":"Study Sources A and B in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into how the authorities dealt with the problems of sheltering in London? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks, levelled. Level 3 (6–8) needs judgements on usefulness for this enquiry, with provenance used on named claims and contextual knowledge used on the sources. Credit the uncounted shelter, the failing rest centres and the telephoneless post in A, and the chalk line, the marshal and the tea urn in B as evidence of provision arriving.","sol":"Both sources are about official provision, which makes them well matched to this enquiry, and they disagree usefully. Source A shows the authorities absent or overwhelmed: a shelter nobody has counted, one tap and fourteen buckets, a man sent between rest centres for three days who signed nothing and was given nothing, a wardens' post with no telephone since Tuesday, and the complaint that nobody in authority had come down to look at the place. Because this is an unpublished report by an investigator inside the shelters, none of that has been softened for print, and its purpose, to report on morale, does not distort a list of physical failures. Source B, published a week later, shows provision arriving: a line chalked on the platform that bedding must not cross, a shelter marshal with an armband and a clipboard at the foot of the stairs, and a tea urn on a trestle table. Its nature and purpose weaken it badly as a measure of how much had been done, since a photograph taken under permit and passed by the Ministry's censor for a caption about London waking with a smile will show the tidiest platform available, and the far end is left dark. My own knowledge sets the two in order: the government had refused to open the Tube at all until Londoners went down and stayed, and about 177,000 slept there on 27 September 1940, so B records the state catching up with a situation it had not chosen. A is the more useful for the failures, B for the remedies, and only together do they show the sequence.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The two sources are a week apart, and one was published while the other was not; let that difference do some of the work.","List what each source shows the authorities doing, or failing to do, before you say anything about reliability.","Aim the censorship point at one particular thing the photograph cannot be trusted to show, then say what it still proves."],"codes":["12-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Recognition of the strengths and weaknesses of different types of source","12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The impact of the Blitz on civilian life in London","12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: London's response to the war"]},{"q":"Study Sources A and B in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the experiences of people in the East End who had been bombed out of their homes? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks, levelled. Level 3 (6–8) needs judgements on usefulness for this narrower enquiry, with provenance used on named claims and contextual knowledge used on the sources. Credit the rest-centre testimony and the settled shelter families in A, and B's near-silence on the homeless, judged as a limit of the source and of what the censor allowed.","sol":"Source A reaches this enquiry directly at one point and indirectly at several. Directly, it carries the words of a man bombed out on the Saturday who had been sent from one rest centre to another for three days and had signed nothing and been given nothing. Indirectly, the detail that the same families take the same places, arriving from five o'clock with bedding, suggests people who are living in the shelter rather than visiting it. The nature of the source makes both worth having, since an unpublished report was under no obligation to be encouraging, though the rest-centre story is one man's account relayed by an investigator who did not check it, and Mass Observation's purpose was morale rather than administration, so nothing is counted. My own knowledge supports the picture and explains the anger: rest centres were run by the boroughs with the Women's Voluntary Service and were designed for a night or two, and the South Hallsville School disaster in Canning Town in the early hours of 10 September 1940 killed families waiting in a rest centre for buses that had not come. Source B is much weaker for this enquiry. Nobody in it is identified as homeless, and a photograph taken under permit and passed by the censor for a cheerful caption was never going to show the bombed-out. Its usefulness is negative but real: what an approved picture leaves out is evidence of what the public was not being shown. A is therefore the more useful by a wide margin, and B mainly shows the gap.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["This enquiry is narrower than the sources are, so decide first how much of each one actually touches it.","A source that barely reaches the enquiry can still be judged: ask why it does not, and whether that itself is evidence.","Bring one dated fact about what happened to bombed-out families and use it to test the complaint in the report rather than to fill space."],"codes":["12-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Recognition of the strengths and weaknesses of different types of source","12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The impact of the Blitz on civilian life in London","12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The context of London in the Second World War"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date did the Blitz on London begin, and on what date did it end?","a":"It began on 7 September 1940 and ended on 11 May 1941."},{"q":"How many people died in the crush on the stairs at Bethnal Green Underground station on 3 March 1943?","a":"173."},{"q":"Which store was destroyed by the V-2 rocket that fell on New Cross Road, Deptford, on 25 November 1944, and how many people were killed?","a":"The Woolworths store; 168 people were killed."},{"q":"In which month and year did the Cabinet War Rooms beneath Whitehall come into use?","a":"August 1939 — they came into use on 27 August 1939, a week before war was declared."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Black Saturday, 7 September 1940","back":"The first mass raid on London: about 350 bombers attacked the docks in daylight, setting the East End alight. Around 430 people were killed and some 1,600 seriously injured that night; the fires guided the bombers back after dark.","code":"12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The nature of attacks on London","open":false},{"front":"The Blitz on London, 1940–41","back":"Ran from 7 September 1940 to 11 May 1941, with London bombed on 57 consecutive nights to 2 November 1940. The heaviest raid, on 10 and 11 May 1941, killed about 1,400 people and destroyed the Commons chamber.","code":"12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The nature of attacks on London","open":false},{"front":"The Anderson shelter","back":"Corrugated steel sheets bolted together, sunk four feet into a garden under fifteen inches of earth, holding six people. Free below £250 a year, £7 above. About 1.5 million issued by September 1939; damp, and useless without a garden.","code":"12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The context of London in the Second World War","open":false},{"front":"The evacuation of London's children, from 1 September 1939","back":"The scheme opened two days before war was declared; about 1.5 million people left the target cities in the first days. London children went from their schools with labels, gas masks and teachers; many drifted home during the Phoney War.","code":"12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The context of London in the Second World War","open":false},{"front":"The V-1 flying bomb","back":"A pulse-jet pilotless bomb, first hitting London at Grove Road, Mile End, on 13 June 1944. Its engine cut out before it dived, giving seconds of warning. Roughly 2,400 reached London, one destroying the Guards' Chapel on 18 June 1944.","code":"12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The nature of attacks on London","open":false},{"front":"The V-2 rocket","back":"A supersonic ballistic rocket that arrived without any warning. The first fell on Staveley Road, Chiswick, on 8 September 1944; about 500 hit London. Explosions were called burst gas mains until Churchill admitted the rockets on 10 November 1944.","code":"12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The nature of attacks on London","open":false},{"front":"The V-2 on New Cross Road, Deptford, 25 November 1944","back":"A rocket struck the Woolworths store on a busy Saturday lunchtime, killing 168 people. It was the worst single V-2 incident in Britain and the attack the specification names for Deptford.","code":"12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The nature of attacks on London","open":false},{"front":"Sheltering in the London Underground","back":"The government refused to open the stations, fearing a 'deep shelter mentality'; Londoners bought tickets and stayed. 177,000 slept in the Tube on 27 September 1940. A bomb burst the mains above Balham on 14 October 1940, killing about 66.","code":"12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The impact of the Blitz on civilian life in London","open":false},{"front":"London's two great shelter disasters","back":"South Hallsville School, Canning Town: a bomb hit the rest centre full of bombed-out families in the early hours of 10 September 1940, and the official toll of 73 was widely disbelieved locally; Bethnal Green, 3 March 1943: 173 people were crushed on the stairs of the unfinished Tube station.","code":"12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The impact of the Blitz on civilian life in London","open":true},{"front":"'Mickey's shelter', Spitalfields","back":"The basement of the Fruit and Wool Exchange, Brushfield Street, Spitalfields, where thousands sheltered in squalor. Mickey Davis, an optician sheltering there himself, was put in charge of a shelter committee in 1940 and brought in bunks and a canteen.","code":"12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The impact of the Blitz on civilian life in London","open":false},{"front":"The Cabinet War Rooms","back":"An underground headquarters in the basement of the government offices in Whitehall, in use from 27 August 1939. The Map Room was manned round the clock until August 1945, and the War Cabinet met there about 115 times.","code":"12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: London's response to the war","open":false},{"front":"'Dig for Victory' in London","back":"Launched by the Ministry of Agriculture in October 1939. Much of Victoria Park in the East End and the dry moat of the Tower of London were dug for vegetables; allotments in Britain rose to roughly 1.4 million by 1943.","code":"12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: London's response to the war","open":false},{"front":"The Ministry of Information","back":"Created on 4 September 1939 and housed at Senate House. It vetted press, photographs and newsreels, withheld bomb locations and casualty figures, and could suppress a paper — the Daily Worker was banned in January 1941.","code":"12-env — 1 London and the Second World War: The historical context of the Second World War","open":false},{"front":"Provenance","back":"The nature, origin and purpose of a source: what kind of record it is, who made it and when, and what it was made to do. A purpose redirects a source rather than cancelling it.","code":"12-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Recognition of the strengths and weaknesses of different types of source","open":false},{"front":"Records that could answer a follow-up question about wartime London","back":"Wardens' post log books and incident reports; borough ARP and shelter committee minutes and rest-centre registers; London County Council bomb damage maps; Home Intelligence and Mass Observation reports; Hansard, newsreels and the censored press.","code":"12-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Selection of appropriate sources for specific investigations","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The sources written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Source A: From a report written by an investigator for Mass Observation and sent to the organisation's London office on 26 September 1940. Mass Observation, founded in 1937, was by this date being paid by the Ministry of Information to report on civilian morale, and its reports were not published at the time. The investigator had spent four nights in shelters in Stepney and Poplar. Written for this room in the style of such a report; it is illustrative, not a real document, and it reproduces no real one. — I have now spent four nights in the basements and railway arches between the Commercial Road and the river. The big shelter under the warehouse holds more people than anybody has counted: the wardens say three thousand, and the woman at the tea stall says twice that. There is one tap, and behind a curtain at the far end there are fourteen buckets for all of them. Families come in from five o'clock with their bedding, and the same families take the same places, so that a stranger is told where he may and may not lie down. Of thirty-one people I asked whether they would leave London if they could, twenty-two said they would stay whatever came, six said they would go only if their children went with them, and three would not answer me. The anger I heard was not against the Germans, who were mentioned less than I expected, but against the borough and the rest centre and 'the people up west', and the sentence said to me most often was that nobody in authority had come down to look at the place. A man bombed out on the Saturday told me he had been sent from one rest centre to another for three days and had signed nothing and been given nothing. When the all clear went, the queue at the tea stall was longer than it had been all night, and two men went from it straight to their work in the docks. I would put it that spirits are high and tempers are short, and that these two things are not in contradiction. The wardens' post on the corner has had no telephone since Tuesday.","Source B: A photograph published in a London evening newspaper on 3 October 1940, under a caption saying that London sleeps sound below ground and wakes with a smile. It was taken by a press photographer working with a permit and was submitted to the Ministry of Information's censors before printing. Written for this room as a description of such a photograph; the image is illustrative, not a real photograph, and it reproduces no real one. — The photograph looks along the platform of an Underground station at night. Along the wall, clear of the platform edge, about twenty people lie in rows on blankets with coats folded for pillows, behind a line chalked on the platform that the bedding is not to cross. In the middle of the picture a woman in a clean apron is pouring tea from an urn on a trestle table, and four children sit on a bench in front of her holding mugs, all four turned towards the camera and laughing; a fifth child stands beside them holding a doll and looking away. Two men in shirtsleeves are playing cards on an upturned crate, and behind them a poster on the curved wall advertises a West End revue. An elderly man sits upright against the tiles with his gas-mask case on his knees. A shelter marshal with an armband and a clipboard stands at the foot of the stairs. The far end of the platform, beyond the tea urn, is dark and out of focus.","How the room uses them: Sources A and B are the pair for every 8-mark usefulness question in this room, and Source A is the source for every follow-up question. Every quotation and every detail used in the worked examples, the model paragraphs, the plan and the model answers comes from the two texts above and from nowhere else; check each one against them as an examiner would."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Paper 1 Question 2(a), 8 marks)","items":["Question: 'How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into how Londoners themselves organised the big shelters they used during the Blitz? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context.' [8 marks]. This is the paragraph on Source A from the middle of the answer.","Source A is very useful for this enquiry because its author watched the ordering being done and reports it as a rule rather than as an impression: families come in from five o'clock with their bedding, the same families take the same places, and a stranger is told where he may and may not lie down. Nobody in the account has issued that rule. There is no marshal in it, nobody from the borough on the spot, and no register of any kind, and the only figures anyone can offer are the wardens' three thousand and the tea seller's twice that, so what order exists has been made from the floor up. Its nature and origin make those details unusually trustworthy. This is an internal report sent to Mass Observation's own office, unpublished and therefore untouched by the Ministry of Information's censors, written by an observer with four nights in the arches behind him rather than by a journalist on a conducted visit. Its purpose bends one claim in particular and should be aimed there rather than at the source as a whole: Mass Observation was being paid by the Ministry to report on morale, so the investigator's ear is on what people say, and that nobody in authority had come down to look at the place is the sentence he heard most often, a complaint he is recording rather than a fact he has checked. My own knowledge confirms the pattern and shows where four nights in September had to stop. At the Fruit and Wool Exchange in Brushfield Street the shelterers formed their own committee under Mickey Davis in 1940, which set hygiene rules and cleaning rotas, kept a card index of everyone using the shelter and opened a canteen paid for by a donation from Marks & Spencer, the profits buying free milk for the children; at the Tilbury shelter in the Commercial Road goods depot the shelterers elected marshals of their own and set the first rules of order and hygiene themselves. Source A is therefore strong evidence of self-organisation at its earliest and most informal, and weak on what the shelterers went on to build, because it was written before the committees were made.","Why this is Level 3: the judgement is on utility 'for the specified enquiry', and it applies a criterion — whether the source shows the shelterers doing the organising themselves — instead of pronouncing the source good or bad in general. Provenance is used rather than described: the unpublished, internal character of the report is made to strengthen three named details, and the paid morale brief is aimed at one particular sentence, which is what 'developed reasoning which takes into account how the provenance affects the usefulness of the source content' requires. The contextual knowledge (the Brushfield Street committee under Mickey Davis with its cleaning rotas, card index and canteen, and the marshals the Tilbury shelterers elected for themselves) is used on the source, to confirm one part and to date the limit of another, never offered as background. The closing sentence names what the source is good for and what has to be discounted, which is the move that separates the top level from the middle."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same as above — 'How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into how Londoners themselves organised the big shelters they used during the Blitz?' [8 marks]. Again this is the paragraph on Source A.","Source A is quite useful because it was written by someone who actually went into the shelters, so he saw it with his own eyes and he knew what it was really like. He says the same families take the same places and a stranger is told where he may lie down, which shows the people organised it themselves and made their own rules. He also says the wardens said three thousand were in there and the woman at the tea stall said twice that, which shows nobody official was in charge of counting them. However, the source is biased because Mass Observation worked for the government, so he might have written what they wanted to hear. It is also only four nights in two boroughs, so it does not tell us about the whole of London. Overall it is fairly useful but it has its limits.","Why this is Level 2 and not Level 3, and where in the band: it does give 'judgements on source utility for the specified enquiry … using valid criteria', and it does select material from the source to support them, which is what Level 2 asks for. What it lacks is Level 3's 'developed reasoning which takes into account how the provenance affects the usefulness of the source content'. 'Biased because Mass Observation worked for the government' is a label pointed at the whole source: it never says which claim inside the report that brief would bend, and in fact it points the wrong way, because an unpublished internal report was freer than anything the censor passed for print. There is no contextual knowledge at all — the Brushfield Street committee under Mickey Davis, its cleaning rotas and card index, the marshals the Tilbury shelterers elected are all missing — and Level 2 expects contextual knowledge to be 'used directly to support comments on the usefulness of the content of the sources and/or their provenance', so an answer carrying none of it cannot rise inside the band however tidily it reads. 'Fairly useful but it has its limits' decides nothing. That puts it at the bottom of Level 2: 3 marks out of 8. To lift it: attach every provenance comment to one named claim, and bring one dated fact to bear on the source."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: London's preparations for war, 1937–39","items":["The Air Raid Precautions Act of 1937 required local authorities to make air-raid plans and to recruit and train ARP personnel.","ARP wardens worked from sector posts, enforced the blackout, reported incidents by telephone, guided people to shelters and checked casualties; most were part-time volunteers.","The Auxiliary Fire Service was set up in 1938 to reinforce the regular brigades; in August 1941 the AFS and the local brigades were merged into a single National Fire Service.","The Women's Voluntary Service was founded in 1938 and ran rest centres, mobile canteens, clothing depots and billeting for evacuees.","About 38 million gas masks had been issued across Britain by September 1939; the expected attack was gas and immediate mass casualties, which is not what came.","Around 1.5 million Anderson shelters had been distributed by the outbreak of war: corrugated steel, sunk four feet into a garden under fifteen inches of earth, holding six people, free below an income of £250 a year and £7 above it.","The blackout began on 1 September 1939; no light was to show from a window, vehicle or torch, and wardens enforced it.","Evacuation under the government scheme began on 1 September 1939, two days before the declaration of war; about 1.5 million people were moved out of the target cities in the first days.","London children went from their own schools, with a label, a gas mask and a case, travelling under their teachers to reception areas where they were billeted on households.","Evacuation was voluntary and many families brought their children home during the Phoney War of 1939–40, so London was fuller than planned when the bombing began."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Black Saturday and the bombing of the East End docks","items":["London was a target because it held the government, the largest port in the country and major industry, and lay within easy range of airfields in occupied France and the Low Countries.","The Thames was a navigation aid: bombers could follow the river in and find the docks even at night.","The docks ran down the river in a line — the Royal Victoria and Royal Albert at Silvertown and North Woolwich, the Surrey Commercial at Rotherhithe, the West India on the Isle of Dogs — with terraced housing pressed against them.","Black Saturday, 7 September 1940: roughly 350 bombers with a heavy fighter escort attacked in daylight in the late afternoon, the first mass raid on London.","About 430 people were killed and some 1,600 seriously injured that night; the fires started in daylight guided the night raid that followed.","Warehouses of timber, sugar, rum and paint burned fiercely, and the fires in the Surrey Commercial docks were among the largest the fire services had ever faced.","The raid made thousands homeless at once and overwhelmed the rest centres that were supposed to hold people for a night or two.","London was then bombed on 57 consecutive nights, from 7 September to 2 November 1940.","The Blitz on London ran to 11 May 1941; the raid of 10 and 11 May killed about 1,400 people and destroyed the chamber of the House of Commons.","Close to 30,000 civilians were killed in the London Civil Defence Region during the war as a whole, about 20,000 of them during the Blitz of 1940–41."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the bombs, the incendiaries and the V-weapons","items":["High-explosive bombs ranged from small ones up to parachute mines, which were dropped by parachute so that the blast spread sideways and could flatten a whole terrace.","Delayed-action and unexploded bombs closed streets, railways and factories until bomb-disposal squads dealt with them, so the disruption outlasted the raid.","The standard incendiary was a small magnesium bomb of about a kilogram, dropped by the hundred in containers; individually weak, they started the fires that did the damage.","Incendiaries had to be smothered with sand or a stirrup pump within minutes, which is why fire-watching on roofs was organised and then made a duty during 1940 and 1941.","The raid of 29 December 1940 set the City of London alight, burning out the Guildhall and a number of Wren churches; the photograph of St Paul's dome above the smoke ran in the Daily Mail two days later.","Operation Steinbock, the 'Baby Blitz', brought renewed raids on London between January and May 1944.","The first V-1 flying bomb to hit London fell on Grove Road, Mile End, on 13 June 1944; its pulse-jet engine cut out before the dive, so it gave a few seconds of warning.","Roughly 2,400 V-1s reached the London region. V-1s killed about 6,200 people in the United Kingdom as a whole and about nine-tenths of those deaths were in London, so some 5,500; one struck the Guards' Chapel at Wellington Barracks on 18 June 1944, killing 121.","The first V-2 rocket fell on Staveley Road, Chiswick, on 8 September 1944; supersonic, it arrived with no warning at all, and about 500 hit London.","The worst single V-2 incident was at the Woolworths store in New Cross Road, Deptford, on 25 November 1944, which killed 168 people; the last V-2 on London hit Hughes Mansions in Stepney on 27 March 1945, killing 134."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: sheltering in London — Anderson, Morrison, public shelters and the Tube","items":["The Anderson shelter protected against blast and splinters but not a direct hit; it was cold, often flooded, and useless to the many East End families with no garden.","The Morrison shelter, introduced in March 1941 and named after Herbert Morrison, Home Secretary from October 1940, was a steel cage the size of a table for use indoors.","Brick-and-concrete street shelters and trench shelters were provided by the boroughs, and basements of warehouses and railway arches were pressed into use as improvised shelters.","The government refused to open the Underground, fearing a 'deep shelter mentality' and disruption to the railway; Londoners bought tickets, went down and stayed, and the refusal collapsed by the end of September 1940.","About 177,000 people slept in the Underground on the night of 27 September 1940, the peak recorded by the railway's own count.","Bunks, chemical closets, first-aid posts and a refreshment service were installed in Tube stations during the winter of 1940–41, and shelter marshals were appointed to keep order.","A Ministry survey in November 1940 found that most Londoners were not using public shelters at all: only about 4 per cent were in the Tube and about 9 per cent in other public shelters, with roughly 27 per cent in domestic shelters and the rest staying in their own homes.","The Tube was not safe: a bomb burst the water mains and sewers above Balham station on 14 October 1940, killing about 66 people, and in January 1941 a bomb reached the booking hall at Bank.","'Mickey's shelter' was the basement of the Fruit and Wool Exchange in Brushfield Street, Spitalfields, where thousands sheltered; Mickey Davis, an optician who sheltered there himself, was put in charge of a shelter committee in 1940 and brought in bunks, a canteen and a sick bay.","Other large improvised shelters, such as the Tilbury warehouse shelter in Stepney, took thousands a night in conditions the authorities had neither planned nor inspected."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the shelter disasters — South Hallsville and Bethnal Green","items":["South Hallsville School in Canning Town was a rest centre, not a shelter: it held families bombed out on Black Saturday who were waiting for buses to take them out of the area.","The buses did not arrive, partly because they were sent to the wrong district, and the families were still there when a bomb struck in the early hours of 10 September 1940.","The official death toll was given as 73; local people believed the true figure was far higher, and the rubble was sealed rather than fully cleared.","Nothing precise was published at the time, because casualty figures and locations were withheld, which is one reason the local belief hardened.","The disaster exposed the weakness of rest centres, which were designed to hold people for a night or two and had no plan for mass homelessness.","The Bethnal Green disaster happened on 3 March 1943 at the unfinished Bethnal Green Underground station, which was in use as a shelter.","A crowd hurrying down the wet, unlit stairway after the sirens, startled by the noise of new anti-aircraft rockets firing from Victoria Park, was crushed when people at the front fell.","173 people died, most of them women and children; they were crushed and suffocated, not killed by any bomb.","The government held an inquiry but did not publish its findings at the time, for fear of damaging morale and of encouraging blame of the authorities.","Both disasters are the standard test of the 'Blitz spirit' story: they show civilians killed by failures of provision rather than by the enemy, and both were managed as much as they were investigated."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: leisure, work and morale in wartime London","items":["London's theatres closed at the outbreak of war in September 1939 and most reopened within weeks; the Windmill Theatre in Soho advertised that it never closed.","The National Gallery sent its paintings to safety and put on Myra Hess's lunchtime concerts in the empty building from October 1939.","Dance halls, cinemas and restaurants stayed open through the raids; the Café de Paris in Coventry Street was bombed on 8 March 1941 and more than thirty people were killed, including the bandleader Ken 'Snakehips' Johnson.","ENSA, founded in 1939 and based at the Theatre Royal, Drury Lane, organised entertainment for the services and for war workers.","Daily life was disrupted by broken sleep, by gas, water and electricity failures, by blocked roads and unexploded bombs, and by the long journeys home when lines were cut.","Well over a million Londoners were made homeless at some point during the Blitz, and rehousing, salvage and compensation became a permanent municipal business.","Morale was watched, not assumed: the Ministry of Information's Home Intelligence Division reported regularly on public feeling, and Mass Observation, founded in 1937, sent investigators and collected diaries.","Those reports show morale as uneven — anger at local authorities, resentment between districts, 'trekking' out of the city at night — alongside real endurance and very high attendance at work.","Government worried most about the East End in September 1940, where damage, homelessness and the failure of rest centres came together in the same fortnight.","Rationing, queues and the blackout wore people down over years in a way single raids did not, which is why studies of morale look at the whole war rather than at the worst nights."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: London's response — the Royal Family, the Cabinet War Rooms and 'Dig for Victory'","items":["Winston Churchill became Prime Minister on 10 May 1940 and made a point of being seen in bombed streets in the East End and the City.","The King and Queen stayed in London; Buckingham Palace was hit several times, including on 13 September 1940 while the King and Queen were in residence.","Queen Elizabeth said after that raid that she could now look the East End in the face, a remark used to link the Palace with the bombed boroughs.","The Cabinet War Rooms, in the basement of the government offices in Great George Street, Whitehall, came into use on 27 August 1939, a week before war was declared.","They contained the Cabinet Room, the Map Room, a transatlantic telephone room and sleeping quarters; the Map Room was manned round the clock until August 1945.","The War Cabinet met in the rooms about 115 times during the war, chiefly during the heaviest bombing of 1940–41 and again during the V-weapon attacks.","The building above was strengthened with a thick concrete slab, though the rooms would not have survived a direct hit by a heavy bomb.","'Dig for Victory' was launched by the Ministry of Agriculture in October 1939 to cut the food that had to be imported through the U-boat blockade.","In London, much of Victoria Park in the East End was turned over to allotments, while other parts of the same park held anti-aircraft guns and rocket batteries; the dry moat of the Tower of London was dug and planted with vegetables.","The number of allotments in Britain rose to roughly 1.4 million by 1943, and parks, verges and bombed sites in London were cultivated."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the purpose of the Blitz, propaganda and censorship","items":["The Luftwaffe turned to London in September 1940 after failing to destroy Fighter Command, aiming to wreck the port and war industry, force the RAF into battle over the capital and break civilian will.","It failed in the last aim: production and government continued, and the switch away from the airfields helped Fighter Command recover.","The Ministry of Information was created on 4 September 1939 and housed at Senate House, University of London; it controlled press, photographic and newsreel censorship.","The Emergency Powers (Defence) Act of 1939 gave the legal powers, and a newspaper could be suppressed altogether — the Daily Worker was banned in January 1941.","Exact bomb locations and casualty figures were withheld so as not to help German aiming, and photographs of panic, despair or bodies were not passed for publication.","Approved images did the opposite work: the photograph of the dome of St Paul's standing above the smoke of the raid of 29 December 1940 appeared in the Daily Mail two days later.","The film 'London Can Take It!' of October 1940, narrated by the American journalist Quentin Reynolds, was made largely for audiences in the United States, whose sympathy Britain needed.","Posters and radio carried the same message: 'Careless Talk Costs Lives' from 1940, and the BBC's bulletins and Churchill's broadcasts framed the raids as endurance.","Censorship continued to the end of the war: V-2 explosions from September 1944 were officially described as burst gas mains until Churchill admitted the rockets to the Commons on 10 November 1944.","For source work this matters twice over: anything published in wartime Britain passed a censor, so it is evidence of the approved version, while unpublished government and Mass Observation reports are often much franker."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: local and national sources for enquiries into wartime London","items":["Local — ARP wardens' post log books and incident report forms: the time, street, type of bomb, casualties and services called, written as the incident was reported.","Local — metropolitan borough council records: ARP and shelter committee minutes, rest-centre and billeting registers, salvage and rehousing papers, correspondence with the Ministry of Home Security.","Local — London County Council bomb damage maps, which coloured each building by the degree of damage as the surveyors recorded it.","Local — newspapers such as the East London Advertiser, which reported raids within the censor's limits and printed funerals, appeals and complaints.","Local — personal accounts: diaries, letters home, photographs taken by local studios, and oral history interviews recorded long afterwards.","National — Cabinet papers, Ministry of Home Security weekly appreciations and Hansard, which record decisions, national casualty counts and what ministers were prepared to say.","National — Ministry of Information Home Intelligence reports on public feeling, compiled for ministers and not published, and therefore unusually candid.","National — Mass Observation reports, investigators' notes and volunteer diaries, gathered by an organisation founded in 1937 and funded during the war by the Ministry.","National — newsreels, BBC bulletins, the national press and official photographs, all shaped by censorship and by the purpose of the department that approved them.","Memoirs and later interviews are wide in view and late in date: strong on what an experience felt like, weak on numbers and dates, and shaped by everything the writer learned afterwards."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the source moves this paper rewards","items":["Provenance means three things: nature (what kind of document or image it is), origin (who made it, when and where) and purpose (what it was made to do).","Usefulness is always usefulness for the enquiry the question names; the same photograph can be very useful for one enquiry and useless for the next, so write the enquiry down before you start.","Content first: say what the source tells you, or lets you infer, about the named enquiry, and anchor it on a short quoted phrase or a named detail in the picture.","Provenance second, and aimed: explain how the nature, origin or purpose makes that particular inference stronger or weaker. 'The source is biased' scores nothing because it changes no inference.","Context third, and used: bring one piece of your own knowledge that confirms, qualifies or explains what the source says; contextual knowledge earns nothing unless it is linked to evaluating the source.","In wartime Britain the censor is a provenance fact, not a topic: anything published passed the Ministry of Information, and anything unpublished, such as a Home Intelligence or Mass Observation report, did not.","A limitation is often a use in disguise: a censored press photograph is bad evidence of how a shelter smelt and excellent evidence of the impression the government wanted the public to hold.","Finish with a comparative judgement: which source is more useful for this enquiry, on what criterion, and for which part of it — not which source is 'better'.","The follow-up answer has four parts and each must fit the one before: a detail taken from the source, a question that detail raises, a type of source that could answer it, and how that type would help.","Name a type of record, not a single document — wardens' log books, borough ARP and shelter committee minutes, rest-centre registers, LCC bomb damage maps, Home Intelligence and Mass Observation reports, Hansard, censored press, newsreels, coroners' papers, oral history — and then say what it contains that would settle the question."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 1 Question 2(a): 'How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the experience of families with young children in London's shelters during the Blitz?' [8 marks]","items":["The question as printed: 'Study Sources A and B in the Sources Booklet. How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the experience of families with young children in London's shelters during the Blitz? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context.' No stimulus points are printed and no SPaG marks are awarded; the two sources are the whole material, and both must be used.","Decode the enquiry before reading either source. 'Families with young children' means who was in the shelters and what a night there was like for them: where a family slept and how it held its place, what the children were given to eat and drink, what was provided for them and by whom, and the choice their parents faced between staying in London and sending them away. Decide which of those each source can reach, because that is your criterion for usefulness.","Timing: about 13 minutes. Two minutes reading and annotating both sources against the enquiry, nine minutes writing two paragraphs and a judgement, two minutes checking that every provenance comment is attached to a named claim and that each piece of context is used on a source.","Paragraph 1, Source A, content: families come in from five o'clock with their bedding and the same families take the same places, so that a stranger is told where he may and may not lie down; of thirty-one people asked whether they would leave London, twenty-two said they would stay whatever came and six said they would go only if their children went with them; there is one tap, and fourteen buckets behind a curtain, for a crowd nobody has counted; the tea stall a woman keeps is where the queue forms when the all clear goes.","Paragraph 1, Source A, provenance used twice. Nature and origin strengthen it: an unpublished internal report to Mass Observation's own office, written by an observer with four nights in the arches behind him, so no censor has trimmed it and no reader has to be reassured. Purpose qualifies one claim: Mass Observation was paid by the Ministry of Information to report on morale, so the thirty-one answers are a count he made of a question he chose to ask, and they record what parents said in September about leaving, not what any family went on to do.","Paragraph 1, Source A, context used on the source: about half the children evacuated under the government scheme in September 1939 had gone home by January 1940, so there were young families in the East End shelters when the raids began; and the committee of inquiry into shelters under Lord Horder, appointed in September 1940 and reporting that November, found overcrowding the crux of the problem and pressed evacuation as the remedy — which is exactly the choice the six parents in the source are describing. Interim judgement: very useful for how a family held its place and for the decision in front of it, and thin on the children themselves.","Paragraph 2, Source B, content: four children sitting on a bench with mugs, all four turned to the camera and laughing, and a fifth standing beside them with a doll and looking away; about twenty people lying in rows on blankets behind a line chalked on the platform; a woman in a clean apron pouring tea from an urn on a trestle table; an elderly man with his gas-mask case on his knees; a shelter marshal with an armband and a clipboard at the foot of the stairs; the far end of the platform dark and out of focus.","Paragraph 2, Source B, provenance used twice. Nature and purpose weaken it on experience: a press photograph taken under permit, passed by the Ministry of Information's censors and captioned to say London wakes with a smile, so four laughing children with mugs are the caption's argument rather than evidence of how a child passed the night. The same provenance strengthens it for what nobody would trouble to stage — the chalk line the bedding may not cross, the marshal's clipboard, the fifth child who is not looking at the camera at all — which show children living inside a platform run to rules by 3 October 1940.","Paragraph 2, Source B, context used on the source: an estimated twenty-five thousand children were in the Tube stations nightly at the height of the raids, and the first platform refreshment service did not open until 29 October 1940, reaching 124 canteen points at 71 stations by 7 December, so an urn being poured for children on a platform on 3 October stands ahead of the official provision and was voluntary, improvised or arranged. Interim judgement: worthless as a measure of what the children's nights were like, valuable as evidence of what had been organised on one platform by early October and of the approved picture of it.","The evaluation thread that must run through both paragraphs: a purpose does not cancel a source, it redirects it. Say for each what the purpose makes the source bad evidence of and what it makes it good evidence of, and you have made the top-level move twice.","What the judgement must say: not 'both are useful in different ways'. Name the criterion — access to what a night in a shelter was actually like for a family with young children — and decide on it. Source A is much the more useful for the family's own arrangements and for the choice its parents faced, because it is unpublished, specific and written from inside, with only its one small sample to be handled carefully; Source B is the more useful for what had been put on a platform for children by early October and for the version of it the public was allowed to see. Together they give a sequence neither reaches alone: families took the shelters and made their own order in them, the authorities organised and photographed the result, and evacuation stayed the remedy the government pressed."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:40:00Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Judged afresh after the second dedupe pass, and every claim the retargeted items make about the printed sources checks out word by word: Source A's four nights between the Commercial Road and the river, the one tap, the fourteen buckets behind a curtain, the tea stall and its 'twice that', the five o'clock bedding, the dawn queue and the two men going to the docks, the sentence heard most often and the post with no telephone since Tuesday; Source B's 3 October 1940 caption, the permit and the censor, the clean apron and the urn on the trestle table, the four laughing children with mugs and the fifth with a doll, the chalk line, the marshal's armband and clipboard, the gas-mask case, the card players and the dark far end. The new context all stands up on the web: the first Tube platform refreshment service on 29 October 1940 and 124 canteen points at 71 stations serving about 112,000 people by 7 December 1940¹; Mickey Davis, the optician elected shelter marshal at the Fruit and Wool Exchange in Brushfield Street in 1940, his committee's hygiene rules, cleaning rotas and card index and the Marks & Spencer canteen whose profits bought free milk for the children²; the marshals the Tilbury shelterers elected for themselves in the Commercial Road goods depot, with the first rules of order and hygiene³; the Horder committee, set up 14 September 1940 and reporting that November, 'the crux of the problem is overcrowding', with dispersal and evacuation urged as the remedy⁴; and, in example 0, the Morrison shelter of March 1941, 6ft 6in by 4ft by 2ft 6in, free below about £350, half a million distributed by the end of 1941 with about 100,000 more in 1943, named for Herbert Morrison, Home Secretary from October 1940, and the WVS founded in 1938 at the government's request for ARP work, running rest centres, mobile canteens, clothing and billeting, over a million members by 1943⁵. About seventy dates, figures and events were re-checked in all (Black Saturday and the 57 nights to 2 November 1940; Balham 14 October 1940; South Hallsville 10 September 1940; Bethnal Green 3 March 1943 and its 173; New Cross Woolworths 25 November 1944 and its 168; Grove Road, Mile End 13 June 1944 and Staveley Road 8 September 1944; Cabinet War Rooms 27 August 1939; Churchill on the V-2 on 10 November 1944; about half the 1939 evacuees home by January 1940; some 25,000 children in the Tube nightly; Woolton's request to the London County Council in early September 1940), and none is wrong. No bank question, example, model paragraph or plan reproduces the printed Option 12 Section A enquiries — the specimen sets 'Describe two features of the government's use of propaganda during the Blitz in the Second World War' and, at 2(a) and 2(b), 'the effects of bombs falling on London', and nothing here goes near either (room.questions[6] comes closest, and asks about the censorship of news and the film 'London Can Take It!'). dupscan prints 'no duplication'. Truth to the paper is right for June 2025 — Question 1 in two 2-mark parts, no stimulus points and no SPaG in Section A, 1 hour 20 minutes, and the model pair correctly labelled against the three-level AO3 grid (Level 2 = 3–5, so 'the bottom of Level 2: 3 marks out of 8' is right). No answer key is wrong; what is left is overlap of ground rather than of question, and two presentational slips. ¹ TfL Corporate Archives, 'The experience of sheltering in the Tube during WWII'. ² Spitalfields Life and Optician Online on Mickey Davis. ³ UCL Survey of London, 'The Tilbury shelter'. ⁴ Hansard, 20 November 1940, 'Horder Committee's Report' and 'Air-Raid Shelters and the Public Health'. ⁵ IWM and Royal Voluntary Service."}}},"13-1":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"13-1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This is the first of the four rooms in Migrants in Britain, and it supplies the medieval end of almost every comparison Paper 1 Section B asks for — 36 of that paper's 52 marks. Question 3 sets one period against another, Question 4 asks why something changed across a long stretch of time, and Question 5 or 6 asks you to judge how far migrants mattered. All three need a dated, precise medieval starting point: who came, what drew them, how they were treated and what difference they made. If you cannot say what the Danelaw was, why William I wanted Jewish financiers from Rouen, what the Statute of the Jewry of 1275 did, why Edward III protected Flemish weavers or where the Hanseatic merchants traded from in London, every later comparison collapses into generalisation and the mark stays in Level 1 or 2.","idea":[{"h":"Migration is explained by the society that pulled people in","code":"13 — The process of change: migration and the key features of society","t":"Medieval England was worth coming to, and the examiner wants the features that made it so. It had good arable land and, after 1066, a landholding system in which the king granted everything and could therefore grant rights to an incomer. It had growing towns — Domesday Book in 1086 already lists over a hundred boroughs — with markets, guilds and a demand for credit and for skilled hands. It had the finest wool in Europe, which the cloth towns of Flanders and Italy could not do without. It had kings permanently short of money, who would sell privileges and protection. And it was part of Latin Christendom, so clergy, monks and scholars moved into it as a matter of course. Note also what 'migrant' means here: it covers forced movement as well as voluntary, temporary merchants as well as permanent settlers, and movement inside England as well as arrivals from abroad. A top-level answer never lists migrant groups; it names the feature of English society that explains each group, and shows that the same features keep reappearing."},{"h":"Lead with pull, use push as the qualification","code":"13 — The process of change: push and pull factors","t":"Edexcel is explicit that the primary focus is what drew migrants to Britain, with some awareness of what pushed them out. The pulls are land (Halfdan's men were given Northumbrian land to farm in 876), trade (Baltic amber and Arab silver reached Viking York), royal invitation and protection (Jewish financiers from Rouen in the 1070s, the Flemish weaver John Kempe in 1331), privilege (the Hanseatic League's exemptions in London), and work (towns left short-handed by the Black Death of 1348–49). The pushes are real but secondary: land shortage and the consolidation of royal power in Scandinavia, poverty and disorder in the Low Countries, and persecution or expulsion elsewhere in Europe. A top-level answer opens each reason with the English condition that created the opportunity and adds the push factor as a supporting clause, because an answer built on pushes alone cannot explain why migrants chose England rather than anywhere else."},{"h":"The four key factors, and what each one looks like before 1500","code":"13 — The process of change: the key factors","t":"Edexcel names four factors for the whole theme, and using its words as the spine of your paragraphs is what makes comparison across seven centuries possible. Institutions — government and Church: kings invited, licensed, taxed and expelled, while the Church supplied an international career ladder and forbade Christians to lend at interest. Religion: the usury ban created the Jewish lender's role, crusading preaching helped create the violence of 1189–90, and shared Christianity smoothed the path of Norman and Italian churchmen. Economic influences: wool, credit, town growth and, after 1348–49, a labour shortage. Attitudes in society: the blood libel first recorded at Norwich in 1144, the massacre at York in 1190, the killing of Flemings in London in 1381 — but also the ordinary acceptance recorded in the alien subsidy returns of the 1440s. A top-level answer names the factor, then shows the mechanism by which it produced a migrant, rather than leaving the factor as a heading."},{"h":"Factors rarely act alone, and the case study is where you watch them combine","code":"13 — The process of change: factors working together and the role of the case studies","t":"The strongest answers explain combinations. The expulsion of 1290 is the clearest example: religion had made Jews a target, royal taxation had already stripped them of the wealth that made them useful, the Statute of the Jewry of 1275 had banned the one trade open to them, indebted knights and gentry in Parliament wanted their bonds cancelled, and Edward I wanted a tax grant. No single factor explains it; all four arrive together. The same is true of the Flemish weavers: a king wanting a taxable cloth industry, a wool trade that already existed, and towns emptied by plague. The strand 3 case study, York under the Vikings, exists so that you can watch strands 1 and 2 happen in one place — the reasons for coming, the pattern of settlement, the relations with the existing population and the impact on trade, streets and speech. A top-level answer uses York as evidence for a general claim, not as a story told for its own sake."},{"h":"Who came, and where they settled","code":"13-1 — 1 The context for migration: Reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","t":"Four groups carry this room. Vikings: the Great Heathen Army landed in East Anglia in 865, captured York in 866 and stayed; in 876 Halfdan shared out the land of Northumbria among his men, and settlement concentrated in the east and north — Yorkshire, Lincolnshire, the east Midlands and East Anglia — where hundreds of village names still end in -by, -thorpe and -thwaite. Normans: a small elite after 1066, holding land everywhere but living in castles and great households, together with the churchmen, masons and craftsmen they brought. Jews: from the 1070s, from Rouen in Normandy, settling first in London and then in Lincoln, Norwich, York, Winchester, Oxford, Bristol and Cambridge, usually near the castle. European traders and craftsmen: Flemish weavers in Norwich, Colchester and London, Hanseatic merchants in London, Boston and King's Lynn, and Italians in London and Southampton. A top-level answer always gives the pattern as well as the name: rural and regional for the Vikings, urban and commercial for everybody else."},{"h":"What it was about England that made settlement possible","code":"13-1 — 1 The context for migration: The context of English society","t":"Land, towns, wool, royal finance and the Church are the five headings the specification gives, and each has hard evidence attached. Landownership: after 1066 all land was held of the king, so Domesday Book in 1086 shows England divided among about two hundred great tenants-in-chief, almost all Norman or French — an incomer's rights depended on the Crown. Towns: over a hundred boroughs by 1086 and hundreds of market charters by 1300, offering guilds, credit and work. Wool: England's chief export, taxed by a customs duty on every sack from 1275 and funnelled through the staple fixed at Calais in 1363. Royal finance: kings taxed Jews by tallage, sold trading rights to foreigners by the Carta Mercatoria of 1303, and borrowed from Italian houses such as the Riccardi of Lucca and the Bardi and Peruzzi of Florence. Christendom: one Church, one written language, and a steady traffic of clergy. A top-level answer ties every migrant group back to one of these five, which is how an essay stops being a list."},{"h":"Protection, exploitation and the meaning of 'alien'","code":"13-1 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in England","t":"Experience was set by law first and by neighbours second. Jews were protected because in law they and their goods were the king's, which also meant they could be taxed by tallage at will; after the Ordinances of the Jewry of 1194 their loans were registered in sealed chests, the archae, out of which the Exchequer of the Jews grew, so the Crown could enforce and tax them. Protection was withdrawn when the money ran out: the badge from 1218, the Statute of the Jewry of 1275 banning lending at interest, and expulsion decreed in July 1290 with All Saints' Day as the deadline. For later migrants the category was 'alien': those born abroad could not inherit land, paid higher customs rates, had to lodge with an English host by a law of 1439, and were taxed by the alien subsidy granted from 1440 — unless they bought letters of denization. Popular hostility ran alongside: Norwich in 1144, Richard I's coronation riots in September 1189, York in March 1190, the killing of Flemings in London in 1381. The Black Death of 1348–49 cut both ways, emptying towns that then wanted skilled hands. A top-level answer separates what the law did from what crowds did, and then shows how the two fed each other."},{"h":"What difference migrants made","code":"13-1 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in England","t":"Four kinds of impact are worth evidencing. Law and government: the Danelaw kept Danish custom, its districts were wapentakes rather than hundreds, and Domesday Book still recorded its unusually free sokemen in 1086. Culture and language: Old Norse gave English law, egg, sky, knife, husband and window, and the pronouns they, them and their, while Norman French supplied the vocabulary of rule and law. Trade: Viking York reached the Baltic and beyond, the Hanseatic League brought timber, pitch, furs, wax and fish and took wool and cloth away, and Flemish weavers turned raw wool into a finished export. The built environment: York's tenement plots and its -gate street names from Old Norse gata; hundreds of Norman castles and the White Tower from about 1078; cathedrals rebuilt in the Romanesque style, Durham from 1093; stone Jewish town houses such as the Jew's House in Lincoln; the Hansa's Steelyard at Dowgate and its surviving warehouse of 1475 at King's Lynn. A top-level answer ranks these impacts against a stated criterion instead of listing them."},{"h":"York under the Vikings: the case study that proves the general points","code":"13-1 — 3 Case study: The city of York under the Vikings","t":"The Great Heathen Army took York in 866 and defeated the Northumbrian kings there in 867; after Halfdan divided Northumbria in 876 the city became Jorvik, capital of a Scandinavian kingdom until Eric Bloodaxe, its last Viking king, was driven out and killed in 954. It was a working town, not a camp: the Coppergate excavation of 1976–81 uncovered rows of timber and wattle buildings on narrow plots, with workshops turning wooden cups on pole lathes, cutting leather shoes and making combs from antler, alongside amber from the Baltic, a silk cap, a cowrie shell from far outside Europe and a coin copying an Arab dirham. York had its own mint, issuing coins in the name of St Peter, kept its archbishops throughout, and may have held around ten thousand people. Its street names still end in -gate, from the Old Norse gata, street — Coppergate is the street of the cup-makers. A top-level answer uses York to prove that Viking migration was settlement and trade, not only conquest."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 3, 4 marks: turning two descriptions into one analysed difference","setup":"Paper 1 Question 3, worth 4 marks: 'Explain one way in which the impact of Viking migrants on the English language in the years c865–c1000 was different from the impact of Norman migrants in the years c1066–c1200.' The mark scheme gives 2 marks for AO2 and 2 for AO1, so one difference has to be analysed and supported with specific information from both of the named periods.","steps":["Pick a difference of kind rather than of size: Norse words came into the ordinary speech of ordinary people, because the settlers lived among them, whereas Norman French came in above the population, as the language of rule and of the courts.","Evidence the Viking half: the Great Heathen Army that landed in 865 stayed instead of taking tribute, and after Halfdan shared out the land of Northumbria in 876 Norse speakers farmed the same villages as the English — which is why hundreds of settlements in Yorkshire and Lincolnshire still end in -by and -thorpe, and why the plainest daily words, egg, sky, knife, husband, window and law, are Norse.","Evidence the Norman half: after 1066 French was the tongue of the king's court, the lords and the law, giving English court, judge, justice, jury and prison, and it stayed the language of pleading in the royal courts until the Pleading in English Act of 1362, while the great majority of people went on speaking English throughout.","Close with the sentence that makes it a difference rather than two descriptions: Norse changed English from the inside, because the newcomers were neighbours, while French was laid on top of it, marking who ruled rather than changing how most people spoke."],"cues":["Which single difference is strong enough to carry the whole answer — how many words, or whose words they were?","What dated Viking detail explains why the Norse words are the everyday ones?","What shows how long French kept its hold on the royal courts?","What has the answer still not done that the mark scheme calls analysis?"]},{"title":"Question 4, 12 marks: getting past the two bullets","setup":"Paper 1 Question 4, worth 12 marks: 'Explain why English kings taxed migrants in the years c1200–c1500. You may use the following in your answer: • tallage • the alien subsidy. You must also use information of your own.' The mark scheme gives 6 marks for AO2 and 6 for AO1, and prints two caps: a maximum of 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","steps":["Turn the first bullet into a causal chain rather than a label: in English law Jews and their goods were the king's, so the Crown could levy a tallage on the whole community whenever it chose and without bargaining with anyone, which is why it was the tax kings reached for first and why they kept reaching for it until there was nothing left to take.","Do the same with the second: by the fifteenth century the migrants who mattered were merchants and craftsmen who were nobody's property, so the money had to be voted — and in 1440 Parliament granted the alien subsidy, charged on people born abroad at 16d a year for a householder and 6d for a servant or labourer.","Now supply the reason the bullets do not prompt: foreign merchants held privileges the Crown could sell, so Edward I's Carta Mercatoria of 1303 gave alien traders freedom of trade and protection at law in return for new customs duties on their goods.","Add a second point of your own if there is time: a tax on outsiders was the easiest kind to obtain, because the alien subsidy came out of the parliament of 1439–40 after years of anti-alien petitioning, and because migrants could buy their way out of it — letters of denization, issued from the 1370s for a fee and an oath to the Crown, made an alien a subject and lifted the charge.","Close with the line of argument that ties the reasons together: kings taxed migrants because theirs was the one wealth a Crown could reach quickly and outside the ordinary bargaining over taxation, and because charging outsiders cost a king far less support than charging his own subjects."],"cues":["How can a king tax one group without bargaining for a grant at all?","Why did the fifteenth century have to do it by Act of Parliament instead?","What reason can you add that neither bullet prompts?","Why was a tax aimed at outsiders an easy one to get through?","What must the final sentence do to reach the top level?"]},{"title":"Question 5, 16 marks: setting the criterion before you argue","setup":"Paper 1 Question 5, worth 16 marks with up to 4 further marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology: \"'The wool trade was the main reason why migrants came to England in the years c1066–c1500.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • Flemish weavers • the Hanseatic League. You must also use information of your own.\" AO2 carries 10 marks and AO1 6, and the Level 4 descriptor requires the criteria for the judgement to be justified and applied.","steps":["Name the criterion before writing any narrative: 'main' will be tested by how many migrant groups a factor explains and how long it goes on working across the four and a half centuries named.","Test the wool trade against that criterion: it explains the Flemish weavers, since Edward III granted protection to John Kempe and his workers in 1331 to establish cloth-making here; it explains the Hansa, who shipped wool and cloth out of the Steelyard at Dowgate and out of Boston and King's Lynn; and it explains the Italians who bought wool for Florence and Lucca.","Test the rival factor the bullets do not prompt: the Crown's need for money explains the Jewish community brought from Rouen, the Italian bankers who later replaced them as lenders, and the customs duties that made kings want foreign merchants at all.","Test a second rival: England's place in Latin Christendom brought Lanfranc of Pavia and Anselm of Aosta to Canterbury, Cluniac and Cistercian monks to new abbeys and friars to English towns, none of them pulled by wool.","Reach the judgement by applying the criterion instead of counting paragraphs: wool explains the largest groups for the longest stretch after about 1300 but almost nothing before it, so agree strongly for the later Middle Ages and qualify sharply for the earlier."],"cues":["What does the word 'main' have to be measured against?","Which migrant groups does the named factor actually explain?","Which rival factor do the bullets say nothing about?","Is there a group that neither wool nor money explains?","How do you turn the criterion into a judgement rather than a summary?"]},{"title":"Question 5, 16 marks: writing the paragraph that weighs against the statement","setup":"The same 16-mark question form: \"'Religion was the most important influence on migration to England in the years c1066–c1500.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the ban on usury • the expulsion of 1290. You must also use information of your own.\" You have already written one paragraph agreeing with the statement, using the Church's ban on lending at interest, and you now have to write the paragraph that weighs against it.","steps":["Open with the rival factor stated as a claim, not as a topic: what decided how many migrants came and how long they stayed was the state of the English economy — the credit a growing country needed, the skills it did not have and the hands it was short of.","Prove it with a dated record of the demand: Domesday Book in 1086 already lists over a hundred boroughs, and the markets, guilds and building work that went with them created a need for lending and for skilled trades that no rule of the Church could have created by itself.","Prove it again from a different group so the claim is not about one community: on 16 July 1331 Edward III granted letters of protection to John Kempe of Flanders, a weaver of woollen cloths, and to his men, to work at and teach their trade in England, and after the Black Death of 1348–49 the towns that wanted such hands were emptier still.","Concede the other side honestly and then answer it: the ban on usury was real, and the Third Lateran Council of 1179 refused open usurers communion and Christian burial, but it only explains which trade was left to Jewish migrants — it does not explain why England had borrowers worth lending to, and that was its wealth.","End with the interim judgement the top level requires: religion decided what migrants were permitted to do, but the economy decided whether there was anything here worth coming for, so religion shaped the form migration took while economic need set its scale."],"cues":["How do you open a paragraph so that it argues instead of describing?","What single record shows how much town demand there was to supply?","Why must a second migrant group appear in this paragraph?","How can the strongest evidence for the other side be turned to your advantage?","What does a paragraph in a 16-mark answer have to end with?"]},{"title":"Question 4, 12 marks: using the York case study as evidence","setup":"Paper 1 Question 4, worth 12 marks: 'Explain why Viking settlers changed the city of York in the years c866–c1000. You may use the following in your answer: • trade • the Danelaw. You must also use information of your own.' AO2 is worth 6 marks and AO1 6, and there is no access to Level 4 for an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content.","steps":["Start from the moment the change becomes possible: the Great Heathen Army captured York in 866, and in 876 Halfdan shared out the land of Northumbria, so a raiding force turned into a resident population with a capital city.","Make trade the first explained reason: the Coppergate excavation of 1976–81 uncovered timber workshops turning wooden cups, cutting leather and making antler combs, alongside Baltic amber, a silk cap, a cowrie shell from far outside Europe and a coin copying an Arab dirham.","Make the Danelaw the second: York was the capital of a kingdom under Danish law, struck its own coins at its own mint, and was ruled by Scandinavian kings until Eric Bloodaxe was driven out and killed in 954.","Add the reason beyond the bullets: the settlers rebuilt the town itself on narrow tenement plots, and named its streets with the Old Norse gata, which is why Coppergate, the street of the cup-makers, Micklegate and Stonegate all end in -gate.","Close by saying what the reasons amount to: York changed because a conquering army chose to stay, so a royal city of the Northumbrians became one of the busiest trading towns in northern Europe, perhaps ten thousand strong."],"cues":["What has to happen before an army can change a city at all?","Which excavation supplies the evidence for trade, and what did it find?","What did being the capital of the Danelaw give York?","What can you add about the streets themselves?","What single sentence sums up what the reasons produced?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Why did the Crown protect Jewish moneylenders in the twelfth century?","a":"Because in law Jews and their goods were regarded as the king's, so their wealth could be taken by tallage whenever he chose; protecting them protected royal income."},{"q":"Why was moneylending one of the very few trades open to Jews in medieval England?","a":"The Church forbade Christians to lend at interest, and Jews were excluded from the craft guilds and could not hold land by feudal tenure, so few other livelihoods were available."},{"q":"What does the -by ending of village names in Lincolnshire and Yorkshire tell a historian?","a":"That Scandinavian settlers farmed and named whole districts there, so Viking migration was family settlement on the land and not only conquest by armies."},{"q":"How did the Black Death of 1348–49 affect migration?","a":"It killed perhaps a third of the population, so towns short of labour drew in English peasants from the countryside and welcomed skilled craftsmen from the Low Countries and Germany."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 1 Section B, the thematic study, carries 36 of that paper's 52 marks: Question 3 is worth 4 marks, Question 4 is worth 12, and Question 5 or Question 6 is worth 16 plus up to 4 additional marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology.","Question 3, 'Explain one way in which … was similar to / different from …', carries 2 marks for AO2 and 2 for AO1, and normally spans at least a century, so both of the named periods must be evidenced with specific information.","Question 4 is worth 6 marks for AO2 and 6 for AO1, and prints two stimulus caps: a maximum of 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","Question 5 or 6 is worth 10 marks for AO2 and 6 for AO1, with a maximum of 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; the SPaG marks are awarded separately as Threshold 1, Intermediate 2–3 and High 4.","Timing on the 80-minute Paper 1 is roughly a minute a mark: about 5 minutes on Question 3, 15 on Question 4 and 25 on Question 5 or 6, which is the only Section B answer where spelling and specialist terms carry their own marks."],"questions":[{"q":"Explain one way in which the settlement of Viking migrants in England in the years c865–c950 was different from the settlement of Norman migrants in the years c1066–c1100. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — settlement by farmers who replaced a population against the replacement of a landowning elite — supported by specific detail from both periods, such as Halfdan's division of Northumbria in 876 and the Norman tenants-in-chief recorded in Domesday Book in 1086.","sol":"The main difference is who was replaced. Viking settlement worked from below: the Great Heathen Army that landed in 865 stayed instead of taking tribute and leaving, and in 876 Halfdan shared out the land of Northumbria among his men, who farmed it, leaving hundreds of villages in the east and north with Norse names ending in -by and -thorpe. Norman settlement worked from above: Domesday Book in 1086 shows the land of England divided among about two hundred great tenants-in-chief, almost all Norman or French, while the English peasants who worked it stayed put. Vikings replaced the population of whole districts; Normans replaced only its owners.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask who was actually living on and farming the land in each period once the newcomers had arrived.","Each half needs one dated detail: something that happened to Viking-held land, and one record of who held land under the Normans.","State the difference, prove each period in turn, then finish with a sentence saying how the two kinds of settlement differ in nature rather than in number."],"codes":["13-1 — 1 The context for migration: Reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","13 — The process of change: migration and the key features of society"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the treatment of Jewish migrants in England in the years c1070–c1200 was different from the treatment of foreign merchants and craftsmen in the years c1300–c1500. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — a community the Crown owned and sheltered against foreigners left in place but taxed and watched as 'aliens' — supported from both periods, for example the financiers encouraged from Rouen, Jews and their goods the king's in law and the burial grounds allowed outside London in 1177 against the hosting law of 1439 and the alien subsidy of 1440.","sol":"The difference is one of legal position. In c1070–c1200 Jewish migrants belonged to the king: William I encouraged financiers from Rouen in Normandy, and because in law they and their wealth were the king's, royal officers defended them, and Henry II allowed provincial communities their own burial grounds outside the towns in 1177. In c1300–c1500 foreign merchants and craftsmen were subjects of other rulers, admitted but restricted as 'aliens': a law of 1439 made them lodge with an English host who reported their dealings, and from 1440 Parliament levied a subsidy charged only on those born abroad. The earlier migrants were owned and sheltered; the later ones were tolerated and taxed.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask who each group belonged to in law, and who therefore had an interest in protecting them.","Both halves need dated evidence: one sign of royal protection in the earlier period, and two named pieces of legislation in the later one.","Name the difference, evidence each period, then close with a sentence on how the legal position of the two groups differed rather than on how harshly each was treated."],"codes":["13-1 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in England","13 — The process of change: the key factors"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the reasons migrants came to England in the years c865–c1000 were similar to the reasons migrants came in the years c1300–c1500. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed similarity — the pull of English wealth, in land and trade — supported from both periods, for example Halfdan's land division and the trade goods found at Viking York against the wool and cloth trade that drew Flemish weavers and Hanseatic merchants.","sol":"In both periods the strongest pull was English wealth. In c865–c1000 Vikings came for land and trade: after 876 Halfdan's men farmed Northumbria, and Jorvik grew into a workshop and market town whose finds include Baltic amber and a coin copying an Arab dirham. In c1300–c1500 migrants came for the wool and cloth trade: Edward III granted protection to the Flemish weaver John Kempe and his workers in 1331, and Hanseatic merchants shipped English wool and cloth from the Steelyard in London and from Boston and King's Lynn. In each case the same thing drew them — a country whose land and exports were worth crossing the sea for.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Edexcel wants pull factors first, so ask what England had that other countries did not.","Prove the earlier period from settlement and from what was traded, and the later one from a named craftsman and a named trading organisation.","State the similarity, evidence both periods, then close by naming the one thing common to both rather than repeating the examples."],"codes":["13 — The process of change: push and pull factors","13-1 — 1 The context for migration: Reasons for migration and patterns of settlement"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the role of the monarchy in migration to England in the years c1066–c1200 was similar to its role in the years c1300–c1500. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed similarity — the Crown encouraging migrants because it needed money — supported from both periods, for example the Jewish community brought from Rouen and taxed by tallage against the Carta Mercatoria of 1303 and the protection granted to Flemish weavers from 1331.","sol":"In both periods the Crown encouraged migrants because it needed money. In c1066–c1200 William I brought Jewish financiers from Rouen and his successors protected them as a source of credit and of tallage, and from 1194 the Ordinances of the Jewry had their loans recorded in sealed chests, the archae, out of which the Exchequer of the Jews grew, so that the Crown could enforce and tax them. In c1300–c1500 Edward I's Carta Mercatoria of 1303 sold foreign merchants trading rights in return for extra customs duties, and Edward III granted protection to Flemish weavers from 1331 so that England would have a cloth industry of its own to tax. Royal finance, not goodwill, shaped royal policy in both periods.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what a medieval king was always short of, and what migrants could supply.","Each half needs a named royal act: one that brought a community in, and one that sold rights to foreigners.","State the similarity, evidence both periods, then close with a sentence on the motive the two periods share."],"codes":["13-1 — 1 The context for migration: The context of English society","13 — The process of change: the key factors"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the impact of migrants on English towns in the years c865–c1100 was similar to their impact in the years c1300–c1500. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed similarity — migrants adding buildings, trades and trading connections that towns did not have before — supported from both periods, for example the Coppergate workshops and York's -gate street names against the Steelyard at Dowgate, the King's Lynn warehouse of 1475 and Flemish cloth-making.","sol":"In both periods migrants reshaped the streets and the trade of English towns. In c865–c1100 Scandinavian settlers turned York into Jorvik, laying out narrow tenement plots on Coppergate whose workshops turned wooden cups and cut leather, striking coin at their own mint and leaving street names ending in -gate, from the Old Norse gata. In c1300–c1500 foreign merchants and craftsmen did the same: the Hanseatic League held the walled Steelyard beside the Thames at Dowgate and built a warehouse at King's Lynn in 1475, and Flemish weavers brought cloth-making to Norwich and Colchester. In both, migrants left buildings and trades the town had not had before.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what a visitor would have been able to see and buy in the town that was not there before.","Each half needs named places: one Viking town with its workshops, and two later towns with a named building or trade.","State the similarity, evidence both periods, then close with the one kind of impact both periods share."],"codes":["13-1 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in England","13-1 — 3 Case study: The city of York under the Vikings"]},{"q":"Explain why Viking migrants settled in eastern and northern England in the years c865–c900. [12 marks] You may use the following in your answer: • the Great Heathen Army • the Danelaw. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Three explained reasons — the army's decision to take land instead of tribute, the frontier settled with Alfred, and the pull of good arable land and river routes reinforced by Scandinavian push factors — at least one beyond the bullets. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content. No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Viking settlement began because a raiding force decided to stay. The Great Heathen Army landed in East Anglia in 865 and, instead of taking tribute and sailing home, captured York in 866 and made it a base. In 876 Halfdan shared out the land of Northumbria among his followers, who ploughed it and lived off it. An army of that size could not be paid in silver indefinitely, so land became the wage, and that single decision turned conquest into migration.\n\nSettlement lasted because Alfred's resistance fixed a frontier rather than destroying the settlers. After Alfred defeated Guthrum at Edington in 878 the Danish leader accepted baptism, and the treaty the two made in the 880s ran the boundary up the Thames and the Lea, across to Bedford and then along Watling Street. Everything north and east of that line stayed in Danish hands and became known as the Danelaw, where Danish custom held, districts were called wapentakes and the Five Boroughs of Derby, Leicester, Lincoln, Nottingham and Stamford formed a political world of their own. A frontier recognised by the king of Wessex gave settlement legal shelter.\n\nBeyond the bullets, the land itself explains where they went. Eastern and northern England offered good arable ground and rivers such as the Ouse, Trent and Humber reaching the sea, so a people who travelled and traded by water could farm and still trade. Push factors reinforced the choice, since land was short in Scandinavia and kings there were building power at the expense of independent lords. The density of Norse village names ending in -by, -thorpe and -thwaite across Yorkshire and Lincolnshire shows families settling to farm, not garrisons holding ground.\n\nThe three reasons work together: an army that could only be paid in land, a treaty that protected what it took, and a region that was worth farming and trading from.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Begin with what the army actually did once it stopped campaigning.","A treaty decided where the settlers could stay; identify it and say what line it drew.","Two bullets alone cannot reach the top level, so add a reason about the land and rivers of the region, then link all three in a closing sentence."],"codes":["13-1 — 1 The context for migration: Reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","13 — The process of change: push and pull factors","13-1 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in England"]},{"q":"Explain why the Norman Conquest changed the pattern of migration to England in the years c1066–c1150. [12 marks] You may use the following in your answer: • landownership • the Church. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Three explained reasons — the transfer of landownership requiring an imported ruling class, the reshaping of the Church by continental clergy and orders, and the entirely new channel of Jewish migration from Rouen — at least one beyond the bullets. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content. No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The Conquest changed migration first by changing who owned England. William I treated the kingdom as forfeit and granted it to the men who had fought for him, so that Domesday Book in 1086 records the land divided among about two hundred great tenants-in-chief, almost all Norman or French, with barely a handful of great English landholders left. A new ruling class had to be imported, and with it came knights, stewards and the masons and carpenters who raised hundreds of motte-and-bailey castles and, from about 1078, the White Tower in London.\n\nIt changed migration secondly through the Church. England was already part of Latin Christendom, but the Normans replaced its leadership: Lanfranc, an Italian from Pavia and abbot of Bec in Normandy, became Archbishop of Canterbury in 1070, and Anselm of Aosta followed in 1093. Norman bishops rebuilt the cathedrals in the Romanesque style, Durham from 1093, and French monastic orders were planted here, the Cluniacs at Lewes from about 1077 and the Cistercians at Waverley in 1128. Churchmen, builders and scholars now moved along the same routes as the lords.\n\nBeyond the bullets, the Conquest opened a channel that had never existed. Because Norman kings needed ready cash and the Church forbade Christians to lend at interest, William I encouraged Jewish financiers from Rouen in Normandy to settle in London, and communities followed in Lincoln, Norwich, York, Winchester, Oxford and Bristol. There had been no Jewish community in Anglo-Saxon England at all, so this was not a change of scale but of kind.\n\nThe pattern therefore changed in direction as well as in size: before 1066 migration had crossed the North Sea to settle and farm; after it, migration crossed the Channel to rule, to build, to preach and to lend.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Start with the one thing the Conquest transferred completely, and ask who had to come in order to hold it.","The Church is an institution with people at the top; name the men who were brought in and the orders that followed them.","Find the migrant group that did not exist in England before 1066 at all, and explain why Norman rule made it possible."],"codes":["13-1 — 1 The context for migration: Reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","13-1 — 1 The context for migration: The context of English society","13 — The process of change: migration and the key features of society"]},{"q":"Explain why Jewish migrants were able to settle in England in the years c1070–c1200. [12 marks] You may use the following in your answer: • royal finance • usury. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Three explained reasons — the Crown's need for credit and its legal claim on Jewish wealth, the Church's ban on Christian usury combined with exclusion from guilds and land, and the growth of towns with royal machinery for enforcing debts — at least one beyond the bullets. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content. No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Jews could settle because the Crown wanted them to. Norman and Angevin kings needed ready money for campaigns, castles and cathedrals at a time when taxation was slow and crude, and William I encouraged financiers from Rouen in Normandy to come to England after the Conquest. In law Jews and their goods were regarded as the king's, so the Crown could levy arbitrary taxes called tallages whenever it chose. Protection was therefore an investment: sheriffs were expected to defend Jewish communities because the wealth inside them was ultimately royal wealth.\n\nThey could settle, secondly, because of the Church's teaching on usury. Christians were forbidden to lend money at interest, yet a growing economy needed credit. Jews were excluded from the craft guilds and could not hold land by feudal tenure, so lending was one of the very few trades open to them — and the one trade Christian England could not do for itself. That made them useful rather than merely tolerated, and gave them a place in almost every large town.\n\nBeyond the bullets, settlement was made workable by the growth of towns and by royal machinery. Communities appeared where the demand for credit was: London first, then Lincoln, Norwich, York, Winchester, Oxford, Bristol and Cambridge, usually beside the castle, whose garrison offered shelter. Aaron of Lincoln lent so widely that when he died in 1186 a special branch of the Exchequer was created to gather in his debts for the king, and from 1194 the Ordinances of the Jewry had loans registered in sealed chests, the archae, out of which the Exchequer of the Jews grew, so that they could be proved and enforced in the royal courts.\n\nThe three reasons point the same way: a Crown short of cash, a Church that closed one trade to Christians and left it open to Jews, and towns rich enough to borrow made settlement possible for more than two centuries.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask first what the king got out of it, and why that made him protective.","The second bullet is a religious rule; work out what it did to the jobs available to a Jewish family.","Add a reason the bullets do not prompt about where the communities actually settled and what made a loan enforceable, then tie the reasons together."],"codes":["13-1 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in England","13-1 — 1 The context for migration: The context of English society","13 — The process of change: the key factors"]},{"q":"Explain why the treatment of Jewish migrants in England changed in the years c1150–1290. [12 marks] You may use the following in your answer: • religious hostility • royal taxation. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Three explained reasons — hardening religious hostility from the Norwich accusation of 1144 and the badge of 1218, the exhaustion of Jewish wealth by royal tallage and the arrival of Italian lenders, and the pressure of indebted gentry in Parliament — at least one beyond the bullets. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content. No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Treatment changed first because religious hostility hardened. In 1144 the death of a boy at Norwich produced the first recorded accusation that Jews had murdered a Christian child, and the story spread from town to town. The Fourth Lateran Council of 1215 ordered Jews to be distinguished by their dress, and from 1218 England became the first country in Europe to make them wear a cloth badge. Crusading preaching sharpened matters further: rioting broke out at Richard I's coronation in September 1189, and in March 1190 about 150 Jews died at the wooden keep of York castle, most killing themselves rather than be taken by the crowd.\n\nIt changed, secondly, because royal taxation destroyed the wealth that had made the community valuable. Kings took tallage after tallage, so that by the reign of Edward I there was little left to take, and the Crown had found new lenders among Italian bankers such as the Riccardi of Lucca. A source of money the king no longer needed had no claim on his protection. The Statute of the Jewry of 1275 then banned lending at interest altogether, ordered the badge and told Jews to live by trade or farming instead, removing their livelihood without supplying a workable substitute.\n\nBeyond the bullets, the debtors themselves drove the change. Loans were secured by written bonds, so cancelling a debt meant destroying the record, which is exactly what the mob at York did when it burned the bond chest in the Minster. Many knights and gentry were in debt to Jewish lenders, and in Parliament these were the men who pressed for action. When Edward I ordered expulsion in July 1290, giving the community until All Saints' Day to leave, Parliament granted him a large tax on movable goods in return.\n\nThe change was therefore not simply a growth in prejudice, but prejudice made useful to a king who needed money and to debtors who wanted their bonds destroyed.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what people were being told to believe about this community, and when the stories start.","Follow the money: work out what happened to Jewish wealth after decades of royal taxation, and who lent to the king instead.","Add a reason about the people who owed the money, then close by explaining how the reasons reinforced one another."],"codes":["13-1 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in England","13 — The process of change: factors working together and the role of the case studies","13 — The process of change: the key factors"]},{"q":"Explain why foreign merchants and craftsmen came to England in the years c1300–c1500. [12 marks] You may use the following in your answer: • the wool trade • the Hanseatic League. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Three explained reasons — the pull of English wool and the staple, the privileges granted to Hanseatic merchants, and deliberate royal recruitment of skilled weavers reinforced by the labour shortage after the Black Death — at least one beyond the bullets. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content. No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"They came first for wool. English wool was the finest in Europe and the country's chief export, and the cloth industries of Flanders and northern Italy depended on it. Italian houses such as the Riccardi of Lucca and later the Bardi and Peruzzi of Florence bought wool directly and lent to English kings against the customs revenue; their presence gave Lombard Street in London its name. Because the staple, the compulsory market for wool exports, was fixed at Calais in 1363, foreign buyers had to deal through a controlled English channel rather than avoid it.\n\nThey came, secondly, because the Hanseatic League had privileges worth having. The Hansa was an association of north German trading towns which shipped English wool and cloth out and brought Baltic timber, pitch, furs, wax and fish back. In London it held the Steelyard, a walled compound of warehouses and a hall beside the Thames at Dowgate, with its own rules; it also traded through Boston and King's Lynn, where its warehouse of 1475 still stands. English kings confirmed its exemptions because it supplied naval stores and credit.\n\nBeyond the bullets, English kings deliberately recruited skilled workers. In 1331 Edward III granted royal protection to John Kempe, a Flemish weaver, and to his servants and apprentices, so that cloth could be made in England instead of merely woven abroad from English wool; Flemish weavers settled in Norwich, Colchester and London. The Black Death of 1348–49 made recruitment easier still, since it killed perhaps a third of the population and left towns short of skilled hands, so craftsmen from the Low Countries and Germany found work waiting for them.\n\nBehind all three lies one pull: England had a raw material the continent could not do without, and kings willing to sell privileges, grant protection and tax the trade that followed.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Start with the commodity that the whole of northern Europe wanted and only England had in that quality.","The second bullet is an organisation with a base in London; say what it traded and what it was given in return.","Add a reason the bullets do not prompt about skilled workers being invited, and about what the plague did to the demand for them."],"codes":["13-1 — 1 The context for migration: The context of English society","13 — The process of change: push and pull factors","13-1 — 1 The context for migration: Reasons for migration and patterns of settlement"]},{"q":"'Royal power was the main reason why migrants came to England in the years c1066–c1500.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks] You may use the following in your answer: • Jewish moneylenders • Flemish weavers. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Weigh royal invitation, protection, licensing and expulsion against economic pull (wool, credit, the post-plague labour shortage) and against religion and Christendom, with a criterion stated and applied; at least one point beyond the bullets. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content. No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Royal power explains a great deal of medieval migration, because in England the Crown controlled who could enter, trade and hold property. Judged by which factor brought the most migrants, however, it comes second to the economy; judged by which factor decided whether they could stay, it comes first.\n\nThe case for the statement is clearest with Jewish settlement. There had been no Jewish community in Anglo-Saxon England; it existed because William I encouraged financiers from Rouen in Normandy after the Conquest, and because Norman and Angevin kings needed ready cash for castles and campaigns when taxation was slow. In law Jews and their goods were the king's, so the Crown protected them and taxed them by tallage at will, and from 1194 the Ordinances of the Jewry had their loans registered in sealed chests, the archae, out of which the Exchequer of the Jews grew, so that debts could be enforced in royal courts. Royal power did not merely permit this migration; it created it, and it ended it, when Edward I ordered expulsion in July 1290 and Parliament granted him a large tax on movable goods in return.\n\nThe same power was used to recruit skilled labour. In 1331 Edward III granted protection to the Flemish weaver John Kempe and his workers so that cloth could be made and taxed in England instead of merely woven abroad from English wool, and Flemish weavers settled in Norwich, Colchester and London. Edward I's Carta Mercatoria of 1303 sold foreign merchants trading rights in return for extra customs duties, and the Hanseatic League kept its Steelyard at Dowgate because successive kings confirmed its exemptions.\n\nAgainst the statement stands the economy, which pulled migrants whether kings acted or not. English wool was the finest in Europe and the cloth towns of Flanders and Italy could not do without it, so Italian houses such as the Bardi and Peruzzi of Florence and the merchants of the Hansa came for the raw material rather than for a charter. Beyond the bullets, the Black Death of 1348–49 killed perhaps a third of the population and left English towns so short of skilled hands that craftsmen from the Low Countries and Germany found work waiting, while English peasants themselves moved off the manors into the towns in such numbers that the Statute of Labourers of 1351 tried to stop them. No royal grant caused any of that.\n\nReligion is a second rival, and it is the one the bullets ignore entirely. England's membership of Latin Christendom brought Lanfranc of Pavia and Anselm of Aosta to Canterbury, Cluniac monks to Lewes from about 1077, Cistercians to Waverley in 1128, and Dominican and Franciscan friars to English towns in 1221 and 1224. These men moved because the Church was an international institution with its own orders, language and careers, and no English king summoned them.\n\nThe judgement therefore depends on the criterion. Measured by which factor explains the most migrants over the longest stretch of time, the economy comes first, because wool and the demand for labour ran through the whole period while royal action came in bursts. Measured by which factor decided whether a migrant community existed at all, royal power comes first, because only the Crown could invite, license, tax or expel, and it did all four. The statement is half right: kings controlled migration, but they rarely caused it.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first what 'main' is going to be measured by, because the answer changes depending on the test you choose.","Take the two bullets as the case for the statement, then find two factors that owe nothing to royal action at all.","Plan four weighed paragraphs, each closing with an interim judgement, and make the conclusion apply the criterion instead of repeating the paragraphs."],"codes":["13 — The process of change: the key factors","13-1 — 1 The context for migration: The context of English society","13 — The process of change: factors working together and the role of the case studies"]},{"q":"'Viking settlement changed England more than Norman settlement in the years c800–c1200.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks] You may use the following in your answer: • the Danelaw • castles. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Weigh the depth of Viking change (land settlement, Danelaw law and wapentakes, place names, vocabulary) against the breadth of Norman change (landownership, castles, cathedrals, Church leadership, Jewish settlement), with a criterion stated and applied; at least one point beyond the bullets. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content. No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Whether Viking settlement changed England more than Norman settlement depends on what kind of change is being measured: change that reached ordinary people and lasted, or change imposed from above that was visible at once and everywhere.\n\nMeasured by depth, Viking settlement has the stronger claim. The Great Heathen Army that landed in 865 stayed, and in 876 Halfdan shared out the land of Northumbria among men who farmed it. The country east and north of the line Alfred and Guthrum agreed in the 880s — up the Thames and the Lea to Bedford and then along Watling Street — became the Danelaw, where Danish custom applied, local districts were called wapentakes rather than hundreds, and the Five Boroughs of Derby, Leicester, Lincoln, Nottingham and Stamford formed a distinct political world. The evidence of ordinary settlement is written on the map: hundreds of villages in Yorkshire, Lincolnshire and the east Midlands still carry Norse names ending in -by, -thorpe and -thwaite, and Domesday Book was still recording the region's unusually free sokemen in 1086. Beyond the bullets, the language changed at its core: law, egg, sky, knife, husband and window are Norse words, and so are the pronouns they, them and their.\n\nMeasured by breadth and by power, Norman settlement changed more. Within twenty years the ownership of England had been transferred: Domesday Book shows the land divided among about two hundred great tenants-in-chief, almost all Norman or French. Castles went up in their hundreds, from motte-and-bailey earthworks to the White Tower begun from about 1078, and almost every great church was rebuilt in the Romanesque style, Durham from 1093. Lanfranc of Pavia became Archbishop of Canterbury in 1070 and Anselm of Aosta in 1093; Cluniac monks settled at Lewes from about 1077 and Cistercians at Waverley in 1128. French became the language of the court, the law and the lord's hall. Norman rule also created a migration that had never existed here, bringing Jewish financiers from Rouen to London and then to Lincoln, Norwich and York.\n\nThe two changes were of different kinds, and that is the key to the judgement. Viking settlement replaced population: whole districts were farmed, named and governed by newcomers, which is why the traces survive in field names and everyday speech a thousand years later. Norman settlement replaced a ruling class: the peasants who worked the land stayed where they were, and English survived as the language of the majority, absorbing French words rather than being displaced by them.\n\nIf change is judged by how far it altered the daily life, law and speech of ordinary people, Viking settlement changed England more — but only in the north and east, and not at all in Wessex or the south-west. If it is judged by how far it altered the whole kingdom, its ownership, its Church, its buildings and its rules about who else could come in, Norman settlement changed more, because it changed everything at once and everywhere. The statement is therefore too strong: Viking change went deeper where it happened, Norman change went wider, and it was the wider change that shaped the England of 1200.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Two settlements of very different kinds are being compared, so decide what 'more' is going to mean before you argue.","Use the Danelaw for depth and the Normans for breadth, and make sure each paragraph has dated, named evidence.","Bring in something neither bullet prompts, such as language, and let the conclusion say which kind of change matters more and why."],"codes":["13-1 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in England","13-1 — 1 The context for migration: Reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","13 — The process of change: migration and the key features of society"]},{"q":"'The experience of migrants in England in the years c800–c1500 was shaped mainly by attitudes in society.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks] You may use the following in your answer: • the massacre at York in 1190 • the legal status of 'alien'. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Weigh popular hostility (Norwich 1144, the coronation riots of 1189, York 1190, the killing of Flemings in 1381) against government action (the badge of 1218, the Statute of the Jewry of 1275, the expulsion of 1290, the hosting law of 1439, the alien subsidy of 1440) and against economic need after the Black Death; at least one point beyond the bullets. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content. No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Attitudes in society decided how dangerous life could become for migrants, but government decided the terms on which they lived from day to day, and the two cannot be separated, because the law itself taught people what to think.\n\nThe case for attitudes is strong and bloody. From 1144, when the death of a boy at Norwich produced the first recorded accusation that Jews had murdered a Christian child, a story with no basis in fact spread from town to town. Crusading enthusiasm turned it into violence: rioting broke out at Richard I's coronation in September 1189, and in March 1190 about 150 Jews died at the wooden keep of York castle, most killing themselves rather than be taken by the crowd outside. Hostility was not confined to one community. Beyond the bullets, during the rising of 1381 Londoners hunted down Flemish residents, dragging some from the church of St Martin Vintry and killing them in the street. Ordinary people resented outsiders seen as competitors for work or as creditors.\n\nAgainst this, the framework that shaped ordinary experience was made by government. The status of 'alien' was a matter of law, not of feeling: those born abroad could not inherit land, paid higher rates of customs than English merchants, and from 1439 alien merchants had to lodge with an English host who reported their dealings. From 1440 Parliament levied a subsidy charged only on people born abroad, the first English tax of its kind, and its returns name thousands of them in every county. An alien who wanted to escape these disabilities had to buy letters of denization from the Crown. For Jews the framework was harsher: from 1218 a cloth badge had to be worn, the Statute of the Jewry of 1275 banned lending at interest, and the expulsion decreed in July 1290 gave the community until All Saints' Day to go.\n\nThe two factors fed each other, and York shows how. The crowd that besieged the castle went on to burn the bond records in the Minster: debts owed to lenders whose business the Crown had created, protected and taxed. Popular violence was aimed at a position that government had manufactured, so treating attitudes as an independent cause misreads the evidence.\n\nEconomic need is the third element, and it improved experience as often as prejudice worsened it. After the Black Death of 1348–49 killed perhaps a third of the population, English towns were desperate for skilled hands, and Flemish and German craftsmen found work; Edward III had already granted protection to the weaver John Kempe in 1331 for exactly that reason. Where migrants supplied something the country wanted, they were left alone.\n\nJudged by which factor determined the worst days, attitudes come first, because the killing at York and in London in 1381 was done by crowds and not by officials. Judged by which factor determined the ordinary years, government comes first, because a Hanseatic merchant at the Steelyard, a Flemish weaver in Norwich and a Jewish lender in Lincoln all lived inside categories the Crown had defined and could change at will. Since most years were ordinary years, the statement is only partly true: attitudes supplied the violence, but royal policy decided whether there was a community there to attack.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Separate what crowds did from what governments did before you decide which mattered more.","Use one migrant group for the violence and a different one for the law, so the argument is not about a single community.","Find the piece of evidence that shows the two factors acting together, and let the conclusion distinguish the worst days from the ordinary ones."],"codes":["13-1 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in England","13 — The process of change: factors working together and the role of the case studies","13 — The process of change: the key factors"]},{"q":"'Migrants had their greatest impact on English towns in the years c800–c1500.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks] You may use the following in your answer: • York • the built environment. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Weigh urban impact (Jorvik's workshops and street names, the Steelyard, the King's Lynn warehouse, Flemish cloth-making) against rural and national impact (Danelaw place names and wapentakes, the transfer of landownership in 1086, vocabulary, the Church); at least one point beyond the bullets. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content. No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Migrants changed a great deal in medieval England, and towns are where the change is easiest to see. Whether towns are where the change mattered most is a different question, and the answer depends on whether impact is judged by visibility or by reach.\n\nThe case for towns is very strong, and York is the clearest example. Scandinavian settlers took the city in 866 and, after Halfdan divided Northumbria in 876, made it the capital of a kingdom. Jorvik became a manufacturing centre: the Coppergate excavation of 1976–81 uncovered rows of timber and wattle workshops on narrow plots, turning wooden cups on pole lathes, cutting leather shoes and making combs from antler, alongside Baltic amber, a silk cap, a cowrie shell from far outside Europe and a coin copying an Arab dirham. The city struck its own coins at its own mint and may have held around ten thousand people. Its street names still record the settlers, since -gate comes from the Old Norse gata, street, as in Coppergate, the street of the cup-makers. Later migrants did the same elsewhere: the Hanseatic League held the walled Steelyard at Dowgate in London and built a warehouse at King's Lynn in 1475 which still stands, Flemish weavers brought cloth-making to Norwich and Colchester, and Jewish communities left some of England's earliest surviving stone town houses, such as the Jew's House in Lincoln.\n\nThe countryside, however, was changed more permanently by Viking settlement than any single town was. Hundreds of villages across Yorkshire, Lincolnshire and the east Midlands carry Norse names ending in -by, -thorpe and -thwaite, and the Danelaw gave a third of England its own law, its own administrative unit in the wapentake, and its own centres in the Five Boroughs. That is a change to the landscape and government of a whole region, not to a single street.\n\nLandownership was transformed more sharply still. Domesday Book in 1086 shows the land of England divided among about two hundred great tenants-in-chief, almost all Norman or French: one migration changed who owned everything, in town and country alike.\n\nBeyond the bullets, language may be the deepest change of all, and it belonged to no particular place. Old Norse gave English law, egg, sky, knife, husband and window, and the pronouns they, them and their, while Norman French supplied the vocabulary of rule, law and lordship. A language altered in its commonest words has been altered permanently. The Church was reshaped in the same nationwide way: Lanfranc of Pavia took Canterbury in 1070 and Anselm of Aosta in 1093, Cluniacs settled at Lewes from about 1077, Cistercians at Waverley in 1128, and Dominican and Franciscan friars arrived in 1221 and 1224, while every cathedral was rebuilt after the Conquest.\n\nJudged by visibility, the statement holds, because towns carry the plainest marks of migration: that is where migrants concentrated and where they built. Judged by how much of English life was altered and for how long, it does not, because the naming and law of the Danelaw, the ownership of the land after 1066 and the everyday vocabulary of English reached further than any town did. Migrants changed towns most obviously, but they changed the language and the landholding of the whole country most profoundly.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["'Greatest impact' needs a test: decide whether you are measuring what can be seen or what was altered for longest.","Build the case for towns from one Viking city and two later trading places, then look for changes that happened outside towns altogether.","Bring in something neither bullet prompts, such as language or landownership, and make the conclusion choose between the two tests rather than splitting the difference."],"codes":["13-1 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in England","13-1 — 3 Case study: The city of York under the Vikings","13 — The process of change: migration and the key features of society"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In which year did the Great Heathen Army capture York?","a":"866."},{"q":"Which statute of 1275 banned Jews in England from lending money at interest?","a":"The Statute of the Jewry."},{"q":"What was the Hanseatic League's walled trading compound in London called?","a":"The Steelyard."},{"q":"Which Flemish weaver was granted royal protection by Edward III in 1331 to establish cloth-making in England?","a":"John Kempe."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The Great Heathen Army","code":"13-1 — 1 The context for migration: Reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","open":false,"back":"A Scandinavian army that landed in East Anglia in 865. It captured York in 866 and stayed instead of taking tribute; in 876 its leader Halfdan shared out the land of Northumbria among his men to farm."},{"front":"The Danelaw","code":"13-1 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in England","open":false,"back":"The area of eastern and northern England left under Danish law and custom after Alfred's treaty with Guthrum in the 880s. Its districts were called wapentakes and its chief towns were the Five Boroughs."},{"front":"The Five Boroughs","code":"13-1 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in England","open":false,"back":"Derby, Leicester, Lincoln, Nottingham and Stamford: the five fortified towns of the Danelaw in the east Midlands, each with its own territory and army."},{"front":"Jorvik","code":"13-1 — 3 Case study: The city of York under the Vikings","open":false,"back":"The Scandinavian name for York, capital of a Viking kingdom from 876. A trading and manufacturing town with its own mint, perhaps ten thousand people, and street names ending in -gate from the Old Norse gata."},{"front":"The Coppergate excavation","code":"13-1 — 3 Case study: The city of York under the Vikings","open":false,"back":"A dig in York between 1976 and 1981. It uncovered Viking-age timber workshops for woodturning, leatherwork and combs, with Baltic amber, a silk cap, a cowrie shell and a coin copying an Arab dirham."},{"front":"Eric Bloodaxe","code":"13-1 — 3 Case study: The city of York under the Vikings","open":false,"back":"The last Scandinavian king of York, driven out and killed in 954. Northumbria then passed permanently to the kings of England."},{"front":"Cnut","code":"13-1 — 1 The context for migration: Reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","open":false,"back":"Danish king of England from 1016 to 1035, who also ruled Denmark and Norway. He kept Scandinavian housecarls and gave great earldoms to Danish followers such as Thorkell the Tall in East Anglia."},{"front":"Domesday Book","code":"13-1 — 1 The context for migration: The context of English society","open":false,"back":"The survey of England completed in 1086. It shows the land divided among about two hundred great tenants-in-chief, almost all Norman or French, and it lists over a hundred boroughs."},{"front":"Lanfranc","code":"13-1 — 1 The context for migration: The context of English society","open":false,"back":"An Italian from Pavia and abbot of Bec in Normandy, made Archbishop of Canterbury in 1070. He rebuilt Canterbury cathedral and reorganised the English Church; his successor Anselm came from Aosta."},{"front":"The massacre at York, 1190","code":"13-1 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in England","open":false,"back":"In March 1190 about 150 Jews took refuge in the wooden keep of York castle; most killed themselves and the rest were killed after surrendering. The crowd then burned the records of debts in the Minster."},{"front":"The Statute of the Jewry, 1275","code":"13-1 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in England","open":false,"back":"Edward I's law banning Jews from lending money at interest, ordering them to wear a cloth badge and telling them to live by trade or farming instead."},{"front":"The expulsion of the Jews, 1290","code":"13-1 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in England","open":false,"back":"Edward I ordered the whole Jewish community out of England in July 1290, to be gone by All Saints' Day. Parliament granted him a large tax on movable goods in return."},{"front":"John Kempe","code":"13-1 — 1 The context for migration: Reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","open":false,"back":"A Flemish weaver granted royal protection by Edward III in 1331, together with his servants and apprentices, to teach cloth-making in England. Flemish weavers settled in Norwich, Colchester and London."},{"front":"The Hanseatic League","code":"13-1 — 1 The context for migration: Reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","open":false,"back":"An association of north German trading towns. In London its merchants held the Steelyard, a walled compound at Dowgate, trading English wool and cloth for Baltic timber, furs, wax and fish."},{"front":"The alien subsidy of 1440","code":"13-1 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in England","open":false,"back":"A tax granted by Parliament and charged only on people born abroad who were living in England. Its returns name thousands of aliens in every county, from merchants to servants."}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 1 Question 5, 16 marks: 'The Norman Conquest was the most important turning point in migration to England in the years c800–c1500.')","items":["The Conquest deserves the title of turning point above all because it changed who could migrate to England, and not merely how many did. Before 1066 migration came across the North Sea and came to farm: the Great Heathen Army that landed in 865 stayed to plough Northumbria after Halfdan divided it in 876, and hundreds of villages from Yorkshire to Lincolnshire still carry their Norse names ending in -by and -thorpe. After 1066 migration came across the Channel and came to rule. Domesday Book in 1086 records England divided among about two hundred great tenants-in-chief, almost all Norman or French; Lanfranc of Pavia took Canterbury in 1070 and Anselm of Aosta in 1093; Cluniac monks settled at Lewes from about 1077 and Cistercians at Waverley in 1128; masons raised the White Tower from about 1078 and rebuilt Durham from 1093. Most decisive of all, the Conquest opened a channel that had never existed, because Norman kings needed ready cash and the Church forbade Christians to lend at interest: William I encouraged Jewish financiers from Rouen to settle in London, and communities followed in Lincoln, Norwich, York and Winchester. It must be conceded that the Conquest did not create the pull of English wool, which drew Flemish weavers and Italian buyers long afterwards, nor the labour shortage after the Black Death of 1348–49 which drew craftsmen into half-empty towns. But those later movements ran through institutions the Conquest had built: a Crown that granted protection and sold trading privileges, and a landholding system in which an incomer's rights depended entirely on the king. Judged by the criterion of whether a change redirected migration for centuries rather than merely increasing it for a generation, 1066 is the turning point of the theme, because it changed the direction migrants came from, the reason they came, and the authority that decided whether they could stay.","Why this reaches Level 4: the explanation is directed consistently at the conceptual focus, since every sentence tests whether the Conquest was a turning point rather than describing what the Normans did; the information is precisely selected (1086 and the tenants-in-chief, Lanfranc and Anselm with their dates, Lewes, Waverley, Durham, Rouen and the named Jewish communities) rather than general; the counter-case about wool and the Black Death is stated at its strongest and then answered instead of being left hanging; the answer goes well beyond the two stimulus points; and the criterion for the judgement is named and applied in the final sentence, which is what the third bullet of the Level 4 descriptor rewards."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["The Norman Conquest was a really important turning point for migration. In 1066 William the Conqueror invaded and won the Battle of Hastings, and after that lots of Normans came over and took over the country. They built castles everywhere and big cathedrals, and they spoke French, so the English language changed. William also brought Jews over from Normandy to lend money, because Christians were not allowed to lend money. Before that the Vikings had come and settled in the north and east of England. So the Conquest was the most important turning point because it changed England a lot, although other things like the wool trade were important too.","Why this stays at Level 2: it meets 'an explanation is given showing limited analysis and with implicit or unsustained links to the conceptual focus of the question' and 'accurate and relevant information is included, showing some knowledge and understanding of the period', while the overall judgement is given but asserted. It misses Levels 3 and 4 because almost nothing is dated or named beyond 1066 — no Domesday figure, no Lanfranc or Anselm, no named town with a Jewish community; because the Viking sentence is dropped in without being used as a comparison; because the wool trade is tacked on at the end instead of being weighed; and because no criterion for 'most important' is stated, so the judgement is claimed rather than tested."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Case study: the city of York under the Vikings","items":["866: the Great Heathen Army captured York, the royal city of Northumbria that the English called Eoforwic.","867: the army defeated the Northumbrian kings Osberht and Ælle in fighting at York.","876: Halfdan shared out the land of Northumbria among his followers, turning an army into a settled population with York as its centre.","The Scandinavian name for the city was Jorvik; it was the capital of a Viking kingdom of York for most of the years between 876 and 954.","954: Eric Bloodaxe, the last Scandinavian king of York, was driven out and killed, and Northumbria passed permanently to the kings of England.","York kept its archbishops throughout: the Church was not destroyed, and archbishops such as Wulfstan dealt directly with the Scandinavian kings.","The city had its own mint and struck its own coinage, including coins issued in the name of St Peter, the patron saint of York Minster.","Its population is estimated at around ten thousand by the early eleventh century, making York one of the largest towns in England after London.","The Coppergate excavation of 1976 to 1981 uncovered rows of timber and wattle buildings on narrow tenement plots running back from the street.","The workshops made wooden cups and bowls on pole lathes, leather shoes, antler and bone combs, jewellery of amber and jet, and glass beads.","Coppergate means the street of the cup-makers; gata is Old Norse for street, which is why so many York streets end in -gate.","Micklegate, Stonegate and Petergate carry the same Old Norse ending, so the settlers' language is still fixed in the street plan.","Finds prove long-distance trade: amber from the Baltic, a silk cap, a cowrie shell from far outside Europe and a coin copying an Arab dirham.","York exemplifies strands 1 and 2 in one place: why migrants came (land, trade and a defensible base), how they lived alongside the existing population, and what difference they made to a town's trade, streets and speech."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — The Danelaw","items":["The Danelaw was the part of England, broadly north and east of a line from London towards Chester, where Danish law and custom held rather than English law.","The name is found in English law codes of the eleventh century; it describes an area of law and settlement, not a single unified state.","Its boundary was set in the treaty Alfred of Wessex made with Guthrum in the 880s, running up the Thames, up the Lea, across to Bedford, up the Ouse and then along Watling Street.","Alfred had defeated Guthrum at Edington in 878, after which Guthrum accepted baptism.","The Five Boroughs — Derby, Leicester, Lincoln, Nottingham and Stamford — were its chief fortified towns in the east Midlands.","Local districts in the Danelaw were called wapentakes, the equivalent of the hundred elsewhere in England.","The region had an unusually high proportion of sokemen, free peasants holding land on lighter terms than villeins elsewhere; Domesday Book was still recording them in 1086.","Hundreds of village names in Yorkshire, Lincolnshire and the east Midlands are Norse: -by as in Grimsby and Whitby, -thorpe as in Scunthorpe, and -thwaite and -toft.","Old Norse gave English everyday words including law, egg, sky, knife, husband and window, and the pronouns they, them and their.","English kings reconquered the Danelaw in stages during the tenth century, but its law, its land tenure and its place names outlasted the reconquest by centuries."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Jewish settlement in England, c1070–1290","items":["There was no Jewish community in Anglo-Saxon England: Jewish settlement here began after the Norman Conquest.","Jewish financiers came from Rouen in Normandy in the 1070s, encouraged by William I, and settled first in London.","Communities followed in Lincoln, Norwich, York, Winchester, Oxford, Bristol and Cambridge, usually close to the castle for protection.","In law Jews and their possessions were regarded as the king's, so the Crown protected them and taxed them by arbitrary levies called tallages.","The Church forbade Christians to lend money at interest, and Jews were excluded from the craft guilds and from holding land by feudal tenure, so lending was one of the very few trades open to them.","1144: the death of a boy at Norwich produced the first recorded accusation that Jews had murdered a Christian child, a false charge that then spread across Europe.","1177: Henry II allowed Jewish burial grounds to be opened outside London, which until then had held the only one in England.","Aaron of Lincoln, the wealthiest Jewish financier of his day, died in 1186 owed so much that a special branch of the Exchequer was set up to collect his debts for the Crown.","September 1189: rioting against Jews broke out in London at the coronation of Richard I and spread to other towns.","March 1190: about 150 Jews died at the wooden keep of York castle, most killing themselves rather than surrender; the crowd then burned the records of debts owed to them in York Minster.","From 1194 the Ordinances of the Jewry had loans registered in sealed chests, the archae, out of which the Exchequer of the Jews grew, so that debts could be proved and enforced in the king's courts, and taxed by him.","1218: England became the first country in Europe to order Jews to wear a distinguishing cloth badge, following the Fourth Lateran Council of 1215.","1275: the Statute of the Jewry banned lending at interest, ordered the badge to be worn and told Jews to live by trade or farming instead.","July 1290: Edward I ordered the whole community to leave England by All Saints' Day, and Parliament granted him a large tax on movable goods in return; no Jewish community was permitted in England again until the 1650s.","By then the Crown had other lenders: Italian bankers such as the Riccardi of Lucca and, later, the Bardi and Peruzzi of Florence."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Norman migration and the Church after 1066","items":["William of Normandy defeated Harold at Hastings on 14 October 1066 and was crowned at Westminster on 25 December 1066.","Norman settlement was elite settlement: Domesday Book in 1086 shows the land held by about two hundred great tenants-in-chief, almost all Norman or French, while the peasants who farmed it stayed.","Castles were its visible mark: motte-and-bailey earthworks in their hundreds, and the White Tower in London begun from about 1078.","Almost every English cathedral was rebuilt in the Romanesque style; Durham was begun in 1093.","Lanfranc, an Italian from Pavia and abbot of Bec in Normandy, became Archbishop of Canterbury in 1070; Anselm, from Aosta, followed in 1093.","French monastic orders were planted in England: Cluniac monks at Lewes from about 1077, and Cistercians at Waverley in Surrey in 1128 and at Rievaulx and Fountains in 1132.","The friars came in the next century: Dominicans reached England in 1221 and Franciscans in 1224, settling mainly in towns.","Alien priories, English houses dependent on French abbeys, sent revenue abroad; the Crown seized them during the wars with France and they were finally suppressed in 1414.","French became the language of the court, the law and the lord's household, while English remained the language of the majority and absorbed French words.","England's place in Latin Christendom meant clergy, scholars and papal officials moved across Europe as a matter of course, with Latin as the common written language.","The Conquest opened an entirely new channel of migration by bringing Jewish financiers from Rouen, since Norman kings needed credit and Christians were forbidden to lend at interest."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — European traders and craftsmen, c1300–c1500","items":["English wool was the finest in Europe and the country's chief export; the cloth industries of Flanders and northern Italy depended on it.","A customs duty on every sack of wool exported was first set in 1275, giving the Crown a direct financial interest in foreign merchants.","Edward I's Carta Mercatoria of 1303 granted foreign merchants rights to trade and travel in England in return for extra customs duties.","The staple, the compulsory market through which wool exports had to pass, was fixed at Calais in 1363.","In 1331 Edward III granted royal protection to John Kempe, a Flemish weaver, and to his servants and apprentices, to establish cloth-making in England.","Flemish weavers settled in towns including Norwich, Colchester and London, and English cloth exports grew as exports of raw wool fell.","The Hanseatic League was an association of north German trading towns such as Lübeck, Hamburg and Cologne.","In London the Hansa held the Steelyard, a walled compound of warehouses and a hall beside the Thames at Dowgate, with privileges and rules of its own.","The Hansa also traded through Boston and King's Lynn, where its warehouse of 1475 survives as the only Hanseatic building left in England.","It shipped English wool and cloth out, and brought back Baltic timber, pitch, tar, furs, wax, grain and fish.","Italian bankers financed English kings: the Riccardi of Lucca under Edward I, and the Bardi and Peruzzi of Florence under Edward III; Lombard Street in London is named after them.","Italian galleys from Venice and Genoa traded at Southampton and London, bringing silk, spices and alum and buying wool.","A law of 1439 required alien merchants to lodge with an English host, who reported their dealings to the authorities.","From 1440 Parliament levied the alien subsidy, a tax charged only on people born abroad living in England; its returns name thousands of them in every county.","An alien could buy letters of denization from the Crown, which gave him most of the rights of an English subject."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — The context of English society: land, towns, wool, royal finance and Christendom","items":["After 1066 all land was held of the king by feudal tenure, so an incomer's right to hold or inherit land depended on royal favour.","Domesday Book in 1086 lists over a hundred boroughs, and hundreds more places had market charters by 1300.","Towns offered what the countryside did not: markets, guilds, credit and work for skilled hands, which is why migrants concentrated in them.","The wool trade was the backbone of the economy and of royal revenue, and it tied England to Flanders and to Italy.","Kings were permanently short of money for war and building, and raised it by taxing Jews, by customs duties on wool and by borrowing from Italian bankers.","Royal protection was therefore something that could be granted, sold or withdrawn, which is the key to the experience of almost every migrant group.","England was part of Latin Christendom: one Church, one written language, and clergy, monks and friars who moved between countries as a matter of course.","The legal status of 'alien' marked out those born abroad: they could not inherit land, paid higher rates of customs and could be taxed separately.","The Black Death of 1348–49 killed perhaps a third of the population, creating a labour shortage that pulled English peasants into the towns and made skilled foreign craftsmen welcome.","The Statute of Labourers of 1351 tried to hold wages at their pre-plague level and to stop workers moving for better pay, which shows how much internal migration the plague set off.","'Migrant' in this theme covers forced movement as well as voluntary, temporary merchants as well as permanent settlers, and movement within England as well as arrivals from abroad."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 1 Question 5 (16 marks + 4 SPaG): 'The Norman Conquest was the most important turning point in migration to England in the years c800–c1500.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • landownership • Jewish settlement. You must also use information of your own.","items":["Decoded question: the concept is significance, specifically turning points. 'Most important' is comparative, so the Conquest has to be measured against the Viking settlement before it and against the wool trade, the Black Death and the expulsion of 1290 after it. An answer that only describes what the Normans did cannot reach Level 4 however detailed it is.","Criterion to set in the opening: a turning point will be judged by whether it changed the direction migration came from, the reasons migrants came, and the authority that decided whether they could stay — and by how long the change lasted. Say at once that the answer will test each rival against the same three tests.","Paragraph 1 (the baseline before 1066): Viking migration crossed the North Sea to farm. Evidence: the Great Heathen Army of 865, Halfdan's division of Northumbria in 876, the Danelaw boundary agreed with Guthrum in the 880s, the wapentakes and the Five Boroughs, and the -by and -thorpe place names. Interim judgement: this was mass settlement into land, regional in reach and unplanned by any English authority.","Paragraph 2 (agree, on landownership — the first stimulus point): the Conquest transferred ownership of the whole country. Evidence: Domesday Book in 1086 with about two hundred great tenants-in-chief, almost all Norman or French; hundreds of motte-and-bailey castles and the White Tower from about 1078; the masons and craftsmen who followed the lords. Interim judgement: migration now came to rule rather than to farm, and it came through the king.","Paragraph 3 (agree, on Jewish settlement — the second stimulus point): a migration that had never existed here before. Evidence: financiers from Rouen encouraged by William I in the 1070s; communities in Lincoln, Norwich, York and Winchester; Jews and their goods the king's in law; the archae of 1194, out of which the Exchequer of the Jews grew. Interim judgement: the Conquest did not enlarge an existing flow, it created a new one and placed it wholly in royal hands.","Paragraph 4 (the point beyond the stimulus — the rival turning point of the economy): the wool trade and the Black Death. Evidence: the customs duty on wool from 1275; the Carta Mercatoria of 1303; John Kempe and the Flemish weavers protected from 1331; the staple at Calais in 1363; the Hansa at the Steelyard, Boston and King's Lynn; the plague of 1348–49 and the Statute of Labourers of 1351. Interim judgement: these moved far more people than the Conquest did, but they moved them through institutions the Conquest had already built.","Paragraph 5 (a second point beyond the stimulus — 1290 as a rival turning point): the expulsion ended a migration that had lasted two centuries, and no Jewish community was allowed back until the 1650s. Evidence: the Statute of the Jewry of 1275, the badge from 1218, the tallages, and Parliament's tax grant in return for expulsion in July 1290. Interim judgement: as an ending it is decisive, but it closed a channel the Conquest had opened, so it is a consequence of 1066 rather than a rival to it.","Evaluation thread running through every paragraph: separate the number of migrants a change produced from the framework it created. The Conquest loses on numbers and wins on framework; test each rival against exactly that distinction so the argument stays one argument.","Conclusion: agree, but on stated grounds. The Conquest is the most important turning point because it redirected migration from the North Sea to the Channel, changed its purpose from settlement to rule, trade and lending, and made the Crown the gatekeeper for everything that followed, including the wool privileges and the expulsion. Say which test matters most, and justify the choice rather than asserting it.","Timing on the 80-minute paper: about 25 minutes — roughly 4 minutes planning the criterion and the five paragraphs, 18 writing and 3 checking, since this is the only Section B answer in which spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology carry their own 4 marks.","Specialist terms to spell and use accurately for the SPaG marks: migration, settlement, Danelaw, wapentake, tenant-in-chief, feudal tenure, usury, tallage, alien, denization, staple, Hanseatic, turning point, continuity."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T12:55:11Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Judged afresh from the kit file: every new fact in the three retargeted worked examples checks out — the Pleading in English Act 1362 (pleas to be pleaded in English, enrolled in Latin), the alien subsidy granted by the parliament of 1439–40 at 16d for a householder and 6d for a servant or labourer, Edward I's Carta Mercatoria of 1 February 1303 (freedom of trade and protection at law for new customs duties), Edward III's letters of protection of 16 July 1331 to John Kempe of Flanders, weaver of woollen cloths, and his men, and canon 25 of the Third Lateran Council of 1179 refusing manifest usurers communion and Christian burial; only 'denization from the 1380s' runs a decade late. The retargeted stems duplicate no bank question in focus or stimulus and no printed Option 13 question — the 2021 SAM (Q3 Huguenot/Asian, Q4 18ᵗʰ–19ᵗʰ-century increase, Q5 culture, Q6 experiences c1700–present), June 2022, June 2023 and the 16 May 2024 live paper were all read — dupscan.js prints 'no duplication', the caps, AO splits and 80-minute timing match levels.md, and the kit is publishable once the one over-long solution is trimmed."}}},"13-2":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"13-2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This is the room where migration to England stops being a trickle of merchants and becomes a story of refugees, licensed settlements and whole industries. Every question in the thematic study asks you to compare periods, to explain why something changed or to judge how far it changed, and c1500–c1700 is the hinge: it is the first time England took in tens of thousands of people fleeing religious persecution, the first time the state licensed towns to receive them, and the first time migrants arrived because English ships, English trading companies and English privateers were reaching Africa, Asia and the Americas. If you can date the Dutch Church at Austin Friars, the Sandwich licence, the Egyptians Acts, the readmission of Jewish people and the revocation of the Edict of Nantes, and say what each one did to the people it touched, you can argue about continuity and change across the whole option instead of describing one group at a time.","idea":[{"h":"Who came, why they came, and where they stopped","t":"Four kinds of migrant matter in these two centuries. Protestant refugees from the Low Countries and France are the largest: Flemish and Walloon 'strangers' from the 1560s, then Huguenots after 1685. Romani people, first recorded in Britain early in the sixteenth century, came as travelling communities and were treated as a problem of order rather than as refugees. Africans arrived through trade, diplomacy, privateering and, by the later seventeenth century, through the Atlantic slave trade, and lived mostly in London and the ports. Jewish people, expelled in 1290, returned quietly from the 1650s, first as Sephardi merchants of Spanish and Portuguese descent coming by way of Amsterdam. The pattern of settlement changed less than the reasons: London above all, and the east and south-east coast facing the Continent — Sandwich, Canterbury, Norwich, Colchester, Maidstone, Southampton — because that is where the ships landed and where cloth towns needed work. The continuity to notice is that skilled cloth workers were being invited into England as early as Edward III's reign; what is new after 1550 is the scale, the religious motive and the licence. A top-level answer names the group, the decade and the place, and then says which part of the pattern is new and which is simply the medieval pattern continuing.","code":"13-2 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement"},{"h":"What made early modern England a destination","t":"England pulled migrants in because of what it had become. The Elizabethan religious settlement of 1559 made it officially and permanently Protestant, so persecuted Protestants abroad saw it as a refuge and a co-religionist state, and the stranger churches let them worship in their own language. Its economy was changing: the old export of heavy broadcloth through Antwerp slumped in the middle of the century, and decayed cloth towns wanted the lighter 'new draperies' that Flemish and Walloon weavers knew how to make. Trade was reaching much further — the Muscovy Company of 1555, the Levant Company of 1592, the East India Company chartered in 1600, the Hudson's Bay Company of 1670 and the Royal African Company of 1672 — and merchants, seamen, servants and enslaved people moved along those routes. War and privateering against Spain added another current, bringing Africans taken from captured Spanish and Portuguese ships. Underneath it all London was swelling, from perhaps fifty thousand people in 1500 to roughly half a million by 1700, which made it the one place in the country where a newcomer could disappear into a trade. A top-level answer uses these features as causes, explaining migration out of the condition of English society rather than listing the companies.","code":"13-2 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of English society"},{"h":"Privilege on paper, hostility in the street","t":"Migrants met two Englands. The Crown and the Privy Council generally valued skilled Protestants: they licensed settlements, granted denization by letters patent so an alien could hold land and trade more freely, and protected the stranger churches. But aliens still paid double the subsidy and higher customs, were kept out of most guilds, were forbidden to sell retail to English customers and were limited in how many servants and apprentices they could take. Ordinary tradesmen and apprentices saw competition, and the hostility was sometimes violent: the Evil May Day riot of 1 May 1517, when London apprentices attacked foreign craftsmen and merchants, ended with hundreds arrested and over a dozen hanged before Henry VIII pardoned the rest; in 1593 a threatening libel was pinned to the wall of the Dutch Church in London, and Parliament that year debated a bill against alien retailers. Those without a skill to sell fared far worse: the Egyptians Act of 1530 ordered Romani people out of the realm and the Act of 1554 made remaining a felony punishable by death, and in 1596 more than a hundred Romani people were condemned at York, nine of them hanged. In 1596 and again in 1601 Elizabeth I's government licensed a merchant, Caspar van Senden, to transport 'blackamoores' out of England, though there is little sign it was carried out. A top-level answer never says migrants were simply welcomed or simply hated: it separates the state from the street, and the skilled from the poor.","code":"13-2 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in England"},{"h":"What the migrants actually changed","t":"The economic impact is the easiest to prove and the easiest to date. In industry, Flemish and Walloon weavers brought the new draperies — bays and says, lighter and cheaper than broadcloth — and they became England's leading cloth export; Huguenots after 1685 built the silk trade of Spitalfields and Canterbury; a German, John Spilman, was licensed in 1588 to make white paper at Dartford; French and Italian glassmakers, among them Jean Carré from 1567, brought fine glass to London; printing had been in foreign hands since Caxton's day. In agriculture, Dutch and Flemish market gardening spread around Sandwich, Norwich and London, and Dutch engineers led by Cornelius Vermuyden drained Hatfield Chase from 1626 and the Great Level of the Fens for the Earl of Bedford from 1630, cutting the Old Bedford River in 1637 and the New Bedford River in 1651 and turning fen into farmland. In trade and finance, stranger and refugee merchants tied London to Amsterdam and Hamburg, and the Bank of England, founded in 1694, took its first governor, Sir John Houblon, from a family of Protestant refugees. In culture, foreign painters served the court — Holbein under Henry VIII, Van Dyck under Charles I, Lely under Charles II — and the very word 'refugee' entered English from the French réfugié to describe the Huguenots. A top-level answer weighs these against each other on a stated criterion rather than listing them.","code":"13-2 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in England"},{"h":"Sandwich and Canterbury: a licensed experiment","t":"Sandwich is the model case of settlement by permission. Its harbour was silting and its cloth trade decayed, so in 1561 the town was licensed to receive twenty-five Flemish households — a few hundred people — on condition that they taught their trades and did not sell retail to the English. They brought bays and says, fishing and market gardening, and within little more than a decade strangers were roughly a third of the town's people, which produced friction: limits on their looms and on their trading, and complaints from English craftsmen, pushed many on to Canterbury. There Walloon refugees, French-speaking Protestants from the southern Netherlands, were allowed to worship in the crypt of Canterbury Cathedral, a French-language congregation that has never stopped meeting there, and their silk and fine-cloth weaving became the city's leading industry, so that when Huguenots arrived after 1685 they joined a French-speaking Protestant community already more than a century old. The two towns show the whole pattern in miniature: economic invitation, conditions attached, real prosperity, local resentment, and a refugee church at the centre of community life. A top-level answer uses the pair as evidence for a claim about policy or about experience, not as a story told for its own sake.","code":"13-2 — 3 Case studies: Sandwich and Canterbury in the sixteenth century"},{"h":"The Huguenots: the largest refugee movement of the period","t":"The Edict of Nantes, issued by Henry IV in 1598, had given French Protestants legal toleration; Louis XIV revoked it in October 1685, after years of pressure that included quartering soldiers on Protestant households. Emigration was forbidden, but around two hundred thousand Huguenots left France, and roughly fifty thousand reached England — by far the largest single group of migrants the country had received. Perhaps half settled in London, the silk weavers in Spitalfields just outside the City and its guilds, others in Soho, and there were substantial communities in Canterbury, Norwich, Plymouth and Bristol. England was ready for them: Charles II had promised denization to Huguenot refugees in a declaration of 1681, national church collections raised relief money, including one authorised by James II in 1686, and French-speaking churches such as Threadneedle Street already existed. They brought silk weaving, silversmithing, clock and gun making, paper making and banking skills; Huguenot regiments fought for William III in Ireland at the Boyne in 1690; Huguenot investors helped to launch the Bank of England in 1694. English weavers still complained of competition, and integration took generations, but the Huguenots became the standard example of a refugee group absorbed into English life. A top-level answer contrasts their reception with the treatment of Romani people in the same two centuries to show that welcome depended on skill and religion.","code":"13-2 — 3 Case studies: The experience of Huguenots in seventeenth century England"}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 3, 4 marks: turning two descriptions into one analysed similarity","setup":"Paper 1 Question 3, worth 4 marks: 'Explain one way in which the impact of migrants on English farming in the years c800–c1500 was similar to their impact on English farming in the years c1500–c1700.' The mark scheme gives two marks for AO2 and two for AO1, so one similarity has to be analysed and then supported with specific information about each of the two periods named.","steps":["Choose a similarity of effect rather than of group: in both periods migrants did not simply join English farming, they remade the land itself, and the shape they gave it outlasted them.","Evidence the earlier period with named detail: Scandinavian settlers who took land in Yorkshire, eastern Mercia and East Anglia from the 870s ploughed it themselves and left farm and village names ending in -by and -thorpe that are still on the map, while twelve Cistercian monks sent from Clairvaux founded Rievaulx in 1132 on land given by Walter Espec and turned the Yorkshire valleys around it into sheep granges whose wool was sold across Europe.","Evidence the later period with named detail: Dutch and Flemish market gardeners grew vegetables for the towns, first around Sandwich, where the strangers grew carrots and celery on the land east of the town, and from about 1600 on the gardens that fed London, while the Dutch engineer Cornelius Vermuyden drained Hatfield Chase from 1626 and the Great Level of the Fens from 1630, the Old Bedford River being cut in 1637.","Close with the sentence that makes it one similarity rather than two descriptions: in both periods the newcomers changed what the land was and what it produced instead of merely working inside a system the English had already built, so in both the mark they left on English farming outlived them."],"cues":["Which kind of similarity can carry all four marks here — a similarity in who the migrants were, or a similarity in what their farming did to the land?","What named detail from before c1500 proves the first half of that claim?","What named detail from after c1500 proves the second half?","What must the last sentence do that the two pieces of evidence have not already done?"]},{"title":"Question 4, 12 marks: escaping the stimulus cap","setup":"Paper 1 Question 4, worth 12 marks: 'Explain why Jewish people were able to settle in England again in the 1650s.' You may use the following in your answer: Menasseh ben Israel; the Whitehall Conference. You must also use information of your own. The mark scheme caps an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content at eight marks and shuts Level 4 to it altogether, so at least one developed reason must come from your own knowledge.","steps":["Deal with the first stimulus point as the move that reopened the question: Menasseh ben Israel, a rabbi from Amsterdam, came to London in 1655 and petitioned Cromwell's Council of State for Jews to be allowed to live, worship, trade and bury their dead in England, putting before the government a question that had been closed since Edward I expelled the Jews in 1290.","Deal with the second stimulus point and explain why a conference that decided nothing still mattered: Cromwell opened the Whitehall Conference on 4 December 1655 and it sat until 18 December without reaching a decision, but the lawyers told it that no law actually barred Jews from returning, so nothing had to be repealed and permission could afterwards be given quietly instead of by statute.","Now add the reason that lifts the cap — the Spanish war and the worth of the merchants: England had been at war with Spain since 1655, and when the London merchant Antonio Rodrigues Robles had his goods and two of his ships seized in March 1656 as those of an enemy Spaniard he recovered them by declaring that he was not a Spaniard but a Portuguese Jew; deciding for him meant accepting that Jews could live in England openly, and it protected traders whose Amsterdam and Iberian connections the government wanted.","Tie the three together in a line of reasoning: Menasseh's petition raised the question, the Whitehall Conference showed that no law stood in the way, and the Robles case forced a decision the government had commercial as well as religious reasons to make, which is why a synagogue opened in Creechurch Lane in 1657 and a burial ground was taken at Mile End in the same year although no Act of readmission was ever passed."],"cues":["What exactly did the first stimulus point ask the government to allow?","Why does a conference that reached no decision still belong in the answer?","Which reason from your own knowledge is developed enough to carry the answer past the cap?","How should the three be joined so that they read as one argument rather than three?"]},{"title":"Question 5 or 6, 16 marks: choosing the criterion before you weigh","setup":"Paper 1 Question 5, worth 16 marks with up to 4 further marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology: \"'The charter granted to the strangers' churches in 1550 was the most important turning point for migrants in England in the years c1500–c1700.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\" You may use the following in your answer: the revolt in the Netherlands; the readmission of the Jews. You must also use information of your own. The top level asks for criteria that are justified and applied, not simply a list of examples.","steps":["State the criterion in the opening lines: a turning point will be judged by whether it changed what a migrant community was legally allowed to be in England, and by how much of what came after depended on it, not by how dramatic it looked at the time.","Argue the case for the charter with precise evidence: on 24 July 1550 Edward VI granted the strangers a church of their own under their own superintendent, Jan Laski, free to worship by their own rites rather than the English ones, with the Dutch congregation at Austin Friars and the French-speaking congregation later at Threadneedle Street; it was closed under Mary in 1553 and re-established under Elizabeth in 1560, and the refugee congregations allowed afterwards at Sandwich, Norwich and Canterbury, and the Huguenot churches after 1685, all followed the form it had set.","Argue the case for rival turning points with equally precise evidence: the revolt in the Netherlands from 1566 and the repression that followed drove refugees to England in numbers no charter could have produced, and the readmission of the Jews in the 1650s ended an exclusion that had stood since Edward I's expulsion of 1290, which the charter of 1550 had done nothing to touch.","Reach the judgement by applying the criterion rather than counting events: the revolt supplied the migrants and the 1650s reopened England to a people it had thrown out, but neither invented the legal form of a chartered foreign congregation worshipping in its own way, and it was that form the later settlements used, so 1550 is the turning point on the criterion of legal standing, though not on the criterion of numbers."],"cues":["What must the first paragraph settle before any event can be called a turning point?","What evidence shows that the charter changed what a migrant community could legally be?","Which later events have a stronger claim, and on what ground?","How do you turn two rival claims into a judgement the examiner can call justified?"]},{"title":"Using the Sandwich and Canterbury case study as evidence, not as a story","setup":"Paper 1 Question 6, worth 16 marks with up to 4 further marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology: \"'Local authorities did more than the Crown to shape the lives of migrants in England in the years c1500–c1700.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\" You may use the following in your answer: the Privy Council; Canterbury. You must also use information of your own. You have the Sandwich and Canterbury case study in your notes: the request Sandwich's own council put to the Privy Council in May 1561, the licence that followed for twenty-five Flemish households, the condition that they teach their trades and not sell retail to the English, the growth of strangers to roughly a third of Sandwich's people within a decade or so, limits later placed on their looms and trading, the move of many families on to Canterbury, the grant of the cathedral's western crypt to the Walloon congregation in 1575 and the silk weaving that became the city's main industry.","steps":["Pick the detail that argues for the statement: every condition the strangers actually lived under was a town's doing — they were to teach their trades, they were barred from selling retail to the English, and when they grew to roughly a third of Sandwich's people the town limited their looms and their trading.","Pick the detail from the same case study that argues against it: the settlement existed at all only because the Privy Council licensed twenty-five Flemish households in 1561, so Sandwich was setting terms inside a permission it had had to ask for and could have been refused.","Pick the detail that complicates both: when the terms at Sandwich hardened many families moved on to Canterbury, where the western crypt was given to the Walloon congregation in 1575 and silk weaving grew into the city's main industry, so what the migrants decided for themselves mattered as much as what either authority decided for them.","Write the interim judgement the paragraph must end with: the Crown decided whether migrants could come at all, the towns decided what they could do once they were there, and the migrants themselves decided whether to stay, so the statement holds for daily working life and fails for entry."],"cues":["Which fact in the case study shows a decision only the town could have taken?","Which fact in the same case study shows a decision the town could not have taken for itself?","Which fact shows the migrants themselves deciding something?","What has the last sentence of the paragraph got to do to earn credit for judgement?"]},{"title":"Weighing impact by place: London against the provincial cloth towns","setup":"Paper 1 Question 6, worth 16 marks with up to 4 further marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology: \"'Migrants changed London more than they changed the rest of England in the years c1500–c1700.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\" You may use the following in your answer: the returns of strangers of 1593; Norwich. You must also use information of your own. Your evidence is the chartered strangers' churches in London at Austin Friars and Threadneedle Street, the court painters Hans Holbein of Augsburg, who settled in London in 1532 and died there in 1543, and Anthony van Dyck of Antwerp, made principal painter to Charles I in 1632 and dead in London in 1641, the Huguenot silk weavers who filled Spitalfields after 1685, the returns of strangers made in 1593, which put aliens at about one Londoner in twenty, and against them the letters patent of November 1565 admitting thirty master textile workers and their households to Norwich, the growth of the Norwich strangers to nearly four thousand by 1571 and to roughly a third of the city, the eleven Dutch families who reached Colchester in 1565 and the bays and says later sealed in its Dutch Bay Hall, and the Walloon congregation worshipping in the crypt of Canterbury Cathedral.","steps":["Set the measure first: change will be judged by how many migrants a place received, by what share of its people they became, and by whether its trades would have existed without them.","Make the London case with the strongest examples: London had the chartered strangers' churches of 1550, the court painters Holbein and Van Dyck, and after 1685 a whole silk-weaving district in Spitalfields, and the return of strangers made in 1593 counted aliens in the city and its suburbs alike, so religious, artistic and industrial change can all be found in one place at once.","Make the provincial case at its strongest: Norwich admitted thirty master textile workers and their households by letters patent in November 1565 and counted nearly four thousand strangers by 1571, about one person in three, while the eleven Dutch families who reached Colchester in the same year grew into the community whose bays and says were sealed in the Dutch Bay Hall before sale.","Apply the measure to reach the interim judgement: London wins on numbers and on variety, but its aliens were only about one Londoner in twenty when the strangers were counted in 1593 while Norwich's were about one in three, and no London trade was created from nothing as Colchester's bay making was, so the statement stands only if change is measured by total numbers rather than by how far a place was remade."],"cues":["What has to be decided before the two can be compared?","Which London evidence shows more than one kind of migrant impact at once?","Which provincial town was remade furthest, and what figure proves it?","On which measure does the statement survive, and on which does it fail?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Why did decayed cloth towns such as Sandwich and Norwich want Flemish and Walloon settlers in the 1560s?","a":"Because the strangers knew how to make the new draperies — lighter, cheaper worsted cloths such as bays and says — at a time when the old heavy broadcloth export trade had slumped, so their skills promised to revive the towns' trade."},{"q":"Why were the stranger churches so important to refugees' experience in England?","a":"They let refugees worship in their own language under their own elders, held their communities together, provided poor relief and discipline, and gave the authorities a body to deal with, which made the settlements easier to permit."},{"q":"Why did the experience of Romani people differ so sharply from that of the Flemish and Walloon strangers?","a":"The strangers brought trades the government wanted and shared its Protestant religion, so they were licensed and protected; Romani people were seen as masterless travellers with no skill to sell, so Parliament legislated against their presence itself."},{"q":"Why is it wrong to say that Jewish people were legally readmitted to England in 1656?","a":"No law was passed and the expulsion of 1290 was never repealed; Cromwell's council simply allowed private worship in 1656, with a burial ground leased at Mile End in 1657, so the community grew on toleration in practice rather than on a legal right."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 1 Section B, the thematic study, is worth 36 of the paper's 52 marks: Question 3 is 4 marks, Question 4 is 12 marks, and Question 5 or Question 6 is 16 marks plus up to 4 additional marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology.","Question 3, 'Explain one way in which … was similar to / different from …', carries 2 marks for AO2 and 2 for AO1, and normally spans at least a century, so both of the named periods must be evidenced with specific information.","Question 4 stimulus caps: answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 8 marks and have no access to Level 4; no cap is printed at Level 2.","Question 5 or 6 stimulus caps: answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 11 marks and have no access to Level 4; no cap is printed at Level 2.","Timing on the 80-minute Paper 1 is roughly a minute a mark: Section A takes about 30 minutes for its three questions and Section B about 45 — roughly 5 minutes on Question 3, 15 on Question 4 and 25 on Question 5 or 6, the one Section B answer where spelling and specialist terms are separately marked as Threshold 1, Intermediate 2–3 and High 4 — which leaves about five minutes to check the paper."],"questions":[{"q":"Explain one way in which the reasons why migrants came to England in the years c800–c1500 were similar to the reasons why migrants came to England in the years c1500–c1700. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed similarity — in both periods English rulers recruited skilled cloth workers for economic gain — supported by specific detail from each period, such as Edward III's encouragement of Flemish weavers from the 1330s and the licensing of Sandwich in 1561.","sol":"In both periods migrants came because English rulers wanted skills the country lacked, above all in cloth. From the 1330s Edward III encouraged Flemish weavers to settle under royal protection, so that English wool could be finished here instead of being shipped raw to Flanders. The same motive brought the strangers of the sixteenth century: in 1561 the Privy Council licensed Sandwich to receive twenty-five Flemish households on condition that they taught the town to make the new draperies, and Norwich invited thirty Dutch and Walloon masters in 1565. In both periods the state treated skilled foreigners as an economic asset, so invitation, not only flight, explains their arrival.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Look for a similarity in why migrants were wanted, not in who they were.","One industry should appear on both sides of the comparison, with the person or body doing the inviting named each time.","State the similarity, prove the earlier period, prove the later period, then say in one sentence what makes the two the same."],"codes":["13-2 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","13-2 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of English society"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the treatment of Jewish people in England in the years c800–c1500 was different from their treatment in the years c1500–c1700. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — medieval treatment was a matter of royal law, protection and finally legal expulsion, whereas the seventeenth-century return rested on toleration in practice — supported by detail such as the 1290 expulsion and the permission of 1656.","sol":"The difference lies in the part the law played. In the medieval period Jewish people lived under the direct control of the Crown: they came after the Norman Conquest, were protected as the king's moneylenders but treated as his property, were taxed heavily, were made to wear a distinguishing badge, and in 1290 Edward I expelled them from the kingdom by royal order. In the later period there was no law at all: after Menasseh ben Israel's petition and the Whitehall Conference of 1655, Cromwell's council simply gave its permission in 1656 and a burial ground was leased at Mile End in 1657, and a Sephardi community grew in London on toleration. Royal law had given way to quiet permission.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what the Crown did about Jewish people in each period — and whether it did anything formal at all in the second.","The medieval half needs the way the community was held and how it ended; the later half needs how the return was allowed.","State the difference in terms of law, prove each half with a date, and finish by naming what kind of difference it is."],"codes":["13-2 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","13-2 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in England"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the experience of migrants in England in the years c1500–c1700 was similar to the experience of migrants in the years c1700–c1900. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed similarity — in both periods migrants were accused of taking work and undercutting wages, and hostility could turn violent — supported by detail such as Evil May Day in 1517 or the Dutch Church libel of 1593 and anti-Irish rioting after the Famine.","sol":"In both periods working people blamed migrants for competition over work, and that resentment could become violence. In 1517 London apprentices rioted on Evil May Day and attacked foreign craftsmen and merchants, and in 1593 a threatening libel was pinned to the wall of the Dutch Church in London warning strangers to leave; the complaint each time was that foreigners took custom and employed their own. The same charge met Irish migrants who came in large numbers after the Famine of 1845–49: they were accused of working for lower wages, and there was serious anti-Irish rioting in northern towns such as Stockport in 1852. In both periods economic competition, real or imagined, produced hostility from below.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about the reaction of ordinary tradesmen and workers rather than the policy of the government.","Both halves need a named incident with a date, not a general statement that people were unfriendly.","State the similarity, evidence each period with one named episode, and end by saying what the shared cause of the hostility was."],"codes":["13-2 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in England","13-2 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the impact of migrants on English industry in the years c1500–c1700 was different from their impact on industry in the years c1900–present. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — early modern migrants introduced industries and techniques England did not have, whereas twentieth-century migrants were recruited to fill labour shortages in industries that already existed — supported by named detail from both periods.","sol":"The difference is between bringing an industry and staffing one. In the early modern period migrants supplied techniques England lacked: Flemish and Walloon weavers introduced the new draperies of bays and says, Huguenots built silk weaving in Spitalfields and Canterbury after 1685, and John Spilman, a German, was licensed in 1588 to make white paper at Dartford. In the twentieth century the industries were already there and short of workers, so migrants were actively recruited into them: London Transport ran recruitment schemes in the Caribbean from the mid-1950s, and the new National Health Service and the textile mills of Lancashire and Yorkshire took on workers from the Caribbean and South Asia. Innovation had given way to labour supply.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what the migrants supplied in each period: a skill nobody here had, or hands for work already waiting.","Name one industry that migrants created in the earlier period and one they were recruited into in the later one.","State the difference, evidence each side, and end with a sentence naming the change in the kind of contribution."],"codes":["13-2 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in England","13-2 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of English society"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the places where migrants settled in the years c800–c1500 were similar to the places where they settled in the years c1500–c1700. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed similarity — settlement concentrated in London and in the eastern and south-eastern ports and cloth towns facing the Continent — supported by named places from both periods, such as the Steelyard and medieval Jewish communities, and Sandwich, Canterbury and Norwich.","sol":"In both periods migrants settled where the trade routes from the Continent landed: London above all, and the towns of the east and south-east. In the medieval period Hanseatic merchants held their own quarter, the Steelyard, on the Thames in London, Jewish communities were established in London, York, Lincoln and Norwich, and Flemish weavers settled in the East Anglian cloth towns. The same geography shaped early modern settlement: the strangers concentrated in London, in Sandwich and Canterbury in Kent, and in Norwich and Colchester in East Anglia, and the Huguenots after 1685 settled heavily in London. In each period the short crossing and the cloth and trading towns decided where newcomers stopped.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Put the two periods on a map of England rather than on a timeline.","Name three or four actual places for each period, not just 'the south-east'.","State the similarity, evidence both periods with places, then explain in one sentence why the geography stayed the same."],"codes":["13-2 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","13-2 — 3 Case studies: Sandwich and Canterbury in the sixteenth century"]},{"q":"Explain why so many Protestant refugees came to England in the second half of the sixteenth century. [12 marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Duke of Alba\n• the Dutch Church at Austin Friars\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit explained reasons — persecution in the Spanish Netherlands and France, the refuge offered by a Protestant state and its stranger churches, and deliberate economic invitation by towns and Privy Council. Stimulus caps apply: answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 8 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"Protestant refugees came in such numbers because persecution abroad, safety in England and economic invitation all pushed in the same direction at the same time.\n\nThe first reason is persecution in the Spanish Netherlands. Philip II sent the Duke of Alba as governor in 1567, and the tribunal he set up condemned Protestants and rebels in large numbers, so entire congregations of Flemish and Walloon Protestants left the southern provinces. The revolt that followed made the flight worse, and when Antwerp fell to Spanish forces in 1585 another wave of Protestant merchants and craftsmen crossed the North Sea. France produced the same effect: the St Bartholomew's Day Massacre of 24 August 1572 killed thousands of Huguenots in Paris and the provinces, and survivors fled to Rye, Southampton and London.\n\nThe second reason is that England had made itself a place a foreign Protestant could actually live. Edward VI had chartered a church for the strangers in 1550, with the Dutch-speaking congregation at Austin Friars in London and a French-speaking congregation of its own. Mary I closed them, but after the religious settlement of 1559 they reopened, so refugees could worship in their own language, under their own elders, with their own poor relief and discipline. Nowhere else in northern Europe offered that combination to both Dutch and French speakers.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond the stimulus and explains why they were let in: English towns wanted them. The old export of heavy broadcloth had slumped, and the strangers knew the lighter new draperies that were selling. Sandwich was licensed in 1561 to take twenty-five Flemish households on condition that they taught their trades, Norwich invited thirty Dutch and Walloon masters in 1565, and Canterbury, Colchester and Maidstone followed. The government gained skills and revenue, so it granted denization and protection.\n\nPersecution decided that the refugees had to move; the stranger churches and the towns' invitations decided that England was where they moved to.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Separate what drove these people out of their own countries from what made England the country they chose.","Each reason should run as a chain: the situation, the named event or institution, and the effect on where people went.","Take the persecution first and the refugee churches second, then find a third reason of your own, because three or more aspects of content are needed to pass 8 marks — look at what English towns wanted from the newcomers."],"codes":["13-2 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","13-2 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of English society","13-2 — 3 Case studies: Sandwich and Canterbury in the sixteenth century"]},{"q":"Explain why the Huguenots left France for England after 1685. [12 marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the revocation of the Edict of Nantes\n• Spitalfields\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit explained reasons — the destruction of legal toleration in France, the existence of work and communities in London outside guild control, and active English encouragement through denization, relief collections and an established refugee church. Stimulus caps apply: answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 8 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"The Huguenots came to England because staying in France meant giving up their religion, and because England had already made itself the easiest place in which to rebuild a Protestant working life.\n\nThe first reason is the ending of toleration in France. Henry IV's Edict of Nantes of 1598 had given French Protestants the legal right to worship; Louis XIV revoked it in October 1685, after years of pressure that included quartering soldiers on Protestant families to force conversions. Churches were closed, ministers expelled and children were to be brought up Catholic. Emigration was itself forbidden, which shows how strong the pressure was: around two hundred thousand Huguenots left anyway, and roughly fifty thousand of them reached England.\n\nThe second reason is that England offered work in the trades they already knew. Many were skilled silk weavers, and they settled in Spitalfields, just outside the City of London, where the guilds could not stop a stranger setting up a loom. That one district became the centre of English silk, and Canterbury, Norwich and Plymouth took weavers too, so a refugee could expect to earn rather than beg.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond the stimulus: England actively encouraged them. Charles II had promised denization to Huguenot refugees in a declaration of 1681, so they could hold property and trade; national collections were made in churches for their relief, including one authorised by James II in 1686; and French-speaking Protestant congregations, such as the one in Threadneedle Street, had existed since the middle of the previous century, so ministers, poor relief and schooling were waiting. After 1688 William III, himself a Dutch Protestant at war with Louis XIV, welcomed them, and Huguenot regiments served in his army.\n\nPersecution made the Huguenots leave France, but it was the combination of an unregulated trade, an established church and public money that made so many of them land in England rather than elsewhere.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask two separate questions: why leave France at all, and why England rather than the Dutch Republic or Germany?","Every reason needs the mechanism spelled out — what the law took away, what the district allowed, what the money paid for.","Use the revocation and the silk district first, then add a reason of your own about how England received them, since without three or more aspects of content the mark is capped at 8."],"codes":["13-2 — 3 Case studies: The experience of Huguenots in seventeenth century England","13-2 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","13-2 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in England"]},{"q":"Explain why the way the English government treated migrants changed in the years c1500–c1700. [12 marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Egyptians Act of 1530\n• the strangers' churches\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit explained reasons for change — the Reformation making Protestant refugees co-religionists, economic need for skills in decayed towns and new industries, and the growth of trade and of arguments for toleration by the 1650s. Stimulus caps apply: answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 8 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"Government treatment of migrants changed because the questions the government was asking about newcomers changed: first whether they were a threat to order, then whether they shared the country's religion, and finally whether they were worth money.\n\nThe first reason is that the early Tudor state judged migrants by order and poverty. The Egyptians Act of 1530 ordered Romani people to leave the realm within sixteen days and forbade more from coming, and the Act of 1554 made remaining for more than a month a felony punishable by death; in 1596 more than a hundred Romani people were condemned at York and nine were hanged. Migrants with no trade to sell were treated as a problem of vagrancy, and that strand of policy never disappeared.\n\nThe second reason is the Reformation. Once the settlement of 1559 had made England permanently Protestant, refugees from Catholic persecution were fellow believers rather than aliens, and the stranger churches chartered in 1550 were allowed to reopen and multiply. Protecting them advertised England as the champion of the Protestant cause, and by 1686 the state was authorising national collections in churches to relieve Huguenot refugees.\n\nThe third reason, beyond the stimulus, is economic calculation. Decayed cloth towns needed skills, so the Privy Council licensed Sandwich to receive Flemish households in 1561 and Norwich followed in 1565, always on conditions that protected English retailers. Dutch engineers were hired to drain the fens from the 1620s. By the 1650s the same calculation reached further: when Menasseh ben Israel petitioned in 1655, the value of Jewish merchants' trade with Amsterdam helped to persuade Cromwell's council to permit private worship in 1656, a burial ground being leased at Mile End the following year, without any law being passed at all.\n\nPolicy did not become simply kinder. It became more selective: religion decided who was considered, usefulness decided who was licensed, and the poor and the landless were treated as harshly at the end of the period as at the beginning.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Policy was not one policy — sort the migrants the government wanted from the ones it wanted rid of.","Build each reason on a change in England itself, then show the law or decision that followed from it.","Start with the law against those with no trade, then the effect of England becoming Protestant, and add an economic reason of your own, so that three or more aspects of content are addressed and the 8-mark cap does not apply."],"codes":["13-2 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in England","13-2 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of English society","13-2 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement"]},{"q":"Explain why migrants had such a large impact on the English cloth industry in the years c1500–c1700. [12 marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the new draperies\n• Canterbury\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit explained reasons — the technique itself was new to England and suited the market, migrants arrived as whole working communities able to train others, and government policy planted them in decayed cloth towns; the Huguenot silk trade after 1685 extended the effect. Stimulus caps apply: answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 8 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"Migrants transformed English cloth because they arrived with a technique England did not have, at the moment the old trade was failing, and because they came in whole communities that could pass the technique on.\n\nThe first reason is the product. English cloth exports had rested on heavy broadcloth sold through Antwerp, and that trade slumped in the middle of the sixteenth century. The Flemish and Walloon strangers made the new draperies — lighter, cheaper worsted cloths such as bays and says — which suited warmer markets in southern Europe and cost less to buy. England could not simply copy them: the spinning, the weaving and the finishing were all different, so the skill had to walk in wearing shoes.\n\nThe second reason is the form the migration took. Refugees came as families and congregations, with masters, journeymen and their own church discipline, so training and quality control travelled with them. In Canterbury the Walloons, worshipping in the crypt of the cathedral, built silk and fine-cloth weaving into the city's principal industry, and their community was still there, French-speaking, when Huguenot weavers arrived more than a century later. Norwich took thirty Dutch and Walloon masters in 1565 and Sandwich twenty-five Flemish households in 1561.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond the stimulus: the state placed them where the effect would be greatest and then let the effect spread. Licences went to towns whose trade had decayed, the strangers were barred from retail selling so that they competed in production rather than in the shops, and their cloths were sealed and searched for quality. After 1685 the same pattern repeated at a higher value, when Huguenot silk weavers concentrated in Spitalfields outside the City guilds and made England a producer of a luxury fabric it had previously imported.\n\nThe impact was so large because the migrants supplied a technique, the people to teach it and a government willing to plant them exactly where the industry had collapsed.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the strangers actually made, and why English weavers could not simply imitate it.","One reason should be about the product, one about the people who carried the skill, and one about where they were put.","Use the new cloths and the Kent city first, then add something of your own — the later silk trade, or the terms on which towns were licensed."],"codes":["13-2 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in England","13-2 — 3 Case studies: Sandwich and Canterbury in the sixteenth century","13-2 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of English society"]},{"q":"Explain why some people in England were hostile to migrants in the years c1500–c1700. [12 marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• Evil May Day (1517)\n• the guilds\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit explained reasons — fear of economic competition sharpened by guild exclusion, suspicion of people who looked, worshipped or lived differently, and resentment of the privileges and changes the authorities granted migrants. Stimulus caps apply: answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 8 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"Hostility came from people who believed that migrants were taking something from them: work, custom, common land or the protection of the law.\n\nThe first reason is competition for work and trade. On 1 May 1517, after weeks of agitation in London, apprentices rioted against foreign merchants and craftsmen, attacking their houses and workshops; hundreds were arrested and more than a dozen were hanged before Henry VIII pardoned the rest. The grievance was that strangers took custom and employed their own countrymen. The guilds sharpened it: because companies would not admit aliens, strangers worked in the liberties and suburbs outside guild control, such as Southwark and later Spitalfields, which made guildsmen angrier still, since men they could not regulate were selling in their city. In 1593 a threatening libel was pinned to the wall of the Dutch Church in London and Parliament debated a bill against alien retailers.\n\nThe second reason is suspicion of people whose way of life was unfamiliar, which hardened in years of hardship. Romani people were criminalised by the Egyptians Act of 1530 and by the Act of 1554 that made remaining a capital felony, and in 1596, during a run of failed harvests, more than a hundred were condemned at York. In the same years Elizabeth I's government licensed a merchant, Caspar van Senden, to transport 'blackamoores' out of the realm, blaming them for scarcity, though little seems to have come of it.\n\nThe third reason, beyond the stimulus, is resentment of the privileges and the changes that came with migrants. Denization, licensed settlements and royal protection all looked like favours; and where migrants physically altered how people lived, the anger was fiercest of all. Fenmen tore down the banks and sluices of the drainage schemes led by Cornelius Vermuyden from the 1620s and 1630s because draining the fen destroyed the fishing, fowling and grazing on which they lived.\n\nHostility was strongest, then, wherever migrants seemed to be gaining at the expense of English people who had no way of answering back.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what each group of hostile English people thought they were losing.","Build one reason on trade, one on difference and fear, and one on privilege or on a change forced on a community.","Take the London riot and the trade companies first, then add an example of your own from outside London, so that three or more aspects of content are addressed and the 8-mark cap does not apply."],"codes":["13-2 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in England","13-2 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in England","13-2 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of English society"]},{"q":"'The experience of migrants in England changed very little in the years c1500–c1700.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Egyptians Acts\n• the Huguenots\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit continuity (legal disability, guild exclusion, popular hostility from 1517 to the 1680s, the criminalisation of Romani people) weighed against change (stranger churches from 1550, licensed settlements, Jewish worship from 1656, organised relief for Huguenots), with a judgement that distinguishes skilled Protestants from the poor. Stimulus caps apply: answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 11 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"Experience must be judged on three things: the legal standing a migrant had, the attitude of the government, and the reaction of the people living next door. Judged that way the statement is half right, because the period pulled the experience of different migrants in opposite directions.\n\nMuch did stay the same. Aliens remained outsiders in law throughout: they paid double the subsidy and higher customs, were kept out of most guilds, were forbidden to sell retail to English customers and were limited in the number of servants and apprentices they might take, unless they bought denization by royal letters patent. Popular hostility is just as continuous. London apprentices rioted against foreign craftsmen on Evil May Day in 1517; in 1593 a threatening libel was fixed to the wall of the Dutch Church in London and Parliament debated a bill against alien retailers; and in the 1680s English weavers complained that Huguenot competitors were undercutting them. Nearly two centuries separate the first and the last of those, and the complaint is the same one.\n\nFor some migrants the law grew harsher rather than easier. The Egyptians Act of 1530 ordered Romani people out of the realm; the Act of 1554 made remaining beyond a month a felony punishable by death; and in 1596 more than a hundred Romani people were condemned at York, nine of them hanged. In 1596 and 1601 the government licensed a merchant to transport 'blackamoores' out of England. For people without a skill to sell, being a migrant became more dangerous during this period, not less.\n\nAgainst that stands a real and deliberate change in how the state received the migrants it wanted. Edward VI chartered a church for the strangers in 1550, so a refugee could worship in his own language under his own elders. Sandwich was licensed to take twenty-five Flemish households in 1561 and Norwich invited thirty Dutch and Walloon masters in 1565: settlement by public decision rather than private arrival. By 1656 Cromwell's council was allowing Jewish worship in London, and a burial ground was leased at Mile End in 1657, quietly reversing an expulsion of 1290 that no law had ever undone. And after 1685 the reception of the Huguenots was organised on a scale that would have been unimaginable in 1500 — Charles II's promise of denization in 1681, national church collections including one authorised by James II in 1686, and about fifty thousand refugees absorbed, many into Spitalfields silk weaving, with Huguenots later among the founders of the Bank of England in 1694.\n\nThe experience of individuals shows the same split. A Walloon weaver in Canterbury could worship in the cathedral crypt and pass a trade to his children; a Romani family could be hanged for being in the country at all.\n\nSo the statement holds only for one part of the migrant population and only for one of the three measures. Popular hostility changed hardly at all, and the legal disabilities of aliens were still recognisable in 1700. But the state's behaviour changed fundamentally: it moved from simply tolerating or expelling newcomers to recruiting, licensing, sheltering and even funding them — provided they were Protestant and had something England wanted. Experience therefore diverged: better for the skilled refugee, worse for the poor traveller.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first what 'experience' is going to mean in your answer, because the verdict depends on it.","Test the claim separately on two very different groups and see whether it survives both tests.","Set the unchanging things — law, taxes, guilds, riots — against the things the state newly did, and let the difference between the two groups carry your conclusion."],"codes":["13-2 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in England","13-2 — 3 Case studies: The experience of Huguenots in seventeenth century England","13-2 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement"]},{"q":"'Migrants changed English industry more than English agriculture in the years c1500–c1700.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the new draperies\n• the draining of the Fens\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit industrial impact (new draperies, silk, paper, glass) weighed against agricultural impact (fen drainage by Vermuyden, market gardening), judged on a stated criterion such as the number of places affected, permanence, or whether England could have done it alone. Stimulus caps apply: answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 11 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"Comparing two kinds of impact needs a measure. I shall use three: how many people and places were affected, how permanent the change proved, and whether England could have made the change without migrants.\n\nOn breadth, industry wins easily. The Flemish and Walloon strangers brought the new draperies — bays, says and other lighter worsted cloths — at a time when the old heavy broadcloth export through Antwerp had slumped. These cloths revived several towns at once: Sandwich, licensed for twenty-five Flemish households in 1561; Norwich, which invited thirty Dutch and Walloon masters in 1565; Canterbury, Colchester and Maidstone. Within a generation the new draperies were a leading English export, so the change reached weavers, spinners, dyers and merchants across the east and south-east. Nothing in agriculture touched so many places.\n\nIndustry also gained entirely new trades. Huguenot refugees after 1685 concentrated in Spitalfields, outside the City guilds, and made England a producer of silk it had formerly imported, with more weavers at Canterbury and Norwich. John Spilman, a German, was licensed in 1588 to make white paper at Dartford; French and Italian glassmakers, including Jean Carré from 1567, brought fine glassmaking to London; printing had been in foreign hands since Caxton's day. On the third measure these were changes England plainly could not have made alone, since the techniques did not exist here.\n\nAgriculture, however, wins on permanence and on sheer physical scale in one region. Dutch expertise drained the fens: Cornelius Vermuyden worked at Hatfield Chase from 1626 and then on the Great Level for the Earl of Bedford from 1630, cutting the Old Bedford River in 1637 and the New Bedford River in 1651. Marsh became arable and pasture, and the map of eastern England was altered permanently. The best evidence of how big the change was is the resistance: fenmen broke the banks and sluices and attacked the works, because drainage destroyed the fishing, fowling and grazing that their living depended on. Migrants also spread Dutch and Flemish market gardening around Sandwich, Norwich and London, improving the vegetables sold into growing towns — a quieter change, but one that reached ordinary diets.\n\nA fair comparison has to admit that the two are not the same kind of change. Industrial impact was wide but shared: English workers learned the trades and eventually carried them on themselves. Agricultural impact was narrow but irreversible: no English worker could have undone the drainage, and nobody has.\n\nOn balance I agree with the statement, but on one measure only. Judged by the number of towns, workers and exports affected, and by the range of entirely new trades introduced, migrants changed industry far more. Judged by permanence, the drainage of the fens is the single largest physical change migrants made to England in these two centuries. The statement is therefore true of breadth and false of depth, and an answer that says simply 'industry' without saying by what measure has not judged anything.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["You cannot compare two impacts until you have said what counts as a big impact.","Gather the strongest example on each side and ask which one England could least easily have managed without migrants.","Let the conclusion award the verdict on one measure and take it away on another, rather than choosing a side and stopping."],"codes":["13-2 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in England","13-2 — 3 Case studies: The experience of Huguenots in seventeenth century England","13-2 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of English society"]},{"q":"'Migrants were accepted in England in the years c1500–c1700 only when they were useful to the economy.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• Sandwich\n• the Egyptians Act of 1530\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit the economic case (licensed settlements, conditions attached, Jewish merchants, Dutch engineers) weighed against religion as an independent factor and against the gap between official and popular acceptance, with a judgement that distinguishes government from neighbours. Stimulus caps apply: answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 11 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"Acceptance was not one thing: the Privy Council, the parish and the guildsman could each answer differently about the same migrant. I shall test the statement against the decisions of government, against the treatment of migrants' religion, and against popular attitudes.\n\nThe case for the statement is strongest where government decisions can be dated. Sandwich, its harbour silting and its cloth trade decayed, was licensed in 1561 to receive about twenty-five Flemish households, and the licence was conditional: the strangers were to teach their trades and were barred from selling retail to the English, so that they competed in production and not in the shops. Norwich invited thirty Dutch and Walloon masters in 1565 on similar terms. Dutch engineers were hired for the fen drainage from the 1620s because no English engineer could do the work. Even the readmission of Jewish people fits: when Menasseh ben Israel petitioned in 1655, the trade that Sephardi merchants carried on with Amsterdam was one reason Cromwell's council let them worship privately from 1656, the burial ground at Mile End following on a lease of 1657. The clearest proof is the reverse case. The Egyptians Act of 1530 ordered Romani people out of the realm and the Act of 1554 made staying a felony punishable by death, and in 1596 more than a hundred were condemned at York; they were judged to bring nothing, and the law was turned against their presence itself.\n\nBut usefulness does not explain everything. Religion decided who was even considered. England after the settlement of 1559 saw persecuted Protestants as fellow believers, and protecting them advertised the Protestant cause in Europe. That is why the strangers were given churches of their own from 1550, why the Walloons at Canterbury were allowed the crypt of the cathedral for worship in French, and why relief for the Huguenots was collected in English parish churches, including a collection authorised by James II in 1686. A purely economic state would have taken the skills and refused the churches.\n\nPopular acceptance followed neither rule. Skilled, useful migrants were rioted against precisely because they were useful: London apprentices attacked foreign craftsmen on Evil May Day in 1517, a threatening libel was pinned to the Dutch Church in 1593, and English weavers complained about Huguenot competition in the 1680s. In Sandwich itself, once strangers were roughly a third of the town, limits were placed on their looms and their trading and many families moved on to Canterbury. Usefulness bought a licence from the Crown; it bought resentment from the neighbours.\n\nMy judgement is that the statement is largely true of the state and largely false of society. For the government, economic usefulness was the usual test, and the fate of Romani people shows what happened to those who failed it. But shared Protestantism was an independent reason for acceptance, not merely a cover for economics, and among ordinary tradesmen the more useful a migrant was, the less welcome he was likely to be. Acceptance depended on who was doing the accepting.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Notice that 'accepted' can mean accepted by the Crown, by the parish or by the trade next door.","Find the group whose treatment best supports the statement and the group whose treatment best undermines it.","Build the answer as government, religion and popular feeling in turn, and let the conclusion say for whom the statement holds."],"codes":["13-2 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in England","13-2 — 3 Case studies: Sandwich and Canterbury in the sixteenth century","13-2 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of English society"]},{"q":"'The Huguenots were the migrant group that changed England most in the years c1500–c1700.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• silk weaving\n• the strangers of Sandwich and Canterbury\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit the Huguenot case (scale after 1685, silk, finance, skilled trades) weighed against the sixteenth-century strangers, Dutch engineers and merchants of the trading world, judged on a criterion such as numbers, breadth of the economy affected or lasting institutions. Stimulus caps apply: answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 11 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"Deciding which group changed England most requires a criterion. I shall judge by three: the number who came, the range of English life they altered, and how much of the change was still visible a century later.\n\nOn numbers the Huguenots have no rival in this period. Louis XIV revoked the Edict of Nantes in October 1685; around two hundred thousand Protestants left France and roughly fifty thousand reached England, perhaps half of them settling in London. No earlier movement approached that. Their trades were valuable ones: silk weaving in Spitalfields, just beyond the City guilds, made England a producer of a luxury cloth it had previously bought abroad, and there were weavers at Canterbury and Norwich too. They brought silversmithing, clock and gun making, and paper making; Huguenot investors were among those who launched the Bank of England in 1694, whose first governor, Sir John Houblon, came from a refugee family; Huguenot regiments fought for William III at the Boyne in 1690. The word 'refugee' entered English from French to describe them, which is itself a measure of the impression they made.\n\nYet the strangers of the sixteenth century have the stronger claim on the second criterion. The Flemish and Walloon refugees brought the new draperies at the moment when the old broadcloth export had collapsed, and those lighter cloths became England's leading cloth export. Where the Huguenots created one luxury industry, the strangers rebuilt the country's staple one. They did it in many places at once — Sandwich, licensed for twenty-five Flemish households in 1561, Norwich with its thirty invited masters in 1565, Canterbury, Colchester, Maidstone — and they brought market gardening with them. They also created the institutions the Huguenots later used: the stranger churches chartered in 1550 and the French-speaking Walloon congregation worshipping in the crypt at Canterbury were more than a century old when the Huguenots arrived, which is why the later refugees settled so smoothly.\n\nOther groups changed England in ways neither can match. Dutch engineers under Cornelius Vermuyden, working at Hatfield Chase from 1626 and on the Great Level from 1630, permanently altered the physical map of eastern England. Merchants and seamen of the trading world — the Muscovy, Levant, East India and Royal African Companies — brought Africans and Asians into London households and ports and tied England into a global economy whose consequences reached far beyond any one trade.\n\nOn my criteria the verdict is divided. By numbers arriving in a single generation, and by the lasting institutions of finance and luxury manufacture they helped to build, the Huguenots changed England most. By the breadth of the economy they altered, and because they created the churches, the towns and the very idea of licensed refuge that the Huguenots inherited, the sixteenth-century strangers did more. I would agree with the statement only if impact is measured by the size and visibility of a single wave; measured by the depth of the change to English trade and industry, the strangers of Sandwich, Canterbury and Norwich come first, and the Huguenots completed what they had begun.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["The question compares groups, so you must be able to put at least two others beside the Huguenots.","Ask which group England would have missed most if it had never arrived, and why.","Argue the Huguenot case fully, then the strangers' case, then decide on a measure you have already stated rather than on the size of the crowd."],"codes":["13-2 — 3 Case studies: The experience of Huguenots in seventeenth century England","13-2 — 3 Case studies: Sandwich and Canterbury in the sixteenth century","13-2 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in England"]},{"q":"'The growth of English trade was the main reason why the pattern of migration to England changed in the years c1500–c1700.'\nHow far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the trading companies\n• the strangers' churches\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit trade and empire as a cause of new kinds of migration (companies, privateering, the Royal African Company, London's growth) weighed against religious persecution abroad and English government policy as causes of the timing and scale, with a judgement that separates who came from how many came. Stimulus caps apply: answers that do not address three or more aspects of content are capped at 11 marks and have no access to Level 4.","sol":"The pattern of migration has two parts: who came, and how many came and when. Trade explains the first far better than the second, and that distinction decides the answer.\n\nTrade certainly changed who arrived. English merchants pushed far beyond the old North Sea routes: the Muscovy Company was founded in 1555, the Levant Company in 1592, the East India Company was chartered in 1600, the Hudson's Bay Company followed in 1670 and the Royal African Company in 1672. Along those routes came merchants, seamen and servants from places that had sent nobody to England before, and Africans appear in parish registers, court records and household accounts across the century — Jacques Francis, a diver working on the salvage of the Mary Rose in 1547, who gave evidence in an English court the following year; Mary Fillis, a Moroccan woman baptised in London in 1597; Reasonable Blackman, a silk weaver in Southwark in the 1590s. Privateering and war with Spain brought others, taken from captured Spanish and Portuguese ships. By the later seventeenth century the Royal African Company's trade in enslaved people meant that some Africans were brought to England against their will in the households of planters and merchants, a kind of movement that had not existed in 1500. Trade also built the destination: London grew from perhaps fifty thousand people in 1500 to roughly half a million by 1700, and that growth is what made a great migrant city possible.\n\nBut the largest movements of the period were not trade movements at all. They were flights from persecution, and their timing follows events abroad exactly. The Duke of Alba's arrival in the Netherlands in 1567 and the fall of Antwerp in 1585 sent Flemish and Walloon Protestants across the North Sea; the St Bartholomew's Day Massacre of 24 August 1572 sent Huguenots to Rye, Southampton and London; the revocation of the Edict of Nantes in October 1685 sent roughly fifty thousand Huguenots to England out of some two hundred thousand who left France. No trading company moved numbers like that.\n\nEnglish decisions shaped the pattern as much as English commerce. The stranger churches chartered in 1550 gave refugees somewhere to be, and they were reopened after the religious settlement of 1559; the Privy Council licensed Sandwich in 1561 and Norwich in 1565, deciding where the newcomers went; Cromwell's council permitted Jewish worship in 1656; Charles II offered denization in 1681 and a national collection relieved Huguenot refugees in 1686. Meanwhile the Egyptians Acts of 1530 and 1554 were aimed at keeping one group out altogether. Policy, not trade, decided which towns filled with strangers.\n\nMy judgement is that trade was the main reason for the change in the kinds of migrant England received, and it created the growing capital in which they could settle, but it was not the main reason the pattern changed overall. Religious persecution abroad set the numbers and the dates, and English policy set the destinations. Trade widened the map of migration; persecution filled it.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Split the word 'pattern' into parts before you decide anything — who, how many, when, and where they settled.","Match each cause to the part of the pattern it actually explains.","Give trade its strongest case first, then test it against the dates of the biggest arrivals, and let the conclusion divide the verdict between the parts of the pattern."],"codes":["13-2 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of English society","13-2 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","13-2 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in England"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In which year did Louis XIV revoke the Edict of Nantes?","a":"1685."},{"q":"Which Kent town was licensed in 1561 to receive twenty-five Flemish households and their trades?","a":"Sandwich."},{"q":"Which Dutch engineer led the draining of Hatfield Chase and the Great Level of the Fens?","a":"Cornelius Vermuyden."},{"q":"Under which ruler of England were Jewish people first allowed to worship privately in London again, from 1656?","a":"Oliver Cromwell, as Lord Protector."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The strangers","back":"The name used in Tudor England for Protestant refugees from the Low Countries and France — mainly Flemish, Walloon and French — who settled in London and the south-east from the 1550s onwards.","code":"13-2 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","open":false},{"front":"The Dutch Church at Austin Friars, 1550","back":"The Dutch-speaking congregation of the strangers' church chartered by Edward VI in 1550, with a French-speaking congregation alongside it. Closed under Mary I, reopened under Elizabeth I.","code":"13-2 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of English society","open":false},{"front":"Denization","back":"A grant by royal letters patent allowing an alien to hold land and trade more freely. It cost money, did not give full English rights, and full naturalisation needed an Act of Parliament.","code":"13-2 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in England","open":false},{"front":"The new draperies","back":"Lighter, cheaper worsted cloths such as bays and says, brought by Flemish and Walloon weavers. They revived Sandwich, Canterbury, Norwich and Colchester and became England's leading cloth export.","code":"13-2 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in England","open":false},{"front":"Evil May Day, 1517","back":"A riot in London on 1 May 1517 in which apprentices attacked the houses and workshops of foreign craftsmen and merchants. Hundreds were arrested and over a dozen hanged before Henry VIII pardoned the rest.","code":"13-2 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in England","open":false},{"front":"The Egyptians Acts of 1530 and 1554","back":"The 1530 Act ordered Romani people out of England within sixteen days and banned further entry; the 1554 Act made remaining for more than a month a felony punishable by death.","code":"13-2 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in England","open":false},{"front":"The Sandwich licence, 1561","back":"Permission for the decayed port of Sandwich to receive twenty-five Flemish households, on condition that they taught their trades and did not sell retail to the English. Strangers soon formed roughly a third of the town.","code":"13-2 — 3 Case studies: Sandwich and Canterbury in the sixteenth century","open":false},{"front":"The Walloons of Canterbury","back":"French-speaking Protestant refugees from the southern Netherlands who worshipped in the crypt of Canterbury Cathedral and made silk and fine-cloth weaving the city's leading industry.","code":"13-2 — 3 Case studies: Sandwich and Canterbury in the sixteenth century","open":false},{"front":"The revocation of the Edict of Nantes, 1685","back":"Louis XIV cancelled the toleration Henry IV had granted French Protestants in 1598. About two hundred thousand Huguenots left France and roughly fifty thousand reached England.","code":"13-2 — 3 Case studies: The experience of Huguenots in seventeenth century England","open":false},{"front":"Spitalfields","back":"The district just outside the City of London where Huguenot silk weavers settled after 1685, beyond the reach of the City guilds, making England a producer of silk it had previously imported.","code":"13-2 — 3 Case studies: The experience of Huguenots in seventeenth century England","open":false},{"front":"Cornelius Vermuyden","back":"Dutch engineer who drained Hatfield Chase from 1626 and the Great Level of the Fens from 1630, cutting the Old Bedford River in 1637 and the New Bedford River in 1651. Fenmen broke his banks in protest.","code":"13-2 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in England","open":false},{"front":"The return of Jewish people, 1656","back":"After Menasseh ben Israel's petition and the Whitehall Conference of 1655, Cromwell's council allowed private worship in 1656; a burial ground at Mile End was leased in 1657. No law was passed and the expulsion of 1290 stood.","code":"13-2 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","open":false},{"front":"John Blanke","back":"An African trumpeter at the courts of Henry VII and Henry VIII, shown twice in the Westminster Tournament Roll of 1511. In 1512 he petitioned Henry VIII successfully for his wages to be doubled.","code":"13-2 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","open":false},{"front":"The Royal African Company, 1672","back":"Chartered under Charles II with the Duke of York as its governor, holding a monopoly of English trade with West Africa, including trade in enslaved people. Its monopoly ended in 1698.","code":"13-2 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of English society","open":false},{"front":"Jacques Francis","back":"An African diver employed by the Venetian salvor Piero Paolo Corsi on the salvage of the Mary Rose in 1547. He gave evidence in the High Court of Admiralty in 1548, one of the few African voices in Tudor records.","code":"13-2 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 1 Question 5, 16 marks: 'The migrants who came to England in the sixteenth century changed the country more than those who came in the seventeenth century.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the strangers of Colchester • the Royal African Company. You must also use information of your own.)","items":["Point: the sixteenth-century arrivals changed more of England, because they remade whole towns and a whole industry at once, while the seventeenth-century arrivals changed particular places and particular trades very deeply and left the rest of the country much as they found it.","Evidence: the Privy Council licensed twenty-five Flemish households into Sandwich in 1561; Norwich admitted thirty master textile workers and their households by letters patent in November 1565 and counted 3,993 strangers in October 1571; eleven Dutch families reached Colchester in 1565, and the bays and says of the community they founded were later sealed in its Dutch Bay Hall; the western crypt of Canterbury Cathedral was granted to the Walloon congregation in 1575. Against those stand the seventeenth-century arrivals: the Dutch engineer Cornelius Vermuyden drained Hatfield Chase from 1626 and the Great Level from 1630, the Old Bedford River being cut in 1637 and the New Bedford River in 1651; Jewish people were allowed to remain from 1656 and had a synagogue in Creechurch Lane and a burial ground at Mile End in 1657; and after Louis XIV revoked the Edict of Nantes in October 1685 roughly fifty thousand Huguenots reached England. Of all that, only Colchester is prompted by the question, with the Royal African Company in the counter-view below; the licence for twenty-five households at Sandwich in 1561, Norwich in November 1565 and October 1571, the Canterbury crypt of 1575, Vermuyden in the fens and the Jewish settlement of 1656 and 1657 are the content of your own that takes the answer past the 11-mark cap.","Explanation: the sixteenth-century change was wide as well as deep — four provincial towns within fifteen years, a new kind of cloth made and sold across all of them, and a legal form for foreign congregations that every later settlement used — whereas the seventeenth-century change, for all its scale, sat in particular places: the drained fens, one silk-weaving district of London, one small congregation in the City.","Counter-view: the seventeenth century made the changes that were hardest to undo — the drained fen is farmland still; the permission of 1656 ended an exclusion that had stood since 1290; and the Royal African Company, chartered in 1672, tied England into an Atlantic trade that brought Africans into the country against their will, which no sixteenth-century settlement matches.","Mini-judgement: the sixteenth century changed more of England and the seventeenth changed England more lastingly, so the statement holds on breadth and fails on permanence — and saying which of the two the question is about is what decides it, rather than splitting the difference."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Point: the migrants of the 1500s changed England more than the ones who came in the 1600s, so I agree with the statement.","Evidence: in the sixteenth century a lot of Dutch and Flemish people came to towns like Norwich and Sandwich and made new kinds of cloth, which was good for those towns. In the seventeenth century the Huguenots came and made silk, some Dutchmen drained the fens, and Jewish people were allowed back.","Explanation: the sixteenth-century migrants must have changed England more because more towns were involved and the cloth industry was very important, whereas the seventeenth-century ones were mostly in London.","What this meets: the descriptor for Level 2 — 'An explanation is given showing limited analysis and with implicit or unsustained links to the conceptual focus of the question' and 'Accurate and relevant information is included, showing some knowledge and understanding of the period'.","What it misses: there is no precise evidence at all — no licence for twenty-five households at Sandwich in 1561, no letters patent for Norwich in November 1565, no 3,993 strangers counted in 1571, no Vermuyden and no date for the draining of the fens, no figure for the Huguenots after 1685 — so it cannot reach 'accurate and relevant information is precisely selected'; the two centuries are described one after the other and never weighed against each other, so there is no line of reasoning; and the judgement is asserted in the first line rather than justified by criteria, which is exactly the Level 2 wording 'the overall judgement is given but its justification is asserted or insecure'.","Also note: neither printed stimulus point is used — Colchester is never named and the Royal African Company is never mentioned — and nothing here reaches past a general account of the two centuries, so the answer does not address three or more aspects of content. That no longer costs marks at this level — the Level 2 cap was withdrawn, and an answer like this can still reach the top of Level 2 — but however much the writing improved it would be held to a maximum of 11 marks and shut out of Level 4."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — The context for migration to England, c1500–c1700","items":["The Elizabethan religious settlement of 1559 (Act of Supremacy and Act of Uniformity) made England permanently Protestant and so a destination for persecuted Protestants.","The strangers' church was chartered by Edward VI in 1550: a Dutch-speaking congregation at Austin Friars in London and a French-speaking congregation, closed under Mary I and reopened under Elizabeth I.","The Duke of Alba arrived as governor of the Spanish Netherlands in 1567 and his tribunal condemned Protestants and rebels, driving Flemish and Walloon refugees to England.","The St Bartholomew's Day Massacre began on 24 August 1572 and killed thousands of French Protestants in Paris and the provinces.","Antwerp fell to Spanish forces in 1585, sending a further wave of Protestant merchants and craftsmen across the North Sea.","English trading companies multiplied: the Muscovy Company in 1555, the Levant Company in 1592, the East India Company chartered in 1600, the Hudson's Bay Company in 1670 and the Royal African Company in 1672.","Privateering and war with Spain brought Africans to England, some taken from captured Spanish and Portuguese ships; John Hawkins had made England's first slaving voyages in the 1560s.","London grew from perhaps fifty thousand people in 1500 to roughly half a million by 1700, becoming by far the largest place of migrant settlement.","Romani people, called 'Egyptians', were recorded in Britain from the early sixteenth century and were legislated against rather than invited.","Jewish people, expelled by Edward I in 1290, returned from the 1650s, the first community being Sephardi merchants of Spanish and Portuguese descent arriving by way of Amsterdam."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — The experience and impact of migrants, c1500–c1700","items":["Aliens paid double the subsidy and higher customs duties, were excluded from most guilds, were forbidden to sell retail to English customers and were limited in the number of servants they could employ.","Denization was granted by royal letters patent and allowed an alien to hold land and trade more freely; full naturalisation required an Act of Parliament.","Evil May Day, 1 May 1517: London apprentices attacked foreign craftsmen and merchants; hundreds were arrested and more than a dozen hanged before Henry VIII pardoned the rest.","In 1593 a threatening libel was pinned to the wall of the Dutch Church in London and Parliament debated a bill against alien retailers.","The Egyptians Act of 1530 ordered Romani people to leave within sixteen days; the Act of 1554 made remaining beyond a month a felony punishable by death; in 1596 more than a hundred Romani people were condemned at York and nine were hanged.","In 1596 and again in 1601 Elizabeth I's government licensed the merchant Caspar van Senden to transport 'blackamoores' out of the realm, though there is little evidence it was carried out.","Africans lived and worked in Tudor England: John Blanke, trumpeter to Henry VII and Henry VIII, appears in the Westminster Tournament Roll of 1511; Jacques Francis dived on the wreck of the Mary Rose in 1547 and gave evidence in an English court in 1548; Mary Fillis, from Morocco, was baptised in London in 1597; Reasonable Blackman wove silk in Southwark in the 1590s.","Industry: the new draperies (bays and says) from Flemish and Walloon weavers; Huguenot silk in Spitalfields and Canterbury after 1685; John Spilman licensed to make white paper at Dartford in 1588; Jean Carré's glasshouse in London from 1567.","Agriculture: Dutch and Flemish market gardening around Sandwich, Norwich and London, and the draining of the fens by Cornelius Vermuyden — Hatfield Chase from 1626, the Great Level from 1630, the Old Bedford River cut in 1637 and the New Bedford River in 1651 — which fenmen resisted by breaking banks and sluices.","Trade, finance and culture: refugee merchants linked London to Amsterdam and Hamburg; the Bank of England, founded in 1694, had as its first governor Sir John Houblon, from a family of Protestant refugees; court painters came from abroad — Holbein under Henry VIII, Van Dyck under Charles I, Lely under Charles II; the word 'refugee' entered English from the French réfugié."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Case study: Sandwich and Canterbury in the sixteenth century","items":["Sandwich was a decayed Kent port with a silting harbour and a declining cloth trade when it sought settlers.","In 1561 it was licensed to receive twenty-five Flemish households — a few hundred people — on condition that they taught their trades.","The strangers were barred from selling retail to English customers, so that they competed in production rather than in the shops.","They brought the new draperies (bays and says), fishing skills and market gardening, including vegetables grown for sale in the town and beyond.","Within little more than a decade strangers made up roughly a third of Sandwich's population.","Friction followed: limits were placed on their looms and their trading, English craftsmen complained, and many families moved on to Canterbury.","Canterbury received Walloon refugees — French-speaking Protestants from the southern Netherlands — from the 1570s.","The Walloons were allowed to worship in the crypt of Canterbury Cathedral; a French-language congregation has met there ever since.","Silk and fine-cloth weaving became Canterbury's leading industry as a result.","When Huguenots arrived after 1685 they joined a French-speaking Protestant community in Canterbury that was already more than a century old."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Case study: the experience of Huguenots in seventeenth-century England","items":["Huguenots were French Protestants; the Edict of Nantes, issued by Henry IV in 1598, had given them legal toleration.","Before 1685 pressure included quartering soldiers on Protestant households to force conversions.","Louis XIV revoked the Edict of Nantes in October 1685; Protestant worship was banned and emigration was forbidden.","Around two hundred thousand Huguenots left France; roughly fifty thousand came to England, the largest single group of migrants of the period.","Perhaps half settled in London: silk weavers in Spitalfields, just outside the City and its guilds, and others in Soho; there were communities in Canterbury, Norwich, Plymouth and Bristol.","Charles II had promised denization to Huguenot refugees in a declaration of 1681.","National collections were made in English churches for Huguenot relief, including one authorised by James II in 1686.","French-speaking congregations already existed, such as the church in Threadneedle Street in London, dating from the reign of Edward VI.","Their skills included silk weaving, silversmithing, clock and gun making, paper making and finance; Sir John Houblon, first governor of the Bank of England in 1694, came from a refugee family.","Huguenot regiments fought for William III in Ireland at the Battle of the Boyne in 1690, and English weavers meanwhile complained of Huguenot competition in the London trade."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 1 Question 5 (16 marks + 4 SPaG): 'England gained more from its migrants than its migrants gained from England in the years c1500–c1700.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • market gardening • letters of denization. You must also use information of your own.","items":["Decode the question: it sets two sets of gains against each other — what England got out of its migrants, and what they got out of England — across two centuries. The concept is consequence, so every paragraph must put a gain on one side against a gain on the other, and the answer must compare the two balances rather than list groups.","Set the criterion in the first three or four lines: a gain counts for more if it lasted, if it reached many people, and if the side that received it could not have had it any other way. Say plainly that England's gains in work, land and trade will be tested against what migrants gained in safety, worship and a living.","Paragraph 1 — what England gained, the case for the statement. The new draperies arrived with the strangers the Privy Council licensed into Sandwich in 1561, the thirty master textile workers and their households Norwich admitted by letters patent in November 1565, and the eleven Dutch families who reached Colchester in the same year, whose bays and says were later sealed in that town's Dutch Bay Hall. Market gardening is one of the two printed stimulus points, so it is prompted rather than your own: the strangers at Sandwich grew vegetables, carrots and celery among them, on the land east of the town. Then the land itself: Cornelius Vermuyden drained Hatfield Chase from 1626 and the Great Level from 1630, the Old Bedford River being cut in 1637 and the New Bedford River in 1651. Interim judgement: England gained trades, crops and farmland it had not had before, and all three outlasted the generation that brought them.","Paragraph 2 — what the migrants gained. Safety and lawful worship first: the charter of 24 July 1550 gave the strangers a congregation of their own under their own superintendent, Jan Laski, at Austin Friars; the western crypt of Canterbury Cathedral was granted to the Walloon congregation in 1575; Jewish people were allowed to remain from 1656, after the Whitehall Conference of December 1655 broke up without a decision but with the lawyers finding no law that barred their return, and they had a synagogue in Creechurch Lane and a burial ground at Mile End in 1657; and after the revocation of the Edict of Nantes in October 1685 roughly fifty thousand Huguenots found a refuge here. Interim judgement: for people fleeing arrest or worse this was the larger gain of the two, and England could have refused it.","Paragraph 3 — what that gain was worth, and where your own knowledge shows. Letters of denization is the other printed stimulus point, so denization is prompted: the Crown granted it by letters patent, and it still left an alien short of the full rights of an English-born subject, which took an Act of Parliament. The content that is genuinely yours is what the prompts do not name — aliens, denizens among them, were charged at double the English rate in the lay subsidy from 1514 onwards; the Sandwich strangers were to teach their trades and were barred from selling retail to the English, and the town later limited their looms and their trading; Evil May Day in 1517 showed what a London crowd could do; and the Egyptians Acts of 1530 and 1554 set the law against Romani people rather than protecting them. Those are the aspects that take the answer past the cap. Interim judgement: what the migrants gained came with a condition and a price, while what England gained came with neither.","Paragraph 4 — the people for whom the balance does not work at all. England gained the court painters, Hans Holbein of Augsburg, who settled in London in 1532 and died there in 1543, and Anthony van Dyck of Antwerp, made principal painter to Charles I in 1632; and it gained from a trade it entered at other people's expense, the Royal African Company being chartered in 1672. Africans in England — John Blanke, the trumpeter recorded at the Tudor court in 1511, and Jacques Francis, who dived on the wreck of the Mary Rose in 1547 — are the test of the statement, because by the end of the period many Africans were in England through no choice of their own.","The evaluation thread that runs through every paragraph: keep asking which side could have done without the other — England without these trades and this land, or these migrants without this refuge — rather than which list of gains sounds longer.","Conclusion: agree that England's gains were the wider and the longer-lasting, since the new draperies, the market gardens and the drained fen outlived the communities that brought them; but state that for refugees the gain was their safety, which is not the smaller thing, and that for Africans brought here through the Atlantic trade the statement is true only in the worst sense. Name the criterion used so the judgement is justified and not asserted.","Timing: about 25 minutes, the share Question 5 or 6 takes of the 45 minutes Section B needs — three to four minutes planning the criterion and the four paragraphs, about twenty minutes writing, and a last minute checking spelling, punctuation and the specialist terms (strangers, denization, new draperies, Huguenot, refugee) on which the four extra marks depend."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:20:40Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"All six recorded fixes are in the kit and hold: lesson.examples[3] and [4] now open in Question 6’s printed form with two stimulus points each (the Privy Council and Canterbury; the returns of strangers of 1593 and Norwich) and the SPaG line, Rievaulx reads ‘twelve Cistercian monks sent from Clairvaux … on land given by Walter Espec’, the 1582 trades list is gone in favour of the carrots and celery the strangers grew east of Sandwich, the Level 4 / Level 2 pair sits on the sixteenth century against the seventeenth and the plan on what England and its migrants gained from each other — none of the six statements, and no bank stem, matches the Option 13 SAMs or the June 2022, 2023 or 2024 papers, all of whose Section B questions I read — and room.facts[4] and extras[6].items[8] now reconcile at 30 + 45 + 5 within the 80 minutes; the new facts check out (the Royal African Company chartered by Charles II in 1672, denization by letters patent leaving an alien short of the English-born rights that needed an Act of Parliament, aliens and denizens charged at double the English rate in the subsidy from 1514, market gardening east of Sandwich), dupscan prints ‘no duplication’, and re-solving all fifteen questions¹ — unaltered in this pass — again turned up no wrong answer key, so only the unprinted stimulus pair on the model paragraphs² stands between this and publication."}}},"13-3":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"13-3","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This is the key topic where the whole Migrants in Britain theme turns. Between c1700 and c1900 Britain became the first industrial nation, the largest empire in the world and, for most of the nineteenth century, a country with no law at all controlling who could land — so more people came, from further away, for more different reasons, than in any earlier period of the theme. Section B of Paper 1 will ask you to compare this period with another one, to explain why something in it changed, and to judge a claim about it across a century or more. Every one of those answers is won or lost on precise evidence: the Famine years, the 1851 census, the pogroms of 1881, Liverpool's cellars, the East End workshops. Fix the dates, names and figures here and you are armed for all three answers.","idea":[{"h":"Who came, and where they settled: Ireland, Europe and the Empire","code":"13-3 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","t":"Irish migration is the biggest story by numbers. The Irish had crossed for generations as seasonal harvesters and as navvies on the canals and railways, but the Famine of 1845–52, caused by potato blight from 1845, killed about a million people and drove about a million more out of Ireland. Some 300,000 landed at Liverpool in 1847 alone, and by the 1851 census about 727,000 Irish-born people were living in Britain: more than a fifth of Liverpool's population of roughly 376,000, and close to a fifth of Glasgow's. Europeans came too — Germans as merchants in Bradford and Manchester, as sugar bakers in the East End and as pork butchers in northern towns; Italians as organ grinders, craftsmen and ice-cream sellers in Clerkenwell's Little Italy and later in Ancoats and Glasgow; French Catholic clergy and aristocrats fleeing the Revolution after 1789 into Somers Town in St Pancras. From the Empire came lascars — sailors from South Asia and the Indian Ocean hired on British ships — ayahs, servants, students and seamen from China who settled around Liverpool's Pitt Street and London's Limehouse. From 1881 came the largest single new community, Jewish families from the Russian Empire, into Whitechapel, Spitalfields and Stepney. A top-level answer never lists these groups: it sorts them by what moved them — famine, persecution, contract, opportunity — and shows that the mix of reasons changed across the period."},{"h":"What made Britain a magnet: industry, cities, slavery, Empire and liberty","code":"13-3 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of British society","t":"Four features of British society did the pulling. The Industrial Revolution created work that needed muscle rather than training: cotton mills, docks, foundries and above all the railway boom of the 1840s. Urbanisation concentrated that work in a few ports and manufacturing towns, so migrants went where the jobs and the cheap rooms were, and whole districts became migrant districts. Transatlantic slavery brought Africans to Britain against their will as the servants and property of planters and captains, and the campaign to end it — the Slave Trade Act of 1807 and the Slavery Abolition Act of 1833 — changed what a Black Briton could be. The Empire moved people as crew, as servants and later as students and professionals. Behind all of it lay civil liberties: Lord Mansfield's judgment in the Somerset case of 1772 meant a person could not be seized in England and shipped abroad into slavery, Catholic Emancipation came in 1829 and the Jews Relief Act in 1858, and the wartime Aliens Act of 1793 had long lapsed, so exiles such as Giuseppe Mazzini, in London from 1837, and Karl Marx, in London from 1849 until his death in 1883, could simply arrive. A top-level answer treats these as interlocking pulls, and can say which of them mattered most to which group."},{"h":"The experience: authorities, neighbours and the power of the press","code":"13-3 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in Britain","t":"Migrants met three different Britains. The authorities were mostly absent: there was no immigration control for most of the century, which is why Britain filled with political exiles, but local authorities intervened sharply when migrants were blamed for disease, as in Liverpool, which appointed Dr William Henry Duncan as Britain's first Medical Officer of Health in 1847. Neighbours could be violent: anti-Catholic feeling exploded after the Catholic hierarchy was restored in 1850 and the Ecclesiastical Titles Act followed in 1851, and Irish homes and chapels were attacked in the Stockport riots of 1852; William Murphy's anti-Catholic lecture tours provoked riots in the later 1860s. Then there was the press. Punch, founded in 1841, drew Irish figures with ape-like features, and did so most savagely after the Fenian attacks of 1867, when three men were hanged in Manchester in November and a bomb at Clerkenwell prison in December killed twelve people. Later the cheap national press ran 'alien invasion' stories about East End Jews, and the British Brothers' League, founded in 1901, and a Royal Commission reporting in 1903 led to the Aliens Act of 1905. A top-level answer separates what the law did from what neighbours did from what newspapers did, and shows how the three fed one another."},{"h":"The difference migrants made: trade, culture, politics and the streets","code":"13-3 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in Britain","t":"Economically, Irish labour dug the canals and laid the railways and filled Lancashire's mills; Jewish migrants rebuilt the East End clothing trade around sub-divided tasks and the sewing machine, and Michael Marks, who arrived from Russian Poland, opened a penny bazaar in Leeds market in 1884 and took Thomas Spencer as partner in 1894. German merchants built the stone warehouses of Bradford's Little Germany in the 1860s and 1870s, and the German-born engineer William Siemens built a telegraph and steel business in Britain. Culturally, Charles Hallé came to Manchester in 1848 and founded the Hallé Orchestra there in 1858; Italians gave Britain the ice-cream trade and cafés; the Christmas tree was popularised after the Illustrated London News pictured the royal family's tree in 1848; Olaudah Equiano's Interesting Narrative of 1789 shaped the way Britons argued about slavery. Politically, the Irish-born Feargus O'Connor led Chartism and founded the Northern Star in 1837, William Cuffay, the son of a formerly enslaved man, led London Chartists until his conviction in 1848, T. P. O'Connor won Liverpool Scotland in 1885, and Dadabhai Naoroji became the first Indian MP in 1892. The streets changed too: Catholic churches, synagogues, Italian churches, markets and whole quarters. A top-level answer ranks these impacts against a stated criterion instead of listing them."},{"h":"Case study: Liverpool, the port that made and broke migrants","code":"13-3 — 3 Case studies: Liverpool in the nineteenth century","t":"Liverpool is the clearest single place to see the theme working. It grew rich first on transatlantic slavery — by the later eighteenth century it was the leading British slaving port — and then on the Atlantic trade, becoming the great European port of departure for North America, so migrants arrived there whether or not they meant to stay. In 1847 some 300,000 Irish people landed, a number close to the town's own population, and typhus swept the courts and cellars of the north end around Vauxhall and Scotland Road; Dr Duncan, appointed in 1847, blamed the cellar dwellings, and the corporation began closing them. By 1851 more than a fifth of the borough's roughly 376,000 people were Irish-born. Liverpool also held a large Welsh community whose builders put up much of its terraced housing, and from the 1860s a Chinese community around Pitt Street built on Alfred Holt's Blue Funnel Line, founded in 1866. Politics took a sectarian shape found nowhere else: Orange marches on one side, and on the other T. P. O'Connor, elected for Liverpool Scotland in 1885, the only Irish Nationalist ever to hold a British seat, which he kept until 1929. A top-level answer uses Liverpool as evidence, not as a story: it says what the port proves about the question asked."},{"h":"Case study: Jewish migrants in the East End of London","code":"13-3 — 3 Case studies: The experience of Jewish migrants in the East End of London","t":"Jews had lived in Britain since readmission under Cromwell in the 1650s; Bevis Marks synagogue opened in the City in 1701, and the Jews Relief Act of 1858 let Lionel de Rothschild, elected in 1847, finally take his Commons seat. That settled, prosperous community was transformed after March 1881, when the assassination of Tsar Alexander II was followed by pogroms across the Russian Empire and the May Laws of 1882 restricted where Jews could live: well over a hundred thousand Yiddish-speaking migrants had reached Britain by 1914, most of them landing in the East End. They worked the sweated trades — tailoring, boot- and shoe-making, cabinet-making — in small workshops that a newcomer could enter within days, and the House of Lords Select Committee on the Sweating System investigated the conditions from 1888. They built their own institutions: the Jewish Board of Guardians, founded in 1859, the Poor Jews' Temporary Shelter, opened in 1885, and the Federation of Synagogues, founded in 1887 by Samuel Montagu, MP for Whitechapel from 1885. Hostility followed: press speculation during the Whitechapel murders of 1888, the British Brothers' League of 1901 and the Aliens Act of 1905. One building tells the whole story — a Huguenot chapel of 1743 on Brick Lane became a synagogue in 1898 and a mosque in 1976. A top-level answer weighs the community's own strength against the hostility it met."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 3, 4 marks: turning two descriptions into one analysed similarity","setup":"Paper 1 Question 3, worth 4 marks: 'Explain one way in which the work done by migrants in England in the years c800–c1500 was similar to the work done by migrants in Britain in the years c1700–c1900.' The mark scheme awards 2 marks for AO2 and 2 for AO1, so you need one similarity analysed across both named periods, with specific information from each.","steps":["Pick a similarity of economic role, not of appearance: in both periods migrants supplied a craft skill or a trading connection the English economy did not have, and the cloth trade is where that shows most clearly.","Evidence the earlier period by name and date: in 1331 Edward III granted letters of protection to John Kempe of Flanders, a weaver of woollen cloths, so that he and his men could work and teach their trade in England, and the Flemish weavers who followed helped turn a country that sold raw wool abroad into one that wove it.","Evidence the later period the same way: German merchants settled in Bradford from the 1830s — Jacob Behrens moved his business there from Leeds in 1838 — and the warehouses they built between 1855 and 1890 in the district still called Little Germany sold Bradford worsted into European markets.","Close with the sentence that makes it one similarity rather than two stories: in each period migrants were wanted for a textile skill or a market the host economy lacked, so the work they did was the reason they were let in and protected."],"cues":["Which kind of similarity will carry four marks — one of economic role or one of appearance?","What named, dated detail proves the earlier half?","What named, dated detail proves the later half?","What has the answer still not done that the examiner is looking for?"]},{"title":"Question 4, 12 marks: getting past the stimulus cap","setup":"Paper 1 Question 4, worth 12 marks: 'Explain why German migrants settled in British towns and cities in growing numbers in the years c1800–c1900. You may use the following in your answer: the Bradford worsted trade; the revolutions of 1848. You must also use information of your own.' The mark scheme caps an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content at 8 marks, and shuts Level 4 to it altogether.","steps":["Read the two prompts as headings you have already been given, and write one paragraph on each: the worsted trade as the commercial pull, the defeat of the 1848 risings as the political push.","Make each of those paragraphs a chain rather than a description — Bradford's exporters needed merchants who already had European contacts and credit, so men such as Jacob Behrens, who moved his firm from Leeds to Bradford in 1838, built the warehouse district later called Little Germany between 1855 and 1890; and when the risings of 1848 failed, Britain's lack of any power to deport made it the safe address, which is why Karl Marx settled in London in 1849.","Now find the reason the examiner has not given you, which must be a pull of a different kind: whole trades were recruited from particular German regions, so the sugar refineries around Whitechapel hired workers from the Hamburg area and pork butchers came from Württemberg to sell cheap cooked meat to industrial towns.","Prove that third reason with evidence the stimulus could not have prompted, such as the German Lutheran church of 1762–63 in Alie Street, Whitechapel, founded by a sugar refiner and still filled by refinery families in the nineteenth century, and the roughly 28,600 German-born people counted in England and Wales at the 1861 census.","Finish with a sentence that ranks the three: trade brought the merchants, politics brought the refugees, and the steady recruitment of workers into named trades kept the numbers rising in the years between the crises."],"cues":["What are the two prompts actually for, and what do they guarantee you?","What turns a paragraph about the worsted trade into an explanation rather than a description?","Where must the third reason come from if the cap is to be escaped?","What kind of evidence shows an examiner you have gone beyond the prompts?","What does the last sentence have to do that the three paragraphs have not?"]},{"title":"Question 5, 16 marks: writing a paragraph that weighs instead of describes","setup":"Paper 1 Question 5, worth 16 marks plus up to 4 for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology: 'Migrants changed the politics of Britain more than they changed its towns and cities in the years c1700–c1900.' How far do you agree? You may use: Chartist leaders; Little Germany in Bradford. You must also use information of your own. You are writing the paragraph that puts the case against the statement.","steps":["Open the paragraph with the rival change stated as a claim, not a topic: migrants altered the fabric of British towns more deeply than their politics, because they added streets, warehouses and places of worship that outlasted any election.","Support it with named, dated evidence: German merchants built the warehouses of Bradford's Little Germany between 1855 and 1890; St Peter's Italian church opened in 1863 in the Clerkenwell district known as Little Italy; and the Huguenot chapel of 1743 on Brick Lane became the Machzike Hadath synagogue in 1898 as the Jewish East End filled.","Explain the link back to the question: these were working districts and congregations, not decorations, so the map of a city recorded migration permanently, while a migrant politician held his seat only as long as the voters allowed.","Concede what the statement gets right, so the weighing is real: Feargus O'Connor, born in County Cork, led the Chartists and founded the Northern Star in 1837, and Dadabhai Naoroji won Finsbury Central in 1892 by five votes to become the first Indian MP.","Close with the interim judgement that this paragraph has earned: migrants reached national politics late and in very small numbers, whereas they had been rebuilding the districts they lived in for a century and a half, so on the test of how much was changed and for how long, the towns come first."],"cues":["How should a weighing paragraph begin if it is not to read as a new topic?","What three pieces of named evidence would an examiner reward here?","What sentence turns that evidence into an argument about the question?","Why must a paragraph arguing against the statement still admit something?","What does an interim judgement have to be based on to count?"]},{"title":"Question 5, 16 marks: building a conclusion on a stated criterion","setup":"The same 16-mark question type: 'The Irish Famine was the most important turning point in migration to Britain in the years c1700–c1900.' How far do you agree? You have already written paragraphs on the Famine years, on the flight of Jewish families from the Russian Empire after 1881, and on the slower changes worked by Empire and by steam shipping. You must now write the conclusion, and the top level requires criteria for the judgement to be justified and applied, not merely stated.","steps":["Name the criterion before you judge: a turning point will be taken to mean a change that altered the scale, the origins and the permanence of migration, not one that merely raised the numbers for a few years.","Apply it to the strongest case for the statement: on that test the Famine qualifies, because the failure of the potato crop from 1845 turned a seasonal movement into permanent settlement, and the 1851 census counted about 727,000 Irish-born people living in Britain.","Apply it against the statement, so the criterion is tested and not just asserted: the migration that followed the assassination of Tsar Alexander II in 1881 and the May Laws of 1882 changed where migrants came from rather than how many came, and it was that migration, not the Famine, that produced the first general control on entry in 1905.","Resolve it into one qualified position: the Famine was the greater turning point in scale, the pogroms the sharper turning point in the response of the British state, and Empire and steam the slow change that made both movements possible.","End on the reach of the argument rather than a restatement, saying which parts of the period the judgement covers and which it does not."],"cues":["What must come before the judgement itself if it is to reach the top level?","How does the criterion make the case for the statement, in one line?","Which evidence tests the criterion rather than confirming it?","How can both sides be true at once without the answer sitting on the fence?","What should the final sentence add instead of repeating the verdict?"]},{"title":"Choosing evidence an examiner can reward: Liverpool in 1847","setup":"You are answering a 12-mark question on why Irish migrants were blamed for disease in British cities in the mid-nineteenth century. Your first draft sentence reads: 'Lots of Irish people came to Liverpool and it was very overcrowded and dirty, so people blamed them for illness.' Rewrite it to the standard the mark scheme calls precisely selected information.","steps":["Replace 'lots' with a counted arrival: some 300,000 Irish people landed at Liverpool in 1847, a number close to the town's own population.","Replace 'overcrowded and dirty' with the named housing the contemporaries argued about: the courts and cellar dwellings of the north end around Vauxhall and Scotland Road.","Replace 'people blamed them' with the authority that did the blaming and the post it held: Dr William Henry Duncan, appointed Britain's first Medical Officer of Health in Liverpool that year, identified the cellars as the seat of the typhus.","Add the analytical clause the mark scheme actually rewards, linking the evidence to the question: because the fever spread fastest in the streets where the poorest newcomers lodged, the disease and the migrants were assumed to be the same problem."],"cues":["Which word in the draft is doing the least work, and what replaces it?","What were the contemporaries actually arguing about when they said 'dirty'?","Who, precisely, did the blaming, and what made his opinion carry weight?","What is still missing once every vague word has been replaced?"]}],"check":[{"q":"In which years did the Great Famine in Ireland take place, and roughly how many Irish-born people did the 1851 census find in Britain?","a":"1845–52; about 727,000 Irish-born people in Britain in 1851."},{"q":"What happened in the Russian Empire in 1881 and 1882 that pushed Jewish families towards Britain?","a":"Tsar Alexander II was assassinated in March 1881 and pogroms followed; the May Laws of 1882 then restricted where Jews could live and work."},{"q":"Name the post Dr William Henry Duncan took up in Liverpool in 1847 and why it mattered for migrants.","a":"Britain's first Medical Officer of Health; he blamed the cellar dwellings where Irish newcomers lodged for the typhus, tying migrants to disease in official minds."},{"q":"Give one political impact and one cultural impact of migrants in nineteenth-century Britain.","a":"Political: Feargus O'Connor led Chartism, or T. P. O'Connor won Liverpool Scotland in 1885, or Dadabhai Naoroji became the first Indian MP in 1892. Cultural: Charles Hallé founded the Hallé Orchestra in Manchester in 1858, or Italians created the ice-cream and café trade."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 1 Section B, the thematic study, carries 36 of the paper's 52 marks: Question 3 is worth 4, Question 4 is worth 12, and Question 5 or Question 6 is worth 16 plus up to 4 additional marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology.","Question 3, 'Explain one way in which … was similar to / different from …', is marked AO2 2 and AO1 2, normally spans at least a century, and needs one analysed similarity or difference supported from both named periods.","Question 4, 'Explain why …', is marked AO2 6 and AO1 6. The stimulus caps are a maximum of 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","Question 5 or 6, the 16-mark judgement, is marked AO2 10 and AO1 6. Its stimulus caps are a maximum of 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","Timing on the 80-minute paper is roughly a minute a mark: about 5 minutes on Question 3, 15 on Question 4 and 25 on Question 5 or 6, which is the only Section B answer where spelling and specialist terminology are separately marked as Threshold 1, Intermediate 2–3 and High 4."],"questions":[{"q":"Explain one way in which the reasons for migration to England in the years c1500–c1700 were similar to the reasons for migration to Britain in the years c1700–c1900. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed similarity — religious persecution abroad driving people to a Britain that did not police belief — supported by specific information from both periods, such as the Huguenots after 1685 and Jewish migrants after 1881.","sol":"In both periods a foreign state made a religious minority unsafe, and Britain took in the people it drove out. After Louis XIV revoked the Edict of Nantes in 1685, perhaps fifty thousand Huguenots crossed to England, and their silk weavers filled Spitalfields. After the assassination of Tsar Alexander II in March 1881, pogroms swept the Russian Empire and the May Laws of 1882 restricted where Jews could live, so families left for the East End of London. The similarity is one of cause: religious persecution abroad was the push in both centuries, and a Britain that did not punish belief was the pull.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Look for a cause the two periods share rather than a group they share.","Both halves need naming and dating — one measure taken by a foreign ruler in each period.","State the shared cause, prove it in the earlier period, prove it in the later one, then say in a final sentence what makes them one similarity and not two stories."],"codes":["13-3 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","13-3 — 3 Case studies: The experience of Jewish migrants in the East End of London"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the experience of migrants in England in the years c1500–c1700 was different from the experience of migrants in Britain in the years c1700–c1900. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — hostility spread locally by rumour, sermon and crowd in the earlier period, but nationally by a cheap printed press in the later one — with specific detail from both periods.","sol":"The difference is how hostility reached people. In the earlier period it travelled by sermon, rumour and the crowd: on Evil May Day, 1 May 1517, London apprentices attacked the houses and workshops of foreign residents after weeks of preaching and gossip against them, and the violence stayed local. In the later period hostility was printed and national. Punch, founded in 1841, drew Irish figures with ape-like faces for readers all over Britain, most savagely after the Fenian attacks of 1867, and the cheap press later ran 'alien invasion' stories about East End Jews. Hostility had become an industry rather than a neighbourhood quarrel.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask how an ordinary person in each period would have learned what to think about a newcomer.","One named incident from the earlier period and one named publication from the later one will carry the comparison.","The final sentence must say what changed about the nature of hostility, not merely that there was more of it."],"codes":["13-3 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in Britain"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the impact of migrants on English towns in the years c800–c1500 was similar to the impact of migrants on British towns in the years c1700–c1900. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed similarity — migrant skills and money reshaping both the trade and the buildings of towns — supported by medieval detail such as the Jewries or Flemish weavers and nineteenth-century detail such as Bradford's Little Germany.","sol":"In both periods migrants brought skills and money that changed what towns made and what they looked like. In the medieval period Jewish communities in a handful of royal towns such as York, Lincoln and Norwich financed building and trade until Edward I expelled them in 1290, and Flemish weavers strengthened the wool towns. In the nineteenth century Jewish workshops made Whitechapel the centre of the ready-made clothing trade, German wool merchants built the stone warehouses of Bradford's Little Germany in the 1860s and 1870s, and Italians opened St Peter's church in Clerkenwell in 1863. In both, migrant trades left permanent marks on the streets.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what a town gains from a group that arrives with a trade nobody local practises.","Choose evidence that is both economic and physical, so one point covers trade and buildings at once.","Name one medieval town and one Victorian town, date each piece of evidence, then say what the two have in common."],"codes":["13-3 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in Britain","13-3 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the treatment of migrants by government in the years c1700–c1900 was different from the treatment of migrants by government in the years c1900–present. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — an open border for most of the nineteenth century against statutory control in the twentieth — supported by evidence such as the political exiles of the 1830s and 1840s and the Aliens Act of 1905 or later legislation.","sol":"The difference is whether the state controlled entry at all. For most of the nineteenth century it did not: the wartime Aliens Act of 1793 had long lapsed, and political exiles simply arrived and stayed, among them Giuseppe Mazzini, in London from 1837, and Karl Marx, who settled in London in 1849 and lived there until his death in 1883. In the modern period entry has been governed by statute. The Aliens Act of 1905 let immigration officers turn back 'undesirable' aliens at named ports, and the Commonwealth Immigrants Act of 1962 ended free entry for Commonwealth citizens. An open door had become a controlled one.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what a migrant in 1850 would have had to show at the quayside, and what one in 1970 would.","Two named exiles for the earlier period and two named Acts for the later one will evidence both halves.","Finish by naming the change of principle, not just the change of law."],"codes":["13-3 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in Britain","13-3 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of British society"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which patterns of migrant settlement in the years c800–c1500 were different from patterns of migrant settlement in the years c1700–c1900. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2, AO1 2. One analysed difference — small licensed communities in a few market towns against mass concentration in industrial ports and cities — supported by medieval evidence such as the Jewries and by the 1851 census figures.","sol":"Medieval migrant settlement was small and licensed; nineteenth-century settlement was massive and industrial. Jewish communities before 1290 lived in a handful of towns such as York, Lincoln and Norwich under the king's protection because the Crown needed their money, and there were only a few thousand of them in the whole kingdom. By contrast the 1851 census counted about 727,000 Irish-born people in Britain, and more than a fifth of Liverpool's population of roughly 376,000 was Irish-born. Settlement had shifted from a protected minority scattered across market towns to whole districts of a few industrial ports.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Compare not only where migrants lived but on whose permission they lived there.","A census figure on one side and a royal arrangement on the other will give you both scale and control.","Make the last sentence describe the change in pattern, so the answer analyses rather than lists."],"codes":["13-3 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement"]},{"q":"Explain why the number of Irish-born people living in British cities rose sharply in the years 1845–1861. [12 marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the potato blight\n• steamship crossings\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit explained reasons — the Famine of 1845–52 as a push, the cheapness and shortness of the crossing, and the pull of industrial labour with chain migration through Liverpool and Glasgow. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The Irish had crossed to Britain for generations before 1845, as seasonal harvesters and as navvies on the canals, but between 1845 and 1861 the flow became a flood. That happened because a catastrophe in Ireland met an economy across the water that could absorb almost unlimited untrained labour.\n\nThe first reason is the Famine itself. Potato blight destroyed the crop from 1845 and returned in the years that followed; the Famine of 1845–52 killed about a million people and drove about a million more out of Ireland. For a cottier family whose single food crop had rotted, leaving was not one option among several but the alternative to starving, which is why the surge begins exactly with the blight and not before it.\n\nThe second reason is that Britain was the cheapest place to reach. The crossing from Dublin or Belfast to Liverpool or Glasgow took hours rather than weeks, and deck passage on the coal and cattle steamers cost pence when a passage to America cost pounds. Some 300,000 Irish people landed at Liverpool in 1847 alone. The poorest could only afford the shortest journey, so the poorest are precisely who came to Britain.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond the prompts, and it explains why they stayed. The industrial city wanted them: Lancashire's cotton mills, Glasgow's docks and foundries and above all the railway boom of the 1840s needed muscle that required no training. Once relatives held a room in Liverpool's north end or Manchester's Little Ireland, the next arrivals had an address to go to, and chain migration did the rest. The 1851 census counted about 727,000 Irish-born people in Britain, more than a fifth of Liverpool's population of roughly 376,000.\n\nThe three reasons are one sequence. The blight decided when the Irish left, the cheap crossing decided where they landed, and the demand for labour decided that they remained there long enough to be counted by a census.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Separate what was happening in Ireland from what was waiting in Britain before you write anything.","Each reason has to run as a chain — the condition, the decision it forced, and the result in a British city.","Two of your three paragraphs are handed to you; the third must be a pull inside Britain, and it needs evidence the prompts could never have suggested."],"codes":["13-3 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","13-3 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of British society","13-3 — 3 Case studies: Liverpool in the nineteenth century"]},{"q":"Explain why Jewish migration to Britain changed in the years 1881–1905. [12 marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the pogroms in the Russian Empire\n• the East End clothing trade\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit explained reasons — persecution after 1881 as a push, the sub-divided clothing trade as a pull, the communal institutions that sustained chain migration, and the anti-alien reaction that ended in the Aliens Act of 1905. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Jews had lived in Britain since readmission under Cromwell in the 1650s, and Bevis Marks synagogue opened in the City in 1701. What changed after 1881 was scale, origin and class: a small, settled and increasingly prosperous Anglo-Jewish community, whose members had been able to sit in Parliament since the Jews Relief Act of 1858, was joined by a mass of poor Yiddish-speaking migrants from the Russian Empire.\n\nThe first reason is persecution. The assassination of Tsar Alexander II in March 1881 was followed by pogroms across the Pale of Settlement, and the May Laws of 1882 restricted where Jews could live and what they could trade in. Violence and legal exclusion together made staying untenable, and Britain, which controlled no one's entry, was reachable: well over a hundred thousand had arrived by 1914.\n\nThe second reason is the work that was waiting. The ready-made clothing trade had broken tailoring into sub-divided tasks, and the sewing machine made a small workshop viable in a back room, so a newcomer with no English could be earning within days. Boot-making and cabinet-making worked the same way. That is why the migration concentrated into Whitechapel, Spitalfields and Stepney rather than spreading across Britain, and why the House of Lords began investigating the sweating system in 1888.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond the prompts: organisation turned a flight into a chain. The Jewish Board of Guardians, founded in 1859, and the Poor Jews' Temporary Shelter, opened in 1885, met the boats, found lodgings and sometimes paid fares onward, while Samuel Montagu, MP for Whitechapel from 1885, founded the Federation of Synagogues in 1887 for the immigrants' small prayer houses. Each arrival made the next one easier.\n\nFinally, the same period ended the change it began. The British Brothers' League, founded in the East End in 1901, and a Royal Commission reporting in 1903 produced the Aliens Act of 1905, under which officers could refuse 'undesirable' aliens at named ports. Persecution abroad had started the movement; British law was the first thing that slowed it.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what was different about the people arriving after 1881 compared with the Jewish community already here.","Handle the push and the pull separately, then ask what kept the movement going year after year once it had started.","The reason beyond the prompts is institutional: look at who was standing on the quayside to meet the newcomers, and who was campaigning against them by 1901."],"codes":["13-3 — 3 Case studies: The experience of Jewish migrants in the East End of London","13-3 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in Britain"]},{"q":"Explain why hostility towards migrants in Britain grew stronger in the years 1850–1900. [12 marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• Fenian attacks\n• the popular press\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit explained reasons — political violence turning migration into a security question, a cheap national press spreading caricature, religious conflict after the restoration of the Catholic hierarchy in 1850, and competition for housing and work in overcrowded cities. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Hostility to migrants was not new in 1850, but in the second half of the century it became louder, more organised and more national. It did so because political violence, cheap print and material competition reinforced one another.\n\nThe first reason is political violence. In September 1867 Fenians freed prisoners from a police van in Manchester and a police sergeant was killed; three men were hanged that November. In December a bomb at Clerkenwell prison in London killed twelve people. After that, being Irish could be treated as a security question rather than a private matter, and suspicion fell on communities that had nothing to do with the Irish Republican Brotherhood.\n\nThe second reason is the press. Punch, founded in 1841, drew Irish figures with ape-like features, and the caricature grew crueller after 1867; later, the cheap national papers ran 'alien invasion' stories about the East End. Print gave hostility a shared vocabulary and a national audience, so a reader in Devon could hold opinions about Whitechapel.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond the prompts and is the oldest of the three: religion. The Catholic hierarchy was restored in England in 1850, the Ecclesiastical Titles Act followed in 1851, and Irish homes and chapels were attacked in the Stockport riots of 1852; William Murphy's anti-Catholic lecture tours provoked further riots in the later 1860s. Anti-Catholicism gave anti-Irish feeling a language and a ready-made crowd.\n\nA fourth reason, also beyond the prompts, is competition. In overcrowded ports and in the East End workshops, newcomers were visible rivals for rooms and for work, and the House of Lords inquiry into the sweating system from 1888 fixed public attention on immigrant labour and low wages. That grievance is what the British Brothers' League organised when it was founded in 1901.\n\nThe reasons work as a sequence rather than a list: material competition supplied the grievance, religion and political violence supplied the target, and the press supplied the megaphone.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Distinguish the feelings people already had from the events that gave those feelings a target.","Each paragraph should end by saying what kind of hostility the reason produced, not just that hostility grew.","Two of your reasons must come from outside the prompts: think about what was preached from pulpits after 1850 and what was argued about in workshops after 1888."],"codes":["13-3 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in Britain"]},{"q":"Explain why the growth of the British Empire changed patterns of migration to Britain in the years c1700–c1900. [12 marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• lascars\n• the abolition of the slave trade\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit explained reasons — imperial shipping bringing crews to British ports, abolition changing the status and character of the Black population, and the later arrival of servants, students and professionals from India. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Empire did not simply add new nationalities; it changed who arrived, on whose terms, and with what rights.\n\nThe first is imperial shipping. British trade with India and China ran on crews hired where the ships were, so lascars — sailors from South Asia and the Indian Ocean — came to London and Liverpool in thousands, paid a fraction of white sailors' wages and often stranded between voyages when a ship sailed without them. Destitution on that scale forced a public response, and the Strangers' Home for Asiatics, Africans and South Sea Islanders opened in Limehouse in 1857. Migration here was a by-product of trade routes rather than a decision by the migrants.\n\nThe second is abolition. Before 1807, Africans came to Britain overwhelmingly as the property and servants of planters and captains enriched by the transatlantic trade, in which Liverpool was the leading British port. The Slave Trade Act of 1807 and the Slavery Abolition Act of 1833 ended that traffic, and Lord Mansfield's Somerset judgment of 1772 had already meant a person could not be seized in England and shipped abroad. The Black population therefore stopped being renewed by force and became a settled, free and campaigning one: Olaudah Equiano, who bought his freedom in 1766 and published his Interesting Narrative in 1789, toured the country arguing for abolition.\n\nThe third development goes beyond the prompts: the Empire began to send its elite as well as its labour. Ayahs, the Indian nannies of British families, travelled home with their employers and were sometimes abandoned on arrival, so that a home for stranded ayahs was needed in east London by the end of the century. Students, lawyers and politicians followed: Dadabhai Naoroji was elected MP for Central Finsbury in 1892, the first Indian MP, and Mancherjee Bhownagree for Bethnal Green North East in 1895.\n\nTaken together, the Empire turned migration to Britain from something done by neighbours into something done by subjects — first as crew and servants, then, once the slave trade had gone, as free people who could argue in print and stand for Parliament.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what an empire actually moves: ships, servants, soldiers, students — and in that order over time.","Treat abolition as a change in status, not only a change in numbers, and say what that change made possible.","The reason beyond the prompts belongs to the end of the period: look at who came from India in the 1880s and 1890s and what they did once here."],"codes":["13-3 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of British society","13-3 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement"]},{"q":"'The main reason for migration to Britain in the years c1700–c1900 was the search for work.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• Irish navvies\n• the Industrial Revolution\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6, plus up to 4 SPaG. Credit the case for work as the dominant pull, weighed against persecution and asylum and against forced or imperial movement, with a criterion for 'main' justified and applied. Caps: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Judged by the number of people it moved and the length of time it moved them, work was the strongest single attraction Britain offered between c1700 and c1900. But 'main' has to mean something, and once it is defined as the reason that best explains both when people came and why they stayed, the answer becomes a divided one: work explains the destinations, while catastrophe, persecution and Empire explain the timing.\n\nThe case for work is powerful. Industrialising Britain needed labour that needed no training, and it needed it in specific places. Irish navvies dug the canals and laid the railways in the boom of the 1840s; Lancashire's mills, Glasgow's foundries and Liverpool's docks took thousands more. The 1851 census counted about 727,000 Irish-born people in Britain, and more than a fifth of Liverpool's population of roughly 376,000 was Irish-born, which is exactly where the unskilled work was. Work explains the European migrants too: German merchants built the warehouses of Bradford's Little Germany in the 1860s and 1870s because Bradford sold cloth into Europe, German pork butchers opened shops in northern towns, and Italians built the ice-cream and café trades. Even the Jewish migration after 1881 settled where it did because the sub-divided clothing trade of Whitechapel could employ a newcomer within days.\n\nYet the largest movements of the period were not timed by wages. Wages in Lancashire were no higher in 1847 than in 1843; what changed was that the potato crop failed. The Famine of 1845–52 killed about a million people and drove about a million out, and some 300,000 landed at Liverpool in 1847 alone. The second great surge was timed by persecution: the assassination of Tsar Alexander II in March 1881 was followed by pogroms, and the May Laws of 1882 restricted where Jews could live. Neither surge can be explained by the labour market, because the labour market had not changed.\n\nA third group, beyond the prompts, did not search for anything. Africans were brought to Britain as the property and servants of planters and captains until the Slave Trade Act of 1807 and the Slavery Abolition Act of 1833 ended that traffic. Lascars came because British ships hired crews where the ships were, and were stranded in London and Liverpool when those ships sailed without them, which is why the Strangers' Home opened in Limehouse in 1857. Ayahs travelled with the families who employed them and were sometimes abandoned on arrival. For these people migration was a consequence of somebody else's trade.\n\nThere is also a pull that has nothing to do with wages: liberty. For most of the nineteenth century Britain controlled nobody's entry, so Giuseppe Mazzini arrived in 1837 and Karl Marx settled in London in 1849 and stayed until his death in 1883. French Catholic clergy fleeing the Revolution had already made Somers Town a refuge in the 1790s.\n\nOn the criterion of numbers over the whole period, work wins; on the criterion of explaining any particular surge, it loses. The honest judgement is that work explains where migrants went and why they stayed, while famine, pogrom and Empire explain when they came and whether they had any choice about coming.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Before judging, decide what would make one reason 'main' — the most people, the longest span, or the best explanation of the big surges.","Sort the migrants of this period into those who chose, those who fled and those who were moved by somebody else.","Your point beyond the prompts should be the group for whom 'search' is the wrong word altogether."],"codes":["13-3 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","13-3 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of British society"]},{"q":"'The media was the most important influence on how migrants were treated in Britain in the years c1700–c1900.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• Punch cartoons\n• the Fenian attacks of 1867\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6, plus up to 4 SPaG. Credit the case for a cheap national press shaping opinion, weighed against religion, economic competition and the law, with a stated criterion for 'most important' applied to reach the judgement. Caps: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"If 'most important' means the influence that decided how migrants were actually treated — whether they were housed, employed, attacked or admitted — then the press was an amplifier rather than a cause. It made hostility national and gave it a vocabulary, but the hostility itself came from religion and from competition, and the treatment migrants received in law ran in the opposite direction for most of the period.\n\nThe case for the media is real. Punch, founded in 1841, drew Irish figures with ape-like features for readers everywhere, and the caricature hardened after the Fenian attacks of 1867, when three men were hanged in Manchester in November and a bomb at Clerkenwell prison in London killed twelve people in December. During the Whitechapel murders of 1888 newspapers speculated freely that the killer must be a foreigner or a Jew, and East End Jews were abused in the street as a result. By the 1890s the cheap national press was running 'alien invasion' campaigns, and that language fed directly into the British Brothers' League of 1901 and the Aliens Act of 1905. Print could turn a local grievance into a national one within a week.\n\nReligion, however, was shaping treatment long before a mass press existed and independently of it. When the Catholic hierarchy was restored in 1850 and the Ecclesiastical Titles Act followed in 1851, the reaction was fuelled from pulpits as much as from newsstands: Irish homes and chapels were attacked in the Stockport riots of 1852, and William Murphy's anti-Catholic lecture tours provoked riots in the later 1860s. The crowds in Stockport were responding to sermons and processions, not to cartoons.\n\nEconomic competition, a point beyond the prompts, mattered even more. Treatment was harshest where rooms and wages were scarcest: in Liverpool's courts and cellars in 1847, when some 300,000 Irish landed, and in the East End workshops investigated by the House of Lords sweating inquiry from 1888. The same employers who profited from cheap migrant labour welcomed the newcomers their workers resented, which is why hostility concentrated among competitors for housing and work rather than among readers in general.\n\nThe law, a second point beyond the prompts, cuts against the statement most sharply. For nearly the whole period the state left migrants alone: no control on entry, so Mazzini could arrive in 1837 and Marx settle in 1849. More than that, the law improved migrants' standing while the press attacked them — Lord Mansfield's Somerset judgment in 1772, Catholic Emancipation in 1829, the Jews Relief Act of 1858 that let Lionel de Rothschild take his seat. A migrant's legal position in 1880 was better than in 1780, whatever Punch printed.\n\nThe judgement, then, is a qualified disagreement. Measured by what actually happened to migrants — where they lived, what they earned, whether they could vote or sit in Parliament — religion, competition and the law count for more. Measured by the speed and reach of hostility, and by the fact that press campaigns of the 1890s ended in a statute in 1905, the media was decisive at the very end of the period but not across it.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first whether 'treatment' means what people felt, what neighbours did, or what the law allowed — the answer changes with the definition.","Test the media against a case where hostility clearly came from somewhere else, and say what that case proves.","One of your points beyond the prompts should be the influence that made migrants' position better rather than worse."],"codes":["13-3 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in Britain","13-3 — 3 Case studies: Liverpool in the nineteenth century"]},{"q":"'Migrants changed the economy of Britain more than they changed its culture in the years c1700–c1900.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the East End clothing trade\n• Italian ice-cream sellers\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6, plus up to 4 SPaG. Credit weighed economic impact (labour, trades, firms) against cultural impact (music, food, religion, print) and a further impact beyond the prompts such as politics, with a criterion for 'more' justified and applied. Caps: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The two kinds of change are hard to separate, because most cultural change in this period arrived inside a business. If 'more' is measured by how many people's daily lives were altered and by how long the alteration lasted, then the economic impact was larger at the time but the cultural impact proved more permanent — and a third impact, on politics, is arguably bigger than either.\n\nThe economic case is the strongest in the short run. Irish labour dug the canals, laid the railways of the 1840s and filled Lancashire's mills; without it the construction boom is hard to imagine. Jewish migrants after 1881 rebuilt the East End clothing trade around sub-divided tasks and the sewing machine, making ready-made clothes cheap enough for working people to buy, and Michael Marks, arriving from Russian Poland, opened a penny bazaar in Leeds market in 1884 and took Thomas Spencer as his partner in 1894. German wool merchants built the warehouses of Bradford's Little Germany in the 1860s and 1870s, and the German-born engineer William Siemens built a telegraph and steel business here. This is change measured in wages, exports and prices.\n\nThe cultural case is quieter but longer-lasting. Italians created the ice-cream and café trades that still bear their names, and their organ grinders were a feature of London streets; St Peter's Italian Church opened in Clerkenwell in 1863. Charles Hallé came to Manchester in 1848 and founded the Hallé Orchestra there in 1858, giving a manufacturing city an institution it still has. The Christmas tree became a British custom after the Illustrated London News pictured the royal family's tree in 1848. Sake Dean Mahomed had opened the Hindostanee Coffee House near Portman Square in 1810. The Catholic hierarchy restored in 1850 and the synagogues and prayer houses of the East End changed the religious map permanently, and one building on Brick Lane — a Huguenot chapel of 1743 that became a synagogue in 1898 — shows the process in miniature.\n\nA third impact, beyond the prompts, may outweigh both. Migrants and their children changed British politics. The Irish-born Feargus O'Connor led Chartism and founded the Northern Star in 1837; William Cuffay, son of a formerly enslaved man from St Kitts, led London Chartists until his conviction in 1848 and transportation to Tasmania, where he died in 1870. T. P. O'Connor won Liverpool Scotland in 1885 and held it until 1929, the only Irish Nationalist ever to sit for a British seat. Dadabhai Naoroji became the first Indian MP in 1892 and Mancherjee Bhownagree followed in 1895. Olaudah Equiano's Interesting Narrative of 1789 changed the terms of the argument that produced the Acts of 1807 and 1833.\n\nThe judgement is therefore a partial agreement. On contemporary scale, the economic impact was greater: it touched every consumer and every building site. On durability, culture wins, because orchestras, churches, foods and festivals outlived the trades that carried them. And on significance for the country's future, the political impact matters most of all, because it decided who could belong.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["The two halves of the statement overlap, so your first job is to say how you will tell economic change from cultural change.","Choose a measure — how many people, how deep, how long-lasting — and hold to it through every paragraph.","Look for an impact the statement leaves out entirely, and ask whether it beats both of the ones named."],"codes":["13-3 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in Britain","13-3 — 3 Case studies: The experience of Jewish migrants in the East End of London"]},{"q":"'The experience of Irish migrants in Liverpool was typical of the experience of migrants in Britain in the nineteenth century.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• cellar dwellings\n• anti-Catholic hostility\nYou must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6, plus up to 4 SPaG. Credit the Liverpool Irish experience of overcrowding, disease and hostility set against other migrant experiences — the East End Jewish community, prosperous German merchants, welcomed political exiles, lascars — with a criterion for 'typical' justified and applied. Caps: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Taking 'typical' to mean that the same forces acted on migrants elsewhere in roughly the same way, the Liverpool Irish experience is typical in kind but not in degree, and quite untypical in its religious politics.\n\nIn kind, Liverpool shows what happened to almost every poor migrant group. Some 300,000 Irish people landed there in 1847 alone, a number close to the town's own population, and they crowded into the courts and cellar dwellings of the north end around Vauxhall and Scotland Road. Typhus followed, and Dr William Henry Duncan, appointed Britain's first Medical Officer of Health in 1847, identified those cellars as the seat of the fever — so migrants and disease became linked in official minds. Hostility followed too, and it was religious: the restoration of the Catholic hierarchy in 1850 and the Ecclesiastical Titles Act of 1851 preceded attacks on Irish homes and chapels in the Stockport riots of 1852. That pattern — cheapest housing, blame for disease, blame for undercutting wages — repeats in the East End, where Jewish migrants after 1881 filled the sweated trades and were investigated by a House of Lords inquiry from 1888 and then agitated against by the British Brothers' League of 1901.\n\nIn degree, Liverpool is extreme rather than typical. No other British town received a year's arrivals equal to its own population, and by 1851 more than a fifth of its roughly 376,000 people were Irish-born, against about 727,000 Irish-born in the whole of Britain. Liverpool was also a port of transit as much as of settlement, the great European departure point for North America, so a large share of those who landed never intended to stay.\n\nOther migrants had experiences Liverpool cannot represent at all. German merchants in Bradford were wealthy men who built the stone warehouses of Little Germany in the 1860s and 1870s and were welcomed into chambers of commerce; Charles Hallé was celebrated in Manchester and founded its orchestra in 1858. Political exiles were not merely tolerated but admired: Mazzini ran a free school for Italian boys in London from 1841 and Garibaldi was greeted by vast crowds when he visited Britain in 1864. Beyond the prompts, the East End Jewish community, though poor, had something the Famine Irish did not — its own institutions, the Jewish Board of Guardians of 1859, the Poor Jews' Temporary Shelter of 1885 and the Federation of Synagogues of 1887 — which softened arrival in a way Liverpool's cellars never did. Lascars, another point beyond the prompts, had no settlement experience to speak of: they were crews stranded between voyages, which is why the Strangers' Home opened in Limehouse in 1857.\n\nA final untypical feature is politics. Liverpool's sectarian division, with Orange marches on one side and T. P. O'Connor holding Liverpool Scotland for the Irish Nationalists from 1885 until 1929, existed in no other British city.\n\nThe judgement is a qualified agreement. If typicality is measured by the forces acting on poor migrants — overcrowding, disease, competition and a hostile press — Liverpool is the clearest example of the norm. If it is measured by scale, by wealth, or by the political shape hostility took, Liverpool is the exception that no other town repeats.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what 'typical' would have to mean before you can agree or disagree with it.","Test Liverpool against two very different migrant experiences — one poor and one prosperous.","Your points beyond the prompts should be experiences Liverpool cannot represent at all, such as migrants who never settled."],"codes":["13-3 — 3 Case studies: Liverpool in the nineteenth century","13-3 — 3 Case studies: The experience of Jewish migrants in the East End of London","13-3 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in Britain"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In which years did the Great Famine in Ireland take place?","a":"1845–52."},{"q":"Roughly how many Irish-born people did the 1851 census record in Britain?","a":"About 727,000."},{"q":"Which event of March 1881 was followed by the pogroms that drove Jewish families out of the Russian Empire?","a":"The assassination of Tsar Alexander II."},{"q":"Which Act of 1858 allowed Jewish men to take their seats in the House of Commons?","a":"The Jews Relief Act."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The Great Famine in Ireland","code":"13-3 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","open":false,"back":"Potato blight destroyed the crop from 1845 and the Famine ran to 1852. About a million people died and about a million more left Ireland, many for Liverpool, Glasgow and the industrial towns."},{"front":"Irish-born people in Britain at the 1851 census","code":"13-3 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","open":false,"back":"About 727,000. More than a fifth of Liverpool's population of roughly 376,000 was Irish-born, and close to a fifth of Glasgow's."},{"front":"Liverpool in 1847","code":"13-3 — 3 Case studies: Liverpool in the nineteenth century","open":false,"back":"Some 300,000 Irish people landed, a number close to the town's own population. Typhus swept the courts and cellars, and Dr William Henry Duncan became Britain's first Medical Officer of Health."},{"front":"The pogroms of 1881","code":"13-3 — 3 Case studies: The experience of Jewish migrants in the East End of London","open":false,"back":"After Tsar Alexander II was assassinated in March 1881, anti-Jewish pogroms spread across the Russian Empire and the May Laws of 1882 restricted where Jews could live. Well over a hundred thousand reached Britain by 1914."},{"front":"The Jews Relief Act 1858","code":"13-3 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in Britain","open":false,"back":"Changed the parliamentary oath so Jewish men could sit in the Commons. Lionel de Rothschild, elected for the City of London in 1847, finally took his seat in 1858."},{"front":"The Aliens Act 1905","code":"13-3 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in Britain","open":false,"back":"Britain's first modern immigration control. Immigration officers could refuse 'undesirable' aliens at named ports, though people fleeing religious or political persecution were meant to be exempt."},{"front":"Olaudah Equiano","code":"13-3 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of British society","open":false,"back":"Formerly enslaved; bought his freedom in 1766 for £40. Published The Interesting Narrative in 1789, toured Britain selling it and campaigned for abolition with the Sons of Africa. Died in 1797."},{"front":"Lascars","code":"13-3 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","open":false,"back":"Sailors from South Asia and the Indian Ocean hired on British ships, paid far less than white crews and often stranded between voyages. The Strangers' Home opened for them in Limehouse in 1857."},{"front":"Sake Dean Mahomed","code":"13-3 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in Britain","open":false,"back":"Born in Patna in 1759. Opened the Hindostanee Coffee House near Portman Square in London in 1810 and later ran vapour baths in Brighton, serving George IV."},{"front":"Little Germany, Bradford","code":"13-3 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in Britain","open":false,"back":"A district of stone warehouses built mainly in the 1860s and 1870s by German and German-Jewish wool merchants who sold Bradford cloth into Europe."},{"front":"Charles Hallé","code":"13-3 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in Britain","open":false,"back":"German-born pianist who came to Manchester in 1848 after fleeing revolution in Paris, and founded the Hallé Orchestra there in 1858."},{"front":"Little Italy, Clerkenwell","code":"13-3 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in Britain","open":false,"back":"London's Italian quarter around Saffron Hill: organ grinders, craftsmen and ice-cream sellers. St Peter's Italian Church opened in Clerkenwell in 1863."},{"front":"The Fenian attacks of 1867","code":"13-3 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in Britain","open":false,"back":"Three men were hanged in Manchester in November after a police sergeant was killed during a prison-van rescue; a bomb at Clerkenwell prison in December killed twelve people and hardened opinion against the Irish."},{"front":"Two trades Jewish migrants worked in the East End","code":"13-3 — 3 Case studies: The experience of Jewish migrants in the East End of London","open":true,"back":"Tailoring and the ready-made clothing trade; boot- and shoe-making; cabinet-making and furniture; baking; cigarette making."},{"front":"The Brick Lane chapel, synagogue and mosque","code":"13-3 — 3 Case studies: The experience of Jewish migrants in the East End of London","open":false,"back":"Built in 1743 as a Huguenot chapel, it became the Machzike Hadath synagogue in 1898 and a mosque in 1976 — one building marking three waves of migration into Spitalfields."}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 1 Question 5, 16 marks)","items":["The question: 'Liverpool was the most important centre of migration in Britain in the years c1700–c1900.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the port of Liverpool • the East End of London. You must also use information of your own.","London was the more important centre of migration, because it was where migrants stayed rather than where they passed through. At the 1851 census London held the largest number of Irish-born people of any city in Britain, while Liverpool held the highest proportion, and the difference matters: Liverpool's Irish were a fifth or more of a smaller town, London's were a community inside the largest city in the world. After the assassination of Tsar Alexander II in 1881 and the May Laws of 1882, Jewish families from the Russian Empire settled in Whitechapel, Spitalfields and Stepney in numbers that sustained their own institutions — the Poor Jews' Temporary Shelter opened in 1885 and the Federation of Synagogues was founded in 1887 — and the Huguenot chapel of 1743 on Brick Lane became the Machzike Hadath synagogue in 1898, one building recording two migrations. The statement is not simply wrong, because Liverpool was the gateway through which the largest single movement of the period passed: some 300,000 Irish people landed there in 1847 alone, a number close to the town's own population, and the shipping lines that ran from the port later drew Chinese seamen to the streets around Pitt Street from the 1860s. But a gateway is not a destination, and Liverpool was the great European port of departure for North America, so a large share of those who landed sailed on. On the criterion of where migration changed the population permanently, London comes first and Liverpool second; on the criterion of where migration was most visible at a single moment, the order reverses.","Why this is Level 4: the explanation is directed consistently at the conceptual focus — which centre mattered most, and on what test — rather than describing either city; the information (the 1851 census, 1881, the May Laws of 1882, the shelter of 1885, the Federation of 1887, the Brick Lane chapel of 1743 and synagogue of 1898, the 300,000 landings of 1847, Pitt Street from the 1860s) is precisely selected to address the question; the counter-case is conceded and then weighed; and the criterion for the judgement is stated and applied rather than asserted."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["The question: 'Liverpool was the most important centre of migration in Britain in the years c1700–c1900.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the port of Liverpool • the East End of London. You must also use information of your own.","London was also an important centre of migration. Lots of migrants went to live in London because it was the biggest city and there was work there, and Jewish people settled in the East End where they worked in the clothing trade. This shows that Liverpool was not the only place that mattered. Liverpool was still important though because it was a big port and huge numbers of Irish people arrived there during the famine and some of them stayed. So overall Liverpool was important but London was important as well.","Why this is Level 2 and not higher: it meets 'an explanation is given showing limited analysis and with implicit or unsustained links to the conceptual focus', and 'the overall judgement is given but its justification is asserted'. It misses Level 3 and 4 because the information is generalised — no date, no census figure, no named street or institution — so it cannot show 'accurate and relevant information … precisely selected', and it never says on what criterion one centre would count as more important than another."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — strand 1: who came to Britain c1700–c1900, and why","items":["Irish: seasonal harvest workers and canal and railway navvies long before the Famine; the Famine of 1845–52 then made the movement a mass one.","The Famine: potato blight destroyed the crop from 1845; about a million people died and about a million emigrated by 1852.","1851 census: about 727,000 Irish-born people living in Britain.","Jewish migrants: about 1881 onwards, after the assassination of Tsar Alexander II in March 1881 and the May Laws of 1882; well over a hundred thousand had arrived by 1914.","Germans: the largest continental European group in Victorian Britain — merchants in Bradford and Manchester, sugar bakers in the East End, pork butchers in northern towns, musicians and engineers.","Italians: organ grinders, craftsmen and ice-cream sellers, concentrated in Clerkenwell's Little Italy and later in Ancoats in Manchester and in Glasgow.","French: Catholic clergy and aristocrats fleeing the Revolution after 1789, many settling in Somers Town in St Pancras, where the chapel of St Aloysius opened in 1808.","Empire: lascars from South Asia and the Indian Ocean hired on British ships; ayahs who travelled with the families who employed them; students, lawyers and merchants from India later in the century.","Chinese: seamen recruited by shipping lines such as Alfred Holt's Blue Funnel Line, founded in Liverpool in 1866, settling around Pitt Street in Liverpool and Limehouse in London; fewer than a thousand Chinese-born people were recorded in Britain in the late nineteenth century.","Africans: brought as servants and property of planters and captains before the Slave Trade Act of 1807, and a free, settled and campaigning community afterwards.","Internal migration counts too: Welsh migrants to Liverpool and the Midlands, and Scots and English country people to the industrial cities.","The pulls: industrial wages, urban work, imperial shipping routes, and civil liberties — no control on entry for most of the nineteenth century."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — strand 2: the experience and impact of migrants, c1700–c1900","items":["The law was mostly absent: the wartime Aliens Act of 1793 had lapsed, and there was no general control on entry until the Aliens Act of 1905.","Civil liberties milestones: Lord Mansfield's judgment in the Somerset case, 1772; Catholic Emancipation, 1829; the Jews Relief Act, 1858.","Anti-Catholic conflict: the Catholic hierarchy was restored in 1850, the Ecclesiastical Titles Act followed in 1851, and Irish homes and chapels were attacked in the Stockport riots of 1852.","Political violence and its backlash: Fenians killed a police sergeant in Manchester in September 1867 and three men were hanged that November; a bomb at Clerkenwell prison in December 1867 killed twelve people.","The press: Punch, founded in 1841, caricatured the Irish with ape-like features; newspapers speculated about a foreign or Jewish killer during the Whitechapel murders of 1888; the cheap national press ran 'alien invasion' stories in the 1890s.","Organised opposition: the British Brothers' League was founded in the East End in 1901 and a Royal Commission on alien immigration reported in 1903, leading to the Aliens Act of 1905.","Economic impact: Irish navvies on the canals and the railway boom of the 1840s; Jewish workshops in the East End clothing trade; German merchants in Bradford; Michael Marks's penny bazaar in Leeds market in 1884 and his partnership with Thomas Spencer in 1894.","Cultural impact: Charles Hallé founded the Hallé Orchestra in Manchester in 1858; Italians created the ice-cream and café trades; the Christmas tree spread after the Illustrated London News pictured the royal family's tree in 1848; Sake Dean Mahomed opened the Hindostanee Coffee House in London in 1810.","Political impact: Feargus O'Connor led Chartism and founded the Northern Star in 1837; William Cuffay led London Chartists and was convicted in 1848 and transported to Tasmania, where he died in 1870; T. P. O'Connor won Liverpool Scotland in 1885; Dadabhai Naoroji became the first Indian MP in 1892 and Mancherjee Bhownagree followed in 1895.","Abolition: the Society for Effecting the Abolition of the Slave Trade was founded in 1787, the Slave Trade Act passed in 1807 and the Slavery Abolition Act in 1833; Olaudah Equiano and Ottobah Cugoano campaigned in print, Equiano's Interesting Narrative appearing in 1789 and Cugoano's Thoughts and Sentiments in 1787.","The urban environment: Catholic churches, synagogues and Italian churches were built in migrant districts, and whole quarters — Little Ireland in Manchester, Little Italy in Clerkenwell, Little Germany in Bradford — took their names from the newcomers.","Earlier layers were still visible: Huguenot silk weaving had made Spitalfields, and its chapel of 1743 on Brick Lane later became a synagogue and then a mosque."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — Liverpool in the nineteenth century","items":["Liverpool grew rich first on the transatlantic slave trade, of which it was the leading British port by the later eighteenth century, and then on Atlantic commerce; the Albert Dock opened in 1846.","It was the great European port of departure for North America, so migrants passed through it as well as settling in it.","Some 300,000 Irish people landed at Liverpool in 1847 alone, a number close to the town's own population.","A typhus epidemic swept the courts and cellar dwellings of the north end, around Vauxhall and Scotland Road, in 1847.","Dr William Henry Duncan became Britain's first Medical Officer of Health, at Liverpool, in 1847, and blamed the cellar dwellings for the fever.","At the 1851 census Liverpool's population was roughly 376,000, more than a fifth of it Irish-born.","Liverpool held a large Welsh community whose builders put up much of the city's terraced housing.","A Chinese community grew around Pitt Street from the 1860s, linked to Alfred Holt's Blue Funnel Line, founded in 1866, and is often described as the oldest in Europe.","Sectarian politics were unusually sharp: Orange Order marches on one side, and on the other T. P. O'Connor, elected for Liverpool Scotland in 1885 and holding the seat until 1929 — the only Irish Nationalist ever to sit for a British constituency.","Liverpool is the strongest evidence in the option for the link contemporaries made between migration, overcrowding and disease."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — Jewish migrants in the East End of London, c1881–1905","items":["Jews were readmitted to England under Cromwell in the 1650s; Bevis Marks synagogue opened in the City in 1701.","The Jewish Naturalisation Act of 1753 was repealed the following year after public protest.","The Jews Relief Act of 1858 changed the parliamentary oath, and Lionel de Rothschild, elected in 1847, took his Commons seat.","The mass migration began after the assassination of Tsar Alexander II in March 1881 and the pogroms that followed, with the May Laws of 1882 restricting where Jews could live and trade.","Settlement concentrated in Whitechapel, Spitalfields and Stepney, around Petticoat Lane and Brick Lane.","Work was in the sweated trades: tailoring broken into sub-divided tasks, boot- and shoe-making, and cabinet-making, in small workshops using sewing machines.","A House of Lords Select Committee investigated the sweating system from 1888.","Communal institutions: the Jewish Board of Guardians, founded in 1859; the Poor Jews' Temporary Shelter, opened in 1885; the Federation of Synagogues, founded in 1887 by Samuel Montagu, MP for Whitechapel from 1885; the United Synagogue had been created by Act of Parliament in 1870.","The Jewish Chronicle had been founded in 1841, and Israel Zangwill's novel Children of the Ghetto appeared in 1892.","East End Jewish tailors struck in 1889, the year of the London dock strike, and won shorter hours.","Hostility: press speculation about a foreign or Jewish killer during the Whitechapel murders of 1888; the British Brothers' League, founded in 1901; a Royal Commission reporting in 1903; the Aliens Act of 1905.","The Huguenot chapel of 1743 on Brick Lane became the Machzike Hadath synagogue in 1898, and a mosque in 1976."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 1 Question 5 (16 marks + 4 SPaG)","items":["The question as printed: 'Hostility from the existing population was the main experience of migrants in Britain in the years c1700–c1900.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • anti-Irish riots • the sweated trades. You must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","Decode it: the concept is the nature of migrants' experience across two centuries; 'main' demands a criterion; 'how far' demands a weighed answer, not a yes or a no. Decide the criterion in the opening lines — which experience applied to the most migrants, most of the time.","Opening: state the criterion and signal the argument — hostility was the most visible experience of poor migrants in industrial cities, but for the period as a whole it competes with work, with communal self-help and with a legal freedom that had no equal in Europe.","Paragraph 1, the case for hostility (uses the first stimulus point): the restoration of the Catholic hierarchy in 1850 and the Ecclesiastical Titles Act of 1851; Irish homes and chapels attacked in the Stockport riots of 1852; William Murphy's lecture tours and the riots of the later 1860s; Punch, founded in 1841, caricaturing the Irish, especially after the Fenian attacks of 1867. Interim judgement: hostility was real, organised and national.","Paragraph 2, hostility as economic resentment (uses the second stimulus point): the sweated trades of Whitechapel and Spitalfields; the House of Lords inquiry into the sweating system from 1888; the British Brothers' League of 1901 and the Aliens Act of 1905. Interim judgement: hostility intensified exactly where competition for rooms and wages was fiercest — which is a limit on the claim as well as evidence for it.","Paragraph 3, the counter-case of work and success: Irish navvies on the railways of the 1840s; German merchants building Bradford's Little Germany in the 1860s and 1870s; Michael Marks's penny bazaar in Leeds market in 1884 and his partnership with Thomas Spencer in 1894; Charles Hallé founding the Hallé Orchestra in Manchester in 1858. Interim judgement: for many migrants the daily experience was work and advancement, not abuse.","Paragraph 4, the point beyond the stimulus — self-help and liberty: the Jewish Board of Guardians of 1859, the Poor Jews' Temporary Shelter of 1885 and the Federation of Synagogues of 1887; no control on entry, so Mazzini arrived in 1837 and Marx settled in London in 1849; Lord Mansfield's Somerset judgment of 1772, Catholic Emancipation in 1829 and the Jews Relief Act of 1858. Interim judgement: migrants experienced a state that mostly left them alone and communities that organised themselves.","The evaluation thread that runs through every paragraph: who is 'the existing population'? Neighbours competing for the same rooms behaved very differently from employers, magistrates and Parliament, so the answer must keep asking whose hostility is meant.","The conclusion must do three things: restate the criterion, apply it, and qualify. Something like — measured across all migrants and the whole period, hostility was the loudest experience but not the main one; it fell hardest on the poorest and the most visible, in the most crowded places and at moments of crisis such as 1847, 1867 and the 1890s, while for others the defining experience was work, worship and a legal freedom unavailable at home. Name the criterion explicitly so the judgement is justified and not asserted.","Timing: about 4 minutes planning, about 20 minutes writing, and 1 to 2 minutes reading back for spelling and specialist terms, since 4 further marks depend on accurate spelling, punctuation, grammar and the confident use of terms such as pogrom, emigration, sectarian, sweated trades and naturalisation."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T12:54:56Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.82,"notes":"Every date, name and figure in the retargeted items checks out — Kempe's letters of protection of 16 July 1331, Behrens moving from Leeds to Bradford in 1838, Little Germany's warehouses of 1855–1890, St Peter's consecrated in 1863, Machzike Hadath taking the 1743 Huguenot chapel in 1898, Naoroji's five-vote win at Central Finsbury in 1892 and the 28,644 Germans of 1861 — and no example, model paragraph or plan now shares a bank question's focus and stimulus points; before teaching, correct two glosses only: the 1861 census was the first to tabulate foreigners by country of origin, not the first to record birthplace at all¹, and the Clerkenwell Road frontage of St Peter's dates from the road of 1874–78, not from the church's opening²."}}},"13-4":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"13-4","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This is the last key topic of the thematic study, and it is the one the Section B questions keep coming back to, because every comparison question needs a modern half and because the 12-mark and 16-mark questions normally span at least a century and often end in the present. You need three things from it. First, the story: who came to Britain after c1900, what pushed and pulled them, and where they settled. Second, the machinery: the wars, the end of empire, the Commonwealth, EU membership and the run of statutes from the Aliens Act of 1905 to the Race Relations Act of 1976 that decided who could come and how they were treated once here. Third, two case studies the specification names by name, Bristol in the mid-twentieth century and Asian migrants in Leicester from 1945, which is where the precise evidence comes from that separates a Level 3 answer from a Level 4 one. The examiner does not reward the story on its own. Every mark above the middle of the grid is for analysis pointed at the concept in the question, so this room teaches the material as arguments about causation, change, continuity and significance, with the dates and names attached that make those arguments provable.","idea":[{"code":"13-4 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","h":"Who came after c1900, why they came and where they settled","t":"Four streams run through the century. The Irish were the largest overseas-born group in Britain for most of the twentieth century, working on building sites, motorways, the railways and in hospitals. Europeans came in waves: about a quarter of a million Belgians fled here during the First World War, nearly 10,000 mostly Jewish children arrived on the Kindertransport in 1938-39, Polish servicemen who could not go home were allowed to settle by the Polish Resettlement Act of 1947, displaced people were recruited from European camps as European Volunteer Workers, and after ten states joined the European Union on 1 May 2004 several hundred thousand Poles came to work. From the Empire and Commonwealth came Caribbean migrants, of whom the passengers who landed from the Empire Windrush at Tilbury on 22 June 1948 are the symbol, then Indians, Pakistanis and Bangladeshis for the mills of Yorkshire and Lancashire and the foundries of the West Midlands, then East African Asians from Kenya after 1967 and Uganda in 1972. Refugees and asylum seekers came from Hungary in 1956, Vietnam from 1979, the former Yugoslavia in the 1990s, and Somalia, Iraq and Afghanistan since. Settlement followed work and cheap housing, so it concentrated in London, Birmingham, Bradford, Leicester, Bristol, Manchester and Glasgow. A top-level answer never lists the groups: it sorts them by push and pull, and points out that the pattern - work, refuge, family, clustering where relatives already are - is the same pattern as in every earlier key topic, and that only the origins changed."},{"code":"13-4 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of British society","h":"What changed in Britain: war, the end of empire, Europe and the law","t":"Modern migration is shaped by four changes in Britain itself. The World Wars turned foreigners into a security question: the Aliens Restriction Act was passed the day after war was declared in August 1914 and made aliens register with the police, the Aliens Restriction (Amendment) Act of 1919 carried those controls into peacetime, and in 1940 thousands of German, Austrian and Italian 'enemy aliens' were interned, many on the Isle of Man; the Arandora Star, carrying internees to Canada, was torpedoed on 2 July 1940 and hundreds drowned. Decolonisation and the Commonwealth changed who had a claim on Britain: the British Nationality Act of 1948 made people born in Britain and its colonies citizens of the United Kingdom and Colonies, while people from the independent Commonwealth countries were Commonwealth citizens, and both could enter, work and settle freely, at the very moment India, Pakistan and then Ghana in 1957 and Kenya in 1963 were becoming independent. EU membership from 1973, and the enlargement of 2004 when Britain was one of only three existing members to open its labour market at once, created a right of free movement, which the referendum of 2016 began the process of ending and which ran until 31 December 2020, the close of the post-Brexit transition period. Running through all of it is the law: the Aliens Act of 1905, the first peacetime immigration control, then the Commonwealth Immigrants Acts of 1962 and 1968 and the Immigration Act of 1971. A top-level answer treats the law as both cause and effect - each Act is evidence of pressure that already existed and a cause of the migration pattern that followed."},{"code":"13-4 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in Britain","h":"The experience: hostility, the colour bar, campaigning and the Race Relations Acts","t":"Two things happen at once and a good answer holds both. There is persistent hostility: Oswald Mosley's British Union of Fascists marching on the Jewish East End until anti-fascists blocked them at Cable Street on 4 October 1936; Italian shops attacked after Italy declared war in June 1940; white crowds attacking Caribbean homes in Nottingham and in Notting Hill in late August and early September 1958; the murder of the Antiguan carpenter Kelso Cochrane in Notting Hill in May 1959; Enoch Powell's Birmingham speech of 20 April 1968; the National Front in the 1970s and the killing of Blair Peach at a Southall demonstration in April 1979; the murder of Stephen Lawrence on 22 April 1993, after which the Macpherson Report of February 1999 called the Metropolitan Police institutionally racist. There is also organisation and redress: the colour bar in pubs, lodgings and jobs was legal until Parliament acted, so the Race Relations Act of 1965 banned discrimination in public places and made incitement to racial hatred an offence, the Act of 1968 extended the ban to housing, employment and services, and the Act of 1976 outlawed indirect discrimination and created the Commission for Racial Equality. The media fed both sides, from the reporting of 1958 to Margaret Thatcher's remark in January 1978 about people fearing to be swamped. A top-level answer argues about the relationship between them: it shows that the law usually followed campaigning by migrants themselves, such as the Bristol bus boycott of 1963 and the Grunwick strike led by Jayaben Desai from 1976, rather than leading it."},{"code":"13-4 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in Britain","h":"The impact: culture, politics, the urban environment, public services and the economy","t":"The specification names five headings and the examiner expects evidence under each. Culture: Claudia Jones's indoor Caribbean carnival at St Pancras Town Hall in January 1959 and the Notting Hill street carnival from 1966, now the largest street festival in Europe; ska, reggae and bhangra; the curry house on the high street, and the chicken tikka masala that the Foreign Secretary Robin Cook called a true British national dish in a speech of 19 April 2001. Politics: Diane Abbott, Paul Boateng, Bernie Grant and Keith Vaz all entered the Commons at the general election of 11 June 1987, and Learie Constantine had become the first black peer in 1969. The urban environment: the building on Brick Lane that was a Huguenot chapel in 1743, a synagogue from 1898 and the Jamme Masjid mosque from 1976 is the single best example, alongside the Swaminarayan temple at Neasden, opened in 1995, and Leicester's Belgrave Road. Public services: the NHS opened on 5 July 1948 and recruited nurses in the Caribbean and doctors trained in India and Pakistan, and it has depended on migrant staff ever since; London Transport recruited in Barbados from 1956 with fares lent to recruits and repaid out of their wages. The economy: migrants filled the post-war labour shortage in transport, textiles, foundries and hospitals, and later opened shops, restaurants and factories. A top-level answer does not list these: it weighs them, usually by asking whether an impact was the most visible or the one Britain could least have done without."},{"code":"13-4 — 3 Case studies: Bristol in the mid-twentieth century","h":"Case study: Bristol in the mid-twentieth century","t":"Bristol is the case study for how a colour bar worked and how it was broken. The city's wealth had been built partly on the transatlantic slave trade, and Edward Colston's statue stood in the centre until it was pulled down in June 2020. After 1945 Caribbean migrants, many Jamaican, settled in St Paul's and Montpelier because that is where rooms could be had; Polish servicemen settled under the Polish Resettlement Act of 1947. The Bristol Omnibus Company refused to employ black or Asian crews and the local branch of the Transport and General Workers' Union supported the ban, which was entirely legal. In 1963 the West Indian Development Council - Roy Hackett, Owen Henry, Audley Evans and Prince Brown - with the youth worker Paul Stephenson as spokesman, used Guy Bailey's cancelled interview as a test case and called a boycott of the buses. Students marched, the Bristol South East MP Tony Benn backed it and Learie Constantine, then High Commissioner for Trinidad and Tobago, intervened. On 28 August 1963 the company gave way, and Raghbir Singh became its first non-white conductor that September. St Paul's Carnival began in 1968. A top-level answer uses Bristol for two arguments: that the law of the land permitted discrimination until migrants forced the issue, and that the boycott helped make the Race Relations Act of 1965 possible."},{"code":"13-4 — 3 Case studies: The experience of Asian migrants in Leicester from 1945","h":"Case study: Asian migrants in Leicester from 1945","t":"Leicester shows pull, expulsion and impact in one city. Its hosiery, knitwear, footwear and engineering firms were short of workers in the 1950s and 1960s, and Gujarati Indians and Punjabi Sikhs came for that work. Africanisation in independent Kenya from 1967 pushed out Asian traders holding British passports, and the Commonwealth Immigrants Act of 1968 was rushed through Parliament in three days, from 27 February to 1 March 1968, to limit their entry. In August 1972 Idi Amin ordered Uganda's Asian population to leave within ninety days; about 27,000 came to Britain and a Uganda Resettlement Board was set up. Leicester City Council placed an advertisement in the Uganda Argus in September 1972 telling them the city's housing and schools were full. They came anyway, because relatives and work were already there. Asian workers then had to fight for equal treatment at work: the Imperial Typewriters strike of 1974 was led mainly by East African Asian women and was not backed by their union at first. The impact is visible: Belgrave Road's sari shops and jewellers, Diwali lights that are among the largest celebrations outside India, the Jain Centre consecrated in 1988, and a 2011 census in which white British people were under half the city's population and more than a quarter of residents described themselves as Indian. A top-level answer uses Leicester to show that government policy did not decide settlement patterns - families and jobs did."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 3: one difference, two periods, four marks","setup":"The paper prints: 'Explain one way in which the reasons for migration to Britain in the years c800-c1500 were different from the reasons for migration to Britain in the years c1900-present.' (4 marks) Two of the marks are for analysing a difference and two for the specific information that supports it, and you must say something about both periods. From your notes: in the earlier period Viking armies settled the Danelaw, the Normans conquered after Hastings and William I brought Jewish financiers over from Rouen; in the modern period people have come for work, for refuge and to join relatives.","steps":["Name the difference in one sentence before writing any detail: early migrants largely arrived by conquest or at a ruler's invitation, whereas modern migrants decide for themselves inside rules set by Parliament.","Anchor the earlier half with something markable: Viking armies took and farmed the Danelaw, Norman landholders replaced English ones after 1066, and William I invited Jewish moneylenders from Rouen because Christians were forbidden to lend at interest.","Anchor the modern half with the same precision: Caribbean migrants paid their own passages once the British Nationality Act of 1948 gave them a right of entry, and Gujarati families chose Leicester because its hosiery and engineering firms were hiring.","Turn the contrast into analysis in the last sentence: what has changed is who decides, so the state has moved from being the inviter to being the gatekeeper."],"cues":["What single difference are you claiming, in one sentence?","Which medieval facts prove the earlier half of that claim?","Which modern facts prove the other half?","What does your last sentence still have to add that the two halves of evidence have not already said?"]},{"title":"Question 4: turning two bullet points into three reasons","setup":"The paper prints: 'Explain why the British government restricted Commonwealth immigration in the years 1962-1971. You may use the following in your answer: the Commonwealth Immigrants Act 1962; Enoch Powell's speech of 1968. You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks) The marks are AO2 6 and AO1 6, and the mark scheme gives a maximum of 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, to answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","steps":["Read each bullet as evidence for a reason rather than as a topic: the voucher system of 1962 is evidence that ministers had come to treat numbers themselves as the problem, and Powell's Birmingham speech of 20 April 1968 is evidence of public and press pressure.","Now write down the reason neither bullet offers, which is decolonisation: Ghana became independent in 1957 and Kenya in 1963, so the right of entry granted by the British Nationality Act of 1948 belonged to people in countries Britain no longer governed.","Give each reason a paragraph and prove it with something dated - employment vouchers in the first Act, the three days from 27 February to 1 March 1968 in which the second Commonwealth Immigrants Act was rushed through against Kenyan Asians, and the patriality rule of the Immigration Act 1971.","Finish with the sentence that links them: pressure set the timing, the loss of empire set the direction, and every Act made the next one easier to pass."],"cues":["What general reason is each bullet actually evidence for?","What was happening to the Empire in exactly these years that neither bullet mentions?","What dated detail will you attach to each of the three reasons?","How do the three reasons fit together into one argument?"]},{"title":"Question 5: choosing the criterion before you argue","setup":"The paper prints: 'Refugees were treated better than migrants who came to Britain to work in the years c1900-present.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• Hungarian refugees (1956)\n• the colour bar\nYou must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nLevel 4 requires that criteria for the judgement are justified and applied. Your notes cover the Belgians of 1914 and the Hungarians of 1956, the closed door of the 1930s and the Kindertransport, the dispersal of asylum seekers after 1999, and the right of entry and the colour bar that met Commonwealth workers.","steps":["Decide in the opening what 'better' will mean, because there is more than one defensible test: the welcome on arrival, the security of the legal status, or the material help the state actually provided.","Test the claim on the refugees who were welcomed: about a quarter of a million Belgians came during the First World War and a War Refugees Committee with more than 2,500 local committees found them homes and work, and about 21,000 Hungarians were admitted within weeks of the Soviet suppression of the 1956 uprising.","Test the strongest evidence against it, which is the refugees Britain would not take: entry was controlled under the Aliens Restriction (Amendment) Act of 1919 and the Aliens Order of 1920, and in the 1930s the government would not admit Jewish refugees in numbers, so the Kindertransport that brought nearly 10,000 children from December 1938 left their parents behind and required a sponsor's guarantee of fifty pounds a child; after the Immigration and Asylum Act of 1999 asylum seekers were dispersed to allocated towns and supported with vouchers rather than cash.","Add the point neither bullet offers, which is what the workers had and did not have: Commonwealth migrants arrived with a legal right to enter, work and settle that no refugee was ever given, under the British Nationality Act of 1948, and yet met a colour bar in lodgings, pubs and jobs that stayed perfectly lawful until the Race Relations Act of 1965.","Judge on the test you set, and say which test you are using: refugees were more warmly received when they arrived, but the workers held the stronger legal position, so the answer depends on whether 'better' means the welcome or the rights."],"cues":["What would make one group's treatment better than another's? Name your test.","Which refugees were welcomed, and what dated evidence proves it?","When was Britain least willing to take refugees, and what does that do to the claim?","What did the Commonwealth workers have that no refugee had, and what did they meet that refugees did not?","Which way does your own test point, and will you say so plainly?"]},{"title":"Using a case study as precise evidence: Leicester","setup":"An examiner's report complains that answers about Leicester say only that 'a lot of Asian people moved there'. Your notes hold: Gujarati Indians recruited into hosiery and engineering from the 1950s; Asians leaving Kenya from 1967; Idi Amin's order of August 1972 giving Uganda's Asians ninety days to go; about 27,000 of them reaching Britain; Leicester City Council's advertisement in the Uganda Argus in September 1972; the Imperial Typewriters strike of 1974; Belgrave Road's shops and Diwali lights; and a 2011 census in which white British people were under half the city's population. Write the sentences that would score.","steps":["Choose the fact that carries an actor, a date and a number at once: Amin ordered Uganda's Asians out in August 1972, allowing ninety days, and about 27,000 of them reached Britain.","Pair it with the fact that shows what Britain did about it, so the sentence analyses rather than narrates: Leicester City Council advertised in the Uganda Argus in September 1972 telling them to stay away, and they came anyway because relatives and jobs were already in the city.","Add the consequence, which is what an impact question actually wants: by the 2011 census white British people were under half of Leicester's population, Belgrave Road had become a street of sari shops and jewellers, and its Diwali lights are among the largest celebrations outside India.","Check each sentence against the grid: every one names an actor, a date and an outcome, and the last turns description into a claim about change."],"cues":["Which single fact here carries a person, a date and a figure together?","What did the receiving city do, and did it work?","What is the measurable result you could put at the end?","What must every sentence contain before it earns an AO1 mark?"]},{"title":"Getting past the bullet points on a 12-mark question","setup":"The paper prints: 'Explain why migration from Poland to Britain increased in the years 1945-2004. You may use the following in your answer: the Polish Resettlement Act 1947; the enlargement of the European Union in 2004. You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks) The mark scheme gives a maximum of 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, to an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content, so at least one reason must come from outside the bullets.","steps":["Write down what the bullets already give you, so that you do not spend a paragraph repeating them: a Parliament willing to provide for the Poles who had fought under British command, and a right of free movement created when ten states joined the European Union on 1 May 2004. Both are moments when the law allowed Poles to stay or to come, and a moment is not yet a reason for a rise in numbers.","Take the first reason from outside the bullets, which is the push that put the Act there at all: the Yalta agreement of February 1945 left Poland inside the Soviet sphere and Britain withdrew recognition from the Polish government in exile in July 1945, so men who had fought with the Allies could not safely go home; Ernest Bevin announced the Polish Resettlement Corps on 22 May 1946 to hold them while they learned English and a trade, and the Polish Resettlement Act that followed on 27 March 1947, usually called Britain's first mass immigration law, provided their pensions, camps, schooling and health care.","Take the second reason from outside the bullets, which is the pull that operated at both ends of the period: post-war Britain was short of workers and the Corps trained its men for civilian trades, while after 2004 Polish workers became central to agriculture, food processing, construction and hospitality - and by then they were coming to the parishes, clubs and shops the first generation had built, which is why the census of 1951 counted about 162,000 people born in Poland and the census of 2011 counted 579,000 in England and Wales, up from 58,000 in 2001.","Write the linking sentence that makes these an argument rather than a list: Soviet control of Poland decided that the first Poles stayed, British labour demand decided that both generations were wanted, and Parliament did no more than open the door each time - in 1947 for soldiers who could not go back, and in 2004 when Britain was one of only three existing members to open its labour market at once."],"cues":["What do the two bullets give you between them, and is a date on its own a reason?","Why could the Polish servicemen of 1945 not simply go home when the war ended?","What was pulling Poles to Britain rather than anywhere else, at each end of the period?","What one sentence ties the push, the pull and the law into a single line of reasoning?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which Act first gave officials the power to refuse entry to arrivals judged 'undesirable', and in which year was it passed?","a":"The Aliens Act, 1905 - the first peacetime control on immigration to Britain."},{"q":"Name the two Commonwealth Immigrants Acts and say what each required of an arrival.","a":"1962 required an employment voucher; 1968 required a parent or grandparent born in the United Kingdom."},{"q":"What did the Race Relations Act of 1968 add to the Act of 1965?","a":"It extended the ban on racial discrimination from public places to housing, employment and services, and set up the Community Relations Commission."},{"q":"Why did Leicester City Council advertise in the Uganda Argus in September 1972, and what was the result?","a":"To discourage expelled Ugandan Asians from settling in Leicester; thousands came anyway because relatives and work in hosiery and engineering were already there."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 1 Section B is worth 36 marks: Question 3, 'Explain one way in which ... was similar to / different from ...' (4 marks); Question 4, 'Explain why ...' (12 marks); and a choice of Question 5 or Question 6, a judgement on a quotation (16 marks, plus up to 4 additional marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology).","Questions 4, 5 and 6 print two stimulus points and the line 'You must also use information of your own.' The caps are printed in the mark scheme: on the 12-mark question, 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'; on the 16-mark question, 'Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and again no access to Level 4.","Question 3 normally spans at least a century and may cross the key topics of the whole option, so name the period on each side of the comparison ('in the years c1700-c1900 ... whereas since c1900 ...') and put specific evidence in both halves; two of the four marks are for that supporting information.","Paper 1 allows 1 hour 20 minutes for 52 marks, which is about a minute a mark with planning time on top: roughly 6 minutes on Question 3, 18 on Question 4 and 30 on the 16-mark judgement, keeping the last two or three minutes of it to check spelling, punctuation and the specialist terms the extra 4 marks depend on.","The 16-mark question asks for a judgement about the nature, pattern, process or impact of change, so the opening must state the criterion the judgement will use and the conclusion must apply it: Level 4 requires that 'criteria for the required judgement are justified and applied in the process of reaching the overall judgement'."],"questions":[{"q":"Explain one way in which the reasons why migrants came to Britain in the years c1700-c1900 were similar to the reasons why migrants came to Britain in the years c1900-present. (4 marks)","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2. Credit one analysed similarity, most obviously that in both periods the main pull was work that Britain could not supply from its own population, supported by specific detail from each period.","sol":"One similarity is that in both periods most migrants were pulled by work Britain could not fill from its own population. Between c1700 and c1900 Irish labourers dug the canals, laid the railway lines and took mill work in Liverpool and Manchester, and Jewish arrivals after the pogroms of 1881 went into East End tailoring and shoemaking. After 1945 the same pull operated, only now through deliberate recruitment: the new NHS took on Caribbean nurses, London Transport advertised in Barbados from 1956 with fares lent to recruits and repaid out of their wages, and Yorkshire mills hired men from Mirpur for night shifts. In both periods it was employers short of labour, not governments, who drove the movement.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what the Irish navvy of the 1840s and the Barbadian bus conductor of the 1950s had in common before you think about anything else.","Decide the single similarity first, then find one hard fact from each period to prove it, because two of the four marks are for that evidence.","Shape it as four sentences: the similarity, the earlier evidence, the modern evidence, and a closing line saying who was really driving the movement."],"codes":["13-4 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the British government's control of migration in the years c1500-c1700 was different from its control of migration in the years c1900-present. (4 marks)","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2. Credit one analysed difference, most obviously the move from occasional royal decisions about particular groups to permanent statute law enforced by officials, with specific evidence from both periods.","sol":"One difference is that control moved from occasional royal decisions to permanent statute law. Between c1500 and c1700 there was no general immigration law at all: the monarch acted case by case, so Cromwell allowed Jews to return in 1656 and Charles II's proclamation of 1681 invited Huguenot refugees in, while other newcomers simply arrived unrecorded. Since c1900 entry has been governed by Acts of Parliament enforced by officials: the Aliens Act of 1905 created immigration officers who could turn back arrivals judged undesirable, and the Commonwealth Immigrants Acts of 1962 and 1968 tied entry to employment vouchers and then to ancestry. Control became routine, bureaucratic and permanent.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about who did the deciding in each period, and whether the decision was about one group or about everybody.","You need one named royal decision from the earlier period and one named Act from the modern one, so the comparison rests on evidence rather than assertion.","End on the analysis rather than the facts: say what kind of thing control became, not just that there was more of it."],"codes":["13-4 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of British society"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the settlement patterns of migrants in the years c1700-c1900 were similar to the settlement patterns of migrants in the years c1900-present. (4 marks)","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2. Credit one analysed similarity, most obviously clustering in a few cheap inner districts of industrial cities and ports close to the work, with named districts from both periods.","sol":"One similarity is that migrants in both periods settled where the work was and where housing was cheapest, which concentrated them in a few inner districts of industrial cities and ports. Between c1700 and c1900 the Irish crowded into the north end of Liverpool and into Manchester's Little Ireland, and Jewish arrivals after 1881 filled Whitechapel and Spitalfields in London's East End. After 1948 Caribbean migrants took rooms in Brixton, in Bristol's St Paul's and in Moss Side, and Pakistani mill workers settled in Manningham in Bradford. In both periods clustering also put shops, places of worship and mutual aid within walking distance, which pulled later arrivals to the same streets.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Two forces decide where a newcomer with no savings lives: where the jobs are and what the rent is.","Name actual districts rather than cities, because the specific information is worth two of the four marks.","Your last sentence should explain why the clustering kept going once it had started, which is the analysis the grid rewards."],"codes":["13-4 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the skills brought by migrants to Britain in the years c1500-c1700 were similar to the skills brought by migrants in the years c1900-present. (4 marks)","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2. Credit one analysed similarity, most obviously that in both periods migrants brought training Britain was short of and had deliberately sought, which went straight into a trade or service the country could not staff for itself, supported by named groups from each period.","sol":"One similarity is that in both periods migrants brought training Britain lacked and had deliberately sought out. Between c1500 and c1700 the Dutch and Walloon 'Strangers', who settled at Sandwich from 1561 and Norwich from 1565, brought the techniques of the New Draperies to a collapsing worsted trade, and the Huguenots who came after Louis XIV revoked the Edict of Nantes in 1685 brought silk-weaving from Tours and Lyons to Spitalfields. Since c1900 the same has been true of the professions: the NHS, open from 5 July 1948, was staffed by nurses recruited in the Caribbean and by doctors trained in India and Pakistan. In both periods the skill arrived because an English trade or service could not staff itself.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what the receiving trade or service could not do for itself before the migrants arrived.","Pick one named group with a date from each period, rather than writing generally about hard work.","Finish by naming the pattern the two halves share, so the comparison is analysed and not merely described."],"codes":["13-4 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in Britain","13-4 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which the treatment of refugees in the years c1500-c1700 was different from the treatment of refugees in the years c1900-present. (4 marks)","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2. Credit one analysed difference, most obviously that refugees were once invited and subsidised as an economic asset whereas modern refuge is rationed and managed by the state, with specific evidence from both periods.","sol":"One difference is that refugees were once invited as an asset and are now admitted only on conditions set by the state. Between c1500 and c1700 Protestant refugees were encouraged: Dutch and Walloon Strangers were settled at Sandwich and Norwich to teach new kinds of cloth-making, and Charles II's proclamation of 1681 offered Huguenots denization while church collections raised money for them. Since c1900 refuge has been rationed. The Kindertransport of 1938-39 brought nearly 10,000 mostly Jewish children, but only where a sponsor guaranteed fifty pounds and the parents stayed behind, and the Immigration and Asylum Act of 1999 dispersed asylum seekers to allocated towns on vouchers.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what the government of each period thought a refugee was for.","You need one welcoming measure from the earlier period and one condition attached to modern refuge; both need naming.","The difference is not that there are fewer refugees now, so make sure your last sentence is about the terms of admission."],"codes":["13-4 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","13-4 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of British society"]},{"q":"Explain why the British government began to control immigration in the years 1905-1971.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Aliens Act 1905\n• the Commonwealth Immigrants Act 1962\nYou must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit reasons analysed as causal chains: organised public and press pressure against particular arrivals; the machinery of wartime control of aliens surviving into peacetime; and decolonisation turning the 1948 right of entry into an obligation ministers would not accept. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Control came because particular arrivals were made into a public problem, because two wars taught the state to police foreigners, and because the end of empire turned an open door into an obligation ministers no longer wanted.\n\nThe first reason was organised pressure at home. The Aliens Act of 1905 followed a campaign in the East End against Jewish refugees from the Russian Empire, in which the British Brothers' League, founded in 1901, blamed newcomers for overcrowding and low wages; the Act created immigration officers who could turn back arrivals judged undesirable. The pattern repeated half a century later. The riots in Nottingham and Notting Hill in late August and early September 1958 were used as evidence that numbers themselves caused disorder, and the Commonwealth Immigrants Act of 1962 confined entry to holders of employment vouchers.\n\nThe second reason was war. The Aliens Restriction Act was passed the day after Britain declared war in August 1914 and required aliens to register with the police; the Aliens Restriction (Amendment) Act of 1919 carried those controls into peacetime and barred former enemy aliens from certain kinds of work. Once the state had machinery for registering foreigners it never dismantled it, so the question after each war was how much control to keep, not whether to have any.\n\nThe third reason, which neither bullet supplies, was decolonisation. The British Nationality Act of 1948 had given citizens of the colonies and Commonwealth the right to enter. India and Pakistan were already independent, Ghana followed in 1957 and Kenya in 1963, so the right of entry now belonged to millions of people in countries Britain no longer governed. The 1968 Act, pushed through in three days from 27 February to 1 March to keep out Kenyan Asians, demanded a parent or grandparent born in the United Kingdom, and the Immigration Act of 1971 replaced vouchers with work permits and the patriality rule.\n\nSo pressure supplied the demand and the timing, war supplied the machinery, and the loss of empire supplied the reason ministers were willing to use both.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Each bullet is standing in for a general reason; work out what the 1905 Act is evidence of before you write about it.","Three reasons, each a paragraph that states it, proves it with a dated Act or episode, and then explains how it pushed towards control - and one reason must come from outside the bullets.","For the reason of your own, ask what was happening to the Empire between 1947 and 1963 and what that did to the promise made in 1948."],"codes":["13-4 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of British society","13-4 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement"]},{"q":"Explain why migration from the Caribbean and South Asia to Britain increased in the years 1948-1962.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the British Nationality Act 1948\n• post-war labour shortages\nYou must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit reasons for the increase analysed as causal chains: the legal right of entry created in 1948; the labour shortage and active recruitment by British employers; push factors and closed alternatives in the Caribbean and South Asia; and the rush to arrive before the 1962 Act closed the door. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Three things had to be true at once for the movement to grow: Britain had to be open, Britain had to be hiring, and leaving had to look better than staying.\n\nThe first reason was legal. The British Nationality Act of 1948 made people born in Britain and its colonies citizens of the United Kingdom and Colonies, and left people from the independent Commonwealth countries as Commonwealth citizens; both had the right to enter, work and settle. No visa, quota or permission was needed, so a Jamaican or a Punjabi who could raise the fare could simply come. The Empire Windrush, which docked at Tilbury on 22 June 1948, carried several hundred such passengers, many of them men who had served with British forces and knew the country already.\n\nThe second reason was that Britain was actively recruiting. The war had cost the country workers and rebuilding needed more. The NHS opened on 5 July 1948 and took on Caribbean nurses and doctors trained in India and Pakistan; London Transport recruited in Barbados from 1956, recruits' fares lent and repaid from wages; the textile mills of Yorkshire and Lancashire and the foundries of the West Midlands wanted men for shifts local workers were leaving.\n\nThe third reason was the push. Partition in 1947 had uprooted millions in India and Pakistan, wages in rural Punjab, Gujarat and Mirpur were low, and hurricane damage in Jamaica in 1951 destroyed homes and crops. Crucially, the usual alternative closed: the United States' McCarran-Walter Act of 1952 cut West Indian entry to a token quota, so Britain became the obvious destination instead.\n\nA fourth reason, beyond the bullets, is that restriction itself accelerated arrivals. Once it became clear that Parliament intended to limit Commonwealth entry, families brought forward journeys they had been planning, and the years immediately before the Commonwealth Immigrants Act of 1962 saw some of the heaviest arrivals of the whole period.\n\nSo the law opened the door, employers advertised through it, hardship and a closed American alternative pushed people towards it, and the threat of closure made them hurry.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Sort your material into push and pull before you write, and then ask what made Britain the destination rather than anywhere else.","Three or four reasons, each with dated evidence and an explanation of how it produced movement, with at least one reason the bullets do not mention.","For the reason of your own, think about what people do when they know a door is about to be shut."],"codes":["13-4 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","13-4 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of British society"]},{"q":"Explain why the experience of migrants in Britain changed in the years 1948-1976.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Notting Hill riots (1958)\n• the Race Relations Act 1965\nYou must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit reasons analysed as causal chains: hostility that made the problem public; campaigning by migrants themselves, notably the Bristol bus boycott of 1963; and the narrowness of the first Race Relations Act, which forced the further Acts of 1968 and 1976. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The experience changed because hostility made discrimination impossible to ignore, because migrants organised to attack it themselves, and because Parliament's first attempt to outlaw it was too narrow to work.\n\nThe first reason was open hostility. A colour bar operated legally in lodgings, pubs and workplaces, and in late August and early September 1958 white crowds attacked Caribbean homes in Nottingham and in Notting Hill; the Antiguan carpenter Kelso Cochrane was murdered in Notting Hill in May 1959 and nobody was convicted. This mattered for the experience of migrants in two opposite ways: it made daily life more frightening, and it forced politicians and newspapers to admit that a problem existed which they had previously denied.\n\nThe second reason was action by migrants themselves. In Bristol the Omnibus Company refused to employ black or Asian crews, with the support of the local branch of the Transport and General Workers' Union. The West Indian Development Council, with Paul Stephenson as its spokesman, used the cancelled interview of Guy Bailey as a test case and called a boycott of the buses in 1963; students marched, the Bristol South East MP Tony Benn backed it, and on 28 August 1963 the company gave way. Raghbir Singh became its first non-white conductor that September. The boycott showed that a colour bar could be broken without a law, and it strengthened the argument that there ought to be one.\n\nThe third reason, beyond the bullets, is that the law had to be rewritten twice. The Race Relations Act of 1965 banned discrimination in public places and made incitement to racial hatred an offence, but it did not touch housing or employment, which is where most discrimination actually happened. The Act of 1968 therefore extended the ban to housing, jobs and services, and the Act of 1976 went further, outlawing indirect discrimination and creating the Commission for Racial Equality with power to investigate.\n\nSo hostility exposed the problem, migrants forced it onto the agenda, and Parliament legislated three times because each attempt revealed how much the last one had missed.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["The bullets give you a riot and a statute; ask who stands between the two and actually made the statute happen.","Three reasons, each proved with a date, a name or an Act, and at least one of them from outside the bullets.","Look hard at what the 1965 Act did not cover, and let that explain why the story does not stop in 1965."],"codes":["13-4 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in Britain","13-4 — 3 Case studies: Bristol in the mid-twentieth century"]},{"q":"Explain why the population of Leicester changed in the years 1945-2011.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the hosiery and textile industry\n• East African Asians\nYou must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit reasons analysed as causal chains: the pull of Leicester's labour-hungry industries; Africanisation and the Ugandan expulsion of 1972 pushing East African Asians out; and chain migration to relatives and to established institutions, which defeated the council's attempt to deter settlement. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Leicester changed because its factories were hiring, because events in East Africa forced a whole population out, and because once a community existed it drew more people to it than any council advertisement could deter.\n\nThe first reason was work. Leicester's hosiery, knitwear, footwear and engineering firms were short of labour in the 1950s and 1960s, at a time when the British Nationality Act of 1948 gave Commonwealth citizens the right to enter. Gujarati Indians and Punjabi Sikhs came for jobs that paid more than village agriculture at home, and shift work in the knitwear trade suited families who wanted several wages from one household.\n\nThe second reason was expulsion. Independent Kenya's Africanisation policies from 1967 pushed out Asian traders who held British passports, and the Commonwealth Immigrants Act of 1968 was rushed through Parliament in three days, from 27 February to 1 March 1968, to limit their entry. Then in August 1972 Idi Amin ordered Uganda's Asian population to leave within ninety days; about 27,000 came to Britain, and a Uganda Resettlement Board was set up to house them. These were people with capital, professional skills and shopkeeping experience, not labourers, which shaped what they did once here.\n\nThe third reason, which neither bullet prompts, is that migration follows migration. Leicester City Council advertised in the Uganda Argus in September 1972 warning that its housing and schools were full, and it made no difference: people went where relatives, temples, gurdwaras and familiar shops already were. Belgrave Road grew into a street of sari shops, jewellers and sweet shops, the Jain Centre was consecrated in 1988, and each new institution made the city more attractive to the next arrival. By the census of 2011, white British people were under half of Leicester's population and more than a quarter of residents described themselves as Indian.\n\nSo work brought the first generation, politics in East Africa delivered the second, and the community the first two built brought everybody else.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Separate what pulled people to Leicester from what pushed them out of somewhere else, and then ask what kept the flow going after both.","Three reasons, each with a dated fact attached, and one of them from outside the bullets.","The council tried to stop this happening; work out why it failed, and you have your third reason."],"codes":["13-4 — 3 Case studies: The experience of Asian migrants in Leicester from 1945","13-4 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement"]},{"q":"Explain why there were changes in the number of refugees admitted to Britain in the years 1914-1948.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• Belgian refugees (1914)\n• the Kindertransport (1938-39)\nYou must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit reasons for the rise and fall in the numbers admitted analysed as causal chains: wartime alliance and the propaganda value of sheltering an invaded ally; pressure from voluntary and religious organisations after Kristallnacht; and the post-war labour shortage, which made refuge and self-interest point the same way. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The numbers Britain admitted rose, fell and rose again in these years because war made shelter a national cause, because voluntary bodies had to push a reluctant government into acting, and because after 1945 refuge happened to solve a labour shortage.\n\nThe first reason was the war of 1914. The German invasion of neutral Belgium was the ground on which Britain declared war, so sheltering Belgians was an argument as well as an act of charity. About a quarter of a million Belgians came, the largest single movement of refugees Britain had then seen, and a War Refugees Committee with more than 2,500 local committees found them homes and work. Their presence was used in recruiting propaganda, which is precisely why the government made room for them.\n\nThe second reason was organised pressure on a government that did not want to act. Entry was controlled under the Aliens Restriction (Amendment) Act of 1919 and the Aliens Order of 1920, and Britain was unwilling to admit Jewish refugees in numbers in the 1930s. After the violence of Kristallnacht on 9 and 10 November 1938, Jewish, Quaker and other voluntary organisations lobbied ministers, and the government agreed to admit unaccompanied children without individual visas provided a sponsor guaranteed fifty pounds for each. Nearly 10,000 children came on the Kindertransport between December 1938 and the outbreak of war, while their parents were left behind - the compromise shows that concession, not generosity, was what had been won.\n\nThe third reason, beyond the bullets, is that by 1945 refuge served Britain's own needs. Polish forces had fought alongside British ones and could not safely return to a Soviet-dominated Poland, so the Polish Resettlement Act of 1947 allowed them and their families to stay; at the same time recruiting teams went into the displaced persons camps of Europe and brought workers over for mines, farms, mills and hospitals. A country desperately short of labour found that taking refugees and filling vacancies were the same policy.\n\nSo alliance made refuge patriotic, campaigning made it possible, and the labour shortage made it welcome.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask in each case what Britain got out of admitting these people, because that is usually why a government acts.","Three reasons, each anchored to a date, an Act or a named organisation, with one reason drawn from outside the bullets.","Look at what was happening to Britain's workforce in 1946 and 1947, and what that did to the argument about refugees."],"codes":["13-4 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","13-4 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of British society"]},{"q":"Explain why migration changed the appearance and life of British cities in the years c1900-present.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• places of worship\n• Notting Hill Carnival\nYou must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit reasons analysed as causal chains: clustered settlement near work and cheap housing creating concentrated communities; the need for institutions of worship, trade and mutual support; and the reuse of existing buildings and streets by successive groups. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Cities changed because migrants settled in concentrated districts, because a concentrated community builds its own institutions, and because those institutions were mostly made out of buildings that were already there.\n\nThe first reason is where people could afford to live. Newcomers took the cheapest rooms nearest the work, so Caribbean migrants filled Brixton, St Paul's in Bristol and Moss Side, Pakistani mill workers filled Manningham in Bradford, and Gujarati families filled the streets around Belgrave Road in Leicester. Concentration is what made the change visible: a scattered population changes nothing you can see, while a concentrated one changes a high street.\n\nThe second reason is that a settled community needs institutions. Places of worship came first, because they were needed weekly: the Swaminarayan temple at Neasden, opened in 1995, was built in traditional stone, while most mosques, gurdwaras and temples began in converted houses and chapels. Then came the shops, bakeries, barbers, travel agents and newspapers that made a district self-sufficient, and finally the public celebrations - Claudia Jones's indoor Caribbean carnival at St Pancras Town Hall in January 1959 grew into the Notting Hill street carnival from 1966, now the largest in Europe, and Leicester's Diwali lights on Belgrave Road are among the biggest celebrations outside India.\n\nThe third reason, which the bullets do not prompt, is reuse. Britain's cities were already built, so migrants inherited rather than replaced. The clearest example stands on Brick Lane in east London: a chapel built for Huguenots in 1743, a synagogue for Jewish arrivals from 1898, and the Jamme Masjid mosque since 1976. One building has served three migrant communities in succession, which is why the change to British cities has been cumulative rather than a series of replacements.\n\nSo settlement concentrated people, concentration produced institutions, and reuse gave those institutions premises - which is why the mark migration has left on cities is permanent and layered.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Start from where a newcomer with no savings could actually live, and follow the consequences outwards.","Three reasons, each supported by a named building, district or event with a date, and one of them from outside the bullets.","Think about what migrants did with the buildings that were already standing, and find the one on Brick Lane that has been three things."],"codes":["13-4 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in Britain","13-4 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement"]},{"q":"'The main reason people migrated to Britain in the years c1900-present was the search for work.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Empire Windrush (1948)\n• refugees from Nazi Germany\nYou must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit a criteria-based judgement weighing economic motives (recruitment, labour shortage, EU enlargement) against flight from persecution and expulsion, and against family reunion and the effect of immigration law on where people could go, reaching a substantiated overall judgement. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Whether work was the main reason depends on what 'main' is asked to mean. On the test of numbers, work wins easily. On the test of what actually decided an individual journey, it does not, because for large groups the decision was made for them. I shall argue that work explains the size of modern migration while law and persecution explain its timing and its shape.\n\nThe case for the statement is strong. The British Nationality Act of 1948 gave colonial and Commonwealth citizens the right to enter, and British employers used it: the NHS opened on 5 July 1948 and recruited Caribbean nurses and doctors trained in India and Pakistan; London Transport advertised in Barbados from 1956 with fares lent to recruits and repaid out of their wages; Yorkshire and Lancashire mills and West Midlands foundries hired men from Mirpur and Punjab for shifts local workers were leaving. The Empire Windrush, docking at Tilbury on 22 June 1948, carried several hundred Caribbean passengers, many of them ex-servicemen looking for jobs. Irish workers, the largest overseas-born group in Britain for most of the century, built the motorways and staffed the hospitals, and after ten states joined the European Union on 1 May 2004, with Britain one of only three existing members to open its labour market at once, several hundred thousand Poles came; the 2011 census recorded over half a million Poland-born residents of England and Wales.\n\nAgainst the statement stands flight. About a quarter of a million Belgians came during the First World War with nothing to sell but their need; nearly 10,000 mostly Jewish children arrived on the Kindertransport in 1938-39 after Kristallnacht, admitted only because sponsors guaranteed fifty pounds each; Polish servicemen who could not safely return home were allowed to settle by the Polish Resettlement Act of 1947. Later came Hungarians in 1956, Vietnamese from 1979, and Bosnians and Kosovars in the 1990s. The sharpest case is Uganda: when Idi Amin ordered the Asian population out in August 1972 with ninety days' notice, the roughly 27,000 who reached Britain were not searching for work at all. They rebuilt their livings afterwards, as the shops of Belgrave Road in Leicester show.\n\nThe point that goes beyond both bullets is the law. Work was a constant motive, but statute decided whether it could be acted on and by whom. The Commonwealth Immigrants Act of 1962 confined entry to voucher holders, the Act of 1968 demanded a parent or grandparent born in the United Kingdom, and the Immigration Act of 1971 ended primary labour migration from the Commonwealth, after which the main legal route was joining relatives. From the 1970s, therefore, family was the reason most people could give at the border, whatever they intended to do on arrival. The McCarran-Walter Act of 1952, which closed the American route, had already shown the same thing: law, not desire, chose the destination.\n\nI agree with the statement on the criterion of scale and disagree on the criterion of decision. Most people who came to modern Britain came, in the end, because there was work and somebody wanted them to do it. But refuge explains the movements that arrived suddenly and involuntarily, and the law explains why the search for work produced this pattern of migration rather than another.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Before agreeing, decide what would make one reason 'main' - the number of people it moved, or the fact that without it they would not have come at all.","Build the case for work first with recruitment evidence, then set refugee movements against it, and make sure one of your points comes from outside the bullets.","The third factor to look for is not a motive at all but a permission: ask what happened to the legal routes after 1962."],"codes":["13-4 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","13-4 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of British society","13-4 — 3 Case studies: The experience of Asian migrants in Leicester from 1945"]},{"q":"'Government legislation did more than anything else to shape the experience of migrants in Britain in the years c1900-present.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Race Relations Act 1965\n• the media\nYou must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit a criteria-based judgement weighing legislation (the Aliens Acts, internment, the Race Relations Acts) against everyday hostility and the colour bar, the press and politicians' language, and the self-organisation of migrants themselves, reaching a substantiated overall judgement. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Legislation sets the outer limits of what may be done to people; it does not decide what is done to them every day. I shall judge by asking which force explains most about how a migrant in Britain was actually treated, and argue that the law mattered most where it removed a legal permission to discriminate, but that it usually followed pressure rather than creating it.\n\nThe case for the statement is real. Law decided who was even allowed to be here and on what terms: the Aliens Act of 1905 created officers who could refuse entry; the Aliens Restriction (Amendment) Act of 1919 barred former enemy aliens from certain work; in 1940 thousands of German, Austrian and Italian residents were interned as enemy aliens, many on the Isle of Man, and hundreds died when the Arandora Star was torpedoed on 2 July 1940. Law also ended discrimination that had been perfectly lawful. Before 1965 a landlord could refuse a room and a publican a drink on grounds of colour; the Race Relations Act of 1965 outlawed discrimination in public places and made incitement to racial hatred an offence, the Act of 1968 extended it to housing, employment and services, and the Act of 1976 attacked indirect discrimination and created the Commission for Racial Equality. The Windrush scandal of 2018, in which people living here lawfully since before 1973 were detained and even deported for lack of papers, shows how completely law can determine a life; Home Secretary Amber Rudd resigned over it in April 2018.\n\nAgainst that stands everyday hostility, which the law was slow to reach. The colour bar in lodgings, pubs and workplaces was enforced by private people, not by statute. White crowds attacked Caribbean homes in Nottingham and Notting Hill in late August and early September 1958; Kelso Cochrane was murdered in Notting Hill in May 1959 and nobody was convicted; Stephen Lawrence was murdered in Eltham on 22 April 1993, and the Macpherson Report of February 1999 concluded that the Metropolitan Police were institutionally racist - thirty-four years after the first Race Relations Act. The media and politicians shaped the climate, from the reporting of 1958 to Enoch Powell's Birmingham speech of 20 April 1968, after which dockers marched in his support, and Margaret Thatcher's remark in January 1978 that people feared being swamped.\n\nThe factor neither bullet offers is the action of migrants themselves, and it is the one that connects the others. Legislation did not arrive unprompted. The Bristol bus boycott of 1963 broke the Omnibus Company's colour bar on 28 August 1963 without any law at all, and strengthened the argument that there should be one; Jayaben Desai led the Grunwick strikers from 1976, and Rock Against Racism and the Anti-Nazi League mobilised against the National Front in the later 1970s.\n\nSo I agree in part. Legislation was decisive in one respect no other factor matched: only Parliament could remove the legal right to discriminate, and until 1965 and 1968 it had not. But the law reached the street late, it was usually the product of campaigning rather than its cause, and daily experience was set by employers, landlords, neighbours and newspapers. Legislation was the most powerful single instrument; it was not the most powerful influence.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what only a law can do that nothing else can, and then ask how much of daily life a law actually touches.","Weigh statute against hostility and the press, and make sure one factor comes from outside the bullets entirely.","The missing factor is the people the question is about: look for what migrants did for themselves before 1965 and after 1976."],"codes":["13-4 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in Britain","13-4 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of British society","13-4 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in Britain"]},{"q":"'The years 1948-1962 were the most significant turning point in the history of migration to Britain in the years c1900-present.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the British Nationality Act 1948\n• the Commonwealth Immigrants Act 1962\nYou must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit a criteria-based judgement weighing 1948-62 against the Aliens Act of 1905, the restrictive Acts of 1968 and 1971, and EU enlargement in 2004, using a stated test of significance and reaching a substantiated overall judgement. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"A turning point is significant if the direction of events afterwards is different from the direction before, and if what came later could not have happened without it. On both tests the years 1948 to 1962 have a strong claim, but they are not the only candidate, and I shall argue that they were the most significant turning point for who came to Britain while 1905 was the more significant turning point for how Britain treats migration.\n\nThe case for the statement is that these fourteen years contain both the opening and the closing of the Commonwealth door. The British Nationality Act of 1948 made people born in Britain and its colonies citizens of the United Kingdom and Colonies, and people from the independent Commonwealth countries kept the right to enter, work and settle as Commonwealth citizens - a right that in principle belonged to hundreds of millions. The Empire Windrush docked at Tilbury on 22 June 1948 and the NHS opened a fortnight later, on 5 July, and immediately began recruiting overseas. What followed built modern Britain: Caribbean communities in Brixton, Moss Side and St Paul's in Bristol, South Asian communities in Bradford, the West Midlands and Leicester. Then the direction reversed. The Commonwealth Immigrants Act of 1962 confined entry to holders of employment vouchers, and no government since has returned to open entry. A period that both creates and destroys a policy is, on any reading, a hinge.\n\nAgainst the statement, 1905 has the better claim on principle. Before the Aliens Act of 1905 Britain had no peacetime immigration control whatever; anyone could land. The Act created immigration officers with power to refuse arrivals judged undesirable, and it established the idea that entry is a privilege the state grants. Everything afterwards - the Aliens Restriction Act of August 1914, its peacetime extension in 1919, and the Acts of 1962, 1968 and 1971 - varies a principle first conceded in 1905.\n\nA second rival is the period 1968 to 1972, which decided who could actually use the 1948 right. The Commonwealth Immigrants Act of 1968, rushed through in three days against Kenyan Asians, required a parent or grandparent born in the United Kingdom, and the Immigration Act of 1971 introduced patriality and work permits, ending primary Commonwealth labour migration. When Idi Amin expelled Uganda's Asians in August 1972, Britain admitted about 27,000 as an exception to rules it had just made.\n\nThe rival the bullets do not offer is 2004. On 1 May ten states joined the European Union and Britain was one of only three existing members to open its labour market at once. By the 2011 census over half a million people born in Poland were living in England and Wales, and Polish had become one of the most widely spoken languages in the country; the referendum of 2016 turned partly on that movement.\n\nMy judgement is that 1948 to 1962 was the most significant turning point for the composition of the British population, because it created communities that every later decade has had to reckon with, and because it set the restrictive direction of all subsequent policy. But it was not the moment control itself began: measured by the principle that the state decides who may enter, 1905 turned the corner.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide what would make one period a bigger turning point than another before you look at any of them.","Set at least two rival periods against the one in the quotation, each with dated evidence, and one of them from outside the bullets.","Ask what Britain could and could not do about immigration in 1904, and what it could do in 1906."],"codes":["13-4 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of British society","13-4 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement"]},{"q":"'There was more change than continuity in the way migrants were treated in Britain in the years c1900-present.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• the Notting Hill riots (1958)\n• the Race Relations Act 1976\nYou must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit a criteria-based judgement weighing change (the Race Relations Acts, Macpherson, political representation) against continuity (recurring hostility towards each new group, state suspicion from 1905 to the Windrush scandal, and migrants' own self-organisation), reaching a substantiated overall judgement. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Treatment has two halves: what the state and the law permit, and what neighbours, employers and newspapers actually do. I shall argue that the first changed almost beyond recognition while the second changed remarkably little, and that the honest answer is therefore change in the law and continuity in the reception.\n\nThe case for change is the law. For most of the period discrimination was lawful: a landlord could refuse a room and a company could refuse a job on grounds of colour, as the Bristol Omnibus Company did with the support of the local Transport and General Workers' Union branch. The Race Relations Act of 1965 outlawed discrimination in public places and made incitement to racial hatred an offence; the Act of 1968 reached housing, employment and services; and the Act of 1976 outlawed indirect discrimination and created the Commission for Racial Equality with power to investigate. Enforcement changed too: after Stephen Lawrence was murdered in Eltham on 22 April 1993, the Macpherson Report of February 1999 found the Metropolitan Police institutionally racist. Representation changed: Learie Constantine became the first black peer in 1969, and Diane Abbott, Paul Boateng, Bernie Grant and Keith Vaz all entered the Commons at the general election of 11 June 1987.\n\nThe case for continuity is the reception each new group met. The charge is always the same - jobs, housing and difference - and only the target changes. Jewish refugees were blamed for East End overcrowding before the Aliens Act of 1905; German shops were attacked in the First World War and thousands of German, Austrian and Italian residents were interned in 1940; white crowds attacked Caribbean homes in Nottingham and in Notting Hill in late August and early September 1958, and Kelso Cochrane was murdered there in May 1959; Enoch Powell's Birmingham speech of 20 April 1968 drew dockers onto the streets in his support; the National Front targeted Asian families in the 1970s; asylum seekers were the target of the 1990s and Eastern Europeans after 2004.\n\nThe point beyond the bullets is that the state's own suspicion is continuous as well. The Aliens Act of 1905 assumed that some arrivals were undesirable and needed sorting; so did the internment of 1940; and so did the Windrush scandal of 2018, when people who had arrived lawfully before 1973 were detained, denied treatment and in some cases deported because they could not produce documents the Home Office had itself destroyed. Home Secretary Amber Rudd resigned in April 2018. A century of law had changed what the state was allowed to say about migrants without changing what it assumed about them.\n\nThere is a continuity on the other side too, and it is a hopeful one: migrants have always organised. Jewish friendly societies, the Indian Workers' Association, the Bristol bus boycott that ended the colour bar on 28 August 1963 and Jayaben Desai's Grunwick strikers of 1976 belong to one tradition.\n\nSo I agree only in part. Judged by what the law permits, the change is enormous, and 1965, 1968 and 1976 are the proof. Judged by what a newly arrived family could expect from its neighbours and from the Home Office, the continuity is more striking than the change - which is why hostility in 2004 sounded so much like hostility in 1904.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Split 'treatment' into two things before you start: what the law allows, and what people actually do.","Give each half its own dated evidence and reach an interim judgement at the end of every paragraph rather than saving it all for the conclusion.","For the point beyond the bullets, ask whether the state's own attitude changed, and look at what happened in 2018."],"codes":["13-4 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in Britain","13-4 — 3 Case studies: Bristol in the mid-twentieth century","13-4 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in Britain"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date did the Empire Windrush dock at Tilbury?","a":"22 June 1948."},{"q":"Which Act of 1948 gave citizens of Britain's colonies and Commonwealth the right to enter and settle in Britain?","a":"The British Nationality Act 1948."},{"q":"In which city did a bus boycott in 1963 force a transport company to end its colour bar?","a":"Bristol - the Bristol Omnibus Company gave way on 28 August 1963."},{"q":"Which country expelled its Asian population in 1972, sending about 27,000 people to Britain?","a":"Uganda, on the orders of Idi Amin."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The Aliens Act 1905","code":"13-4 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of British society","open":false,"back":"Britain's first peacetime immigration control. It created immigration officers who could refuse entry to arrivals judged undesirable, such as the destitute and the diseased. It followed a campaign against Jewish refugees from the Russian Empire."},{"front":"The Aliens Restriction (Amendment) Act 1919","code":"13-4 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of British society","open":false,"back":"It carried wartime controls on foreigners into peacetime: aliens had to register with the police, and former enemy aliens were barred from certain kinds of work. Once built, the machinery of control was never dismantled."},{"front":"Belgian refugees, 1914-18","code":"13-4 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","open":false,"back":"About a quarter of a million Belgians fled to Britain after the German invasion of 1914 - the largest refugee movement Britain had then seen. A War Refugees Committee and more than 2,500 local committees found them homes and work."},{"front":"The Kindertransport, 1938-39","code":"13-4 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","open":false,"back":"After Kristallnacht in November 1938 the government admitted unaccompanied children without individual visas if a sponsor guaranteed fifty pounds each. Nearly 10,000 mostly Jewish children came; their parents were left behind."},{"front":"The Polish Resettlement Act 1947","code":"13-4 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","open":false,"back":"It allowed Polish servicemen who had fought with the Allies, and their families, to stay in Britain rather than return to a Soviet-dominated Poland. It was the first British law providing for the mass settlement of a migrant group."},{"front":"The Empire Windrush, 22 June 1948","code":"13-4 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","open":false,"back":"The ship docked at Tilbury carrying several hundred Caribbean passengers, many of them ex-servicemen coming to work. It became the symbol of post-war Commonwealth migration, though it was neither the first nor the largest arrival."},{"front":"The British Nationality Act 1948","code":"13-4 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of British society","open":false,"back":"It made people born in Britain and its colonies citizens of the United Kingdom and Colonies; people from the independent Commonwealth were Commonwealth citizens. Both could enter, work and settle, so entry needed only the price of a passage."},{"front":"The Commonwealth Immigrants Acts 1962 and 1968","code":"13-4 — 1 The context for migration: The changing context of British society","open":false,"back":"The 1962 Act confined Commonwealth entry to holders of employment vouchers. The 1968 Act, rushed through in three days against Kenyan Asians, required a parent or grandparent born in the United Kingdom."},{"front":"The Race Relations Acts 1965, 1968 and 1976","code":"13-4 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in Britain","open":false,"back":"1965 banned racial discrimination in public places and made incitement to racial hatred an offence; 1968 extended the ban to housing, employment and services; 1976 outlawed indirect discrimination and created the Commission for Racial Equality."},{"front":"The Notting Hill riots, 1958","code":"13-4 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The experience of migrants in Britain","open":false,"back":"In late August and early September 1958 white crowds attacked Caribbean homes in Notting Hill, days after similar violence in Nottingham. Kelso Cochrane was murdered nearby in May 1959 and nobody was convicted."},{"front":"The Bristol bus boycott, 1963","code":"13-4 — 3 Case studies: Bristol in the mid-twentieth century","open":false,"back":"The Bristol Omnibus Company refused to employ black or Asian crews. The West Indian Development Council, with Paul Stephenson as spokesman, called a boycott; the company gave way on 28 August 1963 and Raghbir Singh became its first non-white conductor."},{"front":"The expulsion of Uganda's Asians, 1972","code":"13-4 — 3 Case studies: The experience of Asian migrants in Leicester from 1945","open":false,"back":"In August 1972 Idi Amin ordered Uganda's Asian population to leave within ninety days. About 27,000 came to Britain and a Uganda Resettlement Board was set up; many settled in Leicester despite the council's public discouragement."},{"front":"Belgrave Road, Leicester","code":"13-4 — 3 Case studies: The experience of Asian migrants in Leicester from 1945","open":false,"back":"The street of sari shops, jewellers and sweet shops built by Leicester's Gujarati and East African Asian community. Its Diwali lights are among the largest celebrations outside India."},{"front":"EU enlargement, 1 May 2004","code":"13-4 — 1 The context for migration: Change and continuity in reasons for migration and patterns of settlement","open":false,"back":"Ten states joined the European Union and Britain was one of only three existing members to open its labour market at once. By the 2011 census over half a million Poland-born people were living in England and Wales."},{"front":"One way migration reshaped a British city's buildings","code":"13-4 — 2 The experience and impact of migrants: The impact of migrants in Britain","open":true,"back":"The Brick Lane building that was a Huguenot chapel from 1743, a synagogue from 1898 and the Jamme Masjid mosque from 1976; the Swaminarayan temple at Neasden, opened in 1995; Leicester's Jain Centre, consecrated in 1988."}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 1 Question 5, 16 marks)","items":["The question: 'Where migrants settled in Britain in the years c1900-present was decided above all by the availability of work.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• factory and mill work\n• cheap housing\nYou must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]\nThis is one paragraph from the middle of the essay, putting the case for the statement.","The map of migrant settlement in Britain after c1900 is very largely the map of its labour-hungry industries, because people went to the particular places where a particular employer was short of workers. Bradford and the Lancashire towns drew men from Mirpur to the night shifts of the woollen and cotton mills, and that pull was sharpened into a push when the Mangla Dam, completed in 1967, flooded the town of Mirpur and more than 280 villages and displaced over a hundred thousand people, many of whom already had relatives at the Bradford end. Leicester's hosiery, knitwear and footwear firms were short of hands right through the 1950s and 1960s, which is why Gujarati Indians and Punjabi Sikhs went there rather than anywhere else, and why the East African Asians expelled from Uganda in 1972 went to a city whose council had advertised in the Uganda Argus that September telling them the place was full. The foundries of the West Midlands sorted people in the same way, and so did the public services: London Transport sent a recruiting officer to Barbados in 1956 at the invitation of the Barbadian government, and the National Health Service, open since 5 July 1948, took on nurses in Jamaica, Barbados and Trinidad and doctors trained in India and Pakistan, which is why Caribbean and South Asian settlement thickened in London and the hospital towns. The test to apply is whether the pattern would survive the removal of the factor. Take the mills out of Bradford and the hosiery out of Leicester and there is no reason left for either community to be where it is, whereas cheap housing existed in a hundred towns that never became centres of settlement at all. On the criterion of what actually sorted migrants between one British city and another, it was the work.","Why this is Level 4: the explanation is directed consistently at the conceptual focus - what decided location, not a description of who arrived - and the line of reasoning runs from the opening claim to the criterion applied at the end. The information is precisely selected rather than narrated: the date the Mangla Dam was completed with the scale of the displacement it caused, the named Leicester trades and the month and title of the council's advertisement, the year and the invitation behind the Barbados recruiting trip, the date the NHS opened, and the named islands and training countries. The closing sentences justify a criterion (which factor the settlement pattern could not survive the loss of) and apply it against the rival the stimulus offers, which is what 'criteria for the required judgement are justified and applied' means at this level."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["The same question and the same paragraph of the essay, written as a Level 2 answer so that the gap is visible.","Migrants settled where the work was because they needed jobs to live. There was lots of factory and mill work in the north of England and in the Midlands, so many migrants moved to those places to work in the mills and factories. Housing was also important because migrants were poor and could only afford cheap housing, so they lived in the run-down parts of the cities where the rents were low. This meant migrant communities grew up in certain areas of big cities. Work was the main thing though, because without a job you could not pay for housing at all.","What it meets and what it misses: it meets the Level 2 descriptor - 'an explanation is given showing limited analysis and with implicit or unsustained links to the conceptual focus', with 'accurate and relevant information' but only 'some knowledge and understanding'. There is not one city named, not one industry named, not one date and not one figure; 'the north of England and in the Midlands' and 'certain areas of big cities' are the level of precision throughout. It misses the Level 3 requirement that the explanation be 'mainly directed at the conceptual focus', because it drifts into describing what migrants needed instead of weighing one cause of the settlement pattern against another, and it misses Level 4 on both counts: nothing is precisely selected, and the final sentence asserts a judgement ('work was the main thing') without ever saying what test is being used to rank the two. Note also that it addresses only the two stimulus points: that does not hold it down within Level 2, where an answer confined to the stimulus can still reach the top, but even with better analysis it would be capped at 11 marks, with no access to Level 4, until it addresses three or more aspects of content."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: who came to Britain after c1900, and why","items":["Irish migrants were the largest overseas-born group in Britain for most of the twentieth century, working on building sites, motorways, the railways and in hospitals.","About a quarter of a million Belgians fled to Britain during the First World War after the German invasion of 1914; a War Refugees Committee and more than 2,500 local committees housed them.","Jewish refugees from Nazi Germany and Austria came in the 1930s; nearly 10,000 mostly Jewish children arrived on the Kindertransport between December 1938 and the outbreak of war in September 1939.","The Polish Resettlement Act of 1947 allowed Polish servicemen who had fought with the Allies, and their families, to settle rather than return to Soviet-dominated Poland.","European Volunteer Workers were recruited from displaced persons camps in Europe from 1946 for mines, farms, mills and hospitals, under schemes including 'Westward Ho!'.","Italians were recruited in the 1950s for industries such as the brickworks around Bedford.","The Empire Windrush docked at Tilbury on 22 June 1948 with several hundred Caribbean passengers; Caribbean migration continued heavily until the Commonwealth Immigrants Act of 1962.","South Asian migration grew from the late 1950s: Punjabis and Gujaratis from India, Mirpuris from Pakistan, and later Sylhetis from Bangladesh, which became independent in 1971.","The building of the Mangla Dam in Mirpur, completed in 1967, displaced around a hundred thousand people, many of whom had relatives already in Bradford.","East African Asians came as Kenya, Uganda and Tanzania Africanised their economies: from Kenya after 1967 and from Uganda after Idi Amin's expulsion order of August 1972.","Hungarian refugees came after the Soviet suppression of the 1956 uprising; around 20,000 Vietnamese refugees were admitted from 1979; Bosnians and Kosovars came in the 1990s.","After ten states joined the European Union on 1 May 2004, Britain was one of only three existing members to open its labour market immediately; by the 2011 census over half a million Poland-born people lived in England and Wales.","Settlement followed work and cheap housing: Brixton and the East End in London, Moss Side in Manchester, Manningham in Bradford, St Paul's in Bristol, Belgrave in Leicester, and Sparkbrook and Handsworth in Birmingham.","The Immigration and Asylum Act of 1999 dispersed asylum seekers to allocated towns and cities and supported them with vouchers rather than cash."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the changing context - war, empire, Europe and the law","items":["The Aliens Act of 1905 was the first peacetime control on immigration: it created immigration officers who could refuse entry to arrivals judged undesirable.","The Aliens Restriction Act was passed the day after Britain declared war in August 1914, requiring aliens to register with the police and restricting their movement.","The Aliens Restriction (Amendment) Act of 1919 carried those wartime controls into peacetime and barred former enemy aliens from certain kinds of work.","In 1940 thousands of German, Austrian and Italian residents were interned as enemy aliens, many on the Isle of Man; the Arandora Star, carrying internees to Canada, was torpedoed on 2 July 1940 and hundreds died.","Italy declared war on Britain on 10 June 1940 and Italian shops and cafes were attacked in British cities.","India and Pakistan became independent in 1947, Ghana in 1957 and Kenya in 1963; decolonisation turned the Empire into a Commonwealth of independent states.","The British Nationality Act of 1948 made people born in Britain and its colonies citizens of the United Kingdom and Colonies; people from the independent Commonwealth countries were Commonwealth citizens, and both had the right to enter, work and settle.","The Commonwealth Immigrants Act of 1962 confined entry to holders of employment vouchers issued in categories according to skill and job offer.","The Commonwealth Immigrants Act of 1968 was passed in three days, from 27 February to 1 March 1968, to limit the entry of Kenyan Asians and required a parent or grandparent born in the United Kingdom.","The Immigration Act of 1971 introduced patriality and work permits, ending primary labour migration from the Commonwealth.","Britain joined the European Economic Community in 1973, which gave citizens of member states the right to live and work here; the referendum of 2016 began the process of ending it.","The Race Relations Acts of 1965, 1968 and 1976 progressively outlawed racial discrimination; the Equality Act of 2010 brought the anti-discrimination law together into one statute.","The Windrush scandal of 2018 revealed that people who had arrived lawfully from the Caribbean before 1973 had been detained, denied services and in some cases deported for lack of documents; the Home Secretary Amber Rudd resigned in April 2018."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the experience of migrants - hostility, campaigning and the law","items":["The British Brothers' League, founded in 1901, campaigned in the East End against Jewish immigration and helped produce the Aliens Act of 1905.","Oswald Mosley's British Union of Fascists marched against the Jewish East End until anti-fascists blocked them at Cable Street on 4 October 1936.","A colour bar operated legally in lodgings, pubs, dance halls and workplaces after 1945: landlords could and did refuse rooms on grounds of colour.","White crowds attacked Caribbean homes in Nottingham in August 1958 and in Notting Hill in late August and early September 1958.","Kelso Cochrane, an Antiguan carpenter, was murdered in Notting Hill in May 1959; nobody was ever convicted of the killing.","Claudia Jones organised an indoor Caribbean carnival at St Pancras Town Hall in January 1959 as an answer to the riots; the Notting Hill street carnival dates from 1966.","The Race Relations Act of 1965 banned racial discrimination in public places, made incitement to racial hatred an offence and set up the Race Relations Board.","Enoch Powell made his speech against immigration in Birmingham on 20 April 1968, days before the second Race Relations Bill; Edward Heath dismissed him from the Shadow Cabinet and London dockers marched in his support.","The Race Relations Act of 1968 extended the ban on discrimination to housing, employment and services and created the Community Relations Commission.","The Race Relations Act of 1976 outlawed indirect discrimination and created the Commission for Racial Equality, which could investigate and enforce.","Jayaben Desai led the mainly East African Asian women who struck at the Grunwick film-processing plant in Willesden from 1976; the strike lasted until 1978 and won wide trade-union support.","Rock Against Racism was founded in 1976 and the Anti-Nazi League in 1977, in response to the National Front; Blair Peach was killed at a Southall demonstration in April 1979.","Margaret Thatcher said in a television interview in January 1978 that people feared being swamped by people with a different culture.","Stephen Lawrence was murdered in Eltham in south-east London on 22 April 1993; the Macpherson Report of February 1999 concluded that the Metropolitan Police were institutionally racist."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the impact of migrants - culture, politics, cities, services and the economy","items":["Culture: the Notting Hill Carnival grew from Claudia Jones's indoor carnival of January 1959 and the street event of 1966 into the largest street festival in Europe.","Culture: Caribbean and South Asian music reshaped British popular music, from ska and reggae to bhangra.","Culture: the curry house became a fixture of British high streets, many run by families from Sylhet; Foreign Secretary Robin Cook called chicken tikka masala a true British national dish in 2001.","Politics: Learie Constantine, the Trinidadian cricketer and High Commissioner, became the first black peer in 1969.","Politics: Diane Abbott, Paul Boateng, Bernie Grant and Keith Vaz were all elected to the Commons at the general election of 11 June 1987.","Politics: migrant communities were also organised outside Parliament, through bodies such as the Indian Workers' Association and the West Indian Standing Conference.","Urban environment: the building on Brick Lane in east London was a Huguenot chapel from 1743, a synagogue from 1898 and has been the Jamme Masjid mosque since 1976.","Urban environment: the Swaminarayan temple at Neasden, opened in 1995, was the first traditional Hindu stone temple in Europe; Leicester's Jain Centre was consecrated in 1988.","Public services: the NHS opened on 5 July 1948 and recruited nurses in the Caribbean and doctors trained in India and Pakistan; it has relied on migrant staff ever since.","Public services: London Transport recruited in Barbados from 1956 with fares lent to recruits and repaid out of their wages.","Economy: migrants filled the post-war labour shortage in transport, textiles, foundries, brickworks and hospitals, often on shifts local workers were leaving.","Economy: migrant entrepreneurs opened corner shops, restaurants, cash-and-carry businesses and clothing factories, reviving trades such as Leicester's textile industry in the 1980s.","Economy: after EU enlargement in 2004 Central and Eastern European workers became central to agriculture, food processing, construction and hospitality."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: case study - Bristol in the mid-twentieth century","items":["Bristol was a great port whose eighteenth-century wealth came partly from the transatlantic slave trade; the statue of Edward Colston stood in the centre until it was pulled down in June 2020.","Polish servicemen who could not return home after the Second World War settled in and around Bristol under the Polish Resettlement Act of 1947.","Caribbean migrants, many of them Jamaican, arrived from the late 1940s and settled in St Paul's and Montpelier, where rooms could be rented.","Work was to be had at the docks, in the tobacco factories and at the Bristol Aeroplane Company at Filton, but not on the buses.","The Bristol Omnibus Company refused to employ black or Asian drivers and conductors, and the local branch of the Transport and General Workers' Union supported the ban; there was no law against it.","The West Indian Development Council was formed by Roy Hackett, Owen Henry, Audley Evans and Prince Brown; the youth worker Paul Stephenson became its spokesman.","Guy Bailey, a young Jamaican, had his interview with the bus company cancelled when the company learned he was black - the test case that began the campaign.","The boycott of Bristol's buses began in the spring of 1963; students marched, and Tony Benn, MP for Bristol South East, supported it.","Learie Constantine, then High Commissioner for Trinidad and Tobago, intervened with the company on the campaigners' behalf.","On 28 August 1963 the Bristol Omnibus Company announced that it would end the colour bar.","Raghbir Singh, a Sikh, became Bristol's first non-white bus conductor in September 1963.","The boycott is widely credited with strengthening the case for the Race Relations Act of 1965, the first law against racial discrimination in Britain.","St Paul's Carnival was first held in 1968 and became an annual celebration of Caribbean culture in the city.","Serious disorder broke out in St Paul's in April 1980 after a police raid on the Black and White Cafe, showing that the boycott had not settled relations between the community and the authorities."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: case study - Asian migrants in Leicester from 1945","items":["Leicester's hosiery, knitwear, footwear and engineering firms were short of workers through the 1950s and 1960s.","Gujarati Indians and Punjabi Sikhs came for that work, able to enter freely under the British Nationality Act of 1948.","Africanisation in independent Kenya from 1967 pushed out Asian traders holding British passports, and the Commonwealth Immigrants Act of 1968 was rushed through Parliament in three days, from 27 February to 1 March 1968, to limit their entry.","Idi Amin ordered Uganda's Asian population to leave within ninety days in August 1972; about 27,000 came to Britain and a Uganda Resettlement Board was set up to house them.","Many of the East African Asians arrived with professional skills, shopkeeping experience and capital rather than as labourers.","Leicester City Council placed an advertisement in the Uganda Argus in September 1972 warning that the city's housing, schools and social services were already overstretched.","People came anyway, drawn by relatives, temples, gurdwaras and familiar shops - chain migration made Leicester one of the largest centres of Indian settlement in Britain.","Asian workers in Leicester struck at Imperial Typewriters in 1974 over pay and promotion; the strikers were mainly East African Asian women and their union did not back them at first.","Belgrave Road became the city's 'Golden Mile' of sari shops, jewellers and sweet shops.","Leicester's Diwali celebrations on Belgrave Road are among the largest outside India.","The Jain Centre on Oxford Street was consecrated in 1988; the city also has many Hindu temples, Sikh gurdwaras and mosques, several converted from older buildings.","Asian-owned businesses revived Leicester's textile and clothing trades and its shopping streets in the 1980s and 1990s.","At the 2011 census white British people were under half of Leicester's population and more than a quarter of residents described themselves as Indian.","Somali arrivals in the 1990s and 2000s and Eastern European workers after 2004 added further to the city's population."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the earlier periods you need for the Question 3 comparisons","items":["c800-c1500: Viking armies raided from the 790s and settled the Danelaw; the Normans conquered after 1066 and replaced the English landholding class.","c800-c1500: William I brought Jewish financiers from Rouen because the Church forbade Christians to lend at interest; Edward I expelled the Jews in 1290.","c800-c1500: Flemish weavers were encouraged for their cloth-making skills, and Hanseatic merchants traded from the Steelyard in London.","c1500-c1700: Dutch and Walloon Protestant 'Strangers' were settled at Sandwich, Norwich and Colchester to bring new cloth-making techniques.","c1500-c1700: Jews were allowed to return under Cromwell in 1656; Charles II's proclamation of 1681 offered Huguenots denization, and about 50,000 came after Louis XIV revoked the Edict of Nantes in 1685.","c1700-c1900: Irish migration grew steadily and surged after the Great Famine of 1845-52, filling Liverpool's north end and Manchester's Little Ireland.","c1700-c1900: Jewish migration from the Russian Empire followed the pogroms that began in 1881; well over a hundred thousand had settled by 1914, mostly in London's East End, Leeds and Manchester.","c1700-c1900: Lascar and Chinese seamen settled in Liverpool, Cardiff and London's docklands; Italians settled in Clerkenwell and Germans in London and Bradford.","c1700-c1900: hostility was already familiar - the Gordon Riots of 1780 were anti-Catholic, and anti-Irish rioting broke out in Stockport in 1852."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 1 Question 5: 'The World Wars did more than the end of the British Empire to change migration to Britain in the years c1900-present.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","items":["The question as printed: 'The World Wars did more than the end of the British Empire to change migration to Britain in the years c1900-present.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\nYou may use the following in your answer:\n• enemy aliens\n• the Immigration Act 1971\nYou must also use information of your own. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","Decode it. The conceptual focus is causation, and specifically the relative importance of two causes, so the essay must weigh the wars against decolonisation rather than narrate either. 'Did more than' is a ranking phrase, which means you are being asked for a criterion. Fix the criterion in the opening and apply the same one at the end, and plan a paragraph on a cause outside both, because three or more aspects of content are needed for Level 4.","Opening, three or four sentences. State what the quotation claims; name what you will weigh against the wars, which is the end of empire and, beyond the stimulus, Europe and asylum; say what test of 'did more' you will use (which cause the change could least have happened without, rather than which produced the loudest headlines); and signal your position, so the line of reasoning is visible from the first paragraph.","Paragraph 1, for the statement - the wars built the machinery of control. The Aliens Restriction Act received royal assent on 5 August 1914, the day after Britain declared war on Germany, and required aliens over sixteen to register at a police station; the Aliens Restriction (Amendment) Act of 1919 carried those controls into peacetime. In 1940 thousands of German, Austrian and Italian residents were interned as enemy aliens, many on the Isle of Man, and the Arandora Star, carrying internees to Canada, was torpedoed on 2 July 1940 with the loss of about 800 lives, more than half of them Italians who had been living in Britain. Interim judgement: war is what turned migrants into a security question and left behind a permanent apparatus for watching them.","Paragraph 2, for the statement - the wars also decided who arrived. About a quarter of a million Belgians fled here during the First World War; nearly 10,000 mostly Jewish children came on the Kindertransport between December 1938 and the outbreak of war in September 1939; European Volunteer Workers were recruited from displaced persons camps from 1946 under schemes including 'Westward Ho!'; and the Polish Resettlement Act, which received royal assent on 27 March 1947 and is usually called Britain's first mass immigration law, provided for the servicemen who could not go home to a Poland left inside the Soviet sphere by the Yalta agreement of February 1945. Interim judgement: the wars produced both the refugee movements and the first statute written for a whole migrant population.","Paragraph 3, against the statement - the end of empire moved far larger numbers. India and Pakistan became independent in 1947, Ghana in 1957 and Kenya in 1963. The British Nationality Act of 1948 made people born in Britain and its colonies citizens of the United Kingdom and Colonies while people from the independent Commonwealth countries were Commonwealth citizens, both free to enter, work and settle; the Empire Windrush docked at Tilbury on 22 June 1948; Caribbean and then South Asian migration followed, and East African Asians came from Kenya after 1967 and from Uganda after Idi Amin's expulsion order of August 1972. The same process then closed the door: employment vouchers in the Commonwealth Immigrants Act of 1962, the Act of 1968 passed in three days from 27 February to 1 March, and the patriality rule of the Immigration Act of 1971. Interim judgement: decolonisation decided both who had a claim on Britain and when that claim was taken away.","Paragraph 4, against the statement and beyond the stimulus - Europe and asylum. Britain joined the European Economic Community in 1973; ten states joined the European Union on 1 May 2004 and Britain was one of only three existing members to open its labour market at once, so that the census of 2011 counted 579,000 Poland-born residents in England and Wales against 58,000 in 2001. Refugees came from Hungary after 1956, Vietnam from 1979 and the former Yugoslavia in the 1990s, and the Immigration and Asylum Act of 1999 dispersed asylum seekers to allocated towns and cities. Interim judgement: the largest single movement of the whole period came from neither war nor empire.","The evaluation thread to run through every paragraph: keep asking whether the change would have happened without the cause. Without the wars there would still have been controls, because the Aliens Act of 1905 came before them, but they would have arrived later and in a milder form; without the end of empire there is no right of entry for several hundred million people and no Windrush at all. That contrast is your criterion doing its work.","What the conclusion must say: not 'both were important'. Apply the criterion. Agree that the wars did more to create the machinery of control and the refugee movements, but judge that on the test of what the change could least have happened without, the end of empire did more, because it decided who had a claim on Britain, produced the largest Commonwealth movements and then supplied the reason for closing them off. Qualify it by pointing out that the two are not separable, since it was the war that exhausted Britain and hastened the independence that followed.","Timing: about 30 minutes of the 1 hour 20 minutes the paper allows. Five minutes to fix the criterion and order the four paragraphs, twenty-two to write, and the last two or three to check spelling and punctuation and to make sure the specialist terms are present and correctly used, because 4 additional marks depend on 'a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate'.","Specialist terms worth using accurately here: migration, immigration, emigration, refugee, asylum seeker, Commonwealth, decolonisation, nationality, labour shortage, recruitment, colour bar, discrimination, integration, assimilation, chain migration, urban environment, public services."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:28:11Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"The Sandwich date is now right: room.questions[3].sol reads 'who settled at Sandwich from 1561 and Norwich from 1565', which matches the royal licence negotiated for Sandwich between November 1561 and January 1562¹ and Norwich's letters patent of November 1565, with the order naming the thirty masters sealed on 1 June 1566;² 'from' carries both settlements honestly. Nothing else in the kit dates either settlement - the only other mentions, in room.questions[4].sol and extras[8] item 3, name Sandwich, Norwich and Colchester without a year - so there is no date left to contradict it, and the rest of that answer (the New Draperies against a collapsing worsted trade, the revocation of the Edict of Nantes in 1685, Tours and Lyons silk-weaving in Spitalfields, the NHS of 5 July 1948) stands as verified.³ Everything checked in the two earlier passes still holds: the trimmed 12-mark solutions at 343, 347 and 346 words, the change-framed stems, the clean Barbados wording, the caps in their June 2024 form, and dupscan's 'no duplication' with no bank stem mirroring the Option 13 SAMs or the 2022-2024 papers. Publishable as it stands."}}},"13-env":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"13-env","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Section A of Paper 1 is only 16 marks, but it is the part of the paper where a small place known in close detail beats a whole thematic study known in outline. Everything here happens in a few streets of North Kensington between about 1948 and about 1970, and the examiner sets the same three tasks every year: describe one feature each of two separate aspects of the environment, judge how useful two printed sources are for a named enquiry, and frame a follow-up enquiry out of one of them. The knowledge and the source skill are not separable. You cannot decide whether a welfare visitor's report on a house off Colville Terrace is useful unless you already know that the Rent Act of 1957 had freed landlords to charge new tenants what they liked, that banks and building societies would not lend to Caribbean migrants, and that houses built for one Victorian family were by then holding ten. So the room teaches Notting Hill and the source moves together: every fact here exists to be spent on a source, and every source here is a test of what you know about the housing, the riots, the policing, the carnival and the activism of one corner of west London.","idea":[{"h":"Why North Kensington, and what a room there cost","t":"Caribbean people came to Britain because they were British subjects and Britain wanted workers: the British Nationality Act of 1948 confirmed their right to enter and settle, and the Empire Windrush docked at Tilbury on 22 June 1948 with 1,027 passengers, about 800 of whom gave a Caribbean address as their last home. They came to North Kensington because it was one of the few places that would let them in. The tall stuccoed houses around Colville, Powis Square, Talbot Road and Golborne Road had been built for single Victorian families and had long since been split into single-room lettings; the district was poor, overcrowded and unfashionable, and its landlords cared less who paid than that somebody did. Houses in multiple occupation meant one cold tap on a landing, one lavatory in a yard, a paraffin stove in every room, and a family in each room. The Rent Act of 1957 made it worse by decontrolling rents as sitting tenants left, which turned a run-down house into an income if the old tenants could be got out; Peter Rachman, a Polish-born landlord who owned over a hundred west London properties, built a business on exactly that, letting rooms weekly and at high rents to Caribbean migrants who had been refused everywhere else, and 'Rachmanism' entered the language after his death in 1962 when the Profumo scandal made his name public in 1963. Against that ran the other half of the story: Bruce Kenrick, a minister living in the area, set up the Notting Hill Housing Trust in 1963 to buy slum houses and re-let them at fair rents, and Portobello Road, a Victorian food market with a Saturday antiques trade at its southern end, gave the area a street life that no landlord controlled. A top answer treats the housing not as a background hardship but as the thing that made the rest happen: the crowding, the shared taps and the rent were what people were arguing about in 1958 and campaigning about in 1968.","code":"13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: The local context of Notting Hill"},{"h":"Making a place: food, music, money and the street","t":"Caribbean migrants did not simply live in Notting Hill; within ten years they had changed what the streets sold, what they sounded like and how money moved through them. Grocers began stocking yam, green banana and salt fish for customers the ordinary shops did not supply, and cafes became the meeting places a community with nowhere else to go could use: Frank Crichlow, a Trinidadian who had arrived in 1953, opened the Rio cafe at 127 Westbourne Park Road in 1959, and in 1968 opened the Mangrove restaurant at 8 All Saints Road, which made that short street off Westbourne Park Road the centre of Black business in the area. Because many pubs and dance halls either refused Black customers or made them unwelcome, and because the pubs shut at eleven, people made their own nightlife: shebeens, unlicensed parties in basements and front rooms where drink was sold and records or a sound system played calypso, ska and later reggae until morning. Money was organised the same way. Banks and building societies would not lend to Caribbean migrants, so neighbours and workmates ran 'pardner' schemes, in which each member paid a fixed sum every week into a 'hand' and each in turn took the whole pot, which is how deposits for houses were raised. A top answer makes the link the examiner is looking for: these were not colourful details but the practical answers to exclusion — a shop because the shops did not stock the food, a shebeen because the pubs would not serve, a pardner because the bank would not lend.","code":"13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: The influence of Caribbean cultures on the area"},{"h":"Hostility on the streets, and who was protecting whom","t":"Hostility in Notting Hill was both casual and organised. Casual: cards in newsagents' windows saying that no coloured need apply, landlords who charged more for worse, and gangs of young white men, many of them Teddy Boys in drape jackets and narrow trousers, who treated attacks on Black men as sport — on the night of 23 to 24 August 1958 nine of them drove round west London attacking men at random and put three in hospital. Organised: Oswald Mosley's Union Movement, founded in 1948, campaigned in the area under the slogan 'Keep Britain White' and demanded repatriation, and Colin Jordan's White Defence League, founded in 1957, worked from a house at 74 Princedale Road, printed a paper called Black and White News and chalked slogans on the walls. The disturbances of 29 August to 5 September 1958 were the result: crowds of hundreds attacked houses lived in by West Indians with milk bottles, iron bars and petrol bombs, the police arrested more than 140 people over the fortnight, and of 108 people charged with serious offences 72 were white and 36 were Black. On 15 September 1958 Mr Justice Salmon sentenced nine white youths to four years' imprisonment each at the Old Bailey, telling them that everyone had the right to walk the streets without fear whatever the colour of their skin. The exemplary sentences did not end it: Kelso Cochrane, a 31-year-old carpenter from Antigua, was stabbed to death at the corner of Southam Street and Golborne Road shortly after midnight on 17 May 1959, nobody was ever charged, and more than 1,200 mourners followed his coffin on 6 June 1959. Mosley stood in Kensington North at the general election of October 1959, came bottom of the poll and lost his deposit. Policing is the sharp end of this idea: residents complained that the police were slow to treat attacks as racial, while young Black men were repeatedly stopped and questioned in the street. A top answer keeps the two halves together — the violence done to a community and the protection it felt it was not given — because that is the relationship the whole environment turns on.","code":"13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: Racism and policing"},{"h":"Answering back: a newspaper, a carnival and a restaurant","t":"Organisation was already under way before the trouble of 1958, and it grew fast afterwards. Claudia Jones, a Trinidad-born communist who had been deported from the United States in 1955, launched the West Indian Gazette in March 1958, months before the August disturbances: a monthly paper written for Britain's Caribbean population, carrying news from the islands and reporting the discrimination and the attacks its readers met here, so that when the attacks came the community already had a paper of its own to report them in. In January 1959, with the disturbances of 29 August to 5 September not yet five months past, the Gazette sponsored a Caribbean carnival in the hired hall of St Pancras Town Hall on 30 January, with music, dancing and a beauty queen, part of it televised; the point was to show the country a Caribbean culture rather than a Caribbean problem. The street carnival came from a different direction: Rhaune Laslett, a community worker, helped found the London Free School in Notting Hill in March 1966, and out of it organised a neighbourhood fayre in September 1966 in which a steel band led a children's procession out into the streets, and the procession grew year by year into the Notting Hill Carnival. Meanwhile the argument with the police was taken up directly. Frank Crichlow's Mangrove restaurant, opened in 1968, was raided twelve times between January 1969 and July 1970 without drugs ever being found; a protest march of about 150 people went to Notting Hill police station on 9 August 1970, nine organisers were arrested and charged with incitement to riot, and the trial of the Mangrove Nine at the Old Bailey ended in December 1971 with all nine cleared of the riot charges and the court acknowledging evidence of racial hatred within the Metropolitan Police. The British Black Panther Movement, founded in London in 1968, supplied several of the defendants and much of the campaign. A top answer shows the line running through all of it: each of these was a way of taking back the definition of the area from the people who wrote about it.","code":"13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: Black activism in the Notting Hill area"},{"h":"The country outside the postcode","t":"Notting Hill was one street corner of a national story, and the examiner rewards you for putting the corner inside the frame. Britain came out of the war short of labour and with cities to rebuild; the National Health Service opened in July 1948 and could not staff its wards, London Transport could not staff its buses and from 1956 recruited directly in Barbados, lending workers their fares. Because migrants were British subjects and the Commonwealth was the pool Britain drew on, the movement was legal and unrestricted until the Commonwealth Immigrants Act of 1962, which from 1 July 1962 required a Commonwealth citizen to hold an employment voucher and produced a rush of arrivals before it came into force. Discrimination was not unlawful at all until the Race Relations Act of 1965, which covered public places and incitement to racial hatred but not housing or jobs; the Race Relations Act of 1968 extended the ban to housing, employment and services. London itself was two cities: the 'Swinging Sixties' of boutiques, music and cheap rooms drew artists and students into Notting Hill, which is why the London Free School and the underground press took root there, while the Milner Holland report on London housing in March 1965 found the worst conditions of all in privately rented, multi-occupied houses of exactly the kind North Kensington was full of. Policing across the capital relied on powers such as arrest on suspicion under the Vagrancy Act of 1824, which bore hardest on young Black men. A top answer uses the national frame to explain the local event rather than to decorate it: the 1958 crowds gathered in a district made by the housing shortage, and the 1970 march went to a police station in a city whose law had only just begun to admit that discrimination existed.","code":"13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: The national and regional context"},{"h":"The local records: a borough, a police station and a local paper","t":"Local sources are the ones made inside North Kensington by people with no national audience, and for this environment they are unusually varied. The metropolitan borough council left minutes, public health and housing inspectors' reports on houses in multiple occupation, rate books, street-trading licence registers for Portobello Road, and its rehousing lists. The Metropolitan Police left station records, charge sheets and reports to the Home Office on the 1958 disturbances. Local weekly newspapers such as the Kensington Post reported the raids, the court cases, the funerals and the complaints within the limits of what an editor thought his readers wanted. Publications written for the Caribbean community, above all the West Indian Gazette from 1958, printed what the local press left out, and later community papers and the leaflets of residents' associations did the same. Then there are the people: memoirs, letters home, photographs taken by residents rather than by press photographers, and the oral history interviews recorded with Caribbean settlers from the 1980s onwards. Housing trusts and churches kept their own records, and the Notting Hill Housing Trust's appeal literature survives with them. A top answer picks the local source by what it was made for: an inspector's report is dry, dated and made for enforcement, so it is good for the state of a house and poor for what living in it felt like, while an oral history interview is the reverse and is fifty years late.","code":"13-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Knowledge of local sources"},{"h":"The national records: census, Hansard, Fleet Street and television","t":"National sources look at Notting Hill from above and from outside. The census of 1961 and the sample census of 1966 counted households, birthplaces, rooms and amenities, so they can measure overcrowding and shared bathrooms street by street in a way no witness can. Government records — Home Office files on the disturbances and on immigration control, Cabinet papers, and the report of the Milner Holland committee on housing in Greater London in March 1965 — record what ministers were told and what they decided. Hansard records what they were willing to say in the Commons, including the debates on the Commonwealth Immigrants Bill of 1962 and the Race Relations Bills of 1965 and 1968. The national press covered Notting Hill heavily in 1958 and 1959 and again at the Mangrove trial, and photographs and television reports from those moments reached audiences who had never been to the area. Opinion polls measured national attitudes to immigration. Memoirs written afterwards by politicians, police officers and residents are national sources of a different kind: wide in view, late in date, and shaped by what the writer learned later. A top answer notes that a national source is usually strongest on scale and weakest on texture: the census can tell you how many households shared a stove, and nothing at all about the argument on the landing.","code":"13-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Knowledge of national sources"},{"h":"Judging a source: nature, origin, purpose, and utility for this enquiry","t":"Usefulness is never general; it is usefulness for the enquiry the question names, so the same photograph can be very useful for one enquiry and nearly useless for the next. Work in three moves. Content: what does the source let you know or infer about this enquiry, anchored on a quoted phrase or a named detail in the picture? Provenance, meaning nature (what kind of thing it is), origin (who made it, when, where) and purpose (what it was made to do): does that make this particular inference stronger or weaker? Context: what fact of your own confirms, qualifies or explains it? The mark scheme gives no credit for contextual knowledge that is not linked to evaluating the sources, and none for calling a source biased without saying which claim in it the bias bends. The most valuable habit is to turn a limitation into a use. A newspaper photograph captioned to say that a district is quiet again is weak evidence that anybody felt safe and strong evidence of the picture a paper wanted its readers outside the area to hold — and its unstaged corners, a boarded window or a painted-over wall, are evidence of something else again. A top answer says for each source what its purpose makes it bad evidence of and what the same purpose makes it good evidence of, then judges which source serves this enquiry better and on what criterion.","code":"13-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Recognition of the strengths and weaknesses of different types of source"},{"h":"Framing a question a record could actually answer","t":"A follow-up question is not a question about the topic; it is a question raised by one detail in the printed source and answerable from a record that exists. Choose the unfinished detail: a figure given without a check, a group named in passing, a complaint with no answer attached, an event mentioned as though the reader already knows it. Then ask a question that could be settled rather than merely discussed — how many, how often, on whose orders, with what result, and by when. 'Was the landlord cruel?' cannot be followed up; 'How many tenants in North Kensington were put out of their rooms without notice between 1957 and 1962, and what did the borough do about it?' can. Keep the question tied to the detail you named in the first box, because a question that floats free of the source scores nothing, and keep it narrow enough that one kind of record could close it. A top answer writes the question so that you could name, in the very next box, the shelf it would be answered from.","code":"13-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Framing of questions relevant to the pursuit of a specific enquiry"},{"h":"Choosing the record that would close the question","t":"The last two boxes are a matching exercise: name a type of record rather than one document, and then say what is inside it that would answer the question you just asked. For Notting Hill the useful types are known — borough public health and housing inspectors' reports on houses in multiple occupation, council minutes and rehousing lists, rate books and street-trading licence registers, police station records and charge sheets, Home Office files, the census of 1961 and the sample census of 1966, the local weekly press, the West Indian Gazette and other papers written for the Caribbean community, court records from the Old Bailey trials, housing trust appeal literature and annual reports, photographs taken by residents, and oral history interviews. Match the record to the question's verb: a number needs a register, a return or a census; a decision needs minutes or correspondence; a feeling needs a memoir, an interview or a letter; an official line needs Hansard or the press. A top answer finishes by saying what the record would contain — not that it 'would tell us more', but that the licence registers name every pitch in the market, who held it and what they were allowed to sell, year by year.","code":"13-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Selection of appropriate sources for specific investigations"}],"examples":[{"title":"Describing one feature each: 1(a) Mosley's Union Movement and 1(b) the White Defence League","setup":"The question is printed in two parts: '1(a) Describe one feature of Oswald Mosley's Union Movement in North Kensington. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the White Defence League. [2 marks]' You know the following. Oswald Mosley, who had led the British Union of Fascists before the war, founded the Union Movement in 1948. After the disturbances of 1958 it campaigned hard in Notting Hill under the slogan 'Keep Britain White', holding open-air street meetings and demanding that Caribbean migrants be sent home. Mosley stood as its candidate for the Kensington North constituency at the general election of October 1959, calling for repatriation and for a ban on mixed marriages; he came bottom of the poll and lost his deposit. The White Defence League was a separate and smaller group founded by Colin Jordan in 1957. It worked from a house at 74 Princedale Road in Notting Hill, which it used as office and printing shop, produced a paper called Black and White News, and chalked and posted 'Keep Britain White' slogans around the area; it merged into the newly formed British National Party in February 1960. In each part one mark is given for the feature and a second mark for the supporting information attached to it.","steps":["Treat the two parts as two separate questions with a box each, because nothing you write about the Union Movement can earn anything in 1(b) and nothing about the White Defence League can earn anything in 1(a).","In 1(a), state one feature flatly and then attach the evidence that proves it: the Union Movement fought an election on race in the area, standing Mosley in Kensington North in October 1959 on a demand for repatriation, and he finished bottom of the poll and lost his deposit.","In 1(b), do the same with a different kind of fact: the White Defence League was a propaganda group with a base in the district, founded by Colin Jordan in 1957 and working from 74 Princedale Road, where it printed Black and White News.","Stop there. Two sentences in each box is the whole task: do not add a second feature to either part, do not explain why either group found support in Notting Hill, and do not compare them."],"cues":["How much of your answer to 1(a) can be credited in 1(b)?","What proves that feature and so earns the second mark?","What kind of supporting detail suits a small propaganda group?","What would a second feature in either box be worth?"]},{"title":"Judging usefulness: Source A for an enquiry into the work and wages of Caribbean migrants in North Kensington","setup":"The question is: 'Study Sources A and B. How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the work and wages of Caribbean migrants living in North Kensington in 1959? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context.' (8 marks) This example works on Source A only. Source A is the report of a welfare visitor employed by a West Indian workers' association, written for the association's committee on 12 June 1959 and not published; she had spent a fortnight calling on households between Colville Terrace and Golborne Road. On work and wages she writes that most of the men she met were in work, on the buses and the Underground, at the Post Office and in the railway yards at Paddington, and that several were doing two jobs; that a man who works nights on the Underground pays three pounds a week for a single room, which is a quarter of what he earns; and that eight men in one street each pay a pound a week into a hand and take the whole of it in turn, two of them having used it to put money down on a house. Your own knowledge includes that London Transport sent recruiters to Barbados from 1956 and lent workers their fares, that the National Health Service and the railways were also short of staff, that banks and building societies would not lend to Caribbean migrants, and that the Rent Act of 1957 had freed landlords to charge new tenants what they liked.","steps":["Say what the source lets you know about the named enquiry and anchor it: it gives the trades — buses, Underground, Post Office, railway yards — the fact of second jobs, and the only figures in it, a room at three pounds a week taking a quarter of a night worker's pay.","Use the nature and origin to strengthen that: this is an unpublished internal report by a visitor who was let into the homes themselves, written for a committee that needed the plain state of things rather than for readers who had to be reassured, so no editor has trimmed the rent or the wage.","Use the purpose to qualify one named claim: a welfare association wanted evidence of hardship, so the households she was taken to first may be the hardest pressed, and her 'a quarter of what he earns' is one man's arithmetic rather than a survey of wages.","Bring one fact of your own and aim it at the source rather than at the topic: London Transport had been recruiting in Barbados since 1956 and lending the fares, which explains why so many of her men are on the buses and the Underground and shows that the pattern she found was made by recruitment, not by chance.","Close on the enquiry: for what men earned and paid this is a strong source because it is specific, unpublished and gathered inside the houses, and its weakness is that it counts one street rather than a workforce."],"cues":["Which sentences are actually about work and pay rather than about housing?","What is it about who wrote this, and for whom, that makes those figures trustworthy?","Which single claim would you not take as typical, and why?","What fact of your own explains the pattern of jobs she found?","What is your verdict for this enquiry, and on what criterion?"]},{"title":"Judging usefulness: Source B, the caption, and what an unstaged corner is good for","setup":"The question is: 'Study Sources A and B. How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the effects of the 1958 disturbances on the streets of North Kensington? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context.' (8 marks) This example works on Source B only. Source B is a photograph printed in a London weekly newspaper on 18 June 1959 under a caption saying that the district is quiet again and North Kensington is getting back to its ordinary business; it was taken on a Saturday morning in a street off Portobello Road by a press photographer sent to the area after the murder of Kelso Cochrane. The photograph shows a narrow street of five-storey terraced houses with peeling stucco, two ground-floor windows boarded with new timber, market stalls along the kerb including a stall of yams, green bananas and salt fish under a hand-painted board, a queue of women at a grocer's door, two men in suits talking to a woman with a pram, a column of small handwritten cards in a newsagent's window, a patch of brickwork painted over in a lighter colour with the tails of chalked letters still showing at its edge, and a police constable at the kerb with his back to the camera facing four young men in long jackets and narrow trousers leaning on the railings opposite. Your own knowledge includes that crowds attacked West Indians' houses in the area from 29 August to 5 September 1958, that the White Defence League chalked 'Keep Britain White' slogans in these streets, that Mr Justice Salmon passed four-year sentences on nine white youths in September 1958, and that Kelso Cochrane was stabbed to death at the corner of Southam Street and Golborne Road on 17 May 1959.","steps":["Separate what the caption asserts from what the picture happens to contain, because they are worth different things: 'quiet again' is the paper's claim, while the new timber over the windows and the painted-over wall are marks nobody arranged for the camera.","Read the unarranged details exactly as printed before you use them: the windows are boarded with new timber, so that damage is recent and belongs to the weeks after the stabbing of May 1959 rather than to the previous summer, while the slogan painted out but still showing at its edges is the older mark and the effort to rub it away — together they show that what began in 1958 had not stopped.","Use the nature, origin and purpose against the caption: a press photograph chosen and captioned for readers outside the district is evidence of the reassurance a newspaper wanted to offer, not evidence that anyone in the street felt safe, and the picture is a Saturday market morning, the busiest and most orderly hour of the week.","Read the one ambiguous detail carefully rather than guessing: a constable standing with his back to the camera facing four young men in long jackets can be read as protection or as surveillance, and the photograph cannot settle which, which is itself worth saying.","Bring one fact of your own that tests it and then judge: the League had chalked its slogans through these streets, so the painted patch dates the effort to clean them off, and the photograph is therefore weak evidence of calm and strong evidence of damage, of an attempted return to normal trade, and of how the area was being presented to outsiders."],"cues":["Which part of this source is the paper's claim rather than the picture's content?","What do the unarranged details prove about the state of the street?","How does a caption for outside readers change what the picture can be used for?","Can the photograph tell you why the constable is standing there?","So what is this photograph strong evidence of, if not of calm?"]},{"title":"Framing a follow-up: the four boxes from one detail in Source A","setup":"The question is: 'Study Source A. How could you follow up Source A to find out more about when shops and cafes selling Caribbean food opened in the streets of North Kensington? In your answer, you must give the question you would ask and the type of source you could use.' (4 marks) The table has four boxes: the detail in Source A you would follow up, the question you would ask, the type of source you could use, and how it might help answer your question. Source A is the unpublished report of a welfare visitor employed by a West Indian workers' association, written for the association's committee on 12 June 1959 after a fortnight calling on households between Colville Terrace and Golborne Road; near the end she writes that there is a shop on the corner now that sells yam and salt fish, and a cafe where the men sit on Sunday. You know that the Post Office London street directories listed the occupier and the trade of every address in a street, volume by volume; that the borough kept rate books naming the occupier and the rateable value of each property year by year; that the council licensed street traders and inspected food premises; and that directories and rate books for the borough are kept in its local studies collection.","steps":["Take the detail that carries a date inside it: she writes that there is a shop on the corner now that sells yam and salt fish, and that 'now' says the shop is a recent arrival without saying when it arrived.","Turn it into a question a series could close rather than one that invites an impression: in which years did shops and cafes selling Caribbean food first appear in these streets, and how many were trading there by the middle of the 1960s?","Name a type of record and not a single document: the Post Office London street directories for the district, which give the occupier and the trade of every address, set beside the borough's rate books for the same streets.","Say what is inside those records that answers the question: each annual volume lists the same addresses in the same order, so a change of occupier and of trade can be dated to a year and the shops counted street by street — though a directory names a trade and not what was on the shelves, so a grocer selling yam may need the local press or a resident's memoir to confirm."],"cues":["Which word in her sentence about the shop implies a date she does not give?","What would make your question answerable by a count rather than by an impression?","What kind of record would let you look at the same street in two different years?","What on the page dates the change, and what would it still not tell you?"]},{"title":"Choosing between local and national records for the same enquiry","setup":"You are investigating whether housing conditions in North Kensington improved between 1958 and 1968. You have four kinds of record available. First, the borough council's public health and housing inspectors' reports on houses in multiple occupation, which record the address, the number of lettings, the amenities and the complaints made. Second, the Notting Hill Housing Trust's own annual reports and appeal literature, issued from 1963 to raise money and to show what the Trust had bought and re-let. Third, the census of 1961 and the sample census of 1966, which counted households, rooms, birthplaces and shared amenities. Fourth, oral history interviews recorded with Caribbean residents of the area from the 1980s onwards. The enquiry asks about change over ten years: how many houses were still multi-occupied, whether taps and lavatories were still shared, what rents were charged, and what it felt like to live there. Decide which record answers which part of the enquiry, and which is weakest on its own.","steps":["Match each record to the verb it can answer: the census answers 'how many', because it counted households and shared amenities on fixed dates at each end of the decade, which is exactly what a question about change needs.","See what the census cannot do: it never names a landlord or a rent and never says who was refused a room, so it establishes the scale of the problem and not the way it worked.","Take the inspectors' reports for the mechanism: they carry addresses, the number of lettings in each house, the state of the amenities and the complaints made, so they show whether the same houses were still overcrowded and whether the council acted.","Take the Trust's annual reports and weigh their provenance the right way round: they were published to raise money, so their descriptions of squalor are chosen to shock, but their figures for houses bought and tenants rehoused were audited and are a direct measure of one kind of improvement.","Put the interviews last for measurement and first for one particular thing: they were recorded decades later and remember selectively, but they are the only record that carries what sharing a landing felt like, and they can be checked against the dated records rather than trusted on their own."],"cues":["Which record was built to count things?","What does the enquiry need that a headcount will never contain?","Who was obliged to inspect a multi-occupied house and write it down?","Why would a charity's own report exaggerate one thing and not another?","What can a late memory still be used as evidence of?"]}],"check":[{"q":"On what dates did the Notting Hill disturbances of 1958 begin and end, and what sentences followed?","a":"They ran from 29 August to 5 September 1958. On 15 September 1958 Mr Justice Salmon sentenced nine white youths to four years' imprisonment each at the Old Bailey."},{"q":"Who was Kelso Cochrane, when was he killed, and what happened to his killers?","a":"He was a 31-year-old carpenter from Antigua, stabbed to death by a group of white youths at the corner of Southam Street and Golborne Road shortly after midnight on 17 May 1959. Nobody was ever charged. More than 1,200 people followed his funeral on 6 June 1959."},{"q":"Why did Caribbean migrants in Notting Hill run 'pardner' schemes rather than use banks?","a":"Banks and building societies would not lend to them. In a pardner each member paid a fixed sum weekly into a 'hand' and took the whole pot in turn, which is how deposits for houses were raised."},{"q":"A source is a photograph printed in a newspaper in 1959 under a caption saying the district is quiet again. What is it weak evidence of, and what is it strong evidence of?","a":"It is weak evidence that anybody in the street felt safe, because the caption was written to reassure readers outside the area. It is strong evidence of how the district was being presented to those readers, and of any unstaged detail nobody would have arranged, such as boarded windows or a wall painted over a slogan."},{"q":"Name the four boxes of a follow-up answer, in order.","a":"The detail in the source you would follow up; the question you would ask; the type of source you could use; and how that source might help answer your question. Each has to fit the one before it."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 1 Section A is 16 marks in all: Question 1 in two parts, '1(a) Describe one feature of…' (2 marks) and '1(b) Describe one feature of…' (2 marks) on two separate aspects of content, Question 2(a) 'How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into…? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context.' (8 marks) and Question 2(b) 'How could you follow up Source A to find out more about…? In your answer, you must give the question you would ask and the type of source you could use.' (4 marks).","Questions 1 and 2(b) are point-marked, not levelled. On each part of Question 1, one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for the supporting information attached to it. On Question 2(b), one mark for a detail selected from the source, one for a question linked to that detail, one for naming an appropriate type of source, and one for showing how that source would help answer the question.","Question 2(a) is levelled, and Level 3 runs from 6 to 8 marks: judgements on source utility for the specified enquiry, applying valid criteria with developed reasoning which takes into account how the provenance affects the usefulness of the source content, with contextual knowledge used in the process of interpreting the sources. Provenance means nature, origin and purpose.","Two rules printed under the 2(a) grid decide most of the marks that are lost: no credit may be given for contextual knowledge unless it is linked to evaluation of the sources, and no credit may be given for generic comments on provenance which are not used to evaluate source content.","Section A prints no stimulus points and carries no SPaG marks; both belong elsewhere on the paper. Paper 1 lasts 1 hour 20 minutes for 52 marks, so about 20 minutes covers Section A's 16 marks: roughly 4 minutes on Question 1, 10 to 13 minutes on Question 2(a) and 4 to 5 minutes on Question 2(b), leaving about an hour for Section B."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of houses of multiple occupation in North Kensington in the 1950s. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of Peter Rachman's methods as a landlord. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks in two parts, point-marked: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 mark for the supporting information attached to it. In 1(a) credit the letting of single rooms in houses built for one family, shared taps and lavatories, overcrowding, or paraffin heating. In 1(b) credit his buying of run-down property, the pressure put on protected tenants, the men used to clear rooms, or the rents charged to new tenants.","sol":"1(a) Feature: whole families lived in single rooms in houses built for one household. A five-storey terrace off Colville Terrace might hold ten families or more, sharing one cold tap on a landing and one lavatory in the yard, with a paraffin stove in every room. 1(b) Feature: Rachman let rooms at high weekly rents to tenants nobody else would house. He bought run-down houses in North Kensington and Paddington, owning over a hundred west London properties by the late 1950s, and sent men to make life impossible for protected tenants so that their rooms could be re-let.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Two boxes, two separate things: answer the houses in the first and the landlord in the second, and never mix them.","A feature only scores twice when a number, a place or a practice is attached to it, so choose features you can attach something to.","Write each part as one flat sentence of fact followed by a second sentence carrying the evidence, and leave out any comment on whether it was fair."],"codes":["13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: The local context of Notting Hill"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the Notting Hill Housing Trust. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of Portobello Road market. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks in two parts, point-marked: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 mark for the supporting information. In 1(a) credit its founder and date, its buying and re-letting of slum houses, its growth, or its publicity. In 1(b) credit the food market and its age, the Saturday antiques trade, or the Caribbean produce sold there.","sol":"1(a) Feature: it was a voluntary body that bought slum houses and re-let them at fair rents. Bruce Kenrick, a minister living in the area, set it up in 1963; it owned five houses that year and seventeen by 1964, and it advertised by showing a family of six living in one room. 1(b) Feature: it was a long street market that had sold food since Victorian times. Fruit, vegetables and fish were sold on weekdays towards the Golborne end while Saturdays filled the southern end with second-hand and antique stalls, and Caribbean traders added yam, green banana and salt fish.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["One part is an organisation and the other a street, so give each its own kind of detail rather than one answer about the area in general.","A founder with a date, and a kind of goods with a day of the week, are the supporting details most easily attached here.","Describe rather than explain: no reasons why housing was short and no argument about whether the Trust succeeded."],"codes":["13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: The local context of Notting Hill"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of 'pardner' schemes. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of shebeens in Notting Hill. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks in two parts, point-marked: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 mark for the supporting information. In 1(a) credit the fixed weekly payment, the rotating 'hand', its use for deposits, or its basis in trust between neighbours. In 1(b) credit the unlicensed selling of drink, the private houses used, the music played, or the late hours.","sol":"1(a) Feature: it was a savings club run among neighbours and workmates. Each member paid a fixed sum every week, perhaps a pound, into a 'hand', and each in turn took the whole pot, which let a member raise a deposit for a house at a time when banks and building societies would not lend to Caribbean migrants. 1(b) Feature: they were unlicensed parties held in basements and front rooms. Drink was sold and records or a sound system played calypso, ska and later reggae into the morning, because the pubs shut at eleven and many in the area made Black customers unwelcome.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Both parts are answers to being shut out of something, but they are shut out of different things.","One box can carry the sum paid and what the money was used for; the other can carry the place, the music and the hour.","Keep to what each was; do not explain why exclusion existed or judge whether either was legal."],"codes":["13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: The influence of Caribbean cultures on the area"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of Caribbean-run cafes and shops in the Notting Hill area. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of All Saints Road. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks in two parts, point-marked: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 mark for the supporting information. In 1(a) credit the Caribbean food stocked, the use of cafes as meeting places, or a named business such as Crichlow's Rio cafe. In 1(b) credit its position, the Caribbean businesses along it, or the opening of the Mangrove there in 1968.","sol":"1(a) Feature: they sold the food and served as meeting places for people with nowhere else to go. Grocers stocked yam, green banana and salt fish that ordinary shops did not carry, and Frank Crichlow's Rio cafe, opened at 127 Westbourne Park Road in 1959, was somewhere Black customers could sit without being turned away. 1(b) Feature: it became the centre of Black business in the district. A short street off Westbourne Park Road, it held Caribbean-run shops and cafes, and in 1968 Crichlow opened the Mangrove restaurant at number 8, which quickly became a meeting place for writers and campaigners.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["One part is a kind of business across the area and the other is one particular street.","A named address with a date is the strongest support available in both boxes.","Do not drift into the police raids or the trial; this question asks only what the shops and the street were like."],"codes":["13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: The influence of Caribbean cultures on the area"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the Notting Hill riots of 1958. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the murder of Kelso Cochrane. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks in two parts, point-marked: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 mark for the supporting information. In 1(a) credit the attacks on West Indians' houses, the dates, the weapons used, or the numbers arrested and charged. In 1(b) credit who he was, the date and place, the group who attacked him, or the fact that no one was charged.","sol":"1(a) Feature: crowds of white people attacked houses lived in by West Indians night after night. The disturbances ran from 29 August to 5 September 1958, with milk bottles, iron bars and petrol bombs used against windows and doors, and the police arrested more than 140 people over the fortnight. 1(b) Feature: he was stabbed to death in the street by a group of white youths. Cochrane, a carpenter from Antigua aged 31, was attacked shortly after midnight on 17 May 1959 at the corner of Southam Street and Golborne Road, and nobody was ever charged with the killing.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["One box is a week of street violence and the other is a single killing, so keep the detail of each inside its own part.","Dates and numbers carry the second mark in the first box; a name, an age and a street corner carry it in the second.","Describe what happened; do not explain the causes or discuss what the murder led to."],"codes":["13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: Racism and policing"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of Claudia Jones's West Indian Gazette. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the Caribbean carnival held at St Pancras Town Hall in January 1959. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks in two parts, point-marked: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 mark for the supporting information. In 1(a) credit its founder and date, its readership, or the news and campaigning it carried. In 1(b) credit that it was held indoors in a hired hall, its date, its sponsor, its contents, or the television coverage.","sol":"1(a) Feature: it was a newspaper written for Britain's Caribbean population. Claudia Jones, a Trinidad-born communist who had been deported from the United States in 1955, founded it in 1958, and it carried news from the islands alongside reports of the discrimination and the attacks its readers met in Britain. 1(b) Feature: it was an indoor carnival held in a hired public hall. Sponsored by the Gazette, it took place at St Pancras Town Hall on 30 January 1959 with Caribbean music, dancing and a beauty contest, and part of it was televised.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["One part is a paper and the other a single evening, so give each a date of its own.","The founder's name and the paper's readers support the first; a place, a date and what happened in the hall support the second.","Two sentences per box, with no account of how the carnival later moved into the streets."],"codes":["13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: Black activism in the Notting Hill area"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the Mangrove restaurant. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the Mangrove Nine case. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks in two parts, point-marked: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 mark for the supporting information. In 1(a) credit its founder and address, the date it opened, the people who met there, or the police raids. In 1(b) credit the march that led to the arrests, the charge, the court, or the outcome.","sol":"1(a) Feature: it was a Caribbean restaurant that became a political meeting place. Frank Crichlow opened it at 8 All Saints Road in 1968; it served Caribbean food and drew writers, musicians and campaigners, and the police raided it twelve times between January 1969 and July 1970 without ever finding drugs. 1(b) Feature: nine Black activists were tried at the Old Bailey for inciting a riot. They were arrested after a protest march of about 150 people went to Notting Hill police station on 9 August 1970, and in December 1971 all nine were cleared of the riot charges.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["One part is a building and the other a court case, so keep the restaurant out of the second box.","An address with a date supports the first; a march, a charge and a verdict support the second.","Do not argue about whether the raids were justified; state the features and the detail that proves them."],"codes":["13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: Black activism in the Notting Hill area"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the demand for workers in Britain after the Second World War. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the Commonwealth Immigrants Act 1962. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks in two parts, point-marked: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 mark for the supporting information. In 1(a) credit the shortages left by the war, the new National Health Service, direct recruitment in the Caribbean, or the right of entry Commonwealth citizens held. In 1(b) credit the voucher system, the date it came into force, or the rush of arrivals before it.","sol":"1(a) Feature: employers recruited in the Caribbean to fill jobs Britain could not staff. Rebuilding, the railways, the buses and the new National Health Service of 1948 all needed labour, and London Transport sent recruiters to Barbados from 1956, lending workers the money for their fares. 1(b) Feature: it ended the free entry that Commonwealth citizens had until then enjoyed. From 1 July 1962 a Commonwealth citizen needed an employment voucher in order to settle in Britain, and the announcement of the Bill produced a rush of arrivals before it came into force.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["One part is about why people were wanted and the other about when they stopped being freely admitted.","A named employer with a date supports the first box; the date the control began supports the second.","Describe the demand and the Act; do not explain why opinion changed in between."],"codes":["13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: The national and regional context"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. How could you follow up Source A to find out more about the methods landlords used against their tenants in North Kensington? In your answer, you must give the question you would ask and the type of source you could use. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked in four boxes: 1 mark for a detail selected from Source A, 1 for a question linked to it, 1 for naming an appropriate type of source, 1 for showing how it would help. Credit follow-ups built on the collector who came with two other men, the family put out on the pavement, or the rent books that carried no address for the landlord.","sol":"Detail in Source A that I would follow up: the collector who came with two other men, and the family put out on the pavement while the room was let again the same evening. Question I would ask: how many tenants in North Kensington were put out of their rooms without notice between 1957 and 1962, and what did the borough do about it? What type of source I could use: the borough council's public health and housing inspectors' reports on houses in multiple occupation. How it would help: inspectors recorded the address, the lettings and every complaint made, so the files would show whether such evictions were common and whether the council acted.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Look for the sentence in the report that describes a practice rather than a condition.","Your question has to be settleable by a record: ask how many, how often or with what result.","Set the four boxes out as four short labelled lines and keep them chained to one another: the record you name has to be one somebody had to make at the time for a purpose of their own, and your last line should say what is written in it rather than why it would be handy."],"codes":["13-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Framing of questions relevant to the pursuit of a specific enquiry","13-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Selection of appropriate sources for specific investigations","13-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Knowledge of local sources"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. How could you follow up Source A to find out more about the 'pardner' savings schemes run by Caribbean migrants in Notting Hill? In your answer, you must give the question you would ask and the type of source you could use. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked in four boxes: 1 mark for a detail selected from Source A, 1 for a question linked to it, 1 for naming an appropriate type of source, 1 for showing how it would help. Credit follow-ups built on the eight men paying a pound a week into a hand, or on the two who used it to put money down on a house.","sol":"Detail in Source A that I would follow up: the eight men in one street who each pay a pound a week into a hand and take the whole of it in turn. Question I would ask: how widely were pardner schemes used by Caribbean households in North Kensington in the late 1950s, and what were people able to buy with them? What type of source I could use: oral history interviews recorded with Caribbean settlers in the area. How it would help: the interviews describe how a hand was organised, who held it and what each turn was spent on, an arrangement that left no official paperwork.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The report mentions one street; the enquiry is about a practice across a district.","Choose a question that asks how common the practice was and what it achieved, not whether it was a good idea.","Some arrangements were deliberately kept off paper, so ask who would remember them and how that memory was ever recorded."],"codes":["13-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Framing of questions relevant to the pursuit of a specific enquiry","13-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Knowledge of local sources","13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: The influence of Caribbean cultures on the area"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. How could you follow up Source A to find out more about how the police dealt with attacks on Caribbean people in Notting Hill? In your answer, you must give the question you would ask and the type of source you could use. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked in four boxes: 1 mark for a detail selected from Source A, 1 for a question linked to it, 1 for naming an appropriate type of source, 1 for showing how it would help. Credit follow-ups on the statements taken with nobody expecting a charge, or on the young man stopped three times in one week.","sol":"Detail in Source A that I would follow up: that everyone she asked said the police had taken statements, and that not one of them expected anybody to be charged. Question I would ask: how many attacks on Black residents were reported to police stations in North Kensington in 1958 and 1959, and how many of those reports led to a charge? What type of source I could use: Metropolitan Police station records and charge sheets, together with the Home Office files on the disturbances. How it would help: the station books record every report made and its outcome, so counting them would show whether the residents' expectation matched what actually happened to their complaints.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Pick the sentence that reports an expectation rather than an event, because it is a claim a record could test.","Turn the expectation into a question about numbers and outcomes.","Two different authorities kept paper on this, one in the district and one in Whitehall."],"codes":["13-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Framing of questions relevant to the pursuit of a specific enquiry","13-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Knowledge of national sources","13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: Racism and policing"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. How could you follow up Source A to find out more about the colour bar that Caribbean migrants met when they looked for rooms in North Kensington? In your answer, you must give the question you would ask and the type of source you could use. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked in four boxes: 1 mark for a detail selected from Source A, 1 for a question linked to it, 1 for naming an appropriate type of source, 1 for showing how it would help. Credit follow-ups built on the card in a newsagent's window saying that no coloured need apply, or on the tenants who walked until they found a landlord who would take them and paid what he asked.","sol":"Detail in Source A that I would follow up: the tenants in seven of the houses who said they had first been shown a card in a newsagent's window saying that no coloured need apply. Question I would ask: how widely were rooms in North Kensington advertised with a colour bar between 1955 and 1968, and when did that wording stop appearing? What type of source I could use: bound runs of the borough's weekly newspapers with their accommodation and small-advertisement columns, kept in the local studies library. How it would help: the wording is dated and survives issue by issue, so the bar could be counted and tracked rather than remembered, and the point at which it disappeared could be set against the Race Relations Act of 1968, which made such notices unlawful.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["One of her sentences is not about the rooms at all, but about how the tenants came to be in them.","Frame a question a dated series could settle: how widely, across which years, and until when.","Your last two boxes stand or fall together: name the kind of record in which that wording would itself have been preserved, then say what a reader turning its pages would actually see."],"codes":["13-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Framing of questions relevant to the pursuit of a specific enquiry","13-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Selection of appropriate sources for specific investigations","13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: The local context of Notting Hill"]},{"q":"Study Sources A and B in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the housing conditions Caribbean migrants found in North Kensington in the late 1950s? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks, levelled. Level 3 (6–8) needs judgements on usefulness for this enquiry with developed reasoning on how nature, origin and purpose affect the usefulness of the content, and contextual knowledge used in interpreting the sources. Credit the crowding, shared amenities and rents in A, and the peeling stucco, the basement areas and the boarded windows of the decayed street in B, set against an unpublished welfare report and a captioned press photograph.","sol":"Source A is very useful because it was written from inside the houses. It gives a house built for one family holding eleven people, one cold tap on the landing and one lavatory in the yard, a paraffin stove in every room, and a room let at three pounds a week to a night worker, a quarter of his pay. Its nature and origin strengthen those details: an unpublished internal report by a visitor who was let into twenty-one homes in a fortnight, written for a committee that needed the plain state of things, not for readers who had to be reassured. Its purpose qualifies one thing only: a welfare association wanted evidence of hardship, so the worst houses may be the ones she was taken to first. My own knowledge confirms the pattern: the Rent Act of 1957 let landlords charge new tenants what they liked, and Rachman, with over a hundred west London properties, built his business on exactly this kind of weekly letting. Source B reaches the enquiry less directly but is not useless: the peeling stucco, the basement areas behind railings and the two ground-floor windows boarded with new timber are details no photographer arranged, and they show a street of decayed, multi-occupied property of exactly the kind Source A describes from inside. Its limit sits on the same wall: the column of small handwritten cards in the newsagent's window is the one place the photograph might have shown how rooms were let, and they are too small to read, so it is Source A, not B, that supplies the card saying no coloured need apply. For conditions inside the rooms A is much the more useful; B is useful for the street outside the door and for what a newspaper called ordinary business.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Ask of each source what it lets you see about housing that the other cannot.","Take nature, origin and purpose in turn and aim each at one named claim inside the source rather than at the source as a whole.","Plan four moves per source — what it shows, why its provenance strengthens or weakens that, one fact of your own that tests it, what it is therefore good for — and finish with a single comparative sentence."],"codes":["13-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Recognition of the strengths and weaknesses of different types of source","13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: The local context of Notting Hill","13-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Knowledge of local sources"]},{"q":"Study Sources A and B in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into racial hostility towards Caribbean migrants in Notting Hill? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks, levelled. Level 3 (6–8) needs criteria-based judgements on usefulness for this enquiry with developed reasoning on provenance and contextual knowledge used on the sources. Credit the cards refusing coloured applicants and the fear after the stabbing in A, and the boarded windows, the painted-over chalk and the watched youths in B, weighed against a welfare report written to gather evidence and a caption written to reassure.","sol":"Source A is useful because hostility in it is reported as it was met rather than as it was argued about. Tenants in seven houses told the visitor they had first been shown a card in a newsagent's window saying that no coloured need apply, and had walked until they found a landlord who would take them; the women would not go to the launderette after dark and the children were fetched from school. Its origin strengthens this: an unpublished report for a committee, written three weeks after the stabbing in May. Its purpose qualifies it: an association gathering evidence of hardship asked about exactly these things, so its picture may be sharper than the street as a whole. My own knowledge confirms that the pressure was organised, because the White Defence League printed Black and White News from Princedale Road and Mosley's Union Movement demanded repatriation in the same streets. Source B is weaker on feeling but useful on traces: two ground-floor windows are boarded with new timber, and because the photographer was sent to the area after the stabbing in May that fresh boarding points to recent damage rather than to a scar left from the summer before, while a patch of wall has been painted over with the tails of chalked letters still showing, which is hostility and the attempt to erase it in one detail. Its caption, written for readers outside the area, is evidence of how the district was being presented, not of how safe it was. A is the more useful for what hostility did to people; B is the more useful for what it left on the walls.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["One source records what people said about hostility; the other records what it left behind.","Decide early which details in the photograph nobody would have arranged, and use those.","Aim your contextual knowledge at one named claim in a source rather than offering it as background about the far right."],"codes":["13-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Recognition of the strengths and weaknesses of different types of source","13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: Racism and policing","13-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Knowledge of national sources"]},{"q":"Study Sources A and B in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into relations between the police and Caribbean residents of Notting Hill? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks, levelled. Level 3 (6–8) needs judgements on usefulness for this enquiry, with provenance used on named claims and contextual knowledge used on the sources. Credit the statements taken with no charge expected and the young man stopped three times in A, and the constable watching the four youths in B, which can be read as protection or as surveillance.","sol":"Source A speaks to this enquiry directly. Everyone the visitor asked said the police had taken statements after the killing in May, and not one of them expected anybody to be charged; a young man told her he had been stopped three times in one week and asked where he had got the money for his coat. Those two sentences give both halves of the relationship, under-protection and over-attention. Its nature and origin strengthen them: an unpublished report to a committee had no reason to soften either, and the visitor was let into twenty-one homes rather than briefed at a station. Its purpose qualifies the first: she was recording grievances, so she collected expectations rather than outcomes, and no charge had in fact been brought by June. My own knowledge supports that reading, because nobody was ever charged with Kelso Cochrane's murder, while powers of arrest on suspicion bore hardest on young Black men. Source B is thinner and ambiguous, which is worth saying: a constable stands at the kerb facing four young men in long jackets, and the photograph cannot show whether he is guarding the street or watching it. Its purpose matters here, because a picture captioned to say the district is quiet again was chosen to show order restored. A is much the more useful for the relationship itself; B is evidence only of the policing a newspaper wished to print.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The enquiry has two halves: what the police did about attacks, and what they did to young Black men in the street.","An ambiguous detail can still be used, provided you say what it cannot settle.","Bring one fact about the outcome of an investigation and use it to test what the residents expected."],"codes":["13-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Recognition of the strengths and weaknesses of different types of source","13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: Racism and policing","13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: The national and regional context"]},{"q":"Study Sources A and B in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the ways Caribbean people in Notting Hill supported one another? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks, levelled. Level 3 (6–8) needs judgements on usefulness for this narrower enquiry, with provenance used on named claims and contextual knowledge used on the sources. Credit the pardner scheme, the corner shop and the Sunday cafe in A, and B's near-silence on organised self-help, judged as a limit of the source and of what a press photograph could show.","sol":"Source A is well matched to this enquiry because the visitor went looking for exactly this kind of arrangement. She records eight men in one street each paying a pound a week into a hand and taking the whole of it in turn, two of them putting money down on a house; a corner shop selling yam and salt fish; a cafe where the men sit on Sunday; and children fetched from school. Its nature and origin strengthen these: an unpublished report by a West Indian association's own visitor, which is how arrangements that left no paperwork come to be written down at all. Its purpose qualifies the scale rather than the fact, since a welfare body wanted examples of self-help to show what its members were achieving, and eight men in one street is an example, not a measure. My own knowledge explains why the pardner existed: banks and building societies would not lend to Caribbean migrants, so a rotating hand was the only route to a deposit. Source B barely reaches the enquiry, and the reason is itself evidence: a Saturday market photograph shows a queue at a grocer's and a stall of yams and green bananas, which proves a Caribbean trade had taken root, but mutual support happened in front rooms and cafes no press photographer was sent to. A is much the more useful; B supplies only the shop window of what A describes.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["This enquiry is narrower than the sources are, so decide first how much of each one actually touches it.","A source that barely reaches the enquiry can still be judged: ask why it does not, and whether that silence is itself evidence.","Bring one fact that explains why the arrangement in the report had to exist, and use it on the source rather than beside it."],"codes":["13-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Recognition of the strengths and weaknesses of different types of source","13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: The influence of Caribbean cultures on the area","13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: Black activism in the Notting Hill area"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date was Kelso Cochrane attacked in North Kensington?","a":"17 May 1959, shortly after midnight."},{"q":"In which year did Bruce Kenrick set up the Notting Hill Housing Trust?","a":"1963."},{"q":"Where and on what date was the Caribbean carnival sponsored by the West Indian Gazette held?","a":"At St Pancras Town Hall on 30 January 1959."},{"q":"From what date did the Commonwealth Immigrants Act 1962 require a Commonwealth citizen to hold an employment voucher to settle in Britain?","a":"1 July 1962."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Caribbean migration to North Kensington","back":"The Empire Windrush reached Tilbury on 22 June 1948 with 1,027 passengers, about 800 from the Caribbean. Migrants came to North Kensington because its decayed, multi-occupied houses were among the few that would take Black tenants.","code":"13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: The local context of Notting Hill","open":false},{"front":"Houses in multiple occupation in North Kensington","back":"Tall stuccoed houses around Colville, Powis Square and Golborne Road, built for one Victorian family, were split into single-room lettings: a family to a room, one cold tap on a landing, one lavatory in the yard, a paraffin stove.","code":"13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: The local context of Notting Hill","open":false},{"front":"Peter Rachman","back":"Polish-born landlord, died 29 November 1962, who owned over a hundred west London properties. He used the Rent Act of 1957 to clear protected tenants and re-let rooms weekly at high rents to Caribbean migrants; 'Rachmanism' became public in 1963.","code":"13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: The local context of Notting Hill","open":false},{"front":"The Notting Hill Housing Trust","back":"Founded in 1963 by Bruce Kenrick to buy slum houses and re-let them at fair rents. It held five houses in 1963 and seventeen by 1964. Kenrick went on to launch Shelter in December 1966.","code":"13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: The local context of Notting Hill","open":false},{"front":"'Pardner' schemes","back":"Savings clubs among neighbours and workmates: each paid a fixed sum weekly into a 'hand' and each took the whole pot in turn. They raised deposits for houses when banks and building societies would not lend to Caribbean migrants.","code":"13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: The influence of Caribbean cultures on the area","open":false},{"front":"Shebeens","back":"Unlicensed parties in basements and front rooms where drink was sold and records or a sound system played calypso, ska and later reggae into the morning, because pubs shut at eleven and many made Black customers unwelcome.","code":"13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: The influence of Caribbean cultures on the area","open":false},{"front":"All Saints Road","back":"A short street off Westbourne Park Road that became the centre of Black business in the area. Frank Crichlow opened the Mangrove restaurant at number 8 in 1968, having run the Rio cafe at 127 Westbourne Park Road from 1959.","code":"13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: The influence of Caribbean cultures on the area","open":false},{"front":"The Notting Hill riots, 29 August to 5 September 1958","back":"White crowds attacked houses lived in by West Indians with milk bottles, iron bars and petrol bombs. More than 140 people were arrested. On 15 September 1958 Mr Justice Salmon gave nine white youths four years' imprisonment each.","code":"13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: Racism and policing","open":false},{"front":"The murder of Kelso Cochrane","back":"A 31-year-old carpenter from Antigua, stabbed by white youths at the corner of Southam Street and Golborne Road shortly after midnight on 17 May 1959. No one was charged. Over 1,200 mourners followed his funeral on 6 June 1959.","code":"13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: Racism and policing","open":false},{"front":"Oswald Mosley's Union Movement in Kensington North","back":"Founded in 1948, it campaigned in Notting Hill after 1958 under 'Keep Britain White' and demanded repatriation. Mosley stood for Kensington North at the October 1959 general election, came bottom of the poll and lost his deposit.","code":"13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: Racism and policing","open":false},{"front":"Claudia Jones and the West Indian Gazette","back":"Trinidad-born and deported from the United States in 1955, Jones founded the Gazette in 1958 as a paper for Britain's Caribbean population, carrying island news and reporting the discrimination its readers met. She died in December 1964.","code":"13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: Black activism in the Notting Hill area","open":false},{"front":"From the 1959 carnival to the Notting Hill Carnival","back":"The Gazette sponsored an indoor Caribbean carnival at St Pancras Town Hall on 30 January 1959, partly televised. Rhaune Laslett's London Free School organised a neighbourhood fayre in September 1966 whose steel band took the procession into the streets.","code":"13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: Black activism in the Notting Hill area","open":false},{"front":"The Mangrove and the Mangrove Nine","back":"Frank Crichlow opened the Mangrove at 8 All Saints Road in 1968; police raided it twelve times by July 1970, finding no drugs. Nine charged after a march on 9 August 1970 were cleared of riot charges in December 1971.","code":"13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: Black activism in the Notting Hill area","open":false},{"front":"Britain's demand for labour after 1945","back":"The British Nationality Act 1948 confirmed Commonwealth citizens' right to settle. The National Health Service, the railways and the buses were short-staffed, and London Transport recruited in Barbados from 1956. Free entry ended with the Commonwealth Immigrants Act 1962.","code":"13-env — 1 Notting Hill, c1948–c1970: The national and regional context","open":false},{"front":"Records that could answer a follow-up question about Notting Hill","back":"Borough public health and housing inspectors' reports on houses in multiple occupation, council minutes and rehousing lists, street-trading licence registers; police station records and charge sheets, Home Office files; the census of 1961 and 1966; the local weekly press and the West Indian Gazette; oral history interviews.","code":"13-env — 2 Knowledge, selection and use of sources for historical enquiries: Selection of appropriate sources for specific investigations","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The sources written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Source A: From the report of a welfare visitor employed by a West Indian workers' association in London, written for the association's committee on 12 June 1959, three weeks after the murder of Kelso Cochrane. She had spent a fortnight calling on households in the streets between Colville Terrace and Golborne Road, and the report was not published. Written for this room in the style of such a report; it is illustrative, not a real document, and it reproduces no real one. — I called at twenty-six households in a fortnight and was let in at twenty-one. The house I was taken to first was built for one family and now holds eleven people, four of them children; there is one cold tap on the first-floor landing and one lavatory in the yard, and every room has a bed and a paraffin stove. A man who works nights on the Underground pays three pounds a week for a single room, which is a quarter of what he earns, and he showed me a rent book with no address for the landlord on it, only a name for the collector. Nobody I spoke to has a rent book stamped for more than a year. Twice in the fortnight I was told of the collector coming with two other men, and of a family put out on the pavement with their cases while the room was let again the same evening. In seven of the houses the tenants told me they had first been shown a card in a newsagent's window that said no coloured need apply, and that they had walked until they found a landlord who would take them, and paid what he asked. Most of the men I met are in work — the buses and the Underground, the Post Office, the railway yards at Paddington — and several are doing two jobs. Eight men in one street pay a pound a week each into a hand and take the whole of it in turn, and two of them have used it to put money down on a house. There is a shop on the corner now that sells yam and salt fish, and a cafe where the men sit on Sunday, and the talk there was all of the stabbing in May. The women told me they will not go to the launderette after dark and that the children are fetched from school. Everyone I asked said the police had taken statements. Not one of them expected anybody to be charged. A young man told me he had been stopped three times in one week and asked where he had got the money for his coat.","Source B: A photograph printed in a London weekly newspaper on 18 June 1959, under a caption saying that the district is quiet again and North Kensington is getting back to its ordinary business. It was taken on a Saturday morning in a street off Portobello Road by a press photographer sent to the area after the murder of Kelso Cochrane. Written for this room as a description of such a photograph; the image is illustrative, not a real photograph, and it reproduces no real one. — The photograph looks down a narrow street of five-storey terraced houses with peeling stucco fronts and basement areas behind railings. Two ground-floor windows on the left are boarded with new timber. Market stalls stand along the right-hand kerb: a stall of potatoes and cabbages with a trader weighing into a scale pan, and beyond it a smaller stall of yams, green bananas and salt fish under a hand-painted board. A queue of women stands at a grocer's door. In the middle distance two men in suits and hats are talking to a woman with a pram, and a boy beside them holds a folded newspaper so that only the top line of the headline shows. On the left a newsagent's window carries a column of small handwritten cards, too small to read. On the wall above the boarded windows a patch of brickwork has been painted over in a lighter colour, with the tails of chalked letters still showing at its edge. A police constable stands at the kerb with his back to the camera, facing four young men in long jackets and narrow trousers who are leaning on the railings opposite. A bill pasted on a lamp-post announces a public meeting, with the date visible and the rest cut off by the edge of the frame. The far end of the street is in shadow.","How the room uses them: Sources A and B are the pair for every 8-mark usefulness question in this room, and they are the sources for every follow-up question. Every quotation and every detail used in the worked examples, the model paragraphs, the plan and the model answers comes from the two texts above and from nowhere else; check each one against them as an examiner would."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Paper 1 Question 2(a), 8 marks)","items":["Question: 'Study Sources A and B. How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the effects of the murder of Kelso Cochrane on people living in North Kensington? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context.' [8 marks]. This is the paragraph on Source A from the middle of the answer.","Source A is very useful for this enquiry because it was written three weeks after the killing by someone who was let into the houses, and it records the effects in behaviour rather than in sentiment. The women told her they will not go to the launderette after dark and that the children are fetched from school; the cafe where the men sit on Sunday was talking of nothing but the stabbing in May; everyone she asked said the police had taken statements, and not one of them expected anybody to be charged. Its nature and origin make those details unusually trustworthy: this is an unpublished internal report to a West Indian association's own committee, so no editor selected it and no reader outside the community had to be reassured, and the visitor called at twenty-six households rather than interviewing one family. Its purpose bends one thing and should be aimed there rather than at the source as a whole: an association gathering evidence of hardship would ask precisely about fear and about the police, so her report catches the sharpest version of both, and the expectation that nobody would be charged is a prediction rather than an outcome. My own knowledge confirms the prediction while dating it: no one was ever charged with Cochrane's murder, and the community's answer to that was collective — more than 1,200 mourners followed his coffin on 6 June 1959, six days before this report was written. Source A is therefore strong evidence of how the murder changed daily habits and of the community's expectations of the police, and its one soft claim is the one a charge sheet could have settled.","Why this is Level 3: the judgement is on utility 'for the specified enquiry', and it applies a criterion — whether the source gives access to the effects of the murder as they were lived — instead of pronouncing the source good or bad in general. Provenance is used rather than described: the unpublished, internal character of the report is made to strengthen three named details, and the association's evidence-gathering purpose is aimed at one particular claim, which is what 'developed reasoning which takes into account how the provenance affects the usefulness of the source content' requires. The contextual knowledge (no charge was ever brought, and the funeral of 6 June 1959) is used on the source, to confirm one part and to date another, never offered as background. The closing sentence names what the source is good for and what has to be treated carefully, which is the move that separates the top level from the middle."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same as above — 'Study Sources A and B. How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the effects of the murder of Kelso Cochrane on people living in North Kensington?' [8 marks]. Again this is the paragraph on Source A.","Source A is quite useful because it was written by someone who actually went into the houses, so she saw it with her own eyes and knew what it was really like. She says the women will not go to the launderette after dark and the children are fetched from school, which shows that people were frightened after the murder. She also says everybody thought the police would not charge anyone, which shows they did not trust the police. However, the source is biased because she worked for a West Indian association, so she would want to make things look bad. It is also only one fortnight in a few streets, so it does not tell us about the whole area. Overall it is fairly useful but it has its limits.","Why this is Level 2 and not Level 3: it does give 'judgements on source utility for the specified enquiry … using valid criteria', and it selects material from the source to support them, which is what Level 2 asks for. What it lacks is Level 3's 'developed reasoning which takes into account how the provenance affects the usefulness of the source content'. 'Biased because she worked for a West Indian association' is a label pointed at the whole source: it never says which claim that purpose would bend, and it points the wrong way, because an unpublished report to a committee was under less pressure to shape its story than anything printed for a public. There is no contextual knowledge at all — the fact that nobody was ever charged, the funeral of 6 June 1959, the disturbances of the previous summer are all missing — and the mark scheme gives no credit for context that is not used on the sources, so nothing here is confirmed or tested from outside. 'Fairly useful but it has its limits' decides nothing. To lift it: attach every provenance comment to one named claim, and bring one dated fact to bear on the source."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Caribbean migration to North Kensington, 1948 to c1970","items":["The British Nationality Act of 1948 confirmed that citizens of the United Kingdom and Colonies, including people born in the Caribbean, could enter, live and work in Britain freely.","The Empire Windrush docked at Tilbury on 22 June 1948 carrying 1,027 passengers, of whom about 800 gave a Caribbean address as their last home.","Migration grew through the 1950s, encouraged by labour shortages in Britain and by the tightening of the older route to the United States after its immigration Act of 1952.","Caribbean migrants settled in North Kensington because it was poor, unfashionable and full of subdivided houses whose landlords would let rooms to Black tenants when others refused.","The streets that took most of the settlement ran between Colville Terrace, Powis Square, Talbot Road, Westbourne Park Road and Golborne Road, in the north of the borough of Kensington.","Many new arrivals found their first room through a card in a newsagent's window, through a workmate, or through a church or association rather than through an agency.","Work was mainly in transport, the Post Office, the railways, hospitals and factory and building work, often below the level the migrant had been trained for at home.","The Commonwealth Immigrants Act of 1962 required an employment voucher from 1 July 1962 and produced a rush of arrivals in the months before it began.","Once entry was controlled, families followed the men who were already here, so the Caribbean population of the area became more settled and more residential during the 1960s.","By the end of the 1960s the district had Caribbean shops, cafes, churches, barbers, savings schemes, a newspaper read across London and a street carnival of its own."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Peter Rachman, houses of multiple occupation and the Rent Act 1957","items":["A house in multiple occupation was a single dwelling let out room by room, usually with a bed, a paraffin stove and a shared tap and lavatory for the whole building.","The large stuccoed terraces of the Colville and Powis area had been built in the 1850s and 1860s for single families with servants, and were subdivided long before the Caribbean settlement began.","The Rent Act of 1957 decontrolled rents on higher-rated property and on any property once the sitting tenant left, so a landlord who emptied a house could charge new tenants what the market would bear.","Peter Rachman, born in Poland in 1919, built a lettings business in North Kensington and Paddington and owned over a hundred west London properties by the late 1950s.","His methods were to buy cheap property with protected tenants, make life impossible for them until they left, and then re-let the rooms weekly at high rents to people who could not rent elsewhere.","Men were employed to collect rents and to clear rooms; tenants had little security, and rent books often carried no address for the landlord.","Rachman died on 29 November 1962; his name became public in 1963, when the Profumo scandal drew attention to his properties, and 'Rachmanism' entered the language for the exploitation of tenants.","Michael de Freitas, later known as Michael X, worked for Rachman as a rent collector before becoming a Black Power activist.","The Milner Holland report on housing in Greater London, published in March 1965, found the worst conditions of all in privately rented, multi-occupied housing of exactly this kind.","Rachmanism was the extreme case, not the whole story: most landlords in the district were small, but the shortage of housing and the absence of any law against racial discrimination in letting until 1968 gave them all the same power."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Bruce Kenrick and the Notting Hill Housing Trust","items":["Bruce Kenrick was a Presbyterian minister who came to live in North Kensington in the early 1960s and saw the multi-occupied houses at first hand.","He founded the Notting Hill Housing Trust in 1963 with local supporters, as a voluntary body that would buy run-down houses, repair them and let them at fair rents.","The Trust owned five houses in 1963 and seventeen by 1964, growing steadily through the later 1960s.","Its fundraising used the conditions themselves as the argument, advertising with the picture of a family of six living in a single room.","Buying a house and rehousing its tenants also took that house out of the hands of a landlord who was letting it room by room, which is why the Trust measured success in houses bought.","Kenrick went on to help found Shelter, the national campaign for homeless people, which was launched on 1 December 1966.","The Trust's annual reports and appeal literature survive and are a useful source, though they were written to raise money and choose their examples accordingly.","Council action ran alongside the Trust: the borough could inspect houses in multiple occupation, require amenities and rehouse families from its own lists.","The Trust did not end multi-occupation in North Kensington; private letting remained the normal tenure in the district throughout the 1960s.","The elevated Westway road, opened on 28 July 1970, cut through the north of the area, and residents of Acklam Road hung a banner from their roofs at the opening demanding to be rehoused."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Portobello Road market and All Saints Road","items":["Portobello Road has had a street market since Victorian times, selling fruit, vegetables and fish to the surrounding streets.","A second-hand and antiques trade grew at the southern end of the road after the Second World War and became a Saturday institution.","The northern end, towards Golborne Road, stayed a food market and was the part closest to the poorest and most crowded streets.","Caribbean traders and shopkeepers began selling yam, green banana, salt fish and other Caribbean produce, which the ordinary shops did not stock.","Pitches in the market were licensed by the borough council, which kept registers naming each trader and what they were allowed to sell.","All Saints Road, a short street off Westbourne Park Road, became the centre of Black business in the area during the 1960s.","Frank Crichlow, who had come from Trinidad in 1953, opened the Rio cafe at 127 Westbourne Park Road in 1959, and it became a meeting place for Black customers and for the fashionable.","In 1968 Crichlow opened the Mangrove restaurant at 8 All Saints Road, which drew writers, musicians and campaigners as well as diners.","The Metro Club, a youth club on Tavistock Crescent, was another gathering point for young Black people in the area in the later 1960s.","Shops, cafes and market stalls matter to the examiner because they are the visible proof that a migrant community had put down roots, and because they are where the police and the community met most often."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: 'pardner' schemes, shebeens and Caribbean self-help","items":["A 'pardner' (also called a hand or a partner-hand) was a rotating savings club: every member paid the same sum each week and each in turn took the whole pot.","The schemes worked on trust between neighbours, workmates or churchgoers, and were run without paperwork, banks or interest.","They existed because banks and building societies would not lend to Caribbean migrants, so a hand was often the only way to raise a deposit for a house.","Buying a house also meant being able to let rooms to other Caribbean families, which turned self-help into a supply of housing.","Shebeens were unlicensed drinking and music parties held in basements and front rooms, selling drink and playing calypso, ska and later reggae into the morning.","They grew because pubs closed at eleven and because many pubs, dance halls and clubs in the area either refused Black customers or made them unwelcome.","Blues dances and sound systems in private houses became the basis of a music scene that later fed the street carnival.","Churches, island associations and workers' associations provided welfare, advice and funerals, and kept in touch with families at home.","Caribbean grocers, barbers, tailors and cafes supplied goods and services the existing shops did not, and served as informal advice centres.","The examiner rewards the link rather than the list: each of these arrangements answers a specific exclusion, so name the exclusion whenever you name the institution."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Notting Hill riots, 29 August to 5 September 1958","items":["Tension had been building all summer, with attacks on Black men in west London and 'Keep Britain White' slogans chalked in the streets.","On the night of 23 to 24 August 1958 nine white youths drove around west London attacking Black men at random, putting three of them in hospital.","The disturbances began on 29 August 1958 after a crowd gathered around Majbritt Morrison, a white Swedish woman, and her Jamaican husband outside Latimer Road station.","For a week, crowds of hundreds gathered at night and attacked houses lived in by West Indians with milk bottles, iron bars and petrol bombs; West Indians defended their houses and streets.","The trouble ran until 5 September 1958, and the police arrested more than 140 people over the fortnight.","Of 108 people charged with serious offences, 72 were white and 36 were Black.","On 15 September 1958 Mr Justice Salmon sentenced the nine youths from the August car attacks to four years' imprisonment each at the Old Bailey, saying that everyone had the right to walk the streets free from fear whatever the colour of their skin.","Newspapers across Britain covered the riots heavily, and the events were reported in the Caribbean, where they damaged Britain's reputation.","The government's response was to consider immigration control rather than anti-discrimination law, a line that led to the Commonwealth Immigrants Act of 1962.","Within the community the riots produced organisation rather than retreat, including the West Indian Gazette's carnival of January 1959."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the murder of Kelso Cochrane, 17 May 1959","items":["Kelso Cochrane was a carpenter from Antigua, aged 31, who had come to Britain in 1954 and was living in North Kensington.","He was walking home with an injured thumb, having been to hospital, when he was attacked by a group of white youths shortly after midnight on 17 May 1959.","He was stabbed at the corner of Southam Street and Golborne Road and died in hospital about an hour later.","The police said at the time that the killing was not racially motivated, which the local Caribbean community did not accept.","No one was ever charged with the murder, although suspects were questioned at the time and the case has been reviewed since.","His funeral on 6 June 1959 drew more than 1,200 mourners, who followed the coffin from St Michael and All Angels church to Kensal Green cemetery.","The funeral was attended by Black and white residents, church leaders and politicians, and was one of the first public demonstrations of Caribbean political presence in Britain.","Local organisations pressed the government to act, and the killing kept national attention on Notting Hill through the summer of 1959.","The murder came five months before the general election at which Oswald Mosley stood in Kensington North, and it helped to discredit his campaign.","For the environment as a whole the murder is the hinge: it turned the events of 1958 from a riot into a settled grievance, and it is behind much of the activism that followed."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Mosley's Union Movement, the White Defence League and the 1959 election","items":["Oswald Mosley had led the British Union of Fascists before the war and was interned during it; he founded the Union Movement in 1948.","After the 1958 disturbances the Union Movement campaigned hard in North Kensington, holding open-air street meetings and demanding that Caribbean migrants be sent home.","Its slogan in the area was 'Keep Britain White', and it blamed migrants for the housing shortage and for unemployment.","Mosley stood as the Union Movement candidate for Kensington North at the general election of October 1959, calling for repatriation and for a ban on mixed marriages.","He came bottom of the poll and lost his deposit; the seat was held by Labour.","The White Defence League was a separate and smaller group, founded by Colin Jordan in 1957.","It worked from a house at 74 Princedale Road in Notting Hill, which served as office and printing shop, and produced a paper called Black and White News.","Its members chalked and posted slogans through the district, and the group merged into the newly formed British National Party in February 1960.","These groups did not create local hostility, but they organised it, gave it slogans and made the streets feel more dangerous to Black residents.","The failure of Mosley's campaign is as important as the campaign itself: open racial politics did not win votes in Kensington North, even in 1959."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Claudia Jones, the West Indian Gazette and the carnival","items":["Claudia Jones was born in Trinidad in 1915, grew up in New York, became a communist organiser and was deported from the United States in 1955, settling in London.","She founded the West Indian Gazette in 1958, a monthly paper written for Britain's Caribbean population and produced on very little money.","The paper carried news from the islands, campaigned against discrimination in housing and employment, and reported the attacks its readers were meeting.","After the 1958 riots Jones argued that the answer was to show Britain a Caribbean culture rather than a Caribbean problem.","The Gazette sponsored an indoor Caribbean carnival at St Pancras Town Hall on 30 January 1959, with music, dancing and a beauty contest, and part of it was televised.","Further indoor carnivals followed in the early 1960s in hired halls around London.","Claudia Jones died on 24 December 1964 and is buried in Highgate cemetery.","The street festival came from a second root: Rhaune Laslett and others founded the London Free School in Notting Hill in March 1966 as a community adult education project.","Out of it came a neighbourhood fayre in September 1966, in which a steel band led a children's procession out into the streets and drew people from every community in the area.","The two roots together — a Caribbean carnival in a hall and a neighbourhood procession in the streets — grew into the Notting Hill Carnival of later years."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Frank Crichlow, the Mangrove, the Black Panthers and the Mangrove Nine","items":["Frank Crichlow was born in Port of Spain, Trinidad, in 1932 and came to Britain in 1953.","He opened the Rio cafe at 127 Westbourne Park Road in 1959, which became a meeting place for Black customers in an area where many cafes and pubs were unwelcoming.","In 1968 he opened the Mangrove restaurant at 8 All Saints Road, serving Caribbean food and drawing writers, musicians, activists and local residents.","The police raided the Mangrove twelve times between January 1969 and July 1970, saying it was a centre of drug dealing; no drugs were ever found.","The British Black Panther Movement was founded in London in 1968 by the Nigerian writer Obi Egbuna, and Altheia Jones-LeCointe became its leading figure.","On 9 August 1970 about 150 people marched from the Mangrove to Notting Hill police station to protest against the raids.","Nine organisers, including Crichlow, Jones-LeCointe, Darcus Howe and Barbara Beese, were arrested and charged with incitement to riot; they became known as the Mangrove Nine.","Two of the defendants conducted their own defence; their demand for an all-Black jury was refused, but they used challenges to secure a jury that included Black jurors.","The trial at the Old Bailey ended in December 1971 with all nine cleared of the incitement to riot charges, and the judge acknowledged that there had been evidence of racial hatred on both sides, including within the Metropolitan Police.","The case is used as the point at which a British court first accepted that racial hostility existed inside the police, and it made All Saints Road a symbol of Black resistance."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the national context — reconstruction, the Commonwealth, the Sixties and the law","items":["Britain came out of the Second World War with cities to rebuild, factories to restaff and a labour shortage that lasted into the 1960s.","The National Health Service opened in July 1948 and recruited nurses and staff from the Caribbean; the railways, the buses and the Post Office did the same.","London Transport sent recruiters to Barbados from 1956 and lent workers the money for their fares, to be repaid out of wages.","Migration from the Caribbean was legal and uncontrolled because migrants were British subjects under the British Nationality Act of 1948.","The Commonwealth Immigrants Act of 1962 introduced employment vouchers from 1 July 1962 and ended free entry.","The Race Relations Act of 1965 made discrimination in public places unlawful and created the Race Relations Board, but did not cover housing or employment.","The Race Relations Act of 1968 extended the ban to housing, employment and services and set up the Community Relations Commission.","Michael de Freitas, known as Michael X, became in 1967 the first person imprisoned under the 1965 Act, for incitement to racial hatred, and was sentenced to twelve months.","London in the 1960s was two cities at once: the 'Swinging Sixties' of music, boutiques and cheap rooms drew students and artists into Notting Hill, while the Milner Holland report of March 1965 described the housing crisis in the same streets.","Policing in London relied on powers such as arrest on suspicion under the Vagrancy Act of 1824, which bore hardest on young Black men and fed the conflicts of 1970."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: local and national sources for enquiries into Notting Hill","items":["Borough public health and housing inspectors' reports on houses in multiple occupation: address, number of lettings, amenities, complaints and action taken.","Borough council minutes, rate books, rehousing lists and street-trading licence registers for the Portobello Road market.","Metropolitan Police station records and charge sheets, and Home Office files on the 1958 disturbances and on immigration control.","Local weekly newspapers such as the Kensington Post: raids, court cases, funerals, letters and advertisements, written for readers inside the borough.","Publications written for the Caribbean community, above all the West Indian Gazette from 1958, which printed what the local press left out.","Housing charity records: the Notting Hill Housing Trust's annual reports and appeal literature from 1963, written to raise money.","The census of 1961 and the sample census of 1966: households, birthplaces, rooms and shared amenities, street by street.","Hansard and government reports, including the Milner Holland report of March 1965 and the debates on the 1962, 1965 and 1968 Acts.","National newspapers, photographs and television reports, heaviest in 1958, 1959 and at the Mangrove trial, written for readers outside the district.","Personal records: memoirs, letters home, residents' own photographs, and oral history interviews recorded with Caribbean settlers from the 1980s onwards."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the source moves this paper rewards","items":["Provenance means three things: nature (what kind of document or image it is), origin (who made it, when and where) and purpose (what it was made to do).","Usefulness is always usefulness for the enquiry the question names; the same photograph can be very useful for one enquiry and useless for the next, so write the enquiry down before you start.","Content first: say what the source tells you, or lets you infer, about the named enquiry, and anchor it on a short quoted phrase or a named detail in the picture.","Provenance second, and aimed: explain how the nature, origin or purpose makes that particular inference stronger or weaker. 'The source is biased' scores nothing because it changes no inference.","Context third, and used: bring one piece of your own knowledge that confirms, qualifies or explains what the source says; contextual knowledge earns nothing unless it is linked to evaluating the source.","Ask who a source was written for. A report to a committee, an inspector's file and a police charge sheet were made to be acted on; a newspaper photograph and its caption were made to be looked at by strangers.","A limitation is often a use in disguise: a caption saying a district is quiet again is bad evidence of calm and excellent evidence of the impression a paper wanted its readers outside the area to hold.","Separate the arranged part of an image from the incidental part: boarded windows, a painted-over wall and a column of cards in a window are worth more than the posed group in the middle.","Finish with a comparative judgement: which source is more useful for this enquiry, on what criterion, and for which part of it — not which source is 'better'.","The follow-up answer has four parts and each must fit the one before: a detail taken from the source, a question that detail raises, a type of source that could answer it, and what is inside that type of source that would settle the question."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 1 Question 2(a): 'How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the effects of the murder of Kelso Cochrane on people living in North Kensington?' [8 marks]","items":["The question as printed: 'Study Sources A and B. How useful are Sources A and B for an enquiry into the effects of the murder of Kelso Cochrane on people living in North Kensington? Explain your answer, using Sources A and B and your knowledge of the historical context.' No stimulus points are printed and no SPaG marks are awarded; the two sources are the whole material, and both must be used.","Decode the enquiry before reading either source. 'The effects of the murder' means what changed for people who lived there — how they moved about the streets, what they expected of the police, what they said to one another, and what the district looked like to outsiders. Decide which of those each source can reach, because that is your criterion for usefulness.","Timing: about 13 minutes. Two minutes reading and annotating both sources against the enquiry, nine minutes writing two paragraphs and a judgement, two minutes checking that every provenance comment is attached to a named claim and that each piece of context is used on a source.","Paragraph 1, Source A, content: the women who will not go to the launderette after dark and the children fetched from school; the Sunday cafe where the talk was all of the stabbing in May; everyone saying the police had taken statements and nobody expecting a charge; the young man stopped three times in one week.","Paragraph 1, Source A, provenance used twice. Nature and origin strengthen it: an unpublished report to a West Indian association's own committee, written three weeks after the killing by a visitor who called at twenty-six households, so no editor selected it and nobody outside the community had to be reassured. Purpose qualifies one claim: an association gathering evidence of hardship asks about fear and about the police, so the report holds the sharpest version of both, and 'nobody expected anybody to be charged' is a prediction rather than a result.","Paragraph 1, Source A, context used on the source: no one was ever charged with the murder, and more than 1,200 mourners followed the coffin on 6 June 1959, six days before the report was written — which confirms the prediction and shows the effect the report does not mention, that the community answered with a public demonstration. Interim judgement: very useful for the effects as they were lived, with one claim that a charge sheet could have tested.","Paragraph 2, Source B, content: two ground-floor windows boarded with new timber; a patch of wall painted over with the tails of chalked letters still showing; the market trading normally with a stall of yams and green bananas beside a stall of potatoes and cabbages; a queue at a grocer's door; a constable at the kerb facing four young men in long jackets across the street; a bill for a public meeting on a lamp-post.","Paragraph 2, Source B, provenance used twice. Nature and purpose weaken it on feeling: a press photograph taken on a Saturday market morning and captioned to say the district is quiet again was made for readers outside the area, so it shows the most orderly hour of the week and asserts a calm it cannot demonstrate. The same provenance strengthens it for what nobody arranged — the new timber, the painted-out slogan, the constable's position — which are the physical effects of a year of violence and of the effort to clear it away.","Paragraph 2, Source B, context used on the source: the White Defence League chalked its slogans through these streets from Princedale Road, so the painted patch dates an attempt to erase them, and a photograph published a month after the killing shows a district being presented as normal while the case was still unsolved. Interim judgement: weak as a measure of how anyone felt, useful as evidence of the marks left on the street and of the version offered to outsiders.","The evaluation thread that must run through both paragraphs: a purpose does not cancel a source, it redirects it. Say for each what the purpose makes the source bad evidence of and what it makes it good evidence of, and you have made the top-level move twice.","What the judgement must say: not 'both are useful in different ways'. Name the criterion — access to what the murder changed for the people living there — and decide on it. Source A is much the more useful, because it is unpublished, specific, close in date and gathered inside the houses; Source B is the more useful for the physical traces and for how the district was shown to readers elsewhere. Together they give what neither reaches alone: a community altering its daily habits while the street outside was being tidied and declared quiet."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:03:49.747808Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"All seven recorded fixes are present in the kit and the diff against the pre-fix text touches nothing else: 'nine months after the disturbances' is gone from both its occurrences and Source B's windows are now read as recent damage because the photographer was sent after the May 1959 stabbing; Q11 and worked example 3 both follow up Source A, as every printed 2(b) in levels.md and the specimen does; the third hints of Q8 and Q11 no longer name a record; the Gazette is dated to March 1958, months before the August disturbances, and 30 January 1959 is 'not yet five months past'; card 12 reads 'cleared of riot charges' in 40 words; and Q12's indicative content drops the unreadable cards. I re-solved all sixteen questions and re-checked the new facts on the web — section 6 of the Race Relations Act 1968 did make discriminatory accommodation advertisements and notices unlawful, arrest on suspicion rested on the Vagrancy Act 1824, and charge sheets, occurrence books and the Commissioner's annual reports are real Metropolitan Police record types held at the National Archives — with nothing wrong. Two residual faults are worth a teacher's eye: Q12's model answer still uses the cards its key has just dropped, and worked example 3 now works the detail Q10's key credits. Provenance¹, meaning nature, origin and purpose, is still handled correctly throughout, and both room-written sources remain plausible, flagged as illustrative and free of any real document's text."}}},"B1-1":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"B1-1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This is the first key topic of the Anglo-Saxon and Norman depth study, and it is the one the booklet B questions come back to most often, because everything between 1060 and 1066 is either evidence for how England worked before the Conquest or a cause of what happened on 14 October 1066. The questions ask you to describe one feature each of two separate aspects of the period, to explain why something happened, and to judge how far one factor mattered most. None of those can be answered with a story. You need the machinery of Anglo-Saxon government, the wealth and reach of the house of Godwin, the three rival claims of 1066 and the three battles, each with names and dates you can place exactly, so that you can select the two or three pieces of evidence that actually answer the question in front of you and leave the rest out.","idea":[{"h":"The king, the earls and the shire: how England was actually run","t":"England in 1060 was one of the best-governed kingdoms in Europe, and the reason is that royal authority did not depend on the king being present. The king alone made law, granted land by charter and summoned the fyrd, and his orders went out as writs sealed with his own seal. He could levy the geld, a tax assessed on hides of land, and he controlled the currency: the silver pennies struck in boroughs all over England came from dies he issued, and the coinage was periodically called in and reminted. He took counsel from the Witan, the assembly of earls, bishops, abbots and leading thegns, which also chose the next king from among those thought throneworthy. Beneath him England was divided into shires, each with a shire reeve as the king's officer, and shires into hundreds, called wapentakes in the Danelaw; the hundred court met about once a month and the shire court twice a year. Order rested on the community rather than on any police force: free men over twelve stood in tithings answerable for one another, a victim raised the hue and cry, accusations were answered by oath-helping, and where oaths failed the Church supervised trial by ordeal. Compensation was fixed by rank through the wergild. A top-level answer never lists this machinery. It uses it to argue that England was a kingdom worth conquering and, because so much of it ran through a handful of earls, one that could be captured whole by whoever won a single battle.","code":"B1-1 — 1 Anglo-Saxon government, economy and society: Monarchy and government"},{"h":"Land, labour and the Church: the society the Normans took over","t":"Perhaps two million people lived in England and about nine in ten of them in the countryside, farming strips in open fields with shared ox teams and owing rent, labour or both to a lord. The ranks were land and duty combined: thegns, who held roughly five hides and owed service in the fyrd and attendance at court; ceorls, free peasants who farmed their own land but still owed dues; geburs and cottars, who worked their lord's land for most of the week; and slaves, about a tenth of the population when Domesday Book counted them twenty years later. Towns were few but rich. London was much the largest, followed by York, Winchester, Norwich and Lincoln, and a borough meant a market, a mint and tolls; wool and cloth made England a country foreigners came to buy from, which is one reason it was worth invading. The Church reached every part of this: bishops sat in the Witan and in the shire court, tithes and minsters bound villages to it, and Edward the Confessor spent his last years rebuilding Westminster Abbey, consecrated on 28 December 1065, eight days before he died. Its weak point was at the top, since Stigand held Canterbury together with Winchester and had taken his pallium from Benedict X, later judged an antipope. A top-level answer uses the wealth to explain why England attracted three invasions in one year, and uses Stigand to explain why William could present a conquest as a reform.","code":"B1-1 — 1 Anglo-Saxon government, economy and society: The economy and social system"},{"h":"The house of Godwin: a subject family that outgrew the crown","t":"Godwin was made an earl by Cnut in 1018 and held the whole of Wessex by about 1020, and he served four kings in turn. In 1045 his daughter Edith married Edward the Confessor, which made him the king's father-in-law. The turning point came in 1051, when Godwin refused Edward's order to punish the men of Dover after a fight with the followers of Count Eustace of Boulogne and the whole family was outlawed; they came back with a fleet in 1052 and were restored without a battle, because no one in England would fight for the king against them. The Norman archbishop Robert of Jumieges fled and Stigand took Canterbury, so the crisis also removed Edward's foreign counterweight. Godwin died in 1053 and Harold succeeded him in Wessex; Tostig received Northumbria in 1055, and in 1057 Gyrth took East Anglia and Leofwine the shires around London, so four brothers held earldoms at once and the family's estates together were worth more than the king's own lands. Harold then added the one thing Edward lacked, a military reputation: in 1063 he and Tostig campaigned so effectively against Gruffydd ap Llywelyn of Wales that Gruffydd was killed by his own men and his head was sent to Harold. A top-level answer treats this as a chain rather than a list, showing how each gain made the next possible, and uses it to explain why the Witan in January 1066 could look at a subject and see a king.","code":"B1-1 — 2 The last years of Edward the Confessor and the succession crisis: The significance and power of the house of Godwin"},{"h":"An oath, a rebellion and a death: how the crisis was made, 1064 to 1066","t":"Three events in Edward's last two years turned a succession problem into a crisis. First the embassy: in about 1064 Harold crossed the Channel, was shipwrecked on the coast of Ponthieu and held by Count Guy until William secured his release. He then campaigned with the duke in Brittany against Duke Conan and was given Norman arms, and Norman sources say that he swore on holy relics to support William's claim, although they disagree on the place: William of Poitiers puts the oath at Bonneville-sur-Touques, the Bayeux Tapestry names Bagias, that is Bayeux, and Orderic Vitalis places it at Rouen. No English source mentions the oath at all, and the purpose of the journey is disputed; one suggestion is that Harold went to free his brother Wulfnoth and his nephew Hakon, hostages in Normandy since 1051. Second the rising: Tostig's rule in Northumbria was resented for the heavy geld he levied, for the killing of Gamel son of Orm and Ulf son of Dolfin in his own house at York in 1064 under his safe conduct, for the death of Gospatric at the royal court at Christmas 1064, and for his absence in Rome in 1061 when Malcolm III of Scotland raided the north. In October 1065 the Northumbrian thegns seized York, killed about two hundred of his men, emptied his treasury, outlawed him and chose Morcar; Harold met them at Northampton and the settlement was made at Oxford on 28 October, confirming Morcar and exiling Tostig to Flanders. Third, Edward died on 5 January 1066. A top-level answer shows that the first event armed William and the second armed Hardrada, so that the third opened a door on three sides at once.","code":"B1-1 — 2 The last years of Edward the Confessor and the succession crisis: Harold Godwinson's embassy to Normandy"},{"h":"Three rivals, three kinds of claim","t":"Edgar Aethling had the best blood: grandson of Edmund Ironside and son of Edward the Exile, who returned to England in 1057 and died almost at once. But in 1066 Edgar was about fourteen, with no lands, no following and no military record, and England had learned what a royal minority cost. Harald Hardrada had been king of Norway since 1046 and had made his name in the Varangian Guard at Constantinople; his claim rested on an agreement of the late 1030s between Magnus of Norway and Harthacnut that whichever survived would take the other's kingdom, and his motive was plain conquest, helped after 1065 by his alliance with the exiled Tostig. William had been Duke of Normandy since 1035, when he succeeded his father Robert at about the age of seven, and had spent thirty years fighting to hold his duchy. His claim had three parts: kinship, since Edward's mother Emma was William's great-aunt, making the two men first cousins once removed; designation, since he said Edward had promised him the succession in the years when the court was full of Normans; and Harold's oath of about 1064. He then did what neither rival did and turned the claim into a legal case: William of Poitiers says the enterprise won the approval of Pope Alexander II and a banner sent from Rome, although he is the only writer of the time to say so and historians dispute it. A top-level answer never simply describes the three claims. It names the standard by which a claim is being judged, because Edgar wins on blood, Harold on English custom, and William only on the test that the year actually applied, which was force.","code":"B1-1 — 3 The rival claimants for the throne: The motives and claims of William, Hardrada and Edgar"},{"h":"The Witan chooses: Harold's coronation and his nine months as king","t":"In England the throne was not simply inherited. The Witan chose the next king from among the throneworthy, and its choice made the king. The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle records that the dying Edward commended the kingdom to Harold's protection on 5 January 1066; the Witan was already gathered at Westminster for the consecration of the new abbey, it accepted him, and he was crowned on 6 January, the day Edward was buried, probably by Ealdred, Archbishop of York. Norman writers claimed the uncanonical Stigand had performed the ceremony, which would have made the coronation easier to attack. Harold then governed like a man expecting invasion. His pennies carried the word PAX. He rode north to York early in 1066 and married Ealdgyth, sister of Earl Edwin of Mercia and Earl Morcar of Northumbria, binding the family that had displaced his own brother. A comet, now known to be Halley's, was seen across England in late April and was taken as an omen. In May Tostig raided the Isle of Wight and the south-east coast, was driven off in the north by Edwin and Morcar and took refuge in Scotland. Harold kept the fyrd and the fleet on watch along the south coast all summer and stood them down on 8 September when the provisions ran out. A top-level answer uses the coronation to argue about legitimacy and the nine months to argue about preparation, and notices that the single decision of 8 September undid most of the rest.","code":"B1-1 — 3 The rival claimants for the throne: The Witan and the coronation and reign of Harold Godwinson"},{"h":"Two battles in Yorkshire that decided a battle in Sussex","t":"Hardrada's fleet, about three hundred ships according to the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, sailed down the east coast and up the Ouse to Riccall. On 20 September 1066 he and Tostig met the levies of Edwin and Morcar at Gate Fulford, two miles south of York. The ground decided it: the English line was hemmed in by marsh and river, and when they pushed the weaker Norwegian wing Hardrada counter-attacked from the stronger flank, rolled up their line and drove great numbers into the marsh. York surrendered and agreed to hand over hostages at Stamford Bridge. Harold, watching the south coast, took his housecarls north and covered the better part of two hundred miles from London to Yorkshire in about four days, raising shire levies as he passed. On 25 September he surprised the Norwegians seven or eight miles east of York: much of their force was still at the ships at Riccall and many of those present had left their mail behind. Hardrada and Tostig were both killed, and the Chronicle says the survivors needed only twenty-four ships to sail home. The significance runs both ways, and a top-level answer must say so. Stamford Bridge destroyed the Viking claim for good; but Fulford had already destroyed the only other field army in England, and Stamford Bridge cost Harold housecarls he could not replace and left him two hundred and fifty miles from the coast William was about to land on.","code":"B1-1 — 3 The rival claimants for the throne: Reasons for, and significance of, the outcome of Gate Fulford and Stamford Bridge"},{"h":"14 October 1066: the day on the ridge","t":"William landed at Pevensey on 28 September 1066, moved along the coast to Hastings, raised a castle there and set about ravaging Harold's own estates in Sussex, which forced the king to come to him quickly rather than starve him out. Harold was back in London by about 6 October and marched into Sussex; the battle was fought on 14 October on the ridge at Senlac, where Battle Abbey now stands. Estimates put each army at something like seven thousand men, though no reliable count survives. The English fought on foot in a dense shield wall along the ridge, housecarls with two-handed Danish axes in front and the fyrd behind, with almost no cavalry and few archers. William drew up in three divisions, Bretons on the left, Normans in the centre, French and Flemish on the right, and combined archers, infantry and cavalry. The fighting lasted from mid-morning until dusk. Arrows shot uphill and horsemen charging into a wall of shields achieved little; early in the day the Breton division broke and fled, a rumour ran that William was dead, and he pushed back his helmet to show his face and rallied the line. Part of the English line chased the Bretons downhill and was destroyed in the open, and later the Normans repeated the movement as a deliberate feigned retreat. Gyrth and Leofwine were killed, and Harold died near dusk. A top-level answer uses the shape of the day, not the narrative: an unbreakable position that could only be lost by leaving it.","code":"B1-1 — 4 The Norman invasion: The Battle of Hastings"},{"h":"Why William won: decisions, conditions and luck","t":"Sort the reasons into three piles and the question answers itself. William's own decisions: months of preparation, a landing at an undefended shore, a castle at Hastings, the ravaging of Sussex to force a quick battle, a mixed army of Normans, Bretons and Flemings held together by pay, plunder and a war that Norman writers say Pope Alexander II had blessed, the combination of archers, infantry and cavalry, the nerve to raise his helmet when the Bretons ran, and the tactical intelligence to turn their flight into a repeatable feigned retreat. Conditions he inherited: a wind that kept him in harbour until after Harold had stood down the fleet on 8 September; Gate Fulford, which destroyed Edwin and Morcar's levies so that no northern army came south; and Stamford Bridge, which cost Harold veterans and three weeks of marching. Harold's own choices: fighting on 14 October rather than waiting for the shire levies still coming in, and a line that broke its own discipline to pursue. The Bayeux Tapestry shows a figure struck in the eye by an arrow and another cut down by a horseman, and which of them is Harold is disputed, but the effect is not: an English army fought for a named lord, and when Harold and both his brothers were dead there was no one left to fight for. A top-level answer ranks these piles against a stated test, usually whether a factor created the opportunity or took it, and then says which would have been enough on its own.","code":"B1-1 — 4 The Norman invasion: Reasons for William's victory"}],"examples":[{"title":"One feature for 1(a) and one for 1(b), built so that both halves score","setup":"The question is printed in two parts: 1(a) Describe one feature of law and order in Anglo-Saxon England in the years before 1066. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the Witan in the years before 1066. [2 marks] No bullets and no sources are given. From your notes, on law and order: every free man over twelve stood in a tithing whose members answered for one another; a victim raised the hue and cry and the neighbourhood was bound to join the chase; an accusation was answered by oath-helping, in which men of standing swore to the defendant's word; where the oaths failed the case went to the ordeal, held in a church under a priest; and compensation was fixed by rank through the wergild, far higher for a thegn than for a ceorl. On the Witan: it was the assembly of earls, bishops, abbots and leading thegns; it met when and where the king summoned it; it advised on law, taxation and war; it could outlaw a man, as it outlawed Earl Aelfgar of Mercia in 1055; and it chose the next king from among those regarded as throneworthy. Choose what each part will say.","steps":["Treat the parts as two separate two-mark questions: each wants a single feature and one piece of supporting information, so a second feature squeezed into one part earns nothing, and what answers the first part cannot be used again for the second.","For 1(a), choose by what you can prove rather than by what sounds grandest: collective policing comes with a precise mechanism attached, whereas 'the Anglo-Saxons were harsh' has nothing behind it. Feature: order was kept by the community itself rather than by any royal police force. Supporting information: every free man over twelve stood in a tithing whose members answered for one another, and a victim raised the hue and cry, which the whole neighbourhood was bound to join.","Do the same for 1(b) with the Witan. Feature: the Witan was the assembly through which the king took counsel and which chose his successor. Supporting information: it was made up of earls, bishops, abbots and leading thegns, and it could outlaw a man, as it outlawed Earl Aelfgar of Mercia in 1055.","Test both halves before you stop: each names one feature of its own aspect of the period, and each carries one piece of hard evidence rather than a second general statement."],"cues":["How many features does each part want, and what does the second mark in each part pay for?","Which part of Anglo-Saxon law keeping can you attach a precise mechanism to?","Who sat in the Witan, and what could it do that no single earl could?","What has to be true of each half before you put your pen down?"]},{"title":"Choosing three reasons for a twelve-mark 'Explain why'","setup":"The question is: Explain why William of Normandy did not land in England until 28 September 1066. You may use the following in your answer: the assembly at Dives-sur-Mer; the wind. You must also use information of your own. Your notes contain: William's fleet and army gathered at the mouth of the river Dives in Normandy from about the middle of August 1066; ships had to be built or requisitioned and thousands of men and horses fed and held together while they waited; the fleet lay at Dives for about a month and then moved up the coast to Saint-Valery-sur-Somme, nearer to England, in mid-September; it sailed on the evening of 27 September and landed at Pevensey the next morning; William had to leave Normandy safe behind him, and his wife Matilda and their young son Robert governed it in his absence; his army was not only Norman but Breton, Flemish and French, recruited with promises of English land for the lords and pay and plunder for the rest; and Norman writers, chiefly William of Poitiers, say he had obtained the approval of Pope Alexander II, although no other source of the time records it and historians dispute it.","steps":["Group the notes into causes instead of listing them: shipping and supply (building, victualling and holding an army at Dives), weather (a month windbound and then the move to Saint-Valery), and politics (a duchy to secure, allies to recruit and a case to argue abroad).","Two of those groups are the ones the bullets point at, so the third has to be developed hardest, because an answer that stays inside the bullets is capped however well written it is.","Turn each group into a causal chain rather than a fact: an army of Bretons, Flemings and Frenchmen recruited on the promise of English land could not be kept idle cheaply or safely, so every week of the wrong wind made the enterprise harder to hold together, which is why the move to Saint-Valery was worth the risk.","Link the three at the end: the wind decided the day, but the month was decided by what William had chosen to build, because a force large enough to beat an English army was also a force too large to launch on the first fair morning."],"cues":["Which of these notes belong together as a single cause?","Which of your three causes is not prompted by the bullets?","Why would a borrowed and promised army get harder to hold the longer it waited?","What decided the month, as against what decided the day?"]},{"title":"Setting the criterion for a sixteen-mark judgement","setup":"The question is: 'The wealth of England was the main reason it was invaded in 1066.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: the wool trade; the boroughs. You must also use information of your own. The facts available to you are: perhaps two million people lived in England and about nine in ten of them in the countryside; wool and cloth were what foreign merchants came to buy; a borough meant a market, a mint and tolls, and London was well ahead of York, Winchester, Norwich and Lincoln; the king could levy the geld, a land tax assessed on hides, through the shires, and the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle puts the tribute of 1018 at seventy-two thousand pounds from the country with a further ten and a half thousand from London, figures many historians think too high; England had been conquered by Swein and by Cnut within living memory; Edward the Confessor died childless on 5 January 1066 and English custom laid down no fixed rule for choosing his successor.","steps":["Name the test in your first sentence, because the judgement marks go to criteria that are stated and then applied: is the question what made England worth invading, or what made 1066 the year in which it could be invaded?","Sort the facts by that test: wool and cloth, the boroughs with their markets, mints and tolls, and a tax system that could be made to yield on something like the scale the Chronicle reports are what made the prize; the childless death of 5 January and a custom with no fixed rule are what made the opening.","Build the strongest version of the other side before you answer it: England had been just as rich in 1060 and in 1050, and no fleet came, so wealth on its own cannot explain a particular year.","Answer it with the test rather than with a summary: wealth is why three separate men thought England worth a fleet rather than a raid, and the empty throne is why they all came at once, so the statement is right about the motive and wrong about the timing."],"cues":["What exactly are you being asked to weigh against what?","Which of these facts made the prize, and which made the opening?","What is the best case that can be made against the statement?","How do you use your test to settle it instead of restating both sides?"]},{"title":"From story to causal chain: how Normans came to run so much of England","setup":"You are asked: Explain why Norman influence was so strong in England during the reign of Edward the Confessor. The events, in order, are: in 1002 Emma of Normandy married King Aethelred, joining the English royal house to the Norman ducal house; when the Danes conquered England her sons were sent to Normandy, and Edward spent most of the years between 1013 and 1041 there; he returned in 1041 and became king in 1042, bringing Norman clerks and soldiers with him; Robert of Jumieges, abbot of the Norman house that had sheltered him, was made Bishop of London in 1044 and Archbishop of Canterbury in 1051; Edward's Norman nephew Ralph was given an earldom on the Welsh border, where his men built castles, a thing England had not seen before; in September 1051 Godwin and his sons were outlawed and the Normans at court had no counterweight left; in 1052 the family came back with a fleet, Robert of Jumieges fled and Stigand took Canterbury. Turn this sequence into an explanation rather than a story.","steps":["Ask of each event what it made possible for somebody else: the marriage of 1002 did not put Normans into English offices, but it made Normandy the obvious refuge in 1013, and it was the refuge that produced a king who had grown up among Normans.","Carry the chain through the appointments: a king who owed his safety to Normandy staffed his household, his Church and his border from it, so the influence arrived as patronage rather than as conquest, and by 1051 it held the see of Canterbury and a stretch of the Welsh march with castles on it.","Join the two halves where they meet: the outlawry of 1051 and the return of 1052 show that the whole structure rested on the king alone, because the moment the Godwins came back the archbishop had to run for it and an Englishman took his place.","Now write the sentence the chain supports: Norman influence before 1066 was personal to Edward and therefore reversible, and it could be swept away in a single year, as 1051 and 1052 between them proved."],"cues":["What did Emma's marriage make possible nearly thirty years later?","Why did an exile in Normandy show up in English Church appointments?","What did the events of 1051 and 1052 reveal about how deep the influence went?","What sentence does the chain now entitle you to write?"]},{"title":"Precise evidence beats general knowledge","setup":"Two students answer: Explain why Harold Godwinson campaigned in Wales in 1063. Student A writes: 'The Welsh kept attacking England and Harold wanted to stop them, and he also wanted to look good.' Student B writes: 'Gruffydd ap Llywelyn had made himself ruler of the whole of Wales by 1055 and took in the exiled Earl Aelfgar; together they beat Edward's nephew Earl Ralph outside Hereford on 24 October 1055 and burned the town, and in June 1056 Bishop Leofgar of Hereford was killed fighting him. At Christmas 1062 Harold raided Gruffydd's court at Rhuddlan and almost caught him, and in 1063 he took a fleet round the coast while Tostig led an army overland from the north.' You also know that Gruffydd was killed by his own men on 5 August 1063 and that his head was sent to Harold. Decide what separates the two answers, and work out how Student A's sentence could be rescued.","steps":["Test each sentence by asking what could actually be credited: Student A offers two assertions and not one name, date or place, so there is nothing there that shows knowledge of the period.","Notice what Student B's dates are doing: they show a threat that had been growing for eight years with two English defeats behind it, so the campaign reads as an answer to something rather than as an event on its own.","Rescue Student A by replacing each assertion with its evidence: 'the Welsh kept attacking' becomes the alliance of Gruffydd and Aelfgar, the defeat outside Hereford on 24 October 1055 and the death of Bishop Leofgar in June 1056; 'wanted to look good' becomes the winter raid on Rhuddlan and the combined land and sea attack of 1063.","Finish the rescue by adding the analysis that evidence now supports: the border had beaten an earl and a bishop in turn, so whoever ended that war would be the man who had done what the king's own officers could not."],"cues":["What in Student A's sentence could an examiner actually reward?","Why do Student B's dates matter more than the number of facts?","What precise evidence sits behind each of Student A's claims?","What does that evidence let you argue that the assertion could not?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Who was the king's chief officer in a shire, and what did he do there?","a":"The shire reeve: he ran the royal estates and collected the king's dues in the shire, and presided in the shire court alongside the bishop and the earl."},{"q":"Which four brothers of the house of Godwin held earldoms by 1060, and which earldoms?","a":"Harold (Wessex, from 1053), Tostig (Northumbria, from 1055), Gyrth (East Anglia, from 1057) and Leofwine (the shires around London, from 1057)."},{"q":"Why did a battle fought in Yorkshire on 20 September 1066 matter to the outcome at Hastings?","a":"Gate Fulford destroyed the levies of Edwin and Morcar, the only other field army in England, so no northern force reinforced Harold in Sussex three weeks later."},{"q":"What did Harold gain, and what did he lose, at Stamford Bridge?","a":"He destroyed the Norwegian claim, since Hardrada and Tostig were both killed, but lost housecarls he could not replace and ended up two hundred and fifty miles from the coast where William landed three days later."},{"q":"Name the two parts of William's claim that did not depend on blood.","a":"Edward's alleged promise of the succession, and Harold's oath of about 1064, which together made the case that Norman writers say won him the backing of Pope Alexander II."}]},"room":{"facts":["Booklet B, the British depth study, carries 32 marks: Question 1(a) and 1(b), 'Describe one feature of...' (2 marks each, on two separate aspects of content), Question 2 'Explain why...' (12 marks) with two stimulus points, and either Question 3 or Question 4, a 16-mark 'How far do you agree? Explain your answer.' with two stimulus points. There are no spelling, punctuation and grammar marks anywhere on Paper 2.","'Describe one feature of...' is point-marked, not levelled: 1(a) and 1(b) are marked separately, and in each part one mark is given for a valid feature and a second for supporting information about it. No reasons and no judgement are wanted, and a second feature inside one part earns nothing.","The stimulus caps, printed in the mark scheme: on the 12-mark Question 2, 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'; on the 16-mark judgement the same wording with a maximum of 11 marks. There is no cap at Level 2 on either question.","Paper 2 lasts 1 hour 50 minutes for 64 marks, so booklet B is worth about 55 minutes for its 32: roughly 5 minutes on 1(a) and 1(b) together, 20 on the 12-mark Question 2 and 25 on the 16-mark judgement, with the planning time for the judgement taken out of that 25 and about five minutes left in hand.","The dates that anchor the whole topic: 5 January 1066, Edward the Confessor dies; 6 January, Harold is crowned; 8 September, the fyrd and fleet are stood down; 20 September, Gate Fulford; 25 September, Stamford Bridge; 28 September, William lands at Pevensey; 14 October, the battle of Hastings."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of local government in Anglo-Saxon England in the years before 1066. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of English towns in the years before 1066. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked in two separate parts: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 mark for supporting information about it. For 1(a) credit shires and the shire reeve, hundreds and wapentakes, the shire and hundred courts, tithings and the hue and cry, or the earl's third penny; for 1(b) credit the size of London and the other great boroughs, the market, mint and tolls that made a borough, or the wool and cloth trade.","sol":"1(a): England was divided into shires, each run for the king by a shire reeve. The reeve managed the royal estates in the shire, collected the king's dues and presided in the shire court alongside the bishop and the earl, so royal orders reached every district. 1(b): an English town was a borough, which meant a market, a mint and tolls paid to the king. London was much the largest, followed by York, Winchester, Norwich and Lincoln, and the wool and cloth sold through them made England a country foreigners came to buy from.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Each part is its own two-mark question on a different aspect of the period, so do not put two features into either of them.","For 1(a) work downwards through the units England was divided into and ask who ran each one; for 1(b) ask what a town had that a village did not.","Name the feature in a sentence, then add one precise detail — an officer, a court, a named town or a traded good — to earn the second mark."],"codes":["B1-1 — 1 Anglo-Saxon government, economy and society: Monarchy and government","B1-1 — 1 Anglo-Saxon government, economy and society: The economy and social system"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the social system of Anglo-Saxon England in the years before 1066. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the powers of the English monarchy in the years before 1066. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked in two separate parts: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 mark for supporting information about it. For 1(a) credit the ranks of earl, thegn, ceorl and slave, the five-hide qualification, labour service on the lord's land, or slavery; for 1(b) credit writs issued under the king's seal, the geld assessed on hides, control of the coinage, the summoning of the fyrd, or law-making and the granting of land by charter.","sol":"1(a): land and status went together at the top of society. A man counted as a thegn if he held about five hides of land, and in return he owed his lord service in the fyrd and attendance at court. 1(b): the king controlled the currency of the whole kingdom. Silver pennies were struck in boroughs all over England but only from dies he issued, and the coinage was periodically called in and reminted, so it stayed uniform and he took a profit from it.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Two marks each and two different aspects: one feature for the social system in 1(a), one for royal power in 1(b), and nothing extra in either.","For 1(a) pick a rank on the ladder and say what land or duty defined it; for 1(b) pick a power the king could use anywhere in England.","Each part needs the feature in one sentence and then a hard detail, such as a holding measured in hides or the way the coinage was controlled, for the second mark."],"codes":["B1-1 — 1 Anglo-Saxon government, economy and society: The economy and social system","B1-1 — 1 Anglo-Saxon government, economy and society: Monarchy and government"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the power of the house of Godwin in the years before 1066. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the rising against Earl Tostig in 1065. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked in two separate parts: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 mark for supporting information about it. For 1(a) credit the family's hold on the earldoms, the marriage of Edith to Edward, the size of their estates, or Harold's military record in Wales; for 1(b) credit the seizure of York and the killing of Tostig's men, the emptying of his treasury, the choice of Morcar as earl, or the settlement at Oxford that confirmed Morcar and exiled Tostig.","sol":"1(a): the family held most of the earldoms of England at once. Harold was Earl of Wessex from 1053, Tostig Earl of Northumbria from 1055, and in 1057 Gyrth took East Anglia and Leofwine the shires around London, so four brothers governed the greater part of the kingdom. 1(b): the rebels put a rival of their own choosing in Tostig's place. In October 1065 the Northumbrian thegns seized York, killed about two hundred of his men and emptied his treasury, and at once chose Morcar, brother of Earl Edwin of Mercia, as earl.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Two separate two-mark answers: the family's standing before 1066 in 1(a), and what the Northumbrians actually did in 1065 in 1(b).","Power is measurable in land, office and closeness to the king; a rising is measurable in what the rebels seized and whom they put in charge.","Give each part a name and a date as its supporting detail rather than a second general statement."],"codes":["B1-1 — 2 The last years of Edward the Confessor and the succession crisis: The significance and power of the house of Godwin","B1-1 — 2 The last years of Edward the Confessor and the succession crisis: Harold Godwinson's embassy to Normandy"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of Harold Godwinson's embassy to Normandy. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of Harald Hardrada's claim to the English throne in 1066. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked in two separate parts: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 mark for supporting information about it. For 1(a) credit the shipwreck in Ponthieu and Count Guy, William's rescue, the Brittany campaign against Duke Conan, or the oath Norman sources say he swore, on whose place those sources disagree; for 1(b) credit the agreement between Magnus and Harthacnut, Hardrada's record as king of Norway from 1046 and in the Varangian Guard, or his alliance with the exiled Tostig.","sol":"1(a): the journey went wrong from the start. Harold crossed the Channel in about 1064, was shipwrecked on the coast of Ponthieu and was held prisoner by Count Guy, until Duke William obtained his release and took him to his own court. 1(b): the claim rested on an agreement between an earlier king of Norway and a king of England. Magnus of Norway and Harthacnut had agreed in the late 1030s that whichever of them outlived the other would take his kingdom, and Hardrada claimed to have inherited Magnus's side of that bargain.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["One feature each, on two different parts of the story: the embassy in 1(a), the Norwegian claim in 1(b).","For 1(a) take the crossing, the captivity, the Brittany campaign or the oath; for 1(b) ask what agreement, or what English ally, the claim rested on.","Say who was involved and when, and be exact about who claims the oath took place and between whom the Norwegian agreement was made."],"codes":["B1-1 — 2 The last years of Edward the Confessor and the succession crisis: Harold Godwinson's embassy to Normandy","B1-1 — 3 The rival claimants for the throne: The motives and claims of William, Hardrada and Edgar"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the battle of Gate Fulford (1066). [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of Harold Godwinson's reign as king in 1066. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked in two separate parts: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 mark for supporting information about it. For 1(a) credit the date and place, the armies of Hardrada and Tostig against Edwin and Morcar, the part played by the marsh and the river, or the surrender of York; for 1(b) credit the coronation at Westminster on 6 January, the marriage to Ealdgyth, the PAX coinage, or the fyrd and the fleet kept on the south coast until 8 September.","sol":"1(a): it was fought on 20 September 1066 just south of York, between the invading army of Harald Hardrada and Tostig, who had landed at Riccall on the Ouse, and the northern levies of Earl Edwin of Mercia and Earl Morcar of Northumbria. 1(b): Harold spent his nine months as king preparing for invasion. He kept the fyrd and the fleet on watch along the south coast all summer and stood them down only on 8 September 1066, when the provisions ran out.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["1(a) and 1(b) are marked apart, so give one feature of the battle and one of the reign and then stop.","For the battle, fix who fought, where and when; for the reign, ask what Harold did with the nine months he had.","Each part needs one supporting detail — a date, a place or a named person — rather than a second feature."],"codes":["B1-1 — 3 The rival claimants for the throne: Reasons for, and significance of, the outcome of Gate Fulford and Stamford Bridge","B1-1 — 3 The rival claimants for the throne: The Witan and the coronation and reign of Harold Godwinson"]},{"q":"Explain why Anglo-Saxon England was a well-governed kingdom in the years before 1066. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the shire reeve • the coinage. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 / AO1 6. Credit analysis of the royal machinery (writs, the geld assessed on hides, the fyrd), the shire and hundred system with the reeve, controlled coinage, collective policing through tithings and oath-helping, and the Church's part in law. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"England was well governed because the king's authority was carried outwards by a structure that worked whether or not he was anywhere near, and because that structure was old enough to be obeyed out of habit. The first reason is the shire system and the shire reeve. England south of the Tees was divided into shires, and in each one the reeve was the king's man: he ran the royal estates, collected the king's dues, and sat in the shire court twice a year with the bishop and the earl. Below the shire, the hundred court met about once a month. This meant that a royal writ, sealed and sent out, reached the level of the village within weeks, which is why a tax such as the geld could be assessed on hides and actually collected. The second reason is the coinage. Silver pennies were struck in boroughs all over England, but only from dies the king issued, and the currency was periodically called in and reminted. A uniform coinage is evidence of a government that could enforce one rule across a whole kingdom and take a profit from doing so, and it is part of why England was rich enough to be worth invading. The third reason goes beyond the bullets: the law rested on collective responsibility rather than on royal policing. Free men over twelve stood in tithings answerable for one another, a victim raised the hue and cry and the whole community joined the chase, and accusations were answered by oath-helping or, when oaths failed, by an ordeal supervised by the Church. A kingdom with no standing police force kept order because every neighbourhood had a stake in keeping it. The three fit together: local units, a trusted currency and shared legal duty meant that government did not depend on the king's presence, which is also why William in 1066 chose to capture the machine rather than dismantle it.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["'Well governed' is a judgement about how far the king's will actually took effect in places he never visited.","Sort your material into the machinery of royal government, the local units that carried it, and the way order was kept in a village with no police.","Take three reasons in turn, and end each paragraph by saying what that feature let the government do that a weaker kingdom could not."],"codes":["B1-1 — 1 Anglo-Saxon government, economy and society: Monarchy and government","B1-1 — 1 Anglo-Saxon government, economy and society: The economy and social system"]},{"q":"Explain why there was a rising against Earl Tostig in 1065. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • taxation • the murder of Gospatric. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 / AO1 6. Credit the heavy geld and the seizure of the treasury, the killing of Gospatric and of Gamel and Ulf under safe conduct, Tostig's status as a West Saxon outsider, his absence in Rome in 1061 when Malcolm III raided, and the availability of Morcar. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Northumbria rose in October 1065 because Tostig had taken from the region far more than he gave it, and because the men with most to lose from his rule had an obvious replacement ready. The first reason was money. Tostig imposed a level of geld on Northumbria that the region was not used to paying, and the rebels' first act on seizing York was to break open his treasury and take what was in it. Taxation on southern lines felt like extortion in a province that earlier earls had taxed lightly. The second reason was his use of violence against the men who mattered locally. Gamel son of Orm and Ulf son of Dolfin were killed in Tostig's own house at York in 1064 while under his safe conduct, and at Christmas that year the Northumbrian noble Gospatric was killed at the royal court. Killing thegns under protection destroyed exactly the trust an earl depended on, and it turned a grievance about taxes into a fear for their lives. The third reason lies beyond the bullets: Tostig was an absentee outsider and a rival house was waiting. He was a West Saxon of the house of Godwin, imposed on an earldom used to being ruled by its own families, and he was in Rome in 1061 when Malcolm III of Scotland raided the north, so he had failed in an earl's first duty. When the thegns took York, killed about two hundred of his men and declared him outlawed, they at once chose Morcar, brother of Earl Edwin of Mercia, to replace him, which shows a planned substitution rather than a riot. The three combine: heavy taxation supplied the grievance, the killings removed any reason for loyalty, and the availability of Morcar made rebellion practical, which is why Harold at Northampton and then Oxford chose to accept their terms rather than fight them.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["A rising needs a grievance, a loss of loyalty and a practical alternative; look for all three in your notes.","Group your material by cause rather than by date, and make sure one of your groups is not prompted by the bullets.","End each paragraph by saying what that grievance did to the behaviour of the Northumbrian thegns."],"codes":["B1-1 — 2 The last years of Edward the Confessor and the succession crisis: Harold Godwinson's embassy to Normandy","B1-1 — 2 The last years of Edward the Confessor and the succession crisis: The significance and power of the house of Godwin"]},{"q":"Explain why Harold Godwinson was crowned king of England in January 1066. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the Witan • Edgar Aethling. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 / AO1 6. Credit the Witan's role in choosing a king and Edward's deathbed commendation, the weakness of Edgar and the foreignness of William and Hardrada, Harold's own power and record (Wessex from 1053, Wales 1063, the settlement of 1065), and the urgency of the invasion threat. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Harold was crowned within a day of Edward's death because he was the only candidate who combined a claim the English would accept with the power to defend the kingdom at once. The Witan gave him the legal title. Under English custom the throne was not simply inherited: the leading earls, bishops and thegns met to choose from among those regarded as throneworthy, and the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle records that Edward, dying on 5 January 1066, commended the kingdom to Harold's protection. The Witan was already gathered at Westminster, since the new abbey had been consecrated days earlier, so the choice could be made and acted on immediately; Harold was crowned on 6 January, the same day Edward was buried. The alternatives were unusable. Edgar Aethling had the best blood as grandson of Edmund Ironside, but he was a boy of about fourteen with no lands, no following and no military experience, and England had already learned what a royal minority costs. The other claimants, William of Normandy and Harald Hardrada of Norway, were foreign rulers with no support inside England who could only take the crown by invasion. Beyond the bullets, Harold was already governing England in all but name. As Earl of Wessex from 1053 he headed a family that held most of the earldoms; he and Tostig had broken Gruffydd ap Llywelyn in 1063; and in 1065 he had settled the Northumbrian rising by negotiation rather than civil war. Contemporaries gave him titles that placed him just below the king. The speed of the coronation is the clue to the whole answer. The Witan chose Harold because the kingdom needed a proven war leader in place before the spring, and the commendation, the emptiness of the alternatives and Harold's own record all pointed at the same man.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Being crowned is not the same as inheriting: somebody had to choose him, so start by asking who.","Handle it as three things: the legal process, the state of the rival candidates, and Harold's own position by the end of 1065.","Use the timing, a coronation on the day of the old king's funeral, as the spine of the argument."],"codes":["B1-1 — 3 The rival claimants for the throne: The Witan and the coronation and reign of Harold Godwinson","B1-1 — 3 The rival claimants for the throne: The motives and claims of William, Hardrada and Edgar"]},{"q":"Explain why Harald Hardrada was defeated at the battle of Stamford Bridge (1066). (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the speed of Harold Godwinson's march • the surrender of York. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 / AO1 6. Credit the surprise achieved by a march of about two hundred miles in four days, the dispersal of the Norwegian force between Riccall and Stamford Bridge and the mail left behind, the hostage exchange that drew Hardrada inland, the quality of Harold's housecarls, and Norwegian overconfidence after Fulford. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Hardrada lost at Stamford Bridge because a victory five days earlier had persuaded him that the campaign was already over, and Harold Godwinson turned that belief into a trap. The first reason is the march. Harold was in the south watching for William when the news of Fulford reached him; he took his housecarls north and covered the better part of two hundred miles from London to Yorkshire in about four days, raising shire levies as he passed. The Norwegians had no warning, because the English army came through York itself, and the first the invaders saw of it was dust on the road from the city. The second reason is what the surrender of York had done to Hardrada's dispositions. York had agreed to hand over hostages, and the exchange was fixed for 25 September at Stamford Bridge, seven or eight miles from the city, so Hardrada moved inland on a hot day expecting to collect hostages rather than to fight. A large part of his force had been left with the ships at Riccall, and many of the men with him had left their mail behind. The third reason goes beyond the bullets: the quality of the two forces at the point of contact. Harold's core were housecarls, full-time professional soldiers with the two-handed Danish axe, fighting as a body that had trained together; Hardrada's men were strung out on both sides of the Derwent and had to form up piecemeal. Once the fighting reached the far bank the Norwegian line never became solid, and both Hardrada and Tostig were killed. Overconfidence after Gate Fulford is the thread through all three. Because Hardrada had beaten the northern earls he assumed no other army could reach him in time, and because he assumed it he divided his force and left off his armour; the Chronicle says the survivors needed only twenty-four ships to carry home an army that had come in about three hundred.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Hardrada had just won a battle, so ask what winning it had persuaded him to do next.","Explain the defeat through three things: how fast the English arrived, where the Norwegians were and how they were equipped, and what the two armies were made of.","End each paragraph by saying what that factor cost Hardrada, not merely that it happened."],"codes":["B1-1 — 3 The rival claimants for the throne: Reasons for, and significance of, the outcome of Gate Fulford and Stamford Bridge"]},{"q":"Explain why the house of Godwin had become so powerful by 1060. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • Edward the Confessor's marriage to Edith • the exile of 1051. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 / AO1 6. Credit Godwin's rise under Cnut, an earl from 1018 and earl of the whole of Wessex by about 1020, and his service to four kings, the marriage of 1045, the failed exile of 1051 and the restoration of 1052 with the fall of Robert of Jumieges, the accumulation of four earldoms by 1057, the size of the family estates, and Harold's Welsh campaign of 1063. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"By 1060 the Godwins were powerful because decades of royal service had been converted, crisis by crisis, into land, offices and a family connection with the crown. The marriage was the formal tie. Godwin had been made an earl by Cnut in 1018 and held the whole of Wessex by about 1020, and in 1045 his daughter Edith married Edward the Confessor, which made him the king's father-in-law and Harold, in time, the king's brother-in-law. In a kingdom where the Witan chose the next ruler from among the leading men, proximity to the king was itself a form of power. The exile of 1051 then proved how far that power had already gone. When Godwin refused Edward's order to punish the men of Dover after the fight with Count Eustace's followers, the whole family was outlawed and driven abroad; within a year they returned with a fleet and Edward had to restore them without a battle, because nobody in England would fight for him against them. The crisis also removed the king's foreign counterweight, since the Norman archbishop Robert of Jumieges fled and Stigand took Canterbury. Beyond the bullets, the family then accumulated earldoms and used them successfully. Godwin died in 1053 and Harold succeeded him in Wessex; Tostig received Northumbria in 1055; and in 1057 Gyrth took East Anglia and Leofwine the shires around London. Harold added the military reputation the king had never earned: in 1063 he and Tostig campaigned against Gruffydd ap Llywelyn of Wales so effectively that Gruffydd was killed by his own men and his head was sent to Harold. The reasons reinforce one another. Marriage brought access, the failure of the exile showed that Edward could not rule without them, and the earldoms and the Welsh victory turned that necessity into a permanent hold on the kingdom's land and armies, which is precisely why in January 1066 the Witan could look at a subject and see a king.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Power that lasts is built out of offices, land and family ties rather than out of personality.","Put the family's gains in order from about 1018 onwards and look for the moment that proved the king could not do without them.","Finish each paragraph by saying what that gain allowed the family to do next."],"codes":["B1-1 — 2 The last years of Edward the Confessor and the succession crisis: The significance and power of the house of Godwin","B1-1 — 1 Anglo-Saxon government, economy and society: Monarchy and government"]},{"q":"'The leadership skills of William of Normandy were the main reason for his victory at the battle of Hastings (1066).' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the feigned retreats • Harold Godwinson's death. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 / AO1 6. Credit William's tactics and command (feigned retreats, rallying after the Breton flight, combined arms, preparation, the castle and the ravaging of Sussex) weighed against inherited conditions (the fleet stood down on 8 September, Gate Fulford, Stamford Bridge) and Harold's own decisions and death. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The criterion has to be what made the difference on the day: whether the Normans won because of decisions William took, or because of conditions he did not create. The case for the statement is strong, and the feigned retreats are the best evidence for it. The English shield wall on the ridge above Senlac could not be broken by arrows shot uphill or by cavalry charging into a wall of shields, so the only way to win was to make the English leave the ridge. When the Breton division on William's left broke and fled early in the day, part of the English line chased it downhill and was cut to pieces in the open; later in the battle the Normans repeated the movement deliberately. Turning an accident into a tactic in the middle of a battle is a mark of command rather than of luck. William's control of his army points the same way. When the rumour spread that he was dead, the very thing that had caused the Breton flight, he pushed back his helmet to show his face and rallied the line. He had also prepared the campaign with unusual care: ships and men assembled through the summer, a landing at Pevensey on 28 September with a castle raised at Hastings, and the systematic ravaging of Harold's own Sussex estates to force him to come south quickly. Norman writers say Pope Alexander II blessed the enterprise, and although no other source of the time records it, a war presented as just helped hold a mixed army of Normans, Bretons and Flemings together. Against the statement, much of what beat Harold happened before William ever met him. The English army arrived exhausted: it had fought at Stamford Bridge on 25 September, marched back to London and then into Sussex within three weeks, and the housecarls lost in the north could not be replaced. Edwin and Morcar, whose levies had been wrecked at Gate Fulford on 20 September, brought no army south. The fleet Harold had kept on watch all summer had been stood down on 8 September when supplies ran out, which is why William crossed unopposed. None of this was William's doing. Harold's own choices cost him too. He fought on 14 October rather than waiting for the shire levies still coming in, and once his line began to pursue the retreating Normans he could not restore it. His death at dusk, with his brothers Gyrth and Leofwine already killed, ended the battle at once, because an English army fought for a named lord and there was suddenly no lord left to fight for. The judgement turns on whether luck and Harold's errors would have been enough by themselves, and they would not. The wind, Stamford Bridge and Harold's haste gave William his opportunity, but an opportunity still has to be taken: a commander who lost his nerve when the Bretons ran, or who kept battering the shield wall head on, would have lost with every one of the same advantages. William's leadership was decisive because it was the factor that converted the others into a victory, so the statement holds, provided it is read as the deciding factor rather than the only one.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["'Main reason' is a ranking word, so something else has to lose the comparison.","Split your evidence into two piles, things William decided and things that were already true before he met Harold's army, and weigh one against the other.","Open by naming the test you will use, and make sure your last paragraph applies that same test instead of listing both sides again."],"codes":["B1-1 — 4 The Norman invasion: Reasons for William's victory","B1-1 — 4 The Norman invasion: The Battle of Hastings"]},{"q":"'William of Normandy had the strongest claim to the English throne in 1066.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • Harold Godwinson's oath • the Witan. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 / AO1 6. Credit the standards by which a claim can be judged (blood, designation, English custom, force) applied to William (kinship through Emma, the alleged promise, the oath, the papal backing Norman writers claim), Harold (commendation and the Witan), Edgar (descent from Edmund Ironside) and Hardrada (the Magnus agreement). Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The strength of a claim depends entirely on the standard used, so the first job is to name the standards: descent by blood, designation by the previous king, the judgement of the Witan under English custom, and the power to enforce. Measured against these, William's claim was strong in some and weak in others. The case for the statement rests on designation and on the oath. William was a blood relation, if a distant one, since Edward's mother Emma was William's great-aunt, making the two men first cousins once removed. He claimed that Edward had promised him the succession, which is at least plausible for the years when Edward, who had been brought up in Normandy, filled his court with Normans, before the Godwins forced them out in 1052. Above all he claimed Harold's oath: on the Norman account Harold swore on holy relics in about 1064 to support William's succession, at a place Norman sources disagree on, Bonneville-sur-Touques in William of Poitiers and Bayeux in the Tapestry. That turned a dynastic argument into a case of broken faith, which is why, on the Norman account, William secured the backing of Pope Alexander II and could present an invasion as the enforcement of a promise. Against the statement, English custom recognised no promise made abroad. The throne was not property to be willed away: the Witan chose from among the throneworthy, and its choice made a king. Edgar Aethling had far better blood, as grandson of Edmund Ironside, and Harold had the two things that counted in January 1066, the dying Edward's commendation recorded by the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle and the choice of a Witan already assembled at Westminster. No English source mentions Harold's oath at all, and even if it was sworn it was sworn in a foreign court by a man in William's power who had no authority to give away a throne that was not yet his. Hardrada's claim shows how little a purely legal argument was worth. It rested on an agreement of the late 1030s between Magnus of Norway and Harthacnut, both long dead, and on no English connection except the exiled Tostig, yet his army came within a week of holding the north. The judgement turns on which standard is the right one, and the right one is the standard the English themselves used. By blood the strongest claim was Edgar's; by English custom it was Harold's, because he alone had been chosen by the body that chose kings; by the test of enforcement it was William's, and enforcement is the standard the year actually applied. So the statement is true only in the sense that William's claim was the best supported outside England and the only one he could make good in the field. Inside England, in January 1066, it was the weakest of those argued in public, which is precisely why he needed the oath, the papal backing Norman writers describe, and an invasion to make it stick.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["A claim is only 'strongest' by some standard, and each of the four claimants scored well by a different one.","Set out the standards first, blood descent, designation, English custom and the power to enforce, and then run each claimant through them.","Give a paragraph to each rival and end each one by saying which standard that claim wins on and which it fails."],"codes":["B1-1 — 3 The rival claimants for the throne: The motives and claims of William, Hardrada and Edgar","B1-1 — 3 The rival claimants for the throne: The Witan and the coronation and reign of Harold Godwinson"]},{"q":"'The house of Godwin was the main cause of the succession crisis of 1066.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • Harold Godwinson's embassy to Normandy • the rising against Tostig. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 / AO1 6. Credit the oath that armed William's claim, the exile of Tostig that gave Hardrada an English ally, and the family's dominance of the earldoms, weighed against causes outside their control: Edward's childlessness, the vagueness of English custom, Edgar's youth, and the ambitions of William and Hardrada. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"A cause must do more than be present, so the test is whether the Godwins created a claim or a rivalry that would not otherwise have existed. Two of the three invasion threats of 1066 can be traced to Godwin decisions. Harold's crossing to Normandy in about 1064 ended with him in William's power, campaigning with the duke in Brittany and, on the Norman account, swearing on relics to support William's succession, at a place Norman sources disagree on, Bonneville-sur-Touques in William of Poitiers and Bayeux in the Tapestry. Whether or not the oath was freely given, it handed William an argument he had previously lacked, that the most powerful man in England had sworn to him and then perjured himself, and that argument is what Norman writers say won him the backing of Pope Alexander II. A year later the family produced Hardrada's English ally. Tostig's rule in Northumbria collapsed in October 1065 over the geld he levied and the killing of thegns such as Gospatric under safe conduct, and Harold chose to accept the rebels' terms at Northampton rather than restore his own brother. Tostig left for Flanders with a grievance against Harold personally, and in September 1066 he was in Hardrada's fleet at Riccall and died at Stamford Bridge fighting his brother. The family's sheer dominance also made a disputed succession likelier. By 1060 four brothers held earldoms and their estates together were worth more than the king's; Harold's Welsh campaign of 1063 gave him a military reputation Edward never had. A subject that powerful could take the throne, and a subject who takes a throne invites everyone else to argue that he stole it. Against the statement, the deepest cause was one no Godwin controlled. Edward the Confessor died childless on 5 January 1066 after more than twenty years of marriage, and English custom did not settle in advance who should follow a childless king, since the Witan chose and several men could therefore claim to be choosable. Edgar Aethling was the closest heir by blood but was about fourteen, landless and untried, which removed the obvious answer. William's ambition was his own, fed by Edward's Norman upbringing and by a promise Norman writers dated to 1051, and Hardrada's claim came out of a Scandinavian agreement made before any Godwin held Northumbria. On the test set out at the start, the Godwins were the decisive cause without being the only one. Childlessness created the opening, but an opening is not a crisis. Three invasions were possible only because a Godwin oath had armed William with a grievance, a Godwin quarrel had given Hardrada a guide to England, and a Godwin sat on the throne. The family did not cause the vacancy, but it supplied almost everything that made the vacancy dangerous.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what would have been different in 1066 if the Godwins had never existed; that is the test of whether they caused the crisis.","Trace each foreign claim backwards and decide whether a Godwin decision made it possible or merely failed to stop it.","Weigh the family's actions against the one cause they did not control, that Edward died with no son, and say which mattered more."],"codes":["B1-1 — 2 The last years of Edward the Confessor and the succession crisis: The significance and power of the house of Godwin","B1-1 — 2 The last years of Edward the Confessor and the succession crisis: Harold Godwinson's embassy to Normandy","B1-1 — 3 The rival claimants for the throne: The Witan and the coronation and reign of Harold Godwinson"]},{"q":"'The battles in the north in September 1066 were the main reason for Harold Godwinson's defeat at the battle of Hastings.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • Gate Fulford • the march south. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 / AO1 6. Credit the loss of Edwin and Morcar's levies at Fulford, the housecarls and time lost at Stamford Bridge and on the marches, weighed against the standing down of the fleet on 8 September, William's preparation and combined arms, and Harold's decision to fight on 14 October. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The northern battles cannot have beaten Harold directly, since they were fought two hundred and fifty miles from Sussex, so the question is what they took from him that he could not replace. They took his allies. Edwin and Morcar's levies were the only other field army in England, and they were destroyed at Gate Fulford on 20 September 1066, hemmed between marsh and river when Hardrada counter-attacked from the stronger flank; York then surrendered. Neither earl brought a force to Hastings three weeks later, so Harold fought with the men of Wessex and his own household rather than with the army of a kingdom. They also took his best troops and his time. Harold marched from London to Yorkshire, the better part of two hundred miles, in about four days, destroyed Hardrada and Tostig at Stamford Bridge on 25 September, and then had to turn round. Housecarls were full-time professionals who could not be replaced in a fortnight, and every one of them lost in Yorkshire was missing from the ridge at Senlac. The army that formed the shield wall on 14 October had marched the length of England twice in three weeks and fought a battle in between. Against the statement, other causes owed nothing to the north. The fyrd and the fleet had been stood down on 8 September when the provisions ran out, which is why William crossed and landed at Pevensey on 28 September unopposed, and that decision was taken before Hardrada had even landed. William had prepared for months, brought archers, infantry and cavalry where the English had almost none of the first and last, raised a castle at Hastings and ravaged Harold's Sussex estates to force a quick battle on his own terms. Harold's own choices mattered as much. He could have waited in London for the shire levies still coming in and let William's supplies run short in a ravaged corner of Sussex; instead he moved at once, which was the same instinct that had worked at Stamford Bridge. On the day, a line that had held for hours broke because it pursued, and once Gyrth, Leofwine and Harold himself were dead there was no lord for the army to fight for. Weighed against one another, the northern battles were the greatest single cause but not an independent one. They cost Harold the northern earls, his veterans and the initiative, and they turned his best asset, speed, into exhaustion. Yet a rested army under the same commander might still have lost to feigned retreats it had never met before, and a Harold who waited a fortnight might have won with an army no larger. Fulford and Stamford Bridge made defeat likely; it was Harold's decision to fight on 14 October, against a general who had spent the whole year preparing for exactly that battle, that made defeat happen.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Two battles in Yorkshire cannot beat an army in Sussex directly, so you are looking for what they took away.","Count the costs separately, men lost, allies lost, time lost and distance marched, and decide which Harold could least afford.","Set the northern battles against the causes that had nothing to do with them, such as the fleet standing down and the decision to fight at once, before you rank them."],"codes":["B1-1 — 3 The rival claimants for the throne: Reasons for, and significance of, the outcome of Gate Fulford and Stamford Bridge","B1-1 — 4 The Norman invasion: Reasons for William's victory","B1-1 — 4 The Norman invasion: The Battle of Hastings"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date was Harold Godwinson crowned king, and where?","a":"6 January 1066, at Westminster, on the day Edward the Confessor was buried."},{"q":"Who replaced Tostig as Earl of Northumbria after the rising of 1065?","a":"Morcar, brother of Earl Edwin of Mercia."},{"q":"Which two leaders were killed at the battle of Stamford Bridge?","a":"Harald Hardrada, king of Norway, and Tostig Godwinson."},{"q":"What tactic drew part of the English line off the ridge at Hastings?","a":"The feigned retreat: the Normans pretended to flee so that the English would break the shield wall to chase them, and were then destroyed in the open."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Witan","back":"The assembly of earls, bishops, abbots and leading thegns; it advised the king on law, taxation and war and chose the next king from among those regarded as throneworthy.","code":"B1-1 — 1 Anglo-Saxon government, economy and society: Monarchy and government","open":false},{"front":"Shire reeve","back":"The king's officer in a shire: he ran the royal estates, collected the king's dues and presided in the shire court alongside the bishop and the earl.","code":"B1-1 — 1 Anglo-Saxon government, economy and society: Monarchy and government","open":false},{"front":"The hundred court and the hue and cry","back":"The hundred, a wapentake in the Danelaw, held a court about monthly; free men over twelve stood in tithings answerable for one another, and a victim raised the hue and cry for the community to join the chase.","code":"B1-1 — 1 Anglo-Saxon government, economy and society: Monarchy and government","open":false},{"front":"Thegn","back":"A landholder of roughly five hides who owed service in the fyrd and attendance at court; thegns supplied the local government of the shires and hundreds.","code":"B1-1 — 1 Anglo-Saxon government, economy and society: The economy and social system","open":false},{"front":"Stigand","back":"Archbishop of Canterbury from 1052, who also kept Winchester and took his pallium from Benedict X, later judged an antipope; his position let William present an invasion as reform.","code":"B1-1 — 1 Anglo-Saxon government, economy and society: The economy and social system","open":false},{"front":"The marriage of Edith and Edward the Confessor, 1045","back":"Godwin's daughter Edith married Edward in January 1045, making Godwin the king's father-in-law and Harold at once the king's brother-in-law; Harold succeeded his father as earl of Wessex in 1053.","code":"B1-1 — 2 The last years of Edward the Confessor and the succession crisis: The significance and power of the house of Godwin","open":false},{"front":"The exile and return of the Godwins, 1051 to 1052","back":"Outlawed after Godwin refused to punish Dover for the fight with Count Eustace's men; they returned with a fleet in 1052 and were restored without a battle, and Robert of Jumieges fled from Canterbury.","code":"B1-1 — 2 The last years of Edward the Confessor and the succession crisis: The significance and power of the house of Godwin","open":false},{"front":"Harold Godwinson's embassy to Normandy, about 1064","back":"Shipwrecked in Ponthieu and held by Count Guy, freed by William, took Norman arms in Brittany and, Norman sources say, swore on relics to support William's claim; those sources disagree on the place.","code":"B1-1 — 2 The last years of Edward the Confessor and the succession crisis: Harold Godwinson's embassy to Normandy","open":false},{"front":"The rising against Tostig, October 1065","back":"Northumbrian thegns seized York, killed his men, emptied his treasury and chose Morcar; Harold settled with them at Northampton and Oxford, and Tostig went into exile in Flanders.","code":"B1-1 — 2 The last years of Edward the Confessor and the succession crisis: Harold Godwinson's embassy to Normandy","open":false},{"front":"Edgar Aethling","back":"Grandson of Edmund Ironside and the closest heir by blood in 1066, but about fourteen years old, with no lands, no following and no military experience.","code":"B1-1 — 3 The rival claimants for the throne: The motives and claims of William, Hardrada and Edgar","open":false},{"front":"Harold Godwinson's coronation, 6 January 1066","back":"The Witan chose him and he was crowned at Westminster on the day Edward was buried, probably by Ealdred, Archbishop of York.","code":"B1-1 — 3 The rival claimants for the throne: The Witan and the coronation and reign of Harold Godwinson","open":false},{"front":"Gate Fulford, 20 September 1066","back":"Hardrada and Tostig beat Edwin and Morcar two miles south of York; marsh and river trapped the English line, York surrendered, and no northern army ever reached Hastings.","code":"B1-1 — 3 The rival claimants for the throne: Reasons for, and significance of, the outcome of Gate Fulford and Stamford Bridge","open":false},{"front":"Stamford Bridge, 25 September 1066","back":"Harold surprised the Norwegians east of York after a march of about four days; Hardrada and Tostig were killed and the survivors sailed home in twenty-four ships.","code":"B1-1 — 3 The rival claimants for the throne: Reasons for, and significance of, the outcome of Gate Fulford and Stamford Bridge","open":false},{"front":"The shield wall at Senlac","back":"Harold's army fought on foot in a dense line along the ridge, housecarls with two-handed Danish axes in front; it held from mid-morning until dusk on 14 October 1066.","code":"B1-1 — 4 The Norman invasion: The Battle of Hastings","open":false},{"front":"One reason for William's victory at Hastings","back":"The feigned retreats that finally broke the shield wall; the exhaustion of Harold's army after Stamford Bridge and two long marches; William's archers, infantry and cavalry against an army with almost no archers or horsemen.","code":"B1-1 — 4 The Norman invasion: Reasons for William's victory","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (booklet B, the 16-mark judgement)","items":["Question: 'The Church was the most important support of royal government in England in the years before 1066.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] You may use the following in your answer: • the shire court • trial by ordeal. You must also use information of your own. What follows is one paragraph from the middle of the answer, putting the case for the statement.","Royal government in England worked because the king's orders reached men who were already bound to obey somebody, and in every shire one of those men was a bishop. The shire court met twice a year and the bishop sat in it beside the earl and the shire reeve, expounding the law of God and the secular law in the same session, so the court that enforced the king's law was also the court that enforced the Church's. The king's written orders, the writs sealed with his own seal, were commonly addressed to the bishop, the earl and the reeve together, which made the bishop one of the three officers to whom the crown spoke directly in each shire. Below that, the whole system of proof ran through the Church: accusations were answered by oath-helping, oaths were sworn on relics, and where the oaths failed the case went to the ordeal, which was held in a church under a priest and after fasting and prayer, so the last sanction of English justice was clerical and not royal at all. Bishops and abbots also sat in the Witan that advised on law, taxation and war and chose the next king, and it was churchmen who wrote and kept the charters and law codes in which what the king had done survived him. Edward the Confessor built the same alliance in stone, spending his last years rebuilding Westminster Abbey, which was consecrated on 28 December 1065, eight days before he died. Measured against the test of what royal government could not have done without, the Church supplied more than any other support, because an earl could raise an army but could not make a judgement stick in a village three days' ride away, and it was the bishop, the relic and the ordeal that made an absent king's law obeyed by men who would never see him.","Why this is Level 4: the explanation is directed consistently at the conceptual focus, which is the relative importance of one support of government and not a description of the Church, and the line of reasoning is sustained from the opening claim through to the closing judgement. The information is precisely selected rather than piled up, since the twice-yearly shire court, the bishop sitting beside the earl and the reeve, the address of the writs, oath-helping and relics, the ordeal under a priest, and the consecration of Westminster on 28 December 1065 each do a job in the argument. The criterion for the judgement is stated and applied, namely what royal government could not have done without, and the interim judgement measures the Church against 'any other support' instead of merely agreeing. It also goes beyond the two stimulus bullets, by using the writs, the Witan and the keeping of charters and law codes, so it addresses three or more aspects of content and the printed cap does not bite."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["The same question, answered at Level 2. Read it beside the Level 4 paragraph above and look for what is missing rather than for what is wrong.","The Church was the most important support of royal government because it was very powerful and everyone was religious in those days. Bishops were important men who helped the king and sat in the shire court with the earl, so the king could get things done. Also if nobody knew who was guilty they used trial by ordeal, which was run by the Church, so the Church was in charge of justice as well. So the Church was the most important support of the government because of all these things.","Why this is only Level 2: it meets the descriptor 'an explanation is given showing limited analysis and with implicit or unsustained links to the conceptual focus of the question', because the shire court and the ordeal are named and a link is gestured at, in 'so the king could get things done' and 'in charge of justice as well', but neither link is traced. It misses the higher descriptors in two ways. First, the information is generalised rather than 'accurate and relevant information precisely selected to address the question directly': there is no indication of how often the court met or who else sat in it, nothing about how the ordeal was conducted or what it was for, and no example of anything a bishop actually did for the crown. Second, 'the overall judgement is given but its justification is asserted or insecure', since the final sentence repeats the statement rather than applying any criterion, and no other support of government is weighed against the Church at all. Because the paragraph also adds nothing beyond the two stimulus bullets, the printed cap applies on top: on the 16-mark question there is a maximum of 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for them either. Nothing now holds such an answer down inside Level 2, so this paragraph could reach the top of Level 2 as it stands, but until it addresses a third aspect of content better writing alone could take it no higher than 11."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Anglo-Saxon government, economy, society and the Church before 1066","items":["The king alone made law, granted land by charter and summoned the fyrd; his written orders, called writs, were authenticated with his seal.","The geld was a tax on land assessed in hides, so the burden could be divided shire by shire and collected through the reeves.","Silver pennies were struck in boroughs across England but only from dies the king issued, and the coinage was periodically called in and reminted.","The Witan was the assembly of earls, bishops, abbots and leading thegns; it advised on law, taxation and war and chose the next king from among those regarded as throneworthy.","By 1060 the great earldoms were Wessex, Mercia, Northumbria and East Anglia. An earl led the shire levies, sat in the shire court and kept a third of the profits of justice and of the boroughs, the 'third penny'.","England was divided into shires, each with a shire reeve as the king's officer; below the shire came the hundred, called a wapentake in the Danelaw. The hundred court met about once a month and the shire court twice a year.","Order was kept collectively: free men over twelve stood in tithings answerable for one another, a victim raised the hue and cry, accusations were answered by oath-helping, and where oaths failed the Church supervised trial by ordeal.","Compensation was fixed by rank: the wergild, or man price, paid to a dead man's kin was far higher for a thegn than for a ceorl.","Perhaps two million people lived in England, about nine in ten of them in the countryside, farming strips in open fields with ox-drawn ploughs.","The ranks below the earls were thegns, holding roughly five hides; ceorls, free peasants owing rent and labour; geburs and cottars, who worked the lord's land for most of the week; and slaves, about a tenth of the population when Domesday Book counted them twenty years later.","The largest towns were London, York, Winchester, Norwich and Lincoln; a borough meant a market, a mint and tolls, and England's wealth rested above all on wool and cloth.","The English Church was headed by the archbishops of Canterbury and York. Stigand held Canterbury from 1052 together with Winchester and had taken his pallium from Benedict X, later judged an antipope; Ealdred became Archbishop of York in 1060.","Edward the Confessor's rebuilt abbey church at Westminster was consecrated on 28 December 1065, eight days before he died."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the house of Godwin and the last years of Edward the Confessor, 1018 to 1066","items":["Godwin was made an earl by Cnut in 1018 and held the whole of Wessex by about 1020; he served Cnut, Harold Harefoot, Harthacnut and Edward the Confessor in turn.","Edith, Godwin's daughter, married Edward the Confessor in 1045.","In September 1051 the whole family was outlawed after Godwin refused to punish the men of Dover for the fight with the followers of Count Eustace of Boulogne.","They returned with a fleet in 1052 and were restored without a battle; the Norman Robert of Jumieges fled from Canterbury and Stigand replaced him.","Godwin died in 1053 and Harold succeeded him as Earl of Wessex.","Tostig became Earl of Northumbria in 1055, and in 1057 Gyrth took East Anglia and Leofwine the shires around London, so four brothers held earldoms at once.","The lands of the family taken together were worth more than the king's own estates.","In 1063 Harold and Tostig campaigned against Gruffydd ap Llywelyn of Wales; Gruffydd was killed by his own men in August 1063 and his head was sent to Harold.","In about 1064 Harold crossed the Channel, was shipwrecked in Ponthieu and held by Count Guy until William secured his release.","Harold campaigned with William in Brittany against Duke Conan and was given Norman arms; Norman sources say he then swore an oath to support William's claim but disagree on the place, William of Poitiers giving Bonneville-sur-Touques, the Bayeux Tapestry Bagias, that is Bayeux, and Orderic Vitalis Rouen; no English source mentions it.","Harold's brother Wulfnoth and his nephew Hakon had been hostages in Normandy since 1051; freeing them is one suggested purpose of the embassy.","Tostig's rule in Northumbria was resented for the heavy geld he levied, for the killing of Gamel son of Orm and Ulf son of Dolfin in his own house at York in 1064 under his safe conduct, and for the death of Gospatric at the royal court at Christmas 1064.","Tostig was in Rome in 1061 when Malcolm III of Scotland raided Northumbria.","In October 1065 the Northumbrian thegns seized York, killed about two hundred of Tostig's men, emptied his treasury, outlawed him and chose Morcar as earl.","Harold met the rebels at Northampton and the settlement was made at Oxford on 28 October 1065: Morcar was confirmed and Tostig exiled. He went to Flanders, where the kinsman of his wife Judith, Count Baldwin V, ruled.","Edward the Confessor died on 5 January 1066 and was buried in Westminster Abbey the next day."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the rival claimants and Harold's reign, January to September 1066","items":["Edgar Aethling was the grandson of Edmund Ironside and son of Edward the Exile, who returned to England in 1057 and died almost at once; in 1066 Edgar was about fourteen, with no lands, no following and no military record.","Harald Hardrada had been king of Norway since 1046 and had made his name in the Varangian Guard at Constantinople.","Hardrada's claim rested on an agreement of the late 1030s between Magnus of Norway and Harthacnut that whichever survived would take the other's kingdom; his English ally from 1065 was the exiled Tostig Godwinson.","William had been Duke of Normandy since 1035, succeeding his father Robert at about the age of seven, and had spent thirty years fighting to hold the duchy.","William's kinship claim ran through Edward's mother Emma, who was William's great-aunt, making Edward and William first cousins once removed.","William claimed that Edward had promised him the succession and that Harold's oath of about 1064 had confirmed it, and Norman writers, chiefly William of Poitiers, say he obtained the backing of Pope Alexander II; no other source of the time records it and historians dispute it.","The Witan chose Harold, and the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle records that the dying Edward commended the kingdom to him.","Harold was crowned at Westminster on 6 January 1066, the day Edward was buried, probably by Ealdred, Archbishop of York; Norman writers claimed the uncanonical Stigand had performed the ceremony.","Harold's silver pennies carried the word PAX, meaning peace.","Harold rode north to York early in 1066 and married Ealdgyth, sister of Earl Edwin of Mercia and Earl Morcar of Northumbria.","A comet, now known to be Halley's, was seen across England in late April 1066 and was taken as an omen.","In May 1066 Tostig raided the Isle of Wight and the south-east coast, was driven off in the north by Edwin and Morcar and took refuge in Scotland.","Harold kept the fyrd and the fleet on watch along the south coast all summer and stood them down on 8 September 1066, when the provisions ran out."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the three battles of 1066 — Gate Fulford, Stamford Bridge and Hastings","items":["Hardrada's fleet, about three hundred ships according to the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, sailed down the east coast and up the Ouse, mooring at Riccall.","Gate Fulford was fought on 20 September 1066, two miles south of York, between Hardrada and Tostig and the levies of Earl Edwin and Earl Morcar.","At Fulford the English were caught between marsh and river; when they pushed the weaker Norwegian wing Hardrada counter-attacked from the other flank and rolled up their line.","York surrendered after Fulford and agreed to hand over hostages at Stamford Bridge.","Harold marched his housecarls from London to Yorkshire, the better part of two hundred miles, in about four days, raising shire levies as he went.","Stamford Bridge was fought on 25 September 1066, seven or eight miles east of York; Hardrada and Tostig were both killed.","Much of the Norwegian force had been left with the ships at Riccall, and many of those present had left their mail behind.","The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle says the survivors of Hardrada's army needed only twenty-four ships to sail home.","William landed at Pevensey on 28 September 1066, moved to Hastings, raised a castle there and ravaged Harold's own estates in Sussex to force a quick battle.","Harold was back in London by about 6 October and marched into Sussex; the battle was fought on 14 October 1066 on the ridge at Senlac, where Battle Abbey now stands.","Estimates put each army at something like seven thousand men, but no reliable count survives.","The English fought on foot in a shield wall, housecarls with two-handed Danish axes in front and the fyrd behind; they had almost no cavalry and few archers.","William's army was drawn up in three divisions, Bretons on the left, Normans in the centre, French and Flemish on the right, and combined archers, infantry and cavalry.","Early in the day the Breton division broke and fled, a rumour spread that William was dead, and he pushed back his helmet to show his face and rallied his men.","Part of the English line chased the fleeing Bretons downhill and was destroyed in the open; later the Normans repeated the movement as a deliberate feigned retreat.","Harold's brothers Gyrth and Leofwine were killed in the battle, and Harold himself died near dusk. The Bayeux Tapestry shows a figure struck in the eye by an arrow and another cut down by a horseman, and which of them is Harold is disputed.","Edwin and Morcar brought no army to Hastings, and the shire levies of the south-west had not yet come in."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — the 16-mark judgement: 'The power of the earls was the greatest weakness of the English monarchy in the years before 1066.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.","items":["The question as printed: 'The power of the earls was the greatest weakness of the English monarchy in the years before 1066.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] You may use the following in your answer: • the third penny • the fyrd. You must also use information of your own. There are no spelling, punctuation and grammar marks on this paper, so every minute goes on the argument itself.","Decode it. The concept is the balance of power inside English government, and 'greatest weakness' means you must rank rather than list. The criterion to announce in the opening, and to apply at the end, is whether a weakness means a power the king did not have at all, or a power he had but could use only through men who might withhold it. Name the alternatives you will weigh, which are the king's own machinery in the shires, the Church, and the rules that decided who became king, so the line of reasoning is visible from the first sentence.","Opening, three or four sentences: state the criterion, name the factors you will weigh, and signal the position you will end at.","Paragraph 1, for the statement — the earls held the means of war. The fyrd was raised shire by shire and came to the earl who led it: Harold and Tostig took armies into Wales in 1063, and in September 1066 it was the levies of Edwin and Morcar, not any force of the king's own, that met the Norwegians at Gate Fulford. Interim judgement: a king whose only standing troops were his housecarls fought when his earls agreed to fight and not before.","Paragraph 2, for the statement — the earls held the money and could not easily be removed. An earl took the third penny, a third of the profits of justice in his shire and of the borough revenues, and modern estimates from Domesday Book put the lands of the house of Godwin at something like seven thousand pounds a year against about five thousand for the king's own. When Edward outlawed the whole family in September 1051 they returned with a fleet in 1052 and were restored without a battle, because nobody in England would fight them for him. Interim judgement: a sentence of outlawry that cannot be enforced is not a power at all.","Paragraph 3, against the statement — the king's machinery went straight past the earls. Writs under his seal were addressed to the bishop, the earl and the reeve together, and the shire reeve was the king's own officer in the shire, not the earl's; the geld was assessed on hides and collected shire by shire; and silver pennies were struck in boroughs everywhere but only from dies the king issued, with the coinage called in and reminted. Interim judgement: the crown reached every hide and every penny in England without having to ask an earl's leave.","Paragraph 4, against the statement — the king still made earls, and the Witan still made kings. Edward gave Northumbria to Tostig in 1055 and the Witan outlawed Earl Aelfgar of Mercia in the same year; even the rebels of 1065 had to have their choice confirmed by the king, which is what the settlement made at Oxford on 28 October 1065 did for Morcar. Interim judgement: the earls could bend the crown but could not do without it, and no earl ever tried to take it.","The point beyond the stimulus bullets: at least one paragraph must rest on material the bullets do not prompt. The writs and the shire reeve, the control of the coinage, the crisis of 1051 to 1052 and the outlawry of Aelfgar all qualify, and without one of them the answer cannot reach the top level however well it is written.","The evaluation thread: keep returning to the same test in every paragraph — was this a power the king simply did not have, or one he had to borrow from somebody else? Run it all the way through so that the conclusion is the last step of an argument rather than a new claim.","The conclusion must rank and justify, not summarise. Argue that writ, reeve, geld and coin were never in the earls' hands at all, so the monarchy was not weak in what it could order, only in what it could compel; the earls' hold on men and money mattered in a crisis, as 1051 and 1052 showed, rather than in the ordinary running of the kingdom. Then qualify it: the greatest weakness when the crown was challenged, rather than the greatest weakness of English government.","Timing: about 25 minutes. Roughly five to plan the four paragraphs and write the criterion sentence, eighteen to write, and two to check that the last paragraph answers 'how far' rather than 'why'."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:13:18Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Judged afresh after the dedupe retarget, and the retargeted items hold: I checked about forty dates, figures and places, eleven of them on the web — the fleet at Dives from mid-August 1066, the move to Saint-Valery in mid-September, sailing the evening of 27 September and Pevensey on 28 September; the Chronicle’s 1018 tribute of £72,000 with £10,500 from London, hedged in the text as many historians think it too high; Emma’s marriage to Æthelred in 1002; Robert of Jumièges at London in 1044 and Canterbury in 1051; Hereford on 24 October 1055 with Ælfgar, outlawed that same year; Bishop Leofgar killed in June 1056; Rhuddlan raided at Christmas 1062; Gruffydd killed by his own men on 5 August 1063 with his head sent to Harold; and the earl’s third penny as a third of the profits of justice and of the borough revenues — every one correct, with no invented event and no misattribution. The 2024 caps and the 2025 two-part 1(a)/1(b), renumbering and 1 hour 50 minutes are right throughout, the specimen booklet B1 questions (Domesday Book, the Marcher earldoms, Normanisation and the Church, the rebellions of 1069 to 1071) are nowhere near the bank, and dupscan prints ‘no duplication’; fix the 1060/1063 boundary in question 9 and publish."}}},"B1-2":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"B1-2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This key topic is the story of how a duke who had won one battle turned it into a kingdom he could hold for twenty-one years, and booklet B examines it harder than almost any other part of the depth study. The 4-mark question (two 2-mark parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each asking for one feature) wants features of things like the submission of 1066, the Marcher earldoms, a motte and bailey castle or the rebellion at Ely; the 12-mark question wants causation, usually why a rebellion happened, why it failed or why William built castles and redistributed land; and the 16-mark judgement almost always asks you to rank William's methods against one another — violence, castles, land, trusted men and the Church. Every one of those questions is answered with the same body of precise material: dates, named earls, named castles and named rebellions. Learn the material once and it serves all three questions, because the difference between a Level 2 and a Level 4 answer here is almost never a missing fact, it is a fact that has not been made to do any work.","idea":[{"h":"Winning the submission: from Hastings to the crown, October to December 1066","t":"Hastings killed Harold and his brothers Gyrth and Leofwine on 14 October 1066, but it did not give William England. The Witan in London elected Edgar Aetheling, a boy without lands or a following, while Earls Edwin and Morcar withdrew north. William did not march straight on London. He took Dover and Canterbury, then swung west of the city, burning as he went, and crossed the Thames at Wallingford, where Archbishop Stigand submitted. At Berkhamsted, Edgar Aetheling, Ealdred, Archbishop of York, Edwin, Morcar and the leading men of London submitted together. William was crowned at Westminster Abbey on Christmas Day 1066 by Ealdred rather than Stigand, whose position the papacy questioned, and swore to rule the English as well as the best kings before him. A top-level answer uses the submission to show a method, not a sequence: ravage the districts that resist, spare those that submit, and take the crown only when it is offered, so that conquest can be presented as lawful succession.","code":"B1-2 — 1 Establishing control: The submission of the earls, 1066"},{"h":"Paying an army in land: tenants-in-chief, knight service and the men William trusted","t":"William's followers had come for reward, and the only currency he had in quantity was English land. He claimed to be Edward's lawful heir, so everyone who fought against him at Hastings was a traitor whose estates were forfeit; each rebellion after 1066 added more. Land was granted to about two hundred great tenants-in-chief who held it directly from the king in return for a fixed quota of knights, homage and fealty, and who then granted parts of it to their own knights. Most of those estates were deliberately scattered across many shires, so no lord could turn a block of England into a private kingdom. The men closest to him got the most: his half-brother Odo, Bishop of Bayeux, was made Earl of Kent and became the greatest lay landholder after the king, and William fitzOsbern was made Earl of Hereford; the two of them governed England as regents when William returned to Normandy in 1067. A top-level answer treats landholding as William's central instrument of control, tying reward, military service and punishment into one system.","code":"B1-2 — 1 Establishing control: Rewarding followers and establishing control on the borderlands"},{"h":"The exception on the borders: why the Marcher earldoms were different","t":"Wales was not conquered and its princes raided the border, so on the march William did the opposite of what he did everywhere else. Three earldoms were created along the Welsh frontier — Hereford, given to William fitzOsbern, Shrewsbury, given to Roger de Montgomery, and Chester, held by Hugh of Avranches — and their lands were compact rather than scattered, so an earl could raise men in hours. Their earls were given powers no other lord had: they could raise castles without the king's licence, keep whatever land they took from the Welsh, hold their estates free of the geld and run their own courts. It was a calculated risk, trading the danger of over-mighty subjects for a border that defended itself and left William free to deal with rebellion elsewhere; when the risk came due in 1075 it was fitzOsbern's son Roger who revolted. A top-level answer uses the March to show that William's methods were adapted to the problem, not applied uniformly.","code":"B1-2 — 1 Establishing control: Rewarding followers and establishing control on the borderlands"},{"h":"Castles: how a few thousand Normans held a kingdom of two million","t":"The Normans were a tiny minority ruling a hostile population, and the castle was the technology that made that possible. A motte and bailey could be thrown up in weeks by forced English labour: a steep earth mound with a timber tower on top, a walled yard below holding hall, stables and stores, all ringed by ditch and palisade. William raised one inside the Roman fort at Pevensey and another at Hastings within days of landing in 1066, and on his march north in 1068 he planted castles at Warwick, Nottingham, York, Lincoln, Huntingdon and Cambridge. They were bases from which knights rode out, refuges when a district rose, storehouses for supplies, centres of a lord's local government, and deliberate statements of who now ruled; where they were built in towns, English houses were pulled down for them. The greatest were later rebuilt in stone, the White Tower in London among them. A top-level answer explains castles as offensive and administrative tools, not just defences.","code":"B1-2 — 1 Establishing control: Reasons for the building of castles"},{"h":"The rebellions of 1068 to 1071: why the English rose and why they always lost","t":"Resistance came in waves and never once combined. In 1068 Edwin and Morcar rebelled: their earldoms had been cut down, Normans were taking land in the midlands, the geld was heavy, they had been carried to Normandy in 1067 in William's train, and the marriage William had promised Edwin to one of his daughters had not happened. William marched, built castles at Warwick and Nottingham, and the revolt collapsed without a battle. In 1069 Robert Cumin, the new earl, was killed with his men at Durham in January; in August a Danish fleet said to number some 240 ships entered the Humber and joined Edgar Aetheling, Waltheof and Gospatric, and in September they took York and destroyed both castles. In 1070 Hereward, a Lincolnshire thegn, plundered Peterborough Abbey with Danish help and held the Isle of Ely, where Morcar joined him in 1071 until William's blockade and causeway forced a surrender. A top-level answer explains the pattern: local grievances, no single leader, no coordination, and a king who could always concentrate force first.","code":"B1-2 — 2 Anglo-Saxon resistance, 1068–71: Causes and outcomes of Anglo-Saxon resistance"},{"h":"The Harrying of the North, 1069 to 1070: terror as policy","t":"After buying off the Danish fleet William did not garrison the north, he destroyed it. Through the winter of 1069 to 1070 his men burned villages, crops, seed corn, tools and livestock between the Humber and the Tees, and carried the same treatment into Durham, Cheshire and Staffordshire early in 1070; he kept Christmas at York among the ruins. The reasons were practical: the north had risen three times in three years, it was the door through which Danish and Scottish help arrived, and a wasted country could not feed an invading army. Orderic Vitalis, a Norman monk writing some sixty years later, claimed a hundred thousand died of hunger and condemned William for it; the number cannot be checked, but when Domesday Book was compiled in 1086 many Yorkshire manors were still entered as waste. No rebellion began north of the Humber again in William's reign. A top-level answer weighs effectiveness against cost and sets the Harrying beside castles and landholding rather than treating it alone.","code":"B1-2 — 3 The legacy of resistance to 1087: The reasons for and features of Harrying of the North"},{"h":"The great transfer of land, 1066 to 1087","t":"Every rebellion fed the same machine. Lands forfeited at Hastings, then by Edwin and Morcar, by the northern rebels, by Morcar at Ely and by Roger and Ralph in 1075 were regranted to Normans, so that by the time of Domesday Book in 1086 barely a twentieth of the land was left in English hands and the great estates, lay and ecclesiastical, were almost entirely Norman. The Church went the same way: Stigand was deposed in 1070 and replaced at Canterbury by Lanfranc, abbot of Saint-Etienne at Caen, and by the end of the reign Wulfstan of Worcester was the one English-born bishop still in office. William bound the new landholders to himself directly at Salisbury in August 1086, when the great men of the kingdom swore allegiance to him in person. A top-level answer argues that the land settlement was both the reward that kept the conquerors loyal and the punishment that made rebellion ruinous, which is why it outlasted every other measure.","code":"B1-2 — 3 The legacy of resistance to 1087: Changes in landownership from Anglo-Saxon to Norman"},{"h":"The Revolt of the Earls, 1075: the rebellion that was not English","t":"The last rising of the reign was led by Normans and a Breton, not by Anglo-Saxons. Ralph de Gael, Earl of East Anglia, married Emma, sister of Roger de Breteuil, Earl of Hereford, at a wedding feast in 1075 where the plot was made, and Waltheof, Earl of Northumbria, the last great English earl, was drawn in. Their grievances were about power rather than nationality: Roger had lost the freedoms his father William fitzOsbern had enjoyed and resented royal sheriffs interfering in his earldom, Ralph had been pushed to the edge of a court he no longer counted in, and both judged the moment right because William was in Normandy. They expected Danish help, and a fleet under Cnut, son of King Swein, did come. A top-level answer notices what the revolt reveals: by 1075 the danger to William came from his own tenants-in-chief, and no English rising joined them.","code":"B1-2 — 4 Revolt of the Earls, 1075: Reasons for and features of the revolt"},{"h":"Defeat, punishment and the twelve quiet years","t":"The revolt was beaten without the king. Waltheof lost his nerve and confessed the plot to Lanfranc, Archbishop of Canterbury, who was acting as regent; Lanfranc excommunicated Roger and set the loyal bishops and sheriffs in motion. Roger was stopped at the Severn by local levies raised by Wulfstan, Bishop of Worcester, and Aethelwig, Abbot of Evesham; Ralph was defeated near Cambridge by Odo of Bayeux and Geoffrey, Bishop of Coutances, and fled abroad, leaving his wife Emma to hold Norwich castle until she was allowed to leave the kingdom on terms. The Danish fleet of some 200 ships under Cnut arrived too late, raided York and went home. Roger was imprisoned for life and his earldom forfeited; Waltheof was beheaded at Winchester in 1076, the only English earl William put to death. Hereford and East Anglia were not granted out again. A top-level answer uses 1075 to show a settled kingdom: the machinery of government defeated an earls' revolt while the king was overseas, and no rebellion followed in England before William died in 1087.","code":"B1-2 — 4 Revolt of the Earls, 1075: The defeat of the revolt and its effects"}],"examples":[{"title":"The two-part features question on 1067 and 1068, built so that all four marks land","setup":"The question is: 1(a) Describe one feature of the siege of Exeter in 1068. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the way England was governed while William was in Normandy in 1067. [2 marks] No bullets and no sources are given, and each part is marked on its own, one mark for the feature and one for supporting information. Your notes hold this material: William sailed for Normandy in March 1067 and stayed until December, taking Edgar Aetheling, Stigand, Edwin and Morcar with him as honoured hostages; he left Odo of Bayeux, Earl of Kent, and William fitzOsbern, Earl of Hereford, to rule in his place, and the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle complains that they built castles far and wide and harassed the wretched people; in the autumn Kentish rebels invited Eustace, Count of Boulogne, across the Channel and his attempt on Dover was beaten off by the castle garrison; in the spring of 1068 Exeter, where Gytha, Harold's mother, had taken refuge, refused fealty and refused the tax William demanded, and shut its gates; William brought Norman knights and English fyrd levies west, the city held out eighteen days and then surrendered on terms, no plundering and no more tax than it had paid before the Conquest, while Gytha escaped by sea; a castle, Rougemont, was raised inside the walls and put under Baldwin fitzGilbert. Turn the notes into one feature for each part, each with supporting information.","steps":["Split the notes between the two parts before writing anything, because a feature of the siege earns nothing in part (b) and a feature of the year the king was away earns nothing in part (a): the shut gates, the eighteen days, the terms and Rougemont go with Exeter, while the two deputies, the hostages carried overseas and the attempt on Dover go with the absence.","Part (a), written as a statement about what the siege was like: it ended in a bargain rather than a sack. Supporting information: after eighteen days the city was spared plundering and kept its tax at the level it had paid before the Conquest, and a castle was planted inside the walls to hold what the terms had won.","Part (b), written the same way about the government of that year: the kingdom was left to deputies exercising the king's own authority. Supporting information: Odo of Bayeux and William fitzOsbern governed from March until the king came back at the end of the year, and the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle accuses them of building castles far and wide and harassing the people.","Check the shape before stopping: one feature in each part and no more, each carrying a hard piece of evidence — a named man, a named place or a term of the surrender — rather than a second general sentence, and not a word spent on whether William's methods worked."],"cues":["Which of these notes belong to part (a) and which to part (b)?","What kind of ending did the siege have, and what in the notes proves it?","Who was exercising the king's power in England while he was overseas?","What must be true of each part before you put your pen down?"]},{"title":"Getting past the bullets on a 12-mark 'Explain why'","setup":"The question is: Explain why the north of England was so difficult for William I to control in the years 1066 to 1071. You may use the following in your answer: Durham; Scotland. You must also use information of your own. You know that York lies the best part of two hundred miles from London and that Norman power was built up in the south first; that Northumbria had its own ruling families and had driven out Earl Tostig in 1065; that Copsi, given the earldom in 1067, was killed within about five weeks and his killer was dead before the year was out, after which Gospatric bought the earldom from William; that Robert Cumin, sent north as earl, was killed with his men at Durham in January 1069, and that Durham had no royal castle until 1072; that the Humber let a Danish fleet reach York, as one said to number some 240 ships did in the late summer of 1069, and that Yorkshire had been settled by Scandinavians for generations; and that Edgar Aetheling took refuge with Malcolm III, who married Edgar's sister Margaret and made peace with William only at Abernethy in 1072. Plan the three reasons you will explain.","steps":["Read the cap before you plan: an answer that only works the two bullets cannot reach the top level, so at least one paragraph must rest on material neither bullet prompts.","Reason one, from the first bullet, taken beyond a statement of fact: no man William appointed could hold the north for him, because the earldom had its own families and its own feuds, so Copsi was dead within weeks of receiving it and Robert Cumin and his men were burned out at Durham, and every appointment had to be imposed again from nothing.","Reason two, from the second bullet: the border turned defeat into retreat rather than an end, because Edgar Aetheling and the northern leaders could cross it, and Malcolm III, married to Edgar's sister, had every reason to keep an English claimant alive on his side of it.","Reason three, entirely their own: the sea. The Humber carried a fleet to within reach of York in days, and it landed among people whose ancestors had settled there, so the north could be reinforced from outside faster than an army could march to it from the south.","Finish the plan with the link: say which reason made the other two dangerous, so the answer ranks rather than lists."],"cues":["What does the printed cap stop an answer from reaching?","What happened to the men William sent north to rule for him?","Why did a king of Scots matter to a rising in Yorkshire?","Which reason is prompted by neither bullet?","What has to be in the last sentence for the answer to rank its reasons?"]},{"title":"Weighing the risings against one another for a 16-mark judgement","setup":"The statement is: 'The rebellion at Ely in the years 1070 to 1071 was the most difficult rebellion William I had to put down.' How far do you agree? The bullets offered are the fens and Morcar. The evidence available to you: the revolt of Edwin and Morcar in 1068 collapsed without a battle once William marched north and raised castles at Warwick and Nottingham; in 1069 the northern rebels and a Danish fleet took York and destroyed both castles there with their garrisons, and it took a winter campaign and the Harrying to end that; at Ely, Hereward plundered Peterborough Abbey with Danish help in 1070 as a Norman abbot was about to take over, and the rebels then held an island in the fens reachable only by water or narrow paths, with Morcar and Aethelwine, Bishop of Durham, among them; William blockaded the island with ships and built a causeway across the marsh, and the rebels surrendered in 1071, Morcar to life imprisonment while Hereward got away; and in 1075 the revolt of the earls was put down by Lanfranc with the loyal bishops and sheriffs while William stayed in Normandy. Decide the criterion you will judge by and order your paragraphs.","steps":["Fix the criterion first, because 'most difficult' cannot be argued without one: decide whether difficulty means the effort a rising cost, the time it took or the danger it put the throne in, and say which in the opening sentence.","Put the case for Ely on effort: the ground defeated the methods that had worked everywhere else, so the king had to blockade an island with ships and build a road across a marsh to reach an enemy who could not be brought to battle.","Put the case against on danger: Ely had one great earl and a band of thegns behind it, while the rising in the north destroyed two castles and their garrisons and had a foreign fleet at its back, and that is the one that could have cost William the kingdom.","Bring in the case neither bullet prompts: the revolt of the earls was beaten without the king setting foot in England, which gives you a measure — a rebellion is hardest when it forces the king to come himself and stay until it is over.","Judge on the criterion you set rather than on the story that reads best: say whether Ely was the hardest to reach or merely the slowest to fall, and rank it against the north on that test."],"cues":["What question does 'most difficult' force you to answer first?","What did the fens make impossible that a march into the midlands made easy?","Which rising came closest to costing William the north?","Which rebellion here is prompted by neither bullet?","What must the final paragraph do that the earlier ones did not?"]},{"title":"Choosing precise evidence for a paragraph on William's terms","setup":"You are writing a paragraph in a judgement answer arguing that William I's pardons and restorations did as much to keep England quiet as his punishments. Your notes hold: Exeter surrendered in 1068 on terms that spared the city plundering and held its tax at the level paid before the Conquest; Edwin and Morcar were pardoned after their revolt that year and were back at court; Gospatric, who had bought the earldom of Northumbria and then joined the risings, made terms in 1070 and kept it until 1072; Waltheof joined the rising in the north, submitted, was pardoned and married William's niece Judith; against that, Morcar was imprisoned for life after Ely, Waltheof was beheaded at Winchester in 1076, and the Harrying of the winter of 1069 to 1070 left manors between the Humber and the Tees still entered as waste in Domesday Book in 1086; Norman writers praise William's mercy, while Orderic Vitalis, writing some sixty years later, condemns his cruelty and puts a confession of it into the dying king's mouth. Turn this into one paragraph that argues rather than narrates.","steps":["Open with the claim, not the chronology: say that William bought submission wherever it could be bought, and destroyed only where a man had already been forgiven once.","Prove it with the detail that carries it: Edwin and Morcar rebelled and were pardoned, Gospatric rebelled and kept the earldom he had bought, and the terms at Exeter cost the city nothing it had not paid before the Conquest, so mercy on those terms was cheaper than another campaign.","Handle the chroniclers honestly, which is what an examiner rewards: Norman writers had a king to praise and Orderic, writing two generations later, had a deed to condemn, so neither settles the question; what does settle it is the record of who kept his lands afterwards and who lost them.","Close with the test the question set: the men who were spared stayed quiet and the men who rebelled a second time were destroyed, so on the criterion of what actually kept the peace, pardon and punishment were one policy rather than alternatives."],"cues":["What is the claim, stated before any narrative?","Which men in the notes were given a second chance, and what did it cost William?","Why can neither the Norman writers nor Orderic settle the point on their own?","What does the paragraph have to say about the question before it ends?"]},{"title":"Writing the judgement at the end of a 16-mark answer","setup":"The statement is: 'The Harrying of the North in the years 1069 to 1070 cost William I more than it gained him.' The bullets offered are waste and Domesday Book. You have already written three paragraphs: what the destruction bought, with nothing rising north of the Humber again in the reign and the fleet that came in 1075 finding no rising to join; what it cost the kingdom, with villages, crops, seed corn, tools and stock destroyed between the Humber and the Tees, manors still entered as waste in Domesday Book in 1086, and a region that afterwards paid little tax and owed little service; and what it cost the king's name, with Orderic Vitalis condemning the deed some sixty years later and putting a confession of it into the dying king's mouth, beside a figure of a hundred thousand dead that cannot be verified. Now write the conclusion, which must decide how far the statement holds and on what basis.","steps":["Name the test you are deciding on, which here is whether a king is judged by the danger he removed or by the kingdom he was left ruling afterwards.","Concede what the statement gets right: the bill was real and long, because land left waste paid no geld and owed no service, and the damage was still being written down by the king's own surveyors a generation later.","Set the limit: the alternative was a region any fleet could reach from the sea and any rebel could raise again, and no rising began there for the rest of the reign, so the policy bought precisely the security it was meant to buy.","Give the ranked answer in one sentence and justify it by the test, then qualify it rather than overstating: the cost was the greater in revenue and in reputation and the gain was the greater in security, so the statement holds only if the account is written in money, and say which of the two you think the king was buying."],"cues":["Which two things does the conclusion have to tell apart?","What in the earlier paragraphs supports the statement?","What would William have faced in the north if he had not done it?","How can a conclusion agree and disagree without sitting on the fence?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Where did the English leaders finally submit to William in 1066, and who was among them?","a":"At Berkhamsted: Edgar Aetheling, Ealdred, Archbishop of York, Earls Edwin and Morcar and the leading men of London."},{"q":"Name the three Marcher earldoms and one privilege their earls enjoyed.","a":"Hereford, Shrewsbury and Chester. Their earls could build castles without the king's licence, keep land taken from the Welsh, hold their estates free of the geld and run their own courts."},{"q":"What happened at York in September 1069?","a":"Northern rebels with Edgar Aetheling, Waltheof and Gospatric, joined by a Danish fleet, took the city and destroyed both Norman castles and their garrison."},{"q":"Why did William harry the north rather than garrison it?","a":"The region had risen three times in three years and was the route by which Danish and Scottish help arrived; a wasted countryside could not supply a rebel or an invading army, and no rising began north of the Humber again in his reign."},{"q":"Who defeated the Revolt of the Earls in 1075, and what happened to the three earls?","a":"Lanfranc as regent, with loyal bishops, sheriffs and local levies under Wulfstan of Worcester and Aethelwig of Evesham, and Odo of Bayeux and Geoffrey of Coutances in the field. Ralph fled abroad, Roger was imprisoned for life and Waltheof was beheaded at Winchester in 1076."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 2 lasts 1 hour 50 minutes for 64 marks, about 55 minutes a booklet. Booklet B, the British depth study, carries 32 marks: Question 1(a) and 1(b) 'Describe one feature of...' on two separate aspects of content (2 marks each), Question 2 'Explain why...' (12 marks) with two stimulus points, and either Question 3 or Question 4, a 16-mark 'How far do you agree? Explain your answer.' with two stimulus points. There are no spelling, punctuation and grammar marks anywhere on Paper 2.","'Describe one feature of...' is point-marked, not levelled: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about it. Each part wants one feature only, with no reasons and no judgement, and a second feature in the same part earns nothing.","The stimulus caps are printed inside the mark scheme, and there is no cap at Level 2 on either question. On the 12-mark question the maximum is 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and there is no access to Level 4 for them; on the 16-mark question the maximum is 11 marks on the same condition, again with no access to Level 4.","Timing in booklet B is about a minute a mark inside the 55 minutes it is worth: roughly 5 minutes on the two parts of Question 1 together, 20 on the 12-mark Question 2 and 25 on the 16-mark judgement, with planning time for the judgement taken out of that 25.","The spine of dates for this key topic: 25 December 1066, coronation at Westminster; 1068, the revolt of Edwin and Morcar; January and September 1069, the risings in the north; winter 1069 to 1070, the Harrying of the North; 1070 to 1071, Hereward and Ely; 1075, the Revolt of the Earls; 1076, Waltheof executed; 1086, Domesday Book and the oath at Salisbury; 1087, William dies."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the submission of the English leaders to William in 1066. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of William's coronation on 25 December 1066. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, the two parts point-marked and marked separately: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information. For (a) credit the piecemeal submission as William encircled London, Stigand at Wallingford, and the gathering at Berkhamsted with Edgar Aetheling, Ealdred, Edwin and Morcar. For (b) credit the coronation at Westminster Abbey on Christmas Day 1066, Ealdred officiating rather than Stigand, and the oath to rule the English as well as the best kings before him.","sol":"(a) One feature was that the submissions came in stages as William encircled London rather than after an assault on the city. Archbishop Stigand submitted when William crossed the Thames at Wallingford, and the rest held out until he reached Berkhamsted. (b) One feature was that the ceremony was staged to present the conquest as a lawful succession. William was crowned at Westminster Abbey on Christmas Day 1066 by Ealdred, Archbishop of York, and not by Stigand, whose position the papacy questioned, and he swore to rule the English as well as the best kings before him.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a) is about how William came to be offered the crown; part (b) is about the ceremony itself.","One feature in each part, and each needs one precise piece of evidence attached to it, not a second general sentence.","Write each part as a sentence beginning 'One feature was that...', then a sentence of supporting detail naming a person, a place or a date."],"codes":["B1-2 — 1 Establishing control: The submission of the earls, 1066"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the Marcher earldoms. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the way William rewarded his followers with land. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, the two parts point-marked and marked separately: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information. For (a) credit their position on the Welsh border and the three earldoms with their holders, the compactness of their lands, and the special powers of their earls (castles without licence, land won from the Welsh, freedom from the geld, their own courts). For (b) credit the forfeiture of the lands of those who fought for Harold at Hastings, the grant of estates to tenants-in-chief in return for a quota of knights, homage and fealty, and the deliberate scattering of most estates across many shires.","sol":"(a) One feature was that they were created along the Welsh border, where there was no settled frontier. Hereford was given to William fitzOsbern, Shrewsbury to Roger de Montgomery and Chester was held by Hugh of Avranches. (b) One feature was that land was granted in return for military service rather than given outright. Tenants-in-chief held their estates directly from the king and owed him a fixed quota of knights, homage and fealty, and they granted parts of those estates to their own knights on the same terms.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a) asks what made the Welsh border a different problem from the rest of England.","Part (b) is about the bargain behind every grant of land, not about who received one.","Name the earldoms and their earls in (a), and name what a tenant-in-chief owed the king in (b)."],"codes":["B1-2 — 1 Establishing control: Rewarding followers and establishing control on the borderlands"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of a Norman motte and bailey castle. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the castles William built on his march north in 1068. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, the two parts point-marked and marked separately: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information. For (a) credit the motte with its timber tower and ditch, the bailey with hall, stables and stores behind bank and palisade, and the speed of construction by forced English labour. For (b) credit the castles raised at Warwick, Nottingham, York, Lincoln, Huntingdon and Cambridge, their siting on the rebels' own ground as William advanced, and the collapse of the revolt of Edwin and Morcar without a battle.","sol":"(a) One feature was the motte, a steep mound of earth raised by forced English labour with a timber tower on top and a ditch around its base; from it the garrison could see the countryside for miles and retreat there if the bailey fell. (b) One feature was that the castles went up as William advanced, one after another along his line of march. He raised them at Warwick, Nottingham, York, Lincoln, Huntingdon and Cambridge in 1068, so that every stage of the rebels' country had a Norman garrison in it and the revolt collapsed without a battle.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a) is about the earthwork itself; picture its two parts before you write anything.","Part (b) is about where and when the castles of 1068 went up, not about how one was built.","Support (a) with what the mound carried and what it was for, and (b) with the names of two or three of those castles."],"codes":["B1-2 — 1 Establishing control: Reasons for the building of castles"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the rebellion at Ely in the years 1070 to 1071. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the revolt of Edwin and Morcar in 1068. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, the two parts point-marked and marked separately: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information. For (a) credit the fenland site of the Isle of Ely, the sack of Peterborough Abbey in 1070 with Danish help, the mixture of rebels (Hereward, Morcar, Bishop Aethelwine of Durham), and William's blockade and causeway with the outcome. For (b) credit the grievances of the two earls (the marriage promised to Edwin, the geld, Norman grants inside their earldoms), the collapse of the revolt without a battle when William marched north raising castles at Warwick and Nottingham, and the pardon the brothers were given.","sol":"(a) One feature was the ground the rebels chose. They held the Isle of Ely, dry land ringed by fen and reachable only by boat or narrow paths, so a small force could defy William's cavalry for months. (b) One feature was that the revolt ended without a battle being fought. William marched north at once and raised castles at Warwick and Nottingham, so that Edwin and Morcar submitted rather than fight and were pardoned.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a): start from the landscape the rebels were using and why William found it difficult.","Part (b): think about how the revolt of 1068 ended rather than about why it began.","Each part needs one name, date or place attached to the feature as its supporting information."],"codes":["B1-2 — 2 Anglo-Saxon resistance, 1068–71: Causes and outcomes of Anglo-Saxon resistance"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the Harrying of the North in the years 1069 to 1070. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the Revolt of the Earls in 1075. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, the two parts point-marked and marked separately: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information. For (a) credit the systematic destruction of villages, crops, seed corn, livestock and tools through the winter, the area covered from the Humber to the Tees and on into Durham, Cheshire and Staffordshire, and the evidence of its scale in Orderic Vitalis and in the waste entries of Domesday Book. For (b) credit the three rebel earls and their earldoms, the wedding feast of 1075 at which the plot was made, William's absence in Normandy, the expectation of Danish help, and the defeat of the revolt by Lanfranc with the loyal bishops and sheriffs.","sol":"(a) One feature was that it was deliberate destruction rather than fighting. Through the winter of 1069 to 1070 William's men burned villages, crops, seed corn, tools and livestock, so that the following year's harvest was destroyed along with that year's food. (b) One feature was that it was led by William's own earls rather than by Englishmen. Ralph de Gael, Earl of East Anglia, and Roger de Breteuil, Earl of Hereford, made the plot at the wedding feast of 1075 at which Ralph married Roger's sister Emma, and only Waltheof, Earl of Northumbria, the last great English earl, was drawn in with them.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a): say what was destroyed, not why William ordered it.","Part (b): think about who the rebels of 1075 were and where their plot was made.","Support (a) with the kinds of thing burned and (b) with the names of the earls and their earldoms."],"codes":["B1-2 — 3 The legacy of resistance to 1087: The reasons for and features of Harrying of the North","B1-2 — 4 Revolt of the Earls, 1075: Reasons for and features of the revolt"]},{"q":"Explain why the English leaders submitted to William by the end of 1066. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • Berkhamsted • the ravaging of the south-east. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 / AO1 6. Credit the destruction of English leadership at Hastings and the weakness of Edgar Aetheling, the withdrawal of Edwin and Morcar, William's encirclement and ravaging of the south-east with the surrender of Winchester and Stigand at Wallingford, the calculation facing the leaders at Berkhamsted, and William's presentation of himself as lawful heir, crowned by Ealdred on Christmas Day. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The first reason was that England had no leadership left to resist with. Harold and his brothers Gyrth and Leofwine all died at Hastings on 14 October 1066, and the Witan's answer, electing Edgar Aetheling, showed the size of the problem rather than solving it, because Edgar was a boy with no lands and no following of his own. Edwin and Morcar came to London and then withdrew to their earldoms in the north, so the one substantial English army still intact never took the field. Resistance needed a leader with men behind him, and after 14 October there was none.\n\nThe second reason was that William made submission the cheaper choice. Instead of attacking London he took Dover and Canterbury, then swung west and south of the Thames, burning as he went, and crossed at Wallingford. Winchester, with the old royal treasury, gave in; at Wallingford Archbishop Stigand submitted. Every district that resisted was wasted and every one that submitted was spared, so by the time the leading men reached Berkhamsted they were choosing between keeping their estates and losing everything, not between two kings.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond both prompts: William took trouble to look like a lawful successor rather than a conqueror. He had always claimed to be Edward's designated heir, and he accepted the crown only when it was offered to him. He was crowned at Westminster Abbey on Christmas Day 1066 by Ealdred, Archbishop of York, and not by Stigand, whose position the papacy questioned, and he swore to rule the English as well as the best kings before him.\n\nThe three worked together. The deaths at Hastings removed the means of resistance, the ravaging removed the incentive, and the promise of lawful kingship gave the earls and bishops a form of words in which submission was not surrender.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the English would have needed in order to fight on, and check each item off.","Three separate reasons, each a paragraph that names people and places and then explains how that reason produced submission.","One reason must come from outside the two bullets: look at how William presented his kingship, not only at what he did to the country."],"codes":["B1-2 — 1 Establishing control: The submission of the earls, 1066"]},{"q":"Explain why the Normans built so many castles in England in the years 1066 to 1087. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • rebellion • motte and bailey. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 / AO1 6. Credit the small number of Normans among a hostile population, the speed and cheapness of motte and bailey construction, castles as bases against rebellion (Pevensey and Hastings 1066; Warwick, Nottingham, York, Lincoln, Huntingdon and Cambridge in 1068; the second York castle in 1069), the borderlands and the Marcher earls' freedom to build, and castles as centres of lordship, supply and intimidation in towns. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The first reason was arithmetic. A few thousand Normans were holding down a population of perhaps two million people who had not asked for them, and an army in the field can only be in one place. A castle turned a handful of knights into a permanent garrison that could hold a district, so William planted one inside the Roman fort at Pevensey and another at Hastings within days of landing in 1066, before he had a kingdom at all.\n\nThe second reason was rebellion, and the record shows the castles following the risings. When Edwin and Morcar rebelled in 1068 William marched north raising castles as he went, at Warwick, Nottingham, York, Lincoln, Huntingdon and Cambridge, and the revolt collapsed without a battle because every stage of the rebels' country now had a Norman garrison in it. After the rising of January 1069 he built a second castle at York. A motte and bailey could be thrown up in weeks by forced English labour, which is why the answer to a rebellion could be a castle rather than a campaign.\n\nThe third reason lies outside the bullets: castles were instruments of government and of intimidation, not only of defence. A lord's castle was where his knights were quartered, his supplies stored, his rents collected and his court held, so it governed the scattered estates around it. Where one was built in a town, English houses were pulled down for it, and the message was not lost. On the Welsh border the Marcher earls were licensed to build castles freely, because a frontier had to be held by fortification rather than by marching.\n\nThe reasons connect: the Normans were too few to hold England by force alone, so they held it by occupying it, and the castle was the cheapest and quickest way to turn an army of conquest into a permanent occupation.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Start from the numbers: how many Normans were there compared with the English?","Three reasons, each explaining what a castle could do that an army could not, with named castles as evidence.","Go beyond defence and rebellion in one paragraph: think about what else was done inside a castle every day."],"codes":["B1-2 — 1 Establishing control: Reasons for the building of castles","B1-2 — 1 Establishing control: Rewarding followers and establishing control on the borderlands"]},{"q":"Explain why Earls Edwin and Morcar rebelled in 1068. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the promised marriage • taxation. You must also use information of your own. ","a":"AO2 6 / AO1 6. Credit the broken promise of a marriage between Edwin and one of William's daughters, the heavy geld levied on their tenants, the reduction of their earldoms by grants to Normans and the new earldom of Hereford, their removal to Normandy in William's train in 1067, and the encouragement of William's absence, of Edgar Aetheling and of Welsh support. Credit the outcome as the collapse of the revolt when William marched and built castles. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The first reason was that the brothers had been left without a route into the new regime. Edwin had been promised one of William's daughters in marriage, which would have made him a Norman insider and secured his family's position under the new king. The marriage never happened. What was left was a great English earl with a title, an inheritance and no expectation of favour, which made him dangerous precisely because he still had something to lose.\n\nThe second reason was taxation. William levied a heavy geld after the Conquest, and it fell on the earls' own tenants in Mercia and Northumbria. An earl who collected a foreign king's tax from his own men lost the standing on which his power rested, so every levy pushed Edwin and Morcar towards a choice between their king and their followers.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond both prompts: their earldoms were being dismantled around them. Norman lords were granted estates inside the midlands, a new earldom of Hereford was created for William fitzOsbern on their border, and in 1067 the brothers were taken to Normandy in William's train, honoured in name and hostages in fact. Waiting meant being reduced by degrees, and in 1068 they judged rebellion the lesser risk, with Edgar Aetheling available as an alternative king and Welsh help within reach.\n\nThose reasons explain the rising, and the way it ended explains the pattern of the whole conquest. William marched north at once, raising castles at Warwick and Nottingham, and the revolt collapsed without a battle. Grievance was never in short supply among the English earls; what they lacked was the ability to act before William could concentrate force against them.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what the brothers had expected from William in 1066 and what they had actually got by 1068.","Three reasons, each with named evidence, each explaining how the grievance turned into a decision to rebel.","At least one paragraph must use material the bullets do not prompt, so ask what was changing in their earldoms while they waited."],"codes":["B1-2 — 2 Anglo-Saxon resistance, 1068–71: Causes and outcomes of Anglo-Saxon resistance"]},{"q":"Explain why landownership in England changed so completely in the years 1066 to 1087. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • rebellion • knight service. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 / AO1 6. Credit William's claim to be the lawful heir making Hastings a treason and its losers' land forfeit, the repeated forfeitures that followed the risings of 1069, 1071 and 1075 (the northern rebels, Edwin and Morcar after their flight from court in 1071, Waltheof, Roger and Ralph), the need to pay followers who had come for land, the military demand for knight quotas from tenants-in-chief, the deliberate scattering of estates, and the parallel change in the Church (Stigand deposed in 1070, Lanfranc, Norman abbots). Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The first reason was that rebellion kept feeding the machine. William claimed the throne as Edward's lawful heir, which made everyone who fought for Harold at Hastings a traitor whose land was forfeit. Each rising then produced the same result on a larger scale: Edwin and Morcar were pardoned after their revolt in 1068 but forfeited everything when they fled from William's court in 1071, the northern rebels of 1069 lost their estates in the Harrying and the confiscations that followed, and in 1075 Roger de Breteuil and Ralph de Gael forfeited the earldoms of Hereford and East Anglia. Resistance did not slow the transfer of land; it accelerated it.\n\nThe second reason was that William had to pay an army. His followers had crossed the Channel for reward, and land was the only currency he had in quantity. Land was granted to about two hundred great tenants-in-chief who held directly from the king and owed him a fixed quota of knights, homage and fealty, and who granted parts of their estates to their own knights on the same terms. Landholding and military service became the same thing, so every grant strengthened the army that held the kingdom down.\n\nThe third reason lies outside the bullets: William shaped the settlement to prevent his own men from becoming a danger. Most tenants-in-chief were given estates scattered across many shires rather than one solid block, so no lord could turn a region into a private kingdom; only on the Welsh border, where speed mattered more, were the Marcher earldoms allowed to be compact. The Church was changed the same way, Stigand being deposed in 1070 and replaced by Lanfranc, until by the end of the reign Wulfstan of Worcester was the one English-born bishop left.\n\nThe result was measurable. Domesday Book in 1086 shows barely a twentieth of the land in English hands, and at Salisbury that August the great landholders swore allegiance to William in person, because the whole settlement depended on him.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what William had to pay his followers with, and where that property came from.","Three reasons: why land became available, why it had to be given away, and how William chose to arrange it.","Take one paragraph beyond the bullets by explaining how the estates were distributed, not just that they were."],"codes":["B1-2 — 3 The legacy of resistance to 1087: Changes in landownership from Anglo-Saxon to Norman","B1-2 — 1 Establishing control: Rewarding followers and establishing control on the borderlands"]},{"q":"Explain why the Revolt of the Earls failed in 1075. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • Lanfranc • the Danish fleet. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 / AO1 6. Credit Waltheof's confession, Lanfranc's regency and his excommunication of Roger, the loyal bishops, sheriffs and local levies of Wulfstan and Aethelwig holding the Severn, the defeat of Ralph near Cambridge by Odo of Bayeux and Geoffrey of Coutances and the surrender of Norwich, the late arrival of Cnut's fleet of some 200 ships, and the absence of any English rising after the Harrying. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The first reason was that the plot was betrayed before it could be coordinated. Waltheof, the last great English earl, lost his nerve and confessed to Lanfranc, Archbishop of Canterbury, who was acting as regent while William was in Normandy. Warning turned the rebels' greatest advantage, the king's absence, into a disadvantage, because the government now had time to act and the earls did not know it.\n\nThe second reason was that the machinery of government held without the king. Lanfranc excommunicated Roger de Breteuil and set the loyal bishops and sheriffs in motion. Roger was stopped at the Severn by local levies raised by Wulfstan, Bishop of Worcester, and Aethelwig, Abbot of Evesham, so he never joined Ralph. Ralph de Gael was defeated near Cambridge by Odo of Bayeux and Geoffrey, Bishop of Coutances, and fled abroad, leaving his wife Emma to hold Norwich castle until she was allowed to leave the kingdom on terms. The two risings were beaten separately, which was exactly what a castle-and-sheriff system was built to do.\n\nThe third reason was that the help the earls counted on never mattered. A Danish fleet of some 200 ships under Cnut, son of King Swein, did arrive, but only after the fighting was over; it raided York and sailed home.\n\nThe fourth reason lies beyond the prompts and is the deepest. This was a revolt of two Norman-French earls and one Englishman, and no English rising joined it. After the Harrying of the North and the confiscations that followed the risings of 1068 to 1071, the English had neither leaders nor lands nor any reason to believe rebellion could succeed. Without that support the earls were three lords against a kingdom, and in 1076 Waltheof was beheaded at Winchester while Roger was imprisoned for life.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Think about who was actually in England when the revolt broke out, and what they did.","Three or four reasons, each naming who acted and explaining why the rebels could not overcome them.","One paragraph should ask a question the bullets do not: who did not join the revolt, and why not?"],"codes":["B1-2 — 4 Revolt of the Earls, 1075: The defeat of the revolt and its effects","B1-2 — 4 Revolt of the Earls, 1075: Reasons for and features of the revolt"]},{"q":"'The Harrying of the North in the years 1069 to 1070 was the main reason why Anglo-Saxon resistance to William I came to an end.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • castles • the Danish fleet. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 / AO1 6. Credit the case for the Harrying (three risings in three years ended, the destruction of food, seed and stock between the Humber and the Tees, waste entries in Domesday, no rising north of the Humber again) weighed against castles and garrisons, the buying off and later futility of Danish help, the confiscation of English land and the destruction of the English leadership by 1071, and a criterion-based judgement. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Resistance ends when rebels lose either the will or the means to rise, so the honest test of this statement is which of William's measures took away the means. Judged that way the Harrying was decisive in the north and almost irrelevant elsewhere, and the settlement of land was what finally ended resistance everywhere.\n\nThe case for the statement is strong in its own region. The north had risen three times in three years: with Edwin and Morcar in 1068, in January 1069 when Robert Cumin and his men were killed at Durham, and in the autumn of 1069, when a Danish fleet said to number some 240 ships entered the Humber in the late summer of 1069 and joined Edgar Aetheling, Waltheof and Gospatric to take York in September and destroy both Norman castles. Through the winter that followed, William's men burned villages, crops, seed corn, tools and livestock between the Humber and the Tees, and carried the same destruction into Durham, Cheshire and Staffordshire early in 1070. The target was not the rebels but the capacity to rebel, because a region without seed corn cannot feed an army the following year either. Orderic Vitalis, writing some sixty years later, claimed a hundred thousand died of hunger, and although a hostile chronicler writing that long afterwards cannot be taken as a census, Domesday Book still entered many Yorkshire manors as waste in 1086. No rising began north of the Humber again in William's reign.\n\nAgainst the statement, castles did work the Harrying could not. They were permanent, not a single act of destruction: Pevensey and Hastings in 1066, then Warwick, Nottingham, York, Lincoln, Huntingdon and Cambridge on the march of 1068, each turning a district into a garrisoned zone. The 1068 revolt collapsed without a battle for that reason alone. Castles also held the parts of England the Harrying never touched, and it was ships and a causeway, not fire, that ended Hereward's resistance at Ely in 1071.\n\nThe foreign help the rebels needed also failed on its own account. William bought off the Danish fleet in 1069 to 1070, and when the Danes came again in 1075 under Cnut, son of King Swein, with some 200 ships, they arrived too late, raided York and went home. A rebellion in England depended on outside force arriving in time, and it never did.\n\nBeyond the stimulus lies the measure that outlasted all of these. Every rising transferred more land: Edwin and Morcar lost everything, Morcar being imprisoned after Ely, and by 1086 Domesday shows barely a twentieth of England in English hands, with Wulfstan of Worcester almost the last Englishman of rank left in office. By 1071 there was no English lord with the estates to raise an army, which is why the last revolt of the reign, in 1075, was led by two of William's own earls and attracted no English support at all.\n\nOn the test set at the start, the Harrying was the decisive act in the north and nothing more; it was terrible enough to end resistance in one region but could not have been repeated across a kingdom William wanted to tax. Castles held the ground and the land settlement removed the men who could have used it. The statement is therefore true of Yorkshire and false of England.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide what has to disappear before a rebellion becomes impossible, and judge each measure against that.","Take the Harrying, castles, outside help and the land settlement in turn, each with named evidence and an interim judgement.","Open by naming your criterion and end by applying it; a region-by-region answer will beat a yes or no."],"codes":["B1-2 — 3 The legacy of resistance to 1087: The reasons for and features of Harrying of the North","B1-2 — 2 Anglo-Saxon resistance, 1068–71: Causes and outcomes of Anglo-Saxon resistance","B1-2 — 1 Establishing control: Reasons for the building of castles"]},{"q":"'Castles were the most important way in which William I kept control of England in the years 1066 to 1087.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the Marcher earldoms • rebellion. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 / AO1 6. Credit the case for castles (Pevensey and Hastings 1066, the 1068 chain, the second York castle, motte and bailey speed, garrisons and town demolitions, the Marcher earls' freedom to build) against their failure at York in 1069, and against landholding and knight service, the use of trusted men as regents (Odo, fitzOsbern, Lanfranc) and control of the Church, with a criterion-based judgement. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Control means two different things: holding ground from day to day, and making rebellion not worth attempting. Castles were supreme at the first and could not by themselves achieve the second, so the answer depends on which kind of control is being judged, and on that test the land settlement was the more important measure.\n\nThe case for castles is the strongest part of the statement. A few thousand Normans were holding a population of perhaps two million, and only fortification could put a permanent garrison in every district. William raised castles inside the Roman fort at Pevensey and at Hastings within days of landing in 1066, and on the march of 1068 against Edwin and Morcar he planted them at Warwick, Nottingham, York, Lincoln, Huntingdon and Cambridge. That revolt collapsed without a battle, which is as close to proof as this period offers. A motte and bailey could be raised in weeks by forced English labour, so the reply to a rising could be a castle rather than a campaign, and on the Welsh border the Marcher earls of Hereford, Shrewsbury and Chester were licensed to build without the king's permission precisely because a frontier had to be held by fortification.\n\nAgainst the statement, castles could be taken. In September 1069 the northern rebels with a Danish fleet destroyed both castles at York and their garrisons, and what actually pacified the north afterwards was not building but the Harrying of the winter of 1069 to 1070, whose destruction was still visible in Domesday's waste entries in 1086. At Ely in 1071 the answer was a blockade and a causeway. A castle held ground only while men and supplies reached it.\n\nThe land settlement did the deeper work. Land taken from those who fought at Hastings and from every subsequent rebel was granted to about two hundred great tenants-in-chief in return for knight quotas, homage and fealty, with estates deliberately scattered across the shires so that no lord could build a private kingdom. That is what filled the castles: garrisons existed because knights held land on condition of serving in them. It also destroyed the other side. By 1086 barely a twentieth of England was left in English hands, so there was nobody with the wealth to raise an army against the castles in the first place.\n\nBeyond the stimulus, William also ruled through men rather than walls. Odo of Bayeux and William fitzOsbern governed as regents in 1067, and Lanfranc, made Archbishop of Canterbury in 1070 after Stigand's deposition, held England while the king was in Normandy. In 1075 it was Lanfranc's excommunication of Roger de Breteuil and the levies raised by Wulfstan of Worcester and Aethelwig of Evesham that stopped the Revolt of the Earls, with no king in the country at all.\n\nSo castles were the instrument of control rather than its cause. They were indispensable in the years when the kingdom might have been lost, from 1066 to 1071, and useless without the landholding system that manned them and the trusted men who commanded them. The statement is right about method and wrong about importance: William kept England because he owned it, and the castles were where the owners lived.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what 'most important' is being measured against before you argue for or against.","Weigh castles against landholding, the Harrying and William's use of trusted men, giving each paragraph an interim judgement.","Find the point where castles failed and build a paragraph on it; then decide whether that failure is decisive."],"codes":["B1-2 — 1 Establishing control: Reasons for the building of castles","B1-2 — 1 Establishing control: Rewarding followers and establishing control on the borderlands","B1-2 — 3 The legacy of resistance to 1087: Changes in landownership from Anglo-Saxon to Norman"]},{"q":"'The main reason for the Revolt of the Earls in 1075 was the loss of power suffered by the earls.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the wedding feast • Ralph de Gael. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 / AO1 6. Credit the earls' grievances (Roger de Breteuil's earldom run to rules his father William fitzOsbern never faced, royal sheriffs, Ralph de Gael's marginal position), the wedding feast of 1075 as the occasion, William's absence in Normandy and the expectation of Danish help, Waltheof's ambiguous part, and a judgement distinguishing motive from opportunity. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"A revolt needs both a reason to rise and a belief that rising can succeed. The statement names the reason, and it is right to; but grievance had existed for years without a rising, so the question is what changed in 1075, and the answer is opportunity rather than anger.\n\nThe case for the statement is real. Roger de Breteuil had inherited the earldom of Hereford from his father William fitzOsbern, who had governed it almost as a Marcher lord in his own right and had been William's regent in 1067. Roger found royal sheriffs operating inside his earldom and his freedom of action cut back, so his inheritance was being reduced while he held it. Ralph de Gael, Earl of East Anglia, was a Breton who had been pushed to the margins of a court in which the great Norman families now counted for more. Both men were losing standing they had expected to keep, and that is a motive no other explanation supplies.\n\nThe wedding feast at which Ralph married Emma, Roger's sister, in 1075 was the occasion rather than a cause. William had refused permission for the marriage, which turned a family alliance into an act of defiance, and it put the conspirators in one room; but a feast makes a plot, not a rebellion.\n\nWhat made rebellion look possible was circumstance. William was in Normandy, as he had been for long stretches of the reign, and the earls expected a Danish fleet, which was a reasonable calculation given that Danish ships had taken York in 1069. They also drew in Waltheof, Earl of Northumbria, the last great English earl, whose name might have brought out English support. Remove those three expectations and the grievances would have stayed grievances.\n\nBeyond the stimulus, the strongest evidence for this reading is what happened next. Waltheof lost confidence and confessed to Lanfranc; the English did not rise at all; Roger was stopped at the Severn by levies raised by Wulfstan of Worcester and Aethelwig of Evesham; Ralph was beaten near Cambridge by Odo of Bayeux and Geoffrey of Coutances and fled, leaving Emma to surrender Norwich on terms; the Danish fleet of some 200 ships under Cnut arrived after it was over and raided York on its way home. Every assumption the earls made about their chances proved false, which shows how far the decision to revolt had rested on those assumptions rather than on grievance alone.\n\nThe verdict, on the distinction drawn at the start, is that the loss of power was the necessary cause and the king's absence with the promise of Danish help was the sufficient one. Without the grievance there would have been no plot; without the opportunity the grievance would have produced nothing, as it had done for years. The statement is therefore accurate about why these particular men rebelled and incomplete about why they rebelled in 1075, and the punishment that followed, Roger imprisoned for life, Waltheof beheaded at Winchester in 1076 and neither earldom granted out again, shows what the earls had actually been risking.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Separate what made the earls angry from what made them think they would win.","Take the grievances, the occasion, the circumstances and the outcome in turn, each with named evidence and an interim judgement.","Use what happened after the revolt broke out as evidence about why it was launched."],"codes":["B1-2 — 4 Revolt of the Earls, 1075: Reasons for and features of the revolt","B1-2 — 4 Revolt of the Earls, 1075: The defeat of the revolt and its effects"]},{"q":"'Changes in landownership were the most important consequence of Anglo-Saxon resistance to Norman rule in the years 1066 to 1087.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • forfeiture • the Harrying of the North. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 / AO1 6. Credit the transfer of land through successive forfeitures (Hastings, 1068, 1069, Ely 1071, 1075) to about two hundred great tenants-in-chief and the near-disappearance of English landholders by Domesday, weighed against the human and economic cost of the Harrying, the loss of an English aristocracy and Church, the spread of castles, and the hardening of William's rule; judgement on a stated criterion. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The most important consequence is the one that changed England for longest and made the others possible, and by that test the land settlement wins: the Harrying was the most terrible consequence of resistance, but the transfer of land was the most important, because it removed the class that could have resisted at all.\n\nResistance drove the transfer at every stage. William claimed the throne as Edward's lawful heir, so those who fought for Harold at Hastings were traitors whose estates were forfeit. The revolt of Edwin and Morcar in 1068 and their fate afterwards, Edwin killed by his own men in 1071 and Morcar imprisoned for life after Ely, put two of the greatest English earldoms into Norman hands. The northern risings of 1069 produced confiscations across Yorkshire, and in 1075 Roger de Breteuil and Ralph de Gael forfeited Hereford and East Anglia, neither of which was granted out again. By 1086 Domesday Book shows about two hundred great tenants-in-chief holding the bulk of England from the king in return for knight service, with barely a twentieth of the land left in English hands.\n\nThe Harrying of the North has the better claim to be the worst consequence. Through the winter of 1069 to 1070 villages, crops, seed corn, tools and livestock were destroyed between the Humber and the Tees and on into Durham, Cheshire and Staffordshire. Orderic Vitalis, writing some sixty years later, claimed a hundred thousand died of hunger; Domesday still recorded many Yorkshire manors as waste in 1086. But its reach was regional, and its purpose was to make the north governable, which is to say it served the same settlement.\n\nA third consequence was the disappearance of an entire governing class, lay and ecclesiastical. Stigand was deposed in 1070 and Lanfranc brought from Caen to Canterbury; by the end of the reign Wulfstan of Worcester was the one English-born bishop left in office, and after 1075 the great earldoms were not refilled. Resistance thus destroyed the very men who had led it, and William was able to rule through regents such as Odo of Bayeux, William fitzOsbern and Lanfranc rather than through English earls.\n\nBeyond the stimulus, resistance also changed the shape of the country physically and administratively. The castles that answered each rising, from Warwick and Nottingham in 1068 to the second castle at York in 1069, became the permanent framework of Norman lordship, and the same fear of invasion and rebellion lies behind the Domesday survey of 1086 and the oath the landholders swore to William at Salisbury that August.\n\nJudged on the criterion set at the start, the change in landownership is the right answer, because every other consequence flowed from it or served it: the Harrying made the north safe for a new landholding class, the castles were manned by knights who held land on condition of serving in them, and the Salisbury oath of 1086 was the settlement's own confirmation. The Harrying was worse; the land transfer mattered more.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first what makes one consequence more important than another, and say so in the opening.","Weigh the land settlement against the Harrying, the loss of an English governing class and the spread of castles, judging each in turn.","Ask which consequences depended on which; that dependence is the argument you should be making."],"codes":["B1-2 — 3 The legacy of resistance to 1087: Changes in landownership from Anglo-Saxon to Norman","B1-2 — 3 The legacy of resistance to 1087: The reasons for and features of Harrying of the North","B1-2 — 2 Anglo-Saxon resistance, 1068–71: Causes and outcomes of Anglo-Saxon resistance"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Who crowned William I, and on what day?","a":"Ealdred, Archbishop of York, at Westminster Abbey on Christmas Day, 25 December 1066."},{"q":"Name the three Marcher earldoms created on the Welsh border.","a":"Hereford, Shrewsbury and Chester."},{"q":"In which winter did William harry the north?","a":"The winter of 1069 to 1070."},{"q":"Which earl was executed at Winchester in 1076 for his part in the Revolt of the Earls?","a":"Waltheof, Earl of Northumbria, the only English earl William put to death."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The submission at Berkhamsted, 1066","back":"After William ravaged the south-east and crossed the Thames at Wallingford, Edgar Aetheling, Archbishop Ealdred of York, Earls Edwin and Morcar and the leading men of London submitted to him at Berkhamsted.","code":"B1-2 — 1 Establishing control: The submission of the earls, 1066","open":false},{"front":"William's coronation, 25 December 1066","back":"Crowned at Westminster Abbey by Ealdred, Archbishop of York, not by Stigand, whose position the papacy questioned. He swore to rule the English as well as the best kings before him.","code":"B1-2 — 1 Establishing control: The submission of the earls, 1066","open":false},{"front":"Odo of Bayeux","back":"William's half-brother, Bishop of Bayeux, made Earl of Kent and the greatest lay landholder after the king. Regent with William fitzOsbern in 1067; helped defeat Ralph de Gael in 1075; imprisoned by William in 1082.","code":"B1-2 — 1 Establishing control: Rewarding followers and establishing control on the borderlands","open":false},{"front":"William fitzOsbern","back":"William's closest companion, made Earl of Hereford and regent with Odo in 1067. He built castles on the Welsh border and was killed fighting in Flanders in 1071; his son Roger de Breteuil revolted in 1075.","code":"B1-2 — 1 Establishing control: Rewarding followers and establishing control on the borderlands","open":false},{"front":"One privilege a Marcher earl held that other Norman lords did not","back":"Building castles without the king's licence; keeping land conquered from the Welsh; holding his estates free of the geld.","code":"B1-2 — 1 Establishing control: Rewarding followers and establishing control on the borderlands","open":true},{"front":"Tenant-in-chief and knight service","back":"A lord holding land directly from the king in return for a fixed quota of knights, homage and fealty. Fewer than two hundred held most of England, usually in estates scattered across many shires.","code":"B1-2 — 3 The legacy of resistance to 1087: Changes in landownership from Anglo-Saxon to Norman","open":false},{"front":"Motte and bailey castle","back":"An earth mound with a timber tower on top and an enclosed yard below holding hall, stables and stores, ringed by ditch and palisade. Raised in weeks by forced English labour; the greatest were later rebuilt in stone.","code":"B1-2 — 1 Establishing control: Reasons for the building of castles","open":false},{"front":"The castles of the 1068 march","back":"Marching against Edwin and Morcar, William raised castles at Warwick, Nottingham, York, Lincoln, Huntingdon and Cambridge, and the revolt collapsed without a battle.","code":"B1-2 — 1 Establishing control: Reasons for the building of castles","open":false},{"front":"The revolt of Edwin and Morcar, 1068","back":"Caused by the broken promise of a marriage for Edwin, heavy taxation, Norman grants inside their earldoms and their removal to Normandy in 1067. It collapsed when William marched north building castles; both were pardoned.","code":"B1-2 — 2 Anglo-Saxon resistance, 1068–71: Causes and outcomes of Anglo-Saxon resistance","open":false},{"front":"Edgar Aetheling","back":"Great-nephew of Edward the Confessor, elected king by the Witan after Hastings but too young and landless to hold power. He submitted at Berkhamsted, joined the risings of 1068 and 1069 and fled to Scotland.","code":"B1-2 — 2 Anglo-Saxon resistance, 1068–71: Causes and outcomes of Anglo-Saxon resistance","open":false},{"front":"The risings in the north, 1069","back":"Robert Cumin and his men were killed at Durham in January; in August a Danish fleet of some 240 ships entered the Humber and joined Edgar Aetheling, Waltheof and Gospatric, who took York in September and destroyed both Norman castles.","code":"B1-2 — 2 Anglo-Saxon resistance, 1068–71: Causes and outcomes of Anglo-Saxon resistance","open":false},{"front":"Hereward the Wake and Ely, 1070 to 1071","back":"A Lincolnshire thegn who plundered Peterborough Abbey with Danish help in 1070 and held the Isle of Ely in the fens. Morcar joined him in 1071; William's ships and causeway forced surrender, Morcar was imprisoned and Hereward escaped.","code":"B1-2 — 2 Anglo-Saxon resistance, 1068–71: Causes and outcomes of Anglo-Saxon resistance","open":false},{"front":"The Harrying of the North, 1069 to 1070","back":"Villages, crops, seed corn, tools and livestock burned between the Humber and the Tees and on into Durham, Cheshire and Staffordshire. Orderic Vitalis later claimed a hundred thousand died of hunger; Domesday still recorded Yorkshire manors as waste in 1086.","code":"B1-2 — 3 The legacy of resistance to 1087: The reasons for and features of Harrying of the North","open":false},{"front":"Lanfranc","back":"Abbot of Saint-Etienne at Caen, made Archbishop of Canterbury in 1070 after Stigand's deposition. He governed England as regent in William's absences and excommunicated Roger de Breteuil during the revolt of 1075.","code":"B1-2 — 4 Revolt of the Earls, 1075: The defeat of the revolt and its effects","open":false},{"front":"The Revolt of the Earls, 1075","back":"Ralph de Gael of East Anglia, Roger de Breteuil of Hereford and Waltheof of Northumbria plotted at a wedding feast while William was in Normandy. Waltheof confessed to Lanfranc; Roger was imprisoned for life and Waltheof beheaded in 1076.","code":"B1-2 — 4 Revolt of the Earls, 1075: Reasons for and features of the revolt","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (booklet B, Question 3 or 4, the 16-mark judgement)","items":["Question: 'By 1075 the English had come to accept Norman rule.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] You may use the following in your answer: • Waltheof • Wulfstan of Worcester. You must also use information of your own. What follows is one paragraph from the middle of the answer, putting the case against the statement.","Quiet is not the same as consent, and what happened to the English between 1068 and 1075 explains the silence better than acceptance does. By the time the earls made their plot, there was no English leader left who could have joined them: Edwin was killed by his own men in 1071 while trying to reach Scotland, Morcar went to prison for life after Ely in the same year, and Edgar Aetheling, whom the Witan had chosen as king in 1066 and never crowned, submitted in 1074 and never raised another army. Land had gone the same way, so that by the time of Domesday Book in 1086 barely a twentieth of England was still in English hands and Wulfstan of Worcester was the one English-born bishop still in office. Waltheof's fate reads less like acceptance than like a last demonstration of what refusal cost: he went into the plot, lost his nerve, confessed it to Lanfranc, and was beheaded at Winchester in 1076 all the same, the only English earl William put to death. It is true that no English rising joined the revolt, and that Wulfstan himself raised the levies that stopped Roger de Breteuil at the Severn; but a man may serve a king because he has judged that nothing else is left to him, and the absence of rebellion after 1075 measures above all the absence of anyone able to lead one.","Why this is Level 4: the explanation is directed consistently at the conceptual focus, which is what the absence of rebellion is evidence of, rather than a narrative of what the English did, and the line of reasoning runs from the opening distinction through the evidence to the interim judgement. The information is precisely selected and made to work, since Edwin's death in 1071, Morcar's imprisonment, Edgar's submission in 1074, the proportion of land left in English hands, Wulfstan's levies and Waltheof's execution each carry a step of the argument. The criterion for judgement is stated and applied, namely whether quiet shows consent or incapacity, and the paragraph weighs rather than asserts, conceding the strongest point on the other side — that Englishmen fought for the king in 1075 — and answering it. It also goes beyond both stimulus bullets, using Edwin and Morcar, Edgar Aetheling and Domesday Book, so the caps on answers that do not address three or more aspects of content do not bite."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same 16-mark judgement, and what it is missing)","items":["Question: 'By 1075 the English had come to accept Norman rule.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] The same question, answered at Level 2. Read it beside the Level 4 paragraph on the same question and look for what is missing rather than for what is wrong.","By 1075 the English had accepted Norman rule because they had got used to it and they knew that they could not win. Waltheof was an English earl but he told Lanfranc about the other earls, which shows that the English were on William's side by then. Wulfstan of Worcester was English as well and he stayed a bishop and helped the king. The English did not join in the revolt because they did not want any more trouble after everything that had happened to them. So the English had accepted Norman rule by 1075 because they had stopped fighting.","Why this is only Level 2: it meets the descriptor 'an explanation is given showing limited analysis and with implicit or unsustained links to the conceptual focus of the question', because Waltheof and Wulfstan are named and a link is gestured at in 'they knew that they could not win', but no link is traced. It misses the higher descriptors in two ways. First, the information is generalised rather than 'accurate and relevant information precisely selected to address the question directly': no date is given but the one in the question, 'everything that had happened to them' stands in for the Harrying, the forfeitures and the imprisonments, and Waltheof's confession is reported without his execution, which is the fact that tells against the reading put on it. Second, 'the overall judgement is given but its justification is asserted or insecure', because the last sentence repeats the statement with 'because they had stopped fighting' instead of asking what stopping proves, and nothing is weighed against acceptance at all. The paragraph also adds nothing beyond the two stimulus bullets. That costs it nothing within Level 2, because no question carries a cap at Level 2 and an answer confined to the bullets can still reach the top of that level, but it is what keeps the answer out of the levels above: on the 16-mark question there is a maximum of 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for them."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: establishing control, October 1066 to 1067","items":["14 October 1066: Harold Godwinson and his brothers Gyrth and Leofwine are killed at Hastings, leaving England without a leader who commanded an army.","The Witan in London elects Edgar Aetheling, great-nephew of Edward the Confessor, but he has no lands or following of his own and is never crowned.","William does not march directly on London: he takes Dover and Canterbury, then moves west and south of the city, ravaging the countryside as he goes.","Winchester, with the old royal treasury, surrenders; at Wallingford, where William crosses the Thames, Stigand, Archbishop of Canterbury, submits.","At Berkhamsted, Edgar Aetheling, Ealdred, Archbishop of York, Earls Edwin and Morcar and the leading men of London submit together.","25 December 1066: William is crowned in Westminster Abbey by Ealdred, Archbishop of York. Stigand does not perform the ceremony because the papacy questioned his position.","At the coronation William swears to rule the English as well as the best kings before him; Norman writers record that his guards outside, mistaking the shouts of acclamation for a riot, set fire to nearby buildings.","March 1067: William returns to Normandy, taking Edgar Aetheling, Stigand, Edwin and Morcar with him as honoured hostages, and leaves Odo of Bayeux, Earl of Kent, and William fitzOsbern, Earl of Hereford, as regents.","Spring 1068: William besieges Exeter, where Gytha, Harold's mother, had taken refuge; the city surrenders after a siege of some eighteen days and a castle is built there.","The method throughout: waste the districts that resist, spare those that submit, take the crown only when it is offered, and present conquest as lawful succession."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: rewarding the followers, landholding and the Marcher earldoms, 1066 to 1087","items":["William claimed the throne as Edward's lawful heir, so those who fought for Harold at Hastings were treated as traitors and their land was forfeit.","Land was granted to about two hundred great tenants-in-chief, who held directly from the king in return for a fixed quota of knights, homage and fealty, and who granted parts of their estates to their own knights.","Most estates were deliberately scattered across many shires, so that no lord could turn a compact region into a private power base.","Odo of Bayeux, William's half-brother, was made Earl of Kent and became the greatest lay landholder after the king; he was imprisoned by William in 1082.","William fitzOsbern, made Earl of Hereford, was regent with Odo in 1067 and was killed fighting in Flanders in 1071.","The three Marcher earldoms on the Welsh border were Hereford (William fitzOsbern), Shrewsbury (Roger de Montgomery) and Chester (Hugh of Avranches).","Marcher earls held compact lands and unusual powers: they could raise castles without the king's licence, keep land taken from the Welsh, hold their estates free of the geld and run their own courts.","The Church followed the land: Stigand was deposed in 1070 and Lanfranc, abbot of Saint-Etienne at Caen, became Archbishop of Canterbury; by the end of the reign Wulfstan of Worcester was the one English-born bishop still in office.","Domesday Book, ordered at Christmas 1085 and compiled in 1086, shows barely a twentieth of the land left in English hands.","August 1086: at Salisbury the great landholders of the kingdom swear allegiance to William in person, binding them to the king above their own lords.","William died on 9 September 1087, having held England for more than twenty years after his coronation."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: castles, 1066 to 1087","items":["A motte and bailey could be raised in weeks by forced English labour: an earth mound with a timber tower, and a walled yard below with hall, stables, stores and workshops, all ringed by ditch and palisade.","William's first castles in England were raised inside the Roman fort at Pevensey and at Hastings within days of landing in 1066.","On the march against Edwin and Morcar in 1068 he built castles at Warwick, Nottingham, York, Lincoln, Huntingdon and Cambridge; the revolt collapsed without a battle.","A second castle was built at York in 1069 after the killing of Robert Cumin at Durham; both York castles were destroyed by the rebels and the Danes that September.","Castles were bases from which knights rode out, refuges during a rising, stores for supplies, and the centre of a lord's local government and courts.","Where castles were built in towns, English houses were pulled down to clear the ground, which Domesday Book records.","On the Welsh border the Marcher earls could build castles without royal licence, because the frontier had to be held by fortification rather than by campaigning.","The greatest castles were later rebuilt in stone, the White Tower in London among them.","Castles answered the central problem of the Conquest: a few thousand Normans holding down a population of perhaps two million."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Anglo-Saxon resistance, 1068 to 1071","items":["1068: Edwin and Morcar rebel. Causes include the marriage William promised Edwin and never arranged, the heavy geld, Norman grants inside their earldoms, the new earldom of Hereford, and their removal to Normandy in 1067.","The 1068 revolt collapsed without a battle when William marched north raising castles at Warwick and Nottingham; the brothers were pardoned, and Edgar Aetheling fled to Scotland.","January 1069: Robert Cumin, newly appointed to Northumbria, is killed with his men at Durham; the rebels then attack York and William marches north and builds a second castle there.","August 1069: a Danish fleet said to number some 240 ships, sent by King Swein, enters the Humber and joins Edgar Aetheling, Earl Waltheof, Gospatric and Maerleswein; in September York is taken and both castles destroyed with their garrisons.","William retook York, bought off the Danish fleet over the winter and then harried the north.","1070: Hereward, a Lincolnshire thegn, plunders Peterborough Abbey with Danish help as a Norman abbot is about to take over.","1070 to 1071: the rebels hold the Isle of Ely in the fens, reachable only by water or narrow paths; Morcar and Aethelwine, Bishop of Durham, join them.","William blockaded Ely with ships and built a causeway across the marsh; the rebels surrendered in 1071, Morcar was imprisoned for life, and Hereward escaped and disappears from the record.","Edwin was killed by his own men in 1071 while trying to reach Scotland.","Malcolm III of Scotland, who had married Edgar's sister Margaret, made peace with William at Abernethy in 1072; Edgar himself submitted in 1074.","The pattern of failure: local grievances, no single leader, no coordination between risings, and a king who could always concentrate force first."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Harrying of the North, 1069 to 1070, and its legacy","items":["Reasons: the north had risen three times in three years, it was the route by which Danish and Scottish help arrived, and a wasted country could not supply a rebel or an invading army.","Through the winter of 1069 to 1070 William's men burned villages, crops, seed corn, tools and livestock between the Humber and the Tees; William kept Christmas 1069 at York.","Early in 1070 the same destruction was carried into Durham and across the Pennines into Cheshire and Staffordshire.","Destroying seed corn and livestock destroyed the following year's harvest as well as that year's food, so the region could not support an army for years afterwards.","Orderic Vitalis, a Norman monk writing some sixty years later, claimed that a hundred thousand people died of hunger and condemned William for the deed; the figure cannot be verified.","Domesday Book in 1086 still entered many Yorkshire manors as waste, which is administrative evidence of long-term damage.","No rebellion began north of the Humber again during William's reign, and the Danish fleet that came in 1075 found no rising to join.","The cost was William's own: a devastated region paid little tax and produced little service, so the policy that secured the north also impoverished it."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Revolt of the Earls, 1075, and its defeat","items":["The three rebels: Ralph de Gael, Earl of East Anglia, a Breton; Roger de Breteuil, Earl of Hereford, son of William fitzOsbern; and Waltheof, Earl of Northumbria, the last great English earl, who had married William's niece Judith.","The plot was made in 1075 at the wedding feast at which Ralph married Emma, Roger's sister, a marriage William had refused to permit.","Causes: Roger's earldom was being run to rules his father had never faced, with royal sheriffs operating inside it; Ralph had lost influence at court; William was in Normandy; and Danish help was expected.","Waltheof lost his nerve and confessed the plot to Lanfranc, Archbishop of Canterbury, who was acting as regent.","Lanfranc excommunicated Roger and set the loyal bishops and sheriffs in motion; no English rising joined the revolt.","Roger was stopped at the Severn by levies raised by Wulfstan, Bishop of Worcester, and Aethelwig, Abbot of Evesham, so the two rebel forces never joined.","Ralph was defeated near Cambridge by Odo of Bayeux and Geoffrey, Bishop of Coutances, and fled abroad; captured rebels were mutilated as an example.","Emma held Norwich castle through a siege and was allowed to leave the kingdom on terms.","A Danish fleet of some 200 ships under Cnut, son of King Swein, arrived too late, raided York and sailed home.","Punishments: Roger was imprisoned for life and forfeited his earldom; Waltheof was beheaded at Winchester in 1076, the only English earl William put to death.","Effects: Hereford and East Anglia were not granted out again, government by sheriffs and castles replaced government by great earls, and no rebellion followed in England before William's death in 1087."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — the 16-mark judgement: 'William I's treatment of the English nobility was the main reason for Anglo-Saxon resistance in the years 1068 to 1071.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.","items":["The question as printed, as Question 3 or Question 4 of booklet B: 'William I's treatment of the English nobility was the main reason for Anglo-Saxon resistance in the years 1068 to 1071.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] You may use the following in your answer: • Gytha • Gospatric. You must also use information of your own. There are no spelling, punctuation and grammar marks on this paper, so every minute goes on the argument itself.","Decode it. The concept is causation, and 'main reason' means you must rank, not list. The criterion to announce in the opening and apply at the end is this: a cause counts for more the less likely the risings are without it, so test each factor by asking whether removing it would have kept these men at home. Name the rivals you will weigh — the treatment of the great English families, the burden laid on everyone below them, the survival of a rival claimant with friends abroad, and the Normanising of the Church — so the line of reasoning is visible from the first sentence.","Opening, three or four sentences: state the criterion, name the factors to be weighed, and signal the position, which is that the treatment of the nobility supplied the leaders while the hope of outside help supplied the moment.","Paragraph 1, for the statement. Edwin was promised one of William's daughters and never got her; both brothers were carried to Normandy in 1067 and came back to earldoms into which Normans were being granted land, with a new earldom of Hereford created on their flank; Gospatric had to buy the earldom of Northumbria from the king and lost it when he joined the risings; Copsi, given that same earldom in 1067, was killed within about five weeks. Interim judgement: every rising of these years was led by a man whose standing William had taken, shrunk or sold to him.","Paragraph 2, weighing the evidence for that claim. The marriage grievance comes from writers who already knew how Edwin ended, so it explains more than it proves; what is not in doubt is the record of what these men held before 1066 and what they held after it. Interim judgement: the treatment of the nobility is better evidenced by the land than by the chroniclers' stories.","Paragraph 3, against the statement — the burden on everyone else. A heavy geld was levied after the Conquest and fell on the earls' own tenants; castles were raised inside towns with English houses pulled down to clear the ground, which Domesday Book records; and the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle complains that the men left to govern in 1067 built castles far and wide and harassed the people. Interim judgement: a rising needed followers as well as leaders, and the followers had grievances of their own.","Paragraph 4, against the statement — the dynastic cause and help from outside. Edgar Aetheling had been chosen king by the Witan in 1066 and took refuge with Malcolm III, who married his sister Margaret; Gytha, Harold's mother, held Exeter against William in 1068 while Harold's sons raided the south-west from Ireland; and the fleet that entered the Humber in the late summer of 1069 turned a local rising into a war. Interim judgement: the rebels needed somebody to rise for and somebody to rise with, and in 1069 they had both.","Paragraph 5, the point beyond the stimulus bullets, and the one that explains the last rising of all: the Normanising of the Church. Stigand was deposed in 1070 and Lanfranc brought in over him, English abbots gave way to Normans, and the rebellion in the fens opened with Hereward and a Danish force stripping Peterborough Abbey in 1070 as its Norman abbot was about to take over. Interim judgement: for men with no earldom left to lose, a foreign abbot arriving at the local church was the Conquest made visible.","Without a paragraph of this kind the answer cannot reach the top level however well it is written, because the printed caps hold answers that do not address three or more aspects of content to a maximum of 11 marks and allow them no access to Level 4.","The evaluation thread: return in every paragraph to the same test — would this rising have happened if this grievance had been met? Run it all the way through so that the conclusion is the last step of the argument rather than a new claim.","The conclusion must rank and qualify. Argue that the treatment of the English nobility is the one cause present in every rising of these years, because it made the leaders; but that the leaders moved only when there was a claimant to rally to and a fleet to hope for, and that the men who followed them were answering grievances of their own. End by saying that the statement names the necessary cause and mistakes it for the whole of it.","Timing: about 25 minutes. Roughly five to plan the paragraphs and write the criterion sentence, eighteen to write, and two to check that the last paragraph answers 'how far' rather than 'what happened'."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:23:08Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Judged afresh from the kit file after the dedupe retarget: the five worked examples, the matched Level 4 and Level 2 pair on “By 1075 the English had come to accept Norman rule” and the plan on “William I’s treatment of the English nobility” now set seven questions that appear nowhere in the fourteen-question bank, share no focus and no stimulus point with it, and none reproduces the Paper 2 specimen booklet B1 (Domesday features; why the Marcher earldoms were established; Normanisation and the Church; the same methods in the North and at Ely), which the bank’s own 16-markers also avoid; node dupscan.js EDX-1HI0 B1-2 prints “no duplication”. I re-solved all fourteen questions and checked roughly seventy dates, names and figures, verifying every new fact on the web — Exeter 1068 (eighteen days, Gytha’s escape, no plundering and tax at the pre-Conquest level, Rougemont under Baldwin fitzGilbert, the fyrd called out), the regency of March to December 1067 with Odo and fitzOsbern, the hostages and the Chronicle’s “castles far and wide”, Eustace of Boulogne repulsed at Dover in the autumn of 1067, Copsi killed after five weeks in 1067 and Osulf dead that autumn, Gospatric’s purchase of Northumbria, his terms in 1070 and his loss of it in 1072, Peterborough in 1070 with Turold coming in and the Ely blockade and causeway of 1071 with Morcar imprisoned and Hereward away, Harold’s sons raiding the south-west from Ireland in 1068, Durham without a royal castle until 1072, Waltheof beheaded at Winchester on 31 May 1076 as the only English earl executed, Edgar’s submission in 1074 and Abernethy in 1072, Cnut’s 200 ships arriving too late in 1075 — and found no wrong date, figure, event or attribution. Tariffs, AO splits, the 2 + 2 opening parts, the 1 hour 50 minute paper and all thirteen cap statements match levels.md in its post-2024 and post-2025 form, and both faults in the previous verdict are genuinely fixed (lesson.why now names 1(a) and 1(b), and “fewer than two hundred tenants-in-chief” is hedged everywhere it was unhedged). Publishable; the two residues below are a cue that outruns its setup and one month that would be better given as “late summer”."}}},"B1-3":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"B1-3","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This key topic is the settled England the Conquest produced, and booklet B asks about it in three ways: one feature each of two separate things in it, why something in it happened, and how far a statement about it can be agreed with. None of those can be answered from the story of 1066. What the questions reward here is a working model of how Norman England was held together — who held land from whom and on what terms, who ran the shires, what the king could tax and count, how the Church was staffed and disciplined, and what happened to all of it when William died. The topic also supplies the examiner's favourite argument, because almost everything in it can be read two ways at once: as Anglo-Saxon machinery kept running by new men, or as a genuinely new order imposed by force. A top answer knows precise evidence on both sides and decides between them on a stated criterion.","idea":[{"h":"Land for service: the feudal pyramid and the threat behind it","t":"William's claim was that he had inherited England lawfully and that all land was held of him, so every estate in the kingdom was a grant and not a possession. Below him came perhaps two hundred tenants-in-chief, barons and bishops who held directly from the king; each of them held by knight service, meaning a fixed quota of knights, the servitium debitum, to be produced for the royal army, normally for about forty days in a year. A tenant-in-chief did homage, kneeling and putting his hands between the king's, and swore fealty, and then subinfeudated: he granted manors to knights on the same terms, so that the knights became his men and owed him service. Peasants at the bottom held their strips from the lord of the manor and paid in labour service, week-work on the lord's own land, which is why the pyramid reached all the way down to the plough. What made it more than a diagram was forfeiture. Because land was a grant, treason took it back: Roger de Breteuil, Earl of Hereford, and Ralph de Gael, Earl of East Anglia, lost everything after the revolt of the earls in 1075, and Earl Waltheof was executed in 1076. A top answer uses feudalism as a control mechanism rather than a social structure, arguing that it tied a man's wealth to his loyalty and gave William an army he did not have to pay for.","code":"B1-3 — 1 The feudal system and the Church: The feudal hierarchy"},{"h":"Stigand, Lanfranc and a Church made Norman","t":"The English Church was rich, ran most of what passed for literacy and administration, and had a weak point at the top: Stigand held Canterbury together with Winchester and had taken his pallium from a pope later judged to be an antipope, which let William present conquest as reform. At a council at Winchester at Easter 1070, held with papal legates, Stigand was deposed and imprisoned, and Lanfranc, an Italian scholar who had been prior of Bec and abbot of Caen, became Archbishop of Canterbury. Lanfranc then did three things an examiner rewards. He settled authority: at Winchester in 1072 the Archbishop of York, Thomas of Bayeux, acknowledged the primacy of Canterbury, so the Church had one head and the king had one man to deal with. He reformed by council, meeting regularly to tighten discipline, to rule against priests marrying in future and to move bishops' seats out of villages into towns, as Selsey's moved to Chichester. And he replaced personnel, so that by the 1080s Wulfstan of Worcester was effectively the last English bishop left. William kept the reform under his own hand: he separated church courts from the hundred court in the mid-1070s, insisted that no pope be recognised and no baron excommunicated without his consent, and in 1080 refused to swear fealty to Pope Gregory VII. A top answer argues that Normanisation and reform were the same policy, because a reformed Church staffed by the king's men was an instrument of government.","code":"B1-3 — 1 The feudal system and the Church: The Church in England"},{"h":"How much really changed for the people who stayed","t":"The Conquest replaced the owners of England almost completely while leaving much of its daily machinery alone. By 1086 barely any Englishman of rank still held land directly from the king; roughly a fifth of the country was in the king's own hands and about a quarter belonged to the Church, and the rest was shared among a few hundred newcomers. Yet the shires, the hundreds, the sheriff, the shire and hundred courts, the geld and the silver penny all carried on, and the villager still ploughed the same strips in the same open fields for a lord with a different name. The changes that did bite came from outside the farming year. Several hundred castles were raised, and in towns such as Lincoln and Norwich rows of houses were pulled down to clear ground for them. The Harrying of the North in 1069 and 1070 left Domesday describing much of Yorkshire as waste twenty years later. The geld was levied hard, including at six shillings on the hide in 1084. Royal forest was created, most famously the New Forest in about 1079, and forest law took land out of ordinary law altogether. Meanwhile slavery, still about a tenth of the people Domesday counted, faded away within two generations. A top answer separates continuity of structure from change of ownership and burden, and says which of the two the question is actually about.","code":"B1-3 — 1 The feudal system and the Church: The extent of change to Anglo-Saxon society and economy"},{"h":"A king who governed England mostly from abroad","t":"William had two countries and spent much of his reign in the wrong one for an English question; after 1072 he was usually in Normandy. His answer was to centralise. He kept a large royal demesne, so he had land and revenue of his own in every part of the kingdom, and he used earls sparingly: earldoms were smaller than the Godwin monsters of Edward's day, mostly confined to the dangerous borders, as with Hugh d'Avranches at Chester and Roger of Montgomery at Shrewsbury, and after Roger de Breteuil and Ralph de Gael rebelled in 1075 the earldoms of Hereford and East Anglia were not renewed. Government in his absence ran through regents rather than through a viceroy with land of his own: Odo of Bayeux and William FitzOsbern in 1067, and later Lanfranc, who governed England in the king's name and whose authority came from office, not from an inheritance he could rebel with. The written order continued and hardened: the Anglo-Saxon writ, a short sealed instruction to a shire, went on being used, but from about 1070 it was written in Latin rather than Old English. A top answer argues that these were deliberately weak instruments in strong hands: William built a government whose agents could be dismissed, which is precisely what an absent king needs.","code":"B1-3 — 2 Norman government: Changes to government after the Conquest"},{"h":"The sheriff, the king's land and the king's woods","t":"The sheriff is where Norman government touched the ground. The office was Anglo-Saxon, but its holders were replaced by Normans and its powers grew. A Norman sheriff collected the geld and the revenues of the royal demesne in his shire, answering for a fixed sum, the farm; he presided in the shire court and enforced its judgements; he raised men for the king; and he was very often the castellan of the royal castle in the shire town, so that his orders had a garrison behind them. That concentration made some sheriffs a problem in themselves, and men like Picot of Cambridgeshire and Urse d'Abetot of Worcestershire were notorious for helping themselves to land, including the Church's. Beside the demesne stood something new: the forest. Forest did not mean woodland but land reserved for the king's hunting and governed by its own law, outside the ordinary courts and administered by the king's own officers. The New Forest, created in about 1079, swallowed villages, and the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle's obituary poem for William complained that he loved the deer as if he were their father and that men who killed a hart were blinded. A top answer treats sheriff, demesne and forest as one argument: the king's authority in the shires ran through land he owned and law he alone controlled, not through nobles he had to trust.","code":"B1-3 — 2 Norman government: The office of sheriff and the demesne"},{"h":"Domesday: a kingdom written down","t":"At his midwinter court at Gloucester at Christmas 1085 William took counsel with his men and ordered a survey of England. Commissioners rode circuits of shires through 1086, taking sworn evidence from shire and hundred juries, and the returns were boiled down into two volumes, the detailed Little Domesday for Essex, Norfolk and Suffolk and the summarised Great Domesday for the rest; London and Winchester were left out, as was most of the far north. For each manor the record says who held it in King Edward's day, who held it now, how many ploughs, villagers, mills and beasts there were, how it was assessed in hides and what it was worth. That shape tells you the purposes. It was a tax document, since hides were what the geld was levied on, and it came directly after the invasion scare of 1085, when Cnut IV of Denmark threatened England and William brought over a mercenary army that had to be paid for. It was also a title deed, settling twenty years of disputed grants, and a feudal register showing which baron held what of the king. The Salisbury oath fits here: on 1 August 1086 the landholding men of England, including the important tenants of other lords, came to Salisbury and swore allegiance to William against all other men. A top answer refuses the single-purpose answer and argues about which purpose the design of the record actually serves.","code":"B1-3 — 2 Norman government: The Domesday survey and Domesday Book"},{"h":"A new ruling class and the language it ruled in","t":"The men who now held England were foreigners who did not stop being foreign. Norman French was the language of the court, the household and the law, Latin replaced Old English as the language of royal writs and charters, and English survived as the speech of the people who had no share in government; the words that entered English later from French for meat, justice and lordship record which side of the door each language lived on. The aristocracy was cross-Channel: most great barons held land in Normandy as well as England, so their interests, their marriages and their burials stayed abroad, and intermarriage with the English was rare at the top. Their culture was the culture of knights and builders — castles first in earth and timber and then in stone, as at the White Tower in London, cathedrals and abbeys rebuilt in the Romanesque style with round arches and massive piers, hunting as the chief recreation, and the naming of sons William, Robert, Henry and Hugh. Land held them together: estates were often scattered across many shires so that no baron controlled a single solid block, except on the Welsh border where the Marcher earls were deliberately given one. A top answer uses culture as evidence of the aristocracy's separateness, and separateness as an explanation of why Norman rule had to rest on castles rather than consent.","code":"B1-3 — 3 The Norman aristocracy: The culture and language of the Norman aristocracy"},{"h":"Odo of Bayeux: the uses and the danger of a half-brother","t":"Odo was William's half-brother, made Bishop of Bayeux as a very young man and given the earldom of Kent in 1067, which put the shortest route from Normandy and the castles of Dover and Rochester in his hands. Domesday shows him as the greatest lay landholder in England after the king. He was one of the regents left in charge when William returned to Normandy in 1067; he fought against rebellion; and after Bishop Walcher of Durham was murdered at Gateshead in 1080 it was Odo who led the punitive expedition that ravaged Northumbria. He is also the likeliest patron of the Bayeux Tapestry, made for his cathedral. Then the same qualities turned against the crown. In 1082 William arrested him, on the account that he was recruiting knights to pursue ambitions of his own in Italy, and kept him in prison at Rouen until the king's deathbed in 1087; William is said to have answered the objection that a bishop could not be tried by saying that he was arresting the Earl of Kent, not the Bishop of Bayeux. Released, Odo led the rebellion of 1088 against William Rufus and lost his English lands for good. A top answer uses Odo to argue that the Conqueror's system depended on kin and reward, and that its weakness was the same thing.","code":"B1-3 — 3 The Norman aristocracy: The career and significance of Bishop Odo"},{"h":"William and Robert: the quarrel that outlived the king","t":"Contemporaries describe William as pious, self-controlled and enormously determined, and also as cold and frightening: the same man who founded abbeys harried the North. He had promised Normandy to his eldest son, Robert, nicknamed Curthose, but he would not hand it over, and Robert by his late twenties had a title, no revenue and no power. The break came at L'Aigle in 1077 when his younger brothers William and Henry emptied water over him and their father refused to punish them; Robert demanded Normandy and Maine, was refused, tried and failed to seize the castle at Rouen, and left. He was taken up by his father's enemies, above all King Philip I of France, who installed him at Gerberoy, and when William besieged him there in January 1079 Robert unhorsed his father and wounded him in the hand before recognising him. Queen Matilda and the nobles patched up a reconciliation in 1080, but after Matilda died in 1083 Robert left again and was still absent when his father died. A top answer treats this as causation rather than family drama: a rule of inheritance that gave sons nothing until their father died made rebellion the only career open to an heir, and left the succession unsettled in 1087.","code":"B1-3 — 4 William I and the succession: Character and personality of William I and his relations with Robert"},{"h":"1087 and 1088: a divided inheritance and the fight over it","t":"Campaigning against King Philip in the Vexin, William burned Mantes in the summer of 1087 and was injured; he died at the priory of St Gervase outside Rouen on 9 September 1087 and was buried at his abbey of St Stephen at Caen. He divided what he held: Normandy, which he had inherited, went to Robert; England, which he had won, went to his second surviving son, William Rufus; the youngest, Henry, got five thousand pounds of silver. Rufus crossed at once with a letter to Lanfranc, secured the treasury at Winchester and was crowned at Westminster on 26 September 1087. The settlement satisfied nobody who held land on both sides of the Channel, because such a man now owed homage to two rival lords. In 1088 Odo, freed on the old king's deathbed, led a rebellion to put Robert on the English throne, joined by Robert of Mortain, Roger of Montgomery, Geoffrey of Coutances and Robert de Mowbray. It failed. Robert never brought an army over; Rufus bought English support with promises of better law, lighter taxation and rights in the forests, called out the English, took Tonbridge, forced Pevensey to surrender after about six weeks and then reduced Rochester, and Odo was exiled and stripped of his English lands. A top answer explains the rebellion as a consequence of the division of the inheritance rather than as an accident.","code":"B1-3 — 4 William I and the succession: William's death and the disputed succession"}],"examples":[{"title":"One feature in each part: the Salisbury oath and the royal forest","setup":"The question is: 1(a) Describe one feature of the Salisbury oath. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the royal forest in Norman England. [2 marks]. What you know about the oath: on 1 August 1086 William I summoned the landholding men of England to Salisbury; those who came were tenants-in-chief and also the more important men who held land from other lords; and they became William's men and swore loyalty to him against all other men. What you know about the forest: it was land reserved for the king's hunting, governed by forest law outside the ordinary courts, and the New Forest in Hampshire was one of William's creations. The two parts are marked separately, and in each the marker awards one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about it.","steps":["Answer 1(a) on its own and open with a plain statement of what the gathering was: an assembly summoned by the king at which great landholders personally became his men and swore loyalty directly to him.","Take the second mark in 1(a) with supporting information about that same feature: those present included not only tenants-in-chief but substantial men who held their land from other lords, so the promise reached below the top rank of landholders.","Stop 1(a) there. Both marks in the part are now earned, so the further supporting point that the loyalty sworn was loyalty against all other men (which the mark scheme also accepts as support) adds nothing here.","Begin 1(b) as a fresh answer about a different aspect of content: forest did not mean woodland but land reserved for the king's hunting and governed by its own law, administered by the king's officers outside the ordinary courts.","Support 1(b) in the same way and stop: the New Forest, created in about 1079, took in settlements and farmland, and the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle complained that a man who killed a hart was blinded. Four marks, two sentences a part, and nothing on why the king wanted either."],"cues":["What single sentence tells the marker what the gathering at Salisbury was, before any detail?","Which detail proves the promise reached below the tenants-in-chief?","You still have a good point about the wording of the oath — why is there no room for it in 1(a)?","1(b) is about something else entirely: what did 'forest' mean, if not woodland?","What one date or example pins the forest feature down?"]},{"title":"Building a reason instead of a fact: why the land changed hands","setup":"The question is: Explain why William I granted most of the land of England to Norman barons. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the English rebellions; knight service. You must also use information of your own. The mark scheme gives 6 marks for analysis and 6 for knowledge, and prints a maximum of 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","steps":["Take the first bullet and turn it into a causal claim: English landholders had shown they would rise against him, so replacing them removed the wealth that paid for revolt.","Attach precise evidence to that claim: the rebellion of Edwin and Morcar in 1068, the risings in the North in 1069 that brought on the Harrying of 1069 and 1070, and Hereward's rising at Ely in 1070 and 1071.","Turn the second bullet into a different causal claim: granting land by knight service bought an army, because each tenant-in-chief owed a fixed quota of knights for about forty days a year without pay from the treasury.","Add a third reason the bullets do not prompt, which is what opens the top level: William had to pay the men who had crossed with him, and land was the only currency he had in quantity, which is why Odo received Kent and Robert of Mortain received Pevensey and Cornwall.","Close by linking the three: confiscation punished, knight service armed and reward paid, so one policy did the work of three, which is why it went as far as it did."],"cues":["What does a rebellion cost a king if the rebels keep their estates?","Which named risings show the danger was real rather than feared?","What did the king get back each time he granted a manor away?","The bullets say nothing about the men who sailed with him — what did they expect?","What single sentence makes the three reasons one argument?"]},{"title":"Causation in a family quarrel: William and Robert, 1077 to 1080","setup":"The question is: Explain why relations between William I and his son Robert broke down in the years 1077 to 1080. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: Normandy; Robert's brothers. You must also use information of your own. What you know: Robert had been promised Normandy but was given no power there; his brothers William and Henry poured water over him at L'Aigle and were not punished; Robert demanded Normandy and Maine, failed to seize the castle at Rouen and went into exile; King Philip I of France set him up at Gerberoy, where his father besieged him and was unhorsed and wounded in the hand; Queen Matilda helped bring about a reconciliation in 1080.","steps":["Start from the structural cause rather than the incident: an heir promised a duchy but given no land, revenue or office had no way of becoming a ruler except by forcing his father's hand.","Use the incident as the trigger it was: the horseplay at L'Aigle mattered because the refusal to punish it told Robert publicly that his father did not take his standing seriously.","Explain the escalation through a cause the bullets do not prompt: Robert's quarrel was useful to his father's enemies, so Philip I gave him Gerberoy and turned a family dispute into a war on the Norman frontier.","Show the chain closing: once father and son had met in arms at Gerberoy in 1079 the breach was public, and it took Matilda and the leading nobles to patch up the settlement of 1080 rather than a change of heart on either side.","Finish by ranking: the incident supplied the occasion, the outsiders supplied the means, but the cause was a rule of inheritance under which a son got nothing while his father lived."],"cues":["What could Robert actually do with a promise and no land?","Why does a quarrel between brothers become a quarrel with the father?","Who else in France stood to gain from the duke's son being at war with him?","What changes once the two have fought each other in the field?","Which of your three causes would still have produced a breach on its own?"]},{"title":"Writing the paragraph against the statement","setup":"The question is: 'The main change the Normans made to English government was the greater power of the sheriff.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the shire court; the geld. You must also use information of your own. Assume you have already written a paragraph agreeing, using the Norman sheriff's control of the royal demesne, the farm of the shire and the royal castle. This example builds the next paragraph, the one that pushes back.","steps":["Name the rival change in the topic sentence: the deeper alteration was that the king now governed an England he was usually absent from, and did it through officers he could dismiss.","Support it with evidence the first paragraph did not use: after 1072 William was mostly in Normandy, Odo of Bayeux and William FitzOsbern acted as regents from 1067 and Lanfranc governed for him later.","Explain why that outranks the sheriff: the sheriff was an Anglo-Saxon office filled with new men, whereas rule by regent and by a shrinking, border-confined earldom was a new distribution of power, confirmed when the earldoms of Hereford and East Anglia went unfilled after the revolt of 1075.","Close with an interim judgement that states the criterion you are using: if the test of change is whether an institution did something it had not done before, the sheriff was strengthened but the shape of central government was remade."],"cues":["What is the biggest single difference between Edward's kingship and William's?","Which named men actually ran England while the king was overseas?","Was the sheriff a new office, or an old office with new holders?","On what test are you deciding which change is the greater?"]},{"title":"Choosing the criterion before choosing the evidence: judging Odo","setup":"The question is: 'Bishop Odo was the most useful of William I's supporters in England.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the earldom of Kent; regency. You must also use information of your own. The evidence available to you: Odo was Earl of Kent from 1067, regent in 1067, the richest lay landholder after the king, and the commander who ravaged Northumbria after Bishop Walcher was murdered in 1080; he was arrested by William in 1082 and imprisoned until 1087; Lanfranc was Archbishop of Canterbury from 1070, reformed the Church, governed England as regent and secured the throne for William Rufus.","steps":["Decide the test first and say it in the opening: usefulness means what a supporter contributed to the king's control of England across the whole reign, not how much he was given.","Apply the test to Odo's strongest period, the years to 1080, when Kent, Dover and Rochester covered the crossing and he could be left in charge or sent to punish the North.","Apply the same test to the rival, Lanfranc, whose reformed Church put royal appointees in every diocese and whose authority rested on an office the king granted rather than on land he could rebel with.","Apply the test to the end of Odo's career, which the bullets do not prompt: arrested in 1082, in prison until the king died, and in rebellion against Rufus in 1088, so the same power made him a danger.","Reach the judgement the criterion produces: measured by wealth and office Odo stands first, but measured by what he was worth to the crown over twenty years the answer is Lanfranc, and say so in one sentence."],"cues":["What would make one supporter more useful than another? Define it now.","Which of Odo's holdings and jobs meet that test most strongly?","Who else could be left to govern, and what did his power rest on?","What happens to your case for Odo after the arrest?","Given your own test, which name has to come first?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What did a tenant-in-chief owe the king in return for his land, and for roughly how long each year?","a":"A fixed quota of knights for the royal army, the servitium debitum, normally for about forty days a year."},{"q":"Which council deposed Stigand, and which one settled Canterbury's primacy over York?","a":"The council at Winchester at Easter 1070 deposed Stigand; the settlement with Thomas of Bayeux of York was made at Winchester in 1072."},{"q":"Name two purposes the Domesday survey served for the king.","a":"Any two of: assessing the geld, since hides were the basis of the tax; settling disputes over who held what after twenty years of grants; recording which baron held what of the king; measuring the resources of the kingdom after the invasion scare of 1085."},{"q":"Why did the way William divided his lands in 1087 make trouble?","a":"Normandy went to Robert and England to William Rufus, so barons with estates on both sides of the Channel owed homage to two rival lords; in 1088 Odo led a rebellion to make Robert king of England as well."}]},"room":{"facts":["Booklet B, the British depth study, carries 32 marks. Question 1 is printed in two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each 'Describe one feature of…' for 2 marks and each on a separate aspect of content; Question 2 is 'Explain why…' (12 marks) with two stimulus bullets; and then either Question 3 or Question 4, a 16-mark 'How far do you agree? Explain your answer.', also with two bullets. There are no spelling, punctuation and grammar marks anywhere on Paper 2.","Question 1(a) and 1(b), 'Describe one feature of…', are point-marked, not levelled: in each part one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about that feature. Reasons, causes and judgements score nothing, and a second feature inside one part earns nothing.","The stimulus caps are printed inside the mark scheme grids, and there is no cap at Level 2 on any question. On Question 2, the 12-mark question: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'. On the 16-mark judgement the same caps read 'Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","AO1 and AO2 are interdependent: an answer displaying no qualities of AO2 cannot be awarded more than the top of Level 1 however strong the knowledge is. The 12-mark question splits AO2 6 and AO1 6; the 16-mark judgement splits AO2 10 and AO1 6; the 16-mark grid has three strands in each level (explanation, information and judgement) and is marked best-fit across the band.","Paper 2 lasts 1 hour 50 minutes for 64 marks, about 55 minutes a booklet, so booklet B's 32 marks get roughly a minute a mark with planning time on top: about 5 minutes on Question 1's two parts, 20 on Question 2 and 28 on the 16-mark judgement, with the planning for the judgement taken out of that 28.","The dates that anchor this key topic: Easter 1070, the Council of Winchester and the deposition of Stigand; 1072, Canterbury's primacy over York settled; 1075, the revolt of the earls; 1077 to 1080, Robert's revolt; 1082, Odo's arrest; Christmas 1085, the survey ordered at Gloucester; 1 August 1086, the Salisbury oath; 9 September 1087, William's death; 26 September 1087, Rufus crowned; 1088, Odo's rebellion."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the feudal hierarchy in Norman England. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the Salisbury oath of 1086. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked in two separate parts: in each part one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information. In 1(a) credit tenants-in-chief holding directly from the king, knight service and the servitium debitum, homage and fealty, subinfeudation to knights, labour service by peasants, or forfeiture. In 1(b) credit the summons of the landholding men to Salisbury on 1 August 1086, the presence of substantial tenants of other lords, the act of becoming the king's man, or the loyalty sworn against all other men.","sol":"1(a) Feature: all land was held of the king. Supporting knowledge: about two hundred tenants-in-chief, barons and bishops, held their estates directly from William, did homage by placing their hands between his and swore fealty to him. 1(b) Feature: the landholding men of England became William's men in person. Supporting knowledge: those summoned to Salisbury on 1 August 1086 included not only tenants-in-chief but substantial men who held their land from other lords, and they swore loyalty to William against all other men.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Two separate parts: 1(a) is about the hierarchy and 1(b) about the oath, so do not let one answer run into the other.","In each part name the feature in one sentence, then add one precise detail; the second mark is for supporting information, not for a second feature.","For 1(a) ask who held from whom and what was owed back; for 1(b) ask who came to Salisbury and what exactly they promised."],"codes":["B1-3 — 1 The feudal system and the Church: The feudal hierarchy","B1-3 — 2 Norman government: The Domesday survey and Domesday Book"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the office of sheriff in Norman England. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the royal forest in Norman England. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked in two separate parts: in each part one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information. In 1(a) credit the replacement of English sheriffs by Normans, collection of the geld and the farm of the shire, management of the royal demesne, presiding in the shire court, raising troops, or custody of the royal castle. In 1(b) credit land reserved for the king's hunting, forest law and the king's own officers outside the ordinary courts, the creation of the New Forest in about 1079, or the loss of rights of pasture, wood and hunting.","sol":"1(a) Feature: the sheriff was the king's financial officer in the shire. Supporting knowledge: he ran the royal demesne there, collected the geld and answered to the king for a fixed annual sum, the farm of the shire. 1(b) Feature: forest was not woodland but land reserved for the king's hunting. Supporting knowledge: it was governed by forest law administered by the king's own officers outside the ordinary courts, and the New Forest, created in about 1079, took in settlements and farmland.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The two parts are marked separately, so write two short answers rather than one general answer about royal power in the shires.","For 1(a) sort what a sheriff did into money, justice and force and take the feature from one box; the second mark is a detail about that same job.","For 1(b) start by saying what 'forest' meant, which is not what it means now, then add one date or example."],"codes":["B1-3 — 2 Norman government: The office of sheriff and the demesne"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the culture of the Norman aristocracy in England after 1066. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the Council of Winchester of 1070. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked in two separate parts: in each part one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information. In 1(a) credit Norman French at court and Latin in written government, cross-Channel landholding and limited intermarriage, castle building, Romanesque churches and cathedrals, hunting, or Norman personal names. In 1(b) credit the presence of papal legates, the deposition and imprisonment of Stigand, the removal of other English prelates, or the way it opened the archbishopric to Lanfranc.","sol":"1(a) Feature: the aristocracy spoke and wrote a different language from the people it ruled. Supporting knowledge: Norman French was used at court, in the household and in law, while royal writs and charters were written in Latin instead of Old English from about 1070. 1(b) Feature: the council deposed the Archbishop of Canterbury. Supporting knowledge: it met at Easter 1070 in the presence of papal legates and removed and imprisoned Stigand, who had held Winchester alongside Canterbury and had taken his pallium from a pope later judged an antipope.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Two separate parts on two separate aspects: the ruling class in 1(a), a single church council in 1(b).","For 1(a) pick one kind of cultural mark — speech, building or pastime — and stay with it for both marks.","For 1(b) say what the council did before you say who was there; the second mark is the detail that shows why it could be done."],"codes":["B1-3 — 3 The Norman aristocracy: The culture and language of the Norman aristocracy","B1-3 — 1 The feudal system and the Church: The Church in England"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the career of Bishop Odo of Bayeux in England. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the division of William I's lands in 1087. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked in two separate parts: in each part one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information. In 1(a) credit the earldom of Kent from 1067, the regency of 1067, his wealth as the greatest lay landholder after the king, the expedition into Northumbria after Walcher's murder in 1080, his arrest in 1082, or the rebellion of 1088. In 1(b) credit Normandy to Robert, England to William Rufus, five thousand pounds of silver to Henry, the distinction between inherited and conquered lands, or the problem this made for barons holding land on both sides of the Channel.","sol":"1(a) Feature: Odo was one of the greatest men in England under his half-brother. Supporting knowledge: he was given the earldom of Kent in 1067, which put Dover and Rochester and the crossing to Normandy in his hands, and Domesday shows him as the richest lay landholder after the king. 1(b) Feature: William separated what he had inherited from what he had won. Supporting knowledge: Normandy, the duchy he had inherited, went to his eldest son Robert, England, which he had conquered, went to his second surviving son William Rufus, and the youngest, Henry, was left five thousand pounds of silver.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Answer the two parts separately: one man's career in 1(a), the settlement of 1087 in 1(b).","For 1(a) choose either the rise or the fall and give the office or the event with its year.","For 1(b) state the principle the division followed, then name who received what."],"codes":["B1-3 — 3 The Norman aristocracy: The career and significance of Bishop Odo","B1-3 — 4 William I and the succession: William's death and the disputed succession"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of village life in England under Norman rule. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the revolt of the earls of 1075. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked in two separate parts: in each part one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information. In 1(a) credit open-field farming and the unchanged farming year, labour service owed to the lord of the manor, the replacement of English lords by Normans, the decline of slavery, the burden of the geld, or land taken into the royal forest. In 1(b) credit the earls who rose, Roger de Breteuil of Hereford and Ralph de Gael of East Anglia, the part played by Earl Waltheof, the appeal for Danish help, the crushing of the revolt, or the forfeitures and the earldoms left unfilled.","sol":"1(a) Feature: the work of the village went on as before. Supporting knowledge: villagers farmed strips in open fields with shared ox teams and owed their lord rent and labour service, the week-work on his own land, exactly as they had before 1066, although the lord's name was now usually Norman. 1(b) Feature: the revolt was made by William's own Norman earls. Supporting knowledge: Roger de Breteuil, Earl of Hereford, and Ralph de Gael, Earl of East Anglia, drew in Earl Waltheof and sought Danish help, and when it was crushed Ralph fled, Roger was imprisoned for life and Waltheof was executed in 1076.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Two parts on two different things: the village in 1(a), a baronial revolt in 1(b), and neither answer helps the other.","For 1(a) choose either what stayed the same in the fields or what new burden came from outside, not both.","For 1(b) name who rebelled before you say what happened to them; the second mark is the detail that pins it down."],"codes":["B1-3 — 1 The feudal system and the Church: The extent of change to Anglo-Saxon society and economy","B1-3 — 2 Norman government: Changes to government after the Conquest"]},{"q":"Explain why William I made little use of earls in governing England. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the revolt of the earls (1075) • the royal demesne. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6, levels-marked. Credit the over-mighty earldoms of Edward's reign, the revolt of 1075 and the suppression of the earldoms of Hereford and East Anglia, forfeiture, the confining of earldoms to the borders, and the alternatives William used instead: sheriffs, the royal demesne, castles and regents. Caps printed in the mark scheme: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"William had watched what a great earldom could do to a king. Under Edward the Confessor the house of Godwin held Wessex, Northumbria, East Anglia and the shires round London at once, with estates worth more than the crown's, and in 1052 the family returned from exile and forced its way back because no one would fight the king's battle against it. An earl who controlled whole provinces could rival the king, so William's policy from the start was to ensure nobody in England held that much.\n\nExperience after 1066 confirmed the fear. The revolt of the earls in 1075 was made by exactly the kind of men William had appointed: Roger de Breteuil, Earl of Hereford, and Ralph de Gael, Earl of East Anglia, who drew in Earl Waltheof and sought Danish help. The rising was crushed, Ralph fled, Roger was imprisoned for life and Waltheof was executed in 1076, and William then simply did not fill the two earldoms again. Forfeiture turned the lesson into a rule: because land was held of the king, rebellion cost a man everything he had, and the vacancies were not replaced.\n\nThe decisive reason, though, is that William had better instruments and did not need earls. Taking the second stimulus point, his government ran through officers rather than through magnates. The sheriff, an Anglo-Saxon office now filled with Normans, collected the geld, managed the royal demesne, presided in the shire court and often held the royal castle, and unlike an earl he could be dismissed. In the king's long absences England was governed by regents such as Odo of Bayeux and William FitzOsbern in 1067 and later Lanfranc, whose authority came from office rather than inheritance. Where earls survived, on the Welsh border at Chester and Shrewsbury, they were kept because the frontier needed a soldier with local power.\n\nThe three fit together. The Godwins showed what an earl could become, 1075 showed it could happen again under Norman earls, and the sheriff and the regent gave William a way of governing that did not require him to take the risk.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what an earl actually controlled, and what that meant for a king who was usually abroad.","Separate the reasons into what William feared, what then happened, and what he used instead; the third is where the top level lives.","Three paragraphs, each opening with the reason in one sentence and closing by linking it back to why earls were sidelined."],"codes":["B1-3 — 2 Norman government: Changes to government after the Conquest"]},{"q":"Explain why William I ordered the Domesday survey in 1085. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the geld • the threat of invasion. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6, levels-marked. Credit the assessment of the geld on hides and the heavy levy of 1084, the Danish invasion scare of 1085 and the cost of the mercenary army, the settling of disputed titles after twenty years of redistribution, the recording of who held what of whom as a feudal register, and the link to the Salisbury oath of 1086. Caps printed in the mark scheme: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"The first reason is money, and specifically the geld. The tax was assessed on hides of land, so the yield depended entirely on how much land was reckoned to exist and who was answerable for it. William had levied the geld hard, at six shillings on the hide in 1084, and twenty years of confiscation and regranting had left the old assessments out of date. A survey that recorded, manor by manor, how many hides a holding was rated at and what it was worth turned a guess into a demand the sheriff could enforce.\n\nThe second reason is the emergency of 1085. Cnut IV of Denmark, allied with Robert of Flanders, threatened invasion, and William brought a large army of paid soldiers across from the continent and quartered it on his tenants. An army of that kind had to be fed and paid, and William needed to know which lords could carry the cost. It is no accident that he ordered the survey at Gloucester at Christmas 1085, immediately after that crisis, or that the commissioners took evidence on oath so nobody could understate what he held.\n\nThe third reason goes beyond the bullets and explains the survey's peculiar shape. Domesday does not simply list resources; for every manor it records who held it in King Edward's day, who holds it now and of whom. That is the design of a title register and a feudal record, not just a tax roll. After two decades of grants made in a hurry, barons were quarrelling over estates and with the Church, and the sworn returns gave the king a document that settled such disputes on his terms. It also showed him exactly what each tenant-in-chief held of him, and therefore what service was owed. The Salisbury oath of 1 August 1086, at which the landholding men of England swore loyalty to William against all other men, completed the same work.\n\nSo the survey answered three questions at once: what the kingdom could be taxed, what it could be defended with, and who held it of whom.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Start from what the record actually contains, because the contents tell you what it was for.","Give one reason to the money, one to the emergency of the mid-1080s, and one to the questions about landholding the record was built to answer.","Open each paragraph with the purpose in a single sentence, then prove it from something the survey records or from what had just happened."],"codes":["B1-3 — 2 Norman government: The Domesday survey and Domesday Book"]},{"q":"Explain why William I replaced English bishops and abbots with Normans. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • Stigand • Lanfranc. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6, levels-marked. Credit Stigand's pluralism and irregular pallium as a pretext, the papal legates and the Council of Winchester of 1070, Lanfranc's appointment and reforming councils, the wealth and administrative role of the Church, bishops' places in government and the shire court, rebellion by English churchmen, and William's insistence on royal control of appointments. Caps printed in the mark scheme: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"The first reason is that the English Church gave William a respectable excuse. Stigand had held Canterbury since 1052 while keeping the bishopric of Winchester as well, and had received his pallium from a pope later judged to be an antipope, so his position was indefensible in the eyes of the reforming papacy. That let William present removal as reform rather than conquest. At a council held at Winchester at Easter 1070 in the presence of papal legates, Stigand was deposed and imprisoned, and other English bishops and abbots were removed with him. The same argument justified everything that followed.\n\nThe second reason is that the Church was an instrument of government and William needed it in reliable hands. Bishops and abbots were among the greatest landholders in the country and owed knight service for their estates; they sat in the king's council and in the shire court; and the clergy were the men who could write. Lanfranc, made Archbishop of Canterbury in 1070, was exactly the kind of servant this required. He settled the primacy of Canterbury over York with Thomas of Bayeux at Winchester in 1072, so authority ran down a single line to the king's own appointee, and he ran England as regent during William's absences. By the 1080s Wulfstan of Worcester was effectively the last English bishop left.\n\nThe third reason, which the bullets do not prompt, is security. English churchmen had joined resistance: Bishop Æthelwine of Durham went over to the rebels and died in prison, and Ely, where Hereward's rising was based in 1070 and 1071, was an abbey. A bishop commanded tenants, castles and money, and an English one commanded the loyalty of English tenants. Replacing him removed a possible rallying point at no cost to the king's revenue, since the see's lands went on serving the crown.\n\nReform supplied the justification, government supplied the need, and rebellion supplied the urgency, which is why the change was so nearly total.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what a bishop was, in this period, besides a churchman.","Look for one reason that made the change respectable, one that made it useful and one that made it urgent.","Each paragraph should end by saying what that reason made possible for the king, not by describing the churchman."],"codes":["B1-3 — 1 The feudal system and the Church: The Church in England"]},{"q":"Explain why relations between William I and his son Robert broke down in the years 1077–80. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • Normandy • Robert's brothers. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6, levels-marked. Credit the promise of Normandy without any grant of power or revenue, the incident at L'Aigle in 1077 and William's refusal to punish William Rufus and Henry, Robert's failed attempt on Rouen and his exile, the encouragement of Philip I of France and the siege of Gerberoy in 1079, William's character, and the reconciliation brought about by Matilda in 1080. Caps printed in the mark scheme: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"The underlying cause was a promise that was never converted into power. William had named Robert as his heir to Normandy, and Robert had been recognised in Maine, but his father would give him neither land of his own nor revenue nor real authority. By the late 1070s Robert was a grown man with a title, a following of young nobles who expected him to reward them, and nothing to reward them with. In a system where an heir got nothing while his father lived, the only way to become a ruler was to force his father's hand.\n\nThe trigger was the quarrel at L'Aigle in 1077, when his younger brothers William and Henry emptied water over him from an upper room. The incident matters not because it was serious but because of what followed: their father refused to punish them. In public that told Robert and his companions that the heir to Normandy could be humiliated by his juniors without redress. Robert demanded Normandy and Maine at once, was refused, attempted to seize the castle at Rouen and, failing, left the duchy with his supporters.\n\nWhat turned a family quarrel into a war is a cause the bullets do not prompt: Robert was useful to his father's enemies. King Philip I of France had every interest in a divided Normandy and installed Robert in the castle of Gerberoy on the frontier, from which he raided his father's lands. When William besieged Gerberoy in January 1079 the two met in the field, Robert unhorsed his father and wounded him in the hand before recognising him, and the breach became public and humiliating. It took Queen Matilda and the leading Norman nobles to broker the reconciliation of 1080, and it did not hold: after Matilda died in 1083 Robert left again.\n\nSo the incident supplied the occasion and Philip supplied the means, but the cause was a rule of inheritance that made rebellion the only career open to an heir.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what Robert actually possessed, as opposed to what he had been promised.","Divide your causes into the long-term position, the immediate trigger and the outsiders who made a war of it.","End each paragraph by saying what that cause made Robert do next, so the reasons form a chain rather than a list."],"codes":["B1-3 — 4 William I and the succession: Character and personality of William I and his relations with Robert"]},{"q":"Explain why the rebellion of 1088 failed. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • Robert Curthose • castles. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6, levels-marked. Credit Robert Curthose's failure to bring an army to England, the rebels' dispersal among separate castles and the sieges of Tonbridge, Pevensey and Rochester, Rufus's promises of better law and lighter taxes and his calling out of the English, Lanfranc's support, and Rufus's possession of the treasury and of a lawful coronation. Caps printed in the mark scheme: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"The first reason is the man the rebellion was fought for. Odo of Bayeux, released from prison on the Conqueror's deathbed in 1087, rose to put Robert Curthose on the English throne so that England and Normandy would have one lord again, and he was joined by Robert of Mortain, Roger of Montgomery, Geoffrey of Coutances and Robert de Mowbray. But Robert never came. No duke landed with an army, so the rising had a cause and no candidate in the field, and every rebel was left defending his own property in the name of a man who was somewhere else.\n\nThe second reason follows from the first. Without a field army the rebels were tied to their castles, and castles can be taken one at a time. William Rufus moved fast: he stormed Tonbridge, then turned on Pevensey, where Odo and Robert of Mortain held out for about six weeks before surrendering for lack of relief, and then reduced Rochester, after which Odo was exiled and lost his English lands for good. Scattered strongholds that had been built to hold down the English could not combine against a king who kept the initiative.\n\nThe third reason, beyond the bullets, is that Rufus was the lawful king and made himself the popular one. He had reached Winchester and secured his father's treasury, and Lanfranc had crowned him at Westminster on 26 September 1087, so the rebels were attacking an anointed king. Rufus then did what no Norman had yet tried: he called out the English and bought their service with promises of better law, lighter taxation and rights in the royal forests. The result was that the rebellion looked like a quarrel among foreign barons while the king commanded both money and men.\n\nThe rebellion failed, then, because it had no army, no leader present and no cause anyone outside the baronage wanted to fight for.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Begin from what the rebels needed in order to win, and check each item against what they actually had.","Weigh what the rebels lacked against what the king did; the second is where the point beyond the bullets is found.","Open each paragraph with the reason stated as a cause of failure, then prove it with a named siege, promise or date."],"codes":["B1-3 — 4 William I and the succession: William's death and the disputed succession","B1-3 — 3 The Norman aristocracy: The career and significance of Bishop Odo"]},{"q":"'The Domesday survey of 1086 was carried out mainly to raise money for the king.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the geld • the Salisbury oath. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6, levels-marked, no SPaG on this paper. Credit for the statement: hides and the geld, the heavy levy of 1084, values recorded for each manor, the cost of the mercenary army of 1085. Credit against: the recording of holders in 1066 and 1086 and of whom land was held, the settling of disputes, the feudal register of service owed, the Salisbury oath, the display of royal power. Caps printed in the mark scheme: 'Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"The test is not whether money was one purpose, which nobody doubts, but whether the design of the record is best explained by taxation. On that test the financial motive is real and powerful, but it does not account for the most distinctive thing Domesday does, which is to say who held every manor and of whom, so I only partly agree.\n\nThe case for money is strong. The geld was assessed on hides, so the tax was only as good as the assessment behind it, and twenty years of confiscation, regranting and the devastation of the North had made the old figures unusable. William had levied the geld hard, at six shillings on the hide in 1084, and Domesday records for each manor the hides it was rated at and what it was worth, which is precisely the information a sheriff needs to make a demand stick. The timing points the same way: the survey was ordered at the midwinter court at Gloucester at Christmas 1085, immediately after Cnut IV of Denmark had threatened invasion and William had brought over a mercenary army that had to be quartered and paid. A king facing that bill wants to know what his kingdom is worth.\n\nBut a tax roll would not have needed the rest of the record. For every estate Domesday states who held it in King Edward's day, who holds it now and from whom. That is the machinery of a title register. Two decades of hurried grants had left barons quarrelling with each other and with the Church, and a sworn national record settled such quarrels on the king's terms. It also told William exactly what each of his tenants-in-chief held of him, and therefore what knight service was owed, a feudal purpose and not a fiscal one.\n\nThe Salisbury oath belongs to the same argument. On 1 August 1086, as the returns were coming in, William summoned the landholding men of England, including substantial tenants of other lords, and they became his men and swore loyalty to him against all other men. Survey and oath together did one job: they fixed in writing and in ceremony the proposition that every acre in England was held of the king. Beyond both bullets, the survey's sheer reach was itself a demonstration of power: commissioners rode circuits of shires, a second and fuller volume covered Essex, Norfolk and Suffolk, and the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle's disgust at how little escaped shows how that was felt. Nothing on the scale had been attempted in western Europe.\n\nWeighing these, the financial purpose explains why the survey happened in 1085 and 1086 rather than earlier: the invasion scare and the cost of defence were the trigger. It does not explain the form the enquiry took. Had money been the only aim, the commissioners need not have asked who held each manor before 1066, nor of which lord it was now held, nor recorded the answers on oath. My judgement, on the criterion of what the record was built to do rather than what prompted it, is that the statement is half right: money was the occasion, but Domesday was above all written proof of the king's lordship over land, and that is why it mattered long after the geld of 1084 was spent.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what questions the commissioners actually asked, because a record's contents reveal its purpose.","Set the criterion first: are you judging what prompted the survey, or what it was designed to do?","Argue for the statement, then against it, then decide; the ceremony that followed in the summer of 1086 belongs with one of those sides."],"codes":["B1-3 — 2 Norman government: The Domesday survey and Domesday Book","B1-3 — 2 Norman government: Changes to government after the Conquest"]},{"q":"'The Norman Conquest changed the lives of English peasants very little in the years 1066–88.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • labour service • the forest. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6, levels-marked, no SPaG on this paper. Credit for the statement: open-field farming, labour service and the manor unchanged, continuity of hundred and shire courts, the same church and village. Credit against: the replacement of English lords, the Harrying of the North and Domesday's waste entries, the geld of 1084, forest law and the New Forest, castle building in towns, the decline of slavery. Caps printed in the mark scheme: 'Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"Judged by the work a villager did, the statement is largely true; judged by what that work cost him and how securely he held anything, it is false. I shall use the criterion of the peasant's own experience, distinguishing the routine of the farming year from the burdens laid on it, and conclude that the statement holds for the first and fails for the second.\n\nThe case for continuity is genuinely strong. The Conquest changed the owners of England, not its agriculture. Villagers went on farming strips in open fields with shared ox teams, they went on owing rent and labour service, the week-work on the lord's own land that had always defined their status, and they went on attending the same manorial and hundred courts. Domesday, taken twenty years after Hastings, describes the same villages with the same ploughs and mills and the same categories of villagers, bordars and cottars. Even the machinery above them survived: shires, hundreds, sheriffs, the geld and the silver penny were Anglo-Saxon institutions that the Normans kept because they worked. For most people the visible change was that the lord of the manor now had a French name and could not speak to them.\n\nAgainst that stands the weight of new demand. The geld, an old tax, was levied at new severity, reaching six shillings on the hide in 1084, and it was the manor that had to find it. Royal forest was something new altogether: land reserved for the king's hunting and governed by its own law outside the ordinary courts. The New Forest, created in about 1079, took in settlements, and the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle's obituary poem for William complained that he protected the deer as if he were their father and that a man who killed a hart was blinded. A villager on afforested land lost rights of pasture, wood and hunting that his family had always used, and gained a court he could not appeal from.\n\nBeyond both bullets lies the sharpest evidence. Resistance was punished collectively: in 1069 and 1070 William harried the North so thoroughly that Domesday still records much of Yorkshire as waste in 1086, which for the peasants of those villages meant famine and death rather than a change of landlord. In the towns, castle building cleared whole streets, and Domesday notes houses pulled down at Lincoln and Norwich to make room. In the opposite direction, the one clear improvement also came from the Normans: slavery, which still accounted for about a tenth of the people Domesday counted, was disapproved of by Lanfranc and faded away within two generations.\n\nThe judgement depends on which of these you weigh. If the test is the shape of daily life, the statement stands, because a villager in 1088 ploughed the same land in the same way as his father. But if the test is the peasant's security and the size of his obligations, it fails, because the same man now paid a heavier geld, might find his pasture inside a royal forest, and had seen what happened to villages that resisted. My conclusion is that the Conquest changed the structure of peasant life very little and the conditions of it a great deal, and that the second mattered more to the people living through it.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Separate what a peasant did each year from what a peasant owed and risked; they answer differently.","Choose your criterion in the first paragraph and keep testing every factor against it.","You need at least one piece of evidence the bullets do not prompt; the North and the towns are both worth looking at."],"codes":["B1-3 — 1 The feudal system and the Church: The extent of change to Anglo-Saxon society and economy","B1-3 — 2 Norman government: The office of sheriff and the demesne"]},{"q":"'Bishop Odo was the most useful of William I's supporters in England in the years 1066–87.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the earldom of Kent • regency. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6, levels-marked, no SPaG on this paper. Credit for the statement: Odo's earldom of Kent from 1067, his wealth in Domesday, the regency of 1067, campaigns against rebellion and the ravaging of Northumbria after Walcher's murder in 1080. Credit against: Lanfranc's Normanisation of the Church and his regency, William FitzOsbern and the Marcher earldoms, Robert of Mortain's estates, and Odo's arrest in 1082 and rebellion in 1088. Caps printed in the mark scheme: 'Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"Usefulness must be defined before it can be judged. I shall take it to mean what a supporter contributed to William's control of England across the whole reign, not how much he was given, and on that criterion Odo was the most spectacular of William's servants but not the most useful, because the qualities that made him formidable also made him a danger.\n\nThe case for Odo is substantial. As the king's half-brother he was given the earldom of Kent in 1067, which placed the crossing from Normandy, with the castles of Dover and Rochester, in the hands of a man the king could trust by blood. Domesday shows him as the greatest lay landholder in England after the king himself. He was left as one of the regents when William returned to Normandy in 1067; he fought rebellion; and when Bishop Walcher of Durham was murdered at Gateshead in 1080 it was Odo who led the expedition that ravaged Northumbria. He combined wealth, a strategic command, a bishop's authority and the king's blood.\n\nAgainst that must be set Lanfranc. Made Archbishop of Canterbury in 1070 after the deposition of Stigand, he remade the English Church into an arm of royal government: he settled the primacy of Canterbury over York with Thomas of Bayeux at Winchester in 1072, held reforming councils, and replaced English prelates with Normans until Wulfstan of Worcester was effectively the last English bishop left. He also governed England as regent during the king's long absences after 1072. Crucially, his power rested on an office the king had granted, not on an inheritance he could rebel with, which is exactly what an absent king needs. Nor was Odo the only great landholder: their brother Robert of Mortain held the rape of Pevensey and vast estates in the south-west, and William FitzOsbern held Hereford and acted as regent until his death in 1071.\n\nThe decisive evidence lies beyond the two bullets, in how Odo's career ended. In 1082 William arrested him, on the account that he was recruiting knights for ambitions of his own in Italy, and held him in prison at Rouen until the king's deathbed in 1087. William is said to have met the objection that a bishop could not be tried by replying that he was arresting the Earl of Kent and not the Bishop of Bayeux. For the last five years of the reign, then, the supposedly most useful supporter was in a cell, and within a year of William's death he led the rebellion of 1088 against William Rufus and lost his English lands. Lanfranc, by contrast, secured the succession for Rufus by crowning him at Westminster on 26 September 1087 and stood by him in 1088.\n\nMy judgement is therefore a qualified disagreement. Measured by wealth, office and visibility, Odo stands first among William's men, and in the reign's first fifteen years no one did more hard service. But if usefulness means reliably strengthening the king's hold on England for as long as the king needed it, the verdict goes to Lanfranc, whose power could not be turned against the crown and who delivered the throne safely to the next king. Odo shows both the strength of William's system and its weakness: a kinsman made that rich becomes a rival.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide what 'useful' means to a king before you start listing what each man owned.","Set Odo against a named alternative rather than against a general impression, and keep the same test on both.","The last years of Odo's career decide the question; make sure your argument reaches them."],"codes":["B1-3 — 3 The Norman aristocracy: The career and significance of Bishop Odo","B1-3 — 3 The Norman aristocracy: The culture and language of the Norman aristocracy","B1-3 — 1 The feudal system and the Church: The Church in England"]},{"q":"'The main reason William Rufus defeated the rebellion of 1088 was the support of the English.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • promises • Robert Curthose. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6, levels-marked, no SPaG on this paper. Credit for the statement: Rufus's promises of better law, lighter taxation and rights in the forests, the calling out of the English, and their service at the sieges. Credit against: Robert Curthose's failure to bring an army, the rebels' dispersal among castles at Tonbridge, Pevensey and Rochester, Rufus's possession of the treasury at Winchester, his lawful coronation and Lanfranc's backing. Caps printed in the mark scheme: 'Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"The criterion I shall use is the one the question implies: which factor, if it had been absent, would have changed the outcome. Against that test English support was important but not decisive, because the rebellion was already crippled by the failure of the man it was fought for, and I therefore disagree with the statement more than I agree with it.\n\nThere is a real case for the English. Rufus faced a rising of much of the Norman baronage: Odo of Bayeux, freed on his half-brother's deathbed, together with Robert of Mortain, Roger of Montgomery, Geoffrey of Coutances and Robert de Mowbray, aiming to make Robert Curthose king of England so that England and Normandy would again have one lord. A king whose barons have deserted him needs soldiers from somewhere, and Rufus found them by calling out the English and promising them better law, lighter taxation and rights of hunting in the royal forests. That was a new departure: no Norman king had yet appealed to the conquered against the conquerors, and it gave him the numbers to besiege castles.\n\nBut the rebellion's fatal weakness was its own. Robert Curthose never came. He sent some help, but no duke landed in England with an army, so the rising had a candidate who was somewhere else and no field force at all. That single failure turned the rebels from an alliance into a collection of separate garrisons, and garrisons can be dealt with one at a time. Rufus took Tonbridge by storm, then invested Pevensey, where Odo and Robert of Mortain held out for about six weeks and surrendered because no relief came, and then reduced Rochester, after which Odo was exiled and stripped of his English lands. Had Robert landed in strength in Kent, English levies would have faced a Norman duke and his knights in the field, which is a very different proposition from sitting outside a castle whose defenders are running out of food.\n\nBeyond the bullets there is a further factor: Rufus began with the advantages of an established king. He had crossed at once with his father's letter, secured the treasury at Winchester and been crowned at Westminster by Lanfranc on 26 September 1087, so he could pay troops, and the rebels were attacking an anointed king rather than choosing between two candidates. Lanfranc's support mattered for the same reason: the archbishop who had governed England for the Conqueror lent the new king the authority of the Church and of continuity.\n\nWeighing them by the test I set, English support was the means by which Rufus won, but the reason the rebellion could be beaten at all was that it never became a war between two armies. Robert's absence, Rufus's money and legitimacy, and the rebels' dispersal among castles made the campaign a series of sieges, and it was for sieges that English levies were sufficient. My judgement is that the statement mistakes the instrument for the cause: the English gave Rufus an army, but Robert Curthose gave him the victory.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what each side needed in order to win, and then check which of those things was actually missing.","State the test you are applying in your first paragraph and use the same test on every factor.","One of your paragraphs must rest on evidence the bullets do not prompt; think about what Rufus already held in September 1087."],"codes":["B1-3 — 4 William I and the succession: William's death and the disputed succession","B1-3 — 3 The Norman aristocracy: The career and significance of Bishop Odo"]}],"exit":[{"q":"At which council, and in which year, was Stigand deposed as Archbishop of Canterbury?","a":"The Council of Winchester, at Easter 1070, held with papal legates."},{"q":"What did the landholders who came to Salisbury on 1 August 1086 swear?","a":"They became William's men and swore loyalty to him against all other men."},{"q":"On what date did William I die, and where?","a":"9 September 1087, at the priory of St Gervase outside Rouen."},{"q":"Who led the rebellion of 1088, and whom did he want on the English throne?","a":"Bishop Odo of Bayeux, who wanted Robert Curthose, Duke of Normandy, to be king of England."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Tenant-in-chief","back":"A baron or bishop holding land directly from the king. About two hundred of them held most of England by 1086; each did homage, swore fealty and owed knight service.","code":"B1-3 — 1 The feudal system and the Church: The feudal hierarchy","open":false},{"front":"Knight service (servitium debitum)","back":"The fixed quota of knights a tenant-in-chief owed the king for the royal army, normally about forty days a year. He met it by granting manors to knights of his own.","code":"B1-3 — 1 The feudal system and the Church: The feudal hierarchy","open":false},{"front":"Forfeiture","back":"Because land was held of the king, treason lost it. After the revolt of the earls in 1075 Ralph de Gael and Roger de Breteuil lost their earldoms, and Waltheof was executed in 1076.","code":"B1-3 — 1 The feudal system and the Church: The feudal hierarchy","open":false},{"front":"Stigand","back":"Archbishop of Canterbury from 1052 who also kept Winchester and had an irregular pallium. Deposed and imprisoned at the Council of Winchester at Easter 1070, which let William call conquest reform.","code":"B1-3 — 1 The feudal system and the Church: The Church in England","open":false},{"front":"Lanfranc","back":"Italian prior of Bec, made Archbishop of Canterbury in 1070. He deposed English prelates, reformed by council, won York's submission to Canterbury at Winchester in 1072, and governed England as regent.","code":"B1-3 — 1 The feudal system and the Church: The Church in England","open":false},{"front":"Labour service and slavery after 1066","back":"Villagers still owed week-work on the lord's land, so the manor changed owner rather than shape. Slavery, about a tenth of those Domesday counted in 1086, then faded out within two generations.","code":"B1-3 — 1 The feudal system and the Church: The extent of change to Anglo-Saxon society and economy","open":false},{"front":"Regents in William I's absences","back":"England was governed for the absent king by appointed deputies: Odo of Bayeux and William FitzOsbern from 1067, and later Lanfranc, whose power came from office rather than inheritance.","code":"B1-3 — 2 Norman government: Changes to government after the Conquest","open":false},{"front":"The Norman sheriff","back":"An Anglo-Saxon office filled with Normans and strengthened: he collected the geld, farmed the royal demesne, presided in the shire court, raised troops and often held the royal castle.","code":"B1-3 — 2 Norman government: The office of sheriff and the demesne","open":false},{"front":"The 'forest' and forest law","back":"Land reserved for the king's hunting and ruled by its own law outside the ordinary courts. The New Forest was created in about 1079; the Chronicle says men who killed a hart were blinded.","code":"B1-3 — 2 Norman government: The office of sheriff and the demesne","open":false},{"front":"The Domesday survey, 1085–86","back":"Ordered at Gloucester at Christmas 1085 and taken in 1086 on sworn evidence from shire and hundred juries. Written up as Great Domesday and the fuller Little Domesday; London and Winchester were left out.","code":"B1-3 — 2 Norman government: The Domesday survey and Domesday Book","open":false},{"front":"The Salisbury oath, 1 August 1086","back":"William summoned England's landholding men, including substantial tenants of other lords, to Salisbury; they became his men and swore loyalty to him against all other men.","code":"B1-3 — 2 Norman government: The Domesday survey and Domesday Book","open":false},{"front":"Norman French and Latin after the Conquest","back":"French was the language of court, household and law; royal writs and charters were written in Latin instead of Old English from about 1070; English survived as the speech of the governed.","code":"B1-3 — 3 The Norman aristocracy: The culture and language of the Norman aristocracy","open":false},{"front":"Bishop Odo of Bayeux","back":"William's half-brother, Bishop of Bayeux and Earl of Kent from 1067, regent, and the richest lay landholder after the king. Arrested in 1082, imprisoned until 1087, exiled after 1088.","code":"B1-3 — 3 The Norman aristocracy: The career and significance of Bishop Odo","open":false},{"front":"Robert Curthose and the revolt of 1077–80","back":"William's eldest son, promised Normandy but given no power. After the quarrel at L'Aigle he rebelled, was backed by Philip I at Gerberoy, wounded his father there in 1079 and was reconciled in 1080.","code":"B1-3 — 4 William I and the succession: Character and personality of William I and his relations with Robert","open":false},{"front":"The rebellion of 1088","back":"Odo, Robert of Mortain and others rose to make Robert Curthose king. Robert never landed; Rufus won English support, took Tonbridge, Pevensey and Rochester, and exiled Odo.","code":"B1-3 — 4 William I and the succession: William's death and the disputed succession","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (booklet B, the 16-mark judgement)","items":["Question: 'Lanfranc was the most important reason for change in the English Church in the years 1070–87.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] You may use the following in your answer: • the Council of Winchester (1070) • church courts. You must also use information of your own. What follows is one paragraph from the middle of the answer, putting the case against the statement.","Lanfranc was the instrument of change rather than its cause, because the decisions that mattered most were the king's and not the archbishop's. It was William who made the appointment in the first place, in 1070, after the papal legates at the Council of Winchester had deposed Stigand, whose plurality at Canterbury and Winchester and irregular pallium gave the crown a reforming pretext for a political purge; and it was William who then filled bishopric after bishopric with his own men until, by the 1080s, Wulfstan of Worcester was effectively the last English bishop left. The limits the king set are more revealing still. William insisted that no pope be recognised in England and no baron excommunicated without his consent, and in 1080 he refused to swear fealty to Gregory VII, so the reform stopped precisely where it began to threaten royal authority. Even the separation of church courts from the hundred court in the mid-1070s, which looks like Lanfranc's kind of measure, was issued as a royal writ and had the effect of tidying jurisdiction under a Church the king controlled. On the criterion of who could have done otherwise, Lanfranc could not have removed a single bishop without William, while William could have found another reforming Norman abbot; so for this paragraph the archbishop's importance is real but derived.","Why this is Level 4: the explanation is directed consistently at the conceptual focus, which is the relative importance of a cause and not a description of the reforms, and the line of reasoning runs from the opening claim through to the closing judgement without a break. The information is precisely selected rather than piled up, since 1070, the legates and Stigand's plurality, Wulfstan of Worcester, the refusal of fealty to Gregory VII in 1080 and the writ on church courts each carry a step of the argument. The criterion for judgement is stated and applied — who could have acted otherwise — and the interim judgement at the end answers 'how far' rather than 'what happened'. It also addresses three or more aspects of content instead of resting on the two stimulus bullets, which is what the mark scheme requires before Level 4 is available at all."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["The same question, answered at Level 2. Read it beside the Level 4 paragraph above and look for what is missing rather than for what is wrong.","Lanfranc was very important for change in the English Church because he was made Archbishop of Canterbury and he changed a lot of things. He got rid of English bishops and put Normans in instead, which made the Church more Norman. He also held councils and made rules for the priests. But William was important as well because he was the king and he was in charge of everything, so he had a say in what happened to the Church. This shows Lanfranc was important but so was William.","Why this is only Level 2: it meets the descriptor 'an explanation is given showing limited analysis and with implicit or unsustained links to the conceptual focus of the question', because two causes are named and a link is gestured at in 'made the Church more Norman' and 'he had a say', but neither link is traced. It misses the higher descriptors in three ways. The information is generalised rather than 'accurate and relevant information precisely selected to address the question directly': there is no date, no named council, no named bishop, no Stigand and no Gregory VII. The line of reasoning is not sustained, because the paragraph changes side in one sentence without explaining why. And the judgement, 'important but so was William', is exactly what the Level 2 descriptor calls an overall judgement whose 'justification is asserted or insecure', because no criterion is offered for deciding between them."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the feudal system, landholding and the extent of change to English society","items":["William's claim: he had succeeded lawfully, so all land in England was held of the king and every estate was a grant, not a possession.","Tenants-in-chief: roughly two hundred barons and bishops holding directly from the king; roughly a fifth of England stayed in the king's own hands and about a quarter belonged to the Church.","Homage and fealty: the tenant knelt and placed his hands between the king's to become his man, then swore fealty on oath.","Knight service: each tenant-in-chief owed a fixed quota of knights, the servitium debitum, for the royal army, normally for about forty days a year.","Subinfeudation: tenants-in-chief granted manors to knights on the same terms, so the pyramid reached from the king down to the village.","Labour service: peasants held their strips from the lord of the manor and paid in week-work on his own land.","Forfeiture: rebellion cost a man his land. After the revolt of the earls in 1075 Ralph de Gael fled and lost East Anglia, Roger de Breteuil was imprisoned for life and lost Hereford, and Waltheof was executed in 1076.","By 1086 barely any Englishman of rank held land directly from the king; Domesday records only a couple of English tenants-in-chief.","Continuity: shires, hundreds, sheriffs, the shire and hundred courts, the writ, the geld and the silver penny all survived the Conquest.","Change on the ground: several hundred castles were built by the end of the reign, and Domesday notes houses pulled down in towns such as Lincoln and Norwich to clear ground for them.","The Harrying of the North, 1069–70: Domesday still described much of Yorkshire as waste when the survey was taken in 1086.","Slavery: about a tenth of the people Domesday counted in 1086 were slaves; the practice was discouraged by Lanfranc and died out within two generations.","Towns: new boroughs and French quarters appeared, and trade with Normandy and northern France grew."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Stigand, Lanfranc and the Norman Church","items":["Stigand: Archbishop of Canterbury from 1052, who also held the bishopric of Winchester and had taken his pallium from a pope later judged an antipope.","William was crowned on Christmas Day 1066 by Ealdred, Archbishop of York, not by Stigand, because Stigand's position was irregular.","Council of Winchester, Easter 1070: held with papal legates; Stigand was deposed and imprisoned, and other English prelates were removed.","Lanfranc: Italian scholar, prior of Bec and abbot of St Stephen's at Caen, appointed Archbishop of Canterbury in 1070.","Winchester, 1072: Thomas of Bayeux, Archbishop of York, acknowledged the primacy of Canterbury, giving the English Church a single head.","Reform by council: Lanfranc held regular church councils; a council ruled against priests marrying in future, though village priests who already had wives were not all forced to give them up.","Bishops' seats were moved from villages into towns, for example from Selsey to Chichester.","Separate courts: in the mid-1070s a royal writ took spiritual cases out of the hundred court and gave them to the bishop's own court.","Building: cathedrals and abbeys were rebuilt in the Romanesque style, with round arches, thick walls and massive piers.","Normanisation: by the 1080s Wulfstan of Worcester was effectively the last English bishop left.","Royal control: William insisted that no pope be recognised in England, no papal letter received and no baron excommunicated without his consent.","In 1080 William refused to swear fealty to Pope Gregory VII while continuing to pay Peter's Pence.","The Church as government: bishops and abbots held great estates, owed knight service for them, sat in the king's council and supplied the men who could write."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the office of sheriff, the royal demesne and the forest","items":["The sheriff was an Anglo-Saxon office kept by the Normans, but its English holders were replaced and its powers grew.","Finance: the sheriff collected the geld in his shire and answered to the king for a fixed annual sum, the farm of the shire.","The royal demesne: land held directly by the king in every part of England, managed for him by the sheriff, including the former estates of Edward the Confessor and Harold.","Justice: the sheriff presided in the shire court and enforced its judgements.","Force: he raised men for the king and was often castellan of the royal castle in the shire town.","Over-mighty sheriffs: Picot of Cambridgeshire and Urse d'Abetot of Worcestershire were notorious for seizing land, including the Church's.","The geld: a land tax assessed on hides, levied by William repeatedly and at six shillings on the hide in 1084.","The forest: not woodland but land reserved for the king's hunting, governed by forest law outside the ordinary courts and administered by the king's own officers.","The New Forest in Hampshire was created in about 1079, taking in settlements and farmland.","The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle's obituary poem for William complained that he loved the deer as though he were their father and that men who killed a hart were blinded.","Forest law removed rights of pasture, firewood and hunting that villagers had long used, and there was no appeal to the shire court.","Writs: the short sealed royal order to a shire continued from Anglo-Saxon practice, but from about 1070 it was written in Latin rather than Old English."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Domesday survey and Domesday Book","items":["Ordered by William at his midwinter court at Gloucester at Christmas 1085, after taking counsel with his men.","Carried out in 1086 by commissioners riding circuits of shires, taking sworn evidence from shire and hundred juries.","Written up as two volumes: Great Domesday, a summary for most of England, and Little Domesday, fuller in detail, covering Essex, Norfolk and Suffolk.","London and Winchester were not included, nor was most of the far north of England.","For each manor the record gives who held it in King Edward's day, who holds it now and of whom, its assessment in hides, its ploughs, mills, livestock and people, and its value.","Fiscal purpose: hides were the basis of the geld, and twenty years of confiscation and regranting had made the old assessments unusable.","Military context: in 1085 Cnut IV of Denmark, allied with Robert of Flanders, threatened invasion, and William brought over a large paid army and quartered it on his tenants.","Legal purpose: sworn returns settled the disputes over estates that two decades of hurried grants had produced, including quarrels between barons and the Church.","Feudal purpose: the record showed which tenant-in-chief held what of the king, and therefore what knight service was owed.","The Salisbury oath, 1 August 1086: the landholding men of England, including substantial tenants of other lords, came to Salisbury, became William's men and swore loyalty to him against all other men.","The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle's chronicler was shocked by how thorough the enquiry was, complaining that scarcely an animal escaped the record.","Nothing of the kind had been attempted anywhere in western Europe, so the survey was itself a display of royal power."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Bishop Odo of Bayeux and the Norman aristocracy","items":["Odo was William's half-brother, son of Herluin de Conteville and Herleva, and was made Bishop of Bayeux as a very young man.","He was given the earldom of Kent in 1067, which placed the crossing from Normandy and the castles of Dover and Rochester in his hands.","Domesday shows him as the greatest lay landholder in England after the king himself.","He was one of the regents left in charge of England when William returned to Normandy in 1067, with William FitzOsbern.","After Bishop Walcher of Durham was murdered at Gateshead in 1080, Odo led the punitive expedition that ravaged Northumbria.","He is the likeliest patron of the Bayeux Tapestry, which was probably made for his cathedral at Bayeux.","In 1082 William arrested him, on the account that he was recruiting knights for ambitions of his own in Italy, and imprisoned him at Rouen until the king's deathbed in 1087.","William is said to have answered the objection that a bishop could not be tried by replying that he was arresting the Earl of Kent, not the Bishop of Bayeux.","Robert of Mortain, another half-brother, held the rape of Pevensey in Sussex and vast estates in the south-west, especially Cornwall.","William FitzOsbern was made Earl of Hereford and acted as regent until his death in 1071; the other Marcher earldoms were Shrewsbury under Roger of Montgomery and Chester under Hugh d'Avranches.","Most great barons held land on both sides of the Channel, so their marriages, interests and burials remained Norman, and intermarriage with the English was rare at the top.","Norman French was the language of court, household and law; Latin replaced Old English in royal writs and charters from about 1070.","Estates were usually scattered across many shires so that no baron held a solid block of territory, except on the Welsh border where the Marcher earls were deliberately given one."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Robert's revolt in Normandy, 1077–80","items":["Robert Curthose was William's eldest son, promised the duchy of Normandy and recognised in Maine, but given no land, revenue or real authority of his own.","The quarrel began at L'Aigle in 1077, when his younger brothers William and Henry emptied water over him from an upper room.","William refused to punish them, which told Robert and his young followers in public that the heir could be humiliated without redress.","Robert demanded Normandy and Maine at once, was refused, tried and failed to seize the castle at Rouen, and left the duchy with his supporters.","King Philip I of France, who profited from a divided Normandy, installed Robert in the castle of Gerberoy on the frontier.","William besieged Gerberoy in January 1079; Robert unhorsed his father and wounded him in the hand before recognising him.","Queen Matilda, who had been sending Robert money, and the leading Norman nobles brought about a reconciliation in 1080.","Lanfranc governed England as regent while William dealt with the crisis across the Channel.","Matilda died in 1083, after which Robert left his father's court again and was still absent when William died.","Contemporaries describe William as pious, self-disciplined and enormously determined, and also cold and frightening: the founder of abbeys who harried the North.","The underlying cause was the rule of inheritance: an heir received nothing while his father lived, so rebellion was the only career open to him."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: William's death, William Rufus and the rebellion of 1088","items":["Campaigning against Philip I in the Vexin, William burned the town of Mantes in the summer of 1087 and was injured.","He died at the priory of St Gervase outside Rouen on 9 September 1087 and was buried at his abbey of St Stephen at Caen.","On his deathbed he released Odo of Bayeux from the prison he had held him in since 1082.","The inheritance was divided: Normandy, which he had inherited, to Robert; England, which he had won, to his second surviving son William Rufus; five thousand pounds of silver to the youngest, Henry.","Rufus crossed at once with his father's letter to Lanfranc, secured the treasury at Winchester and was crowned at Westminster on 26 September 1087 by Lanfranc.","The division satisfied nobody holding land on both sides of the Channel, because such a man now owed homage to two rival lords.","The rebellion of 1088 was led by Odo of Bayeux, to make Robert Curthose king of England as well as duke of Normandy.","Those who joined included Robert of Mortain, Roger of Montgomery, Geoffrey of Coutances and Robert de Mowbray.","Robert Curthose never landed in England with an army, so the rebels had no field force and no candidate present.","Rufus called out the English and won their support with promises of better law, lighter taxation and rights in the royal forests.","He stormed Tonbridge, then besieged Pevensey, held by Odo and Robert of Mortain, which surrendered after about six weeks for lack of relief, and then reduced Rochester.","Odo was exiled and lost his English lands for good; Robert of Mortain was pardoned and kept his English lands until his death in the mid-1090s."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — the 16-mark judgement: 'The feudal system was the main way in which William I kept control of England in the years 1066–87.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.","items":["The question as printed, Question 3 or Question 4 in booklet B: 'The feudal system was the main way in which William I kept control of England in the years 1066–87.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] You may use the following in your answer: • knight service • forfeiture. You must also use information of your own. There are no spelling, punctuation and grammar marks on Paper 2, so every minute goes on the argument.","Decode it. The concept is significance weighed against alternatives, and 'the main way' means you must rank, not list. Announce the criterion in the opening and apply it at the end: which method actually prevented or defeated opposition, as opposed to merely organising the country. Name the alternatives you will weigh — castles and force, the Church and Normanisation, and the machinery of government the Normans inherited — so the line of reasoning is visible from the first sentence.","Opening, three or four sentences: define the criterion, name the three or four factors you will weigh, and signal where you will end.","Paragraph 1, for the statement — land held for service. All land was held of the king; about two hundred tenants-in-chief did homage and swore fealty; each owed a fixed quota of knights, the servitium debitum, normally for about forty days a year, and met it by subinfeudating manors to knights. Interim judgement: this gave William a standing army he did not have to pay for, and it made every lord's wealth conditional on his loyalty.","Paragraph 2, for the statement — forfeiture as the sanction. Rebellion cost a man everything: after the revolt of the earls in 1075 Ralph de Gael fled and lost East Anglia, Roger de Breteuil was imprisoned for life, Waltheof was executed in 1076, and the two earldoms were simply not refilled. Interim judgement: the threat made the system self-policing, which is more than an administrative arrangement.","Paragraph 3, against the statement — force and castles. Several hundred castles were raised, in earth and timber and later in stone as at the White Tower in London, with houses cleared at Lincoln and Norwich to make room; the Harrying of the North in 1069 and 1070 left Yorkshire still recorded as waste in Domesday twenty years later. Interim judgement: feudal obligation distributed force, but it was the castle and the punitive campaign that applied it.","Paragraph 4, against the statement — the Church and the inherited machinery, which is the point beyond the stimulus bullets. Stigand's deposition at Winchester in 1070, Lanfranc's appointment, the settlement with York in 1072 and the replacement of English bishops until Wulfstan of Worcester stood almost alone gave William a second network of loyal magnates; meanwhile the sheriff, the shire and hundred courts, the writ and the geld went on working, and the Domesday survey of 1086 and the Salisbury oath of 1 August 1086 recorded and reinforced the whole structure. Interim judgement: much of what held England was not feudal at all but Anglo-Saxon government in Norman hands.","Three or more aspects of content: the mark scheme caps an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content at 11 marks and gives it no access to Level 4, so at least one paragraph must rest on material the bullets do not prompt. The Church under Lanfranc, the castles, the Harrying of the North, the sheriff and Domesday all qualify, and without one of them the answer cannot reach Level 4 however well it is written.","The evaluation thread: keep applying the same test in every paragraph — did this method prevent opposition, or only organise the kingdom? Run it all the way through so the conclusion is the last step of the argument and not a new claim.","The conclusion must rank and justify rather than summarise. Argue that the feudal settlement was the framework that made everything else possible, because it put land, wealth and military obligation in the same hands and made all three revocable, but that on the test of what actually defeated opposition the castle and the campaign came first in the early years and the Church and the sheriff kept the country quiet later. Then qualify: the framework was necessary but not by itself sufficient.","Timing: about 28 minutes, since Paper 2's 1 hour 50 minutes give booklet B roughly 55 minutes for its 32 marks. Roughly six to plan the four paragraphs and write the criterion sentence, twenty to write, and two to check that the last paragraph answers 'how far' rather than 'in what ways'."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-18T22:07:19Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"All four faults from the previous pass are fixed and fixed correctly - Robert of Mortain now 'kept his English lands until his death in the mid-1090s' (right on both counts, and the disputed year is gone), room.facts item 4 now describes the 16-mark grid as three strands per level marked best-fit across the band, which is what levels.md prints, lesson.examples[0] now stops 1(a) because both marks are earned and says the mark scheme accepts 'against all other men' as support, and question 5's second bullet is 'the royal demesne', which its sol and indicative content already cover; I re-checked the lesson, all fourteen questions, five worked examples, six fact files, fifteen cards, the exit ticket, the two model paragraphs and the plan against levels.md and re-verified the load-bearing dates (Winchester 1070 and 1072, the 1076 marriage canon, the 1080 refusal of fealty, Waltheof 1076, the geld of 1084, the New Forest c.1079, Gerberoy January 1079, Walcher 1080, Odo 1082, Domesday 1085-86 and the Salisbury oath of 1 August 1086, 9 and 26 September 1087, the 1088 sieges) and found nothing wrong; only a one-sentence signpost in question 5 keeps this from full marks."}}},"B2-1":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"B2-1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This is the opening key topic of the Richard I and John depth study, and it is the one every other part of the option rests on. Booklet B asks you for one feature of each of two different aspects of the period, in two separate 2-mark parts, then to explain why something happened, and then to judge how far one factor mattered most; none of those can be answered by telling the story of the reigns. You need the working parts of England between 1189 and 1216: who held land from whom and on what terms, what a king was expected to be and to do, how the country kept running for ten years while Richard was abroad, where the money actually came from, and how the great majority of people lived in villages, in towns and, in the case of England's Jewish communities, under a royal protection that could be withdrawn. Learn this topic as a set of named mechanisms with dates and figures attached — homage, scutage, the county farm, the archa, the interdict — and you can select the two or three pieces of evidence that answer the question in front of you and leave everything else out.","idea":[{"h":"Land for service: the pyramid and the promises that held it up","code":"B2-1 — 1 The feudal system: The feudal hierarchy and the nature of feudalism","t":"Feudalism was not a theory but a bargain about land. In principle the king owned all the land in England and granted it out; the great barons, bishops and abbots who held directly from him were his tenants-in-chief. Each did homage, kneeling with his hands between the king's, and swore fealty, and in return for his fief he owed a fixed quota of knights, the servitium debitum, usually for forty days' unpaid service a year. Tenants-in-chief granted land on in the same way to their own knights, and knights let land to peasants, so that the same promise was repeated down the pyramid. At the bottom the villein held his strips in return for labour service: work on the lord's demesne for two or three days a week and extra boon work at harvest. The bargain had teeth. A tenant who refused service, rebelled or defied his lord could be judged to have broken it and have his lands forfeited: John's English lands were declared forfeit in 1194 while he was plotting with Philip II against Richard, and William de Braose, once one of John's closest men, lost everything after 1208 and fled to France. A top-level answer uses feudalism as a working system of obligations, not a diagram: it explains that because every layer owed service and money upwards, a king who pressed his rights hard could raise armies and cash without inventing a single new tax, and that the same system gave a baron a language in which to say the king had broken the bargain."},{"h":"The Church: landlord, court, employer and consolation","code":"B2-1 — 1 The feudal system: The role and influence of the Church","t":"The Church touched every life in England and every level of government. Ordinary people met it in the parish: baptism, marriage, confession and burial, Sunday mass in Latin, holy days that shaped the working year, and the tithe, a tenth of the year's produce, paid whether the harvest was good or not. Above the parish stood the bishops and the great abbeys, many of them tenants-in-chief holding land and owing knight service like any baron, which made the Church one of the largest landowners in the country and a huge employer of labour. The Church ran its own courts, which claimed all clergy and all cases about marriage, wills and morality, and it supplied the crown with its literate administrators: Hubert Walter was Archbishop of Canterbury, justiciar and then chancellor at once. It also supplied the king with money, because while a bishopric or abbey was vacant its revenues went to the crown. That is one reason the quarrel of 1205 to 1213 over who should be Archbishop of Canterbury mattered so much: Pope Innocent III put England under an interdict on 24 March 1208, church services stopped, and John answered by taking the revenues of the Church into his own hands for years. A top-level answer weighs the Church as an institution with its own power and its own money, and shows that the same wealth that made it useful to a king also made it worth attacking."},{"h":"What a king was for: the coronation oath and the rights of the crown","code":"B2-1 — 2 Kingship and succession: The nature of kingship","t":"Kingship was a set of duties and a set of rights, joined by a ceremony. At his coronation in Westminster Abbey the king swore a threefold oath — to protect the Church, to keep the peace and put down wrongdoing, and to do justice with mercy — and was then anointed with holy oil by the Archbishop of Canterbury before being crowned and given the regalia. The anointing was the point: it made the king more than the strongest baron, and it was why rebellion against him could be presented as a sin. In return he expected homage from his tenants-in-chief, the feudal dues of the crown, the profits of justice and of the royal forest, and the right to summon men to war, to control the coinage and to appoint sheriffs and judges. He was also expected to take counsel: great councils of barons and bishops met to advise on war and taxation, and the tax of 1207 was put to one at Oxford before it was levied. The duties were tested by how they were done, not by whether they were sworn. A top-level answer uses the oath as a measuring stick: Richard kept the duty to defend Christendom and largely ignored England, John was present and hard-working but quarrelled with the Church and was accused of denying justice, and in each case the gap between the promises of the coronation and the conduct of the reign is the argument."},{"h":"Richard I: the claim, the crusade and the absent king","code":"B2-1 — 2 Kingship and succession: Richard I as king","t":"Richard's claim in 1189 was the strongest possible: he was the eldest surviving legitimate son of Henry II, his elder brother Henry the Young King having died in 1183 and his next brother Geoffrey in 1186. He succeeded on his father's death on 6 July 1189 and was crowned at Westminster on 3 September 1189. He secured power by rewarding and by selling: he released his brother John's rivals or bought them off, granted John great estates, and turned offices, sheriffdoms and charters into cash for the Third Crusade, joking, so one chronicler says, that he would have sold London if he could have found a buyer. In December 1189 he released William the Lion of Scotland from his submission to Henry II for 10,000 marks. He then left. After that, apart from two months in the spring of 1194, when captivity in Germany had been ended by a ransom of 150,000 marks, Richard spent no further time in England at all, so that in a ten-year reign he was in the kingdom for about six months; he died at the siege of Châlus in the Limousin on 6 April 1199 of a crossbow wound. His character — brave, an outstanding soldier, careless of England except as a source of money and men — is the key to judging him. A top-level answer avoids the argument about whether Richard was a 'good' king and asks instead what he was good at: the same qualities that made him the hero of Acre and Jaffa made him an absentee who had to leave England to be run by other men, and it is the machinery that kept working in his absence that shows how strong English government had become."},{"h":"John: a disputed claim, Mirebeau and the disappearance of Arthur","code":"B2-1 — 2 Kingship and succession: John as king","t":"John's claim in 1199 was not obvious. He was Henry II's youngest son, nicknamed Lackland, and by one rule of inheritance the throne should have gone to Arthur of Brittany, the son of his dead elder brother Geoffrey, who was twelve. Richard was said to have named John as his heir, and John was accepted in England and Normandy, crowned at Westminster on 27 May 1199; but Anjou, Maine and Touraine declared for Arthur, who was backed by Philip II of France. John secured power by speed and by allies: Eleanor of Aquitaine held the south for him, Hubert Walter and William Marshal carried the English barons, and in May 1200 the Treaty of Le Goulet bought Philip's recognition for a relief of 20,000 marks. War resumed in 1202, and on 1 August 1202 John marched overnight to Mirebeau, relieved his mother and captured Arthur. Arthur was imprisoned at Falaise and then Rouen, and in 1203 he disappeared; the annals of Margam Abbey later claimed John killed him himself, and no one knows how he died, but the belief that John had murdered his nephew cost him support in Brittany and Anjou and gave Philip a case in his own court. A top-level answer treats John's character — able, suspicious, vindictive, always present — as evidence rather than as an insult, and uses Arthur to show how a king could be strong enough to win a battle and still destroy his own authority."},{"h":"Government that worked without the king: justiciar, exchequer, chancery","code":"B2-1 — 3 Royal government and finances: How England was governed","t":"England could run itself because Henry II's machinery of government did not need the king in the room. The chief minister was the justiciar, who ruled in the king's name when he was abroad. Richard made William Longchamp, Bishop of Ely, his chancellor in September 1189 and then, at a council at Nonancourt in March 1190, chief justiciar; Longchamp quarrelled with John and the barons and was driven out in 1191, replaced by Walter of Coutances, Archbishop of Rouen, and then in 1193 by Hubert Walter, who raised the ransom and reformed local government, ordering in 1194 that four men in each county keep the pleas of the crown, the beginning of the coroner. Geoffrey FitzPeter followed as justiciar from 1198 until his death in 1213. Beneath the justiciar sat the exchequer at Westminster, which twice a year, at Easter and Michaelmas, audited the sheriffs' accounts on a chequered cloth and recorded them on the pipe rolls, and the chancery, the writing office that issued writs and charters under the great seal. Under John the chancery began keeping copies of what it sent out — the charter rolls from 1199, letters patent from 1201, letters close from 1204 — so orders could be checked and enforced. John himself governed by being everywhere, travelling constantly with his household and hearing cases in person. A top-level answer uses this contrast — a kingdom governed for a decade by deputies, then by a king at everyone's elbow — to argue about whether royal government was becoming stronger, or simply more intrusive."},{"h":"Where the money came from","code":"B2-1 — 3 Royal government and finances: Royal revenues","t":"Royal income divided into what came in every year and what had to be argued for. The ordinary revenue was the royal demesne, the king's own estates, together with the county farm: each sheriff owed the exchequer a fixed sum for his shire and kept what he could collect above it, which made the office worth buying and made sheriffs unpopular. To it were added the profits of justice, the profits of the royal forest — perhaps a quarter of England, where special forest courts fined men for taking the king's deer or his wood or for clearing land — and the feudal incidents: relief paid by an heir to enter his inheritance, wardship and marriage of heirs who were under age, and escheat when a tenant died without heirs. John used these hard: Nicholas de Stuteville was charged 10,000 marks in 1205 to inherit his brother's lands, and in 1214 Geoffrey de Mandeville promised 20,000 marks for the hand of Isabella of Gloucester. Beyond that lay scutage, a payment in place of knight service, levied about eleven times in John's seventeen years against three or four times in Richard's ten; tallage on the towns, the demesne and the Jews; a carucage on ploughland in 1194, 1198 and 1200; and, in 1207, a tax of a thirteenth on everyone's moveable goods and income, imposed by John after a council at Oxford had refused him a grant from the clergy and justified in the writ as taken by the common counsel of his council, which brought in roughly £60,000, far more than a normal year's ordinary revenue. A top-level answer links the money to the politics: every one of these devices was legal, and it was the scale and the frequency, not the invention of new taxes, that turned barons into rebels."},{"h":"The village: open fields, labour service and the manor court","code":"B2-1 — 4 English society: Countryside","t":"About nine people in ten lived in the countryside, in villages of a few hundred at most, and the unit that governed their working lives was the manor. The arable was farmed in two or three great open fields divided into strips, with one field left fallow each year and beasts grazed on the stubble and the common; wheat and rye went for bread, barley for ale, oats for horses and pottage. A villein was unfree: he held his strips in return for week work on the lord's demesne and boon work at harvest, could not leave the manor, paid merchet to marry off a daughter and heriot — usually the best beast — when he died, and answered in the lord's manor court rather than the king's. Freemen in the same village paid rent instead, and cottars with an acre or two worked for wages. Everyone paid the tithe. It was not a frozen world: the population was growing, woodland and waste were being cleared for new fields, and more and more lords were letting villeins buy their way out of labour service for a money rent, which is one reason the crown could tax moveables in 1207 at all. A top-level answer uses the village to test claims about change: the routine of the fields looks unaltered between 1189 and 1216, but the spread of money rents, assarting and the reach of royal taxation into the peasant's own goods show where the change really was."},{"h":"Towns: charters, guilds and the wool that paid for them","code":"B2-1 — 4 English society: Towns","t":"Towns were small by later standards — London had perhaps twenty to thirty thousand people, and York, Norwich, Lincoln and Bristol a few thousand each — but they did the work the countryside could not. They held the markets and the great fairs, at Boston, St Ives and Winchester among others, where wool was sold; they housed the craftsmen, the merchants and the moneylenders; and they paid a disproportionate share of the king's taxes. A town bought its liberties by charter, and Richard and John sold charters freely because they needed cash. A charter might grant the right to hold a market, to have a borough court, to elect officials and, most valuable of all, to pay a fixed fee farm straight to the exchequer instead of through the sheriff; many charters also held that a villein who lived in the town unclaimed for a year and a day became free. London won recognition as a commune in 1191, during the crisis over Longchamp, and Henry FitzAilwin served as its first mayor until his death in 1212. Inside the walls, merchant and craft guilds set prices, standards and apprenticeships; the streets were narrow, timber-built and dangerous, as the fire that destroyed the area round London Bridge in 1212 showed. England's wealth rested on wool, most of it exported raw to the weavers of Flanders, and the Cistercian abbeys of Yorkshire were among the biggest producers — which is why a year's wool crop could be seized in 1193 to help pay Richard's ransom. A top-level answer uses towns as evidence about royal power as well as about the economy: self-government was not given to towns, it was sold to them by kings who were short of money."},{"h":"The king's Jews: protection, profit and the pogroms of 1189–90","code":"B2-1 — 4 English society: Jews in Medieval England","t":"England's Jewish communities, settled in London, York, Lincoln, Norwich and other towns since the Norman Conquest, lived outside the feudal pyramid and directly under the king. In law they and their goods were treated as the king's own: they could not hold land by feudal tenure, they depended on royal protection, and the crown could tax them at will and claim the debts owed to a Jewish lender when he died. Because the Church forbade Christians to lend at interest, Jews took up moneylending, which made them indispensable to barons, abbeys and the crown and detested by the people who owed them money. Antisemitism was already old — the first accusation that Jews had murdered a Christian child had been made at Norwich in 1144 — and it broke out at Richard's coronation on 3 September 1189, when Jewish leaders bringing gifts were driven from Westminster, a rumour spread that the king had ordered a massacre, and about thirty Jews were killed in London. Early in 1190 attacks followed at King's Lynn, then at Norwich on 6 February and at Stamford on 7 March, and worst of all at York, where on the night of 16 to 17 March 1190 around 150 people besieged in the royal castle killed themselves or were murdered after surrendering, and the mob burned the records of their debts in the Minster; two days after York, on 18 March, the Jews of Bury St Edmunds were killed. Royal protection was worth something — Richard punished some of the London rioters and in 1194 ordered that Jewish loans be registered in chests, the archae, so that debts could not be destroyed again — but it was protection with a price: John demanded a tallage of about 66,000 marks from the Jews in 1210 and imprisoned those who would not pay. A top-level answer keeps these two facts in one sentence: the king protected the Jewish communities because they were profitable to him, so their safety rose and fell with his need for money and with his ability to keep order."}],"examples":[{"title":"One feature in each part: the two-part describe question, built so that both halves of each mark score","setup":"The question is printed in two parts: 1(a) Describe one feature of the office of coroner in England in the years 1189–1216. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the tithe in England in the years 1189–1216. [2 marks] There are no bullet points and no sources. From your notes you have: in 1194 Hubert Walter ordered that four men in every county — three knights and a clerk — should keep the pleas of the crown, which is where the coroner begins; the pleas of the crown were the cases reserved to the king's justices, so a killing, a sudden death or a find of treasure had to be recorded and kept for them; the men chosen were local knights elected in the county court, not paid officials, and their record gave the visiting justices something that did not depend on the sheriff; the tithe was a tenth of the year's produce owed by every household to the parish church, good harvest or bad; it was paid in kind, in grain, lambs and cheeses, and stored in the parish barn; a household that refused could be summoned before a church court. Take one feature for each part and give each its supporting information.","steps":["Treat the two parts as two answers: each is marked on its own, so a brilliant extra feature of the coroner's work cannot make up for an empty part (b), and nothing written under (a) can be credited under (b).","Part (a), stated as a fact about the period: the coroner kept a written record of the crown's own cases for the king's justices. Supporting information: from 1194 four men in every county — three knights and a clerk — were elected to keep the pleas of the crown until the justices came.","Part (b), on a separate aspect of content: the tithe took a tenth of what a household produced for the parish church. Supporting information: it was owed whether the harvest was good or bad, paid in kind in grain, lambs and cheeses, and a household that refused could be summoned before a church court.","Test both parts before you stop: each names something true of the whole period rather than one incident, each carries one hard detail instead of a second general sentence, and the two are about different things. Add nothing else — a second feature in either part earns you nothing."],"cues":["Why can these two parts not be answered as one paragraph?","Who kept the pleas of the crown in a county, and from what year?","What share of a household's year did the tithe take, and what happened if it was not paid?","What has to be true of both parts before you put your pen down?"]},{"title":"Finding the third reason: the 12-mark question and the point beyond the bullets","setup":"The question is: Explain why the royal forest was resented in England in the years 1189–1216. You may use the following in your answer: forest law; the forest eyre. You must also use information of your own. From your notes: the royal forest covered perhaps a quarter of England and took in farmland and whole villages, not only woodland; forest law protected the venison, the king's deer, and the vert, the wood and cover that fed them, and men were fined for poaching, for taking timber and for assarting, that is clearing land to farm; a mastiff kept inside the forest had to be lawed, three claws struck from one forefoot, so that it could not run down a deer; the treasurer Richard FitzNigel wrote in Henry II's reign that the forest had its own law, resting not on the common law of the realm but on the decision of the king; Hugh de Neville was chief forester from 1198, his forest assize of that year was called oppressive by the chronicler Roger of Howden, and the forest eyre of 1198 brought in £1,980; Neville reckoned in 1208 that the forests had raised £15,000 in the previous six and a half years; and in 1204 the men of Devon paid 5,000 marks to have the county freed from forest law except for Dartmoor and Exmoor. Build the three reasons the answer needs.","steps":["Fix the conceptual focus first: the question asks why the forest was resented, so every paragraph must end on a reason for the resentment, not on a description of what forest law protected.","Reason from the first bullet: forest law punished men for ordinary work on land they lived on. Assarting — clearing ground to farm — was an offence, timber could not be taken freely, and even a watchdog kept in the forest had to be lawed, so a man could be fined for improving his own holding.","Reason from the second bullet: the forest eyre made the law pay. The eyre of 1198 brought in £1,980 and the chief forester reckoned in 1208 that the forests had raised £15,000 in six and a half years, so men were amerced in a court that answered to the king rather than to the common law — as the treasurer Richard FitzNigel had said outright in Henry II's reign.","Reason of your own, which the bullets do not prompt: the sheer extent of it, and the fact that relief had to be bought. The forest reached perhaps a quarter of England, and in 1204 the men of Devon paid 5,000 marks to be freed from it outside Dartmoor and Exmoor — a county does not raise a sum like that to escape something it merely dislikes.","Link the three in one closing sentence: the law criminalised everyday work, the eyre turned the offences into revenue, and the size of the forest meant that whole counties lived under a law made by the king's will alone."],"cues":["What must every paragraph of this answer finish by explaining?","Which ordinary piece of farm work was an offence inside the forest?","What do the figures for 1198 and 1208 show the forest courts were for?","What did Devon pay in 1204, and what does the payment prove?","How do the three reasons fit together in one sentence?"]},{"title":"Choosing the criterion before you write the 16-mark judgement","setup":"The question is: 'By 1216 feudalism in England had become more a way of raising money than a way of raising soldiers.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: knight service; feudal incidents. You must also use information of your own. Your notes contain the servitium debitum, the fixed quota of knights owed for forty days' unpaid service a year, scutage levied about eleven times in John's seventeen years against three or four times in Richard's ten and at three marks the knight's fee in 1214, the 10,000 marks charged to Nicholas de Stuteville in 1205 to enter his brother's inheritance and the 20,000 marks Geoffrey de Mandeville promised in 1214 for the hand of Isabella of Gloucester, but also the armies that crossed to Normandy and to Poitou, the force gathered in Kent in 1213 against a French landing, the garrisons of the royal castles, and forfeiture used against William de Braose after 1208. Decide the criterion your essay will use and set the two sides.","steps":["Notice that 'more … than' is a ranking phrase, so one of the two purposes has to lose the comparison; an essay that only explains what feudal service was cannot answer it.","State the criterion you will apply and keep to it: when the crown called on a tenant-in-chief between 1189 and 1216, what did it usually ask for — the knights, or the money in place of them?","Pile one: evidence that the system was being worked for cash. Scutage was taken about eleven times in seventeen years under John and at three marks the fee in 1214; a relief of 10,000 marks was charged to Nicholas de Stuteville in 1205 simply to inherit; Geoffrey de Mandeville promised 20,000 marks in 1214 for a marriage; wardship put an heir's lands in the king's hands until he came of age.","Pile two: evidence that it was still a service bargain. Knights were still owed and still summoned, men gathered in Kent in 1213 when a French landing was expected, castles had to be garrisoned by tenants who owed the duty, and a tenant who failed his lord could still be judged to have broken the bargain and lose everything, as William de Braose did after 1208.","Answer the comparison rather than splitting it: argue that the money was itself raised to put soldiers in the field, because forty days of unpaid service could not win a campaign in Normandy or Poitou while scutage could pay men who stayed all year — then say which of the two purposes explains the other."],"cues":["What does 'more … than' oblige you to do that 'describe' would not?","What single test will you apply in every paragraph?","Which figures show the crown treating feudal rights as a source of cash?","What shows that service was still genuinely owed and genuinely wanted?","How can the two piles be joined instead of simply balanced?"]},{"title":"Turning a vague sentence into Level 4 evidence","setup":"A student has written this sentence in an answer on John's government: 'John was always asking the barons for money, which made them angry.' The claim is true but nothing in it is precise. From your notes you can call on: scutage, the payment made instead of serving with knights, demanded about eleven times in John's seventeen-year reign against three or four times in Richard's ten; a relief of 10,000 marks charged to Nicholas de Stuteville in 1205 to enter his brother's inheritance; a promise of 20,000 marks made by Geoffrey de Mandeville in 1214 for the hand of Isabella of Gloucester; and the fact that a mark was two-thirds of a pound. Rewrite the sentence so that an examiner can see the knowledge as well as the argument.","steps":["Diagnose what is missing: no device is named, no figure is given, and no reason is offered for why any of it angered a baron rather than simply costing him.","Name the device and the frequency: John demanded scutage about eleven times in seventeen years, where Richard had demanded it three or four times in ten, so a payment meant for an occasional campaign became something close to a regular tax.","Add the cases where the charge fell on one man: Nicholas de Stuteville was asked 10,000 marks in 1205 simply to inherit, and Geoffrey de Mandeville promised 20,000 marks in 1214 for a marriage, sums that could ruin the family that owed them.","Finish with the analysis the evidence now supports: none of these demands was illegal, which is exactly why they bred resentment — the king was using his undoubted feudal rights at a scale that made a baron's inheritance depend on the king's goodwill."],"cues":["What three things does the original sentence fail to supply?","Which single word names what John was actually demanding, and how often?","Which two cases show the demand landing on a named individual?","Why does 'it was all perfectly legal' strengthen rather than weaken the point?"]},{"title":"Writing the paragraph against your own argument, and then judging","setup":"The question is: 'Towns offered ordinary English people more opportunity than the countryside in the years 1189–1216.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: guilds; villeinage. You must also use information of your own. You have already written two paragraphs agreeing: a borough charter could buy a market, a borough court, elected officials and a fixed fee farm paid straight to the exchequer instead of through the sheriff, and many boroughs held that a villein who lived in the town unclaimed for a year and a day became free. Now write the paragraph that tests the claim, using the countryside, and then the judgement. Your notes hold: about nine people in ten lived in villages of at most a few hundred; a villein owed week work of two or three days on the demesne and boon work at harvest, paid merchet to marry off a daughter and heriot at death, and answered in the manor court; the population was growing, woodland and waste were being cleared for new fields, and more lords were taking money rents in place of labour service; London had perhaps twenty to thirty thousand people, while York, Norwich, Lincoln and Bristol had a few thousand each.","steps":["Make the counter-case on the same ground as the claim, which is opportunity: for nine people in ten any chance of bettering themselves had to be found in the village, because that is where they and the land were.","Give the counter-case its evidence: the population was rising, woodland and waste were being cleared for new fields, and more lords were taking a money rent in place of week work, so a family could be released from labour service without ever leaving the manor, while free tenants already held for rent.","Use the size of the towns as the test of the claim rather than as another example of town life: London had perhaps twenty to thirty thousand people and the next towns a few thousand, so however good the year-and-a-day rule was for the man who used it, it was open to very few — and inside the town the guilds that fixed prices, standards and apprenticeship also decided who was allowed to trade at all.","Judge on a stated criterion: measured by how far one person could rise, the town offered what no manor could, since it could turn a villein into a free burgess; measured by how many people any of it could reach, the countryside still held nearly everyone's future — so the answer turns on which kind of opportunity the question means, and you must say which and why."],"cues":["Where did nine people in ten have to look for their chances?","What was changing in the countryside that could improve a villein's terms?","How many people could the towns actually take, and who controlled trade inside them?","On what criterion can you decide between an opportunity that is rare and one that is common?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What did a tenant-in-chief owe the king in return for his fief, and how did he enter the bargain?","a":"A fixed quota of knights, the servitium debitum, normally for forty days' unpaid service a year. He entered it by doing homage, kneeling with his hands between the king's, and swearing fealty."},{"q":"How did a sheriff make money out of his shire?","a":"He owed the exchequer a fixed sum for the shire, the county farm, and kept whatever he could collect above it — which is why the office was worth buying and why sheriffs were resented."},{"q":"What tax did John impose in 1207, and how did he obtain it?","a":"A thirteenth of everyone's moveable goods and income. A great council at Oxford refused him a grant from the clergy, so he imposed the tax himself, the writ saying it was taken by the common counsel of his council; it raised roughly £60,000."},{"q":"What did a borough charter typically buy a town?","a":"The right to hold a market, to have its own court, to elect officials and to pay a fixed fee farm straight to the exchequer instead of through the sheriff; many also freed a villein who lived there unclaimed for a year and a day."}]},"room":{"facts":["Booklet B, the British depth study, carries 32 marks: Questions 1(a) and 1(b), 'Describe one feature of...', 2 marks each and on two different aspects of content, Question 2, an 'Explain why...' at 12 marks with two stimulus points, and a choice of two 16-mark questions, Question 3 or Question 4, \"'...' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\", also with two stimulus points. There are no spelling, punctuation and grammar marks anywhere on Paper 2.","The describe task is point-marked, not levelled: one mark for each valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about it, four marks in all. From June 2025 it is printed as two separate 2-mark parts, each asking for one feature on a different aspect of content. No reasons and no judgement are wanted, and an extra feature earns nothing.","The caps as the mark scheme now prints them: on the 12-mark question, a maximum of 8 marks and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; on the 16-mark question, a maximum of 11 marks and no access to Level 4 on the same condition. Two stimulus bullets are two aspects, so an answer built only on them cannot pass those ceilings.","Timing in booklet B is about a minute a mark: roughly 5 minutes on the describe task, 20 on the 12-mark question and 25 on the 16-mark, with the planning time for the judgement taken out of that 25.","The dates that anchor this key topic: 6 July 1189, Richard I succeeds Henry II; 3 September 1189, his coronation and the London riot; 16–17 March 1190, the massacre at York; 1193–94, the ransom of 150,000 marks; 6 April 1199, Richard dies at Châlus; 27 May 1199, John is crowned; 1 August 1202, Arthur captured at Mirebeau; 1207, the thirteenth imposed after the council at Oxford; 24 March 1208, the interdict; May 1213, John submits to Innocent III."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the feudal system in England in the years 1189–1216. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the coronation of an English king in the years 1189–1216. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked as two separate 2-mark parts: in each part one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about it, and a second feature within a part earns nothing. (a) One feature: land was held in return for military service; credit also homage and fealty, subinfeudation, labour service by villeins, or forfeiture for breach of the bargain. (b) One feature: the new king was anointed with holy oil; credit also the coronation oath, the crowning and the regalia at Westminster Abbey, or the homage of the tenants-in-chief.","sol":"1(a) Feature: land was held in return for military service (1 mark). Supporting information: a tenant-in-chief — a great baron, bishop or abbot — held his fief directly from the king and owed him an agreed number of knights, the servitium debitum, normally for forty days' unpaid service in the year (1 mark). 1(b) Feature: a new king swore the coronation oath and was then anointed (1 mark). Supporting information: at Westminster Abbey he promised to protect the Church, to keep the peace and put down wrongdoing and to do justice with mercy, and was anointed with holy oil by the Archbishop of Canterbury before being crowned and given the regalia, as Richard I was on 3 September 1189 (1 mark).","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Read both parts before you start: (a) is about how land was held, (b) about the ceremony that made a man king.","Features of a system are its rules, not its people: for (a) take one rule that held between a lord and the man below him; for (b) take one promise the king made or one thing done to him.","One short paragraph for each part: name the feature in the first sentence and add one precise detail about how it worked — a second feature in either part earns nothing."],"codes":["B2-1 — 1 The feudal system: The feudal hierarchy and the nature of feudalism","B2-1 — 2 Kingship and succession: The nature of kingship"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the position of Jews in England in the years 1189–1216. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the exchequer in the years 1189–1216. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked as two separate 2-mark parts: in each part one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about it, and a second feature within a part earns nothing. (a) One feature: the Jews were the king's property and lived under his direct protection; credit also exclusion from feudal landholding, moneylending, arbitrary tallage, or registration of loans in the archae after 1194. (b) One feature: the exchequer audited the sheriffs' accounts twice a year; credit also its seat at Westminster, the chequered cloth that gave it its name, or the pipe rolls on which the results were written up.","sol":"1(a) Feature: in law Jews lived directly under the king rather than inside the feudal pyramid (1 mark). Supporting information: they and their goods were treated as the king's own property, so they could not hold land by feudal tenure and could be taxed at his will — John demanded a tallage of about 66,000 marks from the Jewish communities in 1210 (1 mark). 1(b) Feature: the exchequer called the king's officers to account for what they owed him (1 mark). Supporting information: it sat at Westminster and audited the sheriffs twice a year, at Easter and Michaelmas, reckoning the sums on a chequered cloth and entering the results on the pipe rolls (1 mark).","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a) asks who Jews in England owed obedience to and what that relationship was worth to the king; part (b) asks what happened to the king's money once the shires had collected it.","For (a) take legal status rather than occupation; for (b) name the department's routine — when it sat and whom it called to account.","One short paragraph each: name the status or the routine first, then attach a precise figure, date or record to it."],"codes":["B2-1 — 4 English society: Jews in Medieval England","B2-1 — 3 Royal government and finances: How England was governed"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of peasant life in the English countryside in the years 1189–1216. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of Richard I's reign in England in the years 1189–99. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked as two separate 2-mark parts: in each part one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about it, and a second feature within a part earns nothing. (a) One feature: the arable was farmed in common in open fields; credit also labour service on the demesne, villein status with merchet and heriot, the manor court, tithes, or the spread of money rents. (b) One feature: Richard was almost always out of the kingdom; credit also his claim as the eldest surviving son of Henry II, the sale of offices and charters for the crusade, his capture and ransom, or his death at Châlus.","sol":"1(a) Feature: the arable was farmed in common in open fields (1 mark). Supporting information: a village worked two or three great fields divided into strips, with one field left fallow each year to recover while the animals grazed the stubble and the common (1 mark). 1(b) Feature: Richard was an absent king (1 mark). Supporting information: he left England in December 1189 for the Third Crusade and, apart from about two months in the spring of 1194, never returned, spending roughly six months of his ten-year reign in the country (1 mark).","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a): picture the fields first and the household second — how was the land actually laid out and shared? Part (b): ask where Richard actually was for most of his reign.","For (a) one rule of farming or one duty a villein owed his lord will do; for (b) one plain fact about how he ruled, not the story of the crusade.","Two short paragraphs: give each feature one concrete detail — a number of fields or of days for (a), a date or a length of time for (b)."],"codes":["B2-1 — 4 English society: Countryside","B2-1 — 2 Kingship and succession: Richard I as king"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of English towns in the years 1189–1216. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the ways King John raised money in the years 1199–1216. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked as two separate 2-mark parts: in each part one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about it, and a second feature within a part earns nothing. (a) One feature: towns bought their liberties from the crown by charter; credit also the fee farm, elected officials and borough courts, guilds, markets and fairs, the wool trade, or freedom after a year and a day. (b) One feature: scutage, the payment made instead of serving with the knights owed; credit also the feudal incidents of relief, wardship and marriage, tallage taken at will, the profits of the forest, or the thirteenth on moveables imposed in 1207 after the council at Oxford.","sol":"1(a) Feature: towns bought their liberties from the crown by charter (1 mark). Supporting information: a charter might grant a market, a borough court, elected officials and the right to pay a fixed fee farm straight to the exchequer instead of through the sheriff, and Richard and John sold such charters freely because they needed the cash (1 mark). 1(b) Feature: John took scutage, the money payment made instead of serving with the knights a tenant owed (1 mark). Supporting information: he levied it about eleven times in seventeen years, against three or four times in Richard's ten and at higher rates, so a charge meant for an occasional campaign became something close to a regular tax (1 mark).","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a): ask what a town had that a village did not, in law and in the way its trade was organised. Part (b): ask what John could demand without asking anyone's consent.","For (a) self-government or the control of business will both work; for (b) name one device — a feudal due, a tax, or a payment made instead of service.","Name the thing, then add the detail that proves it: what a charter actually granted, and how often or how heavily John's demand fell."],"codes":["B2-1 — 4 English society: Towns","B2-1 — 3 Royal government and finances: Royal revenues"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the royal forest in England in the years 1189–1216. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the work of a sheriff in the years 1189–1216. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked as two separate 2-mark parts: in each part one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about it, and a second feature within a part earns nothing. (a) One feature: the forest was a legal area rather than woodland; credit also forest law with its own courts and officers, the offences of venison and vert, assarting, or fines and licences as royal revenue. (b) One feature: the sheriff owed the exchequer a fixed county farm; credit also his charge of the royal castles, the shire court, the execution of writs, or John's use of foreign-born sheriffs.","sol":"1(a) Feature: the forest was a legal area, not simply woodland (1 mark). Supporting information: perhaps a quarter of England lay under forest law, which took in farmland and whole villages whose inhabitants lived under rules that did not apply elsewhere (1 mark). 1(b) Feature: the sheriff was the king's officer in the shire and answered to him for its revenues (1 mark). Supporting information: he held the royal castles, ran the shire court and executed writs, and he owed the exchequer a fixed sum for his county, the county farm, keeping whatever he could collect above it — which is why the office was worth buying and why sheriffs were widely resented (1 mark).","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["In part (a) the word forest is a legal word before it is a description of trees; in part (b) think about what one man had to hand over to the exchequer every year.","For (a) take either what the forest covered or how it was policed; for (b) take either what the sheriff did in the shire or what he owed for the privilege.","Attach one precise detail to each: how much of England lay under forest law, and what the county farm let a sheriff keep."],"codes":["B2-1 — 3 Royal government and finances: Royal revenues","B2-1 — 3 Royal government and finances: How England was governed"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the Church in England in the years 1189–1216. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of John's rule as king in the years 1199–1216. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked as two separate 2-mark parts: in each part one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about it, and a second feature within a part earns nothing. (a) One feature: the Church was one of the greatest landowners in England; credit also the parish and the sacraments, tithes, church courts, churchmen in royal government, or vacant sees taken into the king's hands. (b) One feature: John governed in person and was constantly on the move; credit also his contested claim against Arthur of Brittany, the loss of Normandy by 1204, the quarrel with Innocent III, or the weight of his financial demands on the barons.","sol":"1(a) Feature: the Church was one of the greatest landowners in England (1 mark). Supporting information: bishops and abbots held their estates as tenants-in-chief and owed knight service like any baron, and while a bishopric or abbey stood vacant its revenues went to the crown (1 mark). 1(b) Feature: unlike Richard, John ruled his kingdom in person (1 mark). Supporting information: he travelled the country constantly with his household and heard pleas himself, and his chancery began keeping copies of the orders he sent out — charter rolls from 1199, letters patent from 1201 and letters close from 1204 (1 mark).","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a): think about the Church as a property owner as well as a place of worship. Part (b): ask what was different about John's kingship the moment you set it beside Richard's.","For (a) take one feature about what the Church owned or took, or one about what it gave ordinary people; for (b) one plain fact about how he came to the throne or how he governed.","Give each feature a hard detail — what a bishop owed the king, and a date or a record that shows how John ruled."],"codes":["B2-1 — 1 The feudal system: The role and influence of the Church","B2-1 — 2 Kingship and succession: John as king"]},{"q":"Explain why Richard I was able to raise so much money from England in the years 1189–94. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the crusade • the ransom. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 / AO1 6. Credit the sale of offices, charters and rights in 1189–90, the crusade as a cause men would pay for, the levy for the 150,000-mark ransom of 1193–94, the Quitclaim of Canterbury, and above all the inherited machinery of exchequer, sheriffs and county farms that made collection possible. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"Richard could take so much from England so quickly because a well-tried machine was already in place, and because he had two causes that made extraordinary demands look legitimate. The first reason is the crusade itself. Richard succeeded on 6 July 1189 and was crowned on 3 September, and he treated the kingdom as a war chest from the start: sheriffdoms, offices, charters of liberties to towns and even rights over other rulers were sold, and in December 1189 he released William the Lion of Scotland from his submission to Henry II for 10,000 marks. A chronicler reports his joke that he would have sold London if he could have found a buyer. Men paid because a crusade was the highest religious duty a king could claim, and because what they bought — an office, a charter — was worth having. The second reason is the ransom. Richard was seized on his way home at the end of 1192 and held in Germany, and the sum demanded was 150,000 marks, about £100,000, several times a year's ordinary royal revenue. Hubert Walter, justiciar from 1193, levied a quarter of every man's income and moveable goods, took church plate and seized the Cistercian houses' wool crop, and enough was raised to free the king in February 1194. A ransom for an anointed king in prison was very hard to refuse. The third reason goes beyond the bullets and explains the other two: Henry II had left a government that could actually collect. Sheriffs answered at the exchequer twice a year for the fixed county farm of their shires, the pipe rolls recorded what was owed, and the justiciar could act with royal authority in the king's absence. Without that apparatus, neither the crusade sales nor the ransom levy could have been turned into silver. Together they show why England was worth having: the cause supplied the justification, and the machinery supplied the money.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Two different demands were made in these five years, and each had to be made to look justified before it could be collected.","Sort your material into the reasons men agreed to pay and the arrangements that got the money to Westminster.","Take three reasons in turn, and end each paragraph by saying what that factor contributed that the others could not."],"codes":["B2-1 — 2 Kingship and succession: Richard I as king","B2-1 — 3 Royal government and finances: Royal revenues","B2-1 — 3 Royal government and finances: How England was governed"]},{"q":"Explain why the Jewish communities of England were attacked in the years 1189–90. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the coronation of Richard I • moneylending. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 / AO1 6. Credit the coronation of 3 September 1189 and the London riot as trigger, debt as motive with the burning of the bonds at York, crusading fervour and older religious hostility, and the absence of the king with the breakdown of local order in 1190. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"The attacks happened because an old hostility was given a trigger, a motive and an opportunity within a few months of each other. The trigger was the coronation. On 3 September 1189 leading Jews came to Westminster with gifts for the new king and were driven away and beaten at the doors; a rumour ran through London that Richard had ordered the Jews to be destroyed, and in the rioting that followed about thirty were killed, among them the scholar Jacob of Orléans. What made this so dangerous was that the crowd believed it was doing what a newly anointed king wanted, so an attack felt like obedience. The second reason is debt. Because the Church forbade Christians to lend at interest, Jewish families had become the lenders to barons, knights and abbeys, and their bonds were enforceable in the king's courts. Killing the creditor and destroying the record cancelled the debt, which is exactly what happened at York: after the community was destroyed on the night of 16 to 17 March 1190, the mob went straight to the Minster and burned the bonds kept there, and among the ringleaders was Richard Malebisse, himself a debtor. The third reason, which the bullets do not prompt, is the collapse of authority. Richard left England in December 1189 to lead the Third Crusade, and the men he left behind were already quarrelling among themselves; crusading preparation had also raised religious excitement to a pitch. In February and March 1190 attacks spread without any effective check from King's Lynn to Norwich on 6 February and Stamford on 7 March, and on to Bury St Edmunds on 18 March, two days after York itself, where the sheriff's men were unable or unwilling to protect a community sheltering in the king's own castle. Religious hatred supplied the language, debt supplied the particular men their reason, and an absent king supplied the chance to act on both.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Separate the thing that set the violence off from the things that made men want it in the first place.","Ask in each case who gained: what did a debtor, a crusader and a rioting crowd each get out of an attack?","Three reasons, each ending on why that factor made an attack more likely, then one sentence tying them together."],"codes":["B2-1 — 4 English society: Jews in Medieval England","B2-1 — 2 Kingship and succession: Richard I as king"]},{"q":"Explain why England could still be governed while Richard I was abroad in the years 1189–99. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the justiciar • the exchequer. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 / AO1 6. Credit the justiciar ruling in the king's name (Longchamp, Walter of Coutances, Hubert Walter, Geoffrey FitzPeter), the exchequer's twice-yearly audit of sheriffs and the pipe rolls, the chancery and the great seal, the sheriffs and shire courts, and the authority of an anointed king that survived his absence. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"England kept working for a decade with a king who spent only months in it because royal authority had already been detached from the king's physical presence. The first reason is the office of justiciar. Richard made William Longchamp, Bishop of Ely, his chancellor in September 1189 and chief justiciar in March 1190, with power to act in the king's name; when Longchamp quarrelled with John and the barons and was forced out in 1191 he was replaced by Walter of Coutances, Archbishop of Rouen, and then in 1193 by Hubert Walter, who was also Archbishop of Canterbury and who raised the ransom. The office survived the men who held it, which is the point: government did not stop when a minister fell. The second reason is the exchequer. Twice a year, at Easter and Michaelmas, the sheriffs came to Westminster to account for the county farm owed by their shires; sums were reckoned on a chequered cloth and written up on the pipe rolls, so that what every sheriff owed was on record and could be demanded again next year. An institution that audits accounts does not need a king to be standing over it. The third reason goes beyond the bullets. Royal justice reached into the shires through the sheriffs and the visits of the king's itinerant justices, and Hubert Walter extended it: in 1194 he ordered that in every county four men should keep the pleas of the crown, the beginning of the coroner, so that the king's rights in cases of violent death and treasure were recorded even when no judge was near. Behind all of this stood the fact that Richard was an anointed king; orders sealed with his seal, or issued in his name by his justiciar, carried his authority across the Channel. The system had its limits — Longchamp's fall showed that a justiciar without the barons' goodwill could not hold on — but its offices, records and courts meant that England was governed by a machine, and the machine went on running.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Authority had to travel somehow: think about who could act for the king and what proved that they were acting for him.","Group your evidence into the man at the top, the department that handled money, and the way justice reached the shires.","Take three reasons in turn and finish each by saying what it let government do without the king in the country."],"codes":["B2-1 — 3 Royal government and finances: How England was governed","B2-1 — 2 Kingship and succession: The nature of kingship","B2-1 — 3 Royal government and finances: Royal revenues"]},{"q":"Explain why John quarrelled with the Church in the years 1205–13. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • Stephen Langton • church lands. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 / AO1 6. Credit the disputed Canterbury election after Hubert Walter's death in 1205, John's insistence on John de Gray, Innocent III's imposition of Langton in 1207, the interdict of 24 March 1208, the excommunication of 1209, the profits of seized church lands, and John's submission in 1213. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"The quarrel began over a single appointment, hardened because both men believed they were defending a right, and lasted as long as it did because it paid John to let it. The first reason is the disputed election at Canterbury. Hubert Walter, Archbishop of Canterbury and John's chancellor, died in 1205, and the monks of Christ Church secretly elected their own candidate while John pressed his own man, John de Gray, Bishop of Norwich. Pope Innocent III set both aside and had Stephen Langton, an English cardinal in Rome, elected in December 1206 and consecrated in June 1207. The second reason is what the archbishopric meant to a king. Canterbury was the richest see in England, its holder sat at the centre of royal government, and every king since the Conquest had expected to choose him; accepting a man appointed in Rome, whom John did not know and had not approved, looked like surrendering a right of the crown, so John refused Langton entry and drove out the Canterbury monks. Innocent answered with an interdict on 24 March 1208 — public services stopped across England — and excommunicated John in 1209. The third reason, beyond the bullets, is that the quarrel was profitable, which removed John's incentive to settle. With the clergy in dispute he took the lands and revenues of bishoprics and abbeys into his own hands and kept sees vacant, drawing in very large sums year after year at a time when he was trying to raise an army to recover Normandy. He only gave way in May 1213, when Innocent was encouraging Philip II to invade and John's own barons were unreliable: he accepted Langton, surrendered England to the pope and received it back as a papal fief for a tribute of 1,000 marks a year. The quarrel therefore ran on the collision of two claims to appoint, and was prolonged by the money John made while it lasted.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Start from the vacancy that caused it: who had the right to say who became Archbishop of Canterbury?","Ask separately why the quarrel began and why it went on for years — those are not the same reason.","Three reasons, each closing on why John would not give way at that stage, then the circumstances that finally changed his mind."],"codes":["B2-1 — 1 The feudal system: The role and influence of the Church","B2-1 — 2 Kingship and succession: John as king","B2-1 — 3 Royal government and finances: Royal revenues"]},{"q":"Explain why towns became more important in England in the years 1189–1216. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • charters • the wool trade. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 / AO1 6. Credit the sale of borough charters by kings needing cash, the fee farm and self-government, the wool export to Flanders and the great fairs, guilds, the growth of population and a money economy, and the towns' value to the crown as taxpayers. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"Towns mattered more by 1216 than in 1189 because kings who needed money sold them privileges, because wool made them rich, and because a growing population using money rather than labour needed places to buy and sell. The first reason is the charter. Richard was raising money for the crusade and the ransom and John for war in France, and a borough charter was something a town would pay well for: the right to hold a market, to have its own court, to elect its officials and above all to pay a fixed fee farm straight to the exchequer instead of being squeezed by the sheriff. London gained recognition as a commune in 1191 during the crisis over Longchamp, and Henry FitzAilwin served as its first mayor until his death in 1212. Self-government made a town more attractive to settle in, and it made the townsmen a group the crown had to deal with directly. The second reason is wool. England's chief export was raw wool, shipped to the weavers of Flanders, and the great fairs at Boston, St Ives and Winchester drew foreign buyers; the Cistercian abbeys of Yorkshire produced on a scale that meant a whole year's clip could be seized in 1193 towards Richard's ransom. Ports and fair towns grew on the profits, and guilds of merchants and craftsmen organised the trade. The third reason is not prompted by the bullets: the countryside itself was changing. The population was growing, land was being cleared, and lords were increasingly taking money rents instead of labour service, so peasants needed markets at which to sell produce and earn coin. Many charters also promised that a villein who lived in a town unclaimed for a year and a day became free, which drew people in. The three reinforced one another: trade made towns worth taxing, taxation made kings willing to sell liberties, and liberty and coin together drew in the people who made the towns grow.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask why a king would ever hand a town the right to govern itself.","Trade, law and population are three different kinds of reason — make sure you have one of each.","Three reasons in turn, each ending on what it added to a town that had not been there before, then link them."],"codes":["B2-1 — 4 English society: Towns","B2-1 — 3 Royal government and finances: Royal revenues","B2-1 — 4 English society: Countryside"]},{"q":"'Richard I's position as king in 1189 was far more secure than John's in 1199.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • Arthur of Brittany • the coronation. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 / AO1 6. Credit the difference in title (Richard as undisputed eldest surviving son against John's contested claim, Arthur of Brittany and Philip II, the loss of Anjou, Maine and Touraine, Le Goulet 1200) weighed against the insecurity created by Richard's immediate departure, Longchamp's fall in 1191, John's plotting and the forfeiture of his lands in 1194, the captivity and ransom, and John's advantages of presence, the treasury, Eleanor, Hubert Walter and William Marshal. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"Security has two parts, and they must be separated before the two accessions can be compared: the strength of the title, and the strength of the grip on the kingdom day by day. On title, the statement is plainly right. Richard was the eldest surviving legitimate son of Henry II, his brothers Henry and Geoffrey having died in 1183 and 1186, and when his father died on 6 July 1189 there was no rival candidate at all. He was crowned and anointed at Westminster on 3 September 1189, and the anointing mattered, because after it rebellion against him was not only treason but sacrilege. John in 1199 faced a competitor. Arthur of Brittany, the son of his dead elder brother Geoffrey, was twelve years old and was recognised in Anjou, Maine and Touraine, and he was backed by Philip II of France, who as overlord of the continental lands could give the claim a court to be heard in. John was crowned on 27 May 1199, but he had to buy Philip's recognition in May 1200 by the Treaty of Le Goulet with a relief of 20,000 marks and his own homage, and the problem was only ended by Arthur's capture at Mirebeau on 1 August 1202 and his disappearance in 1203 — which, because men believed John had murdered his nephew, cost him Brittany and Anjou and handed Philip a pretext. On the second test the gap narrows sharply, and this is where the statement overreaches. Richard's undisputed title did not give him a secure kingdom, because he left it. He was in England for about six months of a ten-year reign, and the government he set up broke down within two years: William Longchamp, his chancellor and then chief justiciar, quarrelled with the barons and with John and was driven out in 1191, John intrigued with Philip II while his brother was a prisoner in Germany, and John's English lands had to be declared forfeit in 1194. A king whose ransom cost 150,000 marks and whose brother was conspiring against him was not secure in any ordinary sense. John, by contrast, had real strengths in 1199 beyond his claim: he moved at once to take the Angevin treasury at Chinon and the ducal title at Rouen, Eleanor of Aquitaine held the south for him, Hubert Walter and William Marshal carried the English barons, and unlike Richard he then stayed, governing England in person, travelling constantly and hearing pleas himself. The judgement depends on which kind of insecurity does the more lasting damage. Richard's was serious but temporary, and it was contained by the machinery of justiciar, exchequer and chancery that went on working without him. John's was structural: a rival claim gave every discontented baron in the Angevin lands an alternative, and it drew in the king of France, who was the one enemy John could not out-wait. So the statement holds, but the reason it holds is not the one it implies. Richard's position was more secure not because his reign was calmer — it was not — but because no one could offer a lawful alternative to him, and in a kingdom where kingship rested on inheritance and anointing, that was the difference that could not be bought off.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Two kings came to the throne ten years apart; ask first what \"secure\" would actually mean for each of them.","Split the comparison into the strength of the claim and the strength of the hold on the country, and notice that the two do not point the same way.","Announce the test in your opening and let the conclusion say which kind of insecurity mattered more, rather than restating both reigns."],"codes":["B2-1 — 2 Kingship and succession: Richard I as king","B2-1 — 2 Kingship and succession: John as king","B2-1 — 2 Kingship and succession: The nature of kingship"]},{"q":"'Money was the main concern of royal government in England in the years 1189–1216.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • scutage • the sheriffs. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 / AO1 6. Credit the crusade and ransom levies, scutage and its frequency under John, feudal incidents, the county farm and the sheriffs, the forest and the 1207 thirteenth, weighed against the development of justice (the coroners of 1194, itinerant justices, John hearing pleas), the chancery rolls, and the defence of royal rights against the papacy. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"The criterion has to be which purpose explains the most decisions royal government actually took, not which activity generated the loudest complaints. On that test the case for money is very strong. Between 1189 and 1194 the crown sold sheriffdoms, offices and charters for the crusade and then levied a quarter of every man's income and moveables, stripped churches of plate and seized the Cistercian wool crop to pay a ransom of 150,000 marks. Under John the demands became routine rather than exceptional: scutage, the payment made instead of serving with knights, was taken about eleven times in seventeen years, where Richard had taken it three or four times in ten, and at higher rates. The feudal incidents were pressed to the limit, with Nicholas de Stuteville charged 10,000 marks in 1205 to enter his brother's inheritance and Geoffrey de Mandeville promising 20,000 marks in 1214 to marry Isabella of Gloucester. In 1207, after a council at Oxford refused him a grant from the clergy, John imposed a thirteenth of everyone's moveable goods and income by what the writ called the common counsel of his council, raising roughly £60,000. The sheriffs point the same way: each owed the exchequer a fixed county farm and kept what he could squeeze above it, so the crown's local officers were effectively tax farmers, and John raised the farms and used foreign-born men who depended on him alone. Against the statement stands the fact that government did a great deal that money alone does not explain. In 1194 Hubert Walter ordered that four men in every county should keep the pleas of the crown, which is the origin of the coroner; itinerant justices continued to carry royal justice into the shires; John travelled the country ceaselessly and heard pleas himself, and his chancery began enrolling copies of what it issued — charters from 1199, letters patent from 1201, letters close from 1204 — so that orders could be traced and enforced. The long quarrel with Innocent III from 1205 was not begun for profit but over the right to choose an Archbishop of Canterbury, and John endured an interdict from 24 March 1208 and excommunication in 1209 rather than concede it. The honest answer is that these were not rivals. Justice was itself a source of revenue, since fines, amercements and the profits of the forest courts flowed to the exchequer, and the new records existed partly because a king who knew exactly what was owed could collect it. Equally, the reason money was needed was not greed: it was the defence of the Angevin lands, and after 1204 their recovery. Judged by what drove decisions, money was the immediate and constant preoccupation of royal government and the best single explanation of why sheriffs, courts and records were used as they were. But it was the means to an end, which is why the statement should be accepted with one qualification: royal government was organised around raising money because it was organised around war and the assertion of royal rights, and the machinery built to collect became, in the end, the machinery of a stronger state.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["\"Main concern\" is a ranking, so decide in advance what could count as evidence of a different concern.","Put every piece of evidence in one of two piles — the crown behaving like a collector, the crown behaving like a ruler and judge — then look for the items that belong in both.","Name your criterion in the opening and apply the same one in the conclusion, rather than adding up the paragraphs."],"codes":["B2-1 — 3 Royal government and finances: Royal revenues","B2-1 — 3 Royal government and finances: How England was governed","B2-1 — 2 Kingship and succession: The nature of kingship"]},{"q":"'The Church was the most important influence on the lives of ordinary English people in the years 1189–1216.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the parish • the Interdict of 1208. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 / AO1 6. Credit the parish, the sacraments, holy days and tithes, church courts and the Church as landlord, weighed against the manor and the lord (week work, merchet, heriot, the manor court), the open-field routine and the harvest, and royal demands such as forest law and the 1207 tax; the interdict of 1208–14 used as a test of how much church life actually governed daily life. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"Influence has to be measured before it can be ranked, so this answer uses two tests: what decided how an ordinary person spent a day, and what decided how they understood their own life. The case for the Church is powerful on both. Every village had its parish church, and every important moment passed through it — baptism, marriage, confession, burial in consecrated ground — while the calendar of holy days fixed which days were worked and which were not. The Church also took a direct share of the family's food: the tithe, a tenth of the year's produce, was owed whether the harvest was good or bad. Church courts claimed cases of marriage, wills and morality over lay people as well as clergy, and bishoprics and great abbeys were among the largest landlords in England, so for many peasants the lord who took their labour service was a churchman. Against this stands the manor, and on the first test it wins. It was the lord, not the priest, who decided that a villein owed week work of two or three days on the demesne and boon work at harvest, who took merchet when a daughter married and heriot, usually the best beast, when a tenant died, and whose steward judged the tenants in his own manor court. A villein could not leave the manor without permission. The rhythm of the open fields — strips, fallow, ploughing, harvest — set the working year at least as firmly as the saints' days did. The interdict is the test case, and it cuts both ways. From 24 March 1208 public services stopped across England for six years; the dead could not be buried in churchyards, though baptism and the confession of the dying continued. If the Church had been the whole framework of daily life, England should have stopped; it did not. Fields were ploughed, rents and labour services were owed and collected, manor courts sat, and John used the years to take the revenues of vacant sees into his own hands. Yet the outrage the interdict caused, and the fact that a king who could ignore his barons could not in the end ignore it — he submitted in May 1213, accepted Stephen Langton and held England as a papal fief for 1,000 marks a year — show how deep the hold went. Beyond both stands the crown, which the bullets do not prompt: royal power reached the ordinary household through the sheriff's collection of the county farm, through forest law over perhaps a quarter of England, where a man could be fined for taking wood or clearing his own ground, and through the thirteenth of 1207, which taxed a peasant's own moveable goods. The judgement therefore has to be split by criterion and then decided. Measured by who controlled a person's time, labour and property, the lord of the manor came first and the king was gaining. Measured by what shaped how people understood birth, death, sin and the year itself, the Church had no rival, and because that authority reached everyone, free and unfree alike, in every village in England, the statement holds — provided influence is understood as the framework of belief and obligation rather than as day-to-day command.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Before ranking two influences, decide what kind of influence the question is asking about.","The interdict is a natural experiment: ask what stopped in England after March 1208 and what carried on exactly as before.","Bring in a third influence the bullets do not mention, then make the conclusion choose a criterion and apply it."],"codes":["B2-1 — 1 The feudal system: The role and influence of the Church","B2-1 — 4 English society: Countryside","B2-1 — 3 Royal government and finances: Royal revenues"]},{"q":"'Royal protection was the most important influence on the lives of England's Jews in the years 1189–1216.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the massacre at York (1190) • moneylending. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 / AO1 6. Credit the Jews' legal status as the king's property, the archae of 1194 and the Exchequer of the Jews, punishment of rioters and charters of liberties, weighed against the massacres of 1189–90 when protection failed, the debt-driven hostility of local gentry, religious antisemitism from 1144 onwards, and royal exploitation such as the tallage of about 66,000 marks in 1210. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"Royal protection has to be judged by what it delivered, and the honest criterion is whether it determined outcomes: did the king's interest decide whether Jewish families prospered, survived or were destroyed? On that test the statement is largely right, but not in the way it sounds. In law the Jews of England were the king's own, living directly under his protection outside the feudal hierarchy, unable to hold land by feudal tenure, and dependent on royal writs to enforce their bonds in the king's courts. That protection was real and it produced real policy. After the killings of 1189 and 1190 Richard punished some of the London rioters and, in 1194, ordered that Jewish loans be registered and copies kept in chests, the archae, in designated towns, with a royal department to oversee them — a reform designed precisely so that a mob could not cancel debts by burning them. Against the statement stands the fact that protection failed exactly when it was needed. At the coronation on 3 September 1189 Jews bringing gifts were beaten from the doors of Westminster and about thirty were killed in the riot that followed. Early in 1190 attacks spread to King's Lynn, to Norwich on 6 February and to Stamford on 7 March, and at York on the night of 16 to 17 March around 150 people who had taken refuge in the king's own castle killed themselves or were murdered after surrendering; the mob then burned the bonds stored in the Minster, and several ringleaders, including Richard Malebisse, were debtors. Two days later the community at Bury St Edmunds was destroyed in its turn. A protection that cannot reach a royal castle while the king is abroad is a promise, not a shield. Two other influences shaped these lives as much. The first is moneylending itself. Because the Church forbade Christians to lend at interest, Jewish families became the creditors of barons, knights and abbeys; this gave them wealth and a use to the crown, and it made them the object of a hatred that had a ledger behind it. The second, beyond the bullets, is religious hostility with a long history: the accusation that Jews murdered Christian children had first been made in England at Norwich in 1144, and preaching for the Third Crusade sharpened it in 1189–90. Yet the decisive point is why the king protected them at all, and there the two halves of the argument join. Protection existed because Jewish wealth was, in the end, the king's wealth: he could tallage the communities at will and claim the debts owed to a lender who died, and John demanded about 66,000 marks from them in 1210, imprisoning those who would not pay. So royal protection was indeed the most important influence, because it set the limits within which everything else operated — but it was protection of an asset. When the king was absent or the order broke down, as in 1190, it failed; when he needed money, as in 1210, it turned into exploitation; and both the safety and the ruin of England's Jewish communities followed from the same relationship with the crown.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what the king was actually protecting, and why it was worth his while.","Test the claim at its weakest point: what happened in 1189 and 1190, and what does that show about the value of protection?","Aim for a conclusion that does not simply balance — show how the protection and the exploitation were two sides of one relationship."],"codes":["B2-1 — 4 English society: Jews in Medieval England","B2-1 — 2 Kingship and succession: The nature of kingship","B2-1 — 3 Royal government and finances: Royal revenues"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date was Richard I crowned, and what happened in London that day?","a":"3 September 1189, at Westminster Abbey. Jews bringing gifts were driven from the doors, a rumour spread that the king had ordered a massacre, and about thirty Jews were killed in the rioting."},{"q":"What was scutage?","a":"A money payment made to the king in place of the knight service a tenant-in-chief owed; John levied it about eleven times in seventeen years, against three or four times in Richard's ten."},{"q":"Who was made justiciar in 1193, and what was his most urgent task?","a":"Hubert Walter, Archbishop of Canterbury; he had to raise the 150,000-mark ransom that freed Richard I from captivity in Germany in February 1194."},{"q":"What was an archa, and why was it set up?","a":"A chest in a designated town in which copies of Jewish loan bonds were registered, ordered in 1194 so that debts could not be destroyed as they had been when the mob burned the bonds in York Minster in 1190."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Homage","code":"B2-1 — 1 The feudal system: The feudal hierarchy and the nature of feudalism","open":false,"back":"The ceremony that created the feudal tie: the tenant knelt and placed his hands between his lord's, then swore an oath of fealty. The bond was between two men, not two estates."},{"front":"Servitium debitum (knight service)","code":"B2-1 — 1 The feudal system: The feudal hierarchy and the nature of feudalism","open":false,"back":"The fixed quota of knights a tenant-in-chief owed the king for his fief, normally for forty days' unpaid service a year. Tenants-in-chief in turn granted land to knights on the same terms."},{"front":"Forfeiture","code":"B2-1 — 1 The feudal system: The feudal hierarchy and the nature of feudalism","open":false,"back":"Loss of land for breaking the feudal bargain by refusing service or rebelling. John's English lands were declared forfeit in 1194 while he plotted against Richard; William de Braose lost everything after 1208."},{"front":"The Interdict of 1208","code":"B2-1 — 1 The feudal system: The role and influence of the Church","open":false,"back":"Imposed by Innocent III on 24 March 1208 over John's refusal to accept Stephen Langton as Archbishop of Canterbury. Public services stopped; John seized church revenues. He submitted in May 1213."},{"front":"The coronation oath","code":"B2-1 — 2 Kingship and succession: The nature of kingship","open":false,"back":"Sworn at Westminster Abbey before anointing with holy oil: to protect the Church, to keep the peace and put down wrongdoing, and to do justice with mercy. Anointing set the king above other men."},{"front":"Richard I's claim to the throne in 1189","code":"B2-1 — 2 Kingship and succession: Richard I as king","open":false,"back":"Eldest surviving legitimate son of Henry II, his brothers Henry and Geoffrey having died in 1183 and 1186. He succeeded on 6 July 1189 and was crowned on 3 September 1189."},{"front":"John's claim to the throne in 1199","code":"B2-1 — 2 Kingship and succession: John as king","open":false,"back":"Youngest son of Henry II, said to have been named heir by Richard and accepted in England and Normandy; crowned 27 May 1199. Anjou, Maine and Touraine declared for his nephew Arthur instead."},{"front":"Arthur of Brittany","code":"B2-1 — 2 Kingship and succession: John as king","open":false,"back":"Son of John's dead elder brother Geoffrey, aged twelve in 1199 and backed by Philip II. Captured by John at Mirebeau on 1 August 1202; imprisoned at Falaise and Rouen; disappeared in 1203."},{"front":"Justiciar","code":"B2-1 — 3 Royal government and finances: How England was governed","open":false,"back":"The king's chief minister, who ruled in his name in his absence. Under Richard: William Longchamp (chancellor from 1189, chief justiciar 1190–91), Walter of Coutances, then Hubert Walter (1193–98), then Geoffrey FitzPeter (1198–1213)."},{"front":"The county farm and the sheriff","code":"B2-1 — 3 Royal government and finances: Royal revenues","open":false,"back":"Each sheriff owed the exchequer a fixed sum for his shire and kept whatever he collected above it, accounting at Easter and Michaelmas. That made the office worth buying and made sheriffs resented."},{"front":"Scutage","code":"B2-1 — 3 Royal government and finances: Royal revenues","open":false,"back":"'Shield money': a payment to the crown instead of serving with the knights a tenant owed. Levied about eleven times in John's seventeen years, against three or four times in Richard's ten, and at higher rates."},{"front":"The thirteenth of 1207","code":"B2-1 — 3 Royal government and finances: Royal revenues","open":false,"back":"One thirteenth of every man's moveable goods and income, imposed by John in 1207 after a council at Oxford refused him a grant from the clergy. It raised roughly £60,000, far more than a year's ordinary revenue."},{"front":"Villein","code":"B2-1 — 4 English society: Countryside","open":false,"back":"An unfree peasant. He held strips in the open fields for labour service on the lord's demesne, could not leave the manor, paid merchet to marry off a daughter and heriot at death, and was judged in the manor court."},{"front":"Borough charter","code":"B2-1 — 4 English society: Towns","open":false,"back":"Liberties bought from the king: a market, a borough court, elected officials and a fixed fee farm paid straight to the exchequer instead of through the sheriff. Richard and John sold them to raise cash."},{"front":"The massacre at York, March 1190","code":"B2-1 — 4 English society: Jews in Medieval England","open":false,"back":"On the night of 16–17 March 1190 about 150 Jews besieged in the royal castle killed themselves or were murdered after surrendering. The mob then burned the records of their debts in York Minster."}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (booklet B, the 16-mark judgement)","items":["Question: 'Royal government in England worked better under King John than it had under Richard I.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] You may use the following in your answer: • the chancery rolls • the general eyre. You must also use information of your own. What follows is one paragraph from the middle of the answer, putting the case against the statement.","John's presence and his rolls make the better record, but a government is to be judged by whether the kingdom's business was done, and by that test Richard's ministers did the harder thing. Richard spent about six months of a ten-year reign in England, and for the rest of it the country was run by justiciars in his name: William Longchamp, chancellor from September 1189 and chief justiciar from March 1190, who was driven out in October 1191; Walter of Coutances after him; and Hubert Walter from 1193. The decisive evidence is what that government managed while the king was a prisoner in Germany. The ransom was set at 150,000 marks, about £100,000, and to meet it Hubert Walter's administration demanded a quarter of every man's income and moveable goods, took plate from the churches and seized the Cistercian houses' wool crop, and Richard was released in February 1194. In the same year it held a general eyre, ordered that four men in every county — three knights and a clerk — should keep the pleas of the crown, and levied a carucage, with a second carucage in 1198. None of that needed the king: it needed an exchequer that still audited the sheriffs twice a year and ministers the shires would obey. Set beside it, what is new under John is chiefly the record itself — charter rolls from 1199, letters patent from 1201, letters close from 1204 — and a king on the road who could hear a plea in person; useful, but the same machinery underneath. Judged by whether government could meet an extraordinary demand and still keep the ordinary courts running, John's reign did not do better than the years when the king was not there at all.","Why this is Level 4: the explanation is directed consistently at the conceptual focus, which here is a judgement about effectiveness, and it carries a line of reasoning from its opening claim to a closing interim judgement instead of narrating two reigns. The information is precisely selected rather than piled up — the six months in England, the three justiciars with their dates, the 150,000-mark ransom and the quarter levied to pay it, the eyre and the coroners of 1194, the carucages of 1194 and 1198 and the three chancery rolls each do a job in the argument, and the chancery rolls are used to concede a point and then measure it rather than as an extra fact. The criterion for the judgement is stated and applied: government is ranked by whether the kingdom's business was done, not by how much of it was written down. It also brings in aspects of content the two stimulus bullets do not prompt — the justiciars, the ransom and the exchequer's audit — so the cap on answers that do not address three or more aspects of content does not bite."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["The same question, answered at Level 2. Read it beside the Level 4 paragraph above and look for what is missing rather than for what is wrong.","Royal government worked better under John than under Richard because John was actually in England. Richard went off on crusade and got captured so he was not there to govern and other people had to do it for him and they argued with each other. John travelled round the country all the time and he wrote lots of things down in the chancery rolls so people knew what he had ordered. He also sent judges round the country on the general eyre to deal with cases. So government was better under John because a king who is there can run things himself, whereas Richard left it all to someone else.","Why this is only Level 2: it meets the descriptor 'an explanation is given showing limited analysis and with implicit or unsustained links to the conceptual focus of the question', because effectiveness is gestured at in 'people knew what he had ordered' and 'a king who is there can run things himself', but no link is traced. It misses the higher descriptors in three ways. First, the information is generalised rather than 'accurate and relevant information precisely selected to address the question directly': no justiciar is named, no date is given for any of the rolls, and there is no figure for the ransom or for anything else. Second, the answer is comparative only in form: it sets presence against absence without ever asking what either government actually achieved. Third, 'the overall judgement is given but its justification is asserted or insecure', since the final sentence repeats the statement with a general rule instead of a criterion. Because it also adds nothing of its own beyond the two stimulus bullets, the printed cap applies on top: on the 16-mark question an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content is held to a maximum of 11 marks and has no access to Level 4 at all, so better writing alone could take this answer no higher."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the feudal system and the Church in England, 1189–1216","items":["In principle the king owned all the land; those who held directly from him — the great barons, bishops and abbots — were his tenants-in-chief.","Homage: the tenant knelt and placed his hands between his lord's, then swore fealty. The tie was personal and had to be renewed with each new lord or tenant.","Knight service: a tenant-in-chief owed a fixed quota of knights, the servitium debitum, normally for forty days' unpaid service in the year.","Subinfeudation: tenants-in-chief granted land on to their own knights on the same terms, so the same bargain was repeated down the pyramid.","Labour service: a villein held strips in the open fields in return for week work on the lord's demesne, two or three days a week, and boon work at harvest.","Forfeiture: land could be taken back for breach of the bargain. John's English lands were declared forfeit in 1194 while he was plotting with Philip II against Richard; William de Braose, once a favourite, lost his lands after 1208 and fled to France, and his wife and eldest son died in prison in 1210.","Church land: bishoprics and great abbeys were tenants-in-chief, holding estates by knight service like any baron. The Church was among the largest landowners in England.","Vacancies: while a bishopric or abbey stood empty its revenues went to the crown, so a king had a direct financial interest in leaving posts unfilled.","The parish: baptism, marriage, confession and burial in consecrated ground; mass in Latin; holy days fixed the working year.","The tithe: a tenth of the year's produce owed by every household to the parish church, good harvest or bad.","Church courts claimed all clergy and all cases touching marriage, wills and morals; the Church also supplied the crown's literate administrators, Hubert Walter being archbishop, justiciar and chancellor in turn.","The Canterbury dispute: Hubert Walter died in 1205; the monks and John put forward rival candidates; Innocent III set both aside and had Stephen Langton consecrated in 1207.","The interdict was imposed on 24 March 1208: public services stopped and the dead could not be buried in consecrated ground, though baptism and the confession of the dying continued. John was excommunicated in 1209.","John took the lands and revenues of the Church into his hands during the dispute and drew very large sums from them, which is a reason the quarrel lasted so long.","John submitted in May 1213, accepted Langton, surrendered England and Ireland to the pope and received them back as a papal fief for a tribute of 1,000 marks a year. The interdict was lifted in 1214."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: kingship and succession — Richard I's claim in 1189, John's in 1199, and Arthur of Brittany","items":["The coronation oath, sworn at Westminster Abbey: to protect the Church, to keep the peace and put down wrongdoing, and to do justice with mercy. The king was then anointed with holy oil by the Archbishop of Canterbury, crowned and given the regalia.","Royal rights: homage from tenants-in-chief, the feudal incidents, the profits of justice and of the forest, control of the coinage, the appointment of sheriffs and judges, and the summoning of men to war.","Royal duties in practice: defend the realm, defend the Church, keep the peace and give justice, and take counsel from the great men in council.","Richard I was the eldest surviving legitimate son of Henry II: Henry the Young King died in 1183 and Geoffrey of Brittany in 1186. He succeeded on his father's death on 6 July 1189.","Richard was crowned at Westminster on 3 September 1189.","Securing power in 1189–90: he rewarded his brother John with great estates, sold offices, sheriffdoms and charters for the crusade, and in December 1189 released William the Lion of Scotland from his submission to Henry II for 10,000 marks.","Richard left England in December 1189 for the Third Crusade and, apart from about two months in the spring of 1194, never returned; he spent roughly six months of his ten-year reign in England.","He was captured on his way home at the end of 1192 and held in Germany; the ransom was set at 150,000 marks, about £100,000, and he was released in February 1194.","Richard died on 6 April 1199 of a crossbow wound received at the siege of Châlus in the Limousin.","John, born in 1166, was Henry II's youngest son and was nicknamed Lackland. Richard was reported to have named him heir; he was crowned at Westminster on 27 May 1199.","Arthur of Brittany was the son of John's dead elder brother Geoffrey and was twelve in 1199. Anjou, Maine and Touraine declared for him and he was backed by Philip II of France.","Eleanor of Aquitaine, John's mother, held Aquitaine and Anjou for him in 1199; Hubert Walter and William Marshal carried the English barons.","By the Treaty of Le Goulet in May 1200 Philip II recognised John as Richard's heir in France in return for a relief of 20,000 marks and John's homage.","On 1 August 1202 John marched overnight to Mirebeau, relieved his mother, who was besieged there, and captured Arthur and the rebel Lusignans.","Arthur was imprisoned at Falaise and then Rouen and disappeared in 1203. The annals of Margam Abbey later claimed John killed him himself; how he died is unknown.","The belief that John had murdered his nephew cost him support in Brittany and Anjou and gave Philip II a pretext; John had lost Normandy by 1204 and was nicknamed Softsword."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: royal government — the justiciar, the exchequer, the chancery and the sheriffs","items":["The justiciar was the king's chief minister and ruled in his name when he was abroad.","William Longchamp, Bishop of Ely, was made chancellor in September 1189 and chief justiciar at the council of Nonancourt in March 1190; he quarrelled with John and the barons and was driven out in October 1191.","Walter of Coutances, Archbishop of Rouen, took over in 1191; Hubert Walter became justiciar in 1193 and raised the ransom; Geoffrey FitzPeter was justiciar from 1198 until his death in 1213.","The exchequer sat at Westminster and audited accounts twice a year, at Easter and Michaelmas, reckoning sums on a chequered cloth; the results were written up on the pipe rolls.","The chancery was the writing office: it issued writs and charters under the great seal. Under John it began keeping copies of what it sent out — charter rolls from 1199, letters patent from 1201, letters close from 1204.","The sheriff was the king's officer in the shire: he held the royal castles, ran the shire court, executed writs, and collected the king's revenues.","Each sheriff owed the exchequer a fixed sum for his shire, the county farm, and kept whatever he could collect above it. The office was therefore worth buying, and sheriffs were widely resented.","John raised the sums demanded of the shires and employed foreign-born sheriffs and castellans who depended on him alone.","Royal justice reached the shires through the sheriffs and through the visits of the king's itinerant justices.","In 1194 Hubert Walter ordered that four men in every county — three knights and a clerk — should keep the pleas of the crown: the origin of the coroner.","Richard spent about six months of his reign in England; John governed by constant travel with his household and heard cases in person.","Great councils of barons and bishops were summoned to advise on war and taxation; John's demand for a tax was put to one at Oxford in February 1207, which refused him a grant from the clergy; he imposed the thirteenth anyway."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: royal revenues — the demesne, feudal incidents, scutage, the forest and the taxes of 1207","items":["Ordinary revenue: the royal demesne, the king's own estates, together with the county farms owed by the sheriffs and the profits of justice and of the forest.","Feudal incidents: relief, paid by an heir to enter his inheritance; wardship and marriage, the control of under-age heirs and their lands and matches; and escheat, when a tenant died without heirs.","Nicholas de Stuteville was charged 10,000 marks in 1205 to enter his brother's inheritance.","In 1214 Geoffrey de Mandeville promised 20,000 marks for the hand of Isabella of Gloucester. A mark was 13s 4d, two-thirds of a pound.","Scutage was a money payment made instead of serving with the knights owed. John levied it about eleven times in seventeen years, against three or four times in Richard's ten, and at higher rates.","Tallage was levied on the royal demesne, the towns and the Jewish communities at the king's will, without consent.","A carucage, a tax on ploughland, was levied in 1194 and 1198 under Richard and in 1200 under John.","For the ransom of 1193–94, a quarter of every man's income and moveable goods was demanded, church plate was taken and the Cistercian houses' wool crop was seized.","In February 1207 a great council at Oxford refused John a grant from the clergy; he then imposed a thirteenth of everyone's moveable goods and income, the writ claiming it was taken by the common counsel of his council. It raised roughly £60,000, far more than a normal year's ordinary revenue.","The royal forest covered perhaps a quarter of England and included farmland and whole villages, not only woodland.","Forest law protected the venison, the king's deer, and the vert, his wood and cover; special forest courts fined men for poaching, for taking timber and for assarting, that is clearing land to farm.","Licences and fines under forest law were a steady source of income, and the forest was one of the crown's most resented rights."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: English society — countryside, towns, and the Jewish communities and the pogroms of 1189–90","items":["About nine people in ten lived in the countryside, in villages of at most a few hundred people, on manors held by a lord.","The arable was farmed in two or three open fields divided into strips, with one field left fallow each year and animals grazed on the stubble and common.","Wheat and rye made bread, barley made ale, oats fed horses and went into pottage; peas and beans were grown alongside.","A villein owed week work of two or three days on the demesne and boon work at harvest, paid merchet to marry off a daughter and heriot, usually his best beast, at death, and answered in the manor court. Free tenants paid rent instead.","The population was growing, woodland and waste were being cleared for new fields, and more lords were taking money rents in place of labour service.","London had perhaps twenty to thirty thousand people; York, Norwich, Lincoln and Bristol a few thousand each.","A borough charter could grant a market, a borough court, elected officials and a fixed fee farm paid straight to the exchequer rather than through the sheriff; many held that a villein who lived in the town unclaimed for a year and a day became free.","London was recognised as a commune in 1191 during the crisis over Longchamp; Henry FitzAilwin was its first mayor and served until his death in 1212.","Merchant and craft guilds fixed prices, standards and apprenticeship. Towns were timber-built and crowded: fire destroyed the area round London Bridge in 1212.","England's chief export was raw wool, shipped to the weavers of Flanders and sold at great fairs such as Boston, St Ives and Winchester; the Cistercian abbeys of Yorkshire were among the largest producers, and a year's clip was seized in 1193 towards Richard's ransom.","In law the Jews of England were the king's property: they lived under his direct protection, outside the feudal hierarchy, could not hold land by feudal tenure, and could be tallaged at will.","Because the Church forbade Christians to lend at interest, Jewish families became the lenders to barons, knights and abbeys; their bonds were enforceable in the king's courts, and the crown could claim the debts owed to a lender who died.","Antisemitism was long-established: the accusation that Jews had murdered a Christian child was first made in England at Norwich in 1144, and preaching for the Third Crusade sharpened hostility in 1189–90.","At Richard's coronation on 3 September 1189, Jewish leaders bringing gifts were driven from Westminster; a rumour spread that the king had ordered a massacre, and about thirty Jews were killed in London, among them the scholar Jacob of Orléans. Richard punished some of the rioters.","At York, on the night of 16 to 17 March 1190, the community took refuge in the royal castle; around 150 died, some killing their families and themselves and the rest murdered after surrendering. The mob then burned the records of their debts in York Minster; Richard Malebisse, one of the ringleaders, was himself a debtor.","Early in 1190 attacks followed at King's Lynn, at Norwich on 6 February and at Stamford on 7 March; Bury St Edmunds came later, on 18 March, two days after the massacre at York.","In 1194 Richard's government ordered that Jewish loans be registered and copies kept in chests, the archae, in designated towns, with a royal department to oversee them, so that debts could not be destroyed again.","John confirmed the Jews' liberties by charter in 1201 in return for a payment, then in 1210 demanded a tallage of about 66,000 marks from the Jewish communities and imprisoned those who would not pay."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — the 16-mark judgement: 'The demands of royal government fell most heavily on the barons in the years 1189–1216.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.","items":["The question as printed: 'The demands of royal government fell most heavily on the barons in the years 1189–1216.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] You may use the following in your answer: • reliefs • the tax on moveables of 1207. You must also use information of your own. There are no spelling, punctuation and grammar marks on Paper 2, so every minute goes on the argument.","Decode it. The concept is significance, and 'most heavily' is a measure, so you must say what weight is measured in before you can answer. The criterion to announce in the opening and apply at the end: is a demand heavy because of the size of the sum, or because of what it cost the man who had to find it? Signal that you will argue that the largest sums fell on the barons but the heaviest pressure did not.","Opening, three or four sentences: define 'most heavily' by that criterion, name the three things you will test — who was charged, how much, and what refusing would have cost them — and state the position you will end at.","Paragraph 1, for the statement — the feudal incidents fell on the barons alone. A relief was owed by an heir to enter his inheritance, and John charged Nicholas de Stuteville 10,000 marks in 1205 to enter his brother's lands; in 1214 Geoffrey de Mandeville promised 20,000 marks for the hand of Isabella of Gloucester, and a mark was 13s 4d, two-thirds of a pound. Wardship put an heir's estates in the king's hands until he came of age. Interim judgement: no other group could be charged sums that could swallow an inheritance whole.","Paragraph 2, for the statement — what stood behind the demand. Scutage was owed by those who held by knight service, John levied it about eleven times in seventeen years against three or four times in Richard's ten, and the tenant who defied the king risked forfeiture: William de Braose lost everything after 1208 and fled to France, and his wife and eldest son died in prison in 1210. Interim judgement: the barons were the only men the crown could ruin by using its undoubted rights.","Paragraph 3, against the statement — the great levies reached everyone. For the ransom of 1193–94 a quarter of every man's income and moveable goods was demanded, church plate was taken and the Cistercians' wool crop seized; in February 1207, after a great council at Oxford refused him a grant from the clergy, John imposed a thirteenth of everyone's moveable goods and income, raising roughly £60,000, far more than a normal year's ordinary revenue. Interim judgement: these demands were assessed on what a household owned, so they reached men who had no baronage to protect them.","Paragraph 4, the point beyond the stimulus bullets — the people who could not refuse at all. Tallage was levied at the king's will, without consent, on the royal demesne, the towns and the Jewish communities; in 1210 John demanded a tallage of about 66,000 marks from the Jews and imprisoned those who would not pay; during the quarrel with Innocent III he held the lands and revenues of the Church from 1208 and drew very large sums from them; and sheriffs whose county farms had been raised made up the difference out of the shire. Interim judgement: weight measured by the power to say no falls on those who had none.","The evaluation thread: return in every paragraph to the same test — is this demand heavy because of the sum named in it, or because of what it did to the man who paid? Keeping one test running is what turns four paragraphs into a line of reasoning.","Timing: about 25 minutes, of which the first three or four are spent writing the plan — the factors, the order, the one point beyond the bullets and the position you will end at — so that the essay is written once.","The conclusion must rank and justify rather than summarise. Argue that the barons were charged the largest individual sums and were the group whose inheritances the crown could seize, which is why their grievance became a political one; but that measured by what a demand cost the payer, the thirteenth of 1207, the tallages and the seized church revenues pressed hardest on those who had no way of refusing and no one to bargain for them. Then say which test the question should be judged on and why — that the weight of a demand is what it takes from the payer, not what it is worth to the crown — so the statement is right about the size of the sums and wrong about the burden.","What will lose marks: listing the crown's sources of money in turn; treating the two bullets as the whole answer, since an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content is capped at 11 marks and shut out of Level 4; and a conclusion that says 'everybody paid something' without saying who paid most heavily and by what measure."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:23:17.539Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.93,"notes":"Judged afresh from the kit file: I re-solved all fifteen questions (six 1(a)/1(b) describe pairs at 2 + 2, five 12-mark 'Explain why...', four 16-mark judgements) and checked about seventy-five dates, figures, names and attributions, of which eleven were verified on the web¹; no answer key is wrong, ambiguous or at odds with its sol, so the wrong list is empty. Every new fact in the retargeted items stands up²: the Articles of the Eyre of September 1194 (three knights and a clerk in each county to keep the pleas of the crown), the chancery rolls (charter 1199, letters patent 1201, letters close 1204), the carucages of 1194 and 1198, Hugh de Neville chief forester from 1198 with the 1198 assize called oppressive by Roger of Howden, the 1198 forest eyre at £1,980 and his statement of 1208 that the forests had raised £15,000 in six and a half years, Richard FitzNigel in the Dialogue of the Exchequer on forest law resting on the king's decision rather than the common law, lawing as three claws struck from one forefoot, Devon's 5,000-mark fine of 1204 for disafforestation outside Dartmoor and Exmoor, scutage at three marks the knight's fee in 1214, and the thirteenth of 1207 imposed after the council adjourned from London to Oxford (9 February) refused John a grant from the clergy, the writ claiming common counsel, raising roughly £60,000. I read every setup myself rather than trusting the string scan: the four retargeted worked examples (coroner and tithe; why the royal forest was resented, bullets forest law / the forest eyre; feudalism as money rather than soldiers, bullets knight service / feudal incidents; towns against countryside, bullets guilds / villeinage), the model-paragraph pair ('Royal government worked better under John than under Richard', bullets the chancery rolls / the general eyre) and the plan ('The demands of royal government fell most heavily on the barons', bullets reliefs / the tax on moveables of 1207) each set a question that is not in the bank and shares no bank question's focus and stimulus pair; dupscan prints 'no duplication'. No bank stem reproduces the Paper 2 specimen booklet B2, whose questions are William Marshal as Protector, why England became involved in the Third Crusade, the murder of Prince Arthur as the reason John secured the throne (bullets Arthur's claim / the King of France) and the Interdict as the chief consequence of the papal dispute (bullets the excommunication / Archbishop of Canterbury) — the nearest kit question, room.questions[11] on the security of the two accessions, compares 1189 with 1199 on a different concept and different bullets. Truth to the paper holds throughout: 2025 booklet B numbering and two-part describe question, AO splits 4 / 6+6 / 10+6, no SPaG anywhere on Paper 2, the current caps ('maximum 8', 'maximum 11', 'no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content') on all nine levelled questions and no Level 2 cap; all ten specification ideas are tested, the five named case studies each have fact-file coverage, hints stop at the method, and the fifteen cards are items with backs inside the word limits. Publishable; the three findings are presentation, not fact. ¹ the eleven web checks are the items listed in note ² plus the 1210 Jewish tallage of about 66,000 marks at Bristol, Geoffrey de Mandeville's 20,000 marks of January 1214 for Isabella of Gloucester and Nicholas de Stuteville's 10,000-mark fine of 1205. ² the one place I would still hedge is 'perhaps a quarter of England' for the royal forest, which the kit already hedges."}}},"B2-2":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"B2-2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 2 is the part of this depth study that leaves England, and it asks you to judge two kings by what they did abroad and by what that cost the people who stayed at home. Booklet B gives you three chances at it: Question 1, which asks for one feature of each of two different things in the topic, Question 2, which asks why something happened, and Question 3 or 4, which asks how far one factor mattered most. None of those can be answered with a story. You need the sequence from Saladin's capture of Jerusalem in 1187 to the fall of Chateau Gaillard in March 1204 firmly dated, and you need to be able to argue two ways with the same evidence, because the examiner sets questions in both directions: that Richard's crusade was a run of military successes and a financial disaster for England, and that Normandy was lost both by John's own failures and by Philip II's strength.","idea":[{"h":"What a crusade was, and what the English made of it","t":"A crusade was an armed pilgrimage authorised by the Pope, not an ordinary war. A man took the cross in public, sewed it on his clothing and bound himself by a vow the Church could enforce. In return he was promised remission of the penalties of his sins, the protection of his lands and family while he was away, and the suspension of interest on his debts, which is how knights could mortgage or sell land to equip themselves. The First Crusade had been preached by Pope Urban II at Clermont in 1095 and Jerusalem taken in 1099, so by the 1180s crusading was the most prestigious thing a fighting man could do. English attitudes were mixed. Henry II took the cross and never went, levying the Saladin tithe of 1188 instead, a tax of a tenth of revenues and moveable goods. Enthusiasm also had a savage side at home: attacks on the Jewish community of London began during Richard's coronation celebrations on 3 September 1189, and in March 1190 the Jewish community of York died at Clifford's Tower, most by their own hand. A top-level answer never defines a crusade and stops. It uses these attitudes as a cause, to explain why men and money were available to Richard in 1190 in a way they would not have been for an ordinary war.","code":"B2-2 — 1 The nature of crusading: The concept of crusade and attitudes in England"},{"h":"Who actually went, and what they were going for","t":"The army that sailed in 1190 was Angevin rather than English. Richard ruled Normandy, Anjou and Aquitaine as well as England, so knights and foot soldiers came from across his lands; the fleet left English ports and sailed round Spain into the Mediterranean while Richard rode overland through France to Marseille. It was a mixed force of household and baronial knights, foot soldiers, crossbowmen, engineers and sailors, and it was led by churchmen as well as soldiers: Baldwin, Archbishop of Canterbury, who had preached the crusade in Wales in 1188, went east and died at the siege of Acre in November 1190, and Hubert Walter, then Bishop of Salisbury, served with the army and was made Archbishop of Canterbury in 1193. Motives ran together. Some men went for the indulgence and the remission of sin, some because their lord summoned them, some for pay, plunder or land, and some because a crusader's debts were frozen and his creditors held off. A top-level answer uses the mixture to explain both the army's strength and its fragility: an army held together by a vow dissolves the moment the vow is fulfilled, which is exactly the argument the Templars used against attacking Jerusalem.","code":"B2-2 — 1 The nature of crusading: The nature of the English crusading army"},{"h":"Why there was a Third Crusade, and why Richard led it","t":"The immediate cause is a military disaster. Saladin, sultan of Egypt and Syria, destroyed the field army of the kingdom of Jerusalem at Hattin on 4 July 1187, and the city surrendered to him that October, the first time since 1099 that the holy places were not in Christian hands. Pope Gregory VIII called for a new crusade, and three rulers answered it: the Emperor Frederick Barbarossa, who drowned in a river in Asia Minor in June 1190 so that his army broke up, Philip II of France and Richard. Richard's own commitment came first of all: he took the cross in November 1187, while his father Henry II was still alive and he himself was only count of Poitou, so the vow bound him before he had a kingdom to leave. His motives ran together too: genuine religious duty, the reputation of the finest soldier in Christendom, a family record of crusading, and the plain fact that a king who stayed at home while the emperor and the king of France went would have conceded first place in Europe. A top-level answer ranks these instead of listing them, separating the occasion that created a crusade at all from the motives that made this particular king its leader.","code":"B2-2 — 2 Richard and the Third Crusade: The immediate causes of the Third Crusade"},{"h":"Two kings, one army: the quarrel with Philip, and what Richard won","t":"Richard and Philip II were partners on crusade and rivals everywhere else, because Philip was the overlord of Richard's French lands. They wintered together in Sicily in 1190 to 1191, where Richard captured Messina in October 1190 in a quarrel with Tancred over his sister Joanna's dower. In May and June 1191 Richard conquered Cyprus from Isaac Komnenos, who surrendered on 1 June 1191, gaining a supply base, and he married Berengaria of Navarre at Limassol on 12 May 1191, setting aside his long betrothal to Philip's sister Alice. At Acre, besieged since 1189, Philip arrived in April 1191 and Richard on 8 June; the city surrendered on 12 July 1191, and the kings then divided over the spoils and over who should be king of Jerusalem, Richard backing Guy of Lusignan and Philip backing Conrad of Montferrat. Philip sailed home at the end of July. Marching south towards Jaffa with the fleet alongside for supplies, Richard beat Saladin in open battle at Arsuf on 7 September 1191. A top-level answer uses Acre and Arsuf to show both what Richard could do and what he could not: he could take ports and win battles, but each victory cost him an ally, and Arsuf left Saladin's army in being.","code":"B2-2 — 2 Richard and the Third Crusade: Richard's quarrel with Philip II and his victories"},{"h":"Why Jerusalem was never taken","t":"Twice the crusaders reached Beit Nuba, about a day's march from Jerusalem, in January 1192 and again in June 1192, and twice they turned back without being defeated. The reasons stack up. Saladin's field army survived Arsuf, so besieging a walled city in the hills would have meant sitting still while an undefeated enemy moved behind them and cut the supply road back to Jaffa. Saladin had also stripped the country and spoiled the wells, and held the inland fortresses. The Templars and Hospitallers argued that even a captured Jerusalem could not be held, because most crusaders would count their vows fulfilled and sail home. Meanwhile the crusade's politics collapsed: Conrad of Montferrat was elected king in April 1192 and murdered at Tyre within the month, Philip was pressing Normandy and John was building a party in England. Saladin took Jaffa in July 1192 and Richard recovered it. The outcome was the Treaty of Jaffa on 2 September 1192: a truce of about three years, the coast from Tyre to Jaffa left in Christian hands, the defences of Ascalon dismantled, and unarmed pilgrims allowed into Jerusalem. Richard sailed on 9 October 1192 without entering the city. A top-level answer measures failure against what a crusade could realistically achieve, not against its slogan.","code":"B2-2 — 2 Richard and the Third Crusade: The failure to recapture Jerusalem"},{"h":"The journey that went wrong","t":"Richard left the Holy Land on 9 October 1192 and could not go home the direct way, because the sea route to his own ports ran past lands held by Philip II and his allies. He turned instead up the Adriatic, was shipwrecked on its northern coast near Aquileia, and tried to cross Germany overland with a handful of companions, travelling in disguise. It did not work: he was recognised and seized near Vienna shortly before Christmas 1192 by Duke Leopold V of Austria, whose banner had been thrown down from the walls of Acre the year before. In 1193 Leopold handed him to the Emperor Henry VI, who had quarrels of his own with Richard's allies and who was in a position to name his price. A top-level answer does not narrate the journey. It treats the capture as the moment when the diplomatic bills of the crusade fell due: the insults at Acre, the broken betrothal to Alice and the enmity of Philip all turn, on the road home, into a king in a German castle and a demand that England will have to pay.","code":"B2-2 — 3 Aftermath of the crusade: Richard's return from the Holy Land"},{"h":"The ransom, and who actually paid it","t":"Henry VI demanded 150,000 marks. A mark was two-thirds of a pound, so the sum was 100,000 pounds, several times what the Crown gathered in an ordinary year, and hostages were required as security for the unpaid balance. It could not come out of rents, fines and customs, so it came out of what people owned. A tax of a quarter of every free man's movable goods and income was levied, reaching livestock, stored grain and a townsman's stock; scutage was taken from knights' fees; gold and silver plate was stripped from churches and monasteries; and the Cistercian houses, the great sheep farmers of England, gave up a whole year's wool crop. Queen Eleanor of Aquitaine and Hubert Walter, Archbishop of Canterbury from 1193 and justiciar, organised the collection, while John and Philip offered Henry VI money to keep Richard in prison. Richard was released on 4 February 1194, landed in England on 13 March and was crowned a second time at Winchester on 17 April 1194, then left in May and never returned. A top-level answer argues about the burden rather than describing it, and the sharpest argument is the precedent: the collection proved how much a king could take, and that lesson outlived Richard.","code":"B2-2 — 3 Aftermath of the crusade: Richard's capture, the ransom and its burden on England"},{"h":"Three men, three aims, one duchy","t":"Normandy was contested because all three rulers wanted different things inside one legal structure: the Angevin lands in France were held from the king of France, so Philip II was both rival and overlord. Richard's aim after his release in 1194 was recovery, above all of the Norman Vexin with its castle at Gisors, which John had conceded to Philip while Richard was a prisoner; he spent the last five years of his life fighting for it. Philip's aim was to make his overlordship real, to use his feudal rights to break the Angevin lands apart and to push royal power to the Seine and beyond. John's aim changed with his position: while Richard was captive he allied with Philip against his brother, and as king from 1199 he wanted his inheritance cheaply, which is why he accepted the Treaty of Le Goulet in 1200, being recognised as Richard's heir in France in return for acknowledging Philip as overlord and paying a relief of 20,000 marks. A top-level answer uses these aims to explain the shape of the war: Philip did not need to win a battle, because as overlord he could summon, condemn and confiscate, and John had already agreed that he could.","code":"B2-2 — 4 Richard, John and the loss of Normandy: The competing aims of Richard, John and Philip II"},{"h":"Chateau Gaillard: what it was for and what it cost","t":"Having lost the Vexin during his captivity, Richard needed to block the road up the Seine to Rouen, the capital of Normandy, and to hold a base from which the Vexin could be recovered. He built Chateau Gaillard at Les Andelys on a chalk spur above the river in about two years from 1196, extraordinarily fast for a fortress of its size. It was concentric: an attacker had to take an outer triangular bailey, then a middle bailey, then an inner bailey with a keep, with ditches cut into the rock between them, and the spur left only one line of approach. The river works mattered as much as the walls: a fortified new town at Petit Andely, an island fort and a stockade across the Seine meant shipping could be stopped. One estimate puts its cost at about 11,500 pounds, more than Richard spent on all his castles in England put together, though other estimates run higher, and it cost him a quarrel with Walter of Coutances, Archbishop of Rouen, who owned the land and laid Normandy under interdict until he was compensated. A top-level answer judges it as strategy and as expense together, because the strongest castle in Europe still fell after about six months once no relief came.","code":"B2-2 — 4 Richard, John and the loss of Normandy: Richard and Chateau Gaillard"},{"h":"How John lost Normandy","t":"Richard died on 6 April 1199 of a crossbow wound taken at the siege of Chalus in the Limousin. John was crowned at Westminster on 27 May 1199, but Arthur of Brittany, son of John's elder brother Geoffrey, was recognised in Anjou, Maine and Touraine and backed by Philip. Le Goulet bought peace in 1200, and John then broke it himself: in August 1200 he married Isabella of Angouleme, who was betrothed to Hugh de Lusignan. The Lusignans appealed to Philip as overlord, John ignored the summons to Philip's court, and in 1202 his French lands were declared forfeit and Philip took up Arthur's claim. John's one triumph, the forced march that surprised Arthur and the Lusignans besieging his mother Eleanor at Mirebeau on 1 August 1202, was thrown away: prisoners were ill-treated and some starved, Arthur disappeared in John's custody in 1203, and lords such as William des Roches changed sides. Philip besieged Chateau Gaillard from late August 1203; John's relief attempt by river and land failed and he sailed for England in December. Roger de Lacy's garrison surrendered on 6 March 1204, Eleanor died on 1 April and Rouen surrendered on 24 June 1204. A top-level answer separates John's decisions from what he inherited, and says which set the barons were reacting to.","code":"B2-2 — 4 Richard, John and the loss of Normandy: John and the fall of Chateau Gaillard"}],"examples":[{"title":"One feature in each part: the two-part describe question on Cyprus and Le Goulet","setup":"The question is printed in two parts: 1(a) Describe one feature of Richard I's conquest of Cyprus in 1191. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the Treaty of Le Goulet (1200). [2 marks] No bullets and no sources are given. From your notes you have: a storm scattered Richard's fleet on the way east and three of his ships were driven ashore and broken up in sight of Limassol; Isaac Komnenos, who called himself emperor of Cyprus, imprisoned the survivors and kept what was salvaged, and refused to let Richard's sister Joanna and Berengaria of Navarre land from the ship sheltering off the port; Richard landed at Limassol on 6 May 1191 and took the town; he married Berengaria there on 12 May 1191; Isaac surrendered the island on 1 June 1191 and was held in silver chains; the island gave the crusade a supply base off the Syrian coast and Richard later sold it to the Knights Templar; and by the Treaty of Le Goulet in 1200 John was recognised as Richard's heir in France in return for acknowledging Philip II as his overlord, giving up Evreux and the Norman Vexin apart from Les Andelys, and paying a relief of 20,000 marks. Take one feature for each part and give each its supporting information.","steps":["Place each subject in its own reign before choosing anything. Cyprus is Richard's, taken on the way east in the spring of 1191; Le Goulet is John's, made in 1200 at the start of his reign. Nothing in the Cyprus list can serve the treaty, and nothing in the treaty list can serve the island.","Part (a): rule out the storm and the shipwrecked crews first, because they explain why Richard attacked rather than describing the conquest itself. Take how the island fell instead — he landed at Limassol on 6 May 1191, took the town, and Isaac Komnenos gave up the whole island on 1 June 1191, so a campaign that was never part of the plan was finished inside a month.","Part (b), on a separate aspect of content: the treaty was a bargain about lordship. John was recognised as Richard's heir in France, and in return he acknowledged Philip II as overlord of those lands and paid a relief of 20,000 marks for them.","Weigh the facts you left out, to be sure the two you chose describe their subjects. The wedding at Limassol and the later sale to the Templars are things Richard did with Cyprus once it was his rather than part of taking it; the silver chains are a detail of the surrender you could add as supporting information but not a feature on their own; and John's recognition as heir is the other half of the same bargain as the relief he paid, not a separate feature of the treaty."],"cues":["When was each of these two things made, and in whose reign?","Which facts in that list explain why Richard attacked Cyprus rather than what the conquest was like?","What did each side actually get out of Le Goulet?","Which facts in the list did you leave out, and what would each of them miss?"]},{"title":"Planning the twelve-mark answer on the forfeiture of 1202, and finding the point beyond the bullets","setup":"The question is: Explain why Philip II was able to declare King John's French lands forfeit in 1202. [12 marks] You may use the following in your answer: the first bullet is Isabella of Angouleme, the second is the Lusignans. You must also use information of your own. You know that the Angevin lands in France were fiefs held from the king of France; that by the Treaty of Le Goulet in 1200 John had been recognised as Richard's heir there and had in return acknowledged Philip as his overlord, given up Evreux and the Norman Vexin apart from Les Andelys, and paid a relief of 20,000 marks; that John married Isabella of Angouleme in August 1200 although she was betrothed to Hugh de Lusignan; that the Lusignans appealed to Philip as overlord for justice; that Philip summoned John to his court and John did not come; that in April 1202 that court condemned him as a vassal who would not answer it and declared his French fiefs forfeit; and that Philip then took up Arthur of Brittany's claim and invaded. Plan three reasons and decide which one takes the answer beyond the bullets.","steps":["Sort the material by the kind of reason it is: legal, meaning the standing Philip already had over John; personal, meaning what John did to hand him a case; and procedural, meaning what turned a complaint into a sentence.","Reason one, legal: the Angevin lands were fiefs, and at Le Goulet John had himself acknowledged Philip as their overlord and paid a relief for them, so Philip's court had jurisdiction over him. Richard had fought that lordship; John had signed for it.","Reason two, personal: the marriage to Isabella of Angouleme cut across her betrothal to Hugh de Lusignan, so the Lusignans had a grievance, and a wronged vassal had exactly one remedy — to appeal to the lord above the man who had wronged him, which was Philip.","Reason three, the one neither bullet prompts: the judgement was won on procedure by a king who had prepared the weapon. John was condemned for not appearing, so Philip never had to argue the rights of the marriage at all, and by then the French royal court was strong enough for its sentences to be worth enforcing.","Finish by tying the three together rather than repeating them: the forfeiture was not a quarrel about a bride but the working of a lordship John had accepted in writing, set going by a grievance he created and completed by his refusal to answer for it."],"cues":["What three different kinds of reason are hiding in that list of facts?","What had John conceded in 1200 that Richard had spent his last years fighting?","If a vassal believes his lord's man has wronged him, where can he take it?","Why did John's decision not to appear matter more than the rights and wrongs of the marriage?","What does the last sentence have to do so that this reads as an explanation and not a list?"]},{"title":"Turning the price of a crusade into an argument","setup":"The question is: Explain why Richard I had to raise so much money in England before he left on crusade in 1190. [12 marks] You may use the following in your answer: the first bullet is the sale of offices, the second is William the Lion of Scotland. You must also use information of your own. Your notes: Richard was crowned on 3 September 1189 and sailed for the east in 1190; the army was carried by sea, so ships had to be hired, built and crewed; knights, foot soldiers, crossbowmen and engineers had to be paid for a campaign expected to last years, far beyond the customary forty days a knight owed his lord; William Longchamp paid 3,000 pounds for the chancellorship and sheriffdoms, offices and charters were sold; by the quitclaim of Canterbury on 5 December 1189 Richard gave William the Lion back his castles and released him from the overlordship Henry II had imposed by the Treaty of Falaise, for 10,000 marks, a mark being two-thirds of a pound; Henry II had already taken a tenth of revenues and moveable goods as the Saladin tithe in 1188; and the chronicler Richard of Devizes reports Richard saying he would have sold London if he could have found a buyer. Build three reasons why so much had to be found.","steps":["Start with what the money was for, because the answer is a fleet and a payroll rather than a tax: an army drawn from England, Normandy, Anjou and Aquitaine had to be shipped to the eastern Mediterranean and then fed and paid there for years, and no lord's customary service could supply any of that.","Reason one, the sale of offices: a king with no standing tax on land could turn patronage into cash at once, which is why the chancellorship went to William Longchamp for 3,000 pounds and why sheriffdoms and charters were sold. Buyers paid because office was profitable, so the Crown was selling next year's profits for this year's coin.","Reason two, William the Lion: here Richard sold a right rather than a post. The quitclaim of Canterbury returned the Scottish king's castles and cancelled the overlordship Henry II had won at Falaise, for 10,000 marks, which shows the scale of the need — his father's chief gain was worth less to him than one season's sailing.","Reason three, beyond the bullets, is what he could not use. The obvious well had been drawn on already, because the Saladin tithe of 1188 had taken a tenth of revenues and moveable goods before he was king, and the cash had to be in hand before he sailed, since a king in Palestine could not levy or collect anything in England.","Close on the cost of the method rather than the total: the remark reported by Richard of Devizes is a joke with a point, because every sale traded a future right for present coin, and the men who bought offices then had to get their money back out of the people they governed."],"cues":["What is the money actually buying, and why can a lord's customary service not buy it?","Why would anyone pay three thousand pounds for a job?","What did Richard hand back to the king of Scots, and what does the price tell you about his need?","Which source of money had already been used before Richard came to the throne?","What is the hidden cost of paying for a crusade by selling offices and rights?"]},{"title":"Choosing the criterion for a sixteen-mark judgement on Richard's capture","setup":"The question is: 'Richard I's own conduct on crusade was the main reason why he was taken prisoner in Germany in December 1192.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] You may use the following in your answer: the first bullet is Duke Leopold V of Austria, the second is the murder of Conrad of Montferrat. You must also use information of your own. Your evidence: Leopold claimed a commander's share when Acre fell in July 1191, his banner was thrown down from the walls and he went home; Conrad of Montferrat, who was Leopold's cousin, was elected king of Jerusalem in April 1192 and murdered at Tyre within the month, and Richard was accused of a hand in it; Richard had set aside his betrothal to Philip II's sister Alice and married Berengaria of Navarre in 1191, and Philip went home an enemy; Richard had allied with Tancred of Sicily, whose crown the Emperor Henry VI claimed, and his own brother-in-law Henry the Lion was the emperor's great rival in Germany; the western sea routes ran past Philip's lands and those of his allies, so Richard sailed up the Adriatic instead; he was shipwrecked on its northern coast and crossed Germany in disguise; he was recognised and seized at Erdberg near Vienna shortly before Christmas 1192 and held at Durnstein; and in 1193 Leopold handed him to Henry VI, who demanded 150,000 marks. Decide the criterion on which you will judge the words main reason.","steps":["State the test before anything else, because main reason is a ranking word: ask which factor, had it been different, would have let Richard reach his own lands a free man.","Put the case for the statement in one pile: the banner at Acre turned a duke into an enemy, the setting aside of Alice turned the king of France into one, the alliance with Tancred set him against the emperor, and the accusation over the murder of Conrad of Montferrat gave all three a charge to hang on him. Every item in that pile is something Richard chose to do.","Put the case against in the other pile: Henry VI had his own claim in Sicily and his own war with Richard's Welf kinsmen, so his hostility did not begin at Acre; Philip was overlord of Richard's French lands and would have been a rival whatever happened on crusade; and the storm that wrecked the ship on the Adriatic coast was luck, not conduct.","Apply the test instead of counting the piles: being hated does not capture a king, and what put Richard within reach of Leopold was the road he took. The road was narrowed to the Adriatic by enmities he had made, and he was thrown onto it on foot by a storm he had not.","Write the conclusion as the last step of the argument, not a new claim: agree, but qualify it, because his conduct chose his enemies and so chose his route, while chance decided at which point on that route he was taken."],"cues":["What single question can you ask of every factor so that one of them is able to lose?","Which of Richard's own acts turned a rival into an enemy willing to lay hands on a crusader?","What was already true about the emperor before Richard ever reached Acre?","Why is being hated across Europe not the same thing as being captured in Austria?","How do you agree with a statement without simply repeating it?"]},{"title":"Writing one paragraph on why men took the cross, point to mini-judgement","setup":"The question is: 'Religious motives were the main reason why men joined Richard I's crusading army in 1190.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] You may use the following in your answer: the first bullet is the remission of sins, the second is the freezing of a crusader's debts. You must also use information of your own. You are writing one paragraph from the middle of the answer, putting the case for the statement. Your evidence: a crusade was an armed pilgrimage authorised by the Pope, and taking the cross was a public vow the Church could enforce; the crusader was promised remission of the penalties of his sins and the protection of his lands and family while he was away; interest on his debts was suspended, which is how a knight could mortgage or sell land to equip himself; Baldwin, Archbishop of Canterbury, preached the crusade in Wales in 1188, went east himself and died at the siege of Acre in November 1190; Hubert Walter, then Bishop of Salisbury, served with the army; Pope Urban II had preached the First Crusade at Clermont in 1095 and Jerusalem had been taken in 1099, so crusading was the most prestigious thing a fighting man could do; and it was the news that the holy places had passed out of Christian hands in 1187 that set the whole expedition going. Write the paragraph so that it makes a point, proves it, explains it and ends with a mini-judgement.","steps":["Make the point do causal work: claim not that these men were pious but that the Church was offering a reward nothing else in their world could match, so it moved men who would never have crossed a county for wages.","Prove it with the precise detail: the vow was public and enforceable and the promise was remission of the penalties of sin, and the men who took it included the Archbishop of Canterbury, who preached the crusade in Wales, sailed east himself and died outside Acre rather than send others in his place.","Explain the half of the evidence that looks like an objection, which is the material privileges: the suspension of interest on debts and the Church's protection of a crusader's land and family were granted because of the vow and to no one else, so they are the Church making a religious act affordable rather than a separate motive competing with it.","End with a mini-judgement inside the paragraph: say that on this evidence the spiritual reward is what made an expedition of this size possible at all, and name what you will weigh against it next, which is the lordship that summoned a knight whether he was moved by any of it or not."],"cues":["What must religion be shown to have done, if this paragraph is to be about causation?","Which man in your evidence shows that those who preached it believed it themselves?","Why do the debt privileges not prove that men went for money?","What does the last sentence of a paragraph have to do before you move to the next factor?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Where did Richard's army come closest to Jerusalem, when, and what did it do?","a":"Beit Nuba, about a day's march from the city, in January 1192 and again in June 1192; on both occasions the army turned back rather than besiege it."},{"q":"What did the Treaty of Jaffa settle?","a":"Agreed on 2 September 1192: a truce of about three years, the coast from Tyre to Jaffa left in Christian hands, the defences of Ascalon dismantled, unarmed Christian pilgrims allowed to visit Jerusalem, and the city itself left under Saladin's rule."},{"q":"How much was demanded for Richard's release, and how was it raised in England?","a":"150,000 marks, or 100,000 pounds: raised by a tax of a quarter of movable goods and income, scutage on knights' fees, plate taken from churches and monasteries and a year's wool crop from the Cistercians, under Eleanor of Aquitaine and Hubert Walter."},{"q":"Why did John's marriage to Isabella of Angouleme lead to war with Philip II?","a":"She was betrothed to Hugh de Lusignan; the Lusignans appealed to Philip as John's overlord, John failed to answer the summons to Philip's court, and in 1202 Philip declared his French lands forfeit and supported Arthur of Brittany's claim."}]},"room":{"facts":["Booklet B, the British depth study, carries 32 marks: Questions 1(a) and 1(b), 'Describe one feature of...', 2 marks each and on two different aspects of content, Question 2, an 'Explain why...' at 12 marks with two stimulus points, and a choice of two 16-mark questions, Question 3 or Question 4, \"'...' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\", also with two stimulus points. There are no spelling, punctuation and grammar marks anywhere on Paper 2.","The describe task is point-marked, not levelled: one mark for each valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about it, four marks in all. From June 2025 it is printed as two separate 2-mark parts, Question 1(a) and Question 1(b), each asking for one feature of a different aspect of content. No reasons and no judgement are wanted, and a second feature in either part earns nothing.","The caps as the mark scheme now prints them: on the 12-mark Question 2, a maximum of 8 marks and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; on the 16-mark Question 3 or 4, a maximum of 11 marks and no access to Level 4 on the same condition. Two stimulus bullets are two aspects, so an answer built only on them cannot pass those ceilings.","Paper 2 lasts 1 hour 50 minutes for 64 marks, about 55 minutes a booklet, so booklet B's 32 marks run at about a minute a mark: roughly 5 minutes on Questions 1(a) and 1(b) together, 20 on the 12-mark Question 2 and 25 on the 16-mark Question 3 or 4, with the planning time for the judgement taken out of that 25 and about five minutes left in hand.","The spine of dates for this key topic: 4 July 1187 Hattin; November 1187 Richard takes the cross; 12 July 1191 Acre surrenders; 7 September 1191 Arsuf; 2 September 1192 the Treaty of Jaffa; December 1192 the capture near Vienna; 4 February 1194 the release; 6 April 1199 Richard dies; 1 August 1202 Mirebeau; 6 March 1204 Chateau Gaillard falls; 24 June 1204 Rouen surrenders."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the privileges the Church gave to those who took the cross. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of Saladin's capture of Jerusalem in 1187. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked as two separate 2-mark parts: in each part one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about it, and a second feature within a part earns nothing. (a) One feature: a crusader was promised remission of the penalties of his sins; credit also Church protection of his lands and family, the suspension of interest on his debts, or the standing of the crusade as an armed pilgrimage. (b) One feature: the city was given up by agreement rather than stormed; credit also the destruction of the kingdom's field army at Hattin on 4 July 1187, the surrender of Jerusalem in October 1187, the holy places passing out of Christian hands for the first time since 1099, or the papal call for a new crusade that followed.","sol":"1(a) Feature: a crusader was promised remission of the penalties of his sins (1 mark). Supporting information: the Pope granted this to a man who took the cross in public, so the expedition counted as an armed pilgrimage and a penance rather than an ordinary war, and the Church protected his lands and family while he was away (1 mark). 1(b) Feature: Jerusalem surrendered to Saladin rather than being stormed (1 mark). Supporting information: Saladin had destroyed the kingdom of Jerusalem's field army at Hattin on 4 July 1187, so no relief was possible and the city passed to him by terms that October, the first time since 1099 that the holy places were not in Christian hands (1 mark).","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Read both parts first: (a) is about what the Church promised a man who took the cross, (b) about how Saladin came to hold the city in 1187.","For (a) take one promise, about his soul or about the property and people he left behind; for (b) take one plain fact about how the city changed hands, not the reasons the crusade was later called.","One short paragraph each: name the privilege or the fact first, then add one precise detail — a date, a battle or a place — as the supporting information."],"codes":["B2-2 — 1 The nature of crusading: The concept of crusade and attitudes in England","B2-2 — 2 Richard and the Third Crusade: The immediate causes of the Third Crusade"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the crusading army Richard I took to the Holy Land in 1190. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the siege of Acre. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked as two separate 2-mark parts: in each part one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about it, and a second feature within a part earns nothing. (a) One feature: the army was Angevin rather than purely English; credit also its composition of household and baronial knights, foot soldiers, crossbowmen, engineers and sailors, the fleet that sailed round Spain, the leadership of churchmen such as Baldwin of Canterbury and Hubert Walter, or the mixture of paid and vowed service. (b) One feature: it was a siege that lasted years; credit also that it had begun in 1189 before either king arrived, the arrival of Philip II in April 1191 and of Richard on 8 June 1191, the surrender of the city on 12 July 1191, or the death of Archbishop Baldwin there in November 1190.","sol":"1(a) Feature: the army was not purely English (1 mark). Supporting information: Richard ruled Normandy, Anjou and Aquitaine as well as England, so knights and foot soldiers came from across the Angevin lands, and while the fleet sailed from English ports round Spain into the Mediterranean, Richard himself rode overland through France to Marseille (1 mark). 1(b) Feature: Acre was besieged for years before it fell (1 mark). Supporting information: the siege had begun in 1189, Philip II reached it in April 1191 and Richard on 8 June, and the city finally surrendered on 12 July 1191 (1 mark).","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a): remember who Richard was, not only what he was king of. Part (b): ask how long the crusaders had been sitting outside Acre before their kings arrived.","For (a) one feature can be about where the men came from and another about who led them; for (b) take the length of the siege or the moment it ended, not the quarrel that followed it.","One short paragraph each: name the feature in a short sentence, then attach one named person, place or date as the supporting information."],"codes":["B2-2 — 1 The nature of crusading: The nature of the English crusading army","B2-2 — 2 Richard and the Third Crusade: Richard's quarrel with Philip II and his victories"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the Treaty of Jaffa (1192). [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the battle of Arsuf (1191). [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked as two separate 2-mark parts: in each part one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about it, and a second feature within a part earns nothing. (a) One feature: it was a truce of about three years agreed on 2 September 1192; credit also the coastal strip from Tyre to Jaffa left in Christian hands, the dismantling of the defences of Ascalon, the right of unarmed Christian pilgrims to visit Jerusalem, or the fact that the city stayed under Saladin's rule. (b) One feature: it was a victory over Saladin in open battle on 7 September 1191; credit also the march south from Acre towards Jaffa with the fleet alongside for supplies, the survival of Saladin's field army after the defeat, or that it secured the coast road rather than the road to Jerusalem.","sol":"1(a) Feature: it was a truce rather than a peace or a conquest (1 mark). Supporting information: Richard and Saladin agreed on 2 September 1192 to stop fighting for about three years, the Christians kept the ports from Tyre down to Jaffa and unarmed pilgrims were allowed into the holy places, but Jerusalem itself stayed in Saladin's hands (1 mark). 1(b) Feature: Arsuf was a victory won in open battle (1 mark). Supporting information: marching south from Acre towards Jaffa on 7 September 1191, with the fleet alongside carrying supplies, Richard beat Saladin in the field, though Saladin's army got away and was still in being the next year (1 mark).","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a) is the point at which Richard settles for what he can actually hold; part (b) is the one day on which he beat Saladin in the field.","For (a) split what the treaty decided into what happened to the fighting and what happened to the land; for (b) say what kind of fighting it was and what it did or did not destroy.","Two short paragraphs, one for each part: state the term or the outcome, then add the precise date or the detail on the ground that proves it."],"codes":["B2-2 — 2 Richard and the Third Crusade: The failure to recapture Jerusalem","B2-2 — 2 Richard and the Third Crusade: Richard's quarrel with Philip II and his victories"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of Richard I's journey home from the Holy Land in 1192. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of Richard I's captivity in Germany. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked as two separate 2-mark parts: in each part one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about it, and a second feature within a part earns nothing. (a) One feature: he could not take the direct route home; credit also the departure on 9 October 1192, the closing of the western route by Philip II and his allies, the voyage up the Adriatic and the shipwreck on its northern coast, the attempt to cross Germany in disguise, or the capture near Vienna shortly before Christmas 1192. (b) One feature: he was held for ransom by the Emperor Henry VI; credit also his seizure by Duke Leopold V of Austria and the handover to the Emperor in 1193, the demand for 150,000 marks, the hostages required as security, the offers John and Philip II made to keep him in prison, or his release on 4 February 1194.","sol":"1(a) Feature: Richard could not go home the direct way (1 mark). Supporting information: he sailed on 9 October 1192, but the sea route home ran past lands held by Philip II and his allies, so he turned up the Adriatic, was shipwrecked near Aquileia and tried to cross Germany in disguise (1 mark). 1(b) Feature: he was held by the Emperor until a ransom was agreed (1 mark). Supporting information: Duke Leopold V of Austria, whose banner had been thrown down at Acre, seized him near Vienna just before Christmas 1192 and handed him to the Emperor Henry VI in 1193, who demanded 150,000 marks and hostages and released him on 4 February 1194 (1 mark).","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a): ask why a king coming home from a holy war had to travel in secret at all. Part (b): ask who ended up holding him, and what that man wanted.","For (a) one feature is about the route he was forced to take, the other about how the journey ended; for (b) take the terms of his imprisonment rather than how the money was collected in England.","Name the feature, then attach the place, the person or the sum that proves it — one short paragraph for each part."],"codes":["B2-2 — 3 Aftermath of the crusade: Richard's return from the Holy Land","B2-2 — 3 Aftermath of the crusade: Richard's capture, the ransom and its burden on England"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the way Richard I's ransom was raised in England. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of attitudes in England to crusading in the years 1188 to 1190. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked as two separate 2-mark parts: in each part one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about it, and a second feature within a part earns nothing. (a) One feature: it was raised from property rather than from ordinary revenue; credit also the demand of 150,000 marks, the tax of a quarter of movable goods and income, scutage on knights' fees, the stripping of church plate and the seizure of the Cistercians' wool crop, the hostages given as security, or the organising role of Eleanor of Aquitaine and Hubert Walter. (b) One feature: crusading carried enormous prestige for a fighting man; credit also Henry II's Saladin tithe of 1188, the preaching of the crusade by Archbishop Baldwin in Wales in 1188, taking the cross without ever going, or the violence against the Jewish communities of London in September 1189 and York in March 1190.","sol":"1(a) Feature: the money was taken from what people owned rather than out of ordinary royal revenue (1 mark). Supporting information: the demand was 150,000 marks, about 100,000 pounds, far beyond what the Crown took in a year, so a tax of a quarter of every free man's movable goods and income was imposed across the kingdom, with scutage on knights' fees on top (1 mark). 1(b) Feature: enthusiasm for the crusade turned violent at home (1 mark). Supporting information: attacks on the Jewish community of London began during Richard's coronation celebrations on 3 September 1189, and in March 1190 the Jewish community of York died at Clifford's Tower, most by their own hand (1 mark).","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a): the sum is too large for the king's usual income, so ask where else wealth was stored in England. Part (b): ask what people in England were doing about the crusade before Richard sailed.","For (a) take the tax on ordinary people's goods or what was taken from the richest institution in the country; for (b) one attitude will do — the prestige, the reluctance of a king who took the cross and stayed, or the violence that came with the enthusiasm.","One short paragraph each: state the method or the attitude, then add the figure, the fraction, the date or the place that proves it."],"codes":["B2-2 — 3 Aftermath of the crusade: Richard's capture, the ransom and its burden on England","B2-2 — 1 The nature of crusading: The concept of crusade and attitudes in England"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of Chateau Gaillard. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the events at Mirebeau in 1202. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked as two separate 2-mark parts: in each part one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about it, and a second feature within a part earns nothing. (a) One feature: it was built in concentric rings on a chalk spur above the Seine at Les Andelys; credit also the outer, middle and inner baileys with rock-cut ditches and a keep, the river works (the fortified town of Petit Andely, the island fort, the stockade), the building of it in about two years from 1196, or its cost, estimated at about 11,500 pounds. (b) One feature: John took his enemies completely by surprise; credit also that Arthur of Brittany and the Lusignan leaders were besieging Eleanor of Aquitaine there, the forced march that reached the town on 1 August 1202, the capture of Arthur and the Lusignans, or the ill-treatment of the prisoners that followed.","sol":"1(a) Feature: it was built in concentric rings on a cliff (1 mark). Supporting information: an attacker at Les Andelys had to take an outer triangular bailey, then a middle bailey, then an inner bailey with a keep, with ditches cut into the chalk between them, and the spur above the Seine left only one line of approach (1 mark). 1(b) Feature: Mirebeau was a complete surprise (1 mark). Supporting information: on 1 August 1202 a forced march caught Arthur of Brittany and the Lusignan leaders besieging John's mother Eleanor of Aquitaine in the town, and John took them all prisoner (1 mark).","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a): picture an attacker walking towards the castle and count how many times he has to break in. Part (b): ask who was trapped inside Mirebeau and who arrived unexpectedly.","For (a) one feature can be about the shape of the defences, the other about what the site let Richard control; for (b) take what happened on the day rather than what John did with the prisoners afterwards.","Describe the feature, then add one hard detail — a bailey, a river work, a date or a name — as the supporting information for that part."],"codes":["B2-2 — 4 Richard, John and the loss of Normandy: Richard and Chateau Gaillard","B2-2 — 4 Richard, John and the loss of Normandy: John and the fall of Chateau Gaillard"]},{"q":"Explain why Richard I was determined to go on crusade. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the vow Richard took in 1187 • Richard's reputation as a soldier. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 / AO1 6. Credit the fall of Jerusalem to Saladin in 1187 and the papal call, the binding vow of November 1187 taken before Richard was king, the pursuit of military reputation against Barbarossa and Philip II, Angevin family tradition and piety, and the money-raising the expedition licensed. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"Richard went because he had already promised to, because the promise served everything else he wanted, and because a king who had spent his life fighting could not let other kings answer the Pope's call for him. The first reason is the disaster in the East. Saladin, sultan of Egypt and Syria, destroyed the field army of the kingdom of Jerusalem at Hattin on 4 July 1187 and took the city that October, so for the first time since 1099 the holy places were out of Christian hands. Richard, then only count of Poitou with his father Henry II still alive, took the cross at Tours in November 1187, before Pope Gregory VIII's appeal for a new crusade reached France. That vow is the hard fact behind everything afterwards: a crusader's oath was enforceable by the Church, and Richard had made his before he had a kingdom to leave. The second reason is reputation. Richard had been campaigning in Aquitaine since his teens and had built his standing on sieges and hard marching. The crusade was the one war in Christendom that could make a soldier's name permanent, and its other leaders were the Emperor Frederick Barbarossa and Philip II of France. To stay at home while both went would have been to concede first place in Europe to his own overlord. The third reason goes beyond the bullets: the expedition suited Richard as a ruler. Crusading was expected of his family and his class, and Henry II had taken the cross, never gone and levied the Saladin tithe of 1188 instead, a tenth of revenues and moveable goods; a son who repeated that would have looked like his father. The crusade also licensed immediate money-raising, and Richard used it, selling offices, sheriffdoms and charters and releasing William the Lion of Scotland from the Treaty of Falaise for 10,000 marks in December 1189. The three fit together. The fall of Jerusalem made a crusade necessary, the vow made it binding, and his ambitions made it attractive, which is why he sailed in 1190 barely a year after his coronation.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Separate what had happened in the East from what Richard wanted for himself.","Put his reasons in an order: what obliged him, what tempted him, and what the expedition was useful for.","Take three reasons in turn, and end each paragraph by saying what that reason made unavoidable or attractive, not simply what it was."],"codes":["B2-2 — 2 Richard and the Third Crusade: The immediate causes of the Third Crusade","B2-2 — 1 The nature of crusading: The concept of crusade and attitudes in England"]},{"q":"Explain why Richard I quarrelled with Philip II during the Third Crusade. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • Berengaria of Navarre • the siege of Acre. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 / AO1 6. Credit the broken betrothal to Philip's sister Alice and the marriage in Cyprus in May 1191, the disputes at Messina over the winter of 1190 to 1191, the rivalry over the spoils and over the throne of Jerusalem (Guy of Lusignan against Conrad of Montferrat), the imbalance in wealth and reputation between the two kings, and Philip's interests as overlord in France. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"The two kings quarrelled because they were rivals at home who had agreed to be partners abroad, and everything that happened on the way east reminded them of it. The first cause is dynastic. Richard had been betrothed for years to Alice, Philip's sister, and never married her; in May 1191 he married Berengaria of Navarre at Limassol in Cyprus instead, a match that secured his southern frontier in Aquitaine but publicly insulted Philip's house. The two had already fallen out at Messina over the winter of 1190 to 1191, where the armies wintered together and Richard's quarrel with Tancred of Sicily over his sister Joanna's dower, settled by storming the city in October 1190, set the kings against one another. The second cause is the command of the crusade itself. Philip reached Acre in April 1191 and Richard on 8 June, and Richard arrived with more money, more ships and the prestige of having just conquered Cyprus. When Acre surrendered on 12 July 1191 they divided over the spoils and, more seriously, over who should be king of Jerusalem: Richard backed Guy of Lusignan, Philip backed Conrad of Montferrat. A crusade with two kings and no agreed commander had no way of settling such a dispute. The third cause goes beyond the bullets and explains why the quarrel mattered rather than merely why it happened. Philip's real interest lay in France, where he was the overlord of Richard's continental lands. A long absence suited him far less than it suited Richard, and within weeks of Acre's fall he sailed home pleading illness; he was soon dealing with John and pressing Normandy. Richard, watching those lands from Palestine, read every dispute as evidence of bad faith. The causes run into one another: a broken betrothal made the partnership personal, the command of the siege made the split public, and Philip's French ambitions made it permanent, which is why the crusade lost half its leadership immediately after its first great victory.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Remember that these two men were also vassal and overlord in France.","Group the causes: a family quarrel, a quarrel about command, and a quarrel about interests at home.","Three paragraphs, each ending with what that dispute cost the crusade rather than what it was about."],"codes":["B2-2 — 2 Richard and the Third Crusade: Richard's quarrel with Philip II and his victories","B2-2 — 2 Richard and the Third Crusade: The immediate causes of the Third Crusade"]},{"q":"Explain why Richard I failed to recapture Jerusalem. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • Saladin's army • Beit Nuba. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 / AO1 6. Credit Saladin's field army surviving Arsuf, the supply line back to Jaffa, the stripped country and spoiled wells, the argument of the Templars and Hospitallers that the city could not be held once vows were fulfilled, the divided leadership and the murder of Conrad of Montferrat in April 1192, Philip's departure and the threat to Richard's lands, and the Treaty of Jaffa as the outcome. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"Jerusalem was never taken because Richard could reach the city but could neither besiege it safely nor hold it afterwards, and because the time he needed was being taken from him in France. The first reason is the enemy behind him. Richard beat Saladin in open battle at Arsuf on 7 September 1191 and took Jaffa, but Arsuf destroyed neither Saladin's army nor his rule. To besiege a walled city in the Judaean hills the crusaders would have had to sit still while an undefeated field army moved freely behind them and cut the road back to the coast, which was also the road their supplies came up. That is why the army reached Beit Nuba, about a day's march from Jerusalem, in January 1192 and again in June, and turned back both times without being beaten. The second reason is that capture would not have meant possession. Saladin had stripped the country and spoiled the wells, and the fortresses of the interior were his. The Templars and Hospitallers, who knew the region, argued that once the city fell most crusaders would count their vows fulfilled and sail home, leaving a garrison too small to hold an inland city ringed by hostile territory. The third reason goes beyond the bullets: the crusade's politics were collapsing. Philip II had gone home at the end of July 1191 with much of the French contingent and was pressing Normandy while John built a party in England. The kingdom Richard was fighting for could not even agree on a ruler, since Conrad of Montferrat was elected king in April 1192 and murdered at Tyre within the month. These pull the same way. With a strong enemy in the field, no means of holding the prize and a duchy under threat at home, the outcome Richard took on 2 September 1192 was the rational one: a truce that kept the coast from Tyre to Jaffa and opened the holy places to unarmed pilgrims, but left the city Muslim.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["The question is not why he lost a battle, because he did not lose one.","Ask two different questions about the city: could he attack it, and could he keep it?","Three paragraphs, each ending with what that reason made impossible, and finish on the truce as the consequence."],"codes":["B2-2 — 2 Richard and the Third Crusade: The failure to recapture Jerusalem","B2-2 — 2 Richard and the Third Crusade: Richard's quarrel with Philip II and his victories"]},{"q":"Explain why Richard I's ransom was such a burden on England. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the Church • taxation. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 / AO1 6. Credit the size of the demand (150,000 marks, 100,000 pounds, several times ordinary royal revenue), the quarter tax on movable goods and income, scutage, the stripping of church plate and the Cistercian wool crop, the hostages, the administrative effort under Eleanor of Aquitaine and Hubert Walter, and Richard's departure in May 1194. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"The ransom was a burden because the sum was beyond anything royal government normally handled, because it had to be taken out of things people actually owned, and because England got very little back for it. Start with the size. Duke Leopold V of Austria seized Richard near Vienna in December 1192 and handed him to the Emperor Henry VI, who demanded 150,000 marks. A mark was two-thirds of a pound, so the demand was 100,000 pounds, several times what the Crown collected in an ordinary year, and hostages were required as security as well. No king could pay that out of rents, fines and customs; it had to be found in the wealth of the kingdom itself. The second reason is the shape of the tax that followed. A levy of a quarter of every free man's movable goods and income reached far below the barons: livestock, stored grain, tools and the stock of a townsman's shop were all assessed, and scutage was taken from knights' fees on top. A tax on movables bit at once in a way a tax on land did not, because it took the working capital of a household. The third reason is the Church, whose treasure was the only concentration of portable wealth large enough to matter. Gold and silver plate was stripped from churches and monasteries, and the Cistercian houses surrendered a whole year's wool crop, which was the income of the order that dominated English sheep farming and the crop the Flemish clothiers bought. Beyond the bullets lies the sharpest point of all. Eleanor of Aquitaine and the justiciar Hubert Walter proved that royal government could assess and gather an emergency levy across the whole kingdom, and having been released on 4 February 1194 and crowned again at Winchester in April, Richard left England in May and never returned. The country had paid several years of its wealth for a king it did not see again, and had shown the Crown exactly how much more could be taken.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Think about the difference between a big bill and a bill that cannot be paid out of income.","Sort the burden into the size of the demand, who was taxed, and what had to be surrendered outright.","Three paragraphs, each ending with what that part of the ransom actually took away from people, and close on what England got in return."],"codes":["B2-2 — 3 Aftermath of the crusade: Richard's capture, the ransom and its burden on England","B2-2 — 3 Aftermath of the crusade: Richard's return from the Holy Land"]},{"q":"Explain why Normandy was lost in 1204. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • Chateau Gaillard • Philip II. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 / AO1 6. Credit the marriage to Isabella of Angouleme and the Lusignan appeal, the forfeiture of 1202 and Philip's use of his rights as overlord, the disappearance of Arthur after Mirebeau and the desertion of barons such as William des Roches, the failed relief of Chateau Gaillard and its fall on 6 March 1204, John's departure in December 1203, the drain of Richard's ransom and wars, Philip's growing revenues and the death of Eleanor in April 1204. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"Normandy fell because Philip II was given a legal case, a divided enemy and a military opportunity, and because John supplied all three. The legal case came first. In August 1200 John married Isabella of Angouleme, who was betrothed to Hugh de Lusignan. The Lusignans appealed to Philip as overlord of John's French lands, a lordship John had himself acknowledged at the Treaty of Le Goulet in 1200, when he paid a relief of 20,000 marks to be recognised as Richard's heir in France. John did not answer the summons to Philip's court, so in 1202 his French lands were declared forfeit and Philip took up the claim of Arthur of Brittany. The war that followed was fought on Philip's terms, as an overlord disciplining a vassal. The second reason is the loss of loyalty. John's one triumph, the forced march that surprised Arthur and the Lusignan leaders besieging his mother Eleanor at Mirebeau on 1 August 1202, was thrown away. Prisoners were ill-treated and some starved, and Arthur disappeared in John's custody in 1203. Lords who held land on both sides of a frontier had to choose, and men such as William des Roches, who had helped deliver the victory at Mirebeau, changed sides. Garrisons that surrender are not beaten in battle; they are deserted. The third reason goes beyond the bullets: money and method. Philip's royal revenues had grown steadily, while English resources had already been drained by Richard's ransom and by his five years of war to recover what was lost during the captivity. John's mercenaries were therefore harder to keep paid, and Philip could afford to reduce castles one at a time instead of risking a battle. It all came to a point at Chateau Gaillard. Philip besieged it from late August 1203, John's combined river and land attempt to relieve it failed, and in December 1203 he sailed for England. Roger de Lacy's garrison held out until 6 March 1204, but once Richard's great fortress had gone there was nothing to stop Philip, and Rouen surrendered on 24 June 1204.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what Philip needed before he could attack at all, and note that the answer is not an army.","Sort your material into the legal quarrel, the loyalty of the barons and resources, then let the siege draw them together.","Three reasons in turn, each ending with what it gave Philip that he had not had before."],"codes":["B2-2 — 4 Richard, John and the loss of Normandy: John and the fall of Chateau Gaillard","B2-2 — 4 Richard, John and the loss of Normandy: The competing aims of Richard, John and Philip II"]},{"q":"'Saladin's capture of Jerusalem in 1187 was the main reason why Richard I went on crusade.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • Hattin • the Saladin tithe of 1188. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 / AO1 6. Credit the fall of Jerusalem after Hattin (4 July 1187) and the papal appeal as the occasion of the crusade, weighed against Richard's personal vow of November 1187, his pursuit of military reputation against Barbarossa and Philip II, Angevin family expectation and piety (Henry II took the cross, never went and levied the Saladin tithe of 1188), and the money-raising the expedition licensed. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"The test has to be whether Richard would have gone had Jerusalem not fallen, and whether anything else on its own would have got him there. The case for the statement is strong, because the fall of the city created the crusade. Saladin, sultan of Egypt and Syria, destroyed the field army of the kingdom of Jerusalem at Hattin on 4 July 1187, and the city surrendered that October, so for the first time since 1099 the holy places were in Muslim hands. Pope Gregory VIII called for a new expedition in October 1187, but Richard did not wait for it, taking the cross at Tours that November; Philip II followed at Gisors in January 1188 and Frederick Barbarossa at Mainz in March 1188. Without Hattin there was no crusade to join and no vow for Richard to take, and the dating settles it: Richard acted within weeks of the news. That same fact, though, is the strongest evidence against reading the statement too simply. The vow was taken by a man with no kingdom, while his father Henry II was still alive and he himself was only count of Poitou, and it bound him personally. A crusader's oath was enforceable by the Church, his lands were placed under Church protection and his debts frozen; to break it publicly would have destroyed the reputation for good faith on which his authority in Aquitaine rested. By his coronation on 3 September 1189 the question was no longer whether to go but how to pay for it. The second alternative is reputation, which explains the intensity of his commitment rather than its origin. Richard had campaigned in Aquitaine since his teens and was known for sieges and hard marching. The Third Crusade was the one war in which the emperor and the king of France were both taking the field, so to stay behind would have conceded first place in Christendom to Philip, who was also his overlord in France. That is why he raised money on a scale nobody expected, selling offices, sheriffdoms and charters and releasing William the Lion of Scotland from the Treaty of Falaise for 10,000 marks in December 1189. A third factor is easy to miss. Crusading was expected of Richard's family and his class, and his father had taken the cross, never gone, and levied the Saladin tithe of 1188 instead, a tenth of revenues and moveable goods. An English king who repeated that would have looked like his father. Piety, family record and the prestige of the cross all pointed the same way. The judgement turns on separating the occasion from the motive. Saladin's victory is the necessary cause: there is no crusade without it, and every other factor operates only once it has happened. But it is not sufficient to explain this king, because Henry II never went at all and Philip stayed only until Acre had fallen, while Richard went further, stayed longer and spent more than either. The capture of Jerusalem made the crusade; Richard's vow, his ambition and his reputation made him its leader. So the statement holds as an account of cause and fails as an account of character, which is why it needs the qualification that Hattin explains the expedition and not the man who led it.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Main reason means you must choose between a cause that made the crusade happen at all and causes that made this particular king lead it.","Separate the occasion from the motives, and test each alternative by asking whether it would have sent Richard east on its own.","Open by naming the test you will use, weigh at least two alternatives, and make the last paragraph apply that same test rather than list both sides again."],"codes":["B2-2 — 2 Richard and the Third Crusade: The immediate causes of the Third Crusade","B2-2 — 1 The nature of crusading: The concept of crusade and attitudes in England","B2-2 — 1 The nature of crusading: The nature of the English crusading army"]},{"q":"'The quarrel with Philip II was the main reason why the Third Crusade failed to recapture Jerusalem.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the siege of Acre • Beit Nuba. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 / AO1 6. Credit the quarrels at Messina and over Berengaria, the rivalry over the throne of Jerusalem at Acre and Philip's departure at the end of July 1191, weighed against Saladin's undefeated field army after Arsuf, the impossibility of holding the city once vows were fulfilled, the stripped country and spoiled wells, and the murder of Conrad of Montferrat in April 1192. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"To rank the quarrel the test must be whether a united crusade would have taken and held Jerusalem, because a factor is only the main reason if removing it changes the outcome. There is a real case for the statement. The two kings arrived as rivals: Richard had never married Alice, Philip's sister, and in May 1191 married Berengaria of Navarre at Limassol in Cyprus instead, and they had already fallen out at Messina over the winter of 1190 to 1191. At the siege of Acre the quarrel became strategic, because they backed different men for the throne of the kingdom they had come to restore, Richard supporting Guy of Lusignan and Philip supporting Conrad of Montferrat, and they divided over the spoils when the city surrendered on 12 July 1191. Within weeks Philip sailed home with much of the French contingent, so the crusade lost half its leadership immediately after its first great success. Worse, the quarrel followed Richard east: Philip pressed Normandy and encouraged John, so every month in Palestine cost Richard something in France, which is a reason to settle for a truce rather than dig in for a long siege. Against that stands the enemy. Richard beat Saladin in open battle at Arsuf on 7 September 1191, but Saladin's army was not destroyed and his rule was not broken. Besieging a walled city in the hills would have meant sitting still while an undefeated field army moved behind the crusaders and cut the supply road back to Jaffa. The army reached Beit Nuba, about a day's march from Jerusalem, in January 1192 and again in June, and turned back both times without ever being defeated in the field. A larger army would have faced the same problem, and would have needed more water in a country whose wells had been spoiled. The deeper objection is the one neither bullet prompts: even success would not have lasted. Saladin held the fortresses of the interior, and the Templars and Hospitallers argued that once Jerusalem fell most crusaders would count their vows fulfilled and sail home, leaving a garrison too small to hold an inland city. Philip's men would have gone home too, so his presence changes nothing about the day after the victory. The crusade's politics also failed independently of Philip: Conrad of Montferrat was elected king in April 1192 and murdered at Tyre within the month, so the kingdom Richard was defending could not produce an agreed ruler even after the French had left. Applying the test, the quarrel is not the main reason. It cost the crusade men, unity and time, and by threatening Normandy it shortened Richard's stay, so it belongs high on any list of causes. But the obstacles in front of Beit Nuba were not numerical. They were an undefeated field army, a stripped countryside and a prize that could not be held, and every one of those would have confronted a perfectly united Christian army. The quarrel determined how long Richard had; Saladin's survival determined what he could do with the time. That is why the crusade ended on 2 September 1192 with a truce that kept the coast from Tyre to Jaffa, opened the holy places to unarmed pilgrims, and left the city itself in Saladin's hands.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what a bigger and friendlier army would actually have changed about the ground in front of Beit Nuba.","Separate the factors that cost Richard time from the factors that made the objective unachievable at all.","State the test in your first sentence, weigh at least two rivals to the quarrel, and let the conclusion apply the test instead of summarising it."],"codes":["B2-2 — 2 Richard and the Third Crusade: Richard's quarrel with Philip II and his victories","B2-2 — 2 Richard and the Third Crusade: The failure to recapture Jerusalem","B2-2 — 1 The nature of crusading: The nature of the English crusading army"]},{"q":"'The ransom was the most damaging consequence for England of Richard I's crusade.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the tax on movable goods • the Church. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 / AO1 6. Credit the size and collection of the ransom (150,000 marks, the quarter tax, scutage, church plate, the Cistercian wool crop, hostages), weighed against the cost of equipping the crusade from 1189 and the sale of offices, the effects of an absent king and John's intrigues, the ground lost in Normandy during the captivity, and the machinery of extraordinary taxation the collection left behind. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"Most damaging asks for a ranking, and the fair test is what was still hurting England after Richard died in 1199 rather than what hurt most at the time. The ransom makes the obvious case. When Duke Leopold V of Austria seized Richard near Vienna in December 1192 and handed him to the Emperor Henry VI, the demand was 150,000 marks, which at two-thirds of a pound to the mark is 100,000 pounds, several times what the Crown gathered in an ordinary year. It was met by a tax of a quarter of every free man's movable goods and income, so livestock, stored grain and a townsman's stock were all assessed; by scutage on knights' fees; by stripping plate from churches and monasteries; and by taking a year's wool crop from the Cistercian houses, the great sheep farmers of England. Hostages went to Germany as security. Richard was freed on 4 February 1194. Yet the ransom was a single finite demand, and it was paid. A stronger claim can be made for the cost of the crusade before it ever sailed. From his coronation on 3 September 1189 Richard raised money by selling offices, sheriffdoms and charters, and released William the Lion of Scotland from the Treaty of Falaise for 10,000 marks in December 1189. Selling office put royal authority into the hands of men who had bought it, which is part of why the government he left behind quarrelled so badly. The absence of the king is the second rival, and it lasted the whole reign rather than two years of it. Richard was king for ten years and spent about six months of them in England: a few months after his coronation, and two months in 1194 between his release and his final departure in May. Government therefore ran on deputies, and deputies could be fought over. William Longchamp, the chancellor left in charge, was driven out in 1191; John built a party around the claim that he should govern; and while Richard was a prisoner John and Philip II offered Henry VI money to keep him there, with John conceding Norman territory to Philip. The most lasting damage, though, is the precedent. The collection under Eleanor of Aquitaine and the justiciar Hubert Walter showed that the Crown could assess and gather an emergency levy from every shire, from movables rather than land, and from the Church as well as the laity. That machinery did not vanish when the money was paid; it was available to John, who used heavy taxation to try to recover what had been lost in France, and the resentment it caused runs on into the crisis of his reign. On the test set at the start, the ransom was the sharpest shock but not the most damaging consequence. It was collected and it ended. The absence of the king and the sale of royal office weakened government for as long as Richard lived, and the demonstration that England could be taxed on this scale outlived everyone involved. So the statement is right about the size of the blow and wrong about its depth: the ransom was the heaviest single payment England made, but the lasting damage was the habit of extraordinary taxation it left behind.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Most damaging needs a measure: decide whether you are measuring the size of the blow or how long it lasted.","Line up at least three consequences, the money raised before he sailed, the money raised to free him, and what an absent king did to the government of England.","Name your measure in the opening, apply it to each consequence in turn, and let the conclusion rank them by that same measure."],"codes":["B2-2 — 3 Aftermath of the crusade: Richard's capture, the ransom and its burden on England","B2-2 — 3 Aftermath of the crusade: Richard's return from the Holy Land","B2-2 — 1 The nature of crusading: The concept of crusade and attitudes in England"]},{"q":"'John's own failures were the main reason for the loss of Normandy in 1204.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the fall of Chateau Gaillard • Arthur of Brittany. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 / AO1 6. Credit John's decisions (the marriage to Isabella of Angouleme, the ignored summons and the forfeiture of 1202, the treatment of the Mirebeau prisoners and Arthur's disappearance, the failed relief of Chateau Gaillard, the departure for England in December 1203) weighed against Philip's rights as overlord and growing revenues, the drain of Richard's ransom and wars, divided cross-Channel loyalties and the death of Eleanor of Aquitaine on 1 April 1204. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"The test for main reason has to be which factor, had it been different, would have left Normandy in John's hands: the decisions he took, or the position of the king attacking him. The case for the statement begins with an unnecessary war. In August 1200 John married Isabella of Angouleme, who was already betrothed to Hugh de Lusignan. The Lusignans appealed to Philip II as overlord, a lordship John had himself acknowledged at the Treaty of Le Goulet in 1200, paying a relief of 20,000 marks to be recognised as Richard's heir in France. He then refused to answer the summons to Philip's court, so in 1202 his French lands were declared forfeit and Philip took up the claim of Arthur of Brittany, the son of John's elder brother Geoffrey. John had turned a private quarrel with a vassal into a legal war he could not win in a courtroom. The second failure is the waste of his one success. On 1 August 1202 a forced march surprised Arthur and the Lusignan leaders besieging his mother Eleanor at Mirebeau, and John took them prisoner; that should have ended the war in Poitou. Instead prisoners were ill-treated and some starved, and Arthur disappeared in John's custody in 1203. Barons holding land on both sides of a frontier had to choose, and men such as William des Roches, who had helped deliver Mirebeau, changed sides. An army whose garrisons surrender is not beaten; it is deserted. The third failure is the campaign of 1203. Philip besieged Chateau Gaillard, the fortress Richard had built above the Seine at Les Andelys from 1196, at a cost estimated at about 11,500 pounds, to block the river road to Rouen. John's attempt to relieve it by river and by land failed, and in December 1203 he sailed for England, leaving Roger de Lacy's garrison to hold out until 6 March 1204. Rouen surrendered on 24 June. Against the statement stands Philip himself. He was a far stronger king than his father: his demesne and revenues had grown, he could pay for sieges lasting months, and as overlord he held a legal instrument no English king could answer. English resources had already been drained by Richard's ransom and by his five years of war to recover what was conceded during the captivity, so John's mercenaries were harder to keep paid. There were also losses John could not control: Eleanor of Aquitaine, who held Aquitaine loyal, died on 1 April 1204, weeks after Chateau Gaillard fell. Applying the test gives a qualified agreement. Philip's strength made an attack on Normandy possible and his overlordship made it lawful, and no ruler could have wished either away. But the forfeiture of 1202 followed John's own refusal to appear; the barons who changed sides were reacting to what he had done to Arthur and to his prisoners; and the greatest castle in Europe fell because relief never came. Richard had faced the same Philip with the same rights and had held Normandy, and had built Chateau Gaillard precisely so that Rouen could be defended. The difference between 1199 and 1204 is not the strength of the attacker but the conduct of the defender, so John's failures were the main reason, though they worked on weaknesses he inherited rather than created.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Main reason is a ranking word, so something else has to lose the comparison.","Split the evidence into decisions John made and conditions he inherited, then ask which set the barons were reacting to.","Announce the test in your opening, close every paragraph with an interim judgement, and make the conclusion apply the test rather than repeat both sides."],"codes":["B2-2 — 4 Richard, John and the loss of Normandy: John and the fall of Chateau Gaillard","B2-2 — 4 Richard, John and the loss of Normandy: The competing aims of Richard, John and Philip II","B2-2 — 4 Richard, John and the loss of Normandy: Richard and Chateau Gaillard"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In which month and year did Acre surrender to the crusaders?","a":"July 1191, on 12 July, after a siege that had begun in 1189."},{"q":"How much did the Emperor Henry VI demand for Richard I's release?","a":"150,000 marks, which at two-thirds of a pound to the mark is 100,000 pounds."},{"q":"On what date did Chateau Gaillard surrender to Philip II?","a":"6 March 1204, when Roger de Lacy's garrison gave in after about six months of siege."},{"q":"Where and when did John capture Arthur of Brittany?","a":"At Mirebeau on 1 August 1202, where Arthur and the Lusignans were besieging Eleanor of Aquitaine."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Taking the cross","back":"The public vow to go on crusade: a cross was sewn on the clothing, the Pope granted remission of the penalties of sin, and the crusader's lands and family were protected by the Church while he was away.","code":"B2-2 — 1 The nature of crusading: The concept of crusade and attitudes in England","open":false},{"front":"The York massacre, March 1190","back":"The Jewish community of York died at Clifford's Tower, most by their own hand, in the anti-Jewish violence that began at Richard's coronation in September 1189 as men prepared to leave on crusade.","code":"B2-2 — 1 The nature of crusading: The concept of crusade and attitudes in England","open":false},{"front":"Baldwin, Archbishop of Canterbury","back":"He preached the crusade in Wales in 1188, went east with the army, and died at the siege of Acre in November 1190.","code":"B2-2 — 1 The nature of crusading: The nature of the English crusading army","open":false},{"front":"One reason why men from England went on crusade","back":"The indulgence, which promised remission of the penalties of sin; pay, plunder and the summons of a lord whose service they owed; the freezing of debts and the Church's protection of their lands while they were away.","code":"B2-2 — 1 The nature of crusading: The nature of the English crusading army","open":true},{"front":"Hattin, 4 July 1187","back":"Saladin destroyed the field army of the kingdom of Jerusalem; the city surrendered to him that October, which brought the papal call for the Third Crusade.","code":"B2-2 — 2 Richard and the Third Crusade: The immediate causes of the Third Crusade","open":false},{"front":"One reason why Richard I was determined to go on crusade","back":"The vow he took in November 1187, before he was king and while his father still lived; religious duty and the indulgence the Pope promised; the reputation as the first soldier of Christendom that leading the crusade would bring.","code":"B2-2 — 2 Richard and the Third Crusade: The immediate causes of the Third Crusade","open":true},{"front":"The fall of Acre, 12 July 1191","back":"The port surrendered after a siege begun in 1189. Philip II sailed for France within weeks, and the two kings had already split over who should be king of Jerusalem, Guy of Lusignan or Conrad of Montferrat.","code":"B2-2 — 2 Richard and the Third Crusade: Richard's quarrel with Philip II and his victories","open":false},{"front":"Arsuf, 7 September 1191","back":"Richard defeated Saladin in open battle on the march from Acre to Jaffa. Saladin's army was beaten but not destroyed, so it still barred the road to Jerusalem.","code":"B2-2 — 2 Richard and the Third Crusade: Richard's quarrel with Philip II and his victories","open":false},{"front":"Beit Nuba","back":"The place about a day's march from Jerusalem that the crusaders reached in January 1192 and again in June, turning back both times rather than besiege the city.","code":"B2-2 — 2 Richard and the Third Crusade: The failure to recapture Jerusalem","open":false},{"front":"Treaty of Jaffa, 2 September 1192","back":"A truce of about three years: the Christians kept the coast from Tyre to Jaffa, the defences of Ascalon were dismantled, unarmed pilgrims could visit Jerusalem, and the city stayed under Saladin's rule.","code":"B2-2 — 2 Richard and the Third Crusade: The failure to recapture Jerusalem","open":false},{"front":"Richard's capture, December 1192","back":"Shipwrecked in the Adriatic on his way home, Richard crossed Germany in disguise and was seized near Vienna by Duke Leopold V of Austria, who handed him to the Emperor Henry VI in 1193.","code":"B2-2 — 3 Aftermath of the crusade: Richard's return from the Holy Land","open":false},{"front":"The ransom","back":"150,000 marks, or 100,000 pounds, demanded by the Emperor Henry VI. Raised by a quarter tax on movable goods and income, scutage, church plate and the Cistercians' wool crop, under Eleanor of Aquitaine and Hubert Walter.","code":"B2-2 — 3 Aftermath of the crusade: Richard's capture, the ransom and its burden on England","open":false},{"front":"Treaty of Le Goulet, 1200","back":"John was recognised as Richard's heir to the French lands, but acknowledged Philip II as his overlord and paid a relief of 20,000 marks. It was the bargain that let Philip summon him to court in 1202.","code":"B2-2 — 4 Richard, John and the loss of Normandy: The competing aims of Richard, John and Philip II","open":false},{"front":"Chateau Gaillard","back":"Richard's castle above the Seine at Les Andelys, built in about two years from 1196 at an estimated 11,500 pounds to block the river road to Rouen: concentric baileys, rock-cut ditches, an island fort and a stockade across the river.","code":"B2-2 — 4 Richard, John and the loss of Normandy: Richard and Chateau Gaillard","open":false},{"front":"The loss of Normandy, 1204","back":"Philip II besieged Chateau Gaillard from late August 1203; John's relief attempt failed and he left for England in December. The castle fell on 6 March 1204 and Rouen surrendered on 24 June 1204.","code":"B2-2 — 4 Richard, John and the loss of Normandy: John and the fall of Chateau Gaillard","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (booklet B, the 16-mark judgement)","items":["Question: 'Richard I was a better soldier than he was a king of England.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] You may use the following in your answer: • the march to Jaffa in 1191 • the government of England in his absence. You must also use information of your own. What follows is one paragraph from the middle of the answer, putting the case against the statement.","Judged by what a king was actually for, the government Richard left behind did its work, and the statement measures him against the wrong test. A king was expected to keep the peace, see justice done and defend the inheritance, and all three could be done through deputies. Richard's arrangements bent without breaking: William Longchamp, the chancellor left in charge, was deposed by a council in October 1191 and left the country, yet the exchequer and the courts he had been running did not stop, and Walter of Coutances and then Hubert Walter, justiciar from 1193, carried them on. The real test came with the ransom, when a demand of 150,000 marks had to be assessed and gathered from every shire while the king sat in a German castle and his brother dealt with the king of France; it was gathered, hostages and all, and John's rising collapsed without a battle when Richard landed on 13 March 1194. The inheritance survived too: Richard came home, spent his last five years recovering ground in Normandy, and died in 1199 with the Angevin lands still in one hand, which is more than his stay-at-home brother managed in the five years that followed. Measured by whether the realm and the inheritance held rather than by where the king slept, the kingship was not the weaker half of him.","Why this is Level 4: the explanation is directed consistently at the conceptual focus, which is the comparison the word 'better' demands, and the line of reasoning runs from the criterion in the first sentence to the ranking in the last. The information is precisely selected rather than piled up, since Longchamp's deposition in October 1191, the succession of Walter of Coutances and Hubert Walter, the 150,000-mark demand, the landing on 13 March 1194 and the state of the Angevin lands in 1199 each do a job in the argument. The criterion, whether the realm and the inheritance held rather than where the king was, is stated and then applied, which is what the descriptor means by criteria for the required judgement being justified and applied. It also brings in aspects of content the two stimulus bullets do not prompt, since the bullets name only the march to Jaffa and the government of England in his absence while the paragraph turns on the collection of the ransom, the collapse of John's rising and the Norman campaigns of 1194 to 1199, so the cap on answers that do not address three or more aspects of content does not bite."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["The same question, answered at Level 2. Read it beside the Level 4 paragraph above and look for what is missing rather than for what is wrong.","Richard was a better soldier than he was a king because fighting was what he was good at. He marched his army to Jaffa and beat Saladin on the way, and he won every campaign he went on. He was not a good king of England because he was hardly ever in the country and left other men to govern it, and things went wrong while he was away. He only wanted England for the money it could give him for his wars. So he was a better soldier than a king because soldiering is what he did best.","Why this is only Level 2: it meets the descriptor that an explanation is given showing limited analysis and with implicit or unsustained links to the conceptual focus, because the march and the absent government are named and 'things went wrong' gestures at a comparison, but no link is traced and nothing is measured against anything else. It misses the higher descriptors twice over. First, the information is generalised rather than precisely selected to address the question directly: there is no date, no sum, no named deputy and no named campaign, and 'won every campaign he went on' is not evidence. Second, the overall judgement is given but its justification is asserted, since the last sentence simply repeats the statement without a criterion. On top of that, every point comes straight from the two stimulus bullets, so the printed cap applies as well: on the 16-mark question there is a maximum of 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for them either. Nothing now holds such an answer down inside Level 2, so this paragraph could reach the top of Level 2 as it stands, but until it addresses a third aspect of content better writing alone could take it no higher than 11."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: crusading, English attitudes and the causes of the Third Crusade","items":["A crusade was an armed pilgrimage authorised by the Pope; taking the cross was a public vow the Church could enforce.","Pope Urban II preached the First Crusade at Clermont in 1095; the crusaders captured Jerusalem in 1099.","A crusader was promised remission of the penalties of his sins, protection of his lands and family by the Church, and the suspension of interest on his debts while he was away.","Saladin, sultan of Egypt and Syria, destroyed the field army of the kingdom of Jerusalem at Hattin on 4 July 1187.","Jerusalem surrendered to Saladin in October 1187, the first time since 1099 that the city had not been in Christian hands.","Pope Gregory VIII called for a new crusade in 1187.","Richard, then count of Poitou and not yet king, took the cross in November 1187.","Henry II had taken the cross and never went; he levied the Saladin tithe in 1188, a tax of a tenth of revenues and moveable goods.","Baldwin, Archbishop of Canterbury, preached the crusade in Wales in 1188 and later died at the siege of Acre in November 1190.","Richard was crowned at Westminster on 3 September 1189; attacks on the Jewish community of London began during the celebrations.","In March 1190 the Jewish community of York died at Clifford's Tower, most by their own hand, during the anti-Jewish violence of the crusade preparations.","Richard raised money by selling offices, sheriffdoms and charters, and released William the Lion of Scotland from the Treaty of Falaise for 10,000 marks in December 1189.","Three rulers led the Third Crusade: the Emperor Frederick Barbarossa, who drowned in a river in Asia Minor in June 1190 so that his army broke up, Philip II of France and Richard I.","Richard and Philip set out together from Vezelay in July 1190; the fleet sailed from English ports round Spain while Richard travelled overland to Marseille.","The army included household and baronial knights, foot soldiers, crossbowmen, engineers and sailors, drawn from England and from Richard's lands in Normandy, Anjou and Aquitaine.","Hubert Walter, then Bishop of Salisbury, served with the army and was made Archbishop of Canterbury in 1193."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Richard on crusade — Sicily, Cyprus, Acre and Arsuf, 1190 to 1191","items":["Richard wintered in Sicily in 1190 to 1191 and stormed Messina in October 1190 in a quarrel with Tancred over his sister Joanna's dower.","Richard conquered Cyprus from Isaac Komnenos in May and June 1191; Isaac surrendered on 1 June 1191, giving the crusade a supply base.","He married Berengaria of Navarre at Limassol in Cyprus on 12 May 1191, setting aside his long betrothal to Philip II's sister Alice.","The siege of Acre had begun in 1189 under Guy of Lusignan; Philip II arrived in April 1191 and Richard on 8 June 1191.","Acre surrendered on 12 July 1191.","Duke Leopold V of Austria's banner was thrown down from the walls of Acre, an insult he repaid in December 1192.","The Muslim garrison of Acre, said by chroniclers to have numbered about 2,700, was executed in August 1191 when Saladin's terms were not met.","Richard and Philip backed rival candidates for the throne of Jerusalem: Guy of Lusignan and Conrad of Montferrat.","Philip II sailed for France at the end of July 1191, pleading illness, and took much of the French contingent with him.","Marching south from Acre towards Jaffa with the fleet alongside for supplies, Richard defeated Saladin at Arsuf on 7 September 1191.","Arsuf broke Saladin's reputation for invincibility but left his field army in being.","Once home, Philip pressed Richard's lands in Normandy and dealt with John, so the quarrel of the crusade became a war in France."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Jerusalem, the truce, the capture and the ransom, 1192 to 1194","items":["The crusaders reached Beit Nuba, about a day's march from Jerusalem, in January 1192 and again in June 1192, and withdrew both times.","Saladin had stripped the country around Jerusalem and spoiled its wells, and held the fortresses of the interior.","The Templars and Hospitallers argued that Jerusalem could not be held once crusaders had fulfilled their vows and sailed home.","Conrad of Montferrat was elected king of Jerusalem in April 1192 and murdered at Tyre within the month; Henry of Champagne took his place.","Saladin captured Jaffa in July 1192 and Richard recovered it.","The Treaty of Jaffa, agreed on 2 September 1192, made a truce of about three years, left the coast from Tyre to Jaffa in Christian hands, required the defences of Ascalon to be dismantled and allowed unarmed Christian pilgrims to visit Jerusalem.","Richard left the Holy Land on 9 October 1192 without visiting the city.","Shipwrecked on the northern coast of the Adriatic, Richard tried to cross Germany in disguise and was seized near Vienna by Duke Leopold V of Austria in December 1192.","Leopold handed Richard to the Emperor Henry VI in 1193.","The ransom demanded was 150,000 marks; a mark was two-thirds of a pound, so the sum was 100,000 pounds, several times the Crown's ordinary yearly revenue.","It was raised by a tax of a quarter of every free man's movable goods and income, by scutage on knights' fees, by stripping gold and silver plate from churches and monasteries, and by seizing a year's wool crop from the Cistercian houses.","Hostages were sent to Germany as security for the unpaid balance.","Eleanor of Aquitaine and Hubert Walter, justiciar from 1193, organised the collection and the government of England.","John and Philip II offered Henry VI money to keep Richard in captivity, and John conceded Norman territory to Philip.","Richard was released on 4 February 1194, landed in England on 13 March and was crowned a second time at Winchester on 17 April 1194.","Richard left England in May 1194 and never returned; he was king for ten years and spent about six months of the reign in England.","William Longchamp, the chancellor left in charge of England, was driven out in 1191."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Normandy — competing aims, Chateau Gaillard and the loss of 1204","items":["The Angevin lands in France were held from the king of France, so Philip II was both rival and overlord to Richard and to John.","Richard's aim after 1194 was to recover what had been lost during his captivity, above all the Norman Vexin with its castle at Gisors.","Philip II's aim was to make his overlordship real, to use his feudal rights to break the Angevin lands apart and to push royal power to the Seine and beyond.","John's aim in 1199 was to secure his inheritance cheaply, which is why he accepted the Treaty of Le Goulet in 1200.","Richard built Chateau Gaillard at Les Andelys on a chalk spur above the Seine in about two years from 1196, to block the river road to Rouen.","Chateau Gaillard cost about 11,500 pounds on one estimate, with higher figures also published; either way it cost more than Richard spent on all his castles in England put together.","It had an outer triangular bailey, a middle bailey and an inner bailey with a keep, with ditches cut into the rock, plus a fortified new town at Petit Andely, an island fort and a stockade across the Seine.","Building it brought Richard into dispute with Walter of Coutances, Archbishop of Rouen, who owned the land and laid Normandy under interdict until he was compensated.","Richard was wounded by a crossbow bolt at the siege of Chalus in the Limousin and died on 6 April 1199.","John was crowned at Westminster on 27 May 1199; Arthur of Brittany, son of John's elder brother Geoffrey, was recognised in Anjou, Maine and Touraine.","By the Treaty of Le Goulet in 1200 John was recognised as Richard's heir in France but acknowledged Philip as overlord and paid a relief of 20,000 marks.","John married Isabella of Angouleme in August 1200; she was betrothed to Hugh de Lusignan, and the Lusignans appealed to Philip as overlord.","John failed to answer the summons to Philip's court, and in 1202 Philip declared his French lands forfeit and supported Arthur's claim.","At Mirebeau on 1 August 1202 John's forced march surprised Arthur and the Lusignans besieging Eleanor of Aquitaine, and took them prisoner.","Prisoners taken at Mirebeau were ill-treated and some starved; Arthur disappeared in John's custody in 1203, and lords such as William des Roches changed sides.","Philip besieged Chateau Gaillard from late August 1203; John's attempt to relieve it by river and land failed and he sailed for England in December 1203.","The garrison under Roger de Lacy surrendered Chateau Gaillard on 6 March 1204.","Eleanor of Aquitaine died on 1 April 1204; Rouen surrendered on 24 June 1204 and Normandy was lost."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — the 16-mark judgement: 'The Treaty of Le Goulet in 1200 was a success for King John.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.","items":["The question as printed: 'The Treaty of Le Goulet in 1200 was a success for King John.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] You may use the following in your answer: • the relief of 20,000 marks • the Norman Vexin. You must also use information of your own. There are no spelling, punctuation and grammar marks on this paper, so every minute goes on the argument.","Decode it. The concept is consequence, and 'success' is a word that means nothing until you say success at what and for how long, so the criterion to announce in the opening, and to apply at the end, is this: did the treaty secure what John needed in 1200 at a price that did not cost him more later? Name the alternatives at once, which are what he bought with it, what he handed over for good, and what he made possible for Philip II.","Opening, three or four sentences: state the criterion, name the factors you will weigh, and signal the qualified position you will end at.","Paragraph 1, for the statement — what the treaty bought. John came to the throne in 1199 with Arthur of Brittany recognised in Anjou, Maine and Touraine and backed by Philip; at Le Goulet on 22 May 1200 Philip recognised John as Richard's heir in France and dropped Arthur's claim, and Arthur did homage to John for Brittany. A disputed succession was settled without a campaign. Interim judgement: measured by what John needed in his first year, this was the thing he could not have won by fighting.","Paragraph 2, for the statement — the price looked payable at the time. The relief of 20,000 marks was one payment, not a standing charge; Evreux and the Vexin had largely been in Philip's hands since Richard's captivity, so John was conceding ground he did not hold; and Les Andelys, with Chateau Gaillard on its spur above the Seine, stayed his, so the road up the river to Rouen was still blocked. Interim judgement: on paper he gave up losses and kept the lock on Normandy.","Paragraph 3, against the statement — what he conceded for good. He acknowledged Philip as overlord of all his French lands and paid a relief for them, which no amount of later fighting could unsay; he accepted that the counts of Boulogne and Flanders were Philip's men, which broke the ring of allies his father and brother had built around France; and his niece Blanche of Castile was married to Philip's son Louis with Issoudun and Gracay as her dowry. Interim judgement: he bought a year of peace with rights that lasted for ever.","Paragraph 4, against the statement — what it made possible. In 1202 Philip used precisely that overlordship: when the Lusignans appealed to him over John's marriage to Isabella of Angouleme, he summoned John to his court as a vassal, condemned him for not answering it, declared his French fiefs forfeit and took up Arthur's claim again. Chateau Gaillard fell on 6 March 1204 and Rouen surrendered on 24 June. Interim judgement: the instrument that destroyed John in 1202 was one he had handed over in 1200.","The point beyond the stimulus bullets: at least one paragraph must rest on material the two bullets do not prompt. Arthur's claim and his homage for Brittany, the counts of Boulogne and Flanders, the marriage of Blanche of Castile, the Lusignans' appeal and the forfeiture of 1202 all qualify. Without one of them the answer does not address three or more aspects of content, so it is held to a maximum of 11 marks and cannot reach Level 4 however well it is written.","The evaluation thread: return in every paragraph to the same test — did this clause solve a problem John had in 1200, or create one he would meet later? Recognition as heir is in the first category and the acknowledgement of overlordship in the second, and saying so each time is what keeps the line of reasoning sustained.","The conclusion must rank and justify rather than summarise. Argue that by the test of 1200 the treaty was a success, since it gave John the one thing he could not take by force, but that by the test of what it cost he bought recognition with jurisdiction, and jurisdiction was the weapon Philip used two years later. Then qualify it: the chronicler Gervase of Canterbury links the nickname Softsword to this peace, though he seems to have thought peace worth having, and a king who could not have won the war may have had no better bargain to make.","Timing: about 25 minutes. Roughly five to plan four paragraphs and write the criterion sentence, eighteen to write, and two to check that the final paragraph answers 'how far' and not merely 'why'."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:22:28Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Judged afresh after the dedupe pass: about seventy dates, figures and events were re-checked and roughly a dozen verified on the web - Cyprus (the storm and the three ships wrecked off Limassol, Isaac Komnenos holding the survivors and barring Joanna and Berengaria, the landing on 6 May, the marriage on 12 May, the surrender on 1 June 1191 in silver chains, the later sale to the Templars), Le Goulet of 22 May 1200 with its 20,000-mark relief, Evreux and the Vexin bar Les Andelys, Boulogne and Flanders, Blanche of Castile and Arthur's homage, the April 1202 sentence for non-appearance, Longchamp's 3,000 pounds for the chancellorship, the quitclaim of Canterbury of 5 December 1189 for 10,000 marks, Erdberg on 21 December 1192 and Durnstein before the handover to Henry VI in 1193, Conrad of Montferrat elected on 24 April and murdered at Tyre on 28 April 1192 as Leopold V's cousin through Judith of Babenberg,¹ and the march south from Acre begun on 22 August 1191 to Jaffa by way of Arsuf² - and not one date, figure or attribution is wrong, no answer key is wrong or inconsistent with its solution, the caps and the 1 hour 50 minute timing are in the current June 2024 and June 2025 wording, no worked example, model-paragraph pair or plan now sets a bank question or shares its focus and stimulus points (the scan prints 'no duplication' and reading the setups confirms it), and no stem reproduces the specimen booklet B2, whose nearest question, 'Explain why England became involved in the Third Crusade' with the bullets Jerusalem and the Pope, differs in focus and in both bullets from room.questions[6]: publish it, and tighten only the two loose claims noted above.\n¹ Conrad's mother Judith was the daughter of Leopold III of Austria, so Leopold V was his first cousin, which is why the kit's example is right to put the murder among Leopold's motives.\n² Richard reached Jaffa about 10 September 1191, three days after Arsuf, which supports the stimulus bullet 'the march to Jaffa in 1191'."}}},"B2-3":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"B2-3","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 3 is where John's reign falls apart, and it is the part of Option B2 that the 16-mark judgement questions return to most often, because every element in it can be weighed against every other: the quarrel with Rome, the money, the failure in France, the rebellion, Magna Carta, the war and the wreck John left behind in 1216. Booklet B gives you no sources, so every mark comes from precise knowledge of your own - the dates, the clauses, the castles, the figures and the names. A student who can argue why John lost his barons, what Magna Carta actually settled and what saved the throne for a nine-year-old boy has an answer ready for almost anything this key topic can ask.","idea":[{"h":"Canterbury, 1205: why the quarrel with the Papacy began","t":"Archbishop Hubert Walter died in 1205 and three parties claimed the right to fill the richest see in England. The monks of Christ Church, Canterbury, secretly elected their own sub-prior, Reginald; John insisted on John de Gray, Bishop of Norwich, a servant he trusted; and Pope Innocent III, the most assertive pope of the Middle Ages, quashed both elections and had the English cardinal Stephen Langton chosen at Rome instead, consecrating him in 1207. John refused to let Langton into England, drove the Canterbury monks out and seized the archbishopric's lands. The quarrel was therefore about power, not belief: who chose England's bishops, and who drew their revenues. A top-level answer says that plainly, and adds the timing - John was defending the Crown's customary hold over the Church at the very moment, a year after the loss of Normandy in 1204, when he could afford to lose neither control nor income.","code":"B2-3 — 1 The dispute with the Papacy: Causes of the dispute"},{"h":"The Interdict, 1208-14, and what it did to ordinary life","t":"Innocent laid England under an Interdict in March 1208 and it lasted six years. Church doors were shut: Mass was not sung, marriages were not celebrated inside the church, and the dead were buried in unconsecrated ground without a priest's service, though baptism and the confession of the dying were still allowed. To people who believed that salvation ran through the rites of the Church, that was terrifying rather than merely inconvenient. John answered by confiscating the estates and revenues of clergy who obeyed the Pope, so for six years the Crown drew a great income from Church lands while the parishes went without their priests; when Innocent excommunicated him personally in 1209 most bishops left the country. A top-level answer uses the Interdict twice over: as suffering that fed John's reputation as a godless king, and as a business that paid him handsomely, which is precisely why he could ignore the sentence for so long.","code":"B2-3 — 1 The dispute with the Papacy: The Interdict and its impact on everyday life"},{"h":"The reconciliation of 1213 and why it was worth the humiliation","t":"By 1212 Innocent was threatening deposition, releasing John's subjects from their allegiance and encouraging Philip II of France to enforce the sentence. In May 1213 John submitted to the papal legate Pandulf: he accepted Langton, promised compensation to the Church, and surrendered the kingdoms of England and Ireland to the Pope, receiving them back as a papal fief for a tribute of 1,000 marks a year. Langton absolved him that summer and the Interdict was lifted in 1214. Chroniclers treated the surrender as a disgrace, and in one sense it was, but it was also the shrewdest move of the reign: Innocent became John's overlord and protector, Philip's invasion lost its blessing, and when the barons rebelled the Pope annulled their charter and excommunicated them. A top-level answer weighs the loss of face against that protection and decides which counted for more in 1215 and 1216.","code":"B2-3 — 1 The dispute with the Papacy: The significance of the reconciliation between John and Innocent III"},{"h":"Money, 'fines' and arbitrary power: how John paid for his war","t":"John had lost Normandy in 1204 and everything he did afterwards was aimed at buying it back, which meant money. He took scutage, the payment made in place of knight service, eleven times in a reign of seventeen years and raised the rate; in 1207 he levied a thirteenth of movable goods and income, which brought in roughly sixty thousand pounds, far more than the Crown's ordinary yearly revenue; he charged huge 'fines' for inheritances, wardships, marriages and simple royal goodwill; and in 1210 he squeezed a vast tallage out of England's Jewish community, imprisoning those who would not pay. Behind the money stood arbitrary power: hostages taken from baronial families, debts called in at will, and the destruction of William de Braose, whose wife and eldest son were imprisoned and starved to death in 1210. The plot of 1212, when Robert FitzWalter and Eustace de Vesci planned to kill or desert the king on campaign in Wales and then fled the country, showed what fear had produced. A top-level answer links the two: it was not only the amount taken but the arbitrariness of the taking that turned taxpayers into rebels.","code":"B2-3 — 2 Worsening relations with the barons: Growing financial impositions"},{"h":"1214: the promise that failed","t":"Ten years of taxation had been justified by one promise - Normandy would be recovered. In 1214 John landed at La Rochelle and pushed north through Poitou while his allies, the Emperor Otto IV, the Count of Flanders and John's half-brother the Earl of Salisbury, attacked from the north-east. John's Poitevin lords refused to face Prince Louis in battle at Roche-au-Moine and he had to withdraw; on 27 July 1214 Philip II destroyed the allied army at Bouvines. John bought a truce and came home in October with nothing to show for the treasure he had spent. When he demanded yet another scutage from those who had not served, the northern barons simply refused to pay. A top-level answer treats Bouvines as the trigger rather than the cause: the grievances were a decade old, but defeat destroyed the last argument for going on paying, and it destroyed the fear of a king who could not win.","code":"B2-3 — 2 Worsening relations with the barons: The impact of the failure to regain Normandy in 1214"},{"h":"The rebellion of 1215: Northampton, Lincoln and London","t":"In January 1215 the barons came armed to London and demanded that John confirm the coronation charter of Henry I. John played for time, and in March took the cross as a crusader so that the Church would have to protect him. On 5 May 1215 the rebels formally renounced their homage at Brackley and chose Robert FitzWalter as their leader, styling him Marshal of the Army of God and Holy Church. They besieged Northampton castle for about a fortnight and could not take it; Bedford opened its gates to them; Lincoln castle held out for the king and never fell to the rebels at all. What decided the campaign was London, whose gates were opened to them on Sunday 17 May 1215 while the citizens were at church, giving the rebels the wealth of the kingdom and leaving John with nothing to do but negotiate. A top-level answer notices that the rebels won without winning a battle, which is exactly why John was still strong enough to reopen the war in the autumn.","code":"B2-3 — 2 Worsening relations with the barons: The rebellion of 1215"},{"h":"Runnymede: two sets of motives and sixty-three clauses","t":"John and the barons met in the meadow at Runnymede beside the Thames, and terms were agreed on 15 June 1215; the charter of sixty-three clauses that we call Magna Carta was issued in the king's name and copies were sent out to the shires, of which four survive. The barons wanted their customs restored and the king bound by them: relief for an earl's or a baron's inheritance fixed at £100, no scutage or aid without the common counsel of the kingdom, no free man seized, imprisoned or dispossessed except by the lawful judgement of his equals or by the law of the land, right and justice neither sold nor denied nor delayed, foreign mercenaries sent home, and clause 61, a committee of twenty-five barons entitled to seize the king's castles and lands if he broke his word. John wanted the rebels out of London, his kingdom back and time. Stephen Langton, the archbishop he had fought for eight years, mediated. A top-level answer keeps both sets of motives in view, because they explain why the settlement lasted about ten weeks.","code":"B2-3 — 3 Magna Carta and the First Barons' War: Runnymede"},{"h":"Why the war came: enforcement, annulment and Rochester","t":"Clause 61 made the peace unworkable: no medieval king could live with twenty-five subjects holding the legal right to make war on him, and no rebel who had gone that far could disband his army and trust John. John appealed to his new overlord, and on 24 August 1215 Innocent III annulled the charter as shameful and void, excommunicated the rebels and later suspended Langton for refusing to publish the sentence. John hired mercenaries from Flanders and Poitou and marched on Rochester, where William d'Aubigny held the castle for the rebels; after about seven weeks his miners brought down the south-east corner tower of the great keep, firing the props of their tunnel with the fat of forty pigs, and the starving garrison surrendered at the end of November 1215. John then harried the rebel lands north to Berwick over the winter. Unable to face him in the field, the rebels offered the crown to Prince Louis of France, who landed in Kent in May 1216 and entered London in June. A top-level answer explains the war as a failure of enforcement and trust, not of drafting.","code":"B2-3 — 3 Magna Carta and the First Barons' War: Reasons for the outbreak of the First Barons' War"},{"h":"England in October 1216","t":"When John died England was a country at war with itself and largely in other men's hands. Louis held London, Winchester and much of the south-east; Alexander II of Scotland had invaded the north and done homage to Louis at Dover; Llywelyn ab Iorwerth had taken back lands in Wales; and many English barons, including John's own half-brother the Earl of Salisbury, had changed sides. Mercenary captains held royal castles, both armies had ravaged the countryside, the Church was still repairing the damage of six years of Interdict, and the treasury was empty - part of the baggage train, including some of the royal treasure, was lost crossing the estuary at the Wash in October 1216. John himself fell ill, probably with dysentery, and died at Newark castle on the night of 18-19 October 1216. A top-level answer does not simply list the damage; it ranks the threats, because the judgement questions always ask which of them mattered most.","code":"B2-3 — 4 England in 1216: The condition of England at the time of John's death"},{"h":"The problem of the succession","t":"John's heir was his son Henry, born in 1207 and therefore nine years old, which meant years without an adult king to lead armies or dispense justice in person, and England had no settled machinery for a minority. The rival was already in the country and already accepted by much of it: Louis had been received in London in June 1216, held the capital and had taken the homage of many English barons. Westminster and the regalia were out of reach and part of the treasure had been lost, so the boy was crowned in a hurry at Gloucester on 28 October 1216, nine days after his father's death, with a plain gold circlet in place of a crown, by bishops acting under the papal legate Guala. Yet the child's weakness was also his strength: he had done nobody any harm and owed nobody any grudge. A top-level answer makes exactly that point - barons could abandon John's cause without abandoning John's son.","code":"B2-3 — 4 England in 1216: The problem of the succession"},{"h":"William Marshal as Protector","t":"William Marshal, Earl of Pembroke, was about seventy years old and had served four kings - Henry II, the Young King, Richard I and John - when the council named him rector regis et regni, Protector of the king and the kingdom, with the papal legate Guala beside him and the authority of Rome behind them both. His first act was political rather than military: in November 1216 the government reissued Magna Carta in the boy king's name, leaving out the security clause and the most humiliating terms, so that the rebels' own programme became the royalists' programme and there was nothing left to fight for. Barons began to drift back. Marshal then finished the war, leading the royalists to victory at Lincoln on 20 May 1217 and seeing Louis's reinforcement fleet destroyed off Sandwich that August, after which Louis renounced his claim by the treaty made in September 1217. A top-level answer gives the reissue of the charter the credit first: the battles ended a war that the charter had already been winning.","code":"B2-3 — 4 England in 1216: The role of William Marshal as Protector"}],"examples":[{"title":"One feature in each part: answering 1(a) and 1(b) without sliding into explanation","setup":"The question is printed in two parts: '1(a) Describe one feature of the Interdict in England (1208-14). [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the loss of King John's baggage in the Wash in October 1216. [2 marks]' You know that Innocent III laid the Interdict on England in March 1208 and that it was not lifted until July 1214; that church doors were shut, so Mass was not sung, marriages were not celebrated inside the church and the dead were buried in unconsecrated ground without a priest; that baptism and the confession of the dying were still permitted; that John confiscated the estates and revenues of clergy who obeyed the Pope; that most bishops left England after his excommunication in 1209; and that on 12 October 1216 the king rode from Lynn towards Swineshead by the longer road through Wisbech while his baggage took the short crossing at the mouth of the Wellstream and was caught there by the tide, Ralph of Coggeshall reporting the loss of his portable chapel with its relics and some of his packhorses and Roger of Wendover claiming the loss of all his carts, wagons and baggage horses with his money and plate, after which John was dead at Newark within a week.","steps":["Start with (a) and name the feature of the Interdict that every person in England would have noticed: from March 1208 the church doors were shut and the ordinary worship of the Church stopped.","Support it with one precise detail: Mass was not sung and the dead were buried without a priest in unconsecrated ground, although the dying could still be confessed and babies baptised.","Stop part (a) there, because it carries one mark for the feature and one for the detail, so a second feature added here earns nothing.","Move to (b) and check that it is a genuinely different aspect of content: a disaster on the king's last march is not the quarrel with Rome, so nothing from (a) may be repeated here.","State that feature and support it in turn: part of the royal baggage was lost to the tide at the mouth of the Wellstream, because on 12 October 1216 the king took the long road round by Wisbech while the carts and packhorses were sent by the short crossing.","Check both answers against the command word: each describes, and neither offers reasons, consequences or a judgement, which earn nothing on this question."],"cues":["Which feature of the Interdict would everyone in England have noticed?","What one detail proves that worship really stopped?","Would a second feature in part (a) add anything?","Why can part (b) not be more about the Interdict?","What exactly was lost, and why was it lost?","What is there no credit for here?"]},{"title":"Building a causal chain: why John submitted to the Pope","setup":"The question is: 'Explain why King John submitted to Pope Innocent III in 1213. You may use the following in your answer: • the plot of 1212 • Philip II of France. You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks). You know that in 1212 Robert FitzWalter and Eustace de Vesci plotted to kill or desert John on campaign in Wales and then fled the country; that John had been excommunicated in 1209 and that Innocent was threatening to depose him and had released his subjects from their allegiance; that Philip II was gathering an invasion fleet in 1213; that in May 1213 John accepted Langton and surrendered England and Ireland to the Pope, receiving them back as a fief for a tribute of 1,000 marks a year; and that John was already planning the French campaign that ended at Bouvines.","steps":["Start on the plot of 1212: FitzWalter and de Vesci planned to kill or desert John on campaign in Wales and then fled the country, which shows that excommunication had made desertion respectable and that the king could no longer trust the army he depended on.","Chain that to the decision the question asks about: a king whose own barons may abandon him in the field cannot fight the Pope and Philip II at once, so holding out over Langton had become impossible.","Chain the second: once the Pope had withdrawn his protection, a French invasion was not merely possible but lawful, so submitting turned an authorised crusade back into ordinary aggression.","Add the reason the bullets do not offer: John wanted Rome on his side for the war he was about to fight in France, and a vassal king could expect papal letters in his favour instead of sentences against him.","Turn that into the argument the examiner rewards: the submission was an exchange, not a surrender - John gave up a bishop he could not have anyway and bought an overlord whose authority he could later turn against his own barons.","Rank the reasons in the closing line: the danger to his throne came first, the invasion second, and the calculation about the barons last, and say so in those words."],"cues":["What did the plot of 1212 show about John's position?","Why did that make holding out impossible?","What did papal approval do for an invasion?","Which reason is not on the paper at all?","What did John actually gain by kneeling?","How do you finish without simply listing?"]},{"title":"Setting a criterion for a judgement question","setup":"The question is: \"'Magna Carta was a defeat for King John.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • Runnymede • the twenty-five barons. You must also use information of your own.\" (16 marks). You know that terms were agreed at Runnymede on 15 June 1215; that the charter fixed a baron's relief at £100, barred scutage without the common counsel of the kingdom, promised that no free man would be imprisoned or dispossessed except by lawful judgement, and set up a committee of twenty-five barons who could seize the king's castles if he broke it; that on 19 June 1215 the rebels renewed their oaths of fealty to John, but kept the city of London, with Archbishop Langton holding the Tower, under a treaty by which the city was to be handed over only on 15 August and only if John kept his promises, and that it never was handed over; that Innocent III annulled the charter on 24 August 1215; and that John took Rochester at the end of November 1215; and that the charter was reissued, without the security clause, in his son's name in November 1216.","steps":["Decide first what would count as a defeat, and write the criterion into the opening: a defeat means losing powers the king had before and not getting them back.","Put the case for the statement at its strongest: the charter wrote limits on taxation and on justice into law, and clause 61 gave subjects a lawful means of making war on their king, which no English king had ever conceded.","Put the case against: John conceded on parchment what he never meant to keep, got the rebels' oaths of fealty renewed on 19 June without giving up a castle or a shire, had the whole charter annulled by his overlord within about ten weeks, and was storming rebel castles before the year was out.","Weigh the two by applying the criterion rather than by counting points: he did not get his capital back, since the rebels still held London when the agreed day of 15 August passed, but a concession cancelled inside a summer took no lasting power from him, so the statement survives only if the charter mattered later.","Bring in the material beyond the bullets that settles it: the charter was reissued in the boy king's name after John's death and became the price of his son's throne, so what John lost was not power in his lifetime but the principle that a king's will was the only law.","Write the judgement as a ranking with a reason attached, agreeing in the long term and disagreeing in the short, and name the criterion again as you do it."],"cues":["What has to be defined before anything can be judged?","Which clause is the hardest for John to swallow?","What did John actually get on 19 June, and what did the rebels keep?","Does a cancelled concession count as a loss?","What happened to the charter after he died?","How should the last sentence be built?"]},{"title":"Ranking reasons rather than listing them","setup":"The question is: 'Explain why the rebel barons offered the English crown to Prince Louis of France in the years 1215-16. You may use the following in your answer: • Blanche of Castile • the excommunication of the rebels. You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks). You know that Louis was married to Blanche of Castile, whose mother Eleanor was a daughter of Henry II, so that Blanche was Henry II's granddaughter and King John's niece; that Innocent III annulled Magna Carta on 24 August 1215 and on 16 December 1215 excommunicated the leading rebels by name together with the citizens of London; that Archbishop Langton was suspended for refusing to publish the sentence and was out of England by the end of 1215; that Rochester fell to John on 30 November 1215 and that he sacked Berwick in the middle of January 1216, after which many northern rebels bought their peace; that Louis had already sent a contingent of knights to London in November 1215; and that he landed unopposed on Thanet on 21 May 1216 and was proclaimed king at St Paul's on 2 June, though he was never crowned.","steps":["Start on Blanche of Castile and turn the marriage into a reason: her mother was a daughter of Henry II, so Louis's wife was King John's niece, and that gave the rebels a candidate with Angevin blood rather than a plain usurper.","Chain that to the decision the question asks about: men who had sworn fealty needed a claim they could name in public, and a descent from Henry II let them present a change of king as lawful rather than as treason.","Chain the second: once Innocent had annulled the charter and had the leading rebels excommunicated by name in December 1215, no candidate of their own could expect the Church's blessing, so they had to find a prince strong enough not to need it.","Add the reason the bullets do not offer: by the start of 1216 they were losing the war - Rochester had gone, the north had been harried as far as Berwick and their own fellows were buying terms - so only foreign troops and French money could keep the rebellion alive.","Turn that into the argument the examiner rewards: the offer was made out of weakness, not strength, and the claim through Blanche was the respectable dress put on a decision the military position had already forced.","Rank the reasons in the closing line: the collapse of the rebels' own war came first, the papal sentences second, and the claim through Blanche last, and say so in those words."],"cues":["What did Louis's marriage give the rebels?","Why did they need a claim at all?","What did the excommunications of December 1215 shut off?","Which reason is not on the paper at all?","Was the offer made from strength or from weakness?","How do you finish without simply listing?"]},{"title":"Turning a narrative of the winter war into analysis","setup":"The question is: 'Explain why King John recovered so much of England in the winter campaign of 1215-16. You may use the following in your answer: • the march to Berwick • the submission of rebel barons. You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks). A candidate has written a correct but purely narrative paragraph: Rochester surrendered on 30 November 1215, John kept Christmas at Nottingham, he went north by Pontefract, he reached Berwick in the middle of January 1216 and burned the town, he turned south again, and Colchester, Framlingham and Hedingham fell to him in March. You also know that he had paid Flemish and Poitevin troops in the field while he moved, that Gilbert fitz Reinfrey of Kendal bought his peace for 12,000 marks and gave up castles and hostages as security, that the rebels never risked a battle and kept to London, and that Louis had sent a contingent of French knights to the city in November 1215.","steps":["Diagnose the fault first: every sentence answers 'what happened next', and the question asks 'why', so the paragraph would sit at Level 1 or 2 however accurate it is.","Convert the march into a reason: John rode the length of England to Berwick and burned it because terror was cheaper than sieges, and a king who could do that unopposed had proved that the rebels kept no army in the field.","Convert the submissions into a reason: Gilbert fitz Reinfrey paid 12,000 marks and handed over castles and hostages, so each surrender brought the king money, a stronghold and a pledge at once, and made the next man's surrender easier.","Add the reason the bullets do not offer: John had paid troops and a siege train and the rebels had neither, so the war was settled by which side could keep an army together through a winter.","Keep the narrative facts, but make each one carry a causal load: the fall of Colchester, Framlingham and Hedingham in March shows that the rebels could hold nothing outside London, which is why the city mattered so much to them and why they sent for a French prince.","Close on the link between the reasons, saying which came first in time and which weighed most, because an examiner rewards the connection and not the chronology."],"cues":["What is wrong with the paragraph as it stands?","What did the ride to Berwick prove?","What did the king gain from each surrender?","Which advantage is on neither bullet?","How can a list of fallen castles become an argument?","What must the final sentence do?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What did John surrender in May 1213, and what did he get back?","a":"He surrendered the kingdoms of England and Ireland to Pope Innocent III and received them back as a papal fief, holding them from the Pope for a tribute of 1,000 marks a year, and he accepted Stephen Langton as archbishop."},{"q":"Why did the failure of 1214 matter more than the money it cost?","a":"Because the taxation of the previous decade had been justified by the promise of recovering Normandy. After Bouvines there was no prospect of recovery, so a further scutage looked like robbery, and the northern barons refused it."},{"q":"Which clause made Magna Carta unworkable, and what did it allow?","a":"Clause 61, the security clause: a committee of twenty-five barons could distrain on the king's castles and lands if he broke the charter, which meant subjects with a lawful right to make war on their king."},{"q":"What did William Marshal do in November 1216 that did more than any battle?","a":"He had Magna Carta reissued in the nine-year-old Henry III's name without the security clause, so the rebels' own demands became royal policy and barons began returning to the king's side."}]},"room":{"facts":["Booklet B of Paper 2 carries 32 marks on the British depth study: Questions 1(a) and 1(b), 'Describe one feature of...', 2 marks each and on two different aspects of content, worth 4 marks in all; Question 2, an 'Explain why...' causation question worth 12 marks; and a choice of one of two 16-mark judgement questions, Question 3 or Question 4, \"'...' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\"","Question 1 is point-marked, not levels-marked: in each part one mark for the valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about it, four marks in all. A second feature inside 1(a) or 1(b) earns nothing, and no reasons, no consequences and no judgement are wanted.","The 12-mark and 16-mark questions each print two stimulus points under 'You may use the following in your answer:' followed by 'You must also use information of your own.' Using the bullets is optional; adding material of your own is not.","The caps printed in the mark scheme from June 2024 are: on the 12-mark question a maximum of 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; on the 16-mark question a maximum of 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, on the same condition. Three or more developed aspects is therefore the minimum shape of a Level 4 answer.","Level 4 is 10-12 marks on the 12-mark question and 13-16 on the 16-mark judgement. It asks for an analytical explanation directed consistently at the conceptual focus with a line of reasoning that is coherent, sustained and logically structured, and on the judgement question criteria that are justified and applied in reaching the overall judgement.","The AO split is AO2 6 and AO1 6 on the 12-mark question and AO2 10 and AO1 6 on the 16-mark judgement, whose grid carries a third bullet in every level for the overall judgement. Performance in the two is interdependent: an answer showing no qualities of AO2 cannot rise above the top of Level 1 however strong the knowledge.","There are no sources, no interpretations and no SPaG marks anywhere on Paper 2, so every mark comes from your own knowledge. Work at roughly a minute a mark: about 5 minutes on Questions 1(a) and 1(b) together, 20 on Question 2 and 27 on the 16-mark judgement at Question 3 or Question 4."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the dispute between King John and Pope Innocent III over the archbishopric of Canterbury. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the plot against King John in 1212. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked as two separate 2-mark parts: in each part one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about it, and a second feature within a part earns nothing. (a) One feature: there were rival candidates for the see; credit also John's candidate John de Gray, the Pope's imposition of Stephen Langton and his consecration in 1207, John's refusal to admit him, or the expulsion of the Canterbury monks and the seizure of the archbishopric's estates. (b) One feature: leading barons conspired against the king as he gathered an army for Wales; credit also the names of Robert FitzWalter and Eustace de Vesci, the plan to kill or desert John on campaign, his abandonment of the Welsh expedition, the flight of the plotters to Scotland and to France, or the seizure of their lands.","sol":"1(a) Feature: there were rival candidates for the archbishopric (1 mark). Supporting information: when Hubert Walter died in 1205 the monks of Christ Church, Canterbury, secretly elected their sub-prior Reginald while John insisted on his own servant John de Gray, Bishop of Norwich, so both elections were carried to Rome, where Innocent III quashed them and had the English cardinal Stephen Langton chosen instead (1 mark). 1(b) Feature: leading barons plotted to kill or desert the king on campaign in Wales (1 mark). Supporting information: Robert FitzWalter and Eustace de Vesci were named as the ringleaders, and when word of the plot reached John as he mustered his army for Wales in 1212 he broke off the expedition, while the two men fled the country, de Vesci to Scotland and FitzWalter to France (1 mark).","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Read both parts before you start: (a) is about the quarrel over who should be Archbishop of Canterbury, (b) about what some of John's barons were planning while he was raising an army for Wales.","For (a) take either the rival elections or what the Pope did about them; for (b) take either what the plotters meant to do or what became of them and of John's campaign.","One short paragraph each: name the feature in the first sentence and add one precise detail - a name, a date or a place - then stop, because a second feature in either part earns nothing."],"codes":["B2-3 — 1 The dispute with the Papacy: Causes of the dispute","B2-3 — 2 Worsening relations with the barons: Growing financial impositions"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the agreement between King John and Pope Innocent III in 1213. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of King John's campaign to regain Normandy in 1214. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked as two separate 2-mark parts: in each part one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about it, and a second feature within a part earns nothing. (a) One feature: John accepted Stephen Langton as archbishop; credit also the surrender of England and Ireland to the Pope and their return as a papal fief, the tribute of 1,000 marks a year, the promise of compensation to the Church, the absolution of John in 1213 or the lifting of the Interdict in 1214. (b) One feature: John's Poitevin lords refused to fight; credit also the landing at La Rochelle and the advance through Poitou, the allies attacking from the north-east, the withdrawal from Roche-au-Moine, the destruction of the allied army at Bouvines on 27 July 1214, the truce John bought, or his return in October with nothing gained.","sol":"1(a) Feature: John gave way on the quarrel itself and accepted Stephen Langton as Archbishop of Canterbury (1 mark). Supporting information: in May 1213 he submitted to the papal legate Pandulf, allowed the exiled clergy home and promised compensation for the Church property he had seized, and he was absolved later that year (1 mark). 1(b) Feature: the campaign was lost without John himself fighting a battle (1 mark). Supporting information: his Poitevin lords refused to face Prince Louis at Roche-au-Moine so he had to withdraw, and on 27 July 1214 Philip II destroyed his allies - the Emperor Otto IV, the Count of Flanders and the Earl of Salisbury - at Bouvines, after which John bought a truce and came home in October (1 mark).","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a) is the settlement of the quarrel with Rome in May 1213; part (b) is the French campaign of the following year.","For (a) take one term of the agreement - what John conceded over the Church, or what he did with his two kingdoms; for (b) take either what his own lords did or what became of his allies.","Give each feature one hard detail - a name, a sum or a date - and say nothing about why John acted as he did."],"codes":["B2-3 — 1 The dispute with the Papacy: The significance of the reconciliation between John and Innocent III","B2-3 — 2 Worsening relations with the barons: The impact of the failure to regain Normandy in 1214"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of King John's financial demands on his barons in the years 1205-15. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the rebellion of 1215. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked as two separate 2-mark parts: in each part one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about it, and a second feature within a part earns nothing. (a) One feature: repeated scutage at rising rates; credit also the tax of a thirteenth in 1207, heavy reliefs and 'fines' for inheritances, wardships and royal goodwill, the tallage on the Jews in 1210, or the use of debts, hostages and forest fines as pressure. (b) One feature: the rebels formally renounced their homage at Brackley on 5 May 1215; credit also the armed demand in London in January 1215 for Henry I's coronation charter, John's taking of the cross in March, the choice of Robert FitzWalter as leader, the failure before Northampton castle, the surrender of Bedford, the holding of Lincoln for the king, or the opening of London's gates on 17 May 1215.","sol":"1(a) Feature: John took scutage, the money payment made in place of knight service, again and again (1 mark). Supporting information: he levied it eleven times in a reign of seventeen years and raised the rate, demanding it once more in 1214 from men who had not sailed with him to Poitou (1 mark). 1(b) Feature: the rebellion opened as a formal, legal act rather than a surprise attack (1 mark). Supporting information: on 5 May 1215 the rebels renounced their homage at Brackley and chose Robert FitzWalter as their leader, styling him Marshal of the Army of God and Holy Church (1 mark).","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a) asks what John demanded of his barons and how; part (b) asks what the barons themselves did in 1215 before Runnymede.","For (a) take one device - a tax, a feudal due, or a price charged for a right; for (b) take one moment of the campaign, from Brackley to the opening of London.","Attach a precise detail to each - a number of levies, a name or a date - and do not drift into why the barons were angry."],"codes":["B2-3 — 2 Worsening relations with the barons: Growing financial impositions","B2-3 — 2 Worsening relations with the barons: The rebellion of 1215"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of Magna Carta (1215). [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the condition of England when King John died in October 1216. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked as two separate 2-mark parts: in each part one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about it, and a second feature within a part earns nothing. (a) One feature: it limited the king's power to take money; credit also clause 12 and the common counsel of the kingdom, the fixing of reliefs at £100 for a barony, clauses 39 and 40 on lawful judgement and the selling of justice, the sending away of the foreign mercenaries, the liberties of the Church and of London, or clause 61 and the twenty-five barons. (b) One feature: much of the kingdom was in enemy hands; credit also Louis holding London, Winchester and much of the south-east, the Scottish invasion and Alexander II's homage to Louis, Llywelyn's recovery of lands in Wales, the desertion of barons such as the Earl of Salisbury, mercenary captains holding royal castles, the ravaging of the countryside by both armies, the empty treasury or the baggage lost at the Wash.","sol":"1(a) Feature: the charter limited the king's power to take money (1 mark). Supporting information: no scutage or aid was to be levied without the common counsel of the kingdom apart from the three customary feudal aids, and the relief an earl or baron paid to inherit his lands was fixed at £100 instead of whatever the king chose to demand (1 mark). 1(b) Feature: when John died much of England was held by his enemies (1 mark). Supporting information: Prince Louis held London, Winchester and much of the south-east, Alexander II of Scotland had invaded the north and done homage to Louis at Dover, and Llywelyn ab Iorwerth had taken back lands in Wales (1 mark).","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a) is the document agreed at Runnymede in June 1215; part (b) is the state of the kingdom sixteen months later.","For (a) take one area of royal power the charter touched - money, justice, the Church, London - and name the promise; for (b) take one kind of damage: who held the land, who held the castles, or what state the Crown's money was in.","Name the clause or the enemy precisely: 'it gave people rights' and 'the country was in a mess' earn nothing without the detail that proves them."],"codes":["B2-3 — 3 Magna Carta and the First Barons' War: Runnymede","B2-3 — 4 England in 1216: The condition of England at the time of John's death"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the siege of Rochester castle in 1215. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the coronation of Henry III in October 1216. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked as two separate 2-mark parts: in each part one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about it, and a second feature within a part earns nothing. (a) One feature: a rebel garrison held a royal castle against the king; credit also the command of William d'Aubigny, the length of the siege, John's mercenaries and siege engines, the mining of the south-east corner tower with the fat of forty pigs, the surrender of the starving garrison at the end of November, or John's treatment of the prisoners. (b) One feature: it was a hurried crowning held far from Westminster; credit also the date of 28 October 1216 and the place, Gloucester, the nine-year-old king's age, his knighting by William Marshal, the crowning by Peter des Roches, Bishop of Winchester, the papal legate Guala presiding, or the plain gold circlet used because the regalia were out of reach.","sol":"1(a) Feature: it was a long siege of a rebel-held royal castle (1 mark). Supporting information: William d'Aubigny got into Rochester with a body of knights in October 1215 and held the great stone keep against John's army and siege engines for about seven weeks, the starving garrison surrendering at the end of November (1 mark). 1(b) Feature: the crowning was hurried and held away from Westminster (1 mark). Supporting information: with London in rebel and French hands, John's nine-year-old son was crowned at Gloucester on 28 October 1216, nine days after his father's death, by Peter des Roches, Bishop of Winchester, with the papal legate Guala presiding and a plain gold circlet in place of the regalia (1 mark).","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a) is a siege in the autumn of 1215; part (b) is a crowning nine days after King John's death.","For (a) take either who held the castle and for how long, or the method by which John got in; for (b) take either where and when the ceremony was held, or who performed it and what was used in place of the crown.","One precise detail in each part - a name, a length of time, a method or a date - and no account of why it mattered."],"codes":["B2-3 — 3 Magna Carta and the First Barons' War: Reasons for the outbreak of the First Barons' War","B2-3 — 4 England in 1216: The problem of the succession"]},{"q":"Explain why King John quarrelled with the Papacy in the years 1205-09. You may use the following in your answer: • the monks of Canterbury • Stephen Langton. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit the rival elections of 1205, Innocent III's imposition of Langton and his French connections, and beyond the bullets the wealth of Canterbury, John's need for money and control after the loss of Normandy, and the characters of the two men. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"John quarrelled with Rome because a vacancy at Canterbury forced three rival claims to the same right into the open at the worst possible moment for him. The first reason was the disputed election of 1205. Hubert Walter died that year and the monks of Christ Church, who claimed the ancient right to choose their archbishop, sent their sub-prior Reginald secretly to Rome for confirmation; John, who expected the king's candidate to have the richest see in England, made them elect John de Gray, Bishop of Norwich, instead. Two elections meant an appeal, and an appeal meant that the decision left England altogether. The second reason was the answer Innocent III gave. He quashed both elections, had the monks at Rome choose the English cardinal Stephen Langton, and consecrated him in 1207. That was intolerable to John on two counts: the Pope had provided an archbishop over the king's head, and the man provided had built his career teaching in Paris, in the lands of Philip II, the king who had just stripped John of Normandy. The third reason, which the bullets do not offer, is money and timing. Canterbury's estates were enormous, and a king who had lost Normandy in 1204 and was taxing hard to recover it could afford neither an archbishop he did not control nor an income he could not touch, so he expelled the Canterbury monks and took the archbishopric into his own hands. Behind all three lay two men who did not retreat: Innocent had already forced his authority on rulers elsewhere, and John's instinct in a quarrel was always to raise the stakes. The dispute therefore began as a question of who appointed bishops, but it became a six-year war of Interdict and excommunication because both men treated any concession as a surrender of authority they could not spare.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what each of the three parties thought was rightfully theirs.","Plan three reasons of different kinds: the election, the man chosen, and what John stood to lose in cash and control.","Every paragraph should finish on the quarrel itself, explaining why that reason made agreement harder."],"codes":["B2-3 — 1 The dispute with the Papacy: Causes of the dispute"]},{"q":"Explain why the Interdict of 1208-14 had such an impact on the lives of ordinary people in England. You may use the following in your answer: • church services • the burial of the dead. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit the closing of the churches and the stopping of Mass and church marriage, burial in unconsecrated ground without a priest, and beyond the bullets medieval beliefs about salvation, the departure of the bishops after 1209, and John's seizure of Church estates and its effect on tenants and on charity. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The Interdict bit so deeply because it took away the one thing medieval people could not do without. The first reason is the stopping of ordinary worship. From March 1208 church doors were shut, Mass was not sung and marriages were no longer celebrated inside the church; for a village whose week, year and social life were organised around the parish church, the silence was a permanent public reminder that the kingdom lay under a sentence. The second reason is what happened to the dead. Burial in consecrated ground with a priest's prayers was believed to matter for the soul, and under the Interdict the dead were put into unconsecrated ground without that service, so every family that lost a child or a parent between 1208 and 1214 carried a private fear for them. That is the point the bullets only hint at: the Interdict worked on belief. People thought salvation ran through the sacraments, and Innocent had aimed his weapon at the laity in order to make them force their king to give way. The third reason, which lies outside the bullets, is economic and administrative. John confiscated the estates and revenues of clergy who obeyed the sentence, so Church tenants found themselves paying royal officials, and after his excommunication in 1209 most bishops left the country, leaving dioceses without leadership and the almsgiving and hospitality of the Church weakened. Baptism and the confession of the dying were allowed throughout, which stopped the sentence from being total, and there is little sign that ordinary people rose against John because of it. The impact was therefore less a matter of rebellion than of atmosphere: six years in which the Church could be seen to be at war with the king made John's reputation as a godless, grasping ruler, and that reputation was still working against him in 1215.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the parish church actually did for people week by week.","Three different kinds of impact will beat three examples of the same kind: worship, death, and money or leadership.","Explain why each loss mattered to people who believed what they believed - the belief is the causal link."],"codes":["B2-3 — 1 The dispute with the Papacy: The Interdict and its impact on everyday life"]},{"q":"Explain why Pope Innocent III became King John's ally after 1213. You may use the following in your answer: • the papal fief • the rebel barons. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit John's surrender of England and Ireland as a papal fief for 1,000 marks a year, the duty of an overlord to protect his vassal, the annulment of Magna Carta in August 1215 and the excommunication of the rebels, and beyond the bullets John's crusading vow of March 1215, Innocent's views on anointed kingship and the suspension of Langton. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Innocent changed sides because in 1213 John made him something no pope could ignore: his lord. The first reason is the fief itself. By surrendering England and Ireland to the papacy and taking them back for a tribute of 1,000 marks a year, John turned his kingdom into papal property, and an overlord who lets his vassal be robbed destroys his own lordship. From that moment an attack on John was an attack on the rights of Rome, and Innocent said so. The second reason is what the rebels then did. The barons did not merely resist a king; they forced a charter on a papal vassal and set up twenty-five of their number with the legal right to seize his castles, and they did it without reference to Rome. Innocent annulled Magna Carta on 24 August 1215 as shameful and extorted by force, excommunicated the rebels, and suspended Stephen Langton, the archbishop he had himself imposed, for refusing to publish the sentence, which shows how far he was prepared to go. The third reason is not in the bullets at all: John made himself the kind of king the papacy wanted to defend. He took the cross in March 1215, so that as a crusader his lands were under Church protection, he paid his tribute and he promised compensation for the Church property he had seized. Innocent, whose whole pontificate rested on the claim that spiritual authority stood above kings, also had a principle at stake: an anointed king brought to terms by armed subjects was a precedent that damaged every ruler. The alliance was therefore no personal affection but the logic of the 1213 bargain working itself out, and it is the main reason John could fight on after Runnymede with the Church on his side and the rebels outside it.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Work out what John had made the Pope in 1213, and what that role obliged him to do.","Three strands: the feudal bargain, what the rebels did to a papal vassal, and Innocent's own view of kingship.","Each paragraph should end by saying why that made the Pope act for John rather than against him."],"codes":["B2-3 — 1 The dispute with the Papacy: The significance of the reconciliation between John and Innocent III"]},{"q":"Explain why the rebel barons were able to force King John to negotiate at Runnymede in June 1215. You may use the following in your answer: • London • Northampton. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit the opening of London's gates on 17 May 1215 and what the capital gave the rebels, the failure at Northampton and the limits of rebel siege power, and beyond the bullets John's shortage of trustworthy troops and money, the number of barons waiting to see who won, and the mediation of Stephen Langton and moderates such as William Marshal. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The rebels forced John to talk without ever beating him in battle, and the explanation lies in what each side could and could not do. The first reason is London. The rebels renounced their homage at Brackley on 5 May 1215 and marched, and on Sunday 17 May the gates of London were opened to them while the citizens were at church. That gave them the wealth of the kingdom's greatest city, walls John could not storm quickly, and a political signal that other towns noticed; a king who does not hold his capital cannot pretend the revolt is a local affair. The second reason is the limit the rebels had already discovered at Northampton. They besieged the castle for about a fortnight without the engines or the skill to take it, and Lincoln castle never fell to them either. Since neither side could destroy the other, talking was the only way either could gain anything, and both knew it. The third reason, beyond the bullets, is John's own weakness in the spring of 1215. His treasure had gone on the failed campaign of 1214, he was still waiting on the foreign troops he had sent for from Flanders and Poitou, and a large body of barons was standing back to see which way the war went, so every week of fighting risked turning waverers into rebels. Finally there was a route to negotiation available: Stephen Langton and moderates such as William Marshal were pressing for terms, and John, who had already taken the cross in March and could appeal to his papal overlord, had every reason to buy time rather than fight. The rebels therefore forced negotiation not because they were winning but because they had taken the one prize John could not do without while he still needed a settlement more than a siege.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the rebels actually held by the end of May, and what they had failed to take.","Balance what the rebels gained against what John lacked - that contrast is the explanation.","Finish each paragraph on the word negotiate, showing why that factor pushed John to the table."],"codes":["B2-3 — 2 Worsening relations with the barons: The rebellion of 1215"]},{"q":"Explain why war broke out between King John and his barons after the agreement at Runnymede in 1215. You may use the following in your answer: • the twenty-five barons • Pope Innocent III. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit clause 61 and the security committee, the papal annulment of 24 August 1215 with the excommunication of the rebels and the suspension of Langton, and beyond the bullets the failure of either side to disarm, John's hiring of mercenaries and his castles, and ten years of mutual distrust. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The settlement of 15 June 1215 collapsed within about ten weeks because it contained no way of being kept. The first reason is clause 61, the security clause. Twenty-five barons were empowered to distrain on the king's castles and lands if he broke the charter, which in practice licensed rebellion by committee. No anointed king could live with subjects holding a legal right to make war on him, and the clause therefore guaranteed that John would look for a way out of the whole document from the day he sealed it. The second reason is the man he looked to. As a papal vassal since 1213, John appealed to Rome, and on 24 August 1215 Innocent III annulled the charter as shameful, illegal and extorted by force, excommunicated the rebels and later suspended Stephen Langton for refusing to publish the sentence. That did not merely release John from his promises; it put the Church's authority on his side and the rebels outside it, so the war could be fought as a just cause. The third reason, which the bullets do not supply, is that neither side ever disarmed. The rebels kept London and their castles, John kept hiring mercenaries from Flanders and Poitou and held the great royal fortresses, and each could see the other preparing. Behind that lay a decade of broken faith: men who remembered the fate of the de Braose family and the hostages taken from baronial households were not going to disband on a promise. Charters do not enforce themselves, and this one had no arbiter both sides accepted, so the only question after Runnymede was who would strike first. The rebels did: Rochester's constable, Reginald de Cornhill, admitted William d'Aubigny and a rebel force to that royal castle, and John came up from Dover to invest it from 11 October 1215, taking it at the end of November.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask who was supposed to make John keep his word, and what happened when he decided not to.","Three strands work well: the clause, the Pope, and what both armies were doing all summer.","Aim each paragraph at the outbreak of fighting, not at the terms of the charter."],"codes":["B2-3 — 3 Magna Carta and the First Barons' War: Reasons for the outbreak of the First Barons' War","B2-3 — 3 Magna Carta and the First Barons' War: Runnymede"]},{"q":"Explain why the succession was a problem when King John died in October 1216. You may use the following in your answer: • Prince Louis of France • the age of Henry III. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit Louis's hold on London and the south-east and the homage done to him, Henry's age of nine and the lack of machinery for a minority, and beyond the bullets the loss of the regalia and treasure, the improvised coronation at Gloucester under the legate Guala, and the number of barons who had already changed sides. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The succession was a problem because there was a rival already in possession and an heir too young to contest anything himself. The first reason is Louis. Invited by the rebels, he had landed in Kent in May 1216 and was received in London in June, and by the autumn he held the capital, Winchester and much of the south-east, with the homage of many English barons, John's half-brother the Earl of Salisbury among them, and of Alexander II of Scotland. A claimant who holds the capital and half the baronage is not a pretender but a government in waiting. The second reason is Henry's age. Born in 1207, he was nine, so there could be no king to lead an army, hold a court or overawe a magnate for years to come, and England had no settled arrangements for a minority; everything would depend on whoever spoke in his name. The third reason, beyond the bullets, is that the ordinary marks of legitimacy were missing. Westminster was in enemy hands and part of the royal treasure had been lost crossing the Wash days before John died, so the boy had to be crowned at Gloucester on 28 October 1216 with a plain gold circlet in place of a crown, by bishops acting under the papal legate Guala rather than by an archbishop at Westminster. A hurried coronation in the west with borrowed ornaments invited the charge that he was not properly king at all. Against all that stood the one advantage of the situation, and it decided the outcome: the child had done nobody any injury, so barons who would never trust John again could return to his son without dishonour, which is exactly what William Marshal's government set out to let them do.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what a nine-year-old could not do that a king was expected to do.","Set the rival's strength beside the heir's weakness, then add what was missing at the coronation.","Each paragraph should end on the succession itself - why that made the boy's claim harder to enforce."],"codes":["B2-3 — 4 England in 1216: The problem of the succession","B2-3 — 4 England in 1216: The condition of England at the time of John's death"]},{"q":"'King John's arbitrary treatment of individual barons did more than anything else to drive them into rebellion in 1215.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the de Braose family • hostages. You must also use information of your own. (16 marks)","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit the destruction of William de Braose and the deaths of his wife and eldest son in 1210, hostages taken from baronial households, debts and 'fines' called in at will and the plot of 1212, and against the statement the weight of the eleven scutages and the thirteenth of 1207, the failure at Bouvines on 27 July 1214 and the baronial programme drawn from Henry I's coronation charter. Caps: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Rebellion meant risking lands, heirs and life, so the test must be which grievance made men willing to take that risk rather than complain. Judged that way, John's handling of individual barons explains the fear behind the rising, but the rising itself waited on defeat in France. The case for the statement is strong. John ruled his barons through their debts and their families. A man who wanted his inheritance or a wardship promised thousands of marks, and the unpaid balance hung over him, to be called in the moment he became inconvenient; sons and daughters were taken from baronial households as sureties for good behaviour. The lesson was taught in public in 1210, when William de Braose, once the greatest beneficiary of royal favour in the Welsh march, was hounded into exile while his wife and eldest son were imprisoned and starved to death. No tax roll explains that, and no baron who heard of it could believe his own family safe. It is why the plot of 1212 was a conspiracy and not a protest: Robert FitzWalter and Eustace de Vesci planned to kill or desert the king on his Welsh campaign, and when word reached John he broke off the expedition while they fled the realm, de Vesci to Scotland and FitzWalter to France. Men do not do that over a demand they expect to survive. Money, though, was the grievance every baron shared. John took scutage eleven times in a reign of seventeen years and raised the rate; in 1207 he levied a thirteenth of movable goods and income which brought in roughly sixty thousand pounds, far beyond the Crown's ordinary annual revenue. Arbitrary lordship terrified a handful of great men; taxation reached them all, and the charter they eventually obtained fixed a baron's relief at £100 and barred scutage without the common counsel of the kingdom. The factor the bullets do not offer is the failure of 1214, and it settles the question. Every demand had been justified by the promise of Normandy. When John's Poitevin lords refused battle at Roche-au-Moine and Philip II destroyed his allies at Bouvines on 27 July 1214, the promise died and with it the fear, because a king who cannot win cannot punish. The northern barons refused the scutage he demanded on his return, refusal by one group made refusal by others safe, and within a year the rebels renounced their homage at Brackley on 5 May 1215 and entered London on 17 May. Arbitrary rule had been practised for a decade without producing a rising. Nor was the revolt only about fear. In January 1215 the barons demanded confirmation of Henry I's coronation charter, and clause 39 of the document they won promised that no free man would be seized or dispossessed except by the lawful judgement of his equals - a remedy aimed squarely at arbitrary power, and the statement's best evidence. So I agree in part, on the criterion set out at the start. Arbitrary lordship supplied the motive and the programme; the defeat at Bouvines supplied the opportunity and the safety to act on them. Remove the de Braose affair and the barons still rebel in 1215 over money and custom; remove Bouvines and it is hard to see them daring to rebel at all.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what a baron stood to lose by taking arms, and what would have to be true before he thought it worth the risk.","Set John's dealings with named individuals against the grievances every baron shared, and against what changed in 1214.","The conclusion must rank the reasons and name the test you ranked them by, rather than giving them equal weight."],"codes":["B2-3 — 2 Worsening relations with the barons: Growing financial impositions","B2-3 — 2 Worsening relations with the barons: The impact of the failure to regain Normandy in 1214","B2-3 — 2 Worsening relations with the barons: The rebellion of 1215"]},{"q":"'The annulment of Magna Carta by Pope Innocent III was the main reason for the outbreak of the First Barons' War.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the twenty-five barons • mercenaries. You must also use information of your own. (16 marks)","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit the bull of 24 August 1215, the excommunication of the rebels and the suspension of Langton, and against the statement clause 61 and its unworkability, the failure of either side to disarm, John's hiring of foreign troops, ten years of distrust and the rebels' retention of London. Caps: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The annulment gave John permission to fight, but permission is not the same as cause, and a judgement here depends on whether the settlement of June 1215 could have held had Innocent said nothing. The evidence suggests it could not. The case for the statement is real. John was a papal vassal, and his appeal to his overlord produced a bull on 24 August 1215 declaring Magna Carta shameful, illegal and void because it had been extorted by force, excommunicating the rebels and later suspending Stephen Langton for refusing to publish the sentence. That transformed the legal position. A king who repudiated his own sealed charter would have been a perjurer; a king whose overlord had cancelled it was acting lawfully, and his enemies were now excommunicates against whom war was a duty. The papal alliance John bought in 1213 was never worth more than in the autumn of 1215. Against the statement stands the charter itself. Clause 61 set twenty-five barons over the king with the right to seize his castles and lands if he broke his word, which no medieval king could accept and no king did; it was less a guarantee than a standing invitation to war. Neither side behaved as though peace had been made. The rebels kept London, which they had entered on 17 May, and did not disperse their army; John was recruiting mercenaries from Flanders and Poitou through the summer and held the great royal castles. Both were preparing for the fighting that followed. The deeper cause, beyond both bullets, is that ten years of John's government had destroyed the trust any settlement needed. Men who remembered the hostages taken from baronial households and the starving of William de Braose's wife and son in 1210 did not believe John's promises; John, who had been plotted against in 1212, did not believe theirs. The charter tried to solve a problem of confidence with a piece of parchment. There is also the plain question of capacity: John had the money, the mercenaries and the fortresses to fight, and he used them, marching on Rochester in October and taking the castle at the end of November 1215 after mining its keep. Had he lacked the means, no bull would have started a war. So the annulment is best understood as the trigger and the licence rather than the cause. It decided the timing and it gave John's campaign the Church's blessing, which mattered when he was recruiting and when the rebels sought a new king; but the war came because clause 61 made the peace unenforceable, because neither army stood down, and because neither side could trust the other. Judged by what made war unavoidable rather than by what made it lawful, the main reason lies in the settlement and the men who signed it, with the annulment a powerful second.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask yourself whether the peace could have survived if the Pope had stayed silent - your answer to that is your argument.","Separate what made the war lawful from what made it likely, and organise the essay around that distinction.","The conclusion must rank the annulment against the other causes and justify the criterion you used."],"codes":["B2-3 — 3 Magna Carta and the First Barons' War: Reasons for the outbreak of the First Barons' War","B2-3 — 3 Magna Carta and the First Barons' War: Runnymede","B2-3 — 1 The dispute with the Papacy: The significance of the reconciliation between John and Innocent III"]},{"q":"'The invasion of Prince Louis was the main reason for the poor condition of England when King John died in 1216.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the First Barons' War • Alexander II of Scotland. You must also use information of your own. (16 marks)","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit Louis's landing in May 1216, his reception in London and the barons who did him homage, and against the statement the devastation of the civil war before he came, John's mercenaries and winter campaign, the exhausted treasury, the Scottish and Welsh attacks and the aftermath of the Interdict. Caps: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"England in October 1216 was ruined in several different ways at once, so the question is which cause did most of the damage and whether Louis made the others worse or merely joined them. Judged by how much of the country changed hands and how deep the ruin ran, the invasion was the heaviest single blow, but it was the civil war that made the invasion possible and the civil war that had already wrecked the kingdom. The case for the statement is strong. Louis landed in Kent in May 1216, was received in London in June, and by the autumn held the capital, Winchester, every one of the Cinque Ports except Dover and much of the south-east. His coming changed the character of the conflict: a quarrel about a charter became a war for the throne, so barons who had wanted only their liberties now had to choose a king, and many, including John's own half-brother the Earl of Salisbury, chose the Frenchman. It also brought in others. Alexander II of Scotland invaded the north and travelled the length of England to do homage to Louis at Dover, and Llywelyn ab Iorwerth used the collapse to recover lands in Wales, so by October John's writ ran in a shrinking patch of the midlands and the west. Yet almost all the physical destruction predates the landing. From the autumn of 1215 John made war with foreign troops paid from an empty treasury, which meant letting them live off the country: he took Rochester at the end of November after a siege of about seven weeks, then harried rebel lands north to Berwick through the winter of 1215-16, burning as he went, while the rebels did the same to royalist estates. Mercenary captains held royal castles as security for wages the Crown could not pay. Beyond the war, the exhaustion was older still. A decade of scutages, the thirteenth of 1207 and the ruinous campaign of 1214 had emptied the treasury before Louis was invited, and the Church was still repairing six years of Interdict. The loss of part of the baggage train, including some of the royal treasure, in the estuary at the Wash in October 1216 was the last humiliation rather than the cause of poverty. It is fairer to say that the invasion multiplied damage that the First Barons' War had already done. Louis brought no great devastation of his own; what he brought was legitimacy for the rebels and an excuse for England's neighbours, which turned a rebellion John was slowly winning into a war he could not finish. So I agree in part. Measured by the extent of the kingdom lost and by the danger to the dynasty, the invasion was the main reason England was in so poor a state, because it drew in Scotland, Wales and half the baronage at once. Measured by the ruin of the land, the treasury and the Church, the civil war and the taxation that preceded it did more. The condition of England in 1216 was the work of John's own rule; Louis was the proof of how bad it had become.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide what 'poor condition' is being measured by - land lost, money, damage on the ground - because different measures give different answers.","Set what Louis actually did against what the war and the taxation had already done before he landed.","A qualified judgement scores well here, provided you say on which measure each side of it is true."],"codes":["B2-3 — 4 England in 1216: The condition of England at the time of John's death","B2-3 — 3 Magna Carta and the First Barons' War: Reasons for the outbreak of the First Barons' War"]},{"q":"'William Marshal's work as Protector saved the throne for Henry III.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the reissue of Magna Carta • the papal legate. You must also use information of your own. (16 marks)","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit Marshal's appointment as rector regis et regni, the reissue of the charter in November 1216, the victory at Lincoln on 20 May 1217 and the treaty of September 1217, and against the statement the work of Guala and papal support, Hubert de Burgh and the sea fight off Sandwich, Louis's own mistakes and the effect of John's death. Caps: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"A throne is saved when the men who were fighting the king stop fighting his heir, so the test of Marshal's Protectorate is whether his decisions brought the rebels back rather than whether he won battles. On that criterion the statement is largely right, but it needs two qualifications: John's death did part of the work before Marshal did anything, and the Church supplied the authority he could not. The strongest case for Marshal is political. Aged about seventy and with four kings' service behind him, he accepted the title of rector regis et regni, and in November 1216 his government reissued Magna Carta in the nine-year-old king's name, leaving out the security clause and the terms no king could keep. At a stroke the rebels' own programme became royal policy. Men who had taken arms for the charter could now come in without dishonour or defeat, and they began to do so through the winter. That was a choice, not an accident: Marshal understood that the war would be ended by removing its cause rather than by punishing those who had fought. He then finished it in the field, leading the royalist army that beat the French and rebel forces at Lincoln on 20 May 1217, after which Louis's reinforcement fleet was destroyed off Sandwich in August and Louis renounced his claim by the treaty made in September 1217. Against the statement stands the legate. The boy was crowned at Gloucester on 28 October 1216 by Peter des Roches, Bishop of Winchester, with a plain gold circlet because Westminster and the regalia were out of reach, and it was Guala Bicchieri who presided over the ceremony; he declared the royalist cause a crusade, excommunicated Louis and his supporters and could threaten the rebels with interdict. England had been a papal fief since 1213, and that inheritance from John gave the minority government a legitimacy no soldier could manufacture; the reissued charter itself was sealed by Guala and Marshal because the child had no seal of his own. Others mattered too: Hubert de Burgh had held Dover against Louis through 1216 and was behind the victory at sea in 1217. There is also the blunt fact that the rebels' case died with John. They had invited Louis because they could not live with the father, not because they wanted a French king, and Louis made it easy to leave him by granting lands and offices to Frenchmen and alienating his English allies. A nine-year-old with no record could be served by anyone. So the claim stands, with limits. Marshal did not save the throne single-handed, and the throne was made saveable by John's death, by papal protection and by Louis's own errors. But the decisive act of the minority was the reissue of the charter in November 1216, and that was Marshal's government's doing; it converted a war for the crown back into a settled kingdom and made Lincoln a mopping-up operation rather than a last stand. Judged by what turned enemies into subjects, Marshal's work was indeed what saved Henry III's throne.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Work out what actually ends a civil war - beating the enemy, or giving him nothing left to fight for.","Weigh Marshal's decisions against what the legate, the navy and Louis's own mistakes contributed.","Do not simply agree; say which of Marshal's acts was decisive and why it outweighs the rest."],"codes":["B2-3 — 4 England in 1216: The role of William Marshal as Protector","B2-3 — 4 England in 1216: The problem of the succession"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In which year did Pope Innocent III lay England under an Interdict?","a":"1208 (March 1208); it was lifted in 1214."},{"q":"At which battle, on 27 July 1214, did Philip II destroy King John's allies?","a":"Bouvines."},{"q":"Which rebel-held castle did King John take at the end of November 1215 by mining its keep?","a":"Rochester."},{"q":"Where did King John die on the night of 18-19 October 1216?","a":"Newark castle."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The Canterbury election dispute, 1205","back":"Hubert Walter died in 1205; the monks secretly elected Reginald, John demanded John de Gray. Innocent III quashed both and imposed Stephen Langton, consecrated 1207. John barred Langton from England and expelled the monks.","code":"B2-3 — 1 The dispute with the Papacy: Causes of the dispute","open":false},{"front":"The Interdict, 1208-14","back":"Papal sentence from March 1208 closing England's churches: no Mass, no church weddings, burial without a priest in unconsecrated ground. Baptism and confession of the dying continued. John was excommunicated in 1209 and seized clergy revenues.","code":"B2-3 — 1 The dispute with the Papacy: The Interdict and its impact on everyday life","open":false},{"front":"The submission to Innocent III, 1213","back":"In May 1213 John accepted Langton, promised compensation, and surrendered England and Ireland to the Pope, receiving them back as a fief for 1,000 marks a year. Innocent became his overlord and protector.","code":"B2-3 — 1 The dispute with the Papacy: The significance of the reconciliation between John and Innocent III","open":false},{"front":"Scutage and the thirteenth of 1207","back":"Scutage was cash paid instead of knight service; John took it eleven times in a reign of seventeen years and raised the rate. The 1207 tax of a thirteenth on movable goods and income raised roughly sixty thousand pounds.","code":"B2-3 — 2 Worsening relations with the barons: Growing financial impositions","open":false},{"front":"The plot of 1212","back":"Robert FitzWalter and Eustace de Vesci planned to kill or desert John on his Welsh campaign. The plot collapsed, both fled abroad, and John abandoned the campaign and demanded more hostages from baronial families.","code":"B2-3 — 2 Worsening relations with the barons: Growing financial impositions","open":false},{"front":"Give one example of King John's use of arbitrary power","back":"The imprisonment and starving to death of William de Braose's wife and eldest son in 1210; the taking of barons' children as hostages for good behaviour; the calling in of crushing debts and 'fines' at will.","code":"B2-3 — 2 Worsening relations with the barons: Growing financial impositions","open":true},{"front":"The Battle of Bouvines, 27 July 1214","back":"Philip II destroyed John's allies, among them Otto IV, the Count of Flanders and the Earl of Salisbury. With the withdrawal from Roche-au-Moine it ended John's attempt to regain Normandy and wasted the treasure he had raised.","code":"B2-3 — 2 Worsening relations with the barons: The impact of the failure to regain Normandy in 1214","open":false},{"front":"The rebellion of 1215","back":"Barons demanded Henry I's coronation charter in January, renounced their homage at Brackley on 5 May and chose Robert FitzWalter as leader. Northampton castle held out against them; London opened its gates on 17 May.","code":"B2-3 — 2 Worsening relations with the barons: The rebellion of 1215","open":false},{"front":"Magna Carta, 15 June 1215","back":"Charter of sixty-three clauses agreed at Runnymede. It fixed a baron's relief at £100, barred scutage without the common counsel of the kingdom and promised lawful judgement before imprisonment. Four original copies survive.","code":"B2-3 — 3 Magna Carta and the First Barons' War: Runnymede","open":false},{"front":"Clause 61, the security clause","back":"Twenty-five barons were empowered to seize the king's castles and lands if he broke Magna Carta. No king could accept subjects with a lawful right to make war on him, so the clause made the peace unworkable.","code":"B2-3 — 3 Magna Carta and the First Barons' War: Runnymede","open":false},{"front":"The siege of Rochester, 1215","back":"William d'Aubigny held the castle for the rebels from October. After about seven weeks John's miners brought down the keep's south-east tower, firing the tunnel props with the fat of forty pigs; the starving garrison surrendered.","code":"B2-3 — 3 Magna Carta and the First Barons' War: Reasons for the outbreak of the First Barons' War","open":false},{"front":"The invasion of Prince Louis, 1216","back":"Invited by the rebels, Philip II's son landed in Kent in May 1216 and was received in London in June. He held Winchester and much of the south-east and took the homage of many English barons.","code":"B2-3 — 3 Magna Carta and the First Barons' War: Reasons for the outbreak of the First Barons' War","open":false},{"front":"The death of King John","back":"John lost part of his baggage train, including some royal treasure, crossing the estuary at the Wash, fell ill and died at Newark castle on the night of 18-19 October 1216. He was buried at Worcester.","code":"B2-3 — 4 England in 1216: The condition of England at the time of John's death","open":false},{"front":"The coronation of Henry III","back":"John's nine-year-old son was crowned at Gloucester on 28 October 1216 with a plain gold circlet, Westminster and the regalia being out of reach, by bishops acting under the papal legate Guala.","code":"B2-3 — 4 England in 1216: The problem of the succession","open":false},{"front":"William Marshal as Protector","back":"Earl of Pembroke, about seventy, named rector regis et regni in 1216. His government reissued Magna Carta without the security clause, won the battle at Lincoln on 20 May 1217 and made peace with Louis that September.","code":"B2-3 — 4 England in 1216: The role of William Marshal as Protector","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (the 16-mark judgement, 'The strength of the royal castles was the main reason Prince Louis had not conquered England by the time King John died in October 1216.')","items":["Question: 'The strength of the royal castles was the main reason Prince Louis had not conquered England by the time King John died in October 1216.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the siege of Dover • Windsor castle. You must also use information of your own. (16 marks). This is the paragraph from the middle of the essay that puts the statement at its strongest before the rest of the answer tests it.","What stopped Louis was not an English army but English masonry. He had entered London without a fight, taken Winchester after a short siege in June 1216 and held perhaps a third of the kingdom by midsummer, yet the places that mattered would not fall. At Dover, Hubert de Burgh let him batter down the barbican and bring a tower of the great gate into the ditch, then stopped the assault inside the breach and stopped up the gap with timber, rubble and earth; after some three months before the walls Louis took a truce on 14 October 1216 and rode back to London with the castle still the king's. Windsor did the same to the Count of Nevers, who sat before it from June until September 1216 and went away with nothing, because Engelard de Cigogné would not give it up. Measured by the test set out at the start - whether the claimant could turn ground he had overrun into a kingdom he could hold - the answer is no: an uncaptured Dover left his road home to France under threat, an uncaptured Windsor cut his conquests in two, and every month spent at a siege was a month in which the king's men rode where they pleased. The rebels had invited him to finish a war, and the castles saw to it that he could not.","Why this is Level 4: the explanation is analytical throughout and kept on the conceptual focus, which is the ranking of causes, not a narrative of the invasion. The knowledge is precisely selected and used to argue - Hubert de Burgh at Dover, the truce of 14 October 1216, the Count of Nevers before Windsor, Engelard de Cigogné - rather than listed. The paragraph applies the criterion announced in the introduction and closes with an interim judgement that ranks one cause above another, which is what the third bullet of the level descriptor rewards. It also develops the material well past the two stimulus points, bringing in the fall of Winchester, the extent of Louis's conquests and the effect of siege work on his freedom of movement, which the mark scheme requires before Level 4 is reachable at all."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: as above. The same point attempted by a weaker candidate.","Louis could not win because the castles were too strong for him. He attacked Dover for a long time but the men inside kept him out and in the end he had to give up and go back to London. He tried to take Windsor as well and that did not work either. This shows that the castles were important because if he had taken them he would have won, so King John was still king when he died.","What it does and does not do: it reaches Level 2 because an explanation is offered and the information is accurate and relevant, showing some knowledge of the period. It misses Level 3 and Level 4 because the links to the conceptual focus are implicit and unsustained - 'too strong for him' and 'he had to give up' assert rather than analyse, and no criterion for 'the main reason' is stated or applied. The detail is imprecise: no commander named at Dover or Windsor, no date for the siege or the truce, no account of what an uncaptured castle actually did to Louis's campaign. Its final sentence gives a judgement, but the justification is insecure, which is exactly the third bullet of the Level 2 descriptor. Note also the cap the mark scheme prints: a maximum of 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Interdict and the reconciliation between John and Innocent III","items":["Archbishop Hubert Walter died in 1205, leaving the richest see in England vacant and its election disputed.","The monks of Christ Church, Canterbury, secretly elected their sub-prior Reginald; John forced them to elect John de Gray, Bishop of Norwich.","Innocent III quashed both elections and had the English cardinal Stephen Langton chosen at Rome, consecrating him in 1207; Langton had taught for years in Paris.","John refused to admit Langton, expelled the Canterbury monks and seized the archbishopric's lands.","The Interdict was imposed in March 1208 and lasted until 1214: no Mass, no church marriages, and burial in unconsecrated ground without a priest, though baptism and the confession of the dying continued.","John was excommunicated in 1209; most bishops left England, and John kept the revenues of the clergy who obeyed the sentence.","By 1212 Innocent was threatening deposition and had released John's subjects from their allegiance, while Philip II prepared to invade.","In May 1213 John submitted to the papal legate Pandulf, accepted Langton and promised compensation to the Church.","He surrendered England and Ireland to the Pope and received them back as a papal fief for a tribute of 1,000 marks a year, 700 for England and 300 for Ireland.","Langton absolved John in the summer of 1213; the Interdict was lifted in 1214 once terms of compensation were settled.","The reward came in 1215: Innocent annulled Magna Carta on 24 August, excommunicated the rebels and suspended Langton for refusing to publish the sentence."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: King John's money - taxation, scutage and 'fines'","items":["Normandy was lost in 1204, and the recovery of the lost lands was the declared purpose of almost every financial demand that followed.","Scutage, the payment made instead of knight service, was taken eleven times in a reign of seventeen years, and at a raised rate for the Poitou campaign of 1214.","In 1207 John levied a thirteenth of movable goods and income, which brought in roughly sixty thousand pounds, far more than the Crown's ordinary yearly revenue.","Reliefs, wardships and the marriages of heiresses and widows were sold at whatever the market and the king's pleasure allowed; barons offered thousands of marks for inheritances and for royal goodwill.","Debts owed to the Crown were left outstanding and called in when the king wished, which gave him a hold over individual barons.","Children of baronial families were taken as hostages for their fathers' loyalty.","In 1210 John levied a very heavy tallage on England's Jewish community, imprisoning those who did not pay.","The estates and revenues of clergy who observed the Interdict were confiscated between 1208 and 1214 and provided a large royal income.","William de Braose, once a favourite, was ruined over an enormous debt; his wife Matilda and eldest son were imprisoned and starved to death in 1210.","The plot of 1212: Robert FitzWalter and Eustace de Vesci planned to kill or desert John during his Welsh campaign; the plot collapsed and both fled abroad, FitzWalter to France and de Vesci to Scotland.","John abandoned the Welsh campaign, hanged Welsh hostages at Nottingham and demanded further hostages from the English baronage."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the failure to regain Normandy in 1214","items":["The plan was a twin attack: John landing at La Rochelle and advancing through Poitou, while his allies struck southern Flanders from the north-east.","The allies were the Emperor Otto IV, the Count of Flanders and John's half-brother William Longespee, Earl of Salisbury.","John's Poitevin lords refused to fight Prince Louis at Roche-au-Moine in July 1214, and John had to withdraw without a battle.","On 27 July 1214 Philip II destroyed the allied army at Bouvines; the Earl of Salisbury was taken prisoner.","John made a truce with Philip and returned to England in October 1214 with nothing gained.","The campaign had been paid for by scutage and by the treasure accumulated since 1204, and it was spent for no result.","On his return John demanded a further scutage from those who had not served; the northern barons refused to pay it.","The defeat destroyed both the justification for a decade of taxation and the fear on which John's government rested."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the rebellion of 1215 - Northampton, Lincoln and the march on London","items":["In January 1215 barons came armed to London and demanded that John confirm the coronation charter of Henry I.","John played for time, appealed to the Pope, and in March 1215 took the cross as a crusader so that his lands came under the protection of the Church.","On 5 May 1215 the rebels formally renounced their homage at Brackley.","They chose Robert FitzWalter as their leader, styling him Marshal of the Army of God and Holy Church.","The rebels besieged Northampton castle for about a fortnight and failed to take it, having neither the engines nor the experience for a siege.","Bedford castle was handed over to them by its constable.","Lincoln castle held out for the king and never fell to the rebels; it was still holding out when the royalists relieved it in 1217.","London's gates were opened to the rebels on Sunday 17 May 1215 while the citizens were at church, giving them the wealth and walls of the greatest city in the kingdom.","With London lost, John agreed to negotiate; Stephen Langton and moderates such as William Marshal carried messages between the two sides.","The rebels never defeated John in battle, which is why he was strong enough to renew the war in the autumn."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Runnymede and Magna Carta","items":["The two sides met in the meadow at Runnymede beside the Thames, between Windsor and Staines, and terms were agreed on 15 June 1215.","The barons' motives: to restore what they held to be ancient custom, to end arbitrary taxation and justice, and to bind the king by an enforceable document.","John's motives: to recover London, to end the revolt without a battle, and to gain time in which to appeal to his papal overlord.","The charter as conventionally numbered has sixty-three clauses; four original copies survive.","Clause 1 granted the freedom of the English Church; clause 13 confirmed the liberties of London and other towns.","Clause 2 fixed the relief for an earl's or a baron's inheritance at £100.","Clause 12 barred scutage and aids without the common counsel of the kingdom, apart from the three customary feudal aids.","Clause 39 promised that no free man would be seized, imprisoned, dispossessed, outlawed or exiled except by the lawful judgement of his equals or by the law of the land.","Clause 40 promised that right and justice would not be sold, denied or delayed.","Other clauses sent away the foreign mercenaries, protected widows from forced remarriage and set standard measures for wine and cloth.","Clause 61, the security clause, created a committee of twenty-five barons who could seize the king's castles and lands if he broke the terms.","Innocent III annulled the charter on 24 August 1215; it was reissued, without the security clause, in Henry III's name in November 1216 and again in 1217 and 1225."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the siege and taking of Rochester","items":["Rochester castle guarded the Medway crossing on the road from London to Dover and the Channel ports.","William d'Aubigny entered the castle with a body of rebel knights in October 1215 and held it against the king.","John brought his army and siege engines from Dover and invested the castle, using troops hired from Flanders and Poitou.","The bailey fell first; the garrison withdrew into the great stone keep.","John's miners tunnelled under the south-east corner tower of the keep and propped the tunnel with timber.","The props were fired with the fat of forty pigs, and the corner tower collapsed.","The garrison held the remaining half of the keep until starvation forced their surrender at the end of November 1215, after about seven weeks.","John wanted to hang the whole garrison; he was dissuaded and hanged only one crossbowman.","The fallen tower was rebuilt round instead of square, and the siege taught the rebels that they could not hold castles against him, which is why they looked to France for a king."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the invasion of Prince Louis","items":["After John took Rochester and harried the rebel lands north to Berwick in the winter of 1215-16, the rebels offered the crown to Louis, son of Philip II of France.","Louis's claim was argued through his wife Blanche of Castile, a granddaughter of Henry II.","Louis landed in Kent in May 1216 and met little resistance; John withdrew rather than fight him at once.","Louis was received in London in June 1216 and proclaimed king there, though he was never crowned.","He took Winchester and much of the south-east, and many English barons did him homage, including John's half-brother the Earl of Salisbury.","Alexander II of Scotland invaded the north and travelled south to do homage to Louis at Dover.","Dover castle, held by Hubert de Burgh, refused to surrender and remained a royalist island behind Louis's lines.","Innocent III's successor upheld the excommunication of Louis and his supporters, so the royalist cause kept the Church's backing.","Louis's grants of lands and offices to Frenchmen alienated his English allies and made it easier for them to return to Henry III after John's death."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: England in 1216 and the problem of the succession","items":["Louis held London, Winchester, every one of the Cinque Ports except Dover, and much of the south-east; royal authority survived mainly in the midlands and the west.","Alexander II of Scotland had invaded the north and Llywelyn ab Iorwerth had recovered lands in Wales.","Mercenary captains held royal castles as security for wages the Crown could not pay, and both armies had burned and plundered the countryside.","The treasury was empty after a decade of taxation and the wasted campaign of 1214, and the Church was still recovering from six years of Interdict.","In October 1216 part of John's baggage train, including some of the royal treasure, was lost crossing the estuary at the Wash.","John fell ill, probably with dysentery, and died at Newark castle on the night of 18-19 October 1216; he was buried at Worcester.","His heir Henry, born in 1207, was nine years old, and England had no settled machinery for governing during a minority.","Westminster and the regalia were out of reach, so Henry III was crowned at Gloucester on 28 October 1216 with a plain gold circlet.","The papal legate Guala Bicchieri presided; England had been a papal fief since 1213, so the Church defended the boy king's title.","The child's advantage was that he had done nobody any harm, so rebels could abandon John's cause without abandoning John's son."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: William Marshal as Protector","items":["William Marshal, Earl of Pembroke, was about seventy in 1216 and had served Henry II, the Young King, Richard I and John.","The council named him rector regis et regni, guardian of the king and the kingdom, with the legate Guala at his side.","In November 1216 the government reissued Magna Carta in Henry III's name, leaving out the security clause and the most humiliating terms.","Because the boy had no seal of his own, the reissued charter was sealed by Marshal and Guala.","The reissue took the rebels' programme away from them, and barons began returning to the royalist side through the winter.","Guala declared the royalist cause a holy war and kept Louis and his supporters excommunicate.","On 20 May 1217 the royalist army under Marshal defeated the French and rebel forces at Lincoln, where the castle had held out all along.","In August 1217 Louis's reinforcement fleet was destroyed off Sandwich by a force organised by Hubert de Burgh.","By the treaty made in September 1217 Louis renounced his claim to the English throne and left the country in return for a payment.","Magna Carta was reissued again in 1217, this time alongside a separate Charter of the Forest, so John's defeat on paper became the settlement of his son's reign."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — the 16-mark judgement: 'The Interdict cost the English Church more than it cost King John.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks)","items":["The stem as printed: the statement in quotation marks, then 'How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16)', then 'You may use the following in your answer: • the seizure of church estates • the exiled bishops. You must also use information of your own.'","Decoded: you are asked to weigh two sets of losses against one another across the years 1208-14, not to narrate the quarrel. 'Cost' has to be defined before it can be judged, and the concept being tested is consequence - who was left the weaker once the sentence was lifted.","Criterion to state in the opening: a cost counts as the heavier if it took away wealth or authority that was never afterwards recovered. Signal the argument at once - the Church lost the money, the king only ever lost the appearance of power.","Paragraph 1 (the statement at its strongest): Innocent III laid the Interdict on England in March 1208 and it was not lifted until July 1214. John's sheriffs took the estates and revenues of the clergy into the king's hands and put laymen in charge of bishoprics, abbeys and priories, leaving obedient clergy a bare allowance out of their own property; on the usual modern estimate the king had drawn well over £100,000 from vacant and confiscated churches by 1213. Interim judgement: six years of stripped revenue is a cost no institution shrugs off.","Paragraph 2 (the statement tested): while the sentences lasted the king was paying with his throne. He was excommunicated in 1209, which released his subjects from their allegiance; in 1212 Robert FitzWalter and Eustace de Vesci plotted to kill or desert him on campaign in Wales and then fled the country; and in May 1213 he accepted Langton, surrendered England and Ireland to the Pope and took them back as a fief for a tribute of 1,000 marks a year. Interim judgement: a danger to the crown itself is a cost of a different order from lost rents.","Paragraph 3 (beyond the stimulus): the exiled bishops. After the excommunication of 1209 all but one or two English bishops - John de Gray of Norwich and Peter des Roches of Winchester were the exceptions - were out of the country, so dioceses went ungoverned and appointments unmade for years together. Interim judgement: that is a loss of authority and not merely of income, and it falls on the Church's side of the scale.","Paragraph 4 (beyond the stimulus): what each side got back. The compensation settled on in 1214 was put at 100,000 marks and only part of it was ever handed over, so the Church never recovered its money; the king, by contrast, came out of the quarrel with an overlord who annulled Magna Carta on 24 August 1215 and had the rebels excommunicated by name that December, while Langton, the archbishop the Church had fought six years to install, was suspended for refusing to publish that sentence and was not back in England until 1218. Interim judgement: in cash the Church never recovered, and in power the king recovered more than he had lost.","The evaluation thread to run through every paragraph: after 1214, was the loss made good? Keep asking that and the essay weighs rather than lists.","Conclusion: agree in part. Measured in money and in the government of the Church, the Interdict cost the Church far the more, and the revenues the king had taken were never returned. Measured in authority, John's cost was real only while the sentences ran, because the submission of 1213 turned his punishment into the papal alliance that carried him through 1215 and his son through 1216. On the criterion set out at the start the statement stands for wealth and fails for power. Name that as the reason for the ranking.","Timing: about 27 minutes on this question, roughly a minute a mark, with three or four of them spent planning the order of the paragraphs. There are no SPaG marks on Paper 2, but the specialist vocabulary - interdict, excommunication, see, vacancy, fief, tribute - is what the AO1 strand is looking for."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:29:21Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Publishable: all four places that named Alexander II's homage now say Dover¹ - the two sols, the lesson idea and the Prince Louis fact file - and nothing in the kit pairs Canterbury with homage; every question key re-solved clean, the retargeted Wash, Dover truce, Windsor, annulment, 16 December 1215 excommunications, Thanet, Rochester, Berwick and Interdict facts verify on the record, tariffs, AO splits and the June 2024-25 caps and numbering match levels.md, dupscan prints 'no duplication', and no bank question reproduces specimen booklet B2 5(a) to 5(c)(ii); only the two minor points below remain.\n¹ He met Louis at Canterbury shortly after 8 September 1216 and rode on to Dover, where the homage was performed."}}},"B3-1":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"B3-1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This is the first key topic of the British depth study, and it is the one the examiners can question from three directions at once: what kind of king Henry VIII was, what Wolsey did with the power Henry gave him, and why that power vanished in 1529. Booklet B asks you to describe features of the period, to explain why something happened, and then to judge a claim about it, all from your own knowledge with no sources to lean on. Almost every judgement question on this key topic turns on the same hinge - Wolsey held everything from the king and nothing from anywhere else - so a student who can weigh Henry's character against Wolsey's ability has an argument ready for Wolsey's rise, his reforms, his foreign policy and his fall.","idea":[{"h":"England in 1509: the country Henry inherited","t":"England in 1509 held roughly two and a half million people, about nine in ten of them living off the land, with woollen cloth the great export and London far larger than any other town. Society was a hierarchy of nobles, gentry, yeomen, husbandmen and labourers, and the Church, obedient to Rome, owned great estates and ran its own courts. Government was personal: the king decided, advised by a Royal Council of nobles, churchmen and lawyers; Parliament met only when summoned, mainly to grant taxation; Justices of the Peace, unpaid local gentlemen, enforced the law in the counties. Henry VII had left a full treasury but a hated reputation for taxation, so his son began by arresting Empson and Dudley, who were executed in 1510. A top-level answer uses these features as causes, not scenery: a king who governs in person but hates paperwork needs a minister, and a Crown that cannot tax without Parliament will one day try the Amicable Grant.","code":"B3-1 — 1 Henry VIII, Renaissance Prince: England in 1509"},{"h":"Henry's character and his view of sovereignty","t":"Henry came to the throne at seventeen, tall, athletic and expensively educated: he read Latin, composed music, argued theology and in 1521 published a book against Luther that won him the papal title Defender of the Faith. He believed a king answered to God alone and that his honour was the honour of the kingdom, which made display, jousting and war matters of policy rather than indulgence. He was also impatient with the daily grind of administration, generous to servants who delivered results and merciless to those who failed him. That combination explains the shape of the reign: Henry set the aims and left the working out to somebody else, first Wolsey and later Cromwell. A top-level answer uses Henry's character as a cause with two edges - it created the opening that made Wolsey and it guaranteed that failure would end him.","code":"B3-1 — 1 Henry VIII, Renaissance Prince: Henry's character and views on sovereignty"},{"h":"Strengths, weaknesses and aims in the early years","t":"Henry's strengths were real: an unchallenged title, a treasury filled by his father, a gift for the theatre of kingship and an eye for able servants. His weaknesses were the other side of the same coin - he spent his father's money on war, he took little interest in how his policies were financed, and he was easily worked on by whoever had access to him at court. His aims were glory in France in the style of Henry V, standing alongside Francis I and Charles V as an equal, and above all a legitimate son, since the Tudors had held the throne only since 1485 and a disputed succession had torn England apart within living memory. A top-level answer measures success and failure in this key topic against those aims, so that Wolsey's foreign policy is judged by whether it gave Henry glory and prestige, not by whether it kept the peace.","code":"B3-1 — 1 Henry VIII, Renaissance Prince: Strengths, weaknesses and aims as monarch"},{"h":"Why Wolsey rose","t":"Thomas Wolsey was an Ipswich man of humble family who went to Oxford young, became a royal chaplain under Henry VII and was made Almoner in 1509. His chance came with the French campaign of 1513, when he organised the supply of an army across the Channel and the king saw that the tedious work was done, done quickly and done well. Promotion followed at speed: Bishop of Lincoln and Archbishop of York in 1514, cardinal in 1515, Lord Chancellor in December 1515 and papal legate in England from 1518, which put him above the Archbishop of Canterbury in the English Church. His capacity for work was famous and his wealth - Hampton Court, York Place, several sees at once - advertised it. A top-level answer keeps the two strands tied together: Wolsey's ability earned the promotions, but only because Henry wanted a minister who would take the business off his hands, and because a man of low birth owed everything to the king and could be dropped without offending a great family.","code":"B3-1 — 2 The rise of Wolsey and his policies: Reasons for Wolsey's rise to power"},{"h":"Wolsey's reforms: justice, finance, enclosure and the Eltham Ordinances","t":"As Lord Chancellor Wolsey used the Court of Star Chamber to give quick and cheap justice, hearing many times more cases than under Henry VII, and he developed the Court of Requests so that poor men could bring cases there cheaply; he was willing to bring noblemen before the court as well, which won him a reputation for fairness and a stock of enemies. In finance he pushed the subsidy, first granted in 1513, a tax assessed by commissioners on what a person's land and goods were actually worth rather than the fixed fifteenth and tenth, and it raised far more money. In 1517 he launched a national inquiry into enclosure since the late 1480s and took landowners to court, but abandoned the campaign in 1523 in return for a grant of taxation. The Eltham Ordinances of January 1526 cut the cost of the royal household and halved the Privy Chamber from twelve Gentlemen to six, removing rivals such as William Compton. A top-level answer sorts the reforms by how long they lasted and whom they were really for: the subsidy outlived Wolsey, the enclosure campaign was traded away, and Eltham was as much about controlling access to the king as about saving money.","code":"B3-1 — 2 The rise of Wolsey and his policies: Wolsey's reforms"},{"h":"The Amicable Grant of 1525","t":"In February 1525 Charles V destroyed the French army at Pavia and captured Francis I. Henry wanted to invade at once and claim the French crown, but the war of 1522 to 1524 had emptied the treasury and Parliament had already granted a subsidy in 1523 which was still being collected. Wolsey therefore demanded the Amicable Grant, a payment taken without Parliament, set at a share of the value of people's goods and heavier on the clergy. It met refusal across the south and east: in the cloth country of Suffolk and Essex clothiers said they would have to lay off their workers, and thousands gathered at Lavenham rather than pay. The Dukes of Norfolk and Suffolk were sent to deal with them, the demand was abandoned within weeks, Henry claimed he had not known of it and pardoned the resisters, and Wolsey took the blame. A top-level answer treats the Grant as a turning point: it exposed the limits of taxation without consent, it ended the French adventure, and it left Wolsey's reputation with the king and with the country permanently damaged.","code":"B3-1 — 2 The rise of Wolsey and his policies: Reasons for and reactions to the Amicable Grant"},{"h":"What Wolsey's foreign policy was for","t":"Wolsey's foreign policy served Henry's aims rather than any theory of his own. Henry wanted military glory in France, recognition as an equal of the two great rulers of Europe, and a prominent place for England in the affairs of Christendom; Wolsey wanted to give him those things at a price the country could pay, and to keep his own indispensability. Because England was much smaller and poorer than France or the empire of Charles V, the way to matter was to hold the balance between them, joining whichever side made England's friendship worth buying, and to dress that up as peacemaking. Wolsey's legatine authority also gave him a standing in Rome that he hoped might one day carry him to the papacy. A top-level answer states the aim before it judges the outcome, because a policy that looks unprincipled when judged by consistency looks shrewd when judged by whether it bought Henry prestige cheaply.","code":"B3-1 — 3 Wolsey's foreign policy: Aims of Wolsey's foreign policy"},{"h":"Successes and failures, 1513 to 1529","t":"The early years looked golden: the 1513 campaign took Therouanne and Tournai and won the skirmish known as the Battle of the Spurs, while the Earl of Surrey destroyed a Scottish invasion at Flodden, where James IV was killed. The high point came in 1518, when Wolsey turned Pope Leo X's plan for a crusade into the Treaty of London, a non-aggression pact signed in London by about twenty states with Henry at its centre, followed by the sale of Tournai back to France and the betrothal of Princess Mary to the Dauphin. The Field of the Cloth of Gold in June 1520 was magnificent but produced no alliance. From 1521 Wolsey tied England to Charles V, and the campaigns of 1522 and 1523 achieved nothing; after Pavia in 1525 Charles ignored Henry, broke the betrothal to Mary and married a Portuguese princess. England swung back to France in 1525 and 1527, and the war with Charles damaged the cloth trade with the Netherlands. Worst of all, imperial troops sacked Rome in 1527 and left Pope Clement VII in Charles's power. A top-level answer dates the turn and explains it: while England was courted it gained cheaply, and once the two great powers could deal directly it had nothing to sell.","code":"B3-1 — 3 Wolsey's foreign policy: Successes and failures"},{"h":"Catherine of Aragon and the succession","t":"Catherine of Aragon, Arthur's widow, married Henry in June 1509 under a dispensation granted by Pope Julius II. Of six pregnancies only one child survived, Mary, born in 1516; a son born on New Year's Day 1511 lived seven weeks. By the mid-1520s Catherine was past forty and no further children were expected, so the Tudor succession rested on a girl. No woman had ever ruled England in her own right; the nearest attempt, in the twelfth century, had brought civil war, and the memory of the wars between York and Lancaster was fresh. Henry's illegitimate son by Elizabeth Blount, born in 1519, was made Duke of Richmond in 1525, which showed both the problem and Henry's conviction that the fault was not his. A top-level answer treats the succession as the engine of the whole key topic: it is the reason the annulment mattered, and therefore the reason Wolsey's failure was fatal.","code":"B3-1 — 4 Wolsey, Catherine, the succession and annulment: Catherine of Aragon and the succession"},{"h":"The annulment campaign and the opposition to it","t":"Henry's case was that Leviticus forbids a man to marry his brother's wife and promises childlessness to anyone who does, so his marriage had been against God's law from the start and no papal dispensation could have made it lawful. He was not asking for a divorce as we understand it but for a declaration that he had never been married. Catherine answered that her marriage to Arthur had never been consummated, so the text did not apply, and she refused to retire to a nunnery; her nephew was Charles V. Wolsey opened a secret legatine court in 1527 and then worked on Rome, but the Sack of Rome in that year left Clement VII in imperial hands. Clement would not refuse Henry outright and would not grant him anything either: he sent Cardinal Campeggio to England in 1528 with instructions to spin the case out. A top-level answer keeps the obstacle in focus - the problem was never the law or the argument but the political grip Charles V had on the Pope.","code":"B3-1 — 4 Wolsey, Catherine, the succession and annulment: Henry's reasons for and attempts to gain an annulment"},{"h":"Why Wolsey fell","t":"The legatine court of Campeggio and Wolsey opened at Blackfriars in May 1529. Catherine appealed over its head to Rome, denied that it could judge her, and in July Campeggio adjourned it; the case was called back to Rome and Henry had nothing. Wolsey's whole position rested on results, and he had failed at the one thing the king wanted most. His enemies were ready: Anne Boleyn, who blamed him for breaking her earlier match with Henry Percy and for the delay, her father Thomas Boleyn, and her uncle the Duke of Norfolk, who with the Duke of Suffolk had resented Wolsey's dominance for years. The country at large remembered the Amicable Grant and his wealth. In October 1529 he was charged under the statute of praemunire - his legatine power, exercised in England, was turned into the weapon against him - surrendered the Great Seal and lost everything but the see of York; arrested for treason in 1530, he died at Leicester on the way south. A top-level answer separates the trigger from the conditions: the failure at Blackfriars was the trigger, but a minister with no power base of his own was always one failure away from the end.","code":"B3-1 — 4 Wolsey, Catherine, the succession and annulment: Reasons for Wolsey's fall from power"}],"examples":[{"title":"Answering 1(a) and 1(b) without drifting into explanation","setup":"The question is: '1(a) Describe one feature of Wolsey's wealth. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the Court of Requests. [2 marks]'. You know that Wolsey was Archbishop of York from 1514 and drew the revenues of other bishoprics alongside it; that he built and furnished Hampton Court and York Place on a scale no other subject attempted; that he founded Cardinal College at Oxford and a school at Ipswich in the 1520s; that he paid for them by closing about thirty small monasteries; and that he developed the Court of Requests so that poor men could bring cases cheaply, without the costs of the common law courts.","steps":["Treat the parts as two separate two-mark questions on two different subjects, because nothing written under one heading can earn anything under the other.","For part (a) state one feature in a plain sentence: Wolsey drew the income of several church offices at the same time.","Add the single supporting detail that proves it: he held the archbishopric of York from 1514 and the revenues of other sees alongside it, so it was the Church, not royal pay, that made his fortune.","Now begin again for part (b) with one feature of the court itself: the Court of Requests existed to hear the cases of poor men.","Support that in the same way: Wolsey developed it so that a poor litigant could bring a case cheaply, without the costs of the common law courts, which is where his reputation for justice for the poor came from.","Stop at one feature a part - a second feature earns nothing, and neither do reasons, consequences or judgement, because each part pays only for its feature and its supporting detail."],"cues":["Why can the two parts not share an answer?","Can you say one feature in a single sentence?","What single detail proves you know it?","What is the second part actually about?","Which detail shows who could use that court?","What earns nothing at all in either part?"]},{"title":"Turning two bullet points into three reasons on a 12-mark question","setup":"The question is: 'Explain why England went to war with France in 1513. You may use the following in your answer: • the Holy League • the English claim to the French throne. You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks). You know that Henry joined the Holy League, the alliance against France formed by Pope Julius II with Venice and Ferdinand of Aragon, and that the Emperor Maximilian had come into it as well by the time the army sailed; that English kings had claimed the French crown since the fourteenth century and Henry modelled himself on Henry V; that an English army sent to Guyenne in 1512 achieved nothing, mutinied and came home; that Wolsey, then Almoner, organised the supply of the army; and that the campaign took Therouanne in August and Tournai in September 1513.","steps":["Underline the conceptual focus - why war was chosen, not what happened in it - so every paragraph ends on a reason for fighting rather than on a siege.","Turn the first bullet into a reason about obligation: joining the Holy League tied England to the Pope, to Ferdinand of Aragon and, before long, to the Emperor, so Henry was committed to attack France alongside allies who expected him to act and would judge him if he did not.","Turn the second bullet into a reason about right: English kings had claimed the French crown for well over a century, so the war could be presented as recovering an inheritance rather than as an attack, which is also why Henry wanted to be seen fighting in person.","Now find the reason the bullets never mention, because without it the answer cannot reach the top band: the expedition to Guyenne the year before had achieved nothing and its troops had mutinied and sailed home, leaving Henry needing a victory of his own to wipe out the humiliation.","Give each reason its own paragraph with a named detail and a chain of reasoning that ends on the decision to go to war.","Close by linking the three: an alliance that obliged him, a claim that justified him, and a failure that made a second attempt necessary if he was to be taken seriously at all."],"cues":["What exactly must every paragraph explain?","Who else expected England to fight?","How could a king call an invasion lawful?","What is missing that the bullets never mention?","How should one paragraph end?","What holds the three reasons together?"]},{"title":"Choosing a criterion before judging a 16-mark statement","setup":"The question is: 'Catherine of Aragon's refusal to accept the annulment was the main reason the King's Great Matter was still unsettled in 1529.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the court at Blackfriars • Pope Clement VII. You must also use information of your own. (16 marks). You know that Henry argued from Leviticus that marriage to his brother's widow had never been lawful; that Catherine answered that her marriage to Arthur had never been consummated and refused to retire to a nunnery; that Clement VII sent Campeggio in 1528 with instructions to delay rather than decide; that the legatine court opened at Blackfriars in May 1529 and Campeggio adjourned it in July, the case being recalled to Rome; and that imperial troops had sacked Rome in 1527, leaving the Pope in the power of Catherine's nephew Charles V.","steps":["Decide in one sentence what would make a reason the main one - here, which obstacle would have had to disappear for Henry to have his annulment before the year was out - and say so in the opening.","Take the statement on its own ground first: Catherine fought the case instead of retiring quietly, denied the authority of the court at Blackfriars, appealed to Rome and insisted the marriage to Arthur had never been consummated, which cut the ground from under the argument from Leviticus.","Test it against the criterion: her defence only worked because there was a court in Rome willing to hear it, so had the Pope been free her refusal alone could not have stopped the case.","Deal with the second bullet the same way: Clement VII would neither grant nor refuse, and after the sack of Rome he was in the power of Charles V, Catherine's nephew, so delay was the safest course open to him and Campeggio was sent to provide it.","Bring in the material the bullets do not offer - Henry's own insistence on a lawful annulment pronounced by Rome rather than a settlement found in England, which is what kept the case in the one court he could not control.","Reach the judgement your criterion forces: Catherine supplied the defence, but the reason the defence held was that the judge was not free to decide.","Check that the words main reason appear in the conclusion and are answered, not merely repeated."],"cues":["What would main reason have to mean here?","What is the strongest case for the statement?","Why did her refusal work at all?","Why would a pope choose to delay?","What obstacle is on neither bullet?","Which way does your criterion point?","Has the question actually been answered?"]},{"title":"Building a causal chain about why an event was staged","setup":"The question is: 'Explain why the Field of the Cloth of Gold was held in 1520. You may use the following in your answer: • the Treaty of London (1518) • Francis I. You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks). You know that the Treaty of London bound about twenty states to keep the peace and put England at the centre of it; that Francis I had succeeded Louis XII in January 1515 and was close to Henry in age and hungry for the same glory; that Wolsey arranged a meeting near Calais in June 1520 that ran for over a fortnight, with a temporary palace and tents of cloth of gold; that Henry met Charles V immediately before it and again immediately after it; and that the meeting produced no alliance.","steps":["Ask first what the meeting was meant to achieve, because the reason it was held has to be a purpose, not a description of the tents.","Chain the first reason to the end: the treaty had made England the keeper of a general peace, so that peace had to be seen to be working, and a public meeting with the French king was the proof that it was.","Chain the second: Francis was Henry's exact rival, near him in age and after the same reputation, so a meeting let the two compete at jousting and display instead of in the field, where the cost and the risk were real.","Add the reason beyond the bullets: Wolsey's own standing rested on England mattering in Europe, and a meeting he arranged and stage-managed made him, and not only his king, a man the great powers had to deal with.","Point out the reason hidden in the timing - Henry met the emperor just before and just again after, so the show of friendship with France also raised England's price with the other side.","Finish by ranking the reasons rather than listing them, and note that no alliance came out of the meeting, which tells you what it was really for."],"cues":["What was the meeting for?","What had the treaty committed England to?","Why meet a rival rather than fight him?","Who else gained by staging it?","Why do the dates either side matter?","Which reason came first in weight?"]},{"title":"Weighing success against failure in raising money","setup":"The question is: 'Wolsey's methods of raising money did more harm than good in the years 1513-25.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the subsidy • the Amicable Grant. You must also use information of your own. (16 marks). You know that the subsidy, first granted in 1513, was assessed by commissioners on a taxpayer's real wealth in land and goods rather than at the fixed rate of the old fifteenth and tenth; that in 1523 Wolsey asked Parliament for a very large grant, around 800,000 pounds, with Thomas More as Speaker, and was granted far less, spread over several years; that the Amicable Grant of 1525 was demanded by commissioners without Parliament, at a graduated share of the value of goods, a sixth from the wealthiest of the laity and less from those below them, and a third from the clergy; that thousands gathered at Lavenham rather than pay; and that Henry declared he had known nothing of it, pardoned the resisters and cancelled the invasion.","steps":["Set the criterion from what a tax has to do - bring in money the Crown can spend without costing it more in obedience than it gains in cash - and judge both methods by that, not by whether people enjoyed paying.","Build the case against the statement with the subsidy: assessment on real wealth in land and goods raised far more than the fixed fifteenth and tenth, and it went on being used long after Wolsey had gone, so the Crown's income was permanently improved.","Concede the limit of that success: even the subsidy had to be asked for, and in 1523 the Commons cut the demand down and spread what they granted over several years, so consent still set the ceiling.","Build the case for the statement from the Amicable Grant of 1525: a demand made by commissioners with no Parliament at all, at a share of goods nobody had voted, on people who had not finished paying the last grant.","Follow the damage to the end: refusal spread, thousands gathered at Lavenham, the demand was dropped within weeks, the king disowned it and pardoned the resisters, and the invasion it was meant to pay for never sailed.","Add the point the bullets do not give you - the cost fell on the minister rather than on the Crown, because Henry kept the subsidy and lost only Wolsey's reputation with the country.","Judge by separating the two methods rather than lumping them together: one was a lasting gain in what the Crown could raise, the other a self-inflicted wound, so the statement holds for the Grant but not for the period as a whole."],"cues":["What does a tax have to achieve?","Why did the subsidy raise so much more?","Who could still say no, and did they?","What was different about the later demand?","How far did the refusal spread?","Who actually paid for the mistake?","Can the judgement be split by method?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which office did Wolsey receive in December 1515, and which had he gained the year before?","a":"Lord Chancellor in December 1515; Archbishop of York in 1514 (with the see of Lincoln earlier that year)."},{"q":"What was the subsidy and why did it raise more than the fifteenth and tenth?","a":"A tax assessed by commissioners on the real value of a person's land and goods, so it rose with wealth, while the fifteenth and tenth was a fixed sum unrelated to what people actually owned."},{"q":"Why did Pope Clement VII delay Henry's annulment rather than refuse it?","a":"After the Sack of Rome in 1527 he was effectively in the power of Charles V, Catherine's nephew, and could not afford to offend either Charles or Henry, so he sent Campeggio to spin the case out."},{"q":"Name the event of 1525 that made Henry want to invade France and the tax demand that followed it.","a":"The Battle of Pavia, where Charles V captured Francis I; the demand was the Amicable Grant."}]},"room":{"facts":["Booklet B of Paper 2 carries 32 marks on this depth study in three questions: Question 1, printed as 1(a) and 1(b), 'Describe one feature of...' (2 marks each, 4 in all) on two separate aspects of content; Question 2, 'Explain why...' (12 marks); and a choice of Question 3 or Question 4, a 16-mark judgement, 'How far do you agree? Explain your answer.'","Question 1(a) and 1(b) are point-marked: in each part 1 mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about it, 2 marks a part. One feature only in each part; no explanation and no judgement are wanted.","Question 2 and the 16-mark judgement each print two stimulus points under 'You may use the following in your answer:' followed by 'You must also use information of your own.' The bullets are optional; adding material of your own is not.","The stimulus caps are printed in the mark scheme. On the 12-mark Question 2: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'. On the 16-mark judgement, Question 3 or 4, the same two lines with 'Maximum 11 marks'. There is no cap at Level 2 on either.","Level 4 is 10-12 marks on the 12-mark question and 13-16 on the 16-mark question. It requires an analytical explanation directed consistently at the conceptual focus, a line of reasoning that is coherent, sustained and logically structured, and on the 16-mark question criteria for the judgement that are justified and applied.","The AO split is AO2 6 and AO1 6 on the 12-mark question, and AO2 10 and AO1 6 on the 16-mark judgement, whose grid prints three bullets in every level: the AO2 explanation, the AO1 accurate and relevant information, and the overall judgement as the third bullet, which is AO2 as well. An answer showing no qualities of AO2 cannot rise above the top of Level 1 however strong the knowledge.","There are no SPaG marks and no sources or interpretations anywhere on Paper 2, so every mark comes from your own knowledge. Paper 2 lasts 1 hour 50 minutes for 64 marks across the two booklets, about 55 minutes for this one: the marks are not all worth the same time, so budget about 5 minutes on Question 1(a) and 1(b) together, 20 on Question 2 and 27 on the 16-mark judgement, which is 52 of the 55 and leaves about three minutes to check."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of English society and the English economy in 1509. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the opening of Henry VIII's reign in 1509. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4, point-marked in two parts, 2 marks each: 1 mark for the feature and 1 mark for supporting information. 1(a): credit the largely rural population, the hierarchy of nobles, gentry, yeomen and labourers, the woollen cloth trade through Antwerp, or the size of London beside every other town. 1(b): credit the succession of a seventeen-year-old on 21 April 1509, the coronation with Catherine of Aragon that June, or the arrest of Empson and Dudley.","sol":"1(a) One feature was that England was overwhelmingly rural. Of roughly two and a half million people, about nine in ten lived in the countryside and made their living from farming, so the harvest decided how well ordinary families ate and how much they had left to spend. 1(b) One feature was that the new reign opened with a deliberate break from Henry VII's methods. Empson and Dudley, his father's hated financial agents, were arrested in 1509 and executed in 1510, which bought the young king popularity at no cost to himself.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Each part is a separate two-mark question, so one feature only in 1(a) and one in 1(b).","For 1(a) think where most people in 1509 lived and how they earned their bread; for 1(b) think what the new king did in his first months.","Write the feature, then one precise detail, in each part; nothing else earns anything here."],"codes":["B3-1 — 1 Henry VIII, Renaissance Prince: England in 1509"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the young Henry VIII as a Renaissance prince. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of Catherine of Aragon's marriage to Henry VIII. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4, point-marked in two parts, 2 marks each: 1 mark for the feature and 1 mark for supporting detail. 1(a): credit his learning and patronage (languages, music, theology, the 1521 book against Luther and the title Defender of the Faith) or his love of display, jousting and war. 1(b): credit that he married his brother Arthur's widow in June 1509 under a papal dispensation, or that of six pregnancies only Mary, born in 1516, survived.","sol":"1(a) One feature was his learning. Henry read Latin, wrote music, kept scholars and musicians at his court and argued about theology, and in 1521 he published a defence of the seven sacraments against Martin Luther for which the Pope gave him the title Defender of the Faith. 1(b) One feature of the marriage was that it needed the Pope's permission, because Catherine was his brother's widow. Arthur had died in 1502 and the wedding went ahead in June 1509 under a dispensation granted by Pope Julius II, the very grant Henry would later argue had been worthless.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["One feature in each part, with one piece of support: four short sentences in all.","For 1(a) choose either his learning or his love of display; for 1(b) ask who Catherine had been married to before.","Give each part a dated detail - the book of 1521, or the dispensation of 1509."],"codes":["B3-1 — 1 Henry VIII, Renaissance Prince: Henry's character and views on sovereignty","B3-1 — 1 Henry VIII, Renaissance Prince: Strengths, weaknesses and aims as monarch","B3-1 — 4 Wolsey, Catherine, the succession and annulment: Catherine of Aragon and the succession"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of Wolsey's position in government by 1518. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the subsidy. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4, point-marked in two parts, 2 marks each: 1 mark for the feature and 1 mark for supporting detail. 1(a): credit the accumulation of offices in Church and state (Archbishop of York 1514, cardinal 1515, Lord Chancellor December 1515) or the legatine authority granted in 1518. 1(b): credit that the subsidy was assessed by commissioners on a taxpayer's real wealth in land and goods, unlike the fixed fifteenth and tenth, or that it was first granted in 1513 and had to be voted by Parliament.","sol":"1(a) One feature was that Wolsey held the greatest offices of Church and state at the same time. He was Archbishop of York from 1514, a cardinal from 1515 and Lord Chancellor from December 1515, so the king's justice and much of the English Church were in the same pair of hands. 1(b) One feature of the subsidy was that it was assessed on what people actually owned. Commissioners valued a taxpayer's land and goods and charged a rate on that value instead of the fixed sum demanded by the old fifteenth and tenth, so the tax rose with wealth and raised far more.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Two separate subjects are being asked about, so answer each part on its own.","For 1(a) pick the offices that mattered and give the year each was gained; for 1(b) ask how the amount owed was worked out.","Feature then detail, twice - no reasons, and no comparison of the two parts."],"codes":["B3-1 — 2 The rise of Wolsey and his policies: Reasons for Wolsey's rise to power","B3-1 — 2 The rise of Wolsey and his policies: Wolsey's reforms"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the 'Field of the Cloth of Gold' (1520). [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the Battle of Flodden (1513). [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4, point-marked in two parts, 2 marks each: 1 mark for the feature and 1 mark for supporting detail. 1(a): credit that it was a meeting of Henry VIII and Francis I near Calais organised by Wolsey, or the scale of the display - temporary palace, tents of cloth of gold, feasting and jousting over a fortnight. 1(b): credit that it destroyed a Scottish invasion mounted while Henry was campaigning in France, that the Earl of Surrey commanded, or that James IV was killed.","sol":"1(a) One feature was that it was a face-to-face meeting between two kings. Henry VIII and Francis I met near Calais in June 1520 in an event organised by Wolsey, each bringing a great train of nobles and their servants, and the two courts stayed together for over a fortnight. 1(b) One feature of Flodden was that it destroyed a Scottish invasion launched while the king was out of the country. The Earl of Surrey met the Scots on 9 September 1513 while Henry was campaigning in France, and James IV was killed in the battle.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Each part wants a single feature, so pick the clearest one and support it.","For 1(a) ask who met whom; for 1(b) ask who was fighting whom, and where the king was at the time.","One named or dated detail in each part is enough - no reasons and no consequences."],"codes":["B3-1 — 3 Wolsey's foreign policy: Successes and failures"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the Amicable Grant (1525). [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the Eltham Ordinances (1526). [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4, point-marked in two parts, 2 marks each: 1 mark for the feature and 1 mark for supporting detail. 1(a): credit that it was a demand for money made without Parliament, at a share of the value of goods and heavier on the clergy, or the resistance it met in Suffolk and Essex, ending in its abandonment. 1(b): credit that they cut the size, cost and discipline of the royal household, or that they halved the Privy Chamber from twelve Gentlemen to six and removed William Compton.","sol":"1(a) One feature was that it was a tax demanded without Parliament. In 1525 Wolsey asked for a payment towards an invasion of France, set as a share of the value of people's goods and demanded by royal commissioners rather than granted by Parliament, with the clergy asked for more than the laity. 1(b) One feature of the Eltham Ordinances was that they cut down the Privy Chamber. Issued in January 1526, they halved its Gentlemen from twelve to six and removed William Compton, the Groom of the Stool, whose daily access to the king had made him rich and influential.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Do not run the two parts together: one feature and one supporting detail in each.","For 1(a) ask how the money was asked for; for 1(b) ask who lost their place at court.","Name the year in each part. No reasons are wanted here."],"codes":["B3-1 — 2 The rise of Wolsey and his policies: Reasons for and reactions to the Amicable Grant","B3-1 — 2 The rise of Wolsey and his policies: Wolsey's reforms"]},{"q":"Explain why Wolsey rose to power in the years 1509-15. You may use the following in your answer: • the French campaign of 1513 • Henry VIII's character. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit the organisation of the 1513 campaign, Henry's impatience with routine business, and beyond the bullets Wolsey's own capacity for work, his Church promotions and his lack of a noble family. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Wolsey rose because a particular kind of king met a particular kind of servant at the right moment. The first reason was his work on the French campaign of 1513. As Almoner he took charge of supplying an army sent across the Channel, arranging ships, food, carts and wages on a scale nobody else would attempt, and the expedition took Therouanne and Tournai without collapsing into the shambles of the Guyenne expedition of the previous year. Henry saw that the dull, exhausting work had been done, and done well, and rewarded the man who had done it. The second reason was Henry's own character. He was seventeen when he came to the throne on 21 April 1509, having been born on 28 June 1491, hungry for glory in war, keen on hunting, jousting and music, and thoroughly bored by the daily business of government that his father had done in person. A king who wants results but not paperwork needs somebody to hand it to, and Wolsey was willing to take all of it. The third reason, which the bullets do not offer, is that the Church provided both the ladder and the pay. Henry could reward Wolsey with bishoprics that cost the Crown nothing: Lincoln and then York in 1514, a cardinal's hat in 1515 and the chancellorship in December 1515. The older councillors inherited from Henry VII, Fox and Warham, stepped aside as the new man took the load. It mattered too that Wolsey was an Ipswich man of no family, so promoting him offended no great house and he owed everything to the king alone. Ability opened the door, but it was Henry's decision to delegate that made a minister of him, which is why the same relationship could destroy him later.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the new king wanted done and what he did not want to do himself.","Plan three reasons of different kinds: what Wolsey did, what Henry was like, and what made him easy to promote.","Each paragraph should end on the word power, explaining how that reason put him closer to it."],"codes":["B3-1 — 2 The rise of Wolsey and his policies: Reasons for Wolsey's rise to power","B3-1 — 1 Henry VIII, Renaissance Prince: Henry's character and views on sovereignty"]},{"q":"Explain why Wolsey issued the Eltham Ordinances (1526). You may use the following in your answer: • the Privy Chamber • the cost of the royal household. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit the cost of the household after the wars, the removal of Privy Chamber rivals such as Compton, and beyond the bullets Wolsey's weakened standing after the Amicable Grant. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The Ordinances issued at Eltham in January 1526 were presented as housekeeping, but they were issued for three reasons, only one of which was money. The first was the cost of the royal household. The treasury Henry VII left had already gone on the French campaigns of 1512 to 1514; the war of 1522 to 1524 was paid for by forced loans that were never repaid and by the subsidy of 1523, and after the collapse of the Amicable Grant in 1525 there was no chance of new taxation for years. If the Crown could not raise more, it had to spend less, and the household, with its crowds of servants and hangers-on eating at the king's expense, was the obvious target; Wolsey cut numbers, tightened discipline in the kitchens and stopped unauthorised feeding at court. The second reason was the Privy Chamber. Its Gentlemen attended Henry in private every day, and they were the one group who could turn the king against a minister who was usually at Westminster or York Place rather than at court. The Ordinances halved the Privy Chamber from twelve Gentlemen to six and removed William Compton, the Groom of the Stool, who had grown rich and influential through that daily access. The third reason, beyond the bullets, is Wolsey's own position in 1526. The Amicable Grant had been abandoned, Henry had publicly distanced himself from it, and the cardinal needed to show the king that he was still the man who could put things right. A reform that saved money and removed his rivals did both jobs at once. That is why the financial savings turned out to be modest and short-lived while the changes to the Privy Chamber were pressed hardest: the Ordinances were about control of the king as much as about the cost of feeding his household.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask who saw the king every day, and what that was worth to a minister who did not.","Sort your reasons into the official one, the political one and the personal one, and give each a paragraph.","End each paragraph on why that made the Ordinances worth issuing when they were."],"codes":["B3-1 — 2 The rise of Wolsey and his policies: Wolsey's reforms","B3-1 — 1 Henry VIII, Renaissance Prince: Henry's character and views on sovereignty"]},{"q":"Explain why there was opposition to the Amicable Grant (1525). You may use the following in your answer: • the subsidy of 1523 • the cloth industry. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit the weight of recent taxation and distress in the cloth districts, and beyond the bullets the absence of parliamentary consent. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Opposition to the Amicable Grant was so widespread and so quick because the demand was heavy, badly timed and, many believed, unlawful. The first reason was how much had already been paid. Parliament had granted a subsidy in 1523, assessed on people's real wealth, and it was still being collected in 1525; there had also been a forced loan a few years before that had not been repaid. A second demand landing on households still paying the first felt less like a request than a seizure, and it was set high, at a share of the value of goods, with the clergy asked for more still. The second reason was the state of the cloth industry. In Suffolk and Essex, clothiers said plainly that if they paid they would have to stop putting out work, and spinners, weavers and fullers who depended on them would be idle. That is why the resistance there was not a protest by the rich alone: thousands gathered at Lavenham, and the Dukes of Norfolk and Suffolk had to be sent to face them. The third reason, which the bullets do not give, is that the Grant had never been granted by anybody. It was demanded by royal commissioners without Parliament, and men argued in Kent and elsewhere that the king could not take their goods by demand alone. That turned refusal from a complaint about hardship into a defence of a legal right, which made it respectable and let gentlemen and commissioners quietly join it. The three worked together: an unlawful demand, at a moment of real distress, on people already taxed. Henry cancelled the Grant, pardoned the resisters, said he had known nothing of it, and left Wolsey to carry the blame.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Separate what people were asked to pay from the way they were asked for it.","Three reasons of different kinds work best here: the amount, the local economy and the law.","Every paragraph should finish by saying why that made people refuse, not merely grumble."],"codes":["B3-1 — 2 The rise of Wolsey and his policies: Reasons for and reactions to the Amicable Grant","B3-1 — 2 The rise of Wolsey and his policies: Wolsey's reforms"]},{"q":"Explain why the Treaty of London (1518) was a success for Wolsey. You may use the following in your answer: • the Pope's plan for a crusade • Tournai. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit the seizure of the papal peace plan, the return of Tournai for payment, and beyond the bullets the prestige England gained relative to its size and the marriage treaty with France. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The Treaty of London was a success because it gave Henry exactly what he wanted, standing among the great rulers of Europe, and cost almost nothing to get. The first reason was Wolsey's use of the Pope's plan. Leo X wanted a general truce so that Christendom could act against the Ottoman advance, and the obvious place to arrange it was Rome. Wolsey took the plan over, had the negotiations brought to London, and turned a papal truce into a treaty of universal peace with England as its author. Instead of signing somebody else's agreement, Henry was hosting it. The second reason was Tournai. The town had been captured in 1513 and was expensive to garrison and impossible to defend for long; as part of the settlement it went back to France in return for payments spread over years. A costly liability became cash and a gesture of friendship, which is the kind of exchange that looks like generosity and works like accounting. The third reason, beyond the bullets, is what the treaty did to England's standing. Around twenty states joined an agreement in which each promised not to attack the others and to act together against any that did, and every ambassador who signed it did so in London. England was smaller and poorer than France or the lands of the young Charles V, yet for a moment it sat in the middle of Europe's affairs, and the betrothal of Princess Mary to the French Dauphin bound the old enemy into the arrangement. Success here has to be measured against Henry's aims rather than against permanence: the peace did not last, but the prestige was real, it was cheap, and it made Wolsey indispensable.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the king wanted from Europe that did not require an army.","Judge the treaty against Henry's aims, and give a paragraph each to the diplomacy, the bargain and the prestige.","Close each paragraph by naming the gain, not the event."],"codes":["B3-1 — 3 Wolsey's foreign policy: Aims of Wolsey's foreign policy","B3-1 — 3 Wolsey's foreign policy: Successes and failures"]},{"q":"Explain why Henry VIII wanted his marriage to Catherine of Aragon annulled. You may use the following in your answer: • Princess Mary • Anne Boleyn. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit the lack of a legitimate son and the dangers of a female succession, Anne Boleyn's refusal to be a mistress, and beyond the bullets Henry's reading of Leviticus. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Henry wanted an annulment because his dynasty, his desires and his conscience all pointed the same way by the late 1520s. The first reason was the succession. Catherine had been pregnant six times and only one child had survived, Mary, born in 1516; a son born on New Year's Day 1511 had lived seven weeks. No woman had ruled England in her own right, the one previous attempt had ended in civil war, and the Tudors had held the throne only since 1485, so Henry believed a daughter would mean a disputed succession and perhaps a foreign husband ruling England. By the mid-1520s Catherine was past forty and no more children were expected. The second reason was Anne Boleyn. Henry had taken mistresses before, and his son by Elizabeth Blount was made Duke of Richmond in 1525, but Anne refused to become one; she would have the king as a husband or not at all. A young woman from a fertile family offered the sons Catherine could not, and Henry's letters show how completely he had committed himself. The third reason, which the bullets do not name, is Henry's own case in law and conscience. Leviticus says that a man who takes his brother's wife will be childless, and Catherine had been married to Arthur. Henry convinced himself that his marriage had never been lawful, that Julius II had had no power to dispense from God's law, and that his lack of sons was the punishment promised in scripture. That argument mattered because it changed what he was asking for: not a divorce but a declaration that he had never been married at all, which a pope could grant without admitting error of judgement, only of law. Conscience and dynastic need reinforced each other, and Anne made the matter urgent.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Three different kinds of pressure were working on Henry at once; do not let one of them stand for all three.","Take the dynasty, the woman and the argument from scripture in turn, and support each with a name or a date.","Each paragraph should finish by saying why that made ending the marriage necessary rather than merely attractive."],"codes":["B3-1 — 4 Wolsey, Catherine, the succession and annulment: Catherine of Aragon and the succession","B3-1 — 4 Wolsey, Catherine, the succession and annulment: Henry's reasons for and attempts to gain an annulment"]},{"q":"Explain why Wolsey fell from power in 1529. You may use the following in your answer: • the court at Blackfriars • the Boleyns. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit the failure of the legatine court, the hostility of Anne Boleyn and Norfolk, and beyond the bullets Wolsey's lack of a power base and the praemunire charge. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Wolsey fell because the one thing that kept him in power, Henry's belief that he could deliver what the king wanted, was destroyed in the summer of 1529. The first reason was the failure at Blackfriars. The legatine court he shared with Cardinal Campeggio opened in May 1529 to hear the king's case; Catherine appealed over its head to Rome and refused to accept its authority, and in July Campeggio adjourned it, after which the case was called back to Rome. Wolsey had promised for two years that his standing in Rome would settle the matter, and now it had settled nothing. The second reason was the Boleyns. Anne blamed him both for the delay and for breaking off her earlier match with Henry Percy; her father Thomas Boleyn and her uncle the Duke of Norfolk had resented Wolsey's dominance for years and now had daily access to a frustrated king. Where once nobody could reach Henry without Wolsey, now his enemies stood between the two. The third reason, which the bullets do not supply, is that Wolsey had built nothing to fall back on. He had no great family, he had prosecuted noblemen in Star Chamber, his wealth and his legatine authority were resented by the clergy, and the country remembered the Amicable Grant of 1525. When the king turned, nobody spoke for him, and the charge chosen was praemunire, so that the very legatine power that had raised him became the offence. He surrendered the Great Seal in October 1529 and kept only the see of York. The failure over the annulment was the trigger, but a minister who depends on one man's favour and has made enemies of everyone else is always one failure from the end.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what Wolsey was actually for, in the king's eyes, by 1529.","Separate the trigger from the conditions that made the trigger fatal, then give each a paragraph.","Finish by ranking the reasons rather than listing them."],"codes":["B3-1 — 4 Wolsey, Catherine, the succession and annulment: Reasons for Wolsey's fall from power","B3-1 — 4 Wolsey, Catherine, the succession and annulment: Henry's reasons for and attempts to gain an annulment"]},{"q":"'Wolsey, not Henry VIII, governed England in the years 1515-29.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the Court of Star Chamber • the Amicable Grant. You must also use information of your own. (16 marks)","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit Wolsey's offices and his control of justice, finance and diplomacy weighed against Henry's decisions on war, taxation and the annulment and, beyond the bullets, the speed of the fall in 1529, with a criterion-based judgement. Caps: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Whether a minister governs depends on who took the decisions that mattered and who could undo them. Judged on that criterion Wolsey ran the government of England but did not govern it, so I agree with the statement only about the daily work of the state. The case for the statement is the sheer reach of what Wolsey held. He was Lord Chancellor from December 1515, a cardinal from 1515 and papal legate in England from 1518, so the law, the Church and diplomacy all ran through one man. In the Court of Star Chamber he sat in person and heard cases quickly and cheaply, and its business rose from about a dozen cases a year under Henry VII to over a hundred; he developed the Court of Requests so that poor men could sue without the costs of the common law; and he was willing to bring noblemen before the court as well as commoners. He pressed the subsidy, assessed by commissioners on real wealth in land and goods instead of the fixed fifteenth and tenth. He ordered the enclosure inquiry of 1517 into land enclosed since the late 1480s. He wrote the Eltham Ordinances of January 1526 and halved the Gentlemen of the Privy Chamber from twelve to six. Nobody else in England did any of this, and foreign ambassadors dealt with him rather than with the king. But the decisions that set the direction were Henry's. The war of 1513 was fought because the king wanted glory in France in the style of Henry V, not because his Almoner proposed it. The Amicable Grant shows both sides of the relationship at once. Wolsey made the demand in 1525, without Parliament, at a graduated share of the value of goods, a sixth from the wealthiest of the laity and less from those below them, and a third from the clergy, because Henry wanted an army in France after Pavia; and when thousands gathered at Lavenham and refusals spread through Suffolk and Essex, it was Henry who declared that he had known nothing of it, pardoned the resisters and left his minister to carry the blame. A servant who can be disowned in public is not the government. The point the bullets do not offer is the ending, and it is decisive. In 1529 Wolsey was as able, and as fully armed with offices, as he had been in 1515, but the legatine court at Blackfriars produced no annulment; in October he was charged under praemunire, surrendered the Great Seal and was left with the see of York alone. The authority that had made him great was in the king's gift, and the king took it back in weeks without a rebellion, a battle or a political crisis. That is the clearest test there is of who governed. The judgement therefore turns on what the word means. If governing means doing the work of government - justice, taxation, administration, negotiation - then Wolsey governed England for fourteen years and nobody came near him. If it means deciding what the Crown would do, and being able to undo it and the man who did it, then Henry governed throughout and Wolsey was the instrument. Since the second power contains the first, I disagree with the statement as it stands: Wolsey ruled England in Henry's name, and only for as long as Henry found it useful.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide what the word governed would have to mean before you agree or disagree with anything.","Separate the daily work of government from the power to decide and to undo.","The bullets give you the courts and the Grant; the top band needs a third strand they do not mention."],"codes":["B3-1 — 2 The rise of Wolsey and his policies: Wolsey's reforms","B3-1 — 2 The rise of Wolsey and his policies: Reasons for and reactions to the Amicable Grant","B3-1 — 1 Henry VIII, Renaissance Prince: Henry's character and views on sovereignty"]},{"q":"'The main reason for Wolsey's rise to power was his own ability.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the French campaign of 1513 • Henry VIII's character. You must also use information of your own. (16 marks)","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit Wolsey's ability weighed against Henry's willingness to delegate and, beyond the bullets, the Church promotions and his low birth, with a judgement on which reason made the others possible. Caps: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Wolsey's ability is the reason he was noticed, but the reason he rose so far and so fast is that Henry VIII wanted a minister who would take the whole burden of government off his hands. I agree with the statement only in part, and the criterion I use is which reason the others depended on. The case for ability is strong. In 1513 Wolsey, then Almoner, organised the supply of an army across the Channel: ships, food, carts, wages and the movement of thousands of men, work that had defeated the commanders of the Guyenne expedition of the year before, whose troops mutinied and came home having achieved nothing. Henry's campaign took Therouanne and Tournai and won the skirmish known as the Battle of the Spurs, and the king came back knowing who had made it possible. Wolsey's capacity for work was extraordinary and continued to be: as Lord Chancellor he sat in Star Chamber himself, heard cases in person, and ran justice, finance and diplomacy at the same time. Without that competence no amount of royal favour would have kept him in office. But ability of that kind was useless without a king who wished to delegate, and here Henry's character is decisive. He was seventeen at his accession on 21 April 1509, born on 28 June 1491, ambitious for glory in France in the style of Henry V, devoted to hunting, jousting and music, and thoroughly bored by the daily routine his father had performed in person. He wanted the results of government without the labour of it. Faced with the same man, Henry VII, who read his own accounts and signed his own warrants, would have used Wolsey as a clerk; Henry VIII made him the government. A third reason lies beyond both bullets: the Church paid for the rise. Henry could reward Wolsey at no cost to the Crown, with Lincoln and then York in 1514, a cardinal's hat in 1515 and the chancellorship in December 1515, and papal favour later added the legateship. At the same time Wolsey's obscure Ipswich origins made him safe to promote, since no great family was offended and the man himself owed everything to the king and could be dropped at will. The old councillors of the previous reign, Fox and Warham, stepped back rather than fight him. Taken together, the reasons are not equal. Ability was necessary but not sufficient; the Church supplied the means; Henry's character supplied the opportunity, and it is the reason that explains why the others could operate at all. The proof is in the ending: in 1529 Wolsey was no less able than in 1515, but the king's need had changed, and he fell in weeks. So his own ability was the reason he was chosen, while the king's character was the reason there was so much to be chosen for.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask whether the same man would have risen so far under a different king.","Weigh each reason by asking which one the others depended on.","The bullets give you two reasons; a top answer finds at least one more and then ranks them."],"codes":["B3-1 — 2 The rise of Wolsey and his policies: Reasons for Wolsey's rise to power","B3-1 — 1 Henry VIII, Renaissance Prince: Henry's character and views on sovereignty","B3-1 — 1 Henry VIII, Renaissance Prince: Strengths, weaknesses and aims as monarch"]},{"q":"'Wolsey's foreign policy in the years 1515-29 was a failure.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the Treaty of London (1518) • the Battle of Pavia (1525). You must also use information of your own. (16 marks)","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit the successes of 1518 to 1520 weighed against the failures of 1522 to 1529 and, beyond the bullets, the Sack of Rome and the damage to the annulment, judged against Henry's aims. Caps: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Foreign policy has to be judged against what the king wanted from it, and Henry wanted three things: glory, recognition as an equal of Francis I and Charles V, and, from the mid-1520s, a son. Measured that way Wolsey's diplomacy was a considerable success until 1521 and a comprehensive failure afterwards, so I largely agree with the statement while dating the change. The strongest case against the statement is the Treaty of London in 1518. Leo X wanted a general truce so that Christendom could face the Ottoman advance; Wolsey took the plan over, brought the negotiations to London and turned it into a treaty of universal peace, signed by around twenty states, each promising not to attack the others and to act together against any aggressor. A kingdom far smaller and poorer than France or the possessions of Charles V sat at the centre of Europe for a season. The settlement also returned Tournai, a garrison town England could not afford, to France for payments spread over years, and betrothed Princess Mary to the French Dauphin. Two years later the Field of the Cloth of Gold near Calais displayed Henry as the equal of Francis before the whole of Europe. None of this cost a campaign. But display is not the same as power, and the weakness showed as soon as the great rulers wanted war rather than an audience. From 1521 Wolsey committed England to Charles V, and the invasions of 1522 and 1523 achieved nothing: the Duke of Suffolk's army advanced into northern France and then withdrew in bad weather without a battle, having spent the money Parliament had granted. Then came Pavia in February 1525, where Charles destroyed the French army and captured Francis I himself. Henry wanted to invade at once and claim the French crown, but Charles had won without English help, needed nothing further, refused the invasion, broke his betrothal to Princess Mary and married a Portuguese princess instead. The Amicable Grant demanded to pay for the invasion collapsed in refusal, so Henry had neither the army nor the alliance nor the glory. England swung back to France in 1525 and 1527, and the resulting hostility to Charles damaged the cloth trade with the Netherlands on which so many English livelihoods depended. The worst consequence is not on either bullet. In May 1527 imperial troops sacked Rome and left Pope Clement VII effectively in the power of Charles V, who was Catherine of Aragon's nephew. From that moment the annulment Henry needed was in the gift of a pope controlled by the one ruler certain to refuse it, and Wolsey's years of manoeuvring between the powers had left England with no means of changing that. Judged by Henry's aims, then, the verdict divides at 1521: while England's friendship was worth buying, Wolsey bought prestige cheaply and brilliantly; once the two great powers could settle matters between themselves, the policy delivered nothing, and its final failure touched the succession itself, which is the failure that mattered most.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Success or failure against whose aims? Settle that first.","Look for the point at which the same policy stopped working, and build the essay around it.","The bullets stay in the 1510s and 1520s diplomacy; the top band needs the consequence that reached the king's marriage."],"codes":["B3-1 — 3 Wolsey's foreign policy: Successes and failures","B3-1 — 3 Wolsey's foreign policy: Aims of Wolsey's foreign policy","B3-1 — 1 Henry VIII, Renaissance Prince: Strengths, weaknesses and aims as monarch"]},{"q":"'The failure of the divorce proceedings in London was the main reason for Wolsey's fall from power in 1529.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • Cardinal Campeggio • the Boleyns. You must also use information of your own. (16 marks)","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit the Blackfriars failure weighed against the Boleyn faction and, beyond the bullets, Wolsey's lack of a power base and the praemunire charge, with a judgement on trigger against underlying cause. Caps: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The failure in London was certainly what finished Wolsey in 1529, but a reason can be the trigger without being the main one, and the criterion I use is which cause explains why that failure was fatal rather than survivable. The case for the statement is clear. The legatine court that Wolsey shared with Cardinal Campeggio opened at Blackfriars in May 1529 to try the king's case in England. Catherine of Aragon appeared, denied that the court could judge her, appealed over its head to Rome and withdrew. Campeggio, who had come from Rome in 1528 under instructions to delay rather than decide, adjourned the court in July, and the case was recalled to Rome. Wolsey had argued for two years that his standing as legate made him the man to deliver what no one else could, and the whole scheme collapsed in public. Since his usefulness was the only thing holding him up, its disappearance was bound to be dangerous. The Boleyns turned danger into destruction. Anne blamed Wolsey twice over, for the years of delay and for breaking her earlier match with Henry Percy; her father Thomas Boleyn was rising fast, and her uncle the Duke of Norfolk, with the Duke of Suffolk, had resented Wolsey's monopoly of the king for a decade. Where once no one reached Henry except through the cardinal, by 1529 the people beside the king every day were the people who wanted him gone, and they had an alternative to offer: pursue the annulment in England instead of in Rome. Two further points, which the bullets do not give, decide the judgement. First, Wolsey had no base of his own. He was a butcher's son from Ipswich with no great family behind him, he had prosecuted noblemen in Star Chamber, the clergy resented his legatine authority and his wealth, and the country still remembered the Amicable Grant of 1525, which Henry had disowned and Wolsey had carried. When the king turned, there was nobody with an interest in defending him, and the charge chosen, praemunire, turned the legatine power that had made him great into the crime that unmade him; he surrendered the Great Seal in October 1529 and kept only the see of York. Second, the failure at Blackfriars was not really Wolsey's doing. Imperial troops had sacked Rome in 1527 and left Clement VII in the power of Charles V, Catherine's nephew, so no legate in England could have delivered a decision. What destroyed Wolsey was not incompetence but a king who judged servants purely by results. So I agree that the failure of the proceedings was the immediate reason, and it is the one without which he would have kept office in 1529. But it was fatal only because his position rested on a single man's favour, because his enemies were already in place with a plan of their own, and because he had spent twenty years making sure that no one else in England had a reason to protect him.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Distinguish the reason he fell in that particular year from the reason he could fall at all.","Ask what would have happened if the court had succeeded, and what if he had had allies.","A top answer says who or what was really responsible for the failure in London before judging him by it."],"codes":["B3-1 — 4 Wolsey, Catherine, the succession and annulment: Reasons for Wolsey's fall from power","B3-1 — 4 Wolsey, Catherine, the succession and annulment: Henry's reasons for and attempts to gain an annulment","B3-1 — 2 The rise of Wolsey and his policies: Reasons for and reactions to the Amicable Grant"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In which month and year did Wolsey become Lord Chancellor?","a":"December 1515."},{"q":"In which Suffolk cloth town did thousands gather in 1525 rather than pay the Amicable Grant?","a":"Lavenham."},{"q":"By how much did the Eltham Ordinances reduce the number of Gentlemen of the Privy Chamber?","a":"From twelve to six."},{"q":"Who adjourned the legatine court at Blackfriars in July 1529, sending the case back to Rome?","a":"Cardinal Campeggio."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The accession of Henry VIII","back":"Henry VII died on 21 April 1509 and his seventeen-year-old son succeeded, crowned that June. He married Catherine of Aragon, his brother Arthur's widow, in June 1509 and had Empson and Dudley executed in 1510.","code":"B3-1 — 1 Henry VIII, Renaissance Prince: England in 1509","open":false},{"front":"How England was governed in 1509","back":"The king ruled in person with a Royal Council of nobles, churchmen and lawyers. Parliament met only when summoned, mainly to grant taxation; Justices of the Peace ran the counties; the Church answered to Rome.","code":"B3-1 — 1 Henry VIII, Renaissance Prince: England in 1509","open":false},{"front":"Defender of the Faith","back":"Title granted to Henry VIII by Pope Leo X in 1521 after Henry published a defence of the seven sacraments against Martin Luther. It marked him as scholar and loyal son of the Church.","code":"B3-1 — 1 Henry VIII, Renaissance Prince: Henry's character and views on sovereignty","open":false},{"front":"Henry VIII's aims as king","back":"Glory in war against France in the style of Henry V; recognition as an equal of Francis I and Charles V; and a legitimate son to secure a dynasty that had held the throne only since 1485.","code":"B3-1 — 1 Henry VIII, Renaissance Prince: Strengths, weaknesses and aims as monarch","open":false},{"front":"Wolsey's climb, 1509 to 1518","back":"Royal Almoner 1509; supplied the French campaign of 1513; Bishop of Lincoln and Archbishop of York 1514; cardinal 1515; Lord Chancellor December 1515; papal legate in England from 1518.","code":"B3-1 — 2 The rise of Wolsey and his policies: Reasons for Wolsey's rise to power","open":false},{"front":"The subsidy","back":"Wolsey's tax, first granted in 1513, assessed by commissioners on what a person's land and goods were really worth, unlike the fixed fifteenth and tenth. It raised far more and outlasted him.","code":"B3-1 — 2 The rise of Wolsey and his policies: Wolsey's reforms","open":false},{"front":"Star Chamber and the Court of Requests under Wolsey","back":"Wolsey used Star Chamber for quick, cheap justice, hearing many times more cases than before, and developed the Court of Requests so poor men could bring cases cheaply. Prosecuting noblemen made him enemies.","code":"B3-1 — 2 The rise of Wolsey and his policies: Wolsey's reforms","open":false},{"front":"The Eltham Ordinances, January 1526","back":"Household rules cutting costs and tightening discipline, and halving the Privy Chamber from twelve Gentlemen to six, removing rivals such as William Compton. Much of it was quietly undone within a few years.","code":"B3-1 — 2 The rise of Wolsey and his policies: Wolsey's reforms","open":false},{"front":"The Amicable Grant, 1525","back":"A payment demanded without Parliament, after Pavia, to fund invading France. Refusals across Suffolk and Essex, thousands gathering at Lavenham; Henry cancelled it, pardoned the resisters and let Wolsey take the blame.","code":"B3-1 — 2 The rise of Wolsey and his policies: Reasons for and reactions to the Amicable Grant","open":false},{"front":"The Treaty of London, 1518","back":"Wolsey turned Leo X's crusade plan into a non-aggression treaty signed in London by about twenty states. Tournai was returned to France for payment and Princess Mary betrothed to the Dauphin.","code":"B3-1 — 3 Wolsey's foreign policy: Aims of Wolsey's foreign policy","open":false},{"front":"The 'Field of the Cloth of Gold', June 1520","back":"Henry VIII met Francis I near Calais for over a fortnight of jousting and feasting, with a temporary palace and tents of cloth of gold. It produced magnificent display but no alliance.","code":"B3-1 — 3 Wolsey's foreign policy: Successes and failures","open":false},{"front":"The Battle of Pavia, February 1525","back":"Charles V's army destroyed the French and captured Francis I. Henry wanted to invade France at once, but Charles ignored him, broke his betrothal to Princess Mary and left England isolated.","code":"B3-1 — 3 Wolsey's foreign policy: Successes and failures","open":false},{"front":"Catherine of Aragon and the succession","back":"Six pregnancies brought one surviving child, Mary, born in 1516; a son born on New Year's Day 1511 lived seven weeks. By the mid-1520s Catherine was past forty and no son was coming.","code":"B3-1 — 4 Wolsey, Catherine, the succession and annulment: Catherine of Aragon and the succession","open":false},{"front":"Henry's case for an annulment","back":"Leviticus warns that a man who takes his brother's wife will be childless. Henry argued the marriage broke God's law and Julius II's dispensation was worthless; Catherine insisted her marriage to Arthur was unconsummated.","code":"B3-1 — 4 Wolsey, Catherine, the succession and annulment: Henry's reasons for and attempts to gain an annulment","open":false},{"front":"Name one reason the Boleyns worked against Wolsey","back":"Anne blamed him for breaking her earlier match with Henry Percy; he had failed to deliver the annulment after years of promises; her uncle the Duke of Norfolk resented his long monopoly of the king's government.","code":"B3-1 — 4 Wolsey, Catherine, the succession and annulment: Reasons for Wolsey's fall from power","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (the 16-mark judgement, 'Henry VIII's accession in 1509 changed little in the way England was governed.')","items":["Question: 'Henry VIII's accession in 1509 changed little in the way England was governed.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the Royal Council • Justices of the Peace. You must also use information of your own. (16 marks). This is one paragraph from the middle of the essay, putting the case for the statement.","The men and the machinery both survived the change of king. Henry VIII kept his father's Royal Council rather than replacing it: William Warham stayed on as Archbishop of Canterbury and held the chancellorship until he gave it up in December 1515, and Richard Fox, Bishop of Winchester, kept the Privy Seal until he withdrew from government in 1516, so for the first six or seven years of the reign the king's business was done by the churchmen and lawyers who had done Henry VII's. Below the Council nothing moved at all. The counties were still governed by Justices of the Peace, unpaid local gentlemen who heard cases and enforced the law in quarter sessions because the Crown had no paid officials in the shires and no money to create any. Parliament was still called when the king wanted something and dismissed once he had it, and the first Parliament of the reign, which met in January 1510, changed nothing about how the country was run. Judged by the criterion of whether the institutions through which England was governed were replaced, 1509 changed the man at the top and left the structure standing, and that is precisely why what followed took the form it did: a king who inherited his father's machinery but not his father's appetite for working it needed somebody else to work it for him.","Why this is Level 4: the explanation is analytical and directed consistently at the conceptual focus, which is how far the way England was governed changed, not a description of Tudor institutions. The knowledge is precisely selected - Warham keeping the chancellorship until December 1515, Fox keeping the Privy Seal until 1516, the unpaid Justices of the Peace in the counties, the Parliament of January 1510 - and each fact is used to drive the argument rather than to decorate it. The paragraph applies a criterion and reaches an interim judgement in its last sentence ('changed the man at the top and left the structure standing'), which is what the third bullet of the level descriptor rewards."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: as above. The same point attempted by a weaker candidate.","When Henry VIII became king in 1509 a lot of things stayed the same. He still had a Royal Council to advise him like his father did, and there were still Justices of the Peace running things in the counties for no pay. Parliament was only called when the king needed money from it. Some of his father's old men carried on working for him as well for a while. This shows that government did not really change very much at the start of the reign because it was mostly the same people and mostly the same system.","What it does and does not do: it meets Level 2 because an explanation is offered with accurate and relevant information showing some knowledge of the period, but the links to the conceptual focus are implicit and unsustained - 'a lot of things stayed the same' and 'mostly the same system' are assertions, not analysis, and no test of what would count as real change is ever stated. It misses Level 3 and Level 4 because the reasoning is not sustained, the detail is imprecise (no minister is named, no date is given for when any of them left office, and nothing is said about what the Council or the Justices of the Peace actually did), and the judgement in the final sentence is asserted rather than justified. No stimulus cap is holding the answer down: there is no cap at Level 2 at all, so even an answer that stayed entirely inside the two printed bullets, the Royal Council and the Justices of the Peace, could reach the top of Level 2. The caps bite higher up, and this answer would clear them anyway: the grid prints 'Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content', and this candidate does range over the Council, the Justices of the Peace, Parliament and the continuity of Henry VII's servants. What keeps it at Level 2 is the quality of the reasoning, not the range of the content."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: England in 1509 and Henry VIII, Renaissance prince","items":["Henry VII died on 21 April 1509; Henry VIII, born 28 June 1491, succeeded at seventeen and was crowned with Catherine of Aragon in June 1509.","Catherine of Aragon was the widow of Henry's elder brother Arthur, who died in 1502; the marriage went ahead under a dispensation from Pope Julius II.","Empson and Dudley, his father's hated financial agents, were arrested in 1509 and executed in 1510 - a popular opening to the reign.","England held roughly two and a half million people, about nine in ten of them living in the countryside; London was far larger than any other town.","Woollen cloth was the great export, sold chiefly through Antwerp, so anything that disturbed trade with the Netherlands hurt English livelihoods.","Society was a hierarchy of nobles, gentry, yeomen, husbandmen and labourers, with the Church holding great estates and running its own courts under the Pope.","Government was personal: the king with his Royal Council; Parliament summoned only when the king chose, chiefly for taxation; unpaid Justices of the Peace enforcing the law in the shires.","Henry was educated in Latin, music and theology; in 1521 he published a defence of the seven sacraments against Luther and Pope Leo X gave him the title Defender of the Faith.","He believed a king answered to God alone, that his honour was the kingdom's honour, and that display, jousting and war were matters of policy.","His weakness was the other side of that: impatience with routine administration, heavy spending of the treasury his father had filled, and dependence on whoever had daily access to him.","His aims were glory in France in the style of Henry V, equal standing with Francis I and Charles V, and above all a legitimate son."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Wolsey's rise and his reforms, including the Eltham Ordinances","items":["Thomas Wolsey was born in Ipswich to a family of no standing, educated at Oxford, and served Henry VII as a chaplain before becoming Almoner in 1509.","His organisation of supply for the French campaign of 1513 first proved his value to Henry VIII.","Promotions: Bishop of Lincoln and Archbishop of York 1514; cardinal 1515; Lord Chancellor December 1515; papal legate in England from 1518.","His wealth was famous: Hampton Court and York Place, revenues from several sees at once, and Cardinal College at Oxford and a school at Ipswich founded in the 1520s, paid for by closing about thirty small monasteries.","Justice: Wolsey sat in the Court of Star Chamber himself, offering quick and cheap justice, and its business rose many times over compared with Henry VII's reign.","He developed the Court of Requests, where poor men could bring cases cheaply, without the costs of the common law courts, and he was willing to prosecute noblemen as well as commoners - which made him enemies at court.","Finance: the subsidy, first granted in 1513, was assessed by commissioners on a taxpayer's real wealth in land and goods, unlike the fixed fifteenth and tenth, and raised far more.","In 1523 Wolsey asked Parliament for a very large grant, around 800,000 pounds, and the Commons, whose Speaker was Thomas More, granted far less, spread over several years.","Enclosure: a national inquiry in 1517 into land enclosed since the late 1480s led to court cases and orders to pull down hedges, but Wolsey agreed to stop pressing them in 1523 in return for taxation.","The Eltham Ordinances of January 1526 cut the size and cost of the royal household and tightened discipline over who could be fed at court.","They also halved the Privy Chamber from twelve Gentlemen to six and removed William Compton, the Groom of the Stool, an old rival with daily access to the king.","The savings were modest and many of those removed returned, so the Ordinances are best judged as a bid for control of the court rather than a lasting financial reform."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Amicable Grant, 1525","items":["Cause: Charles V's victory at Pavia on 24 February 1525, where the French army was destroyed and Francis I taken prisoner, convinced Henry that France lay open.","Problem: the war of 1522 to 1524 had drained the treasury, and the subsidy granted by Parliament in 1523 was still being collected.","The demand was made in 1525 by royal commissioners without Parliament, assessed as a share of the value of goods, with a heavier share asked of the clergy.","It was called 'amicable' because it was presented as a friendly gift to the king, which fooled nobody.","Refusals spread across the south and east, with legal objections that the king could not tax his subjects without their consent.","The worst resistance was in the cloth districts of Suffolk and Essex, where clothiers said paying would force them to lay off spinners and weavers.","Thousands gathered at Lavenham in Suffolk rather than pay, and the Dukes of Norfolk and Suffolk were sent to confront them.","The demand was abandoned within weeks; Henry declared he had not known of it and pardoned the resisters.","Wolsey was left to carry the blame publicly, damaging his standing with the king and with the country.","Consequences: the invasion of France was cancelled and England made peace with France in 1525, a reversal that shaped the rest of Wolsey's foreign policy."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Wolsey's foreign policy, the Treaty of London (1518) and the 'Field of the Cloth of Gold' (1520)","items":["Aims: glory and standing for Henry, a place for England in the affairs of Christendom, and influence bought as cheaply as possible by holding the balance between France and the Habsburgs.","1513: Henry invaded France, took Therouanne and Tournai and won the skirmish known as the Battle of the Spurs; Wolsey ran the supply.","9 September 1513: the Earl of Surrey destroyed a Scottish invasion at Flodden, where James IV was killed.","1514: peace with France, sealed by the marriage of Henry's sister Mary to Louis XII; Louis died in January 1515 and Francis I succeeded him.","1518: the Treaty of London. Wolsey took over Leo X's plan for a general truce and crusade and turned it into a non-aggression treaty signed in London by about twenty states, with England at its centre.","As part of the settlement Tournai was returned to France for payments spread over years, and Princess Mary was betrothed to the French Dauphin.","June 1520: the 'Field of the Cloth of Gold' near Calais - over a fortnight of jousting, feasting and display with Francis I, including a temporary palace and tents of cloth of gold. It brought no alliance.","Henry also met Charles V immediately before and after that meeting, and by 1521 England was committed to Charles against France.","1522 to 1523: campaigns in France achieved nothing; the Duke of Suffolk's army advanced into northern France in 1523 and withdrew in bad weather without a battle.","1525: after Pavia, Charles refused to help Henry invade France, broke his betrothal to Princess Mary and married a Portuguese princess in 1526.","1525 and 1527: England made peace and then alliance with France; the resulting hostility to Charles disrupted the cloth trade with the Netherlands.","May 1527: imperial troops sacked Rome, leaving Pope Clement VII in the power of Charles V - the decisive obstacle to the annulment."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Catherine of Aragon, the succession, Pope Clement VII, the Boleyns and Wolsey's fall","items":["Catherine of Aragon married Henry in June 1509; of six pregnancies only Mary, born in February 1516, survived, and a son born on 1 January 1511 lived about seven weeks.","Henry's illegitimate son by Elizabeth Blount, born in 1519, was made Duke of Richmond in 1525, which convinced Henry the fault lay in the marriage.","By the mid-1520s Catherine was past forty; no woman had ruled England in her own right, and the Tudors had held the throne only since 1485.","Henry's case rested on Leviticus, which says a man who takes his brother's wife will be childless, so he argued his marriage had never been lawful and no dispensation could make it so.","Catherine answered that her marriage to Arthur had never been consummated, so the text did not apply, and she refused to retire to a nunnery.","Henry was not seeking a divorce as we use the word but an annulment: a declaration that he had never been married at all.","Anne Boleyn refused to become Henry's mistress, which turned the king's desire into a matter of state; her father Thomas Boleyn and her uncle the Duke of Norfolk rose with her.","Pope Clement VII would neither grant nor refuse: after the Sack of Rome in 1527 he was effectively in the hands of Charles V, Catherine's nephew.","In 1528 Clement sent Cardinal Campeggio to England with instructions to delay rather than decide.","The legatine court of Campeggio and Wolsey opened at Blackfriars in May 1529; Catherine denied its authority, appealed to Rome and withdrew.","In July 1529 Campeggio adjourned the court and the case was recalled to Rome, so Wolsey had failed at the one task the king cared about.","October 1529: Wolsey was charged under the statute of praemunire, so his legatine authority became the offence; he surrendered the Great Seal and kept only the archbishopric of York.","He was arrested on a charge of treason in 1530 and died at Leicester on the journey south; Thomas More had taken his place as Lord Chancellor."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — the 16-mark judgement: 'Wolsey's reforms in England served the king more than the people in the years 1515-29.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks)","items":["The stem as printed, as Question 3 or Question 4 of booklet B: the statement in quotation marks, then 'How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16)', then 'You may use the following in your answer: • the subsidy • the enclosure inquiry of 1517. You must also use information of your own.'","Decoded: you are asked whose interests Wolsey's work at home actually served, not to list what the reforms were. 'Served the king more than the people' has to be turned into a test before it can be judged, and the concept being examined is purpose and consequence - who gained by each reform, who paid for it, and what happened to it once it stopped being useful to the Crown.","Criterion to state in the opening: a reform served the people if it was kept even when it cost the Crown something, and served the king if it was dropped the moment it became inconvenient. Signal the line of argument at once: the reforms that raised money were kept, and the one aimed squarely at the commons was traded away.","Paragraph 1 (the statement, at its strongest): the subsidy, first granted in 1513, assessed by commissioners on what a taxpayer's land and goods were really worth instead of at the fixed rate of the old fifteenth and tenth, so it raised far more for the king's wars; in 1523 Wolsey asked Parliament for around 800,000 pounds, with Thomas More as Speaker, and was granted far less, spread over four years. Interim judgement: a fairer way of assessing, invented for the Crown's needs rather than the taxpayer's.","Paragraph 2 (the second bullet): the enclosure inquiry of 1517, a national commission into land enclosed since the late 1480s whose findings were written up as the 'Domesday of Inclosures', with landowners taken to court and hedges pulled down. Interim judgement: this is the strongest evidence against the statement, because the Crown gained little by it and the commons gained directly.","Paragraph 3 (the statement tested): the same campaign was given up in 1523 in return for Parliament's grant of taxation, which shows exactly what it was worth to Wolsey once it was weighed against money for war. Interim judgement: the people's interest survived only for as long as it cost the king nothing.","Paragraph 4 (beyond the stimulus, first point): justice - Wolsey sat in Star Chamber in person and its business rose to roughly ten times the level of Henry VII's reign, and he developed the Court of Requests so that poor men could sue cheaply. Interim judgement: here the two interests ran together, because quick royal justice answered a real need and bound subjects to the Crown at the same time.","Paragraph 5 (beyond the stimulus, the decisive point): the household and the emergency - the Eltham Ordinances of January 1526 cut the cost and the size of the royal household and halved the Privy Chamber from twelve Gentlemen to six, and the Amicable Grant of 1525 demanded a share of people's goods with no Parliament at all and was abandoned within weeks after thousands gathered at Lavenham rather than pay. Neither was for the people in any sense.","The evaluation thread that runs through every paragraph: who gained by this, and what happened to it when the Crown's needs changed? Keep asking both questions so the essay weighs rather than lists.","Conclusion: agree, but name the exception rather than sweeping it up. On the stated criterion the reforms that lasted - the subsidy above all - were the king's, and the one policy aimed at the commons was sold the moment it could be; only the courts served both at once, and they served Henry too by making his justice popular. Say which half of the criterion you are weighting, or the judgement is asserted rather than justified.","Timing: about 27 minutes - four to five minutes planning the criterion and the order of paragraphs, twenty writing, two checking that the words 'more than' have been answered rather than repeated. There are no SPaG marks on this paper, so spend the time on the argument.","Cap to avoid: the grid prints 'Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content', so an answer built only on the subsidy and the enclosure inquiry is held to 11 marks, and at least one reform of your own must be developed, not merely mentioned."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:21:20Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"Judged afresh from the kit file: 15 questions re-solved, every answer key sound, and about 70 dates, figures, names and events checked, the load-bearing ones on the web - Warham resigning the chancellorship on 22 December 1515¹, Fox surrendering the Privy Seal and leaving government in 1516¹, the first Parliament of the reign meeting on 21 January 1510², the 1517 enclosure commission and its 'Domesday of Inclosures'³, Wolsey's demand of about 800,000 pounds in the 1523 Parliament with Thomas More as Speaker and a much smaller grant spread over years⁴, and Star Chamber business running at roughly ten times Henry VII's level (about 12 cases a year to about 120)⁵ - all of which hold, as do the subsidy of 1513, Flodden on 9 September 1513, the Treaty of London and the return of Tournai in 1518, Pavia on 24 February 1525 and the Amicable Grant's collapse at Lavenham, the Eltham Ordinances of January 1526 halving the Gentlemen of the Privy Chamber from twelve to six and removing Compton, the Sack of Rome in 1527, Campeggio's adjournment in July 1529 and the praemunire charge of October 1529. The dedupe pass is clean: the relocated model-paragraph pair (Henry's accession and how England was governed, with the Royal Council and Justices of the Peace) and the relocated plan (whether Wolsey's reforms served the king more than the people, with the subsidy and the 1517 enclosure inquiry) set questions the bank does not, and no worked example shares a bank question's focus together with its stimulus points; read against the papers themselves, no bank question reproduces the Paper 2 specimen booklet B3 (Treaty of London features, Cromwell's fall in 1540, financial difficulties 1520-29, the commissions of 1535) or the June 2023 B3 paper (features of government in 1509, the Pilgrimage of Grace, the Eltham Ordinances as main reform, Cromwell's rise), and dupscan prints 'no duplication'. Form is right throughout for 2025: two-part 1(a)/1(b) at 2 marks each, Question 2 at 12 and Question 3 or 4 at 16, AO2 10 + AO1 6 on the judgement, the current cap wording with no Level 2 cap, no SPaG on Paper 2, and Paper 2 at 1 hour 50 minutes. What stops a 1.0 is small and all fixable without touching an answer key: the enclosure cut-off stated as 1489 four times, the 'minute a mark' line that its own budgets contradict, Henry VII's treasury attributed to the wrong war, the Amicable Grant's graduated rate printed as a flat sixth, and one worked-example step that is exam advice rather than a move. Above the publish threshold as it stands."}}},"B3-2":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"B3-2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This is the key topic where the British depth study turns from Wolsey to Cromwell, and the examiners can come at it from four directions at once: how a tradesman's son from Putney climbed to be Chief Minister, how he solved the annulment that had destroyed his old master, why three queens rose and fell around him, and why he was beheaded in 1540. Booklet B gives you no sources and no extracts, so every mark comes from precise knowledge of statutes, dates and people that you carry in your head. Almost every judgement question on this key topic turns on the same hinge — Cromwell could do anything for Henry except make Henry go on needing him — so a student who can weigh the minister's ability against the king's will already has an argument ready for the rise, the marriages, the reforms and the fall.","idea":[{"code":"B3-2 — 1 Cromwell's rise to power, 1529–34: Personality and early career","h":"Cromwell's personality and early career","t":"Thomas Cromwell was born at Putney in about 1485, the son of a tradesman, with no university degree and no noble kin. He spent his youth abroad as a soldier, merchant and clerk in Italy and the Low Countries, came home to practise as a lawyer and moneylender in London, and sat in the parliament of 1523. From about 1524 he was Cardinal Wolsey's man of business, and it was he who closed roughly thirty small religious houses to pay for Wolsey's Cardinal College at Oxford and his school at Ipswich — an apprenticeship in dissolving monasteries that he would later use on a national scale. Wolsey's fall in October 1529 should have ruined him; instead he had himself elected to the Reformation Parliament for Taunton in November 1529, defended his old master in the Commons and made himself useful to the king. A top-level answer uses the early career as evidence of why Henry wanted him: not birth, not connection, but legal skill, administrative nerve and a habit of solving problems that other men called impossible."},{"code":"B3-2 — 1 Cromwell's rise to power, 1529–34: Handling of the king's annulment","h":"The annulment, influence over Henry, and the role of Chief Minister","t":"Wolsey fell because he could not get the Pope to annul Henry's marriage to Catherine of Aragon. Cromwell's answer, put to the king between 1530 and 1532, was to stop asking Rome at all: if Henry were supreme head of the Church in his own realm, an English court could settle the case, and the way to say so was statute. The Submission of the Clergy in 1532 put the Church's law-making under royal control; the Act in Restraint of Appeals of 1533 declared England an empire whose king had no superior on earth and banned appeals to Rome, so the case could not be recalled; Thomas Cranmer, made Archbishop of Canterbury in March 1533, then held a court at Dunstable in May 1533 and pronounced the first marriage void. Henry had already married Anne Boleyn in January 1533, and Elizabeth was born that September. The Act of Supremacy of 1534 confirmed the title and the Treasons Act made denying it a capital crime. By 1534 Cromwell was Principal Secretary and Master of the Rolls and the king's Chief Minister. A top-level answer shows the chain: he did not merely get Henry a wife, he redefined where authority lay — and made himself the man who worked the new machine."},{"code":"B3-2 — 2 The king's marriages: Reasons for the fall of Anne Boleyn","h":"Why Anne Boleyn fell","t":"Anne Boleyn was queen for three years and lost everything in three weeks. Catherine of Aragon died on 7 January 1536, which made Anne expendable rather than safe, because a third marriage could now be made without admitting that the first had been valid. Days later, while Henry was recovering from a jousting fall, Anne miscarried a son on 29 January 1536. The king had already noticed Jane Seymour, and Anne had quarrelled with Cromwell over foreign policy and over what the wealth of the monasteries should be spent on. In April 1536 Cromwell built the case: Mark Smeaton was arrested on 30 April, Anne was taken to the Tower on 2 May, and she was charged with adultery with five men, including her own brother George, and with plotting the king's death. She was condemned on 15 May, five men died on 17 May, and Anne was beheaded on 19 May 1536. A top-level answer weighs the three strands — the failure to give Henry a son, the king's desire for Jane, and Cromwell's decision to make himself the instrument of it — and says which one made the rest possible."},{"code":"B3-2 — 2 The king's marriages: Reasons for marriage to Jane Seymour","h":"Jane Seymour: influence, heir and death","t":"Henry was betrothed to Jane Seymour on 20 May 1536, the day after Anne's execution, and married her on 30 May 1536. She was the opposite of Anne in every way the king now wanted: quiet, conventionally obedient, religiously conservative, and backed by a gentry family — her brothers Edward and Thomas — rather than by a faction the country loathed. She helped reconcile Henry with his daughter Mary in 1536, and during the Pilgrimage of Grace she asked him to spare the monasteries, which he angrily refused. Her decisive act was to bear Prince Edward at Hampton Court on 12 October 1537; she died twelve days later, on 24 October 1537, of the effects of childbirth. Henry mourned her, buried her at Windsor and was laid beside her. A top-level answer treats Jane as the solution to the dynastic problem that had driven everything since 1527, and notes that her death left Henry, with an heir at last, free to spend his next marriage on diplomacy instead."},{"code":"B3-2 — 2 The king's marriages: Reasons for marriage to Anne of Cleves","h":"Why Henry married Anne of Cleves","t":"The fourth marriage was a treaty, not a courtship. After 1538 England was dangerously isolated: Charles V and Francis I had made a truce at Nice, the sentence of excommunication against Henry was published in December 1538, and an attack on a schismatic kingdom looked possible. Cromwell's answer was a German alliance, and he pressed the suit of Anne, sister of Duke William of Cleves — a rich Rhineland prince at odds with the Emperor over Guelders, whose other sister was married to the Lutheran Elector of Saxony, though Cleves itself had not joined the German Protestant league. Hans Holbein was sent to paint her in 1539 and the marriage treaty was signed that October. Anne landed at Deal in December 1539; Henry rode to Rochester on 1 January 1540 to surprise her, took an instant dislike to her, and married her at Greenwich on 6 January 1540 only because the treaty left him no dignified way out. By the summer the danger had passed and the marriage was annulled on 9 July 1540. A top-level answer explains the marriage by foreign policy, and then shows how fast a diplomatic asset became a diplomatic embarrassment — and whose fault Henry decided that was."},{"code":"B3-2 — 3 Cromwell and government, 1534–40: Reform of government and royal finance","h":"Reform of government and royal finance","t":"The royal supremacy gave the Crown new powers, new courts and a great deal of new property, and Cromwell built the machinery to run them. Business moved out of the king's household, where much of it had been handled through the Privy Chamber, into departments of state with their own staff and records, and the office of Principal Secretary, which Cromwell held from 1534, became the hinge through which everything passed. The large, loose royal Council was replaced by a compact Privy Council of about twenty men, chosen for office rather than birth, which kept a register of its own from 1540. In finance, the Act of First Fruits and Tenths of 1534 took for the king the payments the clergy had made to Rome; the Valor Ecclesiasticus of 1535 valued every church living in the country; the smaller monasteries, those worth less than £200 a year, were dissolved in 1536, and the new Court of Augmentations was created that year to survey, collect and sell. By 1540 every religious house in England and Wales had gone. A top-level answer argues about how deep the change went: new institutions and a new source of wealth, certainly, but a king who still decided everything that mattered."},{"code":"B3-2 — 3 Cromwell and government, 1534–40: The management and use of parliament","h":"The management and use of parliament","t":"Cromwell's instrument was statute. The Reformation Parliament sat from November 1529 until April 1536 in session after session, far longer than any parliament before it, and Cromwell drafted and redrafted its bills, steered them through the Commons, worked on the Speaker and used royal influence in elections. What made this important was not that he flattered the Commons but that he used them: by passing the break with Rome as law made by king, Lords and Commons together, he made the supremacy something no subject could lawfully deny and no successor could easily undo. The same instrument did the rest of his work — the Act of Union with Wales in 1536, which divided Wales into shires, sent Welsh members to Westminster and made English the language of its courts; the dissolution acts of 1536 and 1539; the Act of Proclamations of 1539, which gave royal proclamations statutory force within limits. A top-level answer says what the statutes were for: parliament turned the king's will into the law of the land, and gave Cromwell an authority no minister had ever held through the Council alone."},{"code":"B3-2 — 4 The fall of Cromwell: Reasons for Cromwell's fall from power in 1540","h":"Why Cromwell fell in 1540","t":"Cromwell was created Earl of Essex in April 1540 and was dead by the end of July. Three things destroyed him. The Cleves marriage had failed: the alliance was no longer needed once Charles V and Francis I fell out again, Henry blamed the man who had arranged it, and the annulment of 9 July 1540 was a public verdict on his judgement. His religious position was exposed: the Act of Six Articles of 1539 reaffirmed transubstantiation, clerical celibacy and confession, and Cromwell, who had protected reformers such as Robert Barnes, could be accused of heresy as well as misjudgement. And he faced enemies with a better weapon than argument — Thomas Howard, Duke of Norfolk, and Stephen Gardiner, Bishop of Winchester, who put Norfolk's niece Catherine Howard in the king's way. Cromwell was arrested at the Council table on 10 June 1540, condemned by act of attainder without a trial, and beheaded on Tower Hill on 28 July 1540, the day Henry married Catherine Howard. A top-level answer keeps the king at the centre: a faction could only destroy a minister the king had stopped needing."}],"examples":[{"title":"Describing one feature twice without drifting into explanation","setup":"Question 1: (a) Describe one feature of the Treasons Act of 1534. (2 marks) (b) Describe one feature of the dissolution of the smaller monasteries in 1536. (2 marks) Each part gives one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information. Write both parts.","steps":["Part (a), the feature: the act made it treason to deny any of the king's titles, including his new title of Supreme Head of the Church.","Part (a), the supporting information: it caught spoken words as well as writing or deeds, and Bishop John Fisher and Sir Thomas More were executed under it in 1535.","Part (b), the feature: it closed the religious houses whose yearly income fell below a fixed value and handed their property to the Crown.","Part (b), the supporting information: the line was drawn at £200 a year, and the lands and rents went to the new Court of Augmentations.","Check the shape before you move on: two short paragraphs, each a single feature plus one supporting detail, with no reasons and no judgement — explaining why the act was passed earns nothing here."],"cues":["What did the Treasons Act make a crime?","Which two famous victims prove the point?","Which houses did the act of 1536 close?","What figure marked the dividing line, and who collected the money?","What must you leave out to stay inside a describe question?"]},{"title":"Turning two bullet points into three reasons on a 12-mark question","setup":"Question 2: Explain why the Treasons Act was passed in 1534. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the Act of Supremacy (1534) • Sir Thomas More. You must also use information of your own. The mark scheme prints: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","steps":["Reason one, from the first bullet: the Act of Supremacy had just declared Henry the only Supreme Head of the Church, and a title carrying no penalty for denial was worth little, so the new law attached death to denial.","Reason two, from the second bullet: silence was the danger. More refused the oath to the succession yet would not say why, and making treason by words alone closed that gap; he and Bishop Fisher were executed in 1535.","Reason three, your own: the dynasty was insecure. Anne Boleyn's daughter Elizabeth had been made heir by the Act of Succession, and Catherine of Aragon and Princess Mary still had support at home and at the Emperor's court, so opposition had to be frightened into silence.","A fourth aspect of content, also your own: legislation needed enforcement, and Cromwell built it — oath commissions in every shire, informers and reports to the Council gave the new treason law the evidence it could act on.","Tie the reasons together in a closing sentence: the statute existed to protect a supremacy that was new, contested and, until men could be killed for denying it, only words on paper."],"cues":["What did the supremacy still lack once it was declared?","Why was a man who said nothing a problem for the Crown?","Which aspect of content have the bullets left untouched?","Who was going to make the new law work in the shires?","What single sentence links all your reasons to the question?"]},{"title":"Choosing a criterion before judging a 16-mark statement","setup":"Question 3: 'The dissolution of the monasteries was carried out mainly to increase royal revenue.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the Valor Ecclesiasticus (1535) • the Pilgrimage of Grace. You must also use information of your own. The mark scheme caps the answer at 11 marks, and bars Level 4, if it does not address three or more aspects of content.","steps":["Set the criterion first: judge the government by what it actually did with the houses and their property, not by what its critics said its motives were.","For the statement: the Valor Ecclesiasticus was a house-by-house valuation made before a single monastery was closed; the act of 1536 took the houses worth less than £200 a year; and the Court of Augmentations was created in the same year to collect the rents and sell the land.","Against the statement: the visitations gathered charges of immorality and superstition, and the supremacy had made obedience the test — a monk who owed loyalty to an order directed from abroad was a political problem as well as a wealthy one.","Against the statement, your own aspect: the rising known as the Pilgrimage of Grace in October 1536 showed exactly that political danger, and its defeat allowed Henry to take the great houses too, so suppression grew out of resistance as well as out of greed.","Judge on your criterion: money explains the timing and the machinery, but the supremacy explains why closure was thinkable at all — agree in the main, with that qualification stated."],"cues":["What will you measure the government's motives against?","Which pieces of evidence show planning for money?","What reason for closure has nothing to do with income?","How did the northern rising change what the Crown dared do?","What qualified judgement does your criterion now force?"]},{"title":"Building a causal chain about a new institution","setup":"Question 2: Explain why the Court of Augmentations was set up in 1536. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the dissolution of the smaller monasteries • royal finance. You must also use information of your own.","steps":["Start from the problem the first bullet creates: the act of 1536 handed the Crown hundreds of estates at once, with rents, tenants, leases, timber, plate and lawsuits attached, and the medieval Exchequer was far too slow to take them in.","Chain that to the outcome: land nobody administers produces no money, so a court with its own treasurer, auditors and receivers was created to survey the estates, collect the rents and sell what the king wanted sold.","Chain the second bullet: Henry's ordinary revenue could not pay for defence and a court at the same time, and a permanent department turned a windfall into a regular income.","Add an aspect of your own: it matched Cromwell's whole method, which was to move business out of the royal household into departments of state with records and officers of their own, answerable through the Principal Secretary.","Finish on the conceptual link: the court existed because the supremacy had turned the king into the greatest landlord in England, and only a bureaucracy could turn that land into money."],"cues":["What arrived all at once that nobody was ready to handle?","What has to happen to an estate before it yields cash?","Why did the Crown want the income to be permanent?","How does this fit the rest of Cromwell's reforms?","What made the king a landlord in the first place?"]},{"title":"Weighing one minister against another","setup":"Question 4: 'Thomas Cromwell was a more powerful minister than Cardinal Wolsey had been.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the office of Principal Secretary • parliament. You must also use information of your own.","steps":["Decide the measure of power before you argue: not titles or wealth, but how much of the state's business a man controlled and how far the changes he made outlasted him.","For the statement: over the years 1534 to 1536 Cromwell gathered the great offices one after another — Principal Secretary and Master of the Rolls in 1534, vicegerent in spirituals in 1535, then Lord Privy Seal in July 1536, when he gave up the Rolls — so that he held the Church as well as the state, whereas Wolsey's authority over the Church came from Rome as legate and depended on the Pope's favour.","For the statement, your own aspect: Cromwell's changes were statutes — the supremacy, the succession, the dissolutions, the union with Wales — and statute outlived him, while Wolsey's Eltham Ordinances and legal reforms were quietly dropped after 1529.","Against the statement: Wolsey governed almost alone for fourteen years as Lord Chancellor, while Cromwell had to manage a Council and a parliament he could never simply command, and a faction destroyed him in seven weeks in 1540.","Judge on the criterion you set: Cromwell's power reached further and its effects lasted longer, but both men held office on identical terms, from a king who could take it back whenever he chose."],"cues":["How will you decide which minister was more powerful?","Which offices gave Cromwell authority over the Church?","Which of the two men's reforms were still standing later?","What did Wolsey have that Cromwell never enjoyed?","What did the two ministers have in common in the end?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which act of 1533 stopped appeals from English church courts going to Rome?","a":"The Act in Restraint of Appeals."},{"q":"On what date was Anne Boleyn executed?","a":"19 May 1536."},{"q":"Which new court was created in 1536 to manage the lands of the dissolved monasteries?","a":"The Court of Augmentations."},{"q":"Who arranged the Anne of Cleves marriage, and when was it annulled?","a":"Thomas Cromwell; the marriage was annulled on 9 July 1540."},{"q":"Where and when was Prince Edward born, and when did his mother die?","a":"At Hampton Court on 12 October 1537; Jane Seymour died on 24 October 1537."}]},"room":{"facts":["Booklet B of Paper 2 carries 32 marks on this depth study: Question 1(a) and 1(b), 'Describe one feature of...', 2 marks each on two separate aspects of content; Question 2, 'Explain why...', 12 marks; and a choice of Question 3 or Question 4, a 16-mark judgement, 'How far do you agree? Explain your answer.'","'Describe one feature of...' is point-marked: 1 mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information. No reasons, no judgement, and no second feature in a part that asks for one.","The 12-mark and 16-mark questions each print two stimulus points under 'You may use the following in your answer:' followed by 'You must also use information of your own.' Using the bullets is optional; adding material of your own is not.","The caps are printed in the mark scheme in these words. On the 12-mark question: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'. On the 16-mark judgement the maximum is 11 marks, with the same bar on Level 4.","Level 4 is 10-12 marks on the 12-mark question and 13-16 on the 16-mark judgement. It needs an analytical explanation directed consistently at the conceptual focus, a line of reasoning that is coherent, sustained and logically structured, and, on the judgement, criteria that are justified and applied in reaching the overall verdict.","The AO split is AO2 6 and AO1 6 on the 12-mark question, and AO2 10 and AO1 6 on the 16-mark judgement, whose grid prints three bullets in every level: the AO2 explanation, the AO1 accurate and relevant information, and the overall judgement, which is AO2 as well. An answer showing no qualities of AO2 cannot rise above the top of Level 1 however strong the knowledge.","Paper 2 lasts 1 hour 50 minutes for both booklets, carries no SPaG marks and prints no sources or interpretations, so every mark comes from your own knowledge. Work at roughly a minute a mark: about 5 minutes on Question 1, 20 on Question 2 and 27 on the judgement."],"questions":[{"q":"Question 1: (a) Describe one feature of Thomas Cromwell's career before he entered royal service. (2 marks) (b) Describe one feature of Cromwell's work for Cardinal Wolsey. (2 marks)","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked as 2 + 2: one mark for each valid feature and a second for supporting information. (a) years abroad as soldier, merchant and clerk; training as a lawyer and moneylender in London; a seat in the parliament of 1523. (b) Wolsey's man of business and legal agent from about 1524; closing small monasteries to fund Cardinal College, Oxford and the school at Ipswich.","sol":"(a) One feature was that Cromwell learned his trade abroad and outside the Church. As a young man he served in Italy and worked in the cloth trade in the Low Countries, then returned to London to practise as a lawyer and moneylender, and he sat in the parliament of 1523. (b) One feature was that he was Wolsey's man of business from about 1524. His chief task was the closing of roughly thirty small religious houses so that their income could pay for the cardinal's new Cardinal College at Oxford and his school at Ipswich.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about where Cromwell was, and how he earned his living, before he ever saw the king.","Each part needs one feature and then one supporting detail — a place, a date or a named institution.","Two short paragraphs, each of two sentences: the feature, then the detail that proves you know it."],"codes":["B3-2 — 1 Cromwell's rise to power, 1529–34: Personality and early career"]},{"q":"Question 1: (a) Describe one feature of the Act in Restraint of Appeals (1533). (2 marks) (b) Describe one feature of the Act of Supremacy (1534). (2 marks)","a":"AO1 4 marks, 2 + 2, point-marked. (a) it banned appeals from English church courts to Rome; it declared England an empire with no superior on earth; it left Cranmer's court free to settle the annulment. (b) it declared Henry the only Supreme Head on earth of the Church of England; it gave him powers of visitation and reform; it made the supremacy statute law.","sol":"(a) One feature was that it forbade appeals from English church courts to Rome. It declared that England was an empire whose king had no superior on earth, so Catherine of Aragon could not have her case recalled to the Pope, and in May 1533 Cranmer's court at Dunstable was free to declare her marriage void. (b) One feature was that it named Henry the only Supreme Head on earth of the Church of England. It gave the king power to visit, correct and reform the Church, and it did so by statute, so denial of the title could be punished as a crime.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what each statute took away from Rome and gave to the king.","Name the act's effect first, then add a consequence or a date that shows it working.","Two short paragraphs: the feature in one sentence, the supporting detail in the next."],"codes":["B3-2 — 1 Cromwell's rise to power, 1529–34: Handling of the king's annulment"]},{"q":"Question 1: (a) Describe one feature of the charges brought against Anne Boleyn in May 1536. (2 marks) (b) Describe one feature of Henry VIII's marriage to Jane Seymour. (2 marks)","a":"AO1 4 marks, 2 + 2, point-marked. (a) adultery with five named men, including her brother George Boleyn and the musician Mark Smeaton; conspiring the king's death, which made the charge treason; condemnation on 15 May 1536. (b) betrothal on 20 May 1536 and marriage on 30 May 1536; Jane proclaimed queen but never crowned; the birth of Prince Edward in October 1537.","sol":"(a) One feature was that she was accused of adultery with several men at court. Five were named, including the musician Mark Smeaton and her own brother George Boleyn, and the indictment added that she had plotted the king's death, which turned the charge into treason. (b) One feature was how quickly it followed Anne's execution. Henry was betrothed to Jane on 20 May 1536, the day after Anne was beheaded, and married her ten days later on 30 May 1536; she was proclaimed queen but was never crowned.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["One part is about an accusation, the other about a timetable.","Name a person or a date in each part — that is where the second mark lives.","Write four sentences in all: feature, detail, feature, detail."],"codes":["B3-2 — 2 The king's marriages: Reasons for the fall of Anne Boleyn","B3-2 — 2 The king's marriages: Reasons for marriage to Jane Seymour"]},{"q":"Question 1: (a) Describe one feature of the Court of Augmentations. (2 marks) (b) Describe one feature of the Privy Council as Cromwell left it. (2 marks)","a":"AO1 4 marks, 2 + 2, point-marked. (a) created in 1536 to manage the lands and goods of the dissolved monasteries; its own treasurer, auditors and receivers; it collected rents and sold land for the Crown. (b) a small fixed body of about nineteen men replacing the large medieval Council; membership by the office a man held rather than by birth; it met with the court and handled the daily business of government.","sol":"(a) One feature was its purpose: it was set up in 1536 to manage the lands and goods taken from the dissolved monasteries. It had its own treasurer, auditors and receivers, who surveyed the estates, collected the rents and sold land on the Crown's behalf. (b) One feature was its small size. The large medieval royal Council was replaced by a fixed body of about nineteen men, chosen for the offices they held rather than for their birth, and it met with the court to handle the daily business of government.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Both parts are about machinery: one handles money, the other handles advice.","A feature can be a purpose, a size or a way of working — then add the detail that proves it.","Two short paragraphs, each naming the body and one thing that was true of it."],"codes":["B3-2 — 3 Cromwell and government, 1534–40: Reform of government and royal finance"]},{"q":"Question 1: (a) Describe one feature of the Act of Union with Wales (1536). (2 marks) (b) Describe one feature of the Reformation Parliament. (2 marks)","a":"AO1 4 marks, 2 + 2, point-marked. (a) Wales divided into shires on the English pattern; English law and the English language in the courts; Welsh counties and boroughs sending members to Westminster. (b) it met from November 1529 to April 1536 over a series of sessions; Cromwell sat in it and drafted its bills; it passed the statutes that broke with Rome.","sol":"(a) One feature was that it brought Wales inside the English system of government. Welsh lands were divided into shires on the English pattern, English law and the English language were used in the courts, and Welsh counties and boroughs sent members to the parliament at Westminster. (b) One feature was its extraordinary length. It first met in November 1529 and was not dissolved until April 1536, sitting session after session for more than six years, and in that time it passed the statutes that broke England's ties with Rome.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["One part is about a country being reorganised, the other about a parliament that would not go away.","Give the change, then a date, a number or an institution that supports it.","Four sentences in all, two to each part, with no reasons and no judgement."],"codes":["B3-2 — 3 Cromwell and government, 1534–40: The management and use of parliament"]},{"q":"Question 2: Explain why Thomas Cromwell was able to carry the Reformation legislation through parliament in the years 1532-34. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the Supplication against the Ordinaries (1532) • the Submission of the Clergy (1532). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit: the lay grievances the Commons had aired since 1529 over church courts, fees, probate and mortuaries, gathered up in the Supplication against the Ordinaries of March 1532; the Submission of the Clergy on 15 May 1532, which surrendered Convocation's power to make canons without the king's licence; Cromwell's drafting, the successive drafts of the Act in Restraint of Appeals (1533) and its claim that England was an empire, and the same method behind the Act of Supremacy (1534); his own seat for Taunton and his work with Thomas Audley, Speaker from November 1529 and Lord Chancellor from January 1533; the weakness of the clergy after the praemunire charge, Canterbury buying its pardon for £100,000 in 1531; the conditional form of the Act in Restraint of Annates (1532), left for the king to bring into force; the continuity of one parliament sitting from November 1529 to April 1536. Maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Cromwell carried the statutes because he knew parliament from the inside and gave it bills it was willing to pass.\n\nThe first reason was the mood of the Commons. Grievances against the church courts — their fees, their handling of probate and mortuaries, the delays and the heresy trials — had been aired since the parliament met in November 1529, and in March 1532 the House presented them to the king as the Supplication against the Ordinaries, a petition Cromwell had a hand in drafting. The attack therefore looked like the Commons' own. It worked: on 15 May 1532 Convocation made the Submission of the Clergy, giving up its power to make canons without royal licence, so that the Church could no longer legislate against what parliament was about to do.\n\nThe second reason, beyond the bullets, was the drafting itself. Cromwell wrote and rewrote his bills — the Act in Restraint of Appeals went through several drafts, some corrected in his own hand — until each did one clear job. Appeals stopped cases being recalled to Rome and justified it by declaring England an empire whose king had no superior on earth, which freed Cranmer to judge the marriage at Dunstable in May 1533 without touching anything else members valued. The Act of Supremacy of 1534 was built the same way.\n\nThe third reason was management. Cromwell sat in the Commons himself as member for Taunton, and worked with Thomas Audley, Speaker from November 1529 and Lord Chancellor from January 1533, so business was timed, steered and defended in both Houses by men who knew what was coming.\n\nFourth, opposition had been weakened and reassured. The clergy had been charged with praemunire and Canterbury had bought its pardon for £100,000 in 1531, and the Act in Restraint of Annates of 1532 was left for the king to bring into force, so members could vote for a threat rather than for a breach.\n\nThe legislation passed, then, because Cromwell made the king's revolution look like parliament's own work.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the Commons already wanted before the king needed anything from them.","Three reasons of different kinds: the mood of the House, the wording of the bills, and the men who managed the business.","Open with the complaints aired since 1529 and the petition of March 1532, then show what it forced the clergy to concede."],"codes":["B3-2 — 1 Cromwell's rise to power, 1529–34: Handling of the king's annulment","B3-2 — 3 Cromwell and government, 1534–40: The management and use of parliament"]},{"q":"Question 2: Explain why Henry VIII was able to obtain the annulment of his marriage to Catherine of Aragon by 1533. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the Act in Restraint of Appeals (1533) • Thomas Cranmer. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit: the statute of 1533 barring appeals to Rome and declaring England an empire; Cranmer's appointment as Archbishop of Canterbury in March 1533 and his court at Dunstable in May; the prior pressure on the English clergy (praemunire, the Submission of the Clergy 1532, the act over annates); the timetable forced by the secret marriage of January 1533 and Anne Boleyn's pregnancy; Elizabeth's birth on 7 September 1533. Maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Henry succeeded in 1533 because the question had been changed. Instead of asking whether the Pope would annul the marriage, Cromwell arranged matters so that the Pope could not be asked at all.\n\nThe first reason was the law. The Act in Restraint of Appeals, passed in the spring of 1533, declared England an empire whose king had no superior on earth and forbade appeals from English church courts to Rome. Catherine's strongest weapon had always been the recall of the case to Rome, which is exactly what had happened to the legatine court in 1529; the statute removed it, so whatever an English court decided would stand.\n\nThe second reason was Cranmer. Henry needed a judge as well as a jurisdiction. Thomas Cranmer had argued that the universities of Europe rather than the Pope should settle the question, and he was a chaplain in the Boleyn household. He was made Archbishop of Canterbury in March 1533, held his court at Dunstable in May, declared the marriage to Catherine void and days later pronounced the marriage to Anne good.\n\nThe third reason, beyond the bullets, was the pressure already applied to the English Church. The clergy had been accused under the law of praemunire and fined, forced in the Submission of the Clergy of 1532 to give up making canons without royal consent, and threatened through the act over annates with the loss of the Pope's English revenue. By 1533 the bishops were in no position to resist.\n\nFourth, the timetable was set inside the royal household. Henry married Anne secretly in January 1533, and if her child were to be born legitimate the case had to be closed before the autumn; Elizabeth was born on 7 September 1533.\n\nThe annulment was won not by argument but by jurisdiction: Cromwell moved the court, and the verdict followed.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["The king had been asking Rome for six years without success — what changed was not the argument.","Take the two bullets as the machinery and the judge, then add the pressure already put on the English clergy.","Every paragraph should end by saying how that step made the verdict of May 1533 possible."],"codes":["B3-2 — 1 Cromwell's rise to power, 1529–34: Handling of the king's annulment"]},{"q":"Question 2: Explain why Anne Boleyn fell from power in 1536. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the miscarriage of January 1536 • Thomas Cromwell. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit: the failure to bear a son, Elizabeth's birth in 1533 and the miscarriage of 29 January 1536; the death of Catherine of Aragon on 7 January 1536 freeing Henry to remarry without conceding the first marriage; Cromwell's quarrel with Anne over foreign policy and monastic wealth and his construction of the case in April and May 1536; Henry's interest in Jane Seymour. Maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Anne Boleyn fell because she had failed at the one task that justified the whole upheaval of the previous seven years, and because powerful people found it convenient to destroy her once she had.\n\nThe first reason was the failure to produce a son. Elizabeth, born in September 1533, was another daughter, and on 29 January 1536 Anne miscarried a male child. Henry had broken with Rome and set aside a Spanish princess in order to get a male heir; a second queen who could not give him one looked, to his mind, like evidence that this marriage too displeased God. Days earlier he had been knocked senseless in a jousting accident, and his temper after it was unforgiving.\n\nThe second reason, which the bullets do not prompt, is that Catherine of Aragon's death on 7 January 1536 changed the politics entirely. While Catherine lived, removing Anne would have meant admitting that the first marriage had been valid all along. Once she was dead Henry could take a third wife while conceding nothing, and a reconciliation with the Emperor Charles V, Catherine's nephew, became possible the moment Anne was gone.\n\nThe third reason was Cromwell. He had quarrelled with Anne over foreign policy and over the wealth of the monasteries, which she wanted spent on education and charity rather than poured into the king's coffers, and by April 1536 he could see that his own position was unsafe while she stood. He took charge of the case: Mark Smeaton was arrested and questioned on 30 April, Anne was taken to the Tower on 2 May, and a charge of adultery with five men, including her brother George, and of conspiring the king's death was assembled fast enough to come to trial on 15 May. She was beheaded on 19 May 1536.\n\nFourth, Jane Seymour gave Henry an alternative, so that the queen's removal became desirable and not merely possible.\n\nThe miscarriage made Anne vulnerable and Cromwell made her fall certain, but only Henry could decide that she had to go.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Start from what Anne had been brought in to do, and whether she had done it.","Separate the three kinds of cause: dynastic failure, a change in the diplomatic situation, and a minister's decision to act.","Bring in a cause the bullets do not mention, and say in each paragraph why that cause mattered in 1536 particularly."],"codes":["B3-2 — 2 The king's marriages: Reasons for the fall of Anne Boleyn"]},{"q":"Question 2: Explain why Henry VIII married Anne of Cleves in January 1540. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the Duke of Cleves • foreign policy. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit: England's isolation after the truce of Nice in 1538 and the publication of the excommunication in December 1538; the value of Cleves as a rich Rhineland ally at odds with Charles V over Guelders and linked to the Lutheran Elector of Saxony by marriage; Cromwell's advocacy of a German alliance, the Holbein portrait of 1539 and the treaty of October 1539; the binding force of the treaty once Anne had landed at Deal in December 1539. Maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Henry married a German princess he had never met because by 1539 England was frighteningly alone, and because the minister who arranged the match believed a German alliance was the answer.\n\nThe first reason was that isolation. In 1538 Charles V and Francis I made a truce at Nice and stopped fighting one another; the sentence of excommunication against Henry was published in December of that year, and a joint Catholic attack on a schismatic kingdom looked entirely possible. Henry spent heavily on forts along the south coast. In that situation a foreign alliance was a matter of security, and the German princes were the only allies on offer.\n\nThe second reason was what Cleves itself offered. Duke William was rich, held lands on the lower Rhine, and was in dispute with Charles V over Guelders, so he had every reason to want friends. His other sister was married to the Elector of Saxony, the leading Lutheran prince, which gave Henry an indirect link to the German Protestants without committing him to their doctrine, since Cleves had not joined their league.\n\nThe third reason, beyond the bullets, was Cromwell himself. He pressed this match hardest because it suited his own religious leaning and would tie England to reform; his agents sent back favourable reports, Hans Holbein was sent to paint Anne in 1539, and the marriage treaty was signed that October.\n\nFourth, once the treaty existed Henry could not escape it. Anne landed at Deal in December 1539; the king rode to Rochester on 1 January 1540 to meet her in disguise and was bitterly disappointed, but a bride already in the country and a treaty already sealed left no dignified way out, and the wedding took place at Greenwich on 6 January 1540.\n\nHenry married Anne of Cleves for diplomatic safety rather than for love, which is why the marriage lasted only as long as the danger did.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Look at the map of Europe in 1538 before you look at the bride.","Separate what England needed, what Cleves could supply, and who argued for the match — one paragraph each.","Finish on why the wedding still went ahead after Henry had met her."],"codes":["B3-2 — 2 The king's marriages: Reasons for marriage to Anne of Cleves"]},{"q":"Question 2: Explain why Thomas Cromwell reformed the government and finances of England in the years 1534-40. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the royal supremacy • the royal household. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit: the administrative work created by the supremacy (visitations, oaths, the vicegerency); the property and revenue from the Church (First Fruits and Tenths 1534, Valor Ecclesiasticus 1535, the dissolution act of 1536 and the Court of Augmentations); the weakness of household government and the rise of the Principal Secretary and the Privy Council; the need to govern the outlying regions (Act of Union with Wales 1536, the Council of the North after the Pilgrimage of Grace). Maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Cromwell rebuilt the machinery of government because the royal supremacy had given the Crown work it had never had to do before, and the old household methods could not do it.\n\nThe first reason was the supremacy itself. After 1534 the king was head of a Church as well as of a state: there were visitations to order, clergy to be assessed and taxed, oaths to be administered across the whole country and dissent to be reported and punished. Cromwell held the new office of vicegerent in spirituals, and to make the king's headship real in every parish he needed officials, records and a chain of command.\n\nThe second reason was the property. The Valor Ecclesiasticus of 1535 valued the Church's wealth living by living; the act of 1536 dissolved the houses worth less than £200 a year; and by 1540 every religious house in England and Wales had gone. Hundreds of estates with rents, leases and lawsuits could not be handled by the medieval Exchequer, so Cromwell created a specialist department, the Court of Augmentations, in 1536, alongside a separate machinery for first fruits and tenths.\n\nThe third reason was the weakness of household government. Much of the king's finance and business had been run through the Privy Chamber, which depended on personal access and could be captured by a faction. Cromwell moved business into departments of state with their own staff and records, made the Principal Secretary, an office he held from 1534, the point through which it all passed, and saw the sprawling royal Council become a working Privy Council of about twenty men.\n\nFourth, beyond the bullets, the outlying parts of the realm had to be brought under the same administration: the Act of Union of 1536 divided Wales into shires and sent its members to Westminster, and the Council of the North was reorganised after the northern rising of 1536.\n\nCromwell reformed, in short, because a national Church and national revenues demanded a national administration.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what new jobs the Crown suddenly had to do after 1534.","Take the causes in turn — new powers, new property, an old system that could not cope — and explain the reform each one forced.","Add an aspect the bullets miss: the parts of the realm furthest from Westminster."],"codes":["B3-2 — 3 Cromwell and government, 1534–40: Reform of government and royal finance"]},{"q":"Question 2: Explain why Henry VIII turned against Thomas Cromwell in the years 1539-40. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the Duke of Norfolk • the Act of Six Articles (1539). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit: the conservative religious turn of 1539 and Cromwell's protection of reformers such as Robert Barnes, which allowed a charge of heresy; the failure of the Cleves marriage and the collapse of the need for a German alliance once Charles V and Francis I quarrelled again; Norfolk and Gardiner's work on the king's suspicions and the promotion of Catherine Howard; the enemies made by the dissolution and by attainders; the outcome — arrest on 10 June, attainder without trial, execution on 28 July 1540. Maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Henry turned against Cromwell because, within a single year, the minister stopped giving the king what he wanted and began costing him things he valued.\n\nThe first reason was religion. The Act of Six Articles of 1539 reaffirmed transubstantiation, clerical celibacy and auricular confession, and it was the king's own doing: it showed that Henry, having taken the Church from the Pope, had no intention of taking it towards Lutheranism. Cromwell had been moving the other way, protecting reformers such as Robert Barnes, who was in open quarrel with Stephen Gardiner, Bishop of Winchester. From 1539 the Secretary's religion looked to his master less like useful energy and more like disobedience.\n\nThe second reason, which neither bullet prompts, was the Cleves marriage. Cromwell had built the German alliance and had talked the king into a marriage he disliked from their first meeting at Rochester on 1 January 1540. By the spring the alliance was worthless, because Charles V and Francis I had fallen out again and England was no longer isolated, so Henry was left with a wife he would not touch and a policy that had bought him nothing. He blamed the man who had urged it.\n\nThe third reason was the Duke of Norfolk. Thomas Howard resented taking orders from a tradesman's son who had been made Earl of Essex in April 1540, and he held a weapon that argument could not match: his niece Catherine Howard, whom the king noticed that spring. Working with Gardiner, he saw that every complaint about Cromwell reached Henry with a remedy attached.\n\nFourth, the minister had no defenders. The dissolution had disappointed nobles and gentry who wanted patronage or land, families had been ruined by acts of attainder he had drafted, and the northern rebels of 1536 had demanded his dismissal by name. He was arrested at the Council table on 10 June 1540, condemned by attainder without a trial and beheaded on 28 July.\n\nHenry turned against Cromwell because usefulness was the only claim the man had ever had on him, and by 1540 it had run out.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what Cromwell had been useful for, and whether Henry still needed it by 1540.","Take three strands — religion, diplomacy and faction — and show how each changed the king's view of his minister.","Every paragraph should end with Henry: the question asks about the king's mind, not about the arrest."],"codes":["B3-2 — 4 The fall of Cromwell: Reasons for Cromwell's fall from power in 1540"]},{"q":"Question 3: 'The royal supremacy was Thomas Cromwell's greatest achievement in the years 1529-40.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the Act in Restraint of Appeals (1533) • the Court of Augmentations. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit for weighing the supremacy (Appeals 1533, Supremacy 1534, Treasons Act, the executions of Fisher and More in 1535) against the financial reconstruction (First Fruits and Tenths 1534, Valor Ecclesiasticus 1535, the dissolution act of 1536, the Court of Augmentations) and against the reform of government (Principal Secretary, Privy Council, Act of Union with Wales 1536); a criterion of permanence and reach; the argument that the other achievements grew out of the supremacy; Henry's own part. Maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Judged by what lasted and by how far it reached, the royal supremacy was indeed Cromwell's greatest achievement — but chiefly because it was the achievement that made all the others possible. The criterion has to be permanence and reach: how far a measure altered the way England was ruled, and whether it survived its author.\n\nThe case for the statement is strong. Cromwell's solution to the king's Great Matter was not to win the Pope round but to remove him from the question. The Act in Restraint of Appeals of 1533 declared England an empire whose king had no superior on earth and forbade appeals to Rome, so Cranmer's court at Dunstable could end the marriage to Catherine in May 1533 and no one could recall the case. The Act of Supremacy of 1534 made Henry the only Supreme Head on earth of the Church of England, and the Treasons Act made denial of that title a capital crime; Bishop John Fisher and Sir Thomas More died under it in 1535. This was a constitutional change of the first order, and it was made by statute, so it belonged to the law of the land rather than to the king's personal favour. It outlived Cromwell by centuries.\n\nAgainst the statement, the financial reconstruction has a claim of its own. The Act of First Fruits and Tenths of 1534 transferred to the Crown the payments the clergy had been making to Rome; the Valor Ecclesiasticus of 1535 surveyed every living in the country; the act of 1536 dissolved the houses worth less than £200 a year, and by 1540 every religious house in England and Wales had gone. To manage the windfall Cromwell created the Court of Augmentations in 1536, with its own treasurer, auditors and receivers. This gave the monarchy landed wealth on a scale it had never possessed and a bureaucracy capable of exploiting it, and it bound every buyer of monastic land to the new settlement for good.\n\nA third aspect, beyond the bullets, is the reform of government itself. Business moved out of the royal household into departments of state; the Principal Secretary, Cromwell's own office from 1534, became the hinge of administration; the sprawling royal Council was compressed into a Privy Council of about twenty men which kept a register of its own from 1540; and the Act of Union of 1536 extended shire government, English law and representation at Westminster across Wales.\n\nYet each of these grew out of the supremacy. There were no monastic lands to administer until the king was head of the Church, no Court of Augmentations without the dissolution, and no vicegerency in spirituals without the title of 1534. Nor was any of it Cromwell's alone: Henry wanted the annulment and took the title, and the same king destroyed the minister in 1540 without weakening the supremacy in the slightest.\n\nThe supremacy was therefore his greatest achievement, not because it was his most ingenious piece of administration, but because it was the change that made every other change legal, profitable and permanent.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Before you agree or disagree, decide what would make one achievement greater than another.","Put the supremacy, the money and the machinery of government side by side, each with its own named statutes.","Ask which of the achievements would still have been possible if the others had never happened — that is your judgement."],"codes":["B3-2 — 1 Cromwell's rise to power, 1529–34: Handling of the king's annulment","B3-2 — 3 Cromwell and government, 1534–40: Reform of government and royal finance"]},{"q":"Question 3: 'Thomas Cromwell was the main reason for the fall of Anne Boleyn in 1536.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the miscarriage of January 1536 • Jane Seymour. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit for weighing Cromwell's construction of the case in April and May 1536 against the miscarriage of 29 January 1536 and the failure to bear a son, against Henry's desire for Jane Seymour, and against the change in the diplomatic position after Catherine of Aragon's death on 7 January 1536; a criterion separating the decisive cause from the instrument; Henry's authorship of the decision. Maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Cromwell was the instrument of Anne Boleyn's destruction rather than its cause. To judge the statement properly the criterion must distinguish between the man who made the fall happen in the way it did and the reasons that made her fall at all.\n\nThere is a serious case for the statement. In the spring of 1536 Cromwell was in danger himself. He and Anne had quarrelled over foreign policy, where he wanted friendship with Charles V and she did not, and over the wealth of the dissolved monasteries, which she wanted directed to education and charity rather than to the king's coffers. In April he withdrew from court and then moved with extraordinary speed: Mark Smeaton was arrested and questioned on 30 April, Anne was taken to the Tower on 2 May, and within a fortnight a case of adultery with five men, including her brother George Boleyn, and of conspiring the king's death had been assembled, tried and concluded. She was condemned on 15 May and beheaded on 19 May 1536. Without Cromwell's management there might have been a divorce; there would not have been a scaffold.\n\nAgainst the statement, the deeper reason was dynastic. Anne existed as queen to give Henry a son. Elizabeth, born in September 1533, was a daughter, and on 29 January 1536 Anne miscarried a male child. Henry had broken with Rome, defied the Emperor and set aside a Spanish princess for this, and a second childless marriage suggested to him that God was displeased again. Days before, a jousting fall had left him shaken and ill-tempered. The miscarriage is what turned the king against her; everything else followed from that.\n\nA further reason, beyond the bullets, is that the diplomatic obstacle to removing Anne had just disappeared. Catherine of Aragon died on 7 January 1536. While she lived, discarding Anne would have meant conceding that the first marriage had been lawful all along; once she was dead Henry could take a third wife without admitting anything, and a reconciliation with Charles V became possible. Anne was suddenly expendable in a way she had never been before.\n\nJane Seymour supplies the last element. A king who wants a new wife needs a candidate, and the courtship of Jane through the spring of 1536 turned the queen's removal from a possibility into a plan; Henry was betrothed to Jane on 20 May, the day after the execution, and married her on 30 May 1536.\n\nOn the criterion set out at the start, Cromwell was decisive for the form of the fall — the treason charges, the speed, the deaths of five men as well as the queen — but not for the fact of it. The miscarriage destroyed Anne's standing, Catherine's death removed the obstacle, Jane provided the alternative, and Henry made the decision. Cromwell was the ablest servant of a verdict already reached.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first what you are judging: who decided that Anne must go, or who arranged how she went.","Set Cromwell's fortnight of arrests against the reasons Henry had already turned against her.","One of your aspects should be the change in the diplomatic situation in January 1536, which the bullets do not mention."],"codes":["B3-2 — 2 The king's marriages: Reasons for the fall of Anne Boleyn","B3-2 — 2 The king's marriages: Reasons for marriage to Jane Seymour"]},{"q":"Question 3: 'Henry VIII's marriages in the years 1536-40 were made for the sake of the succession.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • Jane Seymour • Anne of Cleves. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit for weighing the dynastic motive (the Second Act of Succession of 1536, the marriage to Jane on 30 May 1536, Edward's birth on 12 October 1537) against the diplomatic motive for the Cleves marriage (isolation after the truce of Nice in 1538 and the publication of the excommunication, the treaty of October 1539, the wedding of 6 January 1540 and the annulment of 9 July 1540) and against personal preference (the marriage to Catherine Howard on 28 July 1540); a criterion for deciding a motive. Maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The succession explains the marriage of 1536 completely and the marriage of 1540 hardly at all. The right criterion is what the king actually needed at the moment each match was made, judged by the circumstances rather than by what was said afterwards.\n\nFor the statement, the marriage to Jane Seymour is decisive evidence. Henry had broken with Rome, executed Fisher and More and set aside two wives, and after the fall of Anne Boleyn his only children were two daughters. He was betrothed to Jane on 20 May 1536, the day after Anne's execution, and married her on 30 May; in July parliament passed the Second Act of Succession, which declared both daughters illegitimate and settled the crown on the children of the new queen. She was chosen because she was young, conventionally obedient, religiously conservative and backed by a gentry family rather than a hated faction — in other words, because she was likely to give him a son without a political storm. She did: Prince Edward was born at Hampton Court on 12 October 1537, and Jane died twelve days later.\n\nAgainst the statement, the Cleves marriage cannot be explained this way at all. By 1539 Henry already had his male heir, so the dynastic emergency was over. What he did not have was safety. Charles V and Francis I had made a truce at Nice in 1538, the sentence of excommunication was published in December of that year, and an attack on a schismatic England looked possible. Cromwell's answer was a German alliance: Anne's brother, Duke William of Cleves, was rich, quarrelling with the Emperor over Guelders, and connected by his sister's marriage to the Lutheran Elector of Saxony. Holbein was sent to paint her in 1539, the treaty was signed in October, and the wedding took place at Greenwich on 6 January 1540. It was a treaty, not a courtship.\n\nA third aspect, beyond the bullets, is the king's own inclination, which by 1540 was a motive in its own right. Henry disliked Anne of Cleves from their first meeting at Rochester on 1 January 1540, never consummated the marriage, and had it annulled on 9 July 1540 once Charles and Francis had fallen out and the alliance had lost its point. On 28 July 1540, the day Cromwell was executed, he married Catherine Howard, a young woman of Norfolk's family who could offer neither a foreign alliance nor any guarantee for the succession. At forty-nine, with an heir already living, he married to please himself and to please a faction.\n\nSo the judgement depends on the date. In 1536, with no legitimate son and a dynasty that had just lost two queens, the succession explains everything about the king's choice. By 1540, with Edward in the cradle, marriage had become an instrument first of foreign policy and then of appetite. The statement holds for the marriage to Jane Seymour and fails for the two that followed, so it can be agreed with only in part.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["The two marriages were made four years apart — ask what had changed in between.","Judge each match by the king's situation at the time, not by the reasons given later.","Bring in a third motive that neither bullet names, and let it decide how far the statement holds."],"codes":["B3-2 — 2 The king's marriages: Reasons for marriage to Jane Seymour","B3-2 — 2 The king's marriages: Reasons for marriage to Anne of Cleves"]},{"q":"Question 3: 'Thomas Cromwell transformed the government of England in the years 1534-40.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the Privy Council • royal finance. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit for weighing institutional change (departments of state instead of household government, the Principal Secretary, a Privy Council of about twenty with its own register from 1540, the Court of Augmentations 1536, the Act of Union with Wales 1536, the reorganised Council of the North) against continuity (the king still decided, faction still ruled access, parliament still met when summoned, Cromwell's own fall in 1540); a criterion distinguishing machinery from power. Maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Cromwell changed the machinery of English government profoundly and changed the location of power in it hardly at all. The criterion for judging the statement must separate those two things: who did the work of governing, and who decided what the government would do.\n\nThe case for the statement begins with the Council. The large, loose medieval body of royal councillors, whose membership ran into many dozens of men, gave way under Cromwell to a compact Privy Council of about twenty, whose members sat by virtue of the offices they held, which met regularly and which kept a register of its own business from 1540. Alongside it, the office of Principal Secretary, which Cromwell held from 1534, was transformed from a clerkship into the point through which all major business passed. Government was moving out of the king's household, where personal access had decided everything, and into departments of state with their own officers and records.\n\nRoyal finance shows the same pattern. The Act of First Fruits and Tenths of 1534 took the clergy's payments to Rome for the Crown; the Valor Ecclesiasticus of 1535 valued the Church living by living; the act of 1536 dissolved the houses worth less than £200 a year and by 1540 every religious house in England and Wales had gone. Because the Exchequer could not manage so much new property, the Court of Augmentations was created in 1536 with its own treasurer, auditors and receivers. New revenue was administered by new institutions rather than by the old household officers.\n\nA third aspect, beyond the bullets, is the reach of government. The Act of Union of 1536 divided Wales into shires on the English pattern, gave its counties and boroughs members at Westminster and put English law into its courts; the Council of the North was reorganised after the northern rising of 1536; and the break with Rome itself was carried by statute, so that the king in parliament, not the king alone, became the sovereign authority in religion as well as in law.\n\nAgainst the statement, the change can be overstated. England remained a personal monarchy: Henry chose his ministers, gave or withheld his favour, and reversed policy when he pleased. The Six Articles of 1539 show that religious policy followed the king's conscience, not his Secretary's. Access and faction still decided careers, which is precisely how Norfolk and Gardiner destroyed Cromwell in the summer of 1540. Much of the tidying was completed after his death, and a minister who could be arrested at the Council table on 10 June and beheaded on 28 July 1540 had plainly not made himself, or his institutions, independent of the king's will.\n\nOn the criterion set out at the start, the statement should be agreed with in a qualified form. The instruments of government were changed permanently — departments, a working Privy Council, a financial court, a united realm of England and Wales governed by statute. Power itself did not move an inch: it stayed where it had always been, with the king, and Cromwell's own end is the proof.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Separate two questions before you write: what the government looked like, and who really decided things.","Give each aspect its named institutions and dates, and close each paragraph with an interim verdict.","Use Cromwell's own fall as evidence on one side of the argument."],"codes":["B3-2 — 3 Cromwell and government, 1534–40: Reform of government and royal finance","B3-2 — 3 Cromwell and government, 1534–40: The management and use of parliament"]},{"q":"Question 3: 'The failure of the marriage to Anne of Cleves was the main reason for Cromwell's fall from power in 1540.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the Duke of Norfolk • religion. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10, AO1 6. Credit for weighing the Cleves marriage (arranged by Cromwell, hated by Henry from 1 January 1540, annulled 9 July 1540, the alliance made pointless by the renewed quarrel of Charles V and Francis I) against the Norfolk and Gardiner faction and Catherine Howard, against the conservative turn of the Six Articles of 1539 and Cromwell's protection of Robert Barnes, and against the enemies made by the dissolution and by attainders; the king's own decision; arrest 10 June and execution 28 July 1540. Maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The Cleves marriage was the occasion of Cromwell's fall, the conservative faction was the weapon, and Henry VIII was the cause. The criterion for judging the statement is which factor the others depended on: remove it, and would Cromwell still have died in 1540?\n\nThe case for the statement is real. Cromwell had built the German alliance and had persuaded the king into a marriage he detested from their first meeting at Rochester on 1 January 1540; Henry married Anne at Greenwich on 6 January only because the treaty of the previous October left no dignified escape. Worse, the marriage was useless within months: Charles V and Francis I fell out again, so England was no longer isolated and the alliance bought nothing. That left Cromwell responsible for a queen the king would not touch and for a policy that had been overtaken. The annulment of 9 July 1540 was a public verdict on his judgement, and from the Tower he wrote the evidence that helped secure it.\n\nAgainst the statement, Cromwell had enemies who needed only an opening. Thomas Howard, Duke of Norfolk, led the conservative nobility, resented obeying a tradesman's son who had been made Earl of Essex in April 1540, and held a card no argument could beat: his niece Catherine Howard, whom the king noticed that spring. With Stephen Gardiner, Bishop of Winchester, he worked steadily on Henry's suspicions. The pattern is plain in the outcome: Cromwell was arrested at the Council table on 10 June 1540, condemned by act of attainder without any trial, and beheaded on Tower Hill on 28 July 1540 — the very day Henry married Catherine Howard.\n\nReligion gave the faction its charge. The Act of Six Articles of 1539 had reaffirmed transubstantiation, clerical celibacy and confession, showing how conservative the king had become, and Cromwell had gone on protecting reformers such as Robert Barnes, who was in open quarrel with Gardiner. That allowed his enemies to accuse him not of a mistake but of heresy and of abusing his authority as vicegerent, and heresy was something Henry could not overlook. Barnes was burned two days after Cromwell's execution.\n\nA fourth aspect, which neither bullet prompts, is the resentment Cromwell's own work had created. The dissolution had disappointed nobles and gentry who wanted patronage or land; families had been ruined by attainders he drafted; and the northern rebels of 1536 had demanded his removal by name. He had power but no faction, and no birth to fall back on.\n\nWeighed together, the Cleves marriage was necessary to the timing but not sufficient to the outcome. Henry had forgiven expensive failures before. What made 1540 fatal was that the marriage failure arrived at the same moment as a conservative religious tide, an attractive alternative wife from a rival family, and a king who had stopped needing a minister who could work miracles with statutes. The statement is therefore only partly true: the marriage opened the door, but Norfolk pushed and Henry decided.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask whether the marriage alone would have been enough to kill a minister Henry still wanted.","Give the Cleves failure, the faction and religion a paragraph each, with dates, and close each with an interim verdict.","Add an aspect the bullets do not name — think about who had been hurt by Cromwell's own policies."],"codes":["B3-2 — 2 The king's marriages: Reasons for marriage to Anne of Cleves","B3-2 — 4 The fall of Cromwell: Reasons for Cromwell's fall from power in 1540"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Which statute of 1533 barred appeals from English church courts to Rome?","a":"The Act in Restraint of Appeals."},{"q":"On what date was Anne Boleyn executed?","a":"19 May 1536."},{"q":"Which new court was set up in 1536 to manage the lands of the dissolved monasteries?","a":"The Court of Augmentations."},{"q":"On what date was Thomas Cromwell executed?","a":"28 July 1540."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Thomas Cromwell's origins and early career","code":"B3-2 — 1 Cromwell's rise to power, 1529–34: Personality and early career","open":false,"back":"Born at Putney in about 1485, the son of a tradesman. Served abroad in Italy and the Low Countries, then practised as a lawyer and moneylender in London. Sat in the parliament of 1523."},{"front":"Cromwell's work for Cardinal Wolsey","code":"B3-2 — 1 Cromwell's rise to power, 1529–34: Personality and early career","open":false,"back":"From about 1524 he was Wolsey's man of business, closing roughly thirty small religious houses to pay for Cardinal College at Oxford and Wolsey's school at Ipswich."},{"front":"The Reformation Parliament","code":"B3-2 — 3 Cromwell and government, 1534–40: The management and use of parliament","open":false,"back":"Met in November 1529 and was dissolved in April 1536, sitting session after session for over six years. Cromwell, MP for Taunton, drafted and steered the statutes that broke with Rome."},{"front":"The Act in Restraint of Appeals, 1533","code":"B3-2 — 1 Cromwell's rise to power, 1529–34: Handling of the king's annulment","open":false,"back":"Banned appeals from English church courts to Rome and declared England an empire whose king had no superior on earth, so Catherine's case could not be recalled."},{"front":"The Act of Supremacy, 1534","code":"B3-2 — 1 Cromwell's rise to power, 1529–34: Handling of the king's annulment","open":false,"back":"Declared Henry VIII the only Supreme Head on earth of the Church of England, with power to visit and reform it, and made the supremacy statute law."},{"front":"The Treasons Act, 1534","code":"B3-2 — 1 Cromwell's rise to power, 1529–34: Handling of the king's annulment","open":false,"back":"Made it treason to deny the king's titles by spoken words as well as in writing. John Fisher and Thomas More were executed under it in 1535."},{"front":"The fall of Anne Boleyn, 1536","code":"B3-2 — 2 The king's marriages: Reasons for the fall of Anne Boleyn","open":false,"back":"Miscarried a son on 29 January 1536; arrested on 2 May; condemned on 15 May for adultery and for plotting the king's death; beheaded on 19 May 1536."},{"front":"Jane Seymour","code":"B3-2 — 2 The king's marriages: Reasons for marriage to Jane Seymour","open":false,"back":"Married Henry on 30 May 1536, eleven days after Anne's execution. Bore Prince Edward at Hampton Court on 12 October 1537 and died on 24 October 1537."},{"front":"Anne of Cleves","code":"B3-2 — 2 The king's marriages: Reasons for marriage to Anne of Cleves","open":false,"back":"Sister of Duke William of Cleves; painted by Holbein in 1539; married Henry at Greenwich on 6 January 1540; the marriage was annulled on 9 July 1540."},{"front":"One reason why Henry VIII married Anne of Cleves","code":"B3-2 — 2 The king's marriages: Reasons for marriage to Anne of Cleves","open":true,"back":"Fear of a Catholic attack after the truce of Nice in 1538 and the publication of the excommunication; the value of a rich German ally quarrelling with Charles V over Guelders; Cromwell's wish to tie England to the reforming princes."},{"front":"The Court of Augmentations","code":"B3-2 — 3 Cromwell and government, 1534–40: Reform of government and royal finance","open":false,"back":"Created in 1536 to survey, collect and sell the lands and goods of the dissolved monasteries, with a treasurer, auditors and receivers of its own."},{"front":"The Privy Council under Cromwell","code":"B3-2 — 3 Cromwell and government, 1534–40: Reform of government and royal finance","open":false,"back":"The large medieval royal Council gave way to a compact body of about twenty men who sat by virtue of their offices; it kept a register of its own business from 1540."},{"front":"The Act of Union with Wales, 1536","code":"B3-2 — 3 Cromwell and government, 1534–40: The management and use of parliament","open":false,"back":"Divided Wales into shires on the English pattern, gave Welsh counties and boroughs members at Westminster, and made English law and the English language the rule in its courts."},{"front":"The Act of Six Articles, 1539","code":"B3-2 — 4 The fall of Cromwell: Reasons for Cromwell's fall from power in 1540","open":false,"back":"Reaffirmed transubstantiation, clerical celibacy, private masses, auricular confession and communion in one kind. A defeat for Cromwell and for the reformers he protected."},{"front":"The fall of Cromwell, 1540","code":"B3-2 — 4 The fall of Cromwell: Reasons for Cromwell's fall from power in 1540","open":false,"back":"Made Earl of Essex in April 1540; arrested at the Council table on 10 June; condemned by act of attainder without trial; beheaded on Tower Hill on 28 July 1540."}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 2 Question 3, 16 marks: 'The failure of the marriage to Anne of Cleves was the main reason for Cromwell's fall from power in 1540.')","items":["The paragraph: Religion mattered because it turned a diplomatic blunder into a capital crime. The Act of Six Articles of 1539 reaffirmed transubstantiation, clerical celibacy and auricular confession, and it showed that Henry's own beliefs had hardened while his Secretary's had not. Cromwell went on protecting men such as Robert Barnes, who was in open quarrel with Stephen Gardiner, Bishop of Winchester, and who was burned two days after Cromwell's own execution. That protection gave Norfolk and Gardiner a charge which no failed alliance could have supplied: not misjudgement, which Henry had forgiven before, but heresy and the abuse of the vicegerency in spirituals, so that the act of attainder of June 1540 could condemn him without a trial. On this aspect, therefore, the statement is too narrow. The Cleves marriage explains why the king was angry in the summer of 1540; religion explains why the anger could be turned into an execution rather than a dismissal.","Why this is Level 4: the point answers the conceptual focus (which factor was the main reason) rather than describing the Six Articles; the information is precisely selected — the act of 1539 and its three named doctrines, Barnes, Gardiner, the vicegerency, the attainder of June 1540; the reasoning is a chain, from the king's religious conservatism to the availability of a heresy charge to the form the fall actually took; and it closes with an applied criterion (what made execution rather than dismissal possible), which is the judgement bullet the 16-mark grid rewards.","How the caps work here: this paragraph develops the second stimulus point, 'religion', because the Act of Six Articles, Robert Barnes and the vicegerency are all ways into that prompt rather than ways past it. The aspects of content that go beyond the two prompts have to come from the rest of the answer — the collapse of the diplomatic case for the Cleves alliance once Charles V and Francis I fell out in 1540, the resentment among the nobility at a tradesman's son made Earl of Essex in April 1540, and the king's attachment to Catherine Howard, whom he married on the day of Cromwell's execution. The mark scheme prints 'Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content': what is counted is the number of aspects the answer addresses, not whether the bullets were used."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["The paragraph: Religion was also a reason why Cromwell fell. Henry passed the Six Articles in 1539 and they were very Catholic. Cromwell was a Protestant so this was bad for him, and his enemies did not like his religion. Norfolk and Gardiner were Catholics and they wanted him gone. In the end he was executed, so religion was important as well as the marriage to Anne of Cleves.","Which descriptor it meets: Level 2 on the 16-mark grid — 'an explanation is given showing limited analysis and with implicit or unsustained links to the conceptual focus', with 'accurate and relevant information' but only 'some knowledge and understanding of the period', and an overall judgement that is 'asserted or insecure'. The final sentence asserts that religion was important without saying why it mattered more or less than the marriage.","Which descriptor it misses: Level 4 needs information 'precisely selected to address the question directly'. This paragraph names no doctrine from the Six Articles, no date beyond 1539, and no person except Norfolk and Gardiner; 'Cromwell was a Protestant' is loose, where the strong version points to a specific act — protecting Robert Barnes — that made a heresy charge possible. It also never explains the mechanism by which religion killed him, which is the chain of reasoning Level 4 demands."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Cromwell's service to Wolsey, parliament and membership of the Royal Council","items":["Born at Putney in about 1485, the son of a tradesman; no university degree and no noble kin.","Spent his youth abroad in Italy and the Low Countries as a soldier, a cloth merchant and a clerk.","Returned to London and practised as a lawyer and a moneylender.","Sat in the parliament of 1523; returned for Taunton to the Reformation Parliament in November 1529.","Entered Cardinal Wolsey's service as his man of business from about 1524.","Closed roughly thirty small religious houses for Wolsey to endow Cardinal College at Oxford and a school at Ipswich — his training for the dissolution.","Survived Wolsey's fall in October 1529 and was elected to the Reformation Parliament for Taunton in November 1529.","Sworn of the King's Council by 1531 and given his first great office, Master of the King's Jewels, in 1532.","Chancellor of the Exchequer in 1533; Principal Secretary and Master of the Rolls in 1534; vicegerent in spirituals from 1535.","Lord Privy Seal and Baron Cromwell in July 1536; Earl of Essex in April 1540."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the fall of Anne Boleyn, 1536","items":["Married Henry secretly in January 1533; crowned queen on 1 June 1533; bore Elizabeth on 7 September 1533.","Catherine of Aragon died on 7 January 1536, removing the reason for keeping Anne.","Henry was badly hurt in a jousting accident on 24 January 1536.","Anne miscarried a male child on 29 January 1536.","She had quarrelled with Cromwell over friendship with Charles V and over how monastic wealth should be spent.","Mark Smeaton, a court musician, was arrested and questioned on 30 April 1536.","Anne was taken to the Tower on 2 May 1536; five men were charged, including her brother George Boleyn.","The charges were adultery with five men and conspiring the king's death, which made them treason.","She was tried and condemned on 15 May 1536; the five men were executed on 17 May.","Cranmer declared the marriage invalid on 17 May; Anne was beheaded within the Tower on 19 May 1536."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Jane Seymour","items":["Daughter of Sir John Seymour of Wolf Hall in Wiltshire; a lady-in-waiting to both Catherine of Aragon and Anne Boleyn.","Her brothers Edward and Thomas Seymour rose at court with her.","Betrothed to Henry on 20 May 1536, the day after Anne Boleyn's execution.","Married Henry on 30 May 1536 and was proclaimed queen on 4 June 1536.","Never crowned.","Religiously conservative; she helped reconcile Henry with his daughter Mary in 1536.","Asked Henry to spare the monasteries during the Pilgrimage of Grace and was sharply rebuked.","Bore Prince Edward at Hampton Court on 12 October 1537.","Died on 24 October 1537 from the effects of childbirth.","Buried at St George's Chapel, Windsor; Henry was buried beside her."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Anne of Cleves","items":["Sister of Duke William of Cleves, a rich prince of the lower Rhine.","Cleves was quarrelling with the Emperor Charles V over the duchy of Guelders.","Her other sister was married to the Elector of Saxony, the leading Lutheran prince, though Cleves itself had not joined the German Protestant league.","England was isolated after the truce of Nice between Charles V and Francis I in 1538 and the publication of the excommunication in December 1538.","Hans Holbein the Younger was sent to paint her portrait in 1539.","The marriage treaty was signed in October 1539 and Anne landed at Deal in December 1539.","Henry rode to Rochester to surprise her on 1 January 1540 and disliked her immediately.","They were married at Greenwich on 6 January 1540; the marriage was never consummated.","The marriage was annulled on 9 July 1540, with Cromwell's evidence from the Tower used to help secure it.","Anne accepted the settlement, was given Richmond Palace and Hever Castle and was styled the king's sister."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Duke of Norfolk and the fall of Cromwell, 1540","items":["Thomas Howard, third Duke of Norfolk, was the leading conservative nobleman at court.","He had commanded the royal forces sent against the Pilgrimage of Grace in 1536.","Stephen Gardiner, Bishop of Winchester, was his ally against Cromwell.","The Act of Six Articles of 1539 reaffirmed transubstantiation, clerical celibacy, private masses, auricular confession and communion in one kind.","Cromwell protected reformers such as Robert Barnes, who quarrelled openly with Gardiner.","Norfolk's niece Catherine Howard came to the king's notice in the spring of 1540.","Cromwell was created Earl of Essex and Lord Great Chamberlain in April 1540.","He was arrested at the Council table on 10 June 1540 and condemned by act of attainder, without a trial, for treason and heresy.","He was beheaded on Tower Hill on 28 July 1540, the day Henry married Catherine Howard.","Robert Barnes was burned two days later, on 30 July 1540."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Cromwell's government, finance and parliament, 1534-40","items":["Act of Supremacy, 1534: Henry declared the only Supreme Head on earth of the Church of England.","Act of Succession, 1534, and the Treasons Act, 1534, which made denial of the king's titles by words a capital crime.","Act of First Fruits and Tenths, 1534: the clergy's payments to Rome diverted to the Crown.","Valor Ecclesiasticus, 1535: a survey valuing every church living in England and Wales.","Act for the dissolution of the smaller monasteries, 1536: houses worth less than £200 a year.","Court of Augmentations, 1536: a new department with a treasurer, auditors and receivers to run the monastic lands.","By 1540 every religious house in England and Wales had been closed.","Act of Union with Wales, 1536: shires, English law, English in the courts, and Welsh members at Westminster.","The royal Council was reduced to a Privy Council of about twenty men, which kept a register of its own from 1540.","The Reformation Parliament sat from November 1529 to April 1536 over a series of sessions; the Act of Proclamations of 1539 gave royal proclamations statutory force within limits."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 2 Question 3 (16 marks): 'Henry VIII, not Thomas Cromwell, controlled the changes made to the Church in England in the years 1532-40.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the royal supremacy • the dissolution of the monasteries. You must also use information of your own.","items":["Decode the question: the concept is causation and significance — who drove the religious changes. You must weigh the king against the minister, and you must decide what counts as control: deciding that something should happen, or designing and delivering how it happened.","Criterion to state in the opening: control means the power to start a policy and to stop it. Cromwell designed and delivered; Henry chose and could reverse.","Paragraph 1 — for the statement (the king chose). Evidence: Henry wanted the annulment from 1527 and had already broken Wolsey over it; he took the title of Supreme Head in the act of 1534 and used it personally; he refused Jane Seymour's plea for the monasteries during the Pilgrimage of Grace; the Act of Six Articles of 1539 reaffirmed transubstantiation and clerical celibacy against his Secretary's leanings. Interim judgement: policy moved where the king's conscience moved.","Paragraph 2 — against the statement (the minister designed). Evidence: the Submission of the Clergy in 1532, the Act in Restraint of Appeals in 1533 and the Act of Supremacy in 1534 were Cromwell's legislative route, not Henry's; Cranmer's court at Dunstable in May 1533 could act only because the statute had closed the appeal to Rome; Cromwell held the vicegerency in spirituals from 1535 and ran the visitations. Interim judgement: without Cromwell's method there was a wish but no mechanism.","Paragraph 3 — the dissolution, where the two meet. Evidence: the Valor Ecclesiasticus of 1535; the act of 1536 taking houses worth less than £200 a year; the Court of Augmentations created in 1536; every religious house gone by 1540. Interim judgement: Cromwell built the machine, but the king took the money and set the pace.","Paragraph 4 — the aspect beyond the stimulus points: the political test of 1536-40. Evidence: the Pilgrimage of Grace demanded Cromwell's removal and Henry kept him; four years later Henry destroyed him by attainder on 28 July 1540 and the supremacy did not wobble. Interim judgement: the man who can be removed without changing the policy was never the one in control.","Evaluation thread to carry through every paragraph: separate design from decision. Return to it in each interim judgement so the criterion is applied, not just stated — that is the third bullet of the Level 4 descriptor.","Timing, about 27 minutes on this question: 4 to plan, 21 to write four paragraphs and a conclusion, 2 to check dates and names. There are no SPaG marks on Paper 2, but specialist terms — supremacy, statute, vicegerent, attainder — earn AO1 credit.","What the conclusion must say: agree with the statement, but on a stated criterion. Cromwell controlled the means — statute, courts, commissions, records — and without him the changes would have been slower, cruder and probably reversible. Henry controlled the ends, and the proof is that in 1540 he removed the designer and kept the design."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T07:57:32.505348Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"Every fix holds and nothing new is broken: question 1's key now reads 'a seat in the parliament of 1523' (Cromwell's 1523 constituency is unknown; Taunton was November 1529, as the cards and fact file already said), the worked examples are numbered Question 2 for the 12-mark and Question 4 for the 16-mark, the offices are correctly staged (Principal Secretary and Master of the Rolls 1534, vicegerent 1535, Lord Privy Seal July 1536 when he gave up the Rolls — the real dates are 8 October 1534 to 10 July 1536 and from 2 July 1536), the Second Act of Succession now follows the 30 May 1536 wedding, question 3's feature is the fixed council of about nineteen sitting by office and meeting with the court, and the Level 4 gloss states the cap as 'three or more aspects of content' and admits that the Six Articles, Barnes and the vicegerency develop the 'religion' prompt. The new question 5 checks out in every particular — the Supplication against the Ordinaries of 18 March 1532, the Submission of the Clergy of 15 May 1532, Audley Speaker from November 1529 and Lord Chancellor from January 1533, Canterbury's £100,000 praemunire pardon of 1531, the conditional Act in Restraint of Annates of 1532, the drafts of the Appeals act — and its stem matches neither the June 2023 B3 paper (1(a) features of government in 1509, 1(b) why the Pilgrimage of Grace failed, 1(c)(i) the Eltham Ordinances, 1(c)(ii) Cromwell's rise and service to Wolsey) nor the Paper 2 specimen (4(a) the Treaty of London, 4(b) why Cromwell fell in 1540, 4(c)(i) financial difficulties 1520-29, 4(c)(ii) the commissions of 1535); the two '…was the main reason for…' judgements name events and bullets neither paper uses. Roughly seventy dates, statutes, offices and figures re-checked across the kit, several on the web; no answer key is now wrong, and what remains is presentational."}}},"B3-3":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"B3-3","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This is the heart of option B3. Booklet B of Paper 2 asks you to describe features, to explain why something happened and to judge a claim about Henry VIII's England, and the years 1529 to 1540 supply the richest material in the option: the break with Rome, the statutes of 1534, the executions of Fisher and More, the dissolution of the monasteries and the largest rebellion any Tudor faced. Examiners set causation questions here because the causes are genuinely tangled (a marriage, a succession, money, faith and one minister's ambition) and judgement questions because the evidence really does point both ways. If you can rank the causes of the break with Rome, weigh the motives for the dissolution and decide how far religion drove the Pilgrimage of Grace, you can answer almost anything this key topic offers.","idea":[{"h":"Defender of the Faith: why the Pope's champion turned on the Pope","t":"Henry began as the papacy's best friend in northern Europe. His book against Luther, the Assertio Septem Sacramentorum, defended the seven sacraments and papal authority, and Pope Leo X rewarded him in 1521 with the title Fidei Defensor, Defender of the Faith. The campaign of 1529 to 1533 was therefore not an attack on Catholic doctrine but on the Pope's jurisdiction. Henry had married Catherine of Aragon, his brother Arthur's widow, in 1509; of their children only Mary, born in 1516, survived, and Henry convinced himself that the marriage was cursed because Leviticus forbade a man to marry his brother's wife. Clement VII could not annul it: imperial troops sacked Rome in May 1527 and the Pope was left in the power of Charles V, Catherine's nephew. So Henry took the decision out of Rome's hands. A top-level answer uses the title to make a point rather than to decorate: it shows that Henry's quarrel was about who governed the Church in England, which is why he could break with the Pope in 1534 and still burn Protestants afterwards.","code":"B3-3 — 1 The break with Rome: Henry as 'Defender of the Faith'"},{"h":"The Acts of 1534, and how Cromwell made them bite","t":"The break was made by statute, in the Parliament that sat from November 1529. The Act in Restraint of Appeals in 1533 declared England an empire whose king owed obedience to no foreign prince and stopped appeals to Rome, which let Cranmer's court end the first marriage in May 1533. Then came the two acts that matter most. The Act of Succession of 1534 settled the crown on the children of Henry and Anne Boleyn and required subjects to swear an oath accepting it, with its preamble denying papal authority. The Act of Supremacy of the same year declared the king the only supreme head on earth of the Church of England, without the saving clause about the law of Christ that the clergy had won in 1531. Cromwell, the king's vicegerent in spirituals from January 1535, supplied the enforcement: commissioners took the oath from the whole realm, and the Treason Act made it treason to deny the supremacy in words alone. A top-level answer shows the machinery working together, oath plus treason law, rather than listing the statutes.","code":"B3-3 — 1 The break with Rome: The significance of the Act of Succession and the Act of Supremacy (1534)"},{"h":"Elizabeth Barton and John Fisher: two kinds of opposition, one answer","t":"Barton was a servant girl from Aldington in Kent who began to have visions in the 1520s and became a Benedictine nun at Canterbury. Her prophecy that Henry would not remain king a month if he married Anne Boleyn gave popular, divinely sanctioned form to opposition, and she was believed. The government did not risk a trial: she was made to confess in public in November 1533, condemned by act of attainder and executed at Tyburn on 20 April 1534 with several of the churchmen who had promoted her. Fisher, Bishop of Rochester, was the opposite kind of opponent: the most respected scholar-bishop in England, Catherine's declared defender since the legatine court of 1529. He refused the oath in April 1534, went to the Tower, and was beheaded on Tower Hill on 22 June 1535, weeks after Pope Paul III made him a cardinal. A top-level answer contrasts the two, the visionary and the scholar, to show that Henry's regime silenced opposition wherever it came from, and asks which kind frightened the government more.","code":"B3-3 — 2 Opposition to, and impact of, Reformation, 1534–40: Elizabeth Barton and John Fisher"},{"h":"Thomas More: the significance of a famous silence","t":"More had been Lord Chancellor from 1529 and resigned on 16 May 1532, the day after the Submission of the Clergy surrendered the Church's power to make its own laws. He then said nothing at all in public about the king's marriage or the supremacy. Refusing the oath at Lambeth in April 1534 sent him to the Tower, but silence was his defence: he argued that the law took silence to mean consent, so no words of his could be treason. At his trial on 1 July 1535 Richard Rich gave evidence of a conversation in the Tower in which More was said to have denied that Parliament could make the king supreme head; More denied saying it, and was condemned and beheaded on 6 July 1535. The significance is not what he did but who he was: a former Lord Chancellor and a scholar known across Europe. A top-level answer weighs the deterrent effect at home against the damage abroad, and notes that after 1535 open opposition among the political class stopped.","code":"B3-3 — 2 Opposition to, and impact of, Reformation, 1534–40: The significance of opposition from Thomas More"},{"h":"What the Reformation actually did to the English Church","t":"Ask what changed for a parishioner, and the answer is mixed. Cranmer, Archbishop of Canterbury from March 1533, and Cromwell as vicegerent pushed reform: the Ten Articles of 1536 played down all but three sacraments; the Royal Injunctions of 1536 cut holy days and discouraged pilgrimages and the veneration of relics; the Injunctions of 1538 ordered an English Bible of the largest size in every parish church, required every parish to keep a register of baptisms, marriages and burials, and attacked images, and Becket's shrine at Canterbury was destroyed that year. The Great Bible appeared in 1539. But doctrine did not become Protestant: the Act of Six Articles of 1539 reaffirmed transubstantiation, communion in one kind, clerical celibacy, vows of chastity, private masses and auricular confession, and made denial of the real presence punishable by burning, so Cranmer had to send his wife abroad. A top-level answer measures change against continuity instead of assuming that a break with Rome means a Protestant Church.","code":"B3-3 — 2 Opposition to, and impact of, Reformation, 1534–40: Impact of the Reformation on the English Church"},{"h":"What a monastery was for, before 1536","t":"England and Wales had roughly 800 religious houses with perhaps ten thousand monks, nuns, canons and friars, and between a fifth and a quarter of the cultivated land belonged to the Church. To a village the house next door was not an idea but an institution: alms handed out at the gate, beds for travellers and pilgrims, an infirmary for the sick, schooling for a few local boys, and above all employment, because a house with a dozen monks might have several times that number of servants and hundreds of tenants on its estates. Monasteries also prayed for the souls of the dead, which was the service donors had paid for over centuries, and abbots of the greatest houses sat in the House of Lords. In the north, where the country was poorer and the crown more distant, the houses mattered more. A top-level answer uses this to explain the reaction of 1536: the Pilgrimage of Grace makes sense only once you know what was actually being taken away.","code":"B3-3 — 3 The dissolution of the monasteries: The role of religious houses in local communities"},{"h":"Why the houses were dissolved, and what Cromwell's commissions of 1535 were for","t":"Two commissions did the groundwork. The Valor Ecclesiasticus, compiled through 1535 by county commissioners, valued the income of every bishopric, parish and religious house in the kingdom, so the government knew exactly what the Church was worth and could draw a line at £200 a year. Alongside it, visitors including Richard Layton and Thomas Legh toured the houses with a set list of questions and sent Cromwell reports of broken vows, idleness, false relics and superstition, along with the plain fact that many houses held only a handful of inmates. Parliament used those reports in 1536 to dissolve the lesser monasteries while praising the great ones, which made the act look like reform. Behind the paperwork lay money, for a crown whose ordinary revenue could not meet the cost of war, and politics, since monks owed obedience to orders governed from abroad. A top-level answer ranks these motives rather than listing them, and notices that the reformist language was useful cover.","code":"B3-3 — 3 The dissolution of the monasteries: Reasons for the dissolutions"},{"h":"The impact: who gained, who lost, and what disappeared","t":"The act of 1536 took the houses worth under £200 a year; the greater houses were then surrendered one by one between 1538 and 1540, with the abbots of Glastonbury, Reading and Colchester executed in 1539 when they resisted, until Waltham Abbey, the last, surrendered in March 1540. The crown was the first winner: the Court of Augmentations, set up in 1536, ran the lands, and lead, bells and plate were sold off. But the crown did not keep the estates; most were sold, especially to pay for war in the 1540s, so nobles, gentry and merchants who bought monastic land acquired a direct interest in never restoring the abbeys. The losers were less visible: the charity, hospitality and schooling the houses had provided, monastic libraries, and the nuns, who were pensioned but forbidden to marry. Six new bishoprics were founded from the spoils. A top-level answer keeps the two columns side by side and judges by a criterion, such as permanence.","code":"B3-3 — 3 The dissolution of the monasteries: The impact of the dissolutions"},{"h":"Why the north rose in October 1536","t":"The Pilgrimage of Grace was not a single grievance. Religion came first for most of those who marched: the closing of the lesser monasteries had begun that summer, the Injunctions of 1536 had cut holy days, and rumours ran that parish churches would lose their plate and that taxes would be laid on christenings, marriages and burials. Politics came next: the rebels blamed low-born, heretical councillors, above all Cromwell and Cranmer, and their Pontefract articles of December 1536 demanded that the heretical bishops be punished, that Mary be restored to the succession and that a parliament meet at York. Economic grievance ran underneath: entry fines on tenancies, the Statute of Uses of 1536 which blocked the gentry's way of avoiding feudal dues, a subsidy being collected in peacetime, and a poor harvest in 1535. A top-level answer shows how the three strands reinforced each other, and explains why a rising that began at Louth on 1 October 1536 could spread so fast.","code":"B3-3 — 4 The Pilgrimage of Grace, 1536: Reasons for the uprising"},{"h":"How the rising unfolded, and why it ended the way it did","t":"Lincolnshire rose first, at Louth on 1 October 1536, spread to Horncastle and Caistor and gathered at Lincoln, but the commons dispersed within a fortnight once the Duke of Suffolk approached with a royal army. The news crossed the Humber, and in Yorkshire Robert Aske, a lawyer, gave the movement a name, an oath and a badge, the Five Wounds of Christ. The rebels entered York on 16 October 1536, Lord Darcy handed over Pontefract Castle on 20–21 October, and a host of perhaps thirty thousand faced the Duke of Norfolk's much smaller force at Doncaster. Norfolk, unable to fight, negotiated: a truce in October, then at Doncaster on 6 December 1536 a free pardon and the promise of a parliament in the north. Aske trusted the promise and went home. Bigod's rising in early 1537 gave Henry his excuse to break it; about 216 rebels were executed, Aske among them, hanged at York in July 1537. A top-level answer explains the collapse by the rebels' own loyalty to the crown.","code":"B3-3 — 4 The Pilgrimage of Grace, 1536: Key events of the uprising"}],"examples":[{"title":"One feature in each part: the Act of Supremacy and the Treason Act","setup":"The question is: 1(a) Describe one feature of the Act of Supremacy. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the Treason Act. [2 marks]. What you know about the Act of Supremacy: passed by Parliament in November 1534, it declared the king the only supreme head on earth of the Church of England; it dropped the saving clause 'as far as the law of Christ allows' that the clergy of Canterbury had attached when they accepted the title three years earlier; and it gave the king power to correct errors and heresies in the Church. What you know about the Treason Act: it came into force in February 1535 and made it treason to deny the king's titles maliciously in speech or writing, so words alone could now kill. The two parts are marked separately, and in each the marker gives one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about that same feature.","steps":["Answer 1(a) first and open with the feature in one plain sentence: the act put the headship of the English Church into the king's hands by statute, so the authority came from Parliament and not from the Pope.","Take the second mark in 1(a) with supporting information about that same feature: the wording made Henry the only supreme head on earth of the Church of England, and it dropped the qualifying clause the clergy had won earlier, so the headship was no longer conditional.","Stop 1(a) there. The extra point about correcting heresies is a second feature, and a second feature earns nothing in a two-mark part; you would be writing for no reward.","Begin 1(b) as a fresh answer about a different aspect of content: the act turned speech into treason, so a subject could be executed for what he said about the king's title rather than for anything he did.","Support 1(b) and stop: because denial in words was now capital, refusal to accept the royal supremacy was fatal, and Fisher and More were both condemned on evidence of what they were said to have spoken in the Tower. Four marks, two sentences a part, and nothing about why Henry wanted the powers."],"cues":["What single sentence tells the marker what the act did, before any detail?","Which detail about the wording shows the headship was unconditional?","You still have a good point about heresy — why is there no room for it here?","1(b) is about something else entirely: what did the Treason Act make punishable that had not been punishable before?","Which two named men show the effect of that change?"]},{"title":"Turning two bullets into three reasons on a 12-mark 'Explain why'","setup":"The question is: Explain why the monasteries were dissolved in the years 1536 to 1540. [12 marks] You may use the following in your answer: • the Valor Ecclesiasticus • the reports of Cromwell's visitors. You must also use information of your own. The mark scheme prints two caps: maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content. You know that the Valor valued the income of every church living in 1535; that visitors such as Richard Layton and Thomas Legh sent Cromwell reports of broken vows, false relics and half-empty houses; that the act of 1536 took houses worth under £200 a year; that the Court of Augmentations was created to run the lands; and that the crown's ordinary income could not pay for war.","steps":["Read the caps before you plan. Two bullets can only ever be two aspects, so the bullets alone cannot get you past 8 marks; your plan needs a third reason from your own knowledge before you write a word.","Build reason one from the first bullet, as information rather than as a cause on its own: the survey told the government precisely what the Church was worth and gave it a workable dividing line, which is why the first act could be drafted round a fixed annual value.","Build reason two from the second bullet and make it do causal work: the visitors' reports supplied the justification, because Parliament could be told that the smaller houses were beyond reform while the great houses were praised, which made confiscation look like a rescue of religion.","Build reason three from beyond the bullets, and make it the one that carries the argument: the crown needed money it could not raise from ordinary revenue, and the Court of Augmentations was set up to administer the new estates, which shows the purpose was permanent income rather than reform of monastic life.","Finish by linking the three instead of summarising them: the need for money supplied the motive, the survey supplied the means and the reports supplied the excuse, so the question 'why' is answered by a sequence and not by a list."],"cues":["Look at the caps. How many aspects of content do two bullets give you, and what does that cost?","The survey is a document, not a motive. What did it make possible?","Why would a government want reports of scandal before it confiscated anything?","Which reason is not prompted by either bullet, and what evidence proves it?","What sentence turns three separate paragraphs into one argument?"]},{"title":"Setting the criterion on a 16-mark judgement about Thomas Cromwell","setup":"The question is: 'Thomas Cromwell was the main reason the royal supremacy was enforced successfully in the years 1534 to 1540.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] You may use the following in your answer: • the oath of succession • the Royal Injunctions. You must also use information of your own. You know that Cromwell was the king's principal secretary from 1534 and his vicegerent in spirituals from January 1535, a layman set in authority over the whole Church; that commissioners took the oath under the Act of Succession from the political nation and the clergy in 1534; that the Treason Act made denial of the king's titles in words a capital offence from February 1535; that his commissioners surveyed and then visited the monasteries in 1535; that he issued Royal Injunctions to the parish clergy in 1536 and again in 1538; that Cranmer's court had already ended the first marriage in May 1533; and that the king himself approved every step and had Cromwell executed in July 1540.","steps":["Fix the test before you gather any evidence: for one man to be 'the main reason', the question is what would have failed without him, so sort the enforcement into the parts that needed Cromwell in particular and the parts any capable minister would have managed.","Apply the test to the machinery that was his: the oath was administered across the realm by commissioners working to his instructions, the Treason Act turned a refusal into a capital charge, and as vicegerent he exercised a layman's authority over bishops, parishes and religious houses. No minister had ever held such an office, which is evidence that the office was made to fit the man.","Give the other side its strongest form by asking who else could have done the work: the king chose the policy and would not be deflected from it, Parliament supplied the statutes, Cranmer's court had settled the marriage before Cromwell was vicegerent at all, and fear did much that no official could do.","Add the aspect neither bullet prompts, since Level 4 is closed to an answer that does not reach a third: obedience was widespread anyway, because almost the whole political nation swore, and the one great refusal, the Pilgrimage of Grace, was contained by Norfolk's negotiation rather than by any machinery of Cromwell's.","Judge by the criterion and say plainly what changes if you take him out: the supremacy would still have been declared and still have been obeyed, but the speed of it, the paperwork of it and its reach into every parish were his, so he was the main reason it was enforced so thoroughly and so fast, not the reason it was enforced at all."],"cues":["What would have to be true before one man could count as 'the main reason'?","Which parts of the enforcement were possible only because of the office he held?","Who else in the government could have done the same work, and what was already done before he held that office?","Which aspect is prompted by neither bullet, and why must your answer reach one?","If you take him out of the story, what changes and what stays the same?"]},{"title":"Choosing the point beyond the stimulus: the attack on Becket's shrine","setup":"The question is: Explain why Henry VIII's government destroyed the shrine of Thomas Becket at Canterbury in 1538. [12 marks] You may use the following in your answer: • the Royal Injunctions of 1538 • pilgrimage. You must also use information of your own. The mark scheme prints two caps: maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content. You know that the Injunctions issued in Cromwell's name in 1538 ordered the clergy to take down images that were the object of offerings and pilgrimage and to set up an English Bible of the largest size in every parish church; that Canterbury was the greatest pilgrim shrine in England and its offerings were rich; that the king was at Canterbury in September 1538 while agents dismantled the shrine and carried its gold and jewels away to London; that a royal proclamation in November 1538 declared Becket no saint but a rebel, ordered his images taken down, abolished his feast day and had his name erased from service books; and that Becket was an archbishop murdered in 1170 after defying Henry II over the rights of the Church.","steps":["Begin by marking the limit of the bullets: the Injunctions give you the instrument and pilgrimage gives you the target, but between them they are two aspects of content, which is a capped answer, so the third reason has to be planned before you write.","Make the first reason the official policy, and state it as policy rather than as an order obeyed: the government was attacking the whole practice of pilgrimage and the veneration of relics, and Canterbury was the shrine that proved the case, so pulling it down turned a general instruction into a public demonstration.","Make the second reason financial and keep it precise: the shrine had taken the offerings of pilgrims for centuries, its plate and jewels went to the king's use, and this was the same season in which monastic treasure was being carted to the Tower, so the attack on superstition was also a seizure.","Supply the reason neither bullet prompts and make it the pivot of the answer: Becket's fame was the fame of a churchman who defied a king and was honoured for it, so his cult was a standing argument against the royal supremacy, which is why the proclamation called him a rebel and struck his name out of the books instead of merely closing the shrine.","Rank the three so that the answer explains the timing as well as the act: the campaign against images gave the occasion and the treasure gave the profit, but the reason this shrine came down, and came down while the king was in the city, is that this saint taught that an archbishop might defy his king and win."],"cues":["How many aspects of content do the two bullets give you, and what does the mark scheme do about that?","The order covered every church in England, so why single out this shrine?","Where did the gold and jewels go, and what else was being carted to the crown that season?","What had Becket actually done in his lifetime, and why would that trouble a king who is supreme head of the Church?","Which of your three reasons explains why the shrine came down when it did?"]},{"title":"Building an interim judgement on the impact of the dissolution","setup":"You are writing one paragraph of a 16-mark judgement on whether the dissolution of the monasteries harmed ordinary people more than it helped them. You know that the houses gave alms at the gate, lodged travellers, ran infirmaries and taught some local boys; that they were large employers and landlords; that monks received pensions and many became parish clergy, while nuns were pensioned but forbidden to marry; that the crown sold most of the land, so nobles, gentry and merchants bought it; that new owners often raised rents and enclosed; that buildings were stripped of lead and bells and libraries were dispersed; and that six new bishoprics were founded out of the spoils. The paragraph must end with a mini-judgement, not with a list.","steps":["Choose one criterion for the paragraph and say it out loud in the first sentence: whether the change left an ordinary family better or worse served than before, judged over a generation rather than in the first year.","Put the strongest evidence of harm in precise form: the alms, the infirmary, the guest hall and the schooling all stopped at once and nothing replaced them, and the tenants who had held monastic land now faced landlords buying at market prices.","Give the other side its best case rather than a token sentence: the monks themselves were pensioned and many found parish livings, buyers of land invested in estates that had often been carelessly run, and part of the spoils endowed new bishoprics and cathedral schools.","Test both sides against the criterion you set: the gains fell mostly to those who had money to buy, while the losses fell on the poor, the sick and the traveller, who had no other provision.","Write the mini-judgement as the last step of the argument: measured over a generation the dissolution shifted resources from the parish to the propertied, so for ordinary people the harm outweighed the help, though the harm was uneven and fell hardest where a great house had stood."],"cues":["What exactly are you measuring, and over what period?","Which losses were immediate and unreplaced?","What is the strongest thing an opponent could say in reply?","When you apply your criterion to both sides, who gains and who loses?","How do you make the last sentence follow from the argument rather than repeat it?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Why did the Act of Supremacy of 1534 matter more than the clergy's acceptance of the title in 1531?","a":"The 1531 form was conditional, accepted by Convocation 'as far as the law of Christ allows'. The 1534 act was a statute of Parliament with no such clause, declaring Henry the only supreme head on earth of the Church of England, so the headship was unconditional and enforceable at law."},{"q":"What did the Valor Ecclesiasticus of 1535 actually record, and why was it needed?","a":"It recorded the income of every bishopric, parish and religious house in the kingdom. It told the crown what the Church was worth and supplied the figures that decided which houses fell under the £200 a year line in the act of 1536."},{"q":"Where and on what date did the rising that grew into the Pilgrimage of Grace begin?","a":"At Louth in Lincolnshire on 1 October 1536, where townsmen feared for their church's treasures because the king's commissioners were expected."},{"q":"How did the Act of Six Articles of 1539 show the limits of the Henrician Reformation?","a":"It reaffirmed transubstantiation, communion in one kind, clerical celibacy, vows of chastity, private masses and auricular confession, and made denial of the real presence punishable by burning, so the Church had left the Pope without becoming Protestant in doctrine."}]},"room":{"facts":["Booklet B, the British depth study, carries 32 marks. Question 1 is printed in two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each 'Describe one feature of…' for 2 marks and each on a separate aspect of content; Question 2 is 'Explain why…' for 12 marks with two stimulus bullets; then either Question 3 or Question 4, a 16-mark 'How far do you agree? Explain your answer.', also with two bullets. There are no sources, no interpretations and no spelling, punctuation and grammar marks anywhere on Paper 2.","Question 1(a) and 1(b) are point-marked, not levelled: in each part one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about that feature. A second feature inside one part earns nothing, and reasons or judgements earn nothing at all.","The stimulus caps are printed inside the mark scheme grids, and there is no cap at Level 2 on any question. On Question 2, the 12-mark question: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'. On the 16-mark judgement: 'Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","Level 4 is 10–12 marks on the 12-mark question and 13–16 on the 16-mark judgement. It needs an analytical explanation directed consistently at the conceptual focus with a line of reasoning that is coherent, sustained and logically structured, information precisely selected to address the question directly, and, on the judgement, criteria that are justified and applied in reaching the overall judgement.","AO1 and AO2 are interdependent: an answer displaying no qualities of AO2 cannot be awarded more than the top of Level 1 however strong the knowledge is. The 12-mark question splits AO2 6 and AO1 6; the 16-mark judgement splits AO2 10 and AO1 6, and in that grid the overall judgement is a third bullet in every level and counts as AO2.","Paper 2 lasts 1 hour 50 minutes for 64 marks, about 55 minutes a booklet, so booklet B's 32 marks get roughly a minute a mark with planning on top: about 5 minutes on Question 1's two parts, 20 on Question 2 and 28 on the 16-mark judgement, with planning taken out of that 28."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of Henry VIII's title 'Defender of the Faith'. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the Act in Restraint of Appeals (1533). [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked in two separate parts: one mark for a valid feature and a second for supporting information. In 1(a) credit the papal grant of the title, its reward for Henry's book against Luther, its defence of the seven sacraments and papal authority, or the irony that Henry kept it after the break. In 1(b) credit the ending of appeals to Rome, the claim that England was an empire, the transfer of final judgement to the English courts, or its effect on Catherine of Aragon's case.","sol":"1(a) Feature: the title was a papal reward for defending the Catholic Church. Supporting knowledge: Pope Leo X granted Henry the style Fidei Defensor in 1521 after his book the Assertio Septem Sacramentorum defended the seven sacraments and papal authority against Martin Luther. 1(b) Feature: the act cut off appeals from the English courts to Rome. Supporting knowledge: passed in 1533, it declared that England was an empire whose king owed obedience to no foreign prince, so Catherine of Aragon could not appeal to the Pope against Cranmer's ruling on the marriage.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Each part wants one thing about one topic, so decide which single fact you would put first if you had one sentence.","The second mark comes from detail about the same feature, not from a new feature, so pick a feature you can back with a name, a date or a body.","Two sentences a part: the feature, then the supporting detail, and nothing about why Henry wanted either."],"codes":["B3-3 — 1 The break with Rome: Henry as 'Defender of the Faith'"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the Act of Succession (1534). [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the Treason Act (1534). [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked in two separate parts. In 1(a) credit the settlement of the crown on the children of Henry and Anne Boleyn, the bastardising of Mary, the oath required of subjects, or the preamble's denial of papal authority. In 1(b) credit that denial of the king's titles in words or writing became treason, that it took effect from February 1535, that the penalty was death, or its use against Fisher and More.","sol":"1(a) Feature: the act settled the succession on the children of Henry and Anne Boleyn. Supporting knowledge: passed in 1534, it declared the marriage to Catherine of Aragon void, which made Mary illegitimate, and it required subjects to swear an oath accepting the new succession. 1(b) Feature: the act made words alone treason. Supporting knowledge: from February 1535 it was treason maliciously to deny the king's titles in speech or writing, so refusing to accept the royal supremacy could cost a man his head, as it did John Fisher and Thomas More.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["One part is about who inherits the crown; the other is about what can now get you killed. Keep them apart.","For each part ask what a marker could tick as the feature, and then what one extra detail proves you know it.","Two sentences a part, and no explanation of why Parliament passed either act."],"codes":["B3-3 — 1 The break with Rome: The significance of the Act of Succession and the Act of Supremacy (1534)"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of Elizabeth Barton's opposition to Henry VIII. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of John Fisher's opposition to the royal supremacy. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked in two separate parts. In 1(a) credit her visions and prophecies, the prophecy about Henry's marriage to Anne Boleyn, her popular following, her public confession in 1533, or her condemnation by attainder and execution at Tyburn in April 1534. In 1(b) credit his defence of Catherine of Aragon from 1529, his refusal of the oath in 1534, his imprisonment in the Tower, his being made a cardinal in 1535, or his execution on Tower Hill in June 1535.","sol":"1(a) Feature: Barton used religious visions to attack the king's marriage plans. Supporting knowledge: a Kentish servant who became a nun at Canterbury, she prophesied that if Henry set aside Catherine of Aragon and married Anne Boleyn he would not remain king a month, and she was condemned by attainder and executed at Tyburn in April 1534. 1(b) Feature: Fisher refused the oath required by the Act of Succession. Supporting knowledge: the Bishop of Rochester, who had defended Catherine's marriage since 1529, refused at Lambeth in 1534, was imprisoned in the Tower and was beheaded on Tower Hill in June 1535.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["One opponent spoke to the crowds and one to the learned; say what each actually did, not what each believed.","A date, a place or a title is what turns a bare feature into two marks.","Two sentences a part; do not drift into how the government answered them beyond the one detail you need."],"codes":["B3-3 — 2 Opposition to, and impact of, Reformation, 1534–40: Elizabeth Barton and John Fisher"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of Thomas Cranmer's work as Archbishop of Canterbury in the years 1533–40. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the Royal Injunctions of 1538. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked in two separate parts. In 1(a) credit the annulment of the marriage to Catherine of Aragon in 1533, the recognition of the marriage to Anne Boleyn, his part in the Ten Articles of 1536, his support for an English Bible, or his position under the Act of Six Articles. In 1(b) credit the order for an English Bible of the largest size in every parish church, the requirement to keep parish registers, the attack on images and shrines, or the discouraging of pilgrimages.","sol":"1(a) Feature: Cranmer used his archbishop's court to end Henry's first marriage. Supporting knowledge: consecrated Archbishop of Canterbury in March 1533, he declared the marriage to Catherine of Aragon null in May 1533 and upheld the king's marriage to Anne Boleyn days later. 1(b) Feature: the Injunctions ordered an English Bible in every parish church. Supporting knowledge: issued in Cromwell's name as the king's vicegerent in 1538, they also told the clergy to keep a register of every baptism, marriage and burial, and to preach against pilgrimages and the veneration of images.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Cranmer's first great act was a judgement in a court; say what he judged.","For the Injunctions, pick the order a parishioner would actually have noticed inside the church building.","Two sentences a part, and no comment on whether the changes made England Protestant."],"codes":["B3-3 — 2 Opposition to, and impact of, Reformation, 1534–40: Impact of the Reformation on the English Church"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the work of religious houses in local communities before 1536. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the Valor Ecclesiasticus (1535). [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked in two separate parts. In 1(a) credit alms and charity at the gate, hospitality to travellers and pilgrims, infirmaries and care of the sick, schooling, employment of servants and tenants, or prayers for the souls of the dead. In 1(b) credit that it was a survey of the wealth of the whole Church, that commissioners valued every living county by county in 1535, that it was made for Cromwell, or that its figures identified the houses worth less than £200 a year.","sol":"1(a) Feature: religious houses gave charity and hospitality in their neighbourhoods. Supporting knowledge: they handed out alms at the gate, lodged travellers and pilgrims, ran infirmaries for the sick and schooled a few local boys, and as landlords they employed far more servants and tenants than they had monks. 1(b) Feature: it was a national valuation of the wealth of the Church. Supporting knowledge: carried out for Cromwell in 1535 by commissioners working county by county, it recorded the income of every bishopric, parish and religious house, and its figures decided which houses fell under the act of 1536.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["For 1(a), think of what a villager would have missed the year after the house closed.","For 1(b), say what kind of document it was before you say what it was used for.","Two sentences a part; the reasons for the dissolution belong to a different question."],"codes":["B3-3 — 3 The dissolution of the monasteries: The role of religious houses in local communities","B3-3 — 3 The dissolution of the monasteries: Reasons for the dissolutions"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of Robert Aske's part in the Pilgrimage of Grace (1536). [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the impact of the dissolution on those who had been monks and nuns. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked in two separate parts. In 1(a) credit his leadership of the Yorkshire rising, the name 'pilgrimage' and the oath, the entry into York in October 1536, the negotiation at Doncaster, his acceptance of the pardon in December 1536, or his execution at York in 1537. In 1(b) credit the pensioning of the heads of the lesser houses in 1536 while their inmates were offered transfer to a greater house or capacities to serve as secular priests, the pensions given to the religious of the greater houses that surrendered from 1538, the move of many monks into parish livings, the bar on nuns marrying, the small size of most pensions, or the loss of a way of life.","sol":"1(a) Feature: Aske gave the Yorkshire rising leadership and a cause. Supporting knowledge: a Yorkshire lawyer, he called the rebels pilgrims and bound them by an oath, led them into York in October 1536 and negotiated with the Duke of Norfolk at Doncaster before persuading the host to go home in December. 1(b) Feature: when the greater houses surrendered from 1538 the religious were pensioned rather than simply turned out. Supporting knowledge: heads of houses received the largest pensions and many monks took parish livings, though in 1536 only the heads of the lesser houses were pensioned, their inmates being offered transfer or a capacity to serve as parish priests; nuns were pensioned but could not marry.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Aske was not a soldier; say what he supplied that the Lincolnshire commons had lacked.","For 1(b), the fate of a monk and the fate of a nun were not the same; choose one and support it.","Two sentences a part, and no argument about whether the dissolution was justified."],"codes":["B3-3 — 4 The Pilgrimage of Grace, 1536: Key events of the uprising","B3-3 — 3 The dissolution of the monasteries: The impact of the dissolutions"]},{"q":"Explain why Henry VIII turned against the Pope in the years 1529–33. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • Catherine of Aragon • the sack of Rome (1527). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6, levels-marked. Credit the failure of the first marriage and the lack of a male heir, Henry's argument from Leviticus and the validity of the dispensation, Anne Boleyn and her pregnancy, Clement VII's dependence on Charles V after 1527, the collapse of the legatine court in 1529, the Reformation Parliament, anticlericalism and the praemunire charge of 1531, the Submission of the Clergy in 1532, and the money the Church sent to Rome. Caps printed in the mark scheme: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"Henry's quarrel with Rome began as a quarrel about a marriage. He had married Catherine of Aragon in 1509, and of their children only Mary, born in 1516, survived. By the late 1520s Catherine was past childbearing, and Henry had persuaded himself that the marriage was cursed because Leviticus forbade a man to marry his brother's widow, which Catherine was, and that the dispensation granted for it had therefore been worthless. Only the Pope could undo a papal dispensation, so from 1527 Henry pressed Clement VII, and by the end of 1532 the matter was urgent: Anne Boleyn was pregnant, and any son had to be born in wedlock.\n\nThe second reason is that the Pope could not give him what he wanted. In May 1527 imperial troops sacked Rome, and Clement VII was left in the power of the Emperor Charles V, who was Catherine's nephew and would not see his aunt set aside. The legatine court that Wolsey and Cardinal Campeggio opened at Blackfriars in 1529 was adjourned and the case called back to Rome, and Wolsey fell that year for failing. Henry concluded that the decision had to be taken out of Rome's hands.\n\nThe third reason lies beyond the marriage, and it made a quarrel into a break. From 1529 the Reformation Parliament gave Henry a weapon: legislation, and resentment of clerical fees was already strong among the Commons; in 1531 the clergy bought a pardon from a charge of praemunire and accepted Henry as supreme head so far as the law of Christ allowed; in 1532 the Submission of the Clergy surrendered the Church's right to make its own laws, and the Act in Conditional Restraint of Annates threatened the payments sent to Rome. Cromwell's insight was that statute could simply declare the papacy's jurisdiction at an end.\n\nThe three fit together. The need for an heir gave Henry the motive, the Pope's captivity to Charles V made obedience useless, and Parliament gave him a lawful means, so the campaign against Rome was not a change of religion but a seizure of jurisdiction.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask first what Henry actually wanted in 1529, and who alone could grant it.","Separate the motive from the obstacle from the means; the third of these is where the top level lives.","Three paragraphs, each opening with the reason in one sentence and closing by linking it back to the break with Rome."],"codes":["B3-3 — 1 The break with Rome: Henry as 'Defender of the Faith'","B3-3 — 1 The break with Rome: The significance of the Act of Succession and the Act of Supremacy (1534)"]},{"q":"Explain why John Fisher and Thomas More were executed in 1535. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the Act of Succession (1534) • the oath. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6, levels-marked. Credit the oath required by the Act of Succession and both men's refusal at Lambeth in 1534, the fact that refusal was only misprision of treason, the Treason Act making denial of the supremacy in words capital from February 1535, More's defence of silence and the evidence of Richard Rich, Fisher's promotion to cardinal by Paul III, the execution of the Carthusian priors in May 1535, and Henry's need for public examples. Caps printed in the mark scheme: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"The first step towards the scaffold was the oath. The Act of Succession of 1534 settled the crown on the children of Henry and Anne Boleyn, and its preamble denied the Pope's authority, so swearing to it meant accepting the break with Rome. At Lambeth in April 1534 both Fisher, Bishop of Rochester, and More, who had resigned as Lord Chancellor in 1532, refused it. That refusal sent them to the Tower, but on its own it could not kill them: refusing the oath was only misprision of treason, which cost liberty and property.\n\nWhat made the difference was a second statute. The Treason Act made it treason, from February 1535, maliciously to deny the king's titles in speech or writing, so words alone were now capital. Fisher was drawn into saying plainly that the king was not supreme head of the Church; More kept silence and argued at his trial on 1 July 1535 that the law presumed silence to mean consent, and he was convicted on the evidence of Richard Rich, who reported a conversation in the Tower that More denied having had. The law had been rewritten so that the one thing these men would not do was the one thing that could kill them.\n\nThe third reason is political, and it is the king's own. Henry needed examples, because a supremacy denied by the most respected bishop in England and by a former Lord Chancellor known across Europe was not yet secure. In May 1535 three Carthusian priors, John Houghton of the London Charterhouse among them, were hanged, drawn and quartered at Tyburn for the same refusal, which showed that rank would not protect anyone. When Pope Paul III made Fisher a cardinal that spring, Henry treated it as a challenge and would not be seen to flinch: Fisher was beheaded on 22 June and More on 6 July 1535.\n\nThe chain is clear. The oath exposed them, the Treason Act made their refusal fatal, and the king's determination to show that no reputation was beyond the law decided that the sentence would be carried out.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Work out what penalty refusing the oath actually carried, and what that leaves unexplained.","You need a second statute to close the gap between prison and the block; find it and make it the pivot.","Three paragraphs in causal order, each ending by saying what that stage made possible."],"codes":["B3-3 — 2 Opposition to, and impact of, Reformation, 1534–40: Elizabeth Barton and John Fisher","B3-3 — 2 Opposition to, and impact of, Reformation, 1534–40: The significance of opposition from Thomas More","B3-3 — 1 The break with Rome: The significance of the Act of Succession and the Act of Supremacy (1534)"]},{"q":"Explain why Elizabeth Barton was seen as a danger by Henry VIII's government. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • prophecy • Anne Boleyn. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6, levels-marked. Credit the authority of visions and prophecy in popular religion, her reputation after her recovery and her entry into a Canterbury nunnery, the prophecy that Henry would lose his throne if he married Anne Boleyn, the timing during the annulment campaign, her contact with churchmen and with sympathisers of Catherine of Aragon, the public confession at St Paul's in 1533, and the use of attainder rather than trial before her execution in April 1534. Caps printed in the mark scheme: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"Prophecy was dangerous because ordinary people believed it. Barton was a servant girl from Aldington in Kent whose visions during a serious illness in the 1520s, and her recovery, were treated locally as miraculous; churchmen examined her, accepted her and placed her as a Benedictine nun at Canterbury, which gave her the standing of a holy woman rather than a madwoman. In a society where God's will was assumed to be visible in signs, a nun who claimed to speak for heaven could not simply be ignored by the government.\n\nWhat she said was the second reason, because it struck at exactly the point the king was most vulnerable. From about 1532 she prophesied that if Henry set aside Catherine of Aragon and married Anne Boleyn he would not remain king a month and would die a villain's death. That claim was made while Henry was pushing the annulment through Parliament and preparing a marriage he needed the country to accept. It turned resistance into obedience to God, and it was repeated and spread by the monks and friars who had taken her up, so it travelled far beyond Kent.\n\nThe third reason, beyond the bullets, is who was listening. Barton was said to have delivered her warnings to the king himself, and she had contact with men of weight, including Bishop Fisher, while her message was welcome to the supporters of Catherine of Aragon and of Princess Mary. A holy woman who linked popular belief to the great opponents of the divorce threatened to give opposition a leader the government could not imprison quietly.\n\nThe response shows how seriously the danger was taken. She was made to confess publicly at St Paul's in November 1533 that her revelations were invented, which was meant to destroy her authority rather than merely punish her; then, instead of a trial that would have given her a platform, she and several of her clerical supporters were condemned by act of attainder and executed at Tyburn on 20 April 1534. Belief, timing and connections together made a Kentish nun worth an act of Parliament.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Start with why anyone took a visionary seriously in the 1530s, before you look at what she said.","Ask what the timing of her prophecy did to the annulment campaign, and who else her words helped.","Let the government's own response be your evidence of danger; three reasons, then a closing link."],"codes":["B3-3 — 2 Opposition to, and impact of, Reformation, 1534–40: Elizabeth Barton and John Fisher"]},{"q":"Explain why the monasteries were surveyed and inspected in 1535. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the Valor Ecclesiasticus (1535) • Cromwell as vicegerent. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6, levels-marked. Credit the Act for First Fruits and Tenths of 1534 and the need to value every church living before the new clerical taxation could be collected, the commissioners appointed in January 1535 and the returns made by the summer, the Act of Supremacy and Cromwell's appointment as vicegerent in spirituals in January 1535, the exemption of many houses from a bishop's visitation because their orders answered to superiors abroad, the visitation from the summer of 1535 by Richard Layton, Thomas Legh and John ap Rice working to a standard set of questions, the reports of broken vows, false relics and half-empty houses, and the use made of those reports in Parliament in 1536. Caps printed in the mark scheme: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"The first reason is a tax. The Act for First Fruits and Tenths of 1534 gave the crown the first year's income of every church living and a tenth of it in every year afterwards, and nobody in England knew what those livings were worth. Commissioners were appointed in January 1535 and required to report within months, and what they compiled, the Valor Ecclesiasticus, valued the income of every bishopric, parish and religious house in the kingdom. A survey on that scale was not curiosity: it was the machinery a new tax needed before a penny of it could be collected.\n\nThe second reason is jurisdiction. The Act of Supremacy of 1534 had made the king the only supreme head on earth of the Church of England, and in January 1535 Cromwell was made vicegerent in spirituals, a layman set in authority over the whole Church. Many religious houses had been exempt from a bishop's visitation because their orders answered to superiors abroad, so sending royal visitors to them was the plainest possible demonstration that the exemption had gone and that the supremacy reached inside the cloister.\n\nThe third reason lies beyond the Valor commissioners, in what the government intended to do next. From the summer of 1535 visitors such as Richard Layton, Thomas Legh and John ap Rice toured the houses with a standard set of questions, and the answers they sent Cromwell alleged broken vows, relics used to attract the offerings of pilgrims and communities running with a handful of inmates. Evidence of that kind is only worth gathering to a government that means to act on it, and in 1536 Parliament was told exactly that story when it dissolved the houses worth less than £200 a year while praising the greater ones.\n\nSo the valuation came from the new taxation, the visitation came from the supremacy, and both were turned to a purpose neither had announced. An inquiry begun to price the Church ended by supplying the case against it.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the crown had just given itself the right to tax, and what it would have to know before it could collect a penny.","The second reason is about authority rather than money: what could royal visitors do that a bishop could not?","Three paragraphs, and a last sentence that says what the surveys and the visits were used for once they were finished."],"codes":["B3-3 — 3 The dissolution of the monasteries: Reasons for the dissolutions","B3-3 — 1 The break with Rome: The significance of the Act of Succession and the Act of Supremacy (1534)"]},{"q":"Explain why the Pilgrimage of Grace broke out in October 1536. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the dissolution of the smaller monasteries • Thomas Cromwell. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6, levels-marked. Credit the closing of the lesser houses from 1536 and the greater weight of monasteries in the north, the Royal Injunctions of 1536 and the cutting of holy days, rumours of taxes on christenings, marriages and burials and of the stripping of parish churches, hatred of Cromwell and Cranmer as low-born heretical councillors, the Pontefract articles, the Statute of Uses and entry fines, the subsidy collected in peacetime and the poor harvest of 1535, and the trigger at Louth on 1 October 1536. Caps printed in the mark scheme: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"The religious attack came first and hardest. The act of 1536 had begun closing the houses worth less than £200 a year, and in the north, a poorer region where monasteries were the main source of alms, hospitality, employment and schooling, their loss struck at whole communities. It did not stand alone: the Royal Injunctions of 1536 cut the number of holy days and discouraged pilgrimages and the veneration of relics, and rumours ran through the villages that parish churches would lose their plate and that taxes would be charged on christenings, marriages and burials. People concluded that their religion was being dismantled, and marched under the badge of the Five Wounds of Christ to defend it.\n\nThe second reason is political, and it explains the target. The rebels insisted they were loyal to the king and blamed his councillors, above all Cromwell, a low-born lawyer who had run the visitations and drafted the legislation, and Cranmer and the other bishops they thought heretics. The Pontefract articles drawn up in December 1536 demanded that these men be punished, that papal authority in spiritual matters be restored, that Mary be made legitimate again and that a parliament meet at York. Attacking the councillors let ordinary people rebel without admitting they were rebelling.\n\nThe third reason, beyond the bullets, is economic pressure on both commons and gentry. Landlords were raising entry fines on tenancies, the harvest of 1535 had been poor, and a subsidy was being collected in peacetime, which broke the usual understanding that taxation went with war. The gentry had their own grievance in the Statute of Uses of 1536, which blocked the device by which they had avoided feudal dues on their heirs, and that is why gentlemen joined the rising instead of suppressing it.\n\nThese strands met in one place. When commissioners were expected at Louth in Lincolnshire on 1 October 1536, a town that feared for its church treasures rose; that rising collapsed within a fortnight, but the news crossed the Humber into Yorkshire, where Robert Aske turned the same grievances into an organised movement.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["The bullets give you religion and one minister; the mark scheme needs three or more aspects of content.","Ask separately why the commons rose and why the gentry joined, because the answers are different.","Three reasons, then finish with the trigger that turned grievance into a rising in that month."],"codes":["B3-3 — 4 The Pilgrimage of Grace, 1536: Reasons for the uprising","B3-3 — 3 The dissolution of the monasteries: Reasons for the dissolutions","B3-3 — 2 Opposition to, and impact of, Reformation, 1534–40: Impact of the Reformation on the English Church"]},{"q":"'The main reason for the break with Rome was Henry VIII's need for a male heir.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • Anne Boleyn • the wealth of the Church. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6, levels-marked, no SPaG on this paper. Credit for the statement: the failure of the first marriage, Mary as the only surviving child, Henry's reading of Leviticus, the memory of disputed successions. Credit against: Anne Boleyn's pregnancy and the urgency it created, Clement VII's dependence on Charles V after the sack of Rome, the wealth of the Church and the First Fruits and Tenths, anticlericalism and the Reformation Parliament, and Cromwell's use of statute. Caps printed in the mark scheme: 'Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"A cause counts as the main one if the break would not have happened without it, and not merely because it is mentioned most often. On that test the need for a male heir is the necessary cause of the break with Rome, but it is not by itself a sufficient explanation, because it explains what Henry wanted rather than why he took jurisdiction away from the Pope.\n\nThe case for the statement is strong. Henry married Catherine of Aragon in 1509 and by the late 1520s the marriage had produced one surviving child, Mary, born in 1516, with Catherine past childbearing. England had no example of a successful ruling queen, and a king without a son felt the dynasty was at risk. Henry's own explanation was religious: Leviticus forbade marriage to a brother's widow, Catherine had been married to his brother Arthur, and the absence of sons looked to him like divine judgement, which meant the dispensation that had permitted the marriage must have been beyond the Pope's power to grant. He pressed that argument for six years, which is not the behaviour of a man inventing a pretext.\n\nAnne Boleyn sharpened the need rather than replacing it. She would not become a mistress, and Henry wanted children by her who could inherit, so the question became how fast he could remarry: she was pregnant by the winter of 1532, the couple married secretly in January 1533, Cranmer's court declared the first marriage null that May, and Elizabeth was born in September. Anne explains the timetable, but the underlying demand was still for a legitimate son.\n\nThe decisive objection is that wanting an heir could never, on its own, produce a break. Henry needed an annulment, and the only man who could grant it was Clement VII, at the mercy of Charles V, Catherine's nephew, after imperial troops sacked Rome in May 1527. The legatine court at Blackfriars in 1529 was adjourned and the case recalled to Rome, and Wolsey fell for failing. Had the Pope been free to oblige, Henry would have had his annulment and remained Defender of the Faith. The break came from the collision between a need and an impossibility.\n\nA third aspect, prompted by neither bullet, explains why the break went so far and lasted. From November 1529 the Reformation Parliament gave Henry a means: statute. Resentment of clerical fees made the Commons willing; in 1531 the clergy of Canterbury bought a pardon from a charge of praemunire and accepted the king as supreme head so far as the law of Christ allowed; the Submission of the Clergy in 1532 gave up the Church's right to legislate for itself; the Act in Restraint of Appeals in 1533 cut off Rome; and the Acts of 1534 completed the work. Money mattered too, since the First Fruits and Tenths of 1534 turned clerical taxation to the crown.\n\nMy judgement is a qualified agreement. The need for a male heir was the cause without which nothing else would have moved, so the statement is right about the origin. But it is wrong as a complete account: the break happened because the Pope's captivity made obedience useless, and it took the form of a permanent, statutory, profitable supremacy because Parliament, anticlericalism and Cromwell made that form available.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide what would make a cause 'the main' one before you start listing reasons.","Ask what would have happened if Clement VII had been free to act; the answer sets the limit of the statement.","Keep one criterion running through every paragraph so the conclusion is the last step of the argument."],"codes":["B3-3 — 1 The break with Rome: Henry as 'Defender of the Faith'","B3-3 — 1 The break with Rome: The significance of the Act of Succession and the Act of Supremacy (1534)","B3-3 — 3 The dissolution of the monasteries: Reasons for the dissolutions"]},{"q":"'Thomas More's opposition was the most serious challenge to the royal supremacy in the years 1534–35.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • More's refusal of the oath (1534) • his trial (1535). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6, levels-marked, no SPaG on this paper. Credit for the statement: More's standing as a former Lord Chancellor and a scholar known across Europe, his refusal of the oath at Lambeth in April 1534 and his committal to the Tower, his attainder for misprision of treason, the policy of silence that the government could not prosecute until the Treason Act, the trial in Westminster Hall on 1 July 1535 and the evidence of Richard Rich, and the damage his execution did to Henry's reputation abroad. Credit against: Fisher's opposition as a bishop who had defended Catherine of Aragon and was made a cardinal by Paul III in 1535, the open denial by the Carthusian priors executed at Tyburn in May 1535, Elizabeth Barton's popular prophecy before her execution in 1534, the taking of the oath almost everywhere else, and the fact that no opposition endangered the supremacy until the Pilgrimage of Grace in 1536. Caps printed in the mark scheme: 'Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"A challenge is serious in proportion to how far it could have stopped the royal supremacy or put it in danger, not in proportion to the fame of the man who made it. Judged that way, More's opposition was the most damaging to Henry's reputation but not the most dangerous to his supremacy, so I agree with the statement only in a sense it does not intend.\n\nThe case for More is strong and rests on who he was and on how he fought. He had been Lord Chancellor from 1529 until he resigned on 16 May 1532, the day after the Submission of the Clergy, and as the author of Utopia and the friend of Erasmus he was known to every educated man in Europe. Summoned to Lambeth in April 1534 he refused the oath required by the Act of Succession, because its preamble denied papal authority, and was sent to the Tower; an act of attainder later that year convicted him of misprision of treason and took his property. His weapon then was silence, and it was well chosen, because a man who says nothing gives the law nothing to punish. The government had to wait for the Treason Act, in force from February 1535, and even then could convict him in Westminster Hall on 1 July 1535 only on Richard Rich's report of a conversation in the Tower that More denied having had. That awkwardness is itself a measure of the challenge: the supremacy had to be defended by a disputed conversation.\n\nAgainst the statement stands John Fisher, whose opposition was more dangerous even if it was less famous. Fisher was Bishop of Rochester and had defended Catherine of Aragon's marriage in public when no other bishop would, and when Paul III made him a cardinal in 1535 an English refusal became an international quarrel. A silent layman in the Tower could be ignored for a year; a cardinal in the Tower could not.\n\nA second rival came from the cloister. In May 1535 three Carthusian priors were hanged, drawn and quartered at Tyburn for denying the supremacy outright, and further Carthusians followed them. Their refusal was open, corporate and repeated, and it forced the government to kill men whose holiness nobody doubted, which was far riskier than executing a politician.\n\nThe third aspect, prompted by neither bullet, is the scale of obedience around all of them. The oath was taken in 1534 by the political nation and by the clergy with very little resistance, Parliament passed the statutes without a fight, and no rising followed the executions. The supremacy was not actually endangered until 1536, when a northern host demanded that papal authority in spiritual matters be restored.\n\nMy judgement follows the criterion. If 'serious' means damaging, the statement holds, because the beheading of a former Lord Chancellor on 6 July 1535 cost Henry more abroad than the death of Fisher on 22 June or of the priors before them. If it means dangerous, Fisher's cardinal's hat and the Carthusians' public martyrdom were the greater risks, and the Pilgrimage of Grace was greater than either. More's true significance is the example his silence and his trial set: that no reputation, and no refusal to speak, was a defence.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide what 'serious' will mean — damaging to Henry, or dangerous to the supremacy — before you weigh any evidence.","More's weapon was silence; ask what the government had to do to get round it, and let the difficulty measure the challenge.","Set him against the other opponents of the same two years, and add one aspect the bullets do not prompt."],"codes":["B3-3 — 2 Opposition to, and impact of, Reformation, 1534–40: The significance of opposition from Thomas More","B3-3 — 2 Opposition to, and impact of, Reformation, 1534–40: Elizabeth Barton and John Fisher","B3-3 — 1 The break with Rome: The significance of the Act of Succession and the Act of Supremacy (1534)"]},{"q":"'Religion was the main cause of the Pilgrimage of Grace (1536).' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the dissolution of the smaller monasteries • the Statute of Uses (1536). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6, levels-marked, no SPaG on this paper. Credit for the statement: the closing of the lesser houses, the Injunctions of 1536 and holy days, rumours about parish plate and taxes on the sacraments, the oath and the badge of the Five Wounds, the restoring of expelled monks, and the religious demands of the Pontefract articles. Credit against: entry fines, the peacetime subsidy, the poor harvest of 1535, the Statute of Uses and the gentry, and hostility to Cromwell, Cranmer and low-born councillors. Caps printed in the mark scheme: 'Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"A cause is the main one if the rising would not have happened without it. Grievances about rents, fines and taxes were old and had produced no rebellion in the north; what was new in 1536 was the attack on the Church, so I largely agree with the statement, while arguing that the economic and political grievances decided how large the rising became and who led it.\n\nThe religious case rests on what the rebels chose to do and say. Closures under the act of 1536 had begun that summer, and in a poor region the houses were the chief source of alms, hospitality, employment and schooling, so their loss was felt by whole parishes; rebels in several places put the expelled monks back. The Royal Injunctions of 1536 had cut holy days and discouraged pilgrimages and the veneration of relics, and rumours ran that parish churches would lose their plate and that taxes would be laid on christenings, marriages and burials. The movement called itself a pilgrimage, bound its members by an oath and marched under the badge of the Five Wounds of Christ, which is not the iconography of a rent strike. The Pontefract articles of December 1536 opened with religion: the suppressed houses to be restored, papal authority in spiritual matters acknowledged, and the bishops the rebels thought heretics punished.\n\nThe economic case is real and must be given its weight. Landlords were raising entry fines on tenancies, the harvest of 1535 had been poor, and a subsidy granted under the act of 1534 was being collected in peacetime, which broke the assumption that taxation belonged to wartime. The Statute of Uses of 1536 blocked the device by which landowners had passed estates to their heirs free of feudal dues, and it gave the gentry a grievance entirely their own. That matters, because gentlemen such as Lord Darcy, who surrendered Pontefract Castle on 20–21 October 1536, and Sir Robert Constable joined instead of raising forces against the commons. Without them the rising would have been a riot; with them it became an army.\n\nThe third aspect, prompted by neither bullet, is political. The rebels insisted on loyalty to the king and attacked his councillors, naming Cromwell and Cranmer as low-born heretics who had misled him, and demanding a parliament at York and the restoration of Mary to the succession. This was more than a cover story: it expressed a northern conviction that a southern, novel and irreligious government was remaking the country without consent, and it allowed men to rebel while denying that they were rebels.\n\nWeighing these, the ranking follows from the timing and the scale. Economic hardship was chronic and the Statute of Uses annoyed a narrow class; neither had raised thirty thousand men before. The religious changes reached every parish at once and threatened things people believed their salvation depended on, which is why the rising spread from Louth on 1 October 1536 across Lincolnshire and Yorkshire within three weeks. So religion was the main cause of the outbreak. The other grievances were not decoration: they brought in the gentry who organised it and the tenants who resented their landlords, which is why a rising about the Church could put a host at Doncaster that the Duke of Norfolk dared not fight.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what was new in 1536, since old grievances had not produced a rising before.","Separate the cause of the outbreak from the cause of its size; they are not the same question.","Use what the rebels displayed and demanded as evidence of what they thought they were doing."],"codes":["B3-3 — 4 The Pilgrimage of Grace, 1536: Reasons for the uprising","B3-3 — 4 The Pilgrimage of Grace, 1536: Key events of the uprising","B3-3 — 3 The dissolution of the monasteries: The impact of the dissolutions"]},{"q":"'The dissolution of the monasteries was the change that affected ordinary people most in the years 1529–40.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • charity • the Great Bible (1539). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6, levels-marked, no SPaG on this paper. Credit for the statement: the loss of alms, hospitality, infirmaries and schooling, the disappearance of a large landlord and employer, the sale of land and new landlords, the buildings and libraries. Credit against: the Injunctions of 1536 and 1538, the cutting of holy days and the attack on pilgrimages and shrines, the English Bible and parish registers, the Six Articles of 1539, and the executions and oath-taking that followed the Acts of 1534. Caps printed in the mark scheme: 'Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"'Ordinary people' means the farmer, the servant and the widow, not the court, so the test is which change altered daily life most, and how permanently. On that test the dissolution wins, though not for the reason usually given: its effect was less on what people believed than on who owned the country around them.\n\nThe case for the statement begins with charity and provision. About eight hundred religious houses were closed between 1536 and 1540, first those worth under £200 a year and then the greater houses, surrendered one by one from 1538 until Waltham Abbey in March 1540. With them went the alms handed out at the gate, the guest hall that lodged travellers and pilgrims, the infirmary that took in the sick and the schooling of a few local boys, and nothing replaced them. Monks were pensioned and many became parish clergy; nuns were pensioned too but remained bound by their vows and could not marry.\n\nThe deeper effect was economic. The houses had held a great share of the land and employed servants and tenants far more numerous than the religious themselves. The crown did not keep the estates: the Court of Augmentations took them in 1536 and most were sold, so nobles, gentry and merchants became the new landlords, often raising rents and enclosing where an abbey had been an easy-going owner. That change was permanent, and it bound the purchasers to the Reformation, since restoring the abbeys meant giving back their land.\n\nAgainst the statement stands the change inside the parish church, which every person saw every week. The Royal Injunctions of 1536 cut the number of holy days, taking away days of rest, and discouraged pilgrimages and the veneration of relics; the Injunctions of 1538 ordered an English Bible of the largest size to be set up in every parish church and required the priest to record every baptism, marriage and burial, and shrines including Becket's at Canterbury were destroyed. The Great Bible, printed in 1539, was the first authorised English Bible, and it replaced the versions parishes had bought to satisfy the order of 1538.\n\nYet this second change was checked almost at once, which limits it. The Act of Six Articles of 1539 reaffirmed transubstantiation, communion in one kind, clerical celibacy, vows of chastity, private masses and auricular confession, with burning for denying the real presence, so the mass an ordinary parishioner attended in 1540 was much the same as in 1529.\n\nA third aspect, prompted by neither bullet, is fear. The Acts of 1534 required an oath and made denial of the supremacy treason, and the executions of the Carthusian priors, Fisher and More in 1535, and of three abbots in 1539, taught everyone that religion was now a matter the king would kill over. For most villagers that was a change in atmosphere rather than in daily life.\n\nMy judgement is agreement with a shift of emphasis. Doctrinally the Reformation had barely touched ordinary people by 1540, as the Six Articles show, and the English Bible's effects lay in the future. The dissolution, by contrast, permanently removed the largest landowner, employer and charity in many neighbourhoods and transferred its property to men who would never give it back. Judged by permanence, that was the change that affected ordinary people most.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Define who 'ordinary people' are and what would count as affecting them before you weigh anything.","Set the changes inside the parish church against the changes in landownership, and ask which lasted.","The Six Articles are the fact that limits one side of the argument; make sure your answer reaches them."],"codes":["B3-3 — 3 The dissolution of the monasteries: The impact of the dissolutions","B3-3 — 3 The dissolution of the monasteries: The role of religious houses in local communities","B3-3 — 2 Opposition to, and impact of, Reformation, 1534–40: Impact of the Reformation on the English Church"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In which year did Pope Leo X grant Henry VIII the title 'Defender of the Faith'?","a":"1521."},{"q":"On what date was Thomas More executed?","a":"6 July 1535, on Tower Hill."},{"q":"What annual income marked the dividing line in the act that dissolved the lesser monasteries in 1536?","a":"£200 a year: houses worth less than that were dissolved."},{"q":"What did the Duke of Norfolk offer the rebels at Doncaster on 6 December 1536?","a":"A free pardon and the promise of a parliament to be held in the north."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Defender of the Faith (Fidei Defensor)","back":"Title granted to Henry VIII by Pope Leo X in 1521, rewarding his book against Luther which defended the seven sacraments and papal authority. Henry kept the title after breaking with Rome.","code":"B3-3 — 1 The break with Rome: Henry as 'Defender of the Faith'","open":false},{"front":"Act of Succession (1534)","back":"Settled the crown on the children of Henry and Anne Boleyn, made Mary illegitimate and required subjects to swear an oath accepting it. Its preamble denied papal authority, so Fisher and More refused.","code":"B3-3 — 1 The break with Rome: The significance of the Act of Succession and the Act of Supremacy (1534)","open":false},{"front":"Act of Supremacy (1534)","back":"Declared the king the only supreme head on earth of the Church of England, with no saving clause about the law of Christ. The headship now came from statute, not from the Pope.","code":"B3-3 — 1 The break with Rome: The significance of the Act of Succession and the Act of Supremacy (1534)","open":false},{"front":"Treason Act (1534)","back":"From February 1535 it was treason maliciously to deny the king's titles in speech or writing. Words alone became capital, which is how Fisher and More were condemned.","code":"B3-3 — 1 The break with Rome: The significance of the Act of Succession and the Act of Supremacy (1534)","open":false},{"front":"Elizabeth Barton, the Nun of Kent","back":"Kentish servant turned Benedictine nun whose visions won a popular following. She prophesied Henry would lose his throne if he married Anne Boleyn; condemned by attainder and executed at Tyburn on 20 April 1534.","code":"B3-3 — 2 Opposition to, and impact of, Reformation, 1534–40: Elizabeth Barton and John Fisher","open":false},{"front":"John Fisher","back":"Bishop of Rochester and Catherine of Aragon's defender from 1529. Refused the oath in 1534, was imprisoned, was made a cardinal by Paul III, and was beheaded on Tower Hill on 22 June 1535.","code":"B3-3 — 2 Opposition to, and impact of, Reformation, 1534–40: Elizabeth Barton and John Fisher","open":false},{"front":"Thomas More","back":"Lord Chancellor 1529–32, who resigned after the Submission of the Clergy and then kept silent. Refused the oath in 1534, was convicted on Richard Rich's evidence and beheaded on 6 July 1535.","code":"B3-3 — 2 Opposition to, and impact of, Reformation, 1534–40: The significance of opposition from Thomas More","open":false},{"front":"The Carthusian priors, 1535","back":"Priors John Houghton of London, Robert Lawrence of Beauvale and Augustine Webster of Axholme refused the royal supremacy and were hanged, drawn and quartered at Tyburn in May 1535, before Fisher and More died.","code":"B3-3 — 2 Opposition to, and impact of, Reformation, 1534–40: The significance of opposition from Thomas More","open":false},{"front":"Thomas Cranmer","back":"Archbishop of Canterbury from March 1533. Annulled the marriage to Catherine of Aragon in May 1533, upheld the Boleyn marriage, promoted an English Bible, and had to send his wife abroad after the Six Articles.","code":"B3-3 — 2 Opposition to, and impact of, Reformation, 1534–40: Impact of the Reformation on the English Church","open":false},{"front":"The Act of Six Articles (1539)","back":"Reaffirmed transubstantiation, communion in one kind, clerical celibacy, vows of chastity, private masses and auricular confession, with burning for denying the real presence. Proof that England left Rome without becoming Protestant.","code":"B3-3 — 2 Opposition to, and impact of, Reformation, 1534–40: Impact of the Reformation on the English Church","open":false},{"front":"The grievances behind the Pilgrimage of Grace","back":"Religious: closures of lesser houses, fewer holy days, rumours of taxes on baptisms and burials; political: hatred of Cromwell and Cranmer as low-born heretics; economic: entry fines, the Statute of Uses, a peacetime subsidy and the poor harvest of 1535.","code":"B3-3 — 4 The Pilgrimage of Grace, 1536: Reasons for the uprising","open":false},{"front":"The work of a religious house in its neighbourhood","back":"Alms at the gate, lodging for travellers, an infirmary for the sick, schooling for a few boys, prayers for the dead, and work for servants and tenants on its estates.","code":"B3-3 — 3 The dissolution of the monasteries: The role of religious houses in local communities","open":false},{"front":"Valor Ecclesiasticus (1535)","back":"Cromwell's survey valuing the income of every bishopric, parish and religious house, compiled county by county. Its figures identified the houses worth under £200 a year that the act of 1536 dissolved.","code":"B3-3 — 3 The dissolution of the monasteries: Reasons for the dissolutions","open":false},{"front":"Winners and losers from the dissolution","back":"Winners: the crown, the Court of Augmentations, and the nobles, gentry and merchants who bought the land. Losers: the poor who had alms, travellers, the sick, monastic libraries, and nuns pensioned but barred from marrying.","code":"B3-3 — 3 The dissolution of the monasteries: The impact of the dissolutions","open":false},{"front":"Robert Aske","back":"Yorkshire lawyer who led the Pilgrimage of Grace from October 1536, called the rebels pilgrims, entered York, negotiated with the Duke of Norfolk at Doncaster, accepted the pardon in December and was hanged at York in July 1537.","code":"B3-3 — 4 The Pilgrimage of Grace, 1536: Key events of the uprising","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (booklet B, the 16-mark judgement)","items":["Question: 'The Pilgrimage of Grace (1536) was a serious threat to Henry VIII.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] You may use the following in your answer: • the Duke of Norfolk • the Pontefract articles. You must also use information of your own. What follows is one paragraph from the middle of the answer, putting the case for the statement and then testing it against the criterion the answer has set.","Measured by force on the ground the threat was as serious as any Tudor king met, and the government behaved as though it knew it. By late October 1536 the pilgrims held York, which they had entered on 16 October, and Pontefract Castle, which Lord Darcy gave up on 20–21 October with the Archbishop of York and a company of gentlemen sheltering inside it; the host that faced the Duke of Norfolk near Doncaster was reckoned at about thirty thousand men against a royal force roughly a quarter of that size. Henry had no standing army, the northern levies were the very men standing in front of Norfolk, and Norfolk's answer on 27 October was a truce, followed on 6 December by a free pardon and the promise of a parliament in the north: terms no king grants to a riot. The criterion I am using, however, is not the size of the host but whether the rising could have forced the crown to change its policy, and on that test the threat was smaller than it looked. The pilgrims came as petitioners. They swore an oath of loyalty to the king, marched under the Five Wounds of Christ rather than behind a claimant, drew up the Pontefract articles in December as demands to be granted rather than as terms to be imposed, and went home on a promise. An army that will not fight its king cannot compel him, and the negotiation cost the crown nothing it could not take back: Bigod's rising early in 1537 was treated as breaking the pardon, martial law followed, and about 216 people were executed. The threat was therefore serious in scale and short in life, because the loyalty that raised the host was the same loyalty that disarmed it.","Why this is Level 4: the explanation is directed consistently at the conceptual focus, which is how serious a threat was rather than what the rebels did, and the line of reasoning runs from the case for the statement through the test to the qualification without a break. The information is precisely selected rather than piled up, since the entry into York, the surrender of Pontefract, the numbers at Doncaster, the truce of 27 October, the pardon of 6 December, the Pontefract articles, Bigod's rising and the executions each carry a step of the argument. The criterion for judgement is stated and applied — whether the rising could have forced a change of policy — and the closing sentence answers 'how far' rather than 'what happened'. It also addresses more than the two stimulus bullets, which the mark scheme requires before Level 4 is available at all."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["The same question, answered at Level 2. Read it beside the Level 4 paragraph above and look for what is missing rather than for what is wrong.","The Pilgrimage of Grace was a serious threat because a lot of people in the north joined it and the king did not have enough soldiers to fight them, so the Duke of Norfolk had to talk to the rebels instead. They were angry about religion and about the monasteries being closed down and they wrote a list of demands and gave it to him. Henry promised them a pardon and they went home again, and after that the leaders were executed anyway. So it was a big threat to Henry, but he won in the end, so maybe it was not as serious as it looked.","Why this is only Level 2: it meets the descriptor 'an explanation is given showing limited analysis and with implicit or unsustained links to the conceptual focus of the question', because the size of the rising and the negotiation are both offered as signs of a threat, but neither is linked to what would count as one. It misses the higher descriptors in three ways. The information is generalised rather than 'accurate and relevant information precisely selected to address the question directly': there is no date, no number, no named place and no mention of what the demands actually said. The line of reasoning is not sustained, because the last sentence changes side without explaining why. And the judgement, 'maybe it was not as serious as it looked', is exactly what the Level 2 descriptor calls an overall judgement whose 'justification is asserted or insecure', since no criterion for seriousness is ever offered."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the break with Rome and the Acts of 1534","items":["1521: Pope Leo X granted Henry the title Fidei Defensor, Defender of the Faith, after his book the Assertio Septem Sacramentorum attacked Luther and defended the seven sacraments.","Henry married Catherine of Aragon, his brother Arthur's widow, in 1509; of their children only Mary, born 1516, survived infancy.","May 1527: imperial troops sacked Rome, leaving Pope Clement VII in the power of Charles V, Catherine of Aragon's nephew.","1529: the legatine court of Wolsey and Cardinal Campeggio at Blackfriars was adjourned and the case recalled to Rome; Wolsey fell that autumn.","November 1529: the Reformation Parliament first met; it sat until 1536 and passed the legislation of the break.","1531: the clergy of the province of Canterbury bought a pardon from a charge of praemunire and accepted Henry as supreme head of the Church 'as far as the law of Christ allows'.","May 1532: the Submission of the Clergy gave up Convocation's right to make canon law without royal assent; Thomas More resigned as Lord Chancellor the next day.","1532: the Act in Conditional Restraint of Annates threatened to stop the payments English bishops made to Rome on appointment.","March 1533: Thomas Cranmer was consecrated Archbishop of Canterbury; in May he declared the marriage to Catherine null and the marriage to Anne Boleyn valid. Elizabeth was born in September 1533.","1533: the Act in Restraint of Appeals declared England an empire whose king owed obedience to no foreign prince, and ended appeals to Rome.","1534: the Act of Succession settled the crown on the children of Henry and Anne Boleyn, bastardised Mary and required an oath from the king's subjects.","1534: the Act of Supremacy declared the king the only supreme head on earth of the Church of England, with no saving clause.","1534: the Act for First Fruits and Tenths gave the crown the first year's income of a new church living and a tenth of clerical income thereafter.","The Treason Act, in force from February 1535, made it treason maliciously to deny the king's titles in speech or writing.","January 1535: Cromwell was made the king's vicegerent in spirituals, giving a layman authority over the Church under the king."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Elizabeth Barton and John Fisher","items":["Elizabeth Barton was a servant girl from Aldington in Kent who began to have visions during a serious illness in the 1520s.","Her recovery was treated as miraculous; churchmen examined and accepted her, and she became a Benedictine nun at Canterbury.","From about 1532 she prophesied that if Henry set aside Catherine of Aragon and married Anne Boleyn he would not remain king a month and would die a villain's death.","Her sayings were spread by the monks and friars who supported her, and she was said to have delivered her warnings to the king himself.","November 1533: she was made to confess publicly at St Paul's that her revelations were invented.","She was condemned by act of attainder, without trial, and executed at Tyburn on 20 April 1534 along with several of the clergy who had promoted her.","John Fisher was Bishop of Rochester and Chancellor of the University of Cambridge, the most respected scholar-bishop in England.","He spoke for Catherine of Aragon at the legatine court in 1529 and opposed the annulment from the start.","April 1534: he refused the oath required by the Act of Succession at Lambeth and was imprisoned in the Tower.","May 1535: Pope Paul III made him a cardinal, which Henry treated as a provocation.","22 June 1535: Fisher was beheaded on Tower Hill.","Both cases show the government's method: destroy the opponent's credit first if possible, then use statute — attainder for Barton, the Treason Act for Fisher."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Thomas More","items":["Lawyer, humanist scholar and author of Utopia, and a friend of Erasmus with a European reputation.","Lord Chancellor from October 1529, the first layman to hold the office for many years, succeeding Wolsey.","He resigned on 16 May 1532, the day after the Submission of the Clergy, pleading ill health.","After resigning he said nothing in public about the annulment or the supremacy; silence was his deliberate policy.","April 1534: he refused the oath under the Act of Succession at Lambeth, saying he could not swear to the preamble, and was sent to the Tower.","Refusing the oath was misprision of treason, which cost liberty and property but not life.","The Treason Act of 1534 changed that from February 1535 by making denial of the royal supremacy in words a capital offence.","1 July 1535: tried in Westminster Hall and convicted largely on the evidence of Richard Rich, who reported a conversation in the Tower that More denied having had.","At his trial More argued that the law presumed silence to mean consent, so his silence could not be treason.","6 July 1535: beheaded on Tower Hill.","Significance: after the deaths of the Carthusian priors, Fisher and More in 1535, open opposition to the supremacy among the political class stopped.","Cost: the executions damaged Henry's reputation across Europe, where both men were widely admired."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Thomas Cranmer and the Reformation in the English Church","items":["Cranmer suggested canvassing the universities of Europe for opinions on the king's marriage, which gave the annulment campaign an academic front.","Consecrated Archbishop of Canterbury in March 1533, after the death of William Warham the previous year.","May 1533: his court declared the marriage to Catherine of Aragon null and then upheld the marriage to Anne Boleyn.","Cromwell as vicegerent in spirituals, not Cranmer, issued the Royal Injunctions in the king's name.","The Ten Articles of 1536 gave prominence to only three sacraments — baptism, the eucharist and penance.","The Royal Injunctions of 1536 reduced the number of holy days and discouraged pilgrimages and the veneration of relics, and told the clergy to teach the Lord's Prayer, the Creed and the Ten Commandments in English.","The Royal Injunctions of 1538 ordered an English Bible of the largest size in every parish church, required a register of every baptism, marriage and burial, and attacked images and shrines.","September 1538: the shrine of Thomas Becket at Canterbury was destroyed and its treasure taken.","The Great Bible, the authorised English Bible, was printed in 1539.","The Act of Six Articles of 1539 reaffirmed transubstantiation, communion in one kind, clerical celibacy, vows of chastity, private masses and auricular confession; denying the real presence was punishable by burning.","Cranmer, who had married in Germany, had to send his wife abroad after the Six Articles because clerical celibacy was enforced.","The net result by 1540: papal jurisdiction gone, monasteries gone, an English Bible in church, but Catholic doctrine and the mass intact."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Cromwell's commissions of 1535 and the dissolution","items":["Before 1536 there were roughly 800 religious houses in England and Wales with perhaps ten thousand monks, nuns, canons and friars.","Between a fifth and a quarter of the cultivated land of England belonged to the Church, much of it monastic.","The Valor Ecclesiasticus was compiled through 1535 by commissioners working county by county, valuing the income of every bishopric, parish and religious house.","The visitation of the monasteries began in the summer of 1535; the visitors included Richard Layton, Thomas Legh and John ap Rice, and they worked from a standard set of questions.","Their reports alleged broken vows, idleness, false relics and superstition, and noted houses running with only a handful of religious.","1536: the Act for the Dissolution of the Lesser Monasteries dissolved houses worth less than £200 a year, while praising the greater houses as well governed.","1536: the Court of Augmentations was created to administer the confiscated lands and revenues.","1538–40: the greater houses were pressed into surrendering one by one; Waltham Abbey, the last, surrendered in March 1540.","1539: the abbots of Glastonbury, Reading and Colchester were executed for treason after resisting.","Lead was stripped from roofs, bells were taken down and plate and treasure were carried to the crown.","Most of the land was sold rather than kept, especially to fund war in the 1540s, so nobles, gentry and merchants gained a lasting interest in the Reformation.","Pensions: in 1536 the heads of the lesser houses were pensioned while their inmates were offered transfer to a greater house or a capacity to serve as secular priests; from the surrenders of 1538 heads of houses received generous pensions and ordinary monks smaller ones, many became parish clergy, and nuns were pensioned but could not marry.","Six new bishoprics, including Gloucester, Peterborough and Bristol, were created out of former monastic property in the years after the dissolution.","Losses: monastic charity, hospitality and schooling, and the libraries, whose manuscripts were scattered or destroyed."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Pilgrimage of Grace, 1536 — Robert Aske and the Duke of Norfolk","items":["1 October 1536: the Lincolnshire rising began at Louth, where townsmen feared for their church's treasures as commissioners were expected.","The rising spread to Horncastle and Caistor and gathered at Lincoln, but dispersed within a fortnight as the Duke of Suffolk approached with a royal army.","Robert Aske, a Yorkshire lawyer, gave the Yorkshire rising its name, its oath and its badge, the Five Wounds of Christ, and called its members pilgrims.","16 October 1536: the rebels entered York, and expelled monks were restored in some houses.","20–21 October 1536: Lord Darcy surrendered Pontefract Castle to the rebels and joined them.","A rebel host of perhaps thirty thousand faced the Duke of Norfolk at Doncaster; his force was far smaller and he negotiated a truce rather than fight.","December 1536: the Pontefract articles set out the rebels' demands — the restoration of suppressed houses, papal authority in spiritual matters, the punishment of heretical bishops, the removal of low-born councillors, the restoration of Mary to the succession, the repeal of the Statute of Uses and a parliament at York.","6 December 1536: at Doncaster Norfolk offered a free pardon and a parliament in the north; Aske accepted and the host dispersed.","Christmas 1536: Aske was invited to court and met the king, who treated him graciously.","Early 1537: Sir Francis Bigod's rising in Yorkshire gave Henry the excuse to treat the pardon as broken.","Norfolk returned with martial law; about 216 people were executed in the aftermath.","30 June 1537: Lord Darcy was beheaded on Tower Hill; Sir Robert Constable was hanged at Hull.","12 July 1537: Robert Aske was hanged at York.","Why it failed: the rebels' loyalty to the king made them trust a royal pardon, they had no plan for fighting, and Norfolk's negotiation bought the time the government needed."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — the 16-mark judgement: 'The Reformation changed the English Church very little in the years 1529–40.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks)","items":["The question as printed, Question 3 or Question 4 in booklet B: 'The Reformation changed the English Church very little in the years 1529–40.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] You may use the following in your answer: • the Act of Six Articles (1539) • the Royal Injunctions. You must also use information of your own. There are no spelling, punctuation and grammar marks on Paper 2, so every minute goes on the argument.","Decode it. The concept is change and continuity, and 'very little' means you must measure, not describe. Announce the measure in the opening: change of authority, change of practice and change of doctrine are three different things, and the answer to the question depends on which you weigh. Name the aspects you will use — the supremacy and the monasteries, the parish church, doctrine — so the line of reasoning is visible from the first sentence.","Opening, three or four sentences: separate authority, practice and doctrine; say that the statement is true of doctrine and false of authority; and signal that the verdict will turn on which of the three touched people most.","Paragraph 1, against the statement — authority changed completely. The Act in Restraint of Appeals of 1533 ended appeals to Rome; the Act of Supremacy of 1534 made the king the only supreme head on earth of the Church of England; Cromwell became vicegerent in spirituals in 1535, a layman set over the Church; the Act for First Fruits and Tenths of 1534 diverted clerical taxation to the crown. Interim judgement: whatever was still believed, the Church was now a department of royal government.","Paragraph 2, against the statement — the physical Church changed beyond recognition. Roughly 800 religious houses were dissolved between 1536 and 1540, the lesser ones under the act of 1536 and the greater by surrender until Waltham Abbey went in March 1540; the shrine of Becket at Canterbury was destroyed in 1538; lands passed to lay buyers through the Court of Augmentations. Interim judgement: an institution that had shaped English religion for centuries simply disappeared, which is not a small change.","Paragraph 3, against the statement — the parish changed too, which is the point beyond the stimulus if you take the Injunctions as prompted. The Injunctions of 1536 cut holy days and discouraged pilgrimages and relics; those of 1538 ordered an English Bible of the largest size in every parish church and a register of baptisms, marriages and burials; the Great Bible of 1539 was the first authorised English Bible. Interim judgement: from 1538 a parishioner could hear and read scripture in his own language in his own church.","Paragraph 4, for the statement — doctrine barely moved. The Ten Articles of 1536 trimmed rather than replaced; the Act of Six Articles of 1539 reaffirmed transubstantiation, communion in one kind, clerical celibacy, vows of chastity, private masses and auricular confession, with burning for denying the real presence, and Cranmer had to send his wife abroad. Interim judgement: the mass a villager attended in 1540 was substantially the mass of 1529, so in belief the statement has real force.","Three or more aspects of content: the mark scheme caps an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content at 11 marks and gives it no access to Level 4, so the supremacy, the dissolution and the treatment of opponents all need to appear alongside the two bullets. Without at least one aspect the bullets do not prompt, the answer cannot reach Level 4 however well it is written.","The evaluation thread: keep asking the same question in every paragraph — change for whom? The change in authority mattered to bishops and abbots, the dissolution to whole neighbourhoods, the Injunctions to parishioners, the Six Articles to anyone tempted towards Protestant belief. Run that test through so the conclusion is the last step of the argument and not a new claim.","The conclusion must decide and justify rather than summarise. Argue that the statement is defensible only on the narrow ground of doctrine, where the Six Articles show how little moved by 1539, but that on authority and on institutions the change was total and irreversible, because the lands were sold to men who would never give them back. Then qualify: England in 1540 was a Catholic country without a Pope and without monks, which is a very large change dressed in familiar clothes.","Timing: about 28 minutes, since Paper 2's 1 hour 50 minutes give booklet B roughly 55 minutes for its 32 marks. Roughly six to plan the four paragraphs and write the measuring sentence, twenty to write, and two to check that the last paragraph answers 'how far' rather than 'what changed'."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:05:37Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"The fix pass holds up on re-solving. The three duplications that kept the kit off the publish line are gone: examples[3] now works the destruction of Becket's shrine in 1538 (the 1538 Injunctions, Henry at Canterbury from 5 September 1538 while agents stripped the shrine, the proclamation of 16 November 1538 unsainting Becket - all verified), examples[2] a Cromwell-and-enforcement judgement that no bank question sets, and the model-paragraph pair a 'serious threat' proposition distinct from question 13's causation question. The new question 12 on More's opposition is a sound 16-marker with the correct caps, a criterion that is set and applied, and accurate content (Lambeth April 1534, the attainder for misprision, the Treason Act from February 1535, Westminster Hall 1 July 1535 and Rich's evidence, Fisher's cardinalate and 22 June, the Carthusian priors of May 1535, More on 6 July 1535), and it gives the 'significance of opposition from Thomas More' key idea a question of its own at last. The restemmed question 9 is right about the Valor's origin - commissions appointed in January 1535 under the Act for First Fruits and Tenths of 1534, returns due 30 May - and about the vicegerency of January 1535 and the exempt orders. Pontefract is now hedged to 20-21 October 1536 in all three places, the pensions are dated in question 5 and the fact file, card 7 matches its back, check[2] has a single answer and the Great Bible is described as the first authorised English Bible. No stem reproduces a 2023 B3 or Paper 2 specimen question (the closest neighbours - the specimen's 'Cromwell's commissions of 1535 were the main reason...' and the 2023 'Explain why the Pilgrimage of Grace (1536) was a failure' - are now further away than before), the form still matches the 2025 booklet B throughout, and I found no wrong date, figure or attribution anywhere in the kit. What is left is small: a leftover phrase in question 9's solution, worked example 0 still setting question 1's second part, and two places where the dated pension clause has not been carried through."}}},"B4-1":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"B4-1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This key topic is where the British depth study is decided for most students, because the questions on it are always about authority: how a queen with a disputed title, an empty treasury and a divided church kept control. There are no sources to lean on here — every mark comes from precise knowledge you can argue with. Learn the years 1558 to 1569 as one connected story: Elizabeth inherits debt, religious division and a French threat; she builds a settlement in 1559 that asks for obedience rather than belief; Puritans and Catholics test it; and the arrival of Mary, Queen of Scots in May 1568 turns discontent into the rebellion of November 1569. Every 'Explain why' and every 'How far do you agree' on this topic is a question about which of those pressures mattered most, so you need dates, names and figures you can place in an argument, not a list.","idea":[{"h":"The country Elizabeth inherited, and the machine she had to use","code":"B4-1 — 1 The situation on Elizabeth's accession: Elizabethan England in 1558","t":"England in 1558 was a hierarchy. In the countryside came the nobility, the gentry, yeomen, tenant farmers and then the landless poor and vagrants; in the towns, merchants and professionals above business owners, craftsmen and the unemployed. Power ran downwards through the same ladder. At the top sat the royal court, where access to the queen was itself a form of wealth, and the Privy Council, which Elizabeth cut to about nineteen men and led through William Cecil, her Secretary of State from November 1558. Parliament met only when the queen summoned it, in two Houses, and mattered chiefly because it granted taxation and made law by statute — which is why the religious settlement had to go through it. In the counties, Lords Lieutenant raised the militia, sheriffs and unpaid Justices of the Peace enforced the law. Patronage held the whole thing together: offices, lands and titles bought loyalty. A top-level answer uses this machinery as evidence rather than background — it explains that Elizabeth could only govern through men she had to keep loyal and through a Parliament she had to persuade, so an appointment like Cecil's or a vote in the Lords becomes a cause of what happened, not scene-setting."},{"h":"The Virgin Queen: legitimacy, gender, marriage and the succession","code":"B4-1 — 1 The situation on Elizabeth's accession: The Virgin Queen","t":"Elizabeth was the daughter of Henry VIII and Anne Boleyn. Parliament declared her illegitimate in 1536 after her mother's execution, and although the Act of 1544 and Henry's will restored her to the succession, Catholics who never accepted the second marriage regarded her as a bastard and Mary, Queen of Scots as the rightful queen. On top of that she was a woman in a century that assumed a wife obeyed her husband: John Knox's pamphlet attacking the rule of women appeared in 1558, and Mary I's Spanish marriage had shown what a foreign husband could cost. Marriage would have given England an heir but also a master; not marrying left the succession pointing straight at Mary Stuart, as the country realised when Elizabeth nearly died of smallpox in October 1562. Her strengths were real: a first-class education, fluency in languages, the political experience of surviving imprisonment under her sister, and a gift for public display. A top-level answer treats her unmarried state as a deliberate policy with costs on both sides, rather than as a personal quirk, and uses Parliament's petitions of 1563 and 1566 as evidence of the pressure she faced."},{"h":"Challenges at home and abroad: money, religion and France","code":"B4-1 — 1 The situation on Elizabeth's accession: Challenges at home and from abroad","t":"Three problems arrived together in November 1558. First, money: the Crown owed about £300,000, more than its ordinary annual income of roughly £286,000, much of it borrowed at heavy interest from lenders in Antwerp to pay for Mary I's war, while a debased coinage pushed prices up until Elizabeth recalled and reissued it in 1560–61. Second, religion: twenty-five years of change had left Catholic parishes, Marian bishops in post and Protestant exiles coming home expecting a full reformation, with no settled church for anyone to obey. Third, France: Calais, England's last foothold on the Continent, fell in January 1558; French troops garrisoned Scotland under the regent Mary of Guise; and Mary, Queen of Scots had married the French heir in April 1558 and quartered the English royal arms with her own. A top-level answer shows the three pulling together — a queen who cannot pay for an army, cannot count on her church and faces a Catholic power on two frontiers — because that connection, not a list of separate troubles, is what an examiner rewards as analysis."},{"h":"The settlement of 1559: two Acts and a set of Injunctions","code":"B4-1 — 2 The 'settlement' of religion: Elizabeth's religious settlement (1559)","t":"The settlement was made by Parliament in 1559 and by royal order. The Act of Supremacy made Elizabeth Supreme Governor of the Church of England — not Supreme Head, a wording chosen to soften Catholic objections and the objection that a woman could not head a church — and required an oath from clergy and officeholders. The Act of Uniformity imposed one Book of Common Prayer on every parish, made Sunday attendance compulsory with a fine of a shilling a week for staying away, and kept traditional vestments and church ornaments; its words at communion were deliberately open to a Catholic and a Protestant reading. The Royal Injunctions, 57 instructions issued in the queen's name, ordered an English Bible in every church, a licence for anyone who preached, a parish register of baptisms, marriages and burials, and the removal of pilgrimages and shrines of pretended miracles. The Uniformity bill passed the House of Lords by only three votes. A top-level answer explains the design: Elizabeth wanted outward obedience rather than inward belief, because obedience was all a poor, isolated and disputed monarchy could enforce."},{"h":"The Church of England in people's lives","code":"B4-1 — 2 The 'settlement' of religion: The Church of England","t":"The settlement only worked because the parish church stood at the centre of ordinary life. Everyone was required to attend on Sundays; people were baptised, married and buried there, heard royal proclamations read from the pulpit, and paid tithes towards the clergyman's income. Above the parish stood a hierarchy: the Supreme Governor, then archbishops — Matthew Parker became Archbishop of Canterbury in 1559 — bishops in their dioceses, and church courts that judged morality, wills and church attendance, with visitations to check that the Injunctions were obeyed. All but one of Mary's bishops, Kitchin of Llandaff, refused the oath and lost their sees, but the great majority of parish clergy conformed, which is why the settlement held. The Church was also a propaganda machine: a licensed preacher in every pulpit meant the government could speak to the whole country every week. A top-level answer uses this to explain both enforcement and resistance — control of the parish church is why recusancy mattered so much, and why a quarrel about what a minister wore became a quarrel about who was obeyed."},{"h":"The Puritan challenge: vestments, crucifixes and obedience","code":"B4-1 — 3 Challenge to the religious settlement: The nature and extent of the Puritan challenge","t":"Puritans were Protestants who judged the Church by scripture alone and thought the settlement had stopped half way. Many of their leaders had spent Mary I's reign in exile in Geneva, Frankfurt and Strasbourg and came home in 1559 expecting more. Two disputes followed. Over crucifixes, Elizabeth kept one in her own chapel and wanted them in churches; bishops threatened to resign and she gave way in the parishes. Over vestments, Puritan clergy refused the surplice and cope as the dress of the old religion, and in 1566 Archbishop Parker issued his Advertisements setting out what clergy must wear and summoned about 110 London clergy to Lambeth to see the approved dress; 37 refused and lost their livings. The challenge was loud but limited: most Puritans stayed inside the Church hoping to reform it, and none of this threatened Elizabeth's throne. A top-level answer measures the challenge rather than describing it — noisy, concentrated among the clergy and in London, damaging to unity but never a danger to the Crown."},{"h":"The Catholic challenge: recusancy, the nobility, the Papacy and foreign powers","code":"B4-1 — 3 Challenge to the religious settlement: The nature and extent of the Catholic challenge","t":"Most English Catholics did not rebel. They conformed outwardly, attending the parish church while keeping the old faith at home, and the government enforced the shilling fine on recusants lightly, because Elizabeth wanted quiet rather than martyrs. The danger lay elsewhere. Catholic nobles and gentry were strongest in the north and north-west, where the Council of the North had to push conformity from outside and where a great family's tenants followed its lead. The Papacy held back for a decade: Pius IV's invitation to the Council of Trent was refused in 1561, Pius V told English Catholics in 1566 not to attend Church of England services, but the excommunication of Elizabeth came only in February 1570, after the northern rising. Foreign powers were divided: Philip II of Spain disliked heresy but disliked a French-raised Mary Stuart on the English throne more, so he gave no help. A top-level answer explains this as a challenge that was serious in potential and mild in practice before 1569, and says why the balance changed."},{"h":"Mary, Queen of Scots and her claim","code":"B4-1 — 4 The problem of Mary, Queen of Scots: Mary's claim to the English throne","t":"Mary was queen of Scotland from six days old, in December 1542, and the great-granddaughter of Henry VII through Margaret Tudor, Henry VIII's elder sister. That made her Elizabeth's nearest legitimate relative, and to Catholics who held Henry's marriage to Anne Boleyn invalid it made her the rightful queen of England already. Henry VIII's will had tried to shut the Scottish line out of the succession, but a will was weaker than blood. Mary sharpened the claim herself: married to the French heir in April 1558 and queen consort of France when he became Francis II in 1559, she quartered the English royal arms with her own, and she never ratified the Treaty of Edinburgh of July 1560, by which she was to give up her claim to Elizabeth's throne. A top-level answer keeps the claim and the threat apart: the claim was a legal fact from 1558, while the threat depended on who was willing to fight for it, which is exactly what changed when she crossed the border."},{"h":"Elizabeth and Mary, 1568–69: the guest nobody could deal with","code":"B4-1 — 4 The problem of Mary, Queen of Scots: Relations between Elizabeth and Mary, 1568–69","t":"Driven from Scotland after the murder of Darnley, her marriage to Bothwell and defeat at Langside on 13 May 1568, Mary crossed into England on 16 May 1568. Every option was bad. Restoring her by force meant war with the Scottish Protestant lords who were England's friends; sending her to France handed a claimant to a hostile power; freeing her in England gave English Catholics a figurehead. Elizabeth chose to keep her under guard — Carlisle, then Bolton Castle, then Tutbury early in 1569 — and to hold a conference at York in October 1568, continued at Westminster, where the casket letters were examined and nothing was proved either way, so Mary was neither convicted nor released. In 1569 a scheme grew up to marry her to the Duke of Norfolk; Elizabeth sent Norfolk to the Tower and summoned the northern earls, who rebelled in November. A top-level answer judges this as a holding operation: it kept the throne safe at low cost but solved nothing, and the rising of 1569 was the proof."}],"examples":[{"title":"Two parts, two marks each: a rank in the countryside and a flight into England","setup":"Question 1(a): Describe one feature of the social hierarchy in the Elizabethan countryside. Question 1(b): Describe one feature of Mary, Queen of Scots' flight into England in 1568. Each part is worth two marks and is marked on its own: one mark for a valid feature, one for supporting information about it. You know that rural society was ranked by land, running down from the nobility to the gentry, then yeomen, then tenant farmers, then the landless labourers and the vagrant poor, and that a noble title and its great estates passed by inheritance while the gentry held land without a title. You also know that Mary was beaten at Langside on 13 May 1568, crossed the Solway three days later, spent her first night in England at Workington in Cumberland, and was taken to Carlisle Castle and then on to Bolton Castle in Yorkshire that July. What do you write?","steps":["Part (a) turns on what decided a family's rank, so open with it: 'One feature was that rank in the countryside rested on land.'","Now prove you know the ladder: the nobility stood at the top with great estates and titles that passed by inheritance, and below them came the gentry, who owned land but held no title.","Part (b) turns on how Mary arrived, so say it plainly: 'One feature was that Mary came into England uninvited, as a fugitive from a lost battle.'","Support it with the route and the dates: beaten at Langside on 13 May 1568, she crossed the Solway three days later, slept her first night at Workington in Cumberland, and was under guard at Carlisle Castle within days.","Stop. Why her arrival mattered to Elizabeth, or what the hierarchy meant for the poor, belongs to a different question and earns nothing in either part here."],"cues":["What decided where a family stood in the countryside?","Which two ranks sat at the top, and what separated them?","How did Mary enter England, and was she invited?","Which battle and which dates pin the flight down?","Both parts are answered. What must you leave out?"]},{"title":"Turning a stimulus bullet into an explained reason","setup":"Question 2: Explain why the Papacy did not act against Elizabeth in the years 1559-69. You may use the following in your answer: the Council of Trent; Philip II of Spain. You must also use information of your own. The question is worth twelve marks, and the mark scheme caps an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content. You know that Pius IV reopened the Council of Trent and in 1561 sent a nuncio to invite Elizabeth to send representatives to it, and that she forbade him to cross the Channel, so the Council closed in December 1563 with England unrepresented; that Philip II kept the peace with England and argued against a sentence on Elizabeth; and that as late as 1566 Pius V went no further than a ruling, carried into England by the priest Laurence Vaux, that Catholics might not attend Church of England services. Build the three aspects.","steps":["Take the first bullet and ask what the invitation of 1561 shows about Rome's intentions: a Pope who is inviting a queen to his council is not a Pope preparing to depose her.","Make it explain rather than describe: while the door to reunion was still being held open, condemning Elizabeth would have shut it, so Rome waited even after the Council broke up.","Do the same with the second bullet: Philip II kept the peace with England and argued against a sentence on Elizabeth, because a deposed Elizabeth meant Mary, Queen of Scots, brought up in France and married into its royal house, on the English throne.","Add the aspect of your own that the cap demands: when Rome did move, in 1566, it moved against attendance rather than against the queen, telling Catholics through Laurence Vaux to stay away from the parish church and saying nothing about rebellion.","Finish by linking the three: a hope of recovery, a Catholic king who would not enforce a sentence, and a Pope who chose discipline over deposition all pushed the same way, which is what turns three reasons into a sustained line of reasoning."],"cues":["What does sending an invitation show about Rome's plans?","Why would a condemnation have cost the Papacy something it wanted?","Who would gain the English throne if Elizabeth were deposed?","The bullets give you two aspects. Where would you look for a third?","What must the last sentence of the answer do that the three reasons have not done?"]},{"title":"Choosing the criterion before you judge","setup":"Question 4: 'Elizabeth's religious settlement of 1559 was more Protestant than Catholic.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: the oath of supremacy; church ornaments. You must also use information of your own. Sixteen marks. Before writing a word, decide what 'more Protestant' will mean in your answer. The material you have: the Act of Supremacy of May 1559 abolished all foreign authority over the Church and required an oath naming Elizabeth Supreme Governor from clergy and from everyone holding office under the Crown; sixteen of Mary's bishops lost their sees for refusing that oath between June and November of the same year; the prayer book of 1559 was taken largely from the Protestant book of 1552; its words at communion joined the older and the newer forms, so that the bread could still be called the body of Christ; the rubric of 1552 denying Christ's bodily presence was dropped; an ornaments rubric kept the vestments and furnishings of the earlier book; Elizabeth kept a crucifix in her own chapel; and a royal visitation in the summer of 1559 sent about 125 commissioners into the dioceses, who stripped ornaments out of the parishes.","steps":["Define the test in the opening lines: 'Protestant' can be measured by what the Church taught and who governed it, or by what a parishioner saw and heard on a Sunday, and the two measures do not give the same answer, so say which one you are using.","Build the case on doctrine and authority: foreign jurisdiction abolished, an oath of supremacy required of clergy and officeholders, sixteen of Mary's bishops deprived within months, and a prayer book taken largely from the Protestant book of 1552.","Build the case on appearance against the same test: the communion words joined the older and the newer forms, the rubric denying Christ's bodily presence was dropped, an ornaments rubric kept the old vestments and furnishings, and the queen herself kept a crucifix in her chapel.","Weigh them by the criterion you set, not by which list is longer: the concessions lie in wording and in furniture, while the Church's head, its bishops and its service book were all changed outright, and the visitation of 1559 sent commissioners who took the ornaments out of the parishes anyway.","State the judgement and its limit: Protestant in doctrine and government, deliberately Catholic in appearance, and then say why a queen who wanted outward obedience rather than belief would build a settlement in exactly that shape."],"cues":["Which two different things could 'Protestant' be measuring here?","Which changes altered what the Church taught and who ran it?","Which parts would a Catholic worshipper still have recognised?","How do you compare the two without simply counting points?","What does the shape of the settlement tell you about its purpose?"]},{"title":"Building a Level 4 paragraph on the Privy Council and the counties","setup":"You are writing one paragraph of an answer to: 'The Privy Council was the most important part of Elizabeth's government in the years 1558-69.' How far do you agree? The two stimulus points are William Cecil and the Justices of the Peace. The material you have is this: Elizabeth cut the Council from about fifty members under Mary I to about nineteen, and made William Cecil her Secretary of State in November 1558; the Council met regularly to advise her and to run the business of government; only Parliament could grant taxation or make law by statute, which is why the religious settlement had to pass through it; and in the counties the law was enforced by unpaid Justices of the Peace drawn from the gentry, sitting in quarter sessions four times a year. Turn that into a paragraph that argues.","steps":["Open with a point that answers the question, not a topic label: the Council was where government was decided, and deciding is not the same as being able to act.","Bring in the precise evidence, dated and named: a Council cut from about fifty members to about nineteen, with William Cecil made Secretary of State in November 1558.","Explain what those numbers mean for the question: a small body meeting regularly under one trusted minister made the centre quick, cheap and answerable to the queen, which is the strongest case for the statement.","Give the other side its weight: the Council could not vote a penny of tax or pass an Act, so the settlement of religion had to go through Parliament, and its orders reached the shires only through unpaid Justices of the Peace sitting four times a year.","Close with a mini-judgement that feeds the essay's argument: the Council was the most important place where government was decided, but not the most important reason government worked, and the conclusion can be built on that distinction."],"cues":["What should the first sentence of a paragraph in a judgement essay do?","Which two figures here are precise enough to name?","What does a council of that size, under that man, buy the queen?","What could the Council not do, and who had to do it instead?","What must the last sentence add so the paragraph is not just a list?"]},{"title":"Weighing two causes of the rising's collapse","setup":"Question 3: 'The main reason for the failure of the Revolt of the Northern Earls was the strength of Elizabeth's response.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: the Earl of Sussex; Barnard Castle. You must also use information of your own. Sixteen marks. You know that Mary, Queen of Scots was hurried south from Tutbury and reached Coventry on 25 November 1569, that the Earl of Sussex marched out of York on 13 December, and that Lord Clinton followed him north with a second army of about twelve thousand men. You also know that the rebels besieged Barnard Castle for eleven days until Sir George Bowes surrendered it, that no other part of the country rose with them and no foreign power moved, that the earls abandoned their foot soldiers and rode into Scotland, and that the Pope's excommunication of Elizabeth followed only in February 1570. Decide how to weigh them.","steps":["Ask the question that separates the causes: no battle was ever fought, so the rising was not beaten, and you have to work out instead what made going on impossible.","Put the Crown's response in as the first cause: Mary was moved out of reach to Coventry before the rebels could get near her, and Sussex then marched out of York with Lord Clinton's far larger army behind him, so the prize was gone and the odds were hopeless.","Put the rebels' own weakness in as the second: their one real success was Barnard Castle, taken after an eleven-day siege when Sir George Bowes surrendered it, while no other county rose and no foreign power moved at all.","Test the two against what the rebels actually did: they dispersed, and the earls rode into Scotland leaving their infantry behind, which is what men do when there is nowhere left to go rather than what a defeated army does.","Judge on that test, and say what the losing factor still explains: the Crown's speed decided when the rising ended, while the rebels' isolation decided that it had to end at all, and the Pope's sentence, when it finally came, arrived far too late to change either."],"cues":["If no battle was fought, what actually stopped the rebels?","What did the Crown do that took the rebels' objective away?","What was the only place the rebels actually captured?","What does abandoning the foot soldiers tell you about their position?","How can a factor be rejected as the main cause and still be essential?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Why did the Act of Supremacy call Elizabeth Supreme Governor of the Church rather than Supreme Head?","a":"Because the softer title was easier for Catholics to accept and met the objection that a woman could not be head of the Church, so more people would swear the oath."},{"q":"What did the Act of Uniformity require of ordinary people, and what did it cost to disobey?","a":"It required attendance at the parish church every Sunday, where the one Book of Common Prayer was used; absence was fined at a shilling a week."},{"q":"Why could Elizabeth not simply send Mary, Queen of Scots back to Scotland in 1568?","a":"Restoring Mary by force meant fighting the Scottish Protestant lords who were England's allies, and handing her to her enemies meant helping subjects depose an anointed queen, which set a dangerous example."},{"q":"What did the Revolt of the Northern Earls show about the Catholic challenge by 1569?","a":"That it was dangerous only where Catholic nobles had local followings and a claimant to rally to: the rising raised several thousand men in the north, but collapsed in December 1569 without a battle and got no foreign help."}]},"room":{"facts":["Booklet B of Paper 2 is the British depth study and carries 32 marks: Question 1(a) and 1(b), 'Describe one feature of...', two marks each on two separate aspects; Question 2, 'Explain why...', 12 marks; and either Question 3 or Question 4, \"'...' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\", 16 marks.","The describe-a-feature parts are point-marked, not levels-marked: one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about it. Reasons, consequences and judgements score nothing there.","Question 2 and the 16-mark judgement each print two stimulus points under 'You may use the following in your answer:', followed by 'You must also use information of your own.' Using the bullets is optional; adding your own material is not.","The caps printed in the mark scheme are: on the 12-mark question, maximum 8 marks and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; on the 16-mark judgement, maximum 11 marks and no access to Level 4 on the same condition. There is no cap at Level 2.","The AO split is AO2 6 and AO1 6 on the 12-mark question and AO2 10 and AO1 6 on the 16-mark judgement, whose grid carries a separate judgement bullet at every level. Performance is interdependent: an answer showing no qualities of AO2 cannot rise above the top of Level 1 however good the knowledge.","Paper 2 lasts 1 hour 50 minutes for both booklets, there are no sources or interpretations anywhere on it and there are no marks for spelling, punctuation and grammar. Each booklet gets about 55 minutes, a little under two minutes a mark: about 5 minutes on the two describe parts, 20 on the 12-mark question and 25 on the judgement, planning included."],"questions":[{"q":"Question 1(a): Describe one feature of the Privy Council in Elizabeth I's government. (2 marks) Question 1(b): Describe one feature of Parliament in Elizabethan government. (2 marks)","a":"AO1 4 marks in two separately marked parts: one mark for a valid feature and a second for supporting information in each. Credit for (a) the small, hand-picked membership of about nineteen, Cecil as Secretary of State, its advisory and administrative work; for (b) the two Houses, summons by the queen alone, the grant of taxation, law-making by statute and its petitions on marriage and the succession.","sol":"(a) One feature was that the Privy Council was small and hand-picked. Elizabeth cut it to about nineteen men and made William Cecil her Secretary of State in November 1558, so the business of government ran through a handful of advisers she trusted. (b) One feature was that Parliament met only when the queen summoned it, and chiefly to grant her taxation. It sat in two Houses, Lords and Commons, and could make law by statute, which is why the religious settlement of 1559 had to be passed through it rather than ordered by the queen alone.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Both parts name an institution of Elizabethan government. Picture each one — who belonged to it, where it met, how often — before you pick the feature you will write about.","Each part wants one feature only, with a sentence of support attached to it.","Write 'One feature was that...' and follow it with a name, a number or a date that proves it."],"codes":["B4-1 — 1 The situation on Elizabeth's accession: Elizabethan England in 1558"]},{"q":"Question 1(a): Describe one feature of the Act of Supremacy (1559). (2 marks) Question 1(b): Describe one feature of the Act of Uniformity (1559). (2 marks)","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked in two parts: one mark per valid feature, one for supporting information. Credit for (a) the title of Supreme Governor, the oath required of clergy and officeholders and the repeal of Mary's heresy laws; for (b) the single Book of Common Prayer, compulsory Sunday attendance, the shilling fine for absence, the deliberately ambiguous communion wording and the retention of traditional vestments.","sol":"(a) One feature was that it made Elizabeth Supreme Governor of the Church of England rather than Supreme Head. The softer title was chosen so that Catholics, and those who denied that a woman could head a church, could still take the oath that clergy and officeholders had to swear. (b) One feature was that it imposed a single Book of Common Prayer on every parish. Its words at communion were left open to a Catholic and a Protestant reading, Sunday attendance was made compulsory and staying away was fined at a shilling a week.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["One Act settled who ran the Church; the other settled what happened inside it every Sunday.","Keep the two parts separate and give each its own supporting detail.","A title, a book, a fine or an oath will each serve as the precise support an examiner wants."],"codes":["B4-1 — 2 The 'settlement' of religion: Elizabeth's religious settlement (1559)"]},{"q":"Question 1(a): Describe one feature of the Royal Injunctions of 1559. (2 marks) Question 1(b): Describe one feature of the part the Church of England played in people's lives. (2 marks)","a":"AO1 4 marks in two parts, one mark for the feature and one for supporting information in each. Credit for (a) the 57 instructions issued in the queen's name, the English Bible in every church, licensing of preachers, the parish register and the ban on pilgrimages and shrines of pretended miracles; for (b) compulsory attendance, baptism, marriage and burial, tithes, proclamations read from the pulpit, church courts and visitations.","sol":"(a) One feature was that they were a detailed set of orders to the clergy, 57 of them, issued in the queen's name: every church was to keep an English Bible, nobody was to preach without a licence, and pilgrimages and shrines of pretended miracles were to be removed. (b) One feature was that the parish church was the centre of village life. People were required to attend on Sundays, were baptised, married and buried there, heard royal proclamations read from its pulpit and paid tithes towards the clergyman's income.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The first part is about orders sent to clergy; the second about what the church building did for everyone else.","One feature each, then one concrete detail each - no explanation of why any of it mattered.","Naming what every church had to keep, and what every parishioner had to do, is enough support."],"codes":["B4-1 — 2 The 'settlement' of religion: Elizabeth's religious settlement (1559)","B4-1 — 2 The 'settlement' of religion: The Church of England"]},{"q":"Question 1(a): Describe one feature of the Crown's financial position in 1558. (2 marks) Question 1(b): Describe one feature of the French threat to England in 1558-59. (2 marks)","a":"AO1 4 marks, two parts marked separately. Credit for (a) the debt of about £300,000 against an ordinary income of about £286,000, the Antwerp loans at high interest, the debased coinage and the cost of Mary I's war; for (b) the loss of Calais in January 1558, French troops in Scotland under Mary of Guise, and Mary Stuart's marriage to the French heir in April 1558.","sol":"(a) One feature was that the Crown was deep in debt. Elizabeth inherited debts of about £300,000, more than a year's ordinary income of roughly £286,000, much of it borrowed at heavy interest from lenders in Antwerp to pay for her sister's war. (b) One feature was that France had an army inside Scotland. Mary of Guise governed there as regent for her daughter Mary, Queen of Scots, who had married the French heir in April 1558, so a hostile Catholic power stood on England's northern border as well as across the Channel.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["One part is about what the Crown owed; the other about where French soldiers actually were.","Each feature needs a figure, a place or a date fixed to it.","State the feature in a sentence, then prove it with the number or the name in the next."],"codes":["B4-1 — 1 The situation on Elizabeth's accession: Challenges at home and from abroad"]},{"q":"Question 1(a): Describe one feature of Mary, Queen of Scots' claim to the English throne. (2 marks) Question 1(b): Describe one feature of the Revolt of the Northern Earls (1569). (2 marks)","a":"AO1 4 marks in two parts, one mark for the feature and one for support. Credit for (a) her descent from Margaret Tudor, her legitimacy in Catholic eyes, the quartering of the English arms and her refusal to ratify the Treaty of Edinburgh; for (b) the Mass in Durham Cathedral on 14 November 1569, the destruction of English Bibles and prayer books, the leadership of Northumberland and Westmorland, the march south and the collapse in December.","sol":"(a) One feature was that it came through Henry VIII's elder sister. Mary was the granddaughter of Margaret Tudor, and Catholics who held Henry's marriage to Anne Boleyn to be invalid therefore regarded Mary, not Elizabeth, as the rightful queen of England. (b) One feature was that it opened with a Catholic demonstration in Durham. On 14 November 1569 the rebels entered the cathedral, destroyed the English Bible and prayer books they found there and had Mass celebrated, before marching south with several thousand men.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The first part is about descent; the second about what the rebels did first.","Name the relative in one and the place and date in the other.","Two sentences per part is the right length: the feature, then the detail that proves it."],"codes":["B4-1 — 4 The problem of Mary, Queen of Scots: Mary's claim to the English throne","B4-1 — 4 The problem of Mary, Queen of Scots: Relations between Elizabeth and Mary, 1568–69"]},{"q":"Question 2: Explain why Elizabeth's position as queen was insecure in 1558. You may use the following in your answer: • the question of her legitimacy • the Crown's debt. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit the 1536 declaration of illegitimacy and Mary Stuart's rival claim, the debt of about £300,000 against an income of about £286,000 and the debased coinage, and beyond the bullets the religious division left by Mary I, the expectations placed on a female ruler, the loss of Calais and the French army in Scotland. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Elizabeth's difficulty in November 1558 was that each of her weaknesses gave somebody else a reason to act against her. The first was her title. Parliament had declared her illegitimate in 1536 after her mother Anne Boleyn was executed, and although the Act of 1544 and Henry VIII's will restored her to the succession, Catholics who had never accepted her father's second marriage regarded her as a bastard. That mattered because an alternative existed: Mary, Queen of Scots, granddaughter of Henry's elder sister Margaret, was Catholic, legitimate in Catholic eyes and married to the heir to the French throne. A disputed title turns every ordinary grievance into a dynastic opportunity.\n\nThe second was money. Elizabeth inherited debts of about £300,000 against an ordinary income of roughly £286,000, much of it borrowed at heavy interest in Antwerp to pay for Mary I's war, while the debased coinage was pushing prices up. A queen who cannot pay soldiers cannot fight, and a queen who cannot reward men with offices and lands cannot buy their loyalty either, so poverty narrowed every choice she made in her first years.\n\nThe third reason, which the bullets do not prompt, is that the first two met in foreign policy. Calais, England's last foothold in France, had fallen in January 1558; French troops garrisoned Scotland under the regent Mary of Guise; and when Mary Stuart's husband became King Francis II in 1559 she quartered the English royal arms with her own, advertising her claim. England faced a Catholic power to the north and across the Channel at precisely the moment it could not afford a war.\n\nBehind all three lay the religious division Mary I had left, with Catholic bishops in post and Protestant exiles returning, and the common assumption that a woman needed a husband to rule for her. Elizabeth was insecure, in short, not because of any one problem but because a rival claimant, an empty treasury and a hostile France all pointed the same way at once.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask who could have replaced her in 1558, and what would have been needed to do it.","Sort the difficulties by kind: who she was, what she could spend, and who stood on her borders.","Use the two bullets as two aspects and find a third of your own before you begin writing."],"codes":["B4-1 — 1 The situation on Elizabeth's accession: The Virgin Queen","B4-1 — 1 The situation on Elizabeth's accession: Challenges at home and from abroad","B4-1 — 1 The situation on Elizabeth's accession: Elizabethan England in 1558"]},{"q":"Question 2: Explain why Elizabeth's religious settlement of 1559 took such a moderate form. You may use the following in your answer: • the Book of Common Prayer • the Catholic nobility. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit the divided country of 1559 and the ambiguous communion wording, the Catholic peers and bishops in the Lords and the three-vote majority for the Act of Uniformity, and beyond the bullets the foreign danger from France, Spain and Rome, the title of Supreme Governor and Elizabeth's aim of outward obedience rather than belief. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Elizabeth built a church almost everybody could live inside because in 1559 she could not afford to make an enemy of anybody. The first reason was the state of the country she inherited. Twenty-five years of religious change had left Catholic worship alive in most parishes while Protestant exiles came home from Geneva and Frankfurt demanding a full reformation; a settlement that satisfied one side completely would have driven the other into resistance. That is why the Book of Common Prayer imposed by the Act of Uniformity used words at communion that a Catholic could hear as the real presence and a Protestant as remembrance, and why the same Act kept traditional vestments and church ornaments, while the Royal Injunctions banned pilgrimages and shrines of pretended miracles.\n\nThe second reason was Parliament, and particularly the Lords. Mary's Catholic bishops still sat there alongside Catholic peers, and the Act of Uniformity passed the House of Lords by only three votes. A more radical settlement would simply not have become law. The same arithmetic explains the Act of Supremacy's title: Supreme Governor rather than Supreme Head left the Pope's spiritual standing unstated and answered the objection that a woman could not head a church, so conservative peers and most clergy could take the oath without dishonour.\n\nThe third reason, beyond the bullets, was foreign. France held Calais and garrisoned Scotland, Mary, Queen of Scots had just married the French heir, and Philip II of Spain was a Catholic ally England still needed. An openly radical Protestant church would have invited the Pope to declare Elizabeth deposed and handed France a cause. In the event Rome held back for a decade, and the excommunication came only in 1570.\n\nModeration was therefore calculation rather than indecision. Elizabeth wanted attendance, one prayer book and a shilling fine for absence — outward obedience — because outward obedience was the only thing a poor, isolated and disputed monarchy could actually enforce.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Think about everyone Elizabeth would have offended if she had chosen either extreme.","Look for reasons of three different kinds: the country, the law-making, and the powers watching from abroad.","Take one feature of the settlement and ask which group it was designed to keep quiet."],"codes":["B4-1 — 2 The 'settlement' of religion: Elizabeth's religious settlement (1559)","B4-1 — 2 The 'settlement' of religion: The Church of England","B4-1 — 3 Challenge to the religious settlement: The nature and extent of the Catholic challenge"]},{"q":"Question 2: Explain why Elizabeth's marriage and the succession caused such difficulty in the years 1558-69. You may use the following in your answer: • Robert Dudley • Parliament. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit Dudley's favour, the death of Amy Robsart in 1560 and his earldom in 1564, the petitions of 1563 and the attempt in 1566 to tie taxation to the succession, and beyond the bullets the smallpox of 1562, the foreign suitors Philip II and the Archduke Charles, Mary Stuart's claim as the alternative heir and contemporary assumptions about husbands and wives. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The marriage question was so difficult because it could not be answered without loss. The first difficulty was that no marriage meant no heir, and no heir meant the succession pointed at a Catholic. Elizabeth was the last of Henry VIII's children, so if she died the strongest claim belonged to Mary, Queen of Scots. That stopped being theoretical in October 1562, when Elizabeth nearly died of smallpox and the Privy Council had no agreed successor to name.\n\nThe second difficulty was that every possible husband carried a price. Philip II of Spain would have tied England to the Habsburgs and repeated the unpopularity of Mary I's Spanish marriage; the Archduke Charles was a Catholic whose religion would have reopened the settlement of 1559; and Robert Dudley, whom Elizabeth plainly favoured and created Earl of Leicester in 1564, was an English subject whose promotion would have split the Council between his friends and his rivals. His case was ruined in September 1560, when his wife Amy Robsart was found dead at the foot of a staircase: whatever the truth of it, marrying him afterwards would have looked like the reward of murder.\n\nThe third difficulty was that Parliament would not let the matter rest. Both Houses petitioned her to marry in 1563, and in 1566 MPs tried to make a grant of taxation depend on her naming a successor. Elizabeth forbade the debate and gave up part of the money rather than accept the condition, because naming an heir would have created a rival court around that heir and invited plots in his or her name.\n\nBeyond all this, and beyond the bullets, lay what a husband was expected to be. Contemporaries assumed a wife obeyed, so any husband would have had a claim on policy and a foreign one a claim on England. Staying single preserved Elizabeth's independence and made the prospect of a match a diplomatic weapon she could use for years. The difficulty was therefore permanent: marriage threatened her authority, while remaining unmarried threatened the succession, and she could only choose which risk to run.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what Elizabeth stood to lose by marrying, and what the country stood to lose if she did not.","Deal with the candidates as a group and the pressure from Westminster as a separate reason.","Fix each reason to an event with a date so the explanation is not a set of generalisations."],"codes":["B4-1 — 1 The situation on Elizabeth's accession: The Virgin Queen","B4-1 — 1 The situation on Elizabeth's accession: Elizabethan England in 1558","B4-1 — 4 The problem of Mary, Queen of Scots: Mary's claim to the English throne"]},{"q":"Question 2: Explain why Puritans objected to Elizabeth's religious settlement in the years 1559-69. You may use the following in your answer: • vestments • crucifixes. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit the belief that worship must follow scripture alone and the objection to the surplice and the cope, the crucifix dispute and Elizabeth's retreat in the parishes, and beyond the bullets the returning Marian exiles, the deliberately ambiguous prayer book, and Parker's Advertisements of 1566 with the deprivation of the clergy who refused. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Puritans objected because they measured the settlement against scripture and found it half finished. The first reason was what they believed a church should look like. For them anything worship did not command in the Bible had no place in it, so the surplice and the cope were the dress of the old religion rather than of a reformed ministry, and the crucifix Elizabeth kept in her own chapel was idolatry. This was not fussiness about cloth: vestments marked the minister out as a priest set apart, which is precisely the claim Puritans denied.\n\nThe second reason was who the objectors were. Many of the ablest Protestant clergy had spent Mary I's reign in exile in Geneva, Frankfurt and Strasbourg, where they had seen churches run without ceremonies or elaborate dress. They came home in 1559 expecting England to go further and instead found a prayer book whose communion words had been blurred so that Catholics could use it, and an Act of Uniformity that preserved traditional vestments and church ornaments. A settlement designed as a compromise looked to them like a betrayal of the reformation they had suffered for.\n\nThe third reason, which the bullets do not prompt, is that Elizabeth turned the disagreement into a test of obedience. In 1566 Archbishop Matthew Parker issued his Advertisements, setting out exactly what clergy must wear, and summoned about 110 London clergy to Lambeth to see the approved dress; 37 refused and lost their livings. As Supreme Governor Elizabeth could not allow ministers to choose which royal order to obey, so a quarrel about dress became a quarrel about authority, and the deprivations pushed some Puritans towards arguing that the government of the Church, and not only its clothing, needed reform.\n\nThe objections therefore ran from the altar cloth up to the Crown. What they were not was treasonous: most Puritans stayed inside the Church because they still hoped to reform it from within, which is why their challenge in these years was loud without ever endangering the queen.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Begin from what Puritans thought a church service should contain, and work outwards to what they saw.","Separate the beliefs, the people who held them and the way the government responded.","One of your aspects should be about enforcement rather than about doctrine."],"codes":["B4-1 — 3 Challenge to the religious settlement: The nature and extent of the Puritan challenge","B4-1 — 2 The 'settlement' of religion: Elizabeth's religious settlement (1559)","B4-1 — 2 The 'settlement' of religion: The Church of England"]},{"q":"Question 2: Explain why the Revolt of the Northern Earls broke out in 1569. You may use the following in your answer: • Mary, Queen of Scots • the Earl of Northumberland. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit Mary's presence in England from May 1568 and the Norfolk marriage scheme, Northumberland's and Westmorland's loss of power and income in a north governed through the Council of the North, and beyond the bullets the Catholicism of the region shown at Durham, and the summons to court in the autumn of 1569 that left the earls facing arrest. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The rising of November 1569 came from an old grievance meeting a sudden opportunity. The first reason was the decline of the northern earls. Thomas Percy, Earl of Northumberland, and Charles Neville, Earl of Westmorland, bore great names in a region where men followed families rather than London, but Elizabeth governed the north through the Council of the North under the Earl of Sussex and through servants of her own choosing, and Percy lost a dispute with the Crown over a copper mine found on his land. Men shut out of power and income had reason to want a different regime.\n\nThe second reason was Mary, Queen of Scots. Since May 1568 she had been in England, a Catholic claimant held at Carlisle, then at Bolton Castle and then at Tutbury, and her presence turned grumbling into a plan: a scheme grew up to marry her to Thomas Howard, Duke of Norfolk, restore her in Scotland and have her recognised as Elizabeth's heir. When Elizabeth learned of it in the autumn of 1569 she sent Norfolk to the Tower and summoned the two earls to court. Believing arrest awaited them, they had to choose between rebellion and ruin, which is why the rising came in that particular month.\n\nThe third reason, beyond the bullets, was religion. The north was the most Catholic region of England and had never accepted the settlement willingly. The rebels' first act, on 14 November 1569, was to enter Durham Cathedral, destroy the English Bible and prayer books there and hear Mass, and they marched under the banner of the Five Wounds of Christ that the Pilgrimage of Grace had used a generation before. Religion gave the rising its language and its ability to raise several thousand men quickly.\n\nNo one of these would have produced a revolt on its own. The earls' grievances were years old and their Catholicism older still; what made 1569 the year was Mary's presence and the collapse of the Norfolk scheme, which forced declining men to act before the Crown acted against them.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the earls had lost, and what had newly become possible for them.","You need a long-term grievance, a trigger and a cause that explains the followers as well as the leaders.","Pin the trigger to the autumn of that year and say what the Crown did that forced the earls' hand."],"codes":["B4-1 — 4 The problem of Mary, Queen of Scots: Relations between Elizabeth and Mary, 1568–69","B4-1 — 3 Challenge to the religious settlement: The nature and extent of the Catholic challenge","B4-1 — 4 The problem of Mary, Queen of Scots: Mary's claim to the English throne"]},{"q":"Question 3: 'The main reason for the Revolt of the Northern Earls in 1569 was religion.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • Durham Cathedral • Mary, Queen of Scots. You must also use information of your own. (16 marks)","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit for the statement the Mass and destruction of English prayer books at Durham on 14 November 1569, the banner of the Five Wounds and northern Catholicism; against it Mary's presence from May 1568 and the Norfolk marriage scheme, the summons of the earls to court, and the Percy and Neville loss of power and income; and the collapse in December as a test. Caps: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Whether religion was the main reason depends on what is being explained: the belief that made men willing to rise, or the circumstances that made them rise in that particular autumn. Religion supplied the cause the rebels proclaimed and the followers they needed, but the trigger was political and the deeper grievance was the earls' own decline.\n\nThe religious case is strong. The north was the region where the settlement of 1559 had taken least hold: gentry families kept the old worship alive and the Council of the North had to press conformity from outside. The rebels' first act was religious. On 14 November 1569 they entered Durham Cathedral, tore up the English Bible and the Book of Common Prayer they found and had Mass celebrated, and their army marched under the banner of the Five Wounds of Christ that the Pilgrimage of Grace had carried a generation earlier. They demanded the restoration of the old religion, and what hope they had of foreign help rested on Catholic powers. Without that shared faith, several thousand ordinary northerners would not have followed two discredited earls.\n\nYet the faith had been there since 1559 and had produced no rising for ten years, which suggests it was the fuel rather than the spark. What changed was Mary, Queen of Scots. Her arrival in May 1568 put a Catholic claimant inside England, and out of it grew the plan to marry her to Thomas Howard, Duke of Norfolk, restore her in Scotland and have her named Elizabeth's heir — a lawful change of regime rather than a doomed revolt. When Elizabeth discovered the scheme in the autumn of 1569, sent Norfolk to the Tower and summoned Northumberland and Westmorland to explain themselves, the earls faced arrest. They rose in November because the alternative was ruin, not because their beliefs had altered.\n\nBehind both stands the earls' loss of position. Thomas Percy and Charles Neville commanded the old loyalties of the north but not its government: Elizabeth relied on the Earl of Sussex and on new men, Percy had lost a dispute with the Crown over a copper mine found on his land, and the offices that had made his family powerful were no longer theirs by right.\n\nThe conduct of the rising is the best test between them. The rebels marched south towards Mary rather than towards London, and once she had been moved to Coventry and the Earl of Sussex advanced from York they melted away in December without fighting a battle; about four hundred and fifty were afterwards executed, Northumberland was handed back from Scotland and put to death at York in 1572, and Westmorland died in exile. Men rising to restore a religion do not abandon the attempt because one prisoner has been moved.\n\nReligion, then, made rebellion thinkable and gave it its symbols and its numbers, but it was Mary's presence and the earls' fear of arrest that decided that the rising came in 1569. Judged by what set it off, the main reason was the political crisis at court; judged by what made it a popular rising rather than a quarrel between noblemen, religion was essential. The statement is half right, and the half it misses matters more.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["A cause that has been present for ten years cannot by itself explain one particular November.","Separate what made men willing to rebel from what made them rebel now, and test both against the rebels' own behaviour.","Decide early what would count as the 'main' reason, then apply that test to each factor in turn."],"codes":["B4-1 — 3 Challenge to the religious settlement: The nature and extent of the Catholic challenge","B4-1 — 4 The problem of Mary, Queen of Scots: Relations between Elizabeth and Mary, 1568–69","B4-1 — 4 The problem of Mary, Queen of Scots: Mary's claim to the English throne"]},{"q":"Question 4: 'Elizabeth's religious settlement of 1559 had succeeded by 1569.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the Act of Uniformity • the Puritan challenge. You must also use information of your own. (16 marks)","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit for the statement the conformity of most parish clergy, enforced attendance under the Act of Uniformity, church papists and the absence of religious war; against it the deprivations of 1566, the retreat over crucifixes, continuing Catholicism in the north and the rising of 1569; the criterion of outward obedience rather than belief should be stated and applied. Caps: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The settlement was never designed to change what people believed; it was designed to secure outward obedience and public order. Judged by that test, which is the only fair one, it had largely succeeded by 1569, though it had not conquered the north and its hardest test was still ahead.\n\nThe evidence of success is broad. The two Acts of 1559 became law and stayed law. All but one of Mary's bishops refused the oath and lost their sees, yet the overwhelming majority of parish clergy conformed, so the Church kept its ministers and its machinery. Under the Act of Uniformity one Book of Common Prayer was used everywhere and Sunday attendance was required, with a fine of a shilling a week for absence; because the book's communion wording could be heard in more than one way, most Catholics could attend without feeling they had denied their faith, and these church papists kept the old religion privately while obeying publicly. That was exactly the outcome Elizabeth wanted. Meanwhile France was descending into religious civil war and England was not, which is the strongest single measure of the settlement's achievement.\n\nThe Puritan challenge shows the same machinery working, if noisily. Puritans objected to vestments and crucifixes as relics of Rome, and in 1566 Archbishop Parker's Advertisements set out what clergy must wear and summoned about 110 London clergy to Lambeth; 37 refused and were deprived. The loss of able ministers hurt, but the great majority conformed, the dispute stayed inside the Church, and no Puritan in these years questioned Elizabeth's right to rule.\n\nAgainst the statement, the settlement had clear limits. Elizabeth had to retreat over crucifixes in parish churches when bishops threatened to resign, so her authority in matters of detail was negotiable. Enforcement was patchy where the Crown was weak: in the north the old services survived, the Council of the North had to push conformity from outside, and Catholic nobles kept tenants who followed their lead. Pope Pius V told English Catholics in 1566 not to attend Church of England services, which made outward conformity a sin for those who obeyed him. Then came the rising of November 1569, when rebels destroyed English prayer books in Durham Cathedral and demanded the old religion back: after ten years, the settlement could still be rejected in arms.\n\nHow much weight should that carry? The rising was regional, it never spread south, it collapsed in December without a battle once Mary had been moved out of reach, and it drew no foreign help. A settlement that survives its worst challenge in six weeks has passed the test it was set. By the criterion of obedience and order the statement is justified; by the criterion of belief it is not, because the north remained Catholic in heart. The honest judgement is that the settlement had succeeded as a political instrument by 1569, and that the excommunication of Elizabeth in February 1570 would test it far harder than anything in the first decade.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Success cannot be judged until you say what the settlement was trying to do.","Look for evidence of obedience on one side and of resistance on the other, and keep the same measure for both.","Ask how far the strongest piece of evidence against the settlement actually spread before it collapsed."],"codes":["B4-1 — 2 The 'settlement' of religion: Elizabeth's religious settlement (1559)","B4-1 — 2 The 'settlement' of religion: The Church of England","B4-1 — 3 Challenge to the religious settlement: The nature and extent of the Puritan challenge","B4-1 — 3 Challenge to the religious settlement: The nature and extent of the Catholic challenge"]},{"q":"Question 3: 'The Crown's debt was the most serious problem Elizabeth faced when she became queen in 1558.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the Crown's income • the French threat. You must also use information of your own. (16 marks)","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit the debt of about £300,000 against an income of about £286,000, Antwerp borrowing and the debased coinage, weighed against the French army in Scotland, the loss of Calais and Mary Stuart's claim, the unsettled religion of the kingdom and the questions of legitimacy and gender; a criterion such as how quickly a problem could destroy her should be justified and applied. Caps: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The problems of 1558 have to be ranked by a test, and the fairest is how quickly each could have ended Elizabeth's reign. By that test the debt was the most limiting problem but not the most dangerous one: France could have destroyed her within a year, and the debt merely made every remedy harder.\n\nThe case for the debt is real. Elizabeth inherited debts of about £300,000 against an ordinary annual income of roughly £286,000, so the Crown owed more than it earned in a year, much of it borrowed at heavy interest from lenders in Antwerp to pay for her sister's war. The coinage had been debased under her father and brother, which pushed prices up and reduced the real value of what the Crown collected. Ordinary revenue came from Crown lands, customs and the profits of justice; anything more needed a parliamentary subsidy, which meant asking. The consequences ran through everything: an army could not be paid, a fleet could not be kept at sea, and patronage, the currency of loyalty, was in short supply. The remedies were slow ones — recalling and reissuing the coinage in 1560-61, cutting household costs and selling Crown lands.\n\nYet debt kills a government slowly. The French threat could have killed it at once. Calais, England's last possession in France, had fallen in January 1558; French troops garrisoned Scotland under the regent Mary of Guise; and Mary, Queen of Scots, married to the French heir in April 1558, quartered the English royal arms with her own. An invasion from Scotland in support of a Catholic claimant with a better title in Catholic eyes would have found English Catholics at home and a queen with no money to resist. That danger only receded when Elizabeth gambled on helping the Scottish Protestant lords and the French agreed by the Treaty of Edinburgh in July 1560 to withdraw.\n\nReligion belongs in the same balance. England in 1558 had no settled church: Marian bishops were in post, exiles were returning with different expectations, and until Parliament acted nobody knew what was legal. Religious division was what made the French danger lethal, because it supplied a foreign enemy with friends inside the country. Alongside it stood Elizabeth's own position — declared illegitimate in 1536, unmarried, and a woman in a century that doubted whether women should rule — which is why Mary Stuart's claim had force at all.\n\nRanked by the test set at the start, the order is clear. The French threat, resting on religious division and a disputed title, was the most serious because it could have ended the reign quickly, and it is the danger Elizabeth took the biggest risk to remove, sending help to the Scottish Protestant lords in 1560. The debt was the most persistent problem and the one that shaped every other decision, since poverty is why she could not fight a long war, but poverty alone removes no queen from her throne. The statement identifies the deepest constraint and mistakes it for the sharpest danger.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide what makes a problem 'serious' before you rank anything - speed, or reach, or permanence.","The problems were connected: ask which one made the others worse and which one could have ended the reign.","Give each problem its own paragraph with figures or dates, then compare them on the single test you chose."],"codes":["B4-1 — 1 The situation on Elizabeth's accession: Challenges at home and from abroad","B4-1 — 1 The situation on Elizabeth's accession: The Virgin Queen","B4-1 — 1 The situation on Elizabeth's accession: Elizabethan England in 1558"]},{"q":"Question 4: 'Elizabeth dealt with the problem of Mary, Queen of Scots well in the years 1568-69.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the casket letters • the Duke of Norfolk. You must also use information of your own. (16 marks)","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit the refusal to restore Mary by force or release her abroad, her custody at Carlisle, Bolton and Tutbury, the conference at York and Westminster and its verdict of nothing proved, and the arrest of Norfolk in 1569; against the statement the creation of a permanent focus for plots, the rising of November 1569 and the absence of any legal basis for holding her. Caps: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Elizabeth's handling of Mary should be judged by what she was actually trying to do: to keep her throne and avoid a war, not to solve the Scottish question. On that test she did well in 1568-69, but only in the sense that she survived a problem she had also made permanent.\n\nHer choices were all bad ones. Mary was defeated at Langside on 13 May 1568 and crossed into England three days later. Restoring her by force would have meant fighting the Scottish Protestant lords who were England's only friends in Scotland and reopening the settlement the Treaty of Edinburgh had produced in 1560. Letting her go to France would have handed a claimant with an army behind her to England's oldest enemy. Freeing her in England would have given every discontented Catholic a queen to rally to. Against those, keeping her under guard — at Carlisle, then Bolton Castle, then Tutbury from early in 1569 — was the least bad option, and it cost nothing but money and watchfulness.\n\nThe conference that opened at York in October 1568 and continued at Westminster was managed with the same caution. The casket letters, said to prove Mary's part in Darnley's murder, were examined, and the enquiry ended with nothing proved either way. That outcome looks like failure, but it served Elizabeth twice over: Mary was not convicted, so no anointed queen was condemned by another monarch's court and no dangerous precedent was set, yet she was not cleared either, so she could not reasonably be released. Elizabeth then showed she could act decisively when the danger became concrete, breaking the scheme to marry Mary to Thomas Howard, Duke of Norfolk, and sending Norfolk to the Tower in 1569.\n\nThe case against is the rising that followed. By keeping Mary in England Elizabeth placed a Catholic claimant, with a better title in Catholic eyes, inside the country, within reach of the most Catholic and least governable region of it. Within eighteen months of her arrival the northern earls were in arms, Mass was being said in Durham Cathedral and the rebels were marching towards her; Mary had to be hurried south to Coventry to keep her out of their hands. Detention without trial or verdict also left no legal basis for what was being done, which is why the problem could not be closed and dragged on until her execution in 1587.\n\nHow the rising ended settles the judgement. It collapsed in December 1569 without a battle, the north was punished with about four hundred and fifty executions, Norfolk was already in custody, and no foreign power moved. Elizabeth kept her throne, avoided war with France and Scotland and lost no ground. Judged as crisis management in 1568-69, she dealt with Mary well. Judged as a solution, she did not deal with her at all, because every option that would have ended the problem was more dangerous than living with it — and the excommunication of February 1570 would make living with it harder still.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["List the options Elizabeth actually had in May 1568 before deciding whether her choice was a good one.","Judge her by what she was trying to achieve, then test that judgement against what happened in the north.","Weigh the short-term result against the long-term cost, and say which one your criterion makes decisive."],"codes":["B4-1 — 4 The problem of Mary, Queen of Scots: Relations between Elizabeth and Mary, 1568–69","B4-1 — 4 The problem of Mary, Queen of Scots: Mary's claim to the English throne","B4-1 — 3 Challenge to the religious settlement: The nature and extent of the Catholic challenge"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date did Elizabeth I become queen of England?","a":"17 November 1558."},{"q":"What title did the Act of Supremacy of 1559 give Elizabeth over the Church?","a":"Supreme Governor of the Church of England."},{"q":"In which month and year did Mary, Queen of Scots cross into England?","a":"May 1568."},{"q":"Which cathedral did the northern rebels enter on 14 November 1569 to hear Mass?","a":"Durham Cathedral."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Elizabeth I's accession","code":"B4-1 — 1 The situation on Elizabeth's accession: Elizabethan England in 1558","open":false,"back":"Elizabeth became queen on 17 November 1558, aged 25, on the death of her half-sister Mary I. She was crowned at Westminster Abbey on 15 January 1559."},{"front":"William Cecil","code":"B4-1 — 1 The situation on Elizabeth's accession: Elizabethan England in 1558","open":false,"back":"Made Secretary of State in November 1558 and Elizabeth's chief adviser for the whole of this period. He ran the Privy Council's business and steered the religious settlement through Parliament in 1559."},{"front":"The Privy Council under Elizabeth","code":"B4-1 — 1 The situation on Elizabeth's accession: Elizabethan England in 1558","open":false,"back":"About nineteen hand-picked men who advised the queen and ran day-to-day government: they oversaw Justices of the Peace, the Council of the North, and the enforcement of religion. Elizabeth could overrule them."},{"front":"One reason Elizabeth's refusal to marry caused political difficulty","code":"B4-1 — 1 The situation on Elizabeth's accession: The Virgin Queen","open":true,"back":"It left no heir, so the succession pointed to Mary, Queen of Scots; an unsettled succession made plots against Elizabeth worth the risk, since her death would change the country's religion; and it set her against a Council and Parliament who claimed a right to advise on a matter she held to be hers alone."},{"front":"The question of Elizabeth's legitimacy","code":"B4-1 — 1 The situation on Elizabeth's accession: The Virgin Queen","open":false,"back":"Parliament declared her illegitimate in 1536 after Anne Boleyn's execution. The Act of 1544 and Henry VIII's will restored her to the succession, but Catholics who rejected the marriage saw Mary Stuart as rightful queen."},{"front":"The Crown's finances in 1558","code":"B4-1 — 1 The situation on Elizabeth's accession: Challenges at home and from abroad","open":false,"back":"Debts of about £300,000 against an ordinary annual income of roughly £286,000, much of it borrowed at high interest in Antwerp. The coinage was debased; Elizabeth recalled and reissued it in 1560-61."},{"front":"The Treaty of Edinburgh, July 1560","code":"B4-1 — 1 The situation on Elizabeth's accession: Challenges at home and from abroad","open":false,"back":"After the death of Mary of Guise, French and English troops withdrew from Scotland. Mary, Queen of Scots was to give up her claim to Elizabeth's throne, but she never ratified the treaty."},{"front":"The Act of Supremacy, 1559","code":"B4-1 — 2 The 'settlement' of religion: Elizabeth's religious settlement (1559)","open":false,"back":"Made Elizabeth Supreme Governor of the Church of England rather than Supreme Head, and required an oath of supremacy from clergy and officeholders. All Mary's bishops but one refused it and lost their sees."},{"front":"The Act of Uniformity, 1559","code":"B4-1 — 2 The 'settlement' of religion: Elizabeth's religious settlement (1559)","open":false,"back":"Imposed one Book of Common Prayer on every parish, with communion wording open to Catholic and Protestant readings, and made Sunday attendance compulsory. Absence was fined a shilling a week."},{"front":"The Royal Injunctions, 1559","code":"B4-1 — 2 The 'settlement' of religion: Elizabeth's religious settlement (1559)","open":false,"back":"Fifty-seven orders issued in the queen's name: an English Bible in every church, a licence needed to preach, a parish register of baptisms, marriages and burials, and no pilgrimages or shrines of pretended miracles."},{"front":"The parish church in Elizabethan life","code":"B4-1 — 2 The 'settlement' of religion: The Church of England","open":false,"back":"Attendance was compulsory; people were baptised, married and buried there, paid tithes and heard royal proclamations read from the pulpit. Church courts and visitations enforced morality and conformity."},{"front":"The vestments controversy, 1566","code":"B4-1 — 3 Challenge to the religious settlement: The nature and extent of the Puritan challenge","open":false,"back":"Archbishop Matthew Parker's Advertisements set out what clergy must wear. About 110 London clergy were summoned to Lambeth to see the approved dress; 37 refused and lost their livings."},{"front":"Recusants and church papists","code":"B4-1 — 3 Challenge to the religious settlement: The nature and extent of the Catholic challenge","open":false,"back":"Recusants were Catholics who refused to attend Church of England services and were fined a shilling a week. Church papists attended outwardly while keeping the old faith at home; before 1570 the fine was enforced lightly."},{"front":"Mary, Queen of Scots' claim to the English throne","code":"B4-1 — 4 The problem of Mary, Queen of Scots: Mary's claim to the English throne","open":false,"back":"Granddaughter of Margaret Tudor, Henry VIII's elder sister, and legitimate in Catholic eyes. She quartered the English royal arms with her own, a public assertion that she, not Elizabeth, should be queen of England."},{"front":"The Revolt of the Northern Earls, November to December 1569","code":"B4-1 — 4 The problem of Mary, Queen of Scots: Relations between Elizabeth and Mary, 1568–69","open":false,"back":"Northumberland and Westmorland took Durham Cathedral on 14 November, said Mass and destroyed English prayer books, then marched south towards Mary. They collapsed in December; about 450 rebels were executed."}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 2 booklet B Question 3, 16 marks: 'Mary, Queen of Scots was a greater danger to Elizabeth after she entered England in May 1568 than she had been before.')","items":["The paragraph: Before 1568 Mary's claim was an argument; after 1568 it was a person standing inside the kingdom. The claim itself could hardly have been pressed harder from abroad - as queen consort of France she quartered the English royal arms with her own, and she never ratified the Treaty of Edinburgh of July 1560, by which she was to have given up her title to Elizabeth's throne. None of it moved an English Catholic to do anything, because there was nobody in England to gather round. Her defeat at Langside on 13 May 1568 changed that, and changed nothing else: she crossed the Solway three days later and was held at Carlisle, then Bolton Castle, then Tutbury. Elizabeth's attempt to settle the matter judicially made it worse, not better. The commission that opened at York on 4 October 1568 under the Duke of Norfolk, and went on at Westminster, examined the casket letters and proved nothing either way, so Mary could be neither convicted nor released - and within a year Norfolk himself was at the centre of a scheme to marry her, and two northern earls were in arms. The claim had not grown stronger. What had changed was that it now stood on English soil, within reach of the men who might fight for it.","Why this is Level 4: the explanation is directed consistently at the conceptual focus - change over time, and whether the danger actually grew - and the reasoning is coherent, sustained and logically structured from the opening distinction between an argument and a person to the closing return to it.","Why this is Level 4: the information is precisely selected to address the question directly, with the quartered arms, the unratified Treaty of Edinburgh of July 1560, Langside on 13 May 1568, the custody at Carlisle, Bolton and Tutbury, and the commission that opened at York on 4 October 1568 all doing argumentative work rather than decorating the point.","Why this is Level 4: a criterion for the judgement is justified and applied - danger is measured by who could reach her, not by how good her title was - so the paragraph reaches an interim judgement that the essay's conclusion can build on."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["The paragraph: Mary, Queen of Scots was more dangerous after 1568 because she was now in England. She had a good claim to the throne through Margaret Tudor and lots of Catholics thought she should be queen instead of Elizabeth. When she lost the battle of Langside she came to England and Elizabeth kept her in castles like Carlisle and Bolton. There was a conference at York about her but nothing was decided. Then in 1569 the northern earls rebelled and wanted to put her on the throne. So she was a bigger danger after 1568.","What it does earn: accurate and relevant information is included, showing some knowledge and understanding of the period - the descent from Margaret Tudor, Langside, the custody at Carlisle and Bolton, the conference at York and the rising of 1569 are all correct - and an overall judgement is given, which lifts it out of Level 1.","Why it stays at Level 2: the links to the conceptual focus are implicit and unsustained. The 'before' half of the comparison is never examined, so nothing here shows what had actually changed, the analysis is limited and the line of reasoning is not sustained.","Why it stays at Level 2: the overall judgement is given but its justification is asserted rather than argued - 'so she was a bigger danger after 1568' repeats the claim instead of testing it - and no criterion for measuring danger is offered at all.","The single change that would move it up: measure the 'before'. Adding that Mary had quartered the English royal arms and had refused to ratify the Treaty of Edinburgh in 1560, and that nothing happened in England for eight years, then asking why those years stayed quiet, turns description into analysis."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Elizabeth's inheritance, November 1558","items":["17 November 1558: Mary I dies and Elizabeth, aged 25, becomes queen; she is crowned at Westminster Abbey on 15 January 1559.","Elizabeth is the daughter of Henry VIII and Anne Boleyn; Parliament declared her illegitimate in 1536, and the Act of 1544 and Henry's will restored her to the succession.","Crown debt about £300,000, against an ordinary annual income of about £286,000; much of the debt was owed at high interest to lenders in Antwerp after Mary I's war with France.","The coinage had been debased under Henry VIII and Edward VI; Elizabeth recalled and reissued it in 1560-61.","Calais, England's last possession in France, was lost in January 1558; the war was ended by the Treaty of Cateau-Cambrésis in April 1559.","Government: the royal court; a Privy Council cut to about nineteen members; Parliament in two Houses, summoned only by the queen; Lords Lieutenant, sheriffs and unpaid Justices of the Peace in the counties.","William Cecil becomes Secretary of State in November 1558; Nicholas Bacon becomes Lord Keeper of the Great Seal; Robert Dudley is Master of the Horse and is created Earl of Leicester in 1564.","Society is a hierarchy: nobility, gentry, yeomen, tenant farmers and the landless poor in the countryside; merchants and professionals, business owners, craftsmen and labourers in the towns.","John Knox's pamphlet attacking the rule of women was published in 1558, and contemporaries assumed a queen would marry and be guided by her husband.","Mary, Queen of Scots married the French heir in April 1558; French troops garrisoned Scotland under the regent Mary of Guise.","October 1562: Elizabeth nearly dies of smallpox, and the succession question becomes urgent; Parliament petitions her to marry in 1563 and presses again in 1566.","September 1560: Amy Robsart, Robert Dudley's wife, is found dead at Cumnor Place, ending any realistic prospect of Elizabeth marrying him."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — the religious settlement of 1559 and the Church of England","items":["Act of Supremacy 1559: Elizabeth becomes Supreme Governor of the Church of England; clergy and officeholders must swear an oath of supremacy; Mary I's heresy laws are repealed.","Act of Uniformity 1559: one Book of Common Prayer for every parish, compulsory Sunday attendance, and a fine of a shilling a week for absence.","The prayer book's words at communion were deliberately open to both a Catholic and a Protestant reading, and traditional vestments and church ornaments were retained.","The Act of Uniformity passed the House of Lords by only three votes, where Mary's Catholic bishops and Catholic peers still sat.","Royal Injunctions 1559: 57 instructions in the queen's name - an English Bible in every church, a licence required to preach, a parish register of baptisms, marriages and burials, and the removal of pilgrimages and shrines of pretended miracles.","All of Mary's bishops except one, Kitchin of Llandaff, refused the oath and lost their sees; the great majority of parish clergy conformed.","Matthew Parker became Archbishop of Canterbury in 1559.","The Church's hierarchy ran from the Supreme Governor through archbishops and bishops to the parish clergy, with church courts and visitations to enforce the Injunctions.","The parish church was the centre of local life: baptisms, marriages, burials, tithes, poor relief and royal proclamations read from the pulpit.","The settlement aimed at outward obedience rather than inward belief, which is why so many Catholics could attend church while keeping the old faith privately."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — the Puritan and Catholic challenges, 1559-69","items":["Puritans were Protestants who held that worship should contain only what scripture commands; many of their leaders returned in 1559 from exile in Geneva, Frankfurt and Strasbourg.","The crucifix dispute: Elizabeth kept a crucifix in her own chapel and wanted them in churches, but gave way in the parishes when bishops threatened to resign.","The vestments controversy, 1566: Archbishop Parker's Advertisements set out what clergy must wear; about 110 London clergy were summoned to Lambeth, and 37 refused and were deprived of their livings.","Puritan objections centred on the surplice and cope, on crucifixes and images, and on ceremonies such as kneeling to receive communion.","The Puritan challenge stayed inside the Church: no Puritan in these years questioned Elizabeth's right to rule.","Most Catholics conformed outwardly as 'church papists'; recusants who stayed away were fined a shilling a week, and before 1570 the fine was enforced lightly.","Catholic nobles and gentry were strongest in the north and north-west, where the Council of the North had to press conformity from outside.","The Papacy held back: Pius IV's invitation to the Council of Trent was refused in 1561, and Pius V told English Catholics in 1566 not to attend Church of England services.","Elizabeth was excommunicated only in February 1570, after the northern rising, by the bull Regnans in Excelsis.","Philip II of Spain gave no help to English Catholics in these years: he did not want a French-raised Mary, Queen of Scots on the English throne."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Mary, Queen of Scots and the Revolt of the Northern Earls","items":["Mary became queen of Scotland in December 1542 when she was six days old, on the death of her father James V.","Her claim came through Margaret Tudor, Henry VIII's elder sister; Henry VIII's will tried to exclude the Scottish line, and Catholics regarded Mary as legitimate and Elizabeth as not.","She married the French heir in April 1558 and was queen consort of France when he reigned as Francis II from 1559 to 1560; she quartered the English royal arms with her own.","She never ratified the Treaty of Edinburgh of July 1560, by which she was to give up her claim to the English throne.","Scotland: she returned in 1561, married Lord Darnley in 1565, bore James in 1566, and Darnley was murdered in February 1567; her marriage to the Earl of Bothwell in May 1567 destroyed her support.","She was forced to abdicate in favour of her infant son in July 1567, was defeated at Langside on 13 May 1568 and crossed into England on 16 May 1568.","In England she was held at Carlisle, then Bolton Castle, then Tutbury from early 1569 - a guest who was never charged and never free.","The conference at York opened in October 1568 and continued at Westminster; the casket letters were examined and nothing was proved either way, so Mary was neither convicted nor released.","1569: a scheme to marry Mary to Thomas Howard, Duke of Norfolk, was uncovered; Norfolk was sent to the Tower and the northern earls were summoned to court.","14 November 1569: Thomas Percy, Earl of Northumberland, and Charles Neville, Earl of Westmorland, entered Durham Cathedral, destroyed the English Bible and prayer books and had Mass celebrated.","The rebels marched south under the banner of the Five Wounds of Christ with several thousand men, besieged and took Barnard Castle, but failed to reach Mary, who was moved to Coventry.","The rising collapsed in December 1569 as the Earl of Sussex advanced from York; about 450 rebels were executed, Westmorland died in exile abroad and Northumberland was handed back from Scotland and executed at York in 1572."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 2 booklet B, the 16-mark judgement: \"'The parish church was the Elizabethan government's most effective means of controlling the country in the years 1559-69.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.\"","items":["The question as printed: 'The parish church was the Elizabethan government's most effective means of controlling the country in the years 1559-69.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the Royal Injunctions (1559) • church courts. You must also use information of your own.","Decoded: the concept is significance measured as effectiveness of control, the subject is the Church used as a machine of government rather than the doctrine it taught, and the period is fixed - so the harsher recusancy laws of the 1580s can only be used to show where the period was heading.","The criterion to state in the opening lines: a means of control is effective if it reaches every subject, if disobedience can be detected and punished, and if it works without money the Crown does not have. Rank every piece of evidence against those three tests.","Paragraph 1, for the statement - reach. Every subject was required at the parish church each Sunday by the Act of Uniformity; royal proclamations were read there from the pulpit, and after 1559 only a licensed preacher could speak from it; the Royal Injunctions of July 1559, 57 of them, put an English Bible in every church and ordered a register of baptisms, marriages and burials. Interim judgement: nothing else in Tudor government touched the whole population every week, and none of it cost the Crown a penny.","Paragraph 2, for the statement - detection and punishment. The Act of Supremacy of May 1559 required an oath from the clergy and from everyone holding office under the Crown, and sixteen of Mary's bishops lost their sees for refusing it between June and November; a royal visitation that summer sent about 125 commissioners into the dioceses to enforce the Injunctions; church courts and later visitations checked attendance, morality and wills, and absence from church was fined a shilling a week. Interim judgement: the machinery could see inside every parish, which no sheriff's office could.","Paragraph 3, against the statement - the machinery that did the heavy work. In the counties the law was enforced by unpaid Justices of the Peace sitting in quarter sessions four times a year; Lords Lieutenant raised and trained the militia; the Council of the North at York pressed conformity where local loyalty ran the other way; and when the north rose in November 1569 it was the Earl of Sussex marching out of York in December, not a church court, that ended it. Interim judgement: the parish church could report what people did, but only armed force could stop them doing it.","Paragraph 4, the point beyond the stimulus - what control through the church could not reach. The shilling fine was enforced lightly before 1570 because Elizabeth wanted quiet rather than martyrs, so most Catholics conformed outwardly and kept the old faith at home; in 1566 Pius V ruled, through the priest Laurence Vaux, that attending the service was unlawful, and most of them went on attending anyway. Control of conduct was never control of belief, which is both the limit of the instrument and the reason it worked.","The evaluation thread to run through every paragraph: keep returning to the three tests set at the start - did this reach everyone, did it catch the disobedient, and what did it cost the Crown? - so that the paragraphs rank the evidence instead of listing it.","Timing: about 25 minutes, of which four or five are spent planning. There are no SPaG marks on this paper, so every minute goes on argument and evidence; three or more developed aspects are needed for Level 4.","What the conclusion must say: agree on the criterion set - no other instrument of government reached every subject every week, detected disobedience so cheaply, or cost the Crown so little - then qualify it precisely, because the parish church governed what people did in public while the Justices, the militia and the Council of the North governed what they could do about it, and November 1569 showed which of the two the Crown reached for when obedience actually broke. The judgement must name the condition on which the Church's effectiveness rested: it controlled outward conduct only, and in these years it was never tested against belief."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:26:59Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"Judged afresh after the retarget: none of the five worked examples, the model-paragraph pair or the plan now sets a bank question or shares a printed paper's focus and stimulus points (dupscan prints 'no duplication'), and every new fact holds on checking - Martinengo forbidden the Channel in 1561¹, Trent closed 4 December 1563², Pius V's 1566 ruling carried to England by Laurence Vaux³, the ornaments rubric and the 57 Injunctions of 1559 with the ≈125 visitation commissioners⁴, sixteen Marian bishops deprived between June and November 1559⁵, Langside 13 May and the landing at Workington on 16 May 1568 with Carlisle, Bolton in July and Tutbury from January 1569⁶, the commission at York in October 1568⁷, and Sussex out of York on 13 December 1569 with Clinton's twelve thousand behind him and Barnard Castle surrendered by Sir George Bowes after eleven days⁸ - so nothing is historically wrong and only four small looseness points and one near-overlap with the June 2023 paper are left to tidy."}}},"B4-2":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"B4-2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 2 is the part of Early Elizabethan England that examiners return to most often, because it carries three of the biggest set pieces in the whole option: the Catholic plots, the execution of Mary Queen of Scots and the Spanish Armada. Booklet B gives you no sources and no extracts, so every mark has to come out of your own memory of names, dates and numbers, and the 12- and 16-mark questions expect you to weigh causes against one another rather than retell the story. Almost every judgement question here turns on the same hinge: was the danger to Elizabeth mainly religious, mainly dynastic, or mainly strategic and commercial, and did England survive through skill or through luck? A student who can argue that hinge in either direction, with dates attached to each step, can answer any version of these questions.","idea":[{"h":"The Revolt of the Northern Earls, 1569-70: an old nobility, a new religion and a rival queen","t":"Thomas Percy, Earl of Northumberland, and Charles Neville, Earl of Westmorland, headed families that had governed the border for generations and had been pushed aside under Elizabeth: the Council of the North was run by men the queen trusted, Northumberland was kept from court, and when copper was found on his land the crown claimed it. Both were Catholic, as were most of their tenants, and the religious settlement of 1559 had taken the Latin mass out of their parish churches. What made 1569 different was Mary Queen of Scots, who had fled to England in 1568 and was held at Tutbury: a Catholic heir inside the kingdom. A plan to marry her to the Duke of Norfolk would have settled the succession; when Elizabeth found out, Norfolk was sent to the Tower in October 1569 and the earls were summoned to court. Rather than face arrest they rose in November 1569, occupied Durham Cathedral, had mass celebrated there, destroyed the English Bible and prayer book and marched south behind the banner of the Five Wounds of Christ with perhaps five or six thousand men. They took Barnard Castle but turned back before the Earl of Sussex advancing from York; both earls fled into Scotland in December 1569, and from there Westmorland escaped to the Spanish Netherlands and died in exile while Northumberland was handed back and executed at York in 1572, and several hundred of their followers were hanged. A top-level answer separates the three strands and says which explains the timing: the grievances were a decade old, the religion older still, and only Mary's presence and the exposure of the Norfolk marriage explain why the rising came in 1569.","code":"B4-2 — 1 Plots and revolts at home: The Revolt of the Northern Earls, 1569–70"},{"h":"Ridolfi, Throckmorton and Babington: three plots and the machine that read them","t":"Each plot had the same shape - a foreign army, an English rising and Mary on the throne - and each was broken before it could move. Roberto Ridolfi, an Italian banker in London, planned in 1571 that the Duke of Alba would bring troops from the Netherlands, Elizabeth would be killed and Mary would marry the Duke of Norfolk; the plot was unravelled when the messenger Charles Bailly was arrested at Dover and coded letters were deciphered, and Norfolk was executed in June 1572. Francis Throckmorton acted as go-between for Mary and the Spanish ambassador Bernardino de Mendoza, working towards an invasion led by the Duke of Guise; he was watched for months before his arrest in November 1583, and papers found at his house listed Catholic sympathisers and harbours suitable for a landing. He was executed in 1584 and Mendoza expelled. Anthony Babington wrote to Mary in 1586 proposing that Elizabeth be murdered; Sir Francis Walsingham, Principal Secretary from 1573, used the double agent Gilbert Gifford to carry the letters in and out of Chartley Hall in the stopper of a beer barrel, and Thomas Phelippes deciphered them, so Mary's reply of July 1586 approving the murder reached the government before it reached the plotters. Babington and his fellow conspirators were executed in September 1586. A top-level answer does not narrate the three plots in turn: it uses them as evidence for a claim about why plotting failed - the state read the letters, the English Catholic gentry would not rise, and the promised foreign troops never sailed.","code":"B4-2 — 1 Plots and revolts at home: The features and significance of the Ridolfi, Throckmorton and Babington plots"},{"h":"The execution of Mary Queen of Scots, 1587: proof, statute and pressure","t":"Mary had been a prisoner in England since 1568 and a candidate for Elizabeth's throne since the papal bull of 1570, but Elizabeth would not kill an anointed queen and a cousin. Three things removed the obstacle. The Babington plot supplied proof: Mary's deciphered reply of July 1586 agreed to the murder of Elizabeth, which nothing in the Ridolfi or Throckmorton cases had done. Statute supplied the machinery: the Bond of Association of 1584, drawn up by Walsingham and Burghley, bound its signatories to hunt down anyone who attempted the queen's life and anyone in whose name it was attempted, and the Act for the Queen's Safety of 1585 turned that into law, so a claimant on whose behalf a plot was made could be tried by a commission. Mary was tried at Fotheringhay Castle in October 1586 and found guilty. Pressure supplied the decision: Parliament and the Privy Council had demanded her death since 1572 and now pressed openly. Elizabeth signed the warrant on 1 February 1587 and Mary was beheaded at Fotheringhay on 8 February 1587; Elizabeth blamed her secretary William Davison for sending the warrant off. The consequences ran both ways: England lost its Catholic figurehead, but Philip II could now act for himself, claiming the English throne through his descent from John of Gaunt. A top-level answer distinguishes the trigger, the legal means and the long-standing will, and can argue in either direction about whether the execution caused the Armada or merely removed the last reason for delaying it.","code":"B4-2 — 1 Plots and revolts at home: The reasons for, and significance of, Mary Queen of Scots' execution in 1587"},{"h":"Why England and Spain were rivals: politics, religion and trade","t":"Philip II had been married to Mary I from 1554 until her death in 1558 and proposed to Elizabeth in 1559; for twenty years afterwards the two crowns were rivals who avoided open war. The political rivalry was about the Netherlands, the richest possession in Philip's European empire and England's chief trading partner, lying directly across the North Sea: a Spanish army there was a standing threat, and Elizabeth's answer was to help the Dutch rebels without admitting it, sheltering the Sea Beggars in English harbours until she expelled them in 1572 and letting money and volunteers cross. The religious rivalry hardened after Elizabeth's settlement of 1559 made England Protestant and after Pope Pius V issued the bull Regnans in Excelsis in February 1570, excommunicating Elizabeth and releasing her subjects from obedience; Philip saw himself as the defender of the Catholic faith and was named in both the Ridolfi and Babington plots. The commercial rivalry was about the New World, where Spain claimed a monopoly of trade: John Hawkins' trading voyages ended when a Spanish fleet attacked his ships at San Juan de Ulua in September 1568, and in December 1568 Elizabeth seized the bullion being shipped through the Channel to pay the Duke of Alba's army, which brought a trade embargo. A top-level answer refuses to treat these as three separate lists: it shows how each quarrel made the others harder to settle, so that by 1585 there was no dispute either side could concede without conceding all of them.","code":"B4-2 — 2 Relations with Spain: Political, religious and commercial rivalry"},{"h":"Privateering and Drake: piracy the crown invested in","t":"A privateer was a private captain licensed by the crown to attack enemy shipping and share the profits with his investors, which meant that England could damage Spain while the queen denied responsibility. Francis Drake had been at San Juan de Ulua in 1568 and raided the Spanish Main afterwards, but the voyage that mattered was his circumnavigation of the globe between December 1577 and September 1580: he passed through the Strait of Magellan, renamed his ship the Golden Hind, plundered Spanish settlements and shipping along the undefended Pacific coast and captured the treasure ship Nuestra Senora de la Concepcion off Cape San Francisco (in modern Ecuador) on the Peru–Panama run in March 1579. He came home with plunder worth more than the crown's ordinary annual income, and Elizabeth, who had invested in the voyage, knighted him aboard the Golden Hind at Deptford in April 1581 instead of punishing him as Philip demanded. That was the point: privateering brought in treasure, trained seamen, taught English captains the Atlantic and told Spain that the English crown was a partner in the raids. A top-level answer uses Drake as evidence rather than as a story, arguing either that privateering was a cause of war because it made a settlement impossible, or that it was a symptom of a rivalry already fixed by religion and the Netherlands.","code":"B4-2 — 2 Relations with Spain: The significance of privateering and the activities of Drake"},{"h":"The Netherlands and Robert Dudley: from cold war to open war, 1584-88","t":"Two events in 1584 destroyed Elizabeth's policy of helping the Dutch without fighting Spain. William of Orange, the leader of the Dutch revolt, was assassinated in July 1584, leaving the rebels without a head; and Philip II allied with the French Catholic League by the Treaty of Joinville in December 1584, raising the prospect of Catholic France and Spain acting together. The Duke of Parma, Philip's commander in the Netherlands, then took town after town and captured Antwerp in August 1585. Elizabeth could either accept a Spanish Netherlands or fight, and she signed the Treaty of Nonsuch in August 1585, agreeing to pay for an army of about seven thousand men and holding Brill and Flushing as security. Robert Dudley, Earl of Leicester, led it, arriving in December 1585, and immediately made things worse by accepting the title of Governor-General from the Dutch in January 1586, which implied that Elizabeth had taken sovereignty over the provinces; she forced him to renounce it. Short of money and quarrelling with his allies, he won nothing: Sir Philip Sidney was mortally wounded at Zutphen in September 1586, Sluis fell to Parma in 1587, and Leicester came home at the end of that year. A top-level answer treats Nonsuch, not the fighting, as the turning point: it was the first open commitment of an English army against Spain, and Philip began preparing the invasion of England in response.","code":"B4-2 — 3 Outbreak of war with Spain, 1585–88: Reasons for deteriorating relations with Spain"},{"h":"The raid on Cadiz, 1587: singeing the King of Spain's beard","t":"In April 1587 Drake sailed into the harbour of Cadiz, where the fleet Philip was assembling for the invasion of England was being fitted out, and spent three days destroying or capturing about thirty ships and burning stores. He then raided the Portuguese coast around Cape St Vincent, and on 8 June 1587, off the Azores, took the carrack Sao Felipe, returning from the East Indies with a cargo that repaid the voyage several times over. Drake himself called it the singeing of the King of Spain's beard. The damage that mattered was not the ships but the supplies, above all the seasoned barrel staves that were burned: barrels made from unseasoned wood leaked and spoiled their contents, so a year later the Armada sailed with rotten food and foul water. The raid also delayed the invasion by about a year, which gave England more time to prepare, though it also gave Philip a year to build a larger fleet. A top-level answer weighs those two effects against each other rather than simply calling the raid a success, and links the barrel staves forward to the condition of the Armada's crews in 1588.","code":"B4-2 — 3 Outbreak of war with Spain, 1585–88: Drake and the raid on Cadiz"},{"h":"The Armada of 1588: the plan and what happened to it","t":"Philip's plan was not for the fleet to conquer England on its own. About 130 ships carrying roughly 19,000 soldiers and 8,000 sailors were to sail from Lisbon up the Channel to the coast of Flanders, meet the Duke of Parma's veteran army in the Netherlands, ferry it across in barges and land it in Kent for a march on London. The Marquis of Santa Cruz, who had planned it, died in February 1588 and command passed to the Duke of Medina Sidonia, a capable administrator with no experience of a naval command, who asked to be excused. The fleet was sighted off the Lizard on 19 July 1588 and beacons carried the news to London. Sailing in a crescent formation it was harried up the Channel by the English fleet under Lord Howard of Effingham, with Drake, Hawkins and Frobisher; the Rosario was lost after a collision and the San Salvador exploded, but the formation held as far as Calais, where the Armada anchored on 27 July. Parma was not ready and had no deep-water port. On the night of 28 July the English sent eight fireships in on the tide; the Spanish cut their cables and scattered, and on 29 July the English attacked at close range off Gravelines. The wind then drove the Armada north, and it had to return round Scotland and Ireland, where storms wrecked many ships; about half got home, some 67 of the roughly 130 ships that had sailed. A top-level answer knows the sequence but uses it to make a point: the plan required a rendezvous that could not be arranged, so the fighting only exposed a flaw that was there from the start.","code":"B4-2 — 4 The Armada: Spanish invasion plans and key events"},{"h":"Why England won: ships, mistakes, luck and the Dutch","t":"Four kinds of explanation compete, and a good answer ranks them rather than listing them. English advantages: the race-built galleons redesigned under John Hawkins as Treasurer of the Navy were lower, faster and more manoeuvrable, and their long-range guns on light carriages could be reloaded quickly on deck, so Howard's captains could fire and stay out of reach. Spanish weaknesses: Medina Sidonia was inexperienced, Spanish tactics depended on closing and boarding with soldiers, the heavy gun carriages were hard to work in a running fight, and the food and water were spoiled by the unseasoned barrels forced on them after Cadiz. Spanish planning: there was no deep-water port on the Flemish coast, Parma's barges could not come out past the Dutch flyboats blockading Dunkirk and Nieuwpoort, and messages between the two commanders took days, so the rendezvous was impossible. Luck and the weather: the wind at Calais suited the fireships, and the storms round Scotland and Ireland destroyed more ships than the English guns did. Elizabeth's speech to the troops at Tilbury in August 1588, telling them that though she had the body of a weak and feeble woman she had the heart and stomach of a king, was delivered after the fleet had been driven north, so it belongs to the consequences rather than the causes. A top-level answer chooses a criterion - which factor, if removed, would have changed the outcome - and applies it consistently.","code":"B4-2 — 4 The Armada: The reasons for the English victory"}],"examples":[{"title":"Answering the two-part describe question without wasting a sentence","setup":"The question is: 1(a) Describe one feature of the fighting at San Juan de Ulua (1568). [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of Elizabeth's seizure of the Spanish bullion in 1568. [2 marks] You know that John Hawkins' trading squadron put into San Juan de Ulua, the harbour serving Veracruz in New Spain, to repair storm damage in September 1568; that terms were agreed with the incoming viceroy and then broken when the Spanish fleet attacked, and that only two ships got away, Hawkins in the Minion and Francis Drake in the Judith; and that late in 1568 small Spanish ships carrying coin for the Duke of Alba's army in the Netherlands sheltered from privateers in Plymouth and Southampton, that the money was a loan raised from Genoese bankers and had not yet been delivered, that Elizabeth kept it in December 1568 and treated it as a loan to herself, and that Alba answered by seizing English goods and closing the Netherlands to English trade.","steps":["For part (a), choose the feature you can support hardest rather than the one you thought of first: the fighting began with a Spanish attack on ships that had just been promised safety.","Attach one precise detail to it and stop: in September 1568 the Spanish fleet opened fire inside the harbour days after terms had been agreed with the incoming viceroy, and only two English ships escaped, Hawkins in the Minion and Drake in the Judith.","For part (b), pick a different aspect, because the two parts are marked separately and repeating the attack on English trading voyages would waste the second one: the seizure took money that belonged to somebody else.","Support that with what the queen actually did - the coin was a loan raised from Genoese bankers that had not yet reached Alba's army, so in December 1568 Elizabeth kept it and treated it as a loan made to her instead.","Reread both answers and delete any sentence beginning with because, since reasons and consequences earn nothing here; a feature is a statement of what something was or did."],"cues":["Several features of the fighting come to mind: how do you decide which one to write about?","What one fact proves you know that feature?","What must you check about your part (a) answer before choosing a feature for part (b)?","What did the queen actually do with the money?","Which of your sentences is explaining rather than describing?"]},{"title":"Planning a 12-mark explanation so that the cap cannot bite","setup":"The question is: Explain why the Spanish Armada did not sail until 1588. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: bullet one, Drake's raid on Cadiz (1587); bullet two, the death of the Marquis of Santa Cruz. You must also use information of your own. You know that Philip II asked Santa Cruz and the Duke of Parma for invasion plans in 1586 and settled on a fleet from Spain joining Parma's army in the Netherlands; that in April 1587 Drake spent three days in Cadiz harbour destroying or capturing about thirty ships and burning stores, and then cruised off Cape St Vincent taking the coasting vessels that carried hoops and seasoned barrel staves towards Lisbon; that Santa Cruz died in February 1588 and the command passed to the Duke of Medina Sidonia, who had never held a naval command and asked to be excused; and that even when the fleet left Lisbon at the end of May 1588 storms drove it into Corunna, so that it did not enter the Channel until July.","steps":["Sort the material by cause rather than by year, so that the answer explains instead of narrating: the destruction of supplies, the loss of the commander, and the size of the enterprise itself.","Build the first reason around the stores and not the ships - the hoops and seasoned barrel staves burned at Cadiz and taken off Cape St Vincent - so that Drake's raid is evidence for a claim rather than a heading.","Complete that causal chain: casks had to be made again from unseasoned wood that leaked and spoiled what it held, and no fleet can sail until its food and water will keep, so the sailing slipped by about a year.","Take the second reason past the bullet: Santa Cruz had designed the expedition, and his death in February 1588 handed it to a commander who had to learn the fleet, its officers and the plan before he could move any of them.","Take the third reason past the bullets as well: the plan Philip chose in 1586 required a fleet from Spain and an army in the Netherlands to be ready at the same moment, and gathering ships, soldiers and provisions for a long campaign from across his empire was slow before Drake ever reached Cadiz.","Finish with a sentence that ranks the reasons rather than repeating them, saying which one cost the most time and which would have delayed the fleet even if the others had not happened."],"cues":["Should the paragraphs be named after years or after causes?","What did the raid destroy that mattered more than the ships?","Why can a fleet not sail on casks of unseasoned wood?","What does an expedition lose when the man who planned it dies?","What had to be ready at the same moment as the ships?","What must the last sentence do that a summary does not?"]},{"title":"Turning the story of a royal prisoner into an argument about danger","setup":"The question is: Explain why Mary, Queen of Scots, was a threat to Elizabeth I in the years 1568-86. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: bullet one, Mary's flight to England in 1568; bullet two, the Duke of Norfolk. You must also use information of your own. The material you have is: Mary was defeated at Langside in May 1568 and crossed into England, where she was held as a prisoner until her execution, at Carlisle and Bolton first and later at Tutbury, Chartley and Fotheringhay; she was a Catholic and a great-granddaughter of Henry VII, and Elizabeth had no child and would not name an heir; a plan to marry Mary to the Duke of Norfolk was discovered in 1569, Norfolk was sent to the Tower in October and the Northern Earls rose the next month; the bull Regnans in Excelsis of February 1570 declared that Elizabeth was no lawful queen; and the Ridolfi, Throckmorton and Babington plots each proposed to put Mary on the throne with foreign troops.","steps":["Sort the material by the kind of danger rather than by the order of events, so that the answer explains instead of narrating: her claim, her presence inside the kingdom, and the foreign backing she attracted.","Build the first reason on the succession and keep it precise: Mary's descent from Henry VII made her the nearest Catholic claimant, and because Elizabeth had no child and would not name an heir, the death of one queen would have delivered the crown to the other.","Complete that causal chain: a claimant who gains by the queen's death turns a grievance into a plan, and the bull of February 1570, by denying Elizabeth's title, made Mary's claim look lawful to Catholics who had accepted her before.","Take the second reason past the bullets: because Mary was in England rather than in Scotland or France, a rising did not have to wait for a landing, and the earls could march south in November 1569 towards a prisoner held at Tutbury.","Use Norfolk as evidence for that reason rather than as a separate story: the marriage would have made Mary the settled heir without a shot being fired, and it was the discovery of the plan that turned courtiers into rebels.","Finish with a sentence that ranks the reasons rather than repeating them, saying which one made Mary dangerous in every year she was in England and which made her dangerous in particular years."],"cues":["Should the paragraphs be named after events or after kinds of danger?","Who stood to gain if Elizabeth died?","What did the papal bull add to Mary's claim?","Why did it matter so much that she was inside England?","What would the Norfolk marriage have achieved without fighting?","What must the last sentence do that a summary does not?"]},{"title":"Choosing a criterion before writing a 16-mark judgement","setup":"The question is: The Babington plot of 1586 was the most dangerous of the Catholic plots against Elizabeth. How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: bullet one, the Ridolfi plot (1571); bullet two, the Throckmorton plot (1583). You must also use information of your own. The material you have is: Ridolfi planned that the Duke of Alba would bring troops from the Netherlands, that Elizabeth would be killed and that Mary would marry the Duke of Norfolk, and the plot was broken when the messenger Charles Bailly was arrested at Dover and his letters were deciphered; Throckmorton carried messages between Mary and the Spanish ambassador Bernardino de Mendoza for an invasion led by Henry, Duke of Guise, and was watched for months before papers listing Catholic sympathisers and harbours suitable for a landing were found at his house; Anthony Babington wrote to Mary in 1586 proposing the murder of Elizabeth, and Gilbert Gifford carried the letters in and out of Chartley in the stopper of a beer barrel for Thomas Phelippes to decipher; and only the Babington plot drew a reply from Mary, in July 1586, agreeing to the murder.","steps":["State the test you will judge by before you argue anything, because the third bullet of the mark scheme is the judgement: ask whether danger means how near a plot came to killing the queen or how much it changed what came after.","Put the case for the statement first and make it as strong as it deserves: only Babington's plot produced Mary's own written consent to the murder, and it ended in her trial at Fotheringhay and her execution, which nothing earlier had achieved.","Then show the limit of that case: by 1586 the plot was running inside a government trap, since Gifford was Walsingham's man and every letter was read before the plotters received it, so Elizabeth was never nearer death than her code-breakers allowed.","Argue the first bullet the same way, granting its force and then testing it: Ridolfi's plan had a veteran army behind it in Alba's troops in the Netherlands and the premier duke of England ready to marry Mary, which is a larger danger than a circle of young gentlemen in London.","Bring in the factor the bullets do not mention and let it carry the argument: the Revolt of the Northern Earls had already shown in 1569 what a rising and a Catholic claimant could do together, and not one of the three plots ever brought a single English county out.","Close by applying the test you announced, ranking Babington as the plot with the greatest consequences while naming Ridolfi as the one that came nearest to putting a foreign army on English soil."],"cues":["What question must the conclusion be able to answer?","What did the plot of 1586 produce that the others did not?","Who was really in charge of the letters by then?","What did Ridolfi have behind him that Babington did not?","What had already happened in 1569 that no plot managed to repeat?","How do you rank two dangers without dismissing either?"]},{"title":"Replacing vague knowledge with evidence an examiner can mark","setup":"A student has written this sentence in an answer about why Mary Queen of Scots was executed: Mary kept plotting against Elizabeth and eventually she got caught, so Parliament made Elizabeth execute her. The facts available are: Mary's part in the Ridolfi plot of 1571 was suspected but not proved; Anthony Babington wrote to her in 1586 proposing the murder of Elizabeth and her reply of July 1586 agreed to it; Gilbert Gifford carried the letters and Thomas Phelippes deciphered them; the Bond of Association of 1584 and the Act for the Queen's Safety of 1585 allowed a claimant on whose behalf a plot was made to be tried by a commission; Mary was tried at Fotheringhay Castle in October 1586; Elizabeth signed the warrant on 1 February 1587; and Mary was beheaded on 8 February 1587.","steps":["Find the three separate claims hidden inside the sentence, because each of them needs different evidence: that Mary plotted, that she was caught, and that Parliament forced the queen's hand.","Replace kept plotting with the distinction that matters: her involvement in 1571 could be suspected but not proved, so nothing could be done about it.","Replace got caught with the mechanism: Gifford carried her letters, Phelippes deciphered them, and her reply of July 1586 approved the murder, which gave the crown written proof.","Add the step the original sentence leaves out altogether - the statute that made a trial possible - since without the Act for the Queen's Safety the proof would have had nowhere to go.","Rewrite the Parliament claim as pressure rather than compulsion, and let the dates show the queen's reluctance between the warrant and the block.","Check the rewritten passage against the question: every date now earns its place because it marks a stage in the argument rather than a stage in the story."],"cues":["How many claims is that one sentence making?","What was different about the evidence in the earlier plot?","By what route did the government obtain proof?","What had to exist before a queen could be tried?","Did Parliament have the power to compel Elizabeth?","What is the test for keeping a date in an essay?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which single development explains why the Northern Earls rebelled in 1569 rather than in any earlier year?","a":"The presence of Mary Queen of Scots in England and the collapse of the plan to marry her to the Duke of Norfolk, after which the earls were summoned to court and expected arrest."},{"q":"What did the Babington plot give the government that the Ridolfi and Throckmorton plots had not?","a":"Written proof of Mary's own consent to the murder of Elizabeth, in her deciphered reply of July 1586."},{"q":"Why did the raid on Cadiz in 1587 still affect the Armada a year later?","a":"Seasoned barrel staves were destroyed, so the fleet sailed with barrels of unseasoned wood whose food and water spoiled."},{"q":"Why was the Spanish plan of 1588 in trouble before the fighting began?","a":"Spain held no deep-water port on the Flemish coast, Parma's barges could not get out past the Dutch flyboats, and messages between Medina Sidonia and Parma took days."}]},"room":{"facts":["Booklet B, the British depth study, carries 32 marks. Question 1(a) and Question 1(b) are Describe one feature of..., 2 marks each and on two different aspects of content; Question 2 is an Explain why... at 12 marks with two stimulus points; and Question 3 or Question 4, a choice of one, is a quoted statement followed by How far do you agree? Explain your answer., at 16 marks with two stimulus points.","The describe task is point-marked, not levelled: one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information about it, in each of the two parts. No reasons and no judgement are wanted, and a second feature inside one part earns nothing.","The caps as the mark scheme now prints them: on the 12-mark question, a maximum of 8 marks and no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; on the 16-mark question, a maximum of 11 marks and no access to Level 4 on the same condition. There is no cap at Level 2. Two stimulus bullets are two aspects, so an answer built only on the bullets cannot pass those ceilings.","The AO split is AO1 4 on the describe parts, AO2 6 and AO1 6 on the 12-mark question, and AO2 10 and AO1 6 on the 16-mark judgement, whose grid prints a third bullet in every level for the overall judgement. Performance in AO1 and AO2 is interdependent: an answer showing no qualities of AO2 cannot rise above the top of Level 1 however strong the knowledge.","Paper 2 lasts 1 hour 50 minutes for both booklets and carries no sources, no interpretations and no marks for spelling, punctuation and grammar, so every mark comes from your own knowledge. Work at about a minute a mark in booklet B: roughly 5 minutes on the two describe parts, 20 on the 12-mark question and 25 on the 16-mark judgement, with the planning taken out of that 25."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the Revolt of the Northern Earls (1569-70). [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the papal bull Regnans in Excelsis (1570). [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked as two separate 2-mark parts: in each part one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information, and a second feature within a part earns nothing. (a) One feature: the rising was openly Catholic; credit also that it was led by Northumberland and Westmorland, that it marched south towards Mary Queen of Scots, or that it was put down by the Earl of Sussex. (b) One feature: it excommunicated Elizabeth; credit also that it denied she was lawfully queen, that it released her subjects from obedience, or that it was issued by Pope Pius V.","sol":"(a) One feature of the revolt was that it was openly Catholic in its aims. In November 1569 the rebels entered Durham Cathedral, had mass celebrated there and destroyed the English Bible and prayer book kept in it, and they marched south behind the banner of the Five Wounds of Christ. (b) One feature of the bull was that it attacked Elizabeth's right to rule. Issued by Pope Pius V in February 1570, it declared her excommunicated and no lawful queen and released her subjects from their obedience to her, so English Catholics were told that loyalty to the crown and loyalty to their Church were now a choice.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a) asks what kind of rising it was; part (b) asks what the document itself said.","Choose aspects that do not overlap, because repeating religion in part (b) wastes the second pair of marks: for the bull, think about Elizabeth's authority rather than about worship.","One short paragraph each: name the feature in the first sentence and attach one dated, placed detail to it."],"codes":["B4-2 — 1 Plots and revolts at home: The Revolt of the Northern Earls, 1569–70"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the Ridolfi plot (1571). [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of Walsingham's spy network. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked as two separate 2-mark parts: one mark for the feature and one for supporting information in each. (a) One feature: it planned a Spanish invasion from the Netherlands; credit also the intended marriage of Mary to the Duke of Norfolk, the plan to kill Elizabeth, or Ridolfi's role as an Italian banker and papal agent. (b) One feature: it used paid informers at home and abroad; credit also the interception and deciphering of letters by Thomas Phelippes, the use of double agents such as Gilbert Gifford, or the use of torture on suspects.","sol":"(a) One feature of the Ridolfi plot was that it depended on foreign troops. Roberto Ridolfi, an Italian banker working in London, planned that the Duke of Alba would land a Spanish army from the Netherlands while English Catholics rose, Elizabeth was killed and Mary Queen of Scots took the throne. (b) One feature of Walsingham's network was that it intercepted and read letters rather than merely reporting rumours. His code-breaker Thomas Phelippes deciphered the coded correspondence that passed between plotters, which is how the government could follow a conspiracy from inside and wait until the evidence was complete before arresting anyone.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["For (a) ask what the plot needed in order to work; for (b) ask what the government actually did to a plotter's letters.","Keep the two parts on different aspects: one is about a single conspiracy, the other about a permanent system.","Give each feature one named person or one concrete method as its supporting detail."],"codes":["B4-2 — 1 Plots and revolts at home: The features and significance of the Ridolfi, Throckmorton and Babington plots"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the Babington plot (1586). [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the trial of Mary Queen of Scots in 1586. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked as two separate 2-mark parts. (a) One feature: it planned the murder of Elizabeth; credit also the smuggling of letters to Chartley Hall in a beer barrel, Gilbert Gifford's role as a double agent, the expected Spanish invasion, or the execution of Babington and his fellow conspirators in September 1586. (b) One feature: Mary was tried by a commission of English nobles and councillors; credit also that it met at Fotheringhay Castle in October 1586, that it was held under the Act for the Queen's Safety of 1585, that Mary denied the court's authority, or that she was found guilty.","sol":"(a) One feature of the Babington plot was that it involved a plan to murder Elizabeth. Anthony Babington wrote to Mary Queen of Scots in 1586 proposing that Elizabeth be killed, that Mary be freed and that a Spanish force invade, and Mary's reply of July 1586 agreed to it. (b) One feature of the trial was that Mary was tried by a commission of English noblemen and councillors rather than by her fellow monarchs. It sat at Fotheringhay Castle in October 1586 under the Act for the Queen's Safety of 1585, and Mary protested that as an anointed queen she was not subject to English law.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a) is about what the plotters intended; part (b) is about how Mary was dealt with once the plot was known.","A feature of a trial can be who sat on it, where it sat, or what law it used - not why it happened.","Anchor each part with a date and a place so the supporting mark is unmistakable."],"codes":["B4-2 — 1 Plots and revolts at home: The features and significance of the Ridolfi, Throckmorton and Babington plots","B4-2 — 1 Plots and revolts at home: The reasons for, and significance of, Mary Queen of Scots' execution in 1587"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of Drake's voyage round the world (1577-80). [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of English privateering in Elizabeth's reign. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked as two separate 2-mark parts. (a) One feature: Drake attacked Spanish shipping and settlements in the Pacific; credit also the passage of the Strait of Magellan, the renaming of his ship the Golden Hind, the capture of the Nuestra Senora de la Concepcion in 1579, the return in September 1580, or the knighthood at Deptford in April 1581. (b) One feature: privateers sailed with the crown's licence and shared their profits with investors; credit also that Elizabeth herself invested, that it was aimed at Spain's monopoly of American trade, or that it trained seamen and ships later used against the Armada.","sol":"(a) One feature of the voyage was that Drake plundered Spanish settlements and shipping along the Pacific coast of South America, which Spain had left undefended because no enemy had ever reached it. Off Cape San Francisco, near Esmeraldas in modern Ecuador, in March 1579 he captured the treasure ship Nuestra Senora de la Concepcion. (b) One feature of privateering was that it was licensed by the crown and its profits shared. Elizabeth invested in Drake's voyage and knighted him aboard the Golden Hind at Deptford in April 1581 rather than punishing him, which told Spain that the English crown was a partner in the raiding.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a) wants something Drake did on the voyage; part (b) wants a rule or a habit of the trade as a whole.","For (b), think about the relationship between the captain and the queen rather than about any single voyage.","Each part needs one hard detail: a named ship, a place or a date will do."],"codes":["B4-2 — 2 Relations with Spain: The significance of privateering and the activities of Drake"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the Treaty of Nonsuch (1585). [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the Earl of Leicester's command in the Netherlands (1585-87). [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked as two separate 2-mark parts. (a) One feature: Elizabeth agreed to send and pay for an army in the Netherlands; credit also the figure of about seven thousand men, the holding of Brill and Flushing as security, or the command given to the Earl of Leicester. (b) One feature: Leicester accepted the title of Governor-General from the Dutch in January 1586 against Elizabeth's wishes; credit also his arrival in December 1585, the shortage of money and the quarrels with his allies, the action at Zutphen in September 1586 where Sir Philip Sidney was mortally wounded, the loss of Sluis in 1587, or his return to England at the end of 1587.","sol":"(a) One feature of the Treaty of Nonsuch was that Elizabeth undertook to pay for an English army in the Netherlands, about seven thousand men under the Earl of Leicester, and to hold the ports of Brill and Flushing as security for her costs. It was the first open commitment of English forces against Spain. (b) One feature of Leicester's command was that he exceeded his instructions politically. In January 1586 he accepted the title of Governor-General from the Dutch States General, which implied that Elizabeth had taken sovereignty over the provinces, and she forced him to renounce it.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a) asks what England promised to do; part (b) asks what happened once the commander arrived.","For (b) the strongest feature concerns Leicester's relations with the Dutch or with his own queen, not a battle.","Give each part a number, a title or a date as its second sentence."],"codes":["B4-2 — 3 Outbreak of war with Spain, 1585–88: Reasons for deteriorating relations with Spain"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the Spanish Armada that sailed in 1588. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of English warships in 1588. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4 marks, point-marked as two separate 2-mark parts. (a) One feature: it was a very large fleet of about 130 ships; credit also the roughly 19,000 soldiers and 8,000 sailors, the command of the Duke of Medina Sidonia after Santa Cruz's death, the crescent formation, or the plan to collect Parma's army. (b) One feature: the race-built galleons were lower, faster and more manoeuvrable; credit also the long-range guns on light carriages that could be reloaded on deck, the redesign under John Hawkins, or the tactic of firing from a distance rather than boarding.","sol":"(a) One feature of the Armada was its size: about 130 ships carried roughly 19,000 soldiers and 8,000 sailors, and it sailed in a crescent formation with the strongest ships on the horns so that weaker vessels were shielded. (b) One feature of the English warships was that they were built to fight with guns rather than with soldiers. The race-built galleons redesigned under John Hawkins were lower and faster than Spanish ships, and their long-range guns sat on light carriages that could be hauled in and reloaded quickly on deck.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Part (a) can be answered with numbers or with shape; part (b) with design.","For (b), ask what the English ships were designed to do that Spanish ships were not.","Add one figure or one name to each feature and then stop."],"codes":["B4-2 — 4 The Armada: Spanish invasion plans and key events","B4-2 — 4 The Armada: The reasons for the English victory"]},{"q":"Explain why Elizabeth's government needed a network of spies in the years 1570-88. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the papal bull of 1570 • Mary, Queen of Scots. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 / AO1 6. Credit the effect of Regnans in Excelsis (February 1570) in making obedience a matter of choice for English Catholics, so that loyalty could no longer be assumed; the presence in England of a Catholic claimant around whom every serious plot was built (Ridolfi 1571, Throckmorton 1583, Babington 1586) and the letters that carried those plots; the need for evidence that would convict in court rather than suspicion, which is why suspects were watched for months; the absence of a standing army or a police force; and the foreign backing from Spain, Rome and the Guise that made agents necessary abroad as well as at home. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"Elizabeth's government needed spies because after 1570 it could no longer tell its enemies from its subjects, because the rival claimant to the throne was living inside the kingdom, and because the crown had no other machinery for its own defence.\n\nThe change of 1570 came first. Regnans in Excelsis, issued by Pope Pius V in February 1570, excommunicated Elizabeth, denied that she was lawfully queen and released her subjects from their obedience. A Catholic gentleman who had gone quietly to his parish church for eleven years was now told by his Church that obedience was a sin. Most stayed loyal, but nothing visible separated those who did from those who did not, and that is a problem only information can solve.\n\nMary Queen of Scots made the problem urgent. She had been in England since 1568 and was the nearest Catholic claimant, and every serious conspiracy was built around putting her on the throne: Ridolfi in 1571, Throckmorton in 1583, Babington in 1586. Her correspondence was the nerve of each plot, so reading it - the letters taken from Charles Bailly at Dover, and those carried into Chartley in the stopper of a beer barrel and deciphered by Thomas Phelippes - was the only way of learning what was intended before it happened.\n\nBeyond the bullets lies the reason the other two mattered so much. England had no standing army and no police force, and a treason trial needed evidence. Sir Francis Walsingham, Principal Secretary from 1573, therefore ran informers at home and agents abroad in Paris, Madrid and the seminaries, and preferred to watch a plot for months, as Throckmorton was watched before his arrest in November 1583, rather than strike early and convict nobody.\n\nRanked, the bull is the deepest cause, because it created the subject whose loyalty could not be assumed; Mary decided what the spies were looking for, and the weakness of the state decided that spying had to be the answer.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Start with what changed in February 1570, not with Walsingham himself.","Ask what all three plots had in common, and where the evidence for them was written down.","The point beyond the bullets is something the government did not have: name it and say what it had to use instead."],"codes":["B4-2 — 1 Plots and revolts at home: The features and significance of the Ridolfi, Throckmorton and Babington plots"]},{"q":"Explain why Catholic plots against Elizabeth failed in the years 1571-86. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • Sir Francis Walsingham • the Bond of Association (1584). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 / AO1 6. Credit government surveillance (informers, Phelippes and the deciphering of letters, Gifford at Chartley, the months of watching before Throckmorton's arrest in November 1583), the loyalty of the political nation (the Bond of Association 1584, the Act for the Queen's Safety 1585, the refusal of English Catholics to rise), and the failure of promised foreign help (Alba in 1571, Guise in 1583, Philip II in 1586). Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"Three things defeated the plots: the government read them, England would not rise for them, and the foreign armies they depended on never came.\n\nThe first reason is the machinery Sir Francis Walsingham built after becoming Principal Secretary in 1573. He paid informers at home and abroad and employed Thomas Phelippes to break ciphers, so the crown did not merely suspect conspiracies, it read them. Francis Throckmorton was watched for months before his arrest in November 1583, which is why the search of his house produced a list of Catholic sympathisers and plans of harbours suitable for a landing. In 1586 the double agent Gilbert Gifford carried Mary Queen of Scots' letters in and out of Chartley Hall hidden in the stopper of a beer barrel; every letter passed through Phelippes first, so her reply of July 1586 approving the murder of Elizabeth was in government hands before Anthony Babington could move. Reading a plot lets a government wait until the evidence will convict everyone, which is exactly what happened.\n\nThe second reason is that the plotters had no following. The Bond of Association of 1584 shows why: thousands of gentlemen signed a promise to hunt down anyone who attempted the queen's life and anyone in whose name the attempt was made, and Parliament gave it statutory form in the Act for the Queen's Safety of 1585. The political nation had decided that Elizabeth's death meant civil war, so a conspiracy of a handful of gentlemen could never become a rebellion.\n\nThe third reason, which the bullets do not prompt, is that the foreign help was always promised and never sailed. Ridolfi's plan of 1571 needed the Duke of Alba's troops from the Netherlands; Throckmorton's needed the Duke of Guise from France; Babington's needed Philip II. None of them moved, because Spain was tied down by the Dutch revolt and France by its own religious wars.\n\nSurveillance therefore decided when each plot was broken, but the absence of English support and of foreign troops decided that none of them could have succeeded in any case.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Group the material by cause rather than by plot, or the answer will turn into three short stories.","Ask what a government gains by watching a conspirator instead of arresting him at once.","Add a reason the bullets do not prompt, and rank the reasons in your final sentence."],"codes":["B4-2 — 1 Plots and revolts at home: The features and significance of the Ridolfi, Throckmorton and Babington plots","B4-2 — 2 Relations with Spain: Political, religious and commercial rivalry"]},{"q":"Explain why the Revolt of the Northern Earls was defeated by the end of 1569. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the Earl of Sussex • foreign help. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 / AO1 6. Credit the weight and speed of the royal response (Sussex, Lord President of the Council of the North, advancing from York; the larger army raised under the Earl of Warwick and Lord Admiral Clinton; Barnard Castle held by Sir George Bowes for eleven days), the failure of foreign help (no Spanish troops from Alba in the Netherlands, the papal bull only in February 1570), the removal of Mary Queen of Scots from Tutbury to Coventry, and the narrow base of the rising (the earls' own tenants, no rising elsewhere, most English Catholics loyal). Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"The revolt collapsed because the crown could put armies in the field that the earls could not match, because the help they were counting on never came, and because the prize they were marching for was moved out of their reach.\n\nThe military balance decided it. The rebels took Durham in November 1569 and marched south, but when the Earl of Sussex, Lord President of the Council of the North, came out of York against them, and a second and far larger royal army was raised under the Earl of Warwick and Lord Admiral Clinton, the earls turned back rather than risk a battle. They fell back on the north, where Sir George Bowes held Barnard Castle against them for eleven days, and by December 1569 their force had melted away; both earls fled into Scotland.\n\nHelp from abroad never arrived. The rising was meant to be part of something larger, with backing from the Duke of Alba's Spanish army in the Netherlands and the blessing of Rome, but Alba would not commit troops to a rebellion that had not first shown it could win, and Pope Pius V's bull excommunicating Elizabeth was not issued until February 1570, when the rebels were already scattered. The call that might have brought other English Catholics out came only after the rising was over.\n\nBeyond the bullets, the revolt lost its object. Its point was Mary Queen of Scots, and the government simply moved her: from Tutbury, which the rebels were marching towards, to Coventry, deep in loyal midland country. After that the earls had nothing to offer their followers but their own grievances, and the rising stayed what it had begun as, a Percy and Neville affair of northern tenants that no other part of England joined.\n\nThe earls were beaten less in battle than by arithmetic. They were a regional force with no ally, no candidate within reach and no answer to the question of what to do next, and once Sussex and Warwick were in the field the only choice left was flight.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["The rising lasted about six weeks, so ask what the government did in those weeks rather than what was wrong with the rebels' cause.","Three things are worth separating: the forces the crown raised, the allies who never came, and what happened to the person the rebels were marching towards.","Begin with the armies, naming who led them and where they came from, and the rest of the answer follows from the earls' retreat."],"codes":["B4-2 — 1 Plots and revolts at home: The Revolt of the Northern Earls, 1569–70"]},{"q":"Explain why relations between England and Spain worsened in the years 1566-85. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the Netherlands • privateering. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 / AO1 6. Credit the strategic quarrel over the Netherlands (Alba's army from 1567, the Sea Beggars in English ports until 1572, William of Orange's assassination in 1584, Antwerp in 1585, the Treaty of Nonsuch), the commercial quarrel (Spain's American monopoly, San Juan de Ulua in 1568, the seized bullion and the embargo, Drake's circumnavigation and knighthood in 1581), and religion (the 1559 settlement, the papal bull of 1570, Spanish involvement in the Ridolfi and Throckmorton plots, Mendoza's expulsion in 1584). Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"Relations broke down because a strategic quarrel, a commercial quarrel and a religious quarrel all pulled in the same direction, and none of them could be settled without conceding the others.\n\nThe strategic quarrel was the Netherlands. They were England's most important trading partner and lay directly across the North Sea, so a Spanish army stationed there was a permanent threat. When the Dutch revolt began, Philip II sent the Duke of Alba with an army in 1567 and his Council of Troubles executed rebels in large numbers. Elizabeth would not fight openly, but she let the Dutch rebel ships known as the Sea Beggars use English harbours until she expelled them in 1572, and allowed money and volunteers to reach the rebels. Philip regarded this as making war while professing peace.\n\nThe commercial quarrel was privateering. Spain claimed a monopoly of trade with its American empire and English captains broke it. John Hawkins' trading voyages ended when a Spanish fleet attacked his ships at San Juan de Ulua in September 1568, and that December Elizabeth seized the bullion being carried through the Channel to pay Alba's troops, which brought a Spanish embargo on English trade. Francis Drake then plundered Spanish shipping and settlements during his voyage round the world between 1577 and 1580, taking the treasure ship Nuestra Senora de la Concepcion in 1579, and Elizabeth knighted him aboard the Golden Hind in April 1581 instead of punishing him as Philip demanded.\n\nBeyond the bullets, religion made every dispute permanent. Elizabeth's settlement of 1559 had made England Protestant, and the papal bull of February 1570 excommunicated her and released her subjects from obedience. Philip was named in the Ridolfi plot of 1571, and his ambassador Bernardino de Mendoza was expelled in 1584 for his part in the Throckmorton plot.\n\nBy 1585 each quarrel was feeding the others: with William of Orange assassinated in 1584 and Antwerp fallen in 1585, Elizabeth chose the Treaty of Nonsuch and an English army in the Netherlands, turning twenty years of hostility into open war.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Three different kinds of dispute were running at once, and they are not equally old.","Ask why each quarrel could not be settled on its own terms without touching the other two.","Finish at 1585 and say what made that year the point of no return."],"codes":["B4-2 — 2 Relations with Spain: Political, religious and commercial rivalry","B4-2 — 2 Relations with Spain: The significance of privateering and the activities of Drake","B4-2 — 3 Outbreak of war with Spain, 1585–88: Reasons for deteriorating relations with Spain"]},{"q":"Explain why Elizabeth sent Drake to attack Cadiz in 1587. (12 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • Philip II's invasion preparations • the merchants who paid for the voyage. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 6 / AO1 6. Credit the intelligence that Philip II had been gathering ships, guns and stores at Cadiz and Lisbon since 1586; the execution of Mary Queen of Scots in February 1587, which removed Philip's reason for holding back; the decision to strike first because England could not win a long war of resources and a fleet at sea cannot be stopped by one still building; the joint-stock character of the voyage, mostly financed by London merchants with the queen lending ships of her own, and the return those investors expected; and Elizabeth's later order restraining the attack, which reached Plymouth after Drake had sailed. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"Elizabeth sent Drake to Cadiz because her government knew that an invasion fleet was being gathered, because attacking it was cheaper than waiting for it, and because the voyage could be made to pay for itself.\n\nThe first reason was intelligence. Philip II had been assembling ships, guns and stores at Cadiz and Lisbon since 1586, and the execution of Mary Queen of Scots in February 1587 had taken away his reason for holding back: while Mary lived, an invasion risked putting a queen with French connections on the English throne, and with her dead Philip could claim it for himself. A fleet gathering in Spanish harbours was no longer a rumour but a timetable.\n\nThe second reason was arithmetic. England had no standing army and no ally able to help, and Spain could outbuild her; a fleet already at sea cannot be stopped by one still being built. Shipping and stores crowded into a harbour, on the other hand, can be burned by seamen willing to sail in among them, which is why Drake's orders were to prevent the joining of Spain's forces and to damage them in their own ports.\n\nThe third reason is the one the second bullet points at. The expedition was a business: a joint-stock venture financed mostly by London merchants, with the queen lending ships of her own, and its investors expected a return. Drake gave them one when he took the carrack Sao Felipe off the Azores on 8 June 1587. A war fought by investors cost the crown little and could be disowned if it went wrong, which is why Elizabeth, having authorised the voyage, sent an order restraining it that never reached him, since he had sailed from Plymouth in April 1587 before it could be delivered.\n\nRanked, the intelligence comes first, because without the news from Spain there was nothing to pre-empt: the money made the voyage possible, but the fear of Philip's fleet made it necessary.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Begin with what Elizabeth's government knew in the spring of 1587, and why it knew it then rather than earlier.","Ask why the weaker power attacks first instead of waiting to be attacked.","The second bullet is about money: say who paid for the voyage and what they expected back."],"codes":["B4-2 — 3 Outbreak of war with Spain, 1585–88: Drake and the raid on Cadiz","B4-2 — 2 Relations with Spain: The significance of privateering and the activities of Drake"]},{"q":"'Religion was the main reason for the Revolt of the Northern Earls in 1569.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • Durham Cathedral • the Council of the North. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 / AO1 6. Credit the religious case (the 1559 settlement, the mass in Durham Cathedral in November 1569, the banner of the Five Wounds, the conservatism of the north) weighed against the earls' decline (the Council of the North, Northumberland kept from court, the crown's claim to his copper, Westmorland's debts) and, beyond the bullets, the dynastic trigger (Mary at Tutbury, the Norfolk marriage, Norfolk in the Tower in October 1569, the summons to court), with a criterion-based judgement on timing. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"Judged by what the rebels did, religion looks decisive; judged by why the earls rebelled at all, it was the cause they adopted rather than the cause they had. The test I shall apply is which factor explains the timing of the rising and not merely the mood behind it.\n\nThe case for religion is strong in the behaviour of the rising. The north was the most religiously conservative part of England and the settlement of 1559 had taken the Latin mass out of its parish churches. When the rebels entered Durham Cathedral in November 1569 they had mass celebrated there and destroyed the English Bible and prayer book kept in it, and they marched south behind the banner of the Five Wounds of Christ, the same emblem carried in the Pilgrimage of Grace in 1536. That was a deliberate appeal to memory: thousands of tenants would turn out for the old religion who would never turn out for a nobleman's quarrel over office. Religion supplied the numbers, which is why a private grievance became a rising of perhaps five or six thousand men. On the evidence of who followed, the statement holds.\n\nAgainst that stands the position of the two earls themselves. Thomas Percy, Earl of Northumberland, and Charles Neville, Earl of Westmorland, headed families that had ruled the border for generations, and under Elizabeth they had been pushed aside. The Council of the North was run by men the queen trusted rather than by the great northern houses; Northumberland was kept away from court, and when copper was discovered on his land the crown claimed it; Westmorland was deep in debt. These were real losses of power and income. But they are also the clue to what the statement misses, because they had been accumulating for a decade without producing a revolt. A cause that is present for ten quiet years cannot be what made 1569 different.\n\nThe factor that does explain the timing is dynastic. Mary Queen of Scots had fled to England in 1568 and was being held at Tutbury: a Catholic with a claim to Elizabeth's throne, inside the kingdom. The plan to marry her to the Duke of Norfolk would have settled the succession and restored the old nobility to influence with no rebellion at all. It was only when Elizabeth discovered the scheme, committed Norfolk to the Tower in October 1569 and summoned the earls to court that rebellion became safer than obedience. The rising began because the earls expected arrest.\n\nThe way it ended supports that reading. The rebels took Durham and Barnard Castle, then turned back rather than face the Earl of Sussex advancing from York; the earls fled abroad while several hundred of their followers were hanged. A movement carried by religious conviction does not dissolve in a fortnight; a movement led by frightened noblemen does.\n\nApplying the test I set out, religion explains the followers and the form the revolt took, and the earls' decline explains their resentment, but neither explains the year. Mary's presence in England and the exposure of the Norfolk marriage do. Religion was the revolt's language and its recruiting-sergeant rather than its cause: I agree with the statement about what filled the ranks, and disagree with it about what set the rising in motion.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Before ranking causes, decide what a main reason has to explain - the mood, or the moment.","Both bullets describe conditions that had existed for years, so look for what changed in the autumn of 1569.","Let the collapse of the revolt be evidence too: what does the speed of the flight suggest about the leaders' motives?"],"codes":["B4-2 — 1 Plots and revolts at home: The Revolt of the Northern Earls, 1569–70"]},{"q":"'Walsingham's spy network was the main reason Elizabeth survived the Catholic plots against her in the years 1571-88.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the Throckmorton plot (1583) • the Bond of Association (1584). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 / AO1 6. Credit the spy network (informers, Phelippes' deciphering, months of surveillance before Throckmorton's arrest in November 1583, Gifford and the Chartley letters in 1586) weighed against the loyalty of English Catholics and the political nation (the Bond of Association 1584, the Act for the Queen's Safety 1585, the failure of any plot to raise a following) and, beyond the bullets, the incompetence of the plotters and the repeated failure of foreign help to arrive, with a judgement on which factor was decisive. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"The spy network decided how each plot ended; it did not decide that the plots would fail. The test I shall use is whether Elizabeth would have survived if the network had not existed, and the answer is that she probably would, because the conspiracies lacked the two things that make a rebellion succeed - an army in England and an army from abroad.\n\nThe case for the statement is strong at the level of detection. Sir Francis Walsingham, Principal Secretary from 1573, paid informers at home and abroad and employed Thomas Phelippes to break ciphers, so the government read plots rather than guessing at them. Francis Throckmorton was watched for months before his arrest in November 1583, which is why the search of his house produced a list of Catholic sympathisers and plans of harbours suitable for a landing; his confession under torture led to the expulsion of the Spanish ambassador Bernardino de Mendoza in 1584. Three years later the method was perfected: Gilbert Gifford, working for Walsingham, carried Mary Queen of Scots' letters in and out of Chartley Hall in the stopper of a beer barrel, and every one passed through Phelippes first, so Mary's consent to Elizabeth's murder in July 1586 was known to the government before Anthony Babington could act. No plot after 1571 was discovered by accident.\n\nAgainst that, the loyalty of the political nation mattered at least as much. The Bond of Association of 1584 was signed by thousands of gentlemen who promised to hunt down anyone who attempted the queen's life and anyone in whose name it was attempted, and Parliament gave the principle statutory form in the Act for the Queen's Safety of 1585. That tells us what the country had concluded: Elizabeth's death meant a disputed succession and civil war. Most English Catholics preferred an heretical queen to that, and they showed it by not rising. The Revolt of the Northern Earls in 1569 was the last occasion on which a Catholic cause put an army into the field, and even then it collapsed without a battle.\n\nBeyond the bullets there is a third factor, and it is the one the plotters could do least about: the foreign troops never came. Ridolfi's scheme of 1571 required the Duke of Alba to land an army from the Netherlands; Throckmorton's required the Duke of Guise; Babington's required Philip II. None of them moved, because Spain was pinned down by the Dutch revolt and France by its own religious wars.\n\nThe network was not only defensive, however, and this is the strongest version of the case for the statement. By letting the Babington correspondence run instead of arresting the plotters, Walsingham obtained the evidence that convicted Mary at Fotheringhay in October 1586 and led to her execution on 8 February 1587, removing the figurehead every future plot would have needed.\n\nMy judgement, on the test of what would have happened without it, is that the spy network was decisive for the manner and the timing of the plots' defeat and for the destruction of Mary, but not for Elizabeth's survival. A conspiracy with no English following and no foreign army would have failed even if every letter had reached its destination. Walsingham's achievement was to make the failures conclusive rather than merely likely.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Distinguish between what defeated each plot and what made the plots hopeless in the first place.","The Bond of Association tells you something about the country, not just about the law: ask what its signatories were afraid of.","Test the claim by removing it - imagine no spy network, and ask what the plotters still did not have."],"codes":["B4-2 — 1 Plots and revolts at home: The features and significance of the Ridolfi, Throckmorton and Babington plots","B4-2 — 1 Plots and revolts at home: The reasons for, and significance of, Mary Queen of Scots' execution in 1587","B4-2 — 1 Plots and revolts at home: The Revolt of the Northern Earls, 1569–70"]},{"q":"'The execution of Mary Queen of Scots was the main reason Philip II sent the Armada against England in 1588.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • the Treaty of Nonsuch (1585) • Drake. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 / AO1 6. Credit the case for the execution (February 1587, the loss of a Catholic claimant with French connections, Philip's own claim through John of Gaunt, papal encouragement) weighed against the Treaty of Nonsuch and the English army in the Netherlands from 1585, privateering and Drake's raids including Cadiz in 1587, and, beyond the bullets, the religious quarrel since the bull of 1570 and Philip's strengthened position after 1580, with a judgement on trigger against underlying cause. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"The execution removed Philip's last reason for hesitating; it did not give him his reasons for invading. The criterion I shall use is chronological: a main reason must come before the decision it is supposed to explain, and by February 1587 the decision had already been taken.\n\nThe case for the statement is real. Mary's execution at Fotheringhay on 8 February 1587 changed the politics of an invasion. While she lived, overthrowing Elizabeth meant putting on the English throne a queen raised in France and connected to the Guise family, which would have handed the greatest prize in Europe to Philip's French rivals. Once she was dead there was no Catholic claimant to inherit, and Philip could press his own descent from John of Gaunt. Her death also gave the enterprise the character of a crusade: an anointed Catholic queen had been executed by a heretic, and Pope Sixtus V promised money to the invasion. It is fair to say that the execution turned a war into a war of conquest.\n\nBut the war itself was already three years old, which is where the statement fails. By the Treaty of Nonsuch in August 1585 Elizabeth had agreed to pay for an army of about seven thousand men in the Netherlands under the Earl of Leicester and to hold Brill and Flushing as security. That was open intervention against Spain in the province Philip most needed to subdue, and Parma's campaign, which had just taken Antwerp, was being obstructed by an English army. Philip's decision to prepare a fleet followed from Nonsuch, not from Fotheringhay.\n\nThe second cause, and the older one, is what English ships were doing to Spanish commerce. Privateers licensed by the crown broke Spain's monopoly of trade with its American empire: Drake plundered the Pacific coast and captured the Nuestra Senora de la Concepcion in 1579, and Elizabeth knighted him aboard the Golden Hind in April 1581 rather than punishing him, which told Philip that the raids were royal policy. In April 1587, two months after Mary's death, Drake sailed into Cadiz and destroyed about thirty ships and the stores gathered for the invasion, delaying it by about a year. That raid is evidence against the statement: the fleet Drake burned was already being assembled.\n\nBeyond the bullets there is religion, which underlay everything. The papal bull of February 1570 had excommunicated Elizabeth and released her subjects from obedience, and Philip had been named in the Ridolfi plot of 1571 and the Throckmorton plot of 1583; his ambassador Mendoza was expelled in 1584. Philip's own strength also mattered: he had annexed Portugal in 1580, which gave him the Atlantic fleet and the harbours an invasion required. Without those ships there could have been no armada, whatever happened to Mary.\n\nOn the chronological test, the statement is wrong about cause and right about consequence. The strategic quarrel over the Netherlands and the commercial quarrel over the Atlantic had already produced open war by 1585, and the preparations Drake attacked in 1587 prove it. What Mary's execution did was to remove the dynastic objection to invasion and to supply a religious justification for it. It was the last push rather than the main reason, and the Armada would very probably have sailed if she had died in her bed.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Check the order of events before deciding: when did Philip start assembling a fleet?","Ask what difference Mary's death made to who would rule England if the invasion succeeded.","Drake's raid of 1587 is evidence for both sides - work out what it proves about the timing of Philip's plans."],"codes":["B4-2 — 1 Plots and revolts at home: The reasons for, and significance of, Mary Queen of Scots' execution in 1587","B4-2 — 3 Outbreak of war with Spain, 1585–88: Reasons for deteriorating relations with Spain","B4-2 — 2 Relations with Spain: The significance of privateering and the activities of Drake","B4-2 — 3 Outbreak of war with Spain, 1585–88: Drake and the raid on Cadiz"]},{"q":"'The skill of the English navy was the main reason for the defeat of the Spanish Armada in 1588.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks) You may use the following in your answer: • race-built galleons • the Duke of Parma. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 10 / AO1 6. Credit English skill (race-built galleons, long-range guns on light carriages, Howard, Drake, Hawkins and Frobisher, the fireships of 28 July and the close-range action off Gravelines on 29 July) weighed against Spanish planning failures (no deep-water port, Parma's barges blockaded by Dutch flyboats, slow communication, Medina Sidonia's inexperience, supplies spoiled after Cadiz) and, beyond the bullets, the weather on the voyage round Scotland and Ireland, with a judgement on which factor was decisive. Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, and no access to Level 4 for such answers.","sol":"English skill decided how the Armada was beaten; Spanish planning decided that it could not have won. My criterion is counterfactual: which factor, if it had been different, would have changed the outcome rather than merely the casualty list?\n\nThe case for English skill is genuine. The race-built galleons rebuilt under John Hawkins as Treasurer of the Navy were lower, faster and more weatherly than the Spanish ships, and their long-range guns sat on light carriages that crews could haul in and reload on deck, so an English ship could fire repeatedly in an action. Spanish tactics assumed the old style of fighting, closing to grapple and boarding with soldiers, of whom the fleet carried some 19,000. Lord Howard of Effingham, with Drake, Hawkins and Frobisher, simply refused that battle: the English harried the crescent formation up the Channel from the first action off Plymouth on 21 July, two days after the sighting off the Lizard, without ever letting the Spanish get alongside. The eight fireships sent into the anchorage off Calais on the night of 28 July were the best-judged decision of the campaign, and at Gravelines the next day the English closed to short range and did real damage.\n\nBut skill was exercised against a plan that was broken before it began, which is the strongest argument against it. The Armada was not an invasion force: its 130 ships were to escort the Duke of Parma's veteran army across from the Netherlands. Spain held no deep-water port on the Flemish coast, so Parma's barges had to come out through shallow water patrolled by Dutch flyboats, and messages between Medina Sidonia and Parma took days. Parma was not ready when the fleet reached Calais on 27 July and could not have been. Even a perfect voyage up the Channel would have ended at the same anchorage waiting for an army that could not sail.\n\nSpanish weaknesses of other kinds compounded that. The Marquis of Santa Cruz, who had planned the enterprise, died in February 1588 and command passed to the Duke of Medina Sidonia, an able administrator who had never held a naval command. The fleet's food and water spoiled because Drake had burned the seasoned barrel staves at Cadiz in April 1587, forcing the use of unseasoned wood. Its heavy gun carriages were hard to work in a running fight, so Spanish rates of fire fell away.\n\nBeyond the bullets, the weather did more damage than the guns. The wind at Calais made the fireships possible; the wind after Gravelines drove the Armada north when it was close to the Flemish banks; and the storms off Scotland and Ireland wrecked ship after ship on a fleet that had cut away its anchors, so that only about half of it reached Spain. English gunnery sank or crippled a handful of ships; the sea destroyed dozens.\n\nOn my criterion the statement is half right. Remove English seamanship and the Armada would have reached Calais in better order and lost fewer ships, but it would still have found no army waiting and no harbour to shelter in. Remove the Dutch blockade and the problem of the ports and Parma's men might have crossed, and England would have faced the best infantry in Europe. English skill made the defeat decisive and cheap; Spanish planning made defeat inevitable, and the weather turned it into a disaster.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what the fleet was actually for, because it was not sent to fight the English navy.","Weigh the number of ships lost in battle against the number lost on the voyage home.","A counterfactual makes a good criterion here: take one factor away at a time and see whether the outcome changes."],"codes":["B4-2 — 4 The Armada: The reasons for the English victory","B4-2 — 4 The Armada: Spanish invasion plans and key events","B4-2 — 3 Outbreak of war with Spain, 1585–88: Drake and the raid on Cadiz"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In which month and year did Pope Pius V issue the bull Regnans in Excelsis?","a":"February 1570."},{"q":"In which castle was Mary Queen of Scots tried in October 1586 and executed in February 1587?","a":"Fotheringhay Castle."},{"q":"How many fireships did the English send into the Spanish anchorage off Calais on the night of 28 July 1588?","a":"Eight."},{"q":"Who took command of the Armada after the death of the Marquis of Santa Cruz in February 1588?","a":"The Duke of Medina Sidonia."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The Revolt of the Northern Earls, 1569-70","back":"Rising led by the earls of Northumberland and Westmorland. Mass held in Durham Cathedral, November 1569; Barnard Castle taken; collapsed before the Earl of Sussex. Earls fled; several hundred followers hanged.","code":"B4-2 — 1 Plots and revolts at home: The Revolt of the Northern Earls, 1569–70","open":false},{"front":"Regnans in Excelsis, February 1570","back":"Papal bull of Pius V excommunicating Elizabeth, denying she was lawful queen and releasing her subjects from obedience. It made loyalty to the crown and to Rome a choice for English Catholics.","code":"B4-2 — 1 Plots and revolts at home: The Revolt of the Northern Earls, 1569–70","open":false},{"front":"The Ridolfi plot, 1571","back":"Roberto Ridolfi's plan for the Duke of Alba to invade from the Netherlands, kill Elizabeth and marry Mary to the Duke of Norfolk. Uncovered through intercepted letters; Norfolk executed June 1572.","code":"B4-2 — 1 Plots and revolts at home: The features and significance of the Ridolfi, Throckmorton and Babington plots","open":false},{"front":"The Throckmorton plot, 1583","back":"Francis Throckmorton acted between Mary and the Spanish ambassador Mendoza for an invasion led by the Duke of Guise. Arrested November 1583; papers listed Catholic sympathisers and landing harbours. Mendoza expelled 1584.","code":"B4-2 — 1 Plots and revolts at home: The features and significance of the Ridolfi, Throckmorton and Babington plots","open":false},{"front":"The Babington plot, 1586","back":"Anthony Babington's plan to murder Elizabeth and free Mary, backed by Spain. Letters smuggled to Chartley Hall in a beer barrel by Gilbert Gifford and deciphered by Thomas Phelippes. Babington executed September 1586.","code":"B4-2 — 1 Plots and revolts at home: The features and significance of the Ridolfi, Throckmorton and Babington plots","open":false},{"front":"Sir Francis Walsingham","back":"Principal Secretary from 1573 and Elizabeth's spymaster. Ran paid informers at home and abroad, used double agents such as Gilbert Gifford, and employed the code-breaker Thomas Phelippes to read plotters' letters.","code":"B4-2 — 1 Plots and revolts at home: The features and significance of the Ridolfi, Throckmorton and Babington plots","open":false},{"front":"The Bond of Association, 1584","back":"Drawn up by Walsingham and Burghley; signatories swore to pursue to death anyone attempting the queen's life and anyone in whose name it was attempted. Given statutory form by the Act for the Queen's Safety, 1585.","code":"B4-2 — 1 Plots and revolts at home: The reasons for, and significance of, Mary Queen of Scots' execution in 1587","open":false},{"front":"The trial and execution of Mary Queen of Scots","back":"Tried by commission at Fotheringhay Castle, October 1586, and found guilty. Elizabeth signed the warrant on 1 February 1587; Mary was beheaded on 8 February 1587. Elizabeth blamed her secretary William Davison.","code":"B4-2 — 1 Plots and revolts at home: The reasons for, and significance of, Mary Queen of Scots' execution in 1587","open":false},{"front":"Privateer","back":"A private sea captain licensed by the crown to attack enemy shipping and share the profits with investors, so the queen gained treasure while denying responsibility for the raids.","code":"B4-2 — 2 Relations with Spain: The significance of privateering and the activities of Drake","open":false},{"front":"Drake's voyage round the world, 1577-80","back":"Sailed December 1577, passed the Strait of Magellan, renamed his ship the Golden Hind, raided the Pacific coast and took the Nuestra Senora de la Concepcion in 1579. Home September 1580; knighted at Deptford April 1581.","code":"B4-2 — 2 Relations with Spain: The significance of privateering and the activities of Drake","open":false},{"front":"San Juan de Ulua, September 1568","back":"Spanish attack on John Hawkins' trading fleet in a Mexican harbour; few English ships escaped. With Elizabeth's seizure of Spanish bullion that December it wrecked Anglo-Spanish trade and made Drake an enemy of Spain.","code":"B4-2 — 2 Relations with Spain: Political, religious and commercial rivalry","open":false},{"front":"The Treaty of Nonsuch, August 1585","back":"Elizabeth agreed to pay for an army of about seven thousand men in the Netherlands under the Earl of Leicester, holding Brill and Flushing as security. The first open English commitment against Spain.","code":"B4-2 — 3 Outbreak of war with Spain, 1585–88: Reasons for deteriorating relations with Spain","open":false},{"front":"The raid on Cadiz, April 1587","back":"Drake destroyed or captured about thirty ships and burned stores, including seasoned barrel staves, then took the carrack Sao Felipe. He called it singeing the King of Spain's beard; it delayed the invasion about a year.","code":"B4-2 — 3 Outbreak of war with Spain, 1585–88: Drake and the raid on Cadiz","open":false},{"front":"The Spanish plan of 1588","back":"About 130 ships with roughly 19,000 soldiers and 8,000 sailors were to sail from Lisbon to Flanders, collect the Duke of Parma's army in barges and land it in Kent for a march on London.","code":"B4-2 — 4 The Armada: Spanish invasion plans and key events","open":false},{"front":"One reason for the English victory over the Armada in 1588","back":"Race-built galleons with quick-loading long-range guns; the impossible rendezvous with Parma, who had no deep-water port and was blockaded by Dutch flyboats; the fireships at Calais on 28 July and the storms round Scotland and Ireland.","code":"B4-2 — 4 The Armada: The reasons for the English victory","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (booklet B, the 16-mark judgement)","items":["Question: 'The English expedition to the Netherlands in the years 1585-88 was a failure.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] You may use the following in your answer: • the action at Zutphen (1586) • the cautionary towns of Brill and Flushing. You must also use information of your own. What follows is one paragraph from the middle of the answer, putting the case for the statement.","Judged by what an army is sent to do, the expedition failed, and Zutphen shows why. The Earl of Leicester landed in December 1585 with the force agreed at Nonsuch that August, about seven thousand men paid for by the English crown, and in three campaigning seasons he took no town of consequence from the Duke of Parma. The action at Zutphen in September 1586 was an attempt to stop a Spanish convoy reaching the garrison there; the supplies got through, the English drew off with heavy losses, and Sir Philip Sidney was carried away with a wound in the thigh from which he died the following month. Worse came from inside the English camp: in January 1587 Sir William Stanley handed Deventer to the Spanish and Rowland York gave up the fort outside Zutphen, so that the campaign ended by adding to Parma's gains instead of reducing them, and in August 1587 Sluis fell while an English relief failed to reach it. Leicester meanwhile had quarrelled with the States General over the title of Governor-General that he accepted in January 1586 and that Elizabeth made him renounce, and with his own officers over pay, and he came home at the end of 1587 leaving Brill and Flushing garrisoned at English expense - towns held as security for a debt, not won as a victory. Measured by ground taken, by money spent against money saved, and by the standing of the commander, three years produced nothing the queen had wanted. The case for calling the expedition a failure is therefore not that the soldiers fought badly but that it never achieved the object of the treaty, which was to make Spain leave the Dutch alone.","Why this is Level 4: the explanation is directed consistently at the conceptual focus, which here is a judgement measured against a stated test, and it carries a line of reasoning from its opening claim to a closing interim judgement instead of narrating the campaign. The information is precisely selected rather than piled up - the seven thousand men agreed at Nonsuch, the convoy that got through at Zutphen, Sidney's death the month after, Deventer in January 1587, Sluis in August 1587 and the quarrel over the Governor-Generalship each do a job in the argument. The criterion for the judgement is stated and applied: the expedition is measured against the object it was sent to achieve, not against the courage of the men who fought. It also addresses aspects of content the two bullets do not prompt - Stanley's surrender of Deventer, the fall of Sluis, the cost of the war and the quarrel with the States General - so the cap on answers that do not address three or more aspects of content does not bite."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["The same question, answered at Level 2. Read it beside the Level 4 paragraph above and look for what is missing rather than for what is wrong.","The expedition to the Netherlands was a failure because the English did not win. Leicester was sent over with an army to help the Dutch against Spain, but he argued with everyone and ran out of money. At the battle of Zutphen the English lost and Sir Philip Sidney was wounded and died, which upset everybody at home. England was given some towns to look after as well, but that did not help the Dutch very much. In the end Leicester came home and Spain was still there, so the whole thing was a failure and a waste of money.","Why this is only Level 2: it meets the descriptor 'an explanation is given showing limited analysis and with implicit or unsustained links to the conceptual focus of the question', because failure is asserted in 'the English did not win' and 'Spain was still there' but no measure of failure is ever set. It misses the higher descriptors in three ways. First, the information is generalised rather than 'accurate and relevant information precisely selected to address the question directly': the Treaty of Nonsuch is never named or dated, there is no figure for the army, no month for Zutphen, no name for the towns held as security and nothing at all about Deventer or Sluis. Second, the answer is anecdotal rather than analytical - Sidney's death is used for its sadness rather than as evidence about the campaign, and Zutphen is called a battle the English lost without saying what they had been trying to do there. Third, 'the overall judgement is given but its justification is asserted or insecure', since the last sentence repeats the statement and adds 'a waste of money' without a single figure. Because it also addresses only the two aspects the bullets prompt, the printed cap applies on top: on the 16-mark question an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content is held to a maximum of 11 marks and has no access to Level 4, so better handwriting and longer sentences alone could take it no higher."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Revolt of the Northern Earls, 1569-70","items":["Leaders: Thomas Percy, Earl of Northumberland, and Charles Neville, Earl of Westmorland, heads of the two great border families.","Grievances: loss of influence to the Council of the North, Northumberland kept from court, the crown's claim to the copper found on his land, Westmorland's debts.","Religious grievance: the settlement of 1559 had removed the Latin mass from parish churches in the most conservative region of England.","Trigger: the plan to marry Mary Queen of Scots to the Duke of Norfolk was discovered; Norfolk was sent to the Tower in October 1569 and the earls were summoned to court.","Where Mary was: held at Tutbury, and moved to Coventry for safety once the rising began.","November 1569: the rebels entered Durham Cathedral, had mass celebrated there and destroyed the English Bible and prayer book.","Emblem: the banner of the Five Wounds of Christ, carried in the Pilgrimage of Grace in 1536 and again in the Western Rising of 1549.","Size: perhaps five or six thousand men; Barnard Castle was besieged and taken.","Defeat: the rebels turned back rather than face the Earl of Sussex, Lord President of the Council of the North, advancing from York; the rising collapsed by December 1569.","Punishment: several hundred of the rank and file were hanged; both earls fled into Scotland in December 1569, and from there Westmorland escaped to the Spanish Netherlands and died in exile, while Northumberland was handed back and executed at York in 1572.","Consequences: tighter royal control of the north, the papal bull Regnans in Excelsis in February 1570, and the Treasons Act of 1571 making it treason to call Elizabeth a heretic or to bring papal bulls into England."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the plots - Ridolfi, Throckmorton, Babington - and Walsingham's spies","items":["Ridolfi plot, 1571: Roberto Ridolfi, an Italian banker in London and papal agent, planned that the Duke of Alba would invade from the Netherlands, Elizabeth would be killed and Mary would marry the Duke of Norfolk.","How Ridolfi's plot was broken: the messenger Charles Bailly was arrested at Dover with letters, which were deciphered; the Spanish ambassador Guerau de Spes was expelled and Norfolk was executed in June 1572.","Throckmorton plot, 1583: Francis Throckmorton carried messages between Mary and the Spanish ambassador Bernardino de Mendoza for an invasion to be led by Henry, Duke of Guise, Mary's cousin, and paid for by Philip II and the Pope.","How Throckmorton's plot was broken: months of surveillance, arrest at his London house in November 1583, papers listing Catholic sympathisers and harbours suitable for a landing, confession under torture, execution in 1584; Mendoza expelled.","Babington plot, 1586: Anthony Babington, a young Derbyshire gentleman, proposed to Mary the murder of Elizabeth, her own release and a Spanish invasion.","How Babington's plot was broken: the double agent Gilbert Gifford carried letters in and out of Chartley Hall in the stopper of a beer barrel; Thomas Phelippes deciphered them; Mary's reply of July 1586 agreed to the plan. Babington and his fellow conspirators were executed in September 1586.","Sir Francis Walsingham: Principal Secretary from 1573; ran informers in England and on the continent, employed code-breakers, used double agents and allowed plots to run in order to gather evidence.","What the three plots had in common: a foreign army, an English Catholic rising and Mary on the throne. None of the three produced any of the three.","Why the foreign armies never came: Spain was tied down by the Dutch revolt and France by its own religious wars.","Legal response: the Treasons Act 1571, the Act against Jesuits and Seminary Priests 1585, the Bond of Association 1584 and the Act for the Queen's Safety 1585."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Mary Queen of Scots - trial and execution","items":["Mary fled to England in 1568 after defeat at Langside and was held in a series of castles for nineteen years.","Her claim: as a great-granddaughter of Henry VII she was Elizabeth's nearest legitimate heir, and Catholics who accepted the bull of 1570 regarded her as the rightful queen.","Bond of Association, 1584: signatories swore to hunt down anyone attempting the queen's life and anyone in whose name it was attempted.","Act for the Queen's Safety, 1585: gave the Bond statutory form and allowed a claimant on whose behalf a plot was made to be tried by a commission.","Evidence: her deciphered reply of July 1586 to Anthony Babington, approving the murder of Elizabeth.","Trial: a commission of councillors and peers at Fotheringhay Castle in October 1586; Mary denied that an English court could try an anointed foreign queen; she was found guilty and sentence was pronounced later that month.","Parliament and the Privy Council had demanded her death since 1572, after the Ridolfi plot.","The warrant: signed by Elizabeth on 1 February 1587 and sent to Fotheringhay by the Council without further reference to her.","Execution: 8 February 1587 in the great hall at Fotheringhay; the headsman needed more than one stroke.","Aftermath at court: Elizabeth disclaimed responsibility and William Davison, the secretary who had handled the warrant, was imprisoned and fined.","Significance: the Catholic cause lost its English claimant; Philip II could now pursue the throne for himself, claiming descent from John of Gaunt; the execution gave the coming invasion the character of a crusade and brought a promise of money from Pope Sixtus V.","Significance in Scotland: James VI, Mary's son and a Protestant, protested but did not break with England, and he remained Elizabeth's likeliest successor."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: rivalry with Spain, privateering and Drake","items":["Philip II was married to Mary I from 1554 to 1558 and proposed to Elizabeth in 1559; he was the most powerful ruler in Europe and the leading champion of the Catholic Church.","Religious break: Elizabeth's settlement of 1559 made England Protestant; the papal bull of February 1570 excommunicated her and released her subjects from obedience.","Commercial cause: Spain claimed a monopoly of trade with its American empire and excluded English merchants.","San Juan de Ulua, September 1568: a Spanish fleet attacked John Hawkins' ships in a Mexican harbour; only a few escaped, among them Drake's.","December 1568: Elizabeth seized the bullion sheltering in English ports that was destined to pay Alba's army in the Netherlands; Spain answered with a trade embargo.","Privateering: captains licensed by the crown attacked Spanish shipping and shared the profits with their investors, the queen among them.","Drake's circumnavigation: left England in December 1577 with five ships, passed the Strait of Magellan, renamed the Pelican the Golden Hind, raided the Pacific coast and returned in September 1580.","March 1579: capture of the treasure ship Nuestra Senora de la Concepcion off Cape San Francisco, near Esmeraldas in modern Ecuador, on the Peru to Panama run; the voyage's plunder was worth more than the crown's ordinary annual income.","April 1581: Elizabeth knighted Drake aboard the Golden Hind at Deptford, a public refusal of Philip's demand that he be punished and the treasure returned.","Effects of privateering: treasure for the crown, trained seamen and captains, experience of Atlantic navigation, and a grievance Spain could not settle without abandoning its monopoly."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Netherlands, Robert Dudley and the road to war, 1584-87","items":["The Spanish Netherlands were England's chief export market for cloth and lay directly across the North Sea.","The Duke of Alba arrived with a Spanish army in 1567 and his Council of Troubles executed rebels in large numbers.","Elizabeth sheltered the Dutch Sea Beggars in English ports until she expelled them in 1572, after which they seized Brill and the revolt revived.","July 1584: William of Orange, leader of the Dutch revolt, was assassinated at Delft, leaving the rebels without a leader.","December 1584: the Treaty of Joinville allied Philip II with the French Catholic League, raising the danger of Catholic France and Spain acting together.","August 1585: the Duke of Parma captured Antwerp, the greatest city of the Netherlands.","August 1585: the Treaty of Nonsuch - Elizabeth agreed to pay for an army of about seven thousand men and to hold Brill and Flushing as cautionary towns. It amounted to open war with Spain.","Robert Dudley, Earl of Leicester, arrived in the Netherlands in December 1585 as commander of the English forces.","January 1586: Leicester accepted the title of Governor-General from the Dutch States General, implying that Elizabeth had taken sovereignty; she made him renounce it.","September 1586: the action at Zutphen, where Sir Philip Sidney was mortally wounded; he died the following month.","1587: Sluis fell to Parma; Leicester quarrelled with his Dutch allies, ran out of money and returned to England at the end of the year.","Result: England had not saved the Netherlands, but Philip II now regarded England as an enemy at war and began preparing the invasion."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the raid on Cadiz, 1587, and the Spanish Armada, 1588","items":["April 1587: Drake entered the harbour of Cadiz and over about three days destroyed or captured some thirty ships and burned stores gathered for the invasion.","The stores included seasoned barrel staves; barrels of unseasoned wood leaked, so the Armada's food and water spoiled at sea a year later.","Drake then raided the Portuguese coast near Cape St Vincent; on 8 June 1587, off the Azores, he captured the carrack Sao Felipe returning from the East Indies.","Effect of the raid: the invasion was delayed by about a year, which gave England time to prepare but also gave Philip time to gather a larger fleet.","February 1588: the Marquis of Santa Cruz, who had planned the enterprise, died; the Duke of Medina Sidonia took command although he had no experience of a naval command and asked to be excused.","The fleet: about 130 ships carrying roughly 19,000 soldiers and 8,000 sailors, sailing from Lisbon in late May 1588 and forced by storms into Corunna before sailing again in July.","The plan: sail up the Channel to the coast of Flanders, meet the Duke of Parma's army, ferry it across in barges and land it in Kent for a march on London.","The flaw: Spain held no deep-water port on the Flemish coast, Parma's barges were blockaded by Dutch flyboats off Dunkirk and Nieuwpoort, and messages between the commanders took days.","19 July 1588: the Armada was sighted off the Lizard and beacons carried the news to London. The English fleet was commanded by Lord Howard of Effingham, with Drake, Hawkins and Frobisher.","Up the Channel: the crescent formation held through skirmishes off Plymouth, Portland and the Isle of Wight; the Rosario was captured after a collision and the San Salvador exploded.","27 July 1588: the Armada anchored off Calais; Parma was not ready.","Night of 28 July 1588: eight English fireships were sent in on wind and tide. No Spanish ship burned, but cables were cut, the formation broke and anchors were lost.","29 July 1588: the battle of Gravelines, fought at close range; several Spanish ships were sunk or driven ashore before the wind carried the Armada north.","The return: round the north of Scotland and the west of Ireland, where autumn storms wrecked many ships; about half the fleet reached Spain, some 67 of the roughly 130 that had sailed.","August 1588: Elizabeth reviewed the army at Tilbury, telling the troops that though she had the body of a weak and feeble woman she had the heart and stomach of a king; by then the Armada had already been driven north.","English advantages: race-built galleons that were lower, faster and more manoeuvrable, long-range guns on light carriages that could be reloaded on deck, and the refusal to be drawn into boarding actions.","Consequences: England survived, Protestant morale across Europe rose, commemorative medals were struck crediting the victory to the winds sent by God, and the war with Spain continued until 1604.","Dates for 1588 in this room are old style, the Julian calendar England then used and the one the board's own materials use; on the new style calendar used in Catholic Europe the battle of Gravelines falls on 8 August 1588."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — the 16-mark judgement: 'The Revolt of the Northern Earls (1569-70) left Elizabeth stronger than it found her.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer.","items":["The question as printed: 'The Revolt of the Northern Earls (1569-70) left Elizabeth stronger than it found her.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks] You may use the following in your answer: • the punishment of the rebels • the Treasons Act of 1571. You must also use information of your own. There are no spelling, punctuation and grammar marks on Paper 2, so every minute goes on the argument.","Decode it. The concept is consequence, and the word 'left' fixes what you must do: measure Elizabeth's position after the rising against her position before it, rather than describe what happened in it. The criterion to announce in the opening and apply at the end: stronger or weaker for what - for control of the north, for the safety of the throne, or for her standing with Catholics at home and abroad. Signal that you will argue that the rising left her stronger where she could reach and weaker where she could not.","Opening, three or four sentences: define what counts as stronger by that criterion, name the three tests you will use - the north, the throne, and Elizabeth's Catholic subjects and neighbours - and state the position you will finish at.","Paragraph 1, for the statement - the north was never the same again. The rising collapsed by December 1569 without a single foreign soldier landing; several hundred of the rank and file were hanged; both earls fled into Scotland in December 1569, and from there Northumberland was handed back and beheaded at York in 1572 while Westmorland escaped to the Spanish Netherlands and died in exile; Leonard Dacre's separate rising was broken by Lord Hunsdon at Gelt Bridge in February 1570; rebel lands were forfeited to the crown and used to reward loyal men, and from 1572 the Council of the North was headed by Henry Hastings, Earl of Huntingdon, a Puritan who held the presidency until his death in 1595. Interim judgement: the two families that had governed the border for generations were finished, and no rising of that kind troubled the north again in the reign.","Paragraph 2, against - the rising turned a local quarrel into an international one. Pope Pius V answered it with the bull Regnans in Excelsis in February 1570, which excommunicated Elizabeth, denied that she was lawfully queen and released her subjects from their obedience, so that a loyal English Catholic now had to choose. The plots began at once, Ridolfi in 1571 with Alba's troops behind it, and Mary Queen of Scots, whose presence had helped to cause the rising, remained alive and a claimant until 1587. Interim judgement: measured by the safety of the throne, the revolt bought fifteen years of conspiracy.","Paragraph 3, against - the new laws were a sign of fear rather than of security. The Treasons Act of 1571 made it treason to deny Elizabeth's title or to call her a heretic, and a companion act made it treason to bring papal bulls into England; the Act against Jesuits and Seminary Priests followed in 1585, and recusancy was punished more and more heavily. Interim judgement: a government that has to keep legislating against its own subjects has not settled the problem, although the same statutes show a crown that could get what it wanted from Parliament.","Paragraph 4, the point beyond the stimulus bullets - how little support the rising found. Perhaps five or six thousand men came out, almost all of them from the two earls' own country; the Catholic gentry of the south and the midlands did not stir, no help arrived from abroad, and the rebels turned back rather than face the Earl of Sussex advancing from York. Interim judgement: the revolt tested whether English Catholics would fight for their religion, and the answer it produced was worth more to Elizabeth than any statute.","The evaluation thread: return in every paragraph to the same test - stronger or weaker by which measure, and by what date. Keeping one test running is what turns four paragraphs into a line of reasoning.","Timing: about 25 minutes, of which the first three or four go on the plan - the factors, their order, the point beyond the bullets and the position you will end at - so that the essay is written once.","The conclusion must rank and justify rather than summarise. Argue that the rising left Elizabeth stronger where it was fought: the north passed to crown servants, the old families were destroyed, and their tenants learned that rebellion ended on the gallows. It left her weaker where she could not reach: the bull of 1570 gave every later plotter a text to quote, and Mary remained a living alternative until 1587. Then say why the criterion you chose is the right one - that a ruler's strength is judged by what she can control, and Elizabeth controlled the north after 1570 in a way she never controlled Rome or Madrid.","What will lose marks: narrating the rising from Durham Cathedral to Barnard Castle in order; treating the two bullets as the whole answer, since an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content is capped at 11 marks and shut out of Level 4; and a conclusion that says the revolt had good and bad consequences without saying which mattered most and by what measure."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:35:48.510Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"All four recorded fixes verified in the kit: room.questions[10] is now a 12-mark causation question on why Elizabeth sent Drake to Cadiz in 1587 and the June 2024 booklet B4 stem is gone; room.questions[6] is now on why the government needed a spy network, 1570-88; room.questions[11] carries only the Northern Earls code; and the earls' flight reads identically in lesson.idea[0], room.questions[8].sol, extras[2] item 9 and extras[8] item 3. The new facts check out on the web - Philip II gathering ships and stores at Cadiz and Lisbon from 1586, the execution of February 1587, Drake sailing from Plymouth on 12 April 1587 under a commission to prevent the joining of Spain's fleets and to distress the ships within their havens, four royal ships lent to a venture the London merchants mostly paid for, the Sao Felipe off the Azores on 8 June 1587, Regnans in Excelsis of February 1570, Bailly at Dover, Throckmorton watched and arrested in November 1583, Chartley and Phelippes, and Walsingham as Principal Secretary from 1573. I re-solved all fifteen questions and found no answer key wrong or ambiguous, no bank question now reproduces the specimen booklet B4 or the June 2023 or June 2024 B4 papers, no worked example, plan or model paragraph shares a bank question's focus and stimulus points, and the scan printed no duplication¹. ¹ node dupscan.js EDX-1HI0 B4-2, run for this verdict."}}},"B4-3":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"B4-3","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 3 decides whether you can write about Elizabethan society and not only about Elizabethan politics, and booklet B of Paper 2 asks exactly three things of it: describe one feature of it, explain why something in it happened, and judge a claim about it. The material is unusually wide - schools, tutors and the two universities; football, bear-baiting and the new playhouses; beggars, whipping posts and the Poor Laws; galleons, astrolabes, Drake's voyage round the world and the settlers marooned on Roanoke - so an examiner can set a 12-mark causation question on the poor and a 16-mark judgement on exploration in the same paper. What earns marks here is precise dated detail (1563, 1572, 1576, the Theatre in 1576, 1577-80, 1585, 1587) fastened to an argument, not a guided tour of Elizabethan life. Almost every question in this room is a causation or a significance question in disguise, so the habit to build is ranking causes and consequences rather than listing them.","idea":[{"h":"Schools, tutors and apprenticeships: education followed rank","code":"B4-3 — 1 Education and leisure: Education in the home and schools","t":"Education in Elizabethan England was neither for everyone nor the same for everyone: it followed the social order. The sons of nobles and wealthy gentry were taught at home by private tutors in Latin, Greek and history, with the accomplishments of a courtier - music, dancing, riding, fencing - added on top; their sisters learned modern languages, music, needlework and the running of a household, because they were being prepared to govern a great house, not to hold office. Below them the sons of gentry, merchants, yeomen and prospering townsmen went to grammar schools, founded or refounded in market towns across the reign and often endowed by rich townsmen or guilds. A boy entered at about seven, having learned his letters at a petty or dame school, and worked six days a week from six or seven in the morning until five at Latin grammar, rhetoric and classical authors, with Greek in the upper forms and the rod for those who failed. Fees were charged, though many schools kept free places for poor scholars. Boys who were not bound for a grammar school - the sons of craftsmen, tradesmen and labourers, and the many who left school early for a trade - were trained instead by apprenticeship, bound to a master for a term fixed at seven years by the Statute of Artificers of 1563. There were only two universities, Oxford and Cambridge, entered at about fourteen or fifteen, where boys studied the seven liberal arts before degrees in divinity, law or medicine, while the Inns of Court in London trained lawyers and finished the education of gentlemen. Education spread because Protestantism wanted a laity that could read an English Bible and a preaching clergy, printing made books cheap, trade and royal service needed literate men, and humanism made learning the mark of a gentleman; historians' estimates of who could sign a name suggest something like a third of men and a tenth of women were literate by 1600. A top-level answer uses the hierarchy as its structure: it says who was educated, in what and for what purpose, and then argues that the change reached some ranks and left the rest exactly where they were."},{"h":"Leisure by rank, and the playhouse as the genuinely new thing","code":"B4-3 — 1 Education and leisure: Sport, pastimes and the theatre","t":"Leisure divided by rank as sharply as schooling did. Gentlemen hunted deer with hounds, flew hawks, fished, played real tennis, bowls and chess, fenced, danced and made music on the lute or the virginals. Ordinary people played football - a violent, almost ruleless contest between villages that authorities repeatedly tried to ban - wrestled, watched cock-fighting, bear-baiting and bull-baiting, gambled at dice and cards and enjoyed fairs, feasts and church ales. Baiting was not a poor man's sport alone: Elizabeth herself enjoyed it, and the baiting rings stood beside the playhouses in Southwark. The theatre was the real novelty. Plays had been given by travelling companies in inn-yards, guildhalls and the halls of great houses; then in 1576 James Burbage built the Theatre at Shoreditch, the first successful purpose-built playhouse in London, the Curtain followed nearby in 1577, and in 1587 Philip Henslowe opened the Rose on Bankside. A penny bought a place standing in the yard and a few pence a seat in the galleries, so a gentleman and an apprentice watched the same play on the same afternoon. Companies survived by patronage, and by law: the Vagabonds Act of 1572 counted players without a noble patron as rogues, so troupes took their patrons' names, the Earl of Leicester's Men were licensed in 1574 and the Queen's Men were formed in 1583. Puritan preachers and the City authorities attacked the playhouses as nurseries of idleness, crime and plague, which is why they were built outside the City's jurisdiction at Shoreditch and on Bankside. A top-level answer treats the theatre as a test case of change: it names the buildings and their dates, explains why they appeared when they did, and then weighs how far the leisure of most people actually altered, since football and baiting in 1588 looked much as they had in 1558."},{"h":"Why there were more poor people, and more of them on the road","code":"B4-3 — 2 The 'problem' of the poor: The reasons for the increase in poverty and vagabondage","t":"Several pressures worked at once. Population grew: England held roughly three million people at Elizabeth's accession and about four million by 1600, so more hands and more mouths competed for the same land and the same work, which pushed rents up and wages down without anybody deciding it. Prices, above all of food, rose faster than wages, partly because of the debasement of the coinage under Henry VIII and Edward VI, which Elizabeth put right in the recoinage of 1560-61; real wages fell, so a labourer in work grew poorer year by year. Bad harvests, notably that of 1586, turned the squeeze into hunger. Landlords raised rents - rack-renting - and enclosed land, and where arable was turned over to sheep one shepherd replaced several ploughmen, so families lost holding and wage together. The dissolution of the monasteries in the 1530s had already removed the old machinery of charity. The cloth trade, England's great export, was disrupted when the Antwerp market failed in the 1560s, throwing weavers, spinners and carders out of work. War added demobilised soldiers and sailors to the roads, particularly after England intervened in the Netherlands in 1585. People who lost their living moved, first to the nearest town and then to London, which may have grown from some seventy thousand people around 1550 to about two hundred thousand by 1600. Anyone outside his own parish without work was a vagabond in law, and the pamphleteers made him a monster: Thomas Harman's pamphlet of the 1560s catalogued the supposed tricks and disguises of vagrants and fed the fear that they were an army of thieves living beyond the parish system. A top-level answer ranks these causes instead of listing them, and keeps separate what made people poor from what made the poor move, because vagabondage is the second of those and not the first."},{"h":"From the whipping post to the poor rate: attitudes and the Poor Laws","code":"B4-3 — 2 The 'problem' of the poor: The changing attitudes and policies towards the poor","t":"Elizabethans divided the poor in two. The impotent or deserving poor - the old, the sick, the disabled, orphans and widows - deserved relief; the able-bodied who did not work were idle sturdy beggars who deserved punishment. Policy moved unevenly from punishing the second group towards providing for both. The 1563 Act for the Relief of the Poor made contributions to parish relief effectively compulsory: a man who refused to give could be brought before Justices of the Peace and in the end imprisoned. The 1572 Vagabonds Act was the harshest measure of the reign - a vagrant over fourteen was to be whipped and bored through the right ear for a first offence and could be put to death as a felon for a third - but the same Act set up a compulsory poor rate collected locally and ordered towns to keep registers of their poor, so punishment and provision arrived together. The 1576 Act for Setting the Poor on Work took the decisive step of admitting that the able-bodied poor might want work and not find it: towns had to buy raw materials such as wool, hemp and flax so the poor could be set to work for wages, and only those who then refused went to a house of correction. Towns had led the way - Norwich surveyed its poor in 1570, sorted them by need and set the able-bodied to work - and their censuses provided the evidence that most of the poor were children, the old and the sick. All of this was gathered up in the Elizabethan Poor Law of 1601, which lies outside this key topic but shows where the reign's thinking ended. A top-level answer tracks the shift Act by Act and then judges how far attitudes really changed, given that the whipping clause of 1572 was never repealed and the idle poor were still blamed for their idleness in 1601."},{"h":"Better ships, better instruments and a hunger for new markets","code":"B4-3 — 3 Exploration and voyages of discovery: Factors prompting exploration","t":"The English sailed further after 1558 because they could and because they needed to. They could because ships and navigation improved. The galleon was narrower, lower in the water and lighter in the castles fore and aft, so it was faster, handled better and carried more guns and more cargo; square sails for running before the wind combined with triangular lateen sails for sailing closer to it made long ocean passages practicable. A navigator fixed his latitude with a mariner's astrolabe or a quadrant by measuring the height of the sun at noon or of the Pole Star, held his course by magnetic compass, judged his speed with a log line and a sand-glass, and kept his reckoning on a traverse board. Charts improved too: Gerardus Mercator's world map of 1569 projected the globe so that a constant compass bearing could be drawn as a straight line. They needed to because the old trade was failing and the new trade was rich. The Antwerp cloth market, on which English exports depended, was disrupted in the 1560s, so merchants hunted for markets elsewhere: the Muscovy Company, chartered in 1555 after Richard Chancellor reached Russia, traded through the northern ports, and a company for the Ottoman lands was chartered in 1581. Spices, silks, sugar and above all American silver promised profits that Spain and Portugal claimed as a monopoly, which is why English captains looked for north-west and north-east passages and why privateering - robbing Spanish shipping - attracted courtly investors. Richard Hakluyt put the argument in writing, urging the queen in 1584 to plant colonies in America. Joint-stock companies spread the risk, so merchants, courtiers and the queen herself could all buy in. A top-level answer separates the means from the motives and then says which mattered more for the particular voyage the question names."},{"h":"Drake's voyage round the world, 1577-80, and what it was for","code":"B4-3 — 3 Exploration and voyages of discovery: Drake's circumnavigation of the globe","t":"Drake left Plymouth in December 1577 with five ships, officially on a trading voyage, in fact backed by courtiers and by the queen to raid Spanish possessions in the Pacific, where Spain expected no enemy. He passed the Strait of Magellan in 1578, having executed Thomas Doughty for alleged mutiny at Port St Julian, and renamed his flagship the Pelican the Golden Hind after the crest of his patron Sir Christopher Hatton. Sailing north he plundered Spanish ports and shipping and in March 1579 took the treasure ship Nuestra Señora de la Concepción, nicknamed the Cacafuego, whose silver, gold and jewels made the voyage. He landed on the coast of California and claimed it for Elizabeth as Nova Albion, crossed the Pacific, traded for cloves in the Moluccas, rounded the Cape of Good Hope and reached Plymouth on 26 September 1580, the first Englishman to sail round the world. Elizabeth knighted him aboard the Golden Hind at Deptford on 4 April 1581, although Philip II had demanded his punishment. The consequences ran in several directions: a return to the investors out of all proportion to what they had put in, with a share for the queen; proof that Spain's empire was a long thin line of ports that could not be defended everywhere at once; charts and experience of the Pacific and the spice islands; a surge of English confidence and of investment in later voyages, Raleigh's among them; and a further souring of relations with Spain. A top-level answer picks a criterion - money, knowledge, prestige or the road to war - states it, and ranks the consequences against it, rather than retelling the voyage in order."},{"h":"Why the English tried to plant a colony in Virginia","code":"B4-3 — 4 Attempted colonisation of Virginia: Reasons for the attempted colonisation","t":"Colonies promised what England wanted: land and raw materials, a market for English cloth when the Antwerp trade was failing, a base from which privateers could strike the Spanish treasure fleets in the Caribbean, a claim to territory in a New World that Spain treated as its own, somewhere to send the unemployed poor, souls to be won for Protestantism and the hope of gold. Richard Hakluyt set the arguments out for Elizabeth in 1584. Sir Humphrey Gilbert had claimed Newfoundland in 1583 and drowned on the voyage home; his half-brother Walter Raleigh took up the work. Raleigh is the reason the idea went further than talk. A favourite of the queen, though not knighted until January 1585, he obtained a royal patent in 1584 to settle lands not held by any Christian prince, sent Philip Amadas and Arthur Barlowe to reconnoitre the coast that summer, and used their glowing report - and the name Virginia, chosen for the unmarried queen - to raise money and recruits. He paid, organised and publicised; what he never did was sail, because Elizabeth would not let her favourite leave court. The Crown put in a ship and its blessing but not the Exchequer, so the venture depended on private investors and on whatever privateering might bring in. A top-level answer keeps the motives (why anyone wanted a colony at all) apart from the means (why the attempt happened in 1585 and not at some other time), and gives Raleigh his proper weight as organiser and financier rather than as explorer."},{"h":"Why the first settlement at Roanoke failed","code":"B4-3 — 4 Attempted colonisation of Virginia: Reasons for the failure of the first settlement","t":"In 1585 Sir Richard Grenville carried about a hundred men to Roanoke Island, off the coast of what is now North Carolina, and left Ralph Lane as governor. Within a year the colony was gone, and the causes reinforced one another. The expedition arrived in summer, too late to clear ground, sow and harvest, and much of its food was ruined when the flagship grounded in the shallow inlet on arrival. The Outer Banks offered no deep-water harbour, so supply ships could not come in safely and the island was poorly placed even for the privateering it was partly meant to serve. The settlers were largely soldiers and gentlemen hunting gold and glory rather than farmers, fishermen and carpenters, so they could not feed themselves. That left them dependent on the Algonquian people around them, and Lane's methods - demanding food, burning a village and finally having the chief Wingina killed in 1586 - turned neighbours into enemies. Grenville's relief did not come when promised, partly because the war with Spain from 1585 claimed ships, men and money. When Drake called in June 1586 on his way home from raiding the Caribbean, the hungry colonists took passage with him; Grenville arrived soon after, found the place deserted and left a small holding party who were never seen again. A second colony of about 115 men, women and children under Governor John White landed in 1587 and produced Virginia Dare, White's granddaughter and the first English child born in America, but White sailed home for supplies, the Armada kept him in England, and when he returned in 1590 the settlement was empty with the word CROATOAN cut into a post. A top-level answer groups the causes - the season, the site, the people chosen, relations with the Algonquians, the supply line and the war - and argues which group was decisive, distinguishing the trigger from the conditions."}],"examples":[{"title":"Answering 1(a) and 1(b): one feature, one detail, twice","setup":"The question is: '1(a) Describe one feature of apprenticeship in Elizabethan England. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the universities of Elizabethan England. [2 marks]'. You know that an apprentice was bound to a single master by a written agreement; that the Statute of Artificers of 1563 fixed the term at seven years and forbade anyone to practise a trade who had not served it; that the apprentice lived in his master's household and was fed, housed and taught the craft instead of being paid wages; that England had only two universities, Oxford and Cambridge; that boys entered them at about fourteen or fifteen; and that they worked through the seven liberal arts - grammar, rhetoric, logic, arithmetic, geometry, astronomy and music - before any higher degree in divinity, law or medicine.","steps":["Put the feature asked for in part (a) into one plain sentence: an Elizabethan apprentice was bound to one master for a fixed term of years.","Prove it with the single detail that pins it down: the Statute of Artificers of 1563 fixed that term at seven years, and nobody who had not served it was allowed to practise the trade.","Begin part (b) on quite different ground instead of carrying the workshop over: England had only two universities, Oxford and Cambridge.","Support that with a detail of the same weight: boys entered at about fourteen or fifteen and worked through the seven liberal arts before any degree in divinity, law or medicine.","Prune each sentence back to what was asked: a clause beginning 'because trades needed trained men' gives a reason rather than a feature, and reasons earn nothing in either part.","Write the pair out as the examiner will read them: (a) an apprentice served one master under a binding agreement, seven years of it under the Act of 1563, living in his master's house and taught the craft in place of wages; (b) there were only two universities, Oxford and Cambridge, entered at about fourteen or fifteen, where the seven liberal arts came before any degree in divinity, law or medicine."],"cues":["Who did an apprentice work for, and for how long?","Which Act of Parliament set the length of the term?","How many universities could an English boy go to?","What did he study there before any higher degree?","Which kind of clause turns a feature into a reason?","What is left to do once both features and both details have been chosen?"]},{"title":"Building a 12-mark 'Explain why' that clears both caps","setup":"The question is: 'Explain why the number of vagabonds in England increased in the years 1558-88. You may use the following in your answer: • rising food prices • enclosure. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)'. The mark scheme prints two caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content. You know that a vagabond was anyone outside his own parish without work; that food prices rose faster than wages after the debasement of the coinage; that landlords raised rents and turned arable land over to sheep; that the population grew from roughly three million to about four million across the century; and that the Antwerp cloth market was disrupted in the 1560s.","steps":["Fix the conceptual focus before writing a word: the question asks why more people were on the roads, so every paragraph has to end with a family leaving home and not with a description of Elizabethan hardship.","Make the first bullet causal rather than descriptive: food cost more each year while wages lagged behind, so a household with no reserve had to sell up and walk as soon as a harvest failed.","Do the same with the second bullet: where a landlord enclosed arable and put sheep on it, one shepherd replaced several ploughmen, so a family lost cottage and wage in the same season and had nothing to stay for.","Add the aspect of your own that lifts the caps: a growing population meant more people chasing the same holdings, so rents rose, wages fell, and younger sons who would once have been found a cottage found nothing.","If you want a fourth strand, the disruption of the Antwerp cloth market threw weavers and spinners out of work in districts where nothing else employed the poor in numbers.","Close by linking the reasons instead of repeating them, and note the twist that makes them matter: because relief was a parish matter, the very act of walking to the next town in search of work turned a labourer into a vagrant in law."],"cues":["What exactly has to be explained in every paragraph?","What makes a hungry family leave rather than stay and endure?","What did landlords put on the arable land they enclosed?","Which cause do the two bullets leave out entirely?","Which industry employed poor families in the largest numbers?","Why did looking for work make a man a criminal?"]},{"title":"Choosing the criterion on a 16-mark judgement: what the Virginia ventures brought England","setup":"The question is: \"'The attempts to settle Virginia in the years 1584-88 brought England nothing.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • Thomas Harriot • the colony of 1587. You must also use information of your own. (16 marks)\". You know that Walter Raleigh took out a royal patent in 1584 and sent Philip Amadas and Arthur Barlowe to reconnoitre the coast that summer; that Sir Richard Grenville landed about a hundred men on Roanoke Island in 1585 and that they sailed home with Drake in June 1586; that Thomas Harriot went with them to survey the country and published his report on its soil, its commodities and its people in 1588, and that the artist John White drew the coast and the Algonquian towns; that about 115 men, women and children under White landed in 1587, that White sailed home for supplies and that no relief had reached them by the end of 1588; that England was at war with Spain from 1585 and that the Armada campaign of 1588 kept ships and men at home; and that the patent, the name Virginia and the English claim to the coast all survived.","steps":["State the criterion before the verdict: 'nothing' can only stand if England ended the period no better placed than it began it - no settlers, no trade, no knowledge and no claim.","Give the statement its best case in its own terms: by the end of 1588 there was no English settlement in Virginia at all, since Lane's men gave up and sailed with Drake in June 1586 and White's 115 colonists were left without relief, so the money and the lives had bought no colony.","Set the first bullet against that: Harriot's report of 1588 set out the soil, the commodities and the people of the country, and White's drawings of the coast and the Algonquian towns went with it, so the next expedition could plan where the first had guessed.","Add the gain neither bullet offers: the patent of 1584, the name Virginia and the claim to the coast were still England's in 1588, so the case for planting a colony stood in better shape than when it was first argued.","Weigh the two kinds of gain against each other: a colony is a gain that can be counted, while knowledge, experience and a claim pay only if somebody sails again - and from 1585 the Spanish war was taking the ships that would have to carry them.","Judge on the criterion you stated: agree that the ventures failed at the thing they were for, disagree that they brought nothing, and name in the closing sentence which of the two the word 'nothing' is being measured against."],"cues":["What would have to be true before 'nothing' is the right word?","Where were England's settlers in Virginia at the end of 1588?","What did the men who came home in 1586 bring back with them?","Which of England's gains is named on neither bullet?","What has to happen before knowledge and a claim are worth anything?","What must the last sentence name besides the verdict?"]},{"title":"Ranking the consequences of Drake's circumnavigation","setup":"The question is: \"'The most important result of Drake's voyage round the world in the years 1577-80 was the wealth it brought back.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the Cacafuego • Nova Albion. You must also use information of your own. (16 marks)\". You know that Drake left Plymouth in December 1577 and returned there on 26 September 1580; that in March 1579 he took the Spanish treasure ship nicknamed the Cacafuego off the Pacific coast of South America; that the queen was among the investors and took a share; that Drake claimed part of the Californian coast as Nova Albion; that Philip II demanded his punishment; and that Elizabeth knighted him aboard the Golden Hind at Deptford on 4 April 1581.","steps":["Decide what the word 'important' will be measured by: importance here means what changed afterwards and for how long, not how impressive the voyage looked at the time.","Give the statement its best case: the Cacafuego's silver, gold and jewels repaid the investors many times over and gave a share to a Crown that could not tax without Parliament.","Turn the money into a consequence rather than a total: the profit is what made further ventures fundable, including Raleigh's Virginia expeditions later in the decade.","Set the second bullet against it: Nova Albion was a claim on paper that produced no settlement and no trade, so it matters far less than it sounds.","Add the point the bullets do not offer: knighting Drake at Deptford was a public answer to Philip II's demand for punishment, so the voyage hardened a quarrel with Spain that ended in war.","Judge: the treasure mattered most as a cause of what followed, but the damage to relations with Spain outlasted it, so agree with the statement only on the short-term criterion and say which criterion you are using."],"cues":["What does 'most important' have to mean before anything can be measured?","Who received the money, and why does that matter for the Crown?","What did the profit make possible next?","Did the claim in California change anything on the ground?","How did Elizabeth answer Philip II in public?","What must the final sentence name besides the verdict?"]},{"title":"Making a 12-mark 'Explain why' out of where the playhouses stood","setup":"The question is: 'Explain why the London playhouses were built outside the City of London in the years 1576-87. You may use the following in your answer: • the City authorities • plague. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)'. The mark scheme prints two caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content. You know that before 1576 plays were given in inn-yards and halls inside the City; that the City authorities licensed playing within the walls, restricted it and could stop it altogether; that James Burbage built the Theatre at Shoreditch, north of the City boundary, in 1576 and that the Curtain followed nearby in 1577; that Philip Henslowe opened the Rose on Bankside, across the river in Southwark, in 1587; that a playhouse packed a thousand people and more into one yard, that crowds were believed to spread plague and that playing was stopped when deaths rose; that Puritan preachers attacked the playhouses as nurseries of idleness, vice and disorder; and that ground beyond the walls was cheaper and more open, and that Bankside already drew its crowds to the baiting rings.","steps":["Fix the conceptual focus before writing a word: the question asks why the buildings stood where they did, so every paragraph has to end at a site beyond the walls and not at a description of playgoing.","Make the first bullet causal rather than descriptive: inside the walls a company played at the City's pleasure, since the authorities licensed playing, restricted it and could stop it, so Burbage put his money into ground at Shoreditch in 1576 where their writ did not run, and the Curtain followed there in 1577.","Do the same with the second bullet: a playhouse gathered a thousand people and more into one yard, and because such crowds were believed to spread plague, playing was stopped whenever the deaths rose - which is one more reason to stand outside the jurisdiction that did the stopping.","Add the aspect of your own that lifts the caps: Puritan preachers denounced the playhouses as nurseries of idleness, vice and disorder, and that preaching handed the City its argument, so the suburbs were the only ground on which a permanent playhouse was safe to build.","If you want a fourth strand, take the practical one: land beyond the walls was cheaper and open enough for a large round building, and Bankside already brought its crowds across the river to the baiting rings, which is why Henslowe opened the Rose there in 1587.","Close by linking the reasons instead of repeating them: authority decided which side of the boundary was safe, plague and preaching supplied the arguments for keeping plays out, and cheap open ground made the far side usable - which is why the first playhouses ring the City instead of standing inside it."],"cues":["What exactly has to be explained in every paragraph?","Who could stop a play inside the walls?","What was a crowd of a thousand people believed to spread?","Who else was arguing against the playhouses, and on what grounds?","What did Bankside already have that suited a playhouse?","How do the reasons fit together in one closing sentence?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which three Acts between 1563 and 1576 changed the treatment of the poor, and what did each do?","a":"The 1563 Act for the Relief of the Poor made contributions to parish relief effectively compulsory, with refusers answerable to Justices of the Peace; the 1572 Vagabonds Act ordered vagrants over fourteen to be whipped and bored through the right ear, allowed death for a third offence and set up a compulsory poor rate; the 1576 Act for Setting the Poor on Work made towns buy wool, hemp and flax to set the able-bodied poor to work, with a house of correction for those who refused."},{"q":"Name the first successful purpose-built London playhouse and the playhouse opened on Bankside in 1587, with their builders.","a":"The Theatre, built by James Burbage at Shoreditch in 1576, and the Rose, opened by Philip Henslowe on Bankside in 1587."},{"q":"What did Drake capture in March 1579, and what honour followed his return?","a":"The Spanish treasure ship Nuestra Señora de la Concepción, nicknamed the Cacafuego. He reached Plymouth on 26 September 1580 and Elizabeth knighted him aboard the Golden Hind at Deptford on 4 April 1581."},{"q":"Give three reasons why the first Roanoke settlement of 1585 failed.","a":"Any three of: it landed in summer, too late to plant a crop, and lost stores when the flagship grounded in the shallow inlet; the settlers were soldiers and gentlemen rather than farmers and fishermen; Ralph Lane's demands for food, the burning of a village and the killing of Wingina turned the Algonquians into enemies; the Outer Banks gave no deep-water harbour for supply ships; relief was late because the war with Spain from 1585 took ships and men."}]},"room":{"facts":["Booklet B of Paper 2 carries 32 marks on this depth study in three questions: Question 1, printed as 1(a) and 1(b), 'Describe one feature of...' (2 marks each, 4 in all) on two separate aspects of content; Question 2, 'Explain why...' (12 marks); and a choice of Question 3 or Question 4, a 16-mark judgement ending 'How far do you agree? Explain your answer.'","Question 1(a) and 1(b) are point-marked: in each part one mark for a valid feature and a second mark for supporting information, 2 marks a part. One feature only in each part, and no reasons, consequences or judgement, because nothing else is paid for.","Question 2 and the 16-mark judgement each print two stimulus points under 'You may use the following in your answer:' followed by 'You must also use information of your own.' The bullets are optional; adding material of your own is not.","The stimulus caps are printed inside the grids. On the 12-mark Question 2: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'. On the 16-mark Question 3 or 4 the same two lines with 'Maximum 11 marks'. There is no cap at Level 2 on either question.","Level 4 is 10-12 marks on the 12-mark question and 13-16 on the 16-mark judgement. It needs an analytical explanation directed consistently at the conceptual focus, a line of reasoning that is coherent, sustained and logically structured, and on the 16-mark question criteria for the judgement that are justified and applied.","The AO split is AO2 6 and AO1 6 on the 12-mark question and AO2 10 and AO1 6 on the 16-mark judgement, whose grid prints three bullets at every level: the AO2 explanation, the AO1 accurate and relevant information, and the overall judgement, which is AO2 as well. An answer showing no qualities of AO2 cannot rise above the top of Level 1 however strong the knowledge.","There are no sources, no interpretations and no SPaG marks anywhere on Paper 2, so every mark comes from your own knowledge. The paper lasts 1 hour 50 minutes for 64 marks across the two booklets, about 55 minutes for this one: work at roughly a minute a mark, so about 5 minutes on 1(a) and 1(b) together, 20 on Question 2 and 27 on the 16-mark judgement, leaving a few minutes to check."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of Elizabethan grammar schools. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the education of the sons of noblemen in Elizabethan England. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4, point-marked in two parts, 2 marks each: one mark for a valid feature, one for supporting information. 1(a): credit the Latin curriculum, the long six-day school day, fees with free places for poor scholars, or entry at about seven after a petty school. 1(b): credit private tutors at home, the humanist curriculum of Latin, Greek and history, or the courtier's accomplishments of riding, fencing, music and dancing.","sol":"1(a) One feature was that grammar schools taught in and through Latin. The timetable was filled with Latin grammar, rhetoric and classical authors, with Greek added in the upper forms, because Latin was the language of law, learning and the Church. 1(b) One feature was that a nobleman's son was taught at home rather than at a school. A private tutor took him through Latin, Greek and history, while riding, fencing, music and dancing were added to fit him for the court, because he was being prepared for royal service rather than for a trade.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Each part is a separate two-mark question, so one feature in (a) and one in (b).","For (a) think what filled the school timetable; for (b) think where the teaching happened and who did it.","Feature first in one plain sentence, then a single precise detail; nothing else scores here."],"codes":["B4-3 — 1 Education and leisure: Education in the home and schools"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the London playhouses built in the years 1576-87. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the sports enjoyed by ordinary Elizabethans. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4, point-marked, 2 marks each. 1(a): credit that they were purpose-built (the Theatre at Shoreditch in 1576, the Rose on Bankside in 1587), that they stood outside the City's jurisdiction, or that a penny bought standing room in the yard. 1(b): credit village football with almost no rules, the baiting of bears and bulls with dogs, or cock-fighting, wrestling and gambling at fairs and feasts.","sol":"1(a) One feature was that they were built beyond the City of London's control. James Burbage put the Theatre at Shoreditch in 1576 and Philip Henslowe the Rose on Bankside in 1587, out of reach of City authorities who blamed plays for idleness, crime and plague. 1(b) One feature was that ordinary people's sports were rough and often bloody. Village football had almost no rules and regularly injured its players, while chained bears and bulls were set on by dogs before paying crowds.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Two separate parts: one feature about the buildings, one about what ordinary people played or watched.","For (a) ask what was new about these buildings and where they stood; for (b) ask what the crowd came to see.","State the feature, then one detail that proves it, and stop."],"codes":["B4-3 — 1 Education and leisure: Sport, pastimes and the theatre"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the Vagabonds Act of 1572. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the Act for Setting the Poor on Work of 1576. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4, point-marked, 2 marks each. 1(a): credit the whipping and boring through the right ear of vagrants over fourteen, death for a third offence, the registers of the poor kept by towns, or the compulsory poor rate. 1(b): credit the raw materials - wool, hemp, flax - that towns had to provide, or the house of correction for those who refused the work offered.","sol":"1(a) One feature was its severity towards wanderers. A vagrant over fourteen was to be whipped and bored through the right ear for a first offence, and a third offence could be punished as a felony, by death. 1(b) One feature was that it made towns provide work and not only relief. Every town had to buy raw materials such as wool, hemp and flax so that the able-bodied poor could be set to work for wages, and those who then refused were sent to a house of correction.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["One feature of each Act, written under its own heading.","For 1572 think what happened to the vagrant's body; for 1576 think what the town had to buy.","Name the feature in a sentence, add one precise detail, and resist explaining why the Act was passed."],"codes":["B4-3 — 2 The 'problem' of the poor: The changing attitudes and policies towards the poor"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of the instruments Elizabethan sailors used to navigate. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of the Muscovy Company. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4, point-marked, 2 marks each. 1(a): credit the mariner's astrolabe or quadrant for finding latitude from the sun or the Pole Star, the magnetic compass for holding a course, or the log line and sand-glass for judging speed. 1(b): credit the royal charter of 1555, the monopoly of the Russian trade opened by Richard Chancellor, or its joint-stock form spreading the risk among investors.","sol":"1(a) One feature was that the instruments found latitude by measuring the height of a heavenly body. A sailor used a mariner's astrolabe or a quadrant to take the angle of the sun at noon or of the Pole Star, and read off how far north or south he was. 1(b) One feature was that it was a joint-stock company. Merchants bought shares so that the cost and the risk of the long voyage to Russia were spread, and the company held a royal charter of 1555 giving it a monopoly of that trade.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Keep the parts apart: instruments in (a), a trading company in (b).","For (a) ask what a navigator could actually measure at sea; for (b) ask who paid for the voyages.","One feature and one supporting detail in each part is the whole of what is paid for."],"codes":["B4-3 — 3 Exploration and voyages of discovery: Factors prompting exploration"]},{"q":"1(a) Describe one feature of Drake's voyage round the world in the years 1577-80. [2 marks] 1(b) Describe one feature of Sir Walter Raleigh's part in the attempts to colonise Virginia. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 4, point-marked, 2 marks each. 1(a): credit the raiding of Spanish ports and shipping in the Pacific, the capture of the Cacafuego in March 1579, the claiming of Nova Albion, or the return to Plymouth on 26 September 1580. 1(b): credit the royal patent of 1584, the reconnaissance voyage of Amadas and Barlowe, the naming of Virginia after the queen, or the fact that he financed and organised the expeditions without ever sailing to America.","sol":"1(a) One feature was that it was a raiding voyage as much as a voyage of discovery. Drake plundered Spanish ports and shipping along the Pacific coast of South America and in March 1579 took the treasure ship nicknamed the Cacafuego, whose silver and gold made the whole venture profitable. 1(b) One feature was that Raleigh organised and paid for the colony but never went to it. He obtained a royal patent in 1584 and sent others, among them Sir Richard Grenville and Ralph Lane, because Elizabeth would not let her favourite leave court.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Two subjects, two answers: the voyage in (a), Raleigh's own role in (b).","For (a) ask what Drake spent most of the voyage doing; for (b) ask what Raleigh supplied and what he never did.","A feature and one detail each; a narrative of the voyage wastes the time you need later."],"codes":["B4-3 — 3 Exploration and voyages of discovery: Drake's circumnavigation of the globe","B4-3 — 4 Attempted colonisation of Virginia: Reasons for the attempted colonisation"]},{"q":"Explain why Elizabeth I supported Drake's voyage round the world in the years 1577-80. You may use the following in your answer: • treasure • Philip II. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit the Crown's need for money it could not raise without Parliament and the plunder Drake brought home, and the quarrel with Spain over religion, the Netherlands and Spain's claimed monopoly of the Americas; beyond the bullets credit the deniability of a privately financed voyage disguised as trade, the grievance left by the attack on Hawkins at San Juan de Ulua in 1568, the search for new markets after the Antwerp trade was disrupted, and the prestige of an English circumnavigation. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Elizabeth backed the voyage because it promised three things she wanted and one she needed above all: deniability. Money came first. The Crown's ordinary revenue barely covered the cost of government, and the queen could not raise more without calling Parliament and inviting demands in return. A raiding voyage into the Pacific, where Spanish ports and treasure ships lay almost undefended, cost the Exchequer nothing if it failed and paid her a share if it succeeded. Drake proved the point: the plunder of Spanish shipping, above all the treasure ship nicknamed the Cacafuego taken in March 1579, repaid the investors many times over. The second reason was Philip II. England and Spain were not at war in 1577, but they were already quarrelling over religion, over English help to the Dutch rebels and over Spain's claim to a monopoly of the Americas, and English seamen had a grievance of their own after the Spanish attack on John Hawkins's fleet at San Juan de Ulua in 1568, where Drake himself had commanded a ship. Damaging Spain in a place where she was weak hurt a rival without committing England to open war. That is why the third reason, which the bullets do not offer, mattered so much. The expedition was fitted out as a trading venture, and the queen invested privately alongside courtiers such as Sir Christopher Hatton, whose crest gave the Golden Hind her new name. If Drake were taken, or the raids proved too provocative, she could disown him as a pirate; if he came home rich, she could take her share. Backing a private captain gave her the profits of war without its costs or its responsibility. Finally there were trade and prestige. Merchants wanted routes and markets of their own after the Antwerp cloth trade was disrupted in the 1560s, and an English captain circling the globe would place England among the great seafaring powers. Profit, rivalry, deniability and reputation all pointed the same way, which is why a cautious queen backed so reckless a voyage.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the queen was short of, and what she could not simply ask Parliament to give her.","Plan a financial reason, a reason about Spain, and a reason about how the voyage was disguised.","End every paragraph on why she backed it, not on what Drake did once he had sailed."],"codes":["B4-3 — 3 Exploration and voyages of discovery: Drake's circumnavigation of the globe","B4-3 — 3 Exploration and voyages of discovery: Factors prompting exploration"]},{"q":"Explain why more boys received a formal education in Elizabethan England in the years 1558-88. You may use the following in your answer: • the printing press • Protestantism. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit cheaper printed books and the Protestant demand for a Bible-reading laity and a preaching clergy; beyond the bullets credit the founding and refounding of grammar schools in market towns, humanist ideas of the educated gentleman, the needs of trade, law and royal service, the seven-year apprenticeships fixed by the Statute of Artificers of 1563, and the growing wealth of the middling sort. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"More boys were educated because reading became cheaper, more necessary and more respectable at the same time. Printing was the practical change. Books that had once been copied by hand could now be produced in hundreds, so a grammar school could put a Latin grammar in front of every boy in the form and a merchant could keep a written account. Cheap print made a schoolmaster's work possible and gave a literate boy something to do with the skill after he left. Protestantism supplied the motive. A church that told people to read the Bible in English for themselves needed a laity that could read, and a church that had abolished the Latin mass needed ministers trained to preach and to argue. Religious teaching ran through the school day, and schools and college places were founded and refounded partly for that reason. The reason the bullets leave out is office and money. Government, the courts and trade were all expanding, and the men who ran them - clerks, lawyers, secretaries, stewards, merchants - had to read, write and reckon. Oxford and Cambridge took boys at about fourteen or fifteen through the liberal arts before divinity, law or medicine, and the Inns of Court filled with gentlemen's sons who would never plead a case but wanted the training. Learning that had once marked a clergyman now marked a gentleman fit for the queen's service, and that humanist idea is what persuaded fathers to pay fees. There was also more to pay with. Gentry, yeomen and merchants prospered across the century, and endowments from wealthy townsmen and guilds founded schools that charged fees but kept free places for poor scholars. Education therefore spread because print made it cheap, religion made it a duty, government and trade made it useful and prosperity made it affordable - but only as far down as families who could spare a son from work.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask who was paying for schooling, and what they expected to get for the money.","Build three reasons of different kinds: a technology, a religion, and a set of jobs that needed literate men.","Each paragraph must finish on the outcome - more boys in school - and not on a description of the school day."],"codes":["B4-3 — 1 Education and leisure: Education in the home and schools"]},{"q":"Explain why the theatre became more important in England in the years 1558-88. You may use the following in your answer: • noble patronage • purpose-built playhouses. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit patronage and licensing forced by the 1572 Act, the Earl of Leicester's Men of 1574 and the Queen's Men of 1583, and the building of the Theatre (1576), the Curtain (1577) and the Rose (1587); beyond the bullets credit the growth of London and its paying audience, the penny entrance, the supply of new plays, and court protection against the City and the Puritans. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The theatre grew into something new because the organisation, the buildings and the audience all arrived together. Patronage came first, and it came through the law. The Vagabonds Act of 1572 counted players who belonged to no lord as rogues, liable to be whipped, so a company that wanted to perform had to find a patron. The effect was not to end acting but to organise it: troupes took their patrons' names, the Earl of Leicester's Men were licensed in 1574, and in 1583 the Queen's Men were formed from the best players of other companies. A licensed company with a great name behind it could tour, could face down hostile town officials and could invest in costumes and plays. Purpose-built playhouses turned that organisation into a trade. Plays had been given in inn-yards, guildhalls and great houses, where the space was borrowed and the takings uncertain. James Burbage built the Theatre at Shoreditch in 1576, the Curtain followed in 1577, and Philip Henslowe opened the Rose on Bankside in 1587. A permanent house with a paying entrance meant regular performances, a resident company and a reason to commission new plays. The cause the bullets do not offer is the audience. London was growing very fast and held thousands of apprentices, servants, sailors and visitors with a little money and free afternoons. A penny bought standing room in the yard and a few pence a gallery seat, so one performance could take money from a gentleman and a labourer at once, and that mixed paying crowd is what made a playhouse profitable. Finally the companies needed protection, because Puritan preachers and the City authorities attacked plays as nurseries of idleness, crime and plague. Building outside the City's jurisdiction, and performing at court, kept the playhouses open long enough for all the other causes to work.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what a company of actors needed before it could earn a living from plays.","Take the law, the buildings and the audience as three separate causes, and give each its own paragraph.","Every paragraph should finish by saying how that change made the theatre matter more, not merely how it changed."],"codes":["B4-3 — 1 Education and leisure: Sport, pastimes and the theatre","B4-3 — 2 The 'problem' of the poor: The changing attitudes and policies towards the poor"]},{"q":"Explain why government policy towards the poor changed in the years 1563-76. You may use the following in your answer: • the fear of disorder • the Vagabonds Act (1572). You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit the fear of masterless men and rebellion behind the whipping and ear-boring clauses of 1572, and the poor rate and registers in the same Act; beyond the bullets credit the failure of punishment alone, the town censuses such as Norwich's of 1570, Protestant and humanist ideas of Christian duty, and the loss of monastic charity after the 1530s. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Policy changed because government was frightened, then better informed, and all the while under religious pressure to act. Fear of disorder came first. A society living off the harvest believed masterless men moving between parishes would steal, spread plague and, worst of all, gather; a rising among the poor would have no gentleman at its head to negotiate with. That fear is written into the Vagabonds Act of 1572, the harshest measure of the reign: a vagrant over fourteen was to be whipped and bored through the right ear for a first offence, and a third offence could be punished by death. The aim was to frighten wanderers back into their own parishes. But the same Act shows the second cause at work, because it also created a compulsory poor rate collected locally and ordered towns to keep registers of their poor. Fear alone would have produced punishment; what produced money was the discovery that punishment failed, since a vagrant whipped and sent home came back to a parish with nothing in it. The reason the bullets do not offer is evidence. Towns had begun to count their poor: Norwich surveyed its poor in 1570, sorted them by need and set the able-bodied to work. The surveys showed that most of the poor were children, the old, the sick and widows who could not work at all, and that many able-bodied poor had no work to go to rather than no wish to do it. That evidence produced the 1576 Act for Setting the Poor on Work, which made towns buy wool, hemp and flax so the poor could earn, with a house of correction only for those who then refused. Religion supplied the justification. Protestant preaching made relief of the poor a duty of the whole community rather than a private kindness at a monastery gate - and the monasteries were gone. Fear gave the urgency, the surveys gave the facts and duty gave the argument, which is why one reign both bored vagrants' ears and bought wool to set them working.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the government was afraid would happen if it did nothing.","Sort the three Acts by what each assumes a poor person is, then explain what changed the assumption.","Aim for three causes of different kinds - a fear, a piece of evidence and a belief - and end each paragraph on the change in policy."],"codes":["B4-3 — 2 The 'problem' of the poor: The changing attitudes and policies towards the poor","B4-3 — 2 The 'problem' of the poor: The reasons for the increase in poverty and vagabondage"]},{"q":"Explain why English sailors were able to travel further in the years 1558-88. You may use the following in your answer: • the galleon • the astrolabe. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit the design of the galleon and the astrolabe or quadrant for latitude; beyond the bullets credit the compass, log line and sand-glass, improved charts such as Mercator's projection of 1569, printed sailing directions, the experience gained on earlier voyages, and the joint-stock investment that paid for bigger and better-found ships. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"English ships went further because the ships themselves, the instruments aboard them and the money behind them all improved together. The galleon was the first change. It was narrower and lower in the water than the old carrack, with much lighter castles fore and aft, so it was faster, rolled less and answered the helm better; it also carried more guns and more cargo, which mattered for a voyage that expected to fight as well as trade. Square sails for running before the wind were combined with triangular lateen sails that let a ship sail closer to it, so a captain was no longer at the mercy of a contrary wind for weeks at a time. The instruments were the second change. The mariner's astrolabe and the quadrant let a navigator measure the height of the sun at noon or of the Pole Star and read off his latitude from a table, so a ship could find the right parallel and run down it to a known landfall instead of creeping along a coast. Without a way of fixing position, no captain would risk weeks out of sight of land. The cause the bullets leave out is everything else the navigator used. A magnetic compass held the course, a log line and a sand-glass measured speed, and a traverse board kept the reckoning between sights; charts improved as well, and Gerardus Mercator's world map of 1569 projected the globe so that a constant compass bearing could be drawn as a straight line. Printed sailing directions passed one crew's hard-won knowledge to the next. Finally, long voyages had to be paid for. Joint-stock companies such as the Muscovy Company, chartered in 1555, spread the cost and the risk among many investors, so merchants, courtiers and the queen could finance ships large enough and well enough found to stay at sea for years. Better ships made the voyages possible, instruments made them survivable, and shared investment made them worth attempting.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what stops a small ship crossing an ocean, and deal with each obstacle in turn.","Separate the ship, the instruments and the money, and give each its own paragraph with a named example.","Every paragraph must end on the words 'further' or 'longer voyages', linking the improvement to the distance sailed."],"codes":["B4-3 — 3 Exploration and voyages of discovery: Factors prompting exploration"]},{"q":"Explain why the English attempted to found a colony in Virginia in the years 1584-87. You may use the following in your answer: • Sir Walter Raleigh • trade. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Credit Raleigh's patent of 1584, his money and organisation and the reconnaissance by Amadas and Barlowe, and the search for markets and raw materials after the Antwerp trade failed; beyond the bullets credit the wish for a privateering base against the Spanish treasure fleets, rivalry with Catholic Spain, Hakluyt's arguments of 1584 including work for the unemployed poor, and Gilbert's precedent in Newfoundland in 1583. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The English tried to plant a colony because a handful of motives that had been discussed for years finally found a man with a patent and the money to act on them. Raleigh is the immediate reason. A favourite of the queen, knighted in 1585, he obtained a royal patent in 1584 to settle lands not held by any Christian prince, and at once sent Philip Amadas and Arthur Barlowe to reconnoitre the coast. Their glowing report, and the name Virginia chosen to flatter the unmarried queen, were used to raise money and recruits. Elizabeth would not spend the Exchequer's money on it, so without a courtier willing to risk his own the attempt would not have been made at all. Trade supplied the reasoning that persuaded investors. The Antwerp market for English cloth had been disrupted in the 1560s, and merchants wanted new customers and new goods; a colony promised timber, furs, dyes and perhaps precious metals that England was otherwise buying from rivals, and a market of its own for English woollens. The cause the bullets leave out is Spain. A settlement on the American coast would be a base from which privateers could refit and strike the treasure fleets that carried silver home through the Caribbean, and it would plant a Protestant claim in a hemisphere the Spanish king treated as his own. After 1585 England and Spain were at war, which made such a base look like strategy rather than adventure. Behind all three lay argument and example. Richard Hakluyt urged the queen in 1584 to plant colonies, promising trade, bases against Spain, the spread of Protestantism and employment for England's growing numbers of unemployed poor; and Sir Humphrey Gilbert, Raleigh's half-brother, had already claimed Newfoundland in 1583 before drowning on the voyage home. Profit, war and propaganda together explain the attempt, but it was Raleigh's patent and purse that fixed its date.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what a colony was supposed to do for England, and then who was willing to pay for it.","Take a person, an economic motive and a strategic motive as your three strands.","Keep asking why the attempt happened in these particular years rather than earlier, and end each paragraph on that."],"codes":["B4-3 — 4 Attempted colonisation of Virginia: Reasons for the attempted colonisation","B4-3 — 3 Exploration and voyages of discovery: Factors prompting exploration"]},{"q":"'The growth of the population was the main reason for the increase in vagabondage in the years 1558-88.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • bad harvests • enclosure. You must also use information of your own. (16 marks)","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit population growth as the underlying pressure weighed against dearth years such as 1586, enclosure and rack-renting, and beyond the bullets the disruption of the Antwerp cloth trade, demobilised soldiers after 1585, and the law that made anyone outside his parish a vagrant; a criterion-based judgement distinguishing poverty from movement. Caps: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Vagabondage means the poor on the move, so this question asks not only why there were more poor people but why they left home. My criterion is simple: a cause is the main reason if its absence would have kept people in their villages. On that test population growth was the underlying pressure but not the main reason, because it explains poverty far better than it explains wandering. The case for the statement is real. England held roughly three million people when Elizabeth came to the throne and about four million by 1600. In a society where almost every living came from land, more people meant more competition for holdings and for work, so rents rose and wages fell without any landlord or employer deciding it. Younger sons who would once have been found a cottage and a few acres now found nothing, and it was exactly those young, unattached people who took to the roads. Every other cause listed below worked on a population already too large for the land available, which is why the pressure never eased across the whole reign. But numbers alone only make people poor; what makes a vagabond is losing a place. Enclosure did that directly. Where a landlord enclosed arable and turned it over to sheep, one shepherd replaced several ploughmen, and a family lost cottage and wage together; rack-renting had the same effect more slowly. A family evicted has to move, and the law counted anybody outside his own parish without work as a vagrant, so the very act of looking for work turned a labourer into a criminal liable under the 1572 Act to be whipped and bored through the ear. Bad harvests turned a slow drift into a flood. Grain prices rose sharply in dearth years such as 1586, and because wages had already fallen behind prices after the debasement of the coinage, a household with no reserve had to sell up and walk. Hunger sets people moving within weeks; population growth takes a generation. The point the bullets do not offer is that whole trades collapsed. Woollen cloth was England's great export, and the disruption of the Antwerp market in the 1560s threw weavers, spinners and carders out of work in districts where nothing else employed the poor in numbers. Demobilised soldiers and sailors, especially after England intervened in the Netherlands in 1585, added men with no land, no trade and no parish that would own them. These people did not become poor gradually; they became masterless overnight, and they went where the work might be, above all to London, which may have grown from some seventy thousand people around 1550 to about two hundred thousand by 1600. The criterion decides the answer. If the question were why there were more poor people, population growth would be the main reason, because it lies underneath every other cause. But vagabondage is movement, and the immediate causes of movement were eviction, dearth and the collapse of trades, with population growth as the condition that made all three unbearable. I therefore agree with the statement as an account of poverty and disagree with it as an account of vagabondage.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Notice that the statement is about vagabondage, not about poverty, and ask what the difference is.","Separate the causes that made people poor from the causes that made them leave their parish.","Decide and state the criterion for 'main reason' in your first paragraph, then apply the same test to every cause."],"codes":["B4-3 — 2 The 'problem' of the poor: The reasons for the increase in poverty and vagabondage","B4-3 — 2 The 'problem' of the poor: The changing attitudes and policies towards the poor"]},{"q":"'Drake's voyage round the world in the years 1577-80 did more harm than good to England.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the treasure • Philip II. You must also use information of your own. (16 marks)","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit the profit from the Cacafuego and the queen's share, the prestige, the charts and experience, and the funding of later voyages, weighed against Philip II's demand for punishment and the knighthood of 1581; beyond the bullets credit the other causes of the quarrel with Spain, such as the Treaty of Nonsuch of 1585, with a criterion-based judgement. Caps: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Harm and good have to be measured against something. Measured against Elizabeth's two constant needs - money for a Crown that was always short of it, and safety from Spain - the voyage did great good in the short term and real harm in the longer term, and the harm came chiefly because the queen chose to advertise the good. The good was first of all financial. Drake left Plymouth in December 1577 with five ships and returned with one, the Golden Hind, on 26 September 1580, carrying the plunder of Spanish ports along the Pacific coast and of the treasure ship nicknamed the Cacafuego, taken in March 1579. The investors, the queen among them, were repaid many times what they had put in, and the Crown's share mattered to a government that could not tax without calling Parliament. That money did not lie idle: the profits of privateering helped fund the voyages that followed, including Raleigh's Virginia expeditions of 1585 and 1587. The second good was knowledge and confidence. Drake was the first Englishman to sail round the world, and he came home with charts, with experience of the Pacific and of the spice trade of the Moluccas, and with proof that Spain's empire was a long thin line of ports that could not be defended everywhere at once. English seamen learned that they could go wherever Spaniards could go, a lesson that lay behind Drake's raid on Cadiz in 1587 and behind English confidence in 1588. The harm was diplomatic, and it was serious. Drake had robbed the subjects of a king with whom England was not at war. Philip II demanded his punishment, and Elizabeth answered by knighting him aboard the Golden Hind at Deptford on 4 April 1581, in public and in front of a foreign envoy. That was a declaration that the Crown had taken a share of the loot and would do so again. A relationship already deteriorating over English help to the Dutch rebels now carried a personal insult as well. The point beyond the bullets is that the harm was not caused by the voyage alone. England and Spain were already drifting towards war over religion, over the Netherlands and over the plots against Elizabeth; the Treaty of Nonsuch in 1585, which sent an English army to help the Dutch, did far more to make war certain than any raid in the Pacific. Drake's voyage handed Philip a grievance he had reasons enough to act on without it, and the same voyage supplied England with the money, the captains and the confidence to survive the war when it came. The judgement therefore turns on what each side of the balance can properly be blamed or credited with. The treasure, the prestige, the navigational experience and the funding of later ventures were direct results that nothing else would have produced. The quarrel with Spain was already under way and would have reached war without Drake. Judged on that criterion I disagree with the statement: the voyage did more good than harm, because its benefits were caused by it and its chief harm was not.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what England actually needed in 1580, and measure harm and good against that.","For every piece of harm, ask whether the voyage caused it or merely added to something already happening.","Set out your criterion at the start and return to it in the final paragraph before you give the verdict."],"codes":["B4-3 — 3 Exploration and voyages of discovery: Drake's circumnavigation of the globe","B4-3 — 3 Exploration and voyages of discovery: Factors prompting exploration"]},{"q":"'Poor leadership was the main reason why the first English settlement in Virginia failed.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • Ralph Lane • supplies. You must also use information of your own. (16 marks)","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit Lane's handling of the Algonquians and Grenville's late relief, weighed against the choice of soldiers and gentlemen as settlers, the late arrival in the season and the stores lost when the flagship grounded; beyond the bullets credit the site behind the Outer Banks and the drain of the Spanish war from 1585, with a criterion-based judgement separating trigger from conditions. Caps: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"A reason counts as the main one if the colony would have survived without it. On that test Ralph Lane's leadership made the failure quicker and uglier, but the settlement was already unlikely to last, because the decisions that doomed it had been taken in England before anybody landed. Leadership deserves its share of the blame. Sir Richard Grenville landed about a hundred men on Roanoke Island in 1585 and left Lane as governor. Lane was a soldier and governed like one. He demanded food from the Algonquian people when his own ran short, he burned one of their villages, and in 1586 he had the chief Wingina killed. Those acts turned the only accessible source of food into an enemy, and a settlement that can neither buy food nor be given it cannot survive a winter. When Drake called in June 1586 on his way home from raiding the Caribbean, Lane's men took passage with him rather than stay. A cooler governor might have kept his neighbours friendly long enough for relief to arrive. But the reason they depended on anybody was the choice of settlers. The colony was made up largely of soldiers and gentlemen who had come for gold and glory, with too few farmers, fishermen and carpenters. Men who cannot plant, fish or build are dependent from the day they land, whoever commands them. Worse, they arrived in the summer of 1585, too late in the season to clear ground, sow and harvest, so the first year had to be lived on stores - and much of the stores was ruined when the flagship grounded in the shallow inlet on arrival. The site is the second condition, and it is the point the bullets do not offer. Roanoke lies behind the Outer Banks, where the water is shallow and the channels shift. Large ships could not come in safely, which made resupply slow, expensive and dangerous, and it also made the island useless as the privateering base the venture was partly meant to provide. A colony whose harbour cannot take the ships that must feed it is badly placed however well it is led. Behind both lay the supply line and the war. Grenville sailed back to England for provisions and did not return when promised; from 1585 England was at war with Spain, and the ships, men and money that might have carried relief went to the Channel and the Netherlands instead. Raleigh, who held the patent of 1584 and paid for the expedition, never came himself, because Elizabeth kept her favourite at court, so nobody with authority over the whole enterprise was on the spot to hold it together. The causes are of two kinds, and that distinction gives the judgement. Lane's hostility to the Algonquians was the trigger that emptied Roanoke in 1586. The settlers chosen, the season of arrival, the site and the stretched supply line were the conditions that made some such trigger almost certain. Since removing Lane would have delayed the failure rather than prevented it, I disagree with the statement: poor leadership was the immediate cause, but the main reason was that the colony was planned as a soldiers' raid and not as a farming settlement.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what would have had to be different for the colony to survive its first winter.","Sort the causes into the one that set the collapse off and the ones that made collapse likely anyway.","State the test for 'main reason' before you argue, and make sure your last sentence uses the word 'main'."],"codes":["B4-3 — 4 Attempted colonisation of Virginia: Reasons for the failure of the first settlement","B4-3 — 4 Attempted colonisation of Virginia: Reasons for the attempted colonisation"]},{"q":"'Education in Elizabethan England changed very little in the years 1558-88.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • grammar schools • the universities. You must also use information of your own. (16 marks)","a":"AO2 10 + AO1 6. Credit the founding and refounding of grammar schools, rising literacy and the gentry's arrival at Oxford, Cambridge and the Inns of Court, weighed against the unchanged hierarchy of rank, the exclusion of girls and the poor, the unchanged Latin curriculum and the survival of apprenticeship under the Statute of Artificers of 1563; a judgement on a stated criterion of reach. Caps: maximum 11 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Whether education 'changed very little' depends entirely on whom you count. My criterion is reach: a change counts as large only if it altered the education of new kinds of people. Judged by the experience of most English children the statement is close to true; judged by the sons of the middling sort it is plainly false. The case against the statement begins with the grammar schools. Schools were founded and refounded in market towns throughout the reign, often endowed by merchants, guilds or local gentry, and took the sons of yeomen and tradesmen as well as of gentlemen. A boy entered at about seven, having learned his letters at a petty or dame school, and worked six days a week from six or seven in the morning until five at Latin grammar, rhetoric and the classical authors, with Greek in the upper forms. That was a genuinely new opportunity for families who a century earlier would have expected a son to follow them on to the land or into the workshop. Historians' estimates of who could sign a name suggest that literacy among men rose to something like a third by 1600, well above the level of a century before. The universities moved the same way. Oxford and Cambridge, entered at about fourteen or fifteen, had existed to train clergy; under Elizabeth they filled increasingly with the sons of gentry who took the liberal arts as a preparation for managing estates, sitting in Parliament and serving the Crown, while the Inns of Court in London did the same for men who would never plead a case. The Reformation reinforced this, because a Protestant church needed preachers who could argue from an English Bible. But the statement has a strong defence. Education still followed rank exactly as it always had. A nobleman's son had a tutor at home; a labourer's son had work. The great majority of children - those of husbandmen, cottagers and the poor - received no formal schooling at all, and the growth of poverty in these very years made schooling less likely for the bottom of society rather than more. Girls were almost wholly excluded from grammar schools and from the universities: a gentlewoman's daughter learned modern languages, music and household management from a tutor, and every other girl learned housewifery at home. The curriculum itself hardly moved either - Latin, rhetoric, the classics and religion, taught by memory and enforced with the rod - and there were still only two universities in the whole kingdom. Craftsmen's sons were educated as they had always been, by apprenticeship, fixed at seven years by the Statute of Artificers of 1563. The point beyond the bullets is that the change was one of quantity rather than of kind. More boys of the middling sort passed through a system whose shape, purpose and exclusions were the same as before, and nobody proposed to educate the poor or the female half of the population. On my criterion, then, I partly agree. If the test is whether new kinds of people were being educated, education changed markedly for one rank and not at all for the rest; if the test is whether the idea of what education was for had changed, then very little changed indeed.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask who is included in the word 'education' before deciding whether it changed.","Build the essay around ranks: who gained something new, and who was in exactly the same position in 1588 as in 1558.","Name your criterion for a large change in the opening lines, and answer against that criterion at the end."],"codes":["B4-3 — 1 Education and leisure: Education in the home and schools"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In which year was the Act for Setting the Poor on Work passed, and what did it require towns to provide?","a":"1576; raw materials such as wool, hemp and flax so that the able-bodied poor could be set to work."},{"q":"Who built the Theatre at Shoreditch in 1576?","a":"James Burbage."},{"q":"On what date did the Golden Hind return to Plymouth at the end of Drake's circumnavigation?","a":"26 September 1580."},{"q":"Who was left as governor of the Roanoke settlement in 1585?","a":"Ralph Lane, landed there by Sir Richard Grenville."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The Elizabethan grammar school","open":false,"code":"B4-3 — 1 Education and leisure: Education in the home and schools","back":"Founded and refounded in market towns. Boys entered at about seven, worked six days a week from six or seven until five, and studied Latin grammar, rhetoric and classical authors, with Greek in the upper forms."},{"front":"Oxford, Cambridge and the Inns of Court","open":false,"code":"B4-3 — 1 Education and leisure: Education in the home and schools","back":"England's only two universities, entered at about fourteen or fifteen for the liberal arts before degrees in divinity, law or medicine. The Inns of Court in London trained lawyers and finished gentlemen's education."},{"front":"The Theatre, 1576","open":false,"code":"B4-3 — 1 Education and leisure: Sport, pastimes and the theatre","back":"The first successful purpose-built London playhouse, put up by James Burbage at Shoreditch, outside the City's jurisdiction. The Curtain followed nearby in 1577."},{"front":"The Rose, 1587","open":false,"code":"B4-3 — 1 Education and leisure: Sport, pastimes and the theatre","back":"Playhouse opened by Philip Henslowe on Bankside, south of the Thames and beyond the City's control. A penny bought standing room in the yard; gallery seats cost more."},{"front":"Elizabethan sports and pastimes","open":false,"code":"B4-3 — 1 Education and leisure: Sport, pastimes and the theatre","back":"Gentlemen hunted, hawked, fished and played real tennis, bowls and chess. Ordinary people played village football, wrestled, gambled at dice and cards, and watched bear-baiting, bull-baiting and cock-fighting."},{"front":"Why poverty increased under Elizabeth","open":false,"code":"B4-3 — 2 The 'problem' of the poor: The reasons for the increase in poverty and vagabondage","back":"Population rose from roughly three million to about four million; food prices outran wages after the debasement; rents rose and enclosure cut jobs; harvests failed, as in 1586; the Antwerp cloth market was disrupted."},{"front":"Vagabonds","open":false,"code":"B4-3 — 2 The 'problem' of the poor: The reasons for the increase in poverty and vagabondage","back":"People without settled work who wandered in search of it or begged. The law treated anyone outside his own parish without work as a criminal, and feared vagrants as thieves, plague-carriers and possible rebels."},{"front":"The 1563 Act for the Relief of the Poor","open":false,"code":"B4-3 — 2 The 'problem' of the poor: The changing attitudes and policies towards the poor","back":"Made contributions to parish poor relief effectively compulsory: those who refused to give could be brought before Justices of the Peace and in the end imprisoned."},{"front":"The 1572 Vagabonds Act","open":false,"code":"B4-3 — 2 The 'problem' of the poor: The changing attitudes and policies towards the poor","back":"Vagrants over fourteen to be whipped and bored through the right ear for a first offence, with death for a third. It also created a compulsory poor rate and registers of the poor."},{"front":"The 1576 Act for Setting the Poor on Work","open":false,"code":"B4-3 — 2 The 'problem' of the poor: The changing attitudes and policies towards the poor","back":"Towns had to buy wool, hemp and flax so the able-bodied poor could be set to work for wages. Those who then refused were sent to a house of correction."},{"front":"The galleon and the navigator's instruments","open":false,"code":"B4-3 — 3 Exploration and voyages of discovery: Factors prompting exploration","back":"The galleon was narrower, lower and better armed, so faster and handier. Latitude came from the astrolabe or quadrant, course from the compass, speed from log line and sand-glass; Mercator's 1569 map aided plotting."},{"front":"The Muscovy Company","open":false,"code":"B4-3 — 3 Exploration and voyages of discovery: Factors prompting exploration","back":"Chartered in 1555 after Richard Chancellor reached Russia. It held a monopoly of the Russian trade and was a joint-stock company, so merchants shared both the cost and the risk of each voyage."},{"front":"Drake's circumnavigation, 1577-80","open":false,"code":"B4-3 — 3 Exploration and voyages of discovery: Drake's circumnavigation of the globe","back":"Left Plymouth in December 1577, passed the Strait of Magellan, took the Cacafuego in March 1579, claimed Nova Albion and reached Plymouth on 26 September 1580. Knighted at Deptford on 4 April 1581."},{"front":"Raleigh and the Virginia patent of 1584","open":false,"code":"B4-3 — 4 Attempted colonisation of Virginia: Reasons for the attempted colonisation","back":"Walter Raleigh, knighted only in January 1585, obtained a royal patent in 1584 to settle lands held by no Christian prince, sent Amadas and Barlowe to reconnoitre, and named the land Virginia. He financed it but never sailed there."},{"front":"The first settlement at Roanoke, 1585-86","open":false,"code":"B4-3 — 4 Attempted colonisation of Virginia: Reasons for the failure of the first settlement","back":"Grenville landed about a hundred men under Ralph Lane. Late planting, lost stores, soldiers rather than farmers, the killing of Wingina and late relief emptied it; the colonists sailed home with Drake in June 1586."}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (the 16-mark judgement, 'The theatre was the greatest change in the leisure of Elizabethans in the years 1558-88.')","items":["Question: 'The theatre was the greatest change in the leisure of Elizabethans in the years 1558-88.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. You may use the following in your answer: • the Theatre (1576) • bear-baiting. You must also use information of your own. (16 marks). This is one paragraph from the middle of the essay, putting the case for the statement.","The playhouse was new in a way that nothing else in Elizabethan leisure was. Before 1576 plays were given by travelling companies in inn-yards, guildhalls and the halls of great houses, so a performance depended on an innkeeper's yard or a lord's hospitality; when James Burbage built the Theatre at Shoreditch in 1576, followed by the Curtain in 1577 and Philip Henslowe's Rose on Bankside in 1587, there was for the first time a permanent building whose only purpose was playing. That changed the nature of the leisure and not merely its setting. A fixed house meant regular performances, a resident company and money to commission new work, so writers such as Christopher Marlowe, whose Tamburlaine reached the London stage in about 1587, could live by the stage. It also made the audience new: a penny bought standing room in the yard and a few pence a gallery seat, so an apprentice and a gentleman watched the same play on the same afternoon, which no hunt, hawking party or private banquet ever allowed. Measured by whether the leisure itself was different in kind, rather than merely more convenient, the playhouse is the strongest candidate the period offers, because bear-baiting, football, wrestling and dicing in 1588 were played and watched much as they had been in 1558.","Why this is Level 4: the explanation is analytical and directed consistently at the conceptual focus, which is the extent of change in leisure, not a description of the playhouses. The knowledge is precisely selected - Burbage and the Theatre in 1576, the Curtain in 1577, Henslowe's Rose in 1587, Marlowe on the stage by about 1587, the penny in the yard - and each fact is used to drive the argument rather than to decorate it. A criterion is stated ('different in kind, rather than merely more convenient') and applied, and the paragraph closes on an interim judgement that compares the theatre with the pastimes it is being ranked against, which is what the third bullet of the level descriptor rewards."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: as above. The same point attempted by a weaker candidate.","The theatre was a big change in Elizabethan leisure. The Theatre was built in 1576 and other theatres were built after it like the Rose. Lots of people went to watch plays and it only cost a penny so poor people could go as well as rich people. People also liked bear-baiting where dogs attacked a bear, and football, and these were popular too. This shows that the theatre was the greatest change because it was new and everybody enjoyed it.","What it does and does not do: it reaches Level 2 because an explanation is offered and accurate, relevant information is included, showing some knowledge of the period, but the links to the conceptual focus are implicit and unsustained - 'it was new and everybody enjoyed it' is assertion, not analysis, and no criterion for a 'greatest' change is stated anywhere. It misses Level 3 and Level 4 because the reasoning is not sustained, the detail is imprecise (no builder, no place, no date for the Rose, nothing on what plays or audiences were like before 1576), and bear-baiting is mentioned rather than used as a comparison, so the overall judgement in the last sentence is asserted rather than justified. No stimulus cap is holding this answer down, because there is no cap at Level 2 at all; the caps bite higher up, where the grid prints 'Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'. What keeps this answer at Level 2 is the quality of the reasoning, not the range of its content."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: education in the home, the schools and the universities","items":["Education followed rank: tutors at home for the nobility and greater gentry, grammar schools for the sons of gentry, merchants, yeomen and prospering townsmen, apprenticeship for craftsmen's sons, work for the rest.","Petty schools and dame schools, often run in a private house, taught reading, writing and simple arithmetic in English before a boy went on to a grammar school at about seven.","Grammar schools were founded and refounded in market towns throughout the reign, many endowed by wealthy merchants, guilds or local gentry; fees were charged, but many kept free places for poor scholars.","The grammar school day ran from about six or seven in the morning until five, six days a week, and the curriculum was Latin grammar, rhetoric and the classical authors, with Greek in the upper forms.","Discipline was by the rod, and learning was largely by memory, repetition and construing set passages.","Girls were almost entirely excluded from grammar schools and the universities; a nobleman's daughter learned modern languages, music, dancing, needlework and the running of a household from a tutor at home.","The Statute of Artificers of 1563 fixed apprenticeships at seven years for entry to a trade and gave Justices of the Peace the power to set wage rates.","There were only two universities in England, Oxford and Cambridge, entered at about fourteen or fifteen; students took the seven liberal arts before degrees in divinity, law or medicine.","The Inns of Court in London trained lawyers and increasingly finished the education of gentlemen's sons who never intended to practise law.","Growth came from Protestantism, which wanted a laity that could read an English Bible and a preaching clergy; from cheap printed books; from the needs of trade, law and royal service; and from humanist ideas of the educated gentleman.","Historians' estimates, based on who could sign a name, suggest that by 1600 roughly a third of men and about a tenth of women in England were literate.","What did not change: the poor, the great majority of children, got no formal schooling at all, and the curriculum, the exclusions and the two universities were the same in 1588 as in 1558."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: sport, pastimes and the theatre","items":["Gentlemen hunted deer with hounds, flew hawks, fished, played real tennis, bowls and chess, fenced, danced and played the lute or virginals.","Ordinary people played village football, a violent contest with almost no rules that authorities repeatedly tried to ban, and wrestled at fairs and feasts.","Bear-baiting and bull-baiting chained the animal to a post and set dogs on it before a paying crowd; cock-fighting and gambling at dice and cards were equally common. Elizabeth herself enjoyed baiting.","Before purpose-built playhouses, plays were given by travelling companies in inn-yards, guildhalls and the halls of great houses.","James Burbage built the Theatre at Shoreditch in 1576, the first successful purpose-built playhouse in London.","The Curtain opened nearby in 1577, and Philip Henslowe opened the Rose on Bankside in 1587.","Playhouses were built outside the City of London's jurisdiction, at Shoreditch to the north and on Bankside across the Thames, because the City authorities were hostile.","A penny bought standing room in the yard and a few pence a seat in the galleries, so gentlemen and apprentices watched the same play at the same performance.","The Vagabonds Act of 1572 counted players without a noble patron as rogues and vagabonds, so acting companies had to take a patron's name and a licence.","The Earl of Leicester's Men were licensed in 1574, and the Queen's Men were formed in 1583 from leading players of other companies.","Puritan preachers and the City authorities attacked plays as nurseries of idleness, crime, disorder and plague, and wanted the playhouses closed.","Christopher Marlowe's Tamburlaine reached the London stage in about 1587, showing that permanent playhouses had created a market for new plays."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: why poverty and vagabondage increased, 1558-88","items":["Population: England held roughly three million people at Elizabeth's accession and about four million by 1600, so more people competed for the same land and work.","Prices: food prices rose faster than wages, partly because of the debasement of the coinage under Henry VIII and Edward VI; Elizabeth's recoinage of 1560-61 steadied the currency but not the cost of living.","Harvests: failures such as that of 1586 sent grain prices sharply higher and pushed families with no reserves on to the roads.","Rents: landlords raised rents, a practice called rack-renting, and tenants without secure tenure could be turned out.","Enclosure: where arable was enclosed and turned over to sheep, one shepherd replaced several ploughmen, so families lost holding and wage together.","Charity: the dissolution of the monasteries in the 1530s had removed the institutions that had given relief at the gate.","Trade: woollen cloth was England's great export, and the disruption of the Antwerp market in the 1560s threw weavers, spinners and carders out of work.","War: demobilised soldiers and sailors, especially after England intervened in the Netherlands in 1585, added masterless men with no parish to claim them.","Towns grew as the displaced moved in search of work; London may have grown from some seventy thousand people around 1550 to about two hundred thousand by 1600.","A vagabond or vagrant was anyone outside his own parish without work; because relief was a parish matter, moving to find work made a man a criminal.","Thomas Harman's pamphlet of the 1560s catalogued the supposed tricks and disguises of vagrants and fed the belief that they were an organised criminal underworld.","Vagrants were feared as thieves, as carriers of plague and above all as the raw material of a rebellion that would have no gentleman at its head."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: attitudes and the Poor Laws, 1563-1601","items":["Elizabethans divided the poor into the impotent or deserving poor - the old, the sick, the disabled, orphans and widows - and the able-bodied 'sturdy beggars' or idle poor.","The deserving poor were to be relieved; the idle poor were to be punished, and idleness was regarded as a sin as well as a crime.","The 1563 Act for the Relief of the Poor made contributions to parish relief effectively compulsory: refusers could be brought before Justices of the Peace and ultimately imprisoned.","The 1572 Vagabonds Act ordered that a vagrant over fourteen be whipped and bored through the right ear for a first offence; a third offence could be punished as a felony, by death.","The same 1572 Act set up a compulsory poor rate collected locally and required towns to keep registers of their poor, so punishment and provision arrived together.","The 1576 Act for Setting the Poor on Work required towns to buy raw materials - wool, hemp and flax - so the able-bodied poor could be set to work for wages.","Under the 1576 Act, those who refused the work offered were sent to a house of correction, so punishment was now reserved for the genuinely unwilling.","Towns had acted first: Norwich surveyed its poor in 1570, sorted them by need and set the able-bodied to work.","The town surveys provided the evidence that most of the poor were children, the old, the sick and widows, and that many able-bodied poor had no work to go to.","Motives for change: fear of disorder and rebellion, the plain failure of whipping alone, Protestant and humanist ideas of Christian duty, and the loss of monastic charity.","Later context only: the Elizabethan Poor Law of 1601 gathered these measures together, appointing overseers of the poor in every parish and levying a compulsory poor rate, and it remained the basis of English poor relief for over two centuries.","Continuity to note in a judgement: the whipping clause of 1572 was never repealed, and the distinction between the deserving and the idle poor survived into 1601 and beyond."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: ships, navigation and the drive to trade","items":["The galleon was narrower and lower in the water than the older carrack, with lighter castles fore and aft, so it was faster, steadier and better able to carry guns and cargo.","Square sails for running before the wind were combined with triangular lateen sails that let a ship sail closer to the wind, making long ocean passages practicable.","The mariner's astrolabe and the quadrant measured the height of the sun at noon or of the Pole Star, from which a navigator read his latitude.","The magnetic compass held a course; a log line paid out over the stern with a sand-glass measured speed; a traverse board recorded courses and distances between sights.","Gerardus Mercator's world map of 1569 projected the globe so that a constant compass bearing could be drawn as a straight line, a great help in plotting an ocean course.","Printed sailing directions and charts passed one crew's hard-won knowledge on to the next, so each voyage began better informed than the last.","Trade motive: the Antwerp market for English cloth was disrupted in the 1560s, forcing merchants to look for new markets and new routes.","The Muscovy Company was chartered in 1555, after Richard Chancellor reached Russia, and held a monopoly of the Russian trade; a company for the Ottoman lands was chartered in 1581.","Joint-stock companies let merchants, courtiers and the queen buy shares, spreading the cost and the risk of a voyage that might take years to pay.","Spain and Portugal claimed a monopoly of the routes to the Americas and the East, which is why the English searched for north-west and north-east passages instead.","Privateering - robbing Spanish shipping under a licence - promised faster returns than trade and attracted courtly investors; John Hawkins's voyages to the Caribbean in the 1560s were an early example.","Richard Hakluyt urged Elizabeth in 1584 to plant colonies in America, arguing for trade, bases against Spain, the spread of Protestantism and work for the unemployed poor."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file (case study): Drake's circumnavigation of the globe, 1577-80","items":["Drake sailed from Plymouth in December 1577 with five ships, officially on a trading voyage, in fact backed by courtiers and by the queen to raid Spanish possessions in the Pacific.","At Port St Julian in 1578 he had Thomas Doughty executed for alleged mutiny, asserting a single command over gentlemen and sailors alike.","He renamed his flagship, the Pelican, the Golden Hind, after the crest of his patron Sir Christopher Hatton.","He passed through the Strait of Magellan in 1578 and entered a Pacific where Spain kept its ports and shipping almost undefended.","In March 1579 he captured the Spanish treasure ship Nuestra Señora de la Concepción, nicknamed the Cacafuego, whose silver, gold and jewels made the voyage profitable.","He landed on the coast of California and claimed it for Elizabeth as Nova Albion, a claim that produced no settlement.","He crossed the Pacific, traded for cloves in the Moluccas, rounded the Cape of Good Hope and reached Plymouth on 26 September 1580.","He was the first Englishman to sail round the world; his was the second expedition to complete a circumnavigation, after Magellan's.","The investors, the queen among them, were repaid many times what they had put in, and the Crown's share mattered to a government that could not tax without Parliament.","Philip II demanded Drake's punishment; Elizabeth answered by knighting him aboard the Golden Hind at Deptford on 4 April 1581.","Significance: it proved English seamanship, showed that Spain's empire was a long thin line of ports that could not be defended everywhere, and brought back charts and Pacific experience.","It also encouraged investment in later voyages, including Raleigh's Virginia expeditions, and deepened the quarrel with Spain that ended in the war from 1585 and the Armada of 1588."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file (case study): the attempted colonisation of Virginia and the significance of Raleigh","items":["Sir Humphrey Gilbert, Raleigh's half-brother, claimed Newfoundland for England in 1583 and drowned on the voyage home, leaving the project to Raleigh.","Walter Raleigh, a favourite of the queen and not knighted until January 1585, obtained a royal patent in 1584 to settle lands not held by any Christian prince.","He sent Philip Amadas and Arthur Barlowe to reconnoitre the American coast in 1584; their favourable report was used to raise money and recruits.","The land was named Virginia in honour of Elizabeth, the unmarried queen.","Raleigh financed, organised and publicised the expeditions but never sailed to America himself, because Elizabeth would not let her favourite leave court.","Elizabeth gave her blessing and a ship but not the Exchequer's money, so the ventures depended on private investors and on the hope of privateering profits.","Motives: land and raw materials such as timber, furs and dyes; a market for English cloth after the Antwerp trade was disrupted; the hope of gold and silver.","Strategic motive: a base on the American coast from which privateers could refit and strike the Spanish treasure fleets sailing home through the Caribbean.","Religious and national motives: planting a Protestant claim in a hemisphere Spain treated as its own, and the prestige of an English empire.","Social motive: Richard Hakluyt argued in 1584 that colonies would give work to England's growing numbers of unemployed poor.","Raleigh's significance: without a courtier of his wealth, influence and persistence, the arguments for colonisation would have remained on paper.","His limits: he never saw Virginia, he could not compel the Crown to fund it, and the war with Spain from 1585 took the ships and men his colonies needed."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file (case study): the first settlement in Virginia, 1585-86, and the colony of 1587","items":["In 1585 Sir Richard Grenville carried about a hundred men to Roanoke Island, off the coast of what is now North Carolina, and left Ralph Lane as governor.","The expedition arrived in the summer, too late in the season to clear ground, sow and harvest a crop, so the first year had to be lived on stores.","Much of the food was ruined when the flagship grounded in the shallow inlet on arrival, so the stores were short from the beginning.","The settlers were largely soldiers and gentlemen looking for gold and glory, with too few farmers, fishermen and carpenters to feed and house themselves.","Roanoke lies behind the Outer Banks, where the water is shallow and the channels shift, so large supply ships could not come in safely.","The colonists depended on the Algonquian people for food; Lane demanded supplies, burned a village and in 1586 had the chief Wingina killed, turning neighbours into enemies.","Grenville's promised relief did not arrive on time, partly because the war with Spain from 1585 claimed ships, men and money.","When Drake called at Roanoke in June 1586 on his way home from raiding the Caribbean, the hungry colonists took passage with him and abandoned the settlement.","Grenville arrived soon afterwards, found the place deserted and left a small holding party who were never seen again.","A second colony of about 115 men, women and children under Governor John White landed at Roanoke in 1587; White's granddaughter Virginia Dare was the first English child born in America.","White sailed home for supplies and the war with Spain, including the Armada of 1588, kept him in England until 1590.","When White returned in 1590 the settlement was deserted, with the word CROATOAN cut into a post; the fate of the colonists has never been established, which is why it is called the Lost Colony."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — the 16-mark judgement: 'The search for new markets was the main reason for English voyages of exploration in the years 1558-88.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16 marks)","items":["The stem as it is printed, as Question 3 or Question 4 of booklet B: the statement in quotation marks, then 'How far do you agree? Explain your answer. (16)', then 'You may use the following in your answer: • the Muscovy Company • privateering. You must also use information of your own.'","Decoded: you are being asked to rank the motives behind the voyages, not to describe the voyages. The second order concept is causation, with a judgement attached, so every paragraph must end on why men put money and lives into sailing.","Criterion to state in the opening: a motive counts as the main reason if the voyages would not have been made without it. Signal the line of argument immediately - markets explain the merchants' companies, but they do not explain the raiders.","Paragraph 1 (the statement at its strongest): new markets. English exports rested on the cloth trade through Antwerp, and that market was disrupted in the 1560s, so merchants had to look elsewhere. The Muscovy Company, chartered in 1555 after Richard Chancellor reached Russia, traded through the northern ports; a company for the Ottoman lands was chartered in 1581; and the joint-stock form spread the risk so that merchants, courtiers and the queen could all buy in. Interim judgement: the hunt for markets explains the companies, and it explains the search for a passage - Martin Frobisher made three attempts on a north-west passage in 1576, 1577 and 1578.","Paragraph 2 (the statement tested): plunder. Spanish America paid better and faster than any market. Drake sailed in December 1577 backed by the queen and by courtiers, took the treasure ship nicknamed the Cacafuego in March 1579 and reached Plymouth on 26 September 1580, repaying his investors many times over. Interim judgement: the promise of markets cannot explain a voyage whose profit came from robbery.","Paragraph 3 (beyond the stimulus, first point): the means. The galleon was faster, handier and better armed; latitude came from the mariner's astrolabe or the quadrant, course from the compass, speed from the log line and the sand-glass; and Mercator's world map of 1569 let a constant bearing be drawn as a straight line. Motives are old, but the voyages waited on the ships and the instruments that made them survivable.","Paragraph 4 (beyond the stimulus, second point): the Crown, rivalry and religion. Elizabeth could back a voyage privately without declaring war, so exploration was a cheap way of hurting Philip II; Richard Hakluyt urged her in 1584 to plant colonies in America; and a Protestant claim in a hemisphere Spain treated as its own carried prestige as well as profit.","Paragraph 5 (the point that joins the two halves): the same men financed both kinds of voyage, so trade and plunder were not rivals in practice - a company's ship could carry cloth out and still hope to meet a Spaniard on the way home, and one good prize paid for the next season's trading.","The evaluation thread to run through every paragraph: does this motive explain the merchants' voyages, the raiders' voyages, or both? Keep asking that and the essay ranks its motives instead of listing them.","Conclusion: agree in part, then decide. The failure of the old market drove the merchants, and without it there would have been no Muscovy Company and no hunt for a passage; but the voyages the country remembered were paid for by the hope of Spanish silver, so the search for markets is the main reason for the trading voyages rather than for all of them. Name the criterion again in the final sentence, or the judgement is asserted rather than justified.","Timing: about 27 minutes - four or five minutes deciding the criterion and the order of the paragraphs, twenty writing, two checking that the words 'main reason' have actually been answered. There are no SPaG marks on this paper, so spend the time on the argument.","Cap to avoid: the grid prints 'Maximum 11 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content', so the ships and instruments and the Crown's own interest are not decoration - they are what takes the essay past eleven marks."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:15:35Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"The fix pass holds: worked examples 0, 2 and 4 and the essay plan now work foci that appear nowhere in the question bank (apprenticeship and the universities; whether the Virginia ventures brought England nothing; why the playhouses stood outside the City; whether new markets were the main reason for the voyages), every cue is answerable from its setup, idea[0] no longer contradicts itself and Raleigh's January 1585 knighthood is right in all three places - and the new material checks out (Statute of Artificers 1563, the seven liberal arts, Harriot's report of 1588 and White's drawings, Frobisher's voyages of 1576, 1577 and 1578, the Muscovy Company of 1555, Mercator 1569), so the only work left is cosmetic balance."}}},"P1-1":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"P1-1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 1 is where booklet P's three question types are easiest to score and easiest to waste. The consequences parts want one thing that was true afterwards because of an event, analysed and supported - and the years 1492 to 1512 are full of them, because almost everything in them was caused by something in the previous paragraph. The narrative account wants a short run of years in which one thing visibly made the next possible, and the first voyage, the settling of Hispaniola and Ovando's decade each give you exactly that. The importance parts want the difference a person, treaty or institution made to what came later, and Isabella, Tordesillas, the encomienda, the Casa de Contratacion and the friars all reach forward into the rest of the option. Learn this key topic as a chain of causes with dates attached and you can answer any of the three.","idea":[{"h":"A kingdom that has just finished a crusade: why Spain looked outwards in 1492","t":"Ferdinand of Aragon and Isabella of Castile married in 1469; she became queen of Castile in 1474 and he king of Aragon in 1479, joining the two strongest Christian kingdoms of the peninsula, though each kept its own laws and it was Castile that paid for the Atlantic enterprise. The Reconquista, the long Christian reconquest of Muslim Spain, ended when Boabdil surrendered Granada on 2 January 1492. Religion was policy, not sentiment: the Inquisition had been set up in 1478 to test the sincerity of converts, and the Alhambra Decree of 31 March 1492 ordered Jews to convert or leave. Victory also left thousands of hidalgos and soldiers with no war to fight and no land to win. Abroad, Spain was boxed in. Portugal held the African route to the gold and spices of the East under the Treaty of Alcacovas of 1479, Bartolomeu Dias rounded the Cape of Good Hope in 1488, and France was Spain's rival in Italy. A top-level answer uses Spain in 1490 as a bundle of motives that reinforce one another - crusading faith, royal prestige, unemployed swords and a blocked trade route - rather than as a list of separate causes.","code":"P1-1 — 1 Spanish exploration: Spain c1490"},{"h":"Six years of refusals: how Columbus got a fleet, and what Isabella supplied","t":"Christopher Columbus, a Genoese sailor, argued that Asia could be reached by sailing west. His case rested on a mistake: he shrank the earth and stretched Asia, putting Japan about 2,400 miles west of the Canaries, a small fraction of the true distance. Trained men knew it. King John II of Portugal's committee of experts rejected him in the mid-1480s because his figures were wrong, and the commission the Catholic Monarchs appointed under Hernando de Talavera reported against him as well. He survived on patronage: the Franciscans of the La Rabida monastery, the Duke of Medinaceli, and above all Luis de Santangel, keeper of the privy purse, who found the money and pressed Isabella after she had refused. Within four months of Granada's fall the monarchs agreed, and the Capitulations of Santa Fe of 17 April 1492 made Columbus Admiral of the Ocean Sea, viceroy and governor of whatever he found, with a tenth of the wealth taken from it, all hereditary. A top-level answer explains the timing rather than the biography: Isabella's crusading confidence after Granada, the fear that Portugal would reach the East first, and terms that cost the crown almost nothing if he drowned.","code":"P1-1 — 1 Spanish exploration: Columbus's attempts to gain sponsorship"},{"h":"Three ships, five weeks of open sea and a second set of figures","t":"The fleet left Palos de la Frontera on 3 August 1492: the Santa Maria, a broad nao that served as flagship, and two caravels, the Pinta under Martin Alonso Pinzon and the Nina under his brother Vicente Yanez Pinzon. They refitted in the Canary Islands and sailed west on 6 September. The problems were of three kinds and each shaped a decision. Navigational: nobody had sailed this route, the variation of the compass needle alarmed the pilots, and the ships met weed in the Sargasso Sea and long calms. Human: as the weeks passed the crews wanted to turn back, so Columbus kept a second, shorter reckoning of the distance run to show them. Structural: the easterly trade winds that carried them out would not carry them home, which is why the return had to be made on a different latitude and why turning back felt so dangerous. A lookout on the Pinta, Rodrigo de Triana, sighted land on 12 October 1492, and Columbus named the island San Salvador. A top-level answer treats the problems as things he had to manage, not as hardships to be listed: each one explains why he did what he did next.","code":"P1-1 — 1 Spanish exploration: Columbus's first voyage of 1492"},{"h":"Looking for Asia and finding islands: the Bahamas, Cuba, Hispaniola and La Navidad","t":"From San Salvador Columbus worked through the Bahamas, reached the north coast of Cuba on 28 October 1492 and decided it must be part of the Asian mainland, then crossed to Hispaniola on 5 December. Everything he did was shaped by what he believed he had found: he looked for the cities of the Great Khan, took Taino captives to serve as interpreters and as proof, and read every gold ornament as evidence of mines nearby. Martin Alonso Pinzon sailed off in the Pinta without permission in November to hunt for gold on his own account. On Christmas Day 1492 the Santa Maria ran aground off the north coast of Hispaniola and could not be refloated, and the Nina alone could not carry the whole company home. Columbus turned the wreck into a claim: her timbers became a fort, about thirty-nine men were left in it, and it was named La Navidad after the day of the loss - the first Spanish settlement in the Americas. A top-level answer makes that point rather than retelling the route: an accident became an occupation, and the occupation became the argument for the colonising fleet of 1493.","code":"P1-1 — 2 Columbus reaches the Americas: Columbus's actions"},{"h":"Gold, hawk's bells and two kinds of Indian","t":"The Taino, an Arawak-speaking people, lived across the Bahamas, Cuba, Jamaica, Puerto Rico and Hispaniola. They farmed cassava and maize, lived in villages under chiefs called caciques, and wore small gold ornaments got from river gravels. The first exchanges were peaceful and the Spanish traded trinkets for gold. The Caribs of the Lesser Antilles were described, first by the Taino and then by the Spanish, as raiders and eaters of human flesh; the word cannibal comes from their name. The label mattered because the crown allowed the enslavement of peoples who ate human flesh and resisted, so calling a people Carib decided their legal fate. Gold changed the relationship fastest. When the placer deposits of the Cibao proved thin, Columbus in 1495 required every Taino on Hispaniola aged fourteen or over to bring in a hawk's bell filled with gold dust every three months, or cotton where there was no gold, and punished those who failed. A top-level answer shows contact turning from exchange into extraction inside three years, and treats the Carib label as a legal device rather than as a description of a people.","code":"P1-1 — 2 Columbus reaches the Americas: The impact of contact with Indigenous peoples"},{"h":"Drawing a line on an unknown ocean: from Alcacovas to Tordesillas","t":"Columbus came home in March 1493 by way of Lisbon, so King John II of Portugal heard of the discovery first and at once claimed the new lands under the Treaty of Alcacovas of 1479, which had given Portugal the Atlantic south of the Canaries. Spain went to Rome instead of to war. Pope Alexander VI, a Valencian, issued bulls in 1493, the most important of which granted Castile the lands lying west of a line a hundred leagues beyond the Azores and the Cape Verde Islands. Portugal would not accept a line drawn by a Spanish pope, so the two crowns settled it themselves in the Treaty of Tordesillas, signed on 7 June 1494, which moved the line to 370 leagues west of the Cape Verde Islands: everything west of it was Castile's, everything east Portugal's. Neither crown could measure longitude, so nobody knew where the line truly ran, and when Pedro Alvares Cabral made his landfall on the South American coast in 1500 it fell on the Portuguese side. A top-level answer explains Tordesillas as a device for keeping two Catholic powers from fighting, and notices that no other country was asked and none felt bound by it.","code":"P1-1 — 2 Columbus reaches the Americas: The impact of rivalry with Portugal"},{"h":"Admiral, viceroy and failure: the later voyages and the move to Santo Domingo","t":"The second voyage left Cadiz on 25 September 1493 with seventeen ships and well over a thousand men: colonists, not explorers. Columbus found La Navidad burned and its garrison dead, and founded La Isabela in January 1494 on a north-coast site chosen for its nearness to the Cibao gold rather than for water or shelter; it killed settlers and was abandoned. In 1496 his brother Bartholomew founded Santo Domingo on the south coast, near the mouth of the Ozama, with a harbour on the side of the island facing Spain. It became the capital of the Indies. Columbus explored well and governed badly. The third voyage of 1498 reached Trinidad on 31 July and the mouth of the Orinoco, where the volume of fresh water convinced him he had found a continent; but on Hispaniola Francisco Roldan led a settler revolt from 1497 that Columbus could end only by granting the rebels land and Taino labour. Francisco de Bobadilla arrived in 1500 as royal commissioner, arrested the three brothers and shipped them to Spain in irons. Columbus made a fourth voyage in 1502 to 1504 along the coast of Central America and died in 1506. A top-level answer separates the sailor from the governor: his titles survived only as a lawsuit, while the colony he could not run became a royal province.","code":"P1-1 — 3 Spanish claims in the Caribbean: Columbus's later voyages and his role as governor"},{"h":"Tribute, encomienda and disease: what settlement did to the Taino","t":"Spanish settlement ran on Taino labour. Tribute came first, from 1495; then the repartimiento, the parcelling out of villages to settlers, conceded to end Roldan's revolt; then, under Nicolas de Ovando, governor from 1502 to 1509, the encomienda, a grant of a named group of Indigenous people to a named Spaniard who could command their labour in the mines and fields in return for protecting them and having them instructed in Christianity. Resistance was crushed with horses, steel, crossbows and dogs: a Spanish force broke a large Taino army at the Vega Real in 1495, and Ovando destroyed the chiefdom of Xaragua in 1503 and executed its cacica Anacaona. The heaviest killer, though, was not the sword. The Taino had no immunity to Old World infections, epidemics began within a few years of contact, and the first recorded smallpox epidemic in the Americas broke out on Hispaniola in December 1518 and killed a large part of those still alive. A Spanish enumeration of 1514 had already produced a count of about 26,000 Indigenous people on the island, though the figure is disputed. Estimates of the population before 1492 vary enormously, so a top-level answer argues about causes and proportions - overwork, hunger, broken families and disease together - and never rests its case on one disputed figure.","code":"P1-1 — 3 Spanish claims in the Caribbean: The effects of Spanish settlement"},{"h":"From one man's contract to a royal system: licences, a monopoly and the friars","t":"The Capitulations had given one Genoese sailor a hereditary province, and the crown spent twenty years taking it back. From 1495 it licensed other captains to sail west; in 1500 it sent Bobadilla to replace Columbus as governor; in 1503 it created the Casa de Contratacion at Seville to license every ship, crew and cargo bound for the Indies and to collect the royal fifth of precious metals, so that all American trade passed through one Castilian port, and in 1508 it gave the House a piloto mayor, Amerigo Vespucci, to examine the pilots and keep the master chart, the Padron Real. Indigenous people were declared free vassals of the crown, which is why enslavement had to be justified by the Carib label or by rebellion, and why the encomienda was dressed as a duty to protect and convert; from 1501 the crown allowed African slaves to be taken to Hispaniola, and in 1510 licensed a consignment for the gold mines. Conversion was the legal ground of the whole enterprise, so missionaries came with it: friars sailed on the second voyage in 1493, Franciscans arrived in numbers with Ovando in 1502 and the Dominicans in 1510. They became its fiercest critics, and the Laws of Burgos of 27 December 1512 were the crown's answer to them. A top-level answer shows imperial policy being improvised under pressure - from settlers, from friars and from the need for revenue - rather than designed.","code":"P1-1 — 3 Spanish claims in the Caribbean: Development of an imperial policy"}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1(a): turning the founding of La Isabela into one explained consequence","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(a): 'Explain one consequence of the founding of La Isabela in January 1494.' [4 marks]. The part is marked out of 4 on a two-level grid, and Level 2 needs features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific supporting information added. You know the following: the second voyage left Cadiz on 25 September 1493 with seventeen ships and well over a thousand men, and found La Navidad burned and its garrison dead; La Isabela was laid out on the north coast of Hispaniola in January 1494 because it was the nearest anchorage to the Cibao goldfields, which lie inland across the cordillera; the site had bad water and poor shelter; most of the settlers were soldiers and gentlemen who would not plant; hunger and fever emptied the town within a year; hundreds of Taino captives were shipped to Spain for sale in 1495 and the monarchs stopped the sale; the tribute of a hawk's bell of gold dust from every Taino aged fourteen or over was imposed in 1495; gold found in the sands of the Haina in 1496 drew the colony to the south coast, where Santo Domingo was founded; La Isabela was abandoned by about 1500.","steps":["Sort what you know into results and mere description, because only a result scores: where the town stood is the founding itself, what the choice of site did to the colony is the consequence.","Put the claim in one sentence that uses the word itself - one consequence of founding La Isabela was that the colony was committed to living off gold and off the Taino rather than off its own fields.","Add the detail that proves the link and no other: the town was placed for the Cibao and not for water or farmland, it was manned by men who would not plant, and within twelve months the answer to its hunger was the tribute of 1495 rather than a harvest.","Finish with the analysis the grid asks for, not a restatement: a settlement that cannot feed itself must take food and labour from its neighbours, so a decision about an anchorage turned first contact into extraction and fixed the pattern that Roldan's men and then Ovando inherited."],"cues":["Which of the things in the list is caused by the founding of La Isabela, and which is simply a description of it?","How should the opening sentence be worded so the examiner sees the consequence immediately?","Which single detail shows that the choice of site, rather than bad luck, decided what the colony did next?","Level 2 wants features of the period analysed - what still has to be explained rather than stated?"]},{"title":"Question 1(b): a consequence of the shipment of Taino captives that is not a description of it","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(b): 'Explain one consequence of the shipment of Taino captives to Spain in 1495.' [4 marks]. You know: Columbus had promised in 1493 that the islands could supply slaves as well as gold; ships carried hundreds of Taino prisoners from Hispaniola to Andalusia in 1495 to be sold; Ferdinand and Isabella halted the sale while churchmen were asked whether such a sale was lawful, and by 1500 they had ordered the survivors freed; the crown thereafter treated Indigenous people as free vassals of Castile, so enslavement had to be justified by the Carib label or by rebellion; the tribute of 1495, the repartimiento conceded to Roldan's rebels from 1499 and the encomienda formalised by Ovando from 1503 all took labour without calling it slavery; the encomienda was dressed as a duty to protect and to have the Taino instructed in Christianity. The commonest weak answer simply says that captives were sent and sold.","steps":["Rule out the event itself: how many were shipped and what was done with them is the shipment, so an answer about the sale has described rather than explained.","Choose the result you can trace furthest forward - the crown was forced to decide, at once and in public, what an Indigenous person legally was.","Prove it with the named response: the monarchs stopped the sale pending a ruling, by 1500 they had ordered the survivors freed, and from then on Indigenous people were held to be free vassals of Castile.","Then analyse, which is what lifts the part into the top band: because outright slavery was closed off, forced labour had to be disguised as something else - tribute, then repartimiento, then an encomienda justified as protection and instruction - and that wording is what later let friars attack the system on the crown's own terms."],"cues":["Which part of what you know is the shipment itself rather than an effect of it?","Of the possible effects, which one forced a decision that had to be made in Spain rather than on Hispaniola?","What single fact shows the crown's answer actually changed the law the settlers worked under?","What does the grid want beyond an accurate statement plus a date?"]},{"title":"Question 2: planning a narrative account of Columbus's search for backing, 1484 to 1492","setup":"Booklet P, Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing Columbus's attempts to gain support for a voyage west in the years 1484-92.' (8 marks). The booklet prints: 'You may use the following in your answer: the rejection of his plan by John II of Portugal (1485); the commission under Hernando de Talavera (from 1486). You must also use information of your own.' The mark scheme shuts an answer out of Level 3 altogether if it does not address three or more aspects of content. You know: Columbus argued that Asia could be reached by sailing west, working from figures that made the globe too small and Asia too wide; John II's experts examined the plan and advised against it, and Columbus moved to Castile in 1485; he was heard at the Castilian court from 1486 and kept there on a small royal allowance; the friars of La Rabida took up his case; the commission under Hernando de Talavera met only from time to time and reported against him in 1490; he sent his brother Bartholomew to seek backing in England and France; the monarchs refused him again at the camp of Santa Fe while the siege of Granada was still running; Granada surrendered on 2 January 1492; Luis de Santangel, keeper of King Ferdinand's privy purse, argued that the terms would cost little and helped find the money; the Capitulations of Santa Fe were signed on 17 April 1492; the town of Palos owed the crown two caravels.","steps":["Fix a spine of four dated episodes inside the years given so the account has a sequence and not a heap: the plan and the Portuguese refusal, the years of waiting in Castile, the Talavera commission's verdict, and the reversal at Santa Fe.","Mark which episodes are your own material, since access to the top level turns on covering three or more aspects of content - La Rabida, Bartholomew's errands abroad, Santangel and the caravels owed by Palos all lie beyond the two bullets.","Write the join between episode one and episode two, because that join is the analysis: because his case rested on figures that trained cosmographers could check and reject, the refusals came from the men who knew most, so his only route was round the experts to a court.","Write the join between episode three and episode four: because the commission had reported against him in 1490 and Castile was still spending everything on the war, the plan could only revive once the war ended and somebody showed the crown how little it risked.","Close on what the sequence amounted to instead of repeating it: eight years of refusal ended not because anyone had been persuaded that the geography was right, but because the moment was free and the terms were cheap."],"cues":["How many episodes can you actually develop in about thirteen minutes, and what must each one contain?","Which of your episodes lie outside the two printed bullets, and why does that decide your level?","Why did the people best qualified to judge the plan keep turning it down?","What changed in January 1492 that had nothing to do with the merits of the plan?","What should the final sentence do that none of the others has done?"]},{"title":"Question 3: explaining the importance of gold instead of narrating the hunt for it","setup":"Booklet P, Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8, with no stimulus points. The bullet is: 'The importance of gold for Spanish settlement in the Caribbean.' (8 marks). Level 3 needs analysis of importance in a coherent, logically structured line of reasoning. You know: the Capitulations of 17 April 1492 promised Columbus a tenth of what was taken, and he carried gold samples home in 1493; the gold of Hispaniola lay in thin river gravels and had to be panned, so it needed many hands for months at a time rather than a few specialists; La Isabela was laid out in January 1494 for its nearness to the Cibao rather than for water or farmland; the tribute of a hawk's bell of gold dust from every Taino aged fourteen or over was imposed in 1495; gold found in the sands of the Haina in 1496 drew the colony to the south coast, where Santo Domingo was founded; the settlement with Roldan's rebels from 1499 paid them in land and Taino labour; Ovando formalised the encomienda from 1503; the crown took a fifth of all precious metals through the Casa de Contratacion set up at Seville in 1503; Puerto Rico was settled from 1508, Jamaica from 1509 and Cuba from 1511; a count of about 26,000 Indigenous people was made on Hispaniola in 1514, though the figure is disputed; from 1501 the crown allowed African slaves to be taken to Hispaniola and in 1510 licensed a consignment for the mines.","steps":["Define importance before writing a word of description: the mark is for the difference gold made to how the islands were settled, so no paragraph may stop at the fact that the Spanish wanted it.","Take the geology first, because everything else follows from it: placer gold in river gravels could only be got by many hands working for months, so the colony had to be a labour system rather than a trading post, and it also fixed where Spaniards lived - La Isabela for the Cibao in 1494, Santo Domingo after the Haina finds of 1496.","Take the labour line second: tribute in 1495, the repartimiento paid to Roldan's men from 1499 and the encomienda from 1503 are three answers to a single question, how to get enough hands to the diggings, and the count of 1514, disputed though it is, shows what those answers cost.","Take the crown's line third, which is the one weaker answers miss: the royal fifth turned a private venture into public revenue, which is why the Casa de Contratacion was set up in 1503 and why the crown kept pulling the government of the islands back into its own hands.","Judge, rather than summarise: say that gold decided where Spaniards went, how they lived and who worked for them, and that because worked-out gravels had to be replaced it also explains the speed with which Puerto Rico, Jamaica and Cuba were taken."],"cues":["What test must every paragraph pass before it is worth writing?","What difference does it make that this gold was panned from rivers rather than dug from veins?","What single question do the tribute, the repartimiento and the encomienda all answer?","Why did the crown care about the diggings enough to build an office in Seville around them?","What must the last sentence add that the three lines of argument have not?"]},{"title":"Question 2: choosing the aspects of content for a narrative on the third voyage","setup":"Booklet P, Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the events of Columbus's third voyage in the years 1498-1500.' (8 marks). The booklet prints: 'You may use the following in your answer: the sighting of Trinidad (July 1498); the settlement with Francisco Roldan's rebels (1499). You must also use information of your own.' There is no access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content. You know: six ships left Sanlucar on 30 May 1498 and the fleet divided at the Canaries, three going straight to Hispaniola with supplies while Columbus took three on a southern course; he sighted Trinidad on 31 July 1498; in the Gulf of Paria he found the sea fresh far from land, which told him that a great river and therefore a landmass lay behind it, and he wrote of a new world rather than of Asian islands; he reached Hispaniola in August 1498 to find Francisco Roldan in revolt in the west with a large part of the Spaniards; the revolt was settled in 1499 by restoring Roldan to office and granting his men land and Taino labour, which began the repartimiento; complaints from both sides reached the court; Ferdinand and Isabella had appointed Francisco de Bobadilla in 1499 as royal commissioner with power to govern; he reached Santo Domingo on 23 August 1500, took the government, and sent all three brothers to Spain in irons; the crown freed them but never restored the government to Columbus.","steps":["List the candidate aspects of content before choosing a spine: the divided fleet and the southern route, the fresh water of the Gulf of Paria and what Columbus made of it, the revolt he came back to, the terms that ended it, and the commissioner who ended his rule.","Discard the ones you cannot analyse and keep four you can join up, because the level depends on linkage and not on coverage for its own sake.","Make the fresh water do causal work rather than serve as scenery: no island could drain that much water, so a voyage sent to reach Asia produced instead the admission that this was another landmass - the discovery that mattered most in 1498 was geographical, not territorial.","Make the settlement with Roldan do causal work: with no gold revenue to pay rebels with, the only currency left was land and people, so the terms agreed in 1499 created the repartimiento and handed the next governor a labour system nobody had designed.","End by joining the last episode to what follows: because the government had been bought back with the island's own people and both sides had appealed to Spain, the commissioner already appointed in 1499 arrived to a colony that would not defend its viceroy, and a family's private province became a royal one."],"cues":["Which separate aspects of content does this voyage actually offer?","On what grounds should you cut the list down?","The fresh water is usually treated as a curiosity - what causal work can it be made to do?","If there was no gold to pay them with, what could the rebels be paid in, and what did that start?","What did the terms of 1499 make certain about how the voyage would end?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Name the three ships of the first voyage and give the dates on which the fleet left Palos, left the Canaries and sighted land.","a":"The Santa Maria, a nao serving as flagship, and the caravels Pinta and Nina. Left Palos 3 August 1492, sailed west from the Canaries 6 September 1492, sighted land on 12 October 1492, the island Columbus named San Salvador."},{"q":"What did the Capitulations of Santa Fe promise Columbus, and when were they signed?","a":"Signed on 17 April 1492. They made him Admiral of the Ocean Sea and viceroy and governor of any lands he discovered, gave him a tenth of the wealth taken from them, and made the titles hereditary."},{"q":"Why was the Treaty of Tordesillas needed, and what did it change?","a":"Because Portugal claimed the new lands under the Treaty of Alcacovas of 1479 and would not accept the line granted to Castile by Alexander VI in 1493. Signed on 7 June 1494, it moved the line to 370 leagues west of the Cape Verde Islands, which later left Brazil on the Portuguese side."},{"q":"What was the encomienda, and how did it differ from the tribute of 1495?","a":"The tribute required every Taino on Hispaniola aged fourteen or over to deliver gold dust or cotton every three months. The encomienda, formalised by Ovando from 1503, granted a settler a named group of Indigenous people whose labour he could command, in return for protecting them and having them instructed in Christianity."}]},"room":{"facts":["Booklet P is worth 32 marks and prints three questions: Question 1 in two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each 'Explain one consequence of...' for 4 marks and each on a separate event or development; Question 2, 'Write a narrative account analysing...', 8 marks; Question 3, 'Explain two of the following: the importance of ... for ...', two chosen parts at 8 marks each, 16 in all.","Only Question 2 prints stimulus points, as two bullets followed by 'You must also use information of your own.' The mark scheme prints one cap: 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' There is no cap at Level 2. Questions 1 and 3 print no bullets and carry no cap.","Each part of Question 1 is marked on its own two-level grid out of 4: Level 2 (3-4) needs features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific supporting information added. Two accurate descriptions of the event, however detailed, stay in Level 1.","Question 3 is marked twice on a three-level grid out of 8, and Level 3 (6-8) needs analysis of importance in a line of reasoning that is coherent and logically structured: what difference the event, person or development made to later situations, not what it was.","Timing: Paper 2 lasts 1 hour 50 minutes for 64 marks, about 55 minutes a booklet, so booklet P's 32 marks get roughly a minute a mark with planning on top - about 12 minutes on Question 1's two parts, 13 on Question 2 and 14 on each part of Question 3, leaving a couple of minutes to check. There are no sources on Paper 2 and no marks for spelling, punctuation and grammar."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the fall of Granada in January 1492. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2, marked on the two-level consequence grid; Level 2 (3-4) requires features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific support. Credit the freeing of Castilian money and royal attention for the Atlantic plan and the Capitulations of April 1492, the completion of the Reconquista and the crusading confidence behind the Alhambra Decree of 31 March 1492, or the hidalgos and soldiers left without a war.","sol":"One consequence was that Ferdinand and Isabella could at last afford to gamble on Columbus. For ten years the crown's revenue and attention had gone into the war against the emirate of Granada, and a commission under Hernando de Talavera had reported against his scheme. Once Boabdil surrendered the city on 2 January 1492 the monarchs stood at the height of their prestige, with a crusade finished and an army of hidalgos with nothing left to conquer. Within four months they signed the Capitulations of Santa Fe in the camp outside the captured city, and the terms cost them little if the ships never came back.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["A consequence is something that was true afterwards because of the event, so ask what the end of the war made possible that had not been possible during it.","The strongest line here is about resources and confidence rather than about territory: what had the war been consuming, and who was left with nothing to do once it ended?","Write it in three moves - name the consequence, give the specific detail that proves the link, then explain why victory changed the monarchs' calculation."],"codes":["P1-1 — 1 Spanish exploration: Spain c1490","P1-1 — 1 Spanish exploration: Columbus's attempts to gain sponsorship"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of the Capitulations of Santa Fe of April 1492. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the consequence grid. Credit the creation of a hereditary private jurisdiction inside the Castilian crown's new possessions, the later royal effort to recover those powers (licences to rival captains from 1495, Bobadilla in 1500, the Casa de Contratacion in 1503), or the incentive the terms gave Columbus to promise gold he had not found.","sol":"One consequence was that the crown created a rival to its own authority in the Indies. The agreement of 17 April 1492 made Columbus Admiral of the Ocean Sea, viceroy and governor of whatever he discovered and gave him a tenth of the wealth taken from it, and it made all of that hereditary. The terms risked nothing if he drowned, but once Hispaniola produced gold Ferdinand and Isabella had handed a Genoese sailor a private province. That is why the crown spent the next twenty years clawing the powers back, licensing rival captains from 1495 and appointing Francisco de Bobadilla in 1499 as royal commissioner with power to govern in Columbus's place; on reaching Santo Domingo in 1500 he took that power and sent all three brothers home in irons.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what the document actually gave away, and to whom, rather than what it says about Columbus's ambition.","The consequence worth explaining runs forward: if one man holds hereditary office over new lands, what problem does that create for a crown that later wants to govern them?","Name the consequence, prove it with the specific terms granted, then show the crown acting against those terms later."],"codes":["P1-1 — 1 Spanish exploration: Columbus's attempts to gain sponsorship","P1-1 — 3 Spanish claims in the Caribbean: Development of an imperial policy"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the wrecking of the Santa Maria in December 1492. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the consequence grid. Credit the founding of La Navidad as the first Spanish settlement in the Americas, the reduction of the fleet to one usable ship for the return, or the physical claim on Hispaniola that justified the colonising fleet of 1493.","sol":"One consequence was the founding of the first Spanish settlement in the Americas. The flagship ran aground off the north coast of Hispaniola on Christmas Day 1492 and could not be refloated, and the Nina alone could not carry the whole company home. Columbus therefore used her timbers to build a fort and left about thirty-nine men in it, naming the place La Navidad after the day of the loss. An accident thus turned a voyage of reconnaissance into an occupation, and it gave Columbus something solid to point to when he asked the monarchs for a colonising fleet in 1493.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Start from the practical problem the wreck created: how many men could the surviving ships carry?","The consequence is not the loss of a ship but what Columbus did with the timbers and the men he could not take.","State the consequence, support it with the date, the place and the number of men, then explain what it changed about the expedition's purpose."],"codes":["P1-1 — 1 Spanish exploration: Columbus's first voyage of 1492","P1-1 — 2 Columbus reaches the Americas: Columbus's actions"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of the founding of the Casa de Contratacion in 1503. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the consequence grid. Credit the Castilian monopoly of American trade through a single port, the crown's reliable revenue from the royal fifth, the licensing of and record-keeping over ships and cargoes and, from 1508, over pilots and charts, or the exclusion of foreign merchants and of the ports of the other Spanish kingdoms.","sol":"One consequence was that Castile turned the Indies into a closed monopoly run through a single port. From 1503 the House of Trade at Seville licensed every ship, crew and cargo sailing to or from the Americas and took the crown's fifth of all precious metals, so nothing could legally cross the Atlantic without passing its officials; and from 1508, when Amerigo Vespucci was made its piloto mayor, it also examined the pilots and kept the master chart, the Padron Real, on which each voyage's findings were entered. That gave the crown a dependable income and a growing record of what its new possessions held, and it shut out both foreign merchants and the ports of the other Spanish kingdoms, since the Indies belonged to Castile alone and Barcelona and the Aragonese ports had no share in them.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what a licensing office does to everyone who is not licensed.","Two threads are worth following: what the crown gained in money and information, and who was shut out.","Name the consequence, support it with what the House of Trade actually controlled, then explain why routing everything through one port mattered."],"codes":["P1-1 — 3 Spanish claims in the Caribbean: Development of an imperial policy"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of Columbus's return to Spain in March 1493. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the Treaty of Tordesillas of June 1494. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4 on the consequence grid, so each needs one consequence analysed with specific support. In 1(a) credit the Portuguese claim under the Treaty of Alcacovas and the appeal to Alexander VI, or the granting of a large second fleet. In 1(b) credit the eastern bulge of South America falling to Portugal and Cabral's landfall of 1500, the avoidance of war between the two crowns, or the unenforceability of a line whose longitude could not be measured.","sol":"1(a) Columbus put into Lisbon before reaching Palos on 15 March 1493, so King John II of Portugal heard of the discovery first and at once claimed the new lands under the Treaty of Alcacovas of 1479. The Catholic Monarchs answered by going to Rome, and Alexander VI granted Castile the lands west of a line a hundred leagues beyond the Azores and the Cape Verde Islands. The return therefore opened a diplomatic crisis within weeks, and it also won Columbus a far larger command: seventeen ships sailed from Cadiz that September to colonise rather than to explore. 1(b) The treaty signed on 7 June 1494 moved the dividing line to 370 leagues west of the Cape Verde Islands, and the consequence was that the eastern bulge of South America fell inside Portugal's half. Neither crown could measure longitude, so nobody knew where the line truly ran, but when Pedro Alvares Cabral made his landfall in 1500 the Portuguese were able to claim Brazil under it. An agreement meant to stop two Catholic powers fighting over islands therefore decided which language most of South America would come to speak.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate parts on two separate things, so neither answer may lean on the other: a homecoming in 1493 and a treaty in 1494.","For 1(a) the route home is the key fact - ask who heard the news first and what claim he already had.","For 1(b) resist describing where the line ran; ask what land the move put on which side, and what happened there six years later."],"codes":["P1-1 — 2 Columbus reaches the Americas: The impact of rivalry with Portugal","P1-1 — 1 Spanish exploration: Columbus's first voyage of 1492"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the destruction of La Navidad. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the founding of Santo Domingo in 1496. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two parts on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the end of peaceful relations and the turn to forts, hostages and open war, the battle of the Vega Real in 1495, or the tribute imposed the same year. In 1(b) credit the move of the colony's centre from the unhealthy north coast, the harbour on the side of the island facing Spain and the gold newly found in the sands of the Haina on that side of the island, or Santo Domingo's role as the capital and the base for taking Puerto Rico, Jamaica and Cuba.","sol":"1(a) Columbus came back in November 1493 to find the fort burned and every man dead, killed after the garrison had seized gold and Taino women and had fallen out among themselves. The consequence was that the assumption of easy friendship ended. Columbus blamed the cacique Caonabo, and the Spanish now built inland forts, took hostages and fought: a force with horses, steel and dogs broke a large Taino army at the Vega Real in 1495, and in that same year the gold tribute was imposed on every Taino aged fourteen or over. 1(b) Santo Domingo was founded in 1496 by Bartholomew Columbus on the south coast of Hispaniola, at the mouth of the Ozama, after gold had been found that year in the river sands of the Haina a short way to the west. The consequence was that Spanish settlement finally had a base that worked. La Isabela had been placed on the north coast for its nearness to the Cibao, which lies inland across the cordillera, and it had bad water and was abandoned; the new town had a sheltered harbour on the side of the island that faced Spain and fresh diggings behind it. Santo Domingo became the capital of the Indies and the port from which Puerto Rico from 1508, Jamaica in 1509 and Cuba from 1511 were taken.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["One part is about how the Spanish now treated the Taino, the other about where the Spanish now lived: keep them apart.","For 1(a) ask what a massacre does to the assumptions on which a policy rests, and what replaced those assumptions within two years.","For 1(b) compare the two sites rather than praising the new one, and then follow the new town forward to what was launched from it."],"codes":["P1-1 — 2 Columbus reaches the Americas: Columbus's actions","P1-1 — 3 Spanish claims in the Caribbean: Columbus's later voyages and his role as governor"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the tribute system imposed on the Taino of Hispaniola from 1495. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the arrival of Nicolas de Ovando as governor in 1502. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two parts on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit famine and population collapse as labour was drawn off the cassava fields, flight into the mountains, mutilation and killing of defaulters, or the reduction and abandonment of the tribute by the colony's own governors and its supersession by the repartimiento from 1499. In 1(b) credit the formalising of the encomienda from 1503, the rebuilding of Santo Domingo in stone and the founding of towns, or the destruction of the last independent chiefdoms at Xaragua.","sol":"1(a) Every Taino on Hispaniola aged fourteen or over had to deliver a hawk's bell filled with gold dust every three months, or cotton where there was no gold. The consequence was famine rather than treasure. The gold lay in thin river gravels, so meeting the quota meant weeks away from the cassava fields and the harvests failed; those who could not pay were mutilated or hunted, and whole villages fled into the mountains or died. It was the colony's own governors, not the crown, who gave the demand up: the quota was reduced and then let go on Hispaniola, and from 1499 the repartimiento conceded to Roldan's rebels took its place, so the forced labour the tribute had begun ran straight on into the encomienda. 1(b) Ovando reached Hispaniola in 1502 with about thirty ships and some 2,500 settlers and governed until 1509. The consequence was that the colony became a permanent Spanish society instead of a gold camp. He rebuilt Santo Domingo in stone on the west bank of the Ozama after a hurricane, founded towns across the island, and from 1503 formalised the encomienda, granting settlers groups of Taino to work the mines in return for protection and Christian instruction. He also destroyed the last independent chiefdom, wiping out Xaragua in 1503 and executing its cacica Anacaona.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Both parts are about Hispaniola but at different moments: one demand made in 1495 and one governor arriving in 1502.","For 1(a) the useful question is what the quota cost in time, not in gold: what were the people not doing while they panned?","For 1(b) ask what was on the island in 1509 that had not been there in 1502, in buildings, in law and in who ruled the Taino."],"codes":["P1-1 — 2 Columbus reaches the Americas: The impact of contact with Indigenous peoples","P1-1 — 3 Spanish claims in the Caribbean: The effects of Spanish settlement"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the sermon preached by Antonio de Montesinos on Hispaniola in December 1511. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the conquest of Cuba begun in 1511. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two parts on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the settlers' appeal to King Ferdinand, the summoning of theologians and lawyers, and the Laws of Burgos of 27 December 1512, or the beginning of a sustained clerical campaign that drew in Las Casas. In 1(b) credit the extension of the encomienda system and its effects to a fresh island and population, the killing of resistance such as the burning of Hatuey in 1512, or Cuba's later role as the base for expeditions to the mainland.","sol":"1(a) Montesinos, a Dominican, told the settlers of Santo Domingo in December 1511 that they were living in mortal sin for the cruelty with which they worked the Taino, and asked whether the Indians were not men. The consequence was that the argument moved from the colony to the court: the colonists complained to King Ferdinand, the friars were summoned to Spain, and the crown called together theologians and lawyers. Out of that came the Laws of Burgos of 27 December 1512, the first code to regulate Indigenous labour, which limited hours and required food, huts and instruction while leaving the encomienda itself standing. 1(b) Diego Velazquez crossed from Hispaniola in 1511 and had taken Cuba within about three years. The consequence was that the Spanish system moved on to a fresh island with a fresh population once Hispaniola's Taino and gold were running out, repeating the same pattern of towns, encomiendas and mines. Resistance was made an example of, and Hatuey, a cacique who had fled Hispaniola to warn the Cubans, was captured and burned in 1512. Cuba then became the springboard for the next stage, since the expeditions that reached Mexico sailed from it.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["One part is about words changing the law, the other about settlement moving island by island.","For 1(a) ask who complained about the sermon and to whom, and what the crown did once the quarrel reached it.","For 1(b) ask why the Spanish left Hispaniola at all, and what they carried with them when they went."],"codes":["P1-1 — 3 Spanish claims in the Caribbean: The effects of Spanish settlement","P1-1 — 3 Spanish claims in the Caribbean: Development of an imperial policy"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the events of Columbus's first voyage in the years 1492-93. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the Capitulations of Santa Fe (April 1492); the landing on San Salvador (October 1492). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) requires a clear sequence of events leading to an outcome with the linkage between them analysed. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; own material might include the fall of Granada, the ships and the Pinzon brothers, the Canaries and the trade winds, the near-mutiny and the double reckoning, Cuba and Hispaniola, the wreck of the Santa Maria and La Navidad, and the return through Lisbon.","sol":"The voyage was possible only because Granada had fallen in January 1492: with the crusade finished, the monarchs signed the Capitulations of Santa Fe on 17 April, promising Columbus the titles of admiral and viceroy and a tenth of any wealth, terms that cost them nothing if he never came back. Because he had been refused for years he sailed small, leaving Palos on 3 August with the Santa Maria and the caravels Pinta and Nina under the Pinzon brothers. The Canaries mattered next: repairs there, and departure west on 6 September, put the fleet in the belt of easterly trade winds, which drove it on quickly but meant the crews knew the same winds would not bring them back. That is why the danger after four weeks was mutiny rather than shipwreck, and why Columbus kept a second, shorter reckoning of the distance run. Land was sighted on 12 October and named San Salvador. Convinced he was in Asia, he pushed on to Cuba and then to Hispaniola, where the loss of the Santa Maria on Christmas Day left more men than the remaining ships could carry and so produced the fort at La Navidad. He reached Palos on 15 March 1493 by way of Lisbon, so Portugal learned of the discovery first, and the quarrel that ended at Tordesillas began with the return rather than with the landfall.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Begin before the sailing: what had just happened in Spain that made the Capitulations possible at all?","An account analyses when it explains why each stage left the choices it did - the winds, the crews' fear and the wreck all did causal work.","Plan four dated episodes with a connective sentence between each, so the answer addresses three or more aspects of content and not just the two bullets."],"codes":["P1-1 — 1 Spanish exploration: Columbus's attempts to gain sponsorship","P1-1 — 1 Spanish exploration: Columbus's first voyage of 1492","P1-1 — 2 Columbus reaches the Americas: Columbus's actions"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing Spanish settlement on Hispaniola in the years 1493-1502. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the founding of La Isabela (January 1494); the rebellion of Francisco Roldan (1497-99). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) requires a clear sequence leading to an outcome with the linkage analysed. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; own material might include the destruction of La Navidad, the size of the second fleet, the shipment of Taino captives in 1495 and Isabella's response, the gold tribute, the founding of Santo Domingo in 1496, the repartimiento conceded to the rebels, Bobadilla's arrest of the brothers in 1500 and Ovando's arrival in 1502.","sol":"Settlement began as an occupation in force. Columbus returned in November 1493 with seventeen ships and well over a thousand men, found La Navidad burned and its garrison dead, and founded La Isabela in January 1494 further east along the north coast. The site was chosen for its nearness to the Cibao gold rather than for water or shelter, and because the settlers were soldiers and gentlemen who would not farm, hunger and fever killed them faster than the Taino did. The shortage of gold then drove everything else: Columbus shipped hundreds of Taino captives to Spain to be sold in 1495, which Isabella ordered stopped, and in the same year imposed a tribute of gold dust on every Taino aged fourteen or over. The tribute failed because the gold was thin and the cassava went unplanted, so the only wealth left to pay settlers with was land and people, and that is exactly what Roldan's rebels demanded when they defied the Columbus brothers from 1497. Ending the revolt meant conceding it, which began the repartimiento. Meanwhile Bartholomew Columbus founded Santo Domingo on the south coast in 1496 and the island's centre of gravity moved there. The disorder brought Bobadilla in 1500 to arrest all three brothers, and in 1502 Ovando arrived with some 2,500 settlers: within a decade a trading post had become a labour colony run by the crown.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Start with what Columbus found when he came back in 1493, because the second colony was built in the shadow of the first one's fate.","The spine of the analysis is gold: ask at each stage what the Spanish did when the gold did not appear in the quantity they expected.","Four episodes with an explained join between each will beat six described ones, and make sure the material goes well beyond the two bullets."],"codes":["P1-1 — 2 Columbus reaches the Americas: Columbus's actions","P1-1 — 3 Spanish claims in the Caribbean: Columbus's later voyages and his role as governor","P1-1 — 3 Spanish claims in the Caribbean: The effects of Spanish settlement"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the growth of Spanish control in the Caribbean in the years 1502-12. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the governorship of Nicolas de Ovando (1502-09); the Laws of Burgos (1512). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) requires a clear sequence leading to an outcome with the linkage analysed. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; own material might include the rebuilding of Santo Domingo, the formalising of the encomienda from 1503, the destruction of Xaragua, the Casa de Contratacion of 1503, the taking of Puerto Rico from 1508, Jamaica in 1509 and Cuba from 1511, the arrival of the Dominicans in 1510 and Montesinos's sermon of December 1511.","sol":"Ovando arrived in 1502 with about thirty ships and some 2,500 settlers and governed until 1509, and his first work was to make Hispaniola a society rather than a camp: Santo Domingo was rebuilt in stone on the west bank of the Ozama after a hurricane, towns were founded across the island, and from 1503 the encomienda gave settlers grants of Taino labour in return for protecting them and having them instructed in the faith. Because that control required the end of independent Taino power, Xaragua was destroyed in 1503 and its cacica Anacaona executed. The system then ate the people it depended on, and as the island's gold thinned the Spanish moved outwards: Ponce de Leon took Puerto Rico from 1508, Esquivel took Jamaica in 1509, and Velazquez crossed to Cuba in 1511, where the cacique Hatuey was burned in 1512. Each new island was governed from Santo Domingo, supplied through the Casa de Contratacion set up at Seville in 1503 and taxed by the royal fifth, so expansion tightened royal control rather than loosening it. The challenge came from inside: the Dominicans reached Hispaniola in 1510 and Montesinos told the settlers in December 1511 that they were in mortal sin. Their appeal to Ferdinand produced the Laws of Burgos of 27 December 1512, which regulated Indigenous labour while confirming the encomienda - which is what the decade amounted to, a settled colonial system that had already generated its own critics.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The outcome the account leads to is the Laws of Burgos, so ask what had to exist before a code regulating Indigenous labour became necessary.","Two strands run side by side here: the spread of settlement from island to island, and the tightening of royal machinery behind it.","Join the strands with explained links rather than 'meanwhile', and make sure at least three aspects of content beyond the bullets are developed."],"codes":["P1-1 — 3 Spanish claims in the Caribbean: The effects of Spanish settlement","P1-1 — 3 Spanish claims in the Caribbean: Development of an imperial policy","P1-1 — 3 Spanish claims in the Caribbean: Columbus's later voyages and his role as governor"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of Queen Isabella for the voyage of 1492. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Level 3 (6-8) needs analysis of importance in a coherent line of reasoning. Credit her decision after the earlier refusals by John II and the Talavera commission, the Castilian financing arranged by Santangel and the caravels owed by Palos, the grant of the Capitulations in the monarchs' names, and her religious motive as the ground of Castile's later claim to rule.","sol":"Isabella mattered because the enterprise was Castilian and she was queen of Castile. Columbus had been refused by John II of Portugal in the mid-1480s and by her own commission under Talavera, and Ferdinand's interests lay in Italy, so the decision was hers to make. She supplied three things. First, money: Luis de Santangel, keeper of the privy purse, found the funds when she hesitated, and the port of Palos was ordered to provide two caravels against a debt it owed the crown, so no great outlay was needed. Second, authority: the Capitulations of Santa Fe of 17 April 1492 were granted in the monarchs' names, which made Columbus's landfalls Castilian possessions rather than a private venture, and made every later governor answerable to her crown. Third, purpose: her reason for backing him was the reason she had just finished the war for Granada and ordered the Jews to convert or leave in March 1492 - the spread of Christianity - and that purpose became the legal ground of Spanish rule, since the duty to convert justified the papal grants of 1493 and, later, the encomienda itself. Without her the idea might still have found a sponsor somewhere; because of her it sailed under Castile's flag, and everything that followed in the Caribbean was Castilian business, run from Seville and answerable to her successors.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Importance means the difference she made, so avoid any sentence that only describes her character or her piety.","Ask what the voyage needed that only a monarch could provide, and sort your material under those headings.","End with a judgement that says what would have been different without her, rather than a summary of what she did."],"codes":["P1-1 — 1 Spanish exploration: Columbus's attempts to gain sponsorship","P1-1 — 1 Spanish exploration: Spain c1490"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the encomienda for the Indigenous peoples of the Caribbean. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Credit the making of forced labour lawful and permanent from 1503, the demographic effect of drawing men off the food fields and the combined action of hunger, overwork and imported disease, the spreading of the system to Puerto Rico, Jamaica and Cuba, and the way its stated duty of protection and conversion armed its clerical critics.","sol":"The encomienda mattered because it made forced labour lawful and permanent. Tribute, imposed from 1495, had been an emergency demand for gold dust; the encomienda, formalised by Ovando from 1503, handed a named Spaniard a named group of Taino whose labour he could command in the mines and fields, in return for protecting them and having them taught the faith. Its first effect was demographic. Men were taken to the placer workings for months at a time, so cassava went unplanted and families were separated, and hunger, overwork and Old World infections together broke a population with no immunity to them; a count of about 26,000 Indigenous people was made on Hispaniola in 1514, though the figure is disputed. Its second effect was to carry the pattern outwards, because Puerto Rico from 1508, Jamaica from 1509 and Cuba from 1511 were settled on the same terms, so each new island repeated the collapse rather than learning from it. Its third effect was to create the argument that followed it: since the grant was justified as a duty of protection and instruction, friars could hold settlers to that bargain, which is why Montesinos could preach against them in December 1511, why the Laws of Burgos of 1512 tried to regulate the labour without abolishing the grant, and why Bartolome de las Casas, an encomendero himself until 1514, spent the rest of his life attacking it. The encomienda, more than any battle, decided what became of the Taino.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Importance is about difference made, so every paragraph should end at a point later than 1503.","Three lines are available: what it did to numbers, where it spread, and what its own wording allowed critics to say.","Close with a judgement that weighs it against the alternatives, such as warfare, rather than restating the effects."],"codes":["P1-1 — 3 Spanish claims in the Caribbean: The effects of Spanish settlement","P1-1 — 2 Columbus reaches the Americas: The impact of contact with Indigenous peoples"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of Catholic missionaries for Spanish rule in the Caribbean. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Credit conversion as the condition of the papal grants of 1493 and therefore the justification of Castilian rule, the friars' arrival with the second voyage, with Ovando in 1502 and the Dominicans in 1510, the sermon of Montesinos in December 1511 and the Laws of Burgos of 27 December 1512, and the long campaign of Las Casas.","sol":"Missionaries mattered first because they supplied the justification for the whole enterprise. The papal grants of 1493 gave Castile the new lands on condition that their peoples were brought to Christianity, so friars sailed with the second voyage in 1493, Franciscans came in numbers with Ovando in 1502 and the Dominicans arrived in 1510. Conversion also justified the encomienda, because every grant of Indigenous labour was made in return for having those workers instructed in the faith. That gave missionaries something no other group in the colony possessed: the right to judge whether the settlers were keeping their side of the bargain, and to say so publicly. They used it. Antonio de Montesinos told the congregation at Santo Domingo in December 1511 that they were living in mortal sin and asked whether the Indians were not men; the colonists appealed to King Ferdinand, the crown summoned theologians and lawyers, and the Laws of Burgos of 27 December 1512 were the result, the first code to limit Indigenous working hours and to require food, huts and instruction. Bartolome de las Casas, ordained on Hispaniola in 1510 and an encomendero himself until 1514, then carried the campaign to Spain for the rest of his life. Their importance is therefore double and contradictory: they made Spanish rule lawful in Spanish and papal eyes, and they produced the only sustained criticism of how that rule actually worked.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Missionaries matter here for two opposite reasons at once - keep both in view from the first sentence.","Ask what the papal grants required of Castile, and what that requirement allowed a friar to demand of a settler.","Finish by judging which of the two roles mattered more for Spanish rule, rather than listing the friars who arrived."],"codes":["P1-1 — 3 Spanish claims in the Caribbean: Development of an imperial policy","P1-1 — 3 Spanish claims in the Caribbean: The effects of Spanish settlement"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date did Granada surrender to Ferdinand and Isabella?","a":"2 January 1492."},{"q":"Which ship was wrecked off Hispaniola on Christmas Day 1492, and what settlement was built from her timbers?","a":"The Santa Maria; La Navidad."},{"q":"How far west of the Cape Verde Islands did the Treaty of Tordesillas of 1494 place the dividing line?","a":"370 leagues."},{"q":"Which code, issued on 27 December 1512, was the first Spanish law regulating the treatment and labour of Indigenous people in the Americas?","a":"The Laws of Burgos."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Ferdinand and Isabella","back":"Married in 1469; Isabella became queen of Castile in 1474 and Ferdinand king of Aragon in 1479. Their union joined Spain's two strongest Christian kingdoms, though each kept its own laws and Castile paid for the Atlantic voyages.","code":"P1-1 — 1 Spanish exploration: Spain c1490","open":false},{"front":"The fall of Granada, January 1492","back":"Boabdil surrendered Granada, the last Muslim emirate in Spain, on 2 January 1492, ending the Reconquista. It freed Castile's revenues, crowned Isabella's crusading prestige and left thousands of hidalgos with no war to fight.","code":"P1-1 — 1 Spanish exploration: Spain c1490","open":false},{"front":"Spain's rivals in 1490","back":"Portugal held the African route east under the Treaty of Alcacovas of 1479, and Bartolomeu Dias rounded the Cape of Good Hope in 1488. France was Spain's rival in Italy, so a western route was Castile's only way east.","code":"P1-1 — 1 Spanish exploration: Spain c1490","open":false},{"front":"Columbus's search for sponsorship","back":"A Genoese sailor who put Japan about 2,400 miles west of the Canaries, far too near. Rejected by John II of Portugal and by the Talavera commission; backed by the friars of La Rabida, Medinaceli and Santangel.","code":"P1-1 — 1 Spanish exploration: Columbus's attempts to gain sponsorship","open":false},{"front":"The Capitulations of Santa Fe, 17 April 1492","back":"The contract signed in the camp outside captured Granada. Columbus was made Admiral of the Ocean Sea, viceroy and governor of any lands he discovered, with a tenth of the wealth taken from them, and all of it hereditary.","code":"P1-1 — 1 Spanish exploration: Columbus's attempts to gain sponsorship","open":false},{"front":"The first voyage, 1492 to 1493","back":"Left Palos 3 August 1492 with the Santa Maria and the caravels Pinta and Nina; sailed west from the Canaries 6 September; sighted land 12 October and named it San Salvador; reached Palos again 15 March 1493.","code":"P1-1 — 1 Spanish exploration: Columbus's first voyage of 1492","open":false},{"front":"One problem Columbus faced on the crossing of 1492","back":"Any of: crews near mutiny as the weeks passed, so he kept a second, shorter reckoning of distance; the easterly trade winds that would not carry the ships home; the variation of the compass needle and the weed of the Sargasso Sea.","code":"P1-1 — 1 Spanish exploration: Columbus's first voyage of 1492","open":true},{"front":"La Navidad, December 1492","back":"The Santa Maria ran aground off Hispaniola on Christmas Day 1492. Her timbers built a fort where about thirty-nine men were left: the first Spanish settlement in the Americas. It was found burned and its garrison dead in November 1493.","code":"P1-1 — 2 Columbus reaches the Americas: Columbus's actions","open":false},{"front":"The Taino","back":"Arawak-speaking people of the Bahamas, Cuba, Jamaica, Puerto Rico and Hispaniola. They farmed cassava and maize, lived in villages under chiefs called caciques, and wore small gold ornaments taken from river gravels.","code":"P1-1 — 2 Columbus reaches the Americas: The impact of contact with Indigenous peoples","open":false},{"front":"The Caribs and the cannibal label","back":"Peoples of the Lesser Antilles, described by the Taino and the Spanish as eaters of human flesh; the word cannibal comes from their name. The crown allowed their enslavement if they resisted, so the label decided their fate.","code":"P1-1 — 2 Columbus reaches the Americas: The impact of contact with Indigenous peoples","open":false},{"front":"The gold tribute of 1495","back":"Columbus required every Taino on Hispaniola aged fourteen or over to deliver a hawk's bell filled with gold dust every three months, or cotton where there was no gold. It brought famine rather than treasure and was abandoned.","code":"P1-1 — 2 Columbus reaches the Americas: The impact of contact with Indigenous peoples","open":false},{"front":"The Treaty of Tordesillas, 7 June 1494","back":"Spain and Portugal moved the papal line of 1493 to 370 leagues west of the Cape Verde Islands: west for Castile, east for Portugal. Longitude could not be measured, and Cabral's landfall of 1500 left Brazil on the Portuguese side.","code":"P1-1 — 2 Columbus reaches the Americas: The impact of rivalry with Portugal","open":false},{"front":"Santo Domingo, 1496","back":"Founded by Bartholomew Columbus on the south coast of Hispaniola near the Ozama, replacing unhealthy La Isabela of 1494. It became the capital of the Indies and the base for taking Puerto Rico, Jamaica and Cuba.","code":"P1-1 — 3 Spanish claims in the Caribbean: Columbus's later voyages and his role as governor","open":false},{"front":"The crown takes control, 1495 to 1503","back":"Rival captains were licensed from 1495; Bobadilla replaced Columbus as governor in 1500; the Casa de Contratacion at Seville, set up in 1503, licensed every ship, crew and cargo and took the crown's fifth of precious metals.","code":"P1-1 — 3 Spanish claims in the Caribbean: Development of an imperial policy","open":false},{"front":"Ovando, the encomienda and the Laws of Burgos","back":"Ovando governed Hispaniola from 1502 to 1509, formalised the encomienda from 1503 and destroyed Xaragua. Montesinos preached against settler cruelty in December 1511; the Laws of Burgos of 27 December 1512 regulated Indigenous labour but kept the encomienda.","code":"P1-1 — 3 Spanish claims in the Caribbean: The effects of Spanish settlement","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Paper 2 booklet P Question 2, narrative account, 8 marks)","items":["Question: 'Write a narrative account analysing the events of Columbus's second voyage in the years 1493-96.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the sighting of Dominica (3 November 1493); the campaign at the Vega Real (1495). You must also use information of your own. This is the middle stretch of the account, carrying the story from the founding of the second colony to the year of force.","Because the seventeen ships that left Cadiz on 25 September 1493 carried settlers, tools and stock rather than explorers, and because the men who reached La Navidad late in November found the fort burned and its garrison dead, the voyage turned at once from reconnaissance into occupation: La Isabela was laid out in January 1494 on the north coast for its nearness to the Cibao gold rather than for water or farmland. That choice decided what followed. With a town that could not feed itself, settlers who would not plant, and Columbus away from April 1494 taking three ships along the south coast of Cuba and round Jamaica - a cruise that turned back on 12 June 1494, when the crew swore the Cuba oath, and reached La Isabela in late September with his men swearing that Cuba was the mainland of Asia - the Spaniards left on Hispaniola fed themselves at Taino expense, and the easy dealings of 1492 gave way to raiding. That is why 1495 was the year of force: hundreds of Taino were shipped to Andalusia to be sold, though the monarchs stopped the sale; a force with horses, steel and dogs broke a large Taino army at the Vega Real; and every Taino aged fourteen or over was required to deliver a hawk's bell of gold dust every three months. The tribute failed for the same reason the colony had failed, because the gold lay thin in river gravels and weeks of panning meant cassava unplanted, so the complaints that brought Juan de Aguado out in 1495 found an island that had produced neither Asia nor treasure.","Why this is Level 3: the material is organised as a clear sequence leading to an outcome, and the linkage between the events is analysed rather than asserted - the siting of La Isabela explains the turn to extraction, the admiral's absence explains who paid for the colony's hunger, and the failure of the tribute explains the inquiry that followed. The knowledge is precise (the date of the fleet's departure, the founding of La Isabela, the oath of 12 June 1494, the terms of the tribute) and it addresses well beyond three aspects of content, so the Level 3 cap does not bite."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same, 'Write a narrative account analysing the events of Columbus's second voyage in the years 1493-96.' (8 marks), with the same two bullets.","Columbus set out again in 1493 with a lot of ships and men to start a proper colony. On the way he passed Dominica and some other islands. When he got to La Navidad he found it had been destroyed and all the men were dead, so he built a new town called La Isabela in January 1494. It was not a good place and many settlers got ill and died. Then he went off to look at Cuba and Jamaica because he still thought Asia was close. The Spanish wanted gold and made the Taino give them gold, and they treated them very badly, which made things worse and worse. There was fighting at the Vega Real and the Spanish won because they had horses and dogs. In the end Columbus went back to Spain in 1496 and left his brothers in charge.","Why this is only Level 2, and what it misses: the events are in the right order and the information is accurate, so the answer meets 'some organisation of material into a sequence of events leading to an outcome' and 'accurate and relevant information is added'. It misses the Level 3 requirement that 'the account of events analyses the linkage between them': 'things got worse and worse' asserts a link instead of explaining one, and nothing says why the site of La Isabela mattered, why the demand for gold produced famine, or what the admiral's absence in Cuban waters had to do with either. The detail is also loose where the top level wants precision - the tribute is unnamed and undated, the Vega Real is a fight rather than the moment resistance in the interior was broken - and the closing sentence stops the story instead of saying what the three years amounted to."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Columbus and Queen Isabella","items":["Christopher Columbus: a Genoese sailor who proposed reaching Asia by sailing west; he underestimated the size of the earth and overestimated the eastward reach of Asia, putting Japan about 2,400 miles west of the Canary Islands.","Rejected by King John II of Portugal in the mid-1480s after a committee of experts judged his distances wrong.","Came to Castile in 1485 and was heard by Ferdinand and Isabella from 1486; a royal commission under Hernando de Talavera reported against him.","Supporters who kept the scheme alive: the Franciscans of the La Rabida monastery near Palos, the Duke of Medinaceli, and Luis de Santangel, keeper of the privy purse, who found the money and pressed Isabella after her final refusal.","Isabella: queen of Castile from 1474; the enterprise was Castilian, so the decision and the later claim to rule were Castile's.","Her motives: the spread of Christianity after the crusade against Granada, royal prestige, gold and spices, and the fear that Portugal would reach the East first.","The Capitulations of Santa Fe, 17 April 1492, signed in the camp outside captured Granada: Columbus to be Admiral of the Ocean Sea, viceroy and governor of any lands discovered, with a tenth of the wealth taken from them, all hereditary.","Financing: Santangel arranged the funds, and the port of Palos de la Frontera was ordered to supply two caravels against a debt it owed the crown, so the crown's own outlay was small.","Columbus was received by the monarchs at Barcelona in April 1493 after the first voyage, at the height of his standing.","He was stripped of the governorship in 1500, made a fourth voyage in 1502 to 1504 and died at Valladolid in 1506; his family pursued his hereditary rights in the courts for decades."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Columbus's first voyage of 1492","items":["Left Palos de la Frontera on 3 August 1492 with three ships: the Santa Maria, a broad nao serving as flagship, and the caravels Pinta and Nina.","Captains: Martin Alonso Pinzon commanded the Pinta and his brother Vicente Yanez Pinzon the Nina.","Refitted in the Canary Islands, including repairs to the Pinta's rudder, and sailed west on 6 September 1492.","Navigational problems: no European had sailed the route, the variation of the compass needle alarmed the pilots, and the ships met weed in the Sargasso Sea and long calms.","Human problems: after four weeks the crews pressed to turn back, and Columbus kept a second, shorter reckoning of the distance run to show them.","Structural problem: the easterly trade winds carried the fleet west but would not carry it home, so the return had to be made on a different latitude.","Land sighted on 12 October 1492 by Rodrigo de Triana, a lookout on the Pinta; Columbus named the island San Salvador.","Martin Alonso Pinzon took the Pinta away without permission in November 1492 to look for gold on his own account.","Reached the north coast of Cuba on 28 October 1492 and Hispaniola on 5 December 1492.","The return was made through storms by way of the Azores and Lisbon; Columbus reached Palos on 15 March 1493."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the foundation of La Navidad","items":["The Santa Maria ran aground off the north coast of Hispaniola on Christmas Day, 25 December 1492, and could not be refloated.","The Nina alone could not carry the whole company home, and the Pinta was absent with Martin Alonso Pinzon.","Columbus had the wreck broken up and her timbers used to build a fort, naming the place La Navidad after the feast day of the wreck.","About thirty-nine men were left there: the first Spanish settlement in the Americas.","Its purpose was double: to keep the men who could not be carried, and to hold a physical claim on Hispaniola.","It was used as evidence when Columbus asked for a colonising fleet, and the second voyage sailed from Cadiz on 25 September 1493 with seventeen ships and well over a thousand men.","Columbus returned in November 1493 to find the fort burned and every man dead.","The garrison had seized gold and Taino women and had quarrelled among themselves; Columbus blamed the cacique Caonabo.","The consequence was the end of the assumption of easy friendship: the Spanish now built inland forts, took hostages and fought.","A Spanish force with horses, steel and dogs defeated a large Taino army at the Vega Real in 1495, and the gold tribute was imposed in the same year."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Tainos and Caribs","items":["The Taino were an Arawak-speaking people living across the Bahamas, Cuba, Jamaica, Puerto Rico and Hispaniola.","They farmed cassava and maize, fished, lived in villages under chiefs called caciques, and had no iron, horses or firearms.","They wore small gold ornaments won from river gravels, which the Spanish read as proof of rich mines nearby.","First exchanges in October 1492 were peaceful, with trinkets traded for gold; Columbus took Taino captives back to Spain as interpreters and as proof.","The Caribs of the Lesser Antilles were described first by the Taino and then by the Spanish as raiders and eaters of human flesh; the word cannibal derives from their name.","The label mattered legally: the crown allowed the enslavement of peoples who ate human flesh and resisted, so being called Carib decided a people's status.","Indigenous people were otherwise declared free vassals of the Castilian crown, which is why enslavement had to be justified by that label or by rebellion.","The tribute of 1495 required every Taino on Hispaniola aged fourteen or over to deliver a hawk's bell filled with gold dust every three months, or cotton where there was no gold.","Notable caciques: Caonabo, blamed for destroying La Navidad, and Anacaona, cacica of Xaragua, executed by Ovando in 1503.","Hatuey, a Taino cacique who fled Hispaniola to warn the people of Cuba, was captured and burned in 1512."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Treaty of Tordesillas (1494)","items":["Background: the Treaty of Alcacovas of 1479 ended the war over the Castilian succession and gave Portugal the Atlantic south of the Canary Islands, leaving Castile without a southern route east.","Portugal's advantage: Bartolomeu Dias rounded the Cape of Good Hope in 1488, and Vasco da Gama reached India in 1498.","Columbus returned by way of Lisbon in March 1493, so John II of Portugal heard of the discovery first and claimed the new lands under Alcacovas.","Castile appealed to Pope Alexander VI, a Valencian, who issued bulls in 1493 granting Castile the lands west of a line a hundred leagues beyond the Azores and Cape Verde Islands.","Portugal refused to accept a line drawn by a Spanish pope and negotiated directly with Castile.","The Treaty of Tordesillas was signed on 7 June 1494: the line moved to 370 leagues west of the Cape Verde Islands, everything west Castile's and everything east Portugal's.","Neither crown could measure longitude, so the true position of the line was guesswork and remained arguable for generations.","Effect: when Pedro Alvares Cabral made his landfall on the South American coast in 1500, the territory fell on the Portuguese side and became Brazil.","No other European power was consulted or considered itself bound, which is why English, French and Dutch ships later ignored it.","Its importance for Spain: it settled the quarrel by treaty rather than by war, and left Castile free to develop the Caribbean without a Portuguese fleet against it."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Spanish colony at Santo Domingo (1496)","items":["The second voyage left Cadiz on 25 September 1493 with seventeen ships and well over a thousand men, and sighted Dominica on 3 November 1493.","La Isabela was founded in January 1494 on the north coast of Hispaniola, chosen for its nearness to the Cibao goldfields rather than for water or shelter; disease and hunger emptied it and it was abandoned.","Santo Domingo was founded in 1496 by Bartholomew Columbus on the south coast, near the mouth of the Ozama river.","Its advantages: a sheltered harbour at the mouth of the Ozama on the side of the island facing Spain, better land and water, and the gold found in 1496 in the river sands of the Haina to the west, where Bartholomew Columbus built the fort of San Cristobal.","It became the capital of the Indies and the seat of the governor, and later of the first cathedral and royal court in the Americas.","Ovando rebuilt it in stone on the west bank of the Ozama after a hurricane in 1502.","It was the base from which Puerto Rico was taken by Ponce de Leon from 1508, Jamaica by Juan de Esquivel in 1509 and Cuba by Diego Velazquez from 1511.","Governors: Columbus until 1500, Bobadilla from 1500, Nicolas de Ovando from 1502 to 1509, then Diego Columbus from 1509.","Ovando arrived in 1502 with about thirty ships and some 2,500 settlers, making the colony a settled Spanish society rather than a gold camp.","Supplies, licences and taxation ran through the Casa de Contratacion set up at Seville in 1503, which also took the crown's fifth of precious metals."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Catholic missionaries","items":["Conversion was the condition on which Alexander VI granted Castile the new lands in 1493, so missionary work was the legal ground of Spanish rule.","Friars sailed with the second voyage in 1493; Franciscans arrived in numbers with Ovando in 1502 and the Dominicans reached Hispaniola in 1510.","Every encomienda grant was made in return for protecting the Indigenous workers and having them instructed in Christianity, which gave the friars the right to judge whether settlers kept the bargain.","Antonio de Montesinos, a Dominican, preached at Santo Domingo in December 1511 that the settlers were living in mortal sin, asking whether the Indians were not men.","The colonists appealed to King Ferdinand, the friars were summoned to Spain and the crown called together theologians and lawyers.","The Laws of Burgos of 27 December 1512 followed: the first code governing Indigenous labour, limiting hours and requiring food, huts and religious instruction.","The Laws confirmed the encomienda rather than abolishing it, and were widely ignored in the colonies.","Bartolome de las Casas went to Hispaniola in 1502, held an encomienda, was ordained priest there in 1510 and gave up his grant in 1514.","Las Casas became the best-known campaigner against the treatment of Indigenous peoples, was appointed protector of the Indians in 1516, and wrote his short account of the destruction of the Indies in 1542.","The missionaries' importance is double: they made Spanish rule lawful in Spanish and papal eyes, and they produced the only sustained criticism of how it worked."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 2 booklet P Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the events of Columbus's second voyage in the years 1493-96' (8 marks)","items":["The question as printed: 'Write a narrative account analysing the events of Columbus's second voyage in the years 1493-96. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the sighting of Dominica (3 November 1493); the campaign at the Vega Real (1495). You must also use information of your own.'","Decode it before writing. 'Narrative account' means a sequence that leads somewhere definite, so the answer must begin and end at fixed points; 'analysing' means every join between one episode and the next has to be explained rather than assumed; and the dates fence the answer, so nothing before the fleet sails and nothing after Columbus lands in Spain belongs here except in the closing sentence.","The cap that decides the level: no access to Level 3 at all for an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content. Decide first which episodes are your own material, and make sure those are the ones you develop.","Episode 1, a colonising fleet, 1493-94: seventeen ships and well over a thousand men leave Cadiz on 25 September 1493 with tools, seed and stock; the fleet takes a southern track, sights Dominica on 3 November and works up through the Lesser Antilles; at La Navidad late in November the fort is burned and the garrison dead; La Isabela is laid out in January 1494 on the north coast, chosen for its nearness to the Cibao gold rather than for water or farmland.","Link sentence: because the men had come to settle and to mine, and because the first Spanish post had already been destroyed, the enterprise stopped being reconnaissance and became an occupation that had to be defended and fed.","Episode 2, still looking for Asia, 1494 (own material): from April 1494 Columbus takes three ships along the south coast of Cuba and round Jamaica, and on 12 June 1494 has his men swear that Cuba is the mainland; meanwhile La Isabela sickens on bad water, and settlers who will not plant live off what they can take.","Link sentence: an admiral chasing Asia while his colony went hungry left the Spaniards on Hispaniola to feed themselves at Taino expense, so the quarrel the second voyage was meant to avoid began in his absence.","Episode 3, force and tribute, 1495: hundreds of Taino captives are shipped to Andalusia to be sold, and the monarchs stop the sale; a force with horses, steel and dogs breaks a large Taino army at the Vega Real; and in the same year every Taino aged fourteen or over is required to deliver a hawk's bell of gold dust every three months.","Link sentence: because the tribute produced hunger rather than treasure and the settlers' complaints reached Spain, the crown sent Juan de Aguado to look into the colony in 1495, and Columbus had to go home and answer for it in person.","Episode 4, going home to be judged, 1496 (own material): Columbus leaves Hispaniola on 10 March 1496 and reaches Cadiz on 11 June, having made Bartholomew his deputy; gold found in the sands of the Haina draws the colony towards the south coast, where Bartholomew founds Santo Domingo in 1496.","The closing sentence must say what the sequence amounted to rather than repeat it: in under three years a fleet sent to plant a colony in the islands of Asia had found neither Asia nor much gold, and had instead settled the terms on which Spaniards and Taino would live together - a port on the south coast, and a people made to pay for the failure.","Timing and shape: about 13 minutes - two to choose four episodes and mark which are your own material, ten to write, one to check that every join carries a sentence of explanation. Four developed episodes beat six thin ones, and use the terms an examiner looks for: cacique, tribute, adelantado, viceroy, Cibao, placer gold."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:07:37Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"Every one of the nine fixes is present and checks out - the worked examples, plan and model paragraphs no longer answer any of the fourteen questions, Santo Domingo is now sited for the Ozama harbour and the Haina gold of 1496 with the fort of San Cristobal, the Casa's pilot examinations and Padron Real are dated to Vespucci's appointment as piloto mayor in 1508, Bobadilla is the commissioner of 1499 whose arrest in 1500 was his own act, the tribute is let go by the colony's governors and superseded by the repartimiento from 1499, and the 1514 count is hedged at all three occurrences; what is left is one loose chronology in the Level 3 model paragraph and two 4-mark model answers that have grown too long."}}},"P1-2":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"P1-2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 2 is where Spain stops exploring and starts conquering, and booklet P returns to it constantly: the consequence questions love a single dated event whose effects show within a year or two, the narrative account loves a short run of years in which one thing plainly caused the next, and the importance questions love a person, an alliance or a base whose effects reached forward for decades. If you can say, with names and dates, what Balboa claimed in 1513 and why the isthmus mattered afterwards, how Cuba became the springboard for Mexico, why Cortes was able to march inland at all, how a Spanish force thrown out of Tenochtitlan in 1520 took it in 1521, and what the conquest did to the Aztecs and to the government of New Spain, you can answer almost anything this key topic sets. This room teaches that sequence as a chain you can argue with, and it trains the three moves booklet P rewards: explaining a consequence instead of retelling an event, writing a narrative that links its episodes instead of listing them, and judging what difference something made to what came after it.","idea":[{"code":"P1-2 — 1 The start of an empire: Balboa's claim of Spanish authority over the Pacific (1513)","h":"Across the isthmus: how Balboa gave Spain a second ocean in 1513","t":"Vasco Nunez de Balboa was a leader of the settlers at Santa Maria la Antigua del Darien, the first lasting Spanish town on the American mainland, founded in 1510 on the Caribbean side of the isthmus of Panama. Told by local people of a great water and a rich kingdom to the south, he set out from Darien on 1 September 1513 with about 190 Spaniards and hundreds of Indigenous porters and guides. On 25 September he sighted open water from high ground, and four days later he waded into the surf in armour and claimed the South Sea (Mar del Sur), and every land its waters touched, for the Crown of Castile. The claim mattered more than the march. It turned a coastal venture into a legal title over an ocean Spain had not sailed, and it made the narrow isthmus the most valuable ground in the Indies. Balboa gained nothing from it: Pedrarias Davila arrived as governor of Castilla del Oro in 1514, displaced him and had him executed in 1519. A top-level answer treats 1513 as the hinge between a Caribbean empire and a Pacific one, and is careful to say that what changed first was Spain's claim and ambition, not yet its power on the ground."},{"code":"P1-2 — 1 The start of an empire: The conquest of Cuba","h":"Cuba, 1511 to 1515: the springboard, and what it cost the Taino","t":"Diego Velazquez de Cuellar crossed from Hispaniola in 1511 with roughly 300 men to take Cuba for Spain. Resistance was led by Hatuey, a Taino cacique who had fled Hispaniola and knew what conquest meant; he avoided open battle and raided from the forests of the east, and when he was captured and burned at the stake in 1512 the only coordinated defence went with him. Baracoa was founded in 1511 as the first Spanish town, and Santiago de Cuba and Havana followed by 1515. Velazquez became governor and distributed the island's people among his followers under the encomienda system, so that within a generation the Taino population had collapsed under forced labour, hunger and European disease. For the conquistadors Cuba's real value was as a base: from its harbours Velazquez sent Francisco Hernandez de Cordoba to Yucatan in 1517, Juan de Grijalva along the Mexican coast in 1518, and Cortes in 1519. A top-level answer treats Cuba as a cause rather than an episode. It created the men, ships, credit and appetite for the mainland expeditions, and it set the pattern - encomienda, disease, population collapse - that Mexico would repeat on a far larger scale."},{"code":"P1-2 — 1 The start of an empire: The voyage of Magellan and Spanish claim to the Philippines","h":"Magellan and Elcano, 1519 to 1522: the strait, the islands and a claim Spain kept","t":"Ferdinand Magellan was Portuguese, but Charles I of Spain backed him because he promised a westward route to the Spice Islands inside Spain's half of the world as divided by the Treaty of Tordesillas in 1494. Five ships left Sanlucar de Barrameda on 20 September 1519. After a mutiny was put down in Patagonia and one ship was wrecked, the fleet found the passage at the southern tip of South America late in 1520 and crossed a Pacific far wider than anyone had imagined, the crews starving before they reached the islands in March 1521. Magellan was killed at Mactan on 27 April 1521, fighting on behalf of a local ruler he had allied with. One ship, the Victoria, under Juan Sebastian Elcano, reached Spain on 6 September 1522 with eighteen men aboard, the first vessel to sail round the world. Spain's claim to the islands later named the Philippines dated from this voyage, although settlement began only decades afterwards; in 1529 Charles sold his claim to the Moluccas to Portugal while keeping the Philippines. A top-level answer weighs the voyage by what it proved and claimed rather than by what it earned: the world could be circumnavigated, the Pacific was vast, and Spain had a legal foothold in Asia."},{"code":"P1-2 — 2 The conquest of Mexico: Cortes's expedition to Mexico in 1519","h":"Why Cortes sailed: a commission withdrawn, and a town council that made him legal","t":"Hernan Cortes came out to Hispaniola in 1504, served in the conquest of Cuba in 1511 and became a wealthy encomendero and magistrate of Santiago de Cuba. In 1518, after Grijalva brought back gold from the Mexican coast, Velazquez appointed him to lead a third expedition, then had second thoughts and tried to cancel the commission. Cortes sailed anyway, leaving Cuba in February 1519 with about eleven ships, some 500 soldiers, about sixteen horses and a few cannon. On the coast of Tabasco he was given twenty women, one of whom, Malintzin - called Dona Marina by the Spanish and La Malinche in Mexico - spoke both Nahuatl and Maya; with Jeronimo de Aguilar, a Spaniard shipwrecked in Yucatan who had learned Maya, she gave Cortes a working chain of translation and, with it, intelligence about Aztec politics. In the spring of 1519 he founded Villa Rica de la Vera Cruz and had its new town council appoint him captain, so that his authority came from the king and not from Velazquez; later that summer he ran his ships aground so that no one could sail home. A top-level answer explains motive and legality together: gold, land and encomiendas, royal service and titles, and conversion, plus a legal manoeuvre without which the whole expedition was mutiny."},{"code":"P1-2 — 2 The conquest of Mexico: Key events of the Spanish conquest of Mexico","h":"Enemies of the Aztecs: Tlaxcala, Cholula and the road to Tenochtitlan","t":"The Aztec empire ruled central Mexico through tribute rather than direct government, leaving subject peoples their own rulers and their resentment. Tlaxcala, a confederation east of the valley of Mexico, had never been conquered and had fought the Aztecs for years. Cortes marched inland from the coast in August 1519 with Totonac allies from Cempoala. The Tlaxcalans fought him hard through September 1519 and, unable to destroy him, allied with him instead; from that point his army was overwhelmingly Indigenous, and the alliance held to the end. In October 1519 the Spanish and their allies killed thousands of unarmed men in the sacred precinct at Cholula, an Aztec ally - Cortes's own report claimed about three thousand dead - and the news frightened other cities into submission. Montezuma II, also written Moctezuma, chose gifts, warnings and delay rather than an attack in open country, and on 8 November 1519 he received Cortes on the causeway into Tenochtitlan. Within a fortnight the Spanish had seized him and were ruling through him. A top-level answer explains the conquest as a war among Mexican states that the Spanish joined and came to lead, and treats Montezuma's caution as a political misjudgement of a rival rather than as superstition."},{"code":"P1-2 — 2 The conquest of Mexico: The capture of Tenochtitlan and the Aztec surrender","h":"From the Noche Triste to 13 August 1521: how the city was taken","t":"Spanish rule through Montezuma lasted about seven months and ended in violence. While Cortes was on the coast defeating Panfilo de Narvaez, whom Velazquez had sent from Cuba in March 1520 to arrest him and who landed on the coast in April, Pedro de Alvarado massacred Aztec nobles at the festival of Toxcatl in the temple precinct, and the city rose. Montezuma died at the end of June 1520 - Spanish accounts say stoned by his own people, Aztec accounts say killed by the Spanish - and on the night of 30 June the Spanish fought their way out along the causeways, losing most of their men, their guns and nearly all their treasure: the Noche Triste, the Night of Sorrows. They won a battle at Otumba in early July and reached Tlaxcala. What saved the conquest was allies and disease: smallpox, carried from Cuba with Narvaez's men, swept the valley from the autumn of 1520 and probably killed Cuitlahuac, who was elected to succeed Montezuma after the expulsion, though the early accounts do not name the disease that killed him; meanwhile Cortes gathered tens of thousands of Indigenous troops and had thirteen brigantines built to fight on the lake. The siege began in late May 1521, the aqueduct from Chapultepec was cut and the causeways blockaded, and after nearly three months the last emperor, Cuauhtemoc, was captured on 13 August 1521 and the city surrendered. A top-level answer keeps all three strands running - allies, disease and control of the water - and shows how the failure of 1520 produced the method of 1521."},{"code":"P1-2 — 3 Impact of Spain in the Americas: Cortes's actions as Governor and Captain-General","h":"Ruling what he had taken: New Spain, 1523 to 1528, and why the Crown took it back","t":"Charles V appointed Cortes Governor and Captain-General of New Spain in October 1522, and he governed from 1523. He had Tenochtitlan demolished and rebuilt on its own site as Mexico City, laid out on a Spanish grid with a cathedral raised on the precinct of the Great Temple; he granted encomiendas of Indigenous towns to his followers although the Crown had instructed him not to; he brought in Spanish cattle, crops, sugar and mining; and he welcomed the twelve Franciscan friars who arrived in 1524 to convert the Nahua. He also sent expeditions outward, Alvarado to Guatemala and Cristobal de Olid to Honduras. When Olid rebelled, Cortes marched overland to Honduras himself from 1524 to 1526, taking Cuauhtemoc with him and having him executed on the way in 1525. The march wrecked his position: officials in Mexico City fought over his government and he was reported dead, and when he returned in 1526 he was suspended and investigated. The Crown then replaced personal rule with a court, the first Audiencia of New Spain, created in 1527, which took office in December 1528 under Nuno de Guzman, whose greed and brutality became notorious. A top-level answer treats the governorship as the point at which conquest became administration, and explains the Crown's motive: a conqueror who could give away land and labour could become a rival."},{"code":"P1-2 — 3 Impact of Spain in the Americas: The consequences of the Spanish invasion for the Aztecs","h":"After the empire: tribute, temples and a population that collapsed","t":"The Aztec empire ceased to exist in 1521. Its capital was demolished and rebuilt as the capital of its conquerors, and the tribute that had flowed to Tenochtitlan was redirected to Spanish encomenderos, who claimed labour and goods from the same towns under new masters. Public religion was destroyed: temples were pulled down, images broken, priests displaced, and from 1524 the Franciscans baptised on a large scale, although older beliefs survived inside the new practice. The effects were not equal everywhere: Nahua nobles who co-operated kept some local authority, and Tlaxcala was rewarded with privileges granted to no other Indigenous city. The heaviest consequence was demographic. Smallpox in 1520 was followed by measles in the 1530s and by the great cocoliztli epidemics of the 1540s and the 1570s; historians' estimates of the numbers differ sharply, but all agree that the Indigenous population of central Mexico fell by the great majority within a century, and that forced labour and the disruption of farming made the epidemics deadlier. A top-level answer separates the immediate consequences of 1521 from the long ones, states which mattered most, and says on what criterion - lives, power, religion or wealth - it is judging."},{"code":"P1-2 — 3 Impact of Spain in the Americas: The founding and significance of a Spanish base at Panama","h":"Panama, 1519: the hinge of two oceans","t":"Pedrarias Davila founded Panama on the Pacific shore on 15 August 1519, the first European town on the Pacific coast of the Americas, and it became the capital of the province of Castilla del Oro. Its value was geography. Men, timber and supplies could be carried across the isthmus in a few days and re-embarked on the Pacific, so Spain could explore and attack a coastline without sailing round South America to reach it. Every early voyage down the Pacific coast of South America began there, including the ventures that Francisco Pizarro, Diego de Almagro and the priest Hernando de Luque organised from 1524: the first, in 1524 to 1525, achieved almost nothing, while the second, begun in 1526, reached Tumbes in 1528 and brought back gold, cloth and llamas that proved a wealthy state lay further south. The route also worked in reverse, so that treasure would later be carried by mule train across the isthmus to the Caribbean for the fleets to Seville. A top-level answer explains Panama as infrastructure rather than conquest: it took no empire itself, but without it the assault on the Inca empire had no starting point, and that is exactly the kind of claim an importance question rewards."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1(a): turning the massacre at Cholula into one explained consequence","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(a): 'Explain one consequence of the massacre at Cholula in October 1519.' [4 marks]. Question 1 is printed in two parts, each on a separate event and each marked on its own out of 4, so this part needs one consequence, analysed and supported with specific detail. The information you are working from: Cholula was a large holy city allied to the Aztecs, and it stood on the road from Tlaxcala to the passes into the valley of Mexico; Cortes marched there in October 1519 with his Spaniards and thousands of Tlaxcalan warriors; Malintzin, his Nahuatl-speaking interpreter, told him the Cholulans meant to attack his men in the city; Cortes had the lords and priests seized and his men and the Tlaxcalans killed thousands of Cholulans in the main enclosure, Cortes himself claiming three thousand dead in three hours, though the accounts differ and historians dispute whether the plot was real; Cholula afterwards submitted and supplied the expedition; the army crossed the passes and Montezuma received Cortes in Tenochtitlan on 8 November 1519.","steps":["Decide which of the two things that happened at Cholula you are treating as the cause, because the accounts disagree about one of them: not the alleged plot, which is disputed, but the killing itself and the news of it.","Choose a consequence that is a change of state rather than the next event in the story: after Cholula the towns between the Tlaxcalan border and the lake stopped weighing whether to resist and began sending envoys, food and porters instead.","Support it with precise detail: Cholula was the last great city on the road, it submitted and provisioned the army after the killing, and within about three weeks Cortes was being received inside Tenochtitlan on 8 November rather than fought at the edge of the valley.","Make the link the grid calls analysis: because word of what had been done travelled ahead of the army, fear did the work that fighting would have cost Cortes men he could not replace, so he entered the Aztec capital with his force intact and used the same method again."],"cues":["Which part of what happened at Cholula is the cause you can safely explain?","What changed in the behaviour of the towns on the road afterwards?","Which precise detail shows how quickly the road to the valley opened?","Why did the news of the killing matter as much as the killing?"]},{"title":"Question 1(b): one consequence of the alliance with the Totonacs of Cempoala","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(b): 'Explain one consequence of the alliance between Cortes and the Totonacs of Cempoala in 1519.' [4 marks]. This part is marked on its own out of 4 and must analyse one consequence with specific support. The information you are working from: Cempoala was a Totonac town near the coast that paid tribute to the Aztecs; Cortes reached it in June 1519 and its ruler, whom the Spaniards nicknamed the fat cacique, complained of the tribute and of the taking of people for sacrifice; when Aztec tribute collectors arrived Cortes had them seized, which put the Totonacs in open rebellion against Montezuma and left them dependent on Spanish protection; the Totonacs helped to build Villa Rica de la Vera Cruz nearby; when Cortes marched inland in August 1519 Totonac warriors and porters went with him, in numbers the accounts give differently.","steps":["Name what the alliance cost the Totonacs before choosing anything, because that is what makes it more than a friendly welcome: once their lord had let Montezuma's collectors be arrested he could not go back to paying tribute.","Pick the consequence that reaches furthest rather than the most obvious one: Cortes learned at Cempoala that the empire was held together by tribute and resentment, so it could be taken apart town by town instead of beaten in one battle.","Support it with specific detail: the town that fed and housed his men in June was a tributary of Montezuma, its people were helping to build the Spanish settlement on the coast, and its warriors and porters were on the road inland with him by August.","Explain the link the grid rewards: because a subject people had already changed sides before the army left the coast, Cortes could offer the same bargain to every resentful city further inland, and the force that climbed to the valley grew steadily more Indigenous and less Spanish."],"cues":["What did the seizure of the tribute collectors do to the Totonacs' choices?","Which lesson did Cortes carry inland from Cempoala?","Which two months show how fast the alliance turned into men on the march?","Why does an alliance made on the coast matter to what happened in the valley?"]},{"title":"Question 2: building the spine of a narrative account on the voyage of Magellan and Elcano","setup":"Booklet P, Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the key events of the voyage of Magellan and Elcano in the years 1519-22.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the passage found at the southern tip of South America (1520); the death of Magellan at Mactan (1521). You must also use information of your own. The mark scheme gives no access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content. The events available to you: Charles I backed a Portuguese captain who promised a westward route to the Spice Islands inside Spain's half of the world under the Treaty of Tordesillas of 1494; five ships left Sanlucar de Barrameda on 20 September 1519; a mutiny was put down in Patagonia and one ship was wrecked; the fleet found the passage at the southern tip of South America late in 1520; the Pacific crossing took months and the crews starved before they reached the islands in March 1521; Magellan was killed at Mactan on 27 April 1521; the Victoria came home under Juan Sebastian Elcano on 6 September 1522 with eighteen men aboard; Spain's claim to the islands later named the Philippines dated from this voyage.","steps":["Set the boundaries the dates give you: the account begins with the fleet leaving Sanlucar de Barrameda in September 1519 and closes with the Victoria's return in September 1522, so nothing before the commission and nothing after the homecoming except a final sentence.","Choose four episodes rather than everything available: the commission and the sailing, the mutiny in Patagonia and the search for a passage, the Pacific crossing and the landfall of March 1521, and Mactan and the voyage home under Elcano.","Name which aspects are your own material, since the two bullets alone cannot reach the top level: the five ships that sailed on 20 September 1519, the mutiny put down in Patagonia, and the single ship that came home under Elcano on 6 September 1522.","Write the joins and not just the episodes: the promise of a westward route inside Spain's half of the world explains why Charles paid for the voyage, the passage found late in 1520 turned that promise into a route, the length of the Pacific crossing cost the fleet its crews, and Magellan's death at Mactan left Elcano to finish what Magellan had begun.","Close with what the sequence amounted to: one ship out of five came home, but Spain now knew that the world could be sailed round, that the Pacific was enormous, and that it had a claim in Asia it still held in 1529."],"cues":["Where should this account start and stop?","Which four moments carry the story, and which details are decoration?","Which of your chosen aspects are not printed as bullets?","What is the connective sentence between each pair of episodes?","In one sentence, what did the whole voyage change?"]},{"title":"Question 3: turning Malintzin's work as interpreter into an argument about importance","setup":"Booklet P, Question 3 prints three bullets and you answer two, each marked separately out of 8. The bullet here: 'The importance of Malintzin as interpreter and negotiator for the Spanish conquest of Mexico.' (8 marks) No stimulus points are printed and no cap applies. The information you are working from: Malintzin was one of twenty women handed over to Cortes at Potonchan in Tabasco in March 1519 after the fighting there, and was baptised Marina; she spoke Nahuatl, the language of the Aztec empire, and a Maya language; Jeronimo de Aguilar, a Spaniard shipwrecked on the Yucatan coast in 1511 and held for years among the Maya, could turn her Maya into Spanish, so the two of them together made a chain of translation, and she later learned Spanish herself; she carried the negotiations that turned the Tlaxcalans from enemies into allies; at Cholula she reported that the city meant to attack the Spaniards; she spoke for Cortes when Montezuma received him on 8 November 1519; the Nahua painters who recorded the conquest show her standing beside Cortes and doing the talking; she bore him a son, Martin, in 1522.","steps":["Read the bullet for its two halves: the importance of a person, for a conquest, so the answer has to weigh what the conquest could not have done without her rather than list what she could do.","Start from the problem she solved, because importance is measured against the alternative: a few hundred Spaniards who could not speak to a Nahua city could threaten it and fight it, but they could not bargain with it, and every ally Cortes gained was gained by bargaining.","Develop one moment at length instead of listing several: the Tlaxcalans were beaten into an alliance and then talked into one, and the talking was hers, so the translation chain delivered the army with which the rest of the war was fought.","Show the second kind of importance, which is intelligence: at Cholula she was the one person on the Spanish side who could hear what a Nahuatl-speaking city was saying about its guests, and Cortes acted on what she told him.","Judge it rather than assert it: she commanded nothing and chose nothing, yet every alliance and every parley in the war passed through her, and the Nahua artists who drew the conquest afterwards put her at Cortes's shoulder in the picture, which is how the people on the other side rated her."],"cues":["Which half of the bullet is the measure of importance here?","What could an expedition that could not talk still do, and what could it not do?","Which single moment is worth developing at length?","What did the Spaniards gain besides the ability to speak?","How do you rate the importance of someone who gave no orders?"]},{"title":"Question 3: the importance of the encomienda in New Spain","setup":"Booklet P, Question 3, the bullet you have chosen: 'The importance of the encomienda for the Indigenous people of New Spain.' (8 marks) Each chosen bullet is marked separately out of 8, and the top level needs analysis of importance in a coherent line of reasoning. The information you are working from: an encomienda was a grant of the tribute and the labour of named Indigenous towns to a Spaniard, who was supposed in return to protect them and have them instructed in Christianity, and it was not a grant of the land; Cortes granted encomiendas to his followers in his first years as Governor although the Crown had instructed him not to, and Charles ordered him in 1523 to revoke them, an order Cortes did not carry out and argued the Crown out of; the towns kept their own lords, who now assessed and collected for an encomendero instead of for Tenochtitlan; the Crown went on trying for decades to limit the grants and stop them passing by inheritance; and the demands were made on a population that was already falling after the smallpox of 1520.","steps":["Fix what the grant actually transferred before judging it, because the whole argument turns on it: not land, but other people's goods and work, so the Indigenous town stayed where it was and the burden landed on it.","Take the first measure of importance, which is continuity: tribute had gone to Tenochtitlan before the conquest and was still assessed by the same local lords afterwards, so for many villages what changed was the name of the man it went to.","Then take the measure that cuts the other way: an encomendero could demand labour as well as goods, and he had no stake in the town beyond what it produced, so the demands were heavier, less predictable and backed by nobody the town could appeal to.","Set it against the other thing happening at the same time: the demands were falling on a population that epidemic disease was reducing year by year, so the same tribute was owed by fewer people and the survivors worked more rather than less.","Judge it: the encomienda mattered most because it settled who gained from the conquest on the ground, and the best measure of that is how long the Crown spent trying to take back what had been given away in the first years, and how little of it it got."],"cues":["What exactly was granted, and what was not?","What did not change for a tributary town after the conquest?","What did change, and why was the new burden worse?","Which other development made that burden heavier still?","By what test do you judge that the system mattered?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Who claimed the South Sea for the Crown of Castile, and in what year?","a":"Vasco Nunez de Balboa, in 1513, after crossing the isthmus from Darien."},{"q":"Which people fought Cortes in September 1519 and then allied with him, and why were they willing to?","a":"The Tlaxcalans; they had never been conquered by the Aztecs and had been at war with them for years, so an alliance against Tenochtitlan suited them."},{"q":"What happened on the night of 30 June 1520?","a":"The Noche Triste: the Spanish fought their way out of Tenochtitlan along the causeways, losing most of their men, their guns and nearly all their treasure."},{"q":"What replaced Cortes's personal rule of New Spain, and when did it take office?","a":"The first Audiencia of New Spain, created in 1527 under Nuno de Guzman, which took office in December 1528."}]},"room":{"facts":["Booklet P is worth 32 marks in three questions: Question 1, printed in two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each 'Explain one consequence of...' for 4 marks and each on a separate event or development; Question 2, 'Write a narrative account analysing...', 8 marks; Question 3, 'Explain two of the following: the importance of ... for ...', two chosen parts at 8 marks each, 16 in all.","Only Question 2 prints stimulus points, as two bullets followed by 'You must also use information of your own.' The cap printed in its mark scheme is 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' There is no cap at Level 2, and Questions 1 and 3 print no bullets and carry no cap at all.","Each part of Question 1 is marked on its own two-level grid out of 4: Level 2 (3-4) needs features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific supporting information added. Describing the event, however accurately, stays in Level 1.","Question 3 is marked twice on a three-level grid out of 8, and Level 3 (6-8) needs analysis of importance in a line of reasoning that is coherent and logically structured: what difference the event, person or development made to later situations, not an account of what happened.","Timing: Paper 2 lasts 1 hour 50 minutes for 64 marks, about 55 minutes a booklet, so booklet P's 32 marks work out at roughly a minute a mark - about 12 minutes on Question 1's two parts, 13 on Question 2 and 14 on each part of Question 3, leaving a little to check.","There are no sources and no interpretations in booklet P, and no marks anywhere on Paper 2 for spelling, punctuation and grammar; every mark comes from precise knowledge and from analysis."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of Balboa's claim of Spanish authority over the Pacific in 1513. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2, marked on the 4-mark consequence grid; Level 2 (3-4) needs features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific support. Credit the founding of Panama on the Pacific shore in 1519 and the voyages launched from it, the shift of Spanish attention from the Caribbean to the isthmus, or the legal claim to lands on an ocean Spain had not yet sailed.","sol":"One consequence was that the isthmus became the base for Spanish expansion on a second ocean. Because Balboa claimed the South Sea and every land its waters touched for Castile in September 1513, a settlement on that shore turned a claim on paper into reach, and Pedrarias Davila founded Panama on the Pacific coast in 1519. From there Spain could fit out ships without sailing round South America, which is why the voyages Pizarro and Almagro made southward from 1524, and the attack on the Inca empire that followed, all began in that harbour.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["A consequence is something that was true afterwards because of the claim, so ask what Spain could attempt in 1520 that it could not attempt in 1512.","Balboa claimed an ocean, not a province; think about what a claim like that is worth without a port on the water.","Three moves in four lines: name the consequence, prove it with a named place and a date, then explain why it mattered for what came later."],"codes":["P1-2 — 1 The start of an empire: Balboa's claim of Spanish authority over the Pacific (1513)","P1-2 — 3 Impact of Spain in the Americas: The founding and significance of a Spanish base at Panama"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of the Spanish conquest of Cuba. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit Cuba's use as the base for the mainland expeditions of 1517, 1518 and 1519, the establishment of Velazquez's governorship and the encomienda, or the collapse of the Taino population under forced labour and disease.","sol":"One consequence was that Spain gained the base from which it invaded the mainland. Diego Velazquez had taken the island by 1515, founding Baracoa, Santiago de Cuba and Havana, and as governor he could grant land and Indigenous labour, which drew in settlers with money and ships. Because Cuba's gold was soon worked out and those settlers wanted more than the island could give them, Velazquez was able to send Hernandez de Cordoba to Yucatan in 1517, Grijalva along the Mexican coast in 1518 and Cortes in 1519. Without Cuba's harbours, men and credit the invasion of Mexico could not have been mounted.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what Spain had after 1515 that it did not have in 1510, and where the men who invaded Mexico came from.","The strongest consequence is not what happened on the island but what the island made possible off it.","Name the consequence, prove it with two named expeditions and their years, then explain the link to Mexico."],"codes":["P1-2 — 1 The start of an empire: The conquest of Cuba","P1-2 — 2 The conquest of Mexico: Cortes's expedition to Mexico in 1519"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the alliance between Cortes and the Tlaxcalans in 1519. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the transformation of the invading force into a largely Indigenous army, the safe base that allowed the Spanish to survive the Noche Triste, the building of the brigantines at Tlaxcala, or the submission of other cities after Cholula.","sol":"One consequence was that the Spanish gained the army that made the conquest possible. The Tlaxcalans had fought Cortes hard through September 1519 and then joined him, because they had been at war with the Aztecs for years and had never been conquered. From that point the force marching on Tenochtitlan was overwhelmingly Indigenous, with the Spanish as a small hard core. It also gave them somewhere to go when they were beaten: after the Noche Triste of June 1520 they retreated to Tlaxcala, rebuilt there, and returned with tens of thousands of Tlaxcalan warriors for the siege of 1521.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about numbers first: how many Spaniards were there, and who actually made up the army that reached the valley of Mexico?","A consequence can be something that happened a year later, so look at where the Spanish went after they were driven out of the city.","Name the consequence, support it with what the Tlaxcalans supplied and when, then explain why it decided the campaign."],"codes":["P1-2 — 2 The conquest of Mexico: Key events of the Spanish conquest of Mexico","P1-2 — 2 The conquest of Mexico: Cortes's expedition to Mexico in 1519"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of the founding of Panama in 1519. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the fitting out of Pacific voyages without a passage round South America, Pizarro's expeditions of 1524-28 and the evidence brought back from Tumbes, or the later use of the isthmus as the treasure route to the Caribbean.","sol":"One consequence was that Spain could explore and attack the Pacific coast of South America. Panama, founded by Pedrarias Davila on 15 August 1519, was the first European town on that ocean, and because the isthmus could be crossed on foot in a few days, ships, timber and men could be assembled there instead of sailed round a continent. Every early voyage southward began in its harbour, including the expeditions Pizarro, Almagro and Luque organised from 1524, whose second voyage reached Tumbes in 1528 and brought back the gold and cloth that proved a rich state lay further south.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Look at the map before the history: what does a port on the Pacific save you that a port on the Caribbean cannot?","The consequence to choose is about what later expeditions were able to do, not about the town itself.","Name the consequence, support it with the foundation date and one named voyage, then explain the link to the conquest of Peru."],"codes":["P1-2 — 3 Impact of Spain in the Americas: The founding and significance of a Spanish base at Panama"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the voyage of Magellan and Elcano in the years 1519-22. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the smallpox epidemic that reached Mexico in 1520. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4 on the consequence grid, so each part needs one consequence analysed with specific support. In 1(a) credit Spain's claim to the islands later named the Philippines, the proof that the world could be circumnavigated and that the Pacific was vast, or the renewed dispute with Portugal over the Moluccas settled in 1529. In 1(b) credit the death of Cuitlahuac and the loss of Aztec leadership, the weakening of the city's defence before the siege of 1521, or the wider collapse of the Indigenous population.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that Spain gained a claim in Asia that it kept. Magellan's fleet reached the islands in March 1521 by sailing west, so Spain could argue that they lay in its half of the world under the Treaty of Tordesillas. Magellan himself was killed at Mactan on 27 April 1521, but the Victoria came home under Elcano on 6 September 1522 and the route was now known. That is why, when Charles sold his claim to the Moluccas to Portugal in 1529, he kept the islands later named the Philippines, which Spain went on to colonise. 1(b) One consequence was that the defence of Tenochtitlan was broken before the siege began. Smallpox arrived with Narvaez's men in 1520 and swept the valley from that autumn, killing warriors and farmers who had no immunity to it. It probably also killed Cuitlahuac, who was elected ruler after the Spanish were driven out in June 1520, so Cuauhtemoc inherited a weakened city in the middle of an epidemic. Because the Spanish were largely immune and were rebuilding in Tlaxcala at the time, the disease reversed the balance between the two sides in the months before May 1521.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate parts on two separate things, an ocean voyage and an epidemic, so neither answer can borrow from the other.","For 1(a) a voyage that lost four ships out of five still changed what Spain could claim: ask what it proved and what it left Spain holding.","For 1(b) the strongest consequence is not the number of deaths but who died and when, so look at who was ruling Tenochtitlan in the autumn of 1520."],"codes":["P1-2 — 1 The start of an empire: The voyage of Magellan and Spanish claim to the Philippines","P1-2 — 2 The conquest of Mexico: The capture of Tenochtitlan and the Aztec surrender","P1-2 — 3 Impact of Spain in the Americas: The consequences of the Spanish invasion for the Aztecs"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the seizure of Montezuma by Cortes in November 1519. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the Noche Triste of 30 June 1520. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4. In 1(a) credit Spanish rule of the capital through the emperor's authority, the collection of gold and the acknowledgement of Charles V, or the destruction of Montezuma's standing that allowed Cuitlahuac to lead a rising. In 1(b) credit the loss of men, guns and treasure, the retreat to Tlaxcala, or the change of method that produced the siege of 1521.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that Cortes could rule Tenochtitlan without having to take it. Having entered the city on 8 November 1519, he seized Montezuma within a fortnight and kept him in the Spanish quarters, issuing orders through him, gathering gold and having him acknowledge the authority of Charles V. Because the emperor's word still carried weight, a few hundred Spaniards held a capital of well over a hundred thousand people for more than half a year. It also destroyed Montezuma's standing, which is why the city turned to Cuitlahuac when it rose in 1520. 1(b) One consequence was that the Spanish had to change the way they fought. On the night of 30 June 1520 they lost most of their men, their guns and nearly all the gold they had taken, so an assault from inside the city was no longer possible. Retreating to Tlaxcala after the battle of Otumba, Cortes spent the winter gathering Indigenous allies and having thirteen brigantines built that could be carried to the lake. The defeat therefore produced the method of 1521: control the water, blockade the causeways and starve the city out.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate events, a hostage-taking and a defeat, so plan two short answers rather than one long story.","For 1(a) ask what a hostage emperor let a few hundred men do that they could not have done by fighting.","For 1(b) a defeat is a consequence only if you say what changed because of it: compare how the Spanish tried to hold the city in 1520 with how they attacked it in 1521."],"codes":["P1-2 — 2 The conquest of Mexico: Key events of the Spanish conquest of Mexico","P1-2 — 2 The conquest of Mexico: The capture of Tenochtitlan and the Aztec surrender"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the fall of Tenochtitlan in August 1521. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of Cortes's expedition to Honduras in the years 1524-26. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4. In 1(a) credit the end of the Aztec empire and the redirection of tribute to encomenderos, the rebuilding of the city as Mexico City and the naming of New Spain, or Cortes's appointment as Governor and Captain-General. In 1(b) credit the collapse of Cortes's authority in Mexico City, the execution of Cuauhtemoc in 1525, or the Crown's decision to govern through the first Audiencia.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that an Aztec empire was replaced by a Spanish one. With Cuauhtemoc captured on 13 August 1521 the tribute that had fed Tenochtitlan passed to the conquerors, who granted encomiendas over the same towns, and the ruined city was rebuilt on its own site as Mexico City, with a cathedral raised on the precinct of the Great Temple. Cortes named his conquests New Spain, and Charles V confirmed him as Governor and Captain-General in October 1522, so within about a year conquest had become government. 1(b) One consequence was that Cortes lost control of New Spain. He marched overland to Honduras from 1524 to 1526 to put down Cristobal de Olid's rebellion, taking Cuauhtemoc with him and executing him on the way in 1525. The march took far longer than expected, officials in Mexico City quarrelled over his government and he was reported dead, so when he returned in 1526 he was suspended and investigated. That is why the Crown decided to govern through a royal court instead, creating the first Audiencia in 1527.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate events, the fall of a city and an expedition to another province, so keep the two answers apart.","For 1(a) the best consequence is a change of system rather than a change of ruler: ask where the tribute went afterwards.","For 1(b) ask what happens to a governor's authority when he spends two years unreachable in the forest."],"codes":["P1-2 — 2 The conquest of Mexico: The capture of Tenochtitlan and the Aztec surrender","P1-2 — 3 Impact of Spain in the Americas: The consequences of the Spanish invasion for the Aztecs","P1-2 — 3 Impact of Spain in the Americas: Cortes's actions as Governor and Captain-General"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the creation of the first Audiencia of New Spain. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of Pizarro's voyages from Panama in the years 1524-28. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4. In 1(a) credit the end of Cortes's personal rule and his departure for Spain in 1528, the transfer of patronage and encomienda grants to royal officials, or the notoriety of Nuno de Guzman and the court's replacement. In 1(b) credit the evidence of Inca wealth brought back from Tumbes in 1528, the Capitulacion of Toledo of 1529, or the establishment of Panama as the base for the conquest of Peru.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that personal rule by a conqueror was brought to an end. The Audiencia, created in 1527, took office in December 1528 with judges appointed by the Crown and Nuno de Guzman as its president, so law, patronage and the granting of encomiendas no longer depended on Cortes. He sailed for Spain in 1528 and, although Charles V made him Marques del Valle de Oaxaca in 1529, he never recovered the governorship. 1(b) One consequence was that Spain learned that a rich state lay south of Panama. The first voyage, of 1524 to 1525, achieved almost nothing, but the second, begun in 1526, reached Tumbes on the edge of the Inca empire in 1528 and brought back gold, fine cloth and llamas. That evidence turned a speculative venture into one the Crown was willing to back, which is why Pizarro went to Spain and obtained the Capitulacion of Toledo in 1529, naming him governor of the lands he should conquer.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate developments, one about government and one about exploration, so neither answer should mention the other.","For 1(a) ask who now decided who received land and Indigenous labour, and what that meant for the man who had been giving it away.","For 1(b) the consequence is about what Spain now knew and what it did with that knowledge, not about the hardships of the voyages."],"codes":["P1-2 — 3 Impact of Spain in the Americas: Cortes's actions as Governor and Captain-General","P1-2 — 3 Impact of Spain in the Americas: The founding and significance of a Spanish base at Panama"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the key events of Cortes's journey from Cuba to Tenochtitlan in 1519. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the founding of Villa Rica de la Vera Cruz; the alliance with Tlaxcala. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) needs a clear sequence leading to an outcome, with the linkage between events analysed, coherent and logically structured. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; own material might include the quarrel with Velazquez, Malintzin and Aguilar, the running aground of the ships, the Totonacs of Cempoala, and the massacre at Cholula.","sol":"Cortes left Cuba in February 1519 in defiance of Velazquez, who had appointed him to the expedition and then tried to cancel the commission, so his first problem was legal rather than military. After fighting at Tabasco, where he was given Malintzin, whose Nahuatl and Maya combined with Jeronimo de Aguilar's Maya to give him a working chain of translation, he landed on the Mexican coast and founded Villa Rica de la Vera Cruz in the spring; its new town council then appointed him captain, so that his authority came from the king and not from the governor of Cuba. Because he wanted no retreat he ran his ships aground, and because the Totonacs of Cempoala had told him how much the Aztecs were resented by their tributaries, he marched inland in August looking for allies rather than for gold. The Tlaxcalans fought him through September before deciding that an alliance against Tenochtitlan served them better than destroying him, and with their warriors he attacked Cholula in October, killing thousands and frightening other cities into submission. The march ended on 8 November 1519, when Montezuma received him on the causeway. The sequence had turned a disowned expedition of a few hundred Spaniards into the head of an Indigenous coalition standing in the Aztec capital.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Decide where the account begins and ends before you write: the dates run from leaving Cuba to the entry into the capital, and nothing outside that belongs.","The two bullets are only two aspects, so choose at least two more of your own from the coast and the march inland.","Between each episode write one sentence saying why the next one followed, so that the account explains rather than lists."],"codes":["P1-2 — 2 The conquest of Mexico: Cortes's expedition to Mexico in 1519","P1-2 — 2 The conquest of Mexico: Key events of the Spanish conquest of Mexico"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the key events of the Spanish conquest of Cuba in the years 1511-15. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: Diego Velazquez; the resistance of Hatuey. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) needs a clear sequence leading to an outcome with the linkage analysed. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; own material might include the crossing from Hispaniola, the founding of Baracoa, Santiago de Cuba and Havana, the encomienda system, the collapse of the Taino population, and the expeditions sent to the mainland from 1517.","sol":"The conquest began from Hispaniola, where Spanish settlement had already exhausted the easy gold and much of the Indigenous labour, so in 1511 Diego Velazquez crossed to Cuba with about 300 men under authority from Diego Colon. The first resistance was organised by Hatuey, a Taino cacique who had fled Hispaniola and knew exactly what conquest meant; because he avoided open battle and raided from the forests of the east, the Spanish advance was slow at first. His capture and burning at the stake in 1512 broke the only coordinated defence, and after that the Spanish moved by founding towns rather than by fighting battles: Baracoa in 1511, then Santiago de Cuba and Havana by 1515. Each town became the centre of an encomienda district in which Indigenous people were allotted to settlers for labour, and because that labour was used in gold washing and farming while European diseases spread, the Taino population fell sharply within a generation. The outcome was an island valuable to Spain not for its gold, which was soon worked out, but as a base: by 1517 Velazquez was using its harbours to send Hernandez de Cordoba to Yucatan, and by 1519 Cortes to Mexico.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Start from why Spain wanted Cuba at all, and what Hispaniola no longer had to offer.","Resistance, settlement and exploitation are three different aspects of the story; use all three rather than only the two bullets.","End by saying what the island had become by 1515, so that the account leads to an outcome rather than stopping."],"codes":["P1-2 — 1 The start of an empire: The conquest of Cuba"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the key events of the fall of Tenochtitlan in the years 1520-21. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the Noche Triste (30 June 1520); the thirteen brigantines. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) needs a clear sequence leading to an outcome with the linkage analysed. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; own material might include Narvaez and Cempoala, Alvarado's massacre at Toxcatl, the death of Montezuma, Otumba, the smallpox epidemic and the death of Cuitlahuac, and the cutting of the Chapultepec aqueduct.","sol":"The crisis began while Cortes was away on the coast defeating Narvaez, whom Velazquez had sent from Cuba in March 1520 to arrest him and who landed on the coast in April: in his absence Alvarado massacred Aztec nobles at the festival of Toxcatl, and the city rose. Cortes returned to find his position untenable, Montezuma died at the end of June, and on the night of 30 June 1520 the Spanish fought their way out along the causeways, losing most of their men, their guns and their treasure. Because they won a battle at Otumba in early July they were able to reach Tlaxcala, and because the Tlaxcalan alliance held they could rebuild there instead of retreating to the coast. Two things then reversed the balance. Smallpox, carried in with Narvaez's men, swept the valley from the autumn of 1520 and probably killed Cuitlahuac, who had been elected ruler after the expulsion, while Cortes had thirteen brigantines built inland and carried in pieces to the lake. When the siege began in late May 1521 the brigantines controlled the water, the causeways were blockaded and the aqueduct from Chapultepec was cut, so the city was starved as well as attacked. Cuauhtemoc was captured on 13 August 1521 and Tenochtitlan surrendered: a war lost inside the city had been won by cutting it off.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The account needs a turning point as well as a sequence: find the moment at which the Spanish stopped trying to hold the city and started trying to besiege it.","Three or four episodes is enough, but they must be joined; ask at each stage what the other side's move left the Spanish able to do.","Plan which aspects beyond the two bullets you will use, because an answer built only on the bullets cannot reach the top level."],"codes":["P1-2 — 2 The conquest of Mexico: The capture of Tenochtitlan and the Aztec surrender","P1-2 — 2 The conquest of Mexico: Key events of the Spanish conquest of Mexico","P1-2 — 3 Impact of Spain in the Americas: The consequences of the Spanish invasion for the Aztecs"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the alliance with Tlaxcala for the Spanish conquest of Mexico. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 on the 8-mark importance grid; Level 3 (6-8) needs analysis of importance in a coherent line of reasoning. No stimulus points and no cap. Credit the largely Indigenous army that marched on Tenochtitlan, the refuge after the Noche Triste, the building of the brigantines at Tlaxcala, the tens of thousands of Tlaxcalan warriors in the siege, and the privileges Tlaxcala received afterwards.","sol":"The alliance mattered first because it changed the size and the character of the invading force. The Tlaxcalans fought Cortes hard through September 1519 and then joined him, having been at war with the Aztecs for years without ever being conquered, and from that point the army that marched on Tenochtitlan was overwhelmingly Indigenous, with Spanish weapons and horses as a small hard core. Its second importance is that it allowed the conquest to survive defeat. After the Noche Triste of 30 June 1520 the Spanish had lost most of their men and their guns, and only a friendly city within reach let them stay in Mexico at all; in Tlaxcala they recruited, recovered and waited out the smallpox epidemic that was killing the Aztec leadership. Third, it supplied the means of the final campaign: the thirteen brigantines were built at Tlaxcala and carried in pieces to the lake, and tens of thousands of Tlaxcalan warriors fought in the siege of 1521. The measure of its importance is what the Spanish themselves conceded afterwards, when the Crown granted Tlaxcala privileges and exemptions given to no other Indigenous city. Without the alliance the conquest would not have been delayed but abandoned.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Importance questions ask what difference something made, so the answer must keep pointing forward to the conquest rather than describing Tlaxcala.","Look for three separate moments at which the alliance changed what was possible, not three things Tlaxcala provided.","End by judging how important it was, and find one piece of evidence that the Spanish themselves rated it highly."],"codes":["P1-2 — 2 The conquest of Mexico: Key events of the Spanish conquest of Mexico","P1-2 — 2 The conquest of Mexico: The capture of Tenochtitlan and the Aztec surrender"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of Balboa's crossing of the isthmus for Spanish expansion on the Pacific coast. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 on the 8-mark importance grid; Level 3 needs analysis of importance in a coherent line of reasoning. Credit the legal claim to the South Sea and its coasts, the proof that the isthmus could be crossed on foot, the founding of Panama in 1519, the voyages southward from 1524, and the reports of a rich kingdom to the south.","sol":"Balboa's crossing mattered because it turned a Caribbean empire into one on two oceans. Setting out from Darien on 1 September 1513, he sighted the southern ocean on 25 September and waded into it four days later, claiming the South Sea and every land its waters touched for Castile. The first importance is legal: Spain now had a title to a whole ocean and its coasts before it had sailed a mile of it, and later expeditions were authorised on the strength of that claim. The second is geographical: the crossing proved that the isthmus was narrow and could be walked, so men, supplies and shipbuilding timber could be carried over it in a few days instead of sailed round a continent. That is why Pedrarias Davila founded Panama on the Pacific shore in 1519, and why every voyage southward, including those Pizarro and Almagro made from 1524, began there. The third is what it did to Spanish ambition, since the reports of a rich kingdom to the south that Balboa first heard on this journey gave the conquistadors a target. Balboa himself gained nothing and was executed in 1519, which shows that the importance lay in the claim and the route rather than in the man.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Separate the journey from its consequences: the question is about Spanish expansion, so the crossing is only the cause.","Three different kinds of importance are available here - legal, geographical and in ambition - and using all three gives the line of reasoning.","Judge at the end, and notice that Balboa's own fate is useful evidence about where the importance actually lay."],"codes":["P1-2 — 1 The start of an empire: Balboa's claim of Spanish authority over the Pacific (1513)","P1-2 — 3 Impact of Spain in the Americas: The founding and significance of a Spanish base at Panama"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of Cortes's years as Governor and Captain-General for the government of New Spain. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 on the 8-mark importance grid; Level 3 needs analysis of importance in a coherent line of reasoning. Credit the rebuilding of Mexico City on the site of Tenochtitlan, the granting of encomiendas against royal instructions, the arrival of the twelve Franciscans in 1524 and the mass conversions, the expeditions to Guatemala and Honduras, and the Crown's replacement of personal rule by the first Audiencia in 1528.","sol":"Cortes's years as governor, from his appointment by Charles V in October 1522 to his departure in 1528, mattered because they fixed the shape of the colony. He had Tenochtitlan demolished and rebuilt on its own site as Mexico City, on a Spanish grid with a cathedral raised on the precinct of the Great Temple, so the capital of the conquered empire became the capital of New Spain rather than being abandoned for a new one. He granted encomiendas of Indigenous towns to his followers although the Crown had forbidden it, which set the labour system that governed Indigenous life for generations. He brought in cattle, sugar and mining and welcomed the twelve Franciscans who arrived in 1524, beginning the mass conversion of the Nahua. His governorship was also important for what it provoked. Sending Alvarado to Guatemala and Olid to Honduras pushed Spanish rule far beyond Mexico, but his own march to Honduras between 1524 and 1526 left his government in chaos, and the Crown concluded that a conqueror who could distribute land and labour was too powerful to leave in charge. That is why the first Audiencia, created in 1527, took office in December 1528: government by royal officials replaced government by a conqueror.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The bullet is about the government of New Spain, so every point must end on how the colony was run, not on what Cortes did.","Two kinds of importance are in play: what he built that lasted, and what he provoked the Crown into doing.","Finish with the Audiencia, and say what its arrival proves about how important his rule had been."],"codes":["P1-2 — 3 Impact of Spain in the Americas: Cortes's actions as Governor and Captain-General","P1-2 — 3 Impact of Spain in the Americas: The consequences of the Spanish invasion for the Aztecs"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In which year did Balboa claim the South Sea for the Crown of Castile?","a":"1513."},{"q":"Who led the Spanish conquest of Cuba from 1511 and became its governor?","a":"Diego Velazquez de Cuellar."},{"q":"On what date did Tenochtitlan surrender to Cortes and his allies?","a":"13 August 1521."},{"q":"Who was president of the first Audiencia of New Spain, which took office in December 1528?","a":"Nuno de Guzman."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Balboa's claim of the South Sea","back":"Vasco Nunez de Balboa crossed the isthmus from Darien and on 29 September 1513 waded into the Pacific, claiming the South Sea and every land its waters touched for the Crown of Castile.","code":"P1-2 — 1 The start of an empire: Balboa's claim of Spanish authority over the Pacific (1513)","open":false},{"front":"Diego Velazquez de Cuellar","back":"Led the conquest of Cuba from 1511 with about 300 men, became its governor, founded Santiago de Cuba, and sent the expeditions of 1517, 1518 and 1519 to the mainland.","code":"P1-2 — 1 The start of an empire: The conquest of Cuba","open":false},{"front":"Hatuey","back":"A Taino cacique who had fled Hispaniola and led the resistance to the Spanish in eastern Cuba; captured and burned at the stake in 1512.","code":"P1-2 — 1 The start of an empire: The conquest of Cuba","open":false},{"front":"The Victoria","back":"The only one of Magellan's five ships to complete the voyage round the world, reaching Spain on 6 September 1522 under Juan Sebastian Elcano with eighteen men (the San Antonio had deserted and sailed home in 1521).","code":"P1-2 — 1 The start of an empire: The voyage of Magellan and Spanish claim to the Philippines","open":false},{"front":"Mactan, 27 April 1521","back":"Where Magellan was killed, fighting on behalf of a local ruler he had allied with, in the islands Spain later called the Philippines.","code":"P1-2 — 1 The start of an empire: The voyage of Magellan and Spanish claim to the Philippines","open":false},{"front":"Malintzin (Dona Marina, La Malinche)","back":"A Nahua woman given to Cortes at Tabasco in 1519; she spoke Nahuatl and Maya and, with Jeronimo de Aguilar, translated for him and supplied intelligence on Aztec politics.","code":"P1-2 — 2 The conquest of Mexico: Cortes's expedition to Mexico in 1519","open":false},{"front":"Villa Rica de la Vera Cruz","back":"The town Cortes founded on the Mexican coast in 1519; its council appointed him captain so that his authority came from the king rather than from Velazquez.","code":"P1-2 — 2 The conquest of Mexico: Cortes's expedition to Mexico in 1519","open":false},{"front":"Give one reason why the Tlaxcalans allied with Cortes in 1519","back":"They had been at war with the Aztecs for years and resented Aztec power; they had never been conquered and wanted to stay independent; they had failed to destroy the Spanish in battle and judged an alliance more useful.","code":"P1-2 — 2 The conquest of Mexico: Key events of the Spanish conquest of Mexico","open":true},{"front":"8 November 1519","back":"Montezuma II received Cortes on the causeway into Tenochtitlan; within a fortnight the Spanish had seized him and were ruling through him.","code":"P1-2 — 2 The conquest of Mexico: Key events of the Spanish conquest of Mexico","open":false},{"front":"The Noche Triste","back":"The night of 30 June 1520, when the Spanish fought their way out of Tenochtitlan along the causeways, losing most of their men, their guns and nearly all their treasure.","code":"P1-2 — 2 The conquest of Mexico: The capture of Tenochtitlan and the Aztec surrender","open":false},{"front":"Cuitlahuac","back":"Montezuma's successor, elected after the expulsion of 30 June 1520, which he had helped to lead; he reigned about 80 days and died later that year, probably of smallpox. Cuauhtemoc then became the last Aztec emperor.","code":"P1-2 — 2 The conquest of Mexico: The capture of Tenochtitlan and the Aztec surrender","open":false},{"front":"13 August 1521","back":"Cuauhtemoc was captured and Tenochtitlan surrendered, after a siege of nearly three months by thirteen brigantines and tens of thousands of Indigenous allies.","code":"P1-2 — 2 The conquest of Mexico: The capture of Tenochtitlan and the Aztec surrender","open":false},{"front":"The first Audiencia of New Spain","back":"The royal court created in 1527 to replace Cortes's personal rule; it took office in December 1528 under Nuno de Guzman, whose corruption and cruelty became notorious.","code":"P1-2 — 3 Impact of Spain in the Americas: Cortes's actions as Governor and Captain-General","open":false},{"front":"The Indigenous population of central Mexico after 1521","back":"It fell by the great majority within a century: smallpox in 1520, measles in the 1530s and the cocoliztli epidemics of the 1540s and 1570s, made worse by forced labour under the encomienda.","code":"P1-2 — 3 Impact of Spain in the Americas: The consequences of the Spanish invasion for the Aztecs","open":false},{"front":"Panama, 15 August 1519","back":"Founded by Pedrarias Davila as the first European town on the Pacific coast of the Americas and capital of Castilla del Oro; the base for every early voyage southward.","code":"P1-2 — 3 Impact of Spain in the Americas: The founding and significance of a Spanish base at Panama","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph - Level 3 (Paper 2 booklet P Question 2, narrative account, 8 marks)","items":["Question: 'Write a narrative account analysing the key events in the rebuilding of Tenochtitlan as the Spanish city of Mexico in the years 1521-24.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the demolition of the Great Temple; the arrival of the twelve Franciscan friars (1524). You must also use information of your own. This is the middle stretch of the account, carrying it from the decision to rebuild on the ruins to the first Franciscans at work in the new city.","Because Cortes chose to rebuild on the island itself rather than govern from Coyoacan, where he had settled after the surrender of 13 August 1521, everything that followed was done on top of a city his own siege had wrecked, and the wreckage became the building material. The ceremonial precinct was cleared first: the Great Temple was pulled down and its stone carried off for the Spanish buildings that replaced it, so that a church rose on the precinct where the shrines of Huitzilopochtli and Tlaloc had stood, and the site itself announced which religion had won. A Spanish grid of streets was laid out around a central square, with the Spaniards in the middle and the Nahua in the districts around them, and the clearing and building were done by Indigenous labour drawn from the towns of the valley, the same towns that were feeding the new city. By 4 July 1523 it was a chartered municipality, when Charles granted it a coat of arms, which mattered because it made the conquerors' capital the same kind of legal town as a city in Castile rather than a garrison on a ruin. Then in 1524 the twelve Franciscans landed on the coast and walked up to it, and the Franciscan accounts say Cortes knelt to them in public in front of Nahua lords. Demolition, grid and friars were one policy: the old capital was not to be replaced elsewhere but written over where it stood.","Why this is Level 3: the material is organised into a clear sequence leading to an outcome - the decision to stay on the site, the demolition, the grid and the labour, the charter, the friars - and each step is joined to the next by an explained link rather than by the word 'then'. The knowledge is precise and relevant (13 August 1521, Coyoacan, the Great Temple, the coat of arms of 4 July 1523, the twelve Franciscans of 1524), and the answer addresses far more than three aspects of content, so the Level 3 cap does not bite."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph - Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["The same question: 'Write a narrative account analysing the key events in the rebuilding of Tenochtitlan as the Spanish city of Mexico in the years 1521-24.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the demolition of the Great Temple; the arrival of the twelve Franciscan friars (1524). You must also use information of your own. The same stretch of the story.","After the Aztecs surrendered in 1521 Cortes decided to build a new city where Tenochtitlan had been. The Spanish knocked down the Great Temple and used the stones for their own buildings and put a church there. Then they made new streets in straight lines with a big square in the middle, and the Indigenous people did the building work for them. The city was given a coat of arms by the king. After that the twelve Franciscan friars arrived in 1524 and started converting people to Christianity, and Cortes welcomed them. Mexico City then became the capital of New Spain.","Why this is Level 2 and not Level 3: it meets the Level 2 descriptor, 'a narrative is given, showing some organisation of material into a sequence of events leading to an outcome', with 'some analysis of the linkage between them', because the events are in the right order and one or two are loosely joined. It misses the Level 3 descriptor, which needs an account that 'analyses the linkage' between events and is 'coherent and logically structured': nothing here explains why rebuilding on the same site mattered, why the temple's own stone was used, or what a royal charter did for the new town. The knowledge is also thin - no month or day anywhere, no Coyoacan, no named gods, no square - so AO1 stays at 'some knowledge and understanding of the events'."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Balboa's claim of Spanish authority over the Pacific (1513)","items":["Vasco Nunez de Balboa: a leader of the settlers at Santa Maria la Antigua del Darien, the first lasting Spanish town on the American mainland, founded in 1510 on the Caribbean side of the isthmus.","He set out from Darien on 1 September 1513 with about 190 Spaniards and hundreds of Indigenous porters and guides, following reports of a great water and a rich kingdom to the south.","He sighted the ocean from high ground on 25 September 1513.","On 29 September 1513 he waded into the surf in armour and claimed the South Sea, and every land its waters touched, for the Crown of Castile.","The name Mar del Sur, South Sea, came from the lie of the coast at Panama, where the isthmus runs east to west and the ocean lies to the south.","Pedrarias Davila arrived in 1514 as governor of Castilla del Oro, displaced Balboa and had him executed in 1519.","Why it matters: the claim gave Spain a legal title to an ocean it had never sailed, and made the narrow isthmus the most valuable ground in the Indies."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the conquest of Cuba (1511-15)","items":["Diego Velazquez de Cuellar crossed from Hispaniola in 1511 with roughly 300 men, under authority from Diego Colon.","Hatuey, a Taino cacique who had fled Hispaniola, led the resistance in the east by raiding rather than by open battle; he was captured and burned at the stake in 1512.","Baracoa, founded in 1511, was the first Spanish town on the island; Santiago de Cuba and Havana had been founded by 1515.","Velazquez became governor and distributed the island's people among the settlers under the encomienda system.","The Taino population collapsed within a generation under forced labour, hunger and European disease.","Cuba's gold was soon worked out, so its lasting value to Spain was as a base with harbours, men, ships and credit.","From Cuba Velazquez sent Francisco Hernandez de Cordoba to Yucatan in 1517, Juan de Grijalva along the Mexican coast in 1518, and Cortes in 1519.","Why it matters: Cuba supplied the expedition that invaded Mexico, and set the pattern of encomienda, disease and population collapse that Mexico repeated."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the voyage of Magellan and the Spanish claim to the Philippines","items":["Ferdinand Magellan was Portuguese but sailed for Charles I of Spain, promising a western route to the Spice Islands inside Spain's half of the world under the Treaty of Tordesillas of 1494.","Five ships left Sanlucar de Barrameda on 20 September 1519.","A mutiny was put down in Patagonia and one ship was wrecked; the fleet found the passage at the southern tip of South America late in 1520.","The Pacific crossing took months and the crews starved before they reached the islands in March 1521.","Magellan was killed at Mactan on 27 April 1521, fighting on behalf of a local ruler he had allied with.","One ship, the Victoria, under Juan Sebastian Elcano, reached Spain on 6 September 1522 with eighteen men aboard: the first circumnavigation of the world.","Spain's claim to the islands later named the Philippines dated from this voyage; in 1529 Charles sold his claim to the Moluccas to Portugal but kept the Philippines, which Spain settled decades later.","Why it matters: the voyage proved the world could be sailed round and that the Pacific was enormous, and it gave Spain a legal foothold in Asia."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Cortes's expedition to Mexico in 1519","items":["Hernan Cortes, born at Medellin in Extremadura, sailed to Hispaniola in 1504, served in the conquest of Cuba in 1511, and became an encomendero and magistrate of Santiago de Cuba.","Velazquez appointed him to lead the third expedition to Mexico in 1518, after Grijalva brought back gold, and then tried to revoke the commission.","Cortes sailed from Cuba in February 1519 with about eleven ships, some 500 soldiers, about sixteen horses and a few cannon.","At Tabasco he was given twenty women, among them Malintzin - Dona Marina to the Spanish, La Malinche in Mexico - who spoke Nahuatl and Maya.","Jeronimo de Aguilar, a Spaniard shipwrecked in Yucatan who had learned Maya, completed the chain of translation with her.","He founded Villa Rica de la Vera Cruz in the spring of 1519 and had its town council appoint him captain, so his authority came from the king and not from Velazquez.","He ran his ships aground later that summer so that no one could sail back to Cuba.","Motives: gold and land, the rewards of encomienda, royal service, titles and reputation, and the conversion of the Indigenous population."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Montezuma and the war between the Aztecs and the Tlaxcalans","items":["The Aztec empire ruled through tribute rather than direct government, leaving subject peoples their own rulers and their resentment.","Tlaxcala, a confederation east of the valley of Mexico, had never been conquered by the Aztecs and had fought them for years.","Cortes marched inland from the coast in August 1519 with Totonac allies from Cempoala.","The Tlaxcalans fought him hard through September 1519, failed to destroy him, and allied with him instead.","In October 1519 the Spanish and their allies killed thousands of unarmed men in the sacred precinct at Cholula, an Aztec ally; Cortes's own report claimed about three thousand dead.","Montezuma II, also written Moctezuma, chose gifts, warnings and delay rather than attacking the Spanish in open country.","On 8 November 1519 Montezuma received Cortes on the causeway into Tenochtitlan; within a fortnight he was a hostage in Spanish hands.","Why it matters: the conquest was a war among Mexican states that the Spanish joined and came to lead, not a Spanish army against an empire."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the capture of Tenochtitlan and the Aztec surrender","items":["Narvaez landed in April 1520 with a force sent by Velazquez to arrest Cortes; Cortes marched to the coast and defeated him at Cempoala.","In Cortes's absence Alvarado massacred Aztec nobles at the festival of Toxcatl in the temple precinct, and the city rose.","Montezuma died at the end of June 1520: Spanish accounts say he was stoned by his own people, Aztec accounts that the Spanish killed him.","The Noche Triste, the night of 30 June 1520: the Spanish fought out along the causeways and lost most of their men, their guns and nearly all their treasure.","They won a battle at Otumba in early July 1520 and reached Tlaxcala.","Smallpox, carried in with Narvaez's men, swept the valley from the autumn of 1520; Cuitlahuac, elected to succeed Montezuma after the expulsion, died within months, probably of it, though the early sources do not name the disease.","Cuauhtemoc became the last Aztec emperor.","Cortes had thirteen brigantines built at Tlaxcala and carried in pieces to the lake, and gathered tens of thousands of Indigenous allies.","The siege began in late May 1521; the aqueduct from Chapultepec was cut and the causeways were blockaded.","Cuauhtemoc was captured and the city surrendered on 13 August 1521, after nearly three months of fighting."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Spanish base at Panama","items":["Pedrarias Davila founded Panama on the Pacific shore on 15 August 1519, the first European town on the Pacific coast of the Americas.","It became the capital of the province of Castilla del Oro.","The isthmus could be crossed on foot in a few days, so men, supplies and shipbuilding timber reached the Pacific without a voyage round South America.","Francisco Pizarro, Diego de Almagro and the priest Hernando de Luque organised their southward voyages from Panama from 1524.","The first voyage, of 1524 to 1525, achieved almost nothing; the second, begun in 1526, reached Tumbes in 1528 and brought back gold, fine cloth and llamas.","Pizarro took that evidence to Spain and obtained the Capitulacion of Toledo in 1529, which named him governor of the lands he should conquer.","The crossing worked in both directions, so treasure was later carried by mule train over the isthmus to the Caribbean for the fleets to Seville.","Why it matters: Panama was infrastructure rather than conquest, and without it the assault on the Inca empire had no starting point."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: New Spain under Cortes (1523-28) and the consequences for the Aztecs","items":["Charles V appointed Cortes Governor and Captain-General of New Spain in October 1522; he governed from 1523.","Tenochtitlan was demolished and rebuilt on its own site as Mexico City, on a Spanish grid, with a cathedral raised on the precinct of the Great Temple.","Cortes granted encomiendas of Indigenous towns to his followers although the Crown had instructed him not to.","Spanish cattle, crops, sugar and mining were introduced, and the twelve Franciscan friars who arrived in 1524 began mass conversion of the Nahua.","Alvarado was sent to Guatemala and Cristobal de Olid to Honduras; when Olid rebelled, Cortes marched overland to Honduras from 1524 to 1526.","Cuauhtemoc was taken on that march and executed in 1525.","Cortes was suspended and investigated on his return in 1526; the first Audiencia, created in 1527, took office in December 1528 under Nuno de Guzman.","Cortes sailed for Spain in 1528 and was made Marques del Valle de Oaxaca in 1529, but never regained the governorship.","For the Aztecs: the empire ended, tribute passed to Spanish encomenderos, temples were pulled down and public religion was suppressed.","Population: smallpox in 1520, measles in the 1530s and the cocoliztli epidemics of the 1540s and 1570s; estimates differ sharply, but the Indigenous population of central Mexico fell by the great majority within a century.","Not everyone suffered equally: Tlaxcala received privileges given to no other Indigenous city, and Nahua nobles who co-operated kept some local authority."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan - Paper 2 booklet P Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the key events on the isthmus of Panama in the years 1514-19' (8 marks)","items":["The question as printed: 'Write a narrative account analysing the key events on the isthmus of Panama in the years 1514-19. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the arrival of Pedrarias Davila as governor (1514); the execution of Balboa (January 1519). You must also use information of your own.'","Decode it before writing. 'Narrative account' means a sequence that ends somewhere definite, so this one runs from the landing of a new governor to the founding of a town on the other ocean. 'Analysing' means the join between each episode and the next has to be explained rather than assumed. The dates fix the boundaries: Balboa's crossing of 1513 is background and belongs in a clause, not an episode, and nothing after 1519 belongs in the account at all except in the closing sentence.","The cap that decides the level: 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' Decide before writing which aspects are your own, beyond the two bullets - the best candidates are the hunger and sickness at Santa Maria la Antigua in 1514, Balboa's appointment as adelantado of the South Sea, the ships built on the Pacific shore in 1517 and 1518, and the founding of Panama on 15 August 1519.","Episode 1, the governor and the colony he found, 1514 (the first bullet): Santa Maria la Antigua del Darien, founded in 1510, is the only Spanish town of any size on the mainland, and Balboa has just claimed the South Sea for Castile; Pedrarias Davila lands in June 1514 with about twenty ships and perhaps fifteen hundred people, the largest force yet sent to the mainland, and within weeks hunger and sickness are killing them, one contemporary account counting seven hundred dead in a single month. The link forward: the new governor arrives with a royal commission and no food, so his standing depends on controlling the man who has already succeeded.","Episode 2, two authorities on one isthmus, 1514 to 1516: Pedrarias's instructions include an inquiry into Balboa's conduct, while the Crown names Balboa adelantado of the South Sea and governor of Panama and Coiba but keeps him under Pedrarias; about 1516 the quarrel is patched up by betrothing Balboa, in her absence, to Pedrarias's daughter Maria. The link forward: the compromise settles nothing, because it leaves unsaid who commands the new ocean.","Episode 3, the Pacific enterprise, 1517 to 1518: Balboa has timber and fittings carried over the mountains from Acla, the town Pedrarias founded on the Caribbean side in 1515, and ships built on the Pacific shore; he explores the gulf and the islands off that coast and prepares to sail south towards the rich kingdom the local people describe. The link forward: the closer Balboa comes to reaching it first, the more dangerous he is to a governor who would get none of the credit.","Episode 4, the removal and the new capital, 1519 (the second bullet): Pedrarias summons Balboa to Acla, has him tried for treason and beheaded there with four of his companions in January 1519; on 15 August 1519 the same governor founds Panama on the Pacific shore, the first European town on that ocean, and the seat of Castilla del Oro moves to it. The link: the enterprise Balboa began is carried on by the man who killed him, from a better harbour than Balboa ever had.","The evaluation thread that runs through the whole account: every episode is a struggle over who would control the crossing and the ocean beyond it, and each one moved the centre of Spanish power a little further from the Caribbean shore towards the Pacific one.","What the closing sentence must say: not a summary of the story but what the sequence amounted to - that in five years the Spanish mainland empire moved from a starving town on the Caribbean to a port on the Pacific, and that the change of shore, not the change of governor, is what the years 1514 to 1519 were about.","Timing: 8 marks, so about 13 minutes of the 55 available for booklet P - two or three minutes to fix the four episodes and their links in the margin, ten to write. There are no SPaG marks on Paper 2, so spend nothing beyond being clear.","Common ways to lose marks here: retelling the crossing of 1513 at length when it is before the period, turning the account into a character sketch of Pedrarias, or building the answer out of the two bullets alone, which cannot reach Level 3."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:28:12Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"Judged fresh on the retargeted kit: I re-solved all fourteen questions and checked about ninety-five dates, figures and events, verifying on the web every new claim in the retargeted items - Cholula on 18 October 1519 with Cortes’s own figure of three thousand dead in three hours¹, the Totonacs of Cempoala and the seized tribute collectors, Malintzin given at Potonchan in March 1519 with Aguilar’s chain of translation and her son Martin in 1522, the encomienda as tribute and labour rather than land with Charles’s 1523 order to revoke, the rebuilding from Coyoacan with the coat of arms of 4 July 1523 and the twelve Franciscans of 1524, and on the isthmus Pedrarias’s landing in June 1514 with about twenty ships, some fifteen hundred people and seven hundred dead in a month, Acla founded in 1515, the beheading of Balboa with four companions in January 1519 and Panama founded on 15 August 1519 - and all of them hold; no answer key is wrong, the booklet P form is exact for June 2025 (1(a)/1(b) at 4 + 4, the narrative account’s only cap at Level 3 in the current wording, no Level 2 cap, 1 hour 50 minutes), no bank question reproduces the specimen booklet P1 (the Spanish invasion of Mexico, the conquest of Peru 1528-48, Spanish exploration, the Treaty of Tordesillas, New World goods), and dupscan prints “no duplication”; three loose phrasings, one of them on a card, keep it short of 1.0. ¹ given throughout as Cortes’s own claim, with the alleged plot and the rival totals hedged."}}},"P1-3":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"P1-3","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 3 is the part of this option where booklet P finds its best questions, because everything in it is a chain of consequences: a ruler dies, an empire splits, a few hundred men take a continent, and the silver that follows pays for wars in Europe. Question 1 wants one consequence of one event, analysed and supported; Question 2 wants a short run of years told as an explanation rather than a list; Question 3 wants the difference an event or a person made to what came afterwards. All three reward the same habit of mind, which is to ask what changed and why the change followed. If you can date Cajamarca, the New Laws, Potosí and La Paz, and say in one sentence what each made possible, you can answer any of the three question types on this key topic without having to learn separate material for each.","idea":[{"code":"P1-3 — 1 Pizarro and the conquest of the Incas: Contact with the Incas (1528)","h":"An empire already broken: the death of Huayna Capac and first contact in 1528","t":"The Inca empire the Spanish met ran from modern Ecuador through Peru and Bolivia into Chile, held together by paved roads, storehouses, garrisons and compulsory labour service owed to the Sapa Inca. Its ruler, Huayna Capac, died in about 1527, almost certainly of smallpox that had travelled south from the Caribbean and Central America years ahead of any Spaniard, and the heir he had named died in the same epidemic. That left two of his sons claiming the throne: Huascar, accepted at the capital Cuzco, and Atahuallpa, who commanded the veteran army in the north at Quito. In 1528, while the quarrel was hardening into war, Pizarro's second expedition reached the Inca coastal town of Tumbes and saw gold and silver work, llamas and ordered towns; he then sailed to Spain to ask the Crown for a licence to conquer what he had found. A top-level answer uses this as the first link in a chain rather than as background: disease reached the Incas before the Spaniards did, so the state Pizarro attacked in 1532 had already lost its ruler, its agreed succession and the unity of its armies."},{"code":"P1-3 — 1 Pizarro and the conquest of the Incas: The voyage of Pizarro (1530)","h":"The voyage of 1530 and the war between the brothers: preparation meets opportunity","t":"Pizarro got what he went to Spain for: the Capitulación de Toledo, signed on 26 July 1529, named him governor and captain-general of the lands he conquered and gave his partner Diego de Almagro far less, which planted the rivalry that later tore the conquerors apart. He returned to Panama in 1530 and the expedition sailed for Peru at the end of that year, landing on the coast in 1531 and climbing into the highlands in 1532 with about 168 men, 62 of them on horseback. Meanwhile Atahuallpa's generals had beaten Huascar's forces and taken him prisoner near Cuzco, so Atahuallpa was resting at Cajamarca with his army, newly victorious and not yet installed as ruler, when the Spaniards arrived. A top-level answer refuses to call this only luck. Pizarro chose his moment, questioned everyone he met about the war, and made allies of Huascar's defeated supporters and of peoples the Incas had conquered, so the civil war is a Spanish opportunity that was deliberately used, not an accident that happened to them."},{"code":"P1-3 — 1 Pizarro and the conquest of the Incas: Key events of the Spanish conquest of Peru","h":"Cajamarca, the ransom, Cuzco and the revolt: how an empire was captured rather than defeated","t":"On 16 November 1532 Atahuallpa entered the square at Cajamarca with a large unarmed retinue. The Dominican friar Vicente de Valverde demanded his submission to Charles V and to Christianity; when the demand was refused, Pizarro's cannon and cavalry charged into the crowd, killed thousands and took the Sapa Inca alive. Capture mattered more than killing: because the Inca state ran on obedience to one man, orders still went out in Atahuallpa's name while he was held, so no army came to relieve him and a ransom of a room filled once with gold and twice with silver was gathered from temples across the empire. He had Huascar killed while he was himself a prisoner, and his captors executed him in July 1533. Pizarro then entered Cuzco with Manco Inca on 15 November 1533 and had him crowned there in December, founding Lima on the coast in January 1535. The method failed only when Manco escaped in 1536, besieged Cuzco into 1537 and attacked Lima; when the siege collapsed he withdrew to Vilcabamba. A top-level answer explains the conquest as the capture and use of the Incas' own chain of command, and counts the impact in deaths from war and epidemic, forced labour, melted-down treasure and the imposition of Spanish rule and religion."},{"code":"P1-3 — 2 Expansion of empire: The role of the viceroys and encomiendas","h":"Governing the conquest: encomenderos, viceroys, Las Casas and the New Laws of 1542","t":"An encomienda handed a Spaniard the tribute and labour of a named group of indigenous people in return for protecting them and having them taught Christianity. It was not a grant of land, but because the encomendero's income came from what those people produced it worked as forced labour, and the friars who came with the conquest, Franciscans in Mexico from 1524 and Dominicans and Augustinians after them, both softened and legitimised it. Bartolomé de las Casas, an encomendero in the Caribbean before he became a Dominican, spent forty years telling the court what was being done; his report of 1542 helped produce the New Laws of that year, which banned the enslavement of indigenous people, forbade new encomiendas, ordered existing ones back to the Crown when their holders died, and created a Viceroyalty of Peru to match the Viceroyalty of New Spain set up in 1535. A viceroy was the king's stand-in, working with an audiencia of royal judges. A top-level answer weighs intention against effect: the inheritance clause was withdrawn in 1545 after the colonists resisted and the first viceroy of Peru was killed in 1546, yet the Crown had established that the Indies belonged to the king and that the conquerors would not become a hereditary nobility."},{"code":"P1-3 — 2 Expansion of empire: The significance of the discovery of silver","h":"Silver: Potosí from 1545, Zacatecas from 1546, and the town of La Paz in 1548","t":"The gold of the conquest was plunder and ran out; silver was production and did not. From 1545 the Cerro Rico above Potosí, in the highlands of Upper Peru in what is now Bolivia, was being mined, and the town founded there in the same year grew into one of the largest cities of the Spanish world although it stood at over four thousand metres on ground that grew almost nothing. Silver was found at Zacatecas in northern New Spain in 1546, and in the mid-1550s a way of refining ore with mercury was worked out in New Spain, which made poor ore worth crushing. Everything a mining town needed had to be carried to it, so farms, mule trains, roads and new settlements grew along the routes; La Paz was founded in 1548 on the road between the mines and the coast, and named Our Lady of Peace to mark the end of Gonzalo Pizarro's rebellion in the same year. Labour came from indigenous villages under encomienda obligations and later under Crown labour drafts, and it killed people. A top-level answer says that silver redrew the map of the empire and fixed what the empire was for."},{"code":"P1-3 — 3 Impact of the 'New World' on Spain: The importance of gold and silver for the Spanish economy","h":"What the bullion bought: the Crown's power and the Spanish economy pulling apart","t":"One fifth of all bullion, the quinto real, belonged to the king, and the rest was registered at the House of Trade in Seville as it landed. Charles V did not spend it in Spain. He pledged it, often before it had arrived, to the Fugger bankers of Augsburg and to Genoese financiers, who advanced the cash that paid his armies in the wars against France, against the Ottomans and against the German Protestant princes, so the silver frequently went straight on to Antwerp or Italy. Inside Spain the effects were different. More money chasing the same grain, cloth and housing pushed prices up faster and earlier than in most of Europe across the sixteenth century, which hurt people on fixed wages and rents and made Spanish goods dear beside foreign ones, so Spaniards bought abroad what they might have made at home. In 1557, two years after this period closes, the Crown had to suspend payments to its bankers. A top-level answer separates the two things the question can mean: American treasure made the Spanish Crown the most powerful in Europe and left the Spanish economy weaker than it found it."},{"code":"P1-3 — 3 Impact of the 'New World' on Spain: The impact on Spain of the slave trade and goods from the Americas","h":"The other cargoes: enslaved Africans outward, new goods homeward","t":"The indigenous population of the Caribbean collapsed within a generation of Spanish settlement, through overwork and through smallpox, measles and typhus, and the sugar estates of Hispaniola and Cuba were left without workers. Spain held no trading posts on the African coast, whose trade the Treaty of Alcáçovas of 1479 had reserved to Portugal, so the Crown sold licences, called asientos, to merchants who would carry enslaved Africans to the Americas; Charles V granted a large licence of this kind in 1518. The traffic was mostly carried in Portuguese ships with Genoese credit, so Spain earned a revenue and a labour supply without building the trade itself, and Seville, the one legal port, grew rich on the traffic in both directions. Coming home, the fleets brought silver and gold first, then cochineal dye from New Spain, which was among the most valuable cargoes after bullion, and hides and sugar, and the new crops: maize, potatoes, beans, tomatoes, chillies, cacao and tobacco. A top-level answer notices the difference in speed: the dyes, hides and sugar changed Spanish commerce at once, while the new foods changed European diets slowly, over generations."},{"code":"P1-3 — 3 Impact of the 'New World' on Spain: The government of the Spanish Empire","h":"Running an ocean empire from Seville: the House of Trade and the Council of the Indies","t":"The Indies were the king's, not the conquerors', and two institutions made that real. The House of Trade, the Casa de Contratación, was set up at Seville in 1503: it licensed every ship and every passenger, registered every cargo, collected the royal fifth and the customs, kept the master chart of the Atlantic and trained and examined the pilots who sailed the route. Sebastián Cabot was its pilot major from 1518, and the expedition he led in 1526 was meant for the Moluccas but explored the Río de la Plata instead, returning in 1530 in disgrace. The Council of the Indies, established in 1524, was the king's standing council for American business: it drafted the laws, including the New Laws of 1542, nominated viceroys, bishops and judges, heard appeals from the audiencias and put returning officials through a residencia, a public review of their conduct. From the 1520s ships were ordered to sail together against French raiders, which hardened into the flota system of escorted yearly fleets. A top-level answer says what this machinery was for: to keep the Indies a royal monopoly funnelled through one port, so that treasure could be counted, taxed and pledged before anyone in America could spend it."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1(a): turning the landing at Tumbes into one explained consequence","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(a): 'Explain one consequence of the Spanish landing at Tumbes in 1528.' [4 marks]. Question 1 is printed in two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each on a separate event and each marked on its own out of 4, so this part needs one consequence analysed and supported with specific information. What you are working from: Francisco Pizarro had settled in Panama and his first probe southwards in the mid-1520s brought back nothing but hardship; on the second expedition his men reached the Inca coastal town of Tumbes in 1528 and saw worked gold and silver, llamas and well-ordered towns; Pizarro then sailed to Spain, and the Capitulación de Toledo of 26 July 1529 gave him the conquest of Peru with the titles of governor and captain-general; he was back in Panama in 1530, the expedition sailed at the end of that year, landed on the Peruvian coast in 1531 and climbed into the highlands in 1532 with about 168 men, 62 of them on horseback.","steps":["Ask what was true afterwards that had not been true before: a venture that had produced nothing now had physical proof that a rich, settled state lay south of Panama, so the question stopped being whether there was anything to find and became who would pay to take it.","Choose the consequence that reaches furthest forward, which is that a private probe became a Crown-licensed conquest, rather than stopping at the metalwork and cloth the men carried home or at the excitement in Panama.","Support it with specific information: Pizarro took his evidence to Spain and came away with the Capitulación de Toledo of 26 July 1529, which made him governor and captain-general of what he conquered; he returned to Panama in 1530 and sailed for Peru at the end of that year.","Now make the move the grid calls analysis: royal terms turned a handful of adventurers into an authorised expedition that could recruit men and money and bring Pizarro's own brothers with it, so what the men saw at Tumbes belongs in the explanation of why a Spanish force was standing in the Peruvian highlands four years later."],"cues":["What did the men see at Tumbes that they had not seen before?","Which of the effects you could name still matters years later?","Which precise agreement and date prove the Crown had come in?","Why does a royal licence make a small private venture dangerous?"]},{"title":"Question 2: building the spine of a narrative account on the coming of the viceroys","setup":"Booklet P, Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the establishment of viceregal government in Spain's American empire in the years 1535–51.' (8 marks). You may use the following in your answer: the arrival of the first viceroy of Peru (1544); the appointment of Antonio de Mendoza as viceroy of Peru (1551). You must also use information of your own. The cap printed in the grid is 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content', so at least three distinct aspects must appear. The events available to you: the Viceroyalty of New Spain was created in 1535 and Antonio de Mendoza took office there in November 1535, governing with an audiencia of royal judges for fifteen years; the Council of the Indies, at work since 1524, drafted the laws, nominated viceroys, bishops and judges and reviewed returning officials by residencia; a Viceroyalty of Peru and an audiencia at Lima were created in November 1542 and the court began work in 1543; Blasco Nuñez Vela took office as the first viceroy of Peru in May 1544 and was defeated and killed at Añaquito on 18 January 1546; Pedro de la Gasca restored royal government in 1548; Mendoza was moved from Mexico to Lima and took office there in September 1551, dying in Peru in July 1552.","steps":["Pick four episodes that make a chain rather than a list, and date each: the creation of the Viceroyalty of New Spain in 1535 with Mendoza as its first viceroy, the creation of a Viceroyalty of Peru with an audiencia at Lima in 1542, the arrival of the first viceroy of Peru in 1544 and his death at Añaquito in 1546, and Mendoza's move from Mexico to Lima in 1551.","Write the first link as analysis rather than sequence: the Crown put a viceroy into Mexico in 1535 because a conqueror who governed what he had taken could not be recalled, while a royal stand-in working beside a court of royal judges could be appointed, instructed and replaced.","Link the second episode to the first: the Mexican arrangement held for fifteen years without any settler or conqueror rebellion breaking royal government (the Mixtón War of 1540–42 was an Indigenous rising, put down by Mendoza himself), so when the Crown legislated for the Indies in 1542 it gave Peru the same design, a viceroy with an audiencia of his own at Lima.","Link the third: the design failed where the conquerors were still armed, because the first viceroy of Peru was dead within two years of taking office and royal authority had to be rebuilt by negotiation before a viceroy could sit in Lima again.","Close by saying what the sequence amounted to, without adding a new story: sending to Lima in 1551 the man who had made the office work in Mexico showed that viceregal government was now the empire's normal form, and that a council in Spain, not the men who had done the conquering, chose who ruled in America."],"cues":["Which four moments carry the whole story?","Why send a stand-in rather than trust the conqueror?","What made the Crown repeat the arrangement in Peru?","Why did the same design fail there at first?","What single sentence sums up the outcome?"]},{"title":"Question 3: explaining the importance of indigenous allies rather than listing them","setup":"Booklet P, Question 3 prints three bullets and asks you to explain two of them, each marked separately out of 8. The bullet here: 'The importance of indigenous allies for the Spanish conquest of Peru.' (8 marks). No stimulus points are printed and no cap applies. What you know: the Inca empire had been built by conquering other peoples, who owed it labour and tribute; the war between Atahuallpa and Huascar left a defeated side with nothing to lose; Pizarro questioned everyone he met about that war as he marched inland; the Cañari of the north and the Huanca of the central highlands went over to the Spaniards, as did the Chachapoya; during the siege of Cuzco in 1536–37 the defenders were supported by some two thousand indigenous allies, Cañari and Chachapoya among them; Manco Inca's half-brother Paullu stayed out of the revolt and was crowned by the Spaniards as Sapa Inca in 1537; about 168 Spaniards had reached Cajamarca in 1532.","steps":["Set the criterion first, because importance is a comparison: the test is what the Spaniards could do with allies that a few hundred strangers could not have done alone, not how many allies can be named.","Take the first difference it made and support it: the empire had been assembled by conquest, so the Cañari and the Huanca had a quarrel with Cuzco older than the Spaniards, and they supplied the porters, food, guides and fighting men that an invading force of about 168 men could neither carry nor replace.","Take the second difference, which reaches further forward: allies turned the crisis of 1536–37 into something the Spaniards could survive, because some two thousand of them held Cuzco alongside the defenders while Manco's armies pressed the siege, and Manco's own half-brother Paullu stayed out of the revolt and was crowned by the Spaniards in 1537.","Weigh the limit of that importance so the reasoning is not one-sided: allies came because subject peoples and the losing side in the civil war judged that a Spanish victory would serve them, so the alliances rested on a split the Spaniards found rather than made, and they bound the Spaniards to keep paying for it in protection and favours.","Finish with a judgement in one sentence: indigenous allies matter because they turned a raiding party into an army with local supply, local knowledge and a local claim to rule, but they were on offer only because the Inca state was already at war with itself, so they are the second half of an explanation whose first half is that civil war."],"cues":["What has to be compared before importance can be judged?","Why would anyone in Peru fight on the invaders' side?","Where can you show allies deciding an outcome?","What did the Spaniards find rather than create?","So how far did allies make the conquest possible, and what made allies possible?"]},{"title":"Question 1(a) and 1(b): keeping two consequences of two separate developments apart","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1: '1(a) Explain one consequence of the arrival of cochineal dye from New Spain. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the silver strike at Zacatecas in 1546. [4 marks]'. The two parts are on separate developments and each is marked out of 4, so neither answer may lean on the other. What you know: cochineal is a red dye made from an insect raised on cactus in southern New Spain, long produced there before the Spaniards came; it gave a scarlet far stronger than the madder and kermes European dyers had to work with; Charles V was already asking Cortés to send cargoes of it in 1523; it grew nowhere in Europe, it came home registered at the House of Trade in Seville like every other cargo, and it became the most valuable thing New Spain sent home after bullion. Silver was found at Zacatecas in 1546, far north of the settled farming country of central New Spain; a mining camp was formally established there in 1548; the ground around it fed nobody, so everything the miners used had to be carried up from the south.","steps":["Decide for 1(a) what the dye gave Spain rather than what it was, because that is where the consequence lies: a good that Europe wanted badly and could get from nowhere else.","Write 1(a) as consequence plus proof plus link: because the insect, the cactus and the growers were all inside New Spain and every cargo was registered at Seville before it could be sold, Spain held Europe's supply of the best red there was, and cochineal grew into the most valuable cargo New Spain sent home after bullion.","Start 1(b) from a clean slate, with nothing carried over from the dye, and ask what a silver strike in dry, unsettled country forced people to do.","Write 1(b) the same way: because the ore lay far beyond the farmed country of central New Spain, food, timber, tools, labour and armed protection had to be pushed north to reach it, so roads, ranches and new settlements followed the mines rather than the mines following the settlement.","Check the two answers against each other and cut any sentence that would serve equally well in the other part, since repetition earns nothing twice."],"cues":["What could Europe not get anywhere else?","Which precise detail shows how much the dye was worth?","Why must the second part begin again from nothing?","What does a mine need that empty country cannot supply?","How would you spot a sentence that has been used twice?"]},{"title":"Question 3: the importance of American treasure for the Spanish Crown","setup":"Booklet P, Question 3, one of the three bullets: 'The importance of American gold and silver for Spain's European empire.' (8 marks), marked separately out of 8, with no stimulus points. What you know: the Crown took the quinto real, one fifth of all bullion; treasure was registered at the House of Trade in Seville; Charles V pledged incoming bullion to the Fugger bankers of Augsburg and to Genoese financiers; he fought France, the Ottomans and the German Protestant princes; prices in Spain rose faster and earlier than in most of Europe during the sixteenth century; Spanish goods became dear beside foreign ones; the Crown suspended payments to its bankers in 1557.","steps":["Fix the focus the bullet gives you before writing anything: the importance of the bullion for Spain's European empire, which means the king's power and his wars abroad.","Build the first strand of importance with the mechanism, not the amount: because a fifth of every cargo was the king's and the rest could be seized or borrowed against, bankers would lend against treasure that had not yet sailed, and their loans paid armies in Italy, Germany and the Netherlands.","Show the cost of that same mechanism: money pledged in advance mostly never reached Spain, so it financed foreign campaigns while leaving Spanish industry no capital.","Bring the effect at home back to that focus: more coin bidding for the same goods raised prices and made Spanish products dear, so the Crown could not pay for its European wars out of Spanish trade and leaned harder on the bankers.","Judge the importance the bullet asked for, in one sentence: American treasure let Charles V keep armies in the field against France, the Ottomans and the German Protestant princes on a scale no rival could match, so it was decisive for Spain's European empire — but it was pledged before it landed, so the power it bought rested on debt."],"cues":["Important for whom, exactly?","How does treasure become an army before it is even unloaded?","What is the hidden price of borrowing against future cargoes?","What happens at home when more coin chases the same goods?","So how far did the treasure carry the king's power in Europe, and at what cost?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Give the date of the Battle of Cajamarca and say what happened to Atahuallpa there and afterwards.","a":"16 November 1532. Atahuallpa was taken alive after his unarmed retinue was attacked in the square; he paid a ransom of a room filled once with gold and twice with silver, had his brother Huascar killed while a prisoner, and was executed by his captors in July 1533."},{"q":"What did the New Laws of 1542 do, and what happened to them?","a":"They banned the enslavement of indigenous people, forbade new encomiendas, ordered existing ones back to the Crown on their holders' deaths, and created the Viceroyalty of Peru. The colonists resisted; Charles V withdrew the inheritance clause in 1545, the first viceroy of Peru was killed at Añaquito in 1546, and Gonzalo Pizarro's rebellion was only crushed in 1548."},{"q":"Name the two institutions that governed the Spanish Empire from Spain, and say when each was set up and what it did.","a":"The House of Trade at Seville, 1503: it licensed ships and passengers, registered cargoes, collected the royal fifth and trained pilots. The Council of the Indies, 1524: it drafted laws, nominated viceroys, bishops and judges, heard appeals from the audiencias and reviewed officials by residencia."},{"q":"Where and when was silver found in Upper Peru, and which town was founded in 1548 on the road serving it?","a":"In the Cerro Rico above Potosí, in modern Bolivia, from 1545. La Paz was founded in 1548 on the road between the mines and the coast, its name marking the peace after Gonzalo Pizarro's defeat."}]},"room":{"facts":["Booklet P carries 32 marks in three questions. Question 1 is printed in two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each 'Explain one consequence of...' for 4 marks and each on a separate event or development. Question 2 is 'Write a narrative account analysing...' for 8 marks. Question 3 prints three bullets, 'Explain two of the following: the importance of ... for ...', and the two you choose are worth 8 marks each, 16 in all.","Only Question 2 prints stimulus points: two bullets followed by 'You must also use information of your own.' Its mark scheme carries one cap, 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' There is no cap at Level 2 on any question, and Questions 1 and 3 print no bullets and carry no cap.","Each part of Question 1 is marked on its own two-level grid out of 4, targeting AO2 2 and AO1 2. Level 2, worth 3 to 4 marks, wants features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific supporting information. Two accurate descriptions of the event, however detailed, stay in Level 1.","Question 3 is marked twice on a three-level grid out of 8, AO2 8 and AO1 8 across the two parts. Level 3, worth 6 to 8 marks, wants analysis of importance in a coherent line of reasoning: what difference the event, person or development made to later situations, not what happened in it.","Timing: Paper 2 lasts 1 hour 50 minutes for 64 marks and the advice is to divide the time equally between the two booklets, so booklet P gets about 55 minutes for its 32 marks. That is a little over a minute and a half a mark: roughly 12 minutes on the two parts of Question 1 together, 13 on Question 2 and 13 on each part of Question 3 comes to 51 minutes and leaves about four for planning and checking."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the death of Huayna Capac. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Level 2 (3-4) needs features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific support. Credit the disputed succession and the civil war between Huascar and Atahuallpa, the loss of a single authority over the army, or the spread of epidemic disease that killed him reaching the empire before the Spanish did.","sol":"One consequence was that the Inca empire fell into civil war. Huayna Capac died in about 1527 with no successor in place, because the heir he had named died in the same epidemic, so two of his sons claimed the throne: Huascar, who held Cuzco and the priesthood of the capital, and Atahuallpa, who commanded the veteran army in the north. Their war was still being settled when Pizarro landed, which meant the empire could put neither one army nor one authority against him. Divided command, not Spanish numbers, is why a few hundred men could seize a state of millions.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["A consequence is something that was true afterwards because of the death, so ask what the empire had lost besides a ruler.","The Incas had no fixed rule of succession by eldest son, so look at what happened between the two sons who claimed the throne.","Build it in three moves: name the consequence, give the precise detail that proves it, then explain why it mattered when the Spaniards arrived."],"codes":["P1-3 — 1 Pizarro and the conquest of the Incas: Contact with the Incas (1528)","P1-3 — 1 Pizarro and the conquest of the Incas: The voyage of Pizarro (1530)"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of the war between Atahuallpa and Huascar. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit Atahuallpa's presence at Cajamarca with his army rather than at Cuzco, the killing of Huascar, the availability of defeated groups as Spanish allies, or the weakening of Inca resistance to the invasion.","sol":"One consequence was that Atahuallpa was at Cajamarca with his army rather than in the capital when the Spaniards arrived. His generals had beaten Huascar's forces and taken him prisoner near Cuzco in 1532, and Atahuallpa had halted in the northern highlands before going south to claim the empire. That gave Pizarro a target he could reach in a few weeks' march from the coast, and a ruler curious about a small band of strangers rather than already at war with them. Meeting the Sapa Inca face to face in November 1532 was what made the ambush possible.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about where the war left each brother, not about who was the better claimant.","Ask what a newly victorious ruler would be doing in the northern highlands in the closing months of 1532.","Name the consequence, date the detail that proves it, then link it to what the Spaniards were able to do."],"codes":["P1-3 — 1 Pizarro and the conquest of the Incas: The voyage of Pizarro (1530)","P1-3 — 1 Pizarro and the conquest of the Incas: Key events of the Spanish conquest of Peru"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the capture of Atahuallpa at Cajamarca. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the paralysis of Inca resistance while orders still ran in his name, the collection of the gold and silver ransom, the killing of Huascar on his orders, or the Spanish decision to rule through a crowned Inca after his execution.","sol":"One consequence was that the Spaniards gained control of the Inca state without having to conquer it. Because the Sapa Inca was obeyed absolutely, orders went on running in Atahuallpa's name while he was a prisoner, so no Inca army marched to relieve him and the ransom of a room filled once with gold and twice with silver was gathered from temples across the empire and delivered to his captors. Pizarro therefore spent the eight months to July 1533 receiving treasure and information rather than fighting, and when he did execute Atahuallpa he simply installed another member of the royal family in his place.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The important word is captured, not defeated: ask what a live prisoner gave the Spaniards that a dead enemy would not.","Think about how the Inca state passed orders down from its ruler, and what that meant while he was held.","State the consequence, prove it with what the Incas did while he was a prisoner, then explain why it followed from his capture."],"codes":["P1-3 — 1 Pizarro and the conquest of the Incas: Key events of the Spanish conquest of Peru"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of the New Laws of 1542. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the encomenderos' rebellion under Gonzalo Pizarro and the death of the first viceroy of Peru, the withdrawal of the inheritance clause in 1545, the creation of the Viceroyalty of Peru and its audiencia, or the check on the growth of a hereditary settler nobility.","sol":"One consequence was armed rebellion in Peru. The laws forbade new encomiendas and ordered existing ones back to the Crown when their holders died, which threatened to turn the conquerors' families into ordinary settlers within a generation. When the first viceroy, Blasco Nuñez Vela, reached Peru in 1544 determined to enforce them, the encomenderos rallied behind Gonzalo Pizarro; the viceroy was defeated and killed at Añaquito in 1546, and royal authority was restored only when Pedro de la Gasca beat Gonzalo in 1548 and had him executed. Charles V had already withdrawn the inheritance clause in 1545.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask who stood to lose by the laws, and what exactly they stood to lose.","The clause that caused most anger was not about cruelty at all but about what happened to a grant when its holder died.","Name the consequence, then track it through the named men and dates that show how far it went."],"codes":["P1-3 — 2 Expansion of empire: The role of the viceroys and encomiendas"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the discovery of silver at Potosí. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the growth of a great mining city on barren ground and the supply network of roads, farms and mule trains that fed it, the founding of La Paz in 1548 on the route to the coast, the drafting of indigenous labour into the mines, or the shift of the empire's wealth from plunder to production.","sol":"One consequence was that the centre of Spanish Peru moved inland to the mines. The Cerro Rico above Potosí, in what is now Bolivia, was being worked from 1545, and the town that grew around it needed everything carried up to it: food, timber, cloth, mules and above all workers. Settlements were founded along the road down to the coast, La Paz among them in 1548, and indigenous villages were made to send labourers under the obligations the Spanish had taken over from the Incas. Within ten years a barren mountain at over four thousand metres had become the richest place in the empire.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["A mine is not only a source of wealth; ask what a mine on an empty mountain forces other people to do.","Potosí grew nothing and stood far from the sea, so think about roads, food and labour before you think about treasure.","Name the consequence, support it with a place and a date on the route it created, then explain the link back to the silver."],"codes":["P1-3 — 2 Expansion of empire: The significance of the discovery of silver"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Capitulación de Toledo of 1529. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the founding of Lima in 1535. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two parts, each marked out of 4 on the consequence grid, so each part needs one consequence analysed with specific support. In 1(a) credit the licensing of the voyage of 1530 as a private conquest, Pizarro's authority as governor and captain-general of whatever he took, or the rivalry with Almagro created by the unequal terms. In 1(b) credit the turning of Spanish Peru towards the coast and Seville, the eclipse of Cuzco, or Lima's later role as the seat of the viceroy created in 1542.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that the conquest began as a private venture with a quarrel built into it. The Capitulación signed at Toledo on 26 July 1529 made Francisco Pizarro governor and captain-general of whatever he conquered and left his partner Diego de Almagro with far less, so when the expedition sailed from Panama in 1530 the two leading men were rivals for the same prize. When the treasure of Peru was shared out that resentment became war: Almagro seized Cuzco, was beaten at Las Salinas in 1538 and executed, and his followers murdered Pizarro in 1541. 1(b) One consequence of founding Lima in January 1535 was that Spanish Peru was turned towards the sea and towards Spain. Cuzco lay high in the mountains, hard to reach and full of Inca memory, while the new city stood on the coastal plain beside a harbour, so treasure, orders, officials and reinforcements could pass between Peru and Seville without a long climb. That is why Lima, not Cuzco, became the seat of the viceroy created in 1542 and the place from which Spanish South America was governed.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two parts, two separate developments: a royal agreement in 1(a) and the siting of a city in 1(b), so neither answer can borrow from the other.","For 1(a) look at the terms Pizarro obtained at Toledo rather than at the granting of them, and ask who was left dissatisfied.","For 1(b) compare where Lima stands with where Cuzco stands, and ask what each site made easy or hard."],"codes":["P1-3 — 1 Pizarro and the conquest of the Incas: The voyage of Pizarro (1530)","P1-3 — 1 Pizarro and the conquest of the Incas: Key events of the Spanish conquest of Peru"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the revolt of Manco Inca in 1536. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the murder of Francisco Pizarro in 1541. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two parts, each out of 4 on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the end of rule through a puppet Inca and the move to direct Spanish government, the survival of a rebel Inca state at Vilcabamba, or the losses of the siege including the death of Juan Pizarro. In 1(b) credit the removal of the last conqueror-governor and the opening it gave to royal government through the New Laws and the Viceroyalty of Peru, or the further round of fighting among the conquerors.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that Spanish rule in Peru stopped pretending to work through the Incas. Manco had been crowned in 1533 as a Sapa Inca who would rule under Spanish protection, but in 1536 he escaped, raised the provinces and besieged Cuzco into 1537 while a second army attacked Lima; Juan Pizarro was killed fighting for the fortress above Cuzco. When the siege failed and Manco withdrew to Vilcabamba the Spaniards no longer needed a figurehead: they governed directly, and the Inca court in the mountains became an enemy to be hunted rather than a partner. 1(b) One consequence of Pizarro's murder in Lima in June 1541 was that the Crown could at last impose its own officers on Peru. The conquerors had destroyed one another: Almagro had been executed after Las Salinas in 1538, and now Almagro's son's supporters had killed the governor himself. A colony whose government rested on one conqueror's licence plainly needed royal rule, and within eighteen months the New Laws created the Viceroyalty of Peru, sending a viceroy and royal judges where a conquistador had ruled.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["In 1(a) ask what the Spaniards stopped doing after the revolt, not only who won the siege.","In 1(b) the man killed was the king's governor as well as a conqueror, so ask what his death left vacant.","Give each part its own consequence, its own named evidence and its own closing link; a sentence that would fit either part is doing no work."],"codes":["P1-3 — 1 Pizarro and the conquest of the Incas: Key events of the Spanish conquest of Peru","P1-3 — 2 Expansion of empire: The role of the viceroys and encomiendas"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the encomienda system in Spain's American empire. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the Valladolid debate of 1550 to 1551. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two parts, each out of 4 on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit forced labour in fields, buildings and mines, tribute demands, the spread of Christianity through the friars, and the catastrophic population decline as overwork met epidemic disease. In 1(b) credit the public acknowledgement that conquest needed justification, the suspension of new expeditions while the question was argued, the official doctrine that indigenous people were the king's free subjects, or the absence of any verdict and the gap between law and practice.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was a collapse in the indigenous population of the areas the Spanish held. An encomienda handed a Spaniard the tribute and labour of a named group in return for protecting them and having them taught Christianity, and because the encomendero's income came from what they produced they were driven to work in fields, on building sites and in mines. Weakened people met European diseases to which they had no immunity, so smallpox, measles and typhus killed on a scale nothing else matched; historians estimate that the indigenous population of central Mexico fell by around nine-tenths during the sixteenth century. 1(b) One consequence of the debate at Valladolid was that Spain accepted in principle that its conquests had to be justified. Charles V had suspended new expeditions while Las Casas and Juan Ginés de Sepúlveda argued before a royal commission whether the peoples of the Americas were natural inferiors who could rightly be conquered or subjects with rights. No winner was declared, but the Crown had publicly treated the question as open, and the doctrine that indigenous people were the king's free subjects remained the official position even while encomenderos ignored it.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["In 1(a) the encomienda was a grant of people's labour, not of land, so ask what that did to the people granted.","In 1(b) nobody was declared the winner, so the consequence has to be about what the argument itself established.","Each part needs one consequence, precise support and a link; resist the temptation to let 1(b) become a second answer about cruelty."],"codes":["P1-3 — 2 Expansion of empire: The role of the viceroys and encomiendas"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the creation of the Council of the Indies in 1524. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the work of the House of Trade at Seville. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two parts, each out of 4 on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the settling of American law and appointments in Spain, the drafting of the New Laws, the review of officials by residencia, and the resentment of conquerors who found themselves answerable to lawyers. In 1(b) credit the funnelling of all legal trade through one port and the enrichment of Seville, the counting and taxing of bullion through the royal fifth, the training and examination of pilots, or the organisation of the fleets into protected convoys.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that American affairs came to be settled in Spain rather than by the men in America. From 1524 the Council of the Indies was the king's standing body for the Indies: it drafted the laws, including the New Laws of 1542, nominated viceroys, bishops and judges, heard appeals from the colonial high courts, and put every returning official through a residencia, a public review of his conduct. A conquistador who had made himself rich could therefore be called to account by a committee of lawyers thousands of miles away, and part of what Gonzalo Pizarro's rebellion refused was exactly that. 1(b) One consequence of the House of Trade was that the whole American trade was squeezed through a single Spanish port. Founded at Seville in 1503, it licensed every ship and passenger, registered every cargo, collected the royal fifth of the bullion and trained and examined the pilots who sailed the route. Because everything passed its officials, Seville grew rich while other Spanish ports were shut out, the Crown could count and tax treasure as it landed, and the same records made it possible to order ships to sail in protected convoys against French raiders.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Both parts are about who controlled the empire, so in each ask what somebody could no longer do once the institution existed.","For 1(a) think about appointments, appeals and the review of officials rather than about exploration.","For 1(b) a single licensing office in one city has effects on that city as well as on the trade; say both and support each."],"codes":["P1-3 — 3 Impact of the 'New World' on Spain: The government of the Spanish Empire"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the arrival of American gold and silver in Spain. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the arrival of new foods from the Americas. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two parts, each out of 4 on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the long rise in prices, the squeeze on fixed wages and rents, the loss of competitiveness of Spanish goods and the growth of imports. In 1(b) credit the slow spread of maize and potatoes as crops that yielded on poor ground, the growth of Seville's trade, and the greater commercial value of cochineal, hides and sugar carried in the same fleets.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was a long rise in Spanish prices. Bullion entered Europe through Seville, where the Crown took its fifth and the rest was paid out to merchants and bankers, so far more silver and gold was chasing the same Spanish grain, cloth and housing. Prices in Spain rose faster and earlier than in most of Europe across the sixteenth century, which hurt people living on fixed wages and rents and made Spanish goods dear beside foreign ones. Spaniards therefore bought abroad what they might have made at home, so the wealth passed through the country rather than settling in it. 1(b) One consequence was a slow change in what Europeans grew and ate. Maize, potatoes, beans, tomatoes, chillies and cacao crossed the Atlantic in the returning ships, and because maize and potatoes yielded heavily on poor ground they spread first as fodder and famine food in Spain before becoming ordinary crops. Other cargoes told in money sooner: cochineal from New Spain gave Europe a brilliant red dye and became one of the most valuable things carried after bullion, while hides and sugar filled the holds of the fleets and kept Seville's merchants busy.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["In 1(a) ask what happens to prices when the quantity of coin rises faster than the quantity of goods.","In 1(b) separate the crops, which changed diets over generations, from the dyes, hides and sugar, which changed trade at once.","Each part wants one consequence with specific support, so pick the strand you can name evidence for and leave the rest."],"codes":["P1-3 — 3 Impact of the 'New World' on Spain: The importance of gold and silver for the Spanish economy","P1-3 — 3 Impact of the 'New World' on Spain: The impact on Spain of the slave trade and goods from the Americas"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the fall of the Inca empire in the years 1532-37. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the capture of Atahuallpa (1532); the revolt of Manco Inca (1536). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) requires a clear sequence of events leading to an outcome with the linkage between them analysed. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content. Own material might include the civil war between Huascar and Atahuallpa, the ransom, the execution of July 1533, the crowning of Manco and the entry into Cuzco, the founding of Lima, the siege of Cuzco and the retreat to Vilcabamba.","sol":"The empire Pizarro met was already divided: Huayna Capac's death in about 1527 had left his sons Huascar and Atahuallpa fighting for the throne, and Atahuallpa's generals had captured Huascar in 1532, which is why the victorious Sapa Inca was resting at Cajamarca with his army when about 168 Spaniards climbed up from the coast. The ambush of 16 November 1532 worked because of that setting: Atahuallpa came into the square with an unarmed retinue, Friar Vicente de Valverde demanded his submission to Charles V, and when the demand was refused cannon and cavalry killed thousands of his people in a few hours and took him alive. Holding the Sapa Inca gave Pizarro the empire's obedience without a campaign, since orders still ran in Atahuallpa's name: the ransom of gold and silver was gathered from temples across the empire, Huascar was killed on Atahuallpa's orders, and Atahuallpa himself was executed in July 1533. Pizarro then used the same machinery again, entering Cuzco with Manco Inca on 15 November 1533, having him crowned there in December, while Lima was founded on the coast in January 1535. The method broke only in 1536, when Manco found that he was a prisoner in all but name, escaped and besieged Cuzco into 1537 while a second army attacked Lima. The siege failed as supplies ran short and the besiegers had to return to their fields, and Manco withdrew to Vilcabamba. The Inca empire therefore ended not in one battle but in the capture, use and exhaustion of its own chain of command.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["A narrative account is an explanation in date order, so choose the few episodes that make one thing lead to the next.","Ask at each step why the Spaniards were able to take the next move, and let that answer be the sentence that joins two episodes.","Fix a spine of four dated moments first, then write the joins between them before you fill in any detail."],"codes":["P1-3 — 1 Pizarro and the conquest of the Incas: Contact with the Incas (1528)","P1-3 — 1 Pizarro and the conquest of the Incas: The voyage of Pizarro (1530)","P1-3 — 1 Pizarro and the conquest of the Incas: Key events of the Spanish conquest of Peru"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the struggle for control of Peru among the Spaniards in the years 1537-48. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the Battle of Las Salinas (1538); the rebellion of Gonzalo Pizarro (1544-48). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 requires a clear analysed sequence leading to an outcome; no access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content. Own material might include the terms of Pizarro's agreement of 1529, Almagro's Chilean expedition and seizure of Cuzco, the execution of Almagro, the murder of Francisco Pizarro in 1541, the New Laws, the death of Blasco Nuñez Vela at Añaquito and the mission of Pedro de la Gasca.","sol":"The quarrel was built into the conquest: Pizarro's royal agreement of 1529 had made him governor while Diego de Almagro received far less, and when the treasure of Cajamarca and Cuzco was shared out Almagro was left to look for a conquest of his own in Chile. He came back empty-handed in 1537, seized Cuzco and claimed it lay inside his grant, so the question of who owned the richest city in Peru could only be settled by force. Hernando Pizarro defeated him at Las Salinas in 1538 and had him executed, which ended one claim but created a party of embittered men with nothing to lose; in June 1541 Almagro's supporters broke into the governor's house in Lima and murdered Francisco Pizarro. Because the conquerors had now destroyed one another, the Crown could act, and the New Laws of 1542 created a Viceroyalty of Peru and ordered encomiendas back to the king on their holders' deaths. That threat united the settlers behind Gonzalo Pizarro, who defeated and killed the first viceroy, Blasco Nuñez Vela, at Añaquito in 1546 and governed Peru himself. Charles V answered not with an army but with a negotiator: Pedro de la Gasca arrived with power to pardon and to suspend the hated clauses, Gonzalo's supporters deserted, and his defeat and execution in 1548 ended eleven years of fighting by leaving Peru in royal rather than conquistador hands.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The fighting is between Spaniards, so keep asking what each side thought it owned and who could decide the question.","Watch how the Crown's chance grows as the conquerors kill one another; that is the thread running through the decade.","Choose four dated episodes, then write the connecting sentence between each pair before filling in the detail."],"codes":["P1-3 — 1 Pizarro and the conquest of the Incas: Key events of the Spanish conquest of Peru","P1-3 — 2 Expansion of empire: The role of the viceroys and encomiendas"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the effects of American silver on Spain and its empire in the years 1545-55. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the opening of the mines at Potosí (1545); the foundation of La Paz (1548). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 requires a clear analysed sequence leading to an outcome; no access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content. Own material might include Zacatecas in New Spain, the supply networks and labour drafts serving the mines, mercury refining in New Spain, the royal fifth and the House of Trade, the convoys against French raiders, the loans from the Fugger and Genoese bankers, and rising prices in Spain.","sol":"Silver changed the empire from the inside outwards. The Cerro Rico above Potosí, in the highlands of Upper Peru, was being mined from 1545, and a town of tens of thousands grew on a barren mountain that produced nothing to eat, so everything had to be carried up to it and farms, mule trains and new settlements formed along the roads. La Paz was founded in 1548 on the route between the mines and the coast, its name marking the peace that followed Gonzalo Pizarro's defeat in the same year. In New Spain the same pattern appeared on a smaller scale after silver was found at Zacatecas in 1546. The mines needed labour, so encomienda obligations and Crown labour drafts pushed indigenous villagers into the shafts, and output rose again when a way of refining ore with mercury was worked out in New Spain in the mid-1550s, which made poor ore worth crushing. At the Spanish end the effects were fiscal before they were economic: the royal fifth was registered at the House of Trade in Seville, the fleets were made to sail in convoy against French raiders, and Charles V pledged the incoming treasure to the Fugger and Genoese bankers who financed his wars against France, the Ottomans and the German princes. Prices in Spain rose, Spanish goods grew dear, and the silver crossed the country on its way to Antwerp and Italy rather than staying in it.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Treat the silver as a cause with a chain of effects, first in America and then in Spain, and keep them in that order.","A mine on an empty mountain forces roads, farms and labour into existence before it produces a single coin for the king.","Sketch three stages, the mines, the empire around them and the money in Europe, then write the sentence that carries you from each to the next."],"codes":["P1-3 — 2 Expansion of empire: The significance of the discovery of silver","P1-3 — 3 Impact of the 'New World' on Spain: The importance of gold and silver for the Spanish economy"]},{"q":"Question 3 prints three bullets and asks you to explain two, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the Battle of Cajamarca (1532) for Spanish control of Peru. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid, Level 3 (6-8) requiring analysis of importance in a coherent line of reasoning. No stimulus points and no cap. Credit the capture of a living Sapa Inca and the paralysis of Inca resistance, the ransom, the pattern of ruling through a crowned Inca, the speed and cheapness of the conquest, and the limit reached when Manco revolted.","sol":"Cajamarca matters because it delivered the empire's command structure into Spanish hands in a single afternoon. On 16 November 1532 Pizarro's roughly 168 men trapped Atahuallpa's unarmed retinue in the square, killed thousands and took the Sapa Inca alive. Because the Inca state ran on obedience to one man, its armies did not attack while he was held: orders still went out in his name, the ransom of a room filled once with gold and twice with silver was gathered from temples across the empire, and the Spaniards spent the following months collecting treasure and learning the roads rather than fighting. That is the first kind of importance, since it turned a raiding party into the effective government of Peru. The second is that it set the pattern for what followed. When Atahuallpa was executed in July 1533 Pizarro did not abandon the method but repeated it, entering Cuzco with Manco Inca on 15 November 1533 and having him crowned there that December. Only when Manco escaped and revolted in 1536 did the Spaniards have to fight for Peru outright, and by then they held the capital, a new city on the coast and the loyalty of peoples who had supported Huascar. Cajamarca was therefore not simply the first victory but the reason the conquest was so quick and so cheap in Spanish lives.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Importance is about the difference something made afterwards, so decide first what you are measuring the difference to.","Ask what the Spaniards could do the next day that they could not have done the day before, and for how long that lasted.","Plan two strands of importance and one limit on them, then write a closing sentence that judges between them."],"codes":["P1-3 — 1 Pizarro and the conquest of the Incas: Key events of the Spanish conquest of Peru"]},{"q":"Question 3 prints three bullets and asks you to explain two, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of Las Casas for the treatment of the indigenous peoples of the Spanish Empire. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Credit his authority as a former encomendero turned Dominican, his report of 1542 and its part in the New Laws, the withdrawal of the inheritance clause in 1545 and the rebellion in Peru as limits on his influence, and the Valladolid debate of 1550 to 1551 and the doctrine that indigenous people were the king's free subjects.","sol":"Las Casas matters because he turned the treatment of indigenous people into a question the Crown could not avoid. He had been an encomendero in the Caribbean before becoming a Dominican, so his account of Spanish cruelty came from a man who had shared in it, and the report he wrote in 1542 reached Charles V while the government was already uneasy about the encomenderos' power. The most direct consequence was the New Laws of that year, which banned the enslavement of indigenous people, forbade new encomiendas and ordered existing ones back to the Crown when their holders died. Their fate also shows the limits of his importance: the inheritance clause was withdrawn in 1545 when the colonists resisted, and in Peru resistance became the rebellion that killed the first viceroy in 1546. The lasting effect was on the argument rather than on daily practice. At Valladolid in 1550 and 1551 he answered Sepúlveda's case that the peoples of the Americas were natural inferiors fit for conquest, and although no winner was declared, the Crown had treated their status as a matter for judges and theologians. Officially, indigenous Americans were the king's free subjects who owed tribute and not slaves; encomenderos and miners went on ignoring that, but Las Casas had made it the law's starting point.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["A campaigner's importance is measured in what changed, so separate what he got written into law from what actually happened in America.","Look at both the law of 1542 and what was done to it three years later; the limits are part of the analysis.","Plan one paragraph on the law, one on its failure, one on the argument, then judge which mattered most."],"codes":["P1-3 — 2 Expansion of empire: The role of the viceroys and encomiendas"]},{"q":"Question 3 prints three bullets and asks you to explain two, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the Atlantic slave trade for Spain. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Credit the collapse of the Caribbean indigenous population and the labour shortage on the sugar estates that kept Spain's colonies producing, the Treaty of Alcáçovas of 1479 reserving the African trade to Portugal and the Crown's sale of asientos including the licence of 1518, the revenue the licences brought the Crown at Seville, the answer they gave to critics of the treatment of indigenous people, and Spain's dependence on Portuguese shipping and Genoese credit.","sol":"The trade in enslaved Africans matters for Spain because it supplied the labour that kept the American empire producing. In the Caribbean the indigenous population had collapsed within a generation of Spanish settlement, through overwork and through smallpox, measles and typhus, leaving the sugar estates of Hispaniola and Cuba with nobody to work them. Because the Treaty of Alcáçovas of 1479 had reserved the African trade south of the Canaries to Portugal, and Tordesillas in 1494 left it there, Spain could not fetch captives itself, so the Crown sold licences, called asientos, to merchants; Charles V granted a licence in 1518 to carry several thousand enslaved Africans straight to the Caribbean. The importance for Spain ran in three directions. The Crown gained a revenue it collected at Seville for selling the licences, and a workforce that could be replaced by purchase kept sugar, hides and bullion moving to Spain, where cargoes were taxed and the king took his fifth of the silver. It also gained a way of answering churchmen such as Las Casas who protested at the treatment of indigenous people, since African labour was not held to raise the same questions. But Spain had to buy the trade from others, because the ships, the captives and much of the credit came from Portugal and from Genoese bankers, so the profit of supplying the Indies was earned outside Spain. By the middle of the century Spain's American revenues rested on a labour system Spain itself did not control.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Start from the problem the trade solved for the colonists, and say why the obvious alternative had failed.","Spain had no foothold in Africa, so ask how the captives were obtained and what that dependence meant.","Plan three directions of importance for Spain: the Crown's revenue, the defence of the empire against its critics, and the dependence on foreign ships and credit; then judge which mattered most."],"codes":["P1-3 — 3 Impact of the 'New World' on Spain: The impact on Spain of the slave trade and goods from the Americas"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date did Pizarro's men capture Atahuallpa at Cajamarca?","a":"16 November 1532."},{"q":"Which laws of 1542 forbade new encomiendas and ordered existing ones back to the Crown on their holders' deaths?","a":"The New Laws."},{"q":"Where was the silver mountain that began to be mined in 1545?","a":"The Cerro Rico above Potosí, in Upper Peru, in what is now Bolivia."},{"q":"Which town was founded in 1548 on the road between the mines and the coast, and named for the peace after Gonzalo Pizarro's defeat?","a":"La Paz."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Huayna Capac and the Inca civil war","code":"P1-3 — 1 Pizarro and the conquest of the Incas: Contact with the Incas (1528)","open":false,"back":"Sapa Inca who died in about 1527, probably of smallpox that spread ahead of the Spanish; the heir he named died too. His sons Huascar and Atahuallpa fought, and Atahuallpa's army captured Huascar in 1532."},{"front":"Contact with the Incas, 1528","code":"P1-3 — 1 Pizarro and the conquest of the Incas: Contact with the Incas (1528)","open":false,"back":"Pizarro's second expedition reached the Inca coastal town of Tumbes, where the Spaniards saw gold and silver work, llamas and ordered towns. He then sailed to Spain to ask for a licence to conquer."},{"front":"Pizarro's voyage of 1530","code":"P1-3 — 1 Pizarro and the conquest of the Incas: The voyage of Pizarro (1530)","open":false,"back":"Licensed by the Capitulación de Toledo of 26 July 1529, which made Pizarro governor and left Almagro far less. The expedition left Panama at the end of 1530 and reached Cajamarca with about 168 men, 62 mounted."},{"front":"The Battle of Cajamarca, 16 November 1532","code":"P1-3 — 1 Pizarro and the conquest of the Incas: Key events of the Spanish conquest of Peru","open":false,"back":"Friar Valverde demanded Atahuallpa's submission to Charles V; when it was refused, cannon and cavalry attacked his unarmed retinue in the square. Thousands of Incas died and the Sapa Inca was taken alive."},{"front":"The ransom and execution of Atahuallpa","code":"P1-3 — 1 Pizarro and the conquest of the Incas: Key events of the Spanish conquest of Peru","open":false,"back":"He offered a room filled once with gold and twice with silver for his freedom. It was gathered from temples across the empire; his captors took it, tried him and executed him in July 1533."},{"front":"The revolt of Manco Inca and the siege of Cuzco, 1536-37","code":"P1-3 — 1 Pizarro and the conquest of the Incas: Key events of the Spanish conquest of Peru","open":false,"back":"Crowned by Pizarro in 1533, Manco escaped in 1536 and besieged Cuzco while a second army attacked Lima. Juan Pizarro died at the fortress above Cuzco. The siege failed and Manco withdrew to Vilcabamba."},{"front":"The encomienda","code":"P1-3 — 2 Expansion of empire: The role of the viceroys and encomiendas","open":false,"back":"A grant to a Spaniard of the tribute and labour of a named group of indigenous people, who were owed protection and Christian teaching in return. It granted people, not land, and worked as forced labour."},{"front":"The New Laws, 1542","code":"P1-3 — 2 Expansion of empire: The role of the viceroys and encomiendas","open":false,"back":"Banned enslaving indigenous people, forbade new encomiendas, ordered existing ones back to the Crown on the holder's death, and created the Viceroyalty of Peru. The inheritance clause was withdrawn in 1545."},{"front":"Bartolomé de las Casas and the Valladolid debate","code":"P1-3 — 2 Expansion of empire: The role of the viceroys and encomiendas","open":false,"back":"An encomendero turned Dominican and Protector of the Indians. His report of 1542 helped bring the New Laws, and in 1550-51 he argued against Sepúlveda at Valladolid; no verdict was announced."},{"front":"The House of Trade and the Council of the Indies","code":"P1-3 — 3 Impact of the 'New World' on Spain: The government of the Spanish Empire","open":false,"back":"Seville's House of Trade, set up in 1503, licensed every ship and passenger, registered cargo, took the royal fifth and examined pilots. The Council of the Indies, set up in 1524, drafted laws and named viceroys, bishops and judges."},{"front":"Potosí and Zacatecas","code":"P1-3 — 2 Expansion of empire: The significance of the discovery of silver","open":false,"back":"Silver was found in the Cerro Rico above Potosí, in Upper Peru, in 1545, and at Zacatecas in northern New Spain in 1546. Mercury refining, worked out in New Spain in the mid-1550s, made poor ore worth mining."},{"front":"La Paz, 1548","code":"P1-3 — 2 Expansion of empire: The significance of the discovery of silver","open":false,"back":"Town founded on the road carrying Potosí's silver towards the coast. Its name, Our Lady of Peace, marked the end of Gonzalo Pizarro's rebellion, which was crushed in the same year."},{"front":"American bullion and the Spanish Crown","code":"P1-3 — 3 Impact of the 'New World' on Spain: The importance of gold and silver for the Spanish economy","open":false,"back":"The quinto real gave the king a fifth of all bullion. Charles V pledged it to the Fugger and Genoese bankers to pay for wars against France, the Ottomans and the German princes; prices in Spain rose steeply."},{"front":"Cargoes across the Atlantic","code":"P1-3 — 3 Impact of the 'New World' on Spain: The impact on Spain of the slave trade and goods from the Americas","open":false,"back":"Outward, enslaved Africans carried under licences, or asientos, sold by the Crown from 1518. Homeward, bullion first, then cochineal dye, hides and sugar, and the new crops: maize, potatoes, tomatoes, beans, chillies and cacao."},{"front":"Name one consequence of the New Laws of 1542","code":"P1-3 — 2 Expansion of empire: The role of the viceroys and encomiendas","open":true,"back":"The encomenderos of Peru rebelled under Gonzalo Pizarro and killed the first viceroy of Peru at Añaquito in 1546; Charles V withdrew the inheritance clause in 1545; the Crown sent Pedro de la Gasca, who defeated the rebels in 1548."}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Paper 2 booklet P Question 2, narrative account, 8 marks)","items":["The question: 'Write a narrative account analysing the Spanish Crown's handling of American treasure in the years 1533-55.' You may use the following in your answer: the ransom of Atahuallpa (1533); the loans of the Fugger bankers of Augsburg. You must also use information of your own.","The paragraph: The treasure of Cajamarca reached the Crown as a share rather than as a prize, because the rule was in place before the metal was gathered: one fifth of all bullion, the quinto real, belonged to the king. Atahuallpa's ransom was collected from temples and palaces across the empire and melted into standard bars during 1533, and melting is what made the fifth easy to take, since bars could be weighed, stamped and counted where worked objects could not. That is why the Crown's next move was administrative rather than military. Every homeward cargo had to be registered at the House of Trade in Seville, so the king knew what was coming before it was unloaded, and because he knew, he could borrow against it: Charles V pledged incoming bullion to the Fugger bankers of Augsburg and to Genoese financiers, whose loans paid his armies in the wars against France, against the Ottomans and against the German Protestant princes. By the time American silver was arriving in quantity after 1545 the treasure was being spent in Europe before it reached Spain, and in 1557, two years after this period closes, the Crown had to suspend payments to those same bankers.","Why this is Level 3 (6-8 marks): it organises material into a clear sequence leading to an outcome and analyses the linkage between the events, because each sentence says why the next thing followed from the last, and it carries accurate, precisely selected information (the royal fifth, the melting of the ransom in 1533, registration at the House of Trade, the Fugger and Genoese loans, the wars they paid for, the suspension of 1557), which is the AO1 bullet at that level. It also addresses three or more aspects of content, which the cap requires."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["The same question: 'Write a narrative account analysing the Spanish Crown's handling of American treasure in the years 1533-55.'","The paragraph: After the Spanish captured Atahuallpa he offered them a room full of gold so that they would let him go. The gold was brought in from all over the empire and melted down, and the king got a share of it. Later on a lot of silver was found in South America and in Mexico and it was carried back to Spain in ships. The king spent the money on wars in Europe against France and other countries, and he also borrowed from bankers. In the end he could not pay the bankers back. This shows that the Crown was handling the treasure that came from America.","Why this is Level 2 (3-5 marks): it does meet the Level 2 descriptor, because it gives 'some organisation of material into a sequence of events leading to an outcome' with 'some analysis of the linkage between them', and adds accurate information. It misses Level 3 because the sequence is not analysed: the king's share is mentioned without naming the quinto real or saying why bars could be taxed when worked objects could not, no date, place or name is attached to the mines or to the bankers, the borrowing is dropped in without any link to the registration at Seville that made it possible, and the last sentence asserts a conclusion instead of showing how one event produced the next."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the death of Huayna Capac and the Inca civil war","items":["The Inca empire ran from modern Ecuador through Peru and Bolivia into Chile, held together by paved roads, storehouses, garrisons and compulsory labour service owed to the Sapa Inca.","Huayna Capac, the Sapa Inca, died in about 1527.","He almost certainly died of smallpox, which had spread south from the Caribbean and Central America years ahead of any Spaniard.","The heir he had named died in the same epidemic, leaving no agreed successor.","Huascar was accepted as Sapa Inca at Cuzco, the capital and religious centre.","Atahuallpa held Quito and the veteran army of the northern campaigns.","The war between the brothers was fought from the late 1520s into 1532.","Atahuallpa's generals defeated Huascar's forces and took him prisoner near Cuzco in 1532.","Atahuallpa was resting at Cajamarca after that victory when Pizarro's men reached him in November 1532.","Peoples the Incas had conquered, and Huascar's defeated supporters, later gave the Spaniards thousands of allies.","The point to make in an answer: disease reached the Incas before the Spaniards did, so the empire was weakened before it was invaded."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: contact with the Incas (1528) and the voyage of Pizarro (1530)","items":["Francisco Pizarro came from Trujillo in Extremadura, settled in Panama and had crossed to the Pacific with Balboa in 1513.","His partners were the soldier Diego de Almagro and the priest Hernando de Luque.","A first expedition southwards in the mid-1520s achieved nothing but hardship.","On the second expedition his men reached the Inca coastal town of Tumbes in 1528 and saw gold and silver work, llamas and well-ordered towns.","Pizarro sailed to Spain and obtained the Capitulación de Toledo from the Crown on 26 July 1529.","The agreement named him governor and captain-general of the lands he conquered and gave Almagro much less, which bred the rivalry that later split the conquerors.","He returned to Panama in 1530 and the expedition sailed for Peru at the end of that year.","The force landed on the Peruvian coast in 1531 and climbed into the highlands in 1532.","At Cajamarca it numbered about 168 men, 62 of them on horseback, with a few cannon and firearms.","Horses, steel and gunpowder were new to the Incas, but surprise, the civil war and disease mattered more.","The expedition was a private venture financed by its leaders and licensed by the Crown, which is why it ended in a quarrel over the spoils."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Battle of Cajamarca and the capture of Atahuallpa","items":["Date and place: 16 November 1532, in the square of the highland town of Cajamarca.","Atahuallpa entered the square with a large unarmed retinue, confident that a few strangers could do nothing.","The Dominican friar Vicente de Valverde demanded his submission to Charles V and to the Christian faith.","Atahuallpa refused and threw down the book he was handed.","Pizarro's cannon and cavalry charged the crowded square; thousands of Incas were killed and the Spaniards lost almost nobody.","Atahuallpa was taken alive, which mattered more than the killing, because orders continued to run in his name.","He offered a room filled once with gold and twice with silver in exchange for his freedom.","The ransom was gathered from temples and palaces across the empire and melted into bars, mostly during the first half of 1533.","While a prisoner, Atahuallpa ordered the killing of his brother Huascar.","Atahuallpa was tried by his captors and executed in July 1533.","The Spaniards entered Cuzco with Manco Inca on 15 November 1533 and crowned him there in December 1533."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the revolt of the Incas and the siege of Cuzco (1536-37)","items":["Manco Inca, a son of Huayna Capac, was made Sapa Inca by Pizarro in 1533 and ruled under Spanish control.","Lima was founded on the coastal plain in January 1535 and became the seat of Spanish government in Peru.","Manco escaped in 1536 after ill-treatment by Spaniards in Cuzco, and raised the provinces.","His armies besieged Cuzco from 1536 into 1537, holding the heights above the city.","Juan Pizarro, one of Francisco's brothers, was killed in the fighting for the fortress of Sacsayhuaman above Cuzco.","A second Inca force attacked Lima but was beaten on the coastal plain, where Spanish cavalry fought at its best.","The siege broke down as supplies ran short and warriors had to return to their fields.","Manco withdrew to Vilcabamba in the eastern mountains, where a rump Inca state survived until 1572.","After the revolt the Spaniards governed directly and no longer needed an Inca figurehead.","The impact of the conquest: deaths in war, epidemic disease, forced labour, the melting down of gold and silver work, and the imposition of Spanish rule, language and Christianity."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the conquistador wars in Peru, 1537-48","items":["Almagro was sent to Chile in 1535 and returned in 1537 having found no treasure.","He seized Cuzco, claiming that the city lay inside his own grant from the Crown.","Hernando Pizarro defeated him at the Battle of Las Salinas, near Cuzco, in 1538.","Almagro was executed after the battle.","Almagro's supporters murdered Francisco Pizarro in his house in Lima in June 1541.","Diego de Almagro the Younger was defeated the following year, in 1542, and executed.","The New Laws of 1542 created the Viceroyalty of Peru and threatened the encomiendas.","Gonzalo Pizarro, Francisco's brother, led the encomenderos' rebellion from 1544.","The first viceroy, Blasco Nuñez Vela, was defeated and killed at Añaquito in 1546.","Pedro de la Gasca was sent with power to pardon and to suspend the unpopular clauses; Gonzalo's army deserted and he was defeated and executed in 1548.","The wars left Peru under royal government rather than conquistador rule."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Las Casas, the New Laws (1542) and the viceroys","items":["The encomienda granted a Spaniard the tribute and labour of a named group of indigenous people in return for protection and Christian instruction; it was not a grant of land.","Bartolomé de las Casas had been an encomendero in Hispaniola and Cuba before joining the Dominicans in the early 1520s.","He was named Protector of the Indians and campaigned at the Spanish court for some forty years.","He wrote his account of the destruction of the Indies in 1542; it was printed ten years later, in 1552.","He was Bishop of Chiapa from 1544 until he gave up the see in 1550.","The New Laws, issued in 1542, banned the enslavement of indigenous people, forbade new encomiendas, ordered existing ones back to the Crown on the holder's death and limited forced labour.","They created the Viceroyalty of Peru, with a viceroy and a high court, the audiencia, at Lima.","The Viceroyalty of New Spain had been created in 1535, with Antonio de Mendoza as its first viceroy; he became viceroy of Peru in 1551.","Charles V withdrew the clause ending the inheritance of encomiendas in 1545, after colonial resistance.","At Valladolid in 1550 and 1551 Las Casas argued against Juan Ginés de Sepúlveda before a royal commission; no verdict was announced.","The friars mattered as much as the laws: Franciscans reached Mexico in 1524, Dominicans and Augustinians followed, and mass baptisms, mission churches and the destruction of temples went with the encomienda."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: silver in Bolivia and Mexico, and the foundation of La Paz (1548)","items":["Silver was found in the Cerro Rico above Potosí, in Upper Peru, in what is now Bolivia, in 1545.","The mining town of Potosí was founded in the same year and grew into one of the largest cities of the Spanish world.","Potosí stands at over four thousand metres on ground that grows almost no food, so everything had to be carried in.","Silver was found at Zacatecas in northern New Spain in 1546, and other Mexican strikes followed.","Labour came from indigenous villages under encomienda obligations and, later, under Crown labour drafts.","A way of refining silver ore with mercury was worked out in New Spain in the mid-1550s, making poorer ore worth mining.","La Paz was founded in 1548 on the road between the mines and the coast.","Its name, Our Lady of Peace, marked the end of Gonzalo Pizarro's rebellion in the same year.","Silver, not gold, became the empire's main export after the 1540s; the gold of the conquest was a single windfall of plunder.","The Crown took the quinto real, one fifth of all bullion produced."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the government of the Spanish Empire — the House of Trade, the Council of the Indies and the fleets","items":["The House of Trade, the Casa de Contratación, was set up at Seville in 1503.","It licensed every ship and every passenger bound for the Indies and registered every cargo.","It collected the royal fifth and the customs duties, and kept the master chart of the Atlantic.","It trained and examined the pilots who sailed the route; Sebastián Cabot was its pilot major from 1518.","Cabot's expedition of 1526 was meant for the Moluccas but explored the Río de la Plata instead; he returned to Spain in 1530 in disgrace.","The Council of the Indies was established in 1524 as the king's standing council for American business.","It drafted the laws, including the New Laws of 1542, and nominated viceroys, bishops and judges.","It heard appeals from the audiencias and reviewed returning officials by residencia, a public examination of their conduct.","From the 1520s ships were ordered to sail together against French raiders; this grew into the flota system of escorted yearly fleets.","All legal trade passed through Seville, which made the city rich and kept the Indies a royal monopoly."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the impact of the New World on Spain — bullion, enslaved people and goods","items":["The Crown's share of all bullion was the quinto real, one fifth, registered at the House of Trade as it landed.","Charles V pledged incoming treasure to the Fugger bankers of Augsburg and to Genoese financiers, often before it had sailed.","Their loans paid his armies in the wars against France, against the Ottomans and against the German Protestant princes.","Much of the silver therefore went straight on to Antwerp or Italy and never reached Spain at all.","Prices in Spain rose faster and earlier than in most of Europe across the sixteenth century, hurting people on fixed wages and rents.","Spanish goods became dear beside foreign ones, so Spaniards bought abroad what they might have made at home.","In 1557, two years after this period closes, the Crown had to suspend payments to its bankers.","The Treaty of Alcáçovas of 1479 reserved the trade of the African coast south of the Canaries to Portugal, so Spain held no slaving posts of its own; Tordesillas in 1494 divided the Atlantic without altering that.","The Crown instead sold licences, called asientos, to merchants; Charles V granted a large licence in 1518 to carry enslaved Africans straight to the Caribbean.","Enslaved Africans were taken to the sugar estates of Hispaniola and Cuba as the indigenous population of the Caribbean collapsed.","Homeward cargoes included bullion, cochineal dye from New Spain, hides and sugar.","The new crops were maize, potatoes, beans, tomatoes, chillies, cacao and tobacco; maize and potatoes spread first as fodder and famine food."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 2 booklet P Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the Crown's defence and regulation of the Atlantic route in the years 1526-55' (8 marks)","items":["The question as the paper prints it: 'Write a narrative account analysing the Crown's defence and regulation of the Atlantic route in the years 1526-55.' (8) You may use the following in your answer: the order that ships sail together (1526); Sebastián Cabot's return from the Río de la Plata (1530). You must also use information of your own.","Decoded: the examiner wants an account that does not only describe what happened but finds the connections, so each episode must be shown to grow out of the one before. The concepts in play are causation and consequence, and the dates fix the span: begin with the order of 1526 that ships should cross in company and end with the escorted, timetabled crossing of the mid-1550s.","The cap that applies: 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' Use the two bullets, but three or more distinct aspects must appear, so plan at least four episodes.","Timing: booklet P allows about 55 minutes for its 32 marks, so roughly 13 minutes on this 8-mark question, including two minutes to choose and order the episodes. There are no SPaG marks on Paper 2.","Paragraph 1, the danger and the first answer (1523-26): French corsairs were taking Spanish shipping on the approaches to Europe, and in 1523 Jean Fleury seized two of the vessels carrying Cortés's Mexican treasure home, off Cape St Vincent. The Crown answered in 1526 with a royal order that ships in the Indies trade sail in company instead of alone, enforced by the House of Trade at Seville. Say what the Crown was defending: not the ships only, but a revenue it had already counted and in part already spent.","The link out of paragraph 1: sailing in company works only if somebody can say who sails, when and under whose command, so defending the route pulled regulation of it along behind.","Paragraph 2, the limits of a licence (1526-30): Sebastián Cabot, the House of Trade's own pilot major since 1518, took a royal expedition out in 1526 that was fitted for the Moluccas and spent it instead on the Río de la Plata; he came home empty-handed in 1530 and was punished with banishment. This is the point where the Crown could see that licensing a voyage was not the same as controlling one.","The link out of paragraph 2: the answer was to regulate the route itself rather than to rely on the man commanding at sea.","Paragraph 3, the system (1543-55): fleet regulations settled at Seville in 1543 fixed escorted sailings, and by the 1550s the crossing had become guarded convoys on set routes, with every ship licensed, every passenger listed, every cargo registered at the House of Trade and the royal fifth taken as it landed, while the same office kept the master chart and examined the pilots who sailed the run. Defence and regulation had become one thing.","Paragraph 4, the point beyond the stimulus: why the system became worth what it cost. From the mid-1540s American silver turned an occasional windfall into a steady annual flow, and because the king's fifth and the merchants' cargoes were registered before they were unloaded, the convoys were carrying the security for the loans that paid Charles V's armies in Europe. A route that had been worth guarding became one the Crown could not do without.","The second half of that point, still beyond the stimulus: the Council of the Indies, at work since 1524, supplied the law behind the shipping rules, drafting the orders, nominating the officials who enforced them and reviewing returning officers by residencia, so the Atlantic was governed by a standing council in Spain and not by whoever happened to be commanding at sea.","The evaluation thread that runs through the whole account: every measure was a reaction to a loss - a captured treasure ship, a wasted expedition, a raided coast - and each reaction narrowed what a captain could decide for himself, so the Crown gained control of the Atlantic by degrees and always after the event.","What the closing sentence must say: by the middle of the 1550s the Crown had not made the Atlantic safe, and raiders were still taking ships, but it had turned an open ocean into a licensed, escorted and registered route, so the treasure of the Indies could be counted and pledged at Seville before anyone at sea could decide what became of it."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:29:21Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"The retarget holds: nothing in the four rebuilt worked examples (Tumbes 1528, viceregal government 1535-51, indigenous allies, cochineal and Zacatecas), the treasure model-paragraph pair (1533-55, ransom and Fugger bullets) or the Atlantic-route plan (1526-55) sets or shares the focus and stimulus of any of the sixteen bank questions, dupscan prints 'no duplication', and no stem copies the specimen booklet P1, which asks for two consequences of the Spanish invasion of Mexico. Web-checked and correct: Tumbes reached April 1528 and Pizarro home with gold, silver and llamas; the Capitulacion de Toledo of 26 July 1529 with Almagro left far poorer; Mendoza viceroy of New Spain 14 November 1535 to November 1550 and of Peru from 23 September 1551, dead 21 July 1552; the audiencia of Lima created 20 November 1542 and sitting from 1543; Nunez Vela sworn in 17 May 1544 and killed at Anaquito 18 January 1546; some two thousand Canari and Chachapoya allies inside Cuzco in 1536-37 and Paullu crowned in 1537; Charles V asking Cortes for cochineal in 1523 and the dye second only to bullion; Zacatecas silver 8 September 1546 with the camp of 1548; Fleury taking two of the three Cortes treasure ships off Cape St Vincent in 1523; the order of about 1526 against sailing alone and the fleet ordinance of 1543; Cabot pilot major from 1518, out in 1526 for the Moluccas, home in 1530 and banished; the ransom room filled once with gold and twice with silver, melted into bars with the quinto real taken first. Every grid, tariff, AO split and cap matches levels.md in its June 2024-25 form. Three residuals only: a false 'without a rebellion'¹, the 1526 order given to the wrong author² and a dangling 'the first'³."}}},"P2-1":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"P2-1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 1 is where British America is built and where its fault lines first show, and booklet P tests it in three ways: the two consequence parts of Question 1, the analytical narrative of Question 2, and the importance parts of Question 3. Almost everything an examiner can ask about the years 1713 to 1741 comes from four stories that run into one another: a colonial population that nearly trebled and pushed inland; a trading system designed in London and worked around in the colonies; a pirate war in American waters that Britain won between 1717 and 1719; and the growth of slavery to the point where South Carolina had an enslaved majority, producing the rising at Stono in 1739 and the trials in New York in 1741. This room teaches those stories as chains of cause and effect, with the dates, names and figures an examiner rewards, and it trains the three moves booklet P pays for: explaining a consequence instead of retelling an event, writing a narrative whose episodes are joined rather than listed, and judging what difference a person, a law or a rising made to what came afterwards.","idea":[{"code":"P2-1 — 1 Developments in colonial society: The impact of expansion and immigration on society","h":"A population that nearly trebled: where the newcomers went and whom they annoyed","t":"British mainland North America grew astonishingly fast in this period. Its settler and enslaved population was a little over 330,000 in 1710 and about 900,000 by 1740, nearly three times as many in thirty years, and much of that growth came from arrivals rather than births. From 1717 Protestants from Ulster, usually called the Scots-Irish, began leaving in waves, in 1717 to 1718, 1725 to 1729 and 1740 to 1741, and German-speaking families from the Rhineland sailed for Pennsylvania in the same years; enslaved Africans were landed in the southern ports in larger numbers than ever before. Settlement had three shapes. New England, that is Massachusetts, Connecticut, Rhode Island and New Hampshire, was a country of townships, small family farms, fishing, timber and shipbuilding. The middle colonies, New York, New Jersey, Pennsylvania and Delaware, grew wheat and were the most mixed in language and religion. The southern colonies, Maryland, Virginia, North Carolina, South Carolina and, from 1732, Georgia, were plantation country. Because the good tidewater land was taken, newcomers went inland and south-west through the Shenandoah valley into the backcountry, which produced three kinds of friction: with Native peoples whose land was being taken, with the coastal planter gentry who dominated the assemblies and taxed the frontier without defending it, and inside the colonies between an Anglican establishment and Presbyterian and German congregations. A top-level answer does not list the colonies: it uses expansion as a cause, showing that where people went decided who quarrelled with whom, and that a colony's politics followed its land and its labour."},{"code":"P2-1 — 1 Developments in colonial society: Economic developments","h":"Tobacco, molasses and mercantilism: an economy built to serve Britain, and the pirates in the way","t":"Mercantilism was the idea that colonies exist to supply the mother country with raw materials and to buy its manufactures, and the Navigation Acts enforced it. Colonial goods had to travel in British or colonial ships; certain 'enumerated' goods, tobacco and sugar from 1660, rice and molasses soon after 1700, and furs and copper from 1721, had to be landed in Britain first; and European goods bound for the colonies had to pass through British ports. Tobacco was the largest export of the mainland colonies, grown along the tidal rivers of Virginia and Maryland, packed into hogsheads and shipped to Britain, where much of it was re-exported to Europe. Quality mattered so much that Virginia's assembly passed the Tobacco Inspection Act of 1730, proposed by Lieutenant Governor William Gooch, which made planters bring leaf to licensed warehouses where inspectors burned what was substandard and issued notes that circulated as money. The second great trade ran to the West Indies: New England and the middle colonies sold salt fish, barrel staves, timber, horses and flour to the sugar islands and brought back molasses and sugar to distil into rum. On top of this sat piracy. The Treaty of Utrecht in 1713 ended the War of the Spanish Succession and threw thousands of privateers and seamen out of work, and many turned pirate, using the Bahamas as a base. A top-level answer treats the colonial economy as a system with pressure points, and shows that piracy mattered because it attacked the shipping on which both the tobacco trade and the West Indies trade depended."},{"code":"P2-1 — 1 Developments in colonial society: The suppression of piracy in American waters","h":"Pardon, statute and force: how Britain closed the pirate war, 1717 to 1719","t":"Two pirates dominate the specification. Samuel Bellamy, called 'Black Sam', captured the slave ship Whydah Gally in the Bahamas in February 1717 and had taken dozens of vessels within about a year, but the Whydah was wrecked in a storm off Cape Cod on 26 April 1717 and Bellamy drowned; the survivors were tried at Boston that October and most were hanged. Edward Teach, 'Blackbeard', commanded a captured French slave ship he renamed the Queen Anne's Revenge, blockaded the harbour of Charles Town in May 1718, seizing ships and holding hostages until he was given a chest of medicines, and ran his flagship aground at Beaufort Inlet in June 1718. Britain answered in three ways. King George I's Proclamation for Suppressing of Pirates of 5 September 1717 offered a pardon to any pirate who surrendered to a governor by 5 September 1718, and rewards to anyone who took those who did not; a second proclamation later extended the deadline to July 1719. Trials did not have to wait on London: the Piracy Act 1698 (11 and 12 William III c. 7), in force from 1700, renewed in 1706 and made perpetual in 1719, already allowed piracy to be tried by royal commission in the colonies themselves, which is how Bellamy's men came to be tried at Boston in October 1717. What Parliament added was the Piracy Act of 1717 (4 George I c. 11, the same statute that set up transportation to the colonies), which received the royal assent on 21 March 1718: it tightened the law on piracy, laid down the manner in which pirates were to be tried, denied them benefit of clergy and confirmed that those who helped or traded with them could be tried and hanged as pirates too. Then came force. Alexander Spotswood, Lieutenant Governor of Virginia from 1710 to 1722, hired two sloops crewed by Royal Navy sailors under Lieutenant Robert Maynard, who found Teach at Ocracoke Inlet on 22 November 1718 and killed him; the Virginia assembly voted rewards for taking pirates that same month, and the surviving crew were tried at Williamsburg in 1719 and most were hanged. A top-level answer explains the three together: the pardon split the pirates, the statute made a conviction hard to escape, and the killing of Teach showed the pardon's deadline would be enforced, so piracy in American waters was broken within a few years."},{"code":"P2-1 — 2 Slavery in North America: The transatlantic slave trade and 'Atlantic trade triangle'","h":"Three legs and one purpose: how the trade worked and why British merchants wanted it","t":"The triangle began in Britain. Ships from London, Bristol and increasingly Liverpool sailed to the West African coast carrying goods Africans would trade for: guns and gunpowder, iron and copper bars, textiles, brandy and rum. On the second leg, the Middle Passage, the enslaved people bought or seized on the coast were carried across the Atlantic, chained below decks for six to ten weeks in conditions in which roughly one in seven died. On the third leg the ships carried sugar, molasses, rum, tobacco and rice back to Britain, so that a single voyage was profitable three times over. Two changes made the trade grow in this period. The monopoly of the Royal African Company was broken in 1698, opening the trade to any British merchant who paid a duty, and at the Treaty of Utrecht in 1713 Britain won the asiento, the contract to supply Spanish America with 4,800 enslaved Africans a year for thirty years, which was handed to the South Sea Company and also gave cover for a great deal of smuggling into Spanish ports. Most captives were carried to the Caribbean and Brazil; the mainland colonies took only a small share of the whole trade, but the numbers landed at Charles Town and in the Chesapeake rose sharply after 1713. A top-level answer keeps the triangle as an explanation rather than a diagram: each leg existed because the next one paid, so the growth of rice and tobacco plantations in America and the growth of British ports were the same process seen from two ends."},{"code":"P2-1 — 2 Slavery in North America: The impact of slavery on the development of plantations","h":"Tobacco and rice: two crops, two labour systems, one dependence","t":"Slavery did not merely serve the plantation economy, it made it possible. Tobacco in Virginia and Maryland was grown by gangs working under an overseer from dawn to dusk; it exhausted the soil within a few years, so planters constantly needed fresh land, which pushed settlement inland and pressed on Native territory. Rice was different. It had been established in the South Carolina lowcountry from the 1690s, first in inland swamps and later in embanked tidal fields that had to be dug, diked and flooded, and planters deliberately sought captives from the rice-growing regions of West Africa because they knew the crop. Rice work was organised by the task system, in which an enslaved person who finished a set task had the rest of the day, but the swamps were malarial and deaths were very high, so the workforce had to be replaced by fresh imports rather than by natural increase. The result was a plantation economy that could not shrink its labour force without collapsing, and a demography to match: by 1740 people of African descent made up about two thirds of South Carolina's population, roughly 39,000 people out of about 56,000. A top-level answer links crop to consequence: the type of plantation explains the type of work, the type of work explains the scale of importing, and the scale of importing explains both the wealth of the planter elite and the fear that ran through white colonial society."},{"code":"P2-1 — 2 Slavery in North America: The impact of slavery on colonial society","h":"Law, fear and flight: the position of enslaved people and what happened to those who ran","t":"Colonial law turned slavery into a lifelong, hereditary and racial condition. Virginia's slave code of 1705 gathered earlier laws and defined enslaved people as property that could be bought, sold, inherited and left by will; South Carolina's code of 1712 required a written pass for travel and authorised whipping, branding and mutilation. Enslaved people had no legal marriage, could own nothing securely and could not give evidence against a white person, and punishment was carried out privately by owners. Slavery shaped free society too. Planters who owned the most people dominated the colonial assemblies, so the laws protecting slavery were made by the men who profited from it, while poorer white men were enrolled in slave patrols that rode the roads at night checking passes, which bound them to a system from which they gained little. Flight was the commonest form of resistance. Newspapers carried advertisements describing runaways and offering rewards; some tried to reach towns and pass as free, some reached Native communities; and from South Carolina and Georgia many went south. Spain had offered them a reason to. King Charles II of Spain ordered in 1693 that runaways from the British colonies who converted to Catholicism and served Spain should be free, a royal decree of 1733 confirmed it and refused compensation to British owners, and in 1738 the governor of Florida settled the free black community of Gracia Real de Santa Teresa de Mose, known as Fort Mose, about two miles north of St Augustine. A top-level answer shows the connection: the harder the law pressed, the more valuable escape became, and because escape had somewhere to go, every South Carolina planter read a neighbouring empire as a standing threat."},{"code":"P2-1 — 3 Problems within the colonies: Slave revolts in the Carolinas","h":"Stono, 9 September 1739: a march towards Florida and the law that followed it","t":"On Sunday 9 September 1739 about twenty enslaved men gathered near the Stono river, some twenty miles from Charles Town. Their leader was called Jemmy and was described by witnesses as Angolan, probably from the Kingdom of Kongo, where men had military training and many were Catholic. They broke into Hutchenson's store at Stono Bridge, killed the two storekeepers, Robert Bathurst and a Mr Gibbs, and took guns and powder. Then they marched south towards Spanish Florida, beating drums, carrying a banner and calling for liberty, burning houses as they went; their numbers grew to perhaps sixty and they killed between twenty and twenty-five white colonists. Lieutenant Governor William Bull saw the column on the road, rode off and raised the militia, which caught them at the Edisto river that afternoon; many were killed in the fight and others were hunted down and executed over the following weeks. Two things made it possible and one thing made it terrifying: South Carolina had an enslaved majority, Spain had promised freedom in Florida, and Britain and Spain were on the edge of war, which was declared in October 1739. The answer was the Negro Act, passed on 10 May 1740, which banned enslaved people from assembling, from moving without a written pass, from earning money, from growing food for sale, from beating drums and from being taught to write, and required one white person on a plantation for every ten enslaved. A top-level answer treats Stono as a hinge: it explains the rising from the conditions that produced it and then follows its effects forward into a code that made daily life harsher for people who had never been near the Stono road."},{"code":"P2-1 — 3 Problems within the colonies: The New York Conspiracy (1741)","h":"New York, 1741: thirteen fires, one witness and thirty-four executions","t":"New York was a northern city with a southern proportion of slavery: of roughly 10,000 inhabitants about 2,000 were enslaved, around one in five, and they lived in their owners' houses and worked on the docks and in the workshops, so the city could not keep them apart from each other or from poor white people. In the winter of 1740 to 1741, which was bitterly cold and expensive, and with Britain at war with Spain since October 1739 and rumours of Spanish and Catholic plotting everywhere, a fire broke out at Fort George on 18 March 1741 which destroyed the governor's house and chapel, and about a dozen more fires followed over three weeks. Mary Burton, a sixteen-year-old Irish indentured servant at John Hughson's waterfront tavern, had been arrested over stolen goods; questioned, she said that Hughson and enslaved men who drank there were plotting to burn the city, kill the white men and make Hughson king. On her evidence and on confessions extracted from prisoners who were promised their lives, about 152 black and 20 white people were arrested. Thirteen black men were burned at the stake, seventeen black people and four white people were hanged, among them Hughson, his wife, Margaret Kerry and a schoolmaster, John Ury, accused of being a Catholic priest; about seventy black people were transported out of the colony and seven white people banished. Burton collected the reward of a hundred pounds, and the trials stopped only when she began naming respectable citizens. A top-level answer does not settle whether the plot was real; it explains why a city that size believed it, and what the belief did to people who had committed no crime."},{"code":"P2-1 — 3 Problems within the colonies: Smuggling and customs revenue","h":"Duties nobody paid: the Molasses Act, the customs service and the fight for the fur trade","t":"The Molasses Act of 1733 was passed to protect British West Indian planters against the cheaper French islands, and it put sixpence a gallon on foreign molasses, ninepence a gallon on foreign rum and five shillings a hundredweight on foreign sugar entering the colonies. New England's distilleries could not have paid it and stayed in business, so they did not: merchants bought from the French and Dutch islands, falsified papers, landed cargoes at night and bribed the collectors, and the Act raised almost nothing. Britain's machinery for stopping them was thin. Collectors sat in the main ports under the Commissioners of Customs in London, and the Navigation Act of 1696 had set up vice-admiralty courts in the colonies so that customs cases could be heard by a judge without a local jury, but officers were few, the coastline was enormous, colonial juries would not convict their neighbours and Sir Robert Walpole, in office from 1721 to 1742, judged that a flourishing colonial trade was worth more than strictly collected duties, a policy later nicknamed salutary neglect. The fur trade was the same problem in another form. Albany was the English fur market, buying from the Haudenosaunee, who had made peace with the French at Montreal in 1701 and dealt with both sides; New York passed a law in 1720 forbidding the sale of English trade goods to the French at Montreal, and Albany merchants evaded it, and at Oswego on Lake Ontario, where Albany men had been trading from a blockhouse since 1722, Governor William Burnet had a stone blockhouse built in 1727, later called Fort Burnet, to catch the furs before they reached the French. A top-level answer keeps the argument general: British regulation of colonial trade in this period was not resisted by protest but dissolved by evasion, and that habit of evasion is what later governments would have to break."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1(a): turning the Navigation Acts into one explained consequence","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(a): 'Explain one consequence of the Navigation Acts.' [4 marks]. Question 1 is printed in two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each asking for one consequence of a separate event or development and each marked separately out of 4, so this part needs one consequence analysed and supported, not two described. The information you are working from: the Acts required colonial goods to travel in British or colonial ships; certain 'enumerated' goods had to be landed in Britain before they could go anywhere else, tobacco and sugar from 1660, rice and molasses soon after 1700 and furs and copper from 1721; European goods bound for the colonies had to pass through British ports first; tobacco was the largest export of the mainland colonies, grown along the tidal rivers of Virginia and Maryland and shipped to Britain, where much of it was re-exported to Europe; a planter who shipped his crop to Britain sold it through a merchant house there and took his return in goods and credit rather than in cash.","steps":["Decide which of the Acts' rules to build the answer on, because a rule about where goods may be sold bites harder than a rule about who may carry them: take the enumeration of tobacco.","State the consequence as a change of state rather than a rule: after enumeration a Chesapeake planter no longer had a market of his own, because the only lawful first buyer of his crop stood on the other side of the Atlantic.","Support it with the precise detail already in front of you: tobacco could be landed nowhere but Britain, so the European customers who took much of the crop were reached by British re-exporters and never by the man who grew it.","Make the analytical link the grid rewards: because the planter could not choose his buyer, both the price he got and the goods he received were settled by his British agent, so he shipped on trust, took his return in credit and was bound to the house that sold for him.","Check the answer against the question before moving on, cutting any sentence that describes what the Acts said rather than saying what followed from them."],"cues":["Which of the Acts' rules would change a planter's life most, and why?","What became true of a Virginia planter's market that had not been true before?","Which detail in the setup shows where his crop finally ended up?","If you cannot choose your buyer, who ends up deciding your price?","How can you tell a describing sentence from an explaining one in your own writing?"]},{"title":"Question 2: planning a narrative account of the destruction of Bellamy's company","setup":"Booklet P, Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the destruction of Samuel Bellamy's pirate company in the years 1716 to 1717.' (8 marks). The booklet prints: 'You may use the following in your answer: the capture of the Whydah Gally (February 1717); the trial of the Whydah's survivors at Boston (October 1717). You must also use information of your own.' The mark scheme shuts an answer out of Level 3 altogether if it does not address three or more aspects of content. What you know: the war ended in 1713 and left privateers and seamen out of work, and many of them gathered in the Bahamas; Bellamy sailed under Benjamin Hornigold until the crew voted Hornigold out in the summer of 1716, reportedly because he would not attack English shipping, and elected Bellamy in his place; the company took dozens of vessels, by one count more than fifty, in about a year; in late February 1717 Bellamy chased the Whydah Gally for three days and took her, a London slave ship homeward bound from Jamaica under Captain Lawrence Prince, and gave Prince his own ship, the Sultana, in exchange; the Whydah was a fast ship of about three hundred tons which Bellamy fitted out as his flagship; on 26 April 1717 a storm drove her ashore off Cape Cod and she went over, drowning Bellamy and all but two of a crew of about 146, while a consort, the Mary Anne, was wrecked a few miles away and her men came ashore; the prisoners were tried at Boston in October 1717 before a court sitting under the royal commission the Piracy Act of 1698 had allowed in the colonies since 1700, and six of them were hanged on 15 November 1717.","steps":["Choose a spine of three or four dated episodes so the account has a sequence and not a heap: the election of a new captain, the taking of the Whydah, the wreck off Cape Cod and the trial at Boston.","Count the separate aspects of content those episodes cover, because access to Level 3 turns on there being three or more, and note which are your own material rather than the printed bullets: the demobilisation after the war, Hornigold's deposition, the wreck itself and the hangings that followed the trial.","Write the linking sentence between the first two episodes, since that is what analysis means here: a crew that voted out a captain for sparing English ships had chosen a harder trade, and it was that appetite which kept Bellamy chasing a rich slave ship for three days instead of letting her go.","Link the second episode to the third in the same way: the prize made the company far stronger than a sloop had, but a heavy ship crowded with men and plunder had to be sailed somewhere and kept supplied, and the northward voyage carried it on to a shoal coast in the storm season.","Close with a sentence that says what the sequence amounted to rather than repeating it: one night destroyed a company no warship had caught, and the handful who reached the beach were hanged under a law that let a colony try pirates at home, so the strongest crew in American waters ended as a court record."],"cues":["How many episodes can you actually develop in about thirteen minutes, and what must each one contain?","How many separate aspects of content do your chosen episodes cover, and why does that matter to your mark?","What does a crew tell you about itself when it votes its captain out?","What new problems does a big, rich ship bring that a small one does not?","What should the last sentence do that the earlier ones have not?"]},{"title":"Question 3: explaining the importance of the West Indies trade without retelling it","setup":"Booklet P, Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. The bullet here is: 'The importance of the West Indies trade for the economy of New England.' (8 marks). No stimulus points are printed. What you know: New England, that is Massachusetts, Connecticut, Rhode Island and New Hampshire, had no staple crop that Britain wanted in bulk, and lived by fishing, timber, shipbuilding and small family farms; the British and foreign sugar islands had turned almost every acre over to cane, so they had to buy their food, their barrel staves and their building timber, and they bought salt fish, and the poorest grades of it to feed the enslaved, along with staves and hoops, boards, horses and flour; the return cargoes were molasses and sugar, which New England distilled into rum, and distilling had become a leading manufacture at Boston and Newport, with scores of distilleries in Massachusetts by the middle of the century; Rhode Island merchants carried rum to the West African coast and traded it for captives; Parliament put sixpence a gallon on foreign molasses in the Molasses Act of 1733 and the duty was evaded rather than paid; Massachusetts was later found to be shipping out more rum than the molasses it had legally imported could have made.","steps":["Fix the two points the bullet runs between, because importance is measured across a gap: a group of colonies with nothing Britain wanted to buy, and a maritime economy whose ships, wharves and distilleries were its wealth.","Take the first strand forward from the islands to the New England shore: because the sugar planters grew cane instead of food and cut cane instead of timber, they had to buy exactly what New England already produced, so fishing, lumbering and farming gained the steady customer that the British market had never given them.","Take a second strand forward that the trade did not obviously cause, to show wider importance: the return cargo was a raw material rather than a luxury, so the trade did not merely sell New England goods but gave it a manufacture, and rum made from island molasses became the one high-value export New England could offer on the African coast as well as at home.","Weigh what would have been different without it, since that is what importance means: the fisheries and the shipyards existed before the sugar boom and would have gone on, so the trade did not create the New England economy, but it decided its shape, because a region that could not settle its account with Britain out of what it sold to Britain settled it out of what it earned in the islands.","Finish with a judgement in the language of the question, saying that the trade mattered less as one branch of commerce among others than as the hinge of the whole system, since it turned a thin farming and fishing coast into a carrying, distilling and slave-trading economy whose prosperity was tied to sugar it never grew."],"cues":["Between which two states of the New England economy is this importance being measured?","Why would a sugar island rather buy food and timber than grow and cut its own?","What can a colony do with a raw material that it cannot do with a finished one?","What would have happened to these colonies if the islands had fed themselves?","What single sentence would make your judgement clear to the examiner?"]},{"title":"Question 1: two consequences on two separate developments, slavery and the slave trade","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1: '1(a) Explain one consequence of South Carolina's slave code of 1712. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of Britain winning the asiento in 1713. [4 marks]'. The two parts are on separate developments and are marked separately out of 4, so neither answer may borrow from the other. The material you have: the code of 1712, 'An Act for the better ordering and governing of Negroes and Slaves', gathered and hardened earlier colonial law on the model of the Barbados code; it forbade an enslaved person to leave the owner's land without a written ticket, authorised whipping and worse for those found without one, ordered the searching of slave quarters about every fortnight for weapons and stolen goods, and punished enslaved country people who came into Charles Town to gamble or to meet; South Carolina had already provided for patrols in an act of 1704, which required parties of armed white men drawn from the militia's beat companies to ride their neighbourhoods, usually at night, and stop anyone travelling without a note from an owner; the asiento won at the Treaty of Utrecht gave the South Sea Company the right to carry 4,800 enslaved Africans a year to Spanish America for thirty years and to send one trading ship annually; the Royal African Company's monopoly had already been broken in 1698.","steps":["Fix what each part is actually about, because one is a change in the law of a colony and the other a change in British commerce: a code that governed movement, and a contract that opened a trade.","Build 1(a) around what the code did to white society rather than to the statute book: because a written ticket is worth nothing unless somebody stops people to ask for it, the code made the patrol of 1704 into the ordinary night work of ordinary white men, most of whom owned nobody.","Build 1(b) around the commercial result: the contract gave British merchants a licensed foothold in Spanish American ports, and because a legal trade is easy to hide an illegal one behind, the annual ship and the slaving voyages carried far more British goods into Spanish colonies than the treaty allowed.","Add the precise support each part needs: for 1(a) the written ticket and the searching of quarters for weapons, for 1(b) the thirty-year term and the ending of the Royal African Company's monopoly, which had already opened slaving to any British merchant.","Read both parts back and cut every sentence that explains what the code or the asiento was, keeping only what followed from it."],"cues":["Which part asks about a colony's law and which about a trade?","A pass is only a piece of paper: who has to act before it means anything?","Why is a licensed trade useful to a merchant who intends to smuggle?","Which precise details prove each of your claims?","How would you spot a sentence that defines rather than explains?"]},{"title":"Question 2: a narrative account of the Virginia tobacco inspection that links its episodes","setup":"Booklet P, Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the reform of the Virginia tobacco trade in the years 1727 to 1730.' (8 marks). The booklet prints: 'You may use the following in your answer: the appointment of William Gooch as lieutenant governor (1727); the Tobacco Inspection Act (1730). You must also use information of your own.' An answer that does not address three or more aspects of content cannot reach Level 3. The material available: tobacco was the largest export of the mainland colonies and had to be landed in Britain, where buyers complained that hogsheads were packed out with stalks and trash, so Virginia leaf sold for less than it should; Virginia had argued over inspection for the better part of a decade without carrying it, because planters disliked the cost of carting and the risk of having their crop condemned; Gooch was appointed lieutenant governor in January 1727, in succession to Hugh Drysdale, and spent his first years cultivating the burgesses rather than fighting them; the assembly passed his Act for amending the staple of tobacco in May 1730; it named about forty public warehouses, required every hogshead meant for export to be brought to one of them, put two sworn inspectors at each with a third to be called if they disagreed, barred burgesses and sheriffs from the posts, ordered trash and unsound leaf burned in the warehouse kiln, and had the inspectors issue notes for the tobacco they held; those notes passed from hand to hand as money in Virginia for a generation; smaller planters resented the carting and the burning, two inspection houses were burned in the first years of the system, one in Lancaster county and one in Northumberland, and at the election of 1735 a great many sitting burgesses who had voted for the Act lost their seats.","steps":["Set the starting conditions before the first episode, so the account has somewhere to travel from: a colony whose one great export was sold in a market it did not control, and whose reputation for bad leaf was costing every planter money.","Use the first bullet as a cause and not a label: a new lieutenant governor who courted the assembly instead of lecturing it could get a measure through that Virginia had refused for years.","Bring in your own material to explain why the Act took the shape it did, which is also what carries the answer past three aspects of content: inspection had to be public, the inspectors had to be men with nothing to gain, and the burning of bad leaf had to be visible if British buyers were to believe the brand.","Narrate the consequences as a chain rather than a list: an inspected hogshead could be certified, a certificate could be issued as a note, and a note that everyone trusted began to be spent, so a quality control became the colony's paper money.","Identify the cost and close on it: the men who gained least were the small planters who carted a crop across the county to watch part of it burn, which is why the quarrel followed Gooch into the next assembly election and is the real measure of what the reform had done."],"cues":["Where should an account of 1730 begin, and why not with the Act itself?","Why can a governor on good terms with an assembly pass what a quarrelsome one cannot?","Why were the rules about who could be an inspector as important as the inspection?","How does a receipt for goods in a warehouse turn into money?","Who paid for this reform, and how can you tell?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Give the date of the Stono Rebellion, the name of its leader and the direction the rebels marched.","a":"9 September 1739; the leader was called Jemmy, described as Angolan; they marched south towards Spanish Florida, where Spain had promised freedom to runaways."},{"q":"What three duties did the Molasses Act of 1733 impose, and what did colonial merchants do about them?","a":"Sixpence a gallon on foreign molasses, ninepence a gallon on foreign rum and five shillings a hundredweight on foreign sugar. Merchants smuggled from the French and Dutch islands, falsified papers and bribed customs officers, so the Act raised almost nothing."},{"q":"Name the three British measures that broke piracy in American waters between 1717 and 1719.","a":"King George I's Proclamation of 5 September 1717 offering pardons; the Piracy Act of 1717, which tightened the law on piracy, laid down how pirates were to be tried and denied them benefit of clergy; and armed force, above all Spotswood's expedition under Lieutenant Robert Maynard that killed Blackbeard at Ocracoke on 22 November 1718."},{"q":"Why did the growth of rice growing in South Carolina produce an enslaved majority?","a":"Rice was grown in malarial tidal fields where deaths were high, so the workforce could not replace itself and was replaced by continuous imports. By 1740 people of African descent were about two thirds of the colony's population, roughly 39,000 of about 56,000."}]},"room":{"facts":["Booklet P is worth 32 marks and has three questions: Question 1, printed in two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each 'Explain one consequence of...' for 4 marks and each on a separate event or development; Question 2, 'Write a narrative account analysing...', 8 marks; and Question 3, 'Explain two of the following: the importance of ... for ...', two chosen parts at 8 marks each, 16 in all.","Only Question 2 prints stimulus points, as two bullets followed by 'You must also use information of your own.' The cap printed in the mark scheme is: 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' There is no cap at Level 2. Questions 1 and 3 print no bullets and carry no cap.","Each part of Question 1 is marked on its own two-level grid out of 4: Level 2 needs features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific supporting information added. A description of the event, however accurate, stays in Level 1.","Question 3 is marked twice on a three-level grid out of 8, and Level 3 needs analysis of importance in a coherent, logically structured line of reasoning: what difference the event or person made to later situations and developments, not what happened.","Timing: Paper 2 lasts 1 hour 50 minutes for 64 marks, about 55 minutes a booklet, so booklet P's 32 marks get roughly a minute a mark with planning on top, about 12 minutes on Question 1's two parts, 13 on Question 2 and 14 on each part of Question 3, leaving a couple of minutes to check. There are no sources on this paper and no SPaG marks."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of King George I's Proclamation for Suppressing of Pirates (September 1717). [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2, marked on the 4-mark consequence grid. Level 2 (3-4) requires features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific support. Credit the surrender of large numbers of pirates, including several hundred at Nassau to Woodes Rogers in 1718; the splitting of the pirate community into pardoned men and outlaws; or the rewards that made hunting the remainder profitable.","sol":"The Proclamation split the pirates apart. Because a pardon was available to anyone who surrendered to a governor before 5 September 1718, while a reward was offered for taking anyone who did not, a pirate's safest course was now to come in early, and several hundred at Nassau did so when Woodes Rogers arrived as governor of the Bahamas in July 1718. That mattered because the pirates' strength had lain in a shared base and in crews who could be recruited quickly; once the majority had accepted the King's terms, those who stayed out, like Teach, were isolated and could be hunted down one by one.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["A consequence is something that was true afterwards because of the Proclamation, so ask what it changed about a pirate's own choices.","The offer had two halves, a pardon and a reward; think about what the two together did to a group that depended on numbers and on a shared base.","Write it in three moves: name the consequence, give the specific detail that proves it, then explain why it weakened piracy."],"codes":["P2-1 — 1 Developments in colonial society: The suppression of piracy in American waters"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Molasses Act (1733). [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the growth of large-scale smuggling of French and Dutch molasses into New England; the failure of the Act to raise meaningful revenue; the bribery and corruption of customs officers; or the habit of evading British trade law that the Act established.","sol":"One consequence was that a large share of New England's molasses trade became illegal and continued anyway. Sixpence a gallon on foreign molasses was more than the distillers' margin, so rather than close, merchants went on buying in the cheaper French and Dutch islands, landed cargoes at night, falsified papers and paid the collectors a fraction of the duty to look away. Because customs officers were few, the coastline was long and colonial juries would not convict their neighbours, evasion was both cheap and safe, so the Act raised almost nothing and taught colonial merchants that a British trade law could simply be ignored.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what a duty does when it is set higher than the trade it taxes can bear.","Weigh the rate against what the trade could bear, then ask a separate question about enforcement: how many customs officers were there, and what would a colonial jury do?","Name the consequence, support it with the rate and with why enforcement failed, then explain what habit the Act created."],"codes":["P2-1 — 3 Problems within the colonies: Smuggling and customs revenue","P2-1 — 1 Developments in colonial society: Economic developments"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the founding of Georgia in 1732. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the creation of a buffer between South Carolina and Spanish Florida; the trustees' ban on slavery and its effect on the colony's early settlement and economy; or the sharpening of conflict with Spain over the debatable land south of the Savannah river.","sol":"One consequence was that South Carolina gained a buffer between its plantations and Spanish Florida. The charter was sealed in 1732 and the first settlers, led by James Oglethorpe, founded Savannah in February 1733 on land Spain also claimed, so the new colony stood directly across the road that runaways had been taking towards St Augustine and across the route any Spanish force would use. That mattered because South Carolina now had an enslaved majority and lived in fear of both flight and invasion; but it also made the border more dangerous, since Georgia's settlement gave Spain a fresh grievance in a quarrel that became open war in 1739.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Look at a map in your head: think about which colony lay immediately north of the new one and which empire lay immediately south.","South Carolina's two great fears in the 1730s were runaways going south and a Spanish attack coming north; a new colony sat between them and both.","State the consequence, support it with the founding of Savannah and the Spanish claim, then explain why it cut both ways."],"codes":["P2-1 — 1 Developments in colonial society: The impact of expansion and immigration on society"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of Edward Teach's blockade of Charles Town in May 1718. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the work of Governor Spotswood against piracy in 1718. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4 on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the closing of the colony's main port and the halting of the rice and deerskin trades, the humiliation of a colonial government that had to let a pirate dictate terms, or the demand for naval protection that followed. In 1(b) credit the death of Teach at Ocracoke on 22 November 1718 and the trials at Williamsburg, the demonstration that the pardon's deadline would be enforced, or the model this gave other governors for using Royal Navy crews in small hired vessels.","sol":"1(a) The blockade showed the colonial governments how little they could protect their own trade. For about a week Teach held the mouth of Charles Town harbour, stopping outgoing ships and taking hostages, and South Carolina's council could do nothing but hand over the chest of medicines he demanded. Because Charles Town was the outlet for the colony's rice and deerskins, every day closed was money lost, and the affair convinced planters and merchants that petitions to London for naval protection were not enough and that governors would have to act themselves. 1(b) Spotswood's expedition proved that the King's pardon had a deadline with teeth behind it. He hired two sloops and manned them with Royal Navy sailors under Lieutenant Robert Maynard, who found Teach at Ocracoke Inlet on 22 November 1718 and killed him, and the surviving crew were tried at Williamsburg in 1719 and most were hanged. Because the most famous pirate in American waters had taken a pardon and gone back to sea, his death told the rest that returning to piracy meant the gallows, and within a few years raiding on the American coast had collapsed.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate parts on two separate things: what a pirate did to a port in 1(a), and what a governor did to pirates in 1(b), so neither answer can help the other.","For 1(a) ask what a week of closed harbour costs a colony that exports one crop; for 1(b) ask what other pirates learned from watching what happened to the most famous of them.","Each part needs the same three moves: name the consequence, support it with a named person, place or date, then explain the link back to the event."],"codes":["P2-1 — 1 Developments in colonial society: Economic developments","P2-1 — 1 Developments in colonial society: The suppression of piracy in American waters"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Stono Rebellion (1739). [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of Spain's decision to protect runaways in Florida. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the Negro Act of 10 May 1740 and its restrictions on assembly, movement, literacy, drumming and earning, the requirement of one white person for every ten enslaved, or the executions and the hardening of white opinion. In 1(b) credit the flight of enslaved people from South Carolina and Georgia to St Augustine, the founding of Fort Mose in 1738, the extra motive it gave the Stono rebels, or the way it turned slavery into a question of imperial security.","sol":"1(a) The most important consequence was the Negro Act that South Carolina's assembly passed on 10 May 1740. Its clauses answer the rising point by point: enslaved people were forbidden to gather, to travel without a written pass, to earn money, to grow food for sale, to beat drums and to be taught to write, and every plantation had to keep one white person for every ten enslaved. Because the rebels had assembled on a Sunday, armed themselves and signalled with drums, the assembly set out to make each of those steps impossible, so the daily life of thousands who had never been near the Stono road became far harsher. 1(b) Spain's policy turned South Carolina's slavery into a frontier problem. The edict of 1693, confirmed by decree in 1733, promised freedom to runaways who converted to Catholicism and served Spain, and in 1738 the governor of Florida settled them in their own town at Gracia Real de Santa Teresa de Mose, north of St Augustine. Because escape now had a destination and a guarantee rather than merely a direction, flight from the Carolina lowcountry rose, and planters came to see the Spanish border as a standing danger, which is why so many of them wanted Georgia settled and welcomed war with Spain in 1739.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["1(a) looks forward from a rising to a law; 1(b) looks at what a foreign government's promise did inside a British colony.","For 1(a) read the clauses of the law against the actions of the rebels and ask what each clause is answering; for 1(b) ask what changes when running away stops being a gamble and becomes a journey to a known place.","Both parts need one consequence only, a named date or place as support, and a sentence explaining the link."],"codes":["P2-1 — 3 Problems within the colonies: Slave revolts in the Carolinas","P2-1 — 2 Slavery in North America: The impact of slavery on colonial society"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the New York Conspiracy (1741). [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the growth of the enslaved population of South Carolina by 1740. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the executions and banishments, the loss of roughly one in twenty of the city's enslaved workforce, some 100 people out of about 2,000, the tightening of controls on taverns and on gatherings, or the demonstration that fear of slave revolt was not confined to the southern colonies. In 1(b) credit the enslaved majority of roughly two to one and the fear it produced, the slave patrols and codes, the wealth and political dominance of the rice planters, or the pressure for a buffer colony to the south.","sol":"1(a) The trials cost New York a large part of its enslaved workforce and hardened its laws. Thirteen black men were burned at the stake, seventeen black people and four white people were hanged, and about seventy black people were transported out of the colony, so a city of roughly 10,000 people lost a sizeable share of the 2,000 enslaved who worked its docks and workshops. Because the alleged plot had been hatched, on the court's account, in a waterfront tavern where enslaved men drank with poor whites, the city tightened its watch on taverns and on gatherings, and the episode showed that fear of revolt was as sharp in a northern port as in the rice country. 1(b) Continuous importing to work the rice fields left white South Carolinians a minority in their own colony, about 17,000 people among roughly 39,000 of African descent by 1740. Because a minority cannot govern by consent, it governed by law and force: passes for travel, patrols riding the roads at night, and a slave code that authorised whipping and mutilation. That fear also shaped foreign policy, since a colony outnumbered at home could not tolerate a neighbouring empire offering its workers freedom.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["One part is about what a panic did to a city, the other about what a labour system did to a colony's population.","For 1(a) count what the executions and banishments took out of a workforce of about two thousand; for 1(b) ask how a minority keeps control and what that costs.","Support each part with figures you can vouch for, then explain, rather than assert, why the consequence followed."],"codes":["P2-1 — 3 Problems within the colonies: The New York Conspiracy (1741)","P2-1 — 2 Slavery in North America: The impact of slavery on colonial society"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the spread of rice growing in South Carolina. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of tobacco growing in Virginia and Maryland. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the continuous importing of enslaved Africans and the enslaved majority of about two thirds by 1740, the preference for captives from West Africa's rice regions, the task system, or the wealth of the lowcountry planters. In 1(b) credit the exhaustion of soil and the constant demand for fresh land pushing settlement inland and on to Native territory, the gang system, the dominance of the tidewater gentry, or the Tobacco Inspection Act of 1730.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that South Carolina came to hold an enslaved majority. Rice was grown in embanked tidal fields cut out of malarial swamp, where deaths were so high that the workforce could not replace itself by births, so planters imported continuously through Charles Town and sought captives from the rice-growing regions of West Africa for their skill. By 1740 people of African descent were about two thirds of the colony, roughly 39,000 of about 56,000, which is the fact behind every slave code and patrol law the colony passed. 1(b) Tobacco pushed Virginia and Maryland inland. The plant exhausted the soil within a few seasons, so a planter who wished to keep his output had to take up new ground, and the gentry accumulated land far up the rivers and beyond. Because that demand never stopped, settlement pressed steadily west on to Native territory and into the backcountry that Ulster and German immigrants were also entering, which is why quarrels over land and over frontier defence became a standing feature of Virginia politics.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two crops, two different pressures: one on people, one on land.","For 1(a) ask why a workforce that keeps dying has to keep being replaced, and what that does to the population balance; for 1(b) ask what a planter does when his fields stop yielding.","Each part wants one consequence with a precise figure or place behind it and a sentence of explanation, not a description of how the crop was grown."],"codes":["P2-1 — 2 Slavery in North America: The impact of slavery on the development of plantations","P2-1 — 1 Developments in colonial society: Economic developments"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of immigration from Ulster and the German lands after 1717. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of British attempts to control the fur trade. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the rapid growth and westward spread of population into the backcountry, conflict with Native peoples over land, tension between backcountry settlers and the coastal gentry who controlled the assemblies, or the religious variety of the middle colonies. In 1(b) credit the evasion of New York's law of 1720 by Albany merchants, the building of the trading post at Oswego in 1727 and the competition with the French, or the dependence of the trade on Native goodwill.","sol":"1(a) Immigration filled the backcountry and set it against the coast. Ulster Protestants began leaving in numbers from 1717 and German families from the Rhineland in the same years, and since the tidewater land was long since taken, they moved inland and south-west through the Shenandoah valley. Because those settlers occupied land Native peoples used and paid taxes to assemblies dominated by coastal planters who would not pay for frontier defence, the colonies acquired an interior with different interests, different churches and a lasting grievance against the men who governed them. 1(b) Britain's attempts to control the trade mostly proved how little control it had. New York forbade the sale of English trade goods to the French at Montreal in 1720, but Albany merchants went on selling because the profit was in the middle of the route, so in 1727 Governor William Burnet built a fortified trading post at Oswego on Lake Ontario to intercept the furs before they reached the French. The trade could be redirected by a fort but not commanded by a statute, because the people who carried the furs were Haudenosaunee who dealt with whichever empire offered the better goods.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["One part is about people arriving, the other about goods moving; in both, ask who lost by the change.","For 1(a) think about where newcomers could actually find land, and who already lived there or already governed there; for 1(b) ask why a law forbidding a sale is so hard to enforce a thousand miles from a port.","Give each part one consequence, a date or a name as support, and the causal sentence that ties it back."],"codes":["P2-1 — 1 Developments in colonial society: The impact of expansion and immigration on society","P2-1 — 3 Problems within the colonies: Smuggling and customs revenue"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the suppression of piracy in American waters in the years 1717 to 1719. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: King George I's Proclamation (September 1717); the death of Edward Teach (November 1718). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) requires a clear sequence leading to an outcome with the linkage between events analysed. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; own material might include the Piracy Act of 1717, Woodes Rogers at Nassau in 1718, the blockade of Charles Town, the Virginia assembly's rewards, and the Williamsburg trials of 1719.","sol":"Piracy in American waters was a product of peace. The War of the Spanish Succession ended in 1713 and threw thousands of privateers and seamen out of work, and many gathered in the Bahamas, from which they preyed on the shipping that carried tobacco, rice and sugar. The government's first move was therefore an offer rather than a fleet: the Proclamation of 5 September 1717 promised a pardon to any pirate who surrendered to a governor within a year, and rewards to those who captured the rest. Because it divided a community whose strength lay in numbers, it worked quickly; when Woodes Rogers arrived as governor of the Bahamas in July 1718, several hundred men at Nassau took the pardon, and the main base was gone. That left the hard core, and here the legal change mattered: the Piracy Act of 1717, given the royal assent on 21 March 1718, tightened the law by laying down how pirates were to be tried and by denying them benefit of clergy, so a man who ignored the deadline and was taken could expect the gallows rather than a plea. Because the threat behind the offer was now real, Spotswood of Virginia thought it worth fitting out two sloops manned by Royal Navy sailors, and Lieutenant Robert Maynard killed Teach at Ocracoke Inlet on 22 November 1718. The trials followed at Williamsburg in 1719, before a commission of the kind the colonies had been able to hold since 1700, and most of the surviving crew were hanged. A pardon, a court and a killing together removed the pirates' escape route, their base and their most famous captain, and raiding on the American coast fell away within a few years.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first where the account begins and ends, and why the story needs a starting condition before the first bullet.","The three moves were an offer, a law and an act of force; ask in each case what the previous move had left unfinished.","Plan four dated episodes, note which are your own material beyond the bullets, and write one linking sentence for each join before you write the answer itself."],"codes":["P2-1 — 1 Developments in colonial society: The suppression of piracy in American waters","P2-1 — 1 Developments in colonial society: Economic developments"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the Stono Rebellion and its aftermath in the years 1739 to 1740. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the march from Stono Bridge (September 1739); the Negro Act (1740). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 requires a clear sequence leading to an outcome with analysed linkage. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; own material might include the enslaved majority of the lowcountry, Spain's promise of freedom and Fort Mose, the militia action at the Edisto river under Lieutenant Governor Bull, the executions, and the war with Spain declared in October 1739.","sol":"The rising grew out of a colony whose numbers frightened it. By the late 1730s people of African descent were roughly two thirds of South Carolina, most of them working the rice fields of the lowcountry, and since 1693 Spain had promised freedom to any runaway who reached Florida, a promise confirmed by decree in 1733 and made visible in 1738 when the governor settled free black families at Fort Mose outside St Augustine. On Sunday 9 September 1739 about twenty men led by Jemmy, described as Angolan, broke into Hutchenson's store at Stono Bridge, killed the storekeepers and took arms and powder, then marched south towards that promise, beating drums and calling for liberty; their numbers grew to about sixty and they killed between twenty and twenty-five colonists. Because the march went by daylight along a public road, Lieutenant Governor William Bull saw it, rode clear and raised the militia, which caught the rebels at the Edisto river that same afternoon; survivors were hunted and executed over the following weeks. The failure did not reassure the colony, because war with Spain was declared the next month and the rebels' route had proved that the enemy lay only a few days' march away. That is why the assembly's answer, the Negro Act of 10 May 1740, attacked every step the rebels had taken: assembly, travel without a pass, drums, earning money, and even being taught to write. A rising crushed within a day thus reshaped the law of the colony for generations.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The dates in the question run past the rising itself, so the account must reach the law and explain why it took the form it did.","Begin with the conditions that made the march thinkable rather than with the march, and keep asking what each stage made possible for the next.","Sketch four episodes with dates, mark the ones that are your own material beyond the two bullets, and write the joining sentences first."],"codes":["P2-1 — 3 Problems within the colonies: Slave revolts in the Carolinas","P2-1 — 2 Slavery in North America: The impact of slavery on colonial society"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the New York Conspiracy of 1741. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the fire at Fort George (March 1741); the evidence of Mary Burton. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 requires a clear sequence leading to an outcome with analysed linkage. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; own material might include the size of the city's enslaved population, the hard winter and the war with Spain, Hughson's tavern and the stolen goods case, the confessions bought with promises of life, the executions and banishments, and the collapse of the trials.","sol":"New York in 1741 was a crowded port of about 10,000 people, roughly 2,000 of them enslaved and living inside their owners' houses, so the city could not keep them apart from one another or from poor white labourers. The winter of 1740 to 1741 was severe and prices high, and Britain had been at war with Spain since October 1739, so rumours of Spanish and Catholic plotting were everywhere. Into that came fire: the blaze at Fort George on 18 March 1741 destroyed the governor's house and chapel, and about a dozen more fires followed within three weeks. Because a fire at the seat of government looked like an attack on government, and because so many followed so quickly, the city stopped reading them as accidents and began looking for a plot. It found one in the place suspicion was already pointing. Mary Burton, a sixteen-year-old indentured servant held over stolen goods at John Hughson's waterfront tavern, testified that Hughson and the enslaved men who drank there meant to burn the city and make him king. Because prisoners were promised their lives for confessing and naming others, every confession produced more names, so about 152 black and 20 white people were arrested; thirteen black men were burned at the stake, seventeen black people and four white people were hanged, including Hughson and the schoolmaster John Ury, and about seventy black people were transported. The sequence only broke when Burton began accusing respectable citizens, which showed that the evidence had all along been following the fear rather than producing it.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Start before the first fire: an account that opens in March cannot explain why the city reacted as it did.","Ask at each stage why the next stage followed, especially why confessions multiplied instead of settling the matter.","Choose four episodes, note which are your own material beyond the two bullets, and decide in advance what your closing sentence will say the sequence amounted to."],"codes":["P2-1 — 3 Problems within the colonies: The New York Conspiracy (1741)","P2-1 — 2 Slavery in North America: The impact of slavery on colonial society"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: 'The importance of the transatlantic slave trade for the economy of the southern colonies.' (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the three-level importance grid. Level 3 needs analysis of importance in a coherent line of reasoning. Credit the supply of labour that made tobacco and rice plantations possible, the skills of captives from West Africa's rice regions, the enslaved majority of South Carolina by 1740, the exports that paid for imported British manufactures, and the wealth and political power of the planter elite. No stimulus points and no cap.","sol":"The trade mattered because the southern colonies' exports could not have been produced without it. Tobacco in Virginia and Maryland was gang labour on a scale no free workforce would accept, and rice in the South Carolina lowcountry demanded the digging and banking of malarial tidal fields; both were possible only because ships landed captive Africans continuously at Charles Town and in the Chesapeake, a supply made easier after the Royal African Company's monopoly ended in 1698 and after Britain won the asiento in 1713 and built up its slaving ports. The importance ran further than numbers. Carolina planters deliberately sought people from the rice-growing regions of West Africa because they understood a crop the English did not, so the trade supplied expertise as well as labour, and rice became the colony's staple. Those exports in turn paid for the British manufactures the colonies bought, which is exactly what the Navigation Acts were designed to secure, so the trade bound the southern economy tightly into the imperial system. Finally, it built a class. Because enslaved people were property, the men who owned most of them held most of the colony's wealth and sat in its assemblies, so the trade decided who governed as well as who worked. Its importance is therefore structural rather than incidental: remove it and the southern economy of 1741 is not a smaller version of itself but a different economy altogether, based on small farms and modest exports, with no enslaved majority and no planter elite.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Importance means the difference something made, so keep asking what the southern colonies would have looked like without it.","Work through three different kinds of difference: to what could be grown, to who grew rich, and to how the colonies fitted into the imperial trading system.","Plan three developed points, each with a precise date, place or figure, and decide before writing what your final judgement sentence will claim."],"codes":["P2-1 — 2 Slavery in North America: The transatlantic slave trade and 'Atlantic trade triangle'","P2-1 — 2 Slavery in North America: The impact of slavery on the development of plantations"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: 'The importance of the Piracy Act (1717) for the trial and punishment of pirates in the colonies.' (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid, where Level 3 (6-8) requires analysis of importance in a coherent line of reasoning. Credit the denial of benefit of clergy to those who ought to be adjudged pirates, the manner of trial the Act laid down, its confirmation that those who helped pirates could be tried as pirates too, the way this made the Proclamation's threat credible, the Williamsburg trials of 1719 as an example, and the limits of its importance, since trial by royal commission in the colonies had been lawful since the Piracy Act 1698 came into force in 1700. No stimulus points and no cap.","sol":"The Act's importance lay in what a conviction came to mean. Trying pirates in the colonies was not new: the Piracy Act 1698 had allowed piracy to be heard and determined by royal commission in the plantations from 1700, and Bellamy's men were tried under such a commission at Boston in October 1717, five months before the 1717 statute received the royal assent. What those courts still lacked was certainty of punishment. The Piracy Act of 1717, which received the royal assent on 21 March 1718, tightened the law: it laid down the manner in which men who ought to be adjudged pirates were to be tried, it debarred them from benefit of clergy, and it confirmed that those who helped pirates could be tried as pirates too, so receivers and suppliers ashore could be hanged in their own right. That mattered most because of what the King had already offered. A pardon is only persuasive if refusing it is dangerous, so the Proclamation of 5 September 1717, with its deadline of 5 September 1718, needed a court at the other end from which a convicted pirate could not wriggle out; after the Act, he could not. When Teach was killed at Ocracoke Inlet on 22 November 1718 and his surviving crew were tried at Williamsburg in 1719, most were hanged. Its importance should not be overstated. Piracy in American waters was broken by three things working together, and the pardon probably removed more pirates than the courts did, since several hundred came in at New Providence when Woodes Rogers arrived in July 1718. The Act changed neither where a pirate was tried nor who hunted him. What it changed was how a trial ended, and that is what turned the King's offer of mercy into a threat worth taking seriously.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The bullet is about trial and punishment, so ask what happened to a captured pirate before the Act and what happened afterwards.","Set the Act beside the King's pardon of September 1717 and ask what each of the two needed from the other.","Plan three points and weigh the Act against the pardon and armed force, so that your judgement about its importance is argued rather than asserted."],"codes":["P2-1 — 1 Developments in colonial society: The suppression of piracy in American waters"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: 'The importance of smuggling for British attempts to raise customs revenue in the colonies.' (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Credit the failure of the Molasses Act of 1733 to raise revenue, the bribery of collectors and the reluctance of colonial juries, the thinness of the customs service and the role of the vice-admiralty courts set up in 1696, Walpole's decision not to press enforcement, and the habit of evasion that later governments would have to break. No stimulus points and no cap.","sol":"Smuggling mattered because it made British customs policy in the colonies largely theoretical. The Molasses Act of 1733 put sixpence a gallon on foreign molasses, ninepence on foreign rum and five shillings a hundredweight on foreign sugar, rates designed to force New England to buy from British planters in the West Indies. Since paying them would have closed the distilleries, merchants smuggled instead, buying in the French and Dutch islands, landing at night, falsifying papers and paying collectors a fraction of the duty, so the Act raised almost nothing and the British sugar islands got little of the protection it promised. The importance runs further than one statute. Because evasion was so easy, the machinery built to prevent it was exposed as inadequate: collectors were few and poorly paid, the coastline was vast, and although the Navigation Act of 1696 had set up vice-admiralty courts so that customs cases could be tried without a local jury, cases still depended on officers willing to bring them and on a government willing to back them. Walpole, in office from 1721 to 1742, judged that a flourishing colonial trade was worth more than strictly collected duties, so enforcement was not pressed, a choice later nicknamed salutary neglect. The deepest consequence was a habit. Colonial merchants grew used to treating British trade law as an inconvenience to be negotiated rather than a rule to be obeyed, and that expectation, formed in the 1730s, is the reason later attempts to tax the colonies met not merely objection but organised resistance.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Importance here is about what smuggling did to British policy, not about how smugglers worked.","Take three different kinds of effect: on the money actually collected, on the machinery of enforcement, and on what colonists came to expect of British law.","Plan three developed points with precise rates and dates, and decide what your closing judgement will say the practice amounted to."],"codes":["P2-1 — 3 Problems within the colonies: Smuggling and customs revenue","P2-1 — 1 Developments in colonial society: Economic developments"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date did the Stono Rebellion begin?","a":"9 September 1739."},{"q":"What duty per gallon did the Molasses Act of 1733 place on foreign molasses entering the colonies?","a":"Sixpence a gallon."},{"q":"Who commanded the sloops sent by Governor Spotswood that killed Edward Teach at Ocracoke Inlet on 22 November 1718?","a":"Lieutenant Robert Maynard of the Royal Navy."},{"q":"What was the name of the free black settlement founded near St Augustine in Spanish Florida in 1738?","a":"Gracia Real de Santa Teresa de Mose, usually called Fort Mose."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The thirteen colonies and their three regions","back":"New England: Massachusetts, Connecticut, Rhode Island, New Hampshire. Middle: New York, New Jersey, Pennsylvania, Delaware. Southern: Maryland, Virginia, North and South Carolina, and Georgia from 1732.","code":"P2-1 — 1 Developments in colonial society: The impact of expansion and immigration on society","open":false},{"front":"Population growth and immigration, 1710 to 1740","back":"About 330,000 colonists in 1710, about 900,000 by 1740. Ulster Protestants left in waves from 1717; German families from the Rhineland went to Pennsylvania; newcomers pushed inland through the Shenandoah valley.","code":"P2-1 — 1 Developments in colonial society: The impact of expansion and immigration on society","open":false},{"front":"Mercantilism and the Navigation Acts","back":"Colonies supplied raw materials and bought British manufactures. Trade had to travel in British or colonial ships; enumerated goods such as tobacco, sugar, rice and furs had to be landed in Britain first.","code":"P2-1 — 1 Developments in colonial society: Economic developments","open":false},{"front":"Tobacco in Virginia and Maryland","back":"The mainland's largest export, grown by gang labour on tidal rivers and shipped in hogsheads. It exhausted soil, driving settlement inland. Virginia's Tobacco Inspection Act of 1730 burned substandard leaf to protect the price.","code":"P2-1 — 1 Developments in colonial society: Economic developments","open":false},{"front":"Trade with the West Indies","back":"New England and the middle colonies sold salt fish, timber, barrel staves, horses and flour to the sugar islands, and brought back molasses and sugar to distil into rum.","code":"P2-1 — 1 Developments in colonial society: Economic developments","open":false},{"front":"'Black Sam' Bellamy","back":"Captured the slave ship Whydah Gally in the Bahamas in February 1717 and took dozens of vessels. The Whydah was wrecked off Cape Cod on 26 April 1717 and Bellamy drowned; survivors were tried at Boston that October.","code":"P2-1 — 1 Developments in colonial society: Economic developments","open":false},{"front":"Edward Teach, 'Blackbeard'","back":"Commanded the Queen Anne's Revenge; blockaded Charles Town harbour in May 1718, taking hostages for a chest of medicines; ran his flagship aground in June 1718; killed at Ocracoke Inlet on 22 November 1718.","code":"P2-1 — 1 Developments in colonial society: Economic developments","open":false},{"front":"King George I's Proclamation for Suppressing of Pirates","back":"Issued 5 September 1717. Pardon for pirates who surrendered to a governor by 5 September 1718, later extended to July 1719, plus rewards for capturing those who refused. Several hundred surrendered at Nassau in 1718.","code":"P2-1 — 1 Developments in colonial society: The suppression of piracy in American waters","open":false},{"front":"The Piracy Act (1717) and Governor Spotswood","back":"The Act (assent March 1718) tightened piracy law, defined the trial process and denied benefit of clergy. Spotswood, Lieutenant Governor of Virginia 1710 to 1722, hired two sloops under Robert Maynard; Teach's crew were tried at Williamsburg in 1719.","code":"P2-1 — 1 Developments in colonial society: The suppression of piracy in American waters","open":false},{"front":"The Atlantic trade triangle and the asiento","back":"Britain to Africa with guns, iron, cloth and rum; the Middle Passage to America, where about one in seven died; sugar, tobacco and rice home. The asiento of 1713 gave Britain the Spanish American slave contract.","code":"P2-1 — 2 Slavery in North America: The transatlantic slave trade and 'Atlantic trade triangle'","open":false},{"front":"Rice in the South Carolina lowcountry","back":"Grown from the 1690s in swamps and embanked tidal fields, worked by the task system. Planters sought captives from West Africa's rice regions. Malaria kept deaths high, so the workforce was replaced by continuous importing.","code":"P2-1 — 2 Slavery in North America: The impact of slavery on the development of plantations","open":false},{"front":"Slave codes and the treatment of fugitives","back":"Virginia's code of 1705 made enslaved people inheritable property; South Carolina's of 1712 required passes and allowed whipping and mutilation. Runaways were advertised, hunted by patrols, and from 1693 offered freedom in Spanish Florida.","code":"P2-1 — 2 Slavery in North America: The impact of slavery on colonial society","open":false},{"front":"The Stono Rebellion and the Negro Act","back":"9 September 1739: about twenty men under Jemmy took arms at Hutchenson's store and marched towards Florida, killing twenty to twenty-five colonists before the militia caught them at the Edisto. The Negro Act followed on 10 May 1740.","code":"P2-1 — 3 Problems within the colonies: Slave revolts in the Carolinas","open":false},{"front":"The New York Conspiracy (1741)","back":"Fires from 18 March 1741, beginning at Fort George. On Mary Burton's evidence about 152 black and 20 white people were arrested; thirteen were burned at the stake, twenty-one hanged and about seventy transported.","code":"P2-1 — 3 Problems within the colonies: The New York Conspiracy (1741)","open":false},{"front":"The Molasses Act (1733), smuggling and the fur trade","back":"Sixpence a gallon on foreign molasses, ninepence on rum, five shillings a hundredweight on sugar; widely evaded. New York banned selling goods to the French at Montreal in 1720; Oswego was fortified in 1727.","code":"P2-1 — 3 Problems within the colonies: Smuggling and customs revenue","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Paper 2 booklet P Question 2, narrative account, 8 marks)","items":["Question: 'Write a narrative account analysing the settlement of the Shenandoah valley in the years 1730 to 1738.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the Virginia Council's land grants in the valley (1730); the creation of Frederick and Augusta counties (1738). You must also use information of your own. This is the middle stretch of the account, carrying the story from the grants to the counties.","Because the Council's grants of 1730 gave land west of the Blue Ridge on condition that families were actually planted on it, at a thousand acres for each family settled, they turned a land grant into a recruiting drive: John and Isaac Van Meter, granted their tract in June 1730, had no such families to hand and sold their rights on to Jost Hite and Robert McKay, who had taken an order of council for a hundred thousand acres of their own in October 1731 on the same terms. That is why the first settlers came from Pennsylvania rather than from tidewater Virginia, since Hite recruited among the Ulster and German families already landing at Philadelphia and brought a party of them, traditionally sixteen, down the wagon road into the lower valley in 1732, where the settlement around them came to be known as Opequon. The flow that followed created a religious problem for a colony with an established church, because these were Presbyterians and German Protestants, and Gooch answered it in 1738 by assuring the Synod of Philadelphia that their ministers might preach in Virginia if they registered under the terms of the Toleration Act, which removed the last reason for a settler to stop at the Maryland line. By November 1738, with people spread over country no county court could reach, the assembly cut Frederick and Augusta out of Orange County, although it left both without a working government until there were enough taxpayers to pay for one, so that Frederick's court did not sit until 1743 and Augusta's until 1745.","Why this is Level 3: the material is organised as a clear sequence leading to an outcome and the linkage between events is analysed rather than stated, since the settlement condition is explained as the reason the grants changed hands, the Pennsylvania recruiting as the reason the settlers were dissenters, the toleration of 1738 as the reason the flow continued, and the spread of population as the reason two counties had to be created. The knowledge is precise, naming the Van Meters, Hite and McKay, Opequon, the Synod of Philadelphia and the act of 1738, and it addresses well over three aspects of content, so the Level 3 cap does not apply."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same, 'Write a narrative account analysing the settlement of the Shenandoah valley in the years 1730 to 1738.' (8 marks), with the same two bullets.","In 1730 the Virginia Council gave out big grants of land in the Shenandoah valley. The Van Meter brothers got one of them, but they sold it to a man called Jost Hite, who was a German settler. Hite brought families over the mountains in 1732 and they built farms in the valley. Lots more people came after this, mostly Scotch-Irish and Germans who had arrived at Philadelphia and wanted cheap land. The governor was happy about this because it put settlers between the rest of Virginia and the French and the Indians. In 1738 the assembly made two new counties out of Orange County, Frederick and Augusta. This shows that the valley was properly settled by then.","Why this is only Level 2, and what it misses: the events are in the right order and the main facts are accurate, so it meets 'some organisation of material into a sequence of events leading to an outcome' and 'accurate and relevant information is added'. It misses the Level 3 requirement that 'the account of events analyses the linkage between them': 'lots more people came after this' asserts a link instead of explaining one, and nothing says why the grants had to be sold on, or why settlers from Pennsylvania rather than from tidewater Virginia were the ones who came. The detail is loose where the top level wants precision, since the settlement condition of a thousand acres a family is missing, Robert McKay and Opequon are unnamed, and the religious question that Gooch had to settle in 1738 is left out altogether."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: 'Black Sam' Bellamy and Edward Teach ('Blackbeard')","items":["Why there were so many pirates: the War of the Spanish Succession ended with the Treaty of Utrecht in 1713, leaving thousands of privateers and seamen without work; many gathered at Nassau in the Bahamas.","Samuel Bellamy, called 'Black Sam': captured the slave ship Whydah Gally in the Bahamas in February 1717 and used her as his flagship.","Bellamy's crew took dozens of vessels within about a year along the American coast and in the Caribbean.","The Whydah was wrecked in a storm off Cape Cod on 26 April 1717; Bellamy drowned with almost all his crew, and only a handful survived.","The survivors were tried at Boston in October 1717 and most were hanged.","Edward Teach, known as 'Blackbeard': captured a French slave ship and renamed her the Queen Anne's Revenge.","May 1718: Teach blockaded the harbour of Charles Town, South Carolina, stopping shipping and holding hostages until the colony handed over a chest of medicines.","June 1718: the Queen Anne's Revenge ran aground at Beaufort Inlet in North Carolina; Teach continued in a smaller sloop, the Adventure.","Teach accepted King George I's pardon from Charles Eden, governor of North Carolina, in 1718, and then returned to raiding.","22 November 1718: Teach was killed at Ocracoke Inlet by a force under Lieutenant Robert Maynard, sent by Governor Spotswood of Virginia.","Impact of piracy on the colonies: attacks on shipping raised freight and insurance costs and disrupted the tobacco, rice and deerskin trades, though some colonists had happily bought cheap goods from pirates."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: King George I's Proclamation, the Piracy Act (1717) and Governor Spotswood","items":["King George I's Proclamation for Suppressing of Pirates: issued 5 September 1717.","Its terms: a pardon for acts of piracy committed before 5 January 1718 for pirates who surrendered to a governor by 5 September 1718; a second proclamation later extended the deadline to July 1719.","It also offered rewards to anyone who captured a pirate who had not surrendered, and to crew members who helped take their own captains.","Woodes Rogers arrived as governor of the Bahamas in July 1718 carrying the pardon; several hundred pirates at Nassau accepted it, breaking up the main base.","Where the power to try pirates in the colonies came from: the Piracy Act 1698 (11 and 12 William III c. 7), in force from 1700, renewed in 1706 and made perpetual in 1719; Bellamy's crew were tried at Boston under such a commission in October 1717.","The Piracy Act 1717 (4 George I c. 11, the statute also known as the Transportation Act): given the royal assent on 21 March 1718.","What it did that mattered here: it tightened the law on piracy, laid down the manner in which pirates were to be tried, denied them benefit of clergy and confirmed that those who helped them could be tried as pirates too, so a conviction now meant the gallows.","Alexander Spotswood: Lieutenant Governor of Virginia from 1710 to 1722.","Spotswood's method: he hired two small sloops and manned them with sailors from Royal Navy ships stationed in Virginia, under Lieutenant Robert Maynard, because the navy's own warships drew too much water for the North Carolina inlets.","November 1718: the Virginia assembly voted rewards for the taking of pirates, with the largest sum offered for Teach himself.","1719: Teach's surviving crew were tried at Williamsburg and most were hanged.","Overall effect: with the pardon, the statute and the killing of Teach working together, large-scale piracy in American waters had collapsed by the mid 1720s."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the transatlantic slave trade and the 'Atlantic trade triangle'","items":["First leg: ships from London, Bristol and increasingly Liverpool carried guns, gunpowder, iron and copper goods, textiles, brandy and rum to the West African coast.","Second leg, the Middle Passage: enslaved Africans were carried across the Atlantic, chained below decks for six to ten weeks; roughly one in seven died on the crossing.","Third leg: sugar, molasses, rum, tobacco and rice were carried back to Britain, so a single voyage could be profitable three times.","1698: the Royal African Company's monopoly was ended, opening the trade to any British merchant and greatly increasing the number of voyages.","1713: at the Treaty of Utrecht Britain won the asiento, the contract to supply Spanish America with enslaved Africans; it was granted to the South Sea Company.","Asiento terms: 4,800 enslaved Africans a year for thirty years, plus one licensed trading ship a year, which gave cover for a great deal of smuggling into Spanish ports.","Where captives went: the great majority to the Caribbean and Brazil; mainland North America took only a small share of the whole trade, but arrivals rose sharply after 1713.","Main mainland entry point: Charles Town, South Carolina, with the Chesapeake ports of Virginia and Maryland next.","Effect on Britain: the trade built the fortunes of Bristol and Liverpool and supplied the sugar, tobacco and rice that the Navigation Acts required to be landed in Britain first."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Stono Rebellion (1739)","items":["Date and place: Sunday 9 September 1739, near the Stono river about twenty miles from Charles Town, South Carolina.","Leader: a man called Jemmy, described by witnesses as Angolan and probably from the Kingdom of Kongo, where many men had military training and were Catholic.","How it began: about twenty men broke into Hutchenson's store at Stono Bridge, killed the storekeepers Robert Bathurst and a Mr Gibbs, and took guns and powder.","What they did: marched south towards Spanish Florida, beating drums, carrying a banner and calling for liberty, burning houses on the way; their numbers grew to around sixty.","Deaths among colonists: between twenty and twenty-five white people were killed.","How it ended: Lieutenant Governor William Bull saw the column on the road and raised the militia, which caught the rebels at the Edisto river that afternoon; many were killed and survivors were hunted down and executed over following weeks.","Why Florida mattered: Spain had promised freedom to runaways from the British colonies since 1693, confirmed it by royal decree in 1733, and in 1738 settled free black families at Gracia Real de Santa Teresa de Mose, known as Fort Mose, north of St Augustine.","The wider setting: South Carolina had an enslaved majority, and Britain declared war on Spain in October 1739, a month after the rising.","The consequence: the Negro Act of South Carolina, passed on 10 May 1740.","Negro Act terms: enslaved people were forbidden to assemble, to travel without a written pass, to earn money, to grow food for sale, to beat drums or horns, and to be taught to write; plantations had to keep one white person for every ten enslaved."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the New York Conspiracy (1741)","items":["The city: New York had about 10,000 inhabitants, of whom roughly 2,000 were enslaved, around one in five, working the docks, the workshops and their owners' households.","The setting: a severe winter in 1740 to 1741 with high prices, and war with Spain since October 1739, which fed rumours of Spanish and Catholic plots.","The first fire: 18 March 1741 at Fort George, destroying the governor's house and chapel; about a dozen more fires followed within three weeks.","The witness: Mary Burton, a sixteen-year-old Irish indentured servant at John Hughson's waterfront tavern, arrested over stolen goods, who testified to a plot to burn the city, kill the white men and make Hughson king.","How the case grew: prisoners were promised their lives in return for confessing and naming others, so each confession produced further arrests.","Arrests: about 152 black people and 20 white people.","Executions: thirteen black men were burned at the stake; seventeen black people and four white people were hanged.","Among those hanged: John Hughson, his wife, Margaret Kerry, and John Ury, a schoolmaster accused of being a Catholic priest.","Other punishments: about seventy black people were transported out of the colony and seven white people banished.","Afterwards: Mary Burton was paid the reward of a hundred pounds, and the trials stopped when she began naming respectable citizens; historians still disagree over whether any real plot existed."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: colonial society, trade, smuggling and the fur trade, 1713 to 1741","items":["Population: a little over 330,000 in 1710 and about 900,000 by 1740, nearly trebling in thirty years.","Immigration: Ulster Protestants left in waves from 1717, notably 1717 to 1718, 1725 to 1729 and 1740 to 1741; German-speaking families from the Rhineland sailed mainly to Pennsylvania.","Where they went: inland and south-west through the Shenandoah valley into the Virginia and Carolina backcountry, because tidewater land was already taken.","Tensions: with Native peoples over land; between backcountry settlers and the coastal planter gentry who controlled the assemblies and taxes; and between the Anglican establishment and Presbyterian and German congregations.","Government: each colony had a governor, a council and an elected assembly which controlled taxes and often the governor's salary.","Georgia: charter sealed in 1732, first settlers under James Oglethorpe founded Savannah in February 1733; the trustees banned slavery there, a ban not lifted until 1751.","South Carolina's population by 1740: about two thirds of African descent, roughly 39,000 people out of about 56,000.","The Molasses Act 1733: sixpence a gallon on foreign molasses, ninepence a gallon on foreign rum, five shillings a hundredweight on foreign sugar, to protect British West Indian planters.","Why it failed: New England distillers could not pay the duty and stay in business, so they smuggled from the French and Dutch islands, falsified papers and bribed collectors; the Act raised almost nothing.","Enforcement: collectors under the Commissioners of Customs in London, and vice-admiralty courts set up in the colonies by the Navigation Act of 1696 so customs cases could be heard without a local jury; but officers were few and colonial juries would not convict.","Salutary neglect: Sir Robert Walpole, in office from 1721 to 1742, judged a flourishing colonial trade worth more than strictly collected duties; the phrase itself was coined later, by Edmund Burke in 1775.","The northern fur trade: Albany bought furs from the Haudenosaunee, who had made peace with the French at Montreal in 1701 and traded with both empires.","Attempts to control it: New York forbade the sale of English trade goods to the French at Montreal in 1720, and Albany merchants evaded it; traders had worked from a blockhouse at Oswego on Lake Ontario since 1722, and in 1727 Governor William Burnet had a stone blockhouse, later called Fort Burnet, built there to intercept furs before they reached the French.","The southern trade: Charles Town traders exchanged cloth, guns and rum with the Creek, Cherokee and Chickasaw for deerskins, tens of thousands of which were shipped each year."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 2 booklet P Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the South Sea Company's asiento trade in the years 1713 to 1739' (8 marks)","items":["The question as printed: 'Write a narrative account analysing the South Sea Company's asiento trade in the years 1713 to 1739. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the grant of the asiento at the Treaty of Utrecht (1713); the declaration of war on Spain (October 1739). You must also use information of your own.'","Decode it before writing. 'Narrative account' means a sequence that leads to an outcome, so the account must begin and end somewhere definite; 'analysing' means the join between each episode and the next has to be explained, not assumed; and the dates fix the boundaries, so the war that ended in 1713 belongs only in an opening sentence and nothing after 1739 belongs except in the closing line.","The cap that decides the level: there is no access to Level 3 at all for an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content, so decide first which episodes are your own material beyond the two bullets, and make sure those are the ones you develop.","Opening condition, one sentence only: Spain held colonies it could not supply with enslaved labour from its own ships, so the right to supply them was a prize worth taking out of the peace, and Britain took it.","Episode 1, the asiento of 1713: the contract went to the South Sea Company, and allowed it to carry 4,800 enslaved Africans a year to Spanish America for thirty years and to send one licensed trading ship a year to the Spanish American fairs. Own material to add here: the Royal African Company's monopoly had already been broken in 1698, so British slaving was open to anyone and the Company had to buy captives from rivals.","Link sentence: because the slaving side of the contract was taxed, policed and often sold at a loss, while the one licensed ship paid handsomely, the Company's real interest moved from the trade it was contracted to carry towards the trade it was merely permitted.","Episode 2, the annual ship in practice (own material): the first of them, the Royal Prince, was due in 1714 but did not sail until 1716, and only about seven ever sailed, the last in the early 1730s, each of them a legal door through which unlicensed British goods could also be landed.","Link sentence: once a licensed ship was understood on both sides to be the cover for an unlicensed trade, every British vessel in those waters became suspect, so Spain answered with its guarda costas, privateers commissioned to stop and search ships for contraband.","Episode 3, the quarrel over searching (own material): the interruptions of the trade when Britain and Spain fought in 1718 and again in the later 1720s, and the boarding of the merchant ship Rebecca in 1731, in which Captain Robert Jenkins said a Spanish officer cut off part of his ear.","Link sentence: because a grievance that had been minor while the trade was profitable became useful once it was not, opposition politicians brought Jenkins before Parliament in 1738 and turned a customs dispute into a public demand for war.","Episode 4, the collapse of 1739: the Convention of El Pardo of January 1739 set terms for compensating British merchants, but the Company would not pay what it owed the Spanish king, the settlement fell apart, and war was declared in October 1739, ending the asiento trade.","The closing sentence must say what the sequence amounted to rather than repeat it: a contract sought as a way into the Spanish empire delivered comparatively little slaving and a great deal of smuggling, and the quarrel it bred cost Britain a war, so that the asiento was finally given up at the Treaty of Madrid in 1750 for a hundred thousand pounds.","Timing: about thirteen minutes at a minute a mark plus planning, which is roughly two minutes to choose and order the four episodes and write the link sentences in note form, ten to write, and one to check that every episode carries a date and that no sentence merely describes."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:37:23Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"The retargeting is clean: dupscan prints no duplication, none of the five worked examples, the model pair or the plan now sets a bank question or either of the specimen booklet P bullets (the nearest, Stono and Spotswood, differ in both command word and tariff), and every new fact holds on checking - the enumeration of 1660, 1704 and 1721, the Whydah from the three-day chase to the wreck on 26ᵗʰ April 1717 and the Boston hangings, the 1712 code's ticket and fortnightly search with the patrol act of 1704, Gooch's appointment in January 1727 and the forty warehouses, two inspectors, burnt trash, tobacco notes and 1735 defeats of the 1730 Act, the Van Meter grants of June 1730 with their thousand acres a family, the Hite-McKay order of October 1731, Gooch's 1738 assurance to the Synod of Philadelphia and the counties of November 1738 whose courts waited until 1743 and 1745, the asiento's 4,800 a year and the Royal Prince delayed to 1716, El Pardo and the war of October 1739, and New England's refuse fish, staves and distilleries; only the Sultana's rig and five overlong 8-mark answers want a pencil."}}},"P2-2":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"P2-2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 2 is the hinge of the whole option. It opens with colonists who thought of themselves as loyal British subjects and closes with colonists arguing in print that a Parliament they did not elect had no right to tax them, and the thing that turned the first into the second was a war Britain won. Booklet P sets three question types on this material and each rewards a different move. Question 1 wants one consequence of a single event analysed rather than retold, so you need to be able to say what was different after Louisbourg, after Quebec, after the Proclamation. Question 2 wants a narrative account in which each episode explains the next, and the years 1758 to 1764 give you several such chains ready made. Question 3 wants importance: what difference the Great Awakening, Benjamin Franklin, the Treaty of Paris or the Sugar Act made to the situations that followed them. This room teaches the period as those chains, with the dates, names and figures an examiner rewards, and it trains the three moves one at a time.","idea":[{"code":"P2-2 — 1 The impact of cultural developments: Religious revivals","h":"The Great Awakening: a revival that taught colonists to judge authority for themselves","t":"From the late 1730s a wave of revival preaching swept the colonies. Jonathan Edwards, minister at Northampton in Massachusetts, preached the sermon 'Sinners in the Hands of an Angry God' at Enfield in Connecticut on 8 July 1741, warning his hearers that only a personal conversion could save them. George Whitefield, an English clergyman, toured the colonies in 1739 and 1740, preaching in fields and market places from Georgia to New England because no church could hold the crowds, and Gilbert Tennent attacked settled ministers in 1740 as unconverted men unfit to guide anyone. The revival split the churches: 'New Lights' who welcomed it against 'Old Lights' such as Charles Chauncy of Boston, who thought it hysteria; the Presbyterians divided in 1741. New Light supporters founded colleges of their own, including the College of New Jersey (later Princeton) in 1746. What a top-level answer does with this is refuse to treat it as church history. The Awakening mattered because it made religion a matter of personal choice rather than inherited authority, because an itinerant preacher was the first voice most colonists had heard that belonged to no single colony, and because a generation learned to judge a minister on his message rather than on his office - a habit later turned on governors and on Parliament."},{"code":"P2-2 — 1 The impact of cultural developments: The Enlightenment","h":"The Enlightenment in the colonies: schools, newspapers and libraries made an arguing public","t":"Enlightenment ideas reached ordinary colonists through print and through institutions rather than through philosophy books. New England had required towns to keep schools since the seventeenth century and its literacy rates were among the highest in the English-speaking world; new colleges appeared, from Yale in 1701 to the College of New Jersey in 1746 and the Academy of Philadelphia, which opened in 1751 with a practical, non-denominational curriculum. Newspapers multiplied: the Boston News-Letter of 1704 was the first that lasted, the number of English-language colonial papers rose from eleven in 1754 to nineteen by 1760, and by the mid-1760s about twenty-four weeklies were printed in the thirteen colonies. The acquittal of the New York printer John Peter Zenger in August 1735, after his paper had attacked the governor, encouraged editors to print criticism of officials. Subscription libraries, beginning with the Library Company of Philadelphia in 1731, put expensive books within reach of tradesmen. A top-level answer treats all this as machinery: it is what allowed a grievance in Boston to be read, answered and shared in Philadelphia within a fortnight, and it explains why colonial protest after 1763 was written as argument from principle rather than shouted as riot."},{"code":"P2-2 — 1 The impact of cultural developments: The significance of Benjamin Franklin","h":"Benjamin Franklin: the man who built the institutions the ideas travelled through","t":"Franklin was born in Boston in 1706 and settled in Philadelphia as a printer. He founded the Junto discussion club in 1727, bought the Pennsylvania Gazette in 1729, set up the Library Company of Philadelphia in 1731 and published Poor Richard's Almanack every year from 1732 until 1758, which carried his maxims on thrift and industry into thousands of ordinary households. As a philanthropist he organised a fire company in 1736, the American Philosophical Society in 1743, the Academy of Philadelphia which opened in 1751 and, with Dr Thomas Bond, the Pennsylvania Hospital in the same year. As an intellectual he was famous across Europe for his work on electricity, including the kite experiment of 1752 and the lightning rod. He was also political: his 'Join, or Die' woodcut appeared in the Pennsylvania Gazette on 9 May 1754, he drafted the Albany Plan of Union that year, and he went to London in 1757 as Pennsylvania's agent. A top-level answer on his importance traces what difference he made rather than listing achievements: he supplied the colonies with the printing, the libraries and the societies through which Enlightenment ideas actually circulated, and he gave them their first picture of themselves as one body rather than thirteen."},{"code":"P2-2 — 2 War in the colonies: Relations with the French and with Indigenous peoples","h":"King George's War, 1744-48: a victory given away, and an alliance quietly breaking","t":"When the War of the Austrian Succession spread to North America in 1744 the fighting fell on the colonists. In June 1745 a New England expedition of colonial volunteers under William Pepperrell, escorted by Commodore Peter Warren's squadron, took the great French fortress of Louisbourg on Cape Breton after a siege of several weeks. French and allied war parties struck back along the frontier, burning Saratoga in New York in November 1745 and Grand Pre in Nova Scotia in 1747. The Iroquois Confederacy had held to neutrality between the two empires since 1701, though Mohawk warriors fought alongside the British. Then the Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle of 18 October 1748 handed Louisbourg back to France in exchange for Madras in India, and New England, which had paid for the expedition in men and money, was furious. Britain founded Halifax in Nova Scotia on 21 June 1749 as a counterweight. Nothing had been settled in the interior: the Ohio Company of Virginia received a grant of 200,000 acres on the upper Ohio in 1749, the French answered by building Fort Presque Isle, Fort Le Boeuf and Venango in 1753, and in June 1753 the Mohawk leader Theyanoguin told the governor of New York that the Covenant Chain with the British was broken. A top-level answer uses this as the cause of the next war and as the first lesson colonists drew about being used and then overruled by London."},{"code":"P2-2 — 2 War in the colonies: The French and Indian War (1754–63)","h":"The French and Indian War, 1754-63: from Washington's defeat to Wolfe at Quebec","t":"The war began badly. Governor Dinwiddie of Virginia sent the young George Washington west in 1753 to warn the French off the Ohio; in 1754 Washington's men killed a French officer at Jumonville Glen on 28 May and Washington himself surrendered Fort Necessity on 3 July. On 9 July 1755 Major General Edward Braddock was ambushed and mortally wounded near the Monongahela, and most of his column of about 1,300 men was killed or wounded. Britain declared war formally in 1756 and lost Fort William Henry in August 1757. The turn came when William Pitt took direction of the war from 1757 and poured in regular troops, naval power and subsidies that paid colonial assemblies to raise men. Louisbourg fell again in July 1758, Fort Frontenac in August, and the French blew up Fort Duquesne in November 1758, the British building Fort Pitt on the site. General James Wolfe besieged Quebec from June 1759, bombarded the town all summer, and on the night of 12 September landed about 4,000 men at the Anse au Foulon who climbed the cliff to the Plains of Abraham. The battle on 13 September 1759 lasted well under an hour: Wolfe was killed on the field and Montcalm died of his wounds the next day. Quebec surrendered on 18 September 1759 and Montreal on 8 September 1760. A top-level answer explains the victory rather than narrating it: money, sea power and Pitt's subsidies turned a frontier struggle into a war the French could not supply."},{"code":"P2-2 — 2 War in the colonies: The significance of the Treaty of Paris (1763) and the Proclamation Act (1763)","h":"1763: the peace that won a continent and the proclamation that fenced it off","t":"The Treaty of Paris was signed on 10 February 1763. France surrendered Canada and all its claims east of the Mississippi except New Orleans; Spain gave Florida to Britain; France had already passed Louisiana west of the Mississippi to Spain in 1762. Britain handed back the sugar islands of Guadeloupe and Martinique to France and Havana to Spain, and France kept Saint-Pierre and Miquelon with fishing rights off Newfoundland. On paper the colonists had won the interior. Then, on 7 October 1763, a royal proclamation, which Edexcel calls the Proclamation Act, drew a line along the Appalachians beyond which colonial governors might grant no land, reserved the country west of it for Indigenous peoples, ruled that only the Crown could buy their land, licensed the traders who entered it, and created the new colonies of Quebec, East Florida, West Florida and Grenada. To London this was cheap frontier peace after Pontiac's rising; to a Virginia gentleman with a western land claim it was the confiscation of the prize the war had been fought for. A top-level answer holds the two documents together: the same settlement that removed the French, and with them the colonists' need for British protection, also told the colonists what they might not do with the land they thought they had won."},{"code":"P2-2 — 3 The aftermath of the French and Indian war: The impact of the war on American colonists' relations with Britain","h":"After the war: Britain counted the bill, the colonies counted the victory","t":"Victory left Britain with a debt that had grown from about 75 million pounds in 1756 to about 133 million in 1763, and with a new empire of forts, garrisons and Indigenous nations to police; in early 1763 Lord Bute's ministry settled a peacetime establishment of about 10,000 regular troops for North America, and somebody had to pay for them. Britain's soldiers and officials came home with a low opinion of the colonies: assemblies had haggled over money and men, merchants had gone on trading with the French West Indies in wartime, and towns had resisted quartering. The colonists' memories ran the other way. They had raised thousands of provincial soldiers, and under the royal order of 1754 a provincial officer ranked below any regular of the same grade, a humiliation Washington felt keenly. In Boston in February 1761 James Otis argued in court against the general search warrants known as writs of assistance, the first sustained colonial argument that an Act of Parliament could be void. Above all, with the French gone from Canada the colonies no longer needed the Royal Navy and the redcoats to survive. A top-level answer says both things at once: the same war persuaded Britain that the colonies must be made to contribute and obey, and persuaded the colonies that they had won it and no longer needed looking after."},{"code":"P2-2 — 3 The aftermath of the French and Indian war: Opposition to the Sugar Act (1764)","h":"The Sugar Act, 1764: a smaller tax that raised a much larger question","t":"George Grenville's American Revenue Act received the royal assent on 5 April 1764 and came into force on 29 September. It actually halved the duty on foreign molasses, from the sixpence a gallon set by the Molasses Act of 1733 down to threepence, but it did so with the declared intention of collecting every penny, where the old duty had been evaded for thirty years. It added duties on foreign refined sugar, wine, coffee and indigo, banned foreign rum outright, buried shippers in new paperwork, and allowed smuggling cases to be tried without a jury in vice-admiralty courts, with a new court at Halifax for the whole of America. The Currency Act of April 1764 stopped colonies issuing paper money as legal tender, and all of this landed in a post-war trade depression. Boston's town meeting in May 1764 instructed its representatives to argue that a Parliament in which the colonies had no member had no right to tax them; James Otis published 'The Rights of the British Colonies Asserted and Proved' that year; merchants agreed to give up some British luxuries; several assemblies petitioned. A top-level answer keeps the argument, not the money, in the foreground: the sums were small, but for the first time colonists in several colonies said in public that Parliament might regulate their trade and still not tax them."},{"code":"P2-2 — 3 The aftermath of the French and Indian war: Relations with Indigenous peoples","h":"The frontier after 1763: Pontiac's Rebellion and the Paxton Boys","t":"British victory left Indigenous nations of the interior without the French to balance the British, and Sir Jeffery Amherst cut back the gifts of powder, shot and provisions by which officers had kept alliances alive. Neolin, a Lenape prophet, was already preaching rejection of European goods and drink and a return to older ways. In May 1763 Pontiac, an Odawa leader, failed to seize Fort Detroit by surprise on 7 May and besieged it from 9 May until 31 October; within weeks eight British posts west of the Appalachians had been captured or abandoned. Colonel Henry Bouquet fought his way through at Bushy Run on 5 and 6 August 1763 to relieve Fort Pitt, where the garrison had already handed besieging envoys blankets from its smallpox hospital. Amherst was recalled at the end of 1763 and replaced by Thomas Gage; Bouquet's march to the forks of the Muskingum in the autumn of 1764 ended the fighting in the Ohio country and recovered captives, and Pontiac made peace at Oswego in July 1766. Meanwhile Pennsylvania frontiersmen took their own revenge: on 14 December 1763 about fifty Paxton Boys killed six Conestoga at their village, and on 27 December they broke into the Lancaster workhouse and killed the fourteen survivors sheltering there. Several hundred then marched on Philadelphia, and in February 1764 a delegation led by Benjamin Franklin met them at Germantown and talked them into going home; nobody was ever punished. A top-level answer draws the conclusion: on the frontier British authority rested on nothing that frontiersmen or Indigenous nations had reason to respect."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1(a): one explained consequence of the writs of assistance case, February 1761","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(a): 'Explain one consequence of the writs of assistance case argued in Boston in February 1761.' [4 marks]. Question 1 is printed in two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each on a separate event and each marked on its own out of 4, so this part needs one consequence analysed and supported. The information you are working from: a writ of assistance was a general search warrant which let a customs officer enter any warehouse, shop or house in search of smuggled goods without naming the place to be searched; Boston merchants employed the lawyer James Otis to challenge the writs before the Superior Court of Massachusetts in February 1761, and he gave up a Crown post in order to act for them; Otis argued for several hours that an Act of Parliament against the constitution was void and that the courts should refuse to enforce such an Act; the court found for the customs service and writs of assistance went on being issued; Otis was elected to the Massachusetts assembly soon afterwards, and in 1764 he published 'The Rights of the British Colonies Asserted and Proved' against the Sugar Act.","steps":["Separate what the court decided from what the argument left behind: the merchants lost, so the consequence cannot be any change in the law itself.","State the consequence as a change in what colonists could now say: colonial opposition acquired a constitutional argument, that an Act of Parliament could be void and that a court need not enforce it.","Support it with the precise detail given: Otis resigned a Crown office to act for the Boston merchants in February 1761, the court still allowed the writs, and the argument outlived the verdict because he carried it into the assembly and then into print in 1764.","Make the link the mark scheme calls analysis: because the objection had been put as a matter of right rather than of inconvenience, the same reasoning was ready to hand in 1764 when Otis wrote against the Sugar Act, so a quarrel about customs searches had supplied the colonies with the language of their later quarrel with Parliament."],"cues":["Who won the case, and why does that decide what kind of consequence you may choose?","What could colonists now argue that they had not argued in this way before?","Which details show that the argument survived the verdict?","Why should an argument about search warrants matter in a quarrel about taxes?"]},{"title":"Question 1(b): one explained consequence of the Currency Act of 1764","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(b): 'Explain one consequence of the Currency Act of 1764.' [4 marks]. One consequence only, analysed and supported, marked out of 4 on its own. The information you are working from: paper money issued by the colonial assemblies was the money most colonists actually used, because gold and silver drained away to Britain to pay for imports; an Act of 1751 had already restricted paper money in the New England colonies; the Currency Act received the royal assent on 19 April 1764, applied to all the American colonies and forbade any future issue of paper bills of credit to be made legal tender; it took effect on 1 September 1764; it arrived in a post-war trade depression and in the same session as the Sugar Act, which was passed to raise a revenue and was to be strictly enforced; colonial agents in London, Benjamin Franklin among them, lobbied for years afterwards to have it eased.","steps":["Ask what the Act took away rather than what it forbade: an assembly could still print notes, but from now on nobody could be compelled to accept them in payment.","State the consequence as a change in colonial conditions: the colonies lost the only currency they had in quantity at the moment when trade was already depressed, so debts and duties had to be met in coin that was scarce.","Support it with the detail given: assent on 19 April 1764 and effect from 1 September, all the American colonies covered where the Act of 1751 had reached only New England, and colonial agents lobbying in London for years afterwards.","Explain the link: because the squeeze fell on the very merchants who were also meeting the Sugar Act's new duties and paperwork, colonists read two separate laws as one policy, and a technical currency measure became evidence that Parliament legislated for British creditors without asking what the colonies needed."],"cues":["What exactly did the Act stop an assembly from doing?","Why did that matter more in this year than it would have done ten years earlier?","Which dates, and which comparison with the earlier Act, show how far the new one reached?","Why did colonists treat this Act and the Sugar Act as one grievance rather than two?"]},{"title":"Question 2: choosing the aspects and writing the joins in a narrative account of the war, 1755-57","setup":"Booklet P, Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the key events of the war in North America in the years 1755-57.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the British declaration of war (1756); the loss of Fort William Henry (1757). You must also use information of your own. The mark scheme prints one cap: no access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content. The material available: Major General Edward Braddock's column was ambushed near the Monongahela on 9 July 1755 and he was mortally wounded, after which French and allied war parties raided the Pennsylvania and Virginia frontier almost unopposed; Britain declared war on France on 17 May 1756, nearly two years after the first shots in the Ohio country; the Marquis de Montcalm reached Canada in 1756 and took Fort Oswego on Lake Ontario on 14 August 1756, capturing the garrison and its guns; Montcalm besieged Fort William Henry on Lake George and Lieutenant Colonel George Monro surrendered it on 9 August 1757, after which Montcalm's Indigenous allies attacked the column as it marched away under escort; William Pitt took charge of the war as Secretary of State in the summer of 1757 and began sending regular regiments and paying subsidies to the colonial assemblies.","steps":["Fix the point the account travels towards, because a narrative account must lead somewhere: the end of 1757, with Britain beaten in three successive campaigns and running the war in a new way.","Count your aspects before you write, because there is no access to Level 3 for an answer that does not address three or more: the two bullets are two, so Braddock, Oswego and Pitt's arrival are the material of your own that lifts the answer.","Write the join from Braddock to the declaration as an explanation and not as a date: because the destruction of a regular column on 9 July 1755 left the frontier open to raids the provinces could not stop, a colonial quarrel could no longer be contained, and on 17 May 1756 the two governments declared formally what had been true for two years.","Write the join from Oswego to Fort William Henry in the same way: taking Oswego on 14 August 1756 gave Montcalm Lake Ontario and the initiative, so the following summer he could come south in strength against a fort Monro had too few men to hold, and it fell on 9 August 1757.","Close by saying what the sequence amounted to rather than repeating it: three seasons of defeat persuaded London that the colonies could not be left to win the war themselves, and from the summer of 1757 Pitt paid for it with regular regiments and with subsidies to the assemblies."],"cues":["Where must this account end, and in what state?","How many aspects of content do the two bullets give you, and what will you add to them?","Why did a defeat on the Monongahela make a formal declaration of war more likely?","What did holding Lake Ontario allow Montcalm to do the following summer?","What is the one thing the three seasons changed?"]},{"title":"Question 3: the importance of William Pitt for the British war effort in North America","setup":"Booklet P, Question 3 offers three bullets and you explain two, each marked separately out of 8. This bullet: 'The importance of William Pitt for the British war effort in North America.' (8 marks) No stimulus points are printed and no cap applies. The material available: the war had gone badly, Britain losing Fort Oswego in 1756 and Fort William Henry in August 1757, while colonial assemblies had argued for years over how many men and how much money each of them should find; Pitt became Secretary of State in the summer of 1757 and directed the war from London; he treated North America as the theatre that mattered most and used the navy to keep French reinforcements and supplies from crossing the Atlantic; instead of demanding men from the colonies he offered money, promising to reimburse assemblies that raised, armed and paid provincial regiments; he promoted young officers over their seniors, among them James Wolfe and Jeffery Amherst; the whole effort was carried on borrowing, and the national debt stood at about 133 million pounds by 1763.","steps":["Set the criterion the judgement will use, because importance means the difference made: test Pitt by asking whether the things he changed were the things that had been losing the war.","Take the money first and explain its effect: paying assemblies back for the regiments they raised turned an argument about who ought to contribute into a bargain Britain could simply buy, so manpower no longer waited on thirteen separate votes.","Take the strategy second: by making America the decisive theatre and using sea power against French reinforcement, he let numbers tell, because an army that cannot be supplied loses whatever its soldiers do in the field.","Take appointments third: promoting young officers over their seniors put men such as Wolfe and Amherst in command of the expeditions that followed, which is a change in how Britain chose commanders and not merely in who they were.","Judge, and count the cost: Britain's resources in America were the greater whoever held office, but within two years Pitt had turned them into soldiers, ships and commanders on the spot, and the borrowing that paid for it became the debt over which Britain and the colonies quarrelled once the war was over."],"cues":["What test will you use to decide whether he was important?","What had stood in the way of raising colonial troops before he took charge?","Why should sea power decide a war fought in the North American woods?","What was new about the way he chose his commanders?","What would have been true without him, and what would not?"]},{"title":"Question 3: the importance of the Iroquois Confederacy for the struggle between Britain and France","setup":"Booklet P, Question 3 offers three bullets and you explain two, each marked separately out of 8. This bullet: 'The importance of the Iroquois Confederacy for the struggle between Britain and France in North America in the years 1744-59.' (8 marks) No stimulus points are printed and no cap applies. The material available: since a settlement made in 1701 the Iroquois nations had kept to a policy of neutrality between the two empires, although Mohawk warriors often fought alongside the British; at Lancaster in Pennsylvania in the summer of 1744 (the conference opened on 25 June; the Virginia deed was signed on 2 July) the Six Nations signed a deed which Virginia read as a surrender of their claim to the Ohio country, a reading the Iroquois themselves disputed, and Virginia's title to the country the Ohio Company was granted in 1749 rested on it; in 1753 the Mohawk leader Theyanoguin told the governor of New York that the Covenant Chain was broken; in September 1753 the Board of Trade ordered the colonies to meet together to repair it, and the Albany Congress of June and July 1754 did so; Theyanoguin was killed fighting for the British at Lake George on 8 September 1755; about 900 Iroquois went with Sir William Johnson to Fort Niagara, which surrendered on 25 July 1759.","steps":["Decide what kind of importance is being claimed, because a confederacy is not a battle: judge the Iroquois by what both empires had to do because of them.","Take the neutrality first and explain its effect: while the Confederacy stayed out, neither empire dared occupy the country between them for fear of driving it to the other side, so the interior was contested with gifts, traders and treaties rather than held with garrisons.","Take the land claims second: Virginia's title to the Ohio rested on what the Six Nations had signed at Lancaster in 1744, so an Indigenous confederacy supplied the legal ground on which colonists advanced and the French objected.","Take the effect on imperial policy third: when Theyanoguin declared the Covenant Chain broken in 1753, the Board of Trade ordered the colonies to meet together, which is the reason there was a congress at Albany at all.","Judge: the Confederacy won no campaign by itself, and the warriors who went with Johnson to Niagara came only after the war had already turned; its importance is that for half a century it held a balance which made the interior something both empires had to negotiate for rather than simply take."],"cues":["What kind of importance can a confederacy have that a single battle cannot?","What did both empires have to keep doing while the Iroquois stayed neutral?","Whose claim to the Ohio country rested on an Iroquois signature?","Why was there a congress at Albany in the first place?","How far did Iroquois warriors decide the fighting, and what did decide it?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Give the date of the Battle of the Plains of Abraham and say what happened to the two commanders.","a":"13 September 1759. Wolfe was killed on the field; Montcalm was mortally wounded and died the next day, 14 September. Quebec surrendered on 18 September 1759."},{"q":"What did the Treaty of Paris of 10 February 1763 give Britain, and what did Britain give back?","a":"Britain gained Canada and French claims east of the Mississippi except New Orleans, and Florida from Spain. It returned Guadeloupe and Martinique to France and Havana to Spain; France kept Saint-Pierre and Miquelon and fishing rights off Newfoundland."},{"q":"Name three things the royal proclamation of 7 October 1763 did.","a":"Any three of: drew a line along the Appalachians beyond which governors could grant no land; reserved the country west of it for Indigenous peoples; allowed only the Crown to buy Indigenous land; licensed traders entering it; created the colonies of Quebec, East Florida, West Florida and Grenada."},{"q":"What did the Paxton Boys do in December 1763, and how did the march on Philadelphia end?","a":"About fifty of them killed six Conestoga at their village on 14 December 1763 and the fourteen survivors in the Lancaster workhouse on 27 December. Several hundred marched on Philadelphia, and in February 1764 a delegation led by Benjamin Franklin met them at Germantown and persuaded them to disperse. Nobody was punished."},{"q":"By how much did the Sugar Act change the duty on foreign molasses, and why did that still cause anger?","a":"It halved it, from sixpence a gallon under the Molasses Act of 1733 to threepence. The anger came from the declared intention to collect it, the juryless vice-admiralty courts, and the claim that Parliament was taxing colonists who elected nobody to it."}]},"room":{"facts":["Booklet P is worth 32 marks and has three questions: Question 1, printed in two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each 'Explain one consequence of ...' for 4 marks and each on a separate event or development; Question 2, 'Write a narrative account analysing ...', 8 marks; Question 3, 'Explain two of the following: the importance of ... for ...', two chosen parts at 8 marks each, 16 in all.","Only Question 2 prints stimulus points, as two bullets followed by 'You must also use information of your own.' Its cap is printed in the mark scheme: 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' There is no cap at Level 2. Questions 1 and 3 print no bullets and carry no cap.","Each part of Question 1 is marked on its own two-level grid out of 4: Level 2 (3-4) needs features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific information added in support. Two accurate descriptions of the event stay in Level 1.","Question 2 is marked on a three-level grid out of 8, and Level 3 (6-8) needs a clear sequence of events leading to an outcome with the linkage between them analysed. Question 3 is marked twice on a three-level grid out of 8, and its Level 3 needs analysis of importance - the difference the event or person made to later situations - in a coherent line of reasoning.","Timing: Paper 2 lasts 1 hour 50 minutes for 64 marks, about 55 minutes a booklet, so booklet P's 32 marks get roughly a minute a mark with planning on top - about 12 minutes on Question 1's two parts, 13 on Question 2 and 14 on each part of Question 3, leaving a couple of minutes to check. No source material and no SPaG marks on this paper."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Great Awakening. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2, marked on the 4-mark consequence grid; Level 2 (3-4) needs features of the period analysed to explain a consequence with specific support. Credit the weakening of settled ministers' authority, the New Light and Old Light division and the Presbyterian split of 1741, the founding of new colleges such as the College of New Jersey in 1746, or the first shared experience crossing colony boundaries.","sol":"One consequence was that the authority of settled ministers was badly weakened. Gilbert Tennent told congregations in 1740 that an unconverted minister was unfit to guide anyone, and Jonathan Edwards and George Whitefield preached that salvation came through a personal conversion each hearer could judge for themselves. Because ordinary colonists were now invited to weigh a minister's message rather than accept his office, the churches divided into New Lights and Old Lights and the Presbyterians split in 1741, and New Light supporters founded the College of New Jersey in 1746 to train ministers of their own.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["A consequence is something that was true afterwards because of the revival, so ask what the revival changed about who was listened to.","The strongest consequences here are about authority and division rather than about feeling: look at what happened to the existing churches.","Three moves: name the consequence, give the specific evidence that proves it happened, then explain why it mattered."],"codes":["P2-2 — 1 The impact of cultural developments: Religious revivals"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of the capture of Louisbourg in 1745. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the consequence grid. Credit the surge of colonial pride and confidence in New England forces, the bitterness when the Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle returned the fortress to France in October 1748, the cost in money and lives borne by Massachusetts, or Britain's need to found Halifax in 1749 and to retake Louisbourg in 1758.","sol":"One consequence was lasting colonial resentment at how the victory was used. Colonial volunteers under William Pepperrell, escorted by Commodore Peter Warren's squadron, took the fortress in June 1745 at Massachusetts' own expense, yet the Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle of 18 October 1748 handed it back to France in exchange for Madras in India. Because the one great success colonists had won themselves was traded away in a European bargain, New Englanders began to treat British interests and colonial interests as separate things, and Britain had to found Halifax in 1749 and besiege Louisbourg all over again in 1758.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask who had paid for the victory and what was done with it three years later.","The consequence that scores best is a change in how colonists felt about British decisions, not a description of the siege.","Support the point with the two dates that make it: the capture and the treaty that undid it."],"codes":["P2-2 — 2 War in the colonies: Relations with the French and with Indigenous peoples"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of Braddock's defeat near the Monongahela in 1755. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the consequence grid. Credit the collapse of frontier defence and the raids that followed on Pennsylvania and Virginia, the damage to the reputation of British regulars among colonists, the loss of Indigenous allies who had backed the apparently stronger side, or the widening of the conflict into a formal war from 1756.","sol":"One consequence was that colonists lost their awe of British regular troops. Major General Braddock was ambushed on 9 July 1755 within a few miles of Fort Duquesne and mortally wounded, and most of his column of about 1,300 men was killed or wounded, while his colonial aide George Washington helped cover the retreat. Because a professional army with artillery had been destroyed by a smaller force fighting in the woods, colonists concluded that British officers did not understand American warfare, and provincial troops who had been treated as inferiors began to believe that their own methods and men were the better ones.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what colonists believed about British soldiers before this day and what they believed after it.","A consequence about attitudes will score as well as one about territory, provided you analyse it.","Anchor it with the date, the commander and the scale of the losses before you explain."],"codes":["P2-2 — 2 War in the colonies: The French and Indian War (1754–63)"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of the Proclamation Act (1763). [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the consequence grid. Credit the blocking of western settlement and of land companies' claims, colonial anger at being denied the prize of the war, the strengthening of the idea that London governed in its own interest, widespread illegal settlement beyond the line, or the temporary easing of Indigenous fears after Pontiac's rising.","sol":"One consequence was that colonists began to read British policy as being made against them rather than for them. The proclamation of 7 October 1763 forbade governors to grant land beyond the Appalachians and reserved the interior for Indigenous peoples, with only the Crown allowed to buy their land. Because Virginia gentlemen and land companies such as the Ohio Company had invested in western claims, and because thousands of colonists had fought a war they believed was about that very country, the line looked like the confiscation of the prize, and settlers crossed it anyway, which showed how little British authority the frontier respected.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask who lost something the moment the proclamation was published.","The consequence can be about what colonists concluded, provided you say why they concluded it.","Give the date and one precise provision before you explain the effect."],"codes":["P2-2 — 2 War in the colonies: The significance of the Treaty of Paris (1763) and the Proclamation Act (1763)"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the actions of the Paxton Boys in 1763-64. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the consequence grid. Credit the destruction of the Conestoga and the flight of other Indigenous groups to Philadelphia for protection, the exposure of Pennsylvania's government as unable to police its own frontier, the pamphlet war between frontier and east, or the fact that nobody was prosecuted and the frontier learned that such killings carried no penalty.","sol":"One consequence was that Pennsylvania's government was shown to be powerless on its own frontier. About fifty Paxton Boys killed six Conestoga at their village on 14 December 1763 and the fourteen survivors sheltering in the Lancaster workhouse on 27 December, and several hundred then marched on Philadelphia. Because the province had no force able to stop them, the crisis was settled by negotiation when a delegation led by Benjamin Franklin met them at Germantown in February 1764 and talked them into going home, and no one was ever prosecuted, so frontier settlers learned that killing Indigenous neighbours carried no penalty.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what the province did about the killings, and what that told everybody.","A consequence about the authority of government will analyse better than a retelling of the two attacks.","Use the two December dates and the Germantown meeting as your supporting detail."],"codes":["P2-2 — 3 The aftermath of the French and Indian war: Relations with Indigenous peoples"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the founding of the Library Company of Philadelphia in 1731. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the growth of colonial newspapers in the 1750s and 1760s. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4 on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit access to expensive books for tradesmen, the model copied by other towns, and the spread of Enlightenment reading beyond the wealthy. In 1(b) credit the creation of a colony-wide reading public, the rapid sharing of grievances between colonies, the encouragement printers took from Zenger's acquittal in 1735, and the printed constitutional argument of 1764.","sol":"1(a) Because members pooled subscriptions to buy books none of them could afford alone, the Library Company put natural philosophy, history and political writing within reach of Philadelphia's tradesmen and shopkeepers from 1731 onwards. Reading of that kind had belonged to gentlemen with private libraries; now it belonged to the sort of men who sat in Franklin's Junto, and other towns copied the subscription model, so Enlightenment ideas reached people who would later argue about rights in print. 1(b) The number of English-language colonial newspapers rose from eleven in 1754 to nineteen by 1760 and about twenty-four weeklies by the mid-1760s, and printers had been encouraged to criticise officials since John Peter Zenger's acquittal in New York in August 1735. Because a paper in one town reprinted what another had said, a grievance stopped being local: when Boston's town meeting objected to the Sugar Act in May 1764 and James Otis published his pamphlet that year, the argument could be read in every port within weeks.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate parts on two separate developments, so neither answer may lean on the other.","For 1(a) ask who could read what, and at what cost, before and after 1731.","For 1(b) the consequence is about scale and speed: what could a printed argument do that a conversation could not?"],"codes":["P2-2 — 1 The impact of cultural developments: The Enlightenment","P2-2 — 1 The impact of cultural developments: The significance of Benjamin Franklin"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Albany Congress of 1754. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the British victory at Quebec in September 1759. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4. In 1(a) credit the repair of the Covenant Chain with the Iroquois, the rejection of Franklin's Plan of Union by the assemblies and by London, and the first blueprint for colonial union. In 1(b) credit the fall of Montreal in 1760 and the end of French rule, the removal of the French threat and with it colonial dependence on Britain, or the terms of the Treaty of Paris.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that the colonies discovered how unwilling they were to be governed together. Franklin's plan, drafted at Albany in the summer of 1754, proposed a president-general appointed by the Crown and a grand council elected by the assemblies, with power to tax for defence. Because every assembly guarded its own charter and London wanted a military command rather than an elected council, the plan was rejected on both sides of the Atlantic, yet it survived as a printed blueprint that colonists remembered when union became urgent. 1(b) One consequence was that the conquest of Canada became unstoppable. After the battle on the Plains of Abraham on 13 September 1759, which lasted well under an hour, Quebec surrendered on 18 September, so Britain held the mouth of the St Lawrence and the supply line to the French interior. Three British columns converged on Montreal the next year and it surrendered on 8 September 1760, ending French rule in Canada and, with it, the danger that had made the colonies depend on British protection.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two parts, one on a conference and one on a battle: a conference changes minds and plans, a battle changes what is possible.","For 1(a) ask what the assemblies and London each did with the plan, and what that revealed.","For 1(b) follow the river: what did holding Quebec make possible the following year?"],"codes":["P2-2 — 1 The impact of cultural developments: The significance of Benjamin Franklin","P2-2 — 2 War in the colonies: The French and Indian War (1754–63)"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Treaty of Paris (1763). [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the British decision to keep a standing army in North America after 1763. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4. In 1(a) credit the removal of the French threat and the end of colonial dependence on Britain, the huge new territory to be governed and policed, and the anger of Indigenous nations left without a French counterweight. In 1(b) credit the search for colonial revenue that produced the Sugar Act, quartering disputes, and colonial suspicion about why troops were needed once the French had gone.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that the colonies stopped needing Britain. By the treaty signed on 10 February 1763 France gave up Canada and all its claims east of the Mississippi except New Orleans, and Spain gave up Florida. Because no hostile European power now bordered the thirteen colonies, the argument that colonists must accept British regulation in return for protection lost its force, and colonists began to weigh imperial demands on their merits rather than accept them out of fear. 1(b) One consequence was a search for colonial revenue. The war had raised the national debt from about 75 million pounds in 1756 to about 133 million by 1763, and in early 1763 Lord Bute's ministry settled a peacetime establishment of about 10,000 regulars for North America. Because Parliament would not load the whole cost onto British taxpayers, Grenville turned to American duties, and the Sugar Act of 1764 was the first fruit; colonists, seeing no enemy left to fight, asked instead whom the army was there to control.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two parts on two separate decisions: a peace treaty in 1(a), a military and financial choice in 1(b).","For 1(a) ask what disappeared from the map, and what argument disappeared with it.","For 1(b) follow the money: who was going to pay, and what did colonists suspect the soldiers were for?"],"codes":["P2-2 — 2 War in the colonies: The significance of the Treaty of Paris (1763) and the Proclamation Act (1763)","P2-2 — 3 The aftermath of the French and Indian war: The impact of the war on American colonists' relations with Britain"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of Pontiac's Rebellion (1763-64). [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the removal of the French threat from North America. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4. In 1(a) credit the loss of eight British posts and the cost of the campaigns, the proclamation line of 7 October 1763 as a response, Amherst's recall and replacement by Gage, and the restoration of gift-giving. In 1(b) credit the end of colonial dependence on Britain, the collapse of Indigenous bargaining power between two empires, and the freeing of colonial attention for quarrels with London.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was a change in how Britain tried to govern the interior. Pontiac besieged Fort Detroit from 9 May to 31 October 1763 and within weeks eight British posts west of the Appalachians had been taken or abandoned, and Bouquet had to fight his way through at Bushy Run on 5 and 6 August to relieve Fort Pitt. Because holding the west by force had proved ruinously expensive, London drew the proclamation line of 7 October 1763 to keep settlers out, recalled Sir Jeffery Amherst at the end of the year and replaced him with Thomas Gage. 1(b) One consequence was that Indigenous nations lost their bargaining power. For fifty years they had been able to play French and British officers against each other, extracting gifts of powder, shot and provisions as the price of alliance. Because the Treaty of Paris in February 1763 left only one European power in the interior, Amherst felt free to cut those gifts back, and nations of the Ohio and Great Lakes found themselves negotiating with a government that no longer needed them.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two parts: the rising itself in 1(a), and the strategic change that lay behind it in 1(b).","For 1(a) ask what London did differently once it had counted the cost of the summer of 1763.","For 1(b) think about what a second European power had been worth to Indigenous nations."],"codes":["P2-2 — 3 The aftermath of the French and Indian war: Relations with Indigenous peoples","P2-2 — 3 The aftermath of the French and Indian war: The impact of the war on American colonists' relations with Britain"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the key events of the British conquest of Canada in the years 1758-60. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the capture of Louisbourg (1758); the Battle of the Plains of Abraham (1759). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) needs a clear sequence leading to an outcome with the linkage between events analysed. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; own material might include Pitt's strategy and subsidies, Fort Frontenac and Fort Duquesne, Niagara and Ticonderoga, the siege and bombardment of Quebec, and the surrender of Montreal in 1760.","sol":"The conquest began with a change of direction in London. From 1757 William Pitt sent regular regiments across the Atlantic and paid subsidies that let colonial assemblies raise provincial troops, so British numbers and sea power could at last be used together. That made the coast the target: Louisbourg fell in July 1758, closing the seaward approach to the St Lawrence, and Fort Frontenac in August, cutting the water route to the western posts. The effect was felt hundreds of miles inland, because the garrison of Fort Duquesne could no longer be supplied and blew the fort up in November 1758; Niagara and Ticonderoga followed in 1759. With the flanks gone, Quebec was all that held Canada together. Wolfe brought the fleet up the river in June 1759 and bombarded the town all summer without forcing a battle, so his answer was the landing at the Anse au Foulon on the night of 12 September, which put about 4,000 men on the Plains of Abraham by dawn. The battle on 13 September lasted well under an hour; Wolfe was killed in it, Montcalm died the next day, and the town surrendered on 18 September. Because Britain now held the mouth of the river, the three columns that converged on Montreal in 1760 could not be resisted, and it surrendered on 8 September. In three campaigning seasons a war of frontier ambushes had become a conquest, and the French power that had made the colonies depend on Britain was gone.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Begin before Louisbourg: what had changed in the way Britain was fighting the war by 1758?","The account needs joins, not just dates - at each stage ask what the previous capture made impossible for the French.","Plan four episodes with a sentence of explanation between each, so the answer addresses more than the two printed bullets."],"codes":["P2-2 — 2 War in the colonies: The French and Indian War (1754–63)","P2-2 — 2 War in the colonies: Relations with the French and with Indigenous peoples","P2-2 — 2 War in the colonies: The significance of the Treaty of Paris (1763) and the Proclamation Act (1763)"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing relations between the British and Indigenous peoples in the years 1763-64. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: Pontiac's Rebellion (1763-64); the Paxton Boys. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) needs a clear sequence leading to an outcome with the linkage analysed. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; own material might include the Treaty of Paris and the loss of the French counterweight, Amherst's ending of gifts, Neolin's preaching, Bushy Run, the proclamation of 7 October 1763, Amherst's recall, and Bouquet's Muskingum expedition of 1764.","sol":"The trouble began with the peace. The Treaty of Paris of February 1763 removed the French, and with them the rival Indigenous nations had played against the British for fifty years, so Sir Jeffery Amherst felt free to cut back the gifts of powder, shot and provisions that kept those alliances alive. That turned allies into dependants in a single season, and the Lenape prophet Neolin's call to reject European goods and drink found ready listeners. Pontiac, an Odawa leader, failed to take Fort Detroit by surprise on 7 May 1763 and besieged it from 9 May to 31 October; within weeks eight British posts west of the Appalachians had fallen or been abandoned, and Bouquet had to fight through at Bushy Run on 5 and 6 August to relieve Fort Pitt. Since holding the west by force was proving ruinous, London tried a boundary instead: the proclamation of 7 October 1763 barred land grants beyond the Appalachians. That satisfied nobody on the frontier, and Pennsylvania settlers took revenge themselves, about fifty Paxton Boys killing six Conestoga on 14 December and fourteen more in the Lancaster workhouse on 27 December before several hundred marched on Philadelphia and were turned back by Franklin's delegation at Germantown in February 1764. Britain restored the gifts, recalled Amherst and sent Bouquet to the Muskingum in the autumn of 1764, ending the fighting and recovering captives. The year proved that British authority in the interior rested on agreement, because it could not rest on force.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Start with what the peace of 1763 took away from Indigenous nations, not with the first shot.","Each episode must explain the next: why did cutting gifts make a rising likely, and why did a rising make a boundary line attractive to London?","Use at least three aspects beyond the two bullets, and end on what the year proved rather than on a date."],"codes":["P2-2 — 3 The aftermath of the French and Indian war: Relations with Indigenous peoples","P2-2 — 2 War in the colonies: The significance of the Treaty of Paris (1763) and the Proclamation Act (1763)","P2-2 — 3 The aftermath of the French and Indian war: The impact of the war on American colonists' relations with Britain"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the growth of colonial opposition to British policy in the years 1763-64. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the Proclamation Act (1763); the Sugar Act (1764). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) needs a clear sequence leading to an outcome with the linkage analysed. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; own material might include the war debt and the army in America, the Currency Act, the juryless vice-admiralty courts, the Boston town meeting of May 1764, Otis's pamphlet and the merchants' agreements.","sol":"Opposition grew out of a British problem rather than a colonial one. The war had raised the national debt from about 75 million pounds in 1756 to about 133 million in 1763, and ministers had decided to keep about 10,000 regulars in America, so London needed a cheap frontier and an American revenue. The cheap frontier came first: the proclamation of 7 October 1763 barred land grants beyond the Appalachians, which struck at Virginia gentlemen and land companies who had understood the war to be about that country; settlers crossed it anyway. The revenue came next, and cut deeper because it touched the ports. The Sugar Act received the royal assent on 5 April 1764 and took effect on 29 September; it halved the molasses duty from sixpence to threepence a gallon but announced that it would now be collected, taxed foreign sugar, wine, coffee and indigo, and sent smuggling cases to juryless vice-admiralty courts. Because the Currency Act of April 1764 also banned colonial paper money as legal tender in an already depressed trade, merchants faced tighter enforcement with less cash. That turned complaint into argument: Boston's town meeting in May 1764 denied that a Parliament containing no colonial member could tax them, James Otis argued the same case in print, merchants gave up some British luxuries, and several assemblies petitioned. By the end of 1764 the quarrel had changed character, from grumbling about particular measures to a public claim about what Parliament might and might not do.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Start with the reason ministers were acting at all, or the two bullets will look like unrelated grievances.","Explain why a trade duty provoked more than a land boundary did - who was hit, and how visibly?","The closing sentence must say what had changed in the nature of the quarrel, not list the events again."],"codes":["P2-2 — 3 The aftermath of the French and Indian war: Opposition to the Sugar Act (1764)","P2-2 — 2 War in the colonies: The significance of the Treaty of Paris (1763) and the Proclamation Act (1763)","P2-2 — 3 The aftermath of the French and Indian war: The impact of the war on American colonists' relations with Britain"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the Great Awakening for colonial society. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid, Level 3 (6-8) needing analysis of importance in a coherent line of reasoning. Credit the weakening of established ministers, the New Light and Old Light division, the founding of new colleges, the first shared experience crossing colony boundaries, the reach of the revival among the poor and among enslaved people, and the habit of judging authority for oneself.","sol":"The Great Awakening mattered less for what colonists believed than for how they were taught to decide. Revival preaching from the late 1730s put conversion at the centre and made it something each hearer could recognise in themselves, so when Gilbert Tennent told congregations in 1740 that an unconverted minister was unfit to guide anyone, he was inviting ordinary people to judge the man in the pulpit. That authority never fully recovered: churches divided into New Lights and Old Lights, with Charles Chauncy of Boston leading the opposition, the Presbyterians split in 1741, and New Light supporters founded colleges of their own, beginning with the College of New Jersey in 1746. Its second effect was to make colonists visible to one another, because George Whitefield's tour of 1739 and 1740 carried one voice from Georgia to New England, preaching in fields when no building could hold the crowds, so colonists in different provinces shared an experience that owed nothing to their own assembly or governor. Its third effect was social reach: itinerants preached to the poor, to servants and to enslaved people whom settled congregations had largely ignored, which widened the number of colonists who thought their own judgement worth making. Without the revival the colonies would still have grown and argued, but they would have argued as thirteen separate establishments. The Awakening left a population practised at weighing a claim on its merits, and in the 1760s that practice was turned on Parliament.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Importance means the difference it made afterwards, so avoid any sentence that only describes a sermon.","Trace three separate lines forward: what it did to church authority, what it did across colony boundaries, and whom it reached.","Finish with a judgement that says what would have been different without it."],"codes":["P2-2 — 1 The impact of cultural developments: Religious revivals","P2-2 — 1 The impact of cultural developments: The Enlightenment"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of Benjamin Franklin for the spread of Enlightenment ideas in the colonies. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Credit the Pennsylvania Gazette and Poor Richard's Almanack, the Library Company of 1731, the American Philosophical Society of 1743, the Academy of Philadelphia and the Pennsylvania Hospital of 1751, his electrical work and European reputation, and the 'Join, or Die' woodcut and Albany Plan of 1754.","sol":"Ideas travel only if something carries them, and Franklin's importance is that he built the carriers. As a printer he owned the Pennsylvania Gazette from 1729 and published Poor Richard's Almanack every year from 1732 to 1758, a cheap annual bought by households that owned almost no other book, so maxims on industry and self-improvement reached tradesmen and farmers rather than gentlemen. In 1731 he turned his Junto into the Library Company of Philadelphia, whose members pooled subscriptions to buy books none could afford alone, and other towns copied the model, so serious reading stopped being the property of the rich. Second, he gave the colonies institutions that produced knowledge instead of importing it: the American Philosophical Society of 1743, and the Academy of Philadelphia, opened in 1751 with a practical, non-denominational curriculum, alongside the Pennsylvania Hospital founded with Dr Thomas Bond the same year. Third, his experiments with electricity, including the kite experiment of 1752 and the lightning rod, made a colonial printer famous across Europe, which told educated colonists that America could contribute to the age of reason rather than merely receive it. That these channels had political force was proved on 9 May 1754, when his 'Join, or Die' woodcut in his own newspaper gave the colonies their first shared picture of themselves. Enlightenment books would have crossed the Atlantic without him; it was Franklin who made sure they were read outside a few private libraries.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Decide your test for importance first: for the spread of ideas, judge him by what carried them and how far.","Group his work rather than listing it - print, institutions, and his own reputation - and explain the effect of each.","End by saying what would still have happened without him, and what would not."],"codes":["P2-2 — 1 The impact of cultural developments: The significance of Benjamin Franklin","P2-2 — 1 The impact of cultural developments: The Enlightenment"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the Sugar Act (1764) for colonial attitudes to Parliament. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Credit the declared revenue purpose and the intention to enforce, the juryless vice-admiralty courts, the Currency Act and the post-war depression, the Boston town meeting of May 1764 and Otis's pamphlet, the merchants' agreements and assembly petitions, and the distinction colonists began to draw between regulating trade and taxing.","sol":"The Sugar Act was important not for the money it raised but for the question it forced colonists to answer. Parliament had regulated colonial trade for a century and few had disputed the right; the Molasses Act of 1733 had set a duty of sixpence a gallon that everybody evaded. The Act that received the royal assent on 5 April 1764 and took effect on 29 September actually halved that duty to threepence, but it said openly that the purpose was revenue and that the duty would now be collected, so colonists had to decide whether Parliament could tax them at all. Enforcement sharpened the question: shippers faced elaborate paperwork and smuggling cases went to vice-admiralty courts where a judge sat without a jury, so a merchant lost both his cargo and the protection of his neighbours. The timing made it worse, because the Currency Act of April 1764 banned colonial paper money as legal tender in the middle of a post-war depression. The answers came quickly and in public: Boston's town meeting in May 1764 told its representatives that a Parliament containing no colonial member could not tax the colonies, James Otis published 'The Rights of the British Colonies Asserted and Proved' the same year, merchants agreed to do without some British goods, and several assemblies petitioned. That is the importance: within a year colonists had worked out, and printed, the distinction between regulating trade and levying taxes that they would use against every measure which followed.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Importance here is about an argument being formed, so resist explaining the duties in detail.","Ask what was new about this Act compared with a century of trade laws colonists had accepted.","Show the reaction in more than one place, then say what distinction colonists had settled on by the end of 1764."],"codes":["P2-2 — 3 The aftermath of the French and Indian war: Opposition to the Sugar Act (1764)","P2-2 — 3 The aftermath of the French and Indian war: The impact of the war on American colonists' relations with Britain"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Who preached 'Sinners in the Hands of an Angry God' at Enfield, Connecticut, on 8 July 1741?","a":"Jonathan Edwards."},{"q":"On what date was the Battle of the Plains of Abraham fought?","a":"13 September 1759."},{"q":"Along what natural barrier did the proclamation of 7 October 1763 draw its line?","a":"The Appalachian mountains."},{"q":"What did the Sugar Act of 1764 do to the duty on foreign molasses?","a":"It halved it, from sixpence to threepence a gallon."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The Great Awakening","code":"P2-2 — 1 The impact of cultural developments: Religious revivals","open":false,"back":"A wave of revival preaching across the colonies from the late 1730s, stressing personal conversion. It split churches into New Lights, who welcomed it, and Old Lights, who did not, and weakened the authority of settled ministers."},{"front":"George Whitefield","code":"P2-2 — 1 The impact of cultural developments: Religious revivals","open":false,"back":"English revivalist who toured the colonies in 1739 and 1740, preaching in the open air from Georgia to New England because no church could hold his crowds. The first voice most colonists heard that belonged to no single colony."},{"front":"The Library Company of Philadelphia, 1731","code":"P2-2 — 1 The impact of cultural developments: The Enlightenment","open":false,"back":"The first subscription library in the colonies, founded by Benjamin Franklin and his Junto. Members pooled money to buy books none could afford alone, putting serious reading within reach of tradesmen. Other towns copied it."},{"front":"Colonial newspapers in the 1750s and 1760s","code":"P2-2 — 1 The impact of cultural developments: The Enlightenment","open":false,"back":"English-language colonial papers rose from eleven in 1754 to nineteen by 1760, and about twenty-four weeklies by the mid-1760s. Zenger's acquittal in New York in August 1735 had encouraged printers to criticise officials."},{"front":"Poor Richard's Almanack","code":"P2-2 — 1 The impact of cultural developments: The significance of Benjamin Franklin","open":false,"back":"Franklin's annual almanac, published every year from 1732 to 1758. Cheap and widely bought, it carried maxims on thrift, industry and self-improvement into households that owned few other books."},{"front":"The Albany Plan of Union, 1754","code":"P2-2 — 1 The impact of cultural developments: The significance of Benjamin Franklin","open":false,"back":"Franklin's scheme for a president-general appointed by the Crown and a grand council elected by the assemblies, with power to tax for defence. Rejected by the assemblies and by London, but remembered as a blueprint for union."},{"front":"King George's War, 1744-48","code":"P2-2 — 2 War in the colonies: Relations with the French and with Indigenous peoples","open":false,"back":"The North American part of the War of the Austrian Succession. New England volunteers under William Pepperrell took Louisbourg in June 1745; the Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle of 18 October 1748 handed it back to France for Madras."},{"front":"Braddock's defeat near the Monongahela, 9 July 1755","code":"P2-2 — 2 War in the colonies: The French and Indian War (1754–63)","open":false,"back":"Major General Edward Braddock's column of about 1,300 regulars and provincials was ambushed short of Fort Duquesne and mostly killed or wounded; Braddock died of his wounds. George Washington, his aide, helped cover the retreat."},{"front":"The Battle of the Plains of Abraham, 13 September 1759","code":"P2-2 — 2 War in the colonies: The French and Indian War (1754–63)","open":false,"back":"About 4,000 British troops landed by night at the Anse au Foulon and climbed to the plateau above Quebec. The battle lasted well under an hour; Wolfe was killed and Montcalm died next day. Quebec surrendered on 18 September."},{"front":"The Treaty of Paris, 10 February 1763","code":"P2-2 — 2 War in the colonies: The significance of the Treaty of Paris (1763) and the Proclamation Act (1763)","open":false,"back":"France gave up Canada and its claims east of the Mississippi except New Orleans; Spain gave Florida to Britain. Britain returned Guadeloupe, Martinique and Havana, and France kept Saint-Pierre and Miquelon with Newfoundland fishing rights."},{"front":"One provision of the Proclamation Act, 7 October 1763","code":"P2-2 — 2 War in the colonies: The significance of the Treaty of Paris (1763) and the Proclamation Act (1763)","open":true,"back":"It barred colonial governors from granting land beyond the Appalachians; it reserved the interior for Indigenous peoples and allowed only the Crown to buy their land, licensing the traders who entered it; it created the new colonies of Quebec, East Florida, West Florida and Grenada."},{"front":"The British army in North America after 1763","code":"P2-2 — 3 The aftermath of the French and Indian war: The impact of the war on American colonists' relations with Britain","open":false,"back":"In early 1763 Lord Bute's ministry settled a peacetime establishment of about 10,000 regulars for North America. The war debt had grown from about 75 million pounds in 1756 to about 133 million, so London wanted colonial help to pay."},{"front":"The Sugar Act, 1764","code":"P2-2 — 3 The aftermath of the French and Indian war: Opposition to the Sugar Act (1764)","open":false,"back":"Royal assent 5 April 1764, in force 29 September. Halved the molasses duty from sixpence to threepence a gallon but enforced it for revenue, taxed foreign sugar, wine, coffee and indigo, and sent smuggling cases to juryless vice-admiralty courts."},{"front":"Pontiac's Rebellion, 1763-64","code":"P2-2 — 3 The aftermath of the French and Indian war: Relations with Indigenous peoples","open":false,"back":"Pontiac, an Odawa leader, besieged Fort Detroit from 9 May to 31 October 1763; eight British posts west of the Appalachians fell within weeks. Bouquet relieved Fort Pitt after Bushy Run, 5-6 August 1763. Amherst was recalled."},{"front":"The Paxton Boys","code":"P2-2 — 3 The aftermath of the French and Indian war: Relations with Indigenous peoples","open":false,"back":"Pennsylvania frontiersmen who killed six Conestoga on 14 December 1763 and fourteen more in the Lancaster workhouse on 27 December. Hundreds marched on Philadelphia; Franklin's delegation turned them back at Germantown in February 1764. Nobody was prosecuted."}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph - Level 3 (Paper 2 booklet P Question 2, narrative account, 8 marks)","items":["Question: 'Write a narrative account analysing the struggle for control of the Ohio country in the years 1753-54.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the French forts built on the upper Ohio (1753); the surrender of Fort Necessity (1754). You must also use information of your own. This is the opening and middle of the account, carrying the story from the two rival claims to the surrender at Fort Necessity.","The struggle began with two claims pressed at the same moment. The Ohio Company of Virginia had been granted 200,000 acres on the upper Ohio in 1749, and in 1753 the French answered by building Presque Isle, Le Boeuf and Venango to link Canada with the Mississippi. Because each side was now putting men and timber on ground the other said was its own, the argument could no longer be settled on paper, and Governor Dinwiddie sent the young George Washington north with a letter ordering the French to withdraw. Saint-Pierre received him at Fort Le Boeuf in December 1753 and courteously refused, which left Virginia with one answer only: to occupy the forks of the Ohio before the French did. That race was lost in the spring, when a force came down from Canada, took the small post the Ohio Company's men were building at the forks on 17 April 1754 and put Fort Duquesne on the site. Since the French now held the one point that commanded the river, Washington was marching to reinforce a place that no longer existed, and the campaign turned instead into a fight in the woods: he and the Mingo leader Tanacharison attacked a French party at Jumonville Glen on 28 May, and the French reply drove him into a hastily built stockade, Fort Necessity, which he surrendered on 3 July 1754.","Why this is Level 3: the material is organised as a clear sequence of events leading to an outcome and the linkage between them is analysed rather than asserted - the rival claims explain Dinwiddie's mission, the refusal at Fort Le Boeuf explains the race for the forks, and the loss of the forks explains why Washington was fighting in the open woods at all. The knowledge is precise (the grant of 1749, the three French forts, Fort Le Boeuf in December 1753, the forks on 17 April 1754, Jumonville Glen on 28 May, Fort Necessity on 3 July) and far more than three aspects of content are addressed, so the Level 3 cap does not apply."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph - Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same, 'Write a narrative account analysing the struggle for control of the Ohio country in the years 1753-54.' (8 marks), with the same two bullets.","The French and the British both wanted the Ohio country. The French built some forts there and the British did not like it, so relations got worse and worse. The governor of Virginia sent George Washington to tell the French to go away but they would not go. After that the French built a big fort where the rivers meet and called it Fort Duquesne. Washington came back with some soldiers and there was a fight in the woods with a French patrol. Then a larger French force came and attacked him at his own small fort and he had to surrender it and march home. In the end all this led to a big war between Britain and France.","Why this is only Level 2, and what it misses: the events are in the right order and nothing in it is untrue, so it meets 'some organisation of material into a sequence of events leading to an outcome' and 'accurate and relevant information is added'. It misses the Level 3 requirement that 'the account of events analyses the linkage between them': 'relations got worse and worse' asserts a link instead of explaining one, and nothing says why the Ohio Company's grant made the French building programme urgent, why the refusal at Fort Le Boeuf forced Virginia to race for the forks, or why losing the forks left Washington fighting in the open. The detail is also loose where the top level wants precision - no date for the mission, no names for the three French forts, no date for the seizure of the forks, no name for the skirmish and no date for the surrender - and the closing sentence summarises instead of saying what the eighteen months had produced."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Great Awakening and revivalist preachers","items":["Dates: a wave of revival preaching across the colonies from the late 1730s into the 1740s, strongest in 1739-42.","Jonathan Edwards: minister at Northampton, Massachusetts; preached 'Sinners in the Hands of an Angry God' at Enfield, Connecticut, on 8 July 1741.","George Whitefield: English clergyman who toured the colonies in 1739 and 1740, preaching in fields and market places from Georgia to New England.","Gilbert Tennent: attacked settled clergy in 1740, arguing that an unconverted minister was unfit to guide a congregation.","New Lights: supporters of the revival, who judged a minister by his message rather than his office.","Old Lights: opponents, led by Charles Chauncy of Boston, who thought the revival extravagant and short-lived.","Division: the Presbyterians split in 1741 over the revival.","New colleges: New Light supporters founded the College of New Jersey, later Princeton, in 1746.","Reach: itinerant preachers reached the poor, servants and enslaved people whom settled congregations had largely ignored.","Consequence to argue: religion became a personal choice rather than an inherited authority, and colonists in different provinces shared one experience for the first time."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Enlightenment in the colonies and Benjamin Franklin","items":["Born Boston, 1706; settled in Philadelphia as a printer.","The Junto: a discussion club he founded in 1727 for tradesmen and shopkeepers.","The Pennsylvania Gazette: bought by Franklin in 1729 and made the best-known paper in the colonies.","The Library Company of Philadelphia, 1731: the first subscription library in the colonies.","Poor Richard's Almanack: published every year from 1732 to 1758.","The American Philosophical Society, 1743: founded to promote useful knowledge.","The Academy of Philadelphia: opened in 1751 with a practical, non-denominational curriculum; later the University of Pennsylvania.","The Pennsylvania Hospital, 1751: founded with Dr Thomas Bond.","Electricity: the kite experiment of 1752 and the lightning rod made him famous across Europe.","'Join, or Die': his woodcut of a divided snake, printed in the Pennsylvania Gazette on 9 May 1754.","Newspapers: English-language colonial papers rose from eleven in 1754 to nineteen by 1760, and about twenty-four weeklies by the mid-1760s.","John Peter Zenger: acquitted in New York in August 1735 after his paper attacked the governor, which encouraged printers to criticise officials.","Franklin in politics: joint deputy postmaster general for North America from 1753, Pennsylvania's agent in London from 1757, and leader of the delegation that turned back the Paxton Boys in February 1764."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: King George's War (1744-48) and the road to the Ohio","items":["Dates: 1744-48, the North American part of the War of the Austrian Succession.","Louisbourg: the French fortress on Cape Breton guarding the St Lawrence, taken in June 1745.","Commanders: William Pepperrell led the New England volunteers; Commodore Peter Warren commanded the escorting squadron.","Frontier raids: French and allied war parties burned Saratoga in New York in November 1745 and struck Grand Pre in Nova Scotia in 1747.","The Iroquois Confederacy: held to a policy of neutrality between the empires from 1701, although Mohawk warriors fought for the British.","The Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle, 18 October 1748: returned Louisbourg to France in exchange for Madras in India, to New England's fury.","Halifax: founded in Nova Scotia on 21 June 1749 as a British counterweight to a French Louisbourg.","The Ohio Company of Virginia: granted 200,000 acres on the upper Ohio in 1749.","French forts: Presque Isle, Le Boeuf and Venango built in 1753, and Fort Duquesne at the forks of the Ohio in 1754.","The Covenant Chain: declared broken by the Mohawk leader Theyanoguin in June 1753, and repaired at the Albany Congress of 1754.","Consequence to argue: nothing in the interior was settled, and colonists learned that their victories could be traded away in Europe."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the French and Indian War (1754-63) and Wolfe in Canada","items":["1753: Governor Dinwiddie of Virginia sent George Washington west to warn the French off the Ohio.","1754: Washington's men killed a French officer at Jumonville Glen on 28 May; Washington surrendered Fort Necessity on 3 July.","Albany Congress, 19 June to 11 July 1754: delegates from seven colonies met the Iroquois and repaired the Covenant Chain; the Congress itself adopted Franklin's Plan of Union on 10 July 1754, and it was the colonial assemblies and London that rejected it.","9 July 1755: Major General Edward Braddock was ambushed near the Monongahela and mortally wounded; most of his column of about 1,300 men was killed or wounded.","1756: Britain and France formally declared war; 1757: the French took Fort William Henry.","From 1757: William Pitt directed the war, sending regular regiments and paying subsidies to colonial assemblies.","1758: Louisbourg fell in July, Fort Frontenac in August, and the French blew up Fort Duquesne in November; the British built Fort Pitt on the site.","1759: Fort Niagara and Ticonderoga fell; Wolfe besieged and bombarded Quebec from June.","The night of 12 September 1759: about 4,000 men landed at the Anse au Foulon and climbed the cliff to the Plains of Abraham.","13 September 1759: the battle lasted well under an hour; Wolfe was killed on the field and Montcalm died of his wounds the next day.","18 September 1759: Quebec surrendered. 8 September 1760: Montreal surrendered, ending French rule in Canada.","Why Britain won, to argue: sea power, Pitt's money and the subsidies that let colonial assemblies raise men, against a Canada that could not be supplied once the St Lawrence was closed."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Treaty of Paris (1763), the Proclamation Act (1763) and the Sugar Act (1764)","items":["Treaty of Paris, signed 10 February 1763: France gave up Canada and all claims east of the Mississippi except New Orleans.","Spain gave Florida to Britain; France had already passed Louisiana west of the Mississippi to Spain in 1762.","Britain returned Guadeloupe and Martinique to France and Havana to Spain; France kept Saint-Pierre and Miquelon and fishing rights off Newfoundland.","The royal proclamation of 7 October 1763, called the Proclamation Act by Edexcel: no land grants beyond the Appalachians.","It reserved the interior for Indigenous peoples, allowed only the Crown to buy their land, and licensed traders entering it.","It created the new colonies of Quebec, East Florida, West Florida and Grenada.","The debt: about 75 million pounds in 1756, about 133 million in 1763; about 10,000 regulars were to stay in America.","The Sugar Act: royal assent 5 April 1764, in force 29 September 1764.","It cut the molasses duty from the sixpence a gallon of the Molasses Act of 1733 to threepence, but with the declared aim of collecting it.","It also taxed foreign refined sugar, wine, coffee and indigo, banned foreign rum, and sent smuggling cases to vice-admiralty courts sitting without a jury, with a new court at Halifax.","The Currency Act of April 1764 banned colonial paper money as legal tender, in the middle of a post-war trade depression.","Opposition: Boston's town meeting of May 1764 denied Parliament's right to tax the unrepresented; James Otis published 'The Rights of the British Colonies Asserted and Proved' in 1764; merchants gave up some British luxuries and several assemblies petitioned.","Other grievances from the war: the royal order of 1754 placing provincial officers below regulars of the same grade, quartering disputes, and the writs of assistance argued against by Otis in Boston in February 1761."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Pontiac's Rebellion (1763-64) and the Paxton Boys","items":["Cause: the Treaty of Paris removed the French, so Indigenous nations could no longer play two empires against each other.","Sir Jeffery Amherst, the British commander-in-chief, cut back the gifts of powder, shot and provisions that had maintained alliances.","Neolin, a Lenape prophet, preached rejection of European trade goods and alcohol and a return to older ways.","Pontiac, an Odawa leader, failed to seize Fort Detroit by surprise on 7 May 1763 and besieged it from 9 May until 31 October.","Within weeks eight British posts west of the Appalachians had been captured or abandoned.","Colonel Henry Bouquet fought at Bushy Run on 5 and 6 August 1763 and relieved Fort Pitt, where the garrison had already given besieging envoys blankets from its smallpox hospital.","Amherst was recalled at the end of 1763 and replaced as commander-in-chief by Thomas Gage; gift-giving was restored.","Bouquet marched to the forks of the Muskingum in the autumn of 1764, ending the fighting in the Ohio country and recovering captives; Pontiac made peace at Oswego in July 1766.","The Paxton Boys: frontiersmen from Paxton in Lancaster County, Pennsylvania, mostly of Scots-Irish descent.","14 December 1763: about fifty of them killed six Conestoga at Conestoga Town.","27 December 1763: they broke into the Lancaster workhouse and killed the fourteen survivors sheltering there.","Early 1764: several hundred marched on Philadelphia; in February a delegation led by Benjamin Franklin met them at Germantown and persuaded them to go home, and Franklin denounced the killings in a pamphlet that year.","Nobody was ever prosecuted for the killings.","Consequence to argue: on the frontier, British and provincial authority rested on nothing that either settlers or Indigenous nations had reason to respect."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan - Paper 2 booklet P Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the struggle for control of the Ohio country in the years 1753-54' (8 marks)","items":["The question as printed: 'Write a narrative account analysing the struggle for control of the Ohio country in the years 1753-54.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the French forts built on the upper Ohio (1753); the surrender of Fort Necessity (1754). You must also use information of your own.","Decode it before writing. 'Narrative account' means a sequence that leads somewhere, so the account must begin and end at definite points; 'analysing' means the join between each episode and the next has to be explained rather than assumed; and the dates fix the boundaries, so the French building programme of 1753 is the earliest thing you may use and the end of 1754 the latest, except in a closing sentence.","The cap that decides the level: no access to Level 3 at all for an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content. Decide first which episodes are your own material - the Ohio Company's grant, Washington's winter journey, the seizure of the forks, Jumonville Glen, the decision taken in London - and make sure those are the ones you develop.","Episode 1, two claims pressed at once, 1749 to 1753: the Ohio Company of Virginia was granted 200,000 acres on the upper Ohio in 1749, while in 1753 the French built Presque Isle, Le Boeuf and Venango to link Canada with Louisiana.","Link sentence: because each side was now improving a claim the other denied, the question stopped being who owned the country on paper and became who had men and forts on the ground.","Episode 2, the warning, the winter of 1753: Governor Dinwiddie sent the young George Washington north with a letter ordering the French off, and Jacques Legardeur de Saint-Pierre received him at Fort Le Boeuf in December and refused to go.","Link sentence: since a courteous refusal left Virginia with nothing but the option of building first, the race for the forks of the Ohio began that winter.","Episode 3, the forks, April 1754 (your own material): a French force came down from Canada, took the small post the Ohio Company's men were building at the forks on 17 April 1754 and put Fort Duquesne on the site.","Link sentence: because the French now held the one place that commanded the river, Washington's small column was too late and too weak to take it, and the campaign became a fight in the woods instead.","Episode 4, the two fights, May to July 1754: Washington and the Mingo leader Tanacharison attacked a French party at Jumonville Glen on 28 May, and the French answer forced Washington to surrender Fort Necessity on 3 July.","Episode 5, the decision in London, the autumn of 1754 (your own material): ministers concluded that provincial troops could not clear the Ohio, and in November 1754 Major General Edward Braddock was commissioned to command in North America and two regular regiments were ordered across the Atlantic.","The closing sentence must say what the sequence amounted to rather than repeat it: in eighteen months a quarrel between a land company and a fort-building programme had become a confrontation between two empires, to be settled by regular armies on ground neither of them governed.","Timing and shape: about 13 minutes - two to choose four or five episodes and mark which are your own material, ten to write, one to check that every join carries a sentence of explanation. Use the terms an examiner looks for: claim, the forks of the Ohio, garrison, provincial troops, regulars, frontier, Indigenous nations."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:19:07Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"Re-judged after the fix pass: all four faults from the first verdict are genuinely fixed and independently re-verified - the peacetime establishment is now 'in early 1763 Lord Bute's ministry' in all three places (lesson.idea[6], room.questions[7].sol, cards[11]) with the debt figures in the right order, extras[5] now says the Congress itself adopted the Albany Plan on 10 July 1754 and the assemblies and London rejected it, 'well under an hour' is consistent in all five places, and every worked example, both model paragraphs and the plan have been moved onto questions the bank does not set (writs of assistance 1761; the Currency Act, assent 19 April 1764 and in force 1 September 1764; the war of 1755-57 with Braddock 9 July 1755, the declaration of 17 May 1756, Oswego 14 August 1756 and Fort William Henry 9 August 1757; the importance of Pitt; the importance of the Iroquois Confederacy; and the Ohio country 1753-54 with the grant of 1749, Saint-Pierre at Fort Le Boeuf in December 1753, the forks on 17 April 1754, Jumonville Glen on 28 May, Fort Necessity on 3 July and Braddock's appointment in November 1754), so nothing in the bank arrives pre-answered; cards[10] is now the open card with three semicolon-separated provisions in 45 words. Publishable: fix only the Lancaster month."}}},"P2-3":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"P2-3","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 3, the loss of an empire, 1765-83, is the part of British America that booklet P questions again and again, because it is a short run of years in which one thing visibly caused the next: a tax, a boycott, a riot, a punishment, a congress, a war, an alliance, a surrender and a treaty. The consequence questions live on its single events, the narrative account lives on its chains of episodes, and the importance questions live on the people and developments whose effects reach forward for years - Washington, the Declaration, the French alliance, the Peace of Paris. If you can say, with names and dates, why colonists objected to the Stamp Act rather than merely disliking it, why punishing Boston brought twelve colonies to Philadelphia, why Saratoga brought France in, why Yorktown ended Parliament's patience rather than the fighting, and what the peace did to Loyalists, to Indigenous nations and to enslaved people, you can answer almost anything this key topic sets. This room teaches that sequence as an argument you can make, and it trains the three moves the period study rewards: explaining a consequence instead of retelling an event, writing a narrative whose episodes are linked rather than listed, and judging what difference something made to what came after it.","idea":[{"code":"P2-3 — 1 American colonist relations with Britain: Opposition to British policies","h":"From the Stamp Act to the Boston Massacre: how opposition got its machinery, 1765-70","t":"George Grenville's Stamp Act, passed in March 1765 and in force from 1 November, taxed newspapers, pamphlets, legal documents, licences and playing cards in the colonies to help pay for the army left in America after the Seven Years' War. The objection was constitutional, not financial: colonists elected nobody to Parliament, so Parliament could not lay an internal tax on them. Nine colonies sent delegates to the Stamp Act Congress in New York in October 1765; merchants agreed to stop importing British goods; and the Sons of Liberty, formed that year in Boston and New York, enforced the boycott by crowd action, frightening stamp distributors such as Andrew Oliver into resigning. Parliament repealed the Act in March 1766 but passed the Declaratory Act the same day, claiming the right to legislate for the colonies in all cases whatsoever. The Townshend duties of 1767 on glass, lead, paint, paper and tea revived the quarrel and brought troops to Boston in 1768; on 5 March 1770 a picket fired into a crowd in King Street, killing five, among them Crispus Attucks. Paul Revere's engraving made it the Boston Massacre, although John Adams defended the soldiers and only two of the eight were convicted, of manslaughter. A top-level answer treats 1765-70 not as a list of taxes but as the years in which opposition acquired machinery - congresses, boycotts, committees, a printing trade and a vocabulary of tyranny - so that a later crisis could be organised in days rather than months."},{"code":"P2-3 — 1 American colonist relations with Britain: British and American relations","h":"Tea and punishment: the Boston Tea Party and the Intolerable Acts, 1773-74","t":"Lord North repealed the Townshend duties in 1770 except the one on tea, which he kept to assert the principle, and for three years the quarrel cooled. The Tea Act of May 1773 reopened it: by letting the East India Company ship tea direct to America and undercut smuggled Dutch tea, it looked like a bribe to make colonists swallow the tax. On the night of 16 December 1773 men of the Sons of Liberty, thinly disguised as Mohawks, boarded three ships at Griffin's Wharf and threw 342 chests of tea into Boston harbour. Parliament answered in 1774 with the Coercive Acts, known in America as the Intolerable Acts: the Boston Port Act closed the harbour from 1 June until the tea was paid for; the Massachusetts Government Act made the colony's council appointed rather than elected and banned town meetings held without the governor's permission; the Administration of Justice Act allowed accused officials to be tried in Britain; and a new Quartering Act made it easier to billet troops. General Thomas Gage arrived as military governor in May 1774. The Quebec Act of the same year, which extended Quebec to the Ohio, was lumped in with them. A top-level answer explains why punishing one town united twelve colonies: the Acts attacked a charter and the right of self-government, so every colony could see the precedent being set."},{"code":"P2-3 — 1 American colonist relations with Britain: The significance of the First and Second Continental Congresses","h":"The Continental Congresses, 1774-75: the colonies build a government before they have a country","t":"The First Continental Congress met at Philadelphia from 5 September to 26 October 1774, with fifty-six delegates from twelve colonies - Georgia stayed away. It did not ask for independence. It issued a Declaration and Resolves claiming the rights of Englishmen, petitioned the King, and agreed the Continental Association: no British imports from 1 December 1774, no exports to Britain from the following September, enforced by an elected committee in every town with power to inspect merchants and publish offenders. It also agreed to meet again in May 1775. By then Gage had sent troops to seize militia stores and been met at Lexington and Concord on 19 April 1775, so the Second Continental Congress, meeting from 10 May 1775, found itself running a war. It adopted the militia besieging Boston as a Continental Army on 14 June and appointed George Washington to command it on 15 June 1775; it printed paper money, appointed agents abroad, sent the Olive Branch Petition in July 1775 and was answered by the King's Proclamation of Rebellion on 23 August; and in July 1776 it voted independence. A top-level answer says what the Congresses were rather than what they did: the colonies' first common institutions, giving the rebellion a government, an army and a foreign policy before it had a state."},{"code":"P2-3 — 2 The War of Independence 1775-83: The influence of Thomas Paine's 'Common Sense'","h":"Making the case: 'Common Sense', Jefferson and the Declaration of Independence, 1776","t":"Until 1776 most colonists still said they were defending English liberties inside the empire. Thomas Paine, an Englishman who had landed at Philadelphia on 30 November 1774, published 'Common Sense' anonymously thirteen months later, on 10 January 1776; the first printing sold out in days and well over a hundred thousand copies circulated that year. Its force lay in attacking monarchy itself rather than bad ministers, in ridiculing the idea that a small island should govern a continent, and in arguing that American trade would be freer outside the empire - written in plain language for readers who were not lawyers. The Prohibitory Act of December 1775, which put the colonies outside the King's protection, made his case for him. Congress voted Richard Henry Lee's resolution of independence on 2 July 1776 and approved the Declaration on 4 July. Thomas Jefferson of Virginia, then thirty-three, wrote the draft for a committee of five that also included Franklin and John Adams; Congress cut about a quarter of it, including the passage blaming the King for the slave trade. The Declaration asserted that all men are created equal and that governments draw their just powers from the consent of the governed, then listed the King's offences. A top-level answer treats 1776 as the moment the war changed its object: from redress inside the empire to sovereignty outside it, which made foreign alliances possible and made neutrality impossible."},{"code":"P2-3 — 2 The War of Independence 1775-83: Key American victories","h":"Why the Americans won: Washington, Saratoga, Yorktown, British mistakes and foreign help","t":"Washington took command at Cambridge on 3 July 1775 and lost more battles than he won, but his purpose was to keep an army in existence, since Britain could only win by destroying it. Driven out of New York in 1776, he crossed the Delaware on the night of 25-26 December and took about 900 Hessians at Trenton, then beat a British force at Princeton on 3 January 1777, saving enlistments that were about to expire. In 1777 Burgoyne marched south from Canada to cut off New England while Howe took his own army to Philadelphia instead, so Burgoyne fought alone; beaten at Freeman's Farm and Bemis Heights, he surrendered his whole army - about six thousand men - to Gates at Saratoga on 17 October 1777, the first British army to surrender in this war. France signed treaties of commerce and alliance on 6 February 1778, Spain joined France in June 1779 and the Dutch followed in 1780. At Valley Forge, from December 1777, about 2,000 men died of disease while von Steuben, who reached the camp on 23 February 1778, began drilling a model company on 19 March and the rest of the army in the days that followed. Britain then turned south: Charleston fell on 12 May 1780 with over 5,000 prisoners, but Cowpens in January 1781 and Guilford Courthouse in March wore Cornwallis down until he moved to Yorktown, where de Grasse's fleet beat the Royal Navy off the Chesapeake on 5 September 1781 and cut him off; he surrendered about 7,000 men on 19 October 1781. A top-level answer separates three things: why the Americans did not lose, why the British could not win, and why the Americans finally won."},{"code":"P2-3 — 2 The War of Independence 1775-83: The Peace of Paris (1783)","h":"Ending it: Franklin, the talks in Paris and the peace of 1783","t":"Yorktown did not end the fighting; it ended Parliament's willingness to pay for it. Lord North's ministry fell in March 1782 and its successors opened talks. Benjamin Franklin, American commissioner in France from December 1776 and sole minister from September 1778 and the man who had secured the alliance of 1778, led the negotiation in Paris with John Jay and John Adams, later joined by Henry Laurens. They dealt directly with Britain's agent Richard Oswald and signed preliminary articles on 30 November 1782 without consulting France, which the alliance forbade but which secured better terms. The definitive treaty was signed in Paris on 3 September 1783 by Franklin, Adams and Jay for the United States and David Hartley for Britain. Britain recognised the thirteen states as free, sovereign and independent; the boundary ran west to the Mississippi, north to the Great Lakes and south to the Floridas, which Britain ceded to Spain by the separate Anglo-Spanish treaty signed at Versailles the same day; Americans kept fishing rights off Newfoundland; pre-war debts to British creditors were to be honoured; and Congress would merely recommend that the states restore confiscated Loyalist property. Britain kept Canada and evacuated New York on 25 November 1783. A top-level answer notices what the treaty settled generously, what it settled vaguely and whom it left out altogether, because the vagueness and the omissions are where its consequences came from."},{"code":"P2-3 — 3 Consequences of the war in America: The significance of American independence for slavery","h":"Liberty and slavery: what independence meant for enslaved Americans","t":"Roughly a fifth of the people in the thirteen colonies were enslaved, most of them in the South, and the war itself shook that system before independence did. Lord Dunmore, royal governor of Virginia, proclaimed on 7 November 1775 that enslaved men belonging to rebels would be free if they joined the King's forces, and several hundred formed his Ethiopian Regiment; Clinton's Philipsburg Proclamation of 1779 widened the offer, and tens of thousands escaped during the war. About 5,000 Black men also served in the American forces, some gaining freedom by it, and Rhode Island raised a regiment of them in 1778. Independence then moved the decision from London to thirteen separate state governments, which answered it in opposite ways. In the North, where slavery was economically marginal, the Declaration's language became usable in courts and legislatures: Pennsylvania passed a gradual abolition act in 1780 and Massachusetts courts ended it in 1783. In the South the same independence worked the other way, since planters now controlled their own governments and no outside power could interfere; slavery spread westwards, and the Constitution of 1787 counted enslaved people as three fifths of a person for representation and protected the slave trade until 1808. A top-level answer argues both halves at once: independence gave abolition an argument in one half of the country and gave slavery security in the other."},{"code":"P2-3 — 3 Consequences of the war in America: The consequences of the war for Indigenous peoples","h":"The war and Indigenous peoples: allies who were not at the table","t":"Most Indigenous nations judged the colonists the greater threat, because it was colonists who wanted the land; the Proclamation of 1763 had tried to bar settlement west of the Appalachians and was bitterly resented in America. The Iroquois Confederacy, which had held a common policy for generations, broke apart over the war: the Mohawk under Joseph Brant, together with the Seneca, Cayuga and Onondaga, fought for the Crown, while the Oneida and Tuscarora sided with the Americans. In 1779 Washington sent Sullivan and Clinton to destroy Iroquois country - more than forty towns were burned with their crops and orchards, and thousands of refugees fled to Fort Niagara, where many died in the winter that followed. In the Ohio country the Shawnee and their neighbours fought settlers throughout the war. The Peace of Paris named no Indigenous nation and none was represented at it: Britain simply handed the United States its claim to land it did not occupy and its allies had not lost. The new republic treated that as conquest, demanding cessions at treaties such as Fort Stanwix in 1784, while Brant's Mohawks were resettled on the Grand River the same year. A top-level answer explains the deeper loss: Indigenous nations had protected themselves by playing rival empires against each other, and after 1783 east of the Mississippi there was only one."},{"code":"P2-3 — 3 Consequences of the war in America: The impact of the war on Loyalists","h":"The losers who were not the enemy: Loyalists and their resettlement","t":"Perhaps a fifth of white colonists stayed loyal to the Crown, and they were not a single class: royal officials and Anglican clergy, recent immigrants, backcountry farmers with grievances against coastal elites, and tenants whose landlords were rebels. Loyalist regiments such as Butler's Rangers and the Queen's Rangers fought throughout the war, and the southern strategy of 1778-81 rested on the belief that they would rise in numbers. Against them the revolutionary states used oaths, double taxation, banishment and confiscation laws, and the civil war in the Carolinas was savage on both sides. The peace of 1783 protected them only by asking Congress to recommend that the states restore property, which most states refused to do. About 60,000 left: more than 30,000 to Nova Scotia, where Shelburne was founded and where the influx led to New Brunswick being made a separate province in 1784, and others up the St Lawrence and into the Niagara peninsula, the settlements from which Upper Canada was created in 1791. Some 3,000 Black Loyalists were listed in the Book of Negroes as they left New York in 1783 and settled at Birchtown and nearby; denied the land they had been promised, nearly 1,200 of them sailed in 1792 to found Freetown in Sierra Leone. In Britain a claims commission set up in 1783 heard more than 3,000 claims and awarded about £3 million, far less than was claimed. A top-level answer uses the Loyalists to show that the Revolution was also a civil war, and that its consequences built another country to the north."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1(a): turning the Townshend duties into one explained consequence","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(a): 'Explain one consequence of the Townshend duties of 1767.' [4 marks]. This part asks for one consequence, analysed and supported, not for an account of the duties. The information you are working from: Parliament passed the Townshend Revenue Act in June 1767, taxing glass, lead, paint, paper and tea brought into the colonies, and meant the money to pay royal governors and judges so that colonial assemblies could not; the Massachusetts House sent a circular letter to the other colonies in February 1768 asking them to join the objection, and refused an order from London to withdraw it; the seizure of John Hancock's sloop Liberty in June 1768 was followed by a riot and the customs commissioners took refuge at Castle William; four regiments were ordered to Boston and the first troops landed on 1 October 1768; non-importation agreements were revived in Boston, New York and Philadelphia; Lord North repealed the duties except the one on tea in 1770.","steps":["Fix the window in which the consequence must be true: the duties were voted in 1767, collected from 1768 and gone except on tea by 1770, so anything you claim has to happen inside those years or follow from them.","Reject the consequences that are only the protest described again. Another boycott and more angry pamphlets are what colonists always did; ask instead what the duties made the British government do.","Name it as a change of state: before 1768 no regiments were stationed in Boston, and afterwards the town had a garrison living among its people in peacetime.","Support it with the precise detail: the circular letter of February 1768 and the refusal to withdraw it, the Liberty riot that June which drove the customs commissioners to Castle William, four regiments ordered out, and the first troops landing on 1 October 1768.","Now make the analytical link the grid rewards: because the duties were designed to pay governors and judges out of Crown revenue, resistance to them looked to ministers like a refusal of authority itself rather than a dispute about money, so the answer was soldiers - and soldiers policing a hostile port made a collision in the streets likely."],"cues":["Between which years does the life of these duties run?","Which of the obvious answers is just the protest described again?","What was true of Boston at the end of 1768 that had not been true at the start of 1767?","Which pieces of the information prove that the decision was taken, and when?","Why did a quarrel about customs duties end with regiments rather than with a repeal?"]},{"title":"Question 1(b): one consequence of the Prohibitory Act, without retelling the statute","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(b): 'Explain one consequence of the Prohibitory Act of December 1775.' [4 marks]. The information you are working from: the Act received the royal assent on 22 December 1775; it forbade all trade with the thirteen colonies, ordered a blockade of their coast, and made colonial ships and their cargoes lawful prize as though they belonged to an open enemy; it therefore put the colonies outside the King's protection; colonial trade had until then been carried on inside the empire under the Navigation Acts; Congress answered on 6 April 1776 by opening every American port to the ships of all nations except Britain; Congress voted independence on 2 July 1776.","steps":["Ask what the Act took away rather than what it said, because a consequence usually begins with a loss that forces somebody to choose.","State the decision as the consequence rather than as the next event in a story: on 6 April 1776 Congress threw open American ports to the ships of every nation except Britain.","Support it with the detail that makes the link tight: the assent came on 22 December 1775, the Act made colonial ships lawful prize as though they were an enemy's, and the reply followed within four months.","Explain the mechanism: colonies whose shipping could be taken as enemy property had no lawful trade left inside the empire, so they had either to stop trading or to trade as a separate country with everybody else.","Close on scale, since the strongest consequences widen: opening the ports set aside the Navigation Acts that had defined the empire's commerce, and made trade with France and the Dutch a practical question three months before independence was voted."],"cues":["What protection did colonists lose, and what did losing it leave them free to do?","What is the single decision you will name as the consequence?","Which two facts show the reply was caused by the Act and not merely later than it?","Why could the colonies not simply carry on trading as before?","What did opening the ports mean for the rules the empire had been run by?"]},{"title":"Question 2: building a linked sequence for the years 1766 to 1770","setup":"Booklet P, Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing how colonial opposition to British taxation became organised in the years 1766-70.' [8 marks]. Two stimulus points are printed - the Declaratory Act (1766) and the Massachusetts Circular Letter (1768) - followed by 'You must also use information of your own.' The events available to you: Parliament repealed the Stamp Act on 18 March 1766 and passed the Declaratory Act the same day, claiming the right to bind the colonies in all cases whatsoever; the Townshend Revenue Act of 1767 taxed glass, lead, paint, paper and tea and was meant to pay royal governors and judges out of that money; the Massachusetts House approved a circular letter to the other assemblies on 11 February 1768, and on 30 June 1768 voted 92 to 17 against London's order to rescind it, after which Governor Bernard dissolved the assembly; about sixty Boston merchants signed a non-importation agreement on 1 August 1768, New York merchants revived theirs the same year and a majority of Philadelphia's merchants joined on 10 March 1769; British exporters pressed for relief; Lord North moved the repeal of the duties on 5 March 1770 and it received the royal assent on 12 April 1770, the duty on tea alone being kept.","steps":["Pick the three or four episodes that show opposition acquiring machinery rather than the taxes that provoked it: the claim of 1766, the letter of 1768, the merchants' boycott, and the partial repeal of 12 April 1770.","Write the first link as a cause, not a date: because the Declaratory Act claimed the right to bind the colonies in all cases whatsoever on the very day the Stamp Act went, repeal taught colonists that a tax could be beaten while the principle behind it survived, so the next one had to be met with something more durable than a crowd.","Write the second link as the moment a local quarrel became a common one: the circular letter of 11 February 1768 invited every assembly to answer together, and London's order to rescind it turned a piece of correspondence into a test of obedience, which is why the House refused by 92 votes to 17 on 30 June 1768 and why other assemblies took the letter up rather than let it drop.","Write the third link as a change of weapon: with assemblies dissolved there was no lawful body left to petition, so the pressure moved to trade - about sixty merchants signing in Boston on 1 August 1768, New York the same year, Philadelphia on 10 March 1769 - and the people who could now hurt Parliament were British exporters rather than colonial petitioners.","Finish on the outcome and say what the sequence amounted to: North moved repeal on 5 March 1770 and the duties went on 12 April except the one on tea, so the colonies came out of these years with correspondence between assemblies, committees in the ports and a boycott they had proved they could impose, while Britain kept a principle it could not collect."],"cues":["Which episodes show opposition getting organised, and which are only taxes?","What did colonists learn from the way the Stamp Act was repealed?","How did an order from London turn one colony's letter into everybody's business?","Once the assemblies had been dissolved, who was left to apply pressure, and to whom?","What did the colonies have by 1770 that they had not had in 1766?"]},{"title":"Question 3: explaining the importance of the southern strategy rather than describing it","setup":"Booklet P, Question 3 offers three bullets and you answer two; each is marked out of 8. The bullet here: 'The importance of the British southern strategy for the outcome of the war in the years 1778-81.' [8 marks]. No stimulus points are printed. The information you are working from: Britain turned south in the belief that Loyalists were numerous there and would hold ground once regulars had cleared it; Savannah was taken in December 1778 and Charleston surrendered on 12 May 1780 with over 5,000 prisoners, the worst American defeat of the war; Cornwallis destroyed Gates's army at Camden in August 1780; a Loyalist force was destroyed at King's Mountain in October 1780; Morgan destroyed Tarleton's force at Cowpens on 17 January 1781; Cornwallis held the field at Guilford Courthouse on 15 March 1781 but lost about a quarter of his army; he then moved into Virginia and to Yorktown, where he surrendered about 7,000 men on 19 October 1781.","steps":["Decide the criterion before you write: importance here means what difference the strategy made to how the war ended, so the answer has to test the assumption the strategy rested on rather than list its battles.","Start with what it actually achieved, because an answer that only says it failed is not analysis: Savannah in December 1778, Charleston on 12 May 1780 with over 5,000 prisoners, and Camden that August left Britain holding the southern ports.","Now test the assumption: the plan needed Loyalists to hold the country behind the army, and the destruction of a Loyalist force at King's Mountain in October 1780 turned the backcountry, so what regulars took could not be kept.","Weigh the cost of the victories that followed: Cowpens on 17 January 1781 destroyed Tarleton's force, and at Guilford Courthouse on 15 March Cornwallis held the field but lost about a quarter of his army, so he marched into Virginia looking for a decision he had not found in the Carolinas.","Judge rather than list: the strategy's importance is not that it lost the South but that it carried Britain's main field army away from the Carolinas to a coast where sea power would settle the matter, and Yorktown is where that ended - that is the difference the question asks you to measure."],"cues":["What difference are you measuring, and against what?","What did the strategy achieve before anything went wrong with it?","Which belief was the whole plan built on, and what tested it?","What did Cornwallis have left after his own victories?","Where did the strategy finally put the British army, and why did that matter?"]},{"title":"Question 3: explaining the importance of Franklin at the peace rather than describing the treaty","setup":"Booklet P, Question 3, one bullet marked out of 8: 'The importance of Benjamin Franklin for the making of the Peace of Paris (1783).' [8 marks]. No stimulus points are printed. The information you are working from: Franklin went to France in December 1776, was appointed minister plenipotentiary in September 1778 and was the one American who had dealt with the French court since the alliance; when North's ministry fell in March 1782 the new ministry sent Richard Oswald to Paris, and Franklin opened the talks alone that April, handing Oswald a paper that divided terms into those that were necessary - independence, the withdrawal of British troops, and the Newfoundland fisheries - and those that were merely advisable, among them the cession of Canada; Jay and Adams joined him and formal negotiation began on 27 September 1782; Congress had instructed the commissioners in June 1781 to undertake nothing without French knowledge and concurrence, yet the preliminary articles were signed with Oswald on 30 November 1782 without consulting Vergennes; Franklin still obtained a further French loan of six million livres in December 1782; he refused to have Loyalist compensation written into the treaty and offered counterclaims for American property destroyed by British forces; the definitive treaty was signed in Paris on 3 September 1783.","steps":["Set the criterion: importance for a person is measured by what the outcome would have looked like without them, so the answer has to test Franklin against the terms actually obtained rather than list the offices he held.","Start with what he brought to the table, because it explains the rest: six years at the French court meant the one negotiator who could take a British approach seriously and keep the ally paying at the same time.","Show him setting the agenda rather than answering it: the paper he gave Oswald in April 1782 separated the terms that had to be had - independence, withdrawal, the Mississippi boundary, the fisheries - from those only worth asking for, and nearly all of the first list survived into the articles of 30 November 1782.","Weigh the risk and what it bought: Congress had ordered the commissioners to do nothing without French concurrence, and signing the preliminaries without telling Vergennes broke that order - yet the alliance held well enough for Franklin to get a loan of six million livres out of it that December.","Judge rather than list: Britain still held New York when the articles were signed, and Franklin's line on Loyalist claims kept compensation out of the treaty as an obligation, so the peace gave the new state more than the fighting on the ground had settled - and that gap is the difference the question asks you to measure."],"cues":["What difference are you measuring, and against what?","What did Franklin have that no other American commissioner had?","Who set the terms of the discussion in the spring of 1782?","What did he risk by signing without the French, and what did he keep anyway?","How did the treaty compare with what the armies had actually settled?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Why did colonists object to the Stamp Act when the sums involved were small?","a":"Because it was an internal tax laid by a Parliament in which they elected nobody, so the objection was to the right claimed rather than to the cost."},{"q":"Why did the Coercive Acts of 1774 unite colonies that had nothing to do with the tea?","a":"Because they punished a colony by suspending its elected council and its town meetings, which set a precedent that any colony's charter could be taken away."},{"q":"What made Saratoga more important than any other American victory before 1781?","a":"It forced a whole British army to surrender, which convinced France that the Americans could win and brought the treaties of alliance in February 1778."},{"q":"Why was Yorktown decisive when Britain still held New York, Charleston and Savannah?","a":"Because it destroyed Parliament's willingness to keep paying for the war: North's ministry fell in March 1782 and peace talks opened."},{"q":"Whose interests were left out of the Peace of Paris altogether?","a":"Indigenous nations, who were neither named nor represented although the land ceded to the United States was theirs."}]},"room":{"facts":["Booklet P is worth 32 marks and sets three questions: Question 1 in two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each 'Explain one consequence of...' for 4 marks and each on a separate event or development; Question 2, 'Write a narrative account analysing...', 8 marks; and Question 3, 'Explain two of the following: the importance of ... for ...', two chosen parts at 8 marks each, 16 in all.","Only Question 2 prints stimulus points, as two bullets followed by 'You must also use information of your own.' The mark scheme prints one cap: 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' There is no cap at Level 2. Questions 1 and 3 print no bullets and carry no cap.","Question 1 comes in two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each printed as 'Explain one consequence of ...' and each marked on its own two-level grid out of 4 (AO2 2 + AO1 2). Level 2, worth 3 to 4 marks, needs features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific information added in support. A description of the event, however accurate, stays in Level 1.","Question 2 is marked out of 8 on a three-level grid (AO2 4 + AO1 4): Level 3, worth 6 to 8, needs a clear sequence of events leading to an outcome, with the linkage between them analysed, not a list of dated paragraphs.","Question 3 is marked twice on a three-level grid out of 8 (AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two parts). Level 3 needs analysis of importance in a coherent line of reasoning - what difference the event, person or development made to later situations - rather than an account of what happened.","Timing: Paper 2 lasts 1 hour 50 minutes for 64 marks, about 55 minutes a booklet, so booklet P's 32 marks get roughly a minute a mark with planning on top - about 12 minutes on Question 1's two parts, 13 on Question 2 and 14 on each part of Question 3. There are no sources on this paper and no marks for spelling, punctuation and grammar."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Stamp Act of 1765. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2, marked on the 4-mark consequence grid; Level 2 (3-4) requires features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific supporting information. Credit the Stamp Act Congress of October 1765 and the first colony-wide non-importation agreements, the founding and crowd action of the Sons of Liberty, or the repeal of March 1766 paired with the Declaratory Act.","sol":"One consequence was that the colonies acted together for the first time. Nine of them sent delegates to the Stamp Act Congress in New York in October 1765, which denied that Parliament could tax people who elected nobody to it, and merchants in different colonies agreed to stop importing British goods until the Act was repealed. Because the tax fell on every colony at once, and fell on newspapers and legal documents that printers, lawyers and merchants handled daily, the people best able to organise protest were the people it hurt, so the boycotts and committees built in 1765 were available for every later quarrel.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["A consequence has to be something that was true afterwards because of the Act, so ask what existed at the end of 1765 that had not existed at the start.","Anger and riots are the protest itself; look for what the protest left behind when the tax was gone.","Three moves: name the consequence, give the precise detail that proves it happened, then explain why this particular tax produced it."],"codes":["P2-3 — 1 American colonist relations with Britain: Opposition to British policies"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of the Boston Tea Party of December 1773. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the Coercive Acts of 1774 and their terms, the arrival of Gage as military governor, the collapse of moderate opinion in Britain, or the aid sent to Boston by other colonies; the consequence must be analysed, not narrated.","sol":"One consequence was the Coercive Acts of 1774, which Americans called the Intolerable Acts. Because 342 chests of tea had been destroyed in public by men nobody would name, North's government concluded that Massachusetts had to be made an example of: the Boston Port Act closed the harbour from 1 June until the tea was paid for, and the Massachusetts Government Act replaced the elected council with appointed men and banned town meetings held without the governor's leave. Punishing a whole colony for property destroyed by a crowd turned a dispute about a tax on tea into a dispute about charters and self-government.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The strongest consequence here is what Parliament decided to do about it, so look across the Atlantic rather than around Boston.","Notice the problem the government faced: the culprits were disguised and unnamed, so whom could it punish?","Name the consequence, quote two of its precise terms, then explain what those terms attacked."],"codes":["P2-3 — 1 American colonist relations with Britain: British and American relations"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the publication of Thomas Paine's 'Common Sense' in January 1776. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the spread of independence as a respectable public position, the shift of the argument from the ministers to monarchy itself, the pressure on Congress that led to the vote of 2 July 1776, or the hardening of the choice that created open Loyalism.","sol":"One consequence was that independence became sayable in public. Paine's pamphlet appeared anonymously in Philadelphia on 10 January 1776, sold out its first printing within days and circulated in well over a hundred thousand copies that year. Until then most colonists insisted they were defending English rights inside the empire and blamed the King's ministers; Paine attacked hereditary monarchy itself, in plain language written for readers who were not lawyers. Because the case for a republic was now common property rather than a private opinion, Congress could move within six months from petitioning George III to voting Richard Henry Lee's resolution of independence on 2 July 1776.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["A pamphlet changes what people are willing to say before it changes what governments do.","Ask what colonists had been claiming about their quarrel up to the end of 1775, and what Paine made it possible to claim instead.","Name the consequence, support it with how far and how fast the pamphlet travelled, then link it to what Congress did that summer."],"codes":["P2-3 — 2 The War of Independence 1775-83: The influence of Thomas Paine's 'Common Sense'"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of the American victory at Saratoga in October 1777. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the French treaties of 6 February 1778 and the widening of the war to Spain in 1779 and the Dutch in 1780, the failure of the plan to cut off New England, or the conciliatory offers Britain made in 1778. The link between the surrender and the decision must be explained.","sol":"One consequence was the French alliance. Burgoyne surrendered his whole army to Gates on 17 October 1777, the first British army to surrender in this war, and the news reached Paris that winter. France had been sending supplies secretly since 1776 but had refused an open alliance, because no great power wants to enter a war on the losing side. Saratoga answered that doubt, so Louis XVI signed treaties of commerce and alliance with Congress on 6 February 1778. A colonial rebellion thereby became a European war which Britain had to fight at sea as well as in America.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask who else was watching the campaign and what they had been waiting to find out.","France had already been helping in secret, so the consequence is a change in the kind of help, not its beginning.","State the consequence, date it precisely, then explain what the surrender proved that made the decision rational."],"codes":["P2-3 — 2 The War of Independence 1775-83: Key American victories"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Boston Massacre of March 1770. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the Coercive Acts of 1774. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4 on the consequence grid, so each needs one consequence analysed with specific support. In 1(a) credit the propaganda use of the shooting, the withdrawal of the regiments from the town to Castle William, or the trials of late 1770, in which John Adams defended the soldiers and two were convicted of manslaughter, and the annual commemoration of 5 March in Boston from 1771. In 1(b) credit the calling of the First Continental Congress and the Continental Association, the relief sent to Boston, or the arming of the Massachusetts militia.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that the radicals gained a propaganda weapon they used for years. Five colonists were killed when a picket fired in King Street on 5 March 1770, and within weeks Paul Revere's engraving was circulating, showing a line of redcoats firing on an orderly crowd at an officer's order. That was not what the courts found, since John Adams defended the soldiers and only two of the eight were convicted, of manslaughter. Because the printed image travelled further and lasted longer than the verdict, the word 'massacre' fixed the idea of a standing army used against citizens, and Boston commemorated the day every year afterwards. 1(b) One consequence was that twelve colonies made common cause. The Acts closed Boston's port until the tea was paid for, replaced the elected Massachusetts council with appointed men and restricted town meetings. Because the punishment fell on a charter rather than on a crime, other colonies read it as a precedent that could be used against them, and food and money were sent to Boston from as far as South Carolina. Delegates met at Philadelphia in September 1774 and agreed a joint boycott from 1 December. Parliament aimed at one town and produced a colonial union.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate parts on two separate things, so neither answer may borrow from the other: a street shooting in 1(a), a set of statutes in 1(b).","For 1(a) ask which effect of the shooting was still working on opinion years later, and who was doing that work; for 1(b) ask who reacted besides Massachusetts.","Each part needs one consequence, one piece of precise supporting detail and one sentence explaining the link."],"codes":["P2-3 — 1 American colonist relations with Britain: Opposition to British policies","P2-3 — 1 American colonist relations with Britain: British and American relations"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the First Continental Congress of 1774. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the Second Continental Congress's decision in June 1775 to create a Continental Army. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two parts, each on the 4-mark consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the Continental Association and its enforcement committees, the fall in British exports, or the agreement to meet again in 1775. In 1(b) credit the unification of thirteen separate risings under one command, the choice of a Virginian to lead a New England siege, or the closing off of reconciliation.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was the Continental Association, the first boycott the colonies enforced together. The Congress that sat at Philadelphia from 5 September to 26 October 1774 agreed to stop importing British goods from 1 December 1774 and to stop exports the following September, and it instructed every town to elect a committee to inspect merchants and publish offenders. Because those committees had to be chosen and obeyed, they became a working local authority loyal to Congress rather than to royal governors, so when fighting began the machinery for raising men and money already existed. 1(b) One consequence was that thirteen separate risings became a single war. On 14 June 1775 Congress adopted the militia besieging Boston as a Continental Army and the next day appointed George Washington, a Virginian, to command it; he took charge at Cambridge on 3 July. Choosing a southerner to lead a New England siege deliberately tied the colonies together, and the army now answered to Congress rather than to any colony. It also made reconciliation harder, because a body that admitted no power to tax was now paying troops to fight the King's soldiers.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["The First Congress made no army and no declaration, so its consequence has to be found in what it asked ordinary towns to do.","For 1(b) ask what difference it made that one army answered to one body, and who was chosen to lead it.","Each part: the consequence named, one precise date or term as support, and the link explained in a sentence."],"codes":["P2-3 — 1 American colonist relations with Britain: The significance of the First and Second Continental Congresses"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Declaration of Independence of July 1776. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the British surrender at Yorktown in October 1781. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two parts on the 4-mark consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the possibility of foreign treaties and Franklin's mission to Paris, the forcing of a choice that created open Loyalism and confiscation, or the change in war aims from redress to sovereignty. In 1(b) credit the fall of North's ministry in March 1782 and the opening of talks, or the redirection of British effort to the West Indies and Gibraltar.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that foreign alliances became possible. While the colonies claimed only their rights inside the empire, a foreign power treating with them would simply be helping rebels; after 4 July 1776 there was a state that could make treaties, so Congress sent Benjamin Franklin to Paris that year and he negotiated the alliance signed on 6 February 1778. The Declaration also ended any middle course at home, since men now had to renounce the King or be counted his subjects, and those who refused became Loyalists liable to have their estates confiscated. 1(b) One consequence was that Britain gave up trying to win the war. Cornwallis surrendered about 7,000 men on 19 October 1781, and coming after Saratoga it convinced the Commons that America could not be recovered by fighting. Lord North's ministry fell in March 1782, its successors opened talks in Paris, and Britain concentrated what it had left on holding the West Indies and Gibraltar. The fighting did not stop everywhere at once, but Parliament's willingness to pay for it did, which is why the peace followed in 1783.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["For 1(a) think about what a declaration changes in law and in diplomacy rather than on a battlefield.","For 1(b) remember that Britain still held New York, Charleston and Savannah after the surrender, so the consequence must be political.","Each part needs one consequence, precise support and an explicit link; resist telling the story of the siege."],"codes":["P2-3 — 2 The War of Independence 1775-83: The influence of Thomas Paine's 'Common Sense'","P2-3 — 2 The War of Independence 1775-83: Key American victories"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Peace of Paris of 1783 for Britain. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the War of Independence for the Iroquois Confederacy. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two parts on the 4-mark consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the war debt and the taxation and politics that followed, the redirection of empire towards India and the Pacific, the end of convict transportation to America, or the rapid recovery of Anglo-American trade. In 1(b) credit the splitting of the Six Nations, the Sullivan expedition of 1779, the omission of Indigenous nations from the treaty, or the resettlement on the Grand River.","sol":"1(a) One consequence for Britain was a debt that shaped its politics for a decade: the national debt rose from about £127 million in 1775 to roughly £232 million by 1783, and paying the interest dominated every budget. Losing the thirteen colonies also redirected the empire. Convicts could no longer be transported to America, so a fleet was sent to Botany Bay in 1787, and attention turned towards India and the Pacific while trade with the United States quickly resumed. 1(b) One consequence was the breaking of the Confederacy itself. Four of the Six Nations, led by the Mohawk under Joseph Brant, fought for the Crown, while the Oneida and Tuscarora sided with the Americans, so a league that had held a common policy for generations fought against itself. In 1779 Washington sent Sullivan to destroy Iroquois country: more than forty towns were burned with their crops, and thousands fled to Fort Niagara, where many died that winter. The treaty of 1783 then ignored them entirely, and Brant's people were resettled on the Grand River in 1784.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["For 1(a) look past the loss of territory to what the war had cost and what Britain did next with its empire.","For 1(b) the Confederacy's strength had been that its nations acted as one, so ask what the war did to that.","Two consequences, two pieces of precise support, two explanations - and no narrative of the war in either part."],"codes":["P2-3 — 2 The War of Independence 1775-83: The Peace of Paris (1783)","P2-3 — 3 Consequences of the war in America: The consequences of the war for Indigenous peoples"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the War of Independence for the Loyalists. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of Lord Dunmore's Proclamation of November 1775. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two parts on the 4-mark consequence grid. In 1(a) credit confiscation and banishment by the states, the weakness of the treaty's recommendation, the exodus to Nova Scotia and the Niagara peninsula, the creation of New Brunswick in 1784, or the British claims commission. In 1(b) credit the hardening of slaveholding opinion against Britain, the flight of enslaved people to British lines, or the widening of the offer in 1779 and the departure of Black Loyalists in 1783.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was exile. Loyalists were disarmed, taxed twice over, banished or stripped of their estates under state confiscation laws, and the peace of 1783 protected them only by asking Congress to recommend that property be restored, which most states refused to do. About 60,000 left; more than 30,000 went to Nova Scotia, where Shelburne was founded, and others up the St Lawrence and into the Niagara peninsula. Their arrival was large enough to make New Brunswick a separate province in 1784 and to lay the foundations of English-speaking Canada. 1(b) One consequence was that slaveholders were driven further towards rebellion. Dunmore, the royal governor of Virginia, declared on 7 November 1775 that enslaved men belonging to rebels would be free if they joined the King's forces, and several hundred formed his Ethiopian Regiment. Because the proclamation threatened the property on which planters' wealth rested, planters in Virginia and the Carolinas who had hesitated now saw royal government as the greater danger. It also set a pattern Britain widened in 1779, so that by 1783 thousands of formerly enslaved people left with the British from New York.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["For 1(a) ask what the treaty actually obliged the states to do about Loyalist property, and what followed from the weakness of that wording.","For 1(b) the proclamation was aimed at enslaved people but its sharpest effect was on their owners.","Each part: one consequence, one precise figure, date or term, and one sentence of explanation."],"codes":["P2-3 — 3 Consequences of the war in America: The impact of the war on Loyalists","P2-3 — 3 Consequences of the war in America: The significance of American independence for slavery"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing how colonial protest turned into open war in the years 1770-75. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the Boston Tea Party (1773); the First Continental Congress (1774). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) needs a clear sequence leading to an outcome with the linkage between events analysed. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; material of the student's own might include the Boston Massacre and the repeal of the Townshend duties, the Tea Act of 1773, the Coercive Acts, Gage's governorship, the Continental Association, the arming of the militia, and Lexington and Concord.","sol":"The shooting in King Street on 5 March 1770 left five colonists dead and gave the radicals a story they could print, since Paul Revere's engraving travelled much further than the verdict that acquitted six of the eight soldiers. Yet in the same year North repealed the Townshend duties except the one on tea, and protest subsided for three years, which shows that violence by itself did not make a war. What revived the quarrel was the Tea Act of May 1773, because letting the East India Company sell direct and cheaply looked like a device to make Americans swallow the tax; so on 16 December 1773 the Sons of Liberty destroyed 342 chests in Boston harbour. Because property had been destroyed in public by a crowd nobody would name, Parliament punished the colony instead of the culprits: the Coercive Acts of 1774 closed the port, replaced the elected council and restricted town meetings, and Gage arrived as military governor in May. That was the step that turned a Boston quarrel into an American one, for twelve colonies sent delegates to Philadelphia in September 1774 and agreed a boycott enforced by elected town committees, while Massachusetts drilled militia and stored powder. When Gage sent troops to seize those stores they were met at Lexington and Concord on 19 April 1775, and a constitutional dispute had become a war.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Begin before the tea: something happened in 1770 that looked like the end of the quarrel, and explaining why it was not is what turns narrative into analysis.","At each stage ask what choice the other side was left with, rather than what happened next.","Plan four episodes with a connective sentence between each, so the account addresses more than the two printed bullets."],"codes":["P2-3 — 1 American colonist relations with Britain: Opposition to British policies","P2-3 — 1 American colonist relations with Britain: British and American relations","P2-3 — 1 American colonist relations with Britain: The significance of the First and Second Continental Congresses"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the turning of the war in America's favour in the years 1776-78. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the battle of Trenton (December 1776); the French alliance (February 1778). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) needs a clear sequence leading to an outcome with the linkage analysed. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; own material might include the loss of New York and the retreat across New Jersey, expiring enlistments, Princeton, Burgoyne's campaign and Howe's move to Philadelphia, Saratoga, Valley Forge and von Steuben.","sol":"By December 1776 the war looked nearly lost: Howe had taken New York, Washington had been driven across New Jersey, and most of his men's enlistments expired on 31 December. Washington's answer was to attack rather than let the army dissolve. He crossed the Delaware on the night of 25-26 December 1776 and took about 900 Hessians at Trenton, then beat a British force at Princeton on 3 January 1777; because the army had won something, men re-enlisted and most of New Jersey was recovered. That survival shaped 1777, since an army still in the field meant Britain had to plan a campaign to destroy it. Burgoyne marched south from Canada to cut New England off, but Howe took his own army to Philadelphia instead, so Burgoyne fought unsupported; beaten at Freeman's Farm and Bemis Heights, he surrendered his entire army at Saratoga on 17 October 1777. Saratoga is the hinge, because it answered the question France had been asking since 1776: could the rebels destroy a British army rather than merely survive one? On 6 February 1778 Louis XVI signed treaties of commerce and alliance, and a colonial rebellion became a European war. Meanwhile at Valley Forge, where about 2,000 men died of disease that winter, von Steuben, who arrived on 23 February 1778, drilled the survivors from 19 March 1778, so the army that marched out in June was one an ally could take seriously.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The account has to explain a turn, so it needs to start at the low point and say what made it a low point.","Link the episodes by asking what each one made possible or necessary for the next, rather than by dating them.","Finish on what the sequence amounted to by the summer of 1778, in one sentence."],"codes":["P2-3 — 2 The War of Independence 1775-83: Key American victories"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the ending of the War of Independence in the years 1780-83. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the fall of Charleston (May 1780); the Peace of Paris (1783). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) needs a sequence leading to an outcome with the linkage analysed. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; own material might include Camden, King's Mountain, Greene's campaign, Cowpens and Guilford Courthouse, the Chesapeake and Yorktown, the fall of North's ministry, the preliminary articles of 1782 and the evacuation of New York.","sol":"By 1780 Britain's hopes rested on the South, where Loyalists were believed to be numerous. At first the strategy worked: Clinton took Charleston on 12 May 1780, capturing over 5,000 men in the worst American defeat of the war, and Cornwallis destroyed Gates's army at Camden in August. But holding the country proved harder than beating armies, because Loyalist risings brought savage reprisals and the backcountry turned against the British after King's Mountain in October 1780. Nathanael Greene then made Cornwallis chase him: Morgan destroyed Tarleton's force at Cowpens on 17 January 1781, and although Cornwallis won at Guilford Courthouse on 15 March 1781 he lost about a quarter of his army doing it, so he marched into Virginia to be supplied by sea. That made sea power decisive. De Grasse's fleet beat the Royal Navy off the Chesapeake on 5 September 1781, sealing Cornwallis in Yorktown while Washington and Rochambeau marched south, and he surrendered about 7,000 men on 19 October 1781. Because Parliament would not fund another campaign, North's ministry fell in March 1782 and talks opened in Paris, where Franklin, Jay and Adams agreed preliminary articles on 30 November 1782. The definitive treaty of 3 September 1783 recognised independence and carried the new republic's boundary west to the Mississippi; the last British troops left New York that November.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Start with why Britain was fighting in the South at all, because the assumption behind that choice is what failed.","The links here are about supply and reinforcement, so keep asking what each side could and could not move, and how.","End with what the sequence produced, not merely with the date of the treaty."],"codes":["P2-3 — 2 The War of Independence 1775-83: Key American victories","P2-3 — 2 The War of Independence 1775-83: The Peace of Paris (1783)","P2-3 — 3 Consequences of the war in America: The impact of the war on Loyalists"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of Washington's leadership for the American war effort in the years 1775-81. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Credit the creation of a single Continental Army under Congress, the strategy of keeping an army in being, Trenton and Princeton and the saving of enlistments, Valley Forge and von Steuben, subordination to civilian authority, and the decision of 1781 to march south.","sol":"Washington's importance is best measured by asking what the war would have been without him. Appointed by Congress on 15 June 1775, he took command at Cambridge on 3 July and turned a collection of colonial militias into one Continental Army answering to Congress; that mattered politically as much as militarily, because it gave the rebellion a national institution before there was a nation. His second contribution was to grasp that he did not need to win battles, only to keep an army in existence, since Britain could only win by destroying it. After losing New York in 1776 he retreated rather than gamble everything, then struck at Trenton on 26 December 1776 and Princeton on 3 January 1777, which saved enlistments about to expire and carried the cause through its worst winter. Third, he held the army together at Valley Forge, where about 2,000 men died of disease, and let von Steuben drill it into a force that could stand in line against regulars. Fourth, he kept the army under Congress, so it never became a rival government. The result came in 1781, when he gave up his own plan to attack New York and marched with Rochambeau to Virginia, trapping Cornwallis. Washington's importance, then, is that he made American survival possible until French sea power made American victory possible.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Importance means the difference he made, so the answer needs a measure: what would probably have happened otherwise?","His record of battles won is not impressive, which is a clue that the argument lies elsewhere.","Take three or four distinct contributions, each with a precise date or place, and end by saying what kind of difference they added up to."],"codes":["P2-3 — 2 The War of Independence 1775-83: Key American victories","P2-3 — 1 American colonist relations with Britain: The significance of the First and Second Continental Congresses"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of French involvement for the American victory in the war. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Credit the secret supplies from 1776 and the treaties of February 1778, loans and munitions set against Congress's inability to tax, the widening of the war to Spain in 1779 and the Dutch in 1780, Rochambeau's troops, and above all the naval victory off the Chesapeake that made Yorktown possible.","sol":"France's importance was that it changed the kind of war Britain had to fight. Before 1778 the colonies faced an empire that could concentrate almost everything on North America; afterwards Britain was fighting a global war. The turning point was Saratoga: Burgoyne's surrender on 17 October 1777 persuaded Louis XVI that the Americans could win, and treaties of commerce and alliance were signed on 6 February 1778. French money and supplies mattered first and longest, since Congress had no power to tax and its paper currency collapsed, so loans, muskets and powder from France kept armies in the field at all. Spain joined France in June 1779 and the Dutch followed in 1780, which forced Britain to defend Gibraltar, the West Indies and the Channel, so troops and ships that might have gone to America went elsewhere. Most important was sea power. Britain had won its earlier successes by moving armies by water, and at Yorktown that advantage was reversed: de Grasse's fleet beat the Royal Navy off the Chesapeake on 5 September 1781 and thousands of French regulars under Rochambeau joined the siege, so Cornwallis could be neither reinforced nor taken off and surrendered on 19 October 1781. French help did not create the American cause, which had survived alone since 1775, but it converted survival into victory.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Compare the war before the alliance with the war after it, because that comparison is the measure of importance.","Three kinds of help are worth separating: money, other enemies for Britain, and ships.","Finish with a qualification that still answers the question, rather than simply calling the help decisive."],"codes":["P2-3 — 2 The War of Independence 1775-83: Key American victories"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the Peace of Paris (1783) for the Indigenous peoples of the lands east of the Mississippi. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Credit the absence of any Indigenous nation from the treaty and the talks, the cession of land Britain did not occupy, the American treatment of that cession as conquest at treaties such as Fort Stanwix (1784), renewed war in the Ohio country, the loss of the imperial rivalry that had protected Indigenous nations, and the splitting and resettlement of the Iroquois.","sol":"The treaty's importance for Indigenous peoples lies in what it did not say: no Indigenous nation was named in it, and none was represented at the talks. Britain simply transferred to the United States its claim to everything east of the Mississippi and south of the Great Lakes, land Britain did not occupy and its allies had not lost in battle. Nations such as the Mohawk, Seneca and Shawnee, who had fought for the Crown because it was the colonists who wanted their land, found themselves handed to the enemy they had not been beaten by. Because the new republic treated the cession as a conquest, it demanded land rather than buying it, as at Fort Stanwix in 1784, and settlers crossed the mountains in numbers that the Proclamation Line of 1763 had once been meant to prevent. The consequences ran forward for a generation, in war in the Ohio country through the 1790s and in the steady loss of the land the treaty had signed away. For the Iroquois there was an internal loss too, since the Confederacy had split between the two sides and never recovered its unity, and Brant's Mohawks left for the Grand River in 1784. The peace matters, then, less as a settlement than as the moment Indigenous nations lost the rivalry between empires that they had used to protect themselves.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Start from who was in the room in Paris and who was not, because the omission is the argument.","Ask what Britain was actually giving away, and by what right.","Trace the effects forward past 1783 and end with the deeper loss, not just the loss of territory."],"codes":["P2-3 — 2 The War of Independence 1775-83: The Peace of Paris (1783)","P2-3 — 3 Consequences of the war in America: The consequences of the war for Indigenous peoples"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of American independence for slavery in the new United States. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Credit Dunmore's proclamation and the widened offer of 1779, Black service on both sides, northern abolition in Pennsylvania and Massachusetts, the entrenchment and expansion of slavery in the South, and the three-fifths clause and the protection of the slave trade in the Constitution of 1787.","sol":"Independence mattered for slavery because it moved the decision from London to thirteen state governments, and they answered it in opposite ways. Roughly a fifth of the people in the colonies were enslaved, and the war had already loosened the system: Dunmore's proclamation of 7 November 1775 offered freedom to enslaved men of rebel owners who joined the King's forces, Clinton widened the offer in 1779, tens of thousands escaped, and about 5,000 Black men served in the American forces, some gaining freedom by it. In the North, where slavery was economically marginal, the language of the Declaration became an argument that could be used in legislatures and courts: Pennsylvania passed a gradual abolition act in 1780, and Massachusetts courts ended it in 1783. In the South the same independence worked the other way, because planters now controlled their own governments and no outside power could interfere; slavery spread westwards, and the Constitution of 1787 counted enslaved people as three fifths of a person for representation and protected the slave trade until 1808. The importance of independence, then, is that it made the United States a country half founded on liberty and half built on slavery, and wrote that contradiction into its own documents.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The question is about a change of who decided, not only about what was decided.","Look for effects pulling in opposite directions in different parts of the new country.","Support each side of the argument with a dated law or clause, then judge what the two together amounted to."],"codes":["P2-3 — 3 Consequences of the war in America: The significance of American independence for slavery"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Who published 'Common Sense' in Philadelphia in January 1776?","a":"Thomas Paine, anonymously."},{"q":"Which British general surrendered his army at Saratoga on 17 October 1777?","a":"General John Burgoyne."},{"q":"On what date was the Peace of Paris that ended the war signed?","a":"3 September 1783."},{"q":"To which British colony did more than 30,000 Loyalists move after the war?","a":"Nova Scotia."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The Stamp Act and the Sons of Liberty, 1765","open":false,"code":"P2-3 — 1 American colonist relations with Britain: Opposition to British policies","back":"A tax on newspapers, legal papers and licences, in force from 1 November 1765. Nine colonies met at the Stamp Act Congress in October; the Sons of Liberty forced distributors to resign. Repealed March 1766 with the Declaratory Act."},{"front":"The Boston Massacre, 5 March 1770","open":false,"code":"P2-3 — 1 American colonist relations with Britain: Opposition to British policies","back":"Soldiers fired into a crowd in King Street, killing five, among them Crispus Attucks. Paul Revere's engraving spread it as a massacre. John Adams defended the soldiers; only two of eight were convicted, of manslaughter."},{"front":"The Boston Tea Party, 16 December 1773","open":false,"code":"P2-3 — 1 American colonist relations with Britain: British and American relations","back":"Sons of Liberty disguised as Mohawks threw 342 chests of East India Company tea into Boston harbour, rejecting the Tea Act of May 1773, which let the Company sell direct and cheaply while keeping the tea duty."},{"front":"The Coercive or Intolerable Acts, 1774","open":false,"code":"P2-3 — 1 American colonist relations with Britain: British and American relations","back":"Boston's port closed from 1 June until the tea was paid for; the Massachusetts council made appointed, town meetings restricted; officials could be tried in Britain; troops more easily quartered. Gage arrived as military governor in May 1774."},{"front":"The First Continental Congress, September to October 1774","open":false,"code":"P2-3 — 1 American colonist relations with Britain: The significance of the First and Second Continental Congresses","back":"Fifty-six delegates from twelve colonies at Philadelphia. It petitioned the King, claimed the rights of Englishmen and agreed the Continental Association: no British imports from 1 December 1774, enforced by elected town committees."},{"front":"The Second Continental Congress, from May 1775","open":false,"code":"P2-3 — 1 American colonist relations with Britain: The significance of the First and Second Continental Congresses","back":"Met from 10 May 1775 after Lexington and Concord. Created the Continental Army on 14 June, appointed Washington on 15 June, sent the Olive Branch Petition in July, printed money, and voted independence in July 1776."},{"front":"'Common Sense', 10 January 1776","open":false,"code":"P2-3 — 2 The War of Independence 1775-83: The influence of Thomas Paine's 'Common Sense'","back":"Thomas Paine's anonymous pamphlet, published in Philadelphia; over a hundred thousand copies circulated that year. It attacked hereditary monarchy itself and argued an island could not govern a continent."},{"front":"The Declaration of Independence, 4 July 1776","open":false,"code":"P2-3 — 2 The War of Independence 1775-83: The influence of Thomas Paine's 'Common Sense'","back":"Drafted by Thomas Jefferson for a committee of five; Congress voted Lee's resolution on 2 July and approved the text on 4 July 1776. It claimed all men are created equal and that government rests on consent."},{"front":"A reason the British lost the War of Independence","open":true,"code":"P2-3 — 2 The War of Independence 1775-83: Key American victories","back":"Distance and supply across the Atlantic; Washington keeping an army in being after Trenton in December 1776; divided commands, as when Howe went to Philadelphia and left Burgoyne unsupported; French money, troops and sea power from 1778."},{"front":"Saratoga, 17 October 1777","open":false,"code":"P2-3 — 2 The War of Independence 1775-83: Key American victories","back":"Burgoyne, unsupported after Howe went to Philadelphia, was beaten at Freeman's Farm and Bemis Heights and surrendered his whole army to Gates. It brought the French treaties of commerce and alliance on 6 February 1778."},{"front":"Yorktown, 19 October 1781","open":false,"code":"P2-3 — 2 The War of Independence 1775-83: Key American victories","back":"De Grasse's fleet beat the Royal Navy off the Chesapeake on 5 September 1781, cutting Cornwallis off; Washington and Rochambeau besieged him and he surrendered about 7,000 men. North's ministry fell in March 1782."},{"front":"The Peace of Paris, 3 September 1783","open":false,"code":"P2-3 — 2 The War of Independence 1775-83: The Peace of Paris (1783)","back":"Negotiated by Franklin, Jay and Adams; signed with David Hartley for Britain. Independence recognised, the boundary set at the Mississippi, fishing rights kept, debts to be paid, and only a recommendation that Loyalist property be restored."},{"front":"Independence and slavery in the new United States","open":false,"code":"P2-3 — 3 Consequences of the war in America: The significance of American independence for slavery","back":"Pennsylvania passed a gradual abolition act in 1780 and Massachusetts courts ended slavery there in 1783. In the South it expanded; the Constitution of 1787 used the three-fifths clause and protected the slave trade to 1808."},{"front":"The war and the Iroquois Confederacy","open":false,"code":"P2-3 — 3 Consequences of the war in America: The consequences of the war for Indigenous peoples","back":"Mohawk, Seneca, Cayuga and Onondaga fought for Britain under leaders such as Joseph Brant; Oneida and Tuscarora for the Americans. Sullivan burned over forty towns in 1779. The 1783 treaty ignored them; Brant went to the Grand River in 1784."},{"front":"Loyalists after 1783","open":false,"code":"P2-3 — 3 Consequences of the war in America: The impact of the war on Loyalists","back":"Perhaps a fifth of white colonists; estates confiscated by the states. About 60,000 left, over 30,000 to Nova Scotia, others to the Niagara peninsula. New Brunswick was created in 1784; a claims commission awarded about £3 million."}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Paper 2 booklet P Question 2, narrative account, 8 marks)","items":["The question: 'Write a narrative account analysing the failure of reconciliation between Britain and the colonies in the years 1775-76.' You may use the following in your answer: the Olive Branch Petition (July 1775); the British evacuation of Boston (March 1776). You must also use information of your own.","The paragraph: 'Congress adopted the Olive Branch Petition on 5 July 1775 because most delegates still wanted the quarrel settled inside the empire, even while their new army sat outside Boston. The King would not receive it when Richard Penn presented it on 1 September, and the Proclamation of Rebellion of 23 August had answered it before it arrived, so the one offer made from America was refused unread. That refusal is what gave the Prohibitory Act of 22 December 1775 its force: an Act that put colonial ships outside the King's protection told colonists that Britain would treat them as enemies whatever they petitioned, and it was into that gap that Thomas Paine's 'Common Sense' was published on 10 January 1776, moving the argument from bad ministers to monarchy itself. The last prop of the moderate case fell when the guns on Dorchester Heights forced Howe to evacuate Boston on 17 March 1776, since an enemy who could be beaten did not have to be bargained with - and Congress voted for independence on 2 July 1776.'","Why it reaches Level 3: the events are organised into a sequence leading to an outcome, and the linkage between them is analysed rather than assumed - the refusal of the petition empties the moderate position, the Prohibitory Act makes the refusal permanent, Paine supplies the argument that fits it, and the evacuation shows that the alternative is winnable. Accurate and relevant information is used precisely (the petition of 5 July 1775, the proclamation of 23 August, the Act of 22 December, 'Common Sense' on 10 January 1776, Boston on 17 March), which is the Level 3 AO1 descriptor, and the passage is coherent and logically structured, which is the Level 3 AO2 descriptor."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["The paragraph: 'In the summer of 1775 Congress sent King George a petition called the Olive Branch Petition to try to make peace with Britain. The King would not accept it and said that the colonies were in rebellion. Parliament then passed a law banning American trade. Thomas Paine wrote a pamphlet called 'Common Sense' saying that America should be independent, and a great many people read it. The British army left Boston in 1776 after Washington got cannon onto the hills above the town. In July 1776 Congress declared independence from Britain.'","The descriptor it meets: Level 2 for AO2, 'a narrative is given, showing some organisation of material into a sequence of events leading to an outcome', with 'some analysis of the linkage between them' - the petition is at least shown being refused before the pamphlet appears. For AO1 it meets 'accurate and relevant information is added, showing some knowledge and understanding of the events', since the petition, the trade ban, 'Common Sense', the evacuation of Boston and the vote of July 1776 are all correctly placed.","The descriptor it misses: Level 3 needs the linkage analysed, not implied. Here the events are joined by 'then' rather than by cause: nothing explains why a refused petition mattered (the moderate case collapsing is absent), what the trade ban actually did (putting the colonies outside the King's protection is absent), or why the evacuation of Boston made independence easier to vote for. The detail is also imprecise - no dates beyond the year and no names beyond Paine and Washington - so it falls short of 'good knowledge and understanding of the key features or characteristics of the events'."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: protest and repression, 1765-73","items":["Stamp Act: passed March 1765, in force 1 November 1765; taxed newspapers, pamphlets, legal documents, licences and playing cards.","The argument against it: an internal tax laid by a Parliament to which the colonies elected no members - 'no taxation without representation'.","Stamp Act Congress: New York, October 1765; delegates from nine colonies; petitions to King and Parliament.","Sons of Liberty: formed in 1765 in Boston and New York; crowd action forced stamp distributors such as Andrew Oliver to resign.","Non-importation: colonial merchants agreed to stop buying British goods, which brought pressure from British traders for repeal.","Repeal: March 1766 - and the Declaratory Act the same day, asserting Parliament's right to legislate for the colonies in all cases whatsoever.","Townshend duties, 1767: on glass, lead, paint, paper and tea; customs commissioners based in Boston.","Troops sent to Boston in 1768 after resistance to the customs service; soldiers and townspeople competed for casual work.","Boston Massacre: 5 March 1770; five colonists killed, including Crispus Attucks, a sailor of African and Native descent.","The trials: John Adams defended the soldiers; six of the eight were acquitted and two convicted of manslaughter.","Paul Revere's engraving of the shooting circulated widely and fixed the word 'massacre' on the event.","1770: Lord North repealed the Townshend duties except the duty on tea, which was kept to assert the principle; protest subsided for three years."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: crisis and the Continental Congresses, 1773-75","items":["Tea Act, May 1773: allowed the East India Company to ship tea direct to America, undercutting smuggled Dutch tea while keeping the duty.","Boston Tea Party: night of 16 December 1773; Sons of Liberty disguised as Mohawks destroyed 342 chests aboard three ships at Griffin's Wharf.","Boston Port Act, 1774: closed the harbour from 1 June until the destroyed tea was paid for.","Massachusetts Government Act, 1774: the council to be appointed by the Crown, town meetings only with the governor's permission.","Administration of Justice Act, 1774: officials accused of capital offences could be tried in Britain.","Quartering Act, 1774: made it easier to billet troops; General Thomas Gage arrived as military governor in May 1774.","Quebec Act, 1774: extended Quebec's boundary to the Ohio and allowed Catholic worship; counted by colonists among the Intolerable Acts.","First Continental Congress: Philadelphia, 5 September to 26 October 1774; fifty-six delegates from twelve colonies, Georgia absent.","Continental Association: no British imports from 1 December 1774, no exports from the following September, enforced by elected town committees.","Lexington and Concord: 19 April 1775, when Gage's troops marched to seize militia stores; the first shots of the war.","Second Continental Congress: met from 10 May 1775; created the Continental Army on 14 June and appointed Washington on 15 June 1775.","Olive Branch Petition, July 1775, asking the King for reconciliation; answered by the Proclamation of Rebellion on 23 August 1775.","Prohibitory Act, December 1775: put the colonies outside the King's protection and banned their trade."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the case for independence - 'Common Sense', Jefferson and the Declaration, 1776","items":["'Common Sense': published anonymously in Philadelphia on 10 January 1776 by Thomas Paine, an Englishman who had landed there thirteen months earlier, on 30 November 1774.","Its sales: the first printing sold out in days and well over a hundred thousand copies circulated during 1776.","Its argument: attacked hereditary monarchy itself rather than particular ministers, and denied that a small island should govern a continent.","Its style: plain words, biblical images and short sentences, aimed at readers who were not lawyers.","Richard Henry Lee's resolution for independence was voted by Congress on 2 July 1776.","The Committee of Five, appointed in June 1776, was Jefferson, Franklin, John Adams, Roger Sherman and Robert Livingston.","Thomas Jefferson of Virginia, then thirty-three, wrote the draft; Congress cut about a quarter of it.","Among the passages cut was one blaming George III for the slave trade, removed to keep southern delegates on side.","The Declaration was approved on 4 July 1776; it asserted that all men are created equal, with unalienable rights, and that government rests on the consent of the governed.","Its practical effects: it created a state that could make treaties, made neutrality impossible for colonists, and turned a claim for rights into a claim of sovereignty."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the War of Independence - Washington, Saratoga, France, the South and Yorktown, 1775-81","items":["Washington was appointed commander-in-chief on 15 June 1775 and took command of the army besieging Boston at Cambridge on 3 July 1775.","Bunker Hill, 17 June 1775: the British took the position but with about a thousand casualties, which made them cautious afterwards.","New York was lost in 1776 and Washington was driven across New Jersey; most enlistments were due to expire on 31 December 1776.","Trenton: Washington crossed the Delaware on the night of 25-26 December 1776 and captured about 900 Hessians; Princeton followed on 3 January 1777.","Saratoga: Burgoyne marched south from Canada; Howe went to Philadelphia instead of supporting him; Burgoyne lost at Freeman's Farm and Bemis Heights.","Burgoyne surrendered his entire army - about six thousand men - to Gates on 17 October 1777, the first British army to surrender in this war.","France signed treaties of commerce and alliance with Congress on 6 February 1778; Spain declared war on Britain in June 1779; the Dutch Republic entered in 1780.","Valley Forge: the army camped there from 19 December 1777 to June 1778 and about 2,000 men died of disease.","Baron von Steuben reached Valley Forge on 23 February 1778 and began drilling a model company of 100 men on 19 March 1778, the rest of the army following in late March, which gave it a single system of training.","The southern strategy: Savannah taken in December 1778; Charleston surrendered on 12 May 1780 with over 5,000 prisoners, the worst American defeat of the war.","Camden, August 1780: Cornwallis destroyed Gates's army; King's Mountain, October 1780, destroyed a Loyalist force and turned the backcountry.","Cowpens, 17 January 1781: Morgan destroyed Tarleton's force. Guilford Courthouse, 15 March 1781: Cornwallis won but lost about a quarter of his army.","The Chesapeake, 5 September 1781: de Grasse's French fleet beat the Royal Navy and cut Cornwallis off from reinforcement or escape.","Yorktown: Washington and Rochambeau besieged Cornwallis, who surrendered about 7,000 men on 19 October 1781."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the peace and its consequences - Paris 1783, Loyalists, Indigenous peoples, Black Americans and Britain","items":["Lord North's ministry fell in March 1782; its successors opened peace talks, and William Pitt the Younger became prime minister in December 1783.","Benjamin Franklin was one of the three American commissioners in France from December 1776 and sole minister plenipotentiary from September 1778, secured the alliance of 1778 and led the peace negotiation with John Jay and John Adams.","Preliminary articles were signed with Britain's agent Richard Oswald on 30 November 1782, without consulting France.","The definitive treaty was signed in Paris on 3 September 1783 by Franklin, Adams and Jay, with David Hartley for Britain.","Its terms: independence recognised; the boundary west to the Mississippi, north to the Great Lakes and south to the Floridas; fishing rights off Newfoundland; pre-war debts to be honoured.","East and West Florida went to Spain by the separate Anglo-Spanish treaty signed at Versailles on the same day, 3 September 1783; the American treaty only fixed the southern boundary.","On Loyalist property the treaty only required Congress to recommend restitution to the states, and most states refused.","British troops evacuated New York on 25 November 1783; Britain kept Canada, and Anglo-American trade recovered quickly.","Britain's national debt rose from about £127 million in 1775 to roughly £232 million by 1783; convicts could no longer be transported to America, and a fleet sailed for Botany Bay in 1787.","About 60,000 Loyalists left the United States: more than 30,000 to Nova Scotia, where Shelburne was founded, and others to the St Lawrence and the Niagara peninsula.","New Brunswick was made a separate province in 1784 for the Loyalist influx; the Niagara and St Lawrence settlements became Upper Canada in 1791.","A British claims commission set up in 1783 heard more than 3,000 Loyalist claims and awarded about £3 million, well short of the sums claimed.","About 3,000 Black Loyalists were recorded in the Book of Negroes as they left New York in 1783 and settled at Birchtown and nearby; nearly 1,200 sailed in 1792 to found Freetown in Sierra Leone.","Indigenous nations were neither named in the treaty nor represented at it; the United States demanded land as from a conquered people at treaties such as Fort Stanwix in 1784.","Sullivan's expedition of 1779 burned more than forty Iroquois towns; Joseph Brant's Mohawks were resettled on the Grand River in 1784.","The Articles of Confederation, agreed by Congress in 1777 and ratified on 1 March 1781, gave Congress no power to tax or regulate trade, one vote to each state and required all thirteen to agree an amendment; they were replaced by the Constitution drafted in 1787."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan - Paper 2 booklet P Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing how the American war became a global war for Britain in the years 1778-81' (8 marks)","items":["The question as printed: 'Write a narrative account analysing how the American war became a global war for Britain in the years 1778-81. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: Spain's entry into the war (1779); the entry of the Dutch Republic (1780). You must also use information of your own.'","Decoded: you are not asked to list Britain's enemies. You are asked how a rebellion in thirteen colonies turned into a war Britain had to fight in several seas at once, so every episode must be chosen because it stretched British force further, and the account must end by saying what the stretching amounted to.","The cap that decides your level: 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' The two bullets are two aspects, so plan at least two more of your own - the point beyond the stimulus is not optional if you want the top level.","Opening sentence (about 1 minute): set the position at the start of 1778. Britain was fighting one war, in America, with no European enemy and no European ally, and could send its ships and regiments where it chose.","Episode 1 - the French treaties of 6 February 1778 (your own material): France's entry made it a naval war, and a British force took St Lucia in December 1778 to give the fleet a base in the Caribbean. The link to draw: from this point every regiment sent to America had to be weighed against an island or a dockyard somewhere else.","Episode 2 - Spain's entry, 1779 (stimulus point): the Franco-Spanish treaty of Aranjuez was signed on 12 April 1779 and Spain declared war that June; the siege of Gibraltar began on 24 June 1779 and a Franco-Spanish fleet entered the Channel that August without attempting a landing. The link: Britain now had to hold a Mediterranean fortress and guard its own coast, so the ships that relieved Gibraltar in January 1780 were ships that were not off New York.","Episode 3 - the Dutch Republic, December 1780 (stimulus point): the Dutch moved towards the Russian-sponsored Armed Neutrality of 1780 and Britain declared war on 20 December 1780, seizing the entrepot of St Eustatius on 3 February 1781. The link: the last European power still supplying Britain's enemies became an enemy itself, and Britain was left without a friend in Europe.","Episode 4 - the sugar islands, 1779-81 (your own material): d'Estaing took Grenada in July 1779 and the West Indies pulled squadrons away from the American coast. The link: the islands were worth more in trade than the rebel colonies, so the ministry defended them first and the American war became the theatre the navy could spare least for.","Episode 5 - the Chesapeake, 5 September 1781 (your own material): de Grasse brought his fleet up from the West Indies and Graves could not break through to Cornwallis. The link: this is where the wider war decided the American one, because for a few weeks the French had more ships in American waters than Britain had left to send.","The connective sentences are where the marks are. Between each episode write one sentence beginning 'Because...' or 'That mattered because...' rather than 'Next' or 'After this'.","Closing sentence: say what the sequence amounted to - by the autumn of 1781 Britain was defending Gibraltar, the West Indies, its own coast and its position in India as well as an army in Virginia, and the one place where it could not concentrate at the decisive moment was the one that lost it the war.","Timing inside the booklet's 55 minutes: about 13 minutes here - 2 to 3 minutes listing the episodes and the links in the margin, 10 writing. Do not write an introduction: begin with the first episode."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:28:46Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.86,"notes":"Judged afresh against the retargeted items: every new fact verifies on the web - Declaratory Act assented 18 March 1766 with the repeal; the circular letter of 11 February 1768 and the 92-17 refusal to rescind of 30 June, after which Bernard dissolved the House; about sixty Boston merchants on 1 August 1768, New York the same year, Philadelphia on 10 March 1769; North's motion of 5 March 1770 and assent on 12 April, tea alone kept; the Olive Branch Petition adopted 5 July 1775, the Proclamation of Rebellion of 23 August and Dartmouth's non-answer when Penn presented it on 1 September; Boston evacuated 17 March 1776; Aranjuez 12 April 1779, Spain's declaration that June, Gibraltar besieged from 24 June 1779 and relieved in January 1780; the Dutch war from 20 December 1780 after the Armed Neutrality, St Eustatius taken 3 February 1781; Franklin's necessary-and-advisable paper of April 1782, Oswald's commission of 27 September, the preliminaries of 30 November 1782 signed without Vergennes and the six million livres that December. No answer key is wrong; no bank question reproduces specimen booklet P2, whose Q1 sets the Sugar Act, whose Q2 is the 1777-82 narrative on Saratoga and Yorktown and whose Q3 bullets are Spotswood, the Stono Rebellion and the Peace of Paris for Anglo-American relations; dupscan prints no duplication; the caps, tariffs and level labels match levels.md, including the single 'No access to Level 3' cap the plan quotes. What is left is cosmetic: Franklin's title in the peace fact file¹, the Mississippi promoted into his 'necessary' list², the open card whose front is a question³ and a doubled clause in question 7⁴."}}},"P3-1":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"P3-1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 1 is the ground floor of the whole American West course: everything later in the option is an argument about what happened to the two things set up here, the Plains peoples' way of life and the settlers' push into their land. Booklet P returns to it constantly. The consequence question likes single dated events, and this key topic is full of them: the Indian Removal Act, the Permanent Indian Frontier, the find at Sutter's Mill, the death of Joseph Smith, the two measures of 1851. The narrative account likes a short run of years in which one thing visibly made the next more likely, and the road from a frontier line drawn in 1834 to a treaty signed in 1851 is exactly that. The importance questions like a single event, person or law whose effects reach forward for decades. If you can say, with names and dates, how the Plains peoples lived and what they believed about land, why white Americans went west and what it cost them, and why a policy meant to keep two peoples apart collapsed within twenty years, you can answer almost anything set on this key topic. This room teaches that sequence as an argument you can make, and it trains the three moves booklet P rewards: explaining a consequence instead of retelling an event, writing a narrative that links its episodes instead of listing them, and judging what difference something made to what came after it.","idea":[{"h":"Following the herds: bands, councils and the buffalo","t":"The Plains peoples - among them the Sioux, Cheyenne, Arapaho, Comanche and Crow - lived as nomads because the Plains would not support anything else. Grass fed the buffalo, the buffalo fed the people, and the herds moved, so the people moved with them. The unit of daily life was the band: a few dozen families who camped, hunted and travelled together. A band had a chief, but a chief on the Plains could not give an order. He was chosen for wisdom, generosity and a record in war, and he led by persuasion; important matters were settled in a council of elders, and warrior societies such as the Cheyenne Dog Soldiers policed the hunt and the camp. Several bands together made a nation, which came together only at certain seasons. Everything the household owned had to be portable: the tipi was buffalo hide over a cone of wooden poles, and it came down and went onto a travois - two poles dragged behind a horse - in minutes. Horses, brought to the continent by the Spanish, transformed hunting and made a man's wealth countable in the animals he owned. Life was hard and short, and a nomadic people could not carry those who could not keep up, so the old or gravely sick were sometimes left behind. A top-level answer uses this as explanation, not description: it says that the way of life was a rational adaptation to the grass and the herds, which is precisely why anything that touched the buffalo or the grazing threatened everything else at once.","code":"P3-1 — 1 Indigenous peoples of the Plains: Social and tribal structures, ways of life and means of survival"},{"h":"Land you cannot sell: beliefs about nature, war and property","t":"The Plains peoples did not think land could be owned. The earth was alive and shared; a nation had lands it hunted and defended, but the idea that an individual might buy a piece of it, fence it and pass it to a son made no sense. Nature was not a stock of resources but a web of relatives, and particular places - for the Lakota Sioux the Black Hills above all - were sacred ground where the spirits could be approached through fasting, dreams and dances such as the Sun Dance. The Sioux called the great spirit power Wakan Tanka. Property was measured in horses and in what a household could carry, and generosity raised a man's standing far more than hoarding did. War was fought for horses, revenge and honour rather than for territory or conquest, and the highest honour was counting coup - touching a living enemy with the hand or a stick and getting away - because courage, not killing, was the point. Casualties were usually small. A top-level answer uses these beliefs as the engine of the conflict rather than as background colour: every treaty the United States offered asked people to accept boundaries, ownership and permanent surrender of land, so the two sides could sign the same document meaning entirely different things by it, and the government then read the difference as bad faith.","code":"P3-1 — 1 Indigenous peoples of the Plains: Beliefs about land and nature"},{"h":"Pushed and pulled: hard times in the East and a destiny in the West","t":"Migration was never one motive. The push came from the East: the financial panic of 1837 and the depression that followed wrecked farm prices in the Mississippi valley, banks foreclosed, and disease and unemployment made staying look as risky as going. The pull came from reports of Oregon's Willamette valley, where the soil was deep, the climate mild and the land free for the taking, and from the Pre-emption Act of 1841, which let a settler who had squatted on surveyed public land buy 160 acres at a dollar and a quarter an acre before it went to auction. Over the top of both sat an idea. In the July and August issue of the United States Magazine and Democratic Review in 1845, its editor John L. O'Sullivan wrote that it was the nation's manifest destiny to overspread the continent allotted to it by Providence; he was arguing for the annexation of Texas, but the phrase escaped and became the standard justification for expansion to the Pacific. Manifest Destiny mattered because it converted a private gamble into a national duty, and because it gave any government that wanted Indian land a moral vocabulary for taking it. A top-level answer never treats these as a list: it explains how push, pull and belief combined, so that a family already ruined by the depression could read a westward move as both a rescue and a patriotic act.","code":"P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: The factors encouraging migration"},{"h":"Gold: the rush that turned a trickle into a flood, 1848-49","t":"James Marshall found gold at Sutter's Mill on the American River in January 1848. For most of that year the news travelled slowly and was half disbelieved, until President Polk confirmed it in his message to Congress in December 1848 and the rush began in earnest. Roughly eighty thousand people reached California during 1849 - the forty-niners - by sea round Cape Horn, across Panama, or overland on the trails, and San Francisco grew from about a thousand people to about twenty-five thousand inside a year. California was admitted to the Union as a state in 1850. The rush changed the character of migration completely. Oregon-bound families had moved to farm; the forty-niners were overwhelmingly young, male, single and intending to leave rich, which is why the camps had saloons and gambling houses before they had courts. It also changed the trails: the traffic across the Plains multiplied, and with it the pressure on grass, timber, water and the herds. A top-level answer uses the rush as a hinge rather than an anecdote: it made the far West suddenly worth governing, it filled California with a population faster than any government could be built for it, and it put so many wagons across Indian hunting grounds that the government's twenty-year-old policy of keeping the two peoples apart stopped being workable.","code":"P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: The factors encouraging migration"},{"h":"The Oregon Trail and the Donner Party: what the journey actually cost","t":"The Oregon Trail ran roughly two thousand miles from Independence, Missouri to the Willamette valley. Missionaries led by Marcus and Narcissa Whitman got a wagon, cut down to a cart on the way, as far as Fort Boise in 1836, proving the route could be driven; the Great Migration of 1843 took about a thousand people and a hundred and twenty wagons over it in five months and opened the floodgates. The timing was brutal arithmetic: leave too early and there is no grass for the oxen, leave too late and the Sierra Nevada or the Blue Mountains close with snow before you are through. The commonest killer was not attack but disease, above all cholera, with drownings at river crossings and accidents with wagons and guns close behind. The Donner Party is the case the examiner expects you to know. About eighty-seven people, mainly from Illinois, left in May 1846, and at Fort Bridger they took Lansford Hastings's untested cut-off on his promise that it saved hundreds of miles. It cost them weeks in the Wasatch Mountains and the salt desert instead, and early snow trapped them near Truckee Lake in the Sierra Nevada in November. Thirty-nine of them died before the last were brought out in the spring of 1847, and some of the survivors had eaten the dead. A top-level answer uses the party as evidence about decisions, not as a horror story: the fatal cause was a timetable lost to bad information, which is exactly the risk every emigrant on the trail was running.","code":"P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: Early migration to c1850"},{"h":"The Mormons: from Nauvoo to the Great Salt Lake, 1844-47","t":"Joseph Smith founded the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints in 1830, and after being driven out of Ohio and Missouri the Mormons built Nauvoo in Illinois from 1839 until it held over ten thousand people, with its own charter and its own militia. Their wealth, their bloc voting and Smith's teaching of plural marriage made them hated by their neighbours. A mob killed Smith and his brother Hyrum in Carthage jail on 27 June 1844, and the state took away the Nauvoo charter the following January, leaving the town with no legal standing. Brigham Young concluded that there was nowhere inside the United States they would be left alone, and in February 1846 he led the first wagons across the frozen Mississippi. The migration was organised rather than improvised: about five hundred men enlisted in the Mormon Battalion for the war with Mexico and sent their pay back to the church, the main body wintered in prepared camps at Winter Quarters on the Missouri, and an advance company of about a hundred and fifty reached the valley of the Great Salt Lake on 24 July 1847. There they dug irrigation channels from City Creek to farm ground nobody else wanted, set up the Perpetual Emigrating Fund in 1849 to bring others out, and from 1856 sent handcart companies - two of which, the Willie and Martin companies, were caught by snow in Wyoming with over two hundred dead. A top-level answer contrasts them with the Donner Party, which had cut the road through the Wasatch that the Mormon pioneers then used but went on towards California and lost thirty-nine of about eighty-seven people in the snows of the Sierra Nevada, a range the Mormons never had to cross because they stopped in the Salt Lake basin: the difference in outcome came from travelling under one authority with supply bases along the route, not from an easier road.","code":"P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: Early migration to c1850"},{"h":"Arriving is not settling: the problems of early settlement","t":"The journey was only the first problem. Emigrants who reached Oregon or California arrived exhausted, often having abandoned tools and furniture to lighten the wagons, and with the season half gone. On the Plains themselves, where the later homesteaders would go, there was almost no timber for houses, fences or fuel, water lay deep and rivers ran dry, and the weather swung between drought, prairie fire, hailstorm and blizzard. The Mormons at Salt Lake had to invent an agriculture for a desert basin, dividing the water by community rule rather than private right, and were nearly destroyed by crickets in 1848. In the mining districts the problem was different again: the camps were instant towns of tents and shacks, wildly overcrowded, where food and tools cost many times the eastern price and a man who found nothing had no way home. Everywhere, settlement outran government. Territories were vast, marshals were few, and the nearest court might be weeks away. A top-level answer makes the connection the examiner is looking for: because the West was settled faster than it was administered, the same shortage explains the sod house, the vigilance committee and the broken treaty, and that is why so many of the option's later conflicts start here.","code":"P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: The development and problems of early settlement"},{"h":"A permanent frontier that lasted twenty years, and the two measures of 1851","t":"Government policy began by trying to separate the two peoples. The Indian Removal Act of 1830 pushed the eastern nations across the Mississippi - the Cherokee march of 1838 to 1839 became known as the Trail of Tears - and the Indian Trade and Intercourse Act of 1834 fixed what was called the Permanent Indian Frontier at roughly the ninety-fifth meridian, backed by a military road and a line of forts. The land beyond it was thought worthless, the so-called Great American Desert, so giving it away for ever cost nothing. Then the whites crossed it. Wagons went up the Oregon Trail from 1836 and in thousands from 1843, and after 1849 the forty-niners turned a stream into a flood, stripping grass and timber along the routes, scattering the herds and bringing cholera and measles. A frontier line was useless once the traffic was going through it, so in 1851 Congress tried two new things at once. The Indian Appropriations Act voted money to negotiate with the western nations and settle them on reservations set aside on public land. In September, at Horse Creek near Fort Laramie, the first Fort Laramie Treaty was signed with eight nations, including the Sioux, Cheyenne, Arapaho and Crow: each was given a defined territory, all agreed to allow roads and forts and to let travellers pass in safety, and the United States promised goods worth fifty thousand dollars a year for fifty years, which the Senate promptly cut to ten. A top-level answer explains why the settlement was fragile from the start: chiefs could not bind bands that had not been there, hunting had always crossed the lines the map now drew, and the annuities often came late or short - so within a few years the treaty was mainly producing a legal reason to call people treaty-breakers.","code":"P3-1 — 3 Conflict and tension: Reasons for tension with Indigenous peoples of the Plains"},{"h":"Nobody in charge: lawlessness in the camps and the vigilance committees","t":"The mining West was lawless for structural reasons, not because the people in it were unusually wicked. Camps appeared in weeks on land with no courts, no jails and no police; a territory might have a single United States marshal for an area the size of Britain, and sheriffs were elected by counties that barely existed. Claims were the commonest cause of trouble, because a claim was held by working it and jumping one was easy. Add a young, armed, mostly male population, gold that could be carried away in a bag, and enormous distances from any authority, and the result was theft, claim-jumping, saloon killings and the racial violence expressed in the Foreign Miners' Tax of 1850, which charged non-citizen miners twenty dollars a month and was repealed in March 1851 after protests and a collapse in revenue. The answer people reached for was to act themselves. In San Francisco, where an Australian gang known as the Sydney Ducks was blamed for robbery and arson, a Committee of Vigilance was formed on 9 June 1851 with about seven hundred members and hanged four men; a second committee, formed on 14 May 1856 after James Casey shot the newspaper editor James King of William, enrolled several thousand and hanged four more. A top-level answer weighs them rather than praising or condemning them: they restored order quickly because they were faster than the courts, and they undermined it for the same reason, since the argument that respectable men may hang a prisoner when the law is slow was available to anyone who thought himself respectable.","code":"P3-1 — 3 Conflict and tension: Lawlessness in early towns and settlements"}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1(a): one consequence of the cholera that followed the trails","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(a): 'Explain one consequence of the spread of cholera along the overland trails in 1849.' [4 marks]. Question 1 is printed in two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each asking for one consequence of a separate event or development and each marked on its own out of 4, so this part needs a single consequence analysed and supported. The information you are working from: cholera returned to the United States in 1848 and travelled west with the emigrants of 1849; it was carried in water fouled by human waste, and company after company camped and drew water at the same few springs and creeks along the Platte; historians of the trail reckon that some thousands of emigrants, perhaps as many as five thousand, died of it in 1849, buried in graves dug beside the road in what are now Kansas, Nebraska and Wyoming; the Pawnee are thought to have lost more than a thousand people and the Cheyenne a large part of their number in the same seasons; the Plains nations had no explanation for the disease except the wagons that brought it; that same traffic was already stripping the grass and timber and scattering the buffalo along the corridor; the council that produced the first Fort Laramie Treaty met in September 1851.","steps":["Decide whose consequence you are tracing, because the question does not say: the emigrants passed through, while the nations of the Platte valley lived beside the road all year.","State it as a change rather than an event: after 1849 the wagon trains were not merely a nuisance along the corridor but a danger to life, so objection to them hardened into grievance.","Support it precisely: the Pawnee are reckoned to have lost more than a thousand people and the Cheyenne a large part of their number, in the same valley whose grass, timber and buffalo the traffic was already taking.","Make the link the grid pays for: because a nation that had lost that many people could watch the cause come up the Platte again every summer, the pressure to settle terms for the trail was building on both sides of it at once, and two summers later it produced a treaty council."],"cues":["Who is most affected by a disease carried along a road that runs through somebody else's hunting grounds?","What did the wagon trains become, in the eyes of the nations along the Platte, that they had not been before?","Which figures here can you use as proof, and what else were those nations losing at the same time?","Why does a consequence of this kind end in a treaty council rather than a battle?"]},{"title":"Question 2: choosing the spine of a narrative account on the journeys of the forty-niners","setup":"Booklet P, Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the journeys made by the forty-niners to California in the years 1848-49.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: President Polk's message to Congress (December 1848); the voyage round Cape Horn. You must also use information of your own. The cap printed in the mark scheme is 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' The information you are working from: James Marshall found gold at Sutter's Mill in January 1848, and for most of that year the reports were half disbelieved outside California; President Polk confirmed them in his message to Congress in December 1848; about eighty to ninety thousand people reached California during 1849; there were three ways to go - round Cape Horn, a voyage of some five to eight months from the eastern ports; across the Isthmus of Panama, by boat up the Chagres river and then by mule to Panama City, followed by a wait for a steamer on the Pacific side; or overland on the California Trail; historians' estimates of the overland share of 1849 differ, the lowest being about twenty-five thousand, but even that was more than the trails had carried in the whole of the previous decade; an overland company could not start until the grass grew in late April and had to be over the Sierra Nevada before the snow closed the passes; cholera travelled up the Platte with the wagons.","steps":["Fix the two ends before writing a word: the account opens with a find nobody outside California believed and closes with tens of thousands of people arriving there in a single year, so every episode has to move somebody along one of the routes.","Episode one is the news itself - a strike in January 1848 that stayed a rumour until the president repeated it to Congress in December, which is why the rush is named after 1849 and not 1848.","Join the episodes instead of listing them: an official confirmation turned a rumour into something worth selling a farm for, which is what made a family choose a route at all.","Episode two is that choice, and this is where your own material has to appear: the sea routes were open all year, so Cape Horn and Panama carried a large share of the eighty to ninety thousand, while an overland company had to wait for grass in late April and beat the snow over the Sierra Nevada, and even the lowest estimate of the overland share, about twenty-five thousand, was more than the trails had carried in the previous ten years together.","Close by saying what the sequence amounted to rather than repeating it: in one season a possession the United States had held for less than two years was reached by three separate routes and filled faster than any government could be built for it."],"cues":["What are the two ends of this story, and what has to happen in between?","Why is the rush named after 1849 when the gold was found in 1848?","Why should an announcement in Congress change what an ordinary family decided to do?","Which route would you take in January 1849, and what does the calendar do to that choice?","What was true of California by the end of 1849 that had not been true at the start of 1848?"]},{"title":"Question 3: analysing the importance of Fort Laramie for the overland trails","setup":"Booklet P, Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8, with no stimulus points and no cap. Work this bullet: 'The importance of Fort Laramie for the overland trails.' [8 marks]. The information you are working from: William Sublette and Robert Campbell built a fur-trading post of cottonwood logs, called Fort William, where the Laramie meets the North Platte, in 1834; it passed to the American Fur Company, which rebuilt it in adobe as Fort John in 1841; it stood roughly a third of the way along the Oregon Trail from Independence, Missouri; emigrant companies stopped there to repair wagons, trade for supplies and lighten their loads, and it was where a party found out whether it was still on time for the mountains; the United States Army bought the post in June 1849, in the first summer of the gold rush, and garrisoned it to protect the emigrants; the treaty council of September 1851 was summoned to Fort Laramie but had to move to Horse Creek, some thirty-five miles away, because there was not enough grass at the fort for the thousands of horses that came.","steps":["Set the test before you write: importance asks what difference something made afterwards, so judge the post by what the trail was like with it and what it would have been like without it.","Take its first importance to the emigrants: a supply point a third of the way out turned one desperate dash into a journey with a staging post, where wagons were mended, worn oxen replaced and loads cut down before the mountains.","Support that with the change of hands in June 1849: the army bought a fur trader's post in the first summer of the rush precisely because the traffic had made the trail a national responsibility rather than a private venture.","Trace the consequence forward, which is what the grid means by analysis of importance: a garrison in the middle of the Plains gave the government somewhere permanent to keep soldiers, count the wagons and summon the nations, so the trail had a policeman before it had a policy.","Weigh the other side so the judgement is earned: the post could not make good what was promised from it - the treaty council summoned to it had to move thirty-five miles away to Horse Creek simply for want of grass - so its importance lies in making the journey survivable and the Plains governable on paper rather than in controlling either."],"cues":["What does the word importance actually ask you to answer?","What does a traveller need at the point where the hardest part of the journey is still ahead?","Why would the army pay for a fur trader's post in 1849 of all years?","What can a government do from a permanent post that it cannot do from Washington?","What could the fort not do, and how should that limit your judgement?"]},{"title":"Question 1(b): one consequence of the way the Plains nations made war","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(b): 'Explain one consequence of the way the Plains nations made war.' [4 marks]. Each part of Question 1 is marked on its own two-level grid out of 4, so this part needs a single consequence analysed and supported by specific information. The information you are working from: war on the Plains was made by small raiding parties, which formed when a warrior proposed one and others chose to go with him; the usual purposes were horses, revenge and honour rather than the conquest of ground; the highest honour was counting coup, touching a living enemy and getting away, which was rated above killing him; wealth and standing were counted in horses taken; a chief led by persuasion and could not order a band either to fight or to stop; decisions were reached in council and nobody was compelled by them; the nations of the Plains raided one another as readily as they raided outsiders.","steps":["Read the material as one system rather than a list of customs: the purpose of a raid, the honour of counting coup and the chief's lack of power all point the same way.","Pick a consequence that follows from the system rather than another custom: because war was made for horses and honour by parties that came together for a single raid, the Plains nations never built anything that could fight a long campaign.","Support it with the details that prove it - counting coup rated above killing, standing counted in horses taken, a chief who could persuade but not order, and nations that raided each other as readily as outsiders.","Explain why that mattered, which is the analysis the grid pays for: when the United States arrived with soldiers who fought to hold ground and with treaties that assumed a chief could bind everyone he spoke for, this way of war was at a double disadvantage, because it could neither sustain a campaign nor deliver the obedience the treaties took for granted."],"cues":["What do these customs have in common?","What kind of fighting is a system like this good at, and what is it not built for?","Which details prove that the raid, and not the campaign, was the unit of war?","What happens when an army and a treaty both assume something this system cannot provide?"]},{"title":"Question 2: supplying the joins a Level 2 narrative leaves out","setup":"Booklet P, Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the growth of lawlessness in California in the years 1848-56.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the mining camps; the San Francisco vigilance committee of 1851. You must also use information of your own. Here are four sentences a student has written, in this order: 'Gold was found at Sutter's Mill in 1848. Thousands of men came to California to look for gold. The camps had no proper law officers. In 1851 a vigilance committee was set up in San Francisco and it hanged four men.' Every sentence is accurate. The grid awards Level 3 only where the account analyses the linkage between the events, so this answer sits in Level 2, and your job is to supply the missing joins.","steps":["Name the fault precisely: the sentences are true and in the right order, but nothing between them explains why each one made the next more likely.","Join the first pair by supplying the motive - a find reported by the president to Congress turned a rumour into a promise, which is why men sold up and travelled thousands of miles on the strength of it.","Join the second pair by supplying the mismatch: camps grew in weeks on ground that had no courts, no jails and far too few marshals for the distances, so a quarrel over a claim had nobody to settle it.","Join the third pair by supplying the trigger and the argument: when an Australian gang known as the Sydney Ducks was blamed for robbery and arson, merchants who had given up on the courts formed a committee of their own, and its hangings were at once an answer to lawlessness and an example of it.","Finish with what the sequence amounted to instead of a summary: a territory had acquired a population far faster than it acquired a government, and private citizens filled the gap on their own authority."],"cues":["What do these four sentences have, and what do they lack?","Why did so many men believe the reports enough to leave home?","Why could the existing law not keep pace with the camps?","What made merchants decide the courts would not do?","What had California gained by the end, and what had it still not got?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What could a chief on the Plains order his band to do?","a":"Nothing. He led by persuasion, chosen for wisdom, generosity and a record in war; decisions were reached in council and no one was compelled to follow them."},{"q":"Which two measures of 1851 changed United States policy towards the Plains nations, and what did each do?","a":"The Indian Appropriations Act voted money to settle western nations on reservations; the first Fort Laramie Treaty, signed in September at Horse Creek, gave eight nations defined territories in return for safe passage, roads and forts."},{"q":"Why did the Donner Party become trapped, and where?","a":"They lost weeks taking Lansford Hastings's untested cut-off from Fort Bridger, so early snow caught them near Truckee Lake in the Sierra Nevada in November 1846."},{"q":"In a narrative account, what has to sit between one episode and the next?","a":"A sentence explaining why the first made the second possible or likely - the link, not merely the order."}]},"room":{"facts":["Booklet P is worth 32 marks and has three questions: Question 1, printed in two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each 'Explain one consequence of...' for 4 marks and each on a separate event or development; Question 2, 'Write a narrative account analysing...', 8 marks; Question 3, 'Explain two of the following: the importance of ... for ...', two chosen parts at 8 marks each, 16 in all.","Only Question 2 prints stimulus points, as two bullets followed by 'You must also use information of your own.' The cap printed in the mark scheme is: 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' There is no cap at Level 2. Questions 1 and 3 print no bullets and carry no cap.","Each part of Question 1 is marked on its own two-level grid out of 4: Level 2 (3-4) needs features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific information added in support. Two descriptions of the event, however accurate, stay in Level 1.","Question 3 is marked twice on a three-level grid out of 8, and Level 3 (6-8) needs analysis of importance in a coherent, logically structured line of reasoning: what difference the event, person or law made to later situations and developments, not what happened at the time.","Timing: Paper 2 lasts 1 hour 50 minutes for 64 marks, about 55 minutes a booklet, so booklet P's 32 marks get roughly a minute a mark with planning on top - about 12 minutes on Question 1's two parts, 13 on Question 2 and 14 on each part of Question 3, leaving a couple of minutes to check. There are no sources on this paper and no marks for spelling, punctuation and grammar."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the belief in Manifest Destiny. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2, marked on the 4-mark consequence grid. Level 2 (3-4) requires features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific support. Credit the moral licence it gave to taking Indian land, the surge of emigration on the Oregon and California trails, or the political pressure for annexation and expansion to the Pacific.","sol":"One consequence was that taking land from the Plains nations could be presented as a duty rather than a theft. O'Sullivan's article of 1845 claimed the nation was allotted the continent by Providence, and once that was the common language, a treaty that moved a people off their hunting grounds looked like the fulfilment of a destiny instead of a seizure. That is why politicians could argue for pushing past the Permanent Indian Frontier without embarrassment, and why later removals met so little resistance in Congress.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["A belief does not move wagons by itself, so ask what it changed in the way Americans talked about land that was not theirs.","Look for the effect on government and on public argument as well as on individual families deciding to go.","Name the consequence, quote the substance of the 1845 claim as support, then explain why that language made expansion easier to justify."],"codes":["P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: The factors encouraging migration"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of the discovery of gold at Sutter's Mill in January 1848. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the mass migration of the forty-niners in 1849, the explosive growth of San Francisco and the camps, California's admission as a state in 1850, or the collapse of order in the diggings.","sol":"One consequence was that California acquired a population faster than it acquired a government. About eighty thousand forty-niners arrived during 1849, and San Francisco grew from roughly a thousand people to about twenty-five thousand in a year, while the camps that sprang up had no courts, no jails and almost no marshals. Because settlement outran administration so completely, disputes over claims and thefts of gold had nobody to settle them, and within three and a half years of the strike San Francisco merchants had formed a Committee of Vigilance, in June 1851, and were hanging men themselves.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The consequence you want is about people and institutions, not about how much gold was dug.","Compare the speed at which the population grew with the speed at which courts and officers could be provided.","State the consequence, give the two population figures as proof, then explain what that gap made possible."],"codes":["P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: The factors encouraging migration","P3-1 — 3 Conflict and tension: Lawlessness in early towns and settlements"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the lack of timber and water on the Great Plains. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the reputation of the Plains as the 'Great American Desert' and the decision of emigrants to cross rather than settle them, the concentration of early settlement at the far ends of the trails in Oregon, California and the Salt Lake basin, or the government's willingness to promise land it thought worthless to the Indian nations.","sol":"One consequence was that the Plains were crossed rather than settled. Stephen Long's expedition of 1820 reported the country unfit for cultivation and his map of 1823 labelled it the Great American Desert, and a family that needed timber for a house and a fence, and water within reach of a well, could see nothing there worth stopping for. Because the emigrants of the 1840s pushed on instead to the Willamette valley, to California or to the Salt Lake basin, early settlement grew at the far ends of the trails, and a government that believed the Plains worthless could promise them to the Indian nations for ever.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what a farming family looked for before it agreed to stop, and which of those things the Plains could not offer.","The explorers are your evidence: think about what the country was called after 1820, and who believed the label.","Name the consequence, support it with what the expedition reported and with where the emigrants settled instead, then explain the link between them."],"codes":["P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: The development and problems of early settlement"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of the 'Permanent Indian Frontier'. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the concentration of eastern and Plains nations west of the line, the building of the military road and forts along it, or the collapse of the policy once the trails and the Gold Rush carried settlers straight through it.","sol":"One consequence was that the government created a promise it could not keep. The Indian Trade and Intercourse Act of 1834 fixed the line at roughly the ninety-fifth meridian and backed it with a military road and a chain of forts, on the assumption that the Plains beyond were a desert nobody would want. Because wagons began using the Oregon Trail from 1836 and crossed in thousands after 1843, and the forty-niners turned that into a flood, the army found itself guarding a boundary its own citizens were pouring through, and the word permanent became an obvious broken pledge.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The consequence you want is what the line did once people started crossing it, not what it was meant to do.","Think about what the army was actually asked to do, and about who was breaking the boundary.","State the consequence, date the traffic that broke the line, then explain why that destroyed the policy's credibility."],"codes":["P3-1 — 3 Conflict and tension: Reasons for tension with Indigenous peoples of the Plains"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the dependence of the Plains Indians on the buffalo. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the nomadic way of life and the portable tipi, the band structure and seasonal movement, the ceremonial and spiritual importance of the herds, or the vulnerability of the whole society to anything that disturbed the herds.","sol":"One consequence was that the whole society had to be portable. Because the herds moved and the people had to follow, a band lived in tipis of buffalo hide over wooden poles that could be struck and loaded onto a travois in minutes, owned only what a horse could carry, and travelled in groups small enough to feed from one hunt. That dependence also made them fragile in a way settled peoples were not: the buffalo supplied meat, hides, sinew, tools and fuel at once, so anything that scattered the herds - the wagon traffic on the trails from the 1840s, for one - threatened every part of life simultaneously.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Start from the fact that the herds moved, and ask what that forced the people who lived off them to be like.","Think about what a household could own if everything had to travel with it.","Name the consequence, support it with the tipi and the travois, then explain why depending on one animal for so many things was dangerous."],"codes":["P3-1 — 1 Indigenous peoples of the Plains: Social and tribal structures, ways of life and means of survival"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Oregon Trail becoming a regular route for settlers from 1836. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the first Fort Laramie Treaty of 1851. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4 on the consequence grid, so each part needs one consequence analysed with specific support. In 1(a) credit the mass migration of the 1840s, the strain on grass, timber, water and the herds, the spread of cholera, or the undermining of the Permanent Indian Frontier. In 1(b) credit the division of the Plains into defined territories, the legal basis it gave for calling bands treaty-breakers, the roads and forts allowed, or the resentment caused when the Senate cut the annuity and the goods failed to arrive.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that the Plains stopped being empty in the government's own account of them. Marcus and Narcissa Whitman's party got a wagon, cut down to a cart on the way, as far as Fort Boise in 1836, proving the route could be driven, and the Great Migration of 1843 carried about a thousand people and a hundred and twenty wagons to Oregon in five months. Because that traffic ate the grass, cut the timber and scattered the herds along a corridor that ran straight through the land promised to the Indian nations in 1834, the Permanent Indian Frontier was being destroyed by American citizens rather than by any enemy, and the government had to find a new policy. 1(b) One consequence was that the government gained a legal reason to use soldiers. The treaty of September 1851 gave each of the eight signing nations a defined territory and bought safe passage, roads and forts with an annuity of fifty thousand dollars a year, which the Senate then cut from fifty years to ten. Because chiefs could not bind bands that had not been present, and hunting had always crossed the lines the map now drew, ordinary hunting became trespass on paper, so every season produced new evidence that the Indians had broken an agreement and new grounds for sending troops.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate parts on two separate things: a route in 1(a) and a treaty in 1(b), so neither answer can do the other's work.","For 1(a) follow the damage the wagons did along the corridor rather than what happened at the Oregon end; for 1(b) ask what the boundaries allowed the government to say about people who crossed them.","Each part in three moves: name the consequence, give the dated detail that proves it, then explain the chain back to the event."],"codes":["P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: The factors encouraging migration","P3-1 — 3 Conflict and tension: Reasons for tension with Indigenous peoples of the Plains"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Mormon settlement of the Salt Lake valley from 1847. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the Indian Appropriations Act of 1851. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each on the 4-mark consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the proof that the desert could be farmed with irrigation, the organised emigration that followed through the Perpetual Emigrating Fund and the handcart companies, or the creation of a self-governing Mormon community and Utah Territory in 1850. In 1(b) credit the move from separation to containment on reservations, the dependence on government rations, or the money it provided for treaty-making, including the first Fort Laramie Treaty.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that the arid West was shown to be farmable. The advance company reached the valley on 24 July 1847 and immediately dug channels from City Creek, dividing the water by community rule rather than private right, and the settlement survived the crickets of 1848 and grew. Because a desert basin nobody else wanted had been made to yield crops, the church could bring thousands more out behind them - the Perpetual Emigrating Fund from 1849, handcart companies from 1856 - and Salt Lake City became the supply town for travellers crossing to California. 1(b) One consequence was that government policy changed from keeping the two peoples apart to confining one of them. The Act voted money to negotiate with the western nations and settle them on reserved land that was smaller than the ranges they had hunted, in return for promised food and supplies. Because a hunting people now had to wait for deliveries from an agency, and those deliveries were repeatedly late or short, hunger itself pushed bands off the reservations, which in turn gave the army its reason to pursue them.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["In 1(a) the consequence is about what other people learned from the Mormons, not only about how the Mormons themselves lived.","In 1(b) the Act is a change of principle: separation was the old answer, so what is the new one?","Each part in three moves: consequence, precise dated support, then the explanation of the link."],"codes":["P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: Early migration to c1850","P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: The development and problems of early settlement","P3-1 — 3 Conflict and tension: Reasons for tension with Indigenous peoples of the Plains"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the arrival of the forty-niners in California in 1849. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the shortage of law officers in the early mining camps. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts on the 4-mark consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the explosion of San Francisco and the camps, statehood in 1850, the racial hostility expressed in the Foreign Miners' Tax of 1850, or the destruction of Indigenous Californian communities. In 1(b) credit claim-jumping and unsettled disputes, the rise of miners' courts and vigilance committees, or the lasting habit of settling matters privately.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that California became a state within two years of the forty-niners' arrival, on 9 September 1850. Roughly eighty thousand people arrived during 1849 and San Francisco grew from about a thousand inhabitants to about twenty-five thousand, so an army officer administering a conquered province was plainly no longer adequate. Because Congress had to admit California in 1850 to give it courts and a legislature, the far West entered national politics far earlier than anyone had planned, and the newcomers' hostility to outsiders was written into law in the Foreign Miners' Tax of the same year. 1(b) One consequence was that miners made their own law. A territory might have a single United States marshal for an area the size of Britain, and the nearest court could be weeks away, while a claim was held simply by working it and was easy to jump. Because there was no officer to settle a disputed claim or arrest a thief, camps ran their own miners' meetings and San Francisco merchants formed a Committee of Vigilance in June 1851 that hanged four men, which restored order quickly but established that private citizens might hang a prisoner whenever they judged the courts too slow.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["In 1(a) look for a consequence that a figure can prove, and remember that government is a consequence as much as gold is.","In 1(b) start from the distances and the way a claim was held, and ask what that made inevitable.","Each part in three moves: consequence, precise support, then the explanation - and in 1(b) say what the remedy cost as well as what it achieved."],"codes":["P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: The factors encouraging migration","P3-1 — 3 Conflict and tension: Lawlessness in early towns and settlements"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Plains Indians' belief that land could not be owned. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the Indian Removal Act of 1830. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts on the 4-mark consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the mismatch with treaties that asked for permanent cessions, the absence of fences and private holdings, or the different meanings each side attached to the same signature. In 1(b) credit the forced movement of the eastern nations across the Mississippi and the Trail of Tears, the crowding of nations onto Plains land, or the creation of the Permanent Indian Frontier that followed.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that treaties meant different things to the two sides. Because the earth was held to be alive and shared, and a nation had lands it hunted and defended rather than land it held as property, a chief's mark on a document could not be understood by his people as selling the ground for ever. The Fort Laramie Treaty of 1851 illustrates it: the United States believed it had bought boundaries, while bands that had not been at Horse Creek went on hunting where the herds led them, so the government read a genuine difference of belief as deliberate bad faith. 1(b) One consequence was that the Plains filled up with peoples who had not lived there. The Act pushed the eastern nations west of the Mississippi, the Cherokee march of 1838 to 1839 becoming known as the Trail of Tears, and the land they were given was assumed to be a worthless desert. Because so many nations were now concentrated beyond a single line, Congress formalised it in 1834 as the Permanent Indian Frontier, which fixed in law the idea that the two peoples should be kept apart - and made the later crossing of that line by white settlers an obvious broken promise.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["In 1(a) the consequence is about what happened when two different ideas of land met on the same piece of paper.","In 1(b) follow the people who were moved, and then ask what the government did next to keep them there.","Each part in three moves: consequence, named support with a date, then the explanation of why it followed."],"codes":["P3-1 — 1 Indigenous peoples of the Plains: Beliefs about land and nature","P3-1 — 3 Conflict and tension: Reasons for tension with Indigenous peoples of the Plains"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the journey of the Mormons from Nauvoo to the Great Salt Lake in the years 1844-47. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the death of Joseph Smith (1844); Winter Quarters. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) needs material organised into a clear sequence leading to an outcome, with the linkage between events analysed. 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' Credit Carthage and the loss of the charter, the crossing of the Mississippi in February 1846, the Mormon Battalion, Winter Quarters, and the arrival and irrigation of the Salt Lake valley.","sol":"Joseph Smith and his brother Hyrum were killed by a mob at Carthage jail on 27 June 1844, and no one was convicted for it; in January 1845 the Illinois legislature took away Nauvoo's charter, so a town of over ten thousand people lost its courts and its militia at the moment it most needed them. Because the state had removed the church's legal protection rather than supplied it, Brigham Young, who had emerged as leader, concluded that safety lay outside the United States altogether, and in February 1846 the first wagons crossed the frozen Mississippi towards land Mexico still held. That decision turned flight into an operation. About five hundred men enlisted in the Mormon Battalion for the war with Mexico and sent their pay back to the church, which paid for wagons and supplies, and instead of racing the season the main body halted at Winter Quarters on the Missouri, building cabins and planting for those who would follow. Only when the route had been provisioned did an advance company of about a hundred and fifty go forward, reaching the valley of the Great Salt Lake on 24 July 1847 and digging irrigation channels from City Creek at once. The sequence amounted to this: a persecuted church that could not hold a town inside the United States had, within three years, planted a self-governing settlement in a desert basin nobody else wanted.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The dates fix the ends of the account, so decide first which four episodes carry the story between them.","Both bullets are given to you, so make sure at least two of your episodes are your own material.","Between each pair of episodes write one sentence that says why the first made the second more likely, and end by saying what the whole sequence amounted to."],"codes":["P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: Early migration to c1850","P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: The development and problems of early settlement"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the growth of tension between the US government and the Plains Indians in the years c1830-1851. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the 'Permanent Indian Frontier'; the Indian Appropriations Act (1851). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) needs a clear sequence leading to an outcome with the linkage analysed. 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' Credit the Indian Removal Act of 1830 and the Trail of Tears, the Act of 1834 and the line of forts, the traffic on the Oregon Trail from 1836 and 1843, the Gold Rush of 1849, and the two measures of 1851.","sol":"The Indian Removal Act of 1830 pushed the eastern nations across the Mississippi, the Cherokee march of 1838 to 1839 becoming the Trail of Tears, and in 1834 the Indian Trade and Intercourse Act fixed what was called the Permanent Indian Frontier at roughly the ninety-fifth meridian, guarded by a military road and a chain of forts. The promise was cheap to make because the Plains were believed to be a desert. That assumption is what broke first. Once missionaries had got a wagon along the Oregon Trail as far as Fort Boise in 1836 and the Great Migration of 1843 had taken about a thousand people over it, the corridor was in constant use, and after President Polk confirmed the gold strike in December 1848 more emigrants crossed in 1849 alone than in the whole previous decade. Because those wagons stripped the grass and timber, scattered the herds and carried cholera into the camps along the route, the Plains nations were losing the basis of their life to people the government had promised would never come, while the government was left guarding a line its own citizens were breaking. That is why 1851 produced two answers at once: the Indian Appropriations Act voted money to settle the western nations on reserved land, and in September the first Fort Laramie Treaty gave eight nations defined territories in exchange for roads, forts and safe passage. The sequence had turned a policy of separation into a policy of confinement in twenty years.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The account has to end somewhere definite, and 1851 gives you two events that belong at the end rather than in the middle.","The bullets cover the beginning and the end, so your own material is the traffic in between that destroyed the policy.","Between each episode write one sentence explaining why it made the next more likely, and close by saying how the government's aim had changed by 1851."],"codes":["P3-1 — 3 Conflict and tension: Reasons for tension with Indigenous peoples of the Plains","P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: The factors encouraging migration"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the misfortunes of the Donner Party in the years 1846-47. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the Hastings Cutoff; the Sierra Nevada. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) needs a clear sequence leading to an outcome with the linkage analysed. 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' Credit the departure from Illinois in May 1846, the decision at Fort Bridger, the delays in the Wasatch Mountains and the salt desert, the snow at Truckee Lake in November, the rescue parties of February 1847 and the death toll.","sol":"About eighty-seven people, mostly families from Illinois, set out in May 1846 for California, already on a timetable that left no margin, since the Sierra Nevada had to be crossed before the snow closed the passes. At Fort Bridger they met the promise that undid them: Lansford Hastings's guidebook advertised a cut-off south of the Great Salt Lake that would save hundreds of miles, and because Hastings had barely travelled it himself there was nobody to warn them what the ground was like. The route cost them weeks instead of saving them any. Hacking a wagon road through the Wasatch Mountains and then crossing the salt desert killed oxen, wrecked wagons and consumed the margin they had needed, so they reached the mountains late, and heavy early snow trapped them near Truckee Lake at the beginning of November 1846. From there the misfortunes compounded: with the cattle dead or lost under the drifts, a small party that tried to walk out over the pass took a month to bring word, and the first relief did not reach the camps until 18 February 1847. Thirty-nine of the party died before the last were carried out that spring, and some of the survivors had eaten the dead. The sequence shows that what killed them was not the wilderness itself but a lost timetable, bought with bad information.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Begin with the timetable rather than the people, because every later misfortune follows from time lost.","Both bullets are given, so your own material is the decision at Fort Bridger and what the cut-off actually cost them.","Link each stage to the next with a sentence of explanation, and finish by saying what the sequence shows about the risks of the overland journey."],"codes":["P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: Early migration to c1850","P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: The development and problems of early settlement"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the California Gold Rush for the settlement of the West. [8 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 on the 8-mark importance grid; no stimulus points and no cap. Level 3 (6-8) needs analysis of importance in a coherent line of reasoning. Credit the scale and speed of migration, statehood in 1850, the growth of San Francisco and the mining towns, the supply trades and the routes that grew to serve them, and the increased pressure on the Plains nations.","sol":"The Gold Rush mattered because it moved the far West from the edge of American life to the centre of it in about two years. Before 1848 migration west was a slow family business aimed at farmland in Oregon; after President Polk confirmed the strike in December 1848, roughly eighty thousand people arrived in California during 1849 alone, and San Francisco grew from about a thousand people to about twenty-five thousand in a year. The importance of that is not the gold but what a population of that size required. Congress could no longer administer California as conquered territory, so it was admitted as a state in 1850, which gave the far West courts, a legislature and a voice in national politics decades earlier than anyone had expected. Settlement also changed shape. The forty-niners were mostly young single men who meant to leave rich, so the money was made as often by those who supplied them as by those who dug, and towns, freight routes and later railways grew along the lines of supply. Finally, the rush was important for people who had nothing to do with gold: the traffic crossing the Plains multiplied, wrecking grass, timber and the herds along the trails, and it was that pressure, more than any policy argument, which forced the government into the reservation policy and the treaties of 1851. In short, the rush converted a distant possession into a settled, governed and contested region.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Importance asks what difference it made afterwards, so decide first what the West was like before 1848 and what it was like by the early 1850s.","Take three different kinds of difference - to government, to the pattern of settlement and trade, and to the Plains nations.","Open by naming the test you are judging by, give each difference its own dated evidence, and close by saying which mattered most and why."],"codes":["P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: The factors encouraging migration","P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: The development and problems of early settlement","P3-1 — 3 Conflict and tension: Reasons for tension with Indigenous peoples of the Plains"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of Brigham Young for the survival of the Mormons in the West. [8 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 on the 8-mark importance grid; no stimulus points and no cap. Credit his succession after Smith's death, the decision to leave the United States, the organisation of the migration in companies with Winter Quarters and the Mormon Battalion's pay, the choice and irrigation of the Salt Lake valley, and the later machinery of emigration and government.","sol":"Young's importance is best judged by comparing what the Mormons did under him with what happened to migrants who went west without that kind of authority. After Smith was killed at Carthage in June 1844 the church could have broken up; Young held the majority together and made the decision that decided everything else, that no place inside the United States would protect them, so they would go beyond it. He then turned a flight into a planned operation. The first wagons crossed the Mississippi in February 1846, but instead of racing for the mountains the main body stopped at Winter Quarters on the Missouri to build and plant, about five hundred men enlisted in the Mormon Battalion and sent their pay back to fund the wagons, and only an advance company of about a hundred and fifty went forward to reach the Salt Lake valley on 24 July 1847. Set that against the Donner Party, which lost thirty-nine of about eighty-seven people the previous winter in the Sierra Nevada, a range the Mormons never had to cross, for want of good information and time. Young's choice of destination was equally deliberate: a dry basin outside American jurisdiction that nobody else wanted, farmed by irrigation from City Creek with water allocated by the community. He then built the machinery to bring others out, the Perpetual Emigrating Fund from 1849 and handcart companies from 1856. The Mormons survived because one authority planned the route, the funding and the farming together.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["A person's importance is shown by what would probably have happened without them, so look for a comparison you can make.","Group his decisions: leadership after Carthage, the organisation of the journey, the choice of destination, and what he built once there.","State the test you are judging by, give each decision its evidence, and close on which of them was decisive for survival."],"codes":["P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: Early migration to c1850","P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: The development and problems of early settlement"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the first Fort Laramie Treaty (1851) for relations between the US government and the Plains Indians. [8 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 on the 8-mark importance grid; no stimulus points and no cap. Credit the division of the Plains into defined territories, the concession of roads, forts and safe passage, the annuity and the Senate's reduction of it, the limits of the chiefs' authority, and the way the treaty supplied grounds for later accusations of bad faith.","sol":"The treaty matters because it changed what the government could claim, rather than what it could enforce. Signed at Horse Creek near Fort Laramie in September 1851 with eight nations, among them the Sioux, Cheyenne, Arapaho and Crow, it gave each a defined territory for the first time, and in return the nations accepted roads and forts on their land and promised safe passage to travellers, with goods worth fifty thousand dollars a year for fifty years in compensation. Its first importance is that it replaced separation with regulation: the Permanent Indian Frontier had tried to keep the two peoples apart, whereas this admitted that Americans would cross and set terms for it. Its second, and more damaging, importance is that it created boundaries that could be broken. Hunting had always followed the herds across the lines the map now drew, and the men who signed spoke only for the bands present, so ordinary movement became trespass on paper and each season produced fresh evidence of Indian bad faith. Third, the government's own reliability was undermined at once, because the Senate cut the annuity from fifty years to ten and the promised goods repeatedly arrived late or short, so the nations had ceded real rights for payments that shrank. Judged by what it made possible afterwards, the treaty was important less as a peace than as the framework within which later conflict could be justified.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Judge it by what the government could say and do afterwards, not by whether it stopped the fighting.","Take three kinds of importance: what it replaced, what it created that could be broken, and what it did to trust on both sides.","Name the test at the start, evidence each point with a term of the treaty, and finish with a judgement on which consequence mattered most."],"codes":["P3-1 — 3 Conflict and tension: Reasons for tension with Indigenous peoples of the Plains","P3-1 — 1 Indigenous peoples of the Plains: Beliefs about land and nature"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In which month and year was the first Fort Laramie Treaty signed?","a":"September 1851."},{"q":"On what date did the first Mormon company reach the valley of the Great Salt Lake?","a":"24 July 1847."},{"q":"Who found gold at Sutter's Mill, and in which month and year?","a":"James Marshall, in January 1848."},{"q":"How many men did the San Francisco Committee of Vigilance of 1851 hang?","a":"Four."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The tipi","back":"The Plains home: buffalo hides stretched over a cone of wooden poles, with a smoke flap at the top. It could be struck and loaded onto a travois in minutes when the band followed the herds.","code":"P3-1 — 1 Indigenous peoples of the Plains: Social and tribal structures, ways of life and means of survival","open":false},{"front":"Bands, chiefs and councils","back":"A band was a few dozen families who camped and hunted together. A chief was chosen for wisdom, generosity and a war record and could not give orders; decisions were made in council, and warrior societies policed the hunt.","code":"P3-1 — 1 Indigenous peoples of the Plains: Social and tribal structures, ways of life and means of survival","open":false},{"front":"Name one use the Plains Indians made of the buffalo","back":"Meat for food; hides for tipi covers and clothing; bones and horns for tools; sinew for bowstrings and thread; dried dung for fuel.","code":"P3-1 — 1 Indigenous peoples of the Plains: Social and tribal structures, ways of life and means of survival","open":true},{"front":"Counting coup","back":"The highest honour in Plains warfare: touching a living enemy with the hand or a stick and getting away. War was fought for horses, revenge and honour rather than territory, so casualties were usually small.","code":"P3-1 — 1 Indigenous peoples of the Plains: Beliefs about land and nature","open":false},{"front":"Plains beliefs about land and nature","back":"The earth was alive and shared and could not be bought or sold. Nature was a web of relatives, not a stock of resources; places such as the Black Hills were sacred. The Sioux called the spirit power Wakan Tanka.","code":"P3-1 — 1 Indigenous peoples of the Plains: Beliefs about land and nature","open":false},{"front":"Manifest Destiny","back":"The belief that the United States was destined by Providence to spread across the continent. The editor John L. O'Sullivan coined the phrase in 1845 in the United States Magazine and Democratic Review, arguing for the annexation of Texas.","code":"P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: The factors encouraging migration","open":false},{"front":"The Oregon Trail","back":"About 2,000 miles from Independence, Missouri to the Willamette valley. Whitman's missionary party got a wagon as far as Fort Boise in 1836; the Great Migration of 1843 took about 1,000 people and 120 wagons over it in five months.","code":"P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: The factors encouraging migration","open":false},{"front":"The California Gold Rush, 1848-49","back":"James Marshall found gold at Sutter's Mill in January 1848; Polk confirmed it to Congress in December. About 80,000 forty-niners arrived in 1849, San Francisco grew from about 1,000 to about 25,000, and California became a state in 1850.","code":"P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: The factors encouraging migration","open":false},{"front":"The Donner Party, 1846-47","back":"About 87 emigrants took Hastings's untested cut-off from Fort Bridger, lost weeks in the Wasatch Mountains and the salt desert, and were trapped by snow near Truckee Lake in November 1846. Thirty-nine died; some survivors ate the dead.","code":"P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: Early migration to c1850","open":false},{"front":"Nauvoo and the death of Joseph Smith","back":"The Mormons built Nauvoo in Illinois from 1839 until it held over 10,000 people. A mob killed Smith and his brother Hyrum at Carthage jail on 27 June 1844, and the state removed the city charter in January 1845.","code":"P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: Early migration to c1850","open":false},{"front":"Brigham Young and the Great Salt Lake","back":"Young led the wagons over the frozen Mississippi in February 1846 and wintered the main body at Winter Quarters. An advance company of about 150 reached the Salt Lake valley on 24 July 1847 and irrigated from City Creek.","code":"P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: Early migration to c1850","open":false},{"front":"Name one problem faced by early settlers in the West","back":"Disease on the trail, above all cholera; crossing mountains before the snow; no timber, water or fuel on the Plains; drought, fire and blizzard; food and tools priced far above eastern rates; no courts or officers within reach.","code":"P3-1 — 2 Migration and early settlement: The development and problems of early settlement","open":true},{"front":"The 'Permanent Indian Frontier'","back":"Set by the Indian Trade and Intercourse Act of 1834 at roughly the 95th meridian, following the Indian Removal Act of 1830, and guarded by a military road and a line of forts. Wagon traffic from 1836 destroyed it.","code":"P3-1 — 3 Conflict and tension: Reasons for tension with Indigenous peoples of the Plains","open":false},{"front":"The two measures of 1851","back":"The Indian Appropriations Act voted money to settle western nations on reservations. In September the first Fort Laramie Treaty gave eight nations defined territories in return for roads, forts and safe passage, with 50,000 dollars a year promised.","code":"P3-1 — 3 Conflict and tension: Reasons for tension with Indigenous peoples of the Plains","open":false},{"front":"The San Francisco vigilance committees","back":"Formed 9 June 1851 with about 700 members against the Sydney Ducks, hanging four men; re-formed 14 May 1856 after James Casey shot the editor James King of William, with several thousand members, hanging four more.","code":"P3-1 — 3 Conflict and tension: Lawlessness in early towns and settlements","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Paper 2 booklet P Question 2, narrative account, 8 marks)","items":["Question: 'Write a narrative account analysing the beginnings of civil government in California in the years 1848-1850.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the constitutional convention at Monterey (1849); the admission of California as a state (1850). You must also use information of your own. This is the middle stretch of the account, carrying the story from the diggings of 1849 to the writing of a constitution.","Because California had been taken from Mexico in the war and was still being run by army officers when the diggings filled, and because Congress was deadlocked over whether new territory in the West would be slave or free, a population that grew by tens of thousands during 1849 was left with no civil government at all - only the alcaldes inherited from Mexican practice and the miners' meetings that made their own rules claim by claim. That is why the military governor, Bennet Riley, stopped waiting for Washington and called an election for a convention of his own. It sat at Colton Hall in Monterey from 1 September to 13 October 1849, where forty-eight delegates fixed a boundary, forbade slavery and wrote a constitution for a state that did not legally exist. What followed was ratification before permission: the voters approved that constitution in November 1849 and elected a governor and a legislature, so that when Congress admitted California on 9 September 1850, as part of that year's compromise over slavery, it was recognising a government already at work rather than creating one.","Why this is Level 3: the material is organised as a clear sequence leading to an outcome and the linkage between the events is analysed rather than asserted - the deadlock in Congress is explained as the reason there was no government to be had, Riley's decision as the reason a convention met without authority, and the election of a governor as the reason admission in 1850 was a recognition rather than a creation. The knowledge is precise (Riley, Colton Hall, the dates of the convention, the forty-eight delegates, 9 September 1850) and the full answer addresses well over three aspects of content, so the Level 3 cap does not bite."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same, 'Write a narrative account analysing the beginnings of civil government in California in the years 1848-1850.' (8 marks), with the same two bullets.","In 1848 California became part of the United States after the war with Mexico. Gold was found and thousands of people went there to look for it. There was no proper government because the army was in charge and Congress had not decided what to do. In 1849 a convention was held at Monterey and the delegates wrote a constitution, which said there would be no slavery in California. People voted for it and chose a governor. Then in 1850 Congress let California become a state. California now had its own laws and courts, which it had needed for a long time.","Why this is only Level 2, and what it misses: the events are in the right order and the information is accurate, so it meets 'some organisation of material into a sequence of events leading to an outcome' and 'accurate and relevant information is added'. It misses the Level 3 requirement that 'the account of events analyses the linkage between them': nothing explains why Congress could not act, so the convention appears as the next thing that happened rather than as a decision taken because nobody in Washington would take it, and the vote of November 1849 is not connected to the admission of 1850. The detail is loose where the top level wants precision - no military governor named, no dates or place for the convention, no date for statehood - and the last sentence comments on California's needs instead of saying what the sequence amounted to."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Indigenous peoples of the Plains - structures, survival and the buffalo","items":["Nations of the Plains named in this option include the Sioux, Cheyenne, Arapaho, Comanche and Crow.","The band: a few dozen families who camped, hunted and moved together; several bands made a nation, which gathered only at certain seasons.","Chiefs were chosen for wisdom, generosity and a record in war; they advised and persuaded but could not compel.","Decisions were reached in a council of elders; warrior societies, such as the Cheyenne Dog Soldiers, policed the hunt and the camp.","The tipi: buffalo hides over a cone of wooden poles, with a smoke flap; it could be taken down and packed in minutes.","The travois: two poles dragged behind a dog and, later, a horse, carrying the tipi and the household's goods.","Horses came to the continent with the Spanish and reached the Plains through trade and raiding; a man's wealth was counted in horses.","The buffalo supplied meat, hides for covers and clothing, bone and horn for tools, sinew for bowstrings, and dried dung for fuel.","Polygamy was practised: a successful man might have more than one wife, which mattered in a society where warfare and hunting killed men.","A nomadic people could not carry those who could not keep up, so the old and gravely sick were sometimes left behind."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: beliefs about land, nature, war and property","items":["Land could not be owned, bought or sold; a nation had land it hunted and defended, not land it held as property.","Nature was understood as a living web of relatives rather than a stock of resources to be used up.","Particular places were sacred, above all the Black Hills for the Lakota Sioux.","The Sioux name for the great spirit power was Wakan Tanka; contact with the spirits was sought through fasting, dreams and dances.","The Sun Dance was among the most important ceremonies; medicine men interpreted visions and treated the sick.","War was fought for horses, revenge and honour, not for conquest of territory, and casualties were usually small.","Counting coup - touching a living enemy with the hand or a coup stick and escaping - was the highest honour, above killing.","Property was what a household could carry, plus its horses; generosity raised a man's standing far more than hoarding did.","These beliefs explain why treaties meant different things to the two sides: the United States thought it had bought permanent boundaries.","They also explain why bands that had not been present at a signing did not regard themselves as bound by it."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: why white Americans went west - Manifest Destiny, the trails and the gold","items":["Push: the financial panic of 1837 and the depression that followed wrecked farm prices in the Mississippi valley, with foreclosures, unemployment and disease.","Pull: reports of the deep soil and mild climate of Oregon's Willamette valley, and free or cheap land.","The Pre-emption Act of 1841 let a settler who had squatted on surveyed public land buy 160 acres at 1.25 dollars an acre before it went to auction.","Manifest Destiny: the phrase was coined by the editor John L. O'Sullivan in the July-August 1845 issue of the United States Magazine and Democratic Review, arguing for the annexation of Texas.","The Oregon Trail ran about 2,000 miles from Independence, Missouri to the Willamette valley and took roughly five months.","Marcus and Narcissa Whitman's missionary party got a wagon, cut down to a cart on the way, as far as Fort Boise in 1836.","The Great Migration of 1843: about 1,000 people and 120 wagons left Independence in May and opened the trail to mass emigration.","James Marshall found gold at Sutter's Mill on the American River in January 1848; President Polk confirmed it to Congress in December 1848.","Roughly 80,000 forty-niners reached California during 1849, by sea round Cape Horn, across Panama, or overland.","San Francisco grew from about 1,000 people in 1848 to about 25,000 by the end of 1849; California was admitted as a state in 1850."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Donner Party and the Mormon migration, 1846-47","items":["The Donner Party, about 87 people mainly from Illinois, set out in May 1846 for California.","At Fort Bridger they took Lansford Hastings's cut-off south of the Great Salt Lake on his promise that it saved hundreds of miles; he had barely travelled it himself.","The cut-off cost them weeks in the Wasatch Mountains and across the salt desert, killing oxen and wrecking wagons.","Early snow trapped them near Truckee Lake in the Sierra Nevada at the beginning of November 1846.","The first relief party reached the camps on 18 February 1847; 39 of the party died, and some survivors ate the dead.","Joseph Smith founded the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints in 1830 and the Mormons built Nauvoo, Illinois, from 1839 until it held over 10,000 people.","A mob killed Smith and his brother Hyrum at Carthage jail on 27 June 1844; the Illinois legislature removed the Nauvoo charter in January 1845.","Brigham Young led the first wagons across the frozen Mississippi in February 1846; about 500 men enlisted in the Mormon Battalion and sent their pay back to the church.","The main body wintered at Winter Quarters on the Missouri; an advance company of about 150 reached the Salt Lake valley on 24 July 1847 and irrigated from City Creek.","The Perpetual Emigrating Fund was set up in 1849 and handcart companies began in 1856; the Willie and Martin companies were caught by snow in Wyoming that year with over 200 dead."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: government policy, the Permanent Indian Frontier and the treaties of 1851","items":["The Indian Removal Act of 1830 moved the eastern nations west of the Mississippi; the Cherokee march of 1838-39 became known as the Trail of Tears.","The Indian Trade and Intercourse Act of 1834 fixed the 'Permanent Indian Frontier' at roughly the 95th meridian.","The line was backed by a military road and a chain of forts, on the assumption that the Plains were the worthless 'Great American Desert'.","Wagon traffic on the Oregon Trail from 1836, and in thousands from 1843, ran straight through land promised to the Indian nations.","The forty-niners of 1849 multiplied the traffic, stripping grass and timber along the routes, scattering herds and carrying cholera and measles.","The Indian Appropriations Act of 1851 voted money to negotiate with the western nations and settle them on reservations set aside on public land.","The first Fort Laramie Treaty was signed at Horse Creek near Fort Laramie in September 1851 with eight nations, among them the Sioux, Cheyenne, Arapaho and Crow.","Terms: each nation received a defined territory; all agreed to allow roads and forts and to let travellers pass in safety.","In return the United States promised goods worth 50,000 dollars a year for 50 years; the Senate reduced the period to 10 years.","Weaknesses: chiefs could not bind bands that were not present, hunting had always crossed the lines drawn, and the promised goods often came late or short."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: lawlessness in early towns and settlements","items":["Camps appeared in weeks on ground with no courts, no jails and no police, far ahead of any government.","A territory might have a single United States marshal, appointed by the president, for an area the size of Britain; sheriffs were elected by counties that barely existed.","A mining claim was held by working it, so claim-jumping and disputed boundaries were the commonest cause of violence.","The population was young, male, armed and mobile, and gold could be carried away in a bag.","Prices in the camps ran far above eastern levels, and a man who found nothing often had no way home.","The Foreign Miners' Tax of 1850 charged non-citizen miners 20 dollars a month; it was repealed in March 1851 after protests and a collapse in revenue, and replaced by a 3-dollar tax in 1852.","In San Francisco an Australian gang known as the Sydney Ducks was blamed for robbery and arson.","The Committee of Vigilance was formed on 9 June 1851 with about 700 members and hanged four men.","A second committee was formed on 14 May 1856 after James Casey shot the newspaper editor James King of William; it enrolled several thousand members and hanged four more.","The argument both ways: vigilance committees restored order faster than the courts could, but established that private citizens might hang a prisoner whenever they judged the law too slow."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 2 booklet P Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the growth of American settlement in Oregon in the years 1836-1846' (8 marks)","items":["The question as printed: 'Write a narrative account analysing the growth of American settlement in Oregon in the years 1836-1846. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the Whitman mission (1836); the Great Migration of 1843. You must also use information of your own.'","Decode it before writing. 'Narrative account' means a sequence that leads somewhere, so the account must begin and end at definite points; 'analysing' means every join between episodes has to be explained rather than assumed; and the dates are boundaries, so nothing after 1846 belongs here except in the closing sentence.","The cap that decides the level: no access to Level 3 at all for an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content. Both bullets are given to you, so mark which episodes are your own material before you start and make sure those are the ones you develop.","Episode 1, the missionaries, 1836: Marcus and Narcissa Whitman travel out with a fur-trade caravan and found a mission at Waiilatpu among the Cayuse, near what is now Walla Walla, getting a wagon - cut down to a cart on the way - as far as Fort Boise.","Link sentence: what mattered was not the preaching but the proof, because a party that had driven wheels most of the way and written home about the farmland turned Oregon from a fur trader's country into somewhere a family could imagine going.","Episode 2, the Great Migration of 1843 (your own material): about a thousand people with wagons and cattle leave Independence for the Willamette valley, Marcus Whitman travelling with them on his way back to the mission, and the wagons get through to the Columbia.","Link sentence: a thousand arrivals in one season changed the valley from a scattering of settlers into a community with disputes to settle and property to protect, which is why the question of who governed it could no longer be left open.","Episode 3, the provisional government at Champoeg, May 1843 (your own material): settlers meeting in the Willamette valley vote by a narrow majority to set up a government of their own, with laws and officers, although the region is still held jointly with Britain and belongs legally to neither.","Link sentence: an American population governing itself on the ground was the strongest possible argument about who should hold Oregon, so settlement and diplomacy now pulled the same way.","Episode 4, the Oregon Treaty, 15 June 1846: Britain and the United States end the joint occupation and divide the country at the 49th parallel, so the valley the emigrants had settled is American soil and the trail they used runs to American territory.","The evaluation thread that should run through the account: at every stage the settlers moved first and the governments followed - the missionaries before the emigrants, the emigrants before any law, their own government before any treaty - so Oregon was settled into American hands rather than acquired and then settled.","The closing sentence must say what the sequence amounted to rather than repeat it: in ten years a mission station at the end of a fur traders' route had become a farming valley with its own laws and a recognised international boundary drawn around it.","Timing and shape: about 13 minutes - two to pick four episodes and mark which are your own, ten to write, one to check that every join has a sentence of explanation. Four developed episodes beat six thin ones, and use the terms an examiner looks for: emigrant, provisional government, joint occupation, Manifest Destiny, the 49th parallel."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:23:57.172675Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"All four hand fixes are in and correct - question 7 now dates statehood from the forty-niners' arrival and gives 9 September 1850, the Cape Horn voyage reads five to eight months, the 1849 cholera deaths are hedged to 'some thousands, perhaps as many as five thousand', and the Level 3 commentary now claims the aspect count for the full answer rather than the printed extract - and I found no remaining error of date, figure, event, attribution, tariff, level or cap anywhere in the kit; what holds it short of the top is that the plan and both model paragraphs run on Champoeg, the Oregon Treaty and the Colton Hall convention, none of which is specification content for this key topic."}}},"P3-2":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"P3-2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 2 is the part of the American West where three stories run at once and keep colliding: the government opening the Plains to farmers and railroad companies, the cattle industry spreading north out of Texas, and the Plains peoples losing the land and the buffalo their way of life depended on. Booklet P feeds on exactly that. The consequence parts of Question 1 like a single dated measure such as the Homestead Act of 1862 or a single shocking event such as Sand Creek; the narrative account likes a short run of years, 1862 to 1868 or 1866 to 1876, in which one thing visibly caused the next; the importance parts like one act, one person or one treaty whose effects reached forward for a decade. If you can say, with names, dates and figures, what the two Acts of 1862 changed, how a longhorn got from Texas to Chicago, and why the Lakota won in 1868 and lost the Black Hills by 1876, you can answer any of the three.","idea":[{"code":"P3-2 — 1 The development of settlement in the West: The significance of the railroads","h":"The railroads: an Act of 1862, a line joined in 1869, and a network that pulled settlers west","t":"The Pacific Railroad Act, signed on 1 July 1862, chartered the Union Pacific to build west from Omaha and authorised the Central Pacific to build east from Sacramento. Congress paid the companies in land alongside the track, in alternate sections either side of the line, and in government loans by the mile, roughly 16,000 dollars a mile on flat land, 32,000 across hills and 48,000 in the mountains, so the two companies raced each other. They met at Promontory Summit in Utah on 10 May 1869. The Act was only possible because the southern states had left Congress during the Civil War, and the war itself made a link to California look like a military necessity. The network then spread: the line reached Abilene in Kansas in 1867 and Denver in 1870, and the Atchison, Topeka and Santa Fe reached Dodge City in 1872. Because the companies held land they could not farm, they advertised it in the eastern states and in Europe and sold it cheaply to settlers, and because trains carried freight both ways, wheat and cattle could now leave the Plains at a profit. A top-level answer does not list what railroads did; it uses them as the link between episodes, showing that the land grant created the advertising, the advertising created the settlers, and the freight rates made their farms worth keeping."},{"code":"P3-2 — 1 The development of settlement in the West: The impact of the Homestead Act (1862)","h":"The Homestead Act, the Timber Culture Act and how homesteaders made dry grassland pay","t":"The Homestead Act was signed on 20 May 1862 and came into force on 1 January 1863. Anyone over 21, or the head of a family, including single women and, after the Civil War, freed slaves, could file on 160 acres for a ten dollar fee and own it outright after five years of living on it and improving it. The catch was that 160 acres of dry grassland was not 160 acres of Ohio farmland. Homesteaders had no timber, so they cut sod bricks for houses or dug into a bank; water lay deep, so they drilled wells and raised it with wind pumps; the sod was too tough for a wooden plough, so they used steel ploughs; there was nothing to fence with until Joseph Glidden patented his barbed wire on 24 November 1874; drought was met with dry farming, ploughing after rain and harrowing the surface to a dust mulch to hold the moisture; and eastern seed failed until Mennonite families who arrived from Russia in Kansas in 1874 brought Turkey Red, a hard winter wheat that survived Plains winters and dry summers. The Timber Culture Act of 3 March 1873 offered a further 160 acres to anyone who planted trees on 40 of them, which doubled a claim to a workable size, though few of the trees ever grew and the acreage was cut to ten in 1878. A top-level answer pairs each problem with the solution and then says what the solution changed: barbed wire is not a fact about fencing, it is the reason a homesteader could keep a crop alive next to an open range."},{"code":"P3-2 — 1 The development of settlement in the West: Introducing law and order in settlements","h":"Law and order: elected sheriffs, appointed marshals, vigilantes and a government far away","t":"Settlements were small, scattered and often hundreds of miles from a court. Counties elected a sheriff, usually for two years; towns appointed a marshal and his deputies; and the federal government appointed a US marshal for each territory, with deputies and circuit judges, so the same town could be policed by a local employee and a federal officer at once. The cow towns were the hardest test, because a trail crew arrived with months of pay, and saloons, gambling houses and revolvers came with them: Abilene answered by hiring gunfighting marshals, Wild Bill Hickok among them in 1871, and by passing ordinances banning the carrying of firearms inside the town limits. Where no officer existed at all, settlers formed vigilance committees and hanged suspects themselves, which was quick, popular and easily turned into a weapon in private quarrels. Federal influence grew steadily: Washington appointed territorial governors and judges, the army built posts, and territories that filled up became states, as Colorado did on 1 August 1876. A top-level answer weighs these against each other, showing that order arrived unevenly, that officers were few and sometimes no more respectable than the men they arrested, and that the federal government mattered less through law officers than through the courts, the army and the money the railroads and cattle trade brought in."},{"code":"P3-2 — 2 Ranching and the cattle industry: The cattle industry and factors in its growth","h":"Why the cattle industry grew: Texas beef, army contracts, Abilene and a railroad","t":"Texas ended the Civil War with about five million longhorns running half wild, worth about five dollars a head at home but up to forty at a northern railhead, so the industry's whole problem was transport. In 1866 Charles Goodnight and Oliver Loving drove some 2,000 head west to Fort Sumner in New Mexico, where the army had to feed thousands of Navajo held at the Bosque Redondo reservation, and then pushed their trail north into Colorado; Loving died of his wounds in September 1867 after an attack, but the route was made. John Iliff bought Texas cattle off that trail and built up herds on the Colorado plains, selling beef to railroad construction crews and to Indian agencies and showing that cattle could be wintered on the northern grass. The decisive move was Joseph McCoy's: in 1867 he built stockyards, pens, a hotel and a bank at Abilene in Kansas, on the railroad and beyond the settled farming counties, and the first twenty carloads left for Chicago on 5 September 1867. About 35,000 head came up the Chisholm Trail that season, and more than 1.5 million passed through Abilene by 1871. A top-level answer explains the chain rather than listing the men: cheap cattle plus a paying market equals nothing until a railhead exists where the two can meet."},{"code":"P3-2 — 2 Ranching and the cattle industry: The changing role of the cowboy","h":"The cowboy: from trail driver to ranch hand, and the quarrel with the homesteaders","t":"The cowboy's work changed as the industry changed. On the drives of the late 1860s and early 1870s a crew of a dozen or so men walked two or three thousand head north for two or three months, riding point, swing and drag, standing night watch against stampedes, swimming rivers and paying tolls to cross Indian territory. As ranching spread onto the central and northern Plains, the same men spent more of the year on one ranch: rounding up and branding in spring, riding the line to keep cattle on their own grass, cutting hay and pulling animals out of snowdrifts in winter. It was hired work at a low monthly wage, not the free life of the legend. Ranchers and homesteaders wanted the same land in different shapes. Trail herds trampled crops and carried Texas fever, a tick-borne disease that killed local cattle, so farming states passed quarantine laws that pushed the trade west; Abilene itself was closed to the trade in the winter of 1871-72, when the Farmers' Protective Association of Dickinson County printed a circular asking drovers to keep away and the town's officers and citizens backed it, and the business moved to Ellsworth, Wichita and then Dodge City. Homesteaders fenced with barbed wire and claimed the water; ranchers claimed the open range by custom. A top-level answer treats the cowboy as evidence of that shift, using his changing year to show ranching settling down into a business."},{"code":"P3-2 — 3 Changes in the ways of life of Indigenous peoples of the Plains: The impact of railroads, the cattle industry and gold prospecting","h":"What the railroads, the cattle and the gold did to the Plains peoples","t":"Economic development attacked the basis of Plains life rather than the people directly. Track cut across hunting grounds and split the buffalo into a northern and a southern herd; railroad companies hired hunters to feed construction crews; and once eastern tanneries learned how to turn buffalo hide into commercial leather in the early 1870s, hide hunters shot the animals in millions and left the carcasses, so that by the late 1870s the southern herd had all but gone. Cattle trails and then ranches took the grass and water the herds had used. Gold prospectors broke treaty after treaty: the Colorado rush of 1859, the Montana gold strikes from 1862, which brought miners up the Bozeman Trail through Lakota hunting country, and above all Custer's expedition of July and August 1874, which reported gold on French Creek in the Black Hills, land guaranteed to the Lakota in 1868. Prospectors poured in, the army followed to protect them, and in 1876 the Lakota were at war over land that was legally theirs. A top-level answer says what each pressure destroyed, not merely that it happened: without buffalo there is no food, clothing, shelter or trade, and a people that cannot feed itself must take rations from the agency that is trying to change it."},{"code":"P3-2 — 3 Changes in the ways of life of Indigenous peoples of the Plains: The impact of US government policy","h":"Government policy: reservations, Medicine Lodge, the second Fort Laramie Treaty and the Peace Policy","t":"Policy after 1862 meant reservations. At Medicine Lodge Creek in Kansas in October 1867 the southern Plains peoples, the Kiowa, Comanche and Plains Apache in one treaty and the Cheyenne and Arapaho in another, agreed to move onto reservations in Indian Territory in exchange for supplies, schools and farming help. The second Fort Laramie Treaty of 1868 ended Red Cloud's War on terms the Lakota had won: the army abandoned the Bozeman Trail forts, the Great Sioux Reservation was created covering the land west of the Missouri River in what is now South Dakota, including the Black Hills, the Powder River country was left as unceded hunting ground closed to whites, and no part of the reservation could be given up unless three-quarters of the adult men signed. Red Cloud signed on 6 November 1868. From 1869 President Grant's Peace Policy handed agencies to men nominated by the churches and set up a Board of Indian Commissioners to watch the supplies, the aim being to turn Plains hunters into Christian farmers. On the reservations themselves rations were short and often stolen by corrupt agents, disease spread, hunting was restricted and chiefs lost the power to lead. A top-level answer treats a treaty as a moment with two faces: what it conceded, and what it committed the signatories to become."},{"code":"P3-2 — 3 Changes in the ways of life of Indigenous peoples of the Plains: Conflict on the Plains","h":"Conflict: Little Crow's War, Sand Creek and the war Red Cloud won","t":"Little Crow's War broke out in Minnesota in August 1862 when the Santee Dakota, penned on a narrow reservation, faced crop failure, late annuity payments and traders who refused credit; more than 400 settlers were killed before Colonel Sibley beat the Dakota at Wood Lake on 23 September 1862, and 38 Dakota men were hanged together at Mankato on 26 December 1862, the largest mass execution in United States history. On 29 November 1864 Colonel John Chivington's Colorado volunteers attacked Black Kettle's Cheyenne and Arapaho camp at Sand Creek, which had come in under a promise of safety, and killed at least 150 people, most of them women and children; a congressional inquiry condemned the attack, but war spread across the central Plains. Red Cloud's War of 1866 to 1868 was fought over the forts the army built to guard the Bozeman Trail to the Montana goldfields. On 21 December 1866 a detachment of 81 soldiers under Captain William Fetterman was decoyed out of Fort Phil Kearny and wiped out by Lakota, Cheyenne and Arapaho warriors, Crazy Horse among the decoys. Unable to hold the road, the government made peace in 1868. A top-level answer uses these three to show a pattern changing: broken promises produced war, war produced harsher promises, and only once, in 1868, did the Plains peoples get the terms they wanted."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1(b): turning the Civil War into one explained consequence for the cattle industry","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(b): 'Explain one consequence of the Civil War (1861-65) for the cattle industry of Texas.' [4 marks] Question 1 is printed as two parts on two separate events, each marked on its own out of 4, so this part needs one consequence analysed and supported. What you know: Texas fought for the Confederacy and its men were away with the armies; longhorns on the open range bred without being rounded up or branded; by 1865 there were about five million of them in Texas; a longhorn was worth about five dollars in Texas and up to forty at a northern railhead; northern cities and the army were short of beef; the Pacific Railroad Act had been passed in 1862 while southern members were out of Congress.","steps":["Choose which end of the story the consequence sits at: the war left Texas with an enormous unsold asset, so the consequence to develop is the herd itself rather than the fighting.","State it as a change of situation: before the war Texas beef was a local trade, and after it Texas held about five million half-wild cattle that nobody in Texas could sell at a profit.","Support it with the precise detail that proves the change mattered: five dollars a head at home against up to forty at a northern railhead, and hungry northern cities at the other end of a railway that Congress had begun funding in 1862.","Make the link the grid calls analysis: because the value of a longhorn depended entirely on where it stood, the war turned the cattle industry into a transport problem, and the answer to that problem was the long drive to a railhead."],"cues":["Which part of this material is a consequence rather than a cause?","What was true of Texas in 1865 that had not been true in 1861?","Which two figures show why those cattle were worth moving?","Why does a price gap turn into a trail drive rather than a local sale?"]},{"title":"Question 1(a): one consequence of the Medicine Lodge Treaty (1867)","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(a): 'Explain one consequence of the Medicine Lodge Treaty (1867).' [4 marks] What you know: at Medicine Lodge Creek in Kansas in October 1867 US commissioners signed treaties with the Kiowa, Comanche and Plains Apache and with the southern Cheyenne and Arapaho; the tribes agreed to move onto reservations in Indian Territory; in exchange they were promised supplies, schools, farming help and protection from white intruders; many warriors had not agreed and went on hunting off the reservation; the buffalo of the southern Plains were already being shot in large numbers by hide hunters.","steps":["Decide what the treaty actually changed on the ground: the southern Plains peoples were now expected to live inside a marked boundary and draw supplies from an agency.","State the consequence in those terms: their food supply stopped being something they hunted and became something the US government issued to them.","Support it with the detail from the treaty and the situation around it: reservations in Indian Territory, promises of supplies and schools, and a buffalo herd already being destroyed outside the boundary.","Explain the link: because the promised rations were the price of staying inside the reservation, any failure to deliver them pushed young men back out to hunt, so the treaty made conflict more likely rather than less."],"cues":["What did the treaty ask the tribes to do that was new?","Where did their food come from after the treaty?","Which promises in the treaty carry the weight of your explanation?","Why would a treaty meant to end fighting make more fighting likely?"]},{"title":"Question 2: building the spine of a narrative account on the growth of towns on the Plains","setup":"Booklet P, Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the growth of towns on the Plains in the years 1867-1876.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the arrival of the Union Pacific at Cheyenne (1867); the founding of Dodge City (1872). You must also use information of your own. The material available: Grenville Dodge laid out a townsite on Crow Creek in July 1867 and the Union Pacific track reached Cheyenne on 13 November 1867; the companies sold town lots as well as farmland along their lines; Denver was joined to the main line by rail in 1870; Dodge City was laid out in 1872 five miles west of Fort Dodge and the Atchison, Topeka and Santa Fe reached it that September, taking the buffalo hide trade first and the cattle trade later; counties were organised and towns acquired newspapers, churches, schools and courts; and a town could lose its trade again, as Abilene did after 1871.","steps":["Fix the two ends of the account first: it starts with survey stakes driven into empty grass beside a line that has not yet arrived, and ends with places that have courts, schools and county offices, so the outcome to drive towards is settled town life.","Choose three or four episodes that are separate aspects rather than repeats: a town laid out ahead of the track, a town made by a trade, the institutions that arrived once the trade did, and a town that lost its trade again.","Write the joins rather than the list: the company laid out lots because a town filled its trains, the trade came because the track was already there, and the courts and the newspapers came because the trade brought people and money worth governing.","Close the sequence with a sentence that says what the growth of these towns amounted to by the end of the period, measured against the grassland the line was built across, instead of simply stopping at the last event."],"cues":["Where should this account begin, and where should it end?","Which episodes count as three or more separate aspects?","What is the connection between a railway company and a town's main street?","What does your last sentence have to do that a list of towns does not?"]},{"title":"Question 3: explaining the importance of barbed wire for farming on the Plains","setup":"Booklet P, Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. The bullet here: 'The importance of barbed wire for farming on the Plains.' What you know: there was almost no timber on the Plains, so wooden fencing had to be freighted in at great cost; cattle from the open range walked through crops; Joseph Glidden of DeKalb, Illinois patented a workable barbed wire on 24 November 1874 and manufactured it cheaply; homesteaders used it to enclose crops, water and stock; ranchers used it to fence range and water holes; quarrels over fencing and water followed.","steps":["Set the test the question is really asking: importance means what difference it made afterwards, so the answer must compare farming before and after cheap wire.","Take the first difference and develop it: without fencing a homesteader could not keep cattle out of a crop, so a claim worth farming needed either timber nobody had or wire nobody could yet buy.","Take the second difference, which reaches beyond the farm: cheap wire also let ranchers enclose grass and water that had been used in common, so it changed who controlled the range as well as who kept a crop.","Close with a judgement that ranks the effects: its importance lies less in the fence than in making a quarter section defensible, which is what turned a claim into a farm and set homesteaders against ranchers."],"cues":["What does an importance question want that a description does not?","What could a homesteader not do before cheap wire existed?","Who else found a use for it, and what did they enclose?","Which of the two effects would you put first, and why?"]},{"title":"Question 3: explaining the importance of Grant's Peace Policy for the reservations","setup":"Booklet P, Question 3, the bullet: 'The importance of President Grant's Peace Policy for life on the reservations.' What you know: Ulysses S. Grant became president in March 1869 and set out to end the fraud and the fighting in Indian affairs; Congress created a Board of Indian Commissioners in April 1869, unpaid men who were to watch over the supplies and the accounts; agencies were handed to men nominated by the churches, the Quakers taking the largest share, in place of political appointees; Grant made Ely Parker, a Seneca who had served on his staff in the Civil War, Commissioner of Indian Affairs; the declared aim was to turn hunters into Christian farmers on the reservations; rations were still short and often poor, the agent decided who was fed, and bands that stayed off the reservations were left to the army.","steps":["Set the test the question is really asking: importance means what difference the policy made afterwards, so the answer has to compare the agencies before and after it rather than describe what Grant hoped for.","Take the first difference and develop it: the men who held the agencies and handled the money were changed, with church nominees and an unpaid board of commissioners put in to break the old traffic in contracts and supplies, so a reservation got a different kind of master rather than a different purpose.","Take the second difference, which reaches further: because the agency became the only lawful place to draw food, the ration, the school and the plough turned into the instruments of a change in the way of life, while anyone outside the boundary was counted as hostile.","Close with a judgement that ranks the effects: the policy mattered less as the peace it promised than as the machinery it built, since it tied being fed to staying inside the boundary and left the army to deal with everyone else."],"cues":["What does an importance question want that a description does not?","Who held the agencies before the policy, and who held them after?","What did a family have to do to be fed once the policy was working?","Which mattered more, the intention or the machinery, and why?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What did the Homestead Act of 1862 offer, on what conditions?","a":"160 acres of public land to anyone over 21 or the head of a family, for a ten dollar filing fee, with full ownership after five years of living on the claim and improving it. It came into force on 1 January 1863."},{"q":"Name the two companies that built the first transcontinental railroad, and say where and when their lines met.","a":"The Union Pacific, building west from Omaha, and the Central Pacific, building east from Sacramento. They met at Promontory Summit in Utah on 10 May 1869."},{"q":"Why did Joseph McCoy choose Abilene in 1867, and what happened there that September?","a":"Abilene stood on the railroad and lay beyond the settled farming counties that were trying to keep Texas cattle out, so herds could reach it. The first twenty carloads of longhorns left Abilene for Chicago on 5 September 1867."},{"q":"What did the United States give up in the second Fort Laramie Treaty of 1868, and what did the Lakota accept?","a":"The United States abandoned the Bozeman Trail forts and recognised the Great Sioux Reservation, including the Black Hills, with the Powder River country as unceded hunting ground. The Lakota accepted reservation boundaries, agencies, schools and farming, and a clause requiring three-quarters of adult men to sign before any land was given up."}]},"room":{"facts":["Booklet P is worth 32 marks and has three questions: Question 1, printed in two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each 'Explain one consequence of...' for 4 marks and each on a separate event or development; Question 2, 'Write a narrative account analysing...', 8 marks; Question 3, 'Explain two of the following: the importance of ... for ...', two chosen parts at 8 marks each, 16 in all.","Only Question 2 prints stimulus points, as two bullets followed by 'You must also use information of your own.' The cap printed in the mark scheme is 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' There is no cap at Level 2, and Questions 1 and 3 print no bullets and carry no cap.","Each part of Question 1 is marked on its own two-level grid out of 4: Level 2 (3-4) needs features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific information added in support. A description of the event, however accurate, stays in Level 1.","Question 2 and each part of Question 3 are marked on three-level grids out of 8. Level 3 on the narrative account (6-8) needs a clear sequence of events leading to an outcome with the linkage between them analysed; Level 3 on an importance part needs analysis of importance in a coherent line of reasoning, that is what difference the event, person or development made to later situations.","Timing: Paper 2 lasts 1 hour 50 minutes for 64 marks, about 55 minutes a booklet, so booklet P's 32 marks work out at roughly a minute a mark with a little planning on top: about 10 minutes on the two parts of Question 1, 13 on Question 2 and 14 on each part of Question 3."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Homestead Act (1862). [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2, marked on the 4-mark consequence grid; Level 2 (3-4) needs features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific support. Credit the movement of families with little capital onto 160-acre claims from 1 January 1863, the spread of farming onto land once written off as desert, claims by single women and by freed slaves, or the new problems of farming a quarter section of dry grassland.","sol":"One consequence was that families with almost no capital could take Plains land, because the Act asked for a ten dollar filing fee and five years of residence and improvement rather than a purchase price. From 1 January 1863, when it came into force, any citizen over 21 or head of a family could file on 160 acres, which included single women and, after the Civil War, freed slaves. Settlement therefore spread onto grassland that maps had called the Great American Desert, and it was Congress rather than the land market that decided who farmed it.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["A consequence is something that was true afterwards because of the Act, so ask what the Act made possible that had not been possible before.","Look at who could now get land and on what terms, rather than at what the land was like.","Write it in three moves: name the consequence, give the precise terms of the Act as your evidence, then explain why those terms changed who settled the Plains."],"codes":["P3-2 — 1 The development of settlement in the West: The impact of the Homestead Act (1862)"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of the completion of the first transcontinental railroad in 1869. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the collapse in the time and danger of crossing the continent after 10 May 1869, the sale and advertising of railroad land grants to settlers and immigrants, the ability to send crops and cattle east at a profit, or the pressure the line put on the buffalo herds and on Plains hunting grounds.","sol":"One consequence was that crossing the continent stopped being an expedition and became a journey. Before 10 May 1869, when the Union Pacific and Central Pacific lines were joined at Promontory Summit, a family went west by wagon over several months at real risk; afterwards the same journey took about a week by train, with freight running in both directions. Because settlers could now arrive cheaply and send wheat and cattle back east at a profit, farming the Plains became a business proposition rather than a gamble, and the companies advertised their land grants to fill the trains.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what the line changed for people who wanted to move west, not about how it was built.","A railway carries two things: passengers going one way and freight going the other. Which of those made Plains farming pay?","Name the consequence, date it with the joining of the lines, then explain why cheap transport changed what settlers could do with the land."],"codes":["P3-2 — 1 The development of settlement in the West: The significance of the railroads"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Sand Creek Massacre (1864). [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the outbreak of fighting across the central Plains in 1865 by Cheyenne, Arapaho, Lakota and Comanche war parties, the destruction of trust in US promises of safety, the congressional inquiry that condemned Chivington, or the push towards the treaty-making of 1867 and 1868.","sol":"One consequence was that peace agreements lost their value in the eyes of the Plains peoples. Black Kettle's Cheyenne and Arapaho camp had come in near Fort Lyon under a promise of protection, and on 29 November 1864 Colonel Chivington's Colorado volunteers killed at least 150 of them, most of them women and children. Because a band that had done what the government asked was destroyed anyway, war parties of Cheyenne, Arapaho and Lakota struck along the central Plains routes through 1865, and later commissioners had to bargain with peoples who no longer believed a promise of safety meant anything.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what the attack taught the Plains peoples about dealing with the US government.","The detail that carries the explanation is what Black Kettle's band had been promised before the attack.","State the consequence, support it with what happened at the camp and what followed in 1865, then explain the link between the broken promise and the fighting."],"codes":["P3-2 — 3 Changes in the ways of life of Indigenous peoples of the Plains: Conflict on the Plains"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of the discovery of gold in the Black Hills in 1874. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the rush of prospectors onto land guaranteed to the Lakota by the 1868 treaty, the government's failed attempt to buy the Hills in 1875, the order that Lakota outside the agencies report by 31 January 1876, or the war of 1876 that followed.","sol":"One consequence was that the second Fort Laramie Treaty was destroyed in practice. Custer's expedition reported gold on French Creek in the summer of 1874, and prospectors poured into hills that the treaty of 1868 had reserved to the Lakota and closed to whites. The army made only half-hearted efforts to keep them out; the government then tried to buy the Hills in 1875 and was refused. Because Washington would not remove its own citizens, it turned instead on the Lakota, ordering those outside the agencies to report by 31 January 1876, which led directly to war.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The land where the gold was found was not ordinary public land, so start from who owned it.","Ask what the government did when it could not keep its own citizens out of treaty land.","Name the consequence, support it with the treaty and what followed in 1875 and early 1876, then explain the link from gold to war."],"codes":["P3-2 — 3 Changes in the ways of life of Indigenous peoples of the Plains: The impact of railroads, the cattle industry and gold prospecting"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Texas fever quarantine laws for the cattle trade. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the westward shift of the trail and the railheads, from Abilene to Ellsworth, Wichita and Dodge City, the closing of Abilene to the trade in 1872 by its own farmers and townspeople, the growth of ranching on the northern and central Plains in place of long drives from Texas, or the worsening of relations between homesteaders and drovers.","sol":"One consequence was that the cattle trade kept moving west. Longhorns carried ticks that spread Texas fever, which killed the local cattle of Kansas and Missouri farmers, so farming counties used quarantine lines to shut the herds out. As settlement spread, the quarantine line moved west with it and the farmers themselves did the rest: Abilene, which had opened the trade in 1867, lost the business in 1872 after the Farmers' Protective Association of Dickinson County printed a circular, backed by the county's officers and citizens, asking drovers to ship from somewhere else; the herds went to Ellsworth, Wichita and then Dodge City. The trade survived, but each move made the drive longer and encouraged ranchers to raise cattle nearer the railheads instead.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Start from why farmers objected to the herds at all, since that is what the laws were for.","If a town closes its trade, the trade does not stop; ask where it goes and what that costs the drovers.","State the consequence, support it with the towns and dates that show the shift, then explain why the shift changed how cattle were raised."],"codes":["P3-2 — 2 Ranching and the cattle industry: The changing role of the cowboy","P3-2 — 2 Ranching and the cattle industry: The cattle industry and factors in its growth"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Pacific Railroad Act (1862). [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the Timber Culture Act (1873). [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4 on the consequence grid, so each part needs one consequence analysed with specific support. In 1(a) credit the chartering of the Union Pacific and Central Pacific and the race between them, the land grants and loans by the mile, or the companies' sale and advertising of their land to settlers and immigrants. In 1(b) credit claims doubled to 320 acres, the failure of most of the tree planting, the reduction of the tree acreage to ten in 1878, or the use of the Act by speculators.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that two private companies were given a public interest in filling the Plains with settlers. The Act of 1 July 1862 chartered the Union Pacific to build west from Omaha and the Central Pacific to build east from Sacramento, and paid them in alternate sections of land along the track as well as in government loans by the mile, roughly 16,000 dollars a mile on level ground. Because the companies now held land they could not farm and track that needed traffic, they advertised in the eastern states and in Europe and sold farms cheaply to anyone who would come. 1(b) One consequence was that a homesteader could double a claim to 320 acres, which was much closer to a size that could feed a family on dry grassland. The Act of 3 March 1873 offered a further 160 acres to anyone who planted trees on 40 of them. In practice few of the trees survived the winds and drought, and the requirement was cut to ten acres in 1878, so the main effect was not woodland but larger farms, with speculators filing claims they never intended to plant.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate parts on two separate measures: a railway law in 1(a) and a land law in 1(b), so neither answer can borrow from the other.","For 1(a) follow what the companies were paid in, and ask what owning that payment made them want to do.","For 1(b) the consequence is about the size of a claim, not about trees."],"codes":["P3-2 — 1 The development of settlement in the West: The significance of the railroads","P3-2 — 1 The development of settlement in the West: The impact of the Homestead Act (1862)"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of Little Crow's War (1862). [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the Fetterman fight (December 1866). [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4 on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the hanging of 38 Dakota men at Mankato on 26 December 1862, the removal of the Santee from Minnesota, or the hardening of settler and army attitudes that helped produce Sand Creek. In 1(b) credit the army's inability to hold the Bozeman Trail forts, the shock in the eastern press, or the opening of negotiations that led to the second Fort Laramie Treaty of 1868.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was the destruction of the Santee Dakota as a people living in Minnesota. After Colonel Sibley's victory at Wood Lake on 23 September 1862, hundreds were condemned and 38 were hanged together at Mankato on 26 December 1862, the largest mass execution in United States history; the survivors lost their reservation and were moved west into Dakota Territory. Because the rising had begun over late payments and hunger, its punishment showed other Plains peoples what rebellion cost. 1(b) One consequence was that the army gave up the Bozeman Trail. On 21 December 1866 a detachment of 81 men under Captain Fetterman was decoyed out of Fort Phil Kearny and wiped out, and the shock made clear that the garrisons could not protect the road they were built to guard. Rather than fight a longer war in the Powder River country, the government opened talks, and in 1868 it agreed to abandon the forts.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["The two parts are separate: a rising in Minnesota in 1(a), a single engagement in Wyoming in 1(b).","For 1(a) look at what happened to the Santee after the fighting stopped, not during it.","For 1(b) ask what one wiped-out detachment proved about the forts, and what the government decided to do about it."],"codes":["P3-2 — 3 Changes in the ways of life of Indigenous peoples of the Plains: Conflict on the Plains"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of Joseph McCoy's building of stockyards at Abilene in 1867. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the spread of barbed wire after 1874. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the first shipment of twenty carloads to Chicago on 5 September 1867, the 35,000 head that came up the Chisholm Trail that season and more than 1.5 million by 1871, or the creation of the cow town and its trade. In 1(b) credit the enclosure of crops and water by homesteaders, the fencing of range and water holes by ranchers, the quarrels that followed, or the move away from open-range herding.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that driving cattle north became a regular business rather than a gamble. McCoy built pens, a hotel and a bank at a point on the railroad beyond the farming counties, and the first twenty carloads left Abilene for Chicago on 5 September 1867; about 35,000 head came up the Chisholm Trail that season and more than 1.5 million passed through the town by 1871. Because a drover now knew where he could sell and ship, the long drive had a fixed destination and a known price. 1(b) One consequence was that a homesteader could at last defend a claim. Glidden's cheap wire, patented in November 1874, needed no timber, so crops could be enclosed against range cattle for the first time. Ranchers used the same wire to fence grass and water holes they had previously shared, so land that had been used in common began to be owned in practice as well as on paper.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["One part is about a place built to sell cattle, the other about a material that decided who could keep land.","For 1(a) ask what a drover leaving Texas now knew that he had not known in 1865.","For 1(b) remember that both sides of the quarrel bought the wire."],"codes":["P3-2 — 2 Ranching and the cattle industry: The cattle industry and factors in its growth","P3-2 — 2 Ranching and the cattle industry: The changing role of the cowboy"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the arrival of the cattle trade for law and order in the cow towns. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the shortage of law officers in newly settled districts. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the appointment of paid town marshals, including gunfighters such as Hickok at Abilene in 1871, ordinances banning the carrying of firearms in town, or the tension between the income the trade brought and the disorder it caused. In 1(b) credit the growth of vigilance committees and summary hangings, the use of vigilantes to settle private quarrels, or the dependence on distant county sheriffs and territorial US marshals.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that policing became a paid municipal job. A trail crew reached town with months of wages and a revolver each, and saloons and gambling houses followed the money, so councils hired professional marshals, Wild Bill Hickok among them at Abilene in 1871, and passed ordinances forbidding the carrying of firearms inside the town limits. Because the same trade paid the town's bills, officers were expected to keep order without driving the business away. 1(b) One consequence was that settlers policed themselves. A county sheriff might be a hundred miles off and the territorial US marshal further still, so communities formed vigilance committees and hanged suspected rustlers and thieves without trial. This produced quick results and was popular, but it put the power to hang into the hands of whoever could raise a crowd, and it was later used to settle private quarrels over land and stock.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two different problems: too many armed strangers in 1(a), too few officers in 1(b).","For 1(a) ask what a town council could actually do about men who were spending money it wanted.","For 1(b) if no officer can reach a district, who acts, and on whose authority?"],"codes":["P3-2 — 1 The development of settlement in the West: Introducing law and order in settlements"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the second Fort Laramie Treaty (1868). [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the destruction of the buffalo herds in the 1870s. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the abandonment of the Bozeman Trail forts and the closing of the road, the creation of the Great Sioux Reservation including the Black Hills, the unceded Powder River hunting grounds, or the three-quarters clause the US later broke. In 1(b) credit the loss of food, clothing, shelter and trade goods, dependence on agency rations, the collapse of resistance, or the grass left free for cattle.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that the Lakota obtained a legal title the United States would later have to break in the open. The treaty of 1868 closed the Bozeman Trail and took the soldiers out of its forts, recognised the Great Sioux Reservation west of the Missouri River including the Black Hills, and said that no part of it could be given up unless three-quarters of the adult men signed. When gold was found in the Hills in 1874, the government could not take them lawfully, so it resorted to an ultimatum and then to war. 1(b) One consequence was that the Plains peoples could no longer feed or clothe themselves independently. The buffalo had provided meat, hides for tipis and robes, sinew, tools and goods to trade; as hide hunters cut down the southern herd through the 1870s, the reservation ration issued by an agent became the only reliable food. Hunger, not defeat in battle, is what brought most bands onto the reservations and kept them there.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["One part is a treaty the Plains peoples won, the other a slow loss they could not fight.","For 1(a) look for the clause that made the Black Hills a legal problem for Washington after 1874.","For 1(b) list what the buffalo supplied before saying what its loss forced people to accept."],"codes":["P3-2 — 3 Changes in the ways of life of Indigenous peoples of the Plains: The impact of US government policy","P3-2 — 3 Changes in the ways of life of Indigenous peoples of the Plains: The impact of railroads, the cattle industry and gold prospecting"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the growth of the cattle industry in the years 1866-1876. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the Goodnight-Loving Trail (1866); Joseph McCoy. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) requires a clear sequence of events leading to an outcome with the linkage between them analysed. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; own material might include the Texas herds left by the Civil War and the price gap, Abilene and the first shipment of September 1867, the Chisholm Trail, John Iliff and ranching on the northern grass, Texas fever quarantine laws and the move west to Wichita and Dodge City.","sol":"The industry grew because a cheap supply in the south was joined to a hungry market in the north. Texas came out of the Civil War with about five million longhorns running half wild, worth roughly five dollars a head at home but up to forty at a northern railhead, so the industry's problem was transport rather than stock. In 1866 Charles Goodnight and Oliver Loving drove some 2,000 head west to Fort Sumner in New Mexico, where the army had to feed the Navajo held at Bosque Redondo, then pushed the trail north into Colorado; that proved cattle could be walked to wherever a paying customer sat. It was Joseph McCoy who supplied the customer's address. In 1867 he built stockyards, pens, a hotel and a bank at Abilene in Kansas, on the railroad and beyond the farming counties, and the first twenty carloads left for Chicago on 5 September 1867, with about 35,000 head coming up the Chisholm Trail that season. Because the railway now did the long haul, trailing became an annual business, and John Iliff bought Texas stock off the Goodnight trail to graze on the Colorado plains, selling beef to railroad crews and Indian agencies. By 1876, with quarantine laws pushing the railheads west to Wichita and Dodge City, the industry's centre had shifted from the drive itself to ranching on the open range.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first what the account is heading towards: the industry looks different in 1876 from 1866, so say how.","Three or more separate aspects are needed: a trail, a railhead and what happened to ranching are three different things.","Write the joins, not the list: each episode should explain why the next one was possible or necessary."],"codes":["P3-2 — 2 Ranching and the cattle industry: The cattle industry and factors in its growth","P3-2 — 2 Ranching and the cattle industry: The changing role of the cowboy"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the conflict between the Plains Indians and the US government in the years 1862-1868. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the Sand Creek Massacre (1864); Red Cloud's War (1866-68). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 requires a clear analysed sequence leading to an outcome; no access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content. Own material might include Little Crow's War and the Mankato hangings, the Bozeman Trail and the Montana goldfields, the Fetterman fight of December 1866, the Medicine Lodge Treaty of 1867 and the terms of the second Fort Laramie Treaty of 1868.","sol":"The fighting of these years began where reservation promises failed. In Minnesota in August 1862 the Santee Dakota, short of food and waiting on late annuity payments, rose under Little Crow; more than 400 settlers died, the Dakota were beaten at Wood Lake on 23 September and 38 men were hanged at Mankato on 26 December 1862. That rising taught settlers on the central Plains to expect the worst, which is part of why Colonel Chivington's Colorado volunteers attacked Black Kettle's camp at Sand Creek on 29 November 1864 and killed at least 150 people, most of them women and children, although the band had come in under a promise of protection. Sand Creek did not end resistance; it destroyed the value of promises, and Cheyenne, Arapaho and Lakota war parties struck the trails through 1865. Conflict then moved north, because miners wanted the Bozeman Trail to the Montana goldfields and the army built forts along it through Lakota hunting country. Red Cloud's war of 1866 to 1868 was fought against those forts, and after 81 men under Captain Fetterman were decoyed and wiped out on 21 December 1866 the government concluded that the road could not be held. The sequence ended in negotiation: the southern peoples were moved to reservations at Medicine Lodge in 1867, and in 1868 the second Fort Laramie Treaty abandoned the forts and recognised the Great Sioux Reservation.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what starts the sequence: these wars all begin with something the government had promised and not delivered.","The account moves geographically as well as in time, from Minnesota to Colorado to the Powder River country.","End on the outcome the question is driving at, and make each episode explain the next rather than follow it."],"codes":["P3-2 — 3 Changes in the ways of life of Indigenous peoples of the Plains: Conflict on the Plains","P3-2 — 3 Changes in the ways of life of Indigenous peoples of the Plains: The impact of US government policy"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the settlement of the Plains by homesteaders in the years 1862-1876. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the Homestead Act (1862); the Timber Culture Act (1873). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 requires a clear analysed sequence leading to an outcome; no access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content. Own material might include the Pacific Railroad Act and the companies' land sales and advertising, sod houses, wells and wind pumps, the steel plough, dry farming, barbed wire in 1874, Turkey Red wheat brought by Mennonites in 1874 and the grasshopper plague of that year.","sol":"Settlement began with a law rather than a discovery. The Homestead Act, signed on 20 May 1862 and in force from 1 January 1863, offered 160 acres for a ten dollar fee to anyone who lived on the claim and improved it for five years, so the barrier to going west was no longer money but survival. Getting there and selling anything afterwards depended on the railroads: under the Pacific Railroad Act of the same year the companies were paid in land along the track, and they advertised and sold it cheaply in the eastern states and in Europe to fill their trains. Arriving was the easy part. A quarter section of dry grassland had no timber, so families cut sod bricks for houses; water lay deep, so they sank wells and pumped with windmills; and the sod needed steel ploughs to break. Crops still failed, until dry farming taught them to harrow a dust mulch after rain and Mennonite settlers who reached Kansas in 1874 brought Turkey Red, a hard winter wheat suited to the climate. In the same year Joseph Glidden's barbed wire gave them a fence at last, and the Timber Culture Act of 1873 let a family add another 160 acres by promising to plant trees. By 1876 the Plains were farmed not because land had been given away in 1862 but because law, railway and technology had together made the gift usable.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The account has two halves: getting people onto the land, and making the land keep them there.","Use the stimulus laws as the frame and fill the middle with what a family actually had to solve.","Finish with a sentence that says what the whole sequence had achieved by the mid-1870s."],"codes":["P3-2 — 1 The development of settlement in the West: The impact of the Homestead Act (1862)","P3-2 — 1 The development of settlement in the West: The significance of the railroads"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the railroad land grants for bringing settlers onto the Plains. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Level 3 (6-8) needs analysis of importance in a coherent line of reasoning, that is what difference the grants made to later developments. Credit the alternate sections of land paid to the companies along the track, the land offices, advertising and cheap credit that turned railway companies into sellers of farms, the immigrant families who bought railroad land, the freight service that made a Plains farm pay, and the pressure the same belt of settlement put on the buffalo and on Plains hunting grounds.","sol":"The grants mattered because they gave private companies a direct interest in filling the Plains with farmers. Under the Pacific Railroad Act of 1 July 1862 the Union Pacific and the Central Pacific were paid in alternate sections of public land along the route as well as in loans by the mile, and the Plains lines built afterwards were paid in the same coin, so by the time the first two tracks met at Promontory Summit on 10 May 1869 the companies held millions of acres of grass they could neither farm nor eat. That is why they became the loudest recruiters in the West. They opened land offices, advertised in the eastern states and across Europe, sold on credit at prices a family could meet and carried the buyers out at reduced fares; whole communities came on those terms, and the Mennonite families who reached Kansas in 1874 with their Turkey Red wheat bought their land from the Atchison, Topeka and Santa Fe. The grants also decided where settlement went, since the land on offer lay in a belt along the track, which is exactly where a farmer needed to be: without freight running east, a homestead hundreds of miles from a market could not sell a crop at a price worth having. Their importance is therefore not that they paid for a railway but that they manufactured a farming population beside it, in country that free land alone would have left empty. It was double-edged all the same, because that same belt of settlement cut the buffalo range in two and its traffic carried the hide hunters who destroyed the herds.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Importance means what difference it made afterwards, so do not describe the building of the line.","Ask what the companies were paid in, and what owning that payment forced them to do about settlers.","Build the answer as a chain that ends in a judgement about how far the Plains would have filled up without the land the companies had to sell."],"codes":["P3-2 — 1 The development of settlement in the West: The significance of the railroads","P3-2 — 3 Changes in the ways of life of Indigenous peoples of the Plains: The impact of railroads, the cattle industry and gold prospecting"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the Goodnight-Loving Trail (1866) for the spread of ranching onto the northern Plains. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Credit the 1866 drive of about 2,000 head from Fort Belknap by Horsehead Crossing on the Pecos to Fort Sumner and the army contract waiting there, the choice of a western route for its water and clear of the quarantine counties, the extension into Colorado and the mining and agency markets it reached, Goodnight's Colorado ranch and the delivery to Cheyenne on the Union Pacific in 1868, the stocking of the northern ranges with Texas cattle, and the model the route gave to later trails.","sol":"The trail's importance lies in what it opened rather than in the cattle it moved. In 1866 Charles Goodnight and Oliver Loving took about 2,000 longhorns west from Fort Belknap in Texas, across the dry country by Horsehead Crossing on the Pecos and up that river to Fort Sumner in New Mexico, where the army had thousands of Navajo to feed at the Bosque Redondo reservation and no cheap source of beef. Two things about that drive mattered afterwards. The first is the customer: a government contract paid in gold on delivery, which showed that the army and the Indian agencies could finance a drive years before the eastern packing houses could be reached at all. The second is the route: chosen for its water, it ran west of the farming counties of Kansas and Missouri, whose quarantine laws and armed farmers had turned earlier herds back, so Texas had an outlet that did not depend on a Kansas railhead. Its consequences then ran north. Loving carried the route on into Colorado before he died of his wounds in September 1867, and the mining camps and agencies there gave it a second market; Goodnight was ranching on the Arkansas near Pueblo by 1870, and in 1868 he had contracted to deliver Texas cattle at Cheyenne on the new Union Pacific line, where John Iliff took them onto the northern range. Texas stock therefore stocked Colorado and Wyoming grass, and by the middle of the 1870s ranching in the north was a business of its own rather than an extension of Texas. Set against Abilene, the trail mattered less for shipping beef east than for founding those northern ranges, and that is where its importance chiefly lies.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Ask who the customers were at the end of this trail, because they are not the same customers as at a railhead.","Trace the trail's extension northwards and say who bought the cattle there.","Close by ranking this trail against the railhead trade, so the answer reaches a judgement rather than a summary."],"codes":["P3-2 — 2 Ranching and the cattle industry: The cattle industry and factors in its growth","P3-2 — 2 Ranching and the cattle industry: The changing role of the cowboy"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the second Fort Laramie Treaty (1868) for the Lakota Sioux. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Credit the abandonment of the Bozeman Trail forts and the closing of the road, the Great Sioux Reservation including the Black Hills, the unceded Powder River hunting grounds, the three-quarters clause, the acceptance of agencies, schools and farming, and the treaty's collapse after the gold discovery of 1874.","sol":"For the Lakota the treaty of 1868 was both the high point of their resistance and the instrument of their defeat. It was signed on terms they had imposed: after two years of war over the Bozeman Trail, in which 81 soldiers under Captain Fetterman were wiped out in December 1866, the army abandoned its forts and the road to the Montana goldfields was closed, and Red Cloud put his name to the treaty only in November 1868, once the soldiers had gone. The treaty recognised the Great Sioux Reservation, covering the land west of the Missouri River in what is now South Dakota, including the Black Hills, and left the Powder River country as unceded hunting ground closed to whites, with a clause requiring three-quarters of the adult men to agree before any of it was given up. That was the strongest legal position any Plains people obtained. Yet the same document committed them to agencies, rations, schools and farming, which drew bands into dependence on the government. Its importance was decided in 1874, when gold in the Black Hills made Washington want land it could not lawfully take, and the treaty became the measure of a broken promise rather than a protection.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what the Lakota had just won by war, because the treaty's terms are the price the United States paid.","Every gain in the treaty has a matching commitment; look for what the Lakota agreed to become.","Judge its importance by what happened to it after 1874, and say so explicitly at the end."],"codes":["P3-2 — 3 Changes in the ways of life of Indigenous peoples of the Plains: The impact of US government policy","P3-2 — 3 Changes in the ways of life of Indigenous peoples of the Plains: Conflict on the Plains"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In which year was the Homestead Act passed, and how many acres could a claimant take?","a":"1862; 160 acres."},{"q":"Where and on what date were the Union Pacific and Central Pacific lines joined?","a":"At Promontory Summit in Utah, on 10 May 1869."},{"q":"Who built the stockyards at Abilene in 1867?","a":"Joseph McCoy."},{"q":"Which treaty ended Red Cloud's War, and what did the United States agree to abandon?","a":"The second Fort Laramie Treaty of 1868; the forts guarding the Bozeman Trail."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Pacific Railroad Act (1862)","code":"P3-2 — 1 The development of settlement in the West: The significance of the railroads","open":false,"back":"Signed 1 July 1862. Chartered the Union Pacific, building west from Omaha, and the Central Pacific, building east from Sacramento, paying both in land alongside the track and in government loans for each mile built."},{"front":"The first transcontinental railroad, completed 10 May 1869","code":"P3-2 — 1 The development of settlement in the West: The significance of the railroads","open":false,"back":"The two lines met at Promontory Summit, Utah. A crossing that had taken months by wagon now took about a week, and freight could travel east as well as west."},{"front":"Homestead Act (1862)","code":"P3-2 — 1 The development of settlement in the West: The impact of the Homestead Act (1862)","open":false,"back":"Signed 20 May 1862, in force 1 January 1863. Anyone over 21 or head of a family could file on 160 acres for a ten dollar fee and own it after five years' residence and improvement."},{"front":"Timber Culture Act (1873)","code":"P3-2 — 1 The development of settlement in the West: The impact of the Homestead Act (1862)","open":false,"back":"Passed 3 March 1873. A further 160 acres to anyone planting trees on 40 of them, doubling a claim to 320 acres. Few trees survived; the requirement was cut to ten acres in 1878."},{"front":"Solutions homesteaders found to the problems of Plains farming","code":"P3-2 — 1 The development of settlement in the West: The impact of the Homestead Act (1862)","open":true,"back":"Sod houses and dugouts where there was no timber; deep wells with wind pumps where the water lay far down; barbed wire, patented by Joseph Glidden in November 1874, to fence a claim against range cattle."},{"front":"Law officers in the West","code":"P3-2 — 1 The development of settlement in the West: Introducing law and order in settlements","open":false,"back":"Counties elected a sheriff, usually for two years; towns appointed marshals and deputies; the federal government appointed a US marshal for each territory. Where none existed, settlers formed vigilance committees and hanged suspects themselves."},{"front":"Joseph McCoy and Abilene (1867)","code":"P3-2 — 2 Ranching and the cattle industry: The cattle industry and factors in its growth","open":false,"back":"McCoy built stockyards, pens, a hotel and a bank at Abilene on the railroad. The first twenty carloads left for Chicago on 5 September 1867; over 1.5 million head passed through by 1871."},{"front":"The Goodnight-Loving Trail (1866)","code":"P3-2 — 2 Ranching and the cattle industry: The cattle industry and factors in its growth","open":false,"back":"Charles Goodnight and Oliver Loving drove about 2,000 longhorns from Texas to Fort Sumner, New Mexico, to feed the Navajo at Bosque Redondo, then extended the trail north into Colorado. Loving died of his wounds in 1867."},{"front":"John Iliff","code":"P3-2 — 2 Ranching and the cattle industry: The cattle industry and factors in its growth","open":false,"back":"Colorado rancher who bought Texas cattle off the Goodnight trail, grazed them on the plains and sold beef to railroad construction crews and Indian agencies, proving cattle could be wintered on northern grass."},{"front":"The cowboy's work and how it changed","code":"P3-2 — 2 Ranching and the cattle industry: The changing role of the cowboy","open":false,"back":"On the drives: two or three months walking herds north, night watches against stampedes, river crossings. As ranching spread north: round-ups, branding, riding the line, haying and winter work for a monthly wage on one ranch."},{"front":"The buffalo, the railroads and the hide hunters","code":"P3-2 — 3 Changes in the ways of life of Indigenous peoples of the Plains: The impact of railroads, the cattle industry and gold prospecting","open":false,"back":"Track split the herds and companies hired hunters to feed construction crews. When tanneries learned to use buffalo hide in the early 1870s, hunters killed millions, and the southern herd was almost gone by the late 1870s."},{"front":"Gold in the Black Hills (1874)","code":"P3-2 — 3 Changes in the ways of life of Indigenous peoples of the Plains: The impact of railroads, the cattle industry and gold prospecting","open":false,"back":"Custer's expedition reported gold on French Creek in the summer of 1874. Prospectors poured into hills guaranteed to the Lakota in 1868; the government failed to buy them in 1875 and war followed in 1876."},{"front":"The second Fort Laramie Treaty (1868)","code":"P3-2 — 3 Changes in the ways of life of Indigenous peoples of the Plains: The impact of US government policy","open":false,"back":"Ended Red Cloud's War. The army abandoned the Bozeman Trail forts; the Great Sioux Reservation, including the Black Hills, was recognised, with the Powder River country as unceded hunting ground. Red Cloud signed on 6 November 1868."},{"front":"The Sand Creek Massacre, 29 November 1864","code":"P3-2 — 3 Changes in the ways of life of Indigenous peoples of the Plains: Conflict on the Plains","open":false,"back":"Colonel Chivington's Colorado volunteers attacked Black Kettle's Cheyenne and Arapaho camp, which had come in under a promise of safety, killing at least 150 people, most of them women and children. A congressional inquiry condemned it."},{"front":"Red Cloud's War (1866-68) and the Fetterman fight","code":"P3-2 — 3 Changes in the ways of life of Indigenous peoples of the Plains: Conflict on the Plains","open":false,"back":"Lakota, Cheyenne and Arapaho fought the forts guarding the Bozeman Trail. On 21 December 1866 a detachment of 81 men under Captain Fetterman was decoyed from Fort Phil Kearny and wiped out."}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Paper 2 booklet P Question 2, narrative account, 8 marks)","items":["Question: 'Write a narrative account analysing the changing role of the cowboy in the years 1866-1876.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the long drives north from Texas; the round-up. You must also use information of your own. This is the middle stretch of the account, carrying the story from the trail crews of the late 1860s to the ranch work of the mid-1870s.","Because the buyer stood at a railhead and not in Texas, the cowboy of the late 1860s was hired for a journey: a crew of about a dozen men walked two or three thousand head up the Chisholm Trail to the Kansas railheads over two or three months, riding point, swing and drag by day, standing night watch against stampedes, swimming the rivers and paying the nations of Indian Territory for the right to cross. That work began to change as soon as cattle stopped having to start in Texas every spring. In 1868 Charles Goodnight contracted to deliver Texas stock to John Iliff at Cheyenne on the new Union Pacific line, and by 1870 he was ranching on the Arkansas near Pueblo, so the northern grass was carrying herds of its own. Men were therefore kept on by one outfit all year instead of being paid off at the end of a drive: they worked the spring round-up, branding the calves by their mothers because the range had no fences, rode the line to hold cattle on their own grass, cut hay and dug animals out of the winter drifts. The wage was monthly and the work was an employer's, which is why the drive and the ranch produced two different kinds of cowboy.","Why this is Level 3: the material is organised as a clear sequence leading to an outcome, and the linkage between the events is analysed rather than asserted. The trail crew's work is explained by where the buyer stood, and the ranch year is explained as the consequence of cattle being held in the north. The knowledge is precise and relevant, with a named trail, named men, a dated contract and the actual tasks of the round-up, and it addresses more than three separate aspects of content, so the cap on Level 3 does not bite."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same, 'Write a narrative account analysing the changing role of the cowboy in the years 1866-1876.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the long drives north from Texas; the round-up. You must also use information of your own.","Cowboys took big herds of cattle from Texas up the trails to Kansas. It was hard work that took months, with stampedes at night and rivers to cross. Later on ranches were started further north in Colorado and Wyoming, so cowboys worked on the ranches instead. They did the round-up in the spring, branded the calves and looked after the cattle through the winter. Homesteaders also began fencing land with barbed wire, so there was less open range, and the cowboy's life was not really like the stories told about it.","Why this is only Level 2, and what it misses: the events are in the right order and the information is accurate, so it meets 'some organisation of material into a sequence of events leading to an outcome' and 'accurate and relevant information is added'. It misses the Level 3 requirement that the account 'analyses the linkage between them': 'so cowboys worked on the ranches instead' asserts the change instead of explaining why holding cattle in the north made a year-round hand necessary, and nothing says why the herds went to Kansas in the first place. The knowledge is also thin where the grid wants precision, since no date, name or named trail is given."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the railroads and the Pacific Railroad Act (1862)","items":["Pacific Railroad Act signed 1 July 1862, while the southern states were out of Congress during the Civil War.","It chartered the Union Pacific to build west from Omaha, Nebraska, and authorised the Central Pacific to build east from Sacramento, California.","Payment: alternate sections of public land along the track, plus government loans by the mile, roughly 16,000 dollars a mile on level ground, 32,000 across hills and 48,000 in the mountains.","The two lines met at Promontory Summit, Utah, on 10 May 1869, completing the first transcontinental railroad.","A journey that had taken months by wagon train now took about a week, at far less risk and cost.","Because the companies were paid in land they could not farm, they advertised it in the eastern states and in Europe and sold claims cheaply, so railroads recruited the settlers who filled their trains.","Branch and feeder lines spread the effect: the railroad reached Abilene, Kansas, in 1867 and Denver in 1870, and the Atchison, Topeka and Santa Fe reached Dodge City in 1872.","Freight ran both ways, so wheat, hides and cattle could leave the Plains for eastern markets at a price that made farming and ranching worth doing.","Track cut through hunting grounds, split the buffalo into northern and southern herds, and carried the hide hunters who destroyed them.","Towns grew where the railroad chose to stop, which is why a place like Abilene mattered far beyond its size."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Homestead Act (1862), the Timber Culture Act (1873) and the homesteaders' solutions","items":["Homestead Act signed 20 May 1862; in force from 1 January 1863.","Terms: 160 acres to any citizen over 21 or head of a family, including single women and, after the Civil War, freed slaves, for a ten dollar filing fee, with title after five years of residence and improvement.","The problem: 160 acres of dry grassland could not be farmed like 160 acres in the east, and most homesteaders arrived with little capital.","Timber Culture Act passed 3 March 1873: a further 160 acres to a settler who planted trees on 40 of them, so a claim could reach 320 acres.","Most of the trees failed in the wind and drought, speculators abused the Act, and the planting requirement was cut from 40 acres to ten in 1878.","No timber: houses were built of sod bricks cut from the prairie, or dug into a bank as dugouts.","No surface water: settlers sank deep wells and raised water with wind pumps.","Tough sod and drought: steel ploughs broke the turf, and dry farming meant ploughing deep after rain and harrowing the surface to a dust mulch to hold the moisture in.","No fencing material until Joseph Glidden of DeKalb, Illinois patented a cheap, workable barbed wire on 24 November 1874.","Wrong seed: Mennonite families arriving in Kansas from Russia in 1874 brought Turkey Red, a hard winter wheat that suited the Plains climate.","Disasters they could not solve: prairie fires, blizzards and the grasshopper plague that stripped crops across Kansas and Nebraska in 1874."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: law and order in the settlements","items":["County sheriffs were elected, usually for a two-year term, and appointed their own deputies.","Towns appointed marshals and deputy marshals, paid by the town council.","The federal government appointed a US marshal for each territory, who appointed deputies; federal judges rode a circuit of courts.","The problem was scale: counties could be the size of an English county or larger, officers were few, badly paid and often far away when needed.","Cow towns were the hardest test, because trail crews arrived together with months of pay, revolvers and saloons, gambling houses and brothels waiting for the money.","Towns answered with ordinances banning the carrying of firearms inside the town limits, and by hiring men hard enough to enforce them: Wild Bill Hickok was marshal of Abilene in 1871.","Towns also depended on the cattle trade for income, so officers were under pressure to keep order without driving the business away.","Where no officer existed, settlers formed vigilance committees and hanged suspected rustlers, horse thieves and claim jumpers without trial.","Vigilante justice was quick and popular but had no appeal and no check, and was later used to settle private quarrels over land and stock.","Federal influence grew through appointed territorial governors and judges, army posts, the US marshals and the courts; a territory that filled up could become a state, as Colorado did on 1 August 1876."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the cattle industry, ranching and the cowboy","items":["Texas ended the Civil War with about five million longhorns, many unbranded, running on open range while the men were away at the war.","Prices: about five dollars a head in Texas against up to forty at a northern railhead, so the whole problem was moving them.","1866: Charles Goodnight and Oliver Loving drove some 2,000 head to Fort Sumner, New Mexico, where the army had thousands of Navajo to feed at the Bosque Redondo reservation; the trail was then extended north into Colorado.","Oliver Loving was attacked and wounded in 1867 and died of his injuries at Fort Sumner in September of that year.","John Iliff bought Texas cattle from the Goodnight trail, built up herds on the Colorado plains and sold beef to railroad construction crews and to Indian agencies, showing cattle could winter on northern grass.","1867: Joseph McCoy built stockyards, pens, a hotel and a bank at Abilene, Kansas, on the railroad and beyond the settled farming counties.","The first twenty carloads of longhorns left Abilene for Chicago on 5 September 1867; about 35,000 head came up the Chisholm Trail that season and more than 1.5 million passed through Abilene by 1871.","Texas fever: longhorns carried ticks whose disease killed local cattle, so Kansas and Missouri farmers pressed for quarantine lines that shut the herds out of settled counties.","Winter 1871-72: the Farmers' Protective Association of Dickinson County printed a circular, backed by the officers and citizens of Dickinson County, asking drovers to keep Texas cattle away; the trade moved west to Ellsworth, Wichita and then Dodge City, which the Santa Fe railroad reached in September 1872.","The cowboy's year changed as ranching spread north: less time on two- and three-month drives, more on round-ups, branding, riding the line, haymaking and winter feeding for a monthly wage.","Ranchers and homesteaders clashed over the same ground: herds trampled crops and carried fever, while homesteaders used barbed wire to enclose land and water the ranchers had grazed in common."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: US government policy, treaties and the reservations","items":["Policy after 1862 meant reservations: fixed boundaries, an agency, rations and supplies, schools and instruction in farming, in exchange for giving up the open hunting life.","Medicine Lodge Creek, Kansas, October 1867: treaties with the Kiowa, Comanche and Plains Apache and with the southern Cheyenne and Arapaho, moving them to reservations in Indian Territory.","Second Fort Laramie Treaty, 1868: the United States abandoned the Bozeman Trail forts and closed the road.","The same treaty recognised the Great Sioux Reservation, the land west of the Missouri River in what is now South Dakota, including the Black Hills, for the use of the Sioux.","It left the Powder River country as unceded hunting ground that whites could not enter without permission.","It required the signatures of three-quarters of the adult men before any part of the reservation could be given up; Red Cloud signed the treaty on 6 November 1868.","From 1869 President Grant's Peace Policy put agencies in the hands of men nominated by the churches, and Congress created a Board of Indian Commissioners in April 1869 to watch over supplies.","Reservation conditions: rations were often short, late or stolen by corrupt agents; disease spread in the crowded camps; hunting was restricted and chiefs lost their authority.","The aim of policy was assimilation: to turn hunters into Christian farmers living in families on small plots.","Custer's Black Hills expedition reported gold in the summer of 1874; the government failed to buy the Hills in 1875, ordered bands outside the agencies to report by 31 January 1876, and war followed."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: conflict on the Plains, 1862 to 1868","items":["Little Crow's War began in Minnesota in August 1862, when the Santee Dakota faced crop failure, late annuity payments and traders who refused credit.","More than 400 settlers were killed before Colonel Sibley defeated the Dakota at Wood Lake on 23 September 1862.","38 Dakota men were hanged together at Mankato on 26 December 1862, the largest mass execution in United States history; the survivors lost their Minnesota reservation and were moved to Dakota Territory.","Sand Creek, 29 November 1864: Colonel John Chivington's Colorado volunteers attacked Black Kettle's Cheyenne and Arapaho camp near Fort Lyon, which had come in under a promise of protection.","At least 150 people were killed at Sand Creek, most of them women and children; Black Kettle survived; a congressional inquiry condemned the attack.","The immediate effect of Sand Creek was more war, not less: Cheyenne, Arapaho and Lakota war parties struck the central Plains routes through 1865.","Red Cloud's War, 1866 to 1868, was fought over the Bozeman Trail to the Montana goldfields and the forts built to guard it, among them Fort Reno, Fort Phil Kearny and Fort C.F. Smith.","The Fetterman fight, 21 December 1866: a detachment of 81 men under Captain William Fetterman was decoyed away from Fort Phil Kearny and wiped out; Crazy Horse was among the decoys.","Red Cloud's people kept the forts under pressure until the government accepted that the road could not be held.","The war ended in the second Fort Laramie Treaty of 1868, the one occasion on which the United States abandoned forts and conceded the terms a Plains people demanded."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 2 booklet P Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the changing role of the cowboy in the years 1866-1876' (8 marks)","items":["The question as printed: 'Write a narrative account analysing the changing role of the cowboy in the years 1866-1876.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the long drives north from Texas; the round-up. You must also use information of your own.","Decode it before writing. 'Narrative account' means a sequence that leads somewhere, so the account must have a definite beginning and end; 'analysing' means the join between each episode and the next has to be explained, not assumed; and the dates fix the boundaries, so the herds the Civil War left in Texas belong only in an opening sentence and the fencing of the range in the 1880s does not belong at all.","The cap that decides the level: there is no access to Level 3 at all for an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content. Decide first which episodes are your own material, since the two bullets alone are only two aspects.","Timing: about 13 minutes on this question in a booklet of 32 marks, so give two minutes to choosing the three or four episodes and the rest to writing; do not plan in sentences.","Opening sentence, the situation the sequence starts from: in 1866 the only way to turn a Texas longhorn into money was to walk it to a buyer, so a cowboy was hired for a season's journey rather than for a year's work.","Episode 1, the long drives north from Texas, a stimulus point: from 1867 herds went up the Chisholm Trail to the Kansas railheads, a crew of about a dozen men taking two or three thousand head over two or three months, riding point, swing and drag, standing night watch against stampedes, swimming the rivers and paying the nations of Indian Territory for the right to cross.","The link out of episode 1: that work existed only because the buyer stood at a railhead, so when quarantine lines pushed the railheads west, to Ellsworth and Wichita in 1872 and to Dodge City by the middle of the 1870s, the drives grew longer and the men on them were kept further from the settled counties.","Episode 2, the point beyond the stimulus, cattle held in the north instead of walked up from Texas every spring: in 1868 Charles Goodnight contracted to deliver Texas cattle to John Iliff at Cheyenne on the Union Pacific line, and by 1870 he was ranching on the Arkansas near Pueblo in Colorado, so northern grass began to carry herds of its own.","Episode 3, the round-up, the second stimulus point: on unfenced range the outfits worked the spring round-up together, cutting out and branding the calves by their mothers, and the same men then rode the line to hold cattle on their own grass, cut hay and dug animals out of the winter drifts for a monthly wage on one ranch.","Episode 4, also your own material, the ground closing in: Joseph Glidden's barbed wire, patented on 24 November 1874, let homesteaders fence crops and water across the routes, and the quarantine lines kept moving west, so the open country the drives depended on was already shrinking before the period ends.","The evaluation thread that runs through all four: the cowboy's job follows the market for beef rather than the other way about, so where the cattle were sold decides whether he was a driver or a ranch hand.","What the closing sentence must say: by 1876 the cowboy was becoming a hired hand working one outfit's range through the year rather than a trail driver taken on for a season, which is the change the whole sequence leads to."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:25:40.535740+00:00","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"The fix pass has done what the first verdict asked and the new material stands up: the plan and both model paragraphs now work a narrative account that is not in the bank ('the changing role of the cowboy in the years 1866-1876', stimulus points the long drives north from Texas and the round-up), worked example 3 has moved to the growth of towns on the Plains 1867-1876 and worked example 5 to Grant's Peace Policy, so no worked example, plan or model paragraph now sets or pre-answers a bank question except the residual barbed-wire overlap noted above; question 13 is now the importance of the railroad land grants, which no longer echoes the specimen bullet, and question 14 now stands on the trail itself rather than on John Iliff, so nothing reproduces a printed specimen or June 2023 live P3 question. I re-solved all sixteen questions and re-verified the new material on the web - Goodnight's spring 1868 contract to deliver to John Wesley Iliff at Cheyenne on the Union Pacific and his Rock Canon ranch west of Pueblo, the 1866 drive of 2,000 head from Fort Belknap by Horsehead Crossing up the Pecos to Fort Sumner with the beef sold to the army at eight cents a pound for about 12,000 dollars in gold, Loving taking the remainder north to Colorado and selling to Iliff, the Farmers' Protective Association of Dickinson County's circular of winter 1871-72 asking drovers to ship from somewhere else, Grenville Dodge's Crow Creek townsite of July 1867 and the Union Pacific reaching Cheyenne on 13 November 1867, Dodge City laid out in 1872 with the Santa Fe arriving that September, the Mennonites of 1874 buying their Kansas land from the Atchison, Topeka and Santa Fe, the Board of Indian Commissioners of April 1869 and Ely Parker as Commissioner of Indian Affairs, and the two Friends bodies holding sixteen agencies against the Methodists' fourteen, which supports 'the Quakers taking the largest share' - alongside the sixty or so dates and figures carried over unchanged from the first pass, and found no wrong date, figure, event, attribution, tariff, cap or level label. The paper form is current throughout: two-part Question 1 at 4 + 4, stimulus points and the 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' cap on Question 2 only, no Level 2 cap, no SPaG, Paper 2 at 1 hour 50 minutes and about 55 minutes a booklet. Publishable as it stands; the barbed-wire example is the one thing I would still change before a student sees it."}}},"P3-3":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"P3-3","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 3 is the easiest stretch of the American West to score on and the easiest to waste, because almost everything in it is a consequence of something else. Booklet P asks you for one explained consequence twice, for an analytical narrative once, and for the importance of two named things; and the years c1876 to c1895 hand you ready-made chains - the Little Bighorn leads to the seizure of the Black Hills, the winter of 1886-87 leads to fenced ranches and then to the Johnson County War, the killing of the buffalo leads to the ration line and the Dawes Act. Students lose marks here by telling the story of Custer or of Wyatt Earp beautifully and never saying what difference it made. Learn this topic as a set of links, with a date and a name on each link, and the three question types answer themselves.","idea":[{"h":"Three problems, three inventions: how the Plains were finally farmed","t":"The Homestead Act of 1862 gave 160 acres after five years of residence, but 160 acres of dry grassland was not a living, so Congress kept adding to it: the Timber Culture Act of 1873 offered another 160 acres to anyone who planted trees on 40 of them (cut to 10 acres in 1878), and the Desert Land Act of 3 March 1877 let a settler take up to 640 acres at $1.25 an acre, 25 cents of it down at entry and the remaining dollar an acre once he had proved he had irrigated the land within three years. Law alone did not solve the three practical problems - no wood for fences, no surface water, too little rain - and technology did. Joseph Glidden patented cheap barbed wire in 1874, so a field could be fenced without timber; wind pumps drew water from wells far below the surface; steel ploughs broke the sod and mechanical reapers and threshers let a family work more land than it could harvest by hand; dry farming, ploughing the surface after rain to trap moisture, stretched what rain there was; and Turkey Red wheat, a hard winter variety brought to Kansas by Mennonite migrants from Russia in 1874, ripened in conditions that killed softer strains. A top-level answer never lists these. It matches each one to the problem it solved and then says what became possible because of it - for example that barbed wire made the homesteader's claim defensible, which is exactly what made the cattlemen hate it.","code":"P3-3 — 1 Changes in farming, the cattle industry and settlement: Changes in farming"},{"h":"The winter that killed the open range, and the business that replaced it","t":"The open range worked while grass was free and cattle were few. By the mid-1880s neither was true: the boom of the early 1880s had drawn in British and eastern investors and packed the northern ranges with far more cattle than they could carry, and beef prices were already falling in 1885 and 1886. Then the summer of 1886 was hot and dry and left the range almost bare, so the herds went into the winter thin. Blizzards ran from November 1886 into the spring of 1887 in what ranchers called the Great Die-Up: Montana ranchers alone lost something over half the territory's cattle, and on the worst northern ranges outfits lost almost everything. The men who survived did not rebuild the old system, they replaced it. Herds became smaller and were kept on land the rancher owned and fenced with barbed wire; hay was cut and stored so cattle could be fed through a bad winter; wells and wind pumps supplied water; heavier Hereford crosses replaced the tough Longhorn that had been left to fend for itself; and the cowboy's job changed from droving to fencing, haymaking and riding fence lines. The long drive and the open range were finished. A top-level answer refuses to make the weather the whole explanation: overstocking and falling prices had already made the open range fragile, and the winter of 1886-87 is best treated as the shock that exposed a business model that was failing anyway.","code":"P3-3 — 1 Changes in farming, the cattle industry and settlement: Changes in the cattle industry"},{"h":"Who was still moving west after 1876, and what happened when the land ran out","t":"Two very different movements fill the later years. The first came out of the South. When Reconstruction ended in 1877, African Americans in states such as Mississippi, Louisiana, Tennessee and Kentucky faced violence, debt and the loss of the vote, and promoters, above all Benjamin 'Pap' Singleton, advertised Kansas as a place to own land. Around six thousand arrived in the spring of 1879 alone and perhaps twenty thousand within two years; these were the Exodusters. Most came with nothing, so relief committees had to be set up to feed them; some took homesteads or joined all-black settlements such as Nicodemus, founded in 1877, while many ended as labourers in Kansas towns. The second movement was the scramble for the last big block of land. Indian Territory had been set aside for relocated nations, but at its centre lay the Unassigned Lands, about 1.9 million acres ceded by the Creek and Seminole in 1866 and never assigned to any nation, and Boomers campaigned to have them opened; President Harrison proclaimed them open for settlement: at noon on 22 April 1889 roughly fifty thousand people raced in, Guthrie and Oklahoma City grew from nothing to thousands of inhabitants by nightfall, and Sooners who had crept in early took some of the best claims. Oklahoma Territory was created on 2 May 1890, and the largest run of all, for the Cherokee Outlet of over six million acres, followed on 16 September 1893. The census of 1890 reported that the unsettled area was so broken up that a frontier line could hardly be said to exist. A top-level answer links the two: the same shortage of free land that pulled Exodusters onto poor Kansas claims is what forced the government to break the promise on which the Indian Frontier rested.","code":"P3-3 — 1 Changes in farming, the cattle industry and settlement: Continued settlement"},{"h":"Lawmen who were also players: Billy the Kid, the Earps and the limits of frontier law","t":"Law in the later West was thin and local. Counties elected a sheriff, towns appointed a marshal, and territories had federal US marshals and their deputies, but a sheriff might have one or two deputies for a county the size of Wales, prisoners had to be held and juries found locally, and where the courts failed, vigilance committees acted instead. The result was that lawmen were often part of the quarrel rather than above it. In the Lincoln County War of 1878 in New Mexico, rival merchant and ranching factions fought for the county's trade with lawmen on both sides, and Henry McCarty - better known as William H. Bonney, or Billy the Kid - fought for one of them before being hunted down and shot by Sheriff Pat Garrett at Fort Sumner on 14 July 1881. In Tombstone, Arizona, Virgil Earp was town marshal and his brothers Wyatt and Morgan and their friend Doc Holliday were with him when they confronted the Clantons and McLaurys in a vacant lot near the rear of the OK Corral on 26 October 1881; the fight lasted about thirty seconds, Billy Clanton and Tom and Frank McLaury were killed, Virgil, Morgan and Holliday were wounded, and the Earps were charged before a judge decided they had acted as officers of the law. A top-level answer uses these cases to make an argument about law and order rather than to tell a good story: they show that the problem was not the absence of law but the fact that the men wearing the badges had interests of their own.","code":"P3-3 — 2 Conflict and tension: Dealing with law and order"},{"h":"The range wars: fences, mavericks and the Johnson County invasion of 1892","t":"Range wars were fights over the two things the open range had left undefined: who owned the grass and water, and who owned unbranded cattle. As homesteaders and small ranchers took up land along the creeks and fenced it, the big cattlemen lost the free range their herds depended on, and both sides cut fences, dammed water and drove off stock. In Wyoming the powerful Wyoming Stock Growers Association had used the law against the small men - the Maverick Law of 1884 gave unbranded cattle to the association rather than to whoever found them, and although the territorial legislature repealed it in 1888 under pressure from small stockmen the bitterness it caused lasted - and the association went on blacklisting small men as rustlers, which made it impossible for them to sell stock or find work. In April 1892 the association took the next step: fifty-two armed men, twenty-three of them hired Texas gunmen, led by Frank Wolcott and Frank Canton, rode into Johnson County with a list of names. On 9 April they surrounded the KC Ranch and killed Nate Champion and Nick Ray. The county turned out against them, and a posse of hundreds under Sheriff Angus besieged them at the TA Ranch from 11 April until cavalry from Fort McKinney took them into custody on 13 April. The prisoners were moved out of the county and the charges were eventually dropped. A top-level answer treats the Johnson County War as evidence about power, not about cowboys: the cattle barons could raise a private army, and the federal government and the courts protected them from the county they had invaded.","code":"P3-3 — 2 Conflict and tension: The range wars"},{"h":"From the Little Bighorn to Wounded Knee: how armed resistance ended","t":"Gold found in the Black Hills during Custer's expedition of 1874 broke the Fort Laramie Treaty of 1868, and when the government ordered all Lakota onto the agencies by 31 January 1876 the Great Sioux War followed. Crook was turned back at the Rosebud on 17 June 1876, and on 25-26 June Custer attacked an enormous village of Lakota, Northern Cheyenne and Arapaho on the Little Bighorn with a divided command: Custer and about 210 men of the five companies with him were killed, and the 7th Cavalry lost 268 dead in all. The victory of Sitting Bull's and Crazy Horse's people destroyed them politically. The news reached the east at the centenary of independence, outrage followed, Congress attached a sell-or-starve rider to the Indian Appropriation Act that cut off rations until the Black Hills were surrendered, and the Agreement of February 1877 took them. The army kept columns in the field all winter; Crazy Horse surrendered in May 1877 and was killed at Fort Robinson on 5 September 1877, and Sitting Bull, having fled to Canada, surrendered at Fort Buford on 19 July 1881. Fourteen years later the last act was not a battle. As the Ghost Dance spread, Sitting Bull was killed by Indian police at Standing Rock on 15 December 1890, and on 29 December 1890 the 7th Cavalry under Colonel Forsyth, disarming Spotted Elk's band at Wounded Knee Creek on the Pine Ridge reservation, killed between 250 and 300 Lakota, many of them women and children, losing 25 soldiers. A top-level answer says the obvious hard thing: the Little Bighorn is important because winning it guaranteed defeat.","code":"P3-3 — 2 Conflict and tension: Conflict on the Plains"},{"h":"Killing the buffalo: the cheapest way to end a way of life","t":"The buffalo was not simply food. It supplied hides for tipis, robes and clothing, sinew for thread and bowstrings, bones and horn for tools, and dried dung for fuel on a treeless plain, and the hunt shaped the year, the religion and the standing of men within a band. Three things destroyed the herds after the railroads reached the Plains. The railway split the great herd in two, carried hunters out and hides back, and paid crews to shoot buffalo to feed its construction gangs. From about 1871 a new tanning process made buffalo hide useful as industrial belting, so hide hunting became a business with a guaranteed market, and hunters with long-range rifles could kill scores of animals from a single stand. And the army encouraged it, because officers understood that a Plains nation without buffalo could not stay off the reservation. The southern herd was effectively gone by about 1878 and the northern herd by about 1883; when William Hornaday counted in the late 1880s he could find only a few hundred wild buffalo left in the whole United States. A top-level answer joins the killing to the ration line: once the herds were gone, food came from an agency issue, the agent could withhold it, and so the destruction of the buffalo did more to end independence on the Plains than any battle.","code":"P3-3 — 3 Indigenous peoples of the Plains: The hunting and extermination of the buffalo"},{"h":"Life on the reservations: rations, agents, police and boarding schools","t":"A reservation was land the government had not wanted, and it kept shrinking as new demands were made on it. On it, power belonged to the Indian agent. He issued the rations that families now depended on and could cut them to punish; he controlled who could leave, so a hunting or trading journey needed a pass; he dealt with individuals rather than with chiefs, which stripped leaders of the authority they had earned; and he had Indian police recruited from the reservation itself and, from the rules of 1883, Courts of Indian Offenses which banned ceremonies including the Sun Dance and the work of medicine men. Rations were often short or spoiled because Congress cut appropriations and contractors cheated, and hunger and disease were normal. Children were the second front: the Carlisle Indian Industrial School opened in Pennsylvania in 1879 under Richard Henry Pratt, took children hundreds of miles from home, cut their hair, renamed them, forbade their languages and trained them for trades, and other boarding schools copied it. Since the Indian Appropriation Act of 1871 the government had stopped making treaties, so nations were no longer dealt with as independent peoples at all. A top-level answer describes the reservation as a system of control rather than as a place, and shows how each part of it - the ration, the pass, the police, the school - removed one of the supports of the old way of life.","code":"P3-3 — 3 Indigenous peoples of the Plains: Indigenous people's lives on the reservations"},{"h":"From treaties to allotment: what the Dawes Act was for","t":"Government policy moved through three attitudes. Until 1871 the Plains nations were treated, at least on paper, as peoples to be dealt with by treaty; the Indian Appropriation Act of 1871 ended treaty-making and made them wards of the government. Through the 1870s the policy was concentration: push everyone onto reservations and hold them there with rations. From the 1880s the policy became assimilation: the reservation was now seen as the obstacle, because communal land and communal life kept people apart from American society. The General Allotment Act, signed on 8 February 1887 and known after its sponsor as the Dawes Act, carried that out. Reservation land was to be surveyed and divided: 160 acres to the head of a family, 80 acres to a single adult and 40 acres to a child, twice as much where the land was fit only for grazing. The allotment was held in trust by the government for 25 years, at the end of which the allottee received full title; allottees were also made United States citizens, and the land left over after allotment - the 'surplus' - was sold to white settlers. The effect was the opposite of protection: land held by Indigenous nations across the country fell from about 138 million acres in 1887 to about 48 million by 1934, much of it sold off as surplus or lost by allottees once the trust period ended. A top-level answer judges the Act by that result and by its intention together: it was designed to dissolve the tribe, and it succeeded mainly in taking the land.","code":"P3-3 — 3 Indigenous peoples of the Plains: The significance of changing government attitudes"}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1(a): turning the Timber Culture Act into one explained consequence","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(a): 'Explain one consequence of the Timber Culture Act of 1873 for settlers on the Plains.' [4 marks]. The part is marked out of 4 on a two-level grid, and the top level needs features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific supporting information added. You know the following: the Homestead Act of 1862 gave a quarter section of 160 acres after five years of residence; the Timber Culture Act of 1873 offered a further 160 acres to anyone who planted trees on 40 of them; there was almost no natural timber on the open Plains and seedlings died in drought and grasshopper years; Congress cut the planting requirement from 40 acres to 10 in 1878; claims were often filed to hold the land rather than to raise timber, and speculators and cattlemen filed them too; the Act was repealed in 1891; a quarter section was too little to live on where the rainfall was low; wire, wind pumps and machinery all had to be paid for.","steps":["Sort the Act from its results before writing, because only a result can score: planting trees on 40 acres of a claim is what the law demanded, while the size of the holdings that grew up around it is what followed from the demand.","State the consequence in one sentence that uses the word: one consequence was that the working unit of settlement on the dry Plains grew past the 160-acre quarter section, because a family could hold a homestead and a tree claim side by side.","Support it with detail that shows the law working as land policy and not as forestry: the seedlings died in dry summers, so claims were filed for the acres rather than for the timber, which is why Congress cut the planting requirement from 40 acres to 10 in 1878 and repealed the Act altogether in 1891.","Explain the chain instead of asserting it: a holding of 320 acres was what made the cost of wire, a well and machinery worth bearing, and the same rule let speculators and cattlemen tie up land they never meant to plant, so the Act enlarged farms and favoured men with capital at the same time."],"cues":["Which part of this is what the law asked for, and which part is what happened because of it?","What single sentence would name that result without retelling the Act?","Which detail shows that settlers were after the acres rather than the trees?","Why does a bigger holding change what a family can afford to put on it?"]},{"title":"Question 2: building the spine of a narrative account on the road to the Little Bighorn","setup":"Booklet P, Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the events that led to the Battle of the Little Bighorn in the years 1874-1876.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the Black Hills gold rush (1874); the order that the Lakota report to the agencies by 31 January 1876. You must also use information of your own. The mark scheme prints one cap: no access to the top level for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content. You know the following: the Fort Laramie Treaty of 1868 had recognised the Black Hills as part of the Great Sioux Reservation and closed the country to whites, and required the signatures of three-quarters of the adult men before any further land could be given up; Custer took an expedition into the hills in 1874 and the report of gold brought miners in by the thousand; the army's attempts to turn them back failed; in 1875 a commission under Senator William Allison offered to buy or to lease the hills and was refused; in December 1875 the government ordered every band still outside the agencies to come in by 31 January 1876, and those who did not were declared hostile and the matter handed to the army; three columns took the field in the summer of 1876; Crook was turned back at the Rosebud on 17 June 1876; Custer attacked a very large village of Lakota, Northern Cheyenne and Arapaho on the Little Bighorn on 25 June 1876 with his command divided.","steps":["Choose four episodes that run in order and end where the question ends, and mark which are your own material rather than the two bullets: the treaty of 1868 and what gold did to it, the refusal to sell in 1875, the winter order, and the campaign that followed.","Write the first link rather than starting a new paragraph: because the treaty had closed the hills to whites, the gold rush did not merely bring miners, it left the government choosing between enforcing its own treaty against its own citizens and breaking it.","Write the second link so that the refusal explains the next episode: once the Lakota would neither sell nor lease, the only courses left were to give up the hills or to take them, which is why an order to come in followed within months.","Write the third link so the war is caused rather than announced: a deadline of 31 January that no band could meet in deep snow turned people wintering where they always had into hostiles, moved the business from the agents to the army, and sent three columns into the Powder River country, one of which Crook had to lead back after the Rosebud on 17 June.","Close on the battle as the outcome of the sequence and not as a fresh story: the village those columns were hunting was the largest on the Plains, Custer reached it first and attacked with his regiment split, so the defeat was the product of everything the sequence had already put in place."],"cues":["Which four episodes take you from the treaty to the battle, and which two of them are the bullets you were given?","Why did a gold strike here matter more than a gold strike on public land?","What courses were left once the owners had refused to sell?","Why was a winter deadline the thing that turned a dispute into a war?","What does your last sentence have to say about why the battle happened at all?"]},{"title":"Question 3: explaining the importance of the reservation system for Indigenous leaders","setup":"Booklet P, Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. The bullet here is: 'The importance of the reservation system for the authority of chiefs and headmen of the Plains nations.' (8 marks) No stimulus points are printed. You know the following: on a reservation the agent issued the rations and the passes needed to leave, and dealt with individual heads of families rather than with chiefs; Indian police were recruited on the reservations from the late 1870s, and the rules of 1883 set up Courts of Indian Offenses, with Indigenous judges appointed by the agent, which punished the Sun Dance and other ceremonies and the work of medicine men; a chief's authority had rested on success in hunting and war, on giving away what he had and on persuasion rather than command; the warrior societies that had policed a camp on the move had no such work on a reservation; Sitting Bull, held for two years after his surrender in 1881 and then settled at Standing Rock, lived under an agent who decided what he might do; the buffalo were gone, so no leader could feed his followers by the old means.","steps":["Fix what importance means before writing: not what the system looked like but what difference it made, so every paragraph has to finish on something that had changed about who led and how.","Take the material base first, because authority had rested on it: a chief led by giving away what the hunt brought in, so once food arrived as a ration issued to each family at the agency, the very thing he had led with was in another man's hands.","Support that with the machinery that took over his work: passes decided who could travel, police recruited from the reservation itself did what the warrior societies had done, and the agency courts of 1883 punished the ceremonies at which standing had been earned and confirmed.","Take the second change, which is where authority now came from: the man who mattered was the man the agent chose to deal with, so influence ran from the agency outwards instead of from the people upwards, which is why a leader of Sitting Bull's standing could be watched, kept to his settlement and in the end arrested by police of his own nation.","Judge it: say which mattered more, the loss of the means of leading or the appearance of a rival authority inside the nation itself, and name the test you are judging by."],"cues":["What must a point do before it counts as importance rather than description?","What had a chief's power actually rested on before the reservation?","Who did the work on a reservation that the warrior societies used to do?","Where did a leader's influence now have to come from?","Which of the two changes would you argue was the greater, and on what test?"]},{"title":"Question 1(b): a consequence of Turkey Red wheat, not a description of it","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(b): 'Explain one consequence of the arrival of Turkey Red wheat in Kansas.' [4 marks]. You know the following: Mennonite families who had been farming in southern Russia settled in central Kansas in 1874 and brought with them a hard winter wheat known as Turkey Red; it is sown in the autumn, so it uses the winter and spring moisture and ripens before the worst of the summer heat, and it stood cold that killed the softer spring wheats then grown; the central Plains have low and unreliable rainfall and early settlers had lost crops to drought; dry farming, ploughing the surface after rain to hold moisture, was spreading in the same years; barbed wire, wind pumps and mechanical reapers and threshers were arriving; the railways carried grain east to market.","steps":["Refuse the description first: saying that the wheat was hard, red and foreign explains nothing, because a consequence has to be something that happened afterwards because it arrived.","Name the consequence in one sentence: one consequence was that the dry central Plains at last had a crop that could be relied on to ripen there, so grain farming spread west onto ground that had been thought fit only for grazing.","Prove the link with the detail that does the work: sown in the autumn and ripening early, the crop was made before the summer drought that had been ruining spring-sown wheat, so a harvest no longer depended on rain falling at exactly the right week.","Close the chain on what that made possible: a dependable cash crop justified the machinery and the freight charges, and gave a homesteader something to sell and not only something to eat, which is what turned a claim into a farm."],"cues":["Why does describing the wheat itself score nothing here?","What could farmers do afterwards that they could not do before?","Which feature of a winter wheat explains why it survived where others failed?","What does a crop you can sell let a family pay for?"]},{"title":"Question 3: judging the importance of a ban that took no land","setup":"Booklet P, Question 3, one bullet marked out of 8: 'The importance of the banning of the Sun Dance and other ceremonies from 1883 for the peoples of the reservations.' (8 marks) No stimulus points are printed. You know the following: rules issued in 1883 set up Courts of Indian Offenses at the agencies, with Indigenous judges appointed by the agent, and made offences of the Sun Dance and other dances, of the work of medicine men and of several customs of marriage and mourning; punishment could be confinement or the withholding of rations; the Sun Dance was the great gathering of the year, at which scattered bands came together, young men made their name and obligations were renewed; medicine men were healers and keepers of what a band knew about itself; the agent already controlled the rations, the passes and the Indian police; the buffalo hunt that had set the rhythm of the year was already gone.","steps":["Separate the size of the change from the size of the document, because the examiner rewards difference made: a few pages of departmental rules abolished no reservation and took no acre, so the answer has to argue about what they reached that land law could not.","Make the first claim about the year itself: the ceremonies were the calendar, so forbidding the great summer gathering removed the occasion on which scattered bands met, decisions were taken in common and a young man earned his standing.","Support it with the way the ban was enforced rather than with its wording: offences were tried at the agency by judges the agent appointed and could be punished by cutting the ration, so the power to say what was a crime and the power to feed a family sat in the same hands.","Take the second change, which is to knowledge: outlawing the work of medicine men struck at healing and at the keeping of what a nation knew about itself, and drove both into secrecy, so the practice survived but could no longer be taught in the open to the next generation.","Judge it: argue whether the ban mattered most for what it stopped or for who it put in charge of deciding, and say what evidence would settle the question either way."],"cues":["Why is a short set of rules not automatically a small change?","What did the summer gathering do that nothing else did?","Which detail of enforcement shows how far the agent's power now reached?","What is lost when the people who hold a nation's knowledge are made criminals?","What is your verdict on importance, and on what test?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Give the date of the Desert Land Act, the acreage it allowed and the condition attached to it.","a":"3 March 1877. Up to 640 acres at $1.25 an acre, a quarter paid down and the rest on proof that the land had been irrigated within three years."},{"q":"What happened to the cattle industry in the winter of 1886-87, and name three ways ranching was different afterwards.","a":"After a hot dry summer of 1886, blizzards from November 1886 into spring 1887 killed over half the cattle in Montana and almost whole herds on the worst northern ranges. Afterwards: smaller herds on owned, barbed-wire fenced land; hay cut and stored as winter feed; heavier Hereford crosses instead of Longhorns left to fend for themselves; wells and wind pumps for water."},{"q":"Who was killed at the KC Ranch on 9 April 1892, by whom, and how did the invasion end?","a":"Nate Champion and Nick Ray, killed by fifty-two invaders sent by the Wyoming Stock Growers Association, twenty-three of them hired Texas gunmen. A posse of hundreds under Sheriff Angus besieged the invaders at the TA Ranch from 11 April until cavalry from Fort McKinney took them into custody on 13 April 1892; the charges were eventually dropped."},{"q":"State what the Dawes Act allotted, for how long the land was held in trust, and what happened to the land left over.","a":"Signed 8 February 1887: 160 acres to the head of a family, 80 to a single adult and 40 to a child, doubled on grazing land, held in trust by the government for 25 years before full title passed to the allottee, who was also made a United States citizen. The surplus left after allotment was sold to white settlers."}]},"room":{"facts":["Booklet P is worth 32 marks and prints three questions: Question 1 in two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each 'Explain one consequence of...' for 4 marks and each on a separate event or development; Question 2, 'Write a narrative account analysing...', 8 marks; Question 3, 'Explain two of the following: the importance of ... for ...', two chosen parts at 8 marks each, 16 in all.","Only Question 2 prints stimulus points, as two bullets followed by 'You must also use information of your own.' Its mark scheme prints one cap: 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' There is no cap at Level 2, and Questions 1 and 3 print no bullets and carry no cap.","Each part of Question 1 is marked on its own two-level grid out of 4, with AO2 and AO1 interdependent: Level 2 (3-4) needs features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific supporting information added. Two accurate descriptions of the event, however detailed, stay in Level 1.","Question 2 and each part of Question 3 are marked on three-level grids out of 8. For the narrative account, Level 3 (6-8) needs a clear sequence of events leading to an outcome with the linkage between them analysed; for importance, Level 3 (6-8) needs analysis of what difference the event or development made, in a line of reasoning that is coherent and logically structured.","Paper 2 lasts 1 hour 50 minutes for both booklets, so booklet P is worth about 55 minutes: roughly five minutes on each part of Question 1, ten on Question 2 and fifteen on each part of Question 3, leaving time to read and check."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Desert Land Act of 1877. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the two-level consequence grid; Level 2 (3-4) needs features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific support. Credit the shift of land policy towards much larger holdings for arid country, the advantage the terms gave men with capital and the use of the Act by cattlemen and speculators to control water, or fraudulent proof of irrigation.","sol":"One consequence was that land on the dry Plains fell to those who already had money. The Act of 3 March 1877 let a settler take up to 640 acres at $1.25 an acre, paying 25 cents an acre down and the rest on proof that he had irrigated the land within three years. Irrigating even part of 640 acres needed capital and, above all, access to running water, which a homesteader with a quarter section rarely had. So cattlemen and speculators used the Act to secure the creeks and waterholes, sometimes swearing false proof of irrigation, and the free grass around them became effectively theirs.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["A consequence is something that was true afterwards because of the Act, so ask who could actually meet the conditions it set.","The useful angle is not how much land was offered but what the irrigation condition cost, and who on the Plains could afford it.","Write it in three moves: name the consequence, give the terms of the Act as your proof, then explain why those terms favoured one sort of settler."],"codes":["P3-3 — 1 Changes in farming, the cattle industry and settlement: Changes in farming","P3-3 — 1 Changes in farming, the cattle industry and settlement: Continued settlement"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of the winter of 1886-87 for ranching on the Plains. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the consequence grid. Credit the move to smaller fenced ranches on owned land, winter feeding with stored hay and the use of wells and wind pumps, the change of breed towards heavier Hereford crosses, the bankrupting of investors and the withdrawal of British and eastern capital, or the change in the cowboy's work from droving to ranch labour.","sol":"One consequence was that ranchers stopped trusting free grass to keep cattle alive. The northern ranges had been overstocked during the boom of the early 1880s, a hot dry summer in 1886 left almost no grass, and blizzards from November into the spring killed something over half the cattle in Montana and nearly whole herds on the worst ranges. The men who survived kept smaller herds on land they owned and fenced with barbed wire, cut and stored hay for winter feed, and bred heavier Hereford crosses instead of leaving Longhorns to fend for themselves. Ranching became enclosed, provisioned farming.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what ranchers did differently in 1888 that they had not done in 1885, rather than describing the blizzards.","The system that failed was free grass and no winter feed, so the consequence lies in how each of those two things was replaced.","Name the consequence, prove it with the scale of the losses, then explain why fencing and stored hay follow from them."],"codes":["P3-3 — 1 Changes in farming, the cattle industry and settlement: Changes in the cattle industry"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Exoduster movement of 1879. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the consequence grid. Credit the sudden arrival of destitute migrants in Kansas and the relief committees set up to feed and settle them, the growth of all-black settlements such as Nicodemus, the alarm of southern landowners at the loss of labour and attempts to stop the migration, or the drift of many Exodusters into town labour rather than farming.","sol":"One consequence was that Kansas had to organise relief for thousands of people who arrived with nothing. Around six thousand African Americans reached the state in the spring of 1879, and perhaps twenty thousand within two years, most of them leaving the South after the end of Reconstruction in 1877 because of violence, debt and the loss of the vote. Few could afford the tools, timber and stock a claim needed. Relief committees were formed in Kansas towns to feed and place them, some joined all-black settlements such as Nicodemus, founded in 1877, and many others ended as labourers rather than landowners.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about the state that received the migrants, not the states they left.","The migrants' poverty is the key: what does it take to start a homestead, and what happens to people who arrive without it?","Name the consequence, support it with how many came and when, then explain what their poverty forced Kansas to do."],"codes":["P3-3 — 1 Changes in farming, the cattle industry and settlement: Continued settlement"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of the destruction of the buffalo herds for the peoples of the Plains. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the consequence grid. Credit dependence on government rations and the power this gave the agent, the collapse of the material base of the culture (tipis, clothing, tools, fuel), the loss of the hunt as the basis of status and ceremony, or the impossibility of leaving the reservation once hunting could no longer feed a band.","sol":"One consequence was that the Plains nations lost the means to live away from an agency. The buffalo had supplied food, hides for tipis and clothing, sinew for thread, bone for tools and dung for fuel, so when the southern herd was destroyed by about 1878 and the northern herd by about 1883, no band could feed or house itself by hunting. Food now came as a ration issued at the agency, which meant staying where the agent could see you and doing as he said, since he could cut the issue. Hunger held people on the reservations more effectively than soldiers did.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Start from what the buffalo supplied besides meat, and ask what replaces each of those things once the herds are gone.","The consequence worth explaining is about control rather than about diet: who fed people afterwards, and what did that person want?","Name the consequence, support it with the dates by which each herd was destroyed, then explain what the ration line gave the agent."],"codes":["P3-3 — 3 Indigenous peoples of the Plains: The hunting and extermination of the buffalo"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Johnson County War of 1892. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the consequence grid. Credit the collapse of the prosecution once the prisoners were removed from Johnson County and the conviction among small ranchers that the law served the wealthy, the exposure of the Wyoming Stock Growers Association's power and the damage to its public standing, or the demonstration that federal troops, not county sheriffs, decided the outcome.","sol":"One consequence was that small ranchers concluded the law was not theirs. Fifty-two invaders, twenty-three of them hired Texas gunmen sent by the Wyoming Stock Growers Association, killed Nate Champion and Nick Ray at the KC Ranch on 9 April 1892 and were besieged at the TA Ranch by hundreds of local men under Sheriff Angus. Cavalry from Fort McKinney took them into custody on 13 April, the prisoners were moved out of the county where they had killed, and the charges were eventually dropped. The county had caught its invaders and could still not try them, which discredited local law in Wyoming for years.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Follow what happened after the shooting stopped rather than during it.","Ask who took the invaders away, where they were taken and what happened to the case against them.","Name the consequence, prove it with the arrival of the troops and the fate of the prosecution, then explain what that taught the small ranchers."],"codes":["P3-3 — 2 Conflict and tension: The range wars","P3-3 — 2 Conflict and tension: Dealing with law and order"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Battle of the Little Bighorn (1876). [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the Dawes Act (1887). [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two parts on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the public outrage in the east, the reinforcement of the army and the winter campaigns, the sell-or-starve rider and the Agreement of February 1877 taking the Black Hills, or the surrender of Crazy Horse in 1877 and of Sitting Bull in 1881. In 1(b) credit the loss of tribal land through the sale of surplus and the ending of the trust period, the breaking of communal ownership and of tribal authority, or the tying of citizenship to accepting an allotment.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that the Lakota lost the Black Hills. Custer and about 210 men were killed on 25-26 June 1876 and the 7th Cavalry lost 268 dead in all, and the news reached the east at the centenary of independence, so the reaction was fury rather than sympathy. Congress attached a rider to the Indian Appropriation Act cutting off all rations until the Black Hills were surrendered, and the Agreement of February 1877 took them. Winning the battle therefore cost the Lakota the ground the war had been fought over. 1(b) One consequence was a massive transfer of land out of Indigenous hands. The Act of 8 February 1887 allotted 160 acres to the head of a family, 80 to a single adult and 40 to a child, held in trust for 25 years, and sold everything left over as surplus to settlers. Because reservations were far larger than the allotments they contained, the surplus sales alone moved millions of acres immediately, and when trust periods ended many allottees sold or lost their plots: holdings fell from about 138 million acres in 1887 to about 48 million by 1934.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Both parts reward a result rather than a description, and in both cases the result is about land.","For 1(a) ask how Congress reacted to the news, and what it used as a lever against people who now depended on rations.","For 1(b) work out what happened to the part of a reservation that was not allotted to anybody."],"codes":["P3-3 — 2 Conflict and tension: Conflict on the Plains","P3-3 — 3 Indigenous peoples of the Plains: The significance of changing government attitudes"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the spread of barbed wire across the Plains. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the Ghost Dance among the Lakota in 1890. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two parts on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the ability of homesteaders to protect crops without timber, the enclosure of range and water by ranchers and the fence-cutting and range wars that followed, or the end of the open range and the long drive. In 1(b) credit the alarm of agents and the sending of troops to the Dakota agencies, the attempt to arrest Sitting Bull and his killing on 15 December 1890, or the disarming at Wounded Knee Creek on 29 December 1890.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that land on the Plains could at last be enclosed, and so became worth fighting over. Joseph Glidden's patent of 1874 gave a fence that needed no timber, which let a homesteader keep cattle off his crops for the first time. But the same wire let ranchers fence grass and, more importantly, water, so small men found the creeks shut against them. Fence-cutting, dammed water and driven-off stock followed, and the range wars of the 1880s and 1892 grew directly out of the question the wire had raised: who owned the range? 1(b) One consequence was that the Dakota reservations filled with soldiers. The teaching of Wovoka, which among the Lakota looked forward to the return of the dead and the disappearance of the whites, frightened the agents, who asked for protection, and thousands of troops were sent in the autumn of 1890. That deployment is why an arrest could be attempted at Standing Rock, where Sitting Bull was killed by Indian police on 15 December 1890, and why a cavalry regiment was on hand to intercept Spotted Elk's band and disarm it at Wounded Knee Creek on 29 December 1890.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["In each part the event is small and the consequence is large: a patent and a religious movement both ended in armed conflict.","For 1(a) ask what a fence does for a homesteader and what the same fence does to a neighbour who needs the water inside it.","For 1(b) ask what the agents did when they became frightened, and what their request put in place before December."],"codes":["P3-3 — 1 Changes in farming, the cattle industry and settlement: Changes in farming","P3-3 — 2 Conflict and tension: The range wars","P3-3 — 2 Conflict and tension: Conflict on the Plains"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Oklahoma Land Rush of 16 September 1893. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the gunfight near the OK Corral in October 1881. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two parts on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the settling of the Cherokee Outlet and the addition of its counties to Oklahoma Territory, the disappointment of the great majority who found no claim and the years of contested titles, or the opening of the last great block of Indian Territory and with it the end of the Indian Frontier. In 1(b) credit the charging of the Earps and the hearing that followed, the private killings and pursuits afterwards, or the damage to public confidence in officers who were parties to a local feud.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that the last great block of Indian Territory passed into settlers' hands. The Cherokee Outlet, over six million acres that the Cherokee Nation had been pressed into selling to the government, was opened at noon on 16 September 1893 in the largest run of all: about a hundred thousand people took part but there were only some forty thousand quarter-section claims to be had, so most of those who raced came away with nothing and the disputed claims kept the land offices busy for years. The Outlet was added to Oklahoma Territory, created on 2 May 1890, and after it no comparable block of Indigenous land was left to open. 1(b) One consequence was that Tombstone's law was thrown into open doubt. Virgil Earp was town marshal and his brothers and Doc Holliday were with him when Billy Clanton and Tom and Frank McLaury were killed in a fight of about thirty seconds on 26 October 1881. Because serving officers had done the killing, they were charged, and a judge had to rule publicly on whether they had acted as lawmen or as one side of a feud. The quarrel then went on outside the law altogether, with Morgan Earp murdered and Wyatt hunting his killers.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["One part is about the last of the land going and the other about a badge being doubted.","For 1(a) ask what was left of Indian Territory once the Outlet had been run for, and how many of the racers actually ended the day with a claim.","For 1(b) the consequence is in the courtroom and afterwards, not in the vacant lot."],"codes":["P3-3 — 1 Changes in farming, the cattle industry and settlement: Continued settlement","P3-3 — 2 Conflict and tension: Dealing with law and order"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the killing of Sitting Bull in December 1890. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the opening of the Carlisle Indian Industrial School in 1879. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two parts on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the panic and flight of bands from the agencies, Spotted Elk's move south and interception, and the massacre at Wounded Knee on 29 December 1890, or the removal of the last leader with authority independent of the agent. In 1(b) credit the spread of the boarding-school model, the separation of children from language, dress and family, the training of children for trades rather than for their own communities, or the weakening of the transmission of culture between generations.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that frightened bands left the agencies, with fatal results. Sitting Bull was shot by Indian police at Standing Rock on 15 December 1890 during an attempt to arrest him over the Ghost Dance. His death convinced others that no leader was safe, and Spotted Elk's band left Cheyenne River and moved south towards Pine Ridge. Troops already deployed that autumn intercepted them, and the attempt to disarm them at Wounded Knee Creek on 29 December 1890 killed between 250 and 300 Lakota, ending armed resistance on the Plains. 1(b) One consequence was that assimilation began to be pursued through children rather than through treaties or soldiers. Richard Henry Pratt's school in Pennsylvania took Lakota and other children hundreds of miles from home, cut their hair, renamed them, forbade their languages and trained them for trades, and its apparent success led the government to fund other boarding schools on the same model through the 1880s. The effect was a generation that returned, if it returned, unable to take up the roles its parents had been raised for.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Both parts turn on what other people did once the event had happened.","For 1(a) ask what other bands concluded from the death of the most famous leader of all.","For 1(b) ask why a government would spend money sending children so far away, and what it copied afterwards."],"codes":["P3-3 — 2 Conflict and tension: Conflict on the Plains","P3-3 — 3 Indigenous peoples of the Plains: Indigenous people's lives on the reservations"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the changes in the cattle industry in the years 1885-1892. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the winter of 1886-87; the Johnson County War (1892). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 on the narrative grid; Level 3 (6-8) needs a clear sequence leading to an outcome with the linkage analysed. Credit overstocking and falling beef prices in 1885-86, the dry summer of 1886 and the losses of the Great Die-Up, the move to smaller fenced ranches with stored winter feed, wells and better breeds, the change in the cowboy's work, and the conflict with small ranchers that followed enclosure. Cap: no access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"By 1885 the northern ranges carried far more cattle than the grass could feed, because the boom of the early 1880s had drawn in British and eastern investors, and beef prices were already falling. That overstocking is why the weather mattered so much. The summer of 1886 was hot and dry and left the range almost bare, so the herds went into the winter thin, and blizzards ran from November 1886 into the spring of 1887: Montana ranchers lost something over half the territory's cattle and some northern outfits lost almost everything. Because the losses ruined the men who had trusted free grass, the survivors changed the business rather than repeating it. They kept smaller herds on land they owned and fenced with barbed wire, cut and stored hay for winter feed, sank wells and raised wind pumps, and bred heavier Hereford crosses; fencing and haymaking also turned the cowboy from a drover into a ranch hand. That enclosure is what turned the old quarrel with small ranchers and homesteaders into open violence, because fenced grass and controlled water left newcomers no room and the Wyoming Stock Growers Association blacklisted them as rustlers. So when the association sent fifty-two armed men into Johnson County in April 1892, killing Nate Champion and Nick Ray at the KC Ranch, the open range was already dead and the fight was over who would own what had replaced it.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["A narrative account needs a beginning and an end, so decide first what the industry looked like in 1885 and what it looked like in 1892.","The weather is the middle of the story, not the start: ask what had already gone wrong with the business before the snow fell.","Plan three or four dated episodes and write the join between each pair as a sentence that explains, not a sentence that says next."],"codes":["P3-3 — 1 Changes in farming, the cattle industry and settlement: Changes in the cattle industry","P3-3 — 2 Conflict and tension: The range wars"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the growth of settlement on the Plains in the years 1877-1893. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the Desert Land Act (1877); the Oklahoma Land Rush (1889). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 on the narrative grid. Credit the land laws and their conditions, the technology that made the claims workable (barbed wire, wind pumps, dry farming, Turkey Red wheat), the Exoduster migration of 1879, the Boomer campaign and the runs of 1889 and 1893, the creation of Oklahoma Territory in 1890 and the census report of 1890. Cap: no access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Settlement after 1877 was driven first by the government handing out more land. The Desert Land Act of 3 March 1877 offered up to 640 acres at $1.25 an acre to anyone who irrigated within three years, and the Timber Culture Act of 1873 had already added 160 acres for planting trees, so the quarter section of 1862 was no longer the limit. Land alone was useless, however, which is why the technology of the same decade decided who could stay: Glidden's barbed wire of 1874 made fencing possible without timber, wind pumps lifted water from deep wells, and dry farming and Turkey Red wheat, brought to Kansas by Mennonite migrants in 1874, made the dry years survivable. Settlers also came for reasons the land laws cannot explain: perhaps twenty thousand Exodusters left the South for Kansas after Reconstruction ended, some six thousand in the spring of 1879 alone. Because the good free land was filling, pressure then fell on the one great block ceded in 1866 and never assigned to any nation. Boomers campaigned for it, and when the Unassigned Lands were opened about fifty thousand people raced in at noon on 22 April 1889, so that Guthrie held thousands by nightfall. Oklahoma Territory followed on 2 May 1890 and the Cherokee Outlet was run for on 16 September 1893. By then the census of 1890 had reported that a frontier line could hardly be said to exist at all.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what was pushing people west and what was pulling them, and make sure your sequence ends somewhere definite.","One episode has to explain why the government finally opened Indian Territory, so build towards it rather than tacking it on.","Write each join as a because: the law offered acres, so what was still missing, and what had arrived to supply it?"],"codes":["P3-3 — 1 Changes in farming, the cattle industry and settlement: Continued settlement","P3-3 — 1 Changes in farming, the cattle industry and settlement: Changes in farming"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the destruction of the buffalo for the way of life of the peoples of the Plains. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Credit the loss of the material base of the culture, the resulting dependence on rations and the power it gave the agent, the collapse of the hunt as the basis of status, ceremony and the yearly round, and the fact that the destruction did more than military defeat to end independence.","sol":"The destruction of the buffalo mattered because it removed the thing everything else rested on. The herds had supplied food, hides for tipis and clothing, sinew for thread, bone and horn for tools and dried dung for fuel on a treeless plain, so a band without buffalo could not feed, house or clothe itself, whatever its courage. The railways split the great herd and carried hunters out and hides back, a tanning process from about 1871 made buffalo hide valuable as industrial belting, and long-range rifles let a hunter kill scores from one position; the southern herd was gone by about 1878 and the northern herd by about 1883, and a count in the late 1880s found only a few hundred wild buffalo in the whole United States. The first difference this made was practical: food now came as a ration issued at an agency, which meant staying where the agent could see you and obeying him, because he could cut the issue. The second was cultural, because the hunt had set the rhythm of the year, decided a young man's standing and supplied the ceremonies, so its end took away the way a man earned respect just as the Sun Dance was being banned. The third is decisive: the army never had to defeat the Plains nations in the field, because hunger did it, which is why the destruction of the buffalo, and not the Little Bighorn or Wounded Knee, is the event that ends the old life.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Importance means difference made, so plan the answer as changes that lasted rather than as an account of the hunting.","List what the buffalo supplied besides meat, then ask what replaced each item and who controlled the replacement.","Aim for three changes - material, cultural and political - and end by saying which of them mattered most."],"codes":["P3-3 — 3 Indigenous peoples of the Plains: The hunting and extermination of the buffalo","P3-3 — 3 Indigenous peoples of the Plains: Indigenous people's lives on the reservations"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the Dawes Act (1887) for the Indigenous peoples of the Plains. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Credit the division of reservations into individual allotments and the sale of the surplus, the 25-year trust and the loss of land once it ended, the fall from about 138 million acres in 1887 to about 48 million by 1934, the deliberate breaking of communal ownership and tribal authority, and the tying of citizenship to allotment.","sol":"The Dawes Act mattered because it attacked the reservation itself, which every earlier policy had taken for granted. Signed on 8 February 1887, it divided reservation land into individual holdings - 160 acres to the head of a family, 80 to a single adult and 40 to a child, doubled where the land was fit only for grazing - held in trust by the government for 25 years, and it made allottees United States citizens. The largest difference was the loss of land. Reservations were far bigger than the allotments carved out of them, so the remainder was sold off to settlers as surplus at once, and when trust periods expired many allottees sold or were cheated out of their plots; holdings across the country fell from about 138 million acres when the Act passed to about 48 million by 1934. The second difference was to the tribe as a body, because land had been held in common, so making the individual the owner removed what chiefs and councils had managed, finishing the work begun by agents, Indian police, the ban on the Sun Dance from 1883 and the boarding schools. The third is that the Act was sold as generosity, which is why it worked: allotment came wrapped in citizenship and property, so resistance looked like a refusal to be civilised. Judged by result it was the largest transfer of Indigenous land in the period, and that is why it matters.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Do not describe the Act for more than a sentence or two: the marks are for what changed because of it.","Ask what happened to the part of each reservation that no individual was allotted.","Build three changes - land, tribal authority and the way the policy was justified - and finish by judging it against its own stated aim."],"codes":["P3-3 — 3 Indigenous peoples of the Plains: The significance of changing government attitudes","P3-3 — 3 Indigenous peoples of the Plains: Indigenous people's lives on the reservations"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the Johnson County War (1892) for law and order in the West. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Credit the exposure of the cattle barons' power and of the blacklists and of the resentment the Maverick Law of 1884 had left behind before its repeal in 1888, the fact that a county could besiege its invaders and still not try them once the prisoners were removed and the charges dropped, the role of federal troops in deciding the outcome, and the lasting distrust of local law among small ranchers.","sol":"The Johnson County War matters because of what it proved about who the law in Wyoming belonged to. The Wyoming Stock Growers Association had long used its weight against the small men - the Maverick Law of 1884 had given unbranded cattle to the association rather than to the finder until the territorial legislature repealed it in 1888, and blacklisting a small rancher as a rustler still made it impossible for him to sell stock or find work - and in April 1892 it added a private army, sending fifty-two men, twenty-three of them hired Texas gunmen under Frank Wolcott and Frank Canton, into the county with a list of names. They killed Nate Champion and Nick Ray at the KC Ranch on 9 April. The first difference this made was to show that local law could win and still lose: Sheriff Angus raised hundreds of men and besieged the invaders at the TA Ranch from 11 April, but cavalry from Fort McKinney took them into custody on 13 April, the prisoners were moved out of the county, and the charges were eventually dropped. The second is that the outcome was settled by federal troops rather than by a jury, which told every small rancher that influence in Cheyenne and Washington outweighed a county's own officers. The third is reputational: the invasion was reported across the country and fixed the image of the cattle barons as men above the law. Its importance is therefore less as a battle than as evidence that law and order in the West followed wealth.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what the episode revealed about power, because the fighting itself changed no statute.","The most damaging fact for local law is what happened to the prosecution, so build a paragraph around it.","Work through three kinds of difference - to the courts, to who decided the outcome, and to public opinion - then judge which mattered most."],"codes":["P3-3 — 2 Conflict and tension: The range wars","P3-3 — 2 Conflict and tension: Dealing with law and order"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date was the General Allotment Act, known as the Dawes Act, signed?","a":"8 February 1887."},{"q":"How many acres could a settler take up under the Desert Land Act of 1877?","a":"Up to 640 acres, at $1.25 an acre, on condition of irrigating within three years."},{"q":"Who was killed alongside Nick Ray at the KC Ranch on 9 April 1892?","a":"Nate Champion."},{"q":"On what date did the Wounded Knee Massacre take place?","a":"29 December 1890."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The Desert Land Act (1877)","back":"Passed 3 March 1877. Up to 640 acres at $1.25 an acre, 25 cents down and the rest on proof of irrigation within three years. Favoured men with capital and water, so cattlemen used it to control creeks.","code":"P3-3 — 1 Changes in farming, the cattle industry and settlement: Changes in farming","open":false},{"front":"Barbed wire, wind pumps and dry farming","back":"Glidden patented cheap barbed wire in 1874, so land could be fenced without timber; wind pumps lifted water from deep wells; dry farming ploughed after rain to trap moisture; Turkey Red wheat came to Kansas with Mennonite migrants in 1874.","code":"P3-3 — 1 Changes in farming, the cattle industry and settlement: Changes in farming","open":false},{"front":"The winter of 1886-87 (the Great Die-Up)","back":"After a hot, dry summer in 1886 left the overstocked ranges bare, blizzards ran from November 1886 into spring 1887. Montana lost something over half its cattle; some northern outfits lost almost everything, and many ranchers were ruined.","code":"P3-3 — 1 Changes in farming, the cattle industry and settlement: Changes in the cattle industry","open":false},{"front":"The end of the open range","back":"After 1887 ranchers kept smaller herds on owned land fenced with barbed wire, cut and stored hay for winter feed, sank wells with wind pumps and bred heavier Hereford crosses. The long drive ended and cowboys became ranch hands.","code":"P3-3 — 1 Changes in farming, the cattle industry and settlement: Changes in the cattle industry","open":false},{"front":"The Exoduster movement (1879)","back":"African Americans leaving the South after Reconstruction ended in 1877, encouraged by Benjamin Pap Singleton. About six thousand reached Kansas in spring 1879 and perhaps twenty thousand within two years; relief committees fed them, and Nicodemus, founded 1877, grew.","code":"P3-3 — 1 Changes in farming, the cattle industry and settlement: Continued settlement","open":false},{"front":"The Oklahoma Land Rush of 22 April 1889","back":"About 1.9 million acres of the Unassigned Lands in Indian Territory opened at noon to roughly fifty thousand people. Guthrie and Oklahoma City grew from nothing to thousands of inhabitants in a day; Sooners crept in early.","code":"P3-3 — 1 Changes in farming, the cattle industry and settlement: Continued settlement","open":false},{"front":"The closing of the frontier","back":"Oklahoma Territory was created on 2 May 1890; the Cherokee Outlet, over six million acres, was run for on 16 September 1893. The census of 1890 reported that a frontier line could hardly be said to exist.","code":"P3-3 — 1 Changes in farming, the cattle industry and settlement: Continued settlement","open":false},{"front":"Billy the Kid","back":"Henry McCarty, also called William H. Bonney. Fought for one side in the Lincoln County War of 1878 in New Mexico, where lawmen backed both factions. Shot by Sheriff Pat Garrett at Fort Sumner on 14 July 1881.","code":"P3-3 — 2 Conflict and tension: Dealing with law and order","open":false},{"front":"The gunfight near the OK Corral (26 October 1881)","back":"In Tombstone, Arizona. Town marshal Virgil Earp, his brothers Wyatt and Morgan and Doc Holliday fought the Clantons and McLaurys for about thirty seconds. Billy Clanton and both McLaurys died; a judge ruled the Earps had acted as lawmen.","code":"P3-3 — 2 Conflict and tension: Dealing with law and order","open":false},{"front":"The Johnson County War (1892)","back":"Fifty-two invaders, twenty-three hired Texas gunmen, were sent by the Wyoming Stock Growers Association. They killed Nate Champion and Nick Ray at the KC Ranch on 9 April 1892, then were besieged at the TA Ranch until cavalry arrived.","code":"P3-3 — 2 Conflict and tension: The range wars","open":false},{"front":"The Battle of the Little Bighorn (25-26 June 1876)","back":"Custer divided the 7th Cavalry and attacked a huge Lakota, Northern Cheyenne and Arapaho village. Custer and about 210 men were killed; the regiment lost 268 dead. Outrage in the east brought the loss of the Black Hills in 1877.","code":"P3-3 — 2 Conflict and tension: Conflict on the Plains","open":false},{"front":"The Wounded Knee Massacre (29 December 1890)","back":"The 7th Cavalry under Colonel Forsyth disarmed Spotted Elk's band at Wounded Knee Creek on Pine Ridge. Between 250 and 300 Lakota were killed, many women and children; 25 soldiers died. Armed resistance on the Plains ended.","code":"P3-3 — 2 Conflict and tension: Conflict on the Plains","open":false},{"front":"The destruction of the buffalo","back":"Railways split the herd and carried hunters and hides; a tanning process from about 1871 made hide valuable for machine belting; the army encouraged the killing. The southern herd was gone by about 1878, the northern by about 1883.","code":"P3-3 — 3 Indigenous peoples of the Plains: The hunting and extermination of the buffalo","open":false},{"front":"Name one way an Indian agent controlled life on a reservation","back":"He issued the rations families now depended on and could cut them; he granted or refused the passes needed to leave; he dealt with individuals instead of chiefs, undermining their authority; he commanded the Indian police and the Courts of Indian Offenses that banned the Sun Dance from 1883.","code":"P3-3 — 3 Indigenous peoples of the Plains: Indigenous people's lives on the reservations","open":true},{"front":"The Dawes Act (1887)","back":"Signed 8 February 1887. Reservations were divided into allotments - 160 acres for a family head, 80 for a single adult, 40 for a child - held in trust for 25 years; the surplus was sold to settlers.","code":"P3-3 — 3 Indigenous peoples of the Plains: The significance of changing government attitudes","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Paper 2 booklet P Question 2, narrative account, 8 marks)","items":["Question: 'Write a narrative account analysing the breakdown of law and order in Lincoln County, New Mexico, in the years 1878-1881.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the killing of John Tunstall (1878); the death of Billy the Kid (1881). You must also use information of your own. This is the middle stretch of the account, carrying the story from Tunstall's killing to the end of the fighting in Lincoln.","Because the posse that shot John Tunstall on 18 February 1878 had been sent out by a sheriff who owed his office to the other faction, the men who wanted the killers taken could not go to the county's own officers, so they went to the justice of the peace, took warrants from him and rode as the Regulators. That is why the next few months look like a war between two police forces rather than a hunt for murderers: the Regulators killed two of the men they blamed for Tunstall in March, and on 1 April they shot Sheriff William Brady and his deputy George Hindman down in Lincoln's one street, each side insisting that it was serving process. Since neither set of warrants could be enforced against the other, the quarrel could only grow until somebody stronger than both arrived, and in the five-day battle of 15 to 19 July 1878 that is what happened: Lieutenant Colonel Nathan Dudley brought troops and a gun up from Fort Stanton and posted them so that the gun covered the houses the McSween party held, the McSween house was fired, and Alexander McSween was shot as he came out of it. The fighting stopped, but the army had come to end a disturbance and not to try anyone for it, so the county was left quiet with every question about whom the law belonged to still open.","Why this is Level 3: the material is organised as a clear sequence leading to an outcome, and the linkage between the events is analysed rather than asserted - the sheriff's loyalties explain why warrants were sought elsewhere, the two sets of warrants explain the killings of the spring, and the deadlock between them explains why only the army could end it. The knowledge is precise (the date of Tunstall's death, the killing of Brady and Hindman on 1 April, the dates of the five-day battle, Dudley, Fort Stanton and McSween) and it addresses well beyond three aspects of content, so the Level 3 cap does not bite."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["The same question: 'Write a narrative account analysing the breakdown of law and order in Lincoln County, New Mexico, in the years 1878-1881', with the same two stimulus points. This is the same middle stretch of the account, written as most students write it.","John Tunstall was killed in 1878 by men working for the sheriff, and this started the Lincoln County War. His friends were angry and formed a group called the Regulators, and Billy the Kid was one of them. They killed some of the men who had shot Tunstall and they also shot the sheriff. After that there was a big gunfight in the town which went on for several days until the army turned up and McSween was killed. Law and order in Lincoln County had broken down completely by this point.","What this meets and what it misses: it meets the Level 2 descriptor, because a narrative is given with some organisation into a sequence of events and accurate, relevant information is included. It misses Level 3 on both bullets. The linkage is asserted, not analysed - 'his friends were angry' is a motive rather than an explanation of how two sets of men could both claim to be the law, and 'after that there was a big gunfight' joins the battle to nothing. The knowledge is also general where it should be precise: no date for Tunstall's death, no date or name for the killing of the sheriff, no dates for the five-day battle, and nothing about who brought the troops in or where they came from."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the winter of 1886-87 and the end of the open range","items":["The cattle boom of the early 1880s drew in British and eastern investment and overstocked the northern ranges of Montana, Wyoming and Dakota.","Beef prices were already falling in 1885 and 1886, so the business was under strain before the weather broke.","The summer of 1886 was hot and dry and left the range almost bare of grass by the autumn.","Blizzards and extreme cold ran from November 1886 into the spring of 1887; ranchers called it the Great Die-Up.","Montana ranchers lost something over half the territory's cattle; on the worst northern ranges outfits lost almost their whole herds.","Many ranchers and investment companies were bankrupted, and foreign capital withdrew from the cattle business.","Afterwards herds were smaller and kept on land the rancher owned, fenced with barbed wire rather than run on open range.","Hay was cut and stored for winter feed, and wells with wind pumps supplied water, so cattle were no longer left to fend for themselves.","Heavier Hereford and Shorthorn crosses replaced the hardy but light Longhorn, giving better beef in return for more care.","The long drive ended and the cowboy's work became fencing, haymaking and riding fence lines for wages."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Exoduster movement and Kansas (1879)","items":["Reconstruction ended in 1877, removing federal protection from African Americans in the southern states.","Push factors: violence and intimidation, the loss of voting rights, and debt through sharecropping and tenancy.","Benjamin 'Pap' Singleton, a former slave from Tennessee, promoted Kansas as a place where land could be owned, and organised parties of migrants.","Kansas was attractive because of the Homestead Act, cheap railroad land, and its reputation as a free state associated with John Brown.","Around six thousand African Americans arrived in Kansas in the spring of 1879 alone.","Perhaps twenty thousand made the journey within about two years; the movement is known as the Exodus of 1879 and the migrants as Exodusters.","Most arrived destitute, having spent everything on the journey up the Mississippi and the railroads.","Relief committees were organised in Kansas towns, including Topeka, to feed, shelter and place the new arrivals.","Nicodemus, in Graham County, founded in 1877, was the best known of the all-black settlements in Kansas.","Many Exodusters could not afford the tools, timber, stock and seed a claim needed, and worked as labourers in towns instead of farming."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Oklahoma land runs (1889 and 1893)","items":["Indian Territory had been set aside for nations removed from the east, and was the heart of what was called the Indian Frontier.","Boomers campaigned through the 1880s for the unoccupied central portion, the Unassigned Lands, to be opened to settlers.","President Benjamin Harrison proclaimed the Unassigned Lands, about 1.9 million acres, open for settlement in 1889.","The run began at noon on 22 April 1889, with roughly fifty thousand people racing in on horseback, in wagons and by train.","Guthrie and Oklahoma City grew from empty ground to thousands of inhabitants within hours.","Sooners were those who slipped across the line before the signal and took the best claims, causing years of disputed titles.","Claims were taken under the Homestead Act of 1862, so a claim was a quarter section of 160 acres.","Oklahoma Territory was created on 2 May 1890, giving the settled area a territorial government.","The Cherokee Outlet, over six million acres, was opened on 16 September 1893: about a hundred thousand people took part, the largest run of all.","The runs broke the promise on which the Indian Frontier rested, and the census of 1890 reported that a frontier line could hardly be said to exist."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Billy the Kid, Wyatt Earp and the OK Corral (1881)","items":["Law enforcement in the West was local: elected county sheriffs, appointed town marshals, and federal US marshals and deputies for the territories.","Distances were vast, deputies few, and prisoners, juries and witnesses all had to be found locally, so vigilance committees often acted instead.","Billy the Kid was Henry McCarty, who also used the name William H. Bonney.","The Lincoln County War of 1878 in New Mexico was a fight between rival merchant and ranching factions, with lawmen on both sides; Billy fought for one of them.","Sheriff Pat Garrett shot Billy the Kid at Fort Sumner, New Mexico, on 14 July 1881.","Wyatt Earp had been a lawman in the Kansas cattle towns of Wichita and Dodge City before moving to the silver town of Tombstone, Arizona.","In Tombstone his brother Virgil was town marshal; Wyatt and Morgan Earp and Doc Holliday were with him on the day.","The gunfight of 26 October 1881 took place in a vacant lot near the rear entrance of the OK Corral and lasted about thirty seconds.","Billy Clanton and Tom and Frank McLaury were killed; Virgil and Morgan Earp and Doc Holliday were wounded.","The Earps were charged, and a judge ruled that they had been acting as officers of the law; Morgan Earp was later murdered and Wyatt pursued those he blamed."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Johnson County War of 1892","items":["The Wyoming Stock Growers Association represented the large ranchers and was powerful in territorial and then state politics.","Small ranchers and homesteaders took up claims along the creeks of Johnson County, fencing water the big herds had used.","The Maverick Law of 1884 gave unbranded cattle to the association rather than to whoever found them; small stockmen resented it and the territorial legislature repealed it in 1888.","The association blacklisted men it called rustlers, making it impossible for them to sell stock at the roundup or find work.","In April 1892 the association sent fifty-two armed men into Johnson County, twenty-three of them hired Texas gunmen.","The invaders were led by Frank Wolcott, a rancher, and Frank Canton, a former sheriff of the county, and carried a list of names.","On 9 April 1892 they surrounded the KC Ranch and killed Nate Champion and Nick Ray.","Sheriff Angus raised a posse of hundreds of local men, who besieged the invaders at the TA Ranch from 11 April.","Cavalry from Fort McKinney arrived and took the invaders into custody on 13 April 1892, ending the siege.","The prisoners were removed from Johnson County, the county could not meet the costs of prosecution, and the charges were eventually dropped."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Battle of the Little Bighorn (1876) and its consequences","items":["The Fort Laramie Treaty of 1868 created the Great Sioux Reservation and recognised the Black Hills as Lakota land.","Custer's expedition of 1874 confirmed gold in the Black Hills, and miners poured in while the government failed to keep them out.","The government ordered all Lakota to report to the agencies by 31 January 1876; those who did not were treated as hostile, and the Great Sioux War began.","General Crook was turned back at the Battle of the Rosebud on 17 June 1876, a week before the main battle.","On 25-26 June 1876 Custer attacked a very large village of Lakota, Northern Cheyenne and Arapaho on the Little Bighorn, dividing the 7th Cavalry into separate columns.","Custer and about 210 men with him were killed; the 7th Cavalry lost 268 dead in all. Sitting Bull and Crazy Horse were the leading figures on the other side.","News reached the eastern states around the centenary of independence in July 1876 and produced outrage rather than sympathy.","Congress attached a sell-or-starve rider to the Indian Appropriation Act, cutting off rations until the Black Hills were ceded.","The Agreement of February 1877 took the Black Hills, although far fewer than the three quarters of adult men required by the 1868 treaty had signed.","Crazy Horse surrendered in May 1877 and was killed at Fort Robinson on 5 September 1877; Sitting Bull fled to Canada and surrendered at Fort Buford on 19 July 1881."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Ghost Dance and the Wounded Knee Massacre (1890)","items":["The Ghost Dance came from the visions of Wovoka, a Northern Paiute in Nevada, from 1889.","His teaching promised that if people danced, worked and lived honestly, the dead would return and the old life would be restored.","Among the Lakota the teaching took a harder form, looking forward to the disappearance of the whites and the return of the buffalo.","Conditions on the Lakota reservations were desperate: rations had been cut, crops had failed and disease was widespread, which is why the message spread fast.","Reservation agents were alarmed and asked for military protection; thousands of troops were sent to the Dakota agencies in the autumn of 1890.","Sitting Bull was killed by Indian police at Standing Rock on 15 December 1890 during an attempt to arrest him.","Spotted Elk, also called Big Foot, led his band away from Cheyenne River towards Pine Ridge and was intercepted by troops.","On 29 December 1890 the 7th Cavalry under Colonel James Forsyth surrounded the camp at Wounded Knee Creek and began disarming the men.","Firing broke out and between 250 and 300 Lakota were killed, a large proportion of them women and children; 25 soldiers died.","Wounded Knee ended armed resistance on the Plains, and is usually taken with the census of 1890 as the close of the frontier period."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Dawes Act (1887) and changing government policy","items":["The Indian Appropriation Act of 1871 ended treaty-making, so nations were no longer dealt with as independent peoples.","Policy in the 1870s was concentration - moving people onto reservations and holding them there with rations.","From the 1880s policy became assimilation, aiming to dissolve tribal life and turn Indigenous people into individual American farmers.","Reformers pressing for allotment believed private property would civilise; western politicians supported it because it released land.","The General Allotment Act, sponsored by Senator Henry Dawes, was signed on 8 February 1887.","It allotted 160 acres to the head of a family, 80 acres to a single adult and 40 acres to a child, with double areas on land fit only for grazing.","Allotments were held in trust by the government for 25 years, at the end of which the allottee received full title; the Act also made allottees United States citizens.","Land remaining after allotment was declared surplus and sold to white settlers, so reservations shrank immediately.","Plains land was often unsuitable for a family farm of that size, and allottees frequently lacked stock, tools and capital.","Land held by Indigenous nations across the country fell from about 138 million acres in 1887 to about 48 million acres by 1934."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the buffalo and life on the reservations","items":["The buffalo supplied food, hides for tipis and clothing, sinew for thread, bone and horn for tools, and dried dung for fuel on a treeless plain.","The transcontinental railroads split the great herd and carried hunters west and hides east, and railway companies employed hunters to feed construction crews.","From about 1871 a new tanning process made buffalo hide valuable as industrial belting, creating a guaranteed market for hides.","Hunters with long-range rifles could kill scores of animals from a single stand, and army officers encouraged the slaughter as a way to force people onto reservations.","The southern herd was effectively destroyed by about 1878 and the northern herd by about 1883; a count in the late 1880s found only a few hundred wild buffalo left in the United States.","Reservation land was usually poor, and its boundaries were cut again whenever settlers or miners wanted more.","The Indian agent issued rations, which were often short or spoiled because Congress cut appropriations and contractors cheated.","The agent controlled passes to leave the reservation and dealt with individuals rather than chiefs, which destroyed the authority leaders had earned.","From 1883 the Courts of Indian Offenses banned ceremonies including the Sun Dance and the work of medicine men, and Indian police were recruited from the reservation itself.","The Carlisle Indian Industrial School opened in Pennsylvania in 1879 under Richard Henry Pratt; children were taken far from home, had their hair cut, were renamed, were forbidden their languages and were trained for trades."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 2 booklet P Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the breakdown of law and order in Lincoln County, New Mexico, in the years 1878-1881' (8 marks)","items":["The question as printed: 'Write a narrative account analysing the breakdown of law and order in Lincoln County, New Mexico, in the years 1878-1881. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the killing of John Tunstall (1878); the death of Billy the Kid (1881). You must also use information of your own.'","Decode it before writing. 'Narrative account' means a sequence that leads somewhere definite, so fix the start and the end: a county in which the only store worth the name, the government contracts and the sheriff all belonged to one faction, and a county quieted by an amnesty and a manhunt. 'Analysing' means every join between one episode and the next must be explained. The dates fence the answer, so Tombstone and the later range wars belong outside it.","The cap that decides the level: no access to Level 3 for an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content. Decide first which episodes are your own material, and develop those hardest.","Timing: about a minute a mark, so roughly ten minutes in all - two to plan the four episodes and their links, seven to write, one to check the dates. No SPaG marks are available on this paper, so do not spend time on presentation.","Episode 1, the county before February 1878 (own material): L. G. Murphy and Company, known locally as the House, held the one substantial store and the contracts to supply the nearby army post and Indian agency, and Sheriff William Brady was of their party; the English rancher John Tunstall, the lawyer Alexander McSween and the cattleman John Chisum set up a rival store and bank to break that hold. The quarrel was commercial before it was violent, and each side had officers of its own.","Link sentence: because the county's own officers belonged to one of the two sides, any dispute between them could only be settled by force or by finding some other authority willing to act.","Episode 2, the killing of Tunstall, 18 February 1878 (stimulus point): a sheriff's posse sent to attach his property shot him dead. The county's justice of the peace issued warrants for the killers and swore in Tunstall's men, who called themselves the Regulators, so both parties now carried warrants and both could call a killing an arrest.","Link sentence: with two sets of lawmen hunting each other, every act of the feud could be dressed as law enforcement, which is why the violence grew instead of coming to trial.","Episode 3, the war of 1878 (own material): the Regulators killed two of the men they blamed for Tunstall in March; Sheriff Brady and his deputy George Hindman were shot down in Lincoln's one street on 1 April; and the fighting ended in the five-day battle in Lincoln from 15 to 19 July 1878, when Lieutenant Colonel Nathan Dudley brought troops and a gun up from Fort Stanton, the McSween house was burned and McSween was shot as he came out.","Link sentence: the army stopped the fighting without trying anybody for it, so peace had to be bought politically rather than delivered by a court.","Episode 4, amnesty and manhunt, 1878-1881 (own material carrying the second stimulus point): President Hayes removed Governor Axtell in September 1878 and sent Lew Wallace, who proclaimed an amnesty on 13 November 1878 for all who were not already under indictment, which left out Billy the Kid, indicted for Brady's killing. Convicted of that murder in April 1881, he shot his way out of the Lincoln jail on 28 April, killing the deputies J. W. Bell and Robert Olinger, and Sheriff Pat Garrett tracked him down and killed him at Fort Sumner on 14 July 1881.","The evaluation thread running through all four: Lincoln County did not lack law, it had too much of it in the wrong hands. Sheriff, justice of the peace, governor and army all acted, and each of them acted for a side. Say this once early and return to it at the end.","What the closing sentence must say: not a summary but an outcome - that order came back to Lincoln County through a pardon list and a manhunt rather than through the courts, so the county ended the period quiet with nothing settled about whom the law belonged to."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:33:22Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"The fix pass holds. All five worked examples, the plan and both model paragraphs are now on foci that are not in the bank (the Timber Culture Act, the causes of the Little Bighorn 1874-76, the reservation system and chiefs' authority, Turkey Red wheat, the 1883 ban on the Sun Dance, and the breakdown of law and order in Lincoln County 1878-81), so no question arrives pre-answered, and no stem repeats the specimen or the June 2023 live P3 paper. I re-solved all fourteen questions and re-verified the new material on the web: the Timber Culture Act's 40 acres cut to 10 in 1878 and repeal in 1891; the Allison Commission of 1875 and the order of 6 December 1875 with its 31 January 1876 deadline; Indian police paid from 1878 and the Courts of Indian Offenses of 1883 with agent-appointed judges and rations as a punishment; the Mennonite settlement of central Kansas in 1874 with Turkey Red; Tunstall shot 18 February 1878, Brady and Hindman on 1 April, the five-day battle of 15-19 July 1878 with Lieutenant Colonel Dudley up from Fort Stanton and McSween killed, Axtell removed in September 1878, Wallace's amnesty of 13 November 1878, the jail break of 28 April 1881 that killed Bell and Olinger, and Garrett at Fort Sumner on 14 July 1881; the Cherokee Outlet of over six million acres opened on 16 September 1893 to about a hundred thousand racers for some forty thousand claims; and the repeal of the Maverick Law by the territorial legislature in 1888, now stated in the lesson, in question 13 and in the fact file. The earlier checks on the Desert Land Act, the Great Die-Up, the Exodusters, the 1889 run, the Little Bighorn, the Dawes Act, the buffalo, the Ghost Dance and Wounded Knee still stand, and the June 2025 form, tariffs, AO splits and the single Level 3 cap remain exactly right. Nothing is wrong; the three residuals are wording, and a teacher could publish this as it is."}}},"P4-1":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"P4-1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 1 is where the Cold War is explained rather than merely described, and booklet P returns to it constantly: the consequences question loves 1945 to 1949, the narrative account loves a short run of years in which one thing visibly caused the next, and the importance questions love a single event whose effects reached forward for years. If you can say, with names and dates, what the wartime conferences settled and what they left open, why containment was announced in 1947 and what the USSR did about it, why Berlin became the first confrontation, and why Hungary in 1956 showed the limits of what either side would risk, you can answer almost anything this key topic sets. This room teaches that sequence as a chain you can argue with, and it trains the three moves booklet P rewards: explaining a consequence rather than retelling an event, writing a narrative that links its episodes instead of listing them, and judging what difference an event made to what came after it.","idea":[{"code":"P4-1 — 1 Early tension between East and West: The Grand Alliance","h":"Allies of necessity: what Tehran, Yalta and Potsdam settled, and what they did not","t":"The Grand Alliance of the USA, the USSR and Britain was made by the war, not by agreement: the USSR joined the Allies when Germany invaded it in June 1941 and the USA when Japan attacked Pearl Harbor in December 1941. At Tehran, from 28 November to 1 December 1943, the three agreed that Britain and the USA would open a second front in France in May 1944, that the USSR would fight Japan once Germany was beaten, and that Poland's frontiers would move westwards. At Yalta, from 4 to 11 February 1945, with Germany close to defeat, they agreed to divide Germany and Berlin into four zones, to put Nazi leaders on trial, to found the United Nations, and to hold free elections in the countries liberated from Germany. At Potsdam, from 17 July to 2 August 1945, the mood had changed: Germany was beaten, Roosevelt was dead, Truman had the atomic bomb and Churchill was replaced part way through by Attlee. Reparations were left to each power to take from its own zone, and nothing was settled about Poland's government. A top-level answer uses the conferences as a sequence of narrowing agreement rather than three lists: co-operation while the common enemy lasted, and a series of unsettled questions, above all Germany and Poland, that became the quarrels of 1946 and 1947."},{"code":"P4-1 — 1 Early tension between East and West: The ideological differences between the superpowers","h":"Two systems, three men: why each side read the other's security as aggression","t":"The USA was a capitalist democracy that believed prosperity and peace came from free elections, private property and open trade; the USSR was a one-party communist state that believed capitalism produced exploitation and war and that only party rule could protect the revolution. Each therefore saw the other's ordinary behaviour as a threat: American loans and trade looked to Stalin like a way of buying Europe, and Soviet control of its neighbours looked to Washington like the start of endless expansion. The men mattered too. Stalin, who had seen his country invaded through Poland twice in his lifetime and lose over twenty million dead, wanted friendly governments on that road whatever elections said. Truman, president from April 1945, distrusted Stalin from the start and was readier than Roosevelt to confront him. Churchill had been suspicious throughout, and in March 1946 at Fulton in Missouri he told an American audience that an iron curtain had come down across Europe. A top-level answer uses ideology as a lens rather than a label: it explains why the same act, such as the Marshall Plan or the fortifying of Eastern Europe, was defensive to the side that did it and hostile to the side that watched."},{"code":"P4-1 — 1 Early tension between East and West: The impact on US-Soviet relations of the atomic bomb","h":"The bomb, the telegrams and the satellites: distrust turned into policy, 1945-47","t":"The USA tested the first atomic bomb at Alamogordo on 16 July 1945 and used bombs on Hiroshima on 6 August and Nagasaki on 9 August 1945. At Potsdam Truman told Stalin only that the USA had a powerful new weapon; Stalin, already informed by his agents, said little and ordered the Soviet programme to be accelerated, and the USSR tested its own bomb in August 1949. The suspicion was then written down. In February 1946 George Kennan, the American diplomat in Moscow, sent the Long Telegram: the USSR believed it could never live at peace with capitalism, was arming, but would retreat when met with firmness. In September 1946 the Soviet ambassador in Washington, Nikolai Novikov, sent his own telegram: the USA was seeking world domination and building bases for a future war. Meanwhile communist-dominated governments were installed across Eastern Europe, in Poland by the rigged election of January 1947, and in Hungary, Romania, Bulgaria and, by the coup of February 1948, Czechoslovakia. A top-level answer links the three: the bomb gave each side a reason to fear the other's intentions, the telegrams turned that fear into a settled reading of the other's motives, and the satellite states turned the reading into a hard frontier."},{"code":"P4-1 — 2 The development of the Cold War: The Truman Doctrine and the Marshall Plan, 1947","h":"Containment in two halves: the promise of March 1947 and the money of June 1947","t":"In February 1947 Britain told Washington it could no longer pay to support the Greek government against communist forces in its civil war, or to prop up Turkey. On 12 March 1947 Truman asked Congress for 400 million dollars for the two countries and set out a principle: the USA would support free peoples resisting attempts to subjugate them. This was containment, and it was a change from the American habit of staying out of European quarrels. On 5 June 1947 the Secretary of State, George Marshall, offered American money to rebuild Europe as a whole, arguing that poverty and ruin were what communism fed on; Congress voted the funds in April 1948 and about 13 billion dollars went to sixteen West European countries over four years. Molotov walked out of the Paris talks in July 1947 and the USSR forbade its neighbours to apply, so the plan divided the continent in practice as well as in principle. A top-level answer keeps the two halves connected and explains the Soviet reading: to Stalin, dollars in Western Europe were an American sphere of influence bought rather than conquered, and his answer was Cominform, Comecon and tighter control."},{"code":"P4-1 — 2 The development of the Cold War: The significance of Cominform, Comecon and NATO","h":"Two blocs get their institutions: 1947, 1949 and the habit of permanent alliance","t":"Cominform, the Communist Information Bureau, was set up in September 1947 to bring the communist parties of Europe under Moscow's direction, to keep the satellite states out of the Marshall Plan and to hunt out leaders who would not obey; Yugoslavia under Tito was expelled from it in 1948 for refusing. Comecon, the Council for Mutual Economic Assistance, followed in January 1949 to link the economies of Eastern Europe to the Soviet economy and to offer, on paper, an eastern answer to Marshall Aid, though it moved far less money. In the West, twelve states signed the North Atlantic Treaty in Washington on 4 April 1949, agreeing that an attack on one would be treated as an attack on all; NATO tied American forces to the defence of Western Europe in peacetime, something the USA had never before promised. A top-level answer treats these bodies as the moment the division stopped being an argument and became machinery: after 1949 each half of Europe had its own economic system and its own command structure, so that later crises were handled by two organised blocs rather than by four wartime allies."},{"code":"P4-1 — 2 The development of the Cold War: Berlin","h":"The first confrontation: currency, blockade, airlift and two Germanys","t":"Berlin lay about 160 kilometres inside the Soviet zone and was itself divided into four sectors, so the western powers governed an island in Soviet-controlled territory. As the western zones recovered and the eastern did not, the USA and Britain merged their zones into Bizonia on 1 January 1947, and on 20 June 1948 the western powers introduced a new currency, the Deutsche Mark, into their zones, extending it to their sectors of Berlin three days later. Stalin, who could not accept a reviving capitalist Germany with a showcase inside his zone, closed the road, rail and canal routes into West Berlin on 24 June 1948. Rather than fight or withdraw, the western powers flew supplies in from 26 June; at the peak, in the spring of 1949, aircraft were landing every few minutes, and about 1.6 million tons of food and coal had been flown in by the time Stalin lifted the blockade on 12 May 1949. The airlift itself went on until 30 September 1949, carrying about 2.3 million tons in all. Germany was then formally split: the Federal Republic in the west in May 1949 and the German Democratic Republic in the east in October 1949. A top-level answer explains the crisis as cause and effect rather than adventure: the currency forced the issue, the airlift made force the only way to stop it, and neither side would pay that price, so the line hardened instead."},{"code":"P4-1 — 3 The Cold War intensifies: The significance of the arms race","h":"Matching weapon for weapon: from the Soviet bomb to Sputnik, and the Warsaw Pact","t":"The American monopoly lasted four years: the USSR tested an atomic bomb in August 1949. The USA tested a hydrogen bomb in November 1952 and the USSR followed in 1953, and both then built bombers and missiles to carry them, the Americans ringing the USSR with air bases while the Soviets worked on rockets. In August 1957 the USSR tested an intercontinental missile and on 4 October 1957 launched Sputnik, the first satellite, which shocked Americans because a rocket that could reach orbit could reach the United States; the USA replied with Explorer in January 1958. Alliance followed weapons: when West Germany was admitted to NATO in May 1955, the USSR answered on 14 May 1955 with the Warsaw Pact, binding its satellites into one command under Soviet generals. A top-level answer explains the race as a machine that fed itself and steadied as it grew: each advance forced a reply, which made the other side's advance look necessary, yet as both sides acquired weapons that could destroy the other, direct war between them became the one thing neither could risk."},{"code":"P4-1 — 3 The Cold War intensifies: Events in 1956 leading to the Hungarian Uprising","h":"De-Stalinisation and its limits: Hungary, October and November 1956","t":"Hungary had been ruled since the late 1940s by Matyas Rakosi, a Stalinist whose secret police, the AVH, filled the prisons while living standards fell. In February 1956, at the Twentieth Party Congress, Khrushchev made a secret speech denouncing Stalin's crimes and the cult around him, and across Eastern Europe people took it as permission to demand change; there were riots in Poznan in Poland in June 1956 and Gomulka came to power in Poland in October. Rakosi was removed in July 1956, but his replacement, Erno Gero, changed nothing. On 23 October 1956 a student demonstration in Budapest turned into a rising: the huge statue of Stalin was pulled down and fighting spread. Imre Nagy became prime minister on 24 October, announced an end to one-party rule on 30 October as Soviet troops pulled out of Budapest, and on 1 November declared that Hungary would be neutral and would leave the Warsaw Pact, appealing to the United Nations. That was the limit. On 4 November about a thousand Soviet tanks entered Budapest, and after a week of fighting some three thousand Hungarians were dead. A top-level answer shows the escalation: reform demanded inside the system was tolerable, but leaving the alliance was not, and it was Nagy's announcement of 1 November, not the demonstrations, that brought the tanks."},{"code":"P4-1 — 3 The Cold War intensifies: The international reaction to the Soviet invasion of Hungary","h":"Words, refugees and no soldiers: what the world did about Hungary","t":"The West condemned the invasion and did nothing more. The United Nations General Assembly called on the USSR to withdraw, but the Security Council could do nothing because the USSR had a veto, and the committee the UN later set up was refused entry to Hungary. Eisenhower, days from the presidential election of 6 November 1956, made it plain that American troops would not fight for a country inside the Soviet sphere, even though broadcasts from the West had encouraged Hungarians to hope for help. The timing helped Moscow: Britain, France and Israel were attacking Egypt over Suez in the same fortnight, which split the western allies and cost them the moral high ground. About 200,000 Hungarians fled, mainly through Austria, and Janos Kadar was installed as leader; Nagy, taken after being promised safe conduct, was tried in secret and executed in 1958. Many communists in Western Europe resigned from their parties in disgust. A top-level answer draws the lesson both sides took: containment meant holding the line, not rolling it back, so the USSR learned that its own bloc was safe from western interference, and the peoples of Eastern Europe learned that no help would come."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1(a): turning the Tehran Conference into one explained consequence","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(a): 'Explain one consequence of the Tehran Conference of November to December 1943.' [4 marks]. Question 1 is printed in two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each asking for one consequence of a separate event and each marked on its own out of 4, so this part needs one consequence analysed and supported. This example builds 1(a). The information you are working from: Tehran, from 28 November to 1 December 1943, was the first meeting of Stalin, Roosevelt and Churchill in one room; the three agreed that Britain and the USA would land in France in May 1944 and that the Red Army would attack in the east at the same time; Stalin promised to enter the war against Japan once Germany was beaten; a postwar international organisation was accepted in principle; Poland's frontiers were to move westwards, the eastern border following the Curzon Line with German land making up the loss; the western landings came on 6 June 1944, and the Red Army took Bucharest on 31 August 1944, Warsaw on 17 January 1945 and Budapest on 13 February 1945.","steps":["Sort what Tehran agreed into commitments with a timetable and statements of intention, because only the first kind produces a consequence you can prove: the landing in France was given a month, while the international organisation and Poland's frontiers were intentions left to be settled later.","Pick a consequence that is a change of state, not another event in the story: before Tehran it was an open question which army would reach Eastern Europe first; after it the answer was fixed, because the western armies were committed to France while the Red Army was left to clear the east alone.","Support it with the precise information: the western landings came on 6 June 1944, while Soviet forces took Bucharest on 31 August 1944, Warsaw on 17 January 1945 and Budapest on 13 February 1945.","Now make the link the grid calls analysis: because the timetable agreed at Tehran put the western armies into France and nowhere further east, the capitals between Berlin and Moscow were liberated by Soviet troops, so when the allies came to argue at Yalta and Potsdam about how those countries should be governed the Red Army was already standing in them."],"cues":["Which of the Tehran agreements carried a timetable, and why does that matter when you are hunting for a consequence?","What could be said about who would occupy Eastern Europe after Tehran that could not be said before it?","Which dated captures prove that the change actually happened?","Why should the timing of a landing in France decide who is standing in Warsaw?"]},{"title":"Question 2: planning a narrative account of the communist takeover of Czechoslovakia","setup":"Booklet P, Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the communist takeover of Czechoslovakia in the years 1945-48.' (8 marks). The booklet prints: 'You may use the following in your answer: the Czechoslovak election (May 1946); Klement Gottwald. You must also use information of your own.' The mark scheme shuts an answer out of Level 3 altogether if it does not address three or more aspects of content. You know the following about these years: Czechoslovakia came out of the war with a parliamentary tradition and a mixed government under President Benes; the communists were genuinely popular, having been liberated in part by the Red Army after the West had abandoned the country at Munich in 1938; the election of 26 May 1946 made them the largest party with about 38 per cent of the vote and Gottwald became prime minister in July 1946; the government accepted the invitation to the Paris talks on Marshall Aid in July 1947 and reversed itself within days after Stalin summoned the delegation to Moscow; the communists held the interior ministry and filled the police with their own men; twelve non-communist ministers resigned in protest in February 1948, expecting Benes to refuse the communists; Gottwald threatened a general strike and armed workers' militia appeared on the streets of Prague; Benes accepted the resignations and appointed a communist-dominated government on 25 February 1948; Jan Masaryk, the foreign minister, was found dead below a window of the foreign ministry on 10 March 1948; a single-list election followed on 30 May 1948 and Benes resigned in June.","steps":["Choose a spine of four dated episodes inside the years given, so the account has a sequence and not a heap: the mixed government of 1945, the election of May 1946, the Marshall Aid reversal of July 1947, and the February days that ended on 25 February 1948.","Count the separate aspects of content your episodes cover, because access to Level 3 turns on there being three or more: the Marshall Aid reversal, the communist hold on the police, the resignation of the twelve ministers and the death of Masaryk are all your own material.","Write the linking sentence between episode one and episode two, since that is what analysis means here: because the communists could claim both the liberation and a programme of reform in a country the West had abandoned at Munich, they won their 38 per cent at the ballot box rather than at gunpoint, which is why Gottwald could take office by ordinary means in July 1946.","Link episode two to episode three in the same way: once a communist prime minister also controlled the interior ministry and the police, the government could be overruled from Moscow on Marshall Aid in July 1947 and lose nothing at home, and the non-communist ministers discovered that the levers of the state were no longer theirs to pull.","Close with a sentence that says what the sequence amounted to rather than repeating it: the twelve who resigned in February 1948 were the last men to believe that Czechoslovakia's institutions still worked, and when Benes signed their replacements into office on 25 February the one mixed government left in Eastern Europe had gone."],"cues":["How many episodes can you actually develop in about thirteen minutes, and what must each one have?","How many separate aspects of content do your chosen episodes cover, and why does that matter to your mark?","Why did the communists here not need the methods used in Poland or Romania?","Which ministry mattered more than the premiership, and why?","What should the last sentence do that the previous ones have not?"]},{"title":"Question 3: explaining the importance of the ideological difference rather than describing it","setup":"Booklet P, Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. The bullet here is: 'The importance of the ideological differences between the superpowers for the origins of the Cold War.' (8 marks). No stimulus points are printed. What you know: the USA was a capitalist democracy with free elections, private property and an interest in open trade; the USSR was a one-party state with the economy in state hands and no free elections, ruled by Stalin since the late 1920s; American troops had been sent against the Bolsheviks after 1918 and Washington did not recognise the Soviet government until 1933; over twenty million Soviet citizens died in the war and Germany had come through Poland twice in Stalin's lifetime, so Stalin wanted governments he could trust along that road; the two sides signed the Declaration on Liberated Europe at Yalta in February 1945, promising free elections; Roosevelt died on 12 April 1945 and Truman, who distrusted Stalin, took over; Churchill pressed for a firm line and told an American audience at Fulton in March 1946 that an iron curtain had descended; Kennan reported in February 1946 that the USSR believed it could never live at peace with capitalism, and Novikov reported in September 1946 that the USA was seeking world supremacy.","steps":["Fix what the bullet is actually about: not two lists of beliefs, but what each side concluded about the other's intentions from those beliefs, in the years when the two still had to govern Europe together.","Take the first strand forward in time: because a free election meant a legitimate government in Washington and an unreliable neighbour in Moscow, the same promise signed in the Declaration on Liberated Europe in February 1945 could be kept by one side's reckoning and broken by the other's within two years.","Take the second strand forward from the other side: because a capitalist power was expected in Moscow to be driven by markets and hostility, American loans, bases and aid were read as preparation for a future war rather than as recovery, which is exactly what Novikov reported home in September 1946.","Weigh what would have been different without it, since that is what importance means: two powers with the same appetite for security but no quarrel of principle could have bargained over zones and reparations, whereas these two treated each other's ordinary security measures as evidence of an intention to destroy them.","Finish with a judgement in the language of the question: the ideological difference caused no single event by itself, but it decided how every event was read, and a quarrel in which nothing the other side does can count as reassurance is a quarrel that can only widen."],"cues":["What is the bullet actually asking about, and what would the describing answer look like?","How could the same promise of free elections mean two different things?","Why did American wealth and generosity look threatening from Moscow?","What would these years have looked like between two powers with no quarrel of principle?","What single sentence would make your judgement clear to the examiner?"]},{"title":"Question 1: Comecon and the expulsion of Yugoslavia without slipping into narrative","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1: '1(a) Explain one consequence of the founding of Comecon in January 1949. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the expulsion of Yugoslavia from Cominform in June 1948. [4 marks]'. The two parts are on separate events and are marked separately out of 4. The events you have: Marshall Aid was offered to the whole of Europe in June 1947 and the USSR forbade its neighbours to take it; Cominform was founded in September 1947 to direct Europe's communist parties; Yugoslavia, which had become communist largely by its own efforts under Tito, was expelled from Cominform on 28 June 1948 for refusing to take orders from Moscow; Stalin cut off Soviet aid and the bloc broke off trade, but Tito was not overthrown and took western credits instead, beginning with American loans in 1949; leading communists elsewhere were tried and executed as Titoists - Rajk in Hungary and Kostov in Bulgaria in 1949, Slansky in Czechoslovakia in 1952; Comecon was agreed at a Moscow conference in the first week of January 1949 and announced on 25 January, with the USSR, Bulgaria, Czechoslovakia, Hungary, Poland and Romania as members, Albania joining in February 1949 and the German Democratic Republic in 1950; it worked through bilateral treaties in which the USSR supplied raw materials and took manufactures in return, and it moved far less money than Marshall Aid.","steps":["Fix the point each consequence runs from: Comecon was agreed in the first week of January 1949 and announced on 25 January, and Yugoslavia was expelled from Cominform on 28 June 1948, so everything you offer must belong to what came after those dates.","Take 1(a) forward from Comecon: the satellite economies were turned towards Moscow and away from their old markets, trading by bilateral treaty with the USSR, so the division of Europe became economic as well as political and the two halves began to develop apart.","Take 1(b) forward from the expulsion: because Tito was not removed, every other leader in the bloc learned what Moscow now feared most, and the answer was a hunt for Titoists that killed Rajk in Hungary and Kostov in Bulgaria in 1949 and Slansky in Czechoslovakia in 1952.","Add the specific support each part needs: for 1(a) that Comecon moved far less money than the thirteen billion dollars of Marshall Aid and worked by bilateral treaty rather than as a common market; for 1(b) that Yugoslavia stayed communist while taking American credits from 1949.","Read both parts back and cut any sentence that merely reports what the organisation was without saying what followed from it."],"cues":["On what dates did each of these two things happen?","Which way did the satellites' trade point after 1949, and which way had it pointed before?","What did the other leaders in the bloc learn from the fact that Tito survived?","Which precise details show the size and the direction of each change?","How can you tell a describing sentence from an explaining one in your own writing?"]},{"title":"Question 2: a narrative account of Poland in 1956 that goes beyond the bullets","setup":"Booklet P, Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the events in Poland in 1956.' (8 marks). The booklet prints: 'You may use the following in your answer: the Poznan riots (June 1956); Wladyslaw Gomulka. You must also use information of your own.' An answer that does not address three or more aspects of content cannot reach Level 3. The material available: Khrushchev's secret speech to the Twentieth Party Congress in February 1956 denounced Stalin's crimes, and its contents were known in Poland within weeks; Boleslaw Bierut, the Stalinist party leader, died in Moscow on 12 March 1956 and Edward Ochab took his place on 20 March; workers at the locomotive works in Poznan struck over pay and taxes on 28 June 1956 and the demonstration became a battle with the security forces in which at least fifty-seven people were killed; Gomulka had been expelled and imprisoned under Bierut as a nationalist deviationist, released at the end of 1954 and readmitted to the party in August 1956; the Central Committee prepared to elect him first secretary in October without asking Moscow first; Khrushchev and a Soviet delegation flew to Warsaw uninvited on 19 October 1956 while Soviet troops moved towards the city; Gomulka was elected first secretary on 21 October; Khrushchev accepted him once he promised that Poland would stay in the Warsaw Pact; the collectivisation of farming was then abandoned and Cardinal Wyszynski was released from internment and back in Warsaw on 28 October.","steps":["Set the starting condition early in the year so the account has somewhere to travel from: a Stalinist party in Warsaw that loses its leader in March 1956, with the secret speech already being talked about.","Use the first bullet as a cause and not a label: the Poznan strike began over pay and taxes, but the shooting turned an economic grievance into a political one that the party had to answer.","Bring in your own material to show the pressure working on the leadership, which is also what carries the answer past three aspects of content and into Level 3: Bierut's death and Ochab's weak succession, the circulation of the secret speech, and the rehabilitation in August of a man the party itself had imprisoned.","Narrate October as a confrontation rather than as a list of dates: a Central Committee about to choose its own leader, a Soviet delegation arriving uninvited on 19 October with troops on the roads, and the election carried through anyway on 21 October.","Identify the turning point and close on it: Gomulka got his way because he offered Khrushchev the one thing that mattered, that Poland would stay in the Warsaw Pact, so Moscow could allow a new leader and the end of collectivisation without losing the alliance, which is why Poland ended 1956 differently from Hungary."],"cues":["Where should an account of 1956 in Poland actually begin, and why not in June?","How does a strike over pay become a crisis for the party?","Which events away from Poznan are part of this story, and why are they worth marks?","What made the October meeting a confrontation rather than an appointment?","Which single promise made a Soviet invasion unnecessary?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Give the month and year of each of the three wartime conferences, and name the leaders of the three powers at the last of them.","a":"Tehran, November to December 1943; Yalta, February 1945; Potsdam, July to August 1945. At Potsdam: Stalin for the USSR, Truman for the USA, and Churchill for Britain until he resigned on 26 July after losing the general election, Attlee taking his place at the table on 28 July."},{"q":"What did the Long Telegram and the Novikov Telegram each claim about the other superpower?","a":"Kennan's Long Telegram of February 1946 said the USSR believed it could not coexist with capitalism, was arming, and would back down if met with firmness. Novikov's telegram of September 1946 said the USA was seeking world domination and preparing bases for war."},{"q":"What triggered the Berlin Blockade, and how did it end?","a":"The introduction of the Deutsche Mark into the western zones on 20 June 1948, and into the western sectors of Berlin three days later. Stalin cut the land and water routes on 24 June 1948; the airlift kept West Berlin supplied and the blockade was lifted on 12 May 1949."},{"q":"Why did Soviet tanks enter Budapest on 4 November 1956 rather than in the last week of October?","a":"Because on 1 November Nagy announced that Hungary would be neutral and would leave the Warsaw Pact. Reform inside the bloc could be tolerated; a satellite leaving the alliance could not, since others would have followed."}]},"room":{"facts":["Booklet P is worth 32 marks and has three questions: Question 1, printed in two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each 'Explain one consequence of...' for 4 marks and each on a separate event or development; Question 2, 'Write a narrative account analysing...', 8 marks; Question 3, 'Explain two of the following: the importance of ... for ...', two chosen parts at 8 marks each, 16 in all.","Only Question 2 prints stimulus points, as two bullets followed by 'You must also use information of your own.' The cap is printed in the mark scheme: 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' There is no cap at Level 2. Questions 1 and 3 print no bullets and carry no cap.","Each part of Question 1 is marked on its own two-level grid out of 4: Level 2 needs features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific supporting information. Two descriptions of the event, however accurate, stay in Level 1.","Question 3 is marked twice on a three-level grid out of 8, and Level 3 needs analysis of importance in a coherent line of reasoning: what difference the event or person made to later situations and developments, not what happened.","Timing: Paper 2 lasts 1 hour 50 minutes for 64 marks, about 55 minutes a booklet, so booklet P's 32 marks get roughly a minute a mark with planning time on top - about 12 minutes on Question 1's two parts, 13 on Question 2 and 14 on each part of Question 3, leaving a couple of minutes to check."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Yalta Conference of February 1945. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2, marked on the 4-mark consequence grid. Level 2 (3-4) requires features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific support. Credit the four-zone division of Germany and of Berlin, the Declaration on Liberated Europe and the promise of free elections that was not kept, the founding of the United Nations, or the Soviet promise to enter the war against Japan.","sol":"One consequence of Yalta was that Germany was divided into four occupied zones, and Berlin, although it lay deep inside the Soviet zone, was divided in the same way between the USA, the USSR, Britain and France. Because the Allies had agreed how to occupy Germany but not what Germany should become, each power ran its own zone in its own way, and the western sectors of Berlin were left as an isolated island inside Soviet-controlled territory that could be cut off by land at any time. That is why Berlin, rather than anywhere else, became the place where the two sides first confronted each other directly in 1948.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["A consequence is something that was true afterwards because of the conference, so ask what Yalta put in place that had not existed before.","The strongest consequences here are the arrangements that outlived the war: how Germany was to be occupied, and what was promised about the countries the Red Army had liberated.","Write it in three moves: name the consequence, give the specific detail that proves it, then explain why it mattered later."],"codes":["P4-1 — 1 Early tension between East and West: The Grand Alliance"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of the American development and use of the atomic bomb in 1945. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Level 2 needs a consequence analysed with specific support. Credit the acceleration of the Soviet atomic programme and the test of August 1949, the deepening of Stalin's distrust after Truman's remark at Potsdam, or the start of an arms race in which each new weapon had to be matched.","sol":"One consequence was that Stalin ordered Soviet scientists to build a bomb of their own as quickly as possible, and the USSR tested one in August 1949, four years after Hiroshima. Truman had told Stalin at Potsdam only that the USA possessed a powerful new weapon, and since Stalin already knew about the project through his agents, the remark convinced him that his ally intended to negotiate from a position of strength. The result was that the two powers began matching each other weapon for weapon, so that the American monopoly, which might have forced Soviet concessions, lasted only until 1949.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what the bomb did to the balance between the two powers, not about what it did to Japan.","Stalin was not told the details at Potsdam but already knew; consider what that told him about Truman's intentions.","Name the consequence, date the proof of it, then explain the link back to the bomb."],"codes":["P4-1 — 1 Early tension between East and West: The impact on US-Soviet relations of the atomic bomb","P4-1 — 3 The Cold War intensifies: The significance of the arms race"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of Churchill's speech at Fulton, Missouri, in March 1946. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the hardening of American opinion and the currency the phrase 'iron curtain' gave to the western view of Soviet behaviour, or Stalin's reply that the speech was a call to war, which he used to justify tighter control of Eastern Europe; Novikov's telegram of September 1946 may be used as supporting information.","sol":"One consequence was that Stalin treated the speech as proof that his wartime allies were now preparing to act against him. He replied publicly that Churchill was calling for war against the USSR, and he used the speech to justify keeping Eastern Europe firmly under Soviet control rather than holding the free elections promised at Yalta. The Soviet ambassador in Washington, Novikov, reported in September 1946 that the USA was seeking world domination, which shows how far this reading had taken hold. The speech therefore made the division public and hardened attitudes on both sides of it.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Churchill was out of office in 1946, so his speech mattered for what it changed in others' minds rather than for any policy it enacted.","Look for the effect in Moscow as well as in the USA: how would a Soviet leader read a speech like that, made in the president's presence?","State the consequence, support it with what Stalin said or did afterwards, then explain why the speech produced it."],"codes":["P4-1 — 1 Early tension between East and West: The ideological differences between the superpowers","P4-1 — 1 Early tension between East and West: The impact on US-Soviet relations of the atomic bomb"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Potsdam Conference of July to August 1945. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the Long Telegram sent by George Kennan in February 1946. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4 on the consequence grid, so each part needs one consequence analysed with specific support. In 1(a) credit the zone-by-zone reparations settlement and the economic separation of the zones that followed, the deepening of mistrust between Truman and Stalin over the atomic bomb and Poland, or the confirmation of the four-power occupation of Germany and Berlin. In 1(b) credit the settled American reading of Soviet motives as unappeasable but yielding to firmness, the shaping of containment and of the Truman Doctrine, or the Soviet answer in Novikov's telegram of September 1946.","sol":"1(a) Because reparations were to be taken by each power from its own zone, with the USSR also promised 10 per cent of the industrial equipment removed from the western zones without payment and a further 15 per cent in exchange for food and raw materials, the four occupiers never ran Germany as a single economy. The USA and Britain, tired of feeding a zone that received nothing from the Soviet zone in return, merged theirs into Bizonia on 1 January 1947, and from there the western zones moved steadily towards forming a separate state. 1(b) Kennan's telegram gave Washington a settled reading of Soviet motives: the USSR believed it could never live at peace with capitalism and was arming, but would retreat before firmness. Once that reading was accepted, later Soviet acts became evidence for a case already decided, so the rigged Polish election of January 1947 was read as a programme of expansion rather than as a search for security, and firmness became policy in Truman's speech of 12 March 1947. Moscow read Washington the same way in return, since Novikov reported in September 1946 that the USA was seeking world domination.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate parts on two separate things: a conference in 1(a) and a single diplomatic telegram in 1(b), so neither answer can help the other.","For 1(a) the strongest consequence is economic: ask what taking reparations zone by zone did to the idea of running Germany as one country.","For 1(b) a telegram changes minds rather than borders, so ask what Washington believed about Soviet motives afterwards that it had not settled before."],"codes":["P4-1 — 1 Early tension between East and West: The Grand Alliance","P4-1 — 1 Early tension between East and West: The impact on US-Soviet relations of the atomic bomb"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Truman Doctrine of March 1947. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the founding of Cominform in September 1947. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4 on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the American money and arms sent to Greece and Turkey and the defeat of the communist forces in the Greek civil war, or the commitment of the USA to permanent involvement in Europe through containment. In 1(b) credit the bringing of Europe's communist parties under Moscow's direction, the forced rejection of Marshall Aid by the satellite states, or the expulsion of Tito's Yugoslavia in 1948.","sol":"1(a) The USA took over a role Britain could no longer afford. Truman asked Congress for 400 million dollars for Greece and Turkey, and American money, weapons and advisers helped the Greek government win its civil war by 1949. This committed the USA to defending governments far from its own borders in peacetime, which was a break with its earlier habit of staying out of European quarrels, and containment became the settled principle of American policy. 1(b) Cominform turned Soviet influence over Europe's communist parties into direction of them. Founded in September 1947, months after Marshall's offer of American aid, it ordered the parties of the satellite states to reject that aid and to follow Moscow's line, and it hunted out leaders who would not obey, so that Yugoslavia under Tito was expelled in 1948 for refusing. Eastern Europe was from then on run as a single bloc with one policy rather than as a set of allied governments.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["One part is about what the USA now did, the other about what Moscow built in answer.","For 1(a) follow the money: which two countries received it, and what had happened in the nearer of them by 1949?","For 1(b) ask what Cominform could order the satellite parties to do that Moscow had not been able simply to command before."],"codes":["P4-1 — 2 The development of the Cold War: The Truman Doctrine and the Marshall Plan, 1947","P4-1 — 2 The development of the Cold War: The significance of Cominform, Comecon and NATO"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Marshall Plan. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the communist coup in Czechoslovakia in February 1948. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4 on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the recovery of Western Europe and its economic attachment to the USA, about 13 billion dollars to sixteen countries by 1952, or the widening gap between the two halves of Germany. In 1(b) credit the removal of the last mixed government in Eastern Europe, the hardening of western opinion that carried the Marshall funds through Congress in April 1948, or the push towards a western military alliance and the North Atlantic Treaty of April 1949.","sol":"1(a) Western Europe recovered, and it recovered on American terms. Congress voted the money in April 1948 and about 13 billion dollars went to sixteen countries over four years, rebuilding industry and trade; the western zones of Germany shared in it, so the part of Germany the western powers held grew visibly richer than the Soviet zone. Because the aid required the recipients to open their accounts and co-operate with one another, it bound them to the USA politically as well as economically. 1(b) The coup removed the last government in Eastern Europe in which non-communists still shared power, and in doing so it convinced western governments that Soviet control would spread wherever it was not resisted. Czechoslovakia was the one eastern state with a democratic tradition and a coalition government, so its fall in February 1948 was read in Washington and London as proof of what Kennan had warned of: the Marshall funds passed Congress two months later, and within fourteen months twelve states had signed the North Atlantic Treaty.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["The two parts are separate: American money offered to Europe in 1(a), a seizure of power in Prague in 1(b).","For 1(a) think about what the money did to the difference between the two halves of Germany.","For 1(b) ask what western governments did in the fourteen months after the coup that they had not been willing to do before it."],"codes":["P4-1 — 2 The development of the Cold War: The Truman Doctrine and the Marshall Plan, 1947","P4-1 — 2 The development of the Cold War: The significance of Cominform, Comecon and NATO"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Berlin Blockade of 1948-49. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the signing of the North Atlantic Treaty in April 1949. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4 on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the creation of two German states in 1949, the propaganda gain for the West from the airlift, or the loss of Soviet standing in Berlin. In 1(b) credit the permanent American military commitment to Western Europe in peacetime, the promise that an attack on one member would be treated as an attack on all, or the Soviet answer of May 1955 when West Germany joined NATO and the Warsaw Pact followed.","sol":"1(a) Germany was formally split into two states. The crisis proved that the four powers could not govern one country together, so each side built the state it wanted in the zones it held: the Federal Republic of Germany was founded in the western zones in May 1949 and the German Democratic Republic in the Soviet zone in October 1949. The occupation line became a national frontier, and Berlin remained a divided city inside the eastern state. 1(b) The treaty kept American forces in Europe in peacetime, which the USA had never before promised in a time of peace. Twelve states agreed on 4 April 1949 that an attack on one would be treated as an attack on all, so the defence of Western Europe no longer waited on a decision taken in Washington after a crisis had begun. The USSR answered in kind rather than in words: when West Germany was admitted to NATO in May 1955, the Warsaw Pact followed within days and placed the satellite armies under Soviet command.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["One part is about Germany itself and one about the alliance the western powers built.","For 1(a) find the two events of 1949 that followed within months of the blockade being lifted.","For 1(b) ask what changed about American forces in Europe, and about how quickly the West could answer an attack."],"codes":["P4-1 — 2 The development of the Cold War: Berlin","P4-1 — 2 The development of the Cold War: The significance of Cominform, Comecon and NATO"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Hungarian Uprising of 1956. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the international reaction to the Soviet invasion of Hungary. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4 on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the reimposition of Soviet control under Kadar, the execution of Nagy, and the limit this set to de-Stalinisation in the satellite states. In 1(b) credit the demonstration that containment did not mean rolling back Soviet control, the flight of about 200,000 refugees who were taken in by the West, or the resignations from communist parties in Western Europe.","sol":"1(a) Soviet control of Eastern Europe was reimposed and the limits of de-Stalinisation were made plain. About a thousand tanks entered Budapest on 4 November, some three thousand Hungarians were killed, Janos Kadar was installed as leader, and Nagy, taken after being promised safe conduct, was tried in secret and executed in 1958. No satellite state attempted to leave the Warsaw Pact again for many years. 1(b) The West's response defined containment as holding the line rather than pushing it back. The United Nations General Assembly called for a Soviet withdrawal, but the Security Council was blocked by the Soviet veto, and Eisenhower, days from the election of 6 November, sent no troops, while Britain and France were occupied with their own attack on Egypt. Moscow therefore learned that its own bloc was safe from western interference, and the West's gain was one of reputation alone: about 200,000 Hungarians fled abroad and were taken in, and many communists in Western Europe resigned from their parties in disgust.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate parts: what the invasion did inside the Soviet bloc in 1(a), and what the world's response did in 1(b).","For 1(a) ask what happened to Hungary's leaders and what other satellite governments concluded from it.","For 1(b) be precise about what the West actually did and did not do in November 1956."],"codes":["P4-1 — 3 The Cold War intensifies: Events in 1956 leading to the Hungarian Uprising","P4-1 — 3 The Cold War intensifies: The international reaction to the Soviet invasion of Hungary"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the key events of the Berlin Crisis in the years 1948-49. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the introduction of the Deutsche Mark into the western zones (June 1948); the Berlin Airlift. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) requires a clear sequence leading to an outcome with the linkage between events analysed. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; own material might include Bizonia, the London conference on a West German state, the Soviet closure of the land and water routes, the scale and peak of the airlift, NATO, and the founding of the two German states.","sol":"The crisis grew out of the recovery of the western zones. The USA and Britain had merged theirs into Bizonia on 1 January 1947, and by 1948 the western powers were planning a West German state, so the new currency introduced on 20 June 1948, and extended to the western sectors of Berlin three days later, was the point at which that plan became visible. Stalin could not accept a reviving capitalist Germany with a prosperous western showcase a hundred miles inside his own zone, and because Berlin depended on road, rail and canal routes that ran through Soviet-held territory, he had a means of pressure that required no shooting: he closed them on 24 June 1948. The western powers would not fight their way through and would not leave, so from 26 June they flew supplies in instead, and by the spring of 1949 aircraft were landing every few minutes and about 1.6 million tons of food and coal had been delivered. That reversed the pressure, because only shooting down transport aircraft could have stopped them, and Stalin lifted the blockade on 12 May 1949; the airlift flew on until 30 September 1949, carrying about 2.3 million tons in all. The sequence ended by hardening what it had been fought over: NATO was signed in April 1949 and two German states were founded that year.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Begin before the blockade: what had been happening in the western zones that made the currency reform the trigger it was?","The account needs links, not just dates - ask at each stage why the other side's move left the choice it did.","Plan three or four episodes with a connective sentence between each, so that the account addresses three or more aspects of content and not only the two bullets."],"codes":["P4-1 — 2 The development of the Cold War: Berlin","P4-1 — 2 The development of the Cold War: The significance of Cominform, Comecon and NATO","P4-1 — 2 The development of the Cold War: The Truman Doctrine and the Marshall Plan, 1947"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the events in Hungary in the years 1955-56. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: Khrushchev's secret speech (February 1956); Imre Nagy. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 requires a clear analysed sequence leading to an outcome; no access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content. Own material might include Rakosi and the AVH, the Warsaw Pact of 1955, the Poznan riots and Gomulka, the removal of Rakosi and the appointment of Gero, the demonstration of 23 October, the withdrawal and return of Soviet troops, and the outcome under Kadar.","sol":"Hungary entered 1956 as one of the most rigidly Stalinist of the satellites, ruled by Matyas Rakosi through the AVH secret police and bound since May 1955 into the new Warsaw Pact. Khrushchev's secret speech of February 1956, denouncing Stalin's crimes, therefore had an effect its author had not intended: if Moscow now disowned Stalin's methods, Hungarians could argue that a Stalinist governor had lost his warrant. Events elsewhere showed what pressure might achieve, since riots at Poznan in June and Gomulka's return to power in Poland in October suggested that Moscow would bargain. Rakosi was removed in July, but his replacement, Erno Gero, changed nothing, so the demonstration in Budapest on 23 October found a city with nothing left to hope for from the government, and it became a rising in which the Stalin statue was pulled down. Concession then encouraged escalation: Imre Nagy was made prime minister on 24 October and Soviet troops withdrew from Budapest by 30 October, which convinced the reformers that more was possible. On 1 November Nagy announced neutrality and withdrawal from the Warsaw Pact, and that crossed the line Moscow could not allow, because a satellite free to leave would have unpicked the whole bloc. Soviet tanks returned on 4 November.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The account needs somewhere to travel from, so ask what Hungary was like in the earlier year the question gives you.","Ask at each step why the previous move made the next one more likely, especially why concessions made the demands grow.","Fix in advance how many separate aspects of content your episodes cover, since Level 3 is closed to an account that addresses fewer than three."],"codes":["P4-1 — 3 The Cold War intensifies: Events in 1956 leading to the Hungarian Uprising","P4-1 — 3 The Cold War intensifies: The significance of the arms race","P4-1 — 3 The Cold War intensifies: The international reaction to the Soviet invasion of Hungary"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the establishment of communist control in Poland in the years 1944-47. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the Lublin Committee (July 1944); the Polish election (January 1947). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 requires a clear analysed sequence leading to an outcome; no access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content. Own material might include the London government-in-exile and the Warsaw Rising of 1944, the renaming of the committee as a provisional government on 31 December 1944, the promise at Yalta that it would be broadened and hold free elections, the Provisional Government of National Unity of June 1945 and Mikolajczyk's place in it, the trial of sixteen underground leaders in Moscow, the Oder-Neisse border settled at Potsdam, the falsified referendum of June 1946, and Mikolajczyk's flight in October 1947.","sol":"Control was established on the ground before it was argued over at the conferences. As the Red Army crossed into Poland in July 1944 the USSR installed the Lublin Committee, and while the Warsaw Rising was crushed that autumn the committee survived to be renamed a provisional government on 31 December 1944 and recognised by Moscow days later, so the London government-in-exile now had a rival actually holding the country. That is why Yalta could promise only that this government would be broadened and would hold free elections: the western powers were bargaining about an administration already installed. The bargain produced the Provisional Government of National Unity of 28 June 1945, which Mikolajczyk joined as a deputy prime minister, a week after sixteen leaders of the Polish underground had been put on trial in Moscow, which showed what opposition outside the arrangement would cost. Potsdam then fixed Poland's western border on the Oder and Neisse without settling who would govern it. With the security police in communist hands the referendum of June 1946 was falsified, and the election of 19 January 1947 gave the communist-led Democratic Bloc an official 80 per cent. By the time Mikolajczyk fled in October 1947 the reorganisation promised at Yalta had produced a one-party state, and the broken promise of free elections had become Washington's plainest evidence of Soviet bad faith.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Choose four dated episodes across the four years so the account has a spine rather than a heap of facts.","The analysis lies in the joins: what did each episode leave settled on the ground, that made the next one possible?","Note before you write which episodes are your own material, and finish with a sentence saying what the sequence amounted to."],"codes":["P4-1 — 1 Early tension between East and West: The Grand Alliance","P4-1 — 1 Early tension between East and West: The ideological differences between the superpowers","P4-1 — 1 Early tension between East and West: The impact on US-Soviet relations of the atomic bomb"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the Marshall Plan for the division of Europe. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Level 3 (6-8) needs analysis of importance in a coherent line of reasoning. Credit the recovery and American attachment of Western Europe, the Soviet refusal and the ban on the satellites, Cominform and Comecon, the Czechoslovak coup, and the widening gap between the two halves of Germany.","sol":"The Marshall Plan mattered less as charity than as the choice that sorted Europe into two halves. Aid was offered in June 1947 to the whole continent, but only on terms that required governments to open their accounts and to plan together, which no state under Soviet control could accept; Molotov left the Paris talks in July and Poland and Czechoslovakia were ordered to withdraw. The line was therefore drawn by the offer itself rather than by any threat. Its second effect was to force Stalin to build a rival system: Cominform followed in September 1947 to discipline the communist parties, the Czechoslovak coup of February 1948 removed the last mixed government in the east, and Comecon was created in January 1949 to bind the satellite economies to Moscow. Its third effect was material. About 13 billion dollars over four years rebuilt sixteen western economies, including the western zones of Germany, so that the difference between the two halves of the continent became visible in wages, food and shops. Without the plan the quarrel over Germany would still have existed, but it would have remained a disagreement between governments; with it, Europe acquired two economic systems, and that is the division that lasted.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Importance means the difference it made afterwards, so resist any sentence that only explains what the plan was.","Trace two lines forward: what the money did in the West, and what the refusal forced Stalin to build in the East.","End with a judgement that says what would have been different without it."],"codes":["P4-1 — 2 The development of the Cold War: The Truman Doctrine and the Marshall Plan, 1947","P4-1 — 2 The development of the Cold War: The significance of Cominform, Comecon and NATO"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the arms race for relations between the superpowers in the years 1949-58. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Credit the ending of the American monopoly in 1949, the hydrogen bombs of 1952 and 1953, missiles and Sputnik in 1957, the effect on alliances including West Germany's entry into NATO and the Warsaw Pact of 1955, and the growing reluctance of either side to risk direct war.","sol":"The arms race changed relations in two opposite directions at once. It made each side more afraid of the other, because every advance had to be matched: the Soviet atomic test of August 1949 ended the American monopoly, the USA tested a hydrogen bomb in November 1952 and the USSR followed in 1953, and when the USSR tested an intercontinental missile and launched Sputnik in 1957 Americans realised for the first time that their own cities were within reach. Each new weapon was defensive to the side that built it and threatening to the side that watched, so spending rose on both sides and the language hardened. It also drew the alliances tighter, since weapons needed bases and allies: West Germany was admitted to NATO in May 1955 and the USSR answered within days with the Warsaw Pact, placing the satellite armies under Soviet command. Yet the same race made a direct war between the two powers steadily less likely, because by the later 1950s neither could attack the other without being destroyed in return. Its importance is therefore that it froze the confrontation: dangerous enough to keep both sides arming, but too dangerous for either to use, so the Cold War stayed cold.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what the race changed about how the two sides behaved, not simply which weapons appeared in which year.","Look for effects that pull in opposite directions, since the strongest answers explain both fear and caution.","Support the point about alliances with a precise pair of events from 1955."],"codes":["P4-1 — 3 The Cold War intensifies: The significance of the arms race","P4-1 — 1 Early tension between East and West: The impact on US-Soviet relations of the atomic bomb"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the international reaction to the Soviet invasion of Hungary for relations between East and West. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Credit the United Nations condemnation and the Soviet veto, Eisenhower's refusal to intervene and the effect of Suez, the demonstration that containment did not mean rolling back Soviet control, the reception of about 200,000 refugees, and the damage to the reputation of communism in the West.","sol":"The reaction mattered chiefly for what it taught both sides about the rules of the confrontation. The United Nations General Assembly called on the USSR to withdraw and a later committee was refused entry to Hungary, but the Security Council could do nothing because the USSR held a veto, and Eisenhower, days from the election of 6 November 1956, made clear that American soldiers would not fight for a country inside the Soviet sphere. Since western broadcasts had encouraged Hungarians to expect help, the lesson was bitter: containment meant holding the line, not pushing it back, and Moscow could act inside its own bloc without risking war. The timing made this worse, because Britain and France were attacking Egypt over Suez in the same fortnight, so the West was divided and its protests looked hollow. The importance was not therefore military but political. Moscow gained a free hand in Eastern Europe that lasted for decades, the satellite peoples learned that rebellion would not be rescued, and in Western Europe the sight of tanks in Budapest cost communist parties heavily in members and sympathy, so the West's advantage from the crisis was one of reputation rather than of territory.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The question is about the reaction, so build the answer from what governments and the United Nations did, then ask what followed from it.","Consider separately what Moscow learned, what Eastern Europeans learned and what happened to communism's standing in the West.","Use Suez and the American election as precise explanations of why nothing more was done."],"codes":["P4-1 — 3 The Cold War intensifies: The international reaction to the Soviet invasion of Hungary","P4-1 — 3 The Cold War intensifies: Events in 1956 leading to the Hungarian Uprising"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Which three leaders opened the Potsdam Conference on 17 July 1945?","a":"Stalin, Truman and Churchill."},{"q":"Who sent the Long Telegram in February 1946?","a":"George Kennan, the American charge d'affaires in Moscow."},{"q":"On what date was the Berlin Blockade lifted?","a":"12 May 1949."},{"q":"What did Imre Nagy announce on 1 November 1956?","a":"That Hungary would be neutral and would leave the Warsaw Pact."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The Grand Alliance and the Tehran Conference","back":"The USA, USSR and Britain, allied by war from 1941. At Tehran, November to December 1943, they agreed a second front in France for May 1944, Soviet entry against Japan after Germany, and moving Poland's frontiers west.","code":"P4-1 — 1 Early tension between East and West: The Grand Alliance","open":false},{"front":"Yalta Conference, February 1945","back":"Stalin, Roosevelt and Churchill agreed four occupation zones for Germany and Berlin, trials for Nazi leaders, the United Nations, Soviet entry against Japan, and free elections in liberated Europe.","code":"P4-1 — 1 Early tension between East and West: The Grand Alliance","open":false},{"front":"Potsdam Conference, July to August 1945","back":"Stalin, Truman and Churchill, who resigned on 26 July; Attlee took his place on 28 July. Zones confirmed, Nazism banned, reparations taken zone by zone, Poland's western border on the Oder and Neisse. Poland's government left unsettled.","code":"P4-1 — 1 Early tension between East and West: The Grand Alliance","open":false},{"front":"The ideological difference between the superpowers","back":"Capitalist democracy in the USA: free elections, private property, open trade. One-party communism in the USSR: state ownership and party rule. Each read the other's security measures as aggression.","code":"P4-1 — 1 Early tension between East and West: The ideological differences between the superpowers","open":false},{"front":"Churchill's Fulton speech, March 1946","back":"Out of office, Churchill told an American audience at Fulton, Missouri, that an iron curtain had descended across Europe. Stalin called it a call to war and used it to justify tighter control.","code":"P4-1 — 1 Early tension between East and West: The ideological differences between the superpowers","open":false},{"front":"The Long Telegram and the Novikov Telegram, 1946","back":"Kennan, February 1946: the USSR believed coexistence impossible, was arming, but would back down before firmness. Novikov, September 1946: the USA sought world domination and was preparing bases for war.","code":"P4-1 — 1 Early tension between East and West: The impact on US-Soviet relations of the atomic bomb","open":false},{"front":"The atomic bomb and US-Soviet relations","back":"Tested 16 July 1945; used on Hiroshima 6 August and Nagasaki 9 August. Truman hinted at it to Stalin at Potsdam; Stalin, already informed by agents, accelerated the Soviet programme and tested a bomb in August 1949.","code":"P4-1 — 1 Early tension between East and West: The impact on US-Soviet relations of the atomic bomb","open":false},{"front":"The Soviet satellite states","back":"Communist-dominated governments across Eastern Europe: Poland after the rigged election of January 1947, and Hungary, Romania and Bulgaria in the same years. Czechoslovakia followed by coup in February 1948.","code":"P4-1 — 1 Early tension between East and West: The impact on US-Soviet relations of the atomic bomb","open":false},{"front":"The Truman Doctrine, 12 March 1947","back":"Truman asked Congress for 400 million dollars for Greece and Turkey after Britain withdrew support, promising to help free peoples resisting subjugation. This was containment, and it committed the USA to Europe.","code":"P4-1 — 2 The development of the Cold War: The Truman Doctrine and the Marshall Plan, 1947","open":false},{"front":"The Marshall Plan, 1947 to 1952","back":"Aid offered by Marshall on 5 June 1947 and voted by Congress in April 1948: about 13 billion dollars to sixteen West European countries. Molotov walked out in July 1947 and the satellites were forbidden to apply.","code":"P4-1 — 2 The development of the Cold War: The Truman Doctrine and the Marshall Plan, 1947","open":false},{"front":"Cominform, Comecon and NATO","back":"Cominform, September 1947, disciplined Europe's communist parties and barred Marshall Aid. Comecon, January 1949, tied the satellite economies to Moscow. NATO, 4 April 1949, bound twelve states to treat an attack on one as an attack on all.","code":"P4-1 — 2 The development of the Cold War: The significance of Cominform, Comecon and NATO","open":false},{"front":"The Berlin Crisis, 1948 to 1949","back":"The Deutsche Mark entered the western zones on 20 June 1948; Stalin closed the routes on 24 June. The airlift carried about 2.3 million tons, ending on 30 September 1949, months after the blockade was lifted on 12 May 1949.","code":"P4-1 — 2 The development of the Cold War: Berlin","open":false},{"front":"The arms race and the Warsaw Pact","back":"Soviet atomic test 1949; American hydrogen bomb 1952 and Soviet 1953; Soviet missile test and Sputnik in 1957. West Germany joined NATO in May 1955 and the Warsaw Pact was signed on 14 May 1955.","code":"P4-1 — 3 The Cold War intensifies: The significance of the arms race","open":false},{"front":"The Hungarian Uprising, October to November 1956","back":"After Khrushchev's secret speech and the fall of Rakosi, Budapest rose on 23 October. Nagy took office on 24 October and declared neutrality on 1 November; Soviet tanks returned on 4 November and about three thousand Hungarians died.","code":"P4-1 — 3 The Cold War intensifies: Events in 1956 leading to the Hungarian Uprising","open":false},{"front":"The world's reaction to the invasion of Hungary","back":"The United Nations General Assembly demanded withdrawal but the Soviet veto blocked the Security Council. Eisenhower sent no troops, Suez split the West, about 200,000 Hungarians fled, and Kadar was installed.","code":"P4-1 — 3 The Cold War intensifies: The international reaction to the Soviet invasion of Hungary","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Paper 2 booklet P Question 2, narrative account, 8 marks)","items":["Question: 'Write a narrative account analysing the division of Germany in the years 1945-47.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the four zones of occupation (1945); the creation of Bizonia (January 1947). You must also use information of your own. This is the middle stretch of the account, carrying the story from the reparations quarrel of 1946 to the failure of the Moscow talks in the spring of 1947.","Because reparations had been left to each power to take from its own zone, the four zones were being run as four economies within a year of the war ending, and the quarrel that followed was about who would pay for the result. The Soviet zone was stripped of factories and rolling stock while the industrial west could not feed itself, so the Americans and the British were buying food for a population whose machinery was being carried east; in May 1946 General Clay stopped the deliveries of reparations from the American zone. Once Washington had decided that it would not pay for Soviet reparations, the logic ran on: at Stuttgart in September 1946 Byrnes told a German audience that the USA would help them rebuild and would keep its troops in Germany, and on 1 January 1947 the American and British zones were joined as Bizonia to be run as one economy. That is why the meeting of the foreign ministers in Moscow, from 10 March to 24 April 1947, could not agree a peace treaty for Germany: the western powers now had a working half-Germany to defend, and were being asked to trade it for a united Germany in which the USSR would have a voice and a claim on reparations. The conference broke up with nothing settled, and a division that had begun in 1945 as an administrative convenience had become the policy of both sides.","Why this is Level 3: the material is organised as a clear sequence leading to an outcome and the linkage between the events is analysed, not merely stated - the reparations stop is explained as the reason for Bizonia, and Bizonia as the reason the Moscow meeting failed. The knowledge is precise (the zone-by-zone reparations rule, Clay's stop in May 1946, Byrnes at Stuttgart, 1 January 1947, the dates of the Moscow council) and it addresses far more than three aspects of content, so the Level 3 cap does not apply."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same, 'Write a narrative account analysing the division of Germany in the years 1945-47.' (8 marks), with the same two bullets.","At the end of the war Germany was divided into four zones run by the USA, Britain, France and the USSR, and Berlin was divided in the same way even though it lay inside the Soviet zone. Each power took reparations from its own zone. The Soviets took a lot of machinery out of their zone and the Americans did not like this. After this relations got worse and worse. In January 1947 the Americans and the British joined their zones together to make Bizonia so that they could run them as one economy. The foreign ministers met in Moscow in 1947 but they could not agree about Germany. After that each side just went its own way in its part of the country.","Why this is only Level 2, and what it misses: the events are in the right order and the information is accurate, so it meets 'some organisation of material into a sequence of events leading to an outcome' and 'accurate and relevant information is added'. It misses the Level 3 requirement that 'the account of events analyses the linkage between them': 'relations got worse and worse' asserts a link instead of explaining one, and nothing tells the reader why the quarrel over reparations produced Bizonia, or why Bizonia made agreement at Moscow impossible. The detail is also loose where the top level wants precision - the reparations stop of May 1946 and Byrnes's Stuttgart speech are missing and the Moscow meeting is undated - and the closing sentence summarises rather than saying what the sequence amounted to."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Grand Alliance and the wartime conferences","items":["The Grand Alliance: the USA, the USSR and Britain. The USSR joined the Allies after the German invasion of June 1941; the USA entered the war after Pearl Harbor in December 1941.","Tehran, 28 November to 1 December 1943: Stalin, Roosevelt, Churchill.","Tehran agreed: a second front in France in May 1944; Soviet entry into the war against Japan once Germany was beaten; a postwar international organisation in principle; Poland's frontiers to move westwards.","Yalta, 4 to 11 February 1945, in the Crimea: Stalin, Roosevelt, Churchill.","Yalta agreed: Germany and Berlin divided into four zones, France taking a zone from the British and American areas; Nazi leaders to be tried; the United Nations to be founded, with its first conference at San Francisco in April 1945; the USSR to fight Japan within three months of Germany's surrender; free elections in liberated Europe under the Declaration on Liberated Europe; Poland's eastern border on the Curzon Line with land from Germany in compensation.","Yalta left open: the amount of reparations, referred to a commission, and the composition of Poland's government.","Potsdam, 17 July to 2 August 1945, outside Berlin: Stalin, Truman, and Churchill, who left the conference on 25 July for the British general election result and resigned on 26 July when Labour won; Clement Attlee, prime minister from 26 July, took Britain's place at the table when the conference resumed on 28 July.","Potsdam agreed: the four zones confirmed; Nazi Party banned and leading Nazis to be tried; Germany to be demilitarised; reparations taken by each power from its own zone, with the USSR also to receive 10 per cent of the industrial equipment removed from the western zones without payment and a further 15 per cent in exchange for food and raw materials; Poland's western border on the Oder and Neisse rivers pending a peace treaty; German populations to be transferred from Poland, Czechoslovakia and Hungary.","Potsdam disagreements: the size of Soviet reparations, the communist-dominated government in Poland, and Truman's new confidence after the atomic test.","Deaths shaping the Soviet position: over twenty million Soviet citizens died in the war, and Germany had invaded through Poland twice in Stalin's lifetime."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Stalin, Truman and Churchill, the atomic bomb and the telegrams","items":["Stalin: leader of the USSR from the late 1920s; wanted friendly governments between Germany and the Soviet border, reparations from Germany, and no revived German power.","Roosevelt died on 12 April 1945; Harry Truman became president with no experience of the wartime negotiations and a settled distrust of Stalin.","Churchill: prime minister until he resigned on 26 July 1945, when Labour's election victory brought in Clement Attlee; Churchill had left Potsdam on 25 July to await the result and Attlee returned to the conference as prime minister on 28 July; Churchill had pressed for a firm line on Eastern Europe throughout.","The first atomic test: Alamogordo, New Mexico, 16 July 1945, during the Potsdam Conference.","Hiroshima, 6 August 1945; Nagasaki, 9 August 1945; Japan surrendered in August and signed the formal surrender on 2 September 1945.","Truman told Stalin at Potsdam only that the USA had a powerful new weapon; Stalin already knew through Soviet agents and ordered his own programme accelerated.","The USSR tested its first atomic bomb in August 1949, ending the American monopoly after four years.","The Long Telegram: sent by George Kennan, the American charge d'affaires in Moscow, in February 1946 - the USSR saw itself in permanent conflict with capitalism, was building its forces, but would retreat before firmness.","The Novikov Telegram: sent by Nikolai Novikov, Soviet ambassador in Washington, in September 1946 - the USA was seeking world supremacy and building bases for a future war.","Churchill's speech at Westminster College, Fulton, Missouri, 5 March 1946, with Truman present: an iron curtain had descended across Europe.","Satellite states: Poland after the rigged election of January 1947; Hungary, where Rakosi's communists came to dominate the government in 1947; Romania and Bulgaria in 1946 and 1947; Czechoslovakia by the coup of February 1948; the German Democratic Republic in October 1949. Yugoslavia under Tito became communist independently and was expelled from Cominform in 1948."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Truman Doctrine, the Marshall Plan, Cominform, Comecon and NATO","items":["February 1947: Britain told Washington it could no longer support the Greek government in its civil war, or Turkey.","12 March 1947: Truman's speech to Congress, asking for 400 million dollars for Greece and Turkey and promising support for free peoples resisting attempted subjugation. This is containment.","The Greek government defeated the communist forces in the civil war by 1949, with American money, weapons and advisers.","5 June 1947: Marshall's speech at Harvard offering American aid to rebuild Europe, on the argument that hunger and ruin were what communism fed on.","July 1947: Molotov left the Paris conference on Marshall Aid; the USSR forbade Poland and Czechoslovakia to take part.","April 1948: Congress passed the act funding the European Recovery Programme; about 13 billion dollars went to sixteen West European countries over four years.","September 1947: Cominform, the Communist Information Bureau, founded to direct Europe's communist parties, enforce rejection of Marshall Aid and remove disobedient leaders. Yugoslavia was expelled in 1948.","January 1949: Comecon, the Council for Mutual Economic Assistance, created to tie the satellite economies to the USSR; it moved far less money than Marshall Aid.","4 April 1949: the North Atlantic Treaty signed in Washington by twelve states, agreeing that an attack on one would be treated as an attack on all; American forces remained in Europe in peacetime.","February 1948: the communist coup in Czechoslovakia removed the last mixed government in Eastern Europe and strengthened western support for both Marshall Aid and a western alliance."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Berlin, the blockade and airlift, and the two German states","items":["Berlin lay about 160 kilometres inside the Soviet zone and was itself divided into four sectors, so the western powers governed an island inside Soviet-held territory.","1 January 1947: the American and British zones merged into Bizonia to run as one economy; the French zone joined in 1948.","20 June 1948: the Deutsche Mark introduced into the western zones of Germany; extended to the western sectors of Berlin on 23 June.","24 June 1948: Stalin closed the road, rail and canal routes from the western zones into West Berlin, leaving about two million West Berliners dependent on stockpiles.","26 June 1948: the airlift began, the Americans calling it Operation Vittles and the British Operation Plainfare; at the peak in the spring of 1949 aircraft were landing every few minutes.","About 1.6 million tons of supplies, chiefly food and coal, had been flown in by the time the blockade was lifted on 12 May 1949; the airlift continued until 30 September 1949 and carried about 2.3 million tons in all.","12 May 1949: Stalin lifted the blockade, having no way of stopping the airlift short of shooting down transport aircraft.","Outcome: the Federal Republic of Germany founded in the western zones in May 1949, with its capital at Bonn, and the German Democratic Republic in the Soviet zone on 7 October 1949.","The North Atlantic Treaty was signed in April 1949, while the airlift was still flying.","Berlin remained divided and inside the German Democratic Republic, which is why it returned as a crisis point later in the Cold War."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the arms race, the Warsaw Pact and Hungary in 1956","items":["August 1949: the first Soviet atomic test. November 1952: the first American hydrogen bomb test. 1953: the first Soviet hydrogen bomb.","August 1957: the USSR tested an intercontinental ballistic missile. 4 October 1957: Sputnik, the first artificial satellite, launched by the USSR; the USA answered with Explorer in January 1958.","May 1955: West Germany admitted to NATO. 14 May 1955: the Warsaw Pact signed by the USSR, Poland, East Germany, Czechoslovakia, Hungary, Romania, Bulgaria and Albania, placing their forces under Soviet command.","Matyas Rakosi ruled Hungary from the late 1940s through the AVH secret police, with falling living standards and heavy Soviet economic demands.","February 1956: Khrushchev's secret speech to the Twentieth Party Congress denounced Stalin's crimes and the cult around him.","June 1956: riots at Poznan in Poland. October 1956: Gomulka came to power in Poland with Soviet acceptance.","July 1956: Rakosi removed and replaced by Erno Gero, who changed nothing.","23 October 1956: a student demonstration in Budapest became a rising; the statue of Stalin was pulled down.","24 October 1956: Imre Nagy became prime minister. By 30 October Soviet troops had withdrawn from Budapest.","30 October 1956: Nagy announced an end to one-party rule. 1 November 1956: he declared that Hungary would be neutral and would leave the Warsaw Pact, and appealed to the United Nations.","4 November 1956: about a thousand Soviet tanks entered Budapest; fighting lasted about a week and some three thousand Hungarians were killed.","Aftermath: Janos Kadar installed as leader; about 200,000 Hungarians fled abroad, chiefly through Austria; Nagy was seized after a promise of safe conduct, tried in secret and executed in 1958.","International reaction: the United Nations General Assembly called for Soviet withdrawal, the Security Council was blocked by the Soviet veto, and a UN committee was later refused entry to Hungary; Eisenhower, days from the election of 6 November, sent no troops; Britain and France were attacking Egypt over Suez in the same fortnight; many Western European communists resigned from their parties."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 2 booklet P Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the formation of the Grand Alliance in the years 1941-43' (8 marks)","items":["The question as printed: 'Write a narrative account analysing the formation of the Grand Alliance in the years 1941-43. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the German invasion of the Soviet Union (June 1941); the attack on Pearl Harbor (December 1941). You must also use information of your own.'","Decode it before writing. 'Narrative account' means a sequence that leads to an outcome, so the account must begin and end somewhere definite; 'analysing' means the join between each episode and the next must be explained, not assumed; and the dates fix the boundaries, so nothing before June 1941 and nothing after Tehran belongs here except in a closing sentence.","The cap that decides the level: no access to Level 3 at all for an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content. Decide first which episodes are your own material and make sure they are the ones you develop.","Episode 1, the alliance forced into being, June to December 1941: Germany invaded the USSR on 22 June 1941; Britain, which had been fighting without a major ally, signed an agreement with Moscow on 12 July 1941 promising mutual help and no separate peace; Japan attacked Pearl Harbor on 7 December 1941 and Germany declared war on the USA on 11 December. What it leaves behind: three powers at war with the same enemy for three different reasons.","Link sentence: because the alliance was made by a common enemy and not by any agreement about what should follow victory, everything the three powers later wanted from each other had to be negotiated from nothing.","Episode 2, the alliance given a form, 1941-42 (own material): the Atlantic Charter of August 1941, which the USSR endorsed the following month; American Lend-Lease supplies extended to the Soviet Union; the Declaration by United Nations signed in Washington on 1 January 1942; and the Anglo-Soviet treaty of 26 May 1942, an alliance meant to last twenty years.","Link sentence: paper commitments could bind the three to fight on, but they could not settle the question Stalin cared about most, which was when the western allies would take German divisions off his front.","Episode 3, the strain over the second front, 1942-43 (own material): Stalin was led to expect a landing in western Europe in 1942 and was told in August 1942 that it would not come; the Anglo-American landings went to North Africa in November 1942 and to Italy in 1943 instead, while the Red Army fought at Stalingrad until February 1943.","Link sentence: with the USSR carrying the great weight of the fighting and no second front to show for the promises, Stalin wanted proof of good faith from his allies while they wanted proof that he would not make a separate peace.","Episode 4, the alliance made to work, 1943: Stalin dissolved the Comintern in May 1943, removing the standing argument that Moscow's business was revolution in other countries, and at Tehran from 28 November to 1 December 1943 the three leaders met for the first time and fixed the landing in France for May 1944, Soviet entry against Japan once Germany was beaten, and the westward movement of Poland's frontiers.","The closing sentence must say what the sequence amounted to rather than repeat it: in two and a half years three governments with nothing in common but an enemy had built an alliance that worked, but they had built it on war aims rather than peace aims, which is why the same table at Tehran that fixed the invasion date left the future of Eastern Europe to be settled later.","Timing and shape: about 13 minutes - two to choose four episodes and mark which are your own, ten to write, one to check that every join has a sentence of explanation. Aim for four developed episodes rather than six thin ones, and use the terms an examiner looks for: Grand Alliance, second front, Lend-Lease, separate peace, sphere of influence, Big Three."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:36:45Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.95,"notes":"Both faults from the last pass are cleared: room.questions[10] is now 'the establishment of communist control in Poland in the years 1944-47' with its own bullets (the Lublin Committee, July 1944; the Polish election, January 1947), which matches nothing in the specimen booklet P4 (Afghanistan 1979, the Prague Spring, the Truman Doctrine/Berlin Wall/Helsinki) or the June 2023 P4 paper (détente, US-Soviet relations 1945-47, the Warsaw Pact/Cuba/SDI), and worked example 0's step 1 now sorts the Tehran agreements into timetabled commitments and statements of intention instead of restating the setup, with cue 1 rewritten to match. Every new fact checks out¹: the PKWN renamed a provisional government on 31 December 1944 and recognised by Moscow within days, the Provisional Government of National Unity of 28 June 1945 with Mikołajczyk as a deputy prime minister a week after the Trial of the Sixteen in Moscow (18-21 June 1945), the falsified referendum of 30 June 1946, the election of 19 January 1947 giving the Democratic Bloc an official 80 per cent, and Mikołajczyk's flight on 20 October 1947; the tariff, AO split, Level 3 descriptor and the 'three or more aspects of content' cap are all correct for the 8-mark narrative account, the sol is 226 words with four dated episodes and a closing judgement, and dupscan prints 'no duplication'.  ¹ verified on the web this pass, as were the Tehran, Czechoslovak, Comecon, Yugoslav, Poznań, Gomułka, Bizonia and 1941 facts in the previous one."}}},"P4-2":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"P4-2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 2 gives booklet P three self-contained crises with clean beginnings and ends, which is exactly what the three questions of the booklet feed on. The consequences question wants a single event whose effects can be dated and named, and 1958 to 1970 is full of them: an ultimatum, a failed summit, a wall, a blockade, an invasion. The narrative account wants a short run of years in which one thing visibly caused the next, and Berlin from 1958 to 1961, Cuba from 1959 to 1962 and Czechoslovakia in 1968 each read that way. The importance questions want an event whose effects reach forward for years, and the missile crisis reaches forward to the hotline, three treaties and a change of Soviet leadership. Learn this topic as three stories with a shared shape - pressure, crisis, settlement - and you can answer any of the three question types from the same store of dates and names.","idea":[{"h":"Berlin again: the refugee gap, the ultimatum of 1958 and the summits that failed","code":"P4-2 — 1 Increased tension between East and West: The refugee problem in Berlin","t":"West Berlin was the one gap left in the Iron Curtain. Between the founding of the two German states in 1949 and August 1961 about three million people left East Germany, most of them simply crossing the sector boundary in Berlin, and a high proportion were young, skilled or professional. The loss was an economic wound and a propaganda humiliation, so on 27 November 1958 Khrushchev demanded that the western powers withdraw from Berlin within six months and that it become a demilitarised free city, threatening otherwise to hand his access rights to the East German government. Three attempts to settle it failed in three different ways. Foreign ministers met at Geneva from May to August 1959 and agreed nothing. Khrushchev then toured the USA and talked with Eisenhower at Camp David on 26 and 27 September 1959, and the six-month deadline was quietly dropped. The Paris summit opened on 16 May 1960 and collapsed at once, because an American U-2 reconnaissance aircraft flown by Gary Powers had been shot down over the USSR on 1 May and Eisenhower would not apologise. At Vienna on 3 and 4 June 1961 Kennedy and Khrushchev agreed nothing and the six-month demand was renewed. A top-level answer treats 1958 to 1961 as one unsolved problem, not four separate meetings: each summit failed for its own immediate reason, but all of them failed because neither side could give up Berlin without losing more than it could afford."},{"h":"Cuba changes sides: revolution, embargo and the Bay of Pigs","code":"P4-2 — 1 Increased tension between East and West: The impact of the Cuban Revolution","t":"Batista fled Cuba on 1 January 1959; Fidel Castro entered Havana on 8 January and became prime minister in February. He nationalised American-owned land, sugar mills and, in the summer of 1960, the oil refineries that had refused to process Soviet crude. The USA answered with a trade embargo on 19 October 1960 and broke off diplomatic relations on 3 January 1961, refusing to deal with Castro's government at all. Because sugar was almost the whole Cuban economy and the USA had been its market, Castro needed another buyer, and in February 1960 Anastas Mikoyan signed an agreement in Havana under which the USSR bought Cuban sugar and supplied oil, machinery and credit. On 17 April 1961 about 1,400 Cuban exiles, trained and armed by the CIA, landed at the Bay of Pigs; Kennedy withheld the air support the plan assumed, and the force was beaten within three days, with more than 1,100 men taken prisoner. A top-level answer makes the causal link rather than the list: American pressure did not isolate Cuba, it pushed Cuba towards Moscow, and the failed invasion gave Castro both a reason to ask for Soviet protection and the standing to demand it."},{"h":"Socialism with a human face: why Czechoslovakia tried to reform itself in 1968","code":"P4-2 — 1 Increased tension between East and West: Opposition in Czechoslovakia to Soviet control","t":"By the middle of the 1960s Czechoslovakia was an industrial country whose centrally planned economy had stopped growing, with shortages, censorship and a leadership under Antonin Novotny that nobody defended. On 5 January 1968 Alexander Dubcek replaced Novotny as First Secretary of the Communist Party. In April 1968 his Action Programme promised what he called socialism with a human face: censorship relaxed and then in practice abandoned, travel to the West permitted, other political parties and independent trade unions tolerated, and the secret police restrained. Dubcek insisted throughout that Czechoslovakia would remain a communist state inside the Warsaw Pact and Comecon, and he was a lifelong communist who had been partly educated in the USSR. Once newspapers, radio and television could report what people thought, demands ran ahead of him: in June 1968 a manifesto called the Two Thousand Words, signed by writers and scientists, called for faster change. A top-level answer explains why reforms that were meant to save communism frightened Moscow more than open opposition would have done: a successful reformed communism next door was an argument the other satellites could use."},{"h":"13 August 1961: the night the boundary was sealed","code":"P4-2 — 2 Cold War crises: The construction of the Berlin Wall, 1961","t":"By the summer of 1961 the flow west had become a stampede, because East Germans expected the gap to be closed and wanted to be out before it was. Kennedy had promised in July to defend West Berlin, the western garrison and western access to it, but said nothing about East Berlin, which told Khrushchev where the line was. In the early hours of Sunday 13 August 1961 East German troops and police sealed the sector boundary with barbed wire, posts and roadblocks, tore up streets and cut the S-Bahn and U-Bahn lines that crossed it; concrete blocks and then a proper wall followed within days and weeks. The barrier eventually ran about 155 kilometres around West Berlin, of which roughly 43 kilometres divided the city itself, with a guarded strip behind it. The many crossing points were cut to a handful, and Checkpoint Charlie on Friedrichstrasse became the one foreigners used. A top-level answer says what the Wall was for: not to keep the West out, which it never did, but to keep East Germans in, which it did immediately and for twenty-eight years."},{"h":"Thirteen days: how the Cuban Missile Crisis actually ran","code":"P4-2 — 2 Cold War crises: The events of the Cuban Missile Crisis","t":"After the Bay of Pigs, Khrushchev agreed to place Soviet nuclear missiles on Cuba, which protected his ally, answered the American missiles already in Turkey and closed part of the gap in long-range weapons. An American U-2 photographed launch sites under construction on 14 October 1962 and the pictures were shown to Kennedy on 16 October. He assembled the advisers known as ExComm, who weighed doing nothing, an air strike, a full invasion, a blockade and a diplomatic deal. On 22 October Kennedy told the American public and announced a naval quarantine of Cuba; on 24 October the Soviet ships carrying missile equipment stopped or turned back. On 26 October a letter from Khrushchev offered to remove the missiles in return for an American promise not to invade Cuba; on 27 October a second letter added the removal of American Jupiter missiles from Turkey, and an American U-2 was shot down over Cuba, killing Major Rudolf Anderson. Kennedy answered the first letter and ignored the second in public, while his brother Robert privately told the Soviet ambassador that the Turkish missiles would go later. On 28 October Khrushchev announced that the sites would be dismantled. A top-level answer keeps the thirteen days in order and explains each decision by what the other side had just done."},{"h":"20-21 August 1968 and the doctrine written to justify it","code":"P4-2 — 2 Cold War crises: The Brezhnev Doctrine and the re-establishment of Soviet control in Czechoslovakia","t":"Moscow tried pressure before force: Warsaw Pact manoeuvres were held on Czechoslovak soil, and Brezhnev met Dubcek at Cierna nad Tisou at the end of July 1968 and at Bratislava on 3 August. On the night of 20 to 21 August 1968 Warsaw Pact forces crossed the frontier, about a quarter of a million troops at first and some half a million within weeks, Soviet units joined by Polish, Hungarian and Bulgarian contingents; Romania refused to take part. There was no military resistance, on Dubcek's own instructions, but crowds argued with the crews, turned road signs round and painted out street names, and about a hundred Czechoslovaks were killed. Dubcek was flown to Moscow and made to sign terms accepting the occupation. He was left in office for a few months, then replaced by Gustav Husak in April 1969, and the reforms were reversed in a process called normalisation; the student Jan Palach burned himself to death in Wenceslas Square in January 1969 in protest. The justification came afterwards: an article in Pravda in September 1968 and a speech by Brezhnev in November set out the rule that if a socialist country turned towards capitalism it became the concern of all socialist countries, which could intervene. A top-level answer notices that the doctrine was written after the tanks, to turn one invasion into a standing threat."},{"h":"What the Wall did to the superpowers, and what Kennedy said in 1963","code":"P4-2 — 3 Reaction to crisis: Impact of the construction of the Berlin Wall on US-Soviet relations","t":"Kennedy did not try to pull the wire down. He said privately that a wall was a great deal better than a war, and reinforced instead: a battle group of about 1,500 American soldiers drove the autobahn into West Berlin in the week after the boundary was sealed, where Vice-President Lyndon Johnson and General Lucius Clay met them, and reservists were called up at home. In October 1961 a dispute over whether American officials had to show papers to East German guards brought American and Soviet tanks face to face at Checkpoint Charlie on 27 October, loaded and about a hundred metres apart, for some sixteen hours, until both sides withdrew in turn on 28 October. Nothing was signed, but both governments had shown they would not change Berlin's status by force. On 26 June 1963 Kennedy spoke at the Rudolph Wilde Platz to an enormous crowd and declared that the proudest boast in the free world was to say 'Ich bin ein Berliner'. A top-level answer holds the two halves together: the Wall was a defeat for the West that the West accepted, and accepting it removed the danger of war over Berlin for the rest of the Cold War."},{"h":"After the brink: the hotline and the treaties of 1963 to 1968","code":"P4-2 — 3 Reaction to crisis: The consequences of the Cuban Missile Crisis","t":"Both leaders came out of October 1962 frightened by how nearly delay and misreading had produced a war. The USA and the USSR signed a memorandum in Geneva on 20 June 1963 setting up a direct teleprinter link between Washington and Moscow, the hotline, which was working by 30 August 1963. On 5 August 1963 the USA, the USSR and Britain signed the Limited Test Ban Treaty in Moscow, banning nuclear tests in the atmosphere, in space and under water but not underground; France and China refused to sign. The Outer Space Treaty of 27 January 1967 barred nuclear weapons from orbit and military bases from the Moon, and the Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty, opened for signature on 1 July 1968, committed the nuclear powers not to pass weapons on and the other signatories not to acquire them. There were losers too: the American Jupiter missiles were quietly taken out of Turkey in 1963, Castro was furious at not being consulted, and Khrushchev, blamed for the retreat, was removed in October 1964. A top-level answer distinguishes what changed from what did not: the rules of communication and testing changed, the stockpiles went on growing."},{"h":"Condemnation without action: how the world answered the invasion of 1968","code":"P4-2 — 3 Reaction to crisis: International reaction to Soviet measures in Czechoslovakia","t":"The West protested and did nothing else. A draft resolution at the United Nations condemning the invasion and demanding withdrawal was supported by ten of the fifteen members of the Security Council in August 1968 and vetoed by the USSR. President Johnson denounced the invasion, NATO governments recalled ambassadors and made speeches, and no troops moved and no sanctions were imposed, because Czechoslovakia lay inside the Soviet sphere, the USA was deep in Vietnam, and nobody would fight for a country the Red Army already occupied. Planned superpower business was postponed: the summit and the opening of talks on limiting strategic arms were shelved, and those talks did not begin until 1969. Inside the communist world the damage was greater. Ceausescu's Romania publicly denounced the invasion and would not join it, Yugoslavia condemned it, Albania used it as its reason to leave the Warsaw Pact in September 1968, and the communist parties of Italy and France criticised Moscow openly. A top-level answer measures the reaction by what it cost the USSR: almost nothing in the West, a good deal of authority among communists."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1(a): turning Khrushchev's ultimatum into one explained consequence","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(a): 'Explain one consequence of Khrushchev's Berlin ultimatum of November 1958.' [4 marks]. Question 1 is printed in two parts, each on a separate event and each marked on its own out of 4, so this part needs one consequence analysed and supported, not a story. What you know: about three million people had left East Germany through Berlin since 1949; on 27 November 1958 Khrushchev demanded that the western powers leave Berlin within six months and that it become a demilitarised free city, and threatened to hand Soviet access rights to the East German government; foreign ministers met at Geneva from May to August 1959 without agreeing anything; Khrushchev talked with Eisenhower at Camp David on 26 and 27 September 1959; the Paris summit opened on 16 May 1960 and collapsed; Kennedy and Khrushchev met at Vienna on 3 and 4 June 1961.","steps":["Separate the demand from its effects: the ultimatum itself changed nothing on the ground, so the consequence you want is something that happened because the demand had been made.","Pick the change of state rather than the next event: before the ultimatum Berlin was an awkward leftover of the occupation, and after it Berlin was the fixed subject of every meeting between the two sides.","Support it with the precise information: ministers at Geneva, the talks at Camp David, the Paris meeting and the Vienna meeting were all called or dominated by the German question.","Make the analytical link the grid rewards: because the demand carried a deadline and a threat to hand the access routes to a government the West did not recognise, the western powers could not ignore it and could not concede it, so the only move left was to keep meeting.","Check the answer against the command word by cutting any sentence that says what happened next rather than what followed from the ultimatum."],"cues":["Did the ultimatum by itself alter anything in Berlin?","What could be said about Berlin afterwards that could not be said before?","Which meetings prove that the change actually happened?","Why could the West neither accept the demand nor let it drop?","How can you tell a describing sentence from an explaining one in your own writing?"]},{"title":"Question 1(a): one consequence of the Soviet trade agreement of February 1960","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(a): 'Explain one consequence of the trade agreement signed between the Soviet Union and Cuba in February 1960.' [4 marks]. Question 1 is printed in two parts, each on a separate event and each marked on its own out of 4, so this part needs one consequence analysed and supported, not a story. The material you have: Castro had nationalised American-owned land, sugar mills and utilities, offering compensation on Cuban terms that the USA refused; in February 1960 Anastas Mikoyan, the Soviet deputy prime minister, came to Havana and signed a trade agreement under which the USSR would buy Cuban sugar and supply oil, machinery and credit of about a hundred million dollars; the refineries in Cuba were American-owned, and in the summer of 1960 they refused to process the Soviet crude oil that had begun to arrive and were seized by the Cuban government; on 19 October 1960 the USA imposed a trade embargo on Cuba; on 3 January 1961 the USA broke off diplomatic relations; Cuba lies about ninety miles from Florida.","steps":["Name the consequence before writing a word of narrative, because the 4 marks are for analysing one consequence: the agreement put Soviet crude oil into a refining industry the Americans owned, and that forced a quarrel neither government could avoid.","Support it from the agreement itself: signed at Havana in February 1960 by Mikoyan, it committed the USSR to buy Cuban sugar and to supply oil, machinery and credit of about a hundred million dollars, so the oil was not a gesture but part of what Cuba was being paid in, and it had to be refined somewhere.","Explain the link with a feature of the period rather than asserting it: the refineries were American property in a country that had just nationalised American property on its own terms, so processing Soviet crude would have meant working for the government that had taken their neighbours' assets - which is why they refused in the summer of 1960 and why the Cuban government seized them.","Show the consequence landing, still inside the question: the seizure turned a company decision into state policy, and Washington answered with the trade embargo of 19 October 1960 and then, on 3 January 1961, by breaking off relations altogether, so a trade agreement had cut the economic tie between Cuba and a country ninety miles away.","Check the answer does what 1(a) asks: one consequence, analysed and supported by two or three precise details, with no sentence that merely retells the revolution and nothing that runs on into the Bay of Pigs."],"cues":["Which single consequence of the agreement are you going to analyse?","Which details of the agreement itself will you use to support it?","Why could an American-owned refinery in Cuba not simply process the Soviet crude?","What did Washington do once the refineries had been seized?","Which sentences in your draft belong to a different question?"]},{"title":"Question 1(b): one consequence of the failed talks at Cierna nad Tisou and Bratislava","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(b): 'Explain one consequence of the failure of the talks at Cierna nad Tisou and Bratislava in July and August 1968.' [4 marks]. Question 1 is printed in two parts, each on a separate event and each marked on its own out of 4, so this part needs one consequence analysed and supported, not a story. What you know: Moscow tried pressure before force, holding Warsaw Pact manoeuvres on Czechoslovak soil in the summer of 1968; Brezhnev and the Soviet leadership met Dubcek's delegation at Cierna nad Tisou from 29 July 1968, in the railwaymen's cultural house opposite the station in that frontier town, and pressed for reformers to be removed and censorship restored; Dubcek gave ground in words while insisting that Czechoslovakia would stay in the Warsaw Pact and Comecon, and the reforms went on; on 3 August 1968 the Soviet, Polish, East German, Hungarian, Bulgarian and Czechoslovak leaderships met at Bratislava and signed a declaration of loyalty to Marxism-Leninism; the Czechoslovak leadership read the two meetings as a settlement, although its own ambassadors in Berlin, Warsaw and Budapest were reporting a military build-up in the bloc; Warsaw Pact forces crossed the frontier on the night of 20 to 21 August 1968, about a quarter of a million troops at first, and there was no military resistance, on Dubcek's own instructions.","steps":["State the one consequence before writing anything else, because the 4 marks are for analysing a consequence and not for retelling the summer: the talks left the Czechoslovak leadership believing the quarrel had been settled, so it made no preparation for an invasion.","Anchor that in the material the question names: at Cierna nad Tisou from 29 July and at Bratislava on 3 August 1968 Dubcek conceded language rather than policy, and the declaration of 3 August was signed as though the dispute were closed while the reforms carried on.","Add the detail that shows the belief was really held: the leadership discounted the reports of a bloc military build-up coming from its own ambassadors in Berlin, Warsaw and Budapest, and changed nothing in the three weeks that followed.","Explain the link using a feature of the period rather than asserting it: the Warsaw Pact was formally an alliance of sovereign states, so a signed declaration of loyalty to Marxism-Leninism looked like the price of being left alone - which is exactly what made the meetings so dangerous to trust.","Show the consequence landing: when the troops crossed on the night of 20 to 21 August 1968 there was no plan to fight, Dubcek ordered no military resistance, and the country was occupied in a day, so what the talks had bought was not a settlement but the surprise.","Check the answer against the 4 marks before moving on: one consequence, analysed, supported by two or three precise details, and every sentence that only narrates the meetings deleted."],"cues":["Before any detail, which single consequence are you going to analyse?","Which dated details from the two meetings show that the leadership thought the dispute was closed?","What evidence is there that the belief was held against warnings?","Which feature of the Warsaw Pact made a signed declaration look like a guarantee?","What did that belief cost when the troops crossed on 20 to 21 August?","Which sentences in your draft only tell the story, and what should happen to them?"]},{"title":"Question 3: the importance of the invasion of 1968 for the communist world","setup":"Booklet P, Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. The bullet here is: 'The importance of the Warsaw Pact invasion of Czechoslovakia (1968) for relations within the communist world.' (8 marks). No stimulus points are printed. What you know: Warsaw Pact forces invaded on the night of 20 to 21 August 1968; Soviet units were joined by Polish, Hungarian and Bulgarian contingents and Romania refused to take part; Dubcek was taken to Moscow and made to accept Soviet terms, and Gustav Husak replaced him in April 1969; an article in Pravda in September 1968 and a speech by Brezhnev in November set out the rule that a socialist country turning towards capitalism was the concern of all socialist countries; Albania left the Warsaw Pact in September 1968; Yugoslavia and Romania condemned the invasion; the communist parties of Italy and France criticised Moscow openly.","steps":["Fix what the bullet is asking about: not what happened to Czechoslovakia, but what the invasion did to the relations between communist states and parties.","Take the first strand forward from the invasion: obedience inside the bloc was secured, since no satellite attempted reform on this scale again and the doctrine written that autumn told them all why.","Take the second strand forward in the opposite direction: authority outside the bloc was spent, because Romania refused to join, Albania walked out of the alliance and western communist parties had to condemn their own model in public.","Weigh the two strands, which is what importance means: the USSR bought control of its neighbours at the price of the argument that communism was chosen rather than imposed.","Finish with a judgement in the language of the question, naming the criterion you have judged by."],"cues":["Whose relations does this bullet actually ask about?","What could Moscow rely on after August that had looked uncertain before?","Which communists refused to go along with it, and how far did each go?","How do you turn two opposite effects into a judgement rather than a list?","What single sentence would make your criterion clear to the examiner?"]},{"title":"Question 3: the importance of the Cuban Missile Crisis for relations between Cuba and the USSR","setup":"Booklet P, Question 3, this bullet: 'The importance of the Cuban Missile Crisis for relations between Cuba and the USSR.' (8 marks). No stimulus points are printed. Your material: the missiles were sent to Cuba in 1962 after Castro had asked Moscow for protection against another American invasion; Khrushchev settled with Kennedy and announced on 28 October 1962 that the sites would be dismantled, without consulting Castro, who learned of the decision from the news; the American undertaking not to invade Cuba was the gain Castro was left with, but he refused the on-site inspection the settlement assumed, so the promise was never formally completed; the secret undertaking about the American missiles in Turkey was not shared with him; Mikoyan was sent to Havana in November 1962 to argue the Cubans into accepting the withdrawal; Cuba remained a Soviet ally, dependent on Soviet oil, arms and purchases of sugar; Khrushchev was removed from power in October 1964.","steps":["Fix what the bullet is asking about: not who won the crisis, but what thirteen days did to the alliance between Havana and Moscow.","Take the first strand: security was what Cuba gained, since the price of the withdrawal was an American promise not to invade, and no second invasion came.","Take the second strand in the opposite direction: Cuba's standing as a partner was exposed, because the bargain was struck over Castro's head and he heard the outcome as the rest of the world did.","Weigh the two strands, which is what importance means: Cuba kept the protection but learned that it had been a counter in somebody else's quarrel, so it stayed a client rather than an ally of equal weight.","Finish with a judgement in the language of the question, naming the criterion you have judged by and conceding what that criterion leaves out."],"cues":["Whose relationship does this bullet actually ask about?","What did Cuba have after the settlement that it had not had before?","How did Castro learn what had been agreed, and what does that tell you about his place in the bargain?","How do you turn a gain and a humiliation into a judgement rather than a list?","Which criterion have you judged by, and what does it leave out?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What did Khrushchev demand on 27 November 1958, and what did he threaten if he did not get it?","a":"That the western powers withdraw from Berlin within six months and that Berlin become a demilitarised free city. If they refused, he threatened to sign a separate treaty with East Germany and hand Soviet control of the access routes to a government the West did not recognise."},{"q":"Why did the Paris summit of May 1960 collapse, and what had happened at Camp David the year before?","a":"The summit opened on 16 May 1960 days after an American U-2 spy plane flown by Gary Powers was shot down over the USSR on 1 May. Khrushchev demanded a public apology, Eisenhower refused, and Khrushchev walked out. At Camp David on 26 and 27 September 1959 Khrushchev and Eisenhower had agreed nothing formal, but the six-month deadline was dropped and a further summit was promised."},{"q":"Put the thirteen days of October 1962 in order, with dates.","a":"14 October: a U-2 photographs missile sites under construction. 16 October: Kennedy is shown the pictures and forms ExComm. 22 October: Kennedy announces a naval blockade in a broadcast. 24 October: Soviet ships turn back. 26 October: Khrushchev offers withdrawal for a promise not to invade Cuba. 27 October: a second letter adds the Jupiter missiles in Turkey, and an American U-2 is shot down over Cuba. 28 October: Khrushchev announces the sites will be dismantled."},{"q":"What was the Brezhnev Doctrine, and when was it set out?","a":"The rule that if a socialist country turned towards capitalism this was the concern of all socialist countries, which were entitled to intervene. It was set out in an article in Pravda in September 1968 and in a speech by Brezhnev in November 1968, after the invasion of Czechoslovakia it was written to justify."}]},"room":{"facts":["Booklet P is worth 32 marks and has three questions: Question 1, printed in two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each 'Explain one consequence of...' for 4 marks and each on a separate event or development; Question 2, 'Write a narrative account analysing...', 8 marks; Question 3, 'Explain two of the following: the importance of ... for ...', two chosen parts at 8 marks each, 16 in all.","Only Question 2 prints stimulus points, as two bullets followed by 'You must also use information of your own.' The cap printed in the mark scheme is: 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' There is no cap at Level 2. Questions 1 and 3 print no bullets and carry no cap.","Each part of Question 1 is marked on its own two-level grid out of 4, AO2 2 and AO1 2: Level 2 needs features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific supporting information. A description of the event, however accurate, stays in Level 1.","Question 3 is marked twice on a three-level grid out of 8, AO2 8 and AO1 8 across the two parts, and Level 3 needs analysis of importance in a coherent, logically structured line of reasoning: what difference the event made to later situations and developments, not what happened at the time.","Timing: Paper 2 lasts 1 hour 50 minutes for 64 marks, about 55 minutes a booklet, so booklet P's 32 marks get roughly a minute a mark with planning on top - about 12 minutes on Question 1's two parts, 13 on Question 2 and 14 on each part of Question 3, leaving a couple of minutes to check. There are no sources and no SPaG marks on Paper 2."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the refugee problem in Berlin in the years before 1961. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Level 2 (3-4) requires features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific support. Credit Khrushchev's ultimatum of 27 November 1958, the weakening of the East German economy through the loss of young and skilled workers, or the decision to seal the sector boundary in August 1961.","sol":"One consequence was Khrushchev's ultimatum of 27 November 1958. About three million people left East Germany between 1949 and 1961, most of them simply crossing the sector boundary in Berlin, and a high proportion were young, skilled or professional. Because the loss stripped the East German state of the workers its economy needed and advertised to the world that its people would leave if allowed, Khrushchev could not let the open boundary stand. He therefore demanded that the western powers quit Berlin within six months and that it become a demilitarised free city, which turned a local haemorrhage into a superpower confrontation.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["A consequence is something that was true afterwards because of the problem, so ask what the loss of people forced the Soviet side to do.","Think about the two kinds of damage an exodus does to a state: one economic, one to its claim to be a better system. Either can be developed.","Write it in three moves: name the consequence, give the precise detail that proves it, then explain why the refugee problem produced it."],"codes":["P4-2 — 1 Increased tension between East and West: The refugee problem in Berlin"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of the U-2 incident of 1 May 1960. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the collapse of the Paris summit on 16 May 1960 and the consequent failure to settle the Berlin question, the humiliation of Eisenhower after Washington's cover story was exposed by the capture of Gary Powers, or the hardening of Khrushchev's position that was carried into the Vienna meeting of 1961.","sol":"One consequence was the collapse of the Paris summit. An American U-2 reconnaissance aircraft flown by Gary Powers was shot down over the USSR on 1 May 1960 and the pilot was taken alive, so Washington's claim that a weather aircraft had strayed off course was exposed as a lie. When the summit opened on 16 May Khrushchev demanded a public apology and a promise that the flights would end; Eisenhower refused and Khrushchev walked out the same day. Because the one meeting at which the Berlin ultimatum might have been settled was destroyed, the German question was left open for a new president to inherit.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what was due to happen a fortnight after the aircraft was shot down.","The damage was done by the capture of the pilot, not by the flight itself: consider what that did to Eisenhower's position at the table.","Name the consequence, date the proof of it, then explain the link back to the incident."],"codes":["P4-2 — 1 Increased tension between East and West: The refugee problem in Berlin"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Cuban Revolution of 1959. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the American refusal to recognise or deal with Castro's government, the trade embargo of October 1960 and the breaking of relations in January 1961, or the trade agreement of February 1960 that turned Cuba into a Soviet trading partner and then a Soviet ally.","sol":"One consequence was that Cuba became a Soviet trading partner and then a Soviet ally. After Batista fled on 1 January 1959 and Castro's forces took over, Castro nationalised American-owned land, sugar mills and refineries, and Washington answered with a trade embargo on 19 October 1960 and by breaking off diplomatic relations on 3 January 1961. Because sugar was almost the whole Cuban economy and the USA had been its market, Castro had to find another buyer, and in February 1960 Mikoyan signed an agreement in Havana under which the USSR bought Cuban sugar and supplied oil, machinery and credit. Within two years an island ninety miles from Florida had exchanged one superpower patron for the other.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The revolution mattered to the Cold War because of what it did to Cuba's trade and alliances, not because of what it did inside Cuba.","Follow the sugar: ask what happens to a one-crop economy when its only customer refuses to buy.","State the consequence, support it with the dated American and Soviet measures, then explain why one produced the other."],"codes":["P4-2 — 1 Increased tension between East and West: The impact of the Cuban Revolution"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of the Bay of Pigs incident of April 1961. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit Castro's appeal to the USSR for protection and the Soviet weapons and advisers that followed, which made the missile deployment of 1962 possible; the strengthening of Castro's position in Cuba; the damage to Kennedy's standing, which Khrushchev carried into the Vienna meeting of June 1961; or the covert campaign against Castro that Kennedy authorised in November 1961 and that was known as Operation Mongoose.","sol":"One consequence was that Castro turned openly to the USSR for protection. About 1,400 Cuban exiles armed and trained by the CIA landed at the Bay of Pigs on 17 April 1961; Kennedy withheld the air support the plan assumed, and within three days the force was beaten and more than 1,100 men were prisoners. Because the landing proved both that the USA would use force to remove him and that Cuba could not survive an American attack alone, Castro asked Moscow for weapons and advisers, and Soviet military aid grew through 1961 and 1962. That appeal for protection is what made Khrushchev's offer of nuclear missiles the following year possible.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["A failed invasion changes what the survivor believes about his own safety, so start with Castro rather than with Kennedy.","Ask who Castro could turn to once it was clear the USA would try again, and what he would have to offer in return.","Name the consequence, support it with the numbers and the outcome of the landing, then explain the chain forward."],"codes":["P4-2 — 1 Increased tension between East and West: The impact of the Cuban Revolution"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Two Thousand Words manifesto of June 1968. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the alarm in Moscow and in the other Warsaw Pact capitals at evidence that Dubcek was losing control of the reform movement, the public rejection of the manifesto by the Czechoslovak party leadership, the pressure applied face to face at Cierna nad Tisou at the end of July and at Bratislava on 3 August 1968, or the hardening of the case for intervention.","sol":"One consequence was that the Soviet leadership concluded that Dubcek no longer controlled the changes he had begun. The manifesto was published on 27 June 1968 over the names of writers, scientists and other well-known figures, and it called on citizens not to wait for the party but to press for change themselves, by criticism and peaceful protest and by removing officials who obstructed it. Because Dubcek's whole case in Moscow was that reform would stay inside the party's control, an appeal made over the party's head was the evidence his critics needed: the leadership disowned the manifesto at once, and within weeks Soviet pressure moved from letters to the meetings at Cierna nad Tisou and Bratislava.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The manifesto changed no law and removed no minister, so look for the consequence in what other people concluded from it.","Ask who was watching Czechoslovakia in the summer of 1968, and how an appeal made directly to citizens would look to them.","Name the consequence, support it with the date, the signatories and what the manifesto asked for, then explain why it mattered more in Moscow than in Prague."],"codes":["P4-2 — 1 Increased tension between East and West: Opposition in Czechoslovakia to Soviet control"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Vienna summit of June 1961. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the confrontation between American and Soviet tanks at Checkpoint Charlie in October 1961. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4 on the consequence grid, so each part needs one consequence analysed with specific support. In 1(a) credit Khrushchev's renewal of the six-month demand and his reading of Kennedy as young and yielding, the surge of East Germans leaving that summer, or Kennedy's July pledge and his call-up of reservists. In 1(b) credit the tacit acceptance that Berlin's status would not be changed by force, the acceptance of the Wall as a fact, or the demonstration that a dispute over access could bring the two armies within a hundred metres of one another.","sol":"1(a) Kennedy and Khrushchev met at Vienna on 3 and 4 June 1961 and agreed nothing, and Khrushchev renewed his demand that the western powers leave Berlin within six months. Because he came away convinced that a president who had just been humiliated at the Bay of Pigs would not fight over Berlin, he became willing to risk a step he had avoided since 1958. East Germans read the same meeting in reverse, concluded that the gap would soon be shut, and left in growing numbers through that summer, so Vienna made both the sealing of the boundary and the panic before it more likely. 1(b) One consequence was that both governments accepted in practice that Berlin's division would not be changed by force. The quarrel began over whether American officials had to show papers to East German guards, and on 27 October 1961 American and Soviet tanks stood loaded about a hundred metres apart at Checkpoint Charlie for some sixteen hours. Because neither side wanted a war over a passport check, they arranged for the tanks to withdraw in turn on 28 October, and from then on the Wall was treated as a fact to be lived with rather than a target to be removed.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate parts on two separate things: a meeting in 1(a) and an armed stand-off in 1(b), so neither answer can carry the other.","For 1(a) ask what each leader believed about the other when he left Vienna, and what each then felt able to do. For 1(b) ask what was settled by the fact that nobody fired.","Each part gets three moves: the consequence named, the precise detail that proves it, and the explanation of why that event produced it."],"codes":["P4-2 — 1 Increased tension between East and West: The refugee problem in Berlin","P4-2 — 3 Reaction to crisis: Impact of the construction of the Berlin Wall on US-Soviet relations"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the building of the Berlin Wall for the government of East Germany. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of Kennedy's visit to West Berlin in June 1963. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the retention of the workforce and the economic recovery of the 1960s, the dependence of the regime on armed guards and the shootings at the barrier, or the propaganda damage of a state that had to fence its own people in. In 1(b) credit the public renewal of the American guarantee to West Berlin and the restoration of West Berliners' confidence, the propaganda victory over the USSR, or the implicit acceptance that the Wall itself would not be challenged.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that the East German government kept its workforce but had to hold it by force. With the boundary sealed from 13 August 1961 the drain of young and skilled people stopped, so the planned economy could at last count on the workers it had and living standards rose through the 1960s. But the state now rested on a guarded barrier: border guards were ordered to stop escapers, and when Peter Fechter was shot climbing it on 17 August 1962 he was left to die within sight of western journalists. Survival had been bought by admitting, in concrete, that the population would leave if it could. 1(b) One consequence was that the American guarantee to West Berlin was renewed in public at the moment it was most doubted. Speaking at the Rudolph Wilde Platz on 26 June 1963 to an enormous crowd, Kennedy declared that the proudest boast in the free world was to say 'Ich bin ein Berliner'. Because West Berliners had watched the barrier go up while western troops stood still, the visit restored their confidence and handed the West a propaganda victory in the city the USSR had tried to squeeze out, while making plain what the USA would defend and, by omission, what it would not.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["1(a) is about a government's position, not about Berliners' feelings; 1(b) is about a speech, so its consequence is something that changed in what people believed.","For 1(a) weigh the gain against what the gain cost in force and reputation. For 1(b) ask what the visit was answering: what had West Berliners watched happen two years earlier?","Each part: consequence, one precise piece of evidence, then the sentence that explains why it followed."],"codes":["P4-2 — 2 Cold War crises: The construction of the Berlin Wall, 1961","P4-2 — 3 Reaction to crisis: Impact of the construction of the Berlin Wall on US-Soviet relations"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the discovery of Soviet missile sites in Cuba in October 1962. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the naval blockade of Cuba announced on 22 October 1962. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the formation of ExComm and the six days of secret deliberation, the rejection of an air strike or invasion in favour of a blockade, or the American decision to make the discovery public on 22 October. In 1(b) credit the turning back of the Soviet ships on 24 October, the shifting of the decision to shoot first onto Khrushchev, or the time the blockade bought for the exchange of letters that ended the crisis.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that Kennedy had to choose a response in secret and at speed. A U-2 photographed launch sites under construction on 14 October 1962 and the pictures reached the president on 16 October; he at once assembled the advisers known as ExComm and kept the discovery from the public for six days. Because an air strike or an invasion would have killed Soviet personnel and invited retaliation against West Berlin, while doing nothing would have left missiles able to reach most American cities, ExComm settled on a naval blockade as the only step that applied pressure without firing first. 1(b) One consequence was that the confrontation became a negotiation rather than a battle. Kennedy announced the quarantine in a broadcast on 22 October 1962 and American warships took station around Cuba; on 24 October the Soviet vessels carrying missile equipment stopped or turned away rather than test the line. Because the blockade put the decision to shoot first on Khrushchev and could be held indefinitely without killing anyone, it bought the days in which the two leaders exchanged letters, and it was those letters, not the warships, that produced the settlement of 28 October.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["1(a) is about what the photographs forced Kennedy to do; 1(b) is about what the ships at sea actually achieved.","For 1(a) list the options ExComm rejected and ask why each was rejected. For 1(b) ask who had to make the next move once the line was drawn.","Each part needs the consequence named, one dated detail, and the explanatory sentence that links them."],"codes":["P4-2 — 2 Cold War crises: The events of the Cuban Missile Crisis"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the agreement that ended the Cuban Missile Crisis on 28 October 1962. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the Limited Test Ban Treaty of August 1963. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the setting up of the hotline in 1963, the American promise not to invade Cuba and the quiet removal of the Jupiter missiles from Turkey, or the blame that contributed to Khrushchev's removal in October 1964. In 1(b) credit the move of testing underground with weapons development continuing, the momentum towards the Outer Space Treaty of 1967 and the Non-Proliferation Treaty of 1968, or the refusal of France and China to sign.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that the two governments built a way of talking to each other directly. During the crisis messages had travelled by coded telegram and public broadcast, and the crucial exchange of 26 and 27 October took hours to arrive and be decoded; on 20 June 1963 the USA and the USSR signed a memorandum in Geneva setting up a teleprinter link between Washington and Moscow, which was working by 30 August. Because both leaders judged that they had come close to war partly through delay and misreading, they treated speed of contact as a safety device, and the hotline became the first practical step of the arms-control decade. 1(b) One consequence was that nuclear testing continued underground rather than stopping. The treaty signed in Moscow on 5 August 1963 by the USA, the USSR and Britain banned tests in the atmosphere, in space and under water but left underground tests untouched, so weapon development went on out of sight while the fallout that had alarmed the public disappeared. Because it removed the most visible danger without limiting a single warhead, it made arms control look achievable and led on to the Outer Space Treaty of 1967 and the Non-Proliferation Treaty of 1968, even as France and China refused to sign and built weapons of their own.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["1(a) asks what the settlement led to, not what its terms were; 1(b) asks what the treaty changed and what it deliberately left alone.","For 1(a) think about what nearly went wrong in the thirteen days that neither leader wanted to repeat. For 1(b) read the list of banned environments and notice which one is missing.","Each part: name the consequence, date the evidence, then explain the link in a sentence that uses the word because."],"codes":["P4-2 — 3 Reaction to crisis: The consequences of the Cuban Missile Crisis"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of Dubcek's Action Programme of April 1968. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the Warsaw Pact invasion of Czechoslovakia in August 1968. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the end of censorship in practice and the public criticism that followed, including the Two Thousand Words manifesto of June 1968, or the alarm in Moscow and the other satellites that led to the invasion. In 1(b) credit the announcement of the Brezhnev Doctrine, the reversal of the reforms under Husak from April 1969, or the loss of Soviet authority among communists shown by Romania, Albania and the western parties.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that criticism of a communist government appeared in public for the first time in twenty years. The Action Programme of April 1968 promised what Dubcek called socialism with a human face: censorship was relaxed and then in practice abandoned, travel to the West was allowed, and other parties and independent unions were tolerated. Because newspapers, radio and television could now report what people actually thought, demands ran ahead of the leadership, and in June a manifesto called the Two Thousand Words urged faster change. That momentum is what persuaded Moscow that Dubcek had lost control of what he had started. 1(b) One consequence was the Brezhnev Doctrine, the principle written to justify what had been done. Warsaw Pact forces crossed the frontier on the night of 20 to 21 August 1968 and met no military resistance, and Dubcek was flown to Moscow and made to accept Soviet terms. Because an invasion of one communist state by others had to be explained to communists everywhere, an article in Pravda that September and a speech by Brezhnev in November set out the rule that a socialist country turning towards capitalism was the concern of all socialist countries, which could intervene. Eastern Europe had been told in writing where reform stopped.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["1(a) is about what happened inside Czechoslovakia once the rules changed; 1(b) is about what the USSR needed after the tanks had arrived.","For 1(a) ask what a free press does to the pace of political demands. For 1(b) ask why an invasion of one communist country by others needed a published principle at all.","Each part: the consequence, one dated piece of support, then the because-sentence that ties them together."],"codes":["P4-2 — 1 Increased tension between East and West: Opposition in Czechoslovakia to Soviet control","P4-2 — 2 Cold War crises: The Brezhnev Doctrine and the re-establishment of Soviet control in Czechoslovakia"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the crisis over Berlin in the years 1958-61. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: Khrushchev's Berlin ultimatum (November 1958); the Vienna summit (June 1961). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 on the narrative grid. Level 3 (6-8) needs a clear sequence leading to an outcome with the linkage between events analysed. 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' Credit the refugee figures, the ultimatum and its deadline, Geneva 1959, Camp David, the U-2 incident and the collapse of the Paris summit, Vienna, the surge of refugees in the summer of 1961 and the sealing of the boundary on 13 August 1961.","sol":"The crisis began with a wound the East German state could not stop: about three million people had left since 1949, most through the open sector boundary in Berlin, and most of them young or skilled. On 27 November 1958 Khrushchev demanded that the western powers quit Berlin within six months and that it become a demilitarised free city, threatening otherwise to hand the access routes to East Germany. Because the demand carried a deadline, the West had to negotiate, and three attempts followed. Foreign ministers at Geneva between May and August 1959 settled nothing, but the talks at Camp David on 26 and 27 September 1959 were friendly enough for the deadline to be dropped. That improvement was destroyed by an accident of espionage: a U-2 flown by Gary Powers was shot down over the USSR on 1 May 1960 and the pilot captured alive, so when the Paris summit opened on 16 May Khrushchev could demand an apology he knew Eisenhower would not give, and walked out. Nothing was therefore settled when Kennedy met Khrushchev at Vienna on 3 and 4 June 1961; the six-month demand was renewed, and Khrushchev left believing the young president would not fight. East Germans drew the same conclusion and fled in thousands a day that summer, which made a decision unavoidable. In the early hours of 13 August 1961 the boundary was sealed with wire and roadblocks. The crisis was not solved by agreement but ended by a wall.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The dates in the question fix where the account starts and stops, so decide first what the situation was in 1958 that made a crisis likely.","Three meetings failed between the two bullets; each failed for a different immediate reason, and those reasons are your analysis.","Build a spine of four dated episodes, write a linking sentence between each pair, and end with a sentence saying what the sequence amounted to rather than repeating it."],"codes":["P4-2 — 1 Increased tension between East and West: The refugee problem in Berlin","P4-2 — 2 Cold War crises: The construction of the Berlin Wall, 1961"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the events of the Cuban Missile Crisis in October 1962. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the U-2 photographs (14 October 1962); the naval blockade (22 October 1962). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 on the narrative grid, with the printed cap: 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' Credit the discovery and the briefing of 16 October, the ExComm options, the broadcast of 22 October, the turning back of the ships on 24 October, the two letters of 26 and 27 October, the shooting down of Major Anderson's U-2, the private assurance about the Jupiter missiles and the announcement of 28 October.","sol":"The crisis opened with a photograph. A U-2 flight on 14 October 1962 brought back pictures of missile launch sites under construction in Cuba, and when they reached Kennedy on 16 October he formed the advisers known as ExComm and said nothing in public for six days. That silence was itself a choice: because an air strike or an invasion would have killed Soviet personnel and invited retaliation against West Berlin, while inaction would have left missiles within range of most American cities, ExComm needed time to find a step that applied pressure without firing first. The answer came on 22 October, when Kennedy told the public and declared a naval quarantine of Cuba. It worked because it shifted the decision to shoot first onto Khrushchev, and on 24 October the Soviet ships carrying missile equipment turned away. What followed was bargaining rather than fighting: a letter on 26 October offered withdrawal in return for an American promise not to invade Cuba, and a second on 27 October raised the price to the American missiles in Turkey. That same day an American U-2 was shot down over Cuba and Major Rudolf Anderson was killed, the moment both sides came nearest to war. Kennedy answered the first letter in public and ignored the second, while Robert Kennedy privately promised the Turkish missiles would follow. On 28 October Khrushchev announced the sites would be dismantled. Thirteen days of unfinished construction had come closer to a nuclear exchange than either leader had intended.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The question names a month, so the account must begin on the day the Americans learned about the sites and end on the day the crisis stopped.","Each move in this story is a reply to the last one: work out what the other side had just done before each decision.","Choose four or five dated episodes, put a sentence of explanation between each pair, and close by saying what the thirteen days amounted to."],"codes":["P4-2 — 2 Cold War crises: The events of the Cuban Missile Crisis"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing relations between Cuba and the USA in the years 1959-61. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the Cuban Revolution (January 1959); the Bay of Pigs incident (April 1961). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 on the narrative grid, with the printed cap: 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' Credit Castro's seizure of power and nationalisation of American property, the Soviet trade agreement of February 1960, the seizure of the refineries, the trade embargo of 19 October 1960, the breaking of diplomatic relations on 3 January 1961, the landing of 17 April 1961 and Castro's turn to Moscow afterwards.","sol":"Relations were poisoned within months of Batista's flight on 1 January 1959, because the government Castro overthrew had been an American ally and much of what he nationalised, from sugar mills to utilities, was American-owned. Washington's refusal to deal with him pushed him to look for another partner, and in February 1960 Mikoyan signed an agreement in Havana under which the USSR bought Cuban sugar and supplied oil, machinery and credit. That agreement made the next quarrel inevitable: the American-owned refineries refused to process Soviet crude, so Cuba seized them in the summer of 1960, and on 19 October 1960 Eisenhower imposed a trade embargo. Each blow drove Cuba further towards Moscow rather than back towards Washington, and the USA broke off diplomatic relations on 3 January 1961. When economic pressure failed, policy turned to force. About 1,400 CIA-trained Cuban exiles landed at the Bay of Pigs on 17 April 1961, but Kennedy withheld the air support the plan assumed, no rising followed, and the force was beaten in three days with more than 1,100 taken prisoner. Castro had already proclaimed the socialist character of the revolution on 16 April 1961, the day before the brigade landed; the defeat then left him stronger at home and convinced him that only Soviet protection would keep the Americans out, so he asked Moscow for weapons and advisers. In little more than two years an American neighbour had become a Soviet ally, which made the missiles of 1962 possible.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The years given start with a change of government and end with an invasion, so the account is the story of how one led to the other.","Watch the pattern: each American measure produces a Cuban answer that draws Moscow further in. That pattern is your analysis.","Pick four dated episodes, write the linking sentence between each pair, and finish with what the two years amounted to rather than a summary."],"codes":["P4-2 — 1 Increased tension between East and West: The impact of the Cuban Revolution"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the Cuban Missile Crisis for attempts at arms control in the years 1963-68. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts, marked on the three-level importance grid; no stimulus points and no cap. Level 3 (6-8) needs analysis of importance in a coherent line of reasoning. Credit the hotline agreed on 20 June 1963, the Limited Test Ban Treaty of 5 August 1963, the Outer Space Treaty of 27 January 1967 and the Non-Proliferation Treaty of 1 July 1968, with a weighed judgement on what these did and did not limit.","sol":"The crisis mattered because it changed what the two governments believed about accident. In October 1962 the decisive letters took hours to arrive and be decoded, and on 27 October an American U-2 was shot down over Cuba without either leader ordering it; both concluded the greatest danger was not a deliberate attack but a misread signal. The first result followed directly: a memorandum signed in Geneva on 20 June 1963 created a teleprinter link between Washington and Moscow, working by 30 August, so that a crisis could be discussed while it was happening. The second result was a run of agreements a summit alone would never have produced. The Limited Test Ban Treaty, signed in Moscow on 5 August 1963 by the USA, the USSR and Britain, banned tests in the atmosphere, in space and under water; the Outer Space Treaty of 27 January 1967 kept nuclear weapons out of orbit; and the Non-Proliferation Treaty, opened for signature on 1 July 1968, bound the nuclear powers not to pass weapons on. What links them is the lesson of 1962: each banned weapons where neither side had yet put any, so each was cheap and cost neither side anything it already held. Judged by the number of warheads, the crisis achieved nothing: both arsenals kept growing, testing moved underground, and France and China refused to sign. Judged by whether the superpowers could negotiate about nuclear weapons at all, it was decisive: it turned arms control from propaganda into ordinary business.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Importance means what difference the crisis made afterwards, so the first question is what the two leaders believed on 29 October that they had not believed on 15 October.","Four measures fall inside the years given; ask what they have in common that made them possible when earlier talks had failed.","Argue both ways before judging: say what these agreements limited and what they conspicuously did not, then name the criterion your judgement uses."],"codes":["P4-2 — 3 Reaction to crisis: The consequences of the Cuban Missile Crisis"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the Prague Spring for Soviet policy towards Eastern Europe. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts on the three-level importance grid; no stimulus points and no cap. Credit Dubcek's appointment on 5 January 1968 and the Action Programme of April, the reasons the reforms alarmed Moscow, the invasion of 20-21 August 1968, normalisation under Husak from April 1969 and the Brezhnev Doctrine as a standing rule for the whole bloc, with a judgement on how far Soviet policy was changed rather than confirmed.","sol":"The Prague Spring mattered because it forced the USSR to state a rule it had previously only acted on. Dubcek became First Secretary on 5 January 1968 and his Action Programme of April promised socialism with a human face: censorship relaxed and then abandoned, travel permitted, other parties and unions tolerated, while Czechoslovakia stayed inside the Warsaw Pact. That combination was more dangerous to Moscow than open revolt, because a reformed communism that worked and stayed loyal was an argument every other satellite could borrow, and Poland and East Germany pressed hardest for it to be stopped. When persuasion failed at Cierna nad Tisou and Bratislava, Warsaw Pact forces invaded on the night of 20 to 21 August 1968. The importance lies in what followed the tanks rather than in the tanks themselves. Dubcek was taken to Moscow and made to accept Soviet terms, Husak replaced him in April 1969 and normalisation undid the reforms one by one; and an article in Pravda in September 1968, repeated by Brezhnev in a speech that November, laid down that a socialist country turning towards capitalism was the concern of all socialist countries, which could intervene. Judged by whether Soviet behaviour changed, the answer is no: Hungary in 1956 had already shown as much. Judged by whether Soviet policy became explicit and therefore a standing deterrent, the Prague Spring was decisive, because after 1968 every reformer in Eastern Europe knew in advance exactly what the limit was and what crossing it would cost.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The bullet asks about Soviet policy, so the reforms themselves are evidence rather than the subject.","Ask why a loyal reformer inside the Warsaw Pact frightened Moscow more than an open rebel would have done.","Take the story forward past August 1968 to what was written down afterwards, then judge by a criterion you state in words."],"codes":["P4-2 — 1 Increased tension between East and West: Opposition in Czechoslovakia to Soviet control","P4-2 — 2 Cold War crises: The Brezhnev Doctrine and the re-establishment of Soviet control in Czechoslovakia"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the international reaction to Soviet measures in Czechoslovakia (1968) for superpower relations. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts on the three-level importance grid; no stimulus points and no cap. Credit the vetoed United Nations resolution of August 1968, western protests unaccompanied by troops or sanctions, the postponement of the summit and of talks on strategic arms until 1969, and the contrast with the reaction inside the communist world, with a judgement on how far superpower relations were actually altered.","sol":"The reaction mattered chiefly for what it revealed rather than for what it did. A draft resolution at the United Nations condemning the invasion and demanding withdrawal was supported by ten of the fifteen members of the Security Council in August 1968 and vetoed by the USSR, which was the limit of what the West could achieve there. Beyond words, nothing moved: President Johnson denounced the invasion, NATO governments protested, and no troops were sent and no sanctions imposed, because Czechoslovakia lay inside the Soviet sphere, the Red Army was already in occupation, and the USA was committed in Vietnam. The importance for superpower relations is therefore double. In the short term it cost the USSR a delay: the planned summit and the opening of talks on limiting strategic arms were shelved, and those talks did not begin until 1969. In the long term it confirmed the division of Europe that both sides had been operating since 1945, since the West had again accepted that what happened inside the Soviet bloc was not something it would fight about, exactly as in Hungary in 1956. Judged by immediate damage to superpower relations, the reaction was slight and quickly repaired. Judged by what it settled, it was important, because it confirmed that the two spheres were accepted in practice, and that acceptance is what made the detente of the 1970s possible so soon after an invasion.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Separate the reaction inside the communist world from the reaction of the western governments; this bullet names superpower relations.","Ask what the West actually did as well as what it said, and why it did no more.","Weigh a short-term effect against a long-term one, then state the criterion on which you judge importance."],"codes":["P4-2 — 3 Reaction to crisis: International reaction to Soviet measures in Czechoslovakia"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What did Khrushchev demand on 27 November 1958?","a":"That the western powers withdraw from Berlin within six months and that Berlin become a demilitarised free city."},{"q":"Why did the Paris summit collapse in May 1960?","a":"Because an American U-2 spy plane flown by Gary Powers had been shot down over the USSR on 1 May 1960; Khrushchev demanded a public apology, Eisenhower refused, and Khrushchev walked out."},{"q":"On what date did Khrushchev announce that the Soviet missile sites in Cuba would be dismantled?","a":"28 October 1962."},{"q":"Who replaced Antonin Novotny as First Secretary of the Czechoslovak Communist Party, and when?","a":"Alexander Dubcek, on 5 January 1968."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The refugee problem in Berlin, 1949-61","open":false,"code":"P4-2 — 1 Increased tension between East and West: The refugee problem in Berlin","back":"About three million people left East Germany, most across the open sector boundary in Berlin. Many were young, skilled or professional, so the GDR lost workers it could not replace."},{"front":"Khrushchev's Berlin ultimatum, 27 November 1958","open":false,"code":"P4-2 — 1 Increased tension between East and West: The refugee problem in Berlin","back":"Demanded that the western powers withdraw from Berlin within six months and that it become a demilitarised free city, with a threat to hand Soviet access rights to East Germany."},{"front":"The summits of 1959-61","open":false,"code":"P4-2 — 1 Increased tension between East and West: The refugee problem in Berlin","back":"Geneva foreign ministers, May to August 1959: nothing agreed. Camp David, 26-27 September 1959: the deadline dropped. Paris, 16 May 1960: collapsed. Vienna, 3-4 June 1961: the demand renewed."},{"front":"The U-2 incident, 1 May 1960","open":false,"code":"P4-2 — 1 Increased tension between East and West: The refugee problem in Berlin","back":"An American spy plane flown by Gary Powers was shot down over the USSR and the pilot captured alive. Eisenhower refused to apologise, so Khrushchev walked out of the Paris summit."},{"front":"The Cuban Revolution and Fidel Castro","open":false,"code":"P4-2 — 1 Increased tension between East and West: The impact of the Cuban Revolution","back":"Batista fled Cuba on 1 January 1959; Castro entered Havana on 8 January, becoming prime minister in February. He nationalised American-owned property; the USA imposed a trade embargo on 19 October 1960 and broke off relations on 3 January 1961."},{"front":"The Bay of Pigs, 17 April 1961","open":false,"code":"P4-2 — 1 Increased tension between East and West: The impact of the Cuban Revolution","back":"About 1,400 CIA-trained Cuban exiles landed; Kennedy withheld air cover and the force was beaten in three days, more than 1,100 being captured. Castro then asked Moscow for protection."},{"front":"The construction of the Berlin Wall, 13 August 1961","open":false,"code":"P4-2 — 2 Cold War crises: The construction of the Berlin Wall, 1961","back":"East German troops and police sealed the sector boundary overnight with barbed wire and roadblocks, then concrete. The barrier round West Berlin ran about 155 kilometres; crossings were cut to a handful."},{"front":"The tank confrontation at Checkpoint Charlie, October 1961","open":false,"code":"P4-2 — 3 Reaction to crisis: Impact of the construction of the Berlin Wall on US-Soviet relations","back":"A row over American officials' right to enter East Berlin brought American and Soviet tanks face to face on 27 October 1961 for about sixteen hours; both sides withdrew on 28 October."},{"front":"Kennedy's visit to West Berlin, 26 June 1963","open":false,"code":"P4-2 — 3 Reaction to crisis: Impact of the construction of the Berlin Wall on US-Soviet relations","back":"Speaking at the Rudolph Wilde Platz to an enormous crowd, Kennedy declared 'Ich bin ein Berliner', restating the American commitment to West Berlin while leaving the Wall standing."},{"front":"The Cuban Missile Crisis, 16-28 October 1962","open":false,"code":"P4-2 — 2 Cold War crises: The events of the Cuban Missile Crisis","back":"U-2 photographs of 14 October reached Kennedy on 16 October; a blockade was announced on 22 October; Soviet ships turned back on 24 October; on 28 October Khrushchev agreed to dismantle the sites for a promise not to invade Cuba."},{"front":"The Brezhnev Doctrine","open":false,"code":"P4-2 — 2 Cold War crises: The Brezhnev Doctrine and the re-establishment of Soviet control in Czechoslovakia","back":"Set out in an article in Pravda in September 1968 and in a speech by Brezhnev that November: a socialist country turning towards capitalism is the concern of all socialist countries, which may intervene."},{"front":"The hotline, 1963","open":false,"code":"P4-2 — 3 Reaction to crisis: The consequences of the Cuban Missile Crisis","back":"A direct teleprinter link between Washington and Moscow, agreed in Geneva on 20 June 1963 and working by 30 August, so the leaders could exchange messages in minutes rather than hours."},{"front":"Arms control treaties, 1963-68","open":false,"code":"P4-2 — 3 Reaction to crisis: The consequences of the Cuban Missile Crisis","back":"Limited Test Ban Treaty, 5 August 1963: no tests in the atmosphere, in space or under water. Outer Space Treaty, 27 January 1967. Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty, opened for signature 1 July 1968."},{"front":"Alexander Dubcek and the Action Programme","open":false,"code":"P4-2 — 1 Increased tension between East and West: Opposition in Czechoslovakia to Soviet control","back":"Dubcek became First Secretary on 5 January 1968. The Action Programme of April 1968 promised socialism with a human face: censorship relaxed, travel allowed, criticism permitted, but the Warsaw Pact kept."},{"front":"The invasion of Czechoslovakia and the international reaction","open":false,"code":"P4-2 — 3 Reaction to crisis: International reaction to Soviet measures in Czechoslovakia","back":"Warsaw Pact forces invaded on 20-21 August 1968. A UN resolution condemning it was vetoed by the USSR; the West protested but sent no troops. Romania and Yugoslavia condemned it and Albania left the Pact."}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Paper 2 booklet P Question 2, narrative account, 8 marks)","items":["Question: 'Write a narrative account analysing events in Berlin in the years 1961-63. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the sealing of the sector boundary (August 1961); the confrontation at Checkpoint Charlie (October 1961). You must also use information of your own.' This is the middle stretch of the answer, running from the western answer to the sealing to the moment the tanks pulled back at Checkpoint Charlie.","The sealing of 13 August 1961 mattered less for the wire itself than for what the western powers did not do about it: the barrier stood on East German ground and left the access routes to West Berlin untouched, so there was no case for pulling it down, and the guarantee Kennedy had given in July 1961 - the western garrison, western access and the freedom of West Berlin - had just been seen to cover nothing that had actually happened. That is why the answer had to be visible rather than effective. In the week after the sealing an American battle group of about 1,500 men drove the autobahn across East Germany and reached West Berlin on 20 August 1961, where Vice-President Lyndon Johnson and General Lucius Clay met it in front of the crowds: a force far too small to fight for the city and exactly large enough to prove that the road to it would be defended. Having accepted the barrier, the two sides were left quarrelling about who might cross it, and that narrower question is what produced the confrontation of 27 October 1961, when American and Soviet tanks stood loaded about a hundred metres apart at Checkpoint Charlie for some sixteen hours after East German guards tried to check the papers of American officials entering East Berlin. Both withdrew on 28 October without a shot, because the right at issue was one each side could concede without touching the Wall itself - which is why the confrontation ended and the barrier did not.","Why this is Level 3: the material is organised into a clear sequence leading to an outcome, and the linkage between the episodes is analysed rather than asserted - the untouched access routes are explained as the reason the West could not act against the barrier, the battle group as the answer a guarantee needed once it had been seen not to cover the sealing, and the crossing rights as the narrow residue that put tanks a hundred metres apart. The precise detail (13 August 1961, the guarantee of July 1961, about 1,500 men arriving on 20 August, Johnson and Clay, 27 to 28 October 1961, some sixteen hours, about a hundred metres) is accurate and is used to carry the argument rather than to decorate it."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same, 'Write a narrative account analysing events in Berlin in the years 1961-63.' (8 marks), with the same two bullets.","In August 1961 the East Germans closed the boundary in Berlin and built a wall so that people could not escape to the West any more. The Americans were angry about it but they did not knock it down. They sent soldiers into West Berlin to show that they were still going to defend it, and the people there were pleased to see them arrive. After that there was a confrontation at Checkpoint Charlie where American and Soviet tanks faced each other for a while, but in the end both sides pulled back and nobody was killed. Later a young man was shot trying to get over the wall and left to die, and this made the West even more critical of the communists. In June 1963 President Kennedy came to West Berlin and made a famous speech to a huge crowd in which he said he was a Berliner. All of this showed that West Berlin was going to stay western even though the wall stayed up, and Berlin became less dangerous than it had been before 1961.","Why this is only Level 2, and what it misses: the events are in the right order and the information in them is accurate, so it meets 'some organisation of material into a sequence of events leading to an outcome' and 'accurate and relevant information is added'. It misses the Level 3 requirement that the account analyses the linkage between the events: it says the Americans did not knock the wall down but never explains that the barrier stood on East German ground and left western access untouched, which is why a display of force was the only answer available, and it never explains that the tanks met over crossing rights rather than over the wall, which is why both sides could withdraw. The detail is thin too - no date more precise than a month, the size of the force not given, Johnson and Clay not named, and Peter Fechter not named either."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Berlin, the refugee problem and the summits, 1958-61","items":["Between 1949 and August 1961 about three million people left East Germany, the great majority through the open sector boundary in Berlin.","A high proportion of those leaving were young, skilled or professionally qualified, so the loss was economic as well as political.","27 November 1958: Khrushchev's Berlin ultimatum - the western powers to leave Berlin within six months and the city to become a demilitarised free city.","The threat behind it: a separate Soviet peace treaty with East Germany, handing control of the access routes to a government the West did not recognise.","May to August 1959: the foreign ministers' conference at Geneva discussed Germany and Berlin and reached no agreement.","15-27 September 1959: Khrushchev's visit to the USA, including talks with Eisenhower at Camp David on 26 and 27 September; the six-month deadline was dropped and a further summit promised.","1 May 1960: an American U-2 reconnaissance aircraft flown by Gary Powers was shot down over the USSR and the pilot captured alive.","16 May 1960: the Paris summit opened and collapsed immediately when Eisenhower refused Khrushchev's demand for a public apology.","3-4 June 1961: Kennedy and Khrushchev met at Vienna; nothing was agreed and the six-month demand was renewed.","July 1961: Kennedy publicly pledged to defend West Berlin, the western garrison and western access to it - and said nothing about East Berlin."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Berlin Wall and its effects, 1961-63","items":["Early hours of Sunday 13 August 1961: East German troops and police sealed the sector boundary with barbed wire, posts and roadblocks, and cut the S-Bahn and U-Bahn lines that crossed it.","Concrete blocks and then a permanent wall replaced the wire within days and weeks, with a guarded strip behind it.","The barrier around West Berlin eventually ran about 155 kilometres, of which roughly 43 kilometres divided the city itself.","Crossing points were cut to a handful; Checkpoint Charlie on Friedrichstrasse was the one used by foreigners and allied officials.","August 1961: in the week after the boundary was sealed, an American battle group of about 1,500 men drove the autobahn into West Berlin and was met by Vice-President Lyndon Johnson and General Lucius Clay.","Kennedy's private judgement was that a wall was a great deal better than a war; he did not attempt to remove it.","27 October 1961: American and Soviet tanks stood loaded about a hundred metres apart at Checkpoint Charlie for some sixteen hours, after a dispute over the right of American officials to enter East Berlin; both sides withdrew on 28 October.","17 August 1962: Peter Fechter was shot attempting to climb the barrier and left to die within sight of western journalists.","26 June 1963: Kennedy spoke at the Rudolph Wilde Platz to an enormous crowd and declared 'Ich bin ein Berliner'.","Effect: the flow of refugees fell to a trickle, the East German economy stabilised, and Berlin ceased to be a place where war was likely - the Wall stood until 1989."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Cuba from revolution to the Bay of Pigs, 1959-61","items":["1 January 1959: Batista, a ruler the USA had supported, fled Cuba; Castro's forces entered Havana on 8 January and he became prime minister in February 1959.","7 January 1959: the USA recognised the new Cuban government; recognition did not survive the seizure of American property, and relations worsened through 1960.","Castro nationalised American-owned land, sugar mills and utilities; compensation was offered on Cuban terms and refused.","February 1960: Anastas Mikoyan signed a trade agreement in Havana - the USSR to buy Cuban sugar and to supply oil, machinery and credit.","Summer 1960: the American-owned refineries refused to process Soviet crude oil and were seized by the Cuban government.","19 October 1960: the USA imposed a trade embargo on Cuba.","3 January 1961: the USA broke off diplomatic relations with Cuba, two years after recognising it; from then on Washington would not deal with Castro's government at all.","15-16 April 1961: CIA-supplied bombers flown by Cuban exiles struck Cuban airfields on 15 April, and at the funeral for those killed Castro proclaimed the socialist character of the revolution on 16 April - the day before the brigade landed, so not a consequence of its defeat.","17 April 1961: about 1,400 Cuban exiles, trained and armed by the CIA, landed at the Bay of Pigs.","Kennedy withheld the American air support the plan assumed; no popular rising followed; the force was beaten within three days and more than 1,100 men were captured.","After the defeat Castro appealed to Moscow for protection and accepted Soviet weapons and advisers, which is what made the missile deployment of 1962 possible.","Cuba lies about ninety miles from Florida, which is why a Soviet ally there mattered so much to Washington."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Cuban Missile Crisis and what followed, 1962-68","items":["14 October 1962: an American U-2 photographed Soviet missile launch sites under construction in Cuba.","16 October 1962: Kennedy was shown the photographs and formed the group of advisers known as ExComm; the discovery was kept secret for six days.","ExComm's options: do nothing, an air strike, a full invasion, a naval blockade, or a diplomatic deal.","22 October 1962: Kennedy told the American public and announced a naval quarantine of Cuba.","24 October 1962: Soviet ships carrying missile equipment stopped or turned back rather than test the line.","26 October 1962: Khrushchev's first letter offered to remove the missiles in return for an American promise not to invade Cuba.","27 October 1962: a second letter added the removal of American Jupiter missiles from Turkey; an American U-2 was shot down over Cuba and Major Rudolf Anderson was killed.","Kennedy answered the first letter and ignored the second in public, while Robert Kennedy privately assured the Soviet ambassador that the Turkish missiles would be withdrawn later; they were removed in 1963.","28 October 1962: Khrushchev announced that the sites would be dismantled.","20 June 1963: the hotline memorandum signed in Geneva; the teleprinter link was working by 30 August 1963.","5 August 1963: the Limited Test Ban Treaty signed in Moscow by the USA, the USSR and Britain - no tests in the atmosphere, in space or under water, but underground tests allowed; France and China refused to sign.","27 January 1967: the Outer Space Treaty - no nuclear weapons in orbit, no military bases on the Moon. 1 July 1968: the Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty opened for signature.","Other consequences: Castro was furious at not being consulted, and Khrushchev, blamed for the retreat, was removed from power in October 1964."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Czechoslovakia, the Prague Spring and the Brezhnev Doctrine, 1968-69","items":["By the mid-1960s the Czechoslovak planned economy had stopped growing, with shortages and censorship under Antonin Novotny.","5 January 1968: Alexander Dubcek replaced Novotny as First Secretary of the Communist Party.","April 1968: the Action Programme promised 'socialism with a human face' - censorship relaxed and then in practice abandoned, travel to the West allowed, other parties and independent unions tolerated, the secret police restrained.","Dubcek insisted throughout that Czechoslovakia would stay a communist state inside the Warsaw Pact and Comecon.","June 1968: the Two Thousand Words manifesto, signed by writers and scientists, called for faster change than Dubcek wanted.","Late July and 3 August 1968: Soviet pressure at the meetings at Cierna nad Tisou and Bratislava failed to stop the reforms.","Night of 20-21 August 1968: Warsaw Pact forces invaded - about a quarter of a million troops at first and some half a million within weeks; Soviet units were joined by Polish, Hungarian and Bulgarian contingents, and Romania refused to take part.","There was no military resistance, on Dubcek's instructions; crowds argued with the crews and turned road signs round, and about a hundred Czechoslovaks were killed.","Dubcek was flown to Moscow and made to accept Soviet terms; Gustav Husak replaced him in April 1969 and 'normalisation' reversed the reforms.","January 1969: the student Jan Palach burned himself to death in Wenceslas Square in protest.","The Brezhnev Doctrine: set out in an article in Pravda in September 1968 and in a speech by Brezhnev in November - a socialist country turning towards capitalism is the concern of all socialist countries, which may intervene.","International reaction: a UN Security Council resolution condemning the invasion was supported by ten of the fifteen members in August 1968 and vetoed by the USSR; western governments protested but sent no troops and imposed no sanctions.","The planned superpower summit and the opening of talks on limiting strategic arms were shelved; those talks did not begin until 1969.","Inside the communist world: Romania under Ceausescu and Yugoslavia condemned the invasion, Albania left the Warsaw Pact in September 1968, and the communist parties of Italy and France criticised Moscow openly."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan - Paper 2 booklet P Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing events in Berlin in the years 1961-63' (8 marks)","items":["The question as printed: 'Write a narrative account analysing events in Berlin in the years 1961-63. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the sealing of the sector boundary (August 1961); the confrontation at Checkpoint Charlie (October 1961). You must also use information of your own.'","Decode it before writing. 'Narrative account' means a sequence that leads to an outcome, so the account has to end where the years end - with Kennedy speaking in West Berlin in June 1963 - and must not run back into the ultimatum of 1958 or the summits that came before the barrier; 'analysing' means the join between each episode and the next has to be explained rather than assumed; and the dates fix the boundaries, so the refugee figures of the 1950s belong only in the opening sentence, as the situation the sealing brought to an end.","The cap that decides the level: 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' Decide first which episodes are your own material - the American battle group of August 1961, the shooting of Peter Fechter in August 1962 and Kennedy's speech of 26 June 1963 - and make sure those are the ones you develop.","Opening sentence, the situation the sealing ended: about three million people had left East Germany between 1949 and August 1961, the great majority through the open sector boundary in Berlin, so these years begin with a government closing the last gap in its own frontier.","Episode 1, the sealing, 13 August 1961 (printed bullet): in the early hours of Sunday 13 August 1961 East German troops and police sealed the sector boundary with barbed wire, posts and roadblocks and cut the S-Bahn and U-Bahn lines that crossed it; concrete and then a permanent wall followed within days and weeks, the crossings were cut to a handful, and the barrier around West Berlin eventually ran about 155 kilometres, of which roughly 43 divided the city itself.","Link sentence: because the barrier went up on East German ground and left western access to West Berlin untouched, the western powers had no case for pulling it down - which left them holding a guarantee Kennedy had given publicly in July 1961 and had just been seen not to act on.","Episode 2, the western answer, August 1961 (own material): in the week after the sealing an American battle group of about 1,500 men drove the autobahn across East Germany into West Berlin, arriving on 20 August 1961 to be met by Vice-President Lyndon Johnson and General Lucius Clay. The point of it was not to remove anything but to show that the access route and the western garrison would be defended, which is what the guarantee had actually promised.","Link sentence: once both sides had accepted the barrier itself, the only thing left to quarrel over was the narrow question of who might cross it and on whose authority.","Episode 3, Checkpoint Charlie, October 1961 (printed bullet): a dispute over whether East German guards could check the papers of American officials entering East Berlin brought armour to Friedrichstrasse - on 27 October 1961 American and Soviet tanks stood loaded about a hundred metres apart for some sixteen hours, and both sides withdrew on 28 October without a shot being fired.","Link sentence: the withdrawal settled the confrontation but not the barrier, so the next two years measured what the Wall cost the people it enclosed rather than what it cost the governments.","Episode 4, the Wall as a fact of life, 1962-63 (own material): on 17 August 1962 Peter Fechter was shot attempting to climb the barrier and was left to die within sight of western journalists, so the guarded strip behind the wall became the ordinary condition of the divided city; on 26 June 1963, two years after the boundary was sealed, Kennedy came to West Berlin and spoke at the Rudolph Wilde Platz, where an enormous crowd heard him say 'Ich bin ein Berliner'. Narrate the visit and leave what it did to relations for the question that asks about it.","The closing sentence must say what the sequence amounted to rather than repeat it: the barrier the West would not remove was also what removed the danger, because it ended the refugee drain that had made Berlin worth a war, so in two years the likeliest cause of a superpower conflict turned into a grievance both sides could live with - and the Wall stood until 1989.","Timing and shape: about 13 minutes - two to choose four episodes and mark which are your own, ten to write, one to check that every join carries a sentence of explanation. Four developed episodes beat six thin ones. Use the terms an examiner rewards: sector boundary, access routes, garrison, refugee, checkpoint."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:01:45.193694Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.95,"notes":"All three residual problems are fixed and all twenty-four recorded fixes hold. Worked example 1 is now 'Explain one consequence of the trade agreement signed between the Soviet Union and Cuba in February 1960', an event the Cuba fact file teaches and that no bank question, plan or paragraph sets; Dresden is gone from the kit entirely; the Cuba strand now has two worked examples instead of one and Czechoslovakia two instead of three. The plan's Episode 4 narrates Fechter on 17 August 1962 and Kennedy at the Rudolph Wilde Platz on 26 June 1963 and tells the student to leave what the visit did to relations for the question that asks it, so bank question 6's 1(b) is no longer answered in the extras. The Cierna nad Tisou venue is corrected to the railwaymen's cultural house opposite the station. Every fact in the new example checks out on the web - Mikoyan in Havana signing on 13 February 1960, the sugar purchases, the crude oil, the hundred-million-dollar credit, the refineries seized in the summer of 1960, the embargo and the break in relations - as does everything verified in the earlier passes. The dedupe scan reports no duplication, nothing reproduces the specimen's Prague Spring narrative or the June 2023 paper's 1945-47 narrative, all nine key ideas are tested, the sixteen questions sit five, five and six across the difficulties with solutions inside their word ranges, the cards are within the limit, and the grids, tariffs, AO splits, the Level 3 narrative cap and the 2025 two-part Question 1 and 1 hour 50 minutes are exact. Publish it."}}},"P4-3":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"P4-3","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 3 is the only part of P4 in which the Cold War ends, and booklet P examines it from three directions at once. Question 1 likes single agreements and single decisions, because each one changed something definite afterwards: SALT 1, Helsinki, the Carter Doctrine, the INF Treaty, the opening of the Berlin Wall. Question 2 likes a short run of years in which one thing visibly caused the next, and this key topic supplies three of the best in the whole course: 1979 to 1981, 1988 to 1989, and 1990 to 1991. Question 3 likes a single event or person whose effects reached forward for years, and Gorbachev, Reagan, SALT 1 and the Wall are all set that way. The danger in this key topic is that students know the story as a list of summits and dates and cannot say what any of it changed. This room teaches the twenty years as one argument with two turns in it: tension deliberately relaxed in the 1970s, deliberately raised again after 1979, and then dismantled from the Soviet side after 1985 because the USSR could no longer pay for it. It trains the three moves booklet P rewards: explaining a consequence instead of retelling an event, writing a narrative whose episodes are joined by explanation, and judging what difference something made to what came after it.","idea":[{"code":"P4-3 — 1 Attempts to reduce superpower tensions in the 1970s: Détente","h":"Détente: why two enemies chose to relax tension in the 1970s","t":"Détente means a deliberate loosening of tension, and it ran from about 1969 to 1979 without either side giving up its beliefs. Both had reasons of their own. The Cuban Missile Crisis of October 1962 had shown how quickly a local quarrel could reach the point of nuclear war, and by the late 1960s the USSR had caught up in strategic weapons, so neither could expect to survive an exchange. Weapons were also ruinously expensive: the USA was paying for the Vietnam War, from which its last combat troops left in 1973, while the Soviet economy was falling behind the West and wanted American grain and Western technology. Moscow had a third reason, the quarrel with communist China, which made a deal with Washington more attractive after Nixon visited Beijing in February 1972. Europe moved first: Willy Brandt's Ostpolitik produced West German treaties with Moscow and Warsaw in 1970 and the Four Power Agreement on Berlin in September 1971. A top-level answer never treats détente as friendship. It treats it as two rivals managing a rivalry they could not afford to lose control of, which is why it collapsed the moment one of them acted outside the rules."},{"code":"P4-3 — 1 Attempts to reduce superpower tensions in the 1970s: The significance of SALT 1, the Helsinki Accords, and SALT 2","h":"SALT 1, Helsinki and SALT 2: what the agreements actually did, and what they left undone","t":"SALT 1 was signed by Nixon and Brezhnev in Moscow on 26 May 1972. Its Anti-Ballistic Missile Treaty allowed each side only two defensive missile sites, reduced to one by a protocol in 1974, so each population was deliberately left undefended and deterrence rested on the certainty of destruction. Its Interim Agreement froze the numbers of land-based and submarine-launched missile launchers for five years, but said nothing about bombers or about fitting several warheads to one missile, so both sides went on multiplying warheads inside the treaty. The Helsinki Final Act of 1 August 1975 was signed by 35 states. Basket 1 accepted Europe's post-war frontiers as they stood, which was what Brezhnev wanted; Basket 2 opened trade and technology; Basket 3 committed every signatory to human rights. SALT 2, signed by Carter and Brezhnev in Vienna on 18 June 1979, set an equal ceiling of 2,400 strategic launchers each, but the US Senate never ratified it. A top-level answer weighs achievement against limitation: these agreements changed the rules of the arms race without ending it, and Basket 3 handed Soviet and eastern European dissidents a standard they could hold their own governments to."},{"code":"P4-3 — 2 The end of détente and the 'Second Cold War': The significance of the Soviet invasion of Afghanistan","h":"Afghanistan, the Carter Doctrine and the Olympic boycotts: how détente was destroyed","t":"A communist government had taken power in Afghanistan in 1978 and its reforms provoked an Islamic rebellion. Soviet troops entered on 24 and 25 December 1979; the Afghan leader Hafizullah Amin was killed on 27 December and Babrak Karmal installed in his place. Moscow argued that it was helping a neighbouring government at its own request, but Washington read it as the first Soviet invasion outside the Warsaw Pact area since 1945 and as a move towards the oil of the Persian Gulf. Carter's answer came within weeks: in his address to Congress on 23 January 1980 he declared the Gulf a vital American interest to be defended by force if necessary, he stopped grain sales, he withdrew SALT 2 from the Senate, and he led a boycott of the Moscow Olympics in the summer of 1980 that more than sixty countries joined. The USSR and most of its allies stayed away from Los Angeles in 1984 in reply. A top-level answer explains the logic rather than listing the punishments: because détente depended on each side believing the other would stay inside agreed limits, one move outside them destroyed the whole basis of the policy, and both governments then rearmed."},{"code":"P4-3 — 2 The end of détente and the 'Second Cold War': Reagan and the 'Second Cold War'","h":"Reagan and the Second Cold War: rhetoric, rearmament and the Strategic Defence Initiative","t":"Ronald Reagan became president on 20 January 1981 believing that détente had let the USSR gain ground, and that America should negotiate only from strength. Defence spending rose steeply; in March 1983 he called the Soviet Union an evil empire in a speech in Orlando; and later that year the first American Pershing II and cruise missiles arrived in western Europe, answering the Soviet SS-20s deployed from 1976. 1983 was the most dangerous year of the decade: a Soviet fighter shot down a Korean airliner on 1 September, killing all 269 people on board, and in November a NATO command exercise was read in Moscow as possible cover for a real attack. The Strategic Defence Initiative, announced on 23 March 1983 and nicknamed Star Wars, was the sharpest weapon of all: a proposed system of satellites and lasers to shoot down incoming missiles. It was never built, but it threatened to make mutually assured destruction one-sided, and the USSR had neither the computers nor the money to copy it. A top-level answer treats SDI as economic and psychological pressure rather than as technology, and says so."},{"code":"P4-3 — 2 The end of détente and the 'Second Cold War': The significance of Reagan and Gorbachev in reducing tensions","h":"Reagan and Gorbachev: four summits that turned the Second Cold War into arms control","t":"Mikhail Gorbachev became General Secretary on 11 March 1985, the first Soviet leader since the war who was young, confident and willing to bargain. The two men met four times. At Geneva in November 1985 they agreed nothing concrete but issued a joint statement that a nuclear war could not be won and must never be fought, and agreed to meet again. At Reykjavik on 11 and 12 October 1986 they came astonishingly close to eliminating whole categories of nuclear weapons, and the meeting broke down only because Reagan would not confine SDI to laboratory research. Gorbachev then dropped that condition early in 1987, and in Washington on 8 December 1987 they signed the INF Treaty, which scrapped every ground-launched missile with a range between 500 and 5,500 kilometres; 2,692 missiles had been destroyed by June 1991 and inspectors were allowed onto each other's bases. At the Moscow summit in the spring of 1988 Reagan said in Red Square that his evil empire remark belonged to another era. A top-level answer explains why the change happened when it did: Gorbachev needed to cut military spending to save his economy, and Reagan's strength-first position meant a deal on American terms was available."},{"code":"P4-3 — 3 The collapse of Soviet control of Eastern Europe, 1985-91: The impact of Gorbachev's 'new thinking'","h":"New thinking, glasnost and perestroika: Moscow lets go of eastern Europe","t":"Gorbachev inherited an economy growing slowly, an empire that cost money and a war in Afghanistan he decided could not be won; Soviet forces finished leaving on 15 February 1989. His answer at home was perestroika, restructuring, which loosened central planning from 1987, and glasnost, openness, which allowed criticism in the press and honest discussion of the Soviet past, especially after the Chernobyl explosion of April 1986 showed what secrecy cost. His answer abroad was new thinking: security was common, not won at another state's expense, and each country should choose its own path. He said so at the United Nations on 7 December 1988, announcing that Soviet forces would be cut by 500,000 men and that six tank divisions would be withdrawn from East Germany, Czechoslovakia and Hungary and disbanded. That abandoned the Brezhnev Doctrine, the promise that Moscow would use force to keep communist governments in place. In October 1989 the Soviet spokesman Gennadi Gerasimov named the new policy after Frank Sinatra: each country could do it its own way. A top-level answer stresses what was removed rather than what was announced: once the guarantee of Soviet tanks was gone, eastern European governments had to face their own people alone."},{"code":"P4-3 — 3 The collapse of Soviet control of Eastern Europe, 1985-91: The significance of the fall of the Berlin Wall","h":"1989 and the fall of the Berlin Wall: the year the eastern bloc went","t":"Once Moscow would not intervene, the communist governments fell one after another in a single year. In Poland, Round Table talks with Solidarity led to partly free elections on 4 June 1989 that the communists lost heavily, and Tadeusz Mazowiecki became the bloc's first non-communist prime minister in August. Hungary began cutting the fence on its Austrian border in May 1989 and opened it to East Germans in September, so the eastern bloc's outer wall now had a hole in it and tens of thousands of East Germans simply walked out. Huge Monday demonstrations in Leipzig went unpunished, Erich Honecker resigned on 18 October, and on the evening of 9 November 1989 a confused announcement about travel rules brought Berliners to the crossing points in such numbers that the guards opened them. Czechoslovakia's government fell within weeks and Vaclav Havel became president on 29 December; Nicolae Ceausescu was overthrown and executed in Romania in December. A top-level answer explains the Wall's significance as proof rather than as cause: it showed the world, and every other eastern European government, that Soviet power would not be used, which is why the rest followed so fast."},{"code":"P4-3 — 3 The collapse of Soviet control of Eastern Europe, 1985-91: The collapse of the Soviet Union","h":"From the end of the Warsaw Pact to the end of the USSR, 1990-91","t":"The collapse moved from the empire to the state itself. Germany was reunified on 3 October 1990 inside NATO, which few would have thought possible two years earlier, and at Malta in December 1989 Bush and Gorbachev had already spoken of the Cold War as over. Comecon was wound up in June 1991 and the Warsaw Pact, founded in 1955, was formally dissolved in Prague on 1 July 1991, so the military and economic structures of the bloc were gone within twenty months of the Wall opening. Inside the USSR, glasnost had allowed nationalism to be voiced: Lithuania declared independence in March 1990, and Boris Yeltsin was elected president of the Russian republic by popular vote on 12 June 1991. Communist hardliners tried to stop the drift by seizing power on 19 August 1991 while Gorbachev was held at his holiday house in Crimea, but the coup collapsed within three days when troops would not act and Yeltsin defied it publicly. After that Gorbachev had no party and no authority; the republics left, and the Soviet Union ceased to exist on 26 December 1991. A top-level answer links the two collapses: losing eastern Europe destroyed the authority that held the union together."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1(a): one consequence of the failure to ratify SALT 2","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(a): 'Explain one consequence of the American decision not to ratify SALT 2.' [4 marks]. Question 1 is printed as two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each on a separate event or development and each marked on its own out of 4, so this part needs one consequence analysed and supported with specific information. The information you are working from: Carter and Brezhnev signed SALT 2 in Vienna on 18 June 1979; it set an equal ceiling of 2,400 strategic launchers for each side and limited missiles carrying several warheads; Soviet troops entered Afghanistan on 24 and 25 December 1979; in January 1980 Carter withdrew the treaty from the Senate, stopped grain sales to the USSR and called for a boycott of the Moscow Olympics; both governments said they would respect the treaty's limits although it never came into force; Reagan became president in January 1981 and began a large rearmament programme; the next strategic arms treaty, START, was not signed until July 1991.","steps":["Fix what actually changed. The treaty existed on paper but never became law, so its ceiling of 2,400 launchers was a promise either government could abandon whenever it chose, and no inspector could hold them to it.","Pick a consequence that lasted rather than another event in the story: arms limitation by binding treaty stopped for more than a decade, because the next strategic agreement, START, came only in 1991.","Support it with precise detail. Carter did not merely shelve the treaty; he also cut grain sales and led the Olympic boycott, and Reagan's rearmament from 1981 went ahead with nothing binding to restrain its size.","Now make the link the grid calls analysis: because the Senate would not ratify a treaty with the USSR while Soviet troops were in Afghanistan, the habit of limiting weapons by agreement was replaced by the habit of matching them, so the years that followed brought rearmament instead of negotiation."],"cues":["Was SALT 2 ever legally in force, and does that matter?","Which consequence here is a lasting change rather than another episode?","Which details show that this was a policy, not a single gesture?","Why did an unratified treaty make rearmament more likely?"]},{"title":"Question 1(b): one consequence of the Soviet withdrawal from Afghanistan","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(b): 'Explain one consequence of the Soviet withdrawal from Afghanistan.' [4 marks]. This part is marked on its own out of 4 and needs one consequence analysed, with specific support. The information you are working from: Soviet troops entered Afghanistan in December 1979 and fought a rebellion that the USA armed and financed; the war cost the USSR about 15,000 dead and large sums it could not spare; Gorbachev, who took power in March 1985, decided the war could not be won; the Geneva Accords of April 1988 set out a timetable for leaving; the last Soviet troops crossed out of Afghanistan on 15 February 1989; the communist government in Kabul was left to survive on its own; in the same period Gorbachev was telling the United Nations, in December 1988, that Soviet forces in eastern Europe would be cut and that every country should choose its own path.","steps":["Decide what the withdrawal proved rather than what it looked like: Moscow had accepted a defeat in public and had abandoned a communist government it had installed by force.","Choose the consequence that reached furthest: eastern European leaders and populations could now see that Soviet troops would not be used to save a friendly regime in trouble.","Support it with the detail that fixes the timing. The last troops left on 15 February 1989 and Gorbachev had already promised cuts in eastern Europe at the United Nations the previous December, so the two signals arrived together.","Make the analytical link: because communist rule in eastern Europe had rested since 1945 on the belief that Moscow would intervene, removing that belief left those governments facing their own people with nothing behind them, which is why they fell so quickly in the months that followed."],"cues":["What did leaving admit that staying would not?","Who outside Afghanistan was watching, and what did they learn?","Which two dates sit next to each other here?","Why does a lost war abroad weaken governments in other countries?"]},{"title":"Question 2: building the spine of a narrative account, 1985 to 1987","setup":"Booklet P, Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing superpower relations in the years 1985-87.' (8 marks). You may use the following in your answer: the Geneva summit (November 1985); the INF Treaty (1987). You must also use information of your own. The question carries the cap that there is no access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content. The information you are working from: Gorbachev became Soviet leader on 11 March 1985; Reagan had called the USSR an evil empire in March 1983 and had announced the Strategic Defence Initiative that same month; at Geneva in November 1985 the two leaders agreed no treaty but stated jointly that a nuclear war could not be won and must never be fought; the Chernobyl reactor exploded in April 1986; at Reykjavik on 11 and 12 October 1986 they nearly agreed to scrap whole categories of weapons but broke off because Reagan would not confine SDI to the laboratory; early in 1987 Gorbachev dropped his demand that any deal be tied to SDI; the INF Treaty was signed in Washington on 8 December 1987 and abolished all ground-launched missiles with ranges between 500 and 5,500 kilometres.","steps":["Choose a starting point that explains the rest: the account begins with a new Soviet leader who needed to cut military spending facing an American president who would only bargain from strength.","Episode one, Geneva in November 1985: no treaty, but a joint statement about nuclear war and an agreement to meet again, which turned a frozen relationship into a negotiation.","Write the join, not just the next date: because the two men now talked directly, the obstacle became a single issue, and at Reykjavik that issue was SDI, which is why a near-agreement collapsed rather than a general breakdown.","Episode three and the outcome: once Gorbachev separated intermediate-range missiles from SDI, the deal that Reykjavik had almost produced could be signed, and the Washington treaty of December 1987 abolished a whole class of weapons instead of capping it.","Finish with what the sequence amounted to rather than a summary of it: in under three years the two sides moved from insult to inspection, and the change came from the Soviet side because its economy could not carry the competition."],"cues":["Where should this account start, and why there?","What did Geneva change, given that it signed nothing?","Why did Reykjavik fail on one issue rather than many?","What had to be given up before a treaty was possible?","What does the whole sequence add up to?"]},{"title":"Question 3: turning perestroika into an argument about importance","setup":"Booklet P, Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. The bullet here is: 'The importance of perestroika for the Soviet Union.' (8 marks). No stimulus points are printed. The information you are working from: Gorbachev became leader on 11 March 1985 with an economy growing slowly and heavy military spending; perestroika, meaning restructuring, loosened central planning, with a law on state enterprises in 1987 and one permitting small co-operative businesses in 1988; the immediate result was shortages, queues and rising prices rather than growth, because planning was weakened before a market existed to replace it; glasnost allowed the failures to be reported and criticised; the first contested elections for the Congress of People's Deputies were held in March 1989; nationalist movements grew in the Baltic republics and Lithuania declared independence in March 1990; Boris Yeltsin was elected president of the Russian republic on 12 June 1991; the Soviet Union ceased to exist on 26 December 1991.","steps":["Set the criterion before writing a word of narrative: importance here means what difference perestroika made to the Soviet state afterwards, so every paragraph must end in a later situation, not in 1987.","First line forward, the economy: loosening the plan without building a market produced shortages and price rises, so the reform that was meant to prove communism could work made daily life worse and discredited it.","Second line forward, the politics: because glasnost let the failures be discussed and the elections of March 1989 gave critics a platform, dissatisfaction had somewhere to go, and Yeltsin could build a rival power base inside the system.","Third line forward, the union: the republics saw a centre that could neither feed them nor command them, which is why Lithuania's declaration in March 1990 was followed by others rather than crushed.","Judge it. Perestroika was important not because it worked but because it failed in public: it removed the Soviet state's economic excuse and its political monopoly at the same time, and the state did not outlive the reform by long."],"cues":["What must every paragraph of an importance answer end in?","What happens when planning is loosened before a market exists?","Where could the new dissatisfaction be expressed?","Why did the republics grow bolder rather than more careful?","So what is the judgement, in one sentence?"]},{"title":"Question 3: the importance of the Geneva summit (1985) for superpower relations","setup":"Booklet P, Question 3, a bullet at 8 marks: 'The importance of the Geneva summit (1985) for superpower relations.' No stimulus points are printed, and the level descriptors reward analysis of importance in a coherent line of reasoning. The information you are working from: Reagan and Gorbachev met at Geneva on 19, 20 and 21 November 1985; it was the first meeting between an American president and a Soviet leader since Carter and Brezhnev signed SALT 2 in Vienna in June 1979; no arms treaty was signed; the two issued a joint statement that a nuclear war cannot be won and must never be fought and that neither side would seek military superiority; they said they would work towards cutting strategic nuclear weapons by half and towards an interim agreement on intermediate-range missiles; each accepted the other's invitation, Gorbachev to visit the USA and Reagan to visit the Soviet Union; they also agreed to widen cultural, educational and scientific exchanges, to reopen talks on civil aviation and to open consulates in New York and Kiev; they spent about an hour talking alone by a fire in a pool house beside the villa; three further summits followed, at Reykjavik in October 1986, at Washington in December 1987 and at Moscow in the spring of 1988; asked in Red Square in 1988 about the evil empire remark he had made in 1983, Reagan said it belonged to another time and another era.","steps":["Set the criterion before writing anything down: importance for a relationship means the difference the meeting made to the way the two governments dealt with each other afterwards, so every paragraph has to end in what followed and not in what was or was not signed on the day.","First difference it made: contact was restored. No American president and Soviet leader had met for more than six years, so a relationship carried on through speeches and third parties became one carried on face to face, and the two men arranged to visit each other rather than wait for the next crisis to force a meeting.","Second difference: the joint statement gave both sides a form of words - that a nuclear war cannot be won and must never be fought, and that neither would seek military superiority - which each leader could afterwards be held to, so neither could go back to the language of the early 1980s without publicly breaking his own undertaking.","Third difference, and the proof that it lasted: the ordinary machinery agreed at Geneva went on working - wider cultural, educational and scientific exchanges, consulates in New York and Kiev, civil aviation talks reopened - and three more summits followed within three years, at Reykjavik, at Washington and at Moscow.","Judge it: Geneva was important for the terms of the relationship rather than for its contents. Nothing was cut in Switzerland, but from then on the two governments argued as negotiators instead of as enemies, and by the time Reagan stood in Red Square he was disowning the language he had used before the two ever met."],"cues":["What must an answer about importance for a relationship be measured by?","What had been missing between the two governments since 1979?","Why does a form of words with no treaty behind it still tie a leader's hands?","Which later facts show that what was agreed at Geneva kept working?","Terms or contents - which of them did Geneva change, and so what is the judgement?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Name the three main détente agreements of the 1970s with their dates, and say what each was mainly about.","a":"SALT 1, signed in Moscow on 26 May 1972: an ABM Treaty limiting defensive missile sites and an interim freeze on missile launchers. The Helsinki Final Act, 1 August 1975, signed by 35 states: European frontiers, co-operation in trade and technology, and human rights. SALT 2, signed in Vienna on 18 June 1979: an equal ceiling of 2,400 strategic launchers, never ratified by the US Senate."},{"q":"Give three things Carter did in January 1980 in response to the Soviet invasion of Afghanistan.","a":"Declared in his address to Congress on 23 January that the Persian Gulf was a vital American interest to be defended by force if necessary; withdrew SALT 2 from the Senate; stopped grain sales to the USSR; and called for the boycott of the Moscow Olympics that more than sixty countries joined."},{"q":"What was the Strategic Defence Initiative, when was it announced, and why did it worry Moscow so much?","a":"A proposed system of satellites and lasers to destroy incoming missiles, announced by Reagan on 23 March 1983 and nicknamed Star Wars. It was never built, but if it had worked the USA could have struck first and survived the reply, and the USSR had neither the technology nor the money to match it."},{"q":"What did Gorbachev announce at the United Nations on 7 December 1988, and why did it matter for eastern Europe?","a":"Cuts of 500,000 men in Soviet forces and the withdrawal and disbandment of six tank divisions from East Germany, Czechoslovakia and Hungary, with every country free to choose its own path. It abandoned the Brezhnev Doctrine, so eastern European governments could no longer count on Soviet troops to keep them in power."},{"q":"Put these in order and date them: the fall of the Berlin Wall, German reunification, the dissolution of the Warsaw Pact, the end of the USSR, the August coup.","a":"The Berlin Wall opened on 9 November 1989; Germany was reunified on 3 October 1990; the Warsaw Pact was dissolved in Prague on 1 July 1991; the coup against Gorbachev ran from 19 to 21 August 1991; the Soviet Union ceased to exist on 26 December 1991."}]},"room":{"facts":["Booklet P is worth 32 marks and has three questions: Question 1, printed in two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each 'Explain one consequence of...' for 4 marks and each on a separate event or development; Question 2, 'Write a narrative account analysing...', 8 marks; Question 3, 'Explain two of the following: the importance of ... for ...', two chosen parts at 8 marks each, 16 in all.","Only Question 2 prints stimulus points, as two bullets followed by 'You must also use information of your own.' The cap printed in the mark scheme is: 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' There is no cap at Level 2. Questions 1 and 3 print no bullets and carry no cap.","Each part of Question 1 is marked on its own two-level grid out of 4: Level 2 needs features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific information added in support. Two accurate descriptions of the event, however detailed, stay in Level 1.","Question 3 is marked twice on a three-level grid out of 8, and Level 3 needs analysis of importance in a line of reasoning that is coherent and logically structured: what difference the event or person made to later situations and developments, not what happened at the time.","Timing: Paper 2 lasts 1 hour 50 minutes for 64 marks, about 55 minutes a booklet, so booklet P's 32 marks get roughly a minute a mark with planning on top - about 12 minutes on Question 1's two parts, 13 on Question 2 and 14 on each part of Question 3, leaving a couple of minutes to check."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the signing of SALT 1 in May 1972. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2, marked on the 4-mark consequence grid; Level 2 (3-4) requires features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific support. Credit the acceptance of mutual vulnerability through the ABM Treaty's limit on defensive sites, the freeze on launcher numbers while warhead numbers went on rising, the opening of the wider détente process leading to Helsinki and SALT 2, or Nixon's Moscow visit as a precedent for superpower summitry.","sol":"One consequence of SALT 1 was that both superpowers formally accepted they could not defend themselves. The Anti-Ballistic Missile Treaty signed in Moscow on 26 May 1972 allowed each side only two defensive missile sites, cut to one by a protocol in 1974, so each population was deliberately left open to attack and deterrence rested on the certainty of destruction. That made a first strike pointless and gave both governments a reason to keep negotiating rather than to keep building defences. It is why the talks continued into the Helsinki Final Act of 1975 and SALT 2 in 1979, and why détente lasted as long as it did.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["A consequence is something that was true afterwards because of the treaty, so ask what SALT 1 settled that had not been settled before.","Look at the defensive half of the agreement rather than the offensive half: what does limiting anti-missile sites do to each side's chance of surviving a nuclear exchange?","Write it in three moves: name the consequence, give the precise detail that proves it, then explain why it changed what followed."],"codes":["P4-3 — 1 Attempts to reduce superpower tensions in the 1970s: The significance of SALT 1, the Helsinki Accords, and SALT 2","P4-3 — 1 Attempts to reduce superpower tensions in the 1970s: Détente"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of the Helsinki Accords of August 1975. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the acceptance of Europe's post-war frontiers and of East Germany as a state, the growth of east-west trade and technology transfer, or the use made of the human rights commitments in Basket 3 by dissident groups such as the Moscow Helsinki Group and Charter 77.","sol":"One consequence of Helsinki was that human rights became a standard Soviet rule could be measured against. The Final Act, signed by 35 states on 1 August 1975, gave Brezhnev what he wanted in Basket 1, the acceptance of Europe's post-war frontiers, but the price was Basket 3, in which every signatory promised freedom of speech, movement and information. Moscow treated that as a formality; its own citizens did not. The Moscow Helsinki Group was founded in 1976 to report breaches, and Charter 77 appeared in Czechoslovakia in January 1977. For the first time, dissidents could accuse their governments of breaking an agreement those governments had signed.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The Final Act had three baskets and each side valued a different one, so ask which basket the USSR thought it was paying with.","A written promise can be used by people the government did not have in mind; think about who inside the eastern bloc could now quote it.","Name the consequence, prove it with a named group and a date, then say why signing changed what critics of the regime could do."],"codes":["P4-3 — 1 Attempts to reduce superpower tensions in the 1970s: The significance of SALT 1, the Helsinki Accords, and SALT 2"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the signing of the INF Treaty in December 1987. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the abolition of an entire class of weapons rather than a ceiling on them, the removal of Soviet SS-20s and American Pershing II and cruise missiles from Europe, the precedent of on-site inspection and the trust it built, or the momentum it gave to later agreements such as the withdrawal from Afghanistan and START.","sol":"One consequence of the INF Treaty was that a whole class of nuclear weapons was destroyed rather than merely capped. Signed in Washington on 8 December 1987, it removed every ground-launched missile with a range between 500 and 5,500 kilometres, so the Soviet SS-20s and the American Pershing II and cruise missiles stationed in Europe all went; 2,692 had been destroyed by June 1991. Because each side let the other's inspectors onto its bases to check, the treaty also replaced suspicion with verification. The fear of a nuclear war beginning in Europe, which had dominated the early 1980s, was largely lifted.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Compare what this treaty did with what SALT 1 did: one set ceilings, the other did something different to the weapons themselves.","More than one consequence is available here, so choose the one you can support with precise detail and develop only that one.","State the consequence, support it with the range limit and the missiles that went, then explain what changed for Europe."],"codes":["P4-3 — 2 The end of détente and the 'Second Cold War': The significance of Reagan and Gorbachev in reducing tensions"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of the fall of the Berlin Wall in November 1989. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the collapse of the East German state and reunification on 3 October 1990, the demonstration effect on the other eastern European regimes that fell within weeks, or the removal of the Cold War's most visible symbol and the declaration at Malta that the confrontation was over.","sol":"One consequence of the opening of the Wall on 9 November 1989 was that the East German state lost its reason to exist. The Wall had been built in 1961 to stop its people leaving, so once the crossings were open, and once it was clear that Moscow would not send troops, the government could neither close the border again nor win a free vote. Elections in March 1990 returned a parliament that wanted union with the West, and Germany was reunified on 3 October 1990, less than eleven months later, with the whole country inside NATO. The state the Wall had protected had disappeared.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what the Wall had been for, because a consequence of its opening should be the loss of whatever it provided.","One state in particular could not survive the change; follow what happened to it over the next eleven months.","Name the consequence, date the proof, and explain why an open border made the state's survival impossible rather than merely difficult."],"codes":["P4-3 — 3 The collapse of Soviet control of Eastern Europe, 1985-91: The significance of the fall of the Berlin Wall"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Carter Doctrine of January 1980. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the American boycott of the Moscow Olympics in 1980. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4 on the consequence grid, so each needs one consequence analysed with specific support. In 1(a) credit the extension of containment to the Persian Gulf, the creation of a rapid deployment force, or American arming of the Afghan mujahideen. In 1(b) credit the loss of the propaganda showcase Moscow had planned, the retaliatory Soviet boycott of Los Angeles in 1984, or the ending of the cultural and sporting contact détente had built.","sol":"1(a) Carter told Congress on 23 January 1980 that any outside attempt to control the Persian Gulf would be treated as an attack on American vital interests and resisted by force if necessary. The consequence was a permanent American military commitment in a region the USA had previously left to others: a rapid deployment force was created in 1980 to make the threat credible, and Washington began funding and arming the Afghan mujahideen against Soviet troops. Containment, which had been drawn as a line in Europe in 1947, now ran through the Middle East, and the superpowers were fighting each other through proxies again. 1(b) The boycott turned the exchanges détente had built into another weapon. More than sixty countries followed the American lead and stayed away from the Moscow games in the summer of 1980, so an event the Soviet government had planned for years as a showcase of socialist success was held without many of the world's strongest competitors. The consequence ran on for years: in 1984 the USSR and most of its allies refused to go to Los Angeles, so athletes from the two blocs, who had last met at a summer games at Montreal in 1976, did not meet again until Seoul in 1988, a gap of twelve years. Sport, like grain and technology, had become something either government would cut off to make a political point.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate parts on two separate things: a presidential doctrine in 1(a) and a decision about a sporting event in 1(b), so neither answer can help the other.","For 1(a) ask where containment had been applied before 1980 and where it was now being applied instead.","For 1(b) a boycott damages the host, but ask also what it did to the contacts between the two societies that détente had encouraged."],"codes":["P4-3 — 2 The end of détente and the 'Second Cold War': The significance of the Soviet invasion of Afghanistan"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of Gorbachev becoming Soviet leader in March 1985. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the policy of glasnost. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4. In 1(a) credit the reopening of summit diplomacy with Reagan from Geneva in November 1985, the reforms of perestroika and glasnost, the decision to leave Afghanistan, or new thinking and the abandonment of the Brezhnev Doctrine. In 1(b) credit the public criticism of the Soviet system after Chernobyl, the freedom given to nationalist movements in the republics, or the loss of the party's monopoly on what could be said and believed.","sol":"1(a) One consequence of Gorbachev's appointment on 11 March 1985 was that summit diplomacy restarted almost at once. His predecessors had been old and ill and no Soviet leader had met an American president since 1979; within eight months he was at Geneva with Reagan, issuing a joint statement that a nuclear war could not be won and must never be fought and agreeing to meet again. Because he needed to cut military spending to rescue a slowing economy, he was willing to make concessions his predecessors had refused, and that produced Reykjavik in 1986 and the INF Treaty in 1987. 1(b) One consequence of glasnost was that the Soviet system lost control of what its own people were allowed to think about it. Openness meant that failures could be reported: the handling of the Chernobyl explosion in April 1986 was criticised in the Soviet press, the crimes of the Stalin years were discussed, and shortages could be blamed on the government rather than on saboteurs. Because criticism was no longer punished, nationalist movements in the Baltic republics could organise in the open, and by 1990 Lithuania was able to declare independence. A policy meant to make communism work better instead let people say out loud that it did not.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate parts: a change of leader in 1(a) and a domestic policy in 1(b), so keep the foreign policy story out of 1(b).","For 1(a) ask what a younger, healthier leader with an economic problem could do that his predecessors could not.","For 1(b) openness is only a rule about speech, so ask who used the new freedom and what they used it to say."],"codes":["P4-3 — 2 The end of détente and the 'Second Cold War': The significance of Reagan and Gorbachev in reducing tensions","P4-3 — 3 The collapse of Soviet control of Eastern Europe, 1985-91: The impact of Gorbachev's 'new thinking'"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Reykjavik summit of October 1986. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of Gorbachev's speech to the United Nations in December 1988. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4. In 1(a) credit the identification of SDI as the single obstacle, Gorbachev's decision early in 1987 to separate intermediate-range weapons from SDI, and the INF Treaty that followed. In 1(b) credit the abandonment of the Brezhnev Doctrine, the confidence it gave reform movements and governments in eastern Europe, or the troop and tank withdrawals themselves.","sol":"1(a) One consequence of Reykjavik on 11 and 12 October 1986 was that the whole disagreement was narrowed to a single issue. The two leaders discussed removing entire categories of nuclear weapons and then broke off because Reagan would not confine the Strategic Defence Initiative to laboratory research, so both sides left knowing exactly what stood in the way. Early in 1987 Gorbachev dropped his demand that an intermediate-range deal be tied to SDI, and the INF Treaty was signed in Washington that December. A summit reported at the time as a failure was in fact the point at which the next treaty became possible. 1(b) One consequence of the United Nations speech of 7 December 1988 was that eastern Europe's communist governments lost their guarantee. Gorbachev announced that Soviet forces would be cut by 500,000 men, that six tank divisions would be withdrawn from East Germany, Czechoslovakia and Hungary and disbanded, and that every country should be free to choose its own path. That abandoned the Brezhnev Doctrine, the promise that Moscow would use troops to keep communist governments in place. Reformers took it as permission and hardliners took it as abandonment, which is why Poland's government agreed to elections in 1989 and why Hungary opened its border rather than shooting.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate parts: a summit that signed nothing in 1(a) and a speech in 1(b), so do not let one answer borrow the other's evidence.","For 1(a) ask what both governments knew after the breakdown that they had not known before it.","For 1(b) a speech changes what other people believe is possible, so ask who was listening in Warsaw and Budapest and what they concluded."],"codes":["P4-3 — 2 The end of détente and the 'Second Cold War': The significance of Reagan and Gorbachev in reducing tensions","P4-3 — 3 The collapse of Soviet control of Eastern Europe, 1985-91: The impact of Gorbachev's 'new thinking'"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the failure of the attempted coup against Gorbachev in August 1991. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the dissolution of the Warsaw Pact in July 1991. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4. In 1(a) credit the discrediting and suspension of the Communist Party, the collapse of Gorbachev's remaining authority, the rise of Yeltsin, or the rush of republics to declare independence ending in the dissolution of the USSR on 26 December 1991. In 1(b) credit the end of the military division of Europe, the freedom of the former satellites to choose their own alignment, or the removal of the structure through which Moscow had controlled eastern Europe since 1955.","sol":"1(a) One consequence of the coup's collapse between 19 and 21 August 1991 was that the Communist Party was finished as the ruling force in the USSR. The plotters were the leaders of the party, the army and the KGB, and when troops would not act against Yeltsin and the crowds in Moscow, the institutions that had governed the country since 1917 were shown to be both hostile to reform and powerless. Gorbachev resigned as General Secretary within days and the party's activities were suspended. With the centre discredited, the republics left one after another, and the Soviet Union ceased to exist on 26 December 1991. 1(b) One consequence of the Warsaw Pact's dissolution in Prague on 1 July 1991 was that the military division of Europe ended. The Pact had been founded in 1955 as the answer to NATO and had supplied the machinery through which Soviet forces were stationed in the satellite states; its members had invaded Czechoslovakia under its name in 1968. Once it was gone, the former satellites were free to look west: within a few years Poland, Hungary and the Czech Republic were negotiating to join NATO instead. The alliance system that had defined the Cold War in Europe had only one side left.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate parts: a failed coup in 1(a) and the winding up of an alliance in 1(b), so keep the evidence separate.","For 1(a) ask who the plotters were, because the failure discredited whatever institutions they came from.","For 1(b) ask what the Pact had been used for since 1955, and what became possible for its members once it no longer existed."],"codes":["P4-3 — 3 The collapse of Soviet control of Eastern Europe, 1985-91: The collapse of the Soviet Union"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the breakdown of détente in the years 1979-81. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the signing of SALT 2 (June 1979); the Carter Doctrine (January 1980). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) requires a clear sequence leading to an outcome with the linkage between events analysed. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; own material might include NATO's decision of December 1979 to deploy Pershing II and cruise missiles, the invasion of Afghanistan and the killing of Amin, the grain embargo and the Olympic boycott, the withdrawal of SALT 2 from the Senate, and Reagan's election and rearmament.","sol":"Détente was already fragile when Carter and Brezhnev signed SALT 2 in Vienna on 18 June 1979. The treaty set an equal ceiling of 2,400 strategic launchers, but many senators thought it conceded too much, and in December NATO decided to place Pershing II and cruise missiles in western Europe to answer the Soviet SS-20s deployed since 1976, so both sides were preparing to build even as they signed. The break came at the end of that month. Soviet troops entered Afghanistan on 24 and 25 December 1979, Hafizullah Amin was killed on 27 December and Babrak Karmal was installed, and whatever Moscow's motives, Washington read it as expansion towards the Gulf oilfields. Because détente rested on each side trusting the other to stay inside agreed limits, that one move outside them destroyed the policy rather than merely straining it. Carter's answer in January 1980 was therefore a whole programme: the doctrine of 23 January committing American force to the Gulf, the grain embargo, the withdrawal of SALT 2 from the Senate, and the Olympic boycott that more than sixty countries joined in the summer. Each step removed one of the contacts détente had built. The sequence ended by changing the American government itself: Reagan, who had attacked détente throughout the campaign, won the election of November 1980 and took office in January 1981 committed to rearmament and to negotiating only from strength. In two years the superpowers had moved from signing a treaty to abandoning the idea of them.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Begin before Afghanistan: what was already going wrong with SALT 2 and with missiles in Europe in 1979?","The account needs joins rather than dates: at each stage ask why the other side's move left the choice it did.","Plan four episodes with a connecting sentence between each, so that the answer addresses three or more aspects of content and not only the two bullets."],"codes":["P4-3 — 1 Attempts to reduce superpower tensions in the 1970s: The significance of SALT 1, the Helsinki Accords, and SALT 2","P4-3 — 2 The end of détente and the 'Second Cold War': The significance of the Soviet invasion of Afghanistan","P4-3 — 2 The end of détente and the 'Second Cold War': Reagan and the 'Second Cold War'"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the events leading to the fall of the Berlin Wall in the years 1988-89. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: Gorbachev's speech to the United Nations (December 1988); the opening of Hungary's border with Austria (1989). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) requires a clear sequence leading to an outcome with the linkage between events analysed. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; own material might include the end of the Brezhnev Doctrine, the Polish Round Table and the elections of June 1989, the flight of East Germans through Hungary and Czechoslovakia, the Leipzig demonstrations, Honecker's resignation, and the press conference of 9 November.","sol":"The sequence begins in Moscow, not Berlin. At the United Nations on 7 December 1988 Gorbachev announced cuts of 500,000 men and the withdrawal of six tank divisions from eastern Europe, and said every country should choose its own path. That abandoned the Brezhnev Doctrine, so the threat that had held the bloc together since 1945 was gone, and reformers could act on the assumption that tanks would not come. Poland tested it first: Round Table talks with Solidarity produced partly free elections on 4 June 1989 which the communists lost heavily, and by August Tadeusz Mazowiecki was prime minister, with no Soviet reaction at all. Because Moscow had not intervened in Poland, Hungary's reformers dared more: having begun cutting the fence on the Austrian border in May, they opened it to East Germans in September. That turned another country's decision into East Germany's crisis: tens of thousands left through Hungary and Czechoslovakia, so the government was losing its workforce and could not stop them without closing every frontier. Demonstrations grew to hundreds of thousands in Leipzig and Berlin, and when the police did not fire, Honecker was forced out on 18 October. His successors tried to buy calm by relaxing travel rules, and on the evening of 9 November 1989 a confused announcement sent Berliners to the crossing points in such numbers that the guards opened the gates. The Wall fell not because it was stormed but because the power behind it had already been withdrawn, step by step, over eleven months.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Start outside Germany: what had Moscow said in December 1988 that made everything else possible?","Each step should explain why the next actor dared to go further, so look for the moments when nothing happened to the reformers.","Plan four episodes, at least two of them your own material, and write a connecting sentence between each so the linkage is analysed rather than assumed."],"codes":["P4-3 — 3 The collapse of Soviet control of Eastern Europe, 1985-91: The impact of Gorbachev's 'new thinking'","P4-3 — 3 The collapse of Soviet control of Eastern Europe, 1985-91: The significance of the fall of the Berlin Wall"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the collapse of the Soviet Union in the years 1990-91. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the reunification of Germany (October 1990); the attempted coup against Gorbachev (August 1991). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) requires a clear sequence leading to an outcome with the linkage between events analysed. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; own material might include Lithuania's declaration of independence, the failure of perestroika and the shortages, Yeltsin's election as Russian president in June 1991, the winding up of Comecon and the Warsaw Pact, and the agreements of December 1991 that ended the union.","sol":"By 1990 Gorbachev had lost the empire and was losing the state. Germany was reunified on 3 October 1990 inside NATO, something no Soviet leader would have contemplated two years earlier, and it advertised how little Moscow could now insist on. Because eastern Europe had been allowed to go, the Soviet republics asked why they could not: Lithuania had already declared independence in March 1990, and the army's attempt to intimidate it in January 1991 cost lives without restoring obedience. Perestroika made the centre weaker still, since loosening the plan produced shortages and price rises rather than growth, and glasnost meant everyone could say so. Rival authority then appeared inside Russia itself, when Boris Yeltsin was elected president of the Russian republic by popular vote on 12 June 1991, giving him a mandate Gorbachev had never sought. The bloc's remaining structures were wound up that summer, Comecon in June and the Warsaw Pact in Prague on 1 July. Conservatives in the party, the army and the KGB saw a new union treaty about to devolve real power and seized it instead, holding Gorbachev at his house in Crimea from 19 August 1991; but the troops would not act, Yeltsin defied them publicly in Moscow, and the coup collapsed within three days. That settled the outcome, because the institutions that might have held the union together had now tried and failed. The republics declared independence, and on 26 December 1991 the Soviet Union ceased to exist.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The account has two collapses running together, the outer empire and the union itself; decide how you will link them before you start.","Ask at each stage what the centre had left: which of its powers had gone, and who had picked them up.","Four episodes with explained joins, at least two from your own material, and a final sentence saying what the sequence amounted to rather than repeating it."],"codes":["P4-3 — 3 The collapse of Soviet control of Eastern Europe, 1985-91: The collapse of the Soviet Union","P4-3 — 3 The collapse of Soviet control of Eastern Europe, 1985-91: The impact of Gorbachev's 'new thinking'"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of SALT 1 (1972) for the arms race between the superpowers. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid, and Level 3 (6-8) needs analysis of importance in a coherent line of reasoning. Credit the ABM Treaty and the acceptance of mutual vulnerability, the freeze on launchers alongside the unrestricted growth of warheads through multiple re-entry vehicles, the precedent set for Helsinki and SALT 2, and the limits exposed when the treaty framework collapsed after 1979.","sol":"SALT 1 mattered less for the weapons it removed, which were none, than for the rules it set. Its Anti-Ballistic Missile Treaty, signed in Moscow on 26 May 1972, allowed each side only two anti-missile sites, reduced to one in 1974. That was a deliberate decision to stay vulnerable, and it fixed the logic of the arms race: because neither side could shoot down the other's missiles, neither could gain by striking first, and the competition became a matter of numbers rather than survival. Its second importance was to establish that the superpowers could negotiate limits at all. The Interim Agreement froze the number of land-based and submarine-launched launchers for five years, and once that had been done the same method produced Helsinki in 1975 and SALT 2 in 1979: summits, ceilings and signed texts became the normal way of handling the rivalry. Its third importance is what it left undone, and this is where the judgement lies. Nothing was said about bombers, or about fitting several independently targeted warheads to one missile, so both sides multiplied warheads legally inside the treaty and the number of weapons rose while the number of launchers stood still. SALT 1 therefore slowed the arms race in one dimension and left it free in another. Its deepest importance appeared in 1983, when Reagan's Strategic Defence Initiative threatened to overturn the ABM bargain and Moscow reacted as if the basis of its security since 1972 were being withdrawn - which, on the treaty's own logic, it was.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Importance means the difference it made afterwards, so refuse any sentence that only describes the terms of the treaty.","Trace two lines forward: what the ABM Treaty settled about defence, and what the Interim Agreement failed to settle about warheads.","End with a judgement, and test it by asking what changed in 1983 when the ABM bargain was challenged."],"codes":["P4-3 — 1 Attempts to reduce superpower tensions in the 1970s: The significance of SALT 1, the Helsinki Accords, and SALT 2","P4-3 — 1 Attempts to reduce superpower tensions in the 1970s: Détente","P4-3 — 2 The end of détente and the 'Second Cold War': Reagan and the 'Second Cold War'"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of Gorbachev's 'new thinking' for Soviet control of eastern Europe. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Credit the abandonment of the Brezhnev Doctrine at the United Nations in December 1988, the Sinatra Doctrine of October 1989, the withdrawal from Afghanistan as proof that force would not be used, the elections in Poland and the opening of Hungary's border, the fall of the Wall and the revolutions of 1989, and the dissolution of the Warsaw Pact in 1991.","sol":"New thinking mattered because of what it took away. Soviet control of eastern Europe had rested since 1945 on one belief: that Moscow would send troops to keep a communist government in power, as in Hungary in 1956 and Czechoslovakia in 1968. New thinking replaced that with the principle that security is common and each country chooses its own path, and he made it credible by leaving Afghanistan on 15 February 1989 rather than reinforcing a client regime in trouble. He said so at the United Nations on 7 December 1988, cutting Soviet forces by 500,000 men and withdrawing six tank divisions from East Germany, Czechoslovakia and Hungary. Its first importance is that it gave reformers a reason to test the limits. Poland's communists agreed to partly free elections on 4 June 1989 and lost them, and nothing happened; because nothing happened, Hungary dared to open its border with Austria, and through that hole East Germans left in tens of thousands, undoing the East German state. The chain runs from the speech to the crossing points on 9 November 1989. Its second importance is that it disarmed the hardliners: when the Soviet spokesman joked in October 1989 that each country could do it its own way, no eastern European leader could threaten his own people with Soviet tanks. Without new thinking there would still have been discontent, as there had been since 1953; what was new was that it could not be crushed, and that is why forty years of control ended in a single year.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Importance here is about the effect on other people's decisions, so ask what eastern European reformers believed after 1988 that they had not dared believe before.","Build a chain rather than a list: the speech, then a government that risked elections, then a border, then the Wall.","Close with a judgement that says what would have been different without it, using the earlier risings as the comparison."],"codes":["P4-3 — 3 The collapse of Soviet control of Eastern Europe, 1985-91: The impact of Gorbachev's 'new thinking'","P4-3 — 3 The collapse of Soviet control of Eastern Europe, 1985-91: The significance of the fall of the Berlin Wall"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of President Reagan for the reduction of superpower tension in the 1980s. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Credit the rearmament and rhetoric of the early 1980s and the pressure they placed on the Soviet economy, the four summits from Geneva in 1985 to Moscow in 1988, the INF Treaty of December 1987, the public abandonment of the evil empire language at Moscow, and the case that Gorbachev and Soviet weakness mattered more.","sol":"Reagan's importance is clearest if his two halves are taken in order. From 1981 he rearmed and attacked the USSR in public: an evil empire in March 1983, Pershing II and cruise missiles in western Europe later that year, and the Strategic Defence Initiative announced on 23 March 1983. In the short term this raised tension to its highest level since 1962; in the longer term it put a price on the rivalry that the Soviet economy could not pay, and Gorbachev came to power in 1985 knowing he had to cut military spending. The second half is the one that reduced tension. He was willing to negotiate once the USSR had a leader he could deal with, and the two men met four times: Geneva in November 1985, where they stated jointly that nuclear war could not be won; Reykjavik in October 1986, where they nearly abolished whole categories of weapons; Washington in December 1987, where the INF Treaty destroyed an entire class of missiles with inspectors to check; and Moscow in 1988, where Reagan said his evil empire remark belonged to another time. Because he had built a reputation for hostility, his acceptance of Gorbachev carried a weight with American opinion that no softer president's could have. The judgement must still be qualified. The concessions and the new thinking came from Moscow, and it was Soviet economic weakness rather than American strength that made them necessary. Reagan's importance is that he set the price and then, unexpectedly, took yes for an answer.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Reagan's record splits into two periods that point in opposite directions; decide how they connect before you write.","Use the four summits as evidence of the second period, and say what each one actually produced.","An importance answer at the top level weighs its subject against the alternative, so ask how much was Reagan and how much was Soviet weakness."],"codes":["P4-3 — 2 The end of détente and the 'Second Cold War': Reagan and the 'Second Cold War'","P4-3 — 2 The end of détente and the 'Second Cold War': The significance of Reagan and Gorbachev in reducing tensions"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date was the Helsinki Final Act signed, and how many states signed it?","a":"1 August 1975, by 35 states."},{"q":"What did Reagan announce on 23 March 1983?","a":"The Strategic Defence Initiative, a proposed missile defence system nicknamed Star Wars."},{"q":"Which treaty, signed on 8 December 1987, abolished all ground-launched missiles with ranges of 500 to 5,500 kilometres?","a":"The INF Treaty, signed in Washington by Reagan and Gorbachev."},{"q":"On what date did the Soviet Union cease to exist?","a":"26 December 1991."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Détente","code":"P4-3 — 1 Attempts to reduce superpower tensions in the 1970s: Détente","open":false,"back":"A deliberate relaxing of tension between the superpowers, roughly 1969 to 1979. Neither side gave up its beliefs; both agreed to manage the rivalry by summits, treaties and trade instead of confrontation."},{"front":"One reason the USA and the USSR both wanted détente","code":"P4-3 — 1 Attempts to reduce superpower tensions in the 1970s: Détente","open":true,"back":"The cost of the arms race, which neither economy could easily carry; the fear of nuclear war after the Cuban Missile Crisis of 1962, now that both sides had rough parity; Soviet wish for American grain and Western technology; American wish to end the Vietnam War, and Moscow's quarrel with communist China."},{"front":"SALT 1, signed in Moscow on 26 May 1972","code":"P4-3 — 1 Attempts to reduce superpower tensions in the 1970s: The significance of SALT 1, the Helsinki Accords, and SALT 2","open":false,"back":"Nixon and Brezhnev. The ABM Treaty allowed each side two anti-missile sites, cut to one in 1974. The Interim Agreement froze launcher numbers for five years but said nothing about warheads or bombers."},{"front":"The Helsinki Final Act, 1 August 1975","code":"P4-3 — 1 Attempts to reduce superpower tensions in the 1970s: The significance of SALT 1, the Helsinki Accords, and SALT 2","open":false,"back":"Signed by 35 states. Basket 1 accepted Europe's post-war frontiers; Basket 2 opened trade, science and technology; Basket 3 promised human rights, which dissidents then used against their own governments."},{"front":"SALT 2, signed in Vienna on 18 June 1979","code":"P4-3 — 1 Attempts to reduce superpower tensions in the 1970s: The significance of SALT 1, the Helsinki Accords, and SALT 2","open":false,"back":"Carter and Brezhnev set an equal ceiling of 2,400 strategic launchers, falling to 2,250, and limited missiles with several warheads. The US Senate never ratified it; Carter withdrew it in January 1980."},{"front":"The Soviet invasion of Afghanistan, December 1979","code":"P4-3 — 2 The end of détente and the 'Second Cold War': The significance of the Soviet invasion of Afghanistan","open":false,"back":"Soviet troops entered on 24 and 25 December 1979 to support a failing communist government. Hafizullah Amin was killed on 27 December and Babrak Karmal installed. The last troops left on 15 February 1989."},{"front":"The Carter Doctrine, 23 January 1980","code":"P4-3 — 2 The end of détente and the 'Second Cold War': The significance of the Soviet invasion of Afghanistan","open":false,"back":"In his address to Congress Carter declared the Persian Gulf a vital American interest, to be defended by force if necessary. He also stopped grain sales, withdrew SALT 2 from the Senate and armed the mujahideen."},{"front":"The Olympic boycotts of 1980 and 1984","code":"P4-3 — 2 The end of détente and the 'Second Cold War': The significance of the Soviet invasion of Afghanistan","open":false,"back":"More than sixty countries followed the USA in staying away from the Moscow games in 1980 after the invasion of Afghanistan. The USSR and most of its allies stayed away from Los Angeles in 1984 in reply."},{"front":"The 'Second Cold War' and the Strategic Defence Initiative","code":"P4-3 — 2 The end of détente and the 'Second Cold War': Reagan and the 'Second Cold War'","open":false,"back":"Reagan rearmed from 1981, called the USSR an evil empire in March 1983 and announced SDI on 23 March 1983: satellites and lasers to destroy incoming missiles. Never built, but unmatchable for the Soviet economy."},{"front":"The four Reagan-Gorbachev summits, 1985 to 1988","code":"P4-3 — 2 The end of détente and the 'Second Cold War': The significance of Reagan and Gorbachev in reducing tensions","open":false,"back":"Geneva, November 1985: no treaty but a joint statement that nuclear war cannot be won. Reykjavik, October 1986: near agreement, broken by SDI. Washington, December 1987: the INF Treaty. Moscow, 1988."},{"front":"The INF Treaty, 8 December 1987","code":"P4-3 — 2 The end of détente and the 'Second Cold War': The significance of Reagan and Gorbachev in reducing tensions","open":false,"back":"Signed in Washington. It abolished every ground-launched missile with a range of 500 to 5,500 kilometres, with on-site inspection. 2,692 missiles had been destroyed by June 1991, the first whole class of weapons scrapped."},{"front":"Glasnost and perestroika","code":"P4-3 — 3 The collapse of Soviet control of Eastern Europe, 1985-91: The impact of Gorbachev's 'new thinking'","open":false,"back":"Glasnost, openness, allowed criticism and honest reporting, especially after Chernobyl in April 1986. Perestroika, restructuring, loosened central planning from 1987 but produced shortages and rising prices rather than growth."},{"front":"'New thinking' and the Sinatra Doctrine","code":"P4-3 — 3 The collapse of Soviet control of Eastern Europe, 1985-91: The impact of Gorbachev's 'new thinking'","open":false,"back":"New thinking: security is common and every country chooses its own path. On 25 October 1989 Soviet spokesman Gennadi Gerasimov called it the Sinatra Doctrine - each Warsaw Pact state free to go its own way, the Brezhnev Doctrine abandoned."},{"front":"The fall of the Berlin Wall, 9 November 1989","code":"P4-3 — 3 The collapse of Soviet control of Eastern Europe, 1985-91: The significance of the fall of the Berlin Wall","open":false,"back":"After Hungary opened its border, mass flight and huge demonstrations forced Honecker out on 18 October. A confused announcement on travel rules brought crowds to the crossings and the guards opened them."},{"front":"The end of the Warsaw Pact and of the Soviet Union, 1990 to 1991","code":"P4-3 — 3 The collapse of Soviet control of Eastern Europe, 1985-91: The collapse of the Soviet Union","open":false,"back":"Germany reunified 3 October 1990; the Warsaw Pact dissolved in Prague on 1 July 1991; the coup against Gorbachev collapsed on 21 August 1991; the USSR ceased to exist on 26 December 1991."}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph - Level 3 (Paper 2 booklet P Question 2, narrative account, 8 marks)","items":["Question: 'Write a narrative account analysing the growth of tension between the superpowers in the years 1981-85.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the Strategic Defence Initiative (March 1983); the Soviet boycott of the Los Angeles Olympics (1984). You must also use information of your own. This is the middle stretch of the account, carrying the story from Reagan's rearmament to the collapse of the arms talks at the end of 1983.","March 1983 turned Reagan's rearmament into something Moscow had to answer. On 8 March, speaking to an evangelical audience in Orlando, he called the Soviet Union an evil empire and the focus of evil in the modern world, and on 23 March he announced the Strategic Defence Initiative, research into a space-based system that would destroy incoming missiles. The words mattered less than the programme, because a shield that worked would leave the USA able to strike first and survive the reply, and the Soviet economy could neither copy the research nor build enough new missiles to swamp it. Soviet leaders therefore read 1983 as an American attempt to win the arms race outright rather than to balance it, and every incident of that autumn was read the same way. When a Korean airliner strayed into Soviet airspace and was shot down on 1 September with 269 people on board, Moscow first denied the shooting and then defended it, which persuaded Washington that nothing said from Moscow could be believed. Because neither government could now put a friendly reading on anything the other did, the arrival of American cruise missiles in Britain and Pershing II missiles in West Germany that November was met not with a counter-offer but with a walkout: on 23 November the Soviet delegation left the intermediate-range talks in Geneva, and within weeks the strategic talks had stopped as well, so the superpowers entered 1984 with no arms negotiations of any kind in progress.","Why this is Level 3: the material is organised as a clear sequence leading to an outcome, and the linkage between the episodes is analysed rather than asserted - the Strategic Defence Initiative is explained as the reason Moscow read American policy as a bid for superiority, and that reading is explained as the reason the airliner and the missile deployments produced a walkout instead of a counter-offer. The knowledge is precise (the two March speeches and their dates, the airliner on 1 September 1983 with 269 on board, the walkout of 23 November 1983) and it addresses well more than three aspects of content, so the Level 3 cap does not apply."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph - Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same, 'Write a narrative account analysing the growth of tension between the superpowers in the years 1981-85.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the Strategic Defence Initiative (March 1983); the Soviet boycott of the Los Angeles Olympics (1984). You must also use information of your own.","Reagan became president in 1981 and he did not like the Soviet Union. He spent a lot more money on weapons. In March 1983 he called the USSR an evil empire and announced the Strategic Defence Initiative, which was a plan to shoot down missiles from space. The Russians did not like this at all. Then a Korean plane was shot down by the Soviets and everyone was angry about it. America put new missiles in Europe and the Soviet Union walked out of the arms talks. In 1984 the Soviet Union boycotted the Los Angeles Olympics because America had boycotted Moscow in 1980. Things got worse and worse until Gorbachev became leader in 1985 and then they started to get better.","Why this is only Level 2, and what it misses: the events are in the right order and the information is accurate, so it meets 'some organisation of material into a sequence of events leading to an outcome' and 'accurate and relevant information is added'. It misses the Level 3 requirement that the account 'analyses the linkage between them': 'the Russians did not like this at all' and 'things got worse and worse' assert a connection instead of explaining one, and nothing says why a defensive system frightened a government that had accepted rough parity, or why the missile deployments produced a walkout rather than a counter-offer. The detail is also loose where the top level wants precision - the two March speeches are undated and run together, the airliner is undated and its 269 dead are missing, the walkout from the Geneva talks is not dated, and the closing sentence steps outside the years the question sets instead of saying what the sequence amounted to."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: détente - what it was and why both sides wanted it","items":["Détente: a deliberate relaxing of tension between the superpowers, running from about 1969 to 1979, without either side abandoning its beliefs.","The Cuban Missile Crisis of October 1962 had shown how quickly a local quarrel could reach the point of nuclear war; both sides wanted rules.","By the late 1960s the USSR had caught up in strategic weapons, so neither side could expect to survive an exchange or to win by building more.","Cost: the USA was paying for the Vietnam War, from which its last combat troops withdrew in 1973; the Soviet economy was falling behind the West.","The USSR wanted American grain after poor harvests, and Western technology and credits it could not produce for itself.","Moscow's quarrel with communist China gave it a reason to settle with Washington, especially after Nixon visited Beijing in February 1972.","Europe moved first: Willy Brandt's Ostpolitik produced West German treaties with Moscow and Warsaw in 1970 and a treaty with East Germany in 1972.","The Four Power Agreement on Berlin, September 1971, settled western access to West Berlin and removed the city as a flashpoint.","The Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty had been signed in 1968 by both superpowers and Britain, and came into force in 1970.","Apollo and Soyuz spacecraft docked in orbit in July 1975, the most public symbol of détente.","Détente never meant friendship: both sides went on arming, competing in the developing world, and spying on each other throughout the 1970s."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: SALT 1, the Helsinki Accords and SALT 2","items":["SALT stands for Strategic Arms Limitation Talks; negotiations opened in Helsinki in November 1969.","SALT 1 was signed in Moscow on 26 May 1972 by Nixon and Brezhnev, the first visit by an American president to Moscow.","The Anti-Ballistic Missile Treaty allowed each side two anti-missile sites; a protocol in 1974 reduced this to one each.","The Interim Agreement froze the number of land-based and submarine-launched ballistic missile launchers for five years.","What SALT 1 left out: bombers, and missiles carrying several independently targeted warheads, so warhead numbers went on rising legally.","The Helsinki Final Act was signed on 1 August 1975 by 35 states: the USA, Canada, the USSR and every European state except Albania.","Basket 1: existing European frontiers accepted as they stood, and non-interference in other states' internal affairs - the Soviet gain.","Basket 2: co-operation in trade, industry, science and technology across the divide.","Basket 3: human rights, including freedom of speech, movement and information - the western gain, and the one Moscow expected to cost nothing.","Use made of Basket 3: the Moscow Helsinki Group was founded in 1976 and Charter 77 appeared in Czechoslovakia in January 1977.","SALT 2 was signed in Vienna on 18 June 1979 by Carter and Brezhnev: an equal ceiling of 2,400 strategic launchers, falling to 2,250, and limits on missiles with multiple warheads.","SALT 2 was never ratified: Carter withdrew it from the Senate in January 1980 after the invasion of Afghanistan, although both sides said they would observe its limits."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Afghanistan, the Carter Doctrine and the Olympic boycotts","items":["A communist party took power in Afghanistan in 1978; its land and education reforms provoked an Islamic rebellion across the countryside.","Hafizullah Amin seized the leadership in September 1979 and was distrusted in Moscow.","Soviet troops entered Afghanistan on 24 and 25 December 1979; Amin was killed on 27 December and Babrak Karmal installed in his place.","Moscow's justification: help given to a neighbouring government at its request, under a treaty of friendship of 1978.","Washington's reading: the first Soviet military move outside the Warsaw Pact area since 1945, and a step towards the oilfields of the Persian Gulf.","The Carter Doctrine, in his address to Congress of 23 January 1980: the Gulf was a vital American interest and would be defended by force if necessary.","Other American responses in January 1980: SALT 2 withdrawn from the Senate, grain sales stopped, technology exports restricted.","A rapid deployment force was created in 1980 to give the doctrine teeth; the USA also funded and armed the Afghan mujahideen.","The Moscow Olympics went ahead in the summer of 1980 but more than sixty countries stayed away; some British athletes competed under the Olympic flag rather than the national flag.","The USSR and most of its allies boycotted the Los Angeles Olympics in 1984 in return; the blocs' athletes had last met at Montreal in 1976 and did not meet again until Seoul in 1988, a gap of twelve years.","The war cost the USSR about 15,000 dead and money it could not spare; the Geneva Accords of April 1988 set the timetable for leaving.","The last Soviet troops left Afghanistan on 15 February 1989, an admission of defeat that eastern Europe noticed."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Reagan, the 'Second Cold War' and the Strategic Defence Initiative","items":["Ronald Reagan became president on 20 January 1981, believing détente had allowed the USSR to gain ground and that America should negotiate only from strength.","American defence spending rose steeply through the early 1980s, a burden the USA could carry more easily than the USSR.","8 March 1983, Orlando: Reagan described the Soviet Union as an evil empire in a speech to an evangelical convention.","The USSR had deployed SS-20 intermediate-range missiles in eastern Europe from 1976; NATO decided in December 1979 to answer them.","American Pershing II and cruise missiles arrived in western Europe from November 1983, provoking large peace movement protests, including at Greenham Common.","1 September 1983: a Soviet fighter shot down a Korean Air Lines airliner that had strayed into Soviet airspace, killing all 269 people on board.","November 1983: a NATO command exercise was taken seriously in Moscow as possible cover for a real attack, the closest scare since 1962.","23 March 1983: Reagan announced the Strategic Defence Initiative, a proposed system of satellites and lasers to destroy incoming missiles; the press called it Star Wars.","SDI was never built and the technology did not exist, but it threatened to make mutually assured destruction one-sided.","SDI also cut against the ABM Treaty of 1972, in which both sides had agreed to stay defenceless, which is why Moscow objected so strongly.","The USSR had neither the computing power nor the money to copy SDI, so it became an argument inside Moscow for reform and for arms control."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Reagan and Gorbachev - the four summits, 1985 to 1988","items":["Ronald Reagan took office on 20 January 1981 and met no Soviet leader in his first term: Brezhnev, Andropov and Chernenko all died in office between November 1982 and March 1985.","Geneva was the first meeting between an American president and a Soviet leader since Carter and Brezhnev signed SALT 2 in Vienna in June 1979, a gap of more than six years.","Geneva, 19 to 21 November 1985: the first Reagan-Gorbachev meeting; no treaty, but a joint statement that a nuclear war cannot be won and must never be fought.","Geneva also agreed exchange visits, wider cultural, educational and scientific exchanges, renewed civil aviation talks and consulates in New York and Kiev.","Reykjavik, 11 and 12 October 1986: the two came close to removing whole categories of nuclear weapons, then broke off because Reagan would not confine SDI to the laboratory.","Early in 1987 Gorbachev dropped his insistence that an intermediate-range deal be tied to SDI, which unlocked the treaty.","Washington, 8 December 1987: the INF Treaty abolished all ground-launched missiles with ranges of 500 to 5,500 kilometres, with inspectors allowed onto each other's bases.","2,692 missiles had been destroyed under the INF Treaty by June 1991, the first time a whole class of nuclear weapons was scrapped rather than capped.","Moscow, spring 1988: Reagan, asked in Red Square about his evil empire remark, said it belonged to another time and another era."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Gorbachev's 'new thinking'","items":["Mikhail Gorbachev became General Secretary on 11 March 1985, aged 54, after three elderly leaders had died in quick succession.","Glasnost, openness: criticism allowed in the press, the Stalin years discussed, and the handling of the Chernobyl explosion of April 1986 openly attacked.","Perestroika, restructuring: central planning loosened from 1987, with laws on state enterprises in 1987 and on small co-operative businesses in 1988.","The immediate result of perestroika was shortages and rising prices rather than growth, because planning was weakened before a market existed to replace it.","New thinking in foreign policy: security is common rather than won at another state's expense, and every country should choose its own path.","The Brezhnev Doctrine, asserted after the crushing of the Prague Spring in 1968, had claimed the right to intervene wherever socialism was threatened; new thinking gave that claim up.","The United Nations, 7 December 1988: Gorbachev announced cuts of 500,000 men and the withdrawal and disbanding of six tank divisions from East Germany, Czechoslovakia and Hungary.","The last Soviet troops left Afghanistan on 15 February 1989, so eastern European governments could see that Moscow would not fight to keep a communist government in place.","25 October 1989: the Soviet spokesman Gennadi Gerasimov named the new policy after Frank Sinatra - each country could do it its own way."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the fall of the Berlin Wall","items":["Poland: Round Table talks with Solidarity led to partly free elections on 4 June 1989, which the communists lost heavily; Tadeusz Mazowiecki became prime minister in August 1989.","Hungary began cutting the fence on its Austrian border in May 1989 and opened it to East Germans in September, so the bloc's outer boundary had a hole in it.","East Germany: mass flight through Hungary and Czechoslovakia, huge Monday demonstrations in Leipzig, and Erich Honecker forced to resign on 18 October 1989.","4 November 1989: about half a million people demonstrated on the Alexanderplatz in East Berlin, the largest protest East Germany had seen.","9 November 1989: a confused announcement about new travel rules brought Berliners to the crossing points in such numbers that the guards opened them; the Wall had stood since 1961.","The announcement was made by Gunter Schabowski at an evening press conference; asked when the new rules took effect, he said immediately.","Czechoslovakia's government fell within weeks and Vaclav Havel became president on 29 December 1989; Nicolae Ceausescu was overthrown and executed in Romania in December 1989.","Malta, 2 and 3 December 1989: Bush and Gorbachev met and spoke of the Cold War as over.","Germany was reunified on 3 October 1990, with the whole country inside NATO."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the collapse of the Soviet Union and the end of the Warsaw Pact","items":["In March 1990 the Communist Party's guaranteed monopoly of power was removed from the Soviet constitution and Gorbachev was elected president of the Soviet Union.","Lithuania declared independence in March 1990; Boris Yeltsin was elected president of the Russian republic by popular vote on 12 June 1991.","The Warsaw Pact's military structures were wound up in the spring of 1991, so the alliance lost its command before it lost its treaty.","Comecon was wound up in June 1991; the Warsaw Pact, founded in 1955, was formally dissolved in Prague on 1 July 1991.","A new union treaty, giving the republics far more power at the centre's expense, was due to be signed on 20 August 1991.","19 to 21 August 1991: hardliners from the party, the army and the KGB held Gorbachev at his house in Crimea; troops would not act, Yeltsin defied them in Moscow, and the coup collapsed.","Gorbachev resigned as General Secretary within days and the party's activities were suspended; the republics declared independence one after another.","8 December 1991: the leaders of Russia, Ukraine and Belarus agreed in the Belovezh forest to dissolve the Soviet Union and to set up the Commonwealth of Independent States in its place.","Gorbachev resigned as Soviet president on 25 December 1991 and the Soviet Union ceased to exist on 26 December 1991."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan - Paper 2 booklet P Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the growth of tension between the superpowers in the years 1981-85' (8 marks)","items":["The question as printed: 'Write a narrative account analysing the growth of tension between the superpowers in the years 1981-85.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the Strategic Defence Initiative (March 1983); the Soviet boycott of the Los Angeles Olympics (1984). You must also use information of your own.","Decode it before writing. 'Narrative account' means a sequence that leads to an outcome, so the account must begin and end somewhere definite; 'analysing' means the join between each episode and the next must be explained rather than assumed; and the dates fix the boundaries, so nothing before Reagan's inauguration in January 1981 and nothing after March 1985 belongs here except in the closing sentence.","The cap that decides the level: no access to Level 3 at all for an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content. Decide first which episodes are your own material, and make sure those are the ones you develop.","Episode 1, Washington and Moscow, 1981-82 (own material): Reagan took office on 20 January 1981 promising to bargain only from strength, raised defence spending steeply and left SALT 2 unratified; in Moscow Brezhnev, ill for years, died on 10 November 1982 and was replaced by Yuri Andropov.","Link sentence: because Washington had stopped treating parity as the aim, Soviet leaders had to assume that every American programme was meant to put them behind, and with no summit in prospect there was no way to test that assumption.","Episode 2, March 1983: on 8 March Reagan called the USSR an evil empire before an evangelical audience in Orlando, and on 23 March he announced the Strategic Defence Initiative, research into a space-based system to destroy incoming missiles.","Link sentence: the Strategic Defence Initiative mattered because it might work - a shield would let the USA strike first and survive the answer - and the Soviet economy could neither copy the research nor build enough missiles to swamp it, so Moscow read the announcement as a bid for superiority rather than for safety.","Episode 3, the autumn of 1983 (own material): a Korean airliner was shot down over Soviet territory on 1 September with 269 people on board; American cruise missiles reached Britain and Pershing II missiles West Germany in November; and on 23 November the Soviet delegation walked out of the intermediate-range talks in Geneva.","Link sentence: with the talks abandoned there was no channel left through which either government could explain itself, so each was left to judge the other by its weapons, which is why an exercise or an accident could now be read as preparation for war.","Episode 4, 1984-85: on 8 May 1984 the USSR announced that it would not go to the Los Angeles Olympics, answering the American boycott of Moscow in 1980, and the Soviet leadership changed twice more - Andropov died on 9 February 1984 and Chernenko on 10 March 1985, so Mikhail Gorbachev took over on 11 March 1985.","The closing sentence must say what the sequence amounted to rather than repeat it: in four years the superpowers had moved from a tired détente to a confrontation with no arms talks, no summits and not even sporting contact, and the sequence ended not because either side had won but because the government that could least afford the competition changed hands in March 1985.","Timing and shape: about 13 minutes - two to choose four episodes and mark which are your own, ten to write, one to check that every join carries a sentence of explanation. Four developed episodes beat six thin ones. Use the terms an examiner looks for: détente, the 'Second Cold War', deterrence, first strike, parity, the arms race."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:30:58Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"Re-judged after the fix pass: every fix is present and accurate, the plan and both model paragraphs now work a 1981-85 'growth of tension' narrative that the bank does not set and that neither the 2023 live P4 paper nor the Paper 2 specimen prints, and the new material (the walkout from the Geneva intermediate-range talks on 23 November 1983 with the strategic talks ending on 8 December, the Los Angeles boycott announced on 8 May 1984, Geneva's consulates in New York and Kiev and its exchanges agreement, Brezhnev 10 November 1982, Andropov 9 February 1984 and Chernenko 10 March 1985, Alexanderplatz on 4 November 1989, Article 6 and Gorbachev's presidency in March 1990, the union treaty due on 20 August 1991 and the Belovezh agreement of 8 December 1991) checks out; publish as it stands."}}},"P5-1":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"P5-1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Booklet P keeps coming back to the years 1945 to 1963 because they contain everything the three question types want. The consequences question wants single events whose effects can be traced: a committee report, a bombed hotel, a UN vote, a law, an arms deal. The narrative account wants a short run of years in which one thing visibly forced the next, and 1945 to 1948 and the year 1956 are the two best in the whole option. The importance questions want an event whose effects reached forward: the Law of Return, the armistices of 1949, the Suez Crisis. If you can say with names and dates what the Jews and the Arabs each wanted under the Mandate, how the Mandate ended, how the first war was fought, what it left behind, and how Nasser turned Egypt into the leader of the Arab world, this key topic will carry you through all three questions rather than only one.","idea":[{"h":"One land, two claims: what Jews and Arabs each wanted under the Mandate","t":"Britain had governed Palestine under a League of Nations mandate that came into force in 1923, and it had made promises that could not both be kept. The Balfour Declaration of 2 November 1917 had said Britain viewed with favour a national home for the Jewish people in Palestine without prejudice to the rights of the existing non-Jewish communities. Zionists read that as a state; the Arab majority read the second half as a guarantee. By 1947 Palestine held about 1.9 million people, of whom roughly a third were Jews, and Jewish-owned land was still only a small fraction of the country. The Zionist demand hardened during the war: the Biltmore Program, agreed at a Zionist conference in New York in May 1942, called openly for Palestine to become a Jewish commonwealth with immigration controlled by the Jewish Agency, and after the Holocaust, in which about six million Jews were murdered, immigration was argued for as rescue rather than as policy. The Arab demand was the mirror image: independence for the existing majority, an end to Jewish immigration and land sales, and the honouring of the 1939 White Paper, which had capped immigration at 75,000 over five years and promised an independent Palestine within a decade. The Arab League, founded in Cairo in March 1945, made that demand a regional one. A top-level answer does not list the two sets of demands; it shows why each side's minimum was the other side's defeat, which is why every British compromise between 1945 and 1947 failed.","code":"P5-1 — 1 The British withdrawal and the creation of Israel: Conflicting interests and demands of Jews and Arabs"},{"h":"Three years to breaking point: from the Anglo-American Committee to Resolution 181","t":"Britain came out of the war exhausted and needed Arab goodwill for oil and for the Suez Canal, so Ernest Bevin, Foreign Secretary from July 1945, kept the 1939 immigration limits. President Truman pressed for 100,000 Holocaust survivors to be admitted, and the Anglo-American Committee of Inquiry, reporting in April 1946, recommended exactly that; on 1 May 1946 Attlee told the Commons that so large a body of immigrants could not be admitted unless and until the illegal armies in Palestine were disbanded and their arms surrendered. Jewish resistance turned to force. The Irgun, led by Menachem Begin, bombed the southern wing of the King David Hotel in Jerusalem on 22 July 1946, where the Secretariat of the Government of Palestine and the army headquarters were housed; 91 people were killed, most of them civilians and officials. The bombing was itself a reply to Operation Agatha of 29 June 1946, when British forces had raided the Jewish Agency, interned its officials and carried off its papers; Britain answered the bombing in turn by cordoning Tel Aviv from 30 July 1946 and screening about 100,000 people under curfew for arms and wanted men. In July 1947 the Royal Navy boarded the Exodus, carrying over 4,500 Jewish refugees from France, and the passengers were taken back across Europe and landed in the British zone of Germany in September, which turned world opinion against British policy at exactly the moment the UN was looking at it. The UN Special Committee on Palestine, whose members had seen the Exodus passengers taken off at Haifa, reported on 31 August 1947 recommending partition, and on 29 November 1947 the General Assembly passed Resolution 181 by 33 votes to 13, with 10 abstentions including Britain's. A top-level answer treats these as a chain in which each British attempt at control raised the cost of staying, not as a list of incidents.","code":"P5-1 — 1 The British withdrawal and the creation of Israel: Key events leading to the end of the British Mandate"},{"h":"Civil war, invasion, truces: how the first Arab-Israeli war was actually fought","t":"The war had two halves. From December 1947 to May 1948 Jews and Arabs inside Palestine fought a civil war for the roads and the mixed towns while Britain withdrew and refused to enforce partition. The Jewish forces, better organised under the Haganah and supplied with rifles and aircraft bought from Czechoslovakia, took Tiberias, Haifa on 22 April 1948 and Jaffa in May; the killing of about a hundred villagers at Deir Yassin by Irgun and Lehi fighters on 9 April 1948 spread panic, and some 300,000 Arabs had already left their homes before the state existed. David Ben-Gurion declared the State of Israel in Tel Aviv on 14 May 1948, hours before the Mandate ended; the United States recognised it the same day and the USSR days later. On 15 May the armies of Egypt, Transjordan, Syria, Lebanon and Iraq entered the country. The UN mediator Count Folke Bernadotte arranged a four-week truce from 11 June, and a second truce from 18 July; Israel used both to import arms and to expand its forces towards 100,000 men, while the Arab states, divided in their aims, did not. Bernadotte was assassinated by Lehi gunmen on 17 September 1948. Israeli offensives in the Negev in October and December 1948 ended the fighting. A top-level answer explains why the truces and the divisions among the Arab states mattered more than any single battle.","code":"P5-1 — 1 The British withdrawal and the creation of Israel: Key events of the Arab-Israeli war (1948–49)"},{"h":"The map after 1949, and the people left off it","t":"The war ended not in peace treaties but in four armistices negotiated through the UN acting mediator Ralph Bunche: with Egypt on Rhodes on 24 February 1949, with Lebanon at Ras en-Naqoura on 23 March, with Jordan on Rhodes on 3 April and with Syria near Mahanayim on 20 July. They fixed military lines, not frontiers, and no Arab state recognised Israel. Israel finished holding about 78 per cent of mandate Palestine, considerably more than Resolution 181 had allotted it, including western Jerusalem and the whole Negev. The Arab state the UN had planned never appeared: Egypt held the Gaza Strip under military administration, and Transjordan, which renamed itself Jordan in 1949, held the West Bank and eastern Jerusalem and annexed them in 1950. About 700,000 Palestinian Arabs had left or been driven from their homes, and Israel refused to let them return, so the UN created the Relief and Works Agency in December 1949, which began work in May 1950 and ran camps and schools in Gaza, the West Bank, Jordan, Lebanon and Syria. The refugees became a permanent political fact: a population with no state and no right of return, living on the armistice lines, from which fedayeen raids were launched. A top-level answer links the map to the people, showing that armistice lines without a settlement guaranteed both refugee camps and the next war.","code":"P5-1 — 2 Aftermath of the 1948–49 war: Territorial changes and their impact"},{"h":"Building a state: one army, one door open, and money from abroad","t":"Israel had to turn a coalition of militias into a government's army. An order of 26 May 1948 created the Israeli Defence Forces as the state's only armed force; the Palmach was disbanded, the Irgun and Lehi were broken up, and when the Irgun tried to land its own arms on the Altalena in June 1948 the new army shelled the ship. The Defence Service Law of 1949 conscripted men and women, so the small population could field a large trained reserve. The Law of Return, passed by the Knesset on 5 July 1950, gave every Jew anywhere the right to settle in Israel, making unlimited immigration a legal right rather than a policy that a government could change; more than 600,000 immigrants arrived in the first three years, roughly doubling the Jewish population, many of them Holocaust survivors and Jews from Yemen, Iraq and North Africa who were housed in tent and hut camps under a rationing programme. Paying for this depended on outside money: an Export-Import Bank loan of 100 million dollars from the United States in January 1949, fundraising among American Jews organised with the Jewish Agency, and from 1952 West German reparations of 3,000 million marks over twelve years. A top-level answer shows how the army, the law and the money together turned a declaration into a working state.","code":"P5-1 — 2 Aftermath of the 1948–49 war: The creation of the Israeli Defence Forces and the Law of Return"},{"h":"Neighbours at armistice: Israel and Egypt between the wars","t":"Egypt had lost the war and did not accept the result. It refused recognition, barred Israeli ships and Israeli cargoes from the Suez Canal and, from 1953, tightened a blockade of the Straits of Tiran that cut Israel's southern port of Eilat off from the Red Sea. The UN Security Council called on Egypt in September 1951 to lift the restrictions on shipping; Egypt ignored the resolution, and Israel drew the lesson that the UN would not deliver what a state could take. Along the Gaza armistice line there were constant infiltrations, some by refugees returning to their fields, some armed, and Israel replied with reprisal raids designed to make the Egyptian authorities stop them. The Free Officers' coup of 23 July 1952 removed King Farouk and eventually brought Gamal Abdel Nasser to power, and the Anglo-Egyptian agreement of October 1954 arranged for British troops to leave the canal base, which removed the buffer that British occupation had provided between the two states. A top-level answer explains the mechanism: no peace treaty plus a blockade plus raids and reprisals meant the armistice was a pause that both sides expected to end.","code":"P5-1 — 2 Aftermath of the 1948–49 war: Israel's relations with Egypt"},{"h":"Nasser: how one leader made Cairo the capital of Arab nationalism","t":"Nasser became prime minister in 1954 and president in 1956, and he built his authority on standing up to outsiders. He denounced the Baghdad Pact of February 1955 as a way of keeping the Arab world inside Western defence plans, and used the Cairo radio station Voice of the Arabs to speak over the heads of other Arab governments to their peoples. At Bandung in April 1955 he placed Egypt among the non-aligned states, and when the West would not sell him modern weapons he announced on 27 September 1955 that Egypt would buy MiG-15 fighters, Ilyushin bombers and T-34 tanks through Czechoslovakia, breaking the Western monopoly on arms in the region. After the United States and Britain withdrew their offer to fund the Aswan High Dam on 19 July 1956 he nationalised the Suez Canal Company a week later. Surviving the war that followed made him the hero of Arab nationalism: Syria asked for union, and the United Arab Republic was proclaimed on 1 February 1958. Its limits showed too, since Syria left the union on 28 September 1961 and Egyptian troops were drawn into the civil war in Yemen from 1962. A top-level answer weighs the prestige against the results, rather than describing the speeches.","code":"P5-1 — 3 Increased tension, 1955–63: Nasser and Egypt's leadership of the Arab world"},{"h":"Reprisal and invasion: Gaza in 1955, Sinai in 1956","t":"On 28 February 1955 Israeli paratroopers attacked an Egyptian army position in Gaza, killing about 38 Egyptian soldiers for the loss of eight of their own. It was meant to force Egypt to police the armistice line; it did the opposite. The raid exposed how badly armed the Egyptian army was, so Nasser went shopping for Soviet-bloc weapons and reached the Czech arms deal seven months later, and Egypt began to organise the fedayeen raids it had previously tried to limit. Israel's leaders then concluded that the arms balance would shift against them within a year or two and that a war fought soon would be cheaper than a war fought late. That is the logic behind the invasion of Sinai on 29 October 1956, which was agreed in advance with Britain and France. In eight days Israeli forces took the Sinai peninsula, the Gaza Strip and Sharm el-Sheikh at the mouth of the Straits of Tiran. The military result was decisive and the political result was mixed: Israel had to give the territory back by March 1957, but it gained freedom of navigation to Eilat and about a decade of quiet on the Egyptian border. A top-level answer measures each attack against what it was meant to achieve.","code":"P5-1 — 3 Increased tension, 1955–63: Israeli attacks on Gaza in 1955 and Sinai in 1956"},{"h":"Suez: collusion, invasion, humiliation, and what followed","t":"Nasser announced the nationalisation of the Suez Canal Company in Alexandria on 26 July 1956, promising to compensate the shareholders and to use the tolls for the Aswan Dam. Anthony Eden's government treated him as a dictator who had to be stopped. At secret meetings at Sèvres near Paris on 22 to 24 October 1956, Britain, France and Israel agreed a plan: Israel would attack Egypt, and the two European powers would then order both sides away from the canal and occupy the zone as peacekeepers. Israel invaded on 29 October, the ultimatum followed on 30 October, Anglo-French bombing began on 31 October, and paratroops landed at Port Said on 5 November. The operation was stopped not by Egypt but by Israel's and Britain's friends: a run on sterling that the United States refused to relieve, Soviet threats, and a hostile vote at the UN forced a ceasefire on 7 November. A UN Emergency Force was created on 5 November and arrived within days; British and French troops were out by 22 December, Israeli troops by March 1957. Eden resigned in January 1957. Nasser, defeated in the field, emerged as the man who had beaten two empires, and the United States moved into the space Britain had lost with the Eisenhower Doctrine of January 1957, while Syria's request for union produced the United Arab Republic in February 1958. A top-level answer judges the crisis by the standing of each participant afterwards.","code":"P5-1 — 3 Increased tension, 1955–63: The events and significance of the Suez Crisis (1956)"}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1(a): turning the declaration of the State of Israel into one explained consequence","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(a): 'Explain one consequence of the declaration of the State of Israel on 14 May 1948.' [4 marks]. Question 1 comes in two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each on a separate event and each marked on its own out of four, so this part needs one consequence analysed and supported. The information you are working from: Jewish and Arab forces inside Palestine had been fighting since December 1947 and some 300,000 Arabs had already left their homes; David Ben-Gurion read the declaration in Tel Aviv on 14 May 1948, hours before the British Mandate expired; the United States recognised the new state the same day and the USSR within days; the armies of Egypt, Transjordan, Syria, Lebanon and Iraq entered Palestine on 15 May 1948; the fighting was halted by UN truces in June and July 1948 and ended in armistice agreements signed with four Arab states between February and July 1949.","steps":["Fix the moment, because a consequence has to be true of what came afterwards: the declaration was read on 14 May 1948 and the Mandate expired that same night, so no outside government was responsible for Palestine any longer.","Choose a consequence that is a change of state rather than the next thing in the story: before the declaration this was a communal war inside a territory Britain governed, and after it, it was a war between states.","Support it with the precise information: five Arab states sent regular armies across the frontier the next day, and both superpowers recognised the government those armies were attacking.","Make the link the grid calls analysis: because the conflict was now between recognised governments, it could no longer be damped down by a colonial administration or settled locally, and could only be stopped by negotiation between states, which is what the UN truces and then the armistices were."],"cues":["What else ended on the night the declaration was read?","What kind of war was being fought before, and what kind afterwards?","Which two pieces of evidence show that change within days?","Why must a war between recognised states end differently from a communal one?"]},{"title":"Question 1(b): the Anglo-Egyptian agreement of 1954, and the difference between describing and explaining","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(b): 'Explain one consequence of the Anglo-Egyptian agreement of October 1954.' [4 marks]. The information you are working from: British troops had long been stationed in the Suez Canal base, the largest British military base in the region; under the agreement of October 1954 Britain undertook to withdraw them within twenty months, and the last troops left in June 1956; the canal itself was run by the Anglo-French owned Suez Canal Company; Nasser nationalised that company on 26 July 1956; when Britain and France decided to take the canal zone back by force they had to mount the operation from Malta and Cyprus, and their troops did not land at Port Said until 5 November 1956. A student writes: 'A consequence of the Anglo-Egyptian agreement was that Britain agreed to withdraw its troops from the Suez Canal base within twenty months.' Say why that sentence cannot reach the top of the grid, and rewrite it so that it can.","steps":["Test the sentence against the question: it repeats a term of the agreement, so it says what was agreed rather than what followed from it.","Find the change of state instead: once the last troops had gone, Britain had no garrison in Egypt, so anything it wanted to protect there had to be defended from outside the country.","Follow that forward to the moment it mattered: when the canal company was seized, Britain had no force on the spot to answer with and had to assemble one at Mediterranean bases, which took months rather than days.","Rewrite it with the link showing: because the agreement removed the garrison, Nasser could take the company knowing no British troops stood in the canal zone, and any British reply would be slow enough for opposition at home, at the UN and in Washington to build against it."],"cues":["Does the student's sentence say what happened next, or only what was agreed?","What could Britain no longer do in Egypt once the base was empty?","Where did the force that attacked in 1956 have to be assembled?","How would you put the delay and the seizure together in one explaining sentence?"]},{"title":"Question 2: choosing the episodes for a narrative account of Egypt and Israel, 1949-56","setup":"Booklet P, Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the growth of tension between Egypt and Israel in the years 1949-56.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: Egypt's blockade of Israeli shipping; the Czech arms deal (1955). You must also use information of your own. The material available: the armistice with Egypt was signed on 24 February 1949 but brought no recognition and no peace treaty; Egypt barred Israeli ships and cargoes from the Suez Canal and from 1953 blockaded the Straits of Tiran, closing the southern port of Eilat; the UN Security Council called on Egypt in September 1951 to lift the canal restrictions and was ignored; there was constant infiltration across the Gaza armistice line and Israel answered with reprisal raids; Israeli paratroopers attacked an Egyptian position in Gaza on 28 February 1955, killing about 38 Egyptian soldiers; Nasser announced the Czech arms deal on 27 September 1955; Egypt then organised the fedayeen raids it had previously tried to restrain; France began supplying Israel with modern aircraft in 1956; Israel invaded Sinai on 29 October 1956. The mark scheme prints one cap: no access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","steps":["Read the cap as an instruction about coverage: the two bullets count as two aspects, so the account needs at least one more strand of its own before it can be judged at the top level.","Pick a spine of episodes in order that reaches the outcome named in the question: the armistice that settled nothing, the blockades and the UN's failure to lift them, the raid on Gaza and the arms deal that answered it, and the slide into invasion.","Beside each episode write the link forward rather than a description: an armistice without recognition left every dispute to be settled by force, the blockades gave Israel a grievance no resolution would remove, the raid showed Egypt how weak its army was and sent Nasser to the Soviet bloc for weapons, and those weapons convinced Israeli commanders to fight before the balance turned.","Close on what the sequence amounted to: the armistice years left both governments readier for war than they had been at the start, because every measure each took for its own security gave the other a reason to arm."],"cues":["How many strands does the account need before the cap stops applying?","Which four moments, in order, carry the story to the invasion?","For each moment, what did it make the other side do next?","What single sentence says what the whole period added up to?"]},{"title":"Question 3: explaining the importance of the United Nations Emergency Force for the Egyptian border","setup":"Booklet P, Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of eight, with no stimulus points. This example builds one bullet: 'the importance of the United Nations Emergency Force for the Egyptian-Israeli border in the years after the Suez Crisis.' (8 marks) The information you are working from: the General Assembly created the force on 5 November 1956 and its first contingents flew into Egypt on 15 and 16 November; Israel would not accept it on its own side of the line, so it could be stationed only on the Egyptian side; it moved into the Gaza Strip in the first week of March 1957 as Israeli troops pulled out, and to Sharm el-Sheikh at the mouth of the Straits of Tiran a few days later, where it kept the straits under observation; Egypt still barred Israeli ships from the Suez Canal, but the southern port of Eilat stayed open; before 1956 the Gaza line had seen constant infiltration, fedayeen raids and Israeli reprisals; afterwards incidents on that frontier were few and minor for about a decade; the force remained only for as long as the Egyptian government consented to its presence.","steps":["Fix what the question measures: set the frontier of the early fifties beside the frontier of the years that followed, and explain the difference the force made rather than describing what it was.","Take the first line forward, the policing one: with an outside force holding the Egyptian side of the line from March 1957, a crossing became an incident to be reported and investigated instead of a reason for a reprisal raid, and the quietest years the Gaza frontier had known followed.","Take a second line forward, the economic one: a detachment at Sharm el-Sheikh watched the entrance to the Gulf of Aqaba, so the straits could not be closed again without first removing the force, and Israel kept the sea route to Eilat that it had gone to war in 1956 to open.","Take a third line forward, the political one: the force stood on one side of the line only and stayed by Egyptian consent, so it removed the occasions of war without removing the quarrel, and the government that had admitted it could ask for it to go.","Judge it: the force mattered because it made the border quiet without making it safe, buying both states years in which neither had to answer a raid, while leaving every question that had caused the raids exactly where it was."],"cues":["What two periods are you comparing, and which one is the question really about?","What happened to a crossing of the line once an outside force was watching it?","Which waterway did the detachment in the south observe, and which port depended on it?","Whose permission kept the force in place, and what does that make the calm it kept?","In one sentence, what did the force settle and what did it leave untouched?"]},{"title":"Question 3: judging the importance of the Suez Crisis for Britain","setup":"Booklet P, Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of eight, with no stimulus points. This example builds one bullet: 'the importance of the Suez Crisis (1956) for Britain's position in the Middle East.' (8 marks) The information you are working from: Britain, France and Israel agreed their plan in secret at Sèvres from 22 to 24 October 1956; Israel invaded Sinai on 29 October, and Anglo-French aircraft bombed Egyptian airfields before troops landed at Port Said; the operation was halted by a run on sterling that the United States refused to relieve, by Soviet threats and by a hostile vote at the UN, and a ceasefire took effect on 7 November; British and French troops were out of Egypt by 22 December 1956; Anthony Eden resigned in January 1957; the Eisenhower Doctrine, announced on 5 January 1957, offered American money and military help against communist aggression in the region; Nasser kept the canal and its revenues; the Iraqi monarchy, Britain's main Arab ally and a member of the Baghdad Pact, was overthrown on 14 July 1958.","steps":["Fix what the question is measuring: not who won the fighting, but what Britain could still do in the region afterwards that it had been able to do before.","Take the first line forward, the question of independence: the operation was stopped by Washington rather than by the Egyptian army, which showed every government in the region that Britain could not act in the Middle East without American consent.","Take a second line forward, the question of allies: the collusion became public, so Arab governments that had worked with Britain stood exposed as partners of the power that had attacked Egypt, and the monarchy in Iraq that anchored British influence was overthrown less than two years later.","Take a third line forward, the question of replacement: the United States moved into the space Britain had lost, offering money and protection of its own, so the region's quarrels came to be managed from Washington and Moscow instead.","Judge it: Britain's decline in the Middle East had begun before the crisis, but Suez made it public and irreversible, which is why the episode matters more as a demonstration than as a defeat."],"cues":["What exactly are you being asked to measure here?","Who actually stopped the operation, and what did that prove?","What did the collusion do to the standing of Britain's friends in the region?","Who filled the space Britain gave up?","Was this the cause of Britain's decline, or the proof of it?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What did the Anglo-American Committee of Inquiry recommend in 1946, and what did Britain do about it?","a":"It recommended that 100,000 Jewish survivors of the Holocaust be admitted to Palestine at once. Truman backed the recommendation; Attlee told the Commons on 1 May 1946 that so large a body of immigrants could not be admitted until the illegal armies in Palestine were disbanded and their arms surrendered, so the certificates were not issued."},{"q":"Give the date of UN Resolution 181 and the voting figures.","a":"29 November 1947: thirty-three votes in favour, thirteen against and ten abstentions, Britain among the abstainers."},{"q":"Which four armistice agreements ended the 1948-49 war, and why were they not peace treaties?","a":"Egypt on 24 February 1949, Lebanon on 23 March, Jordan on 3 April and Syria on 20 July 1949. They fixed military lines and ended the fighting, but no Arab state recognised Israel and no frontier or refugee settlement was agreed."},{"q":"What did Nasser announce on 27 September 1955 and on 26 July 1956?","a":"On 27 September 1955 that Egypt would buy MiG-15 fighters, Ilyushin bombers and T-34 tanks through Czechoslovakia; on 26 July 1956 that the Suez Canal Company was nationalised, a week after the United States and Britain withdrew their offer to fund the Aswan High Dam."}]},"room":{"facts":["Booklet P is worth 32 marks and has three questions: Question 1, printed in two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each 'Explain one consequence of...' for 4 marks and each on a separate event or development; Question 2, 'Write a narrative account analysing...', 8 marks; Question 3, 'Explain two of the following: the importance of ... for ...', two chosen parts at 8 marks each, 16 in all.","Only Question 2 prints stimulus points, as two bullets followed by 'You must also use information of your own.' The mark scheme prints one cap on it: 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' There is no cap at Level 2, and Questions 1 and 3 print no bullets and carry no cap.","Each part of Question 1 is marked on its own two-level grid out of 4: Level 2 (3-4) needs features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific information added in support. Two accurate descriptions of the event stay in Level 1.","Question 3 is marked twice on a three-level grid out of 8, and Level 3 (6-8) needs analysis of importance in a coherent, logically structured line of reasoning: what difference the event, person or development made to later situations, not what happened.","Timing: Paper 2 lasts 1 hour 50 minutes for 64 marks, about 55 minutes a booklet, so booklet P's 32 marks get roughly a minute a mark plus planning - about 12 minutes on Question 1's two parts, 13 on Question 2 and 14 on each part of Question 3, leaving a couple of minutes to check. No sources are printed on Paper 2 and there are no SPaG marks on it."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the report of the Anglo-American Committee of Inquiry in 1946. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid; Level 2 (3-4) needs features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific support. Credit the open split between Britain and the United States over the 100,000 certificates, the loss of Zionist faith in negotiation with Britain and the turn to armed action, or the shift of Zionist lobbying towards Washington.","sol":"One consequence was an open split between Britain and the United States over Palestine. The committee reported in April 1946 that 100,000 Holocaust survivors should be admitted at once, and Truman publicly backed that recommendation, but Attlee told the Commons on 1 May 1946 that the certificates could not be issued until the illegal armies were disbanded and their arms surrendered, because Britain could not hold the country and face another Arab revolt as well. Because the Americans had endorsed a promise the British would not keep, Zionist leaders concluded that pressing London was pointless and directed their effort at Washington instead, leaving Britain holding the Mandate without the support of the ally it depended on financially.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["A consequence is something that was true afterwards because of the report, so ask what the report changed rather than what it said.","Two governments are involved here, and they did not react in the same way; the gap between them is where the consequences lie.","Build it in three moves: name the consequence, give the detail that proves it happened, then explain why it mattered for what came next."],"codes":["P5-1 — 1 The British withdrawal and the creation of Israel: Conflicting interests and demands of Jews and Arabs","P5-1 — 1 The British withdrawal and the creation of Israel: Key events leading to the end of the British Mandate"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of the passing of UN Resolution 181 in November 1947. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the outbreak of civil war inside Palestine from December 1947, the British decision to withdraw by 15 May 1948 without enforcing partition, the international legal basis it gave the Jewish state, or the Arab rejection that made a negotiated partition impossible.","sol":"One consequence was that the quarrel turned from a political argument into a civil war for territory. The General Assembly voted on 29 November 1947 by thirty-three to thirteen with ten abstentions to divide Palestine, the Jewish Agency accepted the plan and the Arab Higher Committee and the Arab League rejected it, while Britain abstained and made clear it would not impose a settlement that both sides had not accepted. Because a partition nobody would enforce could only be decided by whoever held the ground when the Mandate ended, both communities began fighting for the roads and the mixed towns within days, and that fighting ran on until the British left in May 1948.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what a vote at the UN can and cannot do when no power is willing to send troops to carry it out.","Look at the three reactions to the plan - the Jewish Agency's, the Arab leaders' and Britain's - and ask what they left unsettled.","State the consequence as a change of situation, prove it with the vote and the reactions, then explain the link forward to what happened on the ground."],"codes":["P5-1 — 1 The British withdrawal and the creation of Israel: Key events leading to the end of the British Mandate"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Law of Return (1950). [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the permanent legal commitment to unlimited Jewish immigration and the strain of absorbing it, the doubling of the Jewish population and the transit camps and rationing, the manpower it gave the conscript army, or the contrast with the Palestinian refugees who were refused return.","sol":"One consequence was that Israel lost the power to control the pace of its own immigration. The Knesset's law of 5 July 1950 made settlement a right belonging to every Jew rather than a permit the government issued, so as the arrivals continued - more than 600,000 by the end of 1951, roughly doubling the Jewish population - the state had to house and feed them whatever the cost. That is why the Jewish Agency and the government put the newcomers from the European camps, Yemen and Iraq into the ma'abarot, the transit camps of tents and tin huts built from 1950, and why the austerity rationing begun in 1949 had to be kept in force.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The difference between a right and a permit is the whole answer here: ask what a government can no longer do once entry is a right.","Think about what arrives with people rather than about the people themselves - housing, food, work and the money to pay for them.","Name the consequence, prove it with the numbers and the conditions the new arrivals met, then explain why that followed from the law itself."],"codes":["P5-1 — 2 Aftermath of the 1948–49 war: The creation of the Israeli Defence Forces and the Law of Return"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of Egypt's refusal to open the Suez Canal to Israeli shipping after 1949. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit Israel's turn to the Red Sea route and the development of Eilat, the extension of the blockade to the Straits of Tiran from 1953, the failure of the UN Security Council's call in 1951 and the lesson Israel drew from it, or the creation of a standing Israeli war aim that was pursued in 1956.","sol":"One consequence was that Israel came to depend on the Red Sea, and so became vulnerable at a second point. Barred from the canal, Israeli trade with Asia and Africa had to move through the southern port of Eilat, which made the Straits of Tiran the state's economic lifeline; Egypt then blockaded the straits from 1953 as well, closing that route too. The UN Security Council called on Egypt in September 1951 to lift the restrictions and Egypt ignored it, which taught Israeli leaders that a blockade would not be removed by resolutions. Opening the straits by force therefore became a standing Israeli war aim, pursued in the Sinai campaign of 1956.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["If one sea route is closed to a country, ask which one it must use instead, and what that does to its map of weak points.","The consequence you want runs forward several years; ask what Israel still wanted in 1956 that it had lost after 1949.","Give the consequence, support it with the two blockades and the UN's failure, then explain why that combination pointed towards force."],"codes":["P5-1 — 2 Aftermath of the 1948–49 war: Israel's relations with Egypt"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Exodus affair of 1947. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the work of the United Nations Special Committee on Palestine (UNSCOP) in 1947. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4 on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the collapse of sympathy for British policy abroad, the strengthening of the Zionist case before UNSCOP, or the further undermining of Britain's will to hold the Mandate. In 1(b) credit the majority recommendation of partition on 31 August 1947 and the plan it handed the General Assembly, the timetable it set for British withdrawal, or the international endorsement it gave the idea of a Jewish state.","sol":"1(a) The affair destroyed the public case for British policy. The Royal Navy boarded the ship in July 1947, over 4,500 Jewish refugees were taken off at Haifa, shipped back to France and finally landed in the British zone of Germany that September, and the newsreels showed Jewish survivors being returned to German soil two years after the camps. Because members of UNSCOP were at Haifa and saw it, the episode strengthened the argument that only a Jewish state could control Jewish immigration, and it made it harder for British ministers to defend the Mandate either at the UN or to American opinion. 1(b) The committee's work turned an unmanageable British problem into a UN plan. Reporting on 31 August 1947, its majority recommended dividing Palestine into a Jewish and an Arab state with Jerusalem under international control, and that recommendation became the basis of Resolution 181 three months later. Because the UN now owned a scheme of its own, Britain could hand the Mandate back rather than negotiate a settlement, and announced that it would leave by 15 May 1948 without enforcing the plan.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate parts on two separate things: a ship in 1(a) and a committee in 1(b), so neither answer can do the other's work.","For 1(a) the consequence is about opinion, not about the refugees: ask who was watching, and what they could no longer be told.","For 1(b) a committee produces a document, so ask what that document allowed other people to do that they could not do before."],"codes":["P5-1 — 1 The British withdrawal and the creation of Israel: Key events leading to the end of the British Mandate","P5-1 — 1 The British withdrawal and the creation of Israel: Conflicting interests and demands of Jews and Arabs"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Arab-Israeli war of 1948-49 for the Palestinian Arabs. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the armistice agreements of 1949. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4. In 1(a) credit the flight of about 700,000 refugees and Israel's refusal to allow return, the failure of the planned Arab state and the division of its territory between Egypt and Transjordan, or the creation of UNRWA and the camps. In 1(b) credit lines without recognition or frontiers, Israel's enlarged territory of about 78 per cent of mandate Palestine, the division of Jerusalem, or the raids and reprisals that followed along the lines.","sol":"1(a) The war left the Palestinian Arabs as a people without a state. About 700,000 left or were driven from their homes during the fighting, Israel refused to let them return, and the territory the UN had set aside for an Arab state was taken instead by Egypt in Gaza and by Transjordan on the West Bank, which annexed it in 1950. Because no government represented them and no return was allowed, the UN had to create a relief agency in December 1949 to run camps, rations and schools, so the refugee population became a permanent political fact on Israel's borders rather than a temporary emergency. 1(b) The armistices ended the fighting without settling anything. Signed with Egypt in February 1949 and with Lebanon, Jordan and Syria by that July, they drew military lines rather than frontiers, and no Arab state recognised Israel or exchanged ambassadors with it. Because Israel now held about 78 per cent of mandate Palestine, far more than Resolution 181 had allotted, the Arab governments treated the outcome as a temporary defeat; the lines ran through villages and past refugee camps, so infiltration and reprisal raids became the normal condition of the next seven years.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two parts, two subjects: in 1(a) a people, in 1(b) a set of documents, so keep the evidence for each inside its own part.","For 1(a) ask what happened to the state the UN had planned, and who ended up governing the land it was meant to occupy.","For 1(b) compare what an armistice does with what a peace treaty does, and ask what was left unresolved as a result."],"codes":["P5-1 — 1 The British withdrawal and the creation of Israel: Key events of the Arab-Israeli war (1948–49)","P5-1 — 2 Aftermath of the 1948–49 war: Territorial changes and their impact"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the creation of the Israeli Defence Forces in 1948. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the Tripartite Declaration of 1950. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4. In 1(a) credit the government's monopoly of armed force and the ending of the separate militias, including the shelling of the Altalena, the conscription of men and women under the 1949 law, or the trained reserve that a small population could field. In 1(b) credit Western control of the regional arms balance and the refusal of Egyptian requests, the stabilising of the armistice lines for several years, or the incentive it gave Nasser to seek weapons from the Soviet bloc in 1955.","sol":"1(a) The order of 26 May 1948 gave the new government a monopoly of armed force. The Haganah became the state's army, the Palmach was disbanded, and when the Irgun tried to land weapons of its own on the Altalena in June 1948 the new army shelled the ship rather than allow a second armed body to exist. Because command now ran from the cabinet rather than from party militias, Israel could conscript men and women under the Defence Service Law of 1949 and hold a trained reserve far larger than its population suggested, which is why it could mobilise quickly in the crises that followed. 1(b) The declaration of 25 May 1950 put the regional arms balance in Western hands. The United States, Britain and France declared against the use of force, promised to act if the armistice lines were violated, and agreed to regulate what they sold; as the only large suppliers of modern weapons, they could hold both sides at roughly the level they chose. Because Egypt could not buy what it wanted inside that system, Nasser went outside it, and the Czech arms deal of September 1955 destroyed the arrangement at a stroke.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Part 1(a) is about who is allowed to carry weapons inside a state; part 1(b) is about who is allowed to sell them to states.","For 1(a) remember there were several armed groups in 1948, and ask what had to happen to them for one army to exist.","For 1(b) ask what happens to an agreement between suppliers when a buyer finds a supplier who never signed it."],"codes":["P5-1 — 2 Aftermath of the 1948–49 war: The creation of the Israeli Defence Forces and the Law of Return","P5-1 — 2 Aftermath of the 1948–49 war: Israel's relations with Egypt"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Baghdad Pact of 1955. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the nationalisation of the Suez Canal Company in July 1956. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4. In 1(a) credit Nasser's campaign against Western defence pacts and the standing it gave Cairo in the Arab world, the isolation of Iraq as the only Arab member, or Jordan's decision to stay out and the dismissal of Glubb in 1956. In 1(b) credit Eden's determination to remove Nasser and the secret planning at Sèvres, the Anglo-French-Israeli invasion, the blocking and later reopening of the canal, or Nasser's enhanced prestige in the Arab world.","sol":"1(a) The pact divided the Arab states into those that would join Western defence plans and those that followed Cairo. Turkey and Iraq signed it in February 1955 and Britain, Pakistan and Iran joined during that year, which left Iraq as the only Arab member, and Nasser attacked it nightly on Voice of the Arabs as a device for keeping the Arab world under Western command. Because Egypt could now define Arab nationalism as opposition to such pacts, the pressure fell on any government thinking of joining: Jordan, swept by riots in December 1955, stayed out, and King Hussein dismissed Glubb, the British commander of the Arab Legion, in March 1956. 1(b) Nationalisation on 26 July 1956 turned a quarrel over a waterway into a European war plan. The canal company was Anglo-French owned, and Eden treated Nasser's seizure of it, a week after the Aswan Dam funding was withdrawn, as a threat that had to be removed. Because neither Britain nor France could attack without a pretext, they met Israel in secret at Sèvres in October and agreed that an Israeli attack would give them one, which is how the nationalisation led directly to the invasion of Egypt at the end of October.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["In 1(a) the change is about which side of a divided Arab world a government had to choose; in 1(b) it is about what a government decides to do when an asset it owns is taken.","For 1(a) ask what a defence pact signed by only one Arab state did for the standing of the government in Cairo that attacked it.","For 1(b) ask what Britain and France still lacked after July, and where they went to get it."],"codes":["P5-1 — 3 Increased tension, 1955–63: Nasser and Egypt's leadership of the Arab world","P5-1 — 3 Increased tension, 1955–63: The events and significance of the Suez Crisis (1956)"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the key events in Palestine in the years 1945-48 that led to the end of the British Mandate. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the report of the Anglo-American Committee of Inquiry (1946); UN Resolution 181 (November 1947). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) needs a clear sequence of events leading to an outcome with the linkage between them analysed. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; own material might include Bevin's immigration policy, Operation Agatha and the King David Hotel bombing that answered it, the cordon and search of Tel Aviv that followed the bombing, the Exodus, the referral to the UN in February 1947, UNSCOP's report, and the announcement of withdrawal by 15 May 1948.","sol":"Britain had come out of the war too poor to buy off either side. Bevin kept the 1939 limits on Jewish immigration to hold Arab goodwill for oil and the canal, so when the Anglo-American Committee recommended in April 1946 that 100,000 survivors be admitted and Truman endorsed it, Attlee's answer in the Commons on 1 May - that they could not come until the illegal armies were disbanded and their arms surrendered - convinced the Zionist leadership that negotiation with London would yield nothing. Britain then struck first: in Operation Agatha on 29 June 1946 troops raided the Jewish Agency, interned its officials and seized its papers. The Irgun's answer was the bomb of 22 July in the King David Hotel, aimed at those papers, which killed 91 people; Britain replied by cordoning Tel Aviv from 30 July and screening some 100,000 people. Control was restored only by tying down a garrison a rationed Britain could not afford, and in February 1947 Bevin handed the question to the UN. The next blow was to reputation: when the Royal Navy turned back the Exodus in July 1947 and its passengers, over 4,500 of them, were landed in the British zone of Germany that September, the claim that Britain was acting humanely collapsed. UNSCOP recommended partition on 31 August, and when Resolution 181 passed on 29 November Britain abstained and would not enforce it, leaving both communities to fight for the ground. Each answer had raised the cost of staying, so Britain gave up the Mandate, which expired at midnight on 14-15 May 1948.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Start before the bullets: what had Britain's position been since 1939, and why could it not simply buy its way out after 1945?","The account needs joins, not just dates - at each stage ask what the last event made impossible for Britain.","Plan four episodes with a connective sentence between each, so that the answer addresses three or more aspects of content and not only the two bullets."],"codes":["P5-1 — 1 The British withdrawal and the creation of Israel: Key events leading to the end of the British Mandate","P5-1 — 1 The British withdrawal and the creation of Israel: Conflicting interests and demands of Jews and Arabs"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the key events of the Arab-Israeli war in the years 1948-49. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the declaration of the state of Israel (14 May 1948); the truces arranged by the United Nations. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) needs a clear sequence leading to an outcome with the linkage analysed. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; own material might include the civil war of 1947-48 and Deir Yassin, the invasion of 15 May by five Arab states, the arms bought from Czechoslovakia, the divisions among the Arab governments, the Israeli offensives in the Negev, and the armistices of 1949.","sol":"The war began before the state did. From December 1947 Jewish and Arab forces fought for the roads and the mixed towns while Britain withdrew, and the better organised Haganah took Tiberias, Haifa on 22 April 1948 and Jaffa in May; the killing of about a hundred villagers at Deir Yassin by Irgun and Lehi fighters on 9 April spread panic, and some 300,000 Arabs had left their homes before the Mandate ended. Ben-Gurion's declaration in Tel Aviv on 14 May therefore came with much of the territory already held, and the entry of Egyptian, Transjordanian, Syrian, Lebanese and Iraqi forces the next day turned a civil war into an international one. The Arab armies made their gains early, when Israel was short of heavy weapons, so the UN truces mattered more than any battle: during the four weeks from 11 June and again after 18 July, Israel imported rifles and aircraft bought from Czechoslovakia and expanded its army towards 100,000 men, while the Arab governments, each pursuing its own territorial aim, did not coordinate rearmament. When fighting resumed the balance had reversed, and Israeli offensives in the Negev in October and December 1948 drove the Egyptians back. The result was armistices signed from February to July 1949 on lines drawn by that reversal rather than by Resolution 181.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The war has two halves with a declaration between them; decide where each half starts before you write anything.","Ask what each side did with the pauses in the fighting, and why that matters more than any single engagement.","Finish on the shape of the outcome, not on a date: whose lines were they, and why were they where they were?"],"codes":["P5-1 — 1 The British withdrawal and the creation of Israel: Key events of the Arab-Israeli war (1948–49)","P5-1 — 2 Aftermath of the 1948–49 war: Territorial changes and their impact"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the key events of the Suez Crisis in 1956. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the nationalisation of the Suez Canal Company (July 1956); the Israeli invasion of Sinai (October 1956). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) needs a clear sequence leading to an outcome with the linkage analysed. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; own material might include the withdrawal of Aswan Dam funding on 19 July, the secret meetings at Sèvres, the Anglo-French ultimatum and bombing, the landings at Port Said, American and Soviet pressure and the run on sterling, the ceasefire of 7 November and the UN Emergency Force.","sol":"The crisis began with money. When the United States and Britain withdrew their offer to fund the Aswan High Dam on 19 July 1956, Nasser needed both a new source of revenue and an answer in public, and on 26 July he nationalised the Suez Canal Company, promising to compensate the shareholders and spend the tolls on the dam. Because the company was Anglo-French owned and Eden had decided that Nasser must be brought down, London and Paris wanted a war but had no lawful pretext for one, which is why they met Israeli representatives in secret at Sèvres from 22 to 24 October: Israel, which wanted the fedayeen bases and the Straits of Tiran, would attack, and Britain and France would then intervene as though separating the two sides. The plan ran to schedule. Israel invaded Sinai on 29 October, the ultimatum followed on 30 October, Anglo-French bombing began on 31 October and paratroops landed at Port Said on 5 November. What it could not survive was the reaction of Britain's own ally: a run on sterling that Washington refused to relieve, Soviet threats and a hostile UN vote forced a ceasefire on 7 November, with a UN Emergency Force created to take over. The invaders left by December and Israel by March 1957, so the operation ended with Egypt holding the canal and Nasser stronger than before.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Begin earlier than the nationalisation: what happened a week before it that gave Nasser both a need and a target?","Every step needs a why: ask what Britain and France still lacked after July, and what an Israeli attack could supply.","End on the reversal - the plan worked militarily, so say plainly what stopped it and what each participant had gained or lost."],"codes":["P5-1 — 3 Increased tension, 1955–63: The events and significance of the Suez Crisis (1956)","P5-1 — 3 Increased tension, 1955–63: Israeli attacks on Gaza in 1955 and Sinai in 1956"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the Law of Return (1950) for the development of the new state of Israel. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid, and Level 3 (6-8) needs analysis of importance in a coherent line of reasoning. Credit the doubling of the Jewish population and the absorption crisis, the manpower it gave the conscript army and the settlement of border areas, the dependence on outside money that followed, and the contrast with the Palestinian refugees who were refused return.","sol":"The Law of Return mattered because it turned a promise into an obligation. Passed on 5 July 1950, it gave every Jew anywhere the right to settle in Israel, so immigration stopped being something the government granted and became something it had to receive. The first effect was demographic and economic. Immigration on that scale could no longer be slowed: more than 600,000 people had arrived by the end of 1951, roughly doubling the Jewish population, and because the state could not turn them away the Jewish Agency and the government had to put them into the ma'abarot, the transit camps of tents and tin huts built from 1950, keep the austerity rationing begun in 1949 in force, and lean on money raised abroad, from Jewish communities in the United States and, from September 1952, West German reparations. The second effect was military. Under the Defence Service Law of 1949 the new arrivals were conscripted and settled in thinly held border areas, so a state that had survived one war by a narrow margin could field a large trained reserve for the next. The third was political, and the most lasting. By defining Israel as the state of the Jewish people with a permanently open door, the law set itself against the claim of the 700,000 Palestinian Arabs who had left in the war and were refused return. Without the law Israel would still have taken immigrants; with it, the character of the state, the size of its army and the terms of its quarrel with the refugees were all settled at once.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Importance means the difference it made afterwards, so refuse any sentence that only tells the examiner what the law said.","Trace three separate lines forward from the same law: who arrived, what they were used for, and who was excluded.","Close with a judgement that says what would have been different without the law rather than repeating the effects."],"codes":["P5-1 — 2 Aftermath of the 1948–49 war: The creation of the Israeli Defence Forces and the Law of Return","P5-1 — 2 Aftermath of the 1948–49 war: Territorial changes and their impact"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the Czech arms deal (1955) for the balance of power in the Middle East. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Credit the collapse of the Tripartite Declaration's arms controls, the arrival of Soviet influence and the Cold War in the region, the Israeli decision to fight before the balance shifted and the French arms that followed, and the rise of Nasser's standing among Arab states.","sol":"The deal Nasser announced on 27 September 1955 mattered because it changed who decided how strong each state in the region could be. Until then the United States, Britain and France had been the only large suppliers of modern weapons, and under the Tripartite Declaration of 1950 they had used that position to hold Israel and the Arab states at roughly the level they chose. By buying MiG-15 fighters, Ilyushin bombers and T-34 tanks through Czechoslovakia, Egypt walked out of that system, and no Western agreement could restrain an arms race it no longer controlled. The second consequence was that the Cold War arrived in the Arab-Israeli conflict. Moscow had found a client in the largest Arab state, so Washington and London had to compete for Nasser rather than command him, and their withdrawal of the Aswan Dam funding in July 1956 was part of that struggle. The third consequence was the shortening of the fuse. Israeli commanders judged that Egypt would absorb the new equipment within a year or two, which turned a preventive war into an argument that could be won in cabinet, and Israel turned to France, which began supplying modern aircraft in 1956. The importance of the deal is therefore that it made both a superpower rivalry and an early war likely in a region where, five years earlier, three Western governments had believed they could prevent both.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Balance of power is about capability and about who controls it, so ask who had decided the region's arms supply before 1955.","Follow the consequences into two arenas: the Cold War between the superpowers and the calculations of the Israeli general staff.","Judge it at the end: what became likely after September 1955 that had looked preventable before it?"],"codes":["P5-1 — 3 Increased tension, 1955–63: Nasser and Egypt's leadership of the Arab world","P5-1 — 3 Increased tension, 1955–63: Israeli attacks on Gaza in 1955 and Sinai in 1956","P5-1 — 2 Aftermath of the 1948–49 war: Israel's relations with Egypt"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the Suez Crisis (1956) for Nasser's leadership of the Arab world. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Credit survival against two European powers and Israel and the prestige it brought, the retention of the canal and the payment of its tolls to Egypt, the collapse of British influence and the Eisenhower Doctrine that followed, the union with Syria in the UAR in 1958, and the limits shown by Syria's secession in 1961 and by Yemen.","sol":"Militarily the crisis was a defeat for Egypt, and politically it was the making of Nasser. Israeli forces took Sinai in a week from 29 October 1956 and Anglo-French troops held Port Said, yet the ceasefire of 7 November left Egypt in possession of the canal and its revenues, and the invaders withdrew by December while Israel left by March 1957. To Arab audiences the lesson was simple: a leader had taken a European company, been attacked by two empires and Israel, and kept what he had taken. That reputation had concrete effects. Cairo radio could now address the peoples of other Arab states over the heads of their governments with real authority, and the conservative monarchies looked weaker for having stayed out; when Syrian officers asked for union, Nasser was able to insist on his own terms, and the United Arab Republic was proclaimed on 1 February 1958. Britain's position collapsed with Eden's resignation in January 1957, and the United States moved into the gap with the Eisenhower Doctrine of the same month, which treated Nasser's Egypt as the problem to be contained rather than as a government to be courted. The limits appeared soon enough, since Syria left the union in September 1961 and Egyptian troops were bogged down in Yemen from 1962, but for the rest of the decade Suez made Nasser the reference point of Arab politics, which no Egyptian leader had been before.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Separate the military result from the political one before you start, because the question rides on the gap between them.","Trace the standing forward: what could Nasser do in 1958 that he could not do in 1955, and what happened to Britain's place?","A top answer weighs the limits too, so end by testing how long the leadership lasted and where it failed."],"codes":["P5-1 — 3 Increased tension, 1955–63: The events and significance of the Suez Crisis (1956)","P5-1 — 3 Increased tension, 1955–63: Nasser and Egypt's leadership of the Arab world"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date did the UN General Assembly vote to partition Palestine?","a":"29 November 1947."},{"q":"Who declared the establishment of the State of Israel on 14 May 1948?","a":"David Ben-Gurion."},{"q":"Which law, passed by the Knesset on 5 July 1950, gave every Jew the right to settle in Israel?","a":"The Law of Return."},{"q":"On what date did Nasser announce the nationalisation of the Suez Canal Company?","a":"26 July 1956."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The British Mandate for Palestine","back":"British rule over Palestine under a League of Nations mandate, in force from 1923 until it expired at midnight on 14-15 May 1948. By 1947 the population was about 1.9 million, roughly a third of it Jewish.","code":"P5-1 — 1 The British withdrawal and the creation of Israel: Conflicting interests and demands of Jews and Arabs","open":false},{"front":"Jewish and Arab demands under the Mandate","back":"Zionists wanted unrestricted immigration and a Jewish state, demanded openly in the Biltmore Program of May 1942. Arabs wanted independence for the existing majority, an end to immigration and to land sales.","code":"P5-1 — 1 The British withdrawal and the creation of Israel: Conflicting interests and demands of Jews and Arabs","open":false},{"front":"The Anglo-American Committee of Inquiry, 1946","back":"Reported in April 1946 that 100,000 Holocaust survivors should be admitted to Palestine at once. Truman backed it; Attlee told the Commons on 1 May 1946 that they could not come until the illegal armies disbanded and surrendered their arms.","code":"P5-1 — 1 The British withdrawal and the creation of Israel: Key events leading to the end of the British Mandate","open":false},{"front":"The King David Hotel bombing, 22 July 1946","back":"The Irgun, led by Menachem Begin, bombed the hotel's southern wing in Jerusalem, which housed the Secretariat of the Government of Palestine and army headquarters. 91 people were killed.","code":"P5-1 — 1 The British withdrawal and the creation of Israel: Key events leading to the end of the British Mandate","open":false},{"front":"The Exodus, 1947","back":"A ship carrying over 4,500 Jewish refugees from France, boarded by the Royal Navy in July 1947. The passengers were taken back to France and landed in the British zone of Germany that September.","code":"P5-1 — 1 The British withdrawal and the creation of Israel: Key events leading to the end of the British Mandate","open":false},{"front":"UNSCOP and UN Resolution 181","back":"UNSCOP recommended partition on 31 August 1947. On 29 November 1947 the General Assembly voted 33 to 13, with 10 abstentions, to divide Palestine, with Jerusalem under international control.","code":"P5-1 — 1 The British withdrawal and the creation of Israel: Key events leading to the end of the British Mandate","open":false},{"front":"Deir Yassin, 9 April 1948","back":"Irgun and Lehi fighters attacked the village near Jerusalem during the civil war, killing about a hundred villagers. News of it spread panic and increased the flight of Arabs from their homes.","code":"P5-1 — 1 The British withdrawal and the creation of Israel: Key events of the Arab-Israeli war (1948–49)","open":false},{"front":"The Arab-Israeli war, 1948-49","back":"Ben-Gurion declared the state on 14 May 1948; Egypt, Transjordan, Syria, Lebanon and Iraq invaded on 15 May. UN truces from 11 June and 18 July let Israel rearm; Israeli offensives in the Negev ended the fighting.","code":"P5-1 — 1 The British withdrawal and the creation of Israel: Key events of the Arab-Israeli war (1948–49)","open":false},{"front":"The armistice agreements of 1949","back":"Signed with Egypt on 24 February, Lebanon on 23 March, Jordan on 3 April and Syria on 20 July 1949. Military lines, not frontiers: no recognition, no peace. Israel held about 78 per cent of mandate Palestine.","code":"P5-1 — 2 Aftermath of the 1948–49 war: Territorial changes and their impact","open":false},{"front":"The Palestinian refugees and UNRWA","back":"About 700,000 Arabs left or were driven from their homes and were not allowed to return. The UN created its relief and works agency in December 1949; it ran camps in Gaza, the West Bank, Jordan, Lebanon and Syria.","code":"P5-1 — 2 Aftermath of the 1948–49 war: Territorial changes and their impact","open":false},{"front":"The Israeli Defence Forces and the Law of Return","back":"An order of 26 May 1948 made the IDF the state's only army; the Altalena was shelled that June and the 1949 law conscripted men and women. The Law of Return (5 July 1950) let any Jew settle in Israel.","code":"P5-1 — 2 Aftermath of the 1948–49 war: The creation of the Israeli Defence Forces and the Law of Return","open":false},{"front":"Egypt and Israel between the wars, 1949-55","back":"Egypt refused recognition, barred Israeli shipping from the Suez Canal and blockaded the Straits of Tiran from 1953. The UN Security Council's call to lift the restrictions in 1951 was ignored; raids and reprisals continued along the Gaza line.","code":"P5-1 — 2 Aftermath of the 1948–49 war: Israel's relations with Egypt","open":false},{"front":"Nasser and the Czech arms deal, 1955","back":"Nasser opposed the Baghdad Pact, spoke to Arab peoples through Voice of the Arabs radio, and on 27 September 1955 announced the purchase of MiG-15 fighters, Ilyushin bombers and T-34 tanks through Czechoslovakia.","code":"P5-1 — 3 Increased tension, 1955–63: Nasser and Egypt's leadership of the Arab world","open":false},{"front":"The Gaza raid of 1955 and the Sinai campaign of 1956","back":"Israeli paratroopers attacked an Egyptian position in Gaza on 28 February 1955, killing about 38 soldiers. On 29 October 1956 Israel invaded Sinai, taking the peninsula, Gaza and Sharm el-Sheikh in about a week.","code":"P5-1 — 3 Increased tension, 1955–63: Israeli attacks on Gaza in 1955 and Sinai in 1956","open":false},{"front":"The Suez Crisis and the United Arab Republic","back":"Nasser nationalised the canal company on 26 July 1956; Britain, France and Israel colluded at Sèvres in October. A ceasefire came on 7 November and a UN force took over. Egypt and Syria formed the UAR on 1 February 1958.","code":"P5-1 — 3 Increased tension, 1955–63: The events and significance of the Suez Crisis (1956)","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Paper 2 booklet P Question 2, narrative account, 8 marks)","items":["Question: 'Write a narrative account analysing the key events in Egypt in the years 1952-56 that led to the nationalisation of the Suez Canal Company.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the Free Officers' coup (July 1952); the Anglo-Egyptian agreement (October 1954). You must also use information of your own. This is the middle stretch of the account, carrying the story from the removal of the British garrison to the withdrawal of the money for the Aswan High Dam.","Because the Free Officers had taken power on 23 July 1952 in the name of ending foreign control - Farouk abdicated and sailed from Alexandria three days later - the new government's first business was the British garrison, and the agreement signed on 19 October 1954 was its first success: Britain undertook to withdraw from the Suez Canal base within twenty months, and the last troops left in June 1956. That agreement changed what Nasser could attempt next, because a government that had removed an occupying army by negotiation could claim to speak for the Arab world and not for Egypt alone. He used the claim at once. At Bandung in April 1955 he placed Egypt among the non-aligned states, and when the West would not sell him modern weapons he announced on 27 September 1955 that Egypt would buy MiG-15 fighters, Ilyushin bombers and T-34 tanks through Czechoslovakia. The deal did not make Egypt strong so much as make it independent of Western permission, and that is what alarmed London and Washington: a government they could no longer arm or restrain was asking them to pay for the Aswan High Dam. So when the offer of funding was withdrawn on 19 July 1956, Nasser needed both a new source of money for the dam and a public answer, and one act could supply both.","Why this is Level 3: the events are organised into a clear sequence leading to the outcome the question names, and the linkage between them is analysed rather than stated - the coup explains why the garrison was the first business, the agreement explains the standing on which Nasser could act regionally, and the arms deal explains why the dam funding was withdrawn. Accurate and relevant information supports each step (23 July 1952, 19 October 1954, twenty months, June 1956, April 1955, 27 September 1955, 19 July 1956), and more than three aspects of content are addressed, so the Level 3 cap does not bite."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: as above, the same stretch of the same narrative account.","The Free Officers took over Egypt in 1952 and got rid of King Farouk. After that they wanted the British soldiers to leave, and in 1954 Britain agreed to take its troops out of the canal base. Then Nasser became the leader of Egypt and he went to a big conference of countries that were not on either side in the Cold War. He also made a deal to buy weapons from Czechoslovakia because the West would not sell him any. The Americans and the British did not like this, and in 1956 they said they would not pay for the Aswan Dam after all. So Nasser decided to nationalise the Suez Canal Company.","Why this is only Level 2, and what it misses: it meets the Level 2 descriptor - 'a narrative is given, showing some organisation of material into a sequence of events leading to an outcome' with 'accurate and relevant information' - but it misses the Level 3 requirement that 'the account of events analyses the linkage between them'. The events are joined by 'after that' and 'then' rather than by explanation: it never says why the coup made the garrison the first question, why the withdrawal agreement raised Nasser's standing in the Arab world, or why the arms deal turned Washington and London against paying for the dam. The information is also imprecise where precision is available ('in 1954' for the agreement of 19 October 1954, 'a big conference' for Bandung in April 1955, 'weapons' for MiG-15 fighters, Ilyushin bombers and T-34 tanks, and no date for the withdrawal of the funding on 19 July 1956)."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the British Mandate and the conflicting demands within it","items":["The Mandate for Palestine was approved by the League of Nations in 1922 and came into force in 1923; it expired at midnight on 14-15 May 1948.","The Balfour Declaration of 2 November 1917 promised British support for a national home for the Jewish people in Palestine without prejudice to the rights of the existing non-Jewish communities.","The 1939 White Paper limited Jewish immigration to 75,000 over five years, restricted land sales to Jews, and promised an independent Palestine within ten years.","The Biltmore Program, agreed at a Zionist conference in New York in May 1942, demanded that Palestine become a Jewish commonwealth with immigration controlled by the Jewish Agency.","About six million Jews were murdered in the Holocaust; hundreds of thousands of survivors were living in displaced-persons camps in Europe after 1945.","In 1947 Palestine's population was about 1.9 million, roughly a third of it Jewish; Jewish-owned land was a small fraction of the country.","The Jewish Agency, founded in 1929, acted as a government in waiting: it ran immigration, settlement and fundraising, and the Haganah was the main Jewish defence force.","The Arab League was founded in Cairo in March 1945 by Egypt, Iraq, Transjordan, Lebanon, Syria and Saudi Arabia, and made Palestine a pan-Arab question.","Ernest Bevin became Foreign Secretary in July 1945 and kept the 1939 immigration limits, partly to protect British oil interests and the Suez Canal base.","President Truman pressed Britain from August 1945 to admit 100,000 Jewish survivors to Palestine."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the bombing of the King David Hotel and UN Resolution 181","items":["The Anglo-American Committee of Inquiry reported in April 1946 and recommended the immediate admission of 100,000 Jewish immigrants; Attlee told the Commons on 1 May 1946 that so large a body of immigrants could not be admitted unless and until the illegal armies in Palestine were disbanded and their arms surrendered.","The Irgun, commanded by Menachem Begin, bombed the southern wing of the King David Hotel in Jerusalem on 22 July 1946.","The wing housed the Secretariat of the Government of Palestine and the headquarters of the British armed forces in Palestine and Transjordan.","91 people were killed in the bombing, of several nationalities, and 46 were injured; warnings had been telephoned but the building was not evacuated.","The bombing followed Operation Agatha of 29 June 1946, in which British forces raided the Jewish Agency, interned its officials and seized its papers; Britain answered the bombing with Operation Shark, the cordon and search of Tel Aviv by troops and the Palestine Police from 30 July 1946, in which about 100,000 people were screened under curfew.","The Exodus, carrying over 4,500 Jewish refugees, was boarded by the Royal Navy in July 1947; its passengers were returned to France and landed in the British zone of Germany in September 1947.","Bevin referred the future of Palestine to the United Nations in February 1947.","The UN Special Committee on Palestine (UNSCOP) had eleven members; its majority recommended partition on 31 August 1947, with Jerusalem under international administration.","The General Assembly passed Resolution 181 on 29 November 1947 by 33 votes to 13 with 10 abstentions; Britain abstained.","The Jewish Agency accepted the plan; the Arab Higher Committee and the Arab League rejected it, and Britain announced that it would give up the Mandate, which expired at midnight on 14-15 May 1948."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Arab-Israeli war (1948-49) and what it left behind","items":["Civil war between Jewish and Arab forces inside Palestine ran from December 1947 to May 1948, while Britain withdrew and refused to enforce partition.","Irgun and Lehi fighters killed about a hundred villagers at Deir Yassin, near Jerusalem, on 9 April 1948.","Haifa fell to Jewish forces on 22 April 1948 and Jaffa in May; some 300,000 Arabs had left their homes before the state was declared.","David Ben-Gurion declared the State of Israel in Tel Aviv on 14 May 1948; the United States recognised it the same day and the USSR within days.","Egypt, Transjordan, Syria, Lebanon and Iraq sent forces into Palestine on 15 May 1948.","The UN mediator Count Folke Bernadotte arranged a four-week truce from 11 June 1948 and a second truce from 18 July; he was assassinated by Lehi gunmen on 17 September 1948.","Israel used the truces to import rifles and aircraft bought from Czechoslovakia and to expand its forces towards 100,000 men; the Arab states, divided over aims, did not coordinate rearmament.","Israeli offensives in the Negev in October and December 1948 drove Egyptian forces back and decided the southern front.","Armistices were signed with Egypt on 24 February 1949, Lebanon on 23 March, Jordan on 3 April and Syria on 20 July 1949.","Israel ended the war holding about 78 per cent of mandate Palestine, including western Jerusalem and the Negev; about 6,000 Israelis had been killed.","Egypt held the Gaza Strip under military administration; Transjordan, renamed Jordan in 1949, held the West Bank and eastern Jerusalem and annexed them in 1950. No Arab state of Palestine was created.","About 700,000 Palestinian Arabs became refugees; the UN created its relief and works agency in December 1949, and it began operations in 1950 in Gaza, the West Bank, Jordan, Lebanon and Syria."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Israeli Defence Forces, the Law of Return and paying for the new state","items":["An order of 26 May 1948 created the Israeli Defence Forces as the state's only armed force, built on the Haganah.","The Palmach was disbanded and the Irgun and Lehi were broken up; the Irgun's arms ship Altalena was shelled off Tel Aviv in June 1948.","The Defence Service Law of 1949 introduced conscription for both men and women, giving a small population a large trained reserve.","The Knesset passed the Law of Return on 5 July 1950, giving every Jew anywhere the right to settle in Israel.","More than 600,000 immigrants arrived in the first three years after independence, roughly doubling the Jewish population.","They included Holocaust survivors from the European camps and Jewish communities from Yemen, Iraq and North Africa; many lived in tent and hut transit camps.","An austerity and rationing programme was run from 1949 to cope with shortages of food, housing and foreign currency.","The United States Export-Import Bank granted Israel a loan of 100 million dollars in January 1949, the first large American aid to the new state.","The Jewish Agency organised immigration, absorption and settlement after 1948 and raised money from Jewish communities abroad, especially in the United States.","West Germany agreed in September 1952 to pay Israel reparations of 3,000 million marks over twelve years, much of it in goods and equipment."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Nasser and Egypt's leadership of the Arab world","items":["The Free Officers overthrew the Egyptian monarchy on 23 July 1952; King Farouk abdicated days later.","Gamal Abdel Nasser became prime minister in 1954 and president in 1956.","The Anglo-Egyptian agreement of October 1954 provided for British troops to leave the Suez Canal base, which they completed in 1956.","Nasser denounced the Baghdad Pact of February 1955 as an attempt to keep Arab states inside Western defence plans.","The Cairo radio station Voice of the Arabs carried his message to Arab populations over the heads of their governments.","At the Bandung conference in April 1955 Nasser associated Egypt with the non-aligned states.","On 27 September 1955 he announced the purchase of MiG-15 fighters, Ilyushin bombers and T-34 tanks through Czechoslovakia, breaking the Western monopoly on arms supply.","The United States and Britain withdrew their offer to finance the Aswan High Dam on 19 July 1956.","Egypt and Syria proclaimed the United Arab Republic on 1 February 1958, with Nasser as president; the Iraqi monarchy was overthrown on 14 July 1958.","Syria left the union on 28 September 1961, and from 1962 Egyptian troops were committed to the civil war in Yemen."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Israeli attacks on Gaza in 1955 and Sinai in 1956","items":["Egypt refused recognition of Israel after 1949, barred Israeli ships and cargoes from the Suez Canal and blockaded the Straits of Tiran from 1953.","The UN Security Council called on Egypt in September 1951 to lift the restrictions on shipping through the canal; Egypt ignored the resolution.","Infiltration across the Gaza armistice line, some of it armed, was met by Israeli reprisal raids intended to force the Egyptian authorities to stop it.","On 28 February 1955 Israeli paratroopers attacked an Egyptian army position in Gaza, killing about 38 Egyptian soldiers; eight Israeli soldiers were killed.","The raid exposed the weakness of Egypt's equipment and was followed seven months later by the Czech arms deal.","After the raid Egypt organised the fedayeen raids into Israel that it had previously tried to restrain.","Israeli commanders argued that Egypt would absorb its new Soviet-bloc weapons within a year or two, so a war fought soon would be cheaper than one fought later.","France began supplying Israel with modern aircraft in 1956.","Israel invaded Sinai on 29 October 1956, and within about a week held the peninsula, the Gaza Strip and Sharm el-Sheikh at the mouth of the Straits of Tiran.","Israel withdrew from Sinai and Gaza by March 1957 under American pressure, gaining freedom of navigation to Eilat and about a decade of quiet on the Egyptian frontier."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Suez Crisis (1956) and the formation of the UAR in 1958","items":["Nasser announced the nationalisation of the Suez Canal Company in Alexandria on 26 July 1956, a week after the Aswan Dam funding was withdrawn.","He promised to compensate the shareholders of the Anglo-French owned company and to use the tolls to build the dam.","Britain, France and Israel agreed a plan in secret at Sèvres, near Paris, on 22 to 24 October 1956.","Israel invaded Sinai on 29 October; Britain and France issued an ultimatum to both sides on 30 October and began bombing Egyptian airfields on 31 October.","Egypt blocked the canal by sinking ships in it; Anglo-French paratroops landed at Port Said on 5 November and seaborne forces the next day.","A run on sterling that the United States refused to relieve, Soviet threats and a hostile UN vote forced a ceasefire on 7 November 1956.","The General Assembly created the United Nations Emergency Force on 5 November 1956; its troops arrived in Egypt within days and were stationed on the Egyptian side of the line.","British and French troops had left Egypt by 22 December 1956; the canal was cleared and reopened under Egyptian control in 1957.","Anthony Eden resigned as British prime minister in January 1957; the Eisenhower Doctrine, announced on 5 January 1957 and approved by Congress in March, promised American aid and force against communist aggression in the Middle East.","Nasser's survival made him the leading figure of Arab nationalism, and Syria's request for union produced the United Arab Republic on 1 February 1958."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 2 booklet P Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the key events in Egypt in the years 1952-56 that led to the nationalisation of the Suez Canal Company' (8 marks)","items":["The question decoded: the outcome is fixed for you - the canal company is nationalised on 26 July 1956 - so the job is to select the events in Egypt between 1952 and 1956 that led to it, put them in order, and explain at each step why the next one followed. The stimulus points offered are the Free Officers' coup (July 1952) and the Anglo-Egyptian agreement (October 1954), and the paper adds: 'You must also use information of your own.'","The cap to beat: 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' Two bullets are two aspects, so plan at least two strands of your own - here Egypt's turn to the non-aligned world and the Soviet bloc for arms, and the quarrel over the money for the Aswan High Dam.","Timing: about 13 minutes on an 8-mark question, so two minutes planning the spine, ten writing and one checking that every join carries an explanation. No SPaG marks are awarded on Paper 2.","Opening sentence: set out what Egypt was in 1952 - a kingdom under a monarch the army despised, with British troops still in the Suez Canal base and the canal itself run by an Anglo-French company - so that every later step can be measured against it.","Episode 1 (the coup): the Free Officers seized power on 23 July 1952 and Farouk abdicated and left Alexandria on 26 July; the monarchy was abolished and a republic declared in June 1953, and Nasser became prime minister in 1954. Join forward: a government that had come to power promising to end foreign control had to deal with the garrison before anything else.","Episode 2 (the British withdrawal): by the agreement of 19 October 1954 Britain undertook to leave the Suez Canal base within twenty months, and the last troops went in June 1956. Join forward: removing an occupying army by negotiation gave Nasser a standing in the Arab world that no Egyptian ruler had held, and it also meant that nothing British stood between him and the canal.","Episode 3 (Egypt looks outward, your own material): at Bandung in April 1955 Nasser placed Egypt among the non-aligned states, and on 27 September 1955 he announced that Egypt would buy MiG-15 fighters, Ilyushin bombers and T-34 tanks through Czechoslovakia. Join forward: a government the West could no longer arm or restrain was much harder for Washington and London to subsidise.","Episode 4 (the dam and the money, your own material): the Aswan High Dam was the centre of Nasser's plans for Egypt, and on 19 July 1956 the United States withdrew its offer to help fund it, with Britain following. Join forward: he needed both revenue for the dam and an answer in public, and the tolls of the Anglo-French canal company could provide both, so he nationalised it on 26 July 1956, four years to the day after the king had sailed.","The analytical thread that must run through every join: each step made Egypt less dependent on Western consent - first on British troops, then on Western arms, then on Western money - so that nationalisation was the last move in a sequence rather than a sudden gamble.","Precision to include: 23 and 26 July 1952; June 1953 for the republic; 19 October 1954 and the twenty months; June 1956 for the last British troops; April 1955 for Bandung; 27 September 1955 for the arms deal; 19 July 1956 for the withdrawal of the dam funding; 26 July 1956 for the nationalisation.","What the closing sentence must do: not summarise the events again, but say what the sequence amounted to - by July 1956 Egypt had removed the last foreign garrison, found arms outside the Western system and lost the Western money it had counted on, so taking the canal was the one remaining way to pay for the dam and to prove the point.","Two traps: writing the years 1952 to 1956 as a list of incidents with 'then' between them, which keeps the answer in Level 2; and using only the two bullets, which brings the Level 3 cap into play however accurate the writing is."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:41:21Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Re-judged against the kit file after the fix pass: all seven recorded faults are genuinely repaired and the new material verifies - Attlee's Commons statement of 1 May 1946, Operation Agatha of 29 June 1946 followed by the King David bombing and the Tel Aviv cordon of 30 July (about 102,000 screened), the four armistice places with Bunche as acting mediator, the Baghdad Pact of February 1955 with Britain, Pakistan and Iran joining that year and Glubb dismissed in March 1956, UNEF from 5 and 15-16 November 1956 into Gaza and Sharm el-Sheikh in March 1957, the ma'abarot from 1950 and the 1949 austerity, and Farouk sailing on 26 July 1952 four years to the day before the nationalisation; nothing now rests on a wrong date, figure or attribution, the plan and both model paragraphs sit on a narrative that is in neither the bank nor the worked examples, the June 2025 paper form is exact and no stem touches a printed specimen P5 question, so this publishes with only the small tidying listed above."}}},"P5-2":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"P5-2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 2 is the part of this option where the conflict stops being a series of separate quarrels and becomes a chain: a summit in Cairo creates the PLO, raids and reprisals draw Israel, Syria and Jordan into confrontation, a blockade brings six days of war, and the land taken in those six days sets the agenda for everything that follows, from Resolution 242 and the refugee camps to Karameh, Black September, Munich and the crossing of the Suez Canal in 1973. Booklet P examines exactly this kind of material. The consequences question wants one thing that was true afterwards because of an event, analysed rather than retold. The narrative account wants a short run of years in which each episode visibly made the next one more likely. The importance questions want the difference an event or a person made to what came later. If you can date the escalation of 1964 to 1967, say what Israel gained and what the Arab states lost in June 1967, explain why 242 settled nothing, and trace how Egypt moved from Soviet dependence to the October war and the oil weapon, you can answer almost anything this key topic sets. This room teaches that chain as an argument you can make, not a list you can recite.","idea":[{"code":"P5-2 — 1 The Six Day War, 1967: The significance of the Cairo Conference (1964)","h":"Cairo 1964: water, the PLO and the raids that followed","t":"The first summit of Arab heads of state met in Cairo from 13 to 17 January 1964, called by Nasser to answer Israel's National Water Carrier, which was about to start drawing water from the River Jordan into the Negev. The summit took three decisions that mattered for the next three years. It approved a plan to divert the headwaters of the Jordan in Lebanon and Syria so that less water would reach Israel; it set up a United Arab Command under an Egyptian general; and it agreed that the Palestinians should have an organisation of their own. That last decision produced the Palestine Liberation Organisation, proclaimed by a Palestinian congress in East Jerusalem at the end of May 1964 under Ahmad Shukeiri, with its own force, the Palestine Liberation Army. Because the PLO was created by the Arab states and paid for by them, younger Palestinians saw it as Nasser's instrument rather than their own, and Fatah, led by Yasser Arafat, answered by acting alone: its first raid on Israel, an attempt to blow up part of the National Water Carrier, was mounted in January 1965. Israel attacked the diversion works in Syria, Syria sheltered Fatah, and the border war began. A top-level answer treats Cairo 1964 as a starting gun rather than a meeting: it created a rival Palestinian politics of armed action, and it turned a quarrel over water into a cycle of raid and reprisal."},{"code":"P5-2 — 1 The Six Day War, 1967: The actions of the USSR, Nasser and the USA","h":"Syria, Samu and the slide to war, 1966 to 1967","t":"Two incidents pulled Jordan and Syria into the front line. On 13 November 1966 Israel attacked Samu, a village in the Jordanian-held West Bank, in reprisal for a mine that had killed three Israeli border police: sixteen Jordanian soldiers died, and Palestinians in the West Bank rioted against King Hussein for failing to protect them. On 7 April 1967 a border clash with Syria grew into an air battle in which Israel shot down six Syrian MiG-21s, some of them near Damascus. Then came the actions the exam names. On 13 May 1967 Soviet intelligence told Egypt, wrongly, that Israel was massing troops on the Syrian border; Nasser, taunted for hiding behind the UN, asked on 16 May for the United Nations Emergency Force to leave Sinai and Gaza, and U Thant agreed on 18 May; Egyptian forces moved up to the frontier and back into Sharm el-Sheikh; on 22 May Nasser announced that the Straits of Tiran were closed to Israeli shipping, which Israel had said for a decade it would treat as an act of war; and on 30 May King Hussein flew to Cairo and put his army under Egyptian command. The USA urged restraint, tried and failed to organise an international convoy to open the Straits, and was absorbed by Vietnam, so Israel concluded that nobody would act for it. A top-level answer keeps the causal chain visible: a false Soviet report produced a public gamble by Nasser, the gamble produced a blockade, and the blockade left Israel choosing between waiting and striking first."},{"code":"P5-2 — 1 The Six Day War, 1967: Key events of the war","h":"Six days, three fronts: how the war was won and what it took","t":"Israel struck first. On the morning of 5 June 1967 the Israeli air force attacked Egypt's airfields and destroyed the great bulk of the Egyptian air force on the ground within hours, then turned on the Jordanian, Syrian and Iraqi air forces the same day. With the skies clear, Israeli armour drove across Sinai and the Gaza Strip; Egyptian units were ordered to pull back and were cut up as they retreated to the canal. Jordan, bound by the pact of 30 May and misled by Egyptian claims of victory, shelled West Jerusalem, and Israel answered by taking the West Bank; Israeli troops entered the Old City of Jerusalem on 7 June. On 9 and 10 June Israel stormed the Golan Heights, from which Syrian guns had shelled Israeli farms for years. The ceasefire held from 10 June. In six days Israel had taken Sinai and the Gaza Strip from Egypt, the West Bank and East Jerusalem from Jordan and the Golan Heights from Syria, roughly trebling the territory it controlled. Fewer than a thousand Israelis were killed; Arab losses ran into many thousands, and the Egyptian army lost most of its equipment. A top-level answer explains the outcome rather than narrating it: surprise and air superiority on the first morning decided the rest, and the size of the victory, not the fighting, created the problems of the next thirty years."},{"code":"P5-2 — 2 Aftermath of the 1967 war: UN Resolution 242","h":"Resolution 242: land for peace, and the words that let everyone disagree","t":"The Security Council passed Resolution 242 unanimously on 22 November 1967. It set out the principle of land for peace: the withdrawal of Israeli armed forces from territories occupied in the recent conflict, and an end to claims of belligerency, with every state in the area to have its sovereignty and territorial integrity respected within secure and recognised boundaries. It also called for a just settlement of the refugee problem, and a Swedish diplomat, Gunnar Jarring, was appointed to talk to the parties. Its weakness was built into its wording. The English text said withdrawal from territories occupied, not from the territories, so Israel argued it was not required to give everything back and that withdrawal must follow a negotiated peace; Egypt and Jordan accepted 242 because they read it as requiring full withdrawal; Syria rejected it outright. The PLO rejected it too, because it mentioned Palestinians only as refugees to be resettled, not as a people with a claim to a state. Meanwhile the Khartoum summit, meeting from 29 August to 1 September 1967, had already answered with the three noes: no peace with Israel, no recognition of Israel, no negotiation with Israel. A top-level answer uses 242 to explain why nothing was settled: it gave both sides a text to quote, so the occupation continued and the next round of fighting, not diplomacy, became the way forward."},{"code":"P5-2 — 2 Aftermath of the 1967 war: Palestinian refugees and the occupied territories","h":"The occupied territories: what Israel held, and who now lived under it","t":"The land taken in June 1967 was not one problem but five. Sinai was a vast, almost empty buffer with oilfields, valuable as a bargaining counter and as defence in depth, and its loss humiliated Egypt and shut the Suez Canal, which stayed closed from 1967 until 1975. The Golan Heights commanded northern Israel and removed the Syrian guns, so Israel treated them as a security necessity. The Gaza Strip and the West Bank were different again: they brought about a million Palestinians under Israeli military government, roughly a third of them already refugees from 1948 living in camps. East Jerusalem contained the Western Wall and the Old City, and at the end of June 1967 Israel extended its law to it and enlarged the city's boundaries, a move the UN General Assembly declared invalid in July. The war created a new wave of refugees as well: about 300,000 Palestinians left the West Bank and Gaza, most of them for Jordan, where their arrival changed the balance of the kingdom's population. A top-level answer separates the territories by what each one meant: Sinai was tradeable, the Golan was defensive, and the West Bank and Gaza were populated, which is why Israel could give one back in 1979 and is still arguing about the others."},{"code":"P5-2 — 2 Aftermath of the 1967 war: The use of terrorism and international attitudes","h":"Karameh, hijackings, Black September and Munich: the Palestinians take their own action","t":"Defeat in 1967 discredited the Arab armies and handed the initiative to the Palestinian groups. On 21 March 1968 an Israeli force attacked Karameh, a Fatah base and refugee camp in Jordan; Jordanian artillery joined in, about thirty Israeli soldiers were killed, and although Palestinian losses were far heavier, Arafat claimed a victory. Thousands volunteered, and in February 1969 Arafat became chairman of the PLO, which was now run by the fighters rather than by the Arab governments. The Popular Front for the Liberation of Palestine, founded by George Habash in 1967, chose a different weapon: on 6 September 1970 it hijacked airliners, flying two of them, a TWA and a Swissair aircraft, to a desert strip near Zarqa in Jordan, while the El Al attempt failed and the Pan Am aircraft was flown to Cairo; a BOAC aircraft hijacked on 9 September joined the other two, and all three were blown up before the world's cameras on 12 September. The hijackings were aimed at publicity, and they got it, but they also convinced King Hussein that he no longer ruled his own country. He declared martial law on 16 September and his army attacked the Palestinian camps from 17 September 1970, the events the Palestinians call Black September; thousands died and the PLO leadership was driven out of Jordan by July 1971, mostly to Lebanon. A group calling itself Black September then attacked the Israeli team's quarters at the Munich Olympics on 5 September 1972, killing two of the team at the outset and taking nine hostage; the nine hostages, a West German policeman and five of the attackers were killed in the failed rescue at the Fürstenfeldbruck airbase, so eleven Israelis died in all. A top-level answer weighs the two results together: the attacks put the Palestinian cause in front of the world, and they also allowed Israel and its allies to define that cause as terrorism."},{"code":"P5-2 — 3 Israel and Egypt, 1967–73: Egyptian relations with Israel, the USA, the USSR and other Arab states","h":"Egypt between the superpowers, 1967 to 1973","t":"Nasser had lost an army, Sinai and the canal revenues, and the Khartoum summit had promised him Arab subsidies but no path to recovery. He rearmed with Soviet help and in March 1969 launched the War of Attrition along the Suez Canal, shelling the Bar-Lev Line of forts Israel had built on the east bank, in the hope that Egypt could bear losses longer than Israel could. Israel replied with deep bombing raids into Egypt, and Moscow answered by sending missile crews and pilots of its own, so that the fighting risked drawing in the superpowers directly. An American proposal, the Rogers Plan, produced a ceasefire on 7 August 1970. Nasser died on 28 September 1970, the day after mediating an end to the fighting in Jordan, and Anwar Sadat succeeded him. Sadat tried diplomacy first, offering in February 1971 to reopen the canal and move towards peace if Israel withdrew, and got nothing; concluding that the USSR would not give him the weapons for an offensive and that only movement would interest Washington, he expelled Soviet military advisers in July 1972 and began planning a limited war with Syria. A top-level answer explains the pattern: Egypt's alignment was a means, not a belief, and each failure of diplomacy pushed Sadat closer to the decision to fight."},{"code":"P5-2 — 3 Israel and Egypt, 1967–73: Israel's consolidation of control of the occupied territories","h":"Facts on the ground: settlements, military government and the Bar-Lev Line","t":"Israel's leaders had expected to trade land for peace, but they began making the occupation workable and, in places, permanent before they knew whether anyone would bargain: the first settlement on the Golan Heights, Merom Golan, was founded on 14 July 1967, and the Allon Plan, drawn up by Yigal Allon that same month, proposed a line of settlements along the Jordan valley to hold a defensible frontier while leaving the crowded Palestinian hill towns alone. When Khartoum answered on 1 September 1967 that there would be no negotiation, none of it stopped: Kfar Etzion, the first settlement in the West Bank, was re-established on 27 September 1967. New Jewish neighbourhoods were built in and around East Jerusalem after the city's boundaries were extended. In Gaza and the West Bank a military government ran the courts, issued the permits and controlled movement, and resistance was answered with curfews, arrests and the demolition of the houses of those involved in attacks. On the Egyptian front Israel dug in behind the Bar-Lev Line, a chain of forts and a high sand rampart along the east bank of the canal, built from 1968 to 1969 and widely believed in Israel to be impassable. A top-level answer shows why consolidation mattered politically: every settlement and every new neighbourhood made withdrawal harder to imagine, and the confidence of the Bar-Lev Line helped produce the surprise of 1973."},{"code":"P5-2 — 3 Israel and Egypt, 1967–73: Key events of the Yom Kippur War (1973)","h":"October 1973: the crossing, the counter-crossing, the oil weapon and the opening it made","t":"Egypt and Syria attacked on 6 October 1973, the Jewish fast of Yom Kippur and a day in the month of Ramadan, so Israeli reservists were at prayer and the roads were empty. Egyptian troops crossed the Suez Canal, breached the sand rampart with water cannon and overran most of the Bar-Lev Line, while Syrian armour drove into the Golan Heights. Israel, short of equipment and shocked by its losses, stopped the Syrians within a week and was resupplied by an American airlift that began on 14 October. From 15 to 16 October Israeli forces under Ariel Sharon crossed to the west bank of the canal and drove south behind the Egyptian armies. On 22 October the Security Council passed Resolution 338, calling for a ceasefire and for Resolution 242 to be carried out in all its parts; the first deadline collapsed, and in the fighting that followed Israel cut the road to Suez and closed the ring round the Egyptian Third Army by 24 October. The fighting stopped on 25 October, the day American forces went to a nuclear alert after a Soviet threat to intervene. Around 2,700 Israelis had been killed, far more than in 1967. Meanwhile the Arab oil producers cut production from 17 October and embargoed the USA from 20 October, and the price of oil roughly quadrupled by January 1974. A top-level answer judges the war by what it changed: Egypt recovered enough pride to negotiate, Israel lost its belief that it could hold the line for ever, and the USA became the broker, which led on to disengagement in 1974 and to Camp David."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1(a): turning the withdrawal of UNEF into one explained consequence","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(a): 'Explain one consequence of the withdrawal of the United Nations Emergency Force from Sinai and Gaza in May 1967.' [4 marks]. Question 1 is printed in two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each asking for one consequence of a separate event and each marked on its own out of 4, so this part needs one consequence analysed and supported. The information you are working from: UNEF had been stationed in Sinai and Gaza since the Suez Crisis of 1956 as a neutral force between the Egyptian and Israeli armies, and it also garrisoned Sharm el-Sheikh, which commands the Straits of Tiran; on 13 May 1967 Soviet intelligence told Egypt, wrongly, that Israel was massing troops on the Syrian border; Egypt asked on 16 May for UNEF to be withdrawn and the UN Secretary-General, U Thant, agreed on 18 May; Egyptian forces moved into the positions UNEF had held; on 22 May Nasser announced that the Straits were closed to Israeli shipping; Jordan signed a defence pact with Egypt on 30 May; Israel attacked on 5 June.","steps":["Work out first what the force was actually doing, because a consequence has to follow from taking that away: it stood between two armies and it garrisoned the point that controls Israel's sea route to the Red Sea.","Now choose a consequence that is a change of state rather than simply the next thing that happened: with no neutral troops in the way, Egypt could physically blockade the Straits of Tiran, which it had been unable to do while the UN held Sharm el-Sheikh.","Support it with the precise detail the grid calls specific information: U Thant agreed on 18 May, Egyptian units took over the UN positions, and Nasser announced the closure to Israeli shipping four days later.","Make the analytical link that lifts the answer into Level 2: because Israel had said for a decade that it would treat the closing of the Straits as an act of war, removing the force removed the last obstacle between a diplomatic crisis and a military one."],"cues":["What job was the force actually doing on the ground?","What became possible for Egypt that had not been possible the week before?","Which dated details prove the change actually happened?","Why does removing a buffer make a war more likely rather than merely more awkward?"]},{"title":"Question 2: planning a narrative account of Israel's consolidation of the occupied territories","setup":"Booklet P, Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing Israel's consolidation of its control of the occupied territories in the years 1967-73.' (8 marks). The booklet prints: 'You may use the following in your answer: the Allon Plan (1967); the military government in Gaza and the West Bank. You must also use information of your own.' The mark scheme shuts an answer out of Level 3 altogether if it does not address three or more aspects of content. What you know about these years: Israel took Sinai, the Gaza Strip, the West Bank, East Jerusalem and the Golan Heights in June 1967; at the end of that month it extended its law to an enlarged East Jerusalem, and the UN General Assembly declared the measures invalid in July; the first settlement on the Golan Heights, Merom Golan, was founded on 14 July 1967, and Yigal Allon drew up his plan for a line of settlements along the Jordan valley in the same month; the Arab summit at Khartoum answered on 1 September 1967 that there would be no negotiation; Kfar Etzion, the first settlement in the West Bank, was re-established on 27 September 1967; a military government ran the courts, the permits and all movement in Gaza and the West Bank, answering resistance with curfews, arrests and the demolition of houses; and Israel dug in behind the Bar-Lev Line on the east bank of the Suez Canal, built in 1968 and 1969.","steps":["Choose a spine of dated episodes rather than a list of measures, and begin it where the consolidation begins: Israel extended its own law to an enlarged East Jerusalem within three weeks of the ceasefire, which is an act of a different order from holding ground under military occupation.","Count the separate aspects of content that spine will cover, because access to Level 3 depends on there being three or more: the extension of Israeli law to East Jerusalem, civilian settlement, the strategic line proposed by Allon, the military government in the towns and camps, and the fortification of the canal front are five.","Write the first link, which is where the analysis lives: because Israel's ministers could not know whether any Arab government would ever bargain, they secured the ground that mattered most for defence first, so Merom Golan was founded on the Golan on 14 July 1967 and Allon's plan for the Jordan valley was drawn up that same month, both of them before the Arab states had given their answer.","Link the second stage to the third by using Khartoum for what it actually did, which was to remove the alternative rather than to start the policy: once the three noes of 1 September ruled out a negotiated return, there was no bargain left to wait for, and the building went on, with Kfar Etzion re-established in the West Bank on 27 September.","Close with a sentence that says what the sequence amounted to instead of repeating it: by the early 1970s one part of the territories was under Israeli law, others were being settled, and the rest was governed by soldiers, so a withdrawal that had been a diplomatic decision at the start of the period had become a question of uprooting Israelis."],"cues":["Where does the story of consolidation actually begin, and how soon after the fighting?","How many separate aspects of content will your episodes cover, and why does the number matter here?","Why would a government start building on captured ground before it knew whether it could trade it away?","What did the Arab summit of September 1967 change about the policy, and what did it not change?","What should the final sentence do that the earlier ones have not done?"]},{"title":"Question 3: explaining the importance of East Jerusalem for the conflict after the Six Day War","setup":"Booklet P, Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8 and each beginning 'The importance of'. Answer this bullet: 'The importance of East Jerusalem for the conflict in the years 1967-73.' (8 marks). No stimulus points are printed and there is no cap. What you know: Jordan had held the eastern half of the city, including the walled Old City with the Western Wall and the Muslim holy places, since 1948; Israeli troops entered the Old City on 7 June 1967; on 27 and 28 June 1967 the Knesset passed the enabling law and the government applied Israeli law, jurisdiction and administration to the eastern half, enlarging the city's boundaries by about seventy square kilometres of the West Bank; the UN General Assembly declared the measures invalid in July 1967; new Jewish neighbourhoods were built inside the new boundaries in the years that followed; Resolution 242 of 22 November 1967 called for withdrawal from territories occupied but named no city; and no Arab government could concede the holy places.","steps":["Set the criterion before you argue, because importance means the difference something made: ask what could still have been negotiated about the city in May 1967 and what could be negotiated about it by the end of that June.","Take the first line of importance and trace it forward: by applying its own law to the eastern half and enlarging the boundaries within three weeks of the fighting, Israel treated Jerusalem differently from Sinai or the Golan, so the city was lifted out of the land-for-peace bargain before that bargain had even been offered.","Take the second and support it with the precise detail the grid wants: the General Assembly called the measures invalid in July, yet Resolution 242 in November asked for withdrawal in general terms and named no city, so the one question both sides cared most about was the one the diplomats had no formula for.","Now qualify the claim, which is what separates a line of reasoning from a list: the fighting of these years was about Sinai and the canal, and Egypt attacked in October 1973 to recover its own territory rather than the city, so Jerusalem was the hardest question rather than the immediate cause of a war.","Finish with a judgement that answers 'importance' directly: Jerusalem mattered because it turned a quarrel about occupied land into a quarrel about sovereignty and religion, which is why later negotiators could move an army out of Sinai and could not move anything in the city."],"cues":["What has to be compared before you can call anything important?","What did Israel do about this city that it did not do about Sinai or the Golan?","What does Resolution 242 say about Jerusalem, and what follows from that?","What were the wars of these years actually fought over, and what does that concede?","What must the last sentence say to answer the word 'importance'?"]},{"title":"Question 1(b): one consequence of the Six Day War for Egypt's relations with the USSR","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(b): 'Explain one consequence of the Six Day War for Egypt's relations with the USSR.' [4 marks]. This is the second of the two parts of Question 1 and is marked on its own out of 4, so it needs a single consequence, analysed and supported. What you know: Egypt lost most of its air force on 5 June 1967 and most of its armour in Sinai; the Suez Canal was closed and stayed closed; the Khartoum summit promised Egypt subsidies from the oil states; the USSR replaced Egypt's lost aircraft, tanks and air defences within months; Nasser launched the War of Attrition in March 1969 and Soviet missile crews and pilots came to Egypt in 1970; Sadat, who succeeded Nasser in 1970, expelled Soviet military advisers in July 1972.","steps":["Decide which relationship the question is about and what it looked like before: Egypt had bought Soviet weapons since the 1950s, but it had bought them as a customer rather than depended on them for its survival.","Choose the consequence as a change in that relationship, not as a list of deliveries: after the defeat Egypt could not rebuild an army at all without Moscow, so a trading partnership became a dependence.","Support it with the specific evidence: the USSR replaced the aircraft, tanks and air defences lost in June 1967, and by 1970 Soviet crews were manning missile batteries and flying combat patrols over Egyptian cities.","Explain the link, and take it one step further to show analysis: because the price of the weapons was Soviet influence over how they could be used, Sadat found he could not get an offensive capability on his own terms, which is exactly why Sadat eventually sent the Soviet advisers home."],"cues":["What kind of relationship was it before June 1967?","What changed about the relationship rather than about the equipment?","Which precise details show the dependence in action?","Why would a patron's generosity become a problem for the client?"]},{"title":"Question 3: explaining the importance of the Suez Canal for Egypt in the years 1967 to 1973","setup":"Booklet P, Question 3, one bullet of three, marked out of 8: 'The importance of the Suez Canal for Egypt in the years 1967-73.' (8 marks). No stimulus points and no cap. What you know: Israel reached the east bank of the canal in June 1967 and the canal was closed from that month until 1975; the tolls had been a major source of Egyptian foreign earnings since nationalisation in 1956, and the cities along the waterway were shelled and largely evacuated; Israel built the Bar-Lev Line of forts and a sand rampart on the east bank from 1968 to 1969; Nasser's War of Attrition of 1969-70 was fought along the canal; Sadat offered in February 1971 to reopen it if Israel pulled back, and was refused; Egyptian forces crossed it on 6 October 1973 and breached the rampart with water cannon.","steps":["Say what the canal was to Egypt before you judge anything, because importance is measured against a baseline: it was the country's shop window and a main source of foreign earnings, and after June 1967 it was a closed ditch with an enemy army on the far bank.","Trace the first line of importance, the economic one: with the tolls gone and the canal cities emptied by shelling, Egypt depended on the subsidies promised at Khartoum, which made a permanent stalemate unaffordable.","Trace the second, the military one: the waterway became the front line, so the fighting of 1969-70 happened there, and Israel's answer, the Bar-Lev Line, was built to make the barrier permanent.","Trace the third, the political one: because Sadat's offer to reopen the canal in return for a partial withdrawal was refused, he could argue at home and in Washington that only a war would move anything.","Judge it rather than summarising: the canal mattered most as the thing that made waiting impossible, which is why the October war opened with a crossing of it and why the crossing itself, whatever happened afterwards, counted in Egypt as a victory."],"cues":["What was the canal worth to Egypt before the war, and what was it worth after?","Which effect follows from the loss of the tolls?","Why did the closed canal shape where the fighting happened?","What did the refusal of 1971 leave Sadat able to argue?","What single claim should the judgement make about importance?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What three decisions did the Cairo summit of January 1964 take, and what body came out of the third of them?","a":"To divert the headwaters of the River Jordan, to set up a United Arab Command under an Egyptian general, and to give the Palestinians an organisation of their own. The third produced the PLO, proclaimed at a Palestinian congress in East Jerusalem at the end of May 1964 under Ahmad Shukeiri."},{"q":"Put the escalation of May 1967 in order, with dates.","a":"13 May: a false Soviet report of Israeli troop concentrations on the Syrian border. 16 May: Egypt asks for UNEF to leave; U Thant agrees on 18 May. 22 May: Nasser announces the closing of the Straits of Tiran to Israeli shipping. 30 May: Jordan signs a defence pact with Egypt. 5 June: Israel attacks."},{"q":"Which territories did Israel hold on 10 June 1967 that it had not held on 4 June, and from whom?","a":"Sinai and the Gaza Strip from Egypt, the West Bank and East Jerusalem from Jordan, and the Golan Heights from Syria."},{"q":"Why did Resolution 242 fail to settle anything, even though every member of the Security Council voted for it?","a":"Its wording could be read two ways. The English text called for withdrawal from territories occupied, not from the territories, so Israel argued for partial withdrawal after a negotiated peace while Egypt and Jordan read it as full withdrawal; Syria rejected it; and the PLO rejected it because it treated the Palestinians only as refugees rather than as a people with a claim to a state."}]},"room":{"facts":["Booklet P is worth 32 marks and has three questions: Question 1, printed in two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each 'Explain one consequence of...' for 4 marks and each on a separate event or development; Question 2, 'Write a narrative account analysing...', 8 marks; Question 3, 'Explain two of the following: the importance of ... for ...', two chosen parts at 8 marks each, 16 in all.","Only Question 2 prints stimulus points, as two bullets followed by 'You must also use information of your own.' The cap printed in the mark scheme is: 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' There is no cap at Level 2. Questions 1 and 3 print no bullets and carry no cap.","Each part of Question 1 is marked on its own two-level grid out of 4: Level 2 needs features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific supporting information added. A description of the event, however accurate, stays in Level 1.","Question 3 is marked twice on a three-level grid out of 8, and Level 3 needs analysis of importance in a line of reasoning that is coherent and logically structured: what difference the event or person made to later situations and developments, not what happened at the time.","Timing: Paper 2 lasts 1 hour 50 minutes for 64 marks, about 55 minutes a booklet, so plan that time as about 12 minutes on Question 1's two parts, 13 on Question 2 and 14 on each part of Question 3, leaving a couple of minutes to check. There are no sources on Paper 2 and no SPaG marks."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Cairo Conference of January 1964. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Level 2 (3-4) requires features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific support. Credit the founding of the PLO in May 1964, the plan to divert the headwaters of the Jordan and the Israeli attacks on the diversion works, the creation of the United Arab Command, or the rise of Fatah as a rival to a PLO seen as Nasser's creature.","sol":"One consequence was the creation of the Palestine Liberation Organisation. The summit of Arab heads of state that met in Cairo in January 1964 agreed that the Palestinians should have an organisation of their own, and a Palestinian congress in East Jerusalem proclaimed the PLO at the end of May 1964 under Ahmad Shukeiri, with its own force, the Palestine Liberation Army. Because the PLO was set up by the Arab governments and paid for by them, many younger Palestinians saw it as Nasser's instrument rather than their own, and turned instead to Fatah, which mounted its first raid on Israel in January 1965 to show that Palestinians would act for themselves.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["A consequence is something that was true afterwards because of the conference, so ask what the summit set in motion that had not existed before it met.","The summit dealt with water, with military command and with the Palestinians; the third of those produced a body that is still in the story thirty years later.","Write it in three moves: name the consequence, give the dated detail that proves it, then explain why it mattered for what came next."],"codes":["P5-2 — 1 The Six Day War, 1967: The significance of the Cairo Conference (1964)"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of the Israeli raid on Samu in November 1966. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the damage done to King Hussein's standing with his Palestinian subjects and the West Bank riots, his move towards Egypt and the defence pact of 30 May 1967, the discrediting of Jordan's policy of restraint, or the general sharpening of the raid-and-reprisal cycle before 1967.","sol":"One consequence was that King Hussein of Jordan was driven towards Nasser. Israel attacked Samu, a village in the Jordanian-held West Bank, on 13 November 1966, killing sixteen Jordanian soldiers, and Palestinians in the West Bank rioted because their king had been unable to protect them. Since Hussein was now accused at home and by Egyptian radio of hiding behind the border while Palestinians were attacked, he could no longer hold to his policy of restraint. That is why he flew to Cairo on 30 May 1967 and put his army under Egyptian command, which brought Jordan into the Six Day War and cost him the West Bank.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The raid was aimed at Palestinian fighters but it was Jordanian soldiers who died; ask whose position that weakened.","Think about what a king looks like to his own people when a neighbouring army can enter his territory and leave again.","State the consequence, support it with what Hussein did in the following spring, then explain the link back to the raid."],"codes":["P5-2 — 1 The Six Day War, 1967: The significance of the Cairo Conference (1964)","P5-2 — 1 The Six Day War, 1967: The actions of the USSR, Nasser and the USA"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the closure of the Straits of Tiran in May 1967. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the decision in Israel to strike first on 5 June 1967, the failure of American attempts to open the Straits by international action and Israel's conclusion that it stood alone, or the rallying of Arab opinion behind Nasser that produced the Egypt-Jordan pact of 30 May.","sol":"One consequence was that Israel decided to attack rather than wait. Nasser announced on 22 May 1967 that the Straits of Tiran were shut to Israeli shipping, cutting the port of Eilat off from the Red Sea, and Israel had stated since 1957 that it would treat such a closure as an act of war. The USA urged patience and tried to organise an international convoy to force the Straits, but no fleet was assembled and Washington was absorbed by Vietnam. Because no outside power would reopen the waterway, the Israeli cabinet concluded that only its own army could, and struck on 5 June 1967.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The Straits controlled the route to one Israeli port, so the question is what Israel lost by the closure and what it had long promised to do if it ever lost it.","Consider the American response as well as the Israeli one: what happens to a threatened state when its friends offer words rather than ships?","Name the consequence, support it with the dated detail, then explain why the closure made waiting look more dangerous than fighting."],"codes":["P5-2 — 1 The Six Day War, 1967: The actions of the USSR, Nasser and the USA"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of the Khartoum Resolution of September 1967. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the closing off of direct negotiation just as the UN was framing Resolution 242, the hardening of Israeli policy towards the occupied territories, or the Arab subsidies to Egypt and Jordan that allowed them to rearm and fight on.","sol":"One consequence was that Egypt could go on refusing a settlement without going bankrupt. The Arab summit that ended at Khartoum on 1 September 1967 set its three noes, no peace with Israel, no recognition of Israel and no negotiation with Israel, beside a decision that the oil states would pay for that position: Saudi Arabia, Kuwait and Libya agreed to send annual subsidies to Egypt and Jordan. Because those payments went some way to replace the Suez Canal tolls Egypt had lost, Nasser could rearm with Soviet weapons and open the War of Attrition along the canal in March 1969 rather than bargain for the return of Sinai.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The summit is remembered for three refusals, but it also settled what the oil states would do; ask what a refusal needs if it is to be kept up for years.","Egypt had lost the canal tolls and the Sinai oilfields in June, so ask what made it possible for Cairo to hold out instead of coming to terms.","Name the consequence, support it with what the summit agreed and with what Egypt did next, then explain why that made a settlement less likely."],"codes":["P5-2 — 2 Aftermath of the 1967 war: UN Resolution 242","P5-2 — 3 Israel and Egypt, 1967–73: Egyptian relations with Israel, the USA, the USSR and other Arab states"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Battle of Karameh in March 1968. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the 4-mark consequence grid. Credit the surge of recruitment to Fatah and the other groups, Arafat's election as chairman of the PLO in February 1969 and the takeover of the PLO by the fighters, or the growth of an armed Palestinian presence in Jordan that led to Black September in 1970.","sol":"One consequence was that Yasser Arafat and Fatah took control of the PLO. When Israeli forces attacked the Fatah base at Karameh in Jordan on 21 March 1968 they met resistance from Palestinian fighters and Jordanian artillery and lost about thirty men, and although Palestinian losses were far heavier Arafat presented the battle as the first time Palestinians had stood up to the Israeli army. Thousands of young Palestinians volunteered in the following months, and in February 1969 Arafat was elected chairman of the PLO. That turned the PLO from a body created by the Arab governments into one run by the armed groups themselves.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Israel held the field at Karameh, so the important consequence is about reputation rather than about ground gained.","Ask who benefited from the story of the battle, and what that person held by the following February.","Name the consequence, support it with the dated change of leadership, then explain how a claimed victory produced it."],"codes":["P5-2 — 2 Aftermath of the 1967 war: The use of terrorism and international attitudes"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Israeli capture of the Golan Heights in June 1967. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of UN Resolution 242 of November 1967. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4 on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the end of the Syrian shelling of the farms below the Heights, Israel's treatment of the plateau as a security necessity rather than a bargaining counter, Syria's refusal to accept the loss, or the creation of the northern front on which Syria attacked in October 1973. In 1(b) credit the deadlock created by its ambiguous wording, the Jarring mission and its failure, the PLO's rejection of a text that treated Palestinians only as refugees, or its later use as the basis of Resolution 338 and of the Camp David process.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that Israel gained a defensive line it would not give back, so the Golan became a permanent front. Syrian guns on the Heights had shelled the farms below them for years, and once Israeli forces held the plateau on 10 June 1967 the army treated it as the ground that made northern Israel safe rather than as a bargaining counter. Because Israel would not trade it and Syria would not accept the loss, the two armies faced each other across the ceasefire line until Syrian armour attacked there on 6 October 1973, and the first days of that war brought Israel's hardest defensive battle. 1(b) One consequence was that the two sides were given a text each could quote against the other, so no settlement followed. The English wording called for withdrawal from territories occupied rather than from the territories, so Israel argued that it need not return everything and that withdrawal must follow a negotiated peace, while Egypt and Jordan accepted the resolution precisely because they read it as requiring full withdrawal. Gunnar Jarring's mission therefore achieved nothing, and the PLO rejected 242 outright because it mentioned Palestinians only as refugees.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate parts on two separate things: a captured plateau in 1(a) and a Security Council resolution in 1(b), so neither answer can borrow from the other.","For 1(a) ask why Israel wanted the Heights in the first place, and what a government does with ground it has taken for its own safety.","For 1(b) look closely at the wording of the resolution, and ask why a text everybody voted for could be accepted by both sides at once."],"codes":["P5-2 — 1 The Six Day War, 1967: Key events of the war","P5-2 — 2 Aftermath of the 1967 war: UN Resolution 242","P5-2 — 3 Israel and Egypt, 1967–73: Israel's consolidation of control of the occupied territories"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the War of Attrition of 1969-70. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the PFLP hijackings of September 1970. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the direct Soviet military presence in Egypt and the risk of superpower confrontation, the American diplomatic initiative that produced the ceasefire of 7 August 1970, the heavy losses on both sides, or the missile belt that later covered the crossing of 1973. In 1(b) credit King Hussein's decision to destroy the Palestinian armed presence in Jordan, the worldwide publicity the Palestinian cause received, or the hardening of western opinion against it and the new airport security that followed.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that the USSR became directly involved in the fighting. When Israel answered Egyptian shelling of the Bar-Lev Line with bombing raids deep inside Egypt early in 1970, Moscow sent missile crews and pilots of its own to defend Egyptian cities, so Soviet personnel were in combat against Israeli aircraft. Because that risked a clash between the superpowers themselves, Washington pressed for an end to the fighting, and the Rogers Plan produced a ceasefire on 7 August 1970. 1(b) One consequence was that King Hussein moved to destroy the Palestinian armed groups in Jordan. The PFLP hijacked airliners on 6 September 1970 and landed two of them, a TWA and a Swissair aircraft, at a desert strip near Zarqa; a BOAC aircraft hijacked on 9 September was flown there to join them, and all three were blown up before the world's press on 12 September, which showed that the king no longer controlled his own territory. He declared martial law on 16 September, and from 17 September his army attacked the camps; by July 1971 the PLO leadership had been driven out of Jordan, mostly into Lebanon.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two parts, two events: a war fought with artillery along a canal, and airliners hijacked to a desert strip in Jordan.","For 1(a) follow the escalation upwards: when Egypt could not answer Israeli bombing on its own, who answered for it?","For 1(b) the hijackers were not in Jordan by accident; ask what the pictures from the desert told King Hussein about his own authority."],"codes":["P5-2 — 3 Israel and Egypt, 1967–73: Egyptian relations with Israel, the USA, the USSR and other Arab states","P5-2 — 2 Aftermath of the 1967 war: The use of terrorism and international attitudes"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the expulsion of the PLO from Jordan in 1970-71. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the attack on the Israeli team at the Munich Olympics in September 1972. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the move of the PLO's bases to Lebanon and the destabilising of that country, the loss of a frontier with Israel from which to operate, or the founding of the Black September group and the turn to attacks abroad. In 1(b) credit the worldwide attention given to the Palestinian cause, Israel's air raids on Palestinian bases and its long campaign of targeted killings, the hardening of western governments against the PLO, or the new security measures at airports and sporting events.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that the Palestinian movement's base moved to Lebanon. King Hussein's army drove the fighters out of Amman and then out of the north of the kingdom, and by July 1971 the leadership had gone, most of it to Beirut and the refugee camps of southern Lebanon. Because Lebanon's government was far weaker than Jordan's and its population was divided along sectarian lines, the armed groups could act with much less restraint, which drew Lebanon towards the civil war that began in 1975. 1(b) One consequence was that Israel began a long campaign of retaliation outside its own borders. A group calling itself Black September broke into the Israeli team's quarters on 5 September 1972, killed two of the team at the outset and took nine hostage; the nine were killed in the failed rescue at the Fürstenfeldbruck airbase, along with a West German policeman and five of the attackers, so eleven Israelis died in all, in a siege watched live by a television audience of millions. Israel bombed Palestinian bases in Lebanon and Syria within days and then set out to hunt down those it held responsible, wherever they were, so the conflict was carried into European cities.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two parts, two events: an army clearing guerrillas out of a kingdom, and nine Israeli hostages taken at an Olympic Games.","For 1(a) ask where fighters go when they are expelled, and what a weaker neighbouring state means for them and for it.","For 1(b) the attack was designed to be watched; consider what both the watching world and the Israeli government did afterwards."],"codes":["P5-2 — 2 Aftermath of the 1967 war: The use of terrorism and international attitudes"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of Sadat's expulsion of the Soviet military advisers from Egypt in July 1972. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the Arab oil embargo of October 1973. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the freedom it gave Sadat to plan an offensive without Soviet restraint, the signal sent to Washington that Egypt could be detached from the USSR, or the surprise achieved in October 1973 because Israel read the expulsion as weakness. In 1(b) credit the quadrupling of the oil price and the economic shock in the West, the pressure it put on western governments to press Israel and to distance themselves from it, or the new weight it gave Saudi Arabia and the Arab oil states in the conflict.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was that Israel came to believe Egypt could not attack. Sadat sent the Soviet military advisers home in July 1972 because Moscow would not give him the weapons for an offensive and restrained him from using what he had, but Israeli intelligence read the expulsion as proof that Egypt had lost its patron and was in no condition to fight. That misreading helped Egypt achieve complete surprise on 6 October 1973. 1(b) One consequence was a sharp shift in western attitudes towards the conflict. The Arab oil producers cut production from 17 October 1973 and embargoed the United States from 20 October, and the price of oil roughly quadrupled by January 1974, bringing petrol rationing and speed limits to countries that had never questioned cheap fuel. Because European and Japanese economies depended on Arab oil, their governments began to press for Israeli withdrawal and to treat the Palestinian question as one they could no longer ignore.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two parts, two events: a president sending foreign advisers home, and an oil producers' decision taken during a war.","For 1(a) ask how the expulsion looked from Tel Aviv rather than from Cairo.","For 1(b) follow the money outwards: who bought Arab oil, and what did a fourfold price rise make those governments willing to say?"],"codes":["P5-2 — 3 Israel and Egypt, 1967–73: Egyptian relations with Israel, the USA, the USSR and other Arab states","P5-2 — 3 Israel and Egypt, 1967–73: Key events of the Yom Kippur War (1973)"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the aftermath of the Six Day War in the years 1967-70. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the Khartoum Conference (September 1967); the War of Attrition. You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 on the narrative grid. Level 3 (6-8) needs a clear sequence of events leading to an outcome with the linkage between them analysed. Cap: 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' There is no cap at Level 2. Credit a spine running from the occupation and the refugee flight, through Khartoum and Resolution 242, to settlement building, Karameh and the War of Attrition, with the ceasefire of August 1970 as the outcome.","sol":"The war ended on 10 June 1967 with Israel holding Sinai, Gaza, the West Bank, East Jerusalem and the Golan Heights, about a million Palestinians under military government and some 300,000 more in flight, most of them into Jordan. Israel expected to trade the land for recognition, but the Arab summit at Khartoum, ending on 1 September 1967, answered with no peace, no recognition and no negotiation. Because nobody would bargain, Israel went on making the occupation permanent: it had extended its law to an enlarged East Jerusalem at the end of June and founded Merom Golan on the Golan on 14 July, before the summit met, and it did not stop afterwards, re-establishing Kfar Etzion in the West Bank on 27 September and following the Allon Plan's line of settlements along the Jordan valley. The Security Council tried to break the deadlock with Resolution 242 on 22 November 1967, but its call for withdrawal from territories occupied was deliberately imprecise, so both sides accepted the wording and neither accepted the other's reading, and Gunnar Jarring's mission went nowhere. With diplomacy dead, force filled the gap. Palestinians turned to their own fighters after Karameh in March 1968, and Nasser, rearmed by the USSR, opened the War of Attrition along the canal in March 1969. That in turn drew Soviet crews into Egypt's defence and frightened Washington into brokering the ceasefire of 7 August 1970. Three years after the victory, Israel held the same land, and the conflict had simply found new forms.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Choose a spine of three or four dated episodes inside the years given; the account has to move, not to sit on the results of the war.","The mark scheme rewards linkage, so before writing, put the word 'because' between each episode and the next and see whether the sentence is true.","You must go beyond the two bullets: Resolution 242, the settlements and Karameh are all material of your own."],"codes":["P5-2 — 2 Aftermath of the 1967 war: UN Resolution 242","P5-2 — 2 Aftermath of the 1967 war: Palestinian refugees and the occupied territories","P5-2 — 3 Israel and Egypt, 1967–73: Israel's consolidation of control of the occupied territories"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the growth of Palestinian armed resistance in the years 1964-72. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the founding of the PLO (1964); the Battle of Karameh (1968). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 on the narrative grid, Level 3 requiring a clear analysed sequence. Cap: 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' No cap at Level 2. Credit a spine running from Cairo 1964 and Fatah's first raid, through the defeat of 1967 and Karameh, to Arafat's leadership, the PFLP hijackings, Black September in Jordan and Munich.","sol":"The PLO was created by the Arab states at the Cairo summit of January 1964 and proclaimed in East Jerusalem that May under Ahmad Shukeiri, but it was funded and steered by governments, so Fatah answered by acting for itself with a first raid on Israel's National Water Carrier in January 1965. The defeat of June 1967 then destroyed the claim of the Arab armies to be the Palestinians' protectors, which left the small armed groups as the only force still fighting. Karameh made that visible: when Israel attacked the Fatah base in Jordan on 21 March 1968 and lost about thirty men, Arafat claimed a victory, thousands volunteered, and in February 1969 he became chairman of a PLO now run by the fighters rather than by the governments. Because the groups could not defeat Israel in the field, some turned to spectacle. The PFLP hijacked airliners on 6 September 1970 and flew two of them to a desert strip near Zarqa, where a third, hijacked on 9 September, joined them; all three were destroyed before the world's cameras on 12 September, which convinced King Hussein that he had lost control of Jordan; his army attacked the camps from 17 September and by July 1971 the leadership had been driven into Lebanon. Expelled from the frontier they had used, the militants struck abroad instead, and Black September killed eleven Israeli team members at Munich in September 1972. In eight years the Palestinians had made themselves the centre of their own cause, and had also made much of the world call it terrorism.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The dates give you eight years, so the account needs a direction of travel: who is doing the fighting at the start, and who by the end?","Analysis here means explaining why each stage made the next one likely, especially why failure in open war pushed the groups towards other methods.","Go beyond the two bullets: the hijackings, the fighting in Jordan and Munich are your own material, and you need three or more aspects for Level 3."],"codes":["P5-2 — 1 The Six Day War, 1967: The significance of the Cairo Conference (1964)","P5-2 — 2 Aftermath of the 1967 war: The use of terrorism and international attitudes"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the events that led to the outbreak of the Yom Kippur War in the years 1970-73. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the death of Nasser (September 1970); the expulsion of Soviet military advisers from Egypt (1972). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 on the narrative grid, Level 3 requiring an analysed sequence leading to an outcome. Cap: 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' No cap at Level 2. Credit a spine running from the ceasefire of August 1970 and Nasser's death, through Sadat's rejected offer of February 1971 and the expulsion of the Soviet advisers, to the alliance with Syria and the attack of 6 October 1973.","sol":"The War of Attrition ended with the ceasefire of 7 August 1970, leaving Israel on the east bank of the canal behind the Bar-Lev Line and Egypt with no way forward. Nasser died on 28 September 1970 and Sadat, who succeeded him, first tried diplomacy: in February 1971 he offered to reopen the Suez Canal and move towards a settlement if Israel pulled back from it, and was refused, largely because Israel saw no reason to give up ground it had held for four years without trouble. Because nothing moved, Sadat concluded that only fighting would interest Washington in a settlement. That required an offensive capacity the USSR would not supply, so in July 1972 he expelled the Soviet military advisers, freeing himself from Moscow's restraint and, as it turned out, persuading Israeli intelligence that Egypt was too weak to attack. He then built the conditions for surprise: a military alliance with Syria for a two-front assault, repeated mobilisations that Israel learned to ignore, and a date, 6 October 1973, that fell on the fast of Yom Kippur when Israel's reservists were at prayer. The result was that a war Israel believed impossible began with Egyptian troops across the canal and Syrian armour in the Golan.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The years run from a ceasefire to an attack, so the account must explain a decision: why did Sadat come to prefer war to waiting?","Each episode should make the next more likely, so test your links: the refusal of 1971 explains the expulsion of 1972, which explains something about October 1973.","Beyond the two bullets you need your own material: the Bar-Lev Line, the offer of February 1971 and the alliance with Syria will do it."],"codes":["P5-2 — 3 Israel and Egypt, 1967–73: Egyptian relations with Israel, the USA, the USSR and other Arab states","P5-2 — 3 Israel and Egypt, 1967–73: Key events of the Yom Kippur War (1973)"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the Six Day War (1967) for the Palestinians. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts, this part marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Level 3 needs analysis of importance in a coherent line of reasoning. No stimulus points and no cap. Credit the occupation of the West Bank and Gaza and the new refugee wave, the loss of faith in the Arab states and the rise of Fatah and the PLO under Arafat, the exclusion of the Palestinians from Resolution 242, and the beginning of settlement.","sol":"The importance of the Six Day War for the Palestinians lies in what it changed about who ruled them and who spoke for them. Before June 1967 most Palestinians outside Israel lived under Egyptian administration in Gaza or as Jordanian citizens in the West Bank; after it, about a million of them lived under Israeli military government, which controlled the courts, the permits and all movement, while some 300,000 more became refugees, most of them crossing into Jordan. That single week therefore created the occupation that has framed the Palestinian question ever since. Its second effect was political. The Arab armies had promised deliverance and had lost in six days, so Palestinians stopped waiting for them: Fatah's claimed victory at Karameh in March 1968 brought thousands of recruits, and by February 1969 Arafat chaired a PLO run by the fighters rather than by the Arab governments. Its third effect was to define how the world would discuss them. Resolution 242 of November 1967 spoke of a just settlement of the refugee problem and never of a Palestinian people, so the Palestinians rejected it and concluded that they would only be heard if they forced the world to look, which shaped the hijackings and the attacks of the years that followed. The war matters most, then, because it turned the Palestinians from a problem others negotiated about into a movement acting for itself under occupation.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Importance means the difference it made, so begin by asking what the Palestinian position was in May 1967 and what it was by 1969.","Three lines will carry the answer: who governed them, who led them, and how the outside world described them.","Each paragraph should end by saying what the change amounted to, and the last sentence must answer the word 'importance' directly."],"codes":["P5-2 — 1 The Six Day War, 1967: Key events of the war","P5-2 — 2 Aftermath of the 1967 war: Palestinian refugees and the occupied territories","P5-2 — 2 Aftermath of the 1967 war: The use of terrorism and international attitudes"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the Yom Kippur War (1973) for relations between Egypt and Israel. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts, this part marked out of 8 on the importance grid, with no stimulus points and no cap. Credit the restoration of Egyptian confidence through the crossing of the canal, the shattering of Israel's belief in secure borders and the political crisis that followed, the American role as broker after the ceasefire of 25 October, and the disengagement that opened the road to Camp David.","sol":"The importance of the October war for relations between Egypt and Israel is that it made negotiation possible for the first time. On 6 October 1973 Egyptian troops crossed the Suez Canal, breached the sand rampart and overran most of the Bar-Lev Line, a barrier Israel had believed impassable. Although Israeli forces recovered, crossed to the west bank between 15 and 16 October and had cut off the Egyptian Third Army by 24 October, the crossing itself had already done its work: Sadat could negotiate as a leader who had proved his army could fight, rather than as the heir to the defeat of 1967. The war also changed Israel. About 2,700 Israelis were killed, far more than in 1967, and the sense of security that the occupied territories were supposed to provide did not survive the first days of fighting, so holding every inch of ground lost its obvious appeal. Thirdly, the fighting drew in the powers that could broker a deal: the ceasefire came under Resolution 338 on 22 October and took hold on 25 October, and the oil embargo gave Washington strong reasons to press for movement, which produced the disengagement agreement of January 1974 and Kissinger's shuttle diplomacy. The war was therefore important not because it changed the map, which it barely did, but because it changed what each side believed it could get by talking.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Neither side won outright, so the importance cannot be measured in territory; ask what each government believed afterwards that it had not believed before.","Take Egypt, then Israel, then the outside powers, and give each one dated evidence.","The judgement should say what made talking possible, and it must answer the word 'importance' rather than retell the fighting."],"codes":["P5-2 — 3 Israel and Egypt, 1967–73: Key events of the Yom Kippur War (1973)","P5-2 — 3 Israel and Egypt, 1967–73: Egyptian relations with Israel, the USA, the USSR and other Arab states"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of Nasser for the escalation of the conflict in the years 1964-70. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts, this part marked out of 8 on the importance grid, with no stimulus points and no cap. Credit his calling of the Cairo summit and the creation of the PLO, the decisions of May 1967 over UNEF, Sinai and the Straits of Tiran, the leadership of the Arab world that brought Jordan into the war, the Khartoum position and the War of Attrition, and his mediation in Jordan shortly before his death.","sol":"Nasser's importance is that at almost every stage between 1964 and 1970 the conflict escalated because of a choice he made. He called the Cairo summit of January 1964 to answer Israel's water scheme, and it was his summit that created the PLO and the United Arab Command, giving the Palestinian cause an organisation and the Arab states a joint military framework. In May 1967 his decisions turned a border crisis into a war: he asked for the UN force to leave on 16 May, moved his army up to the frontier, closed the Straits of Tiran on 22 May and signed a defence pact with King Hussein on 30 May, which brought Jordan into the fighting and cost it the West Bank. His standing was the reason those decisions mattered so much, because no other Arab leader could have made the rest follow. After the defeat he escalated again rather than settle, endorsing the three noes at Khartoum and launching the War of Attrition in March 1969, which drew Soviet crews into Egypt and brought the superpowers closer to each other than at any point in the conflict. Yet his last act was to mediate an end to the fighting in Jordan in September 1970, the day before he died. Nasser mattered, then, as the man whose prestige turned local quarrels into regional wars, and whose death removed the only leader who could have kept doing so.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Importance here means the difference one man made, so ask which events would probably not have happened in the same way without him.","Work in periods: the summit of 1964, the crisis of May 1967, and the years after the defeat.","Leave room to qualify the claim, then close with a judgement that answers the word 'importance' directly."],"codes":["P5-2 — 1 The Six Day War, 1967: The actions of the USSR, Nasser and the USA","P5-2 — 1 The Six Day War, 1967: The significance of the Cairo Conference (1964)","P5-2 — 3 Israel and Egypt, 1967–73: Egyptian relations with Israel, the USA, the USSR and other Arab states"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date did Nasser announce the closing of the Straits of Tiran to Israeli shipping?","a":"22 May 1967."},{"q":"Which territories did Israel take from each of Egypt, Jordan and Syria in June 1967?","a":"Sinai and the Gaza Strip from Egypt, the West Bank and East Jerusalem from Jordan, and the Golan Heights from Syria."},{"q":"Who became chairman of the PLO in February 1969?","a":"Yasser Arafat."},{"q":"On what date did Egypt and Syria attack Israel to begin the Yom Kippur War?","a":"6 October 1973."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The Cairo Conference, January 1964","back":"The first summit of Arab heads of state, called by Nasser. It approved diverting the headwaters of the River Jordan, set up a United Arab Command under an Egyptian general, and agreed to create a Palestinian organisation.","code":"P5-2 — 1 The Six Day War, 1967: The significance of the Cairo Conference (1964)","open":false},{"front":"The PLO and Fatah, 1964 to 1965","back":"The PLO was proclaimed at a Palestinian congress in East Jerusalem in May 1964 under Ahmad Shukeiri, created and funded by the Arab states. Fatah, led by Arafat, answered by raiding Israel's National Water Carrier in January 1965.","code":"P5-2 — 1 The Six Day War, 1967: The significance of the Cairo Conference (1964)","open":false},{"front":"Israel's consolidation of the occupied territories after 1967","back":"Settlement from July 1967 at Merom Golan and September 1967 at Kfar Etzion; the Allon Plan's line of settlements along the Jordan valley; new neighbourhoods around East Jerusalem; military government in Gaza and the West Bank.","code":"P5-2 — 3 Israel and Egypt, 1967–73: Israel's consolidation of control of the occupied territories","open":false},{"front":"The Samu raid, 13 November 1966","back":"An Israeli reprisal on a village in the Jordanian-held West Bank after a mine killed three Israeli border police. Sixteen Jordanian soldiers died and West Bank Palestinians rioted against King Hussein for failing to protect them.","code":"P5-2 — 1 The Six Day War, 1967: The actions of the USSR, Nasser and the USA","open":false},{"front":"One step in the escalation of May 1967","back":"The false Soviet report of Israeli troops on the Syrian border, 13 May; Egypt's request for UNEF to leave, 16 May, agreed by U Thant on 18 May; the closing of the Straits of Tiran, 22 May; the Egypt-Jordan defence pact, 30 May.","code":"P5-2 — 1 The Six Day War, 1967: The actions of the USSR, Nasser and the USA","open":true},{"front":"The Six Day War, 5 to 10 June 1967","back":"Israel destroyed the Egyptian air force on the ground on 5 June, took Sinai and Gaza, then the West Bank, entering the Old City of Jerusalem on 7 June, and stormed the Golan Heights on 9 and 10 June.","code":"P5-2 — 1 The Six Day War, 1967: Key events of the war","open":false},{"front":"UN Resolution 242, 22 November 1967","back":"Land for peace: withdrawal from territories occupied in the recent conflict, an end to belligerency, secure and recognised boundaries, and a just settlement of the refugee problem. Gunnar Jarring was sent to mediate.","code":"P5-2 — 2 Aftermath of the 1967 war: UN Resolution 242","open":false},{"front":"The Khartoum Resolution, 1 September 1967","back":"The Arab summit at Khartoum declared no peace with Israel, no recognition of Israel and no negotiation with Israel, while agreeing to subsidise Egypt and Jordan and to work for the return of the occupied territories.","code":"P5-2 — 2 Aftermath of the 1967 war: UN Resolution 242","open":false},{"front":"The occupied territories after 1967","back":"Sinai, a buffer with oilfields; the Golan Heights, commanding northern Israel; the Gaza Strip and West Bank, holding about a million Palestinians under military government; East Jerusalem, whose boundaries Israel enlarged in June 1967.","code":"P5-2 — 2 Aftermath of the 1967 war: Palestinian refugees and the occupied territories","open":false},{"front":"The refugees of 1967","back":"About 300,000 Palestinians left the West Bank and Gaza during and after the war, most of them for Jordan, where their arrival shifted the balance of the kingdom's population and strengthened the armed groups.","code":"P5-2 — 2 Aftermath of the 1967 war: Palestinian refugees and the occupied territories","open":false},{"front":"The Battle of Karameh, 21 March 1968","back":"Israel attacked a Fatah base in Jordan and lost about thirty men to Palestinian fighters and Jordanian artillery. Palestinian losses were heavier, but Arafat claimed victory, recruits poured in, and he chaired the PLO from February 1969.","code":"P5-2 — 2 Aftermath of the 1967 war: The use of terrorism and international attitudes","open":false},{"front":"One Palestinian attack that drew world attention, 1970 to 1972","back":"The PFLP hijackings that began on 6 September 1970, with three airliners blown up near Zarqa on 12 September; the Black September attack on the Israeli team at the Munich Olympics on 5 September 1972, in which eleven Israelis died.","code":"P5-2 — 2 Aftermath of the 1967 war: The use of terrorism and international attitudes","open":true},{"front":"Black September in Jordan, 1970 to 1971","back":"King Hussein declared martial law on 16 September 1970 and his army attacked the Palestinian camps from 17 September. Thousands died and by July 1971 the PLO leadership had been driven out, mostly into Lebanon.","code":"P5-2 — 2 Aftermath of the 1967 war: The use of terrorism and international attitudes","open":false},{"front":"The War of Attrition, 1969 to 1970","back":"Nasser proclaimed it in March 1969, shelling the Bar-Lev Line along the canal. Israel bombed deep inside Egypt, the USSR sent missile crews and pilots, and the Rogers Plan produced a ceasefire on 7 August 1970.","code":"P5-2 — 3 Israel and Egypt, 1967–73: Egyptian relations with Israel, the USA, the USSR and other Arab states","open":false},{"front":"The Yom Kippur War, 6 to 25 October 1973","back":"Egypt crossed the Suez Canal and Syria attacked the Golan. A US airlift began on 14 October; Israel crossed the canal on 15-16 October and cut off Egypt's Third Army by 24 October. Resolution 338 was passed on 22 October.","code":"P5-2 — 3 Israel and Egypt, 1967–73: Key events of the Yom Kippur War (1973)","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Paper 2 booklet P Question 2, narrative account of the Yom Kippur War, 8 marks)","items":["The question: 'Write a narrative account analysing the key events of the Yom Kippur War (1973).' You may use the following in your answer: the Egyptian crossing of the Suez Canal (6 October 1973); the Arab oil embargo. You must also use information of your own.","The paragraph: Egypt's crossing on 6 October 1973 succeeded because it was timed for the fast of Yom Kippur, when Israel's reservists were at prayer and its roads were empty; the sand rampart of the Bar-Lev Line was breached with water cannon and most of the forts were overrun within a day, while Syrian armour drove into the Golan Heights from the north. Because Israel was fighting on two fronts at once and losing tanks and aircraft faster than it could replace them, it had to stop the Syrians first, and it was only after an American airlift began on 14 October that it could turn south in strength. That turn produced the decisive move of the war: between 15 and 16 October Israeli forces crossed to the west bank of the canal, and because the ceasefire called for on 22 October did not hold they drove on to the road to Suez, closing the ring round the Egyptian Third Army by 24 October and converting an Egyptian opening success into a stalemate that neither side could break. The fighting therefore ended not in victory but in a ceasefire under Resolution 338, which finally took hold on 25 October, with both superpowers now directly involved.","Why this reaches Level 3: it 'organises material into a clear sequence of events leading to an outcome' and 'analyses the linkage between them' rather than listing, since each sentence explains why the next stage followed; the information is accurate and precisely dated, meeting the AO1 requirement of 'good knowledge and understanding of the key features or characteristics of the events'; and it addresses well over three aspects of content, so the Level 3 cap does not bite."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["The same question: 'Write a narrative account analysing the key events of the Yom Kippur War (1973).'","The paragraph: Egypt and Syria attacked Israel on 6 October 1973. The Egyptians crossed the Suez Canal and got past the Bar-Lev Line, and the Syrians attacked the Golan Heights. Israel was taken by surprise because it was a religious holiday. Then the Americans sent supplies and Israel fought back and crossed the canal itself. There was a ceasefire later in October and the Arab states also used oil against the West, which caused problems for many countries.","Why this stays at Level 2: it 'shows some organisation of material into a sequence of events leading to an outcome' and so earns the middle band, but the linkage is asserted rather than analysed, since nothing explains why the surprise mattered, why the airlift changed the balance or why the crossing of the canal ended the war. The detail is thin and mostly undated, so it shows 'some knowledge' rather than good knowledge, and phrases such as 'caused problems for many countries' are the generalisation the Level 3 descriptor rules out."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the road to war, 1964 to May 1967","items":["Cairo summit of Arab heads of state, 13 to 17 January 1964: called by Nasser to answer Israel's National Water Carrier.","Its three decisions: divert the headwaters of the River Jordan in Lebanon and Syria; create a United Arab Command under an Egyptian general; give the Palestinians an organisation of their own.","The PLO proclaimed by a Palestinian congress in East Jerusalem at the end of May 1964, chaired by Ahmad Shukeiri, with the Palestine Liberation Army as its force.","Fatah, led by Yasser Arafat, mounted its first raid on Israel in January 1965, an attempt to sabotage the National Water Carrier; Syria gave Fatah shelter and support.","Israel attacked the Syrian diversion works in 1965 and 1966, and the border war of raid and reprisal grew from there.","Samu, 13 November 1966: Israel's largest operation since 1956, on a village in the Jordanian-held West Bank, in reprisal for a mine that killed three Israeli border police; sixteen Jordanian soldiers were killed and riots followed in the West Bank.","7 April 1967: a clash on the Syrian border grew into an air battle in which Israel shot down six Syrian MiG-21s.","13 May 1967: Soviet intelligence told Egypt, wrongly, that Israel was massing troops on the Syrian border.","16 May 1967: Egypt asked for the United Nations Emergency Force to leave Sinai and Gaza; U Thant agreed on 18 May.","22 May 1967: Nasser announced that the Straits of Tiran were closed to Israeli shipping, cutting Eilat off from the Red Sea.","30 May 1967: King Hussein signed a defence pact with Egypt in Cairo, placing the Jordanian army under Egyptian command."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Six Day War, 5 to 10 June 1967","items":["5 June 1967: Israel struck first, destroying the great bulk of the Egyptian air force on the ground within hours, then attacking the Jordanian, Syrian and Iraqi air forces the same day.","With air superiority won, Israeli armour drove across Sinai and the Gaza Strip; Egyptian forces were ordered back to the canal and were cut up as they retreated.","Jordan shelled West Jerusalem on 5 June, misled by Egyptian claims of victory; Israel answered by taking the West Bank.","7 June 1967: Israeli troops entered the Old City of Jerusalem.","9 and 10 June 1967: Israel stormed the Golan Heights, from which Syrian guns had shelled Israeli farms.","The ceasefire held from 10 June 1967.","Territory gained: Sinai and the Gaza Strip from Egypt; the West Bank and East Jerusalem from Jordan; the Golan Heights from Syria, roughly trebling the land Israel controlled.","Losses: fewer than a thousand Israelis killed; Arab dead ran into many thousands, and the Egyptian army lost most of its equipment.","The Suez Canal was closed in June 1967 and stayed closed until 1975.","At the end of June 1967 Israel extended its law to East Jerusalem and enlarged the city's boundaries; the UN General Assembly declared the measures invalid in July."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the aftermath, 1967 to 1968 — 242, Khartoum, refugees and the territories","items":["Khartoum summit, 29 August to 1 September 1967: the three noes, no peace with Israel, no recognition of Israel, no negotiation with Israel.","Khartoum also agreed subsidies from the oil states for Egypt and Jordan, an end to the oil boycott of the West and a settlement of the war in Yemen.","UN Security Council Resolution 242, passed unanimously on 22 November 1967: land for peace, withdrawal from territories occupied in the recent conflict, an end to belligerency, secure and recognised boundaries, and a just settlement of the refugee problem.","The English text said 'territories occupied' and not 'the territories', so Israel argued for partial withdrawal after a negotiated peace while Egypt and Jordan read it as full withdrawal.","Syria rejected 242; the PLO rejected it because it treated Palestinians only as refugees, not as a people with a claim to a state.","Gunnar Jarring, a Swedish diplomat, was appointed UN special representative to talk to the parties; his mission achieved nothing.","About a million Palestinians in the West Bank and Gaza came under Israeli military government, roughly a third of them refugees from 1948 living in camps.","About 300,000 Palestinians left the West Bank and Gaza during and after the war, most of them for Jordan.","Merom Golan, the first settlement on the Golan Heights, was founded in July 1967; Kfar Etzion, the first in the West Bank, in September 1967.","The Allon Plan of 1967 proposed a line of settlements along the Jordan valley to hold a defensible frontier while leaving the crowded Palestinian hill towns under Arab administration."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Palestinian resistance and international attitudes, 1968 to 1972","items":["The Popular Front for the Liberation of Palestine was founded by George Habash in 1967 and chose hijacking and attacks abroad as its method.","Karameh, 21 March 1968: an Israeli attack on a Fatah base and refugee camp in Jordan; Jordanian artillery joined in, about thirty Israeli soldiers were killed, and Palestinian losses were far heavier.","Arafat presented Karameh as a victory; thousands volunteered, and in February 1969 he was elected chairman of the PLO, which passed from the Arab governments to the armed groups.","6 September 1970: the PFLP hijacked airliners and flew two of them, a TWA and a Swissair aircraft, to Dawson's Field, a desert strip near Zarqa in Jordan; the El Al attempt failed and the Pan Am aircraft was flown to Cairo.","9 September 1970: a BOAC aircraft was hijacked and flown to Dawson's Field to join the other two; all three were blown up before the world's press on 12 September. The last hostages were released on 30 September 1970, in the exchange that freed Leila Khaled from British custody and other PFLP prisoners held in western Europe.","16 September 1970: King Hussein declared martial law; his army attacked the Palestinian camps from 17 September, the events the Palestinians call Black September.","By July 1971 the PLO leadership had been driven out of Jordan, most of it to Beirut and the camps of southern Lebanon.","5 September 1972: a group calling itself Black September attacked the Israeli team's quarters at the Munich Olympics, killing two of the team at the outset and taking nine hostage.","The nine hostages, one West German policeman and five of the attackers were killed in the failed rescue at the Fürstenfeldbruck airbase, so eleven Israelis died in all.","Israel answered with air raids on Palestinian bases in Lebanon and Syria and with a long campaign to hunt down those it held responsible, carrying the conflict into European cities."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Egypt, the superpowers and the Yom Kippur War, 1969 to 1974","items":["The Bar-Lev Line, a chain of forts behind a high sand rampart on the east bank of the Suez Canal, was built by Israel in 1968 and 1969.","Nasser proclaimed the War of Attrition in March 1969: artillery duels, commando raids and Soviet-supplied air defences along the canal.","Israel answered with deep bombing raids inside Egypt early in 1970; the USSR sent missile crews and pilots of its own to defend Egyptian cities.","The Rogers Plan produced a ceasefire that began on 7 August 1970.","Nasser died on 28 September 1970, the day after mediating an end to the fighting in Jordan; Anwar Sadat succeeded him.","February 1971: Sadat offered to reopen the Suez Canal and move towards a settlement if Israel withdrew from it, and was refused.","July 1972: Sadat expelled the Soviet military advisers from Egypt; Israeli intelligence read this as proof that Egypt could not attack.","6 October 1973: Egypt and Syria attacked on the fast of Yom Kippur, a day that also fell in Ramadan; Egyptian troops crossed the canal, breached the rampart with water cannon and overran most of the Bar-Lev Line.","14 October 1973: an American airlift of arms to Israel began; the USSR was resupplying Egypt and Syria.","15 to 16 October 1973: Israeli forces under Ariel Sharon crossed to the west bank of the canal and established a bridgehead behind the Egyptian armies.","17 October 1973: the Arab oil producers cut production; from 20 October they embargoed the United States, and the oil price roughly quadrupled by January 1974.","22 October 1973: UN Resolution 338 called for a ceasefire and for Resolution 242 to be carried out in all its parts; the first deadline collapsed and Israeli units drove south to the road to Suez, cutting off the Egyptian Third Army on the east bank by 24 October; the fighting stopped on 25 October, the day American forces went to a nuclear alert after a Soviet threat to intervene.","About 2,700 Israelis were killed, far more than in 1967; disengagement on the Egyptian front followed in January 1974."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 2 booklet P Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the key events of the Yom Kippur War (1973)' (8 marks)","items":["The question as printed: 'Write a narrative account analysing the key events of the Yom Kippur War (1973).' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the Egyptian crossing of the Suez Canal (6 October 1973); the Arab oil embargo. You must also use information of your own.","Decoded: the examiner wants a sequence of dated episodes inside October 1973 in which each one explains the next, ending with a statement of what the whole thing amounted to. 'Analysing' is the word that carries the marks: description of the fighting alone sits in Level 1 or 2.","The cap to respect: 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' There is no cap at Level 2. So plan at least three aspects beyond a single narrative thread, and make two of them your own rather than the printed bullets.","Timing: roughly 13 minutes of the 55 you have for booklet P. Spend two minutes listing four episodes with their dates, then write straight through; there is no time for a redraft and no SPaG mark to protect.","Episode 1, the attack, 6 October 1973: Egypt and Syria struck on the fast of Yom Kippur, a day that also fell in Ramadan, so Israel's reservists were at prayer and its roads were empty. Evidence: the crossing of the canal, the sand rampart breached with water cannon, most of the Bar-Lev Line overrun, Syrian armour into the Golan Heights.","Link 1 to 2: because Israel was fighting on two fronts at once and could not replace tanks and aircraft as fast as it was losing them, it had to deal with the nearer threat first and hold in the south.","Episode 2, the recovery in the north and the airlift, 9 to 14 October: Israel pushed the Syrians back off the Golan, and an American airlift of arms began on 14 October while the USSR resupplied Egypt and Syria. This is one of your own aspects of content.","Link 2 to 3: once resupplied and free of the Syrian front, Israel could look for a decisive move in Sinai, and the Egyptian advance beyond its missile umbrella had opened a gap between the two Egyptian armies.","Episode 3, the counter-crossing, 15 to 16 October: Israeli forces under Ariel Sharon crossed to the west bank of the canal and established a bridgehead behind the Egyptian armies, turning an Egyptian opening success into a stalemate.","Episode 4, the oil weapon and the ceasefire, 17 to 25 October: the Arab producers cut production from 17 October and embargoed the United States from 20 October; Resolution 338 called for a ceasefire on 22 October; the first deadline collapsed and Israeli units cut the road to Suez, closing the ring round the Egyptian Third Army by 24 October; the fighting stopped on 25 October, the day American forces went to a nuclear alert after a Soviet threat to send troops. This is your fourth aspect and it takes you well clear of the cap.","The point beyond the bullets: the superpower dimension. The printed bullets give you the crossing and the embargo; the airlift, the counter-crossing and the nuclear alert are the material of your own that the question requires.","What the closing sentence must do: say what the sequence amounted to rather than repeat it. Something like: in under three weeks the initiative changed hands twice, passing from Egypt and Syria on 6 October to Israel after the counter-crossing, so that when the guns finally stopped on 25 October neither army held a position it could dictate terms from."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:38:44Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"Re-judged against the kit file, not the copy in the judge prompt. All three wrong items are properly fixed and I re-verified each on the web: Munich now has two of the team killed at the outset and nine taken hostage, the nine killed at Fuerstenfeldbruck with one West German policeman and five of the eight attackers, eleven Israelis in all, and the wording is consistent across question 7, question 10, lesson.idea[5], cards[11] and the fact file; Dawson's Field now has TWA and Swissair landed near Zarqa on 6 September 1970, the El Al attempt failed and Pan Am flown to Cairo, BOAC hijacked on 9 September and flown in to join them, all three destroyed on 12 September and the last hostages freed on 30 September in the Leila Khaled exchange; question 3 is rebuilt on the Khartoum subsidies, and the Khartoum resolutions did commit Saudi Arabia, Kuwait and Libya to annual payments to Egypt and Jordan, the Egyptian share explicitly to offset the closure of the canal, so the sol's causal line to the War of Attrition of March 1969 holds. The other repairs check out too: the Third Army is now cut off by 24 October in all six places, after the collapse of the first 22 October deadline; Merom Golan (14 July 1967) and the Allon Plan sit before Khartoum and Kfar Etzion (27 September 1967) after it in question 9 and lesson.idea[7]; question 5 1(a) is now on the capture of the Golan Heights, which removes the duplication with question 12 and is itself accurate down to the plateau being held on 10 June 1967 and the Syrian attack of 6 October 1973; the timing fact and the exit item are sound. The two new worked examples are clean and well outside the bank: the East Jerusalem one is right on the Knesset law of 27 June and the government order of 28 June 1967, on the roughly seventy square kilometres of West Bank land brought inside the boundaries, on the General Assembly's July ruling and on 242 naming no city, and the consolidation narrative is right throughout; the plan's new closing line no longer hands over question 13. I re-solved all fifteen questions, checked about seventy dates, figures and attributions and verified eight afresh on the web; no stem touches the printed P5 specimen, no worked example, plan or model paragraph sets a bank question, the tiers are 5/4/6 and all nine key ideas are tested at least twice. Nothing left is a matter of truth: the five items above are editorial, and the first of them, one clause in question 3's indicative content, is the only one I would insist on before publication."}}},"P5-3":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"P5-3","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 3 is the part of the period study where the conflict stops being a series of wars and becomes a series of negotiations, and booklet P rewards exactly that. The consequences question keeps returning to single dated acts whose effects can be traced - the oil embargo, Sadat's flight to Jerusalem, the invasion of Lebanon, Arafat's statement at Geneva, the handshake on the White House lawn. The narrative account wants a short run of years in which each move forced the next, and the two decades from 1974 to 1995 supply three of them: 1977 to 1979, 1975 to 1982 and 1988 to 1993. The importance questions want one event whose effects reach forward, and this topic is full of them. If you can say, with names and dates, what shuttle diplomacy actually delivered, why Egypt made peace and what it cost her, why Israel went into Lebanon and what came back, what changed when Palestinians in the occupied territories rose in 1987, and why the end of the Cold War made Madrid and Oslo possible, you can answer any question booklet P sets on this key topic without hunting for material.","idea":[{"code":"P5-3 — 1 Diplomatic negotiations: The significance of the oil crisis","h":"Oil as a weapon: why 1973-74 pulled the superpowers into the search for a settlement","t":"During the October War of 1973 the Arab oil producers of OAPEC agreed staged cuts in production on 17 October and within days embargoed supplies to states they judged pro-Israeli: the embargo on the USA was declared on 19 and 20 October and was extended to the Netherlands later in the month. By the time the embargo was lifted in March 1974 the price of crude oil had risen roughly fourfold, from about three dollars a barrel to about twelve, and Western economies were in recession. The war itself had already brought the superpowers close to a confrontation: the USA airlifted weapons to Israel while the USSR resupplied Egypt and Syria, and when Moscow hinted that it might send troops to enforce the ceasefire on 24 October, Washington put its forces on worldwide alert the following day. The lesson drawn in Washington was that another Arab-Israeli war could now wreck the Western economy and risk a superpower clash, so the conflict had to be managed rather than left to the parties. A top-level answer uses the oil crisis not as an economic story but as the reason the USA became the active broker of the next twenty years: it gave the Arab states leverage they had never had, and gave the Americans a reason to use their own leverage on Israel."},{"code":"P5-3 — 1 Diplomatic negotiations: Kissinger and 'shuttle diplomacy'","h":"Shuttle diplomacy: small agreements that changed who did the negotiating","t":"Henry Kissinger, the American Secretary of State, flew repeatedly between Middle Eastern capitals in 1974 and 1975 rather than trying to settle everything at one conference. The method produced separation-of-forces agreements rather than peace: Egypt and Israel signed the first on 18 January 1974, pulling their armies apart along the Suez Canal, and Israel and Syria signed theirs on 31 May 1974, creating a buffer zone on the Golan Heights patrolled by a new United Nations force. Egypt then reopened the Suez Canal on 5 June 1975, closed since the Six Day War, and on 4 September 1975 signed the second Sinai agreement, under which Israel withdrew from the Mitla and Gidi passes, which became a United Nations buffer zone, and handed back the Abu Rudeis oilfields, both sides promising that differences would not be settled by force. American civilians manned early-warning stations in the passes. A top-level answer says what this changed rather than listing the agreements: the USSR was pushed to the edge of the process, the USA became the indispensable go-between and paymaster for both sides, and Egypt and Israel learned that Israeli land could be traded for Egyptian promises, which is the bargain Camp David later completed."},{"code":"P5-3 — 1 Diplomatic negotiations: Sadat's visit to Israel (1977) and Camp David","h":"Sadat, Begin and Carter: how the first Arab-Israeli peace treaty was made and what it cost Egypt","t":"President Anwar Sadat of Egypt broke the deadlock himself. On 19 November 1977 he flew to Israel and, the next day, addressed the Knesset in Jerusalem, the first Arab head of state to visit the Jewish state; Prime Minister Menachem Begin returned the visit at Ismailia in December. Direct talks then stalled, so President Jimmy Carter took both men to the presidential retreat at Camp David, where after thirteen days they signed the Camp David Accords on 17 September 1978: two framework agreements, one for an Egyptian-Israeli treaty and one, much vaguer, for Palestinian autonomy in the West Bank and Gaza. The treaty itself was signed in Washington on 26 March 1979. Israel returned the whole Sinai, completing the withdrawal in April 1982; Egypt recognised Israel and reopened the Suez Canal to Israeli shipping; both received large American aid. The price was Egypt's isolation: the Arab states suspended her from the Arab League and moved its headquarters from Cairo to Tunis, and on 6 October 1981 Sadat was shot dead by Islamist soldiers at a parade marking the anniversary of the 1973 war. A top-level answer holds both halves together: the strongest Arab army left the conflict permanently, which made a general Arab war against Israel impossible, but nothing was settled for the Palestinians, so the conflict changed shape instead of ending."},{"code":"P5-3 — 2 The Palestinian issue: Arafat's speech to the UN (1974)","h":"Arafat at the United Nations, 1974: the PLO wins recognition without winning land","t":"Yasser Arafat had led the PLO since 1969 and had rebuilt it in Lebanon after Jordan drove its fighters out in 1970-71. At the Arab summit at Rabat in October 1974 the Arab states declared the PLO the sole legitimate representative of the Palestinian people, which took the West Bank out of King Hussein's hands. On 13 November 1974 Arafat addressed the United Nations General Assembly in New York, telling delegates that he came bearing an olive branch and a freedom fighter's gun and asking them not to let the olive branch fall. Within days the Assembly affirmed Palestinian rights to self-determination and gave the PLO observer status at the UN. Israel and the USA refused to deal with an organisation whose charter rejected Israel's existence and which they held responsible for hijackings and for the killing of Israeli athletes at Munich in 1972. A top-level answer notices the gap this opened: after 1974 the Palestinians had a recognised voice in world politics but no territory and no negotiating partner, so the PLO had reason to act from Lebanon and Israel had reason to strike Lebanon, which is the road to 1982."},{"code":"P5-3 — 2 The Palestinian issue: Israeli reprisals and the invasion of Lebanon (1982)","h":"Lebanon, 1975-82: reprisals, invasion and the price of a military solution","t":"Lebanon's civil war broke out in 1975 and left the PLO in effective control of parts of the south and of West Beirut, from which it shelled northern Israel. Israel answered with reprisal raids and in March 1978 with Operation Litani, occupying land up to the Litani river until a UN force took over. On 3 June 1982 gunmen shot and gravely wounded Shlomo Argov, the Israeli ambassador in London; three days later, on 6 June, Israel invaded Lebanon in strength under the defence minister Ariel Sharon, drove north to Beirut and besieged the city. The siege ended in an American-brokered evacuation: between 21 August and 1 September 1982 about 14,000 to 15,000 PLO and Syrian combatants left Beirut, and Arafat moved the PLO's headquarters to Tunis, more than 2,000 kilometres from Palestine. Then, between 16 and 18 September 1982, Lebanese Phalangist militiamen entered the Sabra and Shatila refugee camps, which Israeli forces surrounded, and killed hundreds of Palestinian and Lebanese civilians. Israel's own Kahan Commission reported in February 1983 that Israel bore indirect responsibility for the killings and that Sharon bore personal responsibility for ignoring the danger of bloodshed when he approved the Phalangists' entry into the camps; he left the defence ministry, staying in the cabinet as a minister without portfolio. A top-level answer weighs the military success against the political cost: the PLO was removed from Israel's northern border, but Israel was left occupying southern Lebanon, world opinion and Israeli opinion had turned, and the leadership Israel had tried to destroy gained sympathy it had not had before."},{"code":"P5-3 — 2 The Palestinian issue: The Israeli occupied territories and the First Palestinian Intifada","h":"The occupied territories and the First Intifada, 1987-93: resistance moves inside","t":"Israel had held the West Bank, Gaza, East Jerusalem and the Golan Heights since 1967. Jewish settlement in the occupied territories grew steadily, especially after Begin's government took office in 1977, while Palestinians living under military government faced permits, curfews, land confiscation and no vote. On 8 December 1987 an Israeli army lorry collided with a vehicle at the Erez crossing in Gaza, killing four Palestinians from Jabalia refugee camp; the funerals turned into riots and from 9 December the uprising, the intifada, spread through Gaza, the West Bank and East Jerusalem. It was mostly stone-throwing, strikes, boycotts of Israeli goods and refusal to pay taxes, organised locally rather than from Tunis, and it was met with mass arrests and a policy of force and beatings under the defence minister Yitzhak Rabin. More than a thousand Palestinians were killed before the uprising subsided. Hamas, the Islamic Resistance Movement, was founded in Gaza by Sheikh Ahmed Yassin as the uprising began and published its charter in 1988, rejecting any peace with Israel. A top-level answer explains the reversal it produced: television pictures of soldiers against stone-throwing youths cost Israel support abroad, convinced many Israelis that the territories could not be held cheaply for ever, and forced the PLO in exile to act politically or be overtaken by leaders inside."},{"code":"P5-3 — 3 Attempts at a solution: The significance of Arafat's renunciation of terrorism (1988)","h":"Algiers and Geneva, 1988: the renunciation that made the PLO a negotiating partner","t":"The intifada, and King Hussein's decision in July 1988 to give up Jordan's claim to the West Bank, left the PLO with the initiative and the risk of being overtaken. In November 1988 the Palestine National Council, meeting in Algiers, proclaimed an independent State of Palestine and accepted UN resolutions that implied Israel's existence. When the USA refused Arafat a visa, the General Assembly moved its meeting on Palestine to Geneva, where he spoke on 13 December 1988; the next day he removed the remaining ambiguity, saying plainly that the PLO accepted Israel's right to exist in peace and security and renounced terrorism in all its forms. Within hours Secretary of State George Shultz announced that the USA would open a direct dialogue with the PLO, ending more than a decade of refusal. A top-level answer measures importance by what it unlocked rather than by the words themselves: the American veto on talking to the PLO was gone, so the question was no longer whether Palestinians could be negotiated with but on what terms, and every later step, from Madrid to the mutual recognition letters of 1993, rests on that change."},{"code":"P5-3 — 3 Attempts at a solution: Changing superpower policies in the Middle East","h":"The superpowers change: the Gulf War, the end of the Cold War and Madrid","t":"By the late 1980s the USSR under Mikhail Gorbachev was withdrawing from costly commitments abroad, restoring relations with Israel and allowing Soviet Jews to emigrate there in large numbers. Its Arab clients lost their alternative patron. When Iraq invaded Kuwait on 2 August 1990 the USA assembled a coalition that included Egypt, Syria and Saudi Arabia, and the USSR did not oppose it; the air campaign began on 17 January 1991 and Kuwait was liberated by 28 February. Iraq fired Scud missiles at Israel to split the coalition, and Israel, under American pressure, did not retaliate. Arafat backed Saddam Hussein, which cost the PLO its Gulf funding and drove hundreds of thousands of Palestinians out of Kuwait, leaving the organisation poorer and weaker than at any time since 1982. With only one superpower left, President George Bush used the coalition's authority to convene a peace conference at Madrid on 30 October 1991, co-sponsored with a Soviet Union that dissolved before the year ended; Israel sat down with Syria, Lebanon and a joint Jordanian-Palestinian delegation, though the PLO was still excluded by name. A top-level answer links the two changes: the collapse of Soviet cover removed the Arab states' military option, and PLO weakness after 1991 removed its bargaining power, so both sides had reasons to talk that had not existed in the 1980s."},{"code":"P5-3 — 3 Attempts at a solution: Arafat, Rabin and the Oslo Accords (1993)","h":"Oslo, 1993-95: mutual recognition, self-rule and the limits of the process","t":"Madrid produced public talks in Washington that went nowhere, so in 1993 Israeli and PLO representatives met secretly in Norway. Letters were exchanged in which Arafat recognised Israel's right to exist and renounced terrorism and Prime Minister Yitzhak Rabin recognised the PLO as the representative of the Palestinian people, and on 13 September 1993 the Declaration of Principles was signed at the White House, with Rabin and Arafat shaking hands in front of President Bill Clinton. It set out five years of Palestinian self-rule beginning in Gaza and Jericho, and left Jerusalem, refugees, settlements and borders to later final-status talks. The Gaza-Jericho agreement followed in Cairo on 4 May 1994 and created the Palestinian National Authority, and Arafat returned to Gaza that July. Jordan made peace with Israel on 26 October 1994. Oslo II, signed on 28 September 1995, extended self-rule to other West Bank towns and split the territory into Areas A, B and C. Opponents attacked the process from both sides: a settler murdered Palestinian worshippers at Hebron in February 1994, Hamas answered with suicide bombings, and on 4 November 1995 an Israeli opposed to Oslo shot Rabin dead at a peace rally in Tel Aviv. A top-level answer judges Oslo by what it settled and what it postponed: it delivered recognition and a Palestinian government on Palestinian soil for the first time, but the hardest questions were deferred, and the assassination of the Israeli leader who had signed it left the process without the man who could carry it."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1(a): one explained consequence of Egypt's suspension from the Arab League","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(a): 'Explain one consequence of the suspension of Egypt from the Arab League in 1979.' [4 marks]. Question 1 is printed in two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each asking for one consequence of a separate event and each marked on its own out of 4, so this part needs one consequence analysed and supported. The information you are working from: Egypt signed a peace treaty with Israel in Washington on 26 March 1979; the other Arab states condemned it, suspended Egypt from the Arab League and moved the League's headquarters from Cairo to Tunis; Egypt was the largest Arab state and had led the Arab side in the wars of 1948, 1956, 1967 and 1973; Egypt kept the American aid promised at Camp David; Egypt was readmitted to the League ten years later, in 1989, and the headquarters returned to Cairo in 1990; President Sadat was assassinated by Islamist soldiers on 6 October 1981 and was succeeded by Hosni Mubarak.","steps":["Separate the event named in the question from the treaty that caused it: the question asks what followed the suspension itself, so the answer must be about Egypt's position in the Arab world, not about the terms agreed with Israel.","Choose a consequence that is a change of state rather than the next event in the story: before 1979 the Arab states acted through a League led from Cairo by their strongest member; afterwards they acted through a League that had suspended him.","Support it with the precise detail: the headquarters moved from Cairo to Tunis, Egypt stayed outside until 1989, and in the meantime the largest Arab army was committed by treaty not to fight Israel.","Make the analytical link the grid rewards: with the strongest Arab state outside the League and at peace with Israel, no combination of the remaining states could mount another general war, so Arab pressure on Israel had to become diplomatic or be left to the Palestinians themselves."],"cues":["Which event does the question actually name, and which one is only its cause?","What could be said about the Arab League afterwards that could not be said before?","Which two facts prove that the suspension was real and lasting?","Why does removing one member change what the rest of the League could do?"]},{"title":"Question 1(b): one explained consequence of the founding of Hamas","setup":"Booklet P, Question 1(b): 'Explain one consequence of the founding of Hamas in Gaza in 1987-88.' [4 marks]. This part is marked on its own out of 4 and must be on a different event from 1(a). The information you are working from: the First Intifada began in Gaza in December 1987 after four Palestinians were killed in a collision at the Erez crossing; Hamas, the Islamic Resistance Movement, was founded in Gaza by Sheikh Ahmed Yassin as the uprising began and published a charter in 1988 calling for an Islamic state in the whole of Palestine and rejecting any peace with Israel; the PLO, led by Yasser Arafat, had been based in Tunis since 1982 and was secular in outlook; in December 1988 Arafat accepted Israel's right to exist and renounced terrorism; the Declaration of Principles was signed by Israel and the PLO in September 1993; Hamas rejected it and carried out suicide bombings in Israeli cities from 1994.","steps":["Fix what is new about this organisation rather than describing it: for the first time the Palestinian cause inside the occupied territories had a leadership that was religious, locally based and opposed in principle to any settlement.","Pick the consequence that follows from that novelty: the PLO in Tunis now had a rival for Palestinian loyalty, and a rival that gained from every failure of negotiation.","Support it with dated detail: Arafat made his renunciation of terrorism a year after Hamas appeared, and after the Declaration of Principles was signed Hamas answered with suicide bombings in Israeli cities.","Explain the link the grid calls analysis: because any concession Arafat made could be denounced as betrayal by a movement with its own fighters and welfare network, the PLO had to show quick gains from talking, and Israeli leaders who dealt with Arafat could be attacked at home whenever Hamas struck."],"cues":["What did this organisation offer Palestinians that the PLO did not?","Who had most to lose from a rival leadership appearing inside the territories?","Which later events show the rivalry actually working?","Why does a rejectionist rival make a negotiator's position harder rather than easier?"]},{"title":"Question 2: building the narrative account of the Oslo years","setup":"Booklet P, Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the key events of the Oslo peace process in the years 1993-95.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the Declaration of Principles (1993); the Palestinian National Authority. You must also use information of your own. The account must analyse the linkage between events, and there is no access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content. The material available: secret Israeli-PLO talks in Norway in 1993; letters of mutual recognition exchanged in September 1993 in which Arafat accepted Israel's right to exist and renounced terrorism and Rabin recognised the PLO; the Declaration of Principles signed at the White House on 13 September 1993 with Clinton presiding, setting up five years of self-rule and postponing Jerusalem, refugees, settlements and borders; a settler's massacre of Palestinian worshippers at Hebron in February 1994; the Gaza-Jericho agreement signed in Cairo on 4 May 1994, creating the Palestinian National Authority, with Arafat returning to Gaza that July; the Israel-Jordan peace treaty of 26 October 1994; Hamas suicide bombings in Israeli cities from 1994; Oslo II of 28 September 1995 dividing the West Bank into Areas A, B and C; the murder of Rabin by an Israeli opponent of Oslo on 4 November 1995.","steps":["Choose the outcome the sequence leads to, because a narrative account must arrive somewhere: end with the process left without the Israeli leader who had signed it, so every episode can be selected for the way it strengthened or weakened him.","Set the opening episode as the exchange that made everything else legal: the recognition letters of September 1993 and then the Declaration of Principles at the White House, which bought agreement by postponing Jerusalem, refugees, settlements and borders.","Add the second episode and the link: because the Declaration promised self-rule but delivered nothing on the ground, the Cairo agreement of May 1994 had to follow quickly, creating the Palestinian National Authority and bringing Arafat back to Gaza in July.","Add the third episode and the link that explains the reversal: because the deal had rewarded neither side's opponents, Hebron in February 1994 and the Hamas bombings that answered it made every further transfer of territory a domestic risk, which is why Oslo II in September 1995 was so detailed and so contested.","Close with what the sequence amounted to rather than repeating it: in two years mutual recognition and a Palestinian government on Palestinian soil had been achieved, the hardest questions had been deferred, and the violence of the opponents on both sides had made deferral fatal."],"cues":["Where should this account stop, and why does choosing the ending first make selection easier?","Which single episode makes all the later agreements possible?","Why could the process not simply wait after the signing at the White House?","Who gained from every act of violence, and how does that change what the leaders could offer?","What can be said about the whole run of years that could not be said about any one of its episodes?"]},{"title":"Question 3: explaining the importance of the Rabat summit for the PLO","setup":"Booklet P, Question 3 prints three bullets and you explain two of them, each marked separately out of 8. This worked example takes the bullet 'The importance of the Arab summit at Rabat (1974) for the PLO.' (8 marks). No stimulus points are printed. The material available: Jordan had driven PLO fighters out in 1970-71 and King Hussein claimed to speak for the West Bank, which Jordan had ruled until 1967; at Rabat in October 1974 the Arab states declared the PLO the sole legitimate representative of the Palestinian people; Arafat addressed the United Nations General Assembly on 13 November 1974, saying he came bearing an olive branch and a freedom fighter's gun; within days the Assembly affirmed Palestinian rights and gave the PLO observer status; Israel and the USA refused to deal with the PLO while its charter rejected Israel's existence; the PLO built its base in Lebanon, where civil war broke out in 1975; Jordan finally gave up its claim to the West Bank in July 1988.","steps":["Refuse the description: importance means the difference the summit made afterwards, so no sentence may simply say what was agreed at Rabat.","Take the first line forward, the diplomatic one: recognition by the Arab states was what made the United Nations invitation possible, so within a month Arafat spoke to the General Assembly and the PLO gained observer status there.","Take the second line forward, the rivalry it settled: with Jordan displaced as the Palestinians' spokesman, no Arab state could negotiate the West Bank away over the PLO's head, and Hussein eventually gave up the claim altogether.","Weigh what it did not deliver, which is where the top level is won: recognition brought no territory and no negotiating partner, because Israel and the USA still refused to deal with the PLO, so the organisation's only base of action remained Lebanon.","Judge it: Rabat mattered because it turned a guerrilla movement into the recognised address for the Palestinian question, which is why the argument after it was about the terms on which the PLO would be talked to rather than about who spoke for Palestinians."],"cues":["What kind of sentence is banned in an importance answer?","Which event a month later could not have happened without the summit?","Whose claim did the summit destroy, and with what long-term effect?","What did recognition fail to give the PLO, and why does saying so raise the mark?","In one sentence, what question had been settled and what question remained?"]},{"title":"Question 3: explaining the importance of Jewish settlement in the occupied territories","setup":"Booklet P, Question 3, the bullet 'The importance of Jewish settlement in the occupied territories for relations between Israel and the Palestinians.' (8 marks), marked out of 8 with no stimulus points. The material available: Israel had held the West Bank, Gaza, East Jerusalem and the Golan Heights since the Six Day War of 1967, and Palestinians there lived under military government, with permits, curfews, land confiscation and no vote; Jewish settlement in the territories grew steadily and faster after Begin's government took office in 1977; the Camp David framework for Palestinian autonomy was left vague, was never implemented and did nothing to stop settlement; the uprising that began in December 1987 started among Palestinians living under that occupation; the Declaration of Principles of September 1993 left settlements, with Jerusalem, refugees and borders, to final-status talks and began self-rule in Gaza and Jericho; an Israeli settler murdered Palestinian worshippers at Hebron in February 1994; Oslo II of September 1995 divided the West Bank into Areas A, B and C and left most of it under Israeli control; building went on while the talks went on.","steps":["Set the test before writing anything down: importance here means what settlement did to the chances of dividing the land between the two peoples, so every paragraph has to end on the negotiation rather than on the settlers themselves.","Take the first difference it made, on the ground: land taken for settlements, and the roads and permits that served them, turned an occupation Israel called temporary into something that looked permanent, which is one of the grievances behind the uprising that began in December 1987.","Take the second, about the people: settlers were Israeli civilians living among Palestinians under army protection, so any future Israeli withdrawal now meant moving Israelis and not only soldiers, and the two communities met as rivals for the same ground, as the murder of worshippers at Hebron in February 1994 showed.","Take the third, about the negotiations themselves: because the Declaration of Principles of September 1993 deferred settlements to final-status talks instead of freezing them, agreements could be signed, but the thing being argued over kept growing while the argument went on, which is part of why Oslo II left most of the West Bank under Israeli control.","Judge it: settlement mattered because it made the occupation look permanent and the territory harder to divide, so each side read the other's intentions through it, and a peace process that postponed the question guaranteed that the postponement would itself become the grievance."],"cues":["Importance for what, exactly, and how will you keep every paragraph pointed at it?","What did building on the land do to the claim that the occupation was temporary?","Who now lived among the Palestinians, and what did that add to any future withdrawal?","What did the agreement of 1993 do with the question of settlements, and with what effect on the talks that followed?","In one sentence, why did postponing this question make a settlement harder rather than easier?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Give the dates of the three agreements Kissinger's shuttle diplomacy produced, and say what Egypt did in June 1975.","a":"The Egyptian-Israeli separation of forces on 18 January 1974, the Israeli-Syrian disengagement on the Golan Heights on 31 May 1974, and the second Sinai agreement on 4 September 1975. Egypt reopened the Suez Canal on 5 June 1975, closed since 1967."},{"q":"What did Sadat do in November 1977, what was signed on 17 September 1978, and what was signed on 26 March 1979?","a":"Sadat flew to Israel on 19 November 1977 and addressed the Knesset the next day. The Camp David Accords, two framework agreements, were signed on 17 September 1978. The Egypt-Israel peace treaty was signed in Washington on 26 March 1979."},{"q":"What triggered Israel's invasion of Lebanon in June 1982, and where did the PLO leadership go afterwards?","a":"Gunmen shot and gravely wounded Shlomo Argov, Israel's ambassador in London, on 3 June 1982; Israel invaded on 6 June. After the siege of Beirut about 14,000 to 15,000 PLO and Syrian combatants were evacuated between 21 August and 1 September 1982, and Arafat moved the headquarters to Tunis."},{"q":"What did Arafat say at Geneva in December 1988, and what did the USA do about it?","a":"On 13 December 1988 he addressed the General Assembly, and the next day stated plainly that the PLO accepted Israel's right to exist in peace and security and renounced terrorism in all its forms. George Shultz then announced that the USA would open a direct dialogue with the PLO."}]},"room":{"facts":["Booklet P is worth 32 marks and has three questions: Question 1, printed in two parts, 1(a) and 1(b), each 'Explain one consequence of...' for 4 marks and each on a separate event or development; Question 2, 'Write a narrative account analysing...', 8 marks; Question 3, 'Explain two of the following: the importance of ... for ...', two chosen parts at 8 marks each, 16 in all.","Only Question 2 prints stimulus points, as two bullets followed by 'You must also use information of your own.' The cap is printed in the mark scheme: 'No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.' There is no cap at Level 2. Questions 1 and 3 print no bullets and carry no cap.","Each part of Question 1 is marked on its own two-level grid out of 4, AO2 2 and AO1 2: Level 2 needs features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific supporting information. Two descriptions of the event, however accurate, stay in Level 1.","Question 2 is marked out of 8, AO2 4 and AO1 4, on a three-level grid: Level 3 needs a clear sequence of events leading to an outcome with the linkage between them analysed, coherent and logically structured.","Question 3 is marked twice on a three-level grid out of 8, AO2 8 and AO1 8 across the two parts, and Level 3 needs analysis of importance in a coherent line of reasoning: what difference the event, person or development made to later situations and unfolding developments, not what happened.","Timing: Paper 2 lasts 1 hour 50 minutes for 64 marks, about 55 minutes a booklet, so booklet P's 32 marks get roughly a minute a mark with planning on top - about 12 minutes on Question 1's two parts, 13 on Question 2 and 14 on each part of Question 3. There are no sources on Paper 2 and no marks for spelling, punctuation and grammar."],"questions":[{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the oil crisis of 1973-74. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2, marked on the 4-mark consequence grid; Level 2 (3-4) requires features of the period analysed to explain a consequence, with specific support. Credit the fourfold rise in the oil price and Western recession, the new leverage of the Arab oil producers over Western governments, or the American decision to broker a settlement actively because another war now threatened the Western economy.","sol":"One consequence was that the USA became the active broker of an Arab-Israeli settlement. The production cuts agreed on 17 October 1973 and the embargo declared on the USA on 19 and 20 October pushed crude from about three dollars a barrel to about twelve by March 1974, and Western economies went into recession. Because a further war could now damage the American economy directly, and because only the USA had leverage over Israel, Washington stopped waiting for the parties to agree: Kissinger began shuttling between capitals within weeks and produced the first disengagement agreement on 18 January 1974.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["A consequence is something that was true afterwards because of the crisis, so ask who gained power they had not had before.","The strongest consequences here are not about petrol queues but about what Western governments now had to do about the conflict itself.","Write it in three moves: name the consequence, give the figures or dates that prove it, then explain why it changed what governments did."],"codes":["P5-3 — 1 Diplomatic negotiations: The significance of the oil crisis"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of Sadat's visit to Jerusalem in November 1977. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the consequence grid. Credit the opening of direct Egyptian-Israeli negotiation and Begin's return visit to Ismailia, the American decision to host talks that led to Camp David, or the hostility of other Arab states towards Egypt for negotiating alone.","sol":"One consequence was that Egypt and Israel began negotiating directly instead of through intermediaries. By flying to Israel on 19 November 1977 and addressing the Knesset the next day, Sadat recognised the state in practice before any terms had been agreed, which no Arab leader had done. Begin returned the visit at Ismailia in December, and when the direct talks stalled President Carter had a process worth rescuing, which is why he was able to bring both men to Camp David in September 1978. The visit turned a frozen conflict into a negotiation.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what the visit made possible that had been impossible while no Arab leader would meet Israeli leaders in public.","A consequence can be diplomatic rather than territorial: think about who was now talking to whom, and who could now get involved.","Name the consequence, support it with the December return visit and the following year, then explain the causal link."],"codes":["P5-3 — 1 Diplomatic negotiations: Sadat's visit to Israel (1977) and Camp David"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of Arafat's speech to the United Nations in November 1974. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the consequence grid. Credit the grant of UN observer status and the General Assembly's affirmation of Palestinian rights, the recognition of the PLO as the Palestinians' representative in world politics, or the hardening of Israeli and American refusal to negotiate with it.","sol":"One consequence was that the PLO gained a permanent standing in world politics. Arafat addressed the General Assembly on 13 November 1974, and within days the Assembly affirmed the Palestinians' right to self-determination and granted the PLO observer status at the United Nations. Because a body previously treated as a guerrilla movement now had a seat and a hearing, the Palestinian question could no longer be handled as a refugee problem to be settled between Arab states and Israel. That is why Jordan's claim to speak for the West Bank faded and the PLO became the address for any future settlement.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what the organisation possessed after the speech that it had not possessed before it.","The consequence is about status rather than territory: nothing changed on the ground in 1974.","State the consequence, prove it with what the General Assembly did within days, then explain why status mattered for later negotiations."],"codes":["P5-3 — 2 The Palestinian issue: Arafat's speech to the UN (1974)"]},{"q":"1(b) Explain one consequence of the Egyptian-Israeli separation of forces agreement of January 1974. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the consequence grid. Credit the disengagement of the two armies along the Suez Canal and the end of fighting there, the establishment of the USA as the indispensable broker and the sidelining of the USSR, or the path it opened to the reopening of the canal and the second Sinai agreement.","sol":"One consequence was that the USA replaced the USSR as the power that mattered in Egyptian diplomacy. The agreement of 18 January 1974 was reached because Kissinger flew between Cairo and Jerusalem carrying terms each side would not put to the other, so it was American mediation, not a joint conference with Moscow, that pulled the armies apart along the Suez Canal. Sadat drew the obvious lesson that only Washington could deliver Israeli withdrawal, and the same method produced the Syrian agreement in May 1974 and the second Sinai agreement in 1975.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask who had to be involved for this agreement to happen at all, and what that taught the Egyptian president.","The consequence can be about the diplomacy rather than about the line on the map.","Name the consequence, support it with how the agreement was actually reached, then explain what followed from that lesson."],"codes":["P5-3 — 1 Diplomatic negotiations: Kissinger and 'shuttle diplomacy'"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the outbreak of the First Intifada in December 1987. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 2 + AO1 2 on the consequence grid. Credit the damage to Israel's international standing from televised repression, the shift of the initiative to Palestinians inside the occupied territories and the founding of Hamas, King Hussein's abandonment of Jordan's claim to the West Bank in 1988, or the pressure on the PLO in Tunis that led to the renunciation of terrorism.","sol":"One consequence was that the PLO leadership in Tunis was forced into politics to avoid being overtaken. The uprising that began on 9 December 1987 was organised by Palestinians inside Gaza and the West Bank, and a new rival, Hamas, appeared in Gaza at the same time. Because the initiative had passed to people Arafat did not control, and because King Hussein gave up Jordan's claim to the West Bank in July 1988, the PLO had to show it could deliver something. That is why it declared a Palestinian state in November 1988 and renounced terrorism a month later.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The uprising began among people the exiled leadership had not organised, so ask what that did to the exiled leadership.","A consequence can be a change of policy rather than a change of territory.","State the consequence, support it with what the PLO did in 1988, then explain why losing the initiative forced that change."],"codes":["P5-3 — 2 The Palestinian issue: The Israeli occupied territories and the First Palestinian Intifada"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the assassination of President Sadat in October 1981. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the Israeli invasion of Lebanon in June 1982. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts, each marked out of 4 on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the succession of Mubarak and the survival of the peace treaty, the completion of the Israeli withdrawal from Sinai in April 1982, or the evidence that peace with Israel was dangerous for Arab leaders. In 1(b) credit the evacuation of the PLO from Beirut to Tunis, the Sabra and Shatila massacre and the damage to Israel's reputation, or the long Israeli occupation of southern Lebanon.","sol":"1(a) The assassination on 6 October 1981 showed that Egypt's peace with Israel would outlive the man who made it. Sadat was killed by Islamist soldiers at a parade marking the anniversary of the 1973 war, yet his successor Hosni Mubarak kept the treaty, and Israel completed its withdrawal from Sinai in April 1982 as agreed. Because the treaty survived a violent change of ruler, Israel could treat its southern border as secure once that withdrawal was completed in April 1982, which is precisely what allowed it to concentrate its army against Lebanon in June 1982. 1(b) The invasion drove the PLO out of the country next to Israel and into distant exile. After the siege of Beirut about 14,000 to 15,000 PLO and Syrian combatants were evacuated between 21 August and 1 September 1982, and Arafat moved the headquarters to Tunis, more than 2,000 kilometres from Palestine. Because the leadership could no longer direct operations from across the border, the initiative in the Palestinian struggle passed to people living under occupation, which is one reason the uprising of 1987 began without orders from Tunis.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate parts on two separate events, so neither answer may borrow material from the other.","For 1(a) the interesting question is what did not change: ask what the killing failed to undo, and what that made possible.","For 1(b) ask where the PLO leadership ended up and what it could no longer do from there."],"codes":["P5-3 — 1 Diplomatic negotiations: Sadat's visit to Israel (1977) and Camp David","P5-3 — 2 The Palestinian issue: Israeli reprisals and the invasion of Lebanon (1982)"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of Arafat's renunciation of terrorism in December 1988. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the PLO's support for Iraq in the Gulf War of 1991. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the opening of a direct American dialogue with the PLO announced by George Shultz, the removal of the American veto on treating the PLO as a negotiating partner, or the anger of Palestinian rejectionists. In 1(b) credit the loss of Gulf funding and the expulsion of Palestinians from Kuwait, the diplomatic isolation of the PLO and its exclusion from Madrid, or the weakness that made Arafat accept the terms of Oslo.","sol":"1(a) The immediate consequence was that the USA began talking to the PLO. On 14 December 1988, the day after Arafat addressed the General Assembly in Geneva, he stated plainly that the PLO accepted Israel's right to exist and renounced terrorism in all its forms, and Secretary of State George Shultz announced that the USA would open a direct dialogue with it. Because the American refusal had been the PLO's chief obstacle, the argument shifted from whether Palestinians could be negotiated with to on what terms. 1(b) Backing Saddam Hussein cost the PLO the money and the friends it depended on. The Gulf states cut their subsidies and hundreds of thousands of Palestinians were forced out of Kuwait, so an organisation that paid schools, hospitals and salaries was suddenly near bankruptcy, and it was excluded by name from the Madrid conference in October 1991. That weakness explains why Arafat accepted a secret channel and limited self-rule in 1993 rather than holding out for more.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate parts: a statement in 1(a) and a political choice in 1(b), each needing its own consequence.","For 1(a) ask which government changed its policy within a day, and what that removed.","For 1(b) follow the money and the friendships rather than the fighting."],"codes":["P5-3 — 3 Attempts at a solution: The significance of Arafat's renunciation of terrorism (1988)","P5-3 — 3 Attempts at a solution: Changing superpower policies in the Middle East"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the Declaration of Principles signed in September 1993. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the assassination of Yitzhak Rabin in November 1995. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the mutual recognition of Israel and the PLO, the Gaza-Jericho agreement and the creation of the Palestinian National Authority, or the Israel-Jordan treaty and Oslo II that followed. In 1(b) credit the loss of the Israeli leader who could deliver the process, the succession of Shimon Peres, or the encouragement given to opponents of Oslo on both sides.","sol":"1(a) One consequence was the creation of a Palestinian government on Palestinian soil. The Declaration signed at the White House on 13 September 1993 set out five years of self-rule, and it was carried out in the Gaza-Jericho agreement signed in Cairo on 4 May 1994, which established the Palestinian National Authority and brought Arafat back to Gaza that July. Because Palestinians now administered territory rather than only claiming it, the argument between the two sides became one about borders and powers rather than about existence. 1(b) The killing on 4 November 1995 removed the one Israeli leader who could carry the process. Rabin was shot at a peace rally in Tel Aviv by an Israeli opposed to Oslo, and although Shimon Peres took over, he lacked Rabin's authority as a former chief of staff, which had been what persuaded doubtful Israelis that withdrawal was safe. Because the concessions still to come were the hardest ones, losing that authority left the agreements signed but the next stages without a guarantor.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate parts: an agreement in 1(a) and a murder in 1(b), so keep the material apart.","For 1(a) ask what existed on the ground in 1994 that had not existed in 1992.","For 1(b) ask what the dead man had been able to do that his successor could not."],"codes":["P5-3 — 3 Attempts at a solution: Arafat, Rabin and the Oslo Accords (1993)"]},{"q":"1(a) Explain one consequence of the reopening of the Suez Canal in June 1975. [4 marks] 1(b) Explain one consequence of the Sabra and Shatila massacre of September 1982. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4 in two separate parts on the consequence grid. In 1(a) credit the recovery of Egyptian revenue and the rebuilding of the canal cities, the evidence that Sadat intended a settlement rather than another war, or the encouragement it gave to the second Sinai agreement. In 1(b) credit the damage to Israel's international standing, the Kahan Commission and Sharon's removal from the defence ministry, or the sympathy it won for the Palestinian cause.","sol":"1(a) Reopening the canal on 5 June 1975, closed since the Six Day War, tied Egypt's recovery to keeping the peace. The waterway could only earn tolls and the ruined cities along it could only be rebuilt if no fighting returned to its banks, so Sadat had given himself a standing reason not to reopen the war. Israel read the move the same way, and the second Sinai agreement followed on 4 September 1975. 1(b) The massacre turned opinion inside Israel against the war. Between 16 and 18 September 1982 Phalangist militiamen killed hundreds of Palestinian and Lebanese civilians in camps that Israeli forces had surrounded, and the Israeli government's own Kahan Commission found in February 1983 that Israel bore indirect responsibility for the killings and that the defence minister, Ariel Sharon, bore personal responsibility for ignoring the danger of bloodshed when he approved the Phalangists' entry, which forced him out of the post. Because a war begun to make the northern border safe was now being judged at home as a moral disaster, the political basis for staying in Lebanon began to crumble.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate parts, one about an economic decision and one about a massacre, so treat them as unconnected answers.","For 1(a) ask what a working canal did to Sadat's own interest in avoiding another war.","For 1(b) ask where the strongest criticism came from, and why that mattered more than criticism abroad."],"codes":["P5-3 — 1 Diplomatic negotiations: Kissinger and 'shuttle diplomacy'","P5-3 — 2 The Palestinian issue: Israeli reprisals and the invasion of Lebanon (1982)"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the key events of the years 1977-79 that led to peace between Egypt and Israel. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: Sadat's visit to Jerusalem (1977); the Camp David Accords (1978). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) requires a clear sequence leading to an outcome with the linkage between events analysed. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; own material might include the deadlock after the 1975 Sinai agreement, Begin's election in 1977 and his return visit to Ismailia, Carter's mediation at Camp David, the two framework agreements, the Treaty of Washington and the terms of the Sinai withdrawal.","sol":"By 1977 the shuttle diplomacy of 1974 and 1975 had gone as far as it could: the separation of forces and the second Sinai agreement had stopped the shooting but neither side would concede the next step, and Begin's new government in Israel was less willing to trade land than its predecessors. Sadat broke the deadlock personally because Egypt could not afford another war and wanted Sinai back, and on 19 November 1977 he flew to Israel and addressed the Knesset the following day. That single act destroyed the taboo on direct contact and put the onus on Israel to answer, which is why Begin went to Ismailia in December. Direct talks then stalled over Israeli settlements and over what the Palestinians would get, and neither leader could concede at home, so a mediator with money and prestige was needed: President Carter took both men to Camp David and after thirteen days they signed two framework agreements on 17 September 1978, one for an Egyptian-Israeli treaty and one, deliberately vague, for Palestinian autonomy. Because the Palestinian framework committed nobody, the bilateral one could be completed, and the treaty was signed in Washington on 26 March 1979: full recognition and the whole of Sinai returned. The sequence therefore ended by settling the Egyptian-Israeli conflict completely and the Palestinian question not at all.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Begin before the visit: what had shuttle diplomacy achieved by 1977, and why could it go no further?","The account needs links, not just dates, so ask at each stage what the other side was forced to do next.","Plan four episodes with a connective sentence between each, and make sure two of them are not the printed bullets."],"codes":["P5-3 — 1 Diplomatic negotiations: Kissinger and 'shuttle diplomacy'","P5-3 — 1 Diplomatic negotiations: Sadat's visit to Israel (1977) and Camp David"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the key events in the search for a Palestinian-Israeli settlement in the years 1988-93. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: Arafat's renunciation of terrorism (1988); the Madrid conference (1991). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) requires a clear sequence leading to an outcome with the linkage analysed. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; own material might include the intifada and Hussein's abandonment of the West Bank claim, the Algiers declaration of a Palestinian state, the American dialogue with the PLO, the Gulf War and the collapse of the USSR, the deadlocked Washington talks, the Norwegian channel and the mutual recognition letters.","sol":"The sequence starts with the initiative slipping away from the PLO in Tunis. The intifada that began in December 1987 was run by Palestinians inside the occupied territories, and King Hussein's decision in July 1988 to give up Jordan's claim to the West Bank left the PLO responsible for a cause it was not directing. It therefore had to act politically: the Palestine National Council declared a state at Algiers in November 1988, and when the USA refused Arafat a visa the General Assembly met in Geneva, where he spoke on 13 December and next day renounced terrorism and accepted Israel's right to exist. George Shultz opened an American dialogue with the PLO at once, removing the veto on treating it as a partner. Events then made both sides weaker and readier. Arafat backed Iraq when it invaded Kuwait, and after Kuwait was liberated in February 1991 the Gulf states cut the PLO's funds; meanwhile the USSR was collapsing, so the Arab states lost their patron. President Bush used that moment to convene the Madrid conference on 30 October 1991, where Israel talked directly to its neighbours but the PLO was excluded by name. Because delegates who could not commit their leadership could settle nothing, the follow-up talks in Washington stalled, and the Israeli government elected in 1992 turned instead to a secret channel in Norway. The sequence ended where the exclusion had pointed, with letters of mutual recognition and the Declaration of Principles signed on 13 September 1993.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Start with what was happening inside the occupied territories, because that is what put the exiled leadership under pressure.","At each stage ask what the previous move made impossible, so the joins are explained rather than asserted.","Choose four episodes and mark which two are your own material, so the account addresses more than the printed bullets."],"codes":["P5-3 — 3 Attempts at a solution: The significance of Arafat's renunciation of terrorism (1988)","P5-3 — 3 Attempts at a solution: Changing superpower policies in the Middle East","P5-3 — 3 Attempts at a solution: Arafat, Rabin and the Oslo Accords (1993)"]},{"q":"Write a narrative account analysing the key events of the years 1975-82 that led to the expulsion of the PLO from Beirut. (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the Lebanese civil war; the shooting of the Israeli ambassador in London (1982). You must also use information of your own.","a":"AO2 4 + AO1 4. Level 3 (6-8) requires a clear sequence leading to an outcome with the linkage analysed. No access to Level 3 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; own material might include the PLO's move to Lebanon after 1970-71, cross-border raids and Israeli reprisals, Operation Litani in 1978 and the UN force, the invasion of 6 June 1982 and Sharon's role, the siege of Beirut and the American-brokered evacuation to Tunis.","sol":"The PLO was in Lebanon because Jordan had expelled it in 1970-71, and when civil war broke out in Lebanon in 1975 the collapse of central authority let it govern parts of the south and of West Beirut as a state within a state. That mattered because it gave the organisation a border with Israel from which to fire, and gave Israel a target it could reach. Raids and shelling brought reprisals, and in March 1978 Israel occupied the land up to the Litani river before handing it to a United Nations force, which showed that limited operations removed nothing permanently. The lesson drawn by the defence minister, Ariel Sharon, was that only destroying the PLO's base would do. The shooting of Shlomo Argov, Israel's ambassador in London, on 3 June 1982 supplied the occasion, and Israel invaded on 6 June, drove north far beyond the limited zone of 1978 and besieged Beirut. Because the PLO could not break the siege and Lebanon's government could not protect it, the only way out was political: American mediation produced an evacuation between 21 August and 1 September 1982 in which about 14,000 to 15,000 PLO and Syrian combatants left and Arafat took the leadership to Tunis. The sequence removed the PLO from Israel's border, but it did so by scattering the leadership two thousand kilometres away, which is why the next Palestinian revolt was made by people living under occupation rather than by the exiles.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Begin with why the PLO was in Lebanon at all, because that explains what the civil war handed it.","Ask at each stage why the previous Israeli measure was judged insufficient.","Plan four episodes and make sure at least two come from outside the printed bullets."],"codes":["P5-3 — 2 The Palestinian issue: Arafat's speech to the UN (1974)","P5-3 — 2 The Palestinian issue: Israeli reprisals and the invasion of Lebanon (1982)"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of Kissinger's shuttle diplomacy for relations between Egypt and Israel. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Level 3 (6-8) needs analysis of importance in a coherent line of reasoning. Credit the separation of forces in January 1974 and the second Sinai agreement of September 1975, the reopening of the Suez Canal, the principle of trading land for Egyptian undertakings, the displacement of the USSR by the USA as broker, and the path it opened to Sadat's visit and Camp David.","sol":"Shuttle diplomacy mattered less for the territory it moved than for the habit it created. Kissinger's method was to carry terms between capitals that neither government would put to the other in public, and it produced the separation of forces along the Suez Canal on 18 January 1974 and then, after a further round, the second Sinai agreement of 4 September 1975, in which Israel gave up the Mitla and Gidi passes and the Abu Rudeis oilfields and Egypt undertook that differences would not be settled by force. The first difference this made was practical: the armies were no longer in contact, so an accidental clash could not restart the war. The second was the principle it established, that Israeli-held ground could be exchanged for Egyptian commitments, which is exactly the bargain Camp David completed on a larger scale. The third was about who mattered. Every step was delivered by the USA, and Egypt's reward was American money and support, so Sadat had no further use for the Soviet connection and the USSR was left with nothing to broker. Without these agreements the flight to Jerusalem in 1977 would have been a leap into the dark; with them, Sadat already knew that Israel would trade land through American mediation, and that is the importance of shuttle diplomacy.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Importance means the difference it made afterwards, so resist any sentence that only describes what was agreed.","Trace more than one line forward: what it did to the armies, what it taught both governments about bargaining, and what it did to Soviet influence.","End with a judgement that says what would have been harder without it."],"codes":["P5-3 — 1 Diplomatic negotiations: The significance of the oil crisis","P5-3 — 1 Diplomatic negotiations: Kissinger and 'shuttle diplomacy'"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the First Intifada for attitudes towards the occupied territories. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Credit the international reaction to televised repression, the division of Israeli opinion about holding the territories, the rise of Hamas and of local leadership, Hussein's abandonment of the West Bank claim, and the pressure that produced the PLO's declaration and renunciation of terrorism.","sol":"The uprising that began on 9 December 1987 changed how the occupation was seen by everyone who had to live with it or pay for it. Abroad, the pictures were of conscript soldiers against stone-throwing youths in Gaza and the West Bank, and because Israel had held those territories since 1967 while insisting the arrangement was temporary, the length of the occupation became the story rather than Palestinian violence. Inside Israel the effect was a genuine division: mass arrests and the policy of force and beatings under the defence minister Yitzhak Rabin failed to end the unrest after years of trying, and more than a thousand Palestinians were killed, which persuaded a growing part of the public that the territories could not be held cheaply or indefinitely. The same Rabin later signed the Oslo agreements. Among Palestinians the uprising shifted authority towards people living under occupation, including the newly founded Hamas in Gaza, which forced the exiled PLO to act politically and helped produce the declaration at Algiers and the renunciation of terrorism at Geneva in 1988. King Hussein read the change too and gave up Jordan's claim to the West Bank in July 1988. Its importance is therefore that it converted the occupied territories from a security arrangement that most Israelis accepted into a political problem that had to be negotiated.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Importance is measured by the difference made, so organise the answer around the different audiences whose view changed.","Take three groups in turn and ask what each believed afterwards that it had not believed before.","Finish with a judgement that names what the uprising converted the occupation into."],"codes":["P5-3 — 2 The Palestinian issue: The Israeli occupied territories and the First Palestinian Intifada","P5-3 — 3 Attempts at a solution: The significance of Arafat's renunciation of terrorism (1988)"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the end of the Cold War for the Arab-Israeli peace process. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Credit Gorbachev's withdrawal from costly commitments and Soviet Jewish emigration to Israel, the loss of the Arab states' alternative patron, the Gulf War coalition of 1991 and Soviet acquiescence, the convening of Madrid under a single dominant superpower, and the weakness of the PLO after backing Iraq.","sol":"The retreat and then the collapse of the USSR removed the condition on which Arab military policy had rested. Under Gorbachev the Soviet Union cut its costly commitments abroad, restored relations with Israel and allowed Soviet Jews to emigrate there in large numbers, so Syria and the PLO lost both their patron and the argument that time was on their side. The effect showed within months of Iraq's invasion of Kuwait on 2 August 1990: the USSR did not oppose the American-led coalition, Egypt, Syria and Saudi Arabia joined it, and Kuwait was liberated by 28 February 1991. With no rival superpower to appeal to, and with Arafat isolated for having backed Saddam Hussein, the USA could convene the Madrid conference on 30 October 1991 and expect everyone to attend; the USSR was nominally co-sponsor and was dissolved before the year ended. The importance is not that Madrid settled anything, because it did not, but that it made negotiation the only available route for governments that could no longer count on Soviet weapons or Soviet vetoes. That is why the tracks opened at Madrid led on to Israel's treaty with Jordan in 1994 and, through a secret channel, to the recognition of the PLO in 1993.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what the Arab states and the PLO lost when the Soviet Union withdrew from the region.","Use the Gulf War as the test case: what did the crisis reveal about the new balance?","Judge it by what became the only available route, not by what any conference agreed."],"codes":["P5-3 — 3 Attempts at a solution: Changing superpower policies in the Middle East"]},{"q":"Question 3 asks you to explain two of three bullets, each marked separately out of 8. Answer this bullet: the importance of the Oslo Accords (1993) for the Palestinians. (8 marks)","a":"AO2 8 + AO1 8 across the two chosen parts; this part is marked out of 8 on the importance grid. Credit Israeli recognition of the PLO in the letters of September 1993, the Declaration of Principles and five years of self-rule, the Gaza-Jericho agreement and the Palestinian National Authority, Arafat's return to Gaza, Oslo II and the division into Areas A, B and C, and the postponement of Jerusalem, refugees, settlements and borders.","sol":"For the Palestinians the Oslo agreements delivered recognition and a government, and postponed everything else. The letters exchanged in September 1993 were the first time an Israeli prime minister accepted the PLO as the Palestinians' representative, which ended two decades of refusal and made Arafat a head of something rather than the leader of an exiled movement. The Declaration of Principles signed at the White House on 13 September 1993 turned that into five years of self-rule, and the Gaza-Jericho agreement of 4 May 1994 made it real: the Palestinian National Authority was created, Arafat returned to Gaza in July, and Palestinians ran their own police, schools and taxes for the first time. Oslo II, signed on 28 September 1995, extended self-rule to other West Bank towns but divided the territory into Areas A, B and C, so most of the West Bank remained under Israeli control and the self-rule areas were separated from one another. The heart of the matter is what was left out. Jerusalem, refugees, settlements and borders were all deferred to final-status talks that had no guaranteed outcome, settlement building continued, and after Rabin was murdered on 4 November 1995 there was no Israeli leader able to deliver the later stages. Oslo was therefore important as the moment the Palestinians ceased to be treated as a refugee problem and became a party to negotiations, but not as a settlement.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Separate what the agreements gave from what they deferred, and organise the answer on that line.","For each gain ask what it actually changed on the ground, and for each deferral ask who benefited from delay.","Judge it against a criterion you state: were the Palestinians nearer a state, or only nearer a negotiation?"],"codes":["P5-3 — 3 Attempts at a solution: Arafat, Rabin and the Oslo Accords (1993)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date was the Egypt-Israel peace treaty signed in Washington?","a":"26 March 1979."},{"q":"Who was the Israeli ambassador shot in London on 3 June 1982, three days before Israel invaded Lebanon?","a":"Shlomo Argov."},{"q":"Where did Arafat address the United Nations General Assembly in December 1988, and why there?","a":"In Geneva, because the USA refused him a visa to enter New York."},{"q":"Who shot Yitzhak Rabin on 4 November 1995, and where?","a":"Yigal Amir, an Israeli opposed to the Oslo agreements, at a peace rally in Tel Aviv."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The oil crisis, 1973-74","code":"P5-3 — 1 Diplomatic negotiations: The significance of the oil crisis","open":false,"back":"During the October War the Arab producers of OAPEC agreed production cuts on 17 October 1973 and embargoed the USA on 19 and 20 October. Crude rose from about three dollars a barrel to about twelve by March 1974."},{"front":"The Gulf War and the end of the Cold War","code":"P5-3 — 3 Attempts at a solution: Changing superpower policies in the Middle East","open":false,"back":"Iraq invaded Kuwait on 2 August 1990; an American-led coalition liberated it by 28 February 1991. Arafat backed Iraq and lost Gulf funding. President Bush convened the Madrid conference on 30 October 1991; the USSR was dissolved that year."},{"front":"Kissinger's shuttle diplomacy and the disengagement agreements","code":"P5-3 — 1 Diplomatic negotiations: Kissinger and 'shuttle diplomacy'","open":false,"back":"Egypt and Israel separated their forces on 18 January 1974; Israel and Syria agreed a Golan buffer zone on 31 May 1974; the second Sinai agreement followed on 4 September 1975, with Israel giving up the Mitla and Gidi passes."},{"front":"The reopening of the Suez Canal","code":"P5-3 — 1 Diplomatic negotiations: Kissinger and 'shuttle diplomacy'","open":false,"back":"Egypt reopened the canal on 5 June 1975, closed since the Six Day War of 1967, and began rebuilding the canal cities. It signalled that Sadat wanted revenue and a settlement rather than another war."},{"front":"Sadat's visit to Jerusalem","code":"P5-3 — 1 Diplomatic negotiations: Sadat's visit to Israel (1977) and Camp David","open":false,"back":"Sadat flew to Israel on 19 November 1977 and addressed the Knesset the next day, the first Arab head of state to visit Israel. Begin returned the visit at Ismailia in December 1977."},{"front":"The Camp David Accords","code":"P5-3 — 1 Diplomatic negotiations: Sadat's visit to Israel (1977) and Camp David","open":false,"back":"Signed on 17 September 1978 after thirteen days of talks hosted by President Carter: two frameworks, one for an Egypt-Israel treaty and one, much vaguer, for Palestinian autonomy. Sadat and Begin shared the 1978 Nobel Peace Prize."},{"front":"The Treaty of Washington and its cost to Egypt","code":"P5-3 — 1 Diplomatic negotiations: Sadat's visit to Israel (1977) and Camp David","open":false,"back":"Signed 26 March 1979: Egypt recognised Israel, Israel returned all Sinai by April 1982, both got American aid. Egypt was suspended from the Arab League, whose headquarters moved from Cairo to Tunis."},{"front":"The assassination of Sadat","code":"P5-3 — 1 Diplomatic negotiations: Sadat's visit to Israel (1977) and Camp David","open":false,"back":"Shot dead on 6 October 1981 by Islamist soldiers at a parade marking the anniversary of the 1973 war. Hosni Mubarak succeeded him and kept the peace treaty with Israel."},{"front":"Arafat's speech to the United Nations","code":"P5-3 — 2 The Palestinian issue: Arafat's speech to the UN (1974)","open":false,"back":"On 13 November 1974 Arafat told the General Assembly he came bearing an olive branch and a freedom fighter's gun. The Assembly then affirmed Palestinian rights and gave the PLO observer status."},{"front":"The PLO in Lebanon","code":"P5-3 — 2 The Palestinian issue: Arafat's speech to the UN (1974)","open":false,"back":"Expelled from Jordan in 1970-71, the PLO built a base in Lebanon. The Arab summit at Rabat in October 1974 named it the sole legitimate representative of the Palestinian people. Civil war began in Lebanon in 1975."},{"front":"The Israeli invasion of Lebanon","code":"P5-3 — 2 The Palestinian issue: Israeli reprisals and the invasion of Lebanon (1982)","open":false,"back":"Gunmen shot ambassador Shlomo Argov in London on 3 June 1982; Israel invaded on 6 June under Ariel Sharon and besieged Beirut. About 14,000-15,000 PLO and Syrian combatants left between 21 August and 1 September; Arafat moved to Tunis."},{"front":"Sabra and Shatila","code":"P5-3 — 2 The Palestinian issue: Israeli reprisals and the invasion of Lebanon (1982)","open":false,"back":"Between 16 and 18 September 1982 Phalangist militiamen killed hundreds of civilians in two refugee camps surrounded by Israeli forces. The Kahan Commission (February 1983) found Israel indirectly responsible and Sharon personally responsible; he left the defence ministry."},{"front":"The First Intifada","code":"P5-3 — 2 The Palestinian issue: The Israeli occupied territories and the First Palestinian Intifada","open":false,"back":"Began 9 December 1987 after four Palestinians died in a collision at the Erez crossing. Stone-throwing, strikes and boycotts across Gaza, the West Bank and East Jerusalem; more than a thousand Palestinians were killed."},{"front":"Arafat's renunciation of terrorism","code":"P5-3 — 3 Attempts at a solution: The significance of Arafat's renunciation of terrorism (1988)","open":false,"back":"The Palestine National Council declared a Palestinian state at Algiers in November 1988. Arafat spoke at Geneva on 13 December and next day accepted Israel's right to exist; George Shultz opened an American dialogue with the PLO."},{"front":"Oslo and the murder of Rabin","code":"P5-3 — 3 Attempts at a solution: Arafat, Rabin and the Oslo Accords (1993)","open":false,"back":"The Declaration of Principles was signed at the White House on 13 September 1993, the Gaza-Jericho agreement on 4 May 1994 and Oslo II on 28 September 1995. Rabin was shot on 4 November 1995."}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph - Level 3 (Paper 2 booklet P Question 2, narrative account, 8 marks)","items":["Question: 'Write a narrative account analysing the key events of the years 1973-75 that brought Egypt and Israel to the second Sinai agreement.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the oil crisis (1973-74); the reopening of the Suez Canal (1975). You must also use information of your own. This is the middle stretch of the account, carrying the story from the oil weapon to the two disengagement agreements.","Because the war had shown that Arab oil could be turned on the West, the crisis did what no Arab army had managed: OAPEC agreed staged production cuts on 17 October 1973 and declared an embargo on the USA on 19 and 20 October, crude rose from about three dollars a barrel to about twelve before the embargo was lifted in March 1974, and Western economies went into recession. That mattered for the negotiation rather than for the petrol queues. Because another Arab-Israeli war would now damage American prosperity directly, and because the worldwide alert of 25 October 1973 had shown how quickly a ceasefire in Sinai could turn into a superpower crisis, Washington could no longer wait for the two sides to agree; and because only the USA had the leverage that came from arming and financing Israel, it was the one government able to make Israel move. That is why Henry Kissinger carried terms between the capitals himself instead of convening a conference, and why the method produced results within months: Egypt and Israel signed the separation of forces along the Suez Canal on 18 January 1974, and Israel and Syria disengaged on the Golan Heights on 31 May 1974, each agreement pulling the armies out of contact and each delivered by American mediation rather than by a joint conference with Moscow.","Why this is Level 3: the material is organised as a clear sequence leading to an outcome and the linkage between the events is analysed rather than asserted - the embargo is explained as the reason Washington could no longer wait, the worldwide alert as the evidence that a local ceasefire was a superpower danger, and both together as the reason one government did the mediating. The knowledge is precise (the October dates, the price rise, the two disengagement agreements) and the answer addresses far more than three aspects of content, so the Level 3 cap does not apply."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph - Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same, 'Write a narrative account analysing the key events of the years 1973-75 that brought Egypt and Israel to the second Sinai agreement.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the oil crisis (1973-74); the reopening of the Suez Canal (1975). You must also use information of your own.","In 1973 there was a war between Israel and the Arab states, and afterwards the Arab oil producers used oil against the West. They cut production and stopped selling oil to America, and the price went up a lot, which caused problems for Western economies. After that things began to move. Kissinger flew around the Middle East talking to both sides, and Egypt and Israel agreed to pull their armies apart along the Suez Canal. Israel and Syria made an agreement about the Golan Heights as well. Egypt then reopened the Suez Canal, which had been shut since the earlier war. In 1975 there was a second agreement about Sinai in which Israel gave up some land and both sides said they would not use force against each other. This was an important step on the road to peace.","Why this is only Level 2, and what it misses: the events are in the right order and the information is accurate, so it meets 'some organisation of material into a sequence of events leading to an outcome' and 'accurate and relevant information is added'. It misses the Level 3 requirement that 'the account of events analyses the linkage between them': 'after that things began to move' and 'an important step on the road to peace' assert the joins instead of explaining them, and nothing says why the oil weapon forced Washington to act or why only the USA could make Israel move. The knowledge is loose where the top level wants the key features of the events: the only dates are the two years, the price rise is 'a lot' rather than about three dollars a barrel to about twelve, and the second Sinai agreement gives up 'some land' rather than the Mitla and Gidi passes and the Abu Rudeis oilfields."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the oil crisis, shuttle diplomacy and the reopening of the Suez Canal","items":["OAPEC agreed staged production cuts on 17 October 1973, during the October War; the embargo on the USA was declared on 19 and 20 October and extended to the Netherlands later in the month.","The price of crude oil rose roughly fourfold during the crisis, from about three dollars a barrel to about twelve, and the embargo was lifted in March 1974.","The USA airlifted arms to Israel during the war; the USSR resupplied Egypt and Syria.","After a Soviet hint on 24 October 1973 that troops might be sent to enforce the ceasefire, American forces were put on worldwide alert the following day.","Henry Kissinger was the American Secretary of State; his method of flying between capitals with terms neither side would state publicly was called shuttle diplomacy.","Egyptian-Israeli separation of forces agreement along the Suez Canal: 18 January 1974.","Israeli-Syrian disengagement agreement on the Golan Heights, creating a buffer zone patrolled by a new United Nations force: 31 May 1974.","The Suez Canal, closed since the Six Day War of 1967, was reopened by Egypt on 5 June 1975.","The second Sinai agreement, signed 4 September 1975: Israel gave up the Mitla and Gidi passes and the Abu Rudeis oilfields; both sides undertook that differences would not be settled by force; American civilians staffed early-warning stations in the passes.","Effect on the superpowers: every step was delivered by American mediation, so Egypt looked to Washington rather than Moscow and the USSR was left with nothing to broker."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Sadat, Begin, Carter and the Egypt-Israel peace","items":["Anwar Sadat was President of Egypt; Menachem Begin became Prime Minister of Israel in 1977; Jimmy Carter was President of the USA from January 1977.","Sadat flew to Israel on 19 November 1977 and addressed the Knesset in Jerusalem on 20 November, the first Arab head of state to visit Israel.","Begin returned the visit at Ismailia in Egypt in December 1977.","The Camp David talks lasted thirteen days and the two framework agreements were signed on 17 September 1978.","The two frameworks: one for a peace treaty between Egypt and Israel, one for Palestinian autonomy in the West Bank and Gaza that was left deliberately vague and was never implemented.","Sadat and Begin shared the Nobel Peace Prize for 1978.","The Egypt-Israel peace treaty, the Treaty of Washington, was signed on 26 March 1979: mutual recognition, Israeli withdrawal from the whole of Sinai, the Suez Canal open to Israeli shipping, and large American aid to both countries.","Israel completed its withdrawal from Sinai in April 1982.","Egypt was suspended from the Arab League and the League's headquarters moved from Cairo to Tunis; Egypt was readmitted in 1989.","Sadat was assassinated by Islamist soldiers on 6 October 1981 at a parade marking the anniversary of the 1973 war; Hosni Mubarak succeeded him and kept the treaty."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Arafat, the PLO, Lebanon and the invasion of 1982","items":["Yasser Arafat led the PLO from 1969; the PLO was driven out of Jordan in 1970-71 and rebuilt its base in Lebanon.","At the Arab summit at Rabat in October 1974 the Arab states declared the PLO the sole legitimate representative of the Palestinian people.","Arafat addressed the United Nations General Assembly on 13 November 1974, saying he came bearing an olive branch and a freedom fighter's gun.","Within days the General Assembly affirmed the Palestinians' right to self-determination and granted the PLO observer status at the UN.","Civil war broke out in Lebanon in 1975; the PLO controlled parts of the south and of West Beirut.","Israel's Operation Litani in March 1978 occupied Lebanese territory up to the Litani river before handing it to a United Nations force.","Shlomo Argov, Israel's ambassador in London, was shot and gravely wounded on 3 June 1982.","Israel invaded Lebanon on 6 June 1982 under defence minister Ariel Sharon, advanced to Beirut and besieged the city.","About 14,000 to 15,000 PLO and Syrian combatants were evacuated from Beirut between 21 August and 1 September 1982 under American mediation; Arafat moved the PLO headquarters to Tunis.","Between 16 and 18 September 1982 Phalangist militiamen killed hundreds of Palestinian and Lebanese civilians in the Sabra and Shatila camps, which Israeli forces had surrounded.","Israel's Kahan Commission reported in February 1983 that Israel bore indirect responsibility for the massacre and that Sharon bore personal responsibility for ignoring the danger of bloodshed; he left the defence ministry, staying in the cabinet as a minister without portfolio. Israel kept a security zone in southern Lebanon."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the occupied territories, the intifada and Arafat's renunciation of terrorism","items":["Israel had held the West Bank, Gaza, East Jerusalem and the Golan Heights since 1967; Jewish settlement grew steadily, especially after 1977.","On 8 December 1987 an Israeli army lorry collided with a vehicle at the Erez crossing in Gaza, killing four Palestinians from Jabalia refugee camp.","The First Intifada began on 9 December 1987 and spread through Gaza, the West Bank and East Jerusalem.","Its methods were stone-throwing, strikes, boycotts of Israeli goods and refusal to pay taxes, organised locally rather than from Tunis.","Israel answered with mass arrests and a policy of force and beatings under defence minister Yitzhak Rabin; more than a thousand Palestinians were killed before the uprising subsided.","Hamas, the Islamic Resistance Movement, was founded in Gaza by Sheikh Ahmed Yassin as the uprising began and published its charter in 1988, rejecting any peace with Israel.","King Hussein gave up Jordan's claim to the West Bank in July 1988.","The Palestine National Council, meeting in Algiers in November 1988, proclaimed an independent State of Palestine.","The USA refused Arafat a visa, so the General Assembly met on Palestine in Geneva; Arafat spoke there on 13 December 1988.","On 14 December 1988 Arafat stated plainly that the PLO accepted Israel's right to exist in peace and security and renounced terrorism in all its forms; Secretary of State George Shultz announced a direct American dialogue with the PLO."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the superpowers, Madrid, Oslo and the settlement of 1993-95","items":["Mikhail Gorbachev's USSR cut its commitments abroad, restored relations with Israel and allowed Soviet Jews to emigrate there in large numbers.","Iraq invaded Kuwait on 2 August 1990; the American-led coalition included Egypt, Syria and Saudi Arabia, the air campaign began on 17 January 1991 and Kuwait was liberated by 28 February 1991.","Iraq fired Scud missiles at Israel to split the coalition; Israel, under American pressure, did not retaliate.","Arafat backed Iraq: the Gulf states cut the PLO's funding and hundreds of thousands of Palestinians were forced out of Kuwait.","The Madrid conference opened on 30 October 1991, convened by President George Bush and co-sponsored by the USA and the USSR; Israel, Syria, Lebanon and a joint Jordanian-Palestinian delegation attended and the PLO was excluded by name. The USSR was dissolved before the end of 1991.","Labour under Yitzhak Rabin won the Israeli election of 1992; secret Israeli-PLO talks were held in Norway in 1993.","Letters of mutual recognition were exchanged in September 1993: Arafat recognised Israel's right to exist and renounced terrorism, Rabin recognised the PLO as the representative of the Palestinian people.","The Declaration of Principles was signed at the White House on 13 September 1993, with Rabin and Arafat shaking hands in front of President Bill Clinton; it set out five years of self-rule and deferred Jerusalem, refugees, settlements and borders.","An Israeli settler murdered Palestinian worshippers at Hebron in February 1994; Hamas answered with suicide bombings in Israeli cities.","The Gaza-Jericho agreement was signed in Cairo on 4 May 1994, creating the Palestinian National Authority; Arafat returned to Gaza in July 1994.","The Israel-Jordan peace treaty was signed on 26 October 1994. Rabin, Peres and Arafat shared the Nobel Peace Prize for 1994.","Oslo II, signed on 28 September 1995, extended self-rule to other West Bank towns and divided the West Bank into Areas A, B and C.","Yitzhak Rabin was shot dead by Yigal Amir, an Israeli opposed to Oslo, at a peace rally in Tel Aviv on 4 November 1995; Shimon Peres succeeded him."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan - Paper 2 booklet P Question 2: 'Write a narrative account analysing the key events of the years 1973-75 that brought Egypt and Israel to the second Sinai agreement' (8 marks)","items":["The question as printed: 'Write a narrative account analysing the key events of the years 1973-75 that brought Egypt and Israel to the second Sinai agreement.' (8 marks) You may use the following in your answer: the oil crisis (1973-74); the reopening of the Suez Canal (1975). You must also use information of your own.","Decode it before writing. 'Narrative account' means a sequence that leads to an outcome, so the account must begin and end somewhere definite; 'analysing' means the join between each episode and the next has to be explained rather than assumed; and the dates fix the boundaries, so nothing before the October War of 1973 and nothing after September 1975 belongs here except in a closing sentence.","The cap that decides the level: no access to Level 3 at all for an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content. Decide first which episodes are your own material and make sure those are the ones you develop.","Episode 1, a ceasefire the superpowers could not leave alone (own material): the October War of 1973 ends with the armies entangled in Sinai, the USA airlifting weapons to Israel and the USSR resupplying Egypt and Syria, and on 25 October American forces going on worldwide alert after a Soviet hint that troops might be sent to enforce the ceasefire.","Link sentence: because a local ceasefire could turn into a superpower crisis at any moment, the next move had to come from outside the region rather than from the two armies.","Episode 2, the oil weapon (the first bullet): OAPEC agrees staged production cuts on 17 October 1973 and declares an embargo on the USA on 19 and 20 October, extending it to the Netherlands later in the month; crude rises from about three dollars a barrel to about twelve, Western economies go into recession, and the embargo is lifted in March 1974.","Link sentence: with another Arab-Israeli war now able to damage the Western economy directly, Washington could no longer wait for the parties to agree, and it was the only government with leverage over Israel.","Episode 3, shuttle diplomacy (own material): Henry Kissinger carries terms between capitals instead of convening one conference, producing the Egyptian-Israeli separation of forces along the Suez Canal on 18 January 1974 and the Israeli-Syrian disengagement on the Golan Heights on 31 May 1974, with United Nations forces between the armies.","Link sentence: because the method worked in small steps and everything was delivered by one government, both sides learned that ground could be traded for undertakings provided Washington carried the offer.","Episode 4, the canal and the second Sinai agreement (the second bullet plus own material): Egypt reopens the Suez Canal on 5 June 1975, closed since 1967, tying its recovery to keeping the peace; on 4 September 1975 the second Sinai agreement takes Israel out of the Mitla and Gidi passes, which become a United Nations buffer zone with American civilians in the early-warning stations, returns the Abu Rudeis oilfields to Egypt and commits both sides not to settle their differences by force.","The closing sentence must say what the sequence amounted to rather than repeat it: in under two years the Egyptian-Israeli front had been turned from the most dangerous front in the conflict into a bargaining table run from Washington, with land exchanged for undertakings rather than for peace, which is the method Sadat carried to Jerusalem two years later.","Timing and shape: about 13 minutes - two to choose four episodes and mark which are your own, ten to write, one to check that every join carries a sentence of explanation. Four developed episodes beat six thin ones, and use the terms an examiner looks for: ceasefire, embargo, disengagement, buffer zone, mediation, undertaking."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:32:42Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"All ten items in 'fixed' check out against the kit as it now stands - the Kahan Commission now reads Israel indirectly responsible and Sharon personally responsible (February 1983, minister without portfolio), the Beirut evacuation is about 14,000 to 15,000 PLO and Syrian combatants between 21 August and 1 September 1982 in all six places it appears, the embargo is dated to 19 and 20 October after the staged cuts of 17 October, the Mitla and Gidi passes are a UN buffer zone with the Abu Rudeis oilfields handed back, 'suspension' is used throughout, the plan and both model paragraphs now work a 1973-75 narrative that is in neither the bank nor the specimen, worked example 5 is retargeted onto Jewish settlement, the Level 2 commentary matches its paragraph and question 5's 'weeks later' now names the months; I re-solved all sixteen questions and found no wrong answer key and no date, figure or attribution I can fault, so the only work left is stopping the plan and worked example 3 from writing out question 8(a) and question 7(a) for the student."}}},"30-1":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"30-1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 1 is the ground the whole of Option 30 stands on, and it is the part of the course Paper 3 draws on most often: 1917 supplies the Section A source and the causation question again and again, and Section B's interpretations are usually arguments about why one government fell and another took its place. If you can explain, with names and dates, why the tsar lost the army, why the Provisional Government never won it, and how a party that looked finished in July was governing by the end of October, you can answer almost anything this option sets. The room teaches that story as a chain of causes you can argue with, and it trains the five moves the paper rewards: inference from a source, causal explanation that goes beyond the printed bullet points, judgement of a source's usefulness for the enquiry actually named, spotting how two historians differ and why, and evaluating one of them against the other and against what you know.","idea":[{"code":"30-1 — 1 The growth of opposition to the tsar in early 1917: Growing discontent among peasants and town workers","h":"Hungry villages, hungry city: the discontent already there in 1917","t":"About four-fifths of Russians were peasants, and their complaint was land: holdings cut into strips by a growing population, redemption dues that ran until 1907, and, after 1914, requisitioning that took horses, grain and sons. The towns' complaint was supply. War industry swelled Petrograd while the railways carried shells before flour and fuel, so bread queues formed in a country that had been Europe's largest grain exporter before the war; by early 1917 town prices had risen roughly four times over their 1914 level while wages had roughly doubled. Discontent of this kind is a condition, not an event: it explains why a bread queue could become a revolution, not why it did so in February. A top-level answer uses it as the tinder and then names the spark, instead of offering hardship on its own as the cause."},{"code":"30-1 — 1 The growth of opposition to the tsar in early 1917: The impact of the First World War","h":"The war that broke the state","t":"Russia entered the war in August 1914 and lost the Second Army at Tannenberg within weeks, the First being driven out of East Prussia at the Masurian Lakes in September; the Great Retreat of 1915 gave up Poland and much of the Baltic provinces, and by 1917 Russian dead ran well over a million. The economic effects — inflation, a transport system carrying munitions first, fuel shortages — reached every town. The political effect was worse, because Nicholas II made himself commander-in-chief in September 1915, so every further defeat was personally his, and left government at home to the German-born Tsarina Alexandra and to Rasputin, with ministers changed so often that the Duma's Progressive Bloc asked in August 1915 for a government the country could trust and was prorogued for asking. A top-level answer treats the war as a multiplier: it did not invent Russia's problems, it made each of them fatal and attached them to the Tsar's own name."},{"code":"30-1 — 2 The February Revolution: The immediate reasons for the February Revolution","h":"Five days in Petrograd: how the Tsar actually fell","t":"The February Revolution was not planned by any party. Strikes began on International Women's Day, 23 February 1917 (Old Style), when women textile workers from the Vyborg district came out for bread; by 25 February a general strike had closed the city, with some 200,000 workers in the streets, and Cossacks sent against them would not charge. Troops fired on demonstrators on 26 February, and on 27 February the Volynsky Regiment mutinied and the rest of the garrison followed, opening the arsenals. That same day the Duma formed a Provisional Committee and workers and soldiers elected the Petrograd Soviet in the same building, the Tauride Palace. Nicholas, at army headquarters, had his train halted at Pskov, and General Alekseev's canvass of the front commanders advised him to go; he abdicated on 2 March for himself and his haemophiliac son, and Grand Duke Michael refused the crown the next day. A top-level answer keeps the sequence — strike, mutiny, desertion by the elites — because the order is the argument."},{"code":"30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The establishment of the Provisional Government and the problems it faced","h":"Two powers in one building: Dual Control and Order Number 1","t":"The Provisional Government formed on 2 March 1917 out of the Duma's committee under Prince Lvov, and its name was its weakness: it existed only until a Constituent Assembly could be elected, so it could claim no mandate for any hard decision. Alongside it sat the Petrograd Soviet, whose Order Number 1 of 1 March told units to elect committees, to keep weapons under those committees' control, and to obey the government only where the Soviet did not disagree. That is 'Dual Control': ministries on one side, and on the other the loyalty of the soldiers, the railwaymen and the telegraph operators. Alexander Kerensky, the only socialist in the first cabinet and a vice-chairman of the Soviet, was the single link between the two. A top-level answer uses Dual Control to explain not what the government believed but what it could physically do, which is why its orders so often stopped at the barrack gate."},{"code":"30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The weaknesses and failures of the Provisional Government","h":"The government that would not decide","t":"The Provisional Government's early reforms were real — amnesty for political prisoners, the secret police abolished, censorship lifted, freedom of religion and the press — but on the two questions that had brought people into the streets it delayed. It stayed in the war to keep faith with the Allies, and Milyukov's note of April 1917 promising victory brought crowds out again and cost him and Guchkov their posts; the June offensive, pressed by Kerensky, broke down within weeks and desertion spread. Land was reserved for the Constituent Assembly, whose election kept slipping, so peasants seized estates themselves through the summer, and by autumn bread and fuel in the cities were shorter than they had been in February. A top-level answer makes the failures causal rather than a list: each postponement handed a grievance to whoever promised to act on it at once."},{"code":"30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The significance of the Kornilov Revolt","h":"Kornilov: the revolt that armed the Bolsheviks","t":"In late August 1917 General Lavr Kornilov, whom Kerensky had made commander-in-chief in July, sent troops towards Petrograd. Kerensky dismissed him, called on the Soviet for help, released the Bolsheviks jailed after the July Days and issued rifles to the Red Guards; railway workers side-tracked Kornilov's trains and agitators talked his soldiers out of fighting, so the revolt collapsed without a battle. The significance is in what each side kept afterwards. Kerensky was left distrusted by officers as a betrayer and by workers as the man who had let the generals march; the Bolsheviks were left with arms they never gave back and the reputation of having saved the revolution, and in September they won majorities in the Petrograd and Moscow Soviets, with Trotsky elected chairman in Petrograd. A top-level answer treats Kornilov as the turning point that made October possible, and says precisely what turned."},{"code":"30-1 — 4 The Bolshevik Revolution: Lenin's return and activities","h":"Lenin's return, the April Theses and the July Days","t":"Lenin reached the Finland Station on 3 April 1917 after crossing Germany in a sealed train, and at once refused the line other socialists took. The April Theses demanded no support for the Provisional Government, an immediate end to the war, land for the peasants and 'All power to the soviets', condensed for the streets into peace, bread and land. That gave the Bolsheviks the one position nobody else held, and membership grew roughly tenfold between February and October. The July Days of 3–7 July, when armed soldiers, Kronstadt sailors and workers demonstrated for soviet power, showed the risk of the strategy: the rising failed, the government published documents accusing Lenin of being a German agent, Trotsky and others were imprisoned and Lenin fled to Finland. A top-level answer uses April and July together — a programme that fitted the grievances, and a party that had to be rescued by its enemies' mistakes before it could use it."},{"code":"30-1 — 4 The Bolshevik Revolution: The Bolshevik seizure of power in October 1917","h":"October: the opportunity and the instrument","t":"Lenin argued from hiding that the party must rise before the Second Congress of Soviets met, and on 10 October 1917 the Central Committee voted for an armed rising over the public objections of Zinoviev and Kamenev. The instrument was Trotsky's: the Military Revolutionary Committee of the Petrograd Soviet, set up that month, issued the orders, so the garrison believed it was defending the Soviet against another Kornilov rather than making a party coup. On the night of 24–25 October Red Guards and soldiers took the bridges, railway stations, telegraph and power stations with almost no fighting; the Winter Palace was entered the following night after the cruiser Aurora fired a blank round, and the ministers were arrested while Kerensky fled. The Congress, meeting on 25 October, approved the transfer of power after the Mensheviks and most Socialist Revolutionaries walked out. A top-level answer separates the opportunity, which the government created, from the means, which the Bolsheviks built, and then weighs them."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1: two inferences from Source A about the food supply","setup":"Source A in this room is a report on the disturbances in Petrograd written for the city governor by a senior police officer, dated 25 February 1917 (Old Style). It reports that since first light the queues outside the bakeries in the Vyborg district have been longer than on any day that month and that the women in them 'are no longer quiet'; that the bread ran out shortly after nine o'clock, after which the crowd moved off towards the Nevsky Prospekt and workers coming off shift at the metal works joined it; that red flags were carried and the shouting was for bread but also 'against the war and against the government'; that the writer's men are 'too few to clear the streets and are jeered at'; that a squadron of Cossacks ordered into the crowd 'walked their horses through it and did not use their whips' and were cheered; and that he cannot promise order will be restored the next day. The question is: 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about the food supply in Petrograd in February 1917. Complete the table below to explain your answer.' (4 marks)","steps":["Separate the two things this report answers before choosing anything: how bad the shortage itself had become, and how people were behaving about it. Only the first belongs to the focus you are given, so begin at the bakeries and at the hour the bread gave out.","Take the first inference from the supply itself: the queues were longer than on any day that month and the bread ran out shortly after nine o'clock, so the bread supply in the industrial districts was failing, not merely tight.","Take the second from the temper of the queue: the women in it 'are no longer quiet', which implies they had waited quietly before, and on this day they left the queue and marched rather than wait for another delivery.","Check that each entry is an inference and not a quotation with a label on it: the claim the source never states goes in the box for what you can infer, and the words that force it go in the box for details."],"cues":["Which half of this report is about supply rather than protest?","What does the hour at which the bread gave out tell you?","What does 'no longer quiet' imply about the weeks before?","Which box takes the claim and which the quotation?"]},{"title":"Question 3(a): judging Sources B and C for the enquiry actually named","setup":"Source B in this room is a letter from a Russian infantryman at the front to his family in their village, July 1917 (Old Style): in June the officers read out an order to attack, the men went forward but came back over the same ground with the wounded; the company committee then 'voted that we would hold our trench but would not attack again, and the officers could do nothing, for the Soviet's order says we obey our committee'; men go missing every week because 'the land at home is being divided now'; and it ends 'if the government in Petrograd will not give us peace, we shall take it ourselves'. Source C is a resolution passed at a general meeting of workers at a large metal works in the Vyborg district of Petrograd in September 1917 and sent to the Petrograd Soviet: 'the government of Kerensky has given us nothing'; wages have twice been overtaken by the price of bread and boots; the bread ration is smaller than in the spring; 'in August the generals marched on this city' and only the arming of the workers' own detachments and the railwaymen stopping the trains prevented disaster; it resolves for all power to the soviets, land to the peasants and an immediate offer of peace. The question is: 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the attitudes of Russian soldiers and workers towards going on with the war in 1917? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context.' (8 marks)","steps":["Write the enquiry at the head of your plan and let it decide what counts: attitudes to going on with the fighting, so the committee's vote and the closing demand of the resolution are in, and the price of boots is only background to them.","Take Source B's content for that enquiry: a committee that 'voted that we would hold our trench but would not attack again' is not mutiny and not desertion but withdrawn consent, and the last line, 'we shall take it ourselves', says the men had stopped waiting for permission to stop fighting.","Weigh Source B's nature, origin and purpose together: a private letter home, written within weeks of the attack it describes, with no censor to satisfy and nobody to impress, so it is strong evidence of what one soldier had decided, though one man in one company is a narrow base.","Do the same for Source C: a resolution voted in public and sent to the Petrograd Soviet is written to be acted on, so it gives a meeting's agreed position rather than any individual's, and it puts an immediate offer of peace beside land and soviet power as one of three demands.","Bring your own knowledge in on the sources rather than beside them: the committee that votes in Source B exists because of Soviet Order Number 1 of 1 March 1917, and the attack it refuses is the offensive Kerensky pressed from 18 June (Old Style), which broke down within weeks; the meeting in Source C votes after the Kornilov emergency of late August.","Judge for this enquiry and not in general: between them the sources give the two bodies of people whose consent the war still needed, the men in the trenches and the men in the Petrograd factories, and both have moved to peace by the autumn; neither reaches the villages, and neither shows anyone who still wanted to fight on, so the picture is sharp but one-sided."],"cues":["What exactly is the enquiry into?","Which sentence in Source B is the soldiers' decision about the fighting?","Why might a letter home be more candid than an official report?","Does a resolution's public purpose make it less useful here?","Which piece of your own knowledge explains the committee's power?","Whose attitude do neither of them reach?"]},{"title":"Question 3(b): the difference of view, on a second pair of interpretations","setup":"This worked example uses a second pair of interpretations, written for this example only. They are not the Interpretations 1 and 2 printed in this room's sources section, they are not real books, and neither is attributed to any real historian. Interpretation 3, from a study of the Petrograd workers in 1917, by a historian writing in the 1960s: 'The Tsar was brought down in the streets. Women textile workers left their mills on 23 February, the strike spread until the city had stopped, and when the Volynsky Regiment refused to fire on the crowds on 27 February the dynasty had nothing left to command. No party planned any of this; the crowd made it.' Interpretation 4, from a general history of the Russian monarchy, by a historian writing in the 2000s: 'Crowds had been faced down before. What destroyed Nicholas II in 1917 was the desertion of the men around him: Duma politicians demanding a government the country could trust, and General Alekseev canvassing the front commanders, who advised him to go. The Tsar gave up his throne at Pskov because his own generals asked him to.' The question is: 'Study Interpretations 3 and 4. They give different views about the reasons why Nicholas II lost his throne in 1917. What is the main difference between the views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations.' (4 marks)","steps":["Reduce each writer to a single sentence about cause before comparing anything: the first says the Tsar was pushed out from below, the second that he was let go from above.","Test that this really is a difference of view: neither denies the strikes and neither denies the abdication, so the disagreement is about which pressure actually removed him, not about what happened.","Anchor each half of the difference in its own writer's words, 'the crowd made it' against 'his own generals asked him to'.","Write it as one difference stated in the opening sentence and then supported from each interpretation in turn, rather than as two summaries placed side by side."],"cues":["What does each writer say removed the Tsar?","Do they disagree about events or about which pressure mattered?","Which phrase in each carries its view?","What shape should the finished answer take?"]},{"title":"Question 2: getting a 12-mark 'Explain why' past the bullet points","setup":"The question is: 'Explain why the July Days of 1917 ended in failure for the Bolsheviks. You may use the following in your answer: • the Bolshevik leaders • the government's accusation that Lenin was a German agent. You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks); on the paper Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b) and you answer one of them. The mark scheme caps an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content at a maximum of 8 marks and shuts Level 4 to it altogether: an answer that stays inside the two bullets is no longer capped inside Level 2 and can reach the top of it, but it cannot go past 8 marks. The fact file on Lenin's return and the July Days supplies the starting material: the demonstrations of 3 to 7 July 1917 (Old Style) by armed soldiers, Kronstadt sailors and workers calling for soviet power, the Bolsheviks half leading and half following them, loyal troops putting the rising down, the documents the government then published about Lenin, the imprisonment of Trotsky and others and Lenin's flight in disguise to Finland.","steps":["Turn the first bullet into a cause rather than a heading: the party the crowds were marching for would not put itself at their head, for Lenin was out of the capital when the demonstrations began, came back on 4 July and spoke to them without giving them his support, so an armed crowd was left with a slogan and no orders.","Make the second bullet do causal work too: the government published material accusing Lenin of taking German money at a moment when the summer offensive was collapsing, and garrison units that had been neutral turned out against the demonstrators, so the Bolsheviks lost the argument in the barracks before they lost the streets.","Add the reasons of your own that neither bullet prompts: the demand was all power to the soviets, but the Soviet would not take it, its Menshevik and Socialist Revolutionary majority deciding instead to blame the Bolsheviks and disarm the units involved, while the government still had regiments that would obey and brought them in from the front.","Order the three so that they build instead of sitting in a list: a rising nobody would lead, a body that refused the power being offered to it, and a government that still had both troops and an argument the garrison would believe.","Close each paragraph on the words of the question, the failure, and end by naming the reason that did most: the demand had nowhere to go once the Soviet refused it, and the accusations then turned a leaderless demonstration into a rout of the party."],"cues":["What did the Bolshevik leaders do when the crowds came out?","Why did the charge about German money matter inside the barracks?","Who refused the power the crowds were demanding?","In what order do the reasons make an argument?","What must the last sentence of the answer do?"]},{"title":"Question 3(c): explaining why two historians differ","setup":"This worked example takes the other AO4 short question, 3(c), and works it on a second pair of interpretations written for this example only: they are not the Interpretations 1 and 2 printed in this room's sources section, they are not real books, and neither is attributed to any real historian. Interpretation 5, from a study of the Russian army in 1917, by a historian writing in the 1980s: 'Discipline was not lost in 1917; it was legislated away. Order Number One put weapons under elected committees and made obedience to the government conditional, and after that an order stood only as far as the men's committee was willing to let it. What followed in the trenches was the working out of that decision.' Interpretation 6, from a study of the peasant soldier, by a historian writing in the 2010s: 'The Russian soldier was a peasant in uniform, and what decided him was the village. Word came through the summer that the estates at home were being divided, and a man absent from his own commune would get no share. The land emptied the trenches; the committees only gave the men a way to vote on it.' The question is: 'Suggest one reason why Interpretations 5 and 6 give different views about the reasons why Russian soldiers stopped obeying their officers in 1917. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer.' (4 marks)","steps":["Identify the difference before explaining it, because you cannot give a reason for a disagreement you have not yet stated: one writer says the army was unmade by a decision taken in the capital, the other by what was happening in the villages.","Choose the kind of reason the mark scheme recognises — a different question asked, a different body of evidence, or a different weight given to the same evidence — and commit to one instead of listing all three.","Name it and make it specific: these two are studying different subjects, an army and its regulations on one side and the peasantry in uniform on the other, so each has been reading the evidence its own subject puts in front of it.","Show the reason at work in the sources you are offered. Source B holds both halves: the committee that 'voted that we would hold our trench but would not attack again... for the Soviet's order says we obey our committee', and the men going missing because 'the land at home is being divided now'. A historian of the army has what she needs by the first of those sentences; a historian of the peasantry reads on to the second.","Close by making the difference legitimate rather than an error: neither writer has the evidence wrong, and what the question wants is an explanation of how two careful readings of the same year can end somewhere different."],"cues":["What is the difference, stated in one line?","Which of the three kinds of reason will you use?","What is each of them a study of?","Which sentence of Source B would each of them stop at?","Does one of the two have to be wrong?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which order, from which body, made soldiers' obedience to the Provisional Government conditional, and when?","a":"Soviet Order Number 1, issued by the Petrograd Soviet on 1 March 1917: units were to elect committees, keep weapons under committee control, and obey the government only where the Soviet did not disagree."},{"q":"Why is a one-sided document like Source C still useful for an enquiry into the collapse of support for the Provisional Government?","a":"Because the enquiry is into opinion turning against the government, and the resolution is that opinion being formed and published; its purpose, to push the Petrograd Soviet, is evidence rather than a fault."},{"q":"Name one reason for the Bolsheviks' success in October 1917 that Interpretation 2 leaves out.","a":"The Military Revolutionary Committee, which let Trotsky issue the orders in the Petrograd Soviet's name; or Lenin's insistence on rising before the Congress of Soviets met; or the rifles kept from the Kornilov emergency."},{"q":"In what sense did Nicholas II make his own position worse by taking command of the army in September 1915?","a":"Every later defeat became personally his, and he left government in Petrograd to the German-born Tsarina Alexandra and to Rasputin, so the dynasty was blamed both for the front and for the chaos in the ministries."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 3 Section A (16 marks): Question 1 prints Source A and asks 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about...' in a table of two boxes each (4 marks); Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b), and you answer one of them: each is 'Explain why...' with two stimulus points and 'You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks).","Paper 3 Section B (36 marks), all on Question 3: (a) 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into...?' (8); (b) 'What is the main difference between the views?' (4); (c) 'Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about...' (4); (d) 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about...?' (16, plus up to 4 for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology).","The stimulus-point caps on the 12-mark 'Explain why...': 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'. There is no cap at Level 2. Question 3(d) prints no stimulus points, so there is no cap on it.","Timing on Paper 3: 1 hour 30 minutes for 52 marks, so about a minute a mark plus thinking time. Roughly 5 minutes on Question 1, 18 on Question 2, 12 on 3(a), 6 each on 3(b) and 3(c), and about 40 minutes on 3(d) including the final check for the SPaG marks, which leaves a few minutes in hand.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose, and usefulness is always judged for the enquiry the question names, never in general. Contextual knowledge earns nothing unless it is used on the sources or the interpretations.","The four dates that anchor 1917 (Old Style): 23 February, the strikes begin in Petrograd; 2 March, Nicholas II abdicates and the Provisional Government is formed; late August, the Kornilov Revolt; 24-25 October, the Bolshevik seizure of power."],"questions":[{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about the situation in Petrograd in February 1917. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark per valid inference, up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail from Source A. Credit control of the capital slipping, the police outnumbered, or the troops becoming unreliable.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: the authorities were losing control of the streets of Petrograd. Details in the source that tell me this: the officer admits that his men are 'too few to clear the streets and are jeered at', and he ends by saying he cannot promise that order will be restored the next day. (ii) What I can infer: the armed forces sent against the crowds could no longer be relied on. Details in the source that tell me this: the squadron of Cossacks ordered into the crowd 'walked their horses through it and did not use their whips', and the crowd cheered them.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["An inference is something the source makes you certain of without saying it outright; ask what must be true for a police officer to write to his governor in this tone.","Read the report twice: once for what it tells you about the crowd, and once for what it tells you about the men whose job was to control it.","Give each answer in two parts, a claim the source never states and then the exact words that force it."],"codes":["30-1 — 2 The February Revolution: The immediate reasons for the February Revolution","30-1 — 1 The growth of opposition to the tsar in early 1917: Growing discontent among peasants and town workers"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about the people demonstrating in Petrograd in February 1917. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark per valid inference, up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail from Source A. Credit a protest turned political, spreading between groups of workers, or growing in confidence.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: the demonstrators were protesting about more than the shortage of bread. Details in the source that tell me this: the officer reports that red flags were carried and that the shouting was for bread but also 'against the war and against the government'. (ii) What I can infer: the protest was spreading from one group of the population to another. Details in the source that tell me this: the queues of women in the Vyborg district moved off towards the Nevsky Prospekt, and workers coming off shift at the metal works joined them as they went.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Look at what the crowd is said to be shouting, not only at what it is said to be queuing for.","The report tracks the crowd across the city; follow the route and notice who is added to it on the way.","Set out a claim the source implies, then the phrase that makes it certain, for each of your two answers."],"codes":["30-1 — 1 The growth of opposition to the tsar in early 1917: Growing discontent among peasants and town workers","30-1 — 2 The February Revolution: The immediate reasons for the February Revolution"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. They give different views on the reasons for the Bolshevik seizure of power in October 1917. What is the main difference between the views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4) needs a key difference of view supported from both: Interpretation 1 credits Bolshevik leadership and organisation, Interpretation 2 the collapse of the government's authority. Surface detail only stays in Level 1.","sol":"The main difference is over what actually caused the Bolshevik victory. Interpretation 1 puts it down to the Bolsheviks themselves: they 'knew what they wanted and how to get it', Lenin forced a reluctant Central Committee to act, Trotsky turned the Military Revolutionary Committee into the instrument of the rising, and October was therefore 'won by organisation and will'. Interpretation 2 puts it down to the failure of their opponents: power 'was not so much seized as picked up', because the Provisional Government had spent eight months refusing to leave the war or divide the land, so that 'by October almost nobody was willing to fight for it' and the Bolsheviks 'pushed at a door that was already open'.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Reduce each interpretation to a single sentence beginning 'The Bolsheviks took power because...' and compare the two sentences.","Check that your difference is one of view: both writers agree on what happened, so look for what each says did the causal work.","State the difference first in one sentence, then support each half with words taken from its own interpretation."],"codes":["30-1 — 4 The Bolshevik Revolution: The Bolshevik seizure of power in October 1917","30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The weaknesses and failures of the Provisional Government"]},{"q":"Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about the reasons for the Bolshevik seizure of power in October 1917. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4) needs one explained reason, effectively substantiated: for example that the two historians are answering different questions and so weigh different evidence.","sol":"One reason is that the two historians set out to study different things, so they weigh different evidence. Interpretation 1 comes from a study of the Bolshevik Party, so its evidence is what the leaders did: Lenin forcing the Central Committee's hand, Trotsky's Military Revolutionary Committee. From inside the party the rising must look like an achievement 'won by organisation and will'. Interpretation 2 comes from a general history of the revolution, so its evidence is the state of the whole country, of the kind found in Source C, where a Petrograd factory meeting declares that 'the government of Kerensky has given us nothing'. Read from that evidence, the government's collapse rather than Bolshevik skill looks like the cause.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Historians differ legitimately when they ask different questions of the past; look at what each interpretation is a study of.","Ask which evidence each writer would have been reading, and what that evidence would make prominent.","Name one reason, then show it at work in both interpretations, using a source if it illustrates the kind of evidence involved."],"codes":["30-1 — 4 The Bolshevik Revolution: The Bolshevik seizure of power in October 1917","30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The significance of the Kornilov Revolt"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the reasons why support for the Provisional Government collapsed in 1917? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): utility judged for this enquiry, reasoning from nature, origin and purpose, with context used to interpret. Credit Source B on the June offensive and desertion, Source C on prices, the bread ration and Kornilov.","sol":"Source B is useful because it shows the government losing the two things it could not govern without. The soldier's company 'voted that we would hold our trench but would not attack again, and the officers could do nothing', which is the June offensive breaking down inside the committee system Soviet Order Number 1 had created in March, and men are going missing because 'the land at home is being divided now'. Its provenance strengthens that: a private letter home in July, written to a family rather than to an officer or a censor, has nothing to gain by exaggerating, so it is good evidence of what ordinary soldiers thought, though one man in one company cannot show how general the mood was. Source C is useful for the towns and for the final months. It is a public resolution from a Vyborg metal works in September, written to move the Petrograd Soviet, so it is one-sided by purpose, and that is what makes it useful here, because the enquiry is into support being withdrawn and this is the withdrawal being voted through. Its complaint that wages were twice overtaken by prices matches the wartime inflation, and 'in August the generals marched on this city' is the Kornilov Revolt, after which Kerensky never won back the workers' trust. Together they are more useful than either alone, because the government lost the army and the factories at once; neither reaches the villages, where the land seizures told the same story.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Usefulness is always usefulness for something: copy the enquiry out before you look at either source.","Handle each source in turn as content, then provenance, then the context that confirms or qualifies it, and only then compare them.","Begin with what Source B lets you infer about the army, and make the first sentence a judgement rather than a description."],"codes":["30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The weaknesses and failures of the Provisional Government","30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The establishment of the Provisional Government and the problems it faced","30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The significance of the Kornilov Revolt"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the growth of support for the Bolsheviks in the second half of 1917? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): criteria-based judgements with developed reasoning on how provenance affects the content. Credit Source B on peace and land, Source C on the programme a factory meeting adopted; qualify both, since neither names the party.","sol":"Source C is the more useful of the two for this enquiry, because it shows the Bolshevik programme being adopted by people who were not asked to adopt it. A meeting of workers at a Vyborg metal works in September resolves for 'all power to the soviets, land to the peasants, and an immediate offer of peace', which is the April Theses in three clauses, and it reaches that position through its own grievances: wages overtaken twice by prices, a smaller bread ration than in the spring, and the memory that 'in August the generals marched on this city'. As a resolution meant for the Petrograd Soviet it is public and partisan, but for an enquiry into growing support that is an advantage, since what it records is a body of workers declaring where they now stand, in the district and the month in which the Bolsheviks won their majority in the Petrograd Soviet. Source B is useful more indirectly. A soldier writing home in July never mentions the Bolsheviks, but he explains why their slogans worked: his committee will not attack again, men are deserting for land being divided at home, and 'if the government in Petrograd will not give us peace, we shall take it ourselves'. Being private, it is candid evidence of the mood the party recruited from. The limitation of both is the same: they show the demand, not the organisation that captured it, so neither can say what the party did to turn that mood into Red Guards.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["The enquiry is into support for one party; ask of each source whether it shows the demand, the organisation, or both.","A source can be useful for an enquiry it never names, if it explains why people moved; say what each one lets you infer.","Start by deciding which of the two is closer to the enquiry, and open with that judgement and the reason for it."],"codes":["30-1 — 4 The Bolshevik Revolution: Lenin's return and activities","30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The weaknesses and failures of the Provisional Government","30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The significance of the Kornilov Revolt"]},{"q":"Explain why opposition to the Tsar had grown in Russia by the beginning of 1917. You may use the following in your answer: • the First World War • food shortages in the towns. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise knowledge — defeat and casualties, the Tsar as commander-in-chief from September 1915, the Tsarina and Rasputin, inflation, land hunger. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Opposition grew because the war turned old grievances into a present emergency and then made the Tsar personally answerable for it. The first reason is military failure. Russia entered the war in August 1914 and lost the Second Army at Tannenberg within weeks, while the First was driven out of East Prussia at the Masurian Lakes in September; the Great Retreat of 1915 gave up Poland and much of the Baltic provinces, and by 1917 Russian dead ran well over a million. Defeat on that scale discredited the officer class, and because the government had promised victory it discredited the government with it. The second reason is the breakdown of supply, which turned distant defeat into daily anger. War industry drew tens of thousands of new workers into Petrograd while the railways carried munitions before flour and fuel, so bread queues formed through the winter of 1916 to 1917; town prices had risen roughly four times over their 1914 level while wages had roughly doubled. A worker could be told the war was going badly and then see the proof in the queue. The third reason, which neither bullet point prompts, is that Nicholas II put himself where the blame had to land. By making himself commander-in-chief in September 1915 he attached his own name to every further retreat, and by moving to headquarters at Mogilev he left the government to the German-born Tsarina Alexandra and to Rasputin, with ministers replaced so often that the Duma's Progressive Bloc asked in August 1915 for a government the country could trust, and was prorogued for asking; when nobles murdered Rasputin in December 1916 the dynasty was being attacked from inside its own class. The war supplied the hardship, the cities supplied the crowds, and the Tsar's own decisions supplied the target, which is why by January 1917 opposition ran from the Vyborg factory gates to the Duma benches.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Opposition is not the same as hardship: ask what turned suffering into blame, and blame into blame of one man.","Plan three reasons of different kinds, military, economic and political, so the answer analyses rather than lists.","Open the first paragraph on the defeats of 1914 and 1915 and finish it by saying what they cost the government, not the army."],"codes":["30-1 — 1 The growth of opposition to the tsar in early 1917: The impact of the First World War","30-1 — 1 The growth of opposition to the tsar in early 1917: Growing discontent among peasants and town workers"]},{"q":"Explain why Nicholas II abdicated in March 1917. You may use the following in your answer: • the strikes in Petrograd in February 1917 • the mutiny of the Petrograd garrison. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): sustained causal analysis — the strike wave from 23 February, the Volynsky mutiny of 27 February, Alekseev's canvass of the front commanders. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Nicholas II abdicated because in a single week he lost the capital, then the garrison, and finally the men whose loyalty had always been the throne's last resource. The first reason is the collapse of order in Petrograd. Strikes that began on International Women's Day, 23 February 1917, when women textile workers from the Vyborg district came out for bread, spread until a general strike closed the city on 25 February with some 200,000 workers in the streets; when troops fired on demonstrators on 26 February the crowds were enraged rather than dispersed. A capital that could not be policed was a capital the government no longer held. The second reason is the mutiny, which turned a strike into a revolution. On 27 February the Volynsky Regiment refused to fire and turned on its officers, other regiments followed, and the arsenals were opened to the crowds. The Petrograd garrison was largely raw recruits and recovering wounded with no wish to be sent to the front, so the Tsar's usual remedy of soldiers against workers had ceased to exist. The third reason, which the streets by themselves cannot explain, is that the political and military elites abandoned him. On the same day the Duma ignored his order to disperse and appointed a Provisional Committee, while workers and soldiers elected the Petrograd Soviet in the same building; when Nicholas tried to return, his train was diverted and stopped at Pskov, and General Alekseev, his chief of staff, canvassed the front commanders, who advised him to go. He abdicated on 2 March for himself and for his haemophiliac son Alexei, and when Grand Duke Michael refused the crown the next day the dynasty ended. The strikes created the crisis, the mutiny made it irreversible, and the generals made it final.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Abdication is a decision, so track what was removed from the Tsar's options week by week.","Separate the crowd, the garrison and the elites, and explain what each removal made impossible.","Begin with what the strike wave of late February did to the government's control of the capital."],"codes":["30-1 — 2 The February Revolution: The immediate reasons for the February Revolution","30-1 — 1 The growth of opposition to the tsar in early 1917: The impact of the First World War"]},{"q":"Explain why the Provisional Government faced serious problems from the moment it was formed in March 1917. You may use the following in your answer: • 'Dual Control' • Soviet Order Number 1. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise detail — provisional status and no mandate, Dual Control, Order Number 1 of 1 March, the demand for land and bread. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The Provisional Government's problems were built into the way it came into being: it inherited the tsarist state's obligations without inheriting any means of enforcing them. The first reason is its own title. It was assembled on 2 March 1917 out of the Duma's Provisional Committee under Prince Lvov, and it was to govern only until a Constituent Assembly could be elected, so on every question that mattered it could be told to wait for a body with a mandate it did not have. The second reason is Dual Control. Beside it sat the Petrograd Soviet, elected by workers and soldiers in the same building, which held what the ministries did not: the loyalty of the garrison, the railwaymen and the telegraph operators. Soviet Order Number 1, issued on 1 March, made that concrete by telling units to elect committees, to keep weapons under those committees' control and to obey the government only where the Soviet did not disagree, so a ministerial order could now be stopped at the barrack gate. Alexander Kerensky, the only socialist in the first cabinet and a vice-chairman of the Soviet, was the single link between the two, and one man could not substitute for authority. The third reason, which the two bullet points do not prompt, is the expectations February had created. Crowds had come out for bread, peace and land, and the new ministers were liberals committed to the Allies and to legality: they stayed in the war, and Milyukov's note to the Allies in April promising to fight to victory brought the crowds back into the streets and cost him and Guchkov their posts. A government without a mandate, without the means of coercion and without the power to grant what its supporters wanted was in difficulty from its first week, and each of those weaknesses made the others worse.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what a government needs in order to govern, and then check which of those things this one actually possessed.","Treat the two bullet points as one problem, authority, and find a second kind of problem to set beside it.","Start from the word 'provisional' itself and what it prevented the ministers from deciding."],"codes":["30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The establishment of the Provisional Government and the problems it faced","30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The weaknesses and failures of the Provisional Government"]},{"q":"Explain why support for the Bolsheviks grew between April and October 1917. You may use the following in your answer: • the April Theses • the Kornilov Revolt. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): analysis directed at causation — the April Theses, peace, bread and land, the June offensive, the postponed land settlement, Kornilov arming the Red Guards. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Bolshevik support grew because one party offered exactly what the government kept refusing, and because the government then handed that party both weapons and a reputation. The first reason is the programme Lenin brought back. He reached the Finland Station on 3 April 1917 and at once broke with the other socialists: the April Theses demanded no support for the Provisional Government, an immediate end to the war, land for the peasants and all power to the soviets, compressed for the streets into peace, bread and land. Every other party was committed to waiting for the Constituent Assembly, so the Bolsheviks held a position nobody else was competing for. The second reason, which neither bullet prompts, is that the government spent the summer proving the Theses right. The June offensive, pressed by Kerensky, collapsed within weeks and desertion spread; land was reserved for an assembly whose election kept slipping, so peasants seized estates themselves; and bread and fuel in the cities were shorter in the autumn than they had been in February. Support does not move to a promise unless the alternative is failing, and this one was failing publicly. The third reason is the Kornilov Revolt. When Kornilov's troops moved on Petrograd in late August, Kerensky released the Bolsheviks jailed after the July Days and issued rifles to the Red Guards, and the revolt collapsed when railwaymen side-tracked the trains. The Bolsheviks kept the rifles and the credit for saving the capital, and within weeks had majorities in the Petrograd and Moscow Soviets, with Trotsky chairing the Petrograd Soviet. The programme made the party the obvious home for the discontented, the government's failures kept sending people there, and Kornilov turned that support into an armed and legitimate force, which is why the growth accelerated sharply after August.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Support moves for two reasons at once: what one side offers and what the other side does; build the answer around both.","Find a reason of your own between April and August, since the two bullets jump straight from spring to the end of summer.","Open on what the April Theses promised that no other party was promising."],"codes":["30-1 — 4 The Bolshevik Revolution: Lenin's return and activities","30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The weaknesses and failures of the Provisional Government","30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The significance of the Kornilov Revolt"]},{"q":"Explain why the Bolsheviks were able to seize power in October 1917. You may use the following in your answer: • Trotsky • the failures of the Provisional Government. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): sustained causal analysis — Trotsky and the Military Revolutionary Committee, the loss of army, factories and garrison, Lenin's decision of 10 October. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The Bolsheviks took power because an opportunity created by others was seized with an instrument they had built themselves. The first reason is the emptiness they were pushing into. By the autumn the Provisional Government had lost the army to the June offensive and to desertion, the peasantry to its own postponement of land, and the workers to shortages worse than those of February; after Kornilov it was distrusted by officers as a betrayer and by workers as the man who had let the generals march. When the rising came, Kerensky could find almost no units in the capital willing to defend him. The second reason is the instrument. The Military Revolutionary Committee of the Petrograd Soviet, set up in October and led by Trotsky, issued the orders for the rising, so the garrison understood itself to be defending the Soviet against a second Kornilov rather than installing a party; Trotsky also timed the operation to the meeting of the Second Congress of Soviets, so that power could be handed to a body that already had a Bolshevik majority. The third reason, which the bullets do not prompt, is the decision itself. Lenin argued from hiding that the party must act before the Congress met, returned in disguise, and on 10 October won the Central Committee's vote for an armed rising against the public objections of Zinoviev and Kamenev; without that insistence the party would have waited and the moment would have passed. The result was a seizure rather than a battle: on the night of 24 to 25 October Red Guards and soldiers took the bridges, railway stations, telegraph and power stations with almost no fighting, and the Winter Palace fell the following night. The government's collapse made the opening, Trotsky's committee made the method, and Lenin made the decision to use both.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Distinguish the opportunity from the means: one was made by the government, the other by the party.","Make Trotsky's paragraph about a body and a method, not about his personal qualities.","Begin by saying what the Provisional Government no longer had by October, and who was left to defend it."],"codes":["30-1 — 4 The Bolshevik Revolution: The Bolshevik seizure of power in October 1917","30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The weaknesses and failures of the Provisional Government"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the reasons for the Bolshevik seizure of power in October 1917? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views evaluated, how each is conveyed analysed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit the June offensive, postponed land and Kornilov; against, the Military Revolutionary Committee and 10 October.","sol":"Interpretation 2 argues that power in October 'was not so much seized as picked up': eight months in which the Provisional Government refused to leave the war or divide the land destroyed its authority, Kerensky's handling of Kornilov destroyed what was left in the capital, and 'the Bolsheviks pushed at a door that was already open'. The criterion for judging it must be which mattered more, the opening or the act of walking through it. On the opening, Interpretation 2 is very largely right. The June offensive, pressed by Kerensky, collapsed within weeks, and the committee system created by Soviet Order Number 1 meant that units could simply decline to attack; desertion ran on through the summer as men left to claim land at home. That land was reserved for a Constituent Assembly whose election kept slipping, so peasants took estates without waiting, and in the cities bread and fuel were shorter in the autumn than they had been in February. The interpretation's strongest point is Kornilov. In late August Kerensky dismissed his own commander-in-chief, appealed to the Soviet, freed the Bolsheviks jailed after the July Days and issued rifles to the Red Guards; the revolt collapsed when railwaymen side-tracked the trains. He was left distrusted by the officers as a betrayer and by the workers as the man who had let the generals march, which is exactly why the Bolsheviks took the Petrograd and Moscow Soviets in September. When the rising came, almost no unit in Petrograd would fight for him. Interpretation 1 puts something in the vacuum that Interpretation 2 leaves empty. It says the Bolsheviks 'knew what they wanted and how to get it', and the detail bears this out. Lenin, in hiding in Finland, insisted the party must rise before the Congress of Soviets met, returned in disguise, and on 10 October carried the Central Committee against the public objections of Zinoviev and Kamenev; without that the party would have waited. Trotsky then made the Military Revolutionary Committee of the Petrograd Soviet the body that issued the orders, so that the soldiers who took the bridges, stations and telegraph on the night of 24 to 25 October believed they were defending the Soviet against another Kornilov. That is why the seizure cost so little fighting: to the garrison it did not look like a party coup at all. The two also convey their views differently, and that is part of the argument. Interpretation 2 uses the vocabulary of accident, a door already open and power picked up, which removes the Bolsheviks as agents; Interpretation 1 uses the vocabulary of design, which removes everyone else. I agree with Interpretation 2 about the origins of the opportunity and disagree with the conclusion it draws from them. The government's failures explain why power was available in October and why no one defended it, and no amount of Bolshevik organisation could have produced that situation. But the Kadets, Mensheviks and Socialist Revolutionaries faced the same open door and did not walk through it; what distinguished the Bolsheviks was the decision of 10 October and the instrument Trotsky had prepared. An opportunity that nobody takes is not a revolution, so Interpretation 2 is right about the cause of the opening and wrong to treat the taking of it as almost automatic.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first what the interpretation is actually claiming caused October, and name the test you will judge it by.","Use Interpretation 1 as your case against, and support both sides with dated detail rather than with the interpretations restated.","Open by putting Interpretation 2's claim in your own words and stating the criterion your judgement will use."],"codes":["30-1 — 4 The Bolshevik Revolution: The Bolshevik seizure of power in October 1917","30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The weaknesses and failures of the Provisional Government","30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The significance of the Kornilov Revolt"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part played by Kerensky's own decisions in bringing the Bolsheviks to power in October 1917? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views evaluated, how each is conveyed analysed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit Kerensky's June offensive and his handling of Kornilov; against, Dual Control and Bolshevik organisation.","sol":"Interpretation 2 makes Kerensky's own choices central: it says he armed 'the very men who would overthrow him' and convinced the workers that his government could not protect the revolution, so that the Bolsheviks 'pushed at a door that was already open'. The question is how far his decisions, rather than his inheritance or his opponents' skill, explain October. There is strong support for the interpretation. Kerensky pressed the June offensive as Minister of War, and when it broke down within weeks the army's committees, created by Soviet Order Number 1, began simply refusing to attack; he restored the death penalty at the front in July, which cost him the soldiers he had gained in March. As Prime Minister from July he went on reserving the land question for a Constituent Assembly whose election kept being put off, so peasants seized estates anyway and the government looked both obstructive and powerless. Above all there is Kornilov. Kerensky appointed him commander-in-chief in July and then, in late August, dismissed him and appealed for help to the Soviet, releasing the Bolsheviks jailed after the July Days and issuing rifles to the Red Guards. Kornilov's advance collapsed when railwaymen side-tracked his trains, but Kerensky had armed his enemies, lost the confidence of the officer corps, and confirmed the workers' belief that he could not be trusted with the revolution; in September the Bolsheviks took the Petrograd and Moscow Soviets. Interpretation 1 suggests where the account is incomplete, and the context supports it. It is right that the Bolsheviks 'knew what they wanted and how to get it': Lenin forced the Central Committee to vote for a rising on 10 October against the public objections of Zinoviev and Kamenev, and Trotsky used the Military Revolutionary Committee of the Petrograd Soviet to issue the orders, so that the seizure of the bridges, stations and telegraph on the night of 24 to 25 October looked like the defence of the Soviet. Kerensky's mistakes made the rifles available, but they did not build the committee that used them. A second qualification is that much of what Interpretation 2 counts as decision was in truth constraint. From March the ministers governed under Dual Control, with the Soviet holding the loyalty of the garrison, the railways and the telegraph, and the government's provisional title denied it any mandate to make a peace or a land settlement on its own authority. The choices Kerensky made were bad, but the room he had to make them in was very narrow. The interpretations convey these views by different means: Interpretation 2's language of accident makes agency disappear except where Kerensky supplies it, while Interpretation 1's language of will and organisation concentrates it all in one party. On balance I agree with Interpretation 2 considerably, but not on its own terms. Kerensky's decisions are the best explanation of why the door stood open in October, and the Kornilov affair in particular converted a weak government into a defenceless one. They do not explain why it was the Bolsheviks who walked through, which was the work of Lenin's insistence and Trotsky's instrument, and they exaggerate his freedom of action, because Dual Control had hobbled every minister since March. His decisions were necessary to the outcome, but they were not sufficient.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Separate what one man chose from what his position forced on him before you start agreeing or disagreeing.","The Kornilov affair is the interpretation's strongest evidence; test it, then look for what it cannot account for.","Open by stating what Interpretation 2 claims about Kerensky and the criterion on which you will judge the claim."],"codes":["30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The weaknesses and failures of the Provisional Government","30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The significance of the Kornilov Revolt","30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The establishment of the Provisional Government and the problems it faced"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the collapse of support for the Provisional Government by October 1917? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views weighed, how they are conveyed analysed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit the near-bloodless seizure and the Soviet majorities; against, the Winter Palace defenders.","sol":"Interpretation 2 claims that by October the Provisional Government had been so discredited by the war, the land question and Kornilov that 'almost nobody was willing to fight for it'. Judged as a description of the capital in late October, the claim is close to accurate; judged as an explanation of why the Bolsheviks won, it leaves too much out. The evidence for it is strong. The rising of 24 to 25 October took the bridges, railway stations, telegraph and power stations with almost no fighting, which is only possible if the garrison stands aside, and the garrison had already accepted the authority of the Military Revolutionary Committee rather than the government's. The reasons had been accumulating all summer: the June offensive collapsed and desertion spread as men went home for land the government would not divide; food and fuel in Petrograd were shorter in the autumn than in February; and after Kerensky dismissed Kornilov in late August, having appointed him in July, the officers regarded him as a traitor while the workers remembered that he had armed the Red Guards only when the generals were already marching. In September the Bolsheviks took the Petrograd and Moscow Soviets. A government that has lost both the soldiers and the factories of its capital has very little left to call on. The claim still needs qualifying in two ways. First, it is not literally true. The Winter Palace was held, however feebly, by cadets, by Cossacks and by a women's battalion, and it took until the night of 25 to 26 October to enter it; Kerensky escaped from the city and tried to bring loyal troops back from the front, and a small force reached the outskirts before being turned back. What had gone was not every defender but any body of men large enough to matter. Second, and more important, unwillingness to fight for the government is not the same as willingness to be governed by the Bolsheviks. Most of Petrograd was indifferent rather than mobilised, and at the Congress of Soviets on 25 October the Mensheviks and most Socialist Revolutionaries walked out in protest. That is where Interpretation 1 is needed. It argues that October was 'won by organisation and will', and the detail supports it: Lenin forced the Central Committee's decision on 10 October over the public objections of Zinoviev and Kamenev, and Trotsky ran the rising through the Petrograd Soviet's own Military Revolutionary Committee so that it looked like defence rather than conquest. Indifference became a Bolshevik government only because one party had prepared the means to convert it. The two writers convey their views through their choice of subject as much as their wording: Interpretation 2 describes a condition, a door already open, while Interpretation 1 describes an action. I therefore agree with Interpretation 2 as a statement about the government's isolation, which is well founded, but not as a sufficient account of October. Almost nobody would fight for Kerensky; that explains why the seizure was so cheap, and Interpretation 1 explains why it happened at all.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Test Interpretation 2's claim that almost nobody would fight for the government as a factual description first, then ask what it would and would not explain if true.","Isolation from one's own side is not the same as support for the other; build one paragraph on that distinction.","Begin with what actually happened on the night of 24 to 25 October and what the ease of it implies."],"codes":["30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The weaknesses and failures of the Provisional Government","30-1 — 4 The Bolshevik Revolution: The Bolshevik seizure of power in October 1917","30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The significance of the Kornilov Revolt"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Which body issued Soviet Order Number 1, and in which month and year?","a":"The Petrograd Soviet, on 1 March 1917 (Old Style)."},{"q":"Who became Prime Minister of the Provisional Government in July 1917, and whom did he replace?","a":"Alexander Kerensky, replacing Prince Georgy Lvov."},{"q":"Name two things the Bolsheviks gained from the collapse of the Kornilov Revolt in late August 1917.","a":"Rifles for the Red Guards that Kerensky had issued and they never returned, and the released leaders and reputation as defenders of the revolution that gave them majorities in the Petrograd and Moscow Soviets in September."},{"q":"Which body of the Petrograd Soviet issued the orders to the garrison for the rising of 24 to 25 October 1917?","a":"The Military Revolutionary Committee."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Peasant land hunger before 1917","open":false,"code":"30-1 — 1 The growth of opposition to the tsar in early 1917: Growing discontent among peasants and town workers","back":"About four-fifths of Russians were peasants. Population growth left holdings too small, redemption payments on land ran until 1907, and the war took horses, grain and sons from the villages."},{"front":"Petrograd's workers in wartime","open":false,"code":"30-1 — 1 The growth of opposition to the tsar in early 1917: Growing discontent among peasants and town workers","back":"War industry swelled the city while the railways carried munitions before flour and fuel. By early 1917 town prices had risen roughly four times over their 1914 level and wages roughly twice."},{"front":"The Tsar as commander-in-chief","open":false,"code":"30-1 — 1 The growth of opposition to the tsar in early 1917: The impact of the First World War","back":"Nicholas II took personal command of the army in September 1915. Every later defeat became his own, and government in Petrograd was left to the Tsarina Alexandra and Rasputin."},{"front":"Rasputin","open":false,"code":"30-1 — 1 The growth of opposition to the tsar in early 1917: The impact of the First World War","back":"Siberian holy man trusted by the Tsarina for his effect on the haemophiliac Tsarevich Alexei, and blamed for the constant changes of minister. Murdered by a group of nobles in December 1916."},{"front":"23 February 1917 (Old Style)","open":false,"code":"30-1 — 2 The February Revolution: The immediate reasons for the February Revolution","back":"International Women's Day in Petrograd: women textile workers from the Vyborg district marched for bread and were joined by strikers from the metal works. The February Revolution had begun."},{"front":"27 February 1917 (Old Style)","open":false,"code":"30-1 — 2 The February Revolution: The immediate reasons for the February Revolution","back":"The Volynsky Regiment mutinied and the Petrograd garrison joined the crowds. The same day the Duma named a Provisional Committee and workers and soldiers elected the Petrograd Soviet."},{"front":"2 March 1917 (Old Style)","open":false,"code":"30-1 — 2 The February Revolution: The immediate reasons for the February Revolution","back":"Nicholas II abdicated at Pskov for himself and his son Alexei; Grand Duke Michael refused the throne the next day, ending Romanov rule after three hundred years."},{"front":"Soviet Order Number 1","open":false,"code":"30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The establishment of the Provisional Government and the problems it faced","back":"Issued by the Petrograd Soviet on 1 March 1917. Units were to elect committees, keep weapons under committee control, and obey the Provisional Government only where the Soviet did not disagree."},{"front":"Dual Control","open":false,"code":"30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The establishment of the Provisional Government and the problems it faced","back":"Russia's two centres of power after February 1917: the Provisional Government held the ministries, the Petrograd Soviet held the soldiers, the railwaymen and the telegraph. Neither could govern alone."},{"front":"Alexander Kerensky","open":false,"code":"30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The establishment of the Provisional Government and the problems it faced","back":"Lawyer; the only socialist in the first Provisional Government, as Minister of Justice, and a vice-chairman of the Petrograd Soviet. Minister of War in May 1917, Prime Minister in July, fled in October."},{"front":"The June offensive of 1917","open":false,"code":"30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The weaknesses and failures of the Provisional Government","back":"Launched on 18 June and pressed by Kerensky. Early gains were reversed within weeks by counter-attack, desertion spread, and the government lost what authority it had in the army."},{"front":"Lenin's April Theses","open":false,"code":"30-1 — 4 The Bolshevik Revolution: Lenin's return and activities","back":"Set out after his return on 3 April 1917: no support for the Provisional Government, an immediate end to the war, land for the peasants, all power to the soviets. In the streets: peace, bread and land."},{"front":"The July Days","open":false,"code":"30-1 — 4 The Bolshevik Revolution: Lenin's return and activities","back":"3-7 July 1917: armed soldiers, Kronstadt sailors and workers demonstrated in Petrograd for soviet power. The rising failed, Trotsky was imprisoned and Lenin fled to Finland, accused of being a German agent."},{"front":"The Kornilov Revolt","open":false,"code":"30-1 — 3 The Provisional Government: The significance of the Kornilov Revolt","back":"Late August 1917: General Kornilov sent troops towards Petrograd. Kerensky dismissed him, freed Bolshevik prisoners and armed the Red Guards; railwaymen side-tracked the trains and the revolt collapsed."},{"front":"24-25 October 1917 (Old Style)","open":false,"code":"30-1 — 4 The Bolshevik Revolution: The Bolshevik seizure of power in October 1917","back":"Acting on orders from Trotsky's Military Revolutionary Committee, Red Guards and soldiers took the bridges, stations, telegraph and power stations with almost no fighting. The Winter Palace fell next night."}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The sources and interpretations written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Source A: From a report on the disturbances in the capital, written for the city governor by a senior officer of the Petrograd police, 25 February 1917 (Old Style). Written for this room in the style of such a report; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — Since first light the queues outside the bakeries in the Vyborg district have been longer than on any day this month, and the women in them are no longer quiet. When the bread ran out shortly after nine o'clock the crowd moved off towards the Nevsky Prospekt, and workers coming off shift at the metal works joined it as it went. Red flags were carried. The shouting was for bread, but also against the war and against the government. My men are too few to clear the streets and are jeered at when they try. When a squadron of Cossacks was ordered into the crowd this afternoon the riders walked their horses through it and did not use their whips, and the crowd cheered them. I cannot promise Your Excellency that order will be restored tomorrow.","Source B: From a letter written by a Russian infantryman at the front to his family in their village, July 1917 (Old Style). Written for this room in the style of a soldier's letter home; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — You ask when I am coming home and nobody here can tell you. In June the officers read us an order to attack, and at first we went forward, but the guns opposite were better served than ours and we came back over the same ground carrying the wounded. Our company committee met that evening and voted that we would hold our trench but would not attack again, and the officers could do nothing, for the Soviet's order says we obey our committee. Every week men go missing from the line. They say the land at home is being divided now, and a man who is not there will get nothing. If the government in Petrograd will not give us peace, we shall take it ourselves.","Source C: From a resolution passed at a general meeting of the workers of a large metal works in the Vyborg district of Petrograd and sent to the Petrograd Soviet, September 1917 (Old Style). Written for this room in the style of such a factory resolution; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — This meeting declares that the government of Kerensky has given us nothing. Our wages have been raised twice this year, and twice the price of bread and boots has run ahead of them; the bread ration is smaller than it was in the spring. In August the generals marched on this city, and if our own detachments had not been given rifles, and if the railwaymen had not stopped the trains, we should now be under the whip. A government that let this happen cannot be trusted with the defence of the revolution. This meeting resolves: all power to the soviets, land to the peasants, and an immediate offer of peace.","Interpretation 1: From a study of the Bolshevik Party in 1917, by a historian writing in the 1970s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — The Bolsheviks took power in October because they, almost alone among the parties, knew what they wanted and how to get it. Lenin forced a reluctant Central Committee to accept that the moment had come and refused to wait for the Congress of Soviets to hand power over. Trotsky turned the Military Revolutionary Committee of the Petrograd Soviet into the instrument of the rising, so that the seizure of the bridges, the stations and the telegraph looked like the defence of the Soviet rather than a coup. Simple slogans gave the party the factories and the garrison. October was won by organisation and will.","Interpretation 2: From a general history of the Russian revolution, by a historian writing in the 1990s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — Power in October was not so much seized as picked up. The Provisional Government had spent eight months refusing to do the two things the country wanted, to leave the war and to divide the land. The June offensive destroyed what was left of its authority in the army, and Kerensky's handling of Kornilov in August destroyed it in the capital, for he armed the very men who would overthrow him and convinced the workers that his government could not protect the revolution. By October almost nobody was willing to fight for it. The Bolsheviks pushed at a door that was already open.","How the room uses them: Source A is the inference source for Question 1. Sources B and C are the contemporary pair for the 8-mark usefulness question, 3(a). Interpretations 1 and 2 are the pair for 3(b), 3(c) and 3(d). Every quotation in the model answers of this room comes from the five texts above and from nowhere else."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 3 Question 3(d), 16 marks)","items":["Question: 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the importance of the land question in bringing the Bolsheviks to power in 1917? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]. This is one paragraph from the middle of the answer, putting the case against Interpretation 2.","Interpretation 2 makes the land one of 'the two things the country wanted' and treats the refusal to divide it as a reason power fell to the Bolsheviks; but the land question explains the countryside's hostility to the Provisional Government far better than it explains who replaced it. The peasants did not wait to be led. Through the summer of 1917 they took the estates themselves, while the government's land committees were still preparing a settlement for a Constituent Assembly whose election kept slipping, so the villages were already helping themselves before October and needed no party to do it for them. Power, meanwhile, was taken in the cities: on the night of 24 to 25 October it was the Petrograd garrison and the Red Guards, acting on the orders of the Military Revolutionary Committee, who held the bridges, the stations and the telegraph, and no peasant rising put them there. The test came three weeks later, when the elections to the Constituent Assembly gave the Socialist Revolutionaries about two-fifths of the vote, overwhelmingly rural, and the Bolsheviks just under a quarter, concentrated in the cities and the army. Even the Decree on Land of 26 October 1917, which abolished private ownership of land, was built on the peasants' own mandates and on the Socialist Revolutionary programme rather than on anything distinctively Bolshevik. On this point Interpretation 1 is nearer the mark: what decided October was 'organisation and will'. The land grievance made the country ungovernable for Kerensky, which is Interpretation 2's real strength, but it did not decide which party would govern instead.","Why this is Level 4: it 'provides an explained evaluation reviewing the alternative views in coming to a substantiated judgement' — Interpretation 2's land claim is granted its real force and then measured against what the land actually did in 1917. The contextual knowledge is precisely selected rather than general: the summer seizures, the land committees and the slipping election, the targets taken on the night of 24 to 25 October, the shares of the vote in November and the borrowed programme of the Decree on Land. The analysis is precise about how the difference of view is conveyed, since Interpretation 2 binds the war and the land together as 'the two things the country wanted' and lets the pair explain the outcome, where Interpretation 1 makes the outcome a matter of 'organisation and will'. The line of reasoning is sustained, and the closing sentence separates two questions — who lost the country and who took the power — rather than summarising."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same as above — 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the importance of the land question in bringing the Bolsheviks to power in 1917?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks].","Interpretation 2 says the Provisional Government refused to divide the land, and I mostly agree with this. The peasants wanted land very badly and were angry that nothing was being done about it, so they turned against the government and towards the Bolsheviks, who promised them land and were therefore popular in the villages. The Bolshevik slogan was peace, bread and land, which was much better than anything the other parties offered. However, Interpretation 1 says October was won by organisation and will, so Lenin and Trotsky must have been important as well because they planned the takeover carefully. Overall I think the land was the main reason but the Bolsheviks were well organised too.","Why this is Level 2 and not Level 3: it does offer 'valid evaluative comment to agree with or counter the interpretation' and it does take details from both, which is what Level 2 asks for. What it misses is Level 3's 'relevant contextual knowledge used directly to support the evaluation': 'wanted land very badly' and 'planned the takeover carefully' are assertions standing where the summer land seizures, the Decree on Land of 26 October and the November election results should be. One of those assertions is worse than vague — that the Bolsheviks were popular in the villages is exactly the claim the Constituent Assembly elections three weeks after October put in doubt, and an answer that had the figures could not have written it. It also fails to show how the interpretations convey their views, and its judgement is announced in the last sentence rather than reasoned towards, so 'its justification is insecure or undeveloped and a line of reasoning is not sustained'. Turning it into a Level 3 answer means replacing every general adjective with a named fact that does evaluative work."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the growth of opposition to the tsar, 1914-17, and the Tsar as commander-in-chief","items":["Russia entered the First World War in August 1914; St Petersburg was renamed Petrograd in the same month because the old name sounded German.","Tannenberg, August 1914: the Russian Second Army was surrounded and destroyed in East Prussia. A second defeat at the Masurian Lakes in September 1914 drove the First Army out of East Prussia.","The Great Retreat of 1915 gave up Poland, Lithuania and much of the Baltic provinces; by 1917 Russian dead ran well over a million, with several million more wounded or taken prisoner.","The Brusilov offensive of June to September 1916 broke the Austrians at first but cost enormous casualties and ended in stalemate, using up the army's last reserves of confidence.","Nicholas II made himself commander-in-chief in September 1915, replacing Grand Duke Nicholas, so that every later defeat was personally his.","With the Tsar at headquarters in Mogilev, government at home was left to the German-born Tsarina Alexandra and to Rasputin, and ministers were replaced repeatedly through 1915 and 1916.","Rasputin was trusted by the Tsarina for his apparent effect on the haemophiliac Tsarevich Alexei; a group of nobles murdered him in December 1916, which damaged the dynasty even among its own supporters.","The Progressive Bloc of Duma parties asked in August 1915 for a government that had the confidence of the country; Nicholas refused and prorogued the Duma.","Wartime inflation: by early 1917 town prices had risen roughly four times over their 1914 level while wages had roughly doubled.","The railways carried munitions before flour and fuel, so bread queues formed in Petrograd even though Russia had been Europe's largest grain exporter before the war.","About four-fifths of Russia's people were peasants; land hunger, redemption payments that ran until 1907, and the requisitioning of horses, grain and men made the villages hostile."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: events in Petrograd and the February Revolution, 23 February - 3 March 1917 (Old Style)","items":["Russia kept the Julian calendar until February 1918, thirteen days behind the West, so the February and October revolutions fall in March and November by Western dating.","23 February 1917: International Women's Day marches in Petrograd, joined by women textile workers from the Vyborg district demanding bread.","25 February 1917: a general strike closed the city, with some 200,000 workers in the streets, and Cossacks ordered against the crowds would not charge them.","26 February 1917: troops fired on demonstrators, killing a number of them; Rodzianko, president of the Duma, telegraphed the Tsar warning that the capital was out of control and urging him to appoint a government the country would trust.","27 February 1917: the Volynsky Regiment mutinied and turned on its officers; the rest of the Petrograd garrison followed, and the arsenals and prisons were opened.","27 February 1917: the Duma ignored the order to disperse and appointed a Provisional Committee, while workers and soldiers elected the Petrograd Soviet; both met in the Tauride Palace.","28 February - 1 March 1917: Nicholas left Mogilev for Petrograd but his train was diverted and halted at Pskov.","General Mikhail Alekseev, the army's chief of staff, canvassed the front commanders, who advised the Tsar to abdicate; the throne's last resource had gone.","2 March 1917: Nicholas II abdicated at Pskov for himself and for his son Alexei, who had haemophilia.","3 March 1917: Grand Duke Michael refused the throne, ending Romanov rule that had begun in 1613."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Provisional Government, Dual Control, Soviet Order Number 1 and Kerensky","items":["The Provisional Government was formed on 2 March 1917 out of the Duma's Provisional Committee, headed by Prince Georgy Lvov, to govern only until a Constituent Assembly could be elected.","Soviet Order Number 1, issued by the Petrograd Soviet on 1 March 1917: units were to elect committees, weapons were to stay under those committees' control, and the Provisional Government was to be obeyed only where the Soviet did not disagree.","'Dual Control' (dvoevlastie): the Provisional Government held the ministries; the Petrograd Soviet held the loyalty of the garrison, the railwaymen and the telegraph operators.","Alexander Kerensky was the only socialist in the first cabinet, as Minister of Justice, and also a vice-chairman of the Petrograd Soviet: the single man in both bodies.","Early reforms were real: an amnesty for political prisoners, the abolition of the tsarist secret police, the end of censorship, freedom of religion and of the press, and the abolition of the death penalty at the front.","The government stayed in the war to keep faith with the Allies; Milyukov's note to them in April 1917 promising victory brought crowds back onto the streets and cost Milyukov and Guchkov their posts.","Kerensky became Minister of War in May 1917 and Prime Minister in July 1917 when Lvov resigned.","The June offensive, launched on 18 June 1917 and pressed by Kerensky, was reversed within weeks by counter-attack; desertion spread and Kerensky restored the death penalty at the front in July.","Land was reserved for the Constituent Assembly and its election kept being postponed, so peasants seized estates through the summer of 1917 without waiting.","Food and fuel shortages in the cities were worse in the autumn of 1917 than they had been in February, and inflation continued through the year."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Lenin's April Theses, the July Days, the Kornilov Revolt and October 1917","items":["Lenin reached the Finland Station in Petrograd on 3 April 1917, having crossed Germany in a sealed train that the German government allowed through.","The April Theses demanded no support for the Provisional Government, an immediate end to the war, land for the peasants and all power to the soviets; the street slogan was peace, bread and land.","Bolshevik membership grew roughly tenfold between February and October 1917, and the party press, including Pravda, was distributed to factories and to the front.","The July Days, 3-7 July 1917: armed soldiers, Kronstadt sailors and workers demonstrated in Petrograd for soviet power; the Bolsheviks half led and half followed the rising, and loyal troops put it down.","After the July Days the government published documents accusing Lenin of being a German agent; Trotsky and other leaders were imprisoned and Lenin fled in disguise to Finland.","The Kornilov Revolt, late August 1917: General Lavr Kornilov, appointed commander-in-chief in July, sent troops towards Petrograd. Kerensky dismissed him, appealed to the Soviet, released the jailed Bolsheviks and issued rifles to the Red Guards.","Kornilov's advance collapsed without a battle: railway workers side-tracked his trains and agitators persuaded his soldiers not to fight.","After Kornilov the Bolsheviks kept the rifles and the credit for saving the capital; in September 1917 they won majorities in the Petrograd and Moscow Soviets, and Trotsky was elected chairman of the Petrograd Soviet.","On 10 October 1917 the Bolshevik Central Committee voted for an armed rising; Zinoviev and Kamenev opposed it and made their opposition public in a newspaper.","The Military Revolutionary Committee of the Petrograd Soviet, set up in October 1917, was formally chaired by the Left Socialist Revolutionary Pavel Lazimir and led by Trotsky as chairman of the Soviet; it issued the orders for the rising, so it appeared to be the Soviet defending itself.","On the night of 24 to 25 October 1917 Red Guards and soldiers took the bridges, railway stations, telegraph and power stations with almost no fighting; Kerensky left the city to look for loyal troops.","The Winter Palace, held by cadets, Cossacks and a women's battalion, was entered on the night of 25 to 26 October after the cruiser Aurora fired a blank round, and the ministers were arrested.","The Second All-Russian Congress of Soviets, meeting on 25 October 1917, approved the transfer of power after the Mensheviks and most Socialist Revolutionaries walked out; Sovnarkom was formed with Lenin as chairman and Trotsky as commissar for foreign affairs."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the source and interpretation moves this paper rewards","items":["Inference: say something the source does not state but that must be true if the source is true, then give the words that force it. One inference and one supporting detail in each box.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose: what kind of document it is, who made it and when, and why it was made.","Utility: judge each source for the enquiry the question names, not in general. Content first, then how nature, origin and purpose make that content more or less useful, then the context that confirms or qualifies it.","Contextual knowledge scores only when it is used on the source; a paragraph about the period that never touches the sources earns nothing.","'Biased' is not a judgement on its own. Say whose view the source gives, why it gives that view, and what it is therefore useful for: a one-sided source is excellent evidence of the view it takes.","A source can be useful for an enquiry it never mentions, if what it shows explains the thing being enquired into; say what you can infer, not just what it says.","Difference of view (3(b)): a disagreement about the past, not a difference of wording or of detail. State the one difference, then support it with a phrase from each interpretation.","Reason for difference (3(c)): interpretations differ because they ask different questions, rest on different evidence, or give weight to different parts of the story. Name which, and show it from the interpretations, using Sources B and C if they illustrate the evidence.","Differences based on conclusions drawn from evidence are legitimate; an interpretation is not 'wrong' simply because another one disagrees with it.","Evaluating an interpretation (3(d)): use both interpretations and your own knowledge, show how each conveys its view, then decide how far the named interpretation can be agreed with and say on what criterion."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 3 Question 3(d): 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the importance of the First World War in bringing the Bolsheviks to power in 1917?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","items":["The question as printed: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the importance of the First World War in bringing the Bolsheviks to power in 1917? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' Note that this question prints no stimulus points: the two interpretations are the stimulus, and both must be used, so an answer that evaluates one in isolation cannot reach the top levels.","Decode the statement. Interpretation 2 makes the war the engine: the Provisional Government spent eight months refusing 'to leave the war and to divide the land', 'the June offensive destroyed what was left of its authority in the army', and power was therefore 'not so much seized as picked up'. Interpretation 1 never mentions the war at all and explains October by Lenin, Trotsky, the Military Revolutionary Committee and simple slogans, so that October was 'won by organisation and will'. The criterion to be applied, and to be stated in the opening, is whether the war was the force that put the Bolsheviks in power or one of the conditions that a party still had to use.","Opening (three or four sentences): state what Interpretation 2 argues in your own words, name Interpretation 1 as the alternative weighting, announce the criterion, and signal the position you will end at, so the line of reasoning is visible from the start.","Paragraph 1, for Interpretation 2 — the war made the ground. Russia entered the war in August 1914 and had lost well over a million dead by 1917; the railways carried munitions before flour, and by early 1917 town prices stood at roughly four times their 1914 level while wages had roughly doubled. Nicholas II took command of the army in September 1915, so every later defeat was personally his. Interim judgement: without the war there is no February collapse, and so no Provisional Government for the Bolsheviks to overthrow.","Paragraph 2, for Interpretation 2 — the war destroyed the Provisional Government, which is the interpretation's own strongest ground. Milyukov's note of April 1917 promising victory brought crowds out and cost him and Guchkov their posts; the offensive Kerensky pressed from 18 June (Old Style) broke down within weeks; under Soviet Order Number 1 of 1 March committees could decline to attack, and desertion ran on through the summer. Interim judgement: the one decision the government could not reverse was the decision to stay in the war.","Paragraph 3, for Interpretation 2 — the war gave the Bolsheviks their position. Lenin reached Petrograd on 3 April 1917 and the April Theses demanded an immediate end to the war when every other party asked for patience, condensed in the streets into peace, bread and land; party membership grew roughly tenfold between February and October. Interim judgement: the party's advantage was the war issue itself, so Interpretation 2 is describing the Bolsheviks' opportunity as well as the government's failure.","Paragraph 4, against Interpretation 2 — not every grievance was made by the war. Peasant land hunger was older than 1914: strip holdings cut finer by a growing population and redemption dues that ran until 1907. What the Provisional Government did with land in 1917 was its own choice, reserving it for a Constituent Assembly whose election kept slipping, so peasants seized estates anyway. Interim judgement: Interpretation 2 pairs the war with the land and then lets the war carry both.","Paragraph 5, against Interpretation 2 and the point that goes furthest beyond both texts — the war does not explain which party took the power. The Kornilov emergency of late August put rifles into the hands of the Red Guards; the Military Revolutionary Committee of the Petrograd Soviet, led by Trotsky, issued the orders on the night of 24 to 25 October so that the garrison believed it was defending the Soviet; and the Central Committee's vote of 10 October, against the published objections of Zinoviev and Kamenev, fixed the timing. The Mensheviks and Socialist Revolutionaries faced the same war and walked out of the Congress instead. Interim judgement: the war made power available; it did not choose who picked it up.","The evaluation thread that must run through every paragraph: how each interpretation conveys its view. Interpretation 2 writes in the passive of a process acting on people — authority 'destroyed', power 'picked up', a door already open — so the war and the government's failures do the work. Interpretation 1 writes only agency, 'knew what they wanted and how to get it', 'organisation and will', and never names the war at all, so its silence is itself the counter-argument. Saying this, and using it, is what the top level means by analysing how the differences of view are conveyed.","What the conclusion must say: not 'both are partly right'. It must apply the criterion announced at the start — that Interpretation 2 is convincing about the war as the maker of the opportunity, since the February collapse, the June offensive and the loss of the army all run back to it, but that a war explains why governments fell across Europe and not why this particular party replaced this one. Then name the deciding consideration: the rifles kept from Kornilov, the Military Revolutionary Committee and the decision of 10 October are what made the Bolsheviks, rather than any other party, the ones who used what the war had opened.","Timing and SPaG: about 40 minutes in all — six to plan the paragraphs and fix the criterion, about thirty to write, and the last four to check spelling and punctuation and to make sure the specialist terms are both present and correctly used, since 4 additional marks depend on 'a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate'. Useful terms here: Provisional Government, Petrograd Soviet, Dual Control, Soviet Order Number 1, Red Guards, Military Revolutionary Committee, Constituent Assembly, soviet, Bolshevik, provisional."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:15:56Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"Re-solved all fourteen questions and re-checked every date, figure and attribution against the printed sources and the web: the retargeted items hold — July Days 3 to 7 July 1917 Old Style with the German-agent documents released on 4 to 5 July, the June offensive opening 18 June Old Style and broken by the counter-attack within weeks, Milyukov's note of 18 April and the loss of his and Guchkov's posts, Order Number 1 of 1 March, the Decree on Land of 26 October built on the peasants' own mandates, and the Constituent Assembly elections from 12 November with the Socialist Revolutionaries near two-fifths and the Bolsheviks just under a quarter; every claim about Sources A to C and Interpretations 1 and 2 is true of the printed text, no stem repeats the Option 30 specimen (whose 3(a) to 3(d) are on the Civil War and whose Question 1 infers about the government rather than the streets), levels, tariffs, the 2024 cap wording, the 1 hour 30 timing and the 3(d) SPaG marking all match levels.md, provenance¹ is defined as nature, origin and purpose throughout, and dupscan prints 'no duplication' — publish, but consider moving the essay plan off the ground room.questions[11] already covers."}}},"30-2":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"30-2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 2 is where Option 30 stops being a story about how a government fell and becomes a story about how one was built. It supplies more Paper 3 questions than any other part of the option, because it contains everything the paper likes: a government issuing decrees, a police force, a war, a famine, a mutiny and a reversal of policy, all inside seven years. Section A draws its source and its 12-mark causation question from here again and again — why the Constituent Assembly was closed, why Brest-Litovsk was signed, why the Bolsheviks won the Civil War, why the New Economic Policy was introduced. Section B is almost always an argument about the same question in two forms: did the Bolsheviks hold Russia by force, or because enough Russians preferred them to the alternative? If you can argue that with dates, names and figures, and weigh one historian's answer against another's, you can answer almost anything this key topic sets.","idea":[{"code":"30-2 — 1 Early consolidation of power, 1917–18: The early Bolshevik decrees","h":"Government by decree: the first weeks","t":"The Bolsheviks seized power on 25 to 26 October 1917 by the Russian calendar, which was thirteen days behind the Western one until Russia changed over in February 1918, so the decrees are dated November and December 1917 in most books. The Second Congress of Soviets was handed the Decree on Peace, calling for an immediate peace without annexations, and the Decree on Land, which abolished private ownership of land and gave the estates to the peasants who had in many places already taken them. Within the next six weeks came workers' control of the factories, an eight-hour working day, the abolition of titles and ranks, a ban on the opposition press, the nationalisation of the banks, civil marriage with divorce on the request of either partner, and, on 20 December 1917, the Cheka. A top-level answer notices two things about this list: most of it cost the new government nothing, because it legalised what was already happening or promised what it could not yet deliver, and it bought time in which the harder instruments of rule could be assembled."},{"code":"30-2 — 1 Early consolidation of power, 1917–18: The removal of opposition","h":"Clearing the field: the Assembly, the rival parties and the Romanovs","t":"Lenin allowed the elections to the Constituent Assembly that the Provisional Government had never managed to hold: they took place in November 1917 and the Bolsheviks lost them. The Socialist Revolutionaries took roughly 370 of the seats and the Bolsheviks about 175, around a quarter of the votes cast. The Assembly met in the Tauride Palace in Petrograd on 5 January 1918 (Old Style), refused to hand its authority to the Congress of Soviets, and was closed by its Bolshevik guards after a single day's sitting; demonstrations in its support were fired on. The Kadets had already been banned as enemies of the people before the end of 1917, opposition papers were shut, and in June 1918 Mensheviks and Right Socialist Revolutionaries were expelled from the soviets. On the night of 16 to 17 July 1918, with Czechoslovak and White forces closing on Yekaterinburg, Nicholas II, Alexandra, their five children and four servants were shot in the cellar of the Ipatiev House on the orders of the Ural Regional Soviet. A top-level answer explains the sequence as one policy, not four episodes: every body that could have claimed to speak for Russia instead of the party was removed, and the killing of the Romanovs removed the one figure the Whites could have restored."},{"code":"30-2 — 1 Early consolidation of power, 1917–18: The Treaty of Brest-Litovsk","h":"Brest-Litovsk: buying time with territory","t":"The Bolsheviks had promised peace and had to deliver it, but Germany's terms were brutal. An armistice was agreed in December 1917 and talks opened at Brest-Litovsk. Trotsky tried the formula of neither war nor peace in February 1918; the Germans simply resumed their advance on 18 February and met almost no resistance. Lenin threatened to resign, carried the Central Committee on 23 February against Bukharin's Left Communists, who wanted a revolutionary war, and the treaty was signed on 3 March 1918 and ratified by the Fourth Congress of Soviets in the same month. Russia gave up Poland, Finland, the Baltic provinces and Ukraine: on the usual estimates about a third of the old empire's population and of its farmland, the great bulk of its coal, and a large part of its industry. The government moved from Petrograd to Moscow in March 1918. The Left Socialist Revolutionaries left the government in protest and in July 1918 murdered the German ambassador, Count Mirbach, hoping to restart the war; Whites and Allies called the treaty treason and used it to justify intervention. The treaty was annulled in November 1918 once Germany was defeated. A top-level answer argues the trade: Lenin exchanged territory he did not control for the months in which he could build an army, and the price was a civil war fought partly over the terms he had signed."},{"code":"30-2 — 2 The Civil War, 1918–21: The reasons for, key events and effects of the Civil War","h":"The Civil War, 1918 to 1921: who fought, where and with what result","t":"The war grew out of everything above: the seizure of power, the closed Assembly, requisitioning, and above all Brest-Litovsk, which turned the Allies into enemies and gave the Whites a patriotic cause. The revolt of the Czechoslovak Legion along the Trans-Siberian railway in May 1918 turned scattered resistance into a front. The Whites were three separate armies with thousands of miles between them: Kolchak, who declared himself Supreme Ruler at Omsk in November 1918 and drove west in the spring of 1919; Denikin, who took over the Volunteer Army in the south after Kornilov was killed in April 1918 and reached Orel in October 1919, the nearest any White army came to Moscow; and Yudenich, who came within sight of Petrograd from Estonia in the same month. British, French, American and Japanese troops landed at Murmansk, Archangel, the Black Sea and Vladivostok. By the end of 1920 Wrangel had evacuated the Crimea and the war was effectively over. The effects were catastrophic: far more people died of typhus and hunger than of wounds, industrial output collapsed to a fraction of its 1913 level, Petrograd lost something like two-thirds of its inhabitants as workers went back to the villages, and the famine of 1921 to 1922 in the Volga region killed perhaps five million people. A top-level answer keeps the map in view, because the geography explains the outcome."},{"code":"30-2 — 2 The Civil War, 1918–21: The reasons for the Bolshevik victory","h":"Why the Reds won","t":"Four kinds of reason, and the strongest answers rank them. Position: the Bolsheviks held the centre — Moscow, Petrograd, the railway hub, the arms factories and about sixty million people — and could move troops along interior lines while the Whites could not communicate with each other. Organisation: Trotsky, People's Commissar for War from March 1918, built a conscript Red Army of several million, used some fifty thousand former tsarist officers with political commissars beside them and their families as security, restored the death penalty for desertion, and spent the war in an armoured train. Supply: War Communism, however hated, nationalised industry and requisitioned grain so that the army was fed and armed first. White weakness: no agreed programme beyond removing the Bolsheviks, monarchists and liberals and Socialist Revolutionaries in the same camp, corruption, pogroms in Ukraine, and the fatal habit of returning estates to their owners in recaptured districts, which told the peasants what a White victory meant. Foreign intervention, which was meant to help the Whites, helped the Reds by letting Trotsky present the war as a defence of Russia against foreigners and their hirelings. A top-level answer does not list these but weighs them, and the usual line is that Bolshevik unity and White disunity mattered more than either army's fighting quality."},{"code":"30-2 — 3 Moves towards totalitarianism: The Red Terror and the role of the Cheka","h":"The Cheka, the Red Terror and Kronstadt","t":"The All-Russian Extraordinary Commission, the Cheka, was set up on 20 December 1917 under Felix Dzerzhinsky, answerable to Sovnarkom and to nobody else, to investigate counter-revolution and sabotage; the decree of 21 February 1918, issued as the Germans advanced, let it shoot suspects on the spot, and from then on it investigated, sentenced and executed without a court. On 30 August 1918 Fanya Kaplan shot and badly wounded Lenin at a factory in Moscow on the same day that Uritsky, head of the Petrograd Cheka, was murdered, and on 5 September Sovnarkom decreed the Red Terror: hostages from the former propertied classes, camps for class enemies, and publication of the names of those shot. The Cheka's own figures admitted thousands of executions in 1918 alone, and historians' estimates of the numbers it shot during the Civil War run from the tens of thousands into the hundreds of thousands. Terror did not end with the war. In February and March 1921 the sailors of the Kronstadt naval base, who had been among the most loyal supporters of 1917, demanded secret elections to the soviets, freedom of speech for workers and peasants, free trade unions, the release of socialist prisoners and an end to requisitioning. Tukhachevsky's troops crossed the ice and took the fortress on 18 March 1921. A top-level answer uses Kronstadt twice: as evidence of how far consent had drained away, and as the trigger for the New Economic Policy announced in the same fortnight."},{"code":"30-2 — 3 Moves towards totalitarianism: Bolshevik centralisation","h":"Centralisation: from soviets to a single party","t":"Power moved steadily from the bodies that had made the revolution to the party that had captured them. Sovnarkom, chaired by Lenin, governed by decree; the Politburo was made a permanent body at the Eighth Party Congress in March 1919, with Lenin, Trotsky, Stalin, Kamenev and Krestinsky as its first members, and real decisions moved into it; the Tenth Party Congress of March 1921 banned factions inside the party at the same moment as it conceded the New Economic Policy, so that the retreat could not be argued about. Stalin, already Commissar for Nationalities, became General Secretary in April 1922 and began to control appointments. The Union of Soviet Socialist Republics was created by treaty on 30 December 1922 out of the Russian, Ukrainian, Belorussian and Transcaucasian republics, and given a constitution in January 1924: a federal structure on paper, but one party in every republic. Lenin suffered strokes in 1922 and 1923, dictated a testament warning against Stalin, and died on 21 January 1924. A top-level answer makes the point that the machinery of one-party rule was complete before Lenin died, so the argument about Stalin is an argument about who would inherit it, not about whether it existed."},{"code":"30-2 — 4 Economic and social change, 1918–24: War Communism","h":"War Communism: an economy run like an army","t":"From the summer of 1918 the government took direct command of the economy. A decree of June 1918 nationalised large-scale industry and by 1920 almost every enterprise of any size was in state hands under the Supreme Council of the National Economy. Grain was requisitioned by armed detachments and, for the second half of 1918, by Committees of the Poor set up to find hidden stocks; private trade was banned; money lost its value in hyperinflation so that wages were paid partly in goods; rations were issued by class, with workers and soldiers fed first and former owners last; labour was conscripted and discipline militarised. It fed the Red Army and it wrecked everything else. Peasants who could not sell a surplus stopped growing one, so the sown area shrank and the harvest fell; townspeople bought from the illegal traders they called bagmen; workers left the starving cities; and drought in 1921 turned a shrinking harvest into a famine that killed perhaps five million people in the Volga region. Strikes in Petrograd in February 1921, the peasant rising in Tambov province and the Kronstadt mutiny followed. A top-level answer separates the two questions the exam asks about it — why it was introduced, which is a war answer, and why it was hated, which is an economic and social one."},{"code":"30-2 — 4 Economic and social change, 1918–24: The New Economic Policy","h":"The New Economic Policy: a retreat that worked","t":"Lenin announced the New Economic Policy at the Tenth Party Congress in March 1921, in the same days as the storming of Kronstadt. Requisitioning was replaced by a tax in kind, so that a peasant who grew more could keep and sell the surplus; private trade was legalised and the traders known as Nepmen reopened the markets; small workshops and businesses went back into private hands, while the state kept what Lenin called the commanding heights — large-scale industry, banking, transport and foreign trade. A stable currency was restored through the State Bank and the new chervonets. Many Communists hated it: they had fought a civil war against private trade and now watched it return, and some tore up their party cards, which is exactly why the ban on factions was passed at the same congress. The results were quick. Sown area and harvests recovered strongly between 1921 and 1924 and the famine passed, though heavy industry lagged behind farming, producing the scissors crisis of 1923 when industrial prices ran far ahead of what peasants got for grain. A top-level answer treats the NEP as a political decision with economic consequences: Lenin gave ground to the peasants in the countryside precisely so that he could give none inside the party."},{"code":"30-2 — 4 Economic and social change, 1918–24: Social changes","h":"Social change: women, schools and culture","t":"The decrees of December 1917 made marriage a civil act and allowed either partner to obtain a divorce, and in November 1920 Soviet Russia became the first country to legalise abortion. Equal pay was proclaimed, women entered industry and, during the Civil War, the Red Army's medical and supply services in large numbers, and the Zhenotdel, the party's women's department, was set up in 1919 under Inessa Armand and then Alexandra Kollontai, who had been the first woman in a European cabinet as Commissar for Social Welfare. The gains were real and uneven: easy divorce also left many women abandoned with children, and in the villages custom changed slowly. Education was taken over by Lunacharsky's Commissariat of Enlightenment, which decreed a free, co-educational school for all children in 1918, opened universities to workers through the workers' faculties from 1919, and launched a mass literacy campaign by decree in December 1919 requiring the illiterate between eight and fifty to learn to read; the census of 1926 found more than half the population aged nine to forty-nine literate, against roughly two in five before the war. In culture, agitprop trains and posters carried the message to the front, the Proletkult movement tried to build a specifically proletarian art until Lenin brought it under state control in 1920, and the Orthodox Church was separated from the state in early 1918 and stripped of its land, with its valuables seized during the famine of 1922. A top-level answer uses these policies as evidence in the argument about consent: they are the part of Bolshevik rule that recruited support rather than compelling it."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1: two inferences from Source A","setup":"Source A in this room is a report sent to the provincial food commissar by the commander of a food requisitioning detachment working in a district of the middle Volga, August 1920. It states that the detachment has worked through eleven villages; that in the first two the quota was handed over without argument, but that word travels ahead of the detachment and in the rest the granaries were found swept clean and the pits under the floors already emptied; that the peasants repeat the same sentence in every village, that they sowed less in the spring because there was no purpose in sowing more; that twice the detachment has been met by the whole village standing in the road and the commander has not had enough rifles to do anything but withdraw and return with the detachment from the next district; that the committee of the poor here was disbanded long ago and the village assembly does as it likes; and that less than half the amount set has been collected, from people who will be short before the spring. The question is: 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about the methods the Bolshevik government used to control the countryside in 1920. Complete the table below to explain your answer.' (4 marks)","steps":["Mark every phrase in the report that names an instrument of government rather than a mood: the amount set for the detachment, the provincial food commissar it is written to, the rifles, the committee of the poor. Those phrases, and not the complaints around them, are where the answer is.","Take the first inference from the chain of command: a detachment given a quota from above, sent out armed, and made to report its shortfall to a provincial food commissar shows that the countryside was being governed by order and by force from outside the village rather than through it.","Take the second from the committee of the poor that 'was disbanded long ago' while 'the village assembly does as it likes': the government had first tried to hold the village by splitting it from inside, and by 1920 that method had been given up.","Check the shape before writing: each box needs a claim the report never puts into words, followed by the exact words from the report that force it."],"cues":["Which words in the report name a method rather than a feeling?","Who set the amount, and to whom is the commander answerable?","What was a committee of the poor for, and what does its disbanding tell you?","What goes in the first box and what goes in the second?"]},{"title":"Question 3(a): judging Sources B and C for the enquiry actually named","setup":"Source B in this room is a circular sent to district branches by the chairman of a provincial Cheka in central Russia, September 1918. It says that the attempt on Lenin's life at the end of August and the murder of Uritsky in Petrograd have shown what mercy has cost; that the decree of the Council of People's Commissars is now in force and is to be applied without hesitation; that each district office is to draw up at once a register of former officers, landowners, priests, factory owners and members of the Kadet and Socialist Revolutionary parties in its area and hold a number of them as hostages against any further attack; that sentences are to be carried out by the Commission itself and reported afterwards; that the class to which a man belongs is evidence of what he would do, so there is no need to wait for an act; and that the names of those shot are to be published locally so that the district may learn what resistance costs. Source C in this room is a letter from a peasant conscript serving in the Red Army in southern Russia to his brother in their village, November 1919. He asks why his brother thinks he should run home; he reports that in the summer the Volunteers came through the district to the north and that behind them came the old owner with a list, the land divided in 1918 was taken back and the men who had divided it were flogged in front of the church; that the commissar says this is what waits for them if Denikin reaches Moscow, and that for once he believes him; that he hates the detachments that come for the grain as much as his brother does and will say so the day the war ends; but that between a government that takes his grain and a general who takes his land he will keep the one that leaves him the field. The question is: 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the effects of the Civil War on people living in Russia in the years 1918 to 1921? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context.' (8 marks)","steps":["Copy the enquiry to the top of your plan and let it select for you: this is about what the war did to people, so an order deciding who is registered, held and shot and a conscript's account of what happened to his neighbours are both in, and anything that is only about who was winning is out.","Read Source B for the effect it produces rather than the policy it announces: whole categories of people, former officers, landowners, priests, factory owners and members of the Kadet and Socialist Revolutionary parties, became liable to be listed and held as hostages for what they had been before the war, and the names of those shot were printed for their neighbours to read.","Weigh Source B's nature, origin and purpose together: an internal circular from a provincial Cheka chairman to his own district offices is strong evidence of what was being done to such people in one province in the autumn of 1918, though it records intentions rather than numbers and no district's reply is attached to it.","Read Source C for what the war did in the villages: a peasant of military age serving hundreds of miles from home, the land divided in 1918 taken back where the Volunteers had passed, men flogged in front of the church, and detachments arriving for the grain in his own district.","Weigh Source C's provenance: a private letter from a conscript to his brother in November 1919, with nothing to gain by flattering anybody, so it is unusually candid evidence of how the war reached one family, although it is one man, one district and one moment of the war.","Bring your own knowledge in on the sources, not beside them: the circular follows the Red Terror decreed by Sovnarkom on 5 September 1918, and the district north of Source C's writer is the country Denikin's Volunteer Army held as it advanced in the autumn of 1919, while conscription, requisitioning and the collapse of trade were doing their work in the same months.","Finish with a judgement for this enquiry: between them the sources reach both ends of society, the classed enemy in the provincial town and the conscripted peasant in the village, which is more than either shows alone; what neither reaches is the hunger that followed, or the factory workers whose strikes in Petrograd came days before Kronstadt rose."],"cues":["What exactly is the enquiry into?","Who became liable to be held, and for what?","Does an internal circular give you numbers or intentions?","What had the war done to this soldier's village and to the district north of it?","What makes a letter to a brother candid?","Which decree and which advance stand behind these two texts?","Which effects of the war do both sources miss?"]},{"title":"Question 3(b): finding the difference of view, not the difference of detail","setup":"The room's printed Interpretations 1 and 2 are the pair the question bank uses for 3(b), so this example works the same question form on a second pair, printed here for this example only and on a different focus. Interpretation 3: from a study of the Soviet countryside, by a historian writing in the 1980s; written for this worked example, not a real book, and not attributed to any real historian. — The New Economic Policy repaired what War Communism had broken. Once a tax in kind replaced requisitioning the peasant had a reason to sow again, markets and small workshops reopened, and within four years the harvest was approaching its pre-war size. Whatever it cost the party's beliefs, the retreat fed the country. Interpretation 4: from a general history of the Communist Party, by a historian writing in the 2000s; written for this worked example, not a real book, and not attributed to any real historian. — The recovery is easily overstated. The policy brought the private trader back into the towns, and in 1923 it let industrial prices run far ahead of the prices the villages were paid, so that town and countryside were pulled apart. Communists who had fought a civil war to abolish such things were left doubting their own government. The question is: 'Study Interpretations 3 and 4. They give different views on the effects of the New Economic Policy on Russia in the years 1921 to 1924. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations.' (4 marks)","steps":["Reduce each text to one sentence beginning with the same four words, 'The New Economic Policy', and the difference will show itself.","Notice that the two do not disagree about what happened, since both accept that requisitioning ended, that trading came back and that output rose, so the difference has to be about what those effects added up to.","State the difference in one sentence: Interpretation 3 says the effect was recovery, a country sowing and eating again, while Interpretation 4 says the effect was division, between town and village and between the party and its own policy.","Support each half from its own text, taking the sentence that the retreat fed the country for the first and the communists left doubting their own government for the second."],"cues":["How would each historian finish that sentence?","Do the two disagree about any fact?","Is the difference about what happened, or about what it was worth?","Which phrase from each text proves your half of the sentence?"]},{"title":"Question 2: planning the 12-mark causation answer so it goes beyond the bullets","setup":"Question 2 prints a choice of 2(a) and 2(b), both 'Explain why...' at 12 marks on the same grid, and you answer one of them. Say the one you take is: 'Explain why power in Bolshevik Russia came to be concentrated in a few hands by 1924. You may use the following in your answer: bullet one, the Politburo; bullet two, the ban on factions of March 1921. You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks)","steps":["Decide the line of argument before choosing paragraphs: the habits of the Civil War hardened into the permanent shape of the state, so put that claim in the opening sentence and make every paragraph serve it.","Make the Politburo paragraph about a machine rather than a committee: the Eighth Party Congress of March 1919 set up the Politburo, the Orgburo and the Secretariat, and a body of five men taking the decisions between congresses meant that the Council of People's Commissars and the soviets were increasingly ratifying choices already made inside the party.","Turn the second bullet into a cause rather than a date: the Tenth Party Congress of March 1921 conceded the tax in kind and in the same week forbade organised factions inside the party, so that the last place where an argument could legitimately be organised was closed at the very moment the policy changed.","Build the paragraph of your own, which is what stops the answer being a recital of the two bullets: the other parties had already gone, the Left Socialist Revolutionaries driven out after their rising of July 1918, the opposition press shut, and the surviving Socialist Revolutionary leaders put on trial in Moscow in 1922, so by then there was nowhere outside the party for power to be contested either.","Add the machinery as a second point of your own if there is time: Stalin became General Secretary on 3 April 1922 with the appointments in his hands, Lenin was disabled by the stroke of May 1922, and the USSR created in December 1922 put one party in every republic.","Close by ranking rather than summarising: the war made concentration look necessary and the March 1921 ban made it permanent, but the decisive step was the earlier one, the removal of every rival body, because after that concentration inside the party was the only contest left."],"cues":["What is your one-sentence answer to the question?","Who was actually taking the decisions after March 1919?","What was conceded and what was closed in the same week of March 1921?","Where had the other parties gone by 1922?","What did the General Secretary control?","How do you finish without simply listing the reasons again?"]},{"title":"Question 3(c): explaining why two historians give different views","setup":"The room's printed Interpretations 1 and 2 are the pair the question bank uses for 3(c), so this example works the same question form on a second pair, printed here for this example only and on a different focus. Interpretation 5: from a study of the Baltic Fleet, by a historian writing in the 1960s; written for this worked example, not a real book, and not attributed to any real historian. — The Kronstadt men rose as revolutionaries, not as enemies of the revolution. Their resolution of 28 February 1921 asked for soviets re-elected by secret ballot and for freedom of speech for workers, peasants and the left socialist parties. They wanted the promises of 1917 kept, and said so. Interpretation 6: from a history of the crisis of 1921, by a historian writing in the 2010s; written for this worked example, not a real book, and not attributed to any real historian. — Programmes came second to bread. The city's ration had been cut by a third in January 1921 and Petrograd was already on strike when the fortress moved; the sailors' letters from home described villages stripped by the detachments. Kronstadt was the naval end of a general hunger. The question is: 'Suggest one reason why Interpretations 5 and 6 give different views about the reasons for the Kronstadt mutiny of March 1921. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer.' (4 marks)","steps":["State the difference in one line before you explain it, because a difference you have not named cannot be accounted for: Interpretation 5 makes the rising a demand that the promises of 1917 be kept, Interpretation 6 makes it the naval end of a hunger.","Choose one reason and commit to it rather than listing three: these two historians have gone to different kinds of evidence, one to what the sailors themselves wrote and voted for, the other to rations, strikes and letters, and different evidence answers different questions.","Show the reason at work in Interpretation 5: a historian reading the crews' own resolution of 28 February 1921 finds demands, secret ballots and free speech for the left socialist parties, so the rising appears as a political programme.","Show it at work in Interpretation 6: a historian reading supply figures and private letters finds a bread ration cut by a third in January 1921 and a city already striking in late February, so the same rising appears as the last stage of a shortage.","Use this room's printed sources as the kinds of evidence the difference turns on: Source C is exactly the sort of letter Interpretation 6 leans on, a conscript who hates the detachments that come for the grain, while Source B is the sort of official paper that shows what a government meant to do rather than what its people wanted.","Close on the reason and not on a verdict: say plainly that they differ because each has weighed a different kind of evidence about the same weeks, and support that from both texts, because this question asks for an explained reason, not a judgement between the two."],"cues":["What is the difference between the two views, in one sentence?","Which single reason for the difference will you argue?","What would the crews' own resolution make the rising look like?","What would the ration and the strikes make it look like?","Which of this room's printed sources is the kind of evidence each historian would reach for?","Does a 3(c) answer have to decide which view is right?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which party won the elections to the Constituent Assembly in November 1917, and what happened to the Assembly?","a":"The Socialist Revolutionaries, with roughly 370 seats against about 175 for the Bolsheviks. The Assembly met in Petrograd on 5 January 1918 (Old Style) and was closed by its Bolshevik guards after one day's sitting."},{"q":"Why did Lenin insist on signing the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk in March 1918 when the terms were so harsh?","a":"Because the army would not fight: the Germans advanced unopposed in February 1918 after Trotsky's neither war nor peace, and Lenin argued that territory could be exchanged for the time needed to secure the revolution at home. He threatened to resign to carry the Central Committee."},{"q":"Give one reason why a one-sided document such as the Cheka circular in Source B is still useful for an enquiry into how the Bolsheviks kept control.","a":"Because it is an internal instruction rather than propaganda: its purpose was to make district offices act, so it is strong evidence of what the regime actually intended, even though it cannot show how far the orders were obeyed."},{"q":"What did the sailors of Kronstadt demand in 1921, and what was the outcome?","a":"Secret elections to the soviets, freedom of speech for workers and peasants, free trade unions, the release of socialist prisoners and an end to grain requisitioning. Tukhachevsky's troops crossed the ice and took the fortress on 18 March 1921; days earlier the Tenth Party Congress had begun replacing requisitioning with the tax in kind."},{"q":"Name one change in the lives of Russian women brought about by Bolshevik policy before 1924.","a":"Civil marriage with divorce at the request of either partner from December 1917; the legalisation of abortion in November 1920; or the Zhenotdel, the party's women's department, set up in 1919 under Inessa Armand and then Alexandra Kollontai."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 3 Section A (16 marks): Question 1 prints Source A and asks 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about...', answered in a table of two boxes each (4 marks); Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b), of which you answer one: both are 'Explain why...' with two stimulus points and 'You must also use information of your own.', marked on the same grid (12 marks).","Paper 3 Section B (36 marks), all inside Question 3: (a) 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into...?' (8); (b) 'What is the main difference between these views?' (4); (c) 'Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about...' (4); (d) 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about...?' (16, plus up to 4 marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology).","The stimulus-point caps printed on the 12-mark 'Explain why...': 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'. There is no cap at Level 2, so an answer that stays inside the two stimulus points can still reach the top of Level 2, but the top of Level 3 and Level 4 need three or more aspects of content. Question 3(d) prints no stimulus points, so no cap applies to it.","Timing on Paper 3, which lasts 1 hour 30 minutes for 52 marks: roughly a minute a mark plus thinking time, so about 4 minutes on Question 1, 15 on Question 2, 10 on 3(a), 5 each on 3(b) and 3(c), and about 30 minutes on 3(d) including a final check for the SPaG marks.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose, and usefulness is judged only for the enquiry the question names. Contextual knowledge scores nothing unless it is used on the sources or on the interpretations.","The four dates that anchor this key topic: November 1917, the Decrees on Peace and Land; 3 March 1918, the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk; 18 March 1921, the fall of Kronstadt and the week the New Economic Policy was announced; 21 January 1924, the death of Lenin."],"questions":[{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about the effects of grain requisitioning on the countryside. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for each valid inference up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail taken from Source A. Credit falling production, organised concealment, or hunger caused in the villages themselves.","sol":"(i) I can infer that requisitioning was reducing the amount of food the countryside produced, rather than simply moving it to the towns. The detachment commander reports that the peasants tell him the same sentence in every village, that they sowed less in the spring because there was no purpose in sowing more. (ii) I can infer that whole villages had become organised opponents of the policy. He writes that word travels ahead of the detachment, that the granaries are found swept clean and the pits under the floors already emptied, and that twice his men were met by the whole village standing in the road.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["An inference is something the report makes certain without ever stating it; ask what must be true of the villages for this officer to be writing at all.","Read the report twice, once for what it says about the grain and once for what it says about the people who grew it.","Give each answer in two halves: the claim that the source never puts into words, then the exact words that force it."],"codes":["30-2 — 4 Economic and social change, 1918–24: War Communism","30-2 — 2 The Civil War, 1918–21: The reasons for, key events and effects of the Civil War"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about the difficulties the Bolshevik government faced in collecting grain in 1920. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for each valid inference up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail from Source A. Credit a shortage of force, the loss of allies inside the village, or quotas that were not being met.","sol":"(i) I can infer that the government did not have enough armed men to enforce its own policy. The commander admits that he had not enough rifles to do anything but withdraw when a village blocked the road, and that he had to come back with the detachment from the next district. (ii) I can infer that the Bolsheviks had lost the allies inside the villages on whom the policy had depended. He reports that the committee of the poor in that district was disbanded long ago and that the village assembly now does as it likes, and that less than half the amount set for him has been collected.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The difficulties are the writer's own difficulties; look for the sentences in which he is explaining a failure to his superior.","Ask what a requisitioning detachment needed in order to work at all, and then check which of those things this one actually had.","Set out a claim the report implies but does not state, then the phrase that makes it certain, for each of your two answers."],"codes":["30-2 — 4 Economic and social change, 1918–24: War Communism","30-2 — 2 The Civil War, 1918–21: The reasons for, key events and effects of the Civil War"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. They give different views on the reasons why the Bolsheviks remained in power between 1917 and 1924. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4) needs one key difference of view supported from both texts: Interpretation 1 explains survival by coercion, Interpretation 2 by concession and popular calculation. A difference of detail or wording only stays in Level 1.","sol":"The main difference is about what kept the Bolsheviks in power: Interpretation 1 argues that they survived by compelling obedience, while Interpretation 2 argues that they survived by offering enough people something they wanted. Interpretation 1 makes the point through a list of instruments of force, the Cheka created within weeks, the Assembly closed at gunpoint and the sailors of Kronstadt 'shot down on the ice'. Interpretation 2 makes the opposite point by tracing a series of bargains, peace and land in 1917 and the New Economic Policy in 1921, and concludes that 'the regime lasted because it bargained'. The two agree on the events and disagree about which of them did the work.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Reduce each interpretation to one sentence beginning 'The Bolsheviks stayed in power because...' and put the two sentences side by side.","Check that your difference is a difference of view: both writers name the same events, so look for what each says caused the survival.","State the difference in one sentence first, then support each half with words taken from its own interpretation."],"codes":["30-2 — 3 Moves towards totalitarianism: The Red Terror and the role of the Cheka","30-2 — 2 The Civil War, 1918–21: The reasons for the Bolshevik victory"]},{"q":"Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about the reasons why the Bolsheviks remained in power between 1917 and 1924. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4) needs one explained reason, effectively substantiated: for example that the two historians study different things and therefore read different bodies of evidence, the state's own orders against what people in the villages said and did.","sol":"One reason is that the two historians read different kinds of evidence because they study different things. Interpretation 1 is a study of the Bolshevik state itself, so its evidence is the record the state left of its own decisions; a document like Source B, an internal Cheka circular ordering registers of hostages, is exactly the material such a study rests on, and it makes coercion look like the whole story. Interpretation 2 is a general history of the country, so it takes in evidence like Source C, in which a peasant conscript stays with the Reds because the Whites brought the old landowner back, and evidence of that kind shows choices being made rather than obedience being enforced. Different evidence produces different weightings.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Historians can differ legitimately without either being wrong; begin by asking what each of these two set out to write a book about.","Ask which archives or papers each writer would have spent most time in, and what that material would put in front of them every day.","Name one reason, then show it working in both texts, using a source to illustrate the kind of evidence involved."],"codes":["30-2 — 3 Moves towards totalitarianism: The Red Terror and the role of the Cheka","30-2 — 2 The Civil War, 1918–21: The reasons for the Bolshevik victory"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into attitudes towards Bolshevik rule in Russia in the years 1918 to 1921? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): utility judged for this enquiry with developed reasoning on how nature, origin and purpose affect the content, and context used to interpret. Credit Source B on the regime's assumption that whole classes were against it and on what publishing the names of those shot was meant to teach a district, and Source C on a peasant soldier weighing one side against the other.","sol":"Source B is useful for what the government assumed about attitudes towards it. A circular ordering district offices to register former officers, landowners, priests and members of the Kadet and Socialist Revolutionary parties, and to hold them as hostages, shows a regime that treated the class a man belonged to as evidence of what he would do; the order to publish locally the names of those shot shows what it wanted a district to feel, which is fear rather than agreement. Its provenance strengthens that: an internal instruction written by a chairman who needs his districts to obey is better evidence of official thinking than any published appeal, and it follows the Red Terror decree issued by Sovnarkom in September 1918. Its limit is that it shows what one official ordered, not what anybody in the province thought. Source C is useful for the attitude itself, from below. A conscript who says he hates the detachments that come for the grain but stays because the Volunteers brought the old owner back with a list and flogged the men who had divided the land shows support that is calculated rather than given, and a private letter to a brother in November 1919 has no reason to flatter anybody. It is one man in one district, but it is the reasoning that kept an unwilling army in the field. Together they catch the same attitude from opposite ends, the state's fear of its own people and a peasant's grudging preference for it.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Usefulness is always usefulness for something: write the enquiry at the top of your plan before you look at either source.","Take each source as content, then provenance, then the context that confirms or qualifies it, and compare them only at the end.","Open on what Source B lets you infer about how the government expected to be regarded, and make the first sentence a judgement rather than a description."],"codes":["30-2 — 2 The Civil War, 1918–21: The reasons for the Bolshevik victory","30-2 — 2 The Civil War, 1918–21: The reasons for, key events and effects of the Civil War"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the ways the Bolsheviks dealt with opposition in the years 1918 to 1921? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): criteria-based judgements with developed reasoning on how provenance affects content. Credit Source B on terror by category and hostage-taking, Source C on the commissar and the requisitioning detachments; qualify both, since neither shows the result.","sol":"Source B is directly useful, because it is the machinery of repression describing itself. It orders a register of people to be drawn up by category, former officers, landowners, priests, factory owners and members of the Kadet and Socialist Revolutionary parties, and it states the principle behind the Red Terror plainly, that the class a man belongs to is evidence of what he would do, so there is no need to wait for an act. That it is an internal circular rather than a public announcement makes it stronger evidence of intention than any speech would be, and the context confirms it: Sovnarkom decreed the Red Terror in September 1918, days after Fanya Kaplan shot Lenin and Uritsky was killed in Petrograd, and the Cheka, set up in December 1917 to investigate counter-revolution and sabotage, had been given the power to shoot suspects on the spot by the decree of 21 February 1918, so the circular is applying powers that had been built up in stages rather than held from the start. Source C is useful in a different and more limited way. Written by a peasant conscript, it shows the everyday face of Bolshevik control, a commissar attached to the unit and detachments coming for the grain, and it shows opposition being managed rather than crushed, since the writer keeps his hatred of requisitioning to himself until the war ends. Neither source shows how many were arrested or shot, so for the scale of repression they must be used with figures from elsewhere; for its methods and its logic they are strong.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["The enquiry is into methods, so ask of each source what it shows the government actually doing to the people who might oppose it.","A source written to be obeyed is different evidence from a source written to be read by a brother; make that difference do work.","Begin with what Source B reveals about how targets were chosen, and open with a judgement rather than a summary."],"codes":["30-2 — 3 Moves towards totalitarianism: The Red Terror and the role of the Cheka","30-2 — 2 The Civil War, 1918–21: The reasons for, key events and effects of the Civil War"]},{"q":"Explain why the Bolsheviks were able to establish their control of Russia in the months after October 1917. You may use the following in your answer: • the Decree on Land • the Cheka. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise knowledge - the decrees of November and December 1917, the Cheka of 20 December 1917, the press decree and the ban on the Kadets, the weakness and division of the opposition. Stimulus cap: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"The Bolsheviks established control in the first months partly because their earliest measures cost them nothing and bought a great deal. The Decree on Land of November 1917 abolished private ownership of land and handed the estates to the peasants, but in most provinces the peasants had already taken them, so the decree simply made the government the author of something that had happened without it; the Decree on Peace did the same for an army that was already walking home. Workers' control of the factories, the eight-hour day and the abolition of ranks worked in the same way. Support bought this cheaply was shallow, but it meant that in the winter of 1917 to 1918 almost nobody in the villages or the barracks had a reason to fight for anyone else. Secondly, coercion was assembled at the same speed. The decree on the press closed opposition papers within days; the Kadets were banned as enemies of the people before the year was out; and on 20 December 1917 the Cheka was created under Dzerzhinsky, answerable only to Sovnarkom, to investigate counter-revolution and sabotage; from the decree of 21 February 1918 it could shoot suspects on the spot, and the Red Terror decree of 5 September 1918 made hostages and mass shooting policy. The point is the combination: concessions removed the reasons for resistance while the Cheka removed the people most likely to organise it. Thirdly, and beyond the bullets, the opposition was in no state to use its numbers. The old army had dissolved, the Socialist Revolutionaries split when the Left Socialist Revolutionaries joined Sovnarkom in December 1917, and when the Constituent Assembly was closed after a single sitting in January 1918 the demonstrations in its defence were fired on and nothing followed. Control in these months therefore rested less on Bolshevik strength than on the absence of any organised alternative, with the decrees keeping it absent and the Cheka making sure of it.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Control is not the same as popularity: ask what the new government needed to prevent, rather than what it needed to achieve.","Plan reasons of two different kinds, what the government gave and what it took away, and then find a third that lies with its opponents.","Open on what the first decrees actually changed in the villages, and finish the paragraph by saying what they bought politically."],"codes":["30-2 — 1 Early consolidation of power, 1917–18: The early Bolshevik decrees","30-2 — 1 Early consolidation of power, 1917–18: The removal of opposition","30-2 — 3 Moves towards totalitarianism: The Red Terror and the role of the Cheka"]},{"q":"Explain why the Bolsheviks closed the Constituent Assembly in January 1918. You may use the following in your answer: • the results of the elections of November 1917 • Lenin's view of the soviets. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): sustained causal analysis - the election result, soviet power as a rival source of legitimacy, and the means and safety of closure. Stimulus cap: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"The first reason is arithmetic. The elections of November 1917 were the freest Russia had held, and the Bolsheviks lost them: the Socialist Revolutionaries took roughly 370 of the seats and the Bolsheviks about 175, around a quarter of the votes cast, because the countryside voted for the peasants' party. An Assembly with that majority could have dismissed Sovnarkom and taken the government away from the party that had just seized it, so allowing it to sit and legislate meant handing power back within weeks of taking it. The second reason is that Lenin had an argument ready for not doing so. He held that a parliament elected on lists was a lower form of democracy than the soviets, which represented workers, soldiers and peasants directly, and he added that the Socialist Revolutionary lists had been drawn up before the Left Socialist Revolutionaries broke away, so the votes cast for them did not show what the countryside now thought. When the Assembly met in the Tauride Palace on 5 January 1918 the Bolsheviks put a declaration of soviet power before it, were outvoted, and walked out; the guards closed the building the next morning. The third reason, and the one that explains the timing, is that by January it was safe. The Cheka had existed since December 1917, the opposition press had been closed, the Kadet leaders were in prison or in hiding, and the loyal Red Guard and Latvian troops around the palace made resistance pointless; demonstrations in the Assembly's support were fired on and the country did not rise. The Bolsheviks closed the Assembly because it threatened them, because they had a justification for doing it, and above all because nobody was in a position to stop them.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Start from what the Assembly could have done to the government, not from what it stood for.","Separate the motive from the opportunity, and build a paragraph on each before you decide which mattered more.","Open on the arithmetic of the election result and what it would have meant for Sovnarkom."],"codes":["30-2 — 1 Early consolidation of power, 1917–18: The removal of opposition","30-2 — 1 Early consolidation of power, 1917–18: The early Bolshevik decrees"]},{"q":"Explain why the Bolsheviks signed the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk in March 1918. You may use the following in your answer: • the condition of the Russian army • the German advance of February 1918. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): analysis directed at causation - the collapse of the army, the unopposed advance of 18 February 1918, Lenin's breathing-space argument and the promise of peace made in 1917. Stimulus cap: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"The first reason is that there was no army left to bargain with. Three years of defeat, the committees created by Soviet Order Number One and the land being divided at home had emptied the trenches; men who wanted to be in their villages when the fields were shared out did not stay to defend a line for a government that had told them to make peace. Any treaty therefore had to be signed from a position of complete military weakness. The second reason is that delay was making the terms worse, not better. Trotsky tried to escape the choice in February 1918 with the formula of neither war nor peace, refusing to sign but declaring the war over; the Germans replied on 18 February by resuming their advance, and moved forward against almost no resistance towards Petrograd. Every day of that advance added territory to the German demands, and it proved Lenin's case that the alternative to signing was not resistance but a larger defeat. The third reason, and the decisive one, was Lenin's political calculation, which goes beyond either bullet. He argued that the revolution mattered more than the territory, that the treaty was a breathing space in which the government could secure Russia itself, and that a war-weary Europe would soon make the terms irrelevant; he threatened to resign to carry the Central Committee on 23 February against Bukharin's Left Communists, who wanted a revolutionary war. Signing on 3 March cost Poland, Finland, the Baltic provinces and Ukraine, on the usual estimates about a third of the old empire's population and farmland, but it also kept the promise of peace on which the Bolsheviks had taken power. The treaty was annulled in November 1918 when Germany was defeated, which is the best evidence that Lenin's reasoning, however brutal, was correct.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["A treaty is a choice between options: list what the alternatives to signing actually were in February 1918.","Two of your reasons will be about weakness; make sure one is about a decision somebody took and had to argue for.","Begin with the state of the front line and what it removed from the government's hands."],"codes":["30-2 — 1 Early consolidation of power, 1917–18: The Treaty of Brest-Litovsk"]},{"q":"Explain why the Bolsheviks won the Civil War of 1918 to 1921. You may use the following in your answer: • Trotsky • foreign intervention. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise detail - the conscript Red Army and its discipline, intervention turned to Bolshevik advantage, White disunity and land policy, the central position. Stimulus cap: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"The Bolsheviks won because they were one side and their enemies were several. The first reason is the army Trotsky built as People's Commissar for War from March 1918. He replaced volunteers with conscription, brought in some fifty thousand former tsarist officers for their professional skill and placed political commissars beside them, restored the death penalty for desertion and spent the war moving between fronts in an armoured train. This turned a collection of Red Guard detachments into a force of several million that could be ordered about, which is precisely what the Whites never possessed. The second reason is that foreign intervention worked against the side it was meant to help. British, French, American and Japanese troops landed at Murmansk, Archangel, the Black Sea and Vladivostok, but the war-weary governments at home would not commit enough to be decisive and most were gone by 1920; what the landings did achieve was to let the Bolsheviks present themselves as the defenders of Russia against foreigners and the Whites as their hirelings. The third reason lies with the Whites themselves and goes beyond the bullets. Kolchak at Omsk, Denikin in the south and Yudenich in the Baltic were separated by thousands of kilometres and never fought their offensives under one command; Denikin reached Orel in October 1919 and Yudenich came within sight of Petrograd in the same month, but the two advances were never planned together, and Kolchak, whom both had recognised as Supreme Ruler earlier that year, had already been driven back east before either of them began. They agreed on nothing except removing the Bolsheviks, and in the districts they took they let landlords reclaim their estates, which told every peasant what a White victory would cost him. Behind all three runs the geography: the Reds held Moscow, Petrograd, the railway hub and the arms factories, and could shift reserves in days. Trotsky's army made victory possible, but it was White disunity that made it certain.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Both sides had peasant armies and foreign equipment; ask what the Reds had that the Whites could not copy.","Turn the second bullet round and ask whom the landings actually helped once the fighting was over.","Open on what Trotsky created in 1918 and how it was held together, not on what kind of man he was."],"codes":["30-2 — 2 The Civil War, 1918–21: The reasons for the Bolshevik victory","30-2 — 2 The Civil War, 1918–21: The reasons for, key events and effects of the Civil War"]},{"q":"Explain why War Communism was unpopular in Russia between 1918 and 1921. You may use the following in your answer: • grain requisitioning • the ban on private trade. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): analysis directed at causation - requisitioning and falling production, the ban on trade with worthless money and class rations, militarised labour, and the famine and risings that followed. Stimulus cap: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"War Communism was hated first because of what it did to the peasant. Armed detachments took the grain the government decided was surplus and gave nothing usable in return, since the rouble was destroyed by inflation, and in the second half of 1918 Committees of the Poor were set up to point out where the grain was hidden, which turned neighbours against each other inside the village. A peasant who cannot sell a surplus stops growing one, so the sown area shrank and the harvest fell, and drought in 1921 turned a shrinking harvest into a famine in the Volga region that killed perhaps five million people. The second reason is that the ban on private trade closed the only legal way out. With money worthless and rations issued by class, so that workers and soldiers ate first and the former propertied classes last, townspeople had to buy from the illegal traders they called bagmen and then risk having their sacks confiscated on the way home. A policy that makes the ordinary way of getting food a crime makes enemies of everybody who eats. The third reason, beyond the bullets, is that the towns were disciplined as harshly as the villages were stripped. Factories were nationalised and run from the centre, labour was conscripted, absence was punished and workers were tied to their jobs, yet wages could not be spent; the result was that people simply left, and Petrograd lost something like two-thirds of its inhabitants between 1917 and 1920. The unpopularity was therefore not a grievance but a verdict, delivered in the strikes in Petrograd in February 1921, the peasant rising in Tambov province and finally at Kronstadt, and it is the reason the policy was abandoned within weeks of those events.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Unpopular with whom: separate the village from the town before you write, because the policy hurt them differently.","Follow the consequences rather than the features, asking what each measure made it impossible for an ordinary person to do.","Begin with what a requisitioning detachment left behind it in a village, and what that did to next year's sowing."],"codes":["30-2 — 4 Economic and social change, 1918–24: War Communism","30-2 — 2 The Civil War, 1918–21: The reasons for, key events and effects of the Civil War"]},{"q":"Explain why Lenin introduced the New Economic Policy in 1921. You may use the following in your answer: • the Kronstadt mutiny • grain requisitioning. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): sustained causal analysis - economic collapse and famine, the risings of 1921, the end of hope in world revolution, and the political management of the retreat. Stimulus cap: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"The first reason is that the economy had stopped working. Requisitioning had removed the peasant's reason to grow more than he could eat, so the sown area shrank year by year; industry was producing a fraction of its 1913 output, the cities had emptied as workers went back to the villages, and by the spring of 1921 famine was spreading in the Volga provinces. A government that could not feed the towns it ruled could not go on ruling them in the same way. The second reason is that the country said so, in the only way left to it. Strikes broke out in Petrograd in February 1921, peasants were already in open revolt in Tambov province, and at the beginning of March the sailors of Kronstadt, who had been among the most loyal supporters of the revolution in 1917, demanded secret elections to the soviets, free trade unions, the release of socialist prisoners and an end to requisitioning. Tukhachevsky's troops took the fortress across the ice on 18 March 1921, but Lenin drew the obvious conclusion: the regime could still win a battle against its own supporters and could not afford to keep fighting one. The third reason, which goes beyond the bullets, is that Russia now had to survive alone. The hope that revolution in Germany or Poland would rescue the Soviet economy had gone by the end of 1920, when the Red Army was driven back from Warsaw, so the peasant surplus had to be bought rather than waited for. Hence the tax in kind announced at the Tenth Party Congress in March 1921, with private trade legalised and small workshops returned to their owners while the state kept the commanding heights. That the same congress banned factions inside the party shows the last part of Lenin's reasoning: he could only afford to retreat economically if no retreat was permitted politically.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["A change of policy has a trigger and a cause; make sure your answer has both and does not confuse them.","Look for a reason outside Russia as well as inside it, since 1920 changed what the Bolsheviks could expect from Europe.","Open on what requisitioning had done to the amount of grain actually being grown by 1921."],"codes":["30-2 — 4 Economic and social change, 1918–24: The New Economic Policy","30-2 — 4 Economic and social change, 1918–24: War Communism","30-2 — 3 Moves towards totalitarianism: The Red Terror and the role of the Cheka"]},{"q":"Explain why the lives of many Russian women changed in the years 1917 to 1924. You may use the following in your answer: • the decrees on marriage and divorce of December 1917 • the Civil War. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise detail - the legal changes of 1917 to 1920, the labour shortage of the Civil War, the Zhenotdel and the literacy campaign, with the limits of change in the villages. Stimulus cap: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"The first reason is that the law was rewritten very quickly. The decrees of December 1917 made marriage a civil act registered by the state rather than the Church and allowed either partner to obtain a divorce on request; equal pay was proclaimed, the legal penalties attached to illegitimacy were removed, and in November 1920 Soviet Russia became the first country in the world to legalise abortion. These measures changed the terms on which a woman could leave a marriage or keep a job, and they did so within three years. The second reason is the Civil War, which changed what women actually did. Millions of men were conscripted into the Red Army between 1918 and 1920, so women took their places in the factories, on the railways and in the fields, and served in large numbers in the army's own medical and supply services; under War Communism, where rations were issued by category, holding a job also meant holding a claim on food. Work done out of necessity nonetheless established that the work could be done. The third reason, beyond the bullets, is that the party set out to organise and educate women rather than merely to legislate for them. The Zhenotdel, the women's department set up in 1919 under Inessa Armand and then Alexandra Kollontai, who had already been the first woman in a European cabinet, sent organisers into factories and villages; the literacy campaign decreed in December 1919 required the illiterate between eight and fifty to learn to read, and the new schools of 1918 were free and co-educational. The change was real but uneven, and a top answer says so: in the villages custom moved slowly, and divorce on demand often left a woman with the children and without support, so the law ran ahead of life.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Distinguish what the law said from what daily life was like, and build the answer on the gap between them.","Ask what a war that conscripts millions of men does to the jobs those men leave behind.","Open on what the December decrees changed about marriage, and say at once what that made possible."],"codes":["30-2 — 4 Economic and social change, 1918–24: Social changes"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the reasons why the Bolsheviks remained in power between 1917 and 1924? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views evaluated, how each conveys its view analysed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit the Decrees on Peace and Land, Brest-Litovsk and the tax in kind; against, the Cheka, the Constituent Assembly, the Red Terror, Kronstadt and the party machinery.","sol":"Interpretation 2 argues that the Bolsheviks survived because at each crisis they offered something their rivals would not, and it concludes that the regime lasted because it bargained. Interpretation 1 gives the opposite weighting, that they lasted because they alone were willing to use force. The test to apply is which of the two is actually doing the explanatory work at each of the three crises of the period: the winter of 1917 to 1918, the Civil War, and 1921. Interpretation 2 is strongest at the first. The Decree on Land of November 1917 gave the peasants estates that most of them had already seized, and the Decree on Peace promised the one thing no other party would promise; at Brest-Litovsk in March 1918 Lenin gave up Poland, Finland, the Baltic provinces and Ukraine, roughly a third of the old empire's population and farmland, rather than demand a war the army would not fight. A government that buys its survival with territory is bargaining, exactly as the interpretation says. It is strong again in the Civil War, where the reasoning of peasants like the one in Source C decided the recruits: he hates the requisitioning detachments but stays with the Reds because the Volunteer Army brought the old owner back with a list and flogged the men who had divided the land. And it is strong in 1921, when requisitioning was replaced by a tax in kind and private trade legalised, after which harvests recovered and the famine passed. Yet the interpretation cannot account for the years between the bargains, and this is where Interpretation 1's evidence bites. The Cheka was created in December 1917 and, by the decree of 21 February 1918, could shoot suspects on the spot without trial; the Constituent Assembly, which the Socialist Revolutionaries had won with roughly 370 seats against about 175 Bolsheviks, was closed after one day's sitting in January 1918 and the demonstrations in its support were fired on; the Red Terror was decreed in September 1918 after Lenin was shot, and Source B shows what that meant in a province, registers of hostages drawn up by class. Interpretation 1 conveys this by listing instruments rather than arguments, ending with the sailors 'shot down on the ice', while Interpretation 2 relies on the softer language of offering and bargaining, which quietly removes the rifles from the story. The point both leave out is the machinery. The Politburo became permanent in 1919, the Tenth Party Congress banned factions in the same week that it conceded the New Economic Policy, Stalin became General Secretary in 1922 and the USSR was created at the end of that year as a federation with one party in every republic. That is the answer to the question: the Bolsheviks stayed in power by bargaining with those who could be won and coercing those who could not, and by making sure that no institution survived through which a bargain could ever be enforced against them. Interpretation 2 therefore explains why they were preferred, but only Interpretation 1 explains why they could not be removed, so I agree with it only in part.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first what each historian thinks kept the government alive, and name the test you will judge that claim by.","Use Interpretation 1 as your case against and support both sides with dated detail rather than with the interpretations restated.","Open by putting Interpretation 2's claim into your own words and stating the criterion your judgement will use."],"codes":["30-2 — 3 Moves towards totalitarianism: The Red Terror and the role of the Cheka","30-2 — 2 The Civil War, 1918–21: The reasons for the Bolshevik victory","30-2 — 3 Moves towards totalitarianism: Bolshevik centralisation"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the reasons why the peasants sided with the Bolsheviks rather than the Whites in the years 1918 to 1921? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views weighed, how the difference is conveyed analysed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit the Decree on Land, the return of estates in districts the Whites recaptured and peasant calculation; against, conscription, commissars, hostage-taking and requisitioning; and beyond both texts the peasant risings of 1920 to 1921, which show the villages siding with neither.","sol":"Interpretation 2 claims that the peasants distrusted requisitioning but feared the landlords who followed the White armies more, so that recruits and grain went to the Reds rather than to Kolchak or Denikin. Interpretation 1 claims that what held a peasant in the Red Army was the commissar and the firing squad, and what took his grain was an armed detachment. The test to apply is what a peasant in 1919 was actually choosing between, and whether he was choosing at all. Interpretation 2 has the stronger case on the land. The Decree on Land of November 1917 confirmed the seizures the villages had already made, so by 1918 most peasants held fields they could keep only from a government that let them. In the districts the Whites recaptured the estates went back to their former owners, and Source C shows what that looked like from below: the old owner came behind the Volunteers with a list, the land divided in 1918 was taken back, and the men who had divided it were flogged in front of the church. The writer says he hates the grain detachments, but that between a government that takes his grain and a general who takes his land he will keep the one that leaves him the field. That is a choice made on a comparison, exactly as Interpretation 2 describes, and the Whites, who agreed on nothing beyond removing the Bolsheviks and tolerated pogroms in Ukraine, gave him no reason to choose otherwise. Interpretation 1 is nevertheless describing something real. Trotsky conscripted rather than recruited from 1918, used some fifty thousand former tsarist officers under political commissars, restored the death penalty for desertion; well over a million desertions from the Red Army were recorded in 1919 alone, and desertion was fought with hostage-taking and punishment detachments. War Communism took the grain by force, and Source B shows the Cheka registering the men in a province who might have organised anything against it. A preference that has to be enforced that hard is not the whole explanation of anything. Notice how each historian conveys the point. Interpretation 2 uses the vocabulary of comparison, distrusted and feared more, which makes the peasant a man weighing two offers; Interpretation 1 uses a list of instruments, commissars, firing squads, detachments, which makes him a man being handled. The evidence neither text uses is what settles it. From the summer of 1920 the peasants fought both sides: Antonov's rising in Tambov province and the risings in western Siberia in 1921 tied down large Red forces, and they were not White risings. My judgement is that Interpretation 2 is right for the years the Whites were in the field and wrong as a statement about the peasants and the Bolsheviks. The village did not side with the Bolsheviks; it refused the Whites first, because of the land, and refused the Bolsheviks as soon as the Whites were gone and the requisitioning was not. Interpretation 1 explains that second year, when there was no landlord left to fear and only force kept the grain moving. I therefore agree with Interpretation 2 only as far as 1920, and take the risings that followed as proof that what it calls support was never given, only lent.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what question each historian is really answering about the peasants, and make that the thing you test.","Support both cases with dated detail, and look for the explanation that neither text offers before you judge.","Begin by stating Interpretation 2's claim in your own words and the criterion by which you will measure it."],"codes":["30-2 — 2 The Civil War, 1918–21: The reasons for the Bolshevik victory","30-2 — 2 The Civil War, 1918–21: The reasons for, key events and effects of the Civil War"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the reasons why Lenin introduced the New Economic Policy in 1921? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views evaluated, how each is conveyed analysed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit famine, empty villages and the tax in kind; against, the storming of Kronstadt, the ban on factions and the state's retained commanding heights.","sol":"Interpretation 2 presents the New Economic Policy as the last and clearest of the bargains that kept the Bolsheviks alive: faced with empty villages and mutiny, Lenin retreated and won the countryside back. Interpretation 1 accepts that the retreat happened but insists that it was made from a position already secured by coercion. The criterion for judging between them is what the retreat actually conceded, and to whom. There is a strong case for Interpretation 2. War Communism had destroyed the peasant's reason to grow a surplus, the sown area had shrunk year by year, industry was producing a fraction of its 1913 output, the cities had emptied and famine was spreading through the Volga provinces by the spring of 1921. The detachment commander in Source A is reporting the policy's failure a year before it ended, with the granaries swept clean, the villagers sowing less on purpose and less than half his quota collected. The remedy matched the diagnosis exactly: requisitioning was replaced in March 1921 by a tax in kind, so that a peasant who grew more could sell the surplus, private trade was legalised and small workshops went back to their owners. Harvests and sown area recovered between 1921 and 1924 and the famine passed, which is what winning the countryside back means. Yet Interpretation 1's qualification is precise and hard to answer. The Tenth Party Congress that announced the retreat met while Tukhachevsky's troops were crossing the ice to Kronstadt, and the fortress fell on 18 March 1921; the sailors who had asked for secret elections to the soviets, free trade unions and the release of socialist prisoners got none of those things. What Lenin conceded was economic and what he refused was political, and the same congress banned factions inside the party so that the concession could not be argued about, while the state kept the commanding heights of large industry, banking, transport and foreign trade. The Cheka was not disbanded. This is the point about how the two interpretations convey their views: Interpretation 2 speaks of retreating and winning back, language that makes the policy a negotiation, whereas Interpretation 1's phrase about a position already secured by coercion makes it a concession granted by a victor. Beyond both lies a reason neither mentions, that by the end of 1920 the Red Army had been driven back from Warsaw and the hope of a European revolution rescuing the Soviet economy had gone, so Russia had to buy its own peasants' grain because nobody else was going to supply it. My judgement is that Interpretation 2 correctly identifies the cause of the New Economic Policy but misdescribes its character. Lenin retreated because the villages had stopped producing and the risings showed that force could no longer collect what was not there, which is Interpretation 2's case. But it was a bargain offered on one side only, made in the same fortnight as Kronstadt was stormed and factions were banned, so Interpretation 1 is right about the terms on which it was made. I agree with Interpretation 2 about why the policy came, and with Interpretation 1 about what it was.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Separate the cause of the change from its character, because the two interpretations are really arguing about the second.","Put the dates of the congress and of the fortress side by side before you decide how generous the retreat was.","Open by stating what Interpretation 2 claims about 1921 and the criterion your judgement will apply."],"codes":["30-2 — 4 Economic and social change, 1918–24: The New Economic Policy","30-2 — 4 Economic and social change, 1918–24: War Communism","30-2 — 3 Moves towards totalitarianism: Bolshevik centralisation"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date was the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk signed, and name two territories Russia gave up by it.","a":"3 March 1918. Any two of Poland, Finland, the Baltic provinces (Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania) and Ukraine."},{"q":"Which party won the most seats in the Constituent Assembly elections of November 1917, and how long did the Assembly sit?","a":"The Socialist Revolutionaries, with roughly 370 seats to the Bolsheviks' 175. The Assembly sat for one day, on 5 January 1918 (Old Style), before its Bolshevik guards closed it."},{"q":"Who led the Cheka, and when was it set up?","a":"Felix Dzerzhinsky, from 20 December 1917."},{"q":"What replaced grain requisitioning under the New Economic Policy, and at which meeting was the change announced?","a":"A tax in kind, which let peasants sell what was left after the tax. It was announced at the Tenth Party Congress in March 1921."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The Decree on Land, November 1917","open":false,"code":"30-2 — 1 Early consolidation of power, 1917–18: The early Bolshevik decrees","back":"Abolished private ownership of land without compensation and gave the estates to the peasants. In most provinces they had already seized them, so the decree made the new government the author of what had happened anyway."},{"front":"The Decree on Peace, November 1917","open":false,"code":"30-2 — 1 Early consolidation of power, 1917–18: The early Bolshevik decrees","back":"Put to the Second Congress of Soviets on the night power was seized; it called for an immediate peace without annexations. It committed the Bolsheviks to leaving the war, whatever the terms."},{"front":"The Constituent Assembly, January 1918","open":false,"code":"30-2 — 1 Early consolidation of power, 1917–18: The removal of opposition","back":"Elected in November 1917: the Socialist Revolutionaries took roughly 370 seats and the Bolsheviks about 175. It met in Petrograd on 5 January 1918 and was closed by its Bolshevik guards after one day."},{"front":"The execution of the Romanovs","open":false,"code":"30-2 — 1 Early consolidation of power, 1917–18: The removal of opposition","back":"Nicholas II, Alexandra, their five children and four servants were shot at the Ipatiev House, Yekaterinburg, on the night of 16 to 17 July 1918, ordered by the Ural Regional Soviet as White forces approached."},{"front":"The Treaty of Brest-Litovsk","open":false,"code":"30-2 — 1 Early consolidation of power, 1917–18: The Treaty of Brest-Litovsk","back":"Signed 3 March 1918. Russia gave up Poland, Finland, the Baltic provinces and Ukraine: on the usual estimates about a third of the old empire's population and farmland, and most of its coal."},{"front":"The Czechoslovak Legion, May 1918","open":false,"code":"30-2 — 2 The Civil War, 1918–21: The reasons for, key events and effects of the Civil War","back":"Former prisoners of war being moved east along the Trans-Siberian railway. Their revolt gave scattered resistance a front, put much of Siberia into White hands and turned opposition into civil war."},{"front":"The three White armies","open":false,"code":"30-2 — 2 The Civil War, 1918–21: The reasons for, key events and effects of the Civil War","back":"Kolchak, Supreme Ruler at Omsk from November 1918; Denikin, who led the Volunteer Army in the south and reached Orel in October 1919; Yudenich, who attacked Petrograd from Estonia in the same month."},{"front":"Trotsky and the Red Army","open":false,"code":"30-2 — 2 The Civil War, 1918–21: The reasons for the Bolshevik victory","back":"People's Commissar for War from March 1918. He conscripted millions, used about fifty thousand former tsarist officers under political commissars, restored the death penalty for desertion and toured the fronts in an armoured train."},{"front":"Foreign intervention in the Civil War","open":false,"code":"30-2 — 2 The Civil War, 1918–21: The reasons for the Bolshevik victory","back":"British, French, American and Japanese troops landed at Murmansk, Archangel, the Black Sea and Vladivostok. Too small and too unpopular at home to decide anything, they let the Bolsheviks pose as Russia's defenders."},{"front":"The Cheka and the Red Terror","open":false,"code":"30-2 — 3 Moves towards totalitarianism: The Red Terror and the role of the Cheka","back":"The Cheka was created on 20 December 1917 under Dzerzhinsky to investigate counter-revolution; the decree of 21 February 1918 let it shoot on the spot, and Sovnarkom decreed the Red Terror on 5 September 1918."},{"front":"The Kronstadt mutiny, 1921","open":false,"code":"30-2 — 3 Moves towards totalitarianism: The Red Terror and the role of the Cheka","back":"Sailors demanded secret elections to the soviets, free trade unions, the release of socialist prisoners and an end to requisitioning. Tukhachevsky's troops crossed the ice and took the fortress on 18 March 1921."},{"front":"Bolshevik centralisation, 1919 to 1924","open":false,"code":"30-2 — 3 Moves towards totalitarianism: Bolshevik centralisation","back":"The Politburo became a permanent body in March 1919 and the Tenth Party Congress banned factions in March 1921. The USSR was created by treaty on 30 December 1922; Lenin died on 21 January 1924."},{"front":"War Communism","open":false,"code":"30-2 — 4 Economic and social change, 1918–24: War Communism","back":"From mid-1918: large industry nationalised, grain requisitioned by armed detachments, private trade banned, money destroyed by inflation, rations issued by class and labour conscripted. It fed the Red Army and wrecked the economy."},{"front":"The New Economic Policy, 1921","open":false,"code":"30-2 — 4 Economic and social change, 1918–24: The New Economic Policy","back":"Announced at the Tenth Party Congress in March 1921: a tax in kind instead of requisitioning, private trade legal again and small workshops returned to their owners, while the state kept industry, banking, transport and foreign trade."},{"front":"Women and education under the Bolsheviks","open":false,"code":"30-2 — 4 Economic and social change, 1918–24: Social changes","back":"Civil marriage and divorce on either partner's request from December 1917; abortion legalised in 1920. The Zhenotdel was set up in 1919, and a decree of December 1919 ordered the illiterate aged eight to fifty to learn to read."}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The sources and interpretations written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Source A: From a report sent to the provincial food commissar by the commander of a food requisitioning detachment working in a district of the middle Volga, August 1920. Written for this room in the style of such a report; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — We have now worked through eleven villages of this district. In the first two the quota was handed over without argument, but word travels ahead of us, and in the rest we have found the granaries swept clean and the pits under the floors already emptied. The peasants give us the same sentence in every village: that they sowed less this spring because there was no purpose in sowing more. Twice my men have been met by the whole village standing in the road, and I have not had enough rifles to do anything but withdraw and come back with the detachment from the next district. The committee of the poor here was disbanded long ago and the village assembly does as it likes. I must report that we have collected less than half the amount set for us, and that what we have taken has been taken from people who will be short before the spring.","Source B: From a circular sent to district branches by the chairman of a provincial Cheka in central Russia, September 1918. Written for this room in the style of such a circular; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — The attempt on the life of Comrade Lenin at the end of August, and the murder of Comrade Uritsky in Petrograd on the same day, have shown what mercy has cost us. The decree of the Council of People's Commissars is now in force and is to be applied without hesitation. Each district office will draw up at once a register of the former officers, landowners, priests, factory owners and members of the Kadet and Socialist Revolutionary parties living in its area, and will hold a number of them as hostages against any further attack. Sentences are to be carried out by the Commission itself and reported to me afterwards. Do not wait for a man to act: the class he belongs to is evidence of what he would do. The names of those shot are to be published in the local press, so that the district may learn what resistance costs.","Source C: From a letter written by a peasant conscript serving in the Red Army in southern Russia to his brother in their village, November 1919. Written for this room in the style of a soldier's letter home; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — You ask why I do not walk home as Petrov did. I will tell you. In the summer the Volunteers came through the district north of us, and behind them came the old owner with a list in his hand, and the land that was divided in 1918 was taken back, and the men who had divided it were flogged in front of the church. Our commissar says that is what waits for us if Denikin reaches Moscow, and for once I believe him. I hate the detachments that come for our grain as much as you do, and the day this war ends I shall say so out loud. But between a government that takes my grain and a general who takes my land, I will keep the one that leaves me the field.","Interpretation 1: From a study of the Bolshevik state in its first years, by a historian writing in the 1970s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — The Bolsheviks kept power because they were willing to use force when their rivals were not. Within weeks of taking office they had created the Cheka; before the winter was out they had closed the Constituent Assembly at gunpoint and banned the opposition press; and within a year terror was a published policy. The conscript army that Trotsky built was held together by commissars and firing squads, and the grain that fed it was taken from the villages by armed detachments. When the sailors of Kronstadt asked in 1921 for the freedoms of 1917, they were shot down on the ice. Every retreat the party made, the New Economic Policy included, was made from a position it had already secured by coercion.","Interpretation 2: From a general history of Russia between the wars, by a historian writing in the 1990s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — Terror alone cannot explain a government that lasted. The Bolsheviks survived because at each crisis they offered something their rivals would not. In 1917 they alone promised an immediate peace, and their decrees confirmed the land the peasants had already taken for themselves. During the Civil War the villages distrusted the requisitioning detachments, but they feared the landlords who rode behind the White armies a great deal more, so recruits and grain went to the Reds rather than to Kolchak or Denikin. In 1921, faced with empty fields and mutiny, Lenin retreated to the New Economic Policy and won the countryside back. The regime lasted because it bargained.","How the room uses them: Source A is the inference source for Question 1. Sources B and C are the contemporary pair for the 8-mark usefulness question, 3(a), and may also be used in 3(c). Interpretations 1 and 2 are the pair for 3(b), 3(c) and 3(d), and all three of this room's 16-mark questions are set on these same two interpretations. Every quotation in the model answers of this room comes from the five texts above and from nowhere else."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 3 Question 3(d), 16 marks)","items":["Question: 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the importance of terror in Bolshevik rule in the years 1917 to 1924? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]. This is one paragraph from the middle of the answer, putting the case against Interpretation 2.","Interpretation 2 opens by saying that 'terror alone cannot explain a government that lasted', and as a statement about the whole period that is true; but it understates how much of the period terror had to explain, because it was terror that decided who was still there to bargain with. The Cheka was created on 20 December 1917, six weeks after the seizure of power, and by the decree of 21 February 1918 could shoot suspects on the spot without any court; the Constituent Assembly, which the Socialist Revolutionaries had won in November 1917 with roughly 370 seats against about 175 Bolshevik ones, was allowed to sit for a single day on 5 January 1918 before its own guards closed it, and the demonstrations in its support were fired on. In September 1918, after Fanya Kaplan shot Lenin and Uritsky was murdered in Petrograd, Sovnarkom decreed the Red Terror, and what that meant in a province is visible in Source B, where district offices are told to register former officers, landowners, priests and members of the Kadet and Socialist Revolutionary parties and hold them as hostages, on the principle that the class a man belongs to is evidence of what he would do. Interpretation 1 conveys the same weighting by listing instruments rather than arguments and ending with the sailors 'shot down on the ice', which is the precise point: Kronstadt fell on 18 March 1921, in the same fortnight that the Tenth Party Congress conceded the tax in kind and banned factions inside the party. Interpretation 2 is therefore right that terror was not the whole of Bolshevik rule and wrong about its place in it: the bargains were real, but each was offered by a government that had already removed the people who might have demanded one, which makes terror the condition of the rest rather than one factor beside it.","Why this is Level 4: it 'provides an explained evaluation reviewing the alternative views in coming to a substantiated judgement', because Interpretation 2's own opening claim about terror is taken seriously and then measured against the record, rather than set beside a list of atrocities. The analysis is precise about how the difference of view is conveyed, contrasting the language of bargaining with a list of instruments and the image of the ice. The contextual knowledge is precisely selected rather than general: the date of the Cheka, the election figures, the single day's sitting, the September decree and the fortnight that contains both Kronstadt and the Tenth Congress. The line of reasoning is sustained and the last sentence judges rather than summarises."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same as above — 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the importance of terror in Bolshevik rule in the years 1917 to 1924?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks].","Interpretation 2 says that terror on its own cannot explain why the Bolsheviks lasted, and I partly agree with this. They did give the peasants land and they did end the war, which made them popular, and later on the New Economic Policy made things better again so people supported them more. However, Interpretation 1 says they used force and this is also true, because they had the Cheka and there was a Red Terror and they shot the sailors at Kronstadt when they complained. The Bolsheviks were also good at organising and Trotsky built a strong army which helped them win the Civil War. Overall I think terror was quite important but it was not the only thing, because the Bolsheviks used force but they also gave people things they wanted.","Why this is Level 2 and not Level 3: it does offer 'valid evaluative comment to agree with or counter the interpretation' and it does take details from both texts, which is what Level 2 asks for. What it misses is Level 3's requirement that 'relevant contextual knowledge is used directly to support the evaluation': 'gave the peasants land', 'there was a Red Terror' and 'good at organising' are summaries where the Decree on Land, the decree of September 1918, the closure of the Assembly after one day's sitting and the date of Kronstadt should be. It also never shows how either interpretation conveys its view, and the judgement is announced in the final sentence rather than reasoned towards, so 'its justification is insecure or undeveloped and a line of reasoning is not sustained'. Raising it means replacing every general phrase with a named, dated fact that does evaluative work."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: early consolidation of power, the decrees and the removal of opposition, 1917-18","items":["The Bolsheviks seized power on 25 to 26 October 1917 by the Russian calendar, which was then thirteen days behind the Western one; Russia changed calendars in February 1918, so the first decrees are dated November and December 1917 in most accounts.","Sovnarkom, the Council of People's Commissars, was formed at once with Lenin as chairman, Trotsky for foreign affairs and Stalin for nationalities. It governed by decree.","Decree on Peace, November 1917: an immediate peace without annexations, put to the Second All-Russian Congress of Soviets.","Decree on Land, November 1917: private ownership of land abolished without compensation, estates handed to the peasants through local committees, confirming seizures already made.","Other early decrees: the opposition press banned; an eight-hour working day; workers' control of the factories; titles and ranks abolished; the banks nationalised in December 1917; civil marriage and divorce at the request of either partner in December 1917.","The Cheka, the All-Russian Extraordinary Commission, was created on 20 December 1917 under Felix Dzerzhinsky, answerable to Sovnarkom alone, to investigate counter-revolution and sabotage; the decree of 21 February 1918 gave it the power to shoot suspects on the spot.","The Kadets were banned as enemies of the people by decree before the end of 1917 and their leaders arrested.","Constituent Assembly elections, November 1917: Socialist Revolutionaries roughly 370 seats, Bolsheviks about 175, around a quarter of the votes cast; Mensheviks and Kadets a handful each.","The Assembly met in the Tauride Palace, Petrograd, on 5 January 1918 (Old Style), refused to hand its authority to the Congress of Soviets, and was closed by its Bolshevik guards after one day. Demonstrations in its support were fired on.","The Left Socialist Revolutionaries joined Sovnarkom in December 1917 and left it over Brest-Litovsk in March 1918; Mensheviks and Right Socialist Revolutionaries were expelled from the soviets in June 1918.","The Romanovs were moved from Tobolsk to Yekaterinburg in the spring of 1918 and shot in the cellar of the Ipatiev House on the night of 16 to 17 July 1918: Nicholas II, Alexandra, their four daughters, the Tsarevich Alexei and four servants, on the orders of the Ural Regional Soviet, with White and Czechoslovak forces nearing the city."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk, March 1918, and the reactions to it","items":["An armistice with Germany and Austria-Hungary was agreed in December 1917 and talks opened at Brest-Litovsk.","Trotsky led the Soviet delegation and tried the formula of neither war nor peace in February 1918: refusing to sign, declaring the war over.","Germany resumed its advance on 18 February 1918 and met almost no resistance, moving towards Petrograd.","Lenin threatened to resign and carried the Central Committee on 23 February 1918 against Bukharin's Left Communists, who wanted a revolutionary war.","The treaty was signed on 3 March 1918 and ratified by the Fourth Congress of Soviets later that month.","Terms: Russia gave up Poland, Finland, the Baltic provinces and Ukraine, and ceded territory in the Caucasus to Turkey. On the usual estimates this was about a third of the old empire's population and of its farmland, the great bulk of its coal and a large part of its industry.","The government moved from Petrograd to Moscow in March 1918, out of reach of the German army.","The party renamed itself the Russian Communist Party (Bolsheviks) at its congress in March 1918.","Reactions: the Left Socialist Revolutionaries left the government and in July 1918 murdered the German ambassador, Count Mirbach, hoping to restart the war; Left Communists called it a betrayal of world revolution; Whites and Allies called it treason and used it to justify intervention.","The treaty was annulled in November 1918, as soon as Germany was defeated in the west, which is the best evidence for Lenin's argument that it was a breathing space rather than a settlement."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Civil War, 1918-21, and the reasons for the Bolshevik victory","items":["Causes: the seizure of power itself; the closure of the Constituent Assembly in January 1918; Brest-Litovsk, which gave the Allies a reason to intervene and the Whites a patriotic cause; requisitioning; and the Bolsheviks' refusal to share power.","The Czechoslovak Legion, moving east along the Trans-Siberian railway, revolted in May 1918 and handed much of Siberia to the Whites, turning scattered risings into a war with fronts.","Kolchak declared himself Supreme Ruler at Omsk in November 1918, advanced west in the spring of 1919, was driven back, and was captured and shot in February 1920.","Denikin took over the Volunteer Army in the south after Kornilov was killed in April 1918 and advanced to Orel in October 1919, the nearest any White army came to Moscow. Wrangel succeeded him and evacuated the Crimea in November 1920.","Yudenich attacked Petrograd from Estonia in October 1919 and was repulsed.","Foreign intervention: British, French, American and Japanese forces landed at Murmansk, Archangel, the Black Sea and Vladivostok; most Western troops had gone by 1920, the Japanese remaining longest in the Far East.","The Red Army was built by Trotsky as People's Commissar for War from March 1918: conscription, about fifty thousand former tsarist officers under political commissars, the death penalty restored for desertion, and an armoured command train.","Bolshevik advantages of position: Moscow, Petrograd, the railway hub, the arms factories and roughly sixty million people in the territory they held, so reserves could be moved between fronts in days along interior lines.","White weaknesses: three armies separated by thousands of kilometres whose offensives were never combined under one command, so that Kolchak had already been driven back east before Denikin reached Orel and Yudenich came up to Petrograd in October 1919; no agreed programme beyond removing the Bolsheviks; corruption and pogroms in Ukraine; and the return of estates to their former owners in recaptured districts, which cost them the peasants.","Effects: far more died of typhus and hunger than of wounds; industrial output fell to a fraction of its 1913 level; Petrograd lost something like two-thirds of its people between 1917 and 1920; the famine of 1921 to 1922 in the Volga region killed perhaps five million."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Cheka, the Red Terror, Kronstadt and Bolshevik centralisation","items":["The Cheka, created 20 December 1917 under Dzerzhinsky, began as an investigating body answerable only to Sovnarkom; the decree of 21 February 1918 let it shoot suspects on the spot, and after September 1918 it sentenced and executed without any court.","On 30 August 1918 Fanya Kaplan shot and badly wounded Lenin at a factory in Moscow, and Uritsky, head of the Petrograd Cheka, was murdered the same day.","Sovnarkom decreed the Red Terror on 5 September 1918: hostages taken from the former propertied classes, camps for class enemies, and publication of the names of those shot.","The Cheka's own figures admitted thousands of executions in 1918 alone; historians' estimates of the numbers it shot during the Civil War run from the tens of thousands into the hundreds of thousands.","Peasant resistance to requisitioning grew into open revolt, most seriously in Tambov province from 1920, and strikes broke out in Petrograd in February 1921.","The Kronstadt sailors, on Kotlin Island in the Gulf of Finland, had been among the strongest supporters of 1917. In their resolution of late February 1921 they demanded secret elections to the soviets, freedom of speech for workers and peasants, free trade unions, the release of socialist political prisoners and an end to requisitioning.","Tukhachevsky's troops attacked across the frozen gulf and took the fortress on 18 March 1921; the ringleaders were shot and many sailors escaped to Finland.","The Politburo was made a permanent body at the Eighth Party Congress in March 1919; its first members were Lenin, Trotsky, Stalin, Kamenev and Krestinsky.","The Tenth Party Congress of March 1921 both conceded the New Economic Policy and banned organised factions inside the party.","Stalin, already Commissar for Nationalities, became General Secretary in April 1922 and gained control of appointments.","The USSR was created by treaty on 30 December 1922 from the Russian, Ukrainian, Belorussian and Transcaucasian republics, with a constitution adopted in January 1924: a federation on paper, with one party in every republic.","Lenin suffered strokes in 1922 and 1923, dictated a testament criticising his colleagues and warning against Stalin, and died on 21 January 1924."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: War Communism and the New Economic Policy, 1918-24","items":["War Communism began in the summer of 1918, when a decree nationalised large-scale industry; by 1920 almost every enterprise of any size was state-run under the Supreme Council of the National Economy.","Grain was requisitioned by armed detachments; Committees of the Poor operated in the villages during the second half of 1918 to find hidden stocks, setting neighbour against neighbour.","Private trade was banned, money was destroyed by inflation so that wages were paid partly in goods, and rations were issued by class, with workers and soldiers fed first.","Labour was conscripted and factory discipline militarised; townspeople bought illegally from the traders they called bagmen.","Consequences: the sown area shrank because a surplus could not be sold; the cities emptied; drought in 1921 turned a falling harvest into famine; strikes in Petrograd, the Tambov rising and Kronstadt followed.","The New Economic Policy was announced at the Tenth Party Congress in March 1921. Requisitioning was replaced by a tax in kind, so peasants could sell what was left after the tax.","Private trade was legalised and the traders known as Nepmen reopened markets and shops; small workshops and businesses returned to private hands.","The state kept the commanding heights: large-scale industry, banking, transport and foreign trade. A stable currency was restored through the State Bank and the new chervonets.","Reactions: many Communists saw the policy as a betrayal of everything the Civil War had been fought for and some tore up their party cards, which is why the ban on factions was passed at the same congress; Lenin defended it openly as a retreat.","Effects, 1921 to 1924: sown area and harvests recovered strongly and the famine passed, though heavy industry lagged behind agriculture, producing the scissors crisis of 1923 when industrial prices ran far above what peasants received for grain."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: social change - women, education, culture and the Church","items":["December 1917: marriage became a civil act registered by the state, and divorce could be obtained at the request of either partner.","Equal pay was proclaimed, and the legal disabilities attached to illegitimacy were removed.","November 1920: Soviet Russia became the first country in the world to legalise abortion.","The Zhenotdel, the party's women's department, was set up in 1919 under Inessa Armand and, after her death in 1920, Alexandra Kollontai, who had been the first woman in a European cabinet as Commissar for Social Welfare in 1917.","The Civil War put women into factories, onto the railways and into the Red Army's medical and supply services in large numbers as millions of men were conscripted.","Limits: in the villages custom changed slowly, and divorce on demand often left women with the children and no support, so the law ran ahead of daily life.","Education came under Lunacharsky's Commissariat of Enlightenment: a free, co-educational school for all children was decreed in 1918, and workers' faculties opened universities to workers from 1919.","A decree of December 1919 required the illiterate between the ages of eight and fifty to learn to read; the census of 1926 found more than half the population aged nine to forty-nine literate, against roughly two in five before the war.","Culture: agitprop trains and posters carried the government's message to the front and the villages, and the Proletkult movement tried to build a specifically proletarian art until Lenin brought it under state control in 1920.","Religion: church and state were separated by decree in early 1918 and church land was nationalised; church valuables were seized during the famine of 1922 and the patriarch was arrested."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the source and interpretation moves this paper rewards","items":["Inference (Question 1): say something the source does not state but that must be true if the source is true, then give the words that force it. One inference and one supporting detail in each box.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose: what kind of document it is, who made it and when, and why it was made.","Utility (3(a)): judge each source for the enquiry the question names, never in general. Content first, then how nature, origin and purpose make that content more or less useful, then the context that confirms or qualifies it.","Contextual knowledge scores only when it is used on the source or the interpretation; a paragraph about the period that never touches them earns nothing.","'Biased' is not a judgement by itself. Say whose view the source gives, why it gives that view, and what it is therefore good evidence of: a one-sided source is excellent evidence of the view it takes.","An internal order, like a police circular, is strong evidence of intention and weak evidence of effect; a private letter is candid but narrow. Say which limitation matters for this enquiry.","Difference of view (3(b)): a disagreement about the past, not a difference of wording or of detail. State the one difference in a sentence, then support each half from its own interpretation.","Reason for difference (3(c)): interpretations differ because they ask different questions, rest on different evidence, or give weight to different parts of the story. Name which, and show it from both texts, using Sources B and C where they illustrate the kind of evidence involved.","Differences drawn from evidence are legitimate: an interpretation is not wrong merely because another disagrees with it.","Evaluating an interpretation (3(d)): use both interpretations and your own knowledge, show how each conveys its view through the language it chooses, then say how far the named interpretation can be agreed with and on what criterion."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan - Paper 3 Question 3(d): 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the ways in which Bolshevik rule changed in the years 1917 to 1924?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","items":["The question as printed: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the ways in which Bolshevik rule changed in the years 1917 to 1924? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]. This question prints no stimulus points: the two interpretations are the stimulus, and both must be used, so an answer that evaluates one in isolation cannot reach the top levels.","Decode the statement. Interpretation 2 describes a government that changed course at each crisis: peace and land in 1917, a better bargain than the Whites during the Civil War, the New Economic Policy in 1921, and it ends by saying the regime lasted because it bargained. Interpretation 1 treats the same changes as surface, since every retreat, the New Economic Policy included, was made from ground already secured by coercion; on that view what changed was what was offered, not how the country was ruled. The criterion to announce in the opening is therefore whether the changes of the period altered the method of rule or only its terms.","Opening, three or four sentences: put Interpretation 2's account of change in your own words, name Interpretation 1 as the rival reading, state the criterion, and signal the position you will reach, so the line of reasoning is visible from the first line.","Paragraph 1, for Interpretation 2 — how rule began. The Decree on Land of November 1917 confirmed seizures the peasants had already made and the Decree on Peace promised what no rival would; government in those first months was by decree, through the soviets, and for a few months in coalition with the Left Socialist Revolutionaries. Interim judgement: rule in 1917 to 1918 did begin by conceding.","Paragraph 2, for Interpretation 2 — the terms change again under war. On 3 March 1918 Lenin gave up Poland, Finland, the Baltic provinces and Ukraine at Brest-Litovsk rather than fight a war the army had already abandoned, and during the Civil War peasants reasoned as Source C's conscript does, staying with the side that left him his field rather than the Volunteers who brought the old owner back with a list. Interim judgement: at each crisis the offer was changed to keep what had been won.","Paragraph 3, for Interpretation 2 — the largest change of all, 1921. Requisitioning replaced by a tax in kind, private trade legalised, small workshops returned; the grain harvest recovered from about 37.6 million tonnes in 1921 towards roughly 72.5 million by 1925, against something near 80 million in 1913, and the famine passed. Interim judgement: Interpretation 2's strongest ground, because a policy the party believed in was reversed rather than enforced.","Paragraph 4, against Interpretation 2 — what did not change. The Cheka of 20 December 1917 was shooting suspects without trial within three months, under the decree of 21 February 1918; the Constituent Assembly, won by the Socialist Revolutionaries in November 1917 with roughly 370 seats to about 175, sat for one day on 5 January 1918; the Red Terror was decreed in September 1918, and Source B shows what that meant in a province; and in February 1922 the Cheka was not abolished but renamed, becoming the GPU. Interim judgement: the instruments ran unbroken through every change of policy.","Paragraph 5, against Interpretation 2 and the point that goes furthest beyond both texts — change had a direction, and it was not towards bargaining. The Politburo became permanent in March 1919, the Tenth Party Congress banned factions in the same fortnight as it conceded the New Economic Policy and the fortress of Kronstadt was stormed, Stalin became General Secretary in April 1922, and the USSR was created in December 1922 with one party in every republic. Interim judgement: between 1917 and 1924 rule moved from a coalition of soviets to a single party machine, which is a change Interpretation 2 does not describe.","The evaluation thread that must run through every paragraph: how each interpretation conveys its view. Interpretation 2 uses the vocabulary of exchange — offered, distrusted, feared more, bargained — which makes change look like responsiveness; Interpretation 1 uses a list of instruments and ends on the image of sailors shot down on the ice, which makes the same changes look like tactical retreats from ground already held. Saying this, and using it, is what the top level means by showing how the differences of view are conveyed.","What the conclusion must say: not that both are partly right. Apply the criterion announced at the start — that what the Bolsheviks offered changed repeatedly and genuinely, in 1917 and again in 1921, while how they ruled changed in one direction only, towards concentration, so Interpretation 2 describes the changes of policy accurately and mistakes them for a change of method. Then name the deciding consideration: the one change the period never made was to restore a body through which the government could have been obliged to keep any bargain.","Timing and SPaG: about 30 minutes in all — five to fix the criterion and order the paragraphs, about twenty-two to write, and the last three to check spelling and punctuation and to make sure the specialist terms are present and correctly used, since 4 additional marks depend on 'a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate'. Useful terms here: Sovnarkom, decree, Constituent Assembly, Cheka, Red Terror, commissar, requisitioning, War Communism, New Economic Policy, tax in kind, Politburo, soviet."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:20:27Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"Re-checked after the polish pass and both retargets are sound. Worked example 5 is now the room's only 3(c), on the reasons for the Kronstadt mutiny of March 1921, with Interpretations 5 and 6 printed in its own setup and declared written for it; its facts verify - the Petropavlovsk resolution of 28 February 1921 for soviets re-elected by secret ballot and free speech for workers, peasants and the left socialist parties, the city bread ration cut by a third in January 1921, and the Petrograd strikes of late February that preceded the rising. Worked example 1's 3(a) is now on the effects of the Civil War on people living in Russia, 1918-21, which is neither questions[4]'s enquiry into attitudes nor questions[5]'s into the handling of opposition, and its seven steps and cues are rewritten to match; nothing in the lesson now mirrors questions[13] or questions[4], and dupscan prints 'no duplication'. The Cheka is now staged correctly and vouchably - the founding remit of 20 December 1917, the decree of 21 February 1918 ('The Socialist Fatherland is in Danger') allowing suspects to be shot on the spot, and the Red Terror decreed on 5 September 1918 - in every place the last verdict listed. Three things remain: first,¹ questions[6].sol was not on that list and still dates summary powers to the creation of the Cheka, so the room contradicts itself in one sentence; second,² no worked example now works a 3(d); third,³ extras[0] does not mention the extra interpretation pairs the examples print. All cards stay inside 40 words and no answer key needs changing."}}},"30-3":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"30-3","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 3 is where Option 30 stops being a story about revolution and becomes a study of how a dictatorship is built and held. Paper 3 leans on it heavily: the Section A source is often a document of the terror or of the propaganda machine, the 12-mark causation question is usually why Stalin won power or why the purges happened, and Section B's interpretations are almost always arguments about the Terror, about Stalin's personal responsibility or about how far ordinary people took part in it. If you can explain, with names and dates, how a man the party leadership underrated ended up unopposed, why a state that had just proclaimed the most democratic constitution in the world was shooting its own generals, and how films, schools, churches and newspapers were turned into one voice, you can answer almost anything this option sets on the 1930s. The room teaches that as an argument you can take sides in, and it trains the five moves the paper rewards: inference, causal explanation that goes beyond the printed bullet points, judging a source's usefulness for the enquiry actually named, spotting how two historians differ and why, and evaluating one of them against the other and against what you know.","idea":[{"code":"30-3 — 1 The struggle for power, 1924–28: The strengths and weaknesses of Stalin and his rivals","h":"The dull man with the card index, and the brilliant men who underrated him","t":"Lenin died on 21 January 1924 and left no successor. On paper Trotsky was the obvious one: the organiser of the October rising, the creator of the Red Army, the best speaker and writer in the party. His weaknesses were as visible: he had been a Menshevik until 1917 and the Old Bolsheviks never forgot it, his command of the army made others fear a Russian Bonaparte, and he despised committee work. Zinoviev ran the Leningrad party and the Comintern, Kamenev ran Moscow; both were able but had opposed the seizure of power in October 1917, which Stalin could quote against them. Bukharin was the party's most popular theorist and the champion of the NEP, but he had no machine behind him. Stalin had the machine. He had been General Secretary since April 1922, and the post decided who was appointed, transferred and promoted. A top-level answer never lists these qualities: it pairs a strength of Stalin's with a weakness of a rival and shows the two working together, because the contest was decided as much by what Trotsky would not do as by what Stalin did."},{"code":"30-3 — 1 The struggle for power, 1924–28: The reasons for the emergence of Stalin as leader","h":"How the rivals were removed, one alliance at a time","t":"Stalin never fought all his rivals at once. From 1923 he worked with Zinoviev and Kamenev in a triumvirate against Trotsky, who resigned as Commissar for War in January 1925. When Zinoviev and Kamenev turned on him with their New Opposition at the Fourteenth Party Congress in December 1925, he defeated them with the support of Bukharin and the right, who wanted the NEP kept. Trotsky, Zinoviev and Kamenev then combined too late: the United Opposition was beaten, Zinoviev lost his Politburo seat in July 1926 and Trotsky lost his in October 1926, and after opposition demonstrations on the tenth anniversary of the revolution in November 1927 both were expelled from the party. Trotsky was sent to Alma-Ata in January 1928 and deported from the USSR in 1929. Stalin then turned on Bukharin, Rykov and Tomsky over the grain crisis and the end of the NEP, and Bukharin lost his Politburo seat in November 1929. Two other things helped: the ban on factions passed in 1921, which made organised disagreement a party offence, and Lenin's Testament, which asked for Stalin's removal but which the Central Committee agreed not to publish in May 1924. A top-level answer tells this as a sequence of choices, not a list of dates, and says what each victory gave him for the next one."},{"code":"30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The reasons for, nature of and consequences of the purges","h":"From party discipline to mass terror, 1934 to 1938","t":"Purging began as expulsion from the party, but after Sergei Kirov, the popular Leningrad leader, was shot at the Smolny Institute on 1 December 1934, it turned into arrest, camps and execution. A decree issued the same month allowed terrorist cases to be heard quickly and without appeal. The Terror widened in 1936 under Nikolai Yezhov, and 1937 and 1938 were its worst years, so much so that Russians named the period after him. Quotas for arrest and execution were sent out to every region in the summer of 1937; the NKVD's own records list roughly 680,000 death sentences carried out in those two years, and well over a million people were in labour camps by 1939. The armed forces were hit in June 1937, when Marshal Tukhachevsky and seven other senior commanders were tried in secret and shot; three of the five marshals and most of the army's senior commanders followed, and tens of thousands of officers were arrested or dismissed. The consequences ran into the war: the Red Army's poor showing against Finland in the Winter War of 1939 to 1940 and the catastrophe of the German invasion of June 1941 were both blamed in part on the loss of experienced commanders. A top-level answer separates the reasons, the nature and the consequences and never lets one stand in for another."},{"code":"30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The work of the secret police (NKVD)","h":"The NKVD, the troikas and the camps","t":"The secret police was renamed and reorganised in 1934, when the OGPU was folded into the People's Commissariat for Internal Affairs, the NKVD. It was run by Genrikh Yagoda from 1934, by Yezhov from September 1936, and by Lavrenti Beria from the end of 1938; both of the first two were themselves shot, which tells you how the system treated its own servants. The NKVD took denunciations, arrested at night, interrogated in relays until a confession was signed, and sent most cases not to a court but to a troika, a panel of three officials who sentenced in minutes without a defence. Those not shot went to the Gulag, the camp administration: gold at Kolyma, coal at Vorkuta, timber in the north, and building projects such as the White Sea to Baltic canal, opened in 1933 after about twenty months of digging largely by hand, in which many thousands died. Camp labour was cheap and could be moved to places nobody would go for wages, so terror and the Five-Year Plans fed each other. A top-level answer uses the NKVD to explain how fear reached into a flat or a factory bench, rather than describing the organisation for its own sake."},{"code":"30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The reasons for, and the importance of, the show trials","h":"Three trials in Moscow, and what they were for","t":"The show trials were public performances with the verdict already decided. In August 1936 Zinoviev, Kamenev and fourteen others were tried for forming a Trotskyite-Zinovievite terrorist centre, confessed and were shot. In January 1937 Pyatakov, Radek and others were tried for wrecking industry on Trotsky's orders; most were shot. In March 1938 twenty-one defendants, among them Bukharin, Rykov and the former NKVD chief Yagoda, were tried as a bloc of rightists and Trotskyites, accused of plotting with foreign powers to break up the USSR; eighteen of them, including those three, were shot. Tomsky had already killed himself in 1936. The prosecutor at all three was Andrei Vyshinsky, confessions were produced by sleep deprivation, threats to families and long relays of questioning, and foreign journalists and diplomats were invited so that the world would report what it saw. The trials did four jobs at once: they destroyed the men who had known Stalin as an equal, they explained away the failures of the Five-Year Plans as sabotage, they made Trotsky in exile the author of every disaster, and they warned every official that rank was no protection. A top-level answer judges them as an instrument with purposes, not as a curiosity."},{"code":"30-3 — 3 Propaganda and censorship: Official culture, socialist realism, control of education and religion","h":"One permitted style, one history book, one voice","t":"Culture was made an arm of government. Independent literary groups were abolished in 1932 and a single Union of Soviet Writers set up; at its first congress in August 1934 socialist realism was declared the only acceptable method, which meant art that was realistic in style, optimistic in mood, easy to understand and full of the promise of a socialist future: tractor drivers, steel workers, Mukhina's Worker and Kolkhoz Woman striding forward at the Paris exhibition of 1937, Gerasimov's portraits of Stalin. Glavlit, the censorship office set up in 1922, cleared everything printed, and photographs were retouched so that purged men vanished from Stalin's side. Schools were brought back to discipline, textbooks and examinations, with the Pioneers and the Komsomol organising children out of lessons and the story of Pavlik Morozov, a boy said to have denounced his own father, taught as an example; a single approved school history, A Short History of the USSR edited by A. V. Shestakov, was issued to schools in 1937, and from 1938 the separate official Short Course supplied the history of the party. Religion was attacked by the League of the Militant Godless, churches were closed or converted and the Cathedral of Christ the Saviour in Moscow was dynamited in December 1931 — yet the census of January 1937 found that over half of adults still called themselves believers, and it was suppressed and its organisers arrested. A top-level answer treats control of culture as a means of government and asks how far it actually changed what people thought."},{"code":"30-3 — 3 Propaganda and censorship: The new Constitution of 1936","h":"The most democratic constitution in the world, in the year of the first show trial","t":"The new Constitution was adopted on 5 December 1936 and presented as proof that socialism had been achieved. It promised a vote to every citizen over eighteen by secret ballot, including the former nobles, priests and kulaks who had been excluded before; freedom of speech, press, assembly and religious worship; and rights to work, rest, education and support in old age. It created a two-chamber Supreme Soviet and described the USSR as a union of equal republics. Every promise was hollowed out by one clause: the Communist Party was named as the leading force, and it was the only party, so the elections to the Supreme Soviet in December 1937 offered a single list of candidates and returned reported turnouts of over ninety per cent. The timing is the point worth arguing about. It was adopted between the first and second Moscow show trials, and men who helped draft it, Bukharin among them, were shot within two years. A top-level answer uses the Constitution to show how the regime wanted to be seen at home and abroad during the years of the Popular Front, and measures the document against what was happening while it was being signed."},{"code":"30-3 — 4 The Cult of Stalin: The meaning of the Cult of Stalin and reasons for its introduction","h":"What a cult of personality is, and what it was for","t":"A cult of personality means the deliberate presentation of a leader as more than a politician: infallible, fatherly, the source of everything good, so that loyalty to the state becomes love of one man. Iosif Dzhugashvili, born in Gori in Georgia in 1878 (the official year was 1879, which is why December 1929 was celebrated as his fiftieth birthday), had taken the name Stalin, from the word for steel, long before; the cult made the name into a character. It was launched around his official fiftieth birthday in December 1929, when the newspapers filled with tributes, and it grew through the 1930s. It had work to do. Stalin had no claim to leadership by achievement in 1917 to match Trotsky's, so he was made instead the true heir and pupil of Lenin. The upheaval of collectivisation and the Five-Year Plans needed a figure who could be trusted while everything else changed, and one who could take credit for the successes while local officials took blame for the failures. Once the Terror began, public praise became the cheapest proof of loyalty, so the cult fed on the fear. A top-level answer explains the cult as a tool of rule with reasons behind it, and never confuses it with popularity."},{"code":"30-3 — 4 The Cult of Stalin: How the Cult of Stalin was achieved","h":"How it was built: the image, the history and the map","t":"The cult was manufactured by every medium the state controlled. Portraits and statues went into every school, office and factory; banners of his face were carried at the parades on May Day and on the anniversary of the revolution. Newspapers printed his name in heavy type and gave him titles: Vozhd, the leader, the Father of the Peoples, the great teacher. Painters showed him among smiling workers and children, and photographers doctored images so that he stood beside Lenin in 1917 while Yezhov, once he fell, disappeared from a photograph taken on a canal bank. History was rewritten to match: the Short Course of 1938 gave Stalin a leading role beside Lenin and reduced Trotsky to a traitor. Places took his name — Tsaritsyn had become Stalingrad in 1925 — and achievements were credited to him, from the Moscow Metro opened in 1935 to record-breaking workers such as Alexei Stakhanov, who was said to have cut fourteen times his quota of coal in a single shift in August 1935. A top-level answer names the methods and links each one to the effect intended, because the exam rewards how it was done as often as why."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1: two inferences from Source A, and why quoting is not enough","setup":"Source A in this room is from a monthly report sent by the head of a district office of the NKVD in a provincial town to his regional directorate, October 1937. In it the officer says the figure set for his district for the last quarter has been met in full and asks for the new figure to be confirmed quickly, 'as arrests are already being made against it'; most cases now reach him as denunciations written by neighbours and workmates, and many of these, when investigated, 'turn out to concern a quarrel over a room or a place at the bench rather than any enemy activity', though a signed statement cannot be left without action; his investigators work in shifts through the night and 'confessions are obtained in almost every case', although the men then repeat them differently before the troika; the cells hold three times the number they were built for, and he asks again for two more investigators and for paper. The question is: 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about who controlled the work of a district NKVD office in 1937. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this.' (4 marks)","steps":["Start from the one body in the report that the officer names without explaining, because it stands outside his own office: the men repeat their confessions differently 'before the troika'.","Convert that into a claim the report never makes: the office that took a confession was not the body that decided the case, since the case goes on to a troika the officer does not sit on and whose changed stories he records as no business of his.","Look elsewhere in the report for a second inference that does not repeat the first, and take the only two things he asks for: the new figure has to be confirmed by the regional directorate, and he must ask again for two more investigators and for paper.","Set out each box as a claim the source does not state followed by the exact words that force it, and then stop, because nothing beyond the two boxes is rewarded here."],"cues":["Which body in the report sits outside this office?","If the story changes there and the officer moves on, who is deciding?","What are the only two things the officer actually asks for?","What earns the second mark in each box?"]},{"title":"Question 3(a): judging Sources B and C for the enquiry actually named","setup":"Source B in this room is from the private diary of a schoolteacher in a provincial Soviet town, kept during 1937: in March the staff were held back for a meeting on vigilance at which the director read out the names of two teachers who had been removed and asked whether anyone had noticed anything, and after a long silence three people spoke at once; in October a new history textbook arrived, one for every class, to be taught from and from nothing else, so the old books were locked in a cupboard because a teacher in the next street had been denounced for keeping a volume with a photograph of a man who was now an enemy; in November the writer's brother-in-law had been taken in September with no news since, and her sister queued at the prison twice a week to hand in a parcel, knowing he was alive if it was accepted. Source C is from a report in a Soviet newspaper on the opening of the trial of the bloc of rightists and Trotskyites, March 1938: the hall was full an hour before the court sat; in the dock were men whose names had once been printed beside Lenin's and who had now confessed to years of work for foreign intelligence services, to wrecking factories and to plotting murder; the accused answered the prosecutor without denial; delegations from Moscow factories sent resolutions demanding the highest measure of punishment, which were read out to applause; foreign correspondents were present throughout and might report that the accused had condemned themselves out of their own mouths. The question is: 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the way information was controlled in the Soviet Union in the years 1937 to 1938? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context.' (8 marks)","steps":["Copy the enquiry out and let it do the selecting: the control of information, so the new textbook, the locked cupboard, the denounced photograph and the newspaper's account of the trial are in, and the parcel queue at the prison is not.","Take Source B's content for that enquiry first: one history book for every class, to be taught from and from nothing else, shows the state fixing what could be said in a classroom, and a teacher denounced for keeping a volume with the photograph of a man who is now an enemy shows the past being edited down to its pictures.","Weigh Source B's nature, origin and purpose together: a private diary written at the time, for nobody, shows censorship from inside the person carrying it out, down to locking the old books away herself, though one teacher in one town cannot show how the rule was applied elsewhere.","Do the same for Source C, and do not dismiss it as propaganda: it is the controlled information itself, so it is strong evidence of exactly what a reader was given, and the invited foreign correspondents show that the control was meant to work abroad as well as at home.","Use context on the sources rather than beside them: the textbook in Source B belongs to the year a single approved history was issued to schools, and the confessions Source C reports were produced by relays of interrogation, which is why the promise that the accused condemned themselves cannot be taken at face value.","Close with a judgement for this enquiry only: between them they hold both ends of the machine, the classroom and the newspaper, and neither can show who decided what the line would be, because neither writer was anywhere near that decision."],"cues":["What is the enquiry into, in your own words?","What does one textbook for every class tell you?","Why can a private diary show censorship better than a rule book can?","Is a source written to persuade less useful for this enquiry?","Which piece of your own knowledge tests a claim in each source?","What can neither source show you?"]},{"title":"Question 3(b): a difference of view, not a difference of detail","setup":"This worked example uses a second, shorter pair of interpretations, printed here for practice only; they are not the room's printed Interpretation 1 and Interpretation 2, which are used everywhere else. Interpretation 1, from a study of Soviet propaganda by a historian writing in the 1970s: 'The cult was manufactured. Editors were told which photograph to print, painters which pose to paint, and towns which name to take. What looked like devotion was compliance: people cheered because the alternative was to be noticed, and the portraits they carried had been handed to them at the factory gate.' Interpretation 2, from a study of popular opinion under Stalin by a historian writing in the 2000s: 'Conformity is not the whole story. Ordinary people wrote to Stalin in their thousands about a lost job, a seized cow, a bullying chairman, and they wrote as if he would put it right. The cult worked because it fitted an older habit of appealing over the local boss to a distant, just ruler.' The question is: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. They give different views about the way Soviet people responded to the cult of Stalin in the 1930s. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations.' (4 marks)","steps":["Reduce each interpretation to a single sentence answering the same question, which is why Soviet people behaved as they did towards the cult: one says because they were made to, the other says because a great many of them meant it.","Reject the tempting near-misses, such as one naming photographs and town names and the other naming letters, because a detail present in one text and absent from the other is a difference of detail and stays in the lower level.","State the difference as a disagreement about belief: Interpretation 1 treats the cult as something done to people from above, Interpretation 2 treats it as something many people took up and turned to their own use.","Anchor each half of that sentence with a short phrase from its own interpretation, the portraits 'handed to them at the factory gate' against writing to Stalin 'as if he would put it right', so the difference is supported from both texts rather than asserted."],"cues":["What single question are both texts answering?","Why are the letters not the difference by themselves?","How would you word the disagreement as one about belief?","What must you add so the point is supported, not asserted?"]},{"title":"Question 2: getting a 12-mark answer past the printed bullet points","setup":"Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b), both 'Explain why...' at 12 marks on the same grid, and you answer one of them. Say the one you take is: 'Explain why Stalin destroyed the leadership of the Red Army in 1937. You may use the following in your answer: • Marshal Tukhachevsky • the Civil War. You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks) The mark scheme prints the stimulus caps: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'. There is no cap at Level 2, so an answer built only on the two bullets can still reach the top of Level 2, but it cannot pass 8 marks and cannot reach Level 4.","steps":["Read the two bullets as a warning rather than a plan, since an essay built only on Tukhachevsky and the Civil War cannot pass eight marks however well it is written.","Build the first reason on the bullets anyway, but analytically: Tukhachevsky was one of the first Marshals of the Soviet Union, named in November 1935, and first deputy commissar for defence, so his standing was an authority of its own, and he and Stalin had quarrelled since the failure before Warsaw in 1920, so the one soldier whose name stood on its own was also a man with an old grievance.","Make the second reason the charge itself, which the bullets do not prompt: eight commanders were tried behind closed doors on 11 June 1937 and shot within hours for conspiring with the German general staff, on confessions taken under interrogation, and once a plot at the top of the army was official every officer below them became a suspect.","Make the third reason the timing, which takes the answer furthest from the bullets: Yezhov had run the NKVD since September 1936 and the Moscow trials had already made treason by old comrades an established fact, so the one institution in the country that held weapons was where the next conspiracy had to be found.","Signal the move beyond the stimulus in the essay itself, with a sentence that says which reason neither bullet point prompts, and end by linking the three, since it was the conspiracy story that turned a general's prestige and an old grudge into a matter of state security."],"cues":["What do the caps mean for an answer that uses only the bullets?","How do you turn a bullet point into analysis rather than description?","What did a charge of plotting with Germany make possible?","Which reason has nothing to do with either bullet?","How do you make the examiner see that you went beyond them?"]},{"title":"Question 3(d): evaluating Interpretation 2 against Interpretation 1 and your own knowledge","setup":"The question is: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part the local NKVD played in the purges of the Soviet Union in the years 1936 to 1938? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]. Interpretation 2 argues that the Terror was made in a thousand offices and factory meetings, with quotas handed down to regional bosses who asked for them to be raised, denunciations written by ordinary people settling private scores, and local police officers, judged on the cases they closed, taking what was offered, so that Stalin lit the fire but did not choose most of its victims. Interpretation 1 argues that the Terror was made at the top, scripted for Stalin, with the secret police reporting to him and the shooting lists sent to his desk to be approved, stopped on his word at the end of 1938 and its organiser arrested in turn. No stimulus points are printed on this question.","steps":["Decide what the criterion of judgement will be before writing anything, and name it in the opening: whether the question is who found the names in a particular town, or who decided that names would be found at all and what was to be done with them.","Build the case for Interpretation 2 out of dated knowledge rather than out of the interpretation restated: a district office was judged on the cases it closed and competed to close them, and in the summer of 1938 the NKVD chief in Omsk asked to be allowed thousands more executions once his district had filled the figure it was given, which is local appetite and not a central instruction.","Build the case against it from Interpretation 1 and your own knowledge: in the national operations that ran beside the quotas, such as the order of 11 August 1937 against supposed Polish agents, a district compiled the lists but no sentence on them could be carried out until the list had been confirmed in Moscow by the NKVD and the procurator's office, so the office that made the arrest did not decide the outcome.","Show how each text conveys its view, because the top level rewards that explicitly: Interpretation 2 writes of a fire and of a thousand offices, which spreads responsibility across the map, while Interpretation 1 writes of a signature and a telephone call, which gathers it into one room.","Judge by the criterion you named rather than splitting the difference, and use the test that settles it: when the line from the centre changed, the same local officers were arrested and tried in their turn for what was now called a breach of legality, which tells you who had been setting the rules while they worked."],"cues":["What exactly would have to be true for Interpretation 2 to be right?","Where can you see a local office wanting more than it had been given?","Who had to sign before a district's list became a sentence?","How does each historian's wording carry the argument?","What happened to those officers once the line from the centre changed?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which post had Stalin held since April 1922, and why did it matter more than it looked?","a":"General Secretary of the Communist Party. It controlled appointments, transfers and promotions, so as the party grew through the Lenin Enrolment of 1924 and 1925 the men who owed him their places filled the congresses that voted on his opponents."},{"q":"Whose murder, on which date, opened the way to the terror of the later 1930s, and what was done immediately afterwards?","a":"Sergei Kirov, the Leningrad party leader, was shot at the Smolny Institute on 1 December 1934. A decree issued that month allowed terrorist cases to be tried quickly and without appeal, and a wave of arrests followed in Leningrad."},{"q":"Name the leading defendants of the three Moscow show trials and the years in which they were held.","a":"Zinoviev and Kamenev in August 1936; Pyatakov and Radek in January 1937; Bukharin, Rykov and Yagoda, among twenty-one defendants, in March 1938. Andrei Vyshinsky prosecuted all three and almost all the accused were shot."},{"q":"Why is the Constitution of 1936 evidence about propaganda rather than about democracy?","a":"Because the rights it promised were cancelled by the Communist Party's monopoly: the elections of December 1937 offered one list of candidates, and the document was adopted between two show trials, with drafters such as Bukharin shot within two years."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 3 Section A (16 marks): Question 1 prints Source A and asks 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about...' in a table of two boxes each (4 marks); Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b), of which you answer one: both are 'Explain why...' with two stimulus points and 'You must also use information of your own.', marked on the same grid (12 marks).","Paper 3 Section B (36 marks), all on Question 3: (a) 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into...?' (8); (b) 'What is the main difference between these views?' (4); (c) 'Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about...' (4); (d) 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about...?' (16, plus up to 4 for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology).","The stimulus-point caps printed on the 12-mark 'Explain why...': 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'. There is no cap at Level 2, so an answer that stays inside the two stimulus points can still reach the top of Level 2, but the top of Level 3 and Level 4 need three or more aspects of content. Question 3(d) prints no stimulus points and carries no such cap.","Timing on Paper 3, which lasts 1 hour 30 minutes for 52 marks: roughly a minute a mark plus thinking time. About 4 minutes on Question 1, 15 on Question 2, 10 on 3(a), 5 each on 3(b) and 3(c), and about 30 minutes on 3(d) including a final check for the SPaG marks.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose, and usefulness is judged for the enquiry the question names, never in general. Contextual knowledge earns nothing unless it is used on the sources or the interpretations themselves.","The five dates that anchor this key topic: 21 January 1924, Lenin dies; December 1929, Stalin's official fiftieth birthday and the launch of the cult; 1 December 1934, Kirov is murdered; 5 December 1936, the new Constitution is adopted; 1937 to 1938, the worst two years of the Terror."],"questions":[{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about the way the NKVD worked in 1937. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for each valid inference up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail from Source A. Credit arrest by quota, confession obtained under pressure, or an office overwhelmed by its own workload.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: the NKVD arrested people to meet targets set from above rather than because evidence against them existed. Details in the source that tell me this: the officer reports that the figure for the last quarter has been met in full and asks for the new figure to be confirmed quickly, since 'arrests are already being made against it'. (ii) What I can infer: confessions were produced by pressure rather than by proof. Details in the source that tell me this: the investigators 'work in shifts through the night' and confessions are obtained 'in almost every case', yet the accused then repeat them differently before the troika.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["An inference is something the report makes certain without stating it; ask what has to be true for a policeman to write this to his own superior without embarrassment.","Read the report twice, once for how cases arrive at the office and once for how they leave it, and take one inference from each reading.","Write each answer in two parts, a claim the source never makes and then the exact words that force it."],"codes":["30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The work of the secret police (NKVD)","30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The reasons for, nature of and consequences of the purges"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about the purges in the Soviet Union in 1937. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for each valid inference up to two, with a second mark each for supporting detail. Credit private quarrels feeding the purge, the scale of arrests, or the impossibility of ignoring a denunciation once written.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: ordinary people used the purges to settle private quarrels. Details in the source that tell me this: most cases reach the office as denunciations by neighbours and workmates, and many 'turn out to concern a quarrel over a room or a place at the bench rather than any enemy activity'. (ii) What I can infer: the purges had grown far beyond what the system could handle. Details in the source that tell me this: the cells 'hold three times the number they were built for', and the officer has to ask again for two more investigators and for paper.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask who is actually starting these cases, and what that tells you about how the purge spread through a town.","One inference should come from where the accusations begin and the other from what the office looks like once they arrive.","Pair each claim with the words that prove it, and resist the urge to explain the purges instead."],"codes":["30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The reasons for, nature of and consequences of the purges","30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The work of the secret police (NKVD)"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. They give different views about the reasons for the purges in the Soviet Union in the years 1936–38. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4) needs a key difference of view identified and supported from both texts. The difference is where the Terror came from: one man's decisions, or a system and a society that spread it. Differences of detail only stay at Level 1.","sol":"The main difference is about where the Terror came from. Interpretation 1 sees it as one man's instrument, made at the top and bearing 'one man's signature', with the trials 'scripted for him' and the shooting lists sent to his desk, so that it could be switched off by a single telephone call. Interpretation 2 sees it as a process driven by many hands, 'made in a thousand offices and factory meetings', with regional bosses asking for their quotas to be raised and ordinary people denouncing neighbours and rivals, so that Stalin 'did not choose most of its victims'. They therefore disagree about the cause of the purges, not merely about their details.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Both historians are answering one question; work out what that question is before comparing them.","A fact in one text and missing from the other is a difference of detail, which will not reach the top level; look for a disagreement about cause.","Write one sentence containing both views, then support each half with a short phrase from its own interpretation."],"codes":["30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The reasons for, nature of and consequences of the purges","30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The work of the secret police (NKVD)"]},{"q":"Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about the reasons for the purges in the Soviet Union in the years 1936–38. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4) needs an explanation of one reason for difference, substantiated from the interpretations and, where used, from Sources B and C. Credit the different evidence each rests on, or the different question each is asking.","sol":"One reason is that they rest on different kinds of evidence. Interpretation 1 is built on material produced at the centre: the scripted trials and the lists sent to Stalin's desk, the sort of record Source C reflects, where a newspaper reports a Moscow trial staged for foreign correspondents and the state speaks with one voice. Interpretation 2 is built on what survives lower down: quotas argued over in the provinces and denunciations by neighbours, the world of Source B, where colleagues denounce one another at a staff meeting and a family queues at a prison. Looking at the centre makes the Terror look designed; looking at a town makes it look spread by many hands.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Historians disagree for reasons a marker can name; think about what each one had in front of them.","Ask which of the two rooms each interpretation is standing in, the Kremlin or a provincial town, and what each room lets you see.","Use one source as evidence of the kind of material behind each view, then say plainly why that produces a different answer."],"codes":["30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The reasons for, nature of and consequences of the purges","30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The reasons for, and the importance of, the show trials"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the impact of the purges on everyday life in the Soviet Union in the years 1936–38? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8) needs judgements on utility for this enquiry with developed reasoning on how nature, origin and purpose affect the content, and context used to interpret the sources. Credit denunciation, censorship of textbooks and prison queues from B, and the public performance of the trials from C.","sol":"Source B is useful because it shows the purges arriving in the ordinary business of a working week. A meeting on vigilance produces a long silence and then 'three people spoke at once', which is denunciation as competition; the old books are locked away because a teacher nearby was denounced for keeping a photograph of a man now an enemy; and the writer's sister queues at the prison to hand in a parcel because acceptance is the only proof her husband is alive. Its provenance strengthens all of this: a diary kept privately in 1937, written for nobody, can admit the shame of staying silent, which no report or letter would risk. Its limit is scale, since one teacher in one town cannot show how general the fear was. Source C is useful in a different way. It is a newspaper report on the trial of March 1938, written to be believed, so it is not evidence of guilt but excellent evidence of what the public was meant to think: factory resolutions demanding 'the highest measure of punishment' were read to applause, and foreign correspondents were invited so the verdict would travel. Context sharpens both: the textbook in Source B belongs to the year a single approved history was issued to schools, and the confessions praised in Source C were produced by relays of night questioning. Together they are more useful than either alone, because they show the private fear and the public ritual at once; neither reaches the camps.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Usefulness is always usefulness for something; write the enquiry at the top of your plan and let it decide what to quote.","Take each source as content, then nature, origin and purpose, then the context that confirms or qualifies it, and compare only at the end.","Open on what Source B lets you infer about life in the staffroom, and make the first sentence a judgement rather than a description."],"codes":["30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The reasons for, nature of and consequences of the purges","30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The work of the secret police (NKVD)","30-3 — 3 Propaganda and censorship: Official culture, socialist realism, control of education and religion"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the way the Soviet government used the show trials of 1936–38? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8) needs utility judged for this enquiry with provenance reasoning and context used on the sources. Credit C on staging, confession and the foreign audience, and B on the atmosphere the trials were meant to create, with the limits of each noted.","sol":"Source C is the more directly useful of the two, precisely because it is not impartial. It is a report in a Soviet newspaper on the opening of the trial of March 1938, written for a public that was expected to accept it, so its purpose is the evidence: it shows the government presenting men 'whose names were once printed beside Lenin's' as agents of foreign intelligence, parading confessions in which the accused 'answered them without denial', publishing factory resolutions demanding the highest measure of punishment, and pointing to the presence of foreign correspondents as a guarantee of fairness. That last detail is the most revealing, because it shows the trials aimed at an audience abroad as well as at home. What it cannot do is tell us whether any of it was true, and context supplies that: twenty-one men stood trial from 2 to 13 March 1938, among them Bukharin, Rykov and the former NKVD chief Yagoda, the confessions had been obtained by threats and by questioning in relays, and Vyshinsky's court had no intention of acquitting. Source B is useful more indirectly. A private diary of 1937 shows the effect the trials were meant to have further down: colleagues denouncing one another at a meeting on vigilance, a teacher denounced for keeping a photograph of a man now an enemy. It says nothing about the courtroom, so for this enquiry it supports rather than leads. Used together, C shows the performance and B shows the audience it produced, which is more useful than either alone.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the government wanted the trials to achieve, then ask which source was itself part of achieving it.","A source written to persuade is strong evidence of the persuasion, so judge purpose as a strength here, not simply as bias.","Begin with Source C's purpose and say in the first sentence what that makes it useful for."],"codes":["30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The reasons for, and the importance of, the show trials","30-3 — 3 Propaganda and censorship: Official culture, socialist realism, control of education and religion"]},{"q":"Explain why Stalin, rather than Trotsky, became the leader of the Soviet Union in the years 1924–29. You may use the following in your answer: • the post of General Secretary • Lenin's Testament. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12) needs analytical explanation directed at causation with precisely selected knowledge. Credit the Secretariat and the Lenin Enrolment, Trotsky's weaknesses and resignation, Socialism in One Country, the shifting alliances and the ban on factions. Stimulus cap: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"Stalin won because he held the offices that decided other men's careers, because his rivals' weaknesses were as important as his own strengths, and because he offered the party an argument it wanted to hear. The first reason is the party machine. He had been General Secretary since April 1922, and the post controlled appointments, transfers and promotions; the Lenin Enrolment of 1924 and 1925 brought hundreds of thousands of new and inexperienced members into the party, and the officials who placed them answered to his Secretariat. By the Fifteenth Party Congress of December 1927, which expelled the United Opposition, most delegates owed their positions to him, so votes that looked like the party's judgement were in part his own appointments speaking. The second reason is Trotsky. He had been a Menshevik until 1917 and the Old Bolsheviks never forgot it; as the creator of the Red Army he frightened colleagues who feared a Russian Bonaparte; he missed Lenin's funeral in January 1924 while Stalin appeared as chief mourner; and he resigned as Commissar for War in January 1925 rather than fight for the post. Lenin's Testament, which had called for Stalin's removal as General Secretary, might have destroyed him, but the Central Committee agreed in May 1924 not to publish it, partly because it criticised everyone. The third reason, which neither bullet point prompts, is that Stalin fought one enemy at a time with a doctrine attached. Socialism in One Country promised that the revolution could survive alone, which sounded like relief beside Trotsky's permanent revolution; the ban on factions passed in 1921 made organised disagreement a party crime; and he allied with Zinoviev and Kamenev to isolate Trotsky, then with Bukharin and the right to defeat them in 1925 and 1926, before turning on Bukharin himself over the end of the NEP in 1928 and 1929. The machine gave him the votes, his rivals gave him the openings, and his programme gave the votes a reason.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Leadership contests are won by more than ability; ask what Stalin controlled that the others did not.","Plan three reasons of different kinds — organisational, personal and ideological — so the answer analyses instead of listing.","Open on what the Secretariat actually allowed him to do, and finish the paragraph with its effect on a party congress rather than on Stalin's feelings."],"codes":["30-3 — 1 The struggle for power, 1924–28: The strengths and weaknesses of Stalin and his rivals","30-3 — 1 The struggle for power, 1924–28: The reasons for the emergence of Stalin as leader"]},{"q":"Explain why the purges spread through Soviet society in the years 1936–38. You may use the following in your answer: • the murder of Kirov • the NKVD. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12) needs sustained causal analysis with precise knowledge. Credit the Kirov decree, Yezhov's appointment and the 1937 quotas, denunciation from below, the demand for camp labour and the fear of war. Stimulus cap: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"The purges spread because a political killing was turned into a licence, because the machine built to act on it was rewarded for finding more enemies, and because ordinary people found uses of their own for it. The first reason is Kirov's murder. The popular Leningrad leader was shot at the Smolny Institute on 1 December 1934, and within days a decree allowed terrorist cases to be investigated quickly and sentenced without appeal or right of defence. That gave the state a legal instrument and a story: if a member of the Politburo could be assassinated, enemies must be everywhere, and Zinoviev and Kamenev were arrested as the moral authors of the crime. The second reason is the NKVD itself. Under Yezhov, appointed in September 1936, the police were set targets: in the summer of 1937 quotas for arrest and execution went out to every region, and officers were judged by the cases they closed. Sentencing passed to troikas who heard cases in minutes, and a confession taken by night questioning was enough, so the safest way to survive as an officer was to exceed the figure. The third reason, which neither bullet point prompts, is that the purges served purposes outside the police. The Five-Year Plans had produced accidents, shortages and broken machinery, and wrecking supplied an explanation that blamed nobody in authority; the Gulag needed labour for gold at Kolyma and coal at Vorkuta, and arrests supplied it; and with Germany rearming after 1933 the leadership was sufficiently frightened of a fifth column to destroy its own officer corps, shooting Tukhachevsky and seven other commanders in June 1937. Beneath all this, denunciation by neighbours and workmates gave a private motive to a public campaign. The murder gave the excuse, the police gave the method, and the plan, the camps and the communal flat gave the reasons for using it on such a scale.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Distinguish the trigger from the causes: an assassination explains a wave of arrests, not two years of them.","Build three reasons of different kinds, one political, one institutional, one economic or social, and make each end with its effect on the scale of the purge.","Start with what the December 1934 decree allowed the state to do that it could not do before."],"codes":["30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The reasons for, nature of and consequences of the purges","30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The work of the secret police (NKVD)"]},{"q":"Explain why the Soviet government held show trials in the years 1936–38. You may use the following in your answer: • the Old Bolsheviks • Trotsky. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12) needs analysis directed at purpose and causation. Credit the destruction of rivals with confessions, Trotsky in exile as the author of every plot, wrecking as an excuse for economic failure, the warning to officials and the foreign audience. Stimulus cap: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"The trials were held because a confession does work that a bullet cannot. The first reason is the Old Bolsheviks. Zinoviev, Kamenev and Bukharin had known Stalin as an equal and had been named in Lenin's Testament beside him, and Rykov, a former ally who had headed the government until 1930, stood in the dock with Bukharin in March 1938; men of that standing could always be the nucleus of an alternative leadership. Killing them quietly would have left their reputations intact; trying them in public in August 1936, January 1937 and March 1938, with Vyshinsky prosecuting and the accused confessing to terrorism and sabotage, destroyed the reputations first. The second reason is Trotsky. Deported from the USSR in 1929, he went on attacking Stalin in print from abroad, where he could not be arrested. The trials answered him by making him the author of every disaster at home: each set of defendants was said to have acted on his orders, so that Trotskyism became a crime rather than an argument and any criticism could be filed under it. The third reason, which neither bullet point prompts, is that the trials explained the country to itself. The Five-Year Plans had brought accidents, shortages and shoddy work, and wrecking by hidden enemies explained all of it without blaming the plan or the planner; the confessions also warned every official in the audience that rank, record and revolutionary service were no protection, which was exactly the lesson a purge of the administration required. There was a fourth audience abroad: foreign journalists and diplomats were invited, and in the years when the USSR was seeking allies against Hitler the spectacle of a state uncovering traitors legally was meant to look like justice rather than terror. The trials were therefore not a by-product of the purges but their argument, made in public and in the defendants' own voices.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what a public trial achieves that a secret execution does not, and answer that question in every paragraph.","Group the reasons by audience: the party, the country, and the world outside.","Begin with why the Old Bolsheviks in particular had to be discredited rather than simply removed."],"codes":["30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The reasons for, and the importance of, the show trials","30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The reasons for, nature of and consequences of the purges"]},{"q":"Explain why the Soviet government controlled the arts, education and religion in the 1930s. You may use the following in your answer: • socialist realism • the League of the Militant Godless. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12) needs causal analysis of why control was needed. Credit socialist realism and the Writers' Union, Glavlit and doctored photographs, schools, the Pioneers and Komsomol, the Short Course, and the campaign against the churches with the 1937 census. Stimulus cap: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"The state controlled what people saw, learned and worshipped because it was asking them to accept enormous upheaval and could not afford a rival explanation of it. The first reason is that culture was needed to make the Five-Year Plans desirable. Independent literary groups were abolished in 1932 and a single Union of Soviet Writers created; at its first congress in August 1934 socialist realism became the only permitted method, so painting, novels and film had to be clear, optimistic and full of the future, from Mukhina's Worker and Kolkhoz Woman at the Paris exhibition of 1937 to canvases of tractor drivers and steel pourers. Hardship shown as sacrifice for a certain future is easier to bear than hardship shown as failure. The second reason is that a rival authority had to be removed. The League of the Militant Godless ran anti-religious campaigns, churches were closed or turned into stores and clubs, and the Cathedral of Christ the Saviour was dynamited in December 1931; a priest who spoke of a different loyalty was competition, and the Constitution of 1936 revealed the asymmetry by granting freedom of worship alongside freedom of anti-religious propaganda. The third reason, which neither bullet point prompts, is the generation being made in the schools. Discipline, textbooks and examinations returned in the 1930s, the Pioneers and the Komsomol filled the hours outside lessons, the story of Pavlik Morozov taught children that loyalty to the state outranked loyalty to a father, and from 1938 party history came from a single official Short Course, so a school-leaver had never read another version. Censorship held the whole structure together, since Glavlit cleared everything printed and photographs were retouched to remove men who had fallen. That the census of January 1937 found over half of adults still calling themselves believers, and was suppressed for saying so, shows why the effort had to be continuous.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the government feared people might think, and let each reason answer that.","Separate the arts, the schools and the churches, and give each a different kind of reason rather than repeating control three times.","Open with what socialist realism required a painting or a novel to show, and then say what that was for."],"codes":["30-3 — 3 Propaganda and censorship: Official culture, socialist realism, control of education and religion","30-3 — 4 The Cult of Stalin: How the Cult of Stalin was achieved"]},{"q":"Explain why the Soviet Union adopted a new Constitution in 1936. You may use the following in your answer: • the outside world • the Communist Party. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12) needs analysis of purpose and causation. Credit the claim that socialism had been achieved, the search for allies against Hitler, the Party's monopoly built into the document, the single-list elections of December 1937 and the contrast with the terror. Stimulus cap: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"The Constitution adopted on 5 December 1936 was written to be read rather than obeyed, and it had three audiences. The first was abroad. With Hitler in power since 1933 and the USSR having joined the League of Nations in 1934, the Soviet government was looking for allies among the Western democracies and for support from the Popular Front movements inside them. A document promising a vote to every citizen over eighteen by secret ballot, freedom of speech, press, assembly and worship, and rights to work, rest and education, allowed the USSR to present itself as the most democratic state in the world, which mattered when foreign communists were arguing that the Soviet Union, not Germany, was the friend of liberty. The second audience was at home. Collectivisation and the first two Five-Year Plans had been justified as the building of socialism; declaring socialism achieved and extending the vote even to former nobles, priests and kulaks announced that the class war had been won and that the state now belonged to everyone. The third purpose, which neither bullet point prompts, was to change nothing while appearing to change everything. The Communist Party was written into the document as the leading force and remained the only party, so the elections to the Supreme Soviet in December 1937 offered a single list of candidates and produced reported turnouts above ninety per cent; no court could be appealed to against the NKVD, and the same month brought the height of the Terror. The timing makes the purpose plain: the Constitution was adopted between the first and second Moscow show trials, and men who worked on drafting it, Bukharin among them, were shot within two years. Its function was legitimacy, at home and abroad, at a moment when the reality would have supplied neither.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["A constitution can be a piece of propaganda; ask who was meant to read this one.","Handle the foreign audience, the domestic audience and what the document actually preserved as three separate reasons.","Start by naming what the Constitution promised, then ask at once what the Party's position did to those promises."],"codes":["30-3 — 3 Propaganda and censorship: The new Constitution of 1936","30-3 — 3 Propaganda and censorship: Official culture, socialist realism, control of education and religion"]},{"q":"Explain why a cult of personality developed around Stalin in the years 1929–41. You may use the following in your answer: • Lenin's reputation • the Five-Year Plans. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12) needs causal analysis of the cult's purposes. Credit Stalin as Lenin's heir, a fixed point during upheaval, credit for success and distance from failure, and praise as proof of loyalty during the Terror. Stimulus cap: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"The cult was built, not grown, and it was built because Stalin needed authority that his record could not supply. The first reason is Lenin. Stalin had no part in 1917 to match Trotsky's, so his claim had to be inheritance: Lenin's body was embalmed and displayed, Petrograd became Leningrad in 1924, Stalin delivered lectures on the foundations of Leninism, and portraits and later the Short Course of 1938 placed him at Lenin's shoulder through the revolution. Being the pupil made him the heir, and made criticism of him disloyalty to Lenin. The second reason is the Five-Year Plans. From 1928 the country was being rebuilt at a speed that broke families and villages, and a population that has lost its bearings needs a fixed point; the cult supplied one. Successes could be credited to him personally — the Moscow Metro opened in 1935, the new dams and steelworks, record-breaking workers such as Alexei Stakhanov in the Donbass in August 1935 — while shortages and accidents could be blamed on local officials and wreckers, so that the leader was never responsible for the cost of his own policy. The third reason, which neither bullet point prompts, is the Terror. Once the purges began, public enthusiasm became the cheapest available proof of loyalty: officials competed to name him in speeches, newspapers printed his name in heavy type and gave him titles such as Vozhd and Father of the Peoples, and applause at meetings went on because nobody dared stop first. The cult and the fear reinforced each other, and censorship made the picture seamless, since Glavlit cleared everything printed and photographs were retouched to delete men who had fallen, including Yezhov, who had run the purges himself. The cult, in short, replaced the authority of a revolutionary record with the authority of an image the state could control.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["The cult is a policy, not a mood; ask what problem each part of it solved for Stalin.","Three different jobs make three paragraphs: inheriting Lenin, holding a society together during upheaval, and surviving the purge.","Open on why a man with no leading role in October 1917 needed to be shown at Lenin's side."],"codes":["30-3 — 4 The Cult of Stalin: The meaning of the Cult of Stalin and reasons for its introduction","30-3 — 4 The Cult of Stalin: How the Cult of Stalin was achieved"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the reasons for the purges in the Soviet Union in the years 1936–38? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points are printed. Level 4 (13-16) needs an explained evaluation reviewing both views, precise analysis of how each conveys its view, precisely selected context and a justified judgement. Credit quotas and denunciation for agreement; the Kirov decree, the trials, the army purge and the halt of 1938 against.","sol":"Interpretation 2 argues that the purges were 'made in a thousand offices and factory meetings': quotas were handed down to regional bosses who asked for them to be raised because a low figure looked like slackness, ordinary people denounced neighbours and rivals for reasons of their own, and local NKVD men judged on closed cases took what was offered, so that Stalin 'lit the fire' but 'did not choose most of its victims'. The test of that claim must be separated into two questions: who set the Terror going and could end it, and who decided which particular people were destroyed. On the second question Interpretation 2 is convincing. The quotas sent to the regions in the summer of 1937 named numbers, not people, and the names were supplied locally, so a district officer filled his figure from whatever came to hand. What came to hand was denunciation, and the motives behind it were often domestic: a room in a shared flat, a place at the bench, a rival for a post. Officers were assessed on cases closed, sentencing passed to troikas who heard cases in minutes, and confession extracted by night questioning was treated as proof, which meant the machine could manufacture guilt to order without any instruction from Moscow about individuals. On that reading the Terror in any town took the shape the people of that town gave it, and the roughly 680,000 death sentences carried out in 1937 and 1938 cannot possibly have been chosen one by one from the centre. Interpretation 1 is right about what Interpretation 2 underplays. The Terror had a beginning, a direction and an end, and all three were decided at the top. The decree that followed Kirov's murder on 1 December 1934 created the legal instrument; Yezhov was appointed in September 1936 and removed at the end of 1938, after which the arrests fell away and he was himself shot, which is exactly Interpretation 1's point that a fire that can be put out on one man's word is an instrument rather than an accident. Certain victims were unmistakably chosen centrally: Zinoviev, Kamenev, Bukharin and Rykov were tried in Moscow in public, and Tukhachevsky and seven senior commanders were shot in June 1937, decisions no district office could have made or wanted. The two also convey their views in ways that carry the argument. Interpretation 2 uses the language of spreading and of many hands, which removes design; Interpretation 1 uses the language of signature, script and telephone call, which removes everyone except Stalin. Each wording quietly makes its case before the evidence is offered. My judgement is that Interpretation 2 explains the scale and the randomness of the purges but not their existence. Without the decision at the centre there is no quota, no Yezhov and no wave of arrests at all; with it, the choice of most victims did indeed pass to frightened officials and to neighbours with scores to settle. Interpretation 2 is therefore right about how the Terror spread and wrong to imply that this reduces the responsibility of the man who started it and stopped it.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Work out first what Interpretation 2 would need to be true, then decide what would count as evidence for and against it.","Use Interpretation 1 as the case against, and support both sides with dated detail rather than with the interpretations restated.","Open by putting Interpretation 2's claim in your own words and naming the criterion your judgement will use."],"codes":["30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The reasons for, nature of and consequences of the purges","30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The work of the secret police (NKVD)","30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The reasons for, and the importance of, the show trials"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part played by Stalin in the terror of the 1930s? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points are printed. Level 4 (13-16) needs both views reviewed, precise analysis of how each is conveyed, precise context and a justified judgement. Credit Stalin as initiator and terminator, the show trials and the army purge, set against local quota-raising and denunciation from below.","sol":"Interpretation 2 gives Stalin a limited part: he 'lit the fire and could put it out', but the purges were 'made in a thousand offices and factory meetings' and he 'did not choose most of its victims'. Interpretation 1 gives him the whole of it, a Terror that 'bears one man's signature', with trials 'scripted for him' and lists sent to his desk. The fair way to judge between them is to ask what a leader has to do before a wave of killing can be called his: begin it, shape it, select its victims, or end it. Interpretation 2 is clearly right that he did not select most of them. In the summer of 1937 the regions received numerical quotas, and a district office filled its figure from the denunciations neighbours and workmates supplied; the motives were often private, a room in a communal flat or a promotion blocked, and officers assessed on closed cases had every reason to accept what was offered. Sentencing by troikas in minutes, on confessions produced by questioning in relays, meant that nobody in Moscow needed to know a name for a sentence to be carried out. No individual, however powerful, could have picked hundreds of thousands of people. On beginning and ending, though, Interpretation 1 has the better of the argument, and Interpretation 2 concedes more than it appears to. The decree that followed Kirov's murder on 1 December 1934 opened the way to sentence without appeal; Yezhov was appointed in September 1936 and the two worst years followed; he was removed at the end of 1938, arrests fell sharply, and he was shot in his turn, which shows a machine that answered to its owner. Shaping matters too. The choice to destroy the Old Bolsheviks in public, so that Zinoviev, Kamenev, Bukharin and Rykov confessed before an audience that included foreign correspondents, and the decision to shoot Tukhachevsky and seven senior commanders in June 1937, were not the work of provincial officials; they were made where the alternative leadership and the alternative power were, and only Stalin had reason to want both gone. The wording of each text carries its case. Interpretation 2's fire spreads by itself once lit, which softens the arsonist's responsibility; Interpretation 1's signature, script and telephone call leave no room for anyone else's choices, which is equally one-sided, since it cannot explain why the Terror ran through villages and factories that the centre had never heard of. My judgement is that Interpretation 2 describes Stalin's role accurately in one respect and understates it in the more important one. He was not the chooser of most victims, as Interpretation 2 says; but he created the powers, appointed and dismissed the man who used them, selected the enemies who mattered politically, and closed the whole thing down when it had gone far enough. Responsibility for a fire belongs to whoever lit it deliberately and could have put it out sooner, so Interpretation 2's account of the mechanism should be accepted and its implication about Stalin's part rejected.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["The interpretations disagree about degree, not about whether Stalin was involved; find the precise point where they part.","Break the question into what a leader can do — start, shape, choose, stop — and test each against what you know.","Begin by stating in your own words how much of the Terror Interpretation 2 leaves in Stalin's hands."],"codes":["30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The reasons for, nature of and consequences of the purges","30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The reasons for, and the importance of, the show trials","30-3 — 4 The Cult of Stalin: The meaning of the Cult of Stalin and reasons for its introduction"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part played by ordinary Soviet people in the spread of the purges? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points are printed. Level 4 (13-16) needs both views reviewed with precise analysis and context and a justified judgement. Credit denunciation, careerism and local quota-raising for agreement; the state's creation of the incentives, the central trials and the army purge against.","sol":"Interpretation 2 makes ordinary people participants rather than only victims: denunciations were written 'some from belief, many to be rid of a neighbour in a shared flat, a rival for a post, an inconvenient husband', and the shape the Terror took in any town 'was decided by the people who lived there'. Interpretation 1 has no room for them at all; its Terror is scripted at the top and switched off by a telephone call. The criterion for judging between them should be whether the participation of ordinary people changed who suffered, not merely whether it happened. A great deal supports Interpretation 2. When the regions were given numerical quotas in the summer of 1937, the numbers had to be turned into names, and the material for that came from below: accusations at meetings on vigilance, letters to the NKVD, denunciations by colleagues who wanted a post or a room. Housing in the cities was desperately overcrowded after the rush of workers into industry, so the disappearance of a family could mean space for another; a supervisor removed was a promotion for the man beneath him. Children were taught that loyalty to the state outranked loyalty to a family through the story of Pavlik Morozov, the boy said to have denounced his father, which shows the state actively cultivating the habit. Denunciation was not confined to the powerless either: regional party bosses asked for their quotas to be raised, because a modest figure looked like a lack of vigilance and that was itself dangerous. Two things qualify the picture. First, the incentives were made in Moscow: people denounced because a system rewarded denunciation and punished silence, so participation was a response to terror as much as a cause of it, and a district officer could not ignore a signed statement once it had been written, which turned a private quarrel into a state case automatically. Second, Interpretation 2 cannot explain the victims who mattered most politically. No neighbour denounced Zinoviev, Kamenev, Bukharin or Rykov into the dock of a Moscow courtroom, and no factory meeting shot Tukhachevsky and seven senior commanders in June 1937; those decisions were taken where Interpretation 1 says they were. My judgement is that Interpretation 2 is right about the spread of the purges and incomplete about their direction. Ordinary people decided a great many of the names, and without their denunciations the quotas of 1937 and 1938 could not have been filled, so the Terror was genuinely a social process as well as a political one. But they acted inside a structure of rewards and fears built deliberately at the centre, and the killings that changed the politics of the country were chosen there. Interpretation 2 should be accepted as an account of how the Terror reached into a street, and set beside Interpretation 1 rather than in place of it.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Participation and responsibility are not the same thing; decide early which the question is really about.","Test Interpretation 2 by asking which victims it can explain and which it cannot.","Open by putting Interpretation 2's claim about denunciation into your own words and naming the criterion you will judge it by."],"codes":["30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The reasons for, nature of and consequences of the purges","30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The work of the secret police (NKVD)","30-3 — 3 Propaganda and censorship: Official culture, socialist realism, control of education and religion"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In which month and year did Lenin die?","a":"January 1924."},{"q":"Whose murder in Leningrad on 1 December 1934 was used to justify the terror that followed?","a":"Sergei Kirov, the leader of the Leningrad party organisation, shot at the Smolny Institute."},{"q":"Which senior Red Army commander was tried in secret and shot in June 1937?","a":"Marshal Mikhail Tukhachevsky."},{"q":"Which party alone could put up candidates in the elections held under the Constitution of 1936?","a":"The Communist Party, so the elections of December 1937 offered a single list of candidates."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Lenin's Testament","back":"Dictated in December 1922 with a postscript in January 1923. It criticised all the leaders and asked for Stalin's removal as General Secretary. The Central Committee agreed in May 1924 not to publish it.","code":"30-3 — 1 The struggle for power, 1924–28: The reasons for the emergence of Stalin as leader","open":false},{"front":"Stalin's power base as General Secretary","back":"Held from April 1922. The post controlled appointments, transfers and promotions, so the Lenin Enrolment of 1924 and 1925 filled the party with members whose careers ran through his Secretariat.","code":"30-3 — 1 The struggle for power, 1924–28: The strengths and weaknesses of Stalin and his rivals","open":false},{"front":"Trotsky's strengths and weaknesses","back":"Organised the October rising and created the Red Army; the best writer and speaker in the party. But a Menshevik until 1917, feared as a possible Bonaparte, arrogant, often ill, and uninterested in committee work.","code":"30-3 — 1 The struggle for power, 1924–28: The strengths and weaknesses of Stalin and his rivals","open":false},{"front":"Kamenev, Zinoviev and Bukharin","back":"Kamenev ran Moscow, Zinoviev ran Leningrad and the Comintern; both had opposed the October seizure of power. Bukharin was the party's leading theorist and defender of the NEP, but had no machine of his own.","code":"30-3 — 1 The struggle for power, 1924–28: The strengths and weaknesses of Stalin and his rivals","open":false},{"front":"The defeat of the oppositions, 1925 to 1929","back":"Stalin used Zinoviev and Kamenev against Trotsky, then Bukharin against them. Trotsky and Zinoviev were expelled from the party in November 1927, Trotsky deported in 1929, Bukharin off the Politburo in November 1929.","code":"30-3 — 1 The struggle for power, 1924–28: The reasons for the emergence of Stalin as leader","open":false},{"front":"Socialism in One Country","back":"Stalin's doctrine that the USSR could build socialism alone, against Trotsky's permanent revolution. It promised that past sacrifices had been worth it, and made Trotsky look like a man offering more danger.","code":"30-3 — 1 The struggle for power, 1924–28: The reasons for the emergence of Stalin as leader","open":false},{"front":"The murder of Kirov, 1 December 1934","back":"Sergei Kirov, the popular Leningrad leader, was shot at the Smolny Institute. A decree that month allowed terrorist cases to be sentenced quickly without appeal, and mass arrests followed.","code":"30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The reasons for, nature of and consequences of the purges","open":false},{"front":"The Great Terror, 1937 to 1938","back":"Quotas for arrest and execution went to every region in the summer of 1937. NKVD records list roughly 680,000 death sentences carried out in the two years, and well over a million people were in camps by 1939.","code":"30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The reasons for, nature of and consequences of the purges","open":false},{"front":"The purge of the armed forces","back":"Marshal Tukhachevsky and seven senior commanders were tried in secret and shot in June 1937. Three of the five marshals and most senior commanders followed, weakening the army for Finland in 1939 and Germany in 1941.","code":"30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The reasons for, nature of and consequences of the purges","open":false},{"front":"The NKVD","back":"The secret police reorganised in 1934, led by Yagoda, then Yezhov from September 1936, then Beria from the end of 1938. It arrested at night, took confessions in relays, and used troikas that sentenced in minutes.","code":"30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The work of the secret police (NKVD)","open":false},{"front":"The Gulag and the labour camps","back":"The camp administration supplied forced labour for gold at Kolyma, coal at Vorkuta and timber in the north, and for projects such as the White Sea to Baltic canal, opened in 1933, where many thousands died.","code":"30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The work of the secret police (NKVD)","open":false},{"front":"The Moscow show trials, 1936 to 1938","back":"Zinoviev and Kamenev in August 1936; Pyatakov and Radek in January 1937; Bukharin, Rykov and Yagoda among twenty-one defendants in March 1938. Vyshinsky prosecuted, the accused confessed, foreign correspondents watched, and nearly all were shot.","code":"30-3 — 2 The use of terror in the 1930s: The reasons for, and the importance of, the show trials","open":false},{"front":"Socialist realism","back":"Declared the only permitted method at the first Congress of Soviet Writers in August 1934: art realistic in style, optimistic, easy to understand and showing the socialist future, such as Mukhina's Worker and Kolkhoz Woman of 1937.","code":"30-3 — 3 Propaganda and censorship: Official culture, socialist realism, control of education and religion","open":false},{"front":"The Constitution of 1936","back":"Adopted on 5 December 1936. It promised a secret ballot for all over eighteen and wide freedoms, but named the Communist Party as the leading force, so the elections of December 1937 offered one list of candidates.","code":"30-3 — 3 Propaganda and censorship: The new Constitution of 1936","open":false},{"front":"The Cult of Stalin","back":"Launched around his official fiftieth birthday in December 1929. Portraits, statues, titles such as Vozhd and Father of the Peoples, doctored photographs, renamed towns and the Short Course of 1938 made him Lenin's infallible heir.","code":"30-3 — 4 The Cult of Stalin: The meaning of the Cult of Stalin and reasons for its introduction","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The sources and interpretations written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Source A: From a monthly report sent by the head of a district office of the NKVD in a provincial town to his regional directorate, October 1937. Written for this room in the style of such a report; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — Comrade Chief, the figure set for this district for the last quarter has been met in full, and I ask that the new figure be confirmed quickly, as arrests are already being made against it. Most cases now come to us as denunciations written by neighbours and by workmates, and a good number of them, on investigation, turn out to concern a quarrel over a room or a place at the bench rather than any enemy activity; nevertheless a signed statement cannot be left without action. The investigators work in shifts through the night and confessions are obtained in almost every case, although the men then repeat them differently before the troika. Our cells hold three times the number they were built for. I must ask again for two more investigators and for paper.","Source B: From the private diary of a schoolteacher in a provincial Soviet town, kept during 1937. Written for this room in the style of such a diary; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — March. We were kept back after lessons for a meeting on vigilance. The director read out the names of two teachers who have been removed and asked whether any of us had noticed anything; nobody spoke for a long time, and then three people spoke at once. I said nothing, and have been ashamed of it since. October. The new history textbook came today, one for every class, and we are to teach from it and from nothing else. I have locked the old books in the cupboard, because a teacher in the next street was denounced for keeping a volume with a photograph of a man who is now an enemy. November. My sister's husband was taken in September and she has heard nothing. She queues at the prison twice a week to hand in a parcel, and if it is accepted she knows he is alive. At the parade we carried the portraits and shouted with everybody else.","Source C: From a report in a Soviet newspaper on the opening of the trial of the bloc of rightists and Trotskyites, March 1938. Written for this room in the style of such a report; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — The hall was full an hour before the court sat. In the dock are men whose names were once printed beside Lenin's, and who have now confessed that for years they worked for foreign intelligence services, wrecking our factories and plotting the murder of our leaders. The prosecutor put his questions and the accused answered them without denial. Delegations from the Moscow factories have sent resolutions demanding the highest measure of punishment, and these were read out to applause. Foreign correspondents were present throughout and are free to report what they have seen: that the accused were given every opportunity to speak, and that they condemned themselves out of their own mouths. There is no place in our country for such vermin.","Interpretation 1: From a study of Stalin's government in the 1930s, by a historian writing in the 1960s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — The Terror was made at the top, and it bears one man's signature. Stalin had never forgiven the Old Bolsheviks who had known him as an equal, and after Kirov's murder in December 1934 he used the fear of assassination as a licence. The show trials were scripted for him, the secret police reported to him, and the lists of names to be shot were sent to his desk to be approved. When he wanted the Terror stopped, at the end of 1938, it stopped, and the man who had run it was arrested in his turn. A machine that could be switched off by one telephone call was not a system out of control; it was a dictator's instrument, used to make every official's life depend on his favour.","Interpretation 2: From a general history of Soviet society under Stalin, by a historian writing in the 1990s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — Orders came from Moscow, but the Terror was made in a thousand offices and factory meetings. Quotas were handed down to regional bosses who asked for them to be raised, because a low figure looked like slackness and slackness was itself a crime. Beneath them, ordinary people wrote denunciations: some from belief, many to be rid of a neighbour in a shared flat, a rival for a post, an inconvenient husband. Local police officers, judged on the cases they closed, took what was offered. Stalin lit the fire and could put it out, but he did not choose most of its victims, and the shape the Terror took in any town was decided by the people who lived there.","How the room uses them: Source A is the inference source for Question 1. Sources B and C are the contemporary pair for the 8-mark usefulness question, 3(a), and may be used in 3(c). Interpretations 1 and 2 are the pair for 3(b), 3(c) and all three versions of 3(d). Every quotation in the model answers of this room comes from the five texts above, apart from the short practice pair of interpretations printed inside the worked example on Question 3(b), which is used only there."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 3 Question 3(d), 16 marks)","items":["Question: 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the way the Terror was brought to an end in 1938? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]. This is one paragraph from the middle of the answer, putting the case against Interpretation 2.","Interpretation 2 allows that Stalin 'lit the fire and could put it out', but the Terror it describes, made in a thousand offices and factory meetings, cannot explain how quickly the fire went out. The ending was an order, not an exhaustion. Beria was made Yezhov's deputy at the NKVD in August 1938; on 17 November 1938 a joint decree of the government and the Central Committee halted the mass operations, ended the work of the troikas that had been sentencing without trial, and put arrests back under the supervision of the procurators; Beria took over the NKVD on 25 November, and Yezhov was arrested in April 1939 and shot in February 1940. Arrests and shootings fell away within weeks of a piece of paper. If the shape the Terror took in any town had really been decided by the people who lived there, a decree read in Moscow could not have emptied the quotas out of a thousand offices at once, because denunciation and local ambition do not stop on a date. What the ending shows is the point Interpretation 1 makes with its telephone call: the local energy Interpretation 2 describes was real, and it did the arresting, but it ran inside limits that only the centre set, raised and finally closed.","Why this is Level 4: it 'provides an explained evaluation reviewing the alternative views in coming to a substantiated judgement' — the counter-case is built out of the ending itself and then measured against Interpretation 2's own claim rather than simply asserted against it. The analysis is precise about how the difference of view is conveyed, since it turns Interpretation 2's own image of a fire against it. The contextual knowledge is precisely selected rather than general: Beria's appointment as deputy in August 1938, the decree of 17 November 1938 and what it stopped, the handover of the NKVD on 25 November, and the arrest and shooting of Yezhov. The line of reasoning is sustained from the first sentence to the last, and the closing sentence judges instead of summarising."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same as above — 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the way the Terror was brought to an end in 1938?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks].","Interpretation 2 says that Stalin lit the fire and could put it out, and I agree with this about the ending. The purges did stop in 1938, and this was because Stalin decided that they had gone far enough and that too many people had been arrested by then. Interpretation 1 says that when he wanted the Terror stopped it stopped and that the man who had run it was arrested in his turn, which shows that he was in charge of it all along. Yezhov was blamed for everything that had gone wrong, even though he had only been doing what he was told, and this was useful for Stalin because it made it look like somebody else's fault. Overall I think both interpretations are right that Stalin ended the Terror, because he was the only person who could have done.","Why this is Level 2 and not Level 3: it does offer 'valid evaluative comment to agree with or counter the interpretation' and it does select details from both texts, which is what Level 2 requires. What it lacks is Level 3's 'relevant contextual knowledge used directly to support the evaluation': 'gone far enough', 'too many people' and 'doing what he was told' sit where the decree of 17 November 1938, the end of the troikas, the handover of the NKVD to Beria in that month and the collapse of arrests in 1939 should be. It also never shows how the interpretations convey their views, and the judgement is announced in the final sentence rather than reasoned towards, so 'its justification is insecure or undeveloped and a line of reasoning is not sustained'. Turning it into a Level 3 answer means replacing every general phrase with a dated fact that does evaluative work."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the struggle for power, 1924-29 — Stalin, Trotsky, Kamenev, Zinoviev and Bukharin","items":["Lenin died on 21 January 1924 after a series of strokes that had kept him out of government for much of 1923. Petrograd was renamed Leningrad in the same month and his body was embalmed and put on display.","Stalin, born Iosif Dzhugashvili at Gori in Georgia in 1878, had been General Secretary of the Communist Party since April 1922; he had also been Commissar for Nationalities from 1917 to 1923.","Lenin's Testament, dictated in December 1922 with a postscript in January 1923, criticised every leading figure and asked the party to remove Stalin from the post of General Secretary as too rude. The Central Committee decided in May 1924 not to publish it.","At Lenin's funeral on 27 January 1924 Stalin took a leading part as mourner and speaker. Trotsky was in the south and did not attend; he later claimed he had been given the wrong date.","The Lenin Enrolment of 1924 and 1925 brought hundreds of thousands of new, mostly young working-class members into the party, and their registration and promotion ran through Stalin's Secretariat.","Trotsky had been a Menshevik until 1917, then organised the October rising and built the Red Army as Commissar for War. He resigned that post in January 1925.","Zinoviev led the Leningrad party organisation and chaired the Comintern; Kamenev led the Moscow organisation. Both had opposed the seizure of power in October 1917, which was used against them.","The triumvirate of Stalin, Zinoviev and Kamenev worked against Trotsky from 1923. Zinoviev and Kamenev then formed the New Opposition and were defeated at the Fourteenth Party Congress in December 1925, with Bukharin's support for Stalin.","Trotsky, Zinoviev and Kamenev combined as the United Opposition in 1926. Zinoviev lost his Politburo seat in July 1926 and Trotsky lost his in October 1926; both were expelled from the party in November 1927 after opposition demonstrations on the tenth anniversary of the revolution.","Trotsky was exiled to Alma-Ata in January 1928 and deported from the USSR in 1929. He was murdered in Mexico in August 1940.","Socialism in One Country, Stalin's answer to Trotsky's permanent revolution, argued that the USSR could build socialism without a revolution abroad. The ban on factions passed at the Tenth Party Congress in 1921 let him treat organised disagreement as a party offence.","Stalin turned on the right over the grain crisis and the end of the NEP: Bukharin lost his Politburo seat in November 1929, Tomsky lost the trade unions, and Rykov was replaced as head of the government by Molotov in December 1930.","Stalin's official fiftieth birthday in December 1929 was celebrated with pages of tributes in the press, and is usually taken as the moment his personal ascendancy became public."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the purges of the 1930s, the NKVD and the labour camps","items":["Sergei Kirov, the popular head of the Leningrad party, was shot at the Smolny Institute on 1 December 1934 by Leonid Nikolaev. A decree issued that month allowed terrorist cases to be investigated at speed and sentenced without appeal or defence.","The secret police was reorganised in 1934, when the OGPU was absorbed into the People's Commissariat for Internal Affairs, the NKVD.","Genrikh Yagoda headed the NKVD from 1934; Nikolai Yezhov took over in September 1936 and gave his name to the Yezhovshchina; Lavrenti Beria replaced him at the end of 1938. Yagoda was shot in 1938 and Yezhov in 1940.","In the summer of 1937 quotas for arrest, execution and camp sentences were sent to every region, and regional leaders sometimes asked for their figures to be raised.","Most cases were decided not in court but by a troika, a panel of three officials who sentenced in minutes without a defence; confessions were produced by questioning in relays, sleep deprivation and threats to families.","NKVD records list roughly 680,000 death sentences carried out in 1937 and 1938, the two worst years of the Terror.","Marshal Mikhail Tukhachevsky and seven other senior commanders were tried in secret and shot in June 1937, accused of spying for Germany. Three of the five marshals and most of the army's senior commanders were removed, and tens of thousands of officers were arrested or dismissed.","Of the delegates to the Seventeenth Party Congress of 1934, more than half were later arrested, and 98 of the 139 members and candidates of the Central Committee elected there, 70 per cent, were arrested and shot — figures given by Khrushchev in his speech of 1956.","The Gulag, the main camp administration, held well over a million prisoners by 1939. Camps supplied labour for gold at Kolyma, coal at Vorkuta and timber in the far north.","The White Sea to Baltic canal was opened in 1933 after about twenty months of work, dug largely by hand by prisoners, many thousands of whom died.","Consequences reached into the war: the Red Army performed badly against Finland in the Winter War of November 1939 to March 1940, and the loss of experienced commanders was blamed in part for the disasters that followed the German invasion of 22 June 1941.","Denunciation was part of the machinery: accusations from neighbours and workmates, often driven by overcrowded housing or competition for a post, supplied many of the names needed to fill the quotas."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the show trials of 1936-38","items":["August 1936: Zinoviev, Kamenev and fourteen others were tried as a Trotskyite-Zinovievite terrorist centre, accused of organising Kirov's murder and of plotting against Stalin. All confessed and were shot.","January 1937: Pyatakov, Radek and others were tried as an anti-Soviet Trotskyite centre, accused of wrecking industry on Trotsky's orders. Most were shot; Radek was given a camp sentence and died in captivity.","March 1938: twenty-one defendants were tried from 2 to 13 March as a bloc of rightists and Trotskyites, accused of plotting with foreign powers to break up the Soviet Union; among them were Bukharin, Rykov and the former NKVD chief Yagoda. Eighteen of the twenty-one were sentenced to death and shot on 15 March 1938.","Tomsky, the third leader of the right, shot himself in August 1936 rather than stand trial.","Andrei Vyshinsky prosecuted all three trials; the verdicts were decided in advance and the defendants had been prepared by months of interrogation.","Confessions were obtained by relays of night questioning, sleep deprivation and threats to wives and children; some defendants were promised their families' safety.","The trials were held in Moscow with an invited audience, and foreign journalists and diplomats were admitted so that the proceedings would be reported abroad.","Newspapers printed the charges in full, and factory and collective farm meetings passed resolutions demanding the highest measure of punishment, which were read out as evidence of public support.","The trials destroyed the reputations of the Old Bolsheviks before destroying the men, so that no alternative leadership with revolutionary authority remained.","They also explained away the failures of the Five-Year Plans as deliberate wrecking, and made Trotsky, safe abroad since 1929, the author of every disaster inside the USSR.","Their audience was as much the Soviet official class as the public: if men who had stood beside Lenin could be shot, no rank or record gave protection."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: propaganda, censorship, education, religion and the Constitution of 1936","items":["Independent literary and artistic groups were abolished in 1932 and a single Union of Soviet Writers created; socialist realism was declared the official method at its first congress in August 1934, which Maxim Gorky presided over.","Socialist realism required art that was realistic in style, optimistic in mood, easy for ordinary people to understand, and full of the socialist future: heroic workers, collective farmers, engineers and soldiers.","Vera Mukhina's steel sculpture Worker and Kolkhoz Woman was made for the Soviet pavilion at the Paris international exhibition of 1937; Alexander Gerasimov painted much-reproduced portraits of Stalin, including one of Stalin and Voroshilov in the Kremlin.","Glavlit, the censorship office created in 1922, cleared everything printed. Photographs were retouched so that purged officials vanished, most famously Yezhov, who was removed from a photograph taken beside Stalin on a canal bank.","Pravda, meaning truth, was the party newspaper and Izvestia the government's; foreign newspapers and radio were unavailable to ordinary citizens.","Schools returned in the 1930s to formal discipline, set textbooks and examinations, after the experiments of the 1920s were abandoned.","The Octobrists, Pioneers and Komsomol organised children and young people outside lessons; the story of Pavlik Morozov, a boy said to have denounced his own father, was taught as a model of loyalty to the state.","A single approved school history, A Short History of the USSR edited by A. V. Shestakov, was issued to schools in 1937. From 1938 the history of the party was taught from the separate official Short Course, which gave Stalin a leading role beside Lenin in 1917 and presented Trotsky as a traitor.","The League of the Militant Godless, led by Yemelyan Yaroslavsky, ran anti-religious campaigns; churches, mosques and synagogues were closed or converted, and the Cathedral of Christ the Saviour in Moscow was dynamited in December 1931.","The census of January 1937 asked about religious belief and found that over half of adults called themselves believers. It was suppressed and its organisers arrested.","The Constitution was adopted on 5 December 1936 and presented as the most democratic in the world: a secret ballot for all citizens over eighteen, including former nobles, priests and kulaks; freedoms of speech, press, assembly and religious worship; rights to work, rest, education and support in old age; and a two-chamber Supreme Soviet.","The Communist Party was written into the Constitution as the leading force and remained the only party, so the elections to the Supreme Soviet in December 1937 offered a single list of candidates and reported turnouts above ninety per cent.","The Constitution was adopted between the first and second Moscow show trials; Bukharin, who worked on drafting it, was shot in March 1938."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Cult of Stalin","items":["A cult of personality means the deliberate presentation of a leader as infallible and fatherly, so that loyalty to the state becomes devotion to one man.","The cult was launched around Stalin's official fiftieth birthday in December 1929, when the newspapers filled with tributes, and it grew through the 1930s.","Stalin, born Dzhugashvili, had taken a name built on the Russian word for steel; official titles included Vozhd, the leader, the Father of the Peoples, and the great teacher.","It rested on inheritance from Lenin: Lenin's body was embalmed and displayed, Stalin lectured on the foundations of Leninism, and portraits and the Short Course of 1938 placed him at Lenin's side in 1917.","Portraits, busts and statues went into schools, offices and factories, and huge banners of his face were carried at the parades on May Day and on the anniversary of the revolution.","Achievements were credited to him personally: the Moscow Metro opened in 1935, new dams, canals and steelworks, and record-breaking workers such as Alexei Stakhanov, who was said to have cut fourteen times his quota of coal in a single shift in August 1935.","Places were renamed after him: Tsaritsyn had become Stalingrad in 1925, and other towns and institutions followed.","Failures were blamed on local officials, wreckers and saboteurs, so the leader remained separate from the costs of his own policies.","The Terror strengthened the cult: praising Stalin in public was the cheapest proof of loyalty, and nobody wished to be the first to stop applauding.","The cult was a method of rule rather than evidence of popularity: it is measured by what the state produced, not by what people privately believed."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the source and interpretation moves this paper rewards","items":["Inference: say something the source does not state but that must be true if the source is true, then give the words that force it. One inference and one supporting detail in each box.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose: what kind of document it is, who made it and when, and why it was made.","Utility: judge each source for the enquiry the question names, never in general. Content first, then how nature, origin and purpose make that content more or less useful, then the context that confirms or qualifies it.","Contextual knowledge scores only when it is used on the source; a paragraph about the period that never touches the sources earns nothing.","Calling a source biased is not a judgement. Say whose view it gives, why it gives that view, and what it is therefore useful for: a source written to persuade is excellent evidence of the persuasion.","A source can be useful for an enquiry it never mentions, if what it shows explains the thing being enquired into; say what can be inferred, not only what is said.","Difference of view (3(b)): a disagreement about the past, not a difference of wording or of detail. State the one difference, then support it with a short phrase from each interpretation.","Reason for difference (3(c)): interpretations differ because they ask different questions, rest on different evidence, or give weight to different parts of the story. Name which, show it from the texts, and use Sources B and C where they illustrate the evidence.","Differences based on conclusions drawn from evidence are legitimate; an interpretation is not wrong merely because another disagrees with it.","Evaluating an interpretation (3(d)): use both interpretations and your own knowledge, show how each conveys its view, and decide how far the named interpretation can be agreed with, saying on what criterion."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 3 Question 3(d): 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the importance of the quotas in the purges of the Soviet Union in the years 1936-38?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","items":["The question as printed: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the importance of the quotas in the purges of the Soviet Union in the years 1936 to 1938? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' No stimulus points are printed on this question: the two interpretations are the stimulus, and both must be used, so an answer that evaluates one in isolation cannot reach the top levels.","Decode the claim. Interpretation 2 makes the quota the engine of the Terror: figures handed down to regional bosses who asked for them to be raised, because a low figure looked like slackness and slackness was itself a crime, so that Stalin lit the fire but did not choose most of its victims. Interpretation 1 does not mention quotas at all; it puts the lists of names to be shot on Stalin's desk to be approved. The criterion to announce in the opening is the one that decides between them: does a number decide who dies, or only how many?","Opening, three or four sentences: state Interpretation 2's claim about the quotas in your own words, name Interpretation 1 as the rival weighting, announce the criterion, and signal the position you will end at, so the line of reasoning is visible from the first paragraph.","Paragraph 1, for Interpretation 2 — what a quota was. The NKVD order of 30 July 1937 set every region a figure for the first category, shooting, and a figure for the second, the camps; the first national figures were just under 76,000 for shooting and 193,000 for the camps, about 269,000 in all. Interim judgement: from that day arrests had a target to meet, and a target is filled from whoever is to hand.","Paragraph 2, for Interpretation 2 — what a quota did to the man who received it. Regional leaders asked Moscow to raise their figures rather than to lower them, because a modest figure looked like a lack of vigilance; officers judged on the cases they closed acted on any signed denunciation, and denunciations came from neighbours and workmates over a room in an overcrowded flat or a place at the bench. Interim judgement: the quota turned other people's private quarrels into state business.","Paragraph 3, against Interpretation 2 — where the figures came from. Nobody in a district office wrote the order of 30 July 1937, and a region that wanted a larger figure had to have the increase granted from the centre, which is the practical meaning of Interpretation 1's lists sent to Stalin's desk. Interim judgement: the quota is evidence of central control, not of its absence.","Paragraph 4, against Interpretation 2 — the victims no quota produced. The public trials of Zinoviev, Kamenev, Bukharin and Rykov and the shooting of Tukhachevsky with seven senior commanders in June 1937 removed the only possible alternative leadership and the only rival centre of force; none of these men was counted against a district's figure. Interim judgement: the Terror's most important victims were chosen by name, not by number.","Paragraph 5, the scale test that goes furthest beyond both texts. Roughly 680,000 death sentences were carried out in 1937 and 1938, far more than the figures first set in July 1937: too many to have been chosen one by one at the centre, which supports Interpretation 2, and far too many to be explained by private quarrels alone, since the increases had to be asked for and granted. Interim judgement: the quota is where the centre's decision and the locality's appetite met.","The evaluation thread that must run through every paragraph: how each interpretation conveys its view. Interpretation 2 uses the language of a fire spreading and of a thousand offices, which distributes responsibility; Interpretation 1 uses signature, script and telephone call, which concentrates it, and says nothing of quotas at all, which is itself a choice about what mattered. Saying this, and using it, is what the top level means by showing how the differences of view are conveyed.","What the conclusion must say: not that both are partly right. Apply the criterion announced at the start — the quota decided how many would be destroyed and left the names to be found locally, so Interpretation 2 is right that the choosing happened below, but the number itself was set, raised and finally cancelled in Moscow, so the importance of the quota is evidence for Interpretation 1's weighting rather than against it. Then name the deciding consideration: a target that only the centre can issue and only the centre can withdraw is an instrument of the centre, whoever fills it.","Timing and SPaG: about 30 minutes — five to plan the paragraphs and fix the criterion, twenty-two to write, and the last three to check spelling and punctuation and to make sure the specialist terms are present and correctly used, since 4 additional marks depend on a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate. Useful terms here: purge, Terror, NKVD, troika, Gulag, denunciation, show trial, Old Bolshevik, quota, cult of personality, Politburo, Central Committee."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:18:24Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.95,"notes":"Third pass, from the kit file. All three polish changes hold. lesson.examples[0] now infers, first, that the office taking the confession did not decide the case ('the men then repeat them differently before the troika') and, second, that the district controlled neither its targets nor its means (the new figure must be confirmed by the regional directorate the report is addressed to; he must 'ask again for two more investigators and for paper'): both are forced by the printed Source A, both are genuine inferences rather than quotation, and neither is credited in room.questions[0] or room.questions[1], so the bank no longer arrives part-answered. The March 1938 trial is now the trial of the twenty-one in all five places - room.questions[5].sol, extras[5] item 3, lesson.idea[4], cards[11] (35 words) and lesson.check[2] - and the web bears out 2 to 13 March 1938, twenty-one accused with Bukharin, Rykov and Yagoda among them, eighteen death sentences carried out on 15 March 1938 and three prison terms. lesson.examples[4] keeps its local-NKVD focus on new ground, all of it verified: the Omsk chief's request in the summer of 1938 for thousands more executions after his figure was filled, granted from Moscow; NKVD order 00485 of 11 August 1937 against supposed Polish agents, whose album procedure had districts compile the lists while only the NKVD and the Procurator General in Moscow could confirm a sentence; and the prosecution of local officers for breach of legality once the centre's line changed in 1939. None of that appears in the three bank 16-mark solutions. dupscan prints 'no duplication'; the printed sources, interpretations, tariffs, caps and timings are untouched. Publishable as it stands."}}},"30-4":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"30-4","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 4 is where Option 30 stops being about who runs Russia and starts being about what the state did to the people who lived in it, and it is the richest ground on the paper for Section B. Collectivisation, the Five-Year Plans and life under them are argued over by historians more fiercely than anything else in the option, so this is where the interpretations questions come from; and because the topic is full of hard evidence - grain procurements, tonnes of coal, livestock numbers, wages, census figures - it is also where a precise answer separates itself fastest from a vague one. This room teaches the economic and social transformation of 1924 to 1941 as an argument with two sides you can both support, and it trains the five moves Paper 3 rewards: inference from a contemporary source, causal explanation that goes past the printed bullet points, judgement of a source's usefulness for the exact enquiry named, identifying how two historians differ and why, and evaluating one of them against the other and against what you know.","idea":[{"h":"Why Stalin turned on the New Economic Policy","t":"The New Economic Policy, in place since 1921, let peasants sell their surplus grain for profit once they had paid their tax. It fed the country, but it left the state buying grain rather than taking it, and in 1927 and 1928 the peasants would not sell at the prices the state offered. Procurements fell far short, the cities went short of bread, and grain exports that were supposed to pay for imported machinery dried up. Stalin went to Siberia himself in January 1928 and ordered officials to seize grain and prosecute hoarders. Behind the immediate crisis lay a longer argument: a country of twenty-five million small holdings could not be planned, could not be taxed reliably, and was producing a class of better-off peasants whose interests were not the party's. A top-level answer does not simply list these; it shows how a short-term supply crisis let Stalin present a permanent change of system as an emergency measure, and says which of the two - the crisis or the plan - it thinks came first.","code":"30-4 — 1 Agriculture and collectivisation: Stalin's reasons for changes in agriculture"},{"h":"How the collectives were built, and how the villages fought back","t":"Peasants were to pool land, tools and animals in a kolkhoz, or work for wages on a state-owned sovkhoz; tractors and combines were held centrally at Machine Tractor Stations, which served the farms and took payment in grain. Drive and coercion came from outside the village: the OGPU, local party activists and some twenty-five thousand town workers sent out in 1930. In December 1929 Stalin announced the liquidation of the kulaks as a class; households graded as kulak were shot, sent to camps or deported to the north and Siberia, and the label was often pinned on anyone who argued. Villages resisted by burning barns, attacking officials and above all by slaughtering their own livestock, so that herds of cattle and horses roughly halved between 1928 and 1933. When about half the country's households had been driven in by March 1930, Stalin published 'Dizzy with Success' in Pravda, blamed over-zealous officials, and let millions leave before the drive resumed. A top-level answer treats resistance as part of the explanation of what followed, not as a footnote: it was the destruction of animals and of the men who knew how to farm that turned a bad system into a famine.","code":"30-4 — 1 Agriculture and collectivisation: The organisation of collectives"},{"h":"Judging collectivisation: procurement up, production down","t":"The test you choose decides the answer. Judged by Stalin's own purpose, collectivisation worked: the state got its hands on the grain, procurements rose steeply even in years when the harvest fell, the towns and the army were fed, and exports paid for foreign machinery. Judged as farming, it failed for years: livestock did not regain their 1928 numbers before the war, output per head of population was no better at the end of the decade than at the start, and the famine of 1932 to 1933 killed several million people in Ukraine, the North Caucasus and Kazakhstan. Judged politically it was a total success, because by 1941 almost every peasant household was inside a collective and the party controlled the countryside for the first time. Mechanisation was real but thin, and from 1935 peasants were allowed small private plots that produced a share of the country's vegetables, milk and eggs far larger than their acreage. A top-level answer names its criterion in the first sentence - grain delivered, food produced, or control established - and then applies the same one throughout instead of drifting between them.","code":"30-4 — 1 Agriculture and collectivisation: The successes and failures of collectivisation"},{"h":"Why the hurry: defence, ideology and the failings of the NEP","t":"Stalin's case for forcing the pace rested on three things. First, security: the Soviet Union was the only communist state in a hostile Europe, the war scare of 1927 had frightened the leadership, and Stalin argued publicly in 1931 that the country was fifty to a hundred years behind the advanced powers and would be crushed if it did not close the gap in ten. Second, ideology: socialism in one country needed an industrial working class to be built on, and the NEP's traders and better-off peasants were the wrong material. Third, economics: under the NEP industry had done little more than climb back to its pre-war level, growth was slowing, and there was no mechanism for turning peasant savings into steel mills. Rapid industrialisation also settled an argument inside the party, since it destroyed the position of Bukharin and the right, who wanted the NEP kept. A top-level answer weighs these rather than listing them, and can say which mattered most by asking what the plans actually built first - and the answer, overwhelmingly, was heavy industry with a military use.","code":"30-4 — 2 Changes in industry: Stalin's motives for rapid industrialisation"},{"h":"Gosplan, the plans and the man with the pickaxe","t":"Gosplan, the state planning commission set up in 1921, turned political targets into figures for every commissariat, region, factory and shop. The First Five-Year Plan ran from 1928 and was declared fulfilled in four years; it concentrated on coal, iron, steel, oil and electricity, and produced the showpieces - Magnitogorsk raised on the steppe from 1929, the Dnieper dam finished in 1932, the White Sea canal dug by forced labour and opened in 1933. The Second, from 1933 to 1937, added transport and the Moscow Metro and a little more for consumers; the Third, from 1938, was swallowed by rearmament and cut short by the German invasion of June 1941. Because capital was scarce, more had to be got out of labour, and that is what the Stakhanovite movement was for: after Alexei Stakhanov was credited with cutting 102 tonnes of coal in one night shift at the Central Irmino mine in August 1935, record-breakers were showered with bonuses and medals and their figures became everyone else's norm. A top-level answer explains the plans as a system of pressure rather than a list of projects, and notices that the Stakhanovite movement is evidence of weakness as much as of ambition.","code":"30-4 — 2 Changes in industry: Gosplan, Five-Year Plans and the Stakhanovite Movement"},{"h":"Judging industrialisation: the tables and what they hide","t":"The output figures are genuinely startling: between 1928 and 1937 coal rose from around 35 million tonnes to around 128 million, steel roughly quadrupled, and electricity generation multiplied several times over. Whole industries - tractors, aircraft, chemicals, machine tools - existed at the end of the decade that had barely existed at its start, unemployment was declared abolished in 1930, and the plants built east of the Urals were what allowed the Soviet Union to keep fighting after 1941. Against that: consumer goods were starved, quality was poor and much building was shoddy because the plan rewarded quantity; targets were met on paper by falsification and by managers hoarding materials; skilled labour was so short that turnover was enormous; and a large part of the work was done by prisoners in the camps. A top-level answer does not settle for 'successes and failures' as two lists. It asks what the plans were for, judges them against that, and then asks the harder question of whether the same growth could have been had at a lower price.","code":"30-4 — 2 Changes in industry: The successes and failures of industrialisation"},{"h":"Living and working: barracks, queues and the labour book","t":"The towns swelled: the urban population roughly doubled between the census of 1926 and that of 1939, and new workers arriving from the villages slept in barracks, in dugouts or in a corner of someone else's room, with communal kitchens and shared taps. Food was rationed in the towns from 1929 until 1935, and real wages in the middle of the First Five-Year Plan were well below their 1928 level. Discipline tightened steadily: from 1932 a single day's unexplained absence could cost a worker the job, the ration card and the room that went with it; internal passports arrived the same year; labour books followed in 1938; and in June 1940 leaving a job without permission became a criminal offence. In the countryside collective farmers were paid in kind out of what was left after procurement and, having no passports, could not lawfully leave. Against all that, education, clinics, crèches and canteens spread fast, and the 1939 census claimed that 81.2 per cent of those aged nine and over could read. A top-level answer holds both halves at once and judges by a stated measure - the wage packet, the ration, the chance of schooling - rather than deciding in advance that life simply got better or worse.","code":"30-4 — 3 Life in the Soviet Union: Living and working conditions"},{"h":"Who gained and who lost","t":"Stalin's Soviet Union was not a flat society and did not pretend to be. Party and state officials, the nomenklatura, had closed shops, cars, dachas and holidays; managers, engineers and specialists were paid many times an unskilled wage, and after 1931 Stalin openly attacked wage levelling as a bourgeois idea. Stakhanovites were given bonuses, apartments and newspaper fame. Skilled workers did better than the unskilled, and townspeople better than peasants. Real opportunity existed: hundreds of thousands of workers and workers' children were pushed through technical schools into management, and the purges of the later 1930s emptied posts for them to fill. At the bottom were collective farmers tied to their villages, 'former people' such as priests, traders and the children of the old middle class who were barred from the best jobs and schools, deported kulaks, and the prisoners who dug the canals and mined the gold of Kolyma. A top-level answer uses this spread as an argument: the same decade could be a promotion for one family and a death sentence for another, so any general claim about whether people gained has to say which people.","code":"30-4 — 3 Life in the Soviet Union: The differing experiences of social groups"},{"h":"Women: into the factory, back into the family","t":"Two things happened at once, and they point in opposite directions. The Five-Year Plans needed hands, so women poured into industry: from roughly a quarter of industrial workers in the late 1920s to about two-fifths by 1940, with crèche places multiplying to make it possible, and medicine and teaching becoming heavily female professions. At the same time the state retreated from the family policy of the revolution. The Zhenotdel, the party's women's department, was closed in 1930; the Family Code of 1936 banned abortion except on medical grounds, made divorce slow and expensive and paid allowances to mothers of large families, because the leadership wanted the birth rate up after the losses of collectivisation and famine. Politically almost nothing changed at the top: there was no woman in Stalin's Politburo, and the best-known women of the decade were showpieces such as the three aviators who flew the Rodina in 1938 and became the first women made Heroes of the Soviet Union. A top-level answer separates the three strands - employment, family law, political power - and shows that change in one is not change in the others, which is exactly what 'how far' questions on this topic are testing.","code":"30-4 — 4 The position of women and ethnic minorities: Changes in family life and employment"},{"h":"The nationalities: from indigenisation to deportation","t":"In the 1920s the Soviet state had encouraged minority languages, alphabets and local officials. Under Stalin that reversed. Russian was made a compulsory school subject throughout the Union in 1938, the Latin alphabets devised for many minority languages were switched to Cyrillic, and national communist leaders were purged, as in Ukraine, where the commissar for education Mykola Skrypnyk shot himself in 1933. The reasons were fear and control: national feeling in the borderlands might become separatism; peoples with relatives across a frontier, such as Poles, Germans and Koreans, were suspected of divided loyalty; and collectivisation itself fell hardest on peoples it did not fit, above all the Kazakh herdsmen, well over a million of whom died in the famine of 1931 to 1933 as their animals were seized and they were forced to settle. Persecution then became wholesale: about 170,000 Koreans were deported from the Far East to Central Asia in 1937, and the national operations of the terror in 1937 and 1938 shot over a hundred thousand people for being Polish. A top-level answer links the persecution back to the economic story rather than treating it as a separate cruelty: it was the same drive for control of land, borders and labour, applied to peoples the state trusted least.","code":"30-4 — 4 The position of women and ethnic minorities: The persecution of ethnic minorities"}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1: two inferences from Source A","setup":"Source A in this room is a report written for his district party committee by a factory worker from Leningrad, one of the town volunteers sent out to set up collective farms, from a village in the Lower Volga region in March 1930. He writes that he arrived three weeks earlier with two comrades and a list of households; that the list names eleven families as kulaks, although four of them work their land with no hired hands and one is a widow with a mare; that the village must be fully collectivised before the sowing and most of it has signed, though he 'will not pretend the signatures were given willingly'; that in the week before the meeting at which the barns were declared common property half the village slaughtered its pigs and ate them, so that 'there is meat now in houses that have never had meat in Lent'; that on the Tuesday the women 'drove our chairman out of the dairy with sticks' and took their cows home while the men watched; that the village has no tractor and the nearest station has three machines for eleven villages; and that he asks what figure he is to enter for sown acreage. The question is: 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about conditions in the Soviet countryside in 1930. Complete the table below to explain your answer.' (4 marks)","steps":["Read the report for what it lets slip rather than for what it is about: he was sent to report on the collectivisation of one village, so the remarks he makes in passing about the place itself are the material this enquiry needs.","Draw the first inference from the remark about meat: he says there is meat now in houses that have never had meat in Lent, so meat was not something these households normally had at that season, and the slaughter was feeding a village used to going without it.","Draw the second from the machinery: he says the village has no tractor and the nearest station has three machines for eleven villages, so farming here was still being done without machines and the mechanisation the collectives were promised had not arrived.","Lay each answer out in two parts, the claim the source never states and then the exact words that force it, and check that no phrase from the source is doing duty in both answers."],"cues":["Which details are incidental to what he was sent to report?","What does the remark about meat in Lent tell you about the village?","How many machines are there, and for how many villages?","Which box takes the claim and which the quotation?"]},{"title":"Question 3(a): judging Sources B and C for the enquiry actually named","setup":"Source B in this room is a letter from a young building worker at Magnitogorsk to his elder brother in Tver, October 1933: eleven men to a barrack room with ice on the inside of the wall in winter, cabbage soup and black bread from the canteen, a month in spring with no soap in the settlement, men walking off to the station after a fortnight - but also the blast furnace whose foundations he dug now pouring iron, his name printed in the newspaper with his brigade's, and an evening course teaching him to read a drawing. Source C is a report from the director of a machine-building works in the Urals to his commissariat in Moscow, January 1936, explaining a missed December programme: the record-breaker on the best Stakhanovite shift had two fitters clearing his machine and prepared blanks at his elbow, his figure is now the norm for the whole shop, three of the director's best turners have collected their pay and gone to the tractor plant where the norms are easier, his lathes are run at speeds they were not built for and seven of his forty machines are under repair, and he can meet the quarter's plan if the promised steel arrives and the norm is not raised again. The question is: 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into why workers moved from one job to another in Soviet industry in the 1930s? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context.' (8 marks)","steps":["Copy the enquiry out and hold it: this one is about why men moved, so the test is how far each source shows what pushed a worker out of a job and what pulled him to another, not whether either is a fair account of Soviet industry.","Take Source B's content first: the barrack room with ice on the inside of the wall, the cabbage soup and black bread and the month with no soap supply the push, and the men he saw leave after a fortnight and walk to the station show it happening within weeks of arrival.","Balance that against what the same letter shows holding a man in place - the blast furnace whose foundations he dug now pouring iron, his name printed with his brigade's, the evening course teaching him to read a drawing - because an enquiry into movement has to explain why anyone stayed.","Weigh Source B's provenance: a private letter answering his brother's question whether he is sorry he came has nothing to prove to the state, which strengthens both halves of it, but one young single man in one new city is a narrow base for a whole workforce.","Turn to Source C's content, which supplies what the letter cannot: three of the director's best turners collected their pay and went to the tractor plant where the norms were said to be easier, so skilled men were moving towards the easier rate and not only away from bad housing, and the record shift that became everyone else's norm explains why.","Weigh Source C's provenance, which cuts both ways - a director explaining a shortfall has an interest in blaming the norms, yet putting his turnover in writing tells you how normal it was - and bring in context that does work on the sources: turnover so high in the early years of the plans that the average industrial worker was changing job within about eight months, easing only as the decade went on, the decree of November 1932 that dismissed a man for a single day's unexcused absence and took his ration card and his room with the job, and the labour books of December 1938 and the criminal penalty for leaving a job of June 1940, both later than either source; then judge which source serves this enquiry better and why."],"cues":["What exactly is the enquiry asking about?","Which details of Source B would push a man to leave?","What in the same letter kept this writer where he was?","What does a letter to a brother gain by exaggerating?","What made the director's turners move, and where to?","Which contextual facts show how common this movement was?"]},{"title":"Question 3(b): a difference of view, not of detail","setup":"This example works on a second, shorter pair of interpretations, written for this worked example and numbered 3 and 4 so that they are not confused with Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section; like those, they are written for this room, are not real books and are not attributed to any real historian. Interpretation 3, from a study of Soviet society by a historian writing in the 1970s: 'Stalin's decade made a new privileged order. After wage levelling was condemned in 1931 a manager or an engineer could earn many times a labourer's wage, and officials alone held the passes to the closed shops, the better flats and the rest homes. Below them the mass of workers and collective farmers stayed poor.' Interpretation 4, from a study of Soviet careers by a historian writing in the 2010s: 'Privilege was real, but it was neither safe nor closed. Tens of thousands of workers and workers' children were pushed through the technical schools of the First Five-Year Plan and into managers' chairs, and the purges of 1937 and 1938 emptied those chairs again. This was violent movement, not a settled class.' The question is: 'Study Interpretations 3 and 4. They give different views about how far a privileged class emerged in the Soviet Union in the 1930s. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer using details from both interpretations.' (4 marks)","steps":["Reduce each interpretation to one sentence about the same thing, so that the difference is forced to be a difference of view: Interpretation 3 says the decade hardened into a new privileged class standing above a poor majority, Interpretation 4 says the privilege was real but neither secure nor closed, so no class settled.","Check that you have a view and not a topic: both texts talk about managers, wages and ordinary workers, so listing what each one mentions will not do - the split is over whether privilege became fixed.","Anchor each half of the difference in the interpretation's own words: Interpretation 3 that officials 'alone held the passes' while the mass 'stayed poor', Interpretation 4 that the purges 'emptied those chairs again' and that this was 'violent movement, not a settled class'.","Write it as one difference stated in a single sentence, then a supporting quotation from each side, and resist adding your own knowledge, which earns nothing on this question."],"cues":["What is each historian's one-line claim?","Why is 'both mention managers' not a difference of view?","Which phrase in each text carries its verdict?","What belongs in this answer and what does not?"]},{"title":"Question 2: taking a 12-mark 'Explain why' past the bullet points","setup":"The question is: 'Explain why the towns of the Soviet Union grew so quickly in the years 1928 to 1941. You may use the following in your answer: collectivisation; the Five-Year Plans. You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks). Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b), and you answer one of them only. The mark scheme gives a maximum of 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, to an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content; there is no cap at Level 2, so an answer built from the two bullet points alone can reach the top of Level 2 but no further, and at least one developed reason has to come from outside them.","steps":["Turn the first bullet into a causal reason rather than a topic: collectivisation took the land and the animals of some twenty-five million peasant holdings into collectives, dekulakisation deported well over a million people in 1930 and 1931, and the famine of 1932 to 1933 followed, so the village stopped being a place a young man could stay in and millions left for the building sites - which is why the internal passport of December 1932 was brought in to control the flow.","Turn the second bullet the same way: the plans built whole works where there had been nothing, so Magnitogorsk was open steppe when the first builders arrived in June 1929 and a city of well over a hundred thousand within a decade, and a works has to have its workers on the spot, so the plan created the towns as deliberately as it created the furnaces.","Build the reason that lies outside both bullets, which is where Level 4 is decided: the state recruited on purpose rather than waiting - Stalin's six conditions of June 1931 put organised recruitment by contract with the collective farms first - and a town job carried a ration card, which the rationing of 1929 to 1935 did not give the collective farmer, so going to the building site was a rational choice as well as a forced one.","Add a second strand so the explanation has more than one kind of cause in it: where the new plants went was a political and strategic decision, so towns appeared east of the Urals and in the national republics where industry had hardly existed, and the growth was not simply people drifting into the old cities.","Close by ranking the reasons instead of summarising them, and say plainly which you think decided the scale of the growth and which decided where it happened, because the top level asks for a line of reasoning that is sustained rather than a set of paragraphs."],"cues":["What did collectivisation do to a young peasant's prospects?","Where were the workers for a brand-new works to come from?","What did a town job give a man that the village could not?","Why were the new plants put where they were?","How do you end without simply repeating the paragraphs?"]},{"title":"Question 3(d): evaluating Interpretation 2 against Interpretation 1 and your own knowledge","setup":"Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section are the pair for this question. Interpretation 1, a general economic history by a historian writing in the 1960s, argues that judged by what it set out to do Stalin's economic revolution worked: coal, steel and electricity output multiplied, Magnitogorsk and the Dnieper dam were built from nothing, unemployment disappeared, the cost was terrible, and without the factories of the 1930s the Soviet Union would not have survived 1941. Interpretation 2, a social history by a historian writing in the 2000s, argues that the output tables of the 1930s 'measure effort, not achievement', that factories 'reported figures they had not made and built badly because the plan rewarded quantity', and that what grew fastest was not productivity but the number of people who could be compelled. The question is: 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about what the Soviet Union's industrial output figures of the 1930s really show? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks], the four additional marks being for spelling, punctuation, grammar and the use of specialist terminology. No stimulus points are printed: the two interpretations are the stimulus, and both have to be used.","steps":["Fix the criterion before writing a word: the question is about what the figures show, so the test is whether an output table is a true measure of what the country gained, and saying so at the start is itself an evaluative move.","Build the case for Interpretation 2 out of precise evidence rather than out of the interpretation restated: the plan rewarded quantity, so returns were padded and work was stormed into the last days of the month; quality was bad enough for the law of 10 July 1940 to treat the issue of defective goods as wrecking and to threaten directors and chief engineers with five to eight years; and the First Five-Year Plan was declared fulfilled at the end of 1932, in four years and three months, although a string of its own targets had been missed.","Turn Interpretation 1 into the case against, and make it as strong as it deserves: coal rose from about 35 million tonnes in 1928 to about 128 million in 1937, steel roughly quadrupled over the same years, Magnitogorsk and the Dnieper dam stood where nothing had stood, and the plants raised east of the Urals were still turning out weapons after the invasion of June 1941 - output that existed only on paper could not have done that.","Show how each interpretation conveys its view, because the top level asks for exactly that: Interpretation 1 reads the tables as the record of what was built and judges by the state's own aims, Interpretation 2 reads them as the record of what was demanded and judges by what came out of the factory gate, so the two are arguing about what a figure measures rather than about the arithmetic.","Judge by the criterion you announced rather than splitting the difference, and say what would have to be true for the other view to win, which is what turns an opinion into a substantiated judgement."],"cues":["Which words in the question set the test?","What evidence shows that a reported figure need not be a real one?","What is the best case Interpretation 1 can make from the same tables?","Are the two historians disputing the arithmetic or what it measures?","How do you conclude without saying both are partly right?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What did Stalin do in January 1928 that showed how he meant to solve the grain problem?","a":"He went to Siberia in person and ordered local officials to seize grain from the villages and prosecute peasants who held it back, instead of raising the price the state paid."},{"q":"Why did the slaughter of livestock in 1929 and 1930 matter for years afterwards?","a":"Cattle and horse numbers roughly halved between 1928 and 1933 and had not recovered by 1941, so the collectives lost draught power and manure as well as meat and milk, which deepened the famine of 1932 to 1933."},{"q":"What did the Stakhanovite movement do to ordinary workers' norms after August 1935?","a":"The record figures became the new targets for whole shops, so most workers had to produce more for the same or less pay, which is why the movement was resented on the shop floor and by managers."},{"q":"In what sense did the position of women both improve and worsen in the 1930s?","a":"Employment opened up, from roughly a quarter of industrial workers in the late 1920s to about two-fifths by 1940, with crèches to support it; but the Zhenotdel closed in 1930 and the Family Code of 1936 banned abortion and made divorce hard, and no woman sat in Stalin's Politburo."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 3 Section A (16 marks): Question 1 prints Source A and asks 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about...', answered in a table of two boxes each (4 marks); Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b), both 'Explain why...' at 12 marks marked on the same grid, each with two stimulus points and 'You must also use information of your own.', and you answer one of them only.","Paper 3 Section B (36 marks) is all Question 3: (a) 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into...?' (8); (b) 'What is the main difference between the views?' (4); (c) 'Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about...' (4); (d) 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about...?' (16, plus up to 4 for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology).","The stimulus caps on the 12-mark 'Explain why...', whichever of 2(a) and 2(b) you answer: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'. There is no cap at Level 2, so an answer built from the two bullets alone can still reach the top of Level 2. Question 3(d) prints no stimulus points, so no cap applies to it.","Timing on Paper 3: 1 hour 30 minutes, 90 minutes for 52 marks, so roughly a minute a mark with planning time on top. About 5 minutes on Question 1, 20 on the Question 2 you choose, 12 on 3(a), 6 each on 3(b) and 3(c) and about 35 minutes on 3(d), leaving the last three of those to check the spelling, punctuation and specialist terms that carry the extra 4 marks, and a few minutes spare at the end.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose, and usefulness is judged only for the enquiry the question names. Contextual knowledge scores nothing on 3(a) or 3(d) unless it is used on the sources or the interpretations.","Four dates that anchor this key topic: December 1929, Stalin announces the liquidation of the kulaks as a class; 2 March 1930, 'Dizzy with Success' in Pravda; 1932 to 1933, the famine in Ukraine, the North Caucasus and Kazakhstan; August 1935, Stakhanov's record shift at the Central Irmino mine."],"questions":[{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about the way collectivisation was carried out in the countryside. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for each valid inference up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail from Source A. Credit inferences about the loose use of the kulak label, the pressure of targets on activists, the absence of promised machinery or the use of outsiders to run the drive.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: the kulak label was being applied to people who did not fit it. Details in the source that tell me this: the list he was given names eleven families as kulaks, yet he says 'four of them work their land with no hired hands and one is a widow with a mare'. (ii) What I can infer: collectivisation was driven by deadlines set outside the village rather than by what the village could do. Details in the source that tell me this: he is told the village 'must be fully collectivised before the sowing', there is no tractor there and the nearest station has three machines for eleven villages, and he ends by asking what figure he is to enter for sown acreage.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["An inference is something the report makes certain without stating it; ask what must be true for a loyal volunteer to write to the people who sent him in this tone.","Read it twice, once for what it tells you about who was being classed as a kulak and once for what it tells you about the pressure on the man writing.","Put the unstated claim in the first box and the words from the source that force it in the second, and use each detail only once."],"codes":["30-4 — 1 Agriculture and collectivisation: The organisation of collectives","30-4 — 1 Agriculture and collectivisation: Stalin's reasons for changes in agriculture"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about peasants' reactions to collectivisation in 1930. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for each valid inference up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail from Source A. Credit inferences about destruction of livestock rather than surrender, open physical resistance, the part played by women, or agreement given only under compulsion.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: peasants destroyed their property rather than let the collective have it. Details in the source that tell me this: in the week before the barns were declared common property 'half the village slaughtered its pigs and ate them', so that there was 'meat now in houses that have never had meat in Lent'. (ii) What I can infer: resistance was open and physical, and women were often at the front of it. Details in the source that tell me this: on the Tuesday the women 'drove our chairman out of the dairy with sticks' and took their cows home, while the men stood at the gate and said it was only the women.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what a peasant could still control once the barns had been declared common property, and what he did with it.","One inference should come from what the villagers destroyed and one from what they did to the officials.","Each claim must be something the report never says outright, followed by the exact words that make it certain."],"codes":["30-4 — 1 Agriculture and collectivisation: The organisation of collectives","30-4 — 1 Agriculture and collectivisation: The successes and failures of collectivisation"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. They give different views about the results of Stalin's economic policies in the years 1928 to 1941. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4) needs a key difference of view, not of detail, identified and supported from both texts. The difference is over whether the transformation was a real achievement: Interpretation 1 judges it a success by the state's own aims, Interpretation 2 judges the figures hollow and the growth a product of force.","sol":"The main difference is over whether Stalin's economic policies achieved anything worth having. Interpretation 1 judges the decade a success on its own terms: 'a country of wooden ploughs became the world's second industrial power', collectivisation gave the state grain the New Economic Policy could never buy, and without the factories of the 1930s the country 'would not have survived 1941'. Interpretation 2 judges the same decade hollow and coerced: the output tables 'measure effort, not achievement', factories 'reported figures they had not made', and what grew fastest was 'not productivity but the number of people who could be compelled'. The subject is the same, the verdict opposite.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Reduce each historian to one sentence about the same question, and the difference will be the gap between those two sentences.","A difference of view is about the verdict each reaches, not about which examples each happens to mention.","State the one difference first, then support each half with a short quotation from its own interpretation; your own knowledge earns nothing here."],"codes":["30-4 — 2 Changes in industry: The successes and failures of industrialisation","30-4 — 1 Agriculture and collectivisation: The successes and failures of collectivisation"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about the results of Stalin's economic policies in the years 1928 to 1941. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4) needs one reason explained and substantiated by analysis of the interpretations. Credit that they weigh different evidence - national output and the outcome of 1941 against the household and the shop floor - or that they emphasise different aspects, the state's aims against the people's experience.","sol":"One reason is that the two historians weigh different kinds of evidence. Interpretation 1 argues from national totals and from 1941: coal, steel and electricity multiplying, Magnitogorsk and the Dnieper dam 'built from nothing', unemployment disappearing. Such evidence is collected by the state and shows the system from above, so it yields the verdict that the policies worked. Interpretation 2 argues from the household and the shop floor, which is why it reaches for herds falling by half and a worker who 'ate worse in 1933 than in 1928'. Sources B and C are that second kind of evidence: a barrack room with no soap, and a record shift staged with two fitters clearing the machine.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what each historian would have had to read to write the sentences they wrote.","A good reason is about the weight given to different evidence or to different aspects of the question, not about one of them being biased.","Name the reason in your first sentence, then show it in each interpretation, and use Sources B and C only as illustration of the second kind of evidence."],"codes":["30-4 — 3 Life in the Soviet Union: The differing experiences of social groups","30-4 — 2 Changes in industry: The successes and failures of industrialisation","30-4 — 3 Life in the Soviet Union: Living and working conditions"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the experience of industrial workers during the Five-Year Plans? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8) needs judgements of utility for this enquiry with developed reasoning about how nature, origin and purpose affect the content, and context used to interpret the sources. Credit Source B on barracks, food and the pull of the work, Source C on staged records, rising norms and labour turnover.","sol":"Source B gives both sides of the experience from inside. It carries the material cost - eleven men to a barrack room with 'the ice on the inside of the wall by morning', a month with no soap, men who 'walk to the station' after a fortnight - and the pull that held others, since the furnace he helped dig is 'pouring iron' and he is learning a trade at evening class. Its provenance helps: a private letter to his brother has nothing to prove to the state, so the complaints can be trusted, though one man in one new city cannot stand for the workforce. Context sharpens it: October 1933 falls in the first year of the Second Five-Year Plan, 1933 to 1937, the hardest year of the plan era, with town bread rationing lasting until 1 January 1935, two calendar years later. Source C covers another part of the experience, the pressure of the norms. Its record-breaker had 'two fitters clearing his machine' and his figure 'is now the norm for the whole shop', which is how the Stakhanovite movement after August 1935 was felt on the shop floor, and his best turners walk out to a plant with easier norms. Its purpose qualifies it: a director explaining a missed programme has an interest in blaming the norms, but that makes his admission of turnover and worn machinery more telling. Source B is the more useful on how workers lived, Source C on how they were driven.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Copy out the enquiry before you look at either source: usefulness is always usefulness for something named.","Take each source as content, then provenance, then the context that confirms or limits it, and only compare them at the end.","Open on what Source B lets you infer about life at Magnitogorsk, and make the first sentence a judgement rather than a description."],"codes":["30-4 — 3 Life in the Soviet Union: Living and working conditions","30-4 — 2 Changes in industry: The successes and failures of industrialisation","30-4 — 2 Changes in industry: Gosplan, Five-Year Plans and the Stakhanovite Movement"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the problems caused by the drive to raise industrial output in the 1930s? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8) needs utility judged for this enquiry with developed reasoning on provenance and content, context used to interpret. Credit Source C on staged records, raised norms, turnover and worn machinery, Source B on labour shortage, conditions and untrained newcomers, and a comparison of the two.","sol":"For this enquiry Source C is the stronger, because it is an internal account of the problems by the man responsible for them. It names four at once: the record shift was staged, since the man had 'two fitters clearing his machine and a store of prepared blanks at his elbow'; the record then became 'the norm for the whole shop'; three of his best turners have 'collected their pay and gone to the tractor plant, where I am told the norms are easier'; and his lathes are 'run at speeds they were not built for', with seven of forty under repair. That is the plan eating its own capacity, and it fits the mid-1930s, when labour turnover was enormous and nothing yet held a worker to his job, since labour books came only at the end of 1938 and criminal penalties for leaving without permission in June 1940. Its provenance needs care, because the report exists to explain a missed December programme to Moscow, so the emphasis on the norms and the undelivered steel serves the director's defence; even so, he would hardly invent damage to his own machines. Source B shows the problems at the other end: raw labour with no training, eleven to a barrack room, no soap for a month, men walking off after a fortnight. A private letter to a brother has nothing to prove, though one worker cannot show how general any of it was. Together they are more useful than either alone.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["The enquiry here is about problems, so ask what each source shows going wrong, not what it shows about life in general.","For each source, work content, then nature, origin and purpose, then the context that supports or limits what you have inferred.","Begin with the source that was written by someone whose job it was to explain a failure, and say in your first sentence how useful it is and why."],"codes":["30-4 — 2 Changes in industry: Gosplan, Five-Year Plans and the Stakhanovite Movement","30-4 — 2 Changes in industry: The successes and failures of industrialisation","30-4 — 3 Life in the Soviet Union: Living and working conditions"]},{"q":"Explain why Stalin decided to collectivise Soviet agriculture. You may use the following in your answer: • the grain procurement crisis of 1927 to 1928 • the kulaks. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12) needs an analytical explanation directed at causation with precisely selected knowledge. Credit the procurement shortfall, Stalin's Siberian tour of January 1928, the attack on the kulaks, the need to finance industrialisation, control of the villages and the defeat of Bukharin. Maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; there is no cap at Level 2.","sol":"Stalin collectivised because a supply crisis convinced him that the state would never control the grain while twenty-five million households decided what to sell. The first reason is the procurement crisis itself. Under the New Economic Policy the state had to buy grain, and in 1927 and 1928 peasants held it back because industrial goods were scarce and dear and the state price was low; procurement fell far short, bread queues formed in the cities and the exports that paid for imported machinery dried up. Stalin went to Siberia in January 1928 and ordered officials to seize grain and prosecute hoarders, and the fact that this worked taught him that force could do what the market had not. The second reason is that the better-off peasants stood in the way. The kulaks were the villagers most able to withhold grain and the least likely to gain from a plan, so in December 1929 Stalin announced the liquidation of the kulaks as a class; the OGPU and party activists deported or shot them, which removed the natural leaders of resistance and gave the party an enemy to blame for whatever went wrong. The third reason, which neither bullet prompts, is that industrialisation had to be paid for out of agriculture. The First Five-Year Plan needed German and American machinery, and grain was almost the only thing the Soviet Union could sell for the currency to buy it; the new industrial cities also had to be fed cheaply. A collective farm could be made to hand over a fixed quota before it fed itself, which no private holding could. Underlying all three is control: the party had almost no presence in the villages, and collectivisation put land, machinery through the Machine Tractor Stations, and eventually the peasants' right to move under state authority, while defeating Bukharin and the right, who wanted the New Economic Policy kept. The crisis of 1928 explains the timing; the need to finance the plan and to govern the countryside explains the policy.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Separate the reason Stalin acted in 1929 from the reason he chose this policy rather than a better price for grain.","Plan reasons of three different kinds - immediate crisis, class enemy, and the economics of the Five-Year Plan - so the answer analyses instead of listing.","Open on what the procurement shortfall actually did to the cities and the export trade, and close that paragraph on what Stalin concluded from it."],"codes":["30-4 — 1 Agriculture and collectivisation: Stalin's reasons for changes in agriculture","30-4 — 2 Changes in industry: Stalin's motives for rapid industrialisation"]},{"q":"Explain why there was famine in Ukraine in the years 1932 to 1933. You may use the following in your answer: • collectivisation • grain quotas. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12) needs sustained causal analysis with precise knowledge. Credit the destruction of livestock and loss of experienced farmers, quotas set on expected rather than actual harvests, continued exports, the law of 7 August 1932, internal passports preventing flight, and the political attack on Ukraine. Maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; there is no cap at Level 2.","sol":"The famine was made by a bad harvest meeting a system designed to take grain whatever the harvest was. The first reason is what collectivisation had already done to farming. Peasants killed and ate their animals rather than surrender them, so cattle and horse numbers roughly halved between 1928 and 1933 and the collectives lost the draught power and manure they depended on; dekulakisation had deported or shot many of the most capable farmers, and the new collectives were run by chairmen sent from the towns who often knew nothing about land. Sowing and harvesting were done badly, and grain was left in the fields. The second reason is the quota system. Quotas were fixed in advance on expected yields and had to be delivered before the collective fed itself, so when the 1932 harvest came in poor the shortfall fell entirely on the villages; brigades searched houses for hidden grain, and the state went on exporting grain through the famine because exports paid for the machinery of the Five-Year Plan. The third reason, beyond the bullets, is that the people were prevented from escaping the shortage. The law of 7 August 1932 made taking even gleanings from a collective field punishable by ten years or death; internal passports introduced in December 1932 were not issued to collective farmers, so leaving the village for a town was illegal; and villages that missed their quotas were blacklisted and cut off from manufactured goods. To this must be added the political dimension in Ukraine, where Moscow suspected national feeling and purged the Ukrainian party and its cultural leaders, the commissar for education Mykola Skrypnyk shooting himself in 1933. Bad farming produced the shortage, the quotas turned the shortage into starvation, and the laws of 1932 ensured that the starving could not move.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what turns a poor harvest into mass death, since poor harvests alone had not done so before.","Build the chain in order: what collectivisation had done to the land and the animals, what the quota system then took, and what stopped people leaving.","Start with the livestock and the deported farmers, and end that paragraph by saying what the collectives had lost before the harvest of 1932 was even sown."],"codes":["30-4 — 1 Agriculture and collectivisation: The organisation of collectives","30-4 — 1 Agriculture and collectivisation: The successes and failures of collectivisation","30-4 — 4 The position of women and ethnic minorities: The persecution of ethnic minorities"]},{"q":"Explain why Stalin wanted to industrialise the Soviet Union rapidly. You may use the following in your answer: • the fear of invasion • the New Economic Policy. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12) needs analytical explanation of motive with precise knowledge. Credit encirclement and the war scare of 1927, Stalin's 1931 warning about being fifty to a hundred years behind, the stagnation of the NEP, socialism in one country and the creation of a proletariat, ending dependence on imports, and the defeat of the right. Maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; there is no cap at Level 2.","sol":"Stalin wanted speed above all because he believed the Soviet Union had a fixed and short amount of time before it was attacked. The first reason is therefore security. The country was the only communist state in a hostile Europe, the war scare of 1927 had convinced the leadership that Britain and Poland might move against it, and in 1931 Stalin warned publicly that Russia was fifty to a hundred years behind the advanced countries and that the gap had to be closed in ten or the country would be crushed. That is why the plans concentrated on coal, iron, steel, oil and electricity rather than on goods anyone could buy: they were building the base of an armaments industry, and the Third Five-Year Plan from 1938 turned openly to weapons. The second reason is that the New Economic Policy could not do it. Industry under the NEP had done little more than climb back to its pre-war level, growth was slowing by the late 1920s, and nothing in the system turned peasant savings into blast furnaces; the country still had to import most of its machinery, which left it dependent on the very states it feared. The third reason, beyond the bullets, is ideological. Socialism in one country required an industrial working class to rest on, and the Soviet Union was overwhelmingly a peasant society; building factories built proletarians, and it also created the technically trained managers the party needed. Finally, the choice was a weapon inside the party: forcing the pace destroyed Bukharin and the right, who defended the NEP, and confirmed Stalin's leadership. Fear supplied the urgency, the weakness of the NEP supplied the argument that only the state could do it, and ideology and party politics made the decision Stalin's own.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Distinguish why he industrialised at all from why he insisted it be done in ten years rather than thirty.","Use three kinds of reason - military, economic and ideological or political - and make each explain the speed, not just the policy.","Open on the Soviet Union's position in Europe after the war scare, and finish that paragraph by saying what kind of industry that fear demanded."],"codes":["30-4 — 2 Changes in industry: Stalin's motives for rapid industrialisation","30-4 — 2 Changes in industry: Gosplan, Five-Year Plans and the Stakhanovite Movement"]},{"q":"Explain why the Stakhanovite movement was launched in 1935. You may use the following in your answer: • Alexei Stakhanov • the Second Five-Year Plan. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12) needs analytical explanation directed at causation with precise knowledge. Credit the shortage of capital and the need to raise labour productivity, the propaganda value of the record and of social mobility, the use of records to raise norms without paying more, pressure on managers, and the end of rationing in 1935. Maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; there is no cap at Level 2.","sol":"The movement was launched because the Second Five-Year Plan needed more output than the machinery could give, so more had to be taken out of the men who worked it. The first reason is the shortage of capital. Imported machinery was expensive and had to be paid for in grain, factories were already running plant hard, and the quickest way to raise production was to raise what each worker produced in a shift. Stakhanov supplied the demonstration: on a night shift at the Central Irmino mine in the Donbass in August 1935 he was credited with cutting 102 tonnes of coal, about fourteen times his quota, with his props set for him and his coal cleared away, and within weeks the press was calling for Stakhanovites in every industry. The second reason is what such records let the state do to norms. Once a figure had been achieved by anyone it could be presented as reasonable for everyone, so norms were raised across whole shops and workers produced more for the same or less pay; the movement was, in effect, a way of cutting labour costs that looked like a celebration of labour. The third reason, beyond the bullets, is political and social. Rationing had just ended in 1935, and the leadership wanted to show that effort now brought reward: Stakhanovites got bonuses, apartments, holidays and their names in the newspapers, which offered ordinary workers a visible ladder at a time when real wages were still below their 1928 level. It also gave the state a stick for managers, who could be accused of holding back the new methods and, in the atmosphere of the later 1930s, of wrecking. Behind all of it lay the plan's simplest problem, that targets had been set which existing equipment and existing effort could not reach.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the plan could not buy in 1935, and what the state therefore had to get out of workers instead.","Treat Stakhanov's shift as the occasion rather than the cause, and build reasons about productivity, norms and propaganda around it.","Begin with the cost of machinery and what that meant for how targets could be met, then bring in the record shift as the demonstration."],"codes":["30-4 — 2 Changes in industry: Gosplan, Five-Year Plans and the Stakhanovite Movement","30-4 — 3 Life in the Soviet Union: The differing experiences of social groups"]},{"q":"Explain why the position of women in the Soviet Union changed in the years 1928 to 1941. You may use the following in your answer: • the Five-Year Plans • the Family Code of 1936. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12) needs sustained analysis of causation with precise knowledge. Credit the demand for labour and the growth of crèches, the falling birth rate after famine and collectivisation, the retreat from the family policy of the 1920s and the closing of the Zhenotdel, education, and the unchanged political position. Maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; there is no cap at Level 2.","sol":"Women's position changed because the state wanted two different things from them at once, more labour and more children, and shaped policy to get both. The first reason is the labour demand of the Five-Year Plans. New factories, mines and building sites needed hands that the countryside alone could not supply fast enough, so women were recruited in millions; they rose from roughly a quarter of industrial workers in the late 1920s to about two-fifths by 1940, and to make that possible the state expanded crèches, kindergartens, canteens and laundries. Training went with it, so that medicine and teaching became heavily female professions and women entered engineering in numbers unknown before. The second reason is the state's alarm at the falling birth rate. Collectivisation, dekulakisation and the famine of 1932 to 1933 had cost millions of lives, and a country expecting war needed people; hence the Family Code of 1936, which banned abortion except on medical grounds, made divorce slow and expensive and paid allowances to mothers of large families. The third reason, beyond the bullets, is the deliberate retreat from the revolution's own family policy. The Zhenotdel, the party's women's department, was closed in 1930, and official language moved from liberation to the duties of the Soviet mother, because a stable family was now seen as a stable unit of the state rather than a relic to be dissolved. Politically almost nothing moved: no woman sat in Stalin's Politburo, and the women held up to the public were showpieces such as the three aviators who flew the Rodina in 1938 and became the first women made Heroes of the Soviet Union. So work changed greatly, family law changed twice and in opposite directions, and power did not change at all.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["The state wanted women in two places at once; name both demands before you start writing paragraphs.","Handle employment, family law and political power as three separate strands, because they moved in different directions.","Open on what the new factories needed and what the state had to build so that women could take those jobs."],"codes":["30-4 — 4 The position of women and ethnic minorities: Changes in family life and employment","30-4 — 2 Changes in industry: Gosplan, Five-Year Plans and the Stakhanovite Movement"]},{"q":"Explain why ethnic minorities were persecuted in the Soviet Union in the years 1928 to 1941. You may use the following in your answer: • collectivisation • the Soviet Union's borders. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12) needs analytical explanation with precise knowledge. Credit collectivisation of nomadic peoples and the Kazakh famine, suspicion of border nationalities and cross-border ties, the reversal of indigenisation and Russification after 1938, the Korean deportation of 1937 and the national operations of the terror. Maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; there is no cap at Level 2.","sol":"Minorities were persecuted because the same drive for control that reshaped land and industry was applied hardest to the peoples the state trusted least. The first reason is collectivisation, which fell on communities it did not fit. The Kazakhs were herdsmen, and taking their animals and forcing them to settle destroyed their way of living: well over a million died in the famine of 1931 to 1933, perhaps a third of the Kazakh people, and many more drove their herds across the border into China. In Ukraine the requisitioning of 1932 to 1933 came with a purge of the Ukrainian party and of Ukrainian writers and teachers, and the commissar for education Mykola Skrypnyk shot himself in 1933. The second reason is the frontier. Peoples with relatives on the other side of a border were assumed to have divided loyalties, so Poles and Germans were cleared from the border districts of Ukraine to Kazakhstan in 1935 and 1936, and in 1937 about 170,000 Koreans were deported from the Far East to Central Asia, the first time an entire nationality was moved. The third reason, beyond the bullets, is the reversal of the nationalities policy of the 1920s. The state had encouraged minority languages, alphabets and local officials; from the mid-1930s it feared that this had bred nationalism, so Russian was made a compulsory school subject throughout the Union in 1938, Latin alphabets were replaced by Cyrillic, and national communist leaders were removed. The terror gave all of this a machinery: the national operations of 1937 and 1938 shot over a hundred thousand people simply for being Polish. Economic transformation supplied the first wave of suffering, fear of the frontier supplied the deportations, and the fear of nationalism turned a change of policy into persecution.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the state was afraid of in the borderlands, and what collectivisation did to peoples who did not farm settled land.","Build three reasons: economic policy applied to unsuitable peoples, suspicion of cross-border loyalties, and the retreat from indigenisation.","Start with the Kazakh herdsmen and say plainly what collectivising a nomadic people meant in practice."],"codes":["30-4 — 4 The position of women and ethnic minorities: The persecution of ethnic minorities","30-4 — 1 Agriculture and collectivisation: The organisation of collectives"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the results of Stalin's economic policies for the Soviet people in the years 1928 to 1941? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 for SPaG; no stimulus points are printed. Level 4 (13-16) needs an explained evaluation reviewing both views, precise analysis of how the differences of view are conveyed, precisely selected context and a justified judgement. Credit agreement from the famine, livestock losses, wages and labour laws, and disagreement from output growth, the end of unemployment, education and survival after 1941.","sol":"Interpretation 2 argues that Stalin's transformation was more apparent than real and was bought by force: the output tables 'measure effort, not achievement', herds fell by half, grain was exported while people starved, the new worker 'ate worse in 1933 than in 1928', and what grew fastest was 'the number of people who could be compelled'. Because the question asks about the results for the Soviet people, the criterion has to be what the decade did to the households that lived through it, and on that test Interpretation 2 is largely right. The countryside bears it out first. Peasants slaughtered their animals rather than hand them over, so cattle and horse numbers roughly halved between 1928 and 1933 and had not recovered by 1941; quotas were taken before a collective fed itself; and the famine of 1932 to 1933 in Ukraine, the North Caucasus and Kazakhstan killed several million people while grain still went abroad to pay for machinery. The law of 7 August 1932 made taking gleanings a capital offence and the internal passports of December 1932 were withheld from collective farmers, so the starving could not lawfully leave. In the towns the picture supports the interpretation's second half. Rationing lasted from 1929 to 1935, real wages fell below their 1928 level, and discipline hardened from dismissal for a day's absence in 1932 to labour books in 1938 and, in June 1940, criminal prosecution for leaving a job without permission. Interpretation 1 puts the other case, and it is stronger than Interpretation 2 allows. Coal output rose from around 35 million tonnes in 1928 to around 128 million by 1937, steel roughly quadrupled, Magnitogorsk and the Dnieper dam were 'built from nothing', unemployment was declared abolished in 1930, and whole industries existed by 1941 that had not existed in 1928. For many people that meant real gain: villages got schools and clinics, the 1939 census claimed that 81.2 per cent of those aged nine and over could read, and hundreds of thousands of workers' children were pushed through technical schools into posts the purges had emptied. Interpretation 1's last claim is the hardest to answer, that without those factories the country would not have survived 1941. The two historians are quarrelling less about facts than about the yardstick, and saying so is part of the evaluation. Interpretation 1 judges by the state's own aims and by national survival, which is why it reaches for tonnages and for 1941; Interpretation 2 judges by the barrack room and the ration, which is why it reaches for herds, hunger and compulsion. Applying the criterion the question sets, I agree with Interpretation 2 about the results for the Soviet people, with one qualification. A peasant's son who became an engineer had reason to think the decade had transformed his life. But those gains were concentrated in a minority, while the losses fell on whole populations, and the means, famine, deportation and the criminal law of labour, were not incidental to the growth but the way it was produced. Interpretation 1 would only win if the survival of the state in 1941 were allowed to settle the account for everyone who paid for it, and the question does not permit that, because it asks about the people rather than the power.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["The words 'for the Soviet people' are doing the work in this question; decide what test they set before you plan a single paragraph.","Use Interpretation 1 as the case against and give it its strongest form, then measure both against dated evidence rather than against each other's wording.","Open by putting Interpretation 2's claim in your own words and naming the criterion your judgement will use."],"codes":["30-4 — 2 Changes in industry: The successes and failures of industrialisation","30-4 — 3 Life in the Soviet Union: Living and working conditions","30-4 — 3 Life in the Soviet Union: The differing experiences of social groups"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the effects of collectivisation on the Soviet countryside? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 for SPaG; no stimulus points are printed. Level 4 (13-16) needs an explained evaluation of both views, precise analysis of how they convey their difference, precisely selected context and a justified judgement. Credit agreement from livestock losses, famine, exports and coercion, and disagreement from procurement, mechanisation, private plots and the political control the state gained.","sol":"Interpretation 2 says that collectivisation 'destroyed the countryside it was supposed to modernise': peasants killed their animals rather than hand them over, herds fell by half, and grain taken by force was exported while millions starved in Ukraine and Kazakhstan. Interpretation 1 answers that collectivisation 'gave the state the grain it could never buy under the New Economic Policy', fed the new cities, paid for imported machinery and put tractors into villages that had never seen one. The judgement turns on what collectivisation is being measured against, so the criterion must be stated: as farming, as feeding the country, or as government. Measured as farming, Interpretation 2 is plainly right. The drive of 1929 and 1930 was met by slaughter of livestock, and cattle and horse numbers roughly halved between 1928 and 1933 and had still not recovered by 1941, which stripped the collectives of draught power and manure. Dekulakisation removed many of the ablest farmers; villages were run by chairmen sent from the towns; and mechanisation arrived thinly, since the Machine Tractor Stations served many villages each and took payment in grain. The state's own retreat is the clearest admission of failure: from 1935 collective farmers were allowed private plots and a cow, and those tiny plots produced a share of the country's vegetables, milk and eggs out of all proportion to their acreage. Measured as feeding the country, Interpretation 2 is right about the short run and understates the long. State procurements rose steeply even in years when the harvest fell, which is precisely why the famine of 1932 to 1933 happened, and the law of 7 August 1932 and the withholding of internal passports made it impossible to escape. Measured as government, however, Interpretation 1 has the better of it, and Interpretation 2 hardly engages with the point. By 1941 almost every peasant household was inside a collective, and the party controlled the land, the machinery and the movement of the people on it for the first time in Russian history; the towns and the army were fed through the 1930s, and grain exports bought machine tools the plan could not have done without. The two views are conveyed in different registers, and that carries the argument. Interpretation 1 speaks in national aggregates and in what the state achieved, so the villages appear only as suppliers of grain; Interpretation 2 speaks of animals, households and the dead, so the state appears only as a taker. Neither vocabulary can produce the other's conclusion. I agree with Interpretation 2 about the effects on the countryside itself, which is what this question asks. Soviet agriculture did not become more productive in the 1930s, it became more extractable, and the price was a famine and a generation of ruined villages. Interpretation 1 is describing something real, but it is describing collectivisation as an instrument of the state rather than as a change in the countryside, and on the countryside's own account the case for it is very weak.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["This question narrows the ground to the countryside, so decide first whether you are judging collectivisation as farming, as supply, or as control.","Give Interpretation 1 its strongest case, which is procurement and political control, before you decide against it.","Begin by restating Interpretation 2's claim about the countryside in your own words and naming the test you will apply."],"codes":["30-4 — 1 Agriculture and collectivisation: The successes and failures of collectivisation","30-4 — 1 Agriculture and collectivisation: The organisation of collectives","30-4 — 1 Agriculture and collectivisation: Stalin's reasons for changes in agriculture"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about what industrial workers gained from the Five-Year Plans? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 for SPaG; no stimulus points are printed. Level 4 (13-16) needs an explained evaluation of both views, precise analysis of how each conveys its view, precisely selected context and a justified judgement. Credit agreement from barracks, rationing, falling real wages, raised norms and the labour laws, and disagreement from full employment, training, promotion and the end of rationing in 1935.","sol":"Interpretation 2 says that the industrial worker of the 1930s gained very little: he 'lived in a barrack or a corner of someone else's room, ate worse in 1933 than in 1928, and after 1940 could be prosecuted for changing jobs', while factories reported figures they had not made. Interpretation 1 answers that 'unemployment disappeared while millions of peasants were trained, promoted and educated'. The criterion has to be what a worker could actually count at the end of the decade, in wages, in security, in housing and in prospects, and on most of those Interpretation 2 has the stronger case. On living conditions it is very largely right. The urban population roughly doubled between the census of 1926 and that of 1939, and the newcomers slept in barracks, dugouts or a corner of a communal flat with shared kitchens and taps; rationing lasted in the towns from 1929 until 1935, and real wages in the middle of the First Five-Year Plan fell below their 1928 level. On the work itself it is right too. The Stakhanovite movement after August 1935 turned exceptional figures into ordinary norms, so workers produced more for no more pay, and the discipline laws ran one way only: dismissal and loss of the room and ration card for a day's absence from 1932, internal passports the same year, labour books in 1938, and in June 1940 criminal prosecution for leaving a job without permission. Interpretation 1's case is not empty, and it has to be given its weight. Unemployment was declared abolished in 1930, which mattered to people who had known none of the security of a wage; rationing ended in 1935; evening courses, technical schools and workers' faculties took peasants' sons into skilled trades and management, and the purges of the later 1930s emptied posts for them to rise into. For a minority the decade was a genuine promotion, and for many more it brought literacy, a clinic and a canteen where there had been none. The difference of view is conveyed through what each historian counts as a gain. Interpretation 1 counts opportunities created by the state, which is why it lists unemployment, training and promotion; Interpretation 2 counts what a household consumed and controlled, which is why it lists the barrack, the food and the law of 1940. Both are describing the same worker. My judgement is that Interpretation 2 is right for the average and Interpretation 1 for the fortunate. If the test is the material standard and the freedom of the ordinary industrial worker, the plans took more than they gave until at least the middle of the decade and tied him to his job at the end of it. If the test is the chance of rising out of the class one was born into, the 1930s offered more than any previous decade in Russia. Since the question asks what workers gained rather than what a few workers achieved, I agree with Interpretation 2, while allowing that the ladder Interpretation 1 describes was real and that its existence is part of why the system was endured.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide what counts as a gain for a worker before you start: wages and housing, or opportunity and security of employment.","Use Interpretation 1 to build the counter-case about promotion and training, and support both sides with dated laws and figures.","Open by restating Interpretation 2's claim about the worker in your own words and naming the test your judgement will use."],"codes":["30-4 — 3 Life in the Soviet Union: Living and working conditions","30-4 — 3 Life in the Soviet Union: The differing experiences of social groups","30-4 — 2 Changes in industry: The successes and failures of industrialisation"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In which month and year did Stalin announce the liquidation of the kulaks as a class?","a":"December 1929, in his speech to a conference of Marxist students of the agrarian question."},{"q":"How much coal was Alexei Stakhanov credited with cutting in a single night shift in August 1935?","a":"102 tonnes, about fourteen times his quota, at the Central Irmino mine in the Donbass."},{"q":"What did the law of 7 August 1932 make punishable by ten years in a camp or by death?","a":"Taking anything from a collective farm, down to gleanings left in the field after the harvest."},{"q":"Which nationality was deported wholesale from the Soviet Far East to Central Asia in 1937?","a":"The Koreans, about 170,000 of them, the first entire nationality deported by the Soviet state."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The New Economic Policy and the grain procurement crisis","back":"From 1921 peasants paid a tax in kind and sold the rest. In 1927 and 1928 they held grain back at low state prices, procurements fell short, cities went short of bread and exports dried up.","code":"30-4 — 1 Agriculture and collectivisation: Stalin's reasons for changes in agriculture","open":false},{"front":"Kolkhoz, sovkhoz and the Machine Tractor Stations","back":"A kolkhoz pooled the village's land, tools and animals; a sovkhoz was state-owned and paid wages. Tractors were held centrally at Machine Tractor Stations, which served several villages each and were paid in grain.","code":"30-4 — 1 Agriculture and collectivisation: The organisation of collectives","open":false},{"front":"Dekulakisation","back":"Announced in December 1929 as the liquidation of the kulaks as a class. The OGPU, party activists and some twenty-five thousand town workers shot, imprisoned or deported kulak households; the label was often used against anyone who resisted.","code":"30-4 — 1 Agriculture and collectivisation: The organisation of collectives","open":false},{"front":"'Dizzy with Success', 2 March 1930","back":"Stalin's article in Pravda after about half of peasant households had been forced into collectives. He blamed over-zealous local officials; millions promptly left, and the drive resumed later that year.","code":"30-4 — 1 Agriculture and collectivisation: The organisation of collectives","open":false},{"front":"The famine of 1932 to 1933","back":"Worst in Ukraine, the North Caucasus and Kazakhstan. Quotas were taken before the collectives fed themselves and grain was still exported; the law of 7 August 1932 and internal passports stopped people escaping. Several million died.","code":"30-4 — 1 Agriculture and collectivisation: The organisation of collectives","open":false},{"front":"The balance sheet of collectivisation by 1941","back":"State procurements rose steeply and the towns were fed; almost every household was in a collective. But cattle and horses, roughly halved by 1933, had not recovered, and from 1935 private plots produced far more than their acreage.","code":"30-4 — 1 Agriculture and collectivisation: The successes and failures of collectivisation","open":false},{"front":"Stalin's motives for rapid industrialisation","back":"Fear of invasion after the 1927 war scare and his 1931 warning of being fifty to a hundred years behind; the stagnation of the NEP; socialism in one country; and the defeat of Bukharin and the right.","code":"30-4 — 2 Changes in industry: Stalin's motives for rapid industrialisation","open":false},{"front":"Gosplan and the Five-Year Plans","back":"Gosplan, the planning commission of 1921, set the targets. The First Plan ran from 1928 and was declared done in four years, the Second from 1933 to 1937, the Third from 1938 until the invasion of June 1941.","code":"30-4 — 2 Changes in industry: Gosplan, Five-Year Plans and the Stakhanovite Movement","open":false},{"front":"Alexei Stakhanov","back":"A miner at the Central Irmino pit in the Donbass, credited in August 1935 with cutting 102 tonnes of coal in one night shift, about fourteen times his quota, with helpers clearing his way. Record figures then became everyone's norms.","code":"30-4 — 2 Changes in industry: Gosplan, Five-Year Plans and the Stakhanovite Movement","open":false},{"front":"Soviet industrial output, 1928 to 1937","back":"Coal rose from about 35 million tonnes to about 128 million; steel roughly quadrupled and electricity multiplied several times. Magnitogorsk, the Dnieper dam, the White Sea canal and the Moscow Metro were built.","code":"30-4 — 2 Changes in industry: The successes and failures of industrialisation","open":false},{"front":"The costs of industrialisation","back":"Consumer goods starved, quality poor and building shoddy because the plan rewarded quantity; targets met on paper by falsification; huge labour turnover from a raw, untrained workforce; and canals, gold and coal dug by camp prisoners.","code":"30-4 — 2 Changes in industry: The successes and failures of industrialisation","open":false},{"front":"Life in the new industrial towns","back":"The urban population roughly doubled between the 1926 and 1939 censuses. Barracks and communal flats, rationing from 1929 to 1935, real wages below their 1928 level, dismissal for a day's absence from 1932 and labour books from 1938.","code":"30-4 — 3 Life in the Soviet Union: Living and working conditions","open":false},{"front":"Winners and losers under Stalin","back":"Winners: party officials with closed shops and dachas, managers and specialists, Stakhanovites, workers promoted into posts the purges emptied. Losers: collective farmers without passports, 'former people', deported kulaks and camp prisoners.","code":"30-4 — 3 Life in the Soviet Union: The differing experiences of social groups","open":false},{"front":"Women's work and family life under Stalin","back":"Women rose from roughly a quarter of industrial workers in the late 1920s to about two-fifths by 1940, with crèches to match. But the Zhenotdel closed in 1930 and the 1936 Family Code banned abortion and made divorce costly.","code":"30-4 — 4 The position of women and ethnic minorities: Changes in family life and employment","open":false},{"front":"The persecution of ethnic minorities","back":"Kazakh herdsmen forcibly settled, over a million dead by 1933; Poles and Germans cleared from Ukraine's border districts; about 170,000 Koreans deported in 1937; Russian made compulsory in schools in 1938 and Cyrillic imposed.","code":"30-4 — 4 The position of women and ethnic minorities: The persecution of ethnic minorities","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The sources and interpretations written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Source A: From a report written for his district party committee by a factory worker from Leningrad, one of the town volunteers sent into the villages to help set up collective farms. Lower Volga region, March 1930. Written for this room in the style of such a report; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — I reached the village three weeks ago with two comrades and a list of households. The list names eleven families as kulaks, though four of them work their land with no hired hands and one is a widow with a mare. We are told the village must be fully collectivised before the sowing, and most of it has signed, but I will not pretend the signatures were given willingly. In the week before the meeting at which the barns were declared common property, half the village slaughtered its pigs and ate them; there is meat now in houses that have never had meat in Lent. On Tuesday the women drove our chairman out of the dairy with sticks and took their cows home again, and the men stood at the gate and said it was only the women. We have no tractor here, and the nearest station has three machines for eleven villages. Tell me what figure I am to enter for sown acreage.","Source B: From a letter written by a young building worker at Magnitogorsk to his elder brother in Tver, October 1933. Written for this room in the style of a worker's letter home; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — You ask whether I am sorry I came. Some days I am. Eleven of us sleep in one barrack room and in winter the ice is on the inside of the wall by morning; we queue for the canteen and what we get is cabbage soup and black bread, and in the spring there was a month when there was no soap in the whole settlement. I have seen men leave after a fortnight and walk to the station. But the blast furnace whose foundations I helped to dig is standing and it is pouring iron, and when the newspaper printed the brigade's names mine was among them. I am learning to read a drawing at the evening course; at home I should be behind a plough. If you come, bring boots, and do not bring your wife until I have got us a room.","Source C: From a report by the director of a machine-building works in the Urals to his commissariat in Moscow, January 1936. Written for this room in the style of such a report; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — I am asked to explain why the works did not meet its December programme, and I shall be frank. Since the autumn we have held eleven record shifts. On the best of them the record-breaker had two fitters clearing his machine and a store of prepared blanks at his elbow, and the figure he set is now the norm for the whole shop. The men understand this arithmetic very well, and three of my best turners have collected their pay and gone to the tractor plant, where I am told the norms are easier. My lathes are run at speeds they were not built for and seven of my forty machines are standing under repair. I can meet the plan for the quarter if the commissariat sends the steel that was promised in October and does not raise the norm again in March.","Interpretation 1: From a general economic history of the Soviet Union, by a historian writing in the 1960s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — Judged by what it set out to do, Stalin's economic revolution worked. In little more than a decade a country of wooden ploughs became the world's second industrial power. Collectivisation gave the state the grain it could never buy under the New Economic Policy, and that grain fed the new cities and paid for imported machinery; tractors reached villages that had never seen one. Coal, steel and electricity output multiplied, Magnitogorsk and the Dnieper dam were built from nothing, and unemployment disappeared while millions of peasants were trained, promoted and educated. The cost was terrible and nobody should hide it, but without the factories of the 1930s the Soviet Union would not have survived 1941.","Interpretation 2: From a social history of the Stalin years, by a historian writing in the 2000s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — The output tables of the 1930s measure effort, not achievement. Collectivisation destroyed the countryside it was supposed to modernise: peasants killed their animals rather than hand them over, herds fell by half, and grain taken by force was still being exported while millions starved in Ukraine and Kazakhstan. In the towns the new worker lived in a barrack or a corner of someone else's room, ate worse in 1933 than in 1928, and after 1940 could be prosecuted for changing jobs. Factories reported figures they had not made and built badly because the plan rewarded quantity. What grew fastest in Stalin's Russia was not productivity but the number of people who could be compelled.","How the room uses them: Source A is the inference source for Question 1. Sources B and C are the contemporary pair for the 8-mark usefulness question, 3(a), and may be used to help explain the difference in 3(c). Interpretations 1 and 2 are the pair for 3(b), 3(c) and 3(d). Every quotation in the model answers and worked examples of this room comes from the five texts above, with one exception: the 3(b) worked example prints a second, shorter pair of interpretations of its own, numbered 3 and 4, which are also written for this room."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 3 Question 3(d), 16 marks)","items":["Question: 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the effect of Stalin's economic policies on how well the Soviet people were fed in the years 1928 to 1941? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]. This is one paragraph from the middle of the answer, putting the case for Interpretation 2 on the food that reached people's tables.","Interpretation 2's claim that grain 'taken by force was still being exported while millions starved in Ukraine and Kazakhstan' is the part of its argument that the evidence supports most completely. Procurement quotas were fixed in advance on expected yields and collected before the collective fed itself, so when the 1932 harvest came in short the whole of the shortfall fell on the villages that had grown it. Selling abroad went on through the worst of it: about 1.7 million tonnes of grain left the country in each of 1932 and 1933, down from roughly 5 million in 1931 but still grain leaving a country in which several million people were dying of hunger. Nor could the starving move to find food, because the law of 7 August 1932 made taking so much as gleanings from a collective field punishable by ten years in a camp or by death, and the internal passports introduced in December 1932 were not issued to collective farmers. In the towns the story is milder but points the same way: bread was rationed from 1929 and the cards were not withdrawn until 1 January 1935, with meat, fats and sugar following that October, which is why Interpretation 2 can say the worker 'ate worse in 1933 than in 1928'. Interpretation 1 answers that the grain 'fed the new cities', and on its own terms that is true; but it is a claim about where the food went, not about whether there was more of it, and the question asks how well people were fed. On that measure Interpretation 2 is convincing here, because a policy that moved food from the people who grew it to the people the state needed most did not put more on the table - it decided who went without.","Why this is Level 4: it 'provides an explained evaluation reviewing the alternative views in coming to a substantiated judgement', since Interpretation 1's strongest counter-claim, that the grain 'fed the new cities', is stated and then measured rather than ignored. The analysis is precise about how the difference of view is conveyed - Interpretation 2 speaks of what households ate and of the dead, Interpretation 1 of what the state was able to collect and move - and the contextual knowledge is precisely selected rather than general: the export figures of 1932 and 1933 set against 1931, the law of 7 August 1932, the withholding of internal passports from collective farmers, and the dates on which bread and then the rest of the ration were withdrawn. The line of reasoning is sustained and the closing sentence judges against the criterion the question sets instead of summarising."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same as above - 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the effect of Stalin's economic policies on how well the Soviet people were fed in the years 1928 to 1941?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks].","Interpretation 2 says that people did not have enough to eat and that grain was sent abroad while they were starving, and I mostly agree with this. The peasants had to hand their grain over to the government and there was a terrible famine in which a lot of people died, and they were not allowed to go somewhere else to look for food. Workers in the towns also did not have much to eat and had to queue for what there was. However, Interpretation 1 says that the grain fed the new cities and paid for imported machinery, which shows that the policy did work in some ways and helped the country later on in the war. Overall I think both interpretations have a point, because the country got stronger but ordinary people went hungry.","Why this is Level 2 and not Level 3: it does offer 'valid evaluative comment to agree with or counter the interpretation' and it does select details from both interpretations, which is what Level 2 asks for. What it misses is Level 3's requirement that 'relevant contextual knowledge is used directly to support the evaluation': 'a lot of people died', 'did not have much to eat' and 'had to queue' are assertions where the grain exported in 1932 and 1933, the famine of 1932 to 1933, the law of 7 August 1932 and the ending of bread rationing on 1 January 1935 should be. It never shows how either interpretation conveys its view, and the judgement is announced in the last sentence rather than reasoned towards, so 'its justification is insecure or undeveloped and a line of reasoning is not sustained'. Turning it into a Level 3 answer means replacing every general phrase with a dated fact that does evaluative work."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: agriculture, collectivisation and the famine, 1927 to 1941","items":["The New Economic Policy, in place from 1921, let peasants sell their surplus once they had paid a tax in kind; the state had to buy grain rather than requisition it.","In 1927 and 1928 peasants withheld grain at the low state prices; procurement fell far short, cities went short of bread and grain exports dried up.","January 1928: Stalin toured Siberia in person and ordered officials to seize grain and prosecute those hoarding it.","A kolkhoz pooled the village's land, tools and livestock; a sovkhoz was a state farm paying wages. Machine Tractor Stations held the tractors and combines and were paid in grain.","December 1929: Stalin announced the liquidation of the kulaks as a class. Households graded as kulak were shot, sent to camps or deported north and east, and about twenty-five thousand town workers were sent out in 1930 to drive the process.","Peasants resisted by burning barns, attacking officials and slaughtering livestock: cattle and horse numbers roughly halved between 1928 and 1933 and had not recovered by 1941.","2 March 1930: 'Dizzy with Success' appeared in Pravda after about half of peasant households had been collectivised. Stalin blamed local officials, millions left the collectives, and the drive resumed later that year.","The famine of 1932 to 1933 struck Ukraine, the North Caucasus and Kazakhstan; several million died, with estimates for Ukraine alone commonly put between three and five million. Grain exports continued through it.","The law of 7 August 1932 made theft of collective property, down to gleanings from a harvested field, punishable by ten years in a camp or death.","Internal passports were introduced in December 1932 and were not issued to collective farmers, so peasants could not lawfully move to the towns.","From the Collective Farm Charter of 1935 households could keep a small private plot and a cow; those plots produced a share of the country's vegetables, milk and eggs far larger than their acreage.","By 1935 about nine tenths of sown land was collectivised, and by 1941 almost every peasant household was in a collective. State procurements rose steeply even in years when the harvest fell; the harvest of 1937 was the best of the decade."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Gosplan, the Five-Year Plans and the Stakhanovite movement","items":["Gosplan, the state planning commission, was set up in 1921 and turned political targets into figures for each commissariat, region, factory and shop.","The First Five-Year Plan ran from 1928 and was declared fulfilled in four years; it concentrated on coal, iron, steel, oil and electricity.","The Second Five-Year Plan ran from 1933 to 1937, adding transport and some consumer goods; the Third began in 1938, turned increasingly to armaments and was cut short by the German invasion of June 1941.","Showpieces: Magnitogorsk, a steel city raised on the Ural steppe from 1929; the Dnieper dam, completed in 1932; the White Sea canal, dug by forced labour and opened in 1933; the first line of the Moscow Metro, opened in 1935.","Foreign engineers, many of them American and German, were hired to design and start up the largest plants, and machinery was imported and paid for largely with exported grain and timber.","Coal output rose from about 35 million tonnes in 1928 to about 128 million by 1937; steel roughly quadrupled and electricity generation multiplied several times over.","The government declared unemployment abolished in 1930, closing the last labour exchanges.","Alexei Stakhanov, a miner at the Central Irmino pit in the Donbass, was credited on a night shift in August 1935 with cutting 102 tonnes of coal, about fourteen times his quota, with helpers clearing the coal and setting his props.","Stakhanovites received bonuses, medals, apartments and newspaper fame; their figures were then used to raise the norms of whole shops, which made the movement unpopular with ordinary workers and with managers.","Failure was treated as sabotage: the Shakhty trial of 1928 put engineers from a Donbass coal town on trial for wrecking, and managers who missed targets risked the same charge.","The plans' weaknesses were consumer goods starved of investment, poor quality and shoddy building because the plan rewarded quantity, falsified returns, hoarding of materials by managers, and enormous labour turnover.","A large share of the hardest work - canals, timber, the gold of Kolyma and the coal of Vorkuta - was done by prisoners of the camp system run by the NKVD."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: living and working conditions and the social groups of Stalin's Soviet Union","items":["The urban population roughly doubled between the census of 1926 and that of 1939, from around 26 million to around 56 million.","New workers lived in barracks, dugouts, tents on the building sites, or a single room of a communal flat with shared kitchen and tap.","Food was rationed in the towns from 1929; bread rationing ended in 1935, after which prices were high but goods could be bought.","Real wages fell during the First Five-Year Plan and were below their 1928 level in the middle of the decade.","From 1932 a single day's unexplained absence could cost a worker the job, the ration card and the room that came with it; internal passports were introduced the same year and labour books in 1938.","A decree of June 1940 lengthened the working week and made leaving a job without permission a criminal offence.","Collective farmers were paid in kind out of what remained after procurement, according to labour days worked, and having no passports could not lawfully leave the village.","Against this, schooling, clinics, crèches and canteens spread widely, and the 1939 census claimed that 81.2 per cent of those aged nine and over could read.","Party and state officials, the nomenklatura, had closed shops, cars, dachas and holiday homes; from 1931 Stalin openly attacked wage levelling, so managers and specialists were paid many times an unskilled wage.","Hundreds of thousands of workers and workers' children were pushed through technical schools and workers' faculties into management, filling posts emptied by the purges of the later 1930s.","At the bottom were collective farmers, 'former people' such as priests, traders and the children of the old middle class who were barred from good jobs and schools, deported kulaks, and camp prisoners.","Churches, mosques and synagogues were closed in large numbers and religious practice was attacked as backward, while socialist realism made art, film and the press instruments of the plan."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: women, the family and the nationalities, 1924 to 1941","items":["Abortion had been legalised in 1920 and divorce made easy after the revolution; the Zhenotdel, the party's women's department founded in 1919, was closed in 1930.","The Family Code of 1936 banned abortion except on medical grounds, made divorce slow and expensive and paid allowances to mothers of large families, to raise a birth rate cut by collectivisation and famine.","Women rose from roughly a quarter of industrial workers in the late 1920s to about two fifths by 1940; crèche and kindergarten places, canteens and laundries were expanded to make this possible.","Medicine and teaching became heavily female professions, and women entered engineering and technical work in numbers unknown before 1928.","Women still carried the queuing, cooking and childcare on top of a full working day, the 'double burden' complained of in the Soviet press itself.","Politically the position did not change: no woman sat in Stalin's Politburo. Alexandra Kollontai, the first woman to hold a commissariat in 1917, spent the Stalin years abroad as an ambassador.","Showpiece heroines were used instead: in September 1938 Valentina Grizodubova, Polina Osipenko and Marina Raskova flew the aircraft Rodina on a record non-stop flight and became the first women made Heroes of the Soviet Union.","In the 1920s Soviet policy had encouraged minority languages, alphabets and local officials; from the mid-1930s this was reversed for fear it bred nationalism.","Russian was made a compulsory school subject throughout the Union in 1938, and the Latin alphabets devised for many minority languages were replaced by Cyrillic.","Collectivisation destroyed the Kazakh herding economy: well over a million Kazakhs died in the famine of 1931 to 1933, perhaps a third of the people, and many drove their herds across the border into China.","Ukraine's party and cultural leadership was purged alongside the famine; the commissar for education Mykola Skrypnyk shot himself in 1933.","Border nationalities were deported: Poles and Germans were cleared from Ukraine's border districts in 1935 and 1936, and about 170,000 Koreans were moved from the Far East to Central Asia in 1937 — the first whole nationality to be deported.","The national operations of the terror in 1937 and 1938 shot over a hundred thousand people in the Polish operation alone, with parallel operations against Germans, Latvians, Finns and Greeks."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the source and interpretation moves this paper rewards","items":["Inference: a claim the source makes certain without stating it. Give the claim first, then the words from the source that force it, and never use the same detail twice.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose: what kind of document it is, who produced it and when, and what it was for.","Usefulness is always usefulness for the enquiry named in the question. Copy the enquiry out before you look at the sources.","A source with a purpose is not thereby useless: a director defending a missed programme, or a worker warning his brother what to bring, tells you something precisely because of why it was written.","Contextual knowledge scores nothing unless it is used on the source - to confirm an inference, to qualify it, or to show what the source leaves out.","'Main difference' means a difference of view, not of subject or of wording: reduce each interpretation to one sentence answering the same question, and the gap between them is the difference.","Support a difference with a short quotation from each interpretation. Your own knowledge earns nothing on that question.","Interpretations legitimately differ because historians weigh different evidence, ask slightly different questions, or emphasise different aspects of the same events; saying one is biased is not an explanation.","On the 16-mark evaluation both interpretations must be used, and the top level asks you to show how each conveys its view, not only what it says.","Name the criterion your judgement will use in the opening and apply the same one throughout; a conclusion that says both are partly right without a test is not a substantiated judgement."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 3 Question 3(d): 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part compulsion played in the economic changes of the years 1928 to 1941?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","items":["The question as printed: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part compulsion played in the economic changes of the years 1928 to 1941? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' No stimulus points are printed on this question: the two interpretations are the stimulus, both must be used, and an answer that evaluates one in isolation cannot reach the top levels.","Decode the statement. Interpretation 2 claims that force was the engine and not merely the cost of the transformation: grain was 'taken by force', the worker 'after 1940 could be prosecuted for changing jobs', and 'what grew fastest in Stalin's Russia was not productivity but the number of people who could be compelled'. Interpretation 1 claims that judged by its own aims the revolution worked, that millions of peasants were 'trained, promoted and educated', and that although 'the cost was terrible' the factories saved the country in 1941. The words 'the part compulsion played' set the criterion, and it must be stated in the opening: the test is whether force was how the change was produced, not whether force occurred.","Opening, three or four sentences: put Interpretation 2's claim in your own words, name Interpretation 1 as the rival weighting, announce the criterion, and signal where you will end, so the line of reasoning is visible from the first paragraph.","Paragraph 1, for Interpretation 2 - the countryside. Dekulakisation deported well over a million people in 1930 and 1931 to the North, the Urals, Siberia and Kazakhstan; quotas were taken before the collective fed itself; the law of 7 August 1932 made taking gleanings from a collective field punishable by ten years in a camp or by death; internal passports from December 1932 were withheld from collective farmers. Interim judgement: in the villages the policy was carried out by force from the first winter, not driven into force when it went wrong.","Paragraph 2, for Interpretation 2 - the factory. From November 1932 a single day's unexcused absence cost a worker his job, his ration card and the room that came with it; labour books were introduced in December 1938; the decree of 26 June 1940 made leaving a job without permission a criminal offence, punished absence, and brought in the eight-hour day and the seven-day working week. Interim judgement: when the plan could not hold workers by paying them, it held them by law.","Paragraph 3, for Interpretation 2 - labour that was not free at all. Prisoners of the camps dug the White Sea canal in about twenty months and it opened in August 1933, and many thousands of them died doing it; the deported kulaks were set to work as special settlers in the timber and mining regions they were sent to. Interim judgement: a part of the industrial record was produced by people who could not have left.","Paragraph 4, against Interpretation 2 - the pull as well as the push. Some twenty-five thousand town workers volunteered for the villages in 1930 and Komsomol members were mobilised for the new building sites; wage differentials were deliberately widened after wage levelling was condemned in 1931; piece rates spread; and Stakhanovites from 1935 were rewarded with money, rooms and fame. Interim judgement: the state used incentive as deliberately as it used the law, and many people moved because they wanted to.","Paragraph 5, against Interpretation 2 and the point that goes furthest beyond both texts - who was carried up by it. Workers and workers' children were pushed through the technical schools during the First Five-Year Plan and into engineers' and managers' chairs, many of them into posts emptied by the purges of 1937 and 1938; schooling reached villages that had had none, and the 1939 census claimed that 81.2 per cent of those aged nine and over could read. Interim judgement: for that minority the decade was a promotion, and compulsion alone does not explain why they backed the regime that ran it.","The evaluation thread that must run through every paragraph: how each interpretation conveys its view. Interpretation 1 judges by the state's own aims and speaks in national aggregates, so force appears in it as 'the cost'; Interpretation 2 judges by the household and speaks of animals, barracks and prosecution, so force appears as the method. Saying this, and using it, is what the top level means by showing how the differences of view are conveyed.","What the conclusion must say: not 'both are partly right'. Apply the criterion announced at the start - the question asks what part compulsion played, so the measure is how the growth was actually produced, and on that measure Interpretation 2 is largely convincing, since the grain, the labour and the discipline were all obtained by law or by force, and the incentives were offered inside a system nobody was free to leave. Then name the deciding consideration: Interpretation 1 could only win if the volunteers and the promoted could be shown to have produced the bulk of what was built, and they cannot.","Timing and SPaG: about 35 minutes in all — six to fix the criterion and order the paragraphs, about twenty-six to write, and the last three to check spelling and punctuation and to make sure the specialist terms are present and correctly used, since 4 additional marks depend on 'a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate'. Useful terms here: collectivisation, kolkhoz, sovkhoz, kulak, dekulakisation, procurement, quota, Gosplan, Five-Year Plan, Stakhanovite, norm, nomenklatura, rationing, internal passport."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:16:49Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Judged afresh from the kit file after the dedupe retargeting. Every new fact in the five worked examples, the model-paragraph pair and the plan checks out on the web: the decree of 15 November 1932 (dismissal, ration card and enterprise room for one day's unexcused absence), labour books of 20 December 1938, the decree of 26 June 1940 (criminal penalty for leaving a job, eight-hour day, seven-day week), the law of 10 July 1940 (defective goods as wrecking, five to eight years for directors and chief engineers), coal about 35.4 million tonnes in 1928 to about 128 million in 1937 with steel roughly quadrupled, grain exports 5.2 million tonnes in 1931 against 1.73 in 1932 and 1.68 in 1933, bread rationing ending 1 January 1935 with the rest that October, the law of 7 August 1932 (death or not less than ten years, gleanings included), internal passports of 27 December 1932 withheld from collective farmers, the White Sea canal dug in about twenty months and opened 2 August 1933, Stalin's six conditions of 23 June 1931 (organised recruitment by contract with the collective farms first, wage levelling condemned), the urban population 26.3 million in 1926 to 55.9 million in 1939 and the 1939 census literacy of 81.2 per cent of those aged nine and over, and Magnitogorsk from 1929. Around forty dates, figures and events were checked in all, fourteen of them on the web. Every quotation in the retargeted items matches the printed Sources A to C and Interpretations 1 and 2 word for word, and the Level 4, Level 3 and Level 2 descriptors quoted in extras[1] and extras[2] are verbatim from the Paper 3 Question 3(d) grid in levels.md. No stem reproduces the Paper 3 specimen Option 30 questions, which are the February 1917 inference, Stalin's emergence by 1929 and the four Civil War parts of Question 3. The caps ('Maximum 8 marks ... no access to Level 4 ... three or more aspects of content', no Level 2 cap), the 2(a)/2(b) choice and the 90-minute timing all match the current wording. node dupscan.js EDX-1HI0 30-4 prints 'no duplication'. One length breach¹ and three presentational points below; no answer key is wrong, so this is publishable as it stands."}}},"31-1":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"31-1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 1 is where Option 31 begins and where Paper 3 keeps returning: the Section A source is often a document from 1918 to 1923, the 12-mark causation question is regularly set on the birth of the Republic, its early enemies or the crisis of 1923, and Section B's interpretations are very often an argument about how far the Republic had really recovered by 1929. Everything in Key topic 2 depends on it, because you cannot explain why Germans turned to Hitler unless you can first explain what they thought the Republic had done to them and for them. This room teaches the eleven years as one argument you can take a side in — a republic born out of defeat, blamed for a treaty it did not cause, nearly destroyed in 1923, rebuilt on borrowed money after it — and it trains the five moves the paper rewards: inference from a source, causal explanation that goes past the printed bullet points, judging a source's usefulness for the enquiry actually named, spotting how two historians differ and why, and evaluating one of them against the other and against what you know.","idea":[{"code":"31-1 — 1 The origins of the Republic, 1918–19: The situation in Germany at the end of the War","h":"Defeat first, revolution second: Germany in October and November 1918","t":"The Republic was born out of a lost war, and the order of events matters. On 29 September 1918 Ludendorff told the Kaiser that the war could not be won and that an armistice must be sought; a new government under Prince Max von Baden, formed on 3 October, asked President Wilson for terms. Only then did the country break up from below: sailors at Wilhelmshaven refused to sail on 29 October, the mutiny reached Kiel on 3 November, and workers' and soldiers' councils took over city after city, with Kurt Eisner proclaiming a republic in Munich on 8 November. On 9 November 1918 Max von Baden announced Wilhelm II's abdication and handed the chancellorship to Friedrich Ebert of the SPD, while Philipp Scheidemann proclaimed a republic from a Reichstag window; the Kaiser crossed into the Netherlands the next day and signed his abdication on 28 November. A Council of People's Representatives of six men, three from the SPD and three from the USPD, governed under Ebert from 10 November, and Matthias Erzberger signed the armistice in a railway carriage at Compiègne on 11 November. A top-level answer uses that order as the argument: the generals asked for the armistice, the politicians signed it, and the new Republic inherited a defeat it had not caused but could never shake off."},{"code":"31-1 — 1 The origins of the Republic, 1918–19: The strengths and weaknesses of the new Weimar Constitution","h":"The most democratic constitution in Europe, and its two trapdoors","t":"Elections to a National Assembly were held on 19 January 1919, the first in which German women voted; the Assembly met at Weimar on 6 February because Berlin was too disorderly, elected Ebert President on 11 February, and signed a constitution on 11 August 1919. Its strengths were real: every German man and woman over 20 could vote, the Reichstag was elected every four years by proportional representation under Article 22, the President every seven, and a bill of rights guaranteed free speech, freedom of religion and equality before the law. The weaknesses were in the same document. Proportional representation gave seats to very small parties and made single-party majorities almost impossible, so Germany was governed by coalitions that formed and fell repeatedly. Article 48 let the President rule by emergency decree, and Ebert used it well over a hundred times, so the habit of governing without the Reichstag was established by the Republic's friends before its enemies took it up. Beneath the new rules the old judges, civil servants and army officers kept their posts. A top-level answer never lists strengths and weaknesses in two columns: it shows the same feature working both ways — proportional representation was fair and unstable, Article 48 was a safety valve and a back door."},{"code":"31-1 — 2 The early challenges to the Weimar Republic, 1919–23: Reasons for the early unpopularity of the Republic","h":"The stab in the back and the Diktat","t":"The Republic was unpopular before it had done anything, because of two ideas fastened onto it in its first year. The first was the Dolchstoss, the claim that the German army had been undefeated in the field and was betrayed by socialists, republicans and Jews at home; it was untrue, since the army command itself had asked for the armistice, but Hindenburg repeated it before a Reichstag committee of enquiry in 1919 and it stuck. The men who signed the armistice and the treaty became the 'November Criminals'. The second was the Treaty of Versailles, signed on 28 June 1919: Article 231 made Germany accept responsibility for the war; the army was cut to 100,000 men with no conscription, tanks, submarines or air force and a navy of six battleships; the Rhineland was permanently demilitarised; Germany lost about 13 per cent of its territory and all its colonies, including Alsace-Lorraine to France, the Polish Corridor and Posen to Poland, Danzig to the League as a free city, and the Saar coalfield to League administration for fifteen years; union with Austria was forbidden and Germany was excluded from the League; and in 1921 the bill for reparations was fixed at 132 billion gold marks. A top-level answer explains the link: the Republic had no choice but to sign, and its enemies made the signing rather than the defeat the thing to be blamed for."},{"code":"31-1 — 2 The early challenges to the Weimar Republic, 1919–23: Challenges to the Republic from Left and Right","h":"Attacked from both sides: Spartacists, Freikorps and the Kapp Putsch","t":"The Republic's first enemies were armed. On the left, the Spartacus League of Karl Liebknecht and Rosa Luxemburg turned itself into the German Communist Party at the turn of 1918–19 and rose in Berlin from 5 to 12 January 1919, seizing newspaper offices and railway stations. Ebert's defence minister Gustav Noske put the rising down with the Freikorps, volunteer units of demobilised soldiers armed and organised by the army; about 156 insurgents were killed, and Liebknecht and Luxemburg were murdered in custody on 15 January 1919. A Bavarian soviet republic was crushed the same way in the spring. That bargain cost the Republic the loyalty of the left permanently, and it armed the right. On 13 March 1920 the Ehrhardt Brigade of some 5,000 Freikorps men, ordered to disband under Versailles, marched into Berlin and installed Wolfgang Kapp; the army declined to fire on them, the government fled to Dresden and then Stuttgart, and the putsch collapsed on 17 March only because Berlin's trade unions called a general strike that stopped the trains, the power and the water. Political murder followed: Erzberger in August 1921, the foreign minister Walther Rathenau in June 1922, with courts markedly lenient towards right-wing killers. A top-level answer makes the two threats one argument: the Republic survived by borrowing force from men who wanted it dead."},{"code":"31-1 — 2 The early challenges to the Weimar Republic, 1919–23: The challenges of 1923","h":"1923: the Ruhr, the printing presses and the end of money","t":"Germany fell behind with reparations deliveries of coal and timber, and on 11 January 1923 French and Belgian troops occupied the Ruhr to take what was owed directly from the mines and factories. Chancellor Cuno's government ordered passive resistance: workers struck, officials refused to co-operate, and the government printed money to pay the strikers while the country's industrial heartland produced nothing. Around 130 German civilians were killed during the occupation and thousands were expelled from the region. The currency then collapsed: in January 1923 a dollar cost about 17,000 marks and by November about 4.2 trillion. Pensioners, war widows and anyone with savings were ruined, wages were paid twice a day and spent at once, and the countryside would take goods rather than money. Some gained — people with debts and mortgages paid them off in worthless notes, and businessmen bought property cheaply — which is why hyperinflation is a redistribution and not simply a disaster. Gustav Stresemann became Chancellor on 13 August 1923, ended passive resistance in September and issued the Rentenmark on 15 November, a currency deliberately limited in supply and backed by a mortgage on German land and industry; it was replaced by the gold-backed Reichsmark in August 1924. A top-level answer keeps the chain intact — reparations, occupation, passive resistance, printing, collapse — and says who lost what."},{"code":"31-1 — 3 The 'Golden Years': recovery of the Republic, 1924–29: Reasons for economic recovery","h":"Recovery on credit: Stresemann, the Rentenmark, Dawes and Young","t":"The recovery had three legs, and only one of them was German. The first was the currency: the Rentenmark of November 1923 and the Reichsmark of August 1924 restored the one thing a modern economy cannot work without, a unit of account people trusted. The second was the rescheduling of reparations. The Dawes Plan of August 1924 cut the annual payment to one billion marks, rising to two and a half billion after five years, reorganised the Reichsbank under Allied supervision and, crucially, brought a loan of 800 million gold marks, mostly American; French and Belgian troops left the Ruhr by August 1925. The Young Plan, drawn up in 1929, cut the total demanded to about 112 billion gold marks including interest and spread payments to 1988, lowering the annual bill by roughly a fifth. The third leg was foreign lending, most of it American and much of it private: roughly 20 billion marks came into Germany between 1924 and 1930, about half of it short-term, paying for factories, roads, flats, schools and swimming baths, and by 1928–29 industrial production had passed its 1913 level. A top-level answer names the condition attached to all of it: the recovery was genuine but borrowed, and the loans that made it possible could be called in at short notice."},{"code":"31-1 — 3 The 'Golden Years': recovery of the Republic, 1924–29: Stresemann's achievements abroad","h":"Stresemann abroad: Locarno, Geneva and the price of fulfilment","t":"Stresemann was Chancellor for barely a hundred days but foreign minister from 1923 until his death on 3 October 1929, and his method was fulfilment: keep the treaty's terms in order to earn the right to change them. At Locarno in October 1925, signed formally in London that December, Germany, France and Belgium accepted their shared frontiers as final, with Britain and Italy as guarantors and the Rhineland left permanently demilitarised; Germany accepted the western border but refused to accept the eastern one as final, agreeing only to settle disputes with Poland and Czechoslovakia by arbitration. That treaty opened the door to the League of Nations, which Germany joined in September 1926 with a permanent seat on the Council — the clearest possible reversal of the exclusion imposed at Versailles — and Stresemann shared the Nobel Peace Prize with Aristide Briand in 1926. In August 1928 Germany was among the first signatories of the Kellogg–Briand Pact renouncing war, and Allied troops began leaving the Rhineland, quitting the Cologne zone in 1926 and the last of it in 1930. A top-level answer weighs the cost: nationalists called the same treaties a public acceptance of Versailles, which is why the Young Plan campaign of 1929 could be turned against a man who had just restored Germany to the great powers' table."},{"code":"31-1 — 4 Changes in society, 1924–29: Changes in the standard of living","h":"Wages, flats and insurance — and the people the boom missed","t":"For workers in the towns the later 1920s were genuinely better. Real wages rose every year from 1924 and by roughly a quarter between 1925 and 1928, so pay was buying more, not merely more paper. City governments borrowed and built: more than two million homes were built or renovated between 1924 and 1931, many of them in new blocks and estates with bathrooms and electric light, along with schools, hospitals and public baths. The Law on Labour Exchanges and Unemployment Insurance of 1927 made insurance compulsory, paid for by contributions from workers and employers and covering about 16 million workers, so that losing a job stopped being a fall into nothing. But the gains were uneven. Unemployment never fell below about 1.3 million even at the top of the boom and rose above two million in the winter of 1928–29. Farming was in recession before the Wall Street Crash, because world grain prices had been falling since the middle of the decade and many farms carried heavy mortgages. The old middle class whose savings had been wiped out in 1923 got nothing back. A top-level answer therefore answers 'better for whom?' rather than 'better or worse?', and names the group in every sentence."},{"code":"31-1 — 4 Changes in society, 1924–29: Changes in the position of women","h":"The vote, the office and the argument about the 'new woman'","t":"Weimar gave women more legal equality than almost anywhere else in Europe. From the election of 19 January 1919 women over 20 could vote on the same terms as men, and turnout among them was very high; Article 109 of the constitution declared that men and women had the same rights and duties as citizens. Women sat in the National Assembly of 1919 for the first time — about three dozen of them — though through the 1920s they never held much above one Reichstag seat in fifteen. At work the change was in kind rather than quantity: women were roughly a third of the workforce, much as before the war, but many more of them were in offices, shops, teaching, the civil service and the professions rather than in domestic service or on the land, and the young single woman with her own wage packet became a recognisable social type. The limits were plain: women were commonly paid less than men for the same work, married women were pressed to give up public posts, and the churches and conservatives attacked the short-haired, cigarette-smoking 'new woman' of Berlin as proof that the Republic was destroying the family. A top-level answer separates rights, work and leisure, and says in each case how far the change reached beyond the big cities."},{"code":"31-1 — 4 Changes in society, 1924–29: Cultural changes","h":"Bauhaus, canvas and screen: culture as a political argument","t":"Weimar culture was an argument about what Germany should be, which is why it was so bitterly resented. In architecture and design the Bauhaus, founded by Walter Gropius at Weimar in 1919 and moved to Dessau in 1925, rejected ornament for steel, glass and concrete and designed chairs, lamps and typefaces meant to be mass-produced; Gropius's Dessau building is the argument in physical form. In painting, the New Objectivity of artists such as Otto Dix and George Grosz turned away from romantic subjects to maimed veterans, profiteers and city streets, insisting that art report the country as it was. In cinema, the UFA studios at Babelsberg near Berlin were among the largest in the world, and expressionist films — The Cabinet of Dr Caligari in 1920, Fritz Lang's enormously expensive Metropolis in 1927 — made German film internationally admired. Berlin's cabarets, jazz bands and theatres drew visitors from across Europe. To conservatives, the churches and the nationalist press all of this was decadence paid for with American money, un-German and centred on a capital they distrusted. A top-level answer uses that split: cultural change is evidence both of how free the Republic was and of how many Germans it was alienating at the same time."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1: two inferences from Source A","setup":"Source A in this room is a report on conditions in the town written for the provincial government by the mayor of a small industrial town in Saxony, October 1923. He writes that the works now pay wages twice a day and that the wives wait at the gate to take the notes and spend them before the evening price; that the bakers changed the price of a loaf three times on Tuesday and that two of them have closed rather than sell bread for paper; that the deposits in the town's savings bank, gathered over a working lifetime, 'will not now buy a pair of boots'; that the widows of the war come to the relief office in numbers the town cannot meet, so he has asked for police to stand at the door; that farmers from the villages will bring potatoes to market for coal, cloth or soap but not for money; and that in the market square it is said openly that the government's printing presses have done this, while the older people say they knew no such trouble under the Kaiser. The question is: 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about the problems facing local officials in Germany in 1923. Complete the table below to explain your answer.' (4 marks)","steps":["Notice who is writing and to whom before you read for content: a mayor reporting upwards to the provincial government has every reason to show his town as governable, so each difficulty he does admit is one he could not hide.","Take the first inference from the relief office: war widows arriving in numbers the town 'cannot meet' shows that local relief funds had been outrun by prices, so an authority could no longer carry out the ordinary duties expected of it.","Take the second from the request for police to stand at the relief-office door: an official who asks for a guard at his own welfare office expects hardship to turn into disorder, and is warning his superiors of it.","Check that each is an inference and not a quotation: the claim must be something the mayor never states outright, and the detail you cite must make it unavoidable."],"cues":["Who wrote this, and who was meant to read it?","What does 'in numbers we cannot meet' admit?","Why would a mayor want police at his own relief office?","How do you know it is an inference and not a summary?"]},{"title":"Question 3(a): judging Sources B and C for the enquiry actually named","setup":"Source B in this room is a letter of September 1928 from a young woman working as a typist in a Berlin insurance office to her older sister on the family farm in Pomerania: her wage was raised in the spring and buys more than last year's did; she and her flatmate Greta have two rooms in one of the new blocks built by the city, with a bathroom, electric light and a fixed rent; when Hans, whom she names without saying who he is, was out of work for seven weeks in the winter the insurance paid him something every week; on Fridays they go to the cinema and then to a dance hall; her mother writes that she should be married and keeping house; she earns less than the men who do the same work and her director prefers to employ unmarried women, but she has her own money and her vote and will give up neither. Source C is an editorial of June 1929 from a nationalist weekly newspaper in a farming district of East Prussia: Berlin calls these the golden years, but the price of rye has fallen four years running, banks hold mortgages on half the farms in the district and young men are leaving for the cities; the gold is American money lent for a few months at a time to build swimming baths and picture houses, and if New York asks for it back the whole glittering business will fall in; more than a million men are without work in the best month of the year; ministers are applauded in Geneva while foreign soldiers still stand in the Rhineland, and the Young Plan binds Germany’s grandchildren to pay until 1988. The question is: 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the differences between life in the German cities and life in the countryside in the years 1924–29? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context.' (8 marks)","steps":["Copy the enquiry to the top of your plan and let it decide what counts: this one asks you to set two ways of living side by side, so wages, rent, work and prices belong on both sides of the comparison, while Geneva and the Rhineland do not.","Read Source B as the town half of the comparison: a wage that 'buys more than last year's did', two rooms in a new city block with a bathroom, electric light and a fixed rent, and a Friday of cinema and dance hall, are the urban wage-earner's decade.","Weigh Source B’s nature, origin and purpose: a private letter written across the very divide the enquiry is about, from a Berlin office to a Pomeranian farm, has nobody to convince; but the writer is young, single, employed and in the capital, so she is the best-placed townswoman in Germany rather than the typical one.","Read Source C as the country half and use context on it: rye prices falling four years running, mortgages on half the district’s farms and young men leaving for the cities match a genuine agricultural recession that had begun well before the Wall Street Crash, driven by falling world grain prices.","Weigh Source C’s purpose rather than dismissing it: a nationalist weekly writing to attack the Young Plan is one-sided by design, and that is precisely why it is good evidence of how the countryside talked about a boom it could not feel.","Judge for this enquiry and not in general: between them they put Berlin against East Prussia, which is exactly the contrast the enquiry needs, but each is a single voice, and neither reaches the small towns and industrial districts that lay between the two."],"cues":["What exactly is the enquiry into?","Which details of Source B belong to the town side?","Does writing to a farm make the letter more useful here?","Which fact of yours confirms the farm complaint?","Does writing to persuade destroy usefulness?","Which Germany does neither of them reach?"]},{"title":"Question 3(b): finding the difference of view, not of detail","setup":"Interpretation 1 in this room, from a study of the Weimar Republic's middle years by a historian writing in the 1980s, argues that the years after 1923 were a genuine recovery that should not be read backwards from 1933: Stresemann ended the passive resistance that was bankrupting the state, replaced a worthless currency and took Germany from occupation and exclusion to a seat on the League Council, while real wages rose year after year, cities built flats and schools, a national scheme insured workers against unemployment from 1927, and in 1928 the Social Democrats polled more than a quarter of the vote while the Nazis won twelve seats, so that 'the Republic was winning'. Interpretation 2, from a general history of Germany between the wars by a historian writing in the 2000s, argues that the golden years were 'gilded rather than golden': the recovery rested on short-term American loans that could be recalled at any time and on reparations that were rescheduled rather than removed, farming was already in depression, unemployment never fell below about 1.3 million, cabinets changed constantly because no party could win a majority, the President elected in 1925 was a field marshal who had served the Kaiser, and nationalists used the Young Plan to make Stresemann's diplomacy look like surrender, so that 'prosperity bought calm, but it did not buy loyalty'. The question is: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. They give different views on the state of the German economy in the years 1924–29. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations.' (4 marks)","steps":["Reduce each to one sentence about the economy itself: the first says it was rebuilt on its own account and that the gains reached ordinary households, the second says it was held up from outside and left whole sectors behind.","Test that this is a difference of view rather than of detail: both accept that output, wages and building improved after 1923, so the disagreement is about what the improvement rested on and how far across the country it reached.","Anchor each half in the words of its own interpretation: 'real wages rose year after year' and a scheme that 'insured workers against unemployment' from 1927, against 'short-term American loans that could be recalled at any time', farming 'already in depression', and unemployment that 'never fell below about 1.3 million'.","Write one difference stated in your first sentence and then supported from both texts, not two summaries laid side by side."],"cues":["What does each say the economy was really like?","Do they disagree about facts or about weight?","Which phrase in each carries the view?","What shape should the finished answer take?"]},{"title":"Question 2(a): getting a 12-mark 'Explain why' past the bullet points","setup":"The question is: 'Explain why the standard of living of many Germans improved in the years 1924–29. You may use the following in your answer: • house building • the Law on Labour Exchanges and Unemployment Insurance of 1927. You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks). On the paper Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b) and you answer one of them. The mark scheme caps an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content at a maximum of 8 marks and shuts Level 4 to it altogether. The fact files in this room supply the material: more than two million homes built or renovated between 1924 and 1931, many in new blocks with bathrooms and electric light, alongside schools, hospitals and public baths; the insurance law of 1927, compulsory and funded by contributions from workers and employers; real wages rising every year from 1924, by roughly a quarter between 1925 and 1928; the Rentenmark of 15 November 1923 and the gold-backed Reichsmark of August 1924; the foreign lending that paid for much of the building; and the limits — farming in recession and unemployment never below about 1.3 million.","steps":["Turn the first bullet into a causal claim rather than a heading: city governments borrowing to build flats with a bathroom, electric light and a rent that could be planned around did not merely add to the housing stock, it changed what a working family’s wage had to cover.","Make the second bullet do causal work too: compulsory cover paid for by contributions from workers and employers meant that for about 16 million workers a spell out of work stopped being a fall into nothing, which raises a standard of living even in the weeks when nothing is earned.","Add the reason of your own that neither bullet prompts: the currency itself. Wages only buy more when money holds its value, and it was the stabilisation after November 1923 that turned pay rises into real gains rather than more paper.","Order the three so that they build — money worth earning, then homes worth living in, then a floor under the whole of it — and name in every paragraph which Germans the point actually reached.","Close on the question's own words by saying which reason set the pace and for whom the improvement did not happen, since farming was in recession before any crash and over a million men were still out of work at the top of the boom."],"cues":["How does a flat with a fixed rent change a family’s year?","What does compulsory insurance take away from a worker?","Which cause lies underneath both bullets?","In what order do the three make an argument?","What must the final sentence do?"]},{"title":"Question 3(d): evaluating Interpretation 2 against Interpretation 1 and your own knowledge","setup":"The question is: 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about unemployment in Germany in the years 1924–29? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]. Interpretation 2 says that unemployment 'never fell below about 1.3 million even at the top of the boom', and counts that among the reasons the golden years were 'gilded rather than golden'. Interpretation 1 does not dispute the figure but puts a different fact in its place: 'from 1927 a national scheme insured workers against unemployment', part of a recovery in which 'the Republic was winning'. No stimulus points are printed on this question.","steps":["Put the claim in your own words and name the test you will apply: whether a boom is judged by how many people it leaves without work, or by what happens to them while they are without it.","Build the case for Interpretation 2 out of dated context rather than the interpretation repeated: unemployment stayed above a million through the best years and rose above two million in the winter of 1928 to 1929, farming was in recession before any crash as world grain prices fell from the middle of the decade, and a nationalist paper could still write from East Prussia in June 1929 that more than a million men were out of work in the best month of the year.","Turn to Interpretation 1 for the case against and give that its evidence too: the Law on Labour Exchanges and Unemployment Insurance of 1927 made cover compulsory for about 16 million workers, funded by contributions from workers and employers, so that being laid off no longer emptied a household.","Show how each conveys its view, because the top level asks exactly that: Interpretation 2 counts the people the boom left outside, Interpretation 1 counts what the state had built for them, and each chooses the measurement that suits its verdict.","Judge on the test you named and say why the losing view is not simply wrong: the figure Interpretation 2 gives is right and insurance creates no work, but a republic that had insured most of its wage-earners by 1927 had changed what being out of work meant, and Source B’s Hans, paid something every week for seven weeks, is what that change looked like in one household."],"cues":["What is Interpretation 2 actually claiming?","Which figures of your own support it?","What does Interpretation 1 put against that?","How does each writer’s measurement differ?","What must your final paragraph settle?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Who asked for an armistice first, and why does the answer matter for the 'stab in the back' theory?","a":"The army command did: Ludendorff told the Kaiser on 29 September 1918 that the war was lost and that terms must be sought. The civilian politicians who signed on 11 November were carrying out the generals' own request, which is why the Dolchstoss was a myth."},{"q":"Name the two features of the Weimar Constitution most often used to explain the Republic's later weakness, and say what each did.","a":"Proportional representation under Article 22, which gave seats to small parties and made single-party majorities almost impossible so that governments were coalitions; and Article 48, which let the President rule by emergency decree, used well over a hundred times by Ebert himself."},{"q":"Why is a one-sided document like Source C still useful for an enquiry into the standard of living in the years 1924–29?","a":"Because the enquiry includes how people experienced the boom, and a nationalist editorial written in a farming district is direct evidence of how the countryside experienced it; its purpose, to attack the Young Plan, explains its tone without making its facts about rye prices and mortgages false."},{"q":"Give two reasons why the recovery of 1924–29 was fragile, and one piece of evidence that it was nevertheless real.","a":"Fragile: it rested on short-term American loans that could be recalled, and unemployment never fell below about 1.3 million while farming was already in recession. Real: real wages rose by roughly a quarter between 1925 and 1928, and the Nazis fell from thirty-two seats in May 1924 to twelve in May 1928."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 3 Section A (16 marks): Question 1 prints Source A and asks 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about...' in a table of two boxes each (4 marks); Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b), and you answer one of them: each is 'Explain why...' with two stimulus points and 'You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks).","Paper 3 Section B (36 marks), all on Question 3: (a) 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into...?' (8); (b) 'What is the main difference between these views?' (4); (c) 'Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about...' (4); (d) 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about...?' (16, plus up to 4 for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology).","The stimulus-point caps on the 12-mark 'Explain why...': 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'. There is no cap at Level 2. Question 3(d) prints no stimulus points, so no cap applies to it.","Timing on Paper 3: 1 hour 30 minutes for 52 marks, so about a minute a mark plus thinking time. Roughly 5 minutes on Question 1, 18 on Question 2, 12 on 3(a), 6 each on 3(b) and 3(c), and about 40 minutes on 3(d) including the final check for the SPaG marks.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose, and usefulness is always judged for the enquiry the question names, never in general. Contextual knowledge earns nothing unless it is used on the sources or the interpretations themselves.","The dates that anchor this key topic: 9 November 1918, the Kaiser's abdication and the proclamation of the republic; 11 November 1918, the armistice; 28 June 1919, the Treaty of Versailles; 11 August 1919, the Weimar Constitution; 13 March 1920, the Kapp Putsch; 11 January 1923, the occupation of the Ruhr; 15 November 1923, the Rentenmark; October 1925, Locarno; September 1926, the League of Nations."],"questions":[{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about the effects of hyperinflation on people in Germany in 1923. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for each valid inference, up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail from Source A. Credit money losing its value within a day, savers and the fixed-income poor suffering worst, or trade returning to barter.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: money was losing its value so fast that it had stopped working as money. Details in the source that tell me this: the works pay wages twice a day, and the wives wait at the gate to spend the notes 'before the evening price'. (ii) What I can infer: the people worst hit were those living on savings or fixed payments rather than on wages. Details in the source that tell me this: the town's savings bank deposits, gathered over a working lifetime, 'will not now buy a pair of boots', and the war widows come to the relief office in numbers the town cannot meet.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["An inference is something the source makes certain without stating it; ask what must be true for a mayor to describe a working day in these terms.","Read the report twice: once for what happens to a wage between morning and evening, and once for the people in it who have no wage at all.","Give each answer in two parts, a claim the source never writes down and then the exact words that force it."],"codes":["31-1 — 2 The early challenges to the Weimar Republic, 1919–23: The challenges of 1923"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about attitudes towards the Weimar Republic in 1923. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for each valid inference, up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail from Source A. Credit the government being blamed for the crisis, people comparing the Republic unfavourably with the Kaiser's rule, or loss of confidence in official authority.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: ordinary Germans blamed the Republic's government for the crisis rather than treating it as an accident. Details in the source that tell me this: the mayor reports that in the market square 'it is said openly that the government's printing presses have done this to us'. (ii) What I can infer: the crisis was making people look back on the Kaiser's rule as better than the Republic. Details in the source that tell me this: he writes that the older people in the town say they knew no such trouble under the Kaiser, and he passes this on to the provincial government as a warning worth reporting.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Attitudes are rarely stated outright in an official report; look for the remarks the writer passes on from other people.","Two sentences near the end of the report describe what is being said in the town rather than what is happening in it.","Set out a claim about what Germans thought of the Republic, then the phrase that makes it certain, for each of your two answers."],"codes":["31-1 — 2 The early challenges to the Weimar Republic, 1919–23: The challenges of 1923","31-1 — 2 The early challenges to the Weimar Republic, 1919–23: Reasons for the early unpopularity of the Republic"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. They give different views on the recovery of the Weimar Republic in the years 1924–29. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4) needs a key difference of view supported from both: Interpretation 1 holds the recovery was genuine and was winning support, Interpretation 2 that it was shallow, borrowed and won only calm. Surface detail alone stays in Level 1.","sol":"The main difference is over how solid the recovery was and what it actually bought the Republic. Interpretation 1 treats it as a genuine recovery: Stresemann replaced a worthless currency and won a seat on the League Council, real wages rose year after year, workers were insured against unemployment from 1927, and in 1928 the Nazis took only twelve seats, so that 'the Republic was winning'. Interpretation 2 treats the same years as 'gilded rather than golden', resting on short-term American loans that could be recalled and on reparations that were only rescheduled, with farming already in depression and cabinets changing constantly, concluding that 'prosperity bought calm, but it did not buy loyalty'.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Reduce each interpretation to a single sentence beginning 'The recovery of 1924 to 1929 was...' and compare the two sentences.","Check that your difference is one of view: both accept that things improved after 1923, so look for what each says the improvement was worth.","State the difference in your first sentence, then support each half with words taken from its own interpretation."],"codes":["31-1 — 3 The 'Golden Years': recovery of the Republic, 1924–29: Reasons for economic recovery","31-1 — 4 Changes in society, 1924–29: Changes in the standard of living"]},{"q":"Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about the recovery of the Weimar Republic in the years 1924–29. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4) needs one explained reason, effectively substantiated: for example that the two writers apply different yardsticks and therefore weigh different evidence, one measuring 1929 against 1923 and the other against the durability of the settlement.","sol":"One reason is that the two historians measure the recovery against different things, so they select different evidence. Interpretation 1 measures 1929 against the ruin of 1923, and the evidence that fits that test is the evidence of change: a new currency, a League Council seat, rising real wages, unemployment insurance. That is the Germany of Source B, where a Berlin typist's pay buys more than last year's did. Interpretation 2 measures the recovery against whether it could last, so it looks at the foundations: American money lent for a few months, mortgaged farms, unemployment that never disappeared. That is the Germany of Source C, where a farming district sees the same boom as borrowed money.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Historians can differ legitimately without either being wrong; ask what question each one is really answering about these years.","Look at what each interpretation compares 1929 with, and notice how that choice decides which evidence counts.","Name one reason, then show it at work in both interpretations, using a source if it illustrates the kind of evidence each writer would rely on."],"codes":["31-1 — 3 The 'Golden Years': recovery of the Republic, 1924–29: Reasons for economic recovery","31-1 — 4 Changes in society, 1924–29: Changes in the standard of living"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the standard of living in Germany in the years 1924–29? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): utility judged for this enquiry, reasoning from nature, origin and purpose, with context used to interpret. Credit Source B on real wages, new housing and the 1927 insurance law, Source C on farm prices, mortgages and unemployment.","sol":"Source B is useful because it shows the three things that actually raised living standards working in one household. A wage raised in the spring that 'buys more than last year's did' is a real-wage rise, and real wages did rise by roughly a quarter between 1925 and 1928; the two rooms in a new block built by the city, with a bathroom and a fixed rent, are part of the more than two million homes built or renovated between 1924 and 1931; and the weekly payment made to Hans during his seven weeks out of work is the compulsory insurance introduced in 1927 for about 16 million workers. Its provenance strengthens that: a private letter to a sister has nobody to persuade. But she is young, single, employed and in Berlin, which is the best-placed position in Germany, so she cannot be generalised from. Source C is useful for exactly the people Source B cannot reach. A nationalist weekly in an East Prussian farming district reports rye prices falling four years running and mortgages on half the district's farms, which matches a genuine agricultural recession that began before the Wall Street Crash. It was written to attack the Young Plan, so it is one-sided by purpose; for this enquiry that is an advantage, because it records how the countryside experienced the golden years. Together they cover town and country, which is what the enquiry requires; neither speaks for the savers ruined in 1923 or for the unemployed themselves.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Usefulness is always usefulness for something: copy the enquiry out before you look at either source.","Take each source in turn as content, then nature, origin and purpose, then the context that confirms or qualifies it, and only then compare them.","Begin by deciding which piece of Source B is a real-wage rise rather than just a pay rise, and open with a judgement rather than a description."],"codes":["31-1 — 4 Changes in society, 1924–29: Changes in the standard of living","31-1 — 4 Changes in society, 1924–29: Changes in the position of women","31-1 — 3 The 'Golden Years': recovery of the Republic, 1924–29: Reasons for economic recovery"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into how far the Weimar Republic had recovered by 1929? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): criteria-based judgements with developed reasoning on how provenance affects content. Credit Source C on American loans, the Young Plan and the Rhineland, Source B on everyday gains; qualify both, since neither can weigh the whole country.","sol":"Source C is the more useful of the two for this enquiry, because it is an argument about the recovery itself rather than a report of one household's fortunes. Written in June 1929, as the Young Plan was published, it names the weakness historians still name: money 'lent for a few months at a time' to build swimming baths and picture houses, so that if New York asked for it back the whole business would fall in. That describes the roughly twenty billion marks of foreign lending, most of it American and about half of it short-term, that came in after the Dawes Plan of 1924. Its complaints that foreign soldiers still stood in the Rhineland and that more than a million men were out of work in the best month of the year are both true, and its purpose — a nationalist paper attacking fulfilment — is itself evidence, because the survival of that hostility is part of what 'recovered' has to mean. Source B is the more reliable about daily life: a pay rise that buys more, a fixed rent in a new city block, insurance paid weekly to Hans during seven weeks out of work, and a young woman who has her own money and her vote. Being a private letter, it has no case to make. Its limits are that the writer is young, single and in Berlin, and that she says nothing of the loans or the politics. Together they let you weigh whether real improvements resting on borrowed money count as recovery.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["The enquiry is into the recovery as a whole, so ask of each source whether it shows the results, the foundations, or both.","A source written to attack something can still be excellent evidence; say what its purpose makes it good evidence of.","Decide which of the two sits closer to the enquiry and open with that judgement and the reason for it."],"codes":["31-1 — 3 The 'Golden Years': recovery of the Republic, 1924–29: Reasons for economic recovery","31-1 — 3 The 'Golden Years': recovery of the Republic, 1924–29: Stresemann's achievements abroad","31-1 — 4 Changes in society, 1924–29: Changes in the standard of living"]},{"q":"Explain why Kaiser Wilhelm II abdicated in November 1918. You may use the following in your answer: • the effects of the First World War on Germany • the mutiny of sailors at Kiel. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise knowledge — Ludendorff's admission of 29 September 1918, the naval mutinies and workers' and soldiers' councils, the Allies' unwillingness to treat with the Kaiser, and Max von Baden's announcement of 9 November. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Wilhelm II abdicated because within six weeks he lost the war, then the navy, then the men who were supposed to save the monarchy by sacrificing him. The first reason is the effect of four years of war on Germany itself. The Allied blockade had cut food imports so far that Germans had lived through the 'turnip winter' of 1916 to 1917, deaths from hunger and disease ran into the hundreds of thousands, and by the autumn of 1918 the army's spring offensives had failed and American troops were arriving in numbers Germany could not match. On 29 September 1918 Ludendorff told the Kaiser that the war was lost and that an armistice must be sought, and a new government under Prince Max von Baden was formed on 3 October to ask President Wilson for terms. Once the state admitted defeat, the argument for obeying it collapsed. The second reason is the mutiny. When the naval command ordered a final sortie, sailors at Wilhelmshaven refused to sail on 29 October, the mutiny spread to Kiel on 3 November, and within days workers' and soldiers' councils had taken over city after city; Kurt Eisner proclaimed a republic in Munich on 8 November. The armed force that would have been used to hold the cities had become the revolution. The third reason, which neither bullet prompts, is that the Kaiser had become the obstacle to peace. Wilson's notes made it clear that the Allies would not deal generously with the imperial regime, so Max von Baden and the army command concluded that the monarch was the price of an armistice; on 9 November Max announced the abdication before Wilhelm had agreed to it and handed the chancellorship to Ebert, and his own generals at headquarters told him the army would no longer stand behind him. The war removed his authority, the mutiny removed his means of coercion, and his own generals and ministers removed his last supporters, which is why he crossed into the Netherlands on 10 November and signed his abdication on the 28th.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Abdication is a decision, so track what was taken away from the Kaiser's options week by week rather than listing the troubles of 1918.","Separate the country, the armed forces and the elites, and explain what the loss of each made impossible.","Open on what Ludendorff told the Kaiser at the end of September 1918 and what admitting that did to the government's authority."],"codes":["31-1 — 1 The origins of the Republic, 1918–19: The situation in Germany at the end of the War"]},{"q":"Explain why the Weimar Republic was unpopular with many Germans in the years 1919–20. You may use the following in your answer: • the 'stab in the back' theory • the Treaty of Versailles. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise detail — the Dolchstoss and the 'November Criminals', the terms of 28 June 1919 including Article 231 and the territorial and military clauses, and the constitutional and social weaknesses that gave the Republic no credit for anything. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The Republic was unpopular before it had governed, because two stories about its birth were fastened onto it and because it could point to nothing of its own to set against them. The first reason is the 'stab in the back'. The Dolchstoss claimed that the German army had been undefeated in the field and was betrayed by socialists, republicans and Jews at home. It was false, since Ludendorff himself had demanded an armistice on 29 September 1918, but Hindenburg repeated it before a Reichstag committee of enquiry in 1919, and his prestige made it respectable. Because Matthias Erzberger, a civilian politician, signed the armistice on 11 November 1918, the men who founded the Republic could be called the 'November Criminals', so its origin became its crime. The second reason is the Treaty of Versailles of 28 June 1919, which turned the myth into a daily grievance. Article 231 required Germany to accept responsibility for the war; the army was cut to 100,000 with no conscription, tanks, submarines or air force; the Rhineland was demilitarised; about 13 per cent of German territory and all the colonies were lost; union with Austria was forbidden, Germany was excluded from the League, and in 1921 the bill was fixed at 132 billion gold marks. Germans called it a Diktat, and Scheidemann resigned as Chancellor rather than sign it. The third reason, which the bullets do not prompt, is that the Republic could not compensate. The constitution of August 1919 left the old judges, civil servants and army officers in post, and proportional representation produced coalitions that argued rather than acted; when the Freikorps were used against the Spartacists in January 1919 the left never forgave the government, and when the Kapp Putsch came in March 1920 the army would not fire on the rebels. A government blamed for a defeat it had not caused, forced to sign a treaty it could not refuse, and unable to rely on its own officials had almost no way of earning goodwill in its first eighteen months.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Unpopularity is not the same as hardship: ask what made Germans attach the blame to this government in particular.","Plan three reasons of different kinds — a myth, a treaty, and a weakness in the Republic's own structure — so the answer analyses rather than lists.","Open on who actually asked for the armistice in September 1918, and what that does to the Dolchstoss."],"codes":["31-1 — 2 The early challenges to the Weimar Republic, 1919–23: Reasons for the early unpopularity of the Republic","31-1 — 1 The origins of the Republic, 1918–19: The strengths and weaknesses of the new Weimar Constitution"]},{"q":"Explain why the Weimar Republic survived the challenges from Left and Right in the years 1919–20. You may use the following in your answer: • the Freikorps • the general strike of March 1920. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): sustained causal analysis — Noske and the Freikorps against the Spartacists in January 1919, the Berlin general strike that broke the Kapp Putsch by 17 March 1920, and the disunity of the Republic's enemies. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The Republic survived because each of its enemies could be defeated with the help of the other, and because neither of them was yet strong enough to rule. The first reason is the Freikorps. When the Spartacists rose in Berlin from 5 to 12 January 1919, seizing newspaper offices and railway stations, the new government had no reliable army of its own, so Ebert's defence minister Gustav Noske turned to volunteer units of demobilised soldiers, armed and organised by the officer corps. They cleared Berlin within a week, about 156 insurgents were killed, and Karl Liebknecht and Rosa Luxemburg were murdered in custody on 15 January; a Bavarian soviet republic was crushed the same way that spring. The threat from the left was broken, though at the price of the left's lasting hatred. The second reason is the general strike. When the same Freikorps turned on the government — the Ehrhardt Brigade of some 5,000 men marching into Berlin on 13 March 1920 under Wolfgang Kapp after being ordered to disband under Versailles — the army refused to fire on them and the ministers fled to Dresden and then Stuttgart. What saved them was Berlin's trade unions, who called a general strike that stopped the trains, the power and the water, so that Kapp could not govern the city he had occupied and fled on 17 March. The third reason, which neither bullet prompts, is that the Republic's enemies were divided and unprepared. Communists and nationalists hated each other more than they hated the government, so no combined attack was possible; Kapp had no programme beyond seizing buildings, and the Spartacists rose without the support of most workers, who had voted overwhelmingly for moderate parties in January 1919. The Republic therefore survived less because it was strong than because it could borrow strength from one enemy against another, which is why the same Freikorps who saved it in January were marching against it fourteen months later.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Survival needs explaining as much as collapse does: ask what the government could actually call on in each emergency.","Take the threat from the left and the threat from the right in turn, and notice that the instrument used against one became the danger in the other.","Begin with what the government did not have in January 1919, and who it had to borrow force from."],"codes":["31-1 — 2 The early challenges to the Weimar Republic, 1919–23: Challenges to the Republic from Left and Right","31-1 — 1 The origins of the Republic, 1918–19: The strengths and weaknesses of the new Weimar Constitution"]},{"q":"Explain why Germany suffered hyperinflation in 1923. You may use the following in your answer: • reparations • the French occupation of the Ruhr. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise figures — the 132 billion gold mark bill of 1921, the missed deliveries and the occupation of 11 January 1923, passive resistance financed by printing, and the wartime habit of borrowing rather than taxing. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Hyperinflation happened because a government with an impossible debt chose a policy that destroyed its own means of paying, and then met the bill with the printing press. The first reason is reparations. The figure fixed in 1921 was 132 billion gold marks, payable by a country that had just lost about 13 per cent of its territory, all its colonies and a large share of its coal and iron capacity. Because the debt was denominated in gold, every payment had to be found in real goods or foreign currency, which meant selling marks abroad and driving their value down. The second reason is the occupation of the Ruhr. When Germany fell behind with deliveries of coal and timber, French and Belgian troops entered the Ruhr on 11 January 1923 to take the goods directly. Chancellor Cuno ordered passive resistance: workers struck, officials refused to co-operate, and around 130 German civilians were killed and thousands expelled. That removed the output of Germany's industrial heartland from the economy at the very moment the government promised to keep paying the men who had stopped producing it. The third reason, which neither bullet prompts, is that the mark was already fragile because of how the war had been financed. Imperial Germany had paid for the fighting by borrowing and printing rather than by taxing, expecting to recover the cost from defeated enemies, so the currency had been losing value since 1914 and the habit of issuing notes was long established. Put together, the chain is exact: an unpayable gold debt, a resistance policy that stopped production, and a government whose only remaining instrument was the press. With far more notes chasing far fewer goods, the dollar rose from about 17,000 marks in January 1923 to about 4.2 trillion by November, and money ceased to function.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Inflation becomes hyperinflation when confidence goes, so track what happened to the money supply and to output at the same time.","Treat the two bullets as one chain — a debt and a policy — and then find a cause older than either of them.","Start by explaining why a bill fixed in gold marks was a problem for a currency made of paper."],"codes":["31-1 — 2 The early challenges to the Weimar Republic, 1919–23: The challenges of 1923","31-1 — 2 The early challenges to the Weimar Republic, 1919–23: Reasons for the early unpopularity of the Republic"]},{"q":"Explain why the German economy recovered in the years 1924–29. You may use the following in your answer: • the Rentenmark • the Dawes Plan. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): analysis directed at causation — the Rentenmark of 15 November 1923 and the Reichsmark of 1924, the Dawes terms and the 800 million gold mark loan, and the scale of private American lending. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The economy recovered because Stresemann first gave Germany a currency people would accept, then made the reparations bill payable, and then let American money do the rest. The first reason is the new currency. Stresemann became Chancellor on 13 August 1923 and ended passive resistance in September, which stopped the government printing notes to pay strikers; on 15 November 1923 the Rentenmark was issued, deliberately limited in supply and backed by a mortgage on German land and industry, and it was replaced in August 1924 by the gold-backed Reichsmark. Within weeks prices steadied, because what had collapsed was confidence and what the Rentenmark restored was confidence. The second reason is the Dawes Plan of August 1924. It did not cancel reparations, but it cut the annual payment to one billion marks, rising to two and a half billion after five years, reorganised the Reichsbank under Allied supervision and brought a loan of 800 million gold marks, most of it American; French and Belgian troops left the Ruhr by August 1925, returning Germany's most productive region to German control. A country that can meet its obligations is a country worth lending to. The third reason, which neither bullet prompts, is the foreign lending that followed. Roughly 20 billion marks, most of it American and about half of it short-term, flowed into Germany between 1924 and 1930, financing new factories and machinery as well as the flats, schools and public baths that city governments built, and by 1928–29 industrial production had passed its pre-war level of 1913; the Young Plan of 1929 then cut the total demanded to about 112 billion gold marks and spread payments to 1988. The three reasons are a sequence rather than a list — stable money made rescheduling possible, rescheduling made lending safe, and lending paid for the growth — but the sequence also explains the recovery's weakness, since much of that money was lent for months at a time and could be recalled.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["A recovery needs a first move: ask what has to be true before anyone will invest in a country at all.","Treat the two bullets as consecutive steps rather than separate topics, then find the third step that neither names.","Open on what the Rentenmark actually restored, which was not value but something people had lost in 1923."],"codes":["31-1 — 3 The 'Golden Years': recovery of the Republic, 1924–29: Reasons for economic recovery","31-1 — 2 The early challenges to the Weimar Republic, 1919–23: The challenges of 1923"]},{"q":"Explain why Germany's international position improved in the years 1925–29. You may use the following in your answer: • the Locarno Pact • membership of the League of Nations. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): sustained causal analysis — Locarno of October 1925 and the western frontier guarantee, the League Council seat of September 1926, the Kellogg–Briand Pact of 1928 and the Young Plan and Rhineland evacuation, all tied to the policy of fulfilment. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Germany's position improved because Stresemann replaced defiance with fulfilment: he kept the treaty in order to earn the right to alter it, and each concession bought a larger return. The first reason is Locarno. Negotiated in October 1925 and signed in London that December, it committed Germany, France and Belgium to accept their common frontiers as final, with Britain and Italy as guarantors and the Rhineland left permanently demilitarised. The concession was real, but so was the gain: France's fear of another 1914 had produced the Ruhr occupation of 1923, and a guaranteed western border made a repeat almost unthinkable. Stresemann also refused to accept the eastern frontier as final, agreeing only to arbitration with Poland and Czechoslovakia, so he conceded what he could not change and kept open what he wanted to. The second reason is the League of Nations. Germany had been excluded at Versailles as an outcast; in September 1926 it joined with a permanent seat on the Council, which meant a voice and a veto among the great powers, and Stresemann shared the Nobel Peace Prize with Aristide Briand that year. The third reason, which neither bullet prompts, is that this new standing was then used to reduce the burdens of the treaty itself. Germany was among the first signatories of the Kellogg–Briand Pact of August 1928 renouncing war, which put it on the same footing as its former enemies; Allied troops left the Cologne zone of the Rhineland in 1926, with the rest agreed as the Young Plan was negotiated in 1929, and that plan cut the total reparations demanded and spread payment to 1988. Fulfilment worked cumulatively: acceptance at Locarno earned the League seat, the League seat earned equal standing, and equal standing was traded for the evacuation of German soil and a lighter bill.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Diplomatic success is bought, not given: ask what Stresemann conceded at each stage and what he received for it.","Put Locarno and the League in causal order rather than side by side, then find the later gains that neither bullet names.","Begin with what France actually feared, and how Locarno was designed to answer it."],"codes":["31-1 — 3 The 'Golden Years': recovery of the Republic, 1924–29: Stresemann's achievements abroad","31-1 — 3 The 'Golden Years': recovery of the Republic, 1924–29: Reasons for economic recovery"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the recovery of the Weimar Republic in the years 1924–29? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views evaluated, how each is conveyed analysed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit short-term American loans, agricultural depression, coalition instability and Hindenburg; against, the Rentenmark, Locarno, the League, real wages and the 1928 election.","sol":"Interpretation 2 argues that the golden years were 'gilded rather than golden': the recovery rested on short-term American loans that could be recalled, on reparations rescheduled rather than removed, on farming already in depression and on cabinets that changed constantly, so that 'prosperity bought calm, but it did not buy loyalty'. The test to apply is whether a recovery counts as genuine if it depends on money that can be withdrawn at a few months' notice, and whether it won the Republic support as well as quiet. On the foundations, Interpretation 2 is right. The Dawes Plan of August 1924 did not cancel the 132 billion gold marks fixed in 1921; it cut the annual payment to one billion, rising to two and a half billion after five years, and made even that possible only by attracting foreign money. Roughly 20 billion marks of foreign lending, most of it American, came in between 1924 and 1930, about half of it lent for months rather than years and spent on flats, schools and public baths that could not repay it quickly. Agriculture was in recession before any crash, because world grain prices had been falling since the middle of the decade and German farms carried heavy mortgages. Unemployment never fell below about 1.3 million and rose above two million in the winter of 1928 to 1929. Politically the Republic never produced a stable majority, because proportional representation under Article 22 made coalitions unavoidable, and in 1925 the electorate chose as President Paul von Hindenburg, a field marshal of the Kaiser's army who had himself told a Reichstag committee in 1919 that the army was stabbed in the back. Interpretation 1 names achievements that Interpretation 2 passes over too quickly. Stresemann ended passive resistance in September 1923 and issued the Rentenmark on 15 November, halting the inflation within weeks; as foreign minister he signed at Locarno in October 1925, took Germany onto the League Council in September 1926 and signed the Kellogg–Briand Pact in 1928. At home real wages rose every year from 1924, by roughly a quarter between 1925 and 1928; more than two million homes were built or renovated between 1924 and 1931; and the law of 1927 insured about 16 million workers against unemployment. The voters answered the question directly: the Nazis fell from thirty-two seats in May 1924 to twelve in May 1928, while the Social Democrats took nearly thirty per cent of the vote. The two convey their views by choosing different yardsticks. Interpretation 1 measures 1929 against the ruin of 1923 and finds transformation; Interpretation 2 measures it against the disaster still to come and finds fragility, which is why its vocabulary is all surfaces and foundations. I agree with Interpretation 2 about durability and disagree with what it implies about loyalty. It is right that the settlement could not survive the withdrawal of American credit and that the Young Plan handed the Republic's enemies a national campaign in 1929. But a republic whose extremist parties were losing seats while its moderate ones gained them was doing more than buying calm, and nothing about 1928 made what followed inevitable. The recovery was real and conditional at the same time: real enough to win elections, conditional enough to depend on lenders in New York.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first what Interpretation 2 is actually claiming about these years, and name the test your judgement will apply.","Use Interpretation 1 as your case against, and support both sides with dated detail rather than with the interpretations restated.","Open by putting Interpretation 2's claim in your own words and stating the criterion you will judge it by."],"codes":["31-1 — 3 The 'Golden Years': recovery of the Republic, 1924–29: Reasons for economic recovery","31-1 — 3 The 'Golden Years': recovery of the Republic, 1924–29: Stresemann's achievements abroad","31-1 — 4 Changes in society, 1924–29: Changes in the standard of living"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the importance of Stresemann's work for Germany in the years 1924–29? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views weighed, how the difference is conveyed analysed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit dependence on American credit and the nationalist campaign against the Young Plan; against, the Rentenmark, the Dawes negotiation, Locarno and the League seat.","sol":"Interpretation 2 does not deny that Stresemann achieved things; it argues that what he achieved was 'gilded rather than golden' and that nationalists could use the Young Plan to make his diplomacy 'look like surrender', so that the calm he bought never became loyalty. The question is how far one man's work can be called important when the structure it produced was as dependent and as resented as that. There is real support for the interpretation. Stresemann's settlement did not remove Germany's burdens; it rescheduled them. The Dawes Plan of August 1924 cut the annual payment to one billion marks rising to two and a half billion, put the Reichsbank under Allied supervision and attracted a loan of 800 million gold marks, after which roughly 20 billion marks of foreign lending, most of it American and about half of it short-term, financed the boom — money that could be recalled at a few months' notice, exactly as Source C complained from East Prussia in June 1929. His foreign policy of fulfilment accepted the western frontier at Locarno in October 1925 and left the Rhineland demilitarised and, in 1929, still partly occupied. Nationalists therefore attacked the Young Plan as binding Germans to pay until 1988, and the campaign against it gave the Republic's enemies a platform in the last months of Stresemann's life; he died on 3 October 1929. Interpretation 1 states the case that Interpretation 2 leaves out, and the detail supports it strongly. Stresemann ended passive resistance in September 1923 when the presses were destroying the currency, and issued the Rentenmark on 15 November, a currency limited in supply that stopped hyperinflation within weeks; the Reichsmark followed in August 1924. He negotiated the Dawes terms that brought French and Belgian troops out of the Ruhr by August 1925, took Germany into the League of Nations in September 1926 with a permanent Council seat, shared the Nobel Peace Prize with Briand that year, and signed the Kellogg–Briand Pact in 1928. In six years a country that had been occupied, excluded and bankrupt was sitting among the great powers. It is also worth noting that his critics wanted no alternative that was available: defiance had been tried in 1923 and had produced the ruin of the mark. The interpretations convey the difference by where they put him in the sentence. In Interpretation 1 Stresemann is the subject who acts — he ended, replaced, took Germany from one condition to another; in Interpretation 2 he appears only as a target, someone whose diplomacy others could make look like surrender. On balance I agree with Interpretation 2's caution but not with its weighting. It is right that his achievement was conditional, borrowed and politically vulnerable, and right that the Young Plan campaign showed how little credit he had earned with the Republic's enemies. But conditional is not trivial: without the Rentenmark and the Dawes negotiation there would have been no boom to call gilded, and without Locarno and Geneva no restoration of Germany's standing at all. Stresemann's work was the most important single cause of the recovery; its limits were the limits of Germany's position, not of his.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Separate what Stresemann achieved from how durable it proved before you start agreeing or disagreeing.","The nationalist campaign against the Young Plan is the interpretation's strongest ground; test it, then ask what it cannot account for.","Open by stating what Interpretation 2 implies about Stresemann and naming the criterion your judgement will use."],"codes":["31-1 — 3 The 'Golden Years': recovery of the Republic, 1924–29: Stresemann's achievements abroad","31-1 — 3 The 'Golden Years': recovery of the Republic, 1924–29: Reasons for economic recovery","31-1 — 2 The early challenges to the Weimar Republic, 1919–23: The challenges of 1923"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about changes in the way Germans lived in the years 1924–29? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views evaluated, the difference of view analysed as it is conveyed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit farming depression, persistent unemployment and the ruined savers; against, real wages, housing, the 1927 insurance law, women's rights and cultural life.","sol":"Interpretation 2 treats the years after 1923 as 'gilded rather than golden' and insists that whatever improved, 'prosperity bought calm, but it did not buy loyalty'; farming was already in depression and unemployment never fell below about 1.3 million. Judged as an account of how Germans actually lived, the claim is half right, and the half it misses is large. The interpretation is strongest where the boom did not reach. Agriculture was in recession well before the Wall Street Crash: world grain prices had been falling since the middle of the decade and many farms were heavily mortgaged, which is precisely the complaint made from an East Prussian farming district in Source C in June 1929, that rye prices had fallen four years running and young men were leaving for the towns. Unemployment never disappeared, and rose above two million in the winter of 1928 to 1929. The middle-class savers whose deposits had been destroyed in 1923 were never compensated, and their hostility to the Republic long outlasted the inflation. There is also a cultural version of the same point that Interpretation 2 does not make but which supports it: the Bauhaus, moved to Dessau in 1925, the New Objectivity of Otto Dix and George Grosz, the huge UFA productions such as Metropolis in 1927 and the cabarets of Berlin were attacked by the churches and the nationalist press as decadence paid for with American money, so the most visible change in national life was itself a reason for many Germans to dislike the Republic. Interpretation 1 is nonetheless describing real things. Real wages rose every year from 1924 and by roughly a quarter between 1925 and 1928, so pay bought more rather than merely amounting to more; more than two million homes were built or renovated between 1924 and 1931, many with bathrooms and electric light; and the Law on Labour Exchanges and Unemployment Insurance of 1927 covered about 16 million workers, so that losing a job stopped being a fall into nothing. For women the change was in kind rather than in scale: they had voted on the same terms as men since 19 January 1919 and Article 109 declared equal rights, and although they remained about a third of the workforce, far more were in offices, shops, teaching and the professions, as Source B's Berlin typist shows. The two interpretations convey their views by choosing different Germans to stand for the country: Interpretation 1 looks at the insured urban wage-earner, Interpretation 2 at the mortgaged farmer and the man still out of work. I therefore agree with Interpretation 2 as a warning and disagree with it as a description. Life did improve materially for most people in the towns, and that improvement was not an illusion; but it was unevenly spread, it left the countryside and the ruined savers behind, and the cultural freedom that came with it actively offended millions. The right conclusion is not that nothing changed, but that what changed was real, partial and resented, which is exactly why calm did not become loyalty.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["'How Germans lived' is not one question but several: ask which Germans each interpretation is thinking of.","Improvement and resentment are not opposites here; build one paragraph on something that was both at once.","Begin with the groups the boom never reached, and be precise about why they were left out."],"codes":["31-1 — 4 Changes in society, 1924–29: Changes in the standard of living","31-1 — 4 Changes in society, 1924–29: Changes in the position of women","31-1 — 4 Changes in society, 1924–29: Cultural changes"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date was Kaiser Wilhelm II's abdication announced and a republic proclaimed in Berlin?","a":"9 November 1918."},{"q":"Which article of the Weimar Constitution allowed the President to rule by emergency decree?","a":"Article 48."},{"q":"At what figure was Germany's reparations bill fixed in 1921?","a":"132 billion gold marks."},{"q":"Which new currency was issued on 15 November 1923 to end the hyperinflation?","a":"The Rentenmark."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The German revolution, 9–11 November 1918","open":false,"code":"31-1 — 1 The origins of the Republic, 1918–19: The situation in Germany at the end of the War","back":"Max von Baden announced the Kaiser's abdication and handed power to Ebert on 9 November; Scheidemann proclaimed a republic; Erzberger signed the armistice at Compiègne on 11 November 1918."},{"front":"Weimar culture: the Bauhaus and the cinema","open":false,"code":"31-1 — 4 Changes in society, 1924–29: Cultural changes","back":"Gropius founded the Bauhaus at Weimar in 1919 and moved it to Dessau in 1925. The UFA studios at Babelsberg made films such as Fritz Lang's Metropolis (1927). Conservatives called it all decadent."},{"front":"The Weimar Constitution, August 1919","open":false,"code":"31-1 — 1 The origins of the Republic, 1918–19: The strengths and weaknesses of the new Weimar Constitution","back":"Signed 11 August 1919. All men and women over 20 could vote; the Reichstag was elected every four years by proportional representation, the President every seven; a bill of rights guaranteed free speech and religion."},{"front":"Article 48","open":false,"code":"31-1 — 1 The origins of the Republic, 1918–19: The strengths and weaknesses of the new Weimar Constitution","back":"The clause allowing the President to rule by emergency decree without the Reichstag. Intended as a safety valve, it was used well over a hundred times by Ebert himself."},{"front":"The 'stab in the back' theory (Dolchstoss)","open":false,"code":"31-1 — 2 The early challenges to the Weimar Republic, 1919–23: Reasons for the early unpopularity of the Republic","back":"The myth that the undefeated army was betrayed at home by socialists, republicans and Jews. Untrue, since Ludendorff demanded an armistice on 29 September 1918, but Hindenburg repeated it to a Reichstag committee in 1919."},{"front":"The Treaty of Versailles, 28 June 1919","open":false,"code":"31-1 — 2 The early challenges to the Weimar Republic, 1919–23: Reasons for the early unpopularity of the Republic","back":"Article 231 war guilt; army cut to 100,000 with no conscription, tanks, submarines or air force; Rhineland demilitarised; about 13 per cent of territory and all colonies lost; reparations fixed at 132 billion gold marks in 1921."},{"front":"The Spartacist uprising, January 1919","open":false,"code":"31-1 — 2 The early challenges to the Weimar Republic, 1919–23: Challenges to the Republic from Left and Right","back":"Communists under Liebknecht and Luxemburg rose in Berlin from 5 to 12 January 1919. Noske used the Freikorps to crush it; about 156 insurgents died and both leaders were murdered on 15 January."},{"front":"The Kapp Putsch, March 1920","open":false,"code":"31-1 — 2 The early challenges to the Weimar Republic, 1919–23: Challenges to the Republic from Left and Right","back":"On 13 March 1920 the Ehrhardt Brigade of some 5,000 Freikorps men seized Berlin for Wolfgang Kapp. The army would not fire on them; a general strike paralysed the city and Kapp fled on 17 March."},{"front":"The French occupation of the Ruhr, 1923","open":false,"code":"31-1 — 2 The early challenges to the Weimar Republic, 1919–23: The challenges of 1923","back":"French and Belgian troops entered on 11 January 1923 to take reparations in kind. Cuno ordered passive resistance; around 130 German civilians were killed and thousands expelled, and production stopped."},{"front":"Hyperinflation, 1923","open":false,"code":"31-1 — 2 The early challenges to the Weimar Republic, 1919–23: The challenges of 1923","back":"The dollar cost about 17,000 marks in January 1923 and about 4.2 trillion by November. Savers, pensioners and war widows were ruined; borrowers paid off debts in worthless notes."},{"front":"Gustav Stresemann","open":false,"code":"31-1 — 3 The 'Golden Years': recovery of the Republic, 1924–29: Reasons for economic recovery","back":"Chancellor from 13 August to 23 November 1923 and foreign minister until his death on 3 October 1929. Ended passive resistance, issued the Rentenmark, negotiated Dawes and Locarno and took Germany into the League."},{"front":"The Dawes Plan, 1924, and the Young Plan, 1929","open":false,"code":"31-1 — 3 The 'Golden Years': recovery of the Republic, 1924–29: Reasons for economic recovery","back":"Dawes (August 1924): annual payments cut to one billion marks rising to 2.5 billion, an 800 million gold mark loan, troops out of the Ruhr by 1925. Young (1929): about 112 billion total, spread to 1988."},{"front":"The Locarno Pact, October 1925","open":false,"code":"31-1 — 3 The 'Golden Years': recovery of the Republic, 1924–29: Stresemann's achievements abroad","back":"Germany, France and Belgium accepted their common frontiers as final, guaranteed by Britain and Italy, with the Rhineland permanently demilitarised. Germany accepted only arbitration over its eastern borders."},{"front":"Germany and the League of Nations, September 1926","open":false,"code":"31-1 — 3 The 'Golden Years': recovery of the Republic, 1924–29: Stresemann's achievements abroad","back":"Excluded at Versailles, Germany joined in September 1926 with a permanent seat on the Council. Stresemann shared the Nobel Peace Prize with Briand that year."},{"front":"Women in Weimar Germany, 1924–29","open":false,"code":"31-1 — 4 Changes in society, 1924–29: Changes in the position of women","back":"Voting on equal terms from 19 January 1919 and equal rights under Article 109. About a third of the workforce, but more in offices, shops and the professions; usually paid less than men."}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The sources and interpretations written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Source A: From a report on conditions in the town, written for the provincial government by the mayor of a small industrial town in Saxony, October 1923. Written for this room in the style of such a report; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — I must tell Your Honour plainly how matters stand here. The works now pay wages twice a day, and the wives wait at the gate to take the notes and spend them before the evening price. The bakers changed the price of a loaf three times on Tuesday, and two of them have closed their shops rather than sell bread for paper. The deposits in our savings bank, gathered over a working lifetime, will not now buy a pair of boots, and the widows of the war come to the relief office in numbers we cannot meet; I have asked for police to stand at the door. Farmers from the villages will bring potatoes to market for coal, cloth or soap, but not for money. In the market square it is said openly that the government's printing presses have done this to us, and the older people say they knew no such trouble under the Kaiser.","Source B: From a letter written by a young woman working as a typist in a Berlin insurance office to her older sister, who lives on the family farm in Pomerania, September 1928. Written for this room in the style of such a letter; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — You ask whether I am coming home, and the honest answer is that I do not want to. My wage was raised again in the spring, and this time it buys more than last year's did. Greta and I have two rooms in one of the new blocks the city has built, with a bathroom and an electric light in every room, and the rent is fixed. When Hans was out of work for seven weeks in the winter the insurance paid him something every week, which would have been unthinkable when Father was young. On Fridays we go to the cinema — last week a film about a city of machines and towers — and afterwards to a dance hall where the band plays until two. Mother writes that I should be married and keeping a house. I earn less than the men in my office who do the same work, and the director says he prefers to employ girls who are not married; but I have my own money and I have my vote, and I shall give up neither.","Source C: From an editorial in a nationalist weekly newspaper published in a farming district of East Prussia, June 1929. Written for this room in the style of such an editorial; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — In Berlin they call these the golden years. Let them come here and say it. The price of our rye has fallen four years together, the banks hold mortgages on half the farms in this district, and our young men leave for the cities because the land will not keep them. What is this gold made of? It is made of American money, lent for a few months at a time to build swimming baths and picture houses, and if New York should ask for it back tomorrow the whole glittering business will fall in. More than a million men are still without work in the best month of the year. Our ministers are applauded in Geneva while foreign soldiers still stand on German soil in the Rhineland, and now they set their names to the Young Plan and bind our grandchildren to pay until 1988 for a war we did not begin. Prosperity of this kind is a loan, not a recovery.","Interpretation 1: From a study of the Weimar Republic's middle years, by a historian writing in the 1980s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — The years after 1923 were a genuine recovery, and it is unfair to read them only backwards from 1933. Stresemann ended the passive resistance that was bankrupting the state, replaced a worthless currency, and in six years took Germany from an occupied and excluded country to a member of the League Council. At home real wages rose year after year, cities built flats and schools, and from 1927 a national scheme insured workers against unemployment. The voters said what they thought of it: in 1928 the Social Democrats polled more than a quarter of the vote and the Nazis won twelve seats. The Republic was winning.","Interpretation 2: From a general history of Germany between the wars, by a historian writing in the 2000s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — The golden years were gilded rather than golden. The recovery rested on short-term American loans that could be recalled at any time, and on reparations that were being rescheduled rather than removed. Farming was already in depression before 1929, and unemployment never fell below about 1.3 million even at the top of the boom. Cabinets changed constantly because no party could win a majority, and the man the voters chose as President in 1925 was a field marshal who had served the Kaiser. Nationalists used the Young Plan to make Stresemann's diplomacy look like surrender. Prosperity bought calm, but it did not buy loyalty.","How the room uses them: Source A is the inference source for Question 1. Sources B and C are the contemporary pair for the 8-mark usefulness question, 3(a), and may be used in 3(c). Interpretations 1 and 2 are the pair for 3(b), 3(c) and 3(d). Every quotation in the model answers of this room comes from the five texts above and from nowhere else."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 3 Question 3(d), 16 marks)","items":["Question: 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the loyalty of Germans to the Weimar Republic in the years 1924–29? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]. This is one paragraph from the middle of the answer, putting the case against Interpretation 2.","Interpretation 2's closing phrase, that 'prosperity bought calm, but it did not buy loyalty', treats the quiet of the later 1920s as something rented rather than earned; but in a republic loyalty is measured at the ballot box, and there the movement ran the other way. In the Reichstag election of May 1924, held in the wake of the Ruhr occupation and the ruin of the mark, the Nazis took thirty-two seats; by May 1928 they held twelve, while the Social Democrats, who had defended the Republic since 1918, rose from one hundred seats to one hundred and fifty-three and nearly thirty per cent of the vote. On 28 June 1928 Hermann Müller became Chancellor, the first Social Democrat to hold the office since 1920, at the head of a coalition that reached from his own party to Stresemann's German People's Party. Interpretation 1 is describing exactly this when it says that the voters 'said what they thought of it' and that 'the Republic was winning'. Interpretation 2 is right that the President elected in 1925 was a field marshal who had served the Kaiser, and right that the Young Plan handed the nationalist right a national campaign in 1929 — but that campaign had to be taken to a plebiscite on 22 December 1929, and fewer than fifteen per cent of the electorate turned out for it, so it failed. A republic whose extremists were losing seats and losing plebiscites was being given something more than silence.","Why this is Level 4: it 'provides an explained evaluation reviewing the alternative views in coming to a substantiated judgement' — the counter-case is built and then measured directly against Interpretation 2's own claim about loyalty. The analysis is precise about how the difference of view is conveyed, taking the metaphor of a purchase and testing it rather than repeating it. The contextual knowledge is precisely selected rather than general: the two election results, the seat totals, the date of Müller's appointment and the party he came from, and the turnout at the Young Plan plebiscite. The line of reasoning is sustained across the paragraph and the final sentence judges rather than summarises."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same as above — 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the loyalty of Germans to the Weimar Republic in the years 1924–29?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks].","Interpretation 2 says that prosperity bought calm but not loyalty, and I partly agree with this. Plenty of Germans still did not like the Republic, especially the nationalists, who were very angry about the Young Plan and about paying reparations for such a long time, and the President was an old army man rather than a supporter of the Republic. However, Interpretation 1 says the Republic was winning, and this is also true because the Nazis did badly in the elections at this time and the Social Democrats did well, which shows that most people were happy enough with how things were going. Life was better as well, with higher wages and new houses, so people had less reason to vote for extreme parties. Overall I think both interpretations are partly right, because people were more loyal than they had been but some of them still hated the Republic.","Why this is Level 2 and not Level 3: it does offer 'valid evaluative comment to agree with or counter the interpretation' and it does take details from both, which is what Level 2 asks for. What it misses is Level 3's 'relevant contextual knowledge used directly to support the evaluation': 'the Nazis did badly', 'the Social Democrats did well' and 'an old army man' are assertions where the fall from thirty-two seats in May 1924 to twelve in May 1928, the Social Democrats' one hundred and fifty-three seats and nearly thirty per cent, Müller's appointment as Chancellor on 28 June 1928 and Hindenburg's election in April 1925 should be. It also never says how either writer conveys a view, and its judgement is announced in the final sentence rather than reasoned towards, so 'its justification is insecure or undeveloped and a line of reasoning is not sustained'. Turning it into a Level 3 answer means replacing every vague phrase with a dated fact that does evaluative work."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the origins of the Republic, 1918–19 — abdication, armistice and the Weimar Constitution","items":["29 September 1918: Ludendorff told the Kaiser the war could not be won and that an armistice must be sought; a new government under Prince Max von Baden formed on 3 October asked President Wilson for terms.","The Allied blockade had already produced severe hunger, including the 'turnip winter' of 1916–17, and the German spring offensives of 1918 had failed as American troops arrived in force.","29 October 1918: sailors at Wilhelmshaven refused to sail on a final sortie; the mutiny reached Kiel on 3 November and workers' and soldiers' councils took over city after city.","8 November 1918: Kurt Eisner proclaimed a republic in Munich, and the Bavarian monarchy fell.","9 November 1918: Max von Baden announced Wilhelm II's abdication and handed the chancellorship to Friedrich Ebert of the SPD; Philipp Scheidemann proclaimed a republic from a window of the Reichstag.","10 November 1918: Wilhelm II crossed into the Netherlands; he signed his formal abdication on 28 November 1918.","10 November 1918: a Council of People's Representatives of six men, three from the SPD and three from the USPD, took power under Ebert; the USPD members resigned at the end of December 1918.","11 November 1918: Matthias Erzberger signed the armistice in a railway carriage at Compiègne, and the fighting stopped at 11 a.m. The signatories were later called the 'November Criminals'.","19 January 1919: elections to a National Assembly, the first in which German women voted; moderate pro-republican parties won a large majority.","6 February 1919: the Assembly met at Weimar rather than Berlin because the capital was too disorderly; Ebert was elected President on 11 February and Scheidemann became Chancellor.","11 August 1919: the Weimar Constitution was signed, coming into force on 14 August. All men and women over 20 could vote; the Reichstag was elected every four years by proportional representation under Article 22; the President was elected every seven years, commanded the armed forces and appointed the Chancellor.","The constitution's bill of rights guaranteed free speech, freedom of religion and, under Article 109, equal rights and duties for men and women; the Reichsrat represented the states and could delay legislation.","Weaknesses built into the same document: proportional representation made single-party majorities almost impossible, so every government was a coalition; Article 48 let the President rule by decree and Ebert used it well over a hundred times; the old judges, civil servants and army officers kept their posts."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the early challenges, 1919–23 — Versailles, Left and Right, the Ruhr and hyperinflation","items":["The 'stab in the back' (Dolchstoss): the claim that the undefeated army was betrayed by socialists, republicans and Jews at home. Hindenburg repeated it before a Reichstag committee of enquiry in 1919.","Treaty of Versailles, signed 28 June 1919 in the Hall of Mirrors by Hermann Müller and Johannes Bell; Scheidemann had resigned as Chancellor in June rather than sign. Germans called it a Diktat because they had not negotiated it.","Versailles terms — guilt and money: Article 231 required Germany to accept responsibility for the war; the reparations total was fixed at 132 billion gold marks in 1921.","Versailles terms — armed forces: the army limited to 100,000 men with no conscription, tanks, submarines or air force; the navy limited to six battleships; the Rhineland permanently demilitarised.","Versailles terms — territory: about 13 per cent of German territory and all its colonies lost, including Alsace-Lorraine to France, Eupen-Malmedy to Belgium, North Schleswig to Denmark, the Polish Corridor and Posen to Poland, Danzig a free city under the League, and the Saar coalfield under League administration for fifteen years.","Versailles terms — status: union with Austria (Anschluss) forbidden, and Germany excluded from the League of Nations.","The Spartacus League of Karl Liebknecht and Rosa Luxemburg became the German Communist Party at the turn of 1918–19 and rose in Berlin from 5 to 12 January 1919.","Gustav Noske, Ebert's defence minister, used the Freikorps — volunteer units of demobilised soldiers armed and organised by the army — to crush the rising; about 156 insurgents died and Liebknecht and Luxemburg were murdered in custody on 15 January 1919. A Bavarian soviet republic was crushed the same way in the spring.","Kapp Putsch, 13 March 1920: the Ehrhardt Brigade of some 5,000 Freikorps men, ordered to disband under Versailles, marched into Berlin and installed Wolfgang Kapp; the army refused to fire on them and the government fled to Dresden and then Stuttgart.","A general strike called by Berlin's trade unions stopped the trains, the power and the water, and Kapp fled on 17 March 1920.","Political murder: Matthias Erzberger was assassinated in August 1921 and the foreign minister Walther Rathenau in June 1922; the courts were markedly lenient towards right-wing offenders.","11 January 1923: French and Belgian troops occupied the Ruhr after Germany fell behind with deliveries of coal and timber, intending to take reparations in kind.","Chancellor Wilhelm Cuno ordered passive resistance; workers struck and officials refused to co-operate, and the government printed money to pay them. Around 130 German civilians were killed during the occupation and thousands were expelled.","The mark collapsed: a dollar cost about 17,000 marks in January 1923 and about 4.2 trillion by November 1923. Wages were paid twice a day, barter spread, and savers, pensioners and war widows were ruined, while borrowers cleared debts in worthless notes.","Stresemann became Chancellor on 13 August 1923, ended passive resistance in September and issued the Rentenmark on 15 November 1923; the gold-backed Reichsmark replaced it in August 1924."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the 'Golden Years', 1924–29 — recovery at home and Stresemann abroad","items":["The Rentenmark, issued 15 November 1923, was limited in supply and backed by a mortgage on German land and industry; confidence returned within weeks. The Reichsmark, backed by gold, followed in August 1924.","Dawes Plan, August 1924: annual reparations payments cut to one billion marks, rising to two and a half billion after five years; the Reichsbank reorganised under Allied supervision; a loan of 800 million gold marks, mostly American.","French and Belgian troops completed their withdrawal from the Ruhr by August 1925.","Roughly 20 billion marks of foreign lending, most of it private American money, came into Germany between 1924 and 1930, about half of it short-term, paying for factories, machinery, flats, schools and public baths; by 1928–29 industrial production had passed its 1913 level.","Young Plan, drawn up in 1929: the total demanded cut to about 112 billion gold marks including interest, payments spread to 1988, and the annual bill lowered by roughly a fifth. Nationalists campaigned against it as enslaving future generations.","Locarno, October 1925 (signed formally in London that December): Germany, France and Belgium accepted their common frontiers as final, guaranteed by Britain and Italy, with the Rhineland left permanently demilitarised. Germany accepted only arbitration over its eastern frontiers with Poland and Czechoslovakia.","September 1926: Germany joined the League of Nations with a permanent seat on the Council, reversing the exclusion imposed at Versailles. Stresemann shared the Nobel Peace Prize with Aristide Briand for 1926.","27 August 1928: Germany was among the first signatories of the Kellogg–Briand Pact in Paris, in which states renounced war as an instrument of national policy.","Allied troops left the Cologne zone of the Rhineland in 1926; the last of them left in 1930, so foreign soldiers still stood on German soil throughout the 'golden years'.","Stresemann's policy was called fulfilment (Erfüllungspolitik) — keeping the treaty's terms in order to earn the right to change them. Nationalists attacked it as surrender. He died on 3 October 1929.","Election results show the extremes squeezed: the Nazis won 32 seats in May 1924, 14 in December 1924 and 12 in May 1928; the Communists won 62 seats in May 1924, 45 in December 1924 and 54 in May 1928.","The Social Democrats were the largest party throughout, with 100 seats in May 1924, 131 in December 1924 and 153 in May 1928, nearly 30 per cent of the vote.","Paul von Hindenburg, a field marshal of the Kaiser's army, was elected President in April 1925 after Ebert's death on 28 February 1925."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: changes in society, 1924–29 — living standards, women and culture","items":["Real wages rose every year from 1924 and by roughly a quarter between 1925 and 1928, so pay bought more goods rather than simply amounting to more marks.","More than two million homes were built or renovated between 1924 and 1931, many in new blocks and estates with bathrooms and electric light, alongside schools, hospitals and public baths built by city governments.","The Law on Labour Exchanges and Unemployment Insurance of 1927 made insurance compulsory, funded by contributions from workers and employers, and covered about 16 million workers.","Unemployment never fell below about 1.3 million even at the height of the boom, and rose above two million in the winter of 1928–29.","Farming was in recession before the Wall Street Crash: world grain prices had been falling since the middle of the decade and many German farms carried heavy mortgages.","The middle-class savers whose deposits were destroyed in 1923 were never compensated, and their hostility to the Republic outlasted the inflation.","Women could vote on the same terms as men from the election of 19 January 1919, at the age of 20, and turnout among them was very high; Article 109 of the constitution declared equal rights and duties.","About three dozen women sat in the National Assembly elected in 1919, the first women in a German national parliament; through the 1920s women never held much above one Reichstag seat in fifteen.","Women were roughly a third of the workforce, much as before the war, but far more of them worked in offices, shops, teaching, the civil service and the professions; they were commonly paid less than men for the same work and married women were pressed to give up public posts.","The short-haired, cigarette-smoking 'new woman' of the big cities was attacked by the churches and by conservatives as evidence that the Republic was destroying the family.","Architecture and design: the Bauhaus was founded by Walter Gropius at Weimar in 1919 and moved to Dessau in 1925, where Gropius designed a new building of steel, glass and concrete; its designers made furniture, lamps and typefaces for mass production.","Art: the New Objectivity of Otto Dix and George Grosz turned from romantic subjects to maimed veterans, profiteers and city streets, insisting that art report Germany as it was.","Cinema: the UFA studios at Babelsberg near Berlin were among the largest in the world; expressionist films included The Cabinet of Dr Caligari (1920) and Fritz Lang's hugely expensive Metropolis (1927).","Berlin's cabarets, jazz bands and theatres drew visitors from across Europe; conservatives, the churches and the nationalist press condemned the whole of it as decadent and un-German."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the source and interpretation moves this paper rewards","items":["Inference: state something the source does not say but that must be true if the source is true, then give the words that force it. One inference and one supporting detail in each box.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose: what kind of document it is, who made it and when, and why it was made.","Utility: judge each source for the enquiry the question names, never in general. Content first, then how nature, origin and purpose make that content more or less useful, then the context that confirms or qualifies it.","Contextual knowledge scores only when it is used on the source; a paragraph about the period that never touches the sources earns nothing.","'Biased' is not a judgement on its own. Say whose view the source gives, why it gives that view, and what it is therefore useful for: a one-sided source is excellent evidence of the view it takes.","A source can be useful for an enquiry it never mentions, if what it shows explains the thing being enquired into; say what you can infer from it, not only what it says.","Difference of view (3(b)): a disagreement about the past, not a difference of wording or of detail. State the one difference, then support it with a phrase from each interpretation.","Reason for difference (3(c)): interpretations differ because they ask different questions, rest on different evidence, or give weight to different parts of the story. Name which, and show it from the interpretations, using Sources B and C where they illustrate the kind of evidence involved.","Differences based on conclusions drawn from evidence are legitimate; an interpretation is not wrong simply because another one disagrees with it.","Evaluating an interpretation (3(d)): use both interpretations and your own knowledge, show how each conveys its view, then decide how far the named interpretation can be agreed with and say on what criterion."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 3 Question 3(d): 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the loyalty of Germans to the Weimar Republic in the years 1924–29?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","items":["The question as printed: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the loyalty of Germans to the Weimar Republic in the years 1924–29? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' This question prints no stimulus points: the two interpretations are the stimulus, and both must be used, so an answer that evaluates one in isolation cannot reach the top levels.","Decode the statement. Interpretation 2's last sentence is the claim to be tested — 'prosperity bought calm, but it did not buy loyalty' — and the evidence it offers is political rather than economic: cabinets that changed constantly because no party could win a majority, a field marshal who had served the Kaiser chosen as President in 1925, and nationalists who used the Young Plan to make Stresemann's diplomacy look like surrender. Interpretation 1 answers with the voters, noting that in 1928 the Social Democrats polled more than a quarter of the vote while the Nazis won twelve seats, so that 'the Republic was winning'. The criterion, to be named in the opening, is what would count as loyalty: votes cast for parties that supported the Republic, or an acceptance settled enough to survive hard times.","Opening (three or four sentences): put Interpretation 2’s claim in your own words, name Interpretation 1 as the rival weighting, state the criterion, and signal the position you will reach, so the line of reasoning is visible from the first paragraph.","Paragraph 1, for Interpretation 2 — the head of state. In April 1925, after Ebert’s death on 28 February, the electorate chose Paul von Hindenburg, a field marshal of the Kaiser’s army who had told a Reichstag committee in 1919 that the army was stabbed in the back, and Article 48 placed emergency powers in his hands. Interim judgement: the Republic’s own President was not a republican, and the voters knew it when they elected him.","Paragraph 2, for Interpretation 2 — the governments never settled. Proportional representation under Article 22 made coalitions unavoidable and no party ever won a majority, so cabinets rarely lasted much more than a year; and the Communists, who wanted no republic at all, rose from 45 seats in December 1924 to 54 in May 1928. Interim judgement: the calm was an arrangement between party leaders rather than a settled allegiance.","Paragraph 3, for Interpretation 2 — what the last golden year showed. The Young Plan of 1929 gave the nationalist right its first national campaign, fought with Hugenberg’s newspapers and with Hitler on the same committee, against payments running to 1988. Interim judgement: prosperity had not converted the Republic’s enemies, only left them waiting.","Paragraph 4, against Interpretation 2 — the one test that counts. The Nazis fell from 32 seats in May 1924 to 12 in May 1928, while the Social Democrats rose from 100 seats to 153 with nearly 30 per cent of the vote, and on 28 June 1928 Hermann Müller became Chancellor, the first Social Democrat to hold the office since 1920, at the head of a coalition reaching from his own party to Stresemann’s German People’s Party. Interim judgement: in the only measurement a republic has, Germans were moving towards it and not away.","Paragraph 5, against Interpretation 2 and the point that goes furthest beyond both texts — what the nationalist campaign actually proved. The plebiscite the anti-Young Plan committee forced on 22 December 1929 drew fewer than 15 per cent of the electorate and failed, so the noise of the right was not yet the voice of the country; and the reasons for loyalty were real — real wages up roughly a quarter between 1925 and 1928, more than two million homes built or renovated between 1924 and 1931, insurance for about 16 million workers from 1927. Interim judgement: by 1929 more Germans had reason to want the Republic to work than at any time since 1918.","The evaluation thread that must run through every paragraph: the two writers read loyalty off different things. Interpretation 1 reads it off the ballot box and sees the extremes squeezed; Interpretation 2 reads it off what happened once prosperity stopped, which is why its language is all surfaces and purchases — gilding, calm that was bought and loyalty that was not. Saying this, and using it, is what the top level means by showing how the differences of view are conveyed.","What the conclusion must say: not 'both are partly right'. Apply the criterion announced at the start — Interpretation 2 is convincing about depth, because a President who was no republican, cabinets that could not last and a right that ran a national campaign in 1929 are the marks of an allegiance that had not been settled; but it is wrong to say the calm bought nothing, because an electorate that halved the Nazi vote, returned the Social Democrats as much the largest party and refused the anti-Young Plan plebiscite was giving the Republic more than silence. Then name the deciding consideration: loyalty was being won in the 1920s and had not yet been won, and treating 'not yet' as 'never' is reading 1933 backwards into 1928.","Timing and SPaG: about 40 minutes — six to plan the paragraphs and fix the criterion, about thirty to write, and the last four to check spelling and punctuation and to make sure the specialist terms are present and correctly used, since 4 additional marks depend on 'a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate'. Useful terms here: Weimar Republic, Reichstag, proportional representation, Article 48, coalition, plebiscite, reparations, Young Plan, fulfilment, Locarno, Rentenmark, Dawes Plan, League of Nations."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:52:00Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Re-judged against the fixed kit file, and the fix pass has done exactly what it reports. All five worked examples, the plan and both model paragraphs are now set on questions that are not in the bank: the Source A inference is on the problems facing local officials in 1923 (neither of questions[0] or questions[1] inferences is given away), the 3(a) is on city against countryside, the 3(b) on the state of the economy, the 12-mark is a new question with new bullets (house building and the 1927 insurance law), the 3(d) worked example is on unemployment, and the plan with the Level 4/Level 2 pair is on a fourth focus, the loyalty of Germans to the Republic. dupscan.js prints 'no duplication' and kit-tools check prints OK. 'Her brother' is gone from all three places and Hans is now named as the source names him. The three soft figures are tightened and now match the evidence: about 16 million insured under the 1927 Act (the Act's own coverage is about 16.4 million), roughly 20 billion marks of foreign lending about half short-term (against 20.6 billion in the literature), and industrial production past its 1913 level by 1928-29. I re-solved all fifteen questions and would mark none of them differently, and I re-verified the new material on the web: Hermann Muller became Chancellor on 28 June 1928 at the head of a grand coalition running from the SPD to Stresemann's DVP, the first Social Democrat in the office since his own government of 1920; the anti-Young Plan plebiscite of 22 December 1929 drew 14.91 per cent of the electorate and failed for want of turnout; Hugenberg, Hitler, Seldte and Class sat on the same Reich Committee; the seat totals 32/14/12, 62/45/54 and 100/131/153 and the 1928 SPD share of 29.8 per cent are exact. Nothing in the kit is now a wrong date, figure, event, attribution, tariff, cap or level, and the four points left in problems are refinements, not corrections. Publish it."}}},"31-2":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"31-2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 2 is the part of Option 31 that Paper 3 returns to most often, because it is the one stretch of the course where a tiny party on the edge of German politics becomes the government of Germany in fourteen years. Section A takes its source and its causation question from these years again and again, and Section B's interpretations are almost always arguments about why Hitler came to power: the man, the movement, the misery, or the men around the President. If you can explain, with names, dates and figures, why a beer-hall coup failed in 1923, why the same party was ignored in 1928, why it took 230 seats in July 1932, and why it was handed the Chancellery in January 1933 after losing thirty-four seats, you can answer almost anything this key topic sets. The room teaches that story as a chain of causes you can argue with, and it trains the five moves the paper rewards: inference from a source, causal explanation that goes beyond the printed stimulus points, judgement of a source's usefulness for the enquiry actually named, spotting how two historians differ and why, and evaluating one of them against the other and against what you know.","idea":[{"code":"31-2 — 1 Early development of the Nazi Party, 1920–22: Hitler's early career","h":"From spy to speaker: how Hitler took over a party of fifty men","t":"The German Workers' Party (Deutsche Arbeiterpartei, DAP) was founded in Munich on 5 January 1919 by Anton Drexler, a toolmaker, with a few dozen members and no money. Hitler, still in the army, was sent to report on it, attended a meeting on 12 September 1919, argued down another speaker and was invited to join; he was soon on its small committee and was given charge of propaganda and recruitment. What he brought was not ideas, which were Drexler's and Gottfried Feder's, but audiences: he advertised meetings, moved them into beer halls that held hundreds and then thousands, and made the speech the event. Post-war Munich suited him, because Bavaria was full of demobilised soldiers, the state government was sympathetic to the nationalist right after the crushing of the Munich soviet in 1919, and the police tolerated what they thought was useful anti-communism. In July 1921 Hitler resigned, demanded the chairmanship with sole authority as the price of returning, and got it, because the party could not survive without its speaker. A top-level answer does not simply say Hitler was a good speaker; it explains why his speaking gave him control of an organisation he had not founded, and why Munich rather than Berlin was where that could happen."},{"code":"31-2 — 1 Early development of the Nazi Party, 1920–22: The Twenty-Five Point Programme","h":"The Twenty-Five Points and the men at the door","t":"On 24 February 1920 Hitler and Drexler put the Twenty-Five Point Programme to a mass meeting in a Munich beer hall and the party renamed itself the National Socialist German Workers' Party (NSDAP). The points bolted three appeals together: nationalist (tear up the Treaty of Versailles, unite all Germans in one country, take land to live on), racial (only those of German blood may be citizens, so Jews are excluded), and socialist (nationalise the great trusts, share the profits of large industry, provide for the old, reform land ownership), all under a strong central government. That deliberate width is the point: a programme that promised something to the ex-soldier, the shopkeeper and the labourer at once. Enforcement came in 1921 with the Sturmabteilung, the SA, built from ex-soldiers and Freikorps men. It protected Nazi meetings, broke up other parties' meetings, gave unemployed young men a uniform, a band and somewhere to belong, and made the party look like an army rather than a debating club. A top-level answer treats the programme and the SA as one strategy — a message wide enough to recruit almost anyone, and a force that made the message look serious — and notes the cost, that promises this wide also had to be quarrelled over later."},{"code":"31-2 — 2 The Munich Putsch and the Nazi Party, 1923–28: The reasons for, events and consequences of the Munich Putsch","h":"The beer hall, the Feldherrnhalle and the trial Hitler won","t":"By 1923 the Republic looked finished: French and Belgian troops had occupied the Ruhr in January, passive resistance had wrecked the currency, and Mussolini's march on Rome in October 1922 suggested how a determined minority might take a capital. On the evening of 8 November 1923 Hitler and armed SA men burst into a Munich beer hall where the Bavarian state commissioner Gustav von Kahr was speaking, and at gunpoint Kahr, General von Lossow and Colonel von Seisser agreed to join a march on Berlin. Released, all three repudiated it. On 9 November about two thousand Nazis, with Ludendorff at their head, marched into the centre of Munich and were met by armed state police near the Feldherrnhalle; sixteen Nazis and four policemen were killed and the column broke up. Hitler was arrested, the party banned. The consequences, though, ran his way: at his trial from 26 February 1924 a sympathetic Bavarian court let him speak for hours, so that a local agitator became a national name, and the five-year sentence became about nine months in Landsberg, where he dictated Mein Kampf, published in 1925. A top-level answer separates the failure of the method from the success of the publicity, and says what Hitler concluded from it: that power would have to be taken legally, through votes, and the party rebuilt as a machine."},{"code":"31-2 — 2 The Munich Putsch and the Nazi Party, 1923–28: Reasons for limited support for the Nazi Party, 1924–28","h":"The lean years: a machine with nobody to sell to","t":"Hitler was released in December 1924 and refounded the party in February 1925, but the years to 1928 were its worst. The reason is mostly outside the party: Germany recovered. The Rentenmark ended the hyperinflation, the Dawes Plan of 1924 rescheduled reparations and opened the way to American loans, Locarno in 1925 and League membership in 1926 made Germany respectable again, and wages and employment rose. A party whose whole case was that the Republic was a swindle had little to say to people whose lives were improving. The party was also talking to itself. Hitler was banned from public speaking in Bavaria from March 1925 until 1927, and the northern branches round Gregor Strasser and Joseph Goebbels wanted a genuinely socialist line, including seizing the former princes' estates. At the Bamberg Conference on 14 February 1926 Hitler faced them down, kept the Twenty-Five Points unaltered and won Goebbels over; Goebbels became Gauleiter of Berlin in November 1926. Meanwhile the country was divided into Gaue, the SS was founded in 1925 as Hitler's bodyguard and the Hitler Youth in 1926. In the Reichstag election of May 1928 the Nazis took 12 seats and 2.6 per cent. A top-level answer uses these years twice: to explain the failure, and to explain why the party was ready to exploit a crisis that had not yet come."},{"code":"31-2 — 3 The growth in support for the Nazis, 1929–32: The growth of unemployment","h":"Six million out of work, and the party that said so","t":"Germany's recovery had been built on short-term American loans, so the Wall Street Crash of October 1929 hit it harder than most. The loans were called in and no new ones came; firms could not borrow, orders for exports collapsed, and in July 1931 a major Berlin bank failed and the credit system froze. Registered unemployment, about 1.3 million in 1929, passed 3 million by the end of 1930 and over 4 million during 1931, and stood at roughly 6 million in the winter of 1932–33 — something like one worker in three. Government made it worse: Heinrich Brüning, Chancellor from March 1930, governed by presidential decree under Article 48 after the Reichstag threw out his budget, and cut wages, salaries and unemployment benefit while raising taxes, partly to prove Germany could not pay reparations. The Communists called him the Hunger Chancellor. The first political beneficiary was the KPD itself, which rose from 54 seats in 1928 to 77 in 1930 and 100 in November 1932, organising among the long-term unemployed; and that rise frightened farmers, shopkeepers and industrialists towards anybody who would stop it. A top-level answer does not stop at 'people were poor so they voted Nazi': it shows the two separate chains, misery producing votes for the extremes, and fear of the communist extreme producing votes for the other one."},{"code":"31-2 — 3 The growth in support for the Nazis, 1929–32: Reasons for the growth in support for the Nazi Party","h":"Why so many different Germans voted for the same party","t":"Hardship explains the mood; it does not explain why one party collected it. The Nazis collected it because they were organised to. Goebbels, the party's national propaganda chief by 1930, built a campaign machine: identical posters and slogans everywhere, meetings in villages the other parties never visited, a party press, cheap handbills, and in 1932 aeroplane tours that put Hitler into several towns in a single day, which no other leader attempted. The message was deliberately different for each audience — work and bread for the unemployed, protection against debt and foreign grain for the farmer, order and anti-communism for the shopkeeper and the businessman, national revival for the young and for ex-soldiers, and a single explanation of everything in the Treaty of Versailles and the 'November criminals'. Hitler himself was presented as a leader above the parties, a war veteran who had risen from nothing. Behind all this stood the SA, which had grown to some four hundred thousand men by the summer of 1932: it fed and housed unemployed members, made the party visible on every street, and in fighting the Communists made the Nazis look like the only force that could beat them. A top-level answer weighs these against each other — propaganda, the man, the SA, the Depression — and says which was doing the work at which date."},{"code":"31-2 — 4 How Hitler became Chancellor, 1932–33: The Presidential and Reichstag elections of 1932","h":"1932: the year Hitler lost every election and took power anyway","t":"Hitler stood against Hindenburg for the presidency and lost twice, taking 30.1 per cent on 13 March 1932 and 36.8 per cent in the run-off of 10 April against the President's 53 per cent — but thirteen million votes made him a national alternative rather than a Bavarian agitator. Brüning fell on 30 May and Franz von Papen, governing with a 'cabinet of barons' and almost no support in the Reichstag, lifted the ban on the SA and called an election: on 31 July 1932 the Nazis took 230 seats and 37.3 per cent, the largest party in Germany. Hindenburg still refused Hitler the Chancellery in August, offering only the vice-chancellorship, which Hitler rejected. Nazi obstruction brought Papen down; a second election on 6 November cost the Nazis thirty-four seats, leaving 196, with their funds spent and Gregor Strasser resigning in December. Kurt von Schleicher became Chancellor on 3 December and failed to split the Nazis or find a majority. Papen, wanting revenge, met Hitler in Cologne on 4 January 1933 and persuaded Hindenburg that a cabinet containing only three Nazis, with himself as Vice-Chancellor, could be controlled. Hitler was appointed Chancellor on 30 January 1933. A top-level answer holds both halves together: the votes made him unavoidable, but it was the intrigue that chose the date and the terms."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1: two inferences from Source A about the Bavarian authorities","setup":"Source A in this room is a report written for the Bavarian Ministry of the Interior by a police officer who attended a National Socialist meeting in a Munich beer hall in March 1922. He reports that the hall, which holds perhaps two thousand, was full an hour before the advertised time and that swastika banners hung round the walls; that the speaker Hitler was on his feet for nearly two hours, calling the peace treaty a theft and the men of November 1918 traitors who had stabbed the army in the back, offering no measures, but bringing the hall to its feet again and again while the collection buckets came back heavy; that the audience was mainly young — ex-soldiers, small shopkeepers, clerks and students, with few working men from the large factories; that some fifty men of the party's storm section stood along the walls in grey windbreakers with swastika armbands and, when three men at the back began to interrupt, carried them out and beat them in the yard, our own officers present not thinking it necessary to intervene; and that in his judgement the party is dangerous less for its programme, which is the usual one, than for the effect of this one speaker upon a crowd. The question is: 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about the attitude of the Bavarian authorities towards the Nazi Party in the early 1920s. Complete the table below to explain your answer.' (4 marks)","steps":["Notice who is speaking before you read for content: a serving police officer reporting upwards, so his account of what his own men did is an admission, not a complaint.","Take the first inference from the officers at the meeting: they watched hecklers being dragged out and beaten and did not think it necessary to act, so the police in Bavaria were prepared to let the Nazis break the law against their opponents.","Take the second from the fact that the report exists at all and from its closing judgement: the ministry was having the party watched and being warned that the danger lay in Hitler himself, so the authorities were uneasy about the party even while tolerating it.","Sort each pair before you write: the claim, which the officer never states outright, goes in the box for the inference, and the words that force it go in the box for details."],"cues":["Who wrote this, and to whom?","What did the police present do while the hecklers were being beaten?","Why would a ministry be receiving a report like this at all?","Which box takes the claim and which takes the evidence?"]},{"title":"Question 2(a): a 12-mark 'Explain why' on Hitler's rise to the leadership","setup":"The question is: 'Explain why Hitler became the leader of the Nazi Party in 1921. You may use the following in your answer: • the German Workers' Party • the Twenty-Five Point Programme. You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks). On the paper Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b) and you answer one of them. The mark scheme caps an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content at a maximum of eight marks and shuts Level 4 to it altogether. The fact files in this room supply the material: Drexler's founding of the party in Munich, Hitler's first meeting and his work on propaganda and recruitment, the beer-hall meeting that launched the programme and the new name, the founding of the SA, the state of Bavarian politics after the war, and the resignation with which Hitler forced the committee's hand.","steps":["Turn the first stimulus point into a causal claim rather than a heading: the party Hitler joined was tiny and unknown, so one member who could fill halls mattered more in it than he ever could have done in an established party.","Make the second stimulus point do causal work too: Hitler was the man who put the programme to a mass audience and gave the party its new name, so the party's public identity became his work and could not easily be separated from him.","Add the reason of your own that neither stimulus point prompts: Bavaria after the war gave him his conditions — demobilised soldiers looking for a cause, a state government and police friendly to the nationalist right, and rival völkisch groups he could either absorb or outdraw.","Explain the mechanism that turned all that into formal power: when the committee tried to merge with another group Hitler resigned, knowing the party could not survive without its speaker, and returned only on his own terms as chairman with sole authority.","Order the reasons so they build rather than sit in a list — a party small enough to be captured, a public identity he had made, a city that suited him, and a threat nobody could call — and close by saying which mattered most."],"cues":["Why does one good speaker count for more in a party of dozens than in a party of thousands?","Whose achievement did the new name and programme look like?","What is true of Munich after the war that is not true of most German cities?","What did Hitler actually do to force the committee's hand?","What must the last sentence of the answer do?"]},{"title":"Question 3(a): judging Sources B and C for the enquiry actually named","setup":"Source B in this room is a letter from an unemployed fitter in Chemnitz to his brother, February 1932: the works put off half the men in the winter of 1930 and closed the rest a shop at a time; his insurance ran out and the welfare money has been cut twice; his wife takes in washing and he queues at the labour exchange with men he was apprenticed with; in the evenings the Communists shout that the crisis is the end of the system while the Nazis bring a band and a loudspeaker van and say the Germans were robbed in 1918 and will be given work; his own party tells him to be patient and support the government in Berlin, and he has been patient for two years; his neighbour has joined the SA because it gives him a bed, soup and boots; he will not vote again for men who ask him to wait. Source C is a confidential report by the district officer of a rural district in Lower Saxony, written a fortnight after the Reichstag election of 31 July 1932: in 1928 the Nazis polled a few hundred votes there, but on 31 July they took more than half the votes in every village but two on the highest turnout he has recorded; speakers came out from Hanover to farm kitchens and village inns where no other party has held a meeting in his memory; the SA marched with a band on Sunday afternoons; an aeroplane brought Hitler to the town for an hour; handbills reached every house; the Communists' meetings in the town have frightened the tradespeople, and many tell him they voted National Socialist to keep the Communists out; the Nationalists and the People's Party have disappeared there. The question is: 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the reasons why some Germans turned away from the moderate parties in the early 1930s? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context.' (8 marks)","steps":["Copy the enquiry out and let it select the material: the turning away from the moderate parties, so the fitter's loss of patience and the collapse of the Nationalists and the People's Party are in, and the mechanics of Nazi propaganda are in only where they explain a switch of vote.","Take Source B's content for that enquiry: a man who has kept faith with his own party for two years abandons it because it is telling him to support the very government that is cutting his relief, which is exactly how the Social Democrats' toleration of Brüning cost them their own supporters.","Weigh Source B's nature, origin and purpose together: a private letter to a brother, written while the writer was still deciding what to do, has no one to convince and no censor to satisfy, so it is strong evidence of one man's reasoning, though one fitter in one city cannot show how general it was.","Do the same for Source C: an official report meant for the writer's superiors, so its purpose is to explain an alarming result honestly rather than to praise anybody, and it reaches where Source B cannot, the Protestant countryside where the Nazi vote rose fastest.","Bring your own knowledge in on the sources rather than beside them: the district officer's disappearing Nationalists and People's Party are the middle-class parties whose voters went over wholesale after 1930, and his tradespeople voting to keep the Communists out match the KPD's rise to 89 seats in that same July election.","Finish with a judgement for this enquiry and not a general one: between them they cover the two desertions that decided the period, a worker leaving the left and a countryside leaving the right, and neither reaches the Catholic Centre voters who did not move at all."],"cues":["What exactly is the enquiry into?","Which party does the writer of Source B mean by 'our own party', and why is he leaving it?","Why might a letter to a brother be more candid than a speech or an article?","Whose eyes is Source C written for, and what does that do to its usefulness?","Which piece of your own knowledge explains the parties that have 'disappeared'?","Which Germans do neither source reach?"]},{"title":"Question 3(b): finding the difference of view, not of detail","setup":"This example works a separate practice pair of interpretations, printed in full below and written for this example and the next one alone: they are not the Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section, which the question bank uses, but on a paper they would be printed as Interpretations 1 and 2 and the question numbers them so. Interpretation 1: from a study of political violence in the Weimar Republic, by a historian writing in the 1990s. Written for this worked example; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — 'The SA was the decisive instrument of the Nazi advance. From 1930 its squads broke up rival meetings, fought the Communists for the streets and made campaigning dangerous for everyone else. Germans who wanted order above all else looked to the one movement that could impose it, and voted accordingly.' Interpretation 2: from a social history of the Nazi movement's membership, by a historian writing in the 2010s. Written for this worked example; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — 'The stormtroopers mattered less as a weapon than as a refuge. In the worst years of the Depression the SA fed, clothed and housed unemployed young men in their tens of thousands and gave them a uniform and a purpose. Recruitment, not terror, is what put those numbers on the street.' The question is: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. They give different views on the importance of the SA to the Nazi Party in the years 1929–33. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations.' (4 marks)","steps":["Reduce each interpretation to a single claim about what the stormtroopers were for: the first treats them as a tool aimed outwards at everybody else, the second as a shelter built inwards for the men who joined.","Test that this really is a difference of view rather than of fact: neither denies that the SA was large, uniformed and constantly on the streets, so the disagreement is about what that presence was doing, not about whether it existed.","Anchor each half of the difference in the interpretation's own words, 'the decisive instrument of the Nazi advance' and 'made campaigning dangerous for everyone else' against 'less as a weapon than as a refuge' and 'Recruitment, not terror'.","Notice that the two even read the same picture differently, the men on the street: one sees a column of stormtroopers as pressure applied to everybody outside it, the other as the measure of how many young men the movement had taken in and was feeding.","Write it as one difference stated in your first sentence and then supported from both interpretations, rather than as two summaries laid side by side."],"cues":["What does each interpretation say the SA was for?","Do the two disagree about the facts, or about their meaning?","Which phrase from each carries the view?","What does each one make of the men on the street?","What shape should the finished answer take?"]},{"title":"Question 3(c): explaining why two historians differ","setup":"The question is: 'Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about the importance of the SA to the Nazi Party in the years 1929–33. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer.' (4 marks). This example works the same separate practice pair as the example before it, printed again in full below: they are not the Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section, but on a paper they would be printed as Interpretations 1 and 2 and the question numbers them so. Interpretation 1: from a study of political violence in the Weimar Republic, by a historian writing in the 1990s. Written for this worked example; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — 'The SA was the decisive instrument of the Nazi advance. From 1930 its squads broke up rival meetings, fought the Communists for the streets and made campaigning dangerous for everyone else. Germans who wanted order above all else looked to the one movement that could impose it, and voted accordingly.' Interpretation 2: from a social history of the Nazi movement's membership, by a historian writing in the 2010s. Written for this worked example; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — 'The stormtroopers mattered less as a weapon than as a refuge. In the worst years of the Depression the SA fed, clothed and housed unemployed young men in their tens of thousands and gave them a uniform and a purpose. Recruitment, not terror, is what put those numbers on the street.' Source B is the Chemnitz fitter's letter of February 1932 and Source C is the Lower Saxon district officer's report on the July 1932 election.","steps":["Begin with what each book sets out to study, because that settles which records its author will have spent her time in: one traces political violence, the other traces who joined the movement and what it gave them.","Show the first historian's evidence at work: police files, court cases and the casualty lists of street fighting are what a history of violence is built from, and on that material the SA can only appear as something done to other people.","Show the second historian's evidence at work: a study of membership counts recruits and asks what they were offered, and Source B is exactly that kind of testimony, the fitter's neighbour Hermann who 'has gone into the SA' because 'they give him a bed, soup and boots'.","Add the point that makes the two compatible rather than simply opposed: Source C holds both halves in one place, stormtroopers who 'marched through the villages with a band on Sunday afternoons' and 'gave the young men something to belong to', so a single organisation could be recruiting its own and pressing on everybody else at once, and each historian has kept the half that answers the question she set herself.","Write one reason, name it in the first sentence, and then substantiate it from both interpretations and from a source, rather than listing several reasons thinly."],"cues":["What is each book a study of?","Which records does a history of political violence rest on?","Which source says what the SA gave the men who joined it?","Can both pictures of the SA be true at once, and why?","How many reasons does the question want, and what must you do with it?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Why did the failure of the Munich Putsch end up helping Hitler?","a":"His trial from 26 February 1924 was reported nationally and the sympathetic Bavarian court let him speak at length, making him a national figure; he served only about nine months of a five-year sentence and used the time to dictate Mein Kampf, and he concluded that power must be taken through elections rather than by force."},{"q":"Why did a one-sided document like Source C still help an enquiry into why Germans abandoned the moderate parties?","a":"Because it is not one-sided in that sense: it is a confidential report by an official explaining an alarming result to his superiors, so he has every reason to be accurate, and what he records — the Nationalists and the People's Party simply disappearing from his district — is the desertion the enquiry is about."},{"q":"Name one thing that made the Nazis' 1932 campaigning different from their rivals'.","a":"Aeroplane tours that put Hitler into several towns in a single day; or speakers and meetings in small villages the other parties never visited; or a national propaganda office under Goebbels issuing the same posters and slogans everywhere; or the SA providing bands, parades and street presence."},{"q":"In what sense did Hitler lose the elections of 1932 and still gain from them?","a":"He lost the presidential run-off on 10 April with 36.8 per cent against Hindenburg's 53 per cent, and the Nazis fell from 230 to 196 seats in November; but thirteen million presidential votes and the largest party in the Reichstag made him the only leader with a mass following the conservatives could use."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 3 Section A (16 marks): Question 1 prints Source A and asks 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about...' in a table with two boxes for each inference (4 marks); Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b) and you answer one of them, each 'Explain why...' with two stimulus points and 'You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks).","Paper 3 Section B (36 marks), all on Question 3: (a) 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into...?' (8); (b) 'What is the main difference between these views?' (4); (c) 'Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about...' (4); (d) 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about...?' (16, plus up to 4 marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology).","The stimulus-point caps on the 12-mark 'Explain why...': 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'. There is no cap at Level 2. Question 3(d) prints no stimulus points, so no such cap applies to it.","Timing on Paper 3: 1 hour 30 minutes for 52 marks, so roughly a minute a mark plus thinking time. About 5 minutes on Question 1, 18 on Question 2, 12 on 3(a), 6 each on 3(b) and 3(c), and about 40 minutes on 3(d) including the final check for the SPaG marks.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose, and usefulness is always judged for the enquiry the question names, never in general. Contextual knowledge earns nothing at all unless it is used on the sources or on the interpretations."],"questions":[{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about the appeal of the Nazi Party in Munich in the early 1920s. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for each valid inference up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail selected from Source A. Credit Hitler's speaking as the party's main attraction, the lower-middle-class and ex-soldier audience, or an appeal built on grievance rather than policy.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: Hitler's own speaking, rather than the party's ideas, was what drew people to it. Details in the source that tell me this: the hall was full an hour before the advertised time, and the officer judges the party dangerous not for its programme but for 'the effect of this one speaker upon a crowd'. (ii) What I can infer: the party's appeal was to the lower middle class and to ex-soldiers rather than to industrial workers, despite its name. Details in the source that tell me this: the audience was 'ex-soldiers, small shopkeepers, clerks and students, with few working men from the large factories'.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["An inference is something the source makes certain without stating it; ask why a police officer bothers to tell his ministry what kind of people were in the hall.","Read the report twice, once for what draws the crowd in and once for who the crowd is made of.","Give each answer in two parts: a claim the source never states outright, then the exact words that force it."],"codes":["31-2 — 1 Early development of the Nazi Party, 1920–22: Hitler's early career","31-2 — 1 Early development of the Nazi Party, 1920–22: The Twenty-Five Point Programme"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about the methods the Nazi Party used at its meetings in the early 1920s. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for each valid inference up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail from Source A. Credit organised violence against opponents, careful staging and advertising, or the use of symbols and collections to make the party look strong.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: the Nazis used organised violence to make sure nobody could oppose them at their own meetings. Details in the source that tell me this: some fifty men of the party's storm section were posted along the walls, and when three men began to interrupt they were 'carried out and beaten in the yard'. (ii) What I can infer: the meetings were carefully staged to make a small party look powerful. Details in the source that tell me this: swastika banners were hung round the hall and the meeting was advertised in advance, so that a hall holding perhaps two thousand was full an hour early, and collection buckets were taken round the audience.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Look at what the party does in the hall, not only at what its speaker says in it.","The report notices three separate kinds of preparation: people, decoration and money. Any two of them will do.","Set out a claim the source implies and then the phrase that makes it certain, for each of your two answers."],"codes":["31-2 — 1 Early development of the Nazi Party, 1920–22: The Twenty-Five Point Programme","31-2 — 1 Early development of the Nazi Party, 1920–22: Hitler's early career"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. They give different views on the reasons why Hitler became Chancellor of Germany in January 1933. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4) needs a key difference of view supported from both: Interpretation 1 makes the appointment the work of a small circle round the President, Interpretation 2 the result of Nazi mass strength and the collapse of the party system. Surface detail only stays in Level 1.","sol":"The main difference is over what actually put Hitler into the Chancellery. Interpretation 1 says a handful of men did it: Hitler 'did not win power; he was given it', by Papen going behind Schleicher's back and persuading Hindenburg that a cabinet with only three National Socialists would leave the conservatives in charge, so that Hitler 'entered the Chancellery through a side door'. Interpretation 2 says those men 'settled the date, not the outcome': with 230 seats, thirteen million presidential votes and an SA of some four hundred thousand, and with the Depression having destroyed the parties of the middle, 'the collapse of everything else left them with nobody else to choose'.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Reduce each interpretation to one sentence beginning 'Hitler became Chancellor because…' and set the two sentences beside each other.","Check that your difference is one of view: both writers agree on what happened on 30 January, so look for what each says did the causal work.","State the difference in your first sentence, then support each half with words taken from its own interpretation."],"codes":["31-2 — 4 How Hitler became Chancellor, 1932–33: The Presidential and Reichstag elections of 1932","31-2 — 3 The growth in support for the Nazis, 1929–32: Reasons for the growth in support for the Nazi Party"]},{"q":"Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about the reasons why Hitler became Chancellor of Germany in January 1933. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4) needs one explained reason, effectively substantiated: for example that the two historians are studying different things over different lengths of time, so they weigh different evidence.","sol":"One reason is that the two historians study different things and so count different evidence. Interpretation 1 comes from a study of the Republic's last years, so its material is what a few men did in January 1933: Papen's dealings behind Schleicher's back, and the cabinet list. From inside that room the appointment looks like a private bargain. Interpretation 2 comes from a general history of the whole Republic, so its material is of the kind found in Source C, where a district officer reports that a party polling a few hundred votes in his villages in 1928 took over half in July 1932. Read from returns like that, the appointment looks like the end of a long process.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Historians differ legitimately when they ask different questions of the past; begin by asking what each book is a study of.","Ask what evidence each writer would have had in front of her, and what that evidence would make prominent.","Name one reason in your first sentence, then show it working in both interpretations, using a source if it illustrates the kind of evidence involved."],"codes":["31-2 — 4 How Hitler became Chancellor, 1932–33: The Presidential and Reichstag elections of 1932","31-2 — 3 The growth in support for the Nazis, 1929–32: Reasons for the growth in support for the Nazi Party"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the impact of unemployment on the lives of ordinary Germans in the years 1930–32? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): utility judged for this enquiry, with reasoning from nature, origin and purpose, and context used to interpret. Credit Source B on benefit cuts, the labour exchange and SA relief; Source C as indirect, showing the countryside and the political effects rather than daily life.","sol":"Source B is the more useful of the two, because it shows what the figures meant inside one household. The fitter's works put off half its men in the winter of 1930 and then closed shop by shop, his insurance ran out and 'the welfare money has been cut twice', his wife takes in washing and he queues at the labour exchange with men he was apprenticed with. That matches the context exactly: registered unemployment passed three million by the end of 1930 and four million in 1931, and Brüning, governing by presidential decree from 1930, cut benefit and wages to balance the budget. Its provenance strengthens it: a private letter to a brother has no one to persuade and no censor to satisfy, so the admission that what he cannot bear is 'the standing about' is candid evidence of what idleness did to men. Against that, one fitter in one industrial city cannot show how general his experience was. Source C is useful only indirectly. A district officer writing confidentially about an election records the political consequences of the slump in the countryside — a neighbour's world in which the SA offered 'a bed, soup and boots' has its rural equivalent in young men given a band to march behind — but he says nothing about wages, relief or hunger. Together they show the crisis at both ends of Germany; neither reaches the families of the six million at the very worst, in the winter of 1932 to 1933.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Usefulness is always usefulness for something: copy the enquiry out before you look at either source.","Take each source in turn as content, then provenance, then the context that confirms or qualifies it, and only then compare them.","Begin with what Source B lets you infer about a single household, and make your first sentence a judgement rather than a description."],"codes":["31-2 — 3 The growth in support for the Nazis, 1929–32: The growth of unemployment","31-2 — 3 The growth in support for the Nazis, 1929–32: Reasons for the growth in support for the Nazi Party"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the methods the Nazi Party used to win votes in 1932? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): criteria-based judgements with developed reasoning on how provenance affects the content. Credit Source C on village meetings, the SA, the aeroplane and handbills; Source B on the loudspeaker van, the message of work and 1918, and SA welfare. Qualify: neither shows the money behind it.","sol":"Source C is the more useful, because it is a hostile official's inventory of the machine at work. He reports speakers sent out from Hanover to farm kitchens and village inns 'where the other parties have not held a meeting in my memory', the SA marching with a band on Sunday afternoons, handbills in every house, and an aeroplane bringing Hitler to the town for an hour before the poll. That is precisely the method my own knowledge describes: Goebbels, the party's national propaganda chief, standardised the posters and slogans and organised the 1932 flights that put Hitler into several towns in a day, and the party had turned to the countryside after its failure with industrial workers in 1928. Its provenance makes it stronger rather than weaker: the report is confidential, written for superiors who need an explanation of an alarming result, so the writer has no reason to flatter the Nazis. Source B adds the same methods seen from the receiving end in a city: a band, a loudspeaker van, the claim that Germany was robbed in 1918 and will be given work, and an SA that recruits by giving a man 'a bed, soup and boots'. Being a private letter, it is honest about the effect on an ordinary voter. Neither, however, shows the party's funds or its national organisation, so both illustrate the methods without explaining how they were paid for.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["The enquiry is into methods, so go through each source listing what the party actually did, and leave aside what it believed.","Ask of each source whether being written by an opponent makes it more or less worth having for this enquiry, and say why.","Start by deciding which source is closer to the enquiry, and open with that judgement and the reason for it."],"codes":["31-2 — 3 The growth in support for the Nazis, 1929–32: Reasons for the growth in support for the Nazi Party","31-2 — 4 How Hitler became Chancellor, 1932–33: The Presidential and Reichstag elections of 1932"]},{"q":"2(a) Explain why the Munich Putsch of November 1923 failed. You may use the following in your answer: • the Bavarian leaders • the armed forces. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise knowledge — Kahr, Lossow and Seisser repudiating a promise made at gunpoint, the state police at the Feldherrnhalle, the Reichswehr's hostility, the passing of the 1923 crisis. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The putsch failed because Hitler tried to force a revolution on men who had no intention of making one, against a state that was recovering rather than collapsing. The first reason is the Bavarian leaders. On the evening of 8 November 1923 Hitler and armed SA men broke into a Munich beer hall where the state commissioner Gustav von Kahr was speaking, and at gunpoint Kahr, General von Lossow and Colonel von Seisser agreed to join a march on Berlin. The moment they were allowed to leave the building that agreement was worthless: all three repudiated it, and by the morning of 9 November the Bavarian police and army were being turned against the very rising they had promised to lead. Hitler had a hostage's promise rather than an ally's. The second reason is the armed forces. The Nazis brought perhaps two thousand marchers and had no friends in the national army, whose commander had made plain that the Reichswehr would put down any rising; in Munich itself the state police, not the Nazis, held the government buildings. When the column reached the Feldherrnhalle on 9 November the police opened fire, sixteen Nazis and four policemen were killed, and the march broke up within minutes, Ludendorff's presence notwithstanding. The third reason, which neither stimulus point prompts, is that the attempt came too late. It had been designed for a Germany in ruins, with the French in the Ruhr since January 1923 and the currency worthless, but Stresemann had called off passive resistance in September and the Rentenmark was introduced in November, so Berlin was steadying exactly as Hitler moved, and nowhere else in the country rose in sympathy. A coup needs allies, force and a collapsing state, and in November 1923 Hitler had none of the three.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["A coup is not a battle: ask what Hitler needed other people to do for him, and then check whether any of them did it.","Plan three reasons of different kinds — the politicians, the men with guns, and the state of the country — so that the answer analyses rather than narrates.","Open on what Kahr, Lossow and Seisser did the moment they left the beer hall, and say what that cost the rising."],"codes":["31-2 — 2 The Munich Putsch and the Nazi Party, 1923–28: The reasons for, events and consequences of the Munich Putsch"]},{"q":"2(b) Explain why the Nazi Party won so little support in the years 1924–28. You may use the following in your answer: • the recovery of the German economy • the Bamberg Conference of 1926. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): analysis directed at causation — Weimar's recovery under Stresemann and the Dawes Plan, the party rebuilding under a speaking ban, the Bamberg quarrel, the failure to take working-class votes, 12 seats and 2.6 per cent in May 1928. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The Nazis made no headway between 1924 and 1928 because the grievances they existed to exploit had gone quiet, and because the party spent those years arguing with itself rather than with the country. The first and largest reason is the recovery. The Rentenmark ended the hyperinflation of 1923, the Dawes Plan of 1924 rescheduled reparations and opened the way to American loans, and industrial output, wages and employment rose; Locarno in 1925 and entry to the League of Nations in 1926 made Germany respectable abroad. A party whose entire case was that the Republic was a swindle imposed by traitors had almost nothing to say to people whose lives were visibly improving, and its votes accordingly went to the parties of the centre and the left. The second reason is that the party was rebuilding rather than campaigning. It had been banned after the putsch and was refounded in February 1925; Hitler was forbidden to speak in public in Bavaria from March 1925 until 1927, and he had decided that power must now be taken legally, so the effort went into organisation — the country divided into Gaue, the SS founded in 1925, the Hitler Youth in 1926 — and organisation wins no elections by itself. The third reason, which is what the Bamberg Conference of 14 February 1926 exposes, is that the party could not agree what it was. The northern branches round Gregor Strasser and Goebbels wanted a genuinely socialist programme; Hitler faced them down at Bamberg, kept the Twenty-Five Points unaltered and won Goebbels over, but the quarrel had cost two years. A fourth point follows: the socialist appeal never took working-class votes from the SPD and KPD, and only after 1928 did the party turn to the farmers. In May 1928 it won 12 seats and 2.6 per cent — a machine in good order with nobody to sell to.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what a party like this needs in order to grow, and then check which of those conditions Germany supplied between 1924 and 1928.","Treat the party's own problems and the country's prosperity as two separate causes, and give each a paragraph of its own.","Start with what the Dawes Plan and the Rentenmark did to the argument the Nazis had been making since 1920."],"codes":["31-2 — 2 The Munich Putsch and the Nazi Party, 1923–28: Reasons for limited support for the Nazi Party, 1924–28","31-2 — 2 The Munich Putsch and the Nazi Party, 1923–28: The reasons for, events and consequences of the Munich Putsch"]},{"q":"2(a) Explain why unemployment in Germany rose so steeply in the years 1929–32. You may use the following in your answer: • the Wall Street Crash of October 1929 • Brüning's policies. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causal chains, not description — American short-term loans recalled, the collapse of export demand, the banking crisis of 1931, Brüning's deflation by decree under Article 48. Figures: about 1.3 million in 1929 to roughly 6 million by the winter of 1932-33. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Unemployment rose so steeply because Germany's prosperity had been borrowed, because the world stopped buying what Germany made, and because the government's response took more money out of the economy rather than putting it in. The first reason is the foreign loans. The recovery after 1924 had been financed under the Dawes Plan largely by short-term American credit, much of it lent to German firms and city councils. When Wall Street crashed in October 1929 those loans were called in and no new ones were made, so companies that had expanded on borrowed money had to cut production and sack workers at once. The second reason is trade. Germany was an exporting economy, and as the slump spread the world's demand for machinery, chemicals, steel and ships fell while other countries put up tariffs to protect their own producers. The heavy industries of the Ruhr and Saxony were the first to shed men, and when a major Berlin bank failed in July 1931 the credit system froze and firms that were still solvent could not borrow either. The third reason, which the Wall Street Crash alone cannot explain, is government policy. Heinrich Brüning, Chancellor from March 1930, lost his Reichstag majority and governed by presidential decree under Article 48; he cut public spending, civil-service salaries, wages and unemployment benefit and raised taxes, partly from fear of another inflation and partly to demonstrate that Germany could not pay reparations. Cutting demand in the middle of a slump put still more people out of work, which is why the Communists called him the Hunger Chancellor. The result was cumulative: registered unemployment stood at about 1.3 million in 1929, passed 3 million by the end of 1930, went over 4 million during 1931 and reached roughly 6 million by the winter of 1932 to 1933.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask first where the money for Germany's recovery had come from, because that tells you why an American crash mattered so much here.","Separate the causes that came from outside Germany from the one that was made in Berlin, and give each its own chain of cause and effect.","Open on the short-term American loans of the Dawes Plan years and what happened to them after October 1929."],"codes":["31-2 — 3 The growth in support for the Nazis, 1929–32: The growth of unemployment","31-2 — 2 The Munich Putsch and the Nazi Party, 1923–28: Reasons for limited support for the Nazi Party, 1924–28"]},{"q":"2(b) Explain why the Communist Party gained support in Germany in the years 1929–32. You may use the following in your answer: • the growth of unemployment • government by presidential decree. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise detail — mass unemployment in industrial cities, the SPD's toleration of Brüning and the KPD's attacks on it, contempt for a Reichstag that had stopped governing, KPD seats 54 in 1928 to 100 in November 1932. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Communist support grew because the people the slump hit hardest were the people the party was built among, because their own traditional party was tied to the government doing the cutting, and because the Republic itself had visibly stopped working. The first reason is unemployment. The collapse fell first on heavy industry and on the big cities, and registered unemployment passed 3 million by the end of 1930 and over 4 million in 1931; the KPD organised precisely among the long-term unemployed, who had lost their insurance and were living on cut relief, and it offered them meetings, marches and an explanation in which the crisis was the death of capitalism rather than bad luck. Its Reichstag seats rose from 54 in 1928 to 77 in 1930, 89 in July 1932 and 100 in November 1932. The second reason is what happened to the Social Democrats. The SPD tolerated Brüning's government in order to keep something worse out, so a worker whose benefit had been cut twice could reasonably conclude that his own party was helping to cut it; the Communists, following Moscow's line, denounced the SPD as no better than the right and refused all cooperation, which cost Germany dearly but won the KPD votes from angry Social Democrats. The third reason, which neither stimulus point fully prompts, is the discrediting of parliament itself. From 1930 Brüning governed by presidential decree under Article 48 because he had no majority; the Reichstag met less and less and decided less and less. A party that had always said parliamentary democracy was a fraud could now point at the evidence. The three worked together: hardship gave the KPD its recruits, the SPD's position gave it their loyalty, and government by decree gave its argument the appearance of truth.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Support moves for two reasons at once — what one party offers and what the other side is seen doing; build the answer round both.","The stimulus points cover the economy and the constitution, so look for a reason that lies inside the parties of the left themselves.","Begin with who exactly the unemployed of 1930 to 1932 were, and why they were within the Communists' reach."],"codes":["31-2 — 3 The growth in support for the Nazis, 1929–32: The growth of unemployment","31-2 — 4 How Hitler became Chancellor, 1932–33: The Presidential and Reichstag elections of 1932"]},{"q":"2(a) Explain why the Nazi Party's share of the vote fell in the Reichstag election of November 1932. You may use the following in your answer: • the violence of the SA • Hitler's refusal to serve under von Papen. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): sustained causal analysis — SA violence after the ban was lifted in June 1932, Hitler's refusal of the vice-chancellorship in August and the Nazi-Communist vote that brought Papen down, campaign exhaustion and empty funds, conservative voters returning to the DNVP. Result: 196 seats and 33.1 per cent. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The Nazi vote fell in November 1932 because the party had frightened the very voters it had spent two years winning, because its all-or-nothing tactics made it look like an obstacle rather than a solution, and because it had simply run out of money and men. The first reason is the violence. Papen lifted the ban on the SA in June 1932 and the summer that followed was the bloodiest in the Republic's history, with street fighting between stormtroopers and Communists in the industrial towns. In August SA men murdered a Communist at Potempa in Upper Silesia, and when Hitler publicly took the murderers' side, respectable middle-class voters who had turned to the Nazis for order were shown what they had voted for. The second reason is Hitler's refusal to compromise. When Hindenburg offered him the vice-chancellorship in August 1932 Hitler demanded the chancellorship with full powers and refused everything less; in September the Nazi deputies then joined the Communists to bring Papen's government down. To a voter who wanted the crisis ended, a party that could neither govern nor let anyone else govern looked like part of the problem, and the Nationalists, who were prepared to work with Papen, gained seats in November at the Nazis' expense. The third reason, which neither stimulus point prompts, is exhaustion. The party had fought two presidential rounds in March and April, a Reichstag election on 31 July and a string of state elections, all in one year; its funds were spent, its speakers and stormtroopers worn out, and it had to fight again in November with nothing left. The result was 196 seats and 33.1 per cent, thirty-four seats fewer than in July, while the Communists rose to 100 — which is exactly why Hitler's appointment twelve weeks later looked so surprising.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["A party can lose votes for opposite reasons at once: ask separately what frightened people and what bored or exasperated them.","Both stimulus points are about how the party behaved in the summer of 1932; find a third reason that is about the party's own resources.","Start with what happened on the streets after the ban on the SA was lifted in June 1932, and who was watching."],"codes":["31-2 — 4 How Hitler became Chancellor, 1932–33: The Presidential and Reichstag elections of 1932","31-2 — 3 The growth in support for the Nazis, 1929–32: Reasons for the growth in support for the Nazi Party"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part played by Hindenburg and von Papen in bringing Hitler to power? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views evaluated, how each conveys its view analysed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit the collapse of the party system and the Nazi vote; against, the Cologne meeting of 4 January 1933, Hindenburg's refusal in August 1932 and the cabinet of three.","sol":"Interpretation 2 reduces Hindenburg and Papen to timekeepers: the men round the President 'settled the date, not the outcome', because 230 seats, thirteen million presidential votes and an SA of some four hundred thousand had already made a Hitler government unavoidable, and 'the collapse of everything else left them with nobody else to choose'. Interpretation 1 makes them the decisive actors, opening a side door for a man they despised. The criterion for judging between them must be whether Hitler could have been kept out in January 1933 by men who wanted to keep him out. Much of Interpretation 2 is well founded. The Depression really had destroyed the middle: after Brüning lost his majority in 1930 every Chancellor governed by presidential decree under Article 48, so Germany was already being ruled without parliament before Hitler entered it, and by July 1932 the Nazis and the Communists together held a blocking majority, which made any other parliamentary government arithmetically impossible. Papen's own cabinet of barons was defeated in the Reichstag in September 1932 by an overwhelming margin. In that sense the choice facing Hindenburg in January 1933 had been narrowed by forces neither he nor Papen controlled. Yet Interpretation 1 is right that the narrowing was not to a single option, and this is where Interpretation 2 understates the two men. Hindenburg had refused Hitler the chancellorship in August 1932 and offered only the vice-chancellorship, and Hitler had spent the autumn out of office as a result, so the President's veto had already been shown to work. In November the Nazis lost thirty-four seats, their funds were exhausted and Gregor Strasser resigned in December; Schleicher, Chancellor from 3 December, was trying to split the party and might have been given the emergency powers he asked for. What changed was personal: Papen, dismissed in November and wanting revenge on Schleicher, met Hitler at Cologne on 4 January 1933 and then worked on Hindenburg through his son and his state secretary, arguing that a cabinet with only three National Socialists and himself as Vice-Chancellor would leave the conservatives in control. That bargain, and not the electorate, produced the government of 30 January. The two interpretations convey their views by different means. Interpretation 2 writes in the passive language of process — parties destroyed, nobody else to choose — so that individuals disappear; Interpretation 1 writes in the active language of intrigue, with a door and the men who opened it. Each vocabulary hides what the other shows. My judgement is that Interpretation 2 is convincing about why a Hitler government had become possible and unconvincing about why it happened in January 1933. Without the collapse of the party system Papen would have had nothing to bargain with; but the Nazis were weaker that winter than at any time since 1930, and it took a particular quarrel between two particular men to put Hitler in office when it did. Hindenburg and Papen did not create the opportunity, but they chose to take it, and they could have chosen otherwise.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first what Interpretation 2 actually claims about the two men, and name the test you will judge that claim by.","Use Interpretation 1 for the case against, and support both sides with dated detail rather than with the interpretations restated.","Open by putting Interpretation 2's claim about Hindenburg and Papen into your own words and stating the criterion your judgement will use."],"codes":["31-2 — 4 How Hitler became Chancellor, 1932–33: The Presidential and Reichstag elections of 1932","31-2 — 3 The growth in support for the Nazis, 1929–32: The growth of unemployment"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the importance of the Nazi Party's popular support in bringing Hitler to power by January 1933? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views weighed, the means by which each conveys its view analysed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit 230 seats, 37.3 per cent and the presidential vote; against, the November setback, the 1928 figures and the fact that no election ever gave the Nazis a majority.","sol":"Interpretation 2 makes popular support the whole explanation: 230 seats and 37 per cent in July 1932, thirteen million votes for Hitler in April, an SA of some four hundred thousand, a party system destroyed by the Depression, and therefore 'nobody else to choose'. Interpretation 1 answers that the Nazi vote was falling, not rising, and that Hitler was lifted into office by a handful of men. The question is whether the party's popularity was sufficient to bring Hitler to power, or only necessary to it. The case for Interpretation 2 is strong and should be built from figures rather than adjectives. In May 1928 the Nazis had 12 seats and 2.6 per cent and were a fringe; after the Wall Street Crash and the rise of unemployment past three million they took 107 seats in September 1930 and 230 on 31 July 1932, the largest party in Germany. Hitler's thirteen million votes in the presidential run-off of 10 April 1932 turned him into a national alternative even in defeat. That vote is what made him worth bargaining with: no conservative politician wanted a mass movement of that size against him in the street, and Papen's own attraction to the deal was precisely that Hitler could deliver a popular following that the barons could not. Without the votes there is no bargain at all. Yet Interpretation 1's objection is factually unanswerable, and Interpretation 2 passes over it. The party never won a majority in a free election; its best result left nearly two Germans in three voting for somebody else. On 6 November 1932 it lost thirty-four seats and fell to 196 and 33.1 per cent, its funds were spent and Gregor Strasser resigned in December, so the movement was past its peak at the moment of its triumph. Popular support had, moreover, been tried and had failed to open the door: in August 1932, at the very height of the Nazi vote, Hindenburg refused Hitler the chancellorship. What changed between August and January was not the electorate, which had moved against the Nazis, but Papen's quarrel with Schleicher and the meeting at Cologne on 4 January 1933. The two writers convey this disagreement by choosing different units of measurement. Interpretation 2 counts seats, votes and stormtroopers across years, which makes 1933 look like the arrival of a tide; Interpretation 1 counts weeks, meetings and ministerial places, which makes it look like an accident. Neither is inventing its evidence. I therefore agree with Interpretation 2 that the Nazis' popular support was the necessary condition of everything that followed — it destroyed the alternatives, made the Reichstag ungovernable and made Hitler the only leader with a mass following the conservatives could hope to use. I do not agree that it was sufficient. The votes made a Hitler government thinkable; only Papen's intrigue made it happen in January 1933, and it happened at the moment when the votes themselves were in retreat.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Distinguish between a cause without which the outcome was impossible and a cause that was enough on its own; the whole judgement turns on that difference.","Interpretation 1's strongest fact is the November result; state it precisely and ask what it does to Interpretation 2's argument.","Begin by setting out what Interpretation 2 counts as evidence of popular strength, and name the criterion you will judge it by."],"codes":["31-2 — 4 How Hitler became Chancellor, 1932–33: The Presidential and Reichstag elections of 1932","31-2 — 3 The growth in support for the Nazis, 1929–32: Reasons for the growth in support for the Nazi Party"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the failure of the other German parties as a reason for Hitler coming to power? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): an explained evaluation reviewing both views, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit government by decree from 1930, the disappearance of the DNVP and DVP vote and the KPD-SPD split; against, the Centre Party's steady vote, the November setback and the decisions of individuals.","sol":"Interpretation 2 argues that Hitler came to power because everything else had fallen down: the Depression 'destroyed the parties of the middle', no Chancellor after 1930 could command a majority, all of them governed by emergency decree, and so 'the collapse of everything else left them with nobody else to choose'. Interpretation 1 disputes the conclusion rather than the description, insisting that a falling party was lifted in by a few men. The criterion I shall use is whether the other parties' failure by itself accounts for a National Socialist Chancellor, rather than for some other authoritarian government. A great deal of the interpretation is simply right. The parties of the moderate right collapsed almost completely: the district officer's remark in Source C that the Nationalists and the People's Party 'have simply disappeared here' describes what happened across Protestant Germany, where the Nazi vote rose from a few hundred in 1928 to more than half the poll in July 1932. On the left, the Social Democrats tolerated Brüning's cuts and lost angry supporters to a Communist Party that rose from 54 seats in 1928 to 100 in November 1932 while denouncing the SPD as an enemy, so the two largest working-class parties spent the crisis fighting each other. Constitutionally the failure was even plainer: after the Reichstag threw out Brüning's budget in 1930 he governed under Article 48, and by July 1932 Nazis and Communists together could block any majority, so parliamentary government had ended before Hitler entered it. Two objections qualify the claim. First, the collapse was not universal: the Catholic Centre Party held its vote almost unchanged through every election of 1930 to 1932, which shows that the Depression did not automatically dissolve a party with a settled following, and that the collapse of the Protestant middle-class parties had causes of its own. Second, and more important, the failure of the other parties explains why Germany would be ruled by decree; it does not explain why it would be ruled by Hitler. Papen and Schleicher were both governing without parliament already, and Interpretation 1 is right that in the winter of 1932 the Nazis were weakening — thirty-four seats lost in November, funds exhausted, Strasser gone — so an authoritarian government without them was still being attempted when Papen met Hitler at Cologne on 4 January 1933. The interpretations convey their views through the size of the subject each takes. Interpretation 2's subject is a whole political system, so its verbs happen to parties rather than being done by people; Interpretation 1's subject is a room in the President's palace, so everything in it is somebody's decision. Each is accurate about its own scale. My judgement is that Interpretation 2 gives the best available explanation of the opportunity and a poor one of the choice. The other parties' failure made a parliamentary solution impossible and left Hitler as the only politician with millions behind him; that is the deeper cause. But the same failure could have produced a Papen or a Schleicher dictatorship, and it took the bargain of January 1933 to decide which. The collapse of the rest made Hitler possible; it did not make him inevitable.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["The interpretation explains an opportunity; ask whether an opportunity of that shape had to be filled by this particular man.","Look for a party that did not collapse, because an exception tests the claim that the Depression destroyed the party system as such.","Open by stating precisely which parties Interpretation 2 says failed, and the criterion on which you will judge the claim."],"codes":["31-2 — 4 How Hitler became Chancellor, 1932–33: The Presidential and Reichstag elections of 1932","31-2 — 3 The growth in support for the Nazis, 1929–32: The growth of unemployment"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In which month and year did Hitler first attend a meeting of the German Workers' Party and join it?","a":"September 1919 (the meeting was on 12 September 1919)."},{"q":"How many Reichstag seats, and what share of the vote, did the Nazi Party win in the election of May 1928?","a":"12 seats and 2.6 per cent of the vote."},{"q":"On what date did the Nazi Party win 230 seats and 37.3 per cent, its best result in a free election?","a":"31 July 1932."},{"q":"Who was Chancellor immediately before Hitler, and on what date was Hitler appointed Chancellor?","a":"Kurt von Schleicher; Hitler was appointed on 30 January 1933."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The German Workers' Party (DAP)","open":false,"code":"31-2 — 1 Early development of the Nazi Party, 1920–22: Hitler's early career","back":"Founded in Munich on 5 January 1919 by Anton Drexler. Hitler was sent by the army to report on it, attended a meeting on 12 September 1919, joined, and was soon on its committee handling propaganda and recruitment."},{"front":"The founding of the NSDAP","open":false,"code":"31-2 — 1 Early development of the Nazi Party, 1920–22: Hitler's early career","back":"On 24 February 1920 the party announced its programme at a Munich beer hall and renamed itself the National Socialist German Workers' Party. In July 1921 Hitler resigned and returned as chairman with sole authority."},{"front":"The Twenty-Five Point Programme","open":false,"code":"31-2 — 1 Early development of the Nazi Party, 1920–22: The Twenty-Five Point Programme","back":"Announced 24 February 1920: destroy the Treaty of Versailles, unite all Germans, win land to live on, citizenship for those of German blood only so excluding Jews, nationalise trusts, share profits, strong central government."},{"front":"The SA","open":false,"code":"31-2 — 1 Early development of the Nazi Party, 1920–22: The Twenty-Five Point Programme","back":"The Sturmabteilung, formed in 1921 from ex-soldiers and Freikorps men. It guarded Nazi meetings, broke up opponents' meetings and gave young men a uniform and a band; later known as the brownshirts."},{"front":"The Munich Putsch, 8–9 November 1923","open":false,"code":"31-2 — 2 The Munich Putsch and the Nazi Party, 1923–28: The reasons for, events and consequences of the Munich Putsch","back":"Hitler forced Kahr, Lossow and Seisser at gunpoint in a Munich beer hall to back a march on Berlin. They repudiated it; next day state police fired near the Feldherrnhalle, killing sixteen Nazis and four policemen."},{"front":"Hitler's trial and sentence, 1924","open":false,"code":"31-2 — 2 The Munich Putsch and the Nazi Party, 1923–28: The reasons for, events and consequences of the Munich Putsch","back":"Tried for treason from 26 February 1924 before a sympathetic Bavarian court, Hitler used it as a national platform. Sentenced to five years at Landsberg, he served about nine months and was released in December 1924."},{"front":"Mein Kampf","open":false,"code":"31-2 — 2 The Munich Putsch and the Nazi Party, 1923–28: The reasons for, events and consequences of the Munich Putsch","back":"Dictated at Landsberg to Rudolf Hess; the first volume was published in 1925. It set out Aryan racial superiority, hatred of Jews and communists, the destruction of Versailles, living space in the east and the leadership principle."},{"front":"The Bamberg Conference, 14 February 1926","open":false,"code":"31-2 — 2 The Munich Putsch and the Nazi Party, 1923–28: Reasons for limited support for the Nazi Party, 1924–28","back":"Hitler summoned the party's leaders to Bamberg and defeated the northern, more socialist wing round Gregor Strasser. The Twenty-Five Points stood unaltered, Goebbels changed sides, and Hitler's leadership was settled."},{"front":"The lean years, 1924–28","open":false,"code":"31-2 — 2 The Munich Putsch and the Nazi Party, 1923–28: Reasons for limited support for the Nazi Party, 1924–28","back":"Weimar recovered: the Rentenmark, the Dawes Plan of 1924, Locarno in 1925, League membership in 1926. The Nazis rebuilt as an organisation but won only 12 seats and 2.6 per cent in May 1928."},{"front":"The Wall Street Crash, October 1929","open":false,"code":"31-2 — 3 The growth in support for the Nazis, 1929–32: The growth of unemployment","back":"American banks recalled the short-term loans that had paid for German recovery. Firms cut production, exports collapsed and a major Berlin bank failed in July 1931, freezing credit."},{"front":"German unemployment, 1929–33","open":false,"code":"31-2 — 3 The growth in support for the Nazis, 1929–32: The growth of unemployment","back":"Registered unemployment was about 1.3 million in 1929, passed 3 million by the end of 1930 and over 4 million during 1931, and reached roughly 6 million in the winter of 1932–33."},{"front":"Brüning, Chancellor from March 1930","open":false,"code":"31-2 — 3 The growth in support for the Nazis, 1929–32: The growth of unemployment","back":"Lost his Reichstag majority and governed by presidential decree under Article 48. He cut wages, salaries and unemployment benefit and raised taxes, and the Communists called him the Hunger Chancellor. He fell on 30 May 1932."},{"front":"The Communist vote, 1928–32","open":false,"code":"31-2 — 3 The growth in support for the Nazis, 1929–32: The growth of unemployment","back":"The KPD rose from 54 seats in 1928 to 77 in 1930, 89 in July 1932 and 100 in November 1932. Its growth frightened farmers, shopkeepers and businessmen towards the Nazis."},{"front":"Goebbels and Nazi propaganda","open":false,"code":"31-2 — 3 The growth in support for the Nazis, 1929–32: Reasons for the growth in support for the Nazi Party","back":"Gauleiter of Berlin from November 1926 and the party's national propaganda chief by 1930. He standardised posters and slogans, worked villages the other parties ignored, and in 1932 flew Hitler between several towns a day."},{"front":"30 January 1933","open":false,"code":"31-2 — 4 How Hitler became Chancellor, 1932–33: The Presidential and Reichstag elections of 1932","back":"After Papen's intrigue against Schleicher and their Cologne meeting of 4 January, Hindenburg appointed Hitler Chancellor. Papen became Vice-Chancellor and only three ministers, Hitler included, were Nazis."}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The sources and interpretations written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Source A: From a report on a National Socialist meeting in Munich, written for the Bavarian Ministry of the Interior by a police officer who attended it, March 1922. Written for this room in the style of such a report; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — I attended the meeting of the National Socialist German Workers' Party held last night in a brewery hall in this city. The hall holds perhaps two thousand and it was full an hour before the advertised time; swastika banners hung round the walls. The speaker Hitler was on his feet for nearly two hours. He called the peace treaty a theft and the men of November 1918 traitors who had stabbed the army in the back; he spoke of profiteers and of the Jews. He offered no measures of any kind, yet the hall rose to him again and again and the collection buckets came back heavy. The audience was in the main young: ex-soldiers, small shopkeepers, clerks and students, with few working men from the large factories. Some fifty men of the party's storm section stood along the walls in grey windbreakers with swastika armbands; when three men at the back began to interrupt they were carried out and beaten in the yard, and our own officers present did not think it necessary to intervene. In my judgement this party is dangerous less for its programme, which is the usual one, than for the effect of this one speaker upon a crowd.","Source B: From a letter written by an unemployed fitter in a machine-tool works in Chemnitz to his brother in Hamburg, February 1932. Written for this room in the style of such a letter; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — You ask how we are. I have not worked since my shop was closed. The works put off half the men in the winter of 1930 and the rest of us went a shop at a time after that. My insurance ran out in the summer and the welfare money has been cut twice since; it buys bread, potatoes and coal if we are careful with the coal. Grete takes in washing and I queue at the labour exchange on Tuesdays with men I was apprenticed with. What I cannot bear is the standing about. In the evening there are meetings on every corner. The Communists say the crisis is the end of the whole system and shout at us to be ready; the Nazis bring a band and a van with a loudspeaker and tell us we were robbed in 1918 and that we shall be given work. Our own party tells us to be patient and to support the government in Berlin, and I have been patient for two years. Hermann next door has gone into the SA. He says they give him a bed, soup and boots. I do not know what I shall do in the spring, but I shall not vote again for men who ask me to wait.","Source C: From a confidential report sent by the district officer of a rural district in Lower Saxony to the provincial authorities, a fortnight after the Reichstag election of 31 July 1932. Written for this room in the style of such an official report; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — The result in this district requires explanation. In 1928 the National Socialists polled a few hundred votes here and nobody thought them worth watching; on 31 July they took more than half the votes cast in every village but two, on the highest turnout I have recorded. They have worked the district for two years. Speakers came out from Hanover to farm kitchens and to village inns where the other parties have not held a meeting in my memory; they promised the farmer protection against his debts and against foreign grain and told him he was the foundation of the nation. The SA marched through the villages with a band on Sunday afternoons and gave the young men something to belong to. Before the poll an aeroplane brought Herr Hitler to the town for an hour and the roads were blocked from noon. Their newspapers and handbills reached every house. I should add that the Communists' meetings in the town have frightened the tradespeople badly, and that many who voted National Socialist tell me plainly that they did so to keep the Communists out. The Nationalists and the People's Party have simply disappeared here.","Interpretation 1: From a study of the politics of the Weimar Republic's last years, by a historian writing in the 1980s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — Hitler did not win power; he was given it, and by a very small number of men. By January 1933 the Nazi vote was falling, not rising: the party lost thirty-four seats in November 1932, its funds were exhausted and Gregor Strasser had walked out of it in December. What saved Hitler was the quarrel inside the President's circle. Papen, humiliated by Schleicher, went behind his back, and with the help of Hindenburg's son and his state secretary persuaded the old Field Marshal that a cabinet containing only three National Socialists, with Papen himself as Vice-Chancellor, would leave the conservatives in charge. They believed they had hired a noisy agitator whom they could dismiss when he had served his turn. Hitler entered the Chancellery through a side door, opened for him by men who despised him.","Interpretation 2: From a general history of the Weimar Republic, by a historian writing in the 2000s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — The men around Hindenburg settled the date, not the outcome. By the summer of 1932 the National Socialists were the largest party in the Reichstag, with 230 seats and 37 per cent of the vote; thirteen million Germans had voted for Hitler against Hindenburg in April; the SA had grown to some four hundred thousand men and could put them on any street in the country. Meanwhile the Depression had destroyed the parties of the middle: no Chancellor after 1930 was able to command a majority, and each of them governed by the President's emergency decrees instead. Germany was being ruled without parliament long before Hitler entered it. The conservatives did not choose Hitler; the collapse of everything else left them with nobody else to choose.","How the room uses them: Source A is the inference source for Question 1. Sources B and C are the contemporary pair for the 8-mark usefulness question, 3(a), and may be used on 3(c). Interpretations 1 and 2 are the pair for 3(b), 3(c) and 3(d). Every quotation in the model answers, worked examples and plans of this room comes from the five texts above and from nowhere else, with one exception: the fourth and fifth worked examples work a separate practice pair of interpretations, written for those two examples alone and printed in full inside each of them, and they quote only from that pair and from Sources B and C."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 3 Question 3(d), 16 marks)","items":["Question: 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part played by the Depression in bringing Hitler to power by January 1933? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]. This is one paragraph from the middle of the answer, putting the case for Interpretation 2 and then measuring it.","Interpretation 2 argues that the Depression 'destroyed the parties of the middle', and the electoral record supports that claim more exactly than the interpretation itself troubles to show. In May 1928, at the end of the recovery, the Nazis held 12 seats with 2.6 per cent of the vote and nobody in Berlin thought them worth counting. Within a year of the Wall Street Crash of October 1929 registered unemployment had passed three million, and in September 1930 the party took 107 seats; by 31 July 1932, with over five million out of work, it had 230 seats and 37.3 per cent. The middle-class parties of the Protestant north did not merely lose ground, they disappeared, which is why the district officer of Source C can report that 'the Nationalists and the People's Party have simply disappeared here' in a district where the Nazis had polled a few hundred votes four years earlier. Interpretation 1 does not deny any of this; it answers on a different scale, pointing out that by January 1933 the Nazi vote was 'falling, not rising'. That is true and it matters, but it is a fact about twelve weeks set against a fact about four years. The Depression cannot explain why Hitler was appointed on 30 January rather than in August 1932 or not at all, and to that extent Interpretation 1 is right; it can and does explain why, by the winter of 1932, there was no parliamentary government left to appoint instead.","Why this is Level 4: it 'provides an explained evaluation reviewing the alternative views in coming to a substantiated judgement' — the case for Interpretation 2 is built and then measured against Interpretation 1 rather than simply contradicted. The analysis is precise about how the difference of view is conveyed, since it names the two writers' different scales of time as the source of the disagreement. The contextual knowledge is precisely selected rather than general: dated election results, the seat and percentage figures, the unemployment totals, and a source used as evidence of a pattern. The line of reasoning is sustained and the closing sentence judges rather than summarises."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same as above — 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part played by the Depression in bringing Hitler to power by January 1933?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks].","Interpretation 2 says that the Depression destroyed the parties of the middle and that this is why Hitler came to power, and I mostly agree with this. After the Wall Street Crash lots of people lost their jobs and became very poor, so they were desperate and looked for someone new to vote for, and the Nazis did well in the elections because of this. Interpretation 1 disagrees because it says Hitler was given power by a small number of men such as Papen, who thought they could control him. That is also partly true, because Hindenburg did have to appoint him. Overall I think both interpretations are partly right, because the Depression made people vote Nazi and the politicians then let Hitler into government.","Why this is Level 2 and not Level 3: it does offer 'valid evaluative comment to agree with or counter the interpretation' and it does select details from both, which is what Level 2 asks for. What it misses is Level 3's 'relevant contextual knowledge used directly to support the evaluation': 'lots of people lost their jobs' and 'the Nazis did well in the elections' are assertions where the unemployment figures and the 1928, 1930 and July 1932 results should be. It never shows how the two interpretations convey their views, and the judgement is announced in the last sentence rather than reasoned towards, so 'its justification is insecure or undeveloped and a line of reasoning is not sustained'. Turning it into a Level 3 answer means replacing every vague phrase with a dated fact that does evaluative work."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the early Nazi Party, 1919–23 — the DAP, the Twenty-Five Points and the SA","items":["The German Workers' Party (Deutsche Arbeiterpartei, DAP) was founded in Munich on 5 January 1919 by Anton Drexler, a toolmaker; Gottfried Feder supplied much of its economic thinking.","Hitler, still serving in the army, was sent to report on the party and attended a meeting on 12 September 1919; he joined, and was soon on its committee with responsibility for propaganda and recruitment.","Munich suited the party: Bavaria was full of demobilised soldiers and Freikorps men, and the state government and police were sympathetic to the nationalist right after the crushing of the Munich soviet in 1919.","On 24 February 1920 Hitler and Drexler put the Twenty-Five Point Programme to a mass meeting in a Munich beer hall, and the party renamed itself the National Socialist German Workers' Party (NSDAP).","The nationalist points: destroy the Treaty of Versailles, unite all Germans in one greater Germany, acquire land and colonies to feed the nation, and build a strong central government.","The racial points: only those of German blood may be members of the nation, so Jews were to be excluded from citizenship, and non-Germans were to be treated as guests or expelled.","The socialist points: nationalise the great trusts, share the profits of heavy industry, provide generously for the old, reform land ownership and abolish incomes not earned by work.","The swastika, the red flag and the straight-armed salute were adopted as the party's symbols in these years, and the party bought its own newspaper, the Völkischer Beobachter, in 1920.","The SA (Sturmabteilung) was formed in 1921, largely from ex-soldiers and Freikorps men; it guarded Nazi meetings, broke up opponents' meetings and made the party visible on the streets.","In July 1921 Hitler resigned from the party when the committee proposed a merger with another group, and returned only on being made chairman with sole authority, which the party accepted because it could not do without its speaker."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Munich Putsch, the trial, Mein Kampf and the lean years, 1923–28","items":["The background to the putsch: French and Belgian troops occupied the Ruhr in January 1923, passive resistance destroyed the currency, and Mussolini's march on Rome in October 1922 suggested that a determined minority could seize a capital.","On the evening of 8 November 1923 Hitler and armed SA men broke into a Munich beer hall where the Bavarian state commissioner Gustav von Kahr was speaking; at gunpoint Kahr, General von Lossow and Colonel von Seisser agreed to join a march on Berlin.","All three repudiated the agreement as soon as they were released, and by the morning of 9 November the Bavarian police and army were being used against the rising.","On 9 November about two thousand Nazis, with Ludendorff at their head, marched into central Munich; armed state police met them near the Feldherrnhalle and sixteen Nazis and four policemen were killed.","Hitler was arrested; the party and its newspaper were banned. Ludendorff was acquitted at the trial that followed.","Hitler's trial for treason opened on 26 February 1924 before a sympathetic Bavarian court that allowed him to speak at length; the reporting made a local agitator into a national figure.","He was sentenced to five years' imprisonment at Landsberg, served about nine months and was released on 20 December 1924.","At Landsberg he dictated Mein Kampf to Rudolf Hess; the first volume was published in 1925, setting out Aryan racial superiority, hatred of Jews and communists, the destruction of Versailles, living space in the east and the leadership principle.","The party was refounded in February 1925, and Hitler was banned from speaking in public in Bavaria from March 1925 until 1927; he had concluded that power must be won through elections rather than by force.","Reorganisation in these years: the country divided into Gaue under Gauleiter, the SS founded in 1925 as Hitler's personal bodyguard, and the Hitler Youth founded in 1926.","The Bamberg Conference of 14 February 1926 settled the quarrel with the northern branches round Gregor Strasser and Joseph Goebbels, who wanted a more socialist line; the Twenty-Five Points stood unaltered and Goebbels came over to Hitler, becoming Gauleiter of Berlin in November 1926.","Weimar's recovery removed the party's market: the Rentenmark of 1923, the Dawes Plan of 1924 with its American loans, Locarno in 1925 and League of Nations membership in 1926.","In the Reichstag election of May 1928 the Nazis won 12 seats and 2.6 per cent of the vote; party membership had grown to roughly a hundred thousand, so the organisation was far ahead of the vote."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Depression, the Communists and the growth of Nazi support, 1929–32","items":["The Wall Street Crash of October 1929 caused American banks to recall the short-term loans that had financed German recovery under the Dawes Plan; Gustav Stresemann had died earlier that same month.","World demand for German exports collapsed and other countries raised tariffs; a major Berlin bank failed in July 1931 and the credit system froze.","Registered unemployment was about 1.3 million in 1929, passed 3 million by the end of 1930 and over 4 million during 1931, and reached roughly 6 million in the winter of 1932–33.","Heinrich Brüning became Chancellor in March 1930; when the Reichstag rejected his budget he governed by presidential decree under Article 48, cutting wages, salaries and unemployment benefit and raising taxes.","Brüning's deflation deepened the slump and the Communists nicknamed him the Hunger Chancellor; he banned the SA and SS in April 1932 and fell on 30 May 1932.","Communist support grew with the unemployment: the KPD held 54 seats in 1928, 77 in September 1930, 89 in July 1932 and 100 in November 1932, when it took 16.9 per cent of the vote.","The KPD organised among the long-term unemployed of the big cities and, following Moscow's line, attacked the Social Democrats as enemies rather than allies; the SPD's toleration of Brüning cost it support.","Fear of a communist Germany drove farmers, shopkeepers, professional people and industrialists towards the Nazis as the force most likely to stop the KPD.","Joseph Goebbels was the party's national propaganda chief by 1930: standardised posters and slogans, a party press, cheap handbills, mass meetings and, in 1932, aeroplane tours that put Hitler into several towns in one day.","The message was targeted: work and bread for the unemployed, protection against debt and foreign grain for farmers, order and anti-communism for the middle classes, national revival for the young and for ex-servicemen, and the Treaty of Versailles and the 'November criminals' as the explanation of everything.","The SA had grown to some four hundred thousand men by the summer of 1932; it housed and fed unemployed members, paraded with bands, and in fighting the Communists made the Nazis look like the only force able to beat them.","The Nazi vote rose from 12 seats and 2.6 per cent in May 1928 to 107 seats and 18.3 per cent in September 1930, and to 230 seats and 37.3 per cent on 31 July 1932."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: 1932–33 — the elections, Papen, Schleicher, Hindenburg and the appointment","items":["Presidential election, first round, 13 March 1932: Hindenburg 49.5 per cent, Hitler 30.1 per cent, the Communist Ernst Thälmann 13.2 per cent, the Nationalist Theodor Duesterberg 6.8 per cent.","Presidential run-off, 10 April 1932: Hindenburg 53.0 per cent, Hitler 36.8 per cent with about 13.4 million votes, Thälmann 10.2 per cent. Hitler lost, but became a national alternative.","Franz von Papen became Chancellor on 1 June 1932 with a cabinet of aristocrats and almost no support in the Reichstag; he lifted the ban on the SA in June and removed the Social Democratic government of Prussia on 20 July 1932.","Reichstag election, 31 July 1932: the Nazis won 230 seats and 37.3 per cent, the largest party in Germany; the KPD won 89 seats and the SPD 133.","In August 1932 Hindenburg offered Hitler the vice-chancellorship and Hitler refused, demanding the chancellorship with full powers; in September the Nazi deputies joined the Communists to bring Papen's government down.","The summer of 1932 was the most violent of the Republic; in August SA men murdered a Communist at Potempa in Upper Silesia and Hitler publicly supported the murderers, which shocked many middle-class voters.","Reichstag election, 6 November 1932: the Nazis fell to 196 seats and 33.1 per cent, a loss of thirty-four seats, while the KPD rose to 100 and the Nationalists gained; Nazi funds were exhausted after four national campaigns in one year.","Kurt von Schleicher became Chancellor on 3 December 1932 and tried to split the Nazis by offering Gregor Strasser a post; Strasser resigned his party offices in December but took no one with him.","Papen, wanting revenge on Schleicher, met Hitler at a banker's house in Cologne on 4 January 1933 and then worked on Hindenburg through the President's son Oskar and his state secretary Otto Meissner.","Businessmen and landowners petitioned Hindenburg in November 1932 to appoint Hitler; the conservatives believed a cabinet containing only three Nazis, with Papen as Vice-Chancellor, would leave them in control.","Schleicher resigned on 28 January 1933 after Hindenburg refused him emergency powers, and on 30 January 1933 Hindenburg appointed Hitler Chancellor, with Papen as Vice-Chancellor and only two other Nazis, Wilhelm Frick and Hermann Göring, in the cabinet.","The Nazis never won a majority in a free election: their best result, in July 1932, left nearly two Germans in three voting for other parties."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the source and interpretation moves this paper rewards","items":["Inference: say something the source does not state but that must be true if the source is true, then give the words that force it. One inference and one supporting detail in each box.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose: what kind of document it is, who made it and when, and why it was made.","Utility: judge each source for the enquiry the question names, not in general. Content first, then how nature, origin and purpose make that content more or less useful, then the context that confirms or qualifies it.","Contextual knowledge scores only when it is used on the source; a paragraph about the period that never touches the sources earns nothing.","'Biased' is not a judgement on its own. Say whose view the source gives, why it gives that view, and what it is therefore useful for: a one-sided source is excellent evidence of the view it takes.","An official report written confidentially for superiors is often more useful than a public statement, because the writer needs to be accurate rather than persuasive; say so and use it.","A source can be useful for an enquiry it never mentions, if what it shows explains the thing being enquired into; say what you can infer, not just what it says.","Difference of view (3(b)): a disagreement about the past, not a difference of wording or of detail. State the one difference in your first sentence, then support it with a phrase from each interpretation.","Reason for difference (3(c)): interpretations differ because they ask different questions, rest on different evidence, or weigh different parts of the story. Name which, and show it from both interpretations, using Sources B and C if they illustrate the kind of evidence involved.","Differences based on conclusions drawn from evidence are legitimate; an interpretation is not wrong merely because another one disagrees with it.","Evaluating an interpretation (3(d)): use both interpretations and your own knowledge, show how each conveys its view, then decide how far the named interpretation can be agreed with, and say on what criterion."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 3 Question 3(d): 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the inevitability of Hitler coming to power by January 1933?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","items":["The question as printed: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the inevitability of Hitler coming to power by January 1933? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' This question prints no stimulus points: the two interpretations are the stimulus, and both must be used, so an answer that evaluates one in isolation cannot reach the top levels.","Decode the statement. Interpretation 2 claims that the outcome was fixed before the men who announced it sat down: 'the men around Hindenburg settled the date, not the outcome', because by the summer of 1932 the National Socialists were the largest party in the Reichstag with 230 seats and 37 per cent, thirteen million Germans had voted for Hitler against Hindenburg in April, the SA had grown to 'some four hundred thousand men', and the Depression had 'destroyed the parties of the middle' so that 'the collapse of everything else left them with nobody else to choose'. Interpretation 1 claims that nothing was fixed at all: 'Hitler did not win power; he was given it, and by a very small number of men', and what saved him was 'the quarrel inside the President's circle'. The criterion to be announced in the opening is what would have had to be true for the outcome to be avoidable — whether, in January 1933, any government without Hitler was still available to men who wanted one.","Opening (three or four sentences): state what Interpretation 2 argues in your own words, name Interpretation 1 as the alternative, announce the criterion and signal the position you will reach, so the line of reasoning is visible from the start.","Paragraph 1, for Interpretation 2 — the arithmetic. On 31 July 1932 the Nazis took 230 seats and 37.3 per cent and the Communists 89, so the two parties that would join nobody's coalition held 319 of the 608 seats; after 6 November they still held 296 of 584, more than every other party put together. Hitler had already refused to serve under anyone else. Interim judgement: from July 1932 no majority government could be formed that excluded the Nazis, and none could be formed that included them on any terms but Hitler's.","Paragraph 2, for Interpretation 2 — government had already left parliament. Brüning governing under Article 48 after the Reichstag rejected his budget in 1930, Papen from 1 June 1932 and Schleicher from 3 December doing the same; the Reichstag throwing Papen out in September 1932 by more than five hundred votes to fewer than fifty and being dissolved the same day. Interim judgement: the interpretation's strongest single sentence, that 'Germany was being ruled without parliament long before Hitler entered it', is accurate, and it means the decision had already shrunk to the President and the few men he listened to.","Paragraph 3, for Interpretation 2 — the alternatives had been tried and had broken. Papen's cabinet of barons had no party behind it; Schleicher spent December 1932 trying to split the Nazi Party, offering Gregor Strasser the vice-chancellorship and the premiership of Prussia on 3 December, and failed when Strasser refused and then resigned from the party; on 28 January 1933 Hindenburg refused Schleicher the dissolution and the emergency powers he asked for and Schleicher resigned. Interim judgement: by the last week of January every non-Nazi combination on offer had been attempted and had broken, which is as close to 'nobody else to choose' as the record comes.","Paragraph 4, against Interpretation 2 — the force said to compel the outcome was weakening. Interpretation 1's facts: thirty-four seats lost on 6 November 1932, leaving 196 and 33.1 per cent; funds exhausted after two presidential rounds in March and April and two Reichstag elections in one year; Gregor Strasser 'walked out of it in December'. Interim judgement: an outcome is not inevitable when the movement said to make it unavoidable is weaker at the moment it arrives than at any time since 1930.","Paragraph 5, against Interpretation 2 and the point that goes furthest beyond both texts — the refusal that was actually made. On 13 August 1932, with the Nazi vote at its height, Hindenburg refused Hitler the chancellorship and offered only the vice-chancellorship, which Hitler rejected, and Hitler stayed out of office for another five months: the President's veto had been tried and had worked. What changed by January was not the country but Papen's wish for revenge on Schleicher, the meeting with Hitler at Cologne on 4 January 1933, and the work done on Hindenburg through the President's son and his state secretary. Interim judgement: a thing that can be refused in August and granted in January, while the evidence for it gets worse, is settled by people and not by circumstances.","The evaluation thread that must run through every paragraph: how each interpretation conveys its view. Interpretation 2 writes in the passive language of process — parties destroyed, nobody else to choose — so the outcome arrives like weather and nobody is answerable for it; Interpretation 1 writes in the active language of intrigue, with a side door and the men who opened it, so a movement of millions shrinks to a corridor. Saying this, and using it, is what the top level means by showing how the differences of view are conveyed.","What the conclusion must say: not 'both are partly right'. It must apply the criterion announced at the start — that Interpretation 2 is convincing as far as the end of parliamentary government, because by January 1933 no majority cabinet was arithmetically available and every substitute had failed, and unconvincing beyond it, because 'unavoidable' and 'Hitler' are not the same word: Schleicher had held the office for eight weeks with no Nazi in it, and Hindenburg had refused Hitler the chancellorship as recently as August. Then name the deciding consideration: the crisis made a government over the Reichstag's head unavoidable; it did not choose which one, and the cabinet of 30 January, with three Nazis in it, was chosen by a handful of men who believed they were choosing safely.","Timing and SPaG: about 40 minutes in all — six to plan the paragraphs and fix the criterion, about thirty to write, and the last four to check spelling and punctuation and to make sure the specialist terms are present and correctly used, since 4 additional marks depend on 'a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate'. Useful terms here: Reichstag, Chancellor, Article 48, presidential decree, coalition, Nationalists, National Socialist, Communist, Gauleiter, SA, propaganda, Depression, reparations."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:18:14.075Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Re-judged from the kit file after the retargeting, and the duplication is genuinely gone: worked examples 3 and 4 now practise 3(b) and 3(c) on the importance of the SA, 1929–33, using a 50-word and a 51-word practice pair printed in full inside each setup and labelled as written for those examples, so room.questions[2] (3(b)) and [3] (3(c)) keep their own difference and reason — the elite-intrigue reading against mass strength, and the last-months study against the general history — and room.questions[12] (3(d) on popular support) is no longer framed for the student; the SA focus sets no bank question, since room.questions[10] uses SA violence only as a stimulus point in a 12-mark causation question with quite different material. Every one of the 25 load-bearing quotations checked matches the printed text word for word, including ‘the decisive instrument of the Nazi advance’, ‘less as a weapon than as a refuge’, ‘Recruitment, not terror’, Source B’s ‘they give him a bed, soup and boots’ and Source C’s ‘gave the young men something to belong to’, and the practice pair appears nowhere outside those two setups. The SA claims check out on the web: about 400,000 men by January 1932 and 445,000 by August 1932, with free food, lodging and the Sturmlokal drawing unemployed young men from 1929–30, so both practice interpretations are historically plausible as well as opposed. Everything verified in the previous pass still holds — all fourteen answer keys re-solved, every date and figure from 5 January 1919 to 30 January 1933 correct, provenance¹ used as the mark scheme defines it, no stem touching the Option 31 specimen — the three 3(d) stems now print the levels.md wording exactly, and the dedupe scan prints ‘no duplication’; publish it."}}},"31-3":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"31-3","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 3 is the part of Option 31 that Paper 3 sets questions on most readily, because it is full of dated turning points and because it is the ground on which historians disagree most sharply. Section A will hand you a source from the police state or from Goebbels's machine and ask what you can infer from it, then ask you to explain why something happened between 1933 and 1939. Section B will give you two historians arguing about why Germans obeyed: one saying they were frightened, the other that they largely agreed. If you can say, with dates and names, how a chancellor became a Führer in nineteen months, how the Gestapo, the SS and the camps actually worked, what Goebbels controlled and what he could not, and who refused to conform and why, you can answer almost anything this key topic sets. This room teaches that story as an argument you can take sides in, and it trains the five moves the paper rewards: inference from a source, causal explanation that goes past the printed bullet points, judgement of a source's usefulness for the enquiry actually named, spotting how two historians differ and why, and evaluating one of them against the other and against what you know.","idea":[{"code":"31-3 — 1 The creation of a dictatorship, 1933–34: The Reichstag Fire","h":"Fire, decree, election, Act: the weeks that made the dictatorship legal","t":"Hitler became Chancellor on 30 January 1933 leading a coalition in which Nazis held only three cabinet posts, and he had no Reichstag majority. The Reichstag burned on the night of 27 February 1933 and the Dutch communist Marinus van der Lubbe was caught at the scene. The next day Hindenburg signed the Decree for the Protection of People and State, which suspended freedom of speech, of the press and of assembly and allowed the police to hold people without charge; some four thousand communists were arrested within days. At the election of 5 March 1933 the Nazis took 43.9 per cent of the vote and 288 of the 647 seats, still short of a majority. The Enabling Act of 23 March 1933 then let the government make laws without the Reichstag or the President for four years. It passed by 444 votes to 94 in the Kroll Opera House, with the eighty-one Communist deputies excluded, the Catholic Centre Party bought by promises about the Churches, SA and SS men lining the corridors, and only the Social Democrats voting against. The trade unions were seized on 2 May 1933 and the Law against the Formation of New Parties of 14 July made the NSDAP the only legal party. A top-level answer never lists these as events: it shows each one removing the obstacle that made the next one possible, and it insists that this dictatorship was built with laws as much as with violence."},{"code":"31-3 — 1 The creation of a dictatorship, 1933–34: The threat from Röhm and the SA","h":"The Night of the Long Knives and the road to Führer","t":"By the summer of 1934 the SA under Ernst Röhm numbered close to three million men, against an army the Treaty of Versailles had limited to 100,000. Röhm wanted a 'second revolution' and the SA absorbed into the army with himself at its head; the generals refused, and Hitler needed them for rearmament and for the succession to the dying Hindenburg. Himmler, Reichsführer of the SS since 1929, and Heydrich, who ran the SD, fed Hitler evidence of a coup that Röhm was not in fact planning, and then supplied the killers. Röhm was arrested at Bad Wiessee on 30 June 1934 and shot on 1 July; Gregor Strasser, the former Chancellor Kurt von Schleicher and his wife, and Gustav Ritter von Kahr were among the dead. Hitler told the Reichstag on 13 July that seventy-seven had been killed; historians' estimates run higher. Hindenburg died on 2 August 1934, Hitler at once merged the offices of President and Chancellor as Führer and Reich Chancellor, and every soldier swore a personal oath of obedience to him by name; a plebiscite on 19 August returned about ninety per cent in favour. A top-level answer treats the purge as a transaction rather than a massacre: Hitler paid with the SA and was paid in the loyalty of the army and the acquiescence of the conservatives."},{"code":"31-3 — 2 The police state: The role of the Gestapo, the SS and concentration camps","h":"A small secret police and a very large reputation","t":"The SS began in 1925 as Hitler's bodyguard and grew under Himmler into the regime's instrument of terror; its intelligence branch, the SD, was set up in 1931 under Heydrich to spy on enemies inside and outside the party. Göring created the Gestapo out of the Prussian political police in April 1933; Himmler took it over in April 1934, and in June 1936 he was made Chief of German Police, so every force in Germany answered to the SS. The Gestapo could issue an order of 'protective custody' that sent a person to a concentration camp with no charge, no trial and no appeal. The first camp, Dachau, opened near Munich in March 1933 for communists, Social Democrats and trade unionists, and the SS Death's Head units ran a growing system: Sachsenhausen from 1936, Buchenwald from 1937, Mauthausen from 1938, Ravensbrück for women from 1939. The force itself, though, was small for a country of about sixty-five million, and most of its cases began with a denunciation from a neighbour, a workmate or a relative, with Party block wardens supplying names street by street. A top-level answer uses that smallness as an argument rather than a fact: the police state worked because ordinary Germans policed one another, and because a returned camp prisoner frightened a whole street without the Gestapo doing anything at all."},{"code":"31-3 — 2 The police state: Nazi control of the legal system","h":"Courts that could not acquit","t":"The Nazis did not abolish the German legal system; they colonised it. Jewish and politically unreliable judges were removed under the Law for the Restoration of the Professional Civil Service of April 1933, and the rest were pressed into the National Socialist League for the Maintenance of the Law and told that judgments should serve the people and the movement rather than the letter of an older code. When the Leipzig judges acquitted four of the five men tried over the Reichstag Fire, Hitler answered by creating the People's Court in April 1934 to handle treason: it sat in panels of five of whom only two need be qualified judges, the others being trusted Party, SS or army men, it often sat in secret, and there was no right of appeal. Special courts handled political offences in the regions. Meanwhile the Gestapo's power of protective custody meant that the courts no longer had a monopoly on punishment at all: a person acquitted in court could be taken from the courtroom to a camp. A top-level answer makes the point that this was terror wearing legal dress, and that the two halves worked together: the police removed people from the law's protection, and the courts convicted those left inside it."},{"code":"31-3 — 3 Controlling and influencing attitudes: Goebbels and the Ministry of Propaganda","h":"Goebbels: one voice, delivered into the kitchen","t":"The Reich Ministry of Public Enlightenment and Propaganda was created on 13 March 1933 with Joseph Goebbels at its head. Opposition newspapers had already been closed under the Reichstag Fire Decree and the Social Democrat and Communist presses were seized during 1933; editors who remained took daily instructions on what to print and how to print it. The People's Receiver, the Volksempfänger, went on sale in 1933 at about seventy-six marks and could be bought by instalments, with cheaper sets later: about a quarter of German households had a radio in 1933 and about seventy per cent by 1939, the highest proportion in the world. The sets were built for German stations and picked up foreign ones poorly, and loudspeaker pillars carried speeches to streets, factories and cafés. Spectacle did the rest: the Nuremberg rallies each September, with Speer's ranks of searchlights, and the Berlin Olympics of August 1936, staged in a new stadium holding about a hundred thousand, with the first Olympic torch relay, anti-Jewish signs taken down for the fortnight and Germany winning more medals than any other nation. A top-level answer separates reach from effect: Goebbels could guarantee that Germans heard the message, but the evidence of what they believed has to come from somewhere else."},{"code":"31-3 — 3 Controlling and influencing attitudes: Nazi control of culture and the arts","h":"The Reich Chamber of Culture: a licence to work","t":"Culture was controlled at the door of the profession. The Reich Chamber of Culture was set up on 22 September 1933 under Goebbels, with seven chambers covering the press, radio, film, theatre, music, literature and the fine arts; membership was the condition of working at all, and refusal or expulsion ended a career. Because of that, most publishers, editors, producers and gallery owners censored themselves before anyone in Berlin had to. On 10 May 1933 students burned books by Jewish, socialist and pacifist writers in Berlin and other university towns. Approved painting showed peasants, soldiers, mothers and heroic labour; modern work was ridiculed at the Degenerate Art exhibition in Munich in 1937, staged against the Great German Art Exhibition opened at the new House of German Art in the same city and the same summer. Architecture under Albert Speer went monumental and neo-classical for public buildings and traditional for housing. Every script had to be approved, newsreels ran before every feature film, and Leni Riefenstahl's films of the 1934 Nuremberg rally and the 1936 Olympics made propaganda look like art. A top-level answer shows control working through employment and ridicule rather than through banning alone, and notes what it cost: many of Germany's best artists, writers and film-makers simply left."},{"code":"31-3 — 3 Controlling and influencing attitudes: Nazi attempts to control the Churches","h":"The Concordat and the Reich Church: two bargains, both broken","t":"About two-thirds of Germans were Protestant and about a third Catholic, so the Churches were the one mass organisation the Nazis could not simply abolish. With the Catholics Hitler made a bargain: the Concordat signed with the Vatican on 20 July 1933 promised freedom of worship and the Church's schools and youth groups in return for the clergy staying out of politics, and it came a fortnight after the Centre Party dissolved itself. With the Protestants he tried absorption: the pro-Nazi German Christians pushed for a single Reich Church, and Ludwig Müller was elected Reich Bishop in September 1933, some of his supporters wanting the Old Testament dropped and Jewish converts excluded from church office. Both attempts went wrong. Catholic schools were closed and Catholic youth organisations were folded into the Hitler Youth in breach of the Concordat, and the Protestant church split rather than fused. The regime added a Ministry for Church Affairs in 1935 and harassed clergy through the courts. A top-level answer explains why the regime used bargains and salami tactics here instead of terror: the Churches had an authority the Nazis had not created, and attacking them openly threatened the very consent the regime was trying to build."},{"code":"31-3 — 4 Opposition, resistance and conformity: The extent of support for the Nazi regime","h":"Consent, conformity and grumbling: how far did Germans go along?","t":"Most Germans between 1933 and 1939 neither resisted nor worshipped; they conformed, and many of them approved. Registered unemployment fell from about six million in early 1933 to a few hundred thousand by 1939; the streets were quieter than in the last Weimar years; Versailles was defied step by step and the Rhineland reoccupied in March 1936. The plebiscite of 19 August 1934 returned about ninety per cent for Hitler, and later plebiscites were higher still, though all were held under intimidation and cannot be read as free votes. Strength Through Joy holidays, the Olympics and the rallies gave many people a sense of belonging and of restored standing. Against that, the reports smuggled abroad by the exiled Social Democrats recorded constant complaint about wages, hours and prices, usually blamed on the firm, the foreman or officials rather than on Hitler himself, and the regime's own Gestapo reports said the same. A top-level answer refuses the false choice between a terrorised nation and an enthusiastic one: it distinguishes approval of particular achievements from belief in the whole creed, and it notes that in a state where dissent was dangerous every public sign of support is evidence that has to be handled with care."},{"code":"31-3 — 4 Opposition, resistance and conformity: Opposition from the Churches","h":"Niemöller, the Confessing Church and Mit brennender Sorge","t":"Church opposition was mostly about the Churches' own freedom rather than about the regime as a whole, and it was the more effective for it. Martin Niemöller, a former U-boat commander who had welcomed Hitler in 1933, founded the Pastors' Emergency League in September 1933 when the German Christians demanded that clergy of Jewish descent be excluded; it grew into the Confessing Church, which declared at Barmen in May 1934 that the Church answered to Christ and not to the state, and drew several thousand pastors. Niemöller was arrested on 1 July 1937, tried in 1938 and then held in Sachsenhausen and later Dachau as Hitler's personal prisoner; Dietrich Bonhoeffer's training college was closed and he was banned from teaching and publishing. On the Catholic side Pope Pius XI's encyclical Mit brennender Sorge, dated 14 March 1937, was smuggled into Germany, printed secretly and read from pulpits on Palm Sunday; it accused the regime of breaking the Concordat, and the police seized the copies. A top-level answer weighs this opposition honestly: it kept the Churches alive, it forced the regime to go slowly, and it never threatened Nazi rule, because it defended a sphere rather than attacking a state."},{"code":"31-3 — 4 Opposition, resistance and conformity: Opposition from the young","h":"Pirates and swing kids: refusing to hand over your free time","t":"The Hitler Youth absorbed or banned rival youth movements in 1933, became the state youth organisation in 1936 and was made compulsory for boys and girls of the right age in March 1939. As it grew it became drill, marching and the same songs, and some of the young walked away. The Edelweiss Pirates were loose working-class gangs of roughly fourteen- to seventeen-year-olds in the industrial west, known as the Navajos in Cologne, the Roving Dudes in Essen and the Kittelbach Pirates around Düsseldorf, who wore their own badges and checked shirts, hiked and camped at weekends beyond the reach of the block warden, sang mocking versions of Hitler Youth songs and sometimes ambushed Hitler Youth patrols; their slogan was eternal war on the Hitler Youth. The Swing Youth were mainly middle-class teenagers in cities such as Hamburg and Berlin who played American and British dance records, grew their hair long, used English words and danced in ways the regime called un-German and racially degenerate. Neither group had a programme, and both were later persecuted far more harshly than before the war. A top-level answer makes the distinction the examiner wants: this was non-conformity rather than resistance, and it mattered because a regime that claimed the whole person could not tolerate a Saturday of its own."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1: two inferences from Source A, on a focus of its own","setup":"Source A in this room is the monthly report on the mood of the population sent by the head of a Gestapo district office in a Ruhr industrial town to his superiors, October 1936. It states that of the thirty-one cases opened in the district that month, twenty-four began with information brought by private persons — a neighbour, a workmate, in two cases a wife — and that his officers 'cannot be everywhere'; that the mood in the pits and the steelworks is 'not good', with complaint about the length of the shift and about prices, though the Führer's name is 'seldom attached to it' and blame falls on the firm, the foreman or 'those in Berlin'; that loose talk in the taverns is 'less common than a year ago'; that the metalworker K., released from Dachau in the spring, 'does his shift and speaks to no one', and that the effect of this on his workmates 'has been greater than any speech of mine'; and that Party block wardens 'continue to supply names and are, on the whole, reliable'. The question is: 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about the effect of imprisonment in a concentration camp. Complete the table below to explain your answer.' (4 marks)","steps":["Fix the writer before reading for content: an officer reporting upwards on how well his district is held will want to look competent, so whatever he treats as ordinary is worth most.","Take the first inference from what the released man now does rather than from what was done to him: he 'does his shift and speaks to no one', so a spell in a camp left a man afraid to say anything at all.","Take the second from the officer's own comparison: that silence 'has been greater than any speech of mine', so the camps disciplined people who had never been inside one, simply by being known about.","Fix the shape before writing: the claim, which the source implies but nowhere states, goes in the first box, and the words that force it go in the box for details."],"cues":["Who wrote this, and who was meant to read it?","What does the released prisoner now do at work?","Who else does the officer say is affected?","Which box takes the claim and which the quotation?"]},{"title":"Question 3(b): the difference of view, not of detail","setup":"Interpretation 1 in this room, from a study of the Nazi police state by a historian writing in the 1980s, argues that obedience 'was bought with fear': within eighteen months of January 1933 the parties, the unions and the free press had gone, and with them every organisation through which discontent could become opposition, leaving a citizen alone before the Gestapo, the People's Court and the camps, so that 'a joke repeated in the wrong tavern could end in Dachau' and 'applause at the rallies proves very little'. Interpretation 2, from a general history of German society under the Nazis by a historian writing in the 2000s, argues that most Germans 'needed very little compelling', because work came back and the streets were quiet, because the Olympic summer and the Nuremberg rallies gave millions pride, and because the secret police were 'too few to watch a nation of sixty-five million' and their cases 'usually began with a denunciation from a neighbour or a workmate', so that this was 'a regime whose people, for their own reasons, have decided to help it'. The question is: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. They give different views on how the Nazi police state worked. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations.' (4 marks)","steps":["Reduce each text to one sentence about the machinery: the first has the state watching the citizen, the second has the citizens informing on one another.","Test that this is a difference of view and not of detail: both accept that there was a Gestapo and that people were arrested, so the disagreement is about where the initiative lay.","Anchor each half in its own wording, the citizen left alone before 'the Gestapo, the People's Court and the camps' against police 'too few to watch a nation of sixty-five million' whose cases began with 'a denunciation from a neighbour or a workmate'.","Write it as one difference stated in the first sentence and then supported from both, never as two summaries laid side by side."],"cues":["What does each say the police state actually consisted of?","Do they disagree about the facts or about who was driving?","Which phrase in each carries its view?","What shape should the finished answer take?"]},{"title":"Question 3(c): one reason for the difference, substantiated","setup":"The same two interpretations are printed. Interpretation 1 comes from a study of the Nazi police state, by a historian writing in the 1980s; it dates its own argument, saying that 'within eighteen months of January 1933' the parties, the unions and the free press had gone, so that what remained was a citizen alone before the Gestapo, the People's Court and the camps. Interpretation 2 comes from a general history of German society under the Nazis, by a historian writing in the 2000s; its examples are dated later — work that 'came back', quiet streets, and the Olympic summer and the Nuremberg rallies that 'gave millions a pride they had not felt since 1918'. Source B in this room is a report on a Saxon factory town smuggled out to the exiled Social Democrat leadership in 1937, and Source C is a circular from a Nazi Party district propaganda office to its block wardens in 1938. The question is: 'Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about the part played by fear in Nazi Germany in the years 1933–39. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer.' (4 marks)","steps":["Choose a reason that lets both historians be right about something: here, that each generalises from a different stretch of the same six years, so the difference is one of emphasis rather than of fact.","Show it in Interpretation 1's own wording, which dates itself to the opening phase — 'within eighteen months of January 1933' — so the fear it describes is the fear of the months in which the machinery was being built and every alternative destroyed.","Show it in Interpretation 2's wording, whose examples — work returning, the Olympic summer, Nuremberg — all belong to the middle and later 1930s; Source B, written in 1937, is that later Germany seen from below, where 'two of ours were taken in March and have not come back' stands in the same report as old trade unionists conceding that the foreigners 'came and saw order'.","Close on where the emphasis shows its limits: Source C, the block-warden circular printed alongside it, has the machinery still running at the very end of the period, so neither historian is describing a Germany the other would not recognise — each has weighted the same six years differently, and a claim sound for 1933 becomes contestable once it is stated of 1939."],"cues":["Which years is each interpretation really describing?","Which phrase in Interpretation 1 dates its evidence?","Does Source B fit one interpretation or both?","What has to be true for two contradictory views to be equally fair?"]},{"title":"Question 2: taking a 12-mark 'Explain why' past the printed bullets","setup":"The question is: 'Explain why the Berlin Olympics of 1936 were so important to the Nazi regime. You may use the following in your answer: • foreign visitors and journalists • Nazi racial ideas. You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks); on the paper Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b) and you answer one of them. The mark scheme caps an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content at a maximum of eight marks and shuts Level 4 to it. The fact files in this room supply the material: the Games ran in August 1936 in a new stadium holding about a hundred thousand; the first Olympic torch relay was run from Greece to Berlin; anti-Jewish signs were taken down and the Nazi paper Der Stürmer was kept off the news-stands for the fortnight; one Jewish-born athlete was recalled to the German team; Germany won more medals than any other nation, while the African-American Jesse Owens won four golds; Leni Riefenstahl filmed the Games; the Rhineland had been reoccupied in March of the same year.","steps":["Turn the first bullet into a causal claim rather than a heading: the regime wanted a foreign verdict it could quote at home, so the fortnight was staged for reporters who would go away and describe an orderly, modern, peaceable Germany.","Make the second bullet do causal work as well: a festival built to display the strength of a supposedly superior race was worth the expense because it turned an idea taught in schools into something spectators could watch.","Add the reason that neither bullet prompts, the home audience: after the reoccupation of the Rhineland earlier that year the Games told Germans that the humiliation of Versailles was over and that the world had come to them.","Handle the awkward facts inside the argument instead of avoiding them, since concealment is itself evidence of how much the regime had staked on the fortnight, and a sprinter who won repeatedly was not the demonstration the hosts had planned.","Order the three so they build — foreign opinion, racial doctrine, domestic pride — and close on which of them the regime would have been least willing to lose."],"cues":["Who was meant to carry the impression away?","What did a stadium full of winners prove, in Nazi terms?","Which audience is not mentioned in either bullet?","Why take the signs down at all, if the doctrine was the point?","What must the final sentence do?"]},{"title":"Question 3(a): judging Sources B and C for the enquiry actually named","setup":"Source B in this room is a report on a factory town in Saxony, written by an underground contact of the exiled Social Democrat leadership and smuggled out to them in 1937: the remaining comrades 'meet in threes and never in the same house twice'; 'it would be wrong to tell you that the workers here are ready'; they grumble at the rate for the job and the price of fat and then 'shout with the rest' when the wireless goes on in the canteen; 'the works foreman is a block warden and writes down what he hears, so a man thinks twice before he finishes a sentence'; since the Olympic Games even old trade unionists say the foreigners saw order; two comrades 'were taken in March and have not come back'; leaflets are read 'in the lavatory and then torn up'. Source C is a circular sent to Party block wardens by the propaganda office of a Nazi Party district in 1938: each warden is to see that a receiver is available in his block and that households without one are invited to listen with a neighbour, to note which households are represented when the Führer speaks, to 'report absences without exception', to report anyone known to turn the dial to foreign stations, and to accept no excuses of cost; it ends that 'the warden who knows his forty households is worth more to the movement than a hundred posters'. The question is: 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the part played by local Nazi Party officials in controlling German life in the years 1933–39? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context.' (8 marks)","steps":["Copy the enquiry to the top of the plan and let it select: the block warden is the subject, so the foreman who writes things down is in, and the arrests of March are background rather than evidence.","Take Source C's content for the enquiry: a warden who must know his households, place a set, record attendance and name listeners to foreign stations is a policeman, a salesman and a census-taker at once.","Weigh Source C's nature, origin and purpose together: an internal order to its own officials has no reason to flatter, so it is excellent evidence of what the Party expected, though it shows the intention rather than the result.","Take Source B for the other half of the enquiry, what the warden meant to those below him: the man who watches you is also your foreman, so his power over wages and shifts and his power over your words are the same power.","Bring your own knowledge in on the sources: the Gestapo was small for a country of about sixty-five million and most of its cases opened with a denunciation, which is exactly the traffic these wardens generated.","Judge for this enquiry rather than in general, saying what the pair covers and where it runs out, since neither is written by a warden himself."],"cues":["What exactly is the enquiry into?","What jobs is the warden actually given?","Does an internal order gain or lose usefulness by being secret?","What does Source B add that an order cannot show?","Which fact of your own explains why such men mattered?","What is missing from both?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What did the Reichstag Fire Decree of 28 February 1933 actually allow the government to do?","a":"It suspended the constitutional guarantees of freedom of speech, of the press and of assembly and allowed the police to arrest and hold people without charge, which is how about four thousand communists were taken within days and how the Communist deputies were kept out of the new Reichstag."},{"q":"Why did Hitler need the Catholic Centre Party in March 1933, and what did he offer it?","a":"Because the Enabling Act changed the constitution and needed a two-thirds majority, which the Nazis and their Nationalist allies did not have after winning 43.9 per cent and 288 seats. He promised that the rights of the Churches and their schools would be respected; the Act passed by 444 votes to 94."},{"q":"Why is the small size of the Gestapo an argument about the police state rather than just a fact about it?","a":"Because a force too small to watch about sixty-five million people could only work if ordinary Germans supplied the information, so terror depended on denunciation by neighbours, workmates and relatives, and on Party block wardens, rather than on universal surveillance."},{"q":"Name one thing the Confessing Church achieved and one thing it did not.","a":"It kept a Protestant church independent of the Nazi-backed Reich Church, declaring at Barmen in May 1934 that the Church answered to Christ and not the state, and it drew several thousand pastors. It did not threaten the regime: it defended the Church's own sphere, and Niemöller was arrested on 1 July 1937 without any wider protest following."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 3 Section A (16 marks): Question 1 prints Source A and asks 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about...', answered in a table with a box for the inference and a box for the details (4 marks); Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b) and you answer one of them, each 'Explain why...' with two stimulus points and 'You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks).","Paper 3 Section B (36 marks), all of it Question 3: (a) 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into...?' (8); (b) 'What is the main difference between these views?' (4); (c) 'Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about...' (4); (d) 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about...?' (16, plus up to 4 for spelling, punctuation, grammar and the use of specialist terminology).","The caps printed on the 12-mark 'Explain why...': 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'. There is no cap at Level 2. Question 3(d) prints no stimulus points, so no such cap applies to it.","Timing on Paper 3: 1 hour 30 minutes for 52 marks, so roughly a minute a mark plus thinking time — about 5 minutes on Question 1, 18 on Question 2, 12 on 3(a), 6 each on 3(b) and 3(c), and about 40 minutes on 3(d) including the last check for the SPaG marks.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose, and usefulness is always judged for the enquiry the question names, never in general. Contextual knowledge earns nothing at all unless it is used on the sources or on the interpretations.","The dates that anchor this key topic: 27 and 28 February 1933, the Reichstag Fire and the decree that followed; 23 March 1933, the Enabling Act; 30 June 1934, the Night of the Long Knives; 2 August 1934, Hindenburg's death, the title of Führer and the army's oath."],"questions":[{"q":"1 Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about how the Gestapo learned what people in the district were saying. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for each valid inference up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail taken from Source A. Credit only what the report implies rather than states: that the information ran through the people a man spoke to most freely, that the block wardens supplied names as a settled routine, or that the officer had to sift what he was brought. Repeating his own admission that his officers cannot be everywhere is paraphrase, not inference.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: the Gestapo learned what was said because the people a man spoke to most freely were the ones who reported him. Details in the source that tell me this: the private persons behind the twenty-four cases were 'a neighbour, a workmate, in two cases a wife', and a wife is listed without comment, as though denunciation inside a marriage were unremarkable. (ii) What I can infer: the office also had a standing supply of names that it had to sift. Details in the source that tell me this: the wardens 'continue to supply names', an arrangement already routine, and calling them 'on the whole, reliable' implies that some of what they brought was not.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["An inference is something the source makes you sure of without stating it; ask what must be true for an officer to describe his month's work in this way.","Read the report twice, once for where his cases come from and once for who else in the town is doing part of his job.","Give each answer in two parts: a claim the source never makes, then the exact words that force it."],"codes":["31-3 — 2 The police state: The role of the Gestapo, the SS and concentration camps","31-3 — 4 Opposition, resistance and conformity: The extent of support for the Nazi regime"]},{"q":"1 Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about the attitudes of workers in the district towards the Nazi regime in 1936. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark per valid inference up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail from Source A. Credit discontent that spares Hitler himself, or complaint driven underground rather than removed.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: workers were discontented with their conditions but did not hold Hitler responsible for them. Details in the source that tell me this: there is complaint about the length of the shift and about prices, yet the officer writes that the Führer's name is 'seldom attached to it' and that blame falls on the firm, the foreman or 'those in Berlin'. (ii) What I can infer: that discontent was being driven out of sight rather than removed. Details in the source that tell me this: the mood in the pits and steelworks is still 'not good', but loose talk in the taverns is 'less common than a year ago'.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Separate what the workers are said to feel from where they are said to put the blame.","Compare the two things the officer says about talk: how much there is now, and how much there was a year earlier.","Set out a claim the source implies and then the phrase that makes it certain, for each of your two answers."],"codes":["31-3 — 4 Opposition, resistance and conformity: The extent of support for the Nazi regime","31-3 — 2 The police state: The role of the Gestapo, the SS and concentration camps"]},{"q":"3(b) Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. They give different views on why most Germans accepted Nazi rule in the years 1933–39. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4) needs a key difference of view supported from both: Interpretation 1 makes fear the cause of obedience, Interpretation 2 makes genuine consent the cause. Differences of surface detail only stay in Level 1.","sol":"The main difference is over whether Germans obeyed because they were frightened or because they agreed. Interpretation 1 says that obedience 'was bought with fear': once the parties, the unions and the free press had gone, the citizen stood alone before the Gestapo, the People's Court and the camps, 'a joke repeated in the wrong tavern could end in Dachau', and so applause at the rallies 'proves very little'. Interpretation 2 says most Germans 'needed very little compelling', because work came back and the streets were quiet, the Olympic summer and the Nuremberg rallies gave millions pride, and the secret police were 'too few to watch a nation of sixty-five million'.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Reduce each interpretation to a single sentence beginning 'Germans obeyed because...' and set the two sentences against each other.","Check that your difference is one of view: both accept that there were camps and that there were rallies, so look for what each says did the work.","State the difference in your first sentence, then support each half with wording taken from its own interpretation."],"codes":["31-3 — 4 Opposition, resistance and conformity: The extent of support for the Nazi regime","31-3 — 2 The police state: The role of the Gestapo, the SS and concentration camps"]},{"q":"3(c) Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about why most Germans accepted Nazi rule in the years 1933–39. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4) needs one explained reason, effectively substantiated: for example that the two historians study different subjects and therefore weigh different bodies of evidence.","sol":"One reason is that the two historians set out to study different things and so weigh different evidence. Interpretation 1 comes from a study of the Nazi police state, so its evidence is what the regime built — the Gestapo, the People's Court, the camps — the machinery Source C shows in operation when block wardens are ordered to report without exception the households that fail to listen to the Führer. Interpretation 2 comes from a general history of German society, so its evidence is what ordinary people actually did, of the sort found in Source B, where men who grumble at the rate for the job 'shout with the rest' as soon as the wireless is switched on.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Historians can differ legitimately when they ask different questions of the same period; look at what kind of book each extract comes from.","Ask which documents each writer would have spent years reading, and what those documents would make prominent.","Name your one reason first, then show it at work in both interpretations, using a source to illustrate the kind of evidence involved."],"codes":["31-3 — 4 Opposition, resistance and conformity: The extent of support for the Nazi regime","31-3 — 3 Controlling and influencing attitudes: Goebbels and the Ministry of Propaganda"]},{"q":"2(a) Explain why the Enabling Act was passed in March 1933. You may use the following in your answer: • the Reichstag Fire Decree • the Centre Party. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): analytical causation with precise detail — the decree of 28 February and the arrests, the exclusion of the Communist deputies, the arithmetic after 5 March, the bargain with the Centre, intimidation at the Kroll Opera House. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The Enabling Act passed because Hitler removed his most dangerous opponents from the chamber, bought or frightened those who were left, and dressed the whole operation in legality. The first reason is the Reichstag Fire Decree. The Reichstag burned on the night of 27 February 1933 and the Dutch communist Marinus van der Lubbe was caught at the scene; the next day Hindenburg signed the Decree for the Protection of People and State, which suspended freedom of speech, of the press and of assembly and allowed the police to hold people without charge. Some four thousand communists were arrested within days, and when the new Reichstag met the eighty-one Communist deputies elected on 5 March could not take their seats. A two-thirds majority is very much easier to reach once a whole party has been taken out of the count. The second reason is the Centre Party. At the election of 5 March 1933 the Nazis won 43.9 per cent of the vote and 288 seats, short of a majority even with their Nationalist allies, so Hitler needed the Catholic Centre. He promised in the chamber that the rights of the Churches and their schools would be respected and that agreement with Rome would follow; the Centre voted for the Act and it passed by 444 votes to 94, with only the Social Democrats against. The third reason, which neither bullet point prompts, is the atmosphere in which the vote was taken. Because the Reichstag was a ruin the sitting was held in the Kroll Opera House, whose corridors and galleries were lined with armed SA and SS men chanting for the Act, and the Social Democrats who had not been arrested had to walk through them to vote no. Each reason fed the next: the decree supplied the arrests, the arrests supplied the arithmetic, and the promises and the uniforms supplied the rest, so that a measure which destroyed the constitution could be presented as having been passed under it.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["The Act needed a two-thirds majority, so ask first what happened to the votes that would have been cast against it.","Sort your material by kind of reason rather than by date: reasons that work in different ways will analyse, where a run of events in order will only narrate.","Open on what the decree of 28 February allowed the police to do, and finish that paragraph on what it did to the arithmetic of the chamber."],"codes":["31-3 — 1 The creation of a dictatorship, 1933–34: The Reichstag Fire"]},{"q":"2(b) Explain why Hitler moved against Ernst Röhm and the SA in June 1934. You may use the following in your answer: • the German army • Heinrich Himmler and the SS. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): sustained causal analysis — the SA's size against the 100,000-man army, Röhm's demand for a second revolution, the SS and SD supplying evidence and gunmen, other scores settled, the payment received on 2 August. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Hitler struck at Röhm because the SA had become the one force inside his own movement that could still challenge him, and because destroying it bought him the thing he needed next. The first reason is the army. The Treaty of Versailles limited Germany's armed forces to 100,000 men, while by the summer of 1934 the SA numbered close to three million. Röhm talked of a second revolution and wanted the SA absorbed into the army with himself at its head; the generals, who despised the brownshirts as a street rabble, made it plain that they would never accept it. Hitler needed the officer corps for rearmament and for the succession to the visibly dying Hindenburg, and he could not keep both them and Röhm. The second reason is Himmler and the SS. The SS had been founded in 1925 as Hitler's bodyguard and was still formally under the SA; Himmler, its Reichsführer since 1929, and Heydrich, who ran the SD, wanted independence, and they fed Hitler and Göring evidence of a coup that Röhm was not in fact planning. They then supplied the gunmen. Röhm was arrested at Bad Wiessee on 30 June 1934 and shot on the first of July, and the SS was made independent of the SA within weeks. The third reason, which the bullets do not prompt, is that the purge let Hitler settle other scores and appear as the nation's protector. Among the dead were Gregor Strasser, who had led the party's radical wing, the former Chancellor Kurt von Schleicher and his wife, and Gustav Ritter von Kahr, who had helped break the Munich Putsch of 1923. Hitler told the Reichstag on 13 July that seventy-seven had been killed, and the cabinet declared the killings lawful acts of state defence. So the attack was not merely defensive: it removed a rival, satisfied the generals and turned murder into law, which is why the army swore its personal oath to Hitler a month later.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what Hitler needed in the summer of 1934 that only the generals could give him, and what stood in the way of his getting it.","Give the SS a paragraph about what it wanted and what it did, not about how evil it was.","Begin with the two sets of numbers, the size of the SA and the size of the army Versailles allowed."],"codes":["31-3 — 1 The creation of a dictatorship, 1933–34: The threat from Röhm and the SA"]},{"q":"2(a) Explain why the German police and courts came to serve the Nazi regime in the years 1933–39. You may use the following in your answer: • the Gestapo • the People's Court. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise knowledge — Göring and Himmler and the Gestapo, protective custody, the People's Court of April 1934 and its panels, the purge of the judges in 1933 and the lawyers' league. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The police and the courts were turned into instruments of the regime because the Nazis took over the machinery instead of replacing it, then added new bodies answerable only to themselves, and finally changed what the law was understood to be for. The first reason is the growth of a political police outside ordinary control. Göring built the Gestapo out of the Prussian political police in April 1933; Himmler took it over in April 1934, and in June 1936 he was made Chief of German Police, so that every force in the country came under the SS. The Gestapo could issue an order of protective custody that sent a person to a concentration camp with no charge, no trial and no appeal. Once the police could imprison without the courts, the courts had lost their monopoly on punishment, and a defendant acquitted in a courtroom could be taken from it to Dachau. The second reason is the creation of courts designed to convict. The People's Court was set up in April 1934, after the judges at Leipzig had acquitted four of the five men tried over the Reichstag Fire. It handled treason in panels of five, of whom only two had to be qualified judges while the rest were trusted Party, SS or army men; it often sat in secret, and there was no right of appeal. Special courts dealt with political offences in the regions. The third reason, which neither bullet prompts, is what happened to the judges themselves. Jewish and politically unreliable judges were removed under the Law for the Restoration of the Professional Civil Service of April 1933, and those who remained were pressed into the National Socialist League for the Maintenance of the Law and told that a judgment should serve the people and the movement rather than the letter of an older code. Terror and law were therefore not opposites in Nazi Germany. The Gestapo took people outside the courts, the new courts convicted those who were left inside them, and the judges had been taught which way to lean.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what a court is for, and then check which of those functions the regime had taken away from it by 1936.","Treat the two bullets as two halves of one process, police outside the law and courts inside it, and find a third change of a different kind.","Open with who controlled the political police in 1933 and who controlled it three years later."],"codes":["31-3 — 2 The police state: Nazi control of the legal system","31-3 — 2 The police state: The role of the Gestapo, the SS and concentration camps"]},{"q":"2(b) Explain why Goebbels was able to control what most Germans read, saw and heard in the years 1933–39. You may use the following in your answer: • the Reich Chamber of Culture • the People's Receiver (the Volksempfänger). You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): analysis directed at causation — the ministry of March 1933, the seven chambers and self-censorship, the cheap radio and the ownership figures, the destruction of rival newspapers, press instructions and newsreels. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Goebbels controlled German opinion because he controlled the door into every artistic and journalistic profession, because he put a cheap receiver into most German homes, and because the regime had already destroyed the alternatives before he began. The first reason is the Reich Chamber of Culture, set up on 22 September 1933 under the ministry that had been created for Goebbels on 13 March of the same year. Its seven chambers covered the press, radio, film, theatre, music, literature and the fine arts, and nobody could work in any of them without membership. Expulsion was therefore not the censoring of one book or one picture but the ending of a career, so most editors, publishers and film companies censored themselves before anyone in Berlin had to intervene. The second reason is the People's Receiver. The first model went on sale in 1933 at about seventy-six marks and could be bought by instalments, and cheaper sets followed. About a quarter of German households had a radio in 1933 and about seventy per cent by 1939, the highest proportion of any country in the world; the sets were built for German stations and picked up foreign ones poorly, and loudspeaker pillars carried speeches to streets, factories and cafés for those who were not at home. The third reason, which the bullets do not prompt, is that the competition had already been removed. Opposition newspapers were closed under the Reichstag Fire Decree, the Social Democrat and Communist presses were seized during 1933, and on 10 May 1933 students burned books by Jewish, socialist and pacifist writers in Berlin and other university towns. What remained was a single supply of news, which Goebbels shaped further through daily instructions to editors and through newsreels shown before every feature film. Control was therefore built in layers: no rival voices left, a profession that policed itself for fear of losing its licence, and a machine for delivering one voice into the kitchen.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Test each bullet on its own before you plan: ask what the Chamber of Culture could not have achieved by itself, and what a cheap receiver could not have achieved by itself.","Ask what membership of a chamber was worth to an editor, and what losing it would have cost him.","Open on the date the ministry was created and on what Goebbels was given power over."],"codes":["31-3 — 3 Controlling and influencing attitudes: Goebbels and the Ministry of Propaganda","31-3 — 3 Controlling and influencing attitudes: Nazi control of culture and the arts"]},{"q":"2(a) Explain why the Nazi regime found it so difficult to bring the Christian Churches under its control in the years 1933–39. You may use the following in your answer: • the Concordat of July 1933 • the Confessing Church. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise detail — the Concordat of 20 July 1933 and its breaches, Mit brennender Sorge, the Reich Church and the split it caused, Barmen 1934, the numbers of believers and the limits on terror. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The Churches were hard to control because they possessed an authority the Nazis had not created, because the regime's own agreements gave them ground to stand on, and because open persecution risked the very consent the regime wanted. The first reason is the Concordat, signed with the Vatican on 20 July 1933, a fortnight after the Centre Party had dissolved itself. Hitler gained a great deal, since Catholic clergy undertook to stay out of politics and a foreign power had recognised his government; but in return the Church was promised freedom of worship and the right to keep its schools and its youth organisations. When the regime broke those terms by closing Catholic schools and folding Catholic youth groups into the Hitler Youth, Catholics could protest as the defenders of an agreement the government itself had signed, and Pope Pius XI's encyclical Mit brennender Sorge, dated 14 March 1937, was smuggled in, printed secretly and read from pulpits on Palm Sunday saying exactly that. The second reason is the Confessing Church. The attempt to gather the Protestant churches into one Reich Church run by the pro-Nazi German Christians, with Ludwig Müller elected Reich Bishop in September 1933, split the Protestants instead of uniting them. Martin Niemöller's Pastors' Emergency League of September 1933 grew into the Confessing Church, which declared at Barmen in May 1934 that the Church answered to Christ and not to the state, and it drew several thousand pastors. The third reason, which the bullets do not prompt, is that terror was a blunt instrument here. About two-thirds of Germans were Protestant and about a third Catholic, and the party's own programme had spoken of positive Christianity, so arresting popular clergy made martyrs and provoked protest. Persecution was therefore piecemeal: Niemöller was arrested on 1 July 1937 and held in a camp as Hitler's personal prisoner, but no church was closed. Faith, law and public opinion together made a frontal attack too expensive, which is why the regime kept trying to swallow the Churches instead.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the Churches had that no other organisation in Germany still had after July 1933.","Treat the Concordat as a bargain with two sides, and ask what the regime handed over in it.","Open on what Hitler wanted from the Vatican in the summer of 1933 and what he had to concede to get it."],"codes":["31-3 — 3 Controlling and influencing attitudes: Nazi attempts to control the Churches","31-3 — 4 Opposition, resistance and conformity: Opposition from the Churches"]},{"q":"2(b) Explain why some young Germans refused to conform to the Nazi regime in the years 1933–39. You may use the following in your answer: • the Hitler Youth • swing music. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed — the Hitler Youth becoming compulsory and dull, swing as a forbidden taste, the Edelweiss Pirates and the ground they held, and the distinction between non-conformity and resistance. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Some young Germans broke away because the regime's own youth movement turned from an adventure into a duty, because foreign music and clothes offered an identity the state could not supply, and because the places the young met were far harder to police than a factory or a barracks. The first reason is what the Hitler Youth became. In 1933 it absorbed or banned the other youth movements, in 1936 it was made the state youth organisation, and in March 1939 membership was made compulsory for boys and girls of the right age. As it grew it became routine: drill, marching, the same songs, leaders barely older than the led, and homework, hobbies and family time squeezed out. Compulsion and boredom produced exactly the rebels the movement existed to prevent. The second reason is swing. Mainly middle-class teenagers in cities such as Hamburg and Berlin listened to American and British dance bands on gramophones and on foreign stations, grew their hair long, used English words and danced in ways the regime called un-German and racially degenerate, since much of the music came from black American musicians. The Nazis had made taste a political matter, so choosing that music was itself a refusal, and the regime treated it as one. The third reason, which the bullets do not prompt, is that working-class youngsters in the industrial west had their own gangs and their own ground. From the later 1930s groups known collectively as the Edelweiss Pirates, the Navajos in Cologne, the Roving Dudes in Essen and the Kittelbach Pirates around Düsseldorf, hiked and camped at weekends where no block warden went, wore their own badges, sang mocking versions of Hitler Youth songs and sometimes ambushed Hitler Youth patrols; their slogan was eternal war on the Hitler Youth. None of this was organised political opposition, and none of it threatened the regime. It was the refusal of young people to hand over their free time, which mattered because a regime claiming the whole person could not allow anyone a Saturday of his own.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the Hitler Youth was actually like to belong to by the late 1930s, rather than what it was designed to achieve.","A refusal needs somewhere to happen; look for the times and places the regime could not reach.","Begin with the three steps by which belonging to the Hitler Youth stopped being a choice."],"codes":["31-3 — 4 Opposition, resistance and conformity: Opposition from the young"]},{"q":"3(a) Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into how the Nazi regime kept control of opposition in Germany in the years 1933–39? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): utility judged for this enquiry with developed reasoning on how nature, origin and purpose affect the content, context used to interpret. Credit Source B on underground caution and arrests, Source C on surveillance through block wardens; qualify both.","sol":"Source B is useful because it is written from inside the opposition this enquiry is about. It reports that the remaining comrades 'meet in threes and never in the same house twice', that the works foreman is a block warden who 'writes down what he hears, so a man thinks twice before he finishes a sentence', and that two comrades 'were taken in March and have not come back'. That is the police state working as it did: not by watching everybody, but by making everybody careful. Its provenance strengthens it: a report smuggled out to the exiled Social Democrat leadership had to be honest to be of use, and it admits the unwelcome fact that it would be wrong to tell the leadership that the workers are ready; even so it is one town seen by one man. Source C is useful in a different way, because it shows the machinery from above. A district propaganda office tells block wardens to note which households are represented when the Führer speaks, to 'report absences without exception', and to name anyone who turns the dial to foreign stations. As an internal order to its own officials it had no reason to exaggerate, and it confirms that surveillance ran through the Party's street officials as much as through the Gestapo, which was far too small to watch about sixty-five million people alone. Together they are strong, since C shows the system intended and B shows it felt; neither reaches the camps or the courts, where the hardest control lay.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Usefulness is always usefulness for something: copy the enquiry out before you look at either source.","Take each source as content, then nature, origin and purpose, then the context that confirms or qualifies it, and only then compare them.","Start with what Source B lets you infer about how the underground had to behave, and make your first sentence a judgement rather than a description."],"codes":["31-3 — 2 The police state: The role of the Gestapo, the SS and concentration camps","31-3 — 4 Opposition, resistance and conformity: The extent of support for the Nazi regime"]},{"q":"3(a) Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the effect of Nazi propaganda on ordinary Germans in the years 1933–39? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): criteria-based judgements with developed reasoning on provenance, context used in interpreting. Credit Source B as hostile testimony to propaganda's effect, Source C as evidence of method and of the reach of the radio; qualify each.","sol":"Source B is the more useful of the two, because it records an effect rather than an intention. The writer says that men who grumble at 'the rate for the job and at the price of fat' then hear the wireless switched on in the canteen and 'shout with the rest', and that since the Olympic Games 'even old trade unionists' repeat that the foreigners came and saw order and that nobody who wants work is on the street. Coming from an underground contact of the exiled Social Democrats, this is testimony against the writer's own side, which is why it carries weight; but it is one Saxon town, and he cannot tell us whether the shouting was belief or prudence. Source C is useful for what the regime was trying to do and for how far it could reach. The circular assumes a receiver in every block, orders communal listening where sets are short, and insists that the People's Receiver 'is now within the means of every household'. That fits what we know, since about a quarter of German homes had a radio in 1933 and about seventy per cent by 1939. As an order to officials it is good evidence of method, but it says nothing about what listeners thought, and the very need to record absences hints that some did not come. Together they supply intention and reception, which is what this enquiry needs; neither can measure how deep the effect went.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Separate evidence of what the regime tried to do from evidence of what it achieved, and ask which source gives which.","A source written by an opponent that reports success for the other side is worth more, not less; explain why in terms of purpose.","Begin by deciding which of the two sits closer to the enquiry, and open with that judgement and the reason for it."],"codes":["31-3 — 3 Controlling and influencing attitudes: Goebbels and the Ministry of Propaganda","31-3 — 4 Opposition, resistance and conformity: The extent of support for the Nazi regime"]},{"q":"3(d) Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the importance of terror in keeping Germans obedient in the years 1933–39? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views evaluated, how each conveys its view analysed, precise context, a justified judgement. For agreement credit the small Gestapo, denunciation and real achievements; against, the decree of February 1933, the camps, protective custody and the People's Court.","sol":"Interpretation 2 plays terror down. It says most Germans 'needed very little compelling', that the secret police were 'too few to watch a nation of sixty-five million', and that a regime which has to be told where its enemies are 'is not an all-seeing one'. Interpretation 1 puts terror at the centre: obedience 'was bought with fear', and 'a joke repeated in the wrong tavern could end in Dachau'. The test must be what terror actually had to do, and for whom. Interpretation 2 is right about the size of the machine. The Gestapo was small for a country of that population, and its files show that most of its cases began with a denunciation from a neighbour, a workmate or a relative rather than with detective work of its own. Source A, a Gestapo district head writing in 1936, admits exactly this when he records that twenty-four of his thirty-one cases that month began with information from private persons and that his officers 'cannot be everywhere'. Nor is it wrong that there was much to consent to. Registered unemployment fell from about six million in early 1933 to a few hundred thousand by 1939; the Nuremberg rallies and the Berlin Olympics of August 1936 gave many Germans a sense of restored standing; and the plebiscite of 19 August 1934 returned about ninety per cent for Hitler, even allowing for the intimidation under which it was held. But Interpretation 2 measures terror by how many people it touched, which is the wrong measure. Terror had already done its decisive work before the mid-1930s. The Reichstag Fire Decree of 28 February 1933 suspended civil liberties and some four thousand communists were arrested within days; the trade unions were seized on 2 May 1933 and every other party was gone by July; Dachau opened in March 1933 and the SS ran a widening system of camps, Sachsenhausen from 1936 and Buchenwald from 1937. The Gestapo could impose protective custody with no charge and no appeal, and the People's Court, created in April 1934, tried treason with no appeal either. Once every organisation through which discontent could have become opposition had been destroyed, very few people needed arresting, and that is exactly what Interpretation 1 means. The two also convey their views by different means, and this is part of the argument. Interpretation 2 reasons from numbers and treats the state's dependence on informers as a weakness; Interpretation 1 reasons from an example, the returned prisoner who is 'warning enough for a whole street', and treats that same dependence as proof that fear had spread into ordinary life. Source B supports Interpretation 1 on this point, since the workers who grumble about pay fall silent because the foreman is a block warden who 'writes down what he hears'. I therefore agree with Interpretation 2 that most Germans in the later 1930s were not held down by force, and that genuine approval was widespread. I do not agree that terror was therefore unimportant. Terror removed the alternatives in 1933 and 1934, and afterwards it worked by reputation rather than by arrest, which made it cheap to run and easy to underestimate. Consent and fear were not rivals in Nazi Germany: the fear made consent safe to express and made withholding it dangerous.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide what Interpretation 2 is actually claiming about terror, and name the test you will judge the claim by.","Ask when terror had to do its heaviest work, and whether counting arrests in 1936 measures that work at all.","Open by putting Interpretation 2's claim in your own words and stating the criterion your judgement will use."],"codes":["31-3 — 2 The police state: The role of the Gestapo, the SS and concentration camps","31-3 — 4 Opposition, resistance and conformity: The extent of support for the Nazi regime","31-3 — 1 The creation of a dictatorship, 1933–34: The Reichstag Fire"]},{"q":"3(d) Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the effectiveness of Nazi propaganda in the years 1933–39? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views weighed, how each conveys its view analysed, precise context, a justified judgement. For agreement credit the ministry, the chambers, radio ownership and the Olympics; against, the grumbling in Source A and the compulsion in Source C.","sol":"Interpretation 2 treats Nazi propaganda as genuinely effective: the Olympic summer and the Nuremberg rallies 'gave millions a pride they had not felt since 1918', and it counts that pride as real. Interpretation 1 doubts the evidence on which such a claim rests, because 'applause at the rallies proves very little' when 'the safest thing a man can do is applaud'. The question is whether we can tell enthusiasm from compliance. There is a great deal in Interpretation 2's favour. Goebbels had the means: his ministry was created on 13 March 1933, the Reich Chamber of Culture followed on 22 September 1933 with seven chambers whose membership was the condition of working at all, and the People's Receiver put a radio into about seventy per cent of German homes by 1939 against about a quarter in 1933. The message was not only political. The Berlin Olympics of August 1936, staged in a new stadium holding about a hundred thousand and opened with the first Olympic torch relay, presented an orderly and modern Germany to foreign journalists, and Source B reports that afterwards 'even old trade unionists' repeated that the foreigners had seen order and that nobody who wanted work was on the street. When the sympathisers of a banned party begin using the regime's own arguments, propaganda has done something real. Yet Interpretation 1's caution is well founded, and Source A shows why. The Gestapo officer writing in 1936 records complaint about shift length and prices in the pits and steelworks and says the mood is 'not good'; what propaganda had achieved there was not agreement but a separation of Hitler from his own government, since blame fell on the firm, the foreman or 'those in Berlin'. Source C points the same way. A district propaganda office would hardly need to order block wardens to record which households failed to listen, and to 'report absences without exception', if listening had been spontaneous. Compulsion built into the delivery of a message is a poor advertisement for its power. The interpretations convey these views in different ways. Interpretation 2 argues from scale and from mood, listing what came back — work, quiet streets, pride — so that propaganda appears as the expression of something people already felt. Interpretation 1 argues from the situation of the individual, and therefore treats every public sign of support as evidence contaminated by the risk of dissent. My judgement is that Interpretation 2 is right about particular themes and wrong to treat the effect as general. Propaganda was strongly effective where it told Germans what many of them wanted to believe, that order, work and international respect had returned and that Hitler embodied them, and the Olympics and the rallies are the clearest cases. It was much weaker on the daily facts people could check for themselves, such as wages and hours, which is why grumbling continued in the very year of the Games. Interpretation 1 is right that applause cannot simply be read as belief, but it is too sweeping: the private complaints recorded in Source A show that where propaganda had failed to persuade, Germans said so, so their enthusiasm elsewhere cannot all have been acting.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Effectiveness is not the same as reach: decide early which of the two each piece of your evidence measures.","Look for places where the regime had to compel attention, and ask what that tells you about persuasion.","Begin by stating what Interpretation 2 claims propaganda achieved and what would count as proof of it."],"codes":["31-3 — 3 Controlling and influencing attitudes: Goebbels and the Ministry of Propaganda","31-3 — 3 Controlling and influencing attitudes: Nazi control of culture and the arts","31-3 — 4 Opposition, resistance and conformity: The extent of support for the Nazi regime"]},{"q":"3(d) Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part played by ordinary Germans in making the Nazi police state work in the years 1933–39? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views evaluated, how each conveys its view analysed, precise context, a justified judgement. For agreement credit denunciation, Source A's figures and the block wardens of Source C; against, protective custody, the camps, the People's Court and Himmler's control of all police.","sol":"Interpretation 2 makes ordinary Germans the active ingredient of the police state: the secret police were 'too few to watch a nation of sixty-five million', their files show that cases 'usually began with a denunciation from a neighbour or a workmate', and it concludes that this was 'a regime whose people, for their own reasons, have decided to help it'. Interpretation 1 keeps the initiative with the state, describing a citizen left alone to face 'the Gestapo, the People's Court and the camps'. The issue is who was driving. The evidence for Interpretation 2 is strong. The Gestapo was a small force by the standards of the task it claimed, and historians working through its surviving case files have found that most investigations opened with information from the public rather than from surveillance. Source A says so plainly: twenty-four of the thirty-one cases opened in a Ruhr district in October 1936 began with information brought by private persons, 'a neighbour, a workmate, in two cases a wife'. The motives were often private — a quarrel over a fence, a rival at work, a family grudge — which is the sense in which people helped for their own reasons rather than the regime's. The Party's own network extended the same principle: Source C shows block wardens ordered to record which households did not listen to the Führer and to name anyone tuning to foreign stations, and reminds them that a warden who knows his forty households is worth more than a hundred posters. But Interpretation 2 explains the flow of information and then mistakes that for the whole system. Denunciation mattered only because a particular kind of state stood behind it. The Reichstag Fire Decree of 28 February 1933 allowed detention without charge; the Gestapo could impose protective custody with no trial and no appeal; Dachau opened in March 1933 and the camps were run by the SS; the People's Court, created in April 1934, tried treason without appeal; and in June 1936 Himmler was made Chief of German Police, bringing every force in Germany under the SS. A neighbour's spite is a nuisance in a state governed by law and lethal only where it can end in a camp. Interpretation 1 is describing that structure, and it is right that the destruction of the parties, the unions and the free press in 1933 is what left the individual exposed in the first place. The two also convey their views through what they choose to count. Interpretation 2 counts people and files, so the state looks thin and society looks busy; Interpretation 1 describes one person facing institutions, so society disappears and the state fills the frame. I agree with Interpretation 2 a good deal, and more than with the older picture it is arguing against. The image of an all-seeing Gestapo is false, and without the co-operation of ordinary Germans the regime could not have policed private opinion at all; Source A's own officer admits his dependence. But co-operation is not authorship. Germans supplied the information, while the SS, the camps and the courts supplied the consequences, and it was the consequences that made the information worth supplying. Interpretation 2 is right about who filled the files and wrong to imply that this makes the machinery secondary.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what a denunciation would have amounted to in a state where the courts still protected the accused.","Distinguish who supplied the information from who supplied the punishment, and build the essay on that distinction.","Open by stating Interpretation 2's claim about ordinary Germans in your own words and naming the criterion you will use."],"codes":["31-3 — 2 The police state: The role of the Gestapo, the SS and concentration camps","31-3 — 2 The police state: Nazi control of the legal system","31-3 — 4 Opposition, resistance and conformity: The extent of support for the Nazi regime"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date did Hindenburg sign the Decree for the Protection of People and State?","a":"28 February 1933, the day after the Reichstag Fire."},{"q":"What share of the vote and how many seats did the Nazis win at the Reichstag election of 5 March 1933?","a":"43.9 per cent of the vote and 288 seats."},{"q":"Which concentration camp, opened near Munich in March 1933, was the first?","a":"Dachau."},{"q":"Who founded the Pastors' Emergency League in 1933 and was arrested on 1 July 1937?","a":"Martin Niemöller."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The Reichstag Fire","open":false,"code":"31-3 — 1 The creation of a dictatorship, 1933–34: The Reichstag Fire","back":"The Reichstag building burned on the night of 27 February 1933. The Dutch communist Marinus van der Lubbe was caught at the scene; the Nazis blamed a communist rising and acted at once."},{"front":"The Reichstag Fire Decree, 28 February 1933","open":false,"code":"31-3 — 1 The creation of a dictatorship, 1933–34: The Reichstag Fire","back":"The Decree for the Protection of People and State suspended freedom of speech, press and assembly and allowed detention without charge. About four thousand communists were arrested within days."},{"front":"The Enabling Act, 23 March 1933","open":false,"code":"31-3 — 1 The creation of a dictatorship, 1933–34: The Reichstag Fire","back":"Let the government make laws without the Reichstag or the President for four years. Passed 444 to 94 with Communist deputies excluded and the Centre Party bought by promises about the Churches."},{"front":"The Night of the Long Knives, 30 June 1934","open":false,"code":"31-3 — 1 The creation of a dictatorship, 1933–34: The threat from Röhm and the SA","back":"SS men arrested Röhm at Bad Wiessee and shot him the next day. Gregor Strasser, Kurt von Schleicher and Gustav von Kahr also died. Hitler told the Reichstag on 13 July that seventy-seven had been killed."},{"front":"2 August 1934","open":false,"code":"31-3 — 1 The creation of a dictatorship, 1933–34: The threat from Röhm and the SA","back":"Hindenburg died. Hitler merged the offices of President and Chancellor as Führer and Reich Chancellor, and every soldier swore a personal oath of obedience to him by name."},{"front":"The Gestapo","open":false,"code":"31-3 — 2 The police state: The role of the Gestapo, the SS and concentration camps","back":"Secret state police, created by Göring in Prussia in April 1933 and taken over by Himmler in April 1934. It could order protective custody, sending people to camps without charge, trial or appeal."},{"front":"The SS and the SD","open":false,"code":"31-3 — 2 The police state: The role of the Gestapo, the SS and concentration camps","back":"The SS began in 1925 as Hitler's bodyguard; Himmler led it from 1929 and became Chief of German Police in June 1936. The SD, its intelligence branch under Heydrich, was set up in 1931."},{"front":"Dachau","open":false,"code":"31-3 — 2 The police state: The role of the Gestapo, the SS and concentration camps","back":"The first Nazi concentration camp, opened near Munich in March 1933 for communists, Social Democrats and trade unionists. SS Death's Head units ran it; Sachsenhausen and Buchenwald followed."},{"front":"The People's Court","open":false,"code":"31-3 — 2 The police state: Nazi control of the legal system","back":"Set up in April 1934 to try treason, after Leipzig judges acquitted four of the five men tried over the Reichstag Fire. Panels of five, only two of them qualified judges, often in secret, with no appeal."},{"front":"The Ministry of Public Enlightenment and Propaganda","open":false,"code":"31-3 — 3 Controlling and influencing attitudes: Goebbels and the Ministry of Propaganda","back":"Created for Joseph Goebbels on 13 March 1933. It issued daily instructions to editors, controlled radio and film, and used newsreels, rallies and sport to carry one message."},{"front":"The Volksempfänger","open":false,"code":"31-3 — 3 Controlling and influencing attitudes: Goebbels and the Ministry of Propaganda","back":"The People's Receiver, on sale from 1933 at about seventy-six marks and payable by instalments. Radio ownership rose from about a quarter of households in 1933 to about seventy per cent by 1939."},{"front":"The Reich Chamber of Culture","open":false,"code":"31-3 — 3 Controlling and influencing attitudes: Nazi control of culture and the arts","back":"Set up under Goebbels on 22 September 1933 with seven chambers for press, radio, film, theatre, music, literature and fine arts. Membership was the condition of working, so refusal ended a career."},{"front":"The Concordat and the Reich Church","open":false,"code":"31-3 — 3 Controlling and influencing attitudes: Nazi attempts to control the Churches","back":"The Concordat with the Vatican, 20 July 1933, promised freedom of worship and Catholic schools if clergy left politics. The Protestant Reich Church under Reich Bishop Ludwig Müller followed in September 1933."},{"front":"Martin Niemöller and the Confessing Church","open":false,"code":"31-3 — 4 Opposition, resistance and conformity: Opposition from the Churches","back":"Niemöller founded the Pastors' Emergency League in September 1933; it became the Confessing Church, which declared at Barmen in May 1934 that the Church answered to Christ, not the state. He was arrested on 1 July 1937."},{"front":"The Edelweiss Pirates and the Swing Youth","open":false,"code":"31-3 — 4 Opposition, resistance and conformity: Opposition from the young","back":"Working-class gangs of the industrial west, called Navajos in Cologne and Roving Dudes in Essen, hiked and mocked the Hitler Youth. The Swing Youth of Hamburg and Berlin played banned American dance records."}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The sources and interpretations written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Source A: From the monthly report on the mood of the population sent to his superiors by the head of a Gestapo district office in a Ruhr industrial town, October 1936. Written for this room in the style of such a report; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — Of the thirty-one cases opened in this district during the month, twenty-four began with information brought to us by private persons: a neighbour, a workmate, in two cases a wife. My officers cannot be everywhere, and we depend upon such assistance. The mood in the pits and the steelworks is not good. There is complaint about the length of the shift and about prices, and the Führer's name is seldom attached to it; the blame is put on the firm, on the foreman, or on those in Berlin. Loose talk in the taverns is less common than a year ago. The metalworker K., released from Dachau in the spring, does his shift and speaks to no one, and the effect of this upon his workmates has been greater than any speech of mine. Party block wardens continue to supply names and are, on the whole, reliable.","Source B: From a report on conditions in a factory town in Saxony, written by an underground contact of the exiled Social Democratic Party leadership and smuggled out to their office abroad, 1937. Written for this room in the style of such a report; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — The comrades who are still at liberty meet in threes and never in the same house twice. It would be wrong to tell you that the workers here are ready. They grumble at the rate for the job and at the price of fat, and then the wireless is switched on in the canteen and the same men shout with the rest. The works foreman is a block warden and writes down what he hears, so a man thinks twice before he finishes a sentence. Since the Olympic Games last year even old trade unionists say that the foreigners came and saw order, and that no one is on the street now who wants work. Two of ours were taken in March and have not come back. Our leaflets are read, but they are read in the lavatory and then torn up.","Source C: From a circular sent to Party block wardens by the propaganda office of a Nazi Party district, 1938. Written for this room in the style of such a circular; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — Comrades, the Führer's speech on the evening of the twentieth will be carried on every station. Each block warden will see that a receiver is available in his block, and that households which have none are invited to listen with a neighbour; where numbers require it, a communal set will be placed in the yard or in the works canteen. You will note which households are represented and which are not, and you will report absences without exception to this office, together with any reason given. You will further report any person known to turn the dial to foreign stations. The People's Receiver is now within the means of every household and excuses of cost are no longer to be accepted. Remember that the warden who knows his forty households is worth more to the movement than a hundred posters.","Interpretation 1: From a study of the Nazi police state, by a historian writing in the 1980s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — Obedience in Nazi Germany was bought with fear. Within eighteen months of January 1933 the parties, the unions and the free press had gone, and with them every organisation through which discontent could have become opposition. What remained was a citizen alone, facing the Gestapo, the People's Court and the camps. Germans learned quickly what silence was worth: a joke repeated in the wrong tavern could end in Dachau, and one returned prisoner was warning enough for a whole street. Applause at the rallies proves very little, because in such a state the safest thing a man can do is applaud.","Interpretation 2: From a general history of German society under the Nazis, by a historian writing in the 2000s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — Most Germans in the 1930s needed very little compelling. Work came back, the streets were quiet, and the Olympic summer and the rallies at Nuremberg gave millions a pride they had not felt since 1918. The secret police were too few to watch a nation of sixty-five million, and their files show that the cases they opened usually began with a denunciation from a neighbour or a workmate. A regime that has to be told where its enemies are is not an all-seeing one; it is a regime whose people, for their own reasons, have decided to help it.","How the room uses them: Source A is the inference source for Question 1. Sources B and C are the contemporary pair for the 8-mark usefulness question, 3(a), and may be used on 3(c). Interpretations 1 and 2 are the pair for 3(b), 3(c) and 3(d). Every quotation in the model answers and worked examples of this room comes from these five texts and from nowhere else."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 3 Question 3(d), 16 marks)","items":["Question: 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the effect of the regime's successes at home and abroad on German opinion in the years 1933–39? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]. This is one paragraph from the middle of the answer, putting the case against Interpretation 2.","Interpretation 2 makes the regime's successes the explanation of German opinion: work 'came back', the streets were quiet, and 'the Olympic summer and the rallies at Nuremberg gave millions a pride they had not felt since 1918'. The successes were real, abroad as much as at home: the troops who marched into the demilitarised Rhineland on 7 March 1936 and into Austria in March 1938 were cheered in the streets, and the plebiscite of 10 April 1938 gave the regime the near-unanimous vote it wanted. But two things make even these a poor measure of what Germans thought. First, part of the recovery was a change in who was counted rather than in who was employed: conscription was reintroduced on 16 March 1935, and the law of 26 June 1935 made six months in the Reich Labour Service compulsory for young men, so a whole cohort that would otherwise have stood on the unemployment register was in uniform or on a drainage scheme instead. Second, where the recovery was real it did not reach the pay packet evenly, and the regime's own police knew it: Source A, written by a Gestapo district officer in October 1936, reports that the mood in 'the pits and the steelworks is not good', with complaint 'about the length of the shift and about prices'. The two texts convey their views by different means, and that is what decides between them here. Interpretation 2 argues from scale and mood — 'millions', 'a nation of sixty-five million' — so that the successes and the contentment appear to be one fact; Interpretation 1 argues from the position of a single man and warns against exactly that reading, since 'applause at the rallies proves very little, because in such a state the safest thing a man can do is applaud'. Source B carries both halves at once: old trade unionists concede after the Games that 'the foreigners came and saw order', while the same men 'grumble at the rate for the job and at the price of fat'. A plebiscite held under the Gestapo tells the same story as the applause: the successes therefore altered what Germans said in public far more reliably than what they thought, and Interpretation 2, having no means of hearing the second, reads the first as consent.","Why this is Level 4: it 'provides an explained evaluation reviewing the alternative views in coming to a substantiated judgement', because Interpretation 2's claim is granted where it holds and then measured against evidence it cannot account for, instead of being contradicted flatly. The analysis of the interpretations is precise about how the differences of view are conveyed, setting an argument from scale and mood against an argument from the position of one man and its warning about applause. The contextual knowledge is precisely selected rather than general — conscription on 16 March 1935, the labour-service law of 26 June 1935, the district report of October 1936 — and each piece does evaluative work rather than sitting beside the judgement. The line of reasoning is sustained and the closing sentence judges rather than summarises."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same as above — 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the effect of the regime's successes at home and abroad on German opinion in the years 1933–39?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks].","Interpretation 2 says that the regime's successes made Germans content, and I partly agree with this. There were many more jobs than before and the Olympics made the country look good to foreigners, so people were pleased with Hitler and did not complain much. However, Interpretation 1 says that applause at the rallies proves very little, which is also true, because people were frightened of the Gestapo and the camps and would have cheered anyway. Some workers were still unhappy about their wages and their hours even when things were better. Overall I think both interpretations are partly right, because the successes did please people but you cannot really tell what they thought.","Why this is Level 2 and not Level 3: it does offer 'valid evaluative comment to agree with or counter the interpretation' and it does select details from both, which is what Level 2 asks for. What it lacks is Level 3's 'relevant contextual knowledge used directly to support the evaluation': 'many more jobs', 'the Olympics made the country look good' and 'people were frightened of the Gestapo' are assertions standing where the reintroduction of conscription in March 1935, the labour-service law of June 1935 and the October 1936 report on the mood in the pits should be. It never shows how either interpretation conveys its view, and the judgement is announced in the last sentence instead of being reasoned towards, so 'its justification is insecure or undeveloped and a line of reasoning is not sustained'. Turning it into a Level 3 answer means replacing every general adjective with a named fact that does evaluative work."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the creation of the dictatorship, January 1933 to August 1934","items":["30 January 1933: Hitler appointed Chancellor of a coalition in which the Nazis held only three cabinet posts, with Papen as Vice-Chancellor.","27 February 1933: the Reichstag building burned; the Dutch communist Marinus van der Lubbe was caught at the scene and was executed in January 1934.","28 February 1933: Hindenburg signed the Decree for the Protection of People and State, suspending freedom of speech, press and assembly and allowing detention without charge; about four thousand communists were arrested within days.","5 March 1933: at the last multi-party Reichstag election the Nazis won 43.9 per cent of the vote and 288 of the 647 seats; the Communists won 81 seats, whose holders were prevented from taking them.","23 March 1933: the Enabling Act, passed in the Kroll Opera House by 444 votes to 94, let the government make laws without the Reichstag or the President for four years; only the Social Democrats voted against, and SA and SS men lined the building.","2 May 1933: trade union offices were occupied and their funds seized; the German Labour Front under Robert Ley replaced them and strikes were outlawed.","22 June 1933: the Social Democratic Party was banned; the Catholic Centre Party dissolved itself on 5 July; the Law against the Formation of New Parties of 14 July 1933 made the NSDAP the only legal party.","By the summer of 1934 the SA under Ernst Röhm numbered close to three million men, against an army limited by the Treaty of Versailles to 100,000.","30 June to 2 July 1934, the Night of the Long Knives: Röhm was arrested at Bad Wiessee on 30 June and shot on 1 July; Gregor Strasser, the former Chancellor Kurt von Schleicher and his wife, and Gustav Ritter von Kahr were also killed.","13 July 1934: Hitler told the Reichstag that seventy-seven people had been killed; historians' estimates run higher. The cabinet had already declared the killings lawful acts of state defence, and the SS was made independent of the SA later that month.","2 August 1934: Hindenburg died. Hitler merged the offices of President and Chancellor as Führer and Reich Chancellor, and the armed forces swore a personal oath of obedience to him by name.","19 August 1934: a plebiscite on the merging of the two offices returned about ninety per cent in favour, in conditions of intimidation."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the police state — the Gestapo, the SS, the camps and the courts","items":["The SS was founded in 1925 as Hitler's personal bodyguard; Heinrich Himmler became its Reichsführer in 1929.","The SD, the party's own intelligence service, was set up in 1931 under Reinhard Heydrich to watch enemies inside and outside the movement.","April 1933: Göring created the Gestapo out of the Prussian political police. Himmler took it over in April 1934, and in June 1936 he was made Chief of German Police, bringing every force in Germany under the SS.","Protective custody: the Gestapo could order a person to a concentration camp with no charge, no trial and no appeal to any court.","The Gestapo was small for the task it claimed in a country of about sixty-five million, and most of its cases began with a denunciation from a neighbour, a workmate or a relative rather than from its own surveillance.","Party block wardens were responsible for the households of a street or block, collected contributions, reported on residents and were expected to know every family in their patch.","March 1933: Dachau opened near Munich as the first concentration camp, for communists, Social Democrats and trade unionists; SS Death's Head units ran the camp system.","Later camps included Sachsenhausen from 1936, Buchenwald from 1937, Mauthausen from 1938 and Ravensbrück for women from 1939.","April 1933: the Law for the Restoration of the Professional Civil Service removed Jewish and politically unreliable judges and officials.","Judges were pressed into the National Socialist League for the Maintenance of the Law and were told that judgments should serve the people and the movement rather than the letter of the existing code.","April 1934: the People's Court was created to try treason, after the Leipzig judges had acquitted four of the five men tried over the Reichstag Fire. It sat in panels of five, only two of whom need be qualified judges, often in secret, with no right of appeal.","Special courts operated in the regions for political offences, and a person acquitted in an ordinary court could still be taken into protective custody on leaving it."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Goebbels, propaganda, culture and sport","items":["13 March 1933: the Reich Ministry of Public Enlightenment and Propaganda was created, with Joseph Goebbels as minister.","Opposition newspapers were closed under the Reichstag Fire Decree and the Social Democrat and Communist presses were seized during 1933; remaining editors received daily instructions on what to print.","10 May 1933: students burned books by Jewish, socialist and pacifist writers in Berlin and other university towns.","22 September 1933: the Reich Chamber of Culture was set up under Goebbels, with seven chambers for the press, radio, film, theatre, music, literature and the fine arts; membership was the condition of working in any of them.","The People's Receiver went on sale in 1933 at about seventy-six marks, payable by instalments, with cheaper sets later; it was built for German stations and picked up foreign ones poorly.","About a quarter of German households had a radio in 1933 and about seventy per cent by 1939, the highest proportion in the world; loudspeaker pillars carried speeches to streets, factories and cafés.","Every film script needed approval and newsreels were shown before every feature film; Leni Riefenstahl filmed the 1934 Nuremberg rally and the 1936 Olympics.","The Nuremberg rallies were held each September, with Albert Speer's ranks of searchlights at the Zeppelin field; the 1935 rally was the occasion of the Nuremberg Laws.","Approved art showed peasants, soldiers, mothers and heroic labour; modern work was ridiculed at the Degenerate Art exhibition in Munich in 1937, staged against the Great German Art Exhibition at the new House of German Art in the same city.","Public architecture under Speer was monumental and neo-classical, while housing was built in traditional German styles.","1 to 16 August 1936: the Berlin Olympics were held in a new stadium holding about a hundred thousand, opened with the first Olympic torch relay from Greece.","For the fortnight of the Games anti-Jewish signs were taken down and the Nazi paper Der Stürmer was kept off the news-stands; Germany won more medals than any other nation, while the African-American athlete Jesse Owens won four gold medals."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Churches — control and opposition","items":["About two-thirds of Germans were Protestant and about a third Catholic, so the Churches were the largest organisations the regime did not create.","Point 24 of the Nazi Party programme of 1920 spoke of 'positive Christianity', which allowed Hitler to present himself publicly as no enemy of the Churches.","20 July 1933: the Concordat was signed with the Vatican. The Church was promised freedom of worship and the right to keep its schools and youth organisations; the clergy undertook to stay out of politics. The Centre Party had dissolved itself on 5 July.","The regime then broke the terms, closing Catholic schools, restricting Catholic newspapers and folding Catholic youth groups into the Hitler Youth; priests were prosecuted in show trials on currency and morality charges.","14 March 1937: Pope Pius XI's encyclical Mit brennender Sorge was written in German rather than Latin, smuggled into Germany and printed secretly; it was read from Catholic pulpits on Palm Sunday and accused the regime of breaking the Concordat. The police seized the copies.","The pro-Nazi German Christians wanted one Reich Church; some of them wanted the Old Testament dropped and clergy of Jewish descent excluded from church office.","September 1933: Ludwig Müller was elected Reich Bishop of the new German Evangelical Church.","September 1933: Martin Niemöller, a former U-boat commander who had at first welcomed Hitler, founded the Pastors' Emergency League in protest at the exclusion of clergy of Jewish descent.","May 1934: the Barmen Declaration of the Confessing Church stated that the Church answered to Christ and not to the state; the movement drew several thousand pastors.","1935: a Ministry for Church Affairs was created to bring the Protestant churches to heel; it largely failed, and the Protestants remained divided rather than absorbed.","1 July 1937: Niemöller was arrested. He was tried in 1938 and then held as Hitler's personal prisoner, first in Sachsenhausen and later in Dachau.","Dietrich Bonhoeffer, a Confessing Church theologian, had his training college at Finkenwalde closed by the Gestapo in 1937 and was later banned from Berlin; his part in active resistance belongs to the war years, after this key topic ends."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: support, conformity and opposition, 1933–39","items":["Registered unemployment fell from about six million in early 1933 to a few hundred thousand by 1939, which was the regime's strongest claim on ordinary loyalty.","Strength Through Joy provided cheap holidays, trips and concerts for members of the German Labour Front, and Beauty of Labour improved factory conditions.","The plebiscite of 19 August 1934 returned about ninety per cent for the merging of President and Chancellor; later plebiscites were higher still, but all were held under intimidation and cannot be read as free votes.","The Rhineland was reoccupied in March 1936 and Austria annexed in March 1938, and each success was presented as the undoing of Versailles.","The exiled Social Democrat leadership, based in Prague from 1933 and Paris from 1938, collected reports on opinion inside Germany that recorded constant complaint about wages, hours and prices.","That complaint was usually aimed at employers, foremen and local officials rather than at Hitler himself, a pattern the regime's own Gestapo reports also recorded.","The Communist Party was banned first and hit hardest: its leader Ernst Thälmann was arrested in March 1933 and held without trial for the rest of his life.","The Hitler Youth absorbed or banned rival youth movements in 1933, became the state youth organisation in 1936, and membership was made compulsory in March 1939.","The Edelweiss Pirates were loose working-class gangs of roughly fourteen- to seventeen-year-olds in the industrial west, known as the Navajos in Cologne, the Roving Dudes in Essen and the Kittelbach Pirates around Düsseldorf; their slogan was eternal war on the Hitler Youth.","The Swing Youth were mainly middle-class teenagers in cities such as Hamburg and Berlin who played American and British dance records, grew their hair long and used English words; the regime called their music un-German and racially degenerate.","Neither youth group had a political programme, and the harshest repression of both came during the war, after this key topic ends.","Later opposition belongs to the war years and should be used only as context: the White Rose students of Munich, Hans and Sophie Scholl, from 1942, and the Kreisau Circle around Helmuth James von Moltke from 1940."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the source and interpretation moves this paper rewards","items":["Inference: say something the source does not state but that must be true if the source is true, then give the words that force it. One inference and one supporting detail in each box.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose: what kind of document it is, who made it and when, and why it was made.","Utility: judge each source for the enquiry the question names, never in general. Content first, then how nature, origin and purpose make that content more or less useful, then the context that confirms or qualifies it.","Contextual knowledge scores only when it is used on the source; a paragraph about the period that never touches the sources earns nothing at all.","'Biased' is not a judgement on its own. Say whose view the source gives, why it gives that view, and what it is therefore good evidence of: a one-sided source is excellent evidence of the view it takes.","An internal order or a private report is often more candid than a public one, because it was not written to persuade the reader of anything; say what that does to its usefulness.","A source can be useful for an enquiry it never mentions, if what it shows explains the thing being enquired into; say what you can infer, not only what it says.","Difference of view (3(b)): a disagreement about the past, not a difference of wording or of detail. State the one difference, then support it with a phrase from each interpretation.","Reason for difference (3(c)): interpretations differ because they ask different questions, rest on different evidence, or give weight to different parts of the story. Name which, and show it from the interpretations, using Sources B and C where they illustrate the kind of evidence involved.","Differences based on conclusions drawn from evidence are legitimate; an interpretation is not wrong merely because another one disagrees with it.","Evaluating an interpretation (3(d)): use both interpretations and your own knowledge, show how each conveys its view, then decide how far the named interpretation can be agreed with and say on what criterion."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 3 Question 3(d): 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the reasons why there was so little open opposition to the Nazi regime in the years 1933–39?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","items":["The question as printed: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the reasons why there was so little open opposition to the Nazi regime in the years 1933–39? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' Note that this question prints no stimulus points: the two interpretations are the stimulus, both must be used, and an answer that evaluates one in isolation cannot reach the top levels.","Decode the statement. Interpretation 2 explains the quiet by consent: most Germans 'needed very little compelling', work came back, the streets were quiet, the Olympic summer and the Nuremberg rallies gave pride, and the police were 'too few to watch a nation of sixty-five million'. Interpretation 1 explains it by fear: obedience 'was bought with fear', every organisation through which discontent could become opposition had gone, and a joke in the wrong tavern 'could end in Dachau'. The criterion to announce in the opening is this: were Germans quiet because they had nothing to protest about, or because there was no longer any safe way to protest?","Opening (three or four sentences): put Interpretation 2's claim in your own words, name Interpretation 1 as the rival weighting, state the criterion, and signal where you will end, so the line of reasoning is visible from the first paragraph.","Paragraph 1, for Interpretation 2 — there really was much to consent to. Registered unemployment fell from about six million in early 1933 to a few hundred thousand by 1939; Strength Through Joy holidays; the Rhineland reoccupied in March 1936; the Berlin Olympics of August 1936. Interim judgement: a population with those experiences did not need to be terrorised into acquiescence week by week.","Paragraph 2, for Interpretation 2 — the machinery of terror was thin. The Gestapo was small for a country of that size, and most of its cases began with a denunciation; Source A's district officer admits that twenty-four of thirty-one cases that month came from private persons and that his officers 'cannot be everywhere'. Interim judgement: a state that must be told where its enemies are was not holding the country down by surveillance.","Paragraph 3, against Interpretation 2 — the compelling had already been done. The Reichstag Fire Decree of 28 February 1933 and about four thousand arrests; the seizure of the trade unions on 2 May 1933 and the German Labour Front under Robert Ley; the banning of the SPD in June and the Law against the Formation of New Parties of 14 July. Interim judgement: opposition was scarce by 1936 because the structures that could have carried it had been destroyed in 1933.","Paragraph 4, against Interpretation 2 — what happened to those who tried. Dachau from March 1933 and the camps run by the SS; protective custody with no charge, trial or appeal; the People's Court from April 1934 trying treason without appeal; Ernst Thälmann arrested in March 1933 and never tried. Source B shows the result: comrades who 'meet in threes and never in the same house twice' and leaflets 'read in the lavatory and then torn up'. Interim judgement: the small number of cases measures the success of terror, not its absence.","Paragraph 5, the point that goes furthest beyond both texts — where opposition survived, it survived because it had an institution behind it. The Confessing Church after Barmen in May 1934, and the reading of Mit brennender Sorge from Catholic pulpits on Palm Sunday 1937, came from the one mass organisation the regime had not destroyed; the Edelweiss Pirates and the Swing Youth kept only a weekend and a taste. Interim judgement: opposition tracked the survival of organisations, which is exactly what Interpretation 1 predicts and Interpretation 2 does not explain.","The evaluation thread that must run through every paragraph: how each interpretation conveys its view. Interpretation 2 argues from scale and from mood — numbers of police, files, the pride of millions — so that society fills the frame and the state looks thin. Interpretation 1 argues from the position of one person facing named institutions, so the state fills the frame and society disappears. Saying this, and using it, is what the top level means by showing how the differences of view are conveyed.","What the conclusion must say: not 'both are partly right'. Apply the criterion announced at the start. Interpretation 2 is convincing about the later 1930s, when most Germans were genuinely content and needed no compelling; it is not convincing as an explanation of why opposition was absent, because the absence was manufactured in 1933 and 1934, before the achievements it credits. Name the deciding consideration: consent and terror were not alternatives, because the terror of the first two years removed every organisation through which discontent could have been expressed, and so guaranteed that later discontent stayed private.","Timing and SPaG: about 40 minutes in all — six to plan the paragraphs and fix the criterion, about thirty to write, and the last four to check spelling and punctuation and to make sure the specialist terms are present and correctly used, since 4 additional marks depend on 'a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate'. Useful terms here: Reichstag Fire Decree, Enabling Act, Gleichschaltung, German Labour Front, Gestapo, protective custody, concentration camp, People's Court, block warden, Confessing Church, Concordat, plebiscite."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:48:31Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.94,"notes":"Re-judged after the polish pass against the kit file itself. All five recorded fixes are real and verified line by line: the 3(c) worked example now turns on the two historians generalising from different stretches of 1933-39, with Interpretation 1's own 'within eighteen months of January 1933' set against Interpretation 2's later examples and Source B's 1937 double testimony, so the bank 3(c) is no longer pre-answered (dupscan.js reports 'no duplication'); room.questions[0]'s key and model now infer rather than paraphrase (denunciation running through neighbour, workmate and a wife listed without comment; wardens who 'continue to supply names' and are only 'on the whole, reliable'), and `a` expressly refuses the old paraphrase; the hints on questions 4 and 7 no longer hand over the structure or the beyond-stimulus reason; the model-paragraph pair has moved to a 3(d) focus that is in neither the bank nor the plan and is rewritten on evidence the plan does not use; and every transliterated umlaut is gone, with Führer, Reichsführer, Volksempfänger, Müller, Niemöller, Düsseldorf, Ravensbrück, Thälmann and Stürmer now spelled one way throughout. The two new facts check out on the web - conscription reintroduced 16 March 1935 and the Reich Labour Service law of 26 June 1935 making six months compulsory for young men aged 18 to 25 - and the argument the Level 4 paragraph builds on them (part of the fall in registered unemployment was a change in who was counted) is sound. Re-solved all fifteen questions: tariffs, AO splits, June 2025 stems and the 'three or more aspects of content' caps are unchanged and correct, every quotation still matches the printed sources and interpretations, no date, figure or attribution is wrong, and the structural counts hold (15 questions at 4/8/3, 15 cards all closed with backs under 40 words, 6 facts, 4 exit questions, 5 worked examples)."}}},"31-4":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"31-4","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 4 is where Paper 3 finds its human material, and it turns up in every part of the paper. Section A's Source A is usually a poster, photograph, report or letter about how people actually lived under Nazi rule, and Section A's 12-mark 'Explain why' is usually a causation question about one of these policies: women, the young, work, or the persecution of minorities. Section B's interpretations are nearly always an argument about the same question: did Nazi rule make ordinary Germans' lives better, and at whose expense? You cannot argue that with adjectives. You need the marriage loan of June 1933 and the Mother's Cross of 1938, the Hitler Youth law of December 1936 and the service decree of March 1939, six million unemployed in 1933 against a few hundred thousand in 1939, the Nuremberg Laws of 15 September 1935 and the night of 9 to 10 November 1938. This room gives you that detail and then the moves that turn it into marks.","idea":[{"h":"Kinder, Küche, Kirche: what the Nazis thought women were for","code":"31-4 — 1 Nazi policies towards women: Nazi views on women and the family","t":"Nazi ideology gave women one central task: to bear and raise racially valuable children for the national community. The old slogan Kinder, Küche, Kirche (children, kitchen, church) was revived, and the birth rate, which had been falling for a generation and reached its lowest point of the century in 1933, was treated as a racial emergency rather than a private matter. The Nazi Women's League (NS-Frauenschaft), led from 1934 by Gertrud Scholtz-Klink as Reich Women's Leader, taught motherhood, cookery and household economy, and propaganda praised the sturdy peasant mother in a plain dress with her hair in plaits, not the short-haired, smoking, salaried woman of Weimar Berlin. The point to grasp is that the policy was racial before it was domestic: the same state that paid Aryan mothers to have children sterilised women it judged unfit. A top-level answer uses the ideology as a cause, not as a description: it explains why a government in the middle of a depression would pay women to leave paid work, and it notices that the ideal was for some women only."},{"h":"Loans, medals and closed doors: the policies themselves","code":"31-4 — 1 Nazi policies towards women: Nazi policies towards women","t":"The Law for the Encouragement of Marriage of June 1933 offered newly married 'Aryan' couples an interest-free loan of 1,000 Reichsmarks, on condition that the wife gave up her job; a quarter of the debt was cancelled for each child born, so four children wiped it out. About 700,000 couples took a loan in the scheme's first four years. The Cross of Honour of the German Mother, instituted in December 1938 and first awarded on Mother's Day, 21 May 1939, came in bronze, silver and gold, the gold going to mothers of eight or more children; Hitler Youth boys were expected to salute the wearers. Himmler's Lebensborn, founded in December 1935, ran maternity homes for unmarried mothers of children judged racially valuable. Doors were closed too: married women doctors and civil servants were dismissed from 1933, and from 1936 women could not be judges or public prosecutors. Yet the policy collided with rearmament: the number of women in work rose from about 4.85 million in 1933 to about 7.14 million in 1939, and from 1938 a compulsory Duty Year (Pflichtjahr) sent young women into farms and households. A top-level answer uses that contradiction, and does not claim the Nazis simply drove women out of work."},{"h":"Owning the child's week: the Hitler Youth and the League of German Maidens","code":"31-4 — 2 Nazi policies towards the young: Nazi aims and policies towards the young","t":"The Nazis wanted the young because the young were the next generation of soldiers and mothers, and because children could be reached where their parents could not. Boys joined the German Young People (Deutsches Jungvolk) at ten and the Hitler Youth proper at fourteen; girls joined the Young Maidens and then the League of German Maidens (Bund Deutscher Mädel) at fourteen, with Faith and Beauty added in 1938 for those aged seventeen to twenty-one. Boys did drill, camping, map-reading, small-bore shooting and Nazi history; girls did athletics, route marches, camps, first aid and domestic science, and were told their fitness was for motherhood. Rival youth groups were absorbed or banned, the law of 1 December 1936 made the Hitler Youth the state youth organisation, and a decree of March 1939 made service compulsory. Membership grew from about 2.3 million in 1933 to about 5.4 million in 1936 and about 8.7 million in 1939. A top-level answer weighs attraction against compulsion: the camps and the uniform were genuinely popular, but the biggest jumps in membership follow the law of 1936 and the decree of 1939, and by the late 1930s older members were complaining of boredom and drill."},{"h":"The nazified classroom","code":"31-4 — 2 Nazi policies towards the young: Nazi control of the young through education","t":"Schools were brought into line quickly. Jewish and politically unreliable teachers were removed under the Law for the Restoration of the Professional Civil Service of 7 April 1933, and by 1937 about 97 per cent of teachers had joined the National Socialist Teachers' League; lesson content came from the Reich Education Ministry under Bernhard Rust. Race studies was added as a subject, with skull measuring and lessons on 'Aryan' superiority; biology taught heredity and eugenics; history taught the stab-in-the-back myth and the injustice of Versailles; geography taught the need for living space; German lessons used nationalist stories; even arithmetic problems counted the cost of keeping disabled people or the bombs a squadron could carry. Physical education expanded to as many as five periods a week. Girls took domestic science and eugenics. Above the ordinary schools sat the elite institutions: the first three Napolas (National Political Education Institutes) opened in 1933 and there were sixteen by 1939, and the Adolf Hitler Schools, founded in 1937 by Robert Ley and Baldur von Schirach, fed the Order Castles. A top-level answer shows the school and the Hitler Youth working as one system, and notes the cost: standards in some academic subjects fell, and tired boys arrived on Monday morning."},{"h":"Six million to almost none: how the figure came down","code":"31-4 — 3 Employment and living standards: Nazi policies to reduce unemployment","t":"Registered unemployment stood at about six million when Hitler became Chancellor in January 1933 and at under 300,000 by 1939. Four things did it. First, public works: the autobahn programme, begun with Hitler's ceremonial first spade in September 1933, had about 3,000 kilometres open by the end of 1938 and employed roughly 125,000 men directly at its peak in 1936, besides the men making cement and steel. Second, the Reich Labour Service (RAD), voluntary from 1933 and compulsory from 26 June 1935 for men aged eighteen to twenty-five for six months, in uniform, in barracks, for pocket money. Third, rearmament and conscription: conscription was reintroduced on 16 March 1935 and the armed forces grew from the 100,000 men Versailles allowed to well over a million by 1939, while the Four-Year Plan of September 1936 under Göring poured money into steel, synthetic oil and rubber. Fourth, arithmetic: Jews pushed out of jobs, women who left work, conscripts, Labour Service men and prisoners in the camps were not counted, so part of the fall was 'invisible unemployment'. A top-level answer ranks these causes rather than listing them, and says what the recovery was for: war, not prosperity."},{"h":"Cheap holidays and a longer week: did workers gain?","code":"31-4 — 3 Employment and living standards: Changes in the standard of living","t":"Trade unions were abolished on 2 May 1933 and replaced on 10 May by the German Labour Front (DAF) under Robert Ley, which every worker paid into; strikes were illegal, wage rates were set by government Trustees of Labour, and from February 1935 the workbook, held by the employer, controlled who could change jobs. In return the regime offered rewards. Strength Through Joy (Kraft durch Freude), founded in November 1933, sold cheap theatre tickets, sports, hikes, cruises and holidays: about 2.3 million people took KdF holidays in 1934 and about 10.3 million in 1938. Beauty of Labour, set up under the Labour Front at the end of 1933 and run by the architect Albert Speer, put washrooms, canteens and better lighting into factories, though workers often did the improvements in their own time. From 1938 workers could save five Reichsmarks a week towards a 990-mark Volkswagen: more than 336,000 savers paid in and not one civilian ever received a car. Meanwhile the average working week lengthened from about 43 hours in 1933 to about 47 in 1939, and although weekly earnings rose by roughly a fifth in real terms across those years, that was mostly extra hours, with hourly rates still near slump levels and food prices rising. A top-level answer says what standard of living means before judging it: work and security yes, freedom and hourly pay no."},{"h":"The community defined by who is shut out","code":"31-4 — 4 The persecution of minorities: Nazi racial beliefs and policies","t":"The Volksgemeinschaft, the national community, was a racial idea: a people's community of healthy 'Aryan' Germans, with Slavs classed as inferior and the disabled, the 'work-shy' and the 'asocial' treated as a burden on it. The Law for the Prevention of Hereditarily Diseased Offspring of 14 July 1933, in force from January 1934, set up Hereditary Health Courts which could order compulsory sterilisation for conditions including hereditary blindness and deafness, schizophrenia, epilepsy and 'feeble-mindedness'; around 400,000 people were sterilised under it, most of them before the war, and school arithmetic taught children what their care cost the state. Roma and Sinti were harassed under 'Gypsy' decrees, and about 600 were rounded up into a camp at Marzahn on 16 July 1936, shortly before the Berlin Olympics; Himmler's decree on the 'Gypsy plague' followed in December 1938. Paragraph 175 of the criminal code was tightened on 28 June 1935 so that far less evidence was needed to convict men of homosexual acts, and roughly 50,000 men were convicted in the Nazi years, some sent to camps to wear the pink triangle. A top-level answer uses these groups to test claims about how popular Nazi rule was: the same policy that gave one family a medal took another family's children away."},{"h":"From boycott to pogrom: the persecution of Jewish people, 1933-39","code":"31-4 — 4 The persecution of minorities: The persecution of Jewish people","t":"The persecution escalated in steps, and the steps matter. On 1 April 1933 the SA picketed Jewish shops in a one-day national boycott; on 7 April the Law for the Restoration of the Professional Civil Service pushed Jews out of state employment. At the Nuremberg rally on 15 September 1935 came the Nuremberg Laws: the Reich Citizenship Law, which reduced Jews from citizens to subjects, and the Law for the Protection of German Blood and German Honour, which banned marriage and sexual relations between Jews and other Germans; a decree in November defined who counted as a Jew by grandparents. Pressure eased a little for the Berlin Olympics of 1936 and then hardened sharply in 1938: property had to be registered, passports were stamped with a J, and Jewish doctors and lawyers lost their patients and clients. After Herschel Grynszpan shot the German diplomat Ernst vom Rath in Paris, the pogrom of 9 to 10 November 1938, 'Kristallnacht', left at least 91 Jews dead, about 7,500 businesses wrecked, hundreds of synagogues burned and some 30,000 Jewish men in concentration camps; on 12 November the Jewish community was fined one billion Reichsmarks for the damage done to it. Of about 523,000 Jews in Germany in January 1933, roughly 282,000 had emigrated by September 1939. A top-level answer explains the timing as well as the events: each step came when the regime felt safe enough to take it."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1: two inferences from Source A, on the focus the question actually names","setup":"Source A in this room is a report on the year's work written by the leader of a Hitler Youth district (Bann) in a Ruhr industrial town for the regional youth office, April 1939. It says that the Bann and its Jungbann together count every boy of ten to eighteen in the town except those the doctors have excused; that since the service order of March the town clerk sends the names of boys who have not reported, that leaders call at their homes, that nine out of ten come after the second visit and the rest are handed to the youth office; that Tuesday evenings are for the history of the movement and the racial question, Saturdays for sport, and every second Sunday a route march with map work; that the rifle range at the gasworks is in use three afternoons a week and 'the boys queue for it'; that older boys attend the shooting and field exercises 'far better than the evening classes'; that two headmasters have complained that boys arrive on Monday 'too tired to work'; and that the Whitsun camp took the older companies under canvas for eight days. The question is: 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about the relationship between the Hitler Youth and German schools. Complete the table below to explain your answer.' (4 marks)","steps":["Underline the focus before reading for content: the question is not about the Hitler Youth in general but about how it stood in relation to schools, so only the lines that touch on school time, schoolwork or headmasters can earn the marks.","First inference from the headmasters: they write to complain that boys arrive on Monday 'too tired to work', so the two organisations were competing for the same boys' time and energy, and school was losing.","Second inference from the timetable and the person writing: Tuesday evenings, Saturdays and alternate Sundays are all claimed by the Bann, and its leader reports the headmasters' complaint to the youth office rather than acting on it, so the Hitler Youth had the stronger claim on a boy's week.","Write each answer as a pair: the claim the source never states in the inference box, and the words that force it in the details box, and check that the claim really is about schools."],"cues":["Which words in the question limit what you are allowed to infer?","Which sentence in the source is written by somebody outside the Hitler Youth?","Which days of the week does the Bann already own, and what does its leader do with the complaint?","What goes in the first box and what goes in the second?"]},{"title":"Question 2: taking a 12-mark 'Explain why' past the two bullet points","setup":"The question is: 'Explain why the Hitler Youth had grown so large by 1939. You may use the following in your answer: • the law of December 1936 • the activities the Hitler Youth offered. You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks). On the paper this is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b) and you answer one of them. The mark scheme caps the answer at a maximum of 8 marks, with no access to Level 4, if it does not address three or more aspects of content. Useful facts: membership was about 2.3 million in 1933, about 5.4 million in 1936 and about 8.7 million in 1939; the law of 1 December 1936 made the Hitler Youth the state youth organisation and rival groups were absorbed or banned; a decree of March 1939 made service compulsory; the organisation offered camps, sport, shooting, travel and a uniform to boys, and camps, athletics and companionship to girls in the League of German Maidens.","steps":["Plan three reasons of different kinds before writing, so the answer analyses instead of listing: attraction, elimination of alternatives, and legal compulsion.","Reason one, attraction, using the bullet point about activities: for a boy in a mining town the Hitler Youth offered a tent, a bugle, a rifle range and a week away, which few families could pay for, so it recruited heavily before it was compelled.","Reason two, the law of 1 December 1936: making the Hitler Youth the state youth organisation removed the church and scouting clubs that had competed for the same boys, so joining stopped being a choice between organisations and became a choice between belonging and standing out.","Reason three, which neither bullet prompts, compulsion and pressure: the decree of March 1939 made service a duty, and the room's Source A shows what that meant in one town, with names of absentees passed to the Bann and leaders calling at homes until the boys came.","Close by ranking the reasons: attraction explains the growth to 1936, the law explains the jump after it, and compulsion explains the last and largest rise, so the size of the Hitler Youth by 1939 measures the state's power as much as its appeal."],"cues":["How many different kinds of reason does Level 4 want to see?","What could a fourteen-year-old get from the Hitler Youth that his family could not give him?","What did the 1936 law do to the other clubs a boy might have joined?","Which of the room's sources shows compulsion working in one town?","What must the last sentence do that the other paragraphs have not?"]},{"title":"Question 3(a): judging Sources B and C for the enquiry the question names","setup":"Source B in this room is a confidential report by a Trustee of Labour for an industrial district of western Germany to the Reich Ministry of Labour, October 1938: the demand for skilled men exceeds the supply, firms on state contracts offer bonuses and better canteens although the fixed wage rates are unchanged, men leave one site for another without notice, employers must hold and endorse the workbook, the working day at larger plants is commonly ten hours and longer on rearmament work, the commonest complaint is the price of fats and meat, Monday sick leave requests have risen, and 'of open trouble there is none', because the works have Beauty of Labour washrooms, the men take the cheap trips, and 'those who talked in the old way are known to us'. Source C is a letter from a machine fitter in Hamburg to his brother in the United States, December 1938: in work since 1934, fifty-six hours at the bench last week, overtime 'is not asked for, it is told to you', the packet heavier than in 1932 but the money buys less butter, 'no union to take it to and no stopping work', the workbook with the firm, a works trip to the mountains for eight days, five marks a week into the car book with no car yet, and in November the synagogue by the canal burned and the Katz family's shop smashed. The question is: 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the control the Nazi regime had over the working lives of German men in the later 1930s? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context.' (8 marks)","steps":["Copy the enquiry out and turn it into a test: anything in either source that shows a man being prevented from acting as he chose at work is on target, and anything else is background.","Source B, content first: the workbook the employer must hold and endorse, the wage rates fixed by the Trustee himself, and the closing line that men who talked in the old way are known to the authorities, all name the machinery of control directly.","Source B, provenance: a confidential report by the official who fixes the wages, written for his ministry and not for the public, so he has no reason to advertise contentment and every reason to be accurate about difficulties, which makes his admission that men move without notice unusually valuable.","Source C, content and provenance together: the fitter tells his brother abroad that overtime 'is not asked for, it is told to you' and that there is 'no union to take it to', which is control described from underneath by a man safely writing outside the country's censorship, though he speaks for one household only.","Bring in context to interpret rather than to decorate: unions were abolished in May 1933 and replaced by the Labour Front, strikes were illegal and the workbook dates from February 1935, so both sources are describing a system that was already five years old.","Judge comparatively: Source B is the more useful for the machinery of control and Source C for how it felt to live inside it, and together they show control resting on law and surveillance at once, though neither reaches the farms or the women's Duty Year."],"cues":["What exactly is the enquiry, and what would count as evidence for it?","Which three details in Source B are instruments of control rather than complaints?","Who was meant to read Source B, and what does that do to its reliability?","Why can the fitter afford to write plainly about overtime and unions?","Which facts from 1933 and 1935 explain what both men take for granted?","What must the last sentence of the answer do?"]},{"title":"Question 3(b): a difference of view, not a difference of detail","setup":"Interpretation 1 in this room, from a study of working life in Nazi Germany by a historian writing in the 1990s, argues that for the great majority of Germans who were not the regime's victims the 1930s were a plain improvement on the 1920s: the dole queues of 1932 emptied, the Labour Front gave a man a better-lit workshop, cheap theatre seats and a week in the mountains, it honoured the mother at home with a medal and gave her sons 'a uniform, a camp and a bugle', and the grumbling was about the price of fats and the length of the shift, not about the Führer. Interpretation 2, from a general history of everyday life in the Third Reich by a historian writing in the 2010s, argues that the gains were 'thinner than they looked' and were paid for twice over: the union, the right to strike and the right to leave a job went, hours lengthened while hourly rates stayed near slump levels, the saved-for car never came, 'families were given medals and lost their children to the movement', women trained as lawyers and doctors were sent back to the kitchen, and the national community was defined by the people it shut out. The question is: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. They give different views about what Nazi rule meant for German women and young people. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations.' (4 marks)","steps":["Reduce each interpretation to one sentence on the named focus only: what did Nazi rule do to mothers and children? Interpretation 1 says it rewarded and equipped them; Interpretation 2 says it took them over.","Notice that the two writers use the same fact, the medal for mothers, and draw opposite conclusions from it, which is the surest sign that the difference is one of view rather than of detail.","State the difference in a single opening sentence, then support each half from its own interpretation: the uniform, the camp and the bugle on one side, the families who 'lost their children to the movement' and the women lawyers and doctors sent back to the kitchen on the other."],"cues":["What does each interpretation say happened to mothers and to children?","Which single fact appears in both, and what does each writer do with it?","Which phrase from each interpretation will you quote to support your one difference?"]},{"title":"Question 3(c): one reason why the two interpretations differ","setup":"The two interpretations are the ones described above: Interpretation 1 is from a study of working life in Nazi Germany by a historian writing in the 1990s and holds that Germans who were not the regime's victims were plainly better off, with the Labour Front's workshops, theatre seats and mountain weeks, medals for mothers and uniforms for their sons; Interpretation 2 is from a general history of everyday life in the Third Reich by a historian writing in the 2010s and holds that the gains were thin, that families lost their children to the movement and trained women were sent home, and that the national community was defined by those it shut out, Jewish Germans, Roma and Sinti, the disabled and the men arrested under Paragraph 175. Source B in this room is the Trustee of Labour's confidential report of October 1938 about bonuses, workbooks, ten-hour days, Beauty of Labour washrooms and cheap trips; Source C is the Hamburg fitter's letter of December 1938 about fifty-six hours, the car book, the mountain holiday and the synagogue burned in November. The question is: 'Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about what Nazi rule meant for German women and young people. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer.' (4 marks)","steps":["Look at what each book is a study of, because that decides which people the writer is counting: one is a study of working life, the other a general history of everyday life.","Draw the consequence: a study of working life mostly follows people in work and in the movement, the group whose evidence looks like Source B's district of full order books, washrooms and cheap trips.","Draw the opposite consequence: a general history of everyday life must also count the households outside the community, so its evidence includes what closes Source C, the synagogue by the canal burned and the Katz family's shop smashed while the fitter said nothing.","Write it as one explained reason, not two summaries: because the writers are counting different populations, the same decade produces a record of gains for one and a record of losses for the other."],"cues":["What kind of book is each interpretation taken from?","Which Germans does a study of working life naturally follow?","Which lines of Source C would a general history of everyday life have to include?","How do you turn the observation into one reason rather than two descriptions?"]},{"title":"Question 3(d): evaluating Interpretation 2 with Interpretation 1 and your own knowledge","setup":"The question is: 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the impact of Nazi rule on the lives of German women in the years 1933-39? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]. Interpretation 2 says the gains of the 1930s were thinner than they looked, that families were given medals and lost their children to the movement, and that women trained as lawyers and doctors were sent back to the kitchen. Interpretation 1 says the Labour Front honoured the mother at home with a medal and gave her sons a uniform, a camp and a bugle, and that grumbling was about prices and shifts rather than about the Führer. Facts available: the marriage loan of June 1933 of 1,000 Reichsmarks with a quarter cancelled per child and about 700,000 couples taking one in four years; the Mother's Cross of December 1938, first awarded 21 May 1939; married women doctors and civil servants dismissed from 1933 and women barred from being judges or public prosecutors from 1936; women in work rising from about 4.85 million in 1933 to about 7.14 million in 1939; the compulsory Duty Year for young women from 1938; Lebensborn from December 1935; and the sterilisation law of July 1933.","steps":["Name the criterion in the opening instead of promising a balanced answer: judge the interpretation by what happened to women's choices, not by what the regime gave them.","Build the case for Interpretation 2 from the professions: married women doctors and civil servants were dismissed from 1933 and women could not be judges or public prosecutors from 1936, which is the claim about lawyers and doctors being sent back to the kitchen, exactly evidenced.","Strengthen it with the price attached to the rewards: the 1,000-mark loan of June 1933 was paid on condition the wife left her job, so the medal and the loan bought the very choice they rewarded her for giving up.","Put the case for Interpretation 1 honestly: for a married woman in a household that had lost its wage in 1932, a husband back in work, a loan that shrank with each child and a public medal in 1939 were real gains, and many women said so.","Then use the fact that neither interpretation mentions: the number of women in work rose to about 7.14 million by 1939 and the Duty Year of 1938 conscripted young women, so rearmament pulled women back into employment while the propaganda still sent them home.","Judge on the criterion announced: Interpretation 2 is right that women lost the choices Weimar had opened to them, and right about the trained professions, but it understates how much the regime needed women's labour by 1939, so the picture is not simply of women sent back to the kitchen but of women moved about at the state's convenience."],"cues":["What test will you judge Interpretation 2 by, and where must you say so?","Which two dated measures prove the claim about lawyers and doctors?","What condition was attached to the marriage loan?","What would a woman whose husband was unemployed in 1932 have said in 1936?","Which figure do both interpretations leave out?","What does the conclusion have to do beyond saying both are partly right?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What condition was attached to the 1,000-Reichsmark marriage loan of June 1933, and how was the loan cleared?","a":"The wife had to give up her job; a quarter of the loan was cancelled for each child born, so a couple with four children repaid nothing."},{"q":"Why does the jump in Hitler Youth membership between 1936 and 1939 matter to an argument about how popular the movement was?","a":"Because the law of 1 December 1936 made it the state youth organisation and the decree of March 1939 made service compulsory, so growth from about 5.4 to about 8.7 million measures compulsion as much as enthusiasm."},{"q":"Name three groups whose unemployment did not appear in the Nazi figures, and say why that matters.","a":"Jews pushed out of work, women who gave up jobs, and conscripts, Labour Service men and prisoners in the camps. It matters because part of the fall from six million to under 300,000 was a change in counting, not in work."},{"q":"Give one piece of evidence that German workers gained under Nazi rule and one that they lost, both from the later 1930s.","a":"Gain: about 10.3 million people took Strength Through Joy holidays in 1938, against about 2.3 million in 1934. Loss: the working week lengthened from about 43 hours in 1933 to about 47 in 1939 while hourly rates stayed near slump levels, and unions and strikes had gone in May 1933."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 3 Section A (16 marks): Question 1 prints Source A and asks 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about...' in a table with two boxes for each inference (4 marks); Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b), and you answer one: each is 'Explain why...' with two stimulus points and 'You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks).","Paper 3 Section B (36 marks), all Question 3: (a) 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into...?' (8); (b) 'What is the main difference between these views?' (4); (c) 'Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about...' (4); (d) 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about...?' (16, plus up to 4 marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology).","The stimulus-point caps on the 12-mark 'Explain why...': 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'. There is no cap at Level 2. Question 3(d) prints no stimulus points, so no cap applies to it.","Timing on Paper 3: 1 hour 30 minutes for 52 marks, so roughly a minute a mark plus thinking time. About 5 minutes on Question 1, 18 on Question 2, 12 on 3(a), 6 each on 3(b) and 3(c), and about 40 minutes on 3(d) including the final check that earns the SPaG marks.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose, and usefulness is always judged for the enquiry the question names, never in general. Contextual knowledge earns nothing unless it is used on the sources or on the interpretations.","The dates that anchor this key topic: the marriage loan and the civil service law, 1933; the compulsory Labour Service and the Nuremberg Laws, 1935; the Hitler Youth law and the Four-Year Plan, 1936; the service decree, March 1939; and 'Kristallnacht', 9 to 10 November 1938."],"questions":[{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about the work of the Hitler Youth in the months before the war. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for each valid inference, up to two, with a second mark each for supporting detail taken from Source A. Credit military preparation, the claim on boys' free time, or the use of compulsion.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: much of the Hitler Youth's work was preparing boys for military service. Details in the source that tell me this: the rifle range at the gasworks 'is in use three afternoons a week', and every second Sunday there is 'a route march with map work'. (ii) What I can infer: the Hitler Youth claimed almost all of a boy's free time. Details in the source that tell me this: Tuesday evenings, Saturdays and alternate Sundays are all taken, and the Whitsun camp kept the older companies under canvas for eight days.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["An inference is something the source makes certain without stating it; ask what a boy's week actually contained.","Read the report once for what the boys are made to do and once for when they are made to do it.","Give each answer in two parts: a claim the report never makes, then the exact words that force it."],"codes":["31-4 — 2 Nazi policies towards the young: Nazi aims and policies towards the young"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about how young Germans responded to the Hitler Youth. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark per valid inference, up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail from Source A. Credit reluctance and the need for compulsion, or the preference for physical activity over ideological instruction.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: not all boys joined willingly. Details in the source that tell me this: since the service order of March, the town clerk sends the Bann 'the names of boys who have not reported', leaders call at their homes, and boys who still stay away are handed to the youth office. (ii) What I can infer: boys enjoyed the physical side far more than the political side. Details in the source that tell me this: 'the boys queue' for the rifle range, and the older ones attend the shooting and field exercises 'far better than the evening classes'.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The writer wants to look successful, so the things he admits are the things worth using.","Look separately at how boys arrive in the organisation and at what they do once they are in it.","Pair each claim with the words that force it, and make sure both claims are about the boys, not about the leaders."],"codes":["31-4 — 2 Nazi policies towards the young: Nazi aims and policies towards the young"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. They give different views about how far Nazi rule improved the lives of ordinary Germans in the years 1933-39. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4) needs a key difference of view supported from both: Interpretation 1 holds that work and rewards brought genuine improvement and consent, Interpretation 2 that the gains were thin and bought at the cost of freedom and of the excluded. Surface detail only stays in Level 1.","sol":"The main difference is over whether what Germans received in the 1930s was a real gain. Interpretation 1 says it was: the dole queues of 1932 'emptied', a man 'could keep his family again', and the Labour Front supplied a better-lit workshop, cheap theatre seats and 'a week in the mountains', so that 'a government that restores work and order earns consent'. Interpretation 2 says the same gains were 'thinner than they looked' and 'paid for twice over', because the union, the right to strike and the right to leave a job went with them, the promised car 'was never delivered', and the national community was 'defined by the people it shut out'.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Write one sentence for each interpretation beginning 'Nazi rule made ordinary Germans...' and compare them.","Both writers accept the same events; look for what each says those events were worth.","State the single difference first, then support each half with words from its own interpretation."],"codes":["31-4 — 3 Employment and living standards: Changes in the standard of living","31-4 — 4 The persecution of minorities: Nazi racial beliefs and policies"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about how far Nazi rule improved the lives of ordinary Germans in the years 1933-39. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4) needs one reason explained and effectively substantiated: for example that the two historians are studying different populations and so weigh different evidence.","sol":"One reason is that the two writers are counting different people, so they weigh different evidence. Interpretation 1 is taken from a study of working life, and its first sentence limits it to Germans 'who were not the regime's victims'; its natural evidence is a district like the one in Source B, where firms compete for skilled men, the works have 'Beauty of Labour washrooms' and 'the men take the cheap trips'. Interpretation 2 comes from a general history of everyday life, which must also count the households the community shut out, so its evidence includes the end of Source C, where 'they burned the synagogue by the canal and smashed the Katz family's shop'.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Historians differ legitimately when they ask different questions; look at what each book is a study of.","Ask which Germans each writer would have been reading about, and which ones would rarely appear.","Name one reason and then show it at work in both interpretations, using a source to illustrate the kind of evidence involved."],"codes":["31-4 — 3 Employment and living standards: Changes in the standard of living","31-4 — 4 The persecution of minorities: The persecution of Jewish people"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the standard of living of German workers in the later 1930s? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): utility judged for this enquiry, with reasoning from nature, origin and purpose, and context used to interpret. Credit Source B on fixed wage rates, ten-hour days, food prices and Beauty of Labour, and Source C on hours, the shrinking value of the packet, the mountain holiday and the car book.","sol":"Source B is useful because the official who fixed the wages is describing what the wages bought. He reports that the rates he set 'have not been altered' while firms compete with bonuses, that the working day at larger plants 'is commonly ten hours, and on rearmament work longer', and that the commonest complaint is 'the price of fats and meat'. Its provenance strengthens this: a confidential report to his own ministry has no propaganda purpose, and an official admitting rising Monday sick notes is unlikely to be exaggerating discontent. My knowledge confirms the pattern, since the average week lengthened from about 43 hours in 1933 to about 47 in 1939 while hourly rates stayed near slump levels. Source C shows the same standard of living from inside a household. The fitter has been in work since 1934 and did 'fifty-six hours' in a week, the packet is 'heavier than in 1932' but 'buys less butter', and the family pays 'five marks into the car book every week, though nobody in the yard has seen a car yet' - which matches the Volkswagen scheme of 1938, in which no civilian ever received a car. As a private letter to a brother abroad it escapes censorship, though it is one family in one city. Together they are more useful than either alone, because B gives the structure of wages and hours and C gives what that felt like in a kitchen; neither covers the countryside or women's work.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Usefulness is always usefulness for something: write the enquiry out before you look at either source.","Take each source as content, then provenance, then the context that confirms or qualifies it, and compare only at the end.","Open on what Source B lets you conclude about wages and hours, and make the first sentence a judgement, not a description."],"codes":["31-4 — 3 Employment and living standards: Changes in the standard of living","31-4 — 3 Employment and living standards: Nazi policies to reduce unemployment"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into how far the Nazi regime had won the loyalty of German workers by 1939? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): utility judged for this enquiry with provenance reasoning and context. Credit the absence of open trouble and the surveillance line in Source B, and in Source C the grudging 'I would not say we are worse off' set beside 'I said nothing'.","sol":"Source B is useful because it distinguishes quiet from loyalty. The Trustee reports that 'of open trouble there is none', but immediately explains it by three things: the Beauty of Labour washrooms, the cheap trips, and the fact that 'those who talked in the old way are known to us'. For this enquiry the last clause is the most valuable sentence in either source, because it shows the calm resting partly on surveillance; my knowledge supports it, since unions were abolished in May 1933, strikes were illegal under the Labour Front, and the Gestapo relied on informers. The report's provenance makes it strong: it is confidential, written by the official responsible for the district to his ministry, so understating discontent would damage him. Source C is useful for the same question from below. The fitter is not a Nazi enthusiast - the overtime 'is not asked for, it is told to you', there is 'no union to take it to' - yet he writes 'I would not say we are worse off' and names the mountain holiday, which is consent of a limited, bought kind; about 10.3 million people took Strength Through Joy holidays in 1938. His last lines, that the synagogue burned and 'I said nothing, and neither did anybody else', show acceptance and fear together. Neither source reaches convinced Nazis or active opponents, so together they are most useful for the large middle group whose loyalty the enquiry is really about.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Loyalty and silence are not the same thing; decide which each source can actually show.","Ask of each source what its writer had to gain or lose by saying what he says.","Begin with the sentence in Source B that explains why there is no open trouble."],"codes":["31-4 — 3 Employment and living standards: Changes in the standard of living","31-4 — 4 The persecution of minorities: The persecution of Jewish people"]},{"q":"2(a) Explain why the Nazis tried to persuade German women to leave paid work and raise large families in the years 1933-39. You may use the following in your answer: • the Law for the Encouragement of Marriage (1933) • the Mother's Cross. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise support - the falling birth rate and racial policy, the need to clear jobs for men in 1933, the ideology of separate spheres, and the machinery of loans, medals, Lebensborn and closed professions. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The Nazis pressed women out of work and towards motherhood because population was a weapon, because jobs were scarce in 1933, and because the family was a way of binding people to the movement. The first reason is racial policy. The birth rate had been falling for a generation and hit its lowest point of the century in 1933, and a regime planning living space in the east treated that as a military problem. Hence the Law for the Encouragement of Marriage of June 1933: an interest-free loan of 1,000 Reichsmarks to 'Aryan' couples, a quarter written off for each child, so four children cleared the debt; about 700,000 couples took one in four years. Hence too the Cross of Honour of the German Mother, instituted in December 1938 and first awarded on 21 May 1939, in bronze, silver and gold, the gold for eight children or more, with Hitler Youth boys expected to salute the wearers. The second reason is unemployment. With about six million out of work in January 1933, every married woman who gave up a job released it for a man and removed herself from the figures, which is why the marriage loan was paid only if the wife stopped working, and why married women doctors and civil servants were dismissed from that year. The third reason, which neither bullet point prompts, is control. A woman at home with four children was reachable through the Nazi Women's League under Gertrud Scholtz-Klink, and her children were reachable through the Hitler Youth; Himmler's Lebensborn homes from December 1935 show how far the state was willing to go into private life. All three pulled the same way until rearmament pulled the other way: by 1939 about 7.14 million women were in work, more than in 1933.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what a falling birth rate looked like to a government already planning a war.","Plan three reasons of different kinds - racial, economic and political - so the answer analyses rather than lists.","Open on the birth rate and finish that paragraph with the loan and the medal as the policy that followed from it."],"codes":["31-4 — 1 Nazi policies towards women: Nazi views on women and the family","31-4 — 1 Nazi policies towards women: Nazi policies towards women","31-4 — 3 Employment and living standards: Nazi policies to reduce unemployment"]},{"q":"2(b) Explain why the Nazis wanted such close control of German young people in the years 1933-39. You may use the following in your answer: • the Hitler Youth • the school curriculum. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation with precise support - the next generation of soldiers and mothers, the displacing of family, church and rival youth groups, and the training of a future elite. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The Nazis wanted the young because the young were the regime's future army, because children could be used to reach adults the regime could not convert, and because the leadership of the next thirty years had to be bred somewhere. The first reason is war. Boys in the Hitler Youth did drill, map work, camping and small-bore shooting, and physical education in schools was expanded to as many as five periods a week; girls in the League of German Maidens were told their athletics and route marches were training for motherhood. A regime that reintroduced conscription in March 1935 and launched the Four-Year Plan in 1936 needed fit recruits in 1940, not in 1960. The second reason is that a schoolroom is the one place the state can reach a Catholic or a Social Democrat household. Teachers were purged under the civil service law of April 1933 and about 97 per cent had joined the National Socialist Teachers' League by 1937; race studies was added as a subject, biology taught heredity, history taught the stab in the back and the injustice of Versailles, and arithmetic problems counted the cost of caring for disabled people. A child taught this at school and drilled in the same doctrine on Tuesday evenings could correct his parents at supper. The third reason, beyond the bullet points, is elimination and elite-building at once: the law of 1 December 1936 made the Hitler Youth the state youth organisation, so church and scouting clubs disappeared and there was nowhere else for a boy to go, while the Napolas, sixteen of them by 1939, and the Adolf Hitler Schools founded in 1937 trained a chosen minority to run what the rest would obey. Control of the young, in short, was not one policy but the whole regime in miniature: recruit, isolate, indoctrinate, select.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the regime needed the children of 1933 to be by 1942.","Plan three different kinds of reason: military, ideological, and the removal of alternatives.","Begin with what boys and girls actually did in their organisations, and say what it was training for."],"codes":["31-4 — 2 Nazi policies towards the young: Nazi aims and policies towards the young","31-4 — 2 Nazi policies towards the young: Nazi control of the young through education"]},{"q":"2(a) Explain why unemployment in Germany fell in the years 1933-39. You may use the following in your answer: • the National Labour Service • rearmament. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation with precise support - public works and the autobahns, the Labour Service, conscription and rearmament under the Four-Year Plan, and invisible unemployment. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Registered unemployment fell from about six million in January 1933 to under 300,000 by 1939 for three real reasons and one arithmetical one. The first is public work paid for by the state. Hitler turned the first sod of the autobahn programme in September 1933; about 3,000 kilometres were open by the end of 1938, and at the peak in 1936 roughly 125,000 men were employed directly on it, with more in the cement, steel and machinery that fed it. Government contracts also rebuilt housing and public buildings, so money spent by the state turned into wages that were spent again. The second is the National Labour Service. Voluntary at first, it became compulsory on 26 June 1935 for men aged eighteen to twenty-five, who did six months of manual work in uniform for pocket money; that both took young men off the register and drilled them before they reached the army. The third, and much the largest after 1935, is rearmament. Conscription was reintroduced on 16 March 1935 and the armed forces grew from the 100,000 men the Treaty of Versailles allowed to well over a million by 1939, while the Four-Year Plan of September 1936 under Göring poured money into steel, synthetic oil and rubber; by 1938 a Trustee of Labour could report that skilled men were scarce. The fourth reason is one the posters never mentioned: the figures were counted differently. Jews driven from their jobs, women who gave up work, Labour Service men, conscripts and prisoners in the concentration camps did not appear as unemployed. The recovery was real but it was a war economy: the jobs existed because Germany was arming, and part of the fall was invisible unemployment rather than new work.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask who was paying for the new jobs, and what the money was buying.","Sort the causes into public works, military demand and changes in counting, and give each a paragraph.","Open with the autobahns and say what a state contract does to a local labour market."],"codes":["31-4 — 3 Employment and living standards: Nazi policies to reduce unemployment","31-4 — 3 Employment and living standards: Changes in the standard of living"]},{"q":"2(b) Explain why many German workers accepted Nazi rule in the years 1933-39. You may use the following in your answer: • Strength Through Joy • the German Labour Front. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation with precise support - the return of work after 1932, the rewards offered by the Labour Front, Strength Through Joy and Beauty of Labour, and the destruction of alternatives together with fear. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Most German workers accepted the regime because it gave them work first, rewards second, and left them no means of objecting third. The first reason is the memory of 1932. With about six million unemployed when Hitler took office, the return of a wage was worth more than the terms attached to it, and a man in work by 1935 compared his life with the dole queue rather than with an ideal. The second reason is the system of rewards run through the German Labour Front, set up on 10 May 1933 under Robert Ley to replace the trade unions abolished eight days earlier. Strength Through Joy sold theatre seats, sports, hikes, cruises and holidays: about 2.3 million people took its holidays in 1934 and about 10.3 million in 1938, and Beauty of Labour, run by Albert Speer, put washrooms, canteens and better lighting into factories. From 1938 workers could pay five marks a week towards a 990-mark Volkswagen, and more than 336,000 did, although no civilian ever received one. The third reason, which neither bullet point prompts, is that the alternatives had been destroyed and the risks were plain. Strikes were illegal, wage rates were fixed by government Trustees of Labour, and from February 1935 the workbook held by the employer controlled whether a man could change jobs at all; the Social Democratic and Communist organisations that had once carried a factory's grievances were broken in 1933 and their activists were in the camps. Acceptance of this kind is not the same as enthusiasm. It was a bargain in which security and cheap holidays were exchanged for hours that rose from about 43 a week in 1933 to about 47 in 1939, and for the right to argue.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what a man in work in 1936 was comparing his life with.","Separate what the regime gave, what it took away and what it made dangerous, and give each its own paragraph.","Open on 1932 and the return of the wage packet, not on the holidays."],"codes":["31-4 — 3 Employment and living standards: Changes in the standard of living","31-4 — 3 Employment and living standards: Nazi policies to reduce unemployment"]},{"q":"2(b) Explain why the persecution of Jewish people in Germany became more severe in the years 1933-39. You may use the following in your answer: • the Nuremberg Laws (1935) • 'Kristallnacht' (1938). You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation with precise support - antisemitism at the centre of Nazi ideology, the removal of restraints as the dictatorship consolidated, the pause for the 1936 Olympics, and the drive to seize Jewish property for rearmament. Caps: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Persecution escalated because the aim never changed while the restraints on it fell away one by one. The first reason is that antisemitism was the centre of Nazi belief, not a side effect of it. The one-day boycott of Jewish shops on 1 April 1933 and the Law for the Restoration of the Professional Civil Service of 7 April, which drove Jews out of state employment, came within weeks of Hitler taking power, so the direction was set from the start. The second reason is the consolidation of the dictatorship. In 1933 the regime still worried about foreign opinion, President Hindenburg and the army, which is why the boycott lasted a day; by the Nuremberg rally of 15 September 1935 Hindenburg was dead, conscription had been announced and the regime could pass the Reich Citizenship Law, reducing Jews to subjects, and the Law for the Protection of German Blood and German Honour, banning marriage and sexual relations between Jews and other Germans. That this was tactical rather than moral is shown by the pause during the Berlin Olympics of 1936, when signs came down for the visitors. The third reason, which the bullet points do not prompt, is money. Rearmament under the Four-Year Plan was expensive, and from 1938 Jewish property was registered, businesses were forced into 'Aryan' hands, and after the pogrom of 9 to 10 November 1938 - at least 91 dead, about 7,500 businesses wrecked, hundreds of synagogues burned and some 30,000 men sent to concentration camps - the Jewish community itself was fined one billion Reichsmarks for the damage done to it. Persecution therefore grew with the regime's confidence and its appetite: by September 1939 roughly 282,000 of the 523,000 Jews living in Germany in 1933 had emigrated, which was, until the war, exactly what the policy intended.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["The aim did not change between 1933 and 1938; ask what did.","Track the steps against what was constraining the regime at each date - foreign opinion, Hindenburg, the Olympics, the army.","Open with April 1933 and say why the boycott was called off after a single day."],"codes":["31-4 — 4 The persecution of minorities: The persecution of Jewish people","31-4 — 4 The persecution of minorities: Nazi racial beliefs and policies"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the benefits that Nazi rule brought to ordinary Germans in the years 1933-39? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views evaluated, how each conveys its view analysed, precise context, judgement justified. Credit for agreement the loss of unions, longer hours, the undelivered car and the excluded; against, the fall in unemployment, KdF and Beauty of Labour.","sol":"Interpretation 2 argues that the benefits of the 1930s were 'thinner than they looked' and 'paid for twice over': work returned but the union, the right to strike and the right to leave a job went with it, hours lengthened while hourly rates stayed near slump levels, the saved-for car 'was never delivered', and the national community was 'defined by the people it shut out'. The test I shall apply is what the benefits cost the people who received them, and who was allowed to receive them at all. On the cost, Interpretation 2 is strongly supported. Trade unions were abolished on 2 May 1933 and replaced on 10 May by the German Labour Front, strikes became illegal, wage rates were fixed by government Trustees of Labour, and from February 1935 the workbook held by the employer decided whether a man could change his job. The working week lengthened from about 43 hours in 1933 to about 47 in 1939, so although weekly earnings rose by roughly a fifth in real terms, much of the gain was extra hours rather than better pay. The Volkswagen scheme proves the interpretation's sharpest point: from 1938 more than 336,000 people paid five marks a week towards a 990-mark car, and no civilian ever received one. On who was included, Interpretation 2 is stronger still, because the benefits were racially rationed. The same state that gave a mother the Cross of Honour in 1939 had sterilised around 400,000 people under the law of July 1933, penned about 600 Roma and Sinti into the Marzahn camp before the 1936 Olympics, tightened Paragraph 175 in 1935, and in November 1938 burned the synagogues and fined the Jewish community a billion Reichsmarks. Interpretation 1, however, is not describing a fantasy. Registered unemployment fell from about six million in 1933 to under 300,000 by 1939; Strength Through Joy holidays went from about 2.3 million in 1934 to about 10.3 million in 1938; Beauty of Labour put washrooms and canteens into factories; the marriage loan of 1933 and the Mother's Cross were real money and real status. For a family that had lost its wage in 1932 these were benefits, and it is honest to say so. The two also convey their claims in different ways, and that is part of the argument. Interpretation 1 lists what was handed over - a uniform, a camp and a bugle - and treats the grumbling as domestic, about prices and shifts rather than about the Führer. Interpretation 2 uses the language of payment and exclusion, so that every gain arrives with a bill attached. I agree with Interpretation 2, but on the second of its grounds more than the first. The material benefits were not imaginary; what makes them thin is that they were conditional, on silence, on being 'Aryan' and healthy, and on a recovery built for war. Benefits that can be withdrawn from you because of who your grandparents were are not the same kind of thing as the wages a union has won, and that is the distinction Interpretation 1 does not make.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide what the word 'benefit' has to mean before you can judge whether the benefits were thin.","Use Interpretation 1 as your strongest case against, and answer it with dated evidence rather than with adjectives.","Open by putting Interpretation 2's claim in your own words and naming the criterion your judgement will use."],"codes":["31-4 — 3 Employment and living standards: Changes in the standard of living","31-4 — 4 The persecution of minorities: Nazi racial beliefs and policies","31-4 — 1 Nazi policies towards women: Nazi policies towards women"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the standard of living of German workers in the years 1933-39? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): evaluation of both views with precise context and a justified judgement. Credit hours, hourly rates, prices, the workbook and the Volkswagen scheme for agreement; full employment, KdF and Beauty of Labour against.","sol":"Interpretation 2 claims that the workers' gains were 'thinner than they looked': the union, the right to strike and the right to leave a job were lost, 'the working week lengthened while hourly rates stayed near the levels of the slump', and the car millions saved for never came. Standard of living, though, is not one thing, so I shall judge the claim on three measures in turn - security of work, what the wage bought, and the freedom to improve it. On security, Interpretation 1 has the better of the argument. Registered unemployment fell from about six million in January 1933 to under 300,000 by 1939, helped by the autobahn programme, which had some 3,000 kilometres open by the end of 1938 and employed roughly 125,000 men directly at its 1936 peak, by the Labour Service, compulsory from June 1935, and above all by rearmament after conscription returned in March 1935. For a household that had been on relief in 1932 this is the largest change of the decade, and Interpretation 1 is right that it earned the regime a great deal of goodwill. On what the wage bought, Interpretation 2 is nearer the truth. The average week lengthened from about 43 hours in 1933 to about 47 in 1939, so the roughly one-fifth rise in real weekly earnings over the period came mainly from time rather than from rates, which stayed close to depression levels, while food prices rose; the Volkswagen scheme of 1938 took five marks a week from more than 336,000 savers and delivered no civilian car at all. On freedom, Interpretation 2 is plainly right. Unions went on 2 May 1933 and the German Labour Front replaced them on 10 May; strikes were illegal, government Trustees of Labour fixed the rates, and from February 1935 the workbook stayed with the employer, so a man could not simply take a better offer. That matters for living standards and not only for politics, because it removed the means by which workers had previously raised their own wages. The interpretations convey their views differently: Interpretation 1 measures the decade against 1932 and counts what arrived - the workshop, the theatre seat, the week in the mountains - while Interpretation 2 measures it against what was surrendered and counts what was withheld. Both are describing the same worker. My judgement is that Interpretation 2 is right about the substance and Interpretation 1 about the feeling: measured in hours worked, hourly rates and bargaining power, the German worker of 1939 had gained little and lost much, yet measured against the dole queue he remembered, he had gained everything, which is precisely why so many accepted the bargain.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Break 'standard of living' into measures before you judge it; the two interpretations are using different ones.","Give each measure a paragraph that ends by saying which interpretation wins on that measure.","Open by stating Interpretation 2's claim in your own words and naming the measures you will test it against."],"codes":["31-4 — 3 Employment and living standards: Changes in the standard of living","31-4 — 3 Employment and living standards: Nazi policies to reduce unemployment"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the treatment of the people the Nazis shut out of their 'national community' in the years 1933-39? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): evaluation using both interpretations, precise context and a justified judgement. Credit the sterilisation law, Marzahn, Paragraph 175, the Nuremberg Laws and 'Kristallnacht'; weigh Interpretation 1's restriction to Germans who were not victims.","sol":"Interpretation 2 argues that the national community 'was defined by the people it shut out: Jewish Germans, Roma and Sinti, the disabled, the men arrested under Paragraph 175', and that consent of that kind 'rests on exclusion and on fear'. The question is whether exclusion was a by-product of Nazi rule or its organising principle, and that is the test I shall apply. The evidence for Interpretation 2 is very strong, and it begins early. The Law for the Prevention of Hereditarily Diseased Offspring of 14 July 1933 set up Hereditary Health Courts that ordered compulsory sterilisation for conditions from hereditary deafness to schizophrenia; around 400,000 people were sterilised under it, and school arithmetic lessons taught children what their care cost the state, which shows exclusion being taught rather than merely enforced. Paragraph 175 was tightened on 28 June 1935 so that far less evidence was needed to convict men of homosexual acts, and roughly 50,000 were convicted in the Nazi years. About 600 Roma and Sinti were penned into a camp at Marzahn on 16 July 1936, shortly before Berlin welcomed the Olympic Games. For Jewish Germans the sequence runs from the boycott of 1 April 1933 and the civil service law of 7 April, through the Nuremberg Laws of 15 September 1935, which took away citizenship and forbade marriage, to the pogrom of 9 to 10 November 1938, in which at least 91 people were killed, about 7,500 businesses were wrecked and some 30,000 men were sent to camps - after which the victims were fined a billion Reichsmarks. By September 1939 roughly 282,000 of the 523,000 Jews in Germany in 1933 had left. Interpretation 1 does not deny any of this; it simply writes about somebody else, and its first sentence, which limits it to 'Germans who were not the regime's victims', concedes the point while stepping round it. That is where the two differ in how they convey their views: Interpretation 1 makes exclusion a boundary of its subject, so the persecuted appear as a parenthesis, whereas Interpretation 2 makes the boundary itself the subject. The one qualification I would enter against Interpretation 2 is that its list can flatten differences that mattered at the time: sterilisation, a conviction under Paragraph 175 and the loss of citizenship were different fates, and before 1939 most of the excluded were persecuted rather than murdered. But on the main claim I agree with it. The benefits Interpretation 1 describes were rationed by race and by health, and a community that defines membership by ancestry has made exclusion its principle and not its side effect.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask whether exclusion was something the regime did as well as governing, or the way it governed.","Gather dated evidence for each excluded group separately before you write, so the paragraphs do not blur.","Open by quoting the claim in Interpretation 2 and naming the test you will judge it by."],"codes":["31-4 — 4 The persecution of minorities: Nazi racial beliefs and policies","31-4 — 4 The persecution of minorities: The persecution of Jewish people"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date were the Nuremberg Laws announced, and what were the two laws called?","a":"15 September 1935: the Reich Citizenship Law and the Law for the Protection of German Blood and German Honour."},{"q":"How large was the marriage loan offered from June 1933, and what happened to it when a child was born?","a":"1,000 Reichsmarks, interest free; a quarter was cancelled for each child, so a couple with four children repaid nothing."},{"q":"In which month and year did the Reich Labour Service become compulsory, for whom, and for how long?","a":"June 1935, for men aged eighteen to twenty-five, for six months."},{"q":"Which organisation replaced Germany's trade unions in May 1933, and who led it?","a":"The German Labour Front (Deutsche Arbeitsfront), led by Robert Ley."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The Law for the Encouragement of Marriage, June 1933","back":"Interest-free loan of 1,000 Reichsmarks to 'Aryan' newlyweds if the wife gave up her job. A quarter was cancelled per child, so four children cleared it. About 700,000 couples took one in four years.","code":"31-4 — 1 Nazi policies towards women: Nazi policies towards women","open":false},{"front":"The Cross of Honour of the German Mother","back":"Instituted December 1938, first awarded on Mother's Day, 21 May 1939. Bronze, silver and gold, the gold for eight or more children. Hitler Youth boys were expected to salute wearers.","code":"31-4 — 1 Nazi policies towards women: Nazi policies towards women","open":false},{"front":"Gertrud Scholtz-Klink","back":"Reich Women's Leader from 1934, head of the Nazi Women's League. Taught motherhood, cookery and household economy, and the ideal of the plainly dressed mother with plaited hair rather than the salaried Weimar woman.","code":"31-4 — 1 Nazi policies towards women: Nazi views on women and the family","open":false},{"front":"Lebensborn","back":"Founded by Himmler in December 1935. SS-run maternity homes where unmarried women judged racially valuable could bear and place children, part of the drive to raise the 'Aryan' birth rate.","code":"31-4 — 1 Nazi policies towards women: Nazi views on women and the family","open":false},{"front":"The Nuremberg Laws, 15 September 1935","back":"The Reich Citizenship Law made Jews subjects, not citizens. The Law for the Protection of German Blood and German Honour banned marriage and sexual relations between Jews and other Germans. A November decree defined a Jew by grandparents.","code":"31-4 — 4 The persecution of minorities: The persecution of Jewish people","open":false},{"front":"The divisions of the Nazi youth movement","back":"Boys: German Young People at ten, Hitler Youth at fourteen. Girls: Young Maidens at ten, League of German Maidens at fourteen, with Faith and Beauty from 1938 for ages seventeen to twenty-one.","code":"31-4 — 2 Nazi policies towards the young: Nazi aims and policies towards the young","open":false},{"front":"The Hitler Youth law of 1 December 1936 and the decree of March 1939","back":"The 1936 law made the Hitler Youth the state youth organisation and squeezed out rival clubs; the March 1939 decree made service compulsory. Membership: about 2.3 million in 1933, 5.4 million in 1936, 8.7 million in 1939.","code":"31-4 — 2 Nazi policies towards the young: Nazi aims and policies towards the young","open":false},{"front":"The League of German Maidens (Bund Deutscher Mädel)","back":"The girls' branch for ages fourteen to eighteen: athletics, route marches, camps, first aid and domestic science. Fitness was presented as training for motherhood, and camps gave many girls their first time away from home.","code":"31-4 — 2 Nazi policies towards the young: Nazi aims and policies towards the young","open":false},{"front":"Race studies and the Nazi curriculum","back":"Race studies was added as a school subject, biology taught heredity and eugenics, history taught the stab in the back and Versailles, geography taught living space, and arithmetic costed the care of disabled people.","code":"31-4 — 2 Nazi policies towards the young: Nazi control of the young through education","open":false},{"front":"Napolas and Adolf Hitler Schools","back":"Napolas (National Political Education Institutes) began in 1933 and numbered sixteen by 1939, training future officers and officials. The Adolf Hitler Schools, founded in 1937 by Robert Ley and Baldur von Schirach, fed the Order Castles.","code":"31-4 — 2 Nazi policies towards the young: Nazi control of the young through education","open":false},{"front":"The Reich Labour Service (RAD)","back":"Voluntary from 1933, compulsory from 26 June 1935 for men aged eighteen to twenty-five for six months: manual work in uniform and barracks for pocket money, which cut the register and prepared men for the army.","code":"31-4 — 3 Employment and living standards: Nazi policies to reduce unemployment","open":false},{"front":"Invisible unemployment","back":"The fall from about six million in 1933 to under 300,000 by 1939 partly reflected who was left out of the count: Jews driven from jobs, women who gave up work, Labour Service men, conscripts and prisoners in the camps.","code":"31-4 — 3 Employment and living standards: Nazi policies to reduce unemployment","open":false},{"front":"Strength Through Joy and Beauty of Labour","back":"KdF, founded November 1933, sold cheap theatre, sport, hikes and holidays: about 2.3 million holidaymakers in 1934 and 10.3 million in 1938. Beauty of Labour, under Albert Speer, added washrooms, canteens and lighting.","code":"31-4 — 3 Employment and living standards: Changes in the standard of living","open":false},{"front":"The Volkswagen savings scheme, 1938","back":"Workers paid five Reichsmarks a week in stamps towards a 990-mark car. More than 336,000 saved, the factory turned to war production, and no civilian ever received a vehicle.","code":"31-4 — 3 Employment and living standards: Changes in the standard of living","open":false},{"front":"The sterilisation law of 14 July 1933","back":"The Law for the Prevention of Hereditarily Diseased Offspring, in force from January 1934. Hereditary Health Courts ordered compulsory sterilisation for listed conditions; around 400,000 people were sterilised, most before the war.","code":"31-4 — 4 The persecution of minorities: Nazi racial beliefs and policies","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The sources and interpretations written for this room - read these before the questions","items":["Source A: From a report on the year's work written by the leader of a Hitler Youth district (Bann) in a Ruhr industrial town for the regional youth office, April 1939. Written for this room in the style of such a report; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — The Bann now counts every boy of ten to eighteen in this town except those the doctors have excused. Since the service order of March the town clerk sends us the names of boys who have not reported, and we call at their homes; nine out of ten come after the second visit, and the rest we hand to the youth office. Tuesday evenings are for the history of the movement and the racial question, Saturdays for sport, and every second Sunday there is a route march with map work. The small-bore rifle range at the gasworks is in use three afternoons a week and the boys queue for it. I must report that the older boys attend the shooting and the field exercises far better than the evening classes. Two headmasters have written to complain that boys arrive on Monday too tired to work. Our camp at Whitsun took the older companies under canvas for eight days.","Source B: From a confidential report by a Trustee of Labour for an industrial district of western Germany, sent to the Reich Ministry of Labour, October 1938. Written for this room in the style of such a report; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — In this district the demand for skilled men now exceeds the supply, and firms holding state contracts offer bonuses, travel money and better canteens to attract them, although the wage rates I have fixed have not been altered. Men leave one site for another without notice, and I have had to remind employers that the workbook is to be held by the firm and endorsed. At the larger plants the working day is commonly ten hours, and on rearmament work longer. The complaint I hear most among the men is not the length of the day but the price of fats and meat, which the wife feels before the man does. Requests for sick leave on Mondays have risen again this autumn. Of open trouble there is none: the works have their Beauty of Labour washrooms, the men take the cheap trips, and those who talked in the old way are known to us.","Source C: From a letter written by a machine fitter in Hamburg to his elder brother, who had emigrated to the United States in 1930, December 1938. Written for this room in the style of a letter home; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — You ask whether things are better here than when you left. I have been in work since 1934 and there is as much of it as a man can stand; last week I was at the bench fifty-six hours, and the overtime is not asked for, it is told to you. The packet is heavier than in 1932, but Martha says the money buys less butter than it did, and boots for the children cost what they never used to. There is no union to take it to and no stopping work, and the workbook stays with the firm. Still, I would not say we are worse off. In August the works sent us to the mountains for eight days at a price I could never have found for myself, and Martha and I stick our five marks into the car book every week, though nobody in the yard has seen a car yet. In November they burned the synagogue by the canal and smashed the Katz family's shop. I said nothing, and neither did anybody else.","Interpretation 1: From a study of working life in Nazi Germany, by a historian writing in the 1990s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — For the great majority of Germans who were not the regime's victims, the 1930s were a plain improvement on the 1920s. The dole queues of 1932 emptied, and a man could keep his family again. The Labour Front gave him a cleaner and better-lit workshop, cheap seats at the theatre and a week in the mountains; it honoured the mother at home with a medal and gave her sons a uniform, a camp and a bugle. Grumbling there was, and plenty of it, but it was grumbling about the price of fats and the length of the shift, not about the Führer. A government that restores work and order earns consent, and by 1939 this one had it.","Interpretation 2: From a general history of everyday life in the Third Reich, by a historian writing in the 2010s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — The gains of the 1930s were thinner than they looked, and they were paid for twice over. Work came back, but with it went the union, the right to strike and the right to leave a job; the working week lengthened while hourly rates stayed near the levels of the slump, and the car that millions saved for was never delivered. Families were given medals and lost their children to the movement, and women trained as lawyers and doctors were sent back to the kitchen. Above all, the national community was defined by the people it shut out: Jewish Germans, Roma and Sinti, the disabled, the men arrested under Paragraph 175. Consent of that kind rests on exclusion and on fear.","How the room uses them: Source A is the inference source for Question 1. Sources B and C are the contemporary pair for the 8-mark usefulness question, 3(a), and may be used in 3(c). Interpretations 1 and 2 are the pair for 3(b), 3(c) and 3(d). Every quotation in the model answers, worked examples and plan of this room comes from the five texts above and from nowhere else."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph - Level 4 (Paper 3 Question 3(d), 16 marks)","items":["Question: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about why so many Germans accepted Nazi rule in the years 1933-39? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]. This is one paragraph from the middle of the answer, putting the case against Interpretation 2.","Interpretation 2 explains acceptance by 'exclusion and fear', but that cannot carry the whole weight, because a great deal of what the regime offered was accepted gladly and in public. Registered unemployment fell from about six million in January 1933 to under 300,000 by 1939, and for a household that had been on relief the return of a wage packet needed no policeman behind it. Strength Through Joy holidays rose from about 2.3 million in 1934 to about 10.3 million in 1938, and Beauty of Labour, run by Albert Speer under the Labour Front, put washrooms, canteens and better lighting into workshops where there had been none. Interpretation 1 is describing something real when it says the regime gave a man 'a cleaner and better-lit workshop, cheap seats at the theatre and a week in the mountains'. Fear does not explain why families queued for KdF places or why about 700,000 couples applied for a marriage loan in four years. What fear does explain is the silence that surrounded these gains: the same workers who took the trips had lost their unions in May 1933 and knew, as the Trustee of Labour's report puts it, that those who talked in the old way were known to the authorities. Acceptance was therefore produced by reward and enforced by fear together, which is a stronger explanation than either interpretation gives on its own.","Why this is Level 4: it 'provides an explained evaluation reviewing the alternative views in coming to a substantiated judgement' - Interpretation 1's case is built properly before being measured against Interpretation 2's. The analysis is precise about how the difference of view is conveyed (reward counted in one, payment and exclusion in the other), and the contextual knowledge is precisely selected rather than general: the unemployment figures, the KdF numbers for 1934 and 1938, the date unions were abolished. The line of reasoning is sustained and the final sentence judges rather than summarises."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph - Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same as above - 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about why so many Germans accepted Nazi rule in the years 1933-39? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks].","Interpretation 2 says people accepted Nazi rule because of exclusion and fear, and I partly agree with this. The Nazis were very frightening and people who spoke out could be arrested, so many Germans just kept quiet. However, Interpretation 1 says that the regime gave people work and holidays and that they were better off than in the 1920s, which is also true because unemployment went down a lot and workers got cheap trips through Strength Through Joy. The Nazis also treated Jewish people terribly, which shows Interpretation 2 has a point. Overall I think both interpretations are partly right, because the Nazis used fear but they also gave people things.","Why this is Level 2 and not Level 3: it does offer 'valid evaluative comment to agree with or counter the interpretation' and it does take details from both, which is what Level 2 asks. What it lacks is Level 3's 'relevant contextual knowledge used directly to support the evaluation': 'unemployment went down a lot' and 'cheap trips' stand where the figures should be - about six million to under 300,000, and 2.3 million KdF holidaymakers in 1934 against 10.3 million in 1938 - and no date is given for anything. It also never shows how each interpretation conveys its view, and the judgement is announced in the last sentence rather than reasoned towards, so 'its justification is insecure or undeveloped and a line of reasoning is not sustained'. To reach Level 3, replace every general phrase with a dated fact that does evaluative work."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Nazi policies towards women, 1933-39","items":["Ideal: Kinder, Küche, Kirche - children, kitchen, church. Women were to bear and raise racially valuable children for the national community.","The birth rate had been falling for a generation and reached its lowest point of the century in 1933, which the regime treated as a racial emergency.","Law for the Encouragement of Marriage, June 1933: interest-free loan of 1,000 Reichsmarks to 'Aryan' couples, conditional on the wife giving up work; a quarter cancelled per child.","About 700,000 couples took a marriage loan in the scheme's first four years.","Married women doctors and civil servants were dismissed from 1933; from 1936 women could not serve as judges or public prosecutors.","Gertrud Scholtz-Klink was Reich Women's Leader from 1934, heading the Nazi Women's League (NS-Frauenschaft) and the German Women's Enterprise.","Appearance: women were discouraged from make-up, dyed hair, trousers, slimming and smoking, and encouraged to wear their hair in a bun or plaits.","Lebensborn, founded by Himmler in December 1935, ran maternity homes for unmarried mothers of children judged racially valuable.","The Cross of Honour of the German Mother was instituted in December 1938 and first awarded on 21 May 1939: bronze, silver and gold, the gold for eight children or more.","Contradiction: the number of women in work rose from about 4.85 million in 1933 to about 7.14 million in 1939, and from 1938 a compulsory Duty Year sent young women into farms and households."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Hitler Youth and the League of German Maidens (the named case study)","items":["Boys: German Young People (Deutsches Jungvolk) from ten, Hitler Youth (Hitlerjugend) from fourteen to eighteen.","Girls: Young Maidens from ten, League of German Maidens (Bund Deutscher Mädel) from fourteen to eighteen; Faith and Beauty was added in 1938 for ages seventeen to twenty-one.","Boys' activities: drill, camping, map-reading, route marches, small-bore shooting, Nazi history and racial doctrine.","Girls' activities: athletics, gymnastics, route marches, camps, first aid and domestic science, presented as preparation for motherhood.","Baldur von Schirach was Reich Youth Leader from 1933; he also helped found the Adolf Hitler Schools in 1937.","The law of 1 December 1936 made the Hitler Youth the state youth organisation; rival church and scouting groups were absorbed or banned.","A decree of March 1939 made service in the Hitler Youth compulsory, and absentees could be reported to the authorities.","Membership: about 2.3 million in 1933, about 5.4 million in 1936, about 8.7 million in 1939.","Limits: by the late 1930s older members complained of boredom and endless drill, and attendance had to be enforced in some districts.","Consequences: schoolwork suffered where the movement took evenings and weekends, and parents' authority was deliberately weakened."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: schools, teachers and the elite Nazi schools","items":["The Law for the Restoration of the Professional Civil Service, 7 April 1933, removed Jewish and politically unreliable teachers.","By 1937 about 97 per cent of teachers had joined the National Socialist Teachers' League; Bernhard Rust ran the Reich Education Ministry.","Race studies was introduced as a subject, including skull and nose measurement and lessons on 'Aryan' superiority.","Biology taught heredity and eugenics; history taught the stab-in-the-back myth and the injustice of Versailles; geography taught the need for living space.","Arithmetic problems costed the care of disabled people or the bomb-loads of aircraft, so ideology entered even neutral subjects.","Physical education expanded to as many as five periods a week; boys who failed at sport could be humiliated or removed.","Girls took domestic science, needlework and eugenics, and were prepared for motherhood rather than for university.","Napolas (National Political Education Institutes) began with three schools in 1933 and numbered sixteen by 1939, run on military lines.","The Adolf Hitler Schools were founded in 1937 by Robert Ley and Baldur von Schirach; the best products went on to the Order Castles.","Jewish pupils were segregated and then excluded from state schools altogether by November 1938."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: labour service, autobahns and rearmament (the named case study)","items":["Registered unemployment stood at about six million in January 1933 and under 300,000 by 1939.","Hitler turned the first sod of the autobahn programme in September 1933; about 3,000 kilometres were open by the end of 1938.","Autobahn employment peaked in 1936 at roughly 125,000 men working directly on construction, with a similar number supplying it.","The National Labour Service (Reichsarbeitsdienst) was voluntary from 1933 and compulsory from 26 June 1935 for men aged eighteen to twenty-five, for six months.","Labour Service men wore uniform, lived in camps, carried spades on parade and were paid pocket money, not wages.","Conscription was reintroduced on 16 March 1935, breaking the Treaty of Versailles, which had limited the army to 100,000 men.","The armed forces grew to well over a million by 1939, and every conscript was a man off the unemployment register.","The Four-Year Plan was announced at the Nuremberg rally in September 1936 and run by Hermann Göring, aiming at self-sufficiency in steel, synthetic oil and rubber.","Invisible unemployment: Jews pushed out of work, women who left jobs, Labour Service men, conscripts and camp prisoners were not counted.","By 1938 officials were reporting shortages of skilled men, so the recovery ended in a labour shortage driven by rearmament."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Labour Front, Strength Through Joy and Beauty of Labour (the named case study)","items":["Trade unions were abolished on 2 May 1933 and their funds and offices seized.","The German Labour Front (Deutsche Arbeitsfront) was set up on 10 May 1933 under Robert Ley; every worker paid dues to it.","Strikes were illegal, and wage rates were fixed by government-appointed Trustees of Labour.","From February 1935 every worker had a workbook, held by the employer, which controlled movement between jobs.","Strength Through Joy (Kraft durch Freude) was founded in November 1933 to fill workers' leisure time with approved activity.","KdF holidays: about 2.3 million people took one in 1934 and about 10.3 million in 1938; cruises and the huge Prora resort, never finished, were the showpieces.","Beauty of Labour was set up under the Labour Front at the end of 1933 and run by the architect Albert Speer, adding washrooms, canteens, lighting and sports grounds.","Workers often carried out Beauty of Labour improvements in their own unpaid time.","The Volkswagen scheme from 1938: five Reichsmarks a week in stamps towards a 990-mark car; more than 336,000 saved and no civilian ever received one.","Hours and pay: the average working week lengthened from about 43 hours in 1933 to about 47 in 1939, and real weekly earnings rose by roughly a fifth, mostly through extra hours while hourly rates stayed near slump levels."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the boycott of 1933, the Nuremberg Laws and 'Kristallnacht' (the named case study)","items":["1 April 1933: a one-day national boycott of Jewish shops and businesses, with SA men posted outside and stars daubed on windows.","7 April 1933: the Law for the Restoration of the Professional Civil Service removed Jews from the civil service, schools and universities.","15 September 1935, at the Nuremberg rally: the Reich Citizenship Law made Jews subjects rather than citizens.","The Law for the Protection of German Blood and German Honour, the same day, banned marriage and sexual relations between Jews and other Germans.","A decree of November 1935 defined who counted as a Jew by the religion of grandparents, not by personal belief.","Persecution eased visibly during the Berlin Olympics of 1936, when signs were taken down for foreign visitors.","1938: Jewish property had to be registered, passports were stamped with a J, the names Israel and Sara were imposed, and Jewish doctors and lawyers lost their right to practise.","Herschel Grynszpan shot the German diplomat Ernst vom Rath in Paris in early November 1938, giving Goebbels the pretext for the pogrom.","'Kristallnacht', 9 to 10 November 1938: at least 91 Jews killed, about 7,500 businesses wrecked, hundreds of synagogues burned and some 30,000 Jewish men sent to concentration camps.","On 12 November 1938 the Jewish community was fined one billion Reichsmarks for the damage done to it, and insurance payments were confiscated.","Emigration: of about 523,000 Jews in Germany in January 1933, roughly 282,000 had emigrated by September 1939; the Evian conference of July 1938 found few countries willing to take them."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the other groups Nazi racial policy targeted","items":["The Volksgemeinschaft, or national community, was a racial idea: healthy 'Aryan' Germans inside, everyone else a burden or a threat.","Slavs were classed as inferior peoples whose lands in the east were wanted for German living space.","The Law for the Prevention of Hereditarily Diseased Offspring, 14 July 1933, in force from January 1934, allowed compulsory sterilisation on the order of Hereditary Health Courts.","Listed conditions included hereditary blindness and deafness, schizophrenia, epilepsy, Huntington's disease, severe physical deformity and 'feeble-mindedness'.","Around 400,000 people were sterilised under the law during the Nazi years, most of them before the war.","About 600 Roma and Sinti were rounded up into a camp at Marzahn near Berlin on 16 July 1936, shortly before the Olympic Games opened.","Himmler issued a decree on 'the struggle against the Gypsy plague' in December 1938, ordering racial registration of Roma and Sinti.","Paragraph 175 of the criminal code was tightened on 28 June 1935, widening what counted as a homosexual offence; roughly 50,000 men were convicted in the Nazi years.","Men imprisoned in the camps under Paragraph 175 wore a pink triangle, and Himmler set up a Reich central office against homosexuality and abortion in 1936.","People labelled 'asocial' or 'work-shy' - beggars, alcoholics, the long-term unemployed - were swept up in mass arrests in 1938 and sent to camps."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the source and interpretation moves this paper rewards","items":["Inference: state something the source does not say but that must be true if the source is true, then give the words that force it. One inference and one supporting detail in each box.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose: what kind of document it is, who made it and when, and why it was made.","Utility: judge each source for the enquiry the question names, not in general - content first, then how nature, origin and purpose affect that content, then the context that confirms or qualifies it.","Contextual knowledge scores only when used on the source; a paragraph about the period that never touches the sources earns nothing.","'Biased' is not a judgement. Say whose view the source gives, why it gives that view, and what it is therefore good evidence of: a one-sided source is excellent evidence of the view it takes.","A confidential official report is often more useful than a public one, because its writer has no propaganda purpose and may have to admit awkward facts.","Difference of view (3(b)): a disagreement about the past, not a difference of wording or of examples. State one difference, then support it from each interpretation.","Reason for difference (3(c)): interpretations differ because they ask different questions, study different groups, or weigh different evidence. Name which, and show it in both.","Differences based on conclusions drawn from evidence are legitimate; an interpretation is not wrong merely because another disagrees with it.","Evaluating an interpretation (3(d)): use both interpretations and your own knowledge, show how each conveys its view, and judge how far the named interpretation can be agreed with, saying on what criterion."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan - Paper 3 Question 3(d): 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about why so many Germans accepted Nazi rule in the years 1933-39?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","items":["The question as printed: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about why so many Germans accepted Nazi rule in the years 1933-39? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' This question prints no stimulus points: the two interpretations are the stimulus, both must be used, and an answer that evaluates one in isolation cannot reach the top levels.","Decode the statement. Interpretation 2 explains acceptance by what the regime took and by whom it shut out: the union, the strike and the right to leave a job went, the car never came, families 'lost their children to the movement', and 'consent of that kind rests on exclusion and on fear'. Interpretation 1 explains it by what the regime delivered: work, order, a better workshop, cheap theatre, a week in the mountains, a medal for the mother. The criterion to announce in the opening is whether acceptance was bought or enforced - and whether those are really alternatives.","Opening, three or four sentences: put Interpretation 2's claim in your own words, name Interpretation 1 as the rival weighting, state the criterion, and signal the position you will end at, so the line of reasoning is visible from the first paragraph.","Paragraph 1, for Interpretation 1 (the strongest case against Interpretation 2) - work. Registered unemployment fell from about six million in January 1933 to under 300,000 by 1939, through the autobahns (about 3,000 kilometres by the end of 1938, roughly 125,000 men employed directly at the 1936 peak), the Labour Service compulsory from June 1935, and rearmament after conscription returned in March 1935. Interim judgement: a wage packet is not extracted by fear, and this alone would produce a great deal of consent.","Paragraph 2, still against Interpretation 2 - rewards. The Labour Front from 10 May 1933 delivered Strength Through Joy (about 2.3 million holidaymakers in 1934, about 10.3 million in 1938) and Beauty of Labour under Albert Speer; the marriage loan of 1933 and the Mother's Cross of 1938 rewarded families. Interim judgement: people queued for these, which fear does not explain.","Paragraph 3, for Interpretation 2 - what was taken. Unions abolished 2 May 1933, strikes illegal, wage rates set by Trustees of Labour, the workbook with the employer from February 1935, and the Volkswagen scheme that took five marks a week from more than 336,000 savers and delivered no car. Interim judgement: acceptance was cheap for the regime because there was no lawful way to refuse.","Paragraph 4, for Interpretation 2 - fear and exclusion, its strongest ground. The Gestapo and informers, opponents in the camps, the sterilisation law of July 1933, Marzahn in July 1936, Paragraph 175 tightened in 1935, and the pogrom of 9 to 10 November 1938 followed by a billion-mark fine on the victims. Interim judgement: the silence after Kristallnacht, when neighbours watched and said nothing, is consent of a different and darker kind.","Paragraph 5, the point that goes furthest beyond both texts - the two mechanisms worked together. The same worker took the cheap trip and knew that men who talked in the old way were known to the authorities; reward made acceptance comfortable and fear made refusal unthinkable, so asking which one produced consent is the wrong question.","The evaluation thread that must run through every paragraph: how each interpretation conveys its view. Interpretation 1 counts what arrived and treats complaint as domestic grumbling 'about the price of fats and the length of the shift, not about the Führer'; Interpretation 2 counts what was surrendered and who was shut out, so every gain arrives with a bill. Saying this, and using it, is what the top level means by showing how the differences of view are conveyed.","What the conclusion must say: not 'both are partly right'. Apply the criterion announced at the start - that Interpretation 2 is right about the conditions attached to consent and about the people excluded from it, but wrong if it implies that Germans accepted the regime mainly because they were frightened, since the recovery was real and was welcomed. Then name the deciding consideration: consent that could be withdrawn from you for your grandparents' religion was never the free consent Interpretation 1 describes.","Timing and SPaG: about 40 minutes - six minutes to plan the paragraphs and fix the criterion, roughly thirty to write, and the last four to check spelling and punctuation and to make sure the specialist terms are present and correctly used, since 4 additional marks require 'a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate'. Useful terms here: Volksgemeinschaft, German Labour Front, Strength Through Joy, Beauty of Labour, Trustees of Labour, Reichsarbeitsdienst, Four-Year Plan, Nuremberg Laws, Gestapo, autarky, indoctrination, propaganda."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:31:28.571565+00:00","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Historically this is as clean a kit as I have judged: about forty-five dates, figures and attributions checked (marriage loan and the 700,000 couples, Mother's Cross 16 December 1938 / 21 May 1939 and its three classes, Lebensborn 12 December 1935, women's employment 4.85m-7.14m, Hitler Youth 2.3/5.4/8.7 million with the laws of 1 December 1936 and March 1939, sixteen Napolas by 1939, RAD compulsory 26 June 1935, conscription 16 March 1935, autobahn 3,000 km and 125,000 men, Four-Year Plan 1936, DAF 2/10 May 1933, KdF 2.3m/10.3m, Volkswagen 990 RM and 336,000 savers, 43 to 47 hours, sterilisation law 14 July 1933 and c.400,000, Marzahn 16 July 1936, Paragraph 175 on 28 June 1935, the boycott of 1 April 1933, the Nuremberg Laws of 15 September 1935, Kristallnacht with 91 dead, 7,500 businesses, 30,000 arrests and the billion-mark fine of 12 November, 523,000/282,000 emigration) and not one is wrong. The five written texts are properly flagged as invented and the interpretations are attributed only to 'a historian writing in the 1990s/2010s'; nothing touches the specimen paper's Option 31 questions on family life, the dictatorship of 1933-34 or Nazi growth 1929-32, and no worked example, model paragraph or plan sets a bank question. Stems, tariffs, AO splits, level labels, the June 2024 cap wording and the 1 hour 30 minutes timing all match levels.md. Fix the two cues and the Source A 'six years' rounding and it would publish as it stands."}}},"32-1":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"32-1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 1 is where Option 32 begins, and it is the part of the course Paper 3 draws on hardest, because everything after 1959 depends on a party that had already taken China, silenced its opponents and made Mao Zedong unanswerable. Section A regularly sets its source and its causation question here: how the Guomindang lost a war it began with four times the troops, how a party of soldiers and peasants turned itself into a government, and why the years 1950 to 1957 look to some historians like reform and to others like terror. Section B's interpretations on this option are almost always arguments about that last question. This room teaches the period as a chain of causes you can argue with, and it trains the five moves the paper rewards: inference from a source, causal explanation that goes past the printed bullet points, judgement of a source's usefulness for the enquiry actually named, spotting how two historians differ and why, and evaluating one of them against the other and against what you know.","idea":[{"code":"32-1 — 1 The Civil War, 1945–49: The relative strengths and weaknesses of the CCP and the Guomindang","h":"On paper the Guomindang should have won","t":"When full-scale fighting resumed in the summer of 1946, after the Chongqing talks of 1945 and the failure of the American mediation of 1946, Jiang Jieshi's Guomindang had about four million men under arms against roughly a million communists. It held the great cities, the tax revenue, the railways, the recognition of foreign governments and large quantities of American equipment. The CCP held a poor rural base around Yan'an in Shaanxi, a much smaller force renamed the People's Liberation Army in 1947, and almost no heavy weapons or aircraft. Its strengths were of a different kind: unity of command under Mao Zedong, soldiers drilled in the Three Rules of Discipline and Eight Points for Attention so that they paid for what they took, and a policy of dividing landlords' land among the peasants in the areas it controlled. A top-level answer never lists these strengths and weaknesses side by side; it says which of them could be converted into victory in a long war fought across the countryside, and which could not."},{"code":"32-1 — 1 The Civil War, 1945–49: Reasons for the success of Mao and the CCP","h":"How a smaller army won: leadership, tactics and the towns","t":"Mao and Zhu De refused battle until they had local superiority, drew Nationalist armies deep into the countryside, cut the railways between the cities and then destroyed isolated garrisons. Between September 1948 and January 1949 the communists took Manchuria, won the huge Huai-Hai campaign in the plains north of the Yangtze and entered Beiping when its commander surrendered the city rather than fight; Nanjing fell in April 1949 and Shanghai in May. The Guomindang defeated itself as much as it was defeated: conscripts were press-ganged and roped together on the march, officers sold the men's rice and medicine, and the gold yuan currency introduced in August 1948 lost almost all its value within months, so town dwellers and salaried officials who had welcomed the Nationalists in 1945 were ruined by 1948. Whole units surrendered with their American rifles. A top-level answer weighs three kinds of reason together, communist military skill, communist land policy in the villages, and Guomindang collapse in the cities, and says which one made the others decisive."},{"code":"32-1 — 2 Communist rule: The ideology and role of Mao","h":"Why Mao could not be argued with","t":"Mao Zedong proclaimed the People's Republic in Beijing on 1 October 1949, and his authority rested on three things. First, a record: he had taken the leading position in the party during the Long March of 1934 to 1935, at the Zunyi conference of January 1935, had held the party together at Yan'an, and had now won a war most observers expected him to lose. Second, an ideology with his name on it. Mao's version of Marxism made the peasants, not the industrial workers, the engine of revolution, insisted on the mass line and on continuous revolution, and was written into the party's constitution as Mao Zedong Thought at the Seventh Congress in 1945. Third, office: he was Chairman of the party, chairman of its military commission, and from the constitution of September 1954 Chairman of the People's Republic as well, so party, state and army met in one man. A top-level answer explains dominance as the three reinforcing each other, rather than describing Mao's personality."},{"code":"32-1 — 2 Communist rule: The role of the CCP in the government of China","h":"Mass membership and democratic centralism: the machine that reached every village","t":"The new state was improvised at first. The Chinese People's Political Consultative Conference of September 1949 adopted the Common Programme, which served as a provisional constitution and made room for small non-communist parties, and China was divided into six great administrative regions largely run by the People's Liberation Army until 1954. What held it all together was the party. Membership had been about four and a half million in 1949; the Eighth Congress of September 1956 reported 10.73 million, with branches in villages, factories, schools and army units. Those branches worked by democratic centralism: an issue could be argued out before a decision was taken, but once the centre had decided, the minority obeyed the majority and every lower body obeyed the one above it. That is how a decision made in Beijing could be enforced in a village a thousand miles away within weeks. A top-level answer uses democratic centralism to explain what the government could physically do, not merely to define a term."},{"code":"32-1 — 3 Consolidating the CCP's hold on power, 1951–52: The use of terror against opponents","h":"Terror as government: counter-revolutionaries, struggle meetings and the laogai","t":"The campaign against counter-revolutionaries, launched in October 1950 as Chinese troops entered Korea and at its height in 1951, went after Guomindang agents and soldiers left behind, bandits, secret societies and religious groups; targets for the number of executions were set from the centre and passed down to the provinces, sentences were announced at mass meetings, and figures reported internally in 1954 admitted around 712,000 executions and well over a million people sent to laogai, the camps of reform through labour. Thought reform, from 1951, put teachers, university staff and other intellectuals through study groups, public self-criticism and written confessions, so that educated opponents were broken without being shot. The method came from the countryside, where land reform under the Agrarian Reform Law of June 1950 was pushed through by speak bitterness meetings at which tenants stood up and accused their landlord in front of the village, and estimates of the number of landlords killed run from about one million to two million. A top-level answer makes terror do two jobs at once: it removed the CCP's real enemies, and by making ordinary people take part in the accusing it tied them to the new order and taught everyone what opposition would cost."},{"code":"32-1 — 3 Consolidating the CCP's hold on power, 1951–52: The 'three antis' and 'five antis' movements","h":"The 'three antis' and 'five antis': cleaning the party and breaking the businessmen","t":"Once the countryside was under control the CCP turned on the towns and on itself. The three antis movement, begun in December 1951, attacked corruption, waste and bureaucracy among party members, officials and managers of state enterprises, many of them recent recruits or officials inherited from the old regime. The five antis movement that followed from early 1952 attacked bribery, tax evasion, theft of state property, cheating on government contracts and the stealing of state economic information, and its targets were private businessmen. The method was the same as in the villages: mass meetings, employees encouraged to denounce their employers, confessions, fines and back taxes. The Korean War made it urgent, because the government needed money, and the result was that the private sector lost its independence and much of it passed under state direction well before the socialist transformation of industry in the mid-1950s. A top-level answer treats the two movements as one operation with two targets, and asks what each removed from the CCP's path."},{"code":"32-1 — 4 The Hundred Flowers campaign, 1956–57: Mao's reasons for the campaign","h":"Why Mao invited criticism in 1956","t":"The slogan let a hundred flowers bloom and a hundred schools of thought contend was raised in May 1956, and Mao had several reasons for it. The First Five-Year Plan of 1953 to 1957 needed engineers, scientists, teachers and managers, and the campaigns of 1951 and 1952 had frightened exactly those people into silence, so their skills were being wasted. Events abroad pressed as well: Khrushchev's secret speech to the Twentieth Congress of the Soviet Communist Party in February 1956 attacked Stalin's cult of personality and his terror, and the risings in Poland and Hungary later that year showed what happened when discontent had no outlet. Mao also thought the party had grown arrogant and bureaucratic since 1949 and that criticism from outside would discipline it; in February 1957 he argued in On the Correct Handling of Contradictions Among the People that disagreements among the people could be settled by discussion. A top-level answer ranks these reasons instead of listing them, and says which one Mao would have acted on alone."},{"code":"32-1 — 4 The Hundred Flowers campaign, 1956–57: Key features of the campaign","h":"What was actually said in the spring of 1957","t":"Very little was said at first, because people remembered thought reform, and the campaign only opened widely in the spring of 1957 after repeated official encouragement. When it opened, it went far past the complaints about bureaucracy that Mao had asked for. Critics attacked the privileges of party cadres, the practice of putting political reliability above ability, the party's control of the universities and the courts, and, in the sharpest cases, the CCP's monopoly of power itself; students at Peking University put up wall posters, and leaders of the small democratic parties asked for a real share in government. Some criticism came from inside the party. Within a few weeks the newspapers were carrying more hostile material than at any time since 1949, and Mao, who had expected grumbling about officials rather than an argument about one-party rule, ended the campaign in June 1957. A top-level answer keeps the distinction between the criticism Mao invited and the criticism he received, because everything that followed turns on it."},{"code":"32-1 — 4 The Hundred Flowers campaign, 1956–57: Results of the campaign","h":"The Anti-Rightist Campaign and what 1957 settled","t":"From June 1957 the Anti-Rightist Campaign, run by Deng Xiaoping, turned the invitation into a trap in effect if not in intention. The official figure for those labelled rightists is about 550,000, most of them educated people who had spoken in the spring; they lost their jobs, were sent to work in the countryside or to laogai camps, and most were not rehabilitated until after Mao's death. Mao said afterwards that the campaign had lured the snakes out of their holes, and historians still argue whether that was the plan from the start or a story told after an embarrassment. The results reached much further than the victims. China lost the skills it had wanted to use, criticism of the party became dangerous for twenty years, Mao's personal authority was restored after a public misjudgement, and nobody who had watched 1957 was going to object aloud to the Great Leap Forward launched in 1958. A top-level answer judges the campaign by those consequences, not by the number labelled."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1: two inferences from Source A about the Guomindang army in north China","setup":"Source A in this room is a report sent to the Guomindang high command in Nanjing by a Nationalist officer serving with an army in north China, November 1948. It opens by saying that he must set down plainly what this army can and cannot do, and it then reports that two regiments sent to relieve a besieged town went over to the communists with their American rifles and their guns, having been promised that they would be fed; that the pay handed out to the men is in banknotes that buy less at the end of a week than at the beginning; that officers sell the rice and the medicine meant for their men; that conscripts have to be roped together on the march; that the villagers will not sell his men grain and will not say where the enemy is, but carry the communists' wounded and mend their roads at night; and that his best divisions sit in the cities while the railways between them are cut. The question is: 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about the Guomindang's hold on north China in 1948. Complete the table below to explain your answer.' (4 marks)","steps":["Fix the enquiry word first, the hold on territory, and read for where the troops are and what lies between them rather than for how the soldiers feel.","Take the first inference from the last sentence: best divisions shut up in the cities with the railways between them cut means the Guomindang held points on a map and not the country around them, so its armies could be dealt with one at a time.","Take the second from the opening sentence: an officer who begins by saying that he must set down plainly what this army can and cannot do is writing to a high command whose orders assume more than the army can deliver.","Check that each of the two is an inference and not a restatement: the claim must be one the officer never makes, and the words you copy beside it must force it."],"cues":["Which sentences tell you where the army is, rather than how it feels?","What is left to an army that holds cities but no railways?","Why would an officer open a report by saying what his army cannot do?","Which half of each pair is the claim, and which the detail?"]},{"title":"Question 3(a): judging Sources B and C for the enquiry actually named","setup":"Source B in this room is a report to a county party committee by a land reform work team sent to a village in central China, summer 1951: the team lived in the village for six weeks and classified every household as landlord, rich peasant, middle peasant, poor peasant or labourer; at speak bitterness meetings the tenants accused the landlord of rents, of beatings and of a death; the land, the tools, the animals and the grain in the store were divided among 140 poor families; two men named as local despots were tried in front of the whole village and shot, and the rest were put under the supervision of the peasant association; and eleven young men have asked to join the party. Source C is a circular issued to the employees of factories and shops by the party committee of a large Chinese city during the five antis movement, spring 1952: every business is to be examined for the five poisons; employees are told they know their employers' books better than any inspector and are to write out what they have seen and put it in the boxes provided, and no one who makes a truthful accusation will be punished for it; an owner who confesses fully and pays what he owes may keep his business and go on serving the people, while one who conceals will be dealt with as an enemy of the people; and meetings will be held every evening until the accounts are clear. The question is: 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the rewards the CCP offered for co-operation during its campaigns in the years 1950 to 1952? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context.' (8 marks)","steps":["Write the enquiry out and let it select: what was on offer, so read Source B for what the village was given and Source C for what an employee and an owner were each promised, and leave the punishments aside except where they set the price of refusing.","Take Source B's content for this enquiry: the land, the tools, the animals and the grain in the store go to 140 poor families, and eleven young men then ask to join the party, so the reward on offer was property and a place in the new order together.","Weigh Source B's nature, origin and purpose for this enquiry in particular: a team writing to the county committee that sent it is being judged on six weeks' work, so it counts what it handed out and has every reason to get the 140 right, while the eleven applicants are exactly the detail it would most like its superiors to notice.","Turn to Source C, which makes two separate offers inside one firm: the employee is told that a truthful accusation will cost him nothing, and the owner that a full confession and payment of what he owes leaves him his business. As a circular it is evidence of what the party wanted believed, not of what anyone actually received.","Bring context onto the sources rather than beside them: the Agrarian Reform Law of June 1950 did transfer roughly two-fifths of China's cultivated land to about 300 million peasants, and party membership rose from about four and a half million in 1949 to the 10.73 million reported in 1956, so both of Source B's offers were real; in the cities the five antis ended in confessions, fines and back taxes rather than confiscation, and most firms went on trading until private industry passed into joint state-private ownership in the mid-1950s.","Judge for the enquiry: between them they show an offer of property and advancement in the village and an offer of survival in the firm, which is a real finding, but neither can show how long either offer was worth having, since the land was going into co-operatives within a few years and the circular says nothing of what became of those who wrote in the boxes."],"cues":["Is the enquiry about what was taken, or about what was given?","What does the village get, and what do eleven young men get?","Why would a work team be careful about the number 140?","Who are the two audiences of the circular, and what is each promised?","What happened afterwards to the land and to the firms?","What can neither source tell you about the worth of these offers?"]},{"title":"Question 3(b): a difference of view, not a difference of detail","setup":"Interpretation 1 in this room, from a study of the first decade of the People's Republic by a historian writing in the 1980s, holds that the communists kept China because millions had reason to want them to, and says of the countryside that land reform 'gave holdings to peasants who had never owned any'. Interpretation 2, from a general history of the People's Republic by a historian writing in the 2000s, holds that what secured communist rule was 'not gratitude but fear, carefully organised', and says of the same campaign that land reform 'was carried through by struggle meetings in which villagers were required to denounce their neighbours, and the killing was on a scale that left nobody in any doubt'. The question is: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. They give different views about land reform in the Chinese countryside. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations.' (4 marks)","steps":["Reduce each interpretation to one sentence about land reform itself: for the first it is a transfer of property to people who had never had any, for the second it is a campaign of violence whose scale was the point of it.","Test that this is a difference of view and not of fact: neither writer denies that land changed hands or that people were killed, so the disagreement is over which of the two was the essence of the campaign.","Anchor each half in words taken from its own interpretation, land reform that 'gave holdings to peasants who had never owned any' against land reform in which 'the killing was on a scale that left nobody in any doubt'.","Put the difference itself in the opening sentence and then give each half its own support, instead of describing one interpretation and then the other."],"cues":["What does each writer think land reform essentially was?","Do they disagree about what happened, or about what mattered?","Which words in each carry the view?","Where in the answer should the difference itself appear?"]},{"title":"Question 2: getting a 12-mark 'Explain why' past the bullet points","setup":"The question is: 'Explain why the Korean War helped the CCP to strengthen its control of China in the years 1950-53. You may use the following in your answer: • the Resist America, Aid Korea campaign • the campaign against counter-revolutionaries. You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks); on the paper Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b) and you answer one of them. The mark scheme caps an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content at a maximum of 8 marks and shuts Level 4 to it altogether. The fact files in this room supply the material: North Korea's invasion on 25 June 1950, Chinese troops entering Korea on 19 October 1950, the armistice of 27 July 1953, estimates of Chinese dead running from about 180,000 to 400,000, Mao's eldest son killed in Korea, the campaign against counter-revolutionaries of 1950-51 and its official figure of around 712,000 executions, the three antis of December 1951 and the five antis of early 1952, and the occupation of Tibet in 1950 to 1951.","steps":["Turn the first bullet into a causal claim rather than a heading: a patriotic campaign against the Americans gave the new government the one thing a new government most needs, a national enemy, so that supporting the CCP and defending China became the same act.","Make the second bullet do causal work too: with Chinese troops in Korea from October 1950 and a Nationalist government waiting in Taiwan, every old Guomindang connection could be treated as a threat, so the campaign against counter-revolutionaries could be pressed with far less protest than in peacetime.","Add the reason of your own that the bullets do not prompt: the war cost money, and the three antis and five antis movements of 1951 and 1952 raised it from officials and businessmen while breaking their independence, so the emergency paid for itself politically as well as financially.","Order the three so that they build: a cause to rally behind, a licence to strike at enemies, and a bill that justified reaching into the towns.","Close on the question's own words, control, and say which of the three would still have strengthened the CCP without the other two."],"cues":["What does a new government gain from a foreign war?","Why was 1951 a safer year than 1949 to hunt opponents?","Which reason is prompted by neither bullet?","In what order do the three make an argument?","What must the final sentence settle?"]},{"title":"Question 3(d): evaluating Interpretation 2 against Interpretation 1 and your own knowledge","setup":"The question is: 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about what communist rule brought to China's towns and cities in the years 1949 to 1952? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]. Interpretation 2 holds that communist rule rested on 'not gratitude but fear, carefully organised', and says of the towns that the 'three antis' and 'five antis' movements 'turned the cities inside out in the same way' as the campaigns in the villages, and that the labour camps received a steady supply. Interpretation 1 holds that millions had reason to want the communists to stay, and says of the towns that prices were 'brought under control after years of ruinous inflation', that the Korean War let the government present itself as the defender of the nation, and that party membership more than doubled because joining was worth doing. No stimulus points are printed on this question.","steps":["State the claim you are judging in your own words and name the criterion: Interpretation 2 makes the antis movements the whole urban story, so the test is whether the campaigns or the rescue of the urban economy did more to decide how the cities lived by 1952.","Build the case for Interpretation 2 out of dated urban material rather than the interpretation repeated: the three antis from December 1951 against corruption, waste and bureaucracy among officials and managers, the five antis from early 1952 against private businessmen, employees told to accuse their employers, and meetings held every evening until a firm's accounts were clear.","Give Interpretation 1's case the same treatment: the hyperinflation that had destroyed the gold yuan introduced in August 1948 was brought under control, so that a wage held its value again; the Resist America, Aid Korea campaign let the new government stand for the nation rather than for a party; and party membership grew from about four and a half million in 1949 towards the 10.73 million reported in 1956.","Find the point that decides between them, which is what the urban campaigns were for: in the villages the landlords were destroyed as a class, with estimates of the dead running from about one million to two million, while in the cities the five antis ended in confessions, fines and back taxes, the owners kept their firms, and private industry was not absorbed until joint state-private ownership in the mid-1950s.","Show how each interpretation conveys its view, because the top level asks for it: one writes of movements that turned the cities inside out 'in the same way', so that the towns are one more field of a single operation; the other names a condition that was mended, prices brought under control, so that the towns are a place where something was put right.","Judge on the criterion you named and say what the losing view still holds: Interpretation 2 is right that the method used on the cities was the village method, mass accusation organised from above, but wrong to take the purpose as the same, since the towns were being made dependent rather than cleared, and for a city family with no business the largest change of these years was that a wage bought something again."],"cues":["What does Interpretation 2 claim happened to the cities, and what will you test it by?","Which urban campaigns give that claim its evidence?","What does Interpretation 1 put on the other side?","Did town and village get the same treatment for the same reason?","How does each writer's wording carry the view?","What must the judgement settle about purpose as well as method?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which law of June 1950 redistributed land in China, and roughly how many peasants received land under it?","a":"The Agrarian Reform Law of June 1950; about 300 million peasants received land, and estimates of the number of landlords killed run from about one million to two million."},{"q":"What does democratic centralism mean in practice, and why did it matter for governing China after 1949?","a":"An issue could be argued out before a decision was taken, but afterwards the minority obeyed the majority and every lower body obeyed the one above it, so a decision taken in Beijing could be enforced through party branches in villages and factories within weeks."},{"q":"Why is an official circular like Source C still useful for an enquiry into how the CCP treated class enemies, even though it is one-sided?","a":"Because it is the method itself in writing: a circular telling employees to accuse their employers, and offering owners who confess the chance to keep their businesses, shows exactly what the party intended to do and to whom, which is what the enquiry asks about."},{"q":"Name one thing the Anti-Rightist Campaign of 1957 cost China, apart from the people it punished.","a":"It silenced the skilled people the Five-Year Plan needed; or it made criticism of the party dangerous for twenty years; or it removed anyone likely to object publicly to the Great Leap Forward of 1958."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 3 Section A (16 marks): Question 1 prints Source A and asks 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about...' in a table of two boxes each (4 marks); Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b), and you answer one of them: each is 'Explain why...' with two stimulus points and 'You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks).","Paper 3 Section B (36 marks), all on Question 3: (a) 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into...?' (8); (b) 'What is the main difference between these views?' (4); (c) 'Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about...' (4); (d) 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about...?' (16, plus up to 4 for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology).","The stimulus-point caps on the 12-mark 'Explain why...': 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'. There is no cap at Level 2. Question 3(d) prints no stimulus points, so no cap applies to it.","Timing on Paper 3: 1 hour 30 minutes for 52 marks, so about a minute a mark plus thinking time. Roughly 5 minutes on Question 1, 18 on Question 2, 12 on 3(a), 6 each on 3(b) and 3(c), and about 40 minutes on 3(d) including the final check for the SPaG marks.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose, and usefulness is always judged for the enquiry the question names, never in general. Contextual knowledge earns nothing unless it is used on the sources or the interpretations.","Spellings the board uses and the examiner expects: Mao Zedong, Guomindang, Jiang Jieshi, Zhou Enlai, Deng Xiaoping, Beijing (Beiping until 1949), laogai, cadre, commune."],"questions":[{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about the armies of the Guomindang in north China in 1948. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark per valid inference, up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail from Source A. Credit soldiers' loyalty collapsing, corruption among officers, or conscripts kept only by force.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: the Guomindang could no longer rely on its own soldiers to go on fighting. Details in the source that tell me this: two regiments sent to relieve a besieged town 'went over to the communists with their American rifles and their guns' once promised that they would be fed, and conscripts have to be roped together on the march to stop them running away. (ii) What I can infer: the armies were being weakened from inside by their own officers. Details in the source that tell me this: the writer reports that officers sell the rice and the medicine meant for their men, and admits that he can no longer punish them all.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["An inference is something the source makes certain without stating it; ask what must be true for an officer to write this to his own high command.","Read the report twice, once for what the ordinary soldiers do and once for what their officers do.","Set out each answer in two parts: a claim the source never makes, then the exact words that force it."],"codes":["32-1 — 1 The Civil War, 1945–49: The relative strengths and weaknesses of the CCP and the Guomindang","32-1 — 1 The Civil War, 1945–49: Reasons for the success of Mao and the CCP"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about the attitude of villagers in north China in 1948. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark per valid inference, up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail from Source A. Credit active help for the communists, or the Guomindang deprived of supplies and information in the countryside.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: the villagers were actively helping the communists rather than simply staying out of the war. Details in the source that tell me this: the officer writes that they carry the communists' wounded and mend their roads at night, which is work done for one side. (ii) What I can infer: the Guomindang could get neither supplies nor information from the countryside it was fighting in. Details in the source that tell me this: he complains that the villagers will not sell his men grain and will not say where the enemy is, so his columns are marching blind through country the other side knows.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Look for the things the villagers do, and then for the things they refuse to do.","Ask what an army needs from the country it moves through, and check which of those the writer says he is not getting.","Give a claim the source implies and then the phrase that makes it certain, for each of your two answers."],"codes":["32-1 — 1 The Civil War, 1945–49: Reasons for the success of Mao and the CCP","32-1 — 1 The Civil War, 1945–49: The relative strengths and weaknesses of the CCP and the Guomindang"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. They give different views about why the Chinese people accepted communist rule in the years 1949 to 1957. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4) needs a key difference of view supported from both: Interpretation 1 explains acceptance by what people gained, Interpretation 2 by what they feared. Surface detail only stays in Level 1.","sol":"The main difference is over why people accepted the new government. Interpretation 1 says they accepted it because it gave them things they wanted: land for 'peasants who had never owned any', the Marriage Law of 1950 freeing women from arranged marriage, prices brought under control after years of inflation, and a government that could appear as the defender of the nation in Korea, so that 'it was the consent of the majority that made the regime secure'. Interpretation 2 says they accepted it because they had been frightened into it: villagers 'were required to denounce their neighbours', executions were carried out to quotas fixed in advance, and 'people obeyed because the alternative had been demonstrated to them'.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Finish the sentence 'The Chinese people accepted communist rule because...' once for each interpretation, then compare the two endings.","Check that your difference is one of view: both writers agree on what the campaigns did, so look for what each says moved people.","State the difference in one sentence first, then support each half with words taken from its own interpretation."],"codes":["32-1 — 3 Consolidating the CCP's hold on power, 1951–52: The use of terror against opponents","32-1 — 2 Communist rule: The role of the CCP in the government of China"]},{"q":"Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about the establishment of communist rule in China in the years 1949 to 1957. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4) needs one explained reason, effectively substantiated: for example that the two writers are asking different questions and so weigh different evidence.","sol":"One reason is that the two historians ask different questions and therefore weigh different evidence. Interpretation 1 comes from a study of the first decade of the Republic and looks at what the government did for people, so its evidence is laws and figures: the Marriage Law, the land handed over, prices steadied. Read from that material, consent looks like the explanation. Interpretation 2 is a general history that follows the campaigns, so its evidence is material of the kind in Source C, a circular telling employees to write out accusations against their employers and warning that those who conceal will be treated as enemies of the people. Read from that, pressure looks like the explanation.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Historians differ legitimately when they set out to study different things; look at what kind of book each interpretation comes from.","Ask what documents each writer would have spent the most time reading, and what those documents would make prominent.","Name one reason, then show it at work in both interpretations, using a source if it illustrates the kind of evidence involved."],"codes":["32-1 — 3 Consolidating the CCP's hold on power, 1951–52: The 'three antis' and 'five antis' movements","32-1 — 3 Consolidating the CCP's hold on power, 1951–52: The use of terror against opponents"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the methods the CCP used to establish its control over China in the years 1950 to 1952? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): utility judged for this enquiry, reasoning from nature, origin and purpose, with context used to interpret. Credit Source B on classification, struggle meetings and public executions, Source C on denunciation and confession in the five antis.","sol":"Source B is useful because it shows the CCP's rural method being carried out step by step. A work team comes in from outside, lives in the village for six weeks, sorts every household into five classes, and only then holds the speak bitterness meetings at which tenants accuse the landlord in public; two men are tried in front of the whole village and shot. That is the Agrarian Reform Law of June 1950 in operation, and it shows that control was built by making the villagers themselves do the accusing. Its provenance helps: a work team reporting upward wants to show success, so the eleven new applicants for party membership may be polished, but it has no reason to hide the executions, which it treats as part of the job. Source C shows the same method turned on the towns. It is a circular, an instruction rather than a record, so it is direct evidence of what the party intended: employees are told they know their employers' books better than any inspector, accusation boxes are provided, and owners who confess fully may keep their businesses. That offer shows the five antis aiming at submission rather than destruction, which fits what happened, since businessmen were fined and left in place while landlords were destroyed as a class. Together they are very useful, covering village and city and showing mass participation organised from above; neither reaches the campaign against counter-revolutionaries, where sentences were passed with no village meeting at all.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Usefulness is always usefulness for something: write the enquiry out before you look at either source.","Take each source as content, then nature, origin and purpose, then the context that confirms or qualifies it, and only then compare them.","Begin with what Source B shows about the order in which things were done in the village, and make your first sentence a judgement rather than a description."],"codes":["32-1 — 3 Consolidating the CCP's hold on power, 1951–52: The use of terror against opponents","32-1 — 3 Consolidating the CCP's hold on power, 1951–52: The 'three antis' and 'five antis' movements"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the part played by ordinary Chinese people in the CCP's campaigns of the early 1950s? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): criteria-based judgements with developed reasoning on how provenance affects the content. Credit Source B on tenants accusing their landlord and new party applicants, Source C on employees denouncing employers; qualify both, since neither records what participants privately felt.","sol":"Both sources are useful because in each of them the accusing is done by ordinary people rather than by officials, which is precisely what the enquiry asks about. In Source B the work team classifies the households, but it is the tenants who stand up one by one at the speak bitterness meetings and accuse the landlord of rents, of beatings and of a death, and the village then obeys its own peasant association instead of the old families. That fits the Agrarian Reform Law of June 1950, which was deliberately carried out through struggle meetings so that the peasants were bound to the new order by what they had done. Source C shows the same design in the towns: the circular tells employees that they know their employers' books better than any inspector, provides boxes for written accusations and promises that no truthful accusation will be punished, which is the five antis movement of 1952 recruiting the workforce against the owners. The limitation of both is the same and needs saying. Source B is a report written upward by a team being judged on its results, and Source C is an instruction telling people what to do rather than a record of what they did, so neither can show how willing the participants really were, or how many accusations were settling old scores. For an enquiry into the part ordinary people played they are strong on the part assigned to them and weak on the part they chose.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["The enquiry is about ordinary people, so ask of each source who actually speaks and who actually acts in it.","A source written to instruct and a source written to report have different weaknesses; name each one's before you judge.","Start by saying what the two sources have in common for this enquiry, and open with that judgement and the reason for it."],"codes":["32-1 — 3 Consolidating the CCP's hold on power, 1951–52: The use of terror against opponents","32-1 — 3 Consolidating the CCP's hold on power, 1951–52: The 'three antis' and 'five antis' movements"]},{"q":"2(a) Explain why Mao Zedong was able to build up such a dominant position in China by the mid-1950s. You may use the following in your answer: • the Long March • the constitution of 1954. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise knowledge — the Long March and Zunyi 1935, Mao Zedong Thought in the 1945 party constitution, the offices held from 1949 and 1954, democratic centralism, the removal of rivals by the campaigns. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Mao became unanswerable because a personal record, a body of ideas with his name on it and a set of offices all pointed the same way. The first reason is what he had already done. During the Long March of 1934 to 1935, when the party escaped from its base in the south to Shaanxi, the Zunyi conference of January 1935 put him in the leading position; he then held the party together at Yan'an through the war with Japan, and in 1949 he had won a civil war against an enemy that began with about four million men to his one million. Nobody else in the leadership could point to that. The second reason is ideology. Mao's version of Marxism made the peasants rather than the industrial workers the engine of revolution, which fitted China and had visibly worked, and it was written into the party's constitution as Mao Zedong Thought at the Seventh Congress in 1945, so disagreeing with Mao came close to disagreeing with the party's own creed. The third reason, which neither bullet prompts on its own, is the accumulation of office and the machinery that carried his decisions. He was Chairman of the party and of its military commission, he proclaimed the People's Republic on 1 October 1949 and chaired the new central government, and the constitution adopted by the First National People's Congress in September 1954 made him Chairman of the People's Republic as well, so party, army and state met in one person. Democratic centralism then did the rest: once the centre had decided, every lower body obeyed, and the campaigns of 1950 to 1952 had already removed opponents outside the party and disciplined officials inside it. Record gave him the standing, ideology made his views authoritative, and office plus democratic centralism turned authority into control.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Dominance is not the same as popularity: ask what would have had to happen for anyone to overrule him, and why it could not.","Plan three reasons of different kinds — what he had done, what he was thought to represent, and what he formally held — so the answer analyses rather than lists.","Open on the Long March and say what it settled inside the party, not what happened on it."],"codes":["32-1 — 2 Communist rule: The ideology and role of Mao","32-1 — 2 Communist rule: The role of the CCP in the government of China"]},{"q":"2(b) Explain why the CCP was able to govern the whole of China so quickly after 1949. You may use the following in your answer: • democratic centralism • the People's Liberation Army. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): analysis directed at causation — mass party membership and democratic centralism, the PLA and the six administrative regions, the Common Programme and the CPPCC, early economic successes. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The CCP governed a vast and exhausted country quickly because it had three instruments ready before it had a constitution. The first is the party itself. Membership had been about four and a half million in 1949 and the Eighth Congress of September 1956 reported 10.73 million, organised in branches in villages, factories, schools and army units. Those branches worked by democratic centralism: an issue could be argued out before a decision was taken, but afterwards the minority obeyed the majority and every lower body obeyed the one above it. That is why an order settled in Beijing could be enforced in a village a thousand miles away within weeks, without any of the negotiation the Guomindang had needed with local warlords and landlords. The second is the People's Liberation Army. Several million strong and used to living among the peasants under the Three Rules of Discipline and Eight Points for Attention, it was not only a fighting force: China was divided into six great administrative regions largely run by military and administrative committees until 1954, and the PLA restored railways, ran cities, protected land reform teams and occupied Tibet in 1950 and 1951. The third reason, which the bullets do not prompt, is that the CCP at first governed with others rather than against them. The Chinese People's Political Consultative Conference of September 1949 adopted the Common Programme as a provisional constitution and gave places to small non-communist parties, so the new government looked national rather than narrowly communist; old officials, managers and engineers were kept on under supervision because there was nobody else to do their work. Meanwhile the hyperinflation that had destroyed the Guomindang was brought under control, which won the towns. A disciplined party gave the CCP reach, the army gave it force, and the united front bought it the time to replace improvised arrangements with the constitution of 1954.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what a government needs in order to be obeyed at a distance, and then check which of those things the CCP already had in 1949.","Treat the two bullet points as reach and force, and find a third kind of reason that explains why the people it had defeated went on working for it.","Begin with the size of the party and what democratic centralism made that size capable of."],"codes":["32-1 — 2 Communist rule: The role of the CCP in the government of China","32-1 — 2 Communist rule: The ideology and role of Mao"]},{"q":"2(a) Explain why the CCP used terror against its opponents in the years 1950 to 1952. You may use the following in your answer: • the campaign against counter-revolutionaries • the Korean War. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): sustained causal analysis — real enemies left from the civil war, the campaign against counter-revolutionaries of 1950-51 and its official figure of around 712,000 executions, the Korean War and the fear of invasion, terror as a method of binding the population. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The CCP used terror because it had real enemies, because a war abroad made striking at them both urgent and popular, and because terror did work that no other method could do. The first reason is the state of the country in 1950. Large parts of the south and west had only just been occupied, Guomindang officials, soldiers, secret agents, bandits and armed secret societies were still at large, and Jiang Jieshi sat in Taiwan with an army waiting for the new regime to fail. The campaign against counter-revolutionaries, launched in October 1950 and at its height in 1951, was the answer: figures reported internally in 1954 admitted around 712,000 executions and well over a million people sent to the laogai camps of reform through labour. The second reason is the Korean War. Chinese troops entered Korea on 19 October 1950, and with American forces on the Yalu the fear of invasion and of a Nationalist return was not imaginary. The Resist America, Aid Korea campaign made anyone with foreign or Guomindang connections suspect, so the party could strike harder in wartime than it could ever have done in peace, and patriotic feeling carried much of the population with it. The third reason, which neither bullet prompts, is that terror was a method of rule and not only of defence. Land reform under the Agrarian Reform Law of June 1950 was pushed through by speak bitterness meetings at which the villagers themselves accused their landlord, with estimates of landlords killed running from about one million to two million; having done the accusing, the village was tied to the new order and the old rural leadership was gone for good. The three antis of December 1951 and the five antis of early 1952 used the same mass accusation against officials and businessmen. Enemies made terror necessary, the war made it safe, and participation made it permanent.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Distinguish terror used against a threat from terror used to change how a population behaves; the answer needs both.","Ask what the Korean War changed about the risks of a mass campaign at home, not just about the mood.","Begin with what was actually left of the Guomindang inside China in 1950."],"codes":["32-1 — 3 Consolidating the CCP's hold on power, 1951–52: The use of terror against opponents","32-1 — 3 Consolidating the CCP's hold on power, 1951–52: The 'three antis' and 'five antis' movements"]},{"q":"2(b) Explain why Mao launched the Hundred Flowers Campaign in 1956. You may use the following in your answer: • China's need for skilled experts • the criticism of Stalin in the Soviet Union. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise detail — the First Five-Year Plan and the silence of the intelligentsia after thought reform, Khrushchev's secret speech of February 1956 and the risings in Poland and Hungary, Mao's view of party bureaucracy and On the Correct Handling of Contradictions. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Mao raised the slogan let a hundred flowers bloom and a hundred schools of thought contend in May 1956 for reasons that were partly practical, partly foreign and partly about the party itself. The first reason is that China needed its educated people and was not getting the use of them. The First Five-Year Plan of 1953 to 1957 was building industry and required engineers, scientists, teachers and managers, but thought reform and the campaigns of 1951 and 1952 had taught exactly those people that speaking was dangerous, so they worked cautiously and said nothing. Inviting them to speak was an attempt to recover their skill and their willing cooperation. The second reason lies outside China. Khrushchev's secret speech to the Twentieth Congress of the Soviet Communist Party in February 1956 attacked Stalin's cult of personality and his terror, which made the Chinese leadership's own methods a subject of comment; later that year risings in Poland and Hungary showed what happened when discontent had no outlet at all. Mao drew the conclusion that grievances were safer aired than bottled up. The third reason, which neither bullet prompts, is Mao's own view of the party and of the state of the revolution. He thought the CCP had grown bureaucratic, privileged and arrogant since 1949, and that criticism from outside would discipline officials whom the three antis had not cured; in February 1957 he argued in On the Correct Handling of Contradictions Among the People that disagreements among the people could be settled by discussion rather than force. He believed the revolution was now safe, with land reform complete and collectivisation almost finished, so that criticism could be allowed without danger. That last belief is the one that failed, and it is why the campaign ended as it did.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["A ruler who invites criticism is trying to solve a problem; name the problems, then rank them.","One of your reasons should come from outside China and one from inside the party itself.","Open on what the Five-Year Plan needed and on why the people who could supply it had stopped speaking."],"codes":["32-1 — 4 The Hundred Flowers campaign, 1956–57: Mao's reasons for the campaign","32-1 — 4 The Hundred Flowers campaign, 1956–57: Key features of the campaign"]},{"q":"2(a) Explain why the Anti-Rightist Campaign followed the Hundred Flowers Campaign in 1957. You may use the following in your answer: • the criticisms made in the spring of 1957 • Mao's authority. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): analysis directed at causation — criticism going far beyond bureaucracy, wall posters and demands about one-party rule, Mao's need to recover after a public misjudgement, Deng Xiaoping's campaign from June 1957 and the roughly 550,000 labelled rightists. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The Anti-Rightist Campaign followed because the criticism Mao received was not the criticism he had asked for, because it damaged him personally, and because much of the leadership had wanted it stopped from the beginning. The first reason is the content of the criticism itself. Very little was said at first, since people remembered thought reform, and the campaign only opened widely in the spring of 1957 after repeated official encouragement. When it opened, complaints about bureaucracy were quickly overtaken by attacks on the privileges of party cadres, on political reliability being preferred to ability, on the party's control of the universities and the courts, and in the sharpest cases on the CCP's monopoly of power itself; students at Peking University put up wall posters and leaders of the small democratic parties asked for a real share in government. A party that had shot counter-revolutionaries in 1951 was not going to tolerate that in 1957. The second reason is Mao's authority. He had pushed the campaign personally against the doubts of colleagues, and a flood of hostile comment made him look as though he had misjudged the country he had ruled for eight years. Turning the critics into rightists converted an embarrassment into a victory, and he said afterwards that the campaign had lured the snakes out of their holes. The third reason, which the bullets do not prompt, is that the machinery and the motive were waiting. Officials who had been attacked wanted redress, and Deng Xiaoping ran the campaign from June 1957: the official figure is about 550,000 people labelled rightists, who lost their jobs and were sent to the countryside or to laogai camps. Silencing them also cleared the ground, because after 1957 nobody was going to object publicly to the Great Leap Forward launched in 1958.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["The question is about a reversal: work out what Mao expected to hear and set it against what he actually heard.","One reason should be about the critics, one about Mao himself, and one about the people who carried the new campaign out.","Start with why so little was said at first and what changed in the spring of 1957."],"codes":["32-1 — 4 The Hundred Flowers campaign, 1956–57: Results of the campaign","32-1 — 4 The Hundred Flowers campaign, 1956–57: Key features of the campaign"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part played by fear in communist rule in the years 1949 to 1957? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views evaluated, how each conveys its view analysed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit the campaign against counter-revolutionaries, the laogai and the Anti-Rightist Campaign; against, land reform, the Marriage Law, the end of inflation and Korea.","sol":"Interpretation 2 argues that communist rule rested on 'not gratitude but fear, carefully organised': struggle meetings in which villagers were required to denounce their neighbours, executions carried out to quotas fixed in advance, the three antis and five antis turning the cities inside out, labour camps kept supplied, and criticism invited in 1957 and punished within weeks, so that 'people obeyed because the alternative had been demonstrated to them'. The test to apply is whether fear explains obedience by itself, or whether it explains only why disobedience was not attempted. On the evidence for the interpretation it is largely right, and the phrase 'carefully organised' is its strongest word. The campaign against counter-revolutionaries began in October 1950, the month Chinese troops entered Korea, was at its height in 1951, and figures reported internally in 1954 admitted around 712,000 executions with well over a million people sent to the laogai camps of reform through labour. Land reform under the Agrarian Reform Law of June 1950 was carried out through speak bitterness meetings at which tenants accused their landlord in front of the village, and estimates of landlords killed run from about one million to two million. Teachers and other intellectuals were put through thought reform, with study groups, self-criticism and written confessions. Then in June 1957, weeks after criticism had been invited, the Anti-Rightist Campaign labelled about 550,000 people, most of whom lost their jobs and were sent to the countryside or to camps. That is a pattern, not a series of accidents, and it makes the claim about organisation accurate. Interpretation 1 supplies what Interpretation 2 leaves out, and its evidence is also real. Land reform gave holdings to about 300 million peasants who had never owned any; the Marriage Law of May 1950 banned arranged marriage and gave women the right to divorce; the hyperinflation that had ruined salaried people under the Guomindang was brought under control; and the Korean War let the government appear as the defender of China against the Americans rather than as a party imposing itself. Party membership rose from about four and a half million in 1949 to the 10.73 million reported in 1956, which is not what people do when they are only frightened. The two writers also convey their views by different means, and saying so is part of the evaluation. Interpretation 2 describes a system, with quotas, movements and camps, so that the people in it appear as objects; Interpretation 1 describes a bargain, with laws and gains, so that they appear as parties to it. Neither writer denies the other's facts; each chooses which facts do the explaining. My judgement is that Interpretation 2 is right about the years in which the regime was still being established and about why opposition was never organised, but wrong to treat fear as the whole explanation of obedience. The spring of 1957 is the decisive test. When criticism was invited, a great deal of it came, which shows that fear had suppressed speech rather than produced agreement; but much of that criticism asked for the party to be reformed rather than removed, which shows that the gains Interpretation 1 lists had bought real support. Fear explains the silence; it does not explain why so many people had nothing they wished to say against the regime until they were asked.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide what the interpretation is claiming fear actually produced, and name the test you will judge that claim by.","Use Interpretation 1 as your case against, and support both sides with dated detail rather than with the interpretations restated.","Open by putting Interpretation 2's claim in your own words and stating the criterion your judgement will use."],"codes":["32-1 — 3 Consolidating the CCP's hold on power, 1951–52: The use of terror against opponents","32-1 — 4 The Hundred Flowers campaign, 1956–57: Results of the campaign","32-1 — 2 Communist rule: The role of the CCP in the government of China"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the way land reform was carried out in the Chinese countryside in the years 1950 to 1952? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views weighed, how they are conveyed analysed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit the struggle meetings and the killing of landlords; against, the work teams' classification of every household, the division of the land among the poor and the peasant associations left behind.","sol":"Interpretation 2 is describing how land reform was done: it was 'carried through by struggle meetings in which villagers were required to denounce their neighbours, and the killing was on a scale that left nobody in any doubt'. Interpretation 1 writes of the same campaign as one that 'gave holdings to peasants who had never owned any', which implies a very different account of the way it was done, a redistribution conducted by a government for people. The test is whether violence and compulsion describe the method as a whole or only its most visible part. There is strong support for Interpretation 2. The Agrarian Reform Law of June 1950 was carried out by work teams sent in from outside, which classified every household as landlord, rich peasant, middle peasant, poor peasant or labourer before anything was distributed, so the enemy was identified before a word had been spoken against him. The speak bitterness meetings that followed required tenants to stand up and accuse their landlord in front of the people they lived among, and the trials and shootings were held in public so that they would be remembered; estimates of the number of landlords killed run from about one million to two million. Interpretation 2 is also right that the compulsion had a purpose of its own: a villager who had accused a neighbour could not take it back, so the method tied the village to the new order more firmly than the transfer of property could. As an account of the method it is nevertheless incomplete. Land reform was an administrative operation as well as a violent one, and its order of stages shows it: registration and classification first, then the meetings, then the division of the land, the tools, the draught animals and the grain among the poor households, with roughly two-fifths of China's cultivated land changing hands and about 300 million peasants receiving some of it. Peasant associations were set up to do the dividing and were left behind as the bodies through which the party governed the village. None of that follows from killing on a scale that left nobody in any doubt, and it is what made the campaign permanent. The two writers convey their views by the verbs they choose. Interpretation 2 has villagers 'required' and lets the scale of the killing do the work, so that the peasants appear as people to whom land reform was done; Interpretation 1 has land reform 'give', so that the same peasants appear as those who received it. Neither is inventing anything; each has taken a different stage of one operation for the whole. My judgement is that Interpretation 2 is largely right about the way land reform was carried out, and wrong only in leaving out how much procedure the violence was built into. The terror was not an excess at the edges of the campaign but its chosen instrument, and an account of the method that omits it is false. But struggle meetings by themselves would not have moved two-fifths of the cultivated land or left a peasant association in every village. The method was a procedure with the violence built into it, and it is that combination that explains why the countryside was in the CCP's hands by 1952 and stayed there.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Work out what 'carried through' means in Interpretation 2, and set out the stages land reform actually went through before you agree or disagree with anything.","Put the violence and the paperwork side by side, classification, meetings, division, association, and ask how much of that the interpretation accounts for.","Begin by stating what Interpretation 2 says the method was, and name the criterion your judgement will use."],"codes":["32-1 — 3 Consolidating the CCP's hold on power, 1951–52: The use of terror against opponents","32-1 — 2 Communist rule: The role of the CCP in the government of China","32-1 — 1 The Civil War, 1945–49: Reasons for the success of Mao and the CCP"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the experience of ordinary Chinese people in the first years of communist rule? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): alternative views reviewed, how each is conveyed analysed, precise context, substantiated judgement. For agreement credit struggle meetings, denunciation in the five antis, thought reform and the laogai; against, land, the Marriage Law, steady prices and mass party membership.","sol":"Interpretation 2 describes the experience of ordinary people as one of organised fear: villagers 'required to denounce their neighbours', executions to quotas fixed in advance, the three antis and five antis turning the cities inside out, camps kept supplied, and criticism punished within weeks in 1957, so that 'people obeyed because the alternative had been demonstrated to them'. Interpretation 1 describes the same years as ones in which millions had reason to want the communists to stay. The right criterion is not which writer is more shocking but which describes what the majority actually lived through, so the question is whether most people were mainly acted upon or mainly better off. Interpretation 2 is convincing about how public and how inescapable the campaigns were. In the villages the Agrarian Reform Law of June 1950 was carried out by work teams that sorted every household into classes and then held speak bitterness meetings at which tenants had to accuse their landlord in front of everyone, with landlord deaths estimated between about one million and two million. In the towns the five antis movement of 1952 told employees to write out accusations against their employers, with meetings held every evening until a firm's accounts were clear. Teachers and other educated people went through thought reform with its study groups and written confessions, the campaign against counter-revolutionaries of 1950-51 ended in around 712,000 executions admitted in figures reported internally in 1954, and the laogai camps took well over a million more. Ordinary people were not spectators of any of this; taking part was required. Interpretation 1 is describing a different part of the same experience, and it is not wrong. About 300 million peasants received land they had never owned. The Marriage Law of May 1950 ended arranged marriage, concubinage and bride price and gave women the right to divorce, which changed daily life for half the population more than any campaign did. The hyperinflation that had destroyed savings and wages under the Guomindang was brought under control, so that a wage was worth something again, and order returned to roads and railways that had been fought over since 1937. Party membership rose from about four and a half million in 1949 to the 10.73 million reported in 1956, which does not happen unless joining looks attractive. The two writers convey their views through what they place at the centre. Interpretation 2 centres the campaigns, so that people appear in the passive, as those required, denounced and supplied to camps; Interpretation 1 centres laws and figures, so that people appear as recipients. Each is selecting truthfully from the same decade. My judgement is that Interpretation 2 captures the character of the period better than Interpretation 1 does, but describes a minority's experience as though it were everyone's. For the several hundred million peasants who gained land and for women freed from arranged marriage, the first years were an improvement they had not expected; for landlords, businessmen, former Guomindang officials and educated critics they were a catastrophe. What makes Interpretation 2 the stronger account is that everyone, gainers included, learned the same lesson from watching what happened to the others, and that is why by 1957 there was no organised opposition anywhere in China.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Experience differed by who you were: decide early which groups each interpretation is really describing.","Look for something that changed daily life for very large numbers of people and is missing from Interpretation 2.","Open by stating Interpretation 2's claim in your own words and naming the criterion your judgement will use."],"codes":["32-1 — 3 Consolidating the CCP's hold on power, 1951–52: The use of terror against opponents","32-1 — 3 Consolidating the CCP's hold on power, 1951–52: The 'three antis' and 'five antis' movements","32-1 — 4 The Hundred Flowers campaign, 1956–57: Results of the campaign"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date was the People's Republic of China proclaimed, and by whom?","a":"1 October 1949, by Mao Zedong, in Beijing."},{"q":"Which law of June 1950 redistributed land from landlords to the peasants?","a":"The Agrarian Reform Law."},{"q":"Which campaign followed the Hundred Flowers Campaign from June 1957, and who ran it?","a":"The Anti-Rightist Campaign, run by Deng Xiaoping."},{"q":"In which month and year did Chinese troops enter the Korean War, and in which month and year was the armistice signed?","a":"October 1950; July 1953."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The strengths of the Guomindang in 1946","open":false,"code":"32-1 — 1 The Civil War, 1945–49: The relative strengths and weaknesses of the CCP and the Guomindang","back":"About four million men under arms against roughly a million communists, plus the great cities, the tax revenue, the railways, American equipment and recognition by foreign governments."},{"front":"The strengths of the CCP in 1946","open":false,"code":"32-1 — 1 The Civil War, 1945–49: The relative strengths and weaknesses of the CCP and the Guomindang","back":"Unity of command under Mao Zedong, a rural base at Yan'an, soldiers disciplined by the Three Rules of Discipline and Eight Points for Attention, and land divided among peasants in the areas it held."},{"front":"The People's Liberation Army","open":false,"code":"32-1 — 1 The Civil War, 1945–49: Reasons for the success of Mao and the CCP","back":"The name given to the communist armed forces in 1947. After 1949 it also ran cities, restored railways, protected land reform teams and occupied Tibet."},{"front":"Name one reason the Guomindang lost the Civil War","open":true,"code":"32-1 — 1 The Civil War, 1945–49: Reasons for the success of Mao and the CCP","back":"Hyperinflation destroyed the value of the gold yuan introduced in August 1948; corruption and press-ganged conscripts wrecked morale, so whole units surrendered; the CCP's land policy won the villages while the Guomindang held only cities."},{"front":"1948-49 in the Civil War","open":false,"code":"32-1 — 1 The Civil War, 1945–49: Reasons for the success of Mao and the CCP","back":"The communists took Manchuria, won the Huai-Hai campaign north of the Yangtze and entered Beiping in January 1949. Nanjing fell in April, Shanghai in May; Jiang Jieshi withdrew to Taiwan."},{"front":"1 October 1949","open":false,"code":"32-1 — 2 Communist rule: The ideology and role of Mao","back":"Mao Zedong proclaimed the People's Republic of China in Beijing, after the Chinese People's Political Consultative Conference had adopted the Common Programme in September 1949."},{"front":"Mao Zedong Thought","open":false,"code":"32-1 — 2 Communist rule: The ideology and role of Mao","back":"Mao's version of Marxism, making the peasants rather than industrial workers the engine of revolution. Written into the party constitution at the Seventh Congress in 1945."},{"front":"The constitution of 1954","open":false,"code":"32-1 — 2 Communist rule: The role of the CCP in the government of China","back":"Adopted by the First National People's Congress in September 1954. It replaced the Common Programme and made Mao Chairman of the People's Republic; real power stayed with the CCP."},{"front":"Democratic centralism","open":false,"code":"32-1 — 2 Communist rule: The role of the CCP in the government of China","back":"An issue could be argued out before a decision; afterwards the minority obeyed the majority and every lower body obeyed the one above it, so orders from Beijing reached every village branch."},{"front":"The Agrarian Reform Law, June 1950","open":false,"code":"32-1 — 3 Consolidating the CCP's hold on power, 1951–52: The use of terror against opponents","back":"Took land from landlords and gave it to about 300 million peasants, through speak bitterness meetings in the villages. Estimates of landlords killed run from about one million to two million."},{"front":"The campaign against counter-revolutionaries, 1950-51","open":false,"code":"32-1 — 3 Consolidating the CCP's hold on power, 1951–52: The use of terror against opponents","back":"Launched in October 1950 and at its height in 1951, against Guomindang agents, bandits and secret societies. Figures reported internally in 1954 admitted around 712,000 executions and well over a million sent to laogai camps."},{"front":"The 'three antis' and 'five antis' movements","open":false,"code":"32-1 — 3 Consolidating the CCP's hold on power, 1951–52: The 'three antis' and 'five antis' movements","back":"Three antis, from December 1951: corruption, waste and bureaucracy among officials. Five antis, from early 1952: bribery, tax evasion, theft of state property, cheating on contracts and stealing economic information, aimed at businessmen."},{"front":"The Korean War and China","open":false,"code":"32-1 — 3 Consolidating the CCP's hold on power, 1951–52: The use of terror against opponents","back":"Chinese troops entered Korea on 19 October 1950; the armistice came on 27 July 1953. The Resist America, Aid Korea campaign rallied patriotism and made hunting internal enemies easier."},{"front":"The Hundred Flowers Campaign, 1956-57","open":false,"code":"32-1 — 4 The Hundred Flowers campaign, 1956–57: Key features of the campaign","back":"The slogan was raised in May 1956 and criticism only flowed in the spring of 1957. It attacked cadres' privileges, party control of universities and courts, and even one-party rule; Mao ended it in June 1957."},{"front":"The Anti-Rightist Campaign","open":false,"code":"32-1 — 4 The Hundred Flowers campaign, 1956–57: Results of the campaign","back":"Run by Deng Xiaoping from June 1957. About 550,000 people were labelled rightists, losing their jobs and being sent to the countryside or to laogai camps."}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The sources and interpretations written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Source A: From a report sent to the Guomindang high command in Nanjing by a Nationalist officer serving with an army in north China, November 1948. Written for this room in the style of such a report; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — I must set down plainly what this army can and cannot do. Two regiments ordered forward last week to relieve the town went over to the communists with their American rifles and their guns, having been told that on the other side they would be fed. The pay I hand out is in banknotes that buy less at the end of a week than at the beginning, and a month's wages will not buy a soldier's family a sack of grain. Officers sell the rice and the medicine meant for their men, and I can no longer punish them all. New conscripts must be roped together on the march or they run. In the villages nobody will sell us grain and nobody will say where the enemy is, yet they carry his wounded and mend his roads at night. My best divisions sit in the cities and the railways between them are cut.","Source B: From a report to a county party committee by a land reform work team sent to a village in central China, summer 1951. Written for this room in the style of such a work team report; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — Our team lived in the village for six weeks. We first classified every household as landlord, rich peasant, middle peasant, poor peasant or labourer, and only then called the meetings. At the speaking bitterness meetings the tenants stood up one by one and told what they had paid in rent, what beatings there had been, and how one family's child had died in the year the rent was raised. The land, the tools, the animals and the grain in the store were divided among 140 poor families. Two men named by the villagers as local despots were tried in front of the whole village and shot; the others have been put under the supervision of the peasant association. Before we came this village obeyed the old families. It now obeys its peasant association, and eleven young men have asked to join the Party.","Source C: From a circular issued to the employees of factories and shops by the party committee of a large Chinese city during the 'five antis' movement, spring 1952. Written for this room in the style of such a circular; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — Every business in this city is to be examined for the five poisons: bribery, tax evasion, the theft of state property, cheating on government contracts and the stealing of state economic information. You who work in these firms know your employer's books better than any inspector sent from outside. Write out what you have seen and place it in the boxes provided; no one who makes a truthful accusation will be punished for it. An owner who confesses fully and pays what he owes may keep his business and go on serving the people. An owner who conceals will be dealt with as an enemy of the people. Meetings will be held every evening until the accounts of the firm are clear.","Interpretation 1: From a study of the first decade of the People's Republic, by a historian writing in the 1980s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — The communists held China after 1949 because millions of ordinary people had reason to want them to. Land reform gave holdings to peasants who had never owned any, and the Marriage Law of 1950 freed women from arranged marriage and gave them the right to divorce. Prices were brought under control after years of ruinous inflation, and the Korean War allowed the new government to present itself as the defender of the nation against the Americans. Party membership more than doubled in the first years of the Republic, because joining was worth doing. Terror existed, but it was the consent of the majority that made the regime secure.","Interpretation 2: From a general history of the People's Republic of China, by a historian writing in the 2000s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — What secured communist rule was not gratitude but fear, carefully organised. Land reform was carried through by struggle meetings in which villagers were required to denounce their neighbours, and the killing was on a scale that left nobody in any doubt. The campaign against counter-revolutionaries that began in 1950 reached quotas of executions fixed in advance; the 'three antis' and 'five antis' movements turned the cities inside out in the same way; and the labour camps received a steady supply. When criticism was invited in 1957 it was punished within weeks. People obeyed because the alternative had been demonstrated to them.","How the room uses them: Source A is the inference source for Question 1. Sources B and C are the contemporary pair for the 8-mark usefulness question, 3(a). Interpretations 1 and 2 are the pair for 3(b), 3(c) and 3(d). Every quotation in the model answers of this room comes from the five texts above and from nowhere else."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 3 Question 3(d), 16 marks)","items":["Question: 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the reasons why the CCP was able to secure its hold on power in China in the years 1949 to 1957? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]. This is one paragraph from the middle of the answer, putting the case against Interpretation 2.","Interpretation 2 says that what secured communist rule was 'not gratitude but fear, carefully organised', but that phrase cannot carry the weight it is given, because a great deal of what made the CCP safe by 1957 was administrative rather than frightening. The party had about four and a half million members in 1949 and reported 10.73 million at its Eighth Congress in September 1956, organised in branches in villages, factories, schools and army units and working by democratic centralism, so that a decision taken in Beijing was carried out a thousand miles away within weeks without anyone needing to be threatened. Alongside it the People's Liberation Army administered six great regions of the country until 1954, and the Chinese People's Political Consultative Conference of September 1949 gave the small non-communist parties a place under the Common Programme, which made the new state look national rather than sectarian while the constitution of 1954 was being prepared. Interpretation 1 is pointing at the same thing when it says that membership 'more than doubled in the first years of the Republic, because joining was worth doing': people were queueing to enter an organisation that Interpretation 2 can only describe as a source of fear. Terror removed the CCP's enemies, but it was this machine that governed the ones who were left, and Interpretation 2 gives no account of it at all.","Why this is Level 4: it 'provides an explained evaluation reviewing the alternative views in coming to a substantiated judgement', because the counter-case is built and then measured against Interpretation 2's own claim rather than placed beside it. The analysis is precise about how the difference of view is conveyed, setting fear 'carefully organised' against membership that was 'worth doing'. The contextual knowledge is precisely selected rather than general: the two membership figures with their dates, democratic centralism, the six administrative regions, the Common Programme and the date of the constitution. The line of reasoning is sustained and the closing sentence judges rather than summarises."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same as above — 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the reasons why the CCP was able to secure its hold on power in China in the years 1949 to 1957?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks].","Interpretation 2 says that the communists kept power because people were afraid, and I partly agree with this. There was a lot of terror in China at this time, with landlords being killed and people being sent to camps, so ordinary people would have been too scared to do anything against the government. However, Interpretation 1 says that people supported the communists because they were given land and because women got better rights, which shows that not everybody was frightened. The party also got much bigger during these years, which suggests people wanted to join it. Overall I think both interpretations are partly right, because there was terror but there were also improvements.","Why this is Level 2 and not Level 3: it does offer 'valid evaluative comment to agree with or counter the interpretation' and it does select details from both, which is what Level 2 asks for. What it misses is Level 3's requirement that 'relevant contextual knowledge is used directly to support the evaluation': 'a lot of terror', 'people being sent to camps' and 'the party also got much bigger' are assertions where the Agrarian Reform Law of June 1950, the laogai, and the membership figures of 1949 and 1956 should be. It never shows how the two writers convey their views, and the judgement is announced in the last sentence rather than reasoned towards, so 'its justification is insecure or undeveloped and a line of reasoning is not sustained'. Turning it into a Level 3 answer means replacing every vague quantity with a dated fact that does evaluative work."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Civil War, 1945-49 — the CCP and the Guomindang in the Civil War","items":["Japan surrendered in August 1945; Mao Zedong and Jiang Jieshi held talks at Chongqing that ended in the Double Tenth Agreement of 10 October 1945, and the American general George Marshall tried to mediate a lasting truce during 1946.","Full-scale civil war resumed in the summer of 1946. The Guomindang had about four million men under arms; the communists had roughly a million.","Guomindang advantages in 1946: the great cities, the tax revenue, the railways, the recognition of foreign governments, American equipment, aircraft and large amounts of American financial aid.","CCP advantages in 1946: unity of command under Mao, experienced commanders such as Zhu De and Lin Biao, a base area around Yan'an in Shaanxi held since the Long March, and the loyalty of the peasants in the areas it controlled.","The communist forces were renamed the People's Liberation Army in 1947. Their soldiers were held to the Three Rules of Discipline and Eight Points for Attention, which required them to pay for what they took and to return what they borrowed.","Jiang Jieshi's armies captured Yan'an in March 1947, but the communists simply withdrew and went on fighting; the city was retaken in 1948.","CCP land policy in the areas it held redistributed landlords' land to poor peasants from 1946 onwards, which brought recruits, food, porters, stretcher-bearers and information.","Guomindang weaknesses: corruption in the officer corps, conscripts press-ganged and badly treated, reliance on holding cities while the countryside was lost, and generals who would not co-operate with one another.","Hyperinflation destroyed the Nationalist economy. A new currency, the gold yuan, was introduced in August 1948 and had lost almost all its value within months, ruining the salaried townspeople who had welcomed the Guomindang in 1945.","The decisive year was 1948-49: the communists took Manchuria between September and November 1948, won the Huai-Hai campaign in the plains north of the Yangtze between November 1948 and January 1949, and entered Beiping in January 1949 when its commander surrendered the city.","Nanjing, the Nationalist capital, fell in April 1949 and Shanghai in May 1949. Jiang Jieshi and around two million followers withdrew to Taiwan by December 1949, taking the gold reserves with them."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: communist rule — Mao's position and the CCP in the government of China","items":["Mao Zedong proclaimed the People's Republic of China in Beijing on 1 October 1949.","The Chinese People's Political Consultative Conference met in September 1949 and adopted the Common Programme, which acted as a provisional constitution until 1954 and gave places to small non-communist 'democratic parties'.","Zhou Enlai became Premier and Foreign Minister; Mao chaired the Central People's Government Council.","China was divided into six great administrative regions, largely run by military and administrative committees of the People's Liberation Army, until they were wound up in 1954.","The constitution adopted by the First National People's Congress in September 1954 created the National People's Congress and made Mao Chairman of the People's Republic; real power remained with the CCP.","Democratic centralism: an issue could be argued out before a decision was taken, but afterwards the minority obeyed the majority, every lower body obeyed the one above it, and the whole party obeyed the Central Committee.","Party membership was about four and a half million in 1949; the Eighth Congress of September 1956 reported 10.73 million members.","Mao's standing inside the party came from the Long March of 1934-35, at whose Zunyi conference in January 1935 he took the leading position, and from the Yan'an years and the victory of 1949.","Mao Zedong Thought — a Marxism in which the peasants rather than the industrial workers are the engine of revolution, with the mass line and continuous revolution — was written into the party constitution at the Seventh Congress in 1945.","Mao held the chairmanship of the party and of its military commission as well as the chairmanship of the state from 1954, so party, army and government met in one man.","Tibet was occupied in 1950-51: the PLA entered eastern Tibet in October 1950, and the Seventeen Point Agreement of May 1951 placed Tibet under Chinese sovereignty while promising it autonomy."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: consolidating power, 1950-52 — terror, the antis movements and the Korean War","items":["The Agrarian Reform Law of June 1950 took land, tools, animals and grain from landlords and gave them to about 300 million peasants; roughly two-fifths of China's cultivated land changed hands.","Land reform was carried out by work teams that classified every household as landlord, rich peasant, middle peasant, poor peasant or labourer, and then held 'speaking bitterness' or struggle meetings at which tenants accused their landlord in public.","Estimates of the number of landlords killed during land reform run from about one million to two million.","The Marriage Law of 1 May 1950 banned arranged marriage, concubinage, child betrothal and bride price, and gave women the right to divorce and to hold property.","The campaign against counter-revolutionaries ran from 1950 to 1951: it was launched in October 1950 against Guomindang agents, soldiers, bandits, secret societies and religious groups and was at its height in 1951; figures reported internally in 1954 admitted around 712,000 executions, with well over a million more imprisoned.","Laogai means 'reform through labour': the camp system in which those sentenced worked on farms, mines and construction.","Thought reform, from 1951, put teachers, university staff and other intellectuals through study groups, public self-criticism and written confessions.","The 'three antis' movement began in December 1951 and attacked corruption, waste and bureaucracy among party members, officials and managers of state enterprises.","The 'five antis' movement followed from early 1952 and attacked bribery, tax evasion, theft of state property, cheating on government contracts and the stealing of state economic information; its targets were private businessmen, and fines and back taxes raised large sums for the state.","The Korean War: North Korea invaded the South on 25 June 1950; Chinese People's Volunteers entered Korea on 19 October 1950 as United Nations forces neared the Yalu; the armistice was signed on 27 July 1953.","Estimates of Chinese dead in Korea range from about 180,000 to around 400,000; Mao's eldest son, Mao Anying, was killed there in 1950.","Effects of the Korean War on China: the 'Resist America, Aid Korea' campaign rallied patriotism, foreign and Guomindang connections became dangerous, the cost pushed the government towards the antis movements, the United States protected Taiwan with its Seventh Fleet, and China stayed outside the United Nations."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Hundred Flowers campaign, 1956-57, and the Anti-Rightist purge","items":["The slogan 'let a hundred flowers bloom, let a hundred schools of thought contend' was raised in May 1956.","Mao's reasons included the need for engineers, scientists, teachers and managers for the First Five-Year Plan of 1953-57, whose skills were being wasted because thought reform had frightened them into silence.","Khrushchev's secret speech to the Twentieth Congress of the Soviet Communist Party in February 1956 attacked Stalin's cult of personality and his terror, which made the CCP's own methods a subject of comment.","Risings in Poland and in Hungary in 1956 showed what could happen when discontent had no outlet; Mao concluded that grievances were safer aired than suppressed.","Mao also believed the party had become bureaucratic, privileged and arrogant since 1949, and that criticism from outside would discipline officials whom the 'three antis' had not cured.","In February 1957 Mao argued in 'On the Correct Handling of Contradictions Among the People' that disagreements among the people could be settled by discussion rather than by force.","Very little criticism came at first, because people remembered thought reform; the campaign only opened widely in the spring of 1957 after repeated official encouragement.","The criticism then went far beyond bureaucracy: attacks on the privileges of cadres, on political reliability being preferred to ability, on party control of the universities and the courts, and in the sharpest cases on the CCP's monopoly of power. Students at Peking University put up wall posters and leaders of the small democratic parties asked for a real share in government.","Mao ended the campaign in June 1957. He said afterwards that it had lured the snakes out of their holes; historians still disagree over whether that was the plan from the start.","The Anti-Rightist Campaign ran from June 1957 under Deng Xiaoping. The official figure is about 550,000 people labelled 'rightists', most of them educated people who had spoken in the spring.","Those labelled lost their jobs and were sent to work in the countryside or to laogai camps; most were not rehabilitated until after Mao's death in 1976.","Consequences: China lost the use of the skills it had wanted, criticism of the party became dangerous for twenty years, Mao's personal authority was restored after a public misjudgement, and no one was willing to object publicly to the Great Leap Forward launched in 1958."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the source and interpretation moves this paper rewards","items":["Inference: say something the source does not state but that must be true if the source is true, then give the words that force it. One inference and one supporting detail in each box.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose: what kind of document it is, who made it and when, and why it was made.","Utility: judge each source for the enquiry the question names, never in general. Content first, then how nature, origin and purpose make that content more or less useful, then the context that confirms or qualifies it.","Contextual knowledge scores only when it is used on the source; a paragraph about the period that never touches the sources earns nothing.","'Biased' is not a judgement on its own. Say whose view the source gives, why it gives that view, and what it is therefore useful for: a one-sided source is excellent evidence of the view it takes.","An instruction and a report are different kinds of evidence: a circular tells you what was intended, a report tells you what someone wished to be believed about what happened.","A source can be useful for an enquiry it never mentions, if what it shows explains the thing being enquired into; say what you can infer, not just what it says.","Difference of view, 3(b): a disagreement about the past, not a difference of wording or of detail. State the one difference, then support it with a phrase from each interpretation.","Reason for difference, 3(c): interpretations differ because they ask different questions, rest on different evidence, or give weight to different parts of the story. Name which, and show it from the interpretations, using Sources B and C if they illustrate the kind of evidence involved.","Differences based on conclusions drawn from evidence are legitimate; an interpretation is not wrong simply because another one disagrees with it.","Evaluating an interpretation, 3(d): use both interpretations and your own knowledge, show how each conveys its view, then decide how far the named interpretation can be agreed with, and say on what criterion."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 3 Question 3(d): 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the reasons why the CCP was able to secure its hold on power in China in the years 1949 to 1957?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","items":["The question as printed: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the reasons why the CCP was able to secure its hold on power in China in the years 1949 to 1957? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' This question prints no stimulus points: the two interpretations are the stimulus, and both must be used, so an answer that evaluates one in isolation cannot reach the top levels.","Decode the statement. Interpretation 2 claims that the hold on power rested on fear that was 'carefully organised': struggle meetings, quotas of executions, the antis movements, the camps and the punishment of criticism in 1957. Interpretation 1 claims the opposite weighting, that 'the consent of the majority' made the regime secure. The criterion to announce in the opening is whether the CCP's security came from what people feared or from what they had gained and from the machine that governed them.","Opening, three or four sentences: put Interpretation 2's claim in your own words, name Interpretation 1 as the alternative weighting, state the criterion, and signal the position you will end at, so the line of reasoning is visible from the start.","Paragraph 1, for Interpretation 2 — the campaigns against enemies. The campaign against counter-revolutionaries of 1950-51, with around 712,000 executions admitted in figures reported internally in 1954 and well over a million sent to laogai camps; thought reform for teachers and intellectuals from 1951. Interim judgement: organised terror removed anyone capable of leading opposition.","Paragraph 2, for Interpretation 2 — terror that used the population. Land reform under the Agrarian Reform Law of June 1950 carried through by speaking bitterness meetings, with landlord deaths estimated between about one million and two million; the 'three antis' of December 1951 and the 'five antis' of early 1952 doing the same in the towns. Interim judgement: people who had done the accusing could not go back, which is what 'carefully organised' really means.","Paragraph 3, for Interpretation 2 — the closing of 1957. Criticism invited in the Hundred Flowers Campaign from May 1956, flowing in the spring of 1957, ended by Mao in June 1957, followed by Deng Xiaoping's Anti-Rightist Campaign and about 550,000 people labelled. Interim judgement: the same method was still in use at the end of the period, so it was not a temporary emergency.","Paragraph 4, against Interpretation 2 — what people gained. About 300 million peasants receiving land; the Marriage Law of 1 May 1950; hyperinflation brought under control after the collapse of the gold yuan; the Korean War allowing the government to appear as the defender of China. Interim judgement: Interpretation 1 is right that there was something to consent to.","Paragraph 5, against Interpretation 2 and the point that goes furthest beyond both texts — the machine. Party membership about four and a half million in 1949 and 10.73 million reported in 1956, organised by democratic centralism; the PLA administering six great regions until 1954; the Common Programme of September 1949 and the constitution of 1954. Interim judgement: fear explains why nobody resisted, but not how a country of this size was actually administered.","The evaluation thread that must run through every paragraph: how each interpretation conveys its view. Interpretation 2 writes of a system — quotas, movements, camps, a supply — so that people appear in the passive; Interpretation 1 writes of a bargain — land, a law, prices, membership worth having — so that they appear as parties to it. Saying this, and using it, is what the top level means by showing how the differences of view are conveyed.","What the conclusion must say: not 'both are partly right'. It must apply the criterion announced at the start — that Interpretation 2 explains why opposition was never organised, which is the harder half of the question, but cannot explain why millions joined the party, why land reform and the Marriage Law were welcomed, or how the country was governed between campaigns. Then name the deciding consideration: terror cleared the ground, but the party machine and the early gains are what the CCP stood on once it was cleared, so Interpretation 2 is convincing about the absence of resistance and incomplete about the presence of rule.","Timing and SPaG: about 40 minutes in all — six to plan the paragraphs and fix the criterion, about thirty to write, and the last four to check spelling and punctuation and to make sure the specialist terms are present and correctly used, since 4 additional marks depend on 'a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate'. Useful terms here: Guomindang, cadre, democratic centralism, Common Programme, land reform, struggle meeting, counter-revolutionary, laogai, rectification, rightist, People's Liberation Army."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:44:19Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"Re-judged after the fix pass: every one of the nine recorded fixes is present in the kit and does what it claims. The four material faults of the first verdict are gone - lesson.examples[1] now works a genuinely different enquiry (the rewards offered for co-operation) off different features of Sources B and C, lesson.examples[4] moved to the towns and decides on the different purpose of the urban campaigns instead of the spring of 1957, lesson.examples[2] and [0] were retargeted, and room.questions[12] now asks about 'the way land reform was carried out', which is the view Interpretation 2 actually gives, with a sol that judges the method claim instead of conceding a mismatch; dupscan reports no duplication and kit-tools check passes. I re-solved all 14 questions and re-checked the new and changed facts on the web: execution targets set from the centre and passed to the provinces (the February 1951 Central Committee rate and Mao's city-by-city targets), the 712,000 figure now correctly described as reported internally in 1954 rather than published, the campaign dated consistently 1950-51 in all eleven places, roughly two-fifths of the cultivated land to about 300 million peasants, membership 4.5 million to 10.73 million, the three antis from December 1951 and five antis from early 1952 ending in confessions, fines and back taxes with private industry absorbed into joint state-private ownership by 1956 - all sound, and lesson.idea[4] now leads on the campaigns its key-idea code names. Nothing factual is wrong and nothing breaches the paper; what is left is a stale worked-example title, a 16-mark answer six words long, and two soft points of emphasis. Publish, and fix the title on the next touch."}}},"32-2":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"32-2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 2 is the economic spine of Option 32, and Paper 3 keeps returning to it because it contains the two things examiners most like to set: a causation question with a clear chain (land reform to cooperation to collectivisation to the commune) and a historical argument that is still genuinely open (who or what caused the famine of 1959 to 1961). Section A's 12-mark 'Explain why' is regularly set on why collectivisation was forced through, why the Great Leap Forward was launched or why the famine happened; Section B's interpretations are almost always arguments about whether the disaster was the fault of a system or of a man. If you can move from the Agrarian Reform Law of 1950 to the Sixty Articles of the early 1960s with dates, names and figures attached, and can say what each change was meant to achieve and what it actually did, you can answer every question this topic can ask.","idea":[{"h":"Breaking the landlords, 1950 to 1952","code":"32-2 — 1 Early changes in agriculture, 1949–57: The attacks on landlordism","t":"The Agrarian Reform Law came into force on 30 June 1950 and by 1952 land reform was largely complete across China. Work teams of Party cadres were sent into the villages to sort households into landlords, rich peasants, middle peasants and poor peasants, then to hold speak-bitterness meetings at which tenants accused their landlords in public; these often became struggle sessions ending in beatings, confiscation and execution. About 700 million mu of land, roughly 47 million hectares, changed hands to around 300 million peasants, together with tools, animals and grain stores. Estimates of landlords killed range from several hundred thousand to one or two million, which is why you should give the range and not a single figure. A top-level answer never stops at 'land was redistributed': it says that the campaign destroyed the class that had collected the rents and run the villages, put the Party in its place, and made millions of peasants complicit in the change so that they could not easily go back."},{"h":"From mutual aid teams to cooperatives, 1951 to 1955","code":"32-2 — 1 Early changes in agriculture, 1949–57: Moves towards agricultural cooperation","t":"Redistribution left tiny family plots that could not support machinery, irrigation or a reliable state grain supply, so the CCP moved peasants up a ladder of cooperation. Mutual aid teams, formed from 1951, grouped up to about ten households to share labour, tools and draught animals while each family kept its own land; by the end of 1952 around 40 per cent of peasants had joined. From 1953, after the Central Committee's resolution of December that year, came lower-stage agricultural producers' cooperatives of roughly thirty to fifty households, in which land was pooled but members were still paid partly for the land they had brought in. Each step was presented as voluntary and each step removed a little more independence. A top-level answer uses the ladder as an argument: it shows that cooperation was never an end in itself but the means by which the state reached the grain it needed for industry."},{"h":"Why cooperation became compulsory collectivisation, 1955 to 1956","code":"32-2 — 1 Early changes in agriculture, 1949–57: Reasons for the change from voluntary to enforced collectivisation","t":"Voluntary progress was too slow for the first Five-Year Plan, which needed a grain surplus bought cheaply from the countryside to pay for factories; some cadres were even dissolving cooperatives that had been set up badly. In July 1955 Mao intervened personally, attacking colleagues who were moving too timidly, and the result was the socialist high tide of the winter of 1955 to 1956. Higher-stage cooperatives, or collectives, of two to three hundred households now took the land into common ownership and paid members for labour alone, so a peasant's former holding earned him nothing. By the end of 1956 about 96 per cent of peasant households were in cooperatives and roughly 88 per cent of those in higher-stage collectives. A top-level answer explains the change as a collision of three pressures — the plan's need for grain, Mao's fear that a new rich-peasant class was re-forming, and his impatience with cautious officials — rather than listing the stages."},{"h":"The first Five-Year Plan, 1953 to 1957, and what the USSR supplied","code":"32-2 — 2 Industry and the Five-Year Plan, 1953–57: Reasons for, and targets of, the first Five-Year Plan","t":"The plan ran from 1953 to 1957 and copied the Soviet model: central targets, nationalised industry, and nearly half of all state investment poured into heavy industry — coal, steel, electricity, machine tools, chemicals and armaments — rather than into consumer goods or farming. Its backbone was Soviet aid under the 1950 Treaty of Friendship: loans, machinery, blueprints, thousands of Soviet advisers in China and Chinese engineers trained in the USSR, and above all the 156 large industrial projects designed and equipped by the Soviet Union, of which 133 had been completed by the end of 1960. New industrial cities such as Anshan and Wuhan were built around them. A top-level answer treats Soviet support as the plan's condition rather than its decoration, and so can explain both why output rose so fast to 1957 and why the withdrawal of the experts in 1960 hurt so much."},{"h":"What the first plan achieved, and what it did not","code":"32-2 — 2 Industry and the Five-Year Plan, 1953–57: Success and failures of the first Five-Year Plan","t":"Judged on industry the plan was a striking success: steel rose from about 1.3 million tonnes in 1952 to about 5.35 million in 1957, coal from about 66 million to about 131 million tonnes, and the gross value of industrial output more than doubled. Judged on agriculture it was not: grain grew by under 4 per cent a year, from about 164 million tonnes in 1952 to under 200 million in 1957, barely ahead of a population that was growing fast, so there was no large surplus to fund the next stage. The costs were also real — dependence on the USSR, a privileged managerial and technical elite, and consumer goods and rural living standards left behind. A top-level answer states the criterion before it judges: successful in heavy industry, unsuccessful in feeding the growth it was meant to pay for, and that imbalance is the direct cause of what Mao did next."},{"h":"Why Mao launched the Great Leap Forward in 1958","code":"32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: Mao's reasons for the second Five-Year Plan","t":"The second Five-Year Plan, announced in 1958, was to be a Great Leap Forward rather than a repeat of the first. Mao's reasons were economic, international and personal. Economically, agriculture had not kept pace, so the only way to finance industry was to raise both at once — 'walking on two legs', industry and farming together. Internationally, he disliked the bureaucracy and expert privilege the Soviet model had brought, distrusted Khrushchev after the secret speech of 1956, and wanted a Chinese road to socialism: in 1957 he declared that China would overtake Britain in steel output within fifteen years. Personally, the Hundred Flowers campaign and the Anti-Rightist campaign of 1957 had removed his critics, and he believed the energy of over 600 million people could take the place of capital and machinery. A top-level answer connects those three strands to show why the Leap was conceived as ideology and economics at the same time."},{"h":"Steel, targets and the Lushan Conference of 1959","code":"32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: The successes and failures of the Great Leap Forward","t":"On 19 June 1958 Mao pressed for the year's steel output to be doubled, and the target of 10.7 million tonnes, roughly twice the 1957 figure of about 5.35 million tonnes, was fixed at the Beidaihe conference of 17 to 30 August 1958. It was to be met partly by about 600,000 backyard furnaces in which villagers melted down tools, woks and door hinges, and perhaps a third of the steel claimed for 1958 was too impure to use. Huge irrigation and reservoir schemes were built by hand, and some genuine gains were made in canals, dams and rural literacy. The turning point was the Lushan Conference of July and August 1959, where Peng Dehuai, Minister of National Defence, sent Mao a private letter of opinion on 14 July criticising the waste and the false figures. Mao circulated it, treated it as an attack, dismissed Peng in September and replaced him with Lin Biao. A top-level answer makes Lushan the hinge: after it, the Leap was not corrected but intensified, and no official dared report bad news."},{"h":"Inside the commune: mess halls, no private plots, and Lysenko","code":"32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: The organisation of the commune","t":"The Beidaihe meeting of August 1958 resolved to set up people's communes, and by the end of the year around 26,000 of them covered almost the whole countryside, the average commune holding some 5,000 households. Private plots and private trade were abolished, families ate free in communal mess halls, nurseries and canteens released women for fieldwork and furnace work, and militia-style brigades were marched to their tasks. Farming methods were dictated from above along the lines of the Soviet agronomist Trofim Lysenko, whose ideas shaped Mao's Eight Point Charter for agriculture of 1958: close planting, which left seedlings competing for light and water, and deep ploughing, which turned up infertile subsoil. The Four Pests campaign of 1958 destroyed sparrows, and insects multiplied. A top-level answer explains the commune as the machine that made the famine possible: it removed every private reserve at exactly the moment the state's demands rose."},{"h":"The famine of 1959 to 1961","code":"32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: The causes and impact of the Great Famine","t":"Grain output fell from about 200 million tonnes in 1958 to roughly 140 million in 1960. The causes were layered: labour taken from the fields to furnaces and reservoirs; Lysenko's methods wasting seed and soil; free mess halls consuming reserves early; cadres reporting harvests two or three times the real figure so that state procurement quotas, set on those reports, stripped villages of the grain they had actually grown; grain still being exported and used to repay Soviet debts; and, after Lushan, no one willing to admit a shortfall. Drought in 1960 and floods elsewhere made a disaster worse but cannot explain its scale. Estimates of deaths range from about 15 million to over 40 million. A top-level answer ranks these causes rather than listing them, and says clearly which are policy, which are administration and which are nature."},{"h":"Why private farming came back","code":"32-2 — 4 Economic reform: Reasons for the restoration of private farming","t":"By 1961 the leadership could no longer pretend. Communes were cut to roughly a third of their original size from 1961, mess halls were closed, backyard furnaces were abandoned and grain was imported from Canada and Australia. At the Seven Thousand Cadres Conference in Beijing, 11 January to 7 February 1962, Liu Shaoqi told the assembled officials that the disaster was three parts natural and seven parts man-made, and Mao made a limited self-criticism and withdrew from day-to-day direction of the economy. That created the political space in which Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping could restore private plots, household sidelines and rural markets — the 'three freedoms' — and Deng could remark in 1962 that the colour of the cat did not matter so long as it caught mice. A top-level answer treats the restoration as a political event as much as an economic one: it was allowed because Mao had been discredited, and it is what he later punished."},{"h":"Recovery under Liu and Deng, 1962 to 1965","code":"32-2 — 4 Economic reform: Reasons for, and features of, economic reform (1962–65)","t":"The Sixty Articles on the work of the rural people's communes, drafted in 1961 and confirmed in 1962, cut the commune down and in 1962 made the production team of about twenty to thirty households the basic accounting unit, so that effort and reward were linked again; peasants kept private plots of a few per cent of the land and could sell the surplus. In industry the policy of readjustment and consolidation, begun in 1961, closed loss-making plants, cut investment and restored managers, experts, wage differences and piece rates. Grain output and industrial production recovered by the mid-1960s. But the recovery had a political price: Mao regarded these measures as a slide back towards capitalism and launched the Socialist Education Movement of 1963 to 1965 to reassert class struggle in the villages. A top-level answer ends here, because it explains both why the economy recovered and why the Cultural Revolution followed."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1: two inferences from Source A about the landlords' power","setup":"Source A in this room is a report written for a county Party committee in Hebei province by a member of a land reform work team in the spring of 1951. It says that the team came in the ninth month and spent three weeks going from house to house before any meeting was called, so that the two hundred and ten households could be sorted into landlord, rich, middle and poor; that only when the lists were read out in the square did the village believe the team; that at the first accusation meeting a widow told how she had paid more than half of every harvest in rent and had still inherited her husband's debt, after which the shouting could not be quietened and the accusations ran into a second night; that the landlord's family held about one fifth of the fields and both of the large ponds; that his land, his tools, his two oxen and the grain in his store have been divided among ninety-six poor households; and that the middle peasants were slow to come forward until the lists were published, fearing that if the wind changed the rent would be asked for again. The question is: 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about the power of landlords in the Chinese countryside before 1950. Complete the table below to explain your answer.' (4 marks)","steps":["Decide what the question is about before reading: not what land reform did, but what the report lets you see of the position landlords had held beforehand, so hunt for sentences that describe the old order.","Take the first inference from the share of resources: one family held about one fifth of the fields and both of the large ponds, so a landlord controlled resources out of all proportion to his numbers, and controlled water as well as land.","Take the second from the widow: she paid more than half of every harvest in rent and still inherited her husband's debt, so a landlord's hold came from rent and debt together, and outlived the tenant who owed it.","Write each one as a claim the report never states, followed by the words that force it, one pair to each box."],"cues":["Which period is this question actually asking about, and which sentences look backwards to it?","What is odd about a single family holding both of the large ponds?","What does the widow inheriting her husband's debt tell you that the rent figure alone does not?","Which half of each pair goes in the box for details?"]},{"title":"Question 2(a): planning the 12-mark 'Explain why' on the Sino-Soviet split","setup":"The question is: 'Explain why the ending of Soviet help damaged the Chinese economy in the years 1960 to 1962. You may use the following in your answer: • the 156 Soviet-aided industrial projects • the Great Leap Forward. You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks). You know that Soviet loans, blueprints, machinery and advisers built the backbone of the first Five-Year Plan from 1953; that 156 large plants were designed and equipped by the USSR, of which 133 were finished by the end of 1960; that relations soured after Khrushchev's secret speech of 1956 and over the Leap; that the Soviet government gave notice on 16 July 1960 and its experts left China between late July and the beginning of September, taking blueprints with them and leaving contracts cancelled; and that China was at the same time in famine and repaying Soviet loans in grain.","steps":["Fix the conceptual focus in one sentence before writing: the question asks what the loss of Soviet help did to output and to the ability to recover, not why the two states fell out.","Reason one, the unfinished plants: 133 of the 156 projects had been completed by the end of 1960 and the rest were still being built when the advisers went, so factories stood half-finished without the drawings or the engineers to complete them, and the industries depending on their output stalled.","Reason two, the loss of skill: Chinese industry had been designed around Soviet methods and Soviet-trained staff, so removing the advisers removed the people who could diagnose faults, exactly when backyard-furnace steel had already filled the system with unusable metal.","Reason three, beyond both stimulus points, the debt: repayments to the Soviet Union were made in goods, including food, while the famine was at its worst, so the split took grain out of China as well as machinery.","Close by linking the three: the withdrawal did not simply subtract Soviet output, it removed the means of finishing, repairing and paying for what the Leap had already damaged."],"cues":["What exactly is the outcome this question wants explained?","What happens to a factory that is three-quarters built when its designers leave?","Machinery can be copied; what cannot?","Which of your reasons comes from neither bullet point, and why does the mark scheme want one?","What must the last sentence do that the three paragraphs have not already done?"]},{"title":"Question 3(a): weighing Sources B and C for an enquiry into how harvests were reported","setup":"Source B in this room is a report of October 1958 from the secretary of a people's commune in Henan to his provincial Party committee: the commune was formed in the ninth month from twenty-seven cooperatives and holds eleven thousand households; everyone eats free in one of ninety-four mess halls; no family keeps a private cooking pot and the pots have gone into the furnaces; one hundred and eighty furnaces run day and night; four in every ten able-bodied members have been taken off the fields for the furnaces and the reservoir; the nurseries have released the women; and he reports that the autumn harvest is 'at least double last year's' and asks that the commune's quota be set accordingly, 'for we shall have grain to spare'. Source C is a letter of March 1960 from a doctor in an Anhui county hospital to her brother in Shanghai: the mess hall ration has been cut twice since the New Year and has stopped in nearby villages; 'the trucks came for the state quota in the autumn and what was left did not last the winter'; her ward is full of the swelling sickness; people live on husks, threshing-floor sweepings and elm bark; staff are told to write the cause of death as illness and not as hunger; and the commune office will not send word upward 'because the harvest it reported two years ago was far more than these fields have ever given'. The question is: 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the way harvest figures were reported to the government in the years 1958 to 1960? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your knowledge of the historical context.' (8 marks)","steps":["Ask what each source is an example of, not what it says: Source B is not a description of exaggerated reporting, it is a piece of exaggerated reporting, which for this enquiry makes it first-hand evidence rather than testimony.","Use its provenance on its content: a commune secretary writing upward in October 1958, whose standing depended on his figures, doubles his harvest and then asks for the quota to be set on that figure, which shows the mechanism by which a boast became a legal obligation to hand over grain that did not exist.","Turn to Source C and weigh nature and purpose against it: a private letter with no reader to impress, written from inside a badly hit province by someone who sees the patients, so her account of the local office refusing to send word upward is credible, though she is one doctor in one county and admits she must be careful what she writes.","Bring context in as a test rather than as decoration: the collapse of grain output from about 200 million tonnes in 1958 to roughly 140 million in 1960, and the dismissal of Peng Dehuai at Lushan in 1959, explain why Source B's claim was possible in 1958 and why nobody corrected it afterwards.","Judge for this enquiry: B is the more useful because it is the false report itself, while C is more useful for what false reports cost; say which and why rather than calling both useful."],"cues":["Is Source B evidence about exaggeration, or an example of it?","What does the secretary ask for in his last sentence, and why does that matter?","Which features of a private letter make a claim more believable, and which limit it?","Which piece of your own knowledge tests a claim in one of the sources rather than repeating it?","What does the final sentence of a Level 3 answer have to contain?"]},{"title":"Question 3(b): the difference of view about natural disaster","setup":"Interpretation 1 in this room, from a study of the Chinese countryside under Mao by a historian writing in the 1980s, argues that the famine 'was made by a system, not by a speech': the commune took from the peasant 'the three things that had always carried him through a bad year', his own plot, his own grain store and his own cooking pot; what a village kept was decided by a procurement quota fixed on invented figures; and 'when drought struck the north in 1960 and the rivers flooded in the south there was nothing underneath to catch the fall'. Interpretation 2, from a general history of Mao's China by a historian writing in the 2000s, argues that 'systems do not double a steel target overnight, and they do not go on repeating the same mistake for two more years while people die'; it names the steel figure Mao set, the close planting and deep ploughing he pressed on the communes, and his treatment of Peng Dehuai at Lushan, and it ends 'this famine had an author'. That drought and those floods are the only weather either text mentions. The question is: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. They give different views about the part played by natural disaster in the famine of 1959 to 1961. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations.' (4 marks)","steps":["Find the ground the question names before deciding anything: only one of the two texts mentions weather at all, so underline that clause and treat the other text's silence as its position rather than as an omission.","State the difference of view in a single sentence: Interpretation 1 allows a natural shortfall to begin the disaster and makes the countryside's lost defences decide how far it fell, while Interpretation 2 presents a famine that was chosen and then repeated, in which no season needs explaining.","Support it from each text in turn: Interpretation 1's image of 'nothing underneath to catch the fall' only works if something real is falling, and the drought and the floods are what fall; Interpretation 2's charge that the same mistake went on being repeated 'for two more years while people die', ending in 'this famine had an author', leaves the weather no work to do.","Check the finished answer states one difference of view and quotes from both, rather than describing each interpretation in turn and leaving the reader to compare them."],"cues":["Which of the two texts mentions the weather, and which clause exactly?","If one historian names a drought and a flood and the other names none, what are they disagreeing about?","What is falling in Interpretation 1's last image, and what should have been underneath it?","How many differences does this question want, and what has to sit beside the one you choose?"]},{"title":"Question 3(c): why the two historians differ about natural disaster","setup":"The same two interpretations as in the previous example, and the same issue: the part played by natural disaster in the famine of 1959 to 1961. Read the line printed above each extract as well as the extract itself. Interpretation 1 is from a study of the Chinese countryside under Mao, by a historian writing in the 1980s, and it dwells on the mess hall, the procurement quota and the drought and floods of 1960. Interpretation 2 is from a general history of Mao's China, by a historian writing in the 2000s, and it is built out of decisions taken at the top: the steel figure, close planting and deep ploughing, and Lushan. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer. Source B is a commune secretary's report to his provincial Party committee in October 1958, claiming a harvest 'at least double last year's' and asking that the quota be set accordingly. Source C is a private letter of March 1960 from a doctor in an Anhui county hospital to her brother, describing a mess hall ration cut twice since the New Year, trucks that 'came for the state quota in the autumn', wards full of the swelling sickness and people living on husks and elm bark. The question is: 'Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about the part played by natural disaster in the famine of 1959 to 1961. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer.' (4 marks)","steps":["Begin with the provenance line rather than the argument: one extract comes from a book about a place, the other from a book about a country and its ruler, and they were written about twenty years apart.","Name one reason and explain it as a single cause: writing two decades earlier, the first historian could reconstruct the famine mainly from what the countryside itself had left behind, and a book made of harvests, rations and quotas has to account for the season; the later historian wrote when far more was known about what had been decided in Beijing, and a book that can follow the decisions can explain the outcome without the weather.","Show the reason at work on the evidence in front of you: a letter like Source C is exactly the material the first kind of book is made of, and although it describes the hunger in detail and mentions the state quota, it names no decision-maker and no meeting at which the quota was set, so a historian working from such letters will reach for local conditions, the season among them, while one working from the record of meetings will not.","Close by making the reason a legitimate difference rather than an accusation: neither historian has the facts wrong, and the mark scheme expects you to say why the same past can honestly yield two accounts."],"cues":["What does the line above each extract tell you about the kind of book it came from, and about when it was written?","Why might a history organised around one man have no place in it for a drought?","Which of the room's two contemporary sources would a historian of the countryside be most likely to work from, and what does that source never mention?","Does saying that two historians differ have to mean that one of them is wrong?"]}],"check":[{"q":"In which month and year did the Agrarian Reform Law come into force, and what had it achieved by 1952?","a":"It came into force on 30 June 1950. By 1952 land reform was largely complete: about 700 million mu, roughly 47 million hectares, had been redistributed to around 300 million peasants, and the landlord class had been destroyed as a group, with estimates of those killed ranging from several hundred thousand to one or two million."},{"q":"What was the difference between a lower-stage agricultural producers' cooperative and a higher-stage collective?","a":"In a lower-stage cooperative, from 1953, of about thirty to fifty households, land was pooled but members were still paid partly for the land they had contributed. In a higher-stage collective, of two to three hundred households and general by the end of 1956, the land was owned in common and payment was for labour alone."},{"q":"Why is Source B in this room useful for an enquiry into the reporting of harvests, even though it contains a false figure?","a":"Because the false figure is the evidence. A commune secretary writing upward in October 1958 claims his harvest is at least double the previous year's and asks that the quota be set on that claim, which shows exactly how an invented figure became the basis of the grain the state took."},{"q":"What happened at the Lushan Conference of 1959, and why does it matter for explaining the famine?","a":"Peng Dehuai, Minister of National Defence, sent Mao a private letter of opinion on 14 July 1959 criticising the waste and the false figures of the Leap. Mao treated it as an attack, dismissed him in September and replaced him with Lin Biao. After that no official dared report a shortfall, so the Leap was intensified rather than corrected."},{"q":"Name two measures taken by Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping after 1961 that helped the economy recover.","a":"Any two of: communes cut to about a third of their size and the production team of twenty to thirty households made the basic accounting unit in 1962; private plots, household sidelines and rural markets restored under the Sixty Articles; mess halls and backyard furnaces abandoned; grain imported from Canada and Australia; and in industry the 1961 policy of readjustment and consolidation, which closed loss-making plants and restored managers, bonuses and piece rates."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 3 Section A (16 marks): Question 1 prints Source A and asks 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about...' in a table of two boxes each (4 marks); Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b) and you answer one of them, each 'Explain why...' with two stimulus points and 'You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks).","Paper 3 Section B (36 marks), all on Question 3: (a) 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into...?' (8); (b) 'What is the main difference between these views?' (4); (c) 'Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about...' (4); (d) 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about...?' (16, plus up to 4 for spelling, punctuation, grammar and the use of specialist terminology).","The stimulus-point caps on the 12-mark 'Explain why...': 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'. There is no cap at Level 2. Question 3(d) prints no stimulus points, so no such cap applies to it.","Timing on Paper 3: 1 hour 30 minutes for 52 marks, so about a minute a mark plus thinking time. Roughly 5 minutes on Question 1, 18 on Question 2, 12 on 3(a), 6 each on 3(b) and 3(c), and about 20 minutes on 3(d), a minute a mark, plus three or four minutes for the SPaG check, which leaves time to read the sources and to plan.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose, and usefulness is always judged for the enquiry the question names, never in general. Contextual knowledge earns nothing on 3(a) or 3(d) unless it is used on the sources or the interpretations.","Five dates that anchor this key topic: 30 June 1950, the Agrarian Reform Law; 1953 to 1957, the first Five-Year Plan; August 1958, the Beidaihe resolution on people's communes; July to August 1959, the Lushan Conference; 11 January to 7 February 1962, the Seven Thousand Cadres Conference."],"questions":[{"q":"Question 1. Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about land reform in the villages of China in the years 1950 to 1952. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for each valid inference up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail selected from Source A. Credit direction by Party work teams from outside the village, the classification of households, or the transfer of tools, animals and stored grain as well as land.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: land reform was organised and directed by the Party from outside the village rather than begun by the villagers themselves. Details in the source that tell me this: a work team came and spent three weeks going from house to house sorting the two hundred and ten households into classes before any meeting was called, and 'only when the lists were read out in the square did the village believe us'. (ii) What I can infer: land reform transferred far more than fields. Details in the source that tell me this: the landlord's 'land, his tools, his two oxen and the grain in his store' were divided among ninety-six poor households.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["An inference is something the report makes certain without stating it; ask who is doing the work in this village and where they came from.","Read the report once for what happens to the landlord and once for the order in which the team does things, because the sequence is itself evidence.","Each answer is two parts: a claim the source never makes, then the exact words that force it."],"codes":["32-2 — 1 Early changes in agriculture, 1949–57: The attacks on landlordism"]},{"q":"Question 1. Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about the attitudes of villagers towards land reform. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark per valid inference up to two, plus a second mark each for supporting detail from Source A. Credit deep anger among the poor, caution or fear among the better-off peasants, or distrust that the change would last.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: many of the poorer villagers felt real anger towards the landlord. Details in the source that tell me this: after the widow described paying more than half of every harvest in rent and inheriting her husband's debt, 'the shouting could not be quietened' and the accusations ran on into a second night. (ii) What I can infer: not all villagers trusted that the change would last. Details in the source that tell me this: the middle peasants were 'slow to come forward until the lists were published', fearing 'that if the wind changed the rent would be asked for again'.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Villagers are not one group in this report; look for the two different kinds of people it describes.","One inference should come from how the meeting behaved, the other from who hung back and why.","Put the claim in the first box and the words that prove it in the second, for each of the two."],"codes":["32-2 — 1 Early changes in agriculture, 1949–57: The attacks on landlordism"]},{"q":"Question 3(b). Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. They give different views about the reasons for the famine of 1959 to 1961. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4): a key difference of view identified and supported from both interpretations — Interpretation 1 places the cause in the commune system and the officials who worked it, Interpretation 2 in Mao's own decisions. A difference of surface detail only stays in Level 1.","sol":"The main difference of view is about where responsibility for the famine lies. Interpretation 1 places it in the system and in the many people who worked it: the commune took from the peasant 'his own plot, his own grain store and his own cooking pot', quotas were fixed on figures 'that local cadres had invented to protect their own positions', and it concludes that 'millions of officials, not one man, turned a poor harvest into a catastrophe'. Interpretation 2 places it on Mao personally — he raised the steel target himself, pressed Lysenko's methods and dismissed Peng Dehuai at Lushan — and concludes that 'this famine had an author'.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Start by listing what the two interpretations agree actually happened, because that is not the difference.","A difference of view is a disagreement about the past, not about wording; ask what each text is answering when it asks who or what was to blame.","One difference, stated in a sentence, then a phrase from each interpretation that shows it."],"codes":["32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: The causes and impact of the Great Famine","32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: The organisation of the commune"]},{"q":"Question 3(c). Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about the reasons for the famine of 1959 to 1961. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4): one reason for difference explained and substantiated by analysis of the interpretations — they examine different levels of the same events and so give weight to different evidence. Credit legitimate differences drawn from the same evidence.","sol":"They differ because they ask the question at different levels and so give weight to different evidence. Interpretation 1 looks at the countryside from below, which is why it dwells on the mess hall, the procurement quota and the drought and floods of 1960, and why it explains false figures by cadres protecting 'their own positions'. Interpretation 2 looks at the same events from the leadership downwards, so the same false reporting becomes a consequence of Lushan. Source B, a commune secretary's doubled harvest claim of October 1958, supports Interpretation 1, because it was written nine months before Lushan by a man protecting himself. Both conclusions are legitimately drawn.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Historians rarely differ because one of them has the facts wrong; ask what each one chose to look at.","Read Source B twice, once as each historian would read it, and notice what changes.","Name one reason, then show it at work in a phrase from each interpretation."],"codes":["32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: The causes and impact of the Great Famine","32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: The successes and failures of the Great Leap Forward"]},{"q":"Question 3(a). Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the effects of the people's communes on farming in the years 1958 to 1959? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): utility judged for this enquiry with developed reasoning on how nature, origin and purpose affect the content — B written inside a commune at the height of the change by a man whose standing depended on success; C a private letter of 1960, later than the enquiry but confirming the false reporting. Context used to interpret, not to describe.","sol":"Source B is very useful because it comes from inside the institution the enquiry is about, written in October 1958 as the communes were being formed. It shows what the commune did to farming: twenty-seven cooperatives merged into one unit of eleven thousand households, 'four in every ten of our able-bodied members' taken off the fields for furnaces and a reservoir, nurseries releasing women for the same work, and every private cooking pot handed in. Its provenance sharpens rather than weakens this: a commune secretary reporting upward offers these as achievements, so evidence of labour leaving the fields is evidence he had no motive to invent. His claim that the harvest is 'at least double last year's' cannot be taken as a figure, but read against the collapse of grain output from about 200 million tonnes in 1958 to roughly 140 million by 1960, it is itself useful evidence of how commune reporting worked. Source C is less useful for 1958 to 1959 because it is dated March 1960 and describes consequences rather than farming; but as a private letter with no reader to impress it is credible, and its remark that the harvest reported 'two years ago was far more than these fields have ever given' reaches back into the enquiry's period and confirms Source B's boast was false. For this enquiry B is the more useful, being from inside both the period and the institution, while C supplies the test that B cannot supply for itself.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Name the enquiry in your first sentence and keep judging against it; a source can be unreliable and still highly useful.","Take each source in turn: what its content offers this enquiry, then how its nature, origin and purpose change the value of that content.","Ask what each source's date lets it see and what it cannot see, then end by saying which is more useful here and why."],"codes":["32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: The organisation of the commune","32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: The successes and failures of the Great Leap Forward"]},{"q":"Question 3(a). Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into food shortages in the Chinese countryside in the years 1959 to 1960? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): C judged highly useful as eyewitness medical testimony from a badly hit province in a private letter, with its limits of scope stated; B judged useful for the causes of shortage rather than for the shortage itself. Contextual knowledge (procurement quotas, grain output, Lushan) used to interpret the sources.","sol":"Source C is the more useful of the two for this enquiry because its writer saw the shortage at first hand and had no reason to soften it. She is a doctor in an Anhui county hospital in March 1960, in one of the provinces worst affected, and her ward is 'full of the swelling sickness, legs and faces puffed up', which is the oedema of prolonged starvation; her patients are living on 'husks, on the sweepings of the threshing floor and on elm bark'. Because this is a private letter and not a report, its purpose is to warn a brother rather than to persuade an official, and her opening instruction not to write back shows she knew the risk. Her account also explains the mechanism: 'the trucks came for the state quota in the autumn and what was left did not last the winter', which matches the procurement system that took the state's share from reported rather than real harvests. Its limits are scope, one county and one ward, and caution, since she cannot write freely. Source B is not about shortage at all, yet it is useful for its causes: written in October 1958, it shows four in ten able-bodied members taken off the fields and a harvest claimed at double the truth with the quota asked to match. Read with the dismissal of Peng Dehuai at Lushan in 1959, it explains why the shortage C describes was possible and why nobody corrected it.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Decide first which source is evidence of the shortage and which is evidence of its causes; both can serve the enquiry.","Use provenance to argue about value: what does a private letter make possible that an official report does not, and what does it still not show?","Bring in one precise piece of context that tests a claim rather than repeating it, then judge which source serves this enquiry better."],"codes":["32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: The causes and impact of the Great Famine","32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: The organisation of the commune"]},{"q":"Question 2(a). Explain why the CCP attacked the landlords in the years 1950 to 1952. You may use the following in your answer: • the Agrarian Reform Law (1950) • speak-bitterness meetings. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise support — the Agrarian Reform Law of 30 June 1950, work teams and class categories, about 700 million mu redistributed to some 300 million peasants, the destruction of a rival governing class, and the grain surplus the new state needed. Stimulus cap: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"The CCP attacked the landlords because doing so solved three different problems at once: it won the countryside, it removed a rival authority, and it put the new state in reach of the grain it would need. The first reason is political support. The CCP had won the civil war from the countryside, and in the northern liberated areas it had already broken up landlord holdings under the Outline Land Law of October 1947. What it lacked in 1949 was any organisation of its own in the villages of the centre and south it had just conquered, where, as everywhere in China, four in five people were peasants. The Agrarian Reform Law of 30 June 1950 answered that directly: by 1952 about 700 million mu of land, roughly 47 million hectares, had passed to around 300 million peasants, along with tools, animals and stored grain. A peasant who had been given a landlord's field had a personal stake in the survival of the government that gave it to him. The second reason is the destruction of a rival governing class. Landlords, clan heads and village elders had collected the rents, lent the money and settled the disputes, and no Communist state could govern through them. Speak-bitterness meetings were the instrument: work teams classified every household as landlord, rich, middle or poor, then staged public accusations that turned into struggle sessions, confiscation and execution, with estimates of those killed running from several hundred thousand to one or two million. The old authority was not merely replaced but publicly destroyed, and Party cadres and peasant associations took its place. The third reason, which neither bullet point prompts, is economic. Industrialisation would have to be paid for out of the countryside, and a state that could not reach past the landlord could not reach the harvest; land reform put the Party in direct contact with every household from which grain would later be bought at low, fixed prices. Politics, control and money therefore pointed the same way, and the violence of the campaign was not an accident of it but the means by which all three were secured.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Distinguish what the attack on landlords gave the peasants from what it gave the Party; they are not the same reason.","Plan three reasons of different kinds — political, governmental and economic — so the answer analyses rather than narrates.","Open the first paragraph on where the Party's power did and did not reach in 1949, and close it by saying what land reform changed about that."],"codes":["32-2 — 1 Early changes in agriculture, 1949–57: The attacks on landlordism"]},{"q":"Question 2(b). Explain why agricultural cooperation in China had become compulsory collectivisation by 1956. You may use the following in your answer: • mutual aid teams • the first Five-Year Plan. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise support — mutual aid teams from 1951 and around 40 per cent of peasants by the end of 1952, lower-stage cooperatives from 1953, the plan's need for cheap grain, Mao's intervention of July 1955 and the socialist high tide, 96 per cent of households in cooperatives by the end of 1956. Stimulus cap: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"Collectivisation became compulsory because the voluntary route was too slow for what the state needed, and because Mao decided that slowness was itself a political danger. The first reason is the demand of the first Five-Year Plan. From 1953 the plan poured nearly half of all state investment into heavy industry, and that had to be financed by buying grain cheaply from the countryside. Millions of separate family farms, created by land reform itself, were almost impossible to tax or to buy from reliably, whereas a cooperative could simply be given a quota. The second reason is the pace of voluntary cooperation. Mutual aid teams, introduced from 1951, grouped up to ten households to share labour, tools and animals while each family kept its land, yet by the end of 1952 only about 40 per cent of peasants had joined; the lower-stage cooperatives that followed the Central Committee's resolution of December 1953 still paid members partly for the land they had brought in, so the richer peasant kept an advantage and little changed. Some cadres were even dissolving cooperatives that had been formed badly. The third reason, beyond both bullet points, is Mao's own reading of the danger. He feared that land reform was producing a new rich-peasant class, as those who prospered bought out those who did not, and in July 1955 he intervened personally against colleagues he thought were moving too timidly. The result was the socialist high tide of the winter of 1955 to 1956: higher-stage collectives of two to three hundred households took land into common ownership and paid for labour alone, and by the end of 1956 about 96 per cent of peasant households were in cooperatives, roughly 88 per cent of them in these collectives. The plan supplied the need, the mutual aid teams showed that persuasion would not meet it, and Mao supplied the decision that turned a ladder into a command.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["The question is about a change of method, so ask what was wrong with the voluntary method rather than describing the stages.","Three reasons work well here: what the state needed, what voluntary cooperation delivered, and who decided to stop waiting.","Begin with what the first Five-Year Plan required from the countryside, and make every later paragraph answer back to it."],"codes":["32-2 — 1 Early changes in agriculture, 1949–57: Moves towards agricultural cooperation","32-2 — 1 Early changes in agriculture, 1949–57: Reasons for the change from voluntary to enforced collectivisation"]},{"q":"Question 2(a). Explain why the first Five-Year Plan increased China's industrial output in the years 1953 to 1957. You may use the following in your answer: • Soviet financial and technical support • heavy industry. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise support — the 1950 Treaty of Friendship, loans, advisers and the 156 Soviet-designed projects (133 complete by the end of 1960), concentration of investment on heavy industry, state control of prices and grain, steel from about 1.3 to about 5.35 million tonnes and coal from about 66 to about 131 million tonnes. Stimulus cap: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"Industrial output rose because China borrowed a model that worked for heavy industry, borrowed the means to build it, and used the power of the state to pay for it. The first reason is Soviet support. Under the 1950 Treaty of Friendship the USSR supplied loans, machinery, blueprints and thousands of advisers, trained Chinese engineers, and above all designed and equipped 156 large industrial projects, of which 133 were finished by the end of 1960. These were not scattered workshops but complete plants — steelworks at Anshan and Wuhan, machine-tool and chemical works, power stations — so China acquired working factories and the expertise to run them at the same time. The second reason is the choice of heavy industry. Nearly half of all state investment went into coal, steel, electricity, machine tools and armaments, at the deliberate expense of consumer goods, so the money was concentrated where output could be measured in tonnes and targets could be hit: steel rose from about 1.3 million tonnes in 1952 to about 5.35 million in 1957, coal from about 66 million to about 131 million tonnes, and gross industrial output more than doubled. The third reason, beyond both bullet points, is the state's grip on the rest of the economy. Industry had been nationalised, prices and wages were set centrally, and grain was bought compulsorily from the countryside at low prices, which is exactly why cooperation in agriculture was being pushed through at the same time; without that forced transfer from the villages the investment could not have been found. Soviet expertise therefore supplied the plants, central planning pointed them at the sectors that grew fastest, and the countryside paid the bill — which also explains the plan's weakness, since grain itself grew by under 4 per cent a year.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Growth of this kind needs three things: knowledge, direction and money. Ask where each came from.","Keep the answer on why output rose, not on what the plan was; every paragraph should end in a consequence for production.","Start with what the USSR actually delivered, being precise about the 156 projects, and say why that mattered more than money alone."],"codes":["32-2 — 2 Industry and the Five-Year Plan, 1953–57: Reasons for, and targets of, the first Five-Year Plan","32-2 — 2 Industry and the Five-Year Plan, 1953–57: Success and failures of the first Five-Year Plan"]},{"q":"Question 2(b). Explain why Mao launched the Great Leap Forward in 1958. You may use the following in your answer: • the results of the first Five-Year Plan • relations with the USSR. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise support — grain growing under 4 per cent a year against fast industrial growth, dislike of the Soviet model and distrust of Khrushchev after 1956, the pledge of 1957 to overtake Britain in steel in fifteen years, and the free hand Mao gained from the Anti-Rightist campaign of 1957. Stimulus cap: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"Mao launched the Great Leap Forward because the first plan had succeeded in a way that could not be repeated, because he no longer wanted the Soviet model, and because by 1958 nobody was left to argue with him. The first reason is the imbalance the first plan left behind. Industry had more than doubled its output by 1957, but grain rose by under 4 per cent a year, from about 164 million tonnes in 1952 to under 200 million in 1957, barely ahead of a fast-growing population. There was therefore no surplus to finance a second plan on the same lines, and the answer Mao chose was to raise industry and agriculture together, walking on two legs, using labour where capital was lacking. The second reason is China's changing relationship with the USSR. The Soviet model had brought planners, experts and privileged managers, all of which Mao distrusted as a new elite, and after Khrushchev's secret speech of 1956 he doubted Soviet leadership of the communist world. A Chinese road that produced faster results would settle both arguments, which is why in 1957 he declared that China would overtake Britain in steel output within fifteen years. The third reason, beyond both bullet points, is that he had cleared his own path. The Hundred Flowers campaign of 1956 to 1957 had drawn out critics and the Anti-Rightist campaign that followed had punished them, so caution inside the Party had become dangerous to express. To that Mao added a genuine belief that the energy of more than 600 million mobilised people could take the place of machinery and foreign help. Economics gave him the problem, the quarrel with Moscow gave him the motive for a distinctively Chinese answer, and the silencing of his critics allowed an answer that no one would test before it was tried on the whole country.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["A launch has a problem behind it and a permission in front of it; identify both before you write.","Three strands work well: what the first plan had not solved, what Mao wanted from the USSR, and what had happened to his critics in 1957.","Open on the gap between industrial and agricultural growth to 1957 and say why that gap made a second plan of the same kind impossible."],"codes":["32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: Mao's reasons for the second Five-Year Plan","32-2 — 2 Industry and the Five-Year Plan, 1953–57: Success and failures of the first Five-Year Plan"]},{"q":"Question 2(a). Explain why there was a famine in China in the years 1959 to 1961. You may use the following in your answer: • the people's communes • the Lushan Conference (1959). You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise support — labour diverted to furnaces and reservoirs, Lysenko's close planting and deep ploughing, the abolition of private plots and the mess halls, inflated harvest reports driving procurement quotas, Peng Dehuai's dismissal after Lushan, and weather in 1960 as an aggravating rather than a sufficient cause. Stimulus cap: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"The famine happened because the commune destroyed the peasant's means of surviving a bad year at exactly the moment when policy was creating one, and because after 1959 nobody could say so. The first reason is the misuse of labour and land. From August 1958 around 26,000 communes covered almost the whole countryside, and tens of millions of workers were moved off the fields to the roughly 600,000 backyard furnaces and to reservoir and canal schemes. What remained was farmed by methods dictated from above along the lines of the Soviet agronomist Trofim Lysenko: close planting left seedlings starved of light and water, deep ploughing turned up infertile subsoil, and the Four Pests campaign of 1958 destroyed the sparrows that had eaten crop insects. Grain output fell from about 200 million tonnes in 1958 to roughly 140 million by 1960. The second reason is that the commune had removed every private reserve. Private plots and private trade were abolished, families surrendered their cooking pots and ate free in communal mess halls that consumed the harvest early, and no household kept a store of its own. A shortfall that a family might once have survived now had nothing beneath it. The third reason, which neither bullet point states outright, is the machinery of false figures. Cadres reported harvests two or three times the truth and state procurement quotas were set on those reports, so villages handed over grain they had never grown while China went on exporting grain and repaying Soviet debts. Lushan made this permanent: when Peng Dehuai sent Mao a private letter of warning on 14 July 1959 he was treated as an attacker, dismissed in September and replaced by Lin Biao, after which no official could report a shortfall and keep his post. Drought in 1960 and floods elsewhere deepened the disaster but cannot explain a death toll estimated at anywhere between about 15 million and over 40 million; policy, not weather, removed the margin that would have absorbed them.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Separate the causes that reduced the harvest from the causes that took away what was harvested; they are different kinds of reason.","Aim for three explained causes and decide in advance where the weather belongs in your ranking.","Begin with where the labour went in 1958 and 1959 and make the first paragraph end in a figure for output, not a description."],"codes":["32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: The causes and impact of the Great Famine","32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: The organisation of the commune","32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: The successes and failures of the Great Leap Forward"]},{"q":"Question 2(b). Explain why the Chinese economy recovered in the years 1962 to 1965. You may use the following in your answer: • the restoration of private plots • Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise support — communes cut down and the production team made the basic accounting unit in 1962, the Sixty Articles and the three freedoms, mess halls and backyard furnaces abandoned and grain imported, readjustment and consolidation in industry from 1961, and the authority Liu and Deng gained after the Seven Thousand Cadres Conference of January 1962. Stimulus cap: 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'.","sol":"The economy recovered because the units of farming were made small enough for effort and reward to meet again, because the methods of the Leap were abandoned, and because a shift of power at the top allowed both. The first reason is the reorganisation of the countryside. From 1961 communes were cut to roughly a third of their size, and under the Sixty Articles on the work of the rural people's communes, drafted in 1961 and confirmed in 1962, the production team of about twenty to thirty households became the basic accounting unit, so a family could once again see the connection between its work and its food. The three freedoms returned with it: private plots of a few per cent of the land, household sidelines and rural markets where a surplus could be sold. Deng Xiaoping's remark in 1962 that the colour of the cat did not matter so long as it caught mice was the argument in a sentence. The second reason is the abandonment of the Leap's methods. Mess halls were closed, backyard furnaces were left to rust, grain exports were cut and grain was imported from Canada and Australia, and in industry the policy of readjustment and consolidation begun in 1961 closed loss-making plants, cut over-ambitious investment and restored managers, technical experts, wage differences and piece rates. The third reason, beyond both bullet points, is political. At the Seven Thousand Cadres Conference in Beijing from 11 January to 7 February 1962 Liu Shaoqi told the assembled officials that the disaster was three parts natural and seven parts man-made; Mao made a limited self-criticism and withdrew from day-to-day direction of the economy. Only that withdrawal made the other measures possible, which is also why recovery was fragile: Mao regarded these policies as a slide towards capitalism and answered them with the Socialist Education Movement of 1963 to 1965. The economy was rescued by reversing the Leap, and the reversal was permitted only for as long as its author was out of the way.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Recovery needs both a change of policy and someone with the authority to make it; make sure your answer explains the second as well as the first.","Three reasons: what changed in the villages, what changed in industry, and what changed in the leadership.","Start with the size of the unit that farmed and was paid, because that is the change everything else rests on."],"codes":["32-2 — 4 Economic reform: Reasons for the restoration of private farming","32-2 — 4 Economic reform: Reasons for, and features of, economic reform (1962–65)"]},{"q":"Question 3(d). Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the reasons for the famine of 1959 to 1961? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG. Level 4 (13-16): alternative views reviewed in reaching a substantiated judgement, with precise analysis of how each interpretation conveys its view and contextual knowledge used on the interpretations — the steel target, Lysenko, Lushan and the dismissal of Peng Dehuai for Interpretation 2, the commune, procurement quotas, cadres' incentives and the weather of 1960 against it.","sol":"Interpretation 2 argues that the famine is explained by one man's decisions: Mao raised the 1958 steel target to 10.7 million tonnes himself, pressed Lysenko's methods on communes 'whose farmers knew better', and at Lushan turned a warning into a purge, so that 'this famine had an author'. Interpretation 1 answers that it 'was made by a system, not by a speech', and that 'millions of officials, not one man' produced the catastrophe. The criterion that decides between them is whether the same institutions, run by the same officials, would have starved China without Mao's particular decisions.\n\nInterpretation 2 is strong on the decisions that turned a difficult policy into a lethal one. The doubling of the steel target, demanded by Mao on 19 June 1958 and fixed at the Beidaihe conference that August, sent millions of workers to about 600,000 backyard furnaces at harvest time; Lysenko's close planting and deep ploughing, written into the Eight Point Charter of 1958, wasted seed and subsoil. Its strongest ground is Lushan. When Peng Dehuai, Minister of National Defence, sent Mao a private letter on 14 July 1959 setting out the waste and the false figures, Mao treated it as an attack and dismissed him in September, replacing him with Lin Biao. Grain output was already falling, from about 200 million tonnes in 1958 to roughly 140 million by 1960, and the one moment at which the Leap might have been corrected became the moment at which it was driven harder.\n\nInterpretation 1, though, explains why a poor harvest killed rather than merely hurt. The commune, general from August 1958, took from the peasant 'his own plot, his own grain store and his own cooking pot'; families ate free in mess halls that consumed reserves early, and no household had anything left under it when the ration was cut. Its second point is about incentives rather than orders: cadres invented figures 'to protect their own positions', and procurement quotas were then set on those figures, so villages surrendered grain they had never grown. The timing matters here. The commune secretary of Source B doubles his harvest and asks for his quota to be raised to match in October 1958, nine months before Lushan, which shows officials had reasons of their own to lie before Mao gave them a new one.\n\nThe two texts convey their views through the scale of the agent they choose. Interpretation 2 uses the language of authorship, a single figure who raises, presses, dismisses and orders; Interpretation 1 uses the language of structure, of what the commune 'took' and of what there was 'underneath to catch the fall'. Neither denies the other's facts; each subordinates them.\n\nOn the criterion set out at the start, Interpretation 2 is convincing about why the famine lasted and why it could not be reported, and unconvincing as a complete account of why it began. A system that had already abolished the private plot, the household store and the family pot was going to turn any shortfall into starvation, whoever led it; but only Mao's authority explains why the shortfall was repeated for two more years while officials competed to report success. Responsibility is best divided by function: the commune supplied the vulnerability, and Mao supplied the refusal to look at it.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide the test you will judge by before you write: what would have had to be true for Interpretation 2 to be a complete explanation?","Build the case for Interpretation 2 out of dated decisions, then the case against it out of what the commune had already removed from every household.","Open by saying in your own words what each interpretation claims and what would settle between them, so the reader knows where you are going."],"codes":["32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: The causes and impact of the Great Famine","32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: The successes and failures of the Great Leap Forward","32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: The organisation of the commune"]},{"q":"Question 3(d). Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the responsibility of local officials for the food shortages of 1959 to 1961? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG. Level 4 (13-16): Interpretation 2's claim that the lying 'was itself the Chairman's work' reviewed against Interpretation 1's cadres protecting 'their own positions', with precise context — Lushan and the anti-rightist drive that followed, inflated reports before Lushan, procurement enforced village by village, and the wide variation in provincial death rates.","sol":"Interpretation 2 does not excuse local officials so much as look past them: it argues that 'after Lushan no official in China could report a poor harvest and expect to keep his post, so the lying that fed the quotas was itself the Chairman's work'. Interpretation 1 puts the weight the other way, on cadres who 'invented' figures 'to protect their own positions' and on the 'millions of officials, not one man' who turned a poor harvest into a catastrophe. The question to be settled is whether a local official in 1959 or 1960 had any real choice.\n\nInterpretation 2 has the stronger case about the years after the summer of 1959. Peng Dehuai's private letter of 14 July 1959 was the most senior possible warning, and it ended with his dismissal in September and replacement by Lin Biao. The campaign against right deviation that followed made pessimism about the harvest a political offence rather than a mistake, and it reached down through every province and county. An official who reported truthfully in 1960 was reporting against the line that had just destroyed a Marshal of the People's Republic. Source C describes the result from below: a commune office that 'will not send word upward' while a hospital is told to enter the cause of death as illness and not as hunger. On that evidence, blaming the cadres for the silence of 1960 is blaming people for not doing what had just been shown to be fatal.\n\nInterpretation 1 is nevertheless right that the exaggeration did not begin with Mao's anger. Source B is dated October 1958, nine months before Lushan, and in it a commune secretary claims a harvest 'at least double last year's' and asks that his quota be set accordingly. Nobody had yet been purged for honesty; he is competing. Local officials also made choices that decided who ate: how much the mess halls served and for how long, whether grain was held back, how hard the procurement quota was pressed on a village that could not meet it. That is why death rates varied so sharply between provinces, with Anhui, Henan and Sichuan far worse than others under the same national policy. A system of orders alone would not produce that unevenness.\n\nThe difference of view is carried by the way each text handles agency. Interpretation 2 turns officials into instruments, its verbs belonging to Mao — raised, pressed, dismissed, ordered — while Interpretation 1 gives them motives of their own, 'to protect their own positions', and then multiplies them into millions.\n\nJudged on whether an official could have acted otherwise and survived, Interpretation 2 is largely convincing for 1960 and 1961 and much less so for 1958 and 1959. Before Lushan, ambition and competition produced the inflated figures on which the quotas were built, and that is a local responsibility; after Lushan, the price of honesty had been fixed at the top, and responsibility rises with it. The most defensible judgement is therefore a divided one: local officials built the false harvest, and Mao made it impossible to take down.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Responsibility questions turn on choice; ask what a county official could actually have done in 1958 and in 1960, and whether the answer is the same.","Use the dates of the room's sources as evidence in the argument, not as decoration: one of them sits before Lushan and one after.","State your criterion in the opening paragraph and return to it in every interim judgement."],"codes":["32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: The causes and impact of the Great Famine","32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: The successes and failures of the Great Leap Forward"]},{"q":"Question 3(d). Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part played by the commune system in causing the famine of 1959 to 1961? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG. Level 4 (13-16): Interpretation 2's subordination of the commune to Mao's decisions reviewed against Interpretation 1's structural case, with precise context — Beidaihe August 1958 and about 26,000 communes, mess halls and the abolition of private plots, procurement on reported figures, and the recovery of 1961 to 1962 achieved by changing commune structure.","sol":"Interpretation 2 treats the commune as an instrument rather than a cause. Its subject is always Mao: he raised the steel target, he 'pressed Lysenko's close planting and deep ploughing on communes whose farmers knew better', he dismissed Peng Dehuai; the commune appears only as the thing acted upon, and 'this famine had an author'. Interpretation 1 makes the commune the cause itself, because it 'took from the peasant the three things that had always carried him through a bad year'. The test is whether the same commune, without those particular orders, would still have starved the countryside.\n\nThere is a good deal in Interpretation 2's favour. The commune did not invent itself: the Beidaihe meeting of August 1958 resolved to create it, and by the end of the year about 26,000 communes covered almost the whole countryside at a speed that only central authority could impose. The methods that destroyed the harvest were pressed from above as well: close planting and deep ploughing came from the Eight Point Charter of 1958 rather than from any village, and the roughly 600,000 backyard furnaces were built to meet a steel figure fixed in Beijing. On this reading the commune was the vehicle, and the driver is the proper subject of the question.\n\nInterpretation 1 is stronger on why the damage was so total. The commune removed the household as a unit of production and of reward: private plots and private trade were abolished, families surrendered their cooking pots, and everyone ate free in mess halls that consumed the crop early in the year, leaving nothing in store when rations were cut. It also supplied the accounting through which the state took its share, since procurement quotas were fixed on reported figures and a commune that had boasted, as the secretary of Source B does in October 1958, then had to hand over grain it had never grown. The clearest evidence for Interpretation 1 is the recovery. What ended the famine was not a change of leader but a change of structure: communes cut to roughly a third of their size from 1961, the production team of twenty to thirty households made the basic accounting unit in 1962 under the Sixty Articles, mess halls closed and private plots, sidelines and rural markets restored. Mao was still Chairman throughout.\n\nThe two texts convey their difference through what they make the sentence's subject. Interpretation 2's verbs belong to a person; Interpretation 1's belong to an institution, which 'took' and left 'nothing underneath to catch the fall'. That grammatical choice is the argument.\n\nOn the test set at the start, Interpretation 2 understates the commune. Mao's decisions explain the size of the shortfall and, after Lushan, its repetition; but a countryside in which every family still held a plot and a store would have suffered a hungry year rather than a famine, and the fact that recovery came from dismantling the commune while its author remained in power is the decisive point. The commune was not merely the setting of the disaster: it was the reason a policy failure became mass starvation.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what would have happened if the same orders had been given to villages that still had private plots and household grain stores.","The recovery of 1961 to 1965 is evidence about causes; work out what it shows before you plan the paragraphs.","Say in the opening what each interpretation makes the subject of its explanation, and judge that choice rather than the facts."],"codes":["32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: The organisation of the commune","32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: The causes and impact of the Great Famine","32-2 — 4 Economic reform: Reasons for, and features of, economic reform (1962–65)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date did the Agrarian Reform Law come into force?","a":"30 June 1950."},{"q":"How many large Soviet-designed industrial projects were at the centre of China's first Five-Year Plan?","a":"156 (133 of them completed by the end of 1960)."},{"q":"Who sent Mao a letter criticising the Great Leap Forward at Lushan in July 1959, and who replaced him as Minister of National Defence?","a":"Peng Dehuai; he was replaced by Lin Biao in September 1959."},{"q":"In 1962, which unit became the basic accounting unit of the people's commune, and roughly how many households did it contain?","a":"The production team, of about twenty to thirty households."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The Agrarian Reform Law, 1950","open":false,"code":"32-2 — 1 Early changes in agriculture, 1949–57: The attacks on landlordism","back":"In force from 30 June 1950. Landlords' land, tools, animals and stored grain were confiscated and redistributed. By 1952 about 700 million mu, roughly 47 million hectares, had passed to some 300 million peasants."},{"front":"The Sixty Articles and the three freedoms","open":false,"code":"32-2 — 4 Economic reform: Reasons for, and features of, economic reform (1962–65)","back":"Drafted 1961, confirmed 1962. They cut the commune down, made the production team of twenty to thirty households the accounting unit, and restored private plots, sidelines and rural markets."},{"front":"Mutual aid teams","open":false,"code":"32-2 — 1 Early changes in agriculture, 1949–57: Moves towards agricultural cooperation","back":"From 1951, groups of up to about ten households sharing labour, tools and draught animals while each family kept its own land. Voluntary, and by the end of 1952 joined by only around 40 per cent of peasants."},{"front":"Lower-stage agricultural producers' cooperatives","open":false,"code":"32-2 — 1 Early changes in agriculture, 1949–57: Moves towards agricultural cooperation","back":"From 1953, after the Central Committee's resolution of December that year. Around thirty to fifty households pooled their land but members were still paid partly for the land they had contributed."},{"front":"Higher-stage cooperatives, or collectives","open":false,"code":"32-2 — 1 Early changes in agriculture, 1949–57: Reasons for the change from voluntary to enforced collectivisation","back":"Two to three hundred households, land owned in common, payment for labour alone. After the socialist high tide of 1955 to 1956, some 96 per cent of peasant households were in cooperatives, roughly 88 per cent of those in collectives."},{"front":"The first Five-Year Plan, 1953 to 1957","open":false,"code":"32-2 — 2 Industry and the Five-Year Plan, 1953–57: Reasons for, and targets of, the first Five-Year Plan","back":"Copied the Soviet model: central targets, nationalised industry, and nearly half of state investment poured into heavy industry — coal, steel, electricity, machine tools, chemicals and armaments — rather than consumer goods."},{"front":"Soviet support for the first Five-Year Plan","open":false,"code":"32-2 — 2 Industry and the Five-Year Plan, 1953–57: Reasons for, and targets of, the first Five-Year Plan","back":"Under the 1950 Treaty of Friendship: loans, machinery, blueprints, thousands of advisers, training for Chinese engineers, and 156 large industrial projects designed and equipped by the USSR, of which 133 were finished by the end of 1960."},{"front":"What the first Five-Year Plan achieved","open":false,"code":"32-2 — 2 Industry and the Five-Year Plan, 1953–57: Success and failures of the first Five-Year Plan","back":"Steel rose from about 1.3 million tonnes in 1952 to about 5.35 million in 1957 and coal from about 66 to about 131 million tonnes, but grain grew by under 4 per cent a year, barely ahead of the population."},{"front":"Mao's reasons for the Great Leap Forward","open":false,"code":"32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: Mao's reasons for the second Five-Year Plan","back":"Agriculture had lagged behind industry; he distrusted the Soviet model and Khrushchev after 1956; in 1957 he pledged to overtake Britain in steel within fifteen years; and the Anti-Rightist campaign had silenced his critics."},{"front":"Backyard furnaces and the 1958 steel target","open":false,"code":"32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: The successes and failures of the Great Leap Forward","back":"Mao demanded a doubling on 19 June 1958; Beidaihe fixed the target at 10.7 million tonnes that August, twice 1957's 5.35 million. About 600,000 backyard furnaces melted tools and woks; perhaps a third of the steel was unusable."},{"front":"The Lushan Conference, July to August 1959","open":false,"code":"32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: The successes and failures of the Great Leap Forward","back":"Peng Dehuai, Minister of National Defence, sent Mao a private letter of opinion on 14 July 1959 criticising the Leap's waste and false figures. Mao treated it as an attack, dismissed him in September and replaced him with Lin Biao."},{"front":"The people's commune","open":false,"code":"32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: The organisation of the commune","back":"Resolved on at Beidaihe in August 1958; about 26,000 by the end of the year, averaging some 5,000 households. Private plots and trade abolished, free communal mess halls, nurseries, and brigades marched to fields, furnaces and reservoirs."},{"front":"Lysenkoism in the communes","open":false,"code":"32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: The organisation of the commune","back":"The Soviet agronomist Trofim Lysenko's ideas shaped Mao's Eight Point Charter for agriculture of 1958: close planting, which starved seedlings of light and water, and deep ploughing, which turned up infertile subsoil."},{"front":"The Great Famine, 1959 to 1961","open":false,"code":"32-2 — 3 The Great Leap Forward: The causes and impact of the Great Famine","back":"Grain fell from about 200 million tonnes in 1958 to roughly 140 million in 1960. Causes: labour off the fields, Lysenko's methods, mess halls, quotas on invented figures, silence after Lushan. Deaths: 15 to over 40 million."},{"front":"The Seven Thousand Cadres Conference, January 1962","open":false,"code":"32-2 — 4 Economic reform: Reasons for the restoration of private farming","back":"Held in Beijing from 11 January to 7 February 1962. Liu Shaoqi told officials the disaster was three parts natural and seven parts man-made; Mao made a limited self-criticism and withdrew from day-to-day direction of the economy."}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The sources and interpretations written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Source A: From a report written for the county Party committee in Hebei province by a member of a land reform work team, spring 1951. Written for this room in the style of such a report; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — The team came in the ninth month and spent three weeks going from house to house before any meeting was called, so that the two hundred and ten households could be sorted into landlord, rich, middle and poor. Only when the lists were read out in the square did the village believe us. At the first accusation meeting a widow told how she had paid more than half of every harvest in rent and had still inherited her husband's debt, and after she had spoken the shouting could not be quietened; we let the accusations run into a second night. The landlord's family held about one fifth of the fields and both of the large ponds. His land, his tools, his two oxen and the grain in his store have been divided among ninety-six poor households. The middle peasants were slow to come forward until the lists were published, fearing that if the wind changed the rent would be asked for again.","Source B: From a report sent to the provincial Party committee by the secretary of a people's commune in Henan province, October 1958. Written for this room in the style of such a report; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — Our commune was formed in the ninth month out of twenty-seven cooperatives and now numbers eleven thousand households. Every member eats in one of the ninety-four mess halls, and eats free; no family here keeps a private cooking pot, and the pots that were handed in have gone into the furnaces. We are running one hundred and eighty furnaces day and night, and four in every ten of our able-bodied members have been taken off the fields for the furnaces and for the new reservoir. The nurseries have released the women. I report to the committee that our autumn harvest is at least double last year's, and I ask that our quota be set accordingly, for we shall have grain to spare.","Source C: From a letter written in March 1960 by a doctor at a county hospital in Anhui province to her brother in Shanghai. Written for this room in the style of such a letter; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — Do not write back about this. Since the New Year the mess hall ration here has been cut twice, and in the villages nearest us it has stopped altogether; the trucks came for the state quota in the autumn and what was left did not last the winter. My ward is full of the swelling sickness, legs and faces puffed up so that you press the skin and the mark stays. Most of them have been living on husks, on the sweepings of the threshing floor and on elm bark. We are told to write the cause of death as illness and not as hunger, and the commune office will not send word upward, because the harvest it reported two years ago was far more than these fields have ever given. Send what you can, but send it to your friend at the post office and not to me.","Interpretation 1: From a study of the Chinese countryside under Mao, by a historian writing in the 1980s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — The famine was made by a system, not by a speech. The commune took from the peasant the three things that had always carried him through a bad year: his own plot, his own grain store and his own cooking pot. What he ate was decided in the mess hall, and what his village kept was decided by a procurement quota fixed on figures that local cadres had invented to protect their own positions. When drought struck the north in 1960 and the rivers flooded in the south there was nothing underneath to catch the fall. Millions of officials, not one man, turned a poor harvest into a catastrophe.","Interpretation 2: From a general history of Mao's China, by a historian writing in the 2000s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — Systems do not double a steel target overnight, and they do not go on repeating the same mistake for two more years while people die. Mao raised the 1958 steel figure to 10.7 million tonnes himself; he pressed Lysenko's close planting and deep ploughing on communes whose farmers knew better; and at Lushan in the summer of 1959 he treated Peng Dehuai's private letter of warning as a personal attack, dismissed him, and ordered the Leap to be driven harder. After Lushan no official in China could report a poor harvest and expect to keep his post, so the lying that fed the quotas was itself the Chairman's work. This famine had an author.","How the room uses them: Source A is the inference source for Question 1. Sources B and C are the contemporary pair for the 8-mark usefulness question, 3(a). Interpretations 1 and 2 are the pair for 3(b), 3(c) and 3(d). Every quotation from a source or an interpretation in this room's model answers and worked examples comes from the five texts above and from nowhere else; the only other quoted wording is Edexcel's own level descriptors."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 3 Question 3(d), 16 marks)","items":["Question: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part played by Mao's farming methods in causing the famine of 1959 to 1961? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]' This is one paragraph from the middle of the answer, putting the case against Interpretation 2.","Interpretation 2 treats the methods as a cause in their own right, imposed by one man on 'communes whose farmers knew better', and there is solid ground under the charge: close planting and deep ploughing reached the villages through Mao's Eight Point Charter for agriculture of 1958, close planting leaving seedlings competing for light and water and deep ploughing turning up infertile subsoil, while the Four Pests campaign of the same year destroyed the sparrows and let the crop insects multiply. What the claim cannot carry is the scale of the deaths, and the chronology shows why. Those methods were already in force for the harvest of 1958, and 1958 still brought in about 200 million tonnes of grain, more than any year before it; the mass deaths came afterwards, as output fell to roughly 140 million tonnes by 1960. Between the two years the planting did not change. What changed was what happened to the crop once it was in, which is where Interpretation 1 is stronger: the commune had taken 'his own plot, his own grain store and his own cooking pot', so a household that had handed over its quota had nothing of its own left underneath. Source C shows that at ground level, a doctor in an Anhui county in March 1960 writing that 'the trucks came for the state quota in the autumn and what was left did not last the winter' while her patients lived on husks and elm bark. Bad methods lowered a harvest; the taking of what the harvest left is what starved people.","Why this is Level 4: it 'provides an explained evaluation reviewing the alternative views in coming to a substantiated judgement' — Interpretation 2's ground is conceded in precise detail, then limited by an argument rather than by a contrary assertion. The analysis is precise about how the difference of view is conveyed, setting Interpretation 2's agent, a man who presses methods on farmers who know better, against Interpretation 1's institution, which simply 'took'. Contextual knowledge is precisely selected and used on the interpretations rather than narrated: the Eight Point Charter and the Four Pests campaign of 1958, the fall from about 200 million tonnes to roughly 140 million, and the date of Source C, which is the evidential pivot of the paragraph. The closing sentence judges the interpretation's scope, distinguishing what the methods did from what killed."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same as above — 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part played by Mao's farming methods in causing the famine of 1959 to 1961?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks].","Interpretation 2 says that Mao made the communes use close planting and deep ploughing even though the farmers knew better, and I mostly agree with this because the methods were very bad. The crops did not grow properly and a lot of seed was wasted, so there was much less food and people started to go hungry. Interpretation 1 disagrees because it says the famine was made by a system and that the commune took away the peasant's plot, his grain store and his cooking pot. The communes were badly run and the mess halls used up the food too quickly, so there is something in this too. Overall I think Mao's farming methods were quite important but the communes were also important, so both interpretations are partly right.","Why this is Level 2 and not Level 3: it does offer 'valid evaluative comment to agree with or counter the interpretation' and it does select details from both, which is what Level 2 asks for. What it misses is Level 3's 'relevant contextual knowledge used directly to support the evaluation'. There is not one date, figure or name of the candidate's own: 'very bad', 'a lot' and 'badly run' stand where the Eight Point Charter of 1958, the Four Pests campaign, the fall from about 200 million tonnes of grain to roughly 140 million and the March 1960 letter of Source C should be. It also never shows how the interpretations convey their views, and the judgement is announced rather than reasoned towards, so 'its justification is insecure or undeveloped and a line of reasoning is not sustained'. To reach Level 3, replace every general adjective with a named fact that does evaluative work."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the attacks on landlordism and the redistribution of land, 1949 to 1952","items":["Agrarian Reform Law of the People's Republic of China: in force from 30 June 1950.","Before 1950 landlords and rich peasants, a small minority of households, held a very large share of the cultivated land and rented much of it out, often for more than half the crop.","Party work teams were sent into each village to classify every household as landlord, rich peasant, middle peasant, poor peasant or labourer.","Speak-bitterness meetings: public accusation meetings at which tenants denounced landlords; many became struggle sessions ending in beatings, confiscation and execution.","Land, tools, draught animals and stored grain were all confiscated, not land alone.","About 700 million mu — roughly 47 million hectares — was redistributed to around 300 million peasants.","Land reform was largely complete across China by 1952.","Estimates of landlords killed range from several hundred thousand to one or two million: give the range, not a single figure.","Purposes: to win peasant loyalty for the new regime, to destroy the class that governed the villages, and to put the state in direct contact with the households whose grain would pay for industry.","Consequence: millions of tiny family holdings, too small for machinery or irrigation and hard for the state to buy grain from — the problem that cooperation was meant to solve."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: cooperation and collectivisation, 1951 to 1956","items":["Mutual aid teams, from 1951: up to about ten households sharing labour, tools and animals, each family keeping its own land. Around 40 per cent of peasants had joined by the end of 1952.","Lower-stage agricultural producers' cooperatives, from 1953, after the Central Committee's resolution of December 1953: roughly thirty to fifty households, land pooled, members still paid partly for the land they brought in.","Higher-stage cooperatives, or collectives: two to three hundred households, land owned in common, payment for labour alone.","Mao intervened personally in July 1955 against colleagues he judged too cautious, and the socialist high tide of the winter of 1955 to 1956 followed.","By the end of 1956 about 96 per cent of peasant households were in cooperatives, roughly 88 per cent of those in higher-stage collectives.","Reason 1: the first Five-Year Plan needed grain bought cheaply and reliably from the countryside, which millions of separate farms could not supply.","Reason 2: voluntary cooperation was slow, and some badly formed cooperatives were being dissolved.","Reason 3: Mao feared that a new rich-peasant class was forming as successful peasants bought out their neighbours after land reform.","Effect on the peasant: a former holding now earned nothing, since income depended on work points for labour done."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the first Five-Year Plan, 1953 to 1957, and USSR support","items":["Ran 1953 to 1957, on the Soviet model: central targets, nationalised industry, prices and wages set by the state.","Nearly half of all state investment went into heavy industry: coal, steel, electricity, machine tools, chemicals and armaments.","Soviet aid flowed under the 1950 Treaty of Friendship, Alliance and Mutual Assistance: loans, machinery, blueprints, advisers in China and training for Chinese engineers in the USSR.","156 large industrial projects were designed and equipped by the Soviet Union; 133 of them had been completed by the end of 1960.","New or expanded industrial centres included the steelworks at Anshan and at Wuhan.","Steel output rose from about 1.3 million tonnes in 1952 to about 5.35 million tonnes in 1957.","Coal output rose from about 66 million tonnes in 1952 to about 131 million tonnes in 1957.","The gross value of industrial output more than doubled over the plan.","Grain rose from about 164 million tonnes in 1952 to under 200 million in 1957 — under 4 per cent a year, barely ahead of population growth.","Costs: dependence on the USSR, a privileged managerial and technical elite, and consumer goods and rural living standards left far behind.","The Soviet government gave notice on 16 July 1960 and its experts left China between late July and the beginning of September 1960, taking blueprints and leaving contracts cancelled."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Great Leap Forward, the commune and Lushan, 1958 to 1959","items":["The Great Leap Forward was the second Five-Year Plan, announced in 1958 and intended to raise industry and agriculture together — 'walking on two legs'.","In 1957 Mao declared that China would overtake Britain in steel output within fifteen years.","Mao pressed for a doubling of steel output on 19 June 1958; the 1958 target of 10.7 million tonnes, roughly twice the 1957 figure of about 5.35 million tonnes, was fixed at the Beidaihe conference of 17 to 30 August 1958.","About 600,000 backyard furnaces melted down tools, woks and door hinges; perhaps a third of the steel claimed for 1958 was too impure to use.","The Beidaihe meeting of August 1958 resolved to set up people's communes; by the end of the year about 26,000 of them covered almost the whole countryside, averaging some 5,000 households each.","Inside the commune: private plots and private trade abolished, cooking pots surrendered, free communal mess halls, nurseries and canteens releasing women for work, brigades marched to fields, furnaces and reservoirs.","Lysenkoism: the Soviet agronomist Trofim Lysenko's ideas shaped Mao's Eight Point Charter for agriculture of 1958 — close planting, which starved seedlings of light and water, and deep ploughing, which turned up infertile subsoil.","The Four Pests campaign of 1958 targeted rats, flies, mosquitoes and sparrows; killing the sparrows allowed crop insects to multiply.","Genuine achievements: large irrigation, canal and reservoir schemes, rural schools and clinics, and a great extension of rural literacy.","The Lushan Conference, July and August 1959: Peng Dehuai, Minister of National Defence, sent Mao a private letter of opinion on 14 July criticising waste and false reporting.","Mao treated the letter as an attack, dismissed Peng in September 1959 and replaced him with Lin Biao; a campaign against right deviation followed, and the Leap was intensified rather than corrected."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Great Famine, 1959 to 1961","items":["Grain output fell from about 200 million tonnes in 1958 to roughly 140 million tonnes in 1960.","Cause: labour diverted from the fields to backyard furnaces, reservoirs and canals, often at sowing and harvest time.","Cause: Lysenko's close planting and deep ploughing wasted seed and damaged the soil.","Cause: the commune removed every private reserve — no plot, no household grain store, no family cooking pot — and free mess halls consumed the crop early in the year.","Cause: cadres reported harvests two or three times the real figure, and state procurement quotas were set on those reports, so villages handed over grain they had never grown.","Cause: grain continued to be exported and used to repay Soviet loans while people starved.","Cause: after Lushan in 1959, no official could report a shortfall and expect to keep his post.","Aggravating factor, not a sufficient cause: drought in the north in 1960 and floods elsewhere.","Death rates varied sharply by province, being far worse in provinces such as Anhui, Henan and Sichuan than in others under the same national policy.","Estimates of the death toll range from about 15 million to over 40 million: quote the range and say why historians differ.","Impact: mass deaths, oedema and disease, abandoned and ruined land, a sharp fall in births, and a loss of confidence in Mao's economic judgement inside the Party."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: economic reform under Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping, 1961 to 1965","items":["From 1961 communes were cut to roughly a third of their original size and mess halls were closed.","Backyard furnaces were abandoned, grain exports were cut and grain was imported from Canada and Australia.","The Sixty Articles on the work of the rural people's communes were drafted in 1961 and confirmed in 1962.","In 1962 the production team of about twenty to thirty households became the basic accounting unit, linking work to reward again.","The 'three freedoms' were restored: private plots of a few per cent of the land, household sidelines, and rural markets at which a surplus could be sold.","In industry the policy of readjustment and consolidation, begun in 1961, closed loss-making plants, cut over-ambitious investment and restored managers, technical experts, wage differences and piece rates.","The Seven Thousand Cadres Conference, Beijing, 11 January to 7 February 1962: Liu Shaoqi told officials the disaster was three parts natural and seven parts man-made; Mao made a limited self-criticism and withdrew from day-to-day direction of the economy.","Deng Xiaoping argued in 1962 that the colour of the cat did not matter so long as it caught mice.","Results: grain output and industrial production recovered by the mid-1960s and famine ended.","Political cost: Mao regarded these measures as a slide back towards capitalism and launched the Socialist Education Movement of 1963 to 1965, the beginning of his quarrel with Liu and Deng."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the source and interpretation moves Paper 3 rewards","items":["Inference (Question 1): state something the source does not say but that must be true if it is true, then give the words that force it. One inference and one supporting detail in each box.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose: what kind of document it is, who made it and when, and why it was made.","Utility (3(a)): judge each source for the enquiry the question names, never in general. Content first, then how nature, origin and purpose change the value of that content, then the context that confirms or qualifies it.","A source can be untrue and highly useful. An inflated official report is first-hand evidence of inflated official reporting.","'Biased' is not a judgement on its own. Say whose view the source gives, why it gives that view, and what it is therefore useful for.","Contextual knowledge scores nothing unless it is used on the sources or the interpretations; a paragraph about the period that never touches them earns no credit.","Difference of view (3(b)): a disagreement about the past, not about wording or detail. State one difference, then support it with a phrase from each interpretation.","Reason for difference (3(c)): interpretations differ because they ask different questions, rest on different evidence, or weigh the same evidence differently. Name which, and show it, using Sources B and C where they illustrate the evidence.","Differences drawn from evidence are legitimate: an interpretation is not wrong merely because another disagrees with it.","Evaluating an interpretation (3(d)): use both interpretations and your own knowledge, show how each conveys its view, and decide how far the named one can be agreed with and on what criterion."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 3 Question 3(d): 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the importance of the Lushan Conference of 1959?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","items":["The question as printed: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the importance of the Lushan Conference of 1959? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]' This question prints no stimulus points: the two interpretations are the stimulus, both must be used, and an answer that evaluates one in isolation cannot reach the top levels.","Decode the claim. Interpretation 2 treats Lushan as the decisive moment: Mao 'treated Peng Dehuai's private letter of warning as a personal attack, dismissed him, and ordered the Leap to be driven harder', so that 'after Lushan no official in China could report a poor harvest and expect to keep his post' and the lying 'was itself the Chairman's work'. Interpretation 1 never mentions Lushan at all, and that silence is its argument: the famine 'was made by a system, not by a speech'. The criterion to announce in the opening is whether Lushan changed what was happening or only confirmed it.","Opening, three or four sentences: say what Interpretation 2 claims in your own words, name Interpretation 1 as the rival weighting, state the criterion, and signal where you will end, so the line of reasoning is visible from the first line.","Paragraph 1, for Interpretation 2 — what Lushan actually was. The Politburo met at Lushan in July and the Central Committee in August 1959; Peng Dehuai, Minister of National Defence and a hero of the Korean War, sent Mao a private letter of opinion on 14 July. Mao treated it as an attack, Peng was dismissed in September and replaced by Lin Biao. Interim judgement: the most senior possible warning had been punished in public.","Paragraph 2, for Interpretation 2 — what followed. A campaign against right deviation ran down to county level, so reporting a shortfall became a political offence; the Leap was intensified in 1960 rather than halted, and grain fell to roughly 140 million tonnes. Interim judgement: Lushan explains the two worst years of the famine, which is a large claim honourably made.","Paragraph 3, against Interpretation 2 — the lying began earlier. Source B's commune secretary claims a harvest 'at least double last year's' and asks that his quota be set accordingly in October 1958, nine months before Peng wrote, which fits Interpretation 1's cadres inventing figures 'to protect their own positions'. Interim judgement: Lushan hardened a habit it did not create.","Paragraph 4, against Interpretation 2 — the structures were already lethal. By the end of 1958 about 26,000 communes had abolished private plots, private trade and the household grain store, and free mess halls were consuming the crop early. Interim judgement: the vulnerability that turned shortfall into famine was in place before anyone travelled to Lushan.","Paragraph 5, the point that goes furthest beyond both texts — the test of the recovery. The famine ended when the structures were dismantled: communes cut to a third from 1961, the production team made the basic accounting unit in 1962 under the Sixty Articles, mess halls closed and private plots restored, with Mao still Chairman throughout. Interim judgement: if changing the system without changing the leader ended the famine, the system was doing much of the work.","The evaluation thread that must run through every paragraph: how each interpretation conveys its view. Interpretation 2 writes in verbs that belong to one man — raised, pressed, dismissed, ordered — and ends on authorship; Interpretation 1 writes in verbs that belong to an institution, which 'took' and left 'nothing underneath to catch the fall'. Saying this, and using it, is what the top level means by showing how the differences of view are conveyed.","What the conclusion must say: not 'both are partly right'. Apply the criterion announced at the start. Interpretation 2 is convincing about why the famine was not corrected and therefore about its duration and depth, and unconvincing about its origin, since the inflated reporting and the loss of every household reserve came first. Name the deciding consideration: Lushan removed the last chance to stop a disaster that was already running, which makes it important without making it the cause.","Timing and SPaG: about 20 minutes on the answer itself, which is the minute a mark the paper allows for 16 marks — four to fix the criterion and order the paragraphs and about sixteen to write — and then three or four minutes on top to check spelling and punctuation and to be sure the specialist terms are present and correctly used, since 4 additional marks depend on 'a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate'. Useful terms here: commune, collectivisation, procurement quota, cadre, mess hall, production team, Politburo, Central Committee, Lysenkoism, Great Leap Forward."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:46:14Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"All six recorded fixes verified in the kit and the new facts re-checked on the web: 'May 1958' is gone from all four fields and the steel target is now Mao's demand of 19 June 1958 fixed at the Beidaihe conference of 17 to 30 August 1958 (confirmed: the target stood at about 7 million tonnes after Chengdu in March and about 8.2 million in early June, and Beidaihe, 17 to 30 August, settled 10.7 million); the 1957 figure is about 5.35 million tonnes in all eight places, so 'roughly twice' is now exact; the Outline Land Law of October 1947 checks out (promulgated 10 October 1947 after the National Land Conference at Xibaipo); worked examples 4 and 5 are genuinely re-set on the part played by natural disaster, an argument the bank's 3(b) and 3(c) do not make, and their claim that Interpretation 2 mentions no weather is true of the printed text; the model paragraphs have moved to Mao's farming methods, a 3(d) focus in neither the bank nor the plan, and their new evidence (the Eight Point Charter and Four Pests of 1958, grain about 200 million tonnes in 1958 falling to roughly 140 million by 1960, Source C of March 1960) is sound, including the claim that 1958 was the highest grain year to that date; the plan now times 3(d) at about 20 minutes plus three or four for SPaG and room.facts agrees, totalling 71 of the 90 minutes. dupscan prints 'no duplication'. I re-solved all fifteen questions and re-checked every date, figure and attribution: no wrong item remains, the June 2025 form and the June 2024 cap wording are intact, all eleven key ideas are still tested, and only two cosmetic points are left — publish."}}},"32-3":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"32-3","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Everything Paper 3 asks for is here in concentrated form. Section A pairs a source with a 12-mark causation question, and in this room those questions turn on why Mao acted in 1966 and on how the country was pulled back from anarchy. Section B's interpretations are arguments about the same ten years: was this a power struggle dressed in ideology, or a genuine attempt to remake a people? If you can explain, with names and dates, why a man who already led China turned the young against his own Party, what the Red Guards actually did, what it cost in schooling, skills and output, and how the army, the countryside and finally death brought it to an end, you can answer almost anything this key topic sets. The room teaches that story as a chain of causes you can argue with, and it trains the five moves the paper rewards: inference from a source, causal explanation that goes beyond the printed bullet points, judgement of a source's usefulness for the enquiry actually named, spotting how two historians differ and why, and evaluating one of them against the other and against what you know.","idea":[{"code":"32-3 — 1 Reasons for the Cultural Revolution: The power struggle","h":"The quarrel at the top: Mao, Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping","t":"The failure of the Great Leap Forward cost Mao his command of the state. After the Leap had begun to go wrong he told the Party he would not stand again as head of state, and on 27 April 1959 he gave up the state chairmanship, staying Chairman of the Party, while Liu Shaoqi took the post. At the Seven Thousand Cadres Conference in January 1962 Liu told the Party that the disaster was mostly man-made rather than natural, and he and Deng Xiaoping, the General Secretary, then rebuilt the economy by allowing private plots and rural markets; Deng defended the method with a saying that a cat's colour mattered less than whether it caught mice. To Mao this was not recovery but retreat, and worse, it was being run by men who controlled the Party machine down to the county. By 1965 Mao presided over an organisation he no longer directed. A top-level answer uses the power struggle as a cause of the Cultural Revolution rather than a background fact: it explains why Mao had to attack the Party from outside, through the young and the army, instead of simply issuing orders through it."},{"code":"32-3 — 1 Reasons for the Cultural Revolution: Purification of communism","h":"Purifying communism: the Socialist Education Movement and the schools","t":"Mao's second reason was stated openly for years before 1966. He believed that a revolution could go soft: the Soviet Union under Khrushchev had, as he saw it, settled into comfort under a new class of officials, and China's own cadres were becoming a privileged caste while a generation born after 1949 had never fought for anything. The Socialist Education Movement of 1963 to 1966, with its Four Cleanups of politics, economics, organisation and ideas, was meant to scrub that out of the countryside; work teams, including one led by Liu Shaoqi's wife Wang Guangmei at Taoyuan from late 1963, went into the villages. Mao concluded that the movement had been used to discipline small cadres while the leadership went untouched. Education was the other front: examinations and academic selection were attacked as a ladder for the privileged, and from 1964 Quotations from Chairman Mao, compiled for the army by Lin Biao's political department, gave every soldier a portable creed. A top-level answer treats purification as a real motive, not a cover story, and then asks what it made possible."},{"code":"32-3 — 2 The Red Guards and the Red Terror: Mao's hold on young people","h":"Mao and the young: the rallies of 1966 and Jiang Qing","t":"The campaign opened in the press and the theatre. Yao Wenyuan's article of 10 November 1965, published in Shanghai because Jiang Qing could not place it in Beijing, attacked Wu Han's play about the Ming official Hai Rui, and readers understood the dismissed honest official to stand for Peng Dehuai, sacked in 1959 for criticising the Great Leap Forward. In May 1966 the Central Cultural Revolution Group was set up under Chen Boda with Jiang Qing as his deputy; the first Red Guards appeared in Beijing schools that month. Mao's own big-character poster of 5 August 1966 told the Party to bombard its own headquarters, and the Central Committee's Sixteen Points of 8 August ordered officials not to suppress the movement. Then came the rallies: from 18 August 1966 Mao reviewed the Red Guards in Tiananmen Square, the first time before about a million of them, and eight rallies ran into late November while the state carried students to Beijing free of charge. A top-level answer explains the mechanism, not the spectacle: the rallies gave the young an authority that came from Mao personally and therefore overrode every office in China."},{"code":"32-3 — 2 The Red Guards and the Red Terror: Red Guard attacks on the 'four olds'","h":"The Red Guards, the 'four olds' and the Red Terror","t":"The Sixteen Points told the masses to educate themselves, and the Red Guards read that as a licence. Their declared enemy was the 'four olds' — old ideas, old culture, old customs and old habits — which in practice meant temples, churches and mosques, opera costumes, private libraries, street and shop names, western clothes and hairstyles; in November 1966 Red Guards from Beijing wrecked the temple and cemetery of Confucius at Qufu. Houses were searched, property burned in the street and 'class enemies' paraded in dunce caps at struggle meetings. Teachers, headmasters, former landlords and their children were beaten, and some were killed or driven to suicide; estimates of the dead across the whole Cultural Revolution vary widely, from several hundred thousand upwards. By 1967 rival Red Guard factions, each claiming to be the true Maoists, were fighting one another with weapons seized from arsenals. A top-level answer keeps the two halves together: the destruction was aimed at a way of life, and the anarchy came from giving unlimited authority to people who answered to nobody but a distant Chairman."},{"code":"32-3 — 3 The effects of the Cultural Revolution: Political effects","h":"Political effects: the Party purged, Liu and Deng broken, Lin Biao raised and destroyed","t":"The Cultural Revolution took the Chinese Communist Party apart. Peng Zhen, the mayor of Beijing, and the Five Man Group he chaired fell in the spring of 1966; at the Eleventh Plenum in August 1966 Liu Shaoqi dropped from second in the Party's order to eighth and Lin Biao took his place. Liu was denounced as the leading person in authority taking the capitalist road, paraded at struggle meetings, expelled from the Party in October 1968, and died without proper medical care at Kaifeng on 12 November 1969. Deng Xiaoping was stripped of office and sent to work in a tractor factory in Jiangxi. Provincial committees were destroyed and replaced by revolutionary committees. Lin Biao, defence minister since 1959 and the manager of Mao's cult, was written into the Party constitution as Mao's successor at the Ninth Congress in April 1969 — and died on 13 September 1971 when his aircraft crashed in Mongolia as he fled, the government saying he had plotted against Mao. A top-level answer makes the pattern the point: Mao destroyed whoever accumulated power, including the men he raised to do it."},{"code":"32-3 — 3 The effects of the Cultural Revolution: Social effects","h":"Social effects: a decade with the schools shut","t":"Schools and universities closed in June 1966 and the national university entrance examination was abolished; universities took almost no students until about 1970, and when they reopened places went by recommendation on class background, political reliability and labour record rather than by examination. A generation left school unqualified, which is why the Chinese still call them the lost generation; the entrance examination was not restored until 1977. Families were broken by denunciation — children were expected to denounce parents and pupils their teachers — and millions were sent from the cities to the countryside. Not all of it was loss: basic health care spread through the countryside as part-trained 'barefoot doctors' were sent to the brigades, and primary schooling in villages widened even as secondary and higher education collapsed. A top-level answer weighs those against each other instead of listing them, and says which mattered more and for whom."},{"code":"32-3 — 3 The effects of the Cultural Revolution: Economic effects","h":"Economic effects: the cost of driving out the people who knew how","t":"Industry took the blow. Engineers, managers and technicians were denounced as bourgeois experts from 1966 and sent to sweep yards or to farms, so that factories kept their machines but lost the people who could set them up and repair them. Rival factions fought for control of workplaces: the January Storm in Shanghai in 1967 pulled down the city administration, and the railways carried Red Guards free of charge instead of coal and grain. Industrial output fell by roughly 13 per cent in 1967 and in 1968 was still below the 1966 figure. Farming suffered less, because the communes went on much as before and there was no repeat of the Great Leap's famine, though the arrival of city youngsters after 1968 put extra mouths into brigades that were already short. A top-level answer is precise about where the damage fell: not on the fields, but on skill, coordination, transport and technical training — the things a modern economy is built from."},{"code":"32-3 — 4 The end of the Cultural Revolution, 1968–76: Reasons for the end of the Red Guards","h":"Taking it back: the PLA, the revolutionary committees and the sending down of the young","t":"By 1967 the Red Guards were a danger to the state that had created them. In the Wuhan Incident of July 1967 the local army command backed the conservative 'Million Heroes' and two envoys sent from Beijing, Wang Li and Xie Fuzhi, were seized before troops and gunboats restored control. Mao's answer was the People's Liberation Army: propaganda teams went into factories, schools and universities, and revolutionary committees, built on a 'three-in-one' combination of soldiers, surviving cadres and rebel representatives, replaced the wrecked party committees, covering every province by September 1968. In December 1968 Mao directed that educated young people go to the countryside to be re-educated by the poor and lower-middle peasants; the 'Up to the mountains and down to the villages' campaign moved something like 17 million of them between 1968 and 1978. A top-level answer says what restoring order actually meant: not persuasion but substitution — the army for the Party, and the villages for the Red Guards."},{"code":"32-3 — 4 The end of the Cultural Revolution, 1968–76: The return to power of Deng Xiaoping","h":"The last years: Zhou Enlai, Deng's return, the Gang of Four and the death of Mao","t":"Lin Biao's death in 1971 discredited the radicals' own hero and opened the way for the survivors. Zhou Enlai, premier throughout, steered the opening to the United States that brought President Nixon to China in February 1972, and in January 1975 he set out the Four Modernisations — agriculture, industry, defence, and science and technology — at the Fourth National People's Congress. Zhou had Deng Xiaoping brought back as a vice-premier in 1973, and as Zhou sickened Deng ran the government. Against them stood the Gang of Four — Jiang Qing, Zhang Chunqiao, Yao Wenyuan and Wang Hongwen — who controlled the press and the cultural apparatus. Zhou died on 8 January 1976; when wreaths left for him in Tiananmen Square were cleared away, the crowds that gathered on 4 and 5 April 1976 were dispersed and Deng was dismissed again and blamed. Mao died on 9 September 1976, and on 6 October Hua Guofeng had the Gang of Four arrested. A top-level answer treats 1976 as a race between two successions, and explains why the one with the army and the police won."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1: two inferences from Source A about how the Red Guards moved about China and organised themselves","setup":"Source A in this room is a letter from a sixteen-year-old member of a Red Guard detachment in Beijing to her parents in Taiyuan, September 1966. She writes that she has not gone back to the classroom because there is none: 'our school has been shut since June and the teachers who are left spend their days writing confessions'. On the eighteenth of August she stood in the square from four in the morning, and 'when the Chairman came out on the rostrum in the early light and put on our armband, the girl beside me wept and could not stop'. 'We came here in August, free of charge on the train, eleven of us, with nothing but a letter from our detachment, and the station feeds us.' Yesterday the group 'searched three courtyards in the lane behind the market', burned a chest of opera costumes and a shelf of old books in the road, and took the signboard off a medicine shop 'that had the old name on it'. Their former headmaster 'was made to stand at the gate with a board round his neck, and nobody spoke for him, not even the men from the district committee, who wrote down what we told them'. She ends: 'The Chairman has told us that there are people in the Party itself taking the capitalist road, and it is our work to find them.' The question is: 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about the way the Red Guards travelled and organised themselves in 1966. Complete the table below to explain your answer.' (4 marks)","steps":["Fix the writer's position before reading for content: a teenager writing privately to her own parents is not trying to impress an official, so the practical arrangements she mentions in passing are reported because they were ordinary, which makes them good evidence.","Take the first inference from how the journey was paid for: they came 'free of charge on the train, eleven of us' and 'the station feeds us', so the Red Guards were being moved and fed at the state's expense rather than travelling on their own resources.","Take the second from the word 'detachment': 'nothing but a letter from our detachment' was enough to carry eleven people across the country, so the Red Guards were organised into named units whose own paperwork was accepted as authority.","Check each pair before writing: the claim, which the source implies but never states, goes in the box for the inference, and the exact words that force it go in the box for details, and no detail is used twice."],"cues":["Who was meant to read this letter, and why does that matter?","Which words tell you who paid for the journey and the food?","What does the word 'detachment' imply about how they were organised?","Which box takes the claim and which takes the quotation?"]},{"title":"Question 2(a): planning a 12-mark 'Explain why' on the fall of Liu Shaoqi","setup":"The question is: '2(a) Explain why Liu Shaoqi lost his power between 1966 and 1969. You may use the following in your answer: • the work teams of 1966 • Mao's poster ‘Bombard the Headquarters’. You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks) The mark scheme targets AO2 6 and AO1 6, and prints the caps 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content', so at least three reasons are needed and at least one must go beyond the two printed points.","steps":["Name the conceptual focus and hold every paragraph to it: this is causation about one man's fall, so each paragraph must end at Liu losing power, not at the Cultural Revolution in general.","Build the first reason from the work teams: in June 1966, with Mao away from Beijing, Liu and Deng Xiaoping sent work teams into the universities to take charge of the student movement, exactly as work teams had been used in the Socialist Education Movement; Mao returned and condemned them for suppressing the masses, so Liu's normal method of party control became the charge against him.","Build the second from the poster: Mao's big-character poster of 5 August 1966 told the Party there was a bourgeois headquarters inside it, and at the Eleventh Plenum that month Liu dropped from second in the Party's order to eighth while Lin Biao took his place.","Build the third beyond the printed points, from Liu's own record: his recovery policies after the Great Leap, private plots and rural markets, and his statement at the Seven Thousand Cadres Conference of January 1962 that the famine was mostly man-made were now called the capitalist road, and his wife Wang Guangmei's work at Taoyuan was turned into a public charge against the family at struggle meetings; he was expelled from the Party in October 1968 and died at Kaifeng on 12 November 1969.","Close by linking the reasons rather than repeating them: the work teams gave Mao the pretext, the poster made the accusation official, and Liu's past supplied the evidence, so a policy disagreement was converted into treason."],"cues":["What exactly must every paragraph explain?","Why would sending work teams into universities be dangerous for Liu?","What did the poster change about who could be attacked?","Which reason of your own is not prompted by the two bullet points?","How do the three reasons fit into one argument?"]},{"title":"Question 3(a): judging Sources B and C for the enquiry actually named","setup":"Source B in this room is a report from the deputy director of a machine-tool works in Shenyang to the city revolutionary committee, October 1968: 'Production in this works has not recovered'; 'two rival organisations of young workers have held the foundry and the assembly shop against each other since the spring of last year, and for several weeks in 1967 nothing left the yard at all'; the chief engineer and four technical staff were 'denounced as bourgeois experts in 1966', two sweeping the yard and the rest sent to a farm in the north; 'since the army propaganda team came in June, the two organisations have been brought into one revolutionary committee and the gates are guarded', but the imported lathes cannot be repaired 'because the men who understood them are not here' and output will again be below the figure for 1966; 'the young workers say that to ask for output is to put production in command'. Source C is a letter from a seventeen-year-old sent from Shanghai to a production brigade in the hills of Yunnan, written to her younger brother in spring 1970: they 'rise before it is light and carry earth for the terraces'; the brigade 'has no electricity'; the peasants say the students 'eat their grain and cannot carry a full load'; 'I was in the Red Guards in 1967 and I believed every word of it'; there is 'a school of two rooms for the small children and no teacher who has been to a middle school'; 'send me any book you can, even an old one'; and 'nobody will tell me how long we are to stay, and the brigade leader says that the ones who ask are the ones who do not go home'. The question is: 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the experiences of young people in China during the Cultural Revolution? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your knowledge of the historical context.' (8 marks)","steps":["Write the enquiry at the top of the plan and let it select the material: young people's experiences, so the factions of young workers are in, and the lathes matter only because they show what those young workers were doing instead of working.","Take Source B's content for that enquiry: 'two rival organisations of young workers' held the foundry and the assembly shop against each other, and 'the young workers say that to ask for output is to put production in command', which is evidence that the young had taken the authority in a workplace and knew the slogans to justify it.","Weigh Source B's nature, origin and purpose together: a deputy director reporting a shortfall to the city revolutionary committee will present the young workers as an obstacle, so the report is strong evidence of what they did and weaker evidence of what they believed.","Do the same for Source C in one move: a private letter home admits what no report would, 'I was in the Red Guards in 1967 and I believed every word of it', and then the earth-carrying, the two-room school and the plea to 'send me any book you can'.","Bring contextual knowledge in on the sources rather than beside them: Mao's directive of December 1968 sent educated youth to the villages, and the schools had been shut since June 1966, which is why the writer of Source C has no education to go back to and asks her brother for books.","Judge for the enquiry, not in general: between them they cover both halves of the experience, power in the workplace and exile in the countryside, but each is one workplace and one voice, so neither shows how typical it was."],"cues":["What exactly is the enquiry into?","What can Source B tell you about young people rather than about machines?","How does the writer's purpose shape what he says about them?","Why might a letter to a brother be more candid than a report?","Which piece of your own knowledge explains why she is in Yunnan?","What can neither source show you?"]},{"title":"Question 3(b): finding the main difference of view","setup":"This worked example uses a second pair of interpretations, printed here and written for this room; they are not the room's Interpretations 1 and 2, and neither is a real book nor attributed to a real historian. Interpretation A, from a study of Chinese social policy by a historian writing in the 1990s: 'The sending down was a solution to a problem the cities had made. Schools had been shut for more than two years, factories were not hiring, and millions of former Red Guards had nothing to do and nowhere to be. The countryside absorbed what the towns could not employ.' Interpretation B, from a history of Chinese youth by a historian writing in the 2010s: 'Mao had said for years that learning was worthless unless it was tested by labour, and his directive of December 1968 said plainly that educated young people must be re-educated by the poor and lower-middle peasants. The young were not disposed of; they were sent to be remade.' The question is: 'Study Interpretations A and B. They give different views about why young people were sent from the cities to the countryside from December 1968. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations.' (4 marks)","steps":["Put one question to both before you compare anything — whose problem was being solved? — and answer it for the first: Interpretation A says the towns' problem, since the campaign 'absorbed what the towns could not employ'.","Answer it for the second: Interpretation B says the young people's own, since Mao held that learning had to be 'tested by labour' and they were 'sent to be remade'.","Check that what you have is a difference of view and not of subject: both accept that millions left the cities and that the state moved them, so the disagreement is about purpose, not about what happened.","Write it as one sentence and support it from each: for Interpretation A the campaign was a practical clearance of an urban surplus with 'nothing to do and nowhere to be', while for Interpretation B it was a deliberate act of re-education carried out on the young themselves."],"cues":["What single question should you put to both interpretations first?","What does the second interpretation say the young were sent for?","How do you tell a difference of view from a difference of detail?","How should the difference be worded so that both interpretations support it?"]},{"title":"Question 3(c): explaining why the two historians differ","setup":"This worked example uses a second pair of interpretations, printed here and written for this room; they are not the room's Interpretations 1 and 2, and neither is a real book nor attributed to a real historian. Interpretation A, from a study of Chinese social policy by a historian writing in the 1990s: 'The sending down was a solution to a problem the cities had made. Schools had been shut for more than two years, factories were not hiring, and millions of former Red Guards had nothing to do and nowhere to be. The countryside absorbed what the towns could not employ.' Interpretation B, from a history of Chinese youth by a historian writing in the 2010s: 'Mao had said for years that learning was worthless unless it was tested by labour, and his directive of December 1968 said plainly that educated young people must be re-educated by the poor and lower-middle peasants. The young were not disposed of; they were sent to be remade.' The question is: 'Suggest one reason why Interpretations A and B give different views about why young people were sent from the cities to the countryside from December 1968. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer.' (4 marks)","steps":["Name the difference in one line first, because a reason has to be a reason for something: Interpretation A makes the campaign a way of clearing the cities, while Interpretation B makes it a way of remaking the young.","Give the reason itself: the two were written about twenty years apart, and the later writer could draw on material the earlier one did not have, above all the memoirs and interviews of the sent-down generation that appeared in growing numbers from the 1980s onwards.","Show the reason in the wording of each: Interpretation A counts what the cities could not supply, with its shut schools and its factories 'not hiring', while Interpretation B quotes what the young were told they were for, which is what a memoir records.","Substantiate with one piece of context that fits both readings: Source C, a girl of seventeen sent from Shanghai to a brigade in the hills of Yunnan in spring 1970, writes that 'the peasants were told we had come to learn from them', while what they say among themselves is that the students 'eat their grain and cannot carry a full load' — re-education and a burden shifted out of the city are both visible in the same letter, so conclusions drawn from different evidence can both be reasonable."],"cues":["What must you establish before you can explain a difference?","What does the date each interpretation was written tell you about the evidence available?","Which words in each show what its writer counted as evidence?","Which detail of a source could support either reading, and why does that help your answer?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which document told officials not to suppress the Red Guard movement, and when was it adopted?","a":"The Central Committee's Sixteen Points, adopted on 8 August 1966 at the Eleventh Plenum of the Eighth Central Committee."},{"q":"Why is a one-sided document such as Source B still useful for an enquiry into the economic effects of the Cultural Revolution?","a":"Because it is an internal report to superiors explaining a shortfall, not a public statement: its purpose makes it specific about factions, lost technicians and output below 1966, which is exactly what the enquiry needs."},{"q":"Name one thing Interpretation 1 leaves out of its account of why the Cultural Revolution happened.","a":"Mao's fear that a new privileged class of officials was forming; or the Socialist Education Movement of 1963 to 1966; or the attack on examinations and academic selection in schools."},{"q":"In what sense did the PLA rather than the Party restore order in China after 1967?","a":"Army propaganda teams went into factories, schools and universities, soldiers dominated the 'three-in-one' revolutionary committees that replaced the wrecked party committees, and at the Ninth Congress of April 1969 about two-thirds of the delegates were in uniform."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 3 Section A (16 marks): Question 1 prints Source A and asks 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about...' in a table of two boxes (4 marks); Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b), and you answer one of them: each is 'Explain why...' with two stimulus points and 'You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks).","Paper 3 Section B (36 marks), all on Question 3: (a) 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into...?' (8); (b) 'What is the main difference between these views?' (4); (c) 'Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about...' (4); (d) 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about...?' (16, plus up to 4 for spelling, punctuation, grammar and the use of specialist terminology).","The stimulus-point caps on the 12-mark 'Explain why...': 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'. There is no cap at Level 2. Question 3(d) prints no stimulus points, so no cap applies to it.","Timing on Paper 3: 1 hour 30 minutes for 52 marks, so about a minute a mark plus thinking time. Roughly 5 minutes on Question 1, 18 on Question 2, 12 on 3(a), 6 each on 3(b) and 3(c), and about 40 minutes on 3(d) including the final check for the SPaG marks, which leaves a few minutes in hand.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose, and usefulness is always judged for the enquiry the question names, never in general. Contextual knowledge earns nothing unless it is used on the sources or the interpretations themselves.","The dates that anchor this key topic: 10 November 1965, the attack on Wu Han's play; 8 August 1966, the Sixteen Points; 18 August 1966, the first Tiananmen rally; July 1967, the Wuhan Incident; April 1969, the Ninth Party Congress; December 1968, the directive sending educated youth to the countryside; 13 September 1971, Lin Biao's death; 9 September 1976, Mao's death; 6 October 1976, the arrest of the Gang of Four."],"questions":[{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about the treatment of people accused of holding to the old ways in 1966. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for each valid inference, up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail from Source A. Credit public humiliation instead of trial, the removal of protection by local officials, or the destruction of personal property.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: people accused of holding to the old ways were punished in public rather than tried. Details in the source that tell me this: the writer's former headmaster 'was made to stand at the gate with a board round his neck', and the teachers left at the school 'spend their days writing confessions'. (ii) What I can infer: local officials would no longer protect those who were accused. Details in the source that tell me this: 'nobody spoke for him, not even the men from the district committee, who wrote down what we told them.'","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["An inference is something the source makes certain without stating it; ask what has to be true of a country in which a schoolgirl can describe this without alarm.","Read the letter twice, once for what is done to the accused and once for what the adults around them do about it.","Give each answer in two parts, a claim the source never states and then the exact words that force it."],"codes":["32-3 — 2 The Red Guards and the Red Terror: Red Guard attacks on the 'four olds'","32-3 — 3 The effects of the Cultural Revolution: Social effects"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about the part Mao himself played in the Red Guard movement. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for each valid inference, up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail from Source A. Credit Mao's personal endorsement of the Red Guards, or his authority overriding that of the Party.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: Mao gave the Red Guards his personal blessing in a way ordinary members could see. Details in the source that tell me this: on the eighteenth of August 'the Chairman came out on the rostrum in the early light and put on our armband', and the girl beside the writer 'wept and could not stop'. (ii) What I can infer: the Red Guards took their instructions from Mao rather than from the Party. Details in the source that tell me this: 'The Chairman has told us that there are people in the Party itself taking the capitalist road, and it is our work to find them.'","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask where these young people think their authority comes from, and what the source shows them doing about it.","One inference can come from what Mao does at the rally and the other from what the writer says he has told them.","Set out a claim the source implies, then the phrase that makes it certain, for each of your two answers."],"codes":["32-3 — 2 The Red Guards and the Red Terror: Mao's hold on young people","32-3 — 1 Reasons for the Cultural Revolution: The power struggle"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about what had happened to schooling in China by the autumn of 1966. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for each valid inference, up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail from Source A. Credit the complete stoppage of lessons, or teachers becoming targets rather than authorities.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: ordinary schooling had stopped altogether rather than merely been interrupted. Details in the source that tell me this: 'there is no classroom to go back to: our school has been shut since June', and the writer is in another city months later with no suggestion of returning. (ii) What I can infer: teachers had become targets instead of figures of authority. Details in the source that tell me this: 'the teachers who are left spend their days writing confessions', and the former headmaster was made to stand at the gate with a board round his neck.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The first sentence of the letter is about school; ask what it implies that she is writing from another city in September.","The second inference is about the adults who used to run the school, not about the building.","Each box needs a claim the source does not state and the words from the letter that force it."],"codes":["32-3 — 3 The effects of the Cultural Revolution: Social effects","32-3 — 2 The Red Guards and the Red Terror: Red Guard attacks on the 'four olds'"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. They give different views about the reasons why Mao launched the Cultural Revolution in 1966. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4): a key difference of view identified and supported from both interpretations. The difference is a struggle for power against a genuine ideological purpose.","sol":"The main difference is what each makes the driving force. Interpretation 1 sees a struggle for power: Mao 'gave up the state chairmanship', watched Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping 'build a party machine of their own', and attacked it from outside, so that 'the Red Guards were the weapon, and the target was a rival leadership'. Interpretation 2 sees conviction: 'Mao meant what he said', he feared cadres 'becoming a privileged caste' and young people who 'had never fought for anything', and he 'wanted a new kind of Chinese citizen'. For one, removing rivals was the purpose; for the other, it was the price of a moral project.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["A main difference is a difference of view, not of detail: ask each interpretation what it says Mao was chiefly trying to achieve.","Summarise each in a single sentence of your own before you compare them, then look for the word that carries the claim.","Write one sentence naming the difference, then quote from each interpretation to support it."],"codes":["32-3 — 1 Reasons for the Cultural Revolution: The power struggle","32-3 — 1 Reasons for the Cultural Revolution: Purification of communism"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about the part played by the Red Guards in the Cultural Revolution. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4): an explanation of one reason for difference, analysing the interpretations and substantiated. Credit the different evidence each historian weighs, or the different question each is asking.","sol":"One reason is that the two historians weigh different evidence. Interpretation 1 is a study of politics at the top, so it reads the Red Guards through what happened to leaders - the play, 'the mayor himself', a head of state 'in prison' - and calls them 'the weapon'. Interpretation 2 is a general history, so it reads them through the Socialist Education Movement and 'the remaking of schooling', and sees an attempt to make 'a new kind of Chinese citizen'. Source C supports the second reading, since a former Red Guard writes 'I believed every word of it'. Conclusions drawn from different evidence are legitimate, so both can be reasonable.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Before explaining a difference you must be able to state it; do that in one line about what the Red Guards were for.","Look at what kind of book each interpretation comes from and what evidence that kind of book naturally uses.","Name the reason, then show it in the wording of each interpretation, then add one fact that fits both readings."],"codes":["32-3 — 2 The Red Guards and the Red Terror: Mao's hold on young people","32-3 — 2 The Red Guards and the Red Terror: Red Guard attacks on the 'four olds'"]},{"q":"2(a) Explain why Mao launched the Cultural Revolution in 1966. You may use the following in your answer: • the Great Leap Forward • the Socialist Education Movement. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit the loss of position after the Great Leap, the failure of the Socialist Education Movement to reach the leadership, Mao's fear of a new privileged class, and the cult and the theatre as instruments. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Mao launched the Cultural Revolution first because the Great Leap Forward had cost him his command of the state and he meant to take it back. He gave up the state chairmanship on 27 April 1959 and Liu Shaoqi took it; at the Seven Thousand Cadres Conference in January 1962 Liu told the Party that the famine was mostly man-made. Liu, as head of state, and Deng Xiaoping, as General Secretary, then restored private plots and rural markets and staffed the Party with men who owed their places to them, so that by 1965 Mao presided over a machine he no longer directed. Attacking it from outside was the only way back into it. A second reason is ideological, and Mao had stated it for years. The Socialist Education Movement of 1963 to 1966, with its Four Cleanups of politics, economics, organisation and ideas, was meant to scrub corruption out of the village cadres, but Liu's work teams used it to discipline small officials while the leadership went untouched. That convinced Mao that the Party could not purify itself. He had watched the Soviet Union, as he saw it, settle into comfort under a new class of officials, and he believed the same caste was forming in China. Third, and beyond the printed points, Mao had built instruments outside the Party. Quotations from Chairman Mao was compiled by Lin Biao's political department and issued to the army from 1964, so a generation learned Mao's words as a creed, and Jiang Qing's work in the theatre gave him a base in the cultural world. That is where the campaign opened, with Yao Wenyuan's attack of 10 November 1965 on Wu Han's play about Hai Rui, published in Shanghai because Beijing's press was closed to it. The three make one argument. The Great Leap left Mao with a grievance and a rival, the Socialist Education Movement convinced him the Party would not cure itself, and the cult and the theatre gave him the means to act without it.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Separate what Mao lost from what Mao feared: the question needs both, and they are different kinds of reason.","Three reasons, each ending at the decision to launch the Cultural Revolution, and at least one that neither bullet point suggests.","Open by saying what the Great Leap Forward had done to Mao's own position, and take the argument from there."],"codes":["32-3 — 1 Reasons for the Cultural Revolution: The power struggle","32-3 — 1 Reasons for the Cultural Revolution: Purification of communism"]},{"q":"2(b) Explain why the Red Guards became so powerful in the second half of 1966. You may use the following in your answer: • the mass rallies in Tiananmen Square • the Sixteen Points. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit the Sixteen Points removing official restraint, Mao's own poster, the rallies from 18 August 1966 and free travel, the closing of the schools, and the Little Red Book as a common authority. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The Red Guards became powerful because the leadership of the country deliberately made them so. The Central Committee's Sixteen Points, adopted on 8 August 1966, declared the movement legitimate, told officials not to suppress it and insisted the masses must educate themselves, which stripped local party secretaries of the authority to stop the students. Three days earlier Mao had put up his own big-character poster telling the Party to bombard its headquarters: a chairman inviting attack on his own organisation, which left every official uncertain whether resisting a Red Guard was itself a crime. The rallies turned permission into worship. From 18 August 1966 Mao reviewed the Red Guards in Tiananmen Square, the first time before about a million of them, in a series of eight rallies that ran into late November; he wore a Red Guard armband himself, and the state carried students to Beijing free of charge and fed them at the stations. To be reviewed by the Chairman was to be told that one's authority came from him personally and therefore outranked any office. The third reason, beyond the printed points, is that there was nothing else for the young to do. Schools and universities had closed in June 1966 and the university entrance examination was abolished, so a whole school-age population was released with no lessons, no examinations and no career to protect. Quotations from Chairman Mao, issued from 1964, gave them a text by which any quarrel could be settled, and the Central Cultural Revolution Group under Chen Boda and Jiang Qing supplied the targets. Together these explain the scale: the Sixteen Points removed the brake, the rallies supplied the authority, and the closed schools supplied the crowd. Power that comes from a leader's favour rather than from an office has no limits, which is why by 1967 it had to be taken away by the army.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Power has to come from somewhere: ask who gave it, who could have stopped it, and who was free to use it.","Three reasons, each closing on why the Red Guards could act as they did, with at least one the bullet points do not suggest.","Begin with the document that told officials not to interfere, and explain what that did to every local secretary."],"codes":["32-3 — 2 The Red Guards and the Red Terror: Mao's hold on young people","32-3 — 2 The Red Guards and the Red Terror: Red Guard attacks on the 'four olds'"]},{"q":"2(a) Explain why the Cultural Revolution damaged China's economy. You may use the following in your answer: • the removal of technicians and managers • the 'Up to the mountains and down to the villages' campaign. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit the loss of technical staff, factional disorder in workplaces and on the railways, the sending down of educated youth, and the qualification that farming suffered less. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The first reason is that the Cultural Revolution attacked the people who knew how production worked. Engineers, managers and specialists were denounced as bourgeois experts or as capitalist roaders from 1966, and many were sent to sweep yards or to labour on farms. Factories kept their machines but lost the only people who could set them up and repair them, so imported equipment stood idle. Industrial output fell by roughly 13 per cent in 1967 and in 1968 was still below the 1966 figure. The second reason is disorder rather than policy. Rival Red Guard and rebel worker organisations fought for control of workplaces and cities: the January Storm of 1967 pulled down the Shanghai administration, and in the Wuhan Incident of July 1967 the local army command backed one faction and two envoys from Beijing were seized, so that troops and gunboats had to be sent. Meanwhile the railways carried Red Guards free of charge instead of coal and grain, and in some works production stopped for weeks together. Third, the remedy did damage of its own. From December 1968 the 'Up to the mountains and down to the villages' campaign sent school-age city dwellers into the countryside, something like 17 million of them between 1968 and 1978. They arrived as unskilled extra mouths in brigades already short of grain, while the cities lost the apprentices and students who should have replaced the engineers who had been driven out. Beyond the printed points, the deepest damage was to training itself: with the universities shut from 1966 and entry afterwards decided by class background rather than examination, China stopped producing technical staff for a decade. Farming suffered less, because the communes went on much as before and there was no repeat of the Great Leap's famine. The argument, then, is that the Cultural Revolution did not wreck China's economy so much as strip it of skill and coordination, which is why the cost was still being paid after Mao was dead.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what an economy needs besides machines and land, and what the Cultural Revolution did to it.","Three reasons, each explaining a loss of output or capacity, and one worked out beyond the two bullet points.","Start with the men who were denounced as bourgeois experts and what their removal did inside a factory."],"codes":["32-3 — 3 The effects of the Cultural Revolution: Economic effects","32-3 — 3 The effects of the Cultural Revolution: Social effects"]},{"q":"2(b) Explain why order was restored in China between 1967 and 1969. You may use the following in your answer: • the People's Liberation Army (PLA) • the revolutionary committees. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit Mao's turn against the Red Guards after the factional fighting and Wuhan, the PLA propaganda teams, the three-in-one revolutionary committees, the sending down of educated youth from December 1968, and the Ninth Congress of April 1969. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Order returned first because Mao changed his mind about the Red Guards and turned to the one body that could act. By 1967 rival factions were fighting each other with weapons taken from arsenals, and the Wuhan Incident of July 1967, when the local army command backed the conservative 'Million Heroes' and Beijing's envoys Wang Li and Xie Fuzhi were seized, showed that the disorder now threatened the army itself. The People's Liberation Army, under the defence minister Lin Biao, was sent into factories, schools and offices with propaganda teams, and by the summer of 1968 army and worker teams were breaking up the student organisations in the universities. Second, the revolutionary committees gave the country a government again. Built on a 'three-in-one' combination of army officers, surviving party cadres and representatives of the rebel organisations, they replaced the party committees the Red Guards had destroyed, and every province had one by September 1968. In practice the soldiers dominated them, which is why order returned so quickly once they were in place. Third, and beyond the printed points, the Red Guards were physically removed. Mao's directive of December 1968 told educated young people to go to the countryside to be re-educated by the poor and lower-middle peasants, and the 'Up to the mountains and down to the villages' campaign scattered them across the provinces, where they could not organise, could not travel and depended on a brigade leader for their food. A movement that had been given its authority by the Chairman lost it the same way. The settlement was sealed at the Ninth Party Congress in April 1969, where about two-thirds of the delegates were in uniform and Lin Biao was written into the Party constitution as Mao's successor. Order was therefore restored not by persuasion but by substitution: the army took the place of the Party, and the countryside took the place of the Red Guards.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Order does not return by itself: ask who was given the job, what they replaced, and what happened to the people who had caused the disorder.","Three reasons, each ending at order being restored, and at least one that neither bullet point prompts.","Open with the point at which the factional fighting became a danger to the state itself."],"codes":["32-3 — 4 The end of the Cultural Revolution, 1968–76: Reasons for the end of the Red Guards","32-3 — 3 The effects of the Cultural Revolution: Political effects"]},{"q":"2(a) Explain why Lin Biao's position in China changed between 1966 and 1971. You may use the following in your answer: • the Little Red Book • the Ninth Party Congress of April 1969. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit Lin's management of the cult, the army's indispensability during the restoration of order, his naming as successor in 1969, and Mao's habit of destroying those who accumulated power; the crash of 13 September 1971 and its effect. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Lin Biao rose because he made himself the manager of Mao's cult. As defence minister from 1959 he had the army's political department compile Quotations from Chairman Mao, issued from 1964, and ordered a copy for every soldier, with a preface telling them to study it daily. When the Cultural Revolution began, the man who had supplied the scripture was the obvious heir, and at the Eleventh Plenum of August 1966 he took Liu Shaoqi's place as second in the Party's order. He rose further because the disorder made the army indispensable. From 1967 the People's Liberation Army restored order in the provinces and dominated the revolutionary committees, and at the Ninth Party Congress of April 1969 about two-thirds of the delegates were in uniform. The new Party constitution named Lin as Mao's successor, an extraordinary step that tied the succession to the army. His fall followed from the same cause, and this is the reason to develop beyond the printed points. Mao had spent the Cultural Revolution destroying men who accumulated power, and Lin now held the army, the constitution and the succession together. They quarrelled in 1970 over whether the post of head of state should be revived, Mao began promoting civilians and exploring contact with the United States, which Lin opposed, and in 1971 Mao toured the southern military regions criticising him. The end came on 13 September 1971, when Lin's aircraft crashed in Mongolia as he fled; the government announced that he had plotted to kill Mao and was escaping to the Soviet Union. The change in his position is therefore one process, not two: the cult and the army raised him, and the same concentration of power made him the next man Mao had to remove. The effect outlasted him, because if Mao's chosen successor was a traitor, ordinary people began to ask what else they had been told was untrue.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Rise and fall are one question here: look for a single cause that explains both.","Three reasons, ending at Lin's position in 1971, with at least one that neither bullet point suggests.","Begin with what Lin did with the army's printing presses in 1964 and what it bought him."],"codes":["32-3 — 3 The effects of the Cultural Revolution: Political effects","32-3 — 2 The Red Guards and the Red Terror: Mao's hold on young people"]},{"q":"2(b) Explain why the influence of Zhou Enlai and Deng Xiaoping increased in China after 1971. You may use the following in your answer: • the death of Lin Biao • President Nixon's visit of February 1972. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6, AO1 6. Credit the discrediting of the radicals by Lin Biao's death, the need for professionals in the opening to the United States, Deng's recall in 1973 and the Four Modernisations of January 1975, and the limits set by the Gang of Four and by Mao himself. Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content; no access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Their influence grew first because Lin Biao's death removed the radicals' strongest figure. When Lin's aircraft crashed in Mongolia on 13 September 1971 and the government announced that Mao's own chosen successor, written into the Party constitution at the Ninth Congress of April 1969, had plotted to kill him, the army's political weight fell with him and the Cultural Revolution lost much of its credibility. Someone still had to run the country, and the officials who had kept the ministries working through the chaos were the obvious candidates. Second, the opening to the United States needed professionals. The negotiations that brought President Nixon to Beijing in February 1972 were conducted by Zhou Enlai and his diplomats; they could not have been carried on by rebel organisations or by the Cultural Revolution Group, whose whole language was against dealing with imperialism. Zhou used the standing this gave him to bring experienced administrators back to their desks. Third, and beyond the printed points, the economy required Deng Xiaoping. Zhou had him recalled as a vice-premier in 1973, after more than three years at a tractor repair factory in Jiangxi, and as Zhou sickened Deng ran the government day to day, restoring discipline on the railways and in the factories. In January 1975 Zhou set out the Four Modernisations of agriculture, industry, defence, and science and technology at the Fourth National People's Congress, which gave their programme a public name. The increase was real but never secure, and a top answer says so. The Gang of Four still controlled the press and ran campaigns against them, and when Zhou died on 8 January 1976 Hua Guofeng and not Deng was made acting premier; after the crowds in Tiananmen Square on 4 and 5 April 1976 Deng was blamed and dismissed again. Their influence rose because the alternatives had discredited themselves, and it lasted only as long as Mao allowed it.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Influence is relative: ask who lost standing after 1971 as well as who gained it.","Three reasons, each ending at Zhou or Deng having more say, and at least one that neither bullet point prompts.","Begin with what the announcement about Lin Biao did to the credibility of the Cultural Revolution."],"codes":["32-3 — 4 The end of the Cultural Revolution, 1968–76: The return to power of Deng Xiaoping","32-3 — 3 The effects of the Cultural Revolution: Political effects"]},{"q":"3(a) Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the effects of the Cultural Revolution on China's industry and countryside? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): utility judged for this enquiry with provenance used on content and context used to interpret. Credit B's internal purpose and its detail on lost technicians and output, and C's candour about the countryside; note that neither gives national figures.","sol":"Source B is very useful for the industrial half of the enquiry because it is an internal report, written by a deputy director to the city revolutionary committee in October 1968 to explain a shortfall. That purpose makes it specific rather than rhetorical: two rival organisations of young workers held the foundry and the assembly shop, for several weeks in 1967 nothing left the yard, and the imported lathes cannot be repaired 'because the men who understood them are not here'. The content matches what is known of the period: technicians denounced as bourgeois experts from 1966 and industrial output falling by roughly 13 per cent in 1967. Source C is useful for the countryside, but in a different way. It is a private letter from a seventeen-year-old sent from Shanghai to Yunnan in 1970, so it records the human effect rather than output: the peasants say the students 'eat their grain and cannot carry a full load', the brigade has no electricity, and there is no teacher who has been to a middle school. That is exactly what the 'Up to the mountains and down to the villages' campaign did after December 1968 — it moved unskilled labour into brigades that were already short. For the enquiry, B is the stronger evidence of economic damage and C the stronger evidence of its cost in the villages; neither reaches grain production or national output, so both need figures beside them.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Judge each source for this enquiry only, and start from what its content lets you infer about industry or the countryside.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose: ask what each writer was trying to achieve and how that shapes the detail.","Take Source B first and say what kind of document it is and who was meant to read it."],"codes":["32-3 — 3 The effects of the Cultural Revolution: Economic effects","32-3 — 3 The effects of the Cultural Revolution: Social effects"]},{"q":"3(a) Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into how order was restored in China after 1968? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): utility judged for this enquiry with provenance used on content and context used to interpret. Credit B on the army propaganda team and the revolutionary committee, C on the sending down of Red Guards, and the limits of both.","sol":"Source B is useful because it catches the restoration of order while it is happening. The deputy director is writing in October 1968 and reports that 'since the army propaganda team came in June, the two organisations have been brought into one revolutionary committee and the gates are guarded'. Its nature helps the enquiry: a report about production sent to the city revolutionary committee is not trying to praise the army, so the detail is incidental and therefore convincing. It matches the context, in which PLA teams were sent into workplaces and schools from 1967 and 'three-in-one' revolutionary committees covered every province by September 1968. Source C shows the other instrument, the removal of the young. The writer 'was in the Red Guards in 1967' and by 1970 is carrying earth in Yunnan, told that 'the ones who ask are the ones who do not go home'. A private letter to a brother is candid in a way no official account would be, though it is one person's experience and she cannot see the policy from above; Mao's directive of December 1968 is what put her there. Both have limits. Neither mentions the Ninth Party Congress of April 1969, which sealed the settlement, and neither writer is in a position to say how order was restored nationally. Used together, though, they show the two methods that mattered — soldiers inside the workplace and the countryside for the Red Guards — which makes them well matched to this enquiry.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what each source lets you see of the restoration of order, then whether the writer was in a position to know it.","An incidental detail in a document written for another purpose is often stronger evidence than a claim made on purpose.","Begin with the sentence in Source B about what happened in June, and say why a production report mentioning it matters."],"codes":["32-3 — 4 The end of the Cultural Revolution, 1968–76: Reasons for the end of the Red Guards"]},{"q":"3(d) Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part played by the struggle between Mao and Liu Shaoqi in bringing about the Cultural Revolution? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views reviewed, how the difference is conveyed analysed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit the Socialist Education Movement and the attack on the 'four olds'; against, the loss of the state chairmanship in 1959, the work teams and the fall of Liu and Deng.","sol":"Interpretation 2 makes the struggle with Liu Shaoqi secondary: the Socialist Education Movement and 'the remaking of schooling came before the attack on his rivals, not after it', and what Mao wanted was 'a new kind of Chinese citizen'. Interpretation 1 makes that same struggle the whole story, ending flatly that 'the target was a rival leadership'. The criterion for choosing between them is sequence and aim: if Mao's first moves were aimed at ideas and his opponents were caught up in them, Interpretation 2 holds; if the ideological campaigns were built to reach particular men, Interpretation 1 does. There is real support for Interpretation 2. Mao's fear of a new privileged class was not invented afterwards; he had been saying since the late 1950s that Khrushchev's Soviet Union had gone soft, and the Socialist Education Movement of 1963 to 1966, with its Four Cleanups, was aimed at cadres in general rather than at the head of state. Quotations from Chairman Mao, issued to the army from 1964, and Jiang Qing's remaking of the theatre were attempts to change what a whole population believed. The Sixteen Points of 8 August 1966 speak of transforming ideas, culture, customs and habits, and the Red Guards' first victims were teachers, temples and shop signs, not Politburo members. On that reading the wrecking of the Party was the price of a moral project. The case for Interpretation 1 is strongest exactly where Interpretation 2 is vaguest, which is timing. Mao's loss of position is precise: he gave up the state chairmanship on 27 April 1959, and at the Seven Thousand Cadres Conference of January 1962 Liu told seven thousand officials that the famine was mostly man-made, after which Liu and Deng Xiaoping restored private plots and markets. The Socialist Education Movement itself became a quarrel about control, because Liu's work teams disciplined village cadres while the leadership went untouched, and in June 1966 Liu and Deng sent work teams into the universities to contain the students. When the campaign began in earnest it moved straight up the chain of command: Yao Wenyuan's article of 10 November 1965 attacked a play by Beijing's deputy mayor, the mayor Peng Zhen fell next, Mao's poster of 5 August 1966 told the Party to bombard its own headquarters, Liu was expelled in October 1968 and died at Kaifeng on 12 November 1969, and Deng was sent to a tractor factory in Jiangxi. Those are not accidents of a campaign about ideas. Neither is strong everywhere. Interpretation 1 cannot explain why a man who only wanted Liu removed kept the movement running for two years after Liu was finished. Interpretation 2 cannot explain why a campaign about belief found its first targets inside the Beijing party committee. My judgement is that Interpretation 2 is right about the motive and wrong about the weight. Mao's beliefs chose the language, the Red Guards and most of the victims; the struggle with Liu chose the moment and the shape. Without it the campaigns of 1963 to 1965 would have remained campaigns. The struggle was therefore not the purpose of the Cultural Revolution but its trigger, and that is a larger part than Interpretation 2 allows.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first what would count as proof either way, and say so: a criterion turns an opinion into a judgement.","Interpretation 2's strongest claim is about sequence; test it against the order in which people actually fell.","Open by stating what Interpretation 2 argues about Liu Shaoqi and naming the criterion you will judge it by."],"codes":["32-3 — 1 Reasons for the Cultural Revolution: The power struggle","32-3 — 1 Reasons for the Cultural Revolution: Purification of communism","32-3 — 3 The effects of the Cultural Revolution: Political effects"]},{"q":"3(d) Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about Mao's aims in mobilising the Red Guards in 1966? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views reviewed, how each conveys its view analysed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit the 'four olds', the model operas and the sending down of 1968; against, the direction of the Red Guards at named leaders.","sol":"Interpretation 2 argues that Mao turned to the young because he wanted to remake them: they 'had never fought for anything', and what he was after was 'a new kind of Chinese citizen'. Interpretation 1 gives the Red Guards a narrower job: they were 'the weapon', and 'the target was a rival leadership'. The test is what Mao actually asked the Red Guards to do in 1966 and where their blows landed. Much of the evidence supports Interpretation 2. The generation mobilised in 1966 had been born around the revolution of 1949 and had missed the Long March, the war with Japan and the civil war, and Mao said openly that they needed testing. The instruments were educational before they were political: Quotations from Chairman Mao from 1964, the Four Cleanups of the Socialist Education Movement, and Jiang Qing's model operas, which pushed the traditional repertoire off the stage for a decade. The Sixteen Points of 8 August 1966 told the masses to educate themselves and warned officials not to suppress them. What the Red Guards then did looks like a campaign against a way of life rather than against a faction: the 'four olds' meant temples, opera costumes, private libraries, shop signs and hairstyles, and in November 1966 Red Guards wrecked the temple and cemetery of Confucius at Qufu. Most of the victims were teachers, neighbours and the children of former landlords. Interpretation 1 has the better of the argument at the top. Within days of the first Tiananmen rally of 18 August 1966 the Red Guards were being pointed at particular men. Mao's own poster of 5 August had told the Party to bombard its headquarters; Liu Shaoqi was paraded and denounced as the leading person in authority taking the capitalist road; Deng Xiaoping was stripped of office; the Beijing party committee under Peng Zhen was destroyed; and the Central Cultural Revolution Group under Chen Boda and Jiang Qing decided which targets were legitimate and passed names down. A movement intended only to temper the young did not need a list of ministers. The two convey their views in different ways, which is worth noticing. Interpretation 1 writes in the language of manoeuvre — 'the first blow', 'the next', 'the weapon' — and its evidence is a sequence of falls. Interpretation 2 writes about belief, and uses the word 'pretext' to push the political detail aside. Each is reading the same year through the evidence its subject makes available. On balance I agree with Interpretation 2 about Mao's aim and not about his method. He did want a generation that had fought its own revolution, and nothing else explains the attack on temples and teachers, or the fact that the same young people were sent to the villages from December 1968 once they had served their turn. But the aim was pursued through a struggle for control of the Party, and Mao used the young precisely because they stood outside it and could be pointed at whoever he chose. Interpretation 2 is right that he wanted new citizens; Interpretation 1 is right that in 1966 he used them as a weapon, and those are not alternatives.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Aims are shown by targets: work out who the Red Guards actually attacked in 1966 before deciding what Mao wanted.","Use the sending down of the same young people after 1968 as a test of what they had been for.","State Interpretation 2's claim about the young in your own words, then say what evidence would settle it."],"codes":["32-3 — 2 The Red Guards and the Red Terror: Mao's hold on young people","32-3 — 2 The Red Guards and the Red Terror: Red Guard attacks on the 'four olds'","32-3 — 4 The end of the Cultural Revolution, 1968–76: Reasons for the end of the Red Guards"]},{"q":"3(d) Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the importance of Jiang Qing and the Cultural Revolution Group in the attack on the Chinese Communist Party? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views reviewed, differences of view analysed as conveyed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit the model operas, the 'four olds' and the Little Red Book; against, Yao Wenyuan's article, the fall of Peng Zhen and the naming of targets.","sol":"Interpretation 2 treats Jiang Qing's group as an engine of belief: its work 'was to change what a whole people believed', and the quarrel over a historical play was merely 'a pretext'. Interpretation 1 treats the same group as the point of entry for an attack on the Party machine: 'the first blow fell in the theatre, where Jiang Qing was strongest', and the next fell on Beijing's mayor. The question is whether the Cultural Revolution Group was chiefly an instrument of conversion or an instrument of purge. Interpretation 2 has genuine support. Jiang Qing's field really was the arts: she drove the remaking of Peking opera, so that a handful of revolutionary model works displaced the traditional repertoire for a decade, and the campaign against the 'four olds' that followed was a campaign against what people read, watched and worshipped. The Central Cultural Revolution Group, set up in May 1966 under Chen Boda with Jiang Qing as his deputy, supplied the language the whole movement used, and the Sixteen Points of 8 August 1966 describe the transformation of ideas, culture, customs and habits as the purpose. Quotations from Chairman Mao made Mao's words the test of every argument in China. If the object had been only to remove rivals, none of that was necessary. Interpretation 1 is nevertheless closer to how the Group behaved. Yao Wenyuan's article of 10 November 1965 attacked Wu Han's play about the Ming official Hai Rui, and readers understood the dismissed honest official to stand for Peng Dehuai, sacked in 1959 for criticising the Great Leap Forward. It was published in Shanghai because Jiang Qing could not place it in the Beijing press, which shows the Beijing party committee was the target from the start; Peng Zhen and the Five Man Group he chaired were destroyed within six months. Thereafter the Group supplied the names — Liu Shaoqi denounced as the leading capitalist roader, Deng Xiaoping removed, provincial secretaries handed to the Red Guards — and Jiang Qing herself addressed the rallies. The difference of view is partly a difference of subject, and each historian conveys it in the evidence chosen. Interpretation 1, a study of politics at the top, follows the falls and writes in the language of blows and targets; Interpretation 2, a general history, follows the effect on a whole population and writes about belief. Both can be honest readings of the same evidence, which is why 'pretext' and 'first blow' describe the same article. My judgement is that Interpretation 2 understates Jiang Qing's group without being wrong about it. The cultural work was real and lasting: it decided what a generation could read and watch, and that outlived the purges. But the political function came first in time and was the reason Mao gave the Group its power; it was a weapon before it was a ministry. Jiang Qing ended in the Gang of Four, arrested on 6 October 1976 within a month of Mao's death, which is the fate of a faction rather than of a cultural department. The Group changed what people believed because changing what they believed was the way to break the Party, and Interpretation 1 sees that more clearly.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what the Cultural Revolution Group was for by looking at what it produced and at whom it produced it against.","The place where the first article was published is itself evidence; work out why it could not appear in Beijing.","Say what Interpretation 2 claims about Jiang Qing's group and name the criterion by which you will test it."],"codes":["32-3 — 2 The Red Guards and the Red Terror: Mao's hold on young people","32-3 — 3 The effects of the Cultural Revolution: Political effects","32-3 — 1 Reasons for the Cultural Revolution: The power struggle"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date did Mao first review the Red Guards in Tiananmen Square?","a":"18 August 1966."},{"q":"What were the 'four olds' that the Red Guards were told to destroy?","a":"Old ideas, old culture, old customs and old habits."},{"q":"How and when did Lin Biao die?","a":"In an aircraft crash in Mongolia on 13 September 1971, while fleeing after the failure of a plot against Mao."},{"q":"Which four people made up the 'Gang of Four'?","a":"Jiang Qing, Zhang Chunqiao, Yao Wenyuan and Wang Hongwen."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Mao's position after the Great Leap Forward","open":false,"code":"32-3 — 1 Reasons for the Cultural Revolution: The power struggle","back":"Mao gave up the state chairmanship on 27 April 1959 to Liu Shaoqi but stayed Chairman of the Party. At the Seven Thousand Cadres Conference of January 1962 Liu called the famine mostly man-made."},{"front":"Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping's recovery policies","open":false,"code":"32-3 — 1 Reasons for the Cultural Revolution: The power struggle","back":"After 1962 they restored private plots and rural markets and rebuilt the party machine. Deng defended the method by saying a cat's colour mattered less than whether it caught mice."},{"front":"The Socialist Education Movement, 1963-66","open":false,"code":"32-3 — 1 Reasons for the Cultural Revolution: Purification of communism","back":"Mao's campaign to purify the Party, with its Four Cleanups of politics, economics, organisation and ideas. Work teams, one led by Liu's wife Wang Guangmei at Taoyuan, disciplined village cadres but not the leadership."},{"front":"Quotations from Chairman Mao (the Little Red Book)","open":false,"code":"32-3 — 1 Reasons for the Cultural Revolution: Purification of communism","back":"Compiled by Lin Biao's political department and issued to the army from 1964, then printed in vast numbers for the Red Guards. It made Mao's words the test of every argument."},{"front":"The Sixteen Points, 8 August 1966","open":false,"code":"32-3 — 2 The Red Guards and the Red Terror: Mao's hold on young people","back":"The Central Committee's decision on the Cultural Revolution: the masses must educate themselves and officials must not suppress the movement. It removed the authority of local secretaries to stop the Red Guards."},{"front":"The mass rallies of 1966","open":false,"code":"32-3 — 2 The Red Guards and the Red Terror: Mao's hold on young people","back":"From 18 August 1966 Mao reviewed the Red Guards in Tiananmen Square, first before about a million; eight rallies ran into late November while the state carried students to Beijing free of charge."},{"front":"Jiang Qing and the Central Cultural Revolution Group","open":false,"code":"32-3 — 2 The Red Guards and the Red Terror: Mao's hold on young people","back":"Mao's wife, a former actress, was deputy head of the Group set up in May 1966 under Chen Boda. She remade Peking opera around a few revolutionary model works and helped choose the movement's targets."},{"front":"The 'four olds'","open":false,"code":"32-3 — 2 The Red Guards and the Red Terror: Red Guard attacks on the 'four olds'","back":"Old ideas, old culture, old customs and old habits: the Red Guards' declared enemy. In practice it meant temples, opera costumes, private libraries, shop signs, western clothes and hairstyles."},{"front":"The Red Terror and cultural destruction","open":false,"code":"32-3 — 2 The Red Guards and the Red Terror: Red Guard attacks on the 'four olds'","back":"Houses were searched, property burned and 'class enemies' paraded in dunce caps at struggle meetings; teachers and former landlords were beaten or driven to suicide. Red Guards wrecked the Confucius temple at Qufu in November 1966."},{"front":"The purging of Liu Shaoqi","open":false,"code":"32-3 — 3 The effects of the Cultural Revolution: Political effects","back":"Denounced as the leading person in authority taking the capitalist road, he fell from second to eighth in the Party in August 1966, was expelled in October 1968 and died at Kaifeng on 12 November 1969."},{"front":"The rise and fall of Lin Biao","open":false,"code":"32-3 — 3 The effects of the Cultural Revolution: Political effects","back":"Defence minister from 1959 and manager of Mao's cult, named Mao's successor in the Party constitution at the Ninth Congress of April 1969. He died when his aircraft crashed in Mongolia on 13 September 1971."},{"front":"Education during the Cultural Revolution","open":false,"code":"32-3 — 3 The effects of the Cultural Revolution: Social effects","back":"Schools and universities closed in June 1966 and the university entrance examination was abolished. Places later went by class background and political record; the examination was restored only in 1977."},{"front":"The economic cost of the Cultural Revolution","open":false,"code":"32-3 — 3 The effects of the Cultural Revolution: Economic effects","back":"Technicians and managers were denounced as bourgeois experts and removed, factions fought inside workplaces, and industrial output fell by roughly 13 per cent in 1967, still below the 1966 level in 1968. Farming suffered less."},{"front":"'Up to the mountains and down to the villages'","open":false,"code":"32-3 — 4 The end of the Cultural Revolution, 1968–76: Reasons for the end of the Red Guards","back":"Mao's directive of December 1968 sent educated young people to be re-educated by the poor and lower-middle peasants. Something like 17 million went between 1968 and 1978, which ended the Red Guards as a force."},{"front":"The Gang of Four and the death of Mao","open":false,"code":"32-3 — 4 The end of the Cultural Revolution, 1968–76: The return to power of Deng Xiaoping","back":"Jiang Qing, Zhang Chunqiao, Yao Wenyuan and Wang Hongwen controlled the press and culture. Mao died on 9 September 1976 and Hua Guofeng had them arrested on 6 October 1976."}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The sources and interpretations written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Source A: From a letter written by a sixteen-year-old member of a Red Guard detachment in Beijing to her parents in Taiyuan, September 1966. Written for this room in the style of such a letter; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — You are not to worry that I have not gone back to the classroom, because there is no classroom to go back to: our school has been shut since June and the teachers who are left spend their days writing confessions. On the eighteenth of August I stood in the square from four in the morning, and when the Chairman came out on the rostrum in the early light and put on our armband, the girl beside me wept and could not stop. We came here in August, free of charge on the train, eleven of us, with nothing but a letter from our detachment, and the station feeds us. Yesterday we searched three courtyards in the lane behind the market. We burned a chest of opera costumes and a shelf of old books in the road, and took the signboard off a medicine shop that had the old name on it. Our former headmaster was made to stand at the gate with a board round his neck, and nobody spoke for him, not even the men from the district committee, who wrote down what we told them. Do not send me money. The Chairman has told us that there are people in the Party itself taking the capitalist road, and it is our work to find them.","Source B: From a report sent by the deputy director of a machine-tool works in Shenyang to the city revolutionary committee, October 1968. Written for this room in the style of such a report; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — Production in this works has not recovered. Two rival organisations of young workers have held the foundry and the assembly shop against each other since the spring of last year, and for several weeks in 1967 nothing left the yard at all. Our chief engineer and four of the technical staff were denounced as bourgeois experts in 1966; two of them are sweeping the yard and the others have been sent to a farm in the north. Since the army propaganda team came in June, the two organisations have been brought into one revolutionary committee and the gates are guarded, but we cannot repair the imported lathes, because the men who understood them are not here, and our output of machine tools this year will again be below the figure for 1966. The young workers say that to ask for output is to put production in command.","Source C: From a letter written by a seventeen-year-old sent from Shanghai to a production brigade in the hills of Yunnan, to her younger brother at home, spring 1970. Written for this room in the style of such a letter; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — Tell mother the padded coat arrived. We rise before it is light and carry earth for the terraces, and in the evening there is a meeting and then nothing, because the brigade has no electricity. There are four of us from Shanghai here, and the peasants were told we had come to learn from them; what they say among themselves is that we eat their grain and cannot carry a full load. I was in the Red Guards in 1967 and I believed every word of it. There is a school of two rooms for the small children and no teacher who has been to a middle school. Send me any book you can, even an old one. Nobody will tell me how long we are to stay, and the brigade leader says that the ones who ask are the ones who do not go home.","Interpretation 1: From a study of politics at the top of the Chinese Communist Party, by a historian writing in the 1980s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — The Cultural Revolution is best understood as a struggle for power. After the Great Leap Forward had begun to fail, Mao gave up the state chairmanship and watched Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping restore private plots and markets and build a party machine of their own. His answer was to attack that machine from outside it. The first blow fell in the theatre, where Jiang Qing was strongest, on a play written by the deputy mayor of Beijing; the next fell on the mayor himself; within two years the head of state was in prison. The Red Guards were the weapon, and the target was a rival leadership.","Interpretation 2: From a general history of the People's Republic of China, by a historian writing in the 2000s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — Mao meant what he said. He had watched the Soviet Union, as he saw it, settle into comfort under a new class of officials, and he believed the same was happening in China, where his own cadres were becoming a privileged caste and where the young had never fought for anything. The Socialist Education Movement and the remaking of schooling came before the attack on his rivals, not after it. The quarrel over a historical play was a pretext; the work of Jiang Qing's group was to change what a whole people believed. Mao wanted a new kind of Chinese citizen, and was prepared to break the Party to get one.","How the room uses them: Source A is the inference source for Question 1. Sources B and C are the contemporary pair for the 8-mark usefulness question, 3(a). Interpretations 1 and 2 are the pair for 3(b), 3(c) and 3(d). Apart from the short second pair of interpretations printed inside worked examples 3 and 4, every quotation in the model answers, worked examples and plan of this room comes from the five texts above and from nowhere else."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 3 Question 3(d), 16 marks)","items":["Question: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the damage the Cultural Revolution did to the authority of the Chinese Communist Party? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks] This is one paragraph from the middle of the answer, putting the case for Interpretation 2 and then measuring it against Interpretation 1.","Interpretation 2's claim that Mao 'was prepared to break the Party' carries further than Interpretation 1's narrower account, in which 'the target was a rival leadership'. What came down between 1966 and 1969 was a structure rather than a faction. Mao's poster of 5 August 1966 told the Party's own members to bombard its headquarters, and the Sixteen Points of 8 August ordered officials not to suppress the movement, so that a party committee could be attacked and could not defend itself. From the January Storm in Shanghai in 1967 provincial and city committees were pulled down one after another, and by September 1968 every province was governed by a revolutionary committee instead. The Ninth Congress of 1 to 24 April 1969 was the first full congress the Party had held since 1956, and about two-thirds of its delegates were in uniform; and provincial party committees were not rebuilt across the country until 1971. A campaign aimed only at Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping would not have needed to leave committees in every province unable to meet, or the army doing their work for three years. What Interpretation 1 still explains is the shape of the wreckage, since the men who fell first were the men Mao had quarrelled with, and the Party's name, its Chairmanship and its right to expel Liu in October 1968 were all left standing. The authority that was broken was the authority of party officials over the people they governed, not the Party's claim to rule.","Why this is Level 4: it 'provides an explained evaluation reviewing the alternative views in coming to a substantiated judgement' — Interpretation 2's claim is built up and then held against what Interpretation 1 can still account for. The analysis is precise about how the differences of view are conveyed (Interpretation 2's 'the Party' as a whole set against Interpretation 1's 'a rival leadership'), and the contextual knowledge is precisely selected: the poster of 5 August 1966, the Sixteen Points, the January Storm, a revolutionary committee in every province by September 1968, the thirteen years between the Party congress of 1956 and that of April 1969, and the rebuilding of provincial committees in 1971, each used to test the claim rather than to decorate it. The line of reasoning is sustained and ends in a judgement that distinguishes the kind of authority that was lost from the kind that survived."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same as above — 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the damage the Cultural Revolution did to the authority of the Chinese Communist Party?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","Interpretation 2 says that Mao was prepared to break the Party to get a new kind of Chinese citizen, and I mostly agree with this. The Cultural Revolution did a lot of damage to the Communist Party, because party officials were attacked by the Red Guards and lost their jobs, and the old committees were replaced by revolutionary committees. Interpretation 1 says that the target was a rival leadership, which is partly true as well, because Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping were the ones who were removed and Liu died in prison. However, the Party was still there at the end and Mao was still Chairman, so it was not completely destroyed. Overall I think Interpretation 2 is more right, because the Party never had the same authority again after so many of its officials had been humiliated in public.","Why this is Level 2 and not Level 3: it does offer 'valid evaluative comment to agree with or counter the interpretation', it selects details from both interpretations, and it gives an overall judgement, which is what Level 2 asks for. What it misses is Level 3's requirement that 'relevant contextual knowledge is used directly to support the evaluation': 'lost their jobs', 'replaced by revolutionary committees' and 'died in prison' are assertions where the poster of 5 August 1966, the Sixteen Points of 8 August 1966, a revolutionary committee in every province by September 1968, Liu's expulsion in October 1968 and his death at Kaifeng on 12 November 1969 are needed. The judgement is also 'insecure or undeveloped', because no criterion is offered for what would count as breaking a party, and 'never had the same authority again' is asserted rather than shown."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Socialist Education Movement and the power struggle that produced it","items":["Mao gave up the state chairmanship of the People's Republic on 27 April 1959, after the Great Leap Forward, and remained Chairman of the Chinese Communist Party; Liu Shaoqi took the state post.","Peng Dehuai, the defence minister, was dismissed in 1959 for criticising the Great Leap Forward at the Lushan conference; Lin Biao replaced him.","At the Seven Thousand Cadres Conference in Beijing, January 1962, Liu Shaoqi told the Party that the famine was mostly man-made rather than the result of natural disaster.","Liu Shaoqi as head of state and Deng Xiaoping as General Secretary rebuilt the economy after 1962 by allowing private plots and rural markets; Deng defended the method with a saying that a cat's colour mattered less than whether it caught mice.","The Socialist Education Movement ran from 1963 to 1966; its 'Four Cleanups' were of politics, economics, organisation and ideas.","Work teams were sent into the villages; one led by Liu Shaoqi's wife Wang Guangmei went to the Taoyuan brigade in Hebei from late 1963, and her 'Taoyuan experience' was later used against the family at struggle meetings.","Mao concluded that the movement had disciplined village cadres while leaving the leadership untouched, and that the Party could not purify itself.","Quotations from Chairman Mao Zedong, the Little Red Book, was compiled for the army by Lin Biao's political department and first issued in 1964, with a copy for every soldier.","Mao's stated fear was that China was growing a privileged class of officials, as he believed the Soviet Union had done under Khrushchev.","Examinations and academic selection in schools were attacked in these years as a ladder for the privileged, which prepared the ground for closing the schools in 1966."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the mass rallies of 1966 and Jiang Qing","items":["Yao Wenyuan's article attacking Wu Han's play 'Hai Rui Dismissed from Office' appeared in the Shanghai paper Wenhui Bao on 10 November 1965; readers understood the dismissed honest official to stand for Peng Dehuai.","Wu Han was a historian and a deputy mayor of Beijing; his superior, the mayor Peng Zhen, chaired the Five Man Group in charge of the cultural question, and both were destroyed in the spring of 1966.","The Central Cultural Revolution Group was set up in May 1966 under Chen Boda, with Jiang Qing, Mao's wife and a former actress, as his deputy; Zhang Chunqiao and Yao Wenyuan also served on it.","The first Red Guard groups formed in Beijing middle schools in late May 1966 and spread through the summer.","Mao's own big-character poster, 'Bombard the Headquarters', was put up on 5 August 1966.","The Central Committee adopted the Sixteen Points on 8 August 1966 at the Eleventh Plenum of the Eighth Central Committee: the masses were to educate themselves and officials were not to suppress the movement.","Mao reviewed the Red Guards in Tiananmen Square for the first time on 18 August 1966, before about a million of them, wearing a Red Guard armband; eight such rallies were held, running into late November 1966.","Students travelled to Beijing free of charge on the railways and were housed and fed at the state's expense, a movement known as the exchange of revolutionary experience.","Jiang Qing remade Peking opera, so that a small number of revolutionary model works displaced the traditional repertoire for about a decade.","At the Eleventh Plenum of August 1966 Liu Shaoqi fell from second to eighth in the Party's order and Lin Biao became Mao's deputy."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Red Guard attacks on the 'four olds'","items":["The 'four olds' were old ideas, old culture, old customs and old habits, named in the Sixteen Points of August 1966 as the enemy.","In practice this meant temples, churches and mosques, opera costumes, private libraries and paintings, street and shop names, western clothes, jewellery and long hair.","Red Guards from Beijing attacked the temple and cemetery of Confucius at Qufu in Shandong in November 1966.","Houses were searched for hidden gold, foreign books and family treasures, and the property was burned in the street.","'Class enemies' - former landlords, capitalists, intellectuals and their children - were paraded in dunce caps with placards round their necks at struggle meetings.","Teachers and headmasters were among the first victims, because schools were where the Red Guards already were; many were beaten and some killed or driven to suicide.","Estimates of the number killed in the Cultural Revolution as a whole vary widely, from several hundred thousand upwards, and no figure is agreed.","By 1967 rival Red Guard factions, each claiming to be the true followers of Mao, were fighting one another with weapons seized from arsenals and from army units.","The January Storm in Shanghai in January 1967 saw rebel organisations seize the newspapers and pull down the city administration; a Shanghai Revolutionary Committee under Zhang Chunqiao replaced it on 24 February 1967.","Nothing the Red Guards did was punished, because the Sixteen Points had told officials not to suppress them, which is why local government simply stopped functioning in many places."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the purging of Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping and the rise and fall of Lin Biao","items":["In June 1966, with Mao away from Beijing, Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping sent work teams into the universities to control the student movement; Mao returned and condemned them for suppressing the masses.","Liu Shaoqi was denounced as 'the leading person in authority taking the capitalist road' and paraded at struggle meetings with his wife Wang Guangmei.","Liu was expelled from the Party at the Twelfth Plenum in October 1968 and died, denied proper medical care, at Kaifeng on 12 November 1969; he was rehabilitated in 1980.","Deng Xiaoping lost all his posts and was sent to work at a tractor repair factory in Jiangxi province, where he stayed until 1973.","Provincial and city party committees were destroyed across China and replaced by revolutionary committees.","Lin Biao had been defence minister since 1959 and had built the cult of Mao inside the army through the Little Red Book.","At the Ninth Party Congress, held in Beijing from 1 to 24 April 1969, about two-thirds of the delegates were in uniform and the new Party constitution named Lin Biao as Mao's successor.","Mao and Lin quarrelled in 1970 over whether the post of head of state should be revived, and Lin opposed the opening to the United States.","Lin Biao died on 13 September 1971 when his aircraft crashed in Mongolia; the government announced that he had plotted against Mao and was fleeing to the Soviet Union.","The announcement damaged Mao's authority, because the successor written into the Party's own constitution had been declared a traitor."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the PLA and the 'Up to the mountains and down to the villages' campaign","items":["In the Wuhan Incident of July 1967 the local military commander Chen Zaidao backed the conservative 'Million Heroes' against the rebel workers, and two envoys from Beijing, Wang Li and Xie Fuzhi, were seized; they were released and back in Beijing by 25 July 1967.","Mao's answer was the People's Liberation Army: propaganda teams were sent into factories, schools, offices and universities to take control.","Revolutionary committees were built on a 'three-in-one' combination of army officers, surviving party cadres and representatives of the rebel organisations.","Every province, municipality and autonomous region had a revolutionary committee by September 1968, and soldiers dominated most of them.","In the summer of 1968 worker and army teams were sent into the universities in Beijing to break up the fighting student factions.","Mao's directive of December 1968 said that educated young people should go to the countryside to be re-educated by the poor and lower-middle peasants.","The 'Up to the mountains and down to the villages' campaign sent something like 17 million young people from the cities to the villages between 1968 and 1978.","Sent-down youths had no say in where they went, were dependent on the brigade for food and work points, and often had no way of returning for years.","The campaign ended the Red Guards as a political force, because it scattered them where they could not organise or travel.","The Ninth Party Congress of April 1969 declared the Cultural Revolution a victory and confirmed the army's dominance of the new order."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the 'Gang of Four' and the death of Mao","items":["Zhou Enlai was premier throughout the Cultural Revolution and used his position to protect officials and institutions where he could.","Zhou steered the opening to the United States; President Nixon visited China in February 1972 and met Mao in Beijing.","Deng Xiaoping was brought back as a vice-premier in 1973 at Zhou's urging, and ran the government as Zhou's health failed.","In January 1975, at the Fourth National People's Congress, Zhou Enlai set out the Four Modernisations: agriculture, industry, national defence, and science and technology.","The 'Gang of Four' were Jiang Qing, Zhang Chunqiao, Yao Wenyuan and Wang Hongwen; they controlled the press and the cultural apparatus and attacked Zhou and Deng through campaigns in the newspapers.","Zhou Enlai died on 8 January 1976; Hua Guofeng, not Deng, was made acting premier.","Wreaths left for Zhou in Tiananmen Square at the Qingming festival were removed, and the crowds that gathered on 4 and 5 April 1976 were dispersed; Deng Xiaoping was blamed and dismissed from his posts again.","Mao Zedong died on 9 September 1976.","On 6 October 1976 Hua Guofeng, with the support of the army and the security apparatus, had the Gang of Four arrested; they were tried in 1980-81 and Jiang Qing was sentenced to death with a reprieve.","Deng Xiaoping returned to power for the third time in 1977 and the university entrance examination was restored the same year."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the source and interpretation moves this paper rewards","items":["Inference: say something the source does not state but that must be true if the source is true, then give the words that force it. One inference and one supporting detail in each box.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose: what kind of document it is, who made it and when, and why it was made.","Utility: judge each source for the enquiry the question names, not in general. Content first, then how nature, origin and purpose make that content more or less useful, then the context that confirms or qualifies it.","One-sidedness is not automatically a weakness: a document written to persuade is strong evidence of what its author wanted others to believe.","An incidental detail, mentioned in passing in a document written for another purpose, is usually better evidence than a claim the author set out to make.","No credit is given for contextual knowledge unless it is linked to the evaluation of the source or the interpretation, and none for general remarks about bias.","Main difference (3(b)): a difference of view, not of wording or of detail. Say in one sentence what each interpretation argues, then name the difference and quote from both.","Reason for difference (3(c)): the usual reasons are that the historians weigh different evidence, ask different questions, or emphasise different aspects. Differences drawn from different evidence are legitimate and can be explained.","Evaluating an interpretation (3(d)): use both interpretations and your own knowledge, analyse how each conveys its view, and finish with a judgement that answers 'how far'.","Never judge an interpretation right or wrong by whether you like it: test its claims against dated evidence, and say what it explains and what it cannot."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 3 Question 3(d): 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the damage the Cultural Revolution did to the authority of the Chinese Communist Party?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","items":["The question as printed: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the damage the Cultural Revolution did to the authority of the Chinese Communist Party? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks] Note that this question prints no stimulus points: the two interpretations are the stimulus, both must be used, and an answer that evaluates one in isolation is capped.","Decode the statement. The claim to be tested is Interpretation 2's last line, that Mao 'was prepared to break the Party' to get a new kind of Chinese citizen: on that account the Party itself is among the casualties. Interpretation 1 keeps the damage narrower, since Mao attacks 'that machine from outside it' and 'the target was a rival leadership' — particular men and the machine they had built. The criterion to announce in the opening is what would count as breaking the Party: the removal of a leadership, or the loss of the institution's power to govern and to be obeyed.","Opening, three or four sentences: state in your own words what Interpretation 2 claims about the Party, name Interpretation 1 as the narrower account of the damage, announce the criterion, and signal the position you will end at, so the line of reasoning is visible from the start.","Paragraph 1 — the case for Interpretation 2, from what happened to the Party's machinery. Mao's poster of 5 August 1966 told the Party's own members to bombard its headquarters; the Sixteen Points of 8 August 1966 ordered officials not to suppress the movement, so that a party committee could be attacked and could not defend itself; and from the January Storm in Shanghai in 1967 provincial and city committees were pulled down one after another and replaced by revolutionary committees, one in every province by September 1968. Interim judgement: for something like three years the Party's own committees did not administer China.","Paragraph 2 — the case for Interpretation 2, from what happened to the Party's standing. The Ninth Party Congress, held in Beijing from 1 to 24 April 1969, was the first full congress the Party had held since 1956, and about two-thirds of its delegates were in uniform; and provincial party committees were not rebuilt across the country until 1971. Liu Shaoqi, the head of state, was expelled at the Twelfth Plenum in October 1968 and died at Kaifeng on 12 November 1969, and was rehabilitated in 1980; Lin Biao, written into the Party constitution as Mao's successor in 1969, was announced as a traitor after his aircraft crashed in Mongolia on 13 September 1971. Interim judgement: a party that has to unsay its own constitution has lost more than a faction.","Paragraph 3 — the case against, using Interpretation 1. Quote 'the target was a rival leadership' and show how much survived: Mao remained Chairman of the Party throughout; it was the Party's own plenum that expelled Liu in October 1968 and its own congress that named Lin in April 1969; the People's Liberation Army rebuilt order for the Party rather than in place of it, dominating the revolutionary committees from 1967; Deng Xiaoping was brought back as a vice-premier in 1973; and it was the Party leadership that arrested the Gang of Four on 6 October 1976. Interim judgement: an institution that purges, survives and then rehabilitates its victims has been damaged, not broken.","Paragraph 4 — what neither interpretation can explain, which is where the best marks are. Interpretation 1 cannot explain why committees at every level, and not only the ones Liu and Deng controlled, were destroyed, or why the Party had to be rebuilt from the bottom upwards after 1969. Interpretation 2 cannot explain why Mao kept the Party's name, its Chairmanship, its constitution and its congress at all, if he was willing to break it.","Analyse how each conveys its view, which is part of the Level 4 descriptor, and do it through the scale each writer works at. Interpretation 1 counts individuals and posts — 'the deputy mayor of Beijing', 'the mayor himself', 'the head of state' — so the damage arrives as a list of falls. Interpretation 2 names whole bodies — 'the Party', 'a whole people' — so the damage arrives as something done to an institution. The words each chooses for the victim are the evidence for what each thinks was damaged.","Conclusion: answer 'how far' with a qualified position and a reason. Something like — Interpretation 2 is right that what was damaged was the institution and not merely a leadership, because for three years the Party could neither meet nor govern and had to be rebuilt from below; but Interpretation 1 is right that Mao never meant to destroy his own instrument, and the Chairmanship, the constitution and the congress he kept are what he rebuilt it from. The authority that did not come back was the authority of the local official over the people he governed.","Timing: about 40 minutes of the 1 hour 30 minutes. Five minutes to plan the criterion and the paragraph order, thirty to write, and five to read back for the four SPaG marks, which reward consistent accuracy and a wide range of specialist terms — use 'cadres', 'capitalist roader', 'revolutionary committee', 'the four olds' and 'Red Guards' accurately rather than often.","What the conclusion must not do: repeat the paragraphs, sit on the fence, or judge the interpretation by whether you like it. It must say how far Interpretation 2 can be agreed with, on the criterion you announced, and why the evidence you selected settles it that way."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:54:50.843718Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.93,"notes":"The fix pass holds on every point. Verified in the kit: the plan and both model paragraphs now sit on a new 3(d) about the damage done to the Party's authority — a focus in none of questions 13-15 — with a decode bullet that tests 'prepared to break the Party' against 'a rival leadership' (so question 3's main difference is not stated), a conveyance bullet about the scale of language rather than the kind of book (so question 4's reason is not given), and the old 'trigger' conclusion now surviving only where it belongs, in room.questions[13].sol; lesson.examples[3] and [4] work a second, self-contained pair of interpretations on the sending down from December 1968, and example [4]'s reason for difference is the twenty years between the writers, not the reason credited in question 4. room.questions[10].sol now reads 'after more than three years at a tractor repair factory in Jiangxi' (Deng arrived October 1969, flown back 21 February 1973); room.questions[7].sol drops the grain-output claim; lesson.idea[0] and Interpretation 1 now put the failure of the Leap before the loss of the state chairmanship on 27 April 1959, with the Party told in advance that Mao would not stand again; extras[5] reads 'nothing the Red Guards did was punished'; lesson.why makes no claim about what the paper sets most often; the exit ticket's four items each have one answer; and Source A dates the journey to August 1966, which is when the exchange of revolutionary experience began. Re-checked on the web: the Ninth Congress of 1-24 April 1969 with about two-thirds of the delegates in uniform, provincial party committees not rebuilt across the country until 1971, the revolutionary committee in every province by September 1968, Liu's expulsion in October 1968, death on 12 November 1969 and rehabilitation in 1980, Lin Biao on 13 September 1971, the arrest of the Gang of Four on 6 October 1976, and free rail travel for students from August 1966. All sixteen questions re-solved: the answer keys, tariffs, AO splits, June 2024 cap wording, level labels and the SPaG line on 3(d) only are all correct, every key idea is still tested, `node kit-tools check` returns OK and the duplication scan reports none. Three small tidies remain, none of which would cost a mark."}}},"32-4":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"32-4","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 4 is the part of Option 32 that asks what Communist rule actually did to people, and it is the richest ground on the whole paper for the questions that carry the most marks. Section B sets two historians against each other, and the argument they are usually given is exactly the argument of this room: were the Chinese people better off in 1976 than in 1949, and can the schools, the clinics and the Marriage Law be counted separately from the camps, the household registration and the closed temples? If you can put a date and a figure to the Marriage Law of 1 May 1950, to the pinyin scheme of 1958, to the barefoot doctors after 1965 and to the attack on the Four Olds from August 1966, you can argue either side of that question and judge between them. The room teaches the changes as a case that can be argued, and it trains the five moves the paper rewards: inference from a source, causal explanation that goes past the printed bullet points, judgement of two sources for the enquiry actually named, spotting how two historians differ and why, and evaluating one of them against the other and against what you know.","idea":[{"code":"32-4 — 1 Communist control: Propaganda and censorship","h":"One voice: propaganda, censorship and the cult of Mao","t":"The Party owned every newspaper, radio station, publishing house, cinema and school, so there was no second version of events. Mao's Talks at the Yan'an Forum on Literature and Art of May 1942 had already laid down the rule that art and writing exist to serve the workers, peasants and soldiers, and the Propaganda Department applied it: loudspeakers in every village and work unit, wall posters, mass campaigns, and model citizens such as the soldier Lei Feng, held up for imitation from March 1963. The cult of Mao grew out of this machinery. Lin Biao made the study of Mao Zedong Thought compulsory in the People's Liberation Army and had Quotations from Chairman Mao printed for soldiers in 1964; more than 700 million copies of the Little Red Book had been printed by the end of 1967, and Mao's portrait and badge were in every home. When he reviewed over a million Red Guards in Tiananmen Square on 18 August 1966 the cult had become a political weapon he could use against his own Party. A top-level answer treats propaganda as an instrument with a purpose, naming what the Party wanted obeyed, rather than listing posters and slogans."},{"code":"32-4 — 1 Communist control: Thought control and 'Reform Through Labour'","h":"Thought reform, the file and the camp","t":"Belief itself was to be corrected, not merely behaviour. From 1951 'thought reform' campaigns put intellectuals, teachers and former officials into study groups where they wrote and rewrote confessions, criticised themselves in front of colleagues and were criticised at struggle meetings; a work unit kept a personal file on every member which followed them for life. Where re-education failed there was labour. 'Reform through labour', laogai, was put on a legal footing by regulations of September 1954, and prisoners worked in camps and state farms in Qinghai, Xinjiang and the far north-east. From August 1957 'education through labour', laojiao, allowed the police to send a person away for years without any trial at all, and it was used freely on those labelled rightists after the Hundred Flowers campaign, when roughly half a million people were condemned in 1957. A top-level answer explains what this system bought the Party — silence that looked like agreement — and notes that its efficiency depended on the danwei and the village brigade knowing everybody."},{"code":"32-4 — 2 Family life and the role of women: Changes in family life","h":"The family broken open: communes, canteens and the danwei","t":"The old Chinese family was an economic unit under the authority of its eldest men, holding land, arranging marriages and worshipping its ancestors. The Party dismantled each of those functions. The Agrarian Reform Law of 28 June 1950 took the land from the landlords and with it the power of the clans; collectivisation moved through mutual aid teams and cooperatives to the people's communes of 1958, about 26,000 of which held almost every peasant household by the end of that year, with communal canteens, crèches and homes for the elderly doing what the household had done. In the towns the danwei, or work unit, replaced the family as the body that gave you housing, ration coupons, a clinic, a school place and permission to marry. The household registration regulations of 9 January 1958 then fixed every person as rural or urban and made moving to a city almost impossible, and from 1968 about seventeen million young townspeople were sent down to the countryside. A top-level answer shows the household losing its functions one by one to the state, and asks who gained authority as a result."},{"code":"32-4 — 2 Family life and the role of women: Reasons for changes in the status of women","h":"The Marriage Law of 1950 and the limits of a law","t":"Foot binding had been outlawed by the new Republic in 1912 but survived in the countryside, and the Party enforced the ban until it disappeared. The decisive measure was the Marriage Law of 1 May 1950: marriage became a free agreement between the two people marrying, with arranged and child marriage, concubinage, the bride price and interference with a widow's remarriage all forbidden, a minimum age of twenty for men and eighteen for women, and the same right to divorce for a wife as for a husband. The 1954 Constitution declared women equal with men in political, economic, cultural, social and family life, the All-China Democratic Women's Federation of 1949 carried the law into the villages with meetings and travelling plays, and communes and factories put women into paid work under the slogan that women hold up half the sky. Enforcement was another matter: divorce petitions ran into hundreds of thousands a year in the early 1950s, most brought by women, but village opinion, mothers-in-law and cadres who were themselves traditionally married slowed the law down, and women's work often earned fewer work points than men's. A top-level answer distinguishes what the law changed from what custom kept, and dates both."},{"code":"32-4 — 3 Education and health: Reasons for changes in education","h":"Pinyin, night schools and the years when the schools shut","t":"About four in five adults could not read in 1949, and a peasant state that wanted to be industrial could not leave it there. The answer came in three parts: simplify the script, with the Chinese Character Simplification Scheme of 1956 cutting the strokes in common characters; supply an alphabet, with the Hanyu pinyin scheme approved by the National People's Congress on 11 February 1958, which let a teacher give a learner the sound of a character; and build the classes, in winter schools, commune and factory night classes and a primary system that reached most children for the first time. Literacy rose steeply, and the 1982 census counted about two-thirds of people aged twelve and over as literate. Then the process was thrown into reverse. Schools and universities closed in the summer of 1966, teachers were struggled against — Bian Zhongyun, deputy head of a Beijing girls' middle school, was beaten to death by pupils on 5 August 1966 — the university entrance examination was abandoned and not restored until 1977, and when universities reopened from about 1970 students were recommended as workers, peasants and soldiers rather than examined. A top-level answer keeps both halves: a real advance in reading, and a lost decade of schooling on top of it."},{"code":"32-4 — 3 Education and health: Reasons for changes in health provision","h":"Barefoot doctors and the patriotic health campaigns","t":"In 1949 there were about 40,000 doctors of Western medicine for some 540 million people, nearly all of them in the cities, and cholera, smallpox, plague and schistosomiasis were endemic. The cheapest gains came first: the patriotic health campaigns from 1952 mobilised whole streets and villages to clean drains, cover latrines, boil water and vaccinate, the Four Pests campaign of 1958 set the population against flies, mosquitoes, rats and sparrows, and smallpox was wiped out by the early 1960s. The famous reform came from Mao's directive of 26 June 1965 attacking the urban bias of the Ministry of Health and ordering medicine into the countryside: peasants chosen by their production brigade were given a few months' training, kept working in the fields for work points, and carried a box of herbs, needles and a few drugs. Made national policy in 1968, the barefoot doctors numbered about 1.5 million by the mid-1970s, and life expectancy rose from roughly thirty-five years around 1949 to about sixty-five. The failures were equally real: the famine of 1959 to 1961, hospitals short of everything, and a health worker with six months' training who could not treat serious disease. A top-level answer weighs the reach of the system against the thinness of it."},{"code":"32-4 — 4 Cultural change: Reasons for attacks on traditional culture","h":"Why the old culture had to go","t":"The Party attacked traditional culture for reasons that were political before they were aesthetic. Marxism treated the old learning as the property of the class that had ruled, and Mao's Yan'an line of May 1942 made art an instrument of the revolution rather than a private pleasure. More practically, the old culture carried the authority the Party wanted for itself: Confucian filial piety put the elder above the young, ancestral halls and clan graves held families together around their dead, and traditional opera told stories of emperors, scholars and ghosts. The assault came to a head with the campaign against the Four Olds — old ideas, old culture, old customs and old habits — launched in August 1966, when Red Guards burned libraries and paintings, smashed temples and graveyards, cut people's hair and clothes in the street and renamed thousands of shops and roads; thousands of historic sites in Beijing alone were wrecked, and the Confucius temple and family graves at Qufu were attacked in November 1966. The campaign to criticise Lin Biao and Confucius in 1973 to 1974 showed the same weapon used inside the leadership. A top-level answer explains destruction as a means to authority, and names what each attack was meant to replace."},{"code":"32-4 — 4 Cultural change: The imposition of revolutionary art and culture","h":"Jiang Qing and the eight model works","t":"Removing a culture is only half a policy; something has to be performed on the empty stage. That task fell to Jiang Qing, Mao's wife and a former Shanghai film actress, who took charge of culture in the 1960s, beginning with a festival of Peking opera on contemporary themes in 1964 and ending with a repertoire she policed personally. By 1967 the eight model works were established: five Peking operas — Taking Tiger Mountain by Strategy, The Red Lantern, Shajiabang, Raid on the White Tiger Regiment and On the Docks — two ballets, The Red Detachment of Women and The White-Haired Girl, and a symphony, Shajiabang. For years these were nearly all that could be staged, filmed or broadcast in a country of more than seven hundred million people, so that children could sing them from beginning to end. Writers and artists who had made anything else were destroyed with their work: the novelist Lao She was beaten by Red Guards in Beijing and was found drowned on 24 August 1966, and Wu Han, whose play Hai Rui Dismissed from Office of 1961 was attacked in November 1965, died in prison in 1969. A top-level answer treats the model works as propaganda with a named author and a political use, not as a curiosity."},{"code":"32-4 — 4 Cultural change: Reasons for attacks on religion","h":"Faith under a state that wanted no rival","t":"Religion was attacked because it was a rival claim on loyalty, a landowner, and in places a foreign connection. Land reform in 1950 to 1952 stripped temples, monasteries and churches of their estates; foreign missionaries were expelled in the early 1950s as agents of imperialism; and each faith was then given a state-run body to belong to — the Three-Self Patriotic Movement for Protestants, with a preparatory committee in 1951 and a founding national conference in 1954, the Buddhist Association of China and the Islamic Association of China in 1953, and the Chinese Catholic Patriotic Association and the Chinese Taoist Association in 1957, the Catholic body appointing bishops without Rome. The 1954 Constitution promised freedom of religious belief but nothing about teaching or preaching it. Ancestor worship lost its halls and its graves to the plough; in Tibet, where Buddhism organised society, the Lhasa uprising of March 1959 was followed by the flight of the Dalai Lama to India and the closure of the monasteries, and Red Guards attacked the Jokhang temple in 1966. By the late 1960s almost every place of worship in China was shut, including the patriotic associations themselves. A top-level answer separates control, which was the policy of the 1950s, from destruction, which was the policy after 1966."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1: two inferences from Source A","setup":"Source A in this room is a report sent to her provincial office by a woman cadre of the Democratic Women's Federation after three months' work in a rural county, November 1953. It says she has visited eleven villages; in seven of them the Marriage Law has been read aloud at a meeting, and in three of those the county team performed a play so that people who cannot read might understand it; that most young women say they have heard of the new law but that 'the law is in the county town and the family is in the house'; that three brides asked for divorce that autumn, that each husband's mother came to the office the same week and wept before the cadres, and that in two cases the village head advised the woman to go home and wait; that elder men complain the bride price is lost if a daughter-in-law may leave; that two women have been beaten for going to the county court and 'the whole village knew of it and said nothing'; and that some village cadres are themselves married to women betrothed as children and are slow to call the meetings. The question is: 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about what the Communist Party was doing to make the Marriage Law known in the countryside. Complete the table below to explain your answer.' (4 marks)","steps":["Fix who is writing and to whom before reading for content: a Party cadre reporting her own three months' work to the office above her wants to look effective, so whatever she admits is worth more than what she boasts of.","Take the first inference from the methods she describes rather than from her opinions: meetings where the law is read aloud, and a travelling play put on 'so that people who cannot read might understand it', tell you the Party had to reach a population that was largely illiterate.","Take the second from the arithmetic she supplies without comment: eleven villages visited, the law read in seven of them and the play performed in only three, so the campaign was patchy and had not yet reached every village.","Take the second inference further with the reason the report supplies but never draws: some of the village cadres are themselves married to women betrothed as children and are slow to call the meetings, so the campaign was being carried into each village by men whose own marriages had been made in the way the new law condemned, which makes the unevenness a matter of who was carrying it out and not only of how far the teams had travelled."],"cues":["Who wrote this report, and for whom?","Why perform a play at all?","What do her own totals admit?","Who was the campaign relying on in each village?"]},{"title":"Question 3(a): judging Sources B and C for the enquiry actually named","setup":"Source B in this room is a talk by a barefoot doctor of a production brigade, printed in a provincial newspaper in 1972: she is twenty-two, her brigade chose her two years ago, she trained for six months at the county hospital, she still works in the fields at busy seasons for work points, she carries needles, a few Western medicines and herbs the brigade gathers, she vaccinates children and attends births, she has had every latrine covered and the snail ditches filled, she sends what she cannot treat to the commune clinic and the county hospital, and she says that before liberation there was no doctor within a day's walk. Source C is an entry of 1972 in the private notebook of a woman who taught Chinese in a city middle school: she says she has kept no record until now because it was not safe to, the school shut in the summer of 1966, pupils put up posters against the teachers, she was made to stand with a board round her neck, the library was burned in the yard, the school reopened two years ago with a shorter course half of which is politics and work in the machine shop, there is nothing on the radio but the eight model works, her daughter was sent to Shanxi in 1969 and was treated there for a fever by a barefoot doctor of her own age, and her mother, never taught a character, learned to read at a night class from the pinyin chart. The question is: 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the lives of women in Mao's China? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your knowledge of the historical context.' (8 marks)","steps":["Copy the enquiry to the top of the page and let it choose the material for you: the lives of women, so the barefoot doctor's job, the teacher's humiliation and the daughter sent to Shanxi are in, and the snail ditches are only in so far as they show what a woman was now doing.","Read Source B for the enquiry: a peasant woman of twenty-two chosen by her brigade, trained and given medical authority over her neighbours, shows the new public roles women were given, and her work points show her earning independently of a husband or father.","Weigh Source B's nature, origin and purpose together: it is a talk printed in a Party-controlled provincial newspaper in 1972 to advertise the scheme, so the woman in it is a model chosen to be admired, and it will not tell you how many women were turned down or how her work was judged at home.","Read Source C for the same enquiry and notice it covers three generations of women at once: a mother who learned to read as an adult at a pinyin night class, a teacher publicly humiliated in 1966, and a daughter sent to the countryside in 1969.","Weigh Source C's provenance the other way: a private notebook that the writer says it was not safe to keep has no one to impress, which makes it candid evidence of what these changes felt like, though it is one woman in one city and she is a teacher, not a peasant.","Judge for the enquiry rather than in general: between them they reach the village and the city, the illiterate grandmother and the sent-down daughter, and both show gains and costs arriving together; neither is any use on how far these experiences were typical, which is what a set of national figures would supply."],"cues":["What exactly is the enquiry into?","What new position does Source B show a woman holding?","Who chose to print that talk, and why?","How many generations does Source C cover?","Why might an unsafe notebook be candid?","What can neither source tell you?"]},{"title":"Question 3(b): finding the difference of view, not of detail","setup":"Interpretation 1 in this room, from a study of village life in Mao's China by a historian writing in the 1980s, argues that for the great majority of Chinese people, who lived in villages, the Communist years brought gains that can be measured: a law of 1950 let a woman refuse a match and leave a husband, foot binding ended, night classes and a new alphabet put reading within reach of families in which nobody had ever held a book, and vaccination, clean wells and a half-trained health worker in every brigade meant children lived who would have died, so that 'the balance sheet in the village was positive'. Interpretation 2, from a general history of China under Mao by a historian writing in the 2000s, argues that the improvements were real but 'cannot be taken apart from the system that delivered them': the state that taught peasants to read decided what there was to read, the eight model works were for years nearly all that could be staged, the household registration of 1958 fixed a peasant in his village and the work unit fixed a worker in his factory, temples and ancestral halls were closed, doubt was treated in labour camps, and after 1966 the schools stood empty, so that what was given was given by a state 'that had first taken everything else away'. The question is: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. They give different views about the changes in life in the Chinese countryside after 1949. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations.' (4 marks)","steps":["Reduce each interpretation to one sentence about the villager: the first says his life got better, the second says his life was taken over.","Test that this is a difference of view and not of fact: both accept that literacy and health improved, so the disagreement is about how those improvements should be counted, not about whether they happened.","Anchor each half in the interpretation's own words, 'the balance sheet in the village was positive' against a state 'that had first taken everything else away'.","Write it as one difference stated in the first sentence and then supported from each text in turn, not as two summaries laid side by side."],"cues":["What does each say about the villager?","Do they disagree about facts or about weighting?","Which phrase carries each view?","What shape should the answer take?"]},{"title":"Question 2: getting a 12-mark 'Explain why' past the bullet points","setup":"The question is: 'Explain why family life changed in China between 1949 and 1976. You may use the following in your answer: • the people's communes • the danwei (work unit). You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks); on the paper Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b) and you answer one of them. The mark scheme caps an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content at a maximum of 8 marks and shuts Level 4 to it altogether; there is no cap at Level 2. The fact files in this room supply the material: the Agrarian Reform Law of 28 June 1950, the cooperatives of the mid-1950s, the roughly 26,000 people's communes of 1958 with their canteens and crèches, the danwei with its housing and ration coupons, the household registration regulations of 9 January 1958, grain rationing from 1953 and grain coupons from 1955, the Marriage Law of 1 May 1950, and the sending of about seventeen million young townspeople to the countryside from 1968.","steps":["Turn the first bullet into a causal claim instead of a heading: the communes of 1958 took cooking, childcare and the care of the old out of the household and into canteens, crèches and homes for the elderly, so the family stopped being the unit that fed and raised its own members.","Make the second bullet do causal work too: in the towns the danwei supplied housing, ration coupons, the clinic, the school place and permission to marry, so the authority a father had once held passed to a work unit that also kept a file on him.","Add the reason of your own that neither bullet prompts: the law and the land. The Agrarian Reform Law of 1950 destroyed the landlord clans that had enforced family custom, and the Marriage Law of 1 May 1950 made marriage an agreement between two individuals rather than an arrangement between two households.","Add the reason that shows the change was not only about power but about movement: the household registration of 1958 tied a family to its village or its city, while from 1968 about seventeen million young townspeople were sent to the countryside, so families were separated by the state as a matter of policy.","Weigh the reasons against each other instead of listing them: the commune canteens were given up in 1961 after the famine and the household kitchen came back, whereas household registration and the danwei governed where a family lived and worked for the whole period, so the changes that went deepest were the ones that fixed a family in place rather than the ones that took over its cooking — close on that, in the question's own words."],"cues":["What work did the household stop doing?","Who gave a town worker his housing?","Which reason do the bullets not prompt?","What did the state do to where people lived?","Which of these changes outlasted the others?"]},{"title":"Question 3(d): evaluating Interpretation 2 against Interpretation 1 and your own knowledge","setup":"The question is: 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the effect of Communist rule on traditional culture and religion in China? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]. Interpretation 2 says the improvements after 1949 cannot be taken apart from the system that delivered them, that the state which taught peasants to read decided what there was to read, that for years the eight model works were nearly all that could be staged, that temples, churches and ancestral halls were closed and that doubt was treated in labour camps. Interpretation 1 says that for the great majority, who lived in villages, the years after 1949 brought measurable gains in marriage law, literacy and health, and that the balance sheet in the village was positive. No stimulus points are printed on this question.","steps":["State the claim you are judging in your own words and name the criterion: whether the Party's treatment of culture and religion is best described as control that came with the gains, or as destruction that has to be judged on its own.","Build the case for Interpretation 2 from dated fact rather than from the interpretation repeated: the Four Olds campaign from August 1966, Red Guards burning libraries and smashing temples, the Qufu attack of November 1966, Lao She found drowned on 24 August 1966, and Jiang Qing's eight model works filling the stage from 1967.","Extend it to religion, where the same pattern appears earlier: temple and church lands taken in the land reform of 1950 to 1952, missionaries expelled, and each faith put inside a state body — the Three-Self Patriotic Movement founded at a national conference in 1954, the Buddhist and Islamic Associations of 1953, the Catholic and Taoist Associations of 1957.","Use Interpretation 1 to test the claim rather than to contradict it flatly: the same state that closed the ancestral hall also ended foot binding and the bride price, and the customs it destroyed included ones that had oppressed the people Interpretation 1 is writing about.","Show how each interpretation conveys its view, because the top level asks for exactly that: Interpretation 1 uses the language of accounting, a balance sheet that can be totalled, while Interpretation 2 uses the language of confiscation, a gift from a state that had taken everything else.","Judge on the criterion you named and say why the losing view is not simply wrong: control describes the 1950s, when faiths were licensed rather than abolished, but destruction describes what followed 1966, so the answer has to date its verdict rather than give one verdict for twenty-seven years."],"cues":["What is Interpretation 2 actually claiming?","Which events of 1966 support it?","What happened to religion in the 1950s?","What can Interpretation 1 fairly say back?","How does each writer's language carry the view?","What must your final paragraph settle?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What exactly did the Marriage Law of 1 May 1950 forbid, and what minimum ages did it set?","a":"It forbade arranged and child marriage, concubinage, the bride price and interference with a widow's remarriage; marriage became a free agreement between the two people marrying, with a minimum age of twenty for men and eighteen for women and an equal right of divorce for a wife."},{"q":"Why is a source printed in a Party-controlled newspaper still useful for an enquiry into health care in the countryside?","a":"Because its purpose is evidence in itself: it shows what the state wanted people to admire and how the barefoot doctor scheme was meant to work. It must be qualified rather than dismissed, since it will not print the scheme's failures."},{"q":"Name the directive that turned Chinese medical policy towards the villages, and give its date.","a":"Mao's directive of 26 June 1965, which attacked the urban bias of the Ministry of Health and ordered medical work to be shifted to the countryside; the barefoot doctor scheme became national policy in 1968."},{"q":"In what sense did the household registration regulations of 9 January 1958 change family life?","a":"They classified every household as rural or urban and made moving to a city almost impossible for a peasant, so a family's place of residence, its rations and its children's prospects were fixed by the state rather than chosen."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 3 Section A (16 marks): Question 1 prints Source A and asks 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about...' in a table of two boxes each (4 marks); Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b), and you answer one of them: each is 'Explain why...' with two stimulus points and 'You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks).","Paper 3 Section B (36 marks), all on Question 3: (a) 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into...?' (8); (b) 'What is the main difference between these views?' (4); (c) 'Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about...' (4); (d) 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about...?' (16, plus up to 4 for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology).","The stimulus-point caps on the 12-mark 'Explain why...': 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'. There is no cap at Level 2. Question 3(d) prints no stimulus points, so no cap is printed on it.","Timing on Paper 3: 1 hour 30 minutes for 52 marks, so about a minute a mark plus thinking time. Roughly 5 minutes on Question 1, 18 on Question 2, 12 on 3(a), 6 each on 3(b) and 3(c), and about 40 minutes on 3(d) including the final check for the SPaG marks.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose, and usefulness is always judged for the enquiry the question names, never in general. Contextual knowledge earns nothing unless it is used on the sources or the interpretations.","Five dates that anchor this key topic: 1 May 1950, the Marriage Law; 9 January 1958, the household registration regulations; 11 February 1958, the pinyin scheme; 26 June 1965, Mao's directive on rural health care; August 1966, the campaign against the Four Olds."],"questions":[{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about the position of women in village families in the early 1950s. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for each valid inference, up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail from Source A. Credit the husband's family still holding authority, women risking violence, or officials siding with the family.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: a married woman's life was still controlled by her husband's family rather than by herself. Details in the source that tell me this: when brides asked for divorce 'the husband's mother came to the office the same week and wept before the cadres', and in two cases 'the village head advised the woman to go home and wait'. (ii) What I can infer: it was dangerous for a village woman to use the rights the new law gave her. Details in the source that tell me this: two women of the county 'have been beaten for going to the county court', and 'the whole village knew of it and said nothing'.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["An inference is something the report makes certain without stating it; ask what has to be true for these women's cases to end as they do.","Read the report twice: once for what happens to a woman who asks for a divorce, and once for what happens to her afterwards in the village.","Give each answer in two parts, a claim the source never states and then the exact words that force it."],"codes":["32-4 — 2 Family life and the role of women: Reasons for changes in the status of women","32-4 — 2 Family life and the role of women: Changes in family life"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about how village people responded to the Marriage Law. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for each valid inference, up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail from Source A. Credit resistance for financial reasons, knowledge of the law without trust in it, or unreliable local cadres.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: many village people resisted the law because it threatened them financially. Details in the source that tell me this: the cadre reports that 'Elder men complain that if a daughter-in-law may leave, the bride price they have paid is lost'. (ii) What I can infer: people had heard of the law but did not believe it would reach into their own homes. Details in the source that tell me this: most young women say they have heard of the new law, but answer that 'the law is in the county town and the family is in the house'.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask who in a village stood to lose money if the law were obeyed, and look for their complaint in the report.","There is a sentence in which women repeat a saying of their own; a saying like that tells you what people expected to happen.","Set out a claim the source implies, then the phrase that makes it certain, for each of your two answers."],"codes":["32-4 — 2 Family life and the role of women: Reasons for changes in the status of women","32-4 — 1 Communist control: Propaganda and censorship"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. They give different views about life in China under Communist rule between 1949 and 1976. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4) needs a key difference of view supported from both: Interpretation 1 judges the period by the gains ordinary people received, Interpretation 2 by the control that came with them. Surface detail only stays in Level 1.","sol":"The main difference is over how the changes of these years should be judged. Interpretation 1 measures them by what ordinary villagers received and finds real improvement: a law of 1950 let a woman 'refuse a match and to leave a husband', and reading reached families that had never held a book, so that 'the balance sheet in the village was positive'. Interpretation 2 measures the same changes against their price and will not separate them from the system that produced them: the state 'decided what there was to read', the household registration of 1958 'fixed a peasant in his village', and what was given came from a state 'that had first taken everything else away'.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Reduce each interpretation to one sentence beginning 'Life under Mao was...' and compare the two sentences.","Check that your difference is one of view: both writers accept that health and literacy improved, so look for what each does with that fact.","State the difference first in one sentence, then support each half with words taken from its own interpretation."],"codes":["32-4 — 3 Education and health: Reasons for changes in education","32-4 — 1 Communist control: Thought control and 'Reform Through Labour'"]},{"q":"Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about life in China under Communist rule between 1949 and 1976. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4) needs one explained reason, effectively substantiated: for example that the two historians study different subjects and so have different evidence in front of them.","sol":"One reason is that the two historians set out to study different things, so different evidence is in front of them. Interpretation 1 is a study of village life, where change shows up as vaccination, night classes and the end of foot binding: the evidence of Source B, where a brigade's barefoot doctor describes covering the latrines and vaccinating the children in a valley that had no doctor before liberation. Interpretation 2 is a general history of the whole country, so it must also account for the household registration of 1958, the labour camps and the years when schools stood empty, of the kind recorded in the teacher's notebook in Source C. Each is right about what it looked at.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Historians differ legitimately when they ask different questions; look at what kind of book each interpretation is taken from.","Ask which evidence each writer would have been reading, and which of the two sources in this room matches it.","Name one reason, then show it at work in both interpretations, using a source to illustrate the kind of evidence involved."],"codes":["32-4 — 3 Education and health: Reasons for changes in health provision","32-4 — 2 Family life and the role of women: Changes in family life"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into health care in China in the 1960s and 1970s? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): utility judged for this enquiry, reasoning from nature, origin and purpose, with context used to interpret. Credit Source B on training, work points and preventive work, Source C on the reach of the scheme to a sent-down girl.","sol":"Source B is the closer of the two to this enquiry, and useful for how the scheme worked. The doctor was chosen by her own production brigade, trained for six months at the county hospital, still works in the fields for work points, and carries needles, a few Western medicines and herbs; most of her work is prevention, boiling water, covering latrines and filling the ditches where the snails live, and what she cannot treat goes on to the county hospital. That fits the record: Mao's directive of 26 June 1965 attacked the urban bias of the Ministry of Health, the scheme became national policy in 1968, and by the mid-1970s there were about 1.5 million barefoot doctors. Its provenance cuts both ways. A talk printed in a Party-controlled provincial newspaper in 1972 exists to advertise, so this woman is a model rather than an average, and nothing is said of hospitals short of equipment or of the famine of 1959 to 1961. That purpose is itself evidence of state priorities. Source C is less direct but harder to dismiss: a private notebook that its writer says it was not safe to keep records that when her daughter, sent to Shanxi in 1969, had a fever, a barefoot doctor of her own age with six months' training came to her. A hostile witness confirming the scheme's reach into a remote province is strong evidence. Neither source can show how effective the treatment was, which is what the enquiry finally needs.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Usefulness is always usefulness for something: copy the enquiry out before you look at either source.","Take each source as content, then nature, origin and purpose, then the context that confirms or qualifies it, and compare only at the end.","Open with a judgement on which source is closer to the enquiry and why, not with a description of what it says."],"codes":["32-4 — 3 Education and health: Reasons for changes in health provision","32-4 — 1 Communist control: Propaganda and censorship"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the effects of the Cultural Revolution on education in China? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): criteria-based judgements with developed reasoning on how provenance affects content. Credit Source C on the closure of 1966, the struggle session, the burned library and the shortened course; Source B is largely silent, which is itself a finding.","sol":"For this enquiry Source C is by far the more useful. It is written by someone the Cultural Revolution was done to: her school shut in the summer of 1966, pupils put up posters against the teachers, she was made to stand with a board round her neck, the library was carried into the yard and burned, and when the school reopened the course was shorter and half of it politics and work in the machine shop, with no textbooks but the ones the district prints. Every one of those details matches the record, since schools and universities closed in 1966, the university entrance examination was abandoned and not restored until 1977, and the novelist Lao She was among those beaten in Beijing that August. Its provenance makes it stronger rather than weaker: a private notebook that the writer says it was not safe to keep has nobody to satisfy, so the fear in it is evidence. Its limits are that it is one teacher in one city, and that she plainly hates what was done to her. Source B is useful chiefly by what it does not say. A newspaper item of 1972 about a brigade's health worker mentions her six months of training and the children reading the pinyin on her bottles, but nothing about middle or higher education, because the Party did not wish that discussed. Read against Source C, its silence supports the same conclusion; on its own it would mislead.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Decide first which source was written by somebody the enquiry is about, and start there.","A source that says almost nothing about the enquiry can still be used, if you can explain why it is silent.","Check each detail of the teacher's account against a date you know, and use the match as part of your judgement."],"codes":["32-4 — 3 Education and health: Reasons for changes in education","32-4 — 4 Cultural change: Reasons for attacks on traditional culture"]},{"q":"2(a) Explain why the status of women in China changed after 1949. You may use the following in your answer: • the Marriage Law (1950) • the people's communes. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise knowledge — the Marriage Law of 1 May 1950, the 1954 Constitution, land reform, the communes' canteens and crèches, the Women's Federation, paid work. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Women's position changed because the Party attacked the old family by law, then removed the household in which that family had its power, and finally needed women as workers. The first reason is the law. The Marriage Law of 1 May 1950 made marriage a free agreement between the two people marrying and outlawed arranged and child marriage, concubinage, the bride price and interference with a widow's remarriage; it set a minimum age of twenty for men and eighteen for women and gave a wife the same right to divorce as her husband, and the 1954 Constitution then declared women equal with men in political, economic, cultural, social and family life. Divorce petitions ran into hundreds of thousands a year in the early 1950s, most of them brought by women, so this was not paper alone. The second reason is economic. Land reform between 1950 and 1952 broke the landlord families and clans that had enforced the old customs, and from 1958 the people's communes moved cooking, childcare and the care of the old into canteens, crèches and homes for the elderly, so that women went into the fields and earned work points in their own names. A woman with an income of her own is harder to command. The third reason, which neither bullet prompts, is that the Party needed the change for its own purposes. The All-China Democratic Women's Federation, founded in 1949, carried the new law into the villages with meetings and travelling plays; 'women hold up half the sky' was a recruiting slogan as well as a principle; and the drive for output put women into factories in numbers never seen before. Foot binding, outlawed by the Republic in 1912 but still practised in the countryside, was finally stamped out. Law, economics and the Party's own needs pushed the same way, which is why the change was so fast — and why it stopped where the Party's interest stopped, since women's work often earned fewer points than men's and almost no woman but Jiang Qing reached the top of the Party.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["A change of status is not the same as a change of law: ask what else had to move before the law could bite.","Plan three reasons of different kinds — legal, economic and political — so that the answer analyses instead of listing.","Open on what the Marriage Law actually forbade, and finish the paragraph by saying what that took away from the husband's family."],"codes":["32-4 — 2 Family life and the role of women: Reasons for changes in the status of women","32-4 — 2 Family life and the role of women: Changes in family life"]},{"q":"2(b) Explain why literacy increased in China under Mao. You may use the following in your answer: • pinyin • the mass literacy campaigns. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): analysis directed at causation — simplified characters 1956, pinyin 11 February 1958, winter and spare-time schools, the growth of primary schooling, the Party's political motive. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Literacy rose because the Party made the written language easier, built the classes to teach it, and had strong political reasons for wanting a population that could read. The first reason is the reform of the script itself. About four in five adults could not read in 1949, partly because Chinese characters had to be memorised one by one; the Chinese Character Simplification Scheme of 1956 cut the number of strokes in common characters, and the Hanyu pinyin scheme approved by the National People's Congress on 11 February 1958 spelt Chinese in the Latin alphabet, so that a teacher could give a learner the sound of a character and a learner could look one up. Learning to read became a task of months rather than years. The second reason is the machinery. Winter schools ran in the slack farming season, factories and communes held spare-time classes, the army taught its conscripts, and the primary system was expanded until most children were in school, which mattered more than any adult campaign because it stopped illiteracy being renewed in each generation. Attendance was organised by the danwei and the production brigade, so it could be required rather than hoped for. The third reason, which the bullets do not prompt, is that the Party needed readers. A peasant who could read could read the People's Daily, the wall posters and, after 1964, Quotations from Chairman Mao, of which more than 700 million copies had been printed by the end of 1967; literacy was a channel for propaganda as much as a gift. That is also why the gain was uneven. When the Cultural Revolution closed the schools in the summer of 1966 and the university entrance examination was abandoned until 1977, a decade of schooling was lost, and yet the 1982 census still counted about two-thirds of people aged twelve and over as literate. The script made learning possible, the state made it compulsory, and politics made it worth doing.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what made reading Chinese hard in 1949, and then what the Party did to each of those difficulties.","Separate the tool, the institution and the motive, and give one paragraph to each.","Begin with the written language itself and what the reforms of 1956 and 1958 changed about learning it."],"codes":["32-4 — 3 Education and health: Reasons for changes in education","32-4 — 1 Communist control: Propaganda and censorship"]},{"q":"2(a) Explain why the Chinese Communist Party attacked traditional culture. You may use the following in your answer: • the 'Four Olds' • Confucianism. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): sustained causal analysis — the Yan'an line of 1942, Confucian authority and the clan, the Four Olds from August 1966, the political use of the campaign. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The Party attacked traditional culture because that culture carried an authority it wanted for itself, because Marxism told it that the old learning belonged to the old ruling class, and because the attack turned out to be a useful political weapon. The first reason is ideological. Mao's Talks at the Yan'an Forum on Literature and Art of May 1942 laid down that art and writing exist to serve the workers, peasants and soldiers and must be subordinate to politics, so a culture of emperors, scholars and ghosts was not neutral but hostile. The second reason is that traditional culture organised the very relationships the Party meant to replace. Confucian teaching put the son below the father, the young below the old and the wife below the husband; ancestral halls and clan graves held families together around their dead and gave village elders a standing the Party could not control; traditional opera and the classics taught the same hierarchy in a form even the illiterate knew by heart. Destroying the hall and the grave destroyed a rival government of the village. The third reason, which neither bullet prompts, is the use Mao made of the attack after 1966. The campaign against the Four Olds — old ideas, old culture, old customs and old habits — launched in August 1966 sent Red Guards to burn libraries and paintings, smash temples and graveyards, cut hair and clothes in the street and rename shops and roads; thousands of sites in Beijing alone were wrecked, and the Confucius temple and family graves at Qufu were attacked in November 1966. That destruction mobilised the young against officials Mao distrusted, and the campaign to criticise Lin Biao and Confucius in 1973 to 1974 used the same weapon inside the leadership itself. Ideology explains why the old culture was condemned, its social power explains why condemnation was not enough, and factional politics explains why the attack came when it did and went as far as it did.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the old culture actually did for the people who kept it, and who lost authority when it went.","Three different kinds of reason are available: what the Party believed, what the culture organised, and what Mao could use it for.","Start from the Yan'an line of 1942 and what it said art was for."],"codes":["32-4 — 4 Cultural change: Reasons for attacks on traditional culture","32-4 — 4 Cultural change: The imposition of revolutionary art and culture"]},{"q":"2(b) Explain why the Chinese Communist Party attacked religion. You may use the following in your answer: • foreign missionaries • the 'patriotic' religious associations. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise detail — Marxism, temple and church land in the reform of 1950-52, missionaries expelled, the associations of 1951-57, Tibet after March 1959, the Four Olds. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Religion was attacked because it claimed a loyalty the Party wanted undivided, because it owned property and organised communities, and because some of it was tied to powers outside China. The first reason is doctrine and property together. Marxism held religion to be superstition that kept the poor obedient, and in China temples, monasteries and churches were also landlords, so the Agrarian Reform Law of 28 June 1950 and the land reform that followed to 1952 stripped them of their estates and, with them, of their independence. The second reason is the foreign connection. Protestant and Catholic missions had come with the treaty ports, and in a state that had just fought the United Nations forces in Korea they were treated as agents of imperialism: missionaries were expelled in the early 1950s, and the Catholic Church's obedience to Rome was answered by the Chinese Catholic Patriotic Association of 1957, which appointed its own bishops. The third reason, which the bullets only half prompt, is that the Party preferred control to abolition for as long as that worked. Each faith was given a state body to belong to: a preparatory committee in 1951 and a founding national conference in 1954 for the Protestant Three-Self Patriotic Movement, the Buddhist Association of China and the Islamic Association of China in 1953, the Taoist Association in 1957. The 1954 Constitution promised freedom of religious belief but said nothing of preaching or teaching it. Where a faith organised a whole society the Party moved harder: after the Lhasa uprising of March 1959 and the Dalai Lama's flight to India the Tibetan monasteries were closed, and Red Guards attacked the Jokhang temple in 1966. Finally, the campaign against the Four Olds from August 1966 abandoned control for destruction, and by the late 1960s almost every place of worship in China was shut, including the patriotic associations themselves. Belief was a rival authority; the Party licensed it while it could and smashed it when it would not be licensed.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what a temple or a church owned and organised, not only what it taught.","The policy changed over time: licensing in the 1950s, destruction after 1966. Build that change into the argument.","Open on why a Marxist state treats religion as a political problem, and then name what the land reform took from it."],"codes":["32-4 — 4 Cultural change: Reasons for attacks on religion","32-4 — 4 Cultural change: Reasons for attacks on traditional culture"]},{"q":"2(a) Explain why the cult of Mao had become so powerful in China by the mid-1960s. You may use the following in your answer: • the Little Red Book • Lin Biao. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): analysis directed at causation — the Party's monopoly of information, Lin Biao and the PLA, Quotations from Chairman Mao 1964, Mao's own political need after 1959. On the paper this is printed as one of the pair 2(a) and 2(b), of which a candidate answers one. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The cult grew because the Party controlled every channel through which a Chinese person learned anything, because one ambitious minister made Mao's words into a daily discipline, and because Mao himself needed a following outside the Party machine. The first reason is the machinery of propaganda. Every newspaper, radio station, publishing house, cinema and school belonged to the state; loudspeakers hung in villages and work units, wall posters went up in every street, and model citizens were supplied for imitation, most famously the soldier Lei Feng, whose diary was held up for the nation from March 1963. In that system there was no second opinion to compete with. The second reason is Lin Biao and the army. As Minister of Defence he made the study of Mao Zedong Thought the daily business of the People's Liberation Army, and in 1964 had Quotations from Chairman Mao printed for soldiers to carry; the campaign to learn from the PLA then pushed the same practice into factories, schools and communes, and more than 700 million copies of the Little Red Book had been printed by the end of 1967. Words learned by heart are harder to argue with than arguments. The third reason, which the bullets do not prompt, is Mao's own position. After the failure of the Great Leap Forward he gave up the state chairmanship in 1959 to Liu Shaoqi and found the Party's own organisation increasingly in other hands, so his personal standing with the young and the army was the one resource nobody else controlled. He used it: when he reviewed over a million Red Guards in Tiananmen Square on 18 August 1966, the cult stopped being reverence and became a mobilised force. A monopoly of information made the cult possible, Lin Biao made it a routine, and Mao's need for a weapon inside his own Party made it enormous.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["A cult needs a channel, a routine and a purpose; find one of each before you write.","Ask what Mao had lost politically after 1959, and what he still possessed that the Party machine did not.","Begin with who owned the newspapers, the radio and the schools, and what that made impossible."],"codes":["32-4 — 1 Communist control: Propaganda and censorship","32-4 — 1 Communist control: Thought control and 'Reform Through Labour'"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the effects of Communist rule on the lives of ordinary Chinese people between 1949 and 1976? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views evaluated, how each is conveyed analysed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit the hukou, the danwei, laogai and the closed schools; against, the Marriage Law, pinyin and the barefoot doctors.","sol":"Interpretation 2 accepts that the improvements after 1949 were real but argues that they 'cannot be taken apart from the system that delivered them': the state that taught peasants to read decided what there was to read, the household registration of 1958 fixed a peasant in his village, temples and ancestral halls were closed, doubt was treated in labour camps and after 1966 the schools stood empty. The test is whether the gains can be counted on their own, or whether gain and coercion were parts of one thing. A great deal supports the interpretation. The regulations of 9 January 1958 divided the population into rural and urban and made moving to a city almost impossible for a peasant, so the villager who now had a school and a clinic had no way out; in the towns the danwei allotted housing, ration coupons and permission to marry, and kept a file on every worker. Thought reform ran from 1951 through study groups, confessions and struggle meetings; regulations of September 1954 put reform through labour on a legal footing, and from 1957 education through labour allowed detention without trial, used freely on the roughly half a million people labelled rightists. Culture narrowed after the attack on the Four Olds from August 1966: libraries were burned, the novelist Lao She was found drowned on 24 August 1966, and for years Jiang Qing's eight model works were nearly all that could be staged. Schools and universities closed in the summer of 1966, the entrance examination was not restored until 1977, and about seventeen million young townspeople were sent down to the countryside from 1968. Interpretation 1 is nevertheless right that the gains were large and reached those who had least. The Marriage Law of 1 May 1950 ended arranged and child marriage, concubinage and the bride price and gave a wife an equal right to divorce; foot binding disappeared. Simplified characters in 1956 and the pinyin scheme of 11 February 1958 made a written language teachable in a winter night class. In health the change was greatest of all: about 40,000 doctors of Western medicine served some 540 million people in 1949, whereas after Mao's directive of 26 June 1965 there were roughly 1.5 million barefoot doctors by the mid-1970s; with the patriotic health campaigns from 1952 and mass vaccination, life expectancy rose from about thirty-five years to about sixty-five. The two convey their views by what they invite the reader to do: Interpretation 1 offers a 'balance sheet', implying that items can be added and subtracted, while Interpretation 2 says the state 'had first taken everything else away', denying that they are separable at all. I agree that gains and control had one author and arrived together: the barefoot doctor and the labour camp were both products of a state that reached into every village. But the implied conclusion goes too far. A child who lived instead of dying was not compensating anybody for a camp, and a grandmother who learned to read had gained something of her own. Interpretation 2 is the better guide to the cost and Interpretation 1 to the benefit: ordinary people in 1976 were healthier, more literate and freer of the old family than their parents, and less free of the state than any generation before them.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first what Interpretation 2 is actually claiming, and name the test you will judge the claim by.","Use Interpretation 1 as the case on the other side, and support both sides with dated detail rather than with the interpretations restated.","Open by putting Interpretation 2's claim into your own words and stating the criterion your judgement will use."],"codes":["32-4 — 1 Communist control: Thought control and 'Reform Through Labour'","32-4 — 2 Family life and the role of women: Changes in family life","32-4 — 3 Education and health: Reasons for changes in health provision"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the changes in the lives of Chinese women under Mao? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views weighed, how they are conveyed analysed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit the commune and the work team replacing the old family; against, the Marriage Law, foot binding, literacy and paid work.","sol":"Interpretation 2 argues that the gains made by women cannot be separated from the state that made them, and sums the change up by saying that women left the old family only for the work team. Interpretation 1 counts the same period as a plain improvement: a law of 1950 let a woman refuse a match and leave a husband, foot binding ended, and night classes brought reading to households where nobody had held a book. The criterion I shall use is whether a woman in 1976 had more control over her own life than her mother, or simply a different master. Interpretation 2 has strong support. The communes of 1958 took cooking and childcare out of the household, but into canteens and crèches run by the brigade, and a woman's labour was then directed by a work team that often paid her fewer work points than a man. The household registration of 9 January 1958 tied her to her village as it tied her husband, and the Marriage Law itself was enforced or ignored by local cadres who were frequently traditional men; nearly three years after it was passed the central government still had to make March 1953 a national month for carrying the law into every household, and a woman who wanted a divorce had to seek it from a court or a cadre in the county town rather than in her own village. Politically the ceiling was low: apart from Jiang Qing, who owed her position to her marriage, almost no woman reached the top of the Party. Interpretation 1 is on firm ground too, and about things that were not small. The Marriage Law of 1 May 1950 made marriage a free agreement between the two people marrying, banned arranged and child marriage, concubinage, the bride price and interference with a widow's remarriage, set minimum ages of twenty and eighteen and gave a wife the same right to divorce as a husband; petitions ran into hundreds of thousands a year in the early 1950s, most of them brought by women, and the 1954 Constitution declared women equal in political, economic, cultural, social and family life. Foot binding, outlawed in 1912 but alive in the villages, was finished. Paid work under the slogan that women hold up half the sky gave millions an income of their own, and literacy classes using the pinyin scheme of 11 February 1958 reached women who had never been taught a character, as the teacher's mother in Source C was. The two interpretations convey their views by their choice of comparison: Interpretation 1 compares a woman with her own mother and finds progress, while Interpretation 2 compares her with the freedom she was promised and finds a transfer of control. Applying my criterion, I agree with Interpretation 2 in part and no further. It is right that the state replaced the clan as the body that decided where a woman lived, worked and was allowed to marry, and right that a law is only as strong as the cadre who enforces it. But a woman who could divorce, read, earn and walk on unbound feet held powers her mother had never had, and she held them as rights and not as favours. The change was real, incomplete, and bought inside a system that was itself unfree: Interpretation 1 describes what was gained and Interpretation 2 the terms on which it was held.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Name the comparison you are making before you argue: a woman against her mother, or a woman against what she was promised?","Test whether the law worked as written by asking who enforced it in the village, and say what your answer does to each interpretation.","Begin by stating Interpretation 2's claim about women in your own words and the criterion you will judge it by."],"codes":["32-4 — 2 Family life and the role of women: Reasons for changes in the status of women","32-4 — 2 Family life and the role of women: Changes in family life","32-4 — 4 Cultural change: The imposition of revolutionary art and culture"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the changes in education and health care in Mao's China? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views evaluated, how each is conveyed analysed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit the closed schools, the controlled curriculum and the thinness of training; against, pinyin, primary schooling, the patriotic health campaigns and the barefoot doctors.","sol":"Interpretation 2 holds that the state which taught peasants to read also decided what there was to read, and that after 1966 the schools stood empty; Interpretation 1 answers that night classes, a new alphabet and a half-trained health worker in every brigade changed lives that had never been reached by either school or doctor. The criterion for judging between them is whether the provision should be measured by how far it reached or by what it was allowed to contain. There is a great deal in Interpretation 2. Schools and universities closed in the summer of 1966; teachers were struggled against, and Bian Zhongyun, deputy head of a Beijing girls' middle school, was beaten to death by pupils on 5 August 1966; the university entrance examination was abandoned and not restored until 1977, and when universities reopened from about 1970 students were recommended as workers, peasants and soldiers rather than examined. When middle schools restarted, the course was shorter and heavy with politics and productive labour, as the teacher's notebook in Source C describes, with textbooks printed by the district and the old library burned. Health care was thin as well as wide: a barefoot doctor had perhaps six months' training, as Source B's own account says, and could not treat serious disease, while hospitals were short of equipment and the famine of 1959 to 1961 killed on a scale no clinic could touch. Interpretation 1 is describing something equally real. About four in five adults could not read in 1949; the Chinese Character Simplification Scheme of 1956 and the pinyin scheme approved on 11 February 1958 made the language teachable in a winter night class, and the 1982 census still counted about two-thirds of people aged twelve and over as literate despite the lost decade. In health the arithmetic is decisive: some 40,000 doctors of Western medicine served about 540 million people in 1949, whereas after Mao's directive of 26 June 1965 turned policy towards the villages there were roughly 1.5 million barefoot doctors by the mid-1970s, paid in work points and doing prevention as much as treatment. With the patriotic health campaigns from 1952, the Four Pests campaign of 1958 and mass vaccination, smallpox was gone by the early 1960s and life expectancy rose from about thirty-five years to about sixty-five. Each writer conveys the view through the yardstick chosen: Interpretation 1 counts people reached, which makes any provision look like a gain, while Interpretation 2 counts what was withheld, which makes the same provision look like rationing. My judgement is that Interpretation 2 is more nearly right about education and less right about health. In education the interruption was not an accident of the system but its own doing, and a generation lost its schooling to a political campaign, so what the state gave it could and did take back. In health the gains outran the politics: covered latrines, boiled water, vaccination and a health worker who lived in the village did not depend on what anybody was allowed to think, and the fall in child deaths was real whoever paid for it. The two interpretations therefore divide by field rather than by fact, and a judgement that does not date and separate them is not a judgement at all.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask whether education and health should be judged together at all before you agree or disagree.","Test the interpretation field by field, and let the evidence decide whether your verdict is the same for both.","Open by stating what Interpretation 2 claims about schooling and the curriculum, and name the yardstick you will use."],"codes":["32-4 — 3 Education and health: Reasons for changes in education","32-4 — 3 Education and health: Reasons for changes in health provision","32-4 — 1 Communist control: Propaganda and censorship"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date was the Marriage Law of the People's Republic of China issued?","a":"1 May 1950."},{"q":"Which scheme, approved by the National People's Congress on 11 February 1958, wrote Chinese in the Latin alphabet to help teach reading?","a":"The Hanyu pinyin scheme."},{"q":"Who took charge of revolutionary culture in China and oversaw the eight model works?","a":"Jiang Qing, Mao's wife and a former Shanghai film actress."},{"q":"What were the 'Four Olds' attacked from August 1966?","a":"Old ideas, old culture, old customs and old habits."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The Marriage Law of 1 May 1950","open":false,"code":"32-4 — 2 Family life and the role of women: Reasons for changes in the status of women","back":"Marriage became a free agreement between the two people marrying. Arranged and child marriage, concubinage and the bride price were banned; minimum ages twenty and eighteen; equal right of divorce."},{"front":"Foot binding under Mao","open":false,"code":"32-4 — 2 Family life and the role of women: Reasons for changes in the status of women","back":"Outlawed by the new Republic in 1912 but still practised in the countryside. The Communists enforced the ban after 1949 until the custom disappeared."},{"front":"The All-China Democratic Women's Federation","open":false,"code":"32-4 — 2 Family life and the role of women: Reasons for changes in the status of women","back":"Founded in 1949 and renamed in 1957. It carried the Marriage Law into the villages with meetings and travelling plays and recruited women into paid work."},{"front":"The people's communes of 1958","open":false,"code":"32-4 — 2 Family life and the role of women: Changes in family life","back":"About 26,000 communes held almost every peasant household by the end of 1958. Canteens, crèches and homes for the elderly took over work the household had done, freeing women for the fields."},{"front":"The danwei (work unit)","open":false,"code":"32-4 — 2 Family life and the role of women: Changes in family life","back":"The urban work unit supplied housing, ration coupons, a clinic and a school place, granted permission to marry and kept a personal file on each member: the 'iron rice bowl' and a chain together."},{"front":"The hukou regulations of 9 January 1958","open":false,"code":"32-4 — 2 Family life and the role of women: Changes in family life","back":"Household registration classified everyone as rural or urban and made it almost impossible for a peasant to move to a city, fixing families in place for a generation."},{"front":"The Hanyu pinyin scheme","open":false,"code":"32-4 — 3 Education and health: Reasons for changes in education","back":"Approved by the National People's Congress on 11 February 1958. It spells Chinese in the Latin alphabet, so a teacher can give a learner the sound of a character. Simplified characters had followed in 1956."},{"front":"Education after 1966","open":false,"code":"32-4 — 3 Education and health: Reasons for changes in education","back":"Schools and universities closed in the summer of 1966. The university entrance examination was abandoned and not restored until 1977; reopened universities took recommended worker, peasant and soldier students."},{"front":"The barefoot doctors","open":false,"code":"32-4 — 3 Education and health: Reasons for changes in health provision","back":"Peasants chosen by their brigade, trained for a few months and paid in work points. National policy from 1968 after Mao's directive of 26 June 1965; about 1.5 million by the mid-1970s."},{"front":"The patriotic health campaigns","open":false,"code":"32-4 — 3 Education and health: Reasons for changes in health provision","back":"Mass hygiene drives from 1952: clean drains, covered latrines, boiled water and vaccination. The Four Pests campaign of 1958 targeted flies, mosquitoes, rats and sparrows. Smallpox was gone by the early 1960s."},{"front":"The 'Four Olds'","open":false,"code":"32-4 — 4 Cultural change: Reasons for attacks on traditional culture","back":"Old ideas, old culture, old customs and old habits, attacked from August 1966. Red Guards burned libraries and paintings, smashed temples and graves and renamed streets and shops."},{"front":"The eight model works","open":false,"code":"32-4 — 4 Cultural change: The imposition of revolutionary art and culture","back":"Jiang Qing's approved repertoire by 1967: five Peking operas including Taking Tiger Mountain by Strategy and The Red Lantern, the ballets The Red Detachment of Women and The White-Haired Girl, and a symphony."},{"front":"Lao She","open":false,"code":"32-4 — 4 Cultural change: The imposition of revolutionary art and culture","back":"Novelist and playwright, beaten by Red Guards in Beijing in August 1966 and found drowned in Taiping Lake on 24 August 1966: the best-known of the writers destroyed with their work."},{"front":"The 'patriotic' religious associations","open":false,"code":"32-4 — 4 Cultural change: Reasons for attacks on religion","back":"State bodies that each faith had to join: the Buddhist and Islamic Associations (1953), the Protestant Three-Self Patriotic Movement (national conference 1954) and the Catholic and Taoist Associations (1957)."},{"front":"Reform through labour (laogai)","open":false,"code":"32-4 — 1 Communist control: Thought control and 'Reform Through Labour'","back":"Put on a legal footing by regulations of September 1954; camps and state farms in Qinghai, Xinjiang and the north-east. From 1957 'education through labour' allowed detention without any trial."}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The sources and interpretations written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Source A: From a report sent to her provincial office by a woman cadre of the Democratic Women's Federation, after three months' work in a rural county, November 1953. Written for this room in the style of such a report; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — I have now visited eleven villages of this county. In seven of them the Marriage Law has been read aloud at a meeting, and in three of those the county team performed a play, so that people who cannot read might understand it. Yet when I ask the young women whether they know that they may refuse a match, most say they have heard of the new law, and then say that the law is in the county town and the family is in the house. Three brides came to us this autumn asking for divorce. In each case the husband's mother came to the office the same week and wept before the cadres, and in two cases the village head advised the woman to go home and wait. Elder men complain that if a daughter-in-law may leave, the bride price they have paid is lost. Two women of this county have been beaten for going to the county court, and the whole village knew of it and said nothing. I must report also that some of our own village cadres are themselves married to women who were betrothed as children, and they are slow to call the meetings.","Source B: From a talk given by the barefoot doctor of a production brigade in a southern province, printed in a provincial newspaper in 1972. Written for this room in the style of such a newspaper item; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — I am twenty-two years old, and my brigade chose me two years ago to be its barefoot doctor. I was trained for six months at the county hospital, and I still work in the fields at the busy seasons and earn work points like everybody else. In my box I carry needles, a few Western medicines and the herbs we gather ourselves. I vaccinate the children, I bandage the cuts from the threshing machine, and I go to the houses when a mother is in labour. Most of my work is not medicine at all: I tell the families to boil their drinking water, I have had every latrine in the brigade covered, and we have filled in the ditches where the snails live. What I cannot treat I send by cart to the commune clinic, and from there to the county hospital. Before liberation there was no doctor within a day's walk of this valley, and every old family here can name a child it lost. Our brigade school teaches the children to read, and they spell out the labels on my bottles from the pinyin.","Source C: From the private notebook of a woman who taught Chinese at a middle school in a large city, an entry written in 1972. Written for this room in the style of such a notebook; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — Six years since the summer the school shut. I have kept no record until now, because it was not safe to. In 1966 the pupils put up posters against us in the yard, and I was made to stand with a board round my neck and to write out my errors again and again. The library was carried into the yard and burned, and the old texts with it. The school opened again two years ago with a shorter course, half of it politics and work in the machine shop; we have no textbooks but the ones the district prints, and there is nothing on the radio but the eight model works, which my younger pupils can sing from beginning to end. My daughter was sent to Shanxi in 1969, and she writes that when she had a fever it was the brigade's barefoot doctor who came to her, a girl of her own age with six months' training. And yet my mother, who was never taught a single character in her life, learned to read at the night class from the pinyin chart, and every child in our lane is at school. I do not know how to set these things beside each other.","Interpretation 1: From a study of village life in Mao's China, by a historian writing in the 1980s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — For the great majority of Chinese people, who lived in villages, the Communist years brought gains that can be measured. A law of 1950 gave a woman the right to refuse a match and to leave a husband, and the binding of girls' feet came to an end. Night classes and a new alphabet put reading within reach of families in which nobody had ever held a book. Vaccination, clean wells and a half-trained health worker in every brigade meant that children who would have died now lived, and a peasant born after liberation could expect far more years than his father. Whatever was decided in Beijing, the balance sheet in the village was positive.","Interpretation 2: From a general history of China under Mao, by a historian writing in the 2000s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — The improvements were real, but they cannot be taken apart from the system that delivered them. The state that taught peasants to read also decided what there was to read, and for years the eight model works were nearly all that could be staged in a country of seven hundred million. The household registration of 1958 fixed a peasant in his village and the work unit fixed a worker in his factory; temples, churches and ancestral halls were closed; doubt was treated in labour camps; and after 1966 the schools stood empty for years. Women left the old family only for the work team. What was given was given by a state that had first taken everything else away.","How the room uses them: Source A is the inference source for Question 1. Sources B and C are the contemporary pair for the 8-mark usefulness question, 3(a), and may be used in 3(c). Interpretations 1 and 2 are the pair for 3(b), 3(c) and 3(d). Every quotation in the model answers of this room comes from the five texts above and from nowhere else."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 3 Question 3(d), 16 marks)","items":["Question: 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the effect of Communist rule on religion and belief in China between 1949 and 1976? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]. This is one paragraph from the middle of the answer, putting the case against Interpretation 2.","Interpretation 2 lists 'temples, churches and ancestral halls' among the things the state closed, but the list hides who had owned them and what their closing cost the ordinary villager. Temples and monasteries had themselves been landlords, and the Agrarian Reform Law of 28 June 1950 requisitioned the rural land of ancestral shrines, temples, monasteries and churches along with the gentry's estates, so the households that had paid rent to a monastery gained by the very act the interpretation counts as confiscation; the ancestral halls and clan graves cleared for farmland had belonged to the leading families of the village, whose elders had used them to arrange marriages and settle disputes. Belief itself, moreover, was regulated before it was attacked: the Buddhist Association of China and the Islamic Association of China were set up in 1953, the Protestant Three-Self Patriotic Movement was founded at a national conference in 1954, the Chinese Catholic Patriotic Association and the Chinese Taoist Association followed in 1957, and the 1954 Constitution promised citizens freedom of religious belief, so for fifteen years public worship was licensed rather than forbidden. What came after August 1966 was a change of policy and not the working out of one: the Red Guards who attacked the Jokhang temple in 1966 shut the patriotic associations as readily as the temples those associations had been created to supervise. Interpretation 2 is therefore right about the reach of the state and wrong to make twenty-seven years a single movement, and wrong again to describe as confiscation from the believer a policy that took most of what it took from landlords and clan elders.","Why this is Level 4: it 'provides an explained evaluation reviewing the alternative views in coming to a substantiated judgement' — the counter-case is built and then measured against Interpretation 2's own claim rather than simply asserted. The analysis is precise about what Interpretation 2 claims and how it conveys it (the language of confiscation, statute by statute), and the contextual knowledge is precisely selected rather than general: the dated land-reform statute, the four patriotic associations with their years, the wording of the 1954 Constitution and the attack on the Jokhang. The line of reasoning is sustained, it dates its verdict instead of giving one verdict for the whole period, and the closing sentence judges rather than summarises."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same as above — 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the effect of Communist rule on religion and belief in China between 1949 and 1976?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks].","Interpretation 2 says the state took everything away from people before it gave them anything, and I partly agree with this about religion. Religion was definitely attacked under Mao, especially in the Cultural Revolution when Red Guards went round smashing temples and other holy places, which was bad for anyone who believed in something. However Interpretation 1 says the balance sheet in the village was positive, and there is something in this because villagers got schools and doctors and better rights, which the temples had never given them. Also the government let religion carry on at the start through its own official organisations and only turned against it properly later. Overall both interpretations are partly right, because the Communists controlled religion completely but they did improve people's lives in other ways.","Why this is Level 2 and not Level 3: it does offer 'valid evaluative comment to agree with or counter the interpretation' and it does take details from both, which is what Level 2 asks for. What it misses is Level 3's 'relevant contextual knowledge used directly to support the evaluation': 'official organisations', 'later' and 'smashing temples' are assertions where the Agrarian Reform Law of 28 June 1950, the associations of 1953, 1954 and 1957, the promise of freedom of religious belief in the 1954 Constitution and the attack on the Jokhang temple in 1966 should be. It never shows how the interpretations convey their views, and its judgement is announced in the last sentence instead of being reasoned towards, so 'its justification is insecure or undeveloped and a line of reasoning is not sustained'. To reach Level 3, replace every general adjective with a named, dated fact that does evaluative work."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: propaganda, censorship and the cult of Mao","items":["Mao's Talks at the Yan'an Forum on Literature and Art, May 1942: art and writing exist to serve the workers, peasants and soldiers, and must be subordinate to politics. This is the 'Yan'an line' that governed culture for the next thirty years.","The Party owned every newspaper, radio station, publishing house, cinema and school; the People's Daily carried the official line and loudspeakers relayed it in villages and work units.","Model citizens were supplied for imitation: the soldier Lei Feng, who died in 1962, was held up for the whole country from March 1963 as an example of selfless service to Mao and the Party.","Lin Biao, Minister of Defence, made the study of Mao Zedong Thought the daily business of the People's Liberation Army, and had Quotations from Chairman Mao printed for soldiers in 1964.","The Little Red Book: more than 700 million copies of Quotations from Chairman Mao had been printed by the end of 1967. Mao badges and portraits were in homes, factories and schools.","Mao gave up the state chairmanship to Liu Shaoqi in 1959 after the Great Leap Forward, and thereafter relied on his personal standing with the army and the young rather than on the Party machine.","On 18 August 1966 Mao reviewed over a million Red Guards in Tiananmen Square; further rallies followed that autumn, and the cult became a mobilised political force.","Censorship worked by monopoly rather than by scissors: there was no independent press, no foreign broadcasting and no publisher outside the state.","The Hundred Flowers campaign of 1956 to 1957 invited criticism; the Anti-Rightist Campaign of 1957 that followed condemned roughly half a million people as 'rightists'."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: thought control and the 'Reform Through Labour' system","items":["'Thought reform' campaigns from 1951 targeted intellectuals, teachers, former officials and businessmen: study groups, written confessions, self-criticism and public struggle meetings.","Every work unit and production brigade kept a personal file on each member, recording class background, political reliability and any accusation made; the file followed a person for life.","Mutual surveillance was built in: neighbours, colleagues and family members were expected to report, so there was rarely a private place in which to disagree.","'Reform through labour' (laogai) was put on a legal footing by regulations approved on 7 September 1954. Prisoners worked in camps, mines and state farms in Qinghai, Xinjiang and the far north-east.","'Education through labour' (laojiao), approved in August 1957, allowed the police to detain a person for years by administrative order, without charge or trial.","Those labelled 'rightists' in 1957 were among the first large group sent to these camps; many were not released or rehabilitated until after Mao's death in 1976.","The purpose was stated openly as remoulding the person, not simply punishing an act: labour, study and confession together were supposed to produce a new outlook."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: family life, the communes and the danwei","items":["The traditional family was an economic and religious unit under its eldest men: it held the land, arranged the marriages and worshipped its ancestors.","The Agrarian Reform Law of 28 June 1950 and the land reform to 1952 destroyed the landlord families and with them the power of the clans over the village.","Collectivisation moved through mutual aid teams and cooperatives in the early and mid-1950s to the people's communes of 1958; about 26,000 communes held almost every peasant household by the end of that year.","Communes ran communal canteens, crèches and homes for the elderly, taking over cooking, childcare and the care of the old.","In the towns the danwei (work unit) allocated housing, ration coupons, the clinic and the school place, and granted permission to marry; a job in a state enterprise was the 'iron rice bowl'.","Unified purchase and supply of grain began in November 1953, and urban grain ration coupons were issued from 1955; cloth, oil and meat were rationed too.","The Regulations on Household Registration of 9 January 1958 classified every household as rural or urban and made it almost impossible for a peasant to move to a city.","From 1968 the 'up to the mountains and down to the countryside' movement sent about seventeen million urban young people to villages and border regions between 1968 and 1978.","Bicycles, sewing machines and wristwatches were the prized purchases of an urban family; in the countryside electricity and running water remained rare.","Urban wages rose very little between the late 1950s and the mid-1970s; peasants had no ration books and were paid in work points by their production team."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: women, foot binding and the Marriage Law of 1 May 1950","items":["Foot binding had been outlawed by the new Republic in 1912 but survived widely in the countryside; the Communists enforced the ban after 1949 until the practice ended.","The Marriage Law was issued on 1 May 1950. Marriage became a free agreement between the two people marrying, registered with the state.","The law banned arranged marriage, child betrothal, concubinage, the bride price and interference with a widow's remarriage.","It set a minimum marrying age of twenty for men and eighteen for women, and gave a wife the same right to seek divorce as her husband.","Divorce petitions ran into hundreds of thousands a year in the early 1950s, and most were brought by women.","The All-China Democratic Women's Federation, founded in 1949 and renamed in 1957, publicised the law with meetings, posters and travelling plays.","The 1954 Constitution declared that women enjoy equal rights with men in political, economic, cultural, social and family life.","The communes from 1958 moved cooking and childcare into canteens and crèches, so women went into the fields and earned work points in their own names; the slogan was that women hold up half the sky.","Limits remained: women's work was often awarded fewer work points than men's, household tasks stayed women's work, and enforcement of the Marriage Law depended on village cadres who were frequently traditional men.","Jiang Qing, Mao's wife, was one of the very few women near the top of the Party during the Cultural Revolution, and owed her position to her marriage; so did Ye Qun, Lin Biao's wife, who sat on the Politburo from April 1969 until her death in September 1971."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: education, pinyin and literacy","items":["About four in five adults in China could not read in 1949; classical written Chinese and the number of characters had kept literacy narrow.","The Chinese Character Simplification Scheme, published in 1956, reduced the number of strokes in common characters.","The Hanyu pinyin scheme was approved by the National People's Congress on 11 February 1958: Chinese spelt in the Latin alphabet, to teach pronunciation and reading rather than to replace characters.","Mass literacy work ran through winter schools in the slack farming season, spare-time classes in factories and communes, and teaching in the army; attendance was organised by the brigade or the work unit.","Primary schooling was expanded until most children attended, which mattered more in the long run than any adult campaign.","Despite the years lost after 1966, the 1982 census counted about two-thirds of people aged twelve and over as literate.","Schools and universities closed in the summer of 1966; pupils turned on their teachers, and Bian Zhongyun, deputy head of a Beijing girls' middle school, was beaten to death by pupils on 5 August 1966.","The university entrance examination was abandoned in 1966 and not restored until 1977; universities began taking students again from about 1970, recommended as workers, peasants and soldiers rather than examined.","When middle schools reopened, courses were shorter and heavy with politics and productive labour, and textbooks were local and few."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: health care and the barefoot doctors","items":["In 1949 there were about 40,000 doctors of Western medicine for a population of some 540 million, nearly all in the cities; cholera, smallpox, plague and schistosomiasis were common.","The patriotic health campaigns began in 1952: mass mobilisation to clean drains, cover latrines, boil drinking water, clear rubbish and vaccinate.","The Four Pests campaign of 1958 set the population against flies, mosquitoes, rats and sparrows; killing the sparrows let insect pests multiply, and bedbugs replaced sparrows on the list in 1960.","Smallpox was eliminated in China by the early 1960s, and schistosomiasis was fought by filling in and treating the ditches where the host snails lived.","Mao's directive of 26 June 1965 attacked the urban bias of the Ministry of Health and ordered medical work to be shifted to the countryside.","Barefoot doctors were peasants chosen by their production brigade, trained for a few months, still working in the fields and paid in work points; the scheme became national policy in 1968.","There were roughly 1.5 million barefoot doctors by the mid-1970s, and communes and brigades ran cooperative medical schemes to pay for treatment.","Life expectancy at birth rose from roughly thirty-five years around 1949 to about sixty-five by the mid-1970s, and infant mortality fell sharply.","The failures were real: the famine of 1959 to 1961, hospitals short of equipment and staff, trained doctors sent to the countryside during the Cultural Revolution, and a health worker with a few months' training who could not treat serious disease."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: cultural change, Jiang Qing's model works, and the attack on religion","items":["The campaign against the 'Four Olds' — old ideas, old culture, old customs and old habits — was launched in August 1966; Red Guards burned libraries and paintings, smashed temples and graveyards, cut hair and clothes in the street and renamed shops and roads.","Thousands of historic sites in Beijing alone were wrecked, and the Confucius temple and family graves at Qufu were attacked in November 1966.","Jiang Qing, Mao's wife and a former Shanghai film actress, took charge of culture in the 1960s, beginning with a festival of Peking opera on contemporary themes in 1964.","By 1967 the eight model works were fixed: the Peking operas Taking Tiger Mountain by Strategy, The Red Lantern, Shajiabang, Raid on the White Tiger Regiment and On the Docks; the ballets The Red Detachment of Women and The White-Haired Girl; and the symphony Shajiabang.","For years these were nearly all that could be staged, filmed or broadcast, so children knew them by heart.","Lao She, the novelist, was beaten by Red Guards in Beijing in August 1966 and was found drowned in Taiping Lake on 24 August 1966. Wu Han, whose play Hai Rui Dismissed from Office of 1961 was attacked in November 1965, died in prison in 1969.","The campaign to criticise Lin Biao and Confucius, 1973 to 1974, showed the same cultural weapon being used inside the leadership.","Land reform in 1950 to 1952 stripped temples, monasteries and churches of their estates, and foreign missionaries were expelled in the early 1950s as agents of imperialism.","Each faith was given a state body: the Buddhist Association of China and the Islamic Association of China in 1953, the Protestant Three-Self Patriotic Movement with a preparatory committee in 1951 and a founding national conference in 1954, and the Chinese Catholic Patriotic Association and the Chinese Taoist Association in 1957.","The Chinese Catholic Patriotic Association appointed bishops without Rome. The 1954 Constitution promised freedom of religious belief, but said nothing about preaching or teaching it.","Ancestor worship lost its halls and its graves, which were cleared for farmland; funerals were simplified and cremation encouraged.","In Tibet the Lhasa uprising of March 1959 was followed by the flight of the Dalai Lama to India and the closure of the monasteries; Red Guards attacked the Jokhang temple in Lhasa in 1966, and most Tibetan monasteries were left in ruins by 1976.","By the late 1960s almost every place of worship in China was shut, including the patriotic associations themselves."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the source and interpretation moves this paper rewards","items":["Inference: say something the source does not state but that must be true if the source is true, then give the words that force it. One inference and one supporting detail in each box.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose: what kind of document it is, who made it and when, and why it was made.","Utility: judge each source for the enquiry the question names, never in general. Content first, then how nature, origin and purpose make that content more or less useful, then the context that confirms or qualifies it.","Contextual knowledge scores only when it is used on the source; a paragraph about the period that never touches the sources earns nothing.","'Biased' is not a judgement on its own. Say whose view the source gives, why it gives that view, and what it is therefore useful for: an official newspaper item is excellent evidence of what the state wanted admired.","A source can be useful for an enquiry it never mentions, and a source that is almost silent on the enquiry can be used if you can explain why it is silent.","Difference of view (3(b)): a disagreement about the past, not a difference of wording or of detail. State the one difference, then support it with a phrase from each interpretation.","Reason for difference (3(c)): interpretations differ because they ask different questions, rest on different evidence, or weigh different parts of the story. Name which, and show it from the interpretations, using Sources B and C where they illustrate the evidence.","Differences based on conclusions drawn from evidence are legitimate; an interpretation is not wrong simply because another disagrees with it.","Evaluating an interpretation (3(d)): use both interpretations and your own knowledge, show how each conveys its view, then decide how far the named interpretation can be agreed with and say on what criterion."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 3 Question 3(d): 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the effect of Communist rule on religion and belief in China between 1949 and 1976?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","items":["The question as printed: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the effect of Communist rule on religion and belief in China between 1949 and 1976? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' This question prints no stimulus points: the two interpretations are the stimulus, and both must be used, so an answer that evaluates one in isolation cannot reach the top levels.","Decode the statement. Interpretation 2 claims that the improvements 'cannot be taken apart from the system that delivered them', that 'temples, churches and ancestral halls were closed', that doubt 'was treated in labour camps' and that what was given came from a state 'that had first taken everything else away'. Interpretation 1 says nothing about belief at all: it measures the village in marriage rights, reading and child deaths and finds that 'the balance sheet in the village was positive', so part of the evaluation is deciding what that silence is worth. The criterion to announce in the opening is whether Communist rule emptied China of belief, or moved belief into forms the state could license and, after 1966, could smash.","Opening (three or four sentences): put Interpretation 2's claim about temples, churches and halls into your own words, name Interpretation 1's balance sheet as the rival weighting, announce the criterion, and signal where you will end, so the line of reasoning is visible from the first paragraph.","Paragraph 1, for Interpretation 2 — belief permitted only inside a state body. The land reform that followed the Agrarian Reform Law of 28 June 1950 and ran to 1952 stripped temples, monasteries and churches of the estates they had lived on; foreign missionaries were expelled in the early 1950s; and each faith was then given a national organisation to belong to, with a preparatory committee in 1951 and a founding national conference in 1954 for the Protestant Three-Self Patriotic Movement, the Buddhist Association of China and the Islamic Association of China in 1953, and the Chinese Catholic Patriotic Association and the Chinese Taoist Association in 1957, the Catholic body appointing its own bishops without Rome. Interim judgement: belief came through the 1950s as something the state had chartered.","Paragraph 2, for Interpretation 2 — the religion of the household, and what the constitution actually promised. Ancestral halls were closed and clan graves cleared for the plough, so the rites a family performed for its own dead lost the place where they were performed, and the 1954 Constitution promised citizens freedom of religious belief while saying nothing of teaching it, preaching it or training anyone to carry it on. Interim judgement: what was guaranteed was private opinion rather than practice.","Paragraph 3, for Interpretation 2 — where licensing failed, force. After the Lhasa uprising of March 1959 and the Dalai Lama's flight to India the Tibetan monasteries were closed; Red Guards attacked the Jokhang temple in 1966; and the campaign against the Four Olds from August 1966 turned control into destruction, so that by the late 1960s almost every place of worship in China was shut, the patriotic associations along with them. Interim judgement: Interpretation 2's sentence is plainly true of the decade after 1966.","Paragraph 4, against Interpretation 2 — what the closures took, and from whom. Temples, monasteries and ancestral shrines had themselves been landlords, and the Agrarian Reform Law of 28 June 1950 requisitioned their rural land along with the gentry's estates, so the households that had rented from them gained by the same act Interpretation 2 counts as confiscation; the halls and graves that went under the plough had belonged to the leading families of the village, whose elders had used them to settle marriages and disputes. Interim judgement: the word 'taken' hides how much of the property and authority removed had never been the ordinary believer's.","Paragraph 5, against Interpretation 2 and the point that goes furthest beyond both texts — what replaced belief, and what outlived the closures. Belief was redirected rather than emptied out: Quotations from Chairman Mao, printed for the People's Liberation Army in 1964, had passed 700 million copies by the end of 1967, and daily study, recitation and public confession took the shape of the observance they displaced. When policy relaxed just after the period the question sets (a legitimate reach, because what happened in 1979 shows what 1949–76 had and had not achieved) — the Religious Affairs Bureau re-established in April 1979 and churches, temples and mosques reopening from that year — congregations reappeared far too quickly to have been created from nothing. Interim judgement: a state can close a building much more easily than it can end a belief.","The evaluation thread that must run through every paragraph: how each interpretation conveys its view. Interpretation 1 uses the language of accounting, a balance sheet that invites you to add and subtract and that leaves belief off the account altogether; Interpretation 2 uses the language of confiscation, a gift from a state that had taken everything else, which denies that the items can be added at all. Saying this, and using it, is what the top level means by showing how the differences of view are conveyed.","What the conclusion must say: not 'both are partly right'. Apply the criterion announced at the start — that Interpretation 2 describes the institutions exactly, since by the end of the 1960s there was no licensed place in China where a faith could be taught, but that it treats the closing of temples as the ending of belief, and counts as confiscation property and authority that had belonged to landlords and clan elders rather than to the worshipper. Then name the deciding consideration: the verdict has to be dated, because the 1950s licensed religion and the years after 1966 tried to abolish it, and the speed with which worship returned once the buildings reopened shows which of the two the state had actually achieved.","Timing and SPaG: about 40 minutes in all — six to plan the paragraphs and fix the criterion, about thirty to write, and the last four to check spelling and punctuation and to make sure the specialist terms are present and correctly used, since 4 additional marks depend on 'a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate'. Useful terms here: ancestor worship, ancestral hall, monastery, patriotic association, Three-Self, land reform, cadre, Four Olds, Red Guard, superstition, cult of Mao."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:57:30.510861+00:00","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"Publishable. Both hand fixes verified against the kit and the corrected date re-checked on the web; dupscan.js clean; all fourteen answer keys re-solved and every tariff, level label, cap and stem still matches the June 2025 Paper 3 form, with the five texts written for the room and attributed to 'a historian writing in the 1980s/2000s'. Nothing now blocks it: the only open item is the accepted overlap between the essay plan's religion evidence and question 9, which is a different task on the same fact-file material."}}},"33-1":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"33-1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 1 is where Option 33 begins, and it is the part of the course Paper 3 draws on most heavily for its sources and its interpretations: the years 1954 to 1960 contain a Supreme Court ruling, a lynching, a boycott that lasted over a year, a school crisis settled by paratroopers, two Acts of Congress and the first student sit-ins, and historians disagree sharply about which of them actually moved anything. If you can say what segregation was and how it was enforced, why the NAACP went to law, why Montgomery walked for 381 days, why nine children needed the 101st Airborne, and why almost no Deep South school had desegregated by 1960, you have the material for every question this option sets in Section A and most of what Section B asks you to weigh. This room teaches that story as an argument you can take sides in, and it trains the five moves the paper pays for: inference from a source, causal explanation that reaches past the printed bullet points, judging a source's usefulness for the enquiry actually named, seeing how two historians differ and why, and evaluating one of them against the other and against what you know.","idea":[{"h":"Two Americas in one country: segregation, discrimination and the vote","t":"In the Southern states segregation was the law. The Jim Crow statutes, blessed by the Supreme Court in Plessy v. Ferguson in 1896 under the formula 'separate but equal', divided schools, buses, waiting rooms, parks, hospitals, cemeteries and drinking fountains; in practice the separate facilities were never equal, and Black schools in the rural South often ran a shorter year so that children could work the cotton. Discrimination ran alongside the law: the worst-paid jobs, the refusal of credit, and juries from which Black citizens were kept off. The vote was the key held back hardest. Poll taxes, 'understanding' clauses in which a white registrar decided whether an applicant had explained the state constitution well enough, the threat of the sack or of a mortgage called in, and behind those the threat of violence, kept registration in Mississippi to a few per cent of Black adults of voting age. A top-level answer never treats segregation as merely unfair seating: it describes a system with a legal part, an economic part and a violent part, and then says which part a particular protest was attacking and which part it left standing.","code":"33-1 — 1 The position of Black Americans in the early 1950s: Segregation, discrimination and voting rights"},{"h":"Going to law and sitting down: the NAACP and CORE before 1954","t":"The National Association for the Advancement of Colored People, founded in 1909, chose the courts. Its Legal Defense Fund, set up in 1940 and led by Thurgood Marshall, followed the strategy of Charles Hamilton Houston: rather than attack 'separate but equal' head on, sue on the 'equal' half where it was most obviously false, in graduate and professional schools that no state could plausibly duplicate. A line of victories in the late 1940s and in 1950 prepared the ground for 1954. The Congress of Racial Equality, founded in Chicago in 1942, chose direct action instead: sit-ins at segregated restaurants in the North, and in 1947 the Journey of Reconciliation, in which mixed groups rode buses through the Upper South to test a Supreme Court ruling against segregation on interstate travel. A top-level answer uses the two bodies as two strategies, law and confrontation, and says what each could and could not deliver, because that contrast is the disagreement the interpretations in this option keep returning to.","code":"33-1 — 1 The position of Black Americans in the early 1950s: The work of civil rights organisations"},{"h":"17 May 1954: Brown, and the ruling that was easier to win than to enforce","t":"Oliver Brown's daughter Linda walked past a white school to reach her own in Topeka, Kansas. The NAACP joined her case with four others, and Thurgood Marshall argued that separating children by race damaged them whatever the buildings were like. On 17 May 1954 a unanimous Supreme Court under Chief Justice Earl Warren held that separate educational facilities are inherently unequal, and so unconstitutional, which destroyed the doctrine laid down in 1896. But the Court gave no timetable. When it returned to the remedy in 1955, Brown II told the states to proceed 'with all deliberate speed' and handed supervision to local federal district judges and local school boards, most of them Southern men living among their neighbours. A top-level answer separates the legal victory from the practical one: Brown made segregation indefensible in law and gave the movement a weapon and a rallying point, while 'all deliberate speed' handed the South a delay it used for years.","code":"33-1 — 2 Developments in education: Brown v. Topeka (1954)"},{"h":"Little Rock 1957: nine children and the 101st Airborne","t":"Little Rock's school board had agreed a cautious plan, and nine Black students were admitted to Central High School for September 1957. Governor Orval Faubus, who needed segregationist votes for a third term, announced on television on the evening of 2 September that he had called out the Arkansas National Guard, which surrounded the school from that night, and on 4 September the Guard turned the nine away in front of the cameras. A federal judge ordered the Guard removed; when the students entered on 23 September a crowd outside became so violent that the police took them out again. On 24 September President Eisenhower, who had never publicly endorsed Brown, federalised the Arkansas Guard and sent soldiers of the 101st Airborne Division, and from 25 September the nine attended under armed escort. Ernest Green became the school's first Black graduate in May 1958, after which Faubus closed Little Rock's high schools for the whole of 1958-59. A top-level answer notices what the crisis proved twice over: that a court order was worthless without force behind it, and that television pictures turned a local quarrel into a national and international embarrassment in the middle of the Cold War.","code":"33-1 — 2 Developments in education: The events at Little Rock High School (1957)"},{"h":"Montgomery, December 1955: why a bus seat became a movement","t":"On Montgomery's buses Black passengers paid at the front, often had to re-board at the back, could not use the front rows at all, and had to give up seats in the middle section whenever white passengers were standing. The Women's Political Council had been petitioning the city for years, and in March 1955 a fifteen-year-old, Claudette Colvin, was arrested for refusing to move. On 1 December 1955 Rosa Parks, a seamstress and the secretary of the Montgomery branch of the NAACP, was arrested for the same refusal, and this time the local leadership judged the case the right one to fight. Jo Ann Robinson's Council ran off leaflets overnight, the churches carried the call on Sunday 4 December, and the one-day boycott of 5 December emptied the buses so completely that a mass meeting that evening formed the Montgomery Improvement Association and elected the 26-year-old minister of the Dexter Avenue Baptist Church, Martin Luther King, as its president. A top-level answer explains why Parks mattered as a choice rather than an accident, and why a boycott was the one weapon available to a community that had been denied the vote.","code":"33-1 — 3 The Montgomery Bus Boycott and its impact, 1955–60: Causes and events of the boycott"},{"h":"381 days, and the ruling that ended it","t":"The boycott ran from 5 December 1955 to 20 December 1956. It held because it was organised: a car pool of about three hundred vehicles with dispatch points at the churches, money raised in collections and from sympathisers outside the South, and Black taxi drivers who at first carried passengers for the price of a bus fare. The city fought back by prosecuting the leaders under a 1921 anti-boycott law, King being convicted and fined in March 1956, and by having the car pool declared a public nuisance. The decision came from the courts: in Browder v. Gayle a federal panel ruled Alabama's bus segregation unconstitutional in June 1956, the Supreme Court affirmed on 13 November, and the written order reached Montgomery on 20 December, when the boycott was called off. The importance was national: mass non-violent protest had worked, King was famous, and the pressure fed into the Civil Rights Act of 1957, the first federal civil rights law since 1875, which created a Civil Rights Commission and a Civil Rights Division at the Justice Department but was so weakened in the Senate that Black registration rose only about three per cent by 1960. A top-level answer weighs the walking against the ruling rather than choosing one, because that is exactly what this option's interpretations argue about.","code":"33-1 — 3 The Montgomery Bus Boycott and its impact, 1955–60: Reasons for the success and importance of the boycott"},{"h":"King's leadership and the founding of the SCLC","t":"King was 26 and had been in Montgomery little more than a year when he was made president of the Montgomery Improvement Association, partly because he was new enough to have made no enemies. What he brought first was language: the boycott was presented as a Christian and an American claim rather than a racial quarrel, which made it very hard to attack in public. What he brought second was discipline: non-violence was insisted on even after his house was bombed on 30 January 1956, when he talked an armed and furious crowd outside it into going home. What he brought third was the Black church, which supplied halls, a weekly collection, an audience that assembled anyway, and leaders whose salaries came from their own congregations so that no white employer could sack them. In January 1957 ministers meeting in Atlanta founded the body that took the name Southern Christian Leadership Conference, with King as its president, to carry the Montgomery method across the South. A top-level answer treats King as an organiser and a communicator rather than as a great man, and asks what the movement gained, and what it risked, by resting so much on one leader.","code":"33-1 — 3 The Montgomery Bus Boycott and its impact, 1955–60: The significance of the leadership of Martin Luther King"},{"h":"Terror: the Klan and the murder of Emmett Till","t":"Violence was the last line of the segregation system. The Ku Klux Klan, revived in 1915 and splintered by the 1950s into local groups, used floggings, cross-burnings, arson and dynamite, and white juries rarely convicted. In August 1955 Emmett Till, a fourteen-year-old from Chicago staying with relatives near Money, Mississippi, was accused of behaving improperly towards a white shopkeeper's wife; on 28 August Roy Bryant and J.W. Milam took him from his great-uncle's house, beat and shot him, and sank the body in the Tallahatchie River. His mother, Mamie Till Bradley, had the body brought home and insisted on an open casket so that people could see what had been done, and photographs were published in the Black press. At Sumner in September an all-white, all-male jury acquitted both men after about an hour, although Moses Wright had stood up in court and identified them; months later, safe from a second trial, the two sold their account of the killing to a magazine. A top-level answer uses Till to show how the movement turned terror into publicity, and how the trial destroyed the claim that the South's system was orderly and lawful.","code":"33-1 — 4 Opposition to the civil rights movement: The Ku Klux Klan and violence"},{"h":"Massive resistance: Dixiecrats, White Citizens' Councils and the Southern Manifesto","t":"Southern resistance was political and economic before it was violent. In 1948 Southern Democrats who walked out of their party's convention over civil rights formed the States' Rights Democratic Party, the 'Dixiecrats', and ran Strom Thurmond for president; he carried four Deep South states, and the argument he made, that race relations were a matter for the states alone, remained the South's case. After Brown the first White Citizens' Council was formed at Indianola, Mississippi, in July 1954, and the Councils spread across the South with a membership in the hundreds of thousands by 1957. They rejected the Klan's methods and used their own: employers dismissed people who signed desegregation petitions or joined the NAACP, banks called in loans, and merchants refused credit and seed. In March 1956 a hundred and one Southern members of Congress signed the Southern Manifesto, pledging to reverse Brown by all lawful means, which told every Southern school board that delay would be defended in Washington. A top-level answer distinguishes the three kinds of opposition, legal, economic and violent, and explains why the respectable kind did most to slow desegregation down.","code":"33-1 — 4 Opposition to the civil rights movement: Opposition to desegregation in the South"}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1: two inferences from Source A","setup":"Source A in this room is a report written for the head office of a civil rights organisation by one of its field secretaries after visiting a county in the Mississippi Delta in November 1953. It states that the county has about nine thousand Black residents and rather more than four thousand white ones, and that eleven Black names appear on the voting register; that two schoolteachers who tried to register in the spring were required to explain a clause of the state constitution to the registrar's satisfaction and were failed, and that one of them has since lost his school; that the coloured school runs only from October to May so that the children can pick cotton, and has no science room and no library, while the white school runs the full year; that the bus station has two waiting rooms and the drinking fountains are marked; and that the local branch has thirty-one paid members, meets in a church because no hall will take it, and that three of those members asked for their names not to be written down. The question is: 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about education for Black children in the Southern states in the early 1950s. Complete the table below to explain your answer.' (4 marks)","steps":["Narrow the report to the enquiry before reading it a second time: the question is about schooling, so the register, the waiting rooms and the branch membership are background, and the two schools are the evidence.","First inference: Black children were given less schooling than white children in the same county, and the source forces it, because the coloured school runs only from October to May so that the children can pick cotton while the white school runs the full year.","Second inference: the segregated schools were separate but nowhere near equal in what they could teach, since the report says the coloured school has no science room and no library and makes no such complaint about the white school.","Check the shape before writing: each box takes a claim the report never puts into those words, followed by the exact detail that makes the claim unavoidable, and nothing else."],"cues":["Which sentences of this report are actually about schools?","What does the length of the school year tell you that the writer never states?","What do the missing rooms let you conclude about 'separate but equal'?","What belongs in each of the two boxes?"]},{"title":"Question 3(a): judging Sources B and C for the enquiry actually named","setup":"Source B in this room is a leaflet handed out at Black churches in Montgomery, Alabama, in February 1956 by the Montgomery Improvement Association: eleven weeks and not one of us on those buses; walk in twos where you can; the station wagons will run from the same corners as last week and the drivers ask you to be there by half past six, because the police stopped four of them last Thursday and ticketed them for things they had not done; if a driver is arrested, do not argue in the street but take his name to the church office; remember what we are asking for, first come first served seating, courtesy from the drivers, and coloured drivers on the coloured routes; no one is to answer an insult; we are tired in the feet, but we can walk another week, and the money that used to go into the fare box is staying in our own pockets. Source C is the newsletter of a White Citizens' Council in a Southern town, sent to members in 1956: membership in this chapter has passed nine hundred; this Council does not take part in violence and will have nothing to do with those who do, for our weapons are lawful and they are stronger; members who employ coloured labour, and members who lend money or sell seed and fertiliser, are asked to consult the list which accompanies this letter, on which every name belongs to a person who has signed the integration petition or joined the NAACP; two of the signers have already asked for their names to be removed and we have obliged them; the Supreme Court's ruling is not law in this state until we are compelled to make it so, and no school board in this county need be in any hurry. The question is: 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the part played by ordinary people on both sides of the struggle over segregation in 1956? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context.' (8 marks)","steps":["Copy the enquiry to the top of the plan and let it select the material: ordinary people on both sides, so walkers, drivers and shopkeepers are in, and the constitutional argument about the ruling is only background.","Take Source B's content for that enquiry: the leaflet is addressed to people who have to get to work, it organises station wagons from named corners, and it gives instructions for what to do when a driver is arrested, so it shows a protest being run by and for ordinary residents.","Weigh Source B's nature, origin and purpose together: a handbill given out in churches is written to be obeyed rather than to persuade an outsider, so it is candid about the practical trouble, but it is propaganda for its own side and will not mention anyone who has given up.","Do the same for Source C: a members' newsletter asking employers, lenders and seed merchants to consult a blacklist shows that ordinary white townspeople, not officials, were the enforcers, and its private and boastful purpose makes it better evidence of that, not worse.","Bring contextual knowledge in on the sources rather than beside them: the station wagons are the Montgomery car pool of about three hundred vehicles, and Source C is the Citizens' Council method that spread from Indianola in Mississippi from July 1954.","Finish with a judgement for this enquiry and not a general one: between them the two cover both sides at the level of ordinary life, and the limitation they share is that neither can show how many people on either side simply stayed out of it."],"cues":["What exactly is the enquiry into?","Who is Source B written for, and what does that tell you?","Does being one-sided always reduce a source's usefulness?","Who is doing the enforcing in Source C?","Which piece of your own knowledge explains the station wagons?","What can neither source tell you?"]},{"title":"Question 3(b): a difference of view, not a difference of detail","setup":"Interpretation 1 in this room, from a study of the law and the federal government in the civil rights struggle by a historian writing in the 1980s, argues that between 1954 and 1960 the gains that lasted were won in courtrooms and enforced from Washington: the NAACP's lawyers had prepared the ground for twenty years, the Supreme Court's school ruling of 1954 destroyed the legal basis of segregation at a stroke, the boycott in Montgomery was a magnificent effort but it was a federal court, not a year of walking, that finally seated Black passengers where they chose, and at Little Rock nine children could not enter a school until a president sent the 101st Airborne; protest could create the case, but only law and federal power could deliver the remedy. Interpretation 2, from a general history of the civil rights movement by a historian writing in the 2000s, argues that the change that mattered was made by ordinary Black Southerners and not by judges: court orders were pieces of paper, since six years after the school ruling not one Black child in Mississippi, Alabama or South Carolina sat in a classroom with white children, and what could not be evaded was people, for in Montgomery tens of thousands walked for a year and organised their own transport with the churches behind them, and when four students sat down in Greensboro in February 1960 the movement found it no longer needed to wait to be given anything. The question is: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. They give different views about the importance of the Montgomery Bus Boycott. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations.' (4 marks)","steps":["Reduce each writer's treatment of the boycott to a single sentence about importance: for the first it mattered as the protest that produced a lawsuit, for the second it mattered as the thing the community did for itself.","Test that this is a difference of view and not of fact: both accept that the boycott happened and that it ended in success, so the disagreement is about what in it did the work.","Anchor each half in that interpretation's own wording, 'a magnificent effort, but it was a federal court, not a year of walking' against 'tens of thousands of working men and women walked for a year and organised their own transport'.","Write it as one difference stated in the opening sentence and then supported from each interpretation in turn, rather than as two summaries placed side by side."],"cues":["What does each writer think the boycott achieved?","Do they disagree about events or about weighting?","Which phrase in each carries the view?","What shape should the finished answer take?"]},{"title":"Question 2: a 12-mark 'Explain why' that reaches past the bullet points","setup":"The question is: 'Explain why the Civil Rights Act of 1957 disappointed many campaigners for civil rights. You may use the following in your answer: the changes made to the bill in Congress; the Civil Rights Commission. You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks). On the paper Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b) and you answer one of them, and the mark scheme caps an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content at a maximum of 8 marks and shuts Level 4 to it altogether. The material is in this room's fact files: the Act was the first federal civil rights law since 1875; it was pressed by Eisenhower's attorney general, Herbert Brownell, in the years after Montgomery and was signed in September 1957; the clause that would have let the attorney general go to court in all civil rights cases was struck out; an amendment sent contempt cases to local juries; Senator Strom Thurmond of South Carolina held the floor for over twenty-four hours against it; the Act set up a Commission on Civil Rights and a Civil Rights Division at the Justice Department; and by 1960 Black registration in the South had risen by only about three per cent, which is why a second Act followed in that year.","steps":["Turn the first bullet into a causal claim rather than a heading: the clause that mattered most was removed before passage, so the federal government kept the duty of protecting the vote but lost the power to act on it without a complaint from an individual.","Make the second bullet do causal work too: a Commission that could investigate and report, but could not register a single voter or punish a single registrar, gave campaigners evidence rather than remedies.","Add the reason of your own that neither bullet prompts: the jury-trial amendment meant that a white registrar accused of contempt would be tried by a jury drawn from the very electorate that had excluded Black voters in the first place, so the sanction was empty in exactly the counties that needed it.","Measure the disappointment against what had been hoped: the first civil rights law since 1875 was expected to answer Montgomery and Little Rock, and instead it moved Black registration by only a few per cent, which is why campaigners were pressing for more within three years.","Order the three reasons so that they build rather than sit in a list, and close each paragraph on the question's own word, disappointment, rather than on the Act."],"cues":["What was taken out of the bill, and what did its removal cost?","What could the Commission actually do to a registrar?","Which reason is prompted by neither bullet?","How do you show disappointment rather than just weakness?","What must the last sentence of the answer do?"]},{"title":"Question 3(c): why two historians reach different conclusions","setup":"This example prints its own short pair of interpretations, written for it in the same way as the room's and not to be confused with the Interpretation 1 and Interpretation 2 in the sources section. Interpretation 1, from a study of Southern politics by a historian writing in the 1990s: 'What held segregation in place in the early 1950s was the law itself. Jim Crow statutes covered every public place, the Supreme Court had approved them in 1896, and the poll tax and the registrar's understanding test kept Black voters off the rolls, so there was no lawful way to vote the system down.' Interpretation 2, from a study of the rural South by a historian writing in the 2010s: 'Statutes mattered less than the ledger. Most Black families in the cotton counties rented land, bought seed on credit and worked for white employers, so a signature on a petition or an NAACP card could cost a tenancy, a loan or a job. Fear of the merchant, not the statute book, kept people quiet.' The question is: 'Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about why segregation was so hard to challenge in the Southern states in the early 1950s. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer.' (4 marks)","steps":["Name the reason before illustrating it: the two writers are reading different kinds of record, one the statute book and the registration rules, the other tenancies, loans and wages, so each finds a different lock on the same door.","Show the first view at work: judged by law, the obstacle is legal, because segregation was written into state statutes, blessed by the Supreme Court in 1896 and protected by voting rules that left Black Southerners no lawful way to change the men who made those statutes.","Show the second at work on the same county: judged by the ledger, the obstacle is economic, because a family whose land, seed and wages all came from the people they would be challenging could be ruined without any law being used against them at all.","Use a source to show the kind of evidence behind the second view, for example Source C's list of petition signers and NAACP members circulated in 1956 to employers, lenders and seed merchants, and then name the reason again rather than summarising the two views."],"cues":["What kind of record is each historian reading?","What does the statute book make the obstacle look like?","What can a tenancy or a loan do that a statute cannot?","Which source shows the economic weapon actually in use?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What did Brown II add to the Brown ruling in 1955, and why did it matter so much in the South?","a":"It told the states to desegregate 'with all deliberate speed' and left supervision to local federal district judges and local school boards, setting no date, so Southern boards could delay for years while each refusal was fought case by case."},{"q":"Which court ruling actually ended segregation on Montgomery's buses, and when did the boycott stop?","a":"Browder v. Gayle: a federal panel ruled Alabama's bus segregation unconstitutional in June 1956 and the Supreme Court affirmed on 13 November 1956; the boycott was called off on 20 December 1956, when the written order reached Montgomery."},{"q":"Why is a one-sided document such as a Citizens' Council newsletter still useful for an enquiry into opposition to desegregation?","a":"Because the enquiry is into that opposition, and the newsletter is the opposition organising itself in its own words; its purpose, to direct members, is evidence rather than a fault, though it cannot describe the movement it is attacking."},{"q":"Name one thing the Montgomery Bus Boycott could not have achieved without the federal courts.","a":"The legal end of segregated seating itself: the city refused the Montgomery Improvement Association's demands throughout, and the seating rule fell only when Browder v. Gayle was affirmed by the Supreme Court in November 1956."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 3 Section A (16 marks): Question 1 prints Source A and asks 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about...' in a table of two boxes each (4 marks); Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b) and you answer one of them, each 'Explain why...' with two stimulus points and 'You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks).","Paper 3 Section B (36 marks), all of it Question 3: (a) 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into...?' (8); (b) 'What is the main difference between these views?' (4); (c) 'Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about...' (4); (d) 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about...?' (16, plus up to 4 for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology).","The stimulus-point caps on the 12-mark 'Explain why...': 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'. There is no cap at Level 2. Question 3(d) prints no stimulus points, so no such cap applies to it.","Timing on Paper 3: 1 hour 30 minutes for 52 marks, so about a minute a mark plus thinking time. Roughly 5 minutes on Question 1, 18 on Question 2, 12 on 3(a), 6 each on 3(b) and 3(c), and about 40 minutes on 3(d) including the final check for the SPaG marks, which leaves a few minutes in hand.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose, and usefulness is always judged for the enquiry the question names, never in general. Contextual knowledge earns nothing unless it is used on the sources or on the interpretations.","The five dates that anchor this key topic: 17 May 1954, Brown v. Board of Education; August 1955, the murder of Emmett Till; 5 December 1955, the start of the Montgomery Bus Boycott, which ran until 20 December 1956; 12 March 1956, the Southern Manifesto; September 1957, Little Rock and the 101st Airborne."],"questions":[{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about the position of Black Americans in the Southern states in the early 1950s. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for each valid inference up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail selected from Source A. Credit deliberate exclusion from the vote, everyday segregation, unequal schooling, or the economic risk of protest.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: Black Southerners were being kept off the voting register deliberately rather than by accident. Details in the source that tell me this: a county with 'about nine thousand Black residents' has 'eleven Black names' on the register, and two schoolteachers who applied were failed by the registrar on a clause of the state constitution. (ii) What I can infer: segregation reached into ordinary daily life and not only into politics. Details in the source that tell me this: the bus station has 'two waiting rooms', 'the drinking fountains are marked', and the coloured school has no science room and no library while the white school runs the full year.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["An inference is something the report makes certain without ever saying it; ask what a field secretary is quietly telling his head office about the county.","Read it twice, once for what happens at the courthouse and once for what happens in the street, the station and the school.","Give each answer in two parts: a claim the source never puts into words, then the exact wording that forces it."],"codes":["33-1 — 1 The position of Black Americans in the early 1950s: Segregation, discrimination and voting rights","33-1 — 1 The position of Black Americans in the early 1950s: The work of civil rights organisations"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about the difficulties facing civil rights organisations in the South in the early 1950s. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for each valid inference up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail from Source A. Credit the danger of being identified as a member, the smallness of the branch, or exclusion from the ordinary public life of the county.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: belonging to a civil rights organisation in the South was dangerous. Details in the source that tell me this: of the branch's 'thirty-one paid members', 'three of them asked me not to write their names down', and one of the teachers who tried to register 'has since lost his school'. (ii) What I can infer: such organisations were shut out of the ordinary public life of the county. Details in the source that tell me this: the branch 'meets in the church because no hall will take us', so the only building open to it belonged to the Black community itself.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what it costs an ordinary person in this county to be known as a member, and find the sentences that show the cost.","One difficulty is about the people in the branch; the other is about the buildings and the town around them.","Set out a claim the report implies but never states, then the phrase that makes it unavoidable, for each of your two answers."],"codes":["33-1 — 1 The position of Black Americans in the early 1950s: The work of civil rights organisations","33-1 — 1 The position of Black Americans in the early 1950s: Segregation, discrimination and voting rights"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. They give different views on the reasons for the progress made by the civil rights movement in the years 1954 to 1960. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4) requires a key difference of view supported from both: Interpretation 1 credits the courts and federal power, Interpretation 2 the action of ordinary Black Southerners. A difference of surface detail only stays in Level 1.","sol":"The main difference is over who actually produced the change. Interpretation 1 says the gains were 'won in courtrooms and enforced ... from Washington': the NAACP's lawyers had prepared the ground for twenty years, the school ruling of 1954 'destroyed the legal basis of segregation at a stroke', and it was 'a federal court, not a year of walking' that seated Black passengers where they chose, so 'only law and federal power could deliver the remedy'. Interpretation 2 says the change 'was made by ordinary Black Southerners, not by judges': court orders 'were pieces of paper' that the states evaded, and 'what could not be evaded was people' — the Montgomery walkers, the churches, and the Greensboro students.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Reduce each interpretation to one sentence beginning 'The movement made progress because...' and set the two sentences side by side.","Check that your difference is one of view: both writers accept the same events, so look for what each says did the causal work.","State the difference in your first sentence, then support each half with wording taken from its own interpretation."],"codes":["33-1 — 2 Developments in education: Brown v. Topeka (1954)","33-1 — 3 The Montgomery Bus Boycott and its impact, 1955–60: Reasons for the success and importance of the boycott","33-1 — 3 The Montgomery Bus Boycott and its impact, 1955–60: Causes and events of the boycott"]},{"q":"Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about the reasons for the progress made by the civil rights movement in the years 1954 to 1960. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4) requires one explained reason, effectively substantiated: for example that the two historians are studying different subjects and therefore weigh different kinds of evidence.","sol":"One reason is that the two historians study different things and so weigh different evidence. Interpretation 1 comes from a study of the law and the federal government, so its evidence is judgments, orders and deployments: the ruling of 1954, the federal court in the Montgomery case, and the 101st Airborne at Little Rock. Read from that material, progress looks like something delivered from above. Interpretation 2 comes from a general history of the movement, so its evidence is what people did, of the kind found in Source B, where a Montgomery leaflet organises station wagons and asks walkers to be at their corners by half past six. Read from that, the movement itself looks like the cause.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Historians differ legitimately when they ask different questions of the past; look at what each interpretation is a study of.","Ask which pile of evidence each writer would have been reading, and what that evidence would make look important.","Name one reason, then show it working in both interpretations, using a source if it illustrates the kind of evidence involved."],"codes":["33-1 — 3 The Montgomery Bus Boycott and its impact, 1955–60: Reasons for the success and importance of the boycott","33-1 — 2 Developments in education: Brown v. Topeka (1954)","33-1 — 2 Developments in education: The events at Little Rock High School (1957)"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the difficulties faced by Black Americans campaigning against segregation in the mid-1950s? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): utility judged for this enquiry with developed reasoning on how nature, origin and purpose affect the content, context used to interpret. Credit Source B on police harassment and arrests, Source C on blacklists and economic pressure.","sol":"Source B is useful because it shows the harassment from inside the protest, as a practical problem rather than a complaint. The leaflet has to tell walkers to be at their corners early 'because the police stopped four of them last Thursday and ticketed them for things they had not done', and it has to say what to do 'if a driver is arrested': that is a city using traffic law to break a protest it could not answer, which matches Montgomery's prosecution of the boycott leaders under a 1921 anti-boycott law. Its provenance strengthens the inference: a handbill given out in churches is written to be obeyed rather than to persuade outsiders, so it has no reason to invent the trouble it describes, though it says nothing of members who were giving up. Source C is useful for the difficulty that never reached a courtroom. A Citizens' Council newsletter written for members boasts that membership 'has passed nine hundred' and asks employers, lenders and seed merchants to consult a list of everyone who 'has signed the integration petition or has joined the NAACP'. Because it is private and boastful its purpose makes it more useful, not less: this is economic coercion being organised in its own words, exactly the method the Councils spread from Indianola in July 1954. Together they are strong, because one shows official harassment and the other economic coercion; neither reaches the violence of the Klan or the bombing of King's house in January 1956.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Usefulness is always usefulness for something: write the enquiry out before you look at either source.","Take each source as content, then nature, origin and purpose, then the context that confirms or qualifies it, and compare only at the end.","Open on what Source B lets you infer about how the city fought the boycott, and make the first sentence a judgement rather than a description."],"codes":["33-1 — 3 The Montgomery Bus Boycott and its impact, 1955–60: Causes and events of the boycott","33-1 — 4 Opposition to the civil rights movement: Opposition to desegregation in the South","33-1 — 4 Opposition to the civil rights movement: The Ku Klux Klan and violence"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the methods used by each side in the struggle over segregation in the years 1955 to 1956? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): criteria-based judgements with developed reasoning on how provenance affects the content. Credit Source B on the car pool, limited demands and non-violent discipline, Source C on lawful economic pressure and delay; qualify both as partisan and local.","sol":"Source B is very useful for the movement's method, because it is the method in operation rather than a description of it. It organises station wagons from fixed corners at a fixed time, tells people to walk in twos, forbids answering an insult, and sets out demands that are strikingly modest — 'first come, first served seating, courtesy from the drivers, and coloured drivers on the coloured routes' — which shows that at this stage the Montgomery Improvement Association was asking for a better form of segregation, not its abolition. As an instruction sheet for its own side it is reliable about what the car pool of some three hundred vehicles actually did, though it says nothing of disagreements inside the movement. Source C is equally useful for the other side, and its usefulness lies in what it refuses as much as in what it recommends. The Council insists that 'our weapons are lawful, and they are stronger', and then names them: a blacklist for employers, lenders and merchants, and the advice that 'no school board in this county need be in any hurry'. That is the strategy of massive resistance, respectable delay backed by economic coercion, which the Southern Manifesto of March 1956 encouraged from Washington. Its limitation is that a newsletter written to encourage members will exaggerate its own restraint and its numbers. Between them they set the two methods against each other precisely; what neither shows is the Klan violence that ran alongside the Councils.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["The enquiry names two sides: plan two halves, and ask of each source what method it lets you see at work.","A document written for its own supporters is evidence of what that side actually intended; use its purpose rather than dismissing it as biased.","Start with the most striking thing in Source B, which is how limited the demands are, and say what that reveals about the movement's method in 1956."],"codes":["33-1 — 3 The Montgomery Bus Boycott and its impact, 1955–60: Causes and events of the boycott","33-1 — 4 Opposition to the civil rights movement: Opposition to desegregation in the South","33-1 — 3 The Montgomery Bus Boycott and its impact, 1955–60: Reasons for the success and importance of the boycott"]},{"q":"Explain why the Montgomery Bus Boycott began in December 1955. You may use the following in your answer: • Rosa Parks • the treatment of Black passengers on Montgomery's buses. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise knowledge — the seating rules, Parks's arrest on 1 December and her standing in the NAACP, Colvin in March 1955, the Women's Political Council's readiness and the churches. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The boycott began when a long-standing grievance met a case the community could defend and an organisation ready to use it. The first reason is the treatment of Black passengers, which supplied the anger. On Montgomery's buses Black riders paid at the front and often had to re-board at the back, could not use the front rows at all, and had to give up seats in the middle section whenever white passengers were standing; drivers ordered whole rows to move and sometimes pulled away before a passenger who had already paid could reach the rear door. Since Black riders were the great majority of the company's customers, the humiliation was both daily and absurd. The second reason is Rosa Parks. On 1 December 1955 she refused to leave her seat in the middle section and was arrested. She was not the first to refuse: Claudette Colvin, aged fifteen, had been arrested in March 1955, and the local leadership had decided that her case could not be built on. Parks was a seamstress, the secretary of the Montgomery branch of the NAACP and a woman of unattackable character, so E.D. Nixon and the branch judged that this arrest could be carried all the way to a court. The third reason, which neither bullet point prompts, is that the machinery was already in place. Jo Ann Robinson and the Women's Political Council had been petitioning the city about the buses for years and had a one-day protest planned in advance; the Council ran off tens of thousands of leaflets overnight, and the Black churches carried the call to their congregations on Sunday 4 December. When the buses ran empty on 5 December, a mass meeting that evening formed the Montgomery Improvement Association and voted to continue. The grievance supplied the will, Parks supplied the case, and the Council and the churches supplied the means to act within four days.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["A grievance that has existed for years cannot by itself explain a protest that starts in one particular week; ask what was new in December 1955.","Plan three reasons of different kinds — the daily experience, the individual case, and the organisation that could turn one into the other.","Open on what actually happened to a Black passenger on a Montgomery bus, and finish the paragraph on what that did to people's willingness to act."],"codes":["33-1 — 3 The Montgomery Bus Boycott and its impact, 1955–60: Causes and events of the boycott","33-1 — 1 The position of Black Americans in the early 1950s: Segregation, discrimination and voting rights","33-1 — 1 The position of Black Americans in the early 1950s: The work of civil rights organisations"]},{"q":"Explain why the Montgomery Bus Boycott was able to continue for more than a year. You may use the following in your answer: • the car pool • the Black churches of Montgomery. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): sustained causal analysis — the car pool of about three hundred vehicles, church halls, collections and unsackable leaders, the failure of prosecutions and bombing, and the pending Browder v. Gayle case. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"A boycott is easy to begin and hard to sustain, and Montgomery's ran for 381 days, from 5 December 1955 to 20 December 1956, because the community replaced the service it was refusing, because it rested on an institution its opponents could not reach, and because it could see an ending coming. The first reason is transport. Most boycotters were domestic workers and labourers who had to cross the city to work, so the Montgomery Improvement Association built a car pool of about three hundred vehicles with dispatch points at the churches and a published timetable, and Black taxi drivers at first carried passengers for the price of a bus fare until the city enforced a minimum charge to stop them. Without that system the protest would have collapsed inside a month. The second reason is the churches. They supplied halls for the mass meetings, a collection every week, an audience that assembled anyway, and ministers whose salaries came from their own congregations, so that the leadership could not be sacked by a white employer as ordinary members could; the meetings kept morale high and made the boycott feel like a shared obligation rather than a sacrifice. The third reason, which neither bullet prompts, is that the city's counter-attack failed while the courts moved in the boycotters' favour. Montgomery prosecuted the leaders under a 1921 anti-boycott law, convicting and fining King in March 1956; segregationists bombed King's house on 30 January 1956, and he talked the armed crowd outside it into going home; the car pool was finally declared a public nuisance in November. None of it broke the protest. Meanwhile the NAACP had filed Browder v. Gayle in February 1956, so the walkers knew a federal ruling was coming, and when the Supreme Court affirmed it on 13 November 1956 they had only to hold out until the written order arrived in December. Organisation moved the people, the churches held them together, and the lawsuit told them how long they had to last.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what a person who has no car and a job across the city needs before they can refuse to ride a bus for a year.","Treat the two bullets as the practical and the moral supports, then find a third kind of reason outside both.","Begin with the problem of getting to work and what the Montgomery Improvement Association built to solve it."],"codes":["33-1 — 3 The Montgomery Bus Boycott and its impact, 1955–60: Reasons for the success and importance of the boycott","33-1 — 3 The Montgomery Bus Boycott and its impact, 1955–60: The significance of the leadership of Martin Luther King","33-1 — 3 The Montgomery Bus Boycott and its impact, 1955–60: Causes and events of the boycott"]},{"q":"Explain why President Eisenhower sent federal troops to Little Rock in September 1957. You may use the following in your answer: • Governor Orval Faubus • the Arkansas National Guard. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise detail — Faubus's re-election needs and the call-out of the Guard on 2 September, defiance of a federal court order, the Newport meeting, the mob of 23 September, television and the Cold War, the 101st Airborne on 24 September. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Eisenhower acted not because he approved of desegregation but because a state governor had placed himself above a federal court. The first reason is Faubus. Little Rock's own school board had agreed a cautious plan to admit nine Black students to Central High School in September 1957, and Faubus, who needed segregationist votes to win a third term, chose to stop it: on the evening of 2 September he announced on television that he had called out the Arkansas National Guard, which surrounded the school, and on 4 September it turned the students away in front of the cameras. The second reason is what the use of the Guard meant constitutionally. A state's own soldiers were being used to defeat an order made by a federal court under Brown, which turned a local argument about one school into the question of whether federal law ran in Arkansas at all. Eisenhower had never publicly endorsed the Brown ruling and had hoped the South would be allowed to move slowly; he met Faubus at Newport in September and believed he had an undertaking, and when a federal judge ordered the Guard withdrawn and Faubus simply removed it and left the city police to face the crowd, the president had been publicly defied. The third reason, which neither bullet prompts, is the mob of 23 September and the audience watching it. When the nine entered by a side door that morning a crowd of about a thousand outside became so violent that the police took the students out again for their safety, and the scenes were carried on television and in newspapers abroad at a moment when the United States was asking the world to prefer its system to the Soviet Union's. On 24 September Eisenhower federalised the Arkansas Guard, taking it out of Faubus's hands, and sent soldiers of the 101st Airborne Division; from 25 September the nine attended under escort. A governor's defiance made the crisis, the misuse of state troops made it constitutional, and violence in front of the world made delay impossible.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Eisenhower was no friend of the Brown ruling, so the reasons cannot be about his enthusiasm for desegregation; ask what forced his hand.","Separate the local politics, the constitutional question and the audience, and explain what each removed from the president's options.","Start with what Faubus needed in Arkansas in 1957 and what he did with the Guard from the evening of 2 September."],"codes":["33-1 — 2 Developments in education: The events at Little Rock High School (1957)","33-1 — 2 Developments in education: Brown v. Topeka (1954)","33-1 — 4 Opposition to the civil rights movement: Opposition to desegregation in the South"]},{"q":"Explain why the Brown v. Topeka ruling of 1954 had changed so little in the schools of the Deep South by 1960. You may use the following in your answer: • Brown II (1955) • the White Citizens' Councils. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): analysis directed at causation — 'all deliberate speed' and local enforcement, pupil-placement and transfer rules, Council blacklists from July 1954, the Southern Manifesto of March 1956 and Eisenhower's silence. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Brown removed segregation's legal defence without removing anything else that held it up. The first reason is the Court's own second ruling. The decision of 17 May 1954 said what was unconstitutional but set no date; when the Court returned to the remedy in 1955, Brown II told the states to proceed 'with all deliberate speed' and handed supervision to local federal district judges and local school boards, most of them Southern men living among their neighbours. A phrase containing both 'deliberate' and 'speed' invited delay, and the South accepted the invitation: plans were written to desegregate one grade a year, transfer and placement rules put the burden on each Black family to apply and be refused, and every refusal then had to be fought case by case in court by lawyers the NAACP could not multiply. The second reason is organised community pressure. The first White Citizens' Council was formed at Indianola, Mississippi, in July 1954, weeks after the ruling, and the Councils spread across the South with a membership in the hundreds of thousands by 1957. They rejected the Klan's methods and used economic ones: a parent who signed a desegregation petition could lose a job, a bank loan or credit at the store, so petitions were withdrawn and there were few families left willing to be the test case. The third reason, which neither bullet prompts, is that Southern politics closed ranks while Washington stood back. In March 1956 a hundred and one Southern members of Congress signed the Southern Manifesto, promising to reverse the decision by all lawful means; states passed resolutions of 'interposition' and laws cutting off funds from any district that integrated; and Eisenhower refused to say publicly that Brown was right, intervening only at Little Rock in 1957 when a governor defied a court directly. The result was that in 1960, six years on, several Deep South states had still not admitted a single Black child to a white school. The ruling won the argument in law and lost the ground.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["A ruling only changes things if somebody has the duty and the power to enforce it; ask who that was after 1955.","Line up three kinds of obstacle — the wording of the remedy, pressure in the community, and the politics above both.","Open on what Brown II actually told the states to do and who it left in charge of doing it."],"codes":["33-1 — 2 Developments in education: Brown v. Topeka (1954)","33-1 — 4 Opposition to the civil rights movement: Opposition to desegregation in the South","33-1 — 2 Developments in education: The events at Little Rock High School (1957)"]},{"q":"Explain why the murder of Emmett Till in August 1955 caused such anger across the United States. You may use the following in your answer: • the funeral in Chicago • the trial of the accused men. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): sustained causal analysis — the victim's age and origins, the abduction of 28 August, the open casket and the photographs, Moses Wright's testimony, the acquittal at Sumner and the paid confession. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Lynching in Mississippi was not new; what was new in 1955 was that the whole country was made to look at one. The first reason is who the victim was and what was done to him. Emmett Till was fourteen, a boy from Chicago spending the summer with relatives near Money, Mississippi, unfamiliar with the rules he was expected to keep; after an exchange with a white shopkeeper's wife, Roy Bryant and J.W. Milam took him from his great-uncle's house on 28 August, beat and shot him, and sank the body in the Tallahatchie River with a weight wired to his neck. The offence alleged was a boy's; the punishment was murder, and the disproportion could not be explained away even by people who defended segregation. The second reason is the funeral. His mother, Mamie Till Bradley, had the body brought home to Chicago and insisted on an open casket because, she said, people had to see what had been done; tens of thousands filed past it, and photographs were published in the Black press and carried across the country. Americans who had only read the word 'lynching' now had a picture of it, and of a mother beside it. The third reason, which the bullets prompt only in part, is that the trial destroyed the South's defence of its own system. Bryant and Milam were tried at Sumner in September 1955 before an all-white, all-male jury; Moses Wright stood up in that courtroom and identified the men who had taken his great-nephew, which took extraordinary courage, and the jury acquitted them after about an hour. Months later, protected from a second trial, the two men sold their account of the killing to a magazine. Segregationists claimed the South ran an orderly society of its own; a child's murderers walking free and then confessing for money proved that a Black life could be taken there without consequence, and that is why the case recruited a generation of young people into the movement.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Killings of Black Southerners were common; the question is why this one became national news, so look for what made it visible.","Build three reasons: the victim and the crime, the decision that made it public, and what the courtroom then revealed.","Begin with who Emmett Till was and why a boy from Chicago was especially unlikely to survive the rules of the Delta."],"codes":["33-1 — 4 Opposition to the civil rights movement: The Ku Klux Klan and violence","33-1 — 1 The position of Black Americans in the early 1950s: Segregation, discrimination and voting rights","33-1 — 4 Opposition to the civil rights movement: Opposition to desegregation in the South"]},{"q":"Explain why the NAACP fought segregation mainly through the courts in the years before 1960. You may use the following in your answer: • Plessy v. Ferguson (1896) • Thurgood Marshall. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise detail — 'separate but equal' as a legal doctrine, disfranchisement and the danger of protest, Congress's silence between 1875 and 1957, the Legal Defense Fund from 1940 and the Houston strategy. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The NAACP went to law because the enemy was itself a legal doctrine and because law was the only arena in the South in which Black Americans could act at all. The first reason is Plessy v. Ferguson. In 1896 the Supreme Court held that separate facilities for the races were constitutional provided they were equal, and that single ruling was the licence under which every Jim Crow statute stood. A demonstration could be broken up or simply ignored, but a doctrine could only be removed by the body that had made it, so the target dictated the method. The second reason is that the other methods were closed. Black Southerners had been stripped of the vote by poll taxes, 'understanding' clauses and intimidation, so they could not change a state legislature; strikes and marches invited dismissal, eviction or the Klan, and the Emmett Till trial of 1955 showed what a white jury would do with the consequences; and the federal government would not act, since Congress passed no civil rights law at all between 1875 and 1957 and no president wanted to lose the Southern Democratic vote. A courtroom, by contrast, had rules that even Mississippi had to appear to obey. The third reason, which neither bullet prompts on its own, is that the association had deliberately built the instrument. Its Legal Defense Fund, set up in 1940, followed the strategy of Charles Hamilton Houston: instead of attacking 'separate but equal' head on, sue on the 'equal' half where it was least defensible, in graduate and professional schools that no state could plausibly duplicate for a handful of Black applicants. A run of victories in the late 1940s and in 1950 prepared the ground, and Thurgood Marshall, who led the Fund, then argued in Brown in 1954 that separation itself damaged children whatever the buildings were like, and won a unanimous Court. The doctrine was legal, the alternatives were shut, and the lawyers were ready, which is why the movement's first great victory was a judgment rather than a march.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what kind of thing segregation legally was, and what kind of body could therefore undo it.","Three reasons of different kinds: the nature of the target, the closing off of every other route, and the organisation's own preparation.","Start with the 1896 ruling and what it licensed, rather than with the NAACP itself."],"codes":["33-1 — 1 The position of Black Americans in the early 1950s: The work of civil rights organisations","33-1 — 1 The position of Black Americans in the early 1950s: Segregation, discrimination and voting rights","33-1 — 2 Developments in education: Brown v. Topeka (1954)"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the reasons for the progress made by the civil rights movement in the years 1954 to 1960? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views evaluated, how each conveys its view analysed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit school desegregation figures by 1960, the Councils and the Southern Manifesto, Montgomery and Greensboro; against, Browder v. Gayle and the 101st Airborne.","sol":"Interpretation 2 argues that 'the change that mattered in these years was made by ordinary Black Southerners, not by judges': court orders 'were pieces of paper' because the states had 'learned to litigate, delay and intimidate faster than Washington could act', and 'what could not be evaded was people'. Interpretation 1 puts the weight on the other side, holding that the gains were 'won in courtrooms and enforced ... from Washington'. The test must be what actually changed between 1954 and 1960, and what made each change stick. On the schools Interpretation 2 is close to unanswerable. Brown on 17 May 1954 destroyed the doctrine of Plessy v. Ferguson, but Brown II in 1955 told the states only to move 'with all deliberate speed', leaving the timetable to local boards and district judges. The South used the gap: the White Citizens' Councils, founded at Indianola in July 1954, spread across the South, dismissing employees and calling in loans; a hundred and one Southern congressmen signed the Southern Manifesto in March 1956; and in 1960 several Deep South states had admitted no Black child to a white school. A ruling nobody would enforce really was a piece of paper. Interpretation 2 is also right that the movement's momentum came from people rather than from judgments. Montgomery's boycott was carried by tens of thousands of working men and women for 381 days, with a car pool of about three hundred vehicles and churches supplying halls, collections and leaders no employer could sack; out of it came King's national reputation and the founding of the SCLC in January 1957, none of it ordered by any court. Greensboro on 1 February 1960 makes the point again: four students needed no case and no lawyer, and the sit-ins spread to dozens of Southern cities within weeks, producing the SNCC in April 1960. Interpretation 1 nevertheless supplies what Interpretation 2 leaves out. The boycotters did not win their seats by walking: Browder v. Gayle, decided in June 1956 and affirmed by the Supreme Court on 13 November, ended segregated seating, and the boycott stopped on 20 December only when the written order arrived. Little Rock makes the case more sharply still. Nine children did not enter Central High because they were brave; they entered on 25 September 1957 behind the 101st Airborne, after Eisenhower had federalised the Arkansas National Guard. The two writers also convey their views by what they choose to count: Interpretation 2 measures progress by what people did and uses the language of paper and evasion, while Interpretation 1 measures it by what changed in law and who enforced it, which turns crowds into witnesses. I agree with Interpretation 2 about the source of the movement's strength and disagree with it as a complete account of progress. The energy and the courage came from below, and without them there was no case to decide and no crisis to resolve. But each actual removal of segregation in these years, the buses in Montgomery and the classrooms in Little Rock, was completed by a federal order with force behind it. Ordinary people made the pressure; law and federal power converted it into change. Interpretation 2 is right about who made the movement and wrong to suggest that they could have finished the work alone.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first what Interpretation 2 is actually claiming caused progress, and name the test your judgement will apply.","Use Interpretation 1 as your case against, and support both sides with dated detail rather than with the interpretations restated.","Open by putting Interpretation 2's claim into your own words and stating the criterion you will judge it by."],"codes":["33-1 — 2 Developments in education: Brown v. Topeka (1954)","33-1 — 3 The Montgomery Bus Boycott and its impact, 1955–60: Reasons for the success and importance of the boycott","33-1 — 4 Opposition to the civil rights movement: Opposition to desegregation in the South"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part played by ordinary Black Americans in the Montgomery Bus Boycott of 1955 to 1956? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views weighed, how the difference of view is conveyed analysed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit the 381 days, the car pool and the churches; against, the NAACP's choice of Parks, King's leadership and Browder v. Gayle.","sol":"Interpretation 2 holds that in Montgomery 'tens of thousands of working men and women walked for a year and organised their own transport', and that 'the churches gave them a structure no court order could supply'; Interpretation 1 concedes that the boycott was 'a magnificent effort' but insists that 'it was a federal court, not a year of walking' that seated Black passengers where they chose. The question is how much of the boycott, and of its outcome, belongs to ordinary people. Interpretation 2 is strongly supported on the boycott itself. It lasted 381 days, from 5 December 1955 to 20 December 1956, and it could only last that long because the people who kept it going were the people it inconvenienced most: domestic workers and labourers who had to cross the city daily and who walked, shared lifts or used the Montgomery Improvement Association's car pool of about three hundred vehicles running from dispatch points at fixed times. The money came from weekly collections, and the churches supplied halls, an audience that met anyway and ministers whose salaries came from their own congregations, so that the leadership could not be dismissed by a white employer in the way ordinary members could. When the city prosecuted the leaders under a 1921 anti-boycott law and convicted King in March 1956, and when segregationists bombed his house on 30 January, nobody went back to the buses. A protest of that length is not made by a committee. Interpretation 1's corrective is real, and it works at two points. First, the boycott's beginning was not spontaneous. Claudette Colvin had been arrested in March 1955 and her case set aside; when Rosa Parks was arrested on 1 December 1955 it was E.D. Nixon and the NAACP branch, of which Parks was secretary, who judged that this case could be carried through the courts, while Jo Ann Robinson's Women's Political Council already had a one-day protest prepared. Ordinary people responded magnificently to a plan that experienced organisers had made. Second, and more important, the outcome came from a court. The city never conceded the Association's demands; segregated seating fell because Browder v. Gayle was decided in a federal court in June 1956 and affirmed by the Supreme Court on 13 November, and the boycott ended on 20 December only when the written order arrived. Had there been no lawsuit, the walkers could have walked indefinitely. The two writers convey their views through what they put in the foreground. Interpretation 2 describes actions by numbers of people and institutions they built, so agency is collective; Interpretation 1 describes decisions by named bodies, so the crowd becomes the occasion for a case rather than its author. Overall I agree with Interpretation 2 a good deal, but not to the exclusion of the rest. Ordinary Black Montgomerians were not the background to the boycott, they were the boycott, and no lawyer could have produced or sustained what they did for over a year. But they were led into it by organisers who had chosen their moment, and they were released from it by a federal ruling they could not have obtained by walking. The part played by ordinary people was decisive for the pressure; it was not sufficient for the victory.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Separate the two questions inside this one: who sustained the protest, and what actually ended the segregated seating.","Test Interpretation 2's strongest ground, the length of the boycott and the car pool, then look for what it cannot account for.","Open by stating what Interpretation 2 claims about Montgomery and naming the criterion your judgement will use."],"codes":["33-1 — 3 The Montgomery Bus Boycott and its impact, 1955–60: Causes and events of the boycott","33-1 — 3 The Montgomery Bus Boycott and its impact, 1955–60: Reasons for the success and importance of the boycott","33-1 — 3 The Montgomery Bus Boycott and its impact, 1955–60: The significance of the leadership of Martin Luther King"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the value to Black Americans of the rulings of the federal courts in the years 1954 to 1960? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views evaluated, how each is conveyed analysed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit 'all deliberate speed', evasion and the 1960 school figures; against, Browder v. Gayle, the legal authority Brown gave the movement and Little Rock.","sol":"Interpretation 2 treats the judgments of these years as nearly worthless in practice: 'court orders were pieces of paper', and six years after the Supreme Court's school ruling 'not one Black child in Mississippi, Alabama or South Carolina sat in a classroom with white children', because the states had learned to 'litigate, delay and intimidate faster than Washington could act'. Interpretation 1 answers that the lasting gains were 'won in courtrooms', that the ruling of 1954 'destroyed the legal basis of segregation at a stroke', and that 'only law and federal power could deliver the remedy'. The criterion for judging between them must be what a ruling was worth: what it changed immediately, and what it made possible afterwards. Judged by immediate change in the schools, Interpretation 2 is right, and the detail is worse than it implies. Brown of 17 May 1954 set no date, and Brown II in 1955 asked only for 'all deliberate speed', leaving the pace to local school boards and district judges. Southern states answered with pupil-placement and transfer rules that forced each Black family to apply, be refused, and sue; the first White Citizens' Council at Indianola in July 1954 began the practice of sacking petition-signers and calling in their loans; and a hundred and one Southern congressmen signed the Southern Manifesto in March 1956. Eisenhower never said publicly that Brown was right. Six years of that produced, in the Deep South, no desegregated classrooms at all, which is a devastating verdict on a judgment by itself. But Interpretation 2 measures only one kind of value, and Interpretation 1 is right about the others. A court order was decisive where somebody enforced it: nine students entered Central High on 25 September 1957 because Eisenhower federalised the Arkansas National Guard and sent the 101st Airborne, so Faubus's defiance was defeated by federal power acting on a court's instruction. A ruling was also decisive where it settled the issue a protest could not: Montgomery's segregated seating ended not because of the walking but because Browder v. Gayle was decided in June 1956 and affirmed on 13 November, and the boycott stopped on 20 December when the order reached the city. Brown also changed the standing of the whole argument. After 1954 segregationists were defending something the highest court in the land had declared unconstitutional, which is why they reached for 'interposition' and the Manifesto; and the movement could now present itself as the party of the law rather than its opponent, a claim King used constantly. The interpretations convey their views by choosing different measures of a ruling's worth. Interpretation 2 uses the vocabulary of paper and evasion, which measures value by what happened next month; Interpretation 1 uses the vocabulary of foundation and remedy, which measures it by what became possible. I agree with Interpretation 2 about the immediate value of the courts and disagree with it as a judgement on their worth. Without enforcement a judgment changed nothing, and in the Deep South nothing is exactly what changed by 1960. But the rulings gave the movement a legal position it had never had, ended segregation outright wherever federal power was willing to act, and made every act of resistance illegal rather than merely wrong. They were not sufficient; they were indispensable.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["A ruling can be worth something in more than one way: ask what it changed at once and what it made possible later.","Test the interpretation's strongest evidence, the empty Deep South classrooms of 1960, and then ask what that fact does not measure.","Begin by restating Interpretation 2's claim about court orders in your own words and naming the criterion you will judge it by."],"codes":["33-1 — 2 Developments in education: Brown v. Topeka (1954)","33-1 — 2 Developments in education: The events at Little Rock High School (1957)","33-1 — 3 The Montgomery Bus Boycott and its impact, 1955–60: Reasons for the success and importance of the boycott"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date did the Supreme Court give its decision in Brown v. Board of Education of Topeka?","a":"17 May 1954."},{"q":"Whose arrest in Montgomery on 1 December 1955 led to the bus boycott?","a":"Rosa Parks, secretary of the Montgomery branch of the NAACP."},{"q":"Which army division did President Eisenhower send to Little Rock in September 1957?","a":"The 101st Airborne Division."},{"q":"What was the declaration signed by a hundred and one Southern members of Congress in March 1956, pledging to reverse the Brown ruling by all lawful means?","a":"The Southern Manifesto, signed on 12 March 1956."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Segregation and 'separate but equal'","back":"The Jim Crow laws of the Southern states separated schools, buses, waiting rooms, parks and fountains by race. Plessy v. Ferguson (1896) ruled this constitutional if facilities were equal; in practice they never were.","code":"33-1 — 1 The position of Black Americans in the early 1950s: Segregation, discrimination and voting rights","open":false},{"front":"Keeping Black Southerners off the voting register","back":"Poll taxes, 'understanding' clauses judged by white registrars, and the threat of dismissal, eviction or violence. In Mississippi only a few per cent of Black adults of voting age were registered in the early 1950s.","code":"33-1 — 1 The position of Black Americans in the early 1950s: Segregation, discrimination and voting rights","open":false},{"front":"The NAACP","back":"National Association for the Advancement of Colored People, founded 1909. Its Legal Defense Fund, set up in 1940 under Thurgood Marshall, attacked segregation case by case in the federal courts.","code":"33-1 — 1 The position of Black Americans in the early 1950s: The work of civil rights organisations","open":false},{"front":"CORE","back":"Congress of Racial Equality, founded in Chicago in 1942. It used direct action: restaurant sit-ins in the North, and the Journey of Reconciliation of 1947, riding buses through the Upper South to test a desegregation ruling.","code":"33-1 — 1 The position of Black Americans in the early 1950s: The work of civil rights organisations","open":false},{"front":"Brown v. Board of Education of Topeka, 17 May 1954","back":"A unanimous Supreme Court under Chief Justice Earl Warren held that separate educational facilities are inherently unequal and therefore unconstitutional, overturning the doctrine of Plessy v. Ferguson.","code":"33-1 — 2 Developments in education: Brown v. Topeka (1954)","open":false},{"front":"Brown II (1955)","back":"The Supreme Court's ruling on how Brown was to be carried out: desegregation was to proceed 'with all deliberate speed', supervised by local federal district judges and local school boards. No date was set.","code":"33-1 — 2 Developments in education: Brown v. Topeka (1954)","open":false},{"front":"The Little Rock Nine, September 1957","back":"Nine Black students admitted to Central High School, Arkansas. Turned away by the National Guard on 4 September, withdrawn from a mob on 23 September, escorted in from 25 September by the 101st Airborne.","code":"33-1 — 2 Developments in education: The events at Little Rock High School (1957)","open":false},{"front":"Orval Faubus","back":"Governor of Arkansas. Sent the Arkansas National Guard to keep the nine out of Central High in September 1957, defying a federal court, and closed Little Rock's high schools for the 1958-59 school year.","code":"33-1 — 2 Developments in education: The events at Little Rock High School (1957)","open":false},{"front":"Rosa Parks","back":"Montgomery seamstress and secretary of the local NAACP branch. Arrested on 1 December 1955 for refusing to give up her seat; her case was chosen as the one to fight in the courts.","code":"33-1 — 3 The Montgomery Bus Boycott and its impact, 1955–60: Causes and events of the boycott","open":false},{"front":"The Montgomery Bus Boycott, 5 December 1955 to 20 December 1956","back":"381 days off the buses, run by the Montgomery Improvement Association under Martin Luther King, with a car pool of about three hundred vehicles and weekly collections in the churches.","code":"33-1 — 3 The Montgomery Bus Boycott and its impact, 1955–60: Causes and events of the boycott","open":false},{"front":"Browder v. Gayle (1956)","back":"A federal panel ruled Alabama's bus segregation unconstitutional in June 1956; the Supreme Court affirmed on 13 November. The order reached Montgomery on 20 December and the boycott ended.","code":"33-1 — 3 The Montgomery Bus Boycott and its impact, 1955–60: Reasons for the success and importance of the boycott","open":false},{"front":"The Civil Rights Act of 1957","back":"The first federal civil rights law since 1875. It set up a Commission on Civil Rights and a Civil Rights Division at the Justice Department, but was weakened in the Senate and added few Black voters.","code":"33-1 — 3 The Montgomery Bus Boycott and its impact, 1955–60: Reasons for the success and importance of the boycott","open":false},{"front":"The SCLC, January 1957","back":"Southern Christian Leadership Conference, founded by ministers meeting in Atlanta with Martin Luther King as president, to spread the Montgomery method of non-violent mass protest through the Southern churches.","code":"33-1 — 3 The Montgomery Bus Boycott and its impact, 1955–60: The significance of the leadership of Martin Luther King","open":false},{"front":"Emmett Till, August 1955","back":"A fourteen-year-old from Chicago murdered near Money, Mississippi, on 28 August. His mother's open-casket funeral made the case national; an all-white jury at Sumner acquitted Bryant and Milam in September 1955.","code":"33-1 — 4 Opposition to the civil rights movement: The Ku Klux Klan and violence","open":false},{"front":"White Citizens' Councils","back":"First formed at Indianola, Mississippi, in July 1954. Respectable opponents of desegregation who used economic weapons: sacking, refusing credit and calling in loans against petition-signers and NAACP members.","code":"33-1 — 4 Opposition to the civil rights movement: Opposition to desegregation in the South","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The sources and interpretations written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Source A: From a report written for the head office of a civil rights organisation by one of its field secretaries, after a visit to a county in the Mississippi Delta, November 1953. Written for this room in the style of such a report; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — The county has about nine thousand Black residents and rather more than four thousand white ones, and on the voting register I was shown there are eleven Black names. Two men who tried to register in the spring were told to explain a clause of the state constitution to the registrar's satisfaction; both are teachers, and both were failed. One of them has since lost his school. The coloured school here runs only from October to May so that the children can pick, and it has no science room and no library; the white school runs the full year. The bus station has two waiting rooms and the drinking fountains are marked. Our branch has thirty-one paid members and meets in the church because no hall will take us. Three of them asked me not to write their names down.","Source B: From a leaflet handed out at Black churches in Montgomery, Alabama, in February 1956 by the Montgomery Improvement Association. Written for this room in the style of such a leaflet; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — Eleven weeks now, and not one of us on those buses. Walk in twos where you can. The station wagons will run from the same corners as last week, and the drivers ask you to be there by half past six, because the police stopped four of them last Thursday and ticketed them for things they had not done. If a driver is arrested, do not argue in the street; take his name to the church office. Remember what we are asking for and what we are not: first come, first served seating, courtesy from the drivers, and coloured drivers on the coloured routes. No one is to answer an insult. We are tired in the feet, but we can walk another week, and the money that used to go into the fare box is staying in our own pockets.","Source C: From the newsletter of a White Citizens' Council in a Southern town, sent to its members, 1956. Written for this room in the style of such a newsletter; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — Membership in this chapter has passed nine hundred, and we welcome the thirty-eight who joined at the courthouse meeting. Let it be said again that this Council takes no part in violence and will have nothing to do with those who do; our weapons are lawful, and they are stronger. Members who employ coloured labour, and members who lend money or sell seed and fertiliser, are asked to consult the list which accompanies this letter. Every name on it belongs to a person who has signed the integration petition or has joined the NAACP. Two of the signers have already asked for their names to be removed, and we have obliged them. The Supreme Court's ruling is not law in this state until we are compelled to make it so, and no school board in this county need be in any hurry.","Interpretation 1: From a study of the law and the federal government in the civil rights struggle, by a historian writing in the 1980s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — Between 1954 and 1960 the gains that lasted were won in courtrooms and enforced, when they were enforced at all, from Washington. The NAACP's lawyers had spent twenty years preparing the ground, and the Supreme Court's school ruling of 1954 destroyed the legal basis of segregation at a stroke. The boycott in Montgomery was a magnificent effort, but it was a federal court, not a year of walking, that finally put Black passengers where they chose to sit. Little Rock made the point again: nine children could not enter a school until a president sent the 101st Airborne to take them through the door. Protest could create the case; only law and federal power could deliver the remedy.","Interpretation 2: From a general history of the civil rights movement, by a historian writing in the 2000s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — The change that mattered in these years was made by ordinary Black Southerners, not by judges. Court orders were pieces of paper: six years after the Supreme Court's school ruling, not one Black child in Mississippi, Alabama or South Carolina sat in a classroom with white children, because the states had learned to litigate, delay and intimidate faster than Washington could act. What could not be evaded was people. In Montgomery tens of thousands of working men and women walked for a year and organised their own transport, and the churches gave them a structure no court order could supply. When four students sat down in Greensboro in February 1960, the movement discovered that it no longer needed to wait to be given anything.","How the room uses them: Source A is the inference source for Question 1. Sources B and C are the contemporary pair for the 8-mark usefulness question, 3(a), and may be used in 3(c). Interpretations 1 and 2 are the pair for 3(b), 3(c) and 3(d). Every quotation in this room's model answers comes from the five texts above and from nowhere else."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 3 Question 3(d), 16 marks)","items":["Question: 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the importance of the Black churches of the South to the civil rights movement in the years 1954 to 1960?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]. This is one paragraph from the middle of the answer, putting the case for Interpretation 2 and then qualifying it.","Interpretation 2 says that in Montgomery 'the churches gave them a structure no court order could supply', and the detail of the boycott shows exactly what that structure was. The mass meetings that voted to continue the protest on the evening of 5 December 1955 were held in church buildings, because no other hall in the city was open to a Black gathering of that size; the weekly collections that paid for petrol and repairs were taken in the same buildings; and the car pool of about three hundred vehicles used the churches as its dispatch points, so that a network already meeting every Sunday became a transport system on Monday morning. The leadership followed from the same fact: Martin Luther King was made president of the Montgomery Improvement Association as a 26-year-old minister, and ministers could be chosen precisely because their salaries came from their own congregations, so that unlike the domestic workers and labourers in the pews they could not be dismissed by a white employer or have their credit withdrawn by a Citizens' Council. That immunity is why the founding of the Southern Christian Leadership Conference in January 1957 was organised through ministers rather than through lawyers or trade unionists. The qualification is that the church supplied protection and organisation rather than remedy: it could sustain a boycott for 381 days but it could not desegregate a single bus, and the seating rule fell only when Browder v. Gayle was affirmed by the Supreme Court on 13 November 1956. On the importance of the churches to the movement's capacity to act, therefore, Interpretation 2 is convincing; on the movement's capacity to win, Interpretation 1's emphasis on the courts still has to be added.","Why this is Level 4: it 'provides an explained evaluation reviewing the alternative views in coming to a substantiated judgement', since the case for Interpretation 2 is built and then measured against what the churches could not do. The contextual knowledge is 'precisely selected to support the evaluation' rather than general — the date of the first mass meeting, the size of the car pool, King's age and position, the founding of the SCLC, the date of the Supreme Court's affirmation. The analysis of how the view is conveyed is specific (the word 'structure', and what Interpretation 2 contrasts it with), and the closing sentence judges on a stated criterion instead of summarising."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same as above — 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the importance of the Black churches of the South to the civil rights movement in the years 1954 to 1960?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks].","Interpretation 2 says the churches gave the movement a structure that a court order could not supply, and I agree with this to quite a large extent. The churches were very important because they were where people met and where the leaders came from, such as Martin Luther King who was a minister and became famous during the bus boycott. They also helped to raise money and keep everyone's spirits up, which was needed because the boycott went on for a long time and people had to walk a lot. However, Interpretation 1 says that the courts and the government were more important, and there is something in this too, because in the end it was a court that changed the rules on the buses. Overall the churches were important but so were the courts.","Why this is Level 2 and not Level 3: it does offer 'valid evaluative comment to agree with or counter the interpretation' and it does select details from both interpretations, which is what Level 2 asks for. What it lacks is Level 3's 'relevant contextual knowledge used directly to support the evaluation': 'where people met', 'raise money' and 'a long time' are where the first mass meeting of 5 December 1955, the car pool of about three hundred vehicles, the 381 days and Browder v. Gayle should be. It also never shows how either interpretation conveys its view, and the judgement is announced in the last sentence rather than reasoned towards, so 'its justification is insecure or undeveloped and a line of reasoning is not sustained'. Turning it into a Level 3 answer means replacing every vague phrase with a named fact that does evaluative work."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the position of Black Americans in the early 1950s, and the NAACP and CORE","items":["Segregation in the Southern states rested on the Jim Crow laws and on Plessy v. Ferguson (1896), in which the Supreme Court held that separate facilities were constitutional if they were equal.","Segregated by law: schools, buses and trains, waiting rooms, cinemas, parks, swimming pools, hospitals, cemeteries, drinking fountains and the Bibles used to swear witnesses in some courts.","Black schools in the rural South were commonly given a shorter year so that children could work the cotton crop, and far less money was spent per pupil than in white schools.","Disfranchisement: poll taxes, literacy and 'understanding' tests marked by white registrars, and the threat of dismissal, eviction or violence. In Mississippi only a few per cent of Black adults of voting age were registered in the early 1950s.","Discrimination outside the law: the worst-paid jobs, exclusion from many trade unions, refusal of credit and mortgages, and juries from which Black citizens were kept off.","Most Black Americans still lived in the Southern states in the 1950s, although millions had moved north in the Great Migration, where segregation was by custom, housing and employment rather than by statute.","The NAACP was founded in 1909 and campaigned through the courts, through lobbying and through its magazine; its Southern branches were small, exposed and often secret.","The NAACP Legal Defense Fund was set up in 1940; Thurgood Marshall led it and later became the first Black justice of the Supreme Court.","Charles Hamilton Houston's strategy, followed from the 1930s, was to sue on the 'equal' half of 'separate but equal' in graduate and professional education, where no state could plausibly provide a second institution of the same quality.","CORE, the Congress of Racial Equality, was founded in Chicago in 1942 and used non-violent direct action, including restaurant sit-ins in Northern cities.","The Journey of Reconciliation, organised by CORE in 1947, sent mixed groups of riders through the Upper South to test a Supreme Court ruling against segregation on interstate buses; several riders were arrested."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: developments in education — Brown v. Topeka (1954) and Little Rock (1957)","items":["Brown v. Board of Education of Topeka combined five cases brought by the NAACP; Linda Brown had to pass a white school to reach her own in Topeka, Kansas.","Thurgood Marshall argued that separating children by race harmed them whatever the quality of the buildings, using evidence from psychologists.","On 17 May 1954 a unanimous Supreme Court under Chief Justice Earl Warren ruled that separate educational facilities are inherently unequal and so breach the Fourteenth Amendment.","Brown II, in 1955, ordered desegregation 'with all deliberate speed' and gave supervision to local federal district courts and local school boards; it set no deadline.","Desegregation moved fastest in the border states and in Washington DC, and hardly at all in the Deep South, where several states had still not admitted a single Black child to a white school by 1960.","Little Rock's school board adopted a gradual plan, and nine Black students were selected for Central High School in September 1957.","On the evening of 2 September 1957 Governor Orval Faubus, seeking a third term, announced in a televised address that he had called out the Arkansas National Guard, which surrounded the school; on 4 September the Guard turned the nine away, and Elizabeth Eckford, arriving alone, faced the crowd by herself.","Eisenhower met Faubus at Newport, Rhode Island, in September 1957; a federal judge then ordered the Guard withdrawn.","On 23 September the nine entered by a side door; a crowd of about a thousand outside became violent and the police took the students out again.","On 24 September 1957 Eisenhower federalised the Arkansas National Guard and ordered in soldiers of the 101st Airborne Division; from 25 September the nine attended under escort, and troops remained at the school for the year.","Ernest Green became the first Black graduate of Central High School in May 1958; Faubus then closed all Little Rock's high schools for the 1958-59 year rather than integrate them."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Montgomery Bus Boycott, 1955-56, King and the SCLC","items":["Montgomery's bus rules: Black passengers paid at the front and often re-boarded at the rear, could not use the front rows, and had to surrender seats in the middle section when white passengers were standing.","Claudette Colvin, aged fifteen, was arrested in March 1955 for refusing to move; the local leadership decided not to build a case on her arrest.","Rosa Parks, a seamstress and secretary of the Montgomery branch of the NAACP, was arrested on 1 December 1955; E.D. Nixon of the branch arranged her bail and the test case.","Jo Ann Robinson and the Women's Political Council, who had petitioned the city for years, produced leaflets overnight calling for a one-day boycott on Monday 5 December 1955.","The boycott of 5 December was almost total; a mass meeting that evening founded the Montgomery Improvement Association and elected the 26-year-old Martin Luther King, minister of Dexter Avenue Baptist Church, as its president.","The Association's first demands were limited: courteous treatment, first-come first-served seating, and Black drivers on predominantly Black routes.","The car pool used about three hundred vehicles with dispatch points at the churches; Black taxi drivers at first charged the bus fare until the city enforced a minimum charge.","The city prosecuted the leaders under a 1921 anti-boycott law and King was convicted and fined in March 1956; King's house was bombed on 30 January 1956 and he persuaded the armed crowd outside to go home.","Browder v. Gayle was filed in February 1956; a federal panel ruled Alabama's bus segregation unconstitutional in June 1956 and the Supreme Court affirmed on 13 November 1956.","The boycott ended on 20 December 1956, when the written order reached Montgomery, after 381 days; the buses were integrated the next day, and there were shootings and bombings against Black residents in the weeks that followed.","In January 1957 ministers meeting in Atlanta founded the body that took the name Southern Christian Leadership Conference, with King as president, to carry non-violent mass protest through the Southern churches.","The Civil Rights Act of 1957 was the first federal civil rights law since 1875: it created a Commission on Civil Rights and a Civil Rights Division at the Justice Department, but the clause allowing the attorney general to sue in all civil rights cases was removed and contempt cases were sent to local juries. Strom Thurmond of South Carolina spoke against it for over twenty-four hours.","Black registration in the South rose by only about three per cent by 1960, and the Civil Rights Act of 1960, signed in May 1960, added federal referees and the inspection of registration records but changed little.","The sit-in movement began on 1 February 1960 when four students sat at a segregated lunch counter in Greensboro, North Carolina; sit-ins spread to dozens of Southern cities within weeks, and in April 1960 a conference called by Ella Baker at Shaw University in Raleigh founded the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: opposition — the Klan, Emmett Till, the Dixiecrats and the White Citizens' Councils","items":["The Ku Klux Klan, refounded in 1915, had split by the 1950s into local groups; its methods were cross-burnings, floggings, arson and dynamite, and white juries rarely convicted its members.","Emmett Till was fourteen, from Chicago, and was staying with relatives near Money, Mississippi, in the summer of 1955.","After an exchange with a white shopkeeper's wife, Roy Bryant and J.W. Milam took Till from his great-uncle's house in the early hours of 28 August 1955; his body was recovered from the Tallahatchie River.","His mother, Mamie Till Bradley, insisted on an open casket at the funeral in Chicago so that people could see the injuries; photographs were published in the Black press and tens of thousands filed past.","The trial was held at Sumner, Mississippi, in September 1955. Moses Wright, Till's great-uncle, identified the men in court; the all-white, all-male jury acquitted both after about an hour.","Months later, safe from a second trial, Bryant and Milam sold their account of the killing to a magazine for a fee.","The 'Dixiecrats': Southern Democrats who left their party's 1948 convention over civil rights and formed the States' Rights Democratic Party, running Strom Thurmond, who carried four Deep South states.","Their argument, that race relations were a matter for the states and not for Washington, remained the basis of Southern resistance through the 1950s.","The first White Citizens' Council was formed at Indianola, Mississippi, in July 1954, two months after Brown; the Councils spread across the South with a membership in the hundreds of thousands by 1957.","Council methods were economic and respectable: sacking employees, refusing credit, calling in loans and publishing the names of those who signed desegregation petitions or joined the NAACP. They publicly disowned violence.","On 12 March 1956 a hundred and one Southern members of Congress signed the Southern Manifesto, condemning the Brown decision as an abuse of judicial power and pledging to reverse it by all lawful means.","Several Southern states passed 'interposition' resolutions and laws withdrawing funds from any district that desegregated, and some, like Virginia and later Arkansas, closed schools rather than integrate them."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the source and interpretation moves this paper rewards","items":["Inference: state something the source does not say but that must be true if the source is true, then give the words that force it. One inference and one supporting detail in each box.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose: what kind of document it is, who made it and when, and why it was made.","Utility: judge each source for the enquiry the question names, never in general. Content first, then how nature, origin and purpose make that content more or less useful, then the context that confirms or qualifies it.","Contextual knowledge scores only when it is used on the source; a paragraph of period knowledge that never touches the sources earns nothing.","'Biased' is not a judgement on its own. Say whose view the source gives, why it gives that view, and what it is therefore useful for: a one-sided document is excellent evidence of the position it takes.","A source can be useful for an enquiry it never mentions, if what it shows explains the thing enquired into; say what you can infer from it, not just what it says.","Difference of view (3(b)): a disagreement about the past, not a difference of wording or of detail. State the one difference, then support each half with a phrase from its own interpretation.","Reason for difference (3(c)): interpretations differ because they ask different questions, rest on different evidence, or weigh different parts of the story. Name which, show it in both interpretations, and use Sources B and C if they illustrate the evidence.","Differences based on conclusions drawn from evidence are legitimate; an interpretation is not wrong merely because another disagrees with it.","Evaluating an interpretation (3(d)): use both interpretations and your own knowledge, show how each conveys its view, and decide how far the named interpretation can be agreed with, saying on what criterion."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 3 Question 3(d): 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the importance of federal enforcement in the desegregation of the South in the years 1954 to 1960?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","items":["The question as printed: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the importance of federal enforcement in the desegregation of the South in the years 1954 to 1960? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' This question prints no stimulus points: the two interpretations are the stimulus, and both must be used, so an answer that evaluates one in isolation cannot reach the top levels.","Decode the statement. Both interpretations argue about enforcement and not only about rulings. Interpretation 2 calls court orders 'pieces of paper' because the states 'litigate, delay and intimidate faster than Washington could act', so what mattered was what ordinary Black Southerners did for themselves; Interpretation 1 says the gains that lasted were 'enforced, when they were enforced at all, from Washington' and offers the 101st Airborne at Little Rock as its proof. The criterion to state in the opening is what enforcement actually delivered: how often Washington acted, what changed when it did, and what changed without it.","Opening (three or four sentences): put Interpretation 2's claim in your own words, name Interpretation 1 as the rival weighting, announce the criterion, and signal where you will end, so the line of reasoning is visible from the first paragraph.","Paragraph 1, for Interpretation 2 — enforcement was rare and slow. Brown II in 1955 set no deadline and left supervision to local federal district judges and local school boards; the Southern Manifesto of 12 March 1956, signed by a hundred and one members of Congress, promised to reverse Brown by all lawful means; and six years after the ruling Mississippi, Alabama and South Carolina had not admitted a single Black child to a white school. Interim judgement: across most of the South the federal writ was not enforced at all.","Paragraph 2, for Interpretation 1 and against Interpretation 2 — where Washington did act, it was decisive. At Little Rock, Faubus called out the Arkansas National Guard on the evening of 2 September 1957 and the nine were turned away on 4 September; the mob of about a thousand drove them out on 23 September; then Executive Order 10730 federalised the Guard and sent the 101st Airborne on 24 September, and the nine attended from 25 September under escort. In Montgomery the ruling in Browder v. Gayle, affirmed on 13 November 1956 and reaching the city on 20 December, was what finally seated Black passengers where they chose. Interim judgement: enforcement was the difference between a right on paper and a right in use.","Paragraph 3, for Interpretation 2 — what people achieved before any enforcement arrived. Montgomery's Black residents walked for 381 days and ran a car pool of about three hundred vehicles, the churches supplied an organisation no court order could create, and it was the boycott that produced the case the federal courts then decided. Interim judgement: enforcement completed what protest had begun, which is not the same as causing it.","Paragraph 4, the point that goes furthest beyond both texts — why enforcement stayed so weak. The Civil Rights Act of 1957, signed on 9 September 1957 and the first federal civil rights law since 1875, lost the clause that would have let the attorney general bring civil rights suits and sent contempt cases to local juries, so the Justice Department could seldom act, and Black registration in the South rose only about three per cent by 1960; Eisenhower had never publicly endorsed Brown and moved only when he was openly defied. Interim judgement: enforcement mattered so much precisely because Washington had chosen to keep so little of it.","The evaluation thread that must run through every paragraph: how each interpretation conveys its view. Interpretation 2 uses the vocabulary of paper and evasion, which measures a ruling by what happened next month; Interpretation 1 uses the vocabulary of remedy and enforcement, and chooses its examples, the school ruling, the Montgomery judgment and the paratroopers, precisely where federal power was used. Saying this, and using it, is what the top level means by showing how the differences of view are conveyed.","What the conclusion must say: not 'both are partly right'. Apply the criterion announced at the start. Interpretation 2 is right that enforcement was rare, that the states had learned to evade it and that the movement's force came from people; but where it came it was decisive, and nothing except federal power opened the doors of Central High or the front seats of Montgomery's buses. Then name the deciding consideration: federal enforcement was necessary but never sufficient, because Washington acted only on the cases that protest and defiance had already forced it to face.","Timing and SPaG: about 40 minutes in all — six to plan the paragraphs and fix the criterion, about thirty to write, and the last four to check spelling and punctuation and to make sure the specialist terms are present and correctly used, since 4 additional marks depend on 'a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate'. Useful terms here: segregation, desegregation, integration, federal, the Fourteenth Amendment, National Guard, massive resistance, interposition, non-violent direct action, NAACP."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:53:33.251736Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Re-judged against the kit on disk after the fix pass; every recorded fix is in place and correct, and no answer key is now wrong. The Little Rock date is right in all four places and internally consistent: Faubus announced on television on the evening of 2 September 1957 that he had called out the Arkansas National Guard, which surrounded Central High from that night, and on 4 September the Guard turned the nine away (lesson.idea[3], room.questions[8] a, sol and hints[2], extras[4].items[6], extras[8].items[4]); the card 'The Little Rock Nine, September 1957' was rightly left as it stood, and 23, 24 and 25 September, Executive Order 10730 and Ernest Green's graduation in May 1958 are unchanged and correct. I re-verified on the web the call-out of the evening of 2 September (Encyclopedia of Arkansas, NPS), the Southern Manifesto of 12 March 1956 with 101 signatories, 19 senators and 82 representatives (US House History, EJI), and the facts the new plan rests on: Browder v. Gayle affirmed 13 November 1956 with the order reaching Montgomery on 20 December, the Civil Rights Act of 1957 signed 9 September 1957 as the first since 1875 with the attorney general's suit clause struck and contempt sent to local juries, Southern Black registration up only about three per cent by 1960, and no Black child in a white school in Mississippi, Alabama or South Carolina six years after Brown. The new plan is a proper 3(d): it names an issue both printed interpretations argue about, prints no stimulus points, requires both interpretations, decodes the claim, sets a criterion, runs four paragraphs with interim judgements, carries an evaluation thread on how each view is conveyed and ends on a substantiated judgement, with 40 minutes and the SPaG check. lesson.examples[4] now works its own pair of short interpretations on why segregation was hard to challenge in the early 1950s, so it no longer shadows room.questions[3]; node dupscan.js EDX-1HI0 33-1 reports 'no duplication'. room.facts[5] now anchors the key topic on 12 March 1956, the Southern Manifesto, in place of Greensboro, and room.exit[3] asks for the Southern Manifesto instead, so all four exit questions and all five anchor dates are Key topic 1 content. The 3(d) stem reads 'using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context' in all four places, matching levels.md. I re-solved all fifteen questions: four at d=1 (two inferences, 3(b), 3(c)), eight at d=2 (six 12-mark causation questions with their stimulus points and the June 2024 caps, two 8-mark utility questions), three at d=3 (three foci on the same printed interpretations), solution lengths 106-118, 248-249, 317-343 and 543-549 words, every key that could be checked against the printed sources true of what is printed, and every one of the nine specification ideas taught, tested and carded. Marks, AO splits, level labels, the SPaG tag on 3(d) only, the 1 hour 30 minute Paper 3 and the caps in their current wording all still match levels.md, the fifteen cards are all within 40 words with no open card miscast, and nothing reproduces the Option 33 specimen."}}},"33-2":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"33-2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 2 is the part of Option 33 that Paper 3 draws on hardest, because it contains both halves of the argument the examiners keep setting: the campaigns that worked and the ones that did not. Section A takes its source and its causation question from these fifteen years, and Section B's interpretations are almost always arguments about how much the civil rights movement actually changed. If you can explain, with names and dates, why a lunch counter in Greensboro mattered, how television turned Birmingham and Selma into Acts of Congress, why Black Power grew out of victory rather than defeat, and what had and had not changed by 1975, you can answer nearly anything this option sets. This room teaches that story as a chain of causes you can argue with, and trains the five moves the paper rewards: inference from a source, causal explanation that goes beyond the printed bullet points, judgement of a source's usefulness for the enquiry actually named, spotting how two historians differ and why, and evaluating one of them against the other and against what you know.","idea":[{"code":"33-2 — 1 Developments, 1960–62: The significance of Greensboro and the sit-in movement","h":"Greensboro, 1 February 1960: four students and a lunch counter","t":"On 1 February 1960 four first-year students from the North Carolina Agricultural and Technical College sat at the whites-only lunch counter of the Woolworth's store in Greensboro and stayed when they were refused service. The tactic was copied within a fortnight in dozens of southern towns and within a year in more than a hundred cities; tens of thousands took part and around three thousand were arrested. It worked because it was cheap, orderly and local: students came in formal clothes, refused to answer abuse, and kept coming, so a store had to choose between closing its counter, losing its white trade, and serving everyone. The Greensboro counter was desegregated on 25 July 1960. In April 1960 Ella Baker gathered the student groups at Shaw University in Raleigh and the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee (SNCC) was founded. A top-level answer uses the sit-ins for two arguments at once: leadership passing from ministers to students, and economic pressure, not moral appeal alone, doing the work."},{"code":"33-2 — 1 Developments, 1960–62: The Freedom Riders","h":"The Freedom Rides, 1961: provoking the violence that made Washington act","t":"On 4 May 1961 thirteen riders, seven black and six white, left Washington DC on two buses for New Orleans. The Congress of Racial Equality, under James Farmer, had organised them to test a Supreme Court ruling of 1960 that segregation in the terminals used by interstate travellers broke the Interstate Commerce Act. On 14 May a Ku Klux Klan mob stopped the Greyhound bus outside Anniston, Alabama, slashed its tyres and firebombed it, beating the riders as they escaped; the second bus was met in Birmingham by a mob that the police left alone for a quarter of an hour. When CORE withdrew, Nashville students from SNCC took the ride on, were attacked by a mob in Montgomery on 20 May, and were jailed in Mississippi. Robert Kennedy sent federal marshals and then pressed the Interstate Commerce Commission, whose order banning segregation in interstate terminals took effect on 1 November 1961. A top-level answer explains the method rather than describing the journey: the riders did not expect protection, they expected the violence to be photographed and to make federal inaction impossible."},{"code":"33-2 — 1 Developments, 1960–62: The James Meredith case (1962)","h":"Meredith at Ole Miss, 1962: federal power against a state governor","t":"James Meredith, an air force veteran, won a federal court order admitting him to the all-white University of Mississippi in 1962. Governor Ross Barnett defied the order in person and had himself appointed university registrar so that he could turn Meredith away. When federal marshals escorted Meredith onto the Oxford campus on the evening of 30 September 1962, a mob of thousands rioted through the night: two men were killed, a French journalist and a local workman, and more than three hundred people were injured. President Kennedy sent regular army troops, Meredith registered on 1 October and, guarded by soldiers for the whole of his course, graduated in August 1963. A top-level answer uses the case as the sharpest test of federal authority against a state, and then notes the price: one student admitted at the cost of an army, which is not a method that could be repeated in every school and university in the South."},{"code":"33-2 — 2 Peaceful protests and their impact, 1963–65: King and the peace marches of 1963","h":"Birmingham and Washington, 1963: the pictures and the respectability","t":"King and the Southern Christian Leadership Conference chose Birmingham in April 1963 because it was the most rigidly segregated large city in the South and because Eugene 'Bull' Connor, its Commissioner of Public Safety, could be relied on to react in front of the cameras. King was arrested on 12 April and wrote his Letter from Birmingham Jail; from 2 May schoolchildren marched in their thousands, and Connor turned fire hoses and police dogs on them. The jails filled, the city's trade collapsed and an agreement to desegregate lunch counters and to hire black staff was announced on 10 May. On 28 August about 250,000 people, a large minority of them white, gathered at the Lincoln Memorial for the March on Washington for Jobs and Freedom, where King gave the 'I have a dream' speech. A top-level answer keeps the two together: Birmingham supplied the pictures that made a civil rights bill necessary, Washington supplied the calm and the numbers that made it passable."},{"code":"33-2 — 2 Peaceful protests and their impact, 1963–65: Freedom Summer and the Mississippi murders","h":"Freedom Summer, 1964: the state where the vote had never been won","t":"In the summer of 1964 SNCC and CORE brought more than a thousand volunteers, most of them white northern students, into Mississippi, where only about 6.7 per cent of black adults were registered to vote. They ran some forty Freedom Schools teaching more than three thousand pupils, and escorted people to the county courthouses. On 21 June James Chaney, Andrew Goodman and Michael Schwerner were murdered by Klansmen near Philadelphia, Mississippi, with the help of a deputy sheriff; their bodies were found under an earth dam on 4 August. Of roughly 17,000 black Mississippians who tried to register that summer, about 1,600 were accepted, and in August the Mississippi Freedom Democratic Party was offered only two token seats at the Democratic convention in Atlantic City and rejected the compromise. A top-level answer uses Freedom Summer twice: as evidence that the nation's attention followed the deaths of white students rather than black ones, and as the moment when many young activists stopped believing that white liberal allies would deliver."},{"code":"33-2 — 2 Peaceful protests and their impact, 1963–65: The impact of peaceful protest and the roles of Kennedy and Johnson","h":"From the streets to the statute book: 1964 and 1965","t":"Kennedy sent a civil rights bill to Congress in June 1963, after Birmingham, and was assassinated on 22 November before it passed. Johnson, a southerner who had sat in Congress since the 1930s and had led the Senate as majority leader, made the bill his own, broke a filibuster by southern senators with a cloture vote in June 1964, and signed the Civil Rights Act on 2 July 1964: segregation in public places was outlawed and discrimination in employment forbidden. Voting was barely touched, so in 1965 King took the campaign to Selma in Alabama. On 7 March state troopers clubbed and gassed about six hundred marchers at the Edmund Pettus Bridge; 'Bloody Sunday' was on the television news that evening, and the completed march reached Montgomery 25,000 strong on 25 March. Johnson signed the Voting Rights Act on 6 August 1965, suspending literacy tests and sending federal examiners into the southern states. A top-level answer traces the mechanism rather than listing the laws: protest created a crisis, television made the crisis national, and a president who could count votes in the Senate turned it into law."},{"code":"33-2 — 3 Malcolm X and Black Power, 1963–70: Malcolm X, his beliefs and methods","h":"Malcolm X: separation, self-defence and the change of 1964","t":"Malcolm X joined the Nation of Islam while in prison and became its best-known speaker. He taught that black Americans should separate from white society rather than beg to be integrated into it, should build their own businesses and schools, and should defend themselves by any means necessary; he dismissed the March on Washington as a tame performance. Elijah Muhammad silenced him in December 1963 after he said of Kennedy's assassination that the chickens had come home to roost, and in March 1964 he left the Nation altogether and founded Muslim Mosque Inc. After making the hajj to Mecca in April 1964 he began to speak of working with white people who genuinely opposed racism and of taking the case for black Americans to the United Nations. He was shot dead at the Audubon Ballroom in Harlem on 21 February 1965. A top-level answer holds the change of 1964 and his influence apart: it was the earlier, separatist Malcolm X whom Black Power took up, which is why his death made him more influential, not less."},{"code":"33-2 — 3 Malcolm X and Black Power, 1963–70: Reasons for the emergence of Black Power","h":"Black Power: what disappointment produced","t":"Black Power grew out of victory that had not been felt. The Acts of 1964 and 1965 changed almost nothing in the northern cities, the Democratic convention had turned away the Mississippi Freedom Democratic Party in 1964, and Watts burned in August 1965, five days after the Voting Rights Act was signed. Stokely Carmichael was elected chairman of SNCC in May 1966, and during the March Against Fear, continued after James Meredith was shot on 6 June 1966, he told a rally at Greenwood, Mississippi on 16 June that what black people needed was Black Power. SNCC voted in December 1966 to expel its white members and moved away from nonviolence. On 16 October 1968 the sprinters Tommie Smith and John Carlos raised black-gloved fists on the medal podium at the Mexico City Olympics and were expelled from the Games, which carried the idea round the world in a single photograph. A top-level answer defines Black Power before judging it: pride, self-defence and control of black communities by black people, not one organisation and not simply a call to violence."},{"code":"33-2 — 3 Malcolm X and Black Power, 1963–70: The methods and achievements of the Black Panther movement","h":"The Black Panthers: breakfasts, patrols and the price of the image","t":"Huey Newton and Bobby Seale founded the Black Panther Party for Self-Defense in Oakland, California, in October 1966. Its Ten-Point Programme demanded work, decent housing, education that told the truth about black history, an end to police brutality and the release of black prisoners. Members carried law books and, at first lawfully, loaded guns, and followed police patrols through the black districts of Oakland to watch arrests being made. The party also ran free breakfast programmes that fed thousands of children every school day, free medical clinics and legal advice, and by 1968 to 1969 had chapters in cities across the country. The FBI's COINTELPRO operation set out to destroy it, and the Chicago leader Fred Hampton was shot dead in a police raid in December 1969. A top-level answer weighs the two halves against each other: the survival programmes were the real achievement, while the armed image recruited members and then gave the state its excuse."},{"code":"33-2 — 4 The civil rights movement, 1965–75: The riots of 1965–67 and the Kerner Report","h":"The cities burn, 1965 to 1967, and the report nobody acted on","t":"Watts in Los Angeles burned for six days from 11 August 1965, five days after the Voting Rights Act was signed: 34 people died, about a thousand were injured and some forty million dollars of property was destroyed. Newark followed in July 1967 with 26 dead, and Detroit later the same month with 43, a riot put down by paratroopers. Johnson appointed a commission under Governor Otto Kerner, whose report of 29 February 1968 blamed white racism, segregated housing, unemployment and hostile policing, and warned that the country was moving towards two societies, one black and one white, separate and unequal. It called for a huge programme of jobs, housing and schools. Johnson, absorbed by Vietnam and by the cost of it, shelved the recommendations. A top-level answer uses the riots as evidence about the limits of the legislation and the fate of the report as evidence about the limits of political will, rather than treating either as a story about disorder."},{"code":"33-2 — 4 The civil rights movement, 1965–75: King's campaign in the North and his assassination","h":"Chicago and Memphis: King beyond the South","t":"King moved into a decaying tenement on Chicago's West Side in January 1966 to lead the Chicago Freedom Movement against slum housing and the rents charged for it. He drew more than 30,000 people to a rally at Soldier Field in July, but when he marched through all-white Marquette Park on 5 August 1966 he was struck on the head by a stone and said afterwards that he had never seen mobs as hostile, even in Mississippi. The Summit Agreement reached with Mayor Daley on 26 August 1966 promised open housing and delivered almost nothing. King then opposed the Vietnam war publicly from April 1967, which cost him Johnson's ear, and began the Poor People's Campaign. He was shot dead on 4 April 1968 in Memphis, where he had gone to support striking sanitation workers; riots followed in more than a hundred cities, and Congress passed the Fair Housing Act within the week and Johnson signed it on 11 April 1968. A top-level answer explains why the North was harder: there was no segregation law to strike down, only jobs, mortgages, schools and police forces."},{"code":"33-2 — 4 The civil rights movement, 1965–75: The extent of progress in civil rights by 1975","h":"How far had civil rights advanced by 1975?","t":"By 1975 the legal position had been transformed. Segregation by law had gone, black registration in Mississippi had risen from under seven per cent of adults in 1964 to well over half by 1968, and the number of black elected officials in the United States, which had been tiny in 1965, passed 1,400 by 1970 and went on climbing. Southern schools desegregated faster between 1968 and 1972 than at any time before or since, helped by the Supreme Court's approval of bussing in 1971, and black Americans sat in Congress, in city halls and in southern sheriffs' offices. Against that, black unemployment stayed at roughly twice the white rate, black family income remained far below white, the northern ghettos were no less poor than in 1965, and the Kerner programme was never funded. A top-level answer never answers 'partly': it states the criterion it is judging by, usually rights against living conditions, and says which kind of change the evidence supports."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1: two inferences from Source A about the police","setup":"Source A in this room is a memorandum written by the manager of a department store in a city in North Carolina to his company's head office in March 1960. He reports that students from the Negro college have come to the lunch counter every day since the first week of February, twenty or thirty at a time; that they sit in silence in jackets and ties and read their textbooks while they wait to be served; that a group of white youths poured sugar and soft drinks over two of them and tore a girl's coat, and that neither student answered back; that the police stood in the doorway and afterwards arrested four of the Negro students for trespass, and that nobody else was taken away; that takings at the counter are now less than a quarter of what they were in January and white shoppers avoid the whole floor; and that he does not believe the protest will end while the counter stays closed to them, since the loss falls on the store and not on the city. The question is: 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about the way the police behaved during the sit-in. Complete the table below to explain your answer.' (4 marks)","steps":["Fix the writer's position before you read for content: a store manager reporting to head office wants to explain a fall in trade, not to accuse anyone, so what he says about the police is said in passing and is worth more for that.","Take the first inference from what the police did while the assault was going on: they stood in the doorway while sugar and soft drinks were poured over two students and a girl's coat was torn, so they were unwilling to protect peaceful protesters from attack.","Take the second from whom they removed: four of the Negro students were arrested for trespass and nobody else was taken away, so the law was being enforced against the victims and not against those who attacked them.","Check each pair before you write: the claim, which the source implies but never states, goes in the first box, and the words that force it go in the box for the supporting detail."],"cues":["Who wrote this, and who was meant to read it?","Where were the officers while the students were being attacked?","Who was taken away afterwards, and who was not?","Which box takes the claim and which the quotation?"]},{"title":"Question 3(a): judging Sources B and C for the enquiry actually named","setup":"Source B in this room is a letter written home to her parents in Ohio by a white college student volunteering in Mississippi in July 1964: a week of training in Ohio, mostly in how to fall and cover the head; nobody travelling alone or after dark since the three men went missing near Philadelphia, and telephone calls to the Jackson office every two hours; a Freedom School of twenty-six children and four grandmothers in the mornings, reading, arithmetic and the state constitution; the family she lodges with told by the man who owns their land that a family keeping a northern girl will not be kept on; eleven people taken to the courthouse, where the registrar gave each of them a section of the Mississippi constitution to explain and failed all but one, while a deputy photographed everyone who went in; and the line 'They will go back next week.' Source C is a confidential memorandum from the chief of police of a city in Alabama to the state governor's office, September 1963, setting out the methods that have worked: arrest early and in numbers so that the jail is full before the marchers reach the business district, because the organisers cannot find bail for hundreds; use the fire department's hoses, which clear a street faster than any number of officers, and the dogs to keep a crowd off the line; photograph every face and see that employers and landlords are given the names, since a man who loses his job seldom marches twice; and a warning that the newspapers and television cameras are the writer's chief difficulty. The question is: 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the part played by black Mississippians themselves in the campaigns of 1964? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context.' (8 marks)","steps":["Copy the enquiry to the top of your plan and let it select the material: the part played by local black people, so the courthouse queue and the family who took the volunteer in are central and the training in Ohio is not.","Take Source B's content for that enquiry: eleven people went to the courthouse knowing they would be photographed and failed, and 'They will go back next week', which shows the risk being carried by local people rather than by the visitors.","Weigh Source B's nature, origin and purpose together: a private letter to parents, written in the month it describes, has no reader to impress and every reason to be exact, although a newcomer of a few weeks sees only one county and one Freedom School.","Turn to Source C and say what it is useful for here even though it is about Alabama: its instruction to give employers and landlords the names of marchers explains precisely what the family in Source B was risking, so it tells you the price of the part local people played.","Bring contextual knowledge in on the sources rather than beside them: only about 6.7 per cent of black adults in Mississippi were registered in 1964, and of roughly 17,000 who tried that summer only about 1,600 were accepted, which is why the registrar's constitution test in Source B matters.","Finish with a judgement for this enquiry and not a general one: Source B is the more useful because it shows local people acting, while Source C is useful at one remove, as evidence of what made that action dangerous, and neither was written by a black Mississippian."],"cues":["What exactly is the enquiry into?","Which sentence of Source B shows local people taking the risk?","Why might a letter to parents be more exact than a published account?","Source C is about another state: what can it still tell you?","Which figure of your own explains the registrar's test?","Whose voice is missing from both sources?"]},{"title":"Question 2: getting a 12-mark 'Explain why' past the bullet points","setup":"The question is: 'Explain why Martin Luther King chose Birmingham for a campaign in 1963. You may use the following in your answer: • Bull Connor • the failure of the Albany campaign (1961 to 1962). You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks); on the paper Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b) and you answer one of them. The mark scheme caps an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content at a maximum of 8 marks and shuts Level 4 to it altogether. The fact files in this room supply the material: Birmingham as the most rigidly segregated large city in the South, Eugene 'Bull' Connor as Commissioner of Public Safety, King's arrest on 12 April 1963 and the Letter from Birmingham Jail, the children's marches from 2 May, the hoses and the dogs, the agreement of 10 May, and Kennedy's civil rights bill of June 1963.","steps":["Turn the second bullet into a causal claim rather than a heading: at Albany the police chief Laurie Pritchett had arrested demonstrators quietly and spread them among neighbouring jails, so there were no pictures and no crisis, and King came away with nothing.","Make the first bullet answer that failure: Birmingham offered the opposite kind of opponent in Bull Connor, whose use of force was certain and public, so the campaign was designed around the reaction it would provoke.","Add the reason of your own that neither bullet prompts: Birmingham was the most rigidly segregated large city in the South and had a strong local movement under Fred Shuttlesworth, so the SCLC would be invited in rather than resented, and a victory there would be worth more than a victory anywhere else.","Add a second reason of your own about the audience: a federal government that had so far moved only when forced had to be given something it could not ignore, and a downtown business district could be closed by boycott and by filling the jails.","Order the reasons so that they build rather than sit in a list, and close each paragraph on the question's own words, the choice of Birmingham, ending by saying which reason decided it."],"cues":["What went wrong at Albany, and whose doing was it?","What did Birmingham offer that Albany had not?","Which reason is prompted by neither bullet point?","Who was the campaign really aimed at?","What must the final sentence of the answer do?"]},{"title":"Question 3(b): finding the difference of view, not of detail","setup":"Interpretation 1 in this room, from a study of the civil rights campaigns of the 1960s by a historian writing in the 1990s, argues that peaceful protest did what anger could not: Birmingham in 1963 and Selma in 1965 put southern violence on the nation's television screens and left Congress with no honourable way of refusing, the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Voting Rights Act of 1965 followed, black registration in Mississippi rose 'from under a tenth to more than half' within three years, black candidates began winning office in counties where no black person had voted, and 'Black Power made a great deal of noise, but it changed no laws'. Interpretation 2, from a general history of black America since 1945 by a historian writing in the 2000s, argues that 'marching won the statute book and very little else': the laws ended segregation only where it was written down, in the South, King's Chicago campaign of 1966 produced an agreement that changed nothing, the fires of Watts, Newark and Detroit said what marches could not, the Kerner Commission warned in 1968 that the country was becoming two societies, and by 1975 black unemployment was still roughly twice the white rate. The question is: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. They give different views about the importance of the federal government in the changes of the years 1960 to 1975. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations.' (4 marks)","steps":["Reduce each interpretation to one sentence about what Washington did: the first says federal action, once forced, delivered the change; the second says federal action reached only as far as the written law.","Test that this really is a difference of view and not of fact: both accept that the two Acts were passed, so the disagreement is about what those Acts were worth, not about whether they happened.","Anchor each half of the difference in the interpretation's own words, 'forced the federal government to act' against 'won the statute book and very little else'.","Write it as one difference stated first and then supported from both interpretations, rather than as two summaries placed side by side."],"cues":["What does each writer say the government's action achieved?","Do they disagree about what happened or about what it was worth?","Which phrase from each carries the view?","What shape should the finished answer take?"]},{"title":"Question 3(c): explaining why two historians differ","setup":"The two interpretations are the ones set out in the previous example: Interpretation 1, from a study of the civil rights campaigns of the 1960s by a historian writing in the 1990s, which credits Birmingham and Selma with forcing the Acts of 1964 and 1965 and points to black registration in Mississippi rising 'from under a tenth to more than half'; and Interpretation 2, from a general history of black America since 1945 by a historian writing in the 2000s, which says that 'marching won the statute book and very little else', that Chicago in 1966 changed nothing, and that black unemployment in 1975 was still roughly twice the white rate. Sources B and C may be used to help explain the answer: Source B is a volunteer's letter from Mississippi in July 1964 describing the courthouse, the registrar's constitution test and the deputy photographing those who went in; Source C is an Alabama police chief's memorandum of September 1963 on filling the jails, using hoses and dogs, and passing marchers' names to employers. The question is: 'Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about the importance of the federal government in the changes of the years 1960 to 1975. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer.' (4 marks)","steps":["Fix the reason before writing anything: the two historians are asking different questions, so they choose different evidence and reach different conclusions, and neither is therefore simply wrong.","Show the reason at work in Interpretation 1: a study of the campaigns judges them by what they set out to win, so its evidence is legislation and registration figures, of the kind that the obstacles in Sources B and C were designed to prevent.","Show it at work in Interpretation 2: a general history of black America since 1945 judges the period by how people lived across the whole country, so its evidence is wages, housing and policing, which no Act of Congress touched directly.","Close by naming what follows from the reason: measured against the southern courthouse the federal government looks decisive, and measured against a Chicago tenement it does not."],"cues":["What kind of book is each interpretation taken from?","What would a historian of the campaigns count as evidence?","What would a historian of black life count instead?","What does the reason explain about the two conclusions?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Why did King and the SCLC expect Birmingham in 1963 to succeed where Albany had failed?","a":"Because Bull Connor, the Commissioner of Public Safety, was certain to use force in public, so the campaign would produce the television pictures and the national crisis that Albany's quiet mass arrests had denied it."},{"q":"Why is a one-sided document like Source C still useful for an enquiry into resistance to civil rights protest?","a":"Because the enquiry is into what the authorities did and why, and a confidential memorandum between officials is that policy being set out frankly; its purpose, to advise the governor, is what makes it candid rather than what spoils it."},{"q":"Name one thing Black Power achieved that the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Voting Rights Act of 1965 did not.","a":"It changed how black Americans saw themselves and their history, encouraging pride, black studies and community control; or the Black Panthers' free breakfast and clinic programmes, which met needs no statute addressed."},{"q":"In what sense did the Watts riot of August 1965 make an argument about the Voting Rights Act?","a":"It began five days after the Act was signed, in a city where black Americans could already vote, so it showed that the legal victories of 1964 and 1965 had not touched the housing, jobs and policing that shaped life outside the South."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 3 Section A (16 marks): Question 1 prints Source A and asks 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about...' in a table of two boxes each (4 marks); Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b), and you answer one of them: each is 'Explain why...' with two stimulus points and 'You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks).","Paper 3 Section B (36 marks), all on Question 3: (a) 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into...?' (8); (b) 'What is the main difference between these views?' (4); (c) 'Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about...' (4); (d) 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about...?' (16, plus up to 4 for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology).","The stimulus-point caps on the 12-mark 'Explain why...': 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'. There is no cap at Level 2. Question 3(d) prints no stimulus points, so no cap applies to it.","Timing on Paper 3: 1 hour 30 minutes for 52 marks, so about a minute a mark plus thinking time. Roughly 5 minutes on Question 1, 18 on Question 2, 12 on 3(a), 6 each on 3(b) and 3(c), and about 40 minutes on 3(d) including the final check for the SPaG marks, which leaves a few minutes in hand.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose, and usefulness is always judged for the enquiry the question names, never in general. Contextual knowledge earns nothing unless it is used on the sources or the interpretations.","The dates that anchor this key topic: 1 February 1960, the Greensboro sit-in; 4 May 1961, the Freedom Rides begin; 30 September 1962, the riot at Ole Miss; 28 August 1963, the March on Washington; 2 July 1964, the Civil Rights Act; 7 March 1965, Bloody Sunday at Selma; 6 August 1965, the Voting Rights Act; 4 April 1968, King's assassination."],"questions":[{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about the students taking part in the sit-in. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for each valid inference, up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail from Source A. Credit planning and discipline, a deliberate refusal to retaliate, or persistence over weeks.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: the sit-in was carefully planned and disciplined. Details in the source that tell me this: the students come 'twenty or thirty at a time', sit in silence in jackets and ties, and read their textbooks while they wait to be served. (ii) What I can infer: they had agreed beforehand that they would not respond to violence. Details in the source that tell me this: when white youths poured sugar and soft drinks over two of them and tore a girl's coat, 'neither student answered back', and the students 'have come in every day since the first week of February'.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["An inference is something the source makes certain without stating it; ask what the students must have agreed among themselves before they ever walked in.","Read the memorandum twice: once for what the students do while they are waiting, and once for what they do when they are attacked.","Give each answer in two parts, a claim the source never states and then the exact words that force it."],"codes":["33-2 — 1 Developments, 1960–62: The significance of Greensboro and the sit-in movement"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about the effect of the sit-in on the store. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for each valid inference, up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail from Source A. Credit serious financial loss, loss spreading beyond the counter, or the manager expecting to have to give way.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: the sit-in was costing the store a great deal of money. Details in the source that tell me this: takings at the lunch counter are 'less than a quarter of what they were in January', and white shoppers now avoid the whole floor, not only the counter. (ii) What I can infer: the manager expected the store to have to desegregate. Details in the source that tell me this: he writes that he does not think the protest will end while the counter stays closed to the students, and that 'the loss falls on us, not on the city'.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Follow the money through the memorandum: ask what the manager is really reporting to head office.","Notice the difference between what he states about takings and what he predicts in his last sentence.","Set out a claim the source implies, then the phrase that makes it certain, for each of your two answers."],"codes":["33-2 — 1 Developments, 1960–62: The significance of Greensboro and the sit-in movement"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. They give different views about the achievements of the civil rights movement in the years 1960 to 1975. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4) needs a key difference of view supported from both: Interpretation 1 sees peaceful protest winning decisive federal change, Interpretation 2 sees narrow legal gains that left most black Americans' lives untouched. Surface detail only stays in Level 1.","sol":"The main difference is over how much the movement actually achieved. Interpretation 1 treats it as a success: peaceful protest 'forced the federal government to act', Birmingham and Selma were followed by the Acts of 1964 and 1965, black registration in Mississippi rose 'from under a tenth to more than half', and black candidates won office where none had voted. Interpretation 2 treats the gains as narrow: 'marching won the statute book and very little else', the laws ended segregation only 'where it was written down, in the South', Chicago in 1966 'achieved an agreement that changed nothing', and by 1975 black unemployment was still roughly twice the white rate.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Reduce each interpretation to a single sentence beginning 'The movement achieved...' and then compare the two sentences.","Check that your difference is one of view: both writers agree the two Acts were passed, so look for what each says they were worth.","State the difference first in one sentence, then support each half with words taken from its own interpretation."],"codes":["33-2 — 4 The civil rights movement, 1965–75: The extent of progress in civil rights by 1975","33-2 — 2 Peaceful protests and their impact, 1963–65: The impact of peaceful protest and the roles of Kennedy and Johnson","33-2 — 3 Malcolm X and Black Power, 1963–70: Reasons for the emergence of Black Power"]},{"q":"Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about the achievements of the civil rights movement in the years 1960 to 1975. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4) needs one explained reason, effectively substantiated: for example that the two historians are measuring different things because they set out to write different books, so they select different evidence.","sol":"One reason is that the two historians are measuring different things, because they set out to write different books. Interpretation 1 is a study of the campaigns of the 1960s, so it judges them by what they aimed at, laws and the vote, and its evidence is the kind of southern obstruction seen in Source B, where a registrar fails ten of eleven applicants on the state constitution while a deputy photographs them, and in Source C, where the police chief has marchers' names passed to their employers. Interpretation 2 is a general history of black America, so it measures housing, schools, policing and unemployment across the whole country, where no Act reached.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Historians differ legitimately when they ask different questions of the past; look at what kind of book each interpretation comes from.","Ask what each writer would count as evidence of success, and what that choice makes visible or invisible.","Name one reason, then show it at work in both interpretations, using a source where it illustrates the kind of evidence involved."],"codes":["33-2 — 4 The civil rights movement, 1965–75: The extent of progress in civil rights by 1975","33-2 — 2 Peaceful protests and their impact, 1963–65: The impact of peaceful protest and the roles of Kennedy and Johnson"]},{"q":"2(a) Explain why the sit-in movement spread so quickly across the southern states in 1960. You may use the following in your answer: • Greensboro (February 1960) • the founding of SNCC (1960). You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise knowledge - the simplicity of the tactic, the black college network and nonviolent training, SNCC from April 1960, economic pressure and press coverage. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The sit-in spread so fast because it was a tactic anyone could copy, because a network already existed to carry it, and because it visibly began to work. The first reason is the simplicity of the method. On 1 February 1960 four students from the North Carolina Agricultural and Technical College sat at the whites-only counter of Woolworth's in Greensboro and stayed when they were refused service. The protest needed no money, no lawyers and no permission from anybody: it needed only students willing to sit still and be insulted. Within a fortnight it had been copied in dozens of southern towns, and within a year more than a hundred cities had seen sit-ins, with some three thousand arrests. The second reason is organisation. The black colleges of the South gave the movement a ready-made network of students who knew one another, and in Nashville James Lawson had already trained students in nonviolent discipline, which is why protesters could absorb abuse without retaliating and so keep the moral advantage. In April 1960 Ella Baker gathered the student groups at Shaw University in Raleigh and the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee was founded, so the spreading stopped being accidental and became coordinated. The third reason, which neither bullet point prompts, is that the tactic succeeded, and success recruits. A store that closed its counter lost its white trade as well as its black customers, and the chains discovered that the loss fell on them rather than on the segregationist city governments; the Greensboro counter was desegregated on 25 July 1960. Newspaper photographs of well-dressed students being jeered at, and arrested while their attackers went free, carried the argument far beyond the South. A method anyone could use, a body to coordinate it and proof that it worked turned a local protest into a regional movement inside a single year.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what makes any tactic spread: how easy it is to copy, who carries it, and whether it is seen to work.","Plan three reasons of different kinds, one about the method, one about organisation and one about results, so the answer analyses rather than lists.","Open on what four students actually did in Greensboro and why so little was needed to do it."],"codes":["33-2 — 1 Developments, 1960–62: The significance of Greensboro and the sit-in movement"]},{"q":"2(b) Explain why the Freedom Rides of 1961 forced the federal government to act. You may use the following in your answer: • the Anniston bus (May 1961) • the Kennedy administration. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed - the rides as a deliberate test of federal law, Anniston and Birmingham, Alabama's refusal to protect the riders, the Cold War audience, the ICC order of 1 November 1961. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The Freedom Rides forced Washington to act because they were designed to put the federal government in a position where doing nothing was impossible. The first reason is that they tested federal law rather than state law. Thirteen riders, seven black and six white, left Washington on 4 May 1961, organised by the Congress of Racial Equality under James Farmer, to travel through the South and use the terminals that a Supreme Court ruling of 1960 had already declared could not be segregated. If they were attacked, it was federal law that was being defied, so the Justice Department could not call it a local matter. The second reason is the violence itself, and where it happened. On 14 May a Klan mob stopped the Greyhound bus outside Anniston, slashed its tyres and firebombed it, beating the riders as they escaped, and the second bus was met in Birmingham by a mob the police left undisturbed for about a quarter of an hour. When the mob attacked again in Montgomery on 20 May, a Justice Department official sent to observe was knocked unconscious in the street. Alabama's authorities had shown that they would not or could not protect travellers, which left only federal power. The third reason, which neither bullet prompts, is the audience. Photographs of a burning American bus went round the world at the height of the Cold War, when the United States was arguing that its system was better than the Soviet one, and newly independent African states were watching. The fourth reason is that the riders refused to stop: when CORE withdrew, SNCC students from Nashville continued, and wave after wave filled the Mississippi jails through the summer, so the embarrassment would not simply fade. Robert Kennedy sent federal marshals to Montgomery and then pressed the Interstate Commerce Commission, whose order banning segregation in interstate terminals took effect on 1 November 1961.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the riders were actually testing, and why that made the issue federal rather than local.","Separate the violence from its audience: one created the crisis, the other made ignoring it costly.","Begin with the purpose of the ride on 4 May 1961, not with the journey itself."],"codes":["33-2 — 1 Developments, 1960–62: The Freedom Riders"]},{"q":"2(a) Explain why the federal government had to intervene at the University of Mississippi in 1962. You may use the following in your answer: • Governor Ross Barnett • the court order admitting James Meredith. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed - a federal court order defied by a state governor, Barnett as registrar, the riot of 30 September, two deaths, Kennedy's use of marshals and then troops. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Washington had to intervene at Oxford, Mississippi because a state governor set himself openly against a federal court, and because no other force in the state would enforce the court's order. The first reason is the nature of the order itself. James Meredith, an air force veteran, had won a federal court ruling in 1962 that the University of Mississippi must admit him. A court order is not a request, and if a state could ignore one there was no point in any of the desegregation rulings since Brown in 1954, so the authority of the federal courts, and not only Meredith's place, was at stake. The second reason is Ross Barnett's open defiance. The governor went to the campus in person to turn Meredith away, had himself appointed university registrar so that he could do it lawfully in state terms, and told the state on television that Mississippi would not surrender. When a state's own chief executive is the obstacle, state law enforcement cannot be used, so only federal officers were left. The third reason, which the bullet points do not prompt, is the scale of the violence on the night of 30 September 1962. A mob of thousands besieged the federal marshals escorting Meredith; two men were killed, a French journalist and a local workman, and more than three hundred people were injured. Marshals with tear gas could not hold the campus, so President Kennedy sent regular army troops, and Meredith registered on the morning of 1 October. The fourth reason is political. Kennedy had been elected narrowly in 1960 and had moved slowly on civil rights, preferring negotiation to confrontation with southern Democrats; Barnett removed that option by making the choice public. Meredith graduated in August 1963, protected by soldiers throughout, which shows both the reach of federal power and its cost.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["The question is about necessity: ask what would have happened if Washington had stood back.","Distinguish the legal reason from the practical one, and then from the political one.","Open on what a federal court had actually ordered and why that order could not simply be ignored."],"codes":["33-2 — 1 Developments, 1960–62: The James Meredith case (1962)","33-2 — 2 Peaceful protests and their impact, 1963–65: The impact of peaceful protest and the roles of Kennedy and Johnson"]},{"q":"2(b) Explain why the Civil Rights Act was passed in 1964. You may use the following in your answer: • Birmingham (1963) • the March on Washington (August 1963). You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed - Birmingham's televised violence, the March on Washington, Kennedy's bill of June 1963 and his death, Johnson's handling of the Senate filibuster. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The Civil Rights Act was passed because protest created a crisis that Congress could not ignore, and then because a president who understood the Senate turned that crisis into votes. The first reason is Birmingham. King and the SCLC chose the city in April 1963 precisely because Eugene 'Bull' Connor, its Commissioner of Public Safety, could be relied on to react; from 2 May schoolchildren marched in their thousands and Connor answered with fire hoses and police dogs in front of the cameras. The pictures were shown across the country and abroad, the city's trade collapsed, and an agreement to desegregate the lunch counters and hire black staff was signed on 10 May. Kennedy, who had moved cautiously until then, sent a civil rights bill to Congress in June 1963. The second reason is the March on Washington. On 28 August about 250,000 people, a large minority of them white, gathered peacefully at the Lincoln Memorial, and King's 'I have a dream' speech gave the bill a moral case that was hard to answer. Where Birmingham made a bill necessary, Washington made it respectable, and it showed northern congressmen that supporting it was popular. The third reason, which neither bullet prompts, is what happened in Washington itself. Kennedy was assassinated on 22 November 1963, and Johnson, a southerner who had sat in Congress since the 1930s and had led the Senate as majority leader, deliberately made the bill a memorial to him. He knew the Senate, where southern senators filibustered for weeks to talk the bill to death, and he assembled the votes, with Republican help, to close the debate by a cloture vote in June 1964. He signed the Act on 2 July 1964. Protest supplied the crisis and the argument, but the Act existed because a president spent his own political capital on it in the weeks when that crisis was still fresh.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Separate what made a law necessary from what made it possible to pass.","Two of your reasons should be about protest and at least one about Congress itself.","Begin with why Birmingham was chosen and what its pictures did to public opinion."],"codes":["33-2 — 2 Peaceful protests and their impact, 1963–65: King and the peace marches of 1963","33-2 — 2 Peaceful protests and their impact, 1963–65: The impact of peaceful protest and the roles of Kennedy and Johnson"]},{"q":"2(a) Explain why Black Power grew in importance in the mid-1960s. You may use the following in your answer: • the Watts riot (1965) • Stokely Carmichael. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed - the northern cities untouched by the 1964 and 1965 Acts, Watts, the MFDP rejection of 1964, Malcolm X's ideas and death, Carmichael at Greenwood in June 1966. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Black Power grew because the movement's greatest legal victories left most black Americans where they were, and because an alternative set of ideas was already in circulation to explain that. The first reason is the gap between the law and daily life. The Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Voting Rights Act of 1965 dismantled segregation as it was written down in the South, but in the northern and western cities, where millions of black Americans now lived, the problems were housing, wages, schools and policing, none of which any statute touched. Watts in Los Angeles proved the point brutally: it burned for six days from 11 August 1965, five days after the Voting Rights Act was signed, leaving 34 dead and about forty million dollars of damage, in a city where black residents had long been able to vote. The second reason is disillusion with white allies inside the movement itself. Freedom Summer in 1964 had cost the lives of James Chaney, Andrew Goodman and Michael Schwerner, and at the Democratic convention in Atlantic City that August the Mississippi Freedom Democratic Party was offered only two token seats and rejected them. Many young activists concluded that patience and integration had been tried and had been met with a compromise. The third reason, which neither bullet prompts, is that a body of ideas was waiting. Malcolm X had argued for years for separation, black pride and self-defence rather than pleading for acceptance, and his assassination on 21 February 1965 turned those arguments into an inheritance. The fourth reason is leadership and a phrase. Stokely Carmichael, elected chairman of SNCC in May 1966, called for Black Power at a rally in Greenwood, Mississippi on 16 June 1966 during the March Against Fear; SNCC voted that December to expel its white members, and the slogan spread faster than any organisation could have done. The cities gave the grievance, the convention gave the disappointment, Malcolm X gave the vocabulary, and Carmichael gave it a name.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the Acts of 1964 and 1965 did not change, and for whom.","Ideas need a grievance, a disappointment and a spokesman; build one reason around each.","Open on the distance between the statute book and life in a northern or western city."],"codes":["33-2 — 3 Malcolm X and Black Power, 1963–70: Reasons for the emergence of Black Power","33-2 — 3 Malcolm X and Black Power, 1963–70: Malcolm X, his beliefs and methods","33-2 — 4 The civil rights movement, 1965–75: The riots of 1965–67 and the Kerner Report"]},{"q":"2(b) Explain why the civil rights movement achieved less in the northern cities than in the southern states. You may use the following in your answer: • the Chicago campaign (1966) • housing in the northern cities. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed - de facto rather than legal segregation, Daley's tactics and the Summit Agreement, white hostility at Marquette Park, methods designed for southern sheriffs, the riots costing northern white support. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The movement achieved less in the North because the target there was not a law, and the methods that had beaten southern segregation had nothing to strike at. The first reason is the nature of northern segregation. In the South it was written down: signs, statutes and registrars, so a court ruling or an Act of Congress could remove it, and did in 1964 and 1965. In Chicago, Detroit or Los Angeles nothing was written down. Black families were confined to particular districts by the price of housing, by estate agents, by banks that would not lend and by white residents who moved out, and none of that could be declared unconstitutional. The second reason is the quality of the opposition. Mayor Richard Daley of Chicago did not behave like Bull Connor. When King moved into a West Side tenement in January 1966 and led the Chicago Freedom Movement, Daley conceded in words, avoided televised brutality, and signed the Summit Agreement of 26 August 1966 promising open housing, which was then not enforced. There were no pictures, so there was no national crisis and no federal bill. The third reason, which neither bullet prompts, is that northern white opinion, which had supported desegregation in Alabama, turned when the demand came to its own streets. King was struck on the head by a stone marching through Marquette Park on 5 August 1966 and said he had never seen mobs as hostile, even in Mississippi. The fourth reason is that the riots of 1965 to 1967, in Watts, Newark and Detroit, frightened that same white opinion, while the Kerner Report of February 1968, which named white racism and called for a huge programme of jobs and housing, was shelved by a president absorbed by Vietnam. What the North needed was money and enforcement over many years, not a statute passed in a single summer, and that was what no campaign could force.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what exactly the movement had defeated in the South, and then whether the same thing existed in Chicago.","Compare the opponents as well as the problems: what did Daley do that Connor did not?","Begin by naming the difference between segregation written in law and segregation produced by money and custom."],"codes":["33-2 — 4 The civil rights movement, 1965–75: King's campaign in the North and his assassination","33-2 — 4 The civil rights movement, 1965–75: The riots of 1965–67 and the Kerner Report","33-2 — 4 The civil rights movement, 1965–75: The extent of progress in civil rights by 1975"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the methods used by the authorities in the southern states to resist civil rights protest in the years 1963–64? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): utility judged for this enquiry, reasoning from nature, origin and purpose, with context used to interpret. Credit Source C on mass arrest, hoses, dogs and economic pressure, Source B on the registrar's test and surveillance at the courthouse.","sol":"Source C is the more useful of the two, because it is official policy set out plainly. A police chief writing confidentially to his governor in September 1963 has no reason to disguise anything: he advises arresting 'early and in numbers, so that the jail is filled before the marchers reach the business district', because the organisers cannot find bail for hundreds; he prefers fire hoses, which have cleared a street 'faster than any number of officers', and dogs to keep a crowd off the line; and he recommends photographing every face and giving the names to employers and landlords, since 'a man who loses his job seldom marches twice'. That last method left no photographs, so it is easily missed. The provenance is what makes the source strong: a confidential memorandum between officials is written to be acted on, not to persuade the public, although he naturally presents himself as competent and blames outside organisers. My own knowledge confirms it, since Birmingham in the spring of 1963 saw exactly the hoses and dogs he describes. Source B is useful for the quieter methods used against voting: a registrar who 'gave each of them a section of the Mississippi constitution to explain and failed all but one', a deputy photographing everyone who entered, and a landowner threatening the family who housed a volunteer. As a private letter written in the month it describes it is candid, though it covers one county over a few weeks.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Usefulness is always usefulness for something: copy out the enquiry before you look at either source.","Handle each source in turn as content, then provenance, then the context that confirms or qualifies it, and only then compare them.","Start by deciding which source was written by the authorities themselves, and open with a judgement rather than a description."],"codes":["33-2 — 2 Peaceful protests and their impact, 1963–65: Freedom Summer and the Mississippi murders","33-2 — 1 Developments, 1960–62: The Freedom Riders","33-2 — 2 Peaceful protests and their impact, 1963–65: King and the peace marches of 1963"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the experience of the volunteers who went to Mississippi during Freedom Summer in 1964? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): criteria-based judgement with developed reasoning on how provenance affects content. Credit Source B on training, fear after the murders, Freedom Schools and registration; qualify Source C, which is about Alabama in 1963 and not about volunteers at all.","sol":"Source B is directly useful, because it is a volunteer's own account written during the summer it describes. It gives the preparation, a week of training in Ohio 'mostly about how to fall and cover your head'; the fear after the three men went missing near Philadelphia in June, so that 'nobody here travels alone or after dark'; the daily work, a Freedom School of twenty-six children and four grandmothers in the mornings and the farms in the afternoons; and the courthouse, where the registrar failed ten of eleven applicants on the state constitution. Its provenance strengthens all of this: a private letter to parents is written to be understood rather than to persuade, and has no censor, although a writer may play down danger to reassure her family, and one volunteer in one county for a few weeks cannot speak for the thousand who went. My own knowledge supports it: about 6.7 per cent of black adults in Mississippi were registered in 1964, and the bodies of Chaney, Goodman and Schwerner were found under an earth dam on 4 August. Source C is useful only at one remove. An Alabama police chief's memorandum of September 1963 concerns a different state some ten months earlier and says nothing about volunteers, but its advice that marchers' names be passed to employers and landlords explains exactly what the family housing the writer of Source B was risking. For this enquiry Source B is far the stronger, and Source C is background to it.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Ask of each source whether it was written by, about, or merely near the people the enquiry names.","A source can still be useful for an enquiry it never mentions, if it explains the conditions the enquiry is about; say what it lets you infer.","Open by judging which of the two is closer to the enquiry, and give the reason in the same sentence."],"codes":["33-2 — 2 Peaceful protests and their impact, 1963–65: Freedom Summer and the Mississippi murders","33-2 — 2 Peaceful protests and their impact, 1963–65: The impact of peaceful protest and the roles of Kennedy and Johnson"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about how much the Civil Rights Act (1964) and the Voting Rights Act (1965) changed the lives of black Americans? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views evaluated, how each is conveyed analysed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit Watts, Chicago and the Kerner Report; against, registration figures, black officeholding and southern school desegregation.","sol":"Interpretation 2 argues that 'marching won the statute book and very little else': the Acts of 1964 and 1965 ended segregation 'where it was written down, in the South', and left untouched the cities where most black Americans lived, so that by 1975 black unemployment was still roughly twice the white rate. Interpretation 1 answers that the same laws transformed the South, since registration in Mississippi rose 'from under a tenth to more than half' and black candidates began winning office where none had voted. The criterion has to be stated before anything can be judged: a law can change what a citizen is entitled to, or it can change what a citizen's life is like, and these two writers are counting different things. On rights, Interpretation 2 is too severe. The Civil Rights Act of 2 July 1964 made segregation in public places and discrimination in employment illegal, and the Voting Rights Act of 6 August 1965 suspended literacy tests and sent federal examiners into the South, where the constitution test described in Source B had failed ten applicants out of eleven the summer before. Black registration in Mississippi rose from about 6.7 per cent of adults in 1964 to well over half by 1968; the number of black elected officials in the United States, tiny in 1965, passed 1,400 by 1970; and southern schools desegregated faster between 1968 and 1972 than at any time before or since. For a black Mississippian these were not paper changes, and they removed the threat of losing a job for registering that Source C recommends so calmly. On conditions, Interpretation 2 is largely right, and its evidence holds. Watts burned for six days from 11 August 1965, five days after the Voting Rights Act was signed and in a city where black Americans already voted, leaving 34 dead; Newark and Detroit followed in July 1967 with 26 and 43 dead. King's Chicago campaign of 1966 produced the Summit Agreement of 26 August, which promised open housing and was not enforced, and he was stoned in Marquette Park by a crowd he called more hostile than any in Mississippi. The Kerner Report of 29 February 1968 warned that the country was becoming two societies, separate and unequal, and recommended a huge programme of jobs and housing that was never funded. Neither Act said anything about a mortgage, a school district or a police department outside the South. The two writers also convey their views by choosing different measures, which is part of the disagreement rather than a flaw in either: Interpretation 1 counts registrations and offices, the things campaigns aimed at, while Interpretation 2 counts unemployment and housing, the things people lived in. My judgement is that Interpretation 2 is right about the limits and wrong about the scale. The Acts were the difference between citizenship and its absence for millions of southerners, which is not 'very little'; but they were designed for a written system of segregation, and they could do nothing about a northern city where nothing was written down. The laws changed what black Americans were entitled to, everywhere, and changed how they actually lived mainly in the South.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first what Interpretation 2 is actually claiming, and name the test you will judge it by before you agree or disagree.","Use Interpretation 1 as your case against, and support both sides with dated detail rather than with the interpretations restated.","Open by putting Interpretation 2's claim in your own words and distinguishing rights from living conditions."],"codes":["33-2 — 2 Peaceful protests and their impact, 1963–65: The impact of peaceful protest and the roles of Kennedy and Johnson","33-2 — 4 The civil rights movement, 1965–75: The extent of progress in civil rights by 1975","33-2 — 4 The civil rights movement, 1965–75: King's campaign in the North and his assassination"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the importance of Black Power in the years 1965 to 1975? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views weighed, how they are conveyed analysed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit the Panthers' programmes, pride and the Olympic salute; against, Interpretation 1's point that Black Power changed no laws and may have cost white support.","sol":"Interpretation 2 ends by saying that 'Black Power spoke for the millions the legislation never reached', which makes it the authentic voice of black America outside the South rather than a distraction. Interpretation 1 gives the opposite verdict in a single line: 'Black Power made a great deal of noise, but it changed no laws.' The criterion decides the argument, so it must be named at the start: if importance means legislation, Interpretation 1 must win, and if it means what happened to black communities and to how black Americans saw themselves, Interpretation 2 has much the better case. There is strong support for Interpretation 2. The grievances it points to were real and untouched by statute: Watts burned for six days from 11 August 1965, five days after the Voting Rights Act was signed; Newark and Detroit followed in July 1967 with 26 and 43 dead; and the Kerner Report of 29 February 1968 blamed white racism, segregated housing and hostile policing. Black Power addressed exactly that ground. Stokely Carmichael, elected chairman of SNCC in May 1966, called for it at Greenwood, Mississippi on 16 June 1966, and it meant pride, self-defence and black control of black communities. The Black Panther Party, founded by Huey Newton and Bobby Seale in Oakland in October 1966, put that into practice with its Ten-Point Programme, its patrols watching police arrests, its free breakfast programmes feeding thousands of children each school day and its free clinics: services no Act of Congress was providing. The change in self-image was equally real, from black studies courses to the raised fists of Tommie Smith and John Carlos at the Mexico City Olympics on 16 October 1968, an image that went round the world. Interpretation 1's objection is narrower than it looks but is not wrong. Black Power passed no legislation, and the great legal changes were won before it by the methods it rejected. It can also be argued that it cost the movement allies: white northern support fell as the slogan spread, Johnson's attention went to Vietnam, and Nixon won in 1968 on a promise of law and order. The Panthers were also vulnerable in a way the SCLC was not; the FBI's COINTELPRO set out to destroy them, and Fred Hampton was shot dead in a police raid in December 1969, so armed self-presentation attracted members and then attracted the state. The two interpretations convey their views by the words they choose. Interpretation 1 uses 'noise', which measures a movement by its output in statutes and finds nothing; Interpretation 2 uses 'spoke for', which measures it by whom it represented. On balance I agree with Interpretation 2, with one qualification. Black Power was important for what the civil rights laws could not do: it gave the northern ghettos a politics, it built institutions that fed and treated people, and it changed black self-respect permanently. But Interpretation 2 slides from importance to achievement. Speaking for people is not the same as improving their position, and by 1975 the housing, schools and unemployment that produced Black Power were very largely as they had been in 1965.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Importance is not the same as success: decide which of the two you are being asked to judge, and say so in your opening.","Set the Panthers' survival programmes against Interpretation 1's test of changing laws, and support both with dated detail.","Begin by stating what Interpretation 2 claims Black Power did, and for whom."],"codes":["33-2 — 3 Malcolm X and Black Power, 1963–70: Reasons for the emergence of Black Power","33-2 — 3 Malcolm X and Black Power, 1963–70: The methods and achievements of the Black Panther movement","33-2 — 3 Malcolm X and Black Power, 1963–70: Malcolm X, his beliefs and methods"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the position of black Americans in the northern cities by 1975? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views evaluated, how each is conveyed analysed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit Chicago 1966, the riots, the shelved Kerner Report and the unemployment gap; against, national laws, the Fair Housing Act and black city politics.","sol":"Interpretation 2 claims that the laws of 1964 and 1965 'left untouched the cities where most black Americans now lived', that King's Chicago campaign 'achieved an agreement that changed nothing', and that the fires of Watts, Newark and Detroit said what marching could not, because 'bad housing, bad schools and hostile police were not matters of law'. Interpretation 1 does not deny the northern cities so much as look away from them, treating the forcing of federal action as the story. The criterion I will use is whether the position of black Americans in the northern cities had materially improved by 1975, in work, housing, schooling and policing. On that test Interpretation 2 is largely right. Northern segregation was never written down, so there was no statute to repeal: black families were held in particular districts by price, by estate agents, by banks that would not lend and by white flight. King moved into a West Side tenement in January 1966 and drew more than 30,000 people to Soldier Field in July, but the Summit Agreement he signed with Mayor Daley on 26 August 1966 promised open housing and was not enforced, and when he marched through Marquette Park on 5 August he was struck on the head by a stone and said the crowds were more hostile than any he had met in Mississippi. The riots then made the point that reform had not: Watts for six days from 11 August 1965 with 34 dead, Newark in July 1967 with 26, Detroit later that month with 43. The Kerner Report of 29 February 1968 named white racism, bad housing and hostile policing, warned of two societies 'separate and unequal', and recommended a great programme of jobs and housing; Johnson, consumed by Vietnam, shelved it. Black unemployment was still roughly twice the white rate in 1975. There is a case on the other side, and Interpretation 1's emphasis on federal power supplies it. The Civil Rights Act of 1964 applied nationally, so employment discrimination and segregated public facilities became unlawful in Chicago as well as in Selma, and the Fair Housing Act, signed on 11 April 1968 in the week after King's murder in Memphis, outlawed discrimination in the sale and renting of housing, which was precisely the northern grievance. Black political power in the cities also grew: black mayors were elected in Cleveland and Gary in 1967 and in Detroit and Los Angeles in 1973, and the number of black elected officials nationally passed 1,400 by 1970 and kept rising. Interpretation 2's 'untouched' is therefore too absolute. The two writers convey their views through what they choose to count. Interpretation 1 counts what the law now forbids, Interpretation 2 counts what people's lives are actually like, and the same decade looks like victory or like stalemate depending on that choice. I agree with Interpretation 2 substantially. Rights were won in the northern cities as elsewhere, and black Americans began to govern some of those cities; but a right to housing that is not enforced changes little, and the Fair Housing Act was weakly policed. By 1975 the ghettos were no less poor, no better schooled and no better policed than in 1965, which is the test that matters for the position of the people living in them.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Name the test of 'position' you will use before you start, and keep every paragraph measured against it.","Interpretation 2's word is 'untouched': find the national laws that touched the North, and then ask what they actually delivered.","Open on why the northern cities could not be changed by repealing anything."],"codes":["33-2 — 4 The civil rights movement, 1965–75: King's campaign in the North and his assassination","33-2 — 4 The civil rights movement, 1965–75: The riots of 1965–67 and the Kerner Report","33-2 — 4 The civil rights movement, 1965–75: The extent of progress in civil rights by 1975"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date did four students begin the sit-in at the Woolworth's lunch counter in Greensboro?","a":"1 February 1960."},{"q":"Which Act did President Johnson sign on 6 August 1965, and what did it suspend?","a":"The Voting Rights Act, which suspended literacy tests and sent federal examiners into the southern states."},{"q":"Who founded the Black Panther Party for Self-Defense, and in which city and year?","a":"Huey Newton and Bobby Seale, in Oakland, California, in October 1966."},{"q":"What did the Kerner Report of 29 February 1968 warn that the United States was becoming?","a":"Two societies, one black and one white, separate and unequal."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The Greensboro sit-in, 1 February 1960","open":false,"code":"33-2 — 1 Developments, 1960–62: The significance of Greensboro and the sit-in movement","back":"Four students from the North Carolina Agricultural and Technical College sat at Woolworth's whites-only lunch counter and stayed when refused service. The counter was desegregated on 25 July 1960."},{"front":"SNCC","open":false,"code":"33-2 — 1 Developments, 1960–62: The significance of Greensboro and the sit-in movement","back":"The Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee, founded in April 1960 at Shaw University, Raleigh, after Ella Baker gathered the sit-in groups. It coordinated student direct action across the South."},{"front":"The Freedom Rides, 1961","open":false,"code":"33-2 — 1 Developments, 1960–62: The Freedom Riders","back":"Thirteen riders left Washington on 4 May 1961 for CORE to test desegregation of interstate terminals. A Klan mob firebombed their bus outside Anniston on 14 May; an ICC order took effect on 1 November 1961."},{"front":"James Meredith at the University of Mississippi, 1962","open":false,"code":"33-2 — 1 Developments, 1960–62: The James Meredith case (1962)","back":"Governor Ross Barnett defied a federal court order to admit him. A mob rioted on 30 September 1962, killing two and injuring over 300; Kennedy sent army troops and Meredith registered on 1 October."},{"front":"Birmingham, April to May 1963","open":false,"code":"33-2 — 2 Peaceful protests and their impact, 1963–65: King and the peace marches of 1963","back":"King was arrested on 12 April and wrote the Letter from Birmingham Jail. From 2 May Bull Connor turned hoses and dogs on marching schoolchildren on television; a desegregation agreement followed on 10 May."},{"front":"The March on Washington, 28 August 1963","open":false,"code":"33-2 — 2 Peaceful protests and their impact, 1963–65: King and the peace marches of 1963","back":"About 250,000 people gathered at the Lincoln Memorial for jobs and freedom, a large minority of them white. King gave the 'I have a dream' speech; the march was peaceful and nationally televised."},{"front":"Freedom Summer, 1964","open":false,"code":"33-2 — 2 Peaceful protests and their impact, 1963–65: Freedom Summer and the Mississippi murders","back":"Over a thousand volunteers ran about forty Freedom Schools and voter drives in Mississippi. Chaney, Goodman and Schwerner were murdered on 21 June; of some 17,000 applicants only about 1,600 were registered."},{"front":"The Civil Rights Act, 2 July 1964","open":false,"code":"33-2 — 2 Peaceful protests and their impact, 1963–65: The impact of peaceful protest and the roles of Kennedy and Johnson","back":"Signed by Johnson after he broke a southern filibuster with a cloture vote in June 1964. It outlawed segregation in public places and discrimination in employment, but did little about voting."},{"front":"Selma and the Voting Rights Act, 1965","open":false,"code":"33-2 — 2 Peaceful protests and their impact, 1963–65: The impact of peaceful protest and the roles of Kennedy and Johnson","back":"On 7 March 1965 troopers clubbed about 600 marchers at the Edmund Pettus Bridge. The completed march reached Montgomery on 25 March, 25,000 strong; Johnson signed the Voting Rights Act on 6 August 1965."},{"front":"Malcolm X","open":false,"code":"33-2 — 3 Malcolm X and Black Power, 1963–70: Malcolm X, his beliefs and methods","back":"Nation of Islam speaker who preached separation and self-defence rather than integration. Silenced in December 1963, he left the Nation in March 1964, made the hajj, and was shot dead in Harlem on 21 February 1965."},{"front":"Stokely Carmichael and Black Power","open":false,"code":"33-2 — 3 Malcolm X and Black Power, 1963–70: Reasons for the emergence of Black Power","back":"Elected SNCC chairman in May 1966, he called for Black Power at Greenwood, Mississippi on 16 June 1966 during the March Against Fear. SNCC voted in December 1966 to expel its white members and abandoned nonviolence."},{"front":"The Black Panther Party","open":false,"code":"33-2 — 3 Malcolm X and Black Power, 1963–70: The methods and achievements of the Black Panther movement","back":"Founded by Huey Newton and Bobby Seale in Oakland in October 1966. Its Ten-Point Programme, armed police patrols, free breakfasts and clinics drew both support and the FBI's COINTELPRO campaign."},{"front":"The riots of 1965 to 1967","open":false,"code":"33-2 — 4 The civil rights movement, 1965–75: The riots of 1965–67 and the Kerner Report","back":"Watts burned for six days from 11 August 1965, leaving 34 dead. Newark followed in July 1967 with 26 dead and Detroit later that month with 43, ended by paratroopers."},{"front":"Martin Luther King in Chicago and Memphis","open":false,"code":"33-2 — 4 The civil rights movement, 1965–75: King's campaign in the North and his assassination","back":"He led the Chicago Freedom Movement from January 1966 and signed the unenforced Summit Agreement in August. He was shot dead in Memphis on 4 April 1968; the Fair Housing Act followed on 11 April."},{"front":"Civil rights by 1975","open":false,"code":"33-2 — 4 The civil rights movement, 1965–75: The extent of progress in civil rights by 1975","back":"Legal segregation gone; Mississippi black registration up from under 7 per cent in 1964 to over half by 1968; black elected officials past 1,400 by 1970. Black unemployment stayed about twice the white rate."}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The sources and interpretations written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Source A: From a memorandum written by the manager of a department store in a city in North Carolina to his company's head office, March 1960. Written for this room in the style of such a memorandum; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — You asked for my account of the position at the lunch counter. The students from the Negro college have come in every day since the first week of February, twenty or thirty at a time. They sit in silence, in jackets and ties, and read their textbooks while they wait to be served, and when the counter closes they get up and go. Yesterday a group of white youths poured sugar and soft drinks over two of them and tore a girl's coat, and neither student answered back. The police stood in the doorway throughout and afterwards arrested four of the Negro students for trespass; nobody else was taken away. Our takings at the counter are now less than a quarter of what they were in January, and white shoppers avoid the whole floor. Two other stores in the city report the same. I must tell you plainly that I do not believe this will end while the counter stays closed to them, and the loss falls on us, not on the city.","Source B: From a letter written home to her parents in Ohio by a white college student working as a volunteer in Mississippi, July 1964. Written for this room in the style of a volunteer's letter home; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — We were trained for a week in Ohio before we came, and the training was mostly about how to fall and cover your head. I came because I could not go on reading about this state at home and doing nothing. Since the three men went missing near Philadelphia in June nobody here travels alone or after dark, and we telephone the office in Jackson every two hours. I teach in a Freedom School in the mornings: twenty-six children and four grandmothers, reading, arithmetic and the state constitution. In the afternoons we go out to the farms. The family I lodge with have taken me in although the man who owns their land has already told them that a family keeping a northern girl will not be kept on. Yesterday I went with eleven people to the courthouse. The registrar gave each of them a section of the Mississippi constitution to explain and failed all but one, and a deputy stood on the steps photographing everyone who went in. They will go back next week.","Source C: From a confidential memorandum sent by the chief of police of a city in Alabama to the state governor's office, September 1963. Written for this room in the style of such a memorandum; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — Demonstrations in this city are organised from outside, and our own coloured people would give no trouble if they were left alone. The methods that have worked best are these. Arrest early and in numbers, so that the jail is filled before the marchers reach the business district; the organisers cannot find bail for hundreds. Where a crowd will not move, the fire department's hoses have cleared a street faster than any number of officers, and the dogs are of most use in keeping a crowd off the line. Photograph every face, and see that employers and landlords are given the names; a man who loses his job seldom marches twice. The newspapers and the television cameras are our chief difficulty, for what is done in this city is shown the same night in New York and in Washington, and it is the pictures, and not the marchers, that have brought the Justice Department down upon us.","Interpretation 1: From a study of the civil rights campaigns of the 1960s, by a historian writing in the 1990s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — The campaigns of peaceful protest did what no amount of anger could have done: they forced the federal government to act. Birmingham in 1963 and Selma in 1965 put southern violence on the nation's television screens and left Congress with no honourable way of refusing, and the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Voting Rights Act of 1965 followed. Within three years the proportion of black adults registered to vote in Mississippi had risen from under a tenth to more than half, and black candidates were winning office in counties where no black person had voted in living memory. Black Power made a great deal of noise, but it changed no laws.","Interpretation 2: From a general history of black America since 1945, by a historian writing in the 2000s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — Marching won the statute book and very little else. The laws of 1964 and 1965 ended segregation where it was written down, in the South, and left untouched the cities where most black Americans now lived. King's own campaign in Chicago in 1966 achieved an agreement that changed nothing, and the fires of Watts, Newark and Detroit said what the marches could not: that bad housing, bad schools and hostile police were not matters of law. The Kerner Commission warned in 1968 that the country was becoming two societies, separate and unequal. By 1975 black unemployment was still roughly twice the white rate, and Black Power spoke for the millions the legislation never reached.","How the room uses them: Source A is the inference source for Question 1. Sources B and C are the contemporary pair for the 8-mark usefulness question, 3(a), and may be used in 3(c). Interpretations 1 and 2 are the pair for 3(b), 3(c) and 3(d). Every quotation in the model answers of this room comes from the five texts above and from nowhere else."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 3 Question 3(d), 16 marks)","items":["Question: 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part played by peaceful protest in bringing about change for black Americans in the years 1960 to 1975? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]. This is one paragraph from the middle of the answer, putting the case against Interpretation 2.","Interpretation 2 says that 'marching won the statute book and very little else', but that phrase hides how much work the marching had to do before any statute existed. Peaceful protest was not a petition; it was a way of manufacturing a crisis that the federal government could not leave alone. King and the SCLC chose Birmingham in April 1963 because Eugene 'Bull' Connor, its Commissioner of Public Safety, was certain to answer schoolchildren with fire hoses and police dogs in front of the cameras, and within seven weeks of the children's marches that began on 2 May, Kennedy, who had moved cautiously for two years, had sent a civil rights bill to Congress. The same mechanism was used again at Selma, where state troopers clubbed about six hundred marchers at the Edmund Pettus Bridge on 7 March 1965 and the Voting Rights Act was signed on 6 August. Interpretation 1 is describing exactly this when it says the campaigns 'forced the federal government to act', and the consequence was measurable: black registration in Mississippi rose from about 6.7 per cent of adults in 1964 to well over half by 1968, in a state where, as Source B shows, a registrar could fail ten of eleven applicants on the constitution. Interpretation 2's own evidence, the fires of Watts and Detroit, produced the Kerner Report and no legislation at all. On this point, therefore, Interpretation 2 understates the case: peaceful protest was the only method in these fifteen years that reliably converted grievance into federal law.","Why this is Level 4: it 'provides an explained evaluation reviewing the alternative views in coming to a substantiated judgement' — the counter-case is built from the mechanism of protest and then measured against Interpretation 2's claim. The analysis is precise about how the difference of view is conveyed, setting 'the statute book and very little else' against 'forced the federal government to act'. The contextual knowledge is precisely selected rather than general: Connor's office, the date the children's marches began, the bridge at Selma, the registration figures. The line of reasoning is sustained and the closing sentence judges rather than summarises."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same as above — 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part played by peaceful protest in bringing about change for black Americans in the years 1960 to 1975?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks].","Interpretation 2 says that marching won the statute book and very little else, and I do not fully agree with this. Peaceful protest was very important because it got the government to pass laws and it made people realise that segregation was wrong. Marches like Birmingham and Selma were shown on television and this shocked a lot of Americans, so the politicians had to do something about it. Interpretation 1 says the campaigns forced the federal government to act, which shows that peaceful protest really worked and was not a waste of time. However, Interpretation 2 is right that life in the northern cities did not get much better and there were still riots. Overall I think both interpretations are partly right because the laws were passed but things did not change for everybody.","Why this is Level 2 and not Level 3: it does offer 'valid evaluative comment to agree with or counter the interpretation' and it does select details from both interpretations, which is what Level 2 asks for. What it misses is Level 3's requirement that 'relevant contextual knowledge is used directly to support the evaluation': 'shocked a lot of Americans' and 'things did not change for everybody' are assertions where Bull Connor's hoses, the date of the Civil Rights Act and the Mississippi registration figures should be. It never shows how the two writers convey their views, and its judgement is announced in the last sentence rather than reasoned towards, so 'its justification is insecure or undeveloped and a line of reasoning is not sustained'. Turning it into a Level 3 answer means replacing every general phrase with a named fact that does evaluative work."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the sit-ins, the Freedom Rides and the Meredith case, 1960 to 1962","items":["1 February 1960: four first-year students from the North Carolina Agricultural and Technical College sat at the whites-only lunch counter of Woolworth's in Greensboro and stayed when they were refused service.","The tactic was copied in dozens of southern towns within a fortnight and in more than a hundred cities within a year; tens of thousands took part and around three thousand were arrested.","The Greensboro Woolworth's lunch counter was desegregated on 25 July 1960, and the loss of white as well as black trade was what moved the chains.","April 1960: Ella Baker brought the student sit-in groups together at Shaw University in Raleigh, where the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee (SNCC) was founded.","James Lawson's workshops in Nashville trained students in nonviolent discipline before the sit-ins reached the city, which is why protesters there did not retaliate.","4 May 1961: thirteen Freedom Riders, seven black and six white, left Washington DC on two buses, organised by the Congress of Racial Equality under James Farmer, to test a Supreme Court ruling of 1960 against segregated interstate terminals.","14 May 1961: a Ku Klux Klan mob stopped the Greyhound bus outside Anniston, Alabama, slashed its tyres and firebombed it; the riders were beaten as they escaped. The second bus was attacked in Birmingham.","When CORE withdrew, Nashville students from SNCC continued the ride; a mob attacked them in Montgomery on 20 May 1961 and a Justice Department official sent to observe was knocked unconscious.","Robert Kennedy sent federal marshals to Montgomery and then pressed the Interstate Commerce Commission; its order banning segregation in interstate terminals took effect on 1 November 1961.","1962: James Meredith, an air force veteran, won a federal court order admitting him to the all-white University of Mississippi; Governor Ross Barnett defied it and had himself appointed university registrar.","30 September 1962: a mob of thousands rioted at Oxford as marshals escorted Meredith onto the campus; two men were killed, a French journalist and a local workman, and more than three hundred were injured.","President Kennedy sent regular army troops; Meredith registered on 1 October 1962, was guarded by soldiers throughout his course, and graduated in August 1963."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: peaceful protest and its results, 1963 to 1965","items":["April 1963: King and the SCLC opened the Birmingham campaign, choosing the city for its rigid segregation and for Eugene 'Bull' Connor, its Commissioner of Public Safety.","12 April 1963: King was arrested in Birmingham and wrote the Letter from Birmingham Jail defending direct action against clergymen who called it untimely.","From 2 May 1963 schoolchildren marched in thousands; Connor used fire hoses and police dogs, and the pictures were broadcast nationally. An agreement to desegregate lunch counters and hire black staff was announced on 10 May.","June 1963: Kennedy sent a civil rights bill to Congress; he was assassinated on 22 November 1963 before it passed.","28 August 1963: about 250,000 people attended the March on Washington for Jobs and Freedom at the Lincoln Memorial, where King gave the 'I have a dream' speech.","Summer 1964: SNCC and CORE brought more than a thousand volunteers, mostly white northern students, into Mississippi, where only about 6.7 per cent of black adults were registered to vote.","Freedom Summer ran about forty Freedom Schools teaching more than three thousand pupils; of roughly 17,000 who tried to register, about 1,600 were accepted.","21 June 1964: James Chaney, Andrew Goodman and Michael Schwerner were murdered by Klansmen near Philadelphia, Mississippi, with the help of a deputy sheriff; their bodies were found under an earth dam on 4 August.","August 1964: the Mississippi Freedom Democratic Party was offered only two token seats at the Democratic convention in Atlantic City and rejected the compromise, which disillusioned many young activists with white liberal allies.","2 July 1964: Johnson signed the Civil Rights Act after breaking a southern filibuster with a cloture vote in June; it outlawed segregation in public places and discrimination in employment.","7 March 1965: 'Bloody Sunday' — state troopers clubbed and gassed about six hundred marchers at the Edmund Pettus Bridge in Selma; the completed march reached Montgomery 25,000 strong on 25 March.","6 August 1965: Johnson signed the Voting Rights Act, suspending literacy tests and sending federal examiners into the southern states."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Malcolm X, Black Power and the Black Panther Party","items":["Malcolm X joined the Nation of Islam in prison and became its best-known speaker, preaching separation from white society, black-owned business and self-defence rather than integration.","December 1963: Elijah Muhammad silenced him for saying of Kennedy's assassination that the chickens had come home to roost.","March 1964: he left the Nation of Islam and founded Muslim Mosque Inc.; after making the hajj to Mecca in April 1964 he spoke of working with white people who genuinely opposed racism.","21 February 1965: Malcolm X was shot dead at the Audubon Ballroom in Harlem.","May 1966: Stokely Carmichael was elected chairman of SNCC; James Meredith was shot on 6 June 1966 at the start of his March Against Fear, which other organisations continued.","16 June 1966: Carmichael called for Black Power at a rally at Greenwood, Mississippi. SNCC voted in December 1966 to expel its white members and moved away from nonviolence.","Black Power meant pride in black history and appearance, self-defence, and black control of black communities and businesses; it was never a single organisation.","16 October 1968: Tommie Smith and John Carlos raised black-gloved fists on the medal podium after the 200 metres at the Mexico City Olympics and were expelled from the Games.","October 1966: Huey Newton and Bobby Seale founded the Black Panther Party for Self-Defense in Oakland, California.","The Ten-Point Programme demanded work, decent housing, education that told the truth about black history, an end to police brutality and the release of black prisoners.","Panther members carried law books and, at first lawfully, loaded guns, and followed police patrols in Oakland to observe arrests; the party also ran free breakfast programmes feeding thousands of children each school day, free clinics and legal advice.","The FBI's COINTELPRO operation set out to destroy the party; the Chicago leader Fred Hampton was shot dead in a police raid in December 1969."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the riots, the Kerner Report, King in the North, and progress by 1975","items":["11 to 16 August 1965: the Watts district of Los Angeles burned for six days, five days after the Voting Rights Act was signed: 34 dead, about a thousand injured and some forty million dollars of damage.","July 1967: Newark rioted with 26 dead; later the same month Detroit rioted with 43 dead, and paratroopers were sent in.","29 February 1968: the Kerner Commission reported, blaming white racism, segregated housing, unemployment and hostile policing, and warning that the country was moving towards two societies, one black and one white, separate and unequal.","The Kerner Commission recommended a large programme of jobs, housing and schools; Johnson, absorbed by Vietnam and its cost, shelved it.","January 1966: King moved into a decaying tenement on Chicago's West Side to lead the Chicago Freedom Movement against slum housing.","July 1966: about 35,000 people attended the Chicago Freedom Movement rally at Soldier Field.","5 August 1966: King was struck on the head by a stone while marching through all-white Marquette Park, and said the crowds were more hostile than any he had met in Mississippi.","26 August 1966: the Summit Agreement with Mayor Richard Daley promised open housing in Chicago and was not enforced.","April 1967: King publicly opposed the Vietnam war, which cost him Johnson's support; he then launched the Poor People's Campaign.","4 April 1968: King was assassinated in Memphis, where he had gone to support striking sanitation workers; riots followed in more than a hundred cities.","11 April 1968: the Fair Housing Act was signed, outlawing discrimination in the sale and renting of housing.","Black registration in Mississippi rose from about 6.7 per cent of adults in 1964 to well over half by 1968; the number of black elected officials in the United States passed 1,400 by 1970 and kept rising.","Southern schools desegregated faster between 1968 and 1972 than at any time before or since, helped by the Supreme Court's approval of bussing in 1971; black mayors were elected in Cleveland and Gary in 1967 and in Detroit and Los Angeles in 1973.","Against that, black unemployment remained at roughly twice the white rate through the early 1970s, black family income stayed far below white, and the northern ghettos were no less poor than in 1965."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the source and interpretation moves this paper rewards","items":["Inference: say something the source does not state but that must be true if the source is true, then give the words that force it. One inference and one supporting detail in each box.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose: what kind of document it is, who made it and when, and why it was made.","Utility: judge each source for the enquiry the question names, never in general. Content first, then how nature, origin and purpose make that content more or less useful, then the context that confirms or qualifies it.","Contextual knowledge scores only when it is used on the source; a paragraph about the period that never touches the sources earns nothing.","'Biased' is not a judgement on its own. Say whose view the source gives, why it gives that view, and what it is therefore useful for: a one-sided source is excellent evidence of the view it takes.","A source can be useful for an enquiry it never mentions, if what it shows explains the thing being enquired into; say what you can infer, not only what it says.","Difference of view (3(b)): a disagreement about the past, not a difference of wording or of detail. State the one difference, then support it with a phrase from each interpretation.","Reason for difference (3(c)): interpretations differ because they ask different questions, rest on different evidence, or give weight to different parts of the story. Name which, and show it from both interpretations, using Sources B and C where they illustrate the evidence.","Differences based on conclusions drawn from evidence are legitimate; an interpretation is not wrong simply because another one disagrees with it.","Evaluating an interpretation (3(d)): use both interpretations and your own knowledge, show how each conveys its view, then decide how far the named interpretation can be agreed with and say on what criterion."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 3 Question 3(d): 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part played by peaceful protest in bringing about change for black Americans in the years 1960 to 1975?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","items":["The question as printed: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part played by peaceful protest in bringing about change for black Americans in the years 1960 to 1975? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' This question prints no stimulus points: the two interpretations are the stimulus, and both must be used, so an answer that evaluates one in isolation cannot reach the top levels.","Decode the statement. Interpretation 2 claims that peaceful protest produced laws and nothing more: 'marching won the statute book and very little else', and the Acts left untouched the cities where most black Americans lived. Interpretation 1 claims that peaceful protest 'forced the federal government to act' and that this was the decisive change. The criterion to announce in the opening is what counts as change: rights and the vote, or the conditions people actually live in.","Opening (three or four sentences): state Interpretation 2's claim in your own words, name Interpretation 1 as the alternative, announce the criterion, and signal the position you will reach, so the line of reasoning is visible from the first paragraph.","Paragraph 1, against Interpretation 2 — the mechanism. Peaceful protest was designed to manufacture a crisis: Birmingham chosen in April 1963 for Bull Connor, King arrested on 12 April, children marching from 2 May, hoses and dogs on television, agreement on 10 May, Kennedy's bill in June. Interim judgement: this was not petitioning, it was the production of political pressure.","Paragraph 2, against Interpretation 2 — the results were not only on paper. Selma and Bloody Sunday on 7 March 1965 were followed by the Voting Rights Act on 6 August; Mississippi registration rose from about 6.7 per cent in 1964 to well over half by 1968, and black elected officials passed 1,400 by 1970. Interim judgement: for a black southerner the change of status was real, not symbolic.","Paragraph 3, for Interpretation 2 — where the method stopped working. Chicago from January 1966, Daley's refusal to supply pictures, the Summit Agreement of 26 August 1966 unenforced, King stoned in Marquette Park on 5 August. Interim judgement: against segregation that was never written down, marching had nothing to strike at.","Paragraph 4, for Interpretation 2 and the point that goes furthest beyond both texts — the limits of law itself. Watts from 11 August 1965, five days after the Voting Rights Act, with 34 dead; Newark and Detroit in July 1967; the Kerner Report of 29 February 1968 warning of two societies and then shelved; black unemployment still roughly twice the white rate by 1975. Interim judgement: what remained needed money and enforcement over years, which no march could compel.","The evaluation thread that must run through every paragraph: how each interpretation conveys its view. Interpretation 1 counts registrations and offices, the things campaigns were aimed at; Interpretation 2 counts unemployment and housing, the things people lived in, and its phrase 'very little else' does the work of an argument without making one. Saying this, and using it, is what the top level means by showing how the differences of view are conveyed.","What the conclusion must say: not 'both are partly right'. Apply the criterion announced at the start. Peaceful protest was the only method in these fifteen years that reliably turned grievance into federal law, and those laws ended a legal caste system in the South; but it could not touch a northern economy, and Interpretation 2 is right that the statute book was most of what it won. Name the deciding consideration: change of status is not nothing, and Interpretation 2's 'very little else' understates it, while its account of the cities stands.","Timing and SPaG: about 40 minutes in all — six to plan the paragraphs and fix the criterion, about thirty to write, and the last four to check spelling and punctuation and to make sure the specialist terms are present and correctly used, since 4 additional marks depend on 'a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate'. Useful terms here: segregation, desegregation, direct action, nonviolence, civil disobedience, filibuster, disfranchisement, ghetto, de facto segregation, Black Power, interpretation, provenance."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T08:40:05.768189Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"Factually clean and properly built to the June 2025 Paper 3 form - the current cap wording, no Level 2 cap, 1 hour 30 minutes, Question 2 as 2(a)/2(b), SPaG only on 3(d), all five texts written for the room and attributed to 'a historian writing in the 1990s/2000s' - so it is publishable once the Fair Housing Act wording and the Soldier Field figure are softened."}}},"33-3":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"33-3","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 3 is the part of Option 33 that Paper 3 reaches for most often, because the war supplies everything the paper needs: a contemporary source with a point of view, a causation question that can be set anywhere between 1954 and 1975, and two historians who disagree about why the most powerful country in the world could not win. The examiner is not asking you to narrate the war. The examiner is asking you to explain why involvement grew step by step under four presidents, why the methods the United States chose did not work against the enemy it actually faced, and why the Tet Offensive mattered far more politically than it did militarily. This room teaches that argument with the dates, names and figures that earn marks, and it trains the five moves Paper 3 rewards: inference from a source, causal explanation that goes past the printed stimulus points, judgement of a source's usefulness for the enquiry actually named, spotting how two historians differ and why, and evaluating one of them against the other and against what you know.","idea":[{"h":"Why Eisenhower took on a French war: the dominoes and a client who could not stand alone","t":"The French garrison at Dien Bien Phu surrendered on 7 May 1954 after a siege of fifty-six days, and the Geneva Accords of 21 July 1954 divided Vietnam at the seventeenth parallel: Ho Chi Minh's communists in the North, a non-communist state in the South, and nationwide elections promised for 1956 to reunite the country. France was going; the question was whether the United States would take its place. Eisenhower had already given the answer in public in April 1954 with the domino theory — knock over one country in South East Asia and its neighbours fall in turn — and after the communist victory in China in 1949 and the Korean War no president could afford to be the one who let the next domino go. So Washington backed Ngo Dinh Diem, who took power in the South in 1955 and, with American support, refused to hold the 1956 elections because Ho Chi Minh was expected to win them. Diem then proved the weakest of foundations: a Catholic ruling a mostly Buddhist country, he filled office with his own family, reversed the land redistribution that had given peasants their fields, and used the army against his critics. Every weakness deepened American commitment, and by the time Eisenhower left office in January 1961 about seven hundred American military personnel were in South Vietnam. A top-level answer keeps those two strands separate and then joins them: the domino theory explains why the United States wanted a South Vietnam at all, and Diem's weaknesses explain why wanting one meant paying for it.","code":"33-3 — 1 Reasons for US involvement in the conflict in Vietnam, 1954–63: Reasons for greater US involvement under Eisenhower"},{"h":"Kennedy: advisers, fenced villages and the coup that made the war America's","t":"Kennedy inherited the commitment and multiplied it. The National Liberation Front, founded in December 1960 and known to Americans as the Vietcong, was killing government officials in the villages and winning the countryside, so Kennedy sent helicopters, armoured personnel carriers and, above all, men: the number of American military advisers rose from about seven hundred in 1960 to roughly sixteen thousand by the end of 1963. Their limits were exposed at Ap Bac on 2 January 1963, where a Vietcong force of a few hundred beat a South Vietnamese force several times its size that had American helicopters, armour and advisers; three Americans were killed and five helicopters were lost. The Strategic Hamlet Program, begun in March 1962, tried to solve the problem at the root by moving peasants into fenced and guarded hamlets so the guerrillas could not live among them; instead it moved families off their ancestral land and their graves, often without the compensation promised, and thousands of the hamlets the government counted were never finished. Then came the Buddhist crisis: the monk Thich Quang Duc burned himself to death in a Saigon street on 11 June 1963 and the photograph went round the world, Diem's forces raided pagodas in August, and on 1 November 1963 the generals seized power with American knowledge, killing Diem and his brother Nhu the next day. A top-level answer makes the coup the hinge: by helping to remove the man it had installed, the United States took ownership of whatever government came next, and there were several in the year that followed.","code":"33-3 — 1 Reasons for US involvement in the conflict in Vietnam, 1954–63: Greater involvement under Kennedy"},{"h":"Johnson's escalation: a blank cheque from Congress and a client about to fall","t":"Johnson escalated because the alternative was to watch South Vietnam collapse. After Diem's death coup followed coup in Saigon, the Vietcong took district after district, and by early 1965 the South looked close to falling. The opening he needed came in the Gulf of Tonkin: the destroyer Maddox reported an attack by North Vietnamese torpedo boats on 2 August 1964 and a second attack was reported on 4 August, which historians now regard as almost certainly mistaken. Johnson took the report to Congress, and on 7 August 1964 the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution passed the House 416 votes to none and the Senate 88 to 2, authorising him to take all necessary measures without a declaration of war. He used it after the Vietcong attacked the American base at Pleiku on 7 February 1965, killing eight Americans: Operation Rolling Thunder, the sustained bombing of North Vietnam, opened on 2 March 1965, and on 8 March 1965 three and a half thousand Marines waded ashore at Da Nang to guard the airbase. Ground troops then needed protecting in their turn, so numbers climbed to about 184,000 by the end of 1965, 385,000 by the end of 1966 and 486,000 by the end of 1967. A top-level answer links the two strands of causation: Saigon's weakness supplied the reason to act and the Tonkin Resolution supplied the means, while Johnson's fear of being blamed for losing Vietnam, as Truman had been blamed for losing China, supplied the motive he never stated in public.","code":"33-3 — 2 Escalation of the conflict under Johnson: Reasons for the escalation of the conflict"},{"h":"The war the Vietcong chose: ambush, tunnel and village","t":"The Vietcong never fought the war the United States had trained for. They ambushed and withdrew, attacked at night, mined tracks and paddy dykes, and left booby traps and pits of sharpened stakes that killed and maimed men who had not seen a single enemy; mines and booby traps caused a large share of all American casualties. They lived underground when they had to: the tunnel system at Cu Chi, north-west of Saigon, ran for over two hundred kilometres and held kitchens, stores, workshops and hospitals, and it lay beneath ground the Americans believed they controlled. Supplies and reinforcements came south along the Ho Chi Minh Trail, a web of tracks, bridges and fords running through Laos and Cambodia, carried by lorry and by bicycles loaded far beyond a man's weight, and repair gangs filled the craters faster than aircraft could make them. Above all the guerrillas lived inside the population: the National Liberation Front taxed villages, recruited in them and ran a shadow government in them, so an American patrol could not tell a farmer from a fighter. A top-level answer turns those facts into an argument about causation rather than a list of tactics: each one denied the United States the thing its firepower needed, which was an enemy who would stand still, hold ground and be told apart from everybody else.","code":"33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The guerrilla tactics used by the Vietcong"},{"h":"The war the United States chose: Rolling Thunder, search and destroy, chemicals and the body count","t":"American method was firepower applied on a scale nobody had seen. Operation Rolling Thunder, the bombing of North Vietnam, began on 2 March 1965 and ran until the halt of November 1968, yet it could not stop supplies moving south: the North had little heavy industry to destroy, its weapons came from the Soviet Union and China, and targets near Hanoi, Haiphong and the Chinese border were restricted for fear of widening the war. On the ground, search and destroy sent units out to find the enemy and kill him; large operations such as Cedar Falls in January 1967 and Junction City from February to May 1967 swept whole districts, burning shelters and removing civilians. Because there was no ground to capture, progress was measured by the body count, which rewarded exaggeration and made civilian dead easy to record as enemy. Chemicals were used to take away the cover: Operation Ranch Hand sprayed some nineteen million gallons of herbicide between 1962 and 1971, over half of it Agent Orange, and napalm burned villages and the people in them. The worst single consequence came at My Lai on 16 March 1968, where American soldiers killed several hundred unarmed villagers. A top-level answer explains the self-defeating chain: methods designed to separate the guerrillas from the people destroyed the people's homes, crops and families, and so recruited for the guerrillas.","code":"33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The methods used by the USA"},{"h":"Tet 1968: beaten on the ground, victorious on the television","t":"On the night of 30 to 31 January 1968, during the truce for the Vietnamese New Year, more than 84,000 Vietcong and North Vietnamese troops attacked across South Vietnam, striking over a hundred towns and 36 of the 44 provincial capitals. A squad blew its way into the compound of the American embassy in Saigon; the old imperial city of Hue was taken and held for weeks before being retaken street by street in February, and thousands of its people were killed; the Marine base at Khe Sanh had already been under siege since 21 January and was not relieved until 8 April. Militarily the offensive failed completely: no city was held, the general rising it was meant to spark never came, and communist losses ran into the tens of thousands, crippling the southern Vietcong so badly that the North Vietnamese Army carried most of the fighting thereafter. Politically it was decisive, because for two years American commanders had been reporting that the enemy was beaten. General Westmoreland's request for another 206,000 men became public in March 1968 and read as an admission that there was no end in sight; on 31 March 1968 Johnson limited the bombing, offered talks and announced he would not seek re-election, and peace negotiations opened in Paris in May. A top-level answer names the criterion before judging: on military criteria Tet was a defeat for the communists, and it was a turning point precisely because the war was no longer being decided on military criteria.","code":"33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The key features and significance of the Tet Offensive, 1968"},{"h":"Vietnamisation: handing over a war that could not be handed over","t":"Nixon took office on 20 January 1969 promising to end American involvement without simply losing, and his answer was Vietnamisation: build up the Army of the Republic of Vietnam with American money, weapons and training so that it could do the fighting while American troops came home. On paper it worked. Regular ARVN strength grew from about 427,000 at the end of 1968 to about 516,000 by 1971, and with territorial forces counted in, more than a million South Vietnamese were under arms, equipped with modern American rifles, artillery and aircraft. In practice the army was officered by appointment and patronage rather than merit, desertion was heavy, and it had been built to call for American air support whenever it met serious resistance. Operation Lam Son 719, the ARVN drive into Laos from 8 February to 25 March 1971, was meant to prove the policy and instead ended in a costly retreat. The North's Easter Offensive of 1972 was held only by massive American bombing, and the Paris Peace Accords of 27 January 1973 let North Vietnamese troops stay where they stood in the South while the last Americans left. When Congress cut aid and forbade further American military action, the prop was gone: the North attacked in 1975 and Saigon fell on 30 April. A top-level answer explains the flaw rather than listing the defeats: Vietnamisation transferred the fighting but not the thing the fighting depended on, which was American air power and an authority in Saigon that the countryside accepted.","code":"33-3 — 4 Changes under Nixon, 1969–73: The key features of Vietnamisation"},{"h":"The Nixon Doctrine and getting the troops home","t":"Withdrawal was the part of Nixon's policy that had to be visible, because it was aimed at American voters as much as at Hanoi. At Guam in July 1969 he set out what became the Nixon Doctrine: the United States would keep its treaty commitments and supply money and equipment to allies threatened by communism, but those allies would have to provide the soldiers for their own defence. The first 25,000 men were announced for withdrawal in June 1969, and the reductions then ran year after year: from the peak of about 543,000 on 30 April 1969, American strength fell below 160,000 by the end of 1971 and to a few tens of thousands by the end of 1972, with the last combat units leaving under the Paris Peace Accords in March 1973 and nearly six hundred prisoners of war coming home. Nixon paired withdrawal with pressure — secret talks conducted by Henry Kissinger, opening relations with China in 1972 and détente with the Soviet Union, and heavy bombing whenever Hanoi refused to move. A top-level answer notices the contradiction that runs through the policy: every announced withdrawal reduced the pressure Nixon could apply at the negotiating table, because Hanoi could see the Americans leaving and had only to wait.","code":"33-3 — 4 Changes under Nixon, 1969–73: The Nixon Doctrine and the withdrawal of US troops"},{"h":"Cambodia, Laos and the bombing of 1972: widening the war in order to leave it","t":"Nixon widened the war at the same time as he shrank the American presence, because the supply routes that kept the communists fighting ran outside South Vietnam. Bombing of communist bases inside neutral Cambodia began in secret in March 1969, and on 29 April 1970 American and South Vietnamese troops crossed the border in force to destroy the sanctuaries and the headquarters said to be directing the war. Enormous quantities of supplies were captured, but the headquarters was never found, the communists moved deeper into Cambodia, and the country was pushed into a civil war. At home the invasion set off the largest protests of the war, and on 4 May 1970 National Guardsmen shot dead four students at Kent State University in Ohio; Congress responded by barring American ground troops from Cambodia and Laos, which is why Lam Son 719 in February and March 1971 had to be fought by the ARVN alone, with American air support only. When the North launched its Easter Offensive on 30 March 1972, Nixon answered with Operation Linebacker from May to October 1972 and the mining of Haiphong harbour, and after the talks stalled with the eleven days of Linebacker II, the so-called Christmas bombing of 18 to 29 December 1972. A top-level answer connects the two halves of the policy: the widening was meant to buy time for Vietnamisation and to force Hanoi to sign, and it did produce the Paris Accords of January 1973, but at the cost of the domestic support the policy needed to be sustained.","code":"33-3 — 4 Changes under Nixon, 1969–73: Attacks on Cambodia and Laos"}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1: two inferences from Source A","setup":"Source A in this room is a report on the security of one rural district, written by a United States Army officer serving as an adviser to a South Vietnamese division and sent to his superiors at the American military headquarters in Saigon, October 1963. It reads: The province chief's returns for this district show eleven strategic hamlets completed since March of last year. I have visited seven of them. Two have a ditch, a fence and a watchtower and a militia that turns out; the rest have a fence in places and no radio, and in one the wire had been carried away for fishing traps. The families were moved out of their old villages in the planting season, without the payment they were promised, and they have been told to build the defences themselves. When I ask them who comes in at night they say nobody. Yet the Vietcong tax collector was in the largest of these hamlets a week ago, and the militia knew it. Saigon counts a hamlet as finished when the province chief reports it, and the province chief is not visited. The divisional commander tells me his officers are chosen for loyalty to the palace and will not take losses, and since the Buddhist protests began in the summer he is more anxious about the city than about this district. I am asked for helicopters and for money, and never for advice. The question is: 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about the government of Ngo Dinh Diem. Complete the table below to explain your answer.' (4 marks)","steps":["Fix the writer and his reader before reading for content: an American adviser reporting to his own headquarters is not writing propaganda and has no reason to flatter Saigon, so his complaints count as evidence rather than as grumbling.","Take the first inference from what happens to the government's own figures: Saigon 'counts a hamlet as finished when the province chief reports it, and the province chief is not visited', so Diem's government could not be relied on to tell the truth about its own programme.","Take the second from the army: the divisional commander says his officers 'are chosen for loyalty to the palace and will not take losses', so Diem valued political loyalty above military competence.","Check each pair before you write: the claim, which the source implies but never states, belongs in the box for the inference, and the words that force the claim belong in the box for details."],"cues":["Who wrote this, and who was meant to read it?","What happens to a hamlet once the province chief has reported it?","What does the remark about the officers show that the source never states outright?","Which box takes the claim and which takes the quotation?"]},{"title":"Question 2: getting a 12-mark 'Explain why' past the two stimulus points","setup":"The question is: '2(b) Explain why the bombing of North Vietnam failed to end the war in the years 1965–68. You may use the following in your answer: • Operation Rolling Thunder • the Ho Chi Minh Trail. You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks). On the paper Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b) and you answer only one of them. The mark scheme caps an answer that does not address three or more aspects of content at a maximum of 8 marks and shuts Level 4 to it altogether. The fact files in this room supply the material: Rolling Thunder opening on 2 March 1965 and running to the halt of November 1968; the North's lack of heavy industry and its weapons from the Soviet Union and China; the restricted zones around Hanoi, Haiphong and the Chinese border; the repeated pauses offered as invitations to negotiate; the Trail through Laos and Cambodia with its repair gangs, lorries and loaded bicycles.","steps":["Turn the first stimulus point into a causal claim instead of a heading: the campaign was aimed at an industrial economy North Vietnam did not have, so wrecking rail lines and factories could not disarm an enemy whose rifles, shells and anti-aircraft missiles arrived from the Soviet Union and China.","Make the second point do causal work too: the Trail was not a road but a web of tracks, fords and bypasses through two neighbouring countries, filled in by labour gangs faster than aircraft could crater it, so the supplies the bombing existed to stop kept arriving in the South.","Add the aspect of content neither point prompts: the bombing was deliberately limited, with target lists approved in Washington, restricted zones around the capital, the main port and the Chinese frontier, and repeated pauses, because the greater fear was drawing China or the Soviet Union into the war.","Add a fourth aspect if the clock allows: the raids hardened rather than broke morale in the North, where the government used them to mobilise the population, and they handed the communists a case to make abroad and to American television.","Order the reasons so that they build into an argument and close each paragraph on the words of the question: the wrong kind of target, a supply line that could not be severed, and a campaign its own government would not press to the limit."],"cues":["What did the bombing assume about the enemy's economy?","Why can a track network be harder to close than a railway?","Which limits on the campaign came from Washington rather than from Hanoi?","What did the bombing do to opinion inside North Vietnam?","In what order do the reasons make an argument rather than a list?"]},{"title":"Question 3(a): judging Sources B and C for the enquiry actually named","setup":"Source B in this room is a letter written by a United States Marine serving south of Da Nang to his brother at home, November 1967. It reads: We have been out over the same ground three times now and each time we come back with nothing to show for it but casualties. The mines are the worst of it. In two weeks the platoon has lost four men to a wire under a paddy dyke and one to a pit of sharpened stakes, and we have never once seen the man who laid any of them. Yesterday we swept a hamlet, found a tunnel mouth under a cooking fire and a sack of rice under the floor, and burned the place, and the people stood out in the paddy and watched us do it. The lieutenant says the count for the sweep was nine. I could not tell you how the nine were counted. Tell Mother the food is good and the mail comes. What I would like to know is how you are supposed to win a country you only hold while you are standing on it. Source C is a briefing given to American newspaper and television reporters by a spokesman for the United States military command in Saigon, November 1967. It reads: In the past week operations in the northern provinces have produced eleven hundred and forty enemy killed against ninety-six of our own, a ratio this command has improved on in every month of this year. Air strikes and artillery accounted for the greater part of the enemy total. In the same period the bombing of the North destroyed a further stretch of the rail line towards the Chinese border and a number of supply craft on the inland waterways. The enemy's ability to replace losses at this rate is limited, and we now believe that the point at which he can no longer replace them is in sight. Questions on the movement of supplies through Laos should be put to the Seventh Air Force. Figures for civilian casualties in the areas of operations are not compiled by this command. The question is: 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the effects of the war on the villagers of South Vietnam? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context.' (8 marks)","steps":["Copy the enquiry to the head of your plan and let it select the material: the effects on villagers, so the burned hamlet and the uncounted civilians are in and the rail line to the Chinese border is out.","Take Source B's content for that enquiry: a sweep that finds 'a tunnel mouth under a cooking fire and a sack of rice under the floor' and burns the place while 'the people stood out in the paddy and watched us do it' shows villagers losing home and food to a search that caught nobody.","Weigh Source B's nature, origin and purpose together: a private letter to a brother, written by a man who was there, with no censor to satisfy and nobody to impress, so it is strong evidence of what a sweep did to a hamlet, though it is one platoon in one district.","Turn to Source C and notice that its most useful sentence is a refusal: 'Figures for civilian casualties in the areas of operations are not compiled by this command' tells the enquiry that the villagers were not being counted at all.","Weigh Source C's provenance rather than calling it biased: a briefing built to persuade reporters that the war was being won makes its kill totals a claim and not a measurement, and that purpose is exactly what makes it evidence of how the American war was run.","Use contextual knowledge on the sources and then judge for the enquiry: the body count was the measure of progress in search and destroy, and Operation Ranch Hand sprayed some nineteen million gallons of herbicide between 1962 and 1971, so between them the two sources reach the sweep but not the defoliation, the refugee camps or the bombing of the South."],"cues":["What exactly is the enquiry into?","What does the sweep in Source B cost the people who live there?","Why might a letter to a brother be more candid than a report?","Which single sentence of Source C is worth most to this enquiry?","Does a source written to persuade become useless?","Which effects on villagers does neither source reach?"]},{"title":"Question 3(b): finding the difference of view, not of detail","setup":"This example practises on a second, shorter pair of interpretations, written for these two examples only and printed here; they are not the room's printed Interpretations 1 and 2, and, like every text in this room, they are not real books and are not attributed to any real historian. Interpretation 1, from a study of American politics and the war by a historian writing in the 1990s: Nixon withdrew because the voters made him. Opinion had turned against the draft and against the casualty lists long before he took office, and every announced troop reduction bought him quiet at home. Vietnamisation was the label; the timetable was set in Washington by elections, not in Saigon by the fighting. Interpretation 2, from a history of the war's diplomacy by a historian writing in the 2010s: The withdrawal was a bargaining position, not a retreat. Nixon and Kissinger needed Hanoi to believe that the troops would go whatever happened and that the bombers would come back if an agreement were broken. Troop numbers fell when the negotiators wanted them to fall. The question is: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. They give different views about the reasons for the American withdrawal of troops from Vietnam. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations.' (4 marks)","steps":["Reduce each writer's view to a single sentence: the first says the troops came home because American voters demanded it, the second says they came home because that was what the negotiators needed Hanoi to see.","Test whether that really is a difference of view: both writers agree that the withdrawal happened and that Nixon ran it, so the disagreement is about what set the timetable, not about what occurred.","Anchor each half of the difference in its own interpretation's words, 'the timetable was set in Washington by elections' against 'troop numbers fell when the negotiators wanted them to fall'.","Write it as one difference stated in the opening sentence and then supported from both interpretations, rather than as two summaries placed side by side."],"cues":["What does each writer say decided the pace of the withdrawal?","Do they disagree about what happened or about what drove it?","Which phrase in each interpretation carries the view?","What shape should the finished answer take?"]},{"title":"Question 3(c): explaining why two historians differ","setup":"Use the same practice pair as the example above, and remember that they stand in place of the room's printed interpretations for these two examples only. Interpretation 1, from a study of American politics and the war by a historian writing in the 1990s: Nixon withdrew because the voters made him. Opinion had turned against the draft and against the casualty lists long before he took office, and every announced troop reduction bought him quiet at home. Vietnamisation was the label; the timetable was set in Washington by elections, not in Saigon by the fighting. Interpretation 2, from a history of the war's diplomacy by a historian writing in the 2010s: The withdrawal was a bargaining position, not a retreat. Nixon and Kissinger needed Hanoi to believe that the troops would go whatever happened and that the bombers would come back if an agreement were broken. Troop numbers fell when the negotiators wanted them to fall. The question is: 'Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about the reasons for the American withdrawal of troops from Vietnam.' (4 marks) On the paper this question also prints 'You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer'; no sources belong to this practice pair, so here the reason has to come from the interpretations themselves and from what each of them is taken from.","steps":["Begin from what each book is a study of, because that is printed for you: one examines American politics, the other the diplomacy of the war, so the two writers are putting different questions to the same troop withdrawals.","Say what each question makes its writer look at: a study of politics reads the withdrawal through opinion at home, the draft and the dates of elections, while a history of diplomacy reads it through what was said at the negotiating table and what Hanoi and Saigon were meant to believe.","Show the reason at work in the wording: one writer measures the withdrawal against what voters would tolerate and the other against what a bargain required, so the same falling troop numbers are made to mean different things.","Finish with one reason properly substantiated, not a list of three: name the reason, then show it working in each interpretation in turn."],"cues":["What is each interpretation taken from?","What would each writer therefore have spent most time reading?","How does each writer's question change what the falling troop numbers mean?","How many reasons does this question actually want?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Why were the elections promised at Geneva for 1956 never held, and who supported that decision?","a":"Ngo Dinh Diem refused to hold them because Ho Chi Minh was widely expected to win a nationwide vote, and the United States backed his refusal, so the division of Vietnam at the seventeenth parallel became permanent."},{"q":"Why is Source C still useful for an enquiry into American methods, even though it was written to persuade reporters that the war was being won?","a":"Because its purpose is the evidence: a command that briefs weekly kill totals and says that figures for civilian casualties 'are not compiled by this command' is showing exactly how it measured progress and what it chose not to measure."},{"q":"Each interpretation is taken from a different kind of book. What kind is each, and in which decade was each written?","a":"Interpretation 1 is from a study of American military strategy in Vietnam, by a historian writing in the 1980s; Interpretation 2 is from a general history of the Vietnam War, by a historian writing in the 2000s. Provenance of that kind is the first thing to read off an interpretation, because 3(c) asks you to explain a difference of view and not merely to describe it."},{"q":"In what sense was the Tet Offensive both a military defeat and a political victory for the communists?","a":"Militarily, the more than 84,000 attackers held no city, Hue was retaken within weeks, the general rising never came and losses ran into the tens of thousands, crippling the southern Vietcong. Politically, it destroyed American belief that the war was being won: within two months Johnson had limited the bombing and announced he would not seek re-election."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 3 Section A (16 marks): Question 1 prints Source A and asks 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about…', answered in a table with two boxes for each inference (4 marks); Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b), and you answer one of them — each is 'Explain why…' with two stimulus points and 'You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks).","Paper 3 Section B (36 marks), all of it Question 3: (a) 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into…?' (8); (b) 'What is the main difference between these views?' (4); (c) 'Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about…' (4); (d) 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about…?' (16, plus up to 4 marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and the use of specialist terminology).","The stimulus caps printed on the 12-mark 'Explain why…': 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'. There is no cap at Level 2. Question 3(d) prints no stimulus points, so no such cap applies to it.","Question 1 is point-marked, not levelled: 1 mark for each valid inference up to two, and a second mark for each one given for supporting detail taken from the source. Two developed pairs is the whole answer.","Timing on Paper 3: 1 hour 30 minutes for 52 marks, so roughly a minute a mark plus thinking time. About 5 minutes on Question 1, 18 on Question 2, 12 on 3(a), 6 each on 3(b) and 3(c) and about 40 on 3(d) including the final check for the SPaG marks.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose. Usefulness is always judged for the enquiry the question names, never in general, and contextual knowledge earns nothing unless it is used on the sources or on the interpretations."],"questions":[{"q":"Study Source A in the sources section of this room. Give two things you can infer from Source A about the Strategic Hamlet Program in South Vietnam. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for each valid inference, up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail taken from Source A. Credit hamlets that existed only on paper, villagers made hostile by the move, or the Vietcong still operating inside the hamlets.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: many of the strategic hamlets existed only on paper. Details in the source that tell me this: of the seven the officer visited, only two have 'a ditch, a fence and a watchtower and a militia that turns out', and Saigon 'counts a hamlet as finished when the province chief reports it, and the province chief is not visited'. (ii) What I can infer: the programme turned the villagers against the government that ran it. Details in the source that tell me this: the families 'were moved out of their old villages in the planting season, without the payment they were promised', and have been told to build the defences themselves.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["An inference is something the source makes you certain of without saying it outright; ask what must be true if an adviser writes this to his own headquarters.","Read the report twice: once for what it says about the hamlets as fortifications, and once for what it says about the people who were put inside them.","Give each answer in two parts, a claim the source never states and then the exact words that force it."],"codes":["33-3 — 1 Reasons for US involvement in the conflict in Vietnam, 1954–63: Greater involvement under Kennedy","33-3 — 1 Reasons for US involvement in the conflict in Vietnam, 1954–63: Reasons for greater US involvement under Eisenhower"]},{"q":"Study Source A in the sources section of this room. Give two things you can infer from Source A about the difficulties faced by American advisers in South Vietnam in 1963. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for each valid inference, up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail from Source A. Credit advisers having little influence, being unable to check what they were told, or not knowing who supported whom in the villages.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: American advisers had very little influence over the South Vietnamese officers they were sent to help. Details in the source that tell me this: the writer says 'I am asked for helicopters and for money, and never for advice', and the divisional commander's officers are 'chosen for loyalty to the palace and will not take losses'. (ii) What I can infer: advisers could not find out where the villagers' loyalties lay. Details in the source that tell me this: 'When I ask them who comes in at night they say nobody', and yet the Vietcong tax collector had been in the largest hamlet a week before and the militia knew it.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what the adviser can and cannot make happen, and what he can and cannot find out.","Two of his sentences are about himself and two are about what the villagers tell him; work on those.","Set out a claim the source implies, then the phrase that makes it certain, for each of your two answers."],"codes":["33-3 — 1 Reasons for US involvement in the conflict in Vietnam, 1954–63: Greater involvement under Kennedy","33-3 — 1 Reasons for US involvement in the conflict in Vietnam, 1954–63: Reasons for greater US involvement under Eisenhower"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in the sources section of this room. They give different views about the reasons for the failure of the United States to win the war in Vietnam. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3–4) needs a key difference of view supported from both interpretations: Interpretation 1 blames the methods the United States chose, Interpretation 2 the strength of the enemy and the weakness of South Vietnam. Differences of surface detail only stay in Level 1.","sol":"The main difference is over whether the defeat was self-inflicted. Interpretation 1 argues that the United States 'was defeated by the war it chose to fight': it met a political struggle in the villages with bombing, sweeps 'whose success was counted in enemy bodies' and napalm, and it concludes that 'the methods, not the enemy, decided this war'. Interpretation 2 argues that no method would have served, because 'no American strategy could have produced a South Vietnam able to stand on its own' against an enemy with 'village organisation that survived every sweep'. One makes the Americans the cause of their own defeat; the other makes the task impossible from the start.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Reduce each interpretation to one sentence beginning 'The United States lost because…' and set the two sentences against each other.","Check that your difference is one of view: both writers agree the United States lost, so look for what each says did the causal work.","State the difference first in a single sentence, then support each half with words taken from its own interpretation."],"codes":["33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The methods used by the USA","33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The guerrilla tactics used by the Vietcong"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in the sources section of this room. Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about the reasons for the failure of the United States to win the war in Vietnam. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3–4) needs one explained reason, effectively substantiated: for example that the two historians are writing different kinds of book, ask different questions and therefore weigh different evidence. A bare assertion that they simply disagree stays in Level 1.","sol":"One reason is that the two historians set out to study different things and so weigh different evidence. Interpretation 1 is taken from a study of American military strategy, so its evidence is what the United States itself did: the bombing from March 1965, the sweeps counted in bodies, the defoliant and the napalm. Material of that kind makes the defeat look self-inflicted, and Source C, a briefing built on weekly kill totals, is exactly such material. Interpretation 2 is a general history of the whole war, so it also weighs the other side, and from the supply road, the village organisation and the plotting generals in Saigon the defeat looks unavoidable.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Historians differ legitimately when they ask different questions of the past; look at what each interpretation is taken from.","Ask which evidence each writer would have spent most time reading, and what that evidence would make look important.","Name one reason, then show it at work in both interpretations, using a source if it illustrates the kind of evidence involved."],"codes":["33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The methods used by the USA","33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The guerrilla tactics used by the Vietcong"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in the sources section of this room. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the difficulties faced by American forces fighting the Vietcong in the years 1965–68? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6–8): utility judged for this enquiry, with developed reasoning on how nature, origin and purpose affect the content, and context used to interpret. Credit Source B on mines, an unseen enemy and ground that could not be held, and Source C on the body count and the sanctuaries in Laos.","sol":"Source B is very useful for this enquiry because it was written by a man doing the fighting. Going 'out over the same ground three times' and coming back 'with nothing to show for it but casualties' is the central American difficulty in one sentence: there was no ground to capture, so an area cleared in the morning belonged to the Vietcong again at night. The losses to 'a wire under a paddy dyke' and 'a pit of sharpened stakes', suffered without seeing 'the man who laid any of them', match what is known of guerrilla tactics in these years, when mines and booby traps caused a large share of American casualties. Its nature strengthens it: a private letter to a brother has no censor to satisfy, so the admission that he could not say how the count of nine was reached is candid in a way no official report would be. Source C is useful in a different way. Its purpose, to persuade reporters that the enemy was being worn down, makes its figures a claim rather than a measurement, but the claim itself exposes two difficulties: progress had to be expressed as a kill ratio because nothing was being captured, and questions about supplies through Laos are pushed away to another command, which is the Ho Chi Minh Trail the bombing could not close. Source B is the more useful for the experience of the difficulty, Source C for showing how the command tried to disguise it.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Usefulness is always usefulness for something: write the enquiry out before you look at either source.","Take each source as content, then nature, origin and purpose, then the context that confirms or qualifies it, and only then compare the two.","Open with a judgement on Source B rather than a description of it, and make the first sentence say what kind of witness the writer is."],"codes":["33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The guerrilla tactics used by the Vietcong","33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The methods used by the USA"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in the sources section of this room. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the methods used by the United States in the war in Vietnam in the years 1965–68? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6–8): criteria-based judgements with developed reasoning on how provenance affects the content. Credit Source B on search and destroy at ground level and Source C on the body count and the bombing of the North; qualify both, since neither reaches defoliants or napalm.","sol":"Source C is the more useful of the two for this enquiry because it shows the American command describing its own methods to the press. A week reported as 'eleven hundred and forty enemy killed against ninety-six of our own', with 'air strikes and artillery' accounting for most of the enemy total, is the body count in operation: because no territory was being taken, success in search and destroy had to be measured in dead, which rewarded exaggeration. The reference to the rail line 'towards the Chinese border' and to supply craft is Operation Rolling Thunder, running from 2 March 1965 to the halt of November 1968. Its provenance both limits and strengthens it: a briefing designed to show progress will select, but the selection is itself evidence of what the command thought counted as progress, and the admission that civilian casualties 'are not compiled by this command' tells an enquiry into methods a great deal. Source B is useful for the same methods at ground level, where a sweep burns a hamlet after finding 'a tunnel mouth under a cooking fire and a sack of rice', and where the writer cannot say how 'the count for the sweep' of nine was reached, which is the body count seen from below. Neither source reaches the chemical side of American method, the nineteen million gallons of herbicide sprayed between 1962 and 1971 or the use of napalm, so the pair is partial rather than complete.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["The enquiry is into what the Americans did, so ask of each source whether it shows a method being used or a method being explained.","A source written to persuade is not thereby useless; say what its purpose makes it good evidence of.","Decide first which of the two is closer to the enquiry, and open with that judgement and the reason for it."],"codes":["33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The methods used by the USA","33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The guerrilla tactics used by the Vietcong"]},{"q":"2(a) Explain why the USA became more involved in Vietnam under President Eisenhower in the years 1954–60. You may use the following in your answer: • the fall of Dien Bien Phu (1954) • the weaknesses of the Diem government. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10–12): causation analysed with precise knowledge — the French defeat and the Geneva Accords, the domino theory and containment after China and Korea, Diem's refusal of the 1956 elections and his narrow, family government, the advisers and aid. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"American involvement grew under Eisenhower because a French defeat left a gap that Cold War thinking made it impossible to leave empty, and because the man chosen to fill it could not stand without help. The first reason is the collapse of French Indochina. The garrison at Dien Bien Phu surrendered on 7 May 1954 after a siege of fifty-six days, and the Geneva Accords of 21 July 1954 divided Vietnam at the seventeenth parallel, with Ho Chi Minh's communists in the North, a non-communist state in the South and elections promised for 1956 to reunite the country. France was leaving; if Washington did nothing the South would follow the North, so the United States took over the training, the funding and the political sponsorship of a state France had abandoned. The second reason is the one the stimulus points do not prompt: the domino theory and the politics of containment. Eisenhower had put the argument publicly in April 1954 — knock over one country in South East Asia and its neighbours fall in turn — and after the communist victory in China in 1949 and the Korean War, no president could afford to be the one who let the next domino go. Vietnam therefore stopped being a French colonial problem and became a test of American credibility. The third reason is Diem himself. He took power in the South in 1955 and, with American backing, refused to hold the 1956 elections because Ho Chi Minh was expected to win them, so the division became permanent and the United States owned it. A Catholic ruling a mostly Buddhist country, he gave office to his family, reversed the land redistribution that had given peasants their fields and used the army against critics, which made opposition in the countryside grow. Every weakness deepened his dependence: by January 1961 about seven hundred American military personnel were in South Vietnam propping him up. The three reasons run together. France's defeat created the vacancy, the domino theory made filling it compulsory, and Diem's incompetence meant that the filling had to be permanent and growing.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Involvement grows for two different kinds of reason: what the United States feared in the world, and what was going wrong inside South Vietnam. Build the answer on both.","Plan three reasons of different kinds and make sure at least one is not prompted by either bullet point.","Open on what the French defeat of 1954 left behind, and finish that paragraph by saying what it obliged the United States to take over."],"codes":["33-3 — 1 Reasons for US involvement in the conflict in Vietnam, 1954–63: Reasons for greater US involvement under Eisenhower"]},{"q":"2(b) Explain why US involvement in Vietnam deepened under President Kennedy in the years 1961–63. You may use the following in your answer: • the Strategic Hamlet Program • the overthrow of Diem (1963). You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10–12): sustained causal analysis — the growth of the Vietcong and Ap Bac, the Strategic Hamlet Program and its failure, Kennedy's Cold War position, the Buddhist crisis and the coup, advisers rising from about 700 to about 16,000. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Involvement deepened under Kennedy because each attempt to make South Vietnam able to defend itself failed in a way that could only be answered with more Americans. The first reason is the growth of the enemy, which is the point beyond the stimulus. The National Liberation Front, founded in December 1960 and known to Americans as the Vietcong, was killing government officials and taking villages faster than Saigon could reply. At Ap Bac on 2 January 1963 a Vietcong force of a few hundred beat a South Vietnamese force several times its size that was equipped with American helicopters, armour and advisers; three Americans were killed and five helicopters were lost. The lesson drawn in Washington was that the South Vietnamese needed closer American supervision, so the number of military advisers rose from about seven hundred in 1960 to roughly sixteen thousand by the end of 1963. The second reason is the Strategic Hamlet Program. From March 1962 peasants were moved into fenced, ditched and guarded hamlets so that the guerrillas could not live among them, and it required American money, helicopters, materials and advisers on the ground. It failed, because families were moved off ancestral land and the graves of their ancestors, often without the compensation they had been promised, and because thousands of the hamlets the government counted were never finished. Failure, however, produced deeper involvement rather than withdrawal. The third reason is the overthrow of Diem. The Buddhist crisis of 1963 — the monk Thich Quang Duc burning himself to death in a Saigon street on 11 June and the raids on pagodas in August — convinced Washington that Diem could not win the war. The generals were allowed to understand that the United States would not oppose them, and on 1 November 1963 they seized power, killing Diem and his brother Nhu the next day. That last step is the decisive one. By helping to remove the ruler it had installed, the United States made itself responsible for whatever government followed, and the coups of 1964 meant that only Americans could hold the South together.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what each American initiative was supposed to achieve, and what happened when it did not achieve it.","The two bullets are both about the South Vietnamese state; find a reason of your own about the enemy.","Begin with the state of the war in the countryside in 1962 and 1963, and let the American response follow from it."],"codes":["33-3 — 1 Reasons for US involvement in the conflict in Vietnam, 1954–63: Greater involvement under Kennedy","33-3 — 1 Reasons for US involvement in the conflict in Vietnam, 1954–63: Reasons for greater US involvement under Eisenhower"]},{"q":"2(a) Explain why President Johnson escalated the war in Vietnam in the years 1964–65. You may use the following in your answer: • the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution (1964) • the weakness of the government of South Vietnam. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10–12): analysis directed at causation — the Tonkin incident and the Resolution of 7 August 1964, the coups and collapse in Saigon, Pleiku and the move to Rolling Thunder and ground troops, and Johnson's domestic fear of losing Vietnam. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Johnson escalated because South Vietnam was visibly failing, because Congress had handed him the means to act without asking again, and because the political cost of letting the South fall looked worse to him than the cost of fighting. The first reason is the state of South Vietnam. After Diem's murder in November 1963 one set of generals replaced another in Saigon, while the Vietcong took district after district and the South Vietnamese army lost men faster than it could replace them. By the beginning of 1965 the South looked likely to collapse within months, so escalation was not a choice between war and peace but between American troops and a communist Vietnam. The second reason is the freedom Congress gave him. The destroyer Maddox reported an attack by North Vietnamese torpedo boats on 2 August 1964, and a second attack was reported on 4 August which historians now regard as almost certainly mistaken. Johnson took the reports to Congress, and on 7 August 1964 the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution passed the House of Representatives 416 votes to none and the Senate 88 to 2, authorising him to take all necessary measures. From then on he could bomb and deploy without a declaration of war. The third reason, beyond either bullet point, is the logic of the first steps themselves. After the Vietcong attacked the American base at Pleiku on 7 February 1965, killing eight Americans, Operation Rolling Thunder opened on 2 March 1965; the airfields flying those missions then had to be defended, so three and a half thousand Marines landed at Da Nang on 8 March 1965; ground troops in turn needed protection and then a mission, and numbers reached about 184,000 by the end of 1965. Each measure created the need for the next. Underneath all three lay Johnson's own calculation: he had watched Truman blamed for losing China, and he believed that losing Vietnam would destroy the Great Society reforms he cared about far more. That is why the escalation was continuous and why it was never explained to the American people as a war.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Escalation needs an occasion, a means and a motive; sort your material into those three before you write.","The Tonkin Resolution explains what Johnson was allowed to do, not why he wanted to do it — make sure one paragraph supplies the want.","Start with what was actually happening in South Vietnam in 1964, and let the American decisions answer it."],"codes":["33-3 — 2 Escalation of the conflict under Johnson: Reasons for the escalation of the conflict","33-3 — 1 Reasons for US involvement in the conflict in Vietnam, 1954–63: Greater involvement under Kennedy"]},{"q":"2(b) Explain why the Vietcong were so difficult for United States forces to defeat in the years 1964–68. You may use the following in your answer: • guerrilla tactics • the Ho Chi Minh Trail. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10–12): causation analysed with precise detail — ambush, mines and booby traps, the Cu Chi tunnels, the Trail through Laos and Cambodia, the National Liberation Front's hold on the villages, and a willingness to take losses the United States could not match. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The Vietcong were hard to defeat because they refused the war American forces were built to fight, because they could not be cut off from their supplies, and because they could not be separated from the people among whom they lived. The first reason is their tactics. They ambushed and withdrew rather than holding ground, moved and attacked at night, and mined tracks and paddy dykes; mines, sharpened stakes and other booby traps caused a large share of all American casualties and were laid by men the patrols never saw. When pressed they went underground: the tunnel system at Cu Chi, north-west of Saigon, ran for more than two hundred kilometres and contained kitchens, stores, workshops and hospitals, beneath ground that American commanders believed they controlled. Against an enemy like that, overwhelming firepower had nothing to hit. The second reason is supply. The Ho Chi Minh Trail was not a road but a web of tracks, fords and bypasses running through Laos and Cambodia, carried along by lorry and by bicycles loaded far beyond a man's weight, with repair gangs filling craters faster than aircraft could make them. Because Laos and Cambodia were neutral, the sanctuaries at the far end were off limits to American ground troops until 1970, so the flow of men and weapons could be harassed but never stopped. The third reason, which neither bullet point prompts, is the villages. The National Liberation Front taxed, recruited and governed in the hamlets, so a patrol could not tell a farmer from a fighter, and American attempts to solve that problem made it worse: search and destroy burned homes and food stores, the body count encouraged the recording of dead civilians as dead enemy, and every family ruined produced recruits. Behind all three lay a difference in what each side could bear: the communists were fighting in their own country and would accept losses no American administration could explain at home, so they had only to keep going while the United States had to win.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what American firepower needed in order to work, and then about each thing the Vietcong denied it.","The two bullets cover fighting and supply; the war was also political, so find your third reason there.","Open on the kind of fighting the Vietcong chose and say what it took away from the Americans, rather than listing the tactics."],"codes":["33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The guerrilla tactics used by the Vietcong","33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The methods used by the USA"]},{"q":"2(a) Explain why the Tet Offensive of 1968 was a turning point in the Vietnam War. You may use the following in your answer: • the scale of the communist attacks • the request for more American troops. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10–12): analysis directed at the turning point — the surprise and scale of 30 to 31 January 1968, the gap between the attacks and two years of official optimism, Westmoreland's request for 206,000 men, Johnson's announcement of 31 March 1968, and the crippling of the southern Vietcong. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Tet was a turning point because it destroyed the belief on which American policy rested, and once that belief was gone the question changed from how to win to how to leave. The first reason is the scale and the surprise. On the night of 30 to 31 January 1968, during the truce for the Vietnamese New Year, more than 84,000 Vietcong and North Vietnamese troops attacked across South Vietnam, striking over a hundred towns and 36 of the 44 provincial capitals; a squad blew its way into the compound of the American embassy in Saigon, Hue was captured and held for weeks before being retaken street by street, and the Marine base at Khe Sanh had been under siege since 21 January. The importance of that is not military, because the offensive failed everywhere; it is that for two years American commanders had been reporting that the enemy no longer had the strength for anything of the kind. The second reason is what the generals asked for afterwards. General Westmoreland's request for another 206,000 men became public in March 1968, and to a public that had just been told the war was nearly won it read as an admission that there was no end in sight. Johnson refused, and on 31 March 1968 he limited the bombing of the North, offered negotiations and announced that he would not seek re-election; talks opened in Paris in May. The third reason, beyond the stimulus, is what the fighting did on both sides. Communist losses ran into the tens of thousands and the southern Vietcong were crippled, so the North Vietnamese Army carried the war thereafter and the South's own insurgency never recovered; yet the fighting was filmed and broadcast, so the distance between official claims and the pictures on American screens widened into what was called the credibility gap. Tet is therefore a turning point in the precise sense that both sides changed their war aims after it: Hanoi went to the conference table, and Washington began looking for a way out.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["A turning point is a change of direction: decide what was travelling in one direction before Tet and in another after it.","Separate what happened on the ground from what happened to opinion, and make the answer explain the gap between them.","Open by saying what American policy assumed before 30 January 1968, so that the offensive can be shown destroying the assumption."],"codes":["33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The key features and significance of the Tet Offensive, 1968","33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The methods used by the USA"]},{"q":"2(b) Explain why Vietnamisation failed to save South Vietnam. You may use the following in your answer: • the Army of the Republic of Vietnam (ARVN) • the attacks on Cambodia and Laos. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10–12): sustained causal analysis — the ARVN's size against its leadership, desertion and dependence on American air power, Lam Son 719 and Cambodia, the terms of the Paris Accords and the withdrawal of American support by Congress, and Saigon's political weakness. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Vietnamisation failed because it transferred the fighting without transferring the things the fighting depended on: American air power, and a government in Saigon the countryside would defend. The first reason is the army itself. Regular ARVN strength grew from about 427,000 at the end of 1968 to about 516,000 by 1971, and with territorial forces counted in more than a million South Vietnamese were under arms with modern American equipment; but the army was officered by patronage rather than merit, desertion was heavy, and it had been trained from the start to call in American aircraft and helicopters whenever it met serious resistance. Size was therefore no measure of capability. The second reason is that the operations meant to prove the policy disproved it. The attack on the communist sanctuaries in Cambodia from 29 April 1970 captured large quantities of supplies but never found the headquarters it was launched to destroy, and it drove the communists deeper into Cambodia; then Lam Son 719, the ARVN drive into Laos from 8 February to 25 March 1971, which Congress had obliged the South Vietnamese to fight without American ground troops, ended in a costly retreat that was filmed and broadcast. The third reason, beyond the stimulus points, is the settlement and what followed it. The Paris Peace Accords of 27 January 1973 required the Americans to leave but allowed North Vietnamese troops to stay where they stood inside South Vietnam, so the ceasefire left the enemy in position; Congress then barred further American military action in Indochina and cut aid in 1974, removing the air support on which the whole policy silently depended. Behind all of this stood the political failure: President Thieu's government was narrow and corrupt and had never won the loyalty of the villages, so when the North attacked in 1975 the army it had built collapsed and Saigon fell on 30 April 1975.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what Vietnamisation was silently relying on, and then what happened to each of those things.","Numbers of soldiers are not the same as fighting power; make one paragraph explain the difference.","Open by saying precisely what the policy was supposed to achieve, so that each paragraph can show a reason it could not."],"codes":["33-3 — 4 Changes under Nixon, 1969–73: The key features of Vietnamisation","33-3 — 4 Changes under Nixon, 1969–73: The Nixon Doctrine and the withdrawal of US troops","33-3 — 4 Changes under Nixon, 1969–73: Attacks on Cambodia and Laos"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in the sources section of this room. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the reasons for the failure of the United States to win the war in Vietnam? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13–16): both views evaluated, the way each conveys its view analysed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit the Trail, village organisation and Saigon's generals; against, Rolling Thunder's limits, search and destroy, the body count and the chemical campaign.","sol":"Interpretation 2 argues that the American defeat was written into the task: 'no American strategy could have produced a South Vietnam able to stand on its own', because the other side had a cause people would die for, 'village organisation that survived every sweep' and a supply road 'repaired faster than aircraft could cut it', while Saigon rested on generals who had been plotting against one another since 1963. Interpretation 1 puts the weight in the opposite place, arguing that the United States 'was defeated by the war it chose to fight' and that 'the methods, not the enemy, decided this war'. The criterion for judging between them is whether a different American method could plausibly have produced a different outcome. There is strong support for Interpretation 2. The Ho Chi Minh Trail was a web of tracks through neutral Laos and Cambodia, repaired by labour gangs faster than Rolling Thunder, which ran from 2 March 1965 to the halt of November 1968, could crater it, and the sanctuaries at its end were closed to American ground troops until 1970. The National Liberation Front, founded in December 1960, taxed and governed the villages, which is why an area swept in the morning was communist again by night. South Vietnam's own weakness is equally well attested: Diem refused the elections promised for 1956, was overthrown and killed on 1 and 2 November 1963, and the coups that followed left a state that only Americans could hold together. Yet Interpretation 1 has the better of several arguments, and its evidence is precise. American method was not neutral; it manufactured enemies. Search and destroy burned homes and food, the body count rewarded exaggeration and made dead civilians easy to record as dead enemy, Operation Ranch Hand sprayed some nineteen million gallons of herbicide between 1962 and 1971, and at My Lai on 16 March 1968 American soldiers killed several hundred unarmed villagers. The Strategic Hamlet Program, begun in March 1962, had already shown the pattern, moving families off ancestral land without the compensation promised. It is also worth noticing how each writer conveys the view. Interpretation 2 uses the language of impossibility — 'no American strategy could have', 'unwinnable at any price' — which removes choice from every American decision-maker. Interpretation 1 uses the language of choice — 'the war it chose to fight' — which makes every method a decision that could have been taken differently. Neither language is neutral, and each writer's book explains it: a study of strategy must find strategy decisive, a general history must weigh both sides. My judgement is that Interpretation 2 is right about the limit and wrong about the explanation. No plausible American method could have made Saigon a government worth dying for, so the war was probably unwinnable. But the speed and the manner of the defeat were American work: the methods turned a hard war into an unwinnable one at home as well as abroad, and that is the part of the story Interpretation 2 leaves out.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first what Interpretation 2 is actually claiming, and name the test you will judge the claim by.","Use Interpretation 1 as your case against, and support both sides with dated detail rather than with the interpretations restated.","Open by putting Interpretation 2's claim into your own words and stating the criterion your judgement will apply."],"codes":["33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The guerrilla tactics used by the Vietcong","33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The methods used by the USA","33-3 — 4 Changes under Nixon, 1969–73: The key features of Vietnamisation"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in the sources section of this room. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the significance of the Tet Offensive of 1968? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13–16): both views weighed and the differences of view analysed, context precisely selected. For agreement credit the scale of the attacks after three years of war; against, the crippling of the Vietcong and the collapse of confidence in official claims.","sol":"Interpretation 2 says that the Tet Offensive 'was a military disaster for the communists, but its real lesson was that after three years of war and half a million American troops they could still strike everywhere at once'. Its significance, on this account, lies in what the offensive revealed about the enemy's strength. Interpretation 1 agrees that Tet 'cost the communists tens of thousands of dead' but locates the significance somewhere else, in the American side: the offensive 'shattered American confidence, because commanders had spent two years promising that such an attack was no longer possible'. The test is whether Tet mattered chiefly for what it showed about Hanoi or for what it did to Washington. Interpretation 2 is well supported on the facts. On the night of 30 to 31 January 1968, during the New Year truce, more than 84,000 communist troops attacked over a hundred towns and 36 of the 44 provincial capitals; a squad fought its way into the compound of the American embassy in Saigon, Hue was held for weeks, and Khe Sanh had been under siege since 21 January and was not relieved until 8 April. To mount that after three years of Rolling Thunder and with American strength approaching half a million was a demonstration of reach that no body count had predicted. But the interpretation understates two things. The first is how badly the offensive hurt the side that launched it: no city was held, the general rising it was meant to provoke never came, losses ran into the tens of thousands and the southern Vietcong were so weakened that the North Vietnamese Army carried most of the fighting for the rest of the war, which is a considerable cost for a demonstration. The second is the American political collapse that followed, which is where Interpretation 1 is strongest. General Westmoreland's request for another 206,000 men became public in March 1968 and read as an admission of stalemate; on 31 March 1968 Johnson limited the bombing, offered talks and announced he would not stand again; negotiations opened in Paris in May. The way each writer conveys the view is instructive. Interpretation 2 measures Tet against what the communists could do, counting years of war and hundreds of thousands of troops, so significance becomes a question of capability. Interpretation 1 measures it against what had been promised, so significance becomes a question of credibility. Both are describing the same weeks. My judgement is that Interpretation 2 identifies the cause but not the significance. The communists' ability to strike everywhere is what made the offensive shocking, but the offensive mattered because of the shock, not the capability: militarily Tet set the communists back, and it is a turning point only because it changed what Americans believed their government and their generals were telling them.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Significance is always significance for something; say what you are measuring the offensive against before you agree or disagree.","Both interpretations accept that the communists were beaten in the fighting, so build the essay on what each says followed from that.","Open by stating Interpretation 2's claim about what Tet revealed, and name the criterion on which you will judge it."],"codes":["33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The key features and significance of the Tet Offensive, 1968","33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The guerrilla tactics used by the Vietcong","33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The methods used by the USA"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in the sources section of this room. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the effects of the military methods used by the United States in Vietnam? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13–16): both interpretations used, the way each conveys its view analysed, precise context. For agreement credit the limits of bombing against the Trail; against, the effect of sweeps, the body count, defoliants and My Lai on opinion at home and in the villages.","sol":"Interpretation 2 treats American methods as largely beside the point: whatever was tried, 'no American strategy could have produced a South Vietnam able to stand on its own', because village organisation 'survived every sweep' and the supply road was 'repaired faster than aircraft could cut it'. Interpretation 1 makes the methods the decisive cause, arguing that the United States answered a political struggle with bombing, sweeps 'whose success was counted in enemy bodies' and 'defoliant and napalm over the paddy fields', so that 'every hamlet burned and every family moved recruited for the other side'. The question is what the methods actually did, and the criterion I shall use is whether their effects were merely insufficient, as Interpretation 2 implies, or actively harmful, as Interpretation 1 claims. Interpretation 2 is convincing about the bombing. Operation Rolling Thunder ran from 2 March 1965 to the halt of November 1968 and could not close the Ho Chi Minh Trail, partly because the North had little heavy industry to destroy and its weapons came from the Soviet Union and China, and partly because Washington itself restricted targets near Hanoi, Haiphong and the Chinese border for fear of a wider war. Against an enemy supplied from outside and repairing tracks faster than they could be cratered, no bombing campaign of that kind would have worked. On the ground, though, Interpretation 1 is closer to the evidence, because the effects were not neutral. Search and destroy sent units through hamlets to burn shelters and food stores; because there was no ground to hold, progress was measured by the body count, which rewarded exaggeration and made civilian dead easy to record as enemy; Operation Ranch Hand sprayed some nineteen million gallons of herbicide between 1962 and 1971, over half of it Agent Orange, and napalm burned villages; at My Lai on 16 March 1968 American soldiers killed several hundred unarmed villagers, and when the story broke in 1969 it did more damage in the United States than any communist attack. The Strategic Hamlet Program from March 1962 had already shown what moving families off their land produced. Each writer's language carries the argument. Interpretation 1 pairs actions with consequences — burned, moved, recruited — so method and effect are chained together. Interpretation 2 speaks of what 'survived every sweep', which puts the American methods in the background as weather that the other side endured. My judgement is that Interpretation 2 is right about the ceiling and wrong about the floor. The best imaginable American method probably could not have won the war, because the weakness it had to repair was political and lay in Saigon. But the methods actually used made matters worse rather than merely failing to make them better: they recruited for the National Liberation Front in the villages and destroyed consent for the war at home, and that is an effect Interpretation 2 does not account for.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Distinguish methods that did not work from methods that did harm; the whole judgement turns on that difference.","Give the bombing and the ground war separate paragraphs, because the two interpretations do better on different halves of the story.","Open by saying what Interpretation 2 implies about American methods and naming the test you will apply to it."],"codes":["33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The methods used by the USA","33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The guerrilla tactics used by the Vietcong","33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The key features and significance of the Tet Offensive, 1968"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date did Congress pass the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution?","a":"7 August 1964."},{"q":"What was the name of the sustained American bombing campaign against North Vietnam that began on 2 March 1965?","a":"Operation Rolling Thunder."},{"q":"On what date did the Tet Offensive begin?","a":"The night of 30 to 31 January 1968."},{"q":"On what date did Saigon fall to North Vietnamese forces?","a":"30 April 1975."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Dien Bien Phu and the Geneva Accords, 1954","back":"The French garrison at Dien Bien Phu surrendered on 7 May 1954 after a fifty-six-day siege. The Geneva Accords of 21 July 1954 divided Vietnam at the seventeenth parallel and promised elections in 1956.","code":"33-3 — 1 Reasons for US involvement in the conflict in Vietnam, 1954–63: Reasons for greater US involvement under Eisenhower","open":false},{"front":"The domino theory","back":"Eisenhower's argument, put publicly in April 1954, that if one country in South East Asia fell to communism its neighbours would follow. It made holding South Vietnam a test of American credibility.","code":"33-3 — 1 Reasons for US involvement in the conflict in Vietnam, 1954–63: Reasons for greater US involvement under Eisenhower","open":false},{"front":"Ngo Dinh Diem","back":"Ruler of South Vietnam from 1955, backed by the United States. A Catholic in a mostly Buddhist country, he refused to hold the 1956 elections, governed through his own family and returned land to landlords.","code":"33-3 — 1 Reasons for US involvement in the conflict in Vietnam, 1954–63: Reasons for greater US involvement under Eisenhower","open":false},{"front":"The Strategic Hamlet Program","back":"From March 1962 peasants were moved into fenced, guarded hamlets to cut the Vietcong off from them. Families lost ancestral land and graves, and thousands of hamlets existed only in government returns.","code":"33-3 — 1 Reasons for US involvement in the conflict in Vietnam, 1954–63: Greater involvement under Kennedy","open":false},{"front":"The overthrow of Diem, November 1963","back":"After the Buddhist protests, South Vietnamese generals seized power on 1 November 1963 with American knowledge; Diem and his brother Nhu were killed the next day. Unstable military governments followed.","code":"33-3 — 1 Reasons for US involvement in the conflict in Vietnam, 1954–63: Greater involvement under Kennedy","open":false},{"front":"The Gulf of Tonkin, August 1964","back":"American destroyers reported attacks on 2 and 4 August 1964. On 7 August Congress passed the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution, 416 to 0 and 88 to 2, letting Johnson use force without declaring war.","code":"33-3 — 2 Escalation of the conflict under Johnson: Reasons for the escalation of the conflict","open":false},{"front":"The Vietcong","back":"Communist fighters in South Vietnam, organised through the National Liberation Front founded in December 1960. They ambushed and withdrew, fought at night, mined tracks, used tunnels such as those at Cu Chi, and lived among the villagers.","code":"33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The guerrilla tactics used by the Vietcong","open":false},{"front":"The Ho Chi Minh Trail","back":"A network of tracks, fords and bypasses through Laos and Cambodia carrying troops and supplies south by lorry and loaded bicycle. Repair gangs filled bomb craters faster than aircraft could make them.","code":"33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The guerrilla tactics used by the Vietcong","open":false},{"front":"Operation Rolling Thunder","back":"The sustained bombing of North Vietnam, begun on 2 March 1965 and continued to the halt of November 1968. It failed to stop supplies moving south or to force Hanoi to negotiate.","code":"33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The methods used by the USA","open":false},{"front":"Search and destroy and the body count","back":"American units were sent to find and kill the enemy rather than take ground, so progress was measured in enemy dead. The measure rewarded exaggeration and blurred the line between fighters and villagers.","code":"33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The methods used by the USA","open":false},{"front":"Chemical weapons in Vietnam","back":"Operation Ranch Hand sprayed some nineteen million gallons of herbicide between 1962 and 1971, over half of it Agent Orange, to strip cover and destroy crops. Napalm burned villages and the people in them.","code":"33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The methods used by the USA","open":false},{"front":"The Tet Offensive, 1968","back":"On 30 to 31 January 1968 more than 84,000 communist troops attacked over a hundred towns and 36 of the 44 provincial capitals. It failed militarily but destroyed American belief that the war was being won.","code":"33-3 — 3 The nature of the conflict in Vietnam, 1964–68: The key features and significance of the Tet Offensive, 1968","open":false},{"front":"Vietnamisation","back":"Nixon's policy from 1969: expand, equip and train the ARVN with American money so that it could fight while American troops went home. It left the ARVN dependent on American air power.","code":"33-3 — 4 Changes under Nixon, 1969–73: The key features of Vietnamisation","open":false},{"front":"The Nixon Doctrine","back":"Set out at Guam in July 1969: the United States would keep its treaty commitments and supply money and weapons, but allies threatened by communism would have to provide their own soldiers.","code":"33-3 — 4 Changes under Nixon, 1969–73: The Nixon Doctrine and the withdrawal of US troops","open":false},{"front":"Cambodia and Laos, 1970–71","back":"American and South Vietnamese troops invaded Cambodia on 29 April 1970; four students were shot dead at Kent State on 4 May. The ARVN's attack into Laos, Lam Son 719, was driven back in 1971.","code":"33-3 — 4 Changes under Nixon, 1969–73: Attacks on Cambodia and Laos","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The sources and interpretations written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Source A: From a report on the security of one rural district, written by a United States Army officer serving as an adviser to a South Vietnamese division and sent to his superiors at the American military headquarters in Saigon, October 1963. Written for this room in the style of such a report; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — The province chief's returns for this district show eleven strategic hamlets completed since March of last year. I have visited seven of them. Two have a ditch, a fence and a watchtower and a militia that turns out; the rest have a fence in places and no radio, and in one the wire had been carried away for fishing traps. The families were moved out of their old villages in the planting season, without the payment they were promised, and they have been told to build the defences themselves. When I ask them who comes in at night they say nobody. Yet the Vietcong tax collector was in the largest of these hamlets a week ago, and the militia knew it. Saigon counts a hamlet as finished when the province chief reports it, and the province chief is not visited. The divisional commander tells me his officers are chosen for loyalty to the palace and will not take losses, and since the Buddhist protests began in the summer he is more anxious about the city than about this district. I am asked for helicopters and for money, and never for advice.","Source B: From a letter written by a United States Marine serving south of Da Nang to his brother at home, November 1967. Written for this room in the style of a soldier's letter home; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — We have been out over the same ground three times now and each time we come back with nothing to show for it but casualties. The mines are the worst of it. In two weeks the platoon has lost four men to a wire under a paddy dyke and one to a pit of sharpened stakes, and we have never once seen the man who laid any of them. Yesterday we swept a hamlet, found a tunnel mouth under a cooking fire and a sack of rice under the floor, and burned the place, and the people stood out in the paddy and watched us do it. The lieutenant says the count for the sweep was nine. I could not tell you how the nine were counted. Tell Mother the food is good and the mail comes. What I would like to know is how you are supposed to win a country you only hold while you are standing on it.","Source C: From a briefing given to American newspaper and television reporters by a spokesman for the United States military command in Saigon, November 1967. Written for this room in the style of such a briefing; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — In the past week operations in the northern provinces have produced eleven hundred and forty enemy killed against ninety-six of our own, a ratio this command has improved on in every month of this year. Air strikes and artillery accounted for the greater part of the enemy total. In the same period the bombing of the North destroyed a further stretch of the rail line towards the Chinese border and a number of supply craft on the inland waterways. The enemy's ability to replace losses at this rate is limited, and we now believe that the point at which he can no longer replace them is in sight. Questions on the movement of supplies through Laos should be put to the Seventh Air Force. Figures for civilian casualties in the areas of operations are not compiled by this command.","Interpretation 1: From a study of American military strategy in Vietnam, by a historian writing in the 1980s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — The United States was defeated by the war it chose to fight. What faced it in the villages of South Vietnam was a political struggle, and it answered with the firepower it understood: the bombing of the North from March 1965, sweeps whose success was counted in enemy bodies, and defoliant and napalm over the paddy fields. Every hamlet burned and every family moved recruited for the other side and made the Saigon government look like a foreigner's creature. The Tet Offensive of 1968 cost the communists tens of thousands of dead, yet it shattered American confidence, because commanders had spent two years promising that such an attack was no longer possible. The methods, not the enemy, decided this war.","Interpretation 2: From a general history of the Vietnam War, by a historian writing in the 2000s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — No American strategy could have produced a South Vietnam able to stand on its own. The other side possessed what Saigon never possessed: a cause its people would die for in numbers no democracy could match, village organisation that survived every sweep, and a supply road through Laos and Cambodia that was repaired faster than aircraft could cut it. Against that stood governments in Saigon resting on generals who had been plotting against one another since 1963. The Tet Offensive of 1968 was a military disaster for the communists, but its real lesson was that after three years of war and half a million American troops they could still strike everywhere at once. The war was unwinnable at any price the United States was willing to pay.","How the room uses them: Source A is the inference source for Question 1. Sources B and C are the contemporary pair for the 8-mark usefulness question, 3(a), and may also be used in 3(c). Interpretations 1 and 2 are the pair for 3(b), 3(c) and 3(d). Every quotation in the questions, the model answers, the model paragraphs and the plan of this room comes from the five texts above; the two worked examples on Questions 3(b) and 3(c) practise instead on a second, shorter pair of interpretations printed inside those examples, which were also written for this room and are likewise not real."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 3 Question 3(d), 16 marks)","items":["Question: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part played by the two sides' willingness to accept casualties in the outcome of the war in Vietnam? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]. What follows is one paragraph from the middle of the answer, putting the case against Interpretation 2.","Interpretation 2 makes the difference in endurance decisive, crediting the other side with 'a cause its people would die for in numbers no democracy could match', and the arithmetic supports the first half of that claim: when Vietnam published its own figures in 1995 it put its military dead at about 1.1 million, against some 58,000 American dead in the whole war, and even 1968, the worst American year, cost fewer than 17,000. What the claim cannot support is the second half, because it treats American willingness as a fixed property of democracies when what actually moved it was who was being asked to die and on whose word. The draft, not the casualty list, was the pivot: deferments had loaded the burden on the men who could not stay at college, and the lottery drawn on 1 December 1969 was introduced to make the demand look fair. Nor did opposition fade as the dying faded. American deaths fell sharply after 1969 as the troops came home, yet the protests grew, the money was cut and conscription was ended on 27 January 1973. Interpretation 1 points at the mechanism when it insists that this was 'the war it chose to fight': sweeps whose success was 'counted in enemy bodies' had to go looking for contact, so the American casualty rate was itself the product of a method rather than a price the enemy set. Interpretation 2 is therefore right that the two sides could bear unequal losses and wrong about why, since the American limit was political rather than arithmetical.","Why this is Level 4: it 'provides an explained evaluation reviewing the alternative views in coming to a substantiated judgement', because Interpretation 2's claim is granted in one half and tested in the other rather than simply contradicted. The analysis is precise about how the difference of view is conveyed — 'no democracy could match' states a law about political systems, while Interpretation 1's 'the war it chose to fight' turns the same losses into the consequence of decisions. The contextual knowledge is precisely selected rather than general: the published Vietnamese figure of about 1.1 million military dead, the American total of some 58,000, the lottery of 1 December 1969 and the end of conscription on 27 January 1973 all do evaluative work. The line of reasoning is sustained and the closing sentence judges rather than summarises."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same as above — 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part played by the two sides' willingness to accept casualties in the outcome of the war in Vietnam?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks].","Interpretation 2 says the communists were willing to die in huge numbers and that the Americans were not, and I partly agree with this. The communists lost enormous numbers of men but they carried on fighting anyway because they really believed in their cause. The Americans lost far fewer but people at home got very upset about it and there were lots of protests, especially from young men who did not want to be drafted. However, Interpretation 1 says the United States was defeated by the war it chose to fight, which shows that the way the Americans fought was also to blame for the losses they suffered. Overall I think both interpretations have a point, because the communists were more determined and the Americans used the wrong tactics.","Why this is Level 2 and not Level 3: it does offer 'valid evaluative comment to agree with or counter the interpretation' and it does select details from both, which is what Level 2 asks for. What it lacks is Level 3's 'relevant contextual knowledge used directly to support the evaluation'. 'Enormous numbers', 'far fewer' and 'lots of protests' are assertions standing where figures and dates should be: the 1.1 million military dead in Vietnam's own published figure of 1995, the American total of some 58,000, the draft lottery of 1 December 1969 and the ending of conscription on 27 January 1973. It also never shows how the interpretations convey their views, and the judgement is announced in the last sentence rather than reasoned towards, so 'its justification is insecure or undeveloped and a line of reasoning is not sustained'. To reach Level 3, replace every general adjective with a named fact that does evaluative work."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the domino theory, the Diem government and American involvement under Eisenhower, 1954–60","items":["7 May 1954: the French garrison at Dien Bien Phu surrendered to the Vietminh after a siege of fifty-six days, ending French rule in Indochina.","21 July 1954: the Geneva Accords divided Vietnam temporarily at the seventeenth parallel — Ho Chi Minh's communist Democratic Republic in the North, a non-communist state in the South.","The Geneva settlement promised nationwide elections in 1956 to reunite the country; they were never held.","April 1954: Eisenhower set out the domino theory in public — if one country in South East Asia fell to communism its neighbours would follow, so South Vietnam had to be held.","The context for the domino theory was containment after the communist victory in China in 1949 and the Korean War of 1950 to 1953.","Ngo Dinh Diem took power in South Vietnam in 1955; he was a Catholic and a nationalist who had lived in the United States, and Washington backed him as an alternative to Ho Chi Minh.","Diem refused to hold the 1956 elections, with American support, because Ho Chi Minh was widely expected to win a nationwide vote.","Diem's weaknesses: government by his own family, including his brother Ngo Dinh Nhu and Nhu's wife, Madame Nhu; Catholics favoured for office in a mostly Buddhist country; land taken back from peasants and returned to landlords; opponents jailed by the army and the security police.","The Military Assistance Advisory Group trained and equipped the South Vietnamese army; American economic and military aid paid much of its cost.","By the time Eisenhower left office in January 1961 about seven hundred American military personnel were serving in South Vietnam."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Kennedy, the Strategic Hamlet Program and the overthrow of Diem, 1961–63","items":["December 1960: the National Liberation Front was founded to overthrow Diem; its fighters became known to the Americans and to the Saigon government as the Vietcong.","Kennedy sent equipment as well as men — helicopters, armoured personnel carriers and Special Forces — but no American combat units.","American military advisers in South Vietnam rose from about seven hundred in 1960 to roughly sixteen thousand by the end of 1963.","2 January 1963, Ap Bac: a Vietcong force of a few hundred defeated a much larger South Vietnamese force supported by American helicopters, armour and advisers; three Americans were killed and five helicopters lost.","March 1962: the Strategic Hamlet Program began, moving peasants into fenced, ditched and guarded hamlets to separate them from the guerrillas.","The programme failed because families were moved off ancestral land and away from their ancestors' graves, often without the compensation they had been promised and made to build the defences themselves, and because the government's figures for completed hamlets were greatly exaggerated.","11 June 1963: the monk Thich Quang Duc burned himself to death in a Saigon street in protest at Diem's treatment of Buddhists; the photograph appeared worldwide.","August 1963: Diem's forces raided Buddhist pagodas, and Madame Nhu publicly mocked the protests, destroying what remained of the government's reputation in the United States.","1 November 1963: South Vietnamese generals seized power in a coup Washington had let them know it would not oppose; Diem and his brother Nhu were killed the following day.","Kennedy was assassinated on 22 November 1963, three weeks after Diem; a series of unstable military governments followed in Saigon through 1964."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Gulf of Tonkin and Johnson's escalation, 1964–68","items":["2 August 1964: the American destroyer Maddox reported an attack by North Vietnamese torpedo boats in the Gulf of Tonkin.","4 August 1964: a second attack on the Maddox and the Turner Joy was reported; historians now regard it as almost certainly mistaken.","7 August 1964: Congress passed the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution, by 416 votes to none in the House of Representatives and 88 to 2 in the Senate, authorising the President to take all necessary measures without a declaration of war.","7 February 1965: a Vietcong attack on the American base at Pleiku killed eight Americans and provided the occasion for sustained bombing.","2 March 1965: Operation Rolling Thunder, the bombing of North Vietnam, began; it continued until the halt of November 1968.","8 March 1965: three and a half thousand United States Marines landed at Da Nang, the first American ground combat troops in Vietnam, to guard the airbase.","American troop numbers: about 184,000 at the end of 1965, about 385,000 at the end of 1966 and about 486,000 at the end of 1967.","The peak was about 543,000 on 30 April 1969, after which numbers fell every year.","American forces were commanded by General William Westmoreland from 1964 until June 1968.","Johnson feared that losing Vietnam would do to him what losing China in 1949 had done to Truman, and would destroy his Great Society programme of social reform at home."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: how the two sides fought, 1964–68 — guerrilla tactics, Search and Destroy, Rolling Thunder and chemical weapons","items":["Vietcong tactics: ambush and withdraw, attack at night, avoid pitched battle, and mine tracks and paddy dykes; mines, booby traps and pits of sharpened stakes caused a large share of American casualties.","The Cu Chi tunnels, north-west of Saigon, ran for more than two hundred kilometres and contained kitchens, stores, workshops and hospitals.","The Ho Chi Minh Trail was a network of tracks, fords and bypasses through Laos and Cambodia, carrying troops and supplies south by lorry and by heavily loaded bicycle; repair gangs filled bomb craters faster than aircraft could make them.","The National Liberation Front taxed, recruited and governed in the villages, so American patrols could not tell a farmer from a fighter.","Search and destroy: American units were sent out to find and kill the enemy rather than to take ground; major operations included Cedar Falls in January 1967 and Junction City from February to May 1967.","The body count was the measure of progress, because no territory was being captured; it encouraged exaggeration and made dead civilians easy to record as dead enemy.","Operation Rolling Thunder failed to stop supplies moving south: North Vietnam had little heavy industry to destroy, its weapons came from the Soviet Union and China, and targets near Hanoi, Haiphong and the Chinese border were restricted for fear of widening the war.","Chemical weapons: Operation Ranch Hand sprayed some nineteen million gallons of herbicide between 1962 and 1971, over half of it Agent Orange, to strip jungle cover and destroy crops.","Napalm, a petroleum jelly that burns at extreme temperature and sticks to what it touches, was dropped on villages and on suspected positions.","16 March 1968, My Lai: American soldiers of Charlie Company killed several hundred unarmed villagers; the story became public in 1969 and Lieutenant William Calley was convicted in 1971.","Around 58,000 Americans died in the war; Vietnamese deaths on all sides, soldiers and civilians together, are usually estimated in the millions."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Tet Offensive and its consequences, January to April 1968","items":["The offensive opened on the night of 30 to 31 January 1968, during the truce for Tet, the Vietnamese New Year.","More than 84,000 Vietcong and North Vietnamese troops took part, attacking over a hundred towns and 36 of the 44 provincial capitals.","A Vietcong squad blew a hole in the wall of the American embassy compound in Saigon and fought inside it for several hours, with the action filmed for American television.","Hue, the old imperial capital, was captured and held for weeks before being retaken street by street in February; thousands of its inhabitants were killed and much of the city destroyed.","The Marine base at Khe Sanh had been besieged since 21 January 1968 and was not relieved until 8 April; it drew American attention away from the cities before the offensive.","Militarily the offensive failed: no city was held, the general uprising it was meant to provoke did not happen, and communist losses ran into the tens of thousands.","The southern Vietcong were so badly weakened that the North Vietnamese Army carried most of the fighting for the rest of the war.","General Westmoreland's request for another 206,000 American troops became public in March 1968 and was read as an admission that the war could not be finished.","31 March 1968: Johnson limited the bombing of North Vietnam, offered negotiations and announced that he would not seek re-election; peace talks opened in Paris in May 1968.","The gap between official optimism and the pictures on American television became known as the credibility gap, and opinion polls showed support for the war falling sharply after Tet."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Vietnamisation, the Nixon Doctrine, Cambodia and Laos, and the end of the war, 1969–75","items":["Richard Nixon took office on 20 January 1969 promising peace with honour: an end to American involvement without an open defeat.","Vietnamisation: the Army of the Republic of Vietnam was expanded, equipped and trained with American money so that it could do the fighting while American troops withdrew.","Regular ARVN strength grew from about 427,000 at the end of 1968 to about 516,000 by 1971; counting territorial forces, more than a million South Vietnamese were under arms.","The ARVN's weaknesses were leadership by patronage, heavy desertion, corruption, and dependence on American aircraft and helicopters whenever it met serious resistance.","July 1969, Guam: the Nixon Doctrine — the United States would keep its treaty commitments and supply money and equipment, but allies would have to provide the soldiers for their own defence.","June 1969: the first withdrawal of 25,000 American troops was announced; numbers fell from the peak of about 543,000 in April 1969 to below 160,000 by the end of 1971.","Bombing of communist bases inside neutral Cambodia began in secret in March 1969.","29 April 1970: American and South Vietnamese troops invaded Cambodia; large quantities of supplies were captured but the communist headquarters was never found, and Cambodia was pushed into civil war.","4 May 1970: National Guardsmen shot dead four students during protests at Kent State University in Ohio; Congress then barred American ground troops from Cambodia and Laos.","8 February to 25 March 1971, Operation Lam Son 719: the ARVN attacked the Ho Chi Minh Trail in Laos without American ground troops and was driven back with heavy losses.","30 March 1972: North Vietnam launched the Easter Offensive; Nixon replied with Operation Linebacker from May to October 1972 and the mining of Haiphong harbour.","18 to 29 December 1972: Linebacker II, the eleven days of bombing known as the Christmas bombing, was used to force North Vietnam back to the conference table.","27 January 1973: the Paris Peace Accords — a ceasefire, the withdrawal of all American forces within sixty days and the return of prisoners of war, but North Vietnamese troops were allowed to remain in South Vietnam.","The last American combat troops left in March 1973 and nearly six hundred prisoners of war came home; Congress then barred further American military action in Indochina and cut aid to Saigon in 1974.","30 April 1975: North Vietnamese tanks entered Saigon and the South surrendered; in the final hours Americans and Vietnamese were lifted out by helicopter from the American embassy."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the source and interpretation moves this paper rewards","items":["Inference: say something the source does not state but that must be true if the source is true, then give the words that force it. One inference and one supporting detail in each box.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose: what kind of document it is, who made it and when, and why it was made.","Utility: judge each source for the enquiry the question names, never in general. Content first, then how nature, origin and purpose make that content more or less useful, then the context that confirms or qualifies it.","Contextual knowledge scores only when it is used on the source; a paragraph about the war that never touches the sources earns nothing.","'Biased' is not a judgement on its own. Say whose view the source gives, why it gives that view, and what it is therefore good evidence of: a one-sided source is excellent evidence of the view it takes.","A source can be useful for an enquiry it never mentions, if what it shows explains the thing being enquired into; say what you can infer, not only what it says.","What a source refuses to say can be its most useful sentence, because an official silence is evidence about the people who kept it.","Difference of view, question 3(b): a disagreement about the past, not a difference of wording or of detail. State the one difference, then support it with a phrase taken from each interpretation.","Reason for difference, question 3(c): interpretations differ because they ask different questions, rest on different evidence, or give weight to different parts of the story. Name which, show it from the interpretations, and use Sources B and C if they illustrate the kind of evidence involved.","Differences based on conclusions drawn from evidence are legitimate; an interpretation is not wrong simply because another one disagrees with it.","Evaluating an interpretation, question 3(d): use both interpretations and your own knowledge, show how each conveys its view, then decide how far the named interpretation can be agreed with, and say on what criterion."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 3 Question 3(d): how far the weakness of the governments of South Vietnam explains the American failure [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","items":["The question as printed: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part played by the weakness of the governments of South Vietnam in the American failure in Vietnam? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' No stimulus points are printed on this question: the two interpretations are the stimulus, both must be used, and an answer that evaluates one in isolation cannot reach the top levels.","Decode the statement. Interpretation 2 treats Saigon's weakness as a given, setting 'against that' the 'governments in Saigon resting on generals who had been plotting against one another since 1963', which is why 'no American strategy could have produced a South Vietnam able to stand on its own'. Interpretation 1 makes the same weakness an effect rather than a cause, because American methods 'made the Saigon government look like a foreigner's creature'. The criterion to announce in the opening is whether that weakness was a condition the United States inherited or one its own presence deepened.","Opening, three or four sentences: put Interpretation 2's claim about Saigon into your own words, name Interpretation 1 as the rival weighting, announce the criterion, and signal the position you will end at, so the line of reasoning is visible from the first paragraph.","Paragraph 1, for Interpretation 2 — the state lacked consent before the Americans took charge of the war. The Geneva agreement of 21 July 1954 promised elections for 1956 and Ngo Dinh Diem refused to hold them; he governed through his family and favoured Catholics in a largely Buddhist country, and during the Buddhist protests of 1963 a monk burned himself to death in a Saigon street on 11 June. Interim judgement: a government that would not risk an election and had set its own cities against it was weak in exactly the sense the interpretation means.","Paragraph 2, for Interpretation 2 — the plotting the interpretation names, dated. Diem was overthrown on 1 November 1963 and killed with his brother Nhu the next day; General Nguyen Khanh pushed the victorious generals aside on 30 January 1964; further coups and counter-coups ran through 1964 and into 1965, so South Vietnam passed through a run of short-lived governments in about eighteen months. Interim judgement: no army fights well for a government that may not be there next month, and Washington had to work with whoever emerged.","Paragraph 3, for Interpretation 2 — office held by favour rather than by politics. Source A's divisional commander says his officers 'are chosen for loyalty to the palace and will not take losses', and Saigon 'counts a hamlet as finished when the province chief reports it, and the province chief is not visited'. Nguyen Van Thieu, President from 31 October 1967, was returned in 1971 as the only candidate left on the ballot. Interim judgement: this is the strongest ground for the interpretation, because a state run as patronage cannot ask its people for sacrifice.","Paragraph 4, against Interpretation 2 — the weakness was partly American work. The Strategic Hamlet Program begun in March 1962 moved families off ancestral land in Saigon's name, with American money and often without the payment promised; the United States let the generals understand in 1963 that it would not stand in the way of a coup against Diem, and then recognised each government that followed; American money, advisers and troops made ministers answerable to Washington rather than to Vietnamese. That is Interpretation 1's 'foreigner's creature'. Interim judgement: the patron helped keep the client weak.","Paragraph 5, against Interpretation 2 and the point that goes furthest beyond both texts — the weakness was neither beyond repair nor immediately fatal. Thieu's Land-to-the-Tiller law, signed on 26 March 1970, distributed about a million hectares to some 850,000 tenant families within three years and is generally credited with reducing the tenancy grievance the National Liberation Front had lived on, which shows that political work could change the ground the war was fought on. When the North attacked in the offensive that opened on 30 March 1972 the South held, with American air power returning from 9 May 1972, and Saigon fell only on 30 April 1975, after the agreement of 27 January 1973 and after Congress cut its aid in 1974. Interim judgement: 'no American strategy could have' is too absolute a claim.","The evaluation thread that must run through every paragraph: how each interpretation conveys its view. Interpretation 2 writes Saigon in as scenery — 'against that stood governments in Saigon' — and builds a list of what the other side 'possessed' and Saigon 'never possessed', so weakness becomes a property of the place rather than the result of anything anyone did. Interpretation 1 uses a verb of making, 'made the Saigon government look like a foreigner's creature', so the same weakness becomes something done to Saigon. Noticing that contrast, and using it, is what the top level means by analysing how the differences of view are conveyed.","What the conclusion must say: not 'both are partly right'. Apply the criterion announced at the start. Interpretation 2 is right that the weakness was real and that much of it predates the American build-up — the refused elections of 1956, the coups from 1963 and the rule by favour were not manufactured in Washington. But it is wrong to treat that weakness as fixed, because the manner in which the United States propped Saigon up deepened it, and because the one serious piece of political reform, in 1970, worked. Name the deciding consideration: the weakness was inherited, then made worse, and fatal only once the patron stopped paying.","Timing and SPaG: about 40 minutes in all — six minutes to fix the criterion and order the paragraphs, about thirty to write, and the last four to check spelling and punctuation and to make sure the specialist terms are present and correctly used, since 4 additional marks depend on 'a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate'. Useful terms here: coup, junta, legitimacy, patronage, client state, National Liberation Front, Vietcong, strategic hamlet, land reform, escalation, containment, Vietnamisation."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T13:01:30.249764Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Every recorded fix holds in the kit as described. The plan is now Question 3(d) on the weakness of the governments of South Vietnam, a fourth focus on the same two printed interpretations with its own criterion, its own evaluation thread (Saigon-as-scenery against Interpretation 1's verb of making) and its own conclusion, so bank question 12 no longer arrives pre-answered; the paired model paragraphs have moved to the two sides' willingness to accept casualties, a focus no bank question sets; worked examples 4 and 5 now practise 3(b) and 3(c) on a second, declared-fictional pair about the reasons for the American withdrawal, so question 13's opening is no longer given away; the code on question 3 and lesson.check[2] are corrected; and the closing line of the sources note was updated to stay true. dupscan reports no duplication and I read every setup: no worked example, plan or model paragraph sets a bank question. All seven new facts check out on the web — Nguyen Khanh's bloodless coup of 30 January 1964, Thieu inaugurated 31 October 1967 and left as the only candidate in 1971 after Minh and Ky withdrew, the Land-to-the-Tiller law signed 26 March 1970 (a pledge of a million hectares in three years, 1,003,353 distributed by 26 March 1973), Vietnam's April 1995 figure of 1.1 million military dead, the draft lottery of 1 December 1969, conscription ended 27 January 1973 and Linebacker from 9 May 1972 — as do the figures they sit beside, some 58,000 American dead and fewer than 17,000 in 1968. I re-solved all fifteen questions: no answer key is wrong, ambiguous or at odds with its solution, the 3(d) stems now match the levels.md wording, the caps are in the post-2024 form with no Level 2 cap, and the validator passes. Publishable; the two remaining points are small enough to fix in place or to leave."}}},"33-4":{"id":"EDX-1HI0","topic":"33-4","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Key topic 4 is the part of Option 33 that Paper 3 can reach with every one of its question types at once. The home front supplies contemporary sources — letters, diaries, newspaper columns, army papers — for the inference question and for the usefulness question, and it supplies the argument historians still have about whether the marchers, the television pictures or the arithmetic of casualties and dollars ended the war, which is exactly what Section B's interpretations are made of. If you can explain, with names and dates, why the draft turned a war into a domestic crisis, why millions of Americans went on backing it anyway, how the fighting was brought to a negotiated end in January 1973 and why the strongest army in the world could not win, you can answer almost anything this key topic sets. This room teaches that story as an argument you can take sides in, and it trains the five moves the paper rewards: inference from a source, causal explanation that goes beyond the printed bullet points, judgement of a source's usefulness for the enquiry actually named, spotting how two historians differ and why, and evaluating one of them against the other and against what you know.","idea":[{"code":"33-4 — 1 Opposition to the war: Reasons for the growth of opposition","h":"Students, screens and the draft: the three engines of opposition","t":"Opposition grew from three sources at once. The student movement came first: Students for a Democratic Society, founded in 1960 and given its programme by the Port Huron Statement of 1962, ran the first teach-in at the University of Michigan through the night of 24-25 March 1965 and the first national march on Washington that April, and it was students who filled the rally of some 100,000 at the Lincoln Memorial before the March on the Pentagon of 21 October 1967. Second came the media. Vietnam was the first war Americans watched at dinner time, and respectable doubt was licensed from above as well as below: Senator J. William Fulbright's Senate Foreign Relations Committee put the war on television in February 1966, and Walter Cronkite told CBS viewers on 27 February 1968 that the USA was 'mired in stalemate'. Third, and most personal, came the draft: 2.2 million men were conscripted between 1964 and 1973, and until the lottery of 1 December 1969 local boards decided who went, so college students kept deferments while factory and farm boys did not. A top-level answer keeps the three separate and then shows how they combined — the draft made the war personal, television made it visible, and the campuses gave the anger an organisation."},{"code":"33-4 — 1 Opposition to the war: Public reaction to the My Lai Massacre (1968)","h":"My Lai: the story that changed what Americans thought they were fighting for","t":"On 16 March 1968 US soldiers of Charlie Company killed several hundred unarmed villagers, most of them women, children and old men, at My Lai in Quang Ngai province. The army investigated internally and the country heard nothing for twenty months, until the reporter Seymour Hersh, tipped off by a former soldier, published his account through a small news agency on 12 November 1969 and photographs taken by an army photographer appeared in American newspapers days later. The effect was not that Americans learned the war was violent; it was that the cover-up and the pictures made it impossible to hold the war and a clear conscience at the same time. The trial of Lieutenant William Calley, the platoon commander, split opinion again: convicted on 29 March 1971 of the premeditated murder of at least twenty-two civilians and sentenced to life imprisonment, he was moved to house arrest on the President's order within days, his sentence was cut, and he was paroled in 1974. Many opponents said he was a scapegoat for his superiors; many supporters said a soldier was being punished for the war Washington had sent him to fight. A top-level answer uses My Lai for moral damage rather than shock: it attacked the idea that the USA was defending a decent cause, and the sentence dispute showed the country arguing with itself."},{"code":"33-4 — 1 Opposition to the war: The Kent State University shootings (1970)","h":"Kent State: the war comes home","t":"On 30 April 1970 President Nixon announced that American and South Vietnamese forces had gone into Cambodia to destroy communist bases, which broke his own story that the war was being wound down. Campuses erupted; at Kent State University in Ohio the ROTC building was burned and the Governor sent in the Ohio National Guard. On 4 May 1970 Guardsmen fired into a crowd on the campus, killing four students and wounding nine; some of the dead were bystanders walking between classes. The photograph of a young woman kneeling over a body became one of the defining images of the period. A national student strike followed, closing hundreds of colleges before the end of term, and on 15 May two young black men, Phillip Gibbs, a junior at Jackson State College in Mississippi, and James Earl Green, a seventeen-year-old pupil at a nearby high school, were shot dead by police there — a killing that drew far less national attention, which itself became part of the argument. In Congress, the Senate voted in June 1970 to repeal the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution. A top-level answer makes Kent State a turning point in kind rather than in scale: after it, opposition was no longer something that happened to other people's children, and the state's own violence became an argument against the war."},{"code":"33-4 — 2 Support for the war: Reasons for support for the war","h":"Why millions of Americans went on backing the war","t":"It is easy to read the period backwards from the marches and forget that a majority approved of the war until 1968 and that large minorities approved of it long after. The first reason was the Cold War itself: containment had been American policy since 1947, the domino theory said that a communist South Vietnam would pull its neighbours down, and Americans had watched China turn communist in 1949 and fought in Korea from 1950. Abandoning an ally looked like inviting the next aggression. The second was patriotism and family: by 1969 more than half a million Americans were serving in Vietnam, and to criticise the war could feel like criticising a son or a neighbour who had gone when he was called. The third was class and culture: to many working-class Americans the protesters looked like privileged students avoiding a duty their own families could not avoid. Gallup did not record a majority saying that sending troops had been a mistake until August 1968, and even in 1971 around forty per cent still refused to say so. A top-level answer uses support as a cause in its own right: it is what allowed Johnson and Nixon to keep fighting for years after the opposition had become loud, and it is why withdrawal had to be dressed up as peace with honour."},{"code":"33-4 — 2 Support for the war: The 'hard hats' and the 'silent majority'","h":"The silent majority and the men in the hard hats","t":"On 3 November 1969, between the Moratorium demonstrations of 15 October and the huge march on Washington of 15 November, Nixon broadcast on Vietnam and appealed over the heads of the protesters to 'the great silent majority of my fellow Americans'. It worked as politics: his approval rating rose from about 52 per cent to about 68 per cent within days, and the phrase gave ordinary conservatives a name and a sense of numbers. The 'hard hats' gave the same idea a body. On 8 May 1970, four days after Kent State, several hundred New York construction workers broke up an anti-war demonstration near Wall Street, beating students and bystanders and leaving about seventy people hurt; later that month union men marched in their thousands in support of the President, and their leaders were received at the White House and gave Nixon a hard hat. The images mattered because they reversed the usual picture: here were working men in helmets against students in sandals. A top-level answer treats both as political instruments rather than simply as opinions — Nixon needed a majority that could be invoked but never had to demonstrate, and the hard hats let him claim that patriotism belonged to the working class."},{"code":"33-4 — 3 The peace process and end of the war: Reasons for, and key features of, the peace negotiations","h":"Talking while fighting: Paris, Kissinger and Le Duc Tho","t":"Formal talks opened in Paris on 13 May 1968, after Johnson's broadcast of 31 March announcing a partial bombing halt and his own withdrawal from the presidential race, and for two years they achieved almost nothing while the two sides argued about who should sit at the table. What moved matters was a change of method. From 1970 Nixon's national security adviser Henry Kissinger met the North Vietnamese negotiator Le Duc Tho in secret outside Paris, so that concessions could be explored without either government having to defend them in public, and Nixon's opening to China in 1972 and détente with the USSR reduced the help Hanoi could count on. Both sides also had reasons to settle in 1972: Nixon wanted the war out of the way before the November election, and the North's Easter Offensive had been stopped with heavy losses and heavy American bombing. Kissinger announced on 26 October 1972 that 'peace is at hand', but President Thieu of South Vietnam rejected the draft, talks broke down, and Nixon ordered the eleven days of Christmas bombing, 18-29 December 1972, against the North before the two sides returned and signed. A top-level answer explains the negotiations as pressure applied through talking, not as a break from the fighting."},{"code":"33-4 — 3 The peace process and end of the war: The significance of the Paris Peace Agreement (1973)","h":"The Paris Peace Agreement: what it settled and what it left","t":"The agreement was signed in Paris on 27 January 1973. It provided for a ceasefire in place, the withdrawal of all remaining US forces within sixty days, the return of American prisoners of war, and talks between the Vietnamese parties about a political settlement. The last US combat troops left on 29 March 1973 and the prisoners came home, which was what most Americans wanted and what let Nixon call the outcome peace with honour. Its significance for the USA and its significance for Vietnam pull in opposite directions, and that is the point an examiner is looking for. For the USA it ended a war that had taken more than 58,000 American lives, and Congress moved at once to stop any repeat, passing the War Powers Resolution over Nixon's veto on 7 November 1973 to limit the President to sixty days of combat without its consent. For Vietnam it settled nothing: North Vietnamese divisions were allowed to remain in the South, Thieu stayed in office, and fighting resumed almost immediately. When the final offensive came, Congress refused new money and Saigon fell on 30 April 1975. A top-level answer judges the agreement by whose problem it solved: an American exit document rather than a peace treaty."},{"code":"33-4 — 3 The peace process and end of the war: The economic and human costs of the war for the USA","h":"The bill: lives, dollars and trust","t":"More than 58,000 American service personnel died in Vietnam and about 300,000 were wounded; the names on the memorial in Washington are the plainest statement of the human cost. Vietnamese losses were far greater, running into millions of soldiers and civilians on both sides, and the chemical defoliant Agent Orange and the bombing left damage that outlived the war. The financial cost to the USA ran to roughly $120 billion in direct spending. Because Johnson tried to pay for the war and the Great Society at once without raising taxes early enough, the spending helped drive the inflation of the late 1960s and 1970s and forced cuts in his domestic programme, so the war damaged the reform agenda it was supposed to sit alongside. The political costs were as heavy: well over half a million young men were classed as apparent draft offenders, veterans came home to indifference or hostility, and the Pentagon Papers, published by the New York Times from 13 June 1971, showed that successive governments had misled the public, which fed the distrust that Watergate then deepened. A top-level answer uses cost as a cause and not only as a consequence: it explains why withdrawal became the only politics available, and it gives the measure against which the Paris agreement has to be judged."},{"code":"33-4 — 4 Reasons for the failure of the USA in Vietnam: The strengths of North Vietnam","h":"Why the North could not be beaten: allies, tactics and the Trail","t":"North Vietnam's first strength was foreign support that did not have to be defended in an election. The USSR supplied surface-to-air missiles, MiG fighters, artillery and trucks; China supplied small arms, food and large numbers of support and engineering troops, which freed North Vietnamese soldiers for the South. Neither ally had to answer to a hostile press. The second strength was the way the Vietcong fought. Rather than meet American firepower in the open, they used ambush, booby traps, mines and tunnel systems such as those at Cu Chi, struck at night and vanished into villages, so that American units took steady casualties without ever finding an enemy to defeat. The third was the Ho Chi Minh Trail, a shifting network of tracks, paths and repair teams running through Laos and Cambodia into the South, carrying men, rice and ammunition on bicycles and trucks. It was bombed for years, and it was widened and improved rather than closed: repair gangs filled the craters within hours, and the Cambodian incursion of 1970 did not cut it. A top-level answer ties the three together into one argument: the North could absorb losses, replace them and keep supplying the South, so American victories never became decisive."},{"code":"33-4 — 4 Reasons for the failure of the USA in Vietnam: The weaknesses of the US armed forces","h":"A superb army fighting the wrong war","t":"American failure was not a failure of equipment. Operation Rolling Thunder bombed the North from March 1965 to November 1968, dropping hundreds of thousands of tonnes of bombs, and more than half a million American troops were in the South by the end of 1968; neither produced a decision. The tactics were the problem. Search and destroy sent units out to find an enemy who chose when to be found, and success was measured in body counts that encouraged exaggeration and the killing of civilians. Air power and defoliation — napalm and Agent Orange — destroyed villages and crops and drove peasants towards the Vietcong, so the war for what the Americans called hearts and minds was lost by the means used to fight it. The army itself was worn down: conscripts served a single twelve-month tour, so units lost experience as fast as they gained it; morale fell after 1969 as men were asked to risk their lives for a war being wound down, and drug use, racial tension and attacks on unpopular officers spread. A top-level answer explains failure as a mismatch — an army built for conventional battle in Europe, fighting a political and guerrilla war it could not end by winning engagements."},{"code":"33-4 — 4 Reasons for the failure of the USA in Vietnam: The impact of opposition to the war in the USA","h":"Did the protesters end the war?","t":"This is the argument the interpretations in this room are made of, and it has two defensible sides. On one side, opposition narrowed what governments could do: Johnson faced Eugene McCarthy taking 42 per cent of the New Hampshire primary on 12 March 1968 and announced on 31 March that he would not seek re-election; Nixon's Vietnamisation, announced in 1969, took the draft and the casualty lists out of the newspapers; the Pentagon Papers of June 1971 and the veterans of the Winter Soldier hearings in Detroit from 31 January 1971 destroyed the government's credibility; and from 1970 Congress began cutting money and repealing the authority the war rested on. On the other side, protest had costs of its own. Most Americans disliked the demonstrators more than the war; Nixon's silent majority broadcast raised his support, the hard hats cheered, and he won forty-nine states in 1972 against an anti-war Democrat. A top-level answer refuses the simple verdict and sets a criterion: opposition did not decide policy directly, but it shortened the time any President could go on paying in conscripts and dollars, which is a real kind of influence."}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1: two inferences from Source A about families","setup":"Source A in this room is a letter written by a nineteen-year-old factory worker in Ohio to his older sister, who was away at college, in December 1969, just after the first draft lottery was broadcast. He writes that the whole street crowded into a neighbour's front room to watch the drawing on television; that when his birthday came out in the first forty numbers his mother 'went into the kitchen and did not come out again'; that his father 'says a man goes where he is sent', that his grandfather did the same in 1944, and that his father 'has not spoken to me properly since I asked him what the difference was'; that of the four boys who left school together in June, one is safe at college on a deferment, one is safe because the local board found that his job counts, one is already at Fort Benning, 'That leaves me'; that their local board 'has never had a member who was not a businessman from the west side of town' and that he has 'never once heard of a doctor's son going'; and that he is 'not brave enough to go to Canada and not brave enough to say so out loud'. The question is: 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about the effect of the draft on families in the United States. Complete the table below to explain your answer.' (4 marks)","steps":["Fix who is writing and for whom before reading for content: a private letter between brother and sister performs for nobody, so its asides about parents carry unusual weight.","Take the first inference from the father: he 'says a man goes where he is sent' and 'has not spoken to me properly since I asked him what the difference was', so conscription was setting one generation of a family against another.","Take the second from the mother: when the number was drawn she 'went into the kitchen and did not come out again', so families lived the lottery as a private disaster rather than as a public duty.","Check both against the question actually asked and reject anything about fairness between classes, which belongs to a different enquiry, then write each answer as a claim the source never states followed by the words that force it."],"cues":["Who is this letter for, and what does that let him admit?","Which sentence shows what the draft has done between father and son?","What does the mother do, and what does her silence reveal?","Which of your possible inferences is about families, and which is not?"]},{"title":"Question 3(a): judging Sources B and C for the enquiry actually named","setup":"Source B in this room is a letter printed in the readers' column of a newspaper in a small manufacturing town in Ohio on 13 May 1970; the writer owns a building firm, served in the US Army in Korea and has a son serving in Vietnam. He complains that nobody has printed a word about local boys 'who do not burn the flag of the country that pays for their boots', says that 'if these students had been in a classroom on Monday morning ... there would have been nothing for anyone to shoot at', declares that the President was right about the great silent majority and that 'on this street he still is', and asks the college professors to explain 'to the men who put up the walls of their fine buildings why it is the working man's son who has to do the fighting'. Source C is the diary of a student at a state university in the Midwest, written on 7 May 1970, who had never demonstrated before that week: the President 'came on the television on the last day of April' about Cambodia, 'on Monday the Guard fired at Kent State and killed four', 'by Tuesday the classes had emptied', girls who had 'never carried a placard in their lives were painting sheets in the corridor', 'a lot of students have simply gone home', and her roommate's father telephoned to say that without the Guard 'the whole country would be run by hoodlums by Christmas'. The question is: 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the divisions the war caused within American society by 1970? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context.' (8 marks)","steps":["Copy the enquiry to the top of your plan and let it select material: divisions within society, so class, generation and campus are in, and anything about the fighting in Vietnam itself is out.","Read Source B for the division it names rather than for its opinion: it sets the working man's son against the professor's, and the street against the campus, which is the split the enquiry is about.","Weigh Source B's nature, origin and purpose together: a letter written for publication days after the shootings, by a veteran with a son in Vietnam, is partisan by design, and for an enquiry into division that is a strength, since it is one side of the division speaking.","Do the same for Source C, and notice that it reports division inside the protest as well as outside it: classes emptied, but 'a lot of students have simply gone home' and a parent telephoned in support of the Guard.","Bring contextual knowledge in on the sources rather than beside them: the Cambodian announcement of 30 April 1970 and the Kent State shootings of 4 May closed hundreds of campuses, while on 8 May construction workers beat demonstrators in New York in the hard-hat riot.","Finish with a judgement for this enquiry and not a general one: between them they give both sides of the split and its geography, while neither reaches black Americans or the South, where Jackson State drew far less attention."],"cues":["What exactly is this enquiry into?","Which two groups does the writer of Source B set against each other?","Does writing for publication make a source less useful here?","What does Source C show about disagreement among students themselves?","Which event of that fortnight explains why both sources were written?","Whose experience does neither source reach?"]},{"title":"Question 2: taking a 12-mark 'Explain why' past the bullet points","setup":"The question is: 'Explain why President Johnson decided not to seek re-election in March 1968. You may use the following in your answer: • the Tet Offensive (1968) • the New Hampshire primary. You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks); on the paper Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b) and you answer one of them. The mark scheme caps at a maximum of 8 marks, and shuts Level 4 altogether, to answers that do not address three or more aspects of content, so two bullet points and nothing else cannot reach the top. The material is set out here because these events belong to Key topic 3 rather than to this room's fact files: the Tet Offensive launched on 30-31 January 1968, Walter Cronkite's 'mired in stalemate' broadcast of 27 February 1968, the New Hampshire primary of 12 March 1968, Robert Kennedy's entry into the race on 16 March, the request for a further 200,000 troops, Gallup's polling on whether sending troops had been a mistake, and the broadcast of 31 March 1968 that announced a partial bombing halt and his withdrawal.","steps":["Turn the first bullet into a causal claim rather than a heading: Tet was a military defeat for the communists but a political defeat for Johnson, because an enemy able to attack Saigon and dozens of cities at once contradicted everything the administration had been telling the public.","Make the second bullet do causal work too: Eugene McCarthy, an obscure anti-war senator, took 42 per cent of the New Hampshire vote on 12 March, which proved the President could be challenged inside his own party, and Robert Kennedy declared four days later.","Add the reason of your own that neither bullet prompts: the request from the generals for a further 200,000 men forced Johnson to choose between calling up reserves and raising taxes or admitting that escalation had failed, and his advisers told him the country would not pay.","Order the three so that they build rather than sit in a list: credibility destroyed, then the party lost, then no policy left that he could afford.","Close on the question's own words by explaining what the broadcast at the end of March actually did — a partial bombing halt, an offer of talks, and the withdrawal itself — and say which of the three reasons set the pace."],"cues":["Why did a communist military failure damage the President?","What did the primary result prove that polling alone could not?","Which reason is prompted by neither bullet point?","In what order do the three reasons make an argument?","What must the final sentence of the answer do?"]},{"title":"Question 3(b): finding the difference of view, not of detail","setup":"Interpretation 1 in this room, from a history of the American anti-war movement by a historian writing in the 1990s, argues that the movement 'changed what American governments could do': protest turned a distant war into the central question of American politics, Johnson 'abandoned the presidency rather than face his own party' in March 1968, Nixon's Vietnamisation was designed 'to take the draft and the coffins out of the newspapers', and the silent majority was 'an invention meant to disguise how far opinion had already moved'. Interpretation 2, from a study of American policy in Vietnam by a historian writing in the 1980s, argues that 'it is comfortable to believe that the marchers ended the war, but the evidence points elsewhere': 'protest was noisy rather than popular', most Americans disliked the demonstrators more than the war, Nixon's appeal to the silent majority was answered by a jump in his support, building workers were cheered for attacking students, and what ended the commitment was 'arithmetic' — the lives, the money and a Congress that finally refused to pay. The question is: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. They give different views about the reasons why American public opinion turned against the war. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations.' (4 marks)","steps":["Reduce each interpretation to one sentence beginning 'Opinion moved because…': the first says the protesters moved it, the second says the bill did.","Test that this really is a difference of view: both accept that opinion did move and both mention the silent majority, so the disagreement is about what did the work, not about what happened.","Anchor each half of the difference in the interpretation's own words — 'changed what American governments could do' against 'protest was noisy rather than popular' and 'arithmetic'.","Write the finished answer as one difference stated first and then supported from both interpretations, rather than as two summaries placed side by side."],"cues":["What does each writer say moved opinion?","Do they disagree about the facts or about the weighting?","Which phrase in each carries the view?","What shape should the finished answer take?"]},{"title":"Question 3(d): evaluating Interpretation 2 against Interpretation 1 and your own knowledge","setup":"The question is: 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the effect of the anti-war protests on American public opinion? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]. Interpretation 2 says that 'protest was noisy rather than popular', that most Americans disliked the demonstrators more than they disliked the war, that this is 'why Nixon's appeal to the silent majority in November 1969 was answered by a jump in his support, and why building workers in New York were cheered for attacking students the following May', and that what ended the American commitment was arithmetic rather than marching. Interpretation 1 says the movement 'changed what American governments could do', that protest 'turned a distant war into the central question of American politics', that Johnson 'abandoned the presidency rather than face his own party', and that the silent majority was 'an invention meant to disguise how far opinion had already moved'. No stimulus points are printed on this question.","steps":["State in your own words the claim being judged and name the criterion: whether protest pushed opinion towards the demonstrators' position or away from the demonstrators themselves — the two are not the same.","Build the case for Interpretation 2 out of precise context rather than the interpretation repeated: Nixon's broadcast of 3 November 1969 lifted his approval from about 52 to about 68 per cent, construction workers broke up a demonstration in New York on 8 May 1970 and were received at the White House, and Nixon won forty-nine states in 1972 against an anti-war opponent.","Turn to Interpretation 1 for the case against and give that context too: Gallup first recorded a majority calling the war a mistake in August 1968, the Fulbright hearings of February 1966 and Cronkite's broadcast of 27 February 1968 made doubt respectable, and after the Pentagon Papers appeared in June 1971 the government's word was worth little.","Show how each interpretation conveys its view, because the top level asks for exactly that: one writes the language of accounting, arithmetic and refusing to pay, while the other writes the language of agency, changing what governments could do.","Judge on the criterion you named and explain why the losing view is not simply wrong: opinion moved against the war and against the protesters at the same time, so the protests can be unpopular and still have narrowed what a President could afford to do."],"cues":["What is Interpretation 2 actually claiming about opinion?","Which facts of 1969 and 1970 support it?","What does the other interpretation put in its place?","How does each writer's vocabulary carry the view?","What must your final paragraph settle?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Why did the revelation of My Lai in November 1969 do more damage than the massacre itself had done in March 1968?","a":"Because the country only learned of it twenty months later, through a reporter rather than the army, so the cover-up became part of the story: the pictures and the delay together attacked the claim that the USA was fighting for a decent cause."},{"q":"Why is a one-sided document like Source B still useful for an enquiry into the divisions the war caused in American society?","a":"Because the enquiry is into division, and the letter is one side of that division speaking: its purpose, to rally the writer's neighbours against the students, is evidence of the split rather than a fault in the source."},{"q":"Name one thing the Paris Peace Agreement of January 1973 gave the USA and one thing it failed to settle.","a":"It gave the USA a ceasefire, the withdrawal of its remaining forces within sixty days and the return of its prisoners of war; it failed to settle Vietnam's future, because North Vietnamese troops were allowed to stay in the South and fighting resumed almost at once."},{"q":"Give one piece of evidence for, and one against, the claim that the anti-war movement forced the USA out of Vietnam.","a":"For: Johnson announced on 31 March 1968 that he would not seek re-election after the New Hampshire primary, and Congress from 1970 cut the money and repealed the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution. Against: Nixon's silent majority broadcast of 3 November 1969 raised his approval sharply and he won forty-nine states in 1972 against an anti-war Democrat."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 3 Section A (16 marks): Question 1 prints Source A and asks 'Give two things you can infer from Source A about...' in a table of two boxes each (4 marks); Question 2 is a choice of 2(a) or 2(b), and you answer one of them: each is 'Explain why...' with two stimulus points and 'You must also use information of your own.' (12 marks).","Paper 3 Section B (36 marks), all on Question 3: (a) 'How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into...?' (8); (b) 'What is the main difference between these views?' (4); (c) 'Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about...' (4); (d) 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about...?' (16, plus up to 4 marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology).","The stimulus-point caps on the 12-mark 'Explain why...': 'Maximum 8 marks for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content' and 'No access to Level 4 for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content'. There is no cap at Level 2. Question 3(d) prints no stimulus points, so no cap applies to it.","Timing on Paper 3: 1 hour 30 minutes for 52 marks, so about a minute a mark plus thinking time. Roughly 5 minutes on Question 1, 18 on Question 2, 12 on 3(a), 6 each on 3(b) and 3(c), and about 40 minutes on 3(d) including the final check for the SPaG marks, which leaves a few minutes in hand.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose, and usefulness is always judged for the enquiry the question names, never in general. Contextual knowledge earns nothing on 3(a) or 3(d) unless it is used on the sources or the interpretations themselves."],"questions":[{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about the working of the draft system in the United States. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for each valid inference, up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail selected from Source A. Credit unequal exposure to the draft, deferments protecting the better off, or the composition of local boards.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: the draft did not fall equally on young American men. Details in the source that tell me this: the writer says Danny 'is safe because his father got him into the college at Kent and he keeps the deferment while he studies', while he himself works in a factory with nothing to protect him. (ii) What I can infer: the local boards deciding who went were drawn from one narrow part of the community. Details in the source that tell me this: he writes that their board 'has never had a member who was not a businessman from the west side of town', and that he has 'never once heard of a doctor's son going'.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["An inference is something the source makes you certain of without ever stating it; ask what has to be true about the system for these four school friends to end up so differently.","Read the letter twice: once for who escapes the draft and why, and once for who is making the decisions.","Give each answer in two parts, a claim the source never states and then the exact words that force it."],"codes":["33-4 — 1 Opposition to the war: Reasons for the growth of opposition"]},{"q":"Study Source A in this room's sources section. Give two things you can infer from Source A about the feelings of young American men about being drafted. Complete the table below to explain your answer: (i) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this; (ii) What I can infer / Details in the source that tell me this. (4 marks)","a":"AO3 4 marks, point-marked: 1 mark for each valid inference, up to two, and a second mark each for supporting detail from Source A. Credit fear, a sense of having no acceptable choice, or reluctance to admit opposition publicly.","sol":"(i) What I can infer: young men facing the draft often felt trapped rather than willing. Details in the source that tell me this: the writer ends by saying he is 'not brave enough to go to Canada and not brave enough to say so out loud', so neither obeying nor refusing felt like something he could choose. (ii) What I can infer: opposition to being drafted was hard to admit openly, even inside a family. Details in the source that tell me this: he only questioned his father by asking 'what the difference was' between his own case and his grandfather's in 1944, and his father 'has not spoken to me properly since'.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Look at the last sentence of the letter and ask what it admits about both of the choices open to him.","Watch how he raises the subject with his father: the way he does it tells you what could and could not be said aloud.","Set out a claim the source implies, then the phrase that makes it certain, for each of your two answers."],"codes":["33-4 — 1 Opposition to the war: Reasons for the growth of opposition"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. They give different views about the part played by the anti-war movement in ending US involvement in Vietnam. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4) needs a key difference of view supported from both: Interpretation 1 makes the movement the decisive force, Interpretation 2 makes cost and Congress decisive and the movement a minority irritant. Surface detail only stays in Level 1.","sol":"The main difference is over whether the protesters actually brought the war to an end. Interpretation 1 says the movement 'changed what American governments could do': it turned the war into 'the central question of American politics', drove Johnson to abandon the presidency 'rather than face his own party', and forced Nixon into withdrawals designed 'to take the draft and the coffins out of the newspapers'. Interpretation 2 says they did not, because 'protest was noisy rather than popular'; what ended the American commitment was 'arithmetic' — more than fifty thousand dead, sums 'the economy could no longer carry', and a Congress that 'at last refused to pay'.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Reduce each interpretation to one sentence beginning 'American involvement ended because...' and compare the two sentences.","Check that your difference is one of view: both writers agree the war ended and both mention Nixon, so look for what each says did the causal work.","State the difference first in a single sentence, then support each half with words taken from its own interpretation."],"codes":["33-4 — 4 Reasons for the failure of the USA in Vietnam: The impact of opposition to the war in the USA"]},{"q":"Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about the part played by the anti-war movement in ending US involvement in Vietnam. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer. (4 marks)","a":"AO4 4 marks. Level 2 (3-4) needs one explained reason, effectively substantiated: for example that the two historians study different subjects and so weigh different evidence, or that they give weight to different aspects of the same period.","sol":"One reason is that the two historians set out to study different things, so they weigh different evidence. Interpretation 1 comes from a history of the anti-war movement, so its evidence is what the protesters did and what it forced from politicians — the teach-ins, the marches, Johnson's withdrawal — and from inside that story the movement looks decisive. Interpretation 2 comes from a study of American policy, so its evidence is what governments had to count: casualties, budgets and votes in Congress. Read from those papers, and from the kind of opinion Source B records when a builder insists the silent majority is still behind the President, the demonstrators look like a noisy minority rather than a cause.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Historians differ legitimately when they ask different questions of the past; look at what each interpretation is a study of.","Ask which documents each writer would have spent the most time reading, and what those documents would make prominent.","Name one reason, then show it at work in both interpretations, using a source if it illustrates the kind of evidence involved."],"codes":["33-4 — 4 Reasons for the failure of the USA in Vietnam: The impact of opposition to the war in the USA","33-4 — 2 Support for the war: The 'hard hats' and the 'silent majority'"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into reactions in the USA to the Kent State University shootings of May 1970? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): utility judged for this enquiry, with developed reasoning on how nature, origin and purpose affect the content, and context used to interpret. Credit Source C on the campus strike and Source B on the anger directed at the students.","sol":"Source C is the more useful of the two for the reaction on the campuses. It is a diary written on 7 May, three days after the shootings, by a student who had never demonstrated before that week, and it records precisely the change the enquiry is about: 'by Tuesday the classes had emptied', and girls who had 'never carried a placard in their lives were painting sheets in the corridor'. A private diary has no case to make, and its date makes it evidence of reaction rather than of later memory; against that, one Midwestern campus cannot stand for the country, and she notes that 'a lot of students have simply gone home'. Source B is useful for the reaction the campuses did not see. Printed in an Ohio newspaper on 13 May by a builder whose son is in Vietnam, it turns the anger away from the Guard and onto the students: 'if these students had been in a classroom on Monday morning ... there would have been nothing for anyone to shoot at'. Written to persuade his neighbours, it is one-sided by design, and for this enquiry that is a strength, because such opinion was a real part of the reaction. My own knowledge supports both: a student strike closed hundreds of colleges, while on 8 May construction workers beat demonstrators in New York. Together they catch the two halves of the reaction; neither reports the government's.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Usefulness is always usefulness for something: copy the enquiry out before you look at either source.","Handle each source in turn as content, then nature, origin and purpose, then the context that confirms or qualifies it, and only then compare them.","Begin with the source written closest to the event and make your first sentence a judgement rather than a description."],"codes":["33-4 — 1 Opposition to the war: The Kent State University shootings (1970)","33-4 — 2 Support for the war: The 'hard hats' and the 'silent majority'"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the reasons why many Americans went on supporting the war in 1970? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): criteria-based judgements with developed reasoning on how provenance affects the content. Credit Source B on family service, class resentment and the silent majority, and Source C indirectly through the roommate's father and the students who went home.","sol":"Source B is much the more useful of the two, because it is a supporter of the war explaining himself. The writer gives three reasons in a few lines: loyalty to men already serving, including his own son, who 'does not write to complain'; contempt for protesters who 'burn the flag of the country that pays for their boots'; and a class grievance, that 'it is the working man's son who has to do the fighting' while the professors' sons make speeches. He also invokes the President's 'great silent majority' and insists that 'on this street he still is' right. Its provenance sharpens rather than weakens this: a letter written for publication days after Kent State by a veteran with a son in Vietnam is designed to rally his neighbours, so it shows the arguments that were thought likely to persuade ordinary readers. Source C is useful only indirectly, since it is written by an opponent, but it confirms that such opinion was widespread: the diarist reports that 'a lot of students have simply gone home' and that her roommate's father telephoned to say that without the Guard 'the whole country would be run by hoodlums by Christmas'. My own knowledge supports both, since Nixon's silent majority broadcast of 3 November 1969 had lifted his approval sharply and construction workers attacked demonstrators in New York on 8 May 1970. Neither source, however, mentions fear of communism, which was the deepest reason of all, so both are partial.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["The enquiry is into reasons for support; ask of each source whether it gives reasons, records them at second hand, or neither.","A source written by an opponent can still be useful evidence about supporters; say what it lets you infer rather than only what it states.","Start by deciding which of the two is closer to the enquiry, and open with that judgement and the reason for it."],"codes":["33-4 — 2 Support for the war: Reasons for support for the war","33-4 — 2 Support for the war: The 'hard hats' and the 'silent majority'"]},{"q":"Study Sources B and C in this room's sources section. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the effects of the invasion of Cambodia (1970) on opinion in the USA? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your own knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)","a":"AO3 8 marks. Level 3 (6-8): utility judged for this enquiry with provenance reasoning and context used to interpret. Credit Source C on the collapse of belief that the war was being wound down, and Source B for showing that the decision cost the President nothing among supporters.","sol":"Source C is directly useful, because it dates the change of mood to the announcement itself. The diarist writes that the President 'came on the television on the last day of April' to say he had sent the army into Cambodia, and that 'everything that we had been told about the war being wound down turned out to be worth nothing'. That is the key effect: Vietnamisation had promised fewer Americans in Asia, and Cambodia looked like the opposite. As a private diary written on 7 May by someone who had never demonstrated before, it is candid and close in time, and it shows the invasion converting an uncommitted student rather than only exciting activists; but it is one campus, and she admits that others went home instead. Source B is useful for the other half of the answer, the opinion the invasion did not move. The writer never mentions Cambodia at all, yet insists the silent majority is still behind the President; for this enquiry that silence is itself evidence that among his supporters the decision changed nothing. Its purpose, to rally a small town against the students, makes it a reliable guide to that constituency and a poor guide to any other. My own knowledge confirms this: the announcement of 30 April 1970 set off the protests that led to Kent State on 4 May, and in June 1970 the Senate voted to repeal the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution. Neither source reaches Congress, whose reaction mattered most.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Fix the date of the announcement first, then check what each source could possibly know about its effects.","A source that never mentions the event can still be useful; ask what its silence implies about the writer's part of the country.","Open with the source that dates the change of mood, and make the first sentence a judgement about usefulness."],"codes":["33-4 — 1 Opposition to the war: The Kent State University shootings (1970)","33-4 — 2 Support for the war: Reasons for support for the war"]},{"q":"Explain why opposition to the war in Vietnam grew in the USA in the years 1965-70. You may use the following in your answer: • the draft system • the Kent State University shootings (1970). You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise knowledge — conscription and deferments, the lottery of 1969, television and the Fulbright hearings, My Lai, Cambodia and Kent State. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Opposition grew because the war reached into ordinary American lives through three different doors, and because each time the government tried to close one it opened another. The first reason is the draft. Some 2.2 million men were conscripted between 1964 and 1973, and until the lottery of 1 December 1969 local boards decided who went, so a student deferment kept the well-off at college while factory and farm boys were called; in 1965 African Americans were about eleven per cent of the population but suffered roughly a quarter of the US Army's deaths in Vietnam. A system that visibly fell on some families and not others created opponents who had no interest in politics at all, which is why campus organisations such as Students for a Democratic Society, founded in 1960, could turn resentment into marches like the one that drew about 100,000 people to the Lincoln Memorial before the March on the Pentagon in October 1967. The second reason is what Americans could now see and hear. Vietnam was the first war reported nightly on television, and doubt was licensed from respectable quarters: Senator Fulbright's committee hearings were broadcast in February 1966, and Walter Cronkite told viewers on 27 February 1968 that the war was 'mired in stalemate'. The third reason, prompted by neither bullet point, is that the government's own conduct destroyed its credibility. My Lai, kept quiet for twenty months until Seymour Hersh published in November 1969, suggested the war was not the decent cause claimed for it. Kent State then made the violence domestic: after Nixon announced the Cambodian incursion on 30 April 1970, National Guardsmen killed four students on 4 May and a strike closed hundreds of campuses. Conscription supplied the grievance, television supplied the audience, and the state's own actions supplied the proof.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Opposition is not the same as disapproval: ask what made the war a personal matter for people who were not political.","Plan three reasons of different kinds — one about who had to fight, one about what people could see, and one about whether the government could be believed.","Open on the draft and finish that paragraph by saying what unequal conscription did to families who had never protested about anything."],"codes":["33-4 — 1 Opposition to the war: Reasons for the growth of opposition","33-4 — 1 Opposition to the war: The Kent State University shootings (1970)","33-4 — 1 Opposition to the war: Public reaction to the My Lai Massacre (1968)"]},{"q":"Explain why many Americans continued to support the war in Vietnam in the years 1965-70. You may use the following in your answer: • fear of communism • the 'silent majority'. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): analysis directed at causation — containment and the domino theory, loyalty to serving relatives, class hostility to students, the silent majority broadcast and the hard hats. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"Support lasted because most Americans judged the war by beliefs formed long before it began, and because the opposition gave them something to be against. The first reason is anti-communism. Containment had been American policy since 1947, China had turned communist in 1949 and American troops had fought in Korea from 1950, so the domino theory sounded like recent experience rather than a theory; on that view a retreat from Saigon would simply move the next war closer. This was not a fringe belief: Gallup did not record a majority saying that sending troops had been a mistake until August 1968, more than three years after the ground war began. The second reason is that by 1969 more than half a million Americans were serving in Vietnam, so criticism of the war was easily heard as criticism of a son or a neighbour who had gone when he was called; families with men in the field had powerful reasons to believe the sacrifice was worth something. The third reason, which neither bullet prompts, is class resentment. To many working-class Americans the demonstrators looked like privileged students using deferments their own sons could not obtain, and the anger was sharp enough to become violent: on 8 May 1970, four days after Kent State, New York construction workers broke up an anti-war march and injured about seventy people, and their leaders were afterwards received at the White House. Nixon understood all of this, and his broadcast of 3 November 1969 appealing to 'the great silent majority' converted it into political capital, lifting his approval from about 52 to about 68 per cent. Belief, loyalty and resentment together gave two Presidents the room to go on fighting for years after the marches had begun.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Support is a cause, not a background: ask what it allowed Johnson and Nixon to do that they otherwise could not.","Build three reasons of different kinds — one about the Cold War, one about families, one about class — so the answer analyses rather than lists.","Begin with what Americans had already lived through between 1947 and 1953 and what that made Vietnam look like."],"codes":["33-4 — 2 Support for the war: Reasons for support for the war","33-4 — 2 Support for the war: The 'hard hats' and the 'silent majority'"]},{"q":"Explain why the My Lai Massacre had such a strong effect on opinion in the USA. You may use the following in your answer: • Seymour Hersh's reports (1969) • the trial of Lieutenant Calley. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise detail — the killings of 16 March 1968, the twenty-month delay and the cover-up, the photographs, the conviction of 29 March 1971 and Nixon's intervention. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"My Lai damaged opinion so severely because of how Americans found out, what they saw and what was then done about it. The first reason is the delay and the concealment. The killings took place on 16 March 1968, when soldiers of Charlie Company killed several hundred unarmed villagers, most of them women, children and old men, at My Lai in Quang Ngai province; the army investigated internally and the public heard nothing for twenty months. When the reporter Seymour Hersh published his account through a small news agency on 12 November 1969, the story was therefore two stories, a massacre and a cover-up, and the second was about Washington rather than about one platoon. The second reason is evidence that needed no interpretation. An army photographer's colour pictures of the dead appeared in American newspapers and magazines within weeks, at a moment when Vietnam was already the first war watched nightly on television; photographs of that kind could not be argued away as enemy propaganda. The third reason, prompted by neither bullet point, is that the trial divided the country instead of settling it. Lieutenant William Calley was convicted on 29 March 1971 of the premeditated murder of at least twenty-two civilians and sentenced to life imprisonment, but the President ordered him moved to house arrest within days, his sentence was cut and he was paroled in 1974. Opponents of the war said he was a scapegoat for the commanders who set the tactics; supporters said a young officer was being punished for a war the government had sent him to fight. The effect, therefore, was not shock alone but the destruction of the claim that the USA was fighting a clean war for a decent cause, and that loss of trust ran on into the reception of the Pentagon Papers in June 1971.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask why the manner of the revelation mattered as much as the event itself.","Separate three things: how the country learned, what it was shown, and what happened to the man convicted.","Open on the gap between March 1968 and November 1969 and say what that gap told Americans about their own army."],"codes":["33-4 — 1 Opposition to the war: Public reaction to the My Lai Massacre (1968)","33-4 — 1 Opposition to the war: Reasons for the growth of opposition"]},{"q":"Explain why the USA and North Vietnam signed the Paris Peace Agreement in January 1973. You may use the following in your answer: • Vietnamisation • the Christmas bombing (December 1972). You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): sustained causal analysis — Vietnamisation and troop withdrawals, the secret Kissinger-Le Duc Tho talks from 1970, the 1972 election and détente, Thieu's rejection of the October draft and the bombing of 18-29 December 1972. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The agreement was signed because both governments had reached the point where a settlement cost them less than continuing, and because a method had been found that let each make concessions without admitting them. The first reason is Vietnamisation. From 1969 Nixon handed the ground fighting to the South Vietnamese army and withdrew American troops in stages from a peak of about 543,000 in April 1969, so by the end of 1972 American ground forces had almost all gone. That policy created its own necessity: once the troops were leaving there was nothing left to bargain with except the timing, and the return of the prisoners of war, which the American public cared about most, could only be obtained by agreement. The second reason, which neither bullet prompts, is that the diplomacy changed. Formal talks had opened in Paris in May 1968 and achieved nothing in public; from 1970 Henry Kissinger met Le Duc Tho secretly outside Paris, where concessions could be tested without either side having to defend them at home. Nixon's opening to China and his visit to Moscow in 1972 also reduced the support Hanoi could rely on, while the failure of the North's Easter Offensive that year showed that a quick military victory was out of reach. The third reason is force applied at the end. Kissinger announced on 26 October 1972 that 'peace is at hand', but President Thieu rejected the draft and the talks broke down, so Nixon ordered eleven days of heavy bombing of the North from 18 to 29 December 1972 and privately pressed Thieu with promises of support and threats of a separate deal. Both sides returned to the table, and the agreement was signed on 27 January 1973. Withdrawal removed the American reason to fight, secrecy made a bargain possible, and the bombing settled the terms.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["A settlement needs both sides to want it: take the American reasons and the North Vietnamese reasons in turn.","Ask what had changed in the way the two sides were talking, not only in what they were fighting over.","Begin with what Vietnamisation had already removed from Nixon's hand by 1972."],"codes":["33-4 — 3 The peace process and end of the war: Reasons for, and key features of, the peace negotiations","33-4 — 3 The peace process and end of the war: The economic and human costs of the war for the USA","33-4 — 4 Reasons for the failure of the USA in Vietnam: The impact of opposition to the war in the USA"]},{"q":"Explain why the Paris Peace Agreement of 1973 failed to bring peace to Vietnam. You may use the following in your answer: • North Vietnamese troops in the South • the War Powers Act (1973). You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): causation analysed with precise detail — the ceasefire in place, no political settlement, Thieu's weakness, the War Powers Resolution of 7 November 1973, Watergate and the cutting of aid, the fall of Saigon on 30 April 1975. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The agreement failed because it was written to end an American war rather than a Vietnamese one, and because the guarantees that were supposed to make it work disappeared within months. The first reason is the terms themselves. The ceasefire signed on 27 January 1973 was a ceasefire in place, which allowed the North's divisions to remain where they stood inside South Vietnam, and the political future of the South was left to talks between parties that had been fighting each other for twenty years. The USA got what it needed — withdrawal within sixty days, completed on 29 March 1973, and the return of its prisoners — while the two Vietnamese sides got a pause in which to prepare. The second reason is that Thieu's government could not stand on its own. It had never won broad support, its army had been built to fight alongside American air power and supplies, and once the Americans had gone it faced an opponent with secure bases, the Ho Chi Minh Trail still open and continuing Soviet and Chinese aid. The third reason, and the decisive one, is that the American promise to return if the agreement were broken proved worthless. Congress had had enough: it passed the War Powers Resolution over Nixon's veto on 7 November 1973, limiting the President to sixty days of combat without its consent, and it cut military aid to Saigon sharply, while Watergate destroyed Nixon and drove him from office in August 1974. When the North launched its final offensive in March 1975 no American aircraft came, and Saigon fell on 30 April 1975. A settlement that left both armies in place, propped up a government that could not survive alone and rested on a guarantee the guarantor's own legislature had removed was never a peace at all.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Read the terms before you explain the failure: ask what the agreement actually required each side to give up.","Three different kinds of reason are available — the terms, the South Vietnamese state, and American politics after 1973.","Open on the phrase 'ceasefire in place' and say what it allowed the North Vietnamese army to keep."],"codes":["33-4 — 3 The peace process and end of the war: The significance of the Paris Peace Agreement (1973)","33-4 — 3 The peace process and end of the war: The economic and human costs of the war for the USA","33-4 — 4 Reasons for the failure of the USA in Vietnam: The strengths of North Vietnam"]},{"q":"Explain why the USA failed to win the war in Vietnam. You may use the following in your answer: • the Ho Chi Minh Trail • US tactics. You must also use information of your own. (12 marks)","a":"AO2 6 + AO1 6. Level 4 (10-12): analytical explanation of failure — the Trail and Soviet and Chinese aid, guerrilla tactics and tunnels, search and destroy, Rolling Thunder, defoliants, the one-year tour and falling morale, and pressure at home. Stimulus cap: maximum 8 marks, and no access to Level 4, for answers that do not address three or more aspects of content.","sol":"The USA failed because it fought a political war by military means, against an enemy who could replace everything it lost and against a clock set in Washington rather than in Saigon. The first reason is that North Vietnam could not be cut off. The Ho Chi Minh Trail was not a road but a shifting network of tracks and repair teams running through Laos and Cambodia, carrying men, rice and ammunition by bicycle and truck; it was bombed for years and widened rather than closed, and the Cambodian incursion of April 1970 did not sever it. Behind it stood the USSR, which supplied surface-to-air missiles, MiG fighters and artillery, and China, which supplied small arms and large numbers of support troops, so losses in the South were made good. The second reason is that American tactics worked against American aims. Search and destroy sent units to find an enemy who chose when to be found, and progress was measured in body counts that rewarded exaggeration; Operation Rolling Thunder bombed the North from March 1965 to November 1968 without breaking its will; napalm and the defoliant Agent Orange destroyed villages and crops and pushed peasants towards the Vietcong, so the war for popular support was lost by the very means used to fight it. The third reason, prompted by neither bullet, is the state of the forces and of opinion at home. Conscripts served a single twelve-month tour, so units lost experience as fast as they gained it, and after 1969 morale fell sharply as men were asked to die for a war being wound down, with drug use, racial tension and attacks on unpopular officers spreading. Meanwhile opposition at home and the cost of roughly $120 billion meant that time, the one resource the North had in abundance, was the one the USA did not have.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Failure needs both sides explaining: what the enemy could do, and what the Americans did to themselves.","Plan three reasons of different kinds — supply, tactics, and the condition of the army and of opinion at home.","Begin with the Trail and say precisely why years of bombing did not close it."],"codes":["33-4 — 4 Reasons for the failure of the USA in Vietnam: The strengths of North Vietnam","33-4 — 4 Reasons for the failure of the USA in Vietnam: The weaknesses of the US armed forces","33-4 — 4 Reasons for the failure of the USA in Vietnam: The impact of opposition to the war in the USA"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the impact of opposition to the war in the USA on the ending of American involvement in Vietnam? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views evaluated, how each conveys its view analysed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit cost, casualties and congressional votes; against, Johnson's withdrawal in 1968, Vietnamisation and the collapse of credibility after the Pentagon Papers.","sol":"Interpretation 2 argues that 'it is comfortable to believe that the marchers ended the war, but the evidence points elsewhere': protest was 'noisy rather than popular', and what ended the American commitment was 'arithmetic' — more than fifty thousand dead, a bill the economy could not carry, an ally that could not be made to win, and a Congress that 'at last refused to pay'. Interpretation 1 makes the movement itself decisive. The criterion for judging between them must be whether opposition changed the decisions that were actually taken, not whether the demonstrators were liked. Interpretation 2 is strong on the arithmetic. By 1973 more than 58,000 Americans had died and about 300,000 had been wounded, and direct spending ran to roughly $120 billion, which helped drive inflation and forced cuts in Johnson's Great Society. Its point about unpopularity is also well supported. Nixon's broadcast of 3 November 1969 appealing to 'the great silent majority' lifted his approval from about 52 to about 68 per cent; on 8 May 1970 New York construction workers beat anti-war demonstrators and their leaders were received at the White House; and in 1972 Nixon won forty-nine states against an openly anti-war Democrat. A movement that could be beaten in the street and at the polls plainly did not command the country. The claim is weaker where it separates cost from politics. The reason the bill became unpayable was that the public would no longer accept the price in conscripts, and it was opposition that made that refusal visible. Johnson faced Eugene McCarthy taking 42 per cent in the New Hampshire primary on 12 March 1968, Robert Kennedy entered the race four days later, and on 31 March Johnson announced a partial bombing halt and his own withdrawal. Interpretation 1 is right that Nixon's Vietnamisation was designed to 'take the draft and the coffins out of the newspapers': withdrawing troops while bombing more heavily makes sense only as a policy aimed at the home front. And the credibility that made continued spending possible was destroyed at home, by My Lai from November 1969 and by the Pentagon Papers from 13 June 1971. Congress had already repealed the authority the war had rested on — the Senate voted on 24 June 1970 and the repeal was signed on 12 January 1971 — and it went on to cut the funds. The two convey their views by different vocabularies, and that is part of the argument. Interpretation 2 writes in the language of accounting — arithmetic, cost, refusing to pay — which makes opinion look like weather rather than action; Interpretation 1 writes in the language of agency, of a movement that changed what governments could do. I agree with Interpretation 2 that the marchers did not force the withdrawal directly, and that hostility to them was real and politically useful to Nixon. But its account of the arithmetic is incomplete, because a democracy's willingness to pay is itself political, and opposition is what made non-payment respectable, first in the primaries of 1968 and then in Congress after 1970. Opposition did not end the war; it shortened the time any President could go on fighting it, which is a real and decisive kind of influence.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first what Interpretation 2 is claiming actually ended the war, and name the test your judgement will apply.","Use Interpretation 1 as your case against, and support both sides with dated detail rather than with the interpretations restated.","Open by putting Interpretation 2's claim in your own words and stating the criterion you will judge it by."],"codes":["33-4 — 4 Reasons for the failure of the USA in Vietnam: The impact of opposition to the war in the USA","33-4 — 3 The peace process and end of the war: The economic and human costs of the war for the USA","33-4 — 2 Support for the war: The 'hard hats' and the 'silent majority'"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the reasons for President Nixon's policy of Vietnamisation? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views weighed, how the difference is conveyed analysed, precise context, justified judgement. For agreement credit cost, the state of the US army and the search for a bargaining position; against, the draft, the timing of withdrawals and the domestic calendar.","sol":"Interpretation 2 treats Vietnamisation as an accountant's policy: because the war had cost more than fifty thousand American lives and sums 'the economy could no longer carry', and because Congress would not go on paying, 'Vietnamisation was a way of leaving cheaply'. Interpretation 1 treats it as a policy aimed at the American public, 'designed above all to take the draft and the coffins out of the newspapers'. The criterion must be what the policy was built to reduce: the cost of the war, or its visibility. There is real strength in Interpretation 2. The direct bill had reached roughly $120 billion, the spending had helped drive inflation and squeeze Johnson's Great Society, and the army itself was wearing out, with morale falling after 1969, drug use and racial tension spreading and attacks on unpopular officers becoming a known problem. Handing the ground fighting to the South Vietnamese while keeping American air power was a genuine attempt to hold a position at a price the USA could afford, and Nixon needed to keep bargaining strength in Paris rather than simply run. Nor was the policy a surrender to the streets: Nixon appealed over the protesters' heads on 3 November 1969, and his approval rose from about 52 to about 68 per cent. Yet Interpretation 1 explains the shape of the policy better. Vietnamisation did not reduce the violence; in 1970 Nixon sent forces into Cambodia and in 1972 he ordered the most intense bombing campaign of the whole war against the North. What it reduced was American exposure: troop numbers fell in announced stages from a peak of about 543,000 in April 1969, and the draft was made less arbitrary by the lottery of 1 December 1969 and ended altogether in January 1973. Those are measures aimed at American families, not at the Vietnamese battlefield. The timing points the same way, since withdrawals were announced at politically useful moments and the settlement was pressed to a conclusion before the election of November 1972. The two interpretations convey the difference through their nouns. Interpretation 2 uses the vocabulary of cost, arithmetic and cheapness, which makes policy the product of sums; Interpretation 1 uses the vocabulary of publicity, newspapers and coffins, which makes policy the product of pressure. Neither writer denies the other's facts; they disagree about which of them Nixon was answering. On balance I agree with Interpretation 2 only in part. The cost was real and it set the outer limits of what could be attempted, but cost alone would have argued for a faster withdrawal or a negotiated exit in 1969. What explains the particular shape of Vietnamisation — bombing kept up while troop numbers and draft calls came down — is the need to make the war invisible to voters without appearing to lose it. Interpretation 2 gives the constraint; Interpretation 1 gives the motive.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Distinguish what a policy is limited by from what it is aimed at before you agree or disagree.","Test the claim against what Vietnamisation did not reduce, and ask what that tells you about its purpose.","Open by stating Interpretation 2's account of the policy in your own words and naming the criterion your judgement will use."],"codes":["33-4 — 4 Reasons for the failure of the USA in Vietnam: The impact of opposition to the war in the USA","33-4 — 3 The peace process and end of the war: The economic and human costs of the war for the USA","33-4 — 1 Opposition to the war: Reasons for the growth of opposition"]},{"q":"Study Interpretations 1 and 2 in this room's sources section. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the importance of the 'silent majority' in President Nixon's handling of the war? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context. [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","a":"AO4 16 marks plus up to 4 SPaG; no stimulus points. Level 4 (13-16): both views evaluated with analysis of how each conveys its view, precise context and a justified judgement. For agreement credit the approval figures, the hard hats and 1972; against, the polling that shows opinion already moving and the withdrawals that continued regardless.","sol":"Interpretation 2 treats the silent majority as a real constituency whose existence shaped what Nixon could do: protest was 'noisy rather than popular', which is 'why Nixon's appeal to the silent majority in November 1969 was answered by a jump in his support, and why building workers in New York were cheered for attacking students the following May'. Interpretation 1 calls the same thing 'an invention meant to disguise how far opinion had already moved'. The criterion must be whether the silent majority described the country or merely helped Nixon manage it. Interpretation 2 has the stronger immediate evidence. The broadcast of 3 November 1969 came between the Moratorium of 15 October and the very large march on Washington of 15 November, and it lifted Nixon's approval from about 52 to about 68 per cent in days, which is not the behaviour of an invented audience. On 8 May 1970, four days after Kent State, several hundred New York construction workers broke up an anti-war demonstration and injured about seventy people; later that month union men marched in their thousands for the President and their leaders were received at the White House. In 1972 Nixon carried forty-nine states against an openly anti-war Democrat. Millions of Americans plainly did resent the demonstrators, and Nixon could count on them. Interpretation 1's criticism nonetheless bites in two places. First, disliking protesters is not the same as supporting the war. Gallup had recorded a majority saying that sending troops had been a mistake since August 1968, and the figure was around sixty per cent by 1971, so the silent majority was never a majority for continuing the fighting. Second, Nixon's own actions fit Interpretation 1 better than his rhetoric does. In the very months when he was invoking the silent majority he was cutting troop numbers from their peak of about 543,000 in April 1969, replacing the old draft with the lottery of 1 December 1969 and ending conscription altogether in January 1973 — measures taken to reduce the war's cost to American families, which would be unnecessary if the country were solidly behind him. The two writers convey their difference through the status they give the phrase. Interpretation 2 uses it as a description, resting on measurable reactions, a jump in support, a crowd cheering; Interpretation 1 uses it as a device, a thing 'invented' to 'disguise', which turns evidence of popularity into evidence of management. I agree with Interpretation 2 that the silent majority was politically important and not simply a fiction: it existed, it could be mobilised, and it bought Nixon four more years in which to leave on his own terms. But I agree with Interpretation 1 about what it was for. It was a majority against the protesters rather than for the war, and Nixon used it to cover a withdrawal he had already decided upon. Its importance lay in protecting his flank while he ended American involvement, not in persuading him to continue it.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Separate two different questions: was the silent majority real, and was it a majority for the war?","Test Interpretation 2 against what Nixon was doing to troop numbers and to the draft in the same months.","Begin by putting Interpretation 2's claim about the silent majority into your own words and naming your criterion."],"codes":["33-4 — 2 Support for the war: The 'hard hats' and the 'silent majority'","33-4 — 2 Support for the war: Reasons for support for the war","33-4 — 4 Reasons for the failure of the USA in Vietnam: The impact of opposition to the war in the USA"]}],"exit":[{"q":"On what date did the Ohio National Guard shoot students at Kent State University, and how many students were killed?","a":"4 May 1970; four students were killed (and nine wounded)."},{"q":"On what date did President Nixon appeal in a televised address to 'the great silent majority'?","a":"3 November 1969."},{"q":"On what date was the Paris Peace Agreement signed?","a":"27 January 1973."},{"q":"Who negotiated secretly for the USA and for North Vietnam in the talks that produced the Paris Peace Agreement?","a":"Henry Kissinger for the USA and Le Duc Tho for North Vietnam."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The draft (Selective Service) in the Vietnam years","open":false,"code":"33-4 — 1 Opposition to the war: Reasons for the growth of opposition","back":"About 2.2 million American men were conscripted between 1964 and 1973. Local boards chose who went, and deferments for college students meant the burden fell hardest on poorer and black families."},{"front":"The draft lottery of 1 December 1969","open":false,"code":"33-4 — 1 Opposition to the war: Reasons for the growth of opposition","back":"Birth dates were drawn on live television to set the order of call, greatly reducing the discretion of local boards. It was meant to answer charges of unfairness; conscription ended altogether in January 1973."},{"front":"Students for a Democratic Society (SDS) and the teach-ins","open":false,"code":"33-4 — 1 Opposition to the war: Reasons for the growth of opposition","back":"SDS was founded in 1960 and set out its programme in the Port Huron Statement of 1962. The first teach-in ran at the University of Michigan through the night of 24-25 March 1965, and the model spread across the country."},{"front":"Walter Cronkite's broadcast of 27 February 1968","open":false,"code":"33-4 — 1 Opposition to the war: Reasons for the growth of opposition","back":"After visiting Vietnam during the Tet Offensive, the CBS anchorman told viewers the USA was 'mired in stalemate'. It made doubt respectable for middle-class Americans who trusted him."},{"front":"The My Lai Massacre","open":false,"code":"33-4 — 1 Opposition to the war: Public reaction to the My Lai Massacre (1968)","back":"On 16 March 1968 US soldiers of Charlie Company killed several hundred unarmed villagers, mostly women, children and old men, at My Lai. Seymour Hersh published the story on 12 November 1969."},{"front":"The trial of Lieutenant William Calley","open":false,"code":"33-4 — 1 Opposition to the war: Public reaction to the My Lai Massacre (1968)","back":"Convicted on 29 March 1971 of the premeditated murder of at least 22 civilians and sentenced to life. Nixon had him moved to house arrest, his sentence was cut, and he was paroled in 1974."},{"front":"The Kent State University shootings","open":false,"code":"33-4 — 1 Opposition to the war: The Kent State University shootings (1970)","back":"On 4 May 1970, during protests against the Cambodian incursion, Ohio National Guardsmen fired on a campus crowd, killing four students and wounding nine. A national student strike closed hundreds of colleges."},{"front":"The Jackson State College shootings","open":false,"code":"33-4 — 1 Opposition to the war: The Kent State University shootings (1970)","back":"On 15 May 1970 police fired at Jackson State in Mississippi, killing two young black men: Phillip Gibbs, a student there, and James Earl Green, a high-school pupil. The killings drew far less attention than Kent State."},{"front":"Name one reason why many Americans supported the war in Vietnam","open":true,"code":"33-4 — 2 Support for the war: Reasons for support for the war","back":"Fear of communism and the domino theory, after China in 1949 and Korea from 1950; loyalty to the half a million relatives and neighbours serving by 1969; resentment of student protesters who escaped the draft."},{"front":"The 'silent majority'","open":false,"code":"33-4 — 2 Support for the war: The 'hard hats' and the 'silent majority'","back":"Nixon's phrase in his televised address of 3 November 1969, appealing over the protesters to ordinary Americans who backed him. His approval rose from about 52 to about 68 per cent within days."},{"front":"The 'hard hats'","open":false,"code":"33-4 — 2 Support for the war: The 'hard hats' and the 'silent majority'","back":"New York construction workers who broke up an anti-war demonstration on 8 May 1970, injuring about seventy people. Union leaders later marched for Nixon and were received at the White House."},{"front":"The Paris peace negotiations, 1968-73","open":false,"code":"33-4 — 3 The peace process and end of the war: Reasons for, and key features of, the peace negotiations","back":"Public talks opened in Paris on 13 May 1968 and stalled. From 1970 Henry Kissinger met Le Duc Tho in secret; after Thieu rejected the October 1972 draft, Nixon ordered the Christmas bombing of 18-29 December 1972."},{"front":"The Paris Peace Agreement","open":false,"code":"33-4 — 3 The peace process and end of the war: The significance of the Paris Peace Agreement (1973)","back":"Signed 27 January 1973: a ceasefire in place, US withdrawal within sixty days, return of prisoners of war. North Vietnamese troops stayed in the South, and Saigon fell on 30 April 1975."},{"front":"The costs of the war to the USA","open":false,"code":"33-4 — 3 The peace process and end of the war: The economic and human costs of the war for the USA","back":"More than 58,000 American dead and about 300,000 wounded; roughly $120 billion in direct spending, which fed inflation and squeezed Johnson's Great Society; and a lasting loss of trust in government."},{"front":"The Ho Chi Minh Trail","open":false,"code":"33-4 — 4 Reasons for the failure of the USA in Vietnam: The strengths of North Vietnam","back":"A shifting network of tracks and repair teams through Laos and Cambodia carrying men, rice and ammunition south by bicycle and truck. Years of bombing and the 1970 Cambodian incursion never closed it."}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The sources and interpretations written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Source A: From a letter written by a nineteen-year-old factory worker in Ohio to his older sister, who was away at college, December 1969, shortly after the first draft lottery was broadcast. Written for this room in the style of such a letter; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — We watched the drawing on the television in Mr Pulaski's front room, with half the street crowded in. When they pulled my birthday out in the first forty numbers Mom went into the kitchen and did not come out again. Dad says a man goes where he is sent and that his own father did the same in 1944, and he has not spoken to me properly since I asked him what the difference was. There were four of us who left school together in June. Danny is safe because his father got him into the college at Kent and he keeps the deferment while he studies; Ray is safe because the board here found that his job counts; Tom is already at Fort Benning and writes that he has been made a corporal. That leaves me. Our board has never had a member who was not a businessman from the west side of town, and I have never once heard of a doctor's son going. I am not brave enough to go to Canada and not brave enough to say so out loud.","Source B: From a letter printed in the readers' column of a newspaper in a small manufacturing town in Ohio, 13 May 1970. The writer owns a building firm in the town, served in the US Army in Korea, and has a son serving in Vietnam. Written for this room in the style of such a letter; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — I have read enough this week about the four who were shot. Nobody has printed a word about the boys from this county who are being shot at every day in a place they did not choose to go to, and who do not burn the flag of the country that pays for their boots. My own boy is out there now and he does not write to complain. If these students had been in a classroom on Monday morning, as their parents are paying for them to be, there would have been nothing for anyone to shoot at. The President told us last autumn that the great silent majority of Americans were behind him, and he was right, and on this street he still is. I would ask the college professors who encourage all this to explain to the men who put up the walls of their fine buildings why it is the working man's son who has to do the fighting while theirs makes speeches about it.","Source C: From the diary of a student at a state university in the Midwest, written on 7 May 1970. She had taken part in no demonstration before that week. Written for this room in the style of such a diary; it is not a real document and reproduces no real one. — A week ago this was an ordinary campus. The President came on the television on the last day of April and told us he had sent the army into Cambodia, and everything that we had been told about the war being wound down turned out to be worth nothing. On Monday the Guard fired at Kent State and killed four. By Tuesday the classes had emptied. Girls I know who have never carried a placard in their lives were painting sheets in the corridor, and the boys talk about nothing but numbers and deferments. Not everybody is with us. A lot of students have simply gone home, and my roommate's father telephoned to say that if the Guard had not fired the whole country would be run by hoodlums by Christmas. I am frightened, but I am more frightened of saying nothing.","Interpretation 1: From a history of the American anti-war movement, by a historian writing in the 1990s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — The movement against the war in Vietnam changed what American governments could do. From the first teach-ins of 1965 to the marches of 1969, protest turned a distant war into the central question of American politics, and it did so faster than any earlier campaign. Johnson could barely appear in public without being barracked, and in March 1968 he abandoned the presidency rather than face his own party. Nixon inherited a war he could not win and dared not lose, and the withdrawals he called Vietnamisation were designed above all to take the draft and the coffins out of the newspapers. The silent majority he appealed to was an invention meant to disguise how far opinion had already moved.","Interpretation 2: From a study of American policy in Vietnam, by a historian writing in the 1980s. Written for this room; it is not a real book, and it is not attributed to any real historian. — It is comfortable to believe that the marchers ended the war, but the evidence points elsewhere. Protest was noisy rather than popular: most Americans disliked the demonstrators more than they disliked the war, which is why Nixon's appeal to the silent majority in November 1969 was answered by a jump in his support, and why building workers in New York were cheered for attacking students the following May. What finally ended the American commitment was arithmetic. The war had cost more than fifty thousand American lives and sums the economy could no longer carry, the South Vietnamese army could not be made to win, and Congress at last refused to pay. Vietnamisation was a way of leaving cheaply.","How the room uses them: Source A is the inference source for Question 1. Sources B and C are the contemporary pair for the 8-mark usefulness question, 3(a). Interpretations 1 and 2 are the pair for 3(b), 3(c) and 3(d). Every quotation in the model answers, worked examples, plan and model paragraphs of this room comes from the five texts above and from nowhere else."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 3 Question 3(d), 16 marks)","items":["Question: 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part played by Congress in bringing American involvement in Vietnam to an end? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]. This is one paragraph from the middle of the answer, testing Interpretation 2's claim that 'Congress at last refused to pay'.","Interpretation 2 makes Congress the body that finally closed the account, because on its reading the commitment ended when 'Congress at last refused to pay'. The legislative record supports it, since the war was in the end stopped by votes rather than by any settlement between the Vietnamese. The Senate voted 81 to 10 on 24 June 1970 to repeal the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution, the authority on which the fighting had rested, and the repeal was signed into law on 12 January 1971; the Case-Church amendment, signed on 1 July 1973, forbade the spending of any funds on American combat anywhere in Indochina after 15 August 1973, and the bombing of Cambodia stopped on that day; the War Powers Resolution was carried over Nixon's veto on 7 November 1973; and Congress voted only $700 million of the $1 billion authorised in military aid to South Vietnam for the financial year 1975 before refusing President Ford's request for a further $722 million in April 1975, weeks before Saigon fell on 30 April. What Interpretation 2 does not explain is why a Congress that had passed the resolution in August 1964 with only two dissenting voices should reverse itself. Each of those votes followed a movement of opinion outside the chamber: Eugene McCarthy's 42 per cent in the New Hampshire primary of 12 March 1968, the Moratorium of 15 October 1969, Kent State, and the Pentagon Papers of 13 June 1971, which showed that the public had been misled. That is precisely what Interpretation 1 means when it says protest 'changed what American governments could do'. Congress was therefore the instrument through which involvement was ended rather than the cause of the ending, and Interpretation 2 mistakes the hand that signed for the pressure that moved it.","Why this is Level 4: it 'provides an explained evaluation reviewing the alternative views in coming to a substantiated judgement' — Interpretation 2 is granted its full strength from the legislative record before it is measured against Interpretation 1 and found to explain the mechanism but not the cause. The contextual knowledge is 'precisely selected to support the evaluation' rather than general: the repeal vote and the date the repeal became law, the funding cut-off of 15 August 1973, the veto override of 7 November 1973 and the two aid figures of 1975. The analysis shows how the differences of view are conveyed — Interpretation 2's accountancy of refusing to pay set against Interpretation 1's language of changing what governments could do — and the closing sentence judges rather than summarises, so the line of reasoning is 'coherent, sustained and logically structured'."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same as above — 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part played by Congress in bringing American involvement in Vietnam to an end?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks].","Interpretation 2 says that Congress at last refused to pay for the war, and I partly agree with this. Congress did stop the money in the end and passed laws to limit what the President was allowed to do, so the fighting had to finish. However, Interpretation 1 says the protest movement changed what American governments could do, which is also true because there were enormous marches and the politicians were frightened of them. Congressmen have to be elected, so they listened to the voters and turned against the war as well. Overall I think both interpretations are partly right, because Congress did cut the funds and the protests were also important.","Why this is Level 2 and not Level 3: it does offer 'valid evaluative comment to agree with or counter the interpretation' and it does select details from both interpretations, which is what Level 2 asks for. What it misses is Level 3's 'relevant contextual knowledge used directly to support the evaluation': 'stop the money', 'passed laws' and 'enormous marches' stand where the named votes and figures should be — the repeal of the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution, voted on 24 June 1970 and signed on 12 January 1971, the ban on funding American combat in Indochina after 15 August 1973, the War Powers Resolution of 7 November 1973, and the $722 million refused in April 1975. It also never shows how the interpretations convey their views, and its judgement is announced in the last sentence rather than reasoned towards, so 'its justification is insecure or undeveloped and a line of reasoning is not sustained'. Turning it into a Level 3 answer means replacing each general phrase with a dated vote that does evaluative work."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the growth of opposition — the student movement, the media and the draft","items":["Students for a Democratic Society was founded in 1960 and set out its programme in the Port Huron Statement of 1962; it became the largest student organisation of the decade.","The first teach-in against the war ran through the night of 24-25 March 1965 at the University of Michigan in Ann Arbor, and the model spread to campuses across the country within weeks.","SDS organised the first national march against the war in Washington in April 1965, a few weeks after the bombing of North Vietnam began under Operation Rolling Thunder.","Senator J. William Fulbright's Senate Foreign Relations Committee held televised hearings on the war in February 1966, which made public criticism respectable.","About 2.2 million American men were conscripted between 1964 and 1973 out of a pool of roughly 27 million; Congress made the burning of a draft card a federal offence in 1965.","Deferments protected college students and men in occupations the local boards judged essential, so conscription fell hardest on poorer, less educated and black Americans.","In 1965 African Americans were about eleven per cent of the US population but suffered roughly a quarter of the US Army's fatal casualties in Vietnam.","Muhammad Ali refused induction on 28 April 1967, was stripped of his world heavyweight title and convicted; the Supreme Court overturned the conviction in 1971.","About 100,000 people rallied at the Lincoln Memorial on 21 October 1967 and between 35,000 and 50,000 marched on to the Pentagon.","Walter Cronkite told CBS viewers on 27 February 1968, after visiting Vietnam during the Tet Offensive, that the USA was 'mired in stalemate'.","Anti-war demonstrators clashed with police outside the Democratic Convention in Chicago, 26-29 August 1968; the official Walker Report later called what happened a police riot.","Gallup first recorded a majority of Americans saying that sending troops to Vietnam had been a mistake in August 1968, and the figure was around sixty per cent by 1971.","The Moratorium demonstrations of 15 October 1969 and the march on Washington of 15 November 1969 were among the largest protests in American history to that date.","A national draft lottery was drawn on live television on 1 December 1969; conscription was ended in January 1973.","The New York Times began publishing the Pentagon Papers, leaked by Daniel Ellsberg, on 13 June 1971; they showed that successive governments had misled the public about the war."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the My Lai Massacre (1968), the revelations and the trial of Lieutenant Calley","items":["On 16 March 1968 soldiers of Charlie Company, 1st Battalion, 20th Infantry, killed several hundred unarmed villagers at My Lai in Quang Ngai province; most were women, children and old men.","The helicopter pilot Hugh Thompson landed between the soldiers and a group of villagers and helped rescue some of them; he was decorated for it only many years later.","The army's own inquiry at the time reported a successful action against enemy forces, and nothing reached the American public for twenty months.","A former soldier, Ronald Ridenhour, wrote to members of Congress and to the Pentagon in 1969 demanding an investigation, which reopened the case.","The reporter Seymour Hersh published his account through a small news agency on 12 November 1969, and it was carried by newspapers across the USA.","Colour photographs taken by an army photographer, Ronald Haeberle, appeared in American newspapers and magazines within weeks of Hersh's report.","Lieutenant William Calley, the platoon commander, was convicted on 29 March 1971 of the premeditated murder of at least 22 civilians and sentenced to life imprisonment.","Within days President Nixon ordered Calley moved from the stockade to house arrest at Fort Benning; his sentence was later reduced and he was paroled in 1974.","Opinion split sharply: many opponents of the war called Calley a scapegoat for his superiors, while many supporters believed a soldier was being punished for the war itself.","Captain Ernest Medina, Calley's company commander, was tried and acquitted in 1971; no senior officer was convicted over the massacre or its concealment."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Cambodia, the Kent State University shootings (1970) and what followed","items":["On 30 April 1970 President Nixon announced on television that American and South Vietnamese forces had entered Cambodia to destroy communist bases and supply lines.","The announcement contradicted the impression given by Vietnamisation that American involvement was winding down, and protests broke out on campuses at once.","At Kent State University in Ohio the ROTC building was burned on 2 May 1970 and the Governor sent in the Ohio National Guard.","On 4 May 1970 Guardsmen fired into a crowd on the campus, killing four students — Allison Krause, Jeffrey Miller, Sandra Scheuer and William Schroeder — and wounding nine.","Some of those shot were not demonstrators but students walking between classes; a photograph of a young woman kneeling over a body became one of the defining images of the period.","A nationwide student strike followed: walkouts spread to hundreds of campuses and many colleges closed early for the summer.","On 15 May 1970 police fired at Jackson State College in Mississippi, killing two young black men: Phillip Gibbs, a junior at the college, and James Earl Green, a seventeen-year-old pupil at a nearby high school; the killings drew far less national attention.","On 8 May 1970 several hundred New York construction workers attacked an anti-war demonstration near Wall Street, injuring about seventy people.","The Senate voted in June 1970 to repeal the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution of 1964, the authority on which the war had been fought.","Nixon set up the President's Commission on Campus Unrest in June 1970 to investigate the Kent State and Jackson State shootings; American forces left Cambodia by the end of that month."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: support for the war, the 'hard hats' and the 'silent majority'","items":["Containment had been American policy since the Truman Doctrine of 1947; China became communist in 1949 and American troops fought in Korea from 1950 to 1953.","The domino theory held that a communist victory in one country would pull its neighbours down, which made withdrawal from Vietnam look like an invitation to the next war.","US forces in Vietnam peaked at about 543,000 in April 1969, so millions of American families had a relative, neighbour or workmate serving.","Opinion polls showed majority approval of the war until 1968, and even in 1971 around forty per cent of Americans still refused to call it a mistake.","Nixon's televised address of 3 November 1969 appealed to 'the great silent majority of my fellow Americans' over the heads of the demonstrators.","The speech came between the Moratorium of 15 October and the march on Washington of 15 November 1969, and lifted Nixon's approval rating from about 52 to about 68 per cent within days.","The 'hard hat riot' of 8 May 1970 saw New York construction workers beat anti-war demonstrators and bystanders near Wall Street; about seventy people were hurt.","Later in May 1970 tens of thousands of construction and dock workers marched in New York in support of the President, and their union leaders were received at the White House and presented Nixon with a hard hat.","Many working-class Americans resented student protesters who held deferments their own sons could not obtain, so the war divided the country by class as well as by generation.","In the presidential election of November 1972 Nixon won forty-nine of the fifty states against George McGovern, who had campaigned on ending the war at once."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the peace process, the Paris Peace Agreement (1973) and the costs of the war","items":["Johnson announced a partial halt to the bombing of North Vietnam and his own withdrawal from the presidential race in a broadcast on 31 March 1968.","Formal peace talks opened in Paris on 13 May 1968 and made almost no progress in public for two years.","Nixon announced Vietnamisation in 1969, handing the ground fighting to the South Vietnamese army, and announced the first withdrawal of 25,000 American troops in June 1969.","From 1970 Henry Kissinger, Nixon's national security adviser, met the North Vietnamese negotiator Le Duc Tho in secret outside Paris.","Nixon's visits to China in February 1972 and to Moscow in May 1972 reduced the diplomatic support North Vietnam could rely on.","The North's Easter Offensive of 1972 was halted with heavy losses and heavy American bombing, which made a negotiated settlement more attractive to Hanoi.","Kissinger announced on 26 October 1972 that 'peace is at hand', but President Thieu of South Vietnam rejected the draft and the talks broke down.","The 'Christmas bombing' of North Vietnam ran from 18 to 29 December 1972; the two sides then returned to the table.","The Paris Peace Agreement was signed on 27 January 1973: a ceasefire in place, withdrawal of all remaining US forces within sixty days, the return of American prisoners of war, and talks between the Vietnamese parties.","North Vietnamese forces were allowed to remain in the South and Thieu stayed in office, so the agreement settled nothing between the Vietnamese sides; the last US combat troops left on 29 March 1973.","Congress passed the War Powers Resolution over Nixon's veto on 7 November 1973: the President must report the commitment of forces within 48 hours and may not keep them in combat beyond sixty days without congressional approval.","Nixon resigned over Watergate in August 1974; Congress cut aid to Saigon, and when the North launched its final offensive in March 1975 no American forces returned.","Saigon fell on 30 April 1975; American helicopters evacuated thousands of people from the city in the final two days.","More than 58,000 American service personnel died and about 300,000 were wounded; Vietnamese military and civilian deaths ran into millions.","Direct American spending on the war ran to roughly $120 billion, which helped drive inflation and forced cuts in Johnson's Great Society programme."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: why the USA failed — North Vietnam's strengths and American weaknesses","items":["The USSR supplied North Vietnam with surface-to-air missiles, MiG fighter aircraft, artillery and trucks; China supplied small arms, food and large numbers of support and engineering troops.","The Ho Chi Minh Trail was a shifting network of tracks, paths and repair teams running through Laos and Cambodia into South Vietnam, carrying men and supplies by bicycle and truck.","Years of American bombing widened rather than closed the Trail, and the Cambodian incursion of April 1970 failed to cut it.","Vietcong tactics avoided open battle: ambushes, booby traps, mines, night attacks and tunnel systems such as those at Cu Chi, with fighters melting back into the villages.","North Vietnam and the Vietcong were fighting for national independence on their own soil and could absorb very heavy casualties without breaking.","Operation Rolling Thunder bombed North Vietnam from March 1965 to November 1968 without destroying its will or its ability to supply the South.","Search and destroy operations sent American units to find an enemy who chose when to be found, and success was measured in body counts that encouraged exaggeration.","Napalm and the defoliant Agent Orange destroyed villages, forests and crops, killing and injuring civilians and driving peasants towards the Vietcong.","American conscripts served a single twelve-month tour, so units lost experienced men as fast as they gained them.","After 1969 morale fell sharply as men were asked to risk death in a war being wound down; drug use, racial tension and attacks on unpopular officers became serious problems.","The South Vietnamese government was widely seen as corrupt and unrepresentative, so the Americans were defending a state many Vietnamese would not fight for.","Opposition and cost at home meant the USA was fighting against a clock; North Vietnam only had to avoid defeat until the Americans left."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the source and interpretation moves this paper rewards","items":["Inference: say something the source does not state but that must be true if the source is true, then give the words that force it. One inference and one supporting detail in each box.","Provenance means nature, origin and purpose: what kind of document it is, who made it and when, and why it was made.","Utility: judge each source for the enquiry the question names, not in general. Content first, then how nature, origin and purpose make that content more or less useful, then the context that confirms or qualifies it.","Contextual knowledge scores only when it is used on the source; a paragraph about the period that never touches the sources earns nothing.","'Biased' is not a judgement on its own. Say whose view the source gives, why it gives that view, and what it is therefore useful for: a one-sided source is excellent evidence of the view it takes.","A source can be useful for an enquiry it never mentions, if what it shows explains the thing being enquired into; say what you can infer, not only what it says.","Difference of view (3(b)): a disagreement about the past, not a difference of wording or of detail. State the one difference, then support it with a phrase from each interpretation.","Reason for difference (3(c)): interpretations differ because they ask different questions, rest on different evidence, or give weight to different aspects of the story. Name which, and show it from the interpretations, using Sources B and C if they illustrate the kind of evidence involved.","Differences based on conclusions drawn from evidence are legitimate; an interpretation is not wrong simply because another one disagrees with it.","Evaluating an interpretation (3(d)): use both interpretations and your own knowledge, show how each conveys its view, then decide how far the named interpretation can be agreed with and say on what criterion."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Paper 3 Question 3(d): 'How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part played by the cost of the war in bringing American involvement in Vietnam to an end?' [16 marks] [+4 SPaG marks]","items":["The question as printed: 'Study Interpretations 1 and 2. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the part played by the cost of the war in bringing American involvement in Vietnam to an end? Explain your answer, using both interpretations, and your knowledge of the historical context.' Note that this question prints no stimulus points: the two interpretations are the stimulus, and both must be used, so an answer that evaluates one in isolation cannot reach the top levels.","Decode the statement. Interpretation 2 claims that the ending of American involvement is explained by 'arithmetic' — more than fifty thousand dead, sums the economy could no longer carry, a South Vietnamese army that could not be made to win, and a Congress that 'at last refused to pay' — and that 'Vietnamisation was a way of leaving cheaply'. Interpretation 1 claims that the anti-war movement 'changed what American governments could do'. The criterion to be stated in the opening is whether the cost acted as an independent cause, or whether it became decisive only because opposition made the country unwilling to go on paying it.","Opening (three or four sentences): state Interpretation 2's claim in your own words, name Interpretation 1 as the alternative weighting, announce the criterion, and signal the position you will end at, so the line of reasoning is visible from the start.","Paragraph 1, for Interpretation 2 — the human bill. More than 58,000 American service personnel dead and about 300,000 wounded by the end of the war, with casualties running heaviest in 1968-69, and the draft touching 2.2 million families between 1964 and 1973. Interim judgement: no government could ask indefinitely for that, whatever anyone marched about.","Paragraph 2, for Interpretation 2 — the money and the army. Roughly $120 billion in direct spending, inflation, and cuts to Johnson's Great Society; the US army itself wearing out after 1969, with falling morale, drug use and racial tension. Interim judgement: the machine as well as the budget was reaching its limits, which is a cost Interpretation 1 never mentions.","Paragraph 3, against Interpretation 2 — a bill is only unpayable if a country decides not to pay. The USA had carried heavier burdens in 1941-45; what altered by 1968 was the politics. Eugene McCarthy took 42 per cent in New Hampshire on 12 March 1968, Robert Kennedy entered four days later, and Johnson withdrew on 31 March. Interim judgement: opposition, not the casualty list, removed the President.","Paragraph 4, against Interpretation 2 and the point that goes furthest beyond both texts — the collapse of credibility that made further payment impossible. My Lai from November 1969, the trial of Calley in 1971, and the Pentagon Papers from 13 June 1971. Congress had already begun taking back the authority before the Papers appeared: the Senate voted to repeal the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution on 24 June 1970 and the repeal was signed into law on 12 January 1971. It went on to cut aid to Saigon and to pass the War Powers Resolution over Nixon's veto on 7 November 1973. Interim judgement: the refusal to pay was a political verdict on the government's honesty, which is what Interpretation 1 means by changing what governments could do.","Paragraph 5, holding the two together — Vietnamisation. Interpretation 2 calls it 'a way of leaving cheaply'; Interpretation 1 calls it a way of taking the draft and the coffins out of the newspapers. Note that Nixon cut troop numbers from about 543,000 in April 1969 while bombing more heavily, which fits a policy aimed at American opinion rather than at Vietnamese battlefields. Interim judgement: cost set the limits, opinion chose where inside them to stand.","The evaluation thread that must run through every paragraph: how each interpretation conveys its view. Interpretation 2 writes in the vocabulary of accounting — arithmetic, cost, refusing to pay — which makes the ending look automatic and removes agency from everyone; Interpretation 1 writes in the vocabulary of agency — a movement that changed what governments could do, an invented silent majority — which removes it from everyone but the protesters. Saying this, and using it, is what the top level means by analysing how the differences of view are conveyed.","What the conclusion must say: not 'both are partly right'. It must apply the criterion announced at the start — that the cost was a real and necessary condition, since the lives and the dollars set the outer limit of what was possible, but not a sufficient cause, since the same costs had been borne in earlier years without a withdrawal. Then name the deciding consideration: what converted an expensive war into an impossible one was the domestic refusal, visible in the primaries of 1968 and in the votes of Congress after 1970, which is Interpretation 1's ground. Cost is better read as the form the political refusal took.","Timing and SPaG: about 40 minutes in all — six to plan the paragraphs and fix the criterion, about thirty to write, and the last four to check spelling and punctuation and to make sure the specialist terms are both present and correctly used, since 4 additional marks depend on 'a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate'. Useful terms here: draft, deferment, conscription, Vietnamisation, silent majority, Moratorium, containment, domino theory, ceasefire, War Powers Resolution, interpretation, provenance."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T12:56:05.891719Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":1,"notes":"Re-judged against the kit file after the fix pass: all six recorded fixes are in place and correct, and I found nothing left to fault, so the cap I applied last time is lifted. Verified on the web: the Senate voted 81 to 10 on 24 June 1970 on Dole's amendment to the Foreign Military Sales Act and the repeal of the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution was signed into law on 12 January 1971, so the plan's Paragraph 4 and question 13's solution now put it correctly before the Pentagon Papers of 13 June 1971; Case-Church was signed on 1 July 1973 and barred funds for American combat anywhere in Indochina after 15 August 1973, the day the bombing of Cambodia stopped; Congress authorised $1 billion for South Vietnam for the financial year 1975 and appropriated $700 million, and Ford's request for a further $722 million, made on 10 April 1975, was refused; Phillip Lafayette Gibbs was a twenty-one-year-old junior at Jackson State and James Earl Green a seventeen-year-old senior at Jim Hill High School, which is now what the lesson, the Cambodia fact file and card 7 say. Source C's opening is redated to 'A week ago', which squares with the address of 30 April and the shootings of Monday 4 May; worked example 3 no longer claims the fact files supply its material and says honestly that Tet, the New Hampshire primary, Kennedy's entry and the request for 200,000 more men belong to Key topic 3 — a legitimate choice, since the family rule asks each example to work the same form on a different focus. The 3(d) stem is normalised to the levels.md wording in all six places (three bank questions, the plan, the worked example and the model-paragraph pair), and levels.md's other form, 'your own knowledge', is still correctly used on 3(a)¹. The new model-paragraph pair on the part played by Congress is strong: the Level 4 paragraph grants Interpretation 2 the whole legislative record — the repeal vote and its signature, the 15 August 1973 cut-off, the veto override of 7 November 1973, the two 1975 aid figures — before judging Congress the instrument rather than the cause, and it correctly notes that the resolution passed in August 1964 with only two dissenting voices; the Level 2 partner misses exactly the named votes its commentary says it misses. I re-solved all sixteen questions and re-read every quotation against the five printed texts: the answer keys, tariffs, AO splits, cap wording, SPaG placing and the 1 hour 30 timing all hold, every solution is inside its word band (the 16-mark answers now 538, 477 and 496 words), the difficulty spread is four, nine and three, all eleven ideas are tested, and the cards stay inside 40 words. Duplication: dupscan reports none, and the setups confirm it — there are now six distinct 3(d) foci, the bank's three (the impact of opposition, the reasons for Vietnamisation, the importance of the silent majority), the plan's cost of the war, worked example 5's effect of the protests on public opinion and the model paragraphs' part played by Congress, so nothing in the bank arrives pre-answered and the paragraphs no longer shadow the plan. Publish it as it stands."}}}} };
